(window.KG_PARTS = window.KG_PARTS || []).push({"group":"epics_great","chapters":{"cc:1":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1,"title":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து, பதிகம் 1-15 (1/2)","section":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து, பதிகம்","theme":"The invocation goes for refuge to the god of gods, whose bliss is self-won and whose qualities never cease, and to his dharma and assembly; the poet asks the learned to wash his flaws as one washes a gem and to accept his words for their true matter, then begins the patikam's summary of Civakan's story: Saccandan's fall through desire and deafness to counsel, Vijayai's flight to the burning ground, Civakan's birth, learning, first victories, the powder contest of Suramanjari and Gunamalai, the dying dog, and the god who rescues him from prison."},"cc:2":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2,"title":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து, பதிகம் 16-29 (2/2)","section":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து, பதிகம்","theme":"The second half of the patikam runs the story from Civakan's rescue by a god through his wanderings and marriages in the Pallava land, the Takka land and the middle country, his return to his mother, the killing of his enemy and his just rule, to his renunciation at the height of power, the renunciation of his whole household, and the closing verse in which a higher bliss makes women's pleasure unwanted."},"cc:3":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 30-53 (1/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The Namagal ilampakam opens with the land of Emangadam: the poet calls on learning and fortune, then follows the monsoon from clouds grazing the sea to a flood that carries the mountain's wealth like a generous hand and returns to the sea, and then the ploughing, worshipful sowing and transplanting of the fields, ending on ripened paddy that bows like the learned."},"cc:4":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":4,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 54-77 (2/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The second run of the description of Hemangadam: the harvest of red paddy from reaping to threshing, the cane presses and the carts of goods, then the pools, groves, houses and mansions of its towns, its pleasures, its daily thousands of feedings and charities, and a closing claim that it is a place for penance and for wealth alike, as if heaven had come down to earth."},"cc:5":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":5,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 78-101 (3/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The poet describes the kingdom's capital from outside in: its people's refusal of the courtesan's snare, then groves, ponds, elephant enclosures and arenas, training grounds, crowded and gilded streets, the moat and the cloud-clasped wall, ending in a catalogue of the war engines on the wall. No character acts; the city is built up as abundant, pleasure-filled and, at its edge, armed."},"cc:6":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":6,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 102-125 (4/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The description of Rajamapuram continues: the engines on its wall and the wall as a maiden, the heaven-like inner city, the courtesans' quarter where kings linger, the crowded market of honest merchants, the many-sided streets, and the start of the houses; praise of wealth and pleasure is set out without judgement, with one hint (kings held by desire) that the story will take up."},"cc:7":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":7,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 126-149 (5/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The poet completes his praise of Rajamapuram, the city of Emangadam: banners, incense, gold swept away as refuse, guests fed from gold vessels, gifts to beggars, gods who watch unblinking, festivals without end, and wealth credited to the Jina's shrines; he then turns to the king and describes the moat, wall and four gate-towers, the king's bed-pavilion and his built pleasure-ground, whose pull already names the desire that will cost him his kingdom."},"cc:8":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":8,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 150-173 (6/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The account of the city ends with its ball grounds, mansions, palace and unceasing music; then the king Saccandan is praised as terrible to foes, refuge to the frightened, generous, learned and godlike, and at the height of his fame the poet turns to the princess of Vidaiyam and describes her beauty from hair to hips."},"cc:9":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":9,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 174-197 (7/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The narrator finishes the head-to-foot portrait of Vijayai, princess of Videham, which King Sachchandan paints in his heart and carries away; he sends his suit to her father, the elders and ministers give her to him, and the chapter dwells on the couple's mutual, all-absorbing pleasure, the delight that the next verses will name as the king's slipping from the guard of his land."},"cc:10":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":10,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 198-221 (8/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"King Saccandan, unable to leave Queen Vijayai, hands the guarding of his kingdom to his minister Kattiyankaran; a soothsayer and the counsellor Rudradattan warn him, the second with exempla of gods and heroes shamed by lust, but the king answers that past penance measures his joys and goes back to his pleasures, which the narrator ascribes both to a sprouting past destiny and to his own heart. That night Vijayai sees three frightening dreams and at dawn worships the god and then her husband."},"cc:11":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":11,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 222-245 (9/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Vijayai's dream of the falling asoka tree is read by Saccandan, who names the son, his wives and his rise but withholds the tree's fall; she conceives, Fortune quietly leaves the king, his wisdom bids him build a mechanical peacock, and Kattiyangaran, feigning a deity's command, asks the law-learned about killing the king while Dharmadattan, conceding the king's fault, counsels loyalty."},"cc:12":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":12,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 246-269 (10/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Dharmadattan answers Kattiyangaran's plan to kill the king with a long speech on the king's divinity, the hell awaiting regicides and the ruin of a bent sceptre; Madanan and Kattiyangaran reject it, the councillor yields, and the usurper imprisons the king's kin and besieges the palace, where Saccandan prepares to fight, sends Vijayai away, and consoles her with the doctrine that birth and death are the fruit of one's deeds."},"cc:13":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":13,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 270-293 (11/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Saccandan consoles Vijayai with the countless births and two-day kinship of the Jina's teaching and sends her off in the peacock machine; then, alone against Kattiyankaran's army, he slays elephants, is pierced by discus and spear, and dies, and the narrator mourns the just sceptre swallowed by the minister-snake while the king goes as guest to the gods and his women wail."},"cc:14":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":14,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 294-316 (12/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Saccandan's body is burned while Kattiyankaran weeps false tears and the king's women mourn and vow to follow him; the mechanical peacock carries Vijayai down into the burning ground, where she bears Civakan, laments the royal birth he should have had and the deeds she blames for it, takes refuge in the Jina's dharma, and is met by a deity in her maid's form who foretells that someone will come for the child and bids her hide."},"cc:15":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":15,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 317-339 (13/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Vijayai leaves her newborn son among lamps in the burning ground with the king's ring, blesses him from hiding, and watches the merchant Kandukkatan take him in exchange for his own dead son and pass him off to his wife as their revived child, to public rejoicing and gifts; the deity then tells the queen her son's future and leads her toward an ascetics' hermitage in the Dandaka forest."},"cc:16":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":16,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 340-362 (14/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Guided by the deity in the guise of a hunchbacked maid, Vijayai walks barefoot through the forest to a hermitage, where, after the narrator names a former-birth deed as the cause of her suffering, the women ascetics strip her of her ornaments one by one and she takes the ochre cloth and the widow's diet, keeping only a vow for her son; the deity entrusts her to them and leaves on a pretext, and the story turns to the city, where the merchant and his wife name the child Civakan and he grows on his foster mother's milk."},"cc:17":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":17,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 363-385 (15/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan grows up in Kantukkatan's house, is initiated into letters as a marriage to the maiden Knowledge and masters every art, so that all women long for him; his teacher then preaches the Jain path, the net of Death and the fence of vows, Civakan takes refuge in the Arhat and the three jewels, and the teacher leads him apart to reveal his royal birth."},"cc:18":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":18,"title":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம் 386-408 (16/16)","section":"நாமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The last verses of the Namakal Ilampakam: the teacher finishes the story of Civakan's birth, tells the youth it is his own, checks his rage against Kattiyankaran with a promise of a year's restraint, and then tells his own story, a Vidyadhara king turned ascetic whose penance was broken by the elephant-fire hunger and who was cured when the boy fed him; he asks leave to renounce again and departs to wear down his old deeds."},"cc:19":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":19,"title":"கோவிந்தையார் இலம்பகம் 409-429 (1/4)","section":"கோவிந்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The Kovindaiyar ilampakam opens with the release of Civakan's teacher Accanandi and a portrait of Civakan as the young man every maiden takes for Kama, then turns to the cattle raid: hunters seize the king's herd despite their own omen-reader's prophecy that one man in a chariot will break them, the herders ignore an ill omen of their own and are routed, and their outcry is carried to the usurper seated at ease on his throne."},"cc:20":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":20,"title":"கோவிந்தையார் இலம்பகம் 430-450 (2/4)","section":"கோவிந்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Hunters seize Kattiyangaran's herds; the king's great army is routed by them, the herd chief Nandagon offers his daughter to whoever recovers the herd, the city's men refuse, and Civakan vows to protect the herd and drives out alone against the hunters, who taunt him, lose heart before his chariot and, blaming their omen-reader, make a last charge."},"cc:21":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":21,"title":"கோவிந்தையார் இலம்பகம் 451-471 (3/4)","section":"கோவிந்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan routs the hunters who seized the cowherds' herd, turning it back by the sound of his bow with no man lost; the city hails him as an Arjuna, and its women throng the street in a long scene of longing, praising his mother's penance, praying for him in dreams and paling as his chariot passes, until he enters his home and the chariot is housed with offerings."},"cc:22":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":22,"title":"கோவிந்தையார் இலம்பகம் 472-492 (4/4)","section":"கோவிந்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The Govindaiyar ilampakam closes: Civakan is welcomed home after the herd's recovery, and the herd chief Nandagon, telling in confidence how the old king fell and how he himself turned back from suicide in hope that the line survived, offers his daughter Govindai with gold and the herd's wealth and argues from the gods that lineage is no bar; Civakan, holding that a man must first marry a woman of his own rank, accepts her for his friend Padumukan, and the herders' wedding is told in dairy rites and a bridegroom's unbounded pleasure."},"cc:23":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":23,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 493-516 (1/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The Gandharvadattai ilampakam opens with the merchant Sitattan, who, scorning those who blame former deeds and praising wealth as the only true thing, sails to a golden island, trades and enjoys himself for six months, and on the voyage home meets a storm in which he exhorts his crew with maxims on courage, the measured life-span and refuge in the Jina's feet; the ship sinks, all others drown, and he is cast alone on a strange shore."},"cc:24":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":24,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 517-540 (2/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The shipwrecked merchant Sridattan tells his loss to a stranger, Dharan, who promises by magic to restore his drowned companions and goods and flies him on a ram to the Vidyadhara city on the silver mountain, which the poet describes at length; there Dharan begins the story of King Kaluzhavegan's daughter, whose horoscope places her destined husband in Rajamapuram on earth."},"cc:25":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":25,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 541-564 (3/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"At the court of the Vidyadhara king Kaluzhavegan the merchant Sridattan is welcomed as kin of seven generations, shown the princess Gandharvadattai, and entrusted with her: she is to go to his city and belong to whoever defeats her at the vina. The king defends marriage as the gods' own joy, leaves the outcome to destiny, loads her with gifts and lutes, and consoles himself that daughters, like the conch's pearls, are for others; she leaves the grieving palace and boards the aerial car."},"cc:26":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":26,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 565-588 (4/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Gandharvadattai is carried through the sky with a vast army whose elephants out-roar the clouds; Dharan reveals to Sridattan that his shipwreck was a ruse made at Kaluzhavegan's bidding after an astrologer's word, shows him his ship and companions safe, and Sridattan brings the princess home to Rajamapuram, tells his wife, and gains the king's consent for her marriage rite."},"cc:27":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":27,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 589-612 (5/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"With the king's leave, the merchant Sridattan has ten thousand workers build a hall of gold and crystal for the vina contest, which the poet praises at length; drummers proclaim through the city a Jain benediction, a call to give to ascetics and do penance, and the terms by which Tattai will go to whoever of the three classes surpasses her at the vina, and the first suitor kings arrive."},"cc:28":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":28,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 613-636 (6/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Suitor kings and crowds of every rank gather in Rajamapuram for the vina contest for Gandharvadattai; the merchant prepares the way and gives wealth away at the hour an omen-reader fixes, her companion predicts the suitors' defeat, attendants bathe and adorn her, and she goes out with auspicious emblems, the victor's garland, dwarfs and armed women guards, through a crowd whose eyes will not be held back by the guards."},"cc:29":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":29,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 637-660 (7/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"At the vina contest the assembled kings gaze on Gandharvadattai and speak of her as Death and goddess, vowing war or penance if they lose; she enters, plays and sings three spring songs of a cuckoo's estranged mate won back, her friend the pedi Vinapati announces the terms and jests with the crowd, and when the suitors play in turn the birds that fell entranced at her song fly off at theirs."},"cc:30":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":30,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 661-684 (8/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Gandharvadattai defeats kings, Vedic Brahmins and learned merchants at the vina, and Kattiyankaran says only Kama could win her; Civakan resolves to enter, but a palace woman brings Kantukkatan a hidden letter warning that the king means to kill him, and Kantukkatan begins the story behind the king's rage: at a staged dance Civakan adorned the dancer Anangamaalai, the crowd was undone by her, and she sent him her love by her eyes."},"cc:31":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":31,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 685-708 (9/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The dancer Anangamalai, whose eyes went to Civakan, is seized and violated by Kattiyankaran, who learns from his own men of Civakan's strength, his rescue of the herd and his feeding of the routed army, and sees in him the man who will take his kingdom; warned, Civakan laughs at the danger, adorns himself and rides with armed friends to the hall of Gandharvadattai's vina contest, where the city's women take him for Kama."},"cc:32":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":32,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 709-732 (10/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"At the Vidyadhara princess's vina contest Gandharvadattai, seeing Civakan, loses her self-containment and vows to garland him; Civakan rejects her vinas one by one for faults in their wood and strings, plays and sings rain-season songs that overcome gods, beasts and kings, and the princess, judging the music his, begins her answering song of a woman's pallor in separation."},"cc:33":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":33,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 733-756 (11/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Gandharvadattai's song falters and she loses the vina contest, garlands Civakan and is screened from the hall; Kattiyankaran, burning with envy, taunts the defeated suitors into attacking Civakan for the bride, and Civakan's friend Padumukan sees through him, tells the kings that force after a lost contest disgraces them and that the chosen bride is beyond their reach, exposes the usurper's plan to let them destroy one another, and shoots an arrow through stone."},"cc:34":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":34,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 757-780 (12/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"As the suitor kings take up arms over Gandharvadattai, Civakan lays down the vina, comforts the weeping princess, sets Padumukan and armed women to guard her, trades taunts with the kings (they mock him as a merchant's son; he vows to see their backs or lose his name), and the battle opens, pictured as tuskers fighting over a cow-elephant, with the Vidyadharas watching from the clouds."},"cc:35":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":35,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 781-804 (13/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The battle after the vina contest: Civakan's companions, born on his birthday, fight the suitor kings with a warrior's courtesy (sparing a crippled chariot, resting a foe's horse) while Devadattan, Nabulan, Vipulan and Puttisenan cut through the host; then Nandattan speaks the mantra into the horses' ears, the chariot rises and wheels beyond sight, and Civakan's arrows strip and kill the kings, sparing the good-minded, while ghouls make a feast and a dance of the field."},"cc:36":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":36,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 805-827 (14/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The battle over Gandharvadattai ends: Padumukan's elephant and arrows and Civakan's conch rout the envious kings, who take him for Murugan and scatter, and the narrator declares that victory goes to the virtuous mind. Civakan brings the bride home, the wounded are treated and rewarded, he expiates the sin of the battlefield by a golden image of the Jina, a festival and gifts and worship at the Tirthan's feet, and the stores for the great wedding pile up, led by Nandagon's pots of milk, curd and ghee."},"cc:37":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":37,"title":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம் 828-850 (15/15)","section":"காந்தருவதத்தையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The last run of the Gandharvadattai ilampakam: the city throngs the wedding house with gifts, the feast and the fire rite are held, and Civakan, still called the merchant's son, marries the Vidyadhara princess and lives with her in pleasure. A letter from her father Kaluzhavegan recounts the vina contest and the war, offers his arms, and assures him that what destiny brings comes unsought; Civakan gives his bride her father's gifts as the poet turns to the city."},"cc:38":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":38,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 851-875 (1/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Spring comes to Rajamapuram, and the whole city, likened to the Jina's walled assembly, pours out with litters, elephants, liquor, perfumes and powders to the water-play in the riverside groves; the women screen off a place in the grove, and Suramanjari and Gunamalai sit together until one holds out her powder, the start of the rivalry that opens the section."},"cc:39":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":39,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 876-900 (2/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Suramanjari and Gunamalai quarrel over their scented powders and wager on them; their maids take the powders to Civakan, whose verdict for Gunamalai is challenged by Suramanjari's maid, until he proves it by letting the grove's bees choose; the defeated Suramanjari vows to wait until he comes humbled to her feet and withdraws into the maidens' mansion."},"cc:40":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":40,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 901-925 (3/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Suramanjari's mother tells her father of the girl's vow to see or hear of no man; the doting father buys a royal order with a priceless necklace so that men are barred and even statues made female, while Suramanjari vows to keep her nirai for Civakan alone. Gunamalai grieves that a game has cost her a friend and worships the Jina, the town gives to suppliants and the temple at the festival, and the water-play is shown scene by scene in a run of 'behold' verses as Civakan goes to watch."},"cc:41":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":41,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 926-949 (4/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"At the bathing festival the poet shows a run of pleasure sights (a mirror, dice, feasting, the ship of desire, swans and a doe fooled by jewels) and then the one Civakan saw: Brahmins beat a dog to death for touching their rice, a drunkard claims the dog and threatens them, Civakan drives him off, then weeps over the dying dog and whispers the five padas into its ear, and the dog is reborn at once as a god in a golden city."},"cc:42":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":42,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 950-973 (5/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The dog Civakan gave the five padas is reborn a god, Sudanjanan, lord of the yakshas; he comes back, is recognised by a god's signs, thanks Civakan as his father and promises to come whenever he is remembered, while the narrator defends the transformation by alchemy's iron turned to gold and declares worship only for the destroyer of the eight karmas and release as the harvest of the tilled body. The city then plays at war in the pool of the water festival, and as the crowd streams home the musth elephant Asanivegam breaks loose."},"cc:43":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":43,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 974-997 (6/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"A must elephant charges Gunamalai's palanquin; her bearers flee and her companion offers to die with her, and Civakan, judging death better than standing by, draws the beast on himself, escapes its trunk and drives it off, a deed the narrator measures by Krishna's. Taken home withered, Gunamalai turns from her kin like one who knows her births and, alone with her companions, confesses her love-sickness in images of Kama's archery, the sacrificial fire and the want of those who did no penance."},"cc:44":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":44,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 998-1021 (7/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Lovesick Gunamalai, praising a woman's restraint yet thinking of the palmyra horse, sends her parrot to Civakan, who has painted her portrait and is embracing it; his wife Gandharvadattai comes upon the picture, sees through his excuse, scorns his appeasing words and leaves in a sulk, and the parrot, which waited hidden, comes down to his hand."},"cc:45":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":45,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1022-1045 (8/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Gunamalai's parrot carries her love-sickness to Civakan, who pledges marriage and sends back a sealed letter sworn in a warrior's self-curse; she divines with a sand circle, receives the letter with joy, and vows offerings to the gods that he keep his word."},"cc:46":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":46,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1046-1069 (9/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Gunamalai's nurse sounds her with news of a match with a rich cousin; Gunamalai refuses, vowing death or penance unless she is given to the elephant-tamer, and the news passes from nurse to mother to father, who sends envoys to Kantukkatan; the match is agreed, the day fixed, the wedding held with a rich dowry, and Civakan and Gunamalai's union and his teasing close the run."},"cc:47":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":47,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1070-1093 (10/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Gunamalai's lovers' sulk is appeased and the two are joined in play, while the king's elephant, shamed since Civakan checked it, refuses food; the usurper Kattiyangaran, told the cause, sends his men to seize Civakan, who arms to fight but, recalling his teacher's word and at his foster parents' plea, lets himself be bound as the city wails and Sunandai falls in grief."},"cc:48":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":48,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1094-1117 (11/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan, seized at Kattiyangaran's order and bound at his parents' word, tells the grieving women of his house that one who knows births and release does not grieve; the city's women run from their games and crafts to lament, blaming karma, their own want of penance, the king and the father; Kantukkatan forbids the weeping, boasts the house's firmness, and as his son goes like a lion bound with a spider's thread, sends twelve crores of gold to cool the king's anger."},"cc:49":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":49,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1118-1141 (12/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Kantukkatan's plea for his son fails before Kattiyangaran, who swears to brand Civakan a thief; the father comforts Sunandai and Gunamalai with an ascetic's old prophecy that a famous son would come to them and that a yaksha would rescue him from the graceless king, while the friends learn that Civakan let his parents bind him and Buddhisenan plans to burn the city by night to free him."},"cc:50":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":50,"title":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1142-1165 (13/13)","section":"குணமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan's friends gather to free him from the usurper's prison by Padumukan's plan, and Gandharvadattai, able to free him by her spells, holds back because a husband saved by his wife would be shamed; Civakan too refuses her magic and calls on his friend, the god once a dog, who comes down in a storm and carries him off, while Madanan's men kill a stranger in his place and Kattiyangaran rewards the supposed murder."},"cc:51":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":51,"title":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம் 1166-1190 (1/10)","section":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The opening of Padumaiyar Ilampakam: freed from prison, Civakan meets both fortunes with equanimity as former deeds, is carried to the god Sudanjanan's mountain, bathed and renamed, hears the god explain that he is the dog Civakan once saved, declines the divine women's offer to win his kingdom for him, and asks to see the world; the god then maps the road ahead, past Jain ascetics washing their karma, a yakshi's welcome, elephant forests, life-drinking ghoul-women, the Pallava country and a burning desert where animals shelter their own."},"cc:52":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":52,"title":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம் 1191-1215 (2/10)","section":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The god Sudanjanan continues to set out for Civakan the road ahead, hermitages, rivers, forests, hunters and wondrous pools, giving the route as a map of landmarks and closing with a prophecy of the king of the middle country, a marriage and the friends' return."},"cc:53":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":53,"title":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம் 1216-1240 (3/10)","section":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Sudanjanan ends his account of the road, repays Civakan with three mantras, foretells in twelve months his reunion, kingship and final renunciation, and carries him down to earth; walking alone through a mourning forest, Civakan converts a hill hunter from meat, honey and toddy by setting hell against heaven, saves elephants from a forest fire, and reaches and circles a Jina temple on a mountain."},"cc:54":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":54,"title":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம் 1241-1265 (4/10)","section":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan ends his worship at the hill temple with a hymn to the Jina as the one who crossed birth, ended karma and gave the world its teaching, then travels to Chandirapam in the Pallava country, where a dancer falls in love with him on stage and vows herself to him, and the prince, following her gaze, takes him as a friend."},"cc:55":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":55,"title":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம் 1266-1290 (5/10)","section":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"News comes that Padumai, reaching for a flower in her grove, has been bitten by a snake; the prince's mantras, a crowd of rival healers, seven thousand Brahmins and an astrologer all fail, until an omen points to a young man, the prince sends for Civakan, and he diagnoses the bite by its cause, smell and kind and cures her with a spell, so that her first look on reviving falls on him."},"cc:56":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":56,"title":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம் 1291-1315 (6/10)","section":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Padumai, revived, looks at her healer and falls at once into love's sickness; the palace honours Civakan with bath, meal and betel, the king tests and praises his learning, and the chapter closes on the two lovers' separate sleepless laments on the same night."},"cc:57":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":57,"title":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம் 1316-1339 (7/10)","section":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Padumai's companions take the lovesick princess to the pleasure grove against the king's wish; Civakan, wounded by her look, follows, doubts whether she is a goddess, knows her by her human marks, and unites with her in a madhavi bower, praising her and pledging a love like the moon and its mark before hiding from her returning women; her father, knowing nothing of it, resolves to give her to the man who saved her life and bears the marks of a king."},"cc:58":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":58,"title":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம் 1340-1363 (8/10)","section":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"On the minister's counsel and the narrator's word that fate has ripened, the king gives Padumai, whom Civakan cured of snakebite, to Civakan; after the wedding rites and two months of love, which also bring him to Tesikappavai in the grove, he slips from his sleeping wife at night, and she wakes to search for him like a body for its life and to scold her parrot."},"cc:59":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":59,"title":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம் 1364-1387 (9/10)","section":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Waking to find Civakan gone, Padumai laments to her myna, swan, peacock, lamp and mansion, strips off her ornaments, blames her eyes for sleeping, calls on bees, hills, gods and goddesses, reproaches the absent man whose grace failed her, and falls fainting like a hen-swan left at a dry pool, until her friend comes with words of comfort."},"cc:60":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":60,"title":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம் 1388-1411 (10/10)","section":"பதுமையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The close of Padumaiyar Ilampakam: Padumai's friend consoles her for Civakan's night departure, first by belittling men's love, then by teaching the tree of suffering from ignorance to death and the impermanence of youth, wealth and union, and lastly by omens; her mother reads Civakan's veiled sayings as a promise to return, and Civakan, disguised, tells the king's searchers he will be back within ten months."},"cc:61":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":61,"title":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம் 1412-1436 (1/6)","section":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan leaves Padumai, crosses the forest and mountain singing a hymn to the Jina on the fruit of deeds, the soul and the bond of love, and at a hermitage of Vaishnava forest ascetics answers their discourse by mocking their diet, fires, bathing and matted hair with the beasts that already live so, then sets out knowledge, right vision and right conduct as what ends deeds."},"cc:62":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":62,"title":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம் 1437-1460 (2/6)","section":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The ascetics Civakan has argued with leave their households for the Jina, and he goes on to the Takka land and its city Kemamapuram, described as a goddess and a sea of wealth; there the merchant Subhadran's daughter Kemasari has waited twelve years under an astrologer's word that her husband is the man whose sight will shame her, while her father feeds a thousand and eight guests a day, and Civakan, meditating under a banyan outside the city, is met by the father, who begins to read his bodily marks."},"cc:63":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":63,"title":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம் 1461-1484 (3/6)","section":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The merchant Subhadran reads the auspicious marks on Civakan's body, decides he is his daughter's destined husband and brings him home; a hymn to the Jina is sung as they enter, Kemasari and Civakan fall in love at first sight, the house bathes and feasts him, and he lies lamenting the wounds of her beauty."},"cc:64":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":64,"title":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம் 1485-1508 (4/6)","section":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Kemasari's concealed love is announced by her father, the marriage is held, and two months of bliss likened to the state beyond birth and death follow; then, on a night framed by a sunset told as a life's ageing, Civakan slips away while she sleeps, and she wakes to search for him like a serpent that has lost its gem."},"cc:65":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":65,"title":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம் 1509-1532 (5/6)","section":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Kemasari, waking to find that Civakan has left her in the night, laments to him, to the creatures and places around her, to her own eyes, shoulders, breasts and reflection; understanding at last the veiled words he spoke to the bee, she holds back her life for fear of harm to him and takes the bees as household gods, while a companion asks the house deity whether she can survive, and the morning star rises."},"cc:66":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":66,"title":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம் 1533-1556 (6/6)","section":"கேமசரியார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Kemasari's mother Nipputhi consoles her with karma and impermanence but cannot reach her; Civakan walks through the night, and at sunrise meets a wayfarer whose riddle of four wives and one son opens into a Jain sermon on the householder's giving, conduct, fasting and worship, on lust, drink and meat, and on women's beauty, after which Civakan gives him his ornaments and crosses the wilderness."},"cc:67":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":67,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1557-1580 (1/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan crosses from the marutam country through the hills and pastures to the grove of Vanagiri, where a woman like a celestial tries her charms on him; reading her intent, he meditates to his heart on the death that ends a lover's pleasures and on the body as a hut of flesh built by the deed, and she, to move him, tells that a Vidyadhara carried her off and his wife cast her into the forest, giving her name as Anangamaa Veenai."},"cc:68":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":68,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1581-1604 (2/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"In the forest a married woman who has hidden from her husband begs Civakan to take her, and he answers by stripping the body to its humours, tissues and worms and blaming the kavya poets for painting it; her husband Pavadattan arrives lamenting her loss to a yakshi, Civakan consoles him with praise of learning and a two-sided teaching on women's frailty and the rare chaste wife, laughs at his faith in her, and with a mantra sends him to find her, after which Civakan climbs a holy hill thronged by pilgrims shedding their sins."},"cc:69":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":69,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1605-1628 (3/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Crossing the mountain of a Jina shrine, Civakan offers flowers and sings a three-stanza hymn that argues from the Lord's solitary supremacy, the worship even Mal pays him, and his conquest of Kama to the duty of praise; he descends through rich country to Emamapuram in the Middle Country, learns its name from a bridegroom, and resting by a pond, watches a hen swan sulk at her own reflection and be won back by her mate, which turns him to memory of Gandharvadattai and fear that she may die of their parting."},"cc:70":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":70,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1629-1652 (4/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan, by a pool outside Hemamapuram, longs for Gandharvadattai, reasons that she lives, then checks his desire with the thought that lust leads to hell where only the Jina is refuge; Prince Vijayan meets him, receives a false name, sees him shoot down a mango, and brings him to King Tadamittan, who reads his archer's marks and makes him teacher of the princes; the section ends in a spring grove where he strings a garland."},"cc:71":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":71,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1653-1676 (5/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan makes a garland that the maid carries to Kanakamalai; the princess, wasting with love, laments to the evening, the cuckoo and her own life, says she sold her modesty for a garland, and sends her own garland back, which Civakan after a scruple accepts as the work of former deed; the maid's cooling remedies fail and only his garland comforts her, while the princes finish their training in arms."},"cc:72":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":72,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1677-1700 (6/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan finishes training the king's sons in arms; the delighted king, after a mock threat to fetter him, gives him his daughter Kanakamalai as a bride already fated to an archer, and the two are wed and enjoy each other; the narrator then turns to Rajamapuram, where Civakan's brother, searching for him, comes to Gandharvadattai as she sings three stanzas of a deserted woman's lament."},"cc:73":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":73,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1701-1724 (7/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Gandharvadattai's spell shows Civakan to his grieving brother and carries Nandattan to Emamapuram, where Vijayan and Vasundari find a stranger in Civakan's likeness and the brothers meet."},"cc:74":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":74,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1725-1748 (8/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Nandattan reaches Civakan in Kanakamalai's city by Gandharvadattai's spell; after the reunion and Kanakamalai's welcome he tells how the companions debated revenge, took an overheard song as a sign that Civakan lived, and scattered, and how he found Gunamalai wasting and Gandharvadattai sent him on; Civakan begins his own story."},"cc:75":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":75,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1749-1772 (9/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan finishes telling his brother Nandattan how he escaped and wandered, through the god's rescue, the rain on the burning forest, and his marriages to Padumai and Kemasari and the princess of the middle country, and says a brother is the one thing that cannot be gained; at home his four companions suspect Gandharvadattai's calm, learn from her that he is safe, worship her, take her sealed letter and set out with a host of horse."},"cc:76":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":76,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1773-1796 (10/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan's companions ride out with a caravan, after reading the omens, through hill and field country to a hermitage, where they find a weeping woman ascetic worshipping the Jina; she is Vijayai, his mother, though no one names her, and when she will not tell her clan Devadattan answers with their city, their parents and their upbringing with the prince, and begins to tell his deeds."},"cc:77":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":77,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1797-1819 (11/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan's companions, searching for him, tell an ascetic mother in the hermitage that he was seized for death; her lament reveals that she is Vijayai, the dead king's queen, and Civakan his heir, and she charges them in secret to bring him to her, while they vow to crush the usurper."},"cc:78":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":78,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1820-1842 (12/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan's friends reach the outskirts of Emamapuram and plan to raid the king's herd to draw him into battle, until Buddhisenan warns of the blame of retreat and the cost of blood; their three spies then report how Civakan tamed the king's must elephant Valaya Sundaram, rode it before the king, and was acclaimed as the one master of elephants."},"cc:79":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":79,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1843-1865 (13/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"After the elephant's taming the city hails Civakan; his companions, guided by disguised spies, raid the king's herds dressed as hill raiders to draw him out, interrupting Nandattan just as he is about to tell the king their lineage; Civakan vows to bring the raiders captive, but Padumukan's arrow greets him as a crowned king's only son, and the friends bow at his feet and ride with him into the city."},"cc:80":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":80,"title":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம் 1866-1888 (14/14)","section":"கனகமாலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The companions arrive at Kanakamalai's city, are received by the king and feasted by the princess; Civakan reads Gandharvadattai's sulking letter, with its gifts, its cover-story to the Vidyadharas and its report of Gunamalai's wasting; he hears that his mother lives as an ascetic, weeps, the companions declare his Kuru birth to the king, and he takes leave of Kanakamalai and rides out."},"cc:81":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":81,"title":"விமலையார் இலம்பகம் 1889-1910 (1/5)","section":"விமலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Leaving Kanakamalai's city, Civakan hears his brother's counsel against trust and poison, crosses rich mountains to the hermitage, and is reunited with his mother Vijayai, now an ascetic."},"cc:82":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":82,"title":"விமலையார் இலம்பகம் 1911-1931 (2/5)","section":"விமலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan finds his mother Vijayai in the hermitage: she weeps and nurses him in her embrace, both take the family's ruin on themselves, and she yields her motherhood to Sunandai and Kandukkatan and claims Nandattan as her own; after six days she teaches him kingcraft (marriage alliances, just wealth, spies, divide and rule, wealth that buys army and land), measures their exile against it, and when Civakan and his friends answer with lion-like confidence in force, she answers with the crow that killed the owls and the lion caught in a net, and sends him to his maternal uncle."},"cc:83":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":83,"title":"விமலையார் இலம்பகம் 1932-1952 (3/5)","section":"விமலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan answers his mother, travels with his company through forest to a river country, is feasted, sung to and loved at a grove halt, worships the Jina at dawn, and enters the city, where the women cannot tell him from Murugan or Kama and a maiden is playing ball."},"cc:84":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":84,"title":"விமலையார் இலம்பகம் 1953-1973 (4/5)","section":"விமலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"A merchant's daughter plays with five balls; one falls in the street before Civakan, she sees him, turns pale and laments that her modesty and restraint are stolen and that desire has no cure, while he, struck by her glance, sits lovesick on her father's step and laments her eyes as Death, and at that moment her father's long-unsold goods sell."},"cc:85":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":85,"title":"விமலையார் இலம்பகம் 1974-1994 (5/5)","section":"விமலையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The close of the Vimalaiyar ilampakam: the merchant Sagaradattan tells Civakan that a prophecy made at his daughter's birth, that her husband would make the unsold cargo sell, is fulfilled, gives him Vimalai, and the chapter follows their union through one night to the dawn when Civakan, promising a quick return, leaves her in tears."},"cc:86":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":86,"title":"சுரமஞ்சரியார் இலம்பகம் 1995-2016 (1/5)","section":"சுரமஞ்சரியார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Returning from Vimalai, Civakan is teased by his companions that his brides came by their parents' giving, and dared by a Brahmin companion to win Suramanjari, who shuns men; he swears to do it, disguises himself as a decrepit Brahmin, begs his way past the gatekeepers, and is surrounded by her armed women, some of whom hold back for fear of the sin of harming a Brahmin."},"cc:87":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":87,"title":"சுரமஞ்சரியார் இலம்பகம் 2017-2038 (2/5)","section":"சுரமஞ்சரியார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan, disguised as a destitute old brahmin, is received by Suramanjari, who had vowed to look on no man: she laughs at his wish to bathe at Kumari and be young again, feeds, dresses and lodges him in her mansion, and at nightfall, while the city's lovers come together, his singing bewilders her women and reminds them of the youth she keeps in her mind."},"cc:88":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":88,"title":"சுரமஞ்சரியார் இலம்பகம் 2039-2059 (3/5)","section":"சுரமஞ்சரியார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Disguised as a very old Brahmin, Civakan draws Suramanjari and her maidens with song, learns from them that her hatred of men is anger at his own verdict, and sings an evening-song of a woman's longing that undoes her vow; next morning she prays at Kama's temple to be joined to Civakan, and his hidden companion answers in the god's voice."},"cc:89":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":89,"title":"சுரமஞ்சரியார் இலம்பகம் 2060-2080 (4/5)","section":"சுரமஞ்சரியார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan and Suramanjari unite in secret in Kama's shrine; he swears to wed her next day, she discloses the union through friend, foster mother and mother to her father Kuberadattan, who gives her to Civakan before the fire with a great dowry."},"cc:90":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":90,"title":"சுரமஞ்சரியார் இலம்பகம் 2081-2101 (5/5)","section":"சுரமஞ்சரியார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The close of Suramanjari's ilampakam: the first nights of Civakan and Suramanjari, her jealous sulk over the painted figure ended when he lays his head on her anklets as her old vow required, his leave-taking, and his secret homecoming to his foster parents and wives, Gunamalai blaming her own deeds and he crediting her penance, before he leaves the city again as a merchant."},"cc:91":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":91,"title":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம் 2102-2126 (1/9)","section":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan and his company cross the five landscapes in turn, the desert in a single day, reach Videham and send word to its king, who orders a welcome; the city throngs to see him, hails him with the names of Kama and Murugan, and its women plead with his elephants to walk slowly."},"cc:92":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":92,"title":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம் 2127-2151 (2/9)","section":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan enters his maternal uncle Govindan's city and palace, is received with tears, and exchanges first looks with the uncle's daughter; then Govindan in council hears Kattiyankaran's lying letter, which blames Saccandan's death on an elephant, and resolves to answer guile with guile, proclaiming a false alliance while mustering his army."},"cc:93":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":93,"title":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம் 2152-2176 (3/9)","section":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The Videha king musters and reviews a vast army of elephants, horses, chariots and foot, marches under good omens into the Emangatha country and camps near the usurper's city, while ill omens fill Kattiyangaran's palace; the usurper, unaware, exchanges gifts with the king who means to kill him by feigned friendship."},"cc:94":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":94,"title":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம் 2177-2201 (4/9)","section":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"At Govindan's boar contest, staged as a trap for Kattiyangaran, a catalogue of crowned suitors fails one by one, thrown by the machine or shamed by bow and arrow, and after six days Civakan rises, mounts his elephant, terrifies Kattiyangaran, who laments that his victim escaped, and waits a whole day before the machine while the failed kings mock him."},"cc:95":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":95,"title":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம் 2202-2226 (5/9)","section":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan shoots the boar target on its revolving wheel at Videham, King Govindan proclaims his lineage, a voice from the sky foretells that he will kill Kattiyangaran, Madanan musters the usurper's army, and the poet sets out the two hosts and the opening of the battle, naming its cost in widows, dead beasts and a grieving Earth."},"cc:96":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":96,"title":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம் 2227-2251 (6/9)","section":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The great battle of the Earth-lady ilampakam: the clash of horses, chariots and elephants, the arrows that darken the sky, the Earth veiling her eyes in a blanket of blood, the slain bridegrooms feasted on by ghouls and jackals, and the duels in which Vijayan's fight ends Madanan, Buddhisenan seizes kings by the hair, Devadattan routs the Magadha king and Sridattan kills the Kalinga king."},"cc:97":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":97,"title":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம் 2252-2276 (7/9)","section":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The battle of the Manmagal ilampakam at its height: Govindan, Ulokapalan, Nabulan, Vipulan and Padumukan kill or rout the allied kings, Nabulan and Vipulan refuse to strike the frightened, the weaponless, the wounded, the young and the old, and Vipulan's single duel ends with the slain challenger received into heaven."},"cc:98":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":98,"title":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம் 2277-2301 (8/9)","section":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"In the battle for the earth (மண்மகள் இலம்பகம்), champions of both armies fight: men hailing Govindan, Nandan and Civakan stand alone, pluck spears from their bodies and laugh, and die so their lord may take the land; Civakan then mounts his chariot and his arrows send a river of blood over the kings' regalia, and Arichandan tries to stop the rout by shaming the fleeing."},"cc:99":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":99,"title":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம் 2302-2326 (9/9)","section":"மண்மகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The end of the war of மண்மகள் இலம்பகம்: Ariccandan's rally fails, Civakan kills the usurper's sons and then Kattiyangaran, who meets his death speaking of karma and fate and vowing on his honour, and the Earth goddess passes to Civakan."},"cc:100":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":100,"title":"பூமகள் இலம்பகம் 2327-2343 (1/3)","section":"பூமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan enters his father's city in triumph: the women crowd the mansions to praise and worship him and pale with longing as he passes, while his warriors drive the dead usurper's women out of the palace barefoot onto the hot ground, and the question of which of them was queen meets the answer that the king and his sons lie dead."},"cc:101":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":101,"title":"பூமகள் இலம்பகம் 2344-2360 (2/3)","section":"பூமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"After the usurper's fall his queen dies of the news, and Civakan spares his women with a choice of maintenance or gold; he takes the palace, has the wounded tended, sleeps through a night of grief and triumph, worships the Jina at dawn, and holds court among his kin, friends and allied kings."},"cc:102":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":102,"title":"பூமகள் இலம்பகம் 2361-2377 (3/3)","section":"பூமகள் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The Vidyadhara kings and the gods gather for Civakan's crowning; he is bathed, crowned and seated on the lion throne, and his first orders as king, given through his brother and the royal drum, restore the temples' and Brahmins' lands, remit dues, and promise help to the hungry, the sick and the abandoned before the narrator turns to the next ilampakam."},"cc:103":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":103,"title":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம் 2378-2402 (1/9)","section":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The women are brought back across the wasteland and rejoined with the hero in tears and joy; the poet turns to the wedding of Ilakkanai, where an astrologer names the days, a hundred and eight elephants are readied, a herald-drum orders feasting and decking, and the city keeps the days in music, flowers and gifts."},"cc:104":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":104,"title":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம் 2403-2427 (2/9)","section":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"At the wedding of the Ilakkanaiyar ilampakam the city is heaped with goods and decked with flags and pots, the astrologer fixes the hour, the king is bathed by elephant-borne water and anointed with ghee amid blessings, then dressed, perfumed and fanned by countless fly-whisks."},"cc:105":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":105,"title":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம் 2428-2452 (3/9)","section":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"At Lakkanai's wedding the king's daughter is blessed, bathed and seated in a pearl hall, and a skilled dresser does her hair, brow, eyes, ears, wrists, breasts, girdle, feet and hands, until her beauty disarms Kama and bewilders onlookers, and the household hides her with fans and a parasol."},"cc:106":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":106,"title":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம் 2453-2477 (4/9)","section":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"At the wedding of Civakan and his uncle Govindan's daughter Ilakkanai, the attendants praise the bride and the rival kings burn with envy; the priests kindle the fire and Civakan takes the gift-water and circles the fire; the women prepare the bridal bed, and the night is told in the figure of a rain-cloud filling a tank."},"cc:107":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":107,"title":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம் 2478-2502 (5/9)","section":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The wedding of Civakan and Ilakkanai: the bride is fanned and arrayed, her ornaments jest at the groom, the pavilion and emblems are set out, Civakan and the bride sit together, the groomer is introduced and shaves Civakan, and the pair bathe and dress."},"cc:108":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":108,"title":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம் 2503-2526 (6/9)","section":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"After the lovers' quarrel in which Ilakkanai mistakes Civakan's tears for thoughts of other women, the parrot's mock death ends it and the wedding days pass; Civakan then rides the elephant Culamani in state through the city."},"cc:109":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":109,"title":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம் 2527-2550 (7/9)","section":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The city's women crowd the streets, jewelled, bewildered and shamed, to watch Civakan pass in procession, and in the last verses throw powder and flowers, praise him as Murugan's brother and Kama, and beg him with their eyes."},"cc:110":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":110,"title":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம் 2551-2574 (8/9)","section":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The women and people of the city praise Civakan and argue for tapas; he worships the Jina in his temple with hymns and an endowment, then sits among kings and pays out his debts of land, rule and wealth to brother, friends, nurses, kin and uncle, and builds a golden memorial for the friend who saved him."},"cc:111":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":111,"title":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம் 2575-2598 (9/9)","section":"இலக்கணையார் இலம்பகம்","theme":"The eight wives are crowned and the land flourishes under Civakan's single umbrella; then the dancer Anangamalai, whom the usurper once seized, comes to the king with a letter that recalls her devotion, and he receives her, enjoys her dance and takes her to bed."},"cc:112":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":112,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2599-2623 (1/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"The last ilampakam opens with a Jina temple and festival; Vijayai repays those who helped her in her wretchedness, receives the eight wives, and then retells the old king's fall through pleasure and warns her son, in a run of exhortations, that life is short and giving and penance must come first."},"cc:113":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":113,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2624-2648 (2/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"The women take the Jain vow: three sights of fallen wealth, cloth and youth turn Sunandai and Civakan to renunciation, the matrons go in palanquins to Pammai's nunnery, are washed, bound, stripped, shorn and filled with virtue while the king bows at their feet, and he and Nandattan protest and are answered."},"cc:114":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":114,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2649-2673 (3/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"The hero worships the renounced women and a queen of the court gives up pleasure for austerity; the ministers, afraid he will follow, fill a pool for a water-battle in which the women are beaten by the king, and the chapter closes in the pleasures of the couch, the last enjoyment before his own turning away."},"cc:115":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":115,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2674-2698 (4/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"Through the cold and dewy seasons the women of Civakan's household drink, dress and revel; Gunamalai's sulk at the moon in her cup is ended by his flattery, and as spring comes the groves become a stage and the women adorn themselves for the spring sport."},"cc:116":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":116,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2699-2723 (5/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan's last pleasures: women playing in paddy and wine, the birth, naming and schooling of eight sons, the spring outing, and a male monkey's secret tryst, his mate's rebuke and their making up."},"cc:117":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":117,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2724-2748 (6/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan, moved by a monkey quarrel over fruit, sees his own history in it and turns from his wives and pleasures; after one last day of bath, meal and adornment he goes with them to the Jina's temple, hears hymns of praise, asks a Charana sage for grace, and the sage prepares to teach him dharma."},"cc:118":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":118,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2749-2773 (7/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"In the last ilampakam a teacher tells the king how rare it is to win a human birth, a good land, a good family, a sound body and the faultless way, and how death may cut it off at any stage; then he describes the torments of hell that the deeds of the careless ripen into."},"cc:119":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":119,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2774-2798 (8/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"In the last ilampakam the teacher continues to show the king the sufferings of hell, of animals and of men, naming the sins that lead to them, so that the king turns from pleasure and kingship toward renunciation."},"cc:120":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":120,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2799-2823 (9/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan hears the Jain teacher's sermon: all embodied life is suffering, even the gods' pleasures end in the fading garland and the grief of their fall, and release from birth is what even they beg for; he is told of the Lord's marks, karma that spares no rank, and the discipline of sila, ending with the fruit of giving."},"cc:121":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":121,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2824-2848 (10/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"In the last ilampakam the sage Madhavan tells the king what gifts to the restrained and to the unrestrained yield in later births, describes the land of enjoyment and its lovers, and then sets out release as the burning of karma by knowledge, faith and conduct, so that the king bathes in the sermon."},"cc:122":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":122,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2849-2873 (11/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"In the city of Bhumitilaka the old king has the hearer bow to the lord's feet, then the poem shows the prince Asodharan with women at a lotus pool and his father, the king of kings, who calls him and teaches him not to kill, lie, steal, go to another's wife, or crave wealth got by deceit."},"cc:123":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":123,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2874-2898 (12/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"The elder's sermon to the young prince Asodharan, with caged birds, karma that follows like a thread and the dread of rebirth, ends in the prince's freeing of a cygnet, his father's order to rule first, his argument that kin are strangers and his leaving with his wives; the chapter then returns to the king hearing the great ascetic, the Charanas' departure and the grief of the women at his announced renunciation."},"cc:124":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":124,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2899-2923 (13/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan offers the kingdom to Nandan, who refuses even heaven and release for his brother's feet; the king then instructs his eldest son on rule, crowns his heirs and gives away treasure, and goes to his women, who wither when they read that he means to leave."},"cc:125":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":125,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2924-2948 (14/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"In the last ilampakam Civakan preaches to his eight wives on giving, the foulness and fading of the body, and the fear of Death, and, when he tells them they are now strangers, they weep, fall at his feet and ask what fault they committed."},"cc:126":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":126,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2949-2973 (15/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"At the start of Civakan's departure for the ascetic's life, his wives weep and reproach his shoulders, chest, tongue, eyes and crown and recall the loves that brought him to them, and the palace and city fall into a silence of grief."},"cc:127":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":127,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2974-2998 (16/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"The last farewell: the palace falls silent as Civakan prepares to renounce, he teaches the weeping wives that wealth ends at the burning ground and that even the fruit of good deeds binds, they take off their ornaments and choose penance, and after giving away all he has he worships his mother and is carried off by the gods."},"cc:128":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":128,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 2999-3023 (17/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"In the last ilampakam Civakan comes to the assembly of the Jina, with its festival, tree and gods' whisks, bows and circles, hears that a king's renunciation is the excellent course, and the hymns to the Jina's feet place the freedom from deeds in worship and the Jina's word."},"cc:129":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":129,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 3024-3048 (18/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan, having praised the Jina, strips off his ornaments and plucks his hair in the grove, the gods carry the hair to the milk sea, the god Sutanjanan praises him, his followers take the ascetic's vows, and the narrative turns to the Magadha king Senikan, who prepares to feast the Jina's feet."},"cc:130":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":130,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 3049-3073 (19/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"At the Lord's assembly the king and the gods bow, a radiant god beside the Lord is named Civakan, and Sudharman tells how Civakan on the sandalwood hill cut the eight karmas and stood through summer heat, rain and cold to the end of his austerity."},"cc:131":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":131,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 3074-3097 (20/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"Civakan's last war: the hero, as an ascetic, destroys the passions and the four destroying karmas with the arrows of meditation, attains omniscience, and the gods, kings and nuns come and sing hymns to the Lord."},"cc:132":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":132,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 3098-3121 (21/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"The wives praise the Lord and hear the king's sermon on desire, harm and release, the Lord is released and cremated by the gods, and Civakan, embracing the maiden Kevala, and the women, who see their bodies as dirt, take up austerity and reach heaven."},"cc:133":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":133,"title":"முத்தி இலம்பகம் 3122-3145 (22/22)","section":"முத்தி இலம்பகம்","theme":"In the close of the last ilampakam the poem sets the court's lovemaking beside the ascetics' penance, which ends in death on a mountain and rebirth as gods in heavenly pleasure, then ends with the poet's praise of the three-umbrella Lord's feet and of the work's hearers."},"kd:1":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":1,"title":"kundalakesi","section":"குண்டலகேசி","theme":"The nineteen surviving fragments, in the editor's order, open with an invocation to a lord defined by labour for others and an apology that excuses the poet's slips by the worth of his subject; most of the rest argue a renouncer's case: bodily austerities are mere words, desire is not quenched by enjoyment, death is a daily march to execution, the body is carrion that cannot be called 'mine', a fault stays a fault even in a god, and the wise meet pardon and death alike as the fruit of deeds. Two verses praise an unnamed king."},"mm:1":{"book":"manimekalai","num":1,"title":"பதிகம்","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"The patikam tells how Puhar got its two names, from the goddess Champa and from the Kaveri released by Agastya, then runs through the whole story from the festival and the prince's pursuit to the bowl, the prison made a house of charity, the hearing of the schools and Manimekalai's vow in Kanchi to end rebirth; its colophon has Saathan recite the thirty songs of her renunciation to a listening Ilango."},"mm:2":{"book":"manimekalai","num":2,"title":"விழாவறை காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"The opening kaathai tells how Puhar's festival of Indra was founded at a sage's word by the hanging-fort Chola, why it must not be forgotten (the guardian spirit's anger), and gives the hereditary drummer's proclamation: the gods will come down, the city is to be adorned, every god worshipped by its own rite, the schools are to debate without enmity, and hunger, disease and enmity are to leave. The warning about a forgotten festival is the seed of Puhar's later loss to the sea."},"mm:3":{"book":"manimekalai","num":3,"title":"ஊரலர் உரைத்த காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"On the day of Puhar's festival, Chithirapathi, grieved that Madhavi and Manimekalai stay away, sends Vayantamalai to tell Madhavi that the town calls an accomplished dancer's turn to tapas shameful. Madhavi answers that she lost her shame when she outlived the news of her lover's suffering, that Kannaki fits none of the known kinds of pathini, that Manimekalai as Kannaki's daughter will go to tapas and never to the courtesan's trade, and that the Buddhist teacher Aravanar has taught her that birth is suffering born of attachment and given her the five precepts."},"mm:4":{"book":"manimekalai","num":4,"title":"மலர்வனம் புக்க காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Manimekalai's tears for her parents spoil Madhavi's garland, and she must fetch fresh flowers; Sudhamathi, herself once carried off from a garden by a sky-being, rules out every garden but the Buddha's Uvavanam, whose crystal pavilion and lotus seat she describes, the seat built to prove that a deed without intention bears no fruit. Walking through the festival street past a drunk pleading with a naked ascetic, a raving man in ash, a eunuch dancer and the start of Murugan's procession, the girl is surrounded by a crowd that blames her mother for her tapas, and enters the grove."},"mm:5":{"book":"manimekalai","num":5,"title":"பளிக்கறை புக்ககாதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"In the grove Manimekalai's companion Sudhamathi shows her its beauties; Udayakumaran, fresh from taming his maddened elephant, hears from a musician that she is there and comes for her; Sudhamathi locks her in the crystal pavilion and answers the prince's questions with a sermon on the body born of deeds, which the sight of the girl through the crystal outruns."},"mm:6":{"book":"manimekalai","num":6,"title":"மணிமேகலா தெய்வம் வந்து தோன்றிய காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Through the crystal the prince sees Manimekalai, brushes off Sudhamathi's warning of the girl's fated austerity, and hears Sudhamathi's story of how Jain monks turned away her gored Brahmin father and a Buddhist monk carried him to safety; he leaves trusting Chithirapathi to deliver the girl, Manimekalai confesses her heart has followed him and curses desire, and the goddess Manimekala arrives to praise the Buddha's seat as evening falls on Puhar."},"mm:7":{"book":"manimekalai","num":7,"title":"சக்கரவாளக் கோட்டம் உரைத்த காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Manimekala, disguised as a townswoman, sends Sudhamathi and Manimekalai into the Chakravala Kottam beside Puhar's burning ground and explains its name: she describes the cemetery that teaches death spares no one, and tells how the Brahmin boy Sarngalan died of fright there, how the goddess Champapathi told his mother Gotami that his own deed and not a demon took him, refused her life in exchange, and had every god of the cosmos confirm that none can give back a life; on that spot Maya modelled the whole world-system. Then the goddess carries the sleeping girl to Manipallavam."},"mm:8":{"book":"manimekalai","num":8,"title":"துயிலெழுப்பிய காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"In the Thuyilezhuppiya Kaathai the goddess Manimekala, having hidden the girl on Manipallavam, warns the prince that a swerving sceptre ruins planets, rain and life, and wakes Sudhamathi with the news that a ripened cause has set Manimekalai on the Buddha's path; through a sleeping city Sudhamathi goes to the hall of the Chakravala kottam, where the painted pillar image reveals the two women's former births, and at dawn she tells Madhavi, who sits stricken at her daughter's absence."},"mm:9":{"book":"manimekalai","num":9,"title":"மணிபல்லவத்துத் துயருற்ற காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Manimekalai wakes alone on the island of Manipallavam, knowing neither the place nor whether she dreams, calls for Sudhamathi and suspects the goddess who brought her of deceit, then, wandering the shore, laments her father, whose death by the sword in a foreign land she lays to a cruel deed come to ripeness. Before her appears the Dharma seat placed by the king of the gods, over which two Naga kings once warred, each calling it 'ours', until the great sage stopped the war by calling it 'ours' and preached dharma from it."},"mm:10":{"book":"manimekalai","num":10,"title":"பீடிகை கண்டு பிறப்புணர்ந்த காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"At the jewel seat on Manipallavam Manimekalai weeps, falls and rises knowing her former birth, and tells it aloud to the absent sage Brahmadharma: his warning that King Attipathi's city Idavayam would sink, the king's evacuation and the city's fall on the foretold day, her birth as Lakshmi and marriage to Attipathi's son Rahulan, and the sage's prophecy that Rahulan would die of a glance-poison snake, that she would enter his pyre, be reborn in Puhar, be carried by Manimekala to an island and there know her birth at the seat. She ends asking for the deity who was to show her husband's new birth."},"mm:11":{"book":"manimekalai","num":11,"title":"மந்திரம் கொடுத்த காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"At the Buddha's seat on Manipallavam the Manimekala goddess descends, praises the Buddha, and tells Manimekalai her former birth: as Lakshmi she fed a sky-roaming sage, a gift that will end her births; the prince who pursues her is her husband of that birth, Rahulan, and her sisters then are Madhavi and Sudhamathi now, reborn beside her for worshipping the Buddha's footprints; the goddess gives her a mantra to change form and fly, for hearing the false words of other schools, and one against hunger, since the body is a lump of food."},"mm:12":{"book":"manimekalai","num":12,"title":"பாத்திரம் பெற்ற காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"On Manipallavam the guardian goddess Deepathilakai meets Manimekalai, who answers 'who are you?' with her two births; the bowl Amudhasurabhi rises into her hand on the Buddha's day, Deepathilakai preaches that those who feed the hungry give life, Manimekalai names the bowl as the fruit of a meal she gave a holy man in her former life, and she flies home to name Madhavi and Sudhamathi as her sisters of that life."},"mm:13":{"book":"manimekalai","num":13,"title":"அறவணர்த் தொழுத கதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Manimekalai finds Aravana Adigal and tells him all that has happened; he tells how Tarai and Virai, Madhavi and Sudhamathi in their former birth, died, laments the decline of the Buddha's dharma and foretells the next Buddha's coming, defers his own teaching until events have run their course, and gives her the dharma of ending hunger, at which she takes up the bowl."},"mm:14":{"book":"manimekalai","num":14,"title":"ஆபுத்திரன் திறம் அறிவித்த காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Aravana Adigal tells Manimekalai the early life of Aputtiran, first holder of her bowl: abandoned by his brahmin mother, nursed by a cow and adopted by a brahmin, he steals a cow from a sacrifice, answers the brahmins' contempt for his birth with their own seers' animal and courtesan births, and is driven out to live in Madurai as a beggar who feeds the helpless."},"mm:15":{"book":"manimekalai","num":15,"title":"பாத்திர மரபு கூறிய காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Aravana Adigal goes on telling Manimekalai the bowl's history: the goddess Chintadevi gives Aputhiran the never-failing bowl in the Madurai rest hall; his feeding shakes Indra's seat, and when Indra comes offering a reward he laughs at a heaven where no one gives; Indra answers with rain so plentiful that no one needs the bowl, the hall falls to idlers, and Aputhiran, sailing to famine-struck Savakam, is left on empty Manipallavam, where he gives the bowl to the Gomukhi pool for some compassionate hand, starves himself to death, and is born again in Savakam to sustain its people."},"mm:16":{"book":"manimekalai","num":16,"title":"பாத்திரம் கொண்டு பிச்சை புக்க காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Aravanar finishes Aputhiran's story, his rebirth from the cow's womb in Savakam and his rise to kingship, and charges Manimekalai not to lay aside the hunger-ending bowl; she enters Puhar's street in a nun's garb, and on her word that a chaste wife's alms are worthiest, the cursed and hungry Kayasandigai sends her to Athirai's house."},"mm:17":{"book":"manimekalai","num":17,"title":"ஆதிரை பிச்சையிட்ட காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Kayasandigai tells Manimekalai why Aathirai's house should give the first alms: Aathirai's husband Saathuvan, ruined by a courtesan and dice, was wrecked at sea and reported dead; Aathirai entered a funeral fire that would not burn her, and a heavenly voice promised his return; on the island of the naked cannibal Nagas Saathuvan, knowing their language, refused their woman, liquor and meat, argued for rebirth from the corpse and the dream, gave their chief a dharma he could keep, and came home with treasure. Aathirai then fills the bowl Amudasurabi, praying that hunger leave the whole earth."},"mm:18":{"book":"manimekalai","num":18,"title":"உலக அறவி புக்க காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Manimekalai feeds the crowd from the bowl a chaste wife first filled and cures Kayasandigai's insatiable 'elephant-fire' hunger; Kayasandigai tells how, as her evil deed ripened, she crushed the twelve-yearly fruit of the fasting sage Viruchikan and was cursed with hunger and the loss of her sky mantra, then sends Manimekalai to the public hall (உலக அறவி) where the hungry and the friendless sick wait, and Manimekalai, holding up Aputhiran's bowl, calls all who would receive."},"mm:19":{"book":"manimekalai","num":19,"title":"உதயகுமரன் அம்பலம் புக்க காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Chithirapathi, the courtesan grandmother, vows to drag Manimekalai back to the dancers' life and urges Prince Udayakumaran to seize her as royal custom allows; the prince, ignoring the goddess's warning, confronts Manimekalai in the public hall, where she bows to him as her husband of a former birth but answers that the body is a vessel of suffering, then hides from him in Kaayasandigai's form in the old goddess's shrine, where he vows to stay until she is given to him."},"mm:20":{"book":"manimekalai","num":20,"title":"சிறைக்கோட்டம் அறக்கோட்டம் ஆக்கிய காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Rebuked by a deity and pursued by his own desire, the prince withdraws; Manimekalai, to escape him, takes Kayachandigai's form and feeds the king's prisoners from the bowl, and while the king plays in his grove the guard reports her, and at her request he turns the prison into a house of dharma."},"mm:21":{"book":"manimekalai","num":21,"title":"உதயகுமரனைக் காஞ்சனன் வாளால் எறிந்த காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Kanchanan, come to Puhar for his wife Kayasandikai at the end of her curse, takes Manimekalai in her form for his wife and, seeing her teach Udayakumaran the body's decay by pointing to an old woman, judges the prince her lover; when the prince comes to the hall at midnight Kanchanan kills him, and the pillar goddess tells him that his wife died over the Vindhya, that the prince died by his own former deed, and that the killing will still cling to Kanchanan."},"mm:22":{"book":"manimekalai","num":22,"title":"கந்திற்பாவை வருவது உரைத்த காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Waking in Champapati's shrine after the prince's killing, Manimekalai casts off her borrowed form and mourns him; the deity of the pillar image, Thuvathikan, forbids her grief, traces the prince's violent deaths to a cook he killed in anger in a former birth, and foretells her imprisonment and release, her voyage, the schools of Vanji, the famine of Kanchi, her teaching by Aravanar, and her release births later as the Buddha's chief disciple."},"mm:23":{"book":"manimekalai","num":23,"title":"சிறை செய் காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"The ascetics bring the Chola king news of his son's death indirectly, through old stories of Kakandi in which men who desired chaste women were punished by the spirit's warning or by Kakandhan's own sword, and through a tale of lifelong chastity and charity; asked for the present case, they tell how the prince's ripened deed killed him, and the king calls the killing his own duty done by another, orders the body burned in secret and Manimekalai put under guard."},"mm:24":{"book":"manimekalai","num":24,"title":"சிறை விடு காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"The Chola queen, grieving her son, has Manimekalai released into her keeping and tries to destroy her by drug, slander and starvation; each trick fails by the knowledge and mantras Manimekalai has gained, and when the queen repents Manimekalai teaches her that grief for a body is idle, that the prince died by his own past deed, and that lust, killing, drink, falsehood and theft must be renounced, until the queen's grief is quenched and the two bow to each other."},"mm:25":{"book":"manimekalai","num":25,"title":"ஆபுத்திரன் நாடு அடைந்த காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"After the prince's death Chithirapathi begs the queen to release Manimekalai, warning through the old story of Killi and the Naga princess that the city's fate hangs on Indra's festival and on the goddess whose name the girl bears; the queen refuses the courtesan house, Aravanar comes and, answering the queen's wish for his long life, teaches that ageing and death are nature and sets out the twelve links and the ten evil deeds; Manimekalai takes leave and flies to Nagapuram, where Aputhiran is reborn as a king under whom rain never fails."},"mm:26":{"book":"manimekalai","num":26,"title":"ஆபுத்திரனோடு மணிபல்லவம் அடைந்த காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Manimekalai wakes the king of Savakam, Aputhiran reborn, to his former birth: he hears his birth story, resolves to renounce, is argued back to his duty by his minister, sails to Manipallavam where the dharma seat shows him his past, and is shown by the island's goddess the bones of his former body and of those who died following his fast; the goddess tells Manimekalai how Puhar was cursed into the sea, and Manimekalai sends the king home to rule, telling him a king's dharma is food, clothing and shelter for all lives."},"mm:27":{"book":"manimekalai","num":27,"title":"வஞ்சி மாநகர் புக்க காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Manimekalai flies to the shrine of her parents' image, and Kannaki, speaking as the pathini goddess, retells Mathurapathi's word that Kovalan's death was the fruit of a killing in a former birth, confesses that she burned Madurai in anger after hearing that truth and must suffer its fruit once her heavenly merit is spent, foretells her and Kovalan's release on hearing the Buddha, and sends her daughter to hear the schools and find them false; Manimekalai takes an ascetic's form and enters Senguttuvan's Vanchi."},"mm:28":{"book":"manimekalai","num":28,"title":"சமயக் கணக்கர் தம் திறம் கேட்ட காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"In an ascetic's borrowed form Manimekalai hears the schools of Vanji one by one: the Vedic logician's means of knowledge and their fallacies, the Saiva, Brahma, Vaishnava and Vedic accounts of God and scripture, the Ajivika's atoms and fixed fate, the Jain categories and release by exhausting deeds, Sankhya's nature and person, the Vaisheshika categories, and the materialist's denial of any next world, which alone she answers, turning his trust in perception against his knowledge of his own parents. Every school is given in a Buddhist work's account and set aside on the way to the Buddhist teaching of kaathai 29."},"mm:29":{"book":"manimekalai","num":29,"title":"கச்சி மாநகர் புக்க காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Leaving the schools of Vanji, Manimekalai finds Kovalan's father as a Buddhist ascetic there, hears how the Chera kings and her own ancestor took up the Buddha's teaching, that Kovalan's heaven still owes its evil deed and his release waits for the Buddha at Kapilai, and that drought-stricken Kanchi is her charge; she flies there, where the king, unable to tell whether his sceptre, austerity or women's chastity has failed, has built a copy of Manipallavam on a deity's word, and her bowl feeds every creature and brings back the rain before she casts off her disguise at Aravanar's feet."},"mm:30":{"book":"manimekalai","num":30,"title":"தவத்திறம் பூண்டு தருமம் கேட்ட காதை (1/2)","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"In Kanchi Aravanar first tells Manimekalai why she bears the goddess's name and why Puhar fell to the sea, and then, when she asks for the true doctrine after rejecting the five schools, begins the Buddhist account of knowledge: two means only, perception as bare awareness and inference, with the forms of a valid proof and a catalogue of false theses and false reasons, many of whose examples are the doctrines of the schools she heard in kaathai 27."},"mm:31":{"book":"manimekalai","num":31,"title":"தவத்திறம் பூண்டு தருமம் கேட்ட காதை (2/2)","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Aravanar completes his account of faulty inference for Manimekalai: the inconclusive reasons, the four contradictory reasons (two of them turned against Sankhya and Vaisheshika proofs), and the ten faulty examples, ending with the charge to examine and know true from false without doubt. The teacher's examples mostly defend the rival thesis that sound is eternal, and the whole is the method he gives before the doctrine of kaathai 30."},"mm:32":{"book":"manimekalai","num":32,"title":"பவத்திறம் அறுகெனப் பாவை நோற்ற காதை","section":"மணிமேகலை","theme":"Manimekalai takes refuge in the three jewels and her teacher expounds dependent arising: the twelve links forward and in reverse, with their four sections, three junctions, three times, the four truths, the five aggregates, the modes of speech, the four nayas and the four ways of answering, ending in the claim that bondage and release have their own causes and no giver, and in a remedy for each of desire, anger and delusion; hearing it, she vows to end the round of becoming. The kaathai is the Buddhist doctrine the debates of kaathai 27 were set aside for."},"si:1":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":1,"title":"பதிகம்","section":"சிலப்பதிகாரம்","theme":"The patikam frames the epic: hill folk report a one-breasted woman taken to heaven, Saathan tells Ilango the story of Kovalan's death and Kannaki's burning of Madurai and its cause in a curse from a former birth, and Ilango sets down the poem's three morals, the death of an erring king by dharma, the praise of the pathini and the inevitability of the past deed, before the thirty kaathais are listed."},"si:2":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":2,"title":"மங்கல வாழ்த்துப் பாடல்","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"The benediction that opens the epic: three-line venbas hail the moon, the sun, the rain and Puhar, each for being like the Chola's umbrella, wheel, grace and line; the narrative then calls Puhar a city the learned never speak of as declining, introduces Kannaki at twelve and Kovalan at sixteen as children of its two great merchant houses, and tells their wedding, with the Vedic circling of the fire, the women's blessing that she never be parted from her lover, and a closing praise of the Chola's wheel."},"si:3":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":3,"title":"மனையறம்படுத்த காதை","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"The kaathai of setting up the household: in Puhar's inexhaustible wealth the newly married Kannaki and Kovalan, likened to those who enjoy in Uttarakuru the fruit of completed austerity, climb to the terrace of their mansion on a spring evening; Kovalan pours out an unmeasured praise that has the gods surrender their weapons to her body and asks why her maids adorned a beauty that needs nothing; then an elder lady sets Kannaki up in a household of her own for charity and the hosting of guests, some years pass, and a closing venba says the two enjoyed every pleasure inseparably, as if they had seen that nothing on earth lasts."},"si:4":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":4,"title":"அரங்கேற்று காதை","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"Madhavi's debut: after seven years of training she is presented to the Chola king with a troupe each of whose masters is described by his art, on a stage built to the treatise's measures; the talaikkol, a war trophy worshipped as Indra's son, is carried round the town, she dances, wins the title and 1008 kazhanju, and her garland, priced at that sum and offered in the street, is bought by Kovalan, who goes to live with her and forgets his home."},"si:5":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":5,"title":"அந்திமாலைச் சிறப்புசெய் காதை","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"The kaathai of the evening: in one long sentence the poet brings dusk on Puhar like a usurping king, lets the Pandya crescent take the sky, and goes through the city's nights, Madhavi giving Kovalan love and its quarrels, the wives asleep on their husbands' chests, Kannaki stripped of every ornament but the marriage one, the parted women sleepless and weeping, until at dawn the god of desire, who patrolled all night, is declared the city's best watchman; a venba sets the moon as a king's umbrella, shade to Madhavi and heat to Kannaki."},"si:6":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":6,"title":"இந்திர விழவு ஊர் எடுத்த காதை","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"Indra's festival at Puhar: a survey of the city's two quarters and the market between them, the warrior women's possessed blessing and the warriors' head-offerings at the guardian spirit's altar, the halls brought from the north by Thirumavalavan, the five marvellous courts that guard against theft, disease, hidden wrong and a bent sceptre, the bathing of Indra and the rites of all the gods; then Kovalan roaming with Madhavi, the festival's women seen as moon, lotus and Death in disguise, and the omen of Kannaki's and Madhavi's throbbing eyes."},"si:7":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":7,"title":"கடலாடு காதை","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"The sea-bathing chapter: a vidyadhara and his beloved fly from the silver mountain to Puhar's Indra festival, where he explains its guardian bhuta and halls as Indra's gifts to a Chola, traces Madhavi's line to a celestial dancer cursed to earth and names the eleven dances of the gods she performs; after the festival Madhavi adorns herself, gives the sulking Kovalan union and sulk, and at the full moon they ride at dawn through Puhar's streets and lamp-lit market to the crowded shore, where she sits with him in a bower and takes up the lute."},"si:8":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":8,"title":"கானல் வரி (1/3)","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"On the shore after the sea festival, Madhavi tunes the yazh and hands it to Kovalan, who sings the Kaveri as the Chola's wife whose chastity is not to sulk at his other loves, then a run of neithal songs in the voices of the shore girls and of the smitten man, calling the fisher girl moon, goddess and Death in disguise; the songs, chosen by him for pleasure, carry the hint of another love that Madhavi will answer and that ends their union."},"si:9":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":9,"title":"கானல் வரி (2/3)","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"Kovalan ends his songs of the fisher girl whose eyes and brows kill, and Madhavi, reading a hidden meaning in them, takes the yazh in a sulk and answers: she sings the Kaveri as a wife whose grace proves her husband's straight sceptre, mocks a lover who comes to the shore on a trader's pretext, and voices a deserted shore woman who will not forget the one who forgot her and whose last trace of him the tide erases."},"si:10":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":10,"title":"கானல் வரி (3/3)","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"Madhavi's answering shore songs end and the union breaks: she sings, in the voice of the akam heroine, of the tide that wiped out the lover's chariot tracks, the mother who may find out, three evenings asked whether they reach the absent man's land, a man who spoiled their sand-play, and, after changing mode, evening as killer and besieger, closing with a plea to the sea god to pardon a false oath; Kovalan hears the songs as her love for another, and the narrator, giving it to his ripening former deed, has him leave her on the shore."},"si:11":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":11,"title":"வேனில் காதை","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"The kaathai of spring: spring arrives over the Tamil land as the herald and army of Kama, Madhavi, left by the sulking Kovalan, plays the yaazh and writes him a letter on a flower leaf, and Kovalan refuses it, recalling every sign of her love as a dancing woman's role, so that the quarrel at the shore becomes the break that sends him back to Kannaki."},"si:12":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":12,"title":"கனாத்திறம் உரைத்த காதை","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"The kaathai of the dream: as Puhar's women light the evening lamps, the poet tells how the god Pasanda Saathan once took a dead child from a desperate mother, gave her a child that was himself, grew up as a Brahmin youth, married Devanthi and left her for his temple; Devanthi, worshipping there, comes to bless Kannaki, hears her ominous dream of a great city, a false charge, a plea before the king and harm to the city, blames a vow broken in a former birth and urges bathing in the shore tanks, which Kannaki declines as unworthy; then Kovalan comes home ashamed of his lost wealth, takes her anklets as capital and leaves with her for Madurai before dawn, driven, the narrator says, by the deed at its term."},"si:13":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":13,"title":"நாடுகாண் காதை","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"The kaathai of seeing the country: driven by their past deed, Kovalan and Kannaki slip out of Puhar before dawn past its temples and viharas, take the Jain nun Kavunthi as guide, and walk slowly through the Chola country of the Kaveri; a Charana tells Kavunthi that a strong deed returns like a sown seed and cannot be put off, she vows every sense to the Arhat, and at Uraiyur's edge she curses two mockers into jackals and, at the wronged couple's plea, sets them a term of release."},"si:14":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":14,"title":"புகார்க் காண்டம் கட்டுரை","section":"புகார்க் காண்டம்","theme":"The closing summary (கட்டுரை) of the Puhar book: a single long sentence lists what the first ten kaathais set out about the Chola line and its land (righteousness, valour, the old city, its festivals, the divine Kaveri, the unfailing sky) and about its arts of stage, dance, vari songs and the lute, all seen as one; a venba then wishes Puhar, built by fame for the sea-moated earth, to live like the morning sun and the evening moon."},"si:15":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":15,"title":"காடுகாண் காதை","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"On the road from Uraiyur to Madurai the travellers meet a Vaishnava brahmin who praises the Pandya line and maps three roads, with a cave of wish-granting pools on one; the Jain ascetic Kavunthi answers that truth and care for life reach the same ends, and in the forest a goddess in the form of Madhavi's maid tries to delude Kovalan until a mantra unmasks her, before the three reach the hunters' shrine of Aiyai."},"si:16":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":16,"title":"வேட்டுவ வரி (1/2)","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"The first half of the hunters' song: at Aiyai's shrine in the dry country, where Kovalan and Kannaki rest, the possessed Saalini demands the goddess's due because the hunters' raids have failed; they dress a maiden of their clan as Kotravai and worship her; Saalini, still possessed, hails Kannaki as the jewel of the world, which Kannaki takes for confusion; and the hunters sing the goddess as Shiva's half, Mal's sister, the slayer of the buffalo-demon and the light above the Vedas, and their own clan as the first of clans through the girl who wears her form."},"si:17":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":17,"title":"வேட்டுவ வரி (2/2)","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"The second half of the hunters' vari: songs that see the goddess dance her victory and walk before the cattle raider, three songs that show the seized herds filling the yards of smith, bards, toddy seller, augur and elders, and stanzas offering her blood and bali as the hunters' due while praising her as Siva's consort and Mal's kin; it closes by wishing the vetchi on the Pandya of Pothiyil."},"si:18":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":18,"title":"புறஞ்சேரியிறுத்த காதை","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"Guided by the ascetic, Kovalan and Kannaki travel the Pandya land by night trusting its fame for safety; in a Brahmin village Kovalan meets Kausikan with Madhavi's letter, clears her and owns the fault as his own, sends the letter on to his parents, and with bards on the road comes by breeze, dawn sounds and the Vaigai to Madurai, where river, flowers and flags grieve and warn as if they knew what is coming, and the couple enter the outer settlement of the virtuous."},"si:19":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":19,"title":"ஊர்காண் காதை","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"Kovalan, at dawn, leaves Kannaki in Kavunthi's keeping, and the nun answers his self-reproach with a teaching that the deed ripens on its doer and that suffering comes to those bound to women, citing two exiled kings who lost their wives, and assures him that he, unlike them, keeps his wife; he then walks through Madurai's courtesans' quarter through all its seasons, its dancers' streets, bazaar, gem, gold, cloth and grain streets, and goes back outside the walls pleased with the city that will kill him."},"si:20":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":20,"title":"அடைக்கலக் காதை","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"On the eve of entering Madurai, the Brahmin Maadalan recounts Kovalan's good deeds (naming his daughter Manimekalai after the goddess who saved an ancestor for his gifts, saving a Brahmin from an elephant, paying for a Brahmin wife's expiation, offering his life to the spirit for a false witness) and asks whether his suffering is the fruit of a former birth; Kovalan tells a dream of ruin and release, and Kavunthi entrusts Kannaki to the herdswoman Maadari, praising her as a deity and binding Maadari to the charge with a Jain tale of a monkey raised to the gods by a housewife's gift."},"si:21":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":21,"title":"கொலைக்களக் காதை","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"The killing: lodged with the herdswoman Maadari, Kannaki cooks Kovalan's last meal, he confesses his heedlessness and she her silent endurance, and he goes to sell one anklet; the goldsmith who stole the queen's anklet accuses him, the king orders the thief killed without inquiry, the goldsmith talks down the guards' doubts with a lore of thieves' arts, and a drunken guard cuts Kovalan down; the narrator and the closing venba give the king's bent sceptre and Kovalan's former deed as causes of the one death."},"si:22":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":22,"title":"ஆய்ச்சியர் குரவை (1/2)","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"The first half of the herdswomen's kuravai: on the morning after Kovalan's killing, Madari finds the milk, butter and cattle showing signs of a coming sorrow, and to avert it sets seven herd girls, each pledged by the bull-catch formula to a bull's tamer and named for one of the seven notes, to dance before Kannaki the kuravai of Mayavan, his elder brother and Pinnai; the first song asks that the herders' god come among the cows today."},"si:23":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":23,"title":"ஆய்ச்சியர் குரவை (2/2)","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"The second half of the Aaychiyar Kuravai: the herdswomen sing Maayavan's herd-village play with Pinnai, set their ring by the gods they enact, praise the three crowned kings in the ulvari as bearers of Vishnu's deeds, address Maal with the paradox of the cosmic god bound and thieving, define ear, eye and tongue by his praise, and close by asking the god to end the cattle's distress and the Pandya's drum to win daily, on the very day that king's verdict has killed Kovalan."},"si:24":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":24,"title":"துன்ப மாலை","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"Madari returns from her river worship with news heard in the town; Kannaki, not finding her husband, presses for what people say, learns that he is called the thief of the palace anklet and that the guards have killed him, laments that the king's straying sceptre has made her a widow, and calls on the sun as witness, which answers that he is no thief and that fire will eat the city."},"si:25":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":25,"title":"ஊர்சூழ் வரி","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"After the Sun's answer, Kannaki walks the streets of Madurai holding her one anklet and calls the city's chaste women to witness that her husband was no thief; the townspeople read her grief as the bending of the Pandya's sceptre and the coming of a new goddess, show her Kovalan's body at dusk, and hear her lament couple the king's fault with a deed that must be eaten; Kovalan rises in a vision among the immortals, wipes her tears and leaves, and she, refusing to rejoin him until her anger is spent, goes to the king's gate."},"si:26":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":26,"title":"வழக்குரை காதை","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"Kannaki pleads her husband's case before the Pandya: the queen's dream of the falling sceptre is told, Kannaki indicts the king as one who did not inquire and sets the Chola precedents of justice against him, the broken anklet proves her right, and the king, naming himself the thief, dies by his own sentence; three closing venbas restate the moral that dharma is death to the wrongdoer and credit the king's death to the sight and words of the wronged wife."},"si:27":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":27,"title":"வஞ்சின மாலை","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"Kannaki, standing over the fallen king and queen, names herself the heir of the chaste women of Pukar, swears that if she is a true pathini she will not let the kingdom or Madurai survive, tears off her left breast and flings it at the city, and the fire god, who says he long held an order to burn it on the day she was wronged, burns Madurai sparing only those she names; the closing venba says chastity ate the city and its gods withdrew."},"si:28":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":28,"title":"அழற்படு காதை","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"With the Pandya dead on his throne, the fire Kannaki commanded breaks out: the four guardian bhutas of Madurai, each in the form of one order of society, leave the city because its king erred, the fire spares the virtuous, the cows, the children and the elders and burns the rest, the city's housewives judge her revenge no cruelty, all rites cease, and the city goddess, unable to bear the heat, comes before the grieving pathini."},"si:29":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":29,"title":"கட்டுரை காதை","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"In the Katturai Kaathai the goddess of Madurai, the Pandya's family deity, pleads the justice of his line with legends of Pandya self-punishment and repair, then gives Kannaki the cause from a former birth, Kovalan's killing of Sangaman and the widow Neeli's curse, and the pathini goes up from Murugan's hill to heaven with her husband."},"si:30":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":30,"title":"மதுரைக் காண்டம் கட்டுரை","section":"மதுரைக் காண்டம்","theme":"The closing summary (கட்டுரை) of the Madurai book: one long sentence lists what the book set out about the Pandya line and its land (righteousness, valour, the old city, festivals, the gods' visits, the Vaigai's plenty, the unfailing sky, the two dramatic modes and the vari and kuravai songs) and says the book lies viewed as one with the Pandya Nedunjeliyan, victor over the northern Aryan armies, who slept on his royal couch with his queen of flawless chastity."},"si:31":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":31,"title":"குன்றக் குரவை","section":"வஞ்சிக் காண்டம்","theme":"The hill women of the Chera country meet Kannaki under a vengai, hear her name her fate, see the gods take her to heaven with her husband and make her their clan's goddess; their kuravai then weaves the cult into an akam love story, mocking the velan's rite, praying to Murugan for the lover's wedding, and crediting the new pathini of their millet field when the lover comes, before blessing the Chera king."},"si:32":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":32,"title":"காட்சிக் காதை","section":"வஞ்சிக் காண்டம்","theme":"On a journey to the hills, Senguttuvan hears from the hill people of the one-breasted woman taken to heaven and from Saathan of the burning of Madurai; he reads the Pandya's death as a bent sceptre straightened, his queen chooses Kannaki for worship, and he resolves to bring the goddess's stone from the Himalaya, which his ministers turn into a summons to the northern kings."},"si:33":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":33,"title":"கால்கோட் காதை","section":"வஞ்சிக் காண்டம்","theme":"The Chera king vows to bring a stone for a god from the Himalaya on the heads of the northern kings who scorned Tamil strength, marches north with Siva's feet on his crown, is charged by sky-faring sages to protect the northern Brahmins, refuses his allies' offer of a stone, crosses the Ganga, defeats the Aryan kings and captures Kanakan and Vijayan, while the demons hail his battlefield as a field of dharma; he then takes the stone for the pathini."},"si:34":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":34,"title":"நீர்ப்படைக் காதை","section":"வஞ்சிக் காண்டம்","theme":"At the Ganga the Chera Senguttuvan has the pathini's stone bathed, rewards his warriors, and hears from the Brahmin Maadalan the aftermath of Madurai: Madari's fire, Kavunthi's fast, the renunciations and deaths of the parents, Madhavi's taking of robes, the new Pandya's offering of a thousand goldsmiths and the soundness of the Chola sceptre; then he rewards the Brahmin, sends the captives away and returns to Vanchi as the songs of hill, field, pasture and shore announce him."},"si:35":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":35,"title":"நடுகற் காதை","section":"வஞ்சிக் காண்டம்","theme":"Back in Vanchi after the northern campaign, the Chera hears through Neelan the Chola's and Pandya's taunts that captives who fled in ascetics' guise are no victory; the brahmin Maadalan calms his anger and preaches that body, wealth and youth pass and the soul goes by its deeds, and urges a Vedic sacrifice in place of the sacrifice of war; the king performs it, frees the prisoners, and installs the Himalayan stone as the pathini's jewelled image with daily worship, while the narrator sums up the three lessons the pathini gave the three kings."},"si:36":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":36,"title":"வாழ்த்துக் காதை (1/2)","section":"வஞ்சிக் காண்டம்","theme":"The first half of the Vaazhthu Kaathai: a prose summary recalls Senguttuvan's northern campaign for the Himalaya stone and its consecration, then Kannaki's friends from Puhar arrive at her shrine and lament before the image, telling of the deaths of the mothers, the renunciations of both fathers, of Madhavi and Manimekalai, and of Maadhari's death for a broken charge; Kannaki appears in the sky, declares the Pandya faultless and herself his daughter, and the women of Vanchi sing her and bless both the Pandya and the Chera lines."},"si:37":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":37,"title":"வாழ்த்துக் காதை (2/2)","section":"வஞ்சிக் காண்டம்","theme":"The second half of the Vaazhthu Kaathai is a cycle of women's play songs that divides the praise among the three crowns: the ammanai songs sing the Chola's ancestral deeds and Puhar, the ball songs hail the Pandya whom Kannaki has just declared blameless, the swing songs sing the Chera line's deeds, the pounding songs give each city its women and its king, and the close has Kannaki herself praise Senguttuvan; no karma is named in this half."},"si:38":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":38,"title":"வரந்தரு காதை","section":"வஞ்சிக் காண்டம்","theme":"The last kaathai (வரந்தரு காதை) closes the story by showing deeds and loves of the past bearing fruit in the present: Devanthi tells the Chera of Manimekalai's renunciation, is possessed by the god Saathan, and through water from a spring that restores former births three girls in Vanchi are shown to be Kannaki's mother, Kovalan's mother and Maadari reborn near the pathini; Maadalan explains it as the old law of births and deeds, the king endows the pathini's temple, foreign kings beg her presence and a voice grants the boon, the goddess tells the poet's own renunciation, and the poet ends with a string of commands to his hearers."},"si:39":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":39,"title":"வஞ்சிக் காண்டம் கட்டுரை","section":"வஞ்சிக் காண்டம்","theme":"The closing summary (கட்டுரை) of the Vanchi book: one sentence lists what the book set out about the Chera line, rulers of the west (its righteousness, valour and strength, its old city, festivals, the gods' visits, the wealth and grain of its land, its vari and kuravai songs), and says the book lies viewed as one with Senguttuvan, whose army of true swords fought in keeping with aram, drove back the sea and reached the bank of the Ganga."},"si:40":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":40,"title":"நூற் கட்டுரை","section":"வஞ்சிக் காண்டம்","theme":"The closing summary of the whole work (நூற் கட்டுரை): one sentence says that within the Tamil land bounded by Kumari, Venkatam and the two seas, the Silappathikaram gathers the matter of akam and puram, the three aims of virtue, wealth and pleasure for humans and gods alike, song, the lute, melody, rhythm, the stage and dance, the vari, the kuravai and the other forms, and makes its meaning plain as a good mirror shows a tall hill, its story's matter being completed in Manimekalai."},"vp:1":{"book":"valayapathi","num":1,"title":"valayapathi 0-23 (1/3)","section":"வளையாபதி","theme":"The first run of Valayapathi fragments, quoted mostly in the Purattirattu: an invocation to the knower that asks release from old deeds, verses on the rarity of human birth, a cluster of harsh sayings on women's inconstancy set against praise of the chaste wife, and a sermon in plural commands against lying, theft, killing, flesh-eating and other men's wives, closing on penance, release without return, and the blindness of desire."},"vp:2":{"book":"valayapathi","num":2,"title":"valayapathi 24-47 (2/3)","section":"வளையாபதி","theme":"Fragments preserved in the Purattirattu on the madness of love, the dangers and fleetingness of wealth, killing and truth, the late turn to renunciation and the futility of trying to escape karma while still attached; their order is the editor's and no story can be rebuilt from them."},"vp:3":{"book":"valayapathi","num":3,"title":"valayapathi 48-71 (3/3)","section":"வளையாபதி","theme":"The last run of the Valayapathi fragments: a verse against a blind guide to release, then the sequence (49-60) in which an old courtesan gives her daughter ten models, from the river to the chaste wife, and each is glossed as a way of following wealth; gnomic verses on poverty, household virtue and the conditions without which goods are void; and fragments from commentaries, Jain sages and a proud lute master, and two refrains to the heart on the burning of the adorned body and the striving that leads to attainment."}},"verses":{"cc:1":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1,"t":["மூவா முதலா உலகம் ஒரு மூன்றும் ஏத்தத்","தாவாத இன்பம் தலை ஆயது தன்னின் எய்தி","ஓவாது நின்ற குணத்து ஒள் நிதிச் செல்வன் என்ப","தேவாதி தேவன் அவன் சேவடி சேர்தும் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet goes for refuge to the red feet of the god of gods, whom the three worlds praise as the ageless First, and who is said to have reached by himself the supreme bliss that does not fail and to stand rich in qualities that never cease.","n":"The god is defined by what he attained, not by what he made: தன்னின் எய்தி (reached it by himself) makes the bliss self-won, and the qualities (குணம்) are his wealth (நிதிச் செல்வன்). That this is the Jain Arhat is not said in the verse; the school tag rests on these marks and on the 1008 marks of verse 2. மூவா முதலா can be heard as 'the ageless First' or as two negatives, 'unaging, beginningless'; the claim does not turn on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","inbam","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1","lb":"CC1","ev":"","cl":[["the god of gods reached the highest unfailing bliss by his own effort and abides in unceasing qualities","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:2":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2,"t":["செம்பொன் வரை மேல் பசும் பொன் எழுத்து இட்டதே போல்","அம் பொன் பிதிர்வின் மறு ஆயிரத்து எட்டு அணிந்து","வெம்பும் சுடரின் சுடரும் திருமூர்த்தி விண்ணோர்","அம் பொன் முடி மேல் அடித்தாமரை சென்னி வைப்பாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet places on his head the lotus feet, set on the golden crowns of the gods, of the holy form that blazes brighter than flame and bears the thousand and eight auspicious marks like green-gold letters written on a mountain of red gold.","n":"The 1008 (ஆயிரத்து எட்டு) are marks on the body (மறு), not names; the simile makes the body a golden mountain and the marks an inscription on it. The gods' crowns carry the feet before the poet's head does, so the poet's gesture repeats theirs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2","lb":"CC2","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:3":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3,"t":["பல் மாண் குணங்கட்கு இடனாய்ப் பகை நண்பொடு இல்லான்","தொல் மாண்பு அமைந்த புனை நல்லறம் துன்னி நின்ற","சொல் மாண்பு அமைந்த குழுவின் சரண் சென்று தொக்க","நல் மாண்பு பெற்றேன் இது நாட்டுதல் மாண்பு பெற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he has gained a good excellence by going for refuge to the one who is the home of many qualities and has neither enmity nor friendship, to the ancient adorned dharma he stood in, and to the assembly of excellent word, and so has gained the excellence of setting down this work.","n":"The verse goes to refuge in three: the teacher, his dharma, and the assembly (குழு). The threefold refuge is shared by Jain and Buddhist practice, and nothing in the verse decides between them; it is left without a school claim. பகை நண்பொடு இல்லான் makes the god's mark an absence of partiality, not a power to save. The refuge is given as the ground of the poet's right to compose (நாட்டுதல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adaikkalam","aram","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3","lb":"CC3","ev":"The poet goes for refuge to the teacher, his dharma and the assembly before he begins.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:4":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":4,"t":["கற்பால் உமிழ்ந்த மணியும் கழுவாது விட்டால்","நற்பால் அழியும் நகை வெண்மதி போல் நிறைந்த","சொற்பால் உமிழ்ந்த மறுவும் மதியால் கழூஉவிப்","பொற்பா இழைத்துக் கொளல்பாலர் புலமை மிக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"As even a gem thrown up by rock loses its fine quality if left unwashed, so the learned should wash with their wit the flaw thrown up in speech that is full like the bright white moon, and take the work set as an ornament.","n":"மதி is used twice: வெண்மதி (the moon) and மதியால் (with the intellect), so the moon that carries a spot and the mind that cleans it share one word. The poet does not deny the flaw; he hands its removal to the reader, as a jeweller's duty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","sol","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 4","lb":"CC4","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:5":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":5,"t":["முந்நீர்ப் பிறந்த பவழத்தொடு சங்கும் முத்தும்","அந் நீர் உவர்க்கும் எனின் யார் அவை நீக்குகிற்பார்","இந் நீர என் சொல் பழுது ஆயினும் கொள்ப அன்றே","பொய்ந் நீர அல்லாப் பொருளால் விண் புகுதும் என்பார்."],"p":"","r":"If coral, conch and pearl born in the sea taste of its salt, who would cast them away for that; so those who hold that one enters heaven through matter that is not false will accept the poet's words though they are faulty.","n":"The apology moves from verse 4's ornament to substance: the worth of the work lies in its பொருள் (matter), as the worth of a pearl lies in itself and not in the brine it came from. The heaven claim is the readers' maxim, which the poet turns into a reason to accept him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","kutram","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 5","lb":"CC5","ev":"","cl":[["one reaches heaven by a teaching whose substance is true, so its faults of diction do not lessen it","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:6":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":6,"t":["மீன் ஏறு உயர்த்த கொடி வேந்தனை வென்ற பொற்பில்","ஆனேறு அனையான் உளன் சீவகசாமி என்பான்","வான் ஏற நீண்ட புகழான் சரிதம் தன்னைத்","தேன் ஊற நின்று தெருண்டார் அவை செப்பல் உற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet announces his subject: Civakasami, a bull among men in the glory of having defeated the king who raised the fish banner, whose fame reached the sky, and whose story he has set out to tell to the assembly of the discerning as they stand with honey welling up.","n":"மீன் ஏறு உயர்த்த கொடி வேந்தன, the king of the raised fish banner, is the Pandya's emblem, but the fish banner is also the god of love's; if the second is meant, the hero's glory is the conquest of desire that his final renunciation completes. The verse does not settle which, and the reading here keeps both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","vendhan","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 6","lb":"CC6","ev":"Civakan defeats the king of the fish banner.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:7":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":7,"t":["கோடாத செங்கோல் குளிர் வெண்குடைக் கோதை வெள்வேல்","ஓடாத தானை உருமுக் குரல் ஓடை யானை","வாடாத வென்றி மிகு சச்சந்தன் என்ப மன்னன்","வீடாத கற்பின் அவன் தேவி விசயை என்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"The patikam opens the story on King Saccandan, of the unbending sceptre, the cool white umbrella, the army that never turns, the thunder-voiced elephants and victory that does not fade, and his queen Vijayai, whose chastity never gives way.","n":"The king's praise is all outward power and the sceptre is called கோடாத (unbending); the queen's single epithet is வீடாத கற்பு. The next two verses show the king falling through his own desire while the queen's karpu is never in question.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","karpu","vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 7","lb":"CC7","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:8":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":8,"t":["சேந்து ஒத்து அலர்ந்த செழுந்தாமரை அன்ன வாள் கண்","பூந்தொத்து அலர்ந்த பசும் பொன் கொடி அன்ன பொற்பின்","ஏந்து ஒத்து அலர்ந்த முலையின் அமிர்து அன்ன சாயல்","வேந்தற்கு அமுதாய் விளையாடுதற்கு ஏது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai, with eyes like a red lotus in full bloom, the grace of a green-gold creeper and a softness like nectar, became the king's nectar and the cause of his play.","n":"ஏது (cause) is the hinge: her beauty is not blamed, but it is named as the cause of the king's pleasure, which verse 9 turns into the cause of his fall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 8","lb":"CC8","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:9":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":9,"t":["கல்லார் மணிப் பூண் அவன் காமம் கனைந்து கன்றிச்","சொல்லாறு கேளான் நனி சூழ்ச்சியில் தோற்ற வாறும்","புல்லார் புகலப் பொறி மஞ்ஞையில் தேவி போகிச்","செல் ஆறு இழுக்கிச் சுடுகாடு அவள் சேர்ந்த வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"The summary tells how the king, his desire grown dense and hard, would not hear the counsel offered and was beaten by a plot, and how, while his enemies rejoiced, the queen escaped on the mechanical peacock, lost her way and came down at the burning ground.","n":"The cause given is the king's own: காமம் கனைந்து கன்றி and சொல்லாறு கேளான். Neither fate nor a former deed is named here, so the fall is told as a fault of rule, not as karma. The queen's flight ends at a burning ground (சுடுகாடு), where the hero will be born.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kelvi","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 9","lb":"CC9","ev":"Saccandan, deaf to counsel in his desire, is overthrown by a plot, and Vijayai flees on the mechanical peacock to the burning ground.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:10":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":10,"t":["நாள் உற்று நம்பி பிறந்தான் திசை பத்தும் நந்தத்","தோள் உற்று ஓர் தெய்வம் துணையாய்த் துயர் தீர்த்த வாறும்","கோள் உற்ற கோன் போல் அவன் கொண்டு வளர்த்த வாறும்","வாள் உற்ற கண்ணாள் மகன் வாழ்க என நோற்ற வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"It goes on to tell how on the fated day the prince was born and the ten directions flourished, how a deity stood at the mother's shoulder as companion and ended her distress, how one took him up and reared him like a king who has gained a treasure, and how the sword-eyed mother kept a vow that her son might live.","n":"நோற்ற is the verb of the vow: the mother does not stay to rear the child but turns to an ascetic discipline on his behalf. கோள் உற்ற கோன் போல் is hard: 'like a king who has seized a prize' fits the finder's joy, but the phrase may be corrupt or carry another sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thunai","nonbu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 10","lb":"CC10","ev":"Civakan is born at the burning ground, a deity relieves Vijayai, a foster father takes him to rear, and Vijayai takes a vow for her son.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தெய்வம்","கந்துக்கடன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:11":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":11,"t":["நெஞ்சம் புணையாக் கலை மாக் கடல் நீந்தி ஆங்கே","வஞ்சம் மறவர் நிரை வள்ளல் விடுத்த வாறும்","விஞ்சைக்கு இறைவன் மகள் வீணையில் தோற்ற வாறும்","நஞ்சு உற்ற காம நனி நாகரில் துய்த்த வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"It tells how he swam the great sea of learning with his heart as raft, how the liberal one freed the herd taken by deceitful hunters, how the Vidyadhara king's daughter was defeated at the vina, and how he enjoyed among the townsmen a desire that carries poison.","n":"நஞ்சு உற்ற காம places a judgment inside the summary: the pleasures the following books narrate at length are called poisoned before they begin. நாகர் may be the townspeople or the Naga folk; the reading takes the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kaamam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 11","lb":"CC11","ev":"Civakan masters the arts, frees the stolen herd from the hunters and defeats Gandharvadattai at the vina.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மறவர்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:12":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":12,"t":["முந்நீர்ப் படு சங்கு அலற முரசு ஆர்ப்ப மூதூர்ச்","செந்நீர்க் கடியின் விழவாட்டினுள் தேங்கொள் சுண்ணம்","மைந்நீர் நெடுங்கண் இரு மங்கையர் தம்முள் மாறாய்","இந்நீர்ப் படியேம் இவை தோற்றனம் என்ற வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"It tells how, at the festival bath of the old city with conches crying and drums sounding, two dark-eyed young women fell into rivalry over their scented powder and said they would not bathe in this water if they had lost.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 12","lb":"CC12","ev":"Suramanjari and Gunamalai wager on their scented powders at the festival bath.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:13":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":13,"t":["சுண்ணம் உடைந்து சுரமஞ்சரி சோர்ந்து தோழி","வண்ணம் நெடுங் கண் குண மாலையை வைது மாறிப்","புண் மேல் புடையில் புகைந்து ஆண் உரு யாதும் நோக்காள்","கண் நோக்கு உடைந்து கடிமாடம் அடைந்த வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"It tells how Suramanjari, her powder beaten, drooped, reviled Gunamalai, turned away smouldering like a wound struck again, resolved to look on no male form, and withdrew into the guarded mansion.","n":"Her refusal to see any man is a vow of injured pride, not of renunciation; the patikam sets it up here to be undone in her own ilampakam.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","igal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 13","lb":"CC13","ev":"Suramanjari loses the powder contest, abuses Gunamalai and shuts herself away from the sight of men.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:14":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":14,"t":["பொன் துஞ்சு மார்பன் புனல் ஆட்டிடைப் புன்கண் எய்தி","நின்று எஞ்சுகின்ற ஞமலிக்கு அமிர்து ஈந்த வாறும்","அன்றைப் பகலே குண மாலையை அச்சுறுத்த","வென்றிக் களிற்றை விரிதார் அவன் வென்ற வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"It tells how the gold-chested hero gave nectar to a dog that had come to harm at the bathing festival and stood dying, and how that same day he overcame the victorious elephant that had terrified Gunamalai.","n":"புன்கண் எய்தி நின்று எஞ்சுகின்ற qualifies the dog (ஞமலி): it is the animal that came to grief at the festival bath, not the hero. அமிர்து here is not food: in the story it is the sacred formula whispered to the dying animal, and the verse's word lets the gift be both a mercy and a teaching. The pairing of the dog and the elephant on one day sets a kindness to the weakest beside a feat against the strongest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","amudham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 14","lb":"CC14","ev":"Civakan gives the dying dog the nectar of the formula and the same day subdues the elephant that frightened Gunamalai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:15":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":15,"t":["தேன் ஊறு தீம் சொல் குண மாலையைச் சேர்ந்த வாறும்","கோன் ஊறு செய்வான் கருதிச் சிறை கொண்ட வாறும்","வான் ஆறு இழிந்து மழை மின் என வந்த ஓர் தேவன்","ஊன் நாறு ஒளத வேல் உரவோன் கொண்டு எழுந்த வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"It tells how he won Gunamalai of honeyed speech, how the king, meaning him harm, seized him into prison, and how a god came down the sky's path like lightning from a cloud and carried the strong one of the flesh-scented spear away.","n":"The verse does not say who the god is; in the story he is the dog of verse 14, reborn as a god, and the patikam's order sets the kindness and the rescue one verse apart. ஒளத in line 4 is not a word as printed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nanri","thunai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 15","lb":"CC15","ev":"Civakan marries Gunamalai, is imprisoned by the usurping king and is carried off by a god who descends from the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கட்டியங்காரன்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:1"},"cc:16":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":16,"t":["தேங்காத மள்ளர் திரள் தோள் இணை சிக்க யாத்த","பூங் கச்சு நீக்கிப் பொறி மாண்கலம் நல்ல சேர்த்தி","நீங்காத காதல் உடையாய் நினைக்க என்று பின்னும்","பாங்கு ஆய விஞ்சை பணித்து ஆங்கு விடுத்த வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"The god who carried Civakan off unties the flower-cord with which tireless soldiers had bound his arms, adorns him with fine jewels, bids him, as one of unfailing love, to think of him, teaches him fitting spells and sends him on his way.","n":"The verse does not name the god; verse 15 calls him only a god (தேவன்) who came down like lightning. That he is the dog given nectar in verse 14 is the story's later telling, not the patikam's. The words நினைக்க (think, remember) set up the god as a helper to be called on in need, and the spells (விஞ்சை) are given, not earned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","thunai","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 16","lb":"CC16","ev":"The god frees Civakan from his bonds, adorns him, teaches him spells and sends him off.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:17":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":17,"t":["பைந் நாகப் பள்ளி மணி வண்ணனின் பாயல் கொண்டு","கைந் நாகம் துஞ்சும் கமழ் காந்தள் அம் சாரல் போகி","மைந் நாக வேலி மணி பல்லவ தேயம் நண்ணிக்","கொய்ந் நாகச் சோலைக் கொடி அந் நகர் புக்க வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"Civakan crosses the fragrant kanthal slopes where elephants sleep as Vishnu sleeps on his serpent bed, reaches the jewelled Pallava land hedged by dark hills, and enters its city of flags and naga groves.","n":"Every line turns on நாகம் in a different sense (serpent, elephant, hill, the naga tree), a display of the poet's craft inside a bare plot summary. The image of Vishnu (மணி வண்ணன்) on the serpent bed is ornament in a Jain work and carries no claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 17","lb":"CC17","ev":"Civakan travels over the hills and enters the city of the Pallava land.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:18":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":18,"t":["அத்தம் அனைய களிற்று அந் நகர் மன்னன் மங்கை","முத்தம் உரிஞ்சும் முகிழ் மென் முலை மின் அனாளைப்","பைத்து அங்கு ஓர் நாகம் பனி மா மதி என்று தீண்டச்","சித்தம் குழையற்க எனத் தீர்த்து அவள் சேர்ந்த வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"A snake, rising at the face of the city king's daughter as if it were the cool moon, bites her; Civakan, saying 'let not your heart melt', cures her and is joined with her.","n":"The snake's mistake is a praise of her face, so the bite itself is a compliment. The cure is given only as a word of comfort (சித்தம் குழையற்க); the means is not named, though verse 16 has just given Civakan spells.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 18","lb":"CC18","ev":"Civakan cures the Pallava king's daughter of a snakebite and marries her.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:19":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":19,"t":["பொன் பூண் சுமந்த புணர் மெல் முலைக் கோடு போழ","நல் பூங் கழலான் இரு திங்கள் நயந்த வாறும்","கல் பாடு அழித்த கன மா மணித் தூண் செய் தோளான்","வெற்பு ஊடு அறுத்து விரைவின் நெறிக் கொண்ட வாறும்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan delights in her for two months, then, with arms like jewelled pillars, cuts through the mountains and swiftly takes the road again.","n":"The patikam gives union and departure in one verse, a pattern it repeats with the next bride: the hero never stays where he has won.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 19","lb":"CC19","ev":"After two months with his new wife, Civakan leaves and crosses the mountains.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:20":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":20,"t":["தள்ளாத சும்மை மிகு தக்க நல் நாடு நண்ணி","விள்ளா விழுச்சீர் வணிகன் மகள் வேல் கண் நோக்கம்","உள் ஆவி வாட்ட உயிர் ஒன்று ஒத்து உறைந்த வாறும்","கள் ஆவி நாறும் கமழ் கோதையின் போய வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"Reaching the rich Takka land, Civakan is wasted within by the spear-eyed gaze of a merchant's daughter, lives with her as one life, and then leaves her of the fragrant garland.","n":"The woman's eyes act on him (வாட்ட, waste), the language of love's wound, yet the line that joins them as one life is followed at once by his leaving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 20","lb":"CC20","ev":"In the Takka land Civakan marries a merchant's daughter and then leaves her.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:21":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":21,"t":["இன்னீர் அமிர்து அன்னவள் கண் இணை மாரி கற்பப்","பொன் ஊர் கழலான் பொழி மா மழைக் காடு போகி","மின்னீர் வெள் வேலவன் மத்திம தேய மன்னன்","கொன்னூர் கொடு வெம் சிலை கண்டு எதிர் கொண்ட வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"While her nectar-like eyes rain tears, Civakan crosses a forest of heavy rain, and the king of the middle country, seeing his terrible bow, comes out to receive him.","n":"Line 3's வெள் வேலவன் (the one of the bright spear) may be Civakan or the king, and so who sees whose bow in line 4 is not fixed; the rain of her tears and the rain-forest he walks into are set side by side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 21","lb":"CC21","ev":"Civakan leaves his weeping wife, crosses a rain-forest and is received by the king of the middle country.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்","மத்திம தேய மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:22":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":22,"t":["திண் தேர் அரசர் திறல் சிங்கங்கள் வில்லும் வாளும்","கண்டு ஆங்கு உவந்து கடி பெய்து இவண் காத்தும் என்று","கொண்டார் குடங்கை அளவே உள கண்ணினாளைப்","புண் தாங்கு எரிவேல் இளையோற்குப் புணர்த்த வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"The strong lion-like royals of the chariot kings, delighted at his bow and sword, keep him there under guard and give the girl whose eyes are a palmful wide to the young man of the burning spear.","n":"Line 1 may mean the king's lion-like sons or the kings themselves, and கடி பெய்து may mean setting a guard or making the wedding; the reading turns on both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 22","lb":"CC22","ev":"The royals of the middle country, pleased with Civakan's skill in arms, keep him and give him a princess in marriage.","cl":[],"ag":["திறல் சிங்கங்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:23":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":23,"t":["மதியம் கெடுத்த வய மீன் எனத் தம்பி மாழாந்து","உதிதற்கு உரியாள் பணியால் உடன் ஆய வாறும்","நிதியின் நெறியின் அவன் தோழர் நிரந்த வாறும்","பதியின் அகன்று பயந்தாளைப் பணிந்த வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"His younger brother, desolate like the star that has lost the moon, comes to join him at the bidding of a woman; his friends gather to him; and Civakan leaves the city to bow before his mother.","n":"Who the one 'fit to rise' (உதிதற்கு உரியாள்) is, and how the friends come 'by the way of treasure' (நிதியின் நெறியின்), the patikam does not explain; the story may mean Gandharvadattai's arts, but the verse does not name her. The brother's grief is likened to a star losing its moon, a bond in which the younger one shines only beside the elder.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","anbu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 23","lb":"CC23","ev":"Civakan's brother and friends rejoin him, and he leaves the city to bow before his mother.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","தோழர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:24":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":24,"t":["கண் வாள் அறுக்கும் கமழ்தார் அவன் தாயொடு எண்ணி","விண் வாள் அறுக்கும் நகர் வீதி புகுந்த வாறும்","மண் மேல் விளக்காய் வரத்தில் பிறந்தாள் ஒர் கன்னிப்","பெண் ஆர் அமிர்தின் பெரு வாரியுள் பட்ட வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"Having taken counsel with his mother, Civakan of the fragrant garland enters the sky-high streets of the city and falls into the great sea of nectar of a maiden born by a boon, a lamp upon the earth.","n":"The counsel with the mother is the turn from wandering to the recovery of the kingdom. The maiden of lines 3 and 4 is not named; the order of the ilampakams makes her Vimalai, but the verse alone does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 24","lb":"CC24","ev":"After consulting his mother, Civakan enters the city and falls in love with a maiden born by a boon.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:25":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":25,"t":["துஞ்சா மணிப் பூண் சுரமஞ்சரி என்னும் நாமத்து","அம் சாயல் பூத்த அகிலார் துகிலாய் பொன் அல்குல்","எஞ்சாத இன்பக் கொடி தாழ்த்ததும் பன்றி எய்து","நஞ்சு ஊறும் வேலான் பகை நாம் அறக் கொன்ற வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"The girl named Suramanjari, a vine of endless pleasure, lowers her banner; and Civakan shoots the boar and kills the enemy so that all fear of him ends.","n":"Line 4's நஞ்சு ஊறும் வேலான் (he of the poison-dripping spear) can be Civakan or the enemy he kills; the verse names neither the enemy nor the boar contest's prize. Suramanjari's lowered banner reverses the vow told in verse 13.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kolai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 25","lb":"CC25","ev":"Suramanjari yields to Civakan; he shoots the boar target and kills his enemy.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:26":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":26,"t":["புண் தோய்த்து எடுத்த பொரு வேல் எனச் சேந்து நீண்ட","கண் போன்ற மாமன் மகள் கண் மணிப் பாவை அன்ன","பெண் பால் அமிர்தின் நலம் பெற்றதும், பொற்பச் செங்கோல்","தண் பால் மதி தோய் குடை தண் நிழல் பாய வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"Civakan wins the nectar-like beauty of his maternal uncle's daughter, whose red, long eyes are like a spear drawn from a wound, and the cool shade of his moon-touching umbrella and just sceptre spreads over the land.","n":"The cross-cousin marriage and the return of the just sceptre are given in one verse: the recovered kingdom and the last bride arrive together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 26","lb":"CC26","ev":"Civakan marries his uncle's daughter and rules with a just sceptre.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:27":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":27,"t":["திறை மன்னர் உய்ப்பத் திரு நிற்பச் செங்கோல் நடப்பக்","குறைவு இன்றிக் கொற்றம் உயரத் தெவ்வர் தேர் பணிய","உறைகின்ற காலத்து அறம் கேட்டு உரும் உற்ற பாம்பின்","அறிவன் அடிக் கீழ் அரசு அஞ்சித் துறந்த வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"While kings bring tribute, fortune stays, his just sceptre runs and his victories stand with every enemy chariot bowed, Civakan hears the dharma and, afraid of kingship like a snake struck by thunder, renounces at the feet of the Knower.","n":"The renunciation comes at the height of rule, not after loss: lines 1 and 2 list everything a king could want. The motive given is fear of kingship itself (அரசு அஞ்சி), roused by hearing the dharma, and the snake struck by thunder makes the turn sudden and total. The teacher is named only அறிவன் (the Knower), a title for the omniscient one; the verse names no school and narrates the act rather than stating a rule, so no claim is recorded.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","acham","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 27","lb":"CC27","ev":"At the height of his reign Civakan hears the dharma and renounces kingship at the feet of the omniscient teacher.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:28":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":28,"t":["கோணைக் களிற்றுக் கொடித் தேர் இவுளிக் கடல் சூழ்","வாள் மொய்த்த தானை அவன் தம்பியும் தோழன் மாரும்","பூண் மொய்த்த பொம்மல் முலையாரும் புலம் துறப்ப","வீணைக் கிழவன் விருந்து ஆர் கதிச் சென்ற வாறும்,"],"p":"","r":"His younger brother with an army of elephants, chariots and sea-like horse, his friends and his richly adorned wives all renounce the things of sense, and Civakan, lord of the vina, goes to the state that is ever new.","n":"The whole household goes with him: brother, friends and wives renounce together, so renunciation here is not a lone flight from kin. The goal is named only விருந்து ஆர் கதி (the state full of newness); whether it is a heaven or final release the verse does not fix, though the last ilampakam is named for release. Calling him வீணைக் கிழவன் at this moment recalls the vina contest by which he first won a bride.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","veedu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 28","lb":"CC28","ev":"Civakan's brother, friends and wives renounce, and Civakan attains the higher state.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","தோழர்","மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:29":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":29,"t":["தேன் வாய் உமிழ்ந்த அமிர்து உண்டவன் போன்று செல்வன்","வான் வாய் வணக்கும் நலத்தார் முலை போகம் வேண்டான்","ஏனோரும் ஏத்த அவன் எய்திய இன்ப வெள்ளம்","ஈனோர்க்கு உரைப்பாம் பதிகத்துள் இயன்ற வாறே,"],"p":"","r":"Like one who has eaten nectar from a mouth of honey, the prosperous one no longer wants the pleasure of women whose beauty makes the heavens bow; the poet says he will tell those here the flood of bliss Civakan attained, praised by all, as the patikam has set it out.","n":"The simile reverses the usual order: Civakan does not give up women to win bliss; having tasted a greater sweetness, he no longer wants the lesser. This is told of Civakan, not laid down as a rule, so no claim is recorded. The closing words (பதிகத்துள் இயன்ற வாறே) end the summary that began at verse 9.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam","thuravu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 29","lb":"CC29","ev":"Civakan, no longer wanting the pleasures of women, attains a flood of bliss.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:2"},"cc:30":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":30,"t":["நா வீற்று இருந்த புல மா மகளோடு நன் பொன்","பூ வீற்று இருந்த திருமாமகள் புல்ல நாளும்","பா வீற்று இருந்த கலை பார் அறச் சென்ற கேள்விக்","கோ வீற்று இருந்த குடி நாட்டு அணி கூறல் உற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet declares that he begins to tell the splendour of the land where the goddess of learning who sits on the tongue and the goddess of fortune who sits on the golden flower embrace a people whose king's learning in song and the arts has travelled across the earth.","n":"The ilampakam takes its name from the first of the two goddesses: learning (the one on the tongue) and fortune (the one on the flower) are set together as the double mark of the land before any of its rivers or fields appear. The run கேள்விக் கோ is compressed; it is read here as the king whose learning by listening is renowned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","kalvi","selvam","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 30","lb":"CC30","ev":"The poet announces that he will describe the land.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:31":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":31,"t":["காய் மாண்ட தெங்கின் பழம் வீழக் கமுகின் நெற்றிப்","பூ மாண்ட தீம் தேன் தொடை கீறி வருக்கை போழ்ந்து","தேமாங் கனி சிதறி வாழைப் பழங்கள் சிந்தும்","ஏமாங்கதம் என்று இசையால் திசை போயது உண்டே"],"p":"","r":"A ripe coconut falls, tears the honeycomb on the areca's crown, splits a jackfruit, scatters mangoes and knocks down plantains: such is the land whose name, Emangadam, has gone out to every quarter.","n":"Plenty is shown as a chain: one falling fruit sets off four more. The land is named only after the picture of its excess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 31","lb":"CC31","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:32":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":32,"t":["இலங்கல் ஆழியினான் களிற்று ஈட்டம் போல்","கலங்கு தெண் திரை மேய்ந்து கண மழை","பொலம் கொள் கொன்றையினான் சடை போல் மின்னி","விலங்கல் சேர்ந்து விண் ஏறி விட்டு ஆர்த்தவே"],"p":"","r":"Flocks of cloud, like the elephant herds of the god who holds the shining discus, graze on the churning clear waves of the sea, flash like the matted hair of the god with the golden konrai, settle on the mountains, climb the sky and roar.","n":"Vishnu's elephants and Siva's lightning-bright locks are used purely as images; the Jain poet takes the two gods as stock similes, with no claim about them. The verse opens a run (32 to 38) that follows the water from the sea, through the mountains and the fields, back to the sea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 32","lb":"CC32","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:33":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":33,"t":["தேன் நிரைத்து உயர் மொய்வரைச் சென்னியின்","மேல் நிரைத்து விசும்பு உற வெள்ளி வெண்","கோல் நிரைத்தன போல் கொழுந் தாரைகள்","வான் நிரைத்து மணந்து சொரிந்தவே"],"p":"","r":"Massed downpours stand from the honey-laden peaks up to the sky like rows of silver rods, and pour down together.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 33","lb":"CC33","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:34":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":34,"t":["குழவி வெண் மதிக் கோடு உழக் கீண்டு தேன்","முழவின் நின்று அதிர் மொய் வரைச் சென்னியின்","இழியும் வெள் அருவித் திரள் யாவையும்","குழுவின் மாடத் துகில் கொடி போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"From peaks so high that the horn of the young white moon tears their honeycombs, and that thunder like drums, the white streams of the falls hang like the cloth flags on a crowd of mansions.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 34","lb":"CC34","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:35":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":35,"t":["இலங்கு நீள் முடி இந்திரன் மார்பின் மேல்","விலங்கி வீழ்ந்த முத்தாரமும் போன்றவை","நலம் கொள் பொன்னொடு நல் மணி சிந்தலால்","கலன் பெய் பேழை கவிழ்த்தவும் போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The falls look like the pearl strings slipped crooked on the chest of crowned Indra, and, since they scatter gold and good gems, like jewel caskets turned upside down.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 35","lb":"CC35","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:36":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":36,"t":["வள்ளல் கைத்தல மாந்தரின் மால்வரைக்","கொள்ளை கொண்ட கொழு நிதிக் குப்பையை","உள்ளம் இல்லவர்க்கு ஊர் தொறும் உய்த்து உராய்","வெள்ளம் நாடு மடுத்து விரைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Like the hand of a generous man, the flood takes the rich heap of wealth plundered from the great mountain, carries it town after town to those who have nothing, and hurries on to fill the land.","n":"The simile makes the river's silt and gems a gift with recipients: it goes to those who lack, not to the land at large. உள்ளம் இல்லவர் is read as 'those without means'; it could also be heard as 'those without heart', which would turn the gift into a rebuke of the mean, and the reading is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","naadu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 36","lb":"CC36","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:37":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":37,"t":["மையல் யானையின் மும் மதம் ஆர்ந்து தேன்","ஐய பொன் அசும்பு ஆடி அளைந்து உராய்ச்","செய்ய சந்தனம் தீம் பழம் ஆதியா","நைய வாரி நடந்தது நன்று அரோ."],"p":"","r":"Having drunk the triple must of maddened elephants, bathed in honey and fine gold-dust, and rubbed along carrying red sandalwood, sweet fruit and the rest until they were worn, the flood went on its fine way.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 37","lb":"CC37","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:38":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":38,"t":["வீடு இல் பட்டினம் வவ்விய வேந்து எனக்","காடு கையரிக் கொண்டு கவர்ந்து போய்","மோடு கொள் புனல் மூரி நெடுங் கடல்","நாடு முற்றியதோ என நண்ணிற்றே."],"p":"","r":"Sweeping up the forest as a king who has seized a city carries off its spoils, the flood with its swelling water reached the great long sea, so that one might ask whether it had come to besiege the sea's own land.","n":"வீடு இல் பட்டினம் in line 1 is obscure: a city left without houses, or one with no escape; the king's plunder is clear, the qualifier is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 38","lb":"CC38","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:39":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":39,"t":["திரை பொரு கனை கடல் செல்வன் சென்னி மேல்","நுரை எனும் மாலையை நுகரச் சூட்டுவான்","சரை எனும் பெயர் உடைத் தடம் கொள் வெம் முலைக்","குரை புனல் கன்னி கொண்டு இழிந்தது என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"They say the river named Sarai, a maiden with broad warm breasts, came down with her roaring water to set on the head of the lord of the wave-beaten sea a garland of foam for him to enjoy.","n":"The river is a bride going to her husband the sea; என்பவே presents the conceit as a saying. The name சரை is given without anything to tie it to a known river.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 39","lb":"CC39","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:40":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":40,"t":["பழம் கொள் தெங்கு இலை எனப் பரந்து பாய் புனல்","வழங்க முன் இயற்றிய சுதை செய் வாய்த் தலை","தழம் குரல் பம்பையில் சாற்றி நாடு எலாம்","முழங்கு தீம் புனல் அகம் முரிய மொய்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The water spread like the fronds of a fruiting coconut, ran through the mortared sluice-heads built beforehand for it, and, its coming announced through the land with the deep-voiced drum, crowded into the fields with a sweet roar.","n":"The flood is not only wild water: channels prepared in advance and a drum proclaiming its arrival make it an event the land is organized to receive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 40","lb":"CC40","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:41":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":41,"t":["வெலற்கு அரும் குஞ்சரம் வேட்டம் பட்டு எனத்","தலைத் தலை அவர் கதம் தவிர்ப்பத் தாழ்ந்து போய்க்","குலத் தலை மகளிர் தம் கற்பின் கோட்டகம்","நிலைப் படா நிறைந்தன பிறவும் என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Like an unconquerable elephant caught in the hunt, its fury checked at dam after dam, the water sank low and filled the tanks, they say, deep beyond footing like the chastity of high-born women, and filled the other waters too.","n":"நிறைந்தன ('filled') plays on நிறை, the self-containment the epics pair with chastity: the tank's fullness within its banks is the woman's virtue held within bounds. நிலைப் படா is read as 'beyond a footing, unfathomable'; it may instead mean 'not standing still', and the comparison turns on which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","nirai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 41","lb":"CC41","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:42":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":42,"t":["கவ்வையும் கடும் புனல் ஒலியும் காப்பவர்","செவ்வன் நூறு ஆயிரம் சிலைக்கும் பம்பையும்","எவ் எலாத் திசைகளும் ஈண்டிக் காரொடு","பவ்வம் நின்று இயம்புவது ஒத்த என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The clamour, the roar of the fierce water and the drums that a hundred thousand guards beat, gathering from every quarter, sounded, they say, like the sea roaring along with the rain-clouds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 42","lb":"CC42","ev":"Guards gathered along the banks beat drums against the flood.","cl":[],"ag":["காப்பவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:43":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":43,"t":["மாமனும் மருகனும் போலும் அன்பின","காமனும் சாமனும் கலந்த காட்சிய;","பூமனும் அரிசிப் புல் ஆர்ந்த மோட்டின","தாம் இனம் அமைந்து தம் தொழிலின் மிக்கவே."],"p":"","r":"The oxen, loving each other like uncle and nephew and matched like Kaman and Saman together, with humps fed full on grass, keep to their herds and excel at their work.","n":"Kama and his brother Saman give the image of a matched yoke. பூமனும் in line 3 is obscure and its sense is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 43","lb":"CC43","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:44":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":44,"t":["நெறி மருப்பு எருமையின் ஒருத்தல் நீள் இனம்","செறி மருப்பு ஏற்று இனம் சிலம்பப் பண்உறீஇப்","பொறி வரி வராலினம் இரியப் புக்கு உடன்","வெறி கமழ் கழனியுள் உழுநர் வெள்ளமே."],"p":"","r":"Long herds of curve-horned male buffalo and close-horned bulls, with the ploughmen singing to them, rush into the fragrant fields together and scatter the shoals of spotted varal fish: a flood of ploughmen.","n":"The verse ends on வெள்ளமே, turning the word just used for the river's flood onto the crowd of ploughmen: the human work continues the water's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 44","lb":"CC44","ev":"The ploughmen drive buffalo and bulls into the fields to plough, scattering the fish.","cl":[],"ag":["உழவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:45":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":45,"t":["சேறு அமை செறுவினுள் செந்நெல் வால் முளை","வீறொடு விளைக எனத் தொழுது வித்துவார்;","நாறு இது பதம் எனப் பறித்து நாள் செய்வார்;","கூறிய கடைசியர் குழாம் கொண்டு ஏகுவார்."],"p":"","r":"In the worked fields they sow the bright sprouts of red paddy with folded hands, saying 'grow with vigour', pull the seedlings saying 'these are ready', and set them that same day, the field-women going in their bands as they are told.","n":"The sower's தொழுது is a bow to the seed itself, a wish spoken to the crop; no god is named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 45","lb":"CC45","ev":"The farmers sow the paddy with a gesture of worship, and the field-women pull and transplant the seedlings.","cl":[],"ag":["உழவர்","கடைசியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:46":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":46,"t":["முலைத் தடம் சேதகம் பொறிப்ப மற்று அவர்","குலைத்து உடன் பதித்தலின் குதித்த வாள் கயல்","புலத்து இடைக் கவரி கன்று ஊட்டப் போந்த பால்","நிலத்து இடைப் பாய்ந்து அவை பிறழும் நீரவே."],"p":"","r":"As the women press the seedlings in together, the mud marking their broad breasts, bright kayal fish leap out, and where milk ran on the ground as a cow fed her calf in the field, the fish thrash about in it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 46","lb":"CC46","ev":"The field-women transplant, and fish leap into milk spilled by a suckling calf.","cl":[],"ag":["கடைசியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:47":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":47,"t":["பால் சுவை அறிந்து அவை பழனத் தாமரை","மேல் செலப் பாய்தலின் வெரீஇய வண்டு இனம்","கோல் தொடி நுளைச்சியர் முத்தம் கோப்பவர்","ஏற்றிய மாலைத் தேன் இரியப் பாய்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The fish, having tasted the milk, leap onto the lotuses of the field; the startled bees fly off and swarm onto the garlands that bangled fisherwomen stringing pearls have hung up, so that the bees already there flee.","n":"46 to 48 are one chain of startlement passed from creature to creature, as 31 was one chain of falling fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 47","lb":"CC47","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நுளைச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:48":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":48,"t":["இரிந்த தேன் குவளையின் நெற்றி தைவர","முரிந்து போது அவிழ்ந்து கொங்கு உயிர்க்கும் முல்லையின்","அரும்பு சேர்ந்து அணி ஞிமிறு ஆர்ப்ப வாய் பதம்","விருந்து எதிர் கொண்ம் எனத் தழுவி வீழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The fleeing bees brush the brows of the kuvalai, then fall on the jasmine buds that have burst open breathing pollen and embrace them, with the bright bees humming as if to say 'receive us as guests'.","n":"The household virtue of receiving the guest is lent to flowers; the bees are the guests the opened buds take in.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 48","lb":"CC48","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:49":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":49,"t":["வள முடி நடுபவர் வரம்பு இல் கம்பலை","இள மழை முழக்கு என மஞ்ஞை ஏங்கலின்","அளமரு குயிலினம் அழுங்கிப் பூம் பொழில்","உளம் மெலி மகளிரின் ஒடுங்கும் என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Because the boundless din of the transplanters sounds like the thunder of a young cloud, the peacocks cry out, and the troubled cuckoos fall silent and shrink into the flowering groves like women faint of heart, they say.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 49","lb":"CC49","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கடைசியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:50":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":50,"t":["வளைக் கையால் கடைசியர் மட்டு வாக்கலின்","திளைத்தவர் பருகிய தேறல் தேங்குழிக்","களிப்ப உண்டு இள அனம் கன்னி நாரையைத்","திளைத்தலின் பெடை மயில் தெருட்டும் செம்மற்றே."],"p":"","r":"Where the field-women pour out toddy with bangled hands, a young swan drinks itself drunk at the pool where the drinkers' leavings have gathered and dallies with a young heron, so that the peahen must bring it to its senses.","n":"The drink of the labourers spills into the animal world as comedy. The last line, with பெடை மயில் தெருட்டும், is read as the peahen sobering the swan; whom she admonishes is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kal","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 50","lb":"CC50","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கடைசியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:51":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":51,"t":["கண் எனக் குவளையும் கட்டல் ஓம்பினார்","வண்ண வாள் முகம் என மரையின் உள் புகார்","பண் எழுத்து இயல் படப் பரப்பி இட்டனர்","தண் வயல் உழவர் தம் தன்மை இன்னதே."],"p":"","r":"The farmers of the cool fields refrain from weeding out the kuvalai because it is like eyes, and keep out of the lotus because it is like a bright face; such is the nature of these farmers.","n":"Their restraint comes from the flowers' likeness to a beloved's eyes and face, not from any rule about harm. Line 3 (பண் எழுத்து இயல் படப் பரப்பி இட்டனர்) is obscure: something is spread out in the ordered way of musical notation, perhaps the seedlings in rows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 51","lb":"CC51","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:52":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":52,"t":["நித்திலப் பந்துடன் ஈன்று பாதிரி","ஒத்த பூ உடற்றிய நாவின் நாகினால்","தத்து நீர் நாரை மேல் எறியத் தண் கடல்","பைத்து எழு திரை எனப் பறவை ஆலுமே."],"p":"","r":"The patiri bears pearl-like round buds and flowers, and when something is thrown at a leaping heron in the water, the birds rise screaming like the swelling waves of the cool sea.","n":"Line 2 (ஒத்த பூ உடற்றிய நாவின் நாகினால்) is obscure, and who throws, and what, is not clear from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 52","lb":"CC52","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:53":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":53,"t":["சொல் அரும் சூல் பசும் பாம்பின் தோற்றம் போல்","மெல்லவே கரு இருந்து ஈன்று மேல் அலார்","செல்வமே போல் தலை நிறுவித் தேர்ந்த நூல்","கல்வி சேர் மாந்தரின் இறைஞ்சிக் காய்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The paddy, its ear forming within like a green snake carrying young, slowly holds the grain and brings it forth; at first it holds its head up like the wealth of base men, then, heavy with grain, it bows like people of tested learning.","n":"The two halves of the simile are one plant at two ages: the stalk that stands erect while empty is the same that bows when full, so bowing is shown as what fullness does, not a separate virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","panivu","selvam","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 53","lb":"CC53","ev":"","cl":[["the learned bow while the wealth of the base holds its head high","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:3"},"cc:54":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":54,"t":["மீன் கணின் அளவும் வெற்று இடங்கள் இன்மையால்","தேன் கணக் கரும்பு இயல் காடும் செந் நெலின்","வான் புகழ் களிறு மாய் கழனி ஆக்கமும்","ஊன் கணார்க்கு உரைப்ப அரிது ஒல் என் சும்மைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says the wealth of the land, where not a space the size of a fish's eye lies empty, with cane groves swarming with bees and paddy fields tall enough to hide famed elephants, cannot be told to those with eyes of flesh, so loud is its din.","n":"ஊன் கணார் (those with eyes of flesh) sets ordinary sight against a fuller seeing: the poet opens the description by declaring it beyond telling, the standard figure of the country-praise section, and then tells it for twenty-four verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 54","lb":"CC54","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:55":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":55,"t":["ஆய் பிழி விருத்து வண்டு அயிற்றி உண்டு தேன்","வாய் பொழி குவளைகள் சூடி மள்ளர்கள்","தேய் பிறை இரும்பு தம் வலக்கை சேர்த்தினர்","ஆய் செந் நெல் அகன்ற காடு அரிகுற்றார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The field labourers, having drunk filtered toddy and wearing honey-dripping blue lilies, take the iron sickle shaped like a waning crescent in their right hands and reap the broad field of red paddy.","n":"The reapers (மள்ளர்) drink toddy before work and the Jain poet records it without comment; the sickle is seen as a waning moon, the first of many sky images in the section.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 55","lb":"CC55","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:56":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":56,"t":["வலியுடைக் கைகளால் மலர்ந்த தாமரை","மெலிவு எய்தக் குவளைகள் வாடக்கம் பலம்","பொலிவு எய்தப் பூம் பொய்கை சிலம்பிப் பார்ப்பு எழ","மலை பட அரிந்து கூன் குயம் கை மாற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"With strong hands they reap so that the lotuses droop and the lilies wither, the clamour swells, the flowering pond echoes and the young birds start up, and they stack the cut paddy like hills and pass the curved sickles from hand to hand.","n":"The labour is shown through its cost to the pond flowers and fledglings, a small note of damage within the praise of plenty. கம்பலம் is taken here as clamour; the word division of line 2 is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 56","lb":"CC56","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:57":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":57,"t":["வாளையின் இனம் தலை இரிய வண்டு அலர்","தாள் உடைத் தாமரை கிழிய வண் சுமை","கோள் உடை இளையவர் குழாம் கொண்டு ஏகலில்","பாளை வாய் கமுகு இனம் பழங்கள் சிந்துமே."],"p":"","r":"As bands of young men carry off their heavy loads of sheaves, shoals of vaalai fish flee headlong, bee-opened lotuses are torn on their stalks, and the areca palms with their spathes shed their nuts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 57","lb":"CC57","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:58":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":58,"t":["சோர் புயல் முகில் தலை விலங்கித் தூ நிலம்","மார்பு கொண்டு ஆர்ந்து அது நரல வண் சுளை","ஆர்புறு பலாப் பழம் அழிந்த நீள் களம்","போர்பினால் மலிந்து உடன் பொலிந்த நீரவே."],"p":"","r":"The stacks rise as if crossing the heads of the raining clouds, pressing on the clean ground, and the long threshing floors, strewn with bursting jackfruit full of rich segments, are crowded and gleam with stacks.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are compressed: it is not clear whether it is the stacks or the clouds that press on the ground and roar (நரல), and the reading given is one way of construing them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 58","lb":"CC58","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:59":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":59,"t":["ஈடு சால் போர் பழித்து எருமைப் போத்தினால்","மாடு உறத் தெளித்து வை களைந்து கால் உறீஇச்","சேடு உறக் கூப்பிய செந்நெல் குப்பைகள்","கோடு உயர் கொழும் பொனின் குன்றம் ஒத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Having trodden out the great stacks with buffalo bulls, winnowed the grain aside, cleared the straw and let the wind take the chaff, they pile the red paddy in heaps that look like high-peaked hills of rich gold.","n":"The verse walks through the stages of threshing in order, and only at the end turns grain into gold: the plenty is earned by a sequence of work before it becomes an image of wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 59","lb":"CC59","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:60":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":60,"t":["கரும்பு கண் உடைப்பவர் ஆலை தோறெலாம்","விரும்பி வந்து அடைந்தவர் பருகி விஞ்சிய","திருந்து சாறு அடுவுழிப் பிறந்த தீம் புகை","பரந்து விண் புகுதலின் பருதி சேந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"At every press where cane is crushed, those who come eagerly drink the juice, and the sweet smoke from boiling what is left over rises into the sky until the sun turns red.","n":"The juice is drunk by whoever arrives (விரும்பி வந்து அடைந்தவர்), with no word of payment or of a giver: the land's surplus is open to all before it is boiled down.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 60","lb":"CC60","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:61":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":61,"t":["கிணை நிலைப் பொருநர் தம் செல்லல் கீழ்ப் படப்","பணை நிலையாய் செந்நெல் பகரும் பண்டியும்","கணை நிலைக் கரும்பினில் கவரும் பண்டியும்","மண நிலை மலர் பெய்து மறுகும் பண்டியும்."],"p":"","r":"So that the distress of the kinai-drum bards is brought low, there are carts carrying red paddy from the granaries, carts loaded with sugarcane, and carts carrying flowers for weddings through the streets.","n":"The first line takes up the Sangam figure of the hungry bard whose want a patron ends, but no patron is named: here it is the traffic of the land's carts that puts the bards' distress down.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kinai","eegai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 61","lb":"CC61","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:62":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":62,"t":["மல்லல் அம் தெங்கு இள நீர் பெய் பண்டியும்","மெல் இலைப் பண்டியும் கமுகின் மேதகு","பல் பழுக்காய்க் குலை பெய்த பண்டியும்","ஒல்கு தீம் பண்டம் பெய்து ஒழுகும் பண்டியும்."],"p":"","r":"There are carts of tender coconuts from the rich coconut palms, carts of soft betel leaves, carts of clusters of ripe areca nuts, and carts that roll along laden with sweet goods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 62","lb":"CC62","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:63":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":63,"t":["கருங் கடல் வளம் தரக் கரையும் பண்டியும்","நெருங்குபு முதிரையின் நிறைந்த பண்டியும்","பெருங் கலிப் பண்டிகள் பிறவும் செற்றுபு","திருந்தி எத் திசைகளும் செறிந்த என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"There are carts that come groaning with the wealth of the dark sea, carts packed full of pulses and many other noisy carts, and they are said to crowd every direction in good order.","n":"என்பவே (they say) marks the catalogue of carts (61 to 63) as report, keeping the poet's claim of a land beyond telling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 63","lb":"CC63","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:64":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":64,"t":["கிளி வளர் பூ மருது அணிந்து கேடு இலா","வள வயல் வைகலும் இன்னது என்ப தேன்","துளியொடு மதுத் துளி அறாத சோலை சூழ்","ஒளி அமை இருக்கை ஊர் உரைக்க நின்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The rich fields, adorned with marutham trees where parrots grow and never touched by ruin, are said to be like this every day, and the bright settlements ringed by groves that never stop dripping honey stand beyond description.","n":"கேடு இலா (without ruin) and வைகலும் (every day) make the plenty permanent, not seasonal: the closing verse of the field description claims a land with no bad year.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 64","lb":"CC64","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:65":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":65,"t":["சேவல் அன்னம் தாமரையின் தோடு அவிழ்ந்த செவ்விப் பூக்","காவில் கூடு எடுக்கிய கவ்விக் கொண்டு இருந்தன","தாவில் பொன் விளக்கமாத் தண் குயில் முழவமாத்","தூவி மஞ்ஞை நன் மணம் புகுத்தும் தும்பிக் கொம்பரோ."],"p":"","r":"In the groves the swan-cocks carry off the fresh petals opened from the lotus to build their nests, as if at a wedding where flowers were the flawless golden lamps, the cool koel the drum, and the feathered peacock and the bees on the branches led in the marriage.","n":"The grove stages a wedding among birds, each creature given a role in the rite; the line division of 3 and 4 makes it uncertain which creature plays which part beyond the lamp and the drum.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 65","lb":"CC65","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:66":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":66,"t":["கூடினார் கண் அம்மலர்க் குவளை அம் குழி இடை","வாடு வள்ளை மேல் எலாம் வாளை ஏறப் பாய்வன","பாடு சால் கயிற்றில் பாய்ந்து பல் கலன் ஒலிப்பப் போந்து","ஆடு கூத்தி ஆடல் போன்ற நாரை காண்ப ஒத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"In the pond, among blue lilies like the eyes of lovers met together, vaalai fish leap over the withered vallai creepers, and the heron watching them looks like one watching a dancing woman leap along a rope with her many ornaments ringing.","n":"The simile turns the heron, usually a hunter of fish, into a spectator at a rope-dance; the fish are safe for the length of the image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 66","lb":"CC66","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:67":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":67,"t":["காவி அன்ன கண்ணினார் கயம் தலைக் குடைதலின்","ஆவி அன்ன பூந்துகில் அணிந்த அல்குல் பல் கலை","கோவை அற்று உதிர்ந்தன கொள்ளும் நீரர் இன்மையின்","வாவி யாவும் பொன் அணிந்து வானம் பூத்தது ஒத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"When women with eyes like blue lilies plunge into the pools, the strings of their many-stranded girdles, worn over cloth as fine as breath, break and scatter, and since no one cares to gather them, every pool wears gold and looks like the sky in bloom with stars.","n":"கொள்ளும் நீரர் இன்மையின் (because there is none who would take them) is the point: the gold lies because no one in the land wants it, not because anyone guards it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 67","lb":"CC67","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:68":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":68,"t":["பாசவல் இடிப்பவர் உலக்கை வாழைப் பல் பழம்","ஆசினி வருக்கை மா தடிந்து தேம் கனி உதிர்த்து","ஊசல் ஆடும் பைங் கமுகு தெங்கின் ஒண் பழம் பரீஇ","வாசத் தாழை சண்பகத்தின் வான் மலர்கள் நக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"The pestles of those pounding green rice flakes, as they swing, knock down plantains, jackfruits and mangoes and their sweet fruit, shake down the fruit of the swaying areca and coconut, and the fragrant screwpine and champak open their bright flowers as if laughing.","n":"A hyperbole of crowding: the household's work and the orchard's fruit are so close that the pestle's swing harvests the trees.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 68","lb":"CC68","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:69":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":69,"t":["மன்றல் நாறு இலஞ்சி மேய்ந்து மா முலை சுரந்த பால்","நின்ற தாரையால் நிலம் நனைப்ப ஏகி நீள் மனைக்","கன்று அருத்தி மங்கையர் கலம் நிறை பொழிதர","நின்ற மேதியால் பொலிந்த நீர மாட மாலையே."],"p":"","r":"Buffaloes that have grazed the fragrant ponds come to the long houses with milk streaming from their great udders and wetting the ground, feed their calves, and pour out milk until the women's pots are full, and with them the rows of mansions are bright.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 69","lb":"CC69","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:70":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":70,"t":["வெள்ளிப் போழ் விலங்க வைத்து அனைய வாய் மணித் தலை","கொள் பவளம் கோத்த அனைய கால குன்றிச் செங்கண","ஒள் அகில் புகை திரண்டது ஒக்கும் மா மணிப் புறாக்","கிள்ளையோடு பால் உணும் கேடு இல் பூவை பாடவே."],"p":"","r":"Doves with beaks like split silver, red feet like threaded coral, eyes red as kunri seeds and bodies like gathered agil smoke drink milk together with the parrots, while the unfailing myna sings.","n":"The milk of 69 is drunk by the house birds: the surplus feeds even the pets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 70","lb":"CC70","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:71":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":71,"t":["காடி உண்ட பூந் துகில் கழும ஊட்டும் பூம் புகை","மாட மாலை மேல் நலார் மணிக் குழலின் மூழ்கலின்","கோடு உயர்ந்த குன்றின் மேல் குழீஇய மஞ்ஞைதம் சிறகு","ஆடும் மஞ்சினுள் விரித்து இருந்த வண்ணம் அன்னரே."],"p":"","r":"The flower-scented smoke that perfumes the starched cloth sinks into the jewelled tresses of the fair women on the rows of mansions, and they look like peacocks gathered on a high-peaked hill, spreading their wings in the mist.","n":"The mountain mist of the old simile is made here from the household's own incense: the women are peacocks on a hill whose mist is perfume smoke.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 71","lb":"CC71","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:72":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":72,"t":["கண் உளார் நும் காதலர் ஒழிக காமம் ஈங்கு என","உள் நிலாய வேட்கையால் ஊடினாரை ஆடவர்","வண்ண மேகலைகளைப் பற்ற அற்று உதிர்ந்தன","எண் இல் பொன் சுடு நெருப்பு உக்க முற்றம் ஒத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Women sulking from the longing within them say to the men, 'Your loves are in your eyes; let desire end here,' and when the men seize their bright girdles the strings break and scatter, so that the courtyard looks as if fire that smelts countless gold had spilled there.","n":"The sulk ends as a picture of spilled gold: the lovers' quarrel is told as part of the land's wealth, not as a private crisis. Whose eyes hold the rivals, and whether the line plays on the woman's own reflection in the man's eye, the verse does not settle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 72","lb":"CC72","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:73":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":73,"t":["கோட்டு இளந் தகர்களும் கொய் மலர தோன்றி போல்","சூட்டு உடைய சேவலும் தோணிக் கோழி ஆதியா","வேட்டவற்றின் ஊறு உளார் வெருளி மாந்தர் போர்க் கொளீஇக்","காட்டி ஆர்க்கும் கௌவையும் கடியும் கௌவை கௌவையே."],"p":"","r":"Young horned rams, cocks with combs like plucked thonri flowers, water-fowl and the rest are set to fight by men who delight in such contests, and the uproar of their display and shouting, with the din of the festivals, is the only din the land knows.","n":"The animal fights are offered as part of the land's pleasures, with no word against them, in a work held to be Jain where harm to living beings is the gravest wrong; the verse makes no claim either way. The sense of வேட்டவற்றின் ஊறு உளார் and வெருளி and the construction of line 4 are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 73","lb":"CC73","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:74":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":74,"t":["இறு நுசுப்பின் அம் நலார் ஏந்து வள்ளத்து ஏந்திய","நறவம் கொப்புளித்தலின் நாகு புன்னை பூத்தன","சிறகர் வண்டு செவ்வழி பாட மாடத்து ஊடு எலாம்","இறை கொள் வானின் மீன் என அரம்பை முலையின் இருந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"When slender-waisted women spray from their mouths the toddy raised in cups, the young punnai trees burst into flower, winged bees sing the sevvazhi tune, and throughout the mansions they dwell like the stars of the sky at the eaves.","n":"The verse uses the convention that a tree flowers when a woman sprays it with wine from her mouth. Line 4 (அரம்பை முலையின் இருந்தவே) is obscure: who dwells like stars, and whether அரம்பை is the plantain or the celestial dancer, is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 74","lb":"CC74","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:75":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":75,"t":["விலக்கு இல் சாலை யாவர்க்கும் வெப்பின் முப் பழச் சுனைத்","தலைத் தணீர் மலர் அணிந்து சந்தனம் செய் பந்தரும்","கொலைத் தலைய வேல் கணார் கூத்தும் அன்றி ஐம் பொறி","நிலத் தலைய துப்பு எலாம் நிறை துளும்பும் ஊர்களே."],"p":"","r":"The towns brim with halls barred to no one, cool water from springs of the three fruits against the heat, flower-decked pavilions of sandal paste, the dances of women with killing spear-like eyes, and besides these every delight on earth of the five senses.","n":"Open charity (விலக்கு இல் சாலை யாவர்க்கும்) and the pleasures of the five senses (ஐம் பொறி) stand in one list; the Jain epic praises the land for both without ranking them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","eegai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 75","lb":"CC75","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:76":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":76,"t":["அடிசில் வைகல் ஆயிரம் அறப் புறமும் ஆயிரம்","கொடி அனார் செய் கோலமும் வைகல் தோறும் ஆயிரம்","மடிவு இல் கம்மியர்களோடு மங்கலமும் ஆயிரம்","ஒடிவு இலை வேறு ஆயிரம் ஓம்புவாரின் ஓம்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Every day there are a thousand feedings and a thousand works of charity, a thousand adornments made by creeper-like women, a thousand auspicious rites with tireless craftsmen, and a thousand other things kept up by those who keep them.","n":"Charity is counted in the same unit (ஆயிரம்) as adornment and ceremony: giving is a daily quantity of the land's life, kept up like its other works.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 76","lb":"CC76","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:77":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":77,"t":["நல்தவம் செய்வார்க்கு இடம் தவம் செய்வார்க்கும் அஃது இடம்","நல் பொருள் செய்வார்க்கு இடம் பொருள் செய்வார்க்கும் அஃது இடம்","பெற்ற இன்பம் விழைவிப்பான் விண் உவந்து வீழ்ந்து என","மற்ற நாடு வட்டம் ஆக வைகும் மற்ற நாடு அரோ."],"p":"","r":"The land is a place for those who do good penance and for those who do penance, for those who make good wealth and for those who make wealth, as if heaven, to make men long for the joy won there, had gladly fallen to earth, and it rests with the other lands circled about it.","n":"The verse gives the land room for penance and for wealth alike, with no ranking. The contrast of நல்தவம் with தவம் and நல் பொருள் with பொருள் is not explained; one possibility is merit already earned against merit being made, another is good against ordinary pursuits. Heaven is pictured as the joy that penance earns, come down to make men want it, but the verse states no doctrine of how it is won. The repeated மற்ற நாடு of line 4 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","thavam","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 77","lb":"CC77","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:4"},"cc:78":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":78,"t":["கண் வலைக் காமுகர் என்னும் மாபடுத்து","ஒள் நிதித் தசை தழீஇ உடலம் விட்டிடும்","பெண் வலைப் படாதவர் பீடின் ஓங்கிய","அண்ணல் அம் கடிநகர் அமைதி செப்புவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet announces that he will describe the noble, fragrant capital, whose people stand high in worth because they are not caught in the net of women whose eyes trap lustful men like beasts, keep the flesh of their wealth and throw away the body.","n":"The city is praised first for what its men refuse. The hunting figure is exact: the lustful are the game (மா), the eye is the net, wealth is the meat kept (தசை தழீஇ) and the man is the carcass let go (உடலம் விட்டிடும்). The city is not named in these verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 78","lb":"CC78","ev":"","cl":[["women who sell love trap lustful men with their eyes, take their wealth and cast the men off, and worth lies in not being caught","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:79":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":79,"t":["விண்புகு வியன் சினை மெலியப் பூத்தன","சண்பகத்து அணிமலர் குடைந்து தாது உக","வண் சிறைக் குயிலொடு மயில்கண் மாறு கூஉய்க்","கண் சிறைப் படுநிழல் காவு சூழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Groves surround the city, where champak branches reaching the sky bend with blossom, bees burrow in the flowers and scatter pollen, and koels and peacocks call back and forth in a shade that holds the eye.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 79","lb":"CC79","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:80":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":80,"t":["கை புனை சாந்தமும் கடி செய் மாலையும்","மெய் புனை சுண்ணமும் புகையும் மேவிய","நெய்யொடு குங்குமம் நிறைந்த நாணினால்","பொய்கைகள் பூம் படாம் போர்த்த போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The city's ponds, thick with the sandal paste, garlands, body powder, incense, oil and saffron washed off the bathers, look as if covered with a cloth of flowers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 80","lb":"CC80","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:81":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":81,"t":["கடி நலக் கரும்பொடு காய் நெல் கற்றையின்","பிடி நலம் தழீஇ வரும் பெருங் கைக் குஞ்சரம்","அடி நிலை இருப்பு எழு அமைந்த கல் மதில்","புடை நிலை வாரிகள் பொலிந்த சூழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Around the stone wall, set at its foot with iron posts, lie the enclosures where great tuskers come in with their cow elephants, drawn by sugarcane and sheaves of ripe paddy.","n":"வாரி is taken here as the elephant enclosure or stall; if it means the pits or traps where wild elephants are caught, the verse describes their capture rather than their keeping.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 81","lb":"CC81","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:82":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":82,"t":["சல சல மும் மதம் சொரியத் தம் தம்முள்","கொலை மருப்பு இரட்டைகள் குளிப்பப் பாய்ந்து இரு","மலை திளைப்பன என நாகம் ஆன்ற போர்","குலவிய நிலைக்களம் கோலம் ஆர்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"There are arenas fit for elephant fights, where tuskers streaming musth from three places drive their killing tusks into each other like two mountains clashing.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 82","lb":"CC82","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:83":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":83,"t":["முத்து உடை வெண் மருப்பு ஈர்ந்து மொய் கொளப்","பத்தியில் குயிற்றிய மருங்கில் பல்வினைச்","சித்திரக் கிம்புரி வைரம் சேர்த்துநர்","ஒத்துஇயல் இடங்களும் ஒழுங்கு நீண்டவே."],"p":"","r":"Long rows of workshops stand where craftsmen saw the pearl-bearing white tusks and fit them with patterned caps set with diamonds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 83","lb":"CC83","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:84":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":84,"t":["ஓடு தேர்ச் சாரிகை உகு பொன் பூமியும்","ஆடகம் ஆற்றும் தார்ப் புரவி வட்டமும்","கேடக வாள் தொழில் இடமும் கேடு இலாக்","கோடு வெம் சிலைத் தொழில் இடமும் கூடின்றே."],"p":"","r":"Together lie the chariot courses where gold dust flies from the wheels, the circuits for garlanded horses, the grounds for sword and shield and the grounds for the curved bow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 84","lb":"CC84","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:85":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":85,"t":["புடை நகர்த் தொழில் இடம் கடந்து புக்க பின்","இடை நகர்ப் புறம் பணை இயம்பும் ஓசை ஓர்","கடல் உடைந்தது எனக் கலந்தது அக் கடல்","மடை அடைத்து அனையது அம் மாக்கள் ஈட்டமே."],"p":"","r":"Past the working quarters of the outer town, the drums of the inner outskirts sound like a sea that has burst, and the crowd there is like that sea held behind a sluice.","n":"The two halves of the sea simile split sound from mass: the drums are the sea breaking loose, the people the same sea penned in.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 85","lb":"CC85","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:86":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":86,"t":["சிந்துரப் பொடிகளும் செம் பொன் சுண்ணமும்","சந்தன நீரோடு கலந்து தையலார்","பந்தொடு சிவிறியில் சிதறப் பார் மிசை","இந்திர வில் எனக் கிடந்த வீதியே"],"p":"","r":"The streets, where women spray vermilion and gold powder mixed with sandal water from balls and squirts, lie on the ground like a rainbow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 86","lb":"CC86","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:87":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":87,"t":["பாத்தரும் பசும் பொன்னின் மாடத்து உச்சி மேல்","தூத் திரள் மணிக் குடம் நிரைத்துத் தோன்றுவ","பூத்தன வேங்கை மேல் பொலிந்து கார் நினைந்து","ஏத்தரும் மயில் குழாம் இருந்த போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Gem-studded pots set in rows on the tops of golden mansions look like flocks of peacocks perched on flowering vengai trees, longing for the rain clouds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 87","lb":"CC87","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:88":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":88,"t":["நெடுங் கொடி நிழல் மதி நெற்றி தைவர","உடம்பு வேர்த்து இன மழை உரறி நோக்கலின்","நடுங்குபு நல் வரை மாடத்து உச்சியில்","அடங்கி வீழ்ந்து அருவியின் அழுவ போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The shadow of the long banners strokes the moon's brow, and the clouds, sweating and roaring in rage, tremble and drop on the mansion tops, where they run down as falls and seem to weep.","n":"A comic conceit: the clouds are jealous of the banners that touch the moon, and their rain is recast as tears of defeat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 88","lb":"CC88","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:89":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":89,"t":["பொன் சிறு தேர் மிசைப் பைம் பொன் போதகம்","நல் சிறார் ஊர்தலின் நங்கைமார் விரீஇ","உற்றவர் கோழி மேல் எறிந்த ஒண் குழை","மற்று அத் தேர் உருள் கொடா வளமை சான்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Children ride little golden carts topped with golden elephants, and the bright earrings that frightened women fling at the fowl lie so thick that the carts' wheels cannot turn.","n":"Wealth is shown by its waste: gold ear ornaments are thrown like stones and clog the streets.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 89","lb":"CC89","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:90":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":90,"t":["மாலையும் பசும் பொ(ன்)னும் மயங்கி வார் கணைக்","கோல் எயும் குனி சிலை நுதலினாரொடு","வேல் இயல் ஆடவர் விரவி விண்ணவர்","ஆலயம் இது என ஐயம் செய்யுமே."],"p":"","r":"Garlands and gold mingle where women with brows bent like bows mix with spear-bearing men, so that one wonders whether this is the dwelling of the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 90","lb":"CC90","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:91":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":91,"t":["கல் சுணம் செய்த தோள் மைந்தர் காதலால்","நல் சுணப் பட்டு உடை பற்ற நாணினால்","பொன் சுணத்தால் விளக்கு அவிப்பப் பொங்கிய","பொன் சுணம் புறம்பணை தவழும் பொற்பிற்றே."],"p":"","r":"When the strong-shouldered young men in their desire seize the fine silk garment, the women in shame put out the lamp with a throw of gold powder, and the powder rises and drifts out over the outskirts.","n":"The verse turns a bedroom moment into a measure of the city's wealth: so many lamps are put out with gold dust that it spills beyond the walls. The play on சுணம் (powder, also the fine weave of the silk) runs through every line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 91","lb":"CC91","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:92":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":92,"t":["நலத்தகு நானம் நின்று இடிக்கும் நல்லவர்","உலக்கையால் உதிர்ந்தன தெங்கின் ஒண் பழம்","நிலத்து அவை சொரிதலின் வெரீஇய மஞ்ஞை போய்க்","கலத்து உயர் கூம்பின் மேல் ஆடும் கௌவைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Coconuts knocked down by the pestles of women pounding musk fall to the ground, and the startled peacocks fly off to dance on the tall masts of ships.","n":"The masts place the city beside a harbour or navigable water, which the verse gives only in passing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 92","lb":"CC92","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:93":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":93,"t":["இட்ட எள் நிலம்படா வகையில் ஈண்டிய","முட்டு இலா மூவறு பாடை மாக்களால்","புள் பயில் பழு மரம் பொலிவிற்று ஆகிய","மட்டு இலா வள நகர் வண்ணம் இன்னதே."],"p":"","r":"So crowded is the rich city with people of the eighteen tongues that a sesame seed let fall would not reach the ground, and it looks like a fruit tree full of birds.","n":"மூவறு பாடை (thrice six languages) is the conventional count of the world's speeches; the claim is that every people trades or lives here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 93","lb":"CC93","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:94":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":94,"t":["தங்கு ஒளி நித்திலத் தாமம் சூடிய","வெம் களி இள முலை வேல் கண் மாதரார்","பைங்கிளி முன்கை மேல் கொண்டு பார்ப்பு எனும்","கொங்கு அலர் தாமரைக் கிடங்கு கூறுவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet announces that he will describe the moat, full of open lotuses, which the women with pearl strings and spear-like eyes call the nest of the green parrots they carry on their wrists.","n":"The lines are hard to construe: the parrots' young (பார்ப்பு) may be said to be the lotuses, or the women may tell the parrots the moat is their home; the verse in any case moves from the women of the streets to the moat.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aran","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 94","lb":"CC94","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:95":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":95,"t":["கோள் சுறா இனத்தொடு முதலைக் குப்பைகள்","ஆள் பெறா திரிதர அஞ்சிப் பாய்வன","மோட்டு இறா பனிக் கிடங்கு உழக்க மொய்த்து எழுந்து","ஈட்டறாப் புள் இனம் இரற்றும் என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"In the moat, schools of seizing sharks and heaps of crocodiles roam without catching any man, and when the big prawns that leap in fear churn the cold water, flocks of birds rise and cry.","n":"The predators find no prey: no one falls into the moat, which is the moat's success as a defence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 95","lb":"CC95","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:96":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":96,"t":["சிறை அனப் பெடையினோடு ஊடிச் சேவல் போய்","அறு பத வண்டு இனம் ஆர்ப்பத் தாமரை","உறைவது குழுவின் நீங்கி யோகொடு","கறை அற முயல்வது ஓர் கடவுள் ஒத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"A drake swan that has quarrelled with its mate leaves the flock and settles alone in a lotus while the bees hum, like an ascetic who withdraws from company and strives in yoga to be rid of stain.","n":"The simile sets a lover's sulk beside an ascetic's withdrawal, and the joke cuts both ways. கறை அற (to be free of stain) fits the Jain picture of the soul cleansed of karmic matter, but the verse names no doctrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","thavam","yogam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 96","lb":"CC96","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:97":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":97,"t":["அரும் பொனும் வெள்ளியும் மணியும் அல்லது","கருங் கலம் தோய்விலாக் காமர் பூந் துறை","குரும்பை மென் முலையின் மேல் குலாய குங்குமம்","விருந்து செய்திட வெறி மேனி சேந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"At the lovely bathing ghats of the moat no black vessel is ever dipped, only gold, silver and gems, and the saffron washed from the women's young breasts feasts the water till it turns red.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 97","lb":"CC97","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:98":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":98,"t":["பட்டவர்த் தப்பலின் பரவை ஏந்து அல்குல்","அட்டு ஒளி அரத்தம்வாய்க் கணிகை அல்லது","மட்டு உடை மண மகள் மலர்ந்த போதினால்","கட்டு உடைக் காவலின் காமர் கன்னியே."],"p":"","r":"The moat is a courtesan in that it destroys those who fall into it, with its wide expanse for hips and red water for a mouth; but with flowers opening on it like a bride, it is otherwise a lovely maiden held under strict guard.","n":"The courtesan of verse 78, whom the citizens refuse, returns as the image of the moat's threat to an enemy; the guarded maiden gives its inaccessibility. The syntax of lines 1 and 3 is compressed and the split between the two comparisons is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aran","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 98","lb":"CC98","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:99":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":99,"t":["நிரை கதிர் நித்திலம் கோத்து வைத்த போல்","விரை கமழ் கமுகின் மேல் விரிந்த பாளையும்","குரை மதுக் குவளைகள் கிடங்கில் பூத்தவும்","உரையின் ஓர் ஓசனை உலாவி நாறுமே."],"p":"","r":"The spathes of the fragrant areca, opening like strings of pearls, and the honeyed blue lilies flowering in the moat spread their scent for a yojana, it is said.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 99","lb":"CC99","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:100":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":100,"t":["தாய் முலை தழுவிய குழவி போலவும்","மா மலை தழுவிய மஞ்சு போலவும்","ஆய் முகில் தழீஇ அசும்பு அறாத நெற்றிய","சேய் உயர் மதில் வகை செப்புகின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The poet turns to the high wall, whose top, forever wet, is clasped by clouds as a baby clings to its mother's breast and as mist hugs a great hill.","n":"The two similes of embrace make the wall's height tender rather than menacing, just before the catalogue of its weapons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 100","lb":"CC100","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:101":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":101,"t":["மாற்றவர் மறப் படை மலைந்து மதில் பற்றின்","நூற்றுவரைக் கொல்லியொடு நூக்கி எறி பொறியும்","தோற்றம் உறு பேய் களிறு துற்று பெரும் பாம்பும்","கூற்றம் அன கழுகு தொடர் குந்தமொடு கோண்மா"],"p":"","r":"If enemy armies storm the wall, it has engines that hurl the hundred-killer, fearsome demon-elephants, great snakes that swallow, death-like vultures, lances and seizing beasts, the list continuing into the next verse.","n":"These are machines named after what they do, not living creatures: நூற்றுவரைக் கொல்லி renders the weapon that kills a hundred at once. Line 4 has no finite verb; the catalogue runs on into verse 102, outside this chapter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 101","lb":"CC101","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:5"},"cc:102":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":102,"t":["வில் பொறிகள் வெய்ய விடு குதிரை தொடர் அயில் வாள்","கல் பொறிகள் பாவை அனம் மாடம் அடு செந் தீக்","கொல் புனை செய் கொள்ளி பெருங் கொக்கு எழில் செய் கூகை","நல் தலைகள் திருக்கும் வலி நெருக்கும் மர நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"The narrator lists the engines mounted on the city wall: bow-machines, released horses, chained spears and swords, stone-throwers, figures, swans, fire-pots, killing brands, great cranes and owls, and devices that twist off heads and crush.","n":"Many of the names are of devices whose working the verse does not explain; the list is read item by item and some items (பாவை, அனம், கூகை) may be shapes given to engines rather than separate machines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 102","lb":"CC102","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:103":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":103,"t":["செம்பு உருகு வெம் களிகள் உமிழ்வ திரிந்து எங்கும்","வெம்பு உருகு வட்டு உமிழ்வ வெந் நெய் முகத்து உமிழ்வ","அம்பு உமிழ்வ வேல் உமிழ்வ கல் உமிழ்வ ஆகித்","தம் புலங்களால் யவனர் தாள் படுத்த பொறியே."],"p":"","r":"The engines, set in place by the Yavanas with their own skills, turn every way and spit molten copper, red-hot balls, boiling ghee, arrows, spears and stones.","n":"The defence is credited to foreign craftsmen and to machines, not to the city's own fighting men; the wall is shown as self-acting, which is worth holding against how the city is later lost from inside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 103","lb":"CC103","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["யவனர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:104":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":104,"t":["கரும் பொன் இயல் பன்றி கத நாகம் விடு சகடம்","குரங்கு பொரு தகரினொடு கூர்ந்து அரிவ நுண்நூல்","பரந்த பசும் பொன் கொடி பதாகையொடு கொழிக்கும்","திருந்து மதி தெவ்வர் தலை பனிப்பத் திருந்தின்றே"],"p":"","r":"Iron boars, carts that let loose angry snakes, fighting monkeys and rams and cutters of fine thread stand with gold flags and banners, set so that enemies' heads tremble.","n":"The verse ends the wall-catalogue on the enemy's fear (தெவ்வர் தலை பனிப்ப): the engines are meant to deter as much as to kill.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 104","lb":"CC104","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:105":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":105,"t":["வயிர வரை கண் விழிப்ப போன்று மழை உகளும்","வயிர மணித் தாழ்க் கதவு வாயில் முகம் ஆக","வயிரம் அணி ஞாயில் முலை வான் பொன் கொடிக் கூந்தல்","வயிரக் கிடங்கு ஆடை மதில் கன்னியது கவினே"],"p":"","r":"The wall is pictured as a maiden: its diamond-bolted gate is her face, its bastions her breasts, its gold banners her hair and its moat her garment, rising like a mountain that opens its eyes among the clouds.","n":"கன்னி makes the conceit pointed: the wall is an unwed girl, a fortress never taken, and the comparison praises beauty and impregnability in one figure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 105","lb":"CC105","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:106":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":106,"t":["செம் பொன் மழை போன்று அடிதொறு ஆயிரங்கள் சிந்திப்","பைம் பொன் விளை தீவில் நிதி தடிந்து பலர்க்கு ஆர்த்தி","அம் பொன் நிலத்து ஏகு குடி அக நகரம் அது தான்","உம்பர் உலகு ஒப்பது அதன் தன்மை சிறிது உரைப்பாம்"],"p":"","r":"The narrator turns to the inner city, whose families scatter gold like rain at every step, bring treasure from the gold-yielding islands and feed many, and says it is like the world of the gods before telling a little of it.","n":"The wealth is overseas trade wealth (பைம் பொன் விளை தீவில் நிதி), and its use is given at once: பலர்க்கு ஆர்த்தி, filling many. Praise of riches is tied to sharing in the same line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 106","lb":"CC106","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:107":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":107,"t":["துப்பு உறழ் தொண்டைச் செவ்வாய்த் தோழியர் காமத் தூதின்","ஒப்ப ஒன்று ஆதி ஆக ஆயிரத்தோர் எட்டு ஈறாச்","செப்பித் தம் செம்பொன் அல்குல் நலம் வரைவின்றி விற்கும்","உப்பு அமை காமத் துப்பின் அவர் இடம் உரைத்தும் அன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The narrator announces the quarter of the courtesans, whose coral-lipped maids carry love's messages and name a price from one up to a thousand and eight, and who sell their beauty without limit.","n":"வரைவின்றி, 'without limit', is the same word that names the Kural's chapter on such women (வரைவின் மகளிர்). The epic uses it in a passage of praise. The sense of உப்பு அமை காமத் துப்பின் (love's relish, seasoned with salt) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 107","lb":"CC107","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கணிகையர்","தோழியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:108":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":108,"t":["குங்குமம் மெழுகிச் சார்பும் திண்ணையும் குயிற்றி உள்ளால்","தங்கும் மென் சாந்தத்தோடு தாமமும் தாழ நாற்றி","எங்கும் நல் சுவர்கள் தோறும் நாடகம் எழுதி ஏற்பப்","பொங்கு மென் மலர் பெய் சேக்கை பொலிந்து விண் புகற்சி உண்டே"],"p":"","r":"Their houses have floors smeared with saffron, inlaid platforms and seats, hanging sandal-paste and garlands, plays painted on every wall and beds strewn with soft flowers, a delight like heaven's.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 108","lb":"CC108","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:109":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":109,"t":["தூசு சூழ் பரவை அல்குல் சுமக்கலாது என்ன வீழ்த்த","காசு சூழ் கோவை முத்தம் கதிர் முலை திமிர்ந்த சாந்தம்","வாச நல் பொடிகள் மாலை வண்டு உண வீழ்ந்த முற்றம்","ஆசைப் பட்டு அரசு வைக அருங் கடி கமழும் அன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Their courtyards are strewn with pearl girdles dropped because the hips could not bear them, sandal rubbed from breasts, scented powders and garlands for the bees, and their fragrance holds kings who linger there in desire.","n":"ஆசைப் பட்டு அரசு வைக is the one note in the catalogue that points beyond ornament: kings stay because they desire. The book's own king will soon be warned that a king given to women's pleasure earns sin and blame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","varaivinmagalir","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 109","lb":"CC109","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:110":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":110,"t":["அம் சிலம்பு ஒலியோடு அல்குல் கலை ஒலி அணிந்த முன்கைப்","பஞ்சி மெல் விரலில் பாணி பண் ஒலி பவழச் செவ்வாய்","அஞ்சி நேர்ந்து உயிர்க்கும் தேன் சேர் குழல் ஒலி முழவின் ஓசை","துஞ்சல் இல் ஓசை தம்மால் துறக்கமும் நிகர்க்க லாதே"],"p":"","r":"The sounds of anklets, girdles, hand-beats keeping time to song, flutes, and drums that never sleep make the quarter something heaven cannot equal.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 110","lb":"CC110","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:111":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":111,"t":["தேன் உலாம் மதுச் செய் கோதை தேம் புகை கமழ ஊட்ட","வான் உலாம் சுடர்கண் மூடி மா நகர் இரவு செய்யப்","பால் நிலாச் சொரிந்து நல்லார் அணிகலம் பகலைச் செய்ய","வேனிலான் விழைந்த சேரி மேல் உலகு அனையது ஒன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Incense from honeyed garlands veils the stars and makes night in the great city while the women's jewels pour moonlight and make day; the street that the god of spring desires is like the upper world.","n":"வேனிலான் is Kama, the god of the spring season; the quarter is his chosen street, and the verse ends by calling it heaven's equal, the third such comparison in five verses (106, 110, 111).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 111","lb":"CC111","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:112":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":112,"t":["இட்ட நூல் வழாமை ஓடி யோசனை எல்லை நீண்டு","மட்டுவார் மாலை வேய்ந்து சதுக்கங்கள் மலிந்த சும்மைப்","பட்டமும் பசும் பொன் பூணும் பரந்து ஒளி நிழற்றும் தீம் தேன்","அட்டும் தார் அணிந்த மார்பர் ஆவணம் விளக்கல் உற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says he will now describe the market, laid out by the rule-book without fault, a yojana long, its squares loud with crowds and bright with silks, gold ornaments and men in honeyed garlands.","n":"இட்ட நூல் வழாமை: the town is built by a treatise of town-planning. The narrator speaks in the first person singular here (உற்றேன்) and the plural elsewhere (உரைப்பாம், மொழிவாம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 112","lb":"CC112","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:113":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":113,"t":["மணி புனை செம் பொன் கொட்டை வம்பு அணி முத்த மாலைக்","கணி புனை பவழத் திண் காழ் கம்பலக் கிடுகின் ஊன்றி","அணி நிலம் மெழுகிச் சாந்தின் அகில் புகைத்து அம் பொன் போதில்","திணி நிலம் அணிந்து தேம் கொள் ஐயவி சிதறினாரே."],"p":"","r":"The merchants set up their shops with gold knobs, strings of pearl, coral posts and screens, smear the floor with sandal, burn agil and scatter gold flowers and fragrant white mustard.","n":"ஐயவி, white mustard, is scattered for good fortune: the shop is readied like a shrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 113","lb":"CC113","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["வணிகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:114":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":114,"t":["பொன் சொரி கதவு தாழில் திறந்து பொன் யவனப் பேழை","மின் சொரி மணியும் முத்தும் வயிரமும் குவித்துப் பின்னும்","மன் பெரும் பவழக் குப்பை வால் அணிகலம் செய் குப்பை","நண் பகல் இரவு செய்யும் நன் கலம் கூப்பினாரே."],"p":"","r":"They open the gold-bolted Yavana chests and heap up gems, pearls and diamonds, then piles of coral and of white ornaments, and pile the fine pieces that make night at noon.","n":"நண் பகல் இரவு செய்யும் inverts verse 111, where jewels make day of night; here a heap of jewels is said to make night at midday, perhaps by dark stones. The sense is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 114","lb":"CC114","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["வணிகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:115":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":115,"t":["விழுக் கலம் சொரியச் சிந்தி வீழ்ந்தவை எடுத்துக் கொள்ளா","ஒழுக்கினர் அவர்கள் செல்வம் உரைப்பரிது ஒழிக வேண்டா","பழக் குலைக் கமுகும் தெங்கும் வாழையும் பசும் பொன்னாலும்","எழில் பொலி மணியினாலும் கடை தொறும் இயற்றினாரே."],"p":"","r":"They are men whose conduct is not to pick up the costly ornaments that fall and scatter, their wealth is past telling, and at every shop they set up areca, coconut and plantain trees with fruit made of gold and gems.","n":"The honesty is given as their own ஒழுக்கம் (conduct) amid surfeit, with no guard, punishment or spirit to enforce it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kalavu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 115","lb":"CC115","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["வணிகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:116":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":116,"t":["மூசு தேன் இறாலின் மூச மொய் திரை இயம்பி யாங்கும்","ஓசை என்று உணரின் அல்லால் எழுத்து மெய் உணர்த்தல் ஆகாப்","பூசு சாந்து ஒருவர் பூசிற்று எழுவர் தம் அகலம் பூசி","மா சனம் இடம் பெறாது வண் கடை மலிந்தது அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The crowd hums like bees at the hive or the beating waves, so that only noise is heard and no word can be made out, sandal smeared on one man is rubbed onto seven, and the great throng has no room.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 116","lb":"CC116","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:117":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":117,"t":["மெய்யணி பசும் பொன் சுண்ணம் மேதகு நான நீரின்","ஐது பட்டு ஒழுகி யானை அழிமதம் கலந்து சேறாய்ச்","செய் அணி கலன்கள் சிந்தி மாலையும் மதுவும் மல்கி","வெய்து அடி இடுதற்கு ஆகா வீதிகள் விளம்பல் உற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"Gold powder and perfumed water wash down and mix with the musth of elephants into mire, ornaments, garlands and toddy lie thick, and no one can set a foot down hot; the narrator turns to the streets.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 117","lb":"CC117","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:118":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":118,"t":["முழவு அணி முது நகர் முரசொடு வளை விம","விழவு அணி மகளிர் தம் விரை கமழ் இள முலை","இழை அணி ஒளி இள வெயில் செய விடு புகை","மழை என மறையின பொலிவினது ஒருபால்."],"p":"","r":"On one side the old drum-adorned city sounds with drums and conchs, festival women's young breasts breathe fragrance, their jewels shine like the early sun, and the smoke they release hides all like cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 118","lb":"CC118","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:119":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":119,"t":["குடையொடு குடை பல களிறொடு நெரி தர","உடை கடல் ஒலியினொடு உறுவார் பலி செல","முடியொடு முடியுற மிடைதலின் விடு சுடர்","கொடியுடை மழை மினின் குலவியது ஒரு பால்."],"p":"","r":"On another side umbrellas jostle umbrellas and tuskers press, worshippers go with offerings amid a roar like the sea, and crowns press so close that their light flickers like lightning in a bannered cloud.","n":"பலி, the offering, is the one sign of worship in the street scenes; the kings' crowns and the worshippers share the press.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 119","lb":"CC119","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["உறுவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:120":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":120,"t":["பூத்தலை வாரணப் போர்த் தொழில் இளையவர்","நாத் தலை மடி விளிக் கூத்தொடு குயில் தரக்","காய்த்துறு தமனியத் துகளொடு கடிகமழ்","பூத்துகள் கழுமிய பொலிவினது ஒரு பால்."],"p":"","r":"On another side young men who fight crested cocks call out with folded tongues amid dancing and cuckoo-like song, and the place is thick with gold dust and fragrant pollen.","n":"நாத் தலை மடி விளி is read as a whistling call made with the tongue folded; the reading is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 120","lb":"CC120","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:121":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":121,"t":["மைந்தரோடு ஊடிய மகளிரை இளையவர்","அம் துகில் பற்றலின் காசரிந்து அணி கிளர்","சுந்தர நிலமிசைச் சொரிதலின் மின் அணிந்து","இந்திர திருவிலின் எழிலினது ஒருபால்."],"p":"","r":"On another side young men seize the fine garments of women who are sulking with their lovers, so that girdle beads break and spill on the floor and shine like Indra's bow.","n":"The ஊடல் of love-poetry becomes a street sight: the broken girdle, a sign of the lovers' quarrel ending, is valued for its rainbow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 121","lb":"CC121","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["இளையவர்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:122":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":122,"t":["வளை அறுத்து அனையன வால் அரி அமை பதம்","அளவு அறு நறு நெய்யொடு கறி அமை துவை","விளைவு அமை தயிரொடு மிசை குவிர் விரையுமின்","உள அணி கலம் எனும் உரையினது ஒருபால்."],"p":"","r":"On another side rings out the call: here is rice like cut conch, ghee without measure, curried relish and set curd, eat and hurry, there are fine dishes.","n":"The verse gives only the words of the call (உரை), not who calls; it may be householders feeding comers or sellers of food. The insistence (மிசை குவிர் விரையுமின்) is the hospitality formula, so it is tagged virundhu with that doubt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 122","lb":"CC122","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:123":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":123,"t":["வரை நிரை அருவியின் மதம் மிசை சொரிவன","புரை நிரை களிறொடு புனை மணி இயல் தேர்","விரை நிரை இவுளியொடு இளையவர் விரவுபு","குரை நிரை குளிர் புனல் ஆற்றினது ஒருபால்."],"p":"","r":"On another side the street is like a cold, roaring river, where tuskers shed musth like falls from a mountain range and pour along with jewelled chariots, swift horses and young men mingled.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 123","lb":"CC123","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:124":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":124,"t":["வரி வளை அரவமும் மணி முழவு அரவமும்","அரி வளர் கண்ணியர் அணிகல அரவமும்","புரி வளர் குழலொடு பொலி மலி கவினிய","திரு விழை கடி மனை திறவிதின் மொழிவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says he will tell properly of the fine houses, beautiful with the noise of bangles, of jewelled drums and of the ornaments of women with streaked eyes, along with the flute.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 124","lb":"CC124","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:125":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":125,"t":["பாவை அன்னவர் பந்து புடைத்தலில்","தூவி அன்னம் வெரீஇத் துணை என்று போய்க்","கோவை நித்தில மாடக் குழாம் மிசை","மேவி வெண் மதி தன்னொடு இருக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"When girls like dolls strike their balls, a swan takes fright, flies to the pearl-hung terraces taking the white moon for its mate, and settles beside it.","n":"The catalogue of the house begins with a small error of perception: the bird mistakes the moon for a swan, as the city everywhere is made to rival heaven.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 125","lb":"CC125","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:6"},"cc:126":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":126,"t":["திருவ நீள் நகர்ச் செம் பொனின் நீடிய","உருவ ஒண்கொடி ஊழின் நுடங்குவ","பரவை வெம் கதிர்ச் செல்வன பன்மயிர்ப்","புரவி பொங்கு அழல் ஆற்றுவ போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The red-gold banners that rise from the rich city's tall buildings sway in turn, as if fanning the many-maned horses of the hot-rayed sun crossing the sky and cooling their blazing heat.","n":"A fancy of cause (the banners wave in order to cool the sun's horses), the first of several in this run that make the city's height and wealth touch the heavens.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 126","lb":"CC126","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:127":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":127,"t":["இழை கொள் வெம் முலை ஈரம் உலர்த்துவார்","விழைய ஊட்டிய மேதகு தீம் புகை","குழை கொள் வாண் முகம் சூழ் குளிர் அம் கதிர்","மழையுள் மா மதி போன்ம் எனத் தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"The sweet incense smoke with which women dry the dampness of their jewelled breasts drifts round their earringed bright faces, so that each face looks like the great moon with its cool rays seen within a cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 127","lb":"CC127","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:128":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":128,"t":["செம் பொன் கண்ணி சிறார் களைந்திட்டவும்","அம் பொன் மாலை அவிழ்ந்து உடன் வீழ்ந்தவும்","தம் பொன் மேனி திமிர்ந்த தண் சாந்தமும்","வம்பு உண் கோதையர் மாற்றும் அயல் அரோ"],"p":"","r":"The red-gold chaplets children have thrown off, the gold garlands that have come loose and fallen, and the cool sandal paste rubbed from golden bodies are what the fragrant-garlanded women sweep aside as refuse.","n":"The point is in the last line: in this city gold and sandal are what is swept away, so the refuse itself measures the wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 128","lb":"CC128","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:129":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":129,"t":["வேரிஇன் மெழுக்கு ஆர்ந்த மென் பூ நிலத்து","ஆரி ஆக அம் சாந்தம் தளித்தபின்","வாரி நித்திலம் வைப்ப பொன் பூவொடு","சேரி தோறு இது செல்வத்து இயற்கையே."],"p":"","r":"On the soft floors washed with a scented wash, sandal paste is sprinkled in patterns, then pearls are scattered and golden flowers set down; in every quarter, the poet says, this is simply the way of wealth.","n":"ஆரி in line 2 is a rare word; taking it as a pattern or design of the sprinkling is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 129","lb":"CC129","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:130":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":130,"t":["கருனை வாசமும் கார் இருள் கூந்தலார்","அருமை சான்ற அகில் புகை வாசமும்","செருமிச் சேர்ந்து கண்ணீர் வரத் தேம் பொழில்","உரிமை கொண்டன ஒண்புறவு என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The smell of fried food and the smell of the precious agil smoke from the women's dark hair mix and choke, bringing tears to the eyes, so that the bright doves, it is said, have claimed the honeyed groves instead.","n":"The sense of the doves' move (they leave the smoky houses for the groves outside) rests on reading உரிமை கொண்டன as 'took the groves as their own'; the verse leaves the subject's motion implicit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 130","lb":"CC130","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:131":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":131,"t":["நறையும் நானமும் நாறும் நறும் புகை","விறகின் வெள்ளி அடுப்பின் அம் பொன் கலம்","நிறைய ஆக்கிய நெய் பயில் இன் அமுது","உறையும் மாந்தர் விருந்தொடும் உண்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Using fragrant wood as fuel in silver hearths, the residents cook sweet ghee-rich food to the brim in gold vessels amid the smoke of musk and scent, and eat it together with their guests.","n":"The only line in this catalogue of luxury that names a duty is the last: the food is eaten with guests (விருந்தொடும்). The verse states it as the city's custom, not as a precept.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 131","lb":"CC131","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:132":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":132,"t":["பாளை மென் கமுகின் பழம் மெல் இலை","நீள் வெண் மாடத்து நின்று கொண்டு அம்நலார்","ஆளிய மொய்ம்பர்க்கு அளித்து அணி சண்பகம்","நாள் செய் மாலை நகை முடிப் பெய்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Standing in their tall white mansions, the beautiful women give betel, areca fruit with tender leaf, to their lion-strong men and set the day's fresh champak garlands on bright hair.","n":"Whose hair (நகை முடி) receives the garland, the women's own or the men's, the verse does not settle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 132","lb":"CC132","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:133":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":133,"t":["எழுது வாள் நெடும் கண் இணை அம் நலார்","மெழுகு குங்கும மார்பு இடை வெம் முலை","உழுது கோதையும் சாந்தும் உவந்து அவை","முழுதும் வித்தி விளைப்பர் திளைப்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The women with painted, sword-like long eyes plough the saffron-smeared chests of their men with their warm breasts, sow garlands and sandal there with delight, and reap the joy of love.","n":"The whole verse is a farming figure (உழுது, வித்தி, விளைப்பர): lovemaking as ploughing, sowing and harvest, so even pleasure is described in the language of the city's wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 133","lb":"CC133","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:134":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":134,"t":["குஞ்சி மேல் அனிச்ச மலர் கூட்டு உணும்","அஞ்சில் ஓதியர் அம் மலர்ச் சீறடி","மஞ்சு தோய் மணி மாடத்து மல்கு பூம்","பஞ்சி மேலும் பனிக்கும் பனிக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"The flower-soft little feet of the women with fine dark hair, who wear anicham blossoms, shiver even on the heaped flowering cotton of their cloud-touching jewelled mansions.","n":"The conceit of feet too tender for the softest things is the Kural's (1111, 1120 name anicham for it); here it is no lover's praise of one woman but a trait of a whole class of the city's women. Line 1 (குஞ்சி மேல் அனிச்ச மலர் கூட்டு உணும்) is hard: குஞ்சி is usually a man's hair, and கூட்டு உணும் may mean bees feeding on the blossoms; the reading of that line is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nalam","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 134","lb":"CC134","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:135":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":135,"t":["தூமமே கமழும் துகில் சேக்கை மேல்","காமமே நுகர்வார் தம் காதலால்","யாமமும் பகலும் அறியாமையால்","பூமி மா நகர் பொன் உலகு ஒத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Those who enjoy only love on beds of fine cloth fragrant with smoke cannot tell the night watches from the day, so great is their passion, and the great city on earth has become like the golden world of the gods.","n":"The comparison with heaven is praise, but the line about not knowing night from day is the same absorption the king will show in the verses that follow this run (198-201).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 135","lb":"CC135","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:136":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":136,"t":["அரவு கான்றிட்ட அம்கதிர் மா மணி","உரவு நீர் முத்தும் உள் உறுத்து உள்ளன","இரவல் மாந்தர்க்கும் இன்னவை ஈவது ஓர்","புரவு பூண்டனர் பொன் நகர் மாந்தரே."],"p":"","r":"The people of the golden city keep stores of gems spat out by serpents and pearls from the strong sea, and have taken on as their charge the giving of such things to those who come to beg.","n":"புரவு பூண்டனர் makes giving a duty the people have bound themselves to, not occasional largesse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 136","lb":"CC136","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:137":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":137,"t":["முல்லை அம் குழலார் முலைச் செல்வமும்","மல்லல் மா நகர்ச் செல்வமும் வார் கழல்","செல்வர் செல்வமும் காணிய என்பர் போல்","எல்லியும் இமையார் இமையாததே."],"p":"","r":"The gods do not blink even at night, as though, people say, they wished to watch the breast-wealth of women with jasmine-scented hair, the wealth of the great city and the wealth of its anklet-wearing rich.","n":"A fancy built on the gods' epithet இமையார், 'the unblinking': the poet gives their nature a cause in the city's beauty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 137","lb":"CC137","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:138":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":138,"t":["முழவும் சங்கமும் முன்றில் முழங்குவ","விழவும் வேள்வும் விடுத்தல் ஒன்று இன்மையால்","புகழலாம் படித்து அன்று இது பொன்னகர்","அகழ்தல் மாக் கடல் அன்னது ஓர் சும்மைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Drums and conches sound in the courtyards, and since festivals and weddings never cease the golden city is beyond praise; its noise is like the roar of the great sea being dug.","n":"வேள்வு may mean a wedding or a sacrifice; with விழவு it most likely names the city's ceremonies in general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 138","lb":"CC138","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:139":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":139,"t":["திங்கள் முக்குடையான் திரு மாநகர்","எங்கும் எங்கும் இடம் தொறும் உண்மையால்","அம் கண் மா நகர்க்கு ஆக்கம் அறாதது ஓர்","சங்க நீள் நிதியால் தழைக்கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Because the sacred dwellings of the one with the moon-white triple umbrella stand everywhere, in every place, the wealth of the city never fails and it flourishes as if with the conch treasure.","n":"The triple umbrella (முக்குடை) is the Jina's emblem, so this is the first verse of the run to give the city's wealth a religious cause; the verse does not say the Jina grants it, only that his shrines' presence is why it does not fail. திரு மாநகர் may instead mean that the whole city is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 139","lb":"CC139","ev":"","cl":[["the presence of the Jina's shrines throughout a city keeps its prosperity from failing","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:140":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":140,"t":["தேன்தலைத் துவலை மாலை பைந்துகில் செம் பொன் பூத்து","ஞான்றன வயிர மாலை நகு கதிர் முத்த மாலை","கான்று அமிர்து ஏந்தி நின்ற கற்பகச் சோலை யார்க்கும்","ஈன்று அருள் சுரந்த செல்வத்து இராசமா புரம் அதாமே."],"p":"","r":"Such is Rajamapuram: a city whose wealth, like a grove of wish-granting trees hung with honey-dripping garlands, green cloth, gold blossoms, diamond and pearl strands and ambrosia, pours out its gifts to everyone.","n":"The karpaga grove is the wish-tree of the gods' world; the verse names the city (இராசமா புரம்) only at the close of the long praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 140","lb":"CC140","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:141":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":141,"t":["வேக யானை மீளி வேல் வெய்ய தானை ஐய கோல்","மாகம் நீள் மணிமுடி மாரி வண்கை மாசு இல் சீர்","ஏக ஆணை வெண் குடை இந் நகர்க்கு மன்னவன்","நாக நீர நல் நகர் நன்மை தன்னம் செப்புவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet turns to the city's king, with his swift elephants, fierce spears, hot army, gracious sceptre, sky-high jewelled crown, rain-like giving hand, spotless renown, single command and white umbrella, and promises to tell a little of the goodness of the city.","n":"The portrait is the standard catalogue of a just king; the gracious sceptre (ஐய கோல்) and single command are what the events later in this ilampakam (198-205) put in question.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","sengol","arasu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 141","lb":"CC141","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:142":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":142,"t":["நீள் நிலம் வகுத்து நீர் நிரந்து வந்து இழிதரச்","சேண் நிலத்து இயற்றிய சித்திரச் சுருங்கை சேர்","கோள் நிலத்து வெய்யவாம் கொடும் சுறத் தடம் கிடங்கு","பூண் நிலத்து வைத்தது ஓர் பொற்பினில் பொலிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The broad moat, fed by water led across the land and descending through a decorated tunnel dug far underground, full of sharks fierce in their seizing, shone like an ornament the land had put on.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 142","lb":"CC142","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:143":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":143,"t":["இஞ்சி மாகம் நெஞ்சு போழ்ந்து எல்லை காண ஏகலின்","மஞ்சு சூழ்ந்து கொண்டு அணிந்து மாக நீண்ட நாகமும்","அஞ்சு நின்னை என்றலின் ஆண்டு நின்று நீண்ட தன்","குஞ்சி மாண் கொடிக் கையால் கூவி விட்டது ஒத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The wall rose as if to split the sky's chest and see where it ends, adorned with clouds gathered about it; and since the heaven itself said it feared it, the wall seemed to beckon from there with the banner-hands on its crest.","n":"நாகம் in line 2 may be heaven or the serpent; and who calls whom in lines 3-4 is unclear. The reading here, a fancy in which the wall's banners wave back at the frightened sky, is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aran","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 143","lb":"CC143","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:144":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":144,"t":["முத்து மாலை முப்புரி மூரி மா மணிக் கதவு","ஒத்த நான்கு கோபுரம் ஓங்கி நின்று ஒளிர்வன","சத்தி நெற்றி சூட்டிய தாம நீள் மணிவணன்","தத்து ஒளி மணிமுடி தாமம் நால்வ போலுமே."],"p":"","r":"Four matching gate-towers, hung with triple strands of pearl garlands and with great jewelled doors, rise and shine like the four garlands on the jewelled crown of the gem-coloured god.","n":"The simile uses மணிவணன், the gem-coloured god, a name of Tirumal; a Jain epic borrows a Vaishnava image for ornament without any claim. Line 3 (சத்தி நெற்றி சூட்டிய) is hard and the reading of it is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aran","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 144","lb":"CC144","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:145":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":145,"t":["சங்கு விம்மு நித்திலம் சாந்தொடு ஏந்து பூண் முலைக்","கொங்கு விம்மு கோதை தாழ் கூந்தல் ஏந்து","இங்கிதக் களிப்பினால் எய்தி ஆடும் பூம்","செங் கண் இந்திரன் நகர்ச் செல்வம் என்னது அன்னதே."],"p":"","r":"With women in pearl and sandal-decked breasts and fragrant garlands hanging in their hair coming in amorous joy to dance, the wealth of the city is like the wealth of red-eyed Indra's city; what more is there to say?","n":"Lines 2 and 3 seem a foot or two short of this metre, so a word may be lost; the sense of who dances rests on the reading of those lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 145","lb":"CC145","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:146":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":146,"t":["வெள்ளி யானை மென் பிடி மின் இலங்கு பைம் பொனால்","துள்ளும் மான் ஒருத்தலும் செம் பொன் அம் பொன் மான்பிணை","உள்ளு காமம் உள் சுட வேந்தன் ஆங்கு உறைவது ஓர்","பள்ளி மாட மண்டபம் பசுங்கதிர்ப்ப வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"The king's bed-chamber pavilion, with a silver bull elephant and soft cow elephant, a leaping stag of gleaming green gold and a pair of red-gold does, shines with green light; there the king dwells with desire burning within him.","n":"Every figure in the pavilion is a mated pair, and the line about the king (உள்ளு காமம் உள் சுட) is the first time the run names the king's desire. It is stated, not judged, here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 146","lb":"CC146","ev":"The king lives in his bed-chamber pavilion, burning with desire.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:147":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":147,"t":["கோழ் அரை மணி மடல் கூந்தல் நெற்றி ஏந்திய","மாழையம் திரள் கனி மா மணி மரகதம்","சூழ் குலைப் பசுங்கமுகு சூலு பாளை வெண் பொனால்","ஊழ் திரள் மணிக் கயிறு ஊசல் ஆட விட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"In the garden stand areca palms made of jewels: gem fronds like hair, clusters of round golden fruit of great gems and emerald, swelling spathes of white gold, and a swing of jewelled rope let down to sway.","n":"The trees are artificial, made of metal and gems; the garden is a crafted thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 147","lb":"CC147","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:148":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":148,"t":["மென் தினைப் பிறங்கலும் மிளிர்ந்து வீழ் அருவியும்","குன்று அயல் மணிச் சுனைக் குவளை கண் விழிப்பவும்","நின்று நோக்கு மான் பிணை நீல யானை மன்னவன்","கன்று காமம் வெஃகிய காமர் காம பூமியே."],"p":"","r":"Soft heaps of millet, flashing waterfalls, blue lilies opening their eyes in jewelled pools beside the hill, and pairs of does standing to gaze: this is the lovely pleasure-ground that the burning desire of the king of the dark elephant longed for.","n":"The king's park rebuilds the hill landscape of the old poems of love (millet, falls, pool lilies, deer) as a crafted scene. காம பூமி recurs in 189, where the royal couple are likened to the shining ones of the காம பூமி, the land of enjoyment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vendhan","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 148","lb":"CC148","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:149":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":149,"t":["தீம் குயில் மணந்து தேன் துஞ்ச வண்டு பாண் செய","வேங்கை நின்று பொன் உகுக்கும் வெற்பு உடுத்த சந்தனம்","ஓங்கு பிண்டி சண்பகம் ஊழி நாறு நாகமும்","நீங்க நீங்கும் இன் உயிர் நினைப்பின் நின்று இளஃகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Where sweet koels mate, bees sleep in honey and others sing like bards, vengai trees drop gold, and the hill is wrapped in sandal, tall asoka, champak and naga trees give fragrance through the ages; to leave it would be to lose one's sweet life, and even to think of it makes the heart melt.","n":"The last line turns description into attachment: the garden is a place one cannot part from without dying. The asoka (பிண்டி) is also the tree of the Jina, but nothing in the verse uses that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 149","lb":"CC149","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:7"},"cc:150":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":150,"t":["முத்தம் வாய் புரித்தன மொய் கதிர்ப் பசும் பொனால்","சித்திரத்து இயற்றிய செல்வம் மல்கு பன் மணி","பத்தியில் குயிற்றி வான் பதித்து வைத்த போல்வன","இத்திறத்த பந்து எறிந்து இளையர் ஆடு பூமியே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the grounds of the city where the young play with balls that look like gems set in gold and pearls, as if the sky's stars had been inlaid in rows.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","inbam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 150","lb":"CC150","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:151":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":151,"t":["வைத்த பந்து எடுத்தலும் மாலையுள் கரத்தலும்","கைத்தலத்தின் ஓட்டலும் கண்ணி நெற்றி தீட்டலும்","பத்தியில் புடைத்தலும் பை அரவின் ஆடலும்","இத்திறத்த பந்தினோடு இன்பம் எல்லை இல்லையே."],"p":"","r":"The many ways the ball is played, caught, hidden in a garland, run along the hand, struck in rows, made to dance like a hooded snake, give a pleasure without limit.","n":"The verse is a catalogue of play moves; its last line, that the pleasure has no limit, sets the tone of unbounded delight the city enjoys before its fall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 151","lb":"CC151","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:152":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":152,"t":["கூற்றம் அன்ன கூர் நுதிக் குருதி வான் மருப்பு இடைச்","சீற்றம் உற்ற மன்னர் தம் சென்னி பந்து அடிப்பன","ஊற்று இருந்த மும் மதத்து ஓடை யானை பீடுசால்","காற்று இயல் புரவி தேர் கலந்து கௌவை மல்கின்றே."],"p":"","r":"Elsewhere the noise swells from elephants with three streams of must whose tusks, sharp as Death, have borne the heads of angry kings like balls, mixed with wind-swift horses and chariots.","n":"The ball of the previous two verses returns grimly: the war elephants are said to strike enemy kings' heads as balls, so the city's play and its violence share one image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 152","lb":"CC152","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:153":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":153,"t":["கவ்வை அம் கருவி சூழ்ந்து கண் படுக்கும் மாடமும்","தெவ்வர் தந்த நீள் நிதி செம் பொன் மாடமும்","மவ்வல் அம் குழலினார் மணிக்கலம் பெய் மாடமும்","இவ் வலந்த அல்லவும் இடங்கள் எல்லை இல்லையே."],"p":"","r":"The city has mansions where people sleep amid the sound of instruments, mansions of red gold holding the treasure enemies paid, and mansions where women with jasmine hair keep their jewels, and places beyond these without number.","n":"The tribute of enemies (தெவ்வர் தந்த நீள் நிதி) is counted among the city's wealth: its luxury rests on conquest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 153","lb":"CC153","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:154":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":154,"t":["பூத் கோங்கும் வேங்கையும் பொன் இணர் செய் கொன்றையும்","காய்த்து நின்று கண் தெறூஉம் காமர் வல்லி மாதரார்","கூத்து அறாத பள்ளியும் கொற்றம் அன்ன மங்கையர்","ஏத்தல் சான்ற கோயிலும் இடைப் படுத்து இயன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Between flowering kongu, vengai and golden-clustered konrai trees stand the halls where women's dance never ceases and the temple fit to be praised by women like victory itself.","n":"Line 1 reads பூத் கோங்கும், which looks like a dropped letter (for பூத்த); the sense of flowering is clear. Whether கோயில் here is a shrine or the royal palace is not fixed by this verse; the next verse uses கோயில் for the king's palace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","vazhipaadu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 154","lb":"CC154","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:155":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":155,"t":["கந்து மா மணித்திரள் கடைந்து செம் பொன் நீள் சுவர்ச்","சந்து போழ்ந்து இயற்றிய தட்டு வேய்ந்து வெண் பொனால்","இந்திரன் திரு நகர் உரிமையோடு இவ்வழி","வந்து இருந்த வண்ணமே அண்ணல் கோயில் வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"The prince's palace, with pillars of polished gems, long walls of red gold and a roof laid in white gold, looks as if Indra's own city had come down here with all its rights.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 155","lb":"CC155","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:156":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":156,"t":["ஆடலின் அரவமும் அங்கை கொட்டி நெஞ்சு உணப்","பாடலின் அரவமும் பணை முழவு அரவமும்","கூடு கோலத் தீம் சுவைக் கோல யாழ் அரவமும்","வாடல் இல்ல ஓசையால் வைகல் நாளும் வைகிற்றே."],"p":"","r":"The sounds of dance, of songs that clap and melt the heart, of the drum and of the sweet lute blend so that the city passes every day in a sound that never fades.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 156","lb":"CC156","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:157":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":157,"t":["நச்சு நாகத்தின் ஆர் அழல் சீற்றத்தன்","அச்சம் உற்று அடைந்தார்க்கு அமிர்து அன்னவன்","கச்சு உலாம் முலையார்க்கு அணங்கு ஆகிய","சச்சந்தன் எனும் தாமரைச் செங் கணான்."],"p":"","r":"The king is introduced: Saccandan of the red lotus eyes, fierce in wrath as a poisonous serpent's fire, like nectar to those who come to him in fear, and a wounding goddess to women.","n":"Three faces of one king in three lines: terror to enemies, refuge to the frightened, affliction to women. The third, அணங்கு, is the word the love poems use for the woman whose beauty wounds; here the king is the afflictor, before the story reverses it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arul","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 157","lb":"CC157","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:158":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":158,"t":["வண் கையால் கலி மாற்றி வை வேலினால்","திண் திறல் தெவ்வர் தேர்த் தொகை மாற்றினான்","நுண் கலைக்கு இடனாய்த் திரு மா மகள்","கண்களுக்கு இடன் ஆம் கடி மார்பனே."],"p":"","r":"Saccandan drove out want with his generous hand and the massed chariots of strong foes with his spear; his fragrant chest is the home of subtle learning and the place where Lakshmi's eyes rest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","maram","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 158","lb":"CC158","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:159":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":159,"t":["கோதை நித்திலம் சூழ் குளிர் வெண் குடை","ஓத நீர் உலகு ஒப்ப நிழற்றலால்","தாதையே அவன் தாள் நிழல் தங்கிய","காதலால் களிக்கின்றது இவ் வையமே."],"p":"","r":"His cool white umbrella hung with pearls shades the whole sea-girt world alike, so the earth, resting in the shade of his feet as in a father's, rejoices in love for him.","n":"The umbrella's shade is equal (உலகு ஒப்ப): the praise is of impartial protection, the straight sceptre seen as shade rather than as stick.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","ali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 159","lb":"CC159","ev":"","cl":[["a king whose rule shelters all alike stands to the world as a father, and the world rejoices in that care","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:160":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":160,"t":["தருமன் தண் அளியால் தனது ஈகையால்","வருணன் கூற்று உயிர் மாற்றலின் வாமனே","அருமையால் அழகின் கணை ஐந்து உடைத்","திருமகன் திரு மா நில மன்னனே."],"p":"","r":"The king of the great land is likened to gods one by one: to Dharma in cool grace, to Varuna in giving, to Death in taking life, to Vaman in something rare, and to the five-arrowed son of Tiru in beauty.","n":"The list ends with Kama (திருமகன் with five arrows): beauty of the god of desire is made part of the king's divinity, which the story will turn against him. வாமனே in line 2 is hard to place: it may be Vishnu's dwarf form or a Jain name of the Arhat, and which quality it goes with (அருமையால், rarity) is not clear from the line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","iraivan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 160","lb":"CC160","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:161":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":161,"t":["ஏனை மன்னர் தம் இன் உயிர் செற்ற வேல்","தானை மன்னரில் தான் இமில் ஏறு அனான்","தேனை மாரி அன்னான் திசை காவலன்","வானம் தோய் புகழான் மலிவு எய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Saccandan, whose spear killed other kings and who stands among kings like a humped bull, is like a rain of honey and guards the quarters; his fame touches the sky and he has reached fullness.","n":"மலிவு எய்தினான் closes the praise at a peak; the next verse begins the turn of the story with செல்வற்கு இன்னணம் சேறலில், 'while things went thus for the fortunate one'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 161","lb":"CC161","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:162":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":162,"t":["செல்வற்கு இன்னணம் சேறலில் தீம் புனல்","மல்கு நீர் விதையத்து அரசன் மகள்","அல்லி சேர் அணங்கிற்கு அணங்கு அன்னவள்","வில்லின் நீள் புருவத்து எறி வேல் கணாள்."],"p":"","r":"While the fortunate king's life ran thus, there was the daughter of the king of well-watered Vidaiyam, a goddess even to the goddess on the lotus, with bow-long brows and eyes like thrown spears.","n":"She is introduced by her father's land, not yet by name; the verse calls her an அணங்கு to the goddess Tiru herself, matching the அணங்கு the king was to women in 157. Her name, Vijayai, is given in the patikam (verse 7) and again at 182.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 162","lb":"CC162","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:163":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":163,"t":["உருவும் சாயலும் ஒப்ப உரைப்பதற்கு","அரிய ஆயினும் அவ் வளைத் தோளிகண்","பெருகு காரிகை பேசுவல் பெண் அணங்கு","அரிய தேவரும் ஏத்து அரு நீரளே."],"p":"","r":"Her form and grace are too rare to be matched in words, yet the poet says he will speak of the swelling beauty of the bangled girl, whom even the gods find hard to praise.","n":"The poet's first person (பேசுவல்) opens a head-to-foot description (kesadi) that runs from 164 to 179.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 163","lb":"CC163","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:164":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":164,"t":["எண்ணெயும் நானமும் இவை மூழ்கி இருள் திருக்கிட்டு","ஒண்ணறும் துகில் கிழி பொதிந்து உறை கழித்தன போல்","கண் இருண்டு நெறி மல்கிக் கடைகுழன்ற கருங் குழல்கள்","வண்ணப் போது அருச்சித்து மகிழ்வு ஆனாத் தகையவே."],"p":"","r":"Her dark tresses, steeped in oil and musk, curled at the ends and full of waves, look like darkness itself wrapped in fine cloth and drawn from its sheath, and are worshipped with coloured flowers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 164","lb":"CC164","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:165":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":165,"t":["குழவிக் கோட்டு இளம் பிறையும் குளிர் மதியும் கூடின போல்","அழகுகொள் சிறுநுதலும் அணி வட்ட மதி முகமும்","தொழுதற்கு வரம் கொடுக்கும் தொண்டை வாய்த் தூமுறுவல்","ஒழுகு பொன் கொடி மூக்கும் உருப்பசியை உருக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Her small brow like the young crescent and her round moon of a face, her coral mouth whose pure smile grants boons to those who bow, and her nose like a flowing golden creeper would melt Urvasi herself.","n":"The smile 'grants boons to those who worship it' (தொழுதற்கு வரம் கொடுக்கும்): the description borrows the language of worship for a woman's face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 165","lb":"CC165","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:166":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":166,"t":["வண் சிலையை வனப்பு அழித்து வார்ந்து ஒழுகி நிலம் பெறா","நுண் கருமை கொண்டு ஒசிந்து நுதல் இவர்ந்து போந்து உலாய்க்","கண் கூடா கடை புடைத்துக் கைவல்லான் எழுதிய போல்","பண்பு ஆர்ந்த கொடும் புருவம் பழிச்சு ஆனாப் படியவே."],"p":"","r":"Her curved brows, shaming the beauty of the bow, slender and dark, rising over the brow and bending down to the eye's corner as if a master had painted them, are beyond all praise.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 166","lb":"CC166","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:167":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":167,"t":["சேல் அனைய சில்லரிய கடை சிவந்து கரு மணி அம்","பால் அகத்துப் பதித்து அன்ன படியவாய் முனிவரையும்","மால் உறுப்ப மகிழ் செய்வ மாண்பில் நஞ்சும் அமிர்தமுமே","போல் குணத்த பொரு கயல் கண் செவி உறப் போந்து அகன்றனவே."],"p":"","r":"Her eyes, like fighting carp with fine red lines at the corners and dark pupils set as if inlaid, reach to her ears and are at once poison and nectar, maddening and delighting even sages.","n":"The poison-and-nectar eye echoes the king's own description in 157, poison to foes and nectar to the frightened. That the eyes move even முனிவர் (sages) to infatuation is the poem's first hint that desire can overturn the self-controlled, which the story will show in a king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","anangu","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 167","lb":"CC167","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:168":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":168,"t":["மயிர் எறி கத்தரிகை அனையவாய் வள்ளை வாடு","உயிர் செகுத்து முன் ஒன்றிப் பின் பேராது உரு அமைந்த","செயிர் மகர குண்டலமும் திளைப்பு ஆனா வார் காதும்","வயிரவில் முகம் சூடி வண்ணம் வீற்று இருந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Her long ears, shaped like scissors that cut hair, with makara earrings set in diamond that sway without rest, make the vallai leaf wither and sit in splendid colour.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 (மயிர் எறி கத்தரிகை, உயிர் செகுத்து முன் ஒன்றிப் பின் பேராது) are compressed and the exact shape meant for the ear is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 168","lb":"CC168","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:169":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":169,"t":["ஈனாத இளங் கமுகின் மரகத மணிக் கண்ணும்","ஆனாதே இருள் பருகும் அருமணி கடைந்ததூஉம்","தான் ஆகி இருளொடு ஓர் தாமரைப் பூச் சுமந்து அன்ன","கான் ஆர்ந்த திரள் கழுத்துக் கவின் சிறை கொண்டு இருந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Her round, beautiful neck holds in captivity the emerald of an unbearing young areca, and a rare gem that drinks darkness, like a lotus carried on darkness.","n":"The comparison in lines 2 and 3 is dense; the gem that 'drinks darkness' and the lotus borne on darkness are images of the neck against her dark hair, but the syntax can be divided in more than one way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 169","lb":"CC169","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:170":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":170,"t":["மணி மகரம் வாய் போழ்ந்து வாழ் முத்த வடம் சூழ்ந்து ஆங்கு","அணி அரக்கு ஆர் செம் பஞ்சி அணை அனைய ஆடு அமைத் தோள்","துணிகதிர் வளை முன் கைத் தொகுவிரல் செங் காந்தள்","மணி அரும்பு மலர் அங்கை குலிகம் ஆர் வனப்பினவே."],"p":"","r":"Her shoulders, soft as a cushion of red-lac cotton and smooth as swaying bamboo, wear pearl strings from gem makara mouths; her forearms carry bright bangles, her fingers are like red kanthal buds and her palms glow like vermilion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 170","lb":"CC170","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:171":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":171,"t":["தாமச் செப்பு இணை முகட்டுத் தண் கதிர் விடு நீல","மா மணி தாபித்தன போல் மனம் பருகு கருங் கண்ண","ஏம் உற அடி பரந்து இளம் பிறை வடம் சூடி","ஆம் அணங்கு குடி இருந்து அஞ் சுணங்கு பரந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Her breasts, like a pair of caskets topped with sapphire that sends out cool rays, dark-tipped and drinking the heart, broad-based and wearing a crescent chain, are the dwelling of a goddess and spread with fair pallor spots.","n":"The goddess (அணங்கு) is said to dwell in her breasts: the afflicting power of beauty is placed in the body itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","anangu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 171","lb":"CC171","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:172":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":172,"t":["அங் கை போல் வயிறு அணிந்த வலம் சுழி அமை கொப்பூழ்","கங்கையின் சுழி அலைக்கும் கண் கொளா நுடங்கு இடையை","உண்டு எனத் தமர் மதிப்பர் நோக்கினார் பிறர் எல்லாம்","உண்டு இல்லை என ஐயம் அல்லது ஒன்று உணர்வு அரிதே."],"p":"","r":"Her belly with a navel whirling to the right, deeper than Ganga's eddies, and a waist so slight the eye cannot hold it, such that her own people judge it exists while all others who look are left in doubt whether it is or is not.","n":"The joke on the thin waist is put in the vocabulary of knowing: her own people hold by judgement (மதிப்பர்) that it exists, others are left in doubt between உண்டு and இல்லை and cannot know it. The poet plays with the terms of debate; the verse states no doctrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 172","lb":"CC172","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:173":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":173,"t":["மன்நாக இணைப் படமும் தேர்த் தட்டு மதி மயக்கிப்","பொன் ஆல வட்டமும் போல் கலை இமைக்கும் அகல் அல்குல்","கொன் இளம் பருதியும் குறு முயலின் குருதியும் போன்று","இன் அரத்தப் பட்டசைத்து இந்திரற்கும் புகழ்வு அரிதே."],"p":"","r":"Her broad hips, outshining a great cobra's twin hoods, a chariot's platform and a golden fan, wear a girdle that glitters and a silk red as the young sun or a small hare's blood, beyond even Indra's power to praise.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 173","lb":"CC173","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:8"},"cc:174":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":174,"t":["வேழ வெண் திரள் தடக்கை வெருட்டி மற்று இளங் கன்னி","வாழைத் தண்டு எனத் திரண்டு வால் அரக்கு உண் செம்பஞ்சி","தோழமை கொண்டு என மென்மை உடையவாய் ஒளி திகழ்ந்து","மாழை கொள் மணி மகரம் கௌவி வீற்று இருந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator's head-to-foot portrait of the princess reaches her thighs: they shame an elephant's trunk, are round as a plantain stem, soft as cotton dyed with lac, and wear a jewelled girdle clasp shaped like a makara.","n":"The catalogue begun in 163 is the narrator's, offered before the king sees her; its excess is the point, since the next verses make this description the image the king paints in his heart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 174","lb":"CC174","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:175":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":175,"t":["பக்கத்தால் கவிழிய வாய் மேல் பிறங்காப் பாண்டிலா","ஒக்க நன்கு உணராமை பொருந்திய சந்தினவாய்","நெக்குப் பின் கூடாது நிகர் அமைந்த முழந் தாளும்","மக்களுக்கு இல்லாத மாட்சியின் மலிந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Her knees are praised as smooth, rounded and closely jointed, with a beauty the narrator says no human has.","n":"Line 1 (கவிழிய வாய் மேல் பிறங்காப் பாண்டிலா) is hard to construe; the reading of the knee-cap image is a guess. மக்களுக்கு இல்லாத says outright that the beauty is more than human, preparing the king's taking her for an அணங்கு in 181.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 175","lb":"CC175","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:176":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":176,"t":["ஆடு தசை பிறங்காது வற்றாது மயிர் அகன்று","நீடாது குறுகாது நிகர் அமைந்த அளவினவாய்ச்","சேடு ஆவ நாழிகையின் புடை திரண்டு தேன் நெய் பெய்","வாடாத காம்பே போல் கணைக் காலின் வனப்பினவே."],"p":"","r":"Her calves are neither too fleshy nor too thin, hairless and of just measure, rounded like a smooth stalk.","n":"The beauty praised is one of right proportion (நீடாது குறுகாது நிகர் அமைந்த அளவின): the ideal is measure, not abundance. Line 3 (சேடு ஆவ நாழிகையின்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam","alavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 176","lb":"CC176","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:177":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":177,"t":["பசும் பொன் செய் கிண்கிணியும் பாடகமும் பாடு அலைப்ப","நயந்து எரி பொன் சிலம்பு முத்தரி பெய்து அகம் நக","இயைந்து எழிலார் மணி ஆமை இளம் பார்ப்பின் கூன் புறம் போல்","அசைந்து உணர்வு மடிந்து ஒழியும் அடி இணை புகழ்வார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Her pair of feet, ringing with gold anklets, foot-ornaments and pearl-filled சிலம்பு, are curved like a young tortoise's back, and those who praise them find their senses fail.","n":"The last line turns the praise back on the praiser: feeling sinks (உணர்வு மடிந்து ஒழியும்) in those who praise her feet, a first sign that this beauty disables judgment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 177","lb":"CC177","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:178":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":178,"t":["அரக்கு இயல் செங் கழுநீர் அக இதழ் போல் உகிர் சூடிப்","பரப்பு இன்றி நுதி உயர்ந்து பழிப்பு அறத் திரண்டு நீண்டு","ஒருக்கு உற நெருங்கிப் பொன் ஒளி ஆழி அகம் கௌவித்","திருக் கவின் கொள் மெல் விரல்கள் தேன் ஆர்க்கும் தகையவே."],"p":"","r":"Her toes, with nails like the inner petals of the red water-lily, taper, close together and wear gold rings, so lovely that bees swarm to them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 178","lb":"CC178","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:179":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":179,"t":["என்பொடு நரம்பு இன்றி இலவம் பூ அடர் அனுக்கி","இன்புற வரம்பு உயர்ந்து இரு நிலம் உறப் புல்லி","ஒன்பதின் சாண் நடப்பினும் ஒரு காதம் என்று அஞ்சும்","மென் பஞ்சிச் சீறடியும் மேதக்க விழைவினவே."],"p":"","r":"Her small soles, as soft as pressed silk-cotton blossom and as if without bone or sinew, rest on the earth so tenderly that a walk of nine spans feels to her like a league.","n":"என்பொடு நரம்பு இன்றி denies the body its frame: the epic's praise works by imagining the beloved's body free of the very bone and sinew that the ascetic verses make the whole of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 179","lb":"CC179","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:180":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":180,"t":["இவ் உருவு நெஞ்சு என்னும் கிழியின் மேல் இருந்து இலக்கித்து","அவ் உருவு நினைப்பு என்னும் துகிலிகையால் வருத்தித்துக்","கவ்விய தன் நோக்கினால் கண் விடுத்துக் காதல் நீர்","செவ்விதில் தெளித்து ஆனாக் காமப் பூச் சிதறினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king painted this form on the cloth of his heart with the brush of thought, gave it eyes with his own gaze, sprinkled it with the water of love and strewed it with the endless flowers of desire.","n":"The whole catalogue of 163 to 179 is retold as the king's own act of painting: what the narrator described is now the image desire makes, and the verb சிதறினான் (scattered) closes on excess, not on choice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 180","lb":"CC180","ev":"Sachchandan fixes the princess's image in his heart and gives himself to desire for her.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:181":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":181,"t":["மெய் பெறா எழுத்து உயிர்க்கும் மழலை வாய் இன் முறுவல்","தையலாள் நெடுந் தடங்கண் வலைப்பட்டுச் சச்சந்தன்","ஐயுறான் அணங்கு எனவே அகத்து அடக்கிச் செல்கின்றான்","மொய் அறாக் களியானை முழங்கித் தேன் இமிர் தாரான்."],"p":"","r":"Caught in the net of the long eyes of the girl whose lisping mouth has sounds without full letters, Sachchandan, taking her without doubt for a goddess, keeps her in his heart and goes on his way, the king with the bellowing elephant and the bee-hummed garland.","n":"ஐயுறான் அணங்கு எனவே can be read 'not doubting that she is a goddess' or, with the negative taken loosely, 'wondering whether she is a goddess'; the reading changes whether the king is certain or perplexed. அணங்கு also names a being that afflicts, and the king's later ruin makes the double sense apt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 181","lb":"CC181","ev":"Sachchandan, snared by the princess's eyes, carries away her image without speaking of it.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:182":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":182,"t":["வண்டு இனம் முகபடாம் அணிந்து வார் மதம்","உண்டு உகுத்திடு களிற்று உழவன் தன் மகள்","பெண்டிர் தம் பெரு நலம் கடந்து பெற்ற பேர்","விண்டலர் கோதைக்கு விசயை என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The garlanded girl is the daughter of the lord who drives elephants that wear bee-swarms for face-cloths, and the name she has won by surpassing all women's beauty is Vijayai.","n":"The name is held back until the portrait is done, and glossed by her beauty: விசயை (Vijaya, victory) is a name earned by surpassing other women (பெண்டிர் தம் பெரு நலம் கடந்து).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 182","lb":"CC182","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:183":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":183,"t":["அரு மணி மரகதத்து அங் கண் நாறிய","எரி நிறப் பொன் இதழ் ஏந்து தாமரைத்","திருமகள் இவள் எனத் திலக வெண் குடைப்","பெருமகன் கோயிலுள் பேதை வைகுமே."],"p":"","r":"She dwells in the palace of her father, the lord of the white parasol, as if she were Tirumakal, the goddess of fortune who stands on the golden lotus.","n":"Tirumakal here is praise of a princess; the same goddess will be named as fortune that silently leaves Sachchandan once he is lost in Vijayai (232).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 183","lb":"CC183","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:184":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":184,"t":["கலம் புரி அகல் அல்குல் தாயர் தவ்வையர்","சிலம்புரி திருந்து அடி பரவச் செல்பவள்","வலம்புரி சலஞ்சலம் வளை இயது ஒத்தனள்","குலம் புரிந்து அனையது ஓர் கொடியின் நீர்மையள்."],"p":"","r":"Her mothers and elder sisters bow to her anklet-ringed feet as she walks; she is like a right-whorled conch circled by a lesser conch, and like a creeper that bears the honour of her line.","n":"The image of the வலம்புரி among the சலஞ்சலம் makes her the rare and auspicious one among her kin; குலம் புரிந்து அனையது ஓர் கொடி may mean that her family's whole worth has taken the form of a creeper.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 184","lb":"CC184","ev":"Vijayai's mothers and elder sisters honour her as she walks among them.","cl":[],"ag":["தாயர் தவ்வையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:185":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":185,"t":["இன் அகில் கொழும் புகை உயிர்க்கும் ஈர்ங் குழல்","மென் மலர்க் கோதை தன் முலைகள் வீங்கலின்","மின் உருக்குறும் இடை மெலிய மெல்லவே","கன்னி தன் திரு நலம் கனிந்தது என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"As her breasts swell and her lightning-thin waist grows thinner, the girl's beauty ripens into womanhood.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 185","lb":"CC185","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:186":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":186,"t":["முந்து நாம் கூறிய மூரித் தானை அக்","கந்து கொல் கடாக் களி யானை மன்னவன்","பைந்தொடிப் பாசிழைப் பரவை ஏந்து அல்குல்","தந்தை மாட்டு இசைத்தனன் தனது மாற்றமே."],"p":"","r":"The king of the great army and rutting elephants, already spoken of, sent his word to the father of the girl.","n":"The narrator steps forward (முந்து நாம் கூறிய) to tie the king of 157 to 161 to this suit. The desire of 180 and 181 turns at once into a formal proposal through her father, the proper road of marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 186","lb":"CC186","ev":"Sachchandan sends a proposal of marriage to Vijayai's father.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:187":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":187,"t":["மருமகன் வலந்தது மங்கை ஆக்கமும்","அருமதிச் சூழ்ச்சியின் அமைச்சர் எண்ணிய","கருமமும் கண்டவர் கலத்தல் பான்மையில்","பெருமகன் சேர்த்தினார் பிணை அனாளையே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the son-in-law's strength, the maiden's flourishing and the course the wise ministers had weighed, those who judged that the two were fit to be joined gave the doe-like girl to the great king.","n":"கலத்தல் பான்மையில் may mean only 'in the way fit for joining' or 'by the destiny that they should join'; பான்மை means a destined portion in 215, where Sachchandan's fall is said to sprout from it, so the marriage and the ruin may share one cause. The claim is recorded with that doubt. The match is reasoned as well as fated: the ministers' சூழ்ச்சி is named beside it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","oozh","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 187","lb":"CC187","ev":"Vijayai's elders and the ministers approve the match, and she is given in marriage to Sachchandan.","cl":[["a marriage comes about when the couple's apportioned portion is for joining","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அமைச்சர்","தாயர் தவ்வையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:188":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":188,"t":["பொன் அம் கொடி அமிர்து அனாளும் பொன் நெடுங் குன்று அனானும்","அனங்கனுக்கு இலக்கம் ஆகி அம்பு கொண்டு அழுத்த விள்ளார்","இனம் தமக்கு எங்கும் இல்லார் இயைந்தனர் என்ப முக்கண்","சினம் திகழ் விடையினானும் செல்வியும் சேர்ந்தது ஒத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The golden creeper like nectar and the man like a high gold mountain, wounded by Kama's arrows that would not let them go, were united as no other pair anywhere, as the three-eyed god of the angry bull and his goddess are joined.","n":"A Jain epic compares its royal couple to Siva and his consort as plain praise; within twenty verses a counsellor will cite Siva's sharing his body with the mountain king's daughter as a great blame (208).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 188","lb":"CC188","ev":"Sachchandan and Vijayai are married and united.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:189":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":189,"t":["காதலால் காம பூமிக் கதிர் ஒளி அவரும் ஒத்தார்","மாதரும் களிறு அனானும் மாசுண மகிழ்ச்சி மன்றல்","ஆதரம் பெருகுகின்ற அன்பினால் அன்னம் ஒத்தும்","தீது இலார் திளைப்பின் ஆமான் செல்வமே பெரிதும் ஒத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"In their love they were like the radiant couples of the land of pleasure, in their wedded delight like mating serpents, in swelling tenderness like swans, and in their blameless sporting like the wealth of the wild cow.","n":"காம பூமி is the Jain cosmology's land of enjoyment, where couples are born together and live in pleasure without labour; the comparison sets the couple outside the world of work and rule. Line 4's ஆமான் செல்வம் is obscure, and மாசுணம் may name a large serpent.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 189","lb":"CC189","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:190":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":190,"t":["தன் அமர் காதலானும் தையலும் மணந்த போழ்தில்","பொன் அனாள் அமிர்தம் ஆகப் புகழ் வெய்யோன் பருகியிட்டான்","மின் அவிர் பூணினானை வேல் கணார்க்கு இயற்றப் பட்ட","மன்னிய மதுவின் வாங்கி மாதரும் பருகியிட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"When the loving king and the woman were wed, the glorious king drank her as nectar, and she in turn drank the jewelled king like wine prepared for spear-eyed women.","n":"The verse makes the delight mutual: each drinks the other, he as nectar (அமிர்தம்), she as intoxicating மது. The image of drinking returns in 199, where the drinking goes on while fortune slips.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 190","lb":"CC190","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:191":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":191,"t":["பவழவாய் பரவை அல்குல் என்று இவை பருகும் வேலான்","கவழம் ஆர் களிறு போன்றான் காதலி கரும்பை ஒத்தாள்","தவழ் மதுக் கோதை மாதர் தாமரைப் பூ அது ஆக","உமிழ் நகை வேலினானும் ஒண் சிறை மணி வண்டு ஒத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The spear-bearing king, who drank her coral mouth and broad hips, was like a tusker at its fodder, his beloved like sugarcane; she was a lotus and he a shining-winged bee.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 191","lb":"CC191","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:192":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":192,"t":["பளிக்கு அறைப் பவழப் பாவை பரிசு எனத் திகழும் சாயல்","களிக் கயல் பொருவ போன்று கடை சிவந்து அகன்ற கண்ணாள்","ஒளிக் கவின் கொண்ட காமத்து ஊழுறு கனியை ஒத்தாள்","அளித்து அயில்கின்ற வேந்தன் அம் சிறைப் பறவை ஒத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"She, with the grace of a coral doll in a crystal chamber and wide red-cornered eyes like fighting carp, was like a ripe fruit of love, and the king who tasted it tenderly was like a fair-winged bird.","n":"The eyes as fighting carp (களிக் கயல் பொருவ) are a stock praise; the Naladiyar's renunciant names the same carp as the image he refuses (naladiyar:44).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kan","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 192","lb":"CC192","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:193":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":193,"t":["துறு மலர்ப் பிணையலும் சூட்டும் சுண்ணமும்","நறுமலர்க் கண்ணியும் நாறு சாந்தமும்","அறு நிலத்து அமிர்தமும் அகிலும் நாவியும்","பெறு நிலம் பிணித்திடப் பெரியர் வைகினார்."],"p":"","r":"Amid heaped flower wreaths, crowns, scented powder, garlands, sandal paste, delicacies of every land, aloe smoke and musk, the great pair lived as if bound to the earth that bears them.","n":"பெறு நிலம் பிணித்திட is unclear: it may say the riches of every land were gathered to them, or that pleasure held them fast to the ground.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 193","lb":"CC193","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:194":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":194,"t":["துடித்தலைக் கருங் குழல் சுரும்பு உண் கோதை தன்","அடித்தலைச் சிலம்பினோடு அரவ மேகலை","வடித்தலைக் கண் மலர் வளர்த்த நோக்கமோடு","அடுத்து உலப்பு அரிது அவர் ஊறில் இன்பமே."],"p":"","r":"The joy of their touch, with her bee-sipped garland and dark hair, the anklet on her foot, the ringing girdle and the look of her flowering eyes, was hard to exhaust.","n":"Sound (சிலம்பு, அரவ மேகலை), scent (சுரும்பு உண் கோதை), sight (நோக்கம்) and touch (ஊறு) are gathered in one sentence, with taste in the mouth-drinking of 191 and 196: the senses are all fed from one source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 194","lb":"CC194","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:195":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":195,"t":["இழை கிளர் இள முலை எழுது நுண் இடைத்","தழை வளர் மது மலர் தயங்கு பூஞ்சிகைக்","குழை முகக் கொடியொடு குருதி வேலினான்","மழை முகில் மாரியின் வைகும் என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The king of the blood-red spear lingered with his creeper of a wife, with her jewelled young breasts, thin waist and flower-laden hair, as the monsoon cloud lingers in the rain.","n":"வைகும் (lingers, stays) is the hinge: the cloud that stays is the king who stays, and the next verses (198) say what he leaves unguarded while he does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 195","lb":"CC195","ev":"Sachchandan stays on with Vijayai, given over to pleasure.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:196":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":196,"t":["படுதிரைப் பவழவாய் அமுதம் மாந்தியும்","கொடிவளர் குவி முலைத் தடத்துள் வைகியும்","இடியினும் கொடியினும் மயங்கி யாவதும்","கடி மணக் கிழமை ஓர் கடலின் மிக்கதே."],"p":"","r":"Drinking the nectar of her coral lips, resting at her breasts, losing themselves like thunder and lightning, the intimacy of the new marriage was greater than any sea.","n":"கடி மணக் கிழமை, the closeness of the wedding season, is measured by a sea: boundless, and so without a shore to return from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","kizhamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 196","lb":"CC196","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:197":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":197,"t":["கப்புரப் பசுந்திரை கதிர் செய் மா மணிச்","செப்பொடு சிலதியர் ஏந்தத் தீவிய","துப்பு உமிழ்ந்து அலமரும் காமவல்லியும்","ஒப்பரும் பாவை போன்று உறையும் என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai, the creeper of love, dwells like an incomparable doll, while maids hold up camphor betel in jewelled caskets and she spits out the red of the chewed leaf.","n":"காமவல்லி names her as love's own creeper; the chapter closes on her as a doll (பாவை), still and adorned, the object of the pleasure that will cost the realm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 197","lb":"CC197","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:9"},"cc:198":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":198,"t":["மண் அகம் காவலின் வழுக்கி மன்னவன்","பெண் அரும் கலத்தொடு பிணைந்த பேர் அருள்","விண்ணகம் இருள் கொள விளங்கு வெண் மதி","ஒளி நிற உரோணியோடு ஒளித்தது ஒத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The king, slipping from his guard of the land and bound to his rare jewel of a woman, is likened to the white moon hiding with Rohini while the sky goes dark.","n":"The simile is double-edged: the moon with its favourite star is a stock image of love, but here the moon's hiding leaves the sky dark, so the land's darkening is folded into the praise of the lovers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pochchaappu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 198","lb":"CC198","ev":"Saccandan gives himself to Vijayai and lets slip his watch over the kingdom.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:199":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":199,"t":["குங்குமத் தோளினானும் கொழும் கயல் கண்ணி னாளும்","தங்கிய காதல் வெள்ளம் தணப்பு அறப் பருகும் நாளுள்","திங்கள் வெண் குடையினாற்குத் திரு இழுக்குற்ற வண்ணம்","பைங் கதிர் மதியில் தெள்ளிப் பகர்ந்து எடுத்து உரைத்தும் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"While the saffron-shouldered king and the fish-eyed queen drink the flood of their love without pause, the poet announces that he will now tell plainly how fortune slipped from the king of the white umbrella.","n":"The narrator frames the love scene as the start of a fall before any counsel is given, so the reader hears the warnings that follow already knowing they will fail.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kedu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 199","lb":"CC199","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:200":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":200,"t":["களிறு அனான் அமைச்சர் தம்முள் கட்டியங் காரன் என்பான்","ஒளிறு வாள் தடக்கையானுக்கு உயிர் என ஒழுகும் நாளுள்","பிளிறு வார் முரசின் சாற்றிப் பெரும் சிறப்பு இயற்றி வேந்தன்","வெளிறு இலாக் கேள்வியானை வேறு கொண்டு இருந்து சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Among the king's ministers was one called Kattiyankaran, who was to the king as his own life; the king had his honour proclaimed by drum and, taking this man of sound learning aside, spoke to him.","n":"Kattiyankaran is introduced with praise, உயிர் என ஒழுகும் and வெளிறு இலாக் கேள்வி, that the story will turn against him; the narrator does not yet hint at his treachery.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 200","lb":"CC200","ev":"Saccandan honours Kattiyankaran publicly and takes him aside to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:201":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":201,"t":["அசைவு இலாப் புரவி வெள்ளத்து அரிஞ்சயன் குலத்துள் தோன்றி","வசை இலாள் வரத்தின் வந்தாள் வான் சுவை அமிர்தம் அன்னாள்","விசையையைப் பிரிதல் ஆற்றேன் வேந்தன் நீ ஆகி வையம்","இசை படக் காத்தல் வேண்டும் இலங்கு பூண் மார்ப என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king says he cannot bear to part from Vijayai, the faultless woman who came to him by a boon and is like nectar, and asks Kattiyankaran to become king and guard the world so that it sings his fame.","n":"The king does not abdicate in name; he hands over the work of guarding (காத்தல்) while keeping the crown, which is the split of office and power the soothsayer warns against in 204.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","penvazhi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 201","lb":"CC201","ev":"Saccandan asks Kattiyankaran to rule in his place so that he need not leave Vijayai.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சந்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:202":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":202,"t":["அண்ணல் தான் உரைப்பக் கேட்டே அடுகளிற்று எருத்தின் இட்ட","வண்ணப் பூந் தவிசு தன்னை ஞமலி மேல் இட்டது ஒக்கும்","கண் அகல் ஞாலம் காத்தல் எனக்கு எனக் கமழும் கண்ணி","மண் அகம் வளரும் தோளான் மறுத்து நீ மொழியல் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Kattiyankaran, hearing the lord, protests that his guarding the wide world would be like laying the painted seat of a war elephant's neck on a dog, and the king tells him not to refuse.","n":"The verse holds two speeches without naming the first speaker; reading the dog simile as Kattiyankaran's self-abasement and line 4 as the king's insistence fits the நீ of line 4 and the அண்ணல் of line 1. His modesty here sets up the reversal of 241.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arasu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 202","lb":"CC202","ev":"Kattiyankaran demurs, and Saccandan insists that he accept.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:203":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":203,"t":["எழுதரு பருதி மார்பன் இற்றென இசைத்த லோடும்","தொழுது அடி பணிந்து சொல்லும் துன்னலர்த் தொலைத்த வேலோய்","கழி பெரும் காதலாள்கண் கழி நலம் பெறுக வையம்","பழி படா வகையில் காக்கும் படு நுகம் பூண்பல் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When the king had spoken, Kattiyankaran bowed at his feet and said: let the king enjoy to the full his love for the one he loves, and he himself will wear the yoke of guarding the world so that no blame falls on it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","pazhi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 203","lb":"CC203","ev":"Kattiyankaran accepts the rule and promises to guard the land without blame.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:204":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":204,"t":["வலம் புரி பொறித்த வண்கை மதவலி விடுப்ப ஏகிக்","கலந்தனன் சேனை காவல் கட்டியங் காரன் என்ன","உலந்தரு தோளினாய் நீ ஒருவன் மேல் கொற்றம் வைப்பின்","நிலம் திரு நீங்கும் என்று ஓர் நிமித்திகன் நெறியில் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"When the king sent him off and Kattiyankaran took charge of the army, a soothsayer told the king, by the rule of his craft, that if he set sovereignty upon one man, prosperity would leave the land.","n":"The warning is about concentration of power, not about Kattiyankaran's character; the story bears it out when he plots against the king (241), which is why the claim is marked asserted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","arasu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 204","lb":"CC204","ev":"Kattiyankaran takes command of the army, and a soothsayer warns the king against giving power to one man.","cl":[["a king who sets his sovereignty upon a single man drives prosperity from his land","general","asserted",4,"nimiththikan"]],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்","நிமித்திகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:205":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":205,"t":["எனக்கு உயிர் என்னப் பட்டான் என் அலால் பிறரை இல்லான்","முனைத் திறம் உருக்கி முன்னே மொய் அமர் பலவும் வென்றான்","தனக்கு யான் செய்வ செய்தேன் தான் செய்வ செய்க ஒன்றும்","மனக்கு இனா மொழிய வேண்டா வாழியர் ஒழிக என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king answers that the man is called his life, has no one but him, has melted the enemy's front and won many battles; he has done for him what he should, let the man do what he should, and no one is to speak words that grieve his mind; go, and long life to you.","n":"The king's grounds for trust are affection and past service in war, not a testing of the man for rule; and his ban on words that grieve the mind is the very closing-off of rebuke that the ministers try to break through next.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","amaichu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 205","lb":"CC205","ev":"Saccandan dismisses the soothsayer, vouching for Kattiyankaran's loyalty and victories.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சந்தன்","நிமித்திகன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:206":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":206,"t":["காவல குறிப்பு அன்றேனும் கருமம் ஈது அருளிக் கேண்மோ","நாவலர் சொல் கொண்டார்க்கு நன்கு அலால் தீங்கு வாரா","பூ அலர் கொடியனார் கண் போகமே கழுமி மேலும்","பாவமும் பழியும் உற்றார் பற்பலர் கேள் இது என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"A counsellor asks the guardian to hear graciously, even if it is not his wish: those who take the word of the learned meet only good and never harm, and many who sank in pleasure with creeper-like women also met sin and blame.","n":"பாவமும் பழியும் pairs a moral fault with public disgrace; the examples that follow (207 to 211) argue almost wholly from disgrace, the blame that outlives the deed. The speaker is named only in 212; that 206 to 212 are one speech is read from the run of என்றான் endings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idithal","kaamam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 206","lb":"CC206","ev":"A minister, later named Rudradattan, begins to counsel the king against his absorption in pleasure.","cl":[["those who follow the counsel of the learned meet only good","general","asserted",2,"rudradattan"],["those who sink in pleasure with women incur sin and blame","general","asserted",4,"rudradattan"]],"ag":["உருத்திரதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உருத்திரதத்தன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:207":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":207,"t":["பெரும் பெயர்ப் பிரமன் என்னும் பீடினால் பெரிய நீரான்","அரும்பிய முலையினாளுக்கு அணி முகம் நான்கு தோன்ற","விரும்பி ஆங்கு அவளை எய்தான் விண்ணகம் இழந்தது அன்றித்","திருந்தினாற்கு இன்று காறும் சிறு சொல்லாய் நின்றது அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Brahma, great of name, desired a girl whose breasts were budding so that four faces grew on him to see her; he lost his place in heaven and, though he mended his ways, the small talk about him stands to this day.","n":"A counsellor in a Jain epic draws his exempla of lust from the Brahmanical gods, which serves both as warning and as a quiet slight on those gods; the point made is that blame outlasts amendment (திருந்தினாற்கு).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 207","lb":"CC207","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உருத்திரதத்தன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:208":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":208,"t":["கைம் மலர்க் காந்தள் வேலிக் கண மலை அரையன் மங்கை","மைம் மலர்க் கோதை பாகம் கொண்டதே மறுவது ஆகக்","கொய்ம் மலர்க் கொன்றை மாலைக் குளிர்மதிக் கண்ணியாற்குப்","பெய்ம் மலர் அலங்கல் மார்ப பெரும் பழி ஆயிற்று அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"For the one with the konrai garland and the cool moon on his head, taking as half of himself the dark-haired daughter of the mountain king became a mark and a great blame.","n":"The form in which Siva shares his body with Uma, which Saiva texts praise, is here counted as a மறு, a stain like the moon's. This is the counsellor's polemical reading, one a Saiva reader would not accept.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 208","lb":"CC208","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உருத்திரதத்தன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:209":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":209,"t":["நீல் நிற வண்ணன் அன்று நெடுந் துகில் கவர்ந்து தம் முன்","பால் நிற வண்ணன் நோக்கில் பழி உடைத்து என்று கண்டாய்","வேல் நிறத் தானை வேந்தே விரிபுனல் தொழுனை ஆற்றுள்","கோல் நிற வளையினார்க்குக் குருந்து அவன் ஒசித்தது என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The dark-hued one once stole the women's long garments, and, seeing that this was blameworthy in the eyes of his white-hued elder, broke a kurundu tree in the Yamuna for the bangled women.","n":"The episode is Krishna's theft of the herdswomen's clothes; what the breaking of the kurundu tree does in this telling (giving the women cover, or an act of shame before Balarama) the verse does not make plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 209","lb":"CC209","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உருத்திரதத்தன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:210":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":210,"t":["காமமே கன்றி நின்ற கழுதை கண்டு அருளினாலே","வாமனார் சென்று கூடி வருந்தினீர் என்று வையத்து","ஈமம் சேர் மாலை போல இழித்திடப் பட்டது அன்றே","நாம வேல் தடக்கை வேந்தே நாம் இது தெரியின் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When a lust-burnt she-ass was seen, one called Vamana went to her out of pity and joined with her, and the world cast him off like a garland from the pyre, saying he had brought suffering on himself; this is what we learn, O king.","n":"Who வாமனார் is here, and which story is meant, cannot be fixed from the verse; the grim irony is that arul, compassion, is named as the motive of a lustful act.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pazhi","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 210","lb":"CC210","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உருத்திரதத்தன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:211":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":211,"t":["படு பழி மறைக்கல் ஆமோ பஞ்சவர் அன்று பெற்ற","வடுவுரை யாவர் பேர்ப்பார் வாய்ப் பறை அறைந்து தூற்றி","இடுவதே அன்றிப் பின்னும் இழுக்கு உடைத்து அம்ம காமம்","நடுவு நின்று உலகம் ஓம்பல் நல்லதே போலும் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Can great blame be hidden? Who can wipe out the scar-word the Pandavas earned, which the world beat out like a drum? Lust brings disgrace and more besides; to stand fair and guard the world seems the good course.","n":"நல்லதே போலும் is softened to 'seems good', a courtier's tact before a king who has forbidden grieving words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","kaamam","naduvu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 211","lb":"CC211","ev":"","cl":[["the blame that lust brings cannot be hidden and spreads through the world","general","asserted",3,"rudradattan"],["a king's good lies in standing impartial and guarding the world","general","asserted",4,"rudradattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உருத்திரதத்தன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:212":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":212,"t":["ஆர் அறிவு இகழ்தல் செல்லா ஆயிரம் செங் கணனானும்","கூர் அறிவு உடைய நீரார் சொல் பொருள் கொண்டு செல்லும்","பேர் அறிவு உடையை நீயும் பிணை அனாட்கு அவலம் செய்யும்","ஓர் அறிவு உடையை என்றான் உருத்திர தத்தன் என்பான்."],"p":"","r":"Even thousand-eyed Indra, who never scorns wisdom, follows the word of the sharp-minded; you too are of great wisdom, yet you have one single thought that will bring grief to your doe-like queen, said Rudradattan.","n":"ஓர் அறிவு plays against பேர் அறிவு: 'one thought' but also 'one sense', the lowest rank of living beings in the Jain scheme of one- to five-sensed lives, so the king of great wisdom is told he lives like a one-sensed creature. The pun is a reading; the plain sense stands without it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idithal","arivu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 212","lb":"CC212","ev":"Rudradattan closes his counsel by warning that the king's single-minded passion will bring the queen grief.","cl":[["even the greatest ruler should be led by the counsel of the wise","general","asserted",2,"rudradattan"]],"ag":["உருத்திரதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உருத்திரதத்தன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:213":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":213,"t":["அளந்து தாம் கொண்டு காத்த அருந் தவம் உடைய நீரார்க்கு","அளந்தன போகம் எல்லாம் அவர் அவர்க்கு அற்றை நாளே","அளந்தன வாழும் நாளும் அது எனக்கு உரையல் என்றான்","விளங்கு ஒளி மணிகள் வேய்ந்து விடு சுடர் இமைக்கும் பூணான்."],"p":"","r":"The jewelled king replies that for those who guarded a measured, rare penance, all their pleasures are measured out to each of them that very day, and so are the days they live; let no one tell him this.","n":"The king turns the doctrine that past deeds measure present joy into a reason not to listen. The narrator also speaks of past destiny (215), but the work does not endorse its use as an excuse: the minister of 233, later in this ilampakam, names his fall as his own lapse into the wrong path.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","aayul","inbam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 213","lb":"CC213","ev":"Saccandan rejects the counsel, saying his pleasures and lifespan were fixed by past penance.","cl":[["the pleasures and the lifespan one enjoys are measured out by the penance of a former life","general","reported",2,"saccandan"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சந்தன்","உருத்திரதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:214":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":214,"t":["மூரித் தேம் தாரினாய் நீ முனியினும் உறுதி நோக்கிப்","பாரித்தேன் தரும நுண்நூல் வழக்கு அது ஆதல் கண்டே","வேரித் தேம் கோதை மாதர் விருந்து உனக்காக இன்பம்","பூரித்து ஏந்து இளைய கொங்கை புணர்க யான் போவல் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Rudradattan says that, even if the king is angry, he spoke looking to his good, having seen that this is the way of the subtle books of dharma; let the king embrace the young breasts of the fragrant-garlanded woman as his feast; he himself will go.","n":"The parting line can be read as resigned leave or as bitter irony; either way the counsellor withdraws rather than presses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idithal","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 214","lb":"CC214","ev":"Rudradattan defends his counsel as dharma, bids the king enjoy the queen, and leaves the court.","cl":[["a counsellor must speak for his king's good even at the risk of the king's anger, as the books of dharma direct","general","asserted",2,"rudradattan"]],"ag":["உருத்திரதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["உருத்திரதத்தன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:215":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":215,"t":["இனமாம் என்று உரைப்பினும் ஏதம் எணான்","முனம் ஆகிய பான்மை முளைத்து எழலால்","புனமா மலர் வேய் நறும் பூங் குழலாள்","மனமாம் நெறி ஓடிய மன்னவனே."],"p":"","r":"Though told it was harm, the king took no thought of danger, because a destiny formed before sprouted and rose in him, and he ran the way his heart led to the woman with flower-decked hair.","n":"Karma and the king's own heart are both given as the cause in one verse: முனம் ஆகிய பான்மை sprouts, and the king runs மனமாம் நெறி. The narrator does not let destiny excuse him; the minister of 233 blames his choice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","penvazhi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 215","lb":"CC215","ev":"Saccandan ignores all warning and follows his desire for Vijayai.","cl":[["a destiny formed in the past sprouts and drives a man past every warning","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:216":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":216,"t":["கலையார் துகில் ஏந்து அல்குலும் கதிர் சூழ்","முலையார் தடமும் முனியாது படிந்து","உலையாத் திருவின் அமிர்து உண்டு ஒளிசேர்","மலையார் மணி மார்பன் மகிழ்ந்தனனே."],"p":"","r":"Without wearying of her girdle-clad hips and her breast, the king of the jewelled chest drank the nectar of unfailing fortune and rejoiced.","n":"உலையாத் திரு, 'unfailing fortune', is ironic after 199 has told that fortune is about to slip away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 216","lb":"CC216","ev":"Saccandan gives himself wholly to pleasure with Vijayai.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:217":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":217,"t":["விரி மா மணி மாலை விளங்கு முடித்","திரு மா மணி சிந்து திளைப்பினர் ஆய்","எரி மா மணி மார்பனும் ஏந்திழையும்","அரு மா மணி நாகரின் ஆயினரே."],"p":"","r":"Revelling so that gems scattered from his crown, the king and the jewelled queen became like the Nagas of rare jewels.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 217","lb":"CC217","ev":"Saccandan gives himself wholly to pleasure with Vijayai.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:218":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":218,"t":["நறவு ஆர்ந்தது ஓர் நாகு இளம் தாமரை வாய்","உற வீழ்ந்தது ஓர் ஒண் மணி போன்று உரவோன்","அறவு ஆக்கிய இன்பம் அமர்ந்த இருள்","கறை வேல் கணினாள் கனவு உற்றனளே."],"p":"","r":"In the darkness of their pleasure, like a bright gem fallen into a young honey-filled lotus, the spear-eyed queen dreamed.","n":"Line 3 (அறவு ஆக்கிய இன்பம்) is hard to construe; the image of the gem in the lotus may stand for the king in the queen's embrace or for the child she will conceive.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 218","lb":"CC218","ev":"Vijayai dreams in the night beside the king.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:219":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":219,"t":["பஞ்சி அடிப் பவளத் துவர் வாய் அவள்","துஞ்சும் இடைக் கனவு மூன்று அவை தோன்றலின்","அஞ்சி நடுங்கினள் ஆய் இழை ஆயிடை","வெம் சுடர் தோன்றி விடிந்ததை அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Coral-lipped and soft-footed, she saw three dreams as she slept, and trembled with fear; meanwhile the hot sun rose and it was dawn.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 219","lb":"CC219","ev":"Vijayai sees three dreams, wakes trembling, and dawn breaks.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:220":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":220,"t":["பண் கெழு மெல் விரலால் பணைத் தோளி தன்","கண் கழூஉச் செய்து கலை நலந் தாங்கி","விண் பொழி பூ மழை வெல் கதிர் நேமிய","வண் புகழ் மால் அடி வந்தனை செய்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"With soft fingers that know the lute the queen washed her eyes, put on her garments, and worshipped the feet of the great lord of famed bounty, who bears the conquering wheel and on whom the sky rains flowers.","n":"மால் can name Vishnu, but the wheel (நேமி) and the rain of flowers from the sky are marks of the Jina in Jain praise; the god is read as the Jina on those marks.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 220","lb":"CC220","ev":"Vijayai rises, dresses and worships the Lord's feet.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:221":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":221,"t":["இம்பர் இலா நறும் பூவொடு சாந்து கொண்டு","எம் பெருமான் அடிக்கு எய்துக என்று ஏத்தி","வெம் பரி மான் நெடுந் தேர் மிகு தானை அத்","தம் பெருமான் அடி சார்ந்தனள் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Bringing flowers not of this world and sandal paste, praising, 'let them reach the feet of our lord', she then went to the feet of her own lord.","n":"The verse sets எம் பெருமான் அடி (the god's feet) and தம் பெருமான் அடி (her husband's feet) in parallel lines, giving the wife's day an order: the god first, then the husband.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","karpu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 221","lb":"CC221","ev":"Vijayai makes her offering to the god and then goes to her husband.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:10"},"cc:222":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":222,"t":["தான் அமர் காதலி தன்னொடு மா வலி","வானவர் போல் மகிழ்வு உற்ற பின் வார் நறும்","தேன் எனப் பால் எனச் சில் அமிர்து ஊற்று எனக்","கான் அமர் கோதை கனா மொழிகின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"After the king has taken his pleasure with his beloved queen like the gods, she begins, in words sweet as honey, milk and nectar, to tell him a dream.","n":"The dream is set inside the pleasure that is the king's fault: the telling comes at the height of the life the next verses will condemn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 222","lb":"CC222","ev":"Vijayai, after a time of delight with Saccandan, begins to tell him her dream.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:223":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":223,"t":["தொத்து அணி பிண்டி தொலைந்து அற வீழ்ந்தது எண்","முத்து அணி மாலை முடிக்கு இடன் ஆக","ஒத்து அதன் தாள் வழியே முளை ஓங்குபு","வைத்தது போல வளர்ந்ததை அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The dream: the blossom-laden asoka tree fell and was utterly gone, and from its foot a shoot rose and grew as if planted there, bearing a crown fit for eight pearl garlands.","n":"The பிண்டி (asoka) is also the tree of the Jain teacher (பிண்டிப் பெருமான், verse 311), so a royal and a religious emblem share the image. How the eight garlands, the crown and the shoot fit together turns on line 2, which is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 223","lb":"CC223","ev":"Vijayai tells her dream of the falling asoka tree and the shoot that grows from its foot.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:224":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":224,"t":["வார் குழை வில் இட மா முடி தூக்குபு","கார் கெழு குன்று அனையான் கனவின் இயல்","பார் கெழு நூல் விதியால் பயன் தான் தெரிந்து","ஏர் குழையாமல் எடுத்து உரைக்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, mountain-like, lifting his crown, works out the dream's fruit by the rules of the dream-treatise and tells it without disturbing her beauty.","n":"ஏர் குழையாமல் says the king reads so as not to spoil her calm: the interpretation is already shaped by what he will not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","therithal","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 224","lb":"CC224","ev":"Saccandan interprets Vijayai's dream by the rules of the dream-lore.","cl":[["dreams carry a fruit that can be read by the rules of a treatise","general","reported",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:225":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":225,"t":["நன்முடி நின் மகனாம் நறு மாலைகள்","அன்னவனால் அமரப்படும் தேவியர்","நல் முளை நின் மகன் ஆக்கம் அதாம் எனப்","பின்னதனால் பயன் பேசலன் விட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"He reads the crown as her son, the garlands as the queens the son will enjoy, the shoot as the son's rising fortune, and stops without speaking of what the first part, the falling tree, portends.","n":"The silence is the point: பின்னதனால் பயன் பேசலன் விட்டான் makes the king knowingly withhold his own death, which verse 267 later admits.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","makkal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 225","lb":"CC225","ev":"Saccandan explains the crown, garlands and shoot but leaves the tree's fall unexplained.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:226":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":226,"t":["இற்று அதனால் பயன் என் என ஏந்திழை","உற்றது இன்னே இடையூறு எனக்கு என்றலும்","மற்று உரையாடலளாய் மணி மா நிலத்து","அற்றது ஓர் கோதையின் பொன் தொடி சோர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen asks what the fall means, says that harm has now come upon her, and, speaking no more, sinks to the jewelled floor like a cut garland, her gold bangle slipping.","n":"She understands from what he leaves out: the unsaid part of the reading is read anyway.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kurippu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 226","lb":"CC226","ev":"Vijayai reads the king's silence as an omen of disaster and faints.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:227":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":227,"t":["காவி கடந்த கண்ணீரொடு காரிகை","ஆவி நடந்தது போன்று அணி மாழ்கப்","பாவி என் ஆவி வருத்துதியோ எனத்","தேவியை ஆண் தகை சென்று மெய் சார்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"With tears beyond the blue lily's, her beauty fading as if life had left her, the king goes to her crying 'cruel one, will you torment my life?' and takes her in his arms.","n":"பாவி, 'sinner, cruel one', is a lover's reproach, not a moral charge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 227","lb":"CC227","ev":"Saccandan runs to the fainting queen and embraces her.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:228":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":228,"t":["தண் மலர் மார்பு உறவே தழீஇயினான் அவள்","கண் மலர்த் தாள் கனவின் இயல் மெய் எனும்","பெண் மயமோ பெரிதே மடவாய்க்கு எனப்","பண் உரையால் பரவித் துயர் தீர்த்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Holding her to his cool chest, he asks whether a dream's nature is truth and whether a young woman's fancy is so great, and with words like music he eases her grief.","n":"The king who has just read the dream by the treatise now denies dreams are true, to comfort her; the story answers him at verse 267, where he says the dreams have all come to fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","inchol","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 228","lb":"CC228","ev":"Saccandan consoles Vijayai by making light of dreams.","cl":[["dreams are not true and need not be feared","general","reported",2,"saccandan"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:229":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":229,"t":["காதலன் காதலினால் களித்து ஆய் மலர்க்","கோதை அம் கொம்பு அனையார் தம் குழாம் தொழத்","தாது உகு தாமம் அணிந்து அகில் விம்மிய","போது உகு மெல் அணைப் பூ மகள் சேர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Glad in her lover's love, worshipped by her women slender as creepers, she wears a pollen-shedding garland and lies on a soft bed of flowers fragrant with aloe.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 229","lb":"CC229","ev":"Vijayai, comforted, returns to her pleasures among her women.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","கொம்பு அனையார் குழாம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:230":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":230,"t":["பண் கனியப் பருகிப் பயன் நாடகம்","கண் கனியக் கவர்ந்து உண்டு சின்னாள் செல","விண் கனியக் கவின் வித்திய வேல் கணி","மண் கனிப்பான் வளரத் தளர் கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"For some days they drink in song and devour drama with their eyes, and the spear-eyed queen begins to weaken as the one who will gladden the earth grows within her.","n":"மண் கனிப்பான், 'he who will make the earth ripen', names the unborn Civakan by his future rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","karu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 230","lb":"CC230","ev":"Vijayai conceives, and her body weakens as the child grows.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:231":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":231,"t":["கரும்பு ஆர் தோள் முத்தம் கழன்று செவ்வாய் விளர்த்துக் கண் பசலை பூத்த காமம்","விரும்பு ஆர் முலைக் கண் கரிந்து திங்கள் வெண் கதிர்கள் பெய்து இருந்த பொன் செப்பே போல்","அரும்பால் பரந்து நுசுப்பும் கண்ணின் புலன் ஆயிற்று ஆய்ந்த அனிச்ச மாலை","பெரும் பாரமாய்ப் பெரிது நைந்து நல் சூல் சலஞ்சலம் போல் நங்கை நலம் தொலைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The signs of pregnancy: the pearls slip from her cane-like shoulders, her red lips pale, her eyes take on pallor, her nipples darken, her waist shows, the anicham garland becomes a burden, and her loveliness wanes like a pregnant conch.","n":"The catalogue of pregnancy signs is conventional; சலஞ்சலம் is the rare right-whorled conch, so the waning is itself a mark of worth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 231","lb":"CC231","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:232":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":232,"t":["தூம்பு உடை நெடுங் கை வேழம் துற்றிய வெள்ளிலே போல்","தேம்புடை அலங்கல் மார்பில் திருமகன் தமியன் ஆக","ஓம்படை ஒன்றும் செப்பாள் திருமகள் ஒளித்து நீங்க","ஆம் புடை தெரிந்து வேந்தற்கு அறிவு எனும் அமைச்சன் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a wood-apple swallowed by an elephant, whole outside and empty within, the king is left alone as Fortune slips away without a word of leave, and his wisdom, as a minister, tells him what must be done.","n":"அறிவு எனும் அமைச்சன் can be a minister whose name is Wisdom or the king's own wisdom figured as a minister; with the realm already handed to Kattiyangaran the second reading fits, but the line does not settle it. The image keeps the outer shell intact: the king still reigns in form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kedu","arivu","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 232","lb":"CC232","ev":"Fortune secretly abandons Saccandan, and his wisdom speaks to him as a minister.","cl":[["fortune leaves a king sunk in pleasure without warning","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["திருமகள்","அறிவு"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:233":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":233,"t":["காதி வேல் மன்னர் தங்கள் கண் என வைக்கப் பட்ட","நீதி மேல் சேறல் தேற்றாய் நெறி அலா நெறியைச் சேர்ந்து","கோது இயல் காமம் என்னும் மதுவினில் குளித்த ஞான்றே","ஓதிய பொறி அற்றாய் ஓர் அரும் பொறி புனைவி என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Wisdom rebukes him: he did not know how to walk by justice, which kings keep as their eye; taking the wrong path and bathing in the liquor of lust, he lost the senses he was taught; now let him build one rare machine.","n":"The verse turns on பொறி twice: the faculties (ஓதிய பொறி) the king has lost and the machine (அரும் பொறி) he must now build. The remedy offered is not reform but a means of escape, which shows the rule is already lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kaamam","kal","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 233","lb":"CC233","ev":"","cl":[["justice is the eye of kings, and lust like liquor robs a king of his learned sense","general","asserted",2,"arivu"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறிவு","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:234":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":234,"t":["அந்தரத்தார் மயனே என ஐயுறும்","தந்திரத்தால் தம நூல் கரை கண்டவன்","வெம் திறலான் பெரும் தச்சனைக் கூவி ஓர்","எந்திர ஊர்தி இயற்றுமின் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king calls the master carpenter, so skilled in the science of machines that men take him for Mayan of the gods, and orders him to build a mechanical vehicle.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 234","lb":"CC234","ev":"Saccandan orders the master carpenter to build a flying vehicle.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","பெருந்தச்சன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சந்தன்","பெருந்தச்சன்"],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:235":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":235,"t":["பல் கிழியும் பயினும் துகில் நூலொடு","நல் அரக்கும் மெழுகும் நலம் சான்றன","அல்லனவும் அமைத்து ஆங்கு எழு நாள் இடைச்","செல்வது ஓர் மா மயில் செய்தனன் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"With strips of cloth, resin, thread, good lac, wax and other materials, the carpenter makes in seven days a great peacock that flies.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 235","lb":"CC235","ev":"The carpenter builds the mechanical peacock in seven days.","cl":[],"ag":["பெருந்தச்சன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:236":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":236,"t":["பீலி நல் மாமயிலும் பிறிது ஆக்கிய","கோல நல் மாமயிலும் கொடு சென்றவன்","ஞாலம் எல்லாம் உடையான் அடி கைதொழுது","ஆலும் இம் மஞ்ஞை அறிந்து அருள் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He brings a real feathered peacock and the beautiful one he made, bows at the feet of the lord of the whole earth, and asks him to tell which dancing peacock is which.","n":"The test of the craft is that the made bird cannot be told from the living one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 236","lb":"CC236","ev":"The carpenter presents the real and the made peacock side by side to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["பெருந்தச்சன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பெருந்தச்சன்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:237":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":237,"t":["நல் நெறி நூல் நயந்தான் நன்று நன்று இது","கொல் நெறியில் பெரியாய் இது கொள்க என","மின் நெறி பல்கலம் மேதகப் பெய்தது ஓர்","பொன் அறை தான் கொடுத்தான் புகழ் வெய்யோன்."],"p":"","r":"The famed king who loves the books of the good path cries 'good, good' and gives the master of the smith's craft a golden chamber full of shining jewels.","n":"கொல் நெறி here is the smith's craft; the sound of 'killing' is near but the line does not make it a pun.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 237","lb":"CC237","ev":"Saccandan rewards the carpenter with a treasure-room of jewels.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:238":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":238,"t":["ஆடு இயல் மா மயில் ஊர்தியை அவ்வழி","மாடமும் காவும் அடுத்து ஓர் சில் நாள் செலப்","பாடலின் மேல் மேல் பயப்பயத் தான் துரந்து","ஓட முறுக்கி உணர்த்த உணர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"For some days the king flies the dancing peacock vehicle near the mansions and groves, rising higher and higher by stages, winding it to run, and teaches the queen, who learns it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 238","lb":"CC238","ev":"Saccandan teaches Vijayai to fly the mechanical peacock.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:239":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":239,"t":["பண் தவழ் விரலின் பாவை பொறிவலம் திரிப்பப் பொங்கி","விண்தவழ் மேகம் போழ்ந்து விசும்பு இடைப் பறக்கும் வெய்ய","புண் தவழ் வேல் கண் பாவை பொறி இடம் திரிப்பத் தோகை","கண்டவர் மருள வீழ்ந்து கால் குவித்து இருக்கும் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"When the doll-like queen with music-playing fingers turns the pin to the right it rises through the clouds into the sky; when she turns it to the left the peacock comes down, to watchers' wonder, and settles with folded legs.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 239","lb":"CC239","ev":"Vijayai masters the peacock's controls, flying it up and bringing it down.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:240":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":240,"t":["காதி வேல் வல கட்டியங் காரனும்","நீதியால் நிலம் கொண்டபின் நீதி நூல்","ஓதினார் தமை வேறு கொண்டு ஓதினான்","கோது செய் குணக் கோதினுள் கோது அனான்."],"p":"","r":"Kattiyangaran, having taken the land by lawful gift, calls aside those learned in the law books and speaks to them, he who is the dregs within the dregs of bad character.","n":"நீதியால் நிலம் கொண்ட stresses that he holds the land lawfully, which makes the coming plot a betrayal of a lawful trust; the narrator's judgment in line 4 is unusually blunt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kayamai","utpagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 240","lb":"CC240","ev":"Kattiyangaran secretly summons the scholars of law to a private council.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:241":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":241,"t":["மன்னவன் பகை ஆயது ஓர் மாதெய்வம்","என்னை வந்து இடம் கொண்ட அஃது இராப் பகல்","துன்னி நின்று செகுத்திடு நீ எனும்","என்னை யான் செய்வ கூறுமின் என்னவே."],"p":"","r":"He tells them that a great deity hostile to the king has possessed him and presses him night and day to kill the king, and asks what he should do.","n":"Laying the murderous wish on a possessing deity lets him propose regicide while disowning it; asking the law-learned what he should do seeks sanction rather than advice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","anangu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 241","lb":"CC241","ev":"Kattiyangaran claims a deity's possession as pretext and asks counsel on killing the king.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","நீதி நூல் ஓதினார்"],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:242":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":242,"t":["அருமை மா மணி நாகம் அழுங்க ஓர்","உருமு வீழ்ந்து என உட்கினரா அவன்","கருமம் காழ்த்தமை கண்டவர் தம் உளான்","தரும தத்தன் என்பான் இது சாற்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"They shudder like a jewelled serpent struck by a thunderbolt, and Dharmadattan, one of those who saw his purpose had hardened, speaks.","n":"கருமம் காழ்த்தமை, 'that the deed had hardened', may mean that Kattiyangaran's intent was already fixed, so the counsel that follows is spoken knowing it may fail.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 242","lb":"CC242","ev":"The councillors are struck with dread, and Dharmadattan rises to answer.","cl":[],"ag":["நீதி நூல் ஓதினார்","தருமதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:243":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":243,"t":["தவளைக் கிண்கிணித் தாமரைச் சீறடிக்","குவளையே அளவுள்ள கொழுங் கணாள்","அவளையே அமிர்தாக அவ் அண்ணலும்","உவள் அகம் தனது ஆக ஒடுங்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"Dharmadattan grants the king's fault: the noble one took the queen with frog-bell anklets and kuvalai-wide eyes as his nectar and shrank into her, her heart his whole world.","n":"The loyal counsellor does not defend the king's conduct; his case for loyalty starts by conceding it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 243","lb":"CC243","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:244":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":244,"t":["விண்ணி னோடு அமிர்தம் விலைச் செல்வது","பெண்ணின் இன்பம் பெரிது எனத் தாழ்ந்து அவன்","எண்ணம் இன்றி இறங்கி இவ் வையகம்","தண் அம் தாமரை யாளொடும் தாழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Valuing a woman's pleasure above heaven and nectar, the king sank without thought, and this world sank with him and with the lotus goddess of fortune.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","inbam","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 244","lb":"CC244","ev":"","cl":[["a king who prizes a woman's pleasure above all brings down the realm and its fortune with him","general","asserted",2,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:245":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":245,"t":["தன்னை ஆக்கிய தார்ப் பொலி வேந்தனைப்","பின்னை வௌவில் பிறழ்ந்திடும் பூ மகள்","அன்னவன் வழிச் செல்லின் இம் மண்மிசைப்","பின்னைத் தன் குலம் பேர்க்குநர் இல்லையே."],"p":"","r":"If you seize the garlanded king who made you, Fortune will turn from you; if you follow in his way, none on this earth will ever uproot your line.","n":"The argument is prudential as much as moral: Dharmadattan offers a usurper his own line's safety. Madanan answers it with the sword at verse 257.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 245","lb":"CC245","ev":"","cl":[["Fortune turns from one who overthrows the lord who raised him","general","asserted",2,"dharmadattan"],["loyalty to one's maker secures one's lineage on earth","general","asserted",3,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:11"},"cc:246":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":246,"t":["திலக நீள் முடித் தேவரும் வேந்தரும்","உலக மாந்தர்கள் ஒப்ப என்று ஓதுப","குலவு தார் மன்னர்க்கு யான் இது கூறுவன்","பலவும் மிக்கனர் தேவரின் பார்த்திவர்."],"p":"","r":"Dharmadattan tells Kattiyangaran that, though the learned rank gods and kings alike above ordinary people, he holds that kings surpass the gods in many ways.","n":"The speech that runs to 255 answers Kattiyangaran's request in 241 for counsel on the 'deity' that urges him to kill the king; its first move is to put the king above any deity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 246","lb":"CC246","ev":"Dharmadattan begins his answer to Kattiyangaran's question about killing the king.","cl":[["kings surpass the gods in many respects","general","asserted",4,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":["தருமதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:247":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":247,"t":["அருளுமேல் அரசு ஆக்குமன் காயுமேல்","வெருளச் சுட்டிடும் வேந்து எனும் மா தெய்வம்","மருளி மற்று அவை வாழ்த்தினும் வையினும்","அருளி ஆக்கல் அழித்தல் அங்கு ஆபவோ."],"p":"","r":"He argues that the king is a great deity whose favour raises a man to rule and whose anger burns him, while the gods, praised or reviled, do not make and unmake on the spot as he does.","n":"அருளுமேல் அரசு ஆக்கும் glances at Kattiyangaran himself, whom the king's favour has just made ruler (201 to 203).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","iraivan","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 247","lb":"CC247","ev":"","cl":[["the king's favour and anger make and destroy at once, as the gods' do not","general","asserted",1,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:248":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":248,"t":["உறங்கும் ஆயினும் மன்னவன் தன் ஒளி","கறங்கு தெண் திரை வையகம் காக்குமால்","இறங்கு கண் இமையார் விழித்தே இருந்து","அறங்கள் வௌவ அதன் புறம் காக்கலார்."],"p":"","r":"Even asleep, the king's splendour guards the sea-girt earth, whereas the unblinking gods, though always awake, do not stand guard when dharma is plundered.","n":"The story is about to test this: a king sunk in pleasure (261) does not protect even his own palace. The narrator does not repeat the claim, so it stands as Dharmadattan's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 248","lb":"CC248","ev":"","cl":[["a king's power protects the world even while he sleeps","general","reported",1,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:249":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":249,"t":["யாவர் ஆயினும் நால்வரைப் பின்னிடின்","தேவர் என்பது தேறும் இவ் வையகம்","காவல் மன்னவர் காய்வன சிந்தியார்","நாவினும் உரையார் நவை அஞ்சுவார்."],"p":"","r":"The world takes as divine whoever follows after the four, and men do not even think of what angers their guardian kings, nor speak of it, fearing the fault.","n":"Who the நால்வர் of line 1 are is not stated in the verse; the reading of the first two lines is open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","acham","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 249","lb":"CC249","ev":"","cl":[["subjects should not even think or speak against the king","general","asserted",3,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:250":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":250,"t":["தீண்டினார் தமைத் தீச் சுடும் மன்னர் தீ","ஈண்டு தம் கிளையொடும் எரித்திடும்","வேண்டில் இன் அமிர்தும் நஞ்சும் ஆதலான்","மாண்டது அன்று நின் வாய் மொழித் தெய்வமே."],"p":"","r":"The fire that is a king burns those who touch it along with all their kin, and turns nectar or poison as it will, so the deity Kattiyangaran says speaks in his mouth is no worthy guide.","n":"நின் வாய் மொழித் தெய்வம் answers 241 directly: the 'great deity hostile to the king' that Kattiyangaran claims possesses him is dismissed as a deity of his own words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","utpagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 250","lb":"CC250","ev":"","cl":[["whoever lays hands on a king is destroyed with his kin","general","asserted",1,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:251":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":251,"t":["வேலின் மன்னனை விண் அகம் காட்டி இஞ்","ஞாலம் ஆள்வது நன்று எனக்கு என்றியேல்","வாலிது அன்று எனக் கூறினன் வாள் ஞமற்கு","ஓலை வைத்து அன்ன ஒண் திறல் ஆற்றலான்."],"p":"","r":"If Kattiyangaran says that sending the king to heaven and ruling the earth himself would be good, that is not clean, the speaker tells him.","n":"The simile of the last line (வாள் ஞமற்கு ஓலை வைத்து அன்ன) recalls 202, where Kattiyangaran himself likened the throne given him to a seat put on a dog; whom the simile and the epithet ஒண் திறல் ஆற்றலான் describe is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","soozhchi","nanri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 251","lb":"CC251","ev":"","cl":[["killing the king to rule in his place is not pure conduct","general","asserted",3,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:252":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":252,"t":["குழல் சிகை கோதை சூட்டிக் கொண்டவன் இருப்ப மற்று ஓர்","நிழல் திகழ் வேலினானை நேடிய நெடும் கணாளும்","பிழைப்பிலான் புறம் தந்தானும் குரவரைப் பேணல் செய்யா","இழுக்கினார் இவர்கள் கண்டாய் இடும்பை நோய்க்கு இரைகளாவார்."],"p":"","r":"The wife who seeks another spear-bearer while the man who garlanded her lives, the one who turned his back on a faultless lord, and those who fail to honour their elders are, he says, food for suffering.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","nanri","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 252","lb":"CC252","ev":"","cl":[["a wife who seeks another man while her husband lives comes to suffering","general","asserted",1,"dharmadattan"],["one who deserts a faultless lord, or fails to honour elders, comes to suffering","general","asserted",3,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:253":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":253,"t":["நட்பு இடைக் குய்யம் வைத்தான் பிறர் மனை நலத்தைச் சேர்ந்தான்","கட்டு அழல் காமத் தீயில் கன்னியைக் கலக்கினானும்","அட்டு உயிர் உடலம் தின்றான் அமைச்சனாய் அரசு கொன்றான்","குட்ட நோய் நரகம் தம்முள் குளிப்பவர் இவர்கள் கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Betraying a friend's secret, taking another's wife, ravishing a maiden in lust, killing beings and eating their flesh, and killing the king while serving as his minister: these, he says, sink into leprosy and hell.","n":"The list ends on the crime Kattiyangaran is weighing, so the catalogue of sins is aimed at one man; flesh-eating is ranked with regicide.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","pulaal","piranil","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 253","lb":"CC253","ev":"","cl":[["one who kills and eats living beings sinks into hell","general","asserted",3,"dharmadattan"],["a minister who kills his king sinks into leprosy and hell","general","asserted",3,"dharmadattan"],["betraying a friend, adultery and violating a maiden lead to hell","general","asserted",1,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:254":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":254,"t":["பிறை அது வளரத் தானும் வளர்ந்து உடன் பெருகிப் பின் நாள்","குறைபடு மதியம் தேயக் குறுமுயல் தேய்வதே போல்","இறைவனாத் தன்னை ஆக்கி அவன் வழி ஒழுகின் என்றும்","நிறை மதி இருளைப் போழும் நெடும் புகழ் விளைக்கும் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As the hare in the moon grows with the waxing crescent and wanes with the waning moon, a man who takes the king as his lord and follows him will win lasting fame like the full moon that splits the dark.","n":"The image binds the minister's fate to the king's: if the moon wanes, so does the hare on it, which makes regicide self-destruction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","pugazh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 254","lb":"CC254","ev":"","cl":[["a servant's fortune rises and falls with his lord's, so loyal service brings fame","general","asserted",3,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:255":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":255,"t":["கோள் நிலை திரிந்து நாழி குறைபடப் பகல்கண் மிஞ்சி","நீள் நிலம் மாரி இன்றி விளைவு அஃகிப் பசியும் நீடிப்","பூண் முலை மகளிர் பொற்பில் கற்பு அழிந்து அறங்கள் மாறி","ஆணை இவ் உலகு கேடாம் அரசு கோல் கோடின் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"If the king's sceptre bends, he says, the planets leave their courses, the hours fail, the sun's heat runs over, rain stops, harvests shrink, hunger lengthens, women's chastity breaks, dharma turns, and the world is ruined.","n":"Dharmadattan uses the bent-sceptre doctrine against a would-be usurper: the seizure itself is the bending. Women's chastity is listed among the cosmic effects, not as a separate cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","mazhai","karpu","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 255","lb":"CC255","ev":"","cl":[["when the king's sceptre bends, rain fails, chastity breaks and the world is ruined","general","asserted",4,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:256":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":256,"t":["தார்ப் பொலி தரும தத்தன் தக்கவாறு உரப்பக் குன்றில்","கார்த்திகை விளக்கு இட்டு அன்ன கடி கமழ் குவளப் பந்தார்","போர்த்த தன் அகலம் எல்லாம் பொள் என வியர்த்துப் பொங்கி","நீர்க் கடல் மகரப் பேழ்வாய் மதனன் மற்று இதனைச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"When Dharmadattan has rebuked him fittingly, Madanan, his chest under its fragrant kuvalai garland drenched in sweat like a beacon lit on a hill, answers.","n":"The Karthikai lamp on a hill, used here of Madanan's heated body, returns in 262 as the lamp of blame Kattiyangaran lights on a hilltop.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 256","lb":"CC256","ev":"Dharmadattan finishes his rebuke and Madanan, flushed with anger, rises to reply.","cl":[],"ag":["தருமதத்தன்","மதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:257":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":257,"t":["தோளினால் வலியர் ஆகித் தொக்கவர் தலைகள் பாற","வாளினால் பேசல் அல்லால் வாயினால் பேசல் தேற்றேன்","காள மேகங்கள் சொல்லிக் கருனையால் குழைக்கும் கைகள்","வாள் அமர் நீந்தும் போழ்தின் வழு வழுத்து ஒழியும் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Madanan says he knows no way of settling things with the mouth, only with the sword that splits the heads of those who gather, and that hands that melt with pity at talk like thunderclouds will slip in battle.","n":"The third line (காள மேகங்கள் சொல்லிக் கருனையால் குழைக்கும் கைகள்) seems to mock Dharmadattan's speech and its pity; the construction is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vekuli","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 257","lb":"CC257","ev":"Madanan scorns debate and urges force.","cl":[["pity makes a fighter's hands fail in battle, so force and not talk must settle the matter","general","reported",3,"madanan"]],"ag":["மதனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதனன்","தருமதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:258":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":258,"t":["நுண் முத்தம் ஏற்றி ஆங்கு மெய் எல்லாம் வியர்த்து நொய்தின்","வண் முத்தம் நிரை கொள் நெற்றி வார் முரி புருவம் ஆக்கிக்","கண் எரி தவழ வண்கை மணி நகு கடகம் எற்றா","வெண் நகை வெகுண்டு நக்குக் கட்டியங் காரன் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Sweat beading on him like fine pearls, his brow drawn, fire in his eyes, Kattiyangaran strikes his jewelled bracelet and, laughing in anger, speaks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","utpagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 258","lb":"CC258","ev":"Kattiyangaran, enraged, laughs and prepares to answer Dharmadattan.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:259":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":259,"t":["என் அலால் பிறர்கள் யாரே இன்னவை பொறுக்கும் நீரார்","உன்னலால் பிறர்கள் யாரே உற்றவற்கு உறாத சூழ்வார்","மன்னன் போய்த் துறக்கம் ஆண்டு வானவர்க்கு இறைவன் ஆக","பொன் எலாம் விளைந்து பூமி பொலிய யான் காப்பல் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Kattiyangaran says no one but he would bear such words and no one but Dharmadattan would plot ill to one close to him; let the king go to rule heaven as lord of the gods, and he will guard the earth so that it brims with gold.","n":"He uses the very pretext Dharmadattan forestalled in 251, sending the king to heaven, now as a promise of prosperity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","soozhchi","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 259","lb":"CC259","ev":"Kattiyangaran rejects Dharmadattan's counsel and declares he will take the earth while the king goes to heaven.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","தருமதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:260":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":260,"t":["விளைக பொலிக அஃதே உரைத்திலம் வெகுள வேண்டா","களைகம் எழுகம் இன்னே காவலன் கூற்றம் கொல்லும்","வளை கய மடந்தை கொல்லும் தான் செய்த பிழைப்புக் கொல்லும்","அளவு அறு நிதியம் கொல்லும் அருள் கொல்லும் அமைக என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker gives way: let it flourish, they said no more than that, no need for anger, let them rise and do it now; the king will be killed by Death, by the woman, by his own fault, by his boundless wealth and by his own grace.","n":"The speaker is not named; வெகுள வேண்டா answers the anger of 258 and விளைக பொலிக echoes Kattiyangaran's promise in 259, so this is Dharmadattan giving way, in resignation or irony. Death (கூற்றம்), the woman, the king's own fault (தான் செய்த பிழைப்பு), his wealth and his grace are named together as what kills, so fate and the king's own failing are both given as the cause; the narrator bears the claim out in 261.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kaamam","vinai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 260","lb":"CC260","ev":"The councillor withdraws his objection to Kattiyangaran's plan.","cl":[["a king who errs is destroyed by his own fault, his passion, his wealth and his misplaced grace","general","asserted",3,"dharmadattan"]],"ag":["தருமதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தருமதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:261":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":261,"t":["நிலத் தலைத் திருவனாள் தன் நீப்பரும் காதல் கூர","முலைத் தலைப் போகம் மூழ்கி முகிழ் நிலா முடிகொள் சென்னி","வெலற்கு அரும் தானை நீத்த வேந்தனை வெறுமை நோக்கிக்","குலத்தொடும் கோறல் எண்ணிக் கொடியவன் கடிய சூழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the king, sunk in pleasure with his queen, deep in a love he could not leave, stripped of the invincible army he had given up, the cruel one planned to kill him with all his line.","n":"The narrator names the king's own surrender of his army in pleasure as the opening for the treachery; the king's lust and the minister's cruelty are both causes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kaamam","utpagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 261","lb":"CC261","ev":"Kattiyangaran plots to kill the defenceless king and his whole line.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:262":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":262,"t":["கோன் தமர் நிகளம் மூழ்கிக் கோட்டத்துக் குரங்கத் தன் கீழ்","ஏன்ற நன் மாந்தர்க்கு எல்லாம் இரு நிதி முகந்து நல்கி","ஊன்றிய நாட்டை எல்லாம் ஒரு குடை நீழல் செய்து","தோன்றினான் குன்றத்து உச்சிச் சுடர்ப் பழி விளக்கு இட்ட அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He sank the king's kinsmen in chains in the prison, heaped wealth on all who bowed under him, brought the whole land under one umbrella, and stood revealed like one who lights a blazing lamp of infamy on a hilltop.","n":"'One umbrella' is the formula of lawful sovereignty, here won by chains and bribes; the hilltop lamp makes the usurpation visible to all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","pazhi","utpagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 262","lb":"CC262","ev":"Kattiyangaran imprisons the king's kin, buys followers with wealth and takes the land.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:263":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":263,"t":["பருமித்த களிறும் மாவும் பரந்தியல் தேரும் பண்ணித்","திருமிக்க சேனை மூதூர்த் தெருவுதொறும் எங்கும் ஈண்டி","எரி மொய்த்த வாளும் வில்லும் இலங்கு இலை வேலும் ஏந்திச்","செரு மிக்க வேலினான் தன் திருநகர் வளைந்தது அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Readying war elephants, horses and wide-wheeled chariots, the great army fills every street of the old city and, with fiery swords, bows and leaf-bladed spears, surrounds the king's palace.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 263","lb":"CC263","ev":"Kattiyangaran's army surrounds the king's palace.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:264":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":264,"t":["நீள் நில மன்ன போற்றி! நெடு முடிக் குருசில் போற்றி!","பூண் அணி மார்ப போற்றி! புண்ணிய வேந்தே போற்றி!","கோள் நிலைக் குறித்து வந்தான் கட்டியங் காரன் என்று","சேண் நிலத்து இறைஞ்சிச் சொன்னான் செய்ய கோல் வெய்ய சொல்லான்."],"p":"","r":"A man of harsh word with a red staff bows from afar and cries to the king, praising him, that Kattiyangaran has come intending to seize him.","n":"செய்ய கோல் may be the herald's or gatekeeper's red staff; who the speaker is beyond that is not said. கோள் நிலைக் குறித்து may mean intent to capture.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 264","lb":"CC264","ev":"A palace servant warns the king that Kattiyangaran has come against him.","cl":[],"ag":["செய்ய கோல் வெய்ய சொல்லான்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["செய்ய கோல் வெய்ய சொல்லான்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:265":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":265,"t":["திண் நிலைக் கதவம் எல்லாம் திருந்து தாழ் உறுக்க வல்லே","பண்ணுக பசும் பொன் தேரும் படு மதக் களிறும் மாவும்","கண் அகன் புரிசை காக்கும் காவலர் அடைக என்றான்","விண் உரும் ஏறு போன்று வெடிபட முழங்கும் சொல்லான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, his voice like a thunderclap, orders the strong gates barred, chariots, elephants and horses made ready, and the guards to man the wide walls.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 265","lb":"CC265","ev":"Saccandan orders the palace defended.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சந்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:266":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":266,"t":["புலிப் பொறிப் போர்வை நீக்கிப் பொன் அணிந்து இலங்குகின்ற","ஒலிக் கழல் மன்னர் உட்கும் உருச் சுடர் வாளை நோக்கிக்","கலிக்கு இறை ஆய நெஞ்சினி கட்டியங் காரன் நம்மேல்","வலித்தது காண்டும் என்று வாள் எயிறு இலங்க நக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Drawing his gold-hilted sword from its tiger-skin sheath and looking on its blaze, the king laughs, baring his teeth: they will see what Kattiyangaran, whose heart is lord of strife, has resolved against them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","pagai","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 266","lb":"CC266","ev":"Saccandan draws his sword and laughs at Kattiyangaran's challenge.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:267":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":267,"t":["நங்கை நீ நடக்கல் வேண்டும் நன் பொருட்கு இரங்கல் வேண்டா","கங்குல் நீ அன்று கண்ட கனவு எல்லாம் விளைந்த என்னக்","கொங்கு அலர் கோதை மாழ்கிக் குழை முகம் புடைத்து வீழ்ந்து","செங் கயல் கண்ணி வெய்ய திருமகற்கு அவலம் செய்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king tells his lady she must go and not grieve over wealth, since all she dreamt that night has come to pass; she swoons, beats her face and falls, and her grief pains him.","n":"The king now concedes the dream he had brushed aside in 228.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","idumbai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 267","lb":"CC267","ev":"Saccandan bids Vijayai leave; she collapses in grief.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:268":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":268,"t":["மல் அலைத்து எழுந்து வீங்கி மலை திரண்டு அனைய தோளான்","அல்லல் உற்று அழுங்கி வீழ்ந்த அமிர்தம் அன்னாளை எய்திப்","புல்லிக் கொண்டு அவலம் நீக்கிப் பொம்மல் வெம் முலையினாட்குச்","சொல்லுவான் இவைகள் சொன்னான் சூழ் கழல் காலினானே."],"p":"","r":"The king of mountain shoulders goes to the fallen queen, lifts and embraces her, eases her grief, and speaks these words.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 268","lb":"CC268","ev":"Saccandan raises and comforts Vijayai.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:269":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":269,"t":["சாதலும் பிறத்தல் தானும் தம் வினைப் பயத்தின் ஆகும்","ஆதலும் அழிவும் எல்லாம் அவை பொருட்கு இயல்பு கண்டாய்","நோதலும் பரிவும் எல்லாம் நுண் உணர்வு இன்மை அன்றே","பேதை நீ பெரிதும் பொல்லாய் பெய் வளைத் தோளி என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He tells her that dying and being born come from the fruit of one's own deeds, that rise and ruin are the nature of things, and that sorrow and pity come from want of fine understanding, so she errs greatly in grieving.","n":"The king who preaches deed-fruit and detachment is the one whose own pleasure lost him the kingdom (261, and தான் செய்த பிழைப்பு in 260): the story gives his present fault as the cause, while he gives the fruit of deeds, so karma and the king's fault stand side by side, and the doctrine consoles and also indicts him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","nilaiyaamai","samanilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 269","lb":"CC269","ev":"","cl":[["birth and death come from the fruit of one's own deeds","general","asserted",1,"saccandan"],["gain and loss are the nature of all things","general","asserted",2,"saccandan"],["grief and pity arise from lack of subtle understanding","general","asserted",3,"saccandan"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:12"},"cc:270":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":270,"t":["தொல்லை நம் பிறவி எண்ணில் தொடு கடல் மணலும் ஆற்றா","எல்லைய அவற்றுள் எல்லாம் ஏதிலம் பிறந்து நீங்கிச்","செல்லும் அக் கதிகள் தம்முள் சேரலம் சேர்ந்து நின்ற","இல்லினுள் இரண்டு நாளைச் சுற்றமே இரங்கல் வேண்டா."],"p":"","r":"The king tells the queen that their past births, counted, outnumber the sands of the shore, that in all of them they were born strangers and parted, that in the states of birth to come they will not stay joined, and that kinship in a household is a matter of two days, not to be mourned.","n":"கதிகள் is the word for the states of rebirth that the Jain teaching counts as four; the verse itself does not number them, but 271 at once grounds the consolation in the Jina's scripture. The argument from countless births is used here not to urge renunciation but to stop a wife's grief at parting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","sutram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 270","lb":"CC270","ev":"","cl":[["one's past births are beyond counting, more than the sands of the shore","general","asserted",1,"saccandan"],["in the states of birth (கதிகள்) beings pass through, those joined now meet as strangers and do not stay together","jain","asserted",3,"saccandan"],["kinship within a household lasts two days and should not be grieved","general","asserted",4,"saccandan"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:271":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":271,"t":["வண்டு மொய்த்து அரற்றும் பிண்டி வாமனால் வடித்த நுண் நூல்","உண்டு வைத்து அனைய நீயும் உணர்வு இலா நீரை ஆகி","விண்டு கண் அருவி சோர விம் உயிர்த்து இனையை ஆதல்","ஒண் தொடி தகுவது அன்றால் ஒழிக நின் கவலை என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He tells her that she, who has as if drunk in and stored the subtle scripture made by the Jina of the bee-loud asoka, should not become like one without understanding, sobbing with tears streaming; it does not befit her, and she must let her care go.","n":"The Jina is named by the பிண்டி, the asoka, the same tree whose fall in Vijayai's dream (223) foretold the king's death, so the consolation is drawn from the emblem of the very loss it consoles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samanilai","arivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 271","lb":"CC271","ev":"","cl":[["one who has absorbed the Jina's subtle scripture should not grieve like one without understanding","jain","asserted",1,"saccandan"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:272":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":272,"t":["உரிமை முன் போக்கி அல்லால் ஒளி உடை மன்னர் போகார்","கருமம் ஈது எனக்கும் ஊர்தி சமைந்தது கவல வேண்டாம்","புரி நரம்பு இரங்கும் சொல்லாய் போவதே பொருள் மற்று என்றான்","எரி முயங்கு இலங்கு வாள் கை ஏற்று இளஞ் சிங்கம் அன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a young lion holding a fire-bright sword, he says that kings of glory do not go until they have sent their household ahead, that this is his duty too, that the vehicle is ready, and that she must not worry, for her going is what matters now.","n":"உரிமை முன் போக்கி can mean sending the women away or sending them before one in death; here it is the queen's escape in the machine, and the clause tells her, without saying it, that he is going to die.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pirivu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 272","lb":"CC272","ev":"Saccandan tells Vijayai the vehicle is ready and she must leave before he goes.","cl":[["a king of renown does not go to his end until he has sent his household ahead","general","asserted",1,"saccandan"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:273":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":273,"t":["என்பு நெக்கு உருகி உள்ளம் ஒழுகுபு சோர யாத்த","அன்பு மிக்கு அவலித்து ஆற்றா ஆர் உயிர்க் கிழத்தி தன்னை","இன்பம் மிக்கு உடைய கீர்த்தி இறைவனது ஆணை கூறித்","துன்பம் இல் பறவை ஊர்தி சேர்த்தினன் துணைவி சேர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"With love so great that his bones and heart seemed to melt, he speaks of the command of the lord of fame and sets his dear wife, unable to bear it, in the sorrowless bird-vehicle, and his companion goes in.","n":"Whose command (இறைவனது ஆணை) he speaks of, his own as king or a vow by his god, the line does not settle. துன்பம் இல் பறவை ஊர்தி is the peacock machine of 235 to 239.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 273","lb":"CC273","ev":"Saccandan places the grieving Vijayai in the peacock machine and she boards it.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:274":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":274,"t":["நீர் உடைக் குவளையின் நெடுங் கண் நின்ற வெம் பனி","வார் உடை முலை முகம் நனைப்ப மாதர் சென்ற பின்","சீர் உடைக் குருசிலும் சிவந்து அழன்று ஓர் தீத் திரள்","பார் உடைப் பனிக்கடல் சுடுவது ஒத்து உலம்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"After the lady has gone, the tears from her long kuvalai eyes wetting her breasts, the noble prince turns red with fury and roars like a mass of fire burning up the earth's cold sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 274","lb":"CC274","ev":"Vijayai departs in tears and Saccandan, enraged, roars.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:275":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":275,"t":["முழை முகத்து இடி அரி வளைத்த அன்ன மள்ளரில்","குழை முகப் புரிசையுள் குருசில் தான் அகப்பட","இழை முகத்து எறி படை இலங்கு வாள் கடல் இடை","மழை முகத்த குஞ்சரம் வாரிஉள் வளைத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The warriors close round the prince like men hemming a roaring lion at a cave mouth; caught inside the wall of the palace, he is ringed in a sea of flashing swords and cloud-like elephants as in a flood.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 275","lb":"CC275","ev":"Kattiyankaran's warriors and elephants surround Saccandan within the palace wall.","cl":[],"ag":["மள்ளர்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:276":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":276,"t":["அயிலினில் புனைந்த வாள் அழன்று உருத்து உரீஇ உடன்","பயில் கதிர்ப் பருமணிப் பன் மயிர்ச் செய் கேடகம்","வெயில் எனத் திரித்து விண் வழுக்கி வந்து வீழ்ந்தது ஓர்","கயில் அணிக் கதிர் நகைக் கடவுள் ஒத்து உலம்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"Drawing his steel-forged sword in blazing wrath, with his shield of many hairs set with great gems, he whirls it like sunlight and roars like a bright-smiling god slipped down from the sky.","n":"கயில் அணிக் கதிர் நகைக் கடவுள் is hard to construe; the god is unnamed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 276","lb":"CC276","ev":"Saccandan takes up sword and shield and roars.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:277":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":277,"t":["மாரியின் கடுங் கணை சொரிந்து மள்ளர் ஆர்த்த பின்","வீரியக் குரிசிலும் விலக்கி வெம் கணை மழை","வாரியில் கடிந்து உடன் அகற்ற மற்ற வன்படைப்","பேர் இயல் பெருங் களிறு பின்னி வந்து அடைந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"When the warriors have poured arrows like rain and shouted, the valiant prince wards off the hot arrow-rain and drives it back like the sea, and then the enemy army's great elephants close in upon him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 277","lb":"CC277","ev":"The warriors loose arrows; Saccandan beats them back, and the enemy elephants close in.","cl":[],"ag":["மள்ளர்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:278":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":278,"t":["சீற்றம் மிக்க மன்னவன் சேர்ந்த குஞ்சரம் நுதல்","கூற்றரும் குருதிவாள் கோடு உற அழுத்தலின்","ஊற்று உடை நெடு வரை உரும் உடன்று இடித்து என","மாற்று அரும் மதக் களிறு மத்தகம் பிளந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The furious king drives his bloodied sword into the brow and tusk of the elephant that reaches him, and the musth elephants' frontal globes split like a spring-fed mountain struck by thunder.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 278","lb":"CC278","ev":"Saccandan splits the attacking elephants' heads with his sword.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:279":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":279,"t":["வேல் மிடைந்த வேலியும் பிளந்து வெம் கண் வீரரை","வான் மயிர்ச் செய் கேடகத்து இடித்து வாள் வலை அரிந்து","ஊன் உடைக் குருதியுள் உழக்குபு திரி தரத்","தேன் மிடைந்த தாரினான் செங்களம் சிறந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Breaking the hedge of crowded spears, battering fierce warriors with his shield and cutting through the net of swords, he wades in blood, and the red field of the honey-garlanded king is at its height.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 279","lb":"CC279","ev":"Saccandan breaks through spears, warriors and swords, wading in blood.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:280":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":280,"t":["உப்பு உடைய முந் நீர் உடன்று கரை கொல்வது","ஒப்பு உடைய தானையுள் ஒரு தனியன் ஆகி","இப்படி இறை மகன் இரும் களிறு நூற","அப் படையுள் அண்ணலும் அழன்று களிறு உந்தி."],"p":"","r":"Like the salt sea raging at its shore, the king, alone in an army equal to it, crushes great elephants; and in that army the lord, raging, drives his elephant forward, the sentence running into the next verse.","n":"The அண்ணல் of the enemy army is not named until 285; that it is Kattiyankaran, who throws the discus and spear of 281 and 283, is a reading of the sequence, not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 280","lb":"CC280","ev":"Saccandan, alone, crushes elephants while the enemy lord drives his elephant at him.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:281":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":281,"t":["நீடகம் இருந்த நிழல் நேமி வலன் ஏந்திக்","கேடகம் அறுப்ப நடு அற்று அரவு சேர்ந்து ஆங்கு","ஓடு கதிர் வட்டம் என ஒய் என உலம்பிக்","காடு கவர் தீயின் மிகை சீறுபு வெகுண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Raising the wheel kept close at hand, he cuts the shield through the middle, as the snake joins the running disc of the sun, whirling it with a rush, and rages more than a fire devouring a forest.","n":"The image is of an eclipse, the snake meeting the sun; 290 turns it on the king himself. Whether the last line's rage is the thrower's or the king's is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 281","lb":"CC281","ev":"The enemy lord's discus cuts the king's shield in two.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:282":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":282,"t":["நெய்ம் முகம் அணிந்து நிழல் தங்கி அழல் பொங்கி","வைம் முகம் அணிந்த நுதி வாள் அழல வீசி","மைம் முகம் அணிந்த மதயானை தவ நூறிக்","கைம் முதல் துணிந்து களிறு ஆழ அது நோனான்."],"p":"","r":"His ghee-anointed sword with its blazing edge flashing, the king slays musth elephants in numbers and cuts one's trunk at the root so that it sinks; and the enemy lord cannot bear it.","n":"அது நோனான் is taken as the foe's reaction, leading into his spear-throw in 283.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 282","lb":"CC282","ev":"Saccandan's sword kills elephants, one with its trunk cut off; his foe cannot bear it.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:283":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":283,"t":["மாலை நுதி கொண்டு மழை மின் என இமைக்கும்","வேலை வலன் ஏந்தி விரி தாமம் அழகு அழியச்","சோலை மயிலார்கள் துணை வெம்முலைகள் துஞ்சும்","கோல வரை மார்பின் உறு கூற்று என எறிந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Raising a spear whose point flashes like lightning in cloud, he hurls it like Death into the king's handsome mountain chest, where the twin breasts of peacock-like women once slept, spoiling his spread garland.","n":"The chest is described by the women it held: the pleasure that 261 blamed for the king's fall is recalled at the moment of his mortal wound.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 283","lb":"CC283","ev":"The enemy lord hurls a spear into Saccandan's chest.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:284":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":284,"t":["புண் இடம் கொண்ட எஃகம் பறித்தலின் பொன் அனார் தம்","கண் இடம் கொண்ட மார்பில் தடாயின காது வெள்வேல்","மண் இடம் கொண்ட யானை மருப்பு இடை இட்டு அம்ம","விண் இடம் மள்ளர் கொள்ள மிறைக்கொளி திருத்தினானே."],"p":"","r":"Pulling out the white spear lodged in the wound of the chest where golden women's eyes had rested, he sets it between the tusks of an elephant fallen to earth and straightens its bent shaft, while warriors fill the sky.","n":"விண் இடம் மள்ளர் கொள்ள may mean the watching warriors' cries filled the sky or that warriors died and took the sky; மிறைக்கொளி is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 284","lb":"CC284","ev":"Saccandan draws the spear from his own wound and straightens it on a dead elephant's tusk.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:285":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":285,"t":["ஏந்தல் வேல் திருத்த யானை இரிந்தன எரி பொன் கண்ணி","நாந்தக உழவர் நண்ணார் கூற்று என நடுங்கி மள்ளர்","சாய்ந்த பின் தறுகண் ஆண்மைக் கட்டியங் காரன் வேழம்","காய்ந்தனன் கடுக உந்திக் கப்பணம் சிதறினானே."],"p":"","r":"As the lord straightens the spear, the elephants flee and the sword-wielders shrink from him as from Death; when the warriors have given way, Kattiyankaran, fierce and brave, drives his elephant hard at him and scatters caltrops.","n":"The narrator grants the usurper தறுகண் ஆண்மை, fierce courage, in the very line that names him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 285","lb":"CC285","ev":"The enemy breaks before the king; Kattiyankaran charges on his elephant, scattering caltrops.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","மள்ளர்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:286":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":286,"t":["குன்றம் மார்பு அரிந்து வெள் வேல் குடுமி மா மஞ்ஞை ஊர்ந்து","நின்ற மால் புருவம் போல நெரி முறி புருவம் ஆக்கிக்","கொன்று அவன் வேழம் வீழ்ப்ப மற்ற போர் களிற்றில் பாய்ந்து","நின்ற மா மள்ளர்க்கு எல்லாம் நீள் முடி இலக்கம் ஆனான்."],"p":"","r":"Knitting his brows like the great one who rode the crested peacock and split the mountain's chest with his white spear, he kills and fells Kattiyankaran's elephant, leaps onto another war elephant, and becomes the mark for all the great warriors standing there.","n":"The simile is Murugan splitting the mountain, used freely in a Jain epic. நீள் முடி இலக்கம் ஆனான் may mean he became the target of all or stood foremost among crowned warriors.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 286","lb":"CC286","ev":"Saccandan kills Kattiyankaran's elephant and mounts another.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:287":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":287,"t":["நஞ்சு பதி கொண்ட வள நாக அணையினான் தன்","நெஞ்சு பதி கொண்டு படை மூழ்க நிலம் வீசும்","மஞ்சு தவழ் குன்று அனைய தோள் ஒசித்து மாத்தாள்","குஞ்சரங்கள் நூறிக் கொலை வாள் ஒடித்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"With weapons sunk in his chest, like the one who lies on the poison-filled serpent couch, he breaks the cloud-capped mountain shoulders of his foes, destroys great-footed elephants, and stands with his killing sword broken.","n":"Line 1 names Vishnu on the serpent bed; whether he is the king's likeness or part of a compressed simile for the wounded chest is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 287","lb":"CC287","ev":"Saccandan, wounded, fights on until his sword breaks.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:288":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":288,"t":["ஆர் அமருள் ஆண் தகையும் அன்ன வகை வீழும்","வீரர் எறி வெம் படைகள் வீழ இமையான் ஆய்ப்","பேர் அமருள் அன்று பெருந் தாதையொடும் பேராப்","போர் அமருள் நின்ற இளையோனின் பொலிவு உற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"In the fierce battle the manly one, unblinking as the warriors' weapons rain on him, shines like the young one who once stood unretreating in the great war with his great father.","n":"Who the young one fighting with or against his great father (பெருந் தாதை) is, the verse does not say; it points to a known story and I cannot fix it from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 288","lb":"CC288","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:289":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":289,"t":["போழ்ந்து கதிர் நேமியொடு வேல் பொருது அழுந்தத்","தாழ்ந்து தறுகண் இணைகள் தீ அழல விழியா","வீழ்ந்து நில மா மகள் தன் வெம் முலை ஞெமுங்க","ஆழ்ந்து படு வெம் சுடரின் ஆண் தகை அவிந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Pierced by the bright discus and the spear, his fierce eyes still blazing, he falls so that the earth-goddess's breasts are pressed, and the manly one goes out like the setting sun.","n":"The death is told as an embrace of the earth-goddess and a sunset: the king's last union is with the land he neglected.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","maram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 289","lb":"CC289","ev":"Saccandan falls and dies.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:290":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":290,"t":["தோய்ந்த விசும்பு என்னும் தொல் நாட்டு அகம் தொழுது புலம்பு எய்தி மைந்தர் மாழ்க","ஏந்து முலையார் இனைந்து இரங்கக் கொடுங் கோல் இருள் பரப்பவே ஏ பாவம்","ஆய்ந்த குருகுலமாம் ஆழ் கடலின் உள் முளைத்த அறச் செங்கோலாய் கதிரினை","வேந்தர் பெருமானைச் சச்சந்தனை மந்திரி மா நாகமுடன் விழுங்கிற்று அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Men faint and women wail, the cruel sceptre spreads its darkness, alas the sin: the minister, a great snake, has swallowed Saccandan, lord of kings, the sun of the just sceptre risen from the deep sea of the Kuru line.","n":"The narrator names only the minister's treachery here, calling the king அறச் செங்கோல், though 261 blamed his surrender to pleasure; the eclipse image of 281 returns with the minister as the snake. The first line's தோய்ந்த விசும்பு என்னும் தொல் நாட்டு அகம் is compressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","sengol","arasu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 290","lb":"CC290","ev":"The kingdom mourns as the minister's cruel rule replaces the dead king.","cl":[["a cruel sceptre spreads darkness over the land","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:291":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":291,"t":["பால் அருவித் திங்கள் தோய் முத்த மாலை பழிப்பின் நெடுங் குடைக் கீழ்ப் பாய் பரிமான் தேர்க்","கோல் அருவி வெம் சிலையான் கூர் வாளொடு மணிக் கேடகமும் மறமும் ஆற்றி","வால் அருவி வாமன் அடித் தாமரை மலர் சூடி மந்திர மென் சாந்து பூசி","வேல் அருவிக் கண்ணினார் மெய்க்காப்பு ஓம்ப வேந்தன் போய் விண்ணோர்க்கு விருந்து ஆயினானே."],"p":"","r":"Under his tall pearl-hung umbrella, the king of the bow and the horse-drawn chariot, having fought with sharp sword and gem shield, bearing on his head the lotus feet of the Jina and smeared with mantra-sandal, guarded by spear-eyed women, goes and becomes a guest of the gods.","n":"The spear-eyed women who guard his body may be his own or the women of heaven. The Sangam formula of the fallen king as the gods' guest is joined to the Jina's feet as his last adornment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","vazhipaadu","maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 291","lb":"CC291","ev":"Saccandan, dying in battle, goes to the world of the gods.","cl":[["a king who dies fighting, bearing the Jina's feet and the mantra-sandal, goes as guest to the gods","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:292":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":292,"t":["செந் தீக் கருந் துளைய தீம் குழல் யாழ் தேம் தேம் என்னும் மணி முழவமும்","தந்து ஆங்கு இளையார் மெல் விரல்கள் தீண்டத் தாம் தாம் என்று இரங்கும் தண்ணுமைகளும்","அம தீம் கிளவியார் ஐஞ்ஞாற்றுவர் அவை துறை போய் ஆடல் அரம்பை அன்னார்","எந்தாய் வெறு நிலத்துச் சேர்தியோ என்று இனைந்து இரங்கிப் பள்ளி படுத்தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Five hundred sweet-voiced women, skilled in the arts and like dancing Rambhas, bringing flutes, lutes, gem drums and tannumai that young fingers sound, lament 'father, will you lie on the bare ground?' and lay him on his couch.","n":"The instruments of his pleasures are brought to his body; பள்ளி படுத்தார் may be the bier or a last ritual laying out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","idumbai","kaimmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 292","lb":"CC292","ev":"The king's five hundred women lament and lay out his body.","cl":[],"ag":["ஐஞ்ஞாற்றுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:293":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":293,"t":["மடை அவிழ்ந்த வெள்ளி இலை வேல் அம்பு பாய மணிச் செப்பகம் கடைகின்றவே போல்","தொடை அவிழ்ந்த மாலையும் முத்தும் தோய்ந்த துணை முலையின் உள் அரங்கி மூழ்கக் காமன்","படை அவிழ்ந்த கண் பனிநீர் பாய விம்மாப் பருமுத்த நா மழலைக் கிண் கிணியினார்","புடை அவிழ்ந்த கூந்தல் புலவுத் தோயப் பொழி மழையுள் மின்னுப் போல் புலம்பினாரே."],"p":"","r":"As if silver-leafed spears and arrows pierced them, the women beat their breasts like gem caskets, their tears flowing and their loosened hair soaked in the smell of flesh, and wail like lightning in pouring rain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","kaimmai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 293","lb":"CC293","ev":"The women beat their breasts and wail over the king.","cl":[],"ag":["ஐஞ்ஞாற்றுவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:13"},"cc:294":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":294,"t":["அரிமான் ஓர் மெல் அணை மேல் மஞ்ஞை சூழக் கிடந்து ஆங்கு வேந்தன் கிடந்தானைத் தான்","கரிமாலை நெஞ்சினான் கண்டான் கண்டே கைதொழுதான் கண்ணீர் கலுழ்ந்து உகுத்த பின்","எரிமாலை ஈமத்து இழுதார் குடம் ஏனை நூறும் ஏற்பச் சொரிந்து அலறி எம்","பெருமானே எம்மை ஒளித்தியோ என்னாப் பெரிய கண்ணீர் சொரிந்து அலறுவார்."],"p":"","r":"The black-hearted usurper sees the dead king lying like a lion on a soft couch ringed by peacocks, joins his hands and sheds tears; then, as ghee pots by the hundred are poured on the blazing pyre, the women wail, asking their lord whether he has hidden himself from them.","n":"The poet's label கரிமாலை நெஞ்சினான் turns the usurper's worship and tears into a show; who pours the ghee and who wails (the usurper's men or the king's women) is not plain in the run of participles.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","idumbai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 294","lb":"CC294","ev":"Kattiyankaran weeps over the dead king and the body is burned while the women wail.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்","கோல் வளையினார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:295":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":295,"t":["கையார் வளைகள் புடைத்து இரங்குவார் கதிர் முலைமேல் ஆரம் பரிந்து அலறுவார்","நெய்யார் கருங் குழல் மேல் மாலை சிந்தி நிலத்து இடுவார் நின்று திருவில் வீசும்","மையார் கடிப் பிணையும் வார் குழையும் களைந்திடுவார் கையால் வயிறு அதுக்குவார்","ஐயாவே என்று அழுவார் வேந்தன் செய்த கொடுமை கொடிது என்பார் கோல் வளையினார்."],"p":"","r":"The bangled women beat their bangles, tear the necklaces from their breasts, strip garlands, earrings and ear-drops and throw them down, press their bellies, cry 'my lord' and say the cruelty the king has done is cruel.","n":"வேந்தன் செய்த கொடுமை may blame the new ruler's deed or reproach the dead king for leaving them; the verse does not say which king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 295","lb":"CC295","ev":"The king's women cast off their ornaments in mourning.","cl":[],"ag":["கோல் வளையினார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:296":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":296,"t":["பானாள் பிறை மருப்பின் பைங்கண் வேழம் பகுவாய் ஓர் பை அணல் மாநாகம் வீழ்ப்பத்","தேன் ஆர் மலர்ச் சோலைச் செவ்வரையின் மேல் சிறு பிடிகள் போலத் துயர் உழந்து தாம்","ஆனாது அடியேம் வந்து அவ் உலகினில் நின் அடி அடைதும் என்று அழுது போயினார் எம்","கோனார் பறிப்ப நலம் பூத்த இக் கொடி இனிப் பூவா பிறர் பறிப்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Like young she-elephants on a red hillside grieving when a hooded snake has felled their tusker, the women go weeping, saying they will come to that world and reach his feet, and that this creeper which flowered when their lord plucked it will never flower again for others to pluck.","n":"போயினார் leaves open whether the women go to their deaths on the pyre or only depart in grief; the vow to reach his feet in the other world points to the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kaimmai","pirivu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 296","lb":"CC296","ev":"The king's women resolve to follow him to the other world and leave weeping.","cl":[["a wife who flowered for her husband will not flower for another after his death","general","reported",4,"saccandan_queens"]],"ag":["கோல் வளையினார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோல் வளையினார்","சச்சந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:297":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":297,"t":["செங் கண் குறு நரி ஓர் சிங்க ஏற்றை செகுத்து ஆங்கு அதன் இடத்தைச் சேர்ந்தால் ஒப்ப","வெம் கண் களி யானை வேல் வேந்தனை விறல் எரியின் வாய்ப் பெய்து அவன் பெயர்ந்து போய்ப்","பைங் கண் களிற்றின் மேல் தன் பெயரினால் பறை அறைந்தான் வேல் மாரி பெய்தால் ஒப்ப","எம் கணவரும் இனைந்து இரங்கினார் இருள் மனத்தான் பூமகளை எய்தினானே."],"p":"","r":"Like a red-eyed jackal that has killed a lion and taken its den, the usurper threw the spear-bearing king into the fire, went off and had the drum beaten in his own name from an elephant's back; at that rain of spears, 'our husbands too' grieved, and the dark-minded man took Fortune.","n":"எம் கணவரும் puts the verse in the mouth of the city's women, or of the poet speaking with the people; the jackal-and-lion simile makes the seizure unnatural, not only cruel.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","vanjam","arasu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 297","lb":"CC297","ev":"Kattiyankaran has himself proclaimed king by drum, and the people grieve.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:298":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":298,"t":["களிமுகச் சுரும்பு உண் கோதை கயில் எருத்து அசைந்து சோர","வளிமுகச் சுடரின் நில்லா மனத்தொடு மயங்கி இப்பால்","சுளிமுகக் களிறு அனான்தன் சொல் நய நெறியில் போய","கிளி முகக் கிளவிக்கு உற்றது இற்று எனக் கிளக்கல் உற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet turns to tell what befell the parrot-voiced queen who, her garland slipping from her neck and her mind unsteady as a flame in the wind, had gone by the path her elephant-like husband bade her take.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 298","lb":"CC298","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:299":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":299,"t":["எஃகு என விளங்கும் வாள் கண் எறி கடல் அமிர்தம் அன்னாள்","அஃகிய மதுகை தன்னால் ஆய் மயில் ஊரும் ஆங்கண்","வெஃகிய புகழினான் தன் வென்றி வெம் முரசம் ஆர்ப்ப","எஃகு எறி பிணையின் மாழ்கி இறுகி மெய்ம் மறந்து சோர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the victory drum of the man whose fame was coveted roared, the sword-eyed queen, by the strength now worn thin in her, riding the fine peacock, faints like a doe struck by a spear and sinks senseless.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 299","lb":"CC299","ev":"Hearing the usurper's victory drum, Vijayai faints on the flying peacock.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:300":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":300,"t":["மோடு உடை நகரின் நீங்கி முது மரம் துவன்றி உள்ளம்","பீடு உடையவரும் உட்கப் பிணம்பல பிறங்கி எங்கும்","காடு உடை அளவை எல்லாம் கழுகு இருந்து உறங்கும் நீழல்","பாடு உடை மயில் அம் தோகை பைபய வீழ்ந்தது அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Leaving the high-built city, the peacock with its lovely plumes slowly comes down where old trees crowd, corpses lie heaped to daunt even the stout-hearted, and vultures sleep in the shade across the whole forest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 300","lb":"CC300","ev":"The mechanical peacock carries Vijayai out of the city and sinks into the burning ground.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:301":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":301,"t":["மஞ்சு சூழ்வதனை ஒத்துப் பிணப்புகை மலிந்து பேயும்","அஞ்சும் அம் மயானம் தன்னுள் அகில் வயிறு ஆர்ந்த கோதை","பஞ்சிமேல் வீழ்வதே போல் பல் பொறிக் குடுமி நெற்றிக்","குஞ்சி மா மஞ்ஞை வீழ்ந்து கால் குவித்து இருந்தது அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"In that burning ground, thick with corpse-smoke like gathering cloud and feared even by ghosts, the crested peacock falls as a garland falls on cotton and settles with folded feet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 301","lb":"CC301","ev":"The peacock lands in the cremation ground.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:302":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":302,"t":["வார் தரு தடம் கண் நீர் தன் வன முலை நனைப்ப ஏல் பெற்று","ஊர் கடல் அனைய காட்டுள் அச்சம் ஒன்றானும் உள்ளாள்","ஏர் தரு மயில் அம் சாயல் இறைவனுக்கு இரங்கி ஏங்கிச்","சோர் துகில் திருத்தல் தேற்றாள் துணை பிரி மகன்றில் ஒத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her tears wetting her breasts, the queen of peacock grace feels no fear in that sea-wide forest; she grieves and sighs for her lord, cannot even set right her slipping garment, and is like a magandril parted from its mate.","n":"ஏல் பெற்று is obscure; it may say she had come to her time. Grief for the king, not fear of the place, fills her: the poet makes loss outweigh terror.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 302","lb":"CC302","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:303":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":303,"t":["உண்டு என உரையில் கேட்பார் உயிர் உறு பாவம் எல்லாம்","கண்டு இனித் தெளிக என்று காட்டுவாள் போல ஆகி","விண்தொட நிவந்த கோயில் விண்ணவர் மகளின் சென்றாள்","வெண்தலை பயின்ற காட்டுள் விளங்கு இழை தமியள் ஆனாள்."],"p":"","r":"As if to show those who hear only in words that sin exists, and bid them see it now and be sure of all the sin that clings to lives, she who once went into sky-touching palaces like a heavenly maiden is left alone in a forest of white skulls.","n":"The poet frames the queen as an exhibit (காட்டுவாள் போல), so her fall is offered as visible proof of an unseen law; the verse does not say whose sin, and does not blame her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","nilaiyaamai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 303","lb":"CC303","ev":"Vijayai is left alone in the burning ground.","cl":[["the sin that clings to lives, known to most only by hearsay, can be seen in a reversal of fortune","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:304":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":304,"t":["இருள் கெட இகலி எங்கும் மணிவிளக்கு எரிய ஏந்தி","அருள் உடை மனத்த வாகி அணங்கு எலாம் வணங்கி நிற்பப்","பொரு கடல் பருதி போலப் பொன் அனான் பிறந்த போழ்தே","மருள் உடை மாதர் உற்ற மம்மர் நோய் மறைந்தது அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The moment the golden child is born, like the sun from the surging sea, gem lamps blaze all round driving out the dark, the deities of the place bow with gracious hearts, and the bewildered mother's distress vanishes.","n":"The burning ground's own deities (அணங்கு) turn from dread to worship at the birth, so the place of death becomes a birth-hall for a hero.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","anangu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 304","lb":"CC304","ev":"Civakan is born in the burning ground, and the deities there light lamps and bow.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அணங்கு"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:305":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":305,"t":["பூங்கழல் குருசில் தந்த புதல்வனைப் புகன்று நோக்கித்","தேம் கமழ் ஓதி திங்கள் வெண் கதிர் பொழிவதே போல்","வீங்கு இள முலைகள் விம்மித் திறந்து பால் பிலிற்ற ஆற்றாள்","வாங்குபு திலகம் சேர்த்தித் திலகனைத் திருந்த வைத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Looking with longing at the son her warrior lord had given her, the queen's young breasts swell and milk flows like moonlight; unable to bear it she takes him up, sets a tilaka on him and lays the jewel of a child down with care.","n":"திலகம் சேர்த்தி may be the forehead mark or a token of the king set beside the child; the verse plays on திலகம் and திலகன்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 305","lb":"CC305","ev":"Vijayai nurses and marks her newborn son.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:306":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":306,"t":["கறை பன்னீர் ஆண்டு உடன் விடுமின் காமர்சாலை தளி நிறுமின்","சிறை செய் சிங்கம் போல் மடங்கிச் சேரா மன்னர் சினம் மழுங்க","உறையும் கோட்டம் உடன் சீமின் ஒண் பொன் குன்றம் தலை திறந்திட்டு","இறைவன் சிறுவன் பிறந்தான் என்று ஏற்பார்க்கு ஊர்தோறு உய்த்து ஈமின்."],"p":"","r":"The queen voices the orders the birth should have brought: remit dues for twelve years, set up fair halls and shrines, clear the prisons where hostile kings lie like caged lions, open the gold hoards and give in every town to all who will take, saying the king's son is born.","n":"The imperatives are those a court would send its heralds; spoken in a burning ground, the unsent proclamation measures what the child has lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 306","lb":"CC306","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை",""],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:307":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":307,"t":["மாடம் ஓங்கும் வள நகருள் வரம்பு இல் பண்டம் தலை திறந்திட்டு","ஆடை செம் பொன் அணிகலங்கள் யாவும் யாரும் கவர்ந்து எழு நாள்","வீடல் இன்றிக் கொளப் பெறுவார் விலக்கல் வேண்டா வீழ்ந்தீர்க்குக்","கோடி மூன்றோடு அரைச் செம் பொன் கோமான் நல்கும் என அறைமின்."],"p":"","r":"She goes on: proclaim that in the rich city all goods are thrown open, that for seven days anyone may take cloth, gold and jewels unchecked, and that the lord grants three and a half crores of gold to those who have fallen into want.","n":"வீழ்ந்தீர்க்கு is read here as those fallen into want; it could mean those fallen in battle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 307","lb":"CC307","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை",""],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:308":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":308,"t":["அரும் பொன் பூணும் ஆரமும் இமைப்பக் கணிகள் அகன் கோயில்","ஒருங்கு கூடிச் சாதகம் செய்து ஒகை அரசர்க்கு உடன் போக்கிக்","கரும் கைக் களிறும் கம்பலமும் காசும் கவிகள் கொள வீசி","விரும்பப் பிறப்பாய் வினை செய்தேன் காண இஃதோஒ பிறக்குமா."],"p":"","r":"Astrologers would gather in the wide palace to cast his horoscope, the glad news would go to kings, and elephants, blankets and coin be scattered for poets; 'I who did such deeds, see: is this how you, who should have been born amid longing, are born?'","n":"Line 4 can also be read as 'I did deeds so that you would be born to be desired'; either way the mother ties the wretched birth to her own deeds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","makkal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 308","lb":"CC308","ev":"","cl":[["a mother's own past deeds account for the wretched birth of her child","general","reported",4,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:309":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":309,"t":["வெவ் வாய் ஓரி முழவு ஆக விளிந்தார் ஈமம் விளக்கு ஆக","ஒவ்வாச் சுடுகாட்டு உயர் அரங்கில் நிழல் போல் நுடங்கிப் பேய் ஆட","எவ்வாய் மருங்கும் இருந்து இரங்கிக் கூகை குழறிப் பாராட்ட","இவ்வாறு ஆகிப் பிறப்பதோ இதுவோ மன்னர்க்கு இயல் வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"With howling jackals for drums, pyres for lamps, ghosts swaying like shadows on the high stage of the unseemly burning ground, and owls hooting from every side as if in praise, she asks the child whether this is how he is born, whether this is the way of kings.","n":"The burning ground is laid out as a mock birth-festival, its creatures taking the parts of drummers, lamp-bearers, dancers and praise-singers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","piravi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 309","lb":"CC309","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:310":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":310,"t":["பற்றா மன்னன் நகர்ப் புறமால் பாயல் பிணம் சூழ் சுடு காடால்","உற்றார் இல்லாத் தமியேனால் ஒதுங்கல் ஆகாத் தூங்கு இருளால்","மற்று இஞ் ஞாலம் உடையாய்! நீ வளரும் ஆறும் அறியேனால்","எற்றே இது கண்டு ஏகாதே இருத்தியால் என் இன் உயிரே."],"p":"","r":"Because this is the outskirts of a hostile king's city, a burning ground strewn with corpses, because she is alone without kin and the darkness is too thick to move in, she does not know how the child, lord of this earth, will grow, and she cries out that her sweet life stays on, though it sees all this.","n":"என் இன் உயிரே may address her own life, which will not leave her, or the child; on the first reading she reproaches herself for surviving.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","makkal","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 310","lb":"CC310","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:311":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":311,"t":["பிறந்த நீயும் பூம் பிண்டிப் பெருமான் அடிகள் பேர் அறமும்","புறந்தந்து என்பால் துயர்க் கடலை நீந்தும் புணை மற்று ஆகாக்கால்","சிறந்தார் உளரேல் உரையாயால் சிந்தா மணியே! கிடத்தியால்","மறம் கூர் நும் கோன் சொல் செய்தேன் மம்மர் நோயின் வருந்துகோ."],"p":"","r":"If he who is born and the great dharma of the lord of the flowering asoka do not become her raft across the sea of sorrow, she asks whether anyone greater exists; calling the child 'Cintamani', she says she did as his fierce father bade, and asks whether she must now suffer in bewildered grief.","n":"பூம் பிண்டிப் பெருமான், the lord beneath the asoka, is the Jina; the mother here gives her son the name the epic bears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","iraivan","idumbai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 311","lb":"CC311","ev":"","cl":[["the dharma of the Jina is a raft across the sea of sorrow","jain","asserted",1,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:312":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":312,"t":["அந்தோ! விசயை பட்டன கொண்டு அகங்கை புறங்கை ஆனால் போல்","கந்தார் களிற்றுத் தம் கோமான் கழிய மயில் ஓர் மயில் ஊர்ந்து","வந்தாள் போலப் புறம் காட்டுள் வந்தாள் தமியே என மரங்கள்","சிந்தித்து இரங்கி அழுவன போல் பனி சேர் கண்ணீர் சொரிந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Alas, as Vijayai's lot turned like the palm to the back of the hand, with her lord gone and she come alone into the outer forest like a peacock riding a peacock, the trees seem to think on it and weep, shedding dewdrops as tears.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","idumbai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 312","lb":"CC312","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:313":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":313,"t":["அடர் பொன் பைம் பூண் அரசு அழிய அரும் பொன் புதல்வன் பெற்று இருந்த","இடர் கொள் நெஞ்சத்து இறைவியும் இருங் கண் ஞாலத்து இருள் பருகிச்","சுடர் போய் மறையத் துளங்கு ஒளிய குழவி மதிபெற்று அகம் குளிர்ந்த","படர் தீர் அந்தி அது ஒத்தாள் பணை செய் கோட்டுப் படா முலையாள்."],"p":"","r":"The queen who had lost her kingdom and gained a golden son was like an evening whose distress is past, when the sun has drunk the world's darkness and set, and a young crescent moon cools the heart.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","nilaiyaamai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 313","lb":"CC313","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:314":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":314,"t":["தேன் அமர் கோதை மாதர் திருமகன் திறத்தை ஓராள்","யான் எவன் செய்வல் என்றே அவலியா இருந்த போழ்தில்","தான் அமர்ந்து உழையின் நீங்காச் சண்பக மாலை என்னும்","கூனியது உருவம் கொண்டு ஓர் தெய்வதம் குறுகிற்று அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the queen sits grieving, not knowing what to do for her son, a deity takes the shape of the hunchbacked Sanbagamalai, who never left her side, and comes near.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","thunai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 314","lb":"CC314","ev":"A deity comes to Vijayai in the form of her maid Sanbagamalai.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:315":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":315,"t":["விம்முறு விழும வெந் நோய் அவண் உறை தெய்வம் சேரக்","கொம் என உயிர்த்து நெஞ்சில் கொட்புறு கவலை நீங்க","எம் அனை தமியை ஆகி இவ் இடர் உற்றது எல்லாம்","செம் மலர்த் திருவின் பாவாய் யான் செய்த பாவம் என்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"When the deity of that place draws near, the queen sighs and the whirling care in her heart lifts; and the deity, as the maid, says that her lady's being alone and meeting all this sorrow is the maid's own sin.","n":"The speaker of lines 3 and 4 may instead be Vijayai, blaming her own sin for the maid's hardship; the vocative செம் மலர்த் திருவின் பாவாய் suits a servant addressing the queen. Set against 308, the self-blame here is a servant's, for a lapse of service.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thunai","vinai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 315","lb":"CC315","ev":"The disguised deity comforts Vijayai and takes the blame for her solitude.","cl":[],"ag":["தெய்வம்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தெய்வம்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:316":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":316,"t":["பூவினுள் பிறந்த தோன்றல் புண்ணியன் அனைய நம்பி","நாவினுள் உலகம் எல்லாம் நடக்கும் ஒன்றாது நின்ற","கோவினை அடர்க்க வந்து கொண்டு போம் ஒருவன் இன்னே","காவி அம் கண்ணினாய்! யாம் மறைவது கருமம் என்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The deity tells the queen that this prince, like the holy one born among flowers, at whose word the whole world will move, is to crush the king who stands against him; one will come right now and take him away, and it is their part to hide.","n":"Whether the crushing of the hostile king belongs to the child or to the man who will come is not fixed by the word order; the story gives it to the child. பூவினுள் பிறந்த தோன்றல் may be the lotus-born god or the Jina who walks on flowers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","makkal","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 316","lb":"CC316","ev":"The deity foretells that someone will come for the child and bids Vijayai hide.","cl":[],"ag":["தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தெய்வம்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:14"},"cc:317":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":317,"t":["சின் மணி மழலை நாவின் கிண்கிணி சிலம்பொடு ஏங்க","பன் மணி விளக்கின் நீழல் நம்பியைப் பள்ளி சேர்த்தி","மின் மணி மிளிரத் தேவி மெல்லவே ஒதுங்கு கின்றாள்","நன் மணி ஈன்று முந்நீர்ச் சலஞ்சலம் புகுவது ஒத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Laying the boy down in the light of many jewelled lamps, his anklet bells tinkling, the queen slips quietly away, like the conch that has borne a fine pearl sinking back into the sea.","n":"The simile makes the abandonment a natural birth-and-return: the conch (சலஞ்சலம்) leaves its pearl on the shore because that is what conches do, which spares the mother blame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 317","lb":"CC317","ev":"Vijayai lays her newborn son down among lamps in the burning ground and withdraws.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:318":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":318,"t":["ஏதிலார் இடர் பல் நூறு செய்யினும் செய்த எல்லாம்","தீது இல ஆக என்று திரு முலைப் பால் மடுத்துக்","காதலான் பெயர் சுமந்த கதிர் மணி ஆழி சேர்த்திக்","கோதை தாழ் குழலினாளைக் கொண்டு போய் மறைய நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Praying that every one of the hundred harms strangers might do him come to nothing, she suckles him, puts on him the jewelled ring bearing her husband's name, and goes off with the deity in woman's form to stand hidden.","n":"The ring that carries the king's name (காதலான் பெயர் சுமந்த) is the one token of his birth left with him; in 323 the finder hides it at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 318","lb":"CC318","ev":"Vijayai nurses the child, slips the king's signet ring on him and hides with the deity.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:319":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":319,"t":["நல் வினை செய்து இலாதேன் நம்பி நீ தமியை ஆகிக்","கொல் வினை மாக்கள் சூழக் கிடத்தியோ என்று விம்மாப்","புல்லிய கொம்பு தான் ஓர் கருவிளை பூத்ததே போல்","ஒல்கி ஓர் கொம்பு பற்றி ஒரு கணால் நோக்கி நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Sobbing that she, who did no good deeds, must leave her prince alone among killers, she leans on a branch like a slender stem that has put out one blue flower and watches him with one eye.","n":"She takes the blame on herself as she did in 315 (யான் செய்த பாவம்), though the story has just laid it on the usurper and the king's own neglect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","makkal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 319","lb":"CC319","ev":"Vijayai, hidden, watches the abandoned child and laments.","cl":[["a mother's misfortune comes from her own lack of good deeds","general","reported",1,"vijayai"]],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:320":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":320,"t":["நாளொடு நடப்பது வழுக்கி மின்னொடு ஊர்","கோளொடு குளிர் மதி வந்து வீழ்ந்து எனக்","காளக உடையினன் கந்து நாமனும்","வாளொடு கனை இருள் வந்து தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"As if the cool moon had slipped from its course and fallen with the planets and lightning, Kandhunaman, dressed in black and carrying a sword, appears out of the thick darkness.","n":"He arrives exactly as the deity foretold in 316 (கொண்டு போம் ஒருவன் இன்னே); the black clothes and sword are those of a man come to the cremation ground at night.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 320","lb":"CC320","ev":"The merchant Kandukkatan comes into the burning ground at night, sword in hand.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:321":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":321,"t":["வாள் கடைந்து அழுத்திய கண்ணினார்கள் தம்","தோள் கடைந்து அழுத்திய மார்பன் தூங்கு இருள்","நீள் சுடர் நிழல் மணி கிழிப்ப நோக்கினான்","ஆள் கடிந்து அணங்கிய அணங்கு காட்டுளே."],"p":"","r":"The broad-chested man looks about, the long gleam of the jewels tearing the hanging darkness, in that forest where a spirit frightens men away.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 321","lb":"CC321","ev":"Kandukkatan looks around the haunted burning ground by the light of the jewels.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:322":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":322,"t":["அருப்பு இள முலையவர்க்கு அனங்கன் ஆகிய","மருப்பு இளம் பிறை நுதல் மதர்வை வெம் கதிர்","பரப்புபு கிடந்து எனக் கிடந்த நம்பியை","விருப்பு உள மிகுதியின் விரைவின் எய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"With eager longing he hurries to the prince, who lies like the young crescent spreading its hot rays, a Kama to the young women of the future.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 322","lb":"CC322","ev":"Kandukkatan hurries to the child.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:323":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":323,"t":["புனை கதிர்த் திருமணிப் பொன் செய் மோதிரம்","வனை மலர்த் தாரினான் மறைத்து வண் கையால்","துனை கதிர் முகந்து என முகப்பத் தும்மினான்","சினை மறைந்து ஒரு குரல் சீவ என்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The garlanded man hides the gold ring with its jewel and, as he gathers the child up in his hand like scooping light, the child sneezes, and a single voice from behind the branches says 'Jiva', live.","n":"The voice is not named; read with 318 and 319 (மறைய நின்றாள், ஒரு கொம்பு பற்றி) it is the hidden mother, and the word of blessing becomes the hero's name (361).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 323","lb":"CC323","ev":"Kandukkatan hides the signet ring and lifts the child, who sneezes; a hidden voice blesses him with 'Jiva'.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:324":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":324,"t":["என்பு எழுந்து உருகுபு சோர ஈண்டிய","அன்பு எழுந்து அரசனுக்கு அவலித்து ஐயனை","நுன் பழம் பகை தவ நூறுவாய் என","இன்பழக் கிளவியின் இறைஞ்சி ஏத்தினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Love welling up until her bones seemed to melt, grieving for the king, she bows and blesses the child in sweet words: 'You will utterly destroy your old enemy.'","n":"பழம் பகை is the father's enmity made the son's inheritance; the prophecy is fulfilled only in the Manmagal ilampakam (2323).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","anbu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 324","lb":"CC324","ev":"From hiding Vijayai blesses her son and foretells that he will destroy his father's enemy.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:325":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":325,"t":["ஒழுக்கியல் அரும் தவத்து உடம்பு நீங்கினார்","அழிப்பரும் பொன் உடம்பு அடைந்தது ஒப்பவே","வழுக்கிய புதல்வன் அங்கு ஒழிய மாமணி","விழுத் தகு மகனொடும் விரைவின் ஏகினான்."],"p":"","r":"Like those who leave the body through hard penance of right conduct and gain an indestructible golden body, he leaves his own dead son there and goes quickly with the excellent child.","n":"The simile tells the reader what the merchant was doing there: வழுக்கிய புதல்வன் is a son already dead, brought for burial; the exchange of a dead body for a living one is likened to the ascetic's exchange of a mortal body for a divine one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","yaakkai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 325","lb":"CC325","ev":"Kandukkatan leaves his own dead infant in the burning ground and takes Civakan away.","cl":[["those who leave the body through hard penance of right conduct gain an imperishable golden body","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:326":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":326,"t":["மின் அடு கனை இருள் நீந்தி மேதகு","பொன் உடை வள நகர் பொலியப் புக்கபின்","தன் உடை மதிசுடத் தளரும் தையலுக்கு","இன் உடை அருள் மொழி இனிய செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"Crossing the lightning-struck darkness he enters the rich golden city and speaks sweet, kind words to the woman who is wilting as her own thoughts burn her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 326","lb":"CC326","ev":"Kandukkatan returns to Rajamapuram and goes to his grieving wife.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:327":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":327,"t":["பொருந்திய உலகினுள் புகழ் கண் கூடிய","அருந்ததி அகற்றிய ஆசு இல் கற்பினாய்","திருந்திய நின் மகன் தீதின் நீங்கினான்","வருந்தல் நீ எம் மனை வருக என்னவே."],"p":"","r":"He tells her, the woman of flawless chastity, famed in the world and greater than Arundhati, that her fine son has escaped harm, and bids her not grieve but come to the house.","n":"The words are a ruse: their son is dead (325), and 'your son has escaped harm' passes the foundling off as her own revived child; 328 calls his handing over விரகு.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","makkal","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 327","lb":"CC327","ev":"Kandukkatan tells his wife their son has been saved.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:328":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":328,"t":["கள் அலைத்து இழி தரும் களி கொள் கோதை தன்","உள் அலைத்து எழு தரும் உவகை ஊர்தர","வள்ளலை வல் விரைந்து எய்த நம்பியை","வெள் இலை வேலினான் விரகின் நீட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"As joy floods the heart of the garlanded woman and she hurries to the child, the man of the white-leafed spear hands the prince to her with his cunning.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 328","lb":"CC328","ev":"Kandukkatan gives Civakan to his wife as her own son.","cl":[],"ag":["சுநந்தை","கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:329":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":329,"t":["சுரிமுக வலம்புரி துவைத்த தூரியம்","விரிமுக விசும்பு உற வாய் விட்டு ஆர்த்தன","எரிமுக நித்திலம் ஏந்திச் சேந்த போல்","கரிமுக முலையினார் காய் பொன் சிந்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"Conches and drums roar to the open sky, and women scatter fired gold, like reddened pearls held up in flame.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 329","lb":"CC329","ev":"The household celebrates the child's 'recovery' with music and gold.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:330":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":330,"t":["அழுகுரல் மயங்கிய அல்லல் ஆவணத்து","எழுகிளை மகிழ்ந்து எமது அரசு வேண்டினான்","கழிபெரும் காதலான் கந்து நாமன் என்று","உழிதரு பெருநிதி உவப்ப நல்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"In the market quarter that had been full of the sound of weeping, the kin rise rejoicing, and Kandhunaman, of exceeding love, gladly gives away his great wealth.","n":"எமது அரசு வேண்டினான் (line 2) is unclear: it may be the kin's words, or the merchant's, about the kingdom or its king; the reading leaves it open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","eegai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 330","lb":"CC330","ev":"The merchant's kin rejoice and Kandukkatan gives away great wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","கிளை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:331":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":331,"t":["திருமகன் பெற்று எனச் செம் பொன் குன்று எனப்","பெரு நல நிதி தலை திறந்து பீடு உடை","இரு நிலத்து இரவலர்க்கு ஆர்த்தி இன்னணம்","செருநிலம் பயப்பு உறச் செல்வன் செல்லுமே."],"p":"","r":"As though he had begotten a son of fortune, he opens the head of his great hoard like a mountain of red gold and fills the suppliants of the wide earth, and so the wealthy man goes on.","n":"What line 4 (செருநிலம் பயப்பு உற) adds is not clear as printed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 331","lb":"CC331","ev":"Kandukkatan opens his treasury to the suppliants in celebration of the child.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:332":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":332,"t":["நல் உயிர் நீங்கலும் நல் மாண்பு உடையது ஓர்","புல் உயிர் தன்னொடு நின்றுழிப் புல்லுயிர்","கல் உயிர் காட்டில் கரப்பக் கலம் கவிழ்த்து","அல்லல் உற்றாள் உற்றது ஆற்ற உரைப்பாம்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator turns: when the good life had gone and one small life of great worth stayed with her and was then hidden in the forest, we shall now tell fully what befell the woman who suffered like one whose ship had foundered.","n":"The 'good life' is presumably the dead king and the 'small life' the child; line 3 (கல் உயிர் காட்டில்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 332","lb":"CC332","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:333":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":333,"t":["பொறி அறு பாவையின் பொம் என விம்மி","வெறி உறு கோதை வெறு நிலம் எய்த","இறு முறை எழுச்சியின் எய்துவது எல்லாம்","நெறிமையில் கூற நினைவின் அகன்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Sobbing, she drops to the bare ground like a puppet whose strings are cut, and as all that is to come is rightly told in due order, she passes out of her troubled thoughts.","n":"Who tells the future in lines 3 and 4 is not named; 334 shows it is the deity. நினைவின் அகன்றாள் could mean she swoons or that she leaves her grief.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 333","lb":"CC333","ev":"Vijayai collapses, and the deity tells her what will come.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:334":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":334,"t":["பெரு மகற்கு ஆக்கம் பிறழ்வு இன்றிக் கேட்டே","திரு மகள் தான் இனிச் செய்வதை எல்லாம்","ஒரு மனத்து அன்னாய் உரை எனலோடும்","தெரு மரு தெய்வதம் செப்பியது அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Having heard her son's future greatness told without fault, she asks the deity, 'Mother of one mind with me, tell me what I should now do,' and the deity answers.","n":"She is content to leave the child once his future is assured; her question is now only about herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 334","lb":"CC334","ev":"Vijayai asks the deity what she should do next.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:335":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":335,"t":["மணி அறைந்து அன்ன வரி அறல் ஐம்பால்","பணி வரும் கற்பின் படை மலர்க் கண்ணாய்","துணி இருள் போர்வையில் துன்னுபு போகி","அணி மணல் பேர் யாற்று அமரிகை சார்வாம்."],"p":"","r":"The deity addresses her as the woman whose chastity commands reverence and bids her go with it, wrapped in the cloak of dark night, to the great sandy river Amarikai.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 335","lb":"CC335","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தெய்வம்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:336":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":336,"t":["அமரிகைக்கு ஓசனை ஐம்பது சென்றால்","குமரிக் கொடி மதில் கோபுர மூதூர்","தமர் இயல் ஓம்பும் தரணி திலகம்","நமர் அது மற்றது நண்ணலம் ஆகி."],"p":"","r":"Fifty yojanas beyond Amarikai, the deity says, lies the old walled city Dharanitilakam with its banner and towers, which keeps the ways of their kin; it is their own people's, but they will not go near it.","n":"நமர் அது makes Dharanitilakam the city of her own kin; why they must avoid it is not said here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 336","lb":"CC336","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தெய்வம்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:337":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":337,"t":["வண்டார் குவளைய வாவியும் பொய்கையும்","கண்டார் மனம் கவர் காவும் கஞலிய","தண்டாரணியத்துத் தாபதப் பள்ளி ஒன்று","உண்டு ஆங்கு அதனுள் உறைகுவம் என்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"In the Dandaka forest, crowded with bee-filled lily ponds, tanks and heart-stealing groves, there is a hermitage of ascetics, the deity says, and there they will live.","n":"The queen's next home is chosen as a place of austerity rather than of kin: the turn from palace to hermitage is decided for her by the deity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 337","lb":"CC337","ev":"The deity proposes that Vijayai live in an ascetics' hermitage in the Dandaka forest.","cl":[],"ag":["தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தெய்வம்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:338":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":338,"t":["பொருள் உடை வாய் மொழி போற்றினள் கூற","மருள் உடை மாதர் மதித்தனள் ஆகி","அருள் உடை மாதவர் அத்திசை முன்னி","இருள் இடை மின்னின் இலங்கு இழை சென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the deity spoke these meaningful true words with care, the bewildered woman weighed them and set off toward the gracious great ascetics, bright in her jewels like lightning in the dark.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 338","lb":"CC338","ev":"Vijayai accepts the deity's counsel and sets out for the hermitage.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:339":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":339,"t":["உருவ மா மதி வாள் முகத்து ஓடிய","இருவிலும் எறி மா மகரக் குழைத்","திருவிலும் இவை தேமொழி மாதரைப்","பொரு இல் நீள் அதர் போக்குவ போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The flashing makara earrings on either side of her moon face seem to light the sweet-voiced woman along the long matchless road.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 339","lb":"CC339","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:15"},"cc:340":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":340,"t":["சிலம்பு இரங்கிப் போற்று இசைப்பத் திருவில் கை போய் மெய் காப்ப","இலங்கு பொன் கிண்கிணியும் கலையும் ஓங்க எறிவேல் கண்","மலங்க மணி மலர்ந்த பவளக் கொம்பு முழு மெய்யும்","சிலம்பி வலந்தது போல் போர்வை போர்த்துச் செல்லுற்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai sets out, her anklets crying as if singing praise, her gold bells and girdle swaying and her spear-like eyes troubled, her whole coral-branch body wrapped in a cloak as thin as a spider's web.","n":"The ornaments she still wears here are the ones she will take off one by one in 349 to 353; the departure is dressed as a queen's procession, with the anklets as her only heralds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 340","lb":"CC340","ev":"Vijayai, wrapped in a thin cloak, sets out from the burning ground by night.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:341":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":341,"t":["பஞ்சி அடர் அனிச்சம் நெருஞ்சி ஈன்ற பழமால் என்று","அஞ்சும் மலர் அடிகள் அரம் கண்ட அன்ன அரும் காட்டுள்","குஞ்சித்து அசைந்து அசைந்து குருதி கான்று வெய்து உயிரா","வஞ்சி இடை நுடங்க மயில் கை வீசி நடந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Her feet, so soft they shrink even from anicham pressed on cotton and from the nerunji's ripe fruit, curl and falter on the file-rough forest floor and bleed, while she breathes hot and walks on like a peacock swinging its arms, her waist bending.","n":"The conventional hyperbole of the tender foot that fears even the anicham flower is turned into a measure of real injury: the same feet now pour blood (குருதி கான்று).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 341","lb":"CC341","ev":"Vijayai walks barefoot through the rough forest, her feet bleeding.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:342":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":342,"t":["தடங் கொள் தாமரைத் தாது உறை தேவியும்","குடங்கை போல் உண் கண் கூனியும் கூர்ம் பரல்","கடங்களும் மலையும் கடந்து ஆர் புனல்","இடம் கொள் ஆற்றகம் எய்தினர் என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The queen, like the goddess who dwells in the lotus's pollen, and the hunchback with eyes like cupped palms crossed sharp-pebbled wastes and hills and reached a river with abundant water, so it is told.","n":"The கூனி is the deity that took the form of the hunchback maid Chanbakamalai in 314; the verse keeps the disguise, so only the reader knows that the queen's companion is a god. என்பவே presents the journey as a report.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 342","lb":"CC342","ev":"Vijayai and the hunchbacked companion cross wastes and hills and reach a river.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:343":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":343,"t":["எல்லை எய்திய ஆயிரச் செங்கதிர்","மல்லல் மாக் கடல் தோன்றலும் வைகிருள்","தொல்லை நல்வினை முற்படத் தோன்றிய","அல்லல் வெவ்வினை போல அகன்றதே."],"p":"","r":"When the thousand-rayed sun rose from the sea at the edge of the sky, the lingering darkness went away like the suffering of cruel deeds when old good deeds come to the front.","n":"The doctrine is the vehicle of a simile for the dawn, not stated for its own sake, but the narrator places it at the moment the queen's night of loss ends, which presents her rescue as merit coming to fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 343","lb":"CC343","ev":"Dawn breaks over the travellers.","cl":[["when good deeds of the past come to fruit, the suffering due to evil deeds departs","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:344":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":344,"t":["நுணங்கு நுண்கொடி மின் ஓர் மழை மிசை","மணம் கொள் வார் பொழில் வந்து கிடந்தது ஒத்து","அணங்கு நுண் துகில் மேல் அசைந்தாள் அரோ","நிணம் கொள் வைந்நுதி வேல் நெடும் கண்ணினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Like a slender lightning-creeper fallen from a cloud and lying in a fragrant grove, the woman of long spear-like eyes lay resting on a fine cloth.","n":"அணங்கு நுண் துகில் may mean a cloth fine enough for a goddess, or a cloth the deity spread; the verse does not say who laid it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 344","lb":"CC344","ev":"Vijayai lies down to rest by the river at dawn.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:345":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":345,"t":["வைகிற்று எம் அனை வாழிய போழ்து எனக்","கையினால் அடி தைவரக் கண் மலர்ந்து","ஐயவோ என்று எழுந்தனள் ஆய் மதி","மொய் கொள் பூமி முளைப்பது போலவே."],"p":"","r":"Saying 'Night is over, my mother, long may you live, it is time', the companion strokes her feet with her hand; Vijayai opens her eyes, cries out in dismay and rises, like the bright moon rising from the crowded earth.","n":"The deity calls the queen எம் அனை, 'our mother', the word a servant uses; Vijayai's waking cry ஐயவோ shows that sleep has given her no forgetting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 345","lb":"CC345","ev":"The hunchbacked companion wakes Vijayai at dawn and she rises.","cl":[],"ag":["தெய்வம்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:346":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":346,"t":["தூவி அம்சிறை அன்னமும் தோகையும்","மேவி மென் புனம் மான் இனம் ஆதியா","நாவி நாறு எழில் மேனியைக் கண்டு கண்டு","ஆவித்து ஆற்று கிலாது அழுதிட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"Swans with soft-feathered wings, peacocks and herds of deer in the soft fields, seeing again and again her musk-scented lovely body, gasp and, unable to bear it, weep.","n":"The forest's creatures mourn as the palace women did in 292 to 296; the queen has no human mourners left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 346","lb":"CC346","ev":"The birds and deer of the forest weep at the sight of her.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:347":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":347,"t":["கொம்மை வெம்முலைப் போதின் கொடி அனாள்","உம்மை நின்றது ஓர் ஊழ்வினை உண்மையால்","இம்மை இவ் இடர் உற்றனள் எய்தினாள்","செம்மை மாதவர் செய் தவப் பள்ளியே."],"p":"","r":"The creeper-like woman, because a deed of a former birth stood waiting for her, suffered this distress in this life, and she reached the hermitage where the great ascetics of right conduct perform their austerities.","n":"The narrator names ஊழ்வினை of the former birth (உம்மை) as the cause of her suffering in this birth (இம்மை), after the earlier verses had blamed the minister's treachery and the king's infatuation; both causes stand in the story. The same verse carries her to the tapas grove, so the fruit of the old deed and the way out of it arrive together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","piravi","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 347","lb":"CC347","ev":"Vijayai reaches the ascetics' hermitage.","cl":[["a deed done in a former birth brings suffering in this life","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:348":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":348,"t":["வாள் உறை நெடுங் கணாளை மாதவ மகளிர் எல்லாம்","தோள் உறப் புல்லுவார் போல் தொக்கு எதிர் கொண்டு புக்குத்","தாள் உறு வருத்தம் ஓம்பித் தவ நெறிப் படுக்கல் உற்று","நாள் உறத் திங்கள் ஊர நல் அணி நீக்குகின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The women ascetics all gather and receive her as if to embrace her shoulders, take her in, ease the pain of her feet, and, meaning to set her on the path of austerity, on a day when the moon stands in an auspicious asterism, begin to take off her fine ornaments.","n":"The initiation waits for an auspicious day (நாள் உறத் திங்கள் ஊர): the turn to austerity is timed by the same astrology as a wedding, and it is the ascetic women, not Vijayai, who begin to strip the jewels.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 348","lb":"CC348","ev":"The women ascetics receive Vijayai and begin to remove her ornaments to set her on the ascetic path.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:349":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":349,"t":["திருந்து தகரச் செந் நெருப்பில் தேன் தோய்த்து அமிர்தம் கொள உயிர்க்கும்","கருங் காழ் அகிலின் நறும் புகையில் கழுமிக் கோதை கண் படுக்கும்","திருந்து நானக் குழல் புலம்பத் தேனும் வண்டும் இசைப் புலம்ப","அரும் பொன் மாலை அலங்கலோடு ஆரம் புலம்ப அகற்றினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She takes off the precious gold garland and her necklace, while her musk-dressed hair, which had lain steeped in the smoke of black agil sweetened with honey on a takaram fire, grieves, and the bees and honey-bees grieve in song.","n":"Each object she gives up is made to lament (புலம்ப three times), so the renunciation is felt by the things renounced; the verse does not yet say she cuts her hair.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","kaimmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 349","lb":"CC349","ev":"Vijayai removes her garland and necklace.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:350":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":350,"t":["திங்கள் உகிரில் சொலிப்பது போல் திலகம் விரலில் தான் நீக்கிப்","பைம்பொன் மகர குண்டலமும் பாவை கழுத்தின் அணிகலமும்","வெம் கண் வேந்தற்கு அமிர்து ஆகி வேல் கண் பாவை பகை ஆய","அம் கண் முலையின் அணி முத்தும் அரும்பொன் பூணும் அகற்றினாள்."],"p":"","r":"With her finger she removes the tilaka that shone like the moon on a fingernail, the gold makara earrings, the ornaments of her neck, and the pearls and gold on her breasts, which were nectar to the fierce-eyed king.","n":"Line 3 ties the breasts' adornment to the king's pleasure (வெம் கண் வேந்தற்கு அமிர்து ஆகி): what is put off is the queen's beauty as the dead husband's delight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","kaimmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 350","lb":"CC350","ev":"Vijayai removes her tilaka, earrings and breast ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:351":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":351,"t":["பஞ்சி அனைய வேய் மென் தோள் பகுவாய் மகரம் கான்றிட்ட","துஞ்சாக் கதிர் கொள் துணை முத்தம் தொழுதேன் உம்மை எனத் துறந்து","அஞ்சி வருத்து நுசுப்பினாள் வளை கை உடைத்து மணிக் காந்தள்","அஞ்சச் சிவந்த மெல் விரல் சூழ் அரும் பொன் ஆழி அகற்றினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Saying to the paired pearls that the makara clasp spilled on her soft bamboo-like shoulders, 'I worship you', she gives them up; she breaks the bangles on her arms and removes the gold rings from her fingers, so red they frighten the kanthal flower.","n":"தொழுதேன் உம்மை is a courteous farewell spoken to the jewels. Breaking the bangles (வளை கை உடைத்து) is the widow's sign, not only an ascetic's giving up of ornament.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 351","lb":"CC351","ev":"Vijayai takes leave of her armlet pearls, breaks her bangles and removes her rings.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:352":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":352,"t":["பூப் பெய் செம் பொன் கோடிகமும் பொன் ஆர் ஆல வட்டமும்","ஆக்கும் மணி செய் தேர்த்தட்டும் அரவின் பையும் அடும் அல்குல்","வீக்கி மின்னும் கலை எல்லாம் வேந்தன் போகி அரம்பையரை","நோக்கி நும்மை நோக்கான் நீர் நோவது ஒழிமின் எனத் துறந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She gives up all the ornaments that shine on her hips, the flowered gold sash, the gold-fringed girdle and the jewelled band, telling them: 'The king has gone and looks on the heavenly women; he will not look at you; stop grieving.'","n":"She consoles the jewels as a widow might console her women: their purpose was the king's gaze, and the gaze has gone to the அரம்பையர். The verse echoes 291, where the king became a guest of the gods. The reason she gives for the renunciation is widowhood, not release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","saakkaadu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 352","lb":"CC352","ev":"Vijayai takes off her girdle ornaments, telling them the king will not look at them again.","cl":[["the king fallen in battle has gone to heaven, where celestial women are his company","general","reported",3,"vijayai"]],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:353":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":353,"t":["பிடிக்கை போலும் திரள் குறங்கின் அணியும் நீக்கிப் பிணை அன்னாள்","அடிக் கிண்கிணியும் அம் சிலம்பும் விரல் மோதிரத்தோடு அகற்றிய பின்","கொடிப் பூத்து உதிர்ந்த தோற்றம் போல் கொள்ளத் தோன்றி அணங்கு அலற","உடுத்தாள் கல் தோய் நுண் கலிங்கம் உரவோன் சிறுவன் உயர்க எனவே."],"p":"","r":"After removing the ornaments of her thighs, her ankle bells, anklets and toe rings, looking like a creeper whose flowers have bloomed and dropped, she puts on a fine cloth dyed in ochre while the goddesses cry out, saying 'May the mighty king's son rise.'","n":"The vow that ends the stripping (349 to 353) is a wish for her son, உரவோன் சிறுவன் உயர்க: she takes the ascetic's cloth while keeping the one tie that the hermitage will not loosen (355, 359).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","makkal","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 353","lb":"CC353","ev":"Vijayai puts on the ochre cloth of an ascetic, praying for her son's rise.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:354":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":354,"t":["பால் உடை அமிர்தம் பைம் பொன் கலத்திடைப் பாவை அன்ன","நூல் அடு நுசுப்பின் நல்லார் ஏந்தவும் நேர்ந்து நோக்காச்","சேல் அடு கண்ணி காந்தள் திருமணித் துடுப்பு முன் கை","வால் அடகு அருளிச் செய்ய வனத்து உறை தெய்வம் ஆனாள்."],"p":"","r":"When the good women held out milk-nectar in a gold vessel, the fish-eyed one would not even look at it; her forearm a kanthal-flower ladle, she cooked plain greens and became a deity dwelling in the forest.","n":"வால் அடகு, plain leaf greens, is the widow's diet of the old poems, here taken up as tapas. வனத்து உறை தெய்வம் ஆனாள் makes the abstinence a kind of divinity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","thavam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 354","lb":"CC354","ev":"Vijayai refuses the milk offered her and lives on greens she cooks herself.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ மகளிர்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:355":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":355,"t":["மெல் விரல் மெலியக் கொய்த குள நெல்லும் விளைந்த ஆம்பல்","அல்லியும் உணங்கும் முன்றில் அணில் விளித்து இரிய ஆமான்","புல்லிய குழவித் திங்கள் பொழி கதிர்க் குப்பை போலும்","நல் எழில் கவரி ஊட்ட நம்பியை நினைக்கும் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Where wild rice from the pond, gathered until her soft fingers grew thin, and the stamens of ripe water lilies dry in the courtyard, squirrels cry and scatter, and as the forest cow suckles its calf that clings to it, like a heap of moonbeams, she thinks of her son.","n":"குள நெல் and ஆம்பல் அல்லி are the widow's foods of the old poems; the hermitage diet and the widow's merge. The scene of a beast nursing its young is what breaks the austerity's calm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu","kaimmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 355","lb":"CC355","ev":"Seeing a forest cow suckling its calf, Vijayai remembers her son.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:356":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":356,"t":["பெண்மை நாண் வனப்புச் சாயல் பெரு மட மாது பேசின்","ஒண்மையின் ஒருங்கு கூடி உருவு கொண்ட அனைய நங்கை","நண்ணிய நுங்கட்கு எல்லாம் அடைக்கலம் என்று நாடும்","கண்ணிய குலனும் தெய்வம் கரந்து உரைத்து எழுந்தது அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Telling the ascetics, 'This lady, as though femininity, modesty, beauty, grace and innocence had gathered and taken form, is a charge entrusted to all of you who have received her', and keeping secret her land and her high lineage, the deity rose and went.","n":"அடைக்கலம் here is a person given into others' protection, not refuge at a god's feet; the giver of the charge is itself a deity. The concealment of land and lineage (நாடும் ... குலனும் ... கரந்து) keeps the queen hidden from the usurper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adaikkalam","naanam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 356","lb":"CC356","ev":"The deity entrusts Vijayai to the ascetic women, conceals her identity, and leaves.","cl":[],"ag":["தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:357":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":357,"t":["உறுதி சூழ்ந்து அவண் ஓடலின் ஆய் இடை","மறுவில் வெண் குடை மன்னவன் காதல் அம்","சிறுவன் தன்மையைச் சேர்ந்து அறிந்து இவ்வழிக்","குறுக வம் எனக் கூனியைப் போக்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Because the companion, having weighed what was best, was eager to go there, the slender-waisted queen sent the hunchback away, telling her to learn how the beloved son of the white-umbrellaed king fared and come back by this road.","n":"Read with 356, the deity has already resolved to leave; the queen's errand only gives it the occasion. Who is the subject of உறுதி சூழ்ந்து அவண் ஓடலின் in line 1 is not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 357","lb":"CC357","ev":"Vijayai sends the hunchback to learn news of her son and return.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:358":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":358,"t":["நெஞ்சின் ஒத்து இனியாளை என் நீர்மையால்","வஞ்சித்தேன் என வஞ்சி அம் கொம்பு அனாள்","பஞ்சி மெல்லடி பல் கலன் ஆர்ப்பச் சென்று","இஞ்சி மா நகர்த் தன் இடம் எய்தினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Saying to herself, 'By my own nature I have deceived the one who is dear to me as my heart', the creeper-like companion went off with her many ornaments jingling and reached her own place in the walled city.","n":"The deity calls its kindly disguise a deceit (வஞ்சித்தேன்): it will not return with the news, and the queen's wait in 359 is the cost of the pretext.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 358","lb":"CC358","ev":"The deity, having left Vijayai under a pretext, returns to its own abode in the city.","cl":[],"ag":["தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:359":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":359,"t":["தானும் தன் உணர்வில் தளர்ந்து ஆற்றவும்","மானின் நோக்கி வரும் வழி நோக்கி நின்று","ஆனியம் பல ஆசையில் செல்லுமே","தேன் இயம்பும் ஓர் தேம் பொழில் பள்ளியே."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai herself, failing in spirit, stands watching the road by which the companion would return, and many days pass in longing in that honey-sweet grove of the hermitage.","n":"ஆனியம், taken here as 'days', is rare; the sense of a long wait is clear from the context.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 359","lb":"CC359","ev":"Vijayai waits for days in the hermitage watching the road for news.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:360":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":360,"t":["மட்டு அவிழ் கோதை வாள் அன உண்கண் மயில் அன்னாள்","கட்டு அழல் எவ்வம் கைம் மிக நீக்கிக் களிகூர","விட்டு அகல்வு ஆற்றா வேட்கையின் ஓடும் பொழுது இப் பால்","பட்டதை எல்லாம் பல்லவர் கேட்கப் பகர்கு உற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"While the peacock-like woman with the honey-dripping garland and sword-bright eyes lives on there, the narrator turns to tell the learned all that happened on the other side.","n":"The poet's own turn of the story (பகர்கு உற்றேன்), like 298 and 332, marking the shift from the mother to the son.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 360","lb":"CC360","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:361":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":361,"t":["கூற்றம் அஞ்சும் கொல் நுனை எஃகின் இளையானும்","மாற்றம் அஞ்சும் மன்னிய கற்பின் மடவாளும்","போற்றித் தந்த புண்ணியர் கூடிப் புகழோனைச்","சீற்றத் துப்பின் சீவகன் என்றே பெயர் இட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The young merchant with the spear Death itself fears and his wife of steadfast chastity, together with the worthy elders they honoured and brought, gathered and named the renowned child Civakan of fierce strength.","n":"The name answers the unseen voice that said சீவ when Kantukkatan sneezed over the child in 323. Whether the புண்ணியர் are invited elders or the couple themselves depends on how போற்றித் தந்த is construed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","karpu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 361","lb":"CC361","ev":"Kantukkatan, his wife and the elders name the child Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை","புண்ணியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:362":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":362,"t":["மேகம் ஈன்ற மின் அனையாள் தன் மிளிர் பைம் பூண்","ஆகம் ஈன்ற அம் முலை இன் பால் அமிர்து ஏந்தப்","போகம் ஈன்ற புண்ணியன் எய்த கணையே போல்","மாகம் ஈன்ற மா மதி அன்னான் வளர்கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As the woman like lightning born of a cloud gives him the sweet nectar of her breasts, the child, like the great moon born of the sky, grows like an arrow shot by the meritorious one who yields delight.","n":"The verse is built on ஈன்ற four times (born of the cloud, the breast, delight, the sky): the child is fed by a mother who did not bear him. Line 3's புண்ணியன் is not identified in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 362","lb":"CC362","ev":"Civakan grows up nursed by his foster mother.","cl":[],"ag":["சுநந்தை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:16"},"cc:363":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":363,"t":["அம் பொன் கொம்பின் ஆயிழை ஐவர் நலன் ஓம்பப்","பைம் பொன் பூமிப் பல் கதிர் முத்தார் சகடமும்","செம் பொன் தேரும் வேழமும் ஊர்ந்து நிதி சிந்தி","நம்பன் செல்லும் நாளினும் நாளும் நலம் மிக்கே."],"p":"","r":"Guarded by five nurses bright as golden creepers, the lord rides cart, golden chariot and elephant, scattering wealth, and grows in excellence day by day.","n":"ஐவர் are the five nurses of a royal child; the merchant's foster son is raised with a prince's train, though no one yet knows his birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 363","lb":"CC363","ev":"The boy Civakan grows up under five nurses, riding out in carts, chariots and on elephants and scattering wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","ஐவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:364":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":364,"t":["பல் பூம் பொய்கைத் தாமரை போன்றும் பனி வானத்து","எல்லார் கண்ணும் இன்பு உற ஊரும் மதி போன்றும்","கொல்லும் சிங்கக் குட்டியும் போன்று இவ் உலகு ஏத்தச்","செல்லும் மன்னோ சீவகன் தெய்வப் பகை வென்றே."],"p":"","r":"Like a lotus of the pools, like the moon that delights every eye, like a killing lion's cub, Civakan goes about praised by the world.","n":"தெய்வப் பகை வென்றே at the close is unclear: it may say he has overcome the enmity of fate that struck his father, or only that he outshines the gods; the line does not settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","pugazh","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 364","lb":"CC364","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:365":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":365,"t":["மணியும் முத்தும் மாசு அறு பொன்னும் பவளமும்","அணியும் பெய்யும் மாரியின் ஏற்பார்க்கு அவை நல்கிக்","கணிதம் இல்லாக் கற்பகம் கந்துக் கடன் ஒத்தான்","இணை வேல் உண்கண் சுநந்தையும் இன்பக் கொடி ஒத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Kantukkatan, giving gems, pearls, gold and coral like rain to all who ask, is like the wishing tree beyond count, and Sunandai is like a creeper of joy.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 365","lb":"CC365","ev":"Kantukkatan pours out gifts to all who ask while Sunandai rejoices.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:366":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":366,"t":["சாதிப் பைம் பொன் தன் ஒளி வௌவித் தகை குன்றா","நீதிச் செல்வம் மேல் மேல் நீந்தி நிறைவு எய்திப்","போதிச் செல்வம் பூண்டவர் ஏத்தும் பொலிவினால்","ஆதி காலத்து அந்தணன் காதல் மகன் ஒத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Outshining fine gold, swimming ever further into a wealth of right conduct and praised by those rich in wisdom, the boy is like the beloved son of the brahmin of the first age.","n":"Who ஆதி காலத்து அந்தணன் is the line does not say: it may be the first lord of the Jain tradition, whose son is the model of a prince, or Brahma; the comparison sets up the schooling that follows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","arivu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 366","lb":"CC366","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:367":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":367,"t":["நனம் தலை உலகில் மிக்க நல் நுதல் மகளிர் தங்கள்","மனம் தளை பரிய நின்ற மதலை மை ஆடுக என்றே","பொன் அம் கொடி இறைஞ்சி நின்று பூமகள் புலம்பி வைக","அனங்கனுக்கு அவலம் செய்யும் அண்ணல் நற்றாய் உரைத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The boy's good mother, bowing like a golden creeper, says that the child who breaks the bonds of women's hearts and troubles Kama himself should now go through the rite of the ink.","n":"மை ஆடுக is read here as the anointing or ink rite that opens schooling, which the next two verses carry out; நற்றாய் would normally mean the birth mother, but in this house it can only be Sunandai, and the lament of பூமகள் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 367","lb":"CC367","ev":"Civakan's mother proposes the rite that begins his learning.","cl":[],"ag":["சுநந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:368":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":368,"t":["முழவு எனத் திரண்ட திண் தோள் மூரி வெம் சிலையினானும்","அழல் எனக் கனலும் வாள் கண் அவ் வளைத் தோளி னாளும்","மழலை யாழ் மருட்டும் தீம் சொல் மதலையை மயில் அம் சாயல்","குழை முக ஞானம் என்னும் குமரியைப் புணர்க்கல் உற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The father of strong drum-like shoulders and fierce bow and the mother of bangled arms set about wedding their sweet-voiced child to the maiden called Knowledge.","n":"Learning is figured as a bride (குமரி): the first of Civakan's unions is with knowledge, before the eight marriages that name the later ilampakams, and the chapter's own name, நாமகள், is the goddess of learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","gnaanam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 368","lb":"CC368","ev":"Kantukkatan and Sunandai resolve to have Civakan taught.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:369":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":369,"t":["அரும் பொனும் மணியும் முத்தும் காணமும் குறுணி ஆகப்","பரந்து எலாப் பிரப்பும் வைத்துப் பைம் பொன் செய் தவிசின் உச்சி","இருந்து பொன் ஓலை செம் பொன் ஊசியால் எழுதி ஏற்பத்","திருந்து பொன் கண்ணியாற்குச் செல்வியைச் சேர்த்தினாரே."],"p":"","r":"Spreading gold, gems, pearls and coin by the measure, and setting out every offering, they wrote on a gold leaf with a gold stylus from a gold seat and joined the lady Learning to the boy.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","vazhipaadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 369","lb":"CC369","ev":"Civakan is initiated into letters with a writing on a golden leaf amid heaped offerings.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:370":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":370,"t":["நாமகள் நலத்தை எல்லாம் நயந்து உடன் பருகி நல் நூல்","ஏ முதல் ஆய எல்லாப் படைக் கலத் தொழிலும் முற்றிக்","காமனும் கனிய வைத்த புலம் கரை கண்டு கண் ஆர்","பூ மகள் பொலிந்த மார்பன் புவிமிசைத் திலம் ஒத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Drinking in all the beauty of the goddess of learning, mastering every weapon art beginning with the arrow and reaching the far shore of the field Kama made, he shone on earth like its forehead mark.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","padai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 370","lb":"CC370","ev":"Civakan masters the books, all the arts of arms and the arts of love.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:371":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":371,"t":["மின் தெளித்து எழுதி அன்ன விளங்கும் நுண் நுசுப்பின் நல்லார்","பொன் தெளித்து எழுதி அன்ன பூம் புறப் பசலை மூழ்கிக்","குன்று ஒளித்து ஒழிய நின்ற குங்குமத் தோளினாற்குக்","கன்று ஒளித்து அகல வைத்த கறவையில் கனிந்து நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with waists slender as lightning, their skin flooded with the pallor of longing, melt for the saffron-shouldered youth like cows whose calves have been hidden.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 371","lb":"CC371","ev":"The women of the city pale and melt with longing for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:372":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":372,"t":["விலை பகர்ந்து அல்குல் விற்கும் வேலினும் வெய்ய கண்ணார்","முலை முகந்து இளையர் மார்பம் முரிவிலர் எழுதி வாழும்","கலை இகந்து இனிய சொல்லார் கங்குலும் பகலும் எல்லாம்","சிலை இகந்து உயர்ந்த திண் தோள் சீவகற்கு அரற்றி ஆற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Even the courtesans who sell their bodies at a price, with eyes fiercer than spears, who live by marking young men's chests with their breasts, cry for Civakan night and day and cannot bear it.","n":"The verse sets the women who trade love among those undone by him: the youth's beauty overturns even a desire that is for sale.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 372","lb":"CC372","ev":"The courtesans of the city lament day and night with longing for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கணிகையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:373":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":373,"t":["வான் சுவை அமிர்த வெள்ளம் வந்து இவண் தொக்கது என்னத்","தான் சுவைக் கொண்டது எல்லாம் தணப்பு அறக் கொடுத்த பின்றைத்","தேன் சுவைத்து அரற்றும் பைந்தார்ச் சீவக குமரன் என்ற","ஊன் சுவைத்து ஒளிறும் வேலாற்கு உறுதி ஒன்று உரைக்கல் உற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When the teacher had given without stint everything he himself had tasted, as if a flood of heavenly nectar had gathered there, he set out to speak one word of counsel to Prince Civakan.","n":"The teacher is not named in this chapter; the verse only says he gave all he had gathered. உறுதி, counsel for the boy's lasting good, opens a sermon that runs to 379.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","kalvi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 373","lb":"CC373","ev":"The teacher, having taught Civakan all he knows, begins to give him counsel.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:374":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":374,"t":["நூல் நெறி வகையின் நோக்கி நுண்ணிதின் நுழைந்து தீமைப்","பால் நெறி பலவும் நீக்கிப் பருதி அம் கடவுள் அன்ன","கோன் நெறி தழுவி நின்ற குணத்தொடு புணரின் மாதோ","நால் நெறி வகையில் நின்ற நல் உயிர்க்கு அமிர்தம் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that one who examines by the way of the books, enters into them subtly, puts away the many paths of evil and joins the virtue that holds to the path of the Lord who is like the sun, is nectar to good souls in the four states.","n":"நால் நெறி வகையில் நின்ற நல் உயிர் is read as the souls of the four states of birth (gods, humans, animals, hell-beings), which would make the claim Jain; பருதி அம் கடவுள் அன்ன கோன் is the Lord like the sun, not the sun himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","kalvi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 374","lb":"CC374","ev":"","cl":[["one who follows scripture, gives up evil and holds to the Lord's path is nectar to souls in all four states of being","jain","asserted",4,"acchanandi"]],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:375":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":375,"t":["அறிவினால் பெரிய நீரார் அருவினை கழிய நின்ற","நெறியினைக் குறுகி இன்ப நிறை கடல் அகத்து நின்றார்","பொறி எனும் பெயர ஐ வாய்ப் பொங்கு அழல் அரவின் கண்ணே","வெறி புலம் கன்றி நின்றார் வேதனைக் கடலுள் நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Those great in knowledge reach the path where hard deeds wear away and stand in a full sea of bliss, while those burnt by the frenzied fields of the five-mouthed fire-snake called the senses stand in a sea of pain.","n":"The senses (பொறி) are one snake with five mouths breathing fire, not five separate beasts; the two seas, bliss and pain, are the two ends of the one choice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arivu","inbam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 375","lb":"CC375","ev":"","cl":[["by knowledge one reaches the path on which hard karma is worn away, and stands in bliss","jain","asserted",1,"acchanandi"],["those given over to the five senses stand in a sea of suffering","general","asserted",4,"acchanandi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:376":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":376,"t":["கூற்றுவன் கொடியன் ஆகிக் கொலைத் தொழில் கருவி சூழ்ந்து","மாற்ற அரும் வலையை வைத்தான் வைத்ததை அறிந்து நாமும்","நோற்று அவன் வலையை நீங்கி நுகர்ச்சியில் உலகம் நோக்கி","ஆற்று உறப் போதல் தேற்றாம் அளியமோ? பெரியமே காண்."],"p":"","r":"Death, cruel, has laid with his killing tools a net no one can undo; we who know he has laid it do not know how to escape it by austerity and go our way, but gaze on the world's pleasures: are we pitiable, or are we great?","n":"The closing question is ironic: பெரியமே, 'we are great', mocks those who know the net and stay in it; the speaker counts himself among them (நாமும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","thavam","nilaiyaamai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 376","lb":"CC376","ev":"","cl":[["only austerity frees one from the net Death has laid, yet those who know it still turn to pleasure","general","asserted",3,"acchanandi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:377":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":377,"t":["பேர் அஞர் இடும்பை எல்லாம் பிளந்திடும் பிறப்பு நீக்கும்","ஆர் அமிர்து அரிதில் பெற்றாம் அதன் பயன் கோடல் தேற்றாம்","ஓரும் ஐம் பொறியும் ஓம்பி உள பகல் கழிந்த பின்றைக்","கூர் எரி கவரும் போழ்தில் கூடுமோ குறித்த எல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"We have rarely won the nectar that splits all great sorrow and ends birth, yet do not know how to take its fruit; pampering the five senses while our days pass, can we achieve what we intended when the fierce fire takes us?","n":"கூர் எரி கவரும் போழ்து is the cremation fire: the limit is set by the burning of the body, not by the onset of dying.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi","amudham","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 377","lb":"CC377","ev":"","cl":[["the teaching that ends birth is rarely won and must be used before death, since nothing can be done once the pyre takes the body","general","asserted",4,"acchanandi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:378":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":378,"t":["தழங்கு குரல் முரசின் சாற்றித் தத்துவம் தழுவல் வேண்டிச்","செழுங் களியாளர் முன்னர் இருள் அறச் செப்பினாலும்","முழங்கு அழல் நரகின் மூழ்கும் முயற்சியர் ஆகி நின்ற","கொழுங் களி உணர்வினாரைக் குணவதம் கொளுத்தல் ஆமோ."],"p":"","r":"Even if one proclaims the truth with the sounding drum and explains it clearly before the revellers, can one make those of drunken mind, bent on sinking into roaring hell-fire, take the vows of virtue?","n":"குணவதம் is a named class of the Jain layman's vows; the teacher's despair of the many sets off the one pupil who will take them in 380.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","pedhaimai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 378","lb":"CC378","ev":"","cl":[["those bent on hell cannot be brought to take the vows even when the truth is proclaimed to them","jain","asserted",4,"acchanandi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:379":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":379,"t":["பவழவாய்ச் செறுவு தன்னுள் நித்திலம் பயில வித்திக்","குழவிநாறு எழுந்து காளைக் கொழும் கதிர் ஈன்று பின்னாக்","கிழவு தான் விளைக்கும் பைங் கூழ் கேட்டிரேல் பிணி செய் பன்மா","உழவிர்காள்! மேயும் சீல வேலி உய்த்திடுமின் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Ploughmen, if you would not lose the crop sown as pearls in the coral-mouthed field, sprouting as an infant, bearing its ear in youth and ripening in old age, set the fence of conduct against the many disease-bringing beasts that graze, he said.","n":"The vocative உழவிர்காள் makes every hearer a farmer of his own life; சீல after குணவதம் in 378 names the second class of the Jain layman's vows, so the fence is not general virtue but vowed discipline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","yaakkai","noy","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 379","lb":"CC379","ev":"","cl":[["a life from infancy to old age must be fenced with the discipline of the vows against the beasts that bring sickness","jain","asserted",4,"acchanandi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:380":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":380,"t":["சூழ் கதிர் மதியம் அன்ன சுடர் மணிப் பூணினானும்","வீழ் தரு கதியின் நீங்கி விளங்கு பொன் உலகத்து உய்க்கும்","ஊழ் வினை துரத்தலானும் உணர்வு சென்று எறித்தலானும்","ஆழ் கடல் புணையின் அன்ன அறிவரன் சரண் அடைந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Because the deed of old, which lifts one from the falling states and leads to the bright golden world, drove him, and because his understanding went forth and shone, the youth with gem ornaments like the rayed moon took refuge in the Wise One, a raft on the deep sea.","n":"Two causes are given side by side, ஊழ் வினை துரத்தலானும் உணர்வு சென்று எறித்தலானும: the past deed drives, and his own understanding also reaches out; karma here is a good one that pushes toward release, the reverse of the destiny that drove his father to ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","adaikkalam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 380","lb":"CC380","ev":"Civakan takes refuge in the Arhat.","cl":[["a ripening good deed of the past together with the dawning of one's own understanding brings one to refuge in the Arhat","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:381":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":381,"t":["காட்சி நல் நிலையில் ஞானக் கதிர் மணிக் கதவு சேர்த்திப்","பூட்சி சால் ஒழுக்கம் என்னும் வயிரத் தாழ் கொளுவிப் பொல்லா","மாட்சியில் கதிகள் எல்லாம் அடைத்த பின் வரம்பு இல் இன்பத்து","ஆட்சியில் உலகம் ஏறத் திறந்தனன் அலர்ந்த தாரான்."],"p":"","r":"Fitting the jewelled door of knowledge in the frame of right seeing and sliding in the diamond bolt called conduct, he shut all the evil states and opened the way to climb to the world of boundless bliss.","n":"காட்சி, ஞானம், ஒழுக்கம் are the Jain three jewels, built into one door: faith is the frame, knowledge the door, conduct the bolt; the same door that shuts the lower births opens the upper world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","ozhukkam","veedu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 381","lb":"CC381","ev":"Civakan takes up right faith, knowledge and conduct.","cl":[["right seeing, knowledge and conduct together close the evil states of birth and open the way to boundless bliss","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:382":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":382,"t":["நல் அறத்து இறைவன் ஆகி நால்வகைச் சரணம் எய்தித்","தொல் அறக் கிழமை பூண்ட தொடு கழல் காலினாற்குப்","புல் அற நெறிக் கண் நின்று பொருள் வயிற் பிழைத்த வாறும்","இல்லறத்து இயல்பும் எல்லாம் இருள் அறக் கூறி இட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"To the youth who had become lord of good dharma, reached the fourfold refuge and taken on the ancient bond of dharma, the teacher explained without darkness how men on base paths of dharma have erred about the truth, and all the nature of the householder's dharma.","n":"நால்வகைச் சரணம் is the fourfold refuge of the Jain creed. The participles of lines 1 and 2 could describe the teacher rather than Civakan; read with 380, they fit the new convert. The teaching given to him is the householder's dharma, not the monk's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","aram","adaikkalam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 382","lb":"CC382","ev":"The teacher instructs Civakan in the errors of false paths and in the householder's dharma.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:383":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":383,"t":["எரி முயங்கு இலங்கு வை வேல் இளையவர் குழாத்தின் நீங்கித்","திரு முயங்கு அலங்கல் மார்பின் சீவகன் கொண்டு வேறா","விரி மலர்க் கண்ணி கட்டி விழைதக வேய்ந்த போலும்","தெரி மலர்க் காவு சேர்ந்து பிறப்பினைத் தெருட்டல் உற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Taking Civakan away from the band of youths with their fiery spears, the teacher went to a grove of chosen flowers, as if roofed with woven garlands, to make plain to him his birth.","n":"பிறப்பினைத் தெருட்டல் is both the secret of his royal birth and, after 375 to 381, a lesson on birth itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","guru","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 383","lb":"CC383","ev":"The teacher leads Civakan apart into a flower grove to tell him of his birth.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:384":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":384,"t":["பூவையும் கிளியும் மன்னர் ஒற்றென புணர்க்கும் சாதி","யாவையும் இன்மை ஆராய்ந்து அம் தளிர்ப் பிண்டி நீழல்","பூ இயல் தவிசின் உச்சிப் பொலிவினோடு இருந்த போழ்தில்","ஏ இயல் சிலையினானை இப் பொருள் கேண்மோ என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Making sure no myna, parrot or any creature kings use as spies was near, and seated in splendour on a flower seat in the shade of the ashoka's tender leaves, he said to the archer: hear this matter.","n":"The care against talking birds shows that the secret is still dangerous: the usurper rules and the heir's birth is unknown.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","kurippu","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 384","lb":"CC384","ev":"The teacher checks the grove for spies and, seated under an ashoka, bids Civakan listen.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:385":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":385,"t":["வையகம் உடைய மன்னன் சச்சந்தன் அவற்குத் தேவி","பை விரி பசும்பொன் அல்குல் பைந்தொடி விசையை என்பாள்","செய் கழல் மன்னன் தேர்ந்து தேவியைப் பொறியில் போக்கி","மையல் கொள் நெஞ்சில் கல்லா மந்திரி விழுங்கப் பட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher begins: Saccandan was king of the world, and his queen was Vijayai; the king, having thought it through, sent the queen away on the machine, and was swallowed by the unlearned minister of deluded heart.","n":"The teacher's retelling compresses the whole of the king's fall into one verse and lays it on the minister's lack of learning (கல்லா மந்திரி) and delusion, not on the king's pleasure or on fate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","amaichu","kallaamai","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 385","lb":"CC385","ev":"The teacher tells Civakan how his father Saccandan sent Queen Vijayai away on the flying machine and was killed by his minister.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","விசயை","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:17"},"cc:386":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":386,"t":["புலம்பொடு தேவி போகிப் புகற்கு அருங் காடு நண்ணி","வலம்புரி உலகம் விற்கும் மா மணி ஈன்றது என்ன","இலங்கு இழை சிறுவன் தன்னைப் பயந்து பூந் தவிசின் உச்சி","நலம் புரி நங்கை வைத்து நல் அறம் காக்க என்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Continuing the account of Civakan's birth, the teacher tells how the grieving queen went into a forest hard to enter, bore a shining son as a right-whorled conch bears a priceless gem, laid him on a bed of flowers and commended him to good dharma.","n":"The teacher's version says only புகற்கு அருங் காடு, a forest hard to enter, where the patikam (9) names the burning ground; the mother's last act is to hand the child to aram rather than to any person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","aram","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 386","lb":"CC386","ev":"In the teacher's account, Vijayai gives birth in the forest, sets the infant on a flower bed and leaves him with the words நல் அறம் காக்க.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:387":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":387,"t":["வானத்தின் வழுக்கித் திங்கள் கொழுந்து மீன் குழாங்கள் சூழக்","கானத்தில் கிடந்ததே போல் கடல் அகம் உடைய நம்பி","தானத்து மணியும் தானும் இரட்டுறத் தோன்றி னானே","ஊனத்தில் தீர்ந்த சீர்த்தி உத்திரட்டாதி யானே."],"p":"","r":"The child lying in the forest is likened to the young crescent slipped from the sky and lying amid clustering stars; the lord of the sea-girt earth appeared shining doubly with the gem left beside him, born under the star Uttirattathi, of fame without flaw.","n":"தானத்து மணியும் தானும் இரட்டுறத் தோன்றினானே is taken as the infant and a gem set by him shining together; which gem is meant is not stated in the verse. The naming of the birth star prepares the horoscope praise of 404.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 387","lb":"CC387","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:388":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":388,"t":["அருந் தவன் முந்து கூற அலங்கல் வேல் நாய்கன் சென்று","பொருந்துபு சிறுவன் கொண்டு பொலிவொடு புகன்று போகத்","திருந்திய நம்பி ஆரத் தும்மினன் தெய்வம் வாழ்த்திற்று","அரும் பொனாய் கேண்மோ என்றான் அலை கடல் விருப்பில் கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the great ascetic had foretold, the merchant with the garlanded spear went and gladly took up the boy; as he left the child sneezed and a deity spoke a blessing, and the merchant, saying 'listen, my precious one', gave him to his wife, who took him with a love as wide as the sea.","n":"The sneeze answered by a blessing is the household custom of saying 'live long' at a sneeze, here spoken by a deity, so the foundling's first welcome comes from heaven. Whether this is the same deity who stood by Vijayai in the patikam (10) is not said; the one addressed as அரும் பொனாய் is taken to be the merchant's wife.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","anbu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 388","lb":"CC388","ev":"Kantukkatan takes the infant from the forest as an ascetic had foretold, a deity blesses the child's sneeze, and his wife receives him.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","சீவகன்","தெய்வம்","சுநந்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:389":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":389,"t":["கரியவன் கன்னற்கு அன்று பிறப்பினைத் தேற்றி ஆங்கு அப்","பெரியவன் யாவன் என்ன நீ எனப் பேசலோடும்","சொரி மலர்த் தாரும் பூணும் ஆரமும் குழையும் சோரத்","திரு மலர்க் கண்ணி சிந்தத் தெருமந்து மயங்கி வீழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"As Krishna once revealed to Karna his birth, the teacher, asked who that great one was, answered 'you', and at the word Civakan's garland, ornaments, chain and earrings slipped, his flower chaplet scattered, and he reeled, lost his senses and fell.","n":"The simile (கரியவன் கன்னற்கு) brings in the Mahabharata scene where Karna learns his royal birth from Krishna: a foundling raised by a lowlier house who learns he is a king's son, the comparison hinting at the claim to a throne that the news now lays on Civakan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 389","lb":"CC389","ev":"The teacher tells Civakan that the child of the story is himself, and Civakan faints.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:390":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":390,"t":["கற்பகம் கலங்கி வீழ்ந்த வண்ணம் போல் காளை வீழச்","சொல் பகர் புலவன் வல்லே தோன்றலைச் சார்ந்து புல்லி","நல் பல குழீஇய தம்மால் நவை அறத் தேற்றத் தேறிக்","கல் புனை திணி திண் தோளான் கவலை நீர்க் கடலுள் பட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The youth fell like a shaken wish-granting tree; the teacher, skilled in speech, quickly went to him, embraced him and consoled him with many good words till he recovered, but the strong-shouldered prince then sank into a sea of grief.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 390","lb":"CC390","ev":"The teacher revives the fainting Civakan, who wakes into grief.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:391":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":391,"t":["இனையை நீ ஆயது எல்லாம் எம்மனோர் செய்த பாவம்","நினையல் நீ நம்பி என்று நெடுங் கண் நீர் துடைத்து நீவிப்","புனை இழை மகளிர் போலப் புலம்பல் நின் பகைவன் நின்றான்","நினைவு எலாம் நீங்குக என்ன நெடும் தகை தேறினானே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher wipes Civakan's long eyes and tells him that what has befallen him is the sin of such as himself, that he must not brood, nor lament like jewelled women while his enemy still stands, and must put all these thoughts away; and the noble one is comforted.","n":"எம்மனோர் செய்த பாவம் can mean the sin of people like the speaker or, more loosely, a sin done by 'us', that is by those bound up in the story; either way the teacher names sin, not only Kattiyankaran, as the cause. The consolation does two things at once: it gives karma as the cause and then points to the living enemy, and the second half rouses the anger the next verses must check. The remark that lament is for women is the speaker's, reported as the epic gives it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pagai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 391","lb":"CC391","ev":"The teacher dries Civakan's tears and turns his grief toward the enemy who still lives.","cl":[["a misfortune that befalls one is the fruit of sin committed","general","asserted",1,"accanandi"],["lament befits women, not a man whose enemy lives","general","reported",3,"accanandi"]],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:392":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":392,"t":["மலை பக இடிக்கும் சிங்கம் மடங்கலின் மூழங்கி மாநீர்","அலை கடல் திரையின் சீறி அவன் உயிர் பருகல் உற்றுச்","சிலையொடு பகழி ஏந்திக் கூற்று எனச் சிவந்து தோன்றும்","இலை உடைக் கண்ணியானை இன்னணம் விலக்கினானே."],"p":"","r":"Roaring like a mountain-splitting lion, raging like the waves of the sea, bent on drinking his enemy's life, bow and arrow raised and red as Death, the prince in the leaf garland was held back by the teacher in the manner that follows.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","pagai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 392","lb":"CC392","ev":"Civakan takes up his bow to go and kill Kattiyankaran, and the teacher stops him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:393":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":393,"t":["வேண்டுவல் நம்பி யான் ஓர் விழுப் பொருள் என்று சொல்ல","ஆண் தகைக் குரவீர் கொண்மின் யாது நீர் கருதிற்று என்ன","யாண்டு நேர் எல்லை ஆக அவன் திறத்து அழற்சி இன்மை","வேண்டுவல் என்று சொன்னான் வில் வலான் அதனை நேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says he asks one great gift; Civakan bids him take whatever he has in mind; the teacher asks that for the span of one year Civakan bear no anger against that man, and the bowman agrees.","n":"The request is made as a teacher's due, so Civakan cannot refuse it; it binds the time of the revenge, not its end, and the anger is to be suspended (அழற்சி இன்மை) for a fixed term (யாண்டு நேர் எல்லை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","porai","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 393","lb":"CC393","ev":"As his fee the teacher asks Civakan to hold back his anger against Kattiyankaran for a year, and Civakan promises.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:394":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":394,"t":["வெவ் வினை வெகுண்டு சாரா விழுநிதி அமிர்தம் இன்னீர்","கவ்விய எஃகின் நின்ற கயக்கமில் நிலைமை நோக்கி","அவ்வியம் அகன்று பொங்கும் அழல் படு வெகுளி நீக்கி","இவ் இயல் ஒருவற்கு உற்றது இற்றெனக் கிளக்கல் உற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing Civakan's unshaken firmness, like that of tempered steel, a treasure that harsh deeds dare not approach, the teacher, his own disquiet gone and the flaring anger quenched, began to tell what had befallen a certain man.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are dense: அமிர்தம் இன்னீர் கவ்விய எஃகின் is read as steel that has drunk its quenching water, and வெவ் வினை வெகுண்டு சாரா as a treasure that harsh deeds do not come near; other construals are possible. The story is told of ஒருவன், and only in 397 does the teacher say it is himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["porai","vekuli","thitpam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 394","lb":"CC394","ev":"The teacher begins the story of 'a certain man', which is his own.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:395":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":395,"t":["வான் உறை வெள்ளி வெற்பின் வாரணவாசி மன்னன்","ஊன் உறை பருதி வெள் வேல் உலோகமா பாலன் என்பான்","தேன் உறை திருந்து கண்ணிச் சிறுவனுக்கு அரசு நாட்டிப்","பால் நிறக் குருகின் ஆய்ந்து பண்ணவர் படிவம் கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher tells of Lokamapalan, king of Varanavasi on the silver mountain of the sky, who set his young son on the throne and, having weighed it patiently as a heron waits, took on the guise of the ascetics.","n":"பண்ணவர் படிவம் is the outward form of the ascetics of the Jina, so the renunciation is marked as Jain by its dress; the white heron is an image of patient, considered watching, not haste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 395","lb":"CC395","ev":"Lokamapalan, a Vidyadhara king, crowns his son and becomes an ascetic.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:396":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":396,"t":["வெம் சினம் குறைந்து நீங்க விழுத் தவம் தொடங்கி நோற்கும்","வஞ்சம் இல் கொள்கையாற்குப் பாவம் வந்து அடைந்தது ஆகக்","குஞ்சரம் முழங்கு தீயில் கொள்கையின் மெலிந்து இம் மூதூர்","மஞ்சு தோய் குன்றம் அன்ன மாட வீட்டு அகம் புகுந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"This man of guileless resolve, who began a great penance so that fierce anger might wane and leave him, was overtaken by sin: wasted by the hunger-fire that roars like an elephant, he gave way in his vow and came into the mansions of this old city.","n":"The ascetic's penance was undertaken against வெஞ்சினம், the very anger he has just asked Civakan to suspend (393), so his request comes from his own discipline. The verse names no deed as the cause, only that பாவம் வந்து அடைந்தது; the school is Jain as the passage presents it (பண்ணவர் in 395, the people of the Jina's house in 402).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","pasi","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 396","lb":"CC396","ev":"During his penance the ascetic is struck by the elephant-fire hunger and comes to the city to be fed.","cl":[["sin from former deeds can overtake even one who does penance with a guileless mind","jain","asserted",2,"accanandi"]],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:397":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":397,"t":["உரை விளையாமை மைந்தன் கேட்கிய உவந்து நோக்கி","வரை விளையாடு மார்பன் யார் அவன் வாழி என்ன","விரை விளையாடும் தாரோய் யான் என விரும்பித் தீம்பால்","திரை விளை அமிர்தம் அன்ன கட்டுரை செல்க என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the tale before it was finished, the young man looked up gladly and asked who that man was, long may he live; the teacher said 'I am he', and Civakan, delighted, bade him go on with his words sweet as nectar from the milk sea.","n":"The structure doubles the scene of 389: each teller first tells a story in the third person and then says to the listener that its subject is the one before him, Civakan in 389, the teacher in 397.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 397","lb":"CC397","ev":"The teacher reveals that the man of the story is himself, and Civakan asks him to continue.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:398":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":398,"t":["பூத் தின்று புகன்று சேதாப் புணர் முலை பொழிந்த தீம்பால்","நீத்து அறச் செல்ல வேவித்து அட்ட இன் அமிர்தம் உண்பான்","பாத்தரும் பசும் பொன் தாலம் பரப்பிய பைம் பொன் பூமி","ஏத்த அரும் தவிசின் நம்பி தோழரொடு ஏறினானே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher recalls how the boy, to eat the sweet milk-rice cooked from the milk of red cows that graze on flowers, climbed with his companions onto a seat beyond praise on a golden floor spread with golden plates.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 398","lb":"CC398","ev":"The boy Civakan sits down with his friends to a rich meal in his foster father's house.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:399":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":399,"t":["புடை இரு குழையும் மின்னப் பூந்துகில் செறிந்த அல்குல்","நடை அறி மகளிர் ஏந்த நல் அமிர்து உண்ணும் போழ்தின்","இடை கழி நின்ற என்னை நோக்கிப் போந்து ஏறுக என்றான்","கடல் கெழு பருதி அன்ன பொன் கலத்து எனக்கும் இட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"As he ate the nectar-food served by graceful women, he saw the teacher standing in the entrance, came over and said 'come up', and they served the guest too on a golden dish like the sun over the sea.","n":"It is the child, not the householder, who sees the stranger at the door and calls him in.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 399","lb":"CC399","ev":"The boy sees the hungry ascetic at the door and invites him to the meal.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:400":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":400,"t":["கை கவி நறு நெய் பெய்து கன்னல் அம் குடங்கள் கூட்டிப்","பெய் பெய் என்று உரைப்ப யானும் பெருங் கடல் வெள்ளிக் குன்றம்","பெய்து தூர்க்கின்ற வண்ணம் விலாப் புடை பெரிதும் வீங்க","ஐயன் அது அருளினால் யான் அந்தணர் தொழிலன் ஆனேன்."],"p":"","r":"Pouring handfuls of fragrant ghee and bringing pots of jaggery, the boy kept saying 'serve, serve', till the teacher's sides swelled as if the silver mountain were being poured into the great sea to fill it; by the master's grace, he says, he became one who does the work of the learned.","n":"The hunger is the measure of the cure: what is poured in is a mountain into the sea. அந்தணர் தொழிலன் ஆனேன் turns the cured ascetic into a teacher, so the meal is the cause of Civakan's education. The verse credits the cure to the boy's grace (ஐயன் அது அருளினால்), a child's gift doing what a vow could not.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","marundhu","arul","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 400","lb":"CC400","ev":"Fed without stint at the boy's bidding, the ascetic is cured of his hunger and becomes a teacher.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:401":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":401,"t":["சுரும்பு உடை அலங்கல் மாலைச் சுநந்தையும் துணைவன் தானும்","விரும்பினர் எதிர் கொண்டு ஓம்ப வேழ வெந்தீயின் நீங்கி","இருந்தனன் ஏம முந் நீர் எறி சுறவு உயர்த்த தோன்றல்","கரும்பு உடைக் காளை அன்ன காளை நின் வலைப் பட்டு என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Sunandai of the bee-thronged garland and her husband welcomed and cared for him, and freed from the elephant-fire he stayed on, caught, he tells Civakan, in the net of a youth like the god of love with the sugarcane bow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 401","lb":"CC401","ev":"Kantukkatan and Sunandai keep the cured ascetic in their house, and he stays for love of the boy.","cl":[],"ag":["சுநந்தை","கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:402":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":402,"t":["நிலம் பொறுக்கலாத செம் பொன் நீள் நிதி நுந்தை இல்லம்","நலம் பொறுக்கலாத பிண்டி நான் முகன் தமர்கட்கு எல்லாம்","உலம் பொறுக்கலாத தோளாய் ஆதலால் ஊடு புக்கேன்","கலம் பொறுக்கலாத சாயல் அவர் உழை நின்னைக் கண்டேன்."],"p":"","r":"Your father's house, he says, holds red-gold wealth the earth could not bear and is open to all the people of the four-faced Lord under the Pindi tree; therefore he entered it, and there saw the boy among the women.","n":"பிண்டி நான் முகன் is the Jina seated under the asoka (பிண்டி), and தமர் his followers: the foster home is a Jain household, and its hospitality to ascetics is why the meeting happened. நுந்தை here is Kantukkatan, the foster father.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 402","lb":"CC402","ev":"The teacher explains that he came to the merchant's house because it received the Jina's devotees.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:403":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":403,"t":["ஐயனைக் கண்ணில் காண யானைத்தீ அதகம் கண்ட","பை அணல் நாகம் போல வட்க யான் பெரிதும் உட்கித்","தெய்வம் கொல் என்று தேர்வேற்கு அமிர்து உலாய் நிமிர்ந்ததே போல்","மொய் குரல் முரசம் நாணும் தழங்கு குரல் முழங்கக் கேட்டேன்."],"p":"","r":"When he saw the boy, the elephant-fire within him shrank like a hooded snake that has seen the antidote; awed, wondering whether this was a god, he heard a resounding sound, as of nectar rising, that would put a war drum to shame.","n":"The sound of line 4 is not identified: it may be the boy's voice, or a sign that marks him; read as the teacher's sign that the child was more than human. அதகம் is the antidote that subdues a snake.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["noy","marundhu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 403","lb":"CC403","ev":"At the sight of the boy the ascetic's disease abates and he hears a sound like a drum.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:404":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":404,"t":["கோட்டு இளந் திங்கள் சூழ்ந்து குலவிய திருவில் போல","மோட்டு ஒளி முத்தம் சூழ்ந்து முருகு கொப்பளிக்கும் தாரோய்","கேட்டு அளப் பரிய சொல்லும் கிளர் ஒளி வனப்பும் நின்னைச்","சேட்டு இளஞ் சிங்கம் அன்னாய் சாதகம் செய்த என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He tells the young lion, whose garland of pearls rings him like a rainbow about the young crescent, that his words beyond measure and his bright beauty were made by his horoscope.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","azhagu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 404","lb":"CC404","ev":"","cl":[["the birth chart shapes a person's eloquence and beauty","general","reported",4,"accanandi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:405":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":405,"t":["கோள் இயங்கு உழுவை அன்ன கொடும் சிலை உழவன் கேட்டே","தாள் இயல் தவங்கள் தாயாத் தந்தை நீ ஆகி என்னை","வாள் இயங்கு உருவப் பூணோய் படைத்தனை வாழி என்ன","மீளி அம் களிறு அனாய் யான் மெய்ந்நெறி நிற்பல் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The tiger-like bowman, hearing this, says that with penance as mother and the teacher as father he has been created anew, and blesses him; the teacher replies that he will now stand in the true path.","n":"The prince who has just learned that his mother left him in the forest and that he was raised by a foster house now names a third parentage, the teacher's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","nanri","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 405","lb":"CC405","ev":"Civakan calls the teacher his father, and the teacher announces that he will return to the ascetic path.","cl":[["a teacher who forms one is a father, and his penance a mother","general","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:406":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":406,"t":["மறு அற மனையின் நீங்கி மா தவம் செய்வல் என்றால்","பிற அறம் அல்ல பேசார் பேர் அறிவு உடைய நீரார்","துறவறம் புணர்க என்றே தோன்றல் தாள் தொழுது நின்றான்","நறவு அற மலர்ந்த கண்ணி நல் மணி வண்ணன் அன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"If one says 'I will leave home without stain and do great penance', the teacher says, the greatly wise speak of no other dharma but bid him take up renunciation; and he stood worshipping the prince's feet.","n":"Who speaks lines 1 to 3, and who is நல் மணி வண்ணன் அன்னான் in line 4, is not fixed by the verse; it is read as the teacher asking leave and bowing to the prince he has called master (ஐயன், 400), but it could be Civakan giving leave. துறவறம் is set against பிற அறம், which includes the household life the teacher has lived in the merchant's house.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","ilvaazhkkai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 406","lb":"CC406","ev":"The teacher asks Civakan's leave to renounce and bows to him.","cl":[["when one resolves to leave home for penance, the wise urge only the way of renunciation","general","asserted",2,"accanandi"]],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அச்சணந்தி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:407":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":407,"t":["கை வரை அன்றி நில்லாக் கடுஞ் சின மடங்கல் அன்னான்","தெவ்வரைச் செகுக்கும் நீதி மனத்து அகத்து எழுதிச் செம்பொன்","பை விரி அல்குலாட்கும் படுகடல் நிதியின் வைகும்","மை வரை மார்பினாற்கும் மனம் உறத் தேற்றி இட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Having written into the mind of the lion-like prince, whose fierce anger will not stay in bounds, the policy by which enemies are destroyed, the teacher consoled the merchant's wife and the merchant who dwells among sea-borne wealth.","n":"The construal that the teacher wrote the policy into Civakan's mind, rather than into his own, is the more natural reading of lines 1 and 2 but not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arasu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 407","lb":"CC407","ev":"Before leaving, the teacher instructs Civakan in the policy of defeating enemies and comforts his foster parents.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:408":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":408,"t":["அழல் உறு வெண்ணெய் போல அகம் குழைந்து உருகி ஆற்றாள்","குழல் உறு கிளவி சோர்ந்து குமரனைத் தமியன் ஆக","நிழல் உறு மதியம் அன்னாய் நீத்தியோ எனவும் நில்லான்","பழவினை பரிய நோற்பான் விஞ்சையர் வேந்தன் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Sunandai, melting inwardly like butter by the fire, her flute-like voice failing, asks whether he will leave the boy alone; he would not stay, and the Vidyadhara king went away to do the penance that wears down old deeds.","n":"பழவினை பரிய நோற்பான் names the aim of the renewed penance as wearing away (பரிய) old deeds, the Jain account of release; it closes the account begun in 396, where those deeds broke his first penance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vinai","thavam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 408","lb":"CC408","ev":"Ignoring Sunandai's plea, the teacher leaves to resume his penance.","cl":[["penance wears away the deeds of the past","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["சுநந்தை","அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:18"},"cc:409":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":409,"t":["ஆர்வ வேரரிந் தச்ச ணந்திபோய்","வீரன் றாணிழல் விளங்க நோற்றபின்","மாரி மொக்குளின் மாய்ந்து விண்டொழச்","சோர்வில் கொள்கையான் றோற்றம் நீங்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator tells how Accanandi, having cut out the root of desire, went away and performed austerities in the shade of the Hero's feet, and then, while the gods worshipped, left his embodied form, vanishing like a bubble in the rain.","n":"The ilampakam opens by closing the teacher's story before the hero's adventures begin. The order is threefold: the root of desire is cut, austerity follows, and only then does the body go. வீரன் is the Jina's epithet, so the verse places the release under a Jain master without stating a doctrine in so many words. The bubble is not an image of life's frailty here but of how cleanly the embodied form ends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","thavam","veedu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 409","lb":"CC409","ev":"Accanandi, Civakan's teacher, renounces, performs austerities at the Hero's feet and attains release.","cl":[],"ag":["அச்சணந்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:410":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":410,"t":["நம்ப னித்தலை நாக நன்னகர்ப்","பைம்பொ னோடைசூழ் பரும யானையுஞ்","செம்பொ னீள்கொடித் தேரும் வாசியும்","வேம்ப வூர்ந்துலாம் வேனி லானினே."],"p":"","r":"In this fine city like the city of the Nagas, Civakan rides about on gold-caparisoned elephants, chariots with long red-gold banners and horses, like the god of spring whose passing makes hearts burn.","n":"வேம்ப (so that they burn) makes the hero's display a torment to those who watch him, preparing the next two verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 410","lb":"CC410","ev":"Civakan rides through Rajamapuram on elephant, chariot and horse.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:411":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":411,"t":["கலையின தகலமுங் காட்சிக் கின்பமுஞ்","சிலையின தகலமும் வீணைச் செல்வமு","மலையினி னகலிய மார்ப னல்லதிவ்","வுலகினி னிலையென வொருவ னாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"In breadth of learning, delight to the eye, mastery of the bow and the wealth of the vina, the broad-chested Civakan became one without a second in the world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","azhagu","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 411","lb":"CC411","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:412":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":412,"t":["நாம வென்றிவே னகைகொள் மார்பனைக்","காம னேயெனக் கன்னி மங்கையர்","தாம ரைக்கணாற் பருகத் தாழ்ந்துலாங்","கோம கன்றிறத் துற்ற கூறுவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet announces that he will tell what befell the prince whom the young maidens, taking him for Kama, drank in with their lotus eyes as he went about.","n":"கூறுவாம் is the poet's own voice turning to a new episode; the maidens' gaze sets up the marriage that ends this ilampakam.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 412","lb":"CC412","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:413":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":413,"t":["சில்லம் போதின்மேற் றிரைந்து தேனுலா","முல்லை காரெனப் பூப்ப மொய்ந்நிரை","புல்லு கன்றுளிப் பொழிந்து பால்படுங்","கல்லென் சும்மையோர் கடலின் மிக்கதே."],"p":"","r":"The herds of the land are pictured: jasmine blooms as if the rains had come, the cattle crowd round their calves and pour out milk, and the noise of the herds is greater than the sea's.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 413","lb":"CC413","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:414":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":414,"t":["மிக்க நாளினான் வேழ மும்மத","முக்க தேனினோ டூறி வார்சுனை","யொக்க வாய்நிறைந் தொழுகு குன்றின்மேன்","மக்க ளீண்டினார் மடங்கன் மொய்ம்பினார்."],"p":"","r":"On a hill where elephants' musth mixes with honey and fills the springs to overflowing, men strong as lions gathered.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 414","lb":"CC414","ev":"The hunters gather on the hill.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:415":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":415,"t":["மன்ன வன்னிரை வந்து நண்ணுறு","மின்ன நாளினாற் கோடு நாமெனச்","சொன்ன வாயுளே யொருவன் புட்குரன்","முன்னங் கூறினான் முழுது ணர்வினான்."],"p":"","r":"As the hunters said the king's herd was near and that on an auspicious day they would take it, one among them who knew all, reading the cry of a bird, spoke first.","n":"புட்குரன் is read here as புள் குரல் (the bird's cry) run into முன்னம்; the reader is a hunter who interprets bird-signs, not a royal soothsayer.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 415","lb":"CC415","ev":"The hunters plan to seize the king's herd, and an omen-reader among them speaks.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்","புட்குரல் அறிவான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:416":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":416,"t":["அடைது நாநிரை யடைந்த காலையே","குடையும் பிச்சமு மொழியக் கோன்படை","யுடையும் பின்னரே யொருவன் தேரினா","லுடைதுஞ் சுடுவிற்றே னுடைந்த வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"The omen-reader foretells that they will get the herd, and that when they do the king's army will break, leaving its umbrellas and peacock-feather standards, but that afterwards they themselves will be broken by one man in a chariot, as bees scatter from smoked honey.","n":"The prophecy gives the whole battle in advance, both the raiders' first victory and their defeat by Civakan, who is not named. Line 4 is hard to divide and may be corrupt; the smoked-honey simile is read from தேன் உடைந்த.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","padai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 416","lb":"CC416","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புட்குரல் அறிவான்","மறவர்"],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:417":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":417,"t":["என்று கூறலு மேழை வேட்டுவீ","ரொன்று தேரினா லொருவன் கூற்றமே","யென்று கூறினு மொருவ னென்செயு","மின்று கோடுநா மெழுகென் றேகினார்."],"p":"","r":"When he had said this, a hunter answered his fellows that even if the one man in the chariot were Death himself, what could one man do, and urged them to rise and take the herd that day; and they went.","n":"ஏழை வேட்டுவீர் (foolish hunters) may be the answering hunter scorning his fellows' fear, or the poet's own judgment folded into the speech; either way the boast is set up to be broken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 417","lb":"CC417","ev":"The hunters dismiss the warning and set out for the herd.","cl":[],"ag":["வேட்டுவன்","மறவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேட்டுவன்","மறவர்"],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:418":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":418,"t":["வண்டு மூசறா நறவ மார்ந்தவர்","தொண்ட கப்பறை துடியொ டார்த்தெழ","விண்டு தெய்வதம் வணங்கி வெல்கென","மண்டி னார்நிரை மணந்த காலையே."],"p":"","r":"Having drunk honey-toddy swarming with bees, with tondakam drums and thudi sounding, they bowed to their deity, saying 'may we win', and fell on the herd at daybreak.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","vazhipaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 418","lb":"CC418","ev":"The hunters drink, worship their deity and attack the herd at dawn.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:419":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":419,"t":["பூத்த கோங்குபோற் பொன்சு மந்துளா","ராய்த்தி யர்நலக் காசெ றூணனான்","கோத்த நித்திலக் கோதை மார்பினான்","வாய்த்த வந்நிரை வள்ளு வன்சொனான்."],"p":"","r":"A man with a pearl garland on his chest, among herdswomen bearing gold like the flowering kongu, spoke about that herd: he was the herders' valluvan, the announcer.","n":"Line 2 (ஆய்த்தியர் நலக் காசெறூணனான்) cannot be divided with confidence, so who the valluvan is and what he wears is unclear beyond the pearl garland.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nimittam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 419","lb":"CC419","ev":"The herders' announcer speaks of the herd.","cl":[],"ag":["வள்ளுவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:420":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":420,"t":["பிள்ளை யுள்புகுந் தழித்த தாதலா","லெள்ளன் மின்னிரை யின்று நீரென","வெள்ளி வள்ளியின் விளங்கு தோணலார்","முள்கு மாயரும் மொய்ம்பொ டேகினார்."],"p":"","r":"He warned that an ill-omened bird had entered and spoiled the day, so they should not scorn it and should keep the herd in today; yet the herdsmen, shoulders shining like silver bracelets, went out with the cattle in their strength.","n":"பிள்ளை is taken as the name of an omen bird. The herders, like the hunters in 417, hear an omen and go ahead.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 420","lb":"CC420","ev":"The herdsmen take the herd out despite the announcer's warning of an ill omen.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வள்ளுவன்","ஆயர்"],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:421":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":421,"t":["காய மீனெனக் கலந்து கானிரை","மேய வெந்தொழில் வேட ரார்த்துடன்","பாய மாரிபோற் பகழி சிந்தினார்","ஆயர் மத்தெறி தயிரி னாயினார்."],"p":"","r":"While the herd grazed scattered in the forest like the stars, the hunters of cruel trade shouted together and loosed arrows like rain, and the herdsmen were churned like curd under the churning stick.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","pagai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 421","lb":"CC421","ev":"The hunters attack the grazing herd with arrows and throw the herdsmen into confusion.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்","ஆயர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:422":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":422,"t":["குழலு நவியமு மொழியக் கோவலர்","கழலக் காடுபோய்க் கன்று தாம்பரிந்","துழலை பாய்ந்துலா முன்றிற் பள்ளியுண்","மழலைத் தீஞ்சொலார் மறுக வாய்விட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Dropping flute and axe, the cowherds fled through the forest; the calves broke their tethers and ran round the yards, and in the hamlets the sweet-voiced women were thrown into confusion and cried out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 422","lb":"CC422","ev":"The cowherds flee and the hamlet is thrown into uproar.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயர்","ஆய்த்தியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:423":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":423,"t":["மத்தம் புல்லிய கயிற்றின் மற்றவ","ரத்த லைவிடி னித்த லைவிடா","ருய்த்த னரென வுடைத யிர்ப்புளி","மொய்த்த தோணலார் முழுதும் ஈண்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"Saying that the hunters had driven the herd off, as a churning rope let go at one end is not let go at the other, the women smelling of broken sour curd all crowded together.","n":"The churning-rope simile is hard to fix: it may picture the cattle pulled one way while the women hold the other, or the unbroken report passed from mouth to mouth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 423","lb":"CC423","ev":"The herdswomen gather in alarm at the news that the cattle are driven off.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்த்தியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:424":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":424,"t":["வலைப்படு மானென மஞ்ஞை யெனத்தம்","முலைப்படு முத்தொடு மொய்குழல் வேய்ந்த","தலைப்படு தண்மலர் மாலை பிணங்க","வலைத்த வயிற்றின ராயழு திட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Like deer caught in a net, like peacocks, the herdswomen, the pearls on their breasts and the cool garlands in their hair tangled together, beat their bellies and wept.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 424","lb":"CC424","ev":"The herdswomen weep and beat themselves.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்த்தியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:425":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":425,"t":["எம்மனை மாரினி யெங்ஙனம் வாழ்குவிர்","நும்மனை மார்களை நோவ வதுக்கி","வெம்முனை வேட்டுவ ருய்த்தன ரோவெனத்","தம்மனைக் கன்றொடு தாம்புலம் புற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The herdswomen lamented with the calves of their houses, asking them how they would live now that the hunters of the cruel border had driven off their mothers in pain.","n":"The women address the calves as orphans: the loss is framed as a family's, mothers taken from children, not as property.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 425","lb":"CC425","ev":"The herdswomen grieve over the calves whose mothers were taken.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்த்தியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்த்தியர்",""],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:426":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":426,"t":["பாறை படுதயிர் பாலோடு நெய்பொரு","தாறு படப்பள்ளி யாகுல மாக","மாறு படமலைந் தாய்ப்படை நெக்கது","சேறு படமலர் சிந்த விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"With curd, milk and ghee spilled on the rocks like a river and the hamlet in tumult, the herdsmen's force fought back and broke, scattering flowers in the mud as it hurried away.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 426","lb":"CC426","ev":"The herdsmen's attempt to fight back fails.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:427":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":427,"t":["புறவணி பூவிரி புன்புலம் போகி","நறவணி தாமரை நாட்டக நீந்திச்","சுறவணி சூழ்கிடங் காரெயின் மூதூ","ரிறையணிக் கேட்கவுய்த் திட்டனர் பூசல்"],"p":"","r":"Passing through the flowered pasture and crossing the lotus-filled farmland, they carried their outcry to the king of the old city girt with a shark-filled moat and a strong wall.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 427","lb":"CC427","ev":"The herdsmen go to the city to bring their complaint to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:428":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":428,"t":["கொடுமர வெயின ரீண்டிக் கோட்டிமி லேறு சூழ்ந்த","படுமணி நிரையை வாரிப் பைந்துகி லருவி நெற்றி","நெடுமலை யத்தஞ் சென்றா ரென்றுநெய் பொதிந்த பித்தை","வடிமல ராயர் பூசல் வளநகர் பரப்பி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The herdsmen with oiled hair and flower garlands spread the cry through the rich city that the bow-bearing hunters had gathered, swept off the belled herd with its humped bulls and gone up the long hill path where waterfalls hang like silk.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 428","lb":"CC428","ev":"The herdsmen spread the news of the raid through the city.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆயர்",""],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:429":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":429,"t":["காசின் மாமணிச் சாமரை கன்னியர்","வீச மாமக ரக்குழை வில்லிட","வாச வான்கழு நீர்பிடித் தாங்கரி","யாச னத்திருந் தான்டன் மொய்ம்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The strong-shouldered king sat on the lion throne, maidens fanning him with jewelled chamaras, his great makara earrings flashing, holding a fragrant blue water lily.","n":"The king is not named, but 438 shows the complaint reaching Kattiyankaran, the usurper; the picture of him fanned and holding a flower while his herds are being driven away is set against the herders' outcry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 429","lb":"CC429","ev":"The king sits at ease on his throne as the outcry reaches the city.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:19"},"cc:430":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":430,"t":["கொண்ட வாளொடுங் கோலொடுங் கூப்புபு","சண்ட மன்னனைத் தாடொழு தாயிடை","யுண்டொர் பூசலென் றாற்குரை யாயெனக்","கொண்ட னர்நிரை போற்றெனக் கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"A herdsman, sword and herding staff in hand, joins his palms and bows at the fierce king's feet; told to say what the uproar is, he reports that the herd has been seized and begs the king to protect it.","n":"The appeal கொண்டனர் நிரை போற்று puts the protection of the herd on the king, which is the duty the rest of the chapter shows him failing and Civakan taking up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 430","lb":"CC430","ev":"A herdsman reports to Kattiyangaran that hunters have seized the herd and asks him to protect it.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயர்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:431":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":431,"t":["செங்கட் புன்மயிர்த் தோறிரை செம்முக","வெங்க ணோக்கிற்குப் பாய மிலேச்சனைச்","செங்கட் டீவிழி யாத்தெழித் தான்கையு","ளங்கட் போது பிசைந்தடு கூற்றனான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, eyes reddening and blazing, shouts at a jacketed Mleccha with red eyes, coarse hair and a fierce face, crushing the flower in his hand like killing Death.","n":"Who the Mleccha is, an attendant or officer of the court, is not said; குப் பாய is read as குப்பாயம், a jacket, across the printed word break.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 431","lb":"CC431","ev":"Kattiyangaran, enraged, shouts at a Mleccha in his court.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்","மிலேச்சன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:432":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":432,"t":["கூற்றி னிடிக்குங் கொலைவேலவன் கோவ லர்வாய","மாற்ற முணர்ந்து மறங்கூர்கடற் றானை நோக்கிக்","காற்றின் விரைந்து தொறுமீட்கெனக் காவல் மன்ன","னேற்றை யரிமா னிடிபோல வியம்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Understanding the herdsmen's words, the guardian king with the killing spear looks at his sea-like army and roars like a lion's thunder that they must go swift as wind and recover the herd.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 432","lb":"CC432","ev":"Kattiyangaran orders his army to go quickly and recover the herd.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:433":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":433,"t":["கார்விளை மேக மன்ன கவுளழி கடாத்த வேழம்","போர்விளை யிவுளித் திண்டேர் புனைமயிர்ப் புரவி காலாள்","வார்விளை முரசம் விம்ம வானுலாப் போந்த தேபோ","னீர்டிவிளை சுரிசங் கார்ப்ப நிலநெளி பரந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The army sets out: musth elephants like rain clouds, war chariots, plumed horses and foot soldiers, with drums booming and conches roaring, as if the sky were marching, and the earth bends under it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 433","lb":"CC433","ev":"Kattiyangaran's army marches out with all four arms.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:434":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":434,"t":["காலகம் புடைப்ப முந்நீர்க் கடல்கிளர்ந் தெழுந்த தேபோல்","வேலக மிடைந்த தானை வெஞ்சின வெயினர் தாக்க","வால்வளை யலற வாய்விட் டிரலையுந் துடியு மார்ப்பப்","பால்வளைந் திரவு செற்றுப் பகலொடு மலைவ தொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Like the sea rising when the wind beats on it, the spear-thick army meets the fierce hunters' attack amid wailing conches, horns and tudi drums, so that the dark hunters and the army seem like night closing round to fight the day.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 434","lb":"CC434","ev":"The hunters attack the king's army.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை","மறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:435":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":435,"t":["விற்பழுத் துமிழ்ந்த வெய்ய வெந்நுனைப் பகழி மைந்தர்","மற்பழுத் தகன்ற மார்பத் திடங்கொண்டு வைகச் செந்நாச்","சொற்பழுத் தவர்க்கு மாண்மை சொல்லலாந் தன்மைத் தன்றிக்","கொற்பழுத் தெரியும் வேலார் கொடுஞ்சிலை குழைவித் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The hunters' fierce arrows lodge in the broad chests of the king's warriors, whose manhood proves to be a thing only the red-tongued talkers speak of, and the spearmen's bows go slack.","n":"Line 3 is read as the narrator's sarcasm that the army's valour was only boast; whose bows are made to sag in line 4 (the army's own, or the hunters' bent to shoot) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 435","lb":"CC435","ev":"The hunters' arrows strike down the king's warriors, who falter.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்","சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:436":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":436,"t":["வாட்படை யனுங்க வேடர் மாற்றவண்சிலை வளைய வாங்கிக்","கோட்புலி யினத்தின் மொய்த்தார் கொதிநுனைப் பகழி தம்மால்","வீட்டினார் மைந்தர் தம்மை விளிந்தமா கவிழ்ந்த திண்டேர்","பாட்டரும் பகடு வீழ்ந்த பனிவரை குனிவ தொத்தே."],"p":"","r":"As the swordsmen weaken, the hunters swarm like a pack of tigers bending strong bows and destroy the warriors with burning-tipped arrows, leaving dead horses, overturned chariots and fallen elephants like bowed snowy hills.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 436","lb":"CC436","ev":"The hunters slaughter the king's warriors, horses and elephants.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்","சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:437":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":437,"t":["வென்றிநாங் கோடு மின்னே வெள்ளிடைப் படுத்தென் றெண்ணி","யொன்றியுள் வாங்கு கென்ன வொலிகட லுடைந்த தேபோற்","பொன்றவழ் களிறு பாய்மா புனைமயிற் குஞ்சி பிச்சம்","மின்றவழ் கொடியொ டிட்டு வேற்படை யுடைந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Thinking that they will win by drawing the enemy into open ground, the army's men call to fall back together, and the spear-army breaks like a roaring sea, leaving behind gold-hung elephants, horses, peacock plumes, parasols and banners.","n":"The speaker of the call in lines 1 and 2 is not named; read so, the tactical withdrawal is the cause of the rout.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","soozhchi","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 437","lb":"CC437","ev":"The king's army pulls back to draw the hunters into open ground, and the retreat becomes a rout.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:438":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":438,"t":["பல்லினாற் சுகிர்ந்த நாரிற் பனிமலர் பயிலப் பெய்த","முல்லையங் கண்ணி சிந்தக் கால்விசை முறுக்கி யாய","ரொல்லென வொலிப்ப வோடிப் படையுடைந் திட்ட தென்ன","வல்லலுற் றழுங்கி நெஞ்சிற் கட்டியங் கார னாழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The herdsmen run with all their speed, the mullai garlands they had strung on fibre stripped with their teeth scattering, crying out that the army is routed, and Kattiyangaran sinks into grief.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 438","lb":"CC438","ev":"The herdsmen bring news of the rout and Kattiyangaran is plunged in grief.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயர்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:439":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":439,"t":["வம்புகொண் டிருந்த மாதர் வனமுலை மாலைத் தேன்சோர்","கொம்புகொண் டன்ன நல்லார் கொழுங்கயற் றடங்கண் போலு","மம்புகொண் டரசர் மீண்டா ராக்கொண்டு மறவர் போனார்","செம்புகொண் டன்ன விஞ்சித் திருநகர்ச் செல்வ வென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"They tell the lord of the rich city with copper-like walls that his chiefs have come back carrying arrows like the carp eyes of the women they love, and the warriors have gone off with the cows.","n":"The simile turns the wounds into the love-wounds of women's eyes, a mocking report of an army that fled; the speakers are taken to be the herdsmen of 438.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 439","lb":"CC439","ev":"The king is told that his chiefs returned wounded and the hunters kept the cows.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை","மறவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆயர்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:440":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":440,"t":["மன்னிரை பெயர்த்து மைந்தர் வந்தனர் கொள்க வாட்கட்","பொன்னிழை சுடரு மேனிப் பூங்கொடி யனைய பொற்பிற்","கன்னியைத் தருது மென்று கடிமுர சியம்பக் கொட்டி","நன்னகர் வீதி தோறு நந்தகோ னறைவித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandagon has the marriage drum beaten in every street of the city, proclaiming that to the warriors who bring back the king's herd he will give the maiden with sword-like eyes, like a golden creeper.","n":"Where the king's army has failed, the herd chief turns the recovery into a bride contest, the pattern of Civakan's other marriages, which each come as a prize won.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 440","lb":"CC440","ev":"Nandagon proclaims his daughter as the prize for whoever recovers the herd.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:441":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":441,"t":["வெதிர்ங்குதைச் சாபங் கான்ற வெந்நுனைப் பகழி மூழ்க","வுதிர்ந்தது சேனை யீட்டங் கூற்றொடு பொருது கொள்ளுங்","கருந்தடங் கண்ணி யன்றிக் காயமா றாக வேகு","மரும்பெற லவளு மாகென் றாடவர் தொழுது விட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Remembering how the army's host scattered under the hunters' arrows, the men of the city bow and decline, saying that not only the dark-eyed maiden to be won by fighting Death, but even a celestial woman hard to win would not tempt them.","n":"காயம் ஆறாக ஏகும் is read as one who travels by the sky, a heavenly woman; the line is compressed and the refusal is inferred from தொழுது விட்டார்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 441","lb":"CC441","ev":"The city's men refuse the challenge despite the bride offered.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆடவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆடவர்",""],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:442":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":442,"t":["கார்விரி மின்ன னார்மேற் காமுகர் நெஞ்சி னோடுந்","தேர்பரி கடாவித் தேந்தார்ச் சீவக னருளிற் போகித்","தார்பொலி புரவி வட்டந் தான்புகக் காட்டு கின்றாற்","கூர்பரி வுற்ற தெல்லா மொருமகன் உணர்த் தினானே."],"p":"","r":"As the lustful men's hearts follow the lightning-like girl, Civakan is out driving his chariot horses and showing the circling paces of his garlanded horse when a man tells him all that has caused the great distress.","n":"அருளிற் போகி may mean Civakan goes out of compassion or that another goes by his favour; the subject of காட்டுகின்றான் is taken to be Civakan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 442","lb":"CC442","ev":"A man tells Civakan, at his horse exercise, of the raid and the rout.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","ஒருமகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:443":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":443,"t":["தன்பான் மனையா ளயலான்றலைக் கண்டு பின்னு","மின்பா லடிசிற் கிவர்கின்றகைப் பேடி போலா","நன்பால் பசுவே துறந்தார்பெண்டிர் பாலர் பார்ப்பா","ரென்பாரை யோம்பே னெனின்யானவனாக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan swears that if he does not protect those the world names, the good cows, renunciants, women, children and brahmins, let him become like the eunuch who, having seen his wife in another man's arms, still hurries to his sweet rice.","n":"The vow has the shape of the Sangam vanjinam, a self-curse on failure, but its curse is the shame of a பேடி who swallows dishonour for food; the list of the protected is the classical one, given here by a merchant's son and not the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","maanam","niraikol","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 443","lb":"CC443","ev":"Civakan vows to recover the herd.","cl":[["cows, renunciants, women, children and brahmins have a claim on a warrior's protection, and failing them is unmanly shame","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:444":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":444,"t":["போர்ப்பண் ணமைத்து நுகம்பூட்டிப் புரவி பண்ணித்","தேர்ப்பண் ணமைத்துச் சிலைகோலிப் பகழி யாய்ந்து","கார்க் கொண்மு மின்னி னிமிர்ந்தான்கலி மான்கு ளம்பிற்","பார்க்கண் ணெழுந்த துகளாற் பகன் மாய்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He makes ready the war gear, yokes and harnesses the horses, fits out the chariot, strings the bow and chooses arrows, and rises like lightning from a cloud, and the dust from his horses' hooves blots out the sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","ookkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 444","lb":"CC444","ev":"Civakan arms himself and drives out against the hunters.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:445":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":445,"t":["இழுதொன்று வாட்க ணிளையாரிளை யார்க ணோக்கிற்","பழுதின்றி மூழ்கும் பகழித்தொழில் வல்ல காளை","முழுதென்று திண்டேர் முகஞ்செய்தவன் றன்னொ டேற்கும்","பொழுதன்று போது மெனப்புண்மொழிந் தான்மொ ழிந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"As the youth whose arrows sink as flawlessly as young women's glances turns his strong chariot to face them, one hunter speaks a mean word, that this is no time to engage him and they should go.","n":"முழுது என்று and the doubled மொழிந்தான் are hard to construe; புண்மொழி may stand for புன்மொழி, a mean word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 445","lb":"CC445","ev":"Civakan turns his chariot on the hunters, and one of them urges retreat.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மறவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மறவர்",""],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:446":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":446,"t":["மோட்டும் முதுநீர் முதலைக்கு வலிய துண்டேல்","காட்டுண் ணமக்கு வலியாரையுங் காண்டும் நாமென்","றேட்டைப் பசியி னிரைகவ்விய நாக மேபோல்","வேட்டந் நிரையை விடலின்றி விரைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Saying that if the crocodile is strong in deep old water, in the forest they will see off even the strong, the hunting band, like a hungry snake that has seized its prey, hurries on without letting the herd go.","n":"The hunters take the crocodile maxim for themselves: the forest is their deep water. The story keeps them in their forest and still defeats them (453).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali","niraikol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 446","lb":"CC446","ev":"The hunters refuse to give up the herd and press on.","cl":[["each is strongest on its own ground, the crocodile in water and the forest people in the forest","general","reported",1,"cattle_raiders"]],"ag":["மறவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மறவர்",""],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:447":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":447,"t":["கடற்படை யனுங்க வென்ற கானவ ரென்னுங் கூற்றத்","திடைப்படா தோடிப் போமி னுய்யவென் றிரலை வாய்வைத்","தெடுத்தனர் விளியுஞ் சங்கும் வீளையும் பறையுங் கோடுங்","கடத்திடை முழங்கக் காருங் கடலுமொத் தெழுந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Putting horns to their mouths, the hunters cry that the enemy should run and save themselves rather than fall into the Death that is the foresters who routed the sea-like army, and their calls, conches, whistles, drums and horns roar through the wasteland like cloud and sea rising together.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 447","lb":"CC447","ev":"The hunters raise a war cry and taunt Civakan's party to flee.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மறவர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:448":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":448,"t":["கைவிசை முறுக்கி வீசுங் கொள்ளியுங் கறங்கு மேய்ப்பச்","செய்கழற் குருசில் திண்டேர் விசையொடு திசைக ளெல்லா","மையென வளைப்ப வீர ரார்த்தன ரவரு மார்த்தார்","மொய்யமர் நாட்செய் தையன் முதல்விளை யாடி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Like whirled firebrands, the anklet-wearing lord's chariot circles so fast it seems to ring every direction in darkness; his heroes shout and the hunters shout back, and the lord plays the opening game of the crowded battle.","n":"முதல் விளையாடினான் makes the battle Civakan's play, against the army's terror before the same men.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","ookkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 448","lb":"CC448","ev":"Civakan opens the fight, wheeling his chariot round the hunters.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:449":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":449,"t":["ஆழியா னூர்திப் புள்ளி னஞ்சிற கொலியி னாகம்","மாழ்கிப்பை யவிந்த வண்ணம் வள்ளறேர் முழக்கி னானுஞ்","சூழ்துகண் மயக்கத் தானும் புளி ஞருள் சுருங்கிச் சேக்கைக்","கோழிபோற் குறைந்து நெஞ்சி னறமென மறமும் விட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"As snakes faint and fold their hoods at the sound of the wings of Garuda, mount of the discus-bearer, so the hunters, under the roar of the generous one's chariot and the whirl of dust, shrink like coop hens and give up valour from their hearts, as they had given up dharma.","n":"அறமென மறமும் விட்டார் pairs the two losses: men who had no dharma now lose their courage too, so the raiders' fall is given a moral as well as a martial cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","aram","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 449","lb":"CC449","ev":"The hunters lose heart before Civakan's chariot.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:450":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":450,"t":["புள்ளொன்றே சொல்லு மென்றிப் புன்றலை வேடன் பொய்த்தான்","வெள்ளந்தேர் வளைந்த நம்மை வென்றியீங் கரிது வெய்தா","வுள்ளம்போற் போது நாமோ ரெடுப்பெடுத் துய்ய வென்னா","வள்ளன்மே லப்பு மாரி யார்ப்பொடு சிதறி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The hunters say that the scrawny hunter who told them the bird-omen alone decides has lied, that victory is hard now that the flood of chariots surrounds them, and that they must make one charge fast as thought and escape; and with a shout they shower arrows on the generous one.","n":"புள் ஒன்றே சொல்லும் is read as the omen-reader's assurance; the omen that drove the raid fails them, and the blame falls on the augur, not on the raid.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","pedhaimai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 450","lb":"CC450","ev":"The hunters blame their omen-reader and charge Civakan with a rain of arrows.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்","வேடன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மறவர்",""],"ch":"cc:20"},"cc:451":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":451,"t":["மால்வரைத் தொடுத்து வீழ்ந்த மணிநிற மாரி தன்னைக்","காலிரைத் தெழுந்து பாறக் கல்லெனப் புடைத்த தேபோன்","மேனிரைத் தெழுந்த வேடர் வெந்நுனை யப்பு மாரி","கோனிரைத் துமிழும் வில்லாற் கோமகன் விலக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As wind rising in a line blows apart the dark rain that falls clinging to a great hill, the prince with his bow, which spits arrows in a row, wards off the rain of hot-tipped arrows the hunters loose in ranks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","niraikol","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 451","lb":"CC451","ev":"The hunters shower arrows on Civakan, and he parries them with his own arrows.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:452":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":452,"t":["கானவ ரிரிய வில்வாய்க் கடுங்கணை தொடுத்த லோடு","மானிரை பெயர்ந்த வாய ரார்த்தன ரணிசெய் திண்டோ","டானொன்று முடங்கிற் றொன்று நிமிர்ந்தது சரம்பெய் மாரி","போனின்ற வென்ப மற்றப் பொருவரு சிலையி னார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"As the foresters flee before the fierce arrows set to his bow, the herd turns back and the cowherds shout, while of the matchless archer's strong shoulders one bends and one straightens, so that he is said to stand like a cloud raining arrows.","n":"The rhythm of the drawing arm, one shoulder bowed and one straight, is the poet's picture of continuous shooting; the herd is not driven back by him but turns of itself once the raiders run.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 452","lb":"CC452","ev":"The hunters flee, the herd turns back and the cowherds cheer Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மறவர்","ஆயர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:453":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":453,"t":["ஐந்நூறு நூறு தலையிட்ட வாறா யிரவர்","மெய்ந்நூறு நூறு நுதிவெங்கணை தூவி வேடர்","கைந்நூறு வில்லுங் கணையும்மறுத் தான்க ணத்தின்","மைந்நூறு வேற்கண் மடவார்மனம் போல மாய்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"When the six thousand hunters, with five hundred at their head, rain arrows by hundreds on his body, he cuts away the bows and arrows in their hands, and in a moment they vanish as the minds of kohl-eyed women do.","n":"The count in line 1 can be read as six thousand under five hundred leaders or as six thousand five hundred. மாய்ந்தார் may mean they perished or that they vanished from the field; the simile of women's changing minds, the epic's own view of women here, favours vanishing, and the next verse speaks of no blade drawing blood.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 453","lb":"CC453","ev":"Civakan cuts down the hunters' bows and arrows and the hunters vanish.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:454":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":454,"t":["வாள்வாயு மின்றி வடிவெங்கணை வாயு மின்றிக்","கோள்வாய் மதிய நெடியான்விடுத்தாங்கு மைந்தன்","றோள்வாய் சிலையின் னொலியாற்றொறு மீட்டு மீள்வா","னாள்வாய் நிறைந்த நகைவெண்மதி செல்வ தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"With neither the edge of a sword nor the point of an arrow, the young man turns back the herd by the mere twang of the bow on his shoulder and returns, like the full white moon that the tall god freed from the planet's mouth.","n":"Line 1 does not say whose body went unwounded: it can mean the recovery cost the hunters no blood or that Civakan came back untouched; the moon released whole from Rahu fits either. The god is named only as நெடியான், the tall one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 454","lb":"CC454","ev":"Civakan recovers the herd by the sound of his bow and comes back.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:455":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":455,"t":["ஆளற்ற மின்றி யலர்தாரவன் றோழ ரோடுங்","கோளுற்ற கோவ னிரைமீட்டன னென்று கூற","வாளுற்ற புண்ணுள் வடிவேலெறிந் திற்ற தேபோ","னாளுற் றுலந்தான் வெகுண்டானக ரார்த்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"When word comes that, with no man of his lost, he of the blooming garland with his companions has recovered the seized herd of the cowherd, one who heard it raged like a man whose last day had come, as if a spear had been thrown into a sword wound, and the city roared.","n":"The one who rages (வெகுண்டான்) is not named; that he is Kattiyankaran, whose rule Civakan's success shames, is a reading from the story so far, not from the verse. கோவன் here is the herd's owner, not Civakan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","niraikol","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 455","lb":"CC455","ev":"News of the recovery reaches the city, which cheers, while one who hears it rages.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:456":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":456,"t":["இரவிதோய் கொடிகொண் மாடத் திடுபுகை தவழச் சுண்ணம்","விரவிப்பூந் தாம நாற்றி விரைதெளித் தாரந் தாங்கி","யரவுயர் கொடியி னான்ற னகன்படை யனுங்க வென்ற","புரவித்தேர்க் காளை யன்ன காளையைப் பொலிக என்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The townspeople spread incense smoke over the sun-touching bannered mansions, scatter powder, hang flower garlands, sprinkle scent and, holding garlands, hail the young man as like the young hero of the horse chariot who wasted the wide army of the snake-bannered king, saying 'may he flourish'.","n":"The comparison is to the Mahabharata: the snake banner is Duryodhana's, and the hero who routed his host is Arjuna, who in that story too recovered seized cattle; the poet sets the merchant's foster son beside an epic prince.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 456","lb":"CC456","ev":"The city decorates its streets and blesses Civakan on his return.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:457":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":457,"t":["இன்னமு தனைய செவ்வா யிளங்கிளி மழலை யஞ்சொற்","பொன்னவிர் சுணங்கு பூத்த பொங்கிள முலையி னார்தம்","மின்னிவர் நுசுப்பு நோவ விடலையைக் காண வோடி","யன்னமு மயிலும் போல வணிநகர் வீதி கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with red mouths like sweet nectar, the lisping speech of young parrots and rising breasts spotted with gold-bright freckles run to see the young man until their lightning waists ache, and fill the street like swans and peacocks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 457","lb":"CC457","ev":"The young women of the city run into the street to see Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:458":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":458,"t":["சில்லரிச் சிலம்பின் வள்வார்ச் சிறுபறை கறங்கச் செம்பொ","னல்குற்றே ரணிந்து கொம்மை முலையெனும் புரவி பூட்டி","நல்லெழி னெடுங்கண் ணம்பாப் புருவலில் லுருவக் கோலிச்","செல்வப்போர்க் காமன் சேனை செம்மன்மே லெழுந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"With the small drums of their anklets sounding, their hips decked as golden chariots yoked to the horses of their breasts, their long eyes as arrows and their brows as bows bent, Kama's army rises against the young lord.","n":"The whole verse is a war in reverse: the hero just home from routing an army now meets one made of the women's bodies, and it is he who is the target.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 458","lb":"CC458","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:459":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":459,"t":["நூல்பொர வரிய நுண்மை நுசுப்பினை யொசிய வீங்கிக்","கால்பரந் திருந்த வெங்கட் கதிர்முலை கச்சின் வீக்கிக்","கோல்பொரச் சிவந்த கோல மணிவிரற் கோதை தாங்கி","மேல்வரல் கருதி நின்றார் விண்ணவர் மகளி ரொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Binding in their bodices the fiery breasts that swell till their waists, finer than thread, bend, and holding garlands in fingers reddened by the plectrum, they stand waiting for him to come, like the women of the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 459","lb":"CC459","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:460":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":460,"t":["ஆகமு மிடையு மஃக வடிபரந் தெழுந்து வீங்கிப்","போகமும் பொருளு மீன்ற புணர்முலைத் தடங்க டோன்றப்","பாகமே மறைய நின்ற படைமலர்த் தடங்க ணல்லார்","நாகம்விட் டெழுந்து போந்த நாகர்தம் மகளி ரொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with wide flower-like eyes, standing half hidden with only their breasts that bring forth pleasure and wealth showing, look like the daughters of the Nagas risen from their underworld.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 460","lb":"CC460","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:461":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":461,"t":["வாளரந் துடைத்த வைவே லிரண்டுடன் மலைந்த வேபோ","லாள்வழக் கொழிய நீண்ட வணிமலர்த் தடங்க ணெல்லா","நீள்சுடர் நெறியை நோக்கு நிரையிதழ் நெருஞ்சிப் பூப்போற்","காளைதன் றேர்செல் வீதி கலந்துடன் றொக்க வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"All the wide flower eyes, long beyond men's measure, like two whetted spears met in fight, crowd together along the street where his chariot goes, as nerunji flowers with their rows of petals turn to watch the course of the sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 461","lb":"CC461","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:462":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":462,"t":["வடகமுந் துகிலுந் தோடு மாலையு மணியு முத்துங்","கடகமுங் குழையும் பூணுங் கதிரொளி கலந்து மூதூ","ரிடவகை யெல்லை யெல்லா மின்னிரைத் திட்ட தேபோற்","படவர வல்கு லாரைப் பயந்தன மாட மெல்லாம்"],"p":"","r":"Shawls, silks, ear-rolls, garlands, gems, pearls, bracelets, earrings and jewels mingle their light, and as if lightning were ranged along the old city's every boundary, every mansion brings forth women with hips like a snake's hood.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 462","lb":"CC462","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:463":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":463,"t":["மாதுகு மயிலி னல்லார் மங்கல மரபு கூறிப்","போதக நம்பி யென்பார் பூமியும் புணர்க வென்பார்","தோதக மாக வெங்குஞ் சுண்ணமேற் சொரிந்து தண்ணென்","றாதுகு பிணையல் வீசிச் சாந்துகொண் டெறிந்து நிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Peacock-like women speak the customary words of blessing, some calling him a young elephant of a man and some wishing that he wed the earth too, and they shower powder everywhere, toss cool pollen-dropping garlands and stand flinging sandal paste.","n":"பூமியும் புணர்க, 'may he join the earth too', is a wedding blessing that makes rule a second bride; the women do not know he is the dead king's son. போதகம் in line 2 is taken as the elephant calf; the sense of the epithet is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 463","lb":"CC463","ev":"The women bless Civakan with auspicious words and shower him with powder, garlands and sandal.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:464":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":464,"t":["கொடையுளு மொருவன் கொல்லுங் கூற்றினுங் கொடிய வாட்போர்ப்","படையுளு மொருவ னென்று பயங்கெழு பனுவ ணுண்ணூ","னடையுளார் சொல்லிற் றெல்லா நம்பிசீ வகன்கட் கண்டாந்","தொடையலங் கோதை யென்று சொல்லுபு தொழுது நிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Others stand worshipping, saying that all the learned who walk in the fruitful subtle books have told of one unequalled in giving and one in sword-fighting crueller than killing Death, and all that we have seen in Nambi Civakan of the garland.","n":"The pairing of கொடை and படை repeats the Sangam measure of a man, the patron and the fighter, and the women make Civakan the proof of the books rather than the books the measure of him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 464","lb":"CC464","ev":"Women worship Civakan as the one in whom the books' ideal of giver and warrior is seen.","cl":[["the learned books name the unequalled giver and the unequalled warrior as the highest of men","general","reported",2,"city_women"]],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:465":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":465,"t":["செம்மலைப் பயந்த நற்றாய் செய்தவ முடைய ளென்பா","ரெம்மலைத் தவஞ்செய் தாள்கொ லெய்துவம் யாமு மென்பா","ரம்முலை யமுத மன்னா ரகம்புலர்ந் தமர்ந்து நோக்கித்","தம்முறு விழும வெந்நோய் தந்துணைக் குரைத்து நிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Some say the good mother who bore this lord must have done penance; some ask on which mountain she did it, so that they too may attain the like; and women with nectar breasts, dried up within, stare at him and tell their friends of the burning sickness they suffer.","n":"எய்துவம் யாமும் leaves open what they hope to attain, a son like him or the man himself; the next lines on their longing lean to the second.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaamanoy","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 465","lb":"CC465","ev":"Women praise Civakan's mother's penance and tell their friends of their longing.","cl":[["bearing such a son is the fruit of a mother's past penance","general","reported",1,"city_women"]],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:466":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":466,"t":["சினவுநர்க் கடந்த செல்வன் செம்மல ரகல நாளைக்","கனவினி லருளி வந்து காட்டியாங் காண வென்பார்","மனவிரி யல்கு லார்தம் மனத்தொடு மயங்கி யொன்றும்","வினவுந ரின்றி நின்று வேண்டுவ கூறு வாரும்."],"p":"","r":"Some ask that the lord who overcame the angry hunters grace them by coming in a dream tomorrow and showing his red flower-like chest so they may see it, while others, bewildered in their minds, stand saying what they want with no one asking them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam","orutalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 466","lb":"CC466","ev":"Women pray that Civakan visit them in a dream, and others voice their desires unasked.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:467":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":467,"t":["விண்ணகத் துளர்கொன் மற்றிவ் வென்றிவேற் குருசி லொப்பார்","மண்ணகத் திவர்க ளொவ்வார் மழகளி றனைய தோன்றல்","பண்ணகத் துறையுஞ் சொல்லார் நன்னலம் பருக வேண்டி","யண்ணலைத் தவத்திற் றந்தார் யார்கொலோ வளிய ரென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"They say none on earth matches this lord of the victorious spear, perhaps only those in heaven, and wonder who they might be, the poor fortunate ones, whose penance has given them the elephant-like hero so that women whose words dwell in music may drink his beauty.","n":"அளியர் can be pity or envy ('poor things' or 'blessed ones'), and whether the penance is the women's or their families' is not stated. யார்கொலோ அளியர் is the Sangam turn of wonder at a hero (Purananuru 257 has யார்கொலோ அளியன்), here moved from the hero to the women who would have him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 467","lb":"CC467","ev":"","cl":[["union with such a man is gained as the reward of penance","general","reported",4,"city_women"]],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:468":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":468,"t":["வட்டுடைப் பொலிந்த தானை வள்ளலைக் கண்ட போழ்தே","பட்டுடை சூழ்ந்த காசு பஞ்சிமெல் லடியைச் சூழ","அட்டரக் கனைய செவ்வா யணிநலங் கருகிக் காமக்","கட்டழ லெறிப்ப நின்றார் கைவளை கழல நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The moment they see the generous one in his bordered garment, the coins of their silk girdles slip round their soft feet, their lac-red mouths scorch, and they stand burning in the fierce fire of desire with the bangles sliding off their hands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 468","lb":"CC468","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:469":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":469,"t":["வார்செலச் செல்ல விம்மு வனமுலை மகளிர் நோக்கி","யேர்செலச் செல்ல வேத்தித் தொழுதுதோ டூக்க விப்பாற்","பார்செலச் செல்லச் சிந்திப் பைந்தொடி சொரிந்த நம்பன்","றேர்செலச் செல்லும் வீதி பீர்செலச் செல்லு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the women with swelling breasts watch him go, praising his bearing and worshipping him, their shoulders droop, and on this side bangles shower to the ground, so that the street where the lord's chariot runs turns pale as the beerkan flower.","n":"The pallor of longing, which akam poetry lays on one woman's skin, is spread here over a whole street; the chariot leaves a pale track behind it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","melivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 469","lb":"CC469","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:470":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":470,"t":["வாண்முகத் தலர்ந்த போலு மழைமலர்த் தடங்கண் கோட்டித்","தோண்முதற் பசலை தீரத் தோன்றலைப் பருகு வார்போ","னாண்முதற் பாசந் தட்ப நடுங்கினார் நிற்ப நில்லான்","கோண்முகப் புலியோ டொப்பான் கொழுநிதிப் புரிசை புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Tilting their rain-flower eyes as if to drink the young man and cure the pallor on their shoulders, the women stand trembling, held back by the noose of shame; he, like a fierce-faced tiger, does not stop, and enters his rich walled home.","n":"Shame (நாண்) holds the women where they stand, and the hero's not stopping (நிற்ப நில்லான்) is set against their standing; the sequence of the crowd scene ends in one line of his action.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","pasalai","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 470","lb":"CC470","ev":"Civakan passes the women without stopping and enters his walled home.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:471":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":471,"t":["பொன்னுகம் புரவி பூட்டு விட்டுடன் பந்தி புக்க","மன்னுக வென்றி யென்று மணிவள்ள நிறைய வாக்கி","யின்மதுப் பலியும் பூவுஞ் சாந்தமும் விளக்கு மேந்தி","மின்னுகு செம்பொற் கொட்டில் விளங்குதேர் புக்க தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The horses unyoked from the golden yoke go into their stalls, people fill gem bowls wishing that his victory last, and bearing sweet offerings of liquor, flowers, sandal and lamps, the shining chariot is brought into its gold-bright shed.","n":"மதுப் பலி, an offering of liquor or honey, is made to the victorious chariot; the verse gives no sign of any Jain reserve about it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 471","lb":"CC471","ev":"The horses are stabled and the chariot is housed with offerings for lasting victory.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:21"},"cc:472":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":472,"t":["இட்டவுத் தரிய மெல்லென் றிடைசுவல் வருத்த வொல்கி","யட்டமங் கலமு மேந்தி யாயிரத் தெண்ம ரீண்டிப்","பட்டமுங் குழையு மின்னப் பல்கல னொலிப்பச் சூழ்ந்து","மட்டவிழ் கோதை மாதர் மைந்தனைக் கொண்டு புக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"A thousand and eight women with flower garlands, their upper cloths slipping and ornaments ringing, carry the eight auspicious things and lead the young hero into his house.","n":"The welcome continues the procession of 470-471; the number ஆயிரத்தெண்மர் repeats the thousand and eight of the auspicious marks, a ritual figure rather than a count.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 472","lb":"CC472","ev":"Women bearing the eight auspicious objects escort Civakan home after the herd's recovery.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:473":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":473,"t":["தாயுயர் மிக்க தந்தை வந்தெதிர் கொண்டு புக்குக்","காய்கதிர் மணிசெய் வெள்வேற் காளையைக் காவ லோம்பி","யாய்கதி ருமிழும் பைம்பூ ணாயிரச் செங்க ணான்றன்","சேயுய ருலக மெய்தி தோன்றன்மற் றின்ன கூறும்."],"p":"","r":"His foster father, more to him than a mother, comes out to receive the youth of the shining spear and takes him in with care, as if Indra's son had come to the high world, and then, the verse says, the noble one speaks these words.","n":"The closing formula தோன்றன்மற் றின்ன கூறும் is repeated word for word at the end of 474, which introduces Nandagon's speech; no speech of the father follows here, so either the formula belongs to 474 alone or a speech has been lost. Whether தோன்றல் here is the father or Civakan is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 473","lb":"CC473","ev":"Kantukkatan receives Civakan at the house and guards him.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:474":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":474,"t":["தகைமதி யெழிலை வாட்டுந் தாமரைப் பூவி னங்கட்","புகைநுதி யழல வாட்கட் பென்னனாள் புல்ல நீண்ட","வகைமலி வரைசெய் மார்பின் வள்ளலைக் கண்டு வண்டார்த்","தொகைமலி தொறுவை யாளுந் தோன்றன்மற் றின்ன கூறும்."],"p":"","r":"The noble lord who rules the great herds, seeing the bountiful hero whose broad chest Fortune of the lotus embraces, speaks these words.","n":"The herd chief is named by his office (தொறுவை யாளும்), not by name; the name comes from his own mouth in 477.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 474","lb":"CC474","ev":"Nandagon, the herd chief, comes before Civakan and begins to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:475":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":475,"t":["கேட்டிது மறக்க நம்பி கேண்முதற் கேடு சூழ்ந்த","நாட்டிறை விசயை யென்னு நாறுபூங் கொம்ப னாளை","வேட்டிறைப் பார மெல்லாங் கட்டியங் காரன் றன்னைப்","பூட்டிமற் றவன்ற னாலே பொறிமுதல் அடர்க்கப் பட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Nandagon asks Civakan to hear and then forget what he tells: the king of this land, who first brought ruin on himself, desired Vijayai, laid the whole burden of rule on Kattiyankaran, and by that man was crushed at the root of his fortune.","n":"Nandagon blames the king's own choice (முதற் கேடு சூழ்ந்த, 'who first plotted the ruin') and says nothing of a former deed, whereas the narrator at 215 says a destiny formed before sprouted in the king; the epic keeps both causes, in two voices. The irony, which the hero cannot yet share, is that the listener is the dead king's son. பொறிமுதல் may be 'fortune at its root' or point to the machine (பொறி) of the peacock flight; the sense of line 4 is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","penvazhi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 475","lb":"CC475","ev":"Nandagon recounts in confidence how Saccandan handed his rule to Kattiyankaran for love of Vijayai and was destroyed by him.","cl":[["a king who hands his rule to another out of desire for his queen brings about his own ruin","general","asserted",1,"nandagon"]],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தகோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:476":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":476,"t":["கோலிழுக் குற்ற ஞான்றே கொடுமுடி வரையொன் றேறிக்","காலிழுக் குற்று வீழ்ந்தே கருந்தலை களைய லுற்றேன்","மால்வழி உளதன் றாயின் வாழ்வினை முடிப்பல் என்றே","ஆலம்வித் தனைய தெண்ணி அழிவினுள் அகன்று நின்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"Nandagon says that when the sceptre fell he climbed a high peak meaning to throw himself down and end his life, resolving to die if no heir of the great line survived, but thinking of the banyan seed he drew back from destruction.","n":"The banyan seed (ஆலம்வித்து) is the tiny seed of a vast tree: his hope is that one surviving child of the line could regrow the house. மால்வழி is read here as 'the great line'; it could be taken otherwise, and the sense of line 3 turns on it. The servant's loyal suicide is withheld, not condemned.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","kudi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 476","lb":"CC476","ev":"Nandagon tells how, at the king's fall, he went to kill himself and turned back in hope that the royal line survived.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தகோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:477":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":477,"t":["குலத்தொடு முடிந்த கோன்றன் குடிவழி வாரா நின்றே","னலத்தகு தொறுவி னுள்ளே னாமங்கோ விந்த னென்பே.","னிலக்கண மமைந்த கோதா வரியென விசையிற் போந்த","நலத்தகு மனைவி பெற்ற நங்கைகோ விந்தை யென்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"Nandagon names himself: he comes of a family in the service of the king whose line ended with him, he lives among the herds and is called Govindan, and his wife, who came swift as the Godavari, bore the girl called Govindai.","n":"The daughter's name is formed from the father's. 'The king who ended with his clan' is what Nandagon believes of Saccandan; 476 has just shown him hoping it is not so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 477","lb":"CC477","ev":"Nandagon names himself and his daughter Govindai.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தகோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:478":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":478,"t":["வம்புடை முலையி னாளென் மடமகள் மதர்வை நோக்க","மம்படி யிருத்தி நெஞ்சத் தழுத்தியிட் டனைய தொப்பக்","கொம்படு நுசுப்பி னாளைக் குறையிரந் துழந்து நின்ற","நம்படை தம்மு ளெல்லா நகைமுக மழிந்து நின்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"Nandagon says that his young daughter's bold glance went into men's hearts like a pressed-in arrow, and that among all his people who begged for the slender girl and suffered he stood with his smile gone.","n":"Line 4 can also be read of the herd's loss before the raid was undone (நம்படை, 'our men', who had stood beaten); the reading here takes it of the suitors among his own people whom he could not satisfy.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 478","lb":"CC478","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தகோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:479":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":479,"t":["பாடகஞ் சுமந்த செம்பொற் சீறடிப் பரவை யல்குற்","சூடக மணிந்த முன்கைச் சுடர்மணிப் பூணி னாளை","யாடகச் செம்பொற் பாவை யேழுடன் தருவ லைய","வாடலில் வதுவை கூடி மணமக னாக என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Nandagon offers Civakan the girl of the golden anklets and bangled wrists together with seven statues of fine gold, and asks him to join her in an unfailing wedding as her bridegroom.","n":"This keeps the proclamation of 440, that the maiden would go to whoever brought back the herd; the seven golden statues are added here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vathuvai","eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 479","lb":"CC479","ev":"Nandagon offers Govindai to Civakan in marriage with seven golden statues.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தகோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:480":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":480,"t":["வெண்ணெய்போன் றூறினியண் மேம்பால்போறீஞ்சொல்ல","ளுண்ண வுருக்கிய வானெய்போன் மேனியள்","வண்ண வனமுலை மாதர் மடநோக்கி","கண்ணுங் கருவிளம் போதிரண்டே கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Nandagon praises his daughter in the goods of the herd: her sweetness wells like butter, her speech is sweet as the cream of milk, her body is like melted ghee, and her two eyes are karuvilam flowers.","n":"The metre changes here for three verses (480-482). The herdsman's similes are all dairy: the poet gives him the idiom of his trade, as the Sangam mullai poems give it to herders.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 480","lb":"CC480","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தகோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:481":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":481,"t":["சேதா நறுநெய்யுந் தீம்பால் சுமைத்தயிரும்","பாதால மெல்லாம் நிறைத்திடுவல் பைந்தாரோய்","போதார் புனைகோதை சூட்டுன் னடிச்சியை","யாதாவ தெல்லா மறிந்தருளி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Nandagon promises to fill the underworld with the ghee of red cows, sweet milk and thick curd, and asks Civakan to garland his handmaid with flowers, knowing all that must follow.","n":"He calls his daughter உன் அடிச்சி, 'your servant woman', offering her below the hero's station before the hero raises the question of rank in 483.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vathuvai","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 481","lb":"CC481","ev":"Nandagon presses his offer with a promise of boundless dairy wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தகோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:482":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":482,"t":["குலநினைய னம்பி கொழுங்கயற்கண் வள்ளி","நலநுகர்ந்தா னன்றே நறுந்தார் முருக","னிலமகட்குக் கேள்வனு நீணிரைநப் பின்னை","யிலவலர்வா யின்னமிர்த மெய்தினா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Nandagon tells Civakan not to think of lineage: fragrant-garlanded Murukan enjoyed the beauty of Valli, and the husband of the Earth took the sweet nectar of the lips of Nappinnai of the long herds.","n":"Both precedents are gods who married beneath them by birth: Valli is a hunter's daughter and Nappinnai a herdsman's. The father argues from myth, not from doctrine, and the argument is his own interest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu","vathuvai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 482","lb":"CC482","ev":"","cl":[["difference of lineage should not bar a marriage, since gods wed a hill girl and a herd girl","general","reported",1,"nandagon"]],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தகோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:483":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":483,"t":["கன்னியர் குலத்தின் மிக்கார் கதிர்முலைக் கன்னி மார்ப","முன்னினர் முயங்கி னல்லான் முறிமிடை படலை மாலைப்","பொன்னிழை மகளி ரொவ்வா தவரைமுன் புணர்தல் செல்ல","ரின்னதான் முறைமை மாந்தர்க் கெனமனத் தெண்ணி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan reflects that men do not first unite with women unequal to them in birth before they have married a maiden of their own high lineage, and that this is the rule for men.","n":"This is the hero's thought, not his speech. It does not deny 482 that unequal marriage is possible; it orders it, so that rank governs which wife comes first. Lines 1-2 are hard to construe and the reading follows their sense with lines 3-4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","vathuvai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 483","lb":"CC483","ev":"Civakan considers the offer and decides it would breach the order of marriage for him to take Govindai first.","cl":[["a man of high birth should not marry a woman of unequal birth before he has married one of his own rank","general","reported",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:484":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":484,"t":["கோட்டிளங் களிறு போல்வா னந்தகோன் முகத்தை நோக்கி","மோட்டிள முலையி னாணின் மடமக ளெனக்கு மாமான்","சூட்டொடு கண்ணி யன்றே யென்செய்வா னிவைகள் சொல்லி","நீட்டித்தல் குணமோ வென்று நெஞ்சகங் குளிர்ப்பச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, like a young tusker, looks at Nandagon and says, so as to cool his heart, that the girl is to be the crest and garland of one dear to him, and asks whether it is fitting to delay with such speeches.","n":"The sense turns on மாமான் in line 2, a kinship word whose referent is not clear; 489 shows that he accepts Govindai on behalf of Padumukan, and the reading takes the line in that light.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vathuvai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 484","lb":"CC484","ev":"Civakan accepts the offer and urges that the marriage be made without delay.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தகோன்"],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:485":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":485,"t":["தேன்சொரி முல்லைக் கண்ணிச் செந்துவ ராடை யாயர்","கோன்பெரி துவந்து போகிக் குடைதயிர் குழுமப் புக்கு","மான்கறி கற்ற கூழை மௌவல்சூழ் மயிலைப் பந்தர்க்","கான்சொரி முல்லைத் தாரான் கடிவினை முடிக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The herd chief, in his wreath of honeyed mullai and red cloth, goes off in great joy into the crowd where curd is churned and orders that the wedding be performed in the pavilion of mayilai ringed with jasmine.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vathuvai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 485","lb":"CC485","ev":"Nandagon orders the wedding to be held in the herders' pavilion.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:486":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":486,"t":["கனிவளர் கிளவி காமர் சிறுநுதல் புருவங் காமன்","குனிவளர் சிலையைக் கொன்ற குவளைக்கண் கயலைக் கொன்ற","வினியுள ரல்ல ராய ரெனச்சிலம் பரற்றத் தந்து","பனிவளர் கோதை மாதர் பாவையைப் பரவி வைத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The women bring the girl, whose brow outdid Kama's bow and whose kuvalai eyes outdid the kayal fish, her anklets crying out, and set her down with praise.","n":"Line 3 (இனியுளர் அல்லர் ஆயர்) seems to give the anklets' cry that the herders are 'no longer' something to her, perhaps that she now passes out of her people; the sense is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vathuvai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 486","lb":"CC486","ev":"The herdswomen bring Govindai to the pavilion.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்த்தியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:487":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":487,"t":["நாழியு ளிழுது நாகான் கன்றுதின் றொழித்த புல்தோய்த்","தூழிதோ றாவுந் தோழும் போன்றுடன் மூக்க வென்று","தாழிருங் குழலி னாளை நெய்தலைப் பெய்து வாழ்த்தி","மூழைநீர் சொரிந்து மொய்கொ ளாய்த்திய ராட்டி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The herdswomen dip in ghee, in a measure, grass the young cow's calf has eaten from, bless her to grow old with her husband age after age like cow and stall, pour ghee on her head and bathe her with water from a ladle.","n":"The rite is wholly of the herd: calf-grazed grass, ghee, a blessing in the image of cattle. Line 2's comparison (ஆவும் தோழும்) is read as cow and stall; it may be cow and bull.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vathuvai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 487","lb":"CC487","ev":"The herdswomen perform the herders' bridal anointing and bathing of Govindai.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்த்தியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:488":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":488,"t":["நெய்விலைப் பசும்பொற் றோடு நிழன்மணிக் குழையு நீவி","மைவிரி குழலி னாளை மங்கலக் கடிப்புச் சேர்த்திப்","பெய்தனர் பிணையன் மாலை யோரிலைச் சாந்து பூசிச்","செய்தனர் சிறுபுன் கோலம் தொறுத்தியர் திகைத்து நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"They take off the gold ear studs bought with ghee and the gem pendants, fasten the auspicious bridal thread, put on a garland and sandal paste, and make her a small simple adornment, and the herdswomen stand amazed.","n":"நெய்விலைப் பசும்பொன், 'gold bought at the price of ghee', marks the herders' wealth as dairy wealth. The small adornment (சிறுபுன் கோலம்) is plain, yet it amazes the herdswomen themselves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vathuvai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 488","lb":"CC488","ev":"The herdswomen adorn Govindai for the wedding.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்த்தியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:489":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":489,"t":["ஏறங்கோண் முழங்க வாயரெடுத்துக்கொண் டேகி மூதூர்ச்","சாறெங்கு மயரப் புக்கு நந்தகோன் றன்கை யேந்தி","வீறுயர் கலச நன்னீர் சொரிந்தனன் வீர னேற்றான்","பாறுகொள் பருதி வைவேற் பதுமுக குமரற் கென்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the drums sound the herders take her and go through the festive old town, and Nandagon, holding the pitcher, pours its water, which the hero receives, saying it is for prince Padumukan of the spear.","n":"The pouring of water seals the gift of the bride; the giver's promise in 440 is kept, but the winner passes the prize to his friend. ஏறங்கோண் in line 1 is unclear; it may name horns or the bull-catch drums of the herders.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vathuvai","natpu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 489","lb":"CC489","ev":"Nandagon gives Govindai by pouring water, and Civakan receives the gift on behalf of his friend Padumukan.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயர்","நந்தகோன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:490":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":490,"t":["நலத்தகை யவட்கு நாகா னாயிரத் திரட்டி நன்பொ","னிலக்கணப் பாவை யேழுங் கொடுத்தனன் போக விப்பா","லலைத்தது காமன் சேனை யருநுனை யம்பு மூழ்க","முலைக்குவட் டிடைப்பட் டாற்றான் முத்துக முயங்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandagon gives her two thousand young cows and the seven fine golden statues, and then Padumukan, struck by the sharp arrows of Kama's army, embraces her pearl-adorned breasts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 490","lb":"CC490","ev":"Nandagon gives the dowry and Padumukan is united with Govindai.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தகோன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:491":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":491,"t":["கள்வாய் விரிந்த கழுநீர்பிணைந் தன்ன வாகி","வெள்வேன் மிளிர்ந்த நெடுங்கண்விரை நாறு கோதை","முள்வா யெயிற்றூ றமுதம்முனி யாது மாந்திக்","கொள்ளாத வின்பக் கடற்பட்டனன் கோதைவேலான்."],"p":"","r":"Drinking without tiring the nectar of her sharp teeth, whose eyes are like paired kazhuneer flowers and whose garland is fragrant, the garlanded spearman falls into a sea of pleasure that cannot be contained.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 491","lb":"CC491","ev":"Padumukan delights in Govindai.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:492":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":492,"t":["தீம்பாற் கடலைத் திரைபொங்கக் கடைந்து தேவர்","தாம்பாற் படுத்த வமிர்தோதட மாலை வேய்த்தோ","ளாம்பாற் குடவர் மகளோவென் றரிவை நைய","வோம்பா வொழுக்கத் துணர்வொன்றில னாயி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Wondering whether she is the nectar the gods churned from the sweet ocean of milk or a daughter of the herders, while the girl melts, Padumukan becomes one with no awareness left but of his unguarded pleasure.","n":"ஓம்பா ஒழுக்கத்து உணர்வொன்றிலன் can mean he kept no guard on himself or that he lost every awareness but this one; the narrator does not blame him. The nectar churned from the milk ocean answers the dairy similes of 480: the herd girl is made the gods' amrita.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 492","lb":"CC492","ev":"Padumukan is lost in love for Govindai.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்","கோவிந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:22"},"cc:493":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":493,"t":["இங்கிவர்க ளிவ்வா றிருந்தினிது வாழச்","சங்குதரு நீணிதியஞ் சாலவுடை நாய்கன்","பொங்குதிரை மீது பொரு மால்களிறு போன்றோர்","வங்கமொடு போகிநிதி வந்துதர லுற்றான்"],"p":"","r":"While Civakan and his companions live happily, a merchant rich in treasure gathered from the sea sets out to sail with ships, like great elephants on the waves, to bring back wealth.","n":"Line 1 (இங்கு இவர்கள் இவ்வாறு இருந்து இனிது வாழ) closes the previous ilampakam's household and opens a new thread; the merchant is named only in the next verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 493","lb":"CC493","ev":"A wealthy merchant resolves to sail abroad for wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:494":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":494,"t":["மின்னொழுகு சாயன்மிகு பூட்பதுமை கேள்வன்","கொன்னொழுகு வேலியவ தத்தன்குளிர் தூங்குந்","தன்வழிய காளைசீ தத்தனவன் றன்போற்","பொன்னொழுகு குன்றிலுறை போர்ப்புலியோ டொப்பான்."],"p":"","r":"The merchant is introduced: the husband of the bright-jewelled Padumai, a man of the lance-fenced line of Dattan, and his son Sitattan, a bull of that line, like a fighting tiger dwelling on a golden hill.","n":"The word division of line 2 (வேலியவ தத்தன்) leaves the father's name unsure, and whether Padumai's husband in line 1 is the father or Sitattan himself depends on reading அவன் தன் போல் in line 3 as 'like his father'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 494","lb":"CC494","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:495":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":495,"t":["இம்மியன நுண்பொருள்க ளீட்டிநிதி யாக்கிக்","கம்மியரு மூர்வர்களி றோடைநுதல் சூட்டி","யம்மிமிதந் தாழ்ந்துசுரை வீழ்ந்ததறஞ் சால்கென்","றும்மைவினை நொந்துபுலந் தூடலுணர் வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Sitattan reflects that even artisans, by heaping up wealth from things as small as grains, come to ride elephants with frontlets, and that when fortune turns upside down, like a grindstone floating and a gourd sinking, to sulk and blame one's former deeds is not understanding.","n":"The reflection runs from here to 498, closed by என்று எழுந்து போந்தான்; it is the merchant's view, and within a few verses he himself will put his survival down to old deeds (510, 511).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","vinai","madi","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 495","lb":"CC495","ev":"","cl":[["to blame former deeds and sulk when fortune turns is not wisdom","general","reported",4,"sitattan"],["men of low station rise to greatness by accumulating small gains","general","reported",1,"sitattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீதத்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:496":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":496,"t":["உள்ளமுடை யான்முயற்சி செய்யவொரு நாளே","வெள்ளநிதி வீழும்விளை யாததலி னில்லை","தொள்ளையுணர் வின்னவர்கள் சொல்லின்மடி கிற்பி","னெள்ளுநர்கட் கேக்கழுத்தம் போல வினிதன்றே."],"p":"","r":"If a man of resolve strives, a flood of wealth may fall to him in a single day, since nothing fails to yield to effort; but to sink into sloth at the words of hollow-minded men is as bitter as the mockers' jeers.","n":"Line 4's comparison (எள்ளுநர்கட்கு ஏக்கழுத்தம் போல) is compressed; the sense that sloth gives one's mockers their triumph is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","madi","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 496","lb":"CC496","ev":"","cl":[["effort by a resolute man can bring great wealth in a single day","general","reported",1,"sitattan"],["there is nothing that does not yield to effort","general","reported",2,"sitattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீதத்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:497":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":497,"t":["செய்க பொருள் யாருஞ்செறு வாரைச்செறு கிற்கு","மெஃகுபிறி தில்லையிருந் தேயுயிரு முண்ணு","மையமிலை யின்பமற னோடெவையு மாக்கும்","பொய்யில்பொரு ளேபொருண்மற் றல்லபிற பொருளே."],"p":"","r":"Make wealth: there is no other steel that can subdue those who would subdue you, it works even while one sits still, and without doubt it brings pleasure, virtue and everything else; true wealth alone is wealth, and nothing else counts.","n":"The verse gathers the Kural's chapter on making wealth (751, 759, 760) almost clause by clause, but in its own words: செய்க பொருள் and எஃகு are the only shared words, short of an echo. The shipwreck of 512 to 514 puts the creed to the test.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pagai","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 497","lb":"CC497","ev":"","cl":[["wealth is the one weapon that subdues one's enemies","general","reported",1,"sitattan"],["wealth brings pleasure and virtue and all else with it","general","reported",3,"sitattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீதத்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:498":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":498,"t":["தூங்குசிறை வாவலுறை தொன்மரங்க ளென்ன","வோங்குகுல நையவத னுட்பிறந்த வீரர்","தாங்கல்கட னாகுந்தலை சாய்க்கவரு தீச்சொ","னீங்கல்மட வார்கள்கட னென்றெழுந்து போந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"As old trees fill with hanging bats when they decay, so when a lofty lineage weakens the heroes born in it must hold it up, while shunning the evil talk that bows the head is the women's part; with that he rose and went.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 divide duty by sex: upholding the line falls to the men, guarding against shaming talk to the women. Whether line 1 means the lineage is already declining or only that it may decline is open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","aanmai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 498","lb":"CC498","ev":"Sitattan resolves to restore his family's fortune and leaves to make ready.","cl":[["men born into a declining family must bear the burden of restoring it","general","reported",3,"sitattan"]],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீதத்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:499":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":499,"t":["மோதுபடு பண்டமுனி யாதுபெரி தேற்றி","மாதுபடு நோக்கினவர் வாட்கண்வடு வுற்ற","தாதுபடு தார்கெழிய தங்குவரை மார்பன்","கோதுபட லில்லகுறிக் கொண்டெழுந்து போந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Loading great quantities of goods without stint, the hill-chested man whose pollen-thick garland bore the marks of women's sword-like eyes set out with a flawless aim.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 499","lb":"CC499","ev":"Sitattan loads his cargo and sets out.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:500":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":500,"t":["வானமுற நீண்டபுகழ் மாரிமழை வள்ள","றானமென வேண்டுநர்கள் வேண்டுவன நல்கி","நானமிக நாறுகமழ் குஞ்சியவ னேறி","யூனமெனு மின்றியினி தோடுகவி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The patron whose fame reached the sky gave suppliants whatever they asked, like rain, then boarded with his fragrant hair and bade the ship run sweetly without harm.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 500","lb":"CC500","ev":"Sitattan gives gifts to suppliants, boards and orders the ship to sail.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:501":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":501,"t":["ஆடுகொடி யுச்சியணி கூம்பினுயர் பாய்மூன்","றீடுபடச் செய்திளைய ரேத்தவிமிழ் முந்நீர்க்","கோடுபறை யார்ப்பக்கொழுந் தாட்பவழங் கொல்லா","வோடுகளி றொப்பவினி தோடியதை யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"With flags flying from the mast-tops and three sails set high, the young men cheering and conch and drum sounding over the sea, the ship ran sweetly like an elephant that does not trample the coral.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 501","lb":"CC501","ev":"The ship sails out under full sail.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:502":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":502,"t":["திரைகடருஞ் சங்குகலந் தாக்கித் திரண்முத்தம்","கரைகடலுட் காலக்கணை பின்னொழிய முந்நீர்","வரைகிடந்து கீண்டதெனக் கீறிவளர் தீவி","னிரையிடறிப் பாய்ந்திரிய வேகியது மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Striking the conches the waves bring, spilling clustered pearls, leaving the wind's arrows behind, the ship cleft the sea as if splitting a mountain lying in it and sped on, scattering the herds of the islands.","n":"Line 4 (இரை இடறிப் பாய்ந்து இரிய) is open: the ship may scatter flocks or fish feeding at the islands.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 502","lb":"CC502","ev":"The ship crosses the sea at speed.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:503":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":503,"t":["மின்னுமிளிர் பூங்கொடியு மென்மலரு மொப்பார்","அன்னமொடுந் தோகைநடை சாயலமிர் தன்னார்","துன்னியினி தாகவுறை துப்புரவின் மிக்க","நன்மையுடை நன்பொன்விளை தீவமடைந் தஃதே."],"p":"","r":"It reached an island of fine gold, rich in cleanliness and goodness, where women like lightning, flower-creepers and soft blossoms, with the gait of swans and peacocks and a nectar-like grace, live close and happy.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 503","lb":"CC503","ev":"The ship reaches a gold-yielding island.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:504":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":504,"t":["தீவினுள் இழிந்து தேந்தார்ச் செம்மலுந் திருமுத்தாரங்","கோவினைக் குறிப்பிற் கண்டு கொடுத்தருள் சுமந்து செம்பொற்","பூவினுள் ளவளை யன்ன பொங்கிள முலையினார்த","நாவினு ளமிர்தங் கேட்டு நாடக நயந்து சின்னாள்"],"p":"","r":"Landing on the island, the honey-garlanded lord saw its king and, reading his wish, gave him a fine pearl necklace and carried away his favour; then for some days he listened to the nectar of the speech of women like Lakshmi of the lotus and delighted in their dramas.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 504","lb":"CC504","ev":"Sitattan presents a pearl necklace to the island's king, wins his favour and enjoys the island's women and dramas.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","கோ"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:505":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":505,"t":["புணர்ந்தவர் பிரித லாற்றாப் போகமீன் றளிக்குஞ் சாய","லணங்கினுக் கணங்க னாரோ டறுமதி கழிந்த பின்றைக்","கொணர்ந்தன பண்டம் விற்ற கொழுநிதிக் குப்பை யெல்லா","முணர்ந்துதன் மதலை யேற்றி யொருப்படுத் தூர்க்கு மீள்வான்"],"p":"","r":"After six months with women whose grace yields a pleasure lovers cannot bear to leave, he reckoned up the heaps of rich wealth from the goods he had sold, loaded his ship and made ready to return home.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 505","lb":"CC505","ev":"Sitattan sells his goods, loads the proceeds and prepares to sail home.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:506":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":506,"t":["அரசனைக் கண்டு கண்ணுற் றவர்களை விடுத்து நன்னா","ளிரைவதி வியாழ வோரை யிருஞ்சிலை முளைப்ப வேறிக்","கரைகட லழுவ நீந்திக் காற்றினுங் கடுகி யைஞ்ஞூ","றுரையுடைக் காத மோடி யோசனை யெல்லை சார்ந்தே,"],"p":"","r":"Taking leave of the king and of those he had met, on a good day when Revati, Jupiter's hour and the rising of Sagittarius met, he embarked, crossed the deep sea faster than the wind and ran five hundred kathams to the edge of the voyage's yojanas.","n":"The care over the auspicious moment (line 2) sets up the storm that follows: a well-chosen day does not keep off what comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 506","lb":"CC506","ev":"Sitattan departs on an auspicious day and sails most of the way home.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:507":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":507,"t":["களித்தலை மயங்கி யிப்பா லிருத்தலுங் கலந்தோர் காற்றிற்","றுளித்தலை முகில்க ளீண்டித் தூங்கிருண் மயங்கி மான்று","விளிப்பது போல மின்னி வெடிபட முழங்கிக் கூற்று","மொளித்துலைந் தொழிய வெம்பி யுரறிநின் றிடிப்ப நாய்கன்."],"p":"","r":"As they lingered in joy on this side, clouds heavy with drops gathered on a wind, the darkness thickened, lightning flashed as if calling, and the thunder roared and struck so fiercely that Death itself might hide, while the merchant...","n":"The verse ends on நாய்கன், whose verb is கூறும் at the end of 508: the merchant's words are held back through a whole verse of storm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","kootru","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 507","lb":"CC507","ev":"A storm breaks over the homeward ship.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:508":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":508,"t":["எண்டிசை வளியு மீண்டி யெதிரெதிர் கலாவிப் பவ்வங்","கொண்டுமே லெழுவ தொப்பக் குளிறிநின் றதிர்ந்து மேகந்","தண்டுளி பளிக்குக் கோல்போற் றாரையாய்ச் சொரிந்து தெய்வங்","கொண்டதோர் செற்றம் போலுங் குலுங்கன்மி னென்று கூறும்."],"p":"","r":"The winds of the eight quarters met and clashed head-on, the sea seemed to rise up, the clouds roared and poured cold rain in streams like crystal rods, like the wrath of some god, and he says 'do not shake'.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","idukkan","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 508","lb":"CC508","ev":"In the storm Sitattan tells his crew not to tremble.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:509":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":509,"t":["இடுக்கண்வந் துற்ற காலை யெரிகின்ற விளக்குப் போல","நடுக்கமொன் றானு மின்றி நகுகதா நக்க போழ்தவ்","விடுக்கணை யரியு மெஃகா மிருந்தழு தியாவ ருய்ந்தார்","வடுப்படுத் தென்னை யாண்மை வருபவந் துறங்க ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"When trouble comes, laugh without tremor, steady as a burning lamp, for that laughter is a steel that cuts the trouble; who has ever survived by sitting and weeping? Do not shame manliness: what must come will come, he says.","n":"Line 1 opens as Kural 621 opens (இடுக்கண் ... நகுக) and line 3 turns its claim into an image (நக்க போழ்து அவ் இடுக்கணை அரியும் எஃகு), but the run of shared words is under four, so it is not marked as an echo. எஃகு recalls 497, where wealth was the steel; here composure is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","anjaamai","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 509","lb":"CC509","ev":"Sitattan exhorts his crew to face the storm without fear.","cl":[["in trouble one should laugh without fear, since that laughter cuts the trouble","general","asserted",2,"sitattan"],["no one has been saved by weeping","general","asserted",3,"sitattan"]],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீதத்தன்","உள்ளவர்"],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:510":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":510,"t":["ஆடகச் செம்பொற் கிண்ணத் தேந்திய வலங்கற் றெண்ணீர்","கூடகங் கொண்ட வாழ்நா ளுலந்ததேற் கொல்லும் பவ்வத்","தூடகம் புக்கு முந்நீ ரழுந்தினு முய்வர் நல்லார்","பாடகம் போலச் சூழ்ந்த பழவினைப் பயத்தி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"If the span of life held in the body like clear water in a golden cup is spent, the sea will kill; but even plunged into the deep, the good survive by the fruit of their old deeds that surrounds them like an anklet, he says.","n":"The man who in 495 called blaming former deeds a failure of understanding now makes former deeds the ground of hope; the story does not mark the turn, but the two views sit four verses apart. The outcome bears him out: all the rest die (512) and he alone is cast ashore (513).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aayul","vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 510","lb":"CC510","ev":"","cl":[["death comes when one's allotted life-span is spent, and not before","general","asserted",2,"sitattan"],["the fruit of good deeds in former lives keeps the good alive even when they sink in the sea","general","asserted",4,"sitattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீதத்தன்","உள்ளவர்"],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:511":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":511,"t":["வினையது விளைவின் வந்த வீவருந் துன்ப முன்னீர்க்","கனைகட லழுவ நீந்திக் கண்கனிந் திரங்கல் வேண்டா","நனைமலர்ப் பிண்டி நாத னாண்மலர்ப் பாத மூலம்","நினையுமி னீவி ரெல்லா நீங்குமி னச்ச மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Do not swim this sea weeping with melting eyes over the unbearable suffering that ripened deeds have brought; all of you, think on the flower-feet of the lord of the budding pindi tree and cast off fear, he says.","n":"பிண்டி நாதன், the lord of the asoka (pindi), names the Jina. The refuge offered is not rescue: the feet are to be remembered so that fear goes, while what the deeds bring is still to be borne.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thiruvadi","acham","adaikkalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 511","lb":"CC511","ev":"","cl":[["suffering that is the ripening of one's deeds is not to be wept over","general","asserted",1,"sitattan"],["meditating on the feet of the Arhat beneath the asoka tree dispels fear","jain","asserted",3,"sitattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீதத்தன்","உள்ளவர்"],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:512":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":512,"t":["பருமித்த களிற னானும் பையெனக் கவிழ்ந்து நிற்பக்","குருமித்து மதலை பொங்கிக் கூம்பிறப் பாய்ந்து வல்லே","நிருமித்த வகையி னோடி நீர்நிறைந் தாழ்ந்த போதி","லுருமிடித் திட்ட தொப்ப வுள்ளவ ரொருங்கு மாய்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"As the merchant, like a caparisoned war elephant, stood quietly bowed, the ship heaved, the mast broke, it ran as it had been built to run, filled with water and sank, and all aboard perished together as if thunder had struck them.","n":"Line 1 (பருமித்த களிறனானும் பையெனக் கவிழ்ந்து நிற்ப) may describe the merchant standing bowed or the ship itself heeling over; the reading taken here makes it the merchant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","idukkan","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 512","lb":"CC512","ev":"The ship founders and everyone aboard except Sitattan drowns.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","உள்ளவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:513":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":513,"t":["ஓம்பிப் படைத்த பொருளும்முறு காதலாரும்","வேம்புற்ற முந்நீர் விழுங்கவ்விரை யாது நின்றான்","கூம்பிற்ற துண்டந் தழுவிக்கிடந் தான்கொ ழித்துத்","தேம்பெற்ற பைந்தா ரவனைத்திரை யுய்த்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"While the sea, bitter as neem, swallowed the wealth he had guarded and gathered and the people he loved, he stood without haste, clung to a broken piece of the mast, and the waves washed the fresh-garlanded man ashore.","n":"ஓம்பிப் படைத்த பொருள் (line 1) is the wealth 497 called the only wealth; it goes down while the man who made it is kept, as 510 said the good would be.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","samanilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 513","lb":"CC513","ev":"Sitattan clings to a piece of the mast and is washed ashore.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:514":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":514,"t":["நாவா யிழந்து நடுவாருமி லியாம நீந்திப்","போவாய் தமியே பொருளைப் பொரு ளென்று கொண்டாய்","வீவா யெனமுன் படையாய்படைத் தாய்வி னையென்","பாவா யெனப்போய்ப் படுவெண்மணற் றிட்டை சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"'Having lost your ship, you swim alone through the midnight with no one beside you; you took wealth to be the thing of worth; you did not first make what would keep you from death, you made only deeds; my poor self,' he says, and goes on to a dune of white sand.","n":"பொருளைப் பொருள் என்று கொண்டாய் answers 497's பொய்யில் பொருளே பொருள் in the speaker's own mouth. Line 3 (வீவாய் என முன் படையாய் படைத்தாய் வினை) is compressed and the sense of what he failed to make is a reading; என் பாவாய் may be addressed to himself or to some other.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 514","lb":"CC514","ev":"Sitattan reproaches himself and reaches a sandbank.","cl":[["to take wealth for the thing of real worth is an error","general","reported",2,"sitattan"]],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீதத்தன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:515":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":515,"t":["பொரியரை ஞாழலும் புன்னையும் பூத்து","வரிதரு வண்டொடு தேனின மார்க்குந்","திருவிரி பூம்பொழிற் செவ்வனஞ் சேர்ந்தாங்","கருவரை மார்ப னவலித் திருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Reaching a lovely red grove where ஞாழல் and புன்னை trees with rough bark were in flower and striped bees and honey-bees hummed, the hill-chested man sat there grieving.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 515","lb":"CC515","ev":"Sitattan reaches a flowering shore grove and sits in grief.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:516":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":516,"t":["ஓடுந் திரைக ளுதைப்ப வுருண்டுருண்","டாடு மலவனை யன்ன மருள்செய","நீடிய நெய்தலங் கான னெடுந்தகை","வாடி யிருந்தான் வருங்கல நோக்கா."],"p":"","r":"In the long neytal grove, where crabs rolled over and over as the running waves kicked them, making the swans wonder, the noble one sat withering, watching for a ship to come.","n":"The crab tumbled by the waves (lines 1 and 2) mirrors the castaway tossed ashore in 513.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 516","lb":"CC516","ev":"Sitattan waits on the shore watching for a ship.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:23"},"cc:517":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":517,"t":["ஆளிய மொய்ம்ப னிருந்தவப் பூம்பொழிற்","றாள்வலி யானொர் மகனைத் தலைப்பட்டுக்","கேளி ரெனக்குற்ற கேண்மின் னீரெனத்","தோள்வலி மிக்கான் றொடர்ந்துரைக் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Cast up on the shore grove, the strong-shouldered merchant meets a man of strong stride and, calling him kin, begins to tell him what has befallen him.","n":"The stranger is not yet named; 519 calls him Dharan, and 574 reveals he is the Vidyadhara who staged the storm, so the merchant here confides his loss to its author.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","kizhamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 517","lb":"CC517","ev":"Sridattan meets a stranger in the seashore grove and begins to tell him his misfortune.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:518":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":518,"t":["கருங்கடற் போயிற்றுங் காற்றிற் கவிழ்ந்து","திருந்திய தன்பொருள் தீதுற்ற வாறும்","அரும்புணை சார்வா அவண் உய்ந்த வாறும்","இருந்தவற் கெல்லாம் எடுத்து மொழிந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He tells the man how his ship went down in the black sea under the wind, how his goods were ruined, and how he survived by clinging to a hard-won float.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 518","lb":"CC518","ev":"Sridattan recounts the shipwreck and his escape on a piece of wreckage.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:519":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":519,"t":["மானும் மரனும் இரங்க மதவலி","தானுற்ற துன்பந் தரனுக் குரைத்தபின்","றேனு மமிழ்துந் திளைத்தாங் கினியன","வூனமில் கட்டுரைக் குள்ளங் குளிர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"When the mighty merchant has told his grief so that even deer and trees pity him, the stranger's flawless words, sweet as honey and nectar, cool his heart.","n":"The listener is first named here, in the dative தரனுக்கு; the comfort is described before its content is given in 520.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 519","lb":"CC519","ev":"Sridattan finishes his tale to Dharan and is comforted by his reply.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:520":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":520,"t":["விஞ்சைகள் வல்லேன் விளிந்தநின் றோழரொ","டெஞ்சிய வான்பொரு ளெல்லா மிமைப்பினுள்","வஞ்சமொன் றின்றி மறித்தே தருகுவன்","னெஞ்சிற் குழைந்து நினையன்மி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Dharan says he is master of magic arts and will, without any deceit and in the blink of an eye, give back the dead companions together with all the wealth that remains, so the merchant should not grieve.","n":"'Without any deceit' (வஞ்சம் ஒன்று இன்றி) is spoken by the man who, as 574 says, raised the storm by deceit; the promise to return 'dead' companions is kept because they were never dead (580, 582).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 520","lb":"CC520","ev":"Dharan promises to restore Sridattan's companions and goods by his magic arts.","cl":[],"ag":["தரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:521":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":521,"t":["உரையகங் கொள்ள வுணர்த்தின னாகி","வரையக மேற வலிமின மென்னா","விரைசெலல் வெம்பரி மேழக மேற்றிக்","குரைகழன் மைந்தனைக் கொண்டு பறந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Having made his word believed, Dharan says 'let us climb the mountain', mounts the merchant on a swift ram and flies off carrying him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 521","lb":"CC521","ev":"Dharan sets Sridattan on a flying ram and carries him up through the air.","cl":[],"ag":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:522":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":522,"t":["விசும்பிவர் மேகம் விரைவினர் போழ்ந்து","பகும்புயற் றண்டுளி பக்க நனைப்ப","நயந்தனர் போகி நறுமலர்ச் சோலை","யசும்பிவர் சார லருவரை சார்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"They split the sky-climbing clouds at speed, the cold rain wetting their sides, and come gladly to a hard mountain with fragrant groves on its dripping slopes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 522","lb":"CC522","ev":"Dharan and Sridattan fly through rain clouds and land on a mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:523":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":523,"t":["கண்டா லினியன காண்டற் கரியன","தண்டா மரையவ டாழுந் தகையன","கொண்டான் கொழுங்கனி கோட்டிடைத் தூங்குவ","வுண்டா னமிழ்தொத் துடம்பு குளிர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The merchant takes ripe fruits hanging from the branches, sweet to see and rare, fit for the lotus goddess, eats them like nectar, and his body is cooled.","n":"Third use in a few verses of the image of cooling (519 heart, 523 body): the poet stages relief in steps after the wreck.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amudham","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 523","lb":"CC523","ev":"Sridattan eats the mountain's fruits and is refreshed.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:524":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":524,"t":["மழை தவழ் சோலை மலைமிசை நீண்ட","குழைதவழ் குங்குமங் கோழரை நாகந்","தழைதவழ் சந்தனச் சோலையி னோக்கி","யிழைதவழ் மார்ப னினிதி னுவந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Looking on the groves of saffron, naga trees and sandal on the cloud-wrapped mountain, the jewel-chested merchant rejoices.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 524","lb":"CC524","ev":"Sridattan views the mountain groves with delight.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:525":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":525,"t":["கோதை யருவிக் குளிர்வரை மேனின்று","காதங் கடந்தபின் கன்னிக் கொடிமதி","னாத னுறைவதோர் நன்னக ருண்டங்குப்","போது மெழுகெனப் போயினர் சார்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Dharan tells him that a kaatham beyond this cool mountain of garland-like falls lies a fine city where the lord of flag-hung walls dwells, and bids him come; they go and reach it.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 carry Dharan's words (…உண்டு அங்குப் போதும் எழுக) inside the narration without a speech tag.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 525","lb":"CC525","ev":"Dharan leads Sridattan onward to the Vidyadhara king's city.","cl":[],"ag":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:526":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":526,"t":["மேகமே மிடைந்து தாழ விருள்கொண்ட வெள்ளிக் குன்ற","மாகத்து விளங்கித் தோன்றும் வனப்புநாம் வகுக்க லுற்றா","னாகந்தான் கரிய தொன்று கீழ்நின்று நடுங்கக் கவ்விப்","பாகமே விழுங்கப் பட்ட பான்மதி போன்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet begins the description: the silver mountain, cloaked below by dark clouds and shining above, looks like the white moon half swallowed by a black serpent gripping it from beneath.","n":"An eclipse image: the dark serpent of the eclipse holds the moon from below, so the white peak shows only in part. The silver mountain is the Vidyadhara range named in 546 (வெள்ளிவேதண்டம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 526","lb":"CC526","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:527":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":527,"t":["துளங்குபொன் னகரின் றன்மை சொல்லலாஞ் சிறிதோர் தேவன்","விளங்குபொன் னுலகத் துள்ள துப்புர விடங்க ளெல்லா","மளந்துகொண் டின்பம் பூரித் தணிநக ராக்கி மேலா","லிளங்கதிர்ப் பருதி சூட்டி யியற்றிய தென்ன லாமே."],"p":"","r":"The golden city, the poet says, can be described a little as if a god had measured out every fine place of the shining heaven, filled it with delight, built it into a city and crowned it with the young sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 527","lb":"CC527","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:528":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":528,"t":["பொங்கி யாயிரந் தாமரை பூத்தபோற்","செங்க ணாயிரஞ் சேர்ந்தவன் பொன்னகர்","கொங்கு தோய்குழ லாரொடுங் குன்றின்மேற்","றங்கு கின்றது போற்றகை சான்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The golden city looks as though the thousand-eyed god's golden city, red eyes blooming like a thousand lotuses, had come to rest on the mountain with its fragrant-haired women.","n":"The thousand eyes like a thousand lotuses are Indra's; the comparison sets the Vidyadhara city beside heaven's capital, as 535 does again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 528","lb":"CC528","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:529":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":529,"t":["கிடங்கு சூழ்மதிற் கேழ்கிளர் பூங்கொடி","மடங்க னோக்கியர் வாண்முகம் போலுமென்","றுடங்கு வெண்மதி யுள்குளி ரத்தங்","குடங்கை யாற்கொம்மை கொட்டுவ போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The bright flags on the moated walls seem to pat the moon with hollowed palms, as though calling it like the faces of the doe-eyed women, so that the moon is cooled within.","n":"The sense of line 3 (உடங்கு வெண்மதி உள்குளிர) and of கொம்மை கொட்டுதல் is uncertain; the conceit pairs the moon with the women's faces, as 532 does.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 529","lb":"CC529","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:530":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":530,"t":["திருவ மேகலை தெள்ளரிக் கிண்கிணி","பரவை யாழ்குழல் பண்ணமை மென்முழா","வுருவம் யாருடை யாரென் றொளிர் நக","ரரவ வாய்திறந் தார்ப்பது போன்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The sounds of girdles, anklets, harps, flutes and tuned drums make the bright city seem to open a noisy mouth and cry out, 'whose is beauty like this?'","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 530","lb":"CC530","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:531":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":531,"t":["செம்பொன் மாடங்கள் சென்னி யழுத்திய","வம்பொற் றிண்ணிலை யாய்மணித் தூவிகள்","வெம்பு நீள்சுடர் வீழ்ந்து சுடுதலிற்","பைம்பொற் கொப்புள் பரந்தன போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The gem finials on the gold mansions, scorched by the long rays of the burning sun, seem to break out in blisters of gold.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 531","lb":"CC531","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:532":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":532,"t":["உருளி மாமதி யோட்டொழித் தோங்கிய","வெருளி மாடங்கண் மேற்றுயி லெய்தலின்","மருளி மான்பிணை நோக்கினல் லார்முகத்","தருளி னாலழ லாற்றுவ போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Because the moon, its chariot halted, rests on the lofty mansions, the faces of the deer-eyed women seem in kindness to soothe its heat.","n":"Line 1 (உருளி மாமதி ஓட்டொழித்து) and the moon's 'heat' (அழல்) are hard; the reading that the moon pauses on the towers and is cooled by the women's faces is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 532","lb":"CC532","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:533":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":533,"t":["அசும்பு பொன்வரை யாய்மணிப் பூண்களும்","பசும்பொன் மாலையும் பட்டுழிப் பட்டவை","நயந்து கொள்பவ ரின்மையி னன்னகர்","விசும்பு பூத்தது போன்றன வீதியே."],"p":"","r":"Jewels and garlands of fresh gold lie where they fell, with no one wanting to pick them up, so that the streets look as if the sky had come into flower.","n":"The city's wealth is measured by indifference to it: dropped jewels lie unclaimed, a sharp contrast to the merchant who in 514 is reproached for taking wealth to be the only thing of worth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 533","lb":"CC533","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:534":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":534,"t":["தேக்க ணின்னகிற் றேனொடு கூட்டமைத்","தாக்கப் பட்ட வளவில் கொழும்புகை","வீக்கி மாடந் திறந்திட மெல்லவே","வூக்கி வாய்விட் டுயிர்ப்பன போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The rich smoke of teak-dark agil mixed with honey, pent in the mansions, escapes slowly as they open, as though the mansions were breathing out sighs.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 534","lb":"CC534","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:535":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":535,"t":["தப்பில் வாய்மொழித் தானவர் வைகிய","வொப்பின் மாநக ரொண்மைமற் றியாதெனிற்","கப்பத் திந்திரன் காமுறு மாமணிச்","செப்பு வாய்திறந் தன்னதொர் செம்மற்றே."],"p":"","r":"If one asks what the splendour of this matchless city of truthful Vidyadharas is like, it is like the gem casket Indra longs for, opened.","n":"The city's people are called தப்பில் வாய்மொழி, of faultless speech, in the same chapter whose host has just lied to bring the merchant there (520, 574).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 535","lb":"CC535","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:536":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":536,"t":["நன்னகர் நோக்கி நாய்கன் நாகங்கொல் புகுந்த தென்னப்","பொன்னகர் பொலியப் புக்குப்பொங்குமா மழைக டங்கு","மின்னவிர் செம்பொன் மாடத்திருவரு மிழிந்து புக்குப்","பின்னவன் விருந்து பேணிப் பேசினன் பிறங்கு தாரான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the fine city, the merchant wonders whether they have entered the serpents' world; they go in, descend into a gold mansion among the clouds, and Dharan entertains him as a guest and speaks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 536","lb":"CC536","ev":"Dharan brings Sridattan into his mansion in the city, entertains him, and begins to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","தரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:537":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":537,"t":["மாடியந் தானை மன்னர் மாமணி நாக மாகக்","கேடில்சீர்க் கலுழனாய கலுழவே கற்குத் தேவி","தோடலர் கோதைத் தொல்சீர்த் தாரணி சுரும்புண் கண்ணி","யாடவ ரறிவு போழு மணிமுலை யணங்கி னன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Dharan begins: the king Kaluzhavegan, a Garuda to rival kings who are like great gem-bearing serpents, has as queen Dharani of ancient fame, bee-haunted eyes and breasts that pierce men's judgment, a goddess among women.","n":"The king's name is split across words as printed (கலுழனாய கலுழவே கற்கு) and is confirmed in 546; the opening phrase மாடியந் தானை is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 537","lb":"CC537","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:538":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":538,"t":["விண்ணகம் வணங்க வெண்கோட் டிளம்பிறை முளைத்த தேபோற்","பண்ணகத் தினிய சொல்லாள் பாவையைப் பயந்த ஞான்றே","யெண்ணிட மின்றி மன்ன ரிம்மலை யிறைகொண் டீண்டி","யண்ணலங் களிற்றி னுச்சி யருங்கல வெறுக்கை யீந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"When the sweet-voiced queen bore a daughter, like the young white crescent rising for the heavens to worship, kings without number crowded onto this mountain and offered rare jewels and wealth from the heads of their elephants.","n":"The princess is not named here; 550 names her Gandharvadattai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","mannar","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 538","lb":"CC538","ev":"At the princess's birth, kings gather on the Vidyadhara mountain bringing gifts to win her.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:539":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":539,"t":["மந்திரத் தரசன் வல்லே நிமித்திகன் வருக என்றாற்","கந்தரத் தோடு கோளின் சாதக மவனுஞ் செய்தா","னிந்திர திருவி லேய்ப்பக் குலவிய புருவத் தாட்கு","வந்தடை பான்மை மண்மேல் இராசமா புரத்த தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king called for his astrologer, who cast the princess's horoscope from the planets and declared that the one she is destined to join lives on earth, in Rajamapuram.","n":"பான்மை, one's allotted portion, is the word the king will take up in 554 (பான்மையார் விலக்குகிற்பார்). The prophecy points down from the sky-dwellers' city to the human town where Civakan lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","kaalam","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 539","lb":"CC539","ev":"The astrologer reads the princess's horoscope and names Rajamapuram on earth as the place of her destined husband.","cl":[["a woman's destined husband is fixed and can be read from the planets at her birth","general","reported",4,"vidyadhara_nimiththikan"]],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்","நிமித்திகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:540":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":540,"t":["அவனுரை தெளிந்து வேந்த னாசையு ளரசர் நிற்பக்","கவனங்கொள் புரவிக் கொட்பிற் காதலுங் கரந்து வைத்தா","னவனதே கருதிற் றாங்கொ லன்றுகொ லறிய லாகா","திவணது மறிது மென்று கோயிலுக் கேகி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Trusting the astrologer's word, the king kept the suitor kings standing in hope and hid his intent like the wheeling of a swift horse; unable to tell whether he meant it or not, they resolved to learn the matter there, and he went to the palace.","n":"Who speaks lines 3 and 4 (அவனதே கருதிற்றாங்கொல் … அறிதும் என்று) is unclear: the waiting kings guessing the king's mind, or the king weighing the astrologer's word; the subject of ஏகினான் is the king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kurippu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 540","lb":"CC540","ev":"Kaluzhavegan keeps the suitor kings waiting while concealing his plan, and withdraws to his palace.","cl":[],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:24"},"cc:541":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":541,"t":["பால்பரந் தன்ன பட்டார் பூவணை பசும்பொற் கட்டிற்","கால்பரந் திருந்த வெங்கட் கதிர்முலை கச்சின் வீக்கி","வேல்பரந் தனைய கண்ணார் வெண்மதிக் கதிர்பெய் கற்றை","போலிவர் கவரி வீச மன்னவ னிருந்த போழ்தின்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator sets the scene: the king sits on a couch of fresh gold spread with silk soft as milk, while women with spear-like eyes and breasts bound in bodices wave whisks white as the moon's rays.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 541","lb":"CC541","ev":"The Vidyadhara king sits at ease on his couch, fanned by women with whisks.","cl":[],"ag":["வேல் பரந்தனைய கண்ணார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:542":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":542,"t":["என்வர விசைக்க வென்ன வாயிலோ னிசைப்ப வேகி","மன்னர்தம் முடிகள் வேய்ந்த வயிரம்போழ்ந் துழுது சேந்த","பொன்னவிர் கழல்கொள் பாதம் பொழிமழைத் தடக்கை கூப்ப","வின்னுரை முகமன் கூறித் தானத்தி லிருக்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The guest asks the gatekeeper to announce him, enters and joins his hands before the king's gold-anklet feet, reddened by the diamonds of bowing kings' crowns, and the king greets him with sweet words and bids him take a seat.","n":"Who says என்வரவு இசைக்க is not stated; the merchant is the likeliest, but the Vidyadhara who brought him could be meant. The feet that kings' crowns have scored with diamonds make the welcome a condescension from a king of kings.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","inchol","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 542","lb":"CC542","ev":"Sridattan is announced by the gatekeeper, bows before the Vidyadhara king, and is welcomed and seated.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","வாயிலோன்","கலுழவேகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:543":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":543,"t":["முதிர்பெயன் மூரி வான முழங்கிவாய் விட்ட தொப்ப","வதிர்குரன் முரச நாண வமிர்துபெய்ம் மாரி யேய்ப்பக்","கதிர்விரி பூணி னாற்குத் தந்தைதாய் தாரங் காதன்","மதுரமா மக்கள் சுற்றம் வினவிமற் றிதுவுஞ் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"In a voice like ripe thunder that shames the war drum and falls like a rain of nectar, the king asks the jewelled merchant after his father, mother, wife, sons and kin, and goes on to say more.","n":"The voice is thunder and nectar at once: the king's majesty and his kindness are given in a single simile, preparing the speech that will level king and merchant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","inchol","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 543","lb":"CC543","ev":"The Vidyadhara king asks after Sridattan's family.","cl":[],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:544":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":544,"t":["இன்றைய தன்று கேண்மை யெமர்நும ரெழுவர் காறு","நின்றது கிழமை நீங்கா வச்சிர யாப்பின் னூழா","லன்றியு மறனு மொன்றே யரசன்யான் வணிக னீயே","யென்றிரண் டில்லை கண்டாய் யிதுநின தில்ல மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king tells the merchant that their bond is not of today: kinship between his people and the merchant's has held for seven generations, bound by fate in an unbreakable diamond tie, and since their dharma is one there is no 'I a king, you a merchant'; this is his own house.","n":"Two grounds are given for the friendship, ஊழ் and a shared அறம், and the second dissolves rank: the king of the Vidyadharas sets aside the difference of station in so many words. Whether அறன் here means a shared religion or a shared code of conduct the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","natpu","oozh","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 544","lb":"CC544","ev":"The Vidyadhara king declares an old family friendship with Sridattan and makes him at home.","cl":[["a bond of friendship kept for seven generations is fixed by fate and cannot be broken","general","reported",2,"kaluzhavegan"],["those who share one dharma are not divided as king and merchant","general","reported",3,"kaluzhavegan"]],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:545":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":545,"t":["மந்திர மன்னன் சொன்னீர் மாரியால் வற்றி நின்ற","சந்தனந் தளிர்த்த தேபோல் சீதத்தன் றளிர்த்து நோக்கி","யெந்தைக்குத் தந்தை சொன்னா னின்ணை மென்று கேட்ப","முந்தைத் தான் கேட்ட வாறே முழுதெடுத் தியம்புகின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"At the king's words Sridattan revives like a sandal tree withered by drought that puts out shoots in the rain, and when he asks whether it was his grandfather who spoke of the king, the king recounts in full what he had heard of old.","n":"The sense of line 3 is not certain: எந்தைக்குத் தந்தை சொன்னான் நின்னை may be the merchant's question (did my grandfather speak of you?) or part of the king's answer; and who tells the tale in line 4 is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","kudi","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 545","lb":"CC545","ev":"Sridattan, revived, asks about the old tie, and the king begins to tell its history.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","கலுழவேகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:546":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":546,"t":["வெள்ளிவே தண்டத் தங்கண் வீவில்தென் சேடிப் பாலிற்","கள்ளவிழ் கைதை வேலிக் காசில்காந் தார நாட்டுப்","புள்ளணி கிடங்கின் விச்சா லோகமா நகரிற் போகா","வெள்ளிவேற் கலுழ வேகன் வேதண்ட வேந்தர் வேந்தன்."],"p":"","r":"The tale begins: on the southern row of the silver mountain, in the faultless land of Gandhara with its screwpine hedges, in the great city of Vichaloka girt by a moat full of birds, ruled Kaluzhavegan of the silver spear, king of the kings of the mountain.","n":"வெள்ளி வேதண்டம் and its southern row (தென் சேடி) place the Vidyadhara realm in the Jain cosmic geography. Whether Kaluzhavegan names himself here or an ancestor of the line is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","vendhan","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 546","lb":"CC546","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:547":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":547,"t":["சங்குடைந் தனைய வெண்டா மரைமலர்த் தடங்கள் போலும்","நங்குடித் தெய்வங் கண்டீர் நமரங்கா ளறிமி னென்னக்","கொங்குடை முல்லைப் பைம்போ திருவடங் கிடந்த மார்ப,","விங்கடி பிழைப்ப தன்றா லெங்குல மென்று சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Pointing to pools of white lotuses like broken conch, the speaker says 'these are our clan deities, our people, know it', and declares that their clan does not stray from these feet.","n":"The verse nests a speech within a speech (என்ன ... என்று சொன்னான்), and the referent of இங்கு அடி, the feet the clan never fails, is not given; it may be the clan deity or the old pledge to the merchant's line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 547","lb":"CC547","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:548":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":548,"t":["பெருந்தகைக் குருசி றோழன் பெருவிலைக் கடக முன்கை","திருந்துபு வணங்கப் பற்றிச் சென்றுதன் னுரிமை காட்டப்","பொருந்துபு பொற்ப வோம்பிப் பொன்னிழை சுடர நின்ற","கருங்கண்ணி திறத்து வேறாக் கட்டுரை பயிற்று கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Taking the bracelet-adorned forearm of the noble prince's friend as he bowed, the king leads him into his inner quarters, shows him the dark-eyed girl who stands guarded and shining in gold, and begins to speak of her in a new strain.","n":"பெருந்தகைக் குருசில் தோழன் makes the merchant Civakan's friend, linking this distant court to the hero before he appears. Showing a guest the உரிமை, the women's apartments, is itself the proof of the intimacy the king has just claimed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","kizhamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 548","lb":"CC548","ev":"The king leads Sridattan into the women's quarters and shows him his daughter.","cl":[],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:549":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":549,"t":["எரிமணிப் பளிக்கு மாடத் தெழுந்ததோர் காம வல்லி","யருமணிக் கொடிகொன் மின்கொ லமரர்கோ னெழுதி வைத்த","வொருமணி குயின்ற பாவை யொன்று கொலென்று நாய்கன்","றிருமணிக் கொடியை யோரான் றெருமர மன்னன் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The merchant cannot tell whether he sees a creeper of desire risen in the crystal mansion, a vine of rare gems, lightning, or a jewelled doll painted by the king of the gods, and as he stands bewildered the king speaks.","n":"The run of questions with கொல் is the akam device of the man who cannot decide whether the woman is human (compare the Kural's அணங்குகொல்), given here to a merchant who looks on her as a father's guest, not a lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 549","lb":"CC549","ev":"Sridattan is bewildered at the sight of the princess.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","கலுழவேகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:550":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":550,"t":["தூசுலாய்க் கிடந்த வல்கு றுப்புறழ் தொண்டைச் செவ்வாய்","வாசவான் குழலின் மின்போல் யருமணிக் கொடிகொன் மின்கொ","யூசல்பாய்ந் தாடிக் காதிற் குண்டல மிலங்க நின்றாள்","காசில்யாழ்க் கணங்கொள் தெய்வக் காந்தர்வ தத்தை யென்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king names her: she who stands with her garment slipping on her hips, coral-red mouth and fragrant hair, flashing like lightning, her earrings shining as she leaps from the swing, is Gandharvadattai, the divine one who holds the throng of faultless lutes.","n":"Line 2 repeats words from 549 line 2 and ends cut off; the description may be disturbed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 550","lb":"CC550","ev":"The king names his daughter Gandharvadattai to the merchant.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:551":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":551,"t":["விளங்கினா ளுலக மெல்லாம் வீணையின் வனப்பி னாலே","யளந்துணர் வரிய நங்கைக் கருமணி முகிழ்த்த வேபோ","லிளங்கதிர் முலையு மாகத் திடங்கொண்டு பரந்த மின்னிற்","றுளங்குநுண் ணுசுப்புந் தோன்றா துரவரு வென்ன வுண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Her fame lights the whole world through the beauty of her vina playing; she is a girl past measuring, whose young breasts swell like budding dark gems and whose slender waist, fine as lightning, cannot be seen at all, though it must exist.","n":"Her renown is for her art before her beauty: the vina, the instrument of the contest to come, is named first. Whether this is still the king speaking or the narrator is not marked.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 551","lb":"CC551","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:552":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":552,"t":["நின்மக ளிவளை நீயே நின்பதிக் கொண்டு போகி","யின்னிசை பொருது வெல்வா னியாவனே யானு மாக","வன்னவற் குரிய ளென்ன வடிப்பணி செய்வ லென்றான்","றன்னமர் தேவி கேட்டுத் தத்தைக்கே தக்கதென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king tells the merchant to take this girl, now called the merchant's own daughter, to his city: whoever defeats her in a contest of sweet music shall have her, even if it be himself, and he will serve that man at his feet; the queen, hearing it, says this is fitting for Dattai.","n":"நின்மகள் transfers the daughter to the merchant in a word: she leaves as his child. The condition 'even if I be the one' (யானும் ஆக) makes the contest absolute, binding the king himself, and the queen's consent is recorded as a separate act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","varaivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 552","lb":"CC552","ev":"The king entrusts Gandharvadattai to Sridattan, to be given to whoever beats her at music, and the queen consents.","cl":[],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்","தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:553":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":553,"t":["முனிவரும் போக பூமிப் போகம்முட் டாது பெற்றுந்","தனியவ ராகி வாழ்தல் சாதுய ரதனி னில்லை","கனிபடு கிளவி யார்தங் காதலர் கவானிற் றுஞ்சிற்","பனியிரு விசும்பிற் றேவர் பான்மையிற் றென்று சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The king says that even with the tireless pleasures of the land of enjoyment granted without fail, there is no misery like living alone, while to sleep in the lap of a lover sweet of speech is the very state of the gods in the cool sky.","n":"போக பூமி is the Jain cosmology's land of enjoyment, where couples are born together; the king uses a Jain heaven to argue for marriage. முனிவரும் is better taken as முனிவு அரும் (pleasures one never tires of) than as 'even sages'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","punarchi","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 553","lb":"CC553","ev":"","cl":[["to live alone is the greatest misery, even amid unfailing heavenly pleasures, and a lover's embrace equals the gods' state","general","reported",2,"kaluzhavegan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:554":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":554,"t":["நூற்படு புலவன் சொன்ன நுண்பொரு ணுழைந்தி யானும்","வேற்கடற் றானை வேந்தர் வீழ்ந்திரந் தாலு நேரேன்","சேற்கடை மதர்வை நோக்கிற் சில்லரித் தடங்க ணங்கை","பாற்படு காலம் வந்தாற் பான்மையார் விலக்கு கிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The king says that, having grasped the subtle meaning of what the learned seer foretold, he would not give her even if kings with armies like the sea fell at his feet and begged; when the destined time for this wide-eyed girl comes, who can stop those it is destined for?","n":"Fate here does not defeat a plan; it is the king's reason for refusing all suitors and leaving the outcome to the contest. The seer (நூற்படு புலவன்) is mentioned but his prophecy is not told in this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 554","lb":"CC554","ev":"","cl":[["when the destined time comes, no one can prevent what is destined","general","reported",4,"kaluzhavegan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:555":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":555,"t":["படைப்பருங் கற்பி னாடன் பாவையைப் பரிவு நீக்கிக்","கொடைக்குரிப் பால வெல்லாங் கொடுத்தபின் கூற்று முட்கும்","விடைப்பருந் தானை வேந்தன் வேண்டுவ வெறுப்ப நல்கித்","தொடுத்தலர் கோதை வீணா பதிக்கிது சொல்லி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The king of an army that even Death fears puts off his grief for his daughter of matchless chastity, gives all the things proper to a bride's giving and more than was wanted, and says this to Veenapathi, her garlanded companion.","n":"பரிவு நீக்கி: the giving is done only after the father masters his grief, and the dowry is called கொடைக்குரிப்பால, what belongs to the act of giving away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","makkal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 555","lb":"CC555","ev":"The king gives his daughter's dowry and turns to Veenapathi.","cl":[],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்","வீணாபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:556":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":556,"t":["உடம்பினொ டுயிரிற் பின்னி யொருவயி னீங்கல் செல்லா","நெடுங்கணுந் தோளும் போலு நேரிழை அரிவை நீநின்","றடங்கணி தனிமை நீங்கத் தந்தையுந் தாயு மாகி","யடங்கல ரட்ட வேலா னாணையி ராமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king tells Veenapathi that she and the princess are like eye and shoulder, woven together as body and life and never apart; she must be father and mother to the girl so that she never feels alone, and both must live under the command of the spear-bearer who slew his foes.","n":"The spear-bearer of line 4 is unnamed; it may be the merchant, now her guardian, or the king's own authority. The charge 'be her father and mother' answers the king's claim in 553 that living alone is the worst misery.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thunai","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 556","lb":"CC556","ev":"The king charges Veenapathi to be a parent to Gandharvadattai.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","வீணாபதி"],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:557":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":557,"t":["அருமணி வயிரம் வேய்ந்த வருங்கலப் பேழை யைஞ்ஞூ","றெரிமணி செம்பொ னார்ந்த விராயிரம் யவனப் பேழை","திருமணிப் பூணி னாற்குச் சினந்தலை மழுங்க லின்றிக்","குருமணி முடியிற் றேய்த்த தரன்றமர் கொள்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king orders five hundred chests set with rare gems and diamonds and two thousand Yavana chests full of red gold and blazing gems to be carried for the jewelled merchant by Dharan's people.","n":"சினந்தலை மழுங்கல் இன்றி and குருமணி முடியில் தேய்த்த are hard to construe; 'Dharan's people' depends on dividing தரன்றமர் as தரன் தமர்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 557","lb":"CC557","ev":"The king sends chests of gems and gold with the merchant.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்",""],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:558":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":558,"t":["பல்வினைப் பவளப் பாய்காற் பசுமணி யிழிகை வம்பார்","நல்லகில் விம்மு கட்டி றவிசொடு நிலைக்கண் ணாடி","மெல்லிய தூப முட்டி மேதகு நானச் செப்போ","டல்லவுங் கொள்க வென்றா னணங்குடை நிணங்கொள் வேலான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of the fat-smeared spear adds couches of worked coral with gem-set legs and fragrant aloe, seats, standing mirrors, soft incense, caskets of musk and more.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 558","lb":"CC558","ev":"The king adds furniture, mirrors and perfumes to the gifts.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்",""],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:559":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":559,"t":["விளக்கழ லுறுத்த போலும் விசியுறு போர்வைத் தீந்தேன்","றுளக்கற வொழுகி யன்ன துய்யறத் திரண்ட திண்கோல்","கொளத்தகு திவவுத் திங்கட் கோணிரைத் தனைய வாணி","யளப்பருஞ் சுவைகொ ணல்யா ழாயிர மமைக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He orders a thousand fine lutes of boundless sweetness, their skin covering taut as if warmed by lamp-flame, their stems smooth as flowing honey, their frets set like a row of moons.","n":"The last and fullest gift is the vina, the instrument of the contest: a thousand of them go with her, as if the music that will decide her marriage travels as her dowry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 559","lb":"CC559","ev":"The king gives a thousand lutes.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்",""],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:560":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":560,"t":["அரக்கெறி குவளை வாட்க ணவ்வளைத் தோளி னாளைப்","பரப்பமை காதற் றாயர் பற்பல்காற் புல்லிக்கொண்டு","திருப்புறக் கொடுத்த செம்பொற் றாமரை போன்று கோயில்","புரிக்குழன் மடந்தை போகப் புலம்பொடு மடிந்த தன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Her loving mothers embrace the girl of sword-sharp lily eyes again and again, and as she leaves like a red-gold lotus turning its back, the palace sinks in grief.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 560","lb":"CC560","ev":"Gandharvadattai's mothers embrace her and the palace grieves as she departs.","cl":[],"ag":["தாயர்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:561":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":561,"t":["காம்புபொன் செய்த பிச்சங் கதிர்மணி குடையொ டேந்தித்","தாம்பலர் கவரி வீசக் கிண்கிணி ததும்ப நாகப்","பாம்புபைத் தனைய வல்குற் பல்கலை மிழற்ற வேகி","யாம்பனா றமுதச் செவ்வா யரசனைத் தொழுது நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"With parasols of jewelled rays on golden poles and many whisks waving, her anklets jingling and her girdle chiming, she goes and stands with folded hands before the king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 561","lb":"CC561","ev":"Gandharvadattai goes in state and bows before the king.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:562":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":562,"t":["அடிக்கல மரற்ற வேகி யரும்பெறற் றாதை பாத","முடிக்கலஞ் சொரியச் சென்னி யிறைஞ்சலு முரிந்து மின்னுக்","கொடிப்பல நுடங்கி யாங்குத் தோழியர் குழாத்து ணிற்ப","வடுத்தனன் புல்லி வேந்தன் னாற்றுகி லாது சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Her anklets sounding, she bows her head at her father's feet so that her ornaments spill, and stands among her companions like a cluster of swaying lightning-creepers, and the king, unable to bear it, embraces her and speaks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","panivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 562","lb":"CC562","ev":"Gandharvadattai bows at her father's feet, and he embraces her in grief.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","தோழியர்","கலுழவேகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:563":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":563,"t":["வலம்புரி யீன்ற முத்தம்மண்மிசை யவர்கட் கல்லால்","வலம்புரி பயத்தை எய்தா தனையரே மகளி ரென்ன","நலம்புரிந் தனைய காதற் றேவிதன் னவையை நீங்கக்","குலம்புரிந் தனைய குன்றிற் கதிபதி கூறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the mountain says that daughters are like the pearl a right-whorled conch bears, which benefits those on the earth and never the conch, and with this he consoles the queen as she leaves the assembly.","n":"Line 3 (தேவி தன் அவையை நீங்க) can be read as the queen leaving the assembly or as the king dispelling the queen's sorrow; either way the saying is said to console, so it turns a loss into the order of things.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 563","lb":"CC563","ev":"The king consoles the queen with a saying on daughters as the princess departs.","cl":[["daughters, like the conch's pearls, profit others and not those who bore them","general","reported",2,"kaluzhavegan"]],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","தேவி"],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:564":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":564,"t":["இன்சுவை யாழொ டன்ன மிளங்கிளி மழலை மஞ்ஞை","பொன்புனை யூக மந்தி பொறிமயிர்ப் புறவம் பொன்னார்","மென்புன மருளி னோக்கின் மானின மாதி யாகத்","தன்புறஞ் சூழப் போகித் தளிரியல் விமானஞ் சேர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Surrounded by sweet lutes, swans, lisping young parrots, peacocks, golden monkeys, spotted doves and herds of deer with timid eyes, the tender girl goes and reaches the aerial car.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 564","lb":"CC564","ev":"Gandharvadattai leaves with her pets and lutes and boards the aerial car.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:25"},"cc:565":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":565,"t":["வெற்றிவேன் மணிமுடிக்","கொற்றவ னொருமக","ளற்றமில் பெரும்படைச்","சுற்றமோ டியங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The only daughter of the victorious vidyadhara king sets out, moving with a great army as her retinue.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 565","lb":"CC565","ev":"Gandharvadattai sets out from the Silver Mountain with a great armed retinue.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:566":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":566,"t":["கண்ணயற் களிப்பன","வண்ணல்யானை யாயிரம்","விண்ணகத் தியங்குதே","ரெண்ணவற் றிரட்டியே."],"p":"","r":"Her escort counts a thousand elephants rejoicing at the eye's edge and twice that number of chariots that move through the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 566","lb":"CC566","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:567":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":567,"t":["விற்படை விலக்குவ","பொற்படைப் புரவியு","முற்படக் கிளந்தவற்றி","னற்புடைய நாற்றியே"],"p":"","r":"Horses in golden trappings, able to turn back an army of bowmen, go with her in a number multiplied over the troops already named.","n":"The count in நற்புடைய நாற்றியே is not clear: it may be fourfold the chariots or simply a great many.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 567","lb":"CC567","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:568":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":568,"t":["பாறுடைப் பருதிவேல்","வீறுடை யிளையரு","மாறிரட்டி யாயிரர்","கூறுதற் கரியரே."],"p":"","r":"Young warriors bearing sun-bright spears that the vultures follow, twelve thousand of them, beyond telling, march in the train.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 568","lb":"CC568","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:569":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":569,"t":["மாகநீள் விசும்பிடை","மேகநின் றிடித்தலி","னாகநின் றதிர்ந்தவர்க்","கேகலாவ தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"When the clouds thunder in the high sky, the elephants trumpet back at them, and the clouds, shaken, cannot pass on.","n":"Who is shaken and cannot go on (அதிர்ந்தவர்க்கு ஏகலாவது இல்லையே) is not fixed by the words; it may be the clouds or anyone who hears.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 569","lb":"CC569","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:570":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":570,"t":["வெஞ்சின வெகுளியிற்","குஞ்சர முழங்கலி","மஞ்சுதம் வயிறழிந்","தஞ்சிநீ ருகுத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"At the roaring of the furious elephants the clouds split their bellies and shed their water in fear.","n":"The army's passage through the sky is made into a storm: the rain is the clouds' terror at the elephants, not weather.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 570","lb":"CC570","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:571":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":571,"t":["வேழமும் மதத்தொடு","தாழ்புயல் கலந்துட","னாழ்கட லகம்புறம்","வீழ்தர விரைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The elephants' ichor, mingled with the falling rain, pours down into the deep sea, inside and out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 571","lb":"CC571","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:572":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":572,"t":["மல்லன்மாக் கடலிடைக்","கல்லெனக் கலங்கவிழ்த்","தல்லலுற் றழுங்கிய","செல்வனுற்ற செப்புவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet turns to tell what befell the rich man who had grieved when his ship capsized in the wide sea.","n":"The narrator's 'let us tell' (செப்புவாம்) closes the description of the flight and returns to Sridattan, last seen at Kaluzhavegan's court.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 572","lb":"CC572","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:573":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":573,"t":["பானிறப் பனிவரை","மேனிற மிகுத்தன","நீனிற நிழன்மணி","தானிரைத் தகமெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"The milk-white snowy mountain shines whiter still, with gems of blue shadow set in rows all through it.","n":"The verse does not say what it describes; it may be the Silver Mountain the party leaves, or the shining place where the ship is now shown.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 573","lb":"CC573","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:574":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":574,"t":["வஞ்சமின் மனத்தினா","னெஞ்சகம் புகன்றுக","விஞ்சையம் பெருமகன்","வஞ்சமென் றுணர்த்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The great vidyadhara, to the delight of the guileless man's heart, makes known to him that the whole wreck was a deception.","n":"The man deceived is called வஞ்சமின் மனத்தினான், one with no deceit in his mind, in the same verse that names the deceit (வஞ்சம்); the pun sets the guileless merchant against the vidyadhara's ruse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 574","lb":"CC574","ev":"Dharan reveals to Sridattan that the shipwreck was a trick he staged.","cl":[],"ag":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:575":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":575,"t":["நங்கைதன் னலத்தினான்","மங்குல்வெள்ளி மால்வரை","யெங்குமன்ன ரீண்டினார்","சங்குவிம்மு தானையார்."],"p":"","r":"Dharan tells him that because of the maiden's beauty kings with conch-sounding armies thronged everywhere on the cloud-capped Silver Mountain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 575","lb":"CC575","ev":"Suitor kings throng the Silver Mountain for Gandharvadattai.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:576":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":576,"t":["ஈரலங்க லேந்துவே","லாரலங்கன் மார்பினான்","கார்கலந்த கைக்கணி","சீர்கலந்து செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, with dripping garland, spear and ஆர் flowers on his chest, takes counsel with the astrologer of generous hand, who speaks with grace.","n":"Which of the two செப்பினான் makes the speaker, the king or the astrologer, is not settled by the line; the next verse is the astrologer's answer.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 576","lb":"CC576","ev":"Kaluzhavegan consults his astrologer about his daughter's marriage.","cl":[],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்","நிமித்திகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:577":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":577,"t":["மாதர்வாழ்வு மண்ணதே! யாதலா லலங்கலந்","தாதவிழ்ந்த மார்ப! நின், காதலான் கடலுளான்."],"p":"","r":"The astrologer declares that the maiden's life lies on the earth, and that therefore the king's beloved friend is even now out on the sea.","n":"These words are the astrologer's, quoted inside Dharan's report. Who நின் காதலான் is cannot be fixed from this verse alone; 578's 'my close friend' suggests Sridattan, the man who will take her to the earth. The verse survives as two long lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 577","lb":"CC577","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","கலுழவேகன்"],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:578":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":578,"t":["என்றுகூற வென்னையே","துன்றுகாதற் றோழனைச்","சென்றுநீ கொணர்கென","வன்றுவந்த வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"When the astrologer had spoken, Dharan says, the king bade him go and bring his dear friend, and that is how he came that day.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 578","lb":"CC578","ev":"Kaluzhavegan sends Dharan to fetch Sridattan.","cl":[],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்","தரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:579":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":579,"t":["துன்பமுற்ற வர்க்கலா","லின்பமில்லை யாதலி","னன்பமற்றி யானினைத்","துன்பத்தாற் றொடக்கினேன்."],"p":"","r":"Since there is no joy except for those who have first suffered, Dharan says, he bound Sridattan in suffering out of love.","n":"The maxim is offered as a deceiver's apology: the suffering was made on purpose, for the joy it would set off. The work does not comment on it here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","inbam","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 579","lb":"CC579","ev":"","cl":[["joy comes only to those who have first suffered","general","reported",1,"dharan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:580":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":580,"t":["பீழைசெய்து பெற்றனன்","வாழியென்று மாக்கட","லாழ்வித் திட்ட வம்பினைத்","தோழர்ச்சுட்டிக் காட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"Saying that he had gained his friend by causing him pain and wishing him long life, Dharan points out the ship he had sunk in the great sea and the companions in it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 580","lb":"CC580","ev":"Dharan shows Sridattan the sunken ship and his companions, safe.","cl":[],"ag":["தரன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:581":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":581,"t":["தேன்றரு மாரி போன்று திவ்விய கிளவி தம்மா","லூன்றரு குருதி வேலா னுள்ளகங் குளிர்ந்து விஞ்சைக்","கோன்றரு துன்பம் மற்றென் குலத்தொடு முடிக வென்றான்","கான்றுவில் வயிரம் வீசும் கனமணிக் குழையி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Cooled within by Dharan's divine words, like rain that brings honey, the spear-bearer with heavy gem earrings answers with a wish that the suffering the vidyadhara lord gave be brought to its end with his own line.","n":"The wish கோன்தரு துன்பம் மற்றென் குலத்தொடு முடிக may mean that such suffering end here, or that a suffering bringing such gain stay with his line to its end; the words allow both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 581","lb":"CC581","ev":"Sridattan answers Dharan graciously.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீதத்தன்","தரன்"],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:582":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":582,"t":["தோடலர் தெரிய லான்றன் றோழரைக் கண்டு காத","லூடலர்ந் தெழுந்து பொங்க வுருவத்தார் குழையப் புல்லிப்","பாடிரும் பௌவ முந்நீர்ப் பட்டது பகர்த லோடு","நாடக நாங்க ளுற்ற தென்றுகை யெறிந்து நக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing his friends, Sridattan embraces them with love welling up until his garland is crushed; they tell what they went through on the sea and, clapping their hands, laugh that it was all a play.","n":"நாடகம் turns the wreck that drowned all aboard (512) into a staged show, and the laughter (நக்கார்) comes after the danger, not in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","inbam","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 582","lb":"CC582","ev":"Sridattan embraces his companions, who laugh that their wreck was a play.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:583":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":583,"t":["கட்டழற் கதிய புண்ணிற் கருவரை யருவி யாரம்","பட்டது போன்று நாய்கன் பரிவுதீர்ந் தினியர் சூழ","மட்டவிழ் கோதை யோடு மண்கனை முழவ மூதூர்க்","கட்டவிழ் தாரி னான்றன் கடிமனை மகிழ்ந்து புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a mountain waterfall poured on a burning wound, the merchant's grief is quenched, and with his dear ones around him and the garlanded maiden he joyfully enters his guarded house in the old city of drums.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 583","lb":"CC583","ev":"Sridattan comes home to Rajamapuram with Gandharvadattai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:584":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":584,"t":["பெருமனை குறுக லோடும் பிறையென விலங்கித் தோன்றுந்","திருநுதன் மனைவி செம்பொற் கொடியென விறைஞ்சி நிற்ப","வருமுலை பொதிர்ப்ப வாங்கி வண்டின மிரியப் புல்லிக்","கதிர்நகை முறுவன் மாதர் கண்ணுறு கவலை தீர்த்தான்."],"p":"","r":"As he nears the great house his wife, her brow shining like the crescent, stands bowing like a golden creeper; he raises her, embraces her and takes away the worry from her eyes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 584","lb":"CC584","ev":"Sridattan embraces his wife and ends her anxiety.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","மனைவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:585":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":585,"t":["சந்திர காந்த மென்னுந் தண்மணி நிலத்தி னங்கண்","வெந்தெரி பிசும்பொன் வெள்ளி பளிங்கொடு பவளம் பாய்த்திக்","கந்தெரி மணியிற் செய்த கன்னியா மாட மெய்திப்","பைந்தொடிப் பாவை யொன்றும் பரிவிலள் வைகி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The maiden comes to the maidens' mansion built on a floor of cool moonstone with gold, silver, crystal and coral, its pillars set with gems, and dwells there without distress.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 585","lb":"CC585","ev":"Gandharvadattai is lodged in the maidens' mansion.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:586":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":586,"t":["பாசிழைப் பரவை யல்குற் பசுங்கதிர்க் கலாபம் வீங்கக்","காசுகண் பரிய வைகிக் கடன்றலைக் கழிந்த பின்னாத்","தூசணி பரவை யல்குற் றுளங்குநுண் ணுசுப்பிற் பாவை","யாசறு வரவும் தந்தை வலித்தது மறியச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"After the household duties are done, Sridattan tells his wife, so that she knows it, of the faultless maiden's coming and of what her father has resolved.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 describe a woman resting till her duties are done; whether she is the wife or the maiden, and so to whom Sridattan speaks, depends on reading பாவை in line 3 against the subject of வைகி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 586","lb":"CC586","ev":"Sridattan tells his wife of Gandharvadattai's arrival and her father's purpose.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","மனைவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:587":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":587,"t":["வண்டுண மலர்ந்த கோதை வாயொருப் பட்டு நேரத்","தெண்கட லமிர்தம் பெய்த செப்பெனச் செறிந்து வீங்கிப்","பெண்டிரு மாண்மை வெஃகிப் பேதுறு முலையி னாளைக்","கண்டவர் மருள நாளைக் கடிவினை முடித்து மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"His wife consents, and he says that next day they will carry through the rite for the girl whose breasts make even women covet manhood, so that all who see her marvel.","n":"கடிவினை is the rite that leads to her marriage; the verse does not yet name the lute contest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 587","lb":"CC587","ev":"Sridattan's wife consents and he resolves to begin the marriage rite next day.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவி","சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:588":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":588,"t":["மால்வரை வயிறு போழ்ந்து வல்லவர் மதியிற் றந்த","பால்வரை மணியும் பொன்னும் பற்பல கொண்டு புக்குக்","கால்பொரு கழலி னானுங் காவலற் கண்டு சொன்னான்","வேல்பொரு தானை யானும் வேண்டுவ விதியி னேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Taking much gold and many gems that skilled men had cut from the mountain's belly, the anklet-wearer goes in, sees the ruler and tells him; the king of the spear army grants what he asks, as rule allows.","n":"The verse does not name the king; at this point of the story Rajamapuram's ruler is the usurper Kattiyangaran, and his consent is bought with gems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 588","lb":"CC588","ev":"Sridattan brings gifts to Kattiyangaran and gains his consent to what he asks for Gandharvadattai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:26"},"cc:589":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":589,"t":["மையன்மத யானைநிரை மன்னன்மகிழ்ந் தானாப்","பொய்யில்புகழ் நாய்கன்மத வொளியினொடு போகி","நொய்தின்மனை யெய்தியிது செய்கென நொடித்தான்","மொய்கொண்முலை பாயமுகை விண்டலர்ந்த தாரான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of the musth-elephant ranks is pleased, and the merchant of unfailing fame, his garland opened by pressing breasts, goes off glowing with joy, reaches his house quickly and gives the order to begin.","n":"The king who consents here is the usurper Kattiyangaran, so the contest that will bring Civakan his first bride is held by leave of the man who killed his father.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 589","lb":"CC589","ev":"Kattiyangaran approves the merchant's request, and Sridattan hurries home and orders the work begun.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:590":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":590,"t":["நானக்கிடங் காடைநகர் நாகத்திடை நன்பொன்","வானக்கிடு மாட்சியதோர் மண்டபங்செய் கென்ன","மீனத்திடை நாள்கிழமை வெள்ளிசயை பக்கம்","கானத்திடை வேங்கையெழக் கண்ணினர்கள் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He orders a hall of fine gold, fit to be set in the sky, built in the midst of the moated city, and the reckoners fix its time: the month of Meenam, Friday, the tithi Jaya, the bright fortnight, when the vengai blooms in the forest.","n":"The time is fixed by sign, weekday, tithi and fortnight, with the vengai's flowering as the seasonal mark; who reckons it (கண்ணினர்கள்) is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 590","lb":"CC590","ev":"Sridattan orders a gold hall built in the city, and its auspicious time is fixed.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:591":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":591,"t":["நட்புப்பகை யுட்கினொடு நன்பொன்விளை கழனி","பட்டினொடு பஞ்சுதுகில் பைம்பொனொடு கான","மட்டசுவை வல்சியினொ டியாதுமொழி யாம","லொட்டிப்பதி னாயிரவ ருற்றுமுயல் கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Ten thousand workers, friend, foe and those held in awe alike, are given fields that yield gold, silk, cotton cloth, fresh gold and food sweet with forest honey, and without a word set to their labour together.","n":"Reading நட்புப்பகை யுட்கினொடு as the workers' range, friends, enemies and the fearful, all drawn in by the wages, is a guess at a compressed line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 591","lb":"CC591","ev":"Ten thousand workers are paid in land, cloth, gold and food and labour on the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["பதினாயிரவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:592":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":592,"t":["வண்டுபடு தேறனற வாய்விடொடு பருகிக்","கண்டதொழிற் கணிச்சிகளிற் கயம்படநன் கிடித்தாங்","கெண்டிசையு மேற்பப்படுத் தேற்றியதன் மேலாற்","கண்டுருகு பொன்னினிலங் காமுறுவ புனைந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Drinking toddy with its bee-sought fragrance, they break the ground deep with picks, level it to suit all eight directions, and over it lay a floor of melted gold that delights the eye.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 592","lb":"CC592","ev":"The workers dig, level and lay a gold floor for the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["பதினாயிரவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:593":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":593,"t":["பொன்செய்குடங் கோத்தனைய வெருத்திற்பொலி பொற்றூண்","மின்செய் பசும் பொன்னிலத்து வீறுபெற நாட்டி","மன்பவள மேனவின்று பளிக்கலகு பரப்பி","நன்செய்வெளி வேய்ந்துசுவர் தமனியத்தின் அமைத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"They set gold pillars, shaped like stacked gold pots, firmly in the fresh gold floor, lay coral beams, spread crystal slats, roof it well and build the walls of red gold.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 593","lb":"CC593","ev":"The workers raise gold pillars, coral beams, a crystal ceiling and gold walls.","cl":[],"ag":["பதினாயிரவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:594":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":594,"t":["பாவையவ ளிருக்குமிடம் பளிக்குச்சுவ ரியற்றிக்","கோவைகுளிர் முத்தினியல் கோதையொடு கொழும்பன்","மாலையொடு மாலைதலை மணந்துவர நாற்றி","ஆலையமி தோவியர்கட் கென்னவணி யமைத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Where the maiden is to sit they raise walls of crystal, hang strings of cool pearls with garlands meeting garlands, and so adorn it that one would call it a temple for painters.","n":"ஆலயம் இது ஓவியர்கட்கு makes the hall a shrine for the painters, the place their art is worshipped in, not a place of worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 594","lb":"CC594","ev":"The workers build and adorn the crystal seat where the maiden will sit.","cl":[],"ag":["பதினாயிரவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:595":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":595,"t":["ஆயிதழ பொன்னலங்கல் காலசைப்ப வொல்கி","வாயருகு வந்தொசிந்து மறியமழை மின்போற்","சேயவர்க்குந் தோன்றியதோர் திலகமெனுந் தகைத்தாய்ப்","பாயதிரை முத்தமணல் பரந்துபயின் றுளதே"],"p":"","r":"Gold garlands with fine petals sway in the wind and bend near the doors like lightning in the rain-cloud, so that the hall shows even to the far-off like a tilakam, and sea-pearls are spread there as sand.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 595","lb":"CC595","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:596":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":596,"t":["காமர்களி றும்பிடியும் கன்றுங்கலை மானுந்","தாமரைய வாவிகளும் புள்ளுந்தகை நலத்தி","னேமுறுவ பாவையினொ டியக்கிநிலை யெழுதி","யாமொரையம் காண்பவர்க்கி தகம்புறமி தெனவே."],"p":"","r":"Elephants, she-elephants, calves, stags, lotus pools and birds are painted with lifelike grace beside figures and yakshis, so that viewers cannot tell which is inside and which outside.","n":"The doubt (ஐயம்) whether painted creatures are inside the hall or out is the poet's praise of the painting as indistinguishable from the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 596","lb":"CC596","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:597":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":597,"t":["உழந்தவரு நோக்கி மகிழ் தூங்கவொளி வாய்ந்து","விழுங்குமெனப் பறவைகளும் பிறவிலங்கும் அடையா","முழங்குதிரை வேலியினி னில்லையென மொய்கொண்","டெழுந்துகொடி யாடுமிதவ் வெழில்நகரி னியல்பே."],"p":"","r":"Even those who toiled on it look and are filled with joy; birds and beasts will not come near, as if it might swallow them; flags rise and dance, and nothing like it exists within the sea-fenced world: such is this fair mansion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 597","lb":"CC597","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதினாயிரவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:598":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":598,"t":["ஓடுமுகில் கீறியொளிர் திங்கள்சிகை வைத்தே","மாடமது வார்சடைய வள்ளலையு மொக்கும்","நாடிமுக நான்கதனி னான்முகனை யொக்கும்","நேடிநிமிர் தன்மையினி னேமியையு மொக்கும்."],"p":"","r":"Tearing the running clouds and setting the shining moon on its crest, the mansion is like the long-haired lord with the crescent; with its four faces it is like four-faced Brahma; in its towering height it is like Nemi.","n":"The building is compared in turn to the god who wears the moon in his matted hair, to Brahma and to the god of the wheel; a Jain epic by tradition uses the Vedic gods here only as measures of form and height, and claims nothing about them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 598","lb":"CC598","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:599":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":599,"t":["கண்டவர்கள் காமுறலிற் காமனையு மொக்குங்","கொண்டுலக மேத்தலினக் கொற்றவனை யொக்கும்","வண்டெரிய லாரமுலை மாதர்மகி ழமுதம்","உண்டவர்க ளெவ்வகைய ரவ்வகைய தொன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Making all who see it desire it, it is like Kama; praised by the whole world, it is like the victorious king; and like the nectar of a woman's breasts, it is to each who enjoys it just what that person is.","n":"The last line turns the praise inward: the hall's beauty is not one thing but answers the nature of whoever looks, as the woman's nectar does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 599","lb":"CC599","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:600":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":600,"t":["முகிற்றலை மதிய மன்ன முழுமணி மாடத் திட்ட","வகிற்புகை தவழ்ந்து வானத் தருவிசும் பறுத்து நீண்டு","பகற்கதிர்ப் பரப்பிற் றாகிப் பஞ்சவர் விமான முட்டிப்","புகற்கரு மமரர் கற்பம் புக்கயா வுயிர்த்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The agil smoke laid in the jewelled mansion, like the moon above the clouds, creeps up, cuts through the sky, spreads like daylight rays, touches the vimanas of the five and enters the kalpa heavens of the gods, who breathe it in.","n":"கற்பம் and பஞ்சவர் விமானம் read as the heavens of Jain cosmology, the kalpas and five higher abodes; the word division of புக்கயா வுயிர்த்த is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 600","lb":"CC600","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:601":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":601,"t":["அரைசன தருளினொ டகன்மனை யவனெய்தி","யுரைசெலல் வகையினொ டுலகமு மறிவுற","முரைசதி ரிமிழிசை முதுநக ரறைகென","விரைசெல லிளையரை வியவரின் விடவே."],"p":"","r":"Having taken leave by the king's grace and reached his broad house, the merchant, so that the world may know the manner of the announcement, sends swift young men as ordered to have the drum beaten through the old city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 601","lb":"CC601","ev":"Sridattan sends messengers to have the drummers proclaim the contest through the city.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","இளையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:602":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":602,"t":["விடுகணை விசையொடு வெருவரு தகையவர்","படுபணை யவருறை பதியது குறுகி","நெடுமதி யகடுற நிழறவழ் கொடியுயர்","கடிநக ரிடிமுர சறைமின மெனவே."],"p":"","r":"The messengers, swift as a loosed arrow and fearsome, reach the place where the drummers live and tell them to beat the thundering drum in the guarded city whose flags rise to touch the moon.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 602","lb":"CC602","ev":"The messengers order the drummers to beat the proclamation drum.","cl":[],"ag":["இளையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:603":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":603,"t":["மங்கல வணியினர் மலர்க்கதிர் மதியன","புங்கவ னறநெறி பொலிவொடு மலிகென","வங்கதிர் மணிநகை யலமரு முலைவளர்","கொங்கணி குழலவள் கோடணை அறைவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The drummers, in auspicious ornaments, open with the wish that the dharma path of the Excellent One may flourish in splendour, and then say they will proclaim the decree concerning the maiden with gem-bright smile and fragrant hair.","n":"புங்கவன் அறநெறி names the path of the Excellent One, a title of the Jina; the public proclamation of a bride-contest is opened as a Jain benediction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vaazhthu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 603","lb":"CC603","ev":"The drummers begin the proclamation with a blessing on the Jina's path.","cl":[],"ag":["படுபணையவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["படுபணையவர்",""],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:604":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":604,"t":["வான்றரு வளத்த தாகி வையகம் பிணியிற் றீர்க","தேன்றரு கிளவி யாருங் கற்பினிற் றிரித லின்றி","யூன்றுக வூழி தோறு முலகினுண் மாந்த ரெல்லா","மீன்றவர் வயத்த ராகி யில்லறம் புணர்க நாளும்."],"p":"","r":"May the world have the wealth the sky gives and be free of disease; may all honey-voiced women hold to chastity without swerving, firm through every age; may all people, obedient to those who bore them, practise the household dharma every day.","n":"The rain, health, women's chastity and the household life are wished together as the goods of one well-kept world; the verse does not make any one of them the cause of another.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","karpu","ilvaazhkkai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 604","lb":"CC604","ev":"","cl":[["people should live the household dharma daily, obedient to their parents","general","asserted",4,"drummers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["படுபணையவர்",""],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:605":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":605,"t":["தவம்புரிந் தடங்கி நோற்குந் தத்துவர்த் தலைப்பட் டோம்பிப்","பவம்பரி கெமக்கு மென்று பணிந்தவ ருவப்ப வீமி","னவம்புரிந் துடம்பு நீங்கா தருந்தவ முயன்மின் யாருஞ்","சிவம்புரி நெறியைச் சேரச் செப்புமிப் பொருளுங் கேண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Seek out and care for the ascetics who practise penance in self-control, and give so that they rejoice, bowing and wishing that birth may be ended for you too; all of you, strive at hard penance while still in the body; and hear this matter proclaimed, so that you reach the path that leads to bliss.","n":"The school is given as Jain because the proclamation opens on the Jina's path (603); the words here (தவம், பவம், சிவம் புரி நெறி) are shared with other schools. உடம்பு நீங்காது is read as 'before the body leaves', i.e. in this life; it could also mean 'without deserting the body'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","eegai","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 605","lb":"CC605","ev":"","cl":[["giving to ascetics who practise penance is a way toward the ending of births","jain","asserted",2,"drummers"],["everyone, not only the renouncer, should strive at hard penance while in the body to reach release","jain","asserted",3,"drummers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["படுபணையவர்",""],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:606":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":606,"t":["அம்மல ரனிச்சத் தம்போ தல்லியோ டணியி னொந்து","விம்முறு நுசுப்பு நைய வீற்றிருந் தணங்கு சேர்ந்த","வெம்முலைப் பரவை யல்குன் மிடைமணிக் கலாபம் வேய்த்தோட்","செம்மலர்த் திருவின் சாயற் றேமொழி தத்தை யென்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"There is one called Tattai, sweet of speech, with the grace of the goddess of the red lotus, whose waist aches even under anicham flowers and whose jewelled girdle lies on her broad hips.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 606","lb":"CC606","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["படுபணையவர்",""],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:607":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":607,"t":["மற்றவ டந்தை நாய்கன் வண்கைச்சீ தத்தன் என்பான்","கொற்றவன் குலத்தின் வந்தான் கூறிய பொருளி தாகும்","முற்றவம் உடையளாகி மூரிநூற் கலைக ளெல்லாங்","கற்றவள் கணங்கொள் நல்யா ழனங்கனைக் கனிக்கும் நீராள்."],"p":"","r":"Her father is the merchant Sitattan of the generous hand, who has come of a victorious line; this is what he has declared: she, rich in former penance, has learned all the arts of the great treatises and plays the fine yaazh so that she melts even Kama.","n":"The proclamation names the merchant as her father, though he is her guardian on the Vidyadhara king's trust; her skill is credited to முற்றவம், past penance, as well as to learning. கொற்றவன் குலத்தின் வந்தான் may instead mean he came from the king's presence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","isai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 607","lb":"CC607","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["படுபணையவர்",""],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:608":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":608,"t":["தீந்தொடை மகர வீணைத் தெள்விளி யெடுப்பித் தேற்றிப்","பூந்தொடி யரிவை தன்னிற் புலமிகுத் துடைய நம்பிக்","கீந்திடு மிறைவ ராதி மூவகைக் குலத்து ளார்க்கும்","வேந்தடு குருதி வேற்கண் விளங்கிழை தாதை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"To the man of any of the three classes, kings first among them, who draws and makes clear the sweet notes of the makara vina with more skill than this bangled maiden, her father will give her: so the drummer cried.","n":"The contest is open to the three classes headed by kings, so a merchant's son is not shut out; the bride goes to whoever surpasses her in music, not to the highest born.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","kalvi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 608","lb":"CC608","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["படுபணையவர்",""],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:609":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":609,"t":["மண்ணக மடந்தை யாக மார்புற முயங்கி நின்ற","வண்ணலை யாதி யாக வருங்கடி நகரை வாழ்த்தி","விண்ணக முழக்கி னேய்ப்ப வீதிதொ றெருக்கி யெங்குங்","கண்ணொளிர் கடிப்பி னோச்சிக் கடிமுர சறைந்த காலை,"],"p":"","r":"Blessing first the lord who embraces the earth-goddess as his bride, and then the guarded city, the drummers go through every street, beating the drum with gleaming sticks so that it thunders like the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vaazhthu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 609","lb":"CC609","ev":"The drummers bless the king and the city and beat the drum through every street.","cl":[],"ag":["படுபணையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:610":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":610,"t":["வணக்கருந் தானை மன்னர் மத்தகம் பிளந்து வாய்த்த","நிணக்கொழுங் குருதி வாட்கை நிலம்புடை பெயர்க்கு மாற்ற","லணைப்பருங் களிகொள் வேழத்தத்தின புரத்து வேந்தன்","கணைக்கவி னழித்த வுண்கட் கன்னியைக் கருதி வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of Hastinapuram, whose sword hand drips the fat and blood of unbowing kings split at the brow and whose strength could shift the earth, comes with his hard-to-hold elephants, thinking of the maiden whose eyes shame arrows.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 610","lb":"CC610","ev":"The king of Hastinapuram arrives seeking the maiden.","cl":[],"ag":["அத்தினபுரத்து வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:611":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":611,"t":["சிதைப்பருஞ் சீற்றத் துப்பின் செய்கழ னரல வீக்கி","மதக்களி றடர்த்துக் குன்ற மணிவட்டி னுருட்டு மாற்றற்","கதக்களி யொளிறு வைவேற் காம்பிலிக் காவல் மன்னன்","பதைப்பரும் பரும யானைப் பாலமா குமரன் வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The guardian king of Kampili, of unbreakable wrath, who binds sounding anklets, subdues musth elephants and could roll a mountain like a gem-ball, comes, a prince with tall armoured elephants.","n":"பாலமா குமரன் is read as 'the young prince' describing the king of Kampili; it may be a name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 611","lb":"CC611","ev":"The king of Kampili arrives for the contest.","cl":[],"ag":["காம்பிலிக் காவல் மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:612":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":612,"t":["இலைபொர வெழுதி யன்ன வெரிமணிக் கடக முன்கைச்","சிலைபொரத் திரண்ட திண்டோட் சில்லரிச் சிலம்பி னார்த","முலைபொர வுடைந்த தண்டார் மொய்ம்மதுத் துளிப்ப வந்தான்","மலைபொர வரிய மார்பின் வாரண வாசி மன்னன்."],"p":"","r":"The king of Varanavasi comes, with jewelled bracelet on his forearm, shoulders like bows, a chest no mountain could match, and his cool garland, crushed against the breasts of anklet-wearing women, dripping honey.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 612","lb":"CC612","ev":"The king of Varanavasi arrives for the contest.","cl":[],"ag":["வாரணவாசி மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:27"},"cc:613":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":613,"t":["கதிர்முடி மன்னர் சூழ்ந்து கைதொழு திறைஞ்சி மாலைத்","திருமுடி வயிர வில்லாற் சேவடி திளைப்ப வேத்தி","யருமுடி யணிந்த கொற்றத் தவந்தியன் முரச மார்ப்ப","வொருபிடி நுசுப்பி னாளை யுள்ளுபு வந்து விட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Surrounded by kings who bow with joined hands, his crowned head praised, the victorious king of Avanti arrives to his drums, his mind on the maiden of slender waist.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","vendhan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 613","lb":"CC613","ev":"The king of Avanti arrives at Rajamapuram for the vina contest, thinking of the princess.","cl":[],"ag":["அவந்தியன்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:614":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":614,"t":["வெள்ளணி யணிந்த ஞான்றே வேந்தர்தம் முடியிற் கொண்ட","கள்ளணி மாலை மோந்து கனைகழ லிலங்கும் நோன்றாட்","புள்ளணி கொடியி னானிற் போர்பல தொலைத்த வாற்ற","லள்ளிலை யணிந்த வைவே லயோத்திய ரிறையும் வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the Ayodhya people, who on his coronation day sniffed garlands taken from kings' crowns and has won many battles with his leaf-bladed spear, comes too.","n":"The suitors are introduced by their war record, so the coming contest of the lute is set against a field of conquerors whose strength it will not test.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 614","lb":"CC614","ev":"The king of Ayodhya arrives for the contest.","cl":[],"ag":["அயோத்தியர் இறை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:615":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":615,"t":["நீணிதி வணிக ரீறா நிலமிசை யவர்க ளெல்லாம்","வீணையிற் பொருது வெல்வான் விரைவினர் துவன்றி மூதூர்க்","கோணமு மறுகு மெல்லாங் குச்சென நிரைத்தம் மாந்தர்","மாண்மது நசையின் மொய்த்த மதுகர வீட்ட மொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Everyone on earth, down to the merchants of great wealth, hurries to win her at the vina, and the people crowd every corner and street of the old city in rows like swarms of bees drawn by honey.","n":"The contest is open to anyone, merchants included, not only kings: this is what lets a merchant's son win it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 615","lb":"CC615","ev":"Suitors of every rank and crowds of townspeople throng the city.","cl":[],"ag":["நிலமிசையவர்கள்","மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:616":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":616,"t":["உருக்கமைந் தெரியுஞ் செம்பொ னோரைவில் லகல மாகத்","திருக்குழன் மடந்தை செல்லத் திருநிலந் திருத்திப் பின்னர்,","விரைத்தகு நான நீரால் வெண்ணிறப் பொடியை மாற்றிப்","பரப்பினர் படுவண் டார்ப்பப் பன்மலர் பக்கம் எல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"For the maiden to walk on, they level a path five bows wide, lay down molten-bright gold, wash away the white dust with scented water and strew flowers loud with bees along its sides.","n":"The word ஓரைவில் (five bows) as a measure of the path's width is read across the printed word break.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 616","lb":"CC616","ev":"The way to the contest hall is levelled and laid with gold and flowers.","cl":[],"ag":["பணியாளர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:617":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":617,"t":["விலைவரம் பறித லில்லா வெண்டுகி லடுத்து வீதி","அலர்தலை யனிச்சத் தம்போ தைம்முழ வகல மாகப்","பலபடப் பரப்பிப் பாவை மெல்லடிப் பரிவு தீர","நிலவரைத் தன்ன னாரை நிதியினால் வறுமை செய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He spreads white cloth beyond price and soft anicham blossoms along the street to the width of five drums so her soft feet are spared, and with his wealth he makes the needy of the land, many as the earth, forget want.","n":"Line 4, நிலவரைத் தன்ன னாரை நிதியினால் வறுமை செய்தான், literally says he made poverty for them by wealth; the sense that his gifts ended their want is a reading, and the subject is taken from the story.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 617","lb":"CC617","ev":"The merchant carpets the street with cloth and flowers and scatters wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:618":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":618,"t":["மண்டல நிறைந்த மாசின் மதிப்புடை வியாழம் போன்றோர்","குண்டல மிலங்க நின்ற கொடியினைக் குறுகித் தோழி","விண்டலர் கோதை விம்மும் விரைகுழ றொழுது நீவிப்","பண்டியல் மணங்க ளெல்லாம் பரிவறப் பணிந்து சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her companion comes up to the creeper-like girl whose earrings shine like Jupiter beside the full moon, bows, strokes her fragrant hair and, with deference, tells her all the marriage customs of old.","n":"Line 1's comparison (மண்டல நிறைந்த மாசின் மதிப்புடை வியாழம்) is read as the earring beside her moon face; the line could also describe the companion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 618","lb":"CC618","ev":"The companion bows to the princess and speaks to her of the old forms of marriage.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:619":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":619,"t":["எரிமணி நெற்றி வேய்ந்த விளம்பிறை யிதுகொ லென்னப்","புரிமணி சுமந்த பொற்பூண் பொறுக்கலா நுசுப்பிற் பாவை","திருமணி வீணைக் குன்றத் திழிந்ததீம் பாலை நீத்தத்","தருமுடி யரச ராழ்வ ரம்மனை யறிவ லென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The companion tells the girl, whose jewelled brow is like the young crescent and whose waist cannot bear her ornaments, that the crowned kings will drown in the flood of sweet paalai melody pouring down from her gem vina as from a hill: 'lady, I know it.'","n":"அறிவல் makes the forecast a certainty in the companion's mouth; the patikam has already told that the princess is the one who loses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 619","lb":"CC619","ev":"The companion predicts that every royal suitor will be beaten by the princess's playing.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:620":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":620,"t":["மண்ணிட மலிர வெங்கு மாந்தரும் வந்து தொக்கா","ரொண்ணிற வுரோணி யூர்ந்த வொளிமதி யொண்பொ னாட்சித்","தெண்ணிற விசும்பி னின்ற தெளிமதி முகத்து நங்கை","கண்ணிய வீணை வாட்போர்க் கலாமின்று காண்டு மென்றே."],"p":"","r":"People gather from everywhere until the ground overflows, saying 'today we shall see the vina sword-fight of the maiden whose face is like the clear moon in the sky, the moon that rode with Rohini.'","n":"வீணை வாட்போர் names the music contest as a duel with swords, the figure 628 takes up with யாழ்ப் படை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 620","lb":"CC620","ev":"Crowds gather to watch the contest.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:621":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":621,"t":["பசுங்கதிர்க் கடவுள் யோகம் பழிப்பற நுனித்து வல்லான்","விசும்பிவர் கடவு ளொப்பான் விரிச்சிக னறிந்து கூற","வசும்புதே னலங்க லைம்பா லரிவையொ டாய்ந்து நாய்கன்","விசும்புபோன் மாந்த ரார விழுநிதி சிதறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"When the omen-reader, a man like the gods in the sky who has examined without fault the moon's conjunction, has read and declared the hour, the merchant consults with the lady of honeyed garland and scatters great wealth until people are as full as the sky.","n":"விரிச்சிகன் is read as one who reads omens (விரிச்சி); the அரிவை he consults is taken as his wife, though the verse does not name her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 621","lb":"CC621","ev":"An astrologer fixes the auspicious hour and the merchant, after consulting his wife, gives wealth away.","cl":[],"ag":["விரிச்சிகன்","சீதத்தன்","மனைவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:622":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":622,"t":["வாசநெய் வண்டு மூச மாந்தளிர் விரல்கள் சேப்பப்","பூசிவெள் ளிலோத்தி ரத்தின் பூம்பொருக் கரைத்த சாந்தின்","காசறு குவளைக் காம ரகவிதழ் பயில மட்டித்","தாசறத் திமிர்ந்து மாத ரணிநலந் திகழ்வித் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"They rub her with scented oil that draws the bees until their tender fingers redden, then smear her with a paste of white lodhra flowers ground fine to make her bright beauty shine.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 622","lb":"CC622","ev":"The attendants anoint the princess.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:623":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":623,"t":["கங்கையின் களிற்றி னுச்சிக் கதிர்மணிக் குடத்திற் றந்த","மங்கல வாச நன்னீர் மணிநிறங் கழீஇய தொப்ப","நங்கையை நயப்ப வெல்லாம் விரையொடு துவருஞ் சேர்த்தி","யங்கர வல்கு லாளை யாட்டினா ரரம்பை யன்னார்."],"p":"","r":"The Rambha-like women bathe the maiden of serpent-hood loins in auspicious scented water mixed with perfumes and astringents, as if the gem colour were washed with water brought in gem pots on elephants from the Ganges.","n":"The bath by elephants with gold pots of Ganges water recalls the lustration of the goddess of fortune, the figure 627 ends on (திருவிற்கோர் திலகம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 623","lb":"CC623","ev":"The attendants bathe the princess.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னார்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:624":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":624,"t":["வெண்ணிற மழையின் மின்போல் வெண்டுகிற் கலாபம் வீக்கிக்","கண்ணிற முலையுந் தோளுஞ் சந்தனத் தேய்வை கொட்டித்","தெண்ணிறச் சிலம்பு செம்பொற் கிண்கிணி பாதஞ் சேர்த்திப்","பண்ணிறச் சுரும்பு சூழும் பனிமுல்லைச் சூட்டு வேய்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"They bind a girdle of white silk on her, like lightning in a white cloud, spread sandal paste on her breasts and shoulders, put clear anklets and gold bells on her feet and crown her with a wreath of cool jasmine circled by bees.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 624","lb":"CC624","ev":"The attendants dress the princess.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:625":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":625,"t":["எரிமணிச் சுண்ண மின்னு மிருஞ்சிலை முத்தஞ் சேர்த்தித்","திருமணி முலையி னெற்றிச் சிறுபுறஞ் செறியத் தீட்டிப்","புரிமணி யாகத் தைதா விரனுதி கொண்டு பூசி","விரிமணி வியப்ப மேனி யொளிவிட்டு விளங்கிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"They set pearls and a bow of shining gem powder, paint her breasts and back closely, and smear it on with fingertips over her pearl-hung chest, so that her body shines brighter than the gems.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 625","lb":"CC625","ev":"The attendants paint and jewel the princess.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:626":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":626,"t":["அருங்கயம் விசும்பிற் பார்க்கு மணிச்சிறு சிரலை யஞ்சி","இருங்கயந் துறந்து திங்க ளிடங்கொண்டு கிடந்த நீலம்","நெருங்கிய மணிவிற் காப்ப நீண்டுலாய்ப் பிறழ்வ செங்கேழ்க்","கருங்கய லல்ல கண்ணே எனக்கரி போக்கி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Saying 'these are not dark red-streaked carp but blue lilies that left their pond for fear of the kingfisher that watches from the sky and took refuge in the moon, where the gem bow of her brows guards them', they line her eyes with kohl.","n":"The verse turns down one standard comparison of the eye (கயல், carp) for another (நீலம், blue lily), with the moon her face and the bow her brows; the conceit is the attendants' own speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 626","lb":"CC626","ev":"The attendants line the princess's eyes with kohl.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:627":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":627,"t":["பொருந்துபொற் றூண்கள் நான்கிற் பொலிந்துநூற் புலவர் செந்நா","வருந்தியும் புகழ்த லாகா மரகத மணிசெய் கூடத்","திருந்திளை யார்கள் கோல மிந்திர னிருமித் தாற்போற்","திருந்தச்செய் ததற்பி னங்கை திருவிற்கோர் திலக மொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"In an emerald hall on four gold pillars, which the tongues of learned poets could not praise enough though they strained, the young women adorned her so well it was as if Indra had made her, and after that the maiden was like a tilaka on the goddess of fortune.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 627","lb":"CC627","ev":"The adorning of the princess is finished.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னார்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:628":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":628,"t":["மண்கனை முழவம் விம்ம வரிவளை துவைப்ப வள்வார்க்","கண்கனைந் திடியின் வெம்பிக் கடலென முரச மார்ப்ப","விண்கனிந் துருகு நீர்மை வெள்வளைத் தோளி போந்தாள்","பண்கனிந் துருகு நல்யாழ்ப் படைபொரு துடைக்க லுற்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the drums of moulded clay boom, conches blare and great drums roar like the sea and thunder, the white-bangled girl, melting as rain melts from the sky, goes out to break her opponents with the good yaazh, whose melody melts in its mode, as her weapon.","n":"பொருது உடைக்கல் (to fight and shatter) and யாழ்ப் படை (the lute as army or weapon) cast the music contest as war, the language the suitors' introductions used in 613 and 614.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 628","lb":"CC628","ev":"The princess sets out for the contest.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:629":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":629,"t":["பரந்தொளி யுமிழும் பைம்பொற் கண்ணடி பதாகை தோட்டி","விரிந்திருண் மேயுஞ் செம்பொன் விளக்குவெண் முரசு கும்பஞ்","சுரந்தவெண் மதியைச் சூன்று கதிர்கொண்டு தொகுத்த போலும்","பொருந்துபொற் கதிர்பெய் கற்றை புணர்கயல் போந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Ahead go the auspicious things: gold mirrors spreading light, banners, the goad, red-gold lamps that graze the darkness, the white drum, the full pot, and the paired fish emblem with a gold fringe of rays as though gathered from the white moon.","n":"The list (mirror, banner, goad, lamp, drum, pot, paired fish) is the set of auspicious objects carried before a bride or a royal; the verse does not number them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 629","lb":"CC629","ev":"The auspicious emblems are carried in procession before the princess.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:630":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":630,"t":["வென்றவ னகலம் பூட்ட விளங்கொளி மணிசெய் செப்பி","னின்றெரி பசும்பொன் மாலை போந்தது நெறியிற் பின்ன","ரொன்றிய மணிசெய் நல்யாழ் போந்தன வுருவ மாலை","தின்றுதே னிசைகள் பாடத் திருநகர் சுடர வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Next the gold garland to be put on the victor's chest comes out in a shining gem casket, and after it the jewelled yaazhs, garlands whose honey the singing bees drink, and the city blazes with light.","n":"The garland is for வென்றவன், whoever wins: the procession carries the prize before the outcome is known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 630","lb":"CC630","ev":"The victor's garland and the contest lutes are carried in procession.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:631":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":631,"t":["ஆரந் துயல்வர வந்துகில் சோர்தர","வீரம் படக்கையை மெய்வழி வீசித்","தேரை நடப்பன போற்குறள் சிந்தினொ","டோரு நடந்தன வொண்டொடி முன்னே."],"p":"","r":"With necklaces swinging and garments slipping, swinging their arms valiantly, dwarfs and hunchbacks walk ahead of the bangled maiden like walking toads.","n":"The metre changes here to a shorter line, which runs through 635.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 631","lb":"CC631","ev":"Dwarfs and hunchbacks walk before the princess.","cl":[],"ag":["குறள் சிந்து"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:632":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":632,"t":["வட்டச் சூறையர் வார்முலைக் கச்சினர்","பட்டு வீக்கிய வல்குலர் பல்கணை","விட்ட தூணியர் வில்லினர் வாளின","ரொட்டி யாயிரத் தோரெண்மர் முன்னினார்."],"p":"","r":"A thousand and eight women with round spears, bodices on long breasts and silk bound at the hips, with quivers full of arrows, bows and swords, go ahead.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 632","lb":"CC632","ev":"A guard of a thousand and eight armed women goes before the princess.","cl":[],"ag":["எண்மர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:633":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":633,"t":["வம்பு வீக்கி வருமுலை யுட்கரந்","தம்பி னொய்யவ ராணுடைத் தானையர்","பைம்பொற் கேடகம் வாளொடு பற்றுபு","செம்பொற் பாவையைச் சேவித்து முன்னினார்."],"p":"","r":"Hiding their swelling breasts under bodices, light as arrows, in men's dress, holding gold shields and swords, they serve the golden doll and go before her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 633","lb":"CC633","ev":"The armed women in men's dress escort the princess.","cl":[],"ag":["எண்மர்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:634":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":634,"t":["ஆணை யாணை யகலுமி னீரென","வேணுக் கோலின் மிடைந்தவ ரொற்றலி","னாணை யின்றெம தேயென் றணிநகர்","காணுங் காதலிற் கண்ணெருக் குற்றவே."],"p":"","r":"When the crowding guards strike with bamboo staffs crying 'by order, by order, move away', the eyes of the adorned city, in their longing to see, press in all the closer as if saying 'today the order is ours'.","n":"The answering cry ஆணையின்று எமதே is given to the eyes (கண்ணெருக்குற்றவே) rather than to the people, and can also be read 'no order binds us'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 634","lb":"CC634","ev":"The guards try to clear the crowd, which presses in to see the princess.","cl":[],"ag":["எண்மர்","மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:635":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":635,"t":["கண்ணி னோடு பிறந்தது காரிகை","வண்ணங் காண்டற்கன் றோவென்று வைதவர்","விண்ணு மண்ணும் விருந்துசெய் தாலொப்ப","வெண்ணி னெண்ணிட மின்றி நெருங்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who scold the guards, 'was the eye born with us for anything but to see her beauty?', throng so close there is no room even to count them, as if heaven and earth had come to a feast.","n":"The crowd's retort makes seeing beauty the purpose the eye is born for, the view the renunciant of Naladiyar 44 calls blindness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 635","lb":"CC635","ev":"The crowd protests against the guards and packs the street.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:636":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":636,"t":["இனஞ்சேரா வாகி விளையோ ருயிரின்மே லெண்ணங் கொள்வான்","புனஞ்சேர் கொடிமுல்லை பூம்பவளத் துட்புக்குப் பூத்த போலும்","வனஞ்சேர் துவர்ச்செவ்வாய் வாளெயிறுங் கண்மலரும் வனைய லாகாக்","கனஞ்சேர் கதிர்முலையுங் கண்டார்கள் வீட்டுலகங் காணார் போலும்!"],"p":"","r":"Her coral mouth, where jasmine seems to have flowered, with its bright teeth, her flower eyes and her heavy breasts, which stand apart from all their kind and set their thought on young men's lives: those who saw them, it seems, will never look upon the world of release.","n":"The narrator's closing hyperbole makes her beauty a threat to the onlookers' lives and to their release; in a work that ends with its hero renouncing women for release, the line names the stake the story will later settle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","anangu","veedu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 636","lb":"CC636","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:28"},"cc:637":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":637,"t":["மீன்சேர் குழாமனைய மேகலையும் வெம்முலையுங் கூற்றங் கூற்ற","மூன்சே ருயிருய்யக் கொண்டோடிப் போமின்க ளுரைத்தே மென்று","கான்சேர் கமழ்கோதை காறொடர்ந்து கைவிடா தரற்று கின்ற","தேன்சேர் திருவடிமேற் கிண்கிணிபொன் னாவதற்கே தக்க தென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Onlookers say her girdle, like a shoal of fish, and her hot breasts are Death upon Death, and bid the suitors run off saving the lives within them, adding that the anklet-bells on her honeyed feet, which the scented garland trails after and will not let go, were fit only to be made of gold.","n":"The warning, 'we have told you' (உரைத்தேம்), is given before the contest: those who will lose at the vina are told in advance that the prize itself kills. Who speaks is known only from என்பார்; the line could also be read as the suitors' banter among themselves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kaamam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 637","lb":"CC637","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்டார்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:638":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":638,"t":["கள்வாய்ப் பெயப்பட்ட மாலைக் கருங்குழல்கள் கண்டார் நைய","வுள்வாய்ப் பெயப்பட்ட வெம்மதுச்செப் போரிணைமெல் லாக மீன்ற","புள்வாய் மணிமழலைப் பொற்சிலம்பி னிக்கொடியை யீன்றாள் போலுங்","கொள்வா னுலகுக்கோர் கூற்றீன்றா ளம்மவோ கொடிய வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Those who see her say that her mother, in bearing this golden-anklet creeper with the prattling voice of a bird, seems to have borne a Death to take the world, and cry out at how cruel it is.","n":"The praise goes by way of blame of the mother: bearing a beauty is called bearing Death (கூற்றீன்றாள்), so the crowd's wonder is voiced as reproach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 638","lb":"CC638","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்டார்",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:639":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":639,"t":["செய்ய தாமரை மேற்றிரு வேகொலோ","வெய்ய நோக்கின்விச் சாதரி யேகொலோ","மையில் வானவர் தம்மக ளேகொலென்","றைய முற்றலர் தார்மன்னர் கூறினார்."],"p":"","r":"The garlanded kings, in doubt, ask whether she is Tiru on the red lotus, a Vidyadhara woman of fierce glance, or a daughter of the spotless gods.","n":"All three guesses are divine or semi-divine; the second, விச்சாதரி, is in fact true of her by birth, since she is the Vidyadhara king's daughter, so the kings' doubt hits the fact without knowing it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 639","lb":"CC639","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:640":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":640,"t":["வீணை வென்றிவள் வெம்முலைப் பூந்தட","மாணை தோய்வதல் லாற்பிறன் வெளவுமேற்","கோணைப் போரிற் குளிக்குவ மன்றெனின்","மாண நற்றவஞ் செய்குவ மென்மரும்,"],"p":"","r":"Some kings say that unless it is they who win at the vina and lie on her breasts, they will plunge into a war of bent bows if another takes her, and failing that will do great penance.","n":"Two fallbacks are named for defeat, war and penance (நற்றவம்); the contest is already shadowed by the threat that its result will be fought over, and தவம் stands here as a disappointed lover's recourse, not as a turn from desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaamam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 640","lb":"CC640","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:641":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":641,"t":["குலிகச் செப்பன கொம்மை வரிமுலை","நலியு மெம்மையென் பார்நல்ல கண்களால்","வலிய வாங்கியெய் தாளெம்மை வாழ்கலே","மெலிய வாலி விடுக்குமற் றென்மரும்,"],"p":"","r":"Other kings say her lined breasts, like vermilion caskets, torment them, that she has drawn and shot them with her fine eyes, that they cannot live, and that the slender one will now let loose their end.","n":"The sense of line 4 (மெலிய வாலி விடுக்குமற்று) is not secure; the reading as 'the slender one lets fly' carries the eye-as-archer image of line 3 forward.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 641","lb":"CC641","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:642":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":642,"t":["ஊட்டி யன்ன வுருக்கரக் காரடி","நீட்டி மென்மலர் மேல்வந்து நின்னலங்","காட்டி யெம்மைக்கொன் றாயெனக் கைதொழு","தோட்டை நெஞ்சின ராயுழல் வார்களும்."],"p":"","r":"Others, with melted hearts, join their palms in worship and wander, saying that she has stretched out her lac-red feet onto soft flowers, shown her beauty and killed them.","n":"The suitors worship (கைதொழுது) her as one worships a deity while accusing her of murder: the posture of devotion and the charge of killing are the same gesture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nalam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 642","lb":"CC642","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:643":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":643,"t":["திங்கண் மதிமுகத்த சேலும் பவளமுஞ் சிலையு முத்துங்","கொங்குண் குழலாண்மெல் லாகத்த கோங்கரும்புங் கொழிப்பில் பொன்னு","மங்கை குழியா வரக்கீத்த செந்தளிர்நெய் தோய்த்த போலு","மங்கை மலரடியுந் தாமரையே யாமறியே மணங்கே யென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Others list her parts as fish, coral, bow and pearls in a moon face, kongu buds and unworked gold on her chest, hands like red shoots dipped in ghee and feet like lotus, and say they cannot tell, she is a goddess who afflicts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","nalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 643","lb":"CC643","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:644":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":644,"t":["பொன்மகரம் வாய்போழ்ந்த முத்தநூ றோள்யாப்பிற் பொலிந்த வாறு","மின்மகரங் கூத்தாடி வில்லிட் டிருங்குழைக்கீ ழிலங்கு மாறு","மன்மகர வெல்கொடியான் மால்கொள்ளக் கால்கொண்ட முலையி னாளை","யென்னரம்பை யென்னாவா றென்பா ரிமைக்குங்கண் ணிவையோ வென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the pearl strings from golden makara clasps on her shoulders and the lightning-like makara earrings dancing, some ask how she, whose breasts infatuated Kama himself, is not Rambha, and others ask whether these blinking eyes can be hers.","n":"The blink (இமைக்குங்கண்) is the test: the gods are the unblinking ones (இமையார், 647), so the eyes that blink tell the doubters that the goddess-like woman is mortal after all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 644","lb":"CC644","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:645":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":645,"t":["கோள்வயிர நீளருவிக் குன்றிவர்ந்த செஞ்சுடர்போற் கொலைவேன் மன்னர்","நீள்வயிர வெண்மருப்பி னீலக் களிற்றின்மே னிரைத்தார் பொங்கத்","தோள்வயிரந் தோன்றத் தொழுவா ரழுதுநைவார் தொக்கோர் கோடி","வாள்வயிரம் விற்கு மடநோக்கி யார்கொலோ பெறுவ ரென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Kings with murderous spears, high on blue elephants with long white tusks like red flames climbing a hill of diamond streams, their garlands heaving, worship, weep and waste, a crore of them gathered, asking who will win the girl whose eyes outdo the sharpness of swords.","n":"A crore (கோடி) is the poet's hyperbole for the crowd; the warlike picture of the first two lines gives way to tears in the third.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","vendhan","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 645","lb":"CC645","ev":"The assembled kings, mounted on elephants, weep and ask who will win her.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:646":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":646,"t":["பைம்பொன் நிமிர்கொடி பாவை வனப்பென்னுந் தளிரை ஈன்று","செம்பொன் மலர்ந்திளையார் கண்ணென்னுஞ் சீர்மணிவண் டுழலச் சில்லென்","றம்பொற் சிலம்பரற்ற அன்னம்போல் மெல்லவே ஒதுங்கி அம்பூஞ்","செம்பொற் புரிசை அடைந்தாள்செந் தாமரைமேற் றிருவோ டொப்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"Like a gold creeper that puts forth the shoot of a doll's beauty, while the young men's eyes roam round her like bees and her anklets tinkle, she walks softly as a swan and reaches the golden enclosure, like Tiru on the red lotus.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 646","lb":"CC646","ev":"Gandharvadattai walks to the golden enclosure of the contest hall.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:647":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":647,"t":["பட்டியன்ற கண்டத் திரைவளைத்துப் பன்மலர்நன் மாலை நாற்றி","விட்டகலாச் சாந்தி னிலமெழுகி மென்மலர்கள் சிதறித் தூம","மிட்டிளைய ரேத்த விமையார் மடமகள்போ லிருந்து நல்யாழ்","தொட்டெழீஇப் பண்ணெறிந்தாள் கின்னரரு மெய்ம்மறந்து சோர்ந்தா ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"With silk screens set round, garlands hung, the floor smeared with sandal, flowers strewn and incense raised, and young women praising, she sits like a daughter of the unblinking gods, takes up her yazh and plays a mode, and even the kinnaras swoon, forgetting themselves.","n":"The kinnaras are the gods' own musicians; their swoon puts her playing above the celestial standard before any suitor has touched a string.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 647","lb":"CC647","ev":"Her maids prepare the screened seat, and Gandharvadattai plays a mode on the yazh.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","இளையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:648":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":648,"t":["புன்காஞ்சித் தாதுதன் புறம்புதையக் கிளியெனக்கண்","டன்புகொண் மடப்பெடை யலமந்தாங் ககல்வதனை","யென்புருகு குரலழைஇ யிருஞ்சிறகர் குலைத்துகுத்துத்","தன்பெடையைக் குயிறழுவத் தலைவந்த திளவேனில்."],"p":"","r":"In her song, when the cuckoo's back is buried in kanji pollen, his young mate takes him for a parrot and shrinks away, and he calls in a voice that melts the bones, shakes out his wings and embraces her: young spring has come.","n":"The first of three refrain songs (தலைவந்த திளவேனில்) in a new metre; 651 confirms that she has sung them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 648","lb":"CC648","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:649":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":649,"t":["தண்காஞ்சித் தாதாடித் தன்னிறங் கரந்ததனைக்","கண்டானா மடப்பெடை கிளியெனப்போய்க் கையகல","நுண்டூவி யிளஞ்சேவ னோக்கோடு விளிபயிற்றித்","தன்சிறகாற் பெடைதழுவத் தலைவந்த திளவேனில்."],"p":"","r":"In the second song the cock, having bathed in cool kanji pollen, hides his own colour, his mate mistakes him for a parrot and moves off, and the young cock with fine down calls with his gaze and enfolds her with his wing: young spring has come.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 649","lb":"CC649","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:650":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":650,"t":["குறுத்தாட் குயிற்சேவல் கொழுங்காஞ்சித் தாதாடி","வெறுத்தாங்கே மடப்பெடை விழைவகன்று நடப்பதனை","மறுத்தாங்கே சிறகுளர்ந்து மகிழ்வானாக் கொளத்தேற்றி","யுறுப்பினா லடிபணியத் தலைவந்த திளவேனில்."],"p":"","r":"In the third song, when the short-legged cock cuckoo comes dusted with pollen and his mate turns from him in dislike, he will not let it be, ruffles his wings, coaxes her back to joy and bows at her feet with his body: young spring has come.","n":"Across the three songs the mate's estrangement is a mistake about appearance, and it is ended each time by the male's voice and, in the last, by his bowing at her feet; sung by a woman who will give herself only to the one who beats her in music, the songs set out the terms of her contest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 650","lb":"CC650","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:651":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":651,"t":["தளையவிழ் கோதை பாடித் தானமர்ந் திருப்பத் தோழி","விளைமதுக் கண்ணி வீணா பதியெனும் பேடி வேற்க","ணிளையவள் பாட வீர ரெழால்வகை தொடங்க லன்றேல்","வளையவ ளெழாலின் மைந்தர் பாடுக வல்லை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"When the garlanded singer had sung and sat at ease, her friend Vinapati, a pedi with honey-sweet eyes, called out that the heroes should begin to play the yazh while the young spear-eyed one sang, or else the men should quickly sing to the bangled one's yazh.","n":"பேடி names one of neither sex; the friend who announces the rules of a contest of men for a woman is herself outside that contest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 651","lb":"CC651","ev":"Vinapati announces the terms: the suitors are to play while Gandharvadattai sings, or sing while she plays.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","வீணாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["வீணாபதி","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:652":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":652,"t":["வேயே திரண்மென்றோள் வில்லை கொடும்புருவம்","வாயே வளர்பவளம் மாந்தளிரே மாமேனி","நோயே முலைசுமப்ப தென்றார்க் கருகிருந்தா","ரேயே யிவளொருத்தி பேடியோ வென்றா","ரெரிமணிப்பூண் மேகலையாள் பேடியோ வேன்றார்."],"p":"","r":"When some say her shoulders are bamboo, her brow a bow, her mouth coral, her body a mango shoot, and that bearing those breasts is a sickness to her, those sitting near exclaim, 'What, is this one a pedi?', is the girl with the gem girdle a pedi?","n":"The praise seems to be of Vinapati, whose womanly beauty makes the bystanders doubt what they have been told of her; the refrain line repeats the question. Who is praised is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 652","lb":"CC652","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்டார்",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:653":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":653,"t":["பலிகொண்டு பேராத பாச மிவள்கண்","ணொலிகொண் டுயிருண்ணுங் கூற்றமென் றெல்லே","கலிகொண்டு தேவர் முலைகரந்து வைத்தா","ரிலைகொண்ட பூணினீ ரென்றெழினி சேர்ந்தா","ளிலங்குபொற் கிண்கிணியா ணக்கெழினி சேர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She answers the men in leaf-shaped ornaments that the gods, in a clamour, hid her breasts away because her eyes were already a noose that takes its offering and will not go, and a Death that eats lives by sound, and with that she went to the screen.","n":"Read as Vinapati's reply to 652, the jest turns her lack into a boast: breasts were not needed where the eyes already kill. The speaker is inferred from the sequence, and the image of eyes killing 'by sound' (ஒலிகொண்டு) is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","kootru","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 653","lb":"CC653","ev":"Vinapati answers the onlookers' question with a jest and withdraws to the screen.","cl":[],"ag":["வீணாபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["வீணாபதி","கண்டார்"],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:654":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":654,"t":["நுண்டுகி லகலல்கு னொசித்த வெம்முலை","உண்டிவ ணுசுப்பென வுரைப்பி னல்லது","கண்டறி கிலாவிடைக் காம வல்லியாழ்","கொண்டவர் குழாத்திடைக் கொடியி னொல்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She of the waist that can be known only if someone says it is there, under fine silk on wide hips and bending breasts, a creeper of love, carrying the yazh, swayed like a vine through their throng.","n":"Who carries the yazh is not named; the verse may still be about Vinapati after 653, but 655 to 656 have Gandharvadattai take her seat with the yazh, so it is read as her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 654","lb":"CC654","ev":"Carrying the yazh, she moves through the throng to her place.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:655":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":655,"t":["பளிக்கொளி மணிச்சுவ ரெழினி பையவே","கிளிச்சொலி னிளையவர் நீக்கக் கிண்கிணி","யொளிக்குமின் றாடவ ருயிர்க ளென்னநொந்","தளித்தவை யிரங்கச்சென் றணையி லேறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the young women with parrot voices slowly drew back the screen of the crystal-bright gem wall, she went up onto her seat, while her anklet-bells cried in pity as if grieving that the lives of men would vanish today.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 655","lb":"CC655","ev":"The maids draw back the screen and Gandharvadattai takes her seat.","cl":[],"ag":["இளையர்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:656":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":656,"t":["உறைகழித் திலகுவா ளுடற்றுங் கண்ணினாண்","மறையொளி மணிச்சுவ ரிடையிட் டித்தலை","யிறைவளை யாழ்தழீஇ யிருப்ப வத்தலைக்","கறைகெழு வேலினார் கண்ணி தீந்தவே.."],"p":"","r":"With a light-veiling gem wall between, she with eyes like a sword drawn from its sheath sits on this side holding the yazh, and on that side the garlands of the men with blood-stained spears wither.","n":"The withering garland is the warriors' sign of defeat before any note is struck; the spears are bloody (கறைகெழு) but useless here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kan","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 656","lb":"CC656","ev":"Gandharvadattai sits with the yazh behind the gem wall facing the suitors.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:657":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":657,"t":["சிலைத்தொழிற் சிறுநுதற் றெய்வப் பாவைபோற்","கலைத்தொழிற் படவெழீஇப் பாடி னாள்கனிந்","திலைப்பொழிற் குரங்கின வீன்ற தூண்டளிர்","நிலத்திடைப் பறவைமெய்ம் மறந்து வீழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Like a divine image with a brow like a bow, she played with all the art and sang, and the bent trees of the leafy grove put out tender shoots and the birds forgot themselves and fell to the ground.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 657","lb":"CC657","ev":"Gandharvadattai plays and sings; trees bud and birds fall entranced.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:658":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":658,"t":["கருங்கொடிப் புருவ மேறா கயனொடுங் கண்ணு மாடா","வருங்கடி மிடறும் விம்மா தணிமணி யெயிறுந் தோன்றா","விருங்கடற் பவளச் செவ்வாய் திறந்திவள் பாடி னாளோ","நரம்பொடு வீணை நாவி னடந்ததோ வென்று நைந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Her dark brows did not rise, her eyes did not move, her throat did not swell, her teeth did not show; the listeners, melting, asked whether she had opened her coral mouth to sing at all, or whether the strung vina had walked upon her tongue.","n":"Mastery is marked by the absence of effort on the face, so that voice and instrument cannot be told apart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 658","lb":"CC658","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்",""],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:659":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":659,"t":["இசைத்திறத் தனங்கனே யனைய நீரினார்","வசைத்திற மிலாதவர் வான்பொன் யாழெழீஇ","விசைத்தவர் பாடலின் வெருவிப் புள்ளெலா","மசிப்பபோன் றிருவிசும் படைந்த வென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"It is said that when the men, like Ananga himself in skill of music and without fault, took up their great golden yazhs and played and sang, the birds took fright and flew up into the wide sky as if laughing at them.","n":"The praise of the suitors (வசைத்திறமிலாதவர்) is turned by the birds' mockery (அசிப்ப போன்று), and என்பவே keeps the poet at the distance of report.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 659","lb":"CC659","ev":"The suitors play and sing in turn, and the birds fly off in fright.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:660":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":660,"t":["மாதர்யாழ் தடவர வந்த மைந்தர்கைக்","கீதத்தான் மீண்டன கேள்விக் கின்னரம்","போதரப் பாடினாள் புகுந்த போயின","தாதலர் தாரினார் தாங்கள் பாடவே."],"p":"","r":"The listening kinnara birds that came when the girl stroked her yazh turned back at the music of the men's hands: when she sang they came in, and when the garlanded men sang they went away.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 660","lb":"CC660","ev":"The kinnara birds come to her singing and leave at the suitors'.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:29"},"cc:661":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":661,"t":["சுரும்பெழுந் திருத்துணுந் தொங்கல் வார்குழ","லரும்பெற லவட்கிசை யரசர் தோற்றபின்","னரம்பொடு தெள்விளி நவின்ற நான்மறை","வரம்பெறு நெறியவர் மலைதன் மேயினார்."],"p":"","r":"After the kings have lost to the long-haired girl who is so hard to win, men of the way bounded by the four Vedas, practised in the strings and the clear-voiced note, come forward to contest with her.","n":"The Brahmins are defined at once by Veda and by music: the contest is open to learning, not only to royal birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 661","lb":"CC661","ev":"With the kings defeated, Vedic Brahmins skilled in music enter the vina contest.","cl":[],"ag":["நான்மறை நெறியவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:662":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":662,"t":["திருமலர்க் கமலத் தங்கட் டேனின முரல்வ தொப்ப","விரிமலர்க் கோதை பாட வெழால்வகை வீரர் தோற்றார்","எரிமலர்ப் பவளச் செவ்வா யின்னரம் புளர மைந்தர்","புரிநரம் பிசைகொள் பாட லுடைந்தனர் பொன்ன னாட்கே."],"p":"","r":"As bees hum in a lotus, the garlanded girl sings and the heroes of the எழால் kind of music are beaten; when her coral lips and fingers sound the sweet strings, the young men's songs on twisted strings break down before her.","n":"எழால் is a technical word of the lute tradition; whether it names a mode, a kind of instrument or a class of performer is not clear from the verse alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 662","lb":"CC662","ev":"Gandharvadattai sings and plays, and the contestants' music fails against hers.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","நான்மறை நெறியவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:663":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":663,"t":["வாலரக் கெறிந்த காந்தண் மணியரும் பனைய வாகிக்","கோல்பொரச் சிவந்த கோலக் குவிவிரன் மடந்தை வீணை","நூல்பொரப் புகுந்த நுண்ணூல் வணிகருந் தொலைந்து மாதோ","கால்பொரக் கரிந்த காமர் பங்கயப் பழன மொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Her curled fingers, reddened by the plectrum like kanthal buds stained with lac, defeat even the merchants learned in the fine treatise who came to match her vina by the book, and they wilt like a lotus field scorched by the wind.","n":"The sequence of losers (kings in 661, Brahmins, now merchants versed in நூல்) runs through the orders of society, so that Civakan, the merchant's foster son, enters when every class has failed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 663","lb":"CC663","ev":"Merchants learned in music theory contest Gandharvadattai and are beaten.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","வணிகர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:664":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":664,"t":["தேனுயர் மகர வீணைத் தீஞ்சுவை யிவளைவெல்வான்","வானுயர் மதுகை வாட்டும் வார்சிலைக் காமனாகு","மூனுயர் நுதிகொள் வேலீ ரொழிகவீங் கில்லை யென்றான்","கானுய ரலங்கன்மாலைக் கட்டியங் கார னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Kattiyankaran tells the spear-bearing suitors to give up: the one who could beat her at the sweet makara-headed vina would be Kama himself, whose long bow withers the strength of the mighty, and no such one is here.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","isai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 664","lb":"CC664","ev":"Kattiyankaran declares that no one present can defeat the princess and tells the suitors to stop.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:665":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":665,"t":["மறுமுயற் கிவர்ந்த வேக மாசுண மடையப் பட்ட","நிறைமதி போன்று மன்னர் ஒளிகுறைந் துருகி நைய","வறுபகல் கழிந்த பின்றை யந்நகர்க் காதி நாய்கன்","சிறுவனோர் சிங்க வேற்றை சீவக சாமி யென்பான்,"],"p":"","r":"As the kings dim and melt like the full moon caught by the swift serpent that leaps at its hare, six days pass, and then the son of the city's chief merchant, a lion among young men called Civaka Sami, comes into the story.","n":"The verse is the first half of a sentence that runs into 666; Civakan is introduced by his foster father's rank (ஆதி நாய்கன் சிறுவன்), not by his royal birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 665","lb":"CC665","ev":"The kings lose heart, and after six days Civakan is introduced as the next contender.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:666":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":666,"t":["தம்பியுந் தோழன் மாருந் தானுமற் றெண்ணிச் சூழ்ந்து","வெம்பிய வீணைப் போருட் செல்குவம் யாமு முன்னே","தும்பறப் புத்தி சேன சொல்லிது குரவற் கென்ன","கந்துகற் கவனுஞ் சொன்னா னவனிது விளம்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, his younger brother and his companions take counsel and resolve to go first into the fierce vina contest; he asks Buddhisenan to tell this to the elder, Buddhisenan tells Kantukkatan, and Kantukkatan answers.","n":"The decision passes through the foster father before it is acted on: the hero does not go to the contest without the elder's word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","isai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 666","lb":"CC666","ev":"Civakan resolves with his brother and friends to enter the contest and sends word through Buddhisenan to Kantukkatan.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்","தோழர்","புத்திசேனன்","கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:667":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":667,"t":["ஐயனுக் கமைந்த நீரா ரறுபத்து நால்வ ரம்பொன்","வையகத் தமிர்த மன்னார் வாக்கமை பாவை யொப்பா","ரெய்திய விளமை மிக்கா ரியைந்தன ரென்று பின்னுங்","கையமை சிலையி னாற்குக் கந்துக னிதுவுங் கூறும்."],"p":"","r":"Kantukkatan says that sixty-four of a nature fitting for the master, like nectar on earth and like finely made figures, in the fullness of youth, have come together; and further he tells the bowman something more.","n":"Who or what the sixty-four (அறுபத்து நால்வர்) are is not settled by the verse: the figures of nectar, statues and ripe youth suit women, but the number is that of the arts, and the verse may say that all sixty-four have gathered in the master.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 667","lb":"CC667","ev":"Kantukkatan replies to the message and turns to tell Civakan something more.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:668":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":668,"t":["மறைவல்லாற் குரைக்கும் போழ்திற் கோயிலு ணின்று மாலைப்","பிறைவெல்லும் நுதலி னாளோர் பெண்கொடி வந்து கூந்த","லுறைசெல நீக்கிப் பைந்தா ளொண்மணிக் குவளை நீட்ட","நறைவெல்லும் நாக மாலை நோக்கொடு பூக்கொண் டானே."],"p":"","r":"While he is speaking to the learned one, a woman with a brow that outshines the crescent comes from the palace, slips her hair from its sheath and holds out a blue kuvalai on its green stalk, and he takes the flower with a look.","n":"Neither who is addressed as மறைவல்லான் nor who takes the flower is named here; 669 shows that the merchant is the one who reads what it hides.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","otru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 668","lb":"CC668","ev":"A woman from the palace brings Kantukkatan a blue lily as a covert sign.","cl":[],"ag":["பெண்கொடி","கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:669":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":669,"t":["நல்லவ ணோக்க நாய்கன் றேர்ந்துபூங் குவளைப் போதி","னல்லியுட் கிடந்த வோலை தாளது சலாகை யாதல்","சொல்லுமென் றாய்ந்து கொண்டு துகிலிகைக் கணக்கு நோக்கி","வல்லிதிற் சலாகை சுற்றி யோலையை வாசிக் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing her meaning, the merchant searches the flower and finds a palm-leaf letter lying among its petals with the stalk serving as its stylus; he takes it, works out the painter's cipher, unrolls it around the stylus and reads.","n":"The letter is in a cipher (துகிலிகைக் கணக்கு), so the warning comes from someone inside the palace who must hide it from the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","otru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 669","lb":"CC669","ev":"Kantukkatan finds a secret letter in the flower and reads it.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","பெண்கொடி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:670":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":670,"t":["நம்பனை நகரி னீக்கிச் சேமத்தால் வைக்க தீயுட்","செம்பொன்போற் பெரிதுஞ் சேந்து செகுத்திட லுற்று நின்றான்","வெம்பினான் காரி யுண்டிக் கடவுளிற் கனன்று வேந்தன்","இம்பரின் றெனக்குச் சொன்னா னிதுபட்ட தடிகள் என்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The message: take the noble one out of the city and keep him safe, for the king, reddened like gold in fire, stands ready to kill him, burning in anger like the god who swallowed poison; the king said so to me today, and this is what has happened, sir.","n":"The verse ends in என்றாள், so the woman voices the warning; how much of it is the letter's text and how much her own report is not marked. The god compared to the angry king is named only as the one who ate the poison.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vekuli","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 670","lb":"CC670","ev":"The palace woman warns Kantukkatan that the king means to kill Civakan and urges him to send Civakan out of the city.","cl":[],"ag":["பெண்கொடி","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பெண்கொடி","கந்துக்கடன்"],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:671":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":671,"t":["ஓலையை யவட்கு நீட்டி யொண்மணிக் குழையு முத்தும்","மாலையும் படுசொ லொற்றி வம்மென மறைய நல்கி","வேலைநெய் பெய்த திங்கள் விரவிய பெயரி னாற்கு","மேலைநாட் பட்ட தொன்று விளம்புவல் கேளி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Handing the letter back to her, he gives her in secret a gem earring, pearls and a garland as tokens, seals the message and bids her come again; then to the one whose name joins the moon with the sea's nectar he says he will tell something that happened in earlier days.","n":"The hearer is named by a riddling epithet (வேலைநெய் பெய்த திங்கள் விரவிய பெயரினான்) that the verse does not resolve; since Civakan himself took part in the episode now told, the hearer may be someone else.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 671","lb":"CC671","ev":"Kantukkatan rewards the messenger secretly and begins to tell of a past event.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","பெண்கொடி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்",""],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:672":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":672,"t":["கடியரங் கணிந்து மூதூர்க் கடல்கிளர்ந் தனைய தொப்ப","நடையறி புலவ ரீண்டி நாடக நயந்து காண்பான்","குடையுடை யவனொ டெண்ணிச் சீவகற் கொணர்மி னென்னத்","தொடையல்சூழ் வேலி னானுந் தோழருங் காணச் சென்றார்.."],"p":"","r":"When the old city, decked for the festival hall, surged like the sea and poets who knew the art of the stage crowded in, someone who wished to see a drama took counsel with the umbrella-bearing king and had Civakan sent for, and the spear-bearer and his friends went to watch.","n":"Who proposes the summons is left unnamed; the verse says only that it was agreed with the king, which matters for the king's later rage in 670.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 672","lb":"CC672","ev":"At a staged performance before the king, Civakan is summoned and goes with his friends to watch.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:673":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":673,"t":["நிலமறிந் தணிக வையன் சீவகன் நெறியி னென்ன","நலநுதற் பட்டங் கட்டி நகைமுடிக் கோதை சூட்டி","யலர்முலைக் குருதிச் சாந்து மாரமும் பூணுஞ் சேர்த்திக்","குலவிய குருதிப்பட்டிற் கலைநலங்கொளுத்தி யிட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Told to adorn the dancer as the rules of the stage require, Master Civakan ties the band on her fine brow, crowns her with a garland, puts blood-red sandal, a necklace and jewels on her breasts, and fits her with a skirt of red silk.","n":"The adornment is a learned skill (நிலமறிந்து, நெறியின்): Civakan is shown as master of the stage arts, and the same act sets the dancer's love in motion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","azhagu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 673","lb":"CC673","ev":"Civakan adorns the dancer for the performance.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:674":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":674,"t":["திருவிலே சொரிந்து மின்னுங் குண்டலஞ் செம்பொ னோலை","யுருவுகொண் மதிய மன்ன வொளிமுகஞ் சுடர வாக்கிப்","பரியகஞ் சிலம்பு செம்பொற் கிண்கிணி பாதஞ் சேர்த்தி","யரிவையை யரம்பை நாண வணிந்தன னனங்கன ன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The one like Anangan sets flashing earrings and gold ear-rolls so that her moon-like face shines, and puts anklet, foot-ring and gold bells on her feet, adorning the woman until Rambha would be ashamed.","n":"Calling the adorner அனங்கன் (Kama) here answers Kattiyankaran's claim in 664 that only Kama could win the princess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 674","lb":"CC674","ev":"Civakan finishes adorning the dancer.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:675":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":675,"t":["தோற்பொலி முழவும் யாழும் துளைபயில் குழலு மேங்கக்","காற்கொசி கொம்பு போலப் போந்துகைத் தலங்கள் காட்டி","மேற்பட வெருவி நோக்கித் தானைய விட்டிட் டொல்கித்","தோற்றினாள் முகஞ்செய் கோலந் துளக்கினாண் மனத்தை யெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"As skin drum, yazh and pierced flute sound, she comes on like a twig bending in the wind, shows her hand gestures, looks up as if startled, lets her garment trail and sways; the face she has made up shakes every mind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","isai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 675","lb":"CC675","ev":"The dancer enters and begins her dance to drum, lute and flute.","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:676":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":676,"t":["தெண்மட்டுத் துவலை மாலை தேனொடு துளிப்பத் திங்க","ளுண்ணட்ட குவளை போலு முருவக்கண் வெருவி யாட","விண்விட்டுக் கடவுள் வீழ நுடங்கின புருவ நெஞ்சம்","பண்விட்ட திருந்து காணும் பன்மணிக் கழலி னார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Her eyes, like kuvalai planted in the moon, dart as if in fear, her garland drips honey, and her brows bend so that gods would fall from the sky; and the hearts of the jewel-anklet men who sit watching lose their tune.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 676","lb":"CC676","ev":"The dancer's eyes and brows move as she dances, and the watchers are overcome.","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை","கண்டார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:677":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":677,"t":["செங்கதிர்ச் சிலம்பு செம்பொற் கிண்கிணி சிலம்பக் கோதை","பொங்கப்பொன் னோலை வட்டம் பொழிந்துமின் னுகுப்பப் போர்த்த","குங்குமச் சாந்து வேய்ந்து குண்டலந் திருவில் வீச","வங்கதி ரார மின்ன வரிவைகூத் தாடு கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"With anklet and gold bells ringing, her garland swelling, the gold ear-disc showering lightning, saffron paste spread on her, earrings flashing like a rainbow and her necklace gleaming, the woman dances.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 677","lb":"CC677","ev":"The dancer dances in full ornament.","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:678":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":678,"t":["மருங்குலு மொன்று தாய்க்கு மொருமக ளாத லோர்ந்து","மிரும்பினா லியன்ற நெஞ்சத் திவர்களோ விருந்து காண்க","வரங்கின்மே லிவளைத் தந்த தாய் கொலோ கயத்தி யன்றேற்","சுரும்புசூழ் கண்ணி சூட்டி யவர்கொலோ கயவர் செல்லீர்."],"p":"","r":"Some in the crowd say: knowing her waist is a single thread and she is her mother's only daughter, can these iron-hearted people sit and watch? Was it her base mother who brought her onto the stage, or else the base men who crowned her with the bee-haunted garland? Go away!","n":"The outrage is praise by hyperbole: the waist is too slender to bear a dance. Its target includes, without naming him, the one who garlanded her in 673, Civakan himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kayamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 678","lb":"CC678","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கண்டார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்டார்",""],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:679":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":679,"t":["அகிலார் புகையலாற் சாந்தணியாள் பூச்சாரச் செல்லாள் செல்லிற்","பகலே பகைவளர்த்த பாவை சிறுநுசுப் பொன் றுண்டே பாவம்","இகலேந் திளமுலைமேற் சாந்தெழுதி முத்தணிந்து பூவுஞ் சூட்ட","முகிலேந்து மின்மருங்குல் மொய்குழற்றா யிதுகண்டு முளளே பாவம்."],"p":"","r":"She wears no sandal, only agil smoke, and goes nowhere near flowers, for if she did, the doll who breeds enmity even by day would lose her small waist, alas; yet they painted sandal on her young breasts and hung pearls and flowers on her, and her mother, seeing it, still lives, alas.","n":"பகலே பகை வளர்த்த is hard to fix: it may mean the waist that day itself treats as an enemy, or the girl who breeds rivalry among men by day.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 679","lb":"CC679","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கண்டார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்டார்",""],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:680":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":680,"t":["தேந்தாமஞ் செம்பவளத் தாமஞ் செம்பொன் னெரிதாம மின்னுத் திரடா மங்க","டாந்தாந் தாமெனத் தாழ்ந்தபொன் மேகலைத் தாம வரங்கின்மேற் றாதார் முல்லைப்","பூந்தாமக் கொம்பாடக் கண்டா ரெல்லாம் புனமயிலே யன்னமே பொன்னங் கொம்பே","யாந்தா மரைமகளே யல்ல ளாயி னமரர்மக ளென்பாரு மாயி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"As the creeper garlanded with jasmine dances on the stage in strands of honeyed flowers, coral, flame-gold and lightning, her gold girdle swinging, all who see her call her a peacock of the field, a swan, a golden creeper, the lotus goddess, or if not that, a daughter of the immortals.","n":"The crowd's names run from bird to plant to goddess and end in doubt whether she is human at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","azhagu","koothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 680","lb":"CC680","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை","கண்டார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:681":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":681,"t":["கொடியார் குளிர்முத்தஞ் சூட்டி வைத்தார் கொல்வானே குங்குமச்சே றாட்டி னார்க","ளடிசார்ந்து வாழ்வாரை யம்முலைக டாமே யழித்திடு மேற்றாமே யழித்தி டுகவென்","றொடியாத மாத்திரை யாலுண் டேநுசுப் பிருந்து காண்பாரு முளரே செங்க","ணெடியான் மகன்சிலையு மம்பும் வைத்த நிழன்மதியோ வாண்முகமோ நோக்கிக் காணீர்."],"p":"","r":"Others sit saying that whoever hung cool pearls on her and bathed her in saffron is a killer, that her breasts destroy those who live at her feet and may well destroy her too, and wondering whether her waist exists at all; look, they say, is it the moon on which Kama, son of the red-eyed Tall One, has set his bow and arrows, or her bright face?","n":"Kama is named as the son of செங்கண் நெடியான், the red-eyed Tall One. Line 2's sense of who destroys whom is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nalam","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 681","lb":"CC681","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கண்டார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்டார்",""],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:682":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":682,"t":["நெய்பருகி நீண்டஇருட் கற்றை போலுங் குழற்கற்றை கண்டு நிறைக லங்குவார்","மைபருகி நீண்டு மதர்த்த வுண்கண் வாளேறு பெற்று நைவா மாநா கத்தின்","பைபருகு மல்கு லிலயம் பற்றிப் பதனமைத்த பாவை நிருத்த நோக்கி","மெய்யுருகிக் கண்ணுருகி நெஞ்சுருகிக் காம வெயில்வெண்ணெய்ப் பாவைபோன் மெலிகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing her mass of hair like ghee-fed darkness, men lose their self-command; struck by her kohl-drinking eyes as by sword blows, they pine; watching the doll keep time with her hips in the dance, they melt in body, eyes and heart and waste away like a butter doll in the sun of desire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","melivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 682","lb":"CC682","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை","கண்டார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:683":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":683,"t":["ஆடவர் மனங்க ளென்னு மரங்கின்மே லனங்க மாலை","யாடினாண் முறுவ லென்னுந் தோழியை யையன் காண","வோடரி நெடுங்க ணென்னு மோலையை யெழுதி விட்டாள்","வாடிய வாறு நோயு முரைத்துவார் கொடிய னாளே."],"p":"","r":"Anangamaalai dances on the stage of men's hearts, and so that the master may see, she writes a letter called her long streaked eyes and sends it by the friend called her smile, telling how she has withered and the sickness she suffers.","n":"The verse rewrites the plot's real letter (669) as a figure: the secret palm-leaf of the palace and the dancer's glance are both covert messages, and the second is what turned the king against Civakan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kurippu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 683","lb":"CC683","ev":"While dancing, Anangamaalai shows Civakan by glance and smile that she has fallen in love with him.","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:684":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":684,"t":["வளமல ரணியப் பெற்றேன் வால்வளை திருத்தப் பெற்றேன்","களனெனக் கரையு மல்குல் கையினாற் றீண்டப் பெற்றே","னிளமுலை சுமந்து பெற்ற வருத்தமு மின்று தீர்ந்தே","னுளமெலி மகளி ரெய்து மின்பமு மின்று பெற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"I was granted rich flowers to wear and my white bangles set right; I had my hip touched by his hand; today the toil of bearing young breasts is over, and today I have the joy that women pining in love attain.","n":"No speaker is named. The joy over being adorned and touched, and the reference to her own breasts, fit Anangamaalai remembering Civakan's dressing of her in 673-674; the verse may also be the content of the 'letter' of 683.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 684","lb":"CC684","ev":"Anangamaalai rejoices that Civakan adorned her with his own hands.","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அனங்கமாலை",""],"ch":"cc:30"},"cc:685":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":685,"t":["என்றவ ளரசன் றன்னை நோக்கல ளிவன்க ணார்வஞ்","சென்றமை குறிப்பிற் றேறிக் கூத்தெலா மிறந்த பின்றை","நின்றது மனத்திற் செற்ற நீங்கித்தன் கோயில் புக்கான்","மன்றல் மடந்தை தன்னை வலிதிற்கொண் டொலிகொ டாரான்."],"p":"","r":"When the dances are over, the dancer has not once looked at the king, and he, judging from her signs that her longing has gone to Civakan, goes into his palace with resentment fixed in his mind and takes the bridal maiden by force.","n":"The king reads her unspoken sign (குறிப்பின் தேறி) exactly as a good counsellor is meant to read one, but what he learns turns at once to grudge (செற்றம்) and seizure; the reading of signs that the dancer used to speak to Civakan in 683 is used against her here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","azhukkaaru","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 685","lb":"CC685","ev":"Kattiyankaran, seeing that Anangamalai's desire is for Civakan, carries her off by force to his palace.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:686":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":686,"t":["தேனுடைந் தொழுகுஞ் செவ்வித் தாமரைப் போது புல்லி","யூனுடை யுருவக் காக்கை யிதழுகக் குடைந்திட் டாங்குக்","கானுடை மாலை தன்னைக் கட்டியங் காரன் சூழ்ந்து","தானுடை முல்லை யெல்லாந் தாதுகப் பறித்திட் டானே."],"p":"","r":"As a crow with its body of flesh pushes into a honey-dripping lotus and tears its petals away, Kattiyankaran closes on the garlanded woman and strips all her jasmine until its pollen falls.","n":"The violence is told only through the image of the crow in the lotus and the jasmine plucked bare; the crow, a carrion bird set against the honey-bee the lotus expects, is the narrator's judgment on the usurper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","innaa","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 686","lb":"CC686","ev":"Kattiyankaran violates Anangamalai.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:687":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":687,"t":["கலையினிற் கன்னி நீக்கித் தாமரைக் கண்க டம்மான்","முலையினி லெழுதிச் செவ்வாய் பயந்ததேன் பருகி முள்கும்","சிலைவலாய் புல்லு நம்பிசீவக சாமியோ என்","றலைகடற் புலம்பி னோவா தரற்றுமா லணங்கி னன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"The woman like a goddess cries out ceaselessly, like the roaring sea, calling on Civakan as the bowman who took away her maidenhood, painted her breasts with his eyes and drank the honey of her red mouth.","n":"Nothing so far in the story has joined her and Civakan: in 683 she only sends him a message with her eyes. Whether the union she names is memory, fancy or a defiant claim made before her captor is not settled by the verse, and line 1 may be read either as his taking her maidenhood or as loosening her girdle-cloth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ninaivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 687","lb":"CC687","ev":"Anangamalai, in Kattiyankaran's hands, laments aloud and calls on Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:688":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":688,"t":["பிறனல மரற்றக் கேட்டும் பீடினாற் கனிந்த காம","நறுமல ரணிந்த மாலை நாற்றக்கோர் நான்கு காதம்","உறநடந் தறித லில்லா னொண்டொடிக் குருகிப் பின்னுந்","திறனல தமர்க்குச் செப்புந் தீயுமிழ்ந் திலங்கும் வேலான்."],"p":"","r":"Though he hears her cry out another man's beauty, the bright-speared king, who does not know how far that man's fame has spread, still melts for the bangled woman and speaks unfitting words to his own people.","n":"Lines 2 and 3, a garland of love whose scent reaches four kaatham, are hard to construe; the reading taken is that the scent is Civakan's spreading renown, which the king has not perceived. Desire for a woman who openly loves another is the same infatuation for which the story blamed the king he displaced.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pedhaimai","orutalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 688","lb":"CC688","ev":"Kattiyankaran, still infatuated, questions his followers about Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:689":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":689,"t":["விலங்க லன்ன வேக வேழ நான்கும் வெல்லு மாற்றலன்","கலங்க லந்தி லங்கு மார்பிற் கந்து கன்ம கன்னென","நலங்க லந்து ரைக்கு மாலிந் நன்ன கர்க்கு மன்னனோ","வுலங்க லந்த தோளினீரு ரைமி னீவி ரென்னவே."],"p":"","r":"Kattiyankaran asks his men whether this man of the gleaming chest, strong enough to beat four swift elephants like mountains, whom people praise as Kantukkatan's son, is then the king of this fine city, and bids them speak.","n":"The sneering question, is he king of this city?, states the truth the usurper does not know: Civakan is the son of the king whose throne he holds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhukkaaru","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 689","lb":"CC689","ev":"Kattiyankaran asks his followers who Civakan is.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","தமர்"],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:690":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":690,"t":["மட்ட விழ்ந்த தாரி னானிம் மாந கர்க்கு ளாயிரர்","தொட்டெ டுக்க லாவு லம்மொர் தோளி னேந்தி யாடினா","னொட்டி நாக மோரி ரண்டெ டுக்க லாத கல்லினை","விட்ட லர்ந்த போது போல வேந்த லேந்தி நீக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"His men answer that the garlanded youth once lifted on one shoulder and played with a weight a thousand men of this city could not raise, and set aside like a loosened flower a stone that two elephants together could not move.","n":"These feats are not told elsewhere in the chapters marked so far; the verse gives them as common knowledge of the city (இம் மாநகர்க்குள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 690","lb":"CC690","ev":"Kattiyankaran's men tell of Civakan's feats of strength.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தமர்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:691":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":691,"t":["வெஞ்சி லையின் வேடர் வெள்ள மப்பு மாரி தூவலி","னெஞ்ச லின்றி நம் படையி ரும்மு றையு டைந்தபின்","மஞ்சு சூழ்க ணைம்ம ழைபொ ழிந்து மாநி ரைபெயர்த்","தஞ்சி லோதி யார் புனைந்த செஞ்சொன் மாலை சூடினான்."],"p":"","r":"They go on: when the flood of hunters with cruel bows had rained arrows and our army had broken twice without a thought, he poured a cloud-thick rain of arrows, turned back the great herd and wore the garland of fine words that the soft-haired women made for him.","n":"The men who praise him belong to the army that broke (நம் படை), so the tale of his valour is also the admission of their own and their king's failure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 691","lb":"CC691","ev":"Kattiyankaran's men recall Civakan's recovery of the herd from the hunters.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தமர்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:692":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":692,"t":["தீம்பயறி யன்ற சோறு செப்பி னாயி ரம்மிடா","நீங்க லாந றுந்நெய் வெள்ளங் கன்ன லாயி ரங்குட","மேந்துவித்து நாமி சைய வந்து தமந்து நீக்கினா","னாங்குநாம்ப சித்தசைந்த காலை யன்ற வண்ணலே."],"p":"","r":"And when we were weary with hunger, they say, that lord had rice cooked with sweet pulses brought in a thousand pots, floods of fragrant ghee and a thousand jars of jaggery, and came himself to see our hunger ended.","n":"The feeding wins the usurper's own soldiers to Civakan's side in their speech; to the king who hears it, generosity is a political threat (693). Line 3 (வந்து தமந்து) seems disturbed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 692","lb":"CC692","ev":"Kattiyankaran's men tell how Civakan fed them when they were hungry.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தமர்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:693":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":693,"t":["இன்ன னென்ன வின்பு றானிழந்த னன்ன ரசென","வென்னை வெளவு வாயில்தா னென்னுஞ் சூழ்ச்சி தன்னுளா","னன்ன தால ரிறப வறிந்து கூத்தி கூறினா","ளின்ன தாற்ப டையமைத் தெழுமி னென்றி யம்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing what Civakan is, the king takes no pleasure, but thinks he has lost his kingdom and that this man is the way it will be seized from him; the dancer, knowing all this clearly, tells it, and word is given to arm and set out.","n":"Line 3 is corrupt (அன்ன தால ரிறப). Who receives the dancer's warning, and who gives the order in line 4 (இயம்பினான், a man), the verse does not say; 694's mention of what the father said suggests Kantukkatan, warned by her, tells Civakan's friends to go armed. Read so, the order is a precaution on Civakan's side, not the usurper's call to attack.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","soozhchi","arasu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 693","lb":"CC693","ev":"Kattiyankaran begins to plot against Civakan, and the dancer sends warning of it.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்","அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:694":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":694,"t":["தாதைதா னுரைத்த வெல்லாந் தன்னுயிர்த் தோழன் கூறக்","கோதைமுத் தணிந்த மார்பன் கூரெயி றிலங்க நக்காங்","கேதமொன் றில்லை சேறு மென்றலு மிலங்கு வாட்கைப்","போதுலாங் கண்ணி மைந்தர் போர்ப்புலிக் குழாத்திற் சூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"When his dear friend tells him all that his father has said, the youth with the pearl-hung chest laughs, showing his sharp teeth, and says there is no danger and they will go; and the young men with bright swords in hand gather round him like a pack of fighting tigers.","n":"He laughs at the warning as Saccandan laughed at Kattiyankaran's coming (266); the son's laughter is the same fearlessness, now armed with friends the father did not have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","natpu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 694","lb":"CC694","ev":"Civakan, told of the danger, laughs it off and sets out with an armed band of friends.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:695":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":695,"t":["கண்ணுதற் கடவுள் சீறக் கனலெரி குளித்த காமன்","மண்மிசைத் தோன்றி யன்ன வகைநல முடைய காளை","தெண்மணி யார மார்பன் றிருநுதன் மகளிர் நெஞ்சத்","துண்ணிறை பருகும் வண்டா ருருவமை திருவின் மிக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The youth is as if Kama, plunged into blazing fire when the god with the eye in his brow raged, had risen again on the earth: he wears a garland of bees that drink the self-command within the hearts of fair-browed women.","n":"The verse uses the burning of Kama as a measure of beauty; the image that the bee-garland drinks women's நிறை makes his beauty a threat to their self-containment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 695","lb":"CC695","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:696":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":696,"t":["கருநெறி பயின்ற குஞ்சிக் காழகில் கமழ வூட்டி","வரிநிற வண்ண மாலை வலம்பல மிலைச்சி வாளார்","திருநிற முகத்திற் கேற்பச் செம்பொனோ ரோலை சேர்த்தி","யெரிநிறக் குழையோர் காதிற் கிருளறச் சுடர வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He perfumes his dark curled locks with smoke of black agil, sets many striped garlands on them, fixes a gold earring suited to his radiant face, and in one ear hangs a fire-bright kuzhai that drives off the dark.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 696","lb":"CC696","ev":"Civakan adorns himself for the contest.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:697":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":697,"t":["தென்வரைப் பொதியிற் றந்த சந்தனத் தேய்வை தேங்கொண்","மன்வரை யகலத் தம்பி வலம்புரி யாரந் தாங்கி","மின்விரித் தனைய தொத்து விலைவரம் பறிய லாகா","வின்னுரைக் கலிங்க மேற்ப மருங்குலுக் கெழுதி வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He spreads on his broad chest sandal paste brought from the southern Pothiyil hill, wears a pearl string of the right-whorled conch, and girds his waist with a garment of priceless cloth like spread lightning.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 697","lb":"CC697","ev":"Civakan adorns himself for the contest.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:698":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":698,"t":["இரும்பறக் கழுவி யெஃகி னிருளற வடிக்கப் பட்ட","வரும்பெறற் சுரிகை யம்பூங் கச்சிடைக் கோத்து வாங்கிப்","பெருந்தகைக் குருசில் கொண்டு பெருவலஞ் சுடர வீக்கித்","திருந்திழை மகளிர் வெஃகுந் தேவிளங் குமர னொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Taking the precious dagger of steel, washed clean of every speck and its blade dark-free, he threads it into his fine sash and binds it brightly on his right side, and looks like the young god whom bejewelled women long for.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 698","lb":"CC698","ev":"Civakan adorns himself for the contest.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:699":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":699,"t":["வரைவிழித் திமைப்ப தொக்கும் வாளொளி யார மார்பின்","விரைவழித் திளைய ரெல்லாம் விழுமணிக் கலங்க டாங்கி","நுரைகிழித் தனைய நொய்ம்மை நுண்டுகின் மருங்குல் சேர்த்தி","புரைகிழித் துணரு மொப்பி னோவியப் பாவை யொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The youths with him, their chests flashing as if a mountain had opened its eyes and blinked, take up jewelled ornaments, wear fine cloth light as torn foam on their waists, and look like painted figures.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 699","lb":"CC699","ev":"Civakan's companions adorn themselves.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:700":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":700,"t":["அரக்குநீ ரெறியப் பட்ட வஞ்சனக் குன்ற மன்ன","திருக்கிள ரோடை சூழ்ந்த செம்புகர் நெற்றித் தாகி","யுருக்கியூ னுண்ணும் வேகத் துறுபுலி யனைய நாக","மருக்கனோர் குன்றஞ் சேர்ந்தாங் கண்ணறா னேனி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He mounts a tusker like a collyrium hill washed with lac-red water, its fine forehead plate set round its spotted brow, swift as a tiger that eats melted flesh, as the sun climbs a hill.","n":"அண்ணல் தான் ஏறினான் is written run together with the word before (சேர்ந்தாங் கண்ணறா னேனி னானே); the repaired letter in line 4 makes the sense clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 700","lb":"CC700","ev":"Civakan mounts an elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:701":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":701,"t":["விடுகணை விசையின் வெய்ய விளங்கொளி யிவுளித் திண்டேர்","கடுநடைக் கவரி நெற்றிக் காலியற் புரவி காய்ந்து","வடிநுனை யொளிறு மாலை வாட்படை மறவர் சூழ","வடுதிரைச் சங்க மார்ப்ப வணிநகர் முன்னி னானே."],"p":"","r":"With chariots of shining horses swift as a loosed arrow, fierce-paced horses with yak-tail plumes, and sword-bearing warriors round him, the conches sounding like breaking waves, he heads for the adorned city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 701","lb":"CC701","ev":"Civakan rides with his escort toward the city.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:702":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":702,"t":["தோற்றனண் மடந்தை நல்யாழ் தோன்றலுக் கென்று நிற்பார்","நோற்றன ணங்கை மைந்த னிளநல னுகர்தற் கென்பார்","கோற்றொடி மகளிர் செம்பொற் கோதையுங் குழையு மின்ன","வேற்றன சொல்லி நிற்பா ரெங்கணு மாயி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Some stand saying the maiden has already lost at the vina to the prince; others say she must have done penance to enjoy this youth's beauty; and women with fine bangles say such things everywhere about her gold garland and her earrings.","n":"தோற்றனள் is past: the women call the contest lost before it is held, so their gossip runs ahead of the plot; the verse sets defeat (தோற்றனள்) and penance (நோற்றனள்) in the same sound.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 702","lb":"CC702","ev":"The city's women talk of the coming contest as Civakan passes.","cl":[["a woman wins a husband like this through penance done before","general","reported",2,"city_women"]],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:703":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":703,"t":["சுறாநி றக்கொ டுங்குழை சுழன்றெ ருத்த லைத்தர","வறாம லர்ததே ரியலா னழன்று நோக்கி யையெனப்","பொறாம னப்பொ லிவெனு மணிக்கை மத்தி கையினா","லறாவி வந்து தோன்றினா னனங்க னன்ன வண்ணலே."],"p":"","r":"With his shark-shaped earrings swinging on his neck, the lord like the bodiless god, who rides the flower chariot, drives on with a jewelled whip, looking hotly about him, and appears.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 are obscure: the text seems disturbed at வறாம லர்ததே ரியலா, and பொறா மனப் பொலிவு, an unbearable splendour of mind, as the name of his whip is a reading, not a certainty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 703","lb":"CC703","ev":"Civakan arrives at the contest hall.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:704":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":704,"t":["குனிகொள் பாக வெண்மதிக் கூரி ரும்பு தானுறீ இப்","பனிகொண் மால்வ ரையெனப் படும தக்களிறீஇ","இனிதி ழிந்தி ளைய ரேத்த வின்ன கிற்கொ ழும்புகை","முனிய வுண்ட குஞ்சியான் முரண்கொண் மாட முன்னினான்."],"p":"","r":"Goading with a hook bent like the crescent moon, he dismounts gently from the musth tusker like a snow-clad mountain while the youths praise him, and the man whose hair has drunk the rich smoke of agil makes for the hall of contest.","n":"Line 2 (படும தக்களிறீஇ) is run together and doubtful in form, though the sense of a musth elephant is clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 704","lb":"CC704","ev":"Civakan dismounts and goes to the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:705":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":705,"t":["புதிதி னிட்ட பூந்தவிசி னுச்சி மேல்ந டந்தவண்","புதிதி னிட்ட மெல்லணைப் பொலிந்த வண்ணம் போகுயர்","மதிய தேறி வெஞ்சுடர் வெம்மை நீங்க மன்னிய","வுதய மென்னு மால்வரை யுவந்தி ருந்த தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Walking over freshly laid flowered carpets and seated on freshly laid soft cushions, he looks like the moon risen on the great Udaya mountain, glad to sit there, with the heat of the burning sun gone.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 705","lb":"CC705","ev":"Civakan takes his seat in the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:706":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":706,"t":["முருகு விம்மு கோதையார் மொய்ய லங்கல் வண்டுபோற்","பருகு வானி வண்ணலம் பாரித் திட்ட விந்நக","ருருகு மைங்க ணையொழித் துருவி னைய காமனார்","கருதி வந்த தென்றுதங் கண்கள் கொண்டு நோக்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"The women with brimming garlands drink in his beauty as bees drink garlands, and look at him with their eyes, thinking the god Kama has put down his five city-melting arrows and come in person.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 706","lb":"CC706","ev":"The women gaze at Civakan in the hall.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:707":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":707,"t":["முனைத்தி றத்து மிக்கசீர் முனைவர் தம்மு னைவனார்","வனப்பு மிக்க வர்களின் வனப்பு மிக்கி னியனா","நினைத்தி ருந்தி யற்றிய நிருமி தம்ம கனிவன்","கனைத்து வண்டு ளர்ந்ததார்க் காளை சீவ கன்னரோ."],"p":"","r":"The women ask whether this man is Civakan, the youth of the bee-tossed garland, a son fashioned by the chief of sages, who considered at leisure and made him lovelier than the loveliest.","n":"Who the chief of the sages is (முனைவர் தம் முனைவனார்) is not said; it may be the Jina or a creator god, and நிருமிதம் may mean a son fashioned by design or one conjured by power. The women's question is praise, not a doctrine of creation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 707","lb":"CC707","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:708":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":708,"t":["பொன்னை விட்ட சாயலாள் புணர்மு லைத்த டத்தினான்","மின்னை விட்டி லங்குபூண் விரைசெய் மார்ப மோலையா","வென்னை பட்ட வாறரோ வெழுதி நங்கை யாட்கொள்வான்","மன்னும் வந்து பட்டனன் மணிசெய் வீணை வாரியே."],"p":"","r":"They wonder how it has come about that the prince who would claim the maiden, she whose close breasts would write on his fragrant jewelled chest as on a palm leaf, has himself come and been caught in the net of the jewelled vina.","n":"The construction of lines 1 to 3 is difficult and the subject of ஆட்கொள்வான் and of மன்னும் வந்து பட்டனன் is uncertain; the reading taken is that the hunter of the contest is himself the one ensnared. வாரி may be a net or trap or may mean drawing in.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 708","lb":"CC708","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:31"},"cc:709":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":709,"t":["இனைய கூறி மற்றவ டோழி மாரு மின்புற","வனைய லாம்ப டித்தலா வடிவிற் கெல்லை யாகிய","கனைவண் டோதி கைதொழுஉங் கடவுள் கண்ணிற் கண்டவ","ரெனைய தெனைய தெய்தினார் அனைய தனைய தாயினார்."],"p":"","r":"With such words the princess's maids rejoice too, and those who look on the bee-tressed one, the limit of all beauty and a goddess to be worshipped with joined hands, each become fixed on whatever part of her their eyes reach.","n":"Who speaks the 'such words' and whom 'those who saw' see is not settled by the verse: the preceding verses give the women's talk about Civakan, and கடவுள் here can be read as the princess herself or as the man she worships; the last line's repeated எனைய/அனைய makes each viewer become what part they reached.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 709","lb":"CC709","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:710":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":710,"t":["குட்டநீர்க் குவளை யெல்லாங் கூடிமுன் னிற்க லாற்றாக்","கட்டழ கமைந்த கண்ணா ணிறையெனுஞ் சிறையைக் கைபோ","யிட்டநாண் வேலி யுந்திக் கடலென வெழுந்த வேட்கை","விட்டெரி கொளுவ நின்றா ளெரியுறு மெழுகி னின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The girl whose eyes outdo all the pool's lilies stands as desire, rising like a sea, breaks the fence of shame set round the prison of her self-containment and sets her alight, so that she stands like wax in fire.","n":"The architecture is double: நிறை is the prison (சிறை) and நாண் the fence (வேலி) around it; desire does not break nirai directly but pushes over the fence that guards it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nirai","naanam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 710","lb":"CC710","ev":"Gandharvadattai, seeing Civakan, is overcome by desire.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:711":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":711,"t":["நலத்தைமத் தாக நாட்டி நல்வலி யிளமை வாராக்","குலப்பிறப் பொன்னுங் கையாற் கோலப்பா சங்கொ ளுத்திக்","கலக்கியின் காமம் பொங்கக் கடைந்திடு கின்ற காளை","யிலைப்பொலி யலங்கன் மார்ப மியைவதென் றாகுங் கொல்லோ."],"p":"","r":"She wonders whether the chest of the youth who churns her, with her beauty as the churning stick, his strength and youth as the rope and his noble birth as the hand that pulls it, till sweet desire foams up, will ever be joined to her.","n":"The image is the churning of the sea for nectar turned inward: her own beauty is the stick, and his qualities are the rope and hands that work it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nalam","ilamai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 711","lb":"CC711","ev":"Gandharvadattai longs to be joined to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:712":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":712,"t":["தீங்கரும் பெருத்திற் றூங்கி யீயின்றி யிருந்த தீந்தே","னாங்கணாற் பருகி யிட்டு நலனுணப் பட்ட நம்பி","பூங்குழன் மகளிர் முன்னர்ப் புலம்பனீ நெஞ்சே என்றாள்","வீங்கிய காமம் வென்றார் விளைத்தவின் பத்தோ டொப்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"She tells her heart not to lament before the flower-haired women for the man whose beauty she drank with her eyes like untouched honey hanging on a sweet cane, and she seems like the bliss won by those who have conquered swelling desire.","n":"The last line compares the love-struck princess to the joy of those who have overcome desire, a striking figure in a Jain epic: the supreme measure of delight is the ascetic's, applied here to the very desire it conquers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamam","nalam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 712","lb":"CC712","ev":"Gandharvadattai bids her heart hide its longing from the women about her.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:713":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":713,"t":["கண்ணெனும் வலையி னுள்ளான கையகப் பட்டி ருந்தான்","பெண்ணெனு முழலை பாயும பெருவனப் புடைய நம்பி","எண்ணின்மற் றியாவ னாங்கொ லென்னி திற் படுத்த வேந்த","லொண்ணிற வுருவச் செந்தீ யுருவுகொண் டனைய வேலான்"],"p":"","r":"She asks who among men he can be, the man of great beauty caught in the net of her eyes, held in her grasp, who has brought her to this state, bearing a spear like red fire taken form.","n":"The second line, பெண்ணெனும் உழலை பாயும், is hard: it may say that he leaps the barrier that womanhood sets, or that he is the one at whom the bar of women strains; the reading turns on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 713","lb":"CC713","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:714":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":714,"t":["யாவனே யானு மாக வருநிறைக் கதவ நீக்கிக்","காவலென் னெஞ்ச மென்னுங் கன்னிமா டம்பு குந்து","நோவவென் னுள்ளம் யாத்தாய் நின்னையு மாலை யாலே","தேவரிற் செறிய யாப்பன் சிறிதிடைப் படுக வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Whoever he is, she says, he has opened the door of her hard self-containment, entered the virgin chamber of her guarded heart and bound it till it aches, and she in turn will bind him fast with a garland as the gods are bound, if he will only wait a little.","n":"The vow answers binding with binding: he tied her heart, she will tie him with the marriage garland, which she does in 738.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","nenju","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 714","lb":"CC714","ev":"Gandharvadattai resolves to garland the unknown youth.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:715":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":715,"t":["கழித்தவே லேறு பெற்ற கடத்திடைப் பிணையின் மாழ்கி","விழித்துவெய் துயிர்த்து மெல்ல நடுங்கித் தன் றோழி கூந்த","லிழுக்கிவண் டிரியச் சேர்ந்தோர் கொடிப்புல்லுங் கொடியிற் புல்லி","யெழிற்றகை மார்பற் கின்யா ழிதுவுய்த்துக் கொடுமோ வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Fainting like a doe whose mate has taken a thrown spear in the wasteland, she opens her eyes, sighs hotly, trembles, and clinging to her companion as a creeper clings to a creeper so that the bees fly from the companion's hair, asks her to carry this sweet lute to the man of the handsome chest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 715","lb":"CC715","ev":"Gandharvadattai swoons and asks her companion to take a lute to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:716":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":716,"t":["தடங்க ணாள்ப ணியினாற் றானவ் வீணை யொன்றினை","நெடுங்க ணாளெ ழினியை நீக்கி யுய்த்து நீட்டினாள்","மடங்க லன்ன மொய்ம்பினான் வருக வென்று கொண்டுதன்","கிடந்த ஞானத் தெல்லையைக் கிளக்க லுற்று நோக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"At the wide-eyed princess's bidding the long-eyed maid draws back the curtain and holds out a vina, and the lion-strong youth, saying 'let it come', takes it and looks it over, meaning to declare the full reach of his knowledge.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 716","lb":"CC716","ev":"A maid passes a vina through the curtain and Civakan takes it to judge it.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:717":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":717,"t":["சுரந்து வானஞ் சூன்முதிர்ந்து மெய்ந்நொந் தீன்ற துளியேபோற்","பரந்த கேள்வித் துறைபோய பைந்தார் மார்பன் பசும்பொன்யாழ்","நரம்பு தேனார்த் தெனத்தீண்டி நல்லாள் வீணை பொல்லாமை","யிருந்த முலையா ணின்றாளை நோக்கி யிசையி னிதுசொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The man of wide learning, as abundant as the rain the swollen sky bears in labour, touches the strings of the golden lute so they hum like bees, and, looking at the woman who stands by, tells her in music what is wrong with the lady's vina.","n":"His judgement is given 'in music' (இசையின்), so the examination of the instrument is already a performance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","isai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 717","lb":"CC717","ev":"Civakan tries the first vina and begins to name its faults.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:718":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":718,"t":["நீர் நின் றிளகிற் றிதுவேண்டா நீரின் வந்து திதுபோக","வார் நின் றிளகு முலையினாய் வாட் புண்ணுற்ற திது நடக்க","வோரு முருமே றிதுவுண்ட தொழிக வொண்பொன் னுகுகொடியே","சீர்சால் கணிகை சிறுவன்போற் சிறப்பின் றம்ம விதுவென்றான"],"p":"","r":"He rejects vina after vina: this one was softened by standing in water, this one drifted in on a flood, this one bears a sword wound, this one was struck by thunder, and this one has no worth, like the son of a famed courtesan.","n":"Each fault is a fault of the tree the wood came from, so the instruments are judged by their origin; the last is put in human terms, birth from a courtesan, and spoken by a hero whose own birth is at this point hidden and who passes as a merchant's son.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","isai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 718","lb":"CC718","ev":"Civakan rejects several vinas, naming the defect of each one's wood.","cl":[["a courtesan's son lacks the standing of good birth","general","reported",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:719":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":719,"t":["கல்சேர் பூண்கொள் கதிர்முலையாய் காமத் தீயால் வெந்தவர்போற்","கொல்லை யுழவர் சுடப்பட்டுக் குரங்கி வெந்த திதுகளிறு","புல்ல முரிந்த தெனப்போக்கித் தூம மார்ந்த துகிலுறையும்","நல்யாழ் நீட்ட வதுகொண்டு நங்கை நலத்த திதுவென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Telling the girl of the bright breasts that this one, like those burned by the fire of desire, was scorched and warped when farmers burned the clearing, and this one cracked when an elephant rubbed against its tree, he sends them back, and when a good lute in a smoke-scented cloth cover is held out he takes it and says it is sound.","n":"The simile 'like those burned by the fire of desire' brings the princess's state (710) into the inspection of a burnt instrument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 719","lb":"CC719","ev":"Civakan rejects two more vinas and accepts a lute brought in its cover.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:720":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":720,"t":["இருநில மடந்தை யீன்ற திருவிசும் பென்னுங் கைத்தாய்","திருநல மின்னுப் பொன்ஞாண் முகில்முலை மாரித் தீம்பா","லொருநலங் கவினி யூட்ட வுண்டுநோய் நான்கு நீங்கி","யருநலங் கவினி வாள்வா யரிந்திது வந்த தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He tells the lute's descent: its tree was born of the great earth-maiden and nursed by the sky as foster mother, who fed it the sweet milk of rain from cloud breasts with lightning for a golden cord, so that, free of the four diseases, it grew to rare beauty and was cut by the blade's edge.","n":"The good wood is given a pedigree like a person's, a mother who bore it and a foster mother (கைத்தாய்) who reared it; 'the four diseases' are presumably the defects just listed, though the verse does not name them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","mazhai","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 720","lb":"CC720","ev":"Civakan declares the origin of the accepted lute's wood.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:721":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":721,"t":["தீந்தொடை நரம்பின் றீமை சிறிதலாப் பொழுது மோதிப்","பூந்தொடை யரிவை காணப் புரிஞெகிழ்த் துரோமங் காட்டத்","தேங்கம ழோதி தோற்றாள் செல்வனுக் கென்ன மைந்தன்","வாங்குபு நபுலன் கையுள் வார்புரி நரம்பு கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"In no time he tells the faults of the strings, untwists one to show the princess the hair caught in it, and as people say the fragrant-haired one has lost to the rich youth, he takes fresh long-twisted strings from Nabulan's hand.","n":"The first defeat is in judgement, before any playing: the princess's instrument is shown to be flawed down to a hair in a string.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","isai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 721","lb":"CC721","ev":"Civakan exposes a hair in the strings and takes new strings from Nabulan, and the onlookers say the princess has lost.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நபுலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:722":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":722,"t":["பணிவரும் பைம்பொற் பத்தர் பல்வினைப் பவள வாணி","மணிகடை மருப்பின் வாளார் மாடக வயிரத் தீந்தே","னணிபெற வொழுகி யன்ன வமிழ்துறழ் நரம்பி னல்யாழ்","கணிபுகழ் காளை கொண்டு கடலகம் வளைக்க லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Taking the fine lute with its golden bowl hard to fashion, coral pegs of many works, gem-polished ivory stem, diamond tuning knob and strings like nectar flowing as honey, the youth whom the astrologers praised sets out to hold the sea-girt world in its sound.","n":"The parts named (பத்தர், ஆணி, மருப்பு, மாடகம், நரம்பு) are the classical lute's anatomy; கணிபுகழ் காளை recalls the horoscope of 539.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 722","lb":"CC722","ev":"Civakan takes up the restrung lute to play.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:723":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":723,"t":["குரல்குர லாகப் பண்ணிக் கோதைதாழ் குஞ்சி யான்றன்","விரல்கவர்ந் தெடுத்த கீத மிடறெனத் தெரித றேற்றார்","சுரரொடு மக்கள் வீழ்ந்தார் சோர்ந்தன புள்ளு மாவு","முருகின மரமுங் கல்லு மோர்த்தெழீஇப் பாடு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Tuning the kural string as the ground note, the youth with the garland hanging in his locks draws music with his fingers that none can tell from a voice; gods and men fall, birds and beasts droop, trees and stones melt, and having listened he rises into song.","n":"குரல் is both the first note of the scale and the voice, and the line plays on both; whether the falling and melting follow the playing or the singing that begins in line 4 is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["isai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 723","lb":"CC723","ev":"Civakan tunes and plays, and all beings are overcome, before he begins to sing.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:724":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":724,"t":["கன்னி நாகங் கலங்க மலங்கி","மின்னு மிரங்கு மழையென் கோயான்","மின்னு மழையின் மெலியு மரிவை","பொன்னாண் பொருத முலையென் கோயான்."],"p":"","r":"The first song asks whether to call it the cloud that flashes and thunders till the young snake is shaken, or the breast pressed by the golden cord of the girl who wastes away at the lightning-lit rain.","n":"Each stanza of the song sets a rain-season scene against a woman's distress at it; that the rain grieves her implies an absent man, but the song never says so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 724","lb":"CC724","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:725":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":725,"t":["கருவி வானங் கான்ற புயலி","னருவி யரற்று மலையென் கோயா","னருவி யரற்று மலைகண் டழுங்கு","மருவார் சாயன் மனமென் கோயான்."],"p":"","r":"The second asks whether to call it the hill where waterfalls cry under the rain the cloud pours, or the heart of the soft-graced woman who grieves to see that hill of crying falls.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","pirivu","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 725","lb":"CC725","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:726":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":726,"t":["வான மீனி னரும்பி மலர்ந்து","கானம் பூத்த காரென் கோயான்","கானம் பூத்த கார்கண் டழுங்குந்","தேனார் கோதை பரிந்தென் கோயான்."],"p":"","r":"The third asks whether to call it the rain season that set the forest budding and flowering like the stars of the sky, or the honey-garlanded woman who grieves at the sight of that flowering season.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 726","lb":"CC726","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:727":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":727,"t":["அண்ணல்யாழ் நரம்பை யாய்ந்து மணிவிர றவழ்ந்த வாறும்","பண்ணிய விலயம் பற்றிப் பாடிய வனப்பு நோக்கி","விண்ணவர் வீணை வீழ்த்தார் விஞ்சையர் கனிந்து சோர்ந்தார்","மண்ணவர் மருளின் மாய்ந்தார் சித்தரு மனத்துள் வைத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing how the lord's jewelled fingers crept over the strings and the beauty of his singing that kept the rhythm, the gods let fall their vinas, the Vidyadharas melted and drooped, the people of the earth swooned in wonder and the siddhas kept it in their hearts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 727","lb":"CC727","ev":"Gods, Vidyadharas, men and siddhas are overcome by Civakan's playing.","cl":[],"ag":["விண்ணவர்","விஞ்சையர்","மண்ணவர்","சித்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:728":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":728,"t":["வீழ்மணி வண்டு பாய்ந்து மிதித்திடக் கிழிந்த மாலை","சூழ்மணிக் கோட்டு வீணைச் சுகிர்புரி நரம்பு நம்பி","யூழ்மணி மிடறு மொன்றாய்ப் பணிசெய்த வாறு நோக்கித்","தாழ்மணித் தாம மார்பிற் கின்னரர் சாம்பி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing how the bees falling dazed tore the garlands, and how the vina's strings and the man's jewel-like throat worked as one, the kinnaras with their garlands drooped.","n":"ஊழ் in ஊழ்மணி மிடறு here seems to mean order or ripeness of the voice, not fate; the verse makes no claim about karma.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 728","lb":"CC728","ev":"The kinnaras are overcome by the unison of voice and strings.","cl":[],"ag":["கின்னரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:729":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":729,"t":["விண்ணவர் வியப்ப விஞ்சை வீரர்கள் விரும்பி யேத்த","மண்ணவர் மகிழ வான்கட் பறவைமெய்ம் மறந்து சோர","வண்ணறா னனங்க னாணப் பாடினா னரச ரெல்லாம்","பண்ணமைத் தெழுதப் பட்ட பாவைபோ லாயி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"He sang so that the gods marvelled, the Vidyadhara heroes praised him with longing, men rejoiced, the birds of the sky forgot their bodies and dropped, and Kama himself was put to shame, while all the kings became like dolls painted in a picture.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 729","lb":"CC729","ev":"Civakan's song leaves the assembled kings motionless.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:730":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":730,"t":["பருந்து நிழலும்போற் பாட்டு மெழாலுந்","திருந்துதார்ச் சீவகற்கே சேர்ந்தனவென் றெண்ணி","விருந்தாக யாழ்பண்ணி வீணைதான் தோற்பா","னிருந்தா ளிளமயில்போ லேந்திலைவேற் கண்ணாள்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking that song and lute have both gone over to Civakan as a kite's shadow goes with the kite, the spear-eyed girl like a young peahen sits to tune her lute as his guest's due and to lose at the vina.","n":"தோற்பான் can be read as purpose, 'in order to lose', which would make her defeat a choice, or as describing her as the one who is to lose; the verse leaves it open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["isai","kurippu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 730","lb":"CC730","ev":"Gandharvadattai, judging herself outmatched, sits to play her turn.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:731":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":731,"t":["கோதை புறந்தாழக் குண்டலமும் பொற்றோடுங்","காதி னொளிர்ந் திலங்கக் காமர் நுதல்வியர்ப்ப","மாத ரெருத்த மிடங்கோட்டி மாமதுர","கீதம் கிடையிலாள் பாடத் தொடங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her garland hanging down her back, her earrings and gold ear-ornaments gleaming, her lovely brow sweating, the peerless girl turns her neck aside and begins to sing a sweet song.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 731","lb":"CC731","ev":"Gandharvadattai begins to sing.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:732":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":732,"t":["இலையா ரெரிமணிப்பூ ணேந்து முலையுஞ்","சிலையார் திருநுதலுஞ் செம்பசலை மூழ்க","மலையா ரிலங்கருவி வாள்போல மின்னுங்","கலையார்தீஞ் சொல்லினாய் காணார்கொல் கேள்வர்."],"p":"","r":"Her song asks a friend of sweet, skilled speech whether, when the breasts that bear jewels of flame and the bow-curved brow are drowned in red pallor, while the mountain's falls flash like a sword, the lovers do not see.","n":"This is the first of three stanzas (733 and 734 repeat it with variations); the song is a woman's complaint in separation, sung by a princess who has never been separated, and its refrain காணார்கொல் கேள்வர் makes the pallor a sign meant for the absent man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 732","lb":"CC732","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:32"},"cc:733":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":733,"t":["பிறையார் திருநுதலும் பேரமருண் கண்ணும்","பொறையார் வனமுலையும் பூம்பசலை மூழ்க","நிறைவா ளிலங்கருவி நீள்வரைமேன் மின்னுங்","கறைவேலுண் கண்ணினாய் காணார்கொல் கேள்வர்."],"p":"","r":"In her song Gandharvadattai asks a woman with eyes like a stained spear whether her lover does not see that her crescent brow, wide eyes and full breasts are drowned in pallor, while lightning flashes over the long hill with its silver falls.","n":"The stanza repeats the frame of 732 with the refrain காணார்கொல் கேள்வர்: an akam song of a woman pale in separation, sung by a princess who is herself waiting to learn whom she must marry. The addressee is the song's heroine, not anyone in the hall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 733","lb":"CC733","ev":"Gandharvadattai sings the second stanza of her song in the vina contest.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:734":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":734,"t":["அரும்பேர் வனமுலையு மாடமைமென் றோளுந்","திருந்தேர் பிறைநுதலுஞ் செம்பசலை மூழ்க","நெருங்கார் மணியருவி நீள்வரைமேன் மின்னுங்","கரும்பார்தீஞ் சொல்லினாய் காணார்கொல் கேள்வர்."],"p":"","r":"The next stanza asks the woman of sugarcane-sweet speech the same question: does her lover not see her rare breasts, bamboo-soft shoulders and fine crescent brow drowned in red pallor, while lightning flashes over the long hill of crowded gem-bright falls?","n":"Three stanzas (732 to 734) vary only the epithets, as in a vari song; the lightning on the hill is the season sign that the absent lover should have returned by.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 734","lb":"CC734","ev":"Gandharvadattai sings the third stanza of her song.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:735":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":735,"t":["பண்ணொன்று பாட லதுவொன்று பல்வளைக்கை","மண்ணொன்று மெல்விரலும் வாள்நரம்பின் மேல்நடவா","விண்ணின் றியங்கி மிடறு நடுநடுங்கி","எண்ணின்றி மாதர் இசைதோற் றிருந்தனளே."],"p":"","r":"Her tune goes one way and her song another, the soft fingers of her bangled hand no longer move on the bright string, her throat trembles, and the woman sits defeated at music beyond reckoning.","n":"The defeat is told as a body failing at its art (tune, song, finger and throat parting from one another), not as a judgment by referees. எண்ணின்றி (line 4) may mean 'past counting' or 'without her wits'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 735","lb":"CC735","ev":"Gandharvadattai's playing and singing fall apart, and she loses the contest to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:736":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":736,"t":["மையார் நெடுங்கண்ணாண் மாமணியாழ் தானுடைந்து","நையா நடுநடுங்கா நனிநாண மீதூராப்","பொய்யாதோர் குன்றெடுப்பாள் போன்மெலிந்து பொன்மாலை","பெய்பூங் கழலாற்குப் பெண்ணரசி யேந்தினளே."],"p":"","r":"Broken at her great jewelled harp, wasting, trembling and overcome with shame, the kohl-eyed princess, as if lifting a mountain, raises the golden garland for the man of the flowered anklet.","n":"The garland weighs like a mountain: the shame of losing and the shyness of choosing are one weight, and the verse lets the reader feel both in the same gesture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 736","lb":"CC736","ev":"Gandharvadattai, defeated, lifts the bridal garland for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:737":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":737,"t":["மெல்லென் சிலம்பரற்ற மேகலைகண் மின்னுமிழ","நல்ல பெடையன்ன நாண வடியொதுங்கி","யொல்லெ னுயர்தவமே செய்ம்மி னுலகத்தீ","ரெல்லீரு மென்பாள்போ லேந்தன்மேல் வீழ்த்தனளே."],"p":"","r":"With her anklets crying and her girdle flashing she steps softly, shy as a fine hen-swan, and drops the garland on the lord as if telling all the people of the world to make haste and perform high austerity.","n":"The claim is the poet's fancy (என்பாள்போல், 'as if she said'), but it is the narrator's own: a bridal joy is presented as the reward of tapas done before. The verse names no school, though a Jain epic that ends in Civakan's renunciation here turns tapas toward the very pleasures it will later renounce.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","punarchi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 737","lb":"CC737","ev":"Gandharvadattai lets the garland fall on Civakan.","cl":[["such a union is the fruit of austerity, so all should hasten to perform it","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:738":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":738,"t":["நாகத்துப் படங்கொ ளல்கு னலங்கிளர் செம்பொன் மாலை","மேகத்துப் பிறந்தோர் மின்னு மணிவரை வீழ்ந்த தேபோ","லாகத்துப் பூட்டி மைந்த னடிதொழு திறைஞ்சி நின்றாள்","போகத்து நெறியைக் காட்டும் பூமகள் புணர்ந்த தொப்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"She fastens the golden garland on his chest like lightning born of a cloud falling on a gem mountain, bows at the young man's feet in worship and stands like the lotus goddess who shows the way of enjoyment, joined to him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 738","lb":"CC738","ev":"Gandharvadattai garlands Civakan and bows at his feet.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:739":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":739,"t":["செம்மல ரடியு நோக்கித் திருமணி யல்கு னோக்கி","வெம்முலைத் தடமு நோக்கி விரிமதி முகமு நோக்கி","விம்மிதப் பட்டு மாதோ விழுங்குவான் போல வாகி","மைம்மலர்த் தடங்க ணங்கை மரைமலர்த் தேவி என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Gazing at her red-flower feet, her hips, her breasts and her moon face, Civakan is struck with wonder, as if he would swallow her, and says the lady of the wide dark-flower eyes is the lotus goddess herself.","n":"Her gaze went down to his feet (738); his travels over her from feet to face. மரைமலர்த் தேவி picks up the lotus goddess of 738 and is taken up in Kattiyankaran's திருமகள் (744), where the goddess of beauty becomes the goddess of sovereignty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 739","lb":"CC739","ev":"Civakan gazes at his bride and exclaims that she is the lotus goddess.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:740":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":740,"t":["கோதையுந் தோடு மின்னக் குண்டலந் திருவில் வீச","மாதரம் பாவை நாணி மழைமினி னொசிந்து நிற்பக்","காதலந் தோழி மார்கள் கருங்கயற் கண்ணி னாளை","யேதமொன் றின்றிப் பூம்பட் டெந்திர வெழினி வீழ்த்தார்."],"p":"","r":"As her garland and ear-jewels flash and her earrings throw out a rainbow, the girl bends in shyness like lightning in rain-cloud, and her loving companions lower the mechanical silk screen over her without mishap.","n":"எந்திர எழினி: the screen is a machine, like the peacock car of the earlier books; the princess is removed from the hall at the moment the kings' anger is about to break.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 740","lb":"CC740","ev":"Gandharvadattai's companions lower a mechanical curtain to hide her.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:741":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":741,"t":["வெள்ளிலை வேற்க ணாளைச் சீவகன் வீணை வென்றா","னொள்ளிய னென்று மாந்த ருவாக்கடல் மெலிய வார்ப்பக்","கள்ளராற் புலியை யேறு காணிய காவன் மன்ன","னுள்ளகம் புழுங்கி மாதோ வுரைத்தனன் மன்னர்க் கெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"When the crowd roared like the full-moon sea that Civakan, a man of radiance, had won the vina from the spear-eyed girl, the guardian king, burning within, spoke to all the kings.","n":"Kattiyankaran is called only காவல் மன்னன், the guardian king: the usurper holds the title of the protector. Line 3 (கள்ளராற் புலியை ஏறு காணிய) is obscure; it may compare him to a bull that sees a tiger brought on by thieves, or allude to the cattle raid of the earlier book, and the reading here does not rest on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","utpagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 741","lb":"CC741","ev":"The crowd hails Civakan's victory, and Kattiyankaran, burning with envy, addresses the assembled kings.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:742":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":742,"t":["வடதிசைக் குன்ற மன்ன வான்குல மாசு செய்தீர்","விடுகதிர்ப் பருதி முன்னர் மின்மினி விளக்க மொத்தீர்","வடுவுரை யென்று மாயும் வாளமர் அஞ்சி னீரேன்","முடிதுறந் தளியிர் போகி முனிவனம் புகுமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Kattiyankaran tells the kings that they have stained their lineage, lofty as the northern mountain, and are like fireflies before the rayed sun; if they fear the sword battle where men die, the scar of blame will last for ever, so let them give up their crowns and go into the hermits' forest.","n":"Renunciation is used here as an insult: to enter the முனிவனம் is the fate of a shamed king. The speaker means to provoke a fight he will not join, which 755 exposes, so the claim is the villain's and the work does not endorse it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","kudi","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 742","lb":"CC742","ev":"Kattiyankaran taunts the defeated suitors as cowards fit only for the hermit forest.","cl":[["a king who shrinks from battle should give up his crown and leave for the forest of ascetics","general","reported",4,"kattiyangaran"]],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:743":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":743,"t":["மல்லுப்பூத் தகன்ற மார்பீர் புகழெனும் போர்வை போர்த்துச்","செல்வப்பூ மகளு நாளை அவனுழைச் செல்லு மென்றான்.","முல்லைப்பூம் பந்து தன்னை மும்மதக் களிற்று வேலிக்","கொல்லைப்பூங் குன்றஞ் செய்தீர் குங்குமக் குழங்கன் மாலை"],"p":"","r":"He goes on: they who made of the jasmine ball of a girl a flowered hill fenced by three-musth elephants, whose broad chests bear saffron-smeared garlands, should wrap themselves in the cloak of fame, for tomorrow the goddess of fortune too will go to him.","n":"The lines as printed are out of order: line 2 ends with என்றான், and lines 3 and 4 read as the verse's opening, so the sense is taken in the order 3, 4, 1, 2. The image of the jasmine ball and the elephant-fenced hill is hard and may instead mock the kings' display; 'cloak of fame' reads as sarcasm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pugazh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 743","lb":"CC743","ev":"Kattiyankaran warns the kings that royal fortune will follow the bride to the winner.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:744":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":744,"t":["திருமக ளிவளைச் சேர்ந்தான் றெண்டிரை யாடை வேலி","யிருநில மகட்குஞ் செம்பொ னேமிக்கு மிறைவ னாகுஞ்","செருநிலத் திவனை வென்றீர் திருவினுக் குரியீ ரென்றான்","கருமன நச்சு வெஞ்சொற் கட்டியங் கார னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The man who has joined this goddess of fortune, he says, will become lord of the earth-woman clad in the clear sea and of the golden wheel of rule; if they defeat him on the battlefield, fortune is theirs; so spoke Kattiyankaran of black heart and poisoned, cruel words.","n":"The incitement is a lie that tells the truth: Civakan is the heir of the throne Kattiyankaran holds, and will indeed become lord of the earth. The narrator brands the speaker in line 4 (கருமன நச்சு வெஞ்சொல்), so the reader is not to take his promise to the kings as meant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kadunjol","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 744","lb":"CC744","ev":"Kattiyankaran urges the kings to win sovereignty by killing Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:745":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":745,"t":["அனிச்சப்பூங் கோதை சூட்டி னம்மனை யோவென் றஞ்சிப்","பனிக்குநுண் ணுசுப்பிற் பாவை யொருத்திநாம் பலரென் றெண்ணித்","துனித்துநீர் துளங்கல் வேண்டா தூமணிச் சிவிறி நீர்தூய்த்","தனிக்கயத் துழக்கி வென்றீர் தையலைச் சார்மின் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He mocks them further: let them not falter in sullen thought that the frail-waisted girl, who cries 'mother!' in fear at the touch of an anicham garland, is one and they are many; they who have won only by stirring a lone pond with water squirted from jewelled syringes should go and take the lady.","n":"The verse seems to set the girl's softness against the kings' play-battles of the bathing pond, so that seizing her is made to look easy and their hesitation unmanly; the construction of lines 1 to 2 is unclear, and whose fear அம்மனையோ voices is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 745","lb":"CC745","ev":"Kattiyankaran goads the kings to seize the bride.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:746":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":746,"t":["வெந்திற லாளன் கூற வேகமோ டுரறி மன்னர்","பந்தணி விரலி னாடன் படாமுலைப் போகம் வேண்டிக்","கந்தெனத் திரண்ட திண்டோட் கந்துகன் சிறுவன் காயு","மைந்தலை யரவின் சீற்றத் தாரழல் குளிக்க லுற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"At the fierce man's words the kings, roaring in haste and wanting the enjoyment of the unsagged breasts of the ball-playing girl, set out to plunge into the blazing fire of the anger of Kantukkan's son, strong-shouldered as a pillar, a five-headed serpent.","n":"The motive the narrator gives is lust (படாமுலைப் போகம் வேண்டி), not honour, though Kattiyankaran's taunts were all of honour. Civakan is still known only as the merchant Kantukkan's son.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","igal","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 746","lb":"CC746","ev":"The suitor kings rise in uproar to fight Civakan for the bride.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:747":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":747,"t":["பண்ணியல் யானை மேலான் பதுமுகன் பரவைத் தானே","கண்ணிய துணர்ந்து கல்லாக் கட்டியங் கார னெஞ்சி","லெண்ணிய தெண்ணி மன்ன ரிகன்மலைந் தெழுந்த போழ்திற்","றண்ணிய சிறிய வெய்ய தழற்சொலால் சாற்று கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Padumukan, on his trained elephant amid his sea of troops, grasped what the unlearned Kattiyankaran had in mind, and as the kings rose to fight he spoke words at once cool, slight and burning.","n":"கல்லாக் கட்டியங்காரன், 'Kattiyankaran who has no learning', is set against the contest just won by learning and against Padumukan's closing boast இற்று எமர் கல்வி (756).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","soozhchi","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 747","lb":"CC747","ev":"Padumukan, Civakan's friend, sees through Kattiyankaran's scheme and begins to answer the kings.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:748":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":748,"t":["இசையினி லிவட்குத் தோற்றாம் யானையால் வேறு மென்னி","னிசைவதொன் றன்று கண்டீ ரிதனையா னிரந்து சொன்னேன்","வசையுடைத் தரசர்க் கெல்லாம் வழிமுறை வந்த வாறே","திசைமுகம் படர்க வல்லே தீத்தொட்டாற் சுடுவ தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Padumukan says: to argue 'we lost to her at music, so we will win by elephants' is not fitting, and he says so pleading; it is a disgrace to every king to break the way that has come down by rule; let them go quickly to their own quarters, for fire burns when it is touched.","n":"இவட்குத் தோற்றாம் quotes the kings' own position: they had all been beaten by Gandharvadattai before Civakan beat her. The closing proverb makes the hidden prince the fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","aram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 748","lb":"CC748","ev":"Padumukan tells the kings that taking the bride by force after losing the contest disgraces them, and bids them leave.","cl":[["kings who lose a contest held by the agreed rule disgrace themselves if they seek the prize by force","general","asserted",3,"padumukan"]],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:749":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":749,"t":["தோளினான் மிடைந்து புல்லுந் தொண்டைவா யமிர்தம் வேட்டோர்","வாளினான் மலைந்து கொள்ளின் வாழ்கநுங் கலையு மாதோ","கோளுலாஞ் சிங்க மன்னான் கொடியினை யெய்தப் பெற்றீர்","தாளினால் நொய்யீ ராகித் தரணிதா விடுமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Those who craved the nectar of her coral lips, won by the embrace of shoulders, would take it by the sword: long live their arts! Did they get the creeper-like daughter of the lion-like king? Let them grow light of foot and leap away over the earth.","n":"வாழ்க நுங் கலை is sarcasm: the kings who failed at the art of the vina now turn to the sword, and Padumukan blesses their 'learning'. The lion-like king is the bride's father, Kaluzhavegan, whose daughter they could not win by his rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kalvi","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 749","lb":"CC749","ev":"Padumukan mocks the kings and tells them to flee.","cl":[["a woman's love, which is won by embrace, cannot be taken by the sword","general","asserted",2,"padumukan"]],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:750":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":750,"t":["நாறுமும் மதத்தி னாலே நாகத்தை யிரிக்கும் நாக","மாறிய சினத்த தன்றி யதிங்கத்தின் கவளங் கொண்டால்","வேறுநீர் நினைந்து காணீர் யாவர்க்கும் விடுக்க லாகா","நாறித்தே னொழுகுங் கோதை நம்பிக்கும் அன்ன ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"An elephant whose three musths put other elephants to flight changes its anger once it takes a ball of sweet food; let them think again, for she cannot be released to anyone: the honey-dripping garlanded lady is such a one to the Nambi, Padumukan says.","n":"The comparison is hard: அதிங்கம் is a sweet root, and the point may be that Civakan, like a tamed bull elephant, is settled with his portion, or that she is to him what that morsel is to the elephant. The reading follows the last two lines, which are clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 750","lb":"CC750","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:751":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":751,"t":["இளவள நாகு புல்லி யினத்திடை யேறு நின்றா","லுளவளங் கருதி யூக்க லுழப்பெரு துடைய தாமே","தளவள முகைகொள் பல்லாட் சீவகற் றழுவி நின்றாற்","கொளவுளைந் தெழுவ தல்லாற் கூடுத னுங்கட் காமோ."],"p":"","r":"When a bull stands embracing a young heifer among the herd, do the other bulls, thinking of their strength, strive with him? If the girl of jasmine-bud teeth stands embracing Civakan, can they unite with her except by rising up in anguish to seize her?","n":"The first of three animal proofs (751 to 753): bull and heifer, lion and lioness, musth elephant and cow-elephant. Each gives a law of the herd that the kings, as men, are breaking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 751","lb":"CC751","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:752":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":752,"t":["எழுந்துவிண் படருஞ் சிங்கம் பெட்டைமே லிவர்ந்து நின்றான்","மழுங்கமேற் சென்று பாய்தன் மறப்புலி தனக்கு மாமோ","கொழுங்கயற் கண்ணி னாளைச் சீவக குமரன் சூழ்ந்தா","லழுங்கச்சென் றணைதல் பேய்காள் அனங்கற்கு மாவ துண்டே."],"p":"","r":"When a lion that springs into the sky has mounted its lioness, can even a fierce tiger leap on it? If Prince Civakan has embraced the carp-eyed one, ghouls, is it possible even for the bodiless Love god to go and approach her?","n":"பேய்காள், 'you ghouls', is Padumukan's word for the kings. Not even Anangan, desire itself, could come near her now; still less those whom desire drives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 752","lb":"CC752","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:753":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":753,"t":["மத்திரிப் புடைய நாகம் வாய்வழி கடாத்த தாகி","யுத்தமப் பிடிக்க ணின்றா லுடற்றுதல் களபக் காமே","பத்தினிப் பாவை நம்பி சீவகன் பால ளானா","லத்திறங் கருதி யூக்க லரசிர்கா ணுங்கட் காமோ."],"p":"","r":"When a musth elephant with the must flowing from its mouth stands by a noble cow-elephant, is a calf fit to attack it? If the chaste doll of a girl belongs to the Nambi Civakan, kings, are they fit to strive for her?","n":"பத்தினிப் பாவை: the word for the chaste wife is used of a bride on the day she garlands her husband. Her karpu begins with the choice, not with years of marriage, and it is said to make her unreachable by force.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","karpu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 753","lb":"CC753","ev":"","cl":[["a chaste woman who belongs to one man is beyond the reach of any other","general","asserted",3,"padumukan"]],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:754":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":754,"t":["தூமத்தாற் கெழீஇய கோதை தோட்டுணை பிரித்தல் விண்மேற்","றாமத்தாற் கெழீஇய மார்ப னிந்திரன் றனக்கும் ஆகா","தேமுற்றீ ரின்னும் கேண்மி னிரதியைப் புணர்து மென்று","காமத்தாற் கெழுமி னார்க்குக் காமனிற் பிரிக்க லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Not even Indra, whose chest bears a heavenly garland, could part the smoke-scented garlanded lady from the companion of her shoulders; listen further, you who are ruined: can those who burn with desire, thinking to unite with Rati, part her from Kama?","n":"The pair is Kama and Rati: to want this bride is to want Rati away from Kama. The comparison makes the kings' lust its own refutation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","karpu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 754","lb":"CC754","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:755":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":755,"t":["எம்மைநீர் வெல்லப் பெற்றீர் வென்றபி னிருந்த வேந்த","னும்மையும் வேறு செய்து நும்முளே பொருது வீந்தால்","வெம்மைசெய் துலக மெல்லா மாண்டிட விளைக்கும் நீதி","யம்மமற் றதனை யோரீ ரவன்கருத் தன்ன தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Even if they defeat us, Padumukan says, the king who stays behind after their victory will divide them, and when they have fought among themselves and perished he will rule a world made cruel; that is the policy he is breeding, and that is his mind, which they do not see.","n":"இருந்த வேந்தன், the king who sat by, is Kattiyankaran, who urged the fight but did not join it. Padumukan answers his incitement (742 to 745) with its motive: a usurper's policy of letting rival kings destroy one another. நீதி here names a ruler's craft, not justice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam","utpagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 755","lb":"CC755","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:756":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":756,"t":["சொற்றிற லன்றி மன்னீர் தொக்குநீர் காண்மி னெங்கள்","விற்றிற லென்று வில்வாய் வெங்கணை தொடுத்து வாங்கிக்","கற்றிரள் கழிந்து மண்ணுட் கரந்தது குளிப்ப வெய்திட்","டிற்றெமர் கல்வி யென்றா னிடியுரு மேற்றொ டொப்பான்."],"p":"","r":"Not mere strength of words, kings: gather and see the strength of our bows, he says, and fitting a fierce arrow to the bow and drawing it, he shoots it through a mass of stone so that it hides itself in the earth, saying 'such is our people's learning', like a thunderbolt.","n":"The answer to the taunt of cowardice is a display, not a blow: the arrow goes into the earth. கல்வி here is skill at arms, so the chapter's 'learning' runs from the vina to the bow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","kalvi","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 756","lb":"CC756","ev":"Padumukan shoots an arrow through a rock into the ground to show the kings his side's skill at arms.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:33"},"cc:757":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":757,"t":["ஆழியங் கழனி தன்னு ளம்பொடு கணையம் வித்திச்","சூழ்குடர் பிணங்கண் மல்க விளைத்தபின றொழுதிப் பல்பேய்க்","கூழ்படு குருதி நெய்யி னிறைச்சிச்சோ றூட்டி வென்றி","வீழ்தர வேட்டு நின்றா ரெய்துப வெகுளல் வேண்டா."],"p":"","r":"The speaker pictures the battle as a farm: those who sow arrows and darts in the field, raise a crop of corpses and coiled entrails, and feed the many ghosts rice of flesh in blood for ghee will win the victory they want, so there is no call for angry words.","n":"The verse has no speech tag; it reads as the close of Padumukan's challenge to the suitor kings (747-756), but the speaker is inferred from the run of speech, not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 757","lb":"CC757","ev":"","cl":[["victory belongs to those who win it in battle, not to those who rage","general","reported",4,"padumukan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்",""],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:758":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":758,"t":["போர்ப்பறை முழங்கி யெங்கும் பொருவளி புடைக்கப் பட்ட","கார்க்கடல் போன்று சேனை கலக்கமோ டூரறி யார்ப்பத்","தார்ப்பொலி மார்ப னோர்த்துத் தன்கையில் வீணை நீக்கி","வார்ப்பொலி முலையி னாட்கு வாய்திறந் திதனைச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"As war drums sound everywhere and the army roars like a storm-tossed sea, Civakan takes it in, lays the vina down from his hand and speaks to the princess.","n":"The vina that won her is put down in the same line in which he turns to her: the contest of skill gives way to the contest of arms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 758","lb":"CC758","ev":"Hearing the armies gather, Civakan sets down his vina and turns to Gandharvadattai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:759":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":759,"t":["தேய்ந்து நுண்ணிடை நைந்துகச் செப்பினைக்","காய்ந்த வெம்முலை யாய்நின கண்கள்போ","லாய்ந்த வம்பினுக் காரிரை யாகிய","வேந்தர் வேண்டிநின் றார்விம்ம னீயென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He tells her not to sob: the kings who stand there wanting her have already become full food for arrows as keen as her eyes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 759","lb":"CC759","ev":"Civakan comforts Gandharvadattai, telling her the hostile kings are already prey for his arrows.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:760":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":760,"t":["அண்ணல் கூறலு மம்மனை யோவெனாத்","துண்ணெ னெஞ்சின ளாய்த்துடித் தாயிழை","கண்ணி னீர்முலை பாயக் கலங்கினாள்","வண்ண மாக்கவின் சொல்லொடு மாய்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"At his words she cries out in fear, trembles with a startled heart and weeps until her tears run on her breasts, and her bloom fades with the word.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","idumbai","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 760","lb":"CC760","ev":"Gandharvadattai cries out and weeps in terror at the coming battle.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:761":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":761,"t":["மேவி நம்பிக்கு வெம்பகை யாக்கிய","பாவி யேனுயிர் பாழுடல் பற்றுவிட்","டாவி யோநட வாயென் றழுதுதன்","காவி வாட்கண் கலங்க வதுக்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Calling herself a sinner who has made a bitter enemy for her lord, she bids her life leave this ruined body, and weeps with her eyes clouded.","n":"She takes the blame for a war that the kings' desire and Kattiyangaran's scheme (755) have made; the self-accusation is a lover's, not the narrator's judgment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 761","lb":"CC761","ev":"Gandharvadattai blames herself for the war and wishes to die.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:762":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":762,"t":["பாழி நம்படை மேலதிப் பாரெலா","நூழி லாட்டி நுடக்கிக் குடித்திடும்","வாழி நங்கைகண் டாயென்று வாட்கணீர்","தோழி தூத்துகிற் றோகையி னீக்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her companion wipes the tears from her eyes with fine cloth and tells her to see how their army will dance over this whole host in slaughter and drink it up.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 762","lb":"CC762","ev":"Gandharvadattai's companion dries her tears and promises their army's victory.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:763":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":763,"t":["எங்கள் பெண்மையு மீர்மலர்த் தார்மன்னர்","தங்க ளாண்மையுஞ் சால்வது காண்டுமென்","றிங்கு வார்முர லுங்கலை யேந்தல்கு","னங்கை வாட்படை நங்கையைச் குழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Saying they will see whether their womanhood or the garlanded kings' manhood proves the greater, the sword-bearing women of the guard close round the princess.","n":"The women set their பெண்மை against the kings' ஆண்மை as a rival kind of courage, not as its opposite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 763","lb":"CC763","ev":"Armed women surround Gandharvadattai to guard her.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கை வாட்படை"],"ec":[],"spk":["நங்கை வாட்படை",""],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:764":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":764,"t":["கூன்களுங் குறளு மஞ்சிக் குடர்வெந்து கொழும்பொற் பேழை","தான்கொளப் பாய வோடிச் சாந்துக்கோய் புகிய செல்வ","தேன்கொள்பூ மாலை சூடித் தாமமாய்த் திரண்டு நிற்ப","வான்பளிங் குருவத் தூணே மறைபவு மாய வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The hunchbacks and dwarfs, sick with fear, run off into golden caskets and sandal jars, hang themselves among garlands as if they were bunches of flowers, and hide behind crystal pillars.","n":"A comic interlude: the crystal pillars cannot hide anyone, so the joke turns on the hiding place being transparent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 764","lb":"CC764","ev":"The palace's hunchbacks and dwarfs hide in boxes, among garlands and behind pillars.","cl":[],"ag":["கூன்களுங் குறளும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:765":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":765,"t":["இங்கித நிலைமை நோக்கி முறுவலித் தெரிபொன் மார்ப","னங்கையைக் காக்கும் வண்ண நகாநின்று மொழிந்து பேழ்வாய்ச்","சிங்கந்தான் கடிய தாங்கோர் செழுஞ்சிங்க முழுக்கிற் சீறிப்","பொங்கிமேற் செல்வ தேபோற் பொலங்கழ னரலச் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing this, Civakan smiles, laughingly tells them how to guard the lady, and goes out with anklets ringing like a lion that roars and leaps forth in anger.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 765","lb":"CC765","ev":"Civakan laughs at the hiders, gives orders for the princess's guard and goes out to fight.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:766":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":766,"t":["பதுமுக குமரன் மற்றிப் பாவையைக் காவ லோம்பி","மதுமுக மாலை நெற்றி மதகளி றுந்தி நிற்ப","நுதிமுக வாளும் வில்லு நுண்ணிலை வேலு மேந்திச்","சதுமுக மாகச் சேனை நமர்தலைப் பெய்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He charges Padumukan to guard this lady with the musth elephant standing by, and orders that their army, armed with swords, bows and spears, fall on the enemy in a four-sided array.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","thunai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 766","lb":"CC766","ev":"Civakan sets Padumukan to guard Gandharvadattai and orders the army into array.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:767":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":767,"t":["வட்டுடை மருங்குல் சேர்த்தி வாளிரு புடையும் வீக்கித்","தட்டுடைப் பொலிந்த திண்டேர்த் தனஞ்செயன் போல வேறிக்","கட்டளைப் புரவி சூழ்ந்து கால்புடை காப்ப வேவி","யட்டுயிர் பருகுங் கூற்றங் கோளெழுந் தனைய தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Belted, with a sword bound on either side, he mounts a strong chariot like Arjuna, with picked horses round it and men guarding its wheels, and looks like Death rising to seize and drink lives.","n":"Two comparisons in one verse: Arjuna (தனஞ்செயன்) for the chariot-fighter, கூற்றம் for the killer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 767","lb":"CC767","ev":"Civakan arms himself and mounts his chariot.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:768":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":768,"t":["புள்ளிரைப் பன்ன பொற்றார்ப் புரவித்தே ரிரவி போலா","வுள்ளுருத் தெழுந்து பொங்கி யுடல்சினங் கடவ நோக்கி","முள்ளெயி றிலங்க நக்கு முடிக்குழா மன்னர் கேட்பக்","கள்ளவி ழங்கன் மார்பன் கார்மழை முழக்கிற் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Rising in anger on his chariot like the sun, he laughs with his sharp teeth showing and speaks to the crowned kings in a voice like thundering rain-clouds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 768","lb":"CC768","ev":"Civakan, from his chariot, addresses the assembled kings.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:769":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":769,"t":["முருகுலா முல்லை மாலை மூப்பிலா முலையி னார்நும்","மருகுலாம் புலவி நோக்கத் தமிர்தமின் றுகுப்ப கொல்லோ","கருதலாம் படிய தன்றிக் கலதியம் பிவையுங் காய்ந்த","பொருதுலாம் புகழை வேட்டிவ் வெஃகமும் புகைந்த வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He taunts them: will their wives' sulking glances still pour nectar for them after today? These arrows are hot beyond thought, and this spear smokes for the fame that battle brings.","n":"The sense of line 3 (கலதியம்பு) is not clear to me; the taunt rests on the wives' புலவி, a sulk that needs a living husband to answer it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","maram","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 769","lb":"CC769","ev":"Civakan taunts the kings that they will not return to their wives.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:770":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":770,"t":["வாணிக மொன்றுந் தேற்றாய் முதலொடுங் கேடு வந்தா","லூணிகந் தீட்டப் பட்ட வூதிய வொழுக்கி னெஞ்சத்","தேணிகந் திலேசு நோக்கி யிருமுதல் கெடாமை கொள்வார்","சேணிகந் துய்யப் போநின் செறிதொடி யொழிய வென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The kings retort that he knows nothing of trade: when ruin threatens the capital, those who live by profit look to the small loss and keep both capitals safe, so let him run far and save himself, leaving the girl.","n":"The jibe is aimed at his upbringing in a merchant's house (Kantukkatan's), which the kings take as his birth. What the 'two capitals' are (life and goods, or life and the girl) is not fixed by the words; lines 2-3 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kedu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 770","lb":"CC770","ev":"The kings mock Civakan as a merchant's son and tell him to flee and leave the princess.","cl":[["a prudent trader gives up a small thing rather than lose his capital","general","reported",3,"suitor_kings"]],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:771":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":771,"t":["தம்முடைப் பண்டந் தன்னைக் கொடுத்தவ ருடைமை கோட","லெம்முடை யவர்கள் வாழ்க்கை யெமக்குமஃ தொக்கு மன்றே","யம்முடி யரசிர்க் கெல்லா மென்கையி லம்பு தந்து","நும்முடைத் திருவுந் தேசு நோக்குமுன் கொள்வ லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He answers that taking another's goods by giving one's own is the way his people live, and it suits him too: he will hand all the crowned kings arrows from his hand and take their fortune and glory before they know it.","n":"He accepts the merchant identity the kings throw at him and makes the exchange of goods into an exchange of arrows for kingdoms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","selvam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 771","lb":"CC771","ev":"Civakan turns the kings' trade jibe back on them.","cl":[["a merchant's life is taking others' goods in exchange for his own","general","reported",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:772":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":772,"t":["மட்டுலாந் தாரி னாய்நின் வனப்பினோ டிளமை கல்வி","கெட்டுலாய்ச் சிலம்பு செம்பொற் கிண்கிணி மகளிர் கோங்க","மொட்டுலா முலைகள் பாய்ந்த வகலத்துச் சரங்கண் மூழ்கப்","பட்டுலாய்க் கிடக்க லுற்றா யென்சொலாய் பாவி யென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The kings reply that his beauty, youth and learning will be wasted, and he will lie with arrows sunk in the chest that women's breasts have pressed; what has he to say, wretch?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","azhagu","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 772","lb":"CC772","ev":"The kings foretell Civakan's death in battle.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:773":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":773,"t":["எரிசுடர்ப் பருதி முன்ன ரிருளென வுடைந்து நீங்கப்","பொருபடை மன்னர் நுங்கள் புறக்கொடை கண்டு மற்றிம்","முருகுடைக் குழலி னாடன் முகிழ்முலை கலப்ப லன்றே","லிருசுடர் வழங்கும் வையத் தென்பெயர் கெடுக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He vows that unless, after seeing their backs as they break and scatter like darkness before the blazing sun, he embraces this fragrant-haired lady, may his name perish in the world where sun and moon move.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","pugazh","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 773","lb":"CC773","ev":"Civakan swears to rout the kings and win Gandharvadattai or lose his name.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:774":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":774,"t":["ஆண்மர வாணிலத் தப்பு வேல்செய்முட்","காண்வரு காட்டினக் களிற்று நீள்வரை","நீணில வேந்தெனும் வேழப் பேரினம்","பூண்முலைப் பிடிக்கவாய்ப் போர்செய் குற்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the earth's kings as a great herd of elephants, from a forest grown with thorns and spears, going to war out of desire for the she-elephant with the jewelled breasts.","n":"Line 1 (ஆண்மர வாணிலத் தப்பு வேல்செய்முட்) is obscure; the herd image is clear, but how the forest is described is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 774","lb":"CC774","ev":"The kings' armies advance, pictured as a herd of tuskers fighting over a cow-elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:775":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":775,"t":["தாழிருந் தடக்கையு மருப்புந் தம்பியர்","தோழர்தன் றாள்களாச் சொரியு மும்மத","மாழ்கடற் சுற்றமா வழன்று சீவக","வேழுயர் போதக மினத்தொ டேற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Against them comes Civakan the lofty young tusker with his herd: his younger brothers are its long trunk and tusks, his friends its legs, the triple ichor pours, and his kin round him are a deep sea.","n":"Where the kings are a herd, Civakan's side is one animal whose limbs are his kin and friends: the simile makes his force a single body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","sutram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 775","lb":"CC775","ev":"Civakan, his brothers and his friends advance to meet the kings.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தம்பியர்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:776":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":776,"t":["குடையுடை வேந்தெனுங் குழாங்கொ ணாகமுங்","கொடியெனும் பிடியுடைக் குமர வேழமும்","வெடிபடு போர்த்தொழில் காண விஞ்சைய","ரிடியுடை யினமழை நெற்றி யேறினார்."],"p":"","r":"To watch the battle between the herd called the umbrella-bearing kings and the young tusker that has the creeper-like girl for its mate, the Vidyadharas climb onto the brows of the thundering clouds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 776","lb":"CC776","ev":"The Vidyadharas gather on the clouds to watch the battle.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:777":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":777,"t":["கரைபொரு கடலொடு கார்க ணுற்றென","முரைசொடு வரிவளை முழங்கி யார்த்தன","வரைசரு மமர்மலைந் தரணம் வீசினார்","குரைகடற் றானைபோர்க் கோலஞ் செய்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Drums and conches roar like the sea against its shore with clouds upon it; the kings join battle, and the sea-like army arrays itself for war.","n":"அரணம் வீசினார் in line 3 may mean they threw on armour; I take it as arming, without certainty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 777","lb":"CC777","ev":"The armies sound their drums and conches and draw up for battle.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:778":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":778,"t":["தெய்வதம் வணங்குபு செம்பொன் வாயுளிட்","டெய்கணைப் படுமழை சிதறி யெங்கணு","மொய்யமர் மலைந்தனர் முருகு விம்முதார்ச்","செய்கழற் சீவகன் வாழ்க வென்னவே."],"p":"","r":"Worshipping their god and putting red gold in the mouth, they scatter arrows like rain on every side and fight a close battle, crying 'long live Civakan'.","n":"The rite of putting gold in the mouth before battle is not explained in the verse; I read it as a warrior's custom but cannot confirm what it means.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 778","lb":"CC778","ev":"Civakan's warriors worship, then shower arrows and engage the enemy.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:779":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":779,"t":["கலந்தது பெரும்படை கணைபெய்ம் மாரிதூ","யிலங்கின வாட்குழா மிவுளி யேற்றன","விலங்கின தேர்த்தொகை வேழங் காய்ந்தன","சிலம்பின வியமரந் தெழித்த சங்கமே."],"p":"","r":"The great armies close: arrows pour like rain, swords flash in crowds, horses charge, chariots are blocked, elephants rage, and the drums and conches roar.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 779","lb":"CC779","ev":"The two armies clash.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:780":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":780,"t":["சுற்றணி கொடுஞ்சிலை மேகந் தூவிய","முற்றணி பிறையெயிற் றம்பு மூழ்கலி","னற்றுவீழ் குழைமுக மலர்ந்த தாமரை","மற்றவை சொரிவதோர் மாரி யொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Arrows with crescent heads loosed from cloud-like bows sink in, and the fallen faces with their earrings, cut off, look like a rain of opened lotuses.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","padai","kalavazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 780","lb":"CC780","ev":"Heads fall on the field under the arrow-shower.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:34"},"cc:781":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":781,"t":["மறப்படை பசித்தன வயிறின் றார்கெனக்","குறைத்தனர் குஞ்சரம் கூந்தன் மாத்துணித்","திறக்கின ரோடுதேர் மைந்த ரின்னுயிர்","துறக்கம்போய்ப் புகுகெனத் துணிய நூறினார்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan's companions, as if their fierce weapons were hungry and must be fed, cut down elephants, hack horses, smash running chariots and strike so that the warriors' lives may go and enter heaven.","n":"The killing is framed as a gift to the slain: the blow is dealt so that the foe's life may enter துறக்கம், the warrior's heaven, a belief the verse gives to the fighters rather than to a school.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 781","lb":"CC781","ev":"Civakan's companions cut down the elephants, horses, chariots and warriors of the rival kings.","cl":[["a warrior slain in battle goes to heaven","general","reported",4,"civakan_thozhar"]],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:782":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":782,"t":["ஆற்றுவீர் வம்மி னெம்மோ டாண்மைமேம் படீஇய வென்பா","ரேற்றவர் மார்பத் தல்லா லிரும்புமேல் விடாது நிற்பார்","கூற்றம்போற் கொடிய யானைக் கோடுழு தகன்ற மார்பங்","கீற்றுப்பட் டழகி தாகக் கிடக்கெனக் கொடுத்து நிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"They call on all who can to come and prove their manhood against them, loose their iron only at the chest of a man who meets them, and offer their own chests, ploughed by fierce elephants' tusks, to lie scarred and beautiful.","n":"Line 2 states a rule of fair fight: no weapon is thrown except at the chest of one who has accepted the combat (ஏற்றவர்), so a fleeing man is spared.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 782","lb":"CC782","ev":"The companions challenge the enemy and strike only at foes who face them.","cl":[["a chest scarred by an elephant's tusk is a warrior's beauty","general","reported",4,"civakan_thozhar"]],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:783":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":783,"t":["கழித்துவா ளமலை யாடிக் காட்டுவார் கண்கள் செந்தீ","விழித்துமேற் சென்ற வேழம் வேலினால் விலக்கி நிற்பார்","தெழித்துத் தேர்க் கயிறு வாளா லரிந்திட்டுப் புரவி போக்கிப்","பழிப்பில கொணர்ந்து பூட்டு பாகந் யென்று நிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Brandishing their swords, eyes blazing red, they turn back charging elephants with the spear, cut an enemy chariot's ropes with the sword and drive off its horses, and then tell the charioteer to bring faultless ones and yoke them.","n":"The courtesy is the point: having crippled the chariot they do not strike the helpless man in it but bid him re-equip and fight on; பாகந் யென்று in line 4 looks like a misdivided பாக நீ என்று.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 783","lb":"CC783","ev":"The companions disable an enemy chariot and bid its charioteer re-yoke it with fresh horses.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:784":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":784,"t":["ஐங்கதிக் கலினப் பாய்மாச் சிறிதுபோர் களையீ தென்பார்","வெங்கதிர் வேலிற் சுட்டி வேந்தெதிர் கொண்டு நிற்பார்","நங்கைகல் யாணம் நன்றே நமக்கென நக்கு நிற்பார்","சிங்கமும் புலியும் போன்றார் சீவகன் றோழன் மாரே"],"p":"","r":"They tell a foe to rest his tired horse a little from the fight, meet the king with their burning spears, and laugh that the lady's wedding is a fine thing for them; like lions and tigers were Civakan's companions.","n":"The laughter of line 3 makes the wedding a jest: the battle is the companions' share of Civakan's marriage feast.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 784","lb":"CC784","ev":"The companions spare a foe's weary horse, face the enemy king and laugh in battle.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:785":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":785,"t":["ஒருங்கவன் பிறந்த ஞான்றே பிறந்தவ ருதயத் துச்சி","யிரும்பினாற் பின்னி யன்ன யெறுழ்வலி முழவுத் தோளார்","விரும்புவார் வேழ வேற்போர் நூற்றுவர் நூறு கோடிக்","கிருந்தனம் வருக வென்பா ரின்னண மாயி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Born on the very day Civakan was born, with drum-like shoulders as if plaited with iron, lovers of the elephant-and-spear fight, they call out that a hundred of them stand ready for a hundred crore: such were they.","n":"That the companions were born on Civakan's birthday ties their valour to his destiny; the number நூற்றுவர் may be the companions' band or a boast, the verse does not say which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","natpu","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 785","lb":"CC785","ev":"The companions challenge the enemy host, a hundred against a hundred crore.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:786":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":786,"t":["கூட்டுற முறுக்கி விட்ட குயமகன் றிகிரி போல","வாட்டிறற் றேவ தத்தன் கலினமா மாலை வெள்வே","லீட்டம்போழ்ந் தியானை நெற்றி யிருங்குளம் பழுத்தி மன்னர்","சூட்டொடு கண்ணி சூளா மணிசிந்தித் திரியு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Like a potter's wheel spun hard, the bridled horse of Devadattan, mighty with the sword, splits the throng of spears, sinks the elephants' foreheads into pools of blood and wheels about scattering the kings' crests, garlands and crown-jewels.","n":"Line 3's இருங்குளம் பழுத்தி is hard; the sense of the elephants' foreheads sunk in pools is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 786","lb":"CC786","ev":"Devadattan's horse wheels through the enemy, scattering the kings' crowns and jewels.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:787":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":787,"t":["பாய்ந்தது கலின மாவோ பறவையோ வென்ன வுட்கி","வேந்தர்தம் வயிறு வேவ நபுலமா விபுல ரென்பார்","காய்ந்துதம் புரவிக் காமர் குளம்பினால் களிற்றி னோடை","தேய்ந்துகச் சேர்த்தி மாலைத் திருமுடித் திலகங் கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Foes wonder whether a bridled horse leapt or a bird; the kings' bellies burn as they cry 'Nabula! Vipula!', and the two, angered, wear down the elephants' face-plates with their horses' hooves and take the tilaka from the kings' crowned heads.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 787","lb":"CC787","ev":"Nabulan and Vipulan ride down the enemy elephants and strip the kings of their crown ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":["நபுலன்","விபுலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:788":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":788,"t":["காயத்தின் குழம்பு தீற்றிக் காரிரும் பெறிய மேகந்","தோயுமுள் ளிலவின் கூன்காய் சினைதொறு முதிர்வ வேபோன்","மாயங்கொன் மறவர் மாலைப் பைந்தலை யுதிர்ந்த செங்கட்","சேயனான் றிருவின் பேரான் செழுஞ்சிலைப் பகழி யாலே."],"p":"","r":"As the curved pods of the thorny silk-cotton tree, smeared as with red paste, ripen on every branch under the cloud, so the fresh heads of the garlanded warriors fall to the arrows of the red-eyed one who bears the name of Tiru.","n":"திருவின் பேரான் (one who bears Tiru's name) is not resolved in the verse: it could point to a companion whose name holds Sri or the lotus; மாயங்கொல் in line 3 (is it magic?) frames the falling heads as a wonder.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 788","lb":"CC788","ev":"A companion named after Tiru shoots down the enemy warriors' heads.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:789":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":789,"t":["நீனிறப் பௌவ மேய்ந்து சூன்முற்றி நீல மேகம்","வானிற விசும்பி னின்ற மாரியின் மறைவ லாளன்","போனிறப் புத்தி சேனன் பொன்னணி பகழி சிந்தி","வேனிற மன்னர் சேனை கூற்றிற்கு லிருந்து செய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a dark cloud that has drunk the blue sea and stands heavy in the sky, Puttisenan, master of hidden lore, scatters gold-decked arrows and makes the spear-bright army of the kings a feast for Death.","n":"கூற்றிற்கு லிருந்து in line 4 reads as கூற்றிற்கு விருந்து, a feast for Death, the same hospitality figure Civakan turns on the kings in 798.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 789","lb":"CC789","ev":"Puttisenan rains arrows on the kings' army.","cl":[],"ag":["புத்திசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:790":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":790,"t":["வீரவே லுடம்பெலாஞ் சூழ வெம்புலால்","சோருஞ்செங் குருதியுண் மைந்தர் தோன்றுவார்","ஓருமே லொண்மணிச் சூட்டு வைக்கிய","வாரமே யமைந்ததேர்க் குழிசி யாயினார்."],"p":"","r":"Warriors stand with heroic spears all round their bodies and red blood streaming, looking like chariot-wheel hubs set with spokes and capped with a jewelled band.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 790","lb":"CC790","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:791":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":791,"t":["பொன்னனாள் புணர்முலைப் போகம் வேண்டிய","மன்னரோ டிளையவர் மறலி வாளம","ரின்னண மித்தலை மயங்க வத்தலைக்","கொன்னவில் வேலினா னிலைமை கூறுவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet turns: so here the kings who wanted the golden lady's embrace and the young men are locked in confused sword-war; now he will tell of the spear-bearer over there.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 791","lb":"CC791","ev":"The narrator leaves the companions' fight to turn to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:792":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":792,"t":["தம்பியைச் சீவக னோக்கிச் சாமரை","வெம்பரி மான்செவி வீர மந்திர","மிம்பர்நம் மிடர்கெட விரண்டும் வல்லையாய்","நம்பிநீ மொழிகென நயந்து கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan looks at his younger brother and asks him, skilled both in the chowrie-crowned horses' ears and in the heroic mantra, to speak it to end their danger.","n":"Nandattan is his charioteer here; the mantra is a battle craft he has, not a rite of worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 792","lb":"CC792","ev":"Civakan bids Nandattan recite the war mantra into the horses' ears.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:793":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":793,"t":["மந்திரங் கேட்டு நான்கும் வானெட்டிப் புகுவ வேபோ","லந்தரத் திவர்ந்த வாழிக் கானிலம் விட்ட மாலைச்","சுந்தரச் சுண்ண மேனி மகளிர்தங் கண்ணுள் ளிட்ட","மைந்தரு மிரும்பு மொவ்வா வான்புலங் காவல் கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the mantra, the four horses climb as if to leap into the sky, the wheels leave the ground, and the two, held in women's eyes, take guard of a sky-field that neither men nor iron can reach.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are knotted; the reading that the women hold the two heroes in their eyes and that the sky is beyond men and weapons is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 793","lb":"CC793","ev":"Under the mantra the chariot rises into the air.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:794":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":794,"t":["வடிகயி றாய்ந்து முட்கோல் வலக்கையாற் றாங்கி வென்றி","முடிகெனப் புரவி முள்ளா லுறுத்தினான் மொழித றேற்றேன்","கடுகிய வண்ண மாவின் றாரொலி காமர் பொற்றேர்","படையது செவியுங் கண்ணும் பற்றிநின் றிட்ட வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Testing the reins and holding the goad in his right hand, he pricks the horse, saying 'let victory be won'; the poet says he cannot tell it, but the bell-ringing golden chariot seized the ears and eyes of the army.","n":"மொழிதல் தேற்றேன் (I cannot say) is the poet's own aside inside the action.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 794","lb":"CC794","ev":"Nandattan urges the horses on and the army is held by the sight and sound of the chariot.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:795":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":795,"t":["அண்ணறேர் பறவை என்பா ரருவமே யுருவம் என்பார்","மண்ணதே வான தென்பார் மனத்ததே முகத்த தென்பார்","கண்ணதே செவிய தென்பார் கலங்கநூல் கழிய நோக்கிப்","பண்ணிய வீதி பற்றி மண்டலம் பயிற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Men say the lord's chariot is a bird, formless, then with form, on earth, then in the sky, in the mind, then in the face, in the eye, then in the ear, while he, gazing past the rules of the treatise, takes the prescribed track and drives his circles.","n":"அருவம் and உருவம் are the vocabulary of doctrine (the formless and the formed) used for a chariot too swift to place; நூல் கழிய can mean beyond the bounds of the chariot manual.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 795","lb":"CC795","ev":"The enemy cannot follow the chariot as it wheels in circles.","cl":[],"ag":["படை","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:796":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":796,"t":["அகில்கொண்ட கொள்ளி வட்ட மாருயிர் மேயு நேமி","முகில்கொண்ட மின்னுத் தோற்ப முறுகிய விசையிற் றாகி","மிகல்கொண்ட விகலைத் தானே விழுங்கிய சிறகர்த் தோற்றிப்","பகல்கொண்டு பறக்குந் தேராற் காளைதன் பைம்பொற் றேரே."],"p":"","r":"Its wheel a whirled firebrand of aloe-wood that grazes on dear lives, its speed shaming lightning in the clouds, showing wings that swallow all rivalry, the young hero's golden chariot flies like the chariot that carries the day.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 796","lb":"CC796","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:797":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":797,"t":["காலற்ற வயிர மாலை வெண்குடை கவிழ்ந்த பிச்ச","மேலற்ற கவசம் வீழ்ந்த சாமரை யற்ற வின்ஞாண்","மாலுற்ற மன்னர் தங்கண் மனங்கையற் றொழிந்த வள்ளல்","கோலொற்றக் குனிந்த வாறே சிலைகுனிந் தொழிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Diamond garlands with cut strings, white umbrellas overturned, peacock plumes fallen, armour stripped, chowries cut and bowstrings snapped: the deluded kings' hearts fail, and as the generous one's arrow pressed, their bows bent and gave up.","n":"Line 4 is compressed: whether the bow that bends and stops is Civakan's or the kings' is not certain; the overturned white umbrellas are the kings' lost sovereignty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","arasu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 797","lb":"CC797","ev":"Civakan's archery strips the kings of umbrellas, armour and bowstrings and breaks their will.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:798":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":798,"t":["நுங்களை வீணை வென்ற நூபுர வடியி னாடன்","வெங்களித் தடங்கண் கண்டீர் விருந்தெதிர் கொண்மி னென்னா","வங்களி யரசர்க் கெல்லா மோரொன்று மிரண்டு மாகச்","செங்களிப் பகழி யொப்பித் துள்ளவா றூட்டி னானே."],"p":"","r":"'You whom the anklet-footed lady beat at the vina, see her hot wide eyes and welcome your guests!', he says, and offers each king a red arrow or two and feeds them as is fitting.","n":"The taunt is built on hospitality: the kings came as guests to the contest and are now served arrows, உள்ளவாறு ஊட்டினான், fed as their share deserves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","maram","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 798","lb":"CC798","ev":"Civakan taunts the suitor kings and shoots them, one or two arrows each.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:799":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":799,"t":["நன்மன வேந்தர் தங்க ணகைமணி மார்ப நக்கிப்","புன்மன வேந்தர் தங்கள் பொன்னணி கவசங் கீறி","யின்னுயிர் கவர்ந்து தீமை யினிக்கொள்ளு முடம்பி னுலுந்","துன்னன்மி னென்ப வேபோற் சுடுசரம் பரந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The burning arrows only lick the jewelled chests of good-minded kings, but tear the gold armour of mean-minded ones and take their lives, spreading as if to tell them not to go near evil even in the body they will take next.","n":"The arrows judge: the slaughter is sorted by the kings' minds (நன்மனம், புன்மனம்), so the war of desire becomes a moral sentence that reaches into the next birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 799","lb":"CC799","ev":"Civakan's arrows graze the good kings and kill the wicked.","cl":[["one who dies in evil should shun evil in the body of the next birth","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:800":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":800,"t":["மீனெறி தூண்டில் போன்று வெஞ்சிலை நாண்க ளற்ற","தேனெறி குன்ற மொத்த திண்கச்சை துணிந்த வேழ","மானெறி காட்டுந் திண்டேர் கயிறற்று மறிய வேந்த","ரூனெறி யாழி யேந்தி யொய்யென வுலம்பி யார்த்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Bowstrings snapped like fish-lines, the stout girths of hill-like elephants cut through, strong chariots overturned with ropes severed, the kings raise their flesh-smeared discuses and roar.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 800","lb":"CC800","ev":"Their gear cut to pieces, the kings take up their discuses and roar a counter-challenge.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:801":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":801,"t":["ஆர்ப்பெதிர் மாரி பெய்யு மணிநெடுங் குன்றம் போலப்","போர்க்கெதிர்ந் தவரு மார்த்தா ரார்த்தலும் பூண்ட வல்வில்","கார்க்கெதிர் மேகம் போலக் கணைமழை கான்ற திப்பா","லீர்த்தது குருதி வெள்ள மிறைச்சிக்குன் றாக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Like a jewelled hill against which the roaring rain pours, those who faced the fight roared too; at their roar his strung bow poured arrows like a monsoon cloud, and on this side the flood of blood swept along as he heaped a hill of flesh.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 801","lb":"CC801","ev":"Civakan answers the kings' roar with a storm of arrows and a flood of blood.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:802":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":802,"t":["மன்னர்கள் வெகுண்டு விட்ட மறப்படை யழுவ மாரி","கொன்னுனை யெஃகி னீக்கிக் குனிந்துவிற் பகழி கான்ற","மின்னவி ரிலங்கு மொள்வாள் விழித்துயிர் விழுங்க வின்ன","தன்மையாற் றானை நீந்தித் தான்விளை யாடு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Parting with his sharp steel the rain of weapons the angry kings let fly, bending his bow to pour arrows, his shining sword glaring to swallow lives, he swims through the army at play.","n":"விளையாடுகின்றான் (he plays) makes the slaughter a game, the tone the next two verses carry into the ghouls' dance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 802","lb":"CC802","ev":"Civakan beats off the kings' weapons and cuts through their army.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:803":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":803,"t":["வேழவெண் கோட்டு மெல்கோ றின்றுகூன் குருதி வாளா","லாழநா வழித்து நெய்த்தோர் கொப்புளித் தழிந்த மாவின்","சூழ்குடர்க் கண்ணி சூடி நிணத்துகி லுடுத்து வெள்ளென்","பூழ்பெற வணிந்து சூற்பே யாடக்கண் டுவந்து நக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Using an elephant's white tusk as a toothstick, scraping her tongue with a bloody sword, gargling blood, wearing a garland of dead horses' entrails, dressed in fat for cloth and adorned with white bones, the pregnant she-ghoul dances, and he watches and laughs with delight.","n":"The ghoul's acts follow a morning toilette in order (toothstick, tongue-scraping, gargle, garland, cloth, ornaments), so the battlefield becomes her dressing-room.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 803","lb":"CC803","ev":"A pregnant ghoul makes her toilette from the battlefield's remains and dances; Civakan watches and laughs.","cl":[],"ag":["பேய்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:804":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":804,"t":["வெளிற்றுடற் குருதி வெள்ள நிலையிது வென்ப வேபோற்","களிற்றுகிர்ப் பிறழ்பற் பேய்கள் கைகளை யுச்சிக் கூப்பி","யளித்தவை பாடி யாடக் குறுநரி நக்கு வேழம்","விளித்தன கழுகும் பாறும் விலாவிற்றுக் கிடந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As if to say 'this is the state of the blood-flood from the pale bodies', ghouls with teeth like elephants' nails join hands above their heads and sing and dance over what has been given them; little jackals laugh, elephants trumpet, and eagles and vultures lie with their sides split.","n":"அளித்தவை பாடி (singing what was given) casts the ghouls as bards praising a patron's gift; விலாவிற்று in line 4 may mean the birds lie gorged till their sides burst or that the elephants lie with ribs broken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 804","lb":"CC804","ev":"Ghouls sing and dance over the field while jackals, eagles and vultures gorge.","cl":[],"ag":["பேய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:35"},"cc:805":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":805,"t":["கடல்விளை யமுதங் கண்ட பொழுதினெய் கனிந்த தீஞ்சொற்","றடிசிலஞ் சுவைமிக் காங்கு மண்ணலங் குமர னொன்னா","ருடலின்மேற் றிரியுந் திண்டேர் காண்டலு மைந்தர் நெஞ்சத்","திடல்பிளந் திட்ட வெஃகஞ் சுமந்தமர்த் திறத்தின் மிக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"As rice cooked with ghee grows sweeter when nectar from the sea is added, the warriors, seeing the young lord's strong chariot rolling over the bodies of his foes, carry the spears that have split their chests and fight the harder.","n":"The simile makes the sight of the leader's chariot a seasoning: it does not change the fight but sharpens what the warriors already have. Whether மைந்தர் are Civakan's men or the enemy's is not stated; the sense of growing valour points to his own side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 805","lb":"CC805","ev":"Civakan's chariot drives over the enemy dead and his men, though wounded by spears, fight on more fiercely.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:806":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":806,"t":["கடாந்திறந் திட்டு வானிற் களகள முழங்கும் வேழம்","படாந்திறந் தூழித் தீயிற் பதுமுகன் காட்டி யிட்டான்","தடாம்பிறை மருப்புத் திண்கை யபரகாத் திரங்க டம்மாற்","கொடாம்பிற குமரிப் போருட் பிறர்க்கெனக் கொன்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Padumukan drives forward a roaring elephant in musth, like the fire at the end of an age, which with its crescent tusks, strong trunk and hind legs kills in the battle for the maiden as if refusing to give her to others.","n":"குமரிப் போர் names the war for what it is, a fight over a girl; the elephant is made to fight as if it too were a suitor who will not yield her (கொடாம் பிறர்க்கு).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 806","lb":"CC806","ev":"Padumukan sends his war elephant into the battle fought over the princess, and it kills the enemy.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:807":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":807,"t":["மருப்பினால் வேழம் வீழா மன்னரை வாலிற் சீறா","முருக்கித்தேர் தடக்கை தன்னான் முழங்கிப்பாய் மாக்கள் காலி","னெரித்திடாக் கண்ணுட் டீயாற சுட்டுநீ றாக்கி நெய்த்தோ","ரொருக்கிப்பேய் பாடி யாட வுறுசிலை யுடன்று கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Felling kings with its tusks, lashing with its tail, crushing chariots with its trunk, trampling leaping horses under its feet and burning foes to ash with the fire of its eyes, the elephant makes blood flow while demons sing and dance, and the rider angrily takes up his strong bow.","n":"The rider who takes the bow is not named; continuing from 806 he is read here as Padumukan, though the bow-work that follows could belong to Civakan. Line 3 (கண்ணுட் டீயாற சுட்டு) looks corrupt for கண்ணுள் தீயால் சுட்டு.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kolai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 807","lb":"CC807","ev":"The war elephant destroys kings, chariots and horses, and its rider takes up his bow.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:808":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":808,"t":["கொண்டான் பகழி தொடுத்தான் சிலை கால்கு னிந்த","தொண்டேர் மிசையு முருவக்களிற் றுச்சி மேலும்","வண்டார்ப் புரவி நிறத்தும்மற மன்னர் மேலுங்","கண்டான் சொரிந்தான் கணைமாரி கலந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He took his bow, fitted the arrows, the bow bent in a circle, and seeing chariots, elephants' heads, the flanks of bee-swarmed horses and the fierce kings, he poured a rain of arrows that fell among them all.","n":"The verse runs on the verbs கொண்டான், தொடுத்தான், கண்டான், சொரிந்தான், so that the shooting is told at the speed of the act; it picks up கொண்டான் from the end of 807.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 808","lb":"CC808","ev":"The archer looses a shower of arrows on the chariots, elephants, horses and kings.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:809":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":809,"t":["பைம்பொற் புளகப் பருமக்களி யானை யீட்டஞ்","செம்பொ னெடுந்தேர்த் தொகைமாக் கடற்சேனை வெள்ள","நம்பன் சிலைவாய் நடக்குங்கணை மிச்சி லல்லா","லம்பொன் மணிப்பூ ணரசும் மிலையென்று நக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Laughing, he says that the herds of gold-caparisoned elephants, the masses of red-gold chariots and the sea-flood of the army are nothing but the leftovers of the arrows that travel from the lord's bow, and that there are no kings of gold ornament left at all.","n":"நம்பன் is used of Civakan in 811; if the laughing speaker is his friend, he credits the rout to Civakan's bow even while he fights. The speaker is not named, and the verse could also be the narrator's report of the archer's taunt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 809","lb":"CC809","ev":"The archer mocks the enemy, saying their whole army is only what the lord's arrows have left.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்",""],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:810":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":810,"t":["ஒருவனே சிலையு மொன்றே யுடையதோர் களிற்றின் மேலா","னருவரை மார்பிற் சென்ற தறிந்தில னெஃக மின்னும்","பொருவரோ மன்ன ரென்றான் பொருசிலை மடக்கி யிட்டார்","வருகளி யானை மீட்டார் வாட்படை வாங்கிக் கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"'One man alone, one bow alone, on one elephant, and he did not even notice the spear that went into his mountain chest: will kings still fight?' said one, and they unstrung their bows, turned back their elephants and put up their swords.","n":"The one who speaks is unnamed; the plural verbs that follow (மடக்கியிட்டார், மீட்டார்) make it a king among the attackers whose words turn the rest. வாட்படை வாங்கிக் கொண்டார் can mean they drew their swords or withdrew them; the unstrung bows favour withdrawal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 810","lb":"CC810","ev":"Struck by the lone fighter's disregard of a spear wound, the hostile kings give up the fight and turn back.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்",""],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:811":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":811,"t":["செங்கண்மா றெழிக்ப் பட்ட வலம்புரித் துருவங் கொண்ட","சங்குவாய் வைத்து நம்பன் றெழித்தலுந் தறுக ணாளி","பொங்கிய முழக்கின் வேழப் பேரினம் புலம்பி னாற்போற்","றங்குதார் மன்ன ரெல்லாந் தளர்ந்துகண் சாம்பி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"When the lord set to his mouth a conch shaped like the right-whorled conch that red-eyed Mal sounds, and blew it, all the garlanded kings faltered and their eyes dimmed, like a great herd of elephants wailing at the roar of a fierce lion.","n":"The conch is likened to Vishnu's, so the hero's blast borrows the god's; the verse wins the battle by sound rather than by a blow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 811","lb":"CC811","ev":"Civakan blows his conch and the enemy kings lose heart.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:812":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":812,"t":["அருவரை நாகஞ் சுற்றி யாழியான் கடைய வன்று","கருவரை குடையப் பட்ட கடலெனக் கலங்கி வேந்தர்","திருவரை மார்பன் றிண்டேர் மஞ்ஞையே முருகன் தானென்","றொருவரோ டொருவர் கூடா வண்ணமே யுடைய லுற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Shaken like the sea when the one of the wheel churned it with the serpent wound round the mountain, the kings cried that the broad-chested one's chariot was the peacock and he was Murugan himself, and broke so that no two of them stayed together.","n":"Two gods are borrowed in one verse: Vishnu churning the sea for the kings' turmoil, Murugan for the hero they think they see. The kings' cry is a mistake born of fear, not the poet's claim that Civakan is divine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 812","lb":"CC812","ev":"Taking Civakan for Murugan on his peacock, the hostile kings scatter in panic.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:813":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":813,"t":["முளிமரக் காடு மேய்ந்த முழங்கழல் போன்று மைந்தன்","றெளிநலக் குமரர் கூற்றிற் றெழித்தனர் பகழி சிந்தி","யொளிநல வுப்புக் குன்ற மூர்புனற் குடைந்த தேபோற்","களிநல மன்னர் தங்கள் கடற்படை யுடைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Like a roaring fire that grazes through a forest of dry trees, the prince's companions of clear worth roared like Death and scattered arrows, and the sea-like army of the drunk-with-pride kings broke as a shining hill of salt crumbles in a flood.","n":"The salt hill dissolving in water is an image of a mass that has no cohesion of its own: the kings' sea of soldiers is large but melts at the first flood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kootru","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 813","lb":"CC813","ev":"Civakan's companions shower arrows and the kings' great army breaks up.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:814":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":814,"t":["உறுபடை மன்னர் தம்மை யுடற்றியொன் றானு மின்றிச்","சிறுபடை யவர்கள் வென்று செகுப்பவோ வென்ன வேண்டா","செறியெயிற் றாளி வேழப் பேரினஞ் செகுத்த தன்றே","வுறுபுலி யொன்று தானே கலையின முடற்றிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Do not ask whether a small band could beat kings with great armies and come away unharmed: a lion with close-set teeth kills a great herd of elephants, and a single tiger attacks a herd of deer.","n":"The narrator turns to the listener (என்ன வேண்டா) to answer a doubt about the story's plausibility; the answer is from nature, and 815 then gives a moral cause as well.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 814","lb":"CC814","ev":"","cl":[["a single strong one can rout a great host, as one lion scatters a herd of elephants","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:815":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":815,"t":["நல்லவை புரியு மாந்தர் நாந்தகம் பிழைத்து வீழா","வல்லவை புரியு மாந்தர்க் கத்திர மொன்றும் வாயா","வெல்வதோ குணத்தின் மிக்கார் வெற்றிலை விடினும் வேலா","மில்லையே வென்றி தீமை யிடங்கொண்ட மனத்தி னார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do good are missed by the sword and do not fall, and for those who do evil no weapon finds its mark; those great in virtue win even if they throw a betel leaf, for it becomes a spear, and there is no victory for those in whose minds evil has made its home.","n":"வெற்றிலை (betel leaf) puns on வெற்று இலை, a bare leaf: the weakest missile becomes a spear in a good man's hand. The verse does not say the virtuous never fight; it says the outcome of their fighting is decided by the state of the mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","manam","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 815","lb":"CC815","ev":"","cl":[["weapons do not fell those who do good, and those who do evil cannot strike home","general","asserted",1,"narrator"],["victory belongs to the virtuous and never to those whose minds harbour evil","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:816":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":816,"t":["குழையுடை முகத்தி னாள்கண் கோணைப்போர் செய்த மன்னர்","மழையிடை மின்னி னொய்தா மறைந்தனர் விஞ்சை வேந்தர்","முழையிடை சிங்க மன்னான் மொய்யம ரேத்தி யார்த்தார்","விழவுடை வீதி மூதூர் விருப்பொடு மலிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The kings who had waged a war of envy over the eyes of the girl with earrings vanished faster than lightning in a cloud; the Vidyadhara kings praised the hard fighting of the one like a lion in its cave and shouted, and the old city of festive streets overflowed with joy.","n":"கோணைப் போர் is a crooked or spiteful war; the poet names the kings' cause as envy over a girl's eyes, not a claim of right.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 816","lb":"CC816","ev":"The hostile kings flee, the Vidyadhara kings cheer Civakan's fight, and the city rejoices.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்","விஞ்சை வேந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:817":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":817,"t":["பார்மிசை யுலக மேத்தும் படுகளங் கண்டு பற்றார்","போர்முகக் களிற்று வெண்கோ டுழதசெஞ் சால்கொண் மார்பின்","சீர்முகத் தோழர் சூழச் சீவகன் திருவின் சாயல்","வார்முக முலையி னாளை மனைவயிற் கொண்டு புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Having seen the battlefield the world praises, Civakan, his chest scored with red furrows ploughed by the white tusks of the enemy war elephants, and surrounded by his companions, took the girl of fortune's grace and bound breasts into his house.","n":"The tusk wounds are drawn as plough furrows (சால்) on his chest, so that the bridegroom enters the house bearing a field of war on his body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","maram","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 817","lb":"CC817","ev":"Civakan, wounded, leads Gandharvadattai into his house with his companions around him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:818":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":818,"t":["நெய்க்கழி வைக்கப் பட்டார் நெய்ப்பத்தல் கிடத்தப் பட்டார்","புக்குழி யெஃக நாடி யிரும்பினாற் போழப் பட்டார்","மைக்கிழிந் தொழுகுங் கண்ணீர் மாநிலத் துகுக்கப் பட்டார்","கைக்கிழி கொடுக்கப் பட்டார் கலம்பல நல்கப் பட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Some were laid in troughs of ghee, some were stretched in ghee tubs, some had the spearheads sought out where they had entered and cut out with iron, some shed on the earth the tears that ran with their eye-black, some were given purses of coin, and some were given many ornaments.","n":"The passive refrain (பட்டார்) lists the wounded only by what is done to them; healing and reward stand in one list, as if both were care owed to the fighters. Line 3 may describe the pain of the surgery or mourning for the dead; the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","eegai","ali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 818","lb":"CC818","ev":"The wounded are treated with ghee and surgery, and rewarded with money and ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:819":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":819,"t":["முதுமரப் பொந்து போல முழுமெயும் புண்க ளுற்றார்க்","கிதுமருந் தென்ன நல்லா ரிழுதுசேர் கவளம் வைத்துப்","பதுமுகன் பாவை மார்பி னெய்க்கிழி பயிலச் சேர்த்தி","நுதிமயிர்த் துகிற்குப் பாயம் புகுகென நூக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"For those whose whole bodies were holed with wounds like the hollows of an old tree, the good women set balls of food with ghee, saying 'this is medicine', and Padumukan laid ghee-soaked cloths close on their chests and urged them off to beds of soft fur.","n":"பதுமுகன் பாவை மார்பின் is hard to construe; பாவை may go with the women rather than with Padumukan, and the verse's single verb (நூக்கினான்) is his.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","ali","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 819","lb":"CC819","ev":"Padumukan and the women tend the wounded with ghee food and dressings and send them to rest.","cl":[],"ag":["நல்லார்","பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:820":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":820,"t":["பார்கெழு பைம்பொன் றன்னாற் பண்ணவ னுருவ மாக்கி","யூர்கெழு விழவு செய்தாங் குறுபொரு ளுவப்ப நல்கித்","தார்கெழு மின்னு வீசித் தனிவடந் திளைக்கு மார்பன்","போர்கெழு களத்துப் பாவம் புலம்பொடு போக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The lord whose chest gleams with garland and single chain made an image of the Holy One in green gold of the earth, held a festival through the town, gave wealth gladly, and with lament put away the sin of the battlefield.","n":"The epic does not treat the victory as sinless because it was defensive: போர்கெழு களத்துப் பாவம் is named outright, and it is removed with புலம்பு, grief, not only with rite. பண்ணவன் is the Jina as the work's own Lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","vazhipaadu","eegai","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 820","lb":"CC820","ev":"Civakan has a golden image of the Jina made, holds a festival and gives gifts to expiate the killing in battle.","cl":[["killing in battle, even a just one, leaves a sin that worship, festival and giving can put away","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:821":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":821,"t":["செய்தவப் பாவ மெல்லாந் தீர்த்திடுந் தீர்த்தன் பாத","மெய்திய சேடங் கூலித் திறைஞ்சுபு தொழுது வாழ்த்தி","மையறு மணியிற் செய்த வலம்புரி யதனீர் கொண்டான்","வையக மளிக்க நீண்ட வலம்புரித் தடக்கை யானே."],"p":"","r":"He of the long right-whorled-marked hand stretched out to protect the world took the remnant from the feet of the Tirthan who removes all sins committed, bowed, worshipped and praised, and took the water of the right-whorled conch made of flawless gem.","n":"தீர்த்திடும் தீர்த்தன் puns on the Tirthan as the one who removes (தீர்). The claim of sin removed at the Jina's feet sits beside the work's teaching elsewhere (375) that deeds wear away on the path of knowledge. The word read as கூலித் in line 2 is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vinai","thiruvadi","theerththam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 821","lb":"CC821","ev":"Civakan worships at the Tirthankara's feet, receives the offering's remnant and takes the water from the jewelled conch.","cl":[["the feet of the Tirthankara remove all sins one has done","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:822":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":822,"t":["கருமணி யழுத்திய காமர் செங்கதிர்த்","திருமணிச் செப்பெனச் செறிந்த வெம்முலை","யருமணி யலம்வரு மம்பொற் கொம்பனாள்","பெருமணக் கிழமையாம் பேசு கின்றதே"],"p":"","r":"What is now told is the great wedding of the girl like a golden branch, round whose breasts, like a jewel casket set with dark gems and red rays, fine gems swing.","n":"A short verse in a new metre opens the wedding section; the narrator announces the subject (பேசுகின்றதே) rather than telling an act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 822","lb":"CC822","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:823":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":823,"t":["நான்குநூ றாயிரங் குடத்து நல்லன","வான்றயிர் பானெயோ டழகி தாநிறைத்","தூன்றிகழ் வேலினான் வேள்விக் கூர்மருள்","கோன்றொறுக் காவலன் கொண்டு முன்னினான்."],"p":"","r":"The guardian of the king's herds, lord of the shining spear, brought four hundred thousand pots filled fair with fine curd, milk and ghee, so that the town was amazed at the wedding feast.","n":"வேள்வி here is the wedding as a feast, not a Vedic sacrifice. The herd chief is not named; கோன் தொறுக் காவலன் fits Nandagon, keeper of the king's herds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 823","lb":"CC823","ev":"Nandagon brings four hundred thousand pots of curd, milk and ghee for Civakan's wedding.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தகோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:824":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":824,"t":["வளைநிற வார்செநெ லரிசிப் பண்டியோ","டளவறு சருக்கரைப் பண்டி யார்ந்தன","பிளவியல் பயறுபெய் பண்டி யுப்புநீர்","விளைவமை பண்டியின் வெறுத்த தாங்கொர்பால"],"p":"","r":"On one side carts of white long-grained paddy rice, carts of countless sugar, carts of split pulses and carts of salt from sea-water stood crowded.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 824","lb":"CC824","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:825":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":825,"t":["சினைத்துணர் முழவன பலவின் றீங்கனி","கனைத்துவண் டுழல்வன வாழை மாங்கனி","யெனைத்துள கிழங்குகாய் குருகொ டேந்திய","சனத்தினாற் றகைத்திடம் பெறாது தானொர்பால்."],"p":"","r":"On another side, jackfruits heavy as drums on their branches, bananas and mangoes where the bees hum, tubers and vegetables of every kind carried by crowds left no room to stand.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 825","lb":"CC825","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:826":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":826,"t":["மரகத மணிப் பசுங் காய்கொள் வான்குலை","கவர்பழுக் காய்க்குலை கனியக் காவுறீஇ","யிவர்தரும் மெல்லிலைக் காவு மேந்திய","வுவரியாய்ச் சொரிந்திடம் பெறாது தானொர்பால்."],"p":"","r":"On another side, bunches of tender areca green as emerald, ripe areca hung to ripen, and bundles of tender betel leaf were poured out like a sea with no room left.","n":"The refrain ஒர்பால் (on one side) in 824 to 827 builds the wedding stores as a panorama, one heap per verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 826","lb":"CC826","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:827":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":827,"t":["சண்பகந் தமநகந் தமால மல்லிகை","தண்கழு நீரொடு குவளை தாமரை","வண்டின மிசைகொள வாசப் பூச்சுமை","கொண்டவர் குழாம்பொலி வுற்ற தாங்கொர்பால்."],"p":"","r":"On another side, crowds carrying loads of fragrant flowers, champak, tamanakam, tamala, jasmine, cool kazhuneer, kuvalai and lotus, with bees humming over them, made a splendid show.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 827","lb":"CC827","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:36"},"cc:828":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":828,"t":["ஆர்கெழு குறடுசூட் டாழி போன்றவன்","சீர்கெழு வளமனை திளைத்து மாசனங்","கார்கெழு கடலெனக் கலந்த வல்லதூஉம்","பார்கெழு பழுமரப் பறவை யொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says the great crowd that mingled in the hero's rich house, like a dark sea, ringed it as spokes ring a hub and swarmed it like birds on a tree heavy with fruit.","n":"அவன் has no named referent in the verse; the house is the hero's household of the wedding. The two similes do different work: the wheel puts the house at the centre of the town, the fruit tree makes the crowd its dependants.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 828","lb":"CC828","ev":"Great crowds throng the house for the wedding.","cl":[],"ag":["மாசனம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:829":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":829,"t":["கையுறை யெழுதினர் கைந்நொந் தேடறுத்","தையென விருப்பமற் றன்ன தாதலான்","வையக மருங்கினின் வாழ்நர் மற்றிவன்","செய்தவ நமக்கிசை கென்னச் சென்றதே."],"p":"","r":"So many gifts came that those listing them wore out their hands and ran out of palm leaf, and the people of the land went away saying 'may the penance this man did come to us too'.","n":"Line 2 (ஐயென விருப்பமற்று அன்னது ஆதலான்) is hard to construe: it may say the giving went on until desire itself was sated, or that the gifts were too many to want more; the reading of the wish in line 4 does not depend on it. The wish echoes the onlookers of 465 and 467, who ask where his mother did penance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 829","lb":"CC829","ev":"The people bring wedding gifts beyond counting and wish for the hero's merit.","cl":[["a man's present fortune is the fruit of penance he has done","general","reported",4,"gift_bringers"]],"ag":["வாழ்நர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:830":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":830,"t":["வாலரி கழுவிய வண்ணச் செம்புனல்","காலிய லிவுளியும் களிறு மாழ்ந்தவண்","கோலநீர்க் குவளையும் மரையும் பூத்துவண்","டாலிவண் குருகுபாய் தடங்க ளானவே."],"p":"","r":"The red water in which the white rice was washed for the feast flowed into pools where horses and elephants sank, blue lilies and lotuses bloomed, bees hummed and herons dived.","n":"Hyperbole of the feast's scale: the waste water of the kitchen becomes a landscape of tanks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 830","lb":"CC830","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:831":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":831,"t":["உடுப்பன துகில்களு முரைக்கு நானமுந்","தொடுத்தன மாலையுங் குழையுஞ் சாந்தமுங்","கொடுப்பவர் கொள்பவர் வீழ்த்த பல்கல","மடுத்துவிண் பூத்ததோ ரழகின் மிக்கதே."],"p":"","r":"Clothes, musk for rubbing, strung garlands, earrings and sandal paste dropped by those who gave and those who took lay heaped with jewels, lovelier than a sky in bloom with stars.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 831","lb":"CC831","ev":"Gifts change hands so freely that what falls lies heaped on the ground.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:832":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":832,"t":["ரிலங்குபொற் கிண்கிணி யிரங்கு மோசையு","முலம்புமா லுவர்க்கட லொலியின் மிக்கவே.","கலங்கழு மரவமுங் கருனை யாக்குவார்","சிலம்பொலி யரவமு மிச்சில் சீப்பவ"],"p":"","r":"The clatter of vessels being washed, the anklet sounds of the women cooking fried dishes, and the tinkle of the gold bells of those sweeping away leftovers rose louder than the roaring salt sea.","n":"The lines are printed out of order: lines 3 and 4 (கலங்கழும் அரவமும் ... மிச்சில் சீப்பவ) begin the sentence, and the stray ர at the head of line 1 is the end of சீப்பவர், so the verse reads 3-4-1-2.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 832","lb":"CC832","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:833":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":833,"t":["மூழிவாய் முல்லை மாலை முருகுலாங் குழலி னாளு","மூழிவாய்த் தீயொ டொக்கு மொளிறுவாட் டடக்கை யானு","மாழிவாய் விரலிற் காம னம்பொடு சிலைகை யேந்தத்","தாழிவாய்க் குவளை வாட்கட் டையலார் பரவச் சார்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The bride with mullai garlands in her fragrant hair and the hero whose bright sword hand is like the fire of the world's end, like Kama holding bow and arrows in his ringed fingers, came to the wedding place while lily-eyed women praised them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 833","lb":"CC833","ev":"Civakan and Gandharvadattai come to the wedding place, praised by the women.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்","தையலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:834":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":834,"t":["இன்னிய முழங்கி யார்ப்ப வீண்டெரி திகழ வேதந்","துன்னினர் பலாசிற் செய்த துடுப்பினெய் சொரிந்து வேட்ப","சொரிந்தனன் வீர னேற்றான் வலம்புரி யதனீர் கொண்டான்","முன்னுபு விளங்கு வெள்ளி முளைத்தெழ முருக னன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"As instruments thundered and the fire blazed, those versed in the Veda poured ghee from palasa ladles in sacrifice; water was poured and the hero, like Murukan, received it from the right-whorled conch as Venus rose bright.","n":"The Jain epic narrates a Vedic fire rite (வேதம், பலாசு ladle) without comment. Who pours the water (சொரிந்தனன்) is not named; the bride's guardian in the city is the likeliest. Line 3 breaks the second-letter rhyme (ன்) of the other three lines and repeats சொரி from line 2, so it may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","vedham","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 834","lb":"CC834","ev":"The wedding fire is lit, ghee is offered, and Civakan receives the bride-giving water from the conch.","cl":[],"ag":["வேதம் துன்னினர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:835":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":835,"t":["இட்டவுத் தரிய மின்னு மெரிமணிப் பருமுத் தார","மட்டவிழ் கோதை வெய்ய வருமுலை தாங்க லாற்றா","நெட்டிருங் கூந்த லாட னேர்வளை முன்கை பற்றிக்","கட்டழல் வலங்கொண் டாய்பொற் கட்டிறா னேறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Taking by the forearm the long-haired bride, whose shining upper cloth, string of great pearls and honeyed garland sat on breasts almost too heavy to bear, he circled the blazing fire to the right and stepped up onto the fine gold seat.","n":"கட்டில் in line 4 may be a couch or the raised seat of the rite; the verse does not say which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 835","lb":"CC835","ev":"Civakan takes Gandharvadattai's hand, walks round the fire with her and mounts the gold seat.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:836":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":836,"t":["மந்திரத் தரசன் காதன் மாதரம் பாவை தன்னைக்","கந்துகன் சிறுவன் வேட்ட கடிவினை நொடியின் மற்றோ","ரந்தர விசும்பிற் றேவர்க் கதிபதி யாய கோமா","னிந்திரன் றனக்கு மாகா தென்பது நடந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator declares that the wedding the merchant's son made in a moment with the Vidyadhara king's beloved daughter was one that even Indra, lord of the gods of the sky, could not have had.","n":"Civakan is still named as Kantukkatan's son: the king's heir is hidden in the merchant's son, and the Vidyadhara princess is given to the supposed merchant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 836","lb":"CC836","ev":"The wedding is completed.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:837":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":837,"t":["அடிமனை பவள மாக வரும்பொனா லலகு சேர்த்தி","முடிமணி யழுத்திச் செய்த மூரிக்காழ் நெற்றி மூழ்கக்","கடிமலர் மாலை நாற்றிக் கம்பல விதானங் கோலி","யிடுபுகை மஞ்சிற் சூழ மணவறை யியற்றி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"They built the bridal chamber with a coral floor, gold-bound beams, gem-set pillars and great crossbeams, hung it with fragrant garlands, spread a woollen canopy and wrapped it in incense smoke like cloud.","n":"The builders are not named (இயற்றினார்); the attendants are assumed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 837","lb":"CC837","ev":"The bridal chamber is built and decked.","cl":[],"ag":["தையலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:838":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":838,"t":["ஐந்துமூன் றடுத்த செல்வத் தமளிமூன் றியற்றிப் பூம்பட்","டெந்திர வெழினி வாங்கி யின்முக வாசச் செப்புஞ்","சந்தனச் சாந்தச் செப்புந் தண்மலர் மாலை பெய்த","விந்திர நீலச் செப்பு மிளையவ ரேந்தி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"They made three couches of manifold richness, drew a silk screen worked by machinery, and the young women held up a box of mouth-perfume, a box of sandal paste and a sapphire box of cool garlands.","n":"ஐந்துமூன்று அடுத்த is literally 'five and three joined'; it may count layers of the couch or simply mean eightfold wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 838","lb":"CC838","ev":"The couches are made and the women bring perfumes and garlands.","cl":[],"ag":["தையலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:839":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":839,"t":["கடைந்துபெய் மணிக்கைச் செம்பொற் காசறு தட்டிற் சூழ்ந்து","மிடைந்துபெய் மணிக்கட் பீலி மின்னுசாந் தாற்றி பொன்னா","ரடைந்துவீ சால வட்ட மரிவைய ரேந்தி யாற்றத்","தடங்கண்கள் குவளை பூப்பத் தையலோ டாடு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The women held gold trays of jewel-handled peacock-feather fans, sandal-dryers and round fans, and fanned the pair, and he sported with the girl as her wide eyes bloomed like lilies.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 839","lb":"CC839","ev":"Attended by fanning women, Civakan begins to sport with his bride.","cl":[],"ag":["தையலார்","சீவகன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:840":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":840,"t":["பஞ்சுசூழ் பரவை யல்குற் பசுங்கதிர்க் கலாபம் வீங்கச்","செந்தளிர்க் கோதை சோரக் கிண்கிணி சிலம்பொ டேங்க","மைந்தருட் காம னன்னான் மகளிருட் டிருவ னாளை","யந்தரத் தமரர் பெற்ற வமிர்தெனப் பருகி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The Kama among men drank the Lakshmi among women as the gods in the sky drank their nectar, while her girdle swelled, her garland slipped and her bells and anklets cried out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","amudham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 840","lb":"CC840","ev":"Civakan and Gandharvadattai make love.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:841":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":841,"t":["இளமுலை மணிக்கண் சேப்ப வெழுதுவிற் புருவ மேறக்","கிளைநரம் பனைய தீஞ்சொற் பவளவாய் திகழத் தேன்சோர்","வளமலர்க் கோதை தன்னை வாய்விடான் குழையப் புல்லி","யளமர லிலாத வின்பக் கடலகத் தழுந்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Her young breasts reddening and her painted brows lifting, her coral mouth of words sweet as the strings shining, he held her close without parting from her mouth till she melted, and sank into a sea of untroubled joy.","n":"கிளைநரம்பு (the lute's strings) ties her speech to the vina by which she was won.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 841","lb":"CC841","ev":"Civakan embraces Gandharvadattai and is lost in joy.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:842":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":842,"t":["இன்னண மொழுகு நாளு ளிளமரக் காவு காண்பான்","பொன்னணி மார்பன் சென்று புகுதலு மொருவன் றோன்றித்","துன்னியோ ரோலை நீட்டித் தொழுதனன் பெயர்ந்து நிற்ப","மன்னிய குருசில் கொண்டு மரபினால் நோக்கு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As days passed so, the gold-adorned hero went to see a grove of young trees; a man appeared, held out a palm-leaf letter, bowed and stood back, and the lord took it and read it in due form.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kizhamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 842","lb":"CC842","ev":"A messenger brings Civakan a letter in the grove, and he reads it.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:843":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":843,"t":["உருமுக்கதிர் வேற்கலுழ னோலையுல கென்னும்","பருமைக்குருப் பளிங்கிற்புகழ்ப் பஞ்சிமுழு தடுத்த","திருமிக்குடைச் செல்வன்றிறற் சாமிநனி காண்க","அருமையற னின்பம்பொரு ளாகென விடுத்தேன்."],"p":"","r":"The letter opens: Kaluzhan of the thunder-bright spear sends this letter to the lord whose fame fills the world, whose parasol is rich with fortune, wishing him to fare well and wishing him dharma, pleasure and wealth.","n":"The greeting names three of the four aims (அறன், இன்பம், பொருள்) and not release. Line 2 (பருமைக் குருப் பளிங்கில் புகழ்) is hard to construe; the reading of the fame image is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 843","lb":"CC843","ev":"Kaluzhavegan's letter greets Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:844":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":844,"t":["தத்தையொடு வீணைமனர் தாம்பொருது தோற்ப","மொய்த்தகலை நம்பிமுகிழ் முலையையிசை வெல்ல","வைத்தகதிர் வேலின்வலி யார்க்குரிய ளென்னச்","சித்தங்கரிந் தாங்குக்கொடி யான்செரு விளைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king writes that when the kings of the vina contended with Dattai and lost, and the master of the arts won the bud-breasted girl by music, the wicked one, his heart burning, declared she belonged to the strongest spear and stirred up war.","n":"கொடியான் (the wicked one) is unnamed; the usurper Kattiyangaran is the likeliest, but the verse does not say so. The challenge 'she belongs to the strongest spear' sets the law of force against the law of the contest the king laid down.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","maram","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 844","lb":"CC844","ev":"The letter recounts the vina contest and the war started by the wicked one.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:845":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":845,"t":["தேன்முழங்கு தார்க்குருசில் செம்பொனெடுந் தேர்மேல்","வான்முழங்கு வெஞ்சிலையின் வாளிமழை தூவி","யூன்முழங்கு வெங்குருதி வேழமுடன் மூழ்க","வேன்முழங்கு தானைவிளை யாடியதுங் கேட்டேன்."],"p":"","r":"He writes that he has also heard how the honey-garlanded lord, on his tall gold chariot, rained arrows from his thundering bow till elephants sank in hot blood, and played with the army of spears.","n":"விளையாடியது: the king calls the battle play, the word the narrator uses for love in 839.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 845","lb":"CC845","ev":"The letter recounts Civakan's victory in the battle.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:846":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":846,"t":["வந்துதரன் கூறியவிவ் வாய்மொழியு மன்றி","முந்துவரன் மொழிந்தபொருண் முற்றும்வகை நாடிப்","பந்துபுடை பாணியெனப் பாயுங்கலி மான்றே","ரெந்தைதிற முன்னமுணர்ந்த தின்னணம் விடுத்தேன்."],"p":"","r":"He writes that beyond this true account Dharan brought, he weighed fully what had been told before, and having known already the prowess of his lord of the leaping-horse chariot, he sent this letter.","n":"முந்து அவன் மொழிந்த பொருள் may be what was foretold before (the astrologer's word that the destined husband lives in Rajamapuram, 539) or Dharan's earlier report; எந்தை here is a courteous title for the son-in-law rather than 'my father'. Both readings are open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kurippu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 846","lb":"CC846","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தரன்","கலுழவேகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:847":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":847,"t":["எள்ளுநர்கள் சாயவென தோளிரண்டு நோக்கி","வெள்ளிமலை முழுதுங்கொடி யெடுத்ததிக லேத்திக்","கள்செய்மலர் மார்பனுறு காப்பிகழ்த லின்றி","யுள்ளுபொரு ளெம்முணர்த்தி யன்றியுள வேண்டா."],"p":"","r":"He writes that, trusting his two arms that bring scorners low and praising the victory that raised his banner over the whole silver mountain, the hero should not slight the protection he offers, but only let him know what he has in mind; nothing else is needed.","n":"Whose protection (காப்பு) is not to be slighted, and whether the boast of the silver mountain is the king's own or praise of the hero, are not plain. The offer looks forward to the war for Civakan's kingdom.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thunai","natpu","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 847","lb":"CC847","ev":"The king offers Civakan his armed support for whatever he intends.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:848":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":848,"t":["ஆம்பொருள்க ளாகுமது யார்க்குமழிக் கொண்ணாப்","போம்பொருள்கள் போகுமவை பொறியின்வகை வண்ணந்","தேம்புனலை நீர்க்கடலுஞ் சென்றுதர லின்றே","வீங்குபுனல் யாறுமழை வேண்டியறி யாதே."],"p":"","r":"He writes that what is to come will come and none can undo it, and what is to go will go, such being the way of destiny; the sea does not go out to fetch fresh water, and the swelling river never needs to beg the rain.","n":"பொறி (line 2) is destiny as an engraved mark. The images make fate a source of confidence, not resignation: the sea and the river receive without asking. In a letter that has just offered arms, this means Civakan's fortune will come to him, not that he should sit still.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 848","lb":"CC848","ev":"","cl":[["what destiny brings cannot be undone and what it takes cannot be kept","general","asserted",1,"kaluzhavegan"],["what is due to one comes without being sought","general","asserted",3,"kaluzhavegan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கலுழவேகன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:849":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":849,"t":["மன்பெரிய மாமனடி மகிழ்ந்துதிசை வணங்கி","அன்பினக லாதவனை விடுத்தலார்ந்த கோதைக்","கின்பநிலத் தியன்றபொரு ளிவையிவைநுங் கோமான்","றந்தவெனச் சொல்லிநனி சாமிகொடுத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Bowing joyfully toward his great royal father-in-law's feet and sending off the loyal messenger, the lord gave the garlanded bride the things her king had sent, telling her these were made in the land of joy.","n":"இன்ப நிலம் (the land of joy) names the Vidyadhara world as a realm of enjoyment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","eegai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 849","lb":"CC849","ev":"Civakan honours his father-in-law, dismisses the messenger and gives Gandharvadattai her father's gifts.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","ஒருவன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:850":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":850,"t":["குங்குமமுஞ் சந்தனமுங் கூட்டியிடு கொடியா","வெங்கணிள முலையின்மிசை யெழுதிவிளை யாடிக்","கொங்குண்மலர்க் கோதையொடு குரிசில்செலும் வழிநா","ளங்கணகர்ப் பட்டபொரு ளாகியது மொழிவாம்."],"p":"","r":"Painting creepers of saffron and sandal on her young breasts and playing as he went with his garlanded bride, the narrator says, he passed his days, and now the poem will tell what happened in that fair city.","n":"The closing verse of the ilampakam, announcing (மொழிவாம்) the next part of the story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 850","lb":"CC850","ev":"Civakan lives in pleasure with his bride as the poet turns to events in the city.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:37"},"cc:851":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":851,"t":["காசறு துறவின் மிக்க கடவுளர் சிந்தை போல","மாசறு விசும்பின் வெய்யோன் வடதிசை யயண ழன்னி","ஆசற நடக்கு நாளு ளைங்கணைக் கிழவன் வைகிப்","பாசறைப் பரிவு தீர்க்கும் பங்குனிப் பருவஞ் செய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"When the sun, in a stainless sky like the minds of gods who excel in faultless renunciation, has turned to the northern course, the lord of the five arrows arrives and makes the season of Panguni that ends the longing of the war camp.","n":"The section of a new marriage opens by likening the clear spring sky to the minds of renunciant gods: the season of desire is measured by the purity of those who have left it. பாசறைப் பரிவு may mean the war camp's hardship or the longing of those parted by it; the reading takes the second.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaalam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 851","lb":"CC851","ev":"Spring (Panguni) comes, brought by the god of love.","cl":[],"ag":["ஐங்கணைக் கிழவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:852":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":852,"t":["தோடணி மகளிர் போன்ற துணர்மலர்க் கொம்பர் கொம்பி","னாடவர் போல வண்டு மடைந்தன வளியிற் கொல்கி.","யூடிய மகளிர் போல வொசிந்தன வூட றீர்க்குஞ்","சேடரிற் சென்று புல்லிச் சிறுபுறந் தழீஇய தும்பி."],"p":"","r":"Flower-laden branches are like women wearing ear ornaments and the bees reach them like men; swaying in the wind the branches bend like sulking women, and the thumbi, like the go-between who ends a sulk, embraces them from behind.","n":"The whole grove is cast as a scene of lovers' sulking and reconciliation, with a third party, the சேடர், ending the quarrel: the poem stages oodal before the women of the story arrive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 852","lb":"CC852","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:853":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":853,"t":["நான மண்ணிய நன்மண மங்கையர்","மேனி போன்றினி தாய்விரை நாறிய","கானங் காழகி லேகமழ் கண்ணிய","வேனி லாற்கு விருந்தெதிர் கொண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The forest, fragrant like the bodies of brides bathed in perfumed oil and scented with eaglewood, received the spring god as a guest.","n":"Spring is வேனிலான், a person, and the forest plays the bride's household welcoming a guest (விருந்து).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","virundhu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 853","lb":"CC853","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:854":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":854,"t":["கொம்ப ரின்குயில் கூய்க்குடை வாவியுட்","டும்பி வண்டொடு தூவழி யாழ்செய","வெம்பு வேட்கை விரும்பிய வேனில்வந்","தும்பர் நீடுறக் கத்தியல் பொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"With the cuckoo calling from the branches and the thumbi and bees playing the yazh over the bathing pools, the spring that longs with burning desire came and settled in the heights.","n":"Line 4 (தும்பர் நீடுறக் கத்தியல் பொத்ததே) is obscure as divided; the reading of spring taking its place is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 854","lb":"CC854","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:855":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":855,"t":["நாக நாண்மலர் நாறு கடிநக","ரேக வின்பத் திராச புரத்தவர்","மாக நந்து மணங்கமழ் யாற்றயற்","போக மேவினர் பூமரக் காவினே."],"p":"","r":"The people of Rajamapuram, a city fragrant with fresh naga flowers and given wholly to pleasure, went for their enjoyment to the flowering groves beside the river.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","inbam","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 855","lb":"CC855","ev":"The people of Rajamapuram go out to the riverside groves for the spring festival.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:856":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":856,"t":["முழவங் கண்டுயி லாத முதுநகர்","விழவு நீர்விளை யாட்டு விருப்பினாற்","றொழுவிற் றோன்றிய தோமறு கேவலக்","கிழவன் மூதெயில் போற்கிளர் வுற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Wanting the festival's water-play, the old city whose drums never sleep rose up and went out, like the ancient walled precinct of the faultless lord of kevala appearing for worship.","n":"The simile is Jain: கேவலக் கிழவன் is the Jina possessed of kevala, perfect knowledge, and his மூதெயில் the walled assembly where he is worshipped. A pleasure festival is likened to the gathering round the Jina. தொழுவில் is taken as 'for worship'; it could be read otherwise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","punalaadal","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 856","lb":"CC856","ev":"The whole city sets out for the festival of water-play.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:857":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":857,"t":["வள்ள நீரர மங்கைய ரங்கையா","லுள்ளங் கூரத் திமிர்ந்துகுத் திட்டசாந்","தள்ள லாயடி யானை யிழுக்கின","வெள்ள நீர்வளை வெள்ள முரன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The sandal paste that women with water-vessels rubbed on with their palms and scattered turned the ground to slush, the elephants slipped, and the conches in the flood of water roared.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 857","lb":"CC857","ev":"Women scatter sandal paste in the water-play until elephants slip on the ground.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:858":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":858,"t":["நீந்து நித்தில வூர்தி நிழன்மருப்","பேந்து கஞ்சிகை வைய மிளவெயிற்","போந்து காய்பொற் சிவிகைநற் போதகங்","கூந்தன் மாலைக் குமரிப் பிடிக்குழாம்."],"p":"","r":"Pearl-hung litters that seem to swim, curtained carts, gold palanquins glowing in the young sun, fine young elephants and herds of she-elephants with garlanded riders go out.","n":"The line ends in a list with no verb; the poem lets the procession of vehicles stand as a catalogue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 858","lb":"CC858","ev":"The city's conveyances move out toward the grove.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:859":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":859,"t":["ஏறு வாரொலி யேற்றுமி னோவெனக்","கூறு வாரொலி தோடு குலைந்துவீழ்ந்","தாறி னார்ப்பொலி யஞ்சிலம் பின்னொலி","மாறு கொண்டதொர் மாக்கட லொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The cries of those calling 'mount, help them mount', the shouts, the ear ornaments that slip and fall, and the ring of anklets on the road combine like a roaring great sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 859","lb":"CC859","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:860":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":860,"t":["பொன்செய் வேய்த்தலைப் பூமரு மண்டல","மின்செய் வெண்குடை பிச்ச மிடைந்தொளி","யென்செய் கோவென் றிரிந்த திழைநிலா","மன்செய் மாணகர் வட்டம்விட் டிட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The golden-shafted parasols, white umbrellas and peacock-feather fans crowd so that their light wanders as if at a loss, and the great city seems to have left its own circuit.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 (என்செய் கோவென் றிரிந்த திழைநிலா) are hard as divided; the moonlight of jewels 'wondering what to do' is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 860","lb":"CC860","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:861":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":861,"t":["திருந்து சாமரை வீசுவ தெண்கடன்","முரிந்த மொய்திரை போன்ற வகிற்புகை","புரிந்த தாமங்க ளாகவப் பூந்துகள்","விரிந்து வானின் விதானித்த தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The waving chowries are like the broken waves of the clear sea, the eaglewood smoke like twisted garlands, and the spreading pollen like a canopy stretched across the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 861","lb":"CC861","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:862":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":862,"t":["சோலை சூழ்வரைத் தூங்கரு வித்திரண்","மாலை ஊர்திகள் வைய மிவற்றிடைச்","சீலக் கஞ்சிநற் போதகஞ் செல்வன","நீல மேக நிரைத்தன போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Among the litters and carts, which are like waterfalls in the groves round a hill, the curtained elephants go like ranks of dark clouds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 862","lb":"CC862","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:863":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":863,"t":["வழங்கு வங்கக் கலிங்கக் கடகமு","மழுங்கு மாந்தர்க் கணிகலப் பேழையுந்","தழங்கு வெம்மதுத் தண்டுந் தலைத்தலைக்","குழங்கன் மாலையுங் கொண்டு விரைந்தவே"],"p":"","r":"Baskets of Vanga and Kalinga silk, jewel caskets for the men, poles slung with strong liquor, and garlands at every turn are carried in haste.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kal","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 863","lb":"CC863","ev":"The people hurry to the grove carrying silks, jewels, liquor and garlands.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:864":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":864,"t":["வாச வெண்ணெயும் வண்டிமிர் சாந்தமும்","பூச சுண்ணமு முண்ணு மடிசிலுங்","காசில் போகக் கலப்பையுங் கொண்டவண்","மாசின் மாசனம் வாயின் மடுத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Carrying scented butter, sandal paste that bees swarm on, bathing powder, food to eat and baskets of faultless pleasures, the great crowd pours into the gateway of the grove.","n":"The bathing powder (சுண்ணம்) appears here among the stores; it becomes the object of the quarrel that starts the section's story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","inbam","undi","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 864","lb":"CC864","ev":"The crowd enters the grove with its provisions.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:865":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":865,"t":["பாட லோசையும் பண்ணொலி யோசையு","மாட லோசையு மார்ப்பொலி யோசையு","மோடை யானை யுரற்றொலி யோசைய","மூடு போயுயர் வானுல குற்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The sounds of song, of tuned music, of dancing, of shouting and of plated elephants trumpeting rise through and reach the high sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 865","lb":"CC865","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:866":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":866,"t":["பூக்க ணீர்விளை யாடிய பொன்னுல","கோக்க நீள்விசும் பூடறுத் தொய்யென","வீக்க மாநகர் வீழ்ந்தது போன்றவண்","மாக்கண் மாக்கடல் வெள்ள மடுத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"As though the golden heaven where the gods had played in flower-water had split the sky and dropped down whole as a great city, the sea-flood of people poured in there.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","vizhavu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 866","lb":"CC866","ev":"The crowd floods into the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:867":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":867,"t":["மின்னு வாட்டடங் கண்ணியர் வெம்முலைத்","துன்னு வாட்டந் தணித்தலிற் றூநிறத்","தன்ன வாட்டத் தணிமலர்ப் பூம்பொழி","லென்ன வாட்டமு மின்றிச்சென் றெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"Because the grove cooled the heat of the women with flashing sword-like eyes and warm breasts, they reached that grove of fresh flowers with no weariness at all.","n":"The verse plays on வாட்டம் (withering, weariness) four times.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 867","lb":"CC867","ev":"The women arrive at the flowering grove.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:868":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":868,"t":["அள்ளு டைக்குவ ளைக்கய நீடிய","கள்ளு டைக்கழு நீர்ப்புனற் பட்டமும்","புள்ளு டைக்கனி யிற்பொலி சோலையு","முள்ளு டைப்பொலி விற்றொரு பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On one side are pools crowded with lilies, stretches of water full of honeyed red lilies, and groves bright with fruit and birds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 868","lb":"CC868","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:869":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":869,"t":["செம்பு றக்கனி வாழையுந் தேன்சொரி","கொம்பு றப்பழுத் திட்டன கோழரை","வம்பு றக்கனி மாத்தொடு வார்சுளைப்","பைம்பு றப்பல விற்றொரு பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On another side are red-skinned plantains, branches dripping honey, mangoes and jackfruit with long fleshy pods, all ripe.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 869","lb":"CC869","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:870":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":870,"t":["கள்ள வானர முங்கன்னி யூகமுந்","துள்ளு மானொடு வேழத் தொகுதியும்","வெள்ளை யன்னமுந் தோகையும் வேய்ந்தவ","ணுள்ளு மாந்தரை யுள்ளம் புகற்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Thieving monkeys, young black monkeys, leaping deer, herds of elephants, white swans and peacocks crowd the place and draw in the minds of those who look.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 870","lb":"CC870","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:871":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":871,"t":["கோக்க ணங்கொதித் தேந்திய வேலென","நோக்க ணங்கனை யார்நுகர் - வேய்தலிற்","றாக்க ணங்குறை யுந்தடந் தாமரைப்","பூக்க ணம்பொழிற் பட்டது போன்றதே."],"p":"","r":"With the women whose eyes are like spears enjoying the grove, the great lotus pools seem as if a mass of lotuses had fallen into the grove.","n":"The text carries a stray dash in line 2 and the construction is hard; the reading that the women's faces among the trees are like lotuses fallen there is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 871","lb":"CC871","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:872":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":872,"t":["கூறப் பட்டவக் கொய்ம்மலர்க் காவக","மூறித் தேன்றுளித் தொண்மது வார்மண","காரி நாண்மலர் வெண்மணற் றாய்நிழ","றேறித் தெண்கயம் புக்கது போன்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The flowering grove so described, oozing honey and fragrant, with white sand under its shade, seemed as if it had gone down into a clear pond.","n":"The image of the grove 'entering a clear pool' is obscure as divided; the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 872","lb":"CC872","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:873":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":873,"t":["காவிற் கண்டத் திரைவளைத் தாயிடை","மேவி விண்ணவர் மங்கையர் போன்றுதம்","பூவை யுங்கிளி யும்மிழற் றப்புகுந்","தாவி யந்துகி லாரமர்ந் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"In the grove they ringed a space with curtain screens and, entering it like the women of the gods with their mynahs and parrots chattering, sat down in their fine silks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 873","lb":"CC873","ev":"The women screen off a space in the grove and settle there.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:874":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":874,"t":["பௌவ நீர்ப்பவ ளக்கொடி போல்பவண்","மௌவ லங்குழ லாள்சுர மஞ்சரி","கொவ்வை யங்கனி வாய்க்குண மாலையோ","டெவ்வந் தீர்ந்திருந் தாளிது கூறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"There Suramanjari, like a coral creeper of the sea, with jasmine in her hair, sat free of care with Gunamalai of the kovvai-red lips, and said this.","n":"The two sit together எவ்வம் தீர்ந்து, without grief, just before their powders set them against each other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 874","lb":"CC874","ev":"Suramanjari, seated with Gunamalai in the grove, begins to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:875":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":875,"t":["தூமஞ் சூடிய தூத்துகி லேந்தல்குற்","றாமஞ் சூடிய வேற்றடங் கண்ணினா","ணாமஞ் சூடிய நன்னுத னீட்டினாள்","காமஞ் சூடிய கண்ணொளிர் சுண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"She of the silk-clad waist scented with incense smoke, of the large garlanded spear-like eyes and the fair forehead bearing its mark, held out her bright powder that bears desire.","n":"874 ends with இது கூறினாள், but this verse describes, not speaks; the woman is named only by epithets. The reading takes her as Suramanjari, the one who has just begun to speak, but she may be Gunamalai or a maid.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 875","lb":"CC875","ev":"One of the two women holds out her scented powder.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:38"},"cc:876":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":876,"t":["சுண்ண மென்பதோர் பேர்கொடு சோர்குழல்","வண்ண மாலை நுசுப்பு வருத்துவா","னெண்ணி வந்தன கூறிவை யோவென","நண்ணி மாலையை நக்கன ளென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Coming up to Gunamalai, Suramanjari laughs at her and asks whether this, carried under the name of powder, is what she thought would torment the waist of the one it adorns.","n":"The speaker is named only by her loosened hair (சோர்குழல்); the scorn is taken as Suramanjari's because Malai, the one laughed at, is Gunamalai, and lines 2 to 3 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["igal","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 876","lb":"CC876","ev":"Suramanjari mocks Gunamalai's scented powder.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுரமஞ்சரி","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:877":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":877,"t":["பைம்பொ னீளுல கன்றியிப் பார்மிசை","யிம்ப ரென்சுண்ண மேய்ப்ப வுளவெனிற்","செம்பொற் பாவையன் னாய்செப்பு நீயெனக்","கொம்ப னாளுங் கொதித்திது கூறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Seething, Gunamalai answers that if anything on earth, short of the golden heavens, matches her powder, her golden-statue-like rival should say so.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 877","lb":"CC877","ev":"Gunamalai retorts that no powder on earth equals hers.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:878":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":878,"t":["சுண்ணந் தோற்றனந் தீம்புன லாடல","மெண்ணில் கோடிபொன் னீதும்வென் றாற்கென","வண்ண வார்குழ லேழையர் தம்முளே","கண்ணற் றார்கமழ் சுண்ணத்தி னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The women bet that whoever loses at the powder gives up the water-play and pays the winner countless crores of gold, and lose all judgement over the fragrant stuff.","n":"The closing phrase கண்ணற்றார் (they lost sight, lost discernment) is the narrator's verdict on the quarrel before it is judged: the contest is already a loss of measure on both sides.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","maanam","igal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 878","lb":"CC878","ev":"The rival women fix the stakes: the loser forfeits the bathing festival and pays the winner gold.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:879":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":879,"t":["மல்லிகை மாலை மணங்கமழ் வார்குழற்","கொல்லியல் வேனெடுங் கண்ணியர் கூடிச்","சொல்லிசை மேம்படு சுண்ண வுறழ்ச்சியுள்","வெல்வது சூதென வேண்டி விடுத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The spear-eyed women with jasmine-scented hair gather and, holding that the winning of this famous powder contest is a wager, send the powders out to be judged.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 879","lb":"CC879","ev":"The women send the two powders out for a judgement that will settle the wager.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:880":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":880,"t":["இட்டிடை யாரிரு மங்கைய ரேந்துபொற்","றட்டிடை யந்துகில் மூடிய தன்பினர்","நெட்டிடை நீந்துபு சென்றனர் தாமரை","மொட்டன மென்முலை மொய்குழ லாரே."],"p":"","r":"Two slender maids, carrying golden trays covered with fine cloth, cross the long way with the powders.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 880","lb":"CC880","ev":"The two maids carry the covered powders through the city.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:881":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":881,"t":["சீர்தங்கு செம்பொற் கொடிமல்லிகை மாலை சேர்ந்து","வார்தங்கு பைம்பொற் கழன்மைந்தர்கைக் காட்ட மைந்த","ரேர்தங்கு சுண்ண மிவற்றின்நலம் வேண்டின் வெம்போர்க்","கார்தங்கு வண்கைக் கழற்சீவகற் காண்மி னென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The maids show the powders, with jasmine garlands, to the young men of the city, who say that if they want the powders' worth known they must go to Civakan of the warlike, generous hand.","n":"The townsmen defer to one judge rather than vote, which the maid will later turn against him (885) by citing what the whole city praised.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 881","lb":"CC881","ev":"The young men decline to judge and send the maids to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","தோழி","இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:882":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":882,"t":["வாண்மின்னு வண்கை வடிநூற்கடற் கேள்வி மைந்தர்","தாண்மின்னு வீங்கு கழலான்றனைச் சூழ மற்றப்","பூண்மின்னு மார்பன் பொலிந்தாங்கிருந் தான்வி சும்பிற்","கோண்மின்னு மீன்சூழ் குளிர்மாமதித் தோற்ற மொத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan sits ringed by young men whose learning is an ocean of books, like the cool moon among the stars.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kelvi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 882","lb":"CC882","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:883":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":883,"t":["காளை சீவகன் கட்டியங் காரனைத்","தோளை யீர்ந்திட வேதுணி வுற்றநல்","வாளை வவ்வியி கண்ணியர் வார்கழற்","றாளை யேத்துபு தங்குறை செப்பினார்."],"p":"","r":"The maids, whose eyes outdo the good sword with which Civakan has resolved to cut Kattiyangaran's shoulders, praise his feet and put their request.","n":"The enmity with the usurper is slipped into a simile for the maids' eyes: in the middle of a contest of scents the poet reminds the reader that Civakan's sword already has a purpose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 883","lb":"CC883","ev":"The maids bow to Civakan and ask for his judgement.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:884":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":884,"t":["சுண்ண நல்லன சூழ்ந்தறிந் தெங்களுக்","கண்ணல் கூறடி யேங்குறை யென்றலுங்","கண்ணிற் கண்டிவை நல்ல கருங்குழல்","வண்ண மாலையி னீரெனக் கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"Asked to examine the powders and say which are good, Civakan looks and declares that these, from black-haired Vannamalai's side, are the good ones.","n":"வண்ண மாலையி னீர் is taken as 'you of Vannamalai', a name for Gunamalai as in 876; the first two lines are the maids' request and the last two his verdict.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["therithal","naduvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 884","lb":"CC884","ev":"Civakan judges Gunamalai's powder the better at sight.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழி","தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:885":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":885,"t":["மற்றிம் மாநகர் மாந்தர்கள் யாவரு","முற்று நாயியுங் கண்டு முணர்ந்திவை","பொற்ற சுண்ண மெனப்புகழ்ந் தார்நம்பி","கற்ற தும்மவர் தங்களொ டேகொலோ."],"p":"","r":"Suramanjari's maid objects that every person of this great city, the merchant included, has seen these powders and praised them as fine, and asks whether the young lord learned his judgement along with them.","n":"The appeal is to acclaim against judgement; the sarcastic last line also hints that a judge who sides with the crowd has learned nothing of his own. Whose powder 'these' are is inferred from the dispute that follows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["therithal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 885","lb":"CC885","ev":"Suramanjari's maid challenges the verdict by appeal to the city's praise.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:886":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":886,"t":["ஐய னேயறி யும்மென வந்தனம்","பொய்ய தன்றிப் புலமை நுணுக்கிநீ","நொய்திற் றேர்ந்துரை நூற்கட லென்றுதங்","கையி னாற்றொழு தார்கமழ் கோதையார்."],"p":"","r":"With joined hands the women say they came to him because he knows: let him, ocean of learning, refine his scholarship, weigh closely and speak without falsehood.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vaaimai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 886","lb":"CC886","ev":"The maids ask Civakan to judge again, closely and truly.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:887":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":887,"t":["நல்ல சுண்ண மிவையிவற் றிற்சிறி","தல்ல சுண்ண மதற்கென்னை யென்றிரேற்","புல்லு கோடைய பொற்புடைப் பூஞ்சுண்ண","மல்ல சீதஞ்செய் காலத்தி னாயவே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan answers that these powders are good, and if asked why the others fall a little short: they are not the fine flower-powder of the hot season but were made in the cold.","n":"The verdict is grounded in the making, not in the smell at hand: quality is traced to the season, which the maid at once turns against him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 887","lb":"CC887","ev":"Civakan explains the lesser powder was made in the cold season.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:888":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":888,"t":["வாரம் பட்டுழித் தீயவு நல்லவாந்","தீரக் காய்ந்துழி நல்லவுந் தீயவா","மோரும் வையத் தியற்கையன் றோவெனா","வீர வேனெடுங் கண்ணி விளம்பினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The maid with long spear-like eyes replies that when moist the bad becomes good and when wholly dried the good becomes bad, and asks whether that is not the way of the world.","n":"She does not deny his season argument but generalises it until it cuts both ways, so that his reason can no longer settle which powder wins; this is why the dispute moves from argument to a test (889 to 894).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","therithal","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 888","lb":"CC888","ev":"Suramanjari's maid answers that seasons change quality both ways.","cl":[["the worth of things is not fixed but turns good or bad with their condition","general","reported",3,"suramanjari_thozhi"]],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:889":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":889,"t":["உள்ளங் கொள்ள வுணர்த்திய பின்னலால்","வள்ள னீங்கப் பெறாய்வளைத் தேனெனக்","கள்செய் கோதையி னாய்கரி போக்கினாற்","றெள்ளி நெஞ்சிற் றெளிகெனச் செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says she will not be let off until he has convinced her heart, and that if he brings a witness she must be settled in her mind.","n":"வள்ள னீங்கப் பெறாய் வளைத்தேன் is hard to construe; the reading follows கரி (witness) and தெளிகென in lines 3 to 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 889","lb":"CC889","ev":"Civakan offers to prove his verdict by a witness.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:890":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":890,"t":["கண்ணின் மாந்தருங் கண்ணிமை யார்களு","மெண்ணி னின்சொ லிகந்தறி வாரிலை","நண்ணு தீஞ்சொ னவின்றபு ளாதியா","மண்ண னீக்கினஃ தொட்டுவல் யானென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The maid answers that neither men nor gods go beyond his word, and that if, with sweet-voiced birds and the like as witnesses, he removes the doubt, she will accept it.","n":"மண்ண னீக்கின் is obscure; the sense of a condition accepted in advance is clear from ஒட்டுவல் யான் (I agree).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 890","lb":"CC890","ev":"The maid agrees beforehand to abide by a witness.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:891":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":891,"t":["காவில் வாழ்பவர் நால்வ ருளார்கரி","போவர் பொன்னனை யாயெனக் கைதொழு","தேவ லெம்பெரு மான்சொன்ன வாறென்றாள்","கோவை நித்தில மென்முலைக் கொம்பன்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says four dwellers of the grove will go as witnesses, and the maid, joining her hands, answers that it shall be as her lord has said.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 891","lb":"CC891","ev":"Civakan names the grove's four kinds of bee as witnesses and the maid accepts.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:892":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":892,"t":["மங்கை நல்லவர் கண்ணு மனமும்போன்","றெங்கு மோடி யிடறுஞ் சுரும்புகாள்","வண்டு காண்மகிழ் தேனினங் காண்மது","வுண்டு தேக்கிடு மொண்மிஞிற் றீட்டங்காள்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan calls on the bees, the honey-bees and the swarming insects that dart everywhere like the eyes and hearts of fine women and gorge on honey.","n":"The four witnesses of 891 are named by four words for bee (சுரும்பு, வண்டு, தேன், ஞிமிறு), and they are likened to women's restless eyes and hearts, so the jury is itself a figure of desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 892","lb":"CC892","ev":"Civakan summons the four kinds of bees.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரும்பு, வண்டு, தேன், ஞிமிறு"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:893":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":893,"t":["சோலை மஞ்ஞை சுரமைதன் சுண்ணமு","மாலை யென்னு மடமயில் சுண்ணமுஞ்","சால நல்லன தம்முளு மிக்கன","கோல மாகக்கொண் டுண்மி னெனச்சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"He tells them that the powder of Surama, the peahen of the grove, and that of Malai, the young peahen, are both very good, and bids them take the better as their delight and feed.","n":"He praises both before the test, so that the outcome is left to the witnesses and not to his earlier verdict.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","naduvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 893","lb":"CC893","ev":"Civakan sets the two powders before the bees as judges.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரும்பு, வண்டு, தேன், ஞிமிறு"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:894":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":894,"t":["வண்ண வார்சிலை வள்ளல்கொண் டாயிடை","விண்ணிற் றூவியிட் டான்வந்து வீழ்ந்தன","சுண்ண மங்கை சுரமைய மாலைய","வண்ண வண்டொடு தேன்கவர்ந் துண்டவே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the bow throws the powders into the sky; Suramanjari's falls to the ground, and Malai's the bees seize and eat with their honey.","n":"The proof needs no judge's word: the powder that the bees will not touch falls of its own weight, which answers the maid's argument of 888 without arguing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aaraaydhal","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 894","lb":"CC894","ev":"Thrown in the air, Suramanjari's powder falls while the bees take Gunamalai's.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரும்பு, வண்டு, தேன், ஞிமிறு"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:895":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":895,"t":["தத்து நீர்ப்பவ ளத்துறை நித்திலம்","வைத்த போன்முறு வற்றுவர் வாயினீ","ரொத்த தோவென நோக்கிநுந் நங்கைமார்க்","குய்த்து ரைம்மினிவ் வண்ண மெனச்சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"He tells the coral-lipped maids, whose smiles are pearls set in coral, to look at whether this was fair and carry it so to their ladies.","n":"ஒத்ததோ (was it equal, was it fitting) may ask whether the result matched his verdict or simply whether the test was fair.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 895","lb":"CC895","ev":"Civakan sends the maids to report the result to their mistresses.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:896":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":896,"t":["நீல நன்கு தெளித்து நிறங்கொளீஇக்","கோல மாக வெழுதிய போற்குலாய்","ஞாலம் விற்கும் புருவத்து நங்கைகண்","போலும் வேலவ னேபுகழ்ந் தேனென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"A maid answers in praise of the spear-bearer, whose spear is like the eyes of her lady with brows arched as if painted in clear blue.","n":"The verse does not say which maid speaks; praise linked to 'our lady's eyes' fits the winner's maid.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 896","lb":"CC896","ev":"Gunamalai's maid praises Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:897":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":897,"t":["சோலையஞ் சுரும்பிற் சுண்ணந் தேற்றிய தோன்ற றன்னை","வேலையம் படுத்த கண்ணார் தொழுதனர் விரைந்து போகி","மாலைக்கு வென்றி கூற மழையிடிப் புண்டோர் நாக","மாலையத் தழுங்கி யாங்கு மஞ்சரி யவல முற்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The spear-eyed maids bow to the prince who proved the powder by the bees and hurry off; they tell Malai of her victory, and Manjari grieves like a snake struck by thunder in its hole.","n":"The thunder-struck snake is the image the patikam (27) uses for Civakan's dread of kingship before he renounces; here it marks the start of Suramanjari's withdrawal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 897","lb":"CC897","ev":"The maids report the result; Gunamalai hears of her victory and Suramanjari is stricken.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","தோழி","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:898":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":898,"t":["திங்க ளங்கதிர் செற்றுழக் கப்பட்ட","பங்க யப்படு வொத்துளை பாவாய்","நங்கை யென்னொடு ரையாய்நனி யொல்லே","யிங்க ணென்றடி வீழ்ந்திரந் திட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"Falling at her feet, a maid begs Suramanjari, who looks like a lotus crushed by the moon's rays, to speak to her quickly, there and then.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 898","lb":"CC898","ev":"Suramanjari's maid falls at her feet and begs her to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:899":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":899,"t":["மாற்ற மொன்றுரை யாண்மழை வள்ளலென்","னேற்ற சுண்ணத்தை யேற்பில வென்றசொற்","றோற்று வந்தென் சிலம்படி கைதொழ","நோற்ப னோற்றனை நீயென வேகினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Suramanjari answers her not a word, but says that since the generous one declared the powder she offered unacceptable, she will keep a vow until he comes defeated and worships her anklet-feet, and goes.","n":"The vow makes the man who shamed her its condition of release: what looks like a refusal of men is a waiting for one man's surrender. நோற்பன் நோற்றனை நீ is hard to divide; the reading takes நோற்பன் as her vow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nonbu","maanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 899","lb":"CC899","ev":"Suramanjari vows to wait under a vow until Civakan comes humbled to worship her feet.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுரமஞ்சரி",""],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:900":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":900,"t":["கன்னி மாநகர்க் கன்னியர் சூழ்தரக்","கன்னி மாட மடையக் கடிமலர்க்","கன்னி நீலக்கட் கன்னிநற் றாய்க்கவள்","கன்னிக் குற்றது கன்னியர் கூறினார்."],"p":"","r":"Ringed by the maidens of the never-taken city, the maiden with eyes like new blue lilies reaches the maidens' mansion, and the maidens tell her mother what has befallen her.","n":"கன்னி is rung eight times: the unconquered city, the maidens' house, the fresh flower, the girl herself and her companions, so that her chosen seclusion is set inside every sense of the word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 900","lb":"CC900","ev":"Suramanjari withdraws into the maidens' mansion and the girls tell her mother.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","கன்னியர்","நற்றாய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:39"},"cc:901":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":901,"t":["கண்கள் கொண்ட கலப்பின வாயினும்","பெண்கள் கொண்ட விடாபிற செற்றமென்","றொண்க ணாளவ டாயவ டந்தைக்குப்","பண்கொ டேமொழி யாற்பயக் கூறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking that grudges women take up do not leave them, even when bonds begun by the eyes would heal, the bright-eyed girl's mother speaks gently to her father.","n":"The maxim is the mother's own judgment of her daughter (closed by என்று), not the narrator's; it prepares the father to take the vow seriously rather than as a sulk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 901","lb":"CC901","ev":"Suramanjari's mother goes to tell her husband what has happened to their daughter.","cl":[["a grudge a woman has taken up does not let go of her","general","reported",2,"suramanjari_thaai"]],"ag":["தாய்","தந்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தாய்","தந்தை"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:902":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":902,"t":["விண்ணிற் றிங்கள் விலக்குதன் மேயினா","ரெண்ண நும்மக ளெண்ணமற் றியாதெனிற்","கண்ணி னாடவர்க் காணினுங் கேட்பினு","முண்ண லேனினி யென்றுரை யாடினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The mother tells him that their daughter's resolve is like trying to bar the moon from the sky: she will not eat if she so much as sees or hears of men.","n":"The vow is one of fasting tied to the senses (கண்ணின் ... காணினுங் கேட்பினும்): even hearing of a man would break it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 902","lb":"CC902","ev":"The mother reports Suramanjari's vow to refuse food if she sees or hears of men.","cl":[],"ag":["தாய்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தாய்","தந்தை"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:903":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":903,"t":["இன்று நீர்விளை யாட்டினு ளேந்திழை","தொன்று சுண்ணத்திற் றோன்றிய வேறுபா","டின்றே னாவிக்கோர் கூற்ற மெனநையா","நின்று நீலக்கண் ணித்திலஞ் சிந்தினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that today at the water-play, over the difference that showed in the powders, the girl cried that she was undone, that it was death to her life, and stood shedding tears like pearls from her blue eyes.","n":"இன்றேன் is read as 'I am no more'; the line division at 2-3 (வேறுபா / டின்றே னாவிக்கு) makes the exact construction uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","punalaadal","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 903","lb":"CC903","ev":"The mother describes Suramanjari weeping after the powder contest at the water festival.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தாய்","தந்தை"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:904":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":904,"t":["பட்டதென் னங்கைக் கென்னப் பாசிழைப் பசும்பொ னல்குன்","மட்டவிழ் கோதை சுண்ண மாலையோ டிகலித் தோற்றாள்","கட்டவிழ் கண்ணி நம்பி சீவகன் றிறத்திற் காய்ந்தா","ளட்டுந்தே னலங்கன் மார்ப வதுபட்ட தறிமோ வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Asked what has befallen his darling, she answers that the girl strove with Gunamalai over the powder and lost, and burns with anger at Civakan's verdict, and asks whether he knew it.","n":"Line 1 is the father's question (பட்டதென் னங்கைக் கென்ன) folded into the mother's speech; the grievance is aimed at Civakan's judgment, not at Gunamalai alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 904","lb":"CC904","ev":"The father asks what is wrong and the mother tells him of the lost contest and Suramanjari's anger at Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["தந்தை","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தாய்","தந்தை"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:905":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":905,"t":["பள்ளிகொள் களிறு போலப் பரிவுவிட் டுயிர்த்தென் பாவை","யுள்ளிய பொருண்மற் றஃத லோபெரி துவப்பக் கேட்டேன்","வள்ளிதழ்க் கோதை மற்று நகரொடுங் கடியு மேனும்","வெள்ளநீ ணிதியி னின்னே வேண்டிய விளைப்ப லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Sighing like a resting elephant, the father says he gladly hears what his girl has set her mind on, and that even if she would shut men out of the whole city he will bring it about with his flood of wealth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 905","lb":"CC905","ev":"The father agrees to carry out his daughter's wish with his wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["தந்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தந்தை","தாய்"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:906":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":906,"t":["இன்னதோர் காலத் தின்னா னொருமக ளின்ன தொன்றிற்","கின்னதோ ரிடத்தி னெல்லை யாட்கடிந் தொழுகி னாள்போ","லின்னதோர் நகரி லென்றாங் கென்பெயர் நிற்க வேண்டு","மின்னதோ ராரந் தம்மோ வென்றுகொண் டேகி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He wants his name to stand in story as the man whose daughter, at such a time and in such a city, kept men away to such a bound for such a cause, and he takes up a priceless necklace and goes.","n":"The run of இன்னதோர் ('such a') mimics the shape of a told tale: the father wants the vow to become a story with his name in it, so his indulgence is also a bid for fame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 906","lb":"CC906","ev":"The father takes a priceless necklace and sets out, wishing to be remembered for his daughter's vow.","cl":[],"ag":["தந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:907":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":907,"t":["வையக மூன்றும் விற்கு மாமணி யார மேந்திச்","செய்கழன் மன்னற் குய்த்துத் தன்குறை செப்ப லோடு","மையென மன்ன னேவ வாள்வழக் கற்ற தென்ப","கைபுனை பாவை யெல்லாங் கதிர்முலை யாக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He gives the king a jewelled necklace worth the three worlds and states his request; the king orders it, men's coming and going in that place ceases, and he has every carved figure made into a woman with breasts.","n":"வாள்வழக் கற்ற is read as ஆள் வழக்கு அற்றது, the traffic of men ending; the king is the ruler of the city in the story (Kattiyangaran), not named here. A private grief is turned into public law by a gift to the throne.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 907","lb":"CC907","ev":"The father buys a royal order with a necklace; men are barred and even the carved figures are made female.","cl":[],"ag":["தந்தை","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:908":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":908,"t":["சென்று காலங் குறுகினுஞ் சீவகன்","பொன்றுஞ் சாகம் பொருந்திற் பொருந்துக","வன்றி யென்னிறை யாரழிப் பாரெனா","வொன்று சிந்தைய ளாகி யொடுங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Suramanjari resolves with a single mind that even if time runs out, if she is to be joined to Civakan's gold-bright chest let it be so, but otherwise no one shall break her self-containment, and she withdraws.","n":"The vow against all men is not a refusal of love: the one man whose verdict shamed her is the one exception, so the vow is also a claim on him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 908","lb":"CC908","ev":"Suramanjari makes an exception of Civakan in her vow and shuts herself away.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுரமஞ்சரி",""],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:909":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":909,"t":["இன்பக் காரண மாம்விளை யாட்டினுட்","டுன்பக் காரண மாய்த்துறப் பித்திடு","மென்ப தேநினைந் தீர்மலர் மாலை தன்","னன்பி னாலவ லித்தழு திட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking how a game meant to give joy had turned into a cause of sorrow that drove her friend away, Gunamalai wept in anguish out of love.","n":"ஈர்மலர் மாலை is taken as Gunamalai (called மாலை in 904); துறப்பித்திடும் may mean that the game makes one give up a friend or that it makes Suramanjari renounce men, and the subject of தன் அன்பினால் is not explicit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","idumbai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 909","lb":"CC909","ev":"Gunamalai weeps over her friend's estrangement.","cl":[["play undertaken for pleasure can become the cause of grief and separation","general","reported",2,"gunamalai"]],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:910":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":910,"t":["தண்ணந் தீம்புன லாடிய தண்மலர்","வண்ண வார்தளிப் பிண்டியி னானடிக்","கெண்ணி யாயிர மேந்துபொற் றாமரை","வண்ண மாமல ரேற்றி வணங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Having bathed in the cool sweet water, she offers a thousand golden lotuses at the feet of the one under the pindi tree and bows.","n":"பிண்டியினான், the one of the asoka (pindi) tree, is a Jain name for the Jina; the worshipper is taken to be Gunamalai continuing from 909, though the verse names no one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 910","lb":"CC910","ev":"Gunamalai, after bathing, worships the Jina with a thousand golden lotuses.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:911":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":911,"t":["ஆசை மாக்களொ டந்தணர் கொள்கென","மாசை மாக்கடன் மன்னவ னாடலின்","மீசை நீள்விசும் பிற்றலைச் சென்றதோ","ரோசை யாற்சன மொண்ணிதி யுண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"When the king of the wide sea-girt land bathed and proclaimed that the needy and the Brahmins should take, the shout rose to the sky and the people carried off bright treasure.","n":"மாசை (gold) and மீசை (above) are rare; the reading of the king's bath as the occasion of the gift is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","punalaadal","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 911","lb":"CC911","ev":"At the festival bathing the king proclaims a free gift to the needy and Brahmins, and the people take the wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்","மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:912":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":912,"t":["மகர வெல்கொடி மைந்தனை வாட்டிய","சிகரச் செவ்வரைத் தீநிறப் பொன்னெயி","னிகரி னேமிதன் னீணகர்க் காகெனா","நகரம் நாலிரு கோடி நயந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"For the long temple-city of the peerless one who withered the god with the makara banner, walled in gold like fire-red peaks, the city gave eight crores.","n":"The Jina is named only by his victory over Kama (மகர வெல்கொடி மைந்தனை வாட்டிய), a fitting epithet in a chapter about a vow against desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 912","lb":"CC912","ev":"The city gives eight crores of gold to the Jina's temple.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:913":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":913,"t":["உவாமுத லிரவலர்க் குடைமை யுய்த்தவர்","கவான்முதற் கூப்பிய கனக மாழையாற்","றவாவினை யடைகரை தயங்கு சிந்தை நீ","ரவாவெனு முடை கட லடைக்கப் பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"Those who from the new-moon day poured out their wealth to suppliants, gold piled to the lap and offered with joined hands, dammed the sea called craving, whose water is the wavering mind and whose shore is unfailing deed.","n":"The metaphor's parts (தவாவினை, சிந்தை நீர், அவா கடல்) are clear but how they attach is hard to fix: தவாவினை may be the bank that holds the sea or the deed that gives way to it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 913","lb":"CC913","ev":"The townspeople give their wealth to suppliants through the festival.","cl":[["giving away wealth to those who ask holds back the sea of craving","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:914":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":914,"t":["சீரர வச்சிலம் பேந்துமென் சீறடி","யாரர வக்கழ லாடவ ரோடும்","போரர வக்களம் போன்றுபொன் னார்புன","னீரர வம்விளத் தார்நிக ரில்லார்."],"p":"","r":"The peerless women with tinkling anklets on small feet and the men with sounding warrior anklets raise a roar in the golden water like a battlefield.","n":"The water-play is figured as battle (போரரவக் களம்), as in the Paripadal's river festivals.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 914","lb":"CC914","ev":"Men and women of the town plunge into the water-play.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:915":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":915,"t":["கார்விளை யாடிய மின்னனை யார்கதிர்","வார்விளை யாடிய மென்முலை மைந்தர்","தார்விளை யாட்டொடு தங்குபு பொங்கிய","நீர்விளை யாட்டணி நின்றதை யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The water-play stands adorned as women like lightning playing in the clouds, with banded breasts, sport with garlanded young men in the swelling water.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 915","lb":"CC915","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:916":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":916,"t":["விடாக்களி வண்டுண விரிந்த கோதையர்","படாக்களி யிளமுலை பாய விண்டதர்க்","கடாக்களிற் றெறுழ்வலிக் காளை சீவக","னடாக்களி யவர்தொழில் காண வேகினான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, with the strength of a rutting tusker, goes to watch the women in flower-wreaths play, their young breasts pressing as bees drink.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 916","lb":"CC916","ev":"Civakan goes to watch the women's water-play.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:917":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":917,"t":["ஒன்றே யுயிரை யுடையீ ரொருவிப் போமி னிவள்க","ணன்றே கூற்ற மாகி யருளா தாவி போழ்வ","தென்றே கலையுஞ் சிலம்பு மிரங்க வினவண் டர்ப்பப்","பொன்றோய் கொடியி னடந்து புனல்சேர் பவளைக் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"You who have only one life, keep away: look at her walking like a gold-touched creeper to the water, her girdle and anklets crying and bees humming that she has become Death itself, splitting lives without mercy.","n":"No speech tag names the voice of the காண்மின் series (917-925); it is read here as Civakan, who has just gone to watch (916), pointing the scenes out to his companions, but it could be the poet addressing his hearers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kootru","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 917","lb":"CC917","ev":"A young woman walks down to the water.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:918":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":918,"t":["அழல்செய் தடத்துண் மலர்ந்த வலங்கன் மாலை யதனை","நிழல்செய் நீர்கொண் டீர்ப்ப நெடுங்கண் ணினையி னோக்கிக்","குழையும் பூணு நாணுங் கொழுந னுவப்ப வணிகென்","றிழைகொள் புனலுக் கீயு மிளையோ ணிலைமை காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Look at the young woman who, seeing a garland of lotuses from the fiery pool drawn off by the water, gives her earrings, ornaments and string to the stream, telling it to wear them so its husband may rejoice.","n":"She treats the stream as a bride whose husband must be pleased, the river's conventional marriage to the sea; same voice as 917.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 918","lb":"CC918","ev":"A girl gives her jewels to the stream that took her garland.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:919":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":919,"t":["கோல நெடுங்கண் மகளிர் கூந்தல் பரப்பி யிருப்பப்","பீலி மஞ்ஞை நோக்கிப் பேடை மயிலென் றெண்ணி","யாலிச் சென்று புல்லி யன்மை கண்டு நாணிச்","சோலை நோக்கி நடக்குந் தோகை வண்ணங் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Look at the peacock that took a woman's spread hair for a peahen, ran to embrace her, saw its mistake, and walks off ashamed to the grove.","n":"Same voice as 917.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 919","lb":"CC919","ev":"A peacock mistakes a woman's hair for a peahen and slinks away.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:920":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":920,"t":["மின்னொப் புடைய பைம்பூ ணீருள் வீழக் காணா","ளன்னப் பெடையே தொழுதே னன்னை கொடியள் கண்டா","யென்னை யடிமை வேண்டி னாடித் தாவென் றிறைஞ்சிப்","பொன்னங் கொம்பி னின்றாள் பொலிவின் வண்ணங் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Look at the grace of the girl whose bright jewel fell unseen into the water: she begs the swan-hen, saying she worships it, that her mother is harsh, and that it should find the jewel and keep her as its servant.","n":"The inset prayer (அன்னப் பெடையே தொழுதேன்) is a small comic vow: she offers herself as slave for the return of a jewel, set beside Suramanjari's grave vow earlier in the chapter. Same voice as 917.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 920","lb":"CC920","ev":"A girl who has lost her jewel in the water pleads with a swan to find it.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:921":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":921,"t":["தூமங் கமழுங் கோதை தொடுத்த துயரி முலையாத்","தேமென் கீதம் பாலாச் சுரந்து திறத்தி னூட்டிக்","காமக் குழவி வளர்ப்பக் கணவன் புனலு ணீங்கிப்","பூமென் பொழிலுக் கிவர்வான் புகற்சி காண்மி னினிதே."],"p":"","r":"Look at the eagerness of the husband who leaves the water for the flowering grove while his wife, nursing the child of love with sweet songs as milk from her breasts, rears it.","n":"காமக் குழவி, love as an infant fed on song, is the verse's own image; same voice as 917.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 921","lb":"CC921","ev":"A husband leaves the water for the grove, drawn by his wife's songs.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:922":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":922,"t":["கடலம் பவளம் மணையிற் கனபொற் கயிற்றிற் காய்பொன்","மடலங் கமுகி னூசன் மடந்தை யாட நுடங்கி","நடலைந் நடுவின் மகளிர் நூக்கப் பரிந்த காசு","விடலில் விசும்பின் மின்போல் மின்னி வீழ்வகாண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Look how, as a girl swings on a coral seat hung by gold ropes from areca stems and the women in the middle push her, the broken beads of her girdle flash and fall like lightning from the sky.","n":"Same voice as 917; நடலைந் நடுவின் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 922","lb":"CC922","ev":"A girl swings and her girdle beads scatter.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:923":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":923,"t":["நான நீரிற் கலந்து நலங்கொள் பூம்பட் டொளிப்ப","மேனி தோன்ற விளங்கி வெளிப்பட் டதற்கு நாணி","மான மகளிர் போல மணிமே கலைகள் பேசாத்","தானந் தழுவிக் கிடப்பச் செல்வோ டன்மை காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Look at the girl walking on, her fine silk soaked with scented water so that her body shows through, ashamed of being exposed, while her jewelled girdle clings to its place and makes no sound, like proud women keeping silent.","n":"Same voice as 917.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 923","lb":"CC923","ev":"A girl in wet silk walks away in shame.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:924":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":924,"t":["தீம்பாற் பசியி னிருந்த செவ்வாய்ச் சிறுபைங் கிளிதன்","ஓம்பு தாய்நீர் குடைய வொழிக்கும் வண்ண நாடிப்","பாம்பால் என்ன வெருவிப் பைம்பொற் றோடு கழலக்","காம்பேர் தோளி நடுங்கிக் கரைசேர் பவளைக் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Look at the bamboo-shouldered girl who, as the little parrot hungry for milk searched how its mother might be kept from the water, took fright thinking it a snake, lost her gold ear-ring and trembled her way to the bank.","n":"Line 2 (ஓம்பு தாய்நீர் குடைய வொழிக்கும் வண்ண நாடி) is hard to construe; what frightened her is not clear. Same voice as 917.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 924","lb":"CC924","ev":"A girl takes fright at something she thinks a snake and makes for the bank.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:925":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":925,"t":["துணையி றோகை மஞ்ஞை யீயற் கிவரும் வகைபோன்","மணியார் வளைசோர் முன்கை வலனுமிடனும் போக்கி","யிணையி றோழி மார்க ளிறுமா லிடையென் றிரங்க","வணியார் கோதை பூம்பந் தாடு மவளைக் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Look at her playing with the flower-ball, her bangled forearms thrown left and right like a lone peacock leaping at flying ants, while her friends grieve that her waist will break.","n":"Same voice as 917.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 925","lb":"CC925","ev":"A girl plays ball while her friends fear for her waist.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","தோழிமார்கள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:40"},"cc:926":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":926,"t":["திருவின் சாய லொருத்தி சேர்ந்த கோலங் காண்பான்","குருதித் துகிலி னுறையைக் கொழும்பொன் விரலி னீக்கி","அரவ முற்றும் விழுங்கி யுமிழும் பொழுதின் மதிபோன்","றுருவத் தெண்கண் ணாடி காண்மின் தோன்றும் வகையே."],"p":"","r":"See how a woman graceful as Tiru draws the red cloth sheath off a clear mirror with her gold-ringed fingers to look at her adornment, the mirror showing like the moon spat out by the snake that swallowed it.","n":"The verse is one of a run (926-932) of festival sights, each closing with காண்மின் ('see'), addressed to listeners; no character in the story is named as the one showing them. The eclipse image makes uncovering a mirror a small release of the moon from Rahu.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 926","lb":"CC926","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:927":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":927,"t":["பலகை செம்பொன் னாகப் பளிங்கு நாயாப் பரப்பி","யலவ னாடும் வகைபோ லரும்பொன் கவறங் குருளக்","குலவும் பவழ வுழக்கிற் கோதை புரளப் பாடி","யிலவம் போதேர் செவ்வா யிளையோர் பொருவார்க் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"See the young women with lips like silk-cotton buds playing dice on a gold board with crystal pieces, the gold dice rolling like crabs at play and their garlands slipping as they sing over coral cups.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","soodhu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 927","lb":"CC927","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:928":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":928,"t":["தீம்பா லடிசி லமிர்தஞ் செம்பொன் வண்ணப் புழுக்க","லாம்பா லக்கா ரடலை யண்பன் னீரூ றமிர்தம்","தாம்பா லவரை நாடித் தந்தூட் டயர்வார் சொரிய","வோம்பா நறுநெய் வெள்ள மொழுகும் வண்ணங் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"See how the servers seek out guests and press on them sweet milk rice, golden-coloured cooked grain, sugared dishes and rosewater nectar, until fragrant ghee runs like an unchecked flood.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","undi","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 928","lb":"CC928","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:929":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":929,"t":["அள்ளற் சேற்று ளலவ னடைந்தாங் கனைய மெய்யின்","கள்செய் கடலு ளிளமைக் கூம்பின் கடிசெய் மாலைத்","துள்ளு தூமக் கயிற்றிற் பாய்செய் துயரி நிதிய","முள்ளு காற்றா யுழலும் காமக் கலனும் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"See too the ship of desire that wanders in the wind of the mind on a sea of toddy, with youth for its mast, garland and smoke for its ropes and sorrow for its sail, its bodies stuck like a crab in deep mud.","n":"The allegory is dense and some of its parts (the crab in mud, துயரி நிதியம், the 'wind that is in the mind') can be construed more than one way; the reading of the ship as the pleasure-seekers' revel is my best construction. Among the festival sights this is the one that judges: it sets desire as a drifting ship, just before the scene where the Brahmins and the drunkard show the festival's darker side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kal","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 929","lb":"CC929","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:930":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":930,"t":["தாய்தன் கையின் மெல்லத் தண்ணென் குறங்கி னெறிய","வாய்பொன் னமளித் துஞ்சும் மணியார் குழவி போலத்","தோயுந் திரைக ளலைப்பத் தோடார் கமலப் பள்ளி","மேய வகையிற் றுஞ்சும் வெள்ளை அன்னங் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"See the white swans sleeping on beds of petalled lotus as the lapping waves rock them, like a jewelled baby asleep on a gold cot while its mother pats its cool thighs.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 930","lb":"CC930","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:931":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":931,"t":["நீலத் துகிலிற் கிடந்த நிழலார் தழலம் மணிகள்","கோலச் சுடர்விட் டுமிழக் குமரி யன்னங் குறுகிச்","சால நெருங்கிப் பூத்த தடந்தா மரைப்பூ வென்ன","வாலிச் சுடர்கள் கௌவி யழுங்கும் வண்ணங் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"See how young swans, taking the flame-red gems lying shadowed on blue cloth for a thick bloom of lotuses, come close, peck at the bright rays and are dismayed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 931","lb":"CC931","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:932":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":932,"t":["வடிக்கண் மகளிர் வைத்த மரக தந்நன் மணிக","ளொடிக்கச் சுடர்விட் டுமிழ வுழையம் பிணையொன் றணுகிக்","கொடிப்புல் லென்று கறிப்பா னாவிற் குலவி வளைப்பத்","தொடிக்கட் பூவை நோக்கி நகுமா றெளிதோ காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"See how a doe, coming on the fine emeralds the women have set out shining, takes them for creeper-grass and curls its tongue to graze, and how easily the myna laughs to see it.","n":"931 and 932 make the same joke twice: creatures fooled by the city's jewels into taking them for nature.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 932","lb":"CC932","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:933":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":933,"t":["இவையின் னனவும் பிறவு மெரிபொன் னார மார்பன்","கவிஞர் மதியி னகன்று காட்சிக் கினிய விழவின்","சுவையின் மிகுதி யுடைய சோர்வில் பொருளொன் றதுதா","னவையி னகல நோக்கி நயந்த வண்ண மொழிவாம்."],"p":"","r":"Leaving these and other sights to the poets' fancy, the narrator says he will now tell one thing of the festival, of surpassing savour, that the hero with the gold chain looked on closely and with feeling.","n":"The poet turns from the catalogue to the episode with மொழிவாம் ('we shall tell'); the 'thing of surpassing savour' at a festival of pleasures is the dying dog, an ironic choice of word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 933","lb":"CC933","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:934":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":934,"t":["அந்தணர்க் காக்கிய சோற்றுக் குவாலினை","வந்தொரு நாய்கது விற்றது கண்டவ","ருய்ந்தினிப் போதி யெனக்கனக் றோடினர்","சிந்தையி னின்றொளிர் தீயன நீரார்."],"p":"","r":"When a dog came and seized the heap of rice cooked for the Brahmins, those who saw it ran at it shouting that it would not escape now, men whose nature was like the fire that shone in their own minds.","n":"தீயன நீரார் gives men who keep the sacred fire a mind of fire: the Vedic fire becomes their anger. Line 3 (யெனக்கனக் றோடினர்) looks damaged; 'ran in a rage' is the likely sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","vekuli","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 934","lb":"CC934","ev":"A dog snatches the rice cooked for the Brahmins, who chase it in anger.","cl":[],"ag":["நாய்","அந்தணர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:935":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":935,"t":["கல்லொடு வன்றடி கையினர் காற்றினும்","வல்விரைந் தோடி வளைத்தன ராகிக்","கொல்வது மேயினர் கொன்றிடு கூற்றினும்","வல்வினை யார்வலைப் பட்டதை யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Holding stones and hard clubs they ran swifter than the wind, hemmed it in and set about killing it: it had fallen into the net of strong karma, which kills more surely than killing Death.","n":"The narrator gives two causes at once: the men's fury, told in lines 1-3, and the dog's own வல்வினை in line 4. Karma does not excuse the men; the next verses call them brutes (மூர்க்கர்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kolai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 935","lb":"CC935","ev":"The Brahmins surround the dog with stones and clubs to beat it to death.","cl":[["a strong deed of the past snares a creature more surely than Death itself","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["அந்தணர்","நாய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:936":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":936,"t":["வேள்வியி லுண்டி விலக்கிய நீவிர்க","ளாளெனக் கென்ற தறைவது மோரார்","தாளிற மூர்க்க ரதுக்கலிற் றண்டுறை","நீள்கயம் பாய்ந்தது நீந்துத லோடும்,"],"p":"","r":"Not heeding even the cry that the dog was someone's own, the brutes beat it at their feet, and it leapt into the long pond at the cool bathing steps and began to swim.","n":"Lines 1-2 embed a speech (வேள்வியில் உண்டி விலக்கிய நீவிர்கள் ... ஆள் எனக்கு) whose speaker is not named: it may be a bystander's or the owner's protest, addressed to the men 'who keep food from the sacrifice', that the dog belongs to someone. The verse ends in a comma and runs on into 937.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","velvi","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 936","lb":"CC936","ev":"Beaten, the dog leaps into the pond and swims.","cl":[],"ag":["அந்தணர்","நாய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:937":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":937,"t":["மட்குட மல்லன மதியின் வெள்ளிய","கட்குடக் கன்னிய ரிருவ ரோடுடன்","றுட்கென யாவரும் நடுங்கத் தூய்மையி","லுட்குடைக் களிமக னொருவன் றோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"Then, with two maidens carrying toddy-pots of no clay but white as the moon, a drunken man appeared, so fearsome in his uncleanness that everyone trembled.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 937","lb":"CC937","ev":"A drunkard appears with two maidens carrying pots of toddy.","cl":[],"ag":["களிமகன்","கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:938":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":938,"t":["தோன்றிய புண்செய்வே லவற்குத் தூமது","வான்றிகழ் கொடியனார் வெள்ளி வட்டகை","யூன்றிவாய் மடுப்பவோர் முழையுட் டீங்கதிர்","கான்றிடு கதிர்மதி யிரண்டு போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"As the creeper-like women pressed silver cups of pure liquor to the mouth of the man with the wounding spear, they looked like two ray-spitting moons in a cave.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 938","lb":"CC938","ev":"The maidens hold cups of liquor to the drunkard's mouth.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னியர்","களிமகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:939":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":939,"t":["அழலம் பூநற வார்ந்தழ லூர்தரச்","சுழலுங் கண்ணினன் சோர்தரு மாலையன்","கழலன் காழகம் வீக்கிய கச்சையன்","மழலைச் சொற்களின் வைதிவை கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"Drinking flower-toddy that burns like fire until it rises in him, with rolling eyes, a slipping garland, anklets and a dark cloth belt, he spoke these words of abuse in slurred speech.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 939","lb":"CC939","ev":"Drunk, the man turns on the Brahmins with abuse.","cl":[],"ag":["களிமகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:940":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":940,"t":["புடைத்தென் னாயினைப் பொன்றுவித் தீருயிர்","கடுக்கப் பேர்த்தனிர் தம்மின் கலாய்க்குறிற்","றடக்கை மீளிமை தாங்குமி னன்றெனி","னுடைப்பென் கட்குட மென்றுரை யாடினான்."],"p":"","r":"'You beat my dog and killed it, you drove its life out fast; if you want to fight, bear the strength of my big hands, or else I will smash my toddy-pot on you', he said.","n":"The only voice raised for the dog's life is the drunkard's, and he speaks of it as property (என் நாய்). The poem gives the defence of the creature to the man it calls without merit, and the cruelty to the keepers of the Veda.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","kadunjol","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 940","lb":"CC940","ev":"The drunkard claims the dog as his and threatens the Brahmins.","cl":[],"ag":["களிமகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["களிமகன்","அந்தணர்"],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:941":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":941,"t":["நல்வினை யொன்று மிலாதவ னான்மறை","வல்லவர் தம்மை வருத்தலின் வல்லே","செல்சுடர் வேல்வல சீவக சாமிசென்","றல்ல லகற்றி யருந்துயர் தீர்த்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Because that man with no good deed to his name was harrying the men learned in the four Vedas, Civakan, master of the bright spear, went quickly, drove off the trouble and ended their distress.","n":"Civakan first protects the Brahmins, not the dog: the hero keeps the social order before he turns to the creature they have beaten. நல்வினை ஒன்றும் இலாதவன் (line 1) will return in 945 as நற்செய்கை ஒன்றும் இல்லார், said of the dog.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 941","lb":"CC941","ev":"Civakan steps in and rescues the Brahmins from the drunkard.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","களிமகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:942":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":942,"t":["மீண்டவ ரேகுத லும்விடை யன்னவ","னீண்டிய தோழரொ டெய்தின னாகி","மாண்ட வெயிற்றெகி னம்மற மில்லது","காண்டலுங் கட்கினி யான்கலுழ்ந் திட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"As the Brahmins went back, Civakan, bull-like, arriving with his gathered friends, saw the fine-toothed dog lying with no strength left, and wept.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 942","lb":"CC942","ev":"Civakan finds the beaten dog dying and weeps.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:943":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":943,"t":["நாயுடம் பிட்டிவ ணந்திய பேரொளிக்","காய்கதிர் மண்டலம் போன்றொளி கால்வதோர்","சேயுடம் பெய்துவை செல்கதி மந்திர","நீயுடம் பட்டு நினைமதி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He tells the dog that, leaving its dog's body here, it will gain a god's body shining like the burning disc of the sun, if it will consent and think on the mantra of the path it goes to.","n":"The school is assigned from 945-946, where the mantra is the ஐம்பதம் the Lord taught; this verse names it only செல்கதி மந்திரம். The promise is a better birth, not release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","piravi","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 943","lb":"CC943","ev":"Civakan speaks to the dying dog and urges it to fix its mind on the mantra.","cl":[["one who dies thinking on the mantra leaves a low body and gains a god's body","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நாய்"],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:944":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":944,"t":["என்றலுந் தன்செவி யோர்த்திரு கண்களுஞ்","சென்றுகு நீரொடு செம்மலை நோக்கி","யொன்றுபு வால்குழைத் துள்ளுவப் பெய்தலுங்","குன்றனை யான்பதங் கூற வலித்தான்."],"p":"","r":"At this the dog pricked up its ears, looked at the noble one with tears running from both eyes, wagged its tail in joy within, and the mountain-like hero resolved to tell it the padas.","n":"The poem takes care to show the dog's consent (தன்செவி ஓர்த்து, வால்குழைத்து): Civakan asked it to agree (நீ உடம்பட்டு, 943), and the mantra is given only once it does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","mandhiram","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 944","lb":"CC944","ev":"The dog shows it has heard, and Civakan resolves to teach it the padas.","cl":[],"ag":["நாய்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:945":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":945,"t":["நற்செய்கை யொன்று மில்லார் நாளுலக் கின்ற போழ்தின்","முற்செய்த வினையி னீங்கி நல்வினை விளைக்கும் வித்து","மற்செய்து வீங்கு தோளான் மந்திர மைந்து மாதோ","குதற்செய்கை தளிர்ப்பத் தாழ்ந்தாங் கதன்செவிச் செப்பு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The strong-shouldered hero bends low and whispers into its ear the five mantras, the seed that frees those with no good deed from their former karma at the hour their days run out and makes good karma grow.","n":"Line 4 opens குதற்செய்கை, which I cannot construe with certainty; the bending (தாழ்ந்து) and whispering into the ear (செவிச் செப்புகின்றான்) are clear. The verse describes the mantra as a seed of new karma, so it works inside the economy of deeds rather than abolishing it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vinai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 945","lb":"CC945","ev":"Civakan whispers the five mantras into the dying dog's ear.","cl":[["the five mantras, heard at death, free even one with no merit from past karma and sow good karma","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","நாய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:946":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":946,"t":["உறுதிமுன் செய்த தின்றி யொழுகினே னென்று நெஞ்சில்","மறுகனீ பற்றொ டார்வம் விட்டிடு மரண வச்சத்","திறுகனீ யிறைவன் சொன்ன வைம்பத வமிர்தமுண்டாற்","பெறுதிநற் கதியை யென்று பெறுநவை யகற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"'Do not trouble your heart thinking you lived doing nothing good before; let go of attachment and longing; be firm against the fear of death; if you eat the nectar of the five padas the Lord spoke, you will reach a good state', and so he took away its harm.","n":"நற்கதி is a good destination of birth; nothing in the verse speaks of release from birth. The five padas are named only as what இறைவன் spoke, so the Jain identification rests on the work's frame and the five-fold formula.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","pattru","marumai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 946","lb":"CC946","ev":"Civakan counsels the dog to give up attachment and the fear of death and take the five padas.","cl":[["one who lets go of attachment and the fear of death and takes the Lord's five padas reaches a good state after death","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நாய்"],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:947":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":947,"t":["மனத்திடைச் செறும்பு நீக்கி மறவலை யாகி யைந்து","நினைத்திடு நின்க ணின்ற நீனிற வினையி னீங்கி","யெனைப்பக றோறும் விள்ளா வின்பமே பயக்கு மென்றாற்","கனைப்பத வமிர்த நெஞ்சி னயின்றுவிட் டகன்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"'Put anger out of your mind, do not forget, and think on the five; freed of the dark karma lodged in you, it will give unbroken joy for as long as you say it', he said, and the dog swallowed the honeyed nectar of the padas in its heart and left its body.","n":"செறும்பு நீக்கி asks the beaten dog to let go of anger at those who beat it: the dying creature is asked for the forgiveness its killers lacked. The narrator's last line closes the speech; நீனிற வினை colours karma dark, as in Jain teaching of karmic stain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vinai","amudham","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 947","lb":"CC947","ev":"The dog takes the five padas to heart and dies.","cl":[["holding the five in mind without forgetting frees one from dark karma and brings unbroken joy","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","நாய்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நாய்"],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:948":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":948,"t":["பாடு பாணி முகமெனும் பான்மையி","னோடி யாங்கொ ருயர்வரை யுச்சிமேற்","கூடிக் கோலங் குயிற்றிப் படங்களைந்","தாடு கூத்தரி னையெனத் தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"As the stage dancers gather on a high mountain top, make up their costume and, the curtain drawn back, appear dancing in the order of song, beat and opening, so he appeared at once as a god.","n":"The god is not named here; I give him the id used for the god who later rescues Civakan (Sudanjanan, ch. 1-2 marks), on the story's identification, not this verse's. The simile is of the theatre: birth into heaven is instantaneous, like a dancer revealed when the screen is pulled away.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 948","lb":"CC948","ev":"The dog is reborn and appears at once as a god.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:949":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":949,"t":["ஞாயில் சூடிய நன்னெடும் பொன்னகர்க்","கோயில் வட்டமெல் லாங்கொங்கு சூழ்குழல்","வேயி னன்னமென் றோளியர் தோன்றியங்","காயி னார்பரி யாள மடைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Through all the palace circle of the tall, battlemented golden city, women with fragrant hair and shoulders smooth as bamboo appeared, and the retinue gathered around him.","n":"The verse does not name the city; that it is the new god's heaven follows from 948. Line 4 (ஆயினார் பரியாளம் அடைந்ததே) is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marumai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 949","lb":"CC949","ev":"Heavenly women gather as the new god's retinue in his golden city.","cl":[],"ag":["தோளியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:41"},"cc:950":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":950,"t":["மிடைந்த மாமணி மேகலை யேந்தல்குற்","றடங்கொள் வெம்முலைத் தாமரை வாண்முகத்","தடைந்த சாய லரம்பையர் தம்முழை","மடங்க லேறனை யான்மகிழ் வெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The newly made god, lion-like, takes his delight among the celestial women with jewelled girdles, full breasts and lotus faces who have come to attend him.","n":"The lion-like one is read as the reborn dog because 948-949 have just shown him appearing on a mountain top and the women of a golden palace gathering round; a reader coming to 950 alone might take the epithet for Civakan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thevar","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 950","lb":"CC950","ev":"The dog reborn as a god rejoices among the heavenly women who attend him.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:951":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":951,"t":["கற்றவைம் பதங்க ணீராக் கருவினை கழுவப் பட்டு","மற்றவன் றேவ னாகி வானிடு சிலையிற் றோன்றி","யிற்றத னுடம்பு மின்னா விடரொழித் தினிய னாகி","யுற்றத னிலையும் எல்லாம் ஓதியின் உணர்ந்து கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The five padas he learned served as water to wash off his black karma; he became a god, shining like a rainbow in the sky, free of the pain of his broken body, and by avadhi knowledge he understood all that had befallen him.","n":"ஓதி here is the Jain avadhi, the knowledge of past and distant things given with a god's birth, not 'reciting'. The mantra does not end births: it turns the worst one into a better one, and the god still has a body and a station.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vinai","thevar","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 951","lb":"CC951","ev":"The dying dog, having heard the five padas, is reborn a god and knows his former life.","cl":[["hearing and holding the five sacred padas washes away dark karma and wins a god's birth","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"],["a god born in heaven knows his past state by clairvoyant knowledge (avadhi)","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:952":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":952,"t":["இரும்பி னீர்மை கெடுத்தெரி தன்னிறத்","தரும்பொ னாக்கிய வாருயிர்த் தோழனை","விரும்பி விண்ணிறுத் தொய்யெனத் தோன்றினான்","சுரும்புண் கண்ணிச் சுதஞ்சண னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"As fire burns away iron's nature and makes it fine gold of its own colour, so the friend made him; longing for that dear friend, Sudanjanan with his bee-haunted garland swiftly appeared halting in the sky, it is said.","n":"Civakan is the fire and the dog the iron: the image makes the teacher of the mantra, not the god it names, the agent of the change. என்பவே frames the arrival as told tradition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vaadham","nanri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 952","lb":"CC952","ev":"Sudanjanan comes down out of the sky to find Civakan, the friend who transformed him.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:953":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":953,"t":["ஓசனை நறும்புகை கமழ வொண்ணிலா","வீசிய கதிர்பரந் திமைக்கு மேனியன்","மாசறு மணிமுடி மிடைந்த மாலையன்","பூசுறு பருதியின் பொலிந்து தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"He appears with fragrant smoke spreading for a yojana around him, a body that glitters with moon-bright rays, a flawless jewelled crown hung with garlands, shining like the risen sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thevar","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 953","lb":"CC953","ev":"Sudanjanan appears before Civakan in a god's splendour.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:954":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":954,"t":["குன்றெனத் திரண்ட தோளான் குறுகலுங் குமர னோக்கி","நின்றவ னெடுங்க ணொன்று மிமைப்பில நிழலில் யாக்கை","யன்றியுங் கண்ணி வாடா தமரனே யென்று தேறி","நன்றவன் வரவு கேட்பா னம்பி நீயாரை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When the hill-shouldered one draws near, the prince sees that his long eyes do not blink, his body casts no shadow and his garland does not wilt, concludes that he is a god, and asks him, 'Sir, who are you?'","n":"Civakan reads the god by three bodily signs before he speaks, so the recognition is an inference, not a revelation; the question then lets the god tell his own story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","thevar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 954","lb":"CC954","ev":"Civakan recognises the visitor as a god by his unblinking eyes, shadowless body and unfading garland, and asks who he is.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:955":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":955,"t":["குங்குமக் குவட்டின் வீங்கிக் கோலம்வீற் றிருந்த தோளா","யிங்குநின் னருளிற் போகி யியக்கரு ளிறைவ னாகிச்","சங்கவெண் மலையின் மற்றுச் சந்திரோ தயத்தி னுச்சி","யங்கியா னுறைவ லெந்தை யறிகமற் றென்று சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The god tells him that by his grace he went on and became lord of the yakshas, and now dwells on the summit of Chandrodaya on the conch-white mountain, and asks his 'father' to know it.","n":"He calls the young man எந்தை, my father: the one who gave the mantra is the giver of his new birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","arul","thevar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 955","lb":"CC955","ev":"Sudanjanan tells Civakan he has become king of the yakshas on Mount Chandrodaya through Civakan's grace.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:956":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":956,"t":["என்றவ னுரைப்பக் கேட்டே இமயமு நிகர்க்க லாற்றாப்","பொன்றரு மாரி வண்கைப் புரவலன் புகன்று நோக்கி","வென்றவர் உலகம் பெற்ற வேந்துடை யின்ப மெல்லா","மின்றெனக் கெதிர்ந்த தென்றா னெரியுமிழ்ந் திலங்கும் வேலான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing this, the patron of the gold-raining hand looks at him gladly and says that all the joy of the kingship held in the world of the conquerors has come to him today.","n":"வென்றவர் உலகம் may be the heaven won by those who conquer by merit, or the world of the Jinas; either way Civakan counts another's rise as his own highest pleasure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 956","lb":"CC956","ev":"Civakan rejoices to learn what has become of the dog.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:957":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":957,"t":["சூடுறு கழலி னாற்குச் சுதஞ்சண னிதனைச் சொன்னான்","பாடல்வண் டரற்றும் பிண்டிப் பகவன திறைமை போல","மூடியிவ் வுலக மெல்லா நின்னடித் தருவ லின்னே","யாடியுட் பாவை போனீ யணங்கிய தணங்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Sudanjanan tells him that, like the lordship of the Bhagavan of the pindi tree where bees hum, he will now bring the whole world covered to his feet, and that whatever afflicts him will afflict the god as a mirror's image follows its object.","n":"The god measures what he offers by the Jina's sovereignty under the pindi (asoka) tree, so even the gift of the world is ranked below the Arhat's lordship. Line 4 (ஆடியுட் பாவை போல் நீ அணங்கியது அணங்க) is compressed and the sense of the mirror simile is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 957","lb":"CC957","ev":"Sudanjanan promises Civakan the whole world at his feet and a share in every trouble.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:958":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":958,"t":["வாளொடு வயவ ரீண்டி வாரணத் தொழுவின் முற்றி","மீளிமை செய்யின் வெய்ய நண்பநின் னினைப்ப தல்லால்","நாளொடு பக்கம் நைந்து வீழினும் வீழ்த லில்லாக்","கோளுடைக் கிழமை யொப்பாய் குறைவிலன் பிறவி (னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He goes on: should warriors with swords gather and close round him at the elephant stalls and do violence, he need only think of his friend; Civakan is like the bond of intimacy that does not fall even when days and fortnights wane, and the god lacks nothing in this birth.","n":"The danger named, swordsmen closing round him by the elephant stalls, is the one the story is about to bring on: after Civakan masters the king's elephant he is seized and the god carries him off (patikam verse 15).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kizhamai","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 958","lb":"CC958","ev":"Sudanjanan tells Civakan to call him to mind if armed men ever besiege him.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:959":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":959,"t":["இன்னிழ லிவரும் பூணா னிருவிசும் பிவர்த லுற்றப்","பொன்னெழு வனைய தோளாற் புல்லிக்கொண் டினைய கூறி","நின்னிழல் போல நீங்கே னிடவர்வரி னினைக்க வென்று","மின்னெழூஉப் பறப்ப தொத்து விசும்பிவர்ந் தமரன் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The god, wearing jewels of sweet light, about to rise into the sky, embraces him with shoulders like golden bars, says 'Like your shadow I will not leave you; if trouble comes, think of me', and rises into the sky like a flash of lightning.","n":"The promise binds the god's help to Civakan's remembering (நினைக்க): the help is summoned by thought, as the dog's rise was won by holding the padas in mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thunai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 959","lb":"CC959","ev":"Sudanjanan embraces Civakan, promises to come when remembered, and goes back to the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:960":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":960,"t":["சொல்லிய நன்மை யில்லாச் சுணங்கனிவ் வுடம்பு நீங்கி","யெல்லொளித் தேவ னாகிப் பிறக்குமோ வென்ன வேண்டா","கொல்லுலை யகத்திட் டூதிக் கூரிரும் பிரதங் குத்த","வெல்லையில் செம்பொ னாகி யெரிநிறம் பெற்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator tells the doubter not to ask whether this dog, with no good in it, could leave its body and be born a god of shining light: iron set in the smith's forge, blown upon and pierced with mercury becomes boundless red gold and takes the colour of fire.","n":"The narrator answers an objection before it is put: the image is alchemical, mercury (இரதம்) piercing iron in the forge, so the change is presented as a work of a known art, not a miracle. The figure of 952 returns, with the mantra now in the place of the friend's fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaadham","piravi","vinai","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 960","lb":"CC960","ev":"","cl":[["even a creature with no merit can, by the mantra heard at death, be reborn a god","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:961":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":961,"t":["எரிமாலை வேனுதியி னிறக்கிக் காம னடுகணையாற்","றிருமாலை வெம்முலைமேற் றிளைக்குந் தேவர் திருவுறுக","வருமாலை யெண்வினையு மகற்றி யின்பக் கடலாக்கித்","தருமாலை யல்ல தியான் றலையிற் றாழ்ந்து பணிவேனோ."],"p":"","r":"Let the gods who revel on the hot breasts of the women, wounded by Kama's killing arrows, keep their glory; the speaker will bow his head to none but the one who removes the eight karmas that come as a garland and makes a sea of bliss.","n":"The first-person refusal has no speech tag. It reads as the poet's own confession closing the episode; it could also be the reborn god's vow, since he has just been among the heavenly women (950). The gods are not denied; they are set aside as still subject to Kama.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu","vinai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 961","lb":"CC961","ev":"","cl":[["worship belongs only to the one who has destroyed the eight karmas and gives bliss, not to gods who live in desire","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:962":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":962,"t":["ஒன்றாய வூக்கவேர் பூட்டி யாக்கைச் செறுவுழுது","நன்றாய நல்விரதச் செந்நெல் வித்தி யொழுக்கநீர்","குன்றாமற் றாங்கொடுத்தைம் பொறியின் வேலி காத்தோம்பின்","வென்றார் தம் வீட்டின்பம் விளைக்கும் விண்ணோ ருலகீன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Yoke the plough of single-minded zeal, plough the field of the body, sow the red paddy of good vows, keep the water of conduct from failing and guard it with the fence of the five senses, and it will first yield the heavenly world and then the bliss of release of the conquerors.","n":"The harvest comes in two crops: the gods' world is born (ஈன்று) on the way to the bliss of release, so heaven is a by-product, not the goal. Every stage is the practiser's own labour; no god tills the field.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","ozhukkam","adakkam","nonbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 962","lb":"CC962","ev":"","cl":[["zeal, vows, right conduct and guarded senses, worked on the body, yield heaven and then the release of the Jinas","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:963":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":963,"t":["இத்தலை யிவர்க ளேக விமயநட் டரவு சுற்றி","யத்தலை யலற முந்நீர் கடைந்தவ ரரவ மொப்ப","மைத்தலை நெடுங்க ணாரு மைந்தரு மறலி யாட","மொய்த்திரள வன்ன மார்க்கு மோட்டிரும் பொய்கை புக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"While these two part, the dark-eyed women and the young men, with a roar like that of those who churned the ocean round the planted Himalaya with the snake, go into the great pool crowded with swans to fight in play.","n":"The churning of the ocean is used only for its uproar; the poem pictures the mountain as the Himalaya.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","inbam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 963","lb":"CC963","ev":"The townswomen and young men plunge into the great pool for the water festival.","cl":[],"ag":["மைத்தலை நெடுங்கணார்","மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:964":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":964,"t":["கலந்தெழு திரைநுண் ணாடைக் கடிக்கய மடந்தை காம","ரிலங்குபொற் கலாபத் தல்கு லிருகரைப் பரப்பு மாக","வலர்ந்ததண் கமலத் தம்போ தணிதக்க முகத்திற் கேற்ப","நலங்கெழு குவளை வாட்க ணன்னுத னலத்தை யுண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The pool is a bride whose fine garment is its mingling waves, whose two banks are her gold-girdled hips, whose lotus is her face and whose kuvalai are her eyes, and they enjoy her beauty.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 964","lb":"CC964","ev":"The bathers take their pleasure in the pool.","cl":[],"ag":["மைத்தலை நெடுங்கணார்","மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:965":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":965,"t":["தண்ணுமை முழவு மொந்தை தகுணிச்சம் பிறவு மோசை","யெண்ணிய விரலோ டங்கை புறங்கையி னிசைய வாக்கித்","திண்ணிதிற் றெறித்து மோவார் கொட்டியுங் குடைந்து மாடி","யொண்ணுதன் மகளிர் தம்மோ டுயர்மிசை யவர்க ளொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Striking the water hard and without stopping with fingers, palms and the backs of their hands so that it sounds like the tannumai, muzhavu, montai and takunicham drums, diving and playing, the young men with the bright-browed women are like the gods above.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","punalaadal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 965","lb":"CC965","ev":"The bathers drum on the water with their hands and dive and play.","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்","மைத்தலை நெடுங்கணார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:966":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":966,"t":["சிவிறியின் மாறு தூயுங் குங்கும மெறிந்துந் தேங்கொ","ளுவறுநீ ருழக்கி யோட்டி யுடைபுறங் கண்டு நக்குத்","தவறெனத் தாமம் பூட்டித் தருதிறை கொண்டு மின்பத்","திவறினார் காம வெள்ளத் தேத்தருந் தன்மை யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Spraying each other with syringes, flinging saffron, churning the honeyed water and chasing, laughing when the other side turns its back, binding garlands on the loser as a fine and taking tribute, they swim in pleasure; who can praise them in that flood of desire?","n":"The vocabulary is a campaign's: புறங்கண்டு (seeing the back), தவறு (offence), திறை (tribute). The last line can be wonder at a joy past praising or a dry aside on people lost in a flood of desire; the Tamil allows both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 966","lb":"CC966","ev":"The bathers play at war in the water, routing one another and exacting garland-fines and tribute.","cl":[],"ag":["மைத்தலை நெடுங்கணார்","மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:967":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":967,"t":["சாந்தக நிறைந்த தோணி தண்மலர் மாலைத் தோணி","பூந்துகி லார்ந்த தோணி புனைகலம் பெய்த தோணி","கூந்தன்மா மகளிர் மைந்தர் கொண்டுகொண் டெறிய வோடித்","தாந்திரைக் கலங்கள் போலத் தாக்குபு திரியு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Boats filled with sandal paste, boats of cool flower garlands, boats heaped with fine cloth and boats laden with jewels, pushed back and forth by the women and men, run and collide like ships on the waves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 967","lb":"CC967","ev":"The bathers drive little boats of paste, garlands, cloth and jewels at one another across the pool.","cl":[],"ag":["மைத்தலை நெடுங்கணார்","மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:968":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":968,"t":["கலிவளர் களிறு கைந்நீர் சொரிவபோன் முத்த மாலை","பொலிவொடு திவண்டு பொங்கிப் பூஞ்சிகை யலமந் தாடக்","குலிகநீர் நிறைந்த பந்திற் கொம்பனா ரோச்ச மைந்தர்","மெலிவுகண் டுவந்து மாதோ விருப்பொடு மறலி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Pearl strings swaying like water sprayed from a rutting elephant's trunk and their flower tufts tossing, the creeper-like women hurl balls filled with vermilion water, and the young men, glad to see them tire, fight on with desire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 968","lb":"CC968","ev":"The women pelt the men with balls of vermilion water and the men press the play as the women tire.","cl":[],"ag":["மைத்தலை நெடுங்கணார்","மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:969":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":969,"t":["வண்ணவொண் சுண்ணம் பட்டு மாலையுஞ் சாந்து மேந்தி","யெண்ணருந் திறத்து மைந்த ரெதிரெதி ரெறிய வோடி","விண்ணிடை நுடங்கு மின்னு மீன்களும் பொருவ போல","மண்ணிடை அமரர் கொண்ட மன்றலொத் திறந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The countless young men run with coloured powder, silks, garlands and paste and throw them at each other face to face, like lightning and stars fighting in the sky, and it surpasses the wedding the gods held on earth.","n":"Which wedding of the gods on earth is meant is not said.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","vathuvai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 969","lb":"CC969","ev":"The young men pelt each other with powder, silk, garlands and paste.","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:970":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":970,"t":["உரைத்த வெண்ணெயு மொண்ணறுஞ் சுண்ணமு","மரைத்த சாந்தமு நானமு மாலையு","நுரைத்து நோன்சிறை வண்டொடு தேனின","மிரைத்து நீர்கொழித் தின்ப மிறந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Rubbed butter, bright fragrant powder, ground sandal, musk and garlands foam on the water, swarms of strong-winged bees hum, the water is churned, and the pleasure passes its bounds.","n":"இன்பம் இறந்ததே may mean that the pleasure exceeded all measure, as இறந்தது does in 969, or that the bathing pleasure came to its end; the next verse turns to dressing after the bath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 970","lb":"CC970","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:971":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":971,"t":["கத்திகைக் கண்ணி நெற்றிக் கைதொழு கடவு ளன்ன","வித்தக விளைய ரெல்லாம் விழுமணிக் கலங்க டாங்கி","முத்தணிந் தாவி யூட்டி முகிழ் முலை கச்சின் வீக்கிப்","பித்திகைப் பிணையல் சூழ்ந்து பெண்கொடி பொலிந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The skilled young men, like gods worshipped with joined hands, with kaththigai garlands on their brows, all bring jewelled ornaments; the women, decked with pearls, their hair dried in perfumed smoke, their budding breasts bound in bodices and wound with jasmine strings, shine like creepers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 971","lb":"CC971","ev":"After the bath the women are dressed and adorned, and the young men bring them jewels.","cl":[],"ag":["மைந்தர்","மைத்தலை நெடுங்கணார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:972":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":972,"t":["திருந்துபொற் றேருஞ் செம்பொற் சிவிகையு மிடைந்து தெற்றிக்","கருங்கயக் களிறு மாவுங் காலியற் பிடியு மீண்டி","நெருங்குபு மள்ளர் தொக்கு நெடுவரைத் தொடுத்த வெள்ளங்","கருங்கடற் கிவர்ந்த வண்ணங் கடிநகர்க் கெழுந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Fine gold chariots and red-gold palanquins jostle and tangle, dark elephants, horses and swift cow-elephants gather, warriors throng, and the whole crowd rises toward the guarded city like a flood from a tall mountain rushing to the dark sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 972","lb":"CC972","ev":"The festival crowd, with chariots, palanquins, elephants and horses, streams back toward the city.","cl":[],"ag":["மள்ளர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:973":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":973,"t":["கடலெனக் காற்றெனக் கடுங்கட் கூற்றென","வுடல்சின வுருமென வூழித் தீயெனத்","தொடர்பிணி வெளின்முதன் முருக்கித் தோன்றிய","தடலருங் கடாக்களிற் றசனி வேகமே."],"p":"","r":"Like the sea, like the wind, like fierce-eyed Death, like the thunderbolt with angry body, like the fire of the world's end, breaking the chained post at its root, appears the musth elephant Asanivegam, hard to overcome.","n":"The five similes run from nature (sea, wind) to Death and the end of the world; the elephant's name, Thunderbolt-speed, is held to the last word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 973","lb":"CC973","ev":"The musth elephant Asanivegam breaks its chained post and bursts out among the returning crowd.","cl":[],"ag":["அசனிவேகம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:42"},"cc:974":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":974,"t":["பொருதிழி யருவி போன்று பொழிதரு கடாத்த தாகிக்","குருதிகொண் மருப்பிற் றாகிக் குஞ்சரஞ் சிதைந்த தென்னக்","கருதிய திசைக ளெல்லாங் கண்மிசைக் கரந்த மாந்தர்","பருதியின் முன்னர்த் தோன்றா மறைந்தபன் மீன்க ளொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The elephant, streaming must like a falling cascade and with blood on its tusks, breaks loose, and the people who had filled every quarter vanish from sight like the many stars that hide before the sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 974","lb":"CC974","ev":"The must elephant runs amok and the crowds scatter out of sight.","cl":[],"ag":["களிறு","மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:975":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":975,"t":["கருந்தடங் கண்ணி தன்மேற் காமுக ருள்ளம் போல","விருங்களி றெய்த வோடச் சிவிகைவிட் டிளைய ரேக","வரும்பெற லவட்குத் தோழி யாடவ ரில்லை யோஎன்","றோருங்குகை யுச்சிக் கூப்பிக் களிற்றெதி ரிறைஞ்சி நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the great tusker rushes on her as the hearts of lustful men rush on a dark-eyed woman, the bearers drop the palanquin and run, and the girl's companion, crying out whether there are no men, raises her joined hands above her head and bows before the elephant.","n":"The simile of line 1 sets the elephant's charge beside men's desire for the woman, so that the danger and the courtship to come are drawn from one figure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 975","lb":"CC975","ev":"The bearers abandon Gunamalai's palanquin, and her companion bows to the elephant and calls for help.","cl":[],"ag":["களிறு","இளையர்","தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:976":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":976,"t":["என்னைக்கொன் றிவள்க ணோடு மெல்லையி லொருவன் றோன்றி","யின்னுயி ரிவளைக் காக்கு மன்றெனி லென்கண் மாய்ந்தாற்","பின்னைத்தான் ஆவ தாக வென்றெண்ணிப் பிணைக ணோக்கி","மின்னுப்போ னுடங்கி நின்றாள் வீததை பொற்கொம் பொப்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"The companion resolves: let it kill me together with her, or let some man appear in time to save her dear life; if not, whatever comes after my eyes have closed may come; and she stands trembling like lightning, looking at the beast.","n":"The thought (lines 1-3) is the companion's, closed by என்றெண்ணி; whether the one who stands trembling in line 4, likened to a flower-laden golden creeper, is the companion or Gunamalai herself the verse does not settle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","uyir","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 976","lb":"CC976","ev":"Gunamalai's companion resolves to die with her unless a rescuer comes.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி",""],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:977":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":977,"t":["மணியிரு தலையுஞ் சேர்த்தி வான்பொனி னியன்ற நாணா","லணியிருங் குஞ்சி யேறக் கட்டியிட் டலங்கல் சூழ்ந்து","தணிவருந் தோழர் சூழத் தாழ்குழை திருவில் வீசப்","பணிவருங் குருசில் செல்வான் பாவைய திடரைக் கண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, his hair bound up with a gold cord tipped with gems at both ends and wreathed with a garland, his ear-drops swinging and his loyal companions around him, is passing by when he sees the girl's peril.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 977","lb":"CC977","ev":"Civakan, passing with his companions, sees the danger to the girl.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:978":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":978,"t":["பெண்ணுயி ரவல நோக்கிப் பெருந்தகை வாழ்விற் சாத","லெண்ணின னெண்ணி நொய்தா வினமலர் மாலை சுற்றா","வண்ணப்பொற் கடக மேற்றா வார்கச்சிற் றானை வீக்கா","வண்ணலங் களிற்றை வையா ஆர்ததுமே லோடினானே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing a woman's life in danger, the noble one reckons death better than living on; at once he winds up his flower garland, pushes up his golden bracelet, binds his cloth with his belt, curses the elephant and runs at it shouting.","n":"வாழ்வின் சாதல் எண்ணினன் is a judgment the narrator gives the hero at the moment of choice, before any thought of who the woman is: the rescue is first a matter of honour, and love comes after.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","maanam","anjaamai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 978","lb":"CC978","ev":"Civakan girds himself and runs at the elephant.","cl":[["for a noble man, death is better than living on while a woman's life is in danger","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","களிறு"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:979":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":979,"t":["குண்டலங் குமரன் கொண்டு குன்றின்மேல் வீழு மின்போ","லொண்டிறற் களிற்றி னெற்றி யெறிந்துதோ டொலித்து வீழ","மண்டில முத்துந் தாரு மாலையுங் குழலும் பொங்க","விண்டலர் கண்ணி சிந்த மின்னிற்சென் றெய்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The young man leaps like lightning falling on a hill and strikes the forehead of the mighty elephant with a clap of his arms, while his round pearls, chain, garland and hair fly and the open flowers of his wreath scatter.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 979","lb":"CC979","ev":"Civakan leaps at the elephant and strikes its forehead.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","களிறு"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:980":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":980,"t":["படம்விரி நாகஞ் செற்றுப் பாய்ந்தரு கலுழன் போல","மடவர லவளைச் செற்று மதக்களி றிறைஞ்சும் போழ்திற்","குடவரை நெற்றி பாய்ந்த கோளரி போன்று வேழத்","துடல்சினங் கடவக் குப்புற் றுருமென வுரறி யார்த்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Just as the must elephant bends to crush the girl, he springs at it like Garuda upon a hooded cobra, like a lion leaping on the brow of the western mountain, and roars like thunder so that the beast's rage turns on him.","n":"The roar has a purpose the verse states: குப்புற்று ... உடல்சினங் கடவ, he turns the elephant's anger from the girl to himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 980","lb":"CC980","ev":"Civakan springs at the elephant as it stoops over Gunamalai, drawing its fury to himself.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","களிறு"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:981":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":981,"t":["கூற்றென முழங்கிக் கையாற் கோட்டிடைப் புடைப்பக் காய்ந்து","காற்றென வுரறி நாகங் கடாம்பெய்து கனவிற் சீறி","யாற்றலங் குமரன் றன்மே லடுகளி றோட லஞ்சான்","கோற்றொடிப் பாவை தன்னைக் கொண்டுயப் போமின் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The elephant roars like Death, beats at him between its tusks with its trunk, shrieks like the wind and pours must in fury; the strong youth, unafraid as it runs at him, tells those near to take the bangled girl and get her to safety.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 981","lb":"CC981","ev":"As the elephant turns on him, Civakan orders the girl carried away to safety.","cl":[],"ag":["களிறு","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:982":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":982,"t":["மதியினுக் கிவர்ந்த வேக மாமணி நாகம் வல்லே","பதியமை பருதி தன்மேற் படம்விரித் தோடி யாங்குப்","பொதியவிழ் கோதை தன்மேற் பொருகளி றகன்று பெற்றார்க்","கதியமை தோளி னானைக் கையகப் படுத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the great serpent that climbs to seize the moon spreads its hood and runs at the sun, the fighting elephant leaves the girl and seizes in its trunk the man whose shoulders were made for those who have no refuge.","n":"The eclipse simile makes Civakan the sun (பருதி) and the elephant the serpent of eclipse; பெற்றார்க்கு அதியமை தோளினான் praises him as a refuge in the very line in which he is caught.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 982","lb":"CC982","ev":"The elephant leaves Gunamalai and seizes Civakan in its trunk.","cl":[],"ag":["களிறு"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:983":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":983,"t":["கையகப் படுத்த லோடுங் கார்மழை மின்னி னொய்தா","மொய்கொளப் பிறழ்ந்து முத்தார் மருப்பிடைக் குளித்துக் காற்கீ","ழையென வடங்கி வல்லா னாடிய மண்வட் டேய்ப்பச்","செய்கழற் குருசி லாங்கே கரந்துசே ணகற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The moment it seizes him he slips free quick as lightning from a rain cloud, dives between its pearl-tipped tusks, folds himself small beneath its feet like the clay ball an expert plays, and the anklet-wearing hero, keeping out of its reach there, drives it far away.","n":"கரந்துசே ணகற்றினான் can be read as his hiding and driving the beast far off or as his vanishing far away himself; the next verse, in which the elephant withdraws, favours the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vali","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 983","lb":"CC983","ev":"Civakan wriggles free, dodges between the tusks and under the feet, and drives the elephant off.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","களிறு"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:984":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":984,"t":["மல்ல னீர்மணி வண்ணனைப் பண்டொர்நாட்","கொல்ல வோடிய குஞ்சரம் போன்றதச்","செல்வன் போன்றனன் சீவகன் றெய்வம்போற்","பில்கும் மும்ம தவேழம் பெயர்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The elephant was like the tusker that once ran to kill the gem-coloured lord of the wide waters, and Civakan was like that lord; and the elephant dripping triple must, as if a god were in it, gave way.","n":"The simile is the story of Krishna and the elephant sent against him, told in one line without naming either; a Jain epic uses the Vaishnava deed as a measure of its hero without comment. தெய்வம்போல் may go with Civakan (godlike, he stood) or with the elephant (as if possessed); the line division favours the elephant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 984","lb":"CC984","ev":"The elephant gives way before Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","களிறு"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:985":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":985,"t":["ஒருகை யிருமருப்பின் மும்மதத்த தோங்கெழிற்குன் றனைய வேழந்","திருகு கனைகழற்காற் சீவகன்வென் றிளையாட்கு டைந்து தேனார்","முருகு கமழலங்கன் முத்திலங்கு மார்பினனைஞ் ஞாற்று நால்வ","ரருகு கழல்பரவத் தனியேபோ யுய்யான மடைந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Having beaten the one-trunked, two-tusked, triple-must elephant, tall as a fair hill, for the young girl, Civakan of the honey-scented garland and pearl-bright chest goes alone to the pleasure garden while his five hundred and four companions crowd round praising his feet.","n":"ஐஞ்ஞாற்று நால்வர் reads as five hundred and four; the verse sets their praise beside தனியே போய், so 'alone' must mean apart from the girl and her people, not without his friends. The verse is in a longer metre than its neighbours.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 985","lb":"CC985","ev":"Civakan, victorious, goes to the garden while his companions praise him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:986":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":986,"t":["மணிசெய் கந்துபோன் மருள வீங்கிய","திணிபொற் றோளினான் செல்ல னீக்கிய","வணிபொற் கொம்பினை யழுங்க லென்றுதன்","றணிவில் காதலார் தாங்கொ டேகினார்."],"p":"","r":"Her own people, whose love never slackens, take away the golden creeper whose distress the man of shoulders thick as a jewelled post had removed, telling her not to grieve.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 986","lb":"CC986","ev":"Gunamalai's kin take her home and comfort her.","cl":[],"ag":["காதலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:987":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":987,"t":["முழங்கு தெண்டிரை மூரி நீணிதி","வழங்கு நீண்டகை வணிகர்க் கேறனான்","விழுங்கு காதலாள் வேற்கட் பாவைதாய்","குழைந்த கோதையைக் கண்டு கூறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The mother of the spear-eyed girl, beloved wife of the man who is a bull among the merchants with hands that give out great wealth from the roaring sea, sees her daughter wilted like a crushed garland and speaks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 987","lb":"CC987","ev":"Gunamalai's mother sees her wilted and speaks.","cl":[],"ag":["தாய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:988":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":988,"t":["நெய்பெய் நீளெரி நெற்றி மூழ்கிய","கைசெய் மாலைபோற் கரிந்து பொன்னிற","நைய வந்ததென் னங்கைக் கின்றென","வுய்தல் வேட்கையா லுரைத்த லோம்பினார்."],"p":"","r":"'What has come on my girl today, that she has withered and lost her golden colour like a hand-made garland thrust into a high ghee-fed flame?' she asks; and those who knew kept from telling, for wanting to come through safe.","n":"The verse turns from the mother's question (lines 1-3) to the silence of those who know: the companions hide the danger, and with it the cause of her withering, out of fear for themselves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","acham","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 988","lb":"CC988","ev":"The mother asks what has withered her daughter; the companions keep silent.","cl":[],"ag":["தாய்","தோழிமார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தாய்",""],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:989":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":989,"t":["முருகு விண்டுலா முல்லைக் கத்திகை","பருகி வண்டுலாம் பல்கு ழலினாள்","வருக வென்றுதாய் வாட்க ணீர்துடைத்","துருகு நுண்ணிடை யொசியப் புல்லினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Calling 'come', the mother wipes the tears from her daughter's bright eyes and embraces her, whose hair is full of mullai garlands drunk by bees and whose slender waist bends.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 989","lb":"CC989","ev":"The mother calls her daughter to her, wipes her tears and embraces her.","cl":[],"ag":["தாய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:990":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":990,"t":["கடம்பு சூடிய கன்னி மாலைபேற்","றொடர்ந்து கைவிடாத் தோழி மாரொடுங்","குடங்கை யுண்கணாள் கொண்ட பண்ணையு","ளடைந்த துன்பமென் றறிவி னாடினாள்."],"p":"","r":"With the companions who never let go of her hand, the mother searches it out by her own understanding and finds that the girl's grief came upon her at the festive gathering.","n":"Line 1 (கடம்பு சூடிய கன்னி மாலைபேற்) is hard to construe; it may tie the gathering to a rite of the kadambu-wearing god, and the text may be corrupt (மாலைபெற்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 990","lb":"CC990","ev":"The mother, questioning the companions, learns where the trouble began.","cl":[],"ag":["தாய்","தோழிமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:991":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":991,"t":["கம்மப் பல்கலங் களைந்து கண்டெறூஉம்","விம்மப் பல்கலம் நொய்ய மெய்யணிந்","தம்மென் மாலையு மடைச்சிக் குங்குமங்","கொம்மை மட்டித்தார் கொடிய னாளையே."],"p":"","r":"They take off her heavy worked ornaments and put light ones on her body, set a soft garland on her and smear saffron over the creeper-like girl.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 991","lb":"CC991","ev":"The women change Gunamalai's heavy jewels for light ones and anoint her.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழிமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:992":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":992,"t":["அம்பொன் வள்ளத்து ளமிர்த மேந்துமெங்","கொம்பி னவ்வையைக் கொணர்மின் சென்றெனப்","பைம்பொ னல்குலைப் பயிரும் பைங்கிளி","செம்பொற் கொம்பினெம் பாவை செல்கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her green parrot calls to the girl, 'go and fetch our sister who brings nectar in a golden cup', and our girl like a golden creeper goes.","n":"Who is fetched and where the girl goes are not clear: the parrot's words may ask for its keeper, or may be a call the girl, lost in thought, answers by walking away. The verse ends in the voice of her own household (எம் பாவை).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 992","lb":"CC992","ev":"Gunamalai's pet parrot calls out, and she goes.","cl":[],"ag":["பைங்கிளி","குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:993":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":993,"t":["நிறத்தெ றிந்துப றித்தநி ணங்கொள்வேற்","றிறத்தை வெளவிய சேயரிக் கண்ணினாள்","பிறப்பு ணர்ந்தவர் போற்றமர் பேச்செலாம்","வெறுத்தி யாவையும் மேவல ளாயினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The girl whose red-lined eyes have stolen the power of the fat-smeared spear spurns all the talk of her kin, like those who have come to know their births, and wants nothing at all.","n":"பிறப்பு உணர்ந்தவர் may mean those who recall their former births, the knowledge that in the epic's world turns a person to renunciation; the narrator lends the love-struck girl the renouncer's withdrawal, though her wanting nothing is wanting one man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","thuravu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 993","lb":"CC993","ev":"Gunamalai turns from her family's talk and from everything else.","cl":[["those who have come to know their births turn away from the talk of kin and desire nothing","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:994":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":994,"t":["குமரி மாநகர்க் கோதையங் கொம்பனா","டமரி னீங்கிய செவ்வியுட் டாமரை","யமரர் மேவரத் தோன்றிய வண்ணல்போற்","குமர னாகிய காதலிற் கூறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Finding a moment apart from her people, the girl like a creeper in a garland tells of her love for the youth, who is like the lord who appeared on the lotus to the gods' delight.","n":"The lord born on the lotus to the gods' desire, called குமரன், reads as Murugan of the reed pool; the verse names Civakan only by the likeness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 994","lb":"CC994","ev":"Away from her kin, Gunamalai speaks of her love for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:995":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":995,"t":["கலத்தற் காலங்கல் லூரிநற் கொட்டிலா","முலைத்த டத்திடை மொய்யெருக் குப்பையா","விலக்க மென்னுயி ராவெய்து கற்குமா","லலைக்கும் வெஞ்சர மைந்துடை யானரோ."],"p":"","r":"Gunamalai says that the cruel five-arrowed one practises his archery on her, making the time of union his school's hall, the space between her breasts his heap of target earth, and her life his mark.","n":"Kama is an archer in training and she the butt: the conceit turns her pain into his practice, so that it will go on.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 995","lb":"CC995","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை","தோழிமார்"],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:996":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":996,"t":["பூமி யும்பொறை யாற்றருந் தன்மையால்","வேமெ னெஞ்சமும் வேள்வி முளரிபோல்","தாம மார்பனைச் சீவக சாமியைக்","காம னைக்கடி தேதம்மின் றேவிர்காள்."],"p":"","r":"'Since even the earth could not bear it, my heart burns like the fire of a sacrifice: bring me quickly the man of the garlanded chest, Civaka Sami, who is Kama himself, ladies.'","n":"The name சீவக சாமி, by which the invocation calls the hero, is here in the mouth of a girl who wants him as a lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nenju","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 996","lb":"CC996","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை","தோழிமார்"],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:997":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":997,"t":["கையி னாற்சொலக் கண்களிற் கேட்டிடு","மொய்கொள் சிந்தையின் மூங்கையு மாயினேன்","செய்த வம்புரி யாச்சிறி யார்கள்போ","லுய்ய லாவதொர் வாயிலுண் டாங்கொலோ."],"p":"","r":"'I speak with my hands and hear with my eyes; with a mind crowded with thought I have become a mute; like the small folk who did no penance, is there any door by which I can be saved?'","n":"Speaking by hand and hearing by eye describes her silence before others: she cannot say her love aloud. The simile measures a lover's want by the want of those without the merit of past tapas.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","thavam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 997","lb":"CC997","ev":"","cl":[["those who have done no penance have no way to reach what they long for","general","reported",3,"gunamalai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை","தோழிமார்"],"ch":"cc:43"},"cc:998":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":998,"t":["கண்ணும் வாளற்ற கைவளை சோருமாற்","புண்ணும் போன்று புலம்புமென் நெஞ்சரோ","வெண்ணில் காம மெரிப்பினு மேற்செலாப்","பெண்ணின் மிக்கது பெண்ணல தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Gunamalai says her eyes have lost their brightness, her bangles slip, and her heart cries out like a wound; a woman, though desire burns her, does not go forward, and nothing surpasses womanhood.","n":"She praises the very restraint that is hurting her; the next verse shows how close it comes to breaking. The thought is the Kural's on women and the palmyra horse (1137) in other words, not a verbal echo.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nirai","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 998","lb":"CC998","ev":"","cl":[["a woman, however desire burns her, does not make the first advance","general","reported",3,"gunamalai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை",""],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:999":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":999,"t":["சோலை வேய்மருள் சூழ்வளைத் தோளிதன்","வேலை மாக்கடல் வேட்கைமிக் கூர்தர","வோலை தாழ்பெண்ணை மாமட லூர்தலைக்","கால வேற்றடங் கண்ணி கருதினாள்."],"p":"","r":"As her longing rises like the great sea, the girl with spear-like eyes thinks of riding the palmyra-stem horse with its hanging leaves.","n":"The madal is a man's public threat; a heroine only thinking of it shows desire pressing past the rule she has just praised. She thinks of it but turns to the parrot instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 999","lb":"CC999","ev":"Gunamalai, overcome by longing, considers riding the madal.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1000":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1000,"t":["உய்யு மாறுரை யுன்னை யலாலிலேன்","செய்ய வாய்க்கிளி யேசிறந் தாயென","நைய னங்கையிற் நாட்டகத் துண்டெனிற்","றைய லாய்சம ழாதுரை யென்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Gunamalai begs her red-beaked parrot, whom she calls her only refuge, to tell her how she may live, and the parrot tells her not to waste away and to say without shame whom in the land she wants.","n":"The closing என்றதே, a neuter verb, gives lines 3 and 4 to the parrot; the division of the speeches is my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","thoothu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1000","lb":"CC1000","ev":"Gunamalai asks her parrot for help, and the parrot asks her to name the man.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை","கிளி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை","கிளி"],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1001":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1001,"t":["தெளிக யம்மலர் மேலுறை தேவியி","னொளியுஞ் சாயலு மொப்புமை யில்லவள்","களிகொள் காமத்திற் கையற வெய்தித்தன்","கிளியைத் தூதுவிட் டாள்கிளந் தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"They say that she, matchless in radiance and grace even against the goddess on the lotus, helpless in heady desire, told her parrot her message and sent it as her envoy.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1001","lb":"CC1001","ev":"Gunamalai sends her parrot as messenger to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை","கிளி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1002":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1002,"t":["பூணொ டேந்திய வெம்முலைப் பொன்னனா","ணாணுந் தன்குல னுந்நலங் கீழ்ப்பட","வீணை வித்தகற் காணிய விண்படர்ந்","தாணுப் பைங்கிளி யாண்டுப் பறந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"With her modesty, her family and her good name set beneath her longing, the dear green parrot flew through the sky to find the master of the vina.","n":"Sending a go-between is itself counted as a loss of நாண், family standing and name: the price of the message is set before the message arrives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1002","lb":"CC1002","ev":"The parrot flies off to find Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1003":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1003,"t":["கூட்டினான் மணிபல தெளித்துக் கொண்டவன்","றீட்டினான் கிழிமிசைத் திலக வாணுதல்","வேட்டமால் களிற்றிடை வெருவி நின்றதோர்","நாட்டமு நடுக்கமு நங்கை வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan blended and purified many coloured gems and painted on cloth the girl with the tilaka brow, as she was in look and trembling when she stood terrified before the rutting elephant.","n":"He paints her not at ease but at the moment of terror: the danger that brought them together is the image he keeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1003","lb":"CC1003","ev":"Civakan paints Gunamalai's portrait on cloth as she stood before the elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1004":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1004,"t":["நெகிழ்ந்துசோர் பூந்துகி னோக்கி நோக்கியே","மகிழ்ந்துவீழ் மணிக்குழன் மாலை காறொடு","முகிழ்ந்து வீங் கிளமுலை முத்தந் தைவரும்","புகழ்ந்துதன் றோள்களிற் புல்லு மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Gazing and gazing at the painting, he strokes its slipping garment, falling hair, garland and the pearls on her young breasts, praises it, and clasps it to his shoulders.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1004","lb":"CC1004","ev":"Civakan caresses and embraces the portrait.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1005":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1005,"t":["படைமலர் நெடுங்கணாள் பரவை யேந்தல்குன்","மிடைமணி மேகலை நோற்ற வெந்தொழிற்","புடைதிரள் வனமுலைப் பூணு நோற்றன","வடிமலர்த் தாமரைச் சிலம்பு நோற்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says that the jewelled girdle on her broad hips, the ornaments on her breasts and the anklets on her lotus feet must all have done penance to be worn by her.","n":"The verb நோற்ற, repeated three times, turns the language of the vow into a lover's compliment; the fancy assumes that good fortune is earned by austerity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nonbu","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1005","lb":"CC1005","ev":"","cl":[["a fortunate place is the fruit of penance once performed","general","reported",2,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1006":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1006,"t":["மின்னணங் குறுமிடை மேவர் சாயலுக்","கின்னண மிறைமகன் புலம்ப யாவதுந்","தன்னணங் குறுமொழித் தத்தை தத்தையை","மன்னணங் குறலொடு மகிழ்ந்து கண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"While the prince lamented thus over her grace, the parrot of the girl of tormenting speech saw with delight Tattai, the prince's beloved, arriving.","n":"The line puns on தத்தை, parrot and Gandharvadattai's short name: the parrot sees Tattai. The sense of மன்னணங் குறலொடு is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1006","lb":"CC1006","ev":"As Civakan laments, the parrot sees Gandharvadattai approaching.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கிளி","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1007":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1007,"t":["ஆடுபாம் பெனப்புடை யகன்ற வல்குன்மேற்","சூடிய கலைப்புறஞ் சூழ்ந்த பூந்துகி","லோடிய வெரிவளைத் துருவ வெண்புகை","கூடிமற் றதன்புறங் குலாய கொள்கைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"On her hips, wide as a dancing snake's hood, the fine cloth wrapped over her girdle looked as if pale smoke of incense had gathered and curled about it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1007","lb":"CC1007","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1008":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1008,"t":["கொன்வளர் குவிமுலைக் கோட்டிற் றாழ்ந்தன","மின்வளர் திரள்வடம் விளங்கு பைங்கதி","ரின்வள ரிளம்பிறை யெழுதப் பட்டன","பொன்வளர் செப்பின்மேற் பொலிந்த போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The bright strands of pearl hanging over the tips of her rising breasts looked like young crescent moons drawn on a golden casket.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1008","lb":"CC1008","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1009":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1009,"t":["குண்டல மொருபுடை குலாவி வில்லிட","விண்டலர்ந்த தொருபுடை தோடு மின்செய","மண்டல நிறைந்ததோர் மதிய மன்னதே","யொண்டொடி திருமுகத் துருவ மாட்சியே."],"p":"","r":"With an earring arching its light on one side and an ear-roll flashing on the other, the beauty of her face was like a full round moon.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1009","lb":"CC1009","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1010":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1010,"t":["பூணிற முலையவள் பொருவில் பூநுதன்","மாணிறக் கருங்குழன் மருங்கிற் போக்கிய","நாணிற மிகுகதிர்ப் பட்ட நல்லொளி","வானிற மின்னிருள் வளைந்த தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The fine gold thread drawn through the dark hair at her forehead shone like lightning curved through a dark sky.","n":"Which ornament நாணிற of line 3 names is not certain from the line alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1010","lb":"CC1010","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1011":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1011,"t":["கடிகமழ் பூஞ்சிகை காமர் மல்லிகை","வடிவுடை மாலைகால் தொடர்ந்து வாய்ந்தது","நடுவொசிந் தொல்கிய நாறு மாமலர்க்","கொடியின்மேற் குயில்குனிந் திருந்த தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The jasmine garland trailing from her fragrant hair-knot looked like a koel bending on a swaying flowering creeper.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1011","lb":"CC1011","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1012":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1012,"t":["சிலம்பொடு மேகலை மிழற்றத் தேனினம்","அலங்கலுண் டியாழ்செயும் அம்பொன் பூங்கெடி","நலம்பட நன்னடை கற்ற தொக்குமிவ்","விலங்கரித் தடங்கணாள் யாவள் ஆங்கொலோ."],"p":"","r":"The parrot wonders who this wide-eyed woman can be, who walks with anklets and girdle chiming like a golden flowering creeper that has learned to walk while bees hum on it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1012","lb":"CC1012","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கிளி",""],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1013":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1013,"t":["யாவளே ஆயினும் ஆக மற்றிவள்","மேவிய பொருளொடு மீண்ட பின்னலால்","ஏவலாற் சேர்கலேன் என்று பைங்கிளி","பூவலர் சண்பகம் பொருந்திற் றென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Saying that whoever she is, it will not go near on its errand until she has done what she came for and gone, the green parrot settles in a flowering champak.","n":"The envoy keeps the go-between's rule of secrecy: the message is for him alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1013","lb":"CC1013","ev":"The parrot waits hidden in a champak tree.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கிளி",""],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1014":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1014,"t":["மதுக்களி நெடுங்கணாள் வான்பொற் கிண்கிணி","யொதுக்கிடை மிழற்றச்சென் றெய்தி யூன்கவர்","கதக்களி வேலினாற் கண்டு காமநீர்ப்","புதுத்தளி ரனையவள் புலந்து நோக்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The honey-eyed woman, gold anklet-bells tinkling as she walked, came up to the man of the fierce spear, saw, and looked at him in a sulk.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1014","lb":"CC1014","ev":"Gandharvadattai comes upon Civakan with the portrait and sulks.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1015":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1015,"t":["இதுவென வுருவென வியக்கி யென்றலும்","புதிதிது பூந்துகில் குழல்கள் சோர்தலான்","மதுவிரி கோதையம் மாலை நின்மன","மதுமுறை யியக்கலி னியக்கி யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Asked what the figure was, he called it a yakshi, and she answers that this is something new: since his garment and hair hang loose and that garlanded one so moves his mind, she must indeed be an iyakki.","n":"The retort puns on இயக்கி, a yakshi and 'one who sets in motion'. Who asks and who answers in line 1 is my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1015","lb":"CC1015","ev":"Civakan passes the portrait off as a yakshi, and Gandharvadattai turns the word against him.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1016":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1016,"t":["முளைத்தெழு மதியமுத் தரும்பி யாங்கென","விளைத்தது திருமுகம் வியர்ப்பு வெஞ்சிலை","வளைத்தன புருவமு முரிந்த வல்லையே","கிளைக்கழு நீர்க்கணுஞ் சிவப்பிற் கேழ்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Sweat beaded on her face like pearls on a rising moon, her brows bent like a bow, and her eyes reddened like a cluster of water lilies.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1016","lb":"CC1016","ev":"Gandharvadattai shows her anger.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1017":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1017,"t":["பாவைநீ புலவியி னீடல் பாவியேற்","காவியொன் றிரண்டுடம் பல்ல தூற்றுநீர்க்","கூவல்வாய் வெண்மணல் குறுகல் செல்லுமே","மேவிப்பூங் கங்கையுள் விழைந்த வன்னமே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan pleads with her not to draw out her sulk: for him, a wretch, they are one life in two bodies, and would a swan that delighted in the flowering Ganges go near the white sand of a seep-well?","n":"The Ganges and the well rank the two women: to soothe the wife he belittles the girl he has just embraced in paint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1017","lb":"CC1017","ev":"Civakan tries to appease Gandharvadattai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1018":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1018,"t":["பேரினும் பெண்டிரைப் பொறாது சீறுவாள்","நேர்மலர்ப் பாவையை நோக்கி நெய்சொரி","கூரழல் போல்வதோர் புலவி கூர்ந்ததே","யார்வுறு கணவன்மாட் டமிர்தின் சாயற்கே."],"p":"","r":"Looking at the painted figure, she who flares at other women even by name felt her sulk toward her loving husband blaze like fire with ghee poured on it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1018","lb":"CC1018","ev":"Gandharvadattai's anger grows as she looks at the portrait.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1019":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1019,"t":["புலந்தவள் கொடியென நடுங்கிப் பொன்னரிச்","சிலம்பொடு மேகலை மிழற்றச் சென்னிமே","லலங்கல்வா யடிமல ரணிந்து குண்டல","மிலங்கப்பேர்ந் தினமலர் சிதறி யேகினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Trembling like a creeper in her anger, anklets and girdle chiming, she set her flower-feet upon his garlanded head, turned with her earrings flashing, scattered her flowers and left.","n":"Line 3 seems to have him bow and her feet touch his head; the gesture and who makes it are not plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1019","lb":"CC1019","ev":"Gandharvadattai leaves Civakan in anger.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1020":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1020,"t":["துனிப்புறு கிளவியாற் றுணைவி யேகலு","மினிப்பிறர்க் கிடமிலை யெழுவ லீங்கெனாக்","கனிப்புறு சொல்லளைஇப் பறந்து காளைதன்","பனிக்கதிர்ப் பகைமலர்ப் பாதஞ் சேர்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"When his wife had gone with angry words, the parrot, saying that now there was no one else and it would come out, mixed sweet words and flew to the young man's feet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1020","lb":"CC1020","ev":"The parrot comes down to Civakan once Gandharvadattai has gone.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளி","காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1021":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1021,"t":["வாழ்கநின் கழலடி மைந்த வென்னவே","தோழியர் சுவாகதம் போது கீங்கெனச்","சூழ்மணி மோதிரஞ் சுடர்ந்து வில்லிட","யாழறி வித்தக னங்கை நீட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"The parrot hails his anklet-feet and wishes him long life; he answers 'welcome, friend, come here', and the master of the yazh holds out his hand, his gem rings flashing.","n":"The welcome is the borrowed word சுவாகதம், svāgatam, a courtly greeting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1021","lb":"CC1021","ev":"Civakan welcomes the parrot onto his hand.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:44"},"cc:1022":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1022,"t":["பொன்னிய குரும்பையிற் பொலிந்த வெம்முலைக்","கன்னியர் தூதொடு காமர் பைங்கிளி","முன்னமே வந்தென முறுவல் நோக்கமோ","டென்னைகொல் வரவென வினிய செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator tells that the parrot came ahead bearing the maiden's message, and Civakan, with a smiling look, asked it sweetly why it had come.","n":"The message is called கன்னியர் தூது, the maidens' message, with an honorific plural; the sender is Gunamalai, whom the verse names only by her body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1022","lb":"CC1022","ev":"Gunamalai's parrot arrives with her message and Civakan smilingly asks why it has come.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1023":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1023,"t":["மையலங் களிற்றொடு பொருத வண்புக","ழையனைச் செவ்விகண் டறிந்து வம்மெனப்","பையர வல்குலெம் பாவை தூதொடு","கையிலங் கெஃகினாய் காண வந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The parrot answers that its mistress sent it to find the right moment with the famed lord who fought the maddened elephant and to come back, so it has come to see him.","n":"செவ்வி கண்டு, 'having seen the moment', makes the go-between's skill one of timing; the elephant fight it recalls is the rescue from which Gunamalai's love began.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1023","lb":"CC1023","ev":"The parrot tells Civakan that Gunamalai sent it to him.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கிளி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1024":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1024,"t":["வெஞ்சின வேழ முண்ட வெள்ளிலின் வெறிய மாக","நெஞ்சமு நிறையு நீல நெடுங்கணாற் கவர்ந்த கள்வி","அஞ்சனத் துவலை யாடி நடுங்கினா ணிலைமை யென்னை","பைஞ்சிறைத் தத்தை யென்னாப் பசுங்கிளி மொழியும்"],"p":"","r":"The parrot reports that the girl who stole hearts and restraint with her long blue eyes, leaving them hollow like a wood-apple an elephant has swallowed, now trembles bathed in kohl-dark tears, and asks what her state is.","n":"The thief who stole நிறை now has lost her own: the eyes that emptied others like the swallowed wood-apple are full of tears. Whose heart was stolen, and whether line 4 is the parrot's own question or Gunamalai's to her parrot, is not certain from the word order.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","nirai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1024","lb":"CC1024","ev":"The parrot describes Gunamalai trembling in tears.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கிளி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1025":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1025,"t":["பூவணை யழலின்மேற் சேக்கும் பொன்செய்தூண்","பாவைதான் பொருந்துபு நிற்கும் பற்பல்கா","லாவியா வழலென வுயிர்க்கு மையென","மேவிப்பூ நிலமிசை யிருக்கு மெல்லவே."],"p":"","r":"The parrot goes on: she lies on her flower bed as on a fire, leans on a golden pillar like a carved doll, sighs again and again as if breath were flame, and softly sinks to sit on the ground.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1025","lb":"CC1025","ev":"Gunamalai, in the parrot's account, cannot rest on her bed and sinks to the ground.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கிளி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1026":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1026,"t":["பணித்தகு கோலமும் பந்தும் பார்ப்புறாண்","மணிக்கழங் காடலண் மாமைதான் விளர்த்","தணித்தகை யாழினோ டமுதம் விட்டொழீஇத்","துணைப்பெரு மலர்க்கணிற் றுயிலு நீங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She no longer looks at her adornment, her ball or her young birds, does not play with gem jacks, her dark skin has paled, she has left the lute and her food, and sleep has left her eyes.","n":"The list runs from a girl's games to the body's needs, so the account measures love-sickness by what the girlhood world has lost for her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","melivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1026","lb":"CC1026","ev":"Gunamalai gives up play, music, food and sleep.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கிளி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1027":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1027,"t":["திருந்துவேற் சீவக சாமி யோவெனுங்","கருங்கடல் வெள்வளை கழல்ப வோவெனும்","வருந்தினேன் மார்புறப் புல்லு வந்தெனும்","பொருந்துபூங் கொம்பன பொருவின் சாயலே."],"p":"","r":"She cries out to Civaka Saami of the shapely spear, laments that her conch bangles slip off, and begs him to come and hold her to his chest, she of grace like a flowering branch.","n":"The parrot quotes her cries in the first person, so the girl's own appeal reaches the man only in the voice of the bird.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1027","lb":"CC1027","ev":"Gunamalai calls aloud on Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கிளி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1028":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1028,"t":["கன்னிய ருற்றநோய் கண்ண னார்க்குமஃ","தின்னதென் றுரையலர் நாணி னாதலான்","மன்னும்யா னுணரலேன் மாத ருற்றநோய்","துன்னிநீ அறிதியோ தோன்ற லென்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The parrot says that maidens do not tell even those as dear as their eyes what ails them, out of shame, so it, who stays with her, cannot know her illness, and asks whether the prince himself knows it.","n":"The parrot's ignorance is feigned: it has just described the illness and named its cause through her cries in 1027, so the question puts the diagnosis on the man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kaamanoy","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1028","lb":"CC1028","ev":"The parrot asks Civakan whether he knows the cause of Gunamalai's illness.","cl":[["an unmarried girl will not name her love-sickness even to those closest to her, from shame","general","reported",2,"kili"]],"ag":["கிளி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கிளி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1029":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1029,"t":["புள்ளின் வாயுரை கேட்டலும் பொன்செய்வே","லெள்ளி நீண்டகண் ணாடிறத் தின்னுரை","யுள்ளி னாருழைக் கண்டதொத் தானரோ","வள்ளன் மாத்தடிந் தானன்ன மாண்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"On hearing the bird's words, the spear-bearer, noble as the generous one who cut down the mango, felt as though he saw before him the long-eyed girl and her sweet words he had been thinking of.","n":"மாத் தடிந்தான், 'he who cut down the mango', most likely names Murugan, who felled the demon standing as a mango tree; the comparison is the narrator's and the identification is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1029","lb":"CC1029","ev":"Hearing the parrot, Civakan feels Gunamalai is present before him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1030":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1030,"t":["சென்ம ருந்துதந் தாய்சொல்லு நின்மனத்","தென்ன மர்ந்த துரைத்துக்கொ ணீயென","வின்னி மிர்ந்தநின் வீங்கெழிற் றோளவட்","கின்ம ருந்திவை வேண்டுவ லென்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The parrot asks him, as the one who once gave her the medicine of life, to say what has settled in his mind, and says it seeks his bow-straightening shoulders as the sweet medicine for her.","n":"சென்மருந்து தந்தாய் likely recalls the rescue from the elephant: the man who saved her life once is asked to save it again. The compound is hard to divide, so the sense is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","kaamanoy","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1030","lb":"CC1030","ev":"The parrot asks Civakan to declare himself and names him as her cure.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கிளி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1031":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1031,"t":["பொற்குன் றாயினும் பூம்பழ னங்கள்சூழ்","நெற்குன் றாம்பதி நேரினுந் தன்னையான்","கற்குன் றேந்திய தோளிணை கண்ணுறீ இச்","சொற்குன் றாபுணர் கேன்சொல்லு போவென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan replies that, were he offered a hill of gold or a town of paddy heaps ringed by flowering fields, he would still bring his rock-like shoulders to her and wed her without failing his word, and bids the parrot go and tell her.","n":"சொல் குன்றா, 'without my word falling short', is the phrase Gunamalai will turn into her prayer in 1045 (சொற்குன்றான் ஆக).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sool","varaivu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1031","lb":"CC1031","ev":"Civakan pledges to marry Gunamalai and sends the parrot back with his word.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","கிளி"],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1032":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1032,"t":["சேலை வென்றகண் ணாட்கிவை செப்பரி","தோலை யொன்றெழு திப்பணி நீயென","மாலை மார்பன் கொடுப்பத் தினைக்குர","லோலை யோடுகொண் டோங்கிப் பறந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The parrot says these things are hard for it to tell the fish-eyed girl and asks him to write a palm leaf; the garlanded hero gives one, and the parrot rises and flies off with the leaf and an ear of millet.","n":"The bird asks for writing because speech is not enough: the pledge must reach her in a form she can hold, and the letter is read out in 1041 to 1043.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1032","lb":"CC1032","ev":"Civakan writes a palm-leaf letter, and the parrot flies off with it and a millet ear.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கிளி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1033":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1033,"t":["திருந்து கோதைச் சிகழிகைச் சீறடி","மருந்தின் சாயன் மணங்கமழ் மேனியாள்","பொருந்து பூம்பொய்கைப் போர்வையைப் போர்த்துடன்","கருங்கட் பாவை கவின்பெற வைகினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Meanwhile the girl with well-dressed hair, small feet and fragrant body wrapped herself in the cover of the flowering pond and lay there, the dark-eyed doll, lovely in her waiting.","n":"பூம்பொய்கைப் போர்வை, 'the covering of the flower pond', may be the pond's water or lotus leaves used to cool a fevered body; which is meant is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1033","lb":"CC1033","ev":"Gunamalai waits, lying by or in the flower pond.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1034":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1034,"t":["மறங்கொள் வெங்கதிர் வேலவன் வார்கழல்","கறங்க வேகித்தன் காதலி யூடலை","யுறைந்த வொண்மலர்ச் சென்னியி னீக்கினா","னிறைந்த தின்ப நெடுங்கணிக் கென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"It is told that the spear-bearer of fierce bright spear went with his long anklets ringing and took his beloved's sulking away with his flower-crowned head, so that the long-eyed one's joy was full.","n":"The verse ends என்பவே, 'they say', and reads as a told tale rather than an event of the story: probably the god with the spear ending his consort's sulk by bowing his head to her, set beside Gunamalai's waiting as the outcome she hopes for. Neither the god nor its place in the scene is named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1034","lb":"CC1034","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1035":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1035,"t":["தன்றுணைவி கோட்டியினி னீங்கித் தனியிடம்பார்த்","தின்றுணைவற் சேர்வா னிருந்ததுகொல் போந்ததுகொல்","சென்றதுகொல் சேர்ந்ததுகொல் செவ்வி அறிந்துருகு","மென்றுணைவி மாற்றமிஃ தென்றதுகொல் பாவம்."],"p":"","r":"Gunamalai frets whether the parrot has left its mate's company and found a lonely place to reach her beloved, whether it stayed, went or arrived, and whether it has said that this is the word of the girl who melts as she waits for his moment, and she sighs, alas.","n":"The chain of questions with கொல், 'perhaps', follows the bird step by step, so the verse turns waiting into an imagined journey.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","thoothu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1035","lb":"CC1035","ev":"Gunamalai anxiously imagines the parrot's errand.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை",""],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1036":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1036,"t":["செந்தார்ப் பசுங்கிளியார் சென்றார்க்கோ ரின்னுரைதான்","றந்தாரேற் றந்தாரென் னின்னுயிர்தாந் தாராரே","லந்தோ குணமாலைக் காதகா தென்றுலக","நொந்தாங் கழமுயன்று நோற்றானு மெய்துவனே."],"p":"","r":"She says that if the man the parrot went to gives one sweet word he gives her her life, and if he does not, while the world grieves that this is unfit for Gunamalai, she will strive and win him even by penance.","n":"நோற்றானும் எய்துவனே makes தவம் a means to a husband: if this life fails, penance will bring him, presumably in a later birth. The division of காதகா தென்று (ஆ, தகாது?) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","thavam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1036","lb":"CC1036","ev":"Gunamalai resolves that if he refuses she will win him by penance.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை",""],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1037":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1037,"t":["சென்றார் வரைய கருமஞ் செருவேலான்","பொன்றாங் கணியகலம் புல்லப் பொருந்துமேற்","குன்றாது கூடுகெனக் கூறிமுத்த வார்மணன்மே","லன்றாங் கணியிழையா ளாழி யிழைத்தாளே."],"p":"","r":"Praying that if the errand of the one who went succeeds and the spear-warrior's adorned chest is to embrace her, it may come together without fail, the jewelled girl drew a circle on the long pearl-like sand.","n":"The circle must close (கூடுக) for the omen to be good; the verse shows the rite as a girl's private divination, without a diviner.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1037","lb":"CC1037","ev":"Gunamalai draws a circle in the sand to divine whether she and Civakan will unite.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1038":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1038,"t":["பாகவரை வாங்கிப் பழுதாகிற் பாவியேற்","கேகுமா லாவி யெனநினைப்பப் பைங்கிளியார்","மாகமே நோக்கி மடவாளே யவ்விருந்தா","ளாகும்யான் சேர்வ லெனச்சென் றடைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"As she thought that if the drawn line went wrong halfway her life would leave her, sinner that she was, the green parrot looked down from the sky, said that the maiden was there and it would go to her, and came down to her.","n":"The bird arrives in the very moment of the drawing, so the answer to the divination comes as a messenger, not as the shape in the sand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodal","uyir","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1038","lb":"CC1038","ev":"As Gunamalai fears the omen will fail, the parrot sees her from the sky and lands beside her.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை","கிளி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1039":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1039,"t":["கண்டா ணெடிதுயிர்த்தாள் கைதொழுதாள் கையகத்தே","கொண்டா டினைக்குரறான் சூடினாள் தாழ்குழன்மே","னுண்டார்ப் பசுங்கிளியை நோவ அகட்டொடுக்கி","வண்டாரான் செவ்விவாய்க் கேட்டாடன் மெய்ம்மகிழ்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She saw it, sighed deeply, joined her hands in worship, took the millet ear and wore it in her hair, pressed the parrot to her breast till it hurt, heard the garlanded man's message from its beak and rejoiced through her body.","n":"She worships the bird (கைதொழுதாள) and squeezes it till it hurts (நோவ): the messenger is honoured and embraced as a stand-in for the man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","thoothu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1039","lb":"CC1039","ev":"Gunamalai greets the parrot, wears the millet ear and hears Civakan's message.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1040":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1040,"t":["தீம்பா லமிர்தூட்டிச் செம்பொன் மணிக்கூட்டிற்","காம்பேர் பணைத்தோளி மென்பறவை கண்படுப்பித்","தாம்பான் மணிநாம மோதிரந்தொட் டையென்னத்","தேம்பா வெழுத்தோலை செவ்வனே நோக்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She of bamboo-like shoulders fed the soft bird sweet milk, settled it to sleep in its gold and gem cage, touched the seal ring bearing his name, and read the clearly lettered leaf with care.","n":"The ring with his name (மணிநாம மோதிரம்) seals the leaf, so the letter carries proof of its sender as well as words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1040","lb":"CC1040","ev":"Gunamalai tends the parrot and opens Civakan's sealed letter.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1041":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1041,"t":["கொடுஞ்சிலையா னோலை குணமாலை காண்க","வடுந்துயர முள்சுடவெந் தாற்றாதே னாற்ற","விடுந்த சிறுகிளியால் விம்மனோய் தீர்ந்தே","னெடுங்கணா டானு நினைவகல்வா ளாக."],"p":"","r":"The letter begins: the leaf of the man of the curved bow, for Gunamalai to see; he who could not bear the burning grief inside has had his swelling sickness cured by the little parrot sent to comfort him, and may the long-eyed girl too have her sorrowful thoughts depart.","n":"He says the bird, not the girl, cured him: love's medicine here is a message, received before any meeting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1041","lb":"CC1041","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1042":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1042,"t":["ஈட்டஞ்சா னீணிதியு மீர்ங்குவளைப் பைந்தடஞ்சூழ்","மோட்டு வளஞ்சுரக்கு மூரு முழுதீந்து (மென்றோட்","வேட்டார்க்கு வேட்டனவே போன்றினிய வேய்","பூட்டார் சிலைநுதலாட் புல்லா தொழியேனே."],"p":"","r":"The letter goes on that, even giving away all his heaped treasure and the rich towns ringed by cool lily ponds, he will not fail to embrace the girl of bow-like brow, sweet to him as their desire to those who desire.","n":"The verse repeats the wealth-weighing of 1031 in writing, now as the price he would pay rather than a gift he would refuse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sool","selvam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1042","lb":"CC1042","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1043":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1043,"t":["குங்குமஞ்சேர் வெம்முலைமேற் கொய்தார் வடுப்பொறிப்பச்","செங்கயற்கண் வெம்பனியாற் சிந்தை யெரியவித்து","மங்கை மகிழ வுறையேனேல் வாளமருட்","பங்கப்பட் டார்மேற் படைநினைந்தே னாகென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He ends the letter by swearing that if he does not live with her to her joy, his garland marking her saffroned breasts and his presence quenching the fire in her mind with her tears, then let him be one who has thought of turning his weapon on men already beaten in sword battle.","n":"The love-pledge takes the form of a warrior's self-curse: the penalty he names is the worst shame of the battlefield, even to have thought (நினைந்தேன்) of attacking the routed, so the oath measures his word by the code of war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","sool","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1043","lb":"CC1043","ev":"","cl":[["to strike at those already defeated in battle is a disgrace fit to be sworn by","general","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1044":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1044,"t":["நூல்புடைத்தாற் போற்கிடந்த வித்தகஞ்சேர் நுண்வரிகள்","பான்மடுத்துத் தீந்தேன் பருகுவாள் போனோக்கிச்","சேல்படுத்த கண்ணீர் சுமந்தளைஇ மெய்ம்மகிழ்ந்து","மால்படுத்தான் மார்பின் மணந்தாளே போன்மகிழ்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She looked at the fine lines of the letters, laid like drawn thread, as if drinking sweet honey poured into milk, her fish-like eyes brimming with tears, and rejoiced through her body as though she had already embraced the chest of the man who had made her love-mad.","n":"Reading is described as drinking and as embracing, so the written pledge gives her by anticipation the union 1043 promised.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","punarchi","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1044","lb":"CC1044","ev":"Gunamalai reads the letter, weeps and rejoices.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1045":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1045,"t":["பாலவியும் பூவும் புகையும் படுசாந்துங்","காலவியாப் பொன்விளக்குந் தந்தும்மைக் கைதொழுவேன்","கோலவியா வெஞ்சிலையான் சொற்குன்றா னாகெனவே","நூலவையார் போனீங்க ணோக்குமினே யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She vows to offer milk-rice, flowers, incense, sandal paste and gold lamps that no wind puts out, and to worship with joined hands, and asks those she addresses to watch, like the men of the learned assembly, that the man of the fierce bow does not fail his word.","n":"Those addressed (நீங்கள்) are unnamed; the offerings point to household or temple deities. நூலவையார், the assembly versed in the books, makes them witnesses and judges of his oath, as an assembly hears a case.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vaaimai","sool"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1045","lb":"CC1045","ev":"Gunamalai promises offerings to the deities if they keep Civakan true to his word.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை",""],"ch":"cc:45"},"cc:1046":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1046,"t":["மவ்வலங் குழலி னாளை மதியுடம் படுக்க லுற்றுச்","செவ்வியுட் செவிலி சொல்லுஞ் சிலையிவர் நுதலி னாய்நின்","னவ்வைக்கு மூத்த மாம னொருமகற் கின்றுன் றாதை","நவ்வியம் பிணைகொ ணோக்கி நகைமுக விருந்து செய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Setting out to draw Gunamalai's mind into the open, her nurse tells her at a quiet moment that her father has today feasted the only son of her mother's elder brother.","n":"மதியுடம்படுக்கலுற்று names the nurse's purpose: she is sounding the girl, so the news of a match that follows may be a test rather than a settled plan; the cousin is never mentioned again once Gunamalai answers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1046","lb":"CC1046","ev":"Gunamalai's nurse tells her that her father has entertained her maternal cousin.","cl":[],"ag":["செவிலி"],"ec":[],"spk":["செவிலி","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1047":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1047,"t":["பண்டியாற் பண்டி செம்பொன் பல்வளை பரிய மாகக்","கொண்டுவந் தடிமை செய்வான் குறையுறு கின்ற தன்றிக்","கண்டவர் கடக்க லாற்றாக் கிழிமிசை யுருவ தீட்டி","வண்டிமிர் கோதை நின்னை வழிபடு நாளு மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The nurse goes on: the cousin begs to serve as her bondman, bringing cartloads of gold and bangles as bride-price, and daily worships a likeness of her painted on cloth that no one who sees it can pass by.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","selvam","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1047","lb":"CC1047","ev":"The nurse describes the cousin's bride-price and his worship of Gunamalai's painted portrait.","cl":[],"ag":["செவிலி"],"ec":[],"spk":["செவிலி","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1048":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1048,"t":["மைத்துனன் வனப்பின் மிக்கான் வளர்நிதிக் கிழவன் காளை","யுத்தம னுனது நாம மல்லதொன் றுரைத்த றேற்றா","னித்திறத் திவன்க ணின்னை யெண்ணினா ரென்ன லோடுந்","தத்தையங் கிளவி கையாற் செவிமுத லடைச்சிச் சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"The nurse praises the cousin as handsome, heir to growing wealth and speaking no name but hers, says the elders have fixed on him for her, and at this the parrot-voiced girl covers her ears with her hands and speaks.","n":"Gunamalai's first answer is a gesture: she closes her ears before she says a word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","selvam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1048","lb":"CC1048","ev":"Told that her elders intend her for the cousin, Gunamalai covers her ears.","cl":[],"ag":["செவிலி","குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["செவிலி","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1049":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1049,"t":["மணிமதக் களிறு வென்றான் வருத்தச்சொற் கூலி யாக","வணிமதக் களிற னானுக் கடிப்பணி செய்வ தல்லாற்","றுணிவதென் சுடுசொல் வாளாற் செவிமுத லீர லென்றாள்","பணிவரும் பவளப் பாவை பரிவுகொண் டனைய தொப்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"Gunamalai says that, as wages for the toil of the man who conquered the rutting elephant, she can resolve on nothing but service at his feet, and asks the nurse not to cut her ears with the sword of burning words.","n":"She frames her love as a debt (வருத்தச்சொற் கூலி, the wage of his pains), not as desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","nanri","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1049","lb":"CC1049","ev":"Gunamalai refuses the cousin and declares she will serve only Civakan.","cl":[["a woman owes lifelong service to the man who risked himself to save her, as the wage of his labour","general","reported",1,"gunamalai"]],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை","செவிலி"],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1050":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1050,"t":["கந்துகப் புடையிற் பொங்குங் கலினமா வல்லன் காளைக்","கெந்தையும் யாயு நேரா ராய்விடி னிறத்த லொன்றோ","சிந்தனை பிறிதொன் றாகிச் செய்தவ முயற லொன்றோ","வந்ததா னாளை யென்றாள் வடுவெனக் கிடந்த கண்ணாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that if her father and mother will not give her to the young horseman, tomorrow brings one of two things: death, or a mind turned elsewhere to the labour of penance.","n":"The two alternatives are the epic's own exits for women, death and renunciation; she names them before any marriage, so the steadfastness usually asked of a wife is here a maiden's fixity on a man she has chosen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","saakkaadu","thavam","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1050","lb":"CC1050","ev":"Gunamalai declares that if her parents refuse Civakan she will die or take up penance.","cl":[["a maiden denied the man she has chosen should die or renounce rather than marry another","general","reported",2,"gunamalai"]],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை","செவிலி"],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1051":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1051,"t":["தேனெய்போன் றினிய சொல்லாள் சிறுமுதுக் குறைமை கேட்டே","யூனைநைந் துருகிக் கைத்தா யுண்ணிறை யுவகை பொங்க","வானெய்பாற் கிவர்ந்த தொத்த தழேற்கவென் பாவை யென்று","தானையாற் றடங்க ணீரைத் துடைத்துமெய் தழுவிக் கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the small girl's precocious wisdom, the nurse melts with joy, says it is like ghee climbing into milk, tells her not to weep, wipes her eyes with her garment and embraces her.","n":"The nurse's joy confirms that the cousin story was a sounding: she wanted this refusal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1051","lb":"CC1051","ev":"The nurse rejoices at Gunamalai's answer, comforts her and embraces her.","cl":[],"ag":["செவிலி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1052":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1052,"t":["துகண்மனத் தின்றி நோற்ற தொல்வினைப் பயத்தி னன்றே","தகணிலாக் கேள்வி யான்கட் டங்கிய தென்று பின்னு","மகண்மனங் குளிர்ப்பக் கூறி மறுவலும் புல்லிக் கொண்டாங்","ககன்மனைத் தாய்க்குச் சொன்னா ளவளுந்தன் கேட்குச் சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Saying that this man of flawless learning has come to her by the fruit of old deeds of penance done with a mind free of fault, the nurse embraces her again, then tells the girl's mother, who tells her husband.","n":"Two things are joined: the nurse reads the match as karma ripening from former penance, and the news moves up the household in the order of akam disclosure, nurse to mother to father.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arathodunirral","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1052","lb":"CC1052","ev":"The nurse reports Gunamalai's choice to her mother, who reports it to her husband.","cl":[["a worthy husband comes as the fruit of penance done in former births with a faultless mind","general","reported",1,"gunamalai_sevili"]],"ag":["செவிலி","வினையமாமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1053":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1053,"t":["வினையமா மாலை கேள்வன் குபேரமித் திரற்குச் சொல்ல","வனையதே பட்ட தென்றா லையனே நங்கைக் கொத்தான்","வனையவே பட்ட போலு மணிமருண் முலையி னாளைப்","புனையவே பட்ட பொற்றார்ப் புண்ணியற் கீது மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When Vinayamalai tells her husband Kuberamittiran, he says that if it has gone so, the lord is fit for the girl, the gold-garlanded man of merit suits her as though she were made for him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1053","lb":"CC1053","ev":"Kuberamittiran hears his wife's report and approves Civakan as the girl's match.","cl":[],"ag":["வினையமாமாலை","குபேரமித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குபேரமித்திரன்","வினையமாமாலை"],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1054":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1054,"t":["கற்றார் மற்றுங் கட்டுரை வல்லார் கவியென்னு","நற்றேர் மேலார் நால்வரை விட்டாற் கவர்சென்றார்","சுற்றார் வல்விற் சூடுறு செம்பொற் கழனாய்கன்","பொற்றார் மார்பீர் போதுமி னென்றாங் கெதிர்கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"He sends four learned men skilled in speech and verse on a fine chariot; they go, and the merchant with the heated-gold anklet bids them come and receives them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1054","lb":"CC1054","ev":"Kuberamittiran sends four learned envoys to Kantukkatan, who welcomes them.","cl":[],"ag":["குபேரமித்திரன்","நால்வர்","கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1055":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1055,"t":["சீந்தா நின்ற தீமுக வேலான் மணிச்செப்பி","னீந்தான் கொண்டா ரின்முக வாச மெரிசெம்பொன்","காந்தா நின்ற கற்பக மன்னீர் வரப்பெற்றேன்","சேர்ந்தே னின்றே வீடென நாய்கற் கவர்சொன்னார்."],"p":"","r":"Kantukkatan gives them betel from a gem casket and they take it; then words are spoken to the merchant: 'You who are like the glowing kalpaka tree, I have been granted to come; today I have reached heaven.'","n":"The speech is in the first person singular (வரப்பெற்றேன், சேர்ந்தேன்), which fits Kantukkatan's welcome, yet the closing words say it was said to the merchant, as in 1056; read here as the envoys' courtesy to their host, but the speaker is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1055","lb":"CC1055","ev":"Kantukkatan offers the envoys betel, and they greet him with praise.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","நால்வர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நால்வர்","கந்துக்கடன்"],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1056":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1056,"t":["யாமக ளீது நீர்மகட் கொண்மி னெனயாருந்","தாமக ணேரா ராயினுந் தண்ணென் வரைமார்பிற்","பூமகள் வைகும் புண்ணியப் பொற்குன் றனையானுக்","கியாமக ணேர்ந்தே மின்றென நாய்கற் கவர்சொன்னார்."],"p":"","r":"The envoys tell the merchant that though no one offers a daughter with 'we give our girl, take her for your son', they have agreed today to give theirs to the one like a golden hill, on whose chest the lotus goddess dwells.","n":"The bride's side breaking custom to offer the girl is itself the compliment: Civakan's worth justifies it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1056","lb":"CC1056","ev":"The envoys formally offer Gunamalai in marriage to Civakan.","cl":[["a bride's family does not ordinarily offer its daughter unasked","general","reported",2,"kubera_envoys"]],"ag":["நால்வர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நால்வர்","கந்துக்கடன்"],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1057":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1057,"t":["சுற்றார் வல்விற் சூடுறு செம்பொற் கழலாற்குக்","குற்றேல் செய்துங் காளையும் யானுங் கொடியாளை","மற்சேர் தோளான் றன்மரு மானுக் கருள்செய்யப்","பெற்றே னென்னப் பேசினன் வாசங் கமழ்தாரான்."],"p":"","r":"The fragrant-garlanded merchant answers that he and the youth will do humble service to the strong-bowed man, and that he has been granted the favour of the creeper-like girl for the strong-shouldered one's son-in-law.","n":"The phrase சுற்றார் வல்விற் சூடுறு செம்பொற் கழல் describes Kantukkatan in 1054 but here seems to name the bride's father; who is honoured by whom turns on that epithet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1057","lb":"CC1057","ev":"Kantukkatan accepts the offer on Civakan's behalf.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","நால்வர்"],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1058":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1058,"t":["விடைசூ ழேற்றின் வேல்புக ழான்றன் மிகுதாதை","கடல்சூழ் வையங் கைப்படுத் தான் போன் றிதுகூறக்","குடர்சூழ் கோட்ட குஞ்சரம் வென்ற வகையும்மப்","படர்சூழ் நெஞ்சிற் பாவைதன் பண்பு மவர்சொன்னார்."],"p":"","r":"As Civakan's father says this like one who has seized the sea-girt world, the envoys tell how Civakan conquered the elephant and what the girl's nature is.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1058","lb":"CC1058","ev":"Kantukkatan rejoices and the envoys recount the elephant fight and Gunamalai's virtues.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","நால்வர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1059":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1059,"t":["மறையார் வேள்வி மந்திரச் செந்தீக் கொடியேபோற்","குறையாக் கற்பிற் சீவகன் றாயுங் கொலைவேற்கட்","பொறையொன் றாற்றாப் போதணி பொன் கொம்பு அனையாளை","நறையார் கோதை நன்றென வின்புற் றெதிர்கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan's mother, of unfailing chastity like the flame of the Vedic sacrificial fire, gladly welcomes the girl as good.","n":"The Jain epic compares a wife's chastity to the red flame of the mantra-fed Vedic sacrifice, an image of purity it borrows without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1059","lb":"CC1059","ev":"Sunandai, Civakan's foster mother, approves the match.","cl":[],"ag":["சுநந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1060":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1060,"t":["பொற்கச் சார்த்த பூணணி பொம்மன் முலையாளை","யற்கச் செய்த யாப்பின ராகி யவண்வந்தார்","பொற்பக் கூறிப் போகுது மென்றார்க் கெழுகென்றார்","வற்கம் மிட்ட வண்பரி மாவி னவர்சென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Having made the agreement firm, the envoys say they will go; told to leave, they depart on their bridled horses.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1060","lb":"CC1060","ev":"The envoys take their leave and ride home.","cl":[],"ag":["நால்வர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1061":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1061,"t":["மடந்தை திறத்தி னியையம்மகட் கூறி வந்தார்","விடந்தைத்த வேலாற் குரைத்தார்க்கவன் மெய்ம்மகிழ்ந்தா","னுடங்குங் கொடிப்போல் பவணூபுர மார்ப்ப வந்து","தடங்கண் ணவடா யதுகேட்டலுந் தக்க தென்றாள்"],"p":"","r":"They come back and tell the spear-bearing father what they have settled for the girl; he rejoices, and her mother, coming with anklets ringing, hears it and says it is fitting.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1061","lb":"CC1061","ev":"The envoys report to Kuberamittiran and his wife, who both approve.","cl":[],"ag":["நால்வர்","குபேரமித்திரன்","வினையமாமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1062":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1062,"t":["திருவிற் கமைந்தான் றிசைபத்து மறிந்த தொல்சீ","ருருவிற் கமைந்தாற் கமைந்தாளென யாரு மொட்டப்","பெருகுங் கணியிற் கணிபேசிய பேதி னாளாற்","பருகற் கமைந்த வமிர்தின்படர் தீர்க்க லுற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"All agree that she is fit for the man fit for fortune, the astrologers fix the day, and they set out to end the pining of the girl who is nectar to drink.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1062","lb":"CC1062","ev":"The astrologers fix the wedding day.","cl":[],"ag":["கணி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1063":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1063,"t":["கரைகொன் றிரங்குங் கடலிற்கலி கொண்டு கல்லென்","முரசங் கறங்க முழவிம்மவெண் சங்க மார்ப்பப்","பிரசங் கலங்கிற் றெனமாந்தர் பிணங்க வேட்டான்","விரைசென் றடைந்த குழலாளையவ் வேனி லானே."],"p":"","r":"With the shout of a sea that breaks its shore, drums, mulavu and white conches, and crowds jostling, the Kama-like hero weds the girl of fragrant hair.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1063","lb":"CC1063","ev":"Civakan marries Gunamalai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1064":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1064,"t":["மழைமொக்கு ளன்ன வருமென்முலை மாதர் நல்லா","ரிழைமுற் றணிந்தா ரெழுநூற்றுவர் கோடி செம்பொன்","கழைமுற்று தீந்தேன் கரும்பார்வய லைந்து மூதூர்","குழைமுற்று காதின் மணிக்கொம்பொடு நாய்க னீந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"With the girl, the merchant gives seven hundred adorned maidens, a crore of red gold and five old villages of sugarcane fields.","n":"நாய்கன் named Kantukkatan in 1054, but a bride's father gives the dowry; Kuberamittiran, a merchant chief, is the likelier giver.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1064","lb":"CC1064","ev":"Gunamalai's father gives a dowry of maidens, gold and villages.","cl":[],"ag":["குபேரமித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1065":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1065,"t":["பண்ணார் கிளவிப் பவழம் புரைசெவ்வாய்க்","கண்ணார் கதிர்மென் முலைக்காம் படுமென்றோள்","விண்ணோ ருலகின் னொடுமிந் நிலத்தில்லாப்","பெண்ணா ரமிர்தே யவன்பெற் றவமிர்தே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator praises her as a womanly nectar found neither in heaven nor on this earth, the nectar he has won.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1065","lb":"CC1065","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1066":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1066,"t":["தேதா வெனவண் டொடுதேன் வரிசெய்யப்","போதார் குழலாள் புணர்மென் முலைபாயத்","தாதார் கமழ்தார் மதுவிண் டுதுளிப்ப","வீதா மவரெய் தியவின் பமதே."],"p":"","r":"While bees hum on the honey, her breasts press against him and his garland drips nectar: such is the pleasure they come to.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1066","lb":"CC1066","ev":"Civakan and Gunamalai enjoy their first union.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1067":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1067,"t":["முந்நீர்ப் பவளத் துறைநித் திலமுத்த","மந்நீ ரமிர்தீன் றுகொடுப் பவமர்ந்தான்","மைந்நீர் நெடுங்கண் புருவங் கண்மலங்கப்","பொன்னா ரரிக்கிண் கிணிபூ சலிடவே."],"p":"","r":"He delights in her as if the sea's coral and pearl gave him nectar, while her kohl eyes and brows waver and her golden anklet-bells cry out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1067","lb":"CC1067","ev":"The lovers' union continues.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1068":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1068,"t":["கம்பார் களியா னைகலக் கமலங்கி","யம்பே ரரிவா ணெடுங்கண் புதைத்தஞ்சிக்","கொம்பே குழைவா யெனக்கே குழைந்திட்டாய்","வம்பே யிதுவை யகத்தார் வழக்கன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan teases her: when the elephant threw all into turmoil she hid her long eyes in fear, and now the creeper that melts has melted for him, a strange thing and not the world's way.","n":"The last line (வம்பே இது வையகத்தார் வழக்கு அன்றே) can be read as 'this is new, not the world's custom' or as a rhetorical 'is this not the world's custom?'; the tone of the teasing turns on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1068","lb":"CC1068","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1069":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1069,"t":["பூவார் புனலாட் டினுட்பூ நறுஞ்சுண்ணம்","பாவாய் பணைத்தோட் சுரமஞ் சரிதோற்றாள்","காவா தவள்கண் ணறச்சொல் லியவெஞ்சொ","லேவோ வமிர்தோ வெனக்கின் றிதுசொல்லாய்."],"p":"","r":"He asks her to tell him today whether the harsh word spoken unguardedly about Suramanjari, who lost the powder contest at the water festival, is an arrow or nectar to him.","n":"The harsh word is most likely his own verdict on the powders, which won him Gunamalai but drove Suramanjari to vow that he would come and worship her feet (899); whether அவள்கண் means 'about her' or 'to her' is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1069","lb":"CC1069","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:46"},"cc:1070":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1070,"t":["நற்றோ ளவள்சுண் ணநலஞ் சொலுவா","னுற்றீர் மறந்தீர் மனத்துள் ளுறைகின்றாள்","செற்றா லரிதாற் சென்மின்போ மின்றீண்டா","தெற்றே யறியாத வொரேழை யேனோயான்."],"p":"","r":"Gunamalai, in a lovers' sulk, tells Civakan that he came to praise the beauty of another woman's powder, that he has forgotten her and that the other dwells in his heart, and bids him go, asking whether she is a simple woman who cannot see it plainly.","n":"The verse never names the woman of fine shoulders (நற்றோள் அவள்) or whose powder (சுண்ணம்) is meant; the powder recalls the scented-powder contest between Gunamalai and Suramanjari that began this ilampakam, and the charge may be a feigned one turned on his own praise. Who is meant cannot be fixed from this verse alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1070","lb":"CC1070","ev":"Gunamalai sulks and sends Civakan away, accusing him of thinking of another woman.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1071":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1071,"t":["தூமஞ் கமழ்பூந் துகில்சேரா வசையாத்","தாமம் பரிந்தாடு தண்சாந்தந் திமிர்ந்திட்","டேமன் சிலைவா ணுத லேற நெருக்காக்","காமன் கணையேர் கண்சிவந்து புலந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She leaves off her incense-scented silk, tears at her garland, rubs away the cool sandal, draws her bow-like brow tight, and with eyes like Kama's arrows reddening she sulks.","n":"Every act undoes an adornment: the sulk is shown as a woman unmaking the toilette made for love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1071","lb":"CC1071","ev":"Gunamalai strips off her ornaments and sulks with reddened eyes.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1072":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1072,"t":["மின்னே ரிடையா ளடிவீழ்ந் துமிரந்துஞ்","சொன்னீ ரவளற் பழலுட் சொரிந்தாற்ற","விந்நீ ரனகண் புடைவிட் டகன்றின்ப","மன்னார்ந் துமதர்ப் பொடுநோக் கினண்மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Civakan falls at the feet of the lightning-waisted woman and pleads, his words pour like water on the fire of her anger, the tears leave her eyes, and she looks at him with joy and heady pride.","n":"Line 2 is compressed (சொன்னீர் அவள் அழலுட் சொரிந்து ஆற்ற): his words are the water, her anger the fire, the image that the king's fire-like anger will answer a few verses later.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","inbam","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1072","lb":"CC1072","ev":"Civakan falls at Gunamalai's feet and pleads until her sulk gives way to joy.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1073":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1073,"t":["இன்னீ ரெரிமா மணிப்பூண் கிடந்தீன்ற","மின்னா ரிளமென் முலைவேய் மருண்மென்றோட்","பொன்னார் கொடியே புகழிற் புகழ்ஞால","நின்வா ணெடுங்கண் விலையா குநிகர்த்தே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan praises her as a golden creeper with soft shoulders like bamboo and young breasts under a gem ornament, saying that if the world were praised to its full worth it would only equal the price of her long bright eyes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1073","lb":"CC1073","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1074":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1074,"t":["தோளாற் றழுவித் துவர்த்தொண் டையஞ்செவ்வாய்","மீளா மணிமே கலைமின் னின்மிளிர","வாளார் மணிப்பூ ணவன்மாதரம் பாவைதன்னை","நாளாற் பெற்றநல் லமிர்தென்ன நயந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He clasps her in his arms, her coral lips and jewelled girdle gleaming, and desires the doll-like woman as nectar won on a lucky day.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1074","lb":"CC1074","ev":"Civakan embraces Gunamalai and they are reconciled in love.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1075":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1075,"t":["சித்திர மணிக்குழை திளைக்கும் வாண்முகத்","தொத்தொளிர் பவளவா யோவக் கைவினைத்","தத்தரி நெடுங்கணா டன்னொ டாடுநாள்","வித்தகற் குற்றது விளம்பு கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator turns from the bright-faced, coral-mouthed woman of long painted eyes and her days of play with him to tell what befell the skilled one.","n":"A hinge verse: the play is cut off by வித்தகற்கு உற்றது, what came upon him, announcing trouble without naming it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1075","lb":"CC1075","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1076":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1076,"t":["அரும்பெறற் குருசிற் கஞ்ஞான் றோடிய நாக நாணிக்","கரும்பெறி கடிகை யோடு நெய்ம்மலி கவளங் கொள்ளா","திரும்புசெய் குழவித் திங்கண் மருப்பிடைத் தடக்கை நாற்றிச்","சுரும்பொடு வண்டு பாடச் சுளிவொடு நின்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Ashamed since it fled from the prince that day, the elephant will not take its sugarcane and ghee-soaked balls of food, but stands sullen with its curved trunk hung between its moon-like tusks while bees hum about it.","n":"The beast is given the lovers' own state: it stands with சுளிவு, sullen like a sulking woman, so the elephant's shame mirrors Gunamalai's ஊடல் in the verses just before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1076","lb":"CC1076","ev":"The king's elephant, shamed by its defeat by Civakan, refuses its food.","cl":[],"ag":["நாகம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1077":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1077,"t":["பகைபுறங் கொடுத்த வேந்திற் பரிவொடு பகடு நிற்பத்","தகைநிறக் குழைக டாழ்ந்து சாந்தின்வாய் நக்கி மின்னப்","புகைநிறத் துகிலிற் பொன்னாண் டுயல்வரப் போந்து வேந்தன்","மிகைநிறக் களிற்றை நோக்கி வேழமென் னுற்ற தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a king whose enemy has turned his back, the king comes out in his earrings and sandal, his gold thread swaying on smoke-coloured silk, looks at the elephant standing in distress and asks what has come over it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1077","lb":"CC1077","ev":"The king sees his elephant off its food and asks what ails it.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1078":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1078,"t":["கொற்றவன் குறிப்பு நோக்கிக் குஞ்சரப் பாகன் கூறு","மிற்றென வுரைத்த றேற்றே னிறைவநின் னருளி னாங்கொல்","செற்றமிக் குடைமை யாற்கொல் சீவக னின்ன நாளான்","மற்றிதற் குடற்சி செய்ய மதுமிது செறித்த தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Reading the king's mind, the mahout says he cannot tell for certain whether it is by the lord's grace or from great enmity, but that some days ago Civakan, to anger it, checked its must and this has made it so.","n":"Lines 2 to 4 are hard: the mahout's hedge (நின் அருளினாம் கொல், செற்றம் மிக்குடைமையாற் கொல்) may be his own doubt or a sly hint that Civakan acted from enmity to the king; the mahout reads the king's குறிப்பு and words his report to feed it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1078","lb":"CC1078","ev":"The mahout tells the king that Civakan's checking of the elephant has broken its spirit.","cl":[],"ag":["குஞ்சரப் பாகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குஞ்சரப் பாகன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1079":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1079,"t":["ஈண்டழற் குட்டம் போல வெரியெழத் திருகி நோககிக்","கோண்டரு குறும்பர் வெம்போர் கோக்குழர்ம் வென்ற துள்ளி","மாண்டதில் செய்கை சூழ்ந்த வாணிகன் மகனை வல்லே","யாண்டிறங் களைவெ னோடிப் பற்றுபு தம்மி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Twisting like a blazing fire-pit and glaring, the king, recalling that Civakan had beaten the troop of hill-chiefs in fierce battle, orders his men to seize at once and bring the merchant's son who planned a deed beneath his station, saying he will root out his strength.","n":"The elephant is only the occasion: the king remembers the earlier victory over the hill-raiders, so wounded pride over Civakan's rising fame drives the order. Calling him வாணிகன் மகன், the merchant's son, the usurper does not know whose son he has ordered taken. Lines 1 and 2 carry slips (நோககிக், கோக்குழர்ம்) and line 3 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kodungol","oruththal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1079","lb":"CC1079","ev":"The king orders Civakan seized.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","இளையர்"],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1080":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1080,"t":["கன்றிய வெகுளி வேந்தன் கால்வலி யிளையர் காய்ந்து","கொன்றுயிர் கொணர வோடுங் கொழுங்குடர்க் கண்ணி மாலை","யொன்றிய வுதிரச் செச்சை யொண்ணிண மீக்கொ டானைத்","தென்றிசைக் கிறைவன் றூதிற் செம்மலைச் சென்று சேர்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"At the angry king's command the swift-footed young men run to kill and bring his life, wearing garlands of guts, smeared with blood and topped with fat like messengers of the lord of the south, Death, and reach the noble one.","n":"கொன்று உயிர் கொணர, 'kill and bring his life', is the king's order as the runners understand it; it exceeds the 'seize and bring' (பற்றுபு தம்மின்) of 1079, and the gore they wear makes them Death's envoys before any blow is struck.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kootru","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1080","lb":"CC1080","ev":"The king's men run to take Civakan and reach him.","cl":[],"ag":["இளையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1081":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1081,"t":["சண்பக மாலை வேய்ந்து சந்தனம் பளிதந் தீற்றி","விண்புக நாறு சாந்தின் விழுமுலைக் காம வல்லி","கொண்டெழுந் துருவு காட்டி முகத்திடைக் குளித்துத் தோண்மேல்","வண்டளி ரீன்று சுட்டி வாணுதல் பூப்ப வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Meanwhile he dresses her in a champak garland and sandal and camphor, draws with fragrant paste a love-creeper on her breasts that seems to climb to her face and put out tender leaves on her shoulders, and sets an ornament on her bright brow.","n":"The order of the verses sets the lovers' toilette directly against the runners' garlands of guts in 1080: two kinds of adornment, one of love and one of death, converge on the same house.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1081","lb":"CC1081","ev":"Civakan paints and adorns Gunamalai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1082":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1082,"t":["பண்ணடி வீழுந் தீஞ்சொற் பாவைநின் வனப்பிற் கெல்லாங்","கண்ணடி கருங்க ணென்னு மம்பறாத் தூணி தன்னாற்","புண்ணுடை மாம்பத் தோவா தெய்தியா லெங்குப் பெற்றாய்","பெண்ணுடைப் பேதை யென்றோர் நாண்முற்றும் பிதற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan babbles the whole day to her: doll whose sweet speech the very melody bows to, where did you get the never-empty quiver of your dark eyes, from which you shoot without pause at me already wounded, foolish girl?","n":"பிதற்றினான், 'he babbled', is the narrator's word: the lover's talk is shown as delight that has lost its measure just as danger arrives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","anangu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1082","lb":"CC1082","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1083":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1083,"t":["திங்கள்சேர் முடியி னானுஞ் செல்வியும் போன்று செம்பொ","னிங்குவார் கழலி னானுங் கோதையு மிருந்த போழ்திற்","சிங்கவே றெள்ளிச் சூழ்ந்த சிறுநரிக் குழாத்திற் சூழ்ந்தா","ரங்கது கண்ட தாதி யையனுக் கின்ன தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"While he of the golden anklet and his garlanded wife sat like the moon-crowned god and his consort, the king's men closed round like a pack of little jackals round a lion they despise, and the serving woman who saw it told the master.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1083","lb":"CC1083","ev":"The king's men surround the house and the maid warns Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை","இளையர்","தாதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1084":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1084,"t":["என்றவ ளுரைப்பக் கேட்டே யிடிபட முழங்கிச் செந்தீ","நின்றெரி வதனை யொத்து நிண்முழைச் சிங்க வேறு","தன்றுணைப் பெட்டை யோடு தான்புறப் பட்ட தொத்தான்","குன்றிரண் டிருந்த போலுங் குங்குமக் குவவுத் தோளான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing her, the hero of shoulders like twin saffron-smeared hills rose roaring like thunder and blazing like fire, like a maned lion coming out of its cave with its mate.","n":"The lion of 1083, scorned by jackals, now comes out with its mate: the simile keeps Gunamalai at his side even as he turns to fight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1084","lb":"CC1084","ev":"Civakan rises in fury at the news.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1085":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1085,"t":["பொன்னரி மாலை தாழப் போதணி கூந்த லேந்திப்","பன்னரு மாலை யாற்குப் பட்டதை யெவன்கொ லென்னாப்","பின்னரு மாலை யோராள் பெருநடுக் குற்று நின்றாண்","மன்னரு மாலை நாக மழையிடிப் புண்ட தொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Holding up her flower-decked hair, gold-strand garland hanging, she cried 'what has happened to my lord of the garland?', and, unable to grasp more, stood shaking hard, like a great serpent struck by thunder in the rain.","n":"The four lines rhyme on மாலை in four senses (garland, the garlanded one, confusion, a garland-like serpent); the wordplay is the poet's, the terror hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1085","lb":"CC1085","ev":"Gunamalai stands trembling in terror for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1086":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1086,"t":["கடுகிய விளையர் நோக்குங் கண்ணிய பொருளு மெண்ணி","யடுசிலை யழல வேந்தி யாருயிர் பருகற் கொத்த","விடுகணை தெரிந்து தானை வீக்கற விசித்து வெய்தாத்","தொடுகழ னரல வீக்கிச் சொல்லுமின் வந்த தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Weighing the look of the hurrying men and the purpose they had in mind, he took up his killing bow so that it seemed to blaze, chose arrows fit to drink life, girded his garment tight, bound on his anklet and said 'tell me why you have come'.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1086","lb":"CC1086","ev":"Civakan arms himself and demands the men's errand.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இளையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1087":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1087,"t":["அடிநிழற் றருக வென்றெம் மாணைவேந் தருளிச் செய்தான்","வடிமலர்த் தாரி னாய்நீ வருகென வானி னுச்சி","யிடியுரு மேற்றிற் சீறி யிருநிலஞ் சுடுதற் கொத்த","கடிமதின் மூன்று மெய்த கடவுளிற் கனன்று சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The men say that their king of command has graciously ordered that he come to the shade of his feet, and bid him of the fine-flower garland come; at this he flared up like thunder from the height of the sky and, burning like the god who shot the three fortified cities that would scorch the earth, spoke.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are the men's words inside the narration; அடிநிழல் தருக, come to the shade of the feet, is the courtesy of a summons to a subject, which the hero hears as a demand for submission.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1087","lb":"CC1087","ev":"The men deliver the summons and Civakan blazes in anger.","cl":[],"ag":["இளையர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1088":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1088,"t":["வாளிழுக் குற்ற கண்ணாள் வருமுலை நயந்து வேந்தன்","கோளிழுக் குற்ற பின்றைக் வருகென வானி னுச்சி","னாளிழுக் குற்று வீழ்வ தின்றுகொ னந்த திண்டேர்","தோளிழுக் குற்ற மொய்ம்ப பண்ணெனச் சொல்லி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says that after the king, desiring the breasts of a woman whose eyes outdo the sword, lost his hold, today may be the day this man's life slips and he falls, and bids a strong-armed companion harness the sturdy chariot.","n":"Line 2 repeats words of 1087's line 2 (வருகென வானி னுச்சி) and is corrupt, so the sentence cannot be fixed. If the king of line 1 is Saccandan, who gave up rule for love of Vijayai, Civakan is naming his father's fall and the usurper's coming one; நந்த in line 3 may be a call to his brother Nandattan or a verb, 'to perish'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","pagai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1088","lb":"CC1088","ev":"Civakan calls for his chariot to go against the king.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1089":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1089,"t":["வேந்தொடு மாறு கோடல் விளிகுற்றார் தொழில தாகுங்","காய்ந்திடு வெகுளி நீக்கிக் கைகட்டி யிவனை யுய்த்தா","லாய்ந்தடு மழற்சி நீங்கு மதுபொரு ளென்று நல்ல","சாந்துடை மார்பன் றாதை தன்மனத் திழைக்கின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The father of the sandal-chested hero reasons within himself that to contend with the king is the work of those bound to die, and that if they put off their burning anger, tie his hands and send him, the king's killing rage will cease; that is the wise course.","n":"Kantukkatan's prudence assumes the king's anger (அழற்சி) can be appeased by surrender; it echoes in reverse the teacher's demand in 393 that Civakan bear no அழற்சி against the usurper for a year. The merchant counsels submission to the man who killed Civakan's real father.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vendhan","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1089","lb":"CC1089","ev":"Kantukkatan resolves to send Civakan bound to the king.","cl":[["to set oneself against the king is the act of men who have come to their death","general","reported",1,"kantukkatan"]],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1090":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1090,"t":["ஊன்பிறங் கொளிரும் வேலா னோர்த்துத்த னுவாத்தி சொல்லாற்","றான்புறங் கட்டப் பட்டுத் தன்சினந் தணிந்து நிற்பத்","தேன்பிறங் கலங்கன் மாலைச் சுநந்தையுந் துணைவன் றானுங்","கோன்புறங் காப்பச் சேறல் குணமெனக் கூறி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The hero of the bright spear, recalling his teacher's word, let his arms be bound behind him and stood with his anger quenched, while Sunandai and her husband said that going under the king's protection was the right course.","n":"Two motives are given and kept apart: the parents' worldly counsel in line 4 and, decisively, the teacher's word (உவாத்தி சொல்) in line 1, the year's restraint promised in 393. Sunandai and Kantukkatan voice the claim together; voiced_by names him for both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","porai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1090","lb":"CC1090","ev":"Remembering his teacher's word, Civakan lets himself be bound.","cl":[["to go under the king's protection is the virtuous course","general","reported",4,"kantukkatan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுநந்தை","கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1091":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1091,"t":["ஈன்றதாய் தந்தை வேண்ட விவ்விட ருற்ற தென்றாற்","றோன்லுக் காண்மை குன்றா தென்றசொல் லிமிழிற் பூட்டி","மூன்றனைத் துலக மெல்லா முட்டினு முருக்கு மாற்றல்","வான்றரு மாரி வண்கை மதவலி பிணிக்கப் பட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Because the mother who bore him and his father begged, saying this trouble had come, and bound him with the halter of the word that a noble one's manhood is not lessened by it, the mighty one, whose strength could crush the three worlds together and whose giving hand is like rain, was bound.","n":"The verse calls Sunandai ஈன்றதாய், the mother who bore him, though she is his foster mother; it speaks as the household and the city know him. The real bond is the word (சொல் இமிழ்), not the cord. Line 2 (றோன்லுக் காண்மை) is corrupt, likely for தோன்றலுக்கு ஆண்மை.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","sutram","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1091","lb":"CC1091","ev":"At his foster parents' plea Civakan submits to being bound.","cl":[["a noble man's manhood is not diminished by submitting at his parents' plea","general","reported",2,"sunandai"]],"ag":["சுநந்தை","கந்துக்கடன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1092":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1092,"t":["குழலுடைச் சிகழிகைக் குமரன் றோளிணை","கழலுடை யிளையவர் கச்சின் வீக்கலி","னழலுடைக் கடவுளை யரவு சேர்ந்தென","விழவுடை முதுநகர் விலாவிக் கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"As the warriors bound the twin shoulders of the long-haired prince with their belts, the old festive city wailed as if the serpent had seized the fiery god, the sun.","n":"The eclipse simile makes the binding a cosmic and temporary darkening: the serpent swallows the sun only for a while, which forecasts his release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1092","lb":"CC1092","ev":"The king's men bind Civakan's arms and the city laments.","cl":[],"ag":["இளையர்","மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1093":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1093,"t":["தோளார் முத்துந் தொன்முலைக் கோட்டுத் துயன்முத்தும்","வாளா ருண்கண் வந்திழி முத்தும் மிவைசிந்தக்","காளாய் நம்பி சீவக சாமி யெனநற்றாய்","மீளாத் துன்ப நீள்கடன் மின்னின் மிசைவீழ்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Scattering the pearls on her shoulders, the pearls of her breasts and the pearls of tears streaming from her eyes, his foster mother cried 'my young one, Nambi, Civaka Sami!' and fell like lightning into the long sea of unending grief.","n":"She calls him சீவக சாமி, 'lord Civaka', the name the invocation gives him, and the triple pearls (ornament, breast, tears) make one shower of loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1093","lb":"CC1093","ev":"Sunandai falls in grief as Civakan is led away.","cl":[],"ag":["சுநந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:47"},"cc:1094":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1094,"t":["பாலா ராவிப் பைந்துகி லேந்திப் படநாகம்","போலா மல்குற் பொற்றொடி பூங்கட் குணமாலை","யேலா தேலா தெம்பெரு மானுக் கிஃதென்னா","நூலார் கோதை நுங்கெரி வாய்ப்பட் டதுவொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Gunamalai, holding up her silk as the men lead Civakan away, cries twice that this is not fitting for her lord and is like a flower garland fallen into fire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1094","lb":"CC1094","ev":"Gunamalai cries out that the arrest is not fitting for her lord and wilts in grief.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1095":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1095,"t":["எரிதவழ் குன்றத் துச்சி யிரும்பொறிக் கலாப மஞ்ஞை","யிரிவன போன்று மாடத் தில்லுறை தெய்வ மன்னார்","பரிவுறு மனத்தி னோடிப் பட்டதை யுணர்ந்து பொற்றா","ரரியுறழ் மொய்ம்ப வோவென் றாகுலப் பூசல் செய்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The women of the mansion, like goddesses of the house, run out like peacocks fleeing a burning hill, learn what has happened and raise a cry of lament to the lion-shouldered one.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1095","lb":"CC1095","ev":"The women of Civakan's house run out, learn of his arrest and lament aloud.","cl":[],"ag":["இல்லுறை தெய்வம் அன்னார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1096":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1096,"t":["கங்கையின் சுழியிற் பட்ட காமரு பிணையின் மாழ்கி","யங்கவர்க் குற்ற துள்ளி யவலநீ ரழுந்து கின்ற","குங்குமக் கொடியோ டேந்திக் கோலம்வீற் றிருந்த கொம்மைப்","பொங்கிள முலையி னார்க்குப் புலவல னிதனைச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"To these women, sinking in grief like a doe caught in a whirlpool of the Ganges, the learned hero speaks the words that follow.","n":"The narrator names him புலவலன், the one skilled in learning, at the moment he is bound: the title prepares the doctrinal consolation of the next verse rather than any warrior's answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1096","lb":"CC1096","ev":"Civakan turns to the grieving women of his house to speak to them.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1097":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1097,"t":["கட்டுயி லனந்தர் போலக் கதிகளுட் டோன்று மாறும்","விட்டுயிர் போகு மாறும் வீடுபெற் றுயரு மாறு","முட்பட வுணர்ந்த யானே யுள்குழைந் துருகல் செல்லே","னெட்பக வனைத்து மார்வ மேதமே யிரங்கல் வேண்டா."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says that he, who has understood within how souls appear in the states of birth as one wakes from heavy sleep, how life leaves, and how it gains release and rises, will not melt in grief; attachment even the size of a split sesame seed is a fault, so they must not grieve.","n":"கதிகள் (the states of birth) and release as rising (வீடுபெற்று உயருமாறு) are the Jain frame the poem uses elsewhere; the school is the text's vocabulary, not a label added. The sesame measure (எட்பக அனைத்தும்) is the Kural's phrase for a small inner enmity (Kural 889, எட்பக வன்ன), but only two words match, so it is not marked as an echo.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","veedu","pattru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1097","lb":"CC1097","ev":"Civakan, bound and led away, forbids his household to grieve.","cl":[["one who understands how souls are born into the states of existence, leave the body and rise in release does not grieve at misfortune","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"],["attachment, even as small as a split sesame seed, is a fault","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","இல்லுறை தெய்வம் அன்னார்"],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1098":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1098,"t":["நன்மணி யிழந்த நாகர் நல்லிளம் படியர் போல","வின்மணி யிழந்த சாம்பி யிருநில மிவர்க ளெய்த","மின்னணி மதியங் கோள்வாய் விசும்பிடை நடப்ப தேபோற்","கன்மணி யுமிழும் பூணான் கடைபல கடந்து சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As the women, having lost their jewel, fall to the ground wilting like young naga women robbed of their gem, Civakan in gem-bright ornaments passes gate after gate like the moon moving across the sky in the mouth of the eclipse planet.","n":"The eclipse image (மதியம் கோள்வாய்) makes the arrest a temporary swallowing: the moon in the planet's mouth still shines and will come out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1098","lb":"CC1098","ev":"Civakan is led out through the many gates of the house while its women collapse.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இல்லுறை தெய்வம் அன்னார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1099":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1099,"t":["வெந்தனம் மனம்மென வெள்ளைநோக்கின் முள்ளெயிற்","றந்துவர்ப் பவளவா யம்மழலை யின்சொலார்","பந்துபாவை பைங்கழங்கு பைம்பொன்முற்றில் சிற்றிலுண்","ணொந்துவைத்து நூபுரம்மொ லிப்பவோடி நோக்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"The young women of the city, crying that their hearts are burnt, leave their balls, dolls, play-stones and toy houses and run out with tinkling anklets to look.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1099","lb":"CC1099","ev":"Young girls of the city drop their games and run out to see Civakan led away.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1100":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1100,"t":["மல்லிகை மலிந்தமாலை சோரவார்ந்த குண்டலம்","வில்விலங்க மின்னுக்கோட்ட வீணைவிட்டு வெய்துரா","யொல்லெனச் சிலம்பரற்ற வீதிமல்க வோடினார்","சில்சுணங் கிளமுலைச் செழும்மலர்த் தடங்கணார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with jasmine garlands slipping and earrings flashing leave their vinas, breathe hot sighs and run till the street is full, anklets crying out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1100","lb":"CC1100","ev":"Women leave their vinas and crowd the street.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1101":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1101,"t":["நெய்த்தலைக் கருங்குழ னிழன்றெருத் தலைத்தர","முத்தலைத் திளம்முலை முகஞ்சிவந் தலமரக்","கைத்தலங் கடுத்தடித்த பந்துநீக்கி வந்தவண்","மைத்தலை நெடுந்தடங்கண் மங்கையர் மயங்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"Women whose dark hair falls over their necks and whose breasts flush red throw down the balls that have made their palms ache, come there and are bewildered.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1101","lb":"CC1101","ev":"Women at ball-play leave it and come, dazed with grief.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1102":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1102,"t":["கோதைகொண்ட பூஞ்சிகைக் கொம்மைகொண்ட வெம்முலை","மாதுகொண்ட சாயலம் மடந்தையர் மனங்கசிந்","தோதமுத் துகுப்பபோ லுண்கண்வெம் பனியுகுத்","தியாதுசெய்க மையவென் றன்புமிக் கரற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"Garlanded women, their hearts melting, shed hot tears from kohl-lined eyes like the sea casting pearls and cry with great love, 'What can we do, O lord?'","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1102","lb":"CC1102","ev":"Women weep and cry out to Civakan in helpless love.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1103":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1103,"t":["செம்பொனோலை வீழவுஞ் செய்கலங்கள் சிந்தவு","மம்பொன்மாலை யோடசைந் தவிழ்ந்துகூந்தல் சோரவு","நம்பனுற்ற தென்னெனா நாடகம் மடந்தையர்","வெம்பிவீதி யோடினார் மின்னினன்ன நுண்மையார்."],"p":"","r":"The dancing women, slender as lightning, run burning through the street with gold ear-rolls falling, jewels scattering and hair coming loose, asking what has befallen their beloved.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1103","lb":"CC1103","ev":"The dancing women of the city run through the street asking what has happened to him.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1104":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1104,"t":["பூவலர்ந்த தாரினான் பொற்புவாடு மாயிடிற்","போவுடம்பு வாழுயிர் பொன்றுநீயு மின்றெனா","வீகலந்த மஞ்ஞைபோல் வேனெடுங்க ணீர்மல்க","வாகுலத் தரிவைய ரவ்வயி றதுக்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with spear-long eyes brimming beat their bellies in grief, saying that if the garlanded one's beauty fades, the body will go and life itself will perish today.","n":"நீயும் (line 2) addresses someone, perhaps a companion or the woman's own life; the line is read as a lament without fixing the addressee.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1104","lb":"CC1104","ev":"Women beat their bellies and say their lives will end if his beauty is harmed.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1105":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1105,"t":["தேன்மலிந்த கோதைமாலை செய்கலம் முகுத்துராய்க்","கான்மலிந்த காமவல்லி யென்னதன்ன ராயரோ","பான்மலிந்த வெம்முலைப் பைந்துகி லரிவையர்","நூன்மலிந்த நுண்ணுசுப்பு நோவவந்து நோக்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"Young women throw off their honeyed garlands and ornaments and come to look, asking whether he who was like the wish-granting creeper of desire has come to this, their slender waists aching as they run.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1105","lb":"CC1105","ev":"Women cast off their ornaments and run to look at him.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1106":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1106,"t":["மாதரார்கள் கற்பினுக்கு டைந்தமா மணிக்கலைத்","தீதிலார நூற்பெய்வார் சிதர்ந்துபோகச் சிந்துவார்","போதுலா மலங்கலான்முன் போந்துபூந் தெரிவைய","ராதகா தெனக்கலங்கி யவ்வயி றதுக்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"Some women restring the gems of girdles that broke, others scatter them as they run, and the flower-like women come before the garlanded one, crying that this is not fit, and beat their bellies.","n":"Line 1 (மாதரார்கள் கற்பினுக்கு உடைந்த மாமணிக்கலை) is hard to construe: the girdles may have broken in the women's haste, or the line may say that their steadfastness gave way at the sight of him; the reading leaves it open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1106","lb":"CC1106","ev":"Women run before Civakan crying that his arrest is unjust and beat their bellies.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1107":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1107,"t":["வட்டிகை மணிப்பலகை வண்ணநுண் டுகிலிகை","யிட்டிடை நுடங்கநொந் திரியலுற்ற மஞ்ஞையிற்","கட்டழ லுயிர்ப்பின்வெந்து கண்ணிதீந்து பொன்னுக","மட்டவிழ்ந்த கோதையர்கள் வந்துவாயில் பற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"Women who had been at their painting, with brush-box, board and fine colours, leave them, wilt like peacocks in distress, and with garlands scorched by their burning breath come and cling to the gateway.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1107","lb":"CC1107","ev":"Women leave their painting and cling to the gate to see him.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1108":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1108,"t":["வினையது விளைவு காண்மி னென்றுகை விதிர்த்து நிற்பா","ரினையனாய்த் தெளியச் சென்றா லிடிக்குங்கொ லிவனை யென்பார்","புனைநல மழகு கல்வி பொன்றுமா லின்றொ டென்பார்","வனைகலத் திகிரி போல மறுகுமெம் மனங்க ளென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Some women shake their hands and say 'see the fruit of deeds', some fear that if he is taken in this state the king will strike him, some say that his beauty, grace and learning will end today, and some say their hearts whirl like a potter's wheel.","n":"The karma explanation is one voice among several in a crowd; the next verse blames the father and the king in the same breath, so the poem gives the arrest more than one cause without settling which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","idumbai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1108","lb":"CC1108","ev":"The city women speak their fears and griefs as Civakan is led away.","cl":[["what befalls a man is the ripening of his deeds","general","reported",1,"city_women"]],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1109":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1109,"t":["நோற்றிலர் மகளி ரென்பார் நோங்கண்டீர் தோள்க ளென்பார்","கூற்றத்தைக் கொம்மை கொட்டிக் குலத்தொடு முடியு மென்பா","ரேற்றதொன் றன்று தந்தை செய்தவிக் கொடுமை யென்பா","ராற்றலள் சுநந்தை யென்பா ராதகா தறனே யென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Some say the women did no penance to deserve him, some point to his hurt shoulders, some say the king has beckoned Death and will perish with his line, some that the father's act was a cruelty unfit to be done, some that Sunandai cannot bear it, and some cry out to dharma that it is not right.","n":"Merit (நோற்றிலர்), the king's folly and the father's choice are all blamed here together, beside the previous verse's 'fruit of deeds'. The father's act was itself a decision to submit (verses 1089-1091), so the women condemn what the story has framed as prudence. கூற்றத்தைக் கொம்மை கொட்டி recalls Kural 894 (கூற்றத்தைக் கையால் விளித்தற்றால்) in sense, but only one word matches, so it is not marked as an echo.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kootru","aram","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1109","lb":"CC1109","ev":"The city women blame their own lack of merit, the king and Civakan's father for the arrest.","cl":[["those who have done no penance do not get to keep what they love","general","reported",1,"city_women"],["one who provokes the stronger calls Death down on himself and his line","general","reported",2,"city_women"],["a father who hands over his son to harm commits a cruelty","general","reported",3,"city_women"]],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1110":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1110,"t":["தூக்குமின் காளை சீறிற் றுற்றிவ னுளனோ வென்பார்","காக்குமால் வைய மெல்லாங் காவல னாகி யென்பார்","பாக்கயம் பெரிது மேகா ணிதுவுமோர் பான்மை யென்பார்","நோக்கன்மி னாணுங் கண்டீர் நுதிகொ ணாகரிக னென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Some say, 'weigh it: if this bull raged, would the king survive?'; some say he would guard the whole world as its ruler; some say it is great luck and this too is a turn of fate; some say 'do not stare, he is ashamed, the keen and courtly one.'","n":"The women's wish that he would guard the world as king is unknowing irony: he is the dead king's son, though no one here knows it. Line 3 (பாக்கயம் பெரிதுமே காண்) may mean the king's good fortune that Civakan does not resist, or the city's luck in such a man; the reading is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","aanmai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1110","lb":"CC1110","ev":"The city women say Civakan could overpower the king and is fit to rule, and pity his shame.","cl":[["this too is a portion allotted by fate","general","reported",3,"city_women"]],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1111":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1111,"t":["பூவரம் பாய கோதைப் பொன்னனார் புலவி நீக்கி","நூபுரந் திருத்திச் சேந்த நுதிவிர னொந்த வென்பார்","யாவரும் புகழு மைய னழகுகெட் டொழியு மாயிற்","கோபுர மாட மூதூர் கூற்றுண விளிக வென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Some say the sharp fingers that, removing the sulks of golden women, set right their anklets and grew red are now hurt; some say that if the lord all praise loses his beauty, let the old city of towers and mansions perish as food for Death.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","idumbai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1111","lb":"CC1111","ev":"The city women lament his bound hands and curse the city if he is harmed.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1112":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1112,"t":["கருஞ்சிலை மறவர் கொண்ட கணநிரை விடுக்க வல்ல","விருஞ்சிலை பயின்ற திண்டோட் கிதுதகா தென்றுகுன்றிற்","கருங்கட றுளுப்பிட் டாங்குக் கல்லெனக் கலங்கிக் காம","ரருங்கடி யரண மூதூ ராகுல மயங்கிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Crying that this is unfit for the strong shoulders trained with the great bow that freed the herd seized by the black-bowed hunters, the old city behind its guarded walls is thrown into tumult like a dark sea churned by a mountain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","aanmai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1112","lb":"CC1112","ev":"The whole city rises in grief and uproar at the arrest.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1113":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1113,"t":["இங்கன மிவர்க ளேக வெரியகம் விளைக்கப் பட்ட","வெங்கணை விடலை தாதை வியனக ரவல மெய்தி","யங்கவ ருகுத்த கண்ணீ ரடித்துக ளவிப்ப நோக்கிப்","பொங்கம ருழக்கும் வேலான் புலம்புகொண் டழேற்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As the crowds move thus, the father of the hero of the fire-hot arrows, seeing the great city's grief and the tears that laid the dust at their feet, tells them, as the spear-bearer who fights in war, not to weep.","n":"பொங்கமர் உழக்கும் வேலான் (line 4) gives the merchant a warrior's epithet; it may refer to the son rather than the father, which would change who forbids the weeping.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1113","lb":"CC1113","ev":"Kantukkatan sees the city's grief and tells the people not to weep.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1114":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1114,"t":["மின்னினான் மலையை யீர்ந்து வேறிரு கூறு செய்வான்","றுன்னினான் றுளங்கி னல்லாற் றுளங்கலம் மலையிற் குண்டே","யன்னதே துணிந்த நீதி யருநவை நமனு மாற்றா","னென்னைநேர் நின்று வாழ்த லிருநிலத் தாவ துண்டே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that if one with lightning splits a mountain in two, it is the striker who shakes, not 'we', deeper than the mountain; such is the justice resolved, which even Yama cannot overturn; can anyone on the wide earth live standing against me?","n":"The speech is most likely Kantukkatan's, following his 'do not weep' in 1113, but the first person (துளங்கலம், என்னை) could be the house speaking, or Civakan's words reported; the defiant tone sits oddly with the father who just reasoned that opposing a king is the deed of the doomed (1089).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","kootru","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1114","lb":"CC1114","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","மாந்தர்"],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1115":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1115,"t":["வளைகடல் வலையிற் சூழ்ந்து மால்வரை வேலி கோலி","யுளையரி படுக்க லுற்றான் படுப்பினும் படுக்க மற்றென்","கிளையழ வென்னை வாள்வாய்க் கீண்டிட லுற்று நின்றான்","றளையவிழ் கண்ணி சிந்தத் தன்றலை நிலத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says the king has set out to snare a maned lion by netting the curved sea and fencing it with mountains; let him snare it if he can, but if he stands ready to cleave me with the sword while my kin weep, his own head, its garland scattered, will lie on the ground.","n":"The lion is Civakan, and the net of sea and mountains says the king's means are absurdly large and still too weak; who 'me' (என்னை) is, the father or the son, is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kootru","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1115","lb":"CC1115","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","மாந்தர்"],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1116":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1116,"t":["நீரகம் பொதிந்த மேக நீனிற நெடுநல் யானைப்","போரகத் தழலும் வாட்கைப் பொன்னெடுங் குன்ற மன்னா","னார்கலி யாணர் மூதூ ரழுதுபின் செல்லச் செல்வான்","சீருறு சிலம்பி நூலாற் சிமிழ்ப்புண்ட சிங்க மொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, like a long golden hill, whose sword burns in war among dark cloud-coloured elephants, walks on while the festive old city follows weeping, like a lion bound with a spider's thread.","n":"The spider's thread (சிலம்பி நூல்) makes the binding his own consent: the bond could not hold him, and what holds him is the word given to his parents (1091).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","porai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1116","lb":"CC1116","ev":"Civakan is led away bound while the city follows weeping.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1117":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1117,"t":["மன்னர்தம் வெகுளி வெந்தீ மணிமுகில் காண மின்னிப்","பொன்மழை பொழியி னந்து மன்றெனிற் புகைந்து பொங்கித்","துன்னினார் தம்மை யெல்லாஞ் சுட்டிடு மென்று செம்பொன்","பன்னிரு கோடி யுய்த்துக் கந்துகன் பணிந்து சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Kantukkatan, reckoning that a king's anger is a hot fire that will die down if gold rains on it like lightning-lit cloud, and otherwise will smoke, flare and burn all who come near, sends twelve crores of red gold and speaks humbly to the king.","n":"The merchant answers royal anger with money, the means of his own class, after the city's laments and his own defiant words; the plea itself begins in the next verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","vekuli","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1117","lb":"CC1117","ev":"Kantukkatan sends twelve crores of gold to the king and pleads humbly for his son.","cl":[["a king's anger is a fire that burns all near it unless quenched with gifts","general","reported",1,"kantukkatan"]],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:48"},"cc:1118":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1118,"t":["மன்னவ வருளிக் கேண்மோ மடந்தையோர் கொடியை மூதூர்","நின்மதக் களிறு கொல்ல நினக்கது வடுவென் றெண்ணி","யென்மக னதனை நீக்கி யின்னுயி ரவளைக் காத்தா","னின்னதே குற்ற மாயிற் குணமினி யாது வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Kantukkatan pleads with the king: when the king's own rutting elephant was about to kill a young woman of the old city, his son, thinking the death would be a stain on the king, drove it off and saved her life; if that is a fault, what then is a virtue?","n":"The plea turns the charge round: the father argues that the rescue protected the king's name, since a killing by the royal elephant would have been the king's disgrace (வடு).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kollaamai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1118","lb":"CC1118","ev":"Kantukkatan pleads before Kattiyangaran for his son, who saved a girl from the king's elephant.","cl":[["saving a life from the king's own beast cannot be a crime against the king","general","asserted",4,"kantukkatan"]],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1119":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1119,"t":["நாண்மெய்க்கொண் டீட்டப் பட்டார் நடுக்குறு நவையை நீக்க","லாண்மக்கள் கடனென் றெண்ணி யறிவின்மை துணிந்த குற்றம்","பூண்மெய்க்கொண் டகன்ற மார்ப பொறுமதி யென்று பின்னு","நீண்மைக்க ணின்று வந்த நிதியெலாந் தருவ லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He goes on that it is the duty of men to remove the danger that makes those caught by fate tremble, asks the king to forgive the fault of a youth who acted without thinking, and offers all the wealth his line has gathered over long years.","n":"The father both defends the act as a man's duty and calls it a fault of thoughtlessness (அறிவின்மை), conceding the king's frame while begging; the offer of wealth follows the twelve crores of 1117.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","porai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1119","lb":"CC1119","ev":"Kantukkatan asks the king's pardon and offers all his wealth.","cl":[["it is the duty of men to rescue those in danger","general","asserted",2,"kantukkatan"]],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1120":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1120,"t":["வாழிய ரிறைவ தேற்றான் மாநிரை பெயர்த்த காளை","பீழைதான் பொறுக்க வென்னப் பிறங்கிண ரலங்கன் மாலை","சூழ்கதி ராரம் வீழ்நூல் பரிந்தற நிமிர்ந்து திண்டோ","ளூழ்பிணைந் துருமிற் சீறி யுடல்சினங் கடவச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"When the merchant asks the lord to bear with the youth who brought back the king's herds, the king, his garland and pearl strings snapping as he swells with rage, speaks in a fury like thunder.","n":"The father's last argument is service: the same youth had won back the royal herds from the raiders, which only sharpens the king's anger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1120","lb":"CC1120","ev":"Kantukkatan reminds the king that his son recovered the king's cattle; the king flares into rage.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1121":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1121,"t":["ஆய்களிற் றசனி வேக மதன்மருப் பூசி யாகச்","சீவக னகன்ற மார்ப மோலையாகத் திசைகள் கேட்பக்","காய்பவன் கள்வ ரென்ன வெழுதுவித் திடுவ லின்னே","நீபரி வொழிந்து போய்நின் னகம்புகு நினைய லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king says he will have the tusk of his elephant Asanivegam made the stylus and Civakan's broad chest the palm leaf, and have 'thief' written on it for all the quarters to hear; the merchant is to stop grieving, go home and think no more of it.","n":"The punishment answers the rescue with the very beast Civakan subdued: the elephant's tusk is to write the sentence on his body. The charge 'thief' (கள்வர்) has no ground in the story; it is the usurper's word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","vekuli","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1121","lb":"CC1121","ev":"Kattiyangaran refuses the plea and threatens to brand Civakan a thief with the elephant's tusk.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","கந்துக்கடன்"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1122":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1122,"t":["நஞ்சனா னுரைப்பக் கேட்டே நாய்கனு நடுங்கி யுள்ளம்","வெஞ்சின வேழ முண்ட விளங்கனி போன்று நீங்கி","யெஞ்சினான் போல நின்றா னேத்தருந் தவத்தின் மிக்க","வஞ்சமில் கொள்கை யான்சொ லமிர்தினால் வற்புற் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the venomous king speak, the merchant trembles and stands emptied like a wood-apple eaten hollow by the elephant disease, as if his life had gone, but he is held up by the nectar of the words of the ascetic of guileless doctrine, great in penance beyond praise.","n":"The verse turns the plot from the court to memory: what saves the father is not his wealth or his plea but a prophecy, which the following verses tell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","sol","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1122","lb":"CC1122","ev":"Kantukkatan, crushed by the refusal, takes strength from an ascetic's earlier words.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1123":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1123,"t":["மின்னிலங் கெஃகி னானைப் பெறுகலான் றந்தை மீண்டு","தன்னிலங் குறுக லோடுந் தாயழு தரற்று கின்றா","ளென்னிலை யைற் கென்ன யாவதுங் கவல வேண்டா","பொன்னலங் கொடிய னாயோர் பொருளுரை கேளி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Unable to win back his son of the shining spear, the father returns home, where the mother weeps and laments; he tells her not to grieve at all over their son's state, and to hear a true word.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","idumbai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1123","lb":"CC1123","ev":"Kantukkatan returns home and comforts the weeping Sunandai.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1124":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1124,"t":["மதுமடை திறந்து தீந்தேன் வார்தரு கோதை நீமுன்","செதுமகப் பலவும் பெற்றுச் சிந்தைகூர் மனத்தை யாகி","யிதுமக வழியின் வாழே னிறப்பல்யா னென்னு மாங்கட்","கதுமெனக் கடவு டோன்றிக் கடைமுகங் குறுக வந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He reminds her that long ago, after bearing many children who died, she was sunk in grief and said she would not live but die, and at that moment a holy one appeared suddenly and came to their door.","n":"The house's later fortune is rooted in a mother's despair over children born dead (செதுமகப் பலவும்), which the earlier narrative of the burning ground (325) presupposes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","saakkaadu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1124","lb":"CC1124","ev":"Kantukkatan recalls how an ascetic came to their door when Sunandai, her children dead, wished to die.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிவரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1125":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1125,"t":["கறவைகாண் கன்றின் வெஃகிக் கண்டடி பணிந்து காமர்","நறவயா வுயிர்க்கு மாலை நாற்றிய விடத்து ளேற்றி","யறவியாற் காறு மூன்று மமைந்தநா லமிர்த மேந்தப்","பறவைதா துண்ட வண்ணம் பட்டினிப பிரிவு தீர்த்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a cow that sees its calf they longed for him, bowed at his feet, seated him in a place hung with fragrant garlands, and when the four kinds of food fit for the ascetic were set before him he broke his fast as a bird takes pollen.","n":"Line 3 (ஆறும் மூன்றும் அமைந்த நால் அமிர்தம்) names a set of foods by number that the reading does not resolve; the bee-like eating marks the ascetic's restraint in taking alms.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","virundhu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1125","lb":"CC1125","ev":"The couple receive the ascetic, seat him and feed him.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை","முனிவரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1126":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1126,"t":["ஆய்மணிப் பவளத் திண்ணை யரும்பெறற் கரகத் தங்கட்","டூய்மணி வாச நன்னீர் துளங்குபொற் கலத்து ளேற்று","வேய்மணித் தோளி நிற்ப விழுத்தவ னியம முற்றி","வாய்மணி முறுவ றோன்ற வந்தனை விதியிற் செய்தேன்."],"p":"","r":"On the coral seat, with fragrant pure water poured from a rare pitcher into a golden vessel and she of the bamboo shoulders standing by, after the great ascetic had finished his rite, he says, he paid him homage with a smile according to the rule.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1126","lb":"CC1126","ev":"Kantukkatan washes the ascetic's feet and worships him after his observance.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை","முனிவரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1127":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1127,"t":["ஆறெலாங் கடலுள் வைகு மருந்தவத் திறைவ னூலுள்","வேறெலாப் பொருளும் வைகும் விழுத்தவ வறிதி நீயே","யூறிலா வுணர்வி னோக்கி யுரைமதி யெவன்கொன் மக்கட்","பேறிலா ளல்லள் பெற்ற வுயிர்சென்று பிறக்கு மென்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"He had said to the ascetic: as all rivers come to rest in the sea, all things rest in the scripture of the lord of great penance, and you know them; look with your unhindered knowledge and tell us why she is not blessed with children, for those she bears go on to be born again.","n":"பெற்ற உயிர் சென்று பிறக்கும் puts the children's deaths in the language of rebirth: the lives she bears pass on to other births. The 'lord of great penance' (அருந்தவத்து இறைவன்) is the Jina, whose scripture the ascetic is credited with holding whole.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","gnaanam","makkal","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1127","lb":"CC1127","ev":"Kantukkatan asks the ascetic why their children die.","cl":[["all things are contained in the scripture of the lord of great penance, as all rivers in the sea","jain","asserted",1,"kantukkatan"],["an ascetic of great penance sees hidden things with unhindered knowledge","jain","asserted",3,"kantukkatan"]],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","முனிவரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1128":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1128,"t":["வம்பவிழ் கோதை தந்த வான்றுவர்க் காயை வீழ்த்தோர்","செம்பழுக் காயை வாங்கித் திருநிலத் தெடுத்துக் கொண்டாங்","கம்பழ நீண்ட வாட்க ணலமரு மணிசெ யம்பூங்","கொம்படு நுசுப்பி னாய்க்குத் தந்தனென் பேணிக் கொண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"He reminds her that she offered the ascetic an unripe areca nut, which he let fall, and that he picked a ripe red areca from the ground and gave it to her, and she took it with care.","n":"The exchange of the unripe nut for a ripe one is told as the omen the next verse reads; that it figures the dead children replaced by a living son is a reading, not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1128","lb":"CC1128","ev":"The ascetic drops the unripe areca Sunandai gives and hands her a ripe one.","cl":[],"ag":["சுநந்தை","முனிவரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1129":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1129,"t":["பெற்றவந் நிமித்தத் தானும் பிறந்தசொல் வகையி னானு","மற்றமின் மணியை யங்கைக் கொண்டவர் கண்டு காட்டக்","கற்பகங் காம வல்லி யனையநீர் கேண்மி னென்று","முற்றுபு கனிந்த சொல்லான் முனிவரன் மொழியு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"From that omen and from the way the words had fallen, seeing as clearly as those who hold a flawless gem in the palm, the chief of sages spoke in ripe words, addressing them as like the wishing tree and the creeper of desire.","n":"The sage's knowledge is presented as reading signs (நிமித்தம், the manner of speech) and as direct sight like a gem in the hand: omen and insight are joined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","gnaanam","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1129","lb":"CC1129","ev":"The sage reads the omen and begins his prophecy.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிவரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1130":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1130,"t":["ஒன்றுநீர் கவலல் வேண்டா வுலகெலா மாளுஞ் சீர்த்திப்","பொன்றிக ழுருவி னானோர் புண்ணியற் பெறுதி ரென்ன","நின்றநீ யுவந்து நீங்க நிகழ்பொரு ளெனக்குச் செப்பிப்","பின்றையு நிகழ்வ துண்டு பேசுவல் கேளி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage told them not to worry at all, for they would gain a meritorious son of golden form whose fame would rule the whole world; when she had gone off rejoicing, he told the merchant more of what would happen, and the merchant now says he will tell it.","n":"The prophecy was split: the mother heard only the promise of a son, while the father alone kept the second part, the danger. That is why he can now comfort her with something she has never heard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pugazh","nimittam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1130","lb":"CC1130","ev":"The sage foretells that they will gain a famous son, and tells Kantukkatan more in private.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிவரன்","சுநந்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1131":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1131,"t":["நிலவுறழ் பூணி னானை நெடுநக ரிரங்கக் கையாத்","தலபல செய்து கொல்வா னருளிலான் கொண்ட போழ்திற்","குலவிய புகழி னானைக் கொண்டுபோ மியக்க னஞ்சல்","சிலபகல் கழிந்து காண்டி சிந்தியீ தென்று சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage had said that when the graceless one seizes the youth of moon-bright jewels to kill him, binding his hands with many torments while the great city mourns, a yaksha will carry off the famous one; they should not fear, for after some days they will see him again.","n":"The usurper is named only as 'the one without grace' (அருளிலான்), the Jain lack that marks him; the rescuer is an இயக்கன், which the story will show to be Sudanjanan, the dog Civakan once helped.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","kodungol","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1131","lb":"CC1131","ev":"Kantukkatan tells the rest of the prophecy: Civakan will be seized to be killed and rescued by a yaksha.","cl":[],"ag":["முனிவரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1132":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1132,"t":["வசையற நிறைந்த கற்பின் மாலையு மாமி தானுந்","தசையற வுருகி வெந்து தம்முயிர் நீங்கு மாங்க","ணொசிதவன் சொற்க ளென்னு நோன்புணை தழுவி நெஞ்சிற்","கசிவெனுங் கடலை நீந்திக் கரையெனுங் காலை கண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Gunamalai of flawless chastity and her mother-in-law, melting as if their flesh dissolved and about to give up their lives, clung to the strong raft of the ascetic's words, swam the sea of grief in their hearts and saw the shore.","n":"Gunamalai is already called the merchant's daughter-in-law's equal here: மாமி makes Sunandai her mother-in-law, so the household treats the betrothal as binding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","idumbai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1132","lb":"CC1132","ev":"Gunamalai and Sunandai, near death with grief, are steadied by the prophecy.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை","சுநந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1133":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1133,"t":["திருக்குழன் மகளிர் நையச் சீவக சாமி திண்டோள்","வரிக்கச்சிற் பிணிக்கப் பட்டான் மன்னனா லென்னக் கேட்டே","தருக்குடை வேழம் வாளார் ஞாட்பினுட் டகைமை சான்ற","மருப்புட னிழந்த தொத்தார் மன்னுயிர்த் தோழன் மாரே."],"p":"","r":"When the women of beautiful hair were wasting away and Civakan's dear friends heard that Civakasami's strong arms had been bound with a striped belt by the king, they were like a proud war elephant that has lost its tusks in the clash of swords.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1133","lb":"CC1133","ev":"Civakan's friends hear that the king has had him bound.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1134":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1134,"t":["நட்டவற் குற்ற கேட்டே பதுமுக னக்கு மற்றோர்","குட்டியைத் தின்ன லாமே கோட்புலி புறத்த தாகக்","கட்டியங் கார னென்னுங் கழுதைநம் புலியைப் பாய","வொட்டியிஃ துணர லாமே யுரைவல்லை யறிக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing what had befallen their friend, Padumukan laughs: can anyone eat the cub while the fierce tiger is outside? that donkey called Kattiyangaran leaping on our tiger, can such a thing be? you who are skilled in words, understand this.","n":"The friends are the tiger outside the den; the usurper is the donkey. Line 4 (ஒட்டி இஃது உணரலாமே) is hard to construe and the reading of it is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","ookkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1134","lb":"CC1134","ev":"Padumukan scorns the news, calling Kattiyangaran a donkey attacking a tiger.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1135":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1135,"t":["சிலையொடு செல்வ னின்றாற் றேவரும் வணக்க லாற்றார்","முலையுடைத் தாயோ டெண்ணித் தந்தையிக் கொடுமை செய்தான்","கலைவல்லீ ரின்னுங் கேண்மி னின்னதென் றுரைக்கு மாங்கண்","விரைவொடு சென்ற வொற்றாள் விளைந்தவா பேசு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Padumukan says that if their lord stood with his bow even the gods could not bend him, so his father, taking counsel with the mother who nursed him, must have done this cruel thing; as he tells the learned friends to hear more, the spy who had gone quickly returns and tells what happened.","n":"The friend reasons from Civakan's strength to the only power that could bind him: his own parents. கொடுமை is his word for their act, though the parents meant it to save him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","otru","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1135","lb":"CC1135","ev":"Padumukan guesses that Civakan's parents persuaded him to be bound; a spy arrives with the news.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்","ஒற்றன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1136":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1136,"t":["இட்டிவேல் குந்தங் கூர்வா ளிருஞ்சிலை யிருப்புச் சுற்றார்","நெட்டிலைச் சூலம் வெய்ய முளைத்தண்டு நெருங்க வேந்தி","யெட்டெலாத் திசையு மீண்டி யெழாயிரத் திரட்டி மள்ளர்","கட்டழற் கதிரை யூர்கோள் வளைத்தவா வளைத்துக் கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The spy tells how fourteen thousand warriors, bearing lances, spears, sharp swords, great bows, iron discs, long-bladed tridents and fierce spiked clubs, gathered from all eight directions and ringed the house as a halo rings the blazing sun.","n":"எழாயிரத்து இரட்டி is twice seven thousand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1136","lb":"CC1136","ev":"Kattiyangaran's warriors surround Civakan's house.","cl":[],"ag":["மள்ளர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1137":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1137,"t":["பிடியொடு நின்ற வேழம் பெருவளைப் புண்ட வண்ணம்","வடிமலர்க் கோதை யோடும் வளைத்தலின் மைந்தன் சீறி","விடுகணை சிலையொ டேந்தி வெருவரத் தோன்ற லோடு","மடுபுலி கண்ட மான்போ லாறல வாயி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"As an elephant standing with its mate is penned in, so the young man, hemmed in with his flower-garlanded lady, flared up, rose with bow and arrows ready and appeared so terrible that the warriors lost their way like deer that see a killing tiger.","n":"The lady (வடிமலர்க் கோதை) is unnamed; in this section she is most likely Gunamalai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1137","lb":"CC1137","ev":"Civakan takes up his bow and the besiegers scatter in fear.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மள்ளர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1138":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1138,"t":["சூழ்கழன் மள்ளர் பாறச் சூழ்ச்சியிற் றந்தை புல்லி","வீழ்தரு கண்ண டம்மோய் விளங்குதோள் பிணிப்ப மற்றென்","றோழரை வடுச்செய் திட்டே னென்றுதான் றுளங்கி நின்றா","னூழ்திரைப் பாம்பு சேர்ந்த வொளிமிகு பருதி யொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"As the warriors scattered, his father by a stratagem embraced him and his weeping mother bound his shining arms; saying 'I have brought disgrace on my friends', Civakan stood shaken, like the bright sun seized by the serpent of the eclipse.","n":"The hero who routed fourteen thousand is bound by his parents' embrace; his shame is toward his friends, not his foe. Whether the mother's binding is literal or her arms hold him is not plain from line 2.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","makkal","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1138","lb":"CC1138","ev":"Kantukkatan and Sunandai hold and bind Civakan, who submits, ashamed before his friends.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுநந்தை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1139":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1139,"t":["ஒற்றன்வந்த துரைப்பக் கேட்டே யொத்ததோ வென்சொ லென்னாச்","சுற்றினார் முகத்தை நோக்கிச சூழிமால் யானை யன்னா","னுற்றவிவ் விடரைத் தீர்க்கு முபாயநீ ருரைமி னென்றான்","பொற்றிரட் குன்றம் போலப் பொலிவுகொண் டிருந்த தோளான்."],"p":"","r":"When the spy had told it, Padumukan, elephant-like and with shoulders like a hill of gold, asked whether his word had not proved true, looked at the faces of those around him, and asked them to say how this danger might be ended.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1139","lb":"CC1139","ev":"Padumukan, his guess confirmed, asks the friends for a plan.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்","ஒற்றன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1140":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1140,"t":["நிறைத்திங்க ளொளியொ டொப்பான் புத்திசே னினைந்து சொல்லு","மறைத்திங்க ணகரை வல்லே சுடுதுநாஞ் சுடுத லோடு","மிறைக்குற்றேல் செய்த லின்றி யெரியின்வாய்ச் சனங்க ணீங்கச்","சிறைக்குற்ற நீங்கச் செற்றாற் செகுத்துக்கொண் டெழுது மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Buddhisenan, bright as the full moon, thinks and says: tonight we will quickly burn the city, and while it burns, doing no harm to the king's [people] and letting the people escape the fire's mouth, we will kill the enemy, end the imprisonment and carry him off.","n":"The plan includes care that the townspeople escape the fire (சனங்கள் நீங்க). Line 3's இறைக்குற்றேல் செய்தல் இன்றி is obscure: it may mean without harming the king or without rendering him service; the reading leaves it open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","sirai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1140","lb":"CC1140","ev":"Buddhisenan proposes burning the city by night to free Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["புத்திசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்திசேனன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1141":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1141,"t":["காலத்தீ நகரை மேயக் கடியரண் கடிந்த வம்பிற்","சாலத்தீச் சவரர் கோலஞ் செய்துநம் மறவ ரீண்டிக்","கோலத்தீ வேலி னானைக் கோயிலுள் வளைப்ப விப்பர்","லாலைத்தீ யிடங்க டோறு மாகுலஞ் செய்து மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He continues: as the fire like the fire of the world's end feeds on the city, our warriors, gathered in the fierce guise of Savara hunters, will surround the king of the bright spear in his palace, and raise an uproar at every place the fire is lit.","n":"The friends plan to fight disguised as hunters (சவரர்), so that the blame falls elsewhere. Lines 3 to 4 are divided oddly (விப்பர் / லாலைத்தீ) and their last part is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1141","lb":"CC1141","ev":"Buddhisenan lays out the disguise and the attack on the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["புத்திசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்திசேனன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:49"},"cc:1142":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1142,"t":["சிறைப்புறங் காத்துச் செல்லு மதனனைத் தெருவில் வீழப்","பிறைத்தலை யம்பிற் சென்னி பெருநிலத் திடுவ லிட்டான்","மறுக்குற்று மள்ளர் நீங்க மைந்தனைக் கொண்டு போகி","யறைத்தொழி லார்க்குஞ் செல்லா வருமிளை புகுமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The plan goes on: when Madanan, who escorts the prisoner, comes down the street, the speaker will lay his head on the ground with a crescent-headed arrow, and as the warriors scatter in confusion the others are to take the young man and go into a thicket that even men of war cannot enter.","n":"The speaker is not named here; 1145 calls the plan the policy told by the man named for the lotus (தாமரை நாமன்), Padumukan, so these two verses are read as his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","padai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1142","lb":"CC1142","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்",""],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1143":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1143,"t":["மாற்றவர் மலைப்பி னாங்கே வாட்கடாக் கொண்டு நொய்தா","வேற்றுல கேற்றி நும்பின் விரைதர்வே னுலகிற் கெல்லா","மாற்றிய நட்பு வல்லே வலிப்புறீஇ யிடுமி னென்றான்","கூற்றங்கள் பலவுந் தொக்க கூற்றத்திற் கூற்ற மொப்பான்."],"p":"","r":"If the enemy fight there, they are to send them swiftly to the other world with their swords, and he will hurry after them; he bids them make firm at once the friendship that the whole world has proved, he who was like the death of all the deaths gathered together.","n":"Line 3 (மாற்றிய நட்பு வல்லே வலிப்புறீஇ) is hard to construe; the reading takes it as a charge to hold fast to their friendship in the fight.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1143","lb":"CC1143","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்",""],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1144":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1144,"t":["காலனைச் சூழந்த நோய்போ னபுலமா விபுலர் சூழ","வேலினை யேந்தி நந்தன் வெருவரத் தோன்ற லோடு","மாலைதன் றாதை தானு மக்களும் வந்து கூடிப்","பாலவர் பிறரு மீண்டிப் பாய்புலி யினத்திற் சூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Like diseases round Death, Nabulan and Vipulan close round; Nandattan appears with his spear so that all fear him, and Gunamalai's father, his sons and others of their side gather and stand round like a pack of leaping tigers.","n":"The bride's father joins his son-in-law's friends against the usurper: the new marriage has already made a kin party in the city.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","sutram","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1144","lb":"CC1144","ev":"Civakan's companions, his brother and Gunamalai's father and kin gather under arms for the rescue.","cl":[],"ag":["நபுலன்","விபுலன்","நந்தட்டன்","மாலைதன் தாதை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1145":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1145,"t":["மட்டுவா யவிழ்ந்த தண்டார்த் தாமரை நாமன் சொன்ன","கட்டமை நீதி தன்மேற் காப்பமைந் திவர்க ணிற்பப்","பட்டுலாய்க் கிடந்த செம்பொற் கலையணி பரவை யல்கு","லிட்டிடைப் பவளச் செவ்வாய்த் தத்தையு மிதனைக் கேட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"While these stand on guard according to the plan Padumukan told, Gandharvadattai, of the silk-draped waist and coral mouth, hears of what has happened.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1145","lb":"CC1145","ev":"Gandharvadattai learns of Civakan's imprisonment.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1146":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1146,"t":["மணியியல் யவனச் செப்பின் மங்கலத் துகிலை வாங்கிக்","கணைபுரை கண்ணி யேற்ப வுடுத்தபின் செம்பொற் செப்பிற்","பிணையலு நறிய சேர்த்திப் பெருவிலை யாரந் தாங்கித்","துணைவனுக் குற்ற துன்பஞ் சொல்லிய தொடங்கி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"She takes an auspicious cloth from a gem-set Yavana casket and puts it on, sets fragrant garlands from a gold casket and a costly necklace on herself, and begins the rite for the trouble that has come upon her husband.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","idumbai","vinjai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1146","lb":"CC1146","ev":"Gandharvadattai dresses in auspicious cloth and ornaments to begin a rite for her husband.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1147":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1147,"t":["பொன்னணி மணிசெ யோடை நீரின்வெண் சாந்து பூசித்","தன்னுடை விஞ்ஞை யெல்லாந் தளிரிய லோத லோடு","மின்னடு வாளும் வேலுங் கல்லொடு தீயுங் காற்று","மன்னுட னேந்தித் தெய்வ மாதரைச் சூழ்ந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Anointing herself with white paste and water from a gold vessel set with gems, the tender one recites all her spells, and the powers of her lore, bearing sword and spear, stone, fire and wind, come and surround the divine woman.","n":"The princess of the Vidyadharas commands a war magic of her own: the rescue that the men are planning with arrows she could do alone, which is what the next verses weigh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinjai","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1147","lb":"CC1147","ev":"Gandharvadattai recites her Vidyadhara spells and their armed powers gather round her.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","விஞ்ஞை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1148":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1148,"t":["ஆரமின் னும்பணை வெம்முலை யாடமைத் தோளினாள்","வீரனுற் றதுயர் மின்னென நீக்கிய மெல்லவே","நேரமன் னும்வரு கென்றுநின் றாணினைந் தாளரோ","பாருண்மன் னும்பழி பண்பனுக் கின்று விளைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"She of the bamboo shoulders stands thinking that she could take away the hero's pain as fast as lightning, gently, and waits for the moment, and then she reflects that a lasting blame on earth would come of it to the noble one.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","vaazhkkaithunai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1148","lb":"CC1148","ev":"Gandharvadattai, ready to free Civakan by magic, hesitates for fear of the blame it would bring him.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1149":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1149,"t":["மன்னன்செய் தசிறை மாகட லுட்குளித் தாழ்வுழித்","தன்னையெய் திச்சிறை மீட்டன டன்மனை யாளெனி","னென்னையா வதிவ னாற்றலுங் கல்வியு மென்றுடன்","கொன்னும்வை யங்கொழிக் கும்பழிக் கென்செய்கோ தெய்வமே."],"p":"","r":"She asks the god what she is to do: if, while he is sunk in the king's prison as in the great sea, his wife comes to him and frees him, what then are his strength and learning worth, and how is the blame to be borne that would sweep the world for nothing?","n":"The shame of a man rescued by a woman is voiced by the woman who could rescue him, and it holds her hand: the epic lets the wife carry the husband's honour code.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","aanmai","kalvi","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1149","lb":"CC1149","ev":"","cl":[["a man freed from danger by his wife loses the credit of his own strength and learning","general","reported",3,"gandharvadattai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1150":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1150,"t":["செல்வனுற் றசிறை செய்யவ ணீக்குமென் றாற்பழி","யில்லையா யின்னவள் யானெனும் வேற்றுமை யில்லையே","சொல்லின்வெள் ளிம்மலை தோடவிழ் தாமரைப் பொன்மல","ரெல்லையா கும்பொதுப் பெண்ணவள் யான்குல மங்கையே."],"p":"","r":"She goes on: if it is said that Fortune herself freed the lord from prison there is no blame, as if there were no difference between Fortune and herself; but Fortune of the lotus is a woman common to all, while she, of the silver mountain, is a wife of family.","n":"The word division of lines 1-3 is doubtful; the reading takes செய்யவள் as the goddess of fortune and வெள்ளிம்மலை as Gandharvadattai's Vidyadhara home set against the lotus. On that reading she refuses the cover story that his luck saved him, because the help of a wife, unlike the help of fortune, would be his shame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","kudi","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1150","lb":"CC1150","ev":"","cl":[["Fortune is a woman common to all, unlike a wife of good family who belongs to one man","general","reported",4,"gandharvadattai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1151":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1151,"t":["ஆவதா கபுக ழும்புழி யும்மெழு நாளவை","தேவர்மாட் டும்முள மக்களு ளில்வழித் தேர்கலே","னோமெனெஞ் சம்மென நோக்கிநின் றாள்சிறைப் பட்டதன்","காவற்கன் றிற்புனிற் றாவன கார்மயிற் சாயலே."],"p":"","r":"Saying 'let what must be, be; fame and blame last but seven days, and I will not seek them out among gods or men; ache, my heart', the peacock-gentle one stands gazing toward the prisoner like a cow newly calved toward her calf in the pen.","n":"Line 1 (புகழும் புழியும் மெழு நாளவை) is read as fame and blame lasting seven days, which would be her setting reputation aside; the line is garbled as divided and the sense is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","pazhi","nenju","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1151","lb":"CC1151","ev":"Gandharvadattai holds back her magic and stands watching over Civakan in his captivity.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை",""],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1152":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1152,"t":["மாநகர் சுடுத லொன்றோ மதனனை யழித்த லொன்றோ","வானிக ரில்லா மைந்தர் கருதிய ததுவு நிற்க","வேய்நிக ரில்ல தோளி விஞ்சையால் விடுத்துக்கொள்ளப்","போயுயிர் வாழ்தல் வேண்டே னெனப்பொருள் சிந்திக் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan ponders: burning the great city is one course, destroying Madanan another, and the plan of his matchless friends may stand, but he does not want to go on living set free by the spells of his bamboo-shouldered wife.","n":"The husband reaches the same refusal as the wife (1149) without hearing her: both hold that his life is worth less than his name if she saves him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","vinjai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1152","lb":"CC1152","ev":"Civakan, bound, rejects rescue by his wife's magic.","cl":[["it is better not to live than to live freed by one's wife's power","general","reported",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1153":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1153,"t":["கச்சற நிமிர்ந்து மாந்தர்க் கடாவிடு களிறு போல","வுச்சியு மருங்கும் பற்றிப் பிளந்துயிர் பருகிக் கோண்மா","வச்சுற வழன்று சீறி யாட்டினம் புக்க தொப்பக்","குச்சென நிரைத்த யானைக் குழாமிரித் திடுவ லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says he will tear through the massed elephants as a must elephant loosed on men splits them, or as a raging lion leaps into a flock of goats, seizing crown and flank and drinking their lives.","n":"குச்சென நிரைத்த (line 4) is obscure; the elephants are taken as ranked close like a thicket.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1153","lb":"CC1153","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1154":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1154,"t":["மின்னிலங் கெயிற்று வேழம் வேழத்தாற் புடைத்துத் திண்டேர்","பொன்னிலங் கிவுளித் தேராற் புடைத்துவெங் குருதி பொங்க","வின்னுயி ரவனை யுண்ணு மெல்லைநாள் வந்த தில்லை","யென்னையிக் கிருமி கொன்றென் றோழனை நினைப்ப லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Striking elephant with elephant and chariot with chariot till hot blood surges, he could drink the life of that man, but the day of his end has not come; if this worm should kill him, he says, he will think of his friend.","n":"இக்கிருமி, 'this worm', is Civakan's word for the usurper. எல்லைநாள் வந்ததில்லை makes the timing of the enemy's death a set limit, not his own choice; the 'friend' is the god who was the dog of the bathing festival.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","maram","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1154","lb":"CC1154","ev":"Civakan resolves to call on his friend the god if the usurper moves to kill him.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1155":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1155,"t":["தோழனுந் தேவி மார்தங் குழாத்துளான் றுளும்பு முந்நீ","ரேழ்தரு பருதி தன்மே லிளம்பிறை கிடந்த தேபோற்","றாழ்தகை யார மார்பிற் சீவகன் குணங்க டம்மை","யாழெழீஇப் பாடக் கேட்டோ ரரம்பையைச் சேர்ந்திருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"His friend the god sat among his goddesses beside a celestial dancer, shining on his chest like a young crescent on the sun rising from the sea, listening to them sing Civakan's virtues to the yazh.","n":"The heaven he enjoys is the one his friend's mantra won him (947), and what its women sing there is the friend's praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1155","lb":"CC1155","ev":"Sudanjanan, in his heaven, listens to his goddesses sing Civakan's virtues.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1156":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1156,"t":["வயிரம் வேய்ந்த மணிநீண்முடி வாலொளி வானவன்","செயிரிற் றீர்ந்த செழுந்தாமரைக் கண்ணிட னாடலு","முயிரனா னைநினைந் தானுற்ற தோதியி னோக்கினான்","மயிலனார்க் குப்படி வைத்தவன் மால்விசும் பேறினான்."],"p":"","r":"The bright god with the diamond crown felt his left lotus eye throb, thought of the one dear to him as life, saw what had befallen him by his far-seeing knowledge, and leaving a likeness for his peacock-like goddesses rose through the great sky.","n":"ஓதி is read as the gods' far-seeing knowledge (avadhi); மயிலனார்க்குப் படி வைத்து is read as leaving a likeness or token for his consorts, which is doubtful. The throbbing eye works as an omen on a god as on a mortal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","nanri","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1156","lb":"CC1156","ev":"Warned by an omen, Sudanjanan learns of Civakan's peril and sets out from heaven.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1157":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1157,"t":["இடியு மின்னும் முழக்கும்மிவற் றானுல கந்நிறைந்","தொடியு மூழி யிவணின்றுறு கால்வரை கீழ்ந்தென","நடலை நோக்கிக் கதிர்நாணுவ தொப்ப மறைந்தபின்","கடலை யேந்தி நிலத்திட்டென மாரி கலந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Thunder, lightning and roaring filled the world as if the wind of the aeon's end were tearing out the mountains; the sun hid as though ashamed to see the distress, and rain poured down as if the sea had been lifted and thrown upon the land.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1157","lb":"CC1157","ev":"A storm and darkness fall on the city as the god comes down.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1158":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1158,"t":["விண்ணு மண்ணும் மறியாதுவி லங்கொடு மாந்தர்தங்","கண்ணும் வாயும் மிழந்தாங்கடல் கொண்டது காண்கெனப்","பெண்ணு மாணும் மிரங்கப் பெருமான்மகன் சாமியை","யண்ண லேந்தி யகலம்புலிக் கொண்டெழுந் தேகினான்."],"p":"","r":"With sky and earth indistinguishable, beasts and men robbed of sight and speech, and women and men crying 'see, the sea has taken it', the lord lifted the king's son Civakan, held him to his breast, and rose and went.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1158","lb":"CC1158","ev":"Under cover of the storm, Sudanjanan lifts Civakan and carries him away through the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்","மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1159":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1159,"t":["குன்றுண் டோங்கு திரடோளவற் கொண்டெழுந் தேகலு","நன்றுண் டாக வெனநன்னுதல் வாழ்த்தினள் வாழ்த்தலு","மொன்றுண் டாயிற் றவளுள்ளழி நோயுறு காளையை","யென்றுண் டாங்கொல் லினிக்கட்படு நாளெனுஞ் சிந்தையே."],"p":"","r":"As he rose carrying the hill-shouldered one, she of the fair brow blessed him, 'may good come', and with that blessing one thought rose in her: when will be the day her eyes see again the youth for whom her heart aches?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1159","lb":"CC1159","ev":"Gandharvadattai blesses the departing rescuer and longs to see Civakan again.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1160":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1160,"t":["சந்தமா லைத்தொகை தாழ்ந்துசாந் தங்கமழ் பூமியுள்","வந்தவிண் ணோர்களை வாழ்த்தியேத் திம்மலர் மாலைதூ","யெந்தை மார்க ளெழுகென்ன வேகவிடுத் தாள்குரல்","சிந்தை செய்யுஞ் சிறகர்க்கிளி தோற்குமந் தீஞ்சொலாள்."],"p":"","r":"In the fragrant ground strewn with garlands she praised the heavenly ones who had come, scattered flowers, and sent them away, saying 'my fathers, rise', she whose voice outdoes the parrot's.","n":"The powers called up in 1147 go back unused: her restraint, not her magic, is what the episode records of her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinjai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1160","lb":"CC1160","ev":"Gandharvadattai dismisses with worship the spell-powers she had summoned.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","விஞ்ஞை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1161":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1161,"t":["மலைத்தொகை யானை மன்னன் மைத்துனன் மதன னென்பான்","கொலைத்தொகை வேலி னானைக் கொல்லிய கொண்டு போந்தா","னலத்தகை யவனைக் காணா னஞ்சுயிர்த் தஞ்சி நோக்கிச்","சிலைத்தொழிற் றடக்கை மள்ளர்க் கிற்றெனச் செப்பு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Madanan, brother-in-law of the king of many elephants, who had brought the spear-bearing youth out to kill him, cannot find the handsome one; sighing and staring in fear, he speaks to the bowmen.","n":"Madanan is the king's மைத்துனன் here, which ties the executioner to the usurper by marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1161","lb":"CC1161","ev":"Madanan finds Civakan gone and turns in fear to his men.","cl":[],"ag":["மதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1162":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1162,"t":["மன்னனாற் சீறப் பட்ட மைந்தனைக் கொல்லப் போந்தா","மென்னினிச் சொல்லிச் சேறு மென்செய்தும் யாங்க ளெல்லா","மின்னது பட்ட தென்றா லெரிவிளக் குறுக்கு நம்மைத்","துன்னுபு சூழ்ந்து தோன்றச் சொல்லுமின் செய்வ தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says they came to kill the youth the king raged at, and asks what they will now say and do, for if it is known what has happened the king will burn them like lamp wicks; let them gather round and tell him what to do.","n":"எரிவிளக் குறுக்கு நம்மை is read as the king burning them like lamps; the phrase is hard as divided. The fear of the tyrant, not loyalty, drives what follows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1162","lb":"CC1162","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மதனன்","மள்ளர்"],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1163":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1163,"t":["வாழ்வதோ ருபாய நாடி மதியுடம் பட்டு வல்லே","சூழ்வினை யாள ராங்க ணொருவனைத் தொடர்ந்து பற்றிப்","போழ்படப் பிளந்து வாளிற் புரட்டியிட்டரியக் கண்டே","யாழ்கல மாந்தர் போல வணிநக ரழுங்கிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Seeking a way to save their lives, the plotters agreed, seized one man, split him with a sword and rolled his body over, and the fair city grieved like people on a sinking ship.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","vanjam","kollaamai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1163","lb":"CC1163","ev":"Madanan's men kill a bystander and pass his corpse off as Civakan's, and the city mourns.","cl":[],"ag":["மள்ளர்","மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1164":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1164,"t":["காய்சின வெகுளி வேந்தே களிற்றொடும் பொருத காளை","மாசனம் பெரிது மொய்த்து மழையினோ டிருளுங் காற்றும்","பேசிற்றான் பெரிதுந் தோன்றப் பிழைத்துய்யப் போத லஞ்சி","வாசங்கொ டாரி னானை மார்புபோழ்ந் துருட்டி யிட்டேம்."],"p":"","r":"Madanan tells the king: the youth who fought the elephant, with a great crowd thronging and rain, darkness and wind making escape likely, was in fear of losing him split open in the chest and rolled over.","n":"The lie is built on the true storm: the god's darkness becomes the executioner's excuse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1164","lb":"CC1164","ev":"Madanan falsely reports to Kattiyangaran that Civakan has been killed.","cl":[],"ag":["மதனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதனன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1165":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1165,"t":["அருள்வலி யாண்மைகல்வி யழகறி விளமை யூக்கந்","திருமலி யீகை போகந் திண்புகழ் நிண்பு சுற்ற","மொருவரிவ் வுலகில் யாரே சீவக னொக்கு நீரார்","பெரிதரி திவனைக் கொன்றாய் பெறுகெனச் சிறப்புச் செய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king says no one in the world has Civakan's nature, his grace, strength, manliness, learning, beauty, wisdom, youth, zeal, giving, enjoyment, firm fame and kin, that it was very hard to kill such a man, and honours Madanan with a reward.","n":"The usurper lists the very virtues that make Civakan fit to rule and rewards his murder for that reason: the praise is the measure of the threat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kolai","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1165","lb":"CC1165","ev":"Kattiyangaran praises the supposedly dead Civakan and rewards Madanan for killing him.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","மதனன்"],"ch":"cc:50"},"cc:1166":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1166,"t":["வீட்டருஞ் சிறையிற் றேவன் விடுத்துயக் கொள்ளப் பட்ட","கோட்டமில் குணத்தி னான்போ யென்செய்கின் றான்கொ லென்னிற்","கூட்டரக் கெறிந்த பஞ்சிற் கூடிய பளிங்கிற் றோன்றுந்","தீட்டரும் படிவ மன்னான் றிறங்கிளந் துரைத்து மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet turns to ask what the hero of flawless virtue did after the god set him free from the prison none escapes, and promises to tell the deeds of the one whose form, like a figure seen in crystal, no painter could draw.","n":"The opening verse of Padumaiyar Ilampakam bridges the plot: the rescue (வீட்டரும் சிறையில் தேவன் விடுத்து உயக் கொள்ளப்பட்ட) was told at the end of the previous ilampakam, and the poet now resumes with the hero's travels.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1166","lb":"CC1166","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1167":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1167,"t":["விலங்கிவில் லுமிழும் பூணான் விழுச்சிறைப் பட்ட போழ்து","மலங்கலந் தாரி னான்வந் தருஞ்சிறை விடுத்த போழ்தும்","புலம்பலு மகிழ்வு நெஞ்சிற் பொலிதலு மின்றிப் பொன்னார்ந்","துலங்கலந் துயர்ந்த தோளா னூழ்வினை யென்று விட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Neither when he lay bound in the great prison nor when the garlanded god came and freed him did grief or joy rise in the hero's heart; he let both go, saying it was the working of former deeds.","n":"The word is the hero's (ஊழ்வினை என்று விட்டான), and the narrator presents it as his virtue. The plot gives the karma a concrete face: the god who frees him is the dog he helped die well (943-948), as the god says in 1172, so the 'former deed' here is the hero's own act in this very life, not an unknown deed of a past birth. The verse names no school, so the claim is left general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","samanilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1167","lb":"CC1167","ev":"Civakan, freed from prison by the god, meets both captivity and release without grief or joy, ascribing them to former deeds.","cl":[["captivity and release alike come from former deeds, so neither calls for grief or joy","general","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1168":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1168,"t":["வானர முகள நாக மலர்துதைந் தொழுக வஞ்சித்","தேனிரைத் தெழுந்து திங்க ளிறாலெனச் சென்று மொய்க்குங்","கானம ரருவிக் குன்றிற் காய்கதிர் சுமந்தோர் திங்கண்","மேனிமிர்ந் தேறி யாங்குத் தேவன்வெற் பேறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As a moon bearing the burning sun climbs high, so the god climbed his mountain carrying the hero, a mountain whose streams run thick with flowers, where monkeys and elephants roam and honeycombs hang like moons.","n":"காய்கதிர் சுமந்து ஓர் திங்கள்: the god is the moon and the hero the sun he carries, which reverses rank and echoes the dog's promised body 'shining like the burning disc of the sun' (காய்கதிர் மண்டலம், 943).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1168","lb":"CC1168","ev":"The god carries Civakan up to his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1169":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1169,"t":["திங்களைத் தெளித்திட் டன்ன பாற்கடற் றிரைசெய் தெண்ணீர்","வெங்கள்விட் டலர்ந்த கண்ணி விண்ணவ னுரிமை தன்னான்","மங்கல வகையி னாட்டி மணியணி கலங்கள் சேர்த்திப்","பங்கய நெடுங்க ணானைப் பவித்திர குமர னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"With clear water from the waves of the milk ocean, bright as moonlight, the god had his women bathe the hero in the auspicious way and deck him with jewels, and named the lotus-eyed youth the Holy Prince.","n":"பவித்திர குமரன் (the pure or holy prince) is a new name given by the god, in the manner of a consecration bath; it marks the hero's passage from prison to divine favour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1169","lb":"CC1169","ev":"The god's women bathe and adorn Civakan with milk-ocean water, and the god names him Pavittira Kumaran.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்","தோளியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1170":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1170,"t":["பொன்னணி காம்பு செய்த பொழிகதிர்த் திங்கள் போலும்","பின்னிய முத்த மாலைப் பிணையறாழ் குடையி னீழற்","கன்னியர் கவரி வீசக் கனமணிக் குழைவில் வீச","வின்னிசைக் கூத்து நோக்கி யிருந்தனன் றிலக மன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Under a parasol like the full moon on a golden staff, hung with strings of pearls, with maidens waving yak-tail whisks and gem earrings flashing, the hero sat watching the dance to sweet music.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1170","lb":"CC1170","ev":"Civakan sits in state on the god's mountain, fanned by maidens, watching dance and music.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோளியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1171":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1171,"t":["இருமலர்க் குவளை யுண்க ணிமைப்பிலாப் பயத்தைப் பெற்ற","வரிமலர்த் தாரி னான்ற னழகுகண் டளிய வென்னாத்","திருமலர்க் கோதை யைம்பாற் றேவியர் தொடர்பு கேட்ப","வெரிமணிப் பூணி னானு மின்னண மியம்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the beauty of the garlanded youth, the god's queens felt their unblinking eyes had found their reward, pitied him and asked how the god was bound to him, and the jewelled god answered thus.","n":"இமைப்பிலாப் பயத்தைப் பெற்ற: gods do not blink, and the conceit is that their never-closing eyes are at last put to good use. அளிய (pitiable, dear) may be their pity for his sufferings or simply their tenderness; the verse does not say which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1171","lb":"CC1171","ev":"The god's queens ask him what tie binds him to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["தோளியர்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1172":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1172,"t":["பிணிக்குலத் தகவயிற் பிறந்த நோய்கெடுத்","தணித்தகை யுடம்பெனக் கருளி நோக்கினான்","கணிப்பருங் குணத்தொகைக் காளை யென்றனன்","மணிக்கலத் தகத்தமிர் தனைய மாண்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The god tells them that when he was born among dogs this man removed his suffering, looked on him with grace and gave him this cooled and gracious body, and calls him a bull of countless virtues.","n":"பிணிக்குலத்து அகவயின் is unusual for 'in the race of dogs'; the sense is clear from the story (941-948) but the wording is not a common name for dogs. The god gives the credit to the man (எனக்கு அருளி), not to the mantra Civakan whispered, so his rescue in 1167 is gratitude repaying a gift; the poem treats the debt of a benefit as the bond between god and man.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","piravi","yaakkai","nanri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1172","lb":"CC1172","ev":"Sudanjanan tells his queens that Civakan freed him from his dog's birth and gave him his god's body.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","தோளியர்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1173":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1173,"t":["கடற்சுற வுயரிய காளை யன்னவ","னடற்கரும் பகைகெடுத் தகன்ற நீணில","மடத்தகை யவளொடும் வதுவை நாட்டிநாங்","கொடுக்குவ மெனத்தெய்வ மகளிர் கூறினார்."],"p":"","r":"The divine women say that they will destroy the hard enmity of this youth, who is like the god with the shark banner, win him the wide earth, establish his marriage with a gentle maiden and give him all of it.","n":"கடற்சுறவு உயரிய காளை is Kama, whose banner bears the makara; the offer is the whole programme of the rest of the epic (vengeance on Kattiyangaran, the throne, the marriages) handed over as a divine gift, and 1174 shows the hero declining to take it so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arasu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1173","lb":"CC1173","ev":"The god's women offer to destroy Civakan's enemy, win him the land and marry him to a maiden.","cl":[],"ag":["தோளியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோளியர்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1174":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1174,"t":["செருநிலத் தவனுயிர் செகுத்து மற்றெனக்","கிருநில மியைவதற் கெண்ணல் வேண்டுமோ","திருநிலக் கிழமையுந் தேவர் தேயமுந்","தருநிலத் தெமக்கெனிற் றருகுந் தன்மையீர்."],"p":"","r":"The hero answers that they need not plan how the earth may come to him by taking the enemy's life in battle; if they would give him the lordship of earth and the land of the gods, they are surely able to give it.","n":"The sense of the reply turns on tone. எண்ணல் வேண்டுமோ may mean 'need you trouble to plan it' (he will win the land himself) or simply 'is there any need to think about it' (they can do it at once); the second half (தருகுந் தன்மையீர்) may be courtesy, or a polite deflection that their power could give even heaven, so earth is a small thing he will take on his own. Read with 1175, where he asks only to see the world, the first sense, a refusal of a gift he means to earn, is likelier.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","arasu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1174","lb":"CC1174","ev":"Civakan answers the divine women's offer.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோளியர்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1175":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1175,"t":["மண்மிசைக் கிடந்தன மலையுங் கானமு","நண்ணுதற் கரியன நாடும் பொய்கையுங்","கண்மனங் குளிர்ப்பன வாறுங் காண்பதற்","கெண்ணமொன் றுளதெனக் கிலங்கு பூணினாய்."],"p":"","r":"He tells the god that he has one wish: to see the mountains and forests that lie on the earth, the lands and pools hard to reach, and the rivers that cool the eye and the mind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1175","lb":"CC1175","ev":"Civakan asks the god's leave to go and see the lands of the earth.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1176":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1176,"t":["ஊற்றுநீர்க் கூவலு ளுறையு மீனனார்","வேற்றுநாட தன்சுவை விடுத்தன் மேயினார்","போற்றுநீ போவல்யா னென்று கூறினாற்","காற்றின தமைதியங் கறியக் கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"Those who are like fish living in a spring-fed well never set out to taste another land; 'Keep well, I shall go,' the hero said, and the god then told him plainly the nature of the road ahead.","n":"Line 4 (காற்றின தமைதி) is best divided கூறினான் ஆற்றினது அமைதி, 'the nature of the way'; read as காற்றின் (of the wind) it makes poor sense. Lines 1-3 are the hero's speech and line 4 the narrator's; the god's directions fill 1177-1190.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1176","lb":"CC1176","ev":"Civakan takes leave of the god, who begins to describe the road.","cl":[["one who never leaves home, like a fish in a well, knows nothing of other lands","general","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1177":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1177,"t":["இம்மலைக் கிரண்டு காத மிறந்தபி னிருண்டு தோன்று","மம்மலை யரண பாத மென்பதன் றாள்வாய்த் தோன்றுந்","தம்வினை கழுவு கின்றார் சாரணர் தரணி காவல்","வெம்மையி னகன்று போந்து விழைவறத் துறந்து விட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Two kathams beyond this mountain the god says a dark mountain called Aranapatham appears, and at its foot the Caranas wash away their own karma: men who left the cruelty of guarding the earth and renounced without desire.","n":"சாரணர் are the Jain ascetics with the power of sky-going; the verse makes renunciation a king's turn from rule, which it calls cruelty (தரணி காவல் வெம்மை), and the cleansing is their own act (தம்வினை கழுவுகின்றார்). The scene foreshadows the hero's own end in Mutti Ilampakam.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vinai","thavam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1177","lb":"CC1177","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[["kings who give up the violence of rule and renounce without desire wash away their own karma by austerity","jain","asserted",3,"sudanjanan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1178":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1178,"t":["சிந்தையிற் பருதி யன்னார் சேவடி யிறைஞ்ச லோடும்","வெந்திற லியக்கி தோன்றி விருந்தெதிர் கொண்டு பேணித்","தந்தவ ளமிர்த மூட்ட வுண்டவட் பிரிந்த காலைச்","சந்துடைச் சாரல் சேறி தரணிமேற் றிலக மன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"When the hero bows at the red feet of those whose minds are like the sun, the god says a yakshi of fierce power will appear, receive him as a guest and feed him nectar, and after leaving her he should go to the sandal-wood slopes.","n":"The order matters: hospitality from the yakshi (இயக்கி) follows on worship of the ascetics; reverence opens the road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","vazhipaadu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1178","lb":"CC1178","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1179":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1179,"t":["அங்குநின் றகன்றபி னையைங் காவதம்.","வெங்களி விடுமத வேழப் பேரினந்","தங்கிய காடது தனிச்செல் வாரிலை","கங்கையின் கரையது கடலிற் றோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Five kavathams on, the god says, lies a forest where herds of rutting elephants stay, which no one crosses alone, on the bank of the Ganga that looks like a sea.","n":"கங்கை here may be the great river or a name for a local river; the route is a romance geography and the verse does not let one fix it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1179","lb":"CC1179","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1180":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1180,"t":["புனலெரி தவழ்ந்தெனப் பூத்த தாமரை.","வனமது வாளென வாளை பாய்வன","மனமகிழ் பெருந்தடம் வலத்திட் டேகுதி","யினமலர்த் தாரினா யிரண்டு காதமே"],"p":"","r":"Keeping on his right a great joyful pool where lotuses bloom as if fire crept over water and vaalai fish leap like swords, the hero is to go two kathams more.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1180","lb":"CC1180","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1181":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1181,"t":["காந்திய மணியொடு வயிரம் பொன்கலந்","தேந்தனின் றோளென விரண்டு குன்றுபோய்ப்","பூந்துகின் மகளிரிற் பொலிந்து போர்த்ததோர்","பேந்தரு பேய்வனம் பெரிய காண்டியே."],"p":"","r":"Past two hills mixed with gems, diamonds and gold like the hero's own shoulders, the god says he will see a great ghoul forest that is decked like women in flowered cloth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","maayai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1181","lb":"CC1181","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1182":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1182,"t":["இளவெயின் மணிவரை யெறித்திட் டன்னதோ","ரளவரு குங்குமத் தகன்ற மார்பினாய்","களவினி னணிநலங் கவர்ந்த கள்வவென்","றுளர்மணிக் கொம்பனா ருருகி நைபவே."],"p":"","r":"There, says the god to the saffron-chested hero, ghouls like swaying gem-creepers will melt and pine, calling him the thief who stole their beauty by stealth.","n":"The ghouls speak the language of a lovesick heroine (களவினின் அணி நலம் கவர்ந்த கள்வ); the deceit is that akam's own words are the lure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1182","lb":"CC1182","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1183":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1183,"t":["பழங்குழைந் தனையதோர் மெலிவிற் பையென","முழங்கழல் வேட்கையின் முறுகி யூர்தரத்","தழும்பத மிதுவெனச் சார்ந்து புல்லலும்","பிழிந்துயி ருண்டிடும் பேய்க ளாபவே."],"p":"","r":"Softening like ripe fruit and burning with desire, they come near saying this is the moment for an embrace, and when they embrace they wring out a man's life and drink it: they are ghouls.","n":"The claim is put as a fact of this forest, but it carries the ethic of the warning against deceiving women; the final word (பேய்கள் ஆபவே) unmasks the lovers of 1182.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamam","vanjam","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1183","lb":"CC1183","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[["the embrace of a seductive woman who is not what she seems drains a man's life","general","asserted",4,"sudanjanan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1184":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1184,"t":["கண்டபேய் நகரி னீங்கிக் காவதங் கடந்து தோன்றும்","வெண்டலைப் புணரி வீசிக் கிடந்தபொற் றீவிற் றாகிக்","கொண்டுலப் பரிய செந்நெல் கொடிக்கரும் புடுத்த வேலி","நுண்டுகி னுழைந்த வல்குற் பவளமொத் தினியதொன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Leaving the ghoul town and crossing a kavatham, the god says, a land appears beside a golden island washed by white-crested waves, fenced with red paddy and sugarcane, as sweet as coral and the fine-clothed waist of a woman.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1184","lb":"CC1184","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1185":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1185,"t":["படுமழை பருவம் பொய்யாப் பல்லவ தேய மென்னுந்","தடமலர்க் குவளைப் பட்டந் தழுவிய யாணர் நன்னாட்","டிடைநெறி யசைவு தீர விருந்தவ ணேக லுற்றாற்","கடநெறி கடத்தற் கின்னாக் கல்லத ரத்த முண்டே."],"p":"","r":"That good rich land is called the Pallava country, where the rains never fail their season and lily beds lie like a girdle; if he rests there to ease the toil of the road and then goes on, a stony desert path hard to cross awaits him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1185","lb":"CC1185","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1186":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1186,"t":["நுதிகொண்டன வெம்பர னுண்ணிலைவேல்","பதிகொண்டு பரந்தன போன்றுளவால்","விதிகண்டவ ரல்லது மீதுசெலார்","வதிகொண்டதொர் வெவ்வழல் வாய்சொலின்வேம்."],"p":"","r":"The god says the stones there are like the fine sharp spears of a fierce lord set out over the ground; none but those who know the way pass over them, and the very heat that dwells there would burn the mouth that told of it.","n":"The metre changes here. விதி கண்டவர் அல்லது மீது செலார் can mean 'only those who know the rule of the road go over it' or 'only those whose fate has been seen (who are doomed) go there'; வெம்பரன் (a fierce lord, or a hot stone) is also open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1186","lb":"CC1186","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1187":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1187,"t":["குழவிப்பிடி குஞ்சர மாழ்குமெனத்","தழுவிச்சுடு வெவ்வழ றாங்குவன","கெழுவிப்பெடை யைக்கிளர் சேவறழீஇத்","தொழுதிச்சிற கிற்றுய ராற்றுவன."],"p":"","r":"In that heat, the god says, elephants hug their calves and bear the fire themselves lest the young faint, and cock birds fold their hens in their wings and ease their suffering.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1187","lb":"CC1187","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1188":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1188,"t":["கலையின் பிணை கன்றிடு மென்றுகசிந்","திலையின்னிழ லவ்வயி னின்மையினா","னிலையின்னிழ றானது நின்றுகொடுத்","துலையும்வெயி னின்றுரு கும்முரவோய்"],"p":"","r":"Because there is no leaf shade there, the stag, fearing his doe will wither, stands and gives her the shade of his own body and himself melts in the scorching sun, he tells the strong hero.","n":"1187-1188 together are the three tender creatures of the palai tradition (the tusker, the bird with its wings, the stag giving its shadow); the Cintamani shifts the first from the mate to the calf.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","idumbai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1188","lb":"CC1188","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1189":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1189,"t":["கடநாக மதங்கலந் துக்கநிலத்","துடைநாணென மின்னென வொண்மணியம்","படநாக மழன்று பதைத்துவரு","மடனாமய லார்மனம் வைப்பதுவே."],"p":"","r":"Where the must of rutting elephants has dripped on the ground, hooded snakes with bright gems, flashing like a waist-string or lightning, burn and writhe; this is the road, the god says, on which only those foolish in love would set their minds.","n":"Line 4 (மடனாம் அயலார் மனம் வைப்பதுவே) is obscure; அயலார் may be strangers to the road or the deluded, and the construal of the snakes in lines 2-3 is also doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1189","lb":"CC1189","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1190":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1190,"t":["நெறியிற்றளர் வார்தம நெஞ்சுருகிப்","பொறியிற்றளர் வார்புரி வார்சடையா","ரறிமற்றவர் தாபத ரவ்வழியார்","கறைமுற்றிய காமரு வேலவனே."],"p":"","r":"On that road, the god tells the spear-bearing hero, there are matted-haired tapasas who have weakened the hold of the senses and practise austerities, at whose sight those who falter on the road melt in heart.","n":"பொறியில் தளர்வார் likely means those who have slackened the senses (ascetics) but could mean those weakened in body; the tapasas (தாபதர், with சடை) are forest ascetics, not the Jain Caranas of 1177, and the poem keeps the two apart.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1190","lb":"CC1190","ev":"Sudanjanan describes to Civakan the next stage of the way from his mountain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:51"},"cc:1191":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1191,"t":["குலைவாழை பழுத்த கொழும்பழனு","நிலைமாத்தன தேமுறு தீங்கனியும்","பலவீன்றன முள்ளுடை யள்ளமிர்து","மலையாற்றயல் யாவு மடுத்துளவே."],"p":"","r":"Beside the mountain river there are plantains with ripe clustered fruit, mango trees with sweet honeyed fruit, and jackfruit trees that have borne their thorny, nectar-filled fruit.","n":"The god's route speech to Civakan, begun in the previous run, continues here; the abundance is a traveller's assurance of food on the way as much as praise of the place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1191","lb":"CC1191","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1192":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1192,"t":["வளம்பைம்பொனும் வாளொளி நீண்மணியு","மொளிர்கின்றன வோசனை நீணிலமுந்","தளர்வொன்றிலர் தாபதர் தாம்விழையுங்","குளிர்கொண்டதொர் சித்திர கூடமதே"],"p":"","r":"Gold, long gems of bright lustre and land a yojana wide shine there: this is the cool Chitrakuta that the ascetics, never wearied, long for.","n":"சித்திர கூடம் names a hill of hermitages; the verse sets the ascetics' desire on a place rather than on wealth, though the place is itself described as shining with gold and gems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1192","lb":"CC1192","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1193":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1193,"t":["முழவின்னிசை மூரி முழங்கருவி","கழையின்றிறுணி சந்தோடு கல்லெனவீர்த்","திழியும்வயி ரத்தொடி னம்மணிகொண்","டழியும்புன லஞ்சன மாநதியே"],"p":"","r":"The great river Anjana comes down roaring like drums and great cascades, dragging bamboo, sandalwood and diamonds and carrying away heaps of gems.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1193","lb":"CC1193","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1194":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1194,"t":["இதுபள்ளி யிடம்பனி மால்வரைதா","னதுதெள்ளறல் யாறுவை தேமரமாக்","கதிதள்ளி யிராது கடைப்பிடிநீ","மதிதள்ளி யிடும்வழை சூழ்பொழிலே"],"p":"","r":"The god points out the hermitage, the snowy mountain, the river of clear sand and the grove ringed by surapunnai trees that brush the moon, and bids Civakan hold to his way without slackening his pace.","n":"The deictic இது ... அது ('this is ... that is') makes the speech a map shown from above, fitting a god who has carried the hero through the sky.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","naadu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1194","lb":"CC1194","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1195":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1195,"t":["வருந்து நீர்மையம் மாதவர் பள்ளியுட்","குருந்த மேறிய கூரரும் பார்முல்லை","பொருந்து கேள்வரைப் புல்லிய பொன்னனார்","மருங்கு போன்றணி மாக்கவின் கொண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"In the hermitage of the great ascetics who mortify themselves, the jasmine with sharp buds climbing the kurundu tree has the beauty of gold-like women clasping the side of the husbands they are joined to.","n":"The image of the faithful wife embracing her husband is set inside the hermitage of those who have renounced: the poem keeps the householder's ideal and the ascetic's place side by side without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","karpu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1195","lb":"CC1195","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1196":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1196,"t":["குரவ நீடிய கொன்றையங் கானின்வாய்","வரகு வாளிற் றொலைச்சுநர் பாடலி","னரவ வண்டொடு தேனினம் யாழ்செயும்","பரவை மாநிலம் பன்னிரு காதமே."],"p":"","r":"The wide land of the konrai and kura forest, where bees and honey-bees play the yazh to the songs of those who reap the millet with sickles, stretches twelve kaathams.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1196","lb":"CC1196","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1197":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1197,"t":["ஆங்க வெல்லை யிகந்தடு தேறலும்","பூங்கட் பொற்குட முந்நிறைத் தீண்டிய","வேங்கு கம்பலத் தின்னிசை சூழ்வயற்","றாங்கு சீர்த்தக்க நாட்டணி காண்டியே."],"p":"","r":"Beyond that boundary Civakan will see the beauty of a land of fitting renown, with strong toddy, gold pots filled with flowers, and fields ringed by the sweet sound of drums.","n":"சீர்த்தக்க நாட்டு may name the Takka land, where the patikam (20) places Civakan's later marriage to a merchant's daughter, or simply mean 'a land of fitting excellence'; the division of the words does not settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1197","lb":"CC1197","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1198":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1198,"t":["பாளைவாய்க் கமுகி னெற்றிப் படுபழ முதிர விண்டு","நீள்கழைக் கரும்பி னெற்றி நெய்ம்முதிர் தொடையல் கீறி","வாளைவா யுறைப்ப நக்கி வராலொடு மறலு மென்ப","காளைநீ கடந்து செல்லுங் காமரு கவின்கொ ணாடே."],"p":"","r":"In the lovely land Civakan crosses, ripe areca fruit split from their spathes and tear the honey-heavy joints of the tall sugarcane, and the vaalai fish lick the drip and fight with the varaal.","n":"Like the patikam's opening landscape of Emangadam (31), fruit falling on fruit is the stock sign of a rich land; the vocative காளை marks this as speech to the hero.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1198","lb":"CC1198","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1199":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1199,"t":["அங்கதன் றனதிடங் கடந்து போம்வழிப்","பொங்குபூஞ் சண்பகப் போது போர்த்துரா","யங்கநாட் டரிவையர் கூந்த னாறித்தே","னெங்குமொய்த் திழிவதோர் யாறு தோன்றுமே"],"p":"","r":"On the way past that land a river will appear, covered with bunches of champak flowers, scented by the hair of the women of the land, with bees swarming down its whole length.","n":"யங்கநாட் may be read அங்க நாட்டு, the Anga land, or as அங்கு அந் நாட்டு, 'of that land there'; the route's geography turns on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1199","lb":"CC1199","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1200":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1200,"t":["மின்னுடைய மணிபல வரன்றி மேதகு","தன்னுடைய நலம்பகிர்ந் துலக மூட்டலிற்","பொன்னுடைக் கலையல்குற் கணிகைப் பூம்புனன்","மன்னுடை வேலினாய் வல்லை நீந்தினால"],"p":"","r":"That river, the god says, sweeps up many bright gems and, sharing its own excellence to feed the world, is a courtesan with a gold-girdled waist; if Civakan swims across it quickly,","n":"The courtesan here is a figure of open-handed bounty: she shares her நலம் with the whole world as the river shares its gems. The sentence does not end in this verse; it runs into 1201.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","eegai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1200","lb":"CC1200","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1201":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1201,"t":["யானைவெண் மருப்பினா லியற்றி யாவது","மானமாக் கவரிவெண் மயிரின் வேய்ந்தன","தேனெயூன் கிழங்குகாய் பழங்கள் செற்றிய","கானவர் குரம்பைசூழ் காடு தோன்றுமே"],"p":"","r":"a forest will appear, ringed by the huts of the forest people, built of white elephant tusks, thatched with the white hair of the yak, and crammed with honey, meat, roots, unripe and ripe fruit.","n":"The hunters' huts are rich in what the forest gives, meat among them, which prepares the harsher judgment on the forest people in 1205.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1201","lb":"CC1201","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1202":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1202,"t":["கடுந்துடிக் குரலொடு கடையுங் கட்குர","னெடுங்கைமான் குரன்மணி யருவி நீள்குர","லடும்புலிக் குரலொடு மயங்கி யஞ்சிய","விடும்பைமான் குரல்விளி யெங்கு மிக்கவே."],"p":"","r":"Everywhere the sounds mingle and swell: the harsh drum, the voice of those churning toddy, the call of the long-trunked elephant, the long voice of the gem-bearing falls, and the cry of the frightened deer mixed with the roar of the killing tiger.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1202","lb":"CC1202","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1203":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1203,"t":["பொன்னணி திகிரியஞ் செல்வன் பொற்புடைக்","கன்னிய மகளிரிற் காண்டற் கரியன","நன்மணி புரித்தன வாவி நான்குள","கன்னவி றோளினாய் காட்டு வாயவே."],"p":"","r":"The god tells Civakan that there are four pools, set with fine gems, as hard to see as the virgin daughters of a gold-wheeled emperor, which will show themselves to him.","n":"The comparison with a cakravartin's secluded daughters makes the pools a guarded treasure that appears only to one who is worthy, a promise to the hero more than a description.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1203","lb":"CC1203","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1204":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1204,"t":["அருங்கலச் சேயித ழார்ந்த வாவியொன்","றிரும்பெரி பொன்செயு மிரத நீரதொன்","றொருங்குநோய் தீர்ப்பதொன் றமிர்த மல்லதொன்","றரும்பவிழ் குவளைநீர் வாவி யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Of the pools, one is filled with red petals, one has a quickening water that turns dark iron to gold, one cures all diseases together, and one, beyond nectar or other than it, is a pool of opening blue water-lilies.","n":"இரத நீர் is rasa-water, the alchemist's transmuting fluid; the verse lists it beside a healing water without remark. The count of four in 1203 does not fit the items cleanly: அமிர்தம் அல்லது ஒன்று may name a nectar pool and leave the lily pool as a fourth, or describe the lily pool as other than nectar.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","vaadham","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1204","lb":"CC1204","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1205":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1205,"t":["கையடு சிலையினர் காட்டுள் வாழ்பவர்","பையுடை யாக்கையர் பாவ மூர்த்திய","ரையெனத் தோன்றுவர் தோன்றி யாளழித்","துய்வகை யரிதென வுடலங் கொள்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The forest dwellers with killing bows in hand, men of wretched bodies and forms of sin itself, show themselves suddenly, destroy travellers, and take their bodies, thinking escape impossible for them.","n":"The judgment பாவ மூர்த்தியர், 'embodiments of sin', falls on the forest people as a class; the god names it as a danger of the road, not as a teaching, and the closing words (உய்வகை அரிதென) may be either the victims' plight or the killers' reasoning.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kollaamai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1205","lb":"CC1205","ev":"","cl":[["those who live by killing travellers are sin embodied","general","asserted",2,"sudanjanan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1206":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1206,"t":["அண்ணன்மே லரிவையர் கண்ணின் மொய்த்தவண்","மண்ணின்மேன் மாந்தர்கண் மொய்க்கும் வாவியை","யெண்ணமொன் றின்றியே யிடத்திட் டேகினாற்","றுண்ணெனச் சிலையவர் தொழுது காண்பவே"],"p":"","r":"If Civakan passes, with no thought for it, the pool on which men's eyes fasten as women's eyes fasten on a noble man, keeping it on his left, the bowmen will at once bow and gaze at him.","n":"The instruction works like a charm: a ritual way of passing the pool turns the killers of 1205 into worshippers. What makes them bow is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1206","lb":"CC1206","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1207":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1207,"t":["பாடல்வண் டியாழ்செயும் பசும்பொற் கிண்கிணித்","தோடலர் கோதைமின் றுளும்பு மேகலை","யாடிய கூத்தித னசைந்த சாயல்போன்","றூடுபோக் கினியதங் கோரைங் காதமே."],"p":"","r":"Beyond, for five kaathams, the way runs sweetly, like the swaying grace of a dancing woman whose anklet-bells ring and whose girdle flashes as the bees play the yazh in her garland.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1207","lb":"CC1207","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1208":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1208,"t":["கோதைவீழ்ந் ததுவென முல்லை கத்திகைப்","போதுவேய்ந் தினமலர் பொழிந்து கற்புடை","மாதரார் மனமெனக் கிடந்த செந்நெறி","தாதின்மே னடந்ததோர் தன்மைத் தென்பவே"],"p":"","r":"The straight red road, they say, lies on flower-pollen, strewn with fresh blossoms as if a garland had fallen, with jasmine and kaththigai, and is like the minds of chaste women.","n":"The chaste woman's mind is the measure of a road that has no forks: karpu is figured as singleness of direction. Compare 1212, where women's hearts are the figure for a road that divides.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","nirai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1208","lb":"CC1208","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1209":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1209,"t":["மணியியற் பாலிகை யனைய மாச்சுனை","யணிமணி நீண்மல ரணிந்த தாயிடை","யிணைமலர்ப் படலிகை போலு மீர்ம்பொழில்","கணையுமிழ் சிலையினாய் கண்டு சேறியே."],"p":"","r":"The god bids Civakan see, as he goes, the great springs like jewelled bowls and, between them, the cool groves like flower baskets adorned with long gem-like blossoms.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1209","lb":"CC1209","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1210":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1210,"t":["இலைப்பொலி பூண்முலை யெரிபொன் மேகலைக்","குலத்தலை மகளிர்தங் கற்பிற் றிண்ணிய","வலைத்துவீ ழருவிக ளார்க்குஞ் சோலைசூழ்","வலத்தது வனகிரி மதியிற் றோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"On the right, like the moon, will appear Vanagiri, ringed by groves where falls cast down like nets roar, firmer than the chastity of high-born women with jewelled breasts and girdles of gold.","n":"As in 1208, the chastity of women is the poem's standard of firmness, here for a mountain; the qualifier குலத்தலை மகளிர் ties that firmness to women of good family.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1210","lb":"CC1210","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1211":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1211,"t":["கரியவன் றிருமுடி கவிழ்த்த சேவடிப்","பெரியவன் றிருமொழி பிறழ்த லின்றியே","மரியவ ருறைதலின் மதன கீதமே","திரிதரப் பிறந்ததோர் சிலம்பிற் றென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"They say it is a mountain where songs of love arose and wander, because those who live there keep without wavering the holy word of the Great One whose red feet the Dark One bowed his sacred crown upon.","n":"கரியவன் 'the dark one' bowing to பெரியவன் 'the great one' is most naturally Krishna (or Vishnu) bowing to a Jina, a Jain ranking of gods; the verse does not name either. மரியவர் and மதன கீதம் are hard: the dwellers may be ascetics or celestials, and the link between their keeping the word and the songs of love is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1211","lb":"CC1211","ev":"","cl":[["the Dark One (Krishna or Vishnu) bowed his crown at the feet of the Great One whose word the mountain's dwellers keep","jain","asserted",1,"sudanjanan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1212":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1212,"t":["ஏற்றரு மணிவரை யிறந்து போனபின்","மாற்றரு மணநெறி மகளிர் நெஞ்சமே","போற்பல கவர்களும் பட்ட தாயிடை","யாற்றல்சால் செந்நெறி யறியக் கூறுவாம்."],"p":"","r":"After he has passed the jewelled hill hard to climb, the road splits into many forks, like the hearts of women on the path of marriage that may not be changed, and the god says he will tell the true straight way so that Civakan may know it.","n":"The phrase மாற்றரு மணநெறி மகளிர் is doubtful: if it means married women, the verse contradicts 1208's straight road like chaste women's minds; it may instead mean women of an unchanging scent-path, a garland figure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1212","lb":"CC1212","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1213":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1213,"t":["சுரும்புசூழ் குவளையோர் சுனையுண் டச்சுனை","மருங்கிலோர் மணிச்சிலா வட்ட முண்டவண்","விரும்பிவண் டிமிர்வதோர் வேங்கை வேங்கையின்","மருங்கிலோர் செந்நெறி வகுக்கப் பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"There is a spring ringed with blue lilies where bees swarm; beside it a round slab of gem-stone; there a vengai tree where bees hum with longing; and beside the vengai a red road has been laid out.","n":"The chain of landmarks, each placed by the last (அச்சுனை மருங்கில், வேங்கையின் மருங்கில்), is the poem's way of making a route memorable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1213","lb":"CC1213","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1214":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1214,"t":["கைம் மலர்த்த அனைய காந்தள் கடிமலர் நாறு கானம்","மொய்ம் மலர்க் குவளைக் கண்ணி மொய்ம்ப நீ முழுதும் நீந்தி","வைம் மலர்த்து இலங்கும் வெள்வேல் மத்திம தேயம் ஆளும்","கொய்ம் மலர்த் தாரினானைக் கண்ணுறு குணம் அது என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The god tells Civakan that when he has crossed the whole forest scented with kanthal like open hands, he will meet the king of the middle country with his bright spear and garland, and that this meeting is his good fortune.","n":"குணம் அது, 'that is the good', sets the meeting as a fortune to be met rather than a deed; the god foretells what the patikam (21) summarizes as the king coming out to receive the hero.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","natpu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1214","lb":"CC1214","ev":"Sudanjanan tells Civakan that after the kanthal forest he will meet the king of the middle country.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1215":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1215,"t":["மண்ணகங் காவன் மன்னன் மாதரம் பாவை மாசி","லொண்ணுதன் மகளைத் தந்தீங் குறைகென வொழுகு நாளுள்","வெண்மதி யிழந்த மீன்போற் புல்லென வெய்தி நின்ற","வண்ணனின் றோழ ரெல்லா மவ்வழி யடைய ரென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The god says that while Civakan lives there, after the king who guards the earth has given him his flawless daughter, all his friends, drooping like stars that have lost the white moon, will come to him there.","n":"The prophecy names the marriage and the friends' reunion that the patikam (22-23) gives as events; the image of stars bereft of the moon, which the patikam gives to the younger brother, is here given to the friends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","natpu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1215","lb":"CC1215","ev":"Sudanjanan foretells Civakan's marriage to the middle country king's daughter and his friends' coming to him.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:52"},"cc:1216":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1216,"t":["நெட்டிடை நெறிகளு நிகரில் கானமு","முட்டுடை முடுக்கரு மொய்கொள் குன்றமு","நட்புடை யிடங்களு நாடும் பொய்கையு","முட்பட வுரைத்தன னுறுதி நோக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"Looking to his good, the god told him fully of the long roads, the matchless forests, the blocked narrow passes, the crowded hills, the friendly places, the lands and the pools on the way.","n":"உறுதி நோக்கினான் closes the long road-map of the previous verses (1180s-1215): the description was given for the hero's welfare, not as display.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1216","lb":"CC1216","ev":"Sudanjanan finishes describing the route to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1217":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1217,"t":["செல்கதி மந்திரஞ் செவியிற் செப்பிய","மல்லலங் குமரனை வாழ நாட்டவே","வல்லவன் மந்திர மூன்றுங் கொள்கெனச்","சொல்லின னவற்றது தொழிலுந் தோன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"So that the fine prince who had whispered the mantra of the path to come into his ear might live and prosper, the able god told him to take three mantras, and explained what each would do.","n":"Civakan is named here by his own earlier act: செல்கதி மந்திரம் செவியிற் செப்பிய recalls the five padas he whispered to the dying dog (943-947), now repaid with mantras by the god the dog became.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","nanri","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1217","lb":"CC1217","ev":"Sudanjanan gives Civakan three mantras and tells him their uses.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1218":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1218,"t":["கடுந்தொடைக் கவர்கணைக் காமன் காமுறப்","படுங்குர றருமிது பாம்பு மல்லவுங்","கடுந்திற னோய்களுங் கெடுக்கும் வேண்டிய","வுடம்பிது தருமென வுணரக் கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"He made it plain that one mantra gives a voice that even Kama with his flowered arrows would long for, one destroys the poison of snakes and other creatures and fierce diseases, and one gives whatever body is wished for.","n":"These are worldly powers for the road, not the liberating formula of 945; the shape-changing mantra prepares the disguises of the later ilampakams.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","marundhu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1218","lb":"CC1218","ev":"Sudanjanan explains the three mantras: an enchanting voice, a cure for poison and disease, and a body of one's choosing.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1219":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1219,"t":["கந்தடு களிறு கொல்லுங் கருவரை யுழுவை யன்னான்","மந்திர மூன்று மோதி வானவிற் புரையும் பைந்தா","ரிந்திரன் றன்னை நோக்கி யியக்கியர் குழாத்தை நோக்கிச்","சிந்தையிற் செல்வ லென்றான் றேவனுஞ் செலவு நேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The hero like a dark-hill tiger that kills the post-breaking elephant recited the three mantras, looked at the god, Indra-like in his rainbow garland, and at the throng of yakshi women, and said he would go as his mind was set; the god consented to his going.","n":"இந்திரன் is read as a title for Sudanjanan as lord among the gods there, not the god Indra; சிந்தையிற் செல்வல் may also mean 'I will go in thought' (by the god's power), which 1224 would then fulfil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1219","lb":"CC1219","ev":"Civakan learns the mantras and takes leave; Sudanjanan agrees to let him go.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1220":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1220,"t":["மனைப்பெருங் கிழத்தி மாசின் மலைமக டன்னை யான்சென்","றெனைத்தொரு மதியி னாங்கெர் லெய்துவ தென்று நெஞ்சி","னினைத்தலுந் தோழ னக்கு நிழலுமிழ்ந் திலங்கு செம்பொற்","பனைத்திரண் டனைய தோளாய் பன்னிரு மதியி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When he only thought in his heart, 'in how many months shall I go and reach my faultless lady of the house, the mountain's daughter?', his friend smiled and answered, 'In twelve months, you whose shoulders are like gold palmyra trunks.'","n":"The god answers a thought, not a question: நெஞ்சின் நினைத்தலும். மலைமகள் is read as Gandharvadattai, the Vidyadhara princess from the mountain and his first wife; the second line is corrupt (see source_issues), so the exact wording of the question is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1220","lb":"CC1220","ev":"Sudanjanan answers Civakan's unspoken question: he will reach his wife in twelve months.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1221":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1221,"t":["ஆறிரு மதியி னெய்தி யரட்டனை யடர்த்து மற்றுன்","வீறுயர் முடியுஞ் சூடி விழுநிலக் கிழமை பூண்டு","சாறயர்ந் திறைவற் பேணிச் சார்பறுத் துய்தி யென்று","கூறினன் கதிர்கள் பொங்குங் குளிர்மணி முடியினானே."],"p":"","r":"The god of the cool gem crown foretold that in twelve months he would arrive, crush the usurper, wear his own lofty crown, take the earth as his possession, hold festivals honouring the Lord, and then cut off attachment and be saved.","n":"The whole remaining plot in one verse, ending not in kingship but in சார்பறுத்து உய்தி: rule and festival worship are set as stages before release. It is a prophecy, so no doctrinal claim is entered; the Lord (இறைவன்) is not named further.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pattru","veedu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1221","lb":"CC1221","ev":"Sudanjanan foretells Civakan's victory over the usurper, his kingship and his final renunciation.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1222":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1222,"t":["சொற்றிறற் சூழ்ச்சி மிக்க சுதஞ்சணன் சுருங்க நாடி","விற்றிறற் குருசிற் கெல்லாம் வேறுவே றுரைப்பக் கேட்டே","சுற்றிய தோழி மாரை விடுத்தனன் றொழுது நின்றான்","கற்பக மரமுஞ் செம்பொன் மாரியுங் கடிந்த கையான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing Sudanjanan, mighty in word and counsel, tell the bow-strong prince each thing separately and briefly, the one whose hand outdid the wishing tree and the rain of red gold dismissed the companion women around him and stood with folded hands.","n":"I take Civakan as subject of both விடுத்தனன் and தொழுது நின்றான்; Sudanjanan could instead be the one who dismisses the women. The hero worships the god who was once the dog he saved, so the debt now runs both ways.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","soozhchi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1222","lb":"CC1222","ev":"Civakan, having heard the god's counsel, sends away the women attending him and stands in worship before the god.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1223":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1223,"t":["சேட்டிளஞ் செங்கயல் காப்பச் செய்துவிற்","பூட்டிமேல் வைத்தன புருவப் பூமக","டீட்டிருந் திருநுதற் றிலக மேயென","மோட்டிருங் கதிர்திரை முளைத்த தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"As though the flower goddess had set her brows as bows over her young red-carp eyes to guard them, the great sun rose from the waves like the tilaka on her fine forehead.","n":"A dawn verse marking the morning of departure; the sun is the forehead-mark of the goddess Tiru.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1223","lb":"CC1223","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1224":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1224,"t":["அழல்பொதிந்த நீளெஃகி னலர்தார் மார்பற் கிம்மலைமேற்","கழல்பொதிந்த சேவடியாற் கடக்க லாகா தெனவெண்ணிக்","குழல்பொதிந்த தீஞ்சொல்லார் குழாத்தி னீங்கிக் கொண்டேந்தி","நிழல்பொதிந்த நீண்முடியா னினைப்பிற் போகி நிலத்திழிந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"Thinking that the hero of the flowering garland could not cross this mountain on his anklet-bearing feet, the long-crowned god left the throng of sweet-voiced women, lifted him up, travelled by thought and came down to the earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1224","lb":"CC1224","ev":"Sudanjanan carries Civakan down from the mountain to the earth by the power of thought.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1225":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1225,"t":["வண்டளிர்ச் சந்தனமும் வழையு மாவும் வான்றீண்டி","விண்டொழுகு தீங்கனிகள் பலவு மார்ந்த வியன்சோலை","மண்கருதும் வேலானை மறித்துங் காண்க வெனப்புல்லிக்","கொண்டெழுந்தான் வானவனுங் குருசி றானே செலவயர்ந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"In a broad grove of sandal, laurel and mango full of sweet fruit, the god embraced the spear-bearer who would rule the earth, saying 'may I see you again', and rose into the sky, and the prince set out on his way.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1225","lb":"CC1225","ev":"Sudanjanan embraces Civakan in a grove, takes leave and departs; Civakan sets out alone.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1226":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1226,"t":["வாளுழலை பாய்ந்திளைய வளநா கிட்டி னமென்னுந்","தாளொழியப் போரேறு தனியே போந்த தெனவெண்ணி","நீளருவிக் கண்ணீர்வீழ்த் தலறி வண்ணங் கரிந்துருகிக்","கோளுழுவை யன்னாற்குக் குன்ற முந்நின் றழுதனவே."],"p":"","r":"Thinking that the war-bull had come alone, leaving behind his kin and fine land, the hills wept for the tiger-like hero, shedding long waterfalls as tears, crying out, their colour scorched and melting.","n":"Nature mourns his solitude: the waterfalls are tears; the lament fixes the cost of the departure that the god's prophecy made sound easy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1226","lb":"CC1226","ev":"The hills seem to weep as Civakan walks on alone.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1227":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1227,"t":["மிக்கார்தங் கேட்டின்கண் மேன்மை யில்லாச் சிறியார்போ","னக்காங்கே யெயிறுடைந்த நறவ முல்லை நாள்வேங்கை","தக்கார்போற் கைம்மறித்த காந்த ளந்தோ தகாதெனவே","தொக்கார்போற் பன்மாவு மயிலுந் தோன்றித் துளங்கினவே."],"p":"","r":"The honey-jasmine whose buds burst open like mean men who rejoice at the ruin of the great, the vengai, the kanthal that waved its hands like worthy men saying 'alas, this is wrong', and the many beasts and peacocks that gathered like kin all appeared and trembled.","n":"The forest divides like society at a great man's fall: the laughing jasmine as the base who rejoice, the kanthal as the worthy who protest, the creatures as kin who crowd round.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","saandror","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1227","lb":"CC1227","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1228":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1228,"t":["கொல்லை யகடணைந்து குறும்பு சேர்ந்து தமியாரை","முல்லை முறுவலித்து நகுதிர் போலு மினிநும்மைப்","பல்லை யுகுத்திடுவ மென்று பைம்போ தலர்சிந்தித்","தொல்லை நிறங்கருகித் தும்பி பாய்ந்து துகைத்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Saying to the jasmines that crept into the fields and thickets and smiled as if laughing at the lonely, 'we shall knock out your teeth', the bees fell on them, scattered their fresh petals, scorched their old colour and trampled them.","n":"The bees punish the jasmine of the previous verse for its 'smile' at a lonely traveller: a comic revenge that continues the moral reading of the landscape.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1228","lb":"CC1228","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1229":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1229,"t":["தோடேந்து பூங்கோதை வேண்டேங் கூந்தறொடே லெம்மில்","பீடேந் தரிவையரிற் பெயர்கென் றூடு மடவார்போற்","கோடேந்து குஞ்சரங்க டெருட்டக் கூடா பிடிநிற்குங்","காடேந்து பூஞ்சாரல் கடந்தான் காலிற் கழலானே."],"p":"","r":"The hero with anklets on his feet crossed the flowering forest slope where she-elephants, like sulking women who say 'we want no flower garland, do not touch our hair, go to the proud women of your house', would not be won over though the tusked males tried to calm them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1229","lb":"CC1229","ev":"Civakan crosses the forest slope.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1230":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1230,"t":["காழக மூட்டப் பட்ட காரிருட் டுணியு மொப்பா","னாழளை யுடும்பு பற்றிப் பறித்துமார் பொடுங்கி யுள்ளான்","வாழ்மயிர்க் கரடி யொப்பான் வாய்க்கிலை யறித லில்லான்","மேழகக் குரலி னானோர் வேட்டுவன் றலைப்பட் டானே."],"p":"","r":"A hunter came upon him, like a piece of darkness wrapped in black cloth, with a chest shrunk from dragging monitor lizards out of their deep holes, like a shaggy bear, knowing nothing of betel for his mouth, with a voice like a ram's.","n":"The description is contemptuous, and the contempt prepares 1234, where the hunter's low birth is explained as the fruit of killing and eating flesh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1230","lb":"CC1230","ev":"A hunter meets Civakan in the forest.","cl":[],"ag":["குறவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1231":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1231,"t":["கொடிமுதிர் கிழங்கு தீந்தேன் கொழுந்தடி நறவொ டேந்திப்","பிடிமுதிர் முலையி னாடன் றழைத்துகிற் பெண்ணி னோடுந்","தொடுமரைத் தோலன் வில்லன் மரவுரி யுடையன் றோன்ற","வடிநுனை வேலி னான்கண் டெம்மலை யுறைவ தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He came carrying ripe creeper tubers, sweet honey and fat meat with toddy, along with his woman whose breasts were like an old she-elephant's and who wore leaf garments, he himself in deer skin, with a bow and bark cloth; seeing him, the hero of the sharp spear asked on which mountain he lived.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1231","lb":"CC1231","ev":"The hunter appears with his wife and his provisions, and Civakan asks where he lives.","cl":[],"ag":["குறவன்","பெண்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1232":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1232,"t":["மாலைவெள் ளருவி சூடி மற்றிதா தோன்று கின்ற","சோலைசூழ் வரையி னெற்றிச் சூழ்கிளி சுமக்க லாற்றா","மாலையந் தினைகள் காய்க்கும் வண்புன மதற்குத் தென்மேன்","மூலையங் குவட்டுள் வாழுங் குறவருட் டலைவ னென்றான்"],"p":"","r":"The hunter answered that he is the chief of the kuravar who live in the south-western corner peak beyond the rich millet field on the ridge ringed with groves, there where white waterfalls hang like garlands, whose millet heads the parrots cannot carry.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1232","lb":"CC1232","ev":"The hunter tells Civakan he is chief of the hill kuravar.","cl":[],"ag":["குறவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குறவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1233":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1233,"t":["ஊழினீ ருண்ப தென்னென் றுரைத்தலு முவந்து நோக்கி","மோழலம் பன்றி யோடு முளவுமாக் காதி யட்ட","போழ்நிணப் புழுக்க றேனெய் பொழிந்துகப் பெய்து மாந்தித்","தோழயாம் பெரிது முண்டுந் தொண்டிக்க ளிதனை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When Civakan asked what they eat as their custom, the chief looked at him gladly and said they cook boar and deer, pour honey-ghee over the sliced fat and eat it, and said, 'Friend, we eat plenty; take this, with the toddy'.","n":"Line 1 is Civakan's question and lines 2-4 the chief's reply. ஊழின் is read as 'by your custom, in your way of life'; it is not the fate-sense of ஊழ். தொண்டிக்கள் is read as a kind of toddy, but the word is unclear to me. The offer is hospitality, and Civakan's refusal turns it into a sermon.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kal","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1233","lb":"CC1233","ev":"Civakan asks what the hill folk eat, and the chief describes their meat and offers him some with toddy.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","குறவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குறவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1234":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1234,"t":["ஊனொடு தேனுங் கள்ளு முண்டுயிர் கொன்ற பாவத்","தீனராய்ப் பிறந்த திங்ங னினியிவை யொழிமி னென்னக்","கானில்வாழ் குறவன் சொல்லுங் தகள்ளொடூன் றேன்கை விட்டா","லேனையெம் முடம்பு வாட்ட லெவன்பிழைத் துங்கொ லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan tells them that they have been born so low because of the sin of killing living beings and eating meat, honey and toddy, and bids them give these up; the forest-dwelling kuravan answers that if they let go of toddy, meat and honey their bodies will waste, and asks how they could survive.","n":"The first claim makes social rank a karmic fruit: the hunter's birth is itself the punishment of former flesh-eating. The hunter's objection is answered at once in 1235, where Civakan reframes the wasting of the body as the price of a god's birth, and the hunter concedes; hence refuted. The school of the first claim is left general because the verse names no teaching; the Jain frame comes only in 1236.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kal","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1234","lb":"CC1234","ev":"Civakan tells the hunter to give up meat, honey and toddy; the hunter objects that without them they cannot live.","cl":[["a low birth is the fruit of the sin of killing and of eating meat, honey and toddy","general","asserted",1,"civakan"],["those who live in the forest cannot keep their bodies alive without meat, honey and toddy","general","refuted",4,"kuravan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","குறவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குறவன்"],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1235":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1235,"t":["ஊன்சுவைத் துடம்பு வீக்கி நரகத்தி லுறைத னன்றோ","வூன்றினா துடம்பு வாட்டித் தேவரா யுரைத னன்றோ","வூன்றியிவ் விரண்டினுள்ளு முறுதிநீ யுரைத்தி டென்ன","வூன்றினா தொழிந்து புத்தே ளாவதே யுறுதி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan asks him which is better, to fatten the body on the taste of meat and dwell in hell, or to waste the body by not eating meat and dwell as a god, and tells him to say which of the two is right; the hunter answers that giving up meat and becoming a god is right.","n":"Civakan does not deny the hunter's premise that the body will waste; he accepts it and makes the wasting the price of heaven. The teaching is drawn out of the hunter himself as an answer, a question-and-answer conversion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","naragam","marumai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1235","lb":"CC1235","ev":"Civakan puts to the hunter the choice between hell and heaven, and the hunter chooses to give up meat.","cl":[["one who fattens his body by eating meat dwells in hell, and one who lets the body waste by abstaining becomes a god","general","asserted",1,"civakan"],["to give up meat and become a god is the right course","general","asserted",4,"kuravan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","குறவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குறவன்"],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1236":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1236,"t":["உறுதிநீ யுணர்ந்து சொன்னா யுயர்கதிச் சேறி யேடா","குறுகினா யின்ப வெள்ளங் கிழங்குணக் காட்டு ளின்றே","யிறைவனூற் காட்சி கொல்லா வொழுக்கொடூன் றுறத்தல் கண்டா","யிறுதிக்க ணின்பந் தூங்கு மிருங்கனி யிவைகொ ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan tells him that he has understood and spoken the right course and will go to a high state; today in this forest where he eats tubers he has come near a flood of joy; he has seen the vision of the Lord's scripture, the conduct of not killing and the renouncing of meat; and he bids him take these great fruits, in which bliss hangs at the end.","n":"இறைவன் நூல் காட்சி and கொல்லா ஒழுக்கு name vision and conduct, the Jain pairing of right faith and right conduct; the school is assigned on these words. The 'great fruits' at the end are both the forest fruits he hands over and the fruit of the vow, and the hunter's diet is moved from meat to tubers and fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","ozhukkam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1236","lb":"CC1236","ev":"Civakan blesses the hunter's choice and gives him fruits.","cl":[["right vision of the Lord's teaching, the conduct of not killing and the renouncing of meat lead to a higher state and bliss at the end","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குறவன்"],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1237":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1237,"t":["என்றலுந் தேனு மூனும் பிழியலு மிறுக நீக்கிச்","சென்றடி தொழுது செல்கென் றேம்பெய்நீள் குன்றமென்று","குன்றுறை குறவன் போகக் கூரெரி வளைக்கப் பட்ட","பஞ்சவர் போல நின்ற பகட்டினப் பரிவு தீர்த்தான்."],"p":"","r":"At this the kuravan gave up honey, meat and toddy firmly, went and worshipped his feet, and left for his own mountain saying 'go well', and Civakan then relieved the distress of a herd of elephants that stood like the Pandavas when the fierce fire closed round them.","n":"The convert's vow is followed at once by an act of compassion toward beasts, so the doctrine of not harming lives is shown being kept by the teacher. பகட்டினம் is read as a herd of elephants; how he relieved them is not said. The simile assumes the hearer knows the Pandavas' escape from the burning house.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kollaamai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1237","lb":"CC1237","ev":"The hunter renounces meat, honey and toddy, worships Civakan and goes home; Civakan saves a herd of elephants hemmed in by forest fire.","cl":[],"ag":["குறவன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1238":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1238,"t":["இலங்கொளி மரகத மிடறி யின்மணி","கலந்துபொன் னசும்புகான் றொழுகி மானினஞ்","சிலம்புபாய் வருடையொ டுகளுஞ் சென்னிநீள்","விலங்கல்சென் றெய்தினான் விலங்கன் மார்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The hero with a chest broad as a hill came to a long mountain whose peaks shone with emerald and gem-veins, poured out streams of gold, and where herds of deer and mountain goats leapt and played.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1238","lb":"CC1238","ev":"Civakan reaches a high mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1239":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1239,"t":["அந்தர வகடுதொட் டணவு நீள்புகழ்","வெந்தெரி பசும்பொனின் விழையும் வெல்லொளி","மந்திர வாய் மொழி மறுவின் மாதவ","ரிந்திரர் தொழுமடி யினிதி னெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"He gladly reached the feet worshipped by the spotless great ascetics of mantra-true speech and by the Indras, whose long fame touches the vault of the sky and whose victorious light outshines burning pure gold.","n":"I read அடி as the Lord's feet, that is the shrine on the mountain that 1240 names as a temple; it could also be the feet of the ascetics themselves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1239","lb":"CC1239","ev":"Civakan reaches a shrine of the Lord worshipped by ascetics and gods.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1240":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1240,"t":["முனிவரு முயன்று வான்கண் மூப்பிகந் திரிய வின்பக்","கனிகவர் கணனு மேத்தக் காதிகண் ணரிந்த காசி","றனிமுதிர் கடவுள் கோயி றான்வலங் கொண்டு செல்வான்","குனிதிரை முளைத்த வெய்யோன் குன்றுசூழ் வதனை யொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"As he went circling the temple of the flawless, supremely ancient god who cut away the ghati karmas, praised by the striving sages and by the host of the sky who enjoy the fruit of bliss free of age, he was like the sun risen from the curling waves circling the great mountain.","n":"காதி names the obstructing (ghatiya) karmas that a Jina destroys to reach omniscience; the god is defined by what he cut away in himself, not by what he grants. The simile of the sun circling the mountain makes the pradakshina cosmic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1240","lb":"CC1240","ev":"Civakan circumambulates the god's temple on the mountain.","cl":[["the god worshipped is the one who destroyed the ghati karmas","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:53"},"cc:1241":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1241,"t":["தண் கயம் குற்ற போதும் தாழ்சினை இளிந்த வீயும்","வண் கொடிக் கொய்த பூவும் வார்ந்து மட்டு உயிர்ப்ப ஏந்தித்","திண் புகழ் அறிவன் பாதம் திருந்து கைத் தலத்தின் ஏற்றிப்","பண்பு கொள் குணம் கொள் கீதம் பாணியில் பாடுகின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan gathers flowers from the pond, fallen blossoms from low boughs and blooms from creepers, dripping honey, lays them with his own hands on the feet of the Omniscient of firm fame, and sings to the beat a song of the Lord's virtues.","n":"Three sources of flowers (water, tree, creeper) make the offering complete; the god is named only as அறிவன், the Knower, the Jain title of the Jina, not by a personal name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1241","lb":"CC1241","ev":"At the hill temple Civakan offers flowers at the Jina's feet and begins a hymn.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1242":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1242,"t":["ஆதி வேதம் பயந்தோய்நீ யலர்பெய்ம் மாரி யமைந்தோய்நீ","நீதி நெறியை யுணர்ந்தோய்நீ நிகரில் காட்சிக் கிறையோய்நீ","நாத னென்னப் படுவோய்நீ நவைசெய் பிறவிக் கடலகத்துன்","பாத கமலந் தொழுவேங்கன் பசையாப் பவிழப் பணியாயே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan hails the Lord as the one who gave the first Veda, who sat beneath the rain of blossoms, who knew the way of justice and is lord of peerless vision, and asks that the bonds of attachment of those who worship his lotus feet in the harmful sea of births be loosened.","n":"'The first Veda' (ஆதி வேதம்) is claimed for the Jina, not the Brahmins' Veda; the prayer asks not to be carried across the sea but for attachment (பசை) to come untied, the Jain account of what keeps one in it. The division பசை யாப்பு அவிழ is ours.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi","pattru","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1242","lb":"CC1242","ev":"","cl":[["the Jina is the giver of the first Veda and knower of the way of justice","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"],["attachment binds beings in the harmful sea of births","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1243":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1243,"t":["இன்னாப் பிறவி யிகந்தோய்நீ யிணையி லின்ப முடையோய்நீ","மன்னா வுலக மறுத்தோய்நீ வரம்பில் காட்சிக் கிறையோய்நீ","பொன்னா ரிஞ்சிப் புகழ்வேந்தே பொறியின் வேட்கைக் கடலழுந்தி","யொன்னா வினையி னுழல்வேங்க ளுயப்போம் வண்ண முரையாயே."],"p":"","r":"He praises the Lord as one who has passed beyond painful birth, has bliss without equal, has rejected the unstable world and has boundless vision, and asks him to tell the way of rescue to those who sink in the sea of the senses' craving and wander in hostile karma.","n":"What the worshipper asks for is speech, உரையாயே, 'tell': the Lord saves by teaching a way, which fits the hymn's figure of the Jina as the one who himself crossed (இகந்தோய்) rather than one who lifts others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","veedu","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1243","lb":"CC1243","ev":"","cl":[["craving of the senses drowns beings and karma makes them wander","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"],["the Lord has gone beyond birth and rejected the unstable world","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1244":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1244,"t":["உலக மூன்று முடையோய்நீ யொண்பொ னிஞ்சி யெயிலோய்நீ","திலக மாய திறலோய்நீ தேவரேத்தப் படுவோய்நீ","யலகை யில்லாக் குணக்கடலே யாரு மறியப் படாயாதி","கொலையி லாழி வலனுயர்த்த குளிர்முக் குடையி னிழலோய்நீ."],"p":"","r":"He hails the Lord as owner of the three worlds, dweller within the bright golden wall, the foremost in strength, praised by the gods, an endless sea of virtues and the Primal one none can know, who raised the wheel that does not kill and sits in the shade of the cool triple umbrella.","n":"The wheel of dominion, elsewhere the conqueror's sign, is here the wheel 'without killing' (கொலையில் ஆழி): the Jina's sovereignty is non-violence. The golden wall and triple umbrella are the marks of the Jina's preaching hall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","kollaamai","anaadhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1244","lb":"CC1244","ev":"","cl":[["the Jina's wheel of rule is a wheel without killing","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1245":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1245,"t":["அடியுலக மேதிதி யலர்மாரி தூவ","முடியுலக மூர்த்தி யுறநிமிர்ந்தோன் யாரே","முடியுலக மூர்த்தி யுறநிமிர்ந்தோன் மூன்று","கடிமதிலுங் கட்டழித்த காவலனீ யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"In a question and answer, the hymn asks who it is that rose up to the crowned summit of the worlds while the worlds below praised him and showered blossoms, and answers that it is the Lord who destroyed the bonds of the three guarded fortresses.","n":"The epithet of destroying three forts is the Saiva one of Siva and the three cities, taken over for the Jina; the word கட்டு ('bond', also 'fastening') suggests the forts are read as bonds, but the verse does not say which three. The 'crowned world' is the summit of the universe where the freed rest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","iraivan","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1245","lb":"CC1245","ev":"","cl":[["the Lord rose to the summit of the worlds by destroying three fortified bonds","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1246":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1246,"t":["முரணவிய வென்றுலக மூன்றினையு மூன்றிற்","றரணிமேற் றந்தளித்த தத்துவன்றான் யாரே","தரணிமேற் றந்தளித்தான் றண்மதிபோ னேமி","யரணுலகிற் காய வறிவரனீ யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The hymn asks who is the Real One who, having conquered the three worlds so that strife ceased, gave the Three on earth, and answers that it is the Lord, the Knower-Aran whose wheel is like the cool moon and who is the refuge of the world.","n":"'The Three' (மூன்று) are unnamed; in Jain teaching the triad given to the world would be right faith, knowledge and conduct, but that is our gloss. அரன் here is applied to the Jina, a second Saiva name taken over after the three forts of 1245.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thaththuvam","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1246","lb":"CC1246","ev":"","cl":[["the Jina conquered the three worlds without strife and gave the world a threefold teaching","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1247":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1247,"t":["தீராவினை தீர்த்துத் தீர்த்தந் தெரிந்துய்த்து","வாராக் கதியுரைத்த வாமன்றான் யாரே","வாராக் கதியுரைத்த வாமன் மலர்ததைந்த","காரார்பூம் பிண்டிக் கடவுணீ யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The hymn asks who is the Vaaman who ended karma that cannot be ended, discerned and handed on the sacred ford, and taught the state from which none return, and answers that it is the god of the blossoming asoka tree thick with flowers.","n":"வாராக் கதி, 'the going from which none come back', names release as the end of return, not a heaven. Whose karma is ended is not stated; in the Jain frame (and 1240's temple of the one who cut the ghati karmas) it is first the Jina's own, which he then teaches others to end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","theerththam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1247","lb":"CC1247","ev":"","cl":[["karma that cannot otherwise be ended was ended by the Jina, who taught the path of no return","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1248":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1248,"t":["அம்மலைச் சினகரம் வணங்கிப் பண்ணவர்","பொன்மலர்ச் சேவடி புகழ்ந்த பின்னரே","வெம்மலைத் தெய்வதம் விருந்து செய்தபின்","செம்மல்போய்ப் பல்லவ தேய நண்ணினான்."],"p":"","r":"Having bowed at the Jina temple on that hill and praised the golden-flowered red feet of the Lord, and having been feasted by the deity of the fierce mountain, the noble youth went on and reached the Pallava country.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1248","lb":"CC1248","ev":"The mountain deity feasts Civakan, and he leaves the hill and reaches the Pallava country.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","வெம்மலைத் தெய்வதம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1249":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1249,"t":["அரியலார்ந் தமர்த்தலி னனந்தர் நோக்குடைக்","கரியவாய் நெடியகட் கடைசி மங்கையர்","வரிவரால் பிறழ்வயற் குவளை கட்பவ","ரிருவரை வினாய்நகர் நெறியின் முன்னினான்."],"p":"","r":"He asks two field-women, languid-eyed from drinking toddy, who pluck blue lilies in fields where striped varal fish leap, the way to the city and goes on ahead.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1249","lb":"CC1249","ev":"Civakan asks two field-women the way to the city.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கடைசியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1250":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1250,"t":["அன்னமு மகன்றிலு மணிந்து தாமரைப்","பன்மலர்க் கிடங்குசூழ் பசும்பொற் பாம்புரிக்","கன்னிமூ தெயில்கட லுடுத்த காரிகை","பொன்னணிந் திருந்தெனப் பொலிந்து தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"The city, ringed by moats of many lotuses where swans and makanril birds rest and by its ancient unbreached wall coiled like a serpent of green gold, shines like a lovely woman wearing the sea and adorned with gold.","n":"கன்னி மூதெயில், 'the virgin ancient wall', is the wall never taken; the figure of the city as an adorned woman readies the scene of the dancer that follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1250","lb":"CC1250","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1251":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1251,"t":["அகிறரு கொழும்புகை மாடத் தாய்பொனின்","முகிறலை விலங்கிய மொய்கொ ணீள்கொடிப்","பகறலை விலங்குசந் திராபம் பான்மையி","னிகறலை விலங்குவேற் காளை யெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The young bull with the shining spear reaches Chandirapam, where flags on mansions fragrant with agil smoke cut through the clouds and shut out the daylight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1251","lb":"CC1251","ev":"Civakan reaches the city Chandirapam.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1252":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1252,"t":["மலரணி மணிக்குட மண்ணு நீரொடு","பலர்நலம் பழிச்சுபு பரவ வேகினா","னலர்கதிர் கரும்பிளை மடுப்ப வாய்நக.","ருலகளந் தானென வுள்புக் கானரோ."],"p":"","r":"He went on while many praised his beauty and water was brought in flower-decked jewelled pots, and he entered the city like the one who measured the world.","n":"Line 3 (அலர்கதிர் கரும்பு இளை மடுப்ப) is hard to construe; it may say that the sugarcane hedges took in the spreading rays. The comparison to உலகளந்தான் is the Vaishnava Trivikrama, used for the hero's stride into the city.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1252","lb":"CC1252","ev":"Civakan enters Chandirapam amid praise and ceremonial water.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1253":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1253,"t":["சந்தனக் காவு சூழ்ந்து சண்பக மலர்ந்த சோலை","வந்துவீழ் மாலை நாற்றி மணியரங் கணிந்து வானத்","திந்திர குமரன் போல விறைமக னிருந்து காண","வந்தர மகளி ரன்னார் நாடக மியற்று கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"In a garden of sandal groves and blooming champak, with garlands hung and a jewelled stage adorned, women like the celestial maidens perform a drama while the king's son sits watching like Indra's son in heaven.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1253","lb":"CC1253","ev":"The prince of Chandirapam watches a dance-drama staged in the garden.","cl":[],"ag":["உலோகபாலன்","அரம்பை அன்னாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1254":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1254,"t":["குழலெடுத் தியாத்து மட்டார் கோதையிற் பொலிந்து மின்னு","மழலவிர் செம்பொற் பட்டங் குண்டல மாரந் தாங்கி","நிழலவி ரல்குற் காசு சிலம்பொடு சிலம்ப நீடோ","ளழகிகூத் தாடு கின்றா ளரங்கின்மே லரம்பை யன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her hair tied up and bright with honeyed garlands, wearing a gleaming gold forehead band, earrings and a necklace, her girdle bells and anklets chiming together, the long-armed beauty like Rambha dances on the stage.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1254","lb":"CC1254","ev":"A dancer takes the stage.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1255":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1255,"t":["தண்ணுமை முழவம் வீணை குழலொடு குயிலத் தண்பூங்","கண்ணொடு புருவங் கைகால் கலையல்கு னுசுப்புக் காம","ரொண்ணுதல் கொண்ட வாடற் றொட்டிமை யுருவ நோக்கி","வெண்ணெய்தீ யுற்ற வண்ண மாடவர் மெலிகின் றாரே"],"p":"","r":"To drum, muzhavu, vina and flute she dances with eyes, brows, hands, feet, garment, hips and waist, and the men who watch her form melt like butter touched by fire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","isai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1255","lb":"CC1255","ev":"The dancer performs and the men watching are overcome.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1256":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1256,"t":["பாடலொ டியைந்த வாடல் பண்ணமை கருவி மூன்றுங்","கூடுபு சிவணி நின்று குழைந்திழைந் தமிர்த மூற","வோடரி நெடுங்க ணம்பா லுளங்கிழிந் துருவ வெய்யா","வீடமை பசும்பொற் சாந்த மிலயமா வாடு கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her dance is joined to the song, the three tuned instruments blend and flow like nectar, and as the arrows of her long streaked eyes tear into hearts she dances keeping the rhythm.","n":"Line 4's வீடமை பசும்பொற் சாந்தம் is obscure as written; the image of fresh-gold sandal paste does not join clearly to the rest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["koothu","isai","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1256","lb":"CC1256","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1257":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1257,"t":["வாணுதற் பட்ட மின்ன வார்குழை திருவில் வீசப்","பூண்முலை பிறழப் பொற்றோ டிடவயி னுடங்க வொல்கி","மாணிழை வளைக்கை தம்மால் வட்டணை போக்கு கின்றாள்","காண்வரு குவளைக் கண்ணாற் காளைமே னோக்கி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"With her forehead band flashing, her earrings throwing rainbows, her breasts trembling and her gold earring swaying on the left, she bends and circles her bangled hands, and with her blue-lily eyes she looks at the youth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","kan","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1257","lb":"CC1257","ev":"In the middle of the dance she looks at Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1258":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1258,"t":["நோக்கினா ணெடுங்க ணென்னுங் குடங்கையா னொண்டு கொண்டு","வாக்கமை யுருவின் மிக்கான் வனப்பினைப் பருக விப்பா","லாக்கிய விலய நீங்கிற் றணங்கனா ணெடுங்கண் பில்கி","வீக்குவார் முலையி னெற்றி வெண்முத்தஞ் சொரிந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"She looked, and with her long eyes for a cupped palm she scooped up and drank the beauty of the finely formed youth; at that the rhythm she had kept left her, and her long eyes ran with tears that fell on her breasts like white pearls.","n":"The art fails at the moment of desire: the dancer whose eyes were weapons in 1256 becomes the one who drinks through them, and loses the laya, the one thing the stage requires.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","koothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1258","lb":"CC1258","ev":"The dancer loses the rhythm and weeps.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1259":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1259,"t":["செருக்கய னெடுங்க ணாளத் திருமகன் காண்ட லங்சி","நெருக்கித்தன் முலையின் மின்னு நிழன்மணி வடத்தை மாதர்","பொருக்குநூல் பரிந்து சிந்தாப் பூவெலாங் கரிந்து வாடத்","தரிக்கிலாள் காமச் செந்தீத் தலைக்கொளச் சாம்பி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman with proud kayal-fish eyes, shrinking from being seen as she looked at the noble son, pressed and broke the gleaming jewelled string on her breast so its thread snapped and scattered, and as all her flowers scorched and withered she could not bear it and languished in the red fire of desire.","n":"The flowers withering on her body are the tradition's sign of love's fever. 'Shrinking from being seen' depends on reading காண்டல் அஞ்சி for the printed காண்ட லங்சி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1259","lb":"CC1259","ev":"The dancer breaks her necklace and languishes with desire.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1260":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1260,"t":["கன்னிமை கனிந்து முற்றிக் காமுறக் கமழுங் காமத்","தின்னறுங் கனியைத் துய்ப்பா னேந்தலே பிறர்க ளில்லை","பொன்னினா லுடையுங் கற்பென் றுரைத்தவர் பொய்யைச் சொன்னா","ரின்னிசை யிவற்க லாலென் னெஞ்சிட மில்லை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that the sweet fruit of her ripened maidenhood, fragrant with desire, is for this lord alone and no other; those who said that chastity breaks for gold told a lie; there is no room in her heart for anyone but this sweet-voiced man.","n":"A stage dancer, of the class the ethical books call women for hire, claims karpu for herself and calls the saying that gold breaks it a lie. The epic lets her speak it; whether the story bears her out is for later verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kaamam","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1260","lb":"CC1260","ev":"The dancer vows in her heart that she will belong to Civakan alone.","cl":[["it is false that a dancer's chastity breaks for gold","general","reported",3,"dancer"]],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னாள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரம்பை அன்னாள்",""],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1261":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1261,"t":["கருஞ்சிறைப் பறவை யூர்திக் காமரு காளை தான்கொ","லிருஞ்சுற வுயர்த்த தோன்ற லேத்தருங் குருசி றான்கொ","லரும்பெறற் குமர னென்றாங் கறிவயர் வுற்று நின்றா","டிருந்திழை யணங்கு மென்றோட் டேசிகப் பாவை யன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Is he the lovely youth who rides the dark-winged bird, or the lord who raised the great shark banner, or the rare prince Kumaran, she wonders, and stands bewildered, the jewelled one like a lovely doll.","n":"Her guesses are gods: the shark banner is Kama's; the bird-rider and Kumaran are less certain identifications (the bird may be Vishnu's Garuda or Murugan's peacock). Seeing the hero as a god in disguise is a recurring move of the poem.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1261","lb":"CC1261","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னாள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரம்பை அன்னாள்",""],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1262":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1262,"t":["போதெனக் கிடந்த வாட்கண் புடைபெயர்ந் திமைத்தல் செல்லா","தியாதிவள் கண்ட தென்றாங் கரசனு மமர்ந்து நோக்கி","மீதுவண் டரற்றுங் கண்ணி விடலையைத் தானுங் கண்டான்","காதலிற் களித்த துள்ளங் காளையைக் கொணர்மி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Her flower-like eyes turned aside and would not blink; wondering what she had seen, the king too looked carefully and saw the young man whose garland the bees hum over, and with his heart glad with affection he said, 'Bring the youth.'","n":"The ruler reads the dancer's gaze as the usurper's ally did in 685, and draws the opposite conclusion: not a rival to resent but a man to meet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kurippu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1262","lb":"CC1262","ev":"Following the dancer's gaze, the prince sees Civakan and orders him brought.","cl":[],"ag":["உலோகபாலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1263":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1263,"t":["கைவளர் கரும்புடைக் கடவு ளாமெனி","னெய்கணை சிலையினோ டிவன்க ணில்லையான்","மெய்வகை யியக்கருள் வேந்த னாகுமென்","றையமுற் றெவர்களு மமர்ந்து நோக்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"All who looked, in doubt, reasoned that if he were the god with the sugarcane bow he would have his bow and arrows, and since he has none he must be a king of the true Yakshas.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","azhagu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1263","lb":"CC1263","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கண்டார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்டார்",""],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1264":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1264,"t":["மந்திரம் மறந்து வீழ்ந்து மா நிலத்து இயங்கு கின்ற","அந்தர குமரன் என்று ஆங்கு யாவரும் அமர்ந்து நோக்கி","இந்திர திருவற்கு உய்த்தார்க்கு இறைவனும் எதிர் கொண்டு ஓம்பி","மைந்தனை மகிழ்வ கூறி மைத்துனத் தோழன் என்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Everyone decided he was a heavenly prince who had forgotten his spell, fallen and was walking on the earth, and brought him to the prince of Indra's splendour; the lord received and honoured him, spoke to the young man with joy and called him his brother-in-law friend.","n":"'One who forgot his mantra and fell' is the lore of the Vidyadharas, whose flight depends on spells. மைத்துனத் தோழன் names a friend as close as a wife's brother; it may look ahead to a marriage tie the story has not yet made.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","virundhu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1264","lb":"CC1264","ev":"The crowd brings Civakan to the prince, who welcomes him and calls him a brother-in-law friend.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்டார்","உலோகபாலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1265":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1265,"t":["போதவிழ் தெரிய லானும் பூங்கழற் காலி னானுங்","காதலி னொருவ ராகிக் கலந்துட னிருந்த போழ்தி","னூதுவண் டுடுத்த மாலை யுணர்வுபெற் றிலயந் தாங்கிப்","போதுகண் டனைய வாட்கட் புருவத்தாற் கலக்கு கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"When the prince of the flower garland and the youth of the anklet-feet sat together, one in affection, the dancer came back to herself, took up the rhythm again and troubled him with the brows of her flower-like eyes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","koothu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1265","lb":"CC1265","ev":"Civakan and the prince sit together as friends; the dancer recovers the rhythm and plays her glances at him.","cl":[],"ag":["உலோகபாலன்","சீவகன்","அரம்பை அன்னாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:54"},"cc:1266":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1266,"t":["தேனுகுக் கின்ற கண்ணித் திருமக ளாட விப்பா","லூனுகுக் கின்ற வைவே லொருமக னுருமிற் றோன்றி","வானுகுக் கின்ற மீன்போன் மணிபரந் திமைக்கு மார்பிற்","கானுகுக் கின்ற பைந்தார்க் காவலற் றொழுது சொன்னான்"],"p":"","r":"While the princess, garlanded with dripping honey, was at play, a man bearing a spear that drips flesh appears as suddenly as thunder and, bowing, speaks to the prince whose chest glitters with gems like falling stars.","n":"The verse holds back the news: the messenger's sudden coming (உருமின் தோன்றி) is the only sign of disaster, and the next six verses are his report.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1266","lb":"CC1266","ev":"A spear-bearing messenger rushes in and bows to the prince to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒருமகன்","இறைமகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1267":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1267,"t":["கொய்தகைப் பொதியிற் சோலைக் குழவிய முல்லை மௌவல்","செய்யசந் திமயச் சாரற் கருப்புரக் கன்று தீம்பூக்","கைதரு மணியிற் றெண்ணீர் மதுக்கலந் தூட்டி மாலை","பெய்தொளி மறைத்து நங்கை பிறையென வளர்க்கின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"The messenger begins: in the grove of Pothiyil the lady has been rearing a young jasmine and a mauval creeper as one rears a crescent moon, watering them with clear water mixed with honey and screening them from the sun with garlands.","n":"The care given to the plant is the care of a mother for a child (பிறையென வளர்க்கின்றாள்); the scene that follows turns on her delight in what she has raised.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1267","lb":"CC1267","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருமகன்","இறைமகன்"],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1268":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1268,"t":["பவழங்கொள் கோடு நாட்டிப் பைம்பொனால் வேலி கோலித்","தவழ்கதிர் முத்தம் பாய்த்தித் தன்கையாற் றீண்டி நன்னாட்","புகழ்கொடி நங்கை தன்பேர் பொறித்ததோர் கன்னி முல்லை","யகழ்கடற் றானை வேந்தே யணியெயி றீன்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"That maiden jasmine, staked on a coral pole, fenced in gold, strewn with pearls, touched by her own hand and inscribed with her name, has now put out buds like fine teeth, O king of the army wide as the moated sea.","n":"The plant bears her name and is called a maiden (கன்னி முல்லை), so its first flowering mirrors her own coming of age.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1268","lb":"CC1268","ev":"Padumai's own jasmine comes into bud.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருமகன்","இறைமகன்"],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1269":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1269,"t":["வலம்பலர் கோதை சிந்த மயிலென வொருத்தி யோடிக்","கொம்பலர் நங்கை பூத்தாள் பொலிகெனக் குனிந்த விற்கீ","ழம்பலர் கண்ணி யார நிதியறைந் தோகை போக்கிக்","கம்பலம் போர்த்த போலுங் கடிமலர்க் காவு புக்காள்."],"p":"","r":"A woman ran like a peacock, garland scattering, crying that the creeper had flowered and wishing her prosperity; the lady rewarded the good news with treasure and entered the fragrant grove that seemed spread with a woollen cloth of blossoms.","n":"ஓகை போக்கி is the custom of paying the bearer of glad news; the gift marks the joy just before the bite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1269","lb":"CC1269","ev":"A woman brings word that the jasmine has flowered; Padumai rewards her and goes into the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒருத்தி","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருமகன்","இறைமகன்"],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1270":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1270,"t":["நறவிரி சோலை யாடி நாண்மலர்க் குரவம் பாவை","நிறையப்பூத் தணிந்து வண்டுந் தேன்களு நிழன்று பாட","விறைவளைத் தோளி மற்றென் றோழியீ தென்று சேர்ந்து","பெறலரும் பாவை கொள்வாள் பெரியதோ ணீட்டி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the honey-dripping grove, where a kuravam tree had flowered into a doll and bees sang over it, the lady, calling it her companion, stretched out her long arm to take the rare flower-doll.","n":"The kuravam's blossom cluster called பாவை, a doll, lets the girl treat the flower as a playmate (என் தோழி ஈது); the gesture of reaching is the moment of danger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1270","lb":"CC1270","ev":"Padumai reaches out to pick the kuravam flower-doll.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருமகன்","இறைமகன்"],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1271":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1271,"t":["நங்கைதன் முகத்தை நோக்கி நகைமதி யிதுவென் றெண்ணி","யங்குறை யரவு தீண்டி யௌவையோ வென்ற போகக்","கொங்கலர் கோதை நங்கை யடிகளோ வென்று கொம்பேர்","செங்கயற் கண்ணி தோழி திருமகட் சென்று சேர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"A snake living there looked at her face, took it for the laughing moon and bit her; she cried out for her mother, and her carp-eyed companion ran, crying for the lady's elders, and reached the queen.","n":"The bite is told as a praise of her face, the snake that seizes the moon of the eclipse turned on a girl. ஔவை here is 'mother'; திருமகள் in line 4 is most likely the queen, though the same word named the princess in 1266.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1271","lb":"CC1271","ev":"The snake bites Padumai, mistaking her face for the moon, and her companion runs to tell the queen.","cl":[],"ag":["அரவு","பதுமை","தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருமகன்","இறைமகன்"],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1272":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1272,"t":["அடிகளுக் கிறைஞ்சி யைய னடிகளைத் தொழுது நங்கை","யடிகளைப் புல்லி யாரத் தழுவிக்கொண் டௌவை மாரைத்","கொடியனா யென்னை நாளு நினையெனத் தழுவிக் கொண்டு","மிடை மின்னி னிலத்தைச் சேர்ந்தாள் வேந்தமற் றருளு கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Bowing to the elders, worshipping her father's feet, clasping her mothers and asking them to think of her always though she has been cruel to them, she sank to the ground like lightning; 'O king, show grace,' the messenger ended.","n":"Her farewell asks to be remembered as one who wronged them by dying (கொடியனாய்), the voice of a child who expects to die before her parents. Whose feet are meant by அடிகள் and ஐயன் in lines 1 and 2 is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","anbu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1272","lb":"CC1272","ev":"Padumai takes leave of her parents as if dying and collapses.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருமகன்","இறைமகன்"],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1273":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1273,"t":["பதுமையைப் பாம்பு தீண்டிற் றென்றலும் பையு ளெய்திக்","கொதிநுனை வேலி னாயிங் கிருக்கெனக் குருசி லேகிக்","கதுமெனச் சென்று நோக்கிக் காய்சினங் கடிதற் கொத்த","மதிமிகுத் தவல நீக்கு மந்திரம் பலவுஞ் செய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing that a snake had bitten Padumai, the prince was stricken, told Civakan to stay where he was, went at once, looked, and worked many mantras fit to quench the burning venom and remove the grief.","n":"The prince himself knows anti-venom mantras and fails; that he leaves his new friend behind sets up the later call for him (1282). Civakan is the one addressed by the spear epithet of line 2.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","marundhu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1273","lb":"CC1273","ev":"The prince leaves Civakan behind, goes to Padumai and tries many mantras on the venom.","cl":[],"ag":["இறைமகன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1274":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1274,"t":["வள்ளறான் வல்லவெல்லா மாட்டினன் மற்று மாங்க","ணுள்ளவ ரொன்ற லாத செயச்செய வூறு கேளா","தள்ளிலைப் பூணி னாளுக் காவியுண் டில்லை யென்ன","வெள்ளெயிற் றரவு கான்ற வேகமிக் கிட்ட தன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The noble prince tried all he knew, and those there tried every other remedy, but the girl heard nothing; asked whether life remained, they could not say, and the force of the white-fanged snake's venom rose higher.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","noy","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1274","lb":"CC1274","ev":"Every remedy fails and the venom rises.","cl":[],"ag":["இறைமகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1275":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1275,"t":["பைங்கதிர் மதிய மென்று பகையடு வெகுளி நாக","நங்கையைச் செற்ற தீங்குத் தீர்த்துநீர் கொண்மி னாடும்","வங்கமா நிதிய நல்கி மகட்டரு மணிசெய் மான்றே","ரெங்களுக் கிறைவ னென்றாங் கிடிமுர செருக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Proclaiming that whoever cures the lady of the harm the furious snake did her, taking her face for the moon, shall have the land, ships and great treasure and the daughter herself, the lord of the jewelled chariot had the thunder drum beaten.","n":"The reward joins kingdom and bride, the formula of a svayamvara by deed; the Cintamani's marriages are won this way, by a feat rather than a match arranged for birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","selvam","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1275","lb":"CC1275","ev":"The king proclaims by drum that whoever cures Padumai will receive land, wealth and her hand.","cl":[],"ag":["நரபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1276":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1276,"t":["மண்டலி மற்றி தென்பா ரிராசமா நாக மென்பார்","கொண்டது நாக மென்பார் குறைவளி பித்தொ டையிற்","பிண்டித்துப் பெருகிற் றென்பார் பெருநவை யறுக்கும் விஞ்சை","யெண்டவப் பலவுஞ் செய்தா மெனறுகே ளாதி தென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The healers argue: some say it is a மண்டலி snake, some a royal great snake, some that a naga has seized her, some that wind, bile and phlegm, out of balance, have clotted and grown; some say that though they have done many spells that cut great harm, she does not respond.","n":"The scene is comic in its accumulation of என்பார்: each healer names a different snake or a humoral cause, and none agrees. The line on the three humours is the medical theory of the Kural's chapter on medicine, here applied to a bite.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["noy","marundhu","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1276","lb":"CC1276","ev":"The poison-healers give rival diagnoses and report their spells have failed.","cl":[],"ag":["என்பார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1277":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1277,"t":["சிரையைந்தும் விடுது மென்பார் தீற்றுதுஞ் சிருங்கி யென்பார்","குரைபுன லிடுது மென்பார் கொந்தழ லுறுத்து மென்பா","ரிரையென வருத்தக் கவ்வி யென்புறக் கடித்த தென்பா","ருரையன்மி னுதிர நீங்கிற் றுய்யல ணங்கை யென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Others say they will bleed her from the five veins, or feed her the drug சிருங்கி, or plunge her in rushing water, or apply fire; some say the snake bit to the bone as if seizing prey, and some say: speak no more, if the blood has left her the girl will not live.","n":"The verse lists treatments without one being chosen; the remedies contradict each other (water and fire), which is the point. சிருங்கி is a rare word for a drug.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","noy","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1277","lb":"CC1277","ev":"The healers propose bleeding, drugs, water and fire, and some give her up.","cl":[],"ag":["என்பார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1278":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1278,"t":["கையொடு கண்டங் கோப்பார் கனைசுட ருறுப்பின் வைப்பார்","தெய்வதம் பரவி யெல்லாத் திசைதொறுந் தொழுது நிற்பா","ருய்வகை யின்றி யின்னே யுலகுடன் கவிழு மென்பார்","மையலங் கோயின் மாக்கண் மடைதிறந் திட்ட தொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Some bind her hand and neck, some lay bright flames to her limbs, some praise the gods and stand worshipping in every direction, some say the whole world will now topple without escape; the people of the bewildered palace were like a sluice thrown open.","n":"Worship of the gods (தெய்வதம் பரவி) is listed among the failed remedies, beside cautery and binding, with no comment from the narrator.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","idumbai","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1278","lb":"CC1278","ev":"Healers bind and burn her limbs, others pray, and the palace breaks into wailing.","cl":[],"ag":["என்பார்","இறைவளையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1279":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1279,"t":["வெந்தெரி செம்பொற் பூவும் விளங்குபொன் னூலும் பெற்றார்","மந்திர மறையும் வல்லா ரெழாயிரர் மறுவில் வாய்மை","யந்தரத் தறுவை வைப்பா ரந்தண ரங்கை கொட்டிப்","பைந்தொடிப் பாவை யின்னே பரிவொழிந் தெழுக வென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Seven thousand Brahmins, who had received gifts of fiery gold flowers and gleaming gold thread and were masters of mantra and Veda, set cloth aloft in the air with spotless truth, clapped their hands and said, 'Let the girl with green bangles rise now, her pain gone.'","n":"The Brahmins are paid before they act (பெற்றார்), and their command fails; in a Jain epic the scene sets the Vedic ritualists' mantras beside the one that will work. The rite of setting cloth in the air (அந்தரத்து அறுவை வைப்பார்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","mandhiram","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1279","lb":"CC1279","ev":"Seven thousand paid Brahmins chant mantras and command her to rise, without effect.","cl":[],"ag":["அந்தணர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1280":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1280,"t":["பாம்பெழப் பாம்பு கொண்டாற் பகவற்கு மரிது தீர்த்த","றேம்பிழி கோதைக் கின்று பிறந்தநா டெளிமி னென்று","காம்பழி பிச்ச மாகக் கணியெடுத் துரைப்பக் கல்லென்","றூம்பழி குளத்திற் கண்ணீர் துகணிலத் திழிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The astrologer, reading the stars, says that when a snake bites as the serpent rises, curing it is hard even for the Lord, and that today is the very day of the garlanded girl's birth star, so they must understand; at this the tears ran down to the dusty ground like water from a burst tank.","n":"பாம்பு எழ may name the rising of an asterism or the serpent of the eclipse; either way the verdict rests on the hour, not on the venom. Whether the birth-star day aggravates or softens the verdict is not clear from the line, but the weeping shows how it was heard. The prediction is overturned in 1290.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","saakkaadu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1280","lb":"CC1280","ev":"The astrologer declares the bite nearly incurable by the stars, and all weep.","cl":[],"ag":["கணி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கணி",""],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1281":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1281,"t":["நங்கைக்கின் றிறத்த லில்லை நரபதி நீயுங் கேண்மோ","கொங்கலர் கோங்கி னெற்றிக் குவிமுகிழ் முகட்டி னங்கட்","டங்குதே னரவ யாழிற் றானிருந் தாந்தை பாடு","மிங்குநம் மிடரைத் தீர்ப்பா னிளையவ னுளன்மற் றென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Someone tells the king: the lady will not die today, O lord of men, listen: on the closed bud at the crown of the flowering kongu an owl sits and calls with a sound like a bee's yaal, and there is a young man here who will end our distress.","n":"Against the astrologer's verdict a bird omen promises life and points to a rescuer not yet named; the verse makes divination itself split, one sign of death and one of help. Who speaks is not named (என்றான்), and reading ஆந்தை as an owl is likely but not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1281","lb":"CC1281","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நிமித்திகன்","நரபதி"],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1282":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1282,"t":["பன்மணிக் கடகஞ் சிந்தப் பருப்புடைப் பவளத் தூண்மேன்","மன்னவன் சிறுவன் வண்கை புடைத்துமா ழாந்து சொன்னா","னின்னுமொன் றுண்டு சூழ்ச்சி யென்னொடங் கிருந்த நம்பி","தன்னைக்கூய்க் கொணர்மி னென்றான் றரவந்தாங் கவனுங் கண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Shaking the jewelled bracelets on his arm as he struck his hand on the coral pillar in his distress, the king's son said there was one more plan: 'Call and bring the young man who sat with me there'; they brought him, and Civakan saw the scene.","n":"The omen of a young man (1281) is fulfilled by the prince's memory of his new friend, so the help comes through friendship rather than proclamation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","natpu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1282","lb":"CC1282","ev":"The prince remembers Civakan and has him fetched to Padumai.","cl":[],"ag":["இறைமகன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இறைமகன்",""],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1283":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1283,"t":["பறவைமா நாகம் வீழ்ந்து பலவுடன் பதைப்ப போன்றுஞ்","சிறகுறப் பரப்பி மஞ்ஞை செருக்குபு கிடந்த போன்றுங்","கறவைகன் றிழந்த போன்றுங் கிடந்தழு கின்ற கண்ணா","ரிறைவளை யவரை நோக்கி யென்கொடி துற்ற தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the bangled women lying and weeping, like great birds fallen and writhing together, like peacocks sprawled with spread wings, like cows that have lost their calves, Civakan asked what cruel thing had happened.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1283","lb":"CC1283","ev":"Civakan finds the women lamenting and asks what has befallen.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இறைவளையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1284":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1284,"t":["ஊறுகொள் சிங்கம் போல வுயக்கமோ டிருந்த நம்பி","கூறினான் கொற்ற வேந்தன் கொழுநிதி நிலத்து மற்றுன்","வீறுயர் புகழை வித்திக் கேண்மையை விளைத்தி யின்னே","நாறுபூங் கொம்ப னாளை நோக்கென நம்பி சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The one who sat in distress like a wounded lion spoke: 'Sow your towering fame in this victorious king's rich land, and reap kinship from it now; look at the girl who is like a fragrant flowering branch.'","n":"The appeal is put in the language of farming: fame is the seed and kinship (கேண்மை) the harvest, a quiet repetition of the drum's promise of the bride (1275). The verse names the speaker twice, as நம்பி and as கொற்ற வேந்தன், and it is not clear whether the king or the prince speaks, or whether கொற்ற வேந்தன் is the one addressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","sutram","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1284","lb":"CC1284","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நரபதி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1285":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1285,"t":["புற்றிடை வெகுளி நாகம் போக்கறக் கொண்ட தேனு","மற்றிடை யூறு செய்வான் வானவர் வலித்த தேனும்","பொற்றொடிக் கிறத்த லில்லை புலம்புகொண் டழேற்க வென்றான்","கற்றடிப் படுத்த விஞ்சைக் காமரு காம னன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, master of learned spells and handsome as Kama, says: whether the furious snake from the anthill seized her past escape, or the gods willed it to do her harm, the girl with golden bangles will not die; do not weep in grief.","n":"கற்று அடிப்படுத்த விஞ்சை calls his power a learned art mastered by practice, not a gift of the moment, though the story traced it to a god's teaching. He allows that the gods may have caused the bite and still promises life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","kalvi","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1285","lb":"CC1285","ev":"Civakan assures the household that Padumai will not die.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1286":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1286,"t":["பொழிந்துநஞ் சுகுத்த லச்ச மிரைபெரு வெகுளி போகங்","கழிந்துமீ தாடல் காலம் பிழைப்பென வெட்டி னாகும்","பிழிந்துயி ருண்ணுந் தட்ட மதட்டமாம் பிளிற்றி னும்ப","ரொழிந்தெயி றூனஞ் செய்யுங் கோளென மற்றுஞ் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"He adds the lore of bites: a snake bites for eight causes, pouring out venom, fear, prey, great anger, after mating, being trodden on, the season, and by mistake; and he names the kinds of bite, the one that squeezes and drinks life and the others, by how the fangs strike.","n":"Before any cure Civakan classifies: he names causes and kinds of bite as a physician names a disease, and that is what sets him apart from the healers of 1276-1279. Several terms (தட்டம், அதட்டம், பிளிற்று) are technical and their division is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","noy","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1286","lb":"CC1286","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","இறைவளையவர்"],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1287":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1287,"t":["அந்தண னாறு மான்பா லவியினை யலர்ந்த காலை","நந்தியா வட்ட நாறு நகைமுடி யரச னாயிற்","றந்தியா முரைப்பிற் றாழைத் தடமலர் வணிக னாறும்","பந்தியாப் பழுப்பு நாறிற் சூத்திரன் பால தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says a bite smelling of the milk-and-ghee offering at dawn is from a Brahmin snake, one smelling of nandyavattam flowers from a royal snake, one smelling of the great screwpine from a merchant snake, and one smelling of decay from a Sudra snake.","n":"Snakes are sorted into the four varnas, each known by a smell drawn from its varna's world (the sacrificial offering, the royal flower, the merchant's trade flower, decay). This is snake-poison lore, not a statement about people, but it carries the varna order into nature without question.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1287","lb":"CC1287","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","இறைவளையவர்"],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1288":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1288,"t":["கன்னியைக் கடித்த நாகங் கன்னியே கன்னி நோக்க","மன்னதே யரசர் சாதி மூன்றெயி றழுந்தி யாழ்ந்த","கொன்னுமா நாகங் கொண்டாற் கொப்புளாம் விரலிற் றேய்த்தான்","மன்னிய தெண்மட் டாயின் மண்டலிப் பால தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The snake that bit the maiden is itself a maiden, as the girl's look shows; it is of royal caste; and a great snake whose three fangs sink deep leaves blisters when the spot is rubbed with a finger, while one whose mark stays clear is of the மண்டலி kind.","n":"The royal snake that bit a princess matches its victim, a symmetry the lore of 1287 makes possible. How lines 3 and 4 divide the tests is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","noy","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1288","lb":"CC1288","ev":"Civakan diagnoses the bite as from a young female snake of royal caste.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","இறைவளையவர்"],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1289":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1289,"t":["குன்றிரண் டனைய தோளான் கொழுமலர்க் குவளைப் போதங்","கொன்றிரண் டுருவ மோதி யுறக்கிடை மயில னாடன்","சென்றிருண் டமைந்த கோலச் சிகழிகை யழுத்திச் செல்வ","னின்றிரண் டுருவ மோதி நேர்முக நோக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, his shoulders like twin hills, recited the mantra twice, pressed the girl's dark, well-dressed hair knot, and stood reciting it twice more as he looked straight into her face.","n":"The cure is mantra and touch at the crown of the head, with the gaze into her face as part of the rite; the look that heals is also the first look between the two. இரண்டு உருவம் ஓதி most likely counts the repetitions (உரு) of the spell.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","marundhu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1289","lb":"CC1289","ev":"Civakan recites the mantra, pressing her hair knot and gazing into her face.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1290":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1290,"t":["நெடுந்தகை நின்று நோக்க நீள்கடற் பிறந்த கோலக்","கடுங்கதிர்க் கனலி கோப்பக் காரிரு ளுடைந்த தேபோ","லுடம்பிடை நஞ்சு நீங்கிற் றொண்டொடி யுருவ மார்ந்து","குடங்கையி னெடிய கண்ணாற் குமரன்மே னோக்கி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"As the noble one stood gazing, the venom left her body as darkness breaks when the bright sun born of the long sea gathers its rays; the girl regained her form and looked at the young man with long eyes cupped like a palm.","n":"Her first act on returning to life is to look at Civakan (குமரன்மேல் நோக்கினாள்), so revival and falling in love are one moment; the drum's promise of 1275 has found its winner.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1290","lb":"CC1290","ev":"The venom leaves Padumai, she revives and her first look falls on Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:55"},"cc:1291":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1291,"t":["நோக்கினா ணிறையு நாணு மாமையுங் கவினு நொய்திற்","போக்கினாள் வளையும் போர்த்தாள் பொன்னிறப் பசலை மூழ்கிற்","றாக்கினா ளநங்க னப்புத் தூணியை யமரு ளானா","தோக்கிய முருக னெஃக மோரிரண் டனைய கண்ணாள்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator tells that Padumai, once she had looked at Civakan, at once lost her restraint, her modesty, her dark glow and her beauty, let her bangles slip, was covered over in a gold-coloured pallor, and made herself a quiver for Kama's arrows, she whose eyes were like the twin spears of Murugan raised in battle.","n":"The first thing the cured girl does is look, and the look undoes in one line the whole list the akam books treat as a woman's defences (நிறை, நாண்) together with the signs the Kural gives to separation (slipping bangles, பசலை); here they come at the first sight, not after parting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","nirai","pasalai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1291","lb":"CC1291","ev":"Padumai, revived from the snakebite, looks at Civakan and is at once overcome by love.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1292":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1292,"t":["ஆட்சி யைம்பொறி யாள னுடம்பெனும்","பூட்சி நீள்கொடிப் புற்றி னகத்துறை","வாட்க ணோக்கெனும் வையெயிற் றாரழல்","வேட்கை நாகத்தின் மீட்டுங் கொளப்பட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says she was seized again by the snake of desire, whose fangs are the look of sword-like eyes and whose venom is a fierce fire, the snake that dwells within the anthill called the body, ruled by the master of the five senses.","n":"மீட்டும் ('again') ties the figure to the plot: the girl has just been cured of a real snakebite, and the poet makes her love a second bite. The anthill of the body and the five senses (ஐம்பொறி) are the yogic and ascetic image of the body; whose eyes give the bite is not said, and வாட்கண் is usually a woman's epithet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1292","lb":"CC1292","ev":"Padumai, just freed of the snake's poison, is struck anew by desire.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1293":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1293,"t":["மாழ்கி வெய்துயிர்த் தாண்மட வாளெனத்","தோழி மார்களுந் தாயருந் தொக்குடன்","சூழி யானையன் னாய்தொடி னஞ்சறும்","வாழி யென்றனர் வம்பலர் கோதையார்."],"p":"","r":"As she swooned and sighed hotly, her companions and mothers, women with fragrant garlands, gathered round and told Civakan, whom they liken to an adorned elephant, that if he touched her the poison would go, and blessed him.","n":"The women read the swoon of love as a return of the venom; the reader, told in 1292 that the second snake is desire, sees that their remedy (his touch) is the right one for the wrong reason.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1293","lb":"CC1293","ev":"Padumai faints again, and her companions and mothers beg Civakan to touch her and drive out the poison.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1294":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1294,"t":["கண்ணிற் காணினுங் கட்டுரை கேட்பினு","நண்ணித் தீண்டினு நல்லுயிர் நிற்குமென்","றெண்ணி யேந்திழை தன்னை யுடம்பெலாந்","தண்ணென் சாந்தம்வைத் தாலொப்பத் தைவந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking that if she saw him, heard his firm words or felt his touch her life would hold, Civakan stroked the jewelled girl all over her body, as if spreading cool sandal paste on her.","n":"The three channels he counts on (sight, speech, touch) are the senses through which love entered in 1291 and 1292; the cure goes in by the same doors as the sickness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","uyir","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1294","lb":"CC1294","ev":"Civakan strokes Padumai's whole body to keep her alive.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1295":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1295,"t":["மற்ற மாதர்தன் வாட்டடங் கண்களா","லுற்ற நோக்க முறாததொர் நோக்கினிற்","சுற்றி வள்ளலைச் சோர்வின்றி யாத்திட்டா","ளற்ற மில்லமிர் தாகிய வஞ்சொலாள்."],"p":"","r":"Then the girl of flawless, nectar-sweet speech, with a look of her wide sword-like eyes that looked as though it did not look, wound round the generous man and bound him fast.","n":"உற்ற நோக்கம் உறாததொர் நோக்கு, a look that touches and seems not to, is the akam convention of the averted glance; here it is not a sign to be read but the cord that ties him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kurippu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1295","lb":"CC1295","ev":"Padumai binds Civakan with a covert glance.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1296":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1296,"t":["விஞ்சையர் வீர னென்பார் விண்ணவர் குமர னென்பா","ரெஞ்சிய வுயிரை மீட்டா னிவனலா லில்லை யென்பார்","மஞ்சுசூ ழிஞ்சி மூதூர் மாமுடிக் குரிசி னாளை","நஞ்சுசூழ் வேலி னாற்கே நங்கையைக் கொடுக்கு மென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Some said he was a Vidyadhara hero, some that he was a prince of the gods, some that no one but he could have brought back a life that was ebbing away, and some that the great-crowned king of the old walled city would give the maiden this very day to the man with the deadly spear.","n":"Each guess is introduced by என்பார், 'some say'; the crowd does not know who he is, and the hero keeps his identity hidden in this ilampakam.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1296","lb":"CC1296","ev":"Onlookers debate who Civakan is and foretell that the king will give him his daughter.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாந்தர்",""],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1297":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1297,"t":["விளங்கொளி விசும்பில் வெண்கோட் டிளம்பிறை சூழ்ந்த மின்போல்","வளங்கெழு வடத்தைச் சூழ்ந்து வான்பொனாண் டிளைப்பச் சேந்த","விளங்கதிர் முலைக டம்மா லிவனைமார் பெழுதி வைகிற்","றுளங்குபெண் பிறப்புந் தோழி யினிதெனச் சொல்லி நிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Other women stood saying to a friend that if one could lie with this man, having painted his chest with one's breasts, as lightning plays round the young white crescent in a bright sky and as gold chains play over a rich necklace, then even a woman's wavering birth would be sweet.","n":"துளங்கு பெண் பிறப்பு, 'the trembling birth of a woman', is said in passing as common knowledge; the women do not argue it, they only name the one thing that would redeem it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1297","lb":"CC1297","ev":"","cl":[["a woman's birth is a wavering, unsettled lot, made sweet only by union with such a man","general","reported",4,"city_women"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1298":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1298,"t":["அருந்தவஞ் செய்து வந்த வாயிழை மகளிர் யார்கொல்","பெருந்தகை மார்பிற் றுஞ்சிப் பெண்மையாற் பிணிக்கு நீரார்","கருங்கணின் யாமுங் கண்டாங் காமனை யென்று சொல்லித்","திருந்தொளி முறுவற் செவ்வாய்த் தீஞ்சொலார் மயங்கி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Women of sweet speech and bright smiles were bewildered, asking who the jewelled women might be who, having done rare penance, would be born to sleep on this noble man's chest and bind him with their womanhood, and saying that with their own dark eyes they too had now seen Kama.","n":"அருந்தவஞ் செய்து வந்த, 'who came having done rare penance', puts the marriage the story will bring under the law of merit from a past life; the speakers are bewildered onlookers, and the verse does not mark the claim as the poet's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1298","lb":"CC1298","ev":"","cl":[["union with a worthy husband is the fruit of rare penance done in a former life","general","reported",1,"city_women"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1299":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1299,"t":["பன்மலர்ப் படலைக் கண்ணிக் குமரனைப் பாவை நல்லார்","மன்னவன் பணியின் வாழ்த்தி வாசநெய் பூசி நன்னீர்","துன்னின ராட்டிச் செம்பொற் செப்பினுட் டுகிலுஞ் சாந்து","மின்னறும் புகையும் பூவுங் கலத்தொடு மேந்தி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"At the king's command the fair women praised the youth with his garland of many flowers, rubbed him with scented oil, bathed him in clear water, and brought in red-gold caskets clothes, sandal paste, sweet incense and flowers with their vessels.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1299","lb":"CC1299","ev":"By the king's order the palace women bathe and anoint Civakan and bring him clothes and perfumes.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1300":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1300,"t":["ஏந்திய வேற்பத் தாங்கி யெரிமணிக் கொட்டை நெற்றி","வாய்ந்தபொன் குயிற்றிச் செய்த மரவடி யூர்ந்து போகி","யாயந்தநன் மாலை வேய்ந்த வரும்பெறற் கூடஞ் சேர்ந்தான்","பூந்தொடி மகளிர் போற்றிப் பொற்கலம் பரப்பி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Wearing wooden sandals inlaid with gold and with a gleaming gem knob at the front, he went and reached the rare hall decked with chosen garlands, where the women with flowered bangles honoured him and set out golden vessels.","n":"Line 1 (ஏந்திய வேற்பத் தாங்கி) does not divide clearly; the reading of the opening phrase is a guess, and the sandals and the hall are the verse's sure content.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1300","lb":"CC1300","ev":"Civakan goes in jewelled sandals to the garlanded hall, where the women lay out golden dishes.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1301":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1301,"t":["கன்னியர் கரக நீராற் றாமரை கழீஇய தொப்பப்","பொன்னடி கழீஇய பின்றைப் புரிந்துவாய் நன்கு பூசி","யின்மலர்த் தவிசி னுச்சி யிருந்தமிர் தினிதிற் கொண்டான்","மின்விரிந் திலங்கு பைம்பூண் வேற்கணார் வேனிலானே."],"p":"","r":"After maidens had washed his golden feet with water from pitchers, as one washes lotuses, he rinsed his mouth well, sat high on a seat of sweet flowers and ate the nectar-like food with pleasure, he who was the god of spring to spear-eyed women in bright jewels.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1301","lb":"CC1301","ev":"Civakan's feet are washed and he sits on a flower seat and eats.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1302":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1302,"t":["வாசநற் பொடியு நீருங் காட்டிடக் கொண்டு வாய்ப்பப்","பூசறுத் தங்கை நீரை மும்முறை குடித்து முக்காற்","காசறத் துடைத்த பின்றைக் கைவிர லுறுப்புத் தீட்டி","தூசினா லங்கை நீவி யிருந்தனன் றோற்ற மிக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"When fragrant powder and water were brought, he took them, washed off, sipped water from his palm three times, wiped his mouth three times so no stain was left, touched the parts of his face with his fingers, smoothed his palm with a cloth, and sat, splendid to see.","n":"The poet slows the story for the full after-meal rite, three sips, three wipings and the fingers to the face, the very sequence a rule book of conduct sets out; the hero's correctness at table is part of what the king is watching before he tests his learning in 1303.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","thooimai","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1302","lb":"CC1302","ev":"After the meal Civakan performs the mouth-rinsing in full.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1303":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1303,"t":["சீர்கொளச் செய்த செம்பெர் னடைப்பையுட் பாகு செல்ல","வார்கழல் குருசிற் கொண்டு கவுளடுத் திருந்த வாங்கட்","போர்கொள்வேன் மன்ன னெல்லாக் கலைகளும் புகன்று கேட்டு","நீர்கொண்மாக் கடல னாற்கு நிகரில்லை நிலத்தி லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When the hero with warrior's anklets had taken betel and areca from a well-made red-gold pouch and held it in his cheek, the king of the warring spear asked him about all the arts, heard his answers, and said that on earth there was no equal to this man who was like the great sea.","n":"The king tests the stranger's learning only after he has been bathed, fed and given betel; the honour comes before the examination, and the verdict is spoken by the father whose daughter's hand is at stake.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","perumai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1303","lb":"CC1303","ev":"The king questions Civakan on every art and declares him peerless on earth.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தனபதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1304":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1304,"t":["பரிதி பட்டது பன்மணி நீள்விளக்","கெரிய விட்டன ரின்னிய மார்த்தன","வரிய பொங்கணை யம்மெ னமளிமேற்","குரிசி லேறினன் கூர்ந்தது சிந்தையே."],"p":"","r":"The sun set, the long many-jewelled lamps were lit, instruments sounded, the prince climbed onto the soft couch heaped with cushions, and his thoughts grew intent.","n":"கூர்ந்தது சிந்தையே closes the public day and opens the private night; what his thought turns to is left for the next verses to say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1304","lb":"CC1304","ev":"At nightfall Civakan goes to his couch, his mind turning on Padumai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1305":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1305,"t":["பூங்க ணவ்வயி னோக்கம் பொறாதபோல்","வீங்கி வெம்மைகொண் டேந்தின வெம்முலை","யீங்கி தென்னென விட்டிடை நைந்தது","பாங்கி லாரிற் பரந்துள தல்குலே."],"p":"","r":"Her breasts, as if unable to bear his gaze there, swelled hot and rose, her waist gave way and wilted as if asking what this was, and her hips spread wider than men who keep no measure.","n":"The verse has no first-person mark; it is read as the start of Civakan's night thoughts because 1306 and 1307 speak as 'I', but it may be the narrator's. The simile of line 4 (பாங்கிலாரின்) is a reading of a compressed phrase.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1305","lb":"CC1305","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1306":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1306,"t":["முருகு வார்குழ லாண்முகிழ் மென்முலை","பெருகு நீர்மையிற் பேதுற வெய்திநின்","றுருகு நுண்ணிடை யோவியப் பாவைத","னருகு நோக்கமெ னாவி யலைக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says that the tender budding breasts of the girl with fragrant long hair, swelling in their nature, stand there to distress, her slender waist melts, and the side-glance of that painted doll torments his life.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1306","lb":"CC1306","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1307":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1307,"t":["புகைய வாவிய பூந்துகி லேந்தல்குல்","வகைய வாமணி மேகலை வார்மது","முகைய வாவிய மொய்குழல் பாவியேன்","பகைய வாய்ப்படர் நோய்பயக் கின்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says that the smoke-perfumed flower-cloth on her raised hips, the jewelled girdle and her thick hair where honeyed buds open have all turned enemy to wretched him and breed a spreading sickness.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nalam","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1307","lb":"CC1307","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1308":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1308,"t":["போது லாஞ்சிலை யோபொரு வேற்கணோ","மாது லாமொழி யோமட நோக்கமோ","யாது நானறி யேனணங் கன்னவள்","காத லாற்கடை கின்றது காமமே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan asks whether it is her flower bow, her fighting spear-like eyes, her speech full of sweetness or her innocent look, says he cannot tell which, and concludes that it is desire itself that churns him through love for her who is like a goddess who afflicts.","n":"After the list of 'or's he moves the cause from her to himself: the subject of கடைகின்றது is காமம், not any feature of hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1308","lb":"CC1308","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1309":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1309,"t":["அண்ண லவ்வழி யாழ்துயர் நோயுற","வண்ண மாமலர்க் கோதையு மவ்வழி","வெண்ணெய் வெங்கனன் மீமிசை வைத்ததொத்","துண்ணை யாவுரு காவுள ளாயினாள்."],"p":"","r":"While the prince there suffered a sinking grief, the girl with the garland of bright flowers, there too, melted like butter set above hot embers and was barely alive.","n":"The repeated அவ்வழி ('there', 'there too') sets the two nights side by side; the poet now turns from his lament to hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1309","lb":"CC1309","ev":"Civakan and Padumai, in separate rooms, both waste with longing.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1310":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1310,"t":["பெயன்ம ழைப்பிற ழுங்கொடி மின்னிடைக்","கயன்ம ணிக்கணி னல்லவர் கைதொழப்","பயனி ழைத்தமென் பள்ளியுட் பைந்தொடி","மயனி ழைத்தவம் பாவையின் வைகினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The girl with a waist like lightning flickering in rain and eyes like gleaming fish lay, while good women saluted her, in the soft bed made for her pleasure, like a doll fashioned by Maya.","n":"மயன் இழைத்த பாவை, a doll made by the divine craftsman, gives her beauty and her stillness at once: she lies as lifeless as a figure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1310","lb":"CC1310","ev":"Padumai lies motionless in her bed.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1311":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1311,"t":["வணங்கு நோன்சிலை வார்கணைக் காமனோ","மணங்கொள் பூமிசை மைவரை மைந்தனோ","நிணந்தெ னெஞ்ச நிறைகொண்ட கள்வனை","யணங்கு காளறி யேனுரை யீர்களே."],"p":"","r":"Padumai asks her maidens whether the thief who clung to her heart and took her restraint is Kama with his bending bow and long arrows, or the young god of the dark hill on the fragrant flower, saying she does not know, and begs them to tell her.","n":"நிறைகொண்ட கள்வன், 'the thief who took my restraint', names the loss of 1291 from inside. Which god மைவரை மைந்தன் means (perhaps Murugan) the verse does not settle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1311","lb":"CC1311","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1312":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1312,"t":["கடைகந் தன்னதன் காமரு வீங்குதோ","ளடையப் புல்லினன் போன்றணி வெம்முலை","யுடைய வாகத் துறுதுயர் மீட்டவ","ளிடைய தாகுமெ னாருமி லாவியே."],"p":"","r":"Padumai seems to say that her life, which has no one else, now lies with him who, as if he had embraced her fully with his lovely swelling shoulders like gate-posts, drew out the pain lodged in her breast.","n":"Line 3 ends மீட்டவள் (feminine), where the sense seems to want the man who saved her; the verse may instead mean 'my life is in the waist of her who was saved', so the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","punarchi","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1312","lb":"CC1312","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை",""],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1313":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1313,"t":["இறுதி யில்லமிர் தெய்துந ரீண்டியன்","றறிவி னாடிய வம்மலை மத்தமா","நெறியி னின்று கடைந்திடப் பட்டநீர்","மறுகு மாக்கடல் போன்றதென் னெஞ்சமே."],"p":"","r":"Padumai says her heart is like the great sea whose water was churned and tossed when those seeking undying nectar gathered and, with knowledge, used the mountain as a churning stick.","n":"The churning of the ocean for nectar is a Puranic story the Jain poet uses as plain ornament; the nectar sought points back to the life she has been given and the man who gave it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy","amudham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1313","lb":"CC1313","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை",""],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1314":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1314,"t":["நகைவெண் டிங்களு நார்மட லன்றிலுந்","தகைவெள் ளேற்றணற் றாழ்மணி யோசையும்","பகைகொண் மாலையும் பையுள்செ யாம்பலும்","புகையில் பொங்கழல் போற்சுடு கின்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The laughing white moon, the anril in the palmyra frond, the low bell of the fine white bull, the hostile evening and the water-lily that brings grief all burn like a smokeless blazing fire.","n":"The list is the akam evening of separation (the anril, the herd's bell, the moon); no first person marks the speaker, and it is read as Padumai's lament continuing from 1311 to 1313.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1314","lb":"CC1314","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை",""],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1315":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1315,"t":["பூமென் சேக்கையு ணாற்றிய பூந்திரட்","டாமம் வாட்டுந் தகைய வுயிர்ப்பளைஇக்","காமர் பேதைதன் கண்டரு காமநோ","யாமத் தெல்லையோர் யாண்டொத் திறந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says her sighs withered the round flower garlands strewn on her soft bed, and for the lovely girl, sick with the love her own eyes had given her, the length of the night passed like a year.","n":"தன் கண் தரு காமநோய், 'the love-sickness her eyes gave', brings the chapter back to 1291, where the trouble began with her look.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kan","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1315","lb":"CC1315","ev":"Padumai spends a sleepless night of longing that seems a year long.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:56"},"cc:1316":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1316,"t":["மாதி யாழ்மழ லைம்மொழி மாதரா","டாதி யவ்வையுந் தன்னமர் தோழியும்","போது வேய்குழற் பொன்னவிர் சாயலுக்","கியாது நாஞ்செயற் பாலதென் றெண்ணினார்."],"p":"","r":"The elder kinswoman and the dear companion of the girl whose prattle is like the yazh ask each other what they ought to do for the flower-haired one of golden grace.","n":"The word run மாதராடாதி can be divided as மாதராள் தாதி (the girl's nurse) or read with ஆதி (the first, the eldest); 1337 calls the same woman தவ்வை, the elder sister. The verse does not say why she needs their help; the sequel shows it is her longing for Civakan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1316","lb":"CC1316","ev":"Padumai's elder kinswoman and her companion take counsel over what to do for the lovesick girl.","cl":[],"ag":["தவ்வை","தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1317":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1317,"t":["அழுது நுண்ணிடை நைய வலர்முலை","முழுதுங் குங்கும முத்தொ டணிந்தபின்","றொழுது கோதையுங் கண்ணியுஞ் சூட்டினா","ரெழுது கொம்பனை யாரிளை யாளையே"],"p":"","r":"Her slender waist all but weeping under the weight, they cover her swelling breasts with saffron and pearls and then, reverently, set the garland and the chaplet on the young one who is like a painted creeper.","n":"அழுது, weeping, is the poet's conceit for the waist that strains under the breasts; it is not a sign that Padumai weeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1317","lb":"CC1317","ev":"The two women adorn Padumai with saffron, pearls, garland and chaplet.","cl":[],"ag":["தவ்வை","தோழி","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1318":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1318,"t":["வேந்து காயினும் வெள்வளை யாயமோ","டேந்து பூம்பொழி லெய்தியங் காடுத","லாய்ந்த தென்றுகொண் டம்மயில் போற் குழீஇப்","போந்த தாயம் பொழிலும் பொலிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Judging that it is right to go with the white-bangled band to the flowering grove and play there even if the king should be angry, the company gathers like peacocks and comes, and the grove shines with them.","n":"வேந்து காயினும் admits that the outing is against the king's wish, so the grove where the lovers meet is reached by a small defiance of the father who in 1339 gives her away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1318","lb":"CC1318","ev":"Padumai's companions decide, at the risk of the king's anger, to take her to the pleasure grove.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1319":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1319,"t":["அலங்க றான்றொடுப் பாரலர் பூக்கொய்வார்","சிலம்பு சென்றெதிர் கூவுநர் செய்சுனை","கலங்கப் பாய்ந்துட னாடுநர் காதலி","னிலங்கு பாவை யிருமணஞ் சேர்த்துவார்."],"p":"","r":"Some string garlands, some pluck flowers, some call back to the echo off the hill, some leap together into the made pool and stir it, and some, for love, wed one bright doll to another.","n":"The doll wedding (பாவை இருமணம்) is the girls' game, but it foreshadows the union that will take place in the same grove.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1319","lb":"CC1319","ev":"The girls of the company play in the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1320":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1320,"t":["தூசு லாநெடுந் தோகையி னல்லவ","ரூச லாடுந ரொண்கழங் காடுநர்","பாச மாகிய பந்துகொண் டாடுந","ராகி யெத்திசை யும்மமர்ந் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Women in long peacock-like garments swing, play at bright jacks, play with a ball held on a cord, and settle in every corner of the grove.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1320","lb":"CC1320","ev":"The girls swing and play jacks and ball through the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1321":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1321,"t":["முருகு விம்மிய மொய்குழ லேழைத","னுருகு நோக்க முளங்கிழித் துள்சுட","வரிவை யாடிய காவகங் காணிய","வெரிகொள் வேலவ னேகின னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"They say the man of the flaming spear, his heart torn open and burned within by the melting look of the fragrant-haired girl, went to see the grove where she was playing.","n":"The longing is shared: the women's counsel in 1316 and Civakan's wound here make both lovers sick before the meeting. The look that wounds is hers (உருகு நோக்கம், a melting look), and the poet hedges with என்பவே, 'they say'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1321","lb":"CC1321","ev":"Civakan, lovesick from Padumai's look, goes to the grove where she plays.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1322":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1322,"t":["மயிலி னாடலு மந்தியி னூடலுங்","குயிலின் பாடலுங் கூடி மலிந்தவண்","வெயிலி னீங்கிய வெண்மணற் றண்ணிழல்","பயிலு மாதவிப் பந்தரொன் றெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Where the peacocks' dance, the monkeys' sulking and the koels' song all mingle, he comes to a bower of madhavi creeper with cool shade on white sand, out of the sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1322","lb":"CC1322","ev":"Civakan reaches a shaded madhavi bower in the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1323":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1323,"t":["காது சேர்ந்த கடிப்பணை கையது","தாது மல்கிய தண்கழு நீர்மல","ரோத நித்தில வட்டமொர் பொன்செய்நாண்","கோதை வெம்முலை மேற்கொண்ட கோலமே."],"p":"","r":"Her array: the ear ornament set at her ears, a cool blue water-lily heavy with pollen in her hand, a ring of pearls like the sea's and a cord of gold on her warm breasts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1323","lb":"CC1323","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1324":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1324,"t":["விண்பு தைப்பன வெண்மலர் வேய்ந்துளாற்","கண்பு தைப்பன காரிரும் பூம்பொழில்","சண்ப கத்தணி கோதைநின் றாடனி","நண்ப னைந்நினை யாநறு மேனியே."],"p":"","r":"Decked in white flowers that would hide the sky, in a dark grove that hides the eye, the girl with the champak garland stands alone, her fragrant body given over to thought of her beloved.","n":"நினையா can be the verbal participle 'thinking' or the negative 'not thinking'; the reading here takes it as thinking of the friend (நண்பன்), the lover she has met before, but the line is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1324","lb":"CC1324","ev":"Padumai stands alone in the dark grove.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1325":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1325,"t":["கறந்த பாலினுட் காசி றிருமணி","நிறங்கி ளர்ந்துதன் னீர்மைகெட் டாங்கவண்","மறைந்த மாதவி மாமை நிழற்றலிற்","சிறந்த செல்வனுஞ் சிந்தையி னோக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"As a flawless sapphire set in fresh milk breaks out in its colour and changes the milk's own nature, so her dark sheen tints the madhavi that hides her, and the noble one sees her with his mind.","n":"The simile turns concealment into display: the leaves that hide her take on her colour, so he knows her presence before he sees her. சிந்தையின் நோக்கினான் may mean he looked intently, or that he saw her in thought; the reading keeps both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1325","lb":"CC1325","ev":"Civakan glimpses Padumai through the madhavi bower.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1326":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1326,"t":["வரையின் மங்கைகொல் வாங்கிருந் தூங்குநீர்த்","திரையின் செல்விகோ றேமலர்ப் பாவைகொ","லுரையின் சாய லியக்கிகொல் யார்கொலிவ்","விரைசெய் கோலத்து வெள்வளைத் தோளியே."],"p":"","r":"Is she the maiden of the mountain, the lady of the curving deep sea's waves, the woman of the honey lotus, or a famed yakshi? Who is this white-bangled one in fragrant array?","n":"This is the lover's doubt (ஐயம்) of the Tamil code of secret love: he asks whether she is a goddess before he can approach her. The four candidates, Parvati of the mountain, the sea goddess, Lakshmi of the lotus and a yakshi, are a learned list where the Kural names only a goddess, a peahen or a woman.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1326","lb":"CC1326","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1327":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1327,"t":["மாலை வாடின வாட்க ணிமைத்தன","காலும் பூமியைத் தோய்ந்தன காரிகைப்","பாலின் தீஞ்சொற் பதுமையிந் நின்றவள்","சோலை வேய்மருள் சூழ்வளைத் தோளியே."],"p":"","r":"Her garland has withered, her sword-like eyes blink, her feet touch the earth: the one standing here is Padumai of the milk-sweet speech, with arms like the bamboo of the grove.","n":"The doubt is settled by the three marks a goddess lacks: her garland fades, her eyes blink, her feet rest on the ground. It is the clearing (தெளிவு) that follows the doubt, and it ends with her name, for he has met her before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","anangu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1327","lb":"CC1327","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1328":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1328,"t":["தேவர் பண்ணிய தீந்தொடை யின்சுவை","மேவர் தென்றமிழ் மெய்ப்பொரு ளாதலிற்","கோவத் தன்னமென் சீறடிக் கொம்பனாள்","பூவர் சோலை புகுவலென் றெண்ணினான்."],"p":"","r":"Because the sweet taste of the sweet verse that the Devar made is the true matter of southern Tamil, he resolves to enter the flowering grove of the girl with tender feet red as the kovam insect.","n":"The verse grounds the hero's decision in literature: love is the மெய்ப்பொருள், the true subject, of Tamil poetry, so he follows it. தேவர் may be the gods or the poet's own name, Tiruttakkatevar; read as the poet, the line has the epic cite itself as the authority for kalavu. The reading is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","kaamam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1328","lb":"CC1328","ev":"Civakan resolves to go into the grove to Padumai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1329":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1329,"t":["அல்லி சேரணங் கன்னவட் காயிடைப்","புல்லி நின்றமெய்ந் நாண்புறப் பட்டது","கல்செய் தோளவன் காமரு பேருணர்","வெல்லை நீங்கிற் றியைந்தன ரென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Between him and her, who is like the goddess of the lotus, the modesty that had held her fast fell away, and the great awareness of the stone-shouldered man passed its bounds: they were joined, it is said.","n":"The union is told by what gives way, her naanam and his restraint, not by any rite; the father knows nothing of it when he decides on the marriage in 1339.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kalaviyal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1329","lb":"CC1329","ev":"Civakan and Padumai unite in secret in the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1330":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1330,"t":["களித்த கண்ணிணை காம்பென வீங்குதோட்","டெளிர்த்த வெள்வளை சேர்ந்தது மாமையுந்","தளித்த சுண்ணஞ் சிதைந்தன குங்கும","மளித்த பூம்பட் டணிந்து திகழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Her eyes grow drunk, her white bangles clink and meet on her swelling bamboo-like arms, her dark sheen and the sprinkled powder are spoiled, and she shines in the flowered silk the saffron has stained.","n":"A catalogue of the marks union leaves on the woman's body, which in the Tamil code of secret love is what the companions might read.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1330","lb":"CC1330","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1331":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1331,"t":["குவளை ஏய்ந்த கொடுங் குழை கூந்தலுள்","திவளும் வாழிய செம் பொறி வண்டுகாள்","இவள கூர் எயிறு ஈனும் தகையவோ","தவள மெல் இணர்த் தண் கொடி முல்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Long may you live, red-spotted bees who flit in her hair where the curved earring meets the blue lily: say, has the white, soft-clustered cool jasmine creeper the beauty to put forth teeth as sharp as hers?","n":"Civakan praises her (நலம் புனைந்துரைத்தல்) by asking the bees, the creatures who know flowers best, to judge whether the mullai can match her teeth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1331","lb":"CC1331","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரும்பு, வண்டு, தேன், ஞிமிறு"],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1332":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1332,"t":["பொன் துஞ்சு ஆகத்துப் பூங் கண்கள் போழ்ந்த புண்","இன்று இப் பூண் கொள் இள முலைச் சாந்து அலால்","அன்றித் தீர்ப்பன யாவையும் இல்லையே","என்று மாதர் எழில் நலம் ஏத்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The wound her flower eyes cleft in my gold-resting chest, nothing can heal now but the sandal on these young adorned breasts: so he praised the woman's beauty.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu","nalam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1332","lb":"CC1332","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1333":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1333,"t":["கண்ணி வேய்ந்து கருங்குழல் கைசெய்து","வண்ண மாலை நடுச்சிகை யுள்வளைஇச்","செண்ண வஞ்சிலம் பேறு துகளவித்","தண்ண லின்புறுத் தாற்றலி னாற்றினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She sets on the chaplet, dresses her dark hair, binds the coloured garland round the central tuft, wipes away the dust risen on her lovely anklets, and with all her skill gives the lord delight.","n":"Putting the hair and anklets right is also hiding the marks of 1330 before her companions return.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1333","lb":"CC1333","ev":"Padumai restores her hair and dress after the union and pleases Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1334":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1334,"t":["திங்க ளும்மறு வும்மெனச் சேர்ந்தது","நங்க ளன்பென நாட்டி வலிப்புறீஇ","யிங்கொ ளித்திடு வேனும ரெய்தினார்","கொங்கொ ளிக்குழ லாயெனக் கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"Settling and making firm that their love is joined as the moon and its mark, he says: your people have come, girl of fragrant shining hair; I will hide here.","n":"The pledge of permanence and the act of hiding come in one breath: secret love is sworn to last at the moment it has to conceal itself from her kin (நுமர், your people).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kalaviyal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1334","lb":"CC1334","ev":"Civakan pledges his love to Padumai and, hearing her women come, says he will hide.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1335":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1335,"t":["மழையி டைக்குளித் திட்டதோர் வாண்மினிற்","றழையி டைக்குளித் தான்றகை வேலினா","னிழையி டைக்குளித் தேந்திய வெம்முலை","வழையி டைக்குளித் தார்வந்து தோன்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"Like a bright lightning that plunges into a cloud, the man of the fine spear plunged into the foliage, and the women with warm breasts that plunge through their jewels plunged through the vazhai trees and appeared.","n":"The fourfold குளித்து, plunging, binds his vanishing and their arrival into one movement.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1335","lb":"CC1335","ev":"Civakan hides in the leaves as Padumai's companions come through the trees.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தவ்வை","தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1336":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1336,"t":["மின்னொர் பூம்பொழின் மேதகச் செல்வதொத்","தன்ன நாண வசைந்து சிலம்படி","மென்மெ லம்மலர் மேன்மிதித் தேகினா","ணன்ன லம்மவற் கேவைத்த நங்கையே."],"p":"","r":"Like a lightning moving nobly through the flowering grove, putting the swans to shame with her gait, she walked with anklets on her feet, stepping softly on soft flowers, the girl who had laid her goodness with him alone.","n":"நன்னலம் அவற்கே வைத்த, 'who had placed her goodness with him alone', is the narrator's word that the secret union binds her as a wife.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1336","lb":"CC1336","ev":"Padumai walks out to meet her companions.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1337":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1337,"t":["திங்கள் சூழ்ந்தபன் மீனெனச் சென்றெய்தி","நங்கை தவ்வையுந் தோழியு நண்ணினா","ரங்க வாய மடிப்பணி செய்தபின்","றங்கள் காதலி னாற்றகை பாடினார்."],"p":"","r":"Like the stars round the moon, her elder sister and companion reached the girl, and when the company had done service at her feet they sang her excellence out of love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1337","lb":"CC1337","ev":"Padumai's sister, companion and company gather round her and praise her.","cl":[],"ag":["தவ்வை","தோழி","ஆயம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1338":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1338,"t":["தழையுங் கண்ணியுந் தண்ணறு மாலையும்","விழைவ சேர்த்துபு மெல்லென வேகினார்","முழையுண் மூரி முழங்கரி யேறனான்","பழைய நண்பனைப் பண்புளி யெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Gathering the leaf skirts, chaplets and cool fragrant garlands they wanted, they went softly away, and the man like a roaring lion in a cave went, as was fitting, to his old friend.","n":"Who the பழைய நண்பன் is the verse does not say; this chapter does not identify him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1338","lb":"CC1338","ev":"The women leave the grove, and Civakan goes back to an old friend.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயம்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1339":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1339,"t":["பூமியை யாடற் கொத்த பொறியின னாத லானு","மாமக ளுயிரை மீட்ட வலத்தின னாத லானு","நேமியான் சிறுவ னன்ன நெடுந்தகை நேரு மாயி","னாமவற் கழகி தாக நங்கையைக் கொடுத்து மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Since he bears the marks fit to rule the earth and since he is the strong one who brought back my great daughter's life, if this lofty man like the son of the discus-bearer consents, we will give him the girl as is fitting, he said.","n":"The speaker of என்றான் is not named; the story so far makes him Padumai's royal father. He gives two reasons, royal marks on the body and a debt for his daughter's life, and makes the gift wait on the hero's consent. நேமியான் சிறுவன், the son of the discus-bearer, is Kama, Vishnu's son, a figure for beauty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","nanri","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1339","lb":"CC1339","ev":"Padumai's father resolves to give her to Civakan, if he will accept her.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்து"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்து",""],"ch":"cc:57"},"cc:1340":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1340,"t":["மதிதர னென்னு மாசின் மந்திர சொல்லக் கேட்டே","யுதிதர வுணர்வல் யானு மொப்பினு முருவி னானும்","விதிதர வந்த தொன்றே விளங்குபூண் முலையி னாளைக்","கொதிதரு வேலி னாற்கே கொடுப்பது கரும மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king says that, having heard the faultless counsel of his minister Mathidharan, he too sees it clearly: the young man matches her in looks and form, the thing has come as destiny brings it, and the right act is to give his jewelled daughter to the man of the burning spear.","n":"The speaker is named only by the frame (he heard what Mathidharan said and answers with என்றான்); the narrator endorses his reading of fate two verses on, where the portion (பான்மை) is said to have ripened for the spearman (1342).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","amaichu","vathuvai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1340","lb":"CC1340","ev":"The king, after hearing Mathidharan's counsel, decides to give his daughter to Civakan.","cl":[["a match that destiny has brought, shown by likeness of looks and form, should be fulfilled","general","asserted",3,"padumai_thanthai"]],"ag":["வேந்து"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்து","மதிதரன்"],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1341":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1341,"t":["உள்விரித் திதனை யெல்லா முரைக்கென மொழிந்து விட்டான்","றெள்ளிதிற் றெரியச் சென்றாங் குரைத்தலுங் குமரன் றேறி","வெள்ளிலை யணிந்த வேலான் வேண்டிய தாக வென்றா","னள்ளிலை வேல்கொண் மன்னற் கமைச்சனஃ தமைந்த தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king sends the minister to lay all this out plainly; the minister goes and tells it, the prince agrees that it be as the spear-bearing king wishes, and the minister returns to report that the matter is settled.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","thoothu","vathuvai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1341","lb":"CC1341","ev":"Mathidharan carries the offer to Civakan, who accepts, and reports the acceptance to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்து","மதிதரன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1342":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1342,"t":["பொன்றிய வுயிரை மீட்டான் பூஞ்சிகைப் போது வேய்ந்தா","னன்றியு மாமெய் தீண்டி யளித்தன னழகின் மிக்கா","னொன்றிய மகளிர் தாமே யுற்றவர்க் குரிய ரென்னா","வென்றிகொள் வேலி னாற்கே பான்மையும் விளைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator gives the grounds: he brought back her departed life, set flowers in her hair, touched her body and saved her, and is surpassing in beauty; women who have been joined to a man belong to the one who touched them, and so the portion fated for the victorious spearman had ripened.","n":"The touch was the physician's: in 1294 he strokes her whole body to keep her life in. The maxim at line 3 turns that healing touch into a claim on her, so the cure itself becomes the betrothal; the reading of ஒன்றிய and உற்றவர் as touching is the plainest sense of the line, but it is a compressed saying.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","karpu","marundhu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1342","lb":"CC1342","ev":"","cl":[["a woman whose body a man has touched belongs to that man","general","asserted",3,"narrator"],["what is apportioned by fate ripens and comes to the one it is due to","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1343":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1343,"t":["கோப்பெருந் தேவி கொற்றக் கோமக னிவைக ணாடி","யாப்புடைத் தையற் கின்றே நங்கையை யமைக்க வென்னத்","தூப்புரி முத்த மாலை தொடக்கொடு தூக்கி யெங்கும்","பூப்பரிந் தணிந்து கோயில் புதுவது புனைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The great queen and the victorious king, weighing these things, order that the girl be given that very day to the one bound to her, and the palace is newly dressed with hanging pearl garlands and flowers strewn everywhere.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vathuvai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1343","lb":"CC1343","ev":"The queen and king order the wedding for that day, and the palace is decorated.","cl":[],"ag":["கோப்பெருந் தேவி","வேந்து"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1344":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1344,"t":["கணிபுனைந் துரைத்த நாளாற் கண்ணிய கோயி றன்னுண்","மணிபுனை மகளிர் நல்லார் மங்கல மரபு கூறி","யணியுடைக் கமல மன்ன வங்கைசேர் முன்கை தன்மேற்","றுணிவுடைக் காப்புக் கட்டிச் சுற்றுபு தொழுது காத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"On the day the astrologer fixed, inside the chosen palace, jewelled women recite the auspicious forms, tie the protective cord on the bride's wrist above her lotus hand, and stand round her in worship and guard.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vathuvai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1344","lb":"CC1344","ev":"On the fixed day the women tie the protective cord on Padumai's wrist.","cl":[],"ag":["தையலார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1345":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1345,"t":["மழகளிற் றெருத்திற் றந்த மணிக்குட மண்ணு நீரா","லழகனை மண்ணுப் பெய்தாங் கருங்கடிக் கொத்த கோலந்","தொழுதகத் தோன்றச் செய்தார் தூமணிப் பாவை யன்னார்","விழுமணிக் கொடிய னாளும் விண்ணவர் மடந்தை யொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Women like jewelled dolls bathe the handsome bridegroom with water from a gem pot brought on a young elephant's neck and dress him in the attire fit for the wedding, so that he seems worthy of worship; the bride, a gem-like creeper, looks like a maiden of the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vathuvai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1345","lb":"CC1345","ev":"Civakan is bathed and dressed for the wedding, and Padumai is adorned.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னார்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1346":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1346,"t":["கயற்க ணாளையுங் காமனன் னானையு","மியற்றி னார்மண மேத்தருந் தன்மையார்","மயற்கை யில்லவர் மன்றலின் மன்னிய","வியற்கை யன்புடை யாரியைந் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Elders of a dignity hard to praise perform the marriage of the carp-eyed bride and the Kama-like groom, and the two, free of confusion and of a love native to them, are joined.","n":"இயற்கை அன்பு, a love by nature, presents the match as more than the king's arrangement, in line with the fate of 1340 and 1342.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vathuvai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1346","lb":"CC1346","ev":"Elders perform the marriage of Padumai and Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["தன்மையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1347":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1347,"t":["வாளும் வேலு மலைந்தரி யார்ந்தகண்","ணாளும் வார்கழன் மைந்தனு மாயிடைத்","தோளுந் தாளும் பிணைந்துரு வொன்றெய்தி","நாளு நாகர் நுகர்ச்சி நலத்தரே."],"p":"","r":"The bride whose eyes have fought sword and spear and the warrior with the long anklet join shoulder to shoulder and foot to foot as if one form, and day after day share pleasure like the Nagas of the upper world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1347","lb":"CC1347","ev":"Padumai and Civakan begin their married life together.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1348":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1348,"t":["தணிக்குந் தாமரை யாணலந் தன்னையும்","பிணிக்கும் பீடினி யென்செயும் பேதைதன்","மணிக்கண் வெம்முலை தாம்பொர வாயவிழ்ந்","தணிக்கந் தன்னவன் றாரங் குடைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Her hot breasts press against his garland until its buds open and it is crushed, and the poet asks what her pride can now do against such force.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are compressed and the sense of தணிக்கும் தாமரையாள் நலம் and பிணிக்கும் பீடு is hard to fix; the reading follows line 3 and 4, where the crushing of the garland is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1348","lb":"CC1348","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1349":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1349,"t":["பரிந்த மாலை பறைந்தன குங்குமங்","கரிந்த கண்ணி கலந்த மகிழ்ச்சியி","னரிந்த மேகலை யார்த்தன வஞ்சிலம்","பிரிந்த வண்டிளை யார்விளை யாடவே."],"p":"","r":"The signs of their love-play: garlands torn, saffron smeared, wreaths wilted, girdles snapped and the anklets sounding as the two, like bees parted from their kin, sport.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1349","lb":"CC1349","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1350":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1350,"t":["கொழுமெ னின்னகிற் கூட்டுறு மென்புகை","கழுமு சேக்கையும் காலையு மாலையுந்","தழுவு காத றணப்பிலர் செல்பவே","யெழுமை யும்மியைந் தெய்திய வன்பினார்."],"p":"","r":"On a bed thick with the smoke of rich agil, morning and evening, the two whose love has come joined through seven births go on embracing without tiring.","n":"எழுமையும் இயைந்து: the narrator treats the love as a bond carried over seven births, a phrase that will sit hard with the hero's departure ten verses on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","piravi","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1350","lb":"CC1350","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1351":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1351,"t":["நாறி யுஞ்சுவைத் துந்நரம் பின்னிசை","கூறி யுங்குளிர் நாடக நோக்கியு","மூறின் வெம்முலை யாலுழப் பட்டுமவ்","வேற னான்வைகும் வைகலு மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Smelling and tasting, listening to the music of strings and watching cool dances, and pressed by her hot breasts, the spearman spends each day, they say.","n":"The verse runs through the senses one by one, smell, taste, hearing, sight and touch, as the means of his pleasure; என்பவே marks it as report.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam","isai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1351","lb":"CC1351","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1352":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1352,"t":["விரிகதிர் விளங்கு பன்மீன் கதிரொடு மிடைந்து திங்கட்","டெரிகதிர் திரட்டி வல்லான் றெரிந்துகோத் தணிந்த போலுஞ்","சொரிகதிர் முத்த மின்னுந் துணைமுலைத் தடத்தில் வீழ்ந்தான்","புரிகதிர்ப் பொன்செய் மாலைப் புகைநுதிப் புலவு வேலான்."],"p":"","r":"The spearman with the gold garland and the smoke-tipped lance falls upon her breasts, where pearl strings shine as if a master had gathered the moon's rays and the many stars and strung them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1352","lb":"CC1352","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1353":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1353,"t":["எழின்மாலை யென்னுயிரை யான்கண்டே னித்துணையே முலையிற் றாகிக்","குழன்மாலைக் கொம்பாகிக் கூரெயிறு நான்போழ்த லஞ்சி யஞ்சி","யுழன்மாலைத் தீங்கிளவி யொன்றிரண்டு தான்மிழற்று மொருநாட் காறு","நிழன்மாலை வேனாண நீண்டகண்ணே நெய்தோய்ந்த தளிரே மேனி."],"p":"","r":"A voice praises her: I have seen my life, a creeper of a girl with breasts only this much grown, who lisps a sweet word or two as though afraid her sharp teeth might split it, her long eyes shaming a spear, her body a ghee-dipped sprout.","n":"The speaker is not named. என் உயிரை யான் கண்டேன் fits the lover beholding his beloved, and the verse is read as Civakan's; but ஒருநாட்காறும் and the stress on how small she still is could belong to a nurse or mother recalling her growth, and the sense of line 3 is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1353","lb":"CC1353","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1354":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1354,"t":["மாநீர் மணிமுகிலின் மின்னுக் கொடிநுசுப்பின் மயிலஞ் சாய","லேநீ ரிருபுருவ மேறி யிடைமுரிந்து நுடங்கப் புல்லித்","தூநீர் மலர்மார்பன் றொன்னலந் தான்பருகித் துளும்புந் தேறற்","றெனீர் மலர்மாலை தேன்றுளித்து மட்டுயிர்ப்பச் சூட்டி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Embracing the lightning-waisted, peacock-gentle girl so that her two brows rise and her waist bends, the man of the flower-fresh chest drinks her beauty and crowns her with a honey-dripping garland.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1354","lb":"CC1354","ev":"Civakan embraces Padumai and puts a garland on her hair.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1355":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1355,"t":["தேனடைந் திருந்த கண்ணித் தென்மட்டுத் துவலை மாலை","யூனடைந் திருந்த வேற்க ணொண்டொடி யுருவ வீணை","தானடைந் திருந்த காவிற் பாடினா டனிமை தீர்வான்","கூனடைந் திருந்த திங்கட் குளிர்முத்த முலையி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the grove where she stays, a young woman with a honey-filled garland and spear-like eyes plays the vina to relieve her loneliness.","n":"The woman is not named here. Since in the next verse Civakan enters the grove and meets the one called Tesikappavai, and 1358 speaks of two women, she is read as Tesikappavai rather than Padumai; the garland of line 1 could instead link her to 1354.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["isai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1355","lb":"CC1355","ev":"A young woman plays the vina alone in the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["தேசிகப் பாவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1356":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1356,"t":["வார்தளிர் ததைந்து போது மல்கிவண் டுறங்குங் காவிற்","சீர்கெழு குருசில் புக்கான் றேசிகப் பாவை யென்னுங்","கார்கெழு மின்னு வென்ற நுடங்கிடைக் கமழ்தண் கோதை","யேர்கெழு மயில னாளை யிடைவயி னெதிர்ப்பட் டானே."],"p":"","r":"The prince enters the grove where tender leaves crowd, flowers abound and bees sleep, and comes face to face with the peacock-like woman called Tesikappavai, whose swaying waist outdoes the lightning of the rain cloud.","n":"Whether தேசிகப் பாவை is a proper name or an epithet of beauty is not certain from this verse; என்னும் suggests a name, and 1358's two women support a second woman.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1356","lb":"CC1356","ev":"Civakan enters the grove and meets Tesikappavai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தேசிகப் பாவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1357":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1357,"t":["சிலம்பெனும் வண்டு பாட மேகலைத் தேன்க ளார்ப்ப","நலங்கவின் போது பூத்த பூங்கொடி நடுங்கி நாணக்","கலந்தனன் காம மாலை கலையின தியல்பிற் சூட்டப்","புலம்புபோய்ச் சாய லென்னும் புதுத்தளி ரீன்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"With her anklets like humming bees and her girdle like sounding bees, the flower-creeper trembles in shame as he joins her and crowns her with a garland of love according to the art; her loneliness gone, her softness puts out a new sprout.","n":"கலையினது இயல்பின் says the union follows the rule of an art, the poem's sign that love is treated as a learned discipline as well as a feeling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1357","lb":"CC1357","ev":"Civakan unites with Tesikappavai in the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தேசிகப் பாவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1358":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1358,"t":["சாந்திடைக் குளித்த வெங்கட் பணைமுலைத் தாம மாலைப்","பூந்தொடி யரிவை பொய்கைப் பூமக ளனைய பொற்பின்","வேந்தடு குருதி வேற்கண் விளங்கிழை யிவர்க ணாளு","மாய்ந்தடி பரவ வைகு மரிவையர்க் கநங்க னன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"To these two women, the one with sandal-bathed breasts and flower bangles, lovely as the lady of the lotus pool, and the one with spear eyes red as if with kings' blood, who daily bow and praise his feet, he is like Ananga.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1358","lb":"CC1358","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1359":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1359,"t":["இங்ஙன மிரண்டு திங்க ளேகலு மேக வேலா","னங்ஙனம் புணர்ந்த வன்பி னவண்முலைப் போக நீக்கி","யெங்ஙன மெழுந்த துள்ள மிருளிடை யேக லுற்றான்","றங்கிய பொறியி னாக்கந் தனக்கொர்தே ராக நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When two months had passed thus, the spearman put aside the pleasure of her breasts and the love in which they had joined; with his heart risen he set out into the darkness, and the fortune of his settled destiny stood as his chariot.","n":"பொறி, fortune or destiny, is made his vehicle: the departure is his own resolve (எழுந்தது உள்ளம்) and his fate at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1359","lb":"CC1359","ev":"After two months Civakan leaves Padumai and goes out into the night.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1360":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1360,"t":["தயங்கிணர்க் கோதை தன்மேற் றண்ணென வைத்த மென்றோள்","வயங்கிணர் மலிந்த தாரான் வருந்துறா வகையி னீக்கி","நயங்கிள ருடம்பு நீங்கி நல்லுயிர் போவ தேபோ","லியங்கிடை யறுத்த கங்கு லிருளிடை யேகி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Gently, so that she is not hurt, he takes away his soft arm from where it lay across her garlanded body and goes out into the darkness of the night that has stopped all movement, as a good life goes when it leaves the body.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1360","lb":"CC1360","ev":"Civakan slips his arm from Padumai while she sleeps and leaves in the dark.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1361":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1361,"t":["நீனி றக்குழ னேர்வளைத் தோளியைத்","தானு றக்கிடை நீத்தலுந் தன்பினே","வேனி றக்கண் விழித்தன ளென்பவே","பானி றத்துகிற் பையர வல்குலாள்."],"p":"","r":"When he left the dark-haired, bangled girl in her sleep, she woke behind him with her spear-like eyes, they say.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1361","lb":"CC1361","ev":"Padumai wakes after Civakan has gone.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1362":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1362,"t":["ஆக்கை யுள்ளுறை யாவி கெடுத்தவண்","யாக்கை நாடி யயர்வது போலவுஞ்","சேக்கை நாடித்தன் சேவலைக் காணிய","பூக்க ணாடுமொர் புள்ளுமொத் தாளரோ."],"p":"","r":"She is like a body that searches for the life that has left it and faints, and like a bird with flower eyes that searches its nest to find its mate.","n":"The simile reverses the one in 1360: there he went as life leaves the body; here she is the body left behind, which in this image still feels and seeks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1362","lb":"CC1362","ev":"Padumai searches the bed for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1363":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1363,"t":["புல்லும் போழ்தினும் பூணுறி னோமென","மல்லற் காளையை வைது மிழற்றுவா","யில்லி னீக்க முரைத்திலை நீயெனச்","செல்வப் பைங்கிளி தன்னையுஞ் சீறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She reproaches the strong young man who, even when they embraced, would say 'it hurts if the jewel presses', and turns in anger on her fine green parrot too: you did not tell me he was leaving the house.","n":"The tenderness she remembers (he feared even a jewel between them would hurt her) is turned into the charge against him: one so careful of her body left without a word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1363","lb":"CC1363","ev":"Padumai rails at the absent Civakan and scolds her parrot for not warning her.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை","பைங்கிளி"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","பைங்கிளி"],"ch":"cc:58"},"cc:1364":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1364,"t":["ஓவி யக்கொடி யொப்பருந் தன்மையெம்","பாவை பேதுறப் பாயிலி னீங்கிநீ","போவ தோபொரு ளென்றிலை நீயெனப்","பூவை யோடும் புலம்பி மிழற்றினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Padumai reproaches her myna for not asking her lover whether it was right to leave the bed and go while their peerless doll lay distressed.","n":"She scripts for the bird the words it should have said, calling herself 'our doll' (எம் பாவை) in its mouth, so her own grief is voiced through the household's pets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1364","lb":"CC1364","ev":"Padumai, waking to find Civakan gone, laments to her myna.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","பூவை"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1365":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1365,"t":["தன்னொப் பாரையில் லானைத் தலைச்சென்றெம்","பொன்னொப் பாளொடும் போகெனப் போகடாய்","துன்னித் தந்திலை நீயெனத் தூச்சிறை","யன்னப் பேடையொ டாற்றக் கழறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She chides the hen-swan for not going after the peerless one, bidding him go with their golden girl, and bringing him back close.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1365","lb":"CC1365","ev":"Padumai scolds the hen-swan for not stopping or fetching Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","அன்னப் பேடை"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1366":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1366,"t":["மையில் வாணெடுங் கண்வள ராதன","மெய்யெ லாமுடை யாய்மெய்ம்மை காண்டிநீ","யையன் சென்றுழிக் கூறுகென் றாய்மயில்","கையி னாற்றொழு தாள்கயற் கண்ணினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Joining her hands, the carp-eyed girl begs the peacock, whose whole body is eyes that never close, to tell her truly where her lord went.","n":"The peacock's eyed plumage is turned into a witness that cannot have slept, against her own eyes, which she later blames for sleeping (1373).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1366","lb":"CC1366","ev":"Padumai worships the peacock and asks it where Civakan has gone.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","மயில்"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1367":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1367,"t":["வளர்த்த செம்மையை வாலியை வான்பொருள்","விளக்கு வாய்விளக் கேவிளக் காயிவ","ணளித்த காதலொ டாடுமென் னாருயி","ரொளித்த தெங்கென வொண்சுடர் நண்ணினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She goes up to the bright flame and asks the lamp, nourished and pure, that shows great things, why it did not show her where her dear life, who played with her in love, has hidden.","n":"Civakan is called her ஆருயிர், her dear life; the plea to find him is a plea to find her own life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1367","lb":"CC1367","ev":"Padumai turns to the lamp and asks where Civakan is hidden.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","விளக்கு"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1368":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1368,"t":["பருகிப் பாயிரு ணிற்பி னறாதெனக்","கருகி யவ்விருள் கான்றுநின் மெய்யெலா","மெரிய நின்று நடுங்குகின் றாயெனக்","குரிய தொன்றுரைக் கிற்றியென் றூடினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She sulks at the lamp: it drank the spreading dark, blackened and vomited it as soot, and stands trembling with its body burning, yet it will tell her nothing that concerns her.","n":"The flame's flicker is read as guilty trembling, and the verb ஊடினாள் makes her complaint a lover's sulk directed at an object.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1368","lb":"CC1368","ev":"Padumai quarrels with the lamp for its silence.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","விளக்கு"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1369":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1369,"t":["கோடி நுண்டுகி லுங்குழை யுந்நினக்","காடு சாந்தமு மல்லவு நல்குவேன்","மாட மேநெடி யாய்மழை தோய்யந்துளாய்","நாடி நண்பனை நண்ணுக நன்றரோ."],"p":"","r":"She promises the tall mansion that touches the clouds a crore of fine cloths, ornaments, bathing sandal and more if it will seek out her friend and reach him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1369","lb":"CC1369","ev":"Padumai offers gifts to the mansion to send it after Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","மாடம்"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1370":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1370,"t":["ஆட கக்கொழும் பொன்வரை மார்பனைக்","கூடப் புல்லிவை யாக்குற்ற முண்டெனா","நீடெ ரித்திர ணீண்மணித் தூணொடு","சூட கத்திர டோளணி வாடடினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Saying that these were at fault for keeping her from holding close the man with a chest like a mountain of gold, she strips the bracelets from her arms, long as gem pillars.","n":"Line 4 reads வாடடினாள் as printed; the sense of removing or wasting the bracelets is clear from line 2, but the word looks miscopied.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1370","lb":"CC1370","ev":"Padumai pulls off her bracelets, blaming them for hindering her embrace.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1371":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1371,"t":["கொலைகொள் வேலவன் கூடல னேகினா","னிலைகொள் பூணுமனக் கென்செயு மீங்கெனா","மலைகொள் சந்தனம் வாய்மெழுக் கிட்டதன்","முலைகொள் பேரணி முற்றிழை சிந்தினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Saying that the man of the killing spear has left their union and asking what ornaments can do for her here, she smears mountain sandal over her mouth (or face) and scatters the great ornaments from her breasts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1371","lb":"CC1371","ev":"Padumai throws off her breast ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1372":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1372,"t":["அருங்க லக்கொடி யன்னவ னேகினா","னிருந்திவ் வாகத் தெவன்செய்விர் நீரெனா","மருங்கு னோவ வளர்ந்த வனமுலைக்","கருங்கண் சேந்து கலங்க வதுக்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Asking her breasts what they will do in this body now that he has gone, she presses down the full breasts that weigh on her waist, her dark eyes reddening and troubled.","n":"The breasts, praised throughout the love scenes, are addressed as useless parties once the lover is gone; the ornaments and body parts are dismissed in turn (1370 to 1373).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1372","lb":"CC1372","ev":"Padumai beats down her breasts in grief.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1373":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1373,"t":["மஞ்சு சூழ்வரை மார்பனைக் காணிய","துஞ்ச லோம்புமி னெனவுந் துஞ்சினீ","ரஞ்ச னத்தொடு மையணி மின்னென","நெஞ்சி னீணெடுங் கண்மலர் சீறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She rages at her long eyes in her heart: she told them to keep from sleep so as to see the man with a chest like a cloud-girt mountain, yet they slept, so let them now deck themselves with collyrium.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1373","lb":"CC1373","ev":"Padumai blames her eyes for sleeping while Civakan left.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1374":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1374,"t":["அரக்குண் டாமரை யன்னதன் கண்மலர்","விருத்தி மாதர் விலக்க வெரீஇக்கொலோ","வருத்த முற்றன ளென்றுகொன் மேகலை","குரற்கொ டாது குலுங்கிக் குறைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"As her lac-red lotus eyes are held back by the women who tend her, the girdle, as if afraid or thinking she was suffering, gives no sound, shakes and slips down.","n":"The silenced, slipping girdle is the conventional sign of wasting in separation, given a feeling of its own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1374","lb":"CC1374","ev":"Padumai's attendants restrain her, and her girdle falls silent and loosens.","cl":[],"ag":["விருத்தி மாதர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1375":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1375,"t":["துனிவா யினதுன் னுபுசெய் தறியேன்","றனியே னொருபெண் ணுயிரென் னொடுதா","னினியா னிஙனே யுளனே யுரையீர்","பனியார் மலர்மேற் படுவண் டினமே."],"p":"","r":"In a new metre she tells the bees lying on dewy flowers that she never did anything to cause a sulk; she is alone, a single woman's life is all she has, and she asks them to say whether she must now stay like this.","n":"She denies having given cause (துனி), so the departure cannot be read as a lovers' quarrel she started.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1375","lb":"CC1375","ev":"Padumai protests her innocence to the bees.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","வண்டினம்"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1376":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1376,"t":["நிரைவீ ழருவிந் நிமிர்பொன் சொரியும்","வரையே புனலே வழையே தழையே","விரையார் பொழிலே விரிவெண் ணிலவே","யுரையீ ருயிர்கா வலனுள் வழியே."],"p":"","r":"She calls on the rows of falling streams, the gold-pouring hills, the water, the plantain and leaves, the fragrant groves and the spreading moonlight to tell her where the guardian of her life is.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1376","lb":"CC1376","ev":"Padumai asks the hills, waters and moonlight where Civakan has gone.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","வரையே புனலே"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1377":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1377,"t":["எரிபொன் னுலகின் னுறைவீ ர்தனைத்","தெரிவீர் தெரிவில் சிறுமா னிடார்ற்","பரிவொன் றிலிராற் படர்நோய் மிகுமா","லரிதா லுயிர்காப் பமரீ ரருளீர்."],"p":"","r":"She turns to the gods who dwell in the golden heaven and know all: petty humans know nothing, the gods have no pity, so her spreading sickness grows and her life is hard to keep; she begs them for grace.","n":"Line 1 is printed with a break (உறைவீ ர்தனைத்) and line 2 ends மானிடார்ற், so the exact construction is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","uyir","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1377","lb":"CC1377","ev":"Padumai prays to the gods to pity her.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","அமரீர்"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1378":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1378,"t":["புணார்வின் னினிய புலவிப் பொழுதுங்","கணவன் னகலின் னுயிர்கை யகற","லுணார்வீ ரமரார் மகளீ ரருளிக்","கொணார்வீர் கொடியே னுயிரைக் கொணர்வீர்."],"p":"","r":"She asks the daughters of the gods, who know that when a husband leaves even in the sweet hour of a lovers' sulk his wife's life slips from her hand, to be gracious and bring back the life of her, the cruel one.","n":"She calls herself கொடியேன், the cruel one, taking the blame on herself while the next verses blame him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1378","lb":"CC1378","ev":"Padumai begs the goddesses to bring her lover back.","cl":[["a husband's going, even in the sweet hour of a sulk, takes his wife's life from her","general","reported",2,"padumai"]],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","அமரர் மகளிர்"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1379":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1379,"t":["நகைமா மணிமா லைநடைக் கொடிநின்","வகைமா மணிமே கலையா யினதே","லகையா தெனதா விதழைக் குமெனத்","தகைபா டவலாய் ளர்கோ தளர்கோ."],"p":"","r":"She addresses a garland or girdle-like woman and asks whether, if it has become her gem girdle, her breath will not linger, and whether she, who could sing his worth, must droop and droop.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are broken in the text (அகையா தெனதா விதழைக் குமெனத் / தகைபா டவலாய் ளர்கோ), and the addressee is unclear; only the closing cry of drooping is secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1379","lb":"CC1379","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை",""],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1380":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1380,"t":["புனைதார் பொராநொந் துபொதிர்ந் தவென","வினையா ரெரிபூண் முலைகண் குளிர","வுன்கண் மலரா லுழுதோம் பவலாய்","நினையா துநெடுந் தகைநீத் தனையே."],"p":"","r":"Speaking to the absent man, she recalls how, when she said his garland pressed and bruised her, he cooled her burning jewelled breasts with his flower eyes and tended her, and now, without a thought, the great one has left her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1380","lb":"CC1380","ev":"Padumai recalls Civakan's tenderness and reproaches his leaving.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1381":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1381,"t":["அருடேர் வழிநின் றறனே மொழிவாய்","பொருடேர் புலனெய் தியபூங் கழலா","யிருடேர் வழிநின் றினைவேற் கருளா","யுருடே ருயர்கொற் றவன்மைத் துனனே."],"p":"","r":"She calls him one who stands on the road of grace and speaks only dharma, flower-anklet wearer who has gained knowledge that seeks meaning, brother-in-law of the victorious lord of the tall chariot, and asks why he stands on a dark road and shows no grace to her who grieves.","n":"The reproach turns on his own virtues: அருள் தேர் வழி against இருள் தேர் வழி in lines 1 and 3. Who the lord of the tall chariot is (line 4) is not fixed by the verse; it may be her brother, the king's son, which would make Civakan his மைத்துனன்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1381","lb":"CC1381","ev":"Padumai reproaches Civakan for withholding grace.","cl":[["one who walks by grace and speaks dharma should not withhold grace from the one who suffers for him","general","reported",1,"padumai"]],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1382":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1382,"t":["மிகவா யதொர்மீ ளிமைசெய் தனனோ","வுகவா வுனதுள் ளமுவர்த் ததுவோ","விகவா விடரென் வயினீத் திடநீ","தகவா தகவல் லதுசெய் தனையே."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether she did some great wrong or whether his heart, taking no delight, grew sick of her, that he has done what is unfitting and left her in unending sorrow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1382","lb":"CC1382","ev":"Padumai asks what fault of hers drove Civakan away.","cl":[["to abandon a wife in endless sorrow is conduct unfit for a worthy man","general","reported",4,"padumai"]],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1383":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1383,"t":["குளிர்துன் னியபொன் னிலமே குதலாற்","றளரன் னநடை யவடாங் கலளா","யொளிர்பொன் னரிமா லையொசிந் திஙனே","மிளிர்மின் னெனமின் னிலமெய் தினளே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator tells how the swan-gaited girl, unable to hold herself up, her gleaming golden garland crushed, falls to the ground like a flash of lightning.","n":"Line 1 (குளிர்துன் னியபொன் னிலமே குதலாற்) is hard to construe; the fall itself is clear from lines 2 to 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1383","lb":"CC1383","ev":"Padumai faints and falls to the floor.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1384":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1384,"t":["தழுமா வலிமைந் தவெனத் தளரா","வெழுமே ழடியூக் கிநடந் துசெலா","விழுமீ நிலமெய் திமிளிர்ந் துருகா","வழுமா லவலித் தவணங் கிழையே."],"p":"","r":"Crying 'strong youth, embrace me', faltering, rising, she lifts herself and walks seven steps, falls again to the ground, glimmers, melts and weeps, the jewelled one, overwhelmed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1384","lb":"CC1384","ev":"Padumai rises, takes seven steps after him and falls weeping.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1385":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1385,"t":["கரப்புநீர்க் கங்கை யங்கட் கடிமலர்க் கமலப் பள்ளித்","திருத்தகு திரைக டாக்கச் சேப்புழிச் சேவ னீங்கப்","பரற்றலை முரம்பிற் சின்னீர் வறுஞ்சுனைப் பற்று விட்ட","வரத்தவாய்ப் பவளச் செந்தாட் பெடையன்ன மழுவ தொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She is like a coral-footed hen-swan whose mate has left the lotus bed of the Ganges, its banks hidden by waves, and who weeps by a dry pool of little water on stony gravel.","n":"Repaired from eight half-lines in the source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1385","lb":"CC1385","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1386":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1386,"t":["மெழுகினாற் புனைந்த பாவை வெய்துறுத் தாங்கு மோவா","தழுதுநைந் துருகு கின்ற வாயிடைத் தோழி துன்னிக்","கெழீஇயினாள் கேள்வி நல்யாழ்க் கிளைநரம் பனைய சொல்லாள்","கழிபெருங் கவலை நீங்கக் காரண நீர சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"As she weeps and melts ceaselessly like a wax doll held to heat, her friend, whose speech is like the strings of a well-tuned yazh, comes close and speaks reasonable words to end her great grief.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1386","lb":"CC1386","ev":"Padumai's friend comes to console her.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1387":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1387,"t":["தெள்ளறல் யாறு பாய்ந்த திரைதவழ் கடலின் வெஃகி","யள்ளுற வளிந்த காம மகமுறப் பிணித்த தேனு","முள்ளுற வெந்த செம்பொ னுற்றநீர்ப் புள்ளி யற்றாற்","கள்ளுற மலர்ந்த கோதாய் காதலர் காத லென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells her that lovers' love is like the sea's craving for the clear river that flows into it, like honey that binds desire within, and like a drop of water fallen on gold heated through.","n":"The point of the three comparisons is not fixed by this verse alone: they may say that a lover's love never has enough and so he will return, or that it vanishes as fast as water on hot gold; the next verses decide which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1387","lb":"CC1387","ev":"The friend begins her consolation with sayings on lovers' love.","cl":[["a lover's love is insatiable and is drawn in at once, like a drop on hot gold","general","reported",4,"padumai_thozhi"]],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:59"},"cc:1388":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1388,"t":["ஓடரி யொழுகி நீண்ட வொளிமலர் நெடுங்க ணாரைக்","கூடரி யுழுவை போல முயக்கிடைக் குழையப் புல்லி","யாடவ ரழுந்தி வீழ்ந்தும் பிரிவிடை யழுங்கல் செல்லார்","பீடழிந் துருகும் பெண்ணிற் பேதைய ரில்லை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The friend goes on: men who clasp long-eyed women in close embrace, sinking into them like a striped tiger, still do not grieve when they part, and there are none so foolish as women who melt and lose their dignity over them.","n":"The friend's consolation begins by belittling men's love (continuing 1387) before it turns, in 1389, to the nature of birth itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pedhaimai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1388","lb":"CC1388","ev":"","cl":[["men do not grieve at parting, and women who waste away over them are foolish","general","reported",4,"padumai_thozhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1389":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1389,"t":["பேதைமை யென்னும் வித்திற் பிறந்துபின் வினைக ளென்னும்","வேதனை மரங்க ணாறி வேட்கைவேர் வீழ்த்து முற்றிக்","காதலுங் களிப்பு மென்னுங் கவடுவிட் டவலம் பூத்து","மாதுய ரிடும்பை காய்த்து மரணமே கனிந்து நிற்கும்."],"p":"","r":"She sets out a tree of suffering: born from the seed of ignorance, it sprouts deeds as its trunks of pain, sends down desire as its root, branches into love and elation, flowers into sorrow, bears great affliction as fruit and ripens into death.","n":"The verse names no school. Its order (ignorance, then deeds, then feeling and craving, ending in death) resembles the chain of dependent arising taught in Manimekalai 30, but it is cut down to one image and put in a friend's mouth to dry a lover's tears; love (காதல்) itself is one of the branches.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","vinai","idumbai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1389","lb":"CC1389","ev":"","cl":[["from ignorance come deeds, from deeds pain and desire, and from these love, sorrow, affliction and at last death","general","reported",1,"padumai_thozhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1390":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1390,"t":["தேன்சென்ற நெறியுந் தெண்ணீர்ச் சிறுதிரைப் போர்வை போர்த்து","மீன்சென்ற நெறியும் போல விழித்திமைப் பவர்க்குந் தோன்றா","மான்சென்ற நோக்கின் மாதே மாய்ந்துபோ மக்கள் யாக்கை","யூன்சென்று தேயச் சிந்தித் துகுவதோ தகுவ தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the path a bee has flown or a fish has swum under the rippled water leaves no trace even to those who watch unblinking, the human body passes away unseen, so she asks the doe-eyed princess whether it is fitting to waste her flesh in brooding.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1390","lb":"CC1390","ev":"","cl":[["the human body perishes without leaving a trace","general","reported",3,"padumai_thozhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1391":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1391,"t":["பிரிந்தவற் கிரங்கிப் பேதுற் றழுதநங் கண்ணி னீர்கள்","சொரிந்தவை தொகுந்து நோக்கிற் றொடுகடல் வெள்ள மாற்றா","முரிந்தநம் பிறவி மேனாண் முற்றிழை யின்னு நோக்காய்","பரிந்தழு வதற்குப் பாவா யடியிட்ட வாறு கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"If the tears we have wept, pining for those who left us in our former births, were gathered together, the sea would not hold them; look, she says, at how this weeping has only now taken its first step.","n":"Line 4 (பரிந்தழு வதற்குப் பாவா யடியிட்ட வாறு கண்டாய்) is read as 'see how this weeping has set its first foot'; the sense of the half-line is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1391","lb":"CC1391","ev":"","cl":[["in the long round of births one has already shed more tears for parted kin than the sea holds","general","reported",2,"padumai_thozhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1392":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1392,"t":["அன்பினி னவலித் தாற்றா தழுவது மெளிது நங்க","ளென்பினி னாவி நீங்க விறுவது மெளிது சேர்ந்த","துன்பத்தாற் றுகைக்கப் பட்டார் துகைத்தவத் துன்பந் தாங்கி","யின்பமென் றிருத்தல் போலு மரியதிவ் வுலகி லென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"To weep in love and be unable to bear it is easy, and easy too to let the life leave one's bones; what is hard in this world is to be crushed by sorrow and yet carry that sorrow and live as if it were joy.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","idumbai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1392","lb":"CC1392","ev":"","cl":[["bearing sorrow as if it were joy is harder than weeping or dying","general","reported",4,"padumai_thozhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1393":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1393,"t":["மயற்கையிம் மக்கள் யோனிப் பிறத்தலும் பிறந்து வந்தீங்","கியற்கையே பிரிவு சாத லிமைப்பிடைப் படாத தொன்றாற்","கயற்கணி னளவுங் கொள்ளார் கவற்சியுட் கவற்சி கொண்டார்","செயற்கையம் பிறவி நச்சுக் கடலகத் தழுந்து கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Born by delusion into the human womb, parting and death are by nature what comes here in less than a blink; those who will not grant it even the span of a fish-eyed glance take worry on worry and sink in the poison sea of births of their own making.","n":"The play of மயற்கை, இயற்கை and செயற்கை (delusion, nature, what is made) carries the argument: birth comes from delusion, parting is its nature, and the drowning is self-made; the division of line 3 is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","pirivu","saakkaadu","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1393","lb":"CC1393","ev":"","cl":[["parting and death belong to human birth by its very nature","general","reported",2,"padumai_thozhi"],["those who grieve over what is natural sink deeper into the sea of births","general","reported",4,"padumai_thozhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1394":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1394,"t":["இளமையின் மூப்புஞ் செல்வத் திடும்பையும் புணர்ச்சிப் போழ்திற்","கிளைநரிற் பிரிவு நோயில் காலத்து நோயு நோக்கி","விளைமதுக் கமழுங் கோதை வேலினும் வெய்ய கண்ணாய்","களைதுய ரவலம் வேண்டா கண்ணிமைப் பளவு மென்றாள்"],"p":"","r":"Seeing old age within youth, affliction within wealth, parting from kin within union and sickness within health, she tells the princess with eyes fiercer than a spear to put away grief even for the blink of an eye.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilamai","selvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1394","lb":"CC1394","ev":"","cl":[["every good state carries its opposite within it: youth age, wealth affliction, union parting, health disease","general","reported",1,"padumai_thozhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1395":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1395,"t":["முத்திலங் காகந் தோய்ந்த மொய்ம்மலர்த் தாரி னானங்","கைத்தலத் தகன்ற பந்திற் கைப்படுங் கவல வேண்டா","பொத்திலத் துறையு மாந்தை புணர்ந்திருந் துரைக்கும் பொன்னே","நித்தில முறுவ லுண்டா னீங்கினா னல்லன் கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Do not grieve, she says: he will come back to your hand like a ball that has left it, for the owl that lives in the hollow calls in pairs, and the one who tasted your pearl smile has not left you.","n":"The consolation turns from doctrine to omen: the owl's paired call is read as a sign of reunion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","pirivu","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1395","lb":"CC1395","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1396":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1396,"t":["வடிமலர்க் காவி னன்று வண்டளிர்ப் பிண்டி நீழன்","முடிபொருள் பறவை கூற முற்றிழை நின்னை நோக்கிக்","கடியதோர் கௌவை செய்யுங் கட்டெயிற் றரவி னென்றேன்","கொடியனாய் பிழைப்புக் கூறேன் குழையலென் றெடுத்துக் கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"She recalls that in the grove, under the shade of the asoka, when a bird spoke of what was to come, she looked at the princess and foretold a cruel trouble from a fanged snake; she, called cruel then, does not speak falsely now, and telling her not to droop she lifts her up.","n":"கொடியனாய் பிழைப்புக் கூறேன் is read as the friend's 'I, whom you called cruel, do not speak amiss'; the earlier warning she cites is not traced here to a particular verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","vaaimai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1396","lb":"CC1396","ev":"The friend reminds Padumai that her earlier omen of the snake came true and lifts her up.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1397":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1397,"t":["அலங்கலுங் குழலுந் தோழி யங்கையி னடைச்சி யம்பூம்","பொலங்கலக் கொடிய னாடன் கண்பொழி கலுழி யொற்றிக்","கலந்தகி னாறு மல்குற் கவான்மிசைக் கொண்டி ருந்தாள்","புலர்ந்தது பொழுது நல்லா ணெஞ்சமும் புலர்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The friend gathers the princess's garland and hair in her hand, wipes the tears pouring from her eyes and holds her on her lap; the night ends, and with it the good woman's heart too grows dry.","n":"புலர்ந்தது plays on dawn breaking and the heart drying of its tears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","natpu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1397","lb":"CC1397","ev":"The friend dries Padumai's tears and holds her in her lap until dawn.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1398":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1398,"t":["பண்கனிந் தினிய பாடற் படுநரம் பிளகி யாங்குக்","கண்கனிந் தினிய காமச் செவ்வியுட் காளை நீங்கத்","தெண்பனி யனைய கண்ணீர்ச் சேயிழை தாய ரெல்லாந்","தண்பனி முருக்கப் பட்ட தாமரைக் காடு போன்றார்."],"p":"","r":"As the string of a ripe, sweet song snaps in the middle, so the young man left her in the full ripeness of love, and the princess's mothers, their tears like clear dew, became like a lotus grove withered by frost.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","sutram","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1398","lb":"CC1398","ev":"Padumai's mothers learn that Civakan has gone and are stricken.","cl":[],"ag":["தாயர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1399":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1399,"t":["சில்லரிச் சிலம்பு சூழ்ந்த சீறடித் திருவி னற்றாய்","முல்லையங் குழலி னாய்நின் முலைமுதற் கொழுநன் மேனாட்","சொல்லியு மறிவ துண்டோ வெனக்குழைந் துருகி நைந்து","மெல்லியல் கங்குல் சொல்லிற் றிற்றென மிழற்று கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her own mother, melting and worn, asks the jasmine-haired girl whether her husband ever said anything before, and the gentle girl murmurs what he said in the night.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","sol","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1399","lb":"CC1399","ev":"Padumai's mother asks whether Civakan said anything beforehand, and Padumai repeats his night words.","cl":[],"ag":["கோப்பெருந் தேவி","பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1400":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1400,"t":["வினைக்குஞ் செய்பொருட் கும்வெயில் வெஞ்சுர","நினைத்து நீங்குத லாண்கட னீங்கினாற்","கனைத்து வண்டுணுங் கோதையர் தங்கடன்","மனைக்கண் வைகுதன் மாண்பொ டெனச்சொனான்."],"p":"","r":"She reports that he said it is a man's duty to think of the hot wasteland and leave for deeds and for wealth, and a woman's duty, if he goes, to stay at home with dignity.","n":"Civakan's words, given in his voice through Padumai's report, are the akam rule for the man's leaving; but he has not gone for work or wealth, and in hindsight the rule was a hint covering a secret departure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ilvaazhkkai","selvam","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1400","lb":"CC1400","ev":"","cl":[["going away for deeds and wealth is a man's duty, and staying at home with dignity a woman's","general","reported",2,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","கோப்பெருந் தேவி"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1401":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1401,"t":["விரைசெய் தாமரை மேல்விளை யாடிய","வரைச வன்ன மமர்ந்துள வாயினு","நிரைசெய் நீல நினைப்பில வென்றனன்","வரைசெய் கோல மணங்கமழ் மார்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The broad-chested man, she says, also told her that though the royal swan that played on the scented lotus may rest elsewhere, it has no thought for the massed blue lilies.","n":"A second veiled saying: away from the lotus, the swan will not turn to other flowers, a promise of faithfulness disguised as a remark about birds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1401","lb":"CC1401","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","கோப்பெருந் தேவி"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1402":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1402,"t":["பொன்வி ளைத்த புணர்முலை யாள்சொல","வின்ன ளிக்குரல் கேட்ட வசுணமா","வன்ன ளாய்மகிழ் வெய்துவித் தாளரோ","மின்வி ளைத்தன மேகலை யல்குலாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the girl of the gold-bearing breasts told this, she made her mother glad, as the asunam deer is gladdened on hearing the sweet voice of the lute.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","inbam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1402","lb":"CC1402","ev":"Padumai's report of his words gladdens her mother.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1403":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1403,"t":["அன்னந் தானவன் றாமரைப் போதுநீ","நின்னை நீங்கின னீங்கலன் காதலா","னின்ன தாலவன் கூறிற் றெனச்சொனாண்","மன்ன னாருயிர் மாபெருந் தேவியே."],"p":"","r":"The king's great queen reads the riddle: he is the swan and you the lotus; he has left you but has not left you in love, and that is what he told you.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1403","lb":"CC1403","ev":"Padumai's mother interprets Civakan's saying as a promise to return.","cl":[],"ag":["கோப்பெருந் தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோப்பெருந் தேவி","பதுமை"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1404":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1404,"t":["சொரிபனி முருக்க நைந்து சுடர்முகம் பெற்ற போதே","பரிவுறு நலத்த வன்றே பங்கய மன்ன தேபோல்","வரிவளைத் தோளி கேள்வன் வருமென வலித்த சொல்லாற்","றிருநலம் பிறந்து சொன்னா டேனினு மினிய சொல்லாள்."],"p":"","r":"As a lotus bruised by falling frost regains its beauty when it meets the radiant sun, so the bangled girl, at the firm word that her husband will come, regained her beauty, and she spoke with words sweeter than honey.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","pirivu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1404","lb":"CC1404","ev":"Padumai revives at the assurance that Civakan will return and speaks.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1405":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1405,"t":["நஞ்சினை யமுத மென்று நக்கினு மமுத மாகா","தஞ்சிறைக் கலாப மஞ்ஞை யணங்கர வட்ட தேனு","மஞ்சிறைக் கலுழ னாகு மாட்சியொன் றானு மின்றே","வஞ்சனுக் கினைய நீரேன் வாடுவ தென்னை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Poison does not become nectar because one licks it calling it nectar, and a peacock does not become Garuda though it kills a fearful snake; why then, she asks, should I, being such, wither for a deceiver?","n":"The two proverbs can be read against him (he only seemed a Garuda when he cured her snakebite) or against her own grief (calling it sweet does not make it so); the turn from revived beauty in 1404 to 'deceiver' here is abrupt, and the reading is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","pirivu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1405","lb":"CC1405","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமை","கோப்பெருந் தேவி"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1406":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1406,"t":["பொய்கையுட் கமலத் தங்கட் புள்ளெனு முரச மார்ப்ப","வெய்யவன் கதிர்க ளென்னும் விளங்கொளித் தடக்கை நீட்டி","மையிருட் போர்வை நீக்கி மண்ணக மடந்தை கோலம்","பையவே பரந்து நோக்கிப் பனிவரை நெற்றி சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Dawn: the birds in the lotus pools sound like drums, the sun stretches out his rays as bright long hands, draws off the cover of black night, looks slowly over the adorned earth-woman and reaches the brow of the snowy mountain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1406","lb":"CC1406","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1407":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1407,"t":["செவ்வழி யாழி னூறுந் தீஞ்சொலாட் குற்ற தெல்லா","மவ்வழி யரசற் குய்த்தார்க் கரசனு மவல மெய்தி","யெவ்வழி யானு நாடி யிமைப்பின தெல்லை யுள்ளே","யிவ்வழித் தம்மினென்றா னிவுளித்தேர்த் தானை யானே."],"p":"","r":"All that had befallen the girl of lute-sweet speech was carried to the king, and the king of horsed chariots, grieved, ordered them to search by every road and bring the man back within the blink of an eye.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pirivu","otru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1407","lb":"CC1407","ev":"The king hears of Padumai's grief and sends men to search for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்து"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1408":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1408,"t":["மின்னுளே பிறந்ததோர் மின்னின் மேதகத்","தன்னுளே பிறந்ததோர் வடிவு தாங்குபு","முன்னினான் வடதிசை முகஞ்செய் தென்பவே","பொன்னுளே பிறந்தபொன் னனைய பொற்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"Taking on a form born from within himself, as a lightning flash born inside lightning, the one bright as gold born from gold set his face to the north.","n":"தன்னுளே பிறந்ததோர் வடிவு is read as a changed shape he assumes, a disguise; how he takes it is not said here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1408","lb":"CC1408","ev":"Civakan takes on another form and heads north.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1409":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1409,"t":["வீக்கினான் பைங்கழ னரல வெண்டுகி","லாக்கினா னிருதுணி யணிந்த பல்கல","னீக்கினா னொருமகற் கருளி நீணெறி","யூக்கினா னுவவுறு மதியி னொண்மையான்."],"p":"","r":"He bound on his anklets, made his white cloth into two pieces, took off the many ornaments he wore and gave them to a man, and set out on the long road, bright as the full moon.","n":"The putting off of ornaments and the plain white cloth look forward to renunciation, though here they are only a traveller's disguise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1409","lb":"CC1409","ev":"Civakan puts off his ornaments, gives them away, dresses in two white cloths and sets out on the long road.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1410":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1410,"t":["வேந்தனால் விடுக்கப் பட்டார் விடலையைக் கண்டு சொன்னா","ரேந்தலே பெரிது மொக்கு மிளமையும் வடிவு மிஃதே","போந்ததும் போய கங்குல் போம்வழிக் கண்ட துண்டேல்","யாந்தலைப் படுது மைய வறியினீங் குரைக்க வென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The men the king sent meet the young man and say: you closely resemble our lord, in youth and in form this is he; if on the way you saw anyone go by in the night just past, tell us, sir, and we will catch up with him.","n":"The irony is the scene's point: they tell the man himself that he looks like the one they seek.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","otru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1410","lb":"CC1410","ev":"The king's searchers meet the disguised Civakan and ask him about the man they seek.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தனால் விடுக்கப் பட்டார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வேந்தனால் விடுக்கப் பட்டார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1411":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1411,"t":["நெய்கனிந் திருண்ட வைம்பா னெடுங்கணாள் காத லானை","யையிரு திங்க ளெல்லை யகப்படக் காண்பி ரிப்பாற்","பொய்யுரை யன்று காணீர் போமினம் போகி நுங்கண்","மையலங் களிற்று வேந்தன் மைந்தனுக் குரைமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He answers that within the limit of ten months they will see the beloved of the long-eyed girl, that this is no lie, and bids them go and tell it to the son of the king of the elephants in rut.","n":"மைந்தனுக்கு (to the son) is unexpected, since the king himself sent them; it may name the king by his lineage or a prince of the house.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","pirivu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1411","lb":"CC1411","ev":"Civakan, unrecognized, sends the searchers back with word that Padumai's husband will return within ten months.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","வேந்தனால் விடுக்கப் பட்டார்"],"ch":"cc:60"},"cc:1412":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1412,"t":["வானின் வழங்கும் வண்கை மணிசெய் யார மார்பிற்","றேனும் வழங்கும் பைந்தார் விசையை சிறுவன்றேங்கொ","ணானம் வழங்குங் கோதை நைய வெய்ய வாய","கானம் வழங்கன் மேவிக் காலின் னேகி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai's son, whose generous hand gives like the rain cloud and whose fresh garland drips honey, leaves the woman of musk-scented hair to pine and takes on foot to the harsh forest.","n":"The verb வழங்கும் returns in each line (the hand that gives, the garland that sheds honey, the hair that sheds musk, the forest he walks), so the line-by-line play binds his generosity, his finery and her grief to the walk into the wilds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1412","lb":"CC1412","ev":"Civakan sets out alone on foot into the forest, leaving Padumai grieving.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1413":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1413,"t":["சிலைகொ ணாணிற் றீராத் திருந்து கற்பின் னவர்த","மிலைகொள் பூந்தா ருழுத வின்ப வருத்த நீங்க","முலைகொள் கண்கள் கண்ணின் னெழுதி முள்கு மொய்ம்பன்","மலைகொள் கானம் முன்னி மகிழ்வோ டேகு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The strong hero, the weariness of love with women whose chastity holds like a string on a bow now passing, heads gladly for the mountain forest.","n":"Line 3 (முலைகொள் கண்கள் கண்ணின் எழுதி முள்கும்) is hard to construe; it seems to recall his love-play with the wives, but the exact sense is not fixed. The bowstring simile praises the wives' karpu as something that never leaves them, even as he leaves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1413","lb":"CC1413","ev":"Civakan, refreshed from the fatigue of love, presses on toward the hill forest.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1414":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1414,"t":["கனிகொள் வாழைக் காட்டுட் கருமை மெழுகி யவைபோன்","றினிய வல்லா முகத்த முசுவுங் குரங்கு மிரியத்","துனிவு தீர நோக்கித் தோன்றல் செல்லு முன்னாற்","பனிவெண் டிரைசூழ் கடல்போற் பழுவந் தோன்றிற் றவணே."],"p":"","r":"In a forest of fruiting plantains, as langurs and monkeys with faces like smeared black wax scatter, the prince looks about to ease his sorrow, and ahead of him a thicket appears like a sea ringed with cold white waves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1414","lb":"CC1414","ev":"Civakan walks on through the plantain forest and sees a grove ahead.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1415":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1415,"t":["பருகு வாரிற் புல்லிப் பயங்கண் மாறத் துறக்கு","முருகு விம்மு குழலார் போல மொய்கொ டும்பி","யுருவப் பூங்கொம் பொசியப் புல்லித் தீந்தேன் பருகி","யருகு வாய்விட் டார்ப்ப வண்ணன் மெல்லச் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Like women of fragrant hair who embrace their drinkers and cast them off when the gain runs out, the swarming bees cling to a flowering branch until it bends, drink its honey and drone nearby, while the noble one walks slowly on.","n":"The landscape is read through a moral simile: the bees are the courtesans who leave a man once his wealth is gone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1415","lb":"CC1415","ev":"Civakan walks slowly through the flowering grove.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1416":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1416,"t":["செல்வர் மனத்தி னோங்கித் திருவின் மாந்தர் நெஞ்சி","னெல்லை யிருளிற் றாகிப் பூந்தா தினிதி னொழுகிக்","கொல்லும் மரவின் மயங்கிச் சிறியார் கொண்ட தொடர்பிற்","செல்லச் செல்ல வஃகு நெறிசேர் சிலம்பு சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He reaches the mountain by a path that rises like the minds of the rich, is dark as the hearts of the wealthy, sheds pollen sweetly, winds like a deadly snake, and narrows the further one goes, like friendship with the small.","n":"All four similes are moral: the pride and inner darkness of the rich and the dwindling friendship of petty men. The last is the didactic books' image of a bond that shrinks over time, set to a path.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","natpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1416","lb":"CC1416","ev":"Civakan climbs the narrowing path to the mountain.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1417":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1417,"t":["சுனைகள் கண்க ளாகச் சூழ்ந்த குவளை விழியா","வனைய லாகா வுருவ நோக்கி மைந்தற் கிரங்கி","இனைவ போலும் வரையின் னருவி யினிதி னாடி","நனைகொள் போது வேய்ந்து நாதற் பாடு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The mountain, its pools as eyes and its kuvalai flowers as eyes, seems to pity the youth's matchless form and weep with its falls; he bathes gladly in the cascade, crowns himself with buds and sings to the Lord.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1417","lb":"CC1417","ev":"Civakan bathes in a mountain waterfall, garlands himself and begins a hymn to the Jina.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1418":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1418,"t":["செய்தா னிருவினையின் பயத்தைச்சேருஞ் சென்றென்றி","யெய்தா னதன்பயத்தைப் பிறனேதுய்த்த லியல்பென்றி","கொய்தாமந் தாழ்ந்தொசிந்த குளிர்பூம்பிண்டிக் கோமானே","யிஃதேநின்சொ லியல்பென்றா லடியேனின்னைத் தொழுதேனே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan praises the Lord of the cool flowering asoka for teaching that the fruit of the two kinds of deed travels to the one who did them and that no other receives it, and worships him if this is his word.","n":"The refrain பூம்பிண்டிக் கோமான் names the Jina by the asoka (பிண்டி) under which he sits. The hymn's conditional form ('if this is the nature of your word') makes each doctrine the ground of worship, and the doctrine of the first verse is the one Civakan will use against the ascetics: no rite done by the body can carry off a deed's fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1418","lb":"CC1418","ev":"Civakan sings the first verse of a hymn to the Jina of the asoka tree.","cl":[["the fruit of good and evil deeds reaches the one who did them, and no other receives it","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1419":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1419,"t":["உண்டே தனதியல்பி னுணருங்காலை யுயிரென்றி","யுண்டாய வ்வவுயிரே பிறிதினில்லை யெனவுரைத்தி","வண்டார்த்து நாற்காதம் வண்ணமாலை சுமந்தொசிந்து","கொண்டேந்து பூம்பிண்டிக் கோமானின்னைத் தொழுதேனே."],"p":"","r":"He praises the Lord of the asoka, which spreads four kaathams bearing garlands loud with bees, for declaring that the soul exists, known by its own nature when one inquires, and that the soul is not from anything other than itself.","n":"Line 2 is garbled in the text (உண்டாய வ்வவுயிரே); read as உண்டாய அவ்வுயிரே, it says the soul is not produced from another thing, which sets the hymn against those who make the soul a product of the elements.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1419","lb":"CC1419","ev":"","cl":[["the soul exists and is known by its own nature","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"],["the soul does not arise from any other thing","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1420":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1420,"t":["காதலா லெண்வினையுங் கழிபவென்றி யக்காத","லாதலா லெண்வினையுங் கழியாவென்று மறைதியாற்","போதுலாய்த் தேன்றுளித்துப் பொழிந்துவண்டு திவண்டுலாங்","கோதைதாழ் பூம்பிண்டிக் கோமானின்னைத் தொழுதேனே."],"p":"","r":"He praises the Lord of the asoka, over which honey drips and bees roam among the hanging garlands, for teaching both that by love the eight karmas pass and that, because it is still love, the eight karmas do not pass.","n":"The verse is a deliberate paradox, and its sense turns on காதல், which may be love of the Jina or of the path, and on மறைதி, 'you declared' or 'you hid'. Read either way, even devotion is counted as a bond: the eight karmas (எண்வினை) are the Jain reckoning of the kinds of karma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pattru","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1420","lb":"CC1420","ev":"","cl":[["love directed to the right end helps wear away the eight karmas, yet as attachment it cannot itself end them","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1421":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1421,"t":["இனிதி னிங்ஙன மேத்தி வலங்கொண்டு","முனிவர் சித்திர கூடமு னாதெனத்","தனிதி னேகுபு தாபதர் வாழ்வதோர்","பனிகொள் பூம்பொழிற் பள்ளிகண் டானரோ."],"p":"","r":"Having praised thus sweetly and walked round in reverence, he goes on alone and comes upon a cool flowering grove where forest ascetics live, a sages' retreat like Chitrakuta.","n":"முனிவர் சித்திர கூடம் முன்னாது may compare the grove to Chitrakuta, Rama's forest of sages, or say that he went on not thinking of it; the comparison is taken here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1421","lb":"CC1421","ev":"Civakan circumambulates the shrine and reaches the ascetics' hermitage.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1422":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1422,"t":["புல்லு மல்லியும் போகுயர் நீள்கழை","நெல்லு நீர்விளை கேழலுந் தோரையு","மல்ல தீம்பழங் காய்கிழங் காதியா","நல்ல வேநுகர் வார்பள்ளி நண்ணினான்."],"p":"","r":"He reaches the dwelling of those who eat only clean food: grass, lotus stamens, rice of the tall bamboo, wild grains grown in water, sweet fruits, green fruits and roots.","n":"The list of foods is set out with care because Civakan will argue from it (1429, 1430): if such diet burned deeds, the beasts of the forest would be freed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1422","lb":"CC1422","ev":"Civakan enters the hermitage of the forest ascetics.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1423":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1423,"t":["அரிய கொள்கைய ராரழ லைந்தினுண்","மருவி வீடு வளைக்குறு மாட்சியர்","விரிய வேதம் விளம்பிய நாவினர்","தெரிவி றீத்தொழிற் சிந்தையின் மேயினார்."],"p":"","r":"They are men of hard vows who stand amid the five fires to win release, whose tongues have recited the Veda at length, and whose minds are set on the fire rite without discernment.","n":"The five fires (ஆரழல் ஐந்து) are the four fires round the ascetic and the sun above. தெரிவில் may be the narrator's judgment, 'without discernment', or mean only 'chosen'; if the first, the Jain poet has already weighed them before the debate.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aaguthi","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1423","lb":"CC1423","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1424":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1424,"t":["வள்ளி யின்னமு தும்வரை வாழையின்","றெள்ளு தீங்கனி யுஞ்சில தந்தபின்","வெள்ள மாரிய னாய்விருந் தார்கென","வுள்ள மாட்சியி னாருவந் தோம்பினார்."],"p":"","r":"Those of generous heart bring him sweet nectar of the valli root and clear sweet fruit of the mountain plantain and, bidding the noble guest feast, tend him gladly.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1424","lb":"CC1424","ev":"The ascetics feed Civakan with roots and fruit as their guest.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1425":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1425,"t":["பாங்கின் மாதவர் பான்மதி போன்றிவன்","வீங்கு கல்வியன் மெய்ப்பொருட் கேள்விய","னாங்கு நாமு மளக்குவ மென்றுதம்","மோங்கு கட்டுரை யொன்றிரண் டோதினார்."],"p":"","r":"The great ascetics beside him, judging that this youth like the white moon is deep in learning and has heard the true doctrine, resolve to measure him too and recite one or two of their lofty discourses.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","vaadham","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1425","lb":"CC1425","ev":"The ascetics test Civakan with a few discourses of their own.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1426":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1426,"t":["ஐயர் கூறலு மண்ணலுங் கூறுவான்","சையம் பூண்டு சமுத்திர நீந்துவா","னுய்யு மேற்றொடர்ப் பாட்டினிங் கியாவையு","மெய்தி னார்களு முய்பவென் றோதினான்."],"p":"","r":"When the elders had spoken, the noble one answered that if a man who swims the sea with a mountain strapped to him could be saved, then all who are bound here by attachment would be saved as well.","n":"தொடர்ப்பாடு (bond, attachment) is taken as the mountain on the swimmer's back; the image prepares the charge (1432 to 1434) that these ascetics have carried their households into the forest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","vaadham","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1426","lb":"CC1426","ev":"Civakan begins his reply to the ascetics with the image of a man swimming the sea under a mountain.","cl":[["one who keeps his attachments cannot cross the sea of births","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மாதவர்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1427":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1427,"t":["வீடு வேண்டி விழுச்சடை நீட்டன்மெய்ம்","மூடு கூறையின் மூடுதல் வெண்டலை","யோடுகோட லுடுத்தலென் றின்னவை","பீடி லாப்பிற விக்குவித் தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Growing great matted hair in search of release, wrapping the body in cloth, carrying a white skull as a bowl and wearing bark: these, he says, are said to be seeds of a mean birth.","n":"என்பவே ('so it is said') rests the judgment on authority rather than argument; the argument comes in 1429 to 1434. The verse does not say the marks are merely useless but that they seed a lowly birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","piravi","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1427","lb":"CC1427","ev":"","cl":[["the ascetic's marks of matted hair, garb and skull bowl do not bring release but sow further birth","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மாதவர்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1428":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1428,"t":["ஏம நன்னெறி யெந்நெறி யன்னெறி","தூய்மை யின்னெறி யாமுந்து ணிகுவங்","காமன் றாதை நெறியின்கட் காளைநீ","தீமை யுண்டெனிற் செப்பெனச் செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"The ascetic replies: which is the safe good path? We too are resolved on that path of purity; young bull, if there is any evil in the way of Kama's father, tell it.","n":"காமன் தாதை, Kama's father, is Vishnu, so the ascetics name their path as his, though their practices (the five fires, the Veda, the fire rite of 1423) are those of Vedic forest ascetics. The claim is refuted in Civakan's answer, 1429 to 1436, and the ascetics' conversion in 1437.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1428","lb":"CC1428","ev":"An ascetic challenges Civakan to show any fault in their Vaishnava way.","cl":[["the way of Kama's father that we follow is the safe and pure path","vaishnava","refuted",3,"tapasar"]],"ag":["மாதவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1429":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1429,"t":["தூங்குறிக் கிடந்து காயும் பழங்களுந் துய்ப்ப நில்லா","பாங்கலா வினைக ளென்றார் பகவனா ரெங்கட் கென்னி","னோங்குநீண் மரத்திற் றூங்கு மொண்சிறை யொடுங்கல் வாவல்","பாங்கரிற் பழங்க டுய்ப்பப் பழவினை பரியு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"If, he says, the Lord told you that unfit deeds do not stay with those who eat the fruits that hang ripening from the trees, then the bat with its folded wings, hanging from tall trees and eating the fruit nearby, would shed its old deeds.","n":"Each of the next verses has the same form: the ascetics' practice is stated as a conditional and answered with a forest creature that already lives that way. பகவனார் (the Lord) is the ascetics' god as Civakan quotes them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1429","lb":"CC1429","ev":"","cl":[["living on hanging fruit removes evil deeds, as the Lord taught","vaishnava","refuted",2,"tapasar"],["a diet of fruit does not destroy old deeds, or the bat would be freed","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மாதவர்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1430":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1430,"t":["அல்லியும் புல்லு முண்டாங் காரழ லைந்து ணின்று","சொல்லிய வகையி னோற்பத் துணியும்வெவ் வினைக ளென்னிற்","கல்லுண்டு கடிய வெம்புங் கானுறை புறவ மெல்லாம்","புல்லிய வினையை வென்று புறக்கொடை காணு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"If eating lotus stamens and grass and standing amid the five fires in the prescribed way cuts off cruel deeds, then all the forest doves, which eat grit and burn in the heat, would conquer their binding deeds and put them to flight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","vaadham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1430","lb":"CC1430","ev":"","cl":[["eating grass and standing amid the five fires cuts off cruel deeds","vaishnava","refuted",2,"tapasar"],["bodily mortification and diet do not conquer deeds, or the dove would be freed","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மாதவர்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1431":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1431,"t":["நீட்டிய சடைய மாகி நீர்மூழ்கி நிலத்திற் சேர்ந்து","வாட்டிய வுடம்பின் யாங்கள் வரகதி விளைத்து மென்னிற்","காட்டிடைக் கரடி போகிக் கயமூழ்கிக் காட்டி னின்று","வீட்டினை விளைக்க வேண்டும் வெளிற்றுரை விடுமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"If, with long matted hair, plunging in water, lying on the ground and a wasted body, you would win the highest state, then the bear that roams the forest, plunges into pools and stands in the wild would have to win release; give up empty words, he says.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","theerththam","vesham","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1431","lb":"CC1431","ev":"","cl":[["matted hair, ritual bathing, sleeping on the ground and wasting the body win the highest state","vaishnava","refuted",2,"tapasar"],["bathing and bodily hardship do not yield release, or the bear would gain it","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மாதவர்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1432":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1432,"t":["கலைவளர் கிளவி யார்தங் காமர்மென் சேக்கை நீங்கி","யிலைவளர் குரம்பை யங்க ணிருநிலஞ் சேக்கை யாக","முலைவள ராகந் தோய முழுவினை முரியு மாயின்","மலைவளர் குறவர்க் கம்மா வினைகளு மாயு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"If leaving the soft beds of women of artful speech for the bare ground of a leaf hut, while still embracing their breasts there, breaks all one's deeds, then, he says, the deeds of the hill kuravar would perish too.","n":"The ascetics are householders in the forest: they have left the palace bed, not the wife. The kuravar, who live just so in leaf huts on the hill, make the point that a change of bed is not a change of life; 1437 has the converts put away their households.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kaamam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1432","lb":"CC1432","ev":"","cl":[["a forest life that keeps its wives is no renunciation and breaks no deeds","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மாதவர்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1433":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1433,"t":["வெண்ணிறத் துகிலி னாங்கண் வீழ்ந்துமா சாகிநின்ற","வொண்ணிற வுதிரந் தன்னை யுதிரத்தா லொழிக்க லாமே","பண்ணிறக் கிளவி யார்தம் பசையினாற் பிறந்த பாவங்","கண்ணிற முலையி னார்தங் கலவியாற் கழிக்க லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Can bright blood that has fallen on white cloth and stained it be washed out with blood? So can the sin born of clinging to sweet-voiced women be removed by union with women?","n":"The image is of washing a stain with the same stuff that made it: the remedy must be other in kind than the fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pattru","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1433","lb":"CC1433","ev":"","cl":[["sin born of desire for women cannot be removed while one keeps lying with women","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மாதவர்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1434":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1434,"t":["நுண்டுகில் வேத லஞ்சி நெருப்பகம் பொதிந்து நோக்கிக்","கொண்டுபோய் மறைய வைத்தாற் கொந்தழல் சுடாது மாமே","கண்டத்தி னாவி யார்தங் கடிமனை துறந்து காட்டுட்","பண்டைச்செய் தொழிலிற் பாவம் பறைக்குற்றாற் பறைக்க லாமே."],"p":"","r":"If one wraps fire in fine cloth for fear of its burning and hides it away, will the fierce flame not burn? So one who leaves the guarded house of women for the forest, but carries the sin of old deeds, cannot wear it away by wishing to.","n":"Lines 2 and 4 are rhetorical questions in -ஆமே, and the second half of line 4 (பறைக்குற்றாற் பறைக்கலாமே) is hard to construe; it is read as 'can one wear it away merely by setting out to?'. The fire hidden in cloth is the old deed carried into the forest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thuravu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1434","lb":"CC1434","ev":"","cl":[["going to the forest does not wear away old sin that one carries within","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மாதவர்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1435":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1435,"t":["நோய்முதிர் குரங்கு போல நுகர்ச்சிநீர் நோக்கல் வேண்டா","காய்முதிர் கனியி னூழ்த்து வீழுமிவ் வியாக்கை யின்னே","வேய்முதிர் வனத்தின் வென்றா னுருவொடு விளங்க நோற்றுப்","போய்முதிர் துறக்கத் தின்பம் பருகுவ புரிமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Do not gaze on pleasure like a sick old monkey, he says; this body will drop at once like a ripe fruit from its stalk; practise austerity in the bamboo forest in the form of the Conqueror and go to drink the joy of the ripe heaven: strive for that.","n":"Civakan does not reject austerity: he keeps it, in the Jina's form (வென்றான் உருவொடு), and promises heaven (துறக்கம்) rather than release, the nearer fruit; release comes only with the three jewels of 1436.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","thavam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1435","lb":"CC1435","ev":"","cl":[["the body falls soon like ripe fruit, so one must not linger in pleasure","general","asserted",2,"civakan"],["austerity done in the Jina's form leads to the joys of heaven","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மாதவர்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1436":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1436,"t":["மெய்வகை தெரிதன் ஞானம் விளங்கிய பொருள்க டம்மைப்","பொய்வகை யின்றித் தேறல் காட்சியைம் பொறியும் வாட்டி","யுய்வகை யுயிரைத் தேயா தொழுகுத லொழுக்க மூன்று","மிவ்வகை நிறைந்த போழ்தே யிருவினை கழியு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Knowledge is discerning things as they truly are; right vision is trusting without falsehood the things so made clear; conduct is withering the five senses and living without wearing down any soul; when all three are full in this way, he says, both kinds of deed pass away.","n":"These are the Jain three jewels, named here ஞானம், காட்சி and ஒழுக்கம். The weight falls on மூன்றும் and நிறைந்த: no one of them, and none in part, is enough. The answer to the ascetics is not less austerity but a different cause of release, in which conduct is defined by non-harm (உயிரைத் தேயாது).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","ozhukkam","kollaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1436","lb":"CC1436","ev":"","cl":[["knowledge, right vision and right conduct together, each made full, end both good and evil deeds","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"],["right conduct is restraint of the five senses and not harming any living being","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மாதவர்"],"ch":"cc:61"},"cc:1437":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1437,"t":["குன்ற னானுரைப்பக் கேட்டே பாகத்தார் குடும்ப நீக்கி","யின்றுகண் விடுக்கப் பட்டேம் யாமென வெழுந்து போகி","வென்றவன் பாதஞ் சேர்ந்து வீட்டுநன் னெறியைப் பெற்றார்","சென்றது பருதி வட்டஞ் செம்மலு மசைவு தீர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing what the hill-shouldered hero said, the ascetics of that side put away their households, saying that today their eyes had been opened, rose and went to the feet of the Conqueror and gained the good path of release; the sun went down and the noble one was rested.","n":"Their words (இன்று கண் விடுக்கப்பட்டேம்) treat the conversion as the opening of eyes, so their earlier austerities are cast as blindness. That they now leave their families (குடும்ப நீக்கி) answers the jibe of 1432 that these ascetics still lay with women in their leaf huts. பாகத்தார் is taken as 'those on that side', the ascetics.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","thuravu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1437","lb":"CC1437","ev":"The forest ascetics, won over by Civakan's teaching, leave their families and take refuge with the Jina; night falls and Civakan rests.","cl":[["one who takes refuge at the feet of the Jina gains the true path to release","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாதவர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1438":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1438,"t":["அசைவு தீர்ந்திரு ளஃகிய காலையே","வசையி னீங்கியி னார்வழி காட்டலிற்","றிசையும் யாறுந் தெரிந்துகொண் டேகினான்","மிசையு மில்லதோர் மெய்ப்பொறி யாக்கையான்."],"p":"","r":"Rested, at dawn when the darkness thinned, he learned the direction and the river from the blameless converts who showed him the way, and went on, he whose body bore the true marks, with none above him.","n":"மெய்ப்பொறி யாக்கையான், the man whose body bears the true marks, anticipates the reading of his bodily marks that begins at 1460; the construal of மிசையும் இல்லது in line 4 is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thisai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1438","lb":"CC1438","ev":"At dawn the converted ascetics show Civakan the road, and he sets out.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மாதவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1439":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1439,"t":["படம்புனைந் தெழுதிய வடிவிற் பங்கயத்","தடம்பல தழீ இயது தக்க நாடது","வடங்கெழு வருமுலை மகளிர் மாமைபோன்","றிடம்பெரி தினிதத னெல்லை யெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"He reached the border of the Takka land, a fitting country full of lotus ponds like a picture drawn on cloth, wide and sweet as the dark-gold skin of women with garlanded breasts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1439","lb":"CC1439","ev":"Civakan reaches the border of the Takka country.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1440":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1440,"t":["தேங்கயத் தணிமலர் தெகிழ்ந்த நாற்றமும்","பூங்குழன் மடந்தையர் புனைந்த சாந்தமு","மாங்கெலா மகிற்புகை யளாய வாசமுந்","தாங்கலா றக்கநா டாய தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The scent loosed from lotuses of the honeyed ponds, the sandal paste worn by flower-haired women and the fragrance of aghil smoke mingling everywhere make up the Takka land, they say.","n":"Line 4 is divided as printed (தாங்கலா றக்கநா டாய தென்பவே); the reading takes it as naming the Takka land through sandhi, but the first word is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1440","lb":"CC1440","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1441":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1441,"t":["சண்பக நறுமலர் மாலை நாறுசாந்","தொண்பழுக் காயினோ டுருவ மெல்லிலை","யுண்பத மியாவர்க்கு மூன மில்லது","வண்புகழ் நாட்டது வண்ண மின்னதே."],"p":"","r":"Garlands of champak flowers, fragrant sandal, ripe areca with tender betel leaf, and food that fails no one: such is the look of that land of generous fame.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1441","lb":"CC1441","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1442":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1442,"t":["கரும்பணி வளவயற் காமர் தாமரை","வரம்பணைந் ததனுதற் கிடந்த வார்செநெ","லரங்கணி நாடக மகளி ராய்நுதற்","சுரும்புசூ ழிலம்பகத் தோற்ற மொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"In the rich sugarcane-bordered fields the long red paddy lying across the lotuses at the bund looks like the forehead ornament of dancing women on the stage, with bees circling it.","n":"The simile turns on இலம்பகம, the frontlet worn on the brow, the same word that names the epic's books.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","uzhavu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1442","lb":"CC1442","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1443":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1443,"t":["வண்டுவாழ் கொடுந்துறைக் கன்னி வாளைமே","னண்டுகி ருற்றென நடுங்கி நாணினால்","விண்டொளித் தூண்டுறந் தொடுக்கும் வீழ்புனல்","கொண்டபூங் கிடங்கணி நகரம் கூறுவாம்."],"p":"","r":"At the curving bee-haunted ghat the young vaalai fish, trembling as though a crab's claw had touched it, hides for shame and leaves its food; the poet turns to tell of the city ringed by a flowered moat fed by falling water.","n":"The fish that flees in shame is a playful prelude to the heroine, whose sign is to be shame at the sight of the man (1451).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1443","lb":"CC1443","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1444":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1444,"t":["அகழ்கிடங் கந்துகி லார்ந்த பாம்புரி","புகழ்தகு மேகலை நாயில் பூண்முலை","திகழ்மணிக் கோபுரந் திங்கள் வாண்முகஞ்","சிகழிகை நெடுங்கொடி செல்விக் கென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The deep moat is the city goddess's silk, the snake-skin-smooth wall her famous girdle, the battlements her ornamented breasts, the gem-bright gate-tower her moon face, and the long flags her braided hair.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1444","lb":"CC1444","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1445":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1445,"t":["நாட்டிய மணிவரை கடைந்து நல்லமிர்","தூட்டினு மதனைவிட் டுறைந ரின்மையா","லீட்டிய வளநிதி யிறைகொண் மாநகர்ச்","சூட்டுவைத் தனையதச் சுடர்ப்பொ னிஞ்சியே."],"p":"","r":"Since none who live there would leave it even if fed the nectar churned from the planted mountain, its shining golden wall is like a crest set upon the great city that holds heaped wealth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1445","lb":"CC1445","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1446":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1446,"t":["எறிசுற விளையவ ரேந்து பூங்கொடி","மறிதிரை வரைபுரை மாட மாக்கலம்","பெறலருந் திருவனா ரமுதம் பேரொலி","யறைகடல் வளநக ராய தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"With its flags held up like leaping sharks, mansions like mountains of rolling waves, great ships, women like the goddess Tiru and nectar, and its great roar, the rich city is a sounding sea, they say.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1446","lb":"CC1446","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1447":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1447,"t":["மதியக டுரிஞ்சுஞ் சென்னி மாடநீண் மறுகு தோறும்","பொதியவிழ் மாலை வீழ்ந்து பொன்செய்நன் கலன்கள் சிந்தி","நிதியறை திறந்து நோக்கி யன்னதோர் நீர்மை யெய்திப்","புதியவர்க் கியங்க லாகாப் பொற்பொடு பொலிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"In every long street of mansions whose tops graze the moon, fallen opening garlands and dropped gold ornaments make it look like an opened treasure room, and it shines with a splendour strangers cannot walk through.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1447","lb":"CC1447","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1448":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1448,"t":["கேமமா புரமெனுங் கேடி னல்லிசைப்","பூமிமேற் றிலகம்வைத் தனைய பொன்னகர்த்","தாமம்நீ ணெடுங்குடைத் தரணி காவல","னாமம்வே னரபதி தேவ னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The golden city named Kemamapuram, of undying fame, is like a tilaka set on the earth, and its king, guardian of the land under a tall garlanded umbrella, is named Narapatidevan.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1448","lb":"CC1448","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1449":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1449,"t":["அந்நகர்க் கரசனே யனைய வாண்டகை","மெய்ந்நிக ரிலாதவன் வேத வாணிகன்","கைந்நிக ரமைந்தவேற் கமழுந் தாரினான்","மைந்நிகர் மழைக்கணார் மருட்ட வைகுவான்."],"p":"","r":"In that city lived a merchant like a king himself, matchless in person, bearing a well-made spear and fragrant garland, who passed his days beguiled by women with dark rain-cool eyes.","n":"வேத வாணிகன் in line 2 is unusual for a merchant; it may be a scribal form, and the reading keeps only 'merchant'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1449","lb":"CC1449","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1450":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1450,"t":["வார்சிலை வடிப்ப வீங்கி வரையெனத் திரண்ட தோளான்","சோர்புய றொலைத்த வண்கைச் சுபத்திரன் மனைவி பெற்ற","சீர்நலம் கடந்த கேம சரியெனத் திசைக ளெல்லாம்","பேர்நலம் பொறித்த பெண்மைப் பெருவிளக் காகி நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Subhadran, of shoulders swelled by the bow and massed like hills, whose giving hand outdid the pouring cloud, had by his wife a daughter Kemasari, beyond all praise, who stood as the great lamp of womanhood whose fame was written in every direction.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1450","lb":"CC1450","ev":"Kemasari is born to the merchant Subhadran and his wife.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1451":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1451,"t":["மாசிலாள் பிறந்த ஞான்றே மதிவலான் விதியி னெண்ணிக்","காசிலாள் கண்ட போழ்தே கதுமென நாணப் பட்டான்","றூசுலா மல்கு லாட்குத் துணைவனாம் புணர்மி னென்று","பேசினா னன்று கொண்டு பெருவிருந் தோம்பு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"On the day the stainless girl was born a man wise in reckoning worked it out by rule and said: the one at whose first sight she is suddenly overcome with shame will be her husband, so join them; and from that day the father keeps up a great feeding of guests.","n":"The sign is the girl's own shame (நாண்), the virtue that normally keeps a maiden from a stranger's gaze; here it is made the test that marks the man. The feasting is the father's way of bringing men before her eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","oozh","naanam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1451","lb":"CC1451","ev":"An astrologer foretells that Kemasari's husband will be the man whose sight makes her feel shame, and her father begins daily feasts for guests.","cl":[],"ag":["மதிவலான்","சுபத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1452":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1452,"t":["தாழ்தரு பைம்பொன் மாலை தடமலர்த் தாம மாலை","வீழ்தரு மணிசெய் மாலை யிவற்றிடை மின்னி னின்று","சூழ்வளைத் தோளி செம்பொற் றூணையே சார்ந்து நோக்கு","மூழ்படு காத லானை யொருபிடி நுசுப்பி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"Amid hanging garlands of gold, of broad flowers and of gems, the girl with bangled arms and waist a hand's span, shining like lightning, leans on a golden pillar and watches for the lover fate has appointed.","n":"ஊழ்படு காதலான் makes the husband a matter of ūḻ, apportioned before she sees him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vaazhkkaithunai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1452","lb":"CC1452","ev":"Kemasari stands by a golden pillar watching the guests for her destined husband.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1453":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1453,"t":["சேயிழை கணவ னாகுந் திருமகன் றிறத்து நாளு","மாயிரத் தெட்டு நேர்ந்த வாரமு தடிசி லூட்டி","யேயின வகையி னாலே யாறிரண் டெல்லை யாண்டு","போயின வென்ப மற்றப் பூங்கொடிச் சாய லாட்கே."],"p":"","r":"For the sake of the fortunate man who will be the bride's husband, a thousand and eight guests were fed each day with nectar-like rice in fitting ways, and so twelve years passed for that flower-creeper of a girl.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","kaalam","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1453","lb":"CC1453","ev":"For twelve years the household feeds a thousand and eight guests a day while Kemasari waits.","cl":[],"ag":["சுபத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1454":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1454,"t":["முருக்கிதழ் குலிக மூட்டி வைத்தன முறுவற் செவ்வாய்த்","திருக்கவி னிறைந்த வெங்கட் பணைமுலைத் தேம்பெய் கோதைப்","புரிக்குழற் பொன்செய் பைம்பூட் புனையிழை கோல நோக்கித்","தரிக்கிலா துருகி நையுந் தடமலர்க் கோதை நற்றாய்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the beauty of the girl, her smiling red lips as if dyed with vermilion over the murukku petal, her full breasts, honeyed garland, curled hair and golden ornaments, her own mother cannot bear it, and melts and wastes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idumbai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1454","lb":"CC1454","ev":"Kemasari's mother pines at seeing her daughter's beauty go unwed.","cl":[],"ag":["நற்றாய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1455":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1455,"t":["மாவடு மருட்டு நோக்கின் மதிமுக மழைக்கண் மாசில்","பூவொடு புரையுஞ் சாயல் புனைநலந் தனித்து வைக","வேவடு பிணையி னோக்கி யிறைவளை கழல நின்ற","தாய்படுந் துயர மெல்லாந் தாரவ னீக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As her daughter's beauty, eyes like the tender mango, moon face and flowerlike grace, stayed alone and unused, the mother looked on like a doe struck by an arrow, her bangles slipping, and the garlanded father took away all her grief.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1455","lb":"CC1455","ev":"Subhadran consoles his grieving wife.","cl":[],"ag":["நற்றாய்","சுபத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1456":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1456,"t":["போதுவாய் திறந்த போதே பூம்பொறி வண்டு சேர்ந்தாங்","கூதுமே மகளிர்க் கொத்த போகமு மன்ன தொன்றே","யாதுநீ கவல வேண்டா வாரழ குடைய நம்பி","காதலான் றவத்தின் மிக்கான் கண்ணுறு நாளை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says that as the spotted bee comes to a bud the moment its mouth opens, so the pleasure fit for women comes in its season; she need not grieve, for a man of full beauty, a lover great in merit, will meet the girl's eyes tomorrow.","n":"தவத்தின் மிக்கான் is read as 'great by the merit of former austerity', the Jain way of naming the good fortune that brings a husband; the 'tomorrow' (நாளை) is borne out at once in 1457.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","kaamam","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1456","lb":"CC1456","ev":"","cl":[["a woman's joy in love comes at its own ripe time, as the bee comes to the opening bud","general","reported",2,"subhadran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுபத்திரன்","நற்றாய்"],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1457":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1457,"t":["பொன்னிலத் தெழுந்ததோர் பொருவில் பூங்கொடி","மின்னுவிட் டெரிவதோர் நலத்தள் வீங்கிருள்","பின்னிவிட் டனகுழற் பெருங்கட் பேதையூர்","துன்னினன் றொடுகழற் குருசி லென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The prince with the warrior's anklet came near the town of the innocent wide-eyed girl, an unmatched flower creeper risen from golden ground, bright as lightning shot out, her hair braided like thick darkness.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1457","lb":"CC1457","ev":"Civakan comes near Kemasari's city.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1458":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1458,"t":["மல்லிகை மணங்கமழ் மாலை வார்குழற்","சில்சுணங் கிளமுலைச் சிறுமி தந்தையுஞ்","செல்வனைத் திருநகர்ச் சேட்பட் டானரோ","பல்கதிர் மணியொளி பரந்த பூணினான்."],"p":"","r":"The father of the young girl with jasmine-scented hair, a man of jewels spreading many-rayed light, came upon the prince at some distance from the fine city.","n":"சேட்பட் டானரோ is taken as சேண் படுதல், being far off; the line may instead mean that the father went out a long way to find him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1458","lb":"CC1458","ev":"Kemasari's father meets Civakan outside the city.","cl":[],"ag":["சுபத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1459":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1459,"t":["தென்றிசை முளைத்ததோர் கோலச் செஞ்சுட","ரொன்றிமற் றுத்தரம் வருவ தொத்தவண்","மன்றல்கொண் மார்பினான் வந்தொ ரானிழ","னன்றுவந் திருந்தன னாதற் சிந்தியா."],"p":"","r":"Like a fine red sun risen in the south and come round to the north, the man of the wedding-fit chest arrived and sat gladly in the shade of a banyan, meditating on the Lord.","n":"The hero on his way to a marriage he does not know of is shown at meditation, not seeking a bride, so the match comes to him as the prophecy's work rather than his pursuit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1459","lb":"CC1459","ev":"Civakan sits under a banyan tree in contemplation of the Jina.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1460":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1460,"t":["குடைகவித் தனையது கோல மாமுடி","யடியிணை யாமையின் வடிவு கொண்டன","புடைதிரள் விலாவும்வில் வளைந்த பொற்பின","கடிகமழ் தாமரை கண்ணின் வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"His fine crowned head was like a spread umbrella, his two feet had the shape of a tortoise's back, his rounded flanks curved with the grace of a bow, and his eyes were the colour of the fragrant lotus.","n":"This opens a catalogue of auspicious bodily marks running to 1465, where the father reads them and decides that this is his daughter's husband; the marks are the second sign, beside the girl's shame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","yaakkai","nimittam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1460","lb":"CC1460","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:62"},"cc:1461":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1461,"t":["குறங்கணி மயிலொடு கோல மார்ந்தன","பிறங்கிய வுறுப்பின்மேற் பெரிய நோக்கின","கறங்கிசை மணிமுழா வெருத்தங் காண்டகு","மறங்கெழு பெரும்புலி வாயின் வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"The catalogue of the stranger's body goes on: his limbs are full and handsome, his neck is fit to be seen like a sounding gem drum, and his mouth has the colour of a fierce great tiger's.","n":"This continues the head-to-foot survey begun at 1460 as the merchant looks the stranger over; each part is measured against a standard object, as a reader of bodily signs would measure it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1461","lb":"CC1461","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1462":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1462,"t":["வரையகன் மார்பிடை வரியு மூன்றுள","புரைதபு பொன்புரை நாவு முள்ளுடைத்","தருவரைத் தோள்களு மமரர் கோன்களிற்","றுருவுகொ டடக்கையி னுருவு கொண்டவே."],"p":"","r":"On his hill-broad chest there are three lines, his tongue is flawless and gold-like, his shoulders are like hills, and his arms have the shape of the trunk of the king of the gods' elephant.","n":"The three lines on the chest (வரியு மூன்று) and the elephant-trunk arms are marks to be read, not only praised: they are the kind of signs that let a father decide whom to give his daughter to (1465).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1462","lb":"CC1462","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1463":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1463,"t":["இலங்குபொன் னிறுவரை யனைய வேந்தலுக்","கலங்கிதழ்த் தாமரைக் கொட்டை யன்ன தாய்","வலஞ்சுழிந் தமைவரக் குழிந்த வாய்ப்பொடு","நலங்கிளர் நாபியு மினிது நாறுமே."],"p":"","r":"The navel of this prince, who is like a shining golden hill, is like the seed-pod of a petalled lotus, deep and whorled to the right, and gives off a sweet scent.","n":"The rightward whorl (வலஞ்சுழி) is the auspicious turn, as of the right-whorled conch; the description reads the body as a set of good omens.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1463","lb":"CC1463","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1464":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1464,"t":["தடித்திறை திரண்டுதம் மளவிற் கேற்றசூழ்","கெடிற்றழ கழிப்பன கிளர்பொற் றோரைய","கடிப்பகை நுழைவறக் கதிர்த்த கைவிர","லடுத்தமூக் கருமணி வயிரத் தோட்டியே."],"p":"","r":"His wrists are thick and fitted to their measure, his calves outdo the kediru fish in beauty, his fingers are so close that not even a mustard seed can pass between them, and his nose is like a goad set with a rare gem.","n":"Fingers that leave no gap (கடிப்பகை நுழைவற) are a sign of a hand that holds wealth; the survey keeps to the signs a merchant would value.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1464","lb":"CC1464","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1465":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1465,"t":["வார்ந்திலங் கெயிறணி பவழ மாண்டவா","யார்ந்தபூ வங்கையு மடியுந் தாமரை","தேர்ந்தனன் றிருமகள் கணவ னாமெனத்","தீர்ந்தனன் சொல்லளைஇத் தேர்க்கொண் டேறினான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing a mouth set with long shining teeth like coral, and palms and feet like lotuses, the merchant concluded that this was the husband of Tirumagal, settled it, spoke with him and took him up onto his chariot.","n":"திருமகள் கணவன் can be heard two ways: Lakshmi's lord, a man of royal fortune, and the husband of the merchant's own daughter, whom the house has been waiting for (1453 calls him திருமகன்). The decision is made by reading the body, not by asking the stranger's birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1465","lb":"CC1465","ev":"Subhadran, having read the marks on Civakan's body, decides he is the destined husband, speaks with him and takes him onto his chariot.","cl":[],"ag":["சுபத்திரன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1466":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1466,"t":["தேரிவ ரூர்ந்தனர் செல்ல விற்றலைக்","கூருகிர் விடுத்ததோர் கோல மாலையைப்","பேரிசை வீணையிற் சூட்டிப் பெண்கொடிக்","காரிகை யுலகுணர் கடவுட் பாடுமே."],"p":"","r":"As they rode the chariot, a creeper-like woman took a lovely garland, set it on her great-sounding vina, plucked with her sharp nail and sang of the god the world knows.","n":"The text does not name the singer; she may be Kemasari herself or a woman of the house. Garlanding the instrument before playing is a worship of the vina.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["isai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1466","lb":"CC1466","ev":"As Subhadran and Civakan ride in, a woman garlands her vina and begins a hymn to the Jina.","cl":[],"ag":["காரிகை","சுபத்திரன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1467":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1467,"t":["வீங்கோத வண்ணன் விரைததும்பு பூம்பிண்டித்","தேங்கோத முக்குடைக்கீழ்த் தேவர் பெருமானைத்","தேவர் பெருமானைத் தேனார் மலர்சிதறி","நாவி னவிற்றாதார் வீட்டுலக நண்ணாரே."],"p":"","r":"The singer says that those who do not scatter honeyed flowers and praise with their tongues the lord of the gods, sea-coloured, under the three umbrellas beneath the fragrant flowering pindi tree, will not reach the world of release.","n":"The pindi (asoka) tree and the triple umbrella mark the Jina. The repeated half-line (தேவர் பெருமானைத் தேவர் பெருமானை) is the refrain form of a sung hymn, kept in all three stanzas.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","veedu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1467","lb":"CC1467","ev":"","cl":[["those who do not praise the Jina with flowers and the tongue do not reach the world of release","jain","asserted",4,"paadu_magal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காரிகை",""],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1468":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1468,"t":["அடல்வண்ண வைம்பொறியு மட்டுயர்ந்தோர் கோமான்","கடல் வண்ணன் முக்குடைக்கீழ்க் காசின் றுணர்ந்தான்","காசின் றுணர்ந்தான் கமல மலரடியை","மாசின்றிப் பாடாதார் வானுலக நண்ணாரே."],"p":"","r":"She sings that the sea-coloured one under the three umbrellas, lord of those who rose by slaying the mighty five senses, knew without flaw, and that those who do not sing his lotus feet without stain will not reach the world of the gods.","n":"The refrain shifts from வீட்டுலகம் (1467, 1469) to வானுலகம் here: release and the heaven of the gods are not the same goal in Jain teaching, and the hymn names both as the reward of praise. காசின்று உணர்ந்தான் points to the Jina's faultless knowing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","adakkam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1468","lb":"CC1468","ev":"","cl":[["the Jina conquered the five senses and knows without flaw","jain","asserted",2,"paadu_magal"],["those who do not sing the Jina's feet do not reach heaven","jain","asserted",4,"paadu_magal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காரிகை",""],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1469":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1469,"t":["பூத்தொழியாப் பிண்டிக்கீழ்ப் பொங்கோத வண்ணனை","நாத்தழும்ப வேத்தாதார் வீட்டுலக நண்ணாரே","வீட்டுலக நண்ணார் வினைக்கள்வ ராறலைப்ப","வோட்டிடுப வெண்குணனுங் கோட்பட் டுயிராவே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do not praise, until their tongues are calloused, the sea-coloured one beneath the ever-flowering pindi will not reach the world of release; the thieves that are their deeds waylay them, and their souls, their eight qualities seized, are driven off.","n":"On my reading of கோட்பட்டு, the eight qualities (எண்குணன்) here belong to the souls, plundered by deeds pictured as highway robbers (வினைக்கள்வர் ஆறலைப்ப), in keeping with the Jain teaching of eight karmas that cover the soul's eight qualities. The line is compressed and the construction is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","vazhipaadu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1469","lb":"CC1469","ev":"","cl":[["karma waylays the soul like a robber and takes its eight qualities","jain","asserted",3,"paadu_magal"],["those who do not praise the Jina do not reach release","jain","asserted",2,"paadu_magal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காரிகை",""],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1470":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1470,"t":["முத்துமிழு முந்நீர் மணிவண்ணன் மூன்றுலகும்","பத்திமையாற் பாடப் படுவான்றாள் பாடக்கேட்","டொத்தரம்பை யன்னா ளுவந்திவளொ டொப்பானோர்","வித்தகனை யின்னே பெறுக வெனவுரைத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing her sing the feet of the pearl-sea-coloured one whom the three worlds sing with devotion, a woman like Rambha rejoiced and said, 'May she now win a man of skill fit to match her.'","n":"The wish is spoken as the destined husband enters the city, so it works as an auspicious word heard at the right moment. Who speaks and who இவள் is (the singer, or Kemasari) is not clear from the verse alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","paththi","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1470","lb":"CC1470","ev":"A woman who hears the hymn blesses the girl with a fitting husband, just as Civakan arrives.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பை அன்னாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1471":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1471,"t":["நிலந்தினக் கிடந்தன நிதியந் நீணகர்ப்","புலம்பறப் பொலிவொடு புக்க காலையே","யிலங்குபூங் கொடியன வேழை நோக்கமு","முலங்கொடோ ளுறுவலி நோக்கு மொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"When they entered the long city, where treasure lay so thick the ground seemed to eat it, the gaze of the girl like a shining flowering creeper and the gaze of the man with mighty shoulders met as one.","n":"நோக்கும் ஒத்த: the two looks are equal and simultaneous, the first-sight meeting of Tamil love poetry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1471","lb":"CC1471","ev":"As Civakan enters the city with Subhadran, he and Kemasari see each other and their eyes meet.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1472":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1472,"t":["கண்ணுறக் காளையைக் காண்டலுங் கைவளை","மண்ணுறத் தோய்ந்தடி வீழ்ந்தன மாமையு","முண்ணிறை நாணு முடைந்தன வேட்கையு","மொண்ணிறத் தீவிளைத் தாளுருக் குற்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"On seeing the young man, her bangles slipped down to the ground, her fair colour, her inner restraint and her modesty all broke, and desire kindled a bright fire in which she melted.","n":"The signs of a separated woman's wasting (slipping bangles, lost colour) come here at the first instant of sight, before any separation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","naanam","kaamam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1472","lb":"CC1472","ev":"At the sight of Civakan, Kemasari is overcome with desire.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1473":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1473,"t":["வாக்கணங் கார்மணி வீணைவல் லாற்கவ","ணோக்கணங் காய்மன நோய்செய நொந்தவன்","வீக்கணங் கார்முலை வேய்நெடுந் தோளியொர்","தாக்கணங் கோமக ளோனெத் தாழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He who was master of the vina, whose lute was a goddess of speech, was pained as her glance became an afflicting goddess and sickened his mind; wondering whether she was a striking goddess or a mortal daughter, he sank down.","n":"The word play runs on அணங்கு three times: வாக்கணங்கு (the lute as the goddess of speech), நோக்கணங்கு (her look as the goddess that afflicts), தாக்கணங்கு (the goddess who strikes). The vina-master who won Gandharvadattai by sound is here undone by sight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1473","lb":"CC1473","ev":"Struck by Kemasari's look, Civakan wonders whether she is a goddess or a woman and is overcome.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1474":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1474,"t":["நல்வளத் தாமரை நாணிய வாண்முகக்","கொல்வளர் வேற்கணி னாள்குழைந் தாளெனச்","சொல்வளர்த் தாரவ டோழியர் சோர்குழன்","மல்வளர் மார்பனை வந்து வளைந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Saying that their mistress, whose face shamed the lotus and whose eyes were like killing spears, had melted, her companions spread the word and came and gathered round the strong-chested man.","n":"The companions read her state from her body and act on it at once, the friend's role of Tamil love poetry turned into a crowd.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1474","lb":"CC1474","ev":"Kemasari's companions see she is in love and crowd round Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1475":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1475,"t":["நினைப்பரு நீணிறை நிப்புதி சேர்ந்தாங்","கினத்திடை யேறனை யானெழி னோக்கிப்","புனக்கொடி பொற்பொடு புண்ணிய நம்பி","வனப்பினை யேகண்டு வாட்க ணகன்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Gazing at the beauty of the man of merit, like a bull among the herd, as if she had reached a long, full bliss beyond thought, the field-creeper girl with her grace looked once on his loveliness and turned her sword-like eyes away.","n":"நிப்புதி is a rare word; it seems to be a loan of Prakrit nivvui (nirvrti), the bliss of release, used here as a simile for the joy of a lover's gaze. Turning her eyes away (வாட்கண் அகன்றாள்) is the shyness the astrologer named as the sign of the destined husband (1451).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","naanam","inbam","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1475","lb":"CC1475","ev":"Kemasari looks at Civakan in rapture and then turns her eyes away.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1476":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1476,"t":["வள்ளலை வாசநெய் பூசி மணிக்குடத்","தெள்ள னீர்சொரிந் தாட்டினர் தேம்புகை","யுள்ளுற வுண்ட கலிங்க முடுத்தபின்","கள்ளவிழ் கண்ணி கலத்தொ டணிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"They anointed the generous man with scented oil, bathed him with clear water poured from jewelled pots, dressed him in cloth that had drunk in sweet incense, and adorned him with a honey-dripping garland and ornaments.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1476","lb":"CC1476","ev":"The women of the house bathe Civakan, dress him and adorn him.","cl":[],"ag":["கோதையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1477":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1477,"t":["மங்கல வெள்ளை வழித்துமுத் தீர்த்தபின்","கொங்கலர் கோதையர் கொண்டக மெய்தி","யங்கதிர்ப் பொற்கலத் தாரமிர் தேந்தினர்","செங்கயற் கண்ணியர் சீரி னயின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"After they had wiped on the auspicious white paste and drawn the pearls into place, the women with fragrant garlands took him inside and brought nectar-like food in bright golden vessels, and he ate it in proper style.","n":"மங்கல வெள்ளை and முத்து ஈர்த்த are hard to fix; the sense of the second half, the meal, is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1477","lb":"CC1477","ev":"The women lead Civakan in and serve him food in golden vessels, and he eats.","cl":[],"ag":["கோதையர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1478":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1478,"t":["பத்தியிற் குயிற்றிய பைம்பொற் றிண்ணைமேற்","சித்திரத் தவிசினுட் செல்வன் சீர்பெற","நித்தில மணியுறழ் கரக நீரினா","லத்துறை விடுத்தன னலர்ந்த தாரினான்."],"p":"","r":"Seated on a painted cushion upon a golden platform set with inlay rows, so that his dignity was honoured, the garlanded lord rinsed with water from a pitcher like pearl and gem and finished that rite.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1478","lb":"CC1478","ev":"Civakan washes after the meal, seated on a cushion on a golden platform.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1479":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1479,"t":["இளிந்தகாய் கமழ்திரை வாச மீண்டியோர்","பளிங்குபோழ்ந் தருகுபொன் பதித்த பத்தியின்","விளிம்புமுத் தழுத்திய யவனக் கைவினைத்","தெளிந்தபொன் னடைப்பையுட் பாகு சென்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The areca pieces, scented with plump fruit and fragrant spice, came in a golden betel bag of Yavana workmanship, with crystal split and set, gold inlaid at its edges and pearls pressed along the rim.","n":"The Yavana craftsmanship (யவனக் கைவினை) marks the merchant house as rich in foreign goods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1479","lb":"CC1479","ev":"Betel and areca are brought to Civakan in a jewelled bag.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1480":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1480,"t":["பாசிலை சுருட்டுபு கறித்துப் பல்லினைத்","தேசிகம் படத்துடைத் துமிழ்ந்து தேங்கமழ்","வாசம்வாய்க் கொண்டனன் மணிசெய் குண்டலம்","வீசிவில் விலங்கிவிட் டுமிழ வென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"He rolled the green leaves, chewed them, wiped his teeth till they shone, spat, and took the sweet fragrance into his mouth, while his gem earrings swung and shot their light aside, as they say.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1480","lb":"CC1480","ev":"Civakan chews betel after the meal.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1481":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1481,"t":["பண்ணுலாங் கிளவிதன் பரவை யேந்தல்குல்","வண்ணமே கலையிவை வாய்ந்த பூந்துகி","லுண்ணிலாய்ப் பசுங்கதி ருமிழ்வ பாவியேன்","கண்ணையு மனத்தையுங் களங்கொண் டிட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says: her girdle and fine cloth, spread over the broad hips of the girl whose speech is melody, pour out a fresh light from within and have captured the eyes and heart of wretched me.","n":"The lament begins after the feast: the guest has been honoured in every way and is alone with longing. பாவியேன் is the lover's self-pity, not a confession of sin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kan","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1481","lb":"CC1481","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1482":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1482,"t":["கடிகமழ் குழலினாற் கட்டி மெய்யெலா","நடுவொசி நோன்சிலைப் புருவத் தாற்புடைத்","தடுமலர் நெடுங்கணா லாவி போழ்ந்திடாக்","கொடியவ ளிளமுலை கொல்லுந் கொல்லுமே."],"p":"","r":"He says the cruel girl binds his whole body with her fragrant hair, beats him with the strong bow of her brows bent at the middle, and, before her long flower eyes have even split his life, her young breasts kill and kill again.","n":"Each feature is a weapon in a sequence of capture and execution: hair the rope, brow the bow, eyes the blade, breasts the death stroke. The doubled கொல்லும் is the climax.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","anangu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1482","lb":"CC1482","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1483":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1483,"t":["கடியன கச்சினாற் கட்டப் பட்டன","கொடியன குங்குமங் கொட்டப் பட்டன","வடிநிலம் பரந்துமுத் தணிந்த வெம்முலை","யிடைநிலஞ் செகுப்பன வென்னை யென்செயா."],"p":"","r":"He says her hot breasts, cruel enough to be bound by a bodice, smeared with saffron, spread over with pearls on their sloping ground, destroy even the ground of her waist; what will they not do to me?","n":"The bodice (கச்சு) is described as a restraint put on something dangerous, which the next verse repeats for the hair.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1483","lb":"CC1483","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1484":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1484,"t":["கரியவுள் வெறியன கட்டப் பட்டன","புரிவொடு புறத்திடப் பட்ட பூங்குழல்","தெரியின்மற் றென்செயா செய்ய நீண்டன","பெரியகண் போலவும் பேது செய்யுமே."],"p":"","r":"He says her flower-decked hair, dark and fragrant within, is bound and coiled and thrown behind her; if it is like that, what will it not do? It bewilders like her large eyes, which are long and red-lined.","n":"கட்டப் பட்டன is repeated from 1483: hair and breasts are both things that had to be bound, and still they overpower him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1484","lb":"CC1484","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:63"},"cc:1485":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1485,"t":["காதன்மை கண்ணுளே யடக்கிக் கண்ணெனுந்","தூதினாற் றுணிபொரு ளுணர்த்தித் தான்றமர்க்","கேதின்மை படக்கரந் திட்ட வாட்கணோக்","கோதநீ ரமுதமு முலகும் விற்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Keeping her love shut inside her eyes, she makes the eye her messenger to tell him what she has resolved, and hides it so well that her kin find no fault; such a sword-eyed glance would outsell the sea's nectar and the whole world.","n":"The verse turns on கரந்திட்ட: her love is real but concealed, and the concealment is itself praised because it spares her kin any blame (ஏதின்மை). It may be Civakan's thought rather than the narrator's; the speech of 1487 makes the point uncertain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","thoothu","naanam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1485","lb":"CC1485","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1486":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1486,"t":["மிகுகொடா முத்தஞ் சூட்டி மீளிமை தீர்த்து மின்னு","நகுகொடா மணிக ணல்ல தெளித்துக்கொண் டெழுதி நன்பொன்","முகபடாம் வைப்ப வாட்செற் றழன்றுகண் கரிந்த முல்லைத்","தொகுகடாங் கோதை வெய்ய துணைமணி முலைக டாமே."],"p":"","r":"Her breasts are figured as war-elephants: crowned with pearls, their fury cooled, decked with bright gems and given a face-cloth of fine gold, beside the garland of massed jasmine.","n":"The elephant figure (முகபடாம், கடாம்) makes the beloved's body an armed force against the man who sees her, preparing Civakan's complaint in 1487 that her features are Death and Yama's noose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1486","lb":"CC1486","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1487":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1487,"t":["தேன்கறி கற்ற கூழைச் செண்பக மாலை வேற்க","ணூன்கறி கற்ற கால","னொண்மணித் தடக்கை வைவேல்","கூன்பிறை நுதலோர் கூற்றங் குவிமுலை நமன்கைப் பாசம்","யான்பிற னளியன் வாழ்வா னாசைப்பட் டிருக்கின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says her hair has learned to feed on honey, her spear-eye on flesh, her curved brow is Death and her breasts are Yama's noose, and yet he, a pitiable stranger, sits longing to live.","n":"The list of killers (கூற்றம், நமன்கைப் பாசம்) ends in the wish to live (வாழ்வான் ஆசைப்பட்டு): the lover desires the very thing he names as death. பிறன் marks him as an outsider in the Takka land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kootru","kan","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1487","lb":"CC1487","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1488":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1488,"t":["திருவிற்குங் கற்பகத் தெரியன் மாலையா","ருருவிற்கோர் விளக்கமா மொண்பொற் பூங்கொடி","முருகற்கு மநங்கற்கு மெனக்கு மொய்சடை","யொருவற்கும் பகைத்தியா லொருத்தி வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"He tells the golden flower-creeper, a lamp to the beauty of goddesses wearing kalpaka garlands, that one woman's loveliness has made her an enemy to Murugan, to Kama, to himself and to the god of dense matted hair.","n":"Setting himself beside Murugan, Anangan and the ascetic god of matted locks (மொய்சடை ஒருவன்) makes her beauty a rival even to the god who renounced and burned desire; how பகைத்தி is to be taken, as rivalry or as hostility, is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1488","lb":"CC1488","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","கேமசரி"],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1489":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1489,"t":["கலைத்தொகை நலம்பல கடந்த காளைதா","னலத்தகை யவணல நினைப்ப நாய்கனு","மலைத்தொகை மதந்தவழ் யானை மன்னவ","னிலத்தவர்க் கறிவுற நெறியிற் செப்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"While the youth who had passed beyond the many arts brooded on her beauty, the merchant told the king of the land, lord of musth elephants, by due custom, so that the people of the land should know.","n":"The marriage moves at once from the glance of 1485 to a public act: the father's report to the king (நெறியிற் செப்பினான்) is what turns concealed love into an approved union.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","arasu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1489","lb":"CC1489","ev":"Kemasari's father, the merchant, formally announces the match to the king and the land.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நாய்கன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1490":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1490,"t":["இடியுமி ழெறிதிரை முழக்கிற் பல்லியங்","கொடியணி வியனகர்க் குழுமி யார்த்தெழக்","கடிமண மியற்றினார் கடவு ணாளினால்","வடிமலர்க் கோதையை மைந்தற் கென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"With the many instruments of the flag-decked city roaring like the thunder-spitting surf, they held the wedding on an auspicious day and gave the flower-garlanded maiden to the young man, so it is told.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1490","lb":"CC1490","ev":"Kemasari is married to Civakan on an auspicious day amid the city's music.","cl":[],"ag":["சுற்றம்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1491":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1491,"t":["மணிக்குட மழுத்திவைத் தனைய தோளினான்","கணிக்கிடங் கொடாநலங் கதிர்த்த காரிகை","யணிக்கிட னாயி வரிவை தன்னொடும்","பிணித்திடை விடாதவன் பெற்ற வின்பமே."],"p":"","r":"Such was the joy of the man with shoulders like gem-pots pressed close, who never let go of the woman whose beauty no astrologer could measure.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1491","lb":"CC1491","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1492":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1492,"t":["பூந்துகில் பொருதிரை பொம்மல் வெம்முலை","யேந்திய மணிவரை யிரக்க நீர்த்தரங்","காய்ந்தவன் றோளிணை நாக மாகவைத்","தீந்ததக் கடலவற் கமுத மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Her silk-clad breasts became the mountain, his twin shoulders the serpent, and the sea of her compassion, churned, gave him nectar, as they say.","n":"The union is told as the churning of the ocean: she is mountain and sea, he the cord-serpent, and the nectar is what she gives; the borrowed myth makes the pleasure divine in kind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","amudham","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1492","lb":"CC1492","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1493":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1493,"t":["சந்தனச் சேற்றிடைத் தாம வார்குழற்","பைந்தொடி படாமுலை குளிப்பப் பாய்தலின்","மைந்தன தார்குலைந் துடைய வாய்திறந்","தஞ்சிலம் பணியல்குற் கலையொ டார்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"As the bangled girl's breasts plunged into the sandal mire and she leapt to him, the youth's garland was crushed and her anklets and girdle cried out with open mouths.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1493","lb":"CC1493","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1494":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1494,"t":["கோதையுங் குழலும் பொங்கக் குவிமுலைக் குழங்கன் மாலைப்","போதுகப் பொருது பூணும் பொருகடன் முத்து மூழ்கக்","காதலுங் களிப்பு மிக்குக் கங்குலும் பகலும் விள்ளார்","சாதலும் பிறப்பு மில்லாத் தன்மைபெற் றவர்க ளொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Garlands and hair tumbling, pearls of the ornaments sinking as they struck, their love and delight swelling, they did not part night or day and were like those who have won the state with neither death nor birth.","n":"சாதலும் பிறப்பும் இல்லாத் தன்மை is the language of release from rebirth, and the narrator gives it to two lovers in bed; it is a simile (ஒத்தார்), not a claim that pleasure frees, and the same night of 1504 ends it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1494","lb":"CC1494","ev":"Civakan and Kemasari stay together without parting, night and day.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1495":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1495,"t":["புனைமலர்த் தாரி னானும் போதணி கொம்ப னாளு","நனைமலர்க் காவு மந்தண் வாலியு நல்ல வாடிச்","சுனைமலர்க் குவளை குற்றுச் சூழ்மலர்க் கண்ணி சூட்டி","வினைநல நுகர்ந்து செல்வார் விதியினான் மிக்க நீரார்."],"p":"","r":"The garlanded man and the woman like a flowering branch went through budding groves and cool clear streams, plucked lilies from the springs and crowned each other, enjoying the good of their deeds, people made eminent by fate.","n":"வினைநலம் நுகர்ந்து and விதியினான் put the honeymoon in the book of karma: their delight is not earned in this life but eaten as the ripened good of earlier deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","inbam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1495","lb":"CC1495","ev":"The couple wander the pleasure groves and pools together.","cl":[["the pleasures one enjoys are the fruit of one's good deeds, given by fate","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1496":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1496,"t":["பொழிந்துகு காதல் பூண்டு புல்லுகை விடாது செல்லக்","கழிந்தன விரண்டு திங்கள் காளையு மற்றோர் நாளாற்","பிழிந்துகொள் வனைய பெண்மைப் பெய்வளைத் தோளி தன்னோ","டழிந்துவீ ழருவிக் குன்றி லாய்மலர்க் காவு புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"As their overflowing love went on with no loosening of embrace, two months passed, and one day the youth went with the woman of pressed-out femininity into a grove of chosen flowers on a hill of falling cascades.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1496","lb":"CC1496","ev":"After two months of marriage, Civakan takes Kemasari to a flower grove on a hill with waterfalls.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1497":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1497,"t":["காஞ்சனக் கமுகு காய்பொற் கனிக்குலை வாழை சூழ்ந்து","பூஞ்சினை நாகந் தீம்பூ மரக்கருப் பூரச் சோலை","மாஞ்சினை மயில்க ளாடச் சண்பக மலர்கள் சிந்துந்","தீஞ்சுனை யருவிக் குன்றஞ் சீர்பெற வேறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He climbed with splendour the hill of sweet springs and falls, ringed by golden areca, plantains with ripe clusters, flowering naga trees and camphor groves, where peacocks dance on mango boughs and champak drops its flowers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1497","lb":"CC1497","ev":"Civakan climbs the wooded hill.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1498":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1498,"t":["தினைவிளை சாரற் செவ்வாய்ச் சிறுகிளி மாத ரோப்பப்","புனைவளைத் தோளி சொல்லைக் கிளியெனக் கிள்ளை போகா","நனைவினை கோதை நாணிப் பொன்னரி மாலை யோச்சக்","கனைகழற் குருசி னண்ணிக் கவர்கிளி யோப்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"On the millet slope the little red-beaked parrots, taking the bangled woman's voice for a parrot's, would not fly off; ashamed, she waved her golden garland, and the hero with ringing anklets came near and drove the parrots away himself.","n":"The scene is the akam scene of the girl guarding the millet field (தினை, கிளி ஓப்புதல்) moved into marriage; the joke that her voice is too parrot-like to frighten parrots makes her embarrassment (நாணி) the point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1498","lb":"CC1498","ev":"Kemasari tries to scare the parrots from the millet; they take her voice for their own, and Civakan drives them off.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி","கிள்ளை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1499":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1499,"t":["கொந்தழல் வேற்க ணாலென்னாவிகூட் டுண்ட கொம்பே","செந்தழை யலங்க லேந்திச் சீறடி பரவ வந்தே","னுய்ந்தினிப் பணிசெய் வேனோ வுடம் பொழித் தேகு வேனோ","பைந்தழை யல்குற் பாவாய் பணியெனப் பரவி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He praised her: creeper who ate up my life with your spear-like eyes, I have come bearing a garland of red leaves to worship your small feet; shall I live on and serve you, or leave this body and go? Doll, command me.","n":"The choice he puts to her, to live and serve or to leave the body and go (உடம்பு ஒழித்து ஏகுவேனோ), is a lover's hyperbole, but its verb of going returns as the plain event of 1505, when he leaves her without asking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","uyir","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1499","lb":"CC1499","ev":"Civakan offers Kemasari a leaf garland and asks her to command him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","கேமசரி"],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1500":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1500,"t":["வீணையுஞ் குழலும் பாலு மமுதமுங் கரும்புந் தேனும்","பாணியாழ் கனியும் வென்ற பைங்கிளி மழலைத் தீஞ்சொல்","வாணிக மகளிர் தாமே வாணிகம் வல்ல ரென்னாப்","பூண்முலை பொதிர்ப்பப் புல்லிப் புனைநலம் பருகி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Saying that merchants' daughters are the ones truly skilled in trade, since her parrot-sweet lisping speech outsold vina, flute, milk, nectar, sugarcane, honey and the music of the lute, he embraced her till her ornamented breasts swelled and drank her beauty.","n":"The pun on வாணிகம் plays on her birth: a merchant's daughter who has 'sold' him sweetness outbidding every sweet thing; it is also the second time in the chapter (after விற்குமே in 1485) that her worth is put as a price.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1500","lb":"CC1500","ev":"Civakan praises Kemasari's speech and embraces her.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1501":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1501,"t":["திங்களங் குழவி செவ்வா னிடைக்கிடந் திமைப்ப தேபோற்","குங்கும மார்பிற் பூண்ட மங்கையோ டிருந்த போழ்தோர்","வாளொளித் தடங்க ணீலம் மணிவண்டு கண்டு சொன்னான்","கங்குறா னீங்க லுற்றுக் கமழ்மல ரணிந்த தாரான்."],"p":"","r":"As the young moon glimmers against the red sky, so she lay on his saffron chest; sitting with her, the man of fragrant garland, minded to leave in the night, saw a gem-bee at the lily of her bright wide eye and spoke.","n":"கங்குல் தான் நீங்கல் உற்று is read here as his intent to leave by night, which makes the bee-speech that follows a covert farewell; it may instead mean only that night was passing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1501","lb":"CC1501","ev":"Civakan, sitting with Kemasari, sees a bee near her eye and speaks to it.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1502":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1502,"t":["மணிவண்டிம் மாதர் கோதை மதுவுண வந்த போழ்தங்","கிணைவண்டங் கிறந்து பாடின் றிருக்குமே யிரங்க லின்றாய்த்","துணைவண்டு துஞ்சி னீயுந் துஞ்சுவை யென்று நின்கட்","பணிகொண்ட தின்மை யாற்றான் பரிவொடு மிருக்கு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He tells the gem-bee that when it came to drink honey from this lady's garland, its mate, left behind, would sit grieving without song, and that if its mate sleeps it too will sleep; so its partner stays on with tender grief.","n":"Spoken to a bee in her presence, the verse seems to rehearse his own leaving: the one who goes for honey and the mate who stays behind grieving. The second half is hard to construe, and the sense of பணிகொண்டது இன்மை is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1502","lb":"CC1502","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மணிவண்டு"],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1503":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1503,"t":["குழவியாய்ப் பிறந்து வெய்யோன் குமரனாய் குறுகி யிப்பால்","விழைவுதீர் கிழவ னாகி விழுக்கதி ருலந்து வீழ","மழலைவண் டுழல நக்க மல்லிகை யலங்கல் சூட்டிக்","குழல்புரை கிளவி யோடுங் கொழும்புகை யமளி சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The sun, born an infant, grown a young man and then a desireless old man whose bright rays fade, fell; and he crowned the parrot-voiced woman with a jasmine garland where lisping bees wandered and went with her to the couch fragrant with smoke.","n":"The sunset is told as a whole human life in a day (குழவி, குமரன், கிழவன்), and விழைவுதீர் gives the old sun the very loss of desire that the lovers do not yet feel; the image of impermanence frames the last night before he leaves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kaalam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1503","lb":"CC1503","ev":"At sunset Civakan garlands Kemasari and goes to bed with her.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1504":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1504,"t":["திருத்துயில் பெற்ற மார்பன் றிருந்துதா ருழக்க வின்ப","வருத்தமுற் றசைந்த கோதை வாளொளித் தடங்க ணீலம்","பொருத்தலும் பொன்ன னாளைப் புறக்கணித் தெழுந்து போகிப்","பருச்சுடர்ப் பவழ நோன்றாழ்ப் பன்மணிக் கதவு சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"When the woman, wearied by the toil of pleasure after his garland had crushed her, closed her sword-bright lily eyes, he rose, turned from the golden one and went to the many-gemmed door with its strong coral bolt.","n":"புறக்கணித்து, 'turning his back on', is a strong verb for a hero leaving a bride of two months; the verse gives no reason for the going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1504","lb":"CC1504","ev":"While Kemasari sleeps, Civakan rises and goes to the door.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1505":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1505,"t":["அல்லியுட் பாவை யன்னா ளறிவுறா வகையி னொற்றி","மெல்லவே திறந்து நீக்கி மின்னுவிட் டிலங்கு பைம்பூட்","கொல்சின மடங்க லன்னான் கொழுநிதி மாட நீந்திப்","பல்கதிர்ப் பரிதி போலப் பாயிரு ளேகி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Pressing the door softly so that the lady like the doll in the lotus should not know, he opened it, and the man like a lion of killing rage crossed the treasure-filled mansion and went into the spreading dark like the many-rayed sun.","n":"The simile of the sun going into darkness reverses the sun of 1503, which fell; here the hero is the light that leaves and the house is left dark. The secrecy (அறிவுறா வகையின்) is the centre of the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","iravu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1505","lb":"CC1505","ev":"Civakan opens the door secretly and leaves Kemasari's house in the dark.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1506":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1506,"t":["தாளுடைத் தடங்கொள் செவ்வித் தாமரைப் போது போலும்","வாளுடை முகத்தி னாடன் வருமுலைத் தடத்தின் வைகி","நாளினும் பெருகு கின்ற நகைமதி யனைய காதற்","கேள்வனைக் கனவிற் காணாள் கிளர்மணிப் பூணி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The lotus-faced woman with bright ornaments, who had rested on his chest, does not see even in dream the husband whose love grew daily like the laughing moon.","n":"கனவிற் காணாள: she does not see him even in dream, so the dream that in akam poetry consoles the separated woman is denied her; her sleep is unbroken while he goes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1506","lb":"CC1506","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1507":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1507,"t":["அரந்தினப் பிறந்த பைம்பொ னரும்பிய முலையி னாளைக்","கரந்தவன் கங்கு னீங்கக் கதிர்வளை யணங்கு மென்றோள்","வரந்தரு தெய்வ மன்னாள் வைகிரு ளனந்த றேறிப்","பரந்தெலாத் திசையு நோக்கிப் பையவே பரிவு கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"When he had slipped away as night ended, she of gold-sprouting breasts, like a boon-giving goddess, woke from her heavy sleep, looked in every direction, and slowly grew distressed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1507","lb":"CC1507","ev":"Kemasari wakes, finds Civakan gone, and searches with her eyes.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1508":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1508,"t":["திருமணி குயின்ற செம்பொற் றிருந்துபூங் கொம்ப னாடன்","கருமணிப் பாவை யன்னான் கரந்துழிக் காண்டல் செல்லா","ளெரிமணி விளக்க மாடத் திருளறு காறு மோடி","யருமணி யிழந்தோர் நாக மலமரு கின்ற தொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Unable to find the man like the dark pupil of an eye where he had hidden, the woman like a flowering golden branch ran through the lamp-lit mansion until the dark lifted, like a serpent that has lost its rare gem.","n":"Two images of loss meet: he is கருமணிப் பாவை, the pupil of her eye, so his going leaves her blind; and she is the serpent without its gem, a creature that in lore cannot see or live without the jewel it carries.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1508","lb":"CC1508","ev":"Kemasari runs through the mansion searching for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:64"},"cc:1509":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1509,"t":["யாண்டை யாயைய வஞ்சினெ னாருயி","ரீண்டு டம்பொழித் தேக வலிக்குமா","னீண்ட தோளவ னேநிறை யானிலேன்","றீண்டு வந்தெனத் தேனின் மிழற்றினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Finding him gone, Kemasari cries out asking where her lord is, says she is afraid because her dear life strains to quit her body, confesses she has no restraint left, and begs the long-armed one to come back to her.","n":"நிறையான் இலேன், 'I have no nirai', is her own admission that the self-containment a woman is meant to keep has gone; the narrator still calls her speech honey-sweet (தேனின் மிழற்றினாள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nirai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1509","lb":"CC1509","ev":"Kemasari wakes, finds Civakan gone, and calls out to him.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1510":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1510,"t":["கனிகொள் காமங் கலந்துயி ரொன்றலி","னினியர் மங்கைய ரென்பது கூறுவாய்","பனிகொண் மாமதி போற்பசப் பூரயான்","றனிய ளாவது தக்கது வோசொலாய்."],"p":"","r":"She throws back at him his own saying that women are sweetest when ripe love mingles and two lives become one, and asks whether it is fitting that she, pale as the dewy moon, should now be alone.","n":"Her argument turns his own doctrine of love, that the lovers' lives become one, into the charge against him: if their lives are one, his leaving her alone contradicts what he taught.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1510","lb":"CC1510","ev":"Kemasari, alone at dawn, laments to the absent Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1511":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1511,"t":["கழலு நெஞ்சொடு கைவளை சோருமாற்","சுழலுங் கண்களுஞ் சூடுறு பொன்னென","வழலு மேனியு மாற்றலெ னையவோ","நிழலி னீப்பருங் காதலு நீத்தியோ."],"p":"","r":"Her heart slipping, her bangles loosening, her eyes wheeling and her body burning like heated gold, she says she cannot bear it and asks whether he has cast off even the love that should be as hard to shed as one's shadow.","n":"The simile நிழலின் நீப்பருங் காதல், love as inseparable as a shadow, is taken up literally in 1524 and 1525, where the only thing that stays with her is her own reflection.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1511","lb":"CC1511","ev":"Kemasari, alone at dawn, laments to the absent Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1512":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1512,"t":["திருந்து மல்லிகைத் தேங்கமழ் மாலையான்","புரிந்து சூடினும் பூங்கொடி நுண்ணிடை","வருந்து மான்மட வாயெனும் வஞ்சநீ","கரிந்தி யானையக் காண்டலும் வல்லையோ."],"p":"","r":"She calls him a deceiver who used to say that her slender waist suffered even under a jasmine garland, and asks whether he can now bear to see her scorched.","n":"வஞ்ச நீ is the first time she names him a deceiver; his remembered tenderness is offered as evidence against him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1512","lb":"CC1512","ev":"Kemasari, alone at dawn, laments to the absent Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1513":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1513,"t":["தொண்டை வாயிவ டொய்யில் வனமுலை","கண்டு தேவர் கனிபவென் றேத்துவாய்","வண்டு கூறிய வண்ண மறிந்திலேன்","விண்டு தேன்றுளிக் கும்விரைத் தாரினாய்."],"p":"","r":"She recalls how he praised her coral lips and breasts, saying the gods would melt to see them, and says she did not grasp the meaning of what was said about the bee, he of the honey-dripping garland.","n":"Line 3 looks back to 1501 and 1502, where Civakan, about to leave in the night, spoke to the bee on her garland of a bee whose mate stays on without grief; she now sees the words were a covert notice of his going. வண்டு கூறிய can be 'what the bee said' or 'what was said of (to) the bee'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","pirivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1513","lb":"CC1513","ev":"Kemasari, alone at dawn, laments to the absent Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1514":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1514,"t":["முலைவைத் ததடத் திடைமுள் கலுறிற்","றலைவைத் துநிலத் தடிதை வருவாய்","சிலைவித் தகனே தெருளே னருளா","யுலைவித் தனையென் னுயிர்கா வலனே."],"p":"","r":"She recalls that if a thorn so much as touched her on the way he would bow his head to the ground and stroke her foot, says she is bewildered, and asks the master of the bow, the guardian of her life who has now shaken it, for grace.","n":"She calls him என் உயிர் காவலனே, guardian of my life, in the same line that says he has shaken that life (உலைவித்தனை).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ali","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1514","lb":"CC1514","ev":"Kemasari, alone at dawn, laments to the absent Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1515":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1515,"t":["கடனித் திலம்வைத் தகதிர்ம் முலையி","னிடனெத் துணையத் துணையும் மெழுதி","யுடனொத் துறைவா னுழைவா ரலனேன்","மடனொத் துளதென் னுயிர்வாழ் வதுவே."],"p":"","r":"She recalls him painting designs over every part of her pearl-strung breasts and living as one with her, and says that for one who cannot follow him, living on is mere folly.","n":"Line 3 (உழைவார் அலனேன்) is hard to divide; the sense 'I am not one who can go where you are' is the likeliest but not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1515","lb":"CC1515","ev":"Kemasari, alone at dawn, laments to the absent Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1516":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1516,"t":["பெறுமன் பினளென் பதுபே சினலா","லறுமன் பினளென் றறைவா ரிலையா","லிறுமென் பொடினைந் துநைவேற் கருளி","நறுமென் கமழ்தா ரவனே நணுகாய்."],"p":"","r":"Because he had said she was one who would always have love, and no one had said her love would be cut off, she asks him to pity her as she wastes to the bone and to come near.","n":"The verse plays பெறும் அன்பினள், she who receives love, against அறும் அன்பினள், she whose love is severed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1516","lb":"CC1516","ev":"Kemasari, alone at dawn, laments to the absent Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1517":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1517,"t":["நுனசீ றடிநோ வநடந் துசெலே","லெனதா வியகத் துறைவா யெனுநீ","புனைதா ரவனே பொயுரைத் தனையால்","வினையே னொழியத் தனியே கினையே."],"p":"","r":"She quotes his words, 'if you walk your small feet will ache; you live within my life', tells him that he lied, and says he has gone alone and left her, the one of evil deeds.","n":"வினையேன், 'I of (bad) deeds', is the usual self-name of one in grief, but it places a cause in her own past deeds in the same breath as she calls his word a lie (பொய்யுரைத்தனை): she blames both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vaaimai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1517","lb":"CC1517","ev":"Kemasari, alone at dawn, laments to the absent Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1518":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1518,"t":["பருமுத் துறையும் பணைவெம் முலைநின்","றிருமுத் தகலந் திளையா தமையா","வெரிமொய்த் தனலு மிகல்வே லெரிபுண்","மருமத் தனலும் வகைசெய் தனையே."],"p":"","r":"She says that, torn from his pearl-hung chest before her breasts had had their fill of it, she has been given two burnings, the blaze of desire and the burning of a spear wound in the breast.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are obscure as divided; the two fires may be desire and grief rather than any literal wound.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1518","lb":"CC1518","ev":"Kemasari, alone at dawn, laments to the absent Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1519":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1519,"t":["புனமா மயிலே பொழிலே புனலே","வனமார் வழையே வரையே திரையே","யினமா மணிசூ ழெரிபூ ணவனைத்","துனயான் பெறுகோ தொழுதே னுரையீர்."],"p":"","r":"She calls on the peacock of the field, the grove, the stream, the plantain, the hill and the waves, says she worships them, and asks whether through them she may find again the man of the fiery jewels.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1519","lb":"CC1519","ev":"Kemasari appeals to the creatures and places around her to help her find Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","மயில், பொழில், புனல்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1520":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1520,"t":["கொடுவெஞ் சிலைவாய்க் கணையிற் கொடிதாய்","நடுநா ளிரவின் னவைதான் மிகுமா","னெடுவெண் ணிலவின் னிமிர்தேர் பரியா","தடுமால் வழிநின் றறனே யருளாய்."],"p":"","r":"She says her pain, crueller than an arrow, grows at midnight, the chariot of the long white moonlight does not move on, and she asks Virtue standing on the way to show her grace.","n":"அறனே அருளாய் addresses Aram itself as a power that could end the night; she appeals not to a god but to virtue, which she implies her desertion has offended.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1520","lb":"CC1520","ev":"Kemasari, sleepless at midnight, appeals to Virtue.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி",""],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1521":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1521,"t":["கயலா லிவையென் றுகவிழ்ந் துகிடந்","தயலே னறியா மையுரைத் ததெலா","மியலா ததுவோ வினியேற் கினியீ","ருயலா வதுகண் மலர்கா ளுரையீர்."],"p":"","r":"Turning to her flower-like eyes, which he once likened to kayal fish, she says that all he said of her innocence has come to nothing and asks them to tell her how she may survive.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are hard to divide (கவிழ்ந்து கிடந்து, அயலேன் அறியாமை); the reading of what he said of her is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1521","lb":"CC1521","ev":"Kemasari, alone at dawn, laments to the absent Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","மயில், பொழில், புனல்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1522":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1522,"t":["நெறிநீர் வளையு நிழனித் திலமும்","பொறிநீ ரபுனைந் தெழுதிப் புகழும்","வெறிதா ரவனெவ் வழியே கினனீ","ரறிவீ ருரையீ ரமர்தோ ளிணைகாள்."],"p":"","r":"She asks her shoulders, on which he painted bangles and pearls and which he praised, which way the fragrant-garlanded one went, since they must know.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1522","lb":"CC1522","ev":"Kemasari questions her own shoulders about where Civakan went.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","மயில், பொழில், புனல்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1523":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1523,"t":["இழுதார் சுடர்வே லிளையா னகலத்","துழுதீ ருடன்வெம் முலைகாள் வயிரத்","தொழுவாய் விடையைத் தொடர்கிற் றிலிரென்","றழுதா டடமா கவணங் கிழையே."],"p":"","r":"She asks her breasts, which ploughed the chest of the young man of the bright spear, why they did not follow the bull when it left the diamond stall, and the jewelled one weeps.","n":"Civakan is the bull (விடை) leaving its stall; the image makes his going a matter of nature as much as of fault.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1523","lb":"CC1523","ev":"Kemasari reproaches her breasts and weeps.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","மயில், பொழில், புனல்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1524":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1524,"t":["தகைவா டியதன் னிழல்கண் ணுகுநீர்","வகைவா டிவருந் தியழு வதுகண்","டகையே லமர்தோ ழியழே லவரோ","பகையா பவரென் றனள்பான் மொழியே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing her own reflection, its beauty wasted, shedding tears and weeping, she tells it not to grieve and not to weep, her dear friend, asking whether he could ever be an enemy.","n":"She gives her image the consolation a friend would give her: அவரோ பகையாபவர், 'would he turn enemy?'. The hope she cannot hold for herself she offers to her reflection.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thunai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1524","lb":"CC1524","ev":"Kemasari takes her reflection for a weeping companion and comforts it.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","நிழல்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1525":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1525,"t":["வெறிமா லைகள்வீழ்ந் துநிலம் புதையப்","பொறிமா லைபுனை நிழல்கா ணலளாய்","நெறிநா டியபோ யினணீ டினள்கண்","டெறிவால் வளைகொண் டுவரும் மினியே."],"p":"","r":"When the fallen garlands covered the floor and she could no longer see her reflection, she thought it had gone to search out the way he took, and waited long, saying it would come back now.","n":"Line 4 (எறிவால் வளை கொண்டு வரும்) is obscure; it may say the reflection will come back bringing him, or her bangles.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1525","lb":"CC1525","ev":"Kemasari loses sight of her reflection under the fallen garlands and waits for it to return.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1526":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1526,"t":["மடமா மயிலே குயிலே மழலை","நடைமா ணனமே நலமார் கிளியே","யுடனா டுமெனை யனையென் றுருகாத்","தொடையாழ் மழலை மொழிசோர்ந் தனளே."],"p":"","r":"She calls on the peahen, the cuckoo, the stately swan and the parrot who played with her, melting as she calls them her own, until her speech, sweet as the yazh, fails.","n":"எனை அனை may be 'like me' or 'my mother'; either way she treats the birds as kin.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1526","lb":"CC1526","ev":"Kemasari calls on her pet birds until her voice fails.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","மயில், பொழில், புனல்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1527":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1527,"t":["மல்லுறை யலங்கன் மார்பன் பிரிவெனு மெரியுள் வீழ்ந்து","கல்லுறை நாகு வேய்த்தோட் கதிர்மணி முறுவற் செவ்வாய்","வில்லுறை புருவ மாதர் வெந்தனள் கிடப்ப மின்றோ","யில்லுறை தெய்வ நோக்கி யிரங்கிநின் றுரைக்கு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the woman, fallen into the fire of separation from the garlanded warrior, lies burnt, one of her household looks to the deity of the house and speaks in pity.","n":"The verse does not name who speaks to the house deity (இல்லுறை தெய்வம்); a companion is the likeliest, and the speech follows in 1528 and 1529.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1527","lb":"CC1527","ev":"While Kemasari lies spent, someone of her household turns to the house deity and speaks.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி","தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1528":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1528,"t":["புண்ணவாம் புலவு வாட்கைப் பொலன்கழற் புனைந்த பைந்தார்","கண்ணவாம் வனப்பி னானைக் காமனே கண்ட போழ்தும்","பண்ணவாம் பவளச் செவ்வாய்ப் படாமுலைப் பரவை யல்குற்","பெண்ணவா நிற்கு மென்றாற் பிணையனாட் குய்த லுண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks the house deity whether, when even Kama seeing the warrior's beauty would long to become a woman, this doe-like girl can survive.","n":"பெண் அவா நிற்கும்: Kama himself would long to be a woman for Civakan's sake, a hyperbole that makes her grief beyond blame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1528","lb":"CC1528","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","இல்லுறை தெய்வம்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1529":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1529,"t":["கடத்திடைக் கவளந் தேனெய் கனியைத்தோய்த் தினிய துற்றத்","தடக்கையாற் கொடுத்துப் புல்லுந் தவழ்மதக் களிறு நீங்கின்","மடப்பிடிக் குய்த லுண்டோ வாலடிக் குஞ்சி சூட்டுங்","கொடைக்கையான் பிரிந்த பின்றைக் கோதையாட் குய்த லுண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks whether a young cow elephant can live when the rutting bull who fed her honeyed morsels with his trunk has left her, and so whether the garlanded girl can live now that the generous-handed one has gone.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1529","lb":"CC1529","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","இல்லுறை தெய்வம்"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1530":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1530,"t":["முயங்கினான் சொன்ன வண்டாய் முகிழ்முலைத் தெய்வஞ் சேர","வுயங்குவா ளுணர்ந்து கேள்வற் கூனமும் பிரிவு மஞ்சி","யியங்குவா னின்ற வாவி தாங்கின ளென்ப போலும்","வயங்குபொன் னீன்ற நீல மாமணி முலையி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says that, taking the bee as the one her lover had spoken of, the woman turned to the deity and, understanding, fearing both harm to her husband and their parting, held back the life that was leaving her.","n":"She lives on for fear of harm (ஊனம்) to her husband, not only in hope of his return; the narrator hedges with என்ப போலும். முகிழ்முலைத் தெய்வம் சேர is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","karpu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1530","lb":"CC1530","ev":"Kemasari understands Civakan's words about the bee and resolves to live.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1531":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1531,"t":["வங்சவாய்க் காமன் சொன்ன மணிநிற வண்டு காணீர்","துஞ்சுவேன் றுயரந் தீரத் தொழுதகு தெய்வ மாவீர்","மஞ்சுதோய் செம்பொன் மாடத் தென்மனை தன்னு ளென்றாள்","பஞ்சிமேன் மிதிக்கும் போதும் பனிக்குஞ்சீ றடியி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"She tells all to see the gem-coloured bee that the deceiving Kama spoke of, and asks the bees to be gods worthy of worship in her golden mansion so that her grief may end.","n":"She calls Civakan காமன் and his mouth deceitful (வஞ்சவாய்), yet makes the bee he addressed an object of worship.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","pirivu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1531","lb":"CC1531","ev":"Kemasari takes the bees as household gods.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கேமசரி","சுரும்பு, வண்டு, தேன், ஞிமிறு"],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1532":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1532,"t":["நொந்தெடுக் கலாது வீங்கும் வனமுலை நுசுப்பிற் றேய்ந்தோர்","பந்தெடுக் கலாத நங்கை பால்கடை வெண்ணெய்ப் பாவை","வெந்துடன் வெயிலுற் றாங்கு மெலிந்துக விளங்கும் வெள்ளி","வந்துவா னிட்ட சுட்டி வனப்பொடு முளைத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The girl, so frail that a ball was too heavy for her, wasted like a butter doll left in the sun, and the morning star rose in the sky like a jewel on its brow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1532","lb":"CC1532","ev":"As Kemasari wastes, the morning star rises and the night ends.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:65"},"cc:1533":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1533,"t":["எரிநுதி யுற்ற மாவி னிளந்தளிர் போன்று மாழ்கிப்","புரிநரம் பிசையிற் றள்ளிப் புன்கணுற் றழுத லாலே","யரிகுரற் கொண்ட பூச லகத்தவர்க் கிசைப்ப வீண்டித்","திருவிரி கோதை நற்றாய் நிப்புதி சேர்ந்து சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Kemasari wilts like a mango shoot touched by fire and weeps in a voice that falters like a plucked string, and when the cry reaches the household her mother Nipputhi comes and speaks to her.","n":"This is the only place in the ilampakam where the mother is named; earlier chapters call her only நற்றாய்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1533","lb":"CC1533","ev":"Kemasari weeps aloud, and her mother Nipputhi comes to her and speaks.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி","நற்றாய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1534":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1534,"t":["விழுத்திணைப் பிறந்து வெய்ய வேட்கைவே ரரிந்து மெய்ந்நின்","றிழுக்கமொன் றானு மின்றி யெய்திய தவத்தின் வந்து","வழுக்குத லின்றி விண்ணோன் வச்சிர நுதியி னிட்ட","வெழுத்தனான் றந்த வின்ப மின்னுநீ பெறுதி யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The mother tells her that the joy given by her husband, who is as unfailing as letters cut by the god's vajra point, came to her from austerity she attained by being born in a noble house, cutting the root of fierce desire and standing in truth without a lapse, and that she will have it again.","n":"The consolation is karmic: the marriage is a reward already earned, so the parting cannot cancel it. The likeness of letters cut with the vajra makes Civakan himself, or his bond to her, the unalterable thing; the construction of line 3-4 allows either.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","inbam","vinai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1534","lb":"CC1534","ev":"Kemasari's mother consoles her weeping daughter.","cl":[["joy in this life is the fruit of austerity done before, and such fruit does not fail","general","reported",2,"kemasari_thaay"]],"ag":["நற்றாய்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நற்றாய்","கேமசரி"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1535":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1535,"t":["பிறங்கின கெடுங்கள் யாவும் புணர்ந்தவர் பிரிவர் பேசி","னிறங்கின வீழு மேலா யோங்கிய வெண்ணில் யோனிப்","பிறந்தவர் சாவர் செத்தார் பிறப்பவே யென்ன நோக்கிக்","கறங்கிசை வண்டு பாடுங் கோதைநீ கவல லென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She reminds her that all that rises falls, that those who join must part, and that among the countless kinds of birth those born die and the dead are born again, and tells her to see it so and not grieve.","n":"The mother answers a bride's grief for a living husband with the doctrine usually spoken over the dead: parting in love is set inside the round of birth and death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1535","lb":"CC1535","ev":"Kemasari's mother consoles her weeping daughter.","cl":[["whatever rises falls and whoever is joined parts","general","reported",1,"kemasari_thaay"],["those born die and the dead are born again through countless kinds of birth","general","reported",3,"kemasari_thaay"]],"ag":["நற்றாய்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நற்றாய்","கேமசரி"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1536":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1536,"t":["எரிதலைக் கொண்ட காமத் தின்பநீர்ப் புள்ளி யற்றாற்","பிரிவின்கட் பிறந்த துன்பம் பெருங்கட லனைய தொன்றா","லுருகிநைந் துடம்பு நீங்கி னிம்மயோ டும்மை யின்றி","யிருதலைப் பயனு மெய்தா ரென்றுயாங் கேட்டு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"She says, as something they have heard, that the pleasure of fiery desire is a drop of water while the grief born of parting is like the great sea, and that those who melt and leave the body from that grief gain the fruit of neither this life nor the next.","n":"The mother gives this as hearsay (யாங் கேட்டும்), not as her own doctrine; it turns the akam convention of the woman wasting with separation into a loss in both worlds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1536","lb":"CC1536","ev":"Kemasari's mother consoles her weeping daughter.","cl":[["the pleasure of desire is small and the grief of parting vast","general","reported",1,"kemasari_thaay"],["one who dies of grief at parting loses the fruit of both this world and the next","general","reported",3,"kemasari_thaay"]],"ag":["நற்றாய்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நற்றாய்","கேமசரி"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1537":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1537,"t":["மன்னுநீர் மொக்கு ளொக்கு மானிட ரிளமை யின்ப","மின்னினொத் திறக்குஞ் செல்வம் வெயிலுறு பனியி னீங்கு","மின்னிசை யிரங்கு நல்யா ழிளியினு மினிய சொல்லா","யன்னதால் வினையி னாக்க மழுங்குவ தென்னை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that the pleasures of youth are like a bubble on water, that wealth vanishes like lightning and melts like dew in the sun, and asks why, since the gains of deeds are such, her sweet-voiced daughter should grieve.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilamai","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1537","lb":"CC1537","ev":"Kemasari's mother consoles her weeping daughter.","cl":[["youth and wealth, the gains of deeds, are fleeting, so grieving their loss is pointless","general","reported",4,"kemasari_thaay"]],"ag":["நற்றாய்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நற்றாய்","கேமசரி"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1538":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1538,"t":["பஞ்சிறை கொண்ட பைம்பொற் கலைபுறஞ் சூழ்ந்து வைத்து","நஞ்சிறை கொண்ட நாகப் படம்பழித் தகன்ற வல்குல்","வெஞ்சிறைப் பள்ளி யாக விழுமுலைத் தடத்து வைகத்","தஞ்சிறைப் படுக்க லாதார் தம்பரி வொழிக வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She ends by saying that women who could not keep him captive in the prison of their girdled hips and great breasts should let their grief go.","n":"The sense of தஞ்சிறைப் படுக்க லாதார் is not clear: it may be a general saying that one who could not hold a man by her charms gains nothing by grieving, or a rueful turn on the daughter herself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1538","lb":"CC1538","ev":"Kemasari's mother consoles her weeping daughter.","cl":[],"ag":["நற்றாய்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நற்றாய்","கேமசரி"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1539":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1539,"t":["வாசமிக் குடைய தாரான் வண்டினுக் குரைத்த மாற்றப்","பாசத்தா லாக்கப் பட்ட வாவிய ளல்ல தெல்லாம்","பேசினார் பிணையன் மாலை பிசைந்திடப் பட்ட தொத்தா","டூசுலாம் பரவை யல்குற் றூமணிப் பாவை யன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Though they said everything to her, Kemasari was like a crushed garland, alive only by the bond of the words her husband had spoken to the bee.","n":"பேசினார் can be the consolers who said everything, which is how it is read here; the verse sets all their talk against the single covert sentence that actually keeps her alive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kurippu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1539","lb":"CC1539","ev":"Kemasari stays unconsoled, kept alive only by Civakan's words to the bee.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1540":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1540,"t":["பையர விழுங்கப் பட்ட பசுங்கதிர் மதிய மொத்து","மெய்யெரி துயரின் மூழ்க விதிர்விதிர்த் துருகி நையு","மையிருங் குழலி னாடன் மைந்தனை வலையிற் சூழ்ந்து","கையரிக் கொண்டுங் காணாள் காளையுங் காலிற் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Like the moon swallowed by the serpent, she trembles and melts in burning grief, the dark-haired one who had netted the youth and yet cannot find him though she gropes, while the youth goes on foot.","n":"The serpent-swallowed moon is the eclipse image; the verse turns from her to him in its last half-line, காளையுங் காலிற் சென்றான், and the story leaves the town with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1540","lb":"CC1540","ev":"Kemasari wastes in grief while Civakan goes on his way on foot.","cl":[],"ag":["கேமசரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1541":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1541,"t":["காழகச் சேற்றுட் டீம்பால் கதிர்மணிக் குடத்தி னேந்தி","வீழ்தரச் சொரிவ தேபோல் விளங்கொளித் திங்கட் புத்தேள்","சூழிருட் டொழுதி மூழ்கத் தீங்கதிர் சொரிந்து நல்லார்","மாழைகொண் முகத்திற் றோன்றி வளைகடன் முளைத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The bright moon god rises from the sea, pouring his sweet rays to drown the encircling dark as one might pour sweet milk from a jewelled pot into black mud, and appears like the faces of fair women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1541","lb":"CC1541","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1542":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1542,"t":["ஏறனாற் கிருளை நீங்கக் கைவிளக் கேந்தி யாங்கு","வீறுயர் மதியந் தோன்ற விரைவொடு போய பின்றை","மாறிலாப் பருதி வட்டம் வருதிரை முளைத்த வாங்க","ணாறுசெ லொருவற் கண்ண லணிகல மருள லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The moon having risen as if holding a hand-lamp to clear the dark for the bull-like man, and the sun's disc having risen from the waves after he hurried on, there the lord sets about giving ornaments to a man going along the road.","n":"ஏறனாற் is read as ஏறு அனாற்கு, 'for the one like a bull', i.e. Civakan; the moon is made his lamp for the night's walk.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1542","lb":"CC1542","ev":"After a night's walk, at sunrise, Civakan meets a wayfarer and begins to give him his ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1543":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1543,"t":["எவ்வூரீர் ரெப்பதிக்குப் போந்தீர்நும் மனைவியர்தா மெனைவர் மக்க","ளொவ்வாதார் தாமெனைவ ரொப்பார்மற் றெனைவர்நீ ருரைமி னென்றாற்","கிவ்வூரே னிப்பதிக்குப் போந்தேனென் மனைவியரு நால்வர் மக்க","ளொவ்வாதார் தாமில்லை யொப்பா னொருவனென வுரைத்தான் சான்றோன்."],"p":"","r":"Asked where he comes from, where he is going, how many wives he has and how many of his children are unlike and how many like him, the worthy man answers that he is of this town, bound for this place, has four wives, no children unlike him and one son like him.","n":"The questions in lines 1-2 are taken as Civakan's and the answer as the wayfarer's; the verse names only the answerer (சான்றோன்). 'This town' and 'this place' already hint that his journey is not one on the road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","makkal","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1543","lb":"CC1543","ev":"Civakan questions the wayfarer, who answers in a riddle.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1544":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1544,"t":["ஒப்பா னொருமகனே நால்வ ரொருவயிற்றுட் பிறந்தா னென்ன","நக்கான் பெருஞ்சான்றோ னம்பிபோல் யாருலகி லினியா ரென்ன","மிக்கா னுரைப்பதுவு மிக்கதே போலுமால் வினவிக் கேட்பேன்","றக்காய் குறித்த துரையென்றான் றானுரைப்பக் கேட்கின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"When Civakan wonders that the one son should be born of four from one womb, the great worthy laughs and praises him, and Civakan, saying the excellent man's words seem excellent indeed, asks him to tell what he meant and listens.","n":"The verse runs three speeches together without naming each speaker; the attribution of line 2's praise (நம்பிபோல் யாருலகி லினியார்) to the wayfarer and of lines 3-4 to Civakan is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1544","lb":"CC1544","ev":"Civakan, puzzled by the riddle, asks the wayfarer to explain it.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சான்றோன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1545":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1545,"t":["நற்றானஞ் சீல நடுங்காத் தவமறிவர் சிறப்பிந் நான்கு","மற்றாங்குச் சொன்ன மனைவியரிந் நால்வரவர் வயிற்றுட் டோன்றி","யுற்றா னொருமகனே மேற்கதிக்குக் கொண்டுபோ முரவோன் றன்னைப்","பெற்றார் மகப்பெற்றா ரல்லாதார் பிறர்மக்கள் பிறரே கண்டீர்."],"p":"","r":"The wayfarer explains that his four wives are giving, right conduct, unshaken austerity and the worship of the Omniscient, and that the one son born of them is the merit that carries a man to the higher state; those who have him have a son, and other people's children are only others.","n":"அறிவர் சிறப்பு, 'honour to the Knower', is the Jain fourfold of the householder (dana, sila, tapas, puja) spoken as a family; the riddle remakes the household itself as a set of virtues.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","ozhukkam","thavam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1545","lb":"CC1545","ev":"The wayfarer expounds his riddle to Civakan as a sermon on the layman's virtues.","cl":[["giving, right conduct, austerity and worship of the Omniscient beget the merit that carries one to a higher state","jain","asserted",1,"sanron"],["only that merit is truly one's child; children of the body belong to others","jain","asserted",4,"sanron"]],"ag":["சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1546":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1546,"t":["படநாகந் தோலுரித்தாற் போற்றுறந்து கண்டவர்மெய் பனிப்ப நோற்றிட்","டுடனாக வைம்பொறியும் வென்றார்க் குவந்தீத றான மாகுந்","திடனாகத் தீந்தேனுந் தெண்மட்டு முயிர்க்குழா மீண்டி நிற்றற்","கிடனாகு மூனுமிவை துறத்தலே சீலமென் றுரைத்தார் மிக்கோர்."],"p":"","r":"He says the great ones teach that giving is gladly giving to those who have renounced as a serpent sloughs its skin, done austerities that make onlookers shiver and conquered the five senses together, and that right conduct is giving up honey, toddy and meat, in which hosts of living things gather.","n":"The reason given for abstaining from honey and toddy as well as meat, that they are a place where living beings swarm (உயிர்க்குழா மீண்டி நிற்றற் கிடனாகும்), is the Jain one of harm to minute lives, not a reason of purity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pulaal","kal","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1546","lb":"CC1546","ev":"The wayfarer expounds his riddle to Civakan as a sermon on the layman's virtues.","cl":[["true giving is giving gladly to renouncers who have conquered the senses","jain","asserted",2,"sanron"],["right conduct is abstaining from honey, toddy and meat, because hosts of living beings dwell in them","jain","asserted",4,"sanron"]],"ag":["சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1547":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1547,"t":["ஓவா திரண்டுவவு மட்டமியும் பட்டினிவிட் டொழுக்கங் காத்த","றாவாத் தவமென்றார் தண்மதிபோன் முக்குடைக்கீழ்த் தாதை பாதம்","பூவே புகைசாந்தஞ் சுண்ணம் விளக்கிவற்றாற் புனைத னாளு","மேவா விவைபிறவும் பூசனையென் றீண்டியநூல் கரைகண் டாரே."],"p":"","r":"He says austerity is fasting without fail on the two moon days and the eighth day while guarding one's conduct, and that worship is adorning daily the feet of the Father under the moonlike triple umbrella with flowers, incense, sandal, powder and lamps, as those who crossed the scripture teach.","n":"The triple umbrella (முக்குடை) marks the Jina; here தவம் is a householder's calendar of fasts, not the forest ascetic's discipline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vazhipaadu","ozhukkam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1547","lb":"CC1547","ev":"The wayfarer expounds his riddle to Civakan as a sermon on the layman's virtues.","cl":[["a householder's austerity is fasting on the new and full moon days and the eighth day while keeping conduct","jain","asserted",1,"sanron"],["worship is offering flowers, incense, sandal, powder and lamps to the feet of the Lord under the triple umbrella","jain","asserted",3,"sanron"]],"ag":["சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1548":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1548,"t":["இந்நால்வர் துணைவியராக் காதன் மகனிவனா வுடையார் போகிப்","பொன்னார மார்பிற் புரந்தரராய்ப் பூமி முழுது மாண்டு","மன்னாகி முக்குடைக்கீழ் வாமன் சிறப்பியற்றி வரம்பி லின்பம்","பின்னா விளைவித்துப் பிறவா வுலகெய்தல் பேச லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have these four as consorts and this son as their beloved child, he says, go on to become Indras, then rule the whole earth as kings and honour the Lord under the triple umbrella, and at the end reach the world where there is no birth.","n":"The path described is graded: heaven, then kingship, then release (பிறவா வுலகு), which is the shape of Civakan's own life in the epic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","marumai","arasu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1548","lb":"CC1548","ev":"The wayfarer expounds his riddle to Civakan as a sermon on the layman's virtues.","cl":[["the layman's four virtues lead through rebirth as Indra and as an earthly king to release from birth","jain","asserted",4,"sanron"]],"ag":["சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1549":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1549,"t":["மட்டார்பூம் பிண்டி வளங்கெழு முக்குடைக்கீழ் மாலே கண்டீர்","முட்டாத வின்பப் புதாத்திறக்குந் தாளுடைய மூர்த்தி பாத","மெட்டானும் பத்தானு மில்லாதார்க் கிவ்வுலகி லின்ப மேபோ","லொட்டாவே கண்டீர் வினையவனைத் தேறாதார்க் குணர்ந்தீ ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He says the Lord under the triple umbrella of the honeyed asoka is the Great One whose feet open the gate of unfailing bliss, and that deeds do not cling to those who hold his feet, as pleasure in this world does not cling to the destitute, while those who do not trust him are bound.","n":"Line 3 (எட்டானும் பத்தானும் இல்லாதார்) and the construction of line 4 are hard; the comparison with the poor is a reading. மால் here names the Jina, not Vishnu: the pindi (asoka) and triple umbrella are his marks.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1549","lb":"CC1549","ev":"The wayfarer expounds his riddle to Civakan as a sermon on the layman's virtues.","cl":[["karma does not cling to those who hold to the feet of the Jina","jain","asserted",4,"sanron"]],"ag":["சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1550":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1550,"t":["வேற்றுவ ரில்லா நுமரூர்க்கே செல்லினும் வெகுண்டீர் போல","வாற்றுணாக் கொள்ளா தடிபுறத்து வைப்பீரே யல்லிர் போலுங்","கூற்றங்கொண் டோடத் தமியே கொடுநெறிக்கட் செல்லும் போழ்தி","னாற்றுணாக் கொள்ளீ ரழகலா லறிவொன்று மிலிரே போலும்."],"p":"","r":"He says that even going to their own kinsmen's town people take food for the way, yet when Death seizes them and drives them alone down the cruel road they take no provisions, as if they had no sense at all.","n":"The address shifts to the plural (கொள்ளீர், இலிரே): the sermon to one traveller becomes a sermon to all; the journey Civakan is making lends the figure of road provisions its point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aram","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1550","lb":"CC1550","ev":"The wayfarer expounds his riddle to Civakan as a sermon on the layman's virtues.","cl":[["merit is the provision one must take for the road of death, which one walks alone","general","asserted",4,"sanron"]],"ag":["சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1551":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1551,"t":["அளைவது காம மடுநறவு நெய்யொழுகு மூனும் பின்னா","விளைவது தீவினையே கண்டீ ரிவைமூன்றும் விடுமி னென்றாற்","றளையவிழ் கோதையார் தாமஞ்சேர் வெம்முலைபோல் வீங்கிக் கண்சேந்","துறைய வுறுதி யுரைப்பாரை யோஒபாவ முணரா ரேகாண்."],"p":"","r":"He says that lust, intoxicating drink and fat-dripping meat ripen afterwards only into evil deeds and must all three be given up, yet when told so people swell and redden with anger and, alas, do not understand those who speak for their good.","n":"The anger of the hearers is compared to women's swelling breasts, so the simile itself keeps desire before the reader while the verse condemns it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kal","pulaal","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1551","lb":"CC1551","ev":"The wayfarer expounds his riddle to Civakan as a sermon on the layman's virtues.","cl":[["lust, drink and meat ripen into evil deeds and must be renounced","general","asserted",2,"sanron"]],"ag":["சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1552":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1552,"t":["இழுதன்ன வெண்ணிணத்த செந்தடிக்கே யேட்டைப்பட் டிரும்பிற் போர்த்த","பழுதெண்ணும் வன்மனத்தா ரோட்டைமரச் செவியர் கேளார் பால்போன்","றறொழுகியமு தூறுநல் லறத்தை யோர்கிலரூன் செய்கோட் டக்குக்","கழுகுண்ண வள்ளூர மேசுமந்து புள்ளிற்கே புறஞ்செய் கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Hard-hearted men who hunt for red flesh with fat like butter, with ears of wood, do not listen to the good dharma that flows like milk and nectar; they carry a body fattened for the vultures to eat and serve only the birds.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","yaakkai","aram","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1552","lb":"CC1552","ev":"The wayfarer expounds his riddle to Civakan as a sermon on the layman's virtues.","cl":[["those who eat flesh fatten a body that will only feed vultures","general","asserted",4,"sanron"]],"ag":["சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1553":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1553,"t":["கையாற் பொதித்துணையே காட்டக் கயற்கண்ணா ளதனைக் காட்டா","ளையா விளாம்பழமே யென்கின்றீ ராங்கதற்குப் பருவ மன்றென்","செய்கோ வெனச்சிறந்தாள் போற்சிறவாக் கட்டுரையாற் குறித்தவெல்லாம்","பொய்யே பொருளுரையா முன்னே கொடுத்துண்டல் புரிமின் கண்டீர்."],"p":"","r":"In an obscure comparison of a woman who answers a request with an excuse that it is not the season, he declares that all fine-sounding words are false and bids his hearers give before they eat while there is time.","n":"Lines 1-3 (பொதித்துணை, விளாம்பழம், பருவம்) look like a proverb or scene now hard to recover; only the closing injunction, முன்னே கொடுத்துண்டல் புரிமின், is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","sol","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1553","lb":"CC1553","ev":"The wayfarer expounds his riddle to Civakan as a sermon on the layman's virtues.","cl":[["one should give to others before one eats oneself","general","asserted",4,"sanron"]],"ag":["சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1554":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1554,"t":["பனிமதி யின்கதிர் பருகு மாம்பல்போன்","முனிமதி முகத்தியர் முறுவ னம்பினார்","துனிவளர் கதிகளுட் டோன்றி நாடகங்","கனியநின் றாடுவர் கடையில் காலமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who trust the smiles of moon-faced women, he says, are born in the grief-filled states of birth and dance there, as on a stage, through time without end.","n":"கதிகள் are the Jain states (gati) of birth; the stage image makes rebirth a performance repeated without end. The verse is said to a man just married, and soon to marry again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","piravi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1554","lb":"CC1554","ev":"The wayfarer expounds his riddle to Civakan as a sermon on the layman's virtues.","cl":[["trusting women's smiles leads to endless rebirth in states of sorrow","jain","asserted",3,"sanron"]],"ag":["சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1555":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1555,"t":["நிழனிமிர் நெடுமதி நிகரி றீங்கதிர்ப்","பழனவெண் டாமரை பனிக்கு மாறுபோற்","குழனிமிர் கிளவியார் கோல மஞ்சினார்","தொழநிமிர்ந் தமரராய்த் துறக்க மாள்வரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who fear the beauty of women whose speech is like the flute, as the white lotus shrinks from the moon's rays, rise to be gods worshipped by others and rule heaven.","n":"1554 and 1555 are a pair: the water lily that drinks the moon's rays and the lotus that closes from them, so the same moon stands for women's beauty taken and refused.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","adakkam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1555","lb":"CC1555","ev":"The wayfarer expounds his riddle to Civakan as a sermon on the layman's virtues.","cl":[["those who shun women's beauty are reborn as gods and rule heaven","jain","asserted",4,"sanron"]],"ag":["சான்றோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1556":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1556,"t":["இன்னவா றுறுதி கூறி யெரிமணி வயிர மார்ந்த","பொன்னவிர் கலங்க ளெல்லாம் பொலிவொடு புகன்று நீட்டிச்","சென்மினீ ரென்று கூற வலங்கொண்டு தொழுது சென்றான்","வின்மரீஇ நீண்ட தோளான் வெயிற்கட நீந்த லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When the wayfarer had given this counsel, Civakan gladly held out all his gem-set gold ornaments, and at the word 'go' circled him in reverence, bowed and left, and the long-armed bowman set out across the sun-scorched wilderness.","n":"The verse does not name the subjects of நீட்டி, கூற and சென்றான்; it may instead be the wayfarer who, given the ornaments, circles Civakan and goes. The reading here follows 1542, where Civakan begins to give the ornaments, and makes the gift the fee for the teaching.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1556","lb":"CC1556","ev":"Civakan gives the wayfarer his ornaments, takes leave of him with reverence and crosses the wilderness.","cl":[],"ag":["சான்றோன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:66"},"cc:1557":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1557,"t":["இரங்கு மேகலை யல்கு லின்கனித் தொண்டையந் துவர்வா","யரங்கக் கூத்திக ணன்பின் மனையவட் டுறந்துசெல் பவர்போற்","பரந்த தீம்புனன் மருதம் பற்றுவிட் டினமயி லகவு","மரங்கொல் யானையின் மதநா றருஞ்சுர மவன்செலற் கெழுந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Like men who leave behind the stage dancers with their girdles and red lips and the loving wife of the house, Civakan gives up the marutam land of sweet waters, where peacock flocks call, and sets out for the hard wasteland that smells of the musth of tree-felling elephants.","n":"The simile for his leaving is the man who renounces both courtesan and wife (அன்பின் மனையவள் துறந்து செல்பவர்), set just after he has left Kemasari in the night; the word துறந்து looks ahead to the renunciation the patikam promises.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thuravu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1557","lb":"CC1557","ev":"Civakan sets out from the marutam country into the wasteland.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1558":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1558,"t":["கலவ மாமயி லெருத்திற் கடிமல ரவிழ்ந்தன காயா","வுலக மன்னவன் றிருநா ளொளிமுடி யணியந்துநின் றவர்போற்","பலவும் பூத்தன கோங்கம் பைந்துகின் முடியணிந் தவர்பின்","னுலவு காஞ்சுகி யவர்போற் பூத்தன மரவமங் கொருங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The kaya blossoms on the peacock's neck-like slopes, the kongu trees flower like men wearing bright crowns on the king's festival day, and the maravam trees flower together like the chamberlains in cloth turbans who walk behind them.","n":"The forest is described in the terms of a royal procession, so the landscape the hero walks through is dressed as the court he will later rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1558","lb":"CC1558","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1559":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1559,"t":["ஓங்கு மால்வரை வரையா டுழக்கலி னுடைந்துகு பெருந்தேன்","றாங்கு சந்தனந் தளரத் தழுவி வீழ்வன தகைசா","லாங்கண் மாலுல களப்பா னாழி சங்கமொ டேந்தி","தேங்கொண் மார்பிடைத் திளைக்குஞ் செம்பொ னாரமொத் துளவே."],"p":"","r":"Great honeycombs broken by mountain goats on the high hills spill and run down the sandalwood trunks, like the red-gold chain that plays on the chest of Vishnu who holds the discus and conch.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1559","lb":"CC1559","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1560":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1560,"t":["வீழ்ந்து வெண்மழை தவழும் விண்ணுறு பெருவரை பெரும்பாம்","பூழ்ந்து தோலுரிப் பனபோ லொத்த மற்றவற் றருவி","தாழ்ந்து வீழ்ந்தவை முழவிற் றதும்பின மதுகரம் பாடச்","சூழ்ந்து மாமயி லாடி நாடகந் துளக்குறுத் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Waterfalls on the cloud-crossed peaks look like great snakes shedding their skins, beat like drums as they fall, and to the bees' song the peacocks gather and dance a drama.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1560","lb":"CC1560","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1561":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1561,"t":["கருவி தேனெனத் தூங்குங் கதிரணி யிறுங்கொடு தினைசூழ்","பொருவில் யானையின் பழுப்போற் பொங்கு காய்க்குலை யவரை","யருவி யைவனங் கரும்பு மடக்கருங் கவைக்கதிர் வரகு","முருவ வெட்பய றுழுந்து மல்லவு மெல்லையின் றுளவே."],"p":"","r":"Millet with heads hanging like honeycombs, avarai pods in clusters like an elephant's ripe fruit, hill rice, sugarcane, varagu, white gram, black gram and other crops grow there without limit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1561","lb":"CC1561","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1562":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1562,"t":["யானை வெண்மருப் புலக்கை யறையுர லைவன மிடித்த","தேனெய் வாசவற் குவவித் தீங்கனிவாழையின் பழனு","முனை யுண்டவருருகும் பசுந்தினைப் பிண்டியு மொருங்கே","மானி னோக்கியர் நோக்கி வழிதொறு மீவதவ் வழியே."],"p":"","r":"Along every stretch of the road, doe-eyed women give travellers hill rice pounded with an ivory pestle in a rock mortar, honey, sweet plantains and fresh millet cakes that melt those who eat them.","n":"The giving is the hill people's, not the hero's: the road itself is a place of hospitality.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1562","lb":"CC1562","ev":"The hill women give food to those who pass along the road.","cl":[],"ag":["மானி னோக்கியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1563":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1563,"t":["குறிஞ்சி யெல்லையி னீங்கிக் கொடிமுல்லை மகண்மொழிந் தாடச்","செறிந்த பொன்னிதழ்ப் பைந்தார்க் கொன்றையஞ் செல்வதற்குக் குரவ","மறிந்து பாவையைக் கொடுப்பத் தோன்றி யஞ்சுட ரேந்து","நிறைந்த பூங்குருந் துகுதே னீர்பெய் தார்த்தன சுரும்பே."],"p":"","r":"Leaving the bounds of the kurinji, the mullai land appears: the jasmine creeper dances as if speaking, the kuravam offers its flower-dolls to the golden konrai, the tonri holds up its lamp, and bees hum as the full kurundham drips honey.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1563","lb":"CC1563","ev":"Civakan passes from the hill country into the forest-pasture land.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1564":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1564,"t":["அரக்குண் பஞ்சிக டிரட்டி யருமணி மரகதப் பலகைப்","பரப்பி யிட்டன போலக் கோபங்கள் பயிர்மிசைப் பரவ","வுரைத்த மென்றயிர்ப் பித்தைக் கோவலர் தீங்குழ லுலவ","நிரைக்கண் மாமணி கறங்க நீணிலங் கடந்தன னெடியோன்."],"p":"","r":"With cochineal insects spread over the crops like lac-dyed cotton on an emerald board, herdsmen with curd-smeared hair playing sweet flutes and the cattle bells ringing, the tall one crosses the long land.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1564","lb":"CC1564","ev":"Civakan crosses the pastoral country.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1565":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1565,"t":["வள்ளி வாரிய குழியின் வளர்பொன்னும் வயிரமு மிமைக்குஞ்","சுள்ளி வேலியி னீங்கித் துறக்கம்புக் கிடுமெனச் சூழ்ந்து","வெள்ளி வெண்டிரள் விசித்து நிலத்தொடு தறிபுடைத் தவைபோற்","றுள்ளி வீழுய ரருவி வனகிரி தோன்றிய தவணே."],"p":"","r":"There appears the mountain Vanagiri, where gold and diamonds glitter in the pits dug for valli roots, and whose leaping waterfalls look like strands of white silver fastened from sky to ground.","n":"The waterfalls seem to set out to enter heaven (துறக்கம் புக்கிடும் என); the name of the mountain is given here, ahead of the grove in which the meeting takes place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1565","lb":"CC1565","ev":"Vanagiri comes into view.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1566":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1566,"t":["அண்ண றான்செலு முன்னா லணிமலர்ப் பூம்பொழி லதனுள்","வண்ண மாச்சுனை மாநீர் மணிதெளித் தனையது ததும்பித்","தண்ணென் றாமரை கழுநீர் நீலத் தாதவி ழாம்ப","லெண்ணில் பன்மலர் கஞலி யினவண்டு பாண்முரன் றுளதே."],"p":"","r":"Ahead of where he goes lies a flowering grove with a pool as clear as washed gems, crowded with lotus, red lily, blue lily and white water lily, with bees humming like bards.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1566","lb":"CC1566","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1567":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1567,"t":["கானத்தி னேகு கின்றான் கடிபொழிற் கவின்கண் டெய்தித்","தானத்தி லிருந்த லோடுந் தையலா ளொருத்தி தானே","வானத்தி னிழிந்து வந்த வானவர் மகளு மொப்பா","ணானமும் பூவுஞ் சாந்து நாறவந் தருகு நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Going through the forest, he sees the beauty of the fragrant grove and sits down there, and at once a young woman like a daughter of the gods come down from the sky stands near him, breathing scent of musk, flowers and sandal.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1567","lb":"CC1567","ev":"Civakan sits in the grove and a woman like a celestial appears and stands near him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1568":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1568,"t":["குறிஞ்சிப்பூங் கோதை போலுங் குங்கும முலையி னாட","னிறைந்தபொற் கலாபந் தோன்ற நெடுந்துகில் விளிம்பொன் றேந்திச்","செறிந்ததோர் மலரைக் கிள்ளித் தெறித்திடாச் சிறிய நோக்கா","நறும்புகைத் தூது விட்டு நகைமுகங் கோட்டி நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her breasts dressed in saffron and her garland like kurinji flowers, she shows her golden girdle, lifts one edge of her long cloth, pinches a flower without flicking it, sends her glance as a messenger like fragrant smoke and stands with her smiling face turned aside.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1568","lb":"CC1568","ev":"The woman shows herself to him with signs of invitation.","cl":[],"ag":["அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1569":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1569,"t":["அணிகல வரவத் தாலு மமிழ்துறழ் நாற்றத் தாலும்","பணிவருஞ் சிங்க நோக்கிற் பணையெருத் துறழ நோக்கி","மணிமலர் நாகஞ் சார்ந்து வழையொடு மரவ நீழற்","றுணிவருஞ் சாய னின்றா டோன்றறன் கண்ணிற் கண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Drawn by the sound of her ornaments and her nectar-like scent, he turns his broad neck with a lion's look and sees with his eyes the one of matchless grace standing in the shade of the naga, vazhai and maravam trees.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1569","lb":"CC1569","ev":"Civakan turns and sees the woman.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1570":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1570,"t":["கண்டவன் கண்ணி னோக்க நடுங்கித்தன் காதிற் றாழ்ந்த","குண்டலஞ் சுடர வொல்கிக் கொடிநடுக் குற்ற தொப்ப","நுண்டுகிற் போர்வை சோர நுழைமழை மின்னி னிற்ப","வெண்டிசை மருங்கு நோக்கி யியக்கிகொ லிவண்மற் றென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When his eyes meet hers she trembles, her earrings flash as she sways like a shaken creeper and her fine cloth slips, and he, looking all around, asks whether she is a yakshi.","n":"இயக்கி (a yakshi) is his first guess at what the forest beauty is; the epic leaves it as his guess, and in 1580 she gives another account of herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1570","lb":"CC1570","ev":"Civakan, seeing her tremble, wonders aloud whether she is a yakshi.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1571":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1571,"t":["எண்ணத்தி லியக்கி யென்றே சோ?யிருப்பமற் றெழுத லாகா","வண்ணப்பூங் கண்க ளம்பா சோ?வாணுதற் புருவம் வில்லா","வுண்ணிறை யுடைய வெய்வா னுருவச்சா தகத்துக் கேற்பப்","பெண்ணலங் கிடந்த பேதை சோ?பெண்ணலங் கனிய நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"While he sits holding the thought that she is a yakshi, she stands with her flower-eyes as arrows and her brow as a bow, as if to shoot at the man of inner restraint, ripe with a woman's beauty.","n":"உண்ணிறை உடைய, 'having inner restraint', is said of the target: the hero's nirai is what she aims at.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nirai","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1571","lb":"CC1571","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1572":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1572,"t":["முறுவன்முன் சிறிய தோற்றா முகைநெறித் தனைய வுண்கட்","குறுநெறி பயின்ற கூந்தல் குறும்பல்கா லாவிக் கொள்ளாச்","சிறுநுதற் புருவ மேற்றாச்சோ? துகிற் றானை சோர","வறியுந ராவி போழு மநங்கனைங் கணையு மெய்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Without letting her smile show first, with eyes like a bud split open, hair in short curls and her small brow raised as her cloth slips, she lets fly even Ananga's five arrows that pierce the lives of the knowing.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1572","lb":"CC1572","ev":"The woman tries her charms on Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1573":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1573,"t":["வடுப்பிள வனைய கண்ணாள் வல்லவ னெழுதப் பட்ட","படத்திடைப் பாவை போன்றார் நோக்கின ளாகி நிற்ப","வடிப்பொலிந் தார்க்குஞ் செம்பொ னணிமணிக் கழலி னானம்","மடத்தகை குறிப்பு நோக்கி மனத்திது சிந்திக் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As the woman with eyes like split mango stands looking like a doll in a picture drawn by a master, the hero with the ringing golden anklet sees her innocent intent and thinks this in his mind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1573","lb":"CC1573","ev":"Civakan reads her intent and begins to reflect.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1574":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1574,"t":["கடிமாலை சூடிக் கருப்பூர முக்கித்","தொடைமாலை மென்முலையார் தோடோய்ந்த மைந்தர்","கடைமாலை மற்றவரே கண்புதைப்பச் செல்லு","நடைமாலைத் திவ்வுலக நன்றரோ நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"He asks his heart whether this world is good, where men who wore garlands, dabbed camphor and lay on the shoulders of soft-breasted women at their last evening have their eyes closed by those same women and go.","n":"The metre changes to a venba-like quatrain, and the refrain நெஞ்சே, kept through 1576, turns the hero's reply to temptation into a meditation addressed to himself; the women's hands that caressed are the hands that close the dead man's eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kaamam","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1574","lb":"CC1574","ev":"","cl":[["the pleasures of women in this world end at death, when the beloved herself closes the lover's eyes, so the world is not good","general","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நெஞ்சு"],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1575":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1575,"t":["நாவி யகல மெழுதி நறுநுதலா","ராவி தளிர்ப்ப வவர்தோண்மேற் றுஞ்சினார்","தூவியொழி புள்ளிற் றோன்றித் துயருழப்பக்","காவிநெடுங் கண்புதைத் தாங்ககல்வர் நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"He tells his heart that men who painted musk on the chests of fair-browed women and slept on their shoulders as their lives quickened will suffer like birds that have lost their feathers, and go when their women cover their long lily-eyes.","n":"தூவியொழி புள்ளில் தோன்றி, 'appearing as a bird with its feathers gone', may be either old age or a rebirth; the verse does not say which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kaamam","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1575","lb":"CC1575","ev":"","cl":[["those who sleep in women's arms come to suffering and death","general","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நெஞ்சு"],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1576":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1576,"t":["இன்புகை யார்ந்த விழுதார்மென் பள்ளிமே","லன்புருகு நல்லா ரவர்தோண்மேற் றுஞ்சினார்","தம்புலன்கள் குன்றித் தளரத்தங் காதலா","ரன்புருகு கண்புதைத் தாங்க கல்வர் நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"He tells his heart that men who slept on soft beds full of sweet smoke on the shoulders of good women melting with love will go, their senses failing, when their own beloved, melting with love, cover their eyes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1576","lb":"CC1576","ev":"","cl":[["the senses fail and the lover dies in the beloved's arms","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நெஞ்சு"],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1577":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1577,"t":["என்பினை நரம்பிற் பின்னி யுதிரந்தோய்த் திறைச்சி மெத்திப்","புன்புறந் தோலைப் போர்த்து மயிர்புறம் பொலிய வேய்ந்திட்","டொன்பது வாயி லாக்கி யூன்பயில் குரம்பை செய்தான்","மன்பெருந் தச்ச னல்லன் மயங்கினார் மருள வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says that the one who wove bone with sinew, soaked it in blood, packed it with flesh, covered it with a thin skin, thatched it over with hair and made it a hut of flesh with nine doors is a cunning great carpenter, who makes the deluded more deluded.","n":"மன்பெருந் தச்சன் அல்லன் can be divided as நல்லன் ('is skilled') or அல்லன் ('is no'); with 1578, which names the builder வினைப்பெருந் தச்சன், the builder is the deed, not a god, and the praise of his skill is ironic.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","pedhaimai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1577","lb":"CC1577","ev":"","cl":[["the body is a hut of bone, sinew, blood, flesh, skin and hair with nine openings, built so as to delude","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நெஞ்சு"],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1578":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1578,"t":["வினைப்பெருந் தச்ச னல்லன் மெய்ம்மைநா நோக்க லுற்றா","புலெனக்குற்றுக் கிடந்த தென்றாங் கிருகணும் புதைத்து வைக்கு","நினைப்பினாற் பெரிய ரென்னா னீந்தினார் கலைக ளென்னான்","மனத்தையுங் குழையச் செத்து மாண்பினன் மாதோ வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says the deed is the great carpenter and a skilled one: when we begin to look at the truth it covers both our eyes as if the thing were ours, takes no account of those great in thought or those who have swum through learning, and so works that even the mind melts.","n":"The text of line 2 is damaged (see source_issues), so the sense of 'covers both eyes' is reconstructed. Read with 1574 to 1576, the eyes closed by the beloved at death and the eyes closed by the deed in life are the same image.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pedhaimai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1578","lb":"CC1578","ev":"","cl":[["one's deeds blind a person to the truth about the body, and neither wisdom nor learning protects against it","general","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நெஞ்சு"],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1579":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1579,"t":["என்றவ னிருப்ப மாத ரென்வர விசைப்பி னல்லா","புலொன்றுமற் றுருகல் செல்லா னென்றெடுத் தோது கின்றாண்","மன்றலந் தோழி மாருள் வனத்திடைப் பண்ணை யாடக்","குன்றிடைக் குளிர்க்கு மின்போற் குழாமழை முகட்டிற் செல்வான்."],"p":"","r":"As he sits so, the woman, thinking he will not melt at all unless she tells him how she came, begins: while she played in the forest among her bridal companions, a man went off like lightning cooling on a hill and rising to the top of the clouds.","n":"The narrator gives her motive before her story: she tells it to make him melt, so the story is offered as a means of seduction and may not be true.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1579","lb":"CC1579","ev":"The woman begins to tell Civakan how she came to the forest.","cl":[],"ag":["அநங்கமா வீணை","விஞ்சையன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அநங்கமா வீணை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1580":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1580,"t":["மயிலின மிரிய வாங்கோர் மடமயி றழுவிக் கொண்ட","வெயிலிளஞ் செல்வன் போல விஞ்சைய னெற்கொண் டேகத்","துயிலிய கற்பி னாடன் றுணைவிகண் டிடுவித் திட்டா","ளயிலியல் காட்டுள் வீழ்ந்தே னநங்கமா வீணை யென்பேன்."],"p":"","r":"She says a Vidyadhara carried her off as the young sun bears away a peahen while the flock scatters, his wife of unsleeping chastity saw it and had her thrown down, and she fell into this spear-sharp forest; her name is Anangamaa Veenai.","n":"In her account the wife's karpu is the force that removes a rival; துயிலிய கற்பு is unclear ('sleeping' or 'unsleeping' chastity) and may be ironic.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","karpu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1580","lb":"CC1580","ev":"Anangamaa Veenai tells Civakan that a Vidyadhara abducted her and his wife cast her into the forest.","cl":[],"ag":["அநங்கமா வீணை","விஞ்சையன்","துணைவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அநங்கமா வீணை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:67"},"cc:1581":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1581,"t":["தாயிலாக் குழவி போலச் சாதுய ரெய்து கின்றேன்","வேயுலாந் தோளி னார்தம் விழுத்துணைக் கேள்வ நிற்கண்","டாயினேன் றுறக்கம் பெற்றே னளித்தரு ளாது விட்டாற்","றீயினு ளமிர்தம் பெய்தாங் கென்னுயிர் செகுப்ப லென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The woman pleads that she suffers like a motherless child, that seeing him, the cherished husband of women with bamboo-like shoulders, she has reached heaven, and that if he leaves her without grace she will destroy her life, as if nectar were poured into fire.","n":"She has just told him (1580) that she is a woman dropped in the forest by a Vidyadhara; the husband's lament that follows shows she is a married woman who has hidden from her husband, so the plea is an attempt on another man's wife's part to seduce the hero.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1581","lb":"CC1581","ev":"The woman in the grove begs Civakan to take her and threatens to die if he refuses.","cl":[],"ag":["அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அநங்கமா வீணை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1582":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1582,"t":["மணியெழு வனைய தோளும் வரையென வகன்ற மார்புந்","தணிவருங் கயத்துப் பூத்த தாமரை யானைய கண்ணும்","பணிவரும் பருதி யன்ன முகமுமென் றயர்ந்து காமப்","பிணியெழுந் தவலிக் கின்ற பேதைநீ கேளி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan answers the girl sick with desire, who has fainted thinking of his shoulders like bars of gems, his chest broad as a hill, his lotus eyes and his sun-like face: listen to this.","n":"He first recites her praise of his own body, so that the lecture that follows takes apart the very features she desires, starting with his and moving to hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","yaakkai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1582","lb":"CC1582","ev":"Civakan turns to answer the woman's advance.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1583":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1583,"t":["போதொடு நான மூழ்கிப் பூம்புகை தவழ்ந்து முல்லைக்","கோதைகண் படுக்குங் கூந்தல் குரைவளி பித்தோ டையே","யேதஞ்செய் மலங்க ணெய்த்தோ ரிறைச்சியென் பீருண் மூளை","கோதஞ்செய் குடர்கள் புன்றோ னரம்பொடு வழும்பி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says that the hair which is bathed in flowers and musk, wreathed in fragrant smoke and where jasmine garlands sleep is in truth wind, bile and phlegm, harmful filth, blood, flesh, bone, fat, marrow, entrails, mean skin, sinews and grease.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are the love poet's adorned hair; lines 3 and 4 strip it in one breath into the three humours and the body's tissues, so that the sentence turns at குரைவளி பித்தோ டையே.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1583","lb":"CC1583","ev":"","cl":[["the adorned body is only humours and foul matter beneath its scents and flowers","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1584":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1584,"t":["விழுக்கொடு வெண்ணஞ் சல்லா வுகிர்மயி ருமிழ்கட் பீளைப்","புழுப்பயில் குரம்பை பொல்லாத் தடிதடித் கீழ்ந்த போழ்தில்","விழித்தியார் நோக்கு கிற்பார் பிள்ளையார் கண்ணுட் காக்கை","கொழிப்பாரும் பொன்னிற் றோன்றுங் கொள்கைத்தாற் கொடியே யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says it is a worm-filled hut of fat, white poison, nails, hair and the rheum the eyes spit out; when the miserable flesh is cut and split open, who could bear to look, with crows pecking at the eyes, though by its nature it appears like gold, O creeper.","n":"Line 2 (தடிதடித் கீழ்ந்த) and line 3 (பிள்ளையார் கண்ணுட் காக்கை) are hard to construe; the general sense, the opened body as carrion, is clear, but the precise picture is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1584","lb":"CC1584","ev":"","cl":[["the body is a worm-infested hut that only appears golden","general","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1585":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1585,"t":["உருவமென் றுரைத்தி யாயி னிறைந்ததோற் றுருத்தி தன்னைப்","புருவமுங் கண்ணு மூக்கும் புலப்பட வெழுதி வைத்தாற்","கருதுவ தங்கொன் றுண்டோ காப்பியக் கவிகள் காம","வெரியெழ விகற்பித் திட்டா ரிறைச்சிப்போ ரிதனை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says that if she calls it beauty, it is a full bag of skin on which brows, eyes and nose have been painted to be seen: is there anything in it worth a thought? The poets of kavya have dressed up this mass of flesh so that the fire of desire may rise.","n":"காப்பியக் கவிகள் names the kind of poem the Cintamani itself is: the hero condemns the genre that has just spent many verses (1482-1484 and the descriptions of this grove) doing what he describes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","azhagu","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1585","lb":"CC1585","ev":"","cl":[["beauty is paint on a skin bag, and poets elaborate the flesh in order to kindle desire","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1586":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1586,"t":["காதன் மாமன் மடமகளே கருங்குழன் மேல்வண் டிருப்பினு","மேத முற்று முரியு நுசுப்பென் றுன்னியல் பேத்துவே","னோதம் போல வுடன்றுடன்று நைய நீயொண் டாமரைக்","கோதை பேறால்வா யொளித்தொழிதல் கொம்பே குண னாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The husband, searching, cries to the young daughter of his loving uncle that he would praise her nature, saying that her waist would break even if a bee sat on her dark hair; while he wastes in pain like the surging sea, is it a virtue in her, the creeper, to hide herself away?","n":"The metre changes here and the voice is new: the man is named only at 1594. Line 4 (பேறால்வா யொளித்தொழிதல்) says she hid herself; he takes her vanishing as a game or a cruelty, not yet as a theft.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vaazhkkaithunai","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1586","lb":"CC1586","ev":"A man searching the grove laments to his vanished wife, his uncle's daughter.","cl":[],"ag":["பவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவதத்தன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1587":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1587,"t":["வண்ணத் திங்கண் மதிமுகத்த வாளோ கருங்க யல்களோ","வுண்ணுங் கூற்றோ வொளிவேலோ போதோ வுணர்க லேனாற்","பண்ணின் றீஞ்சொலாய் படாமுலைப் பாவாய் கொடியே பாங்கி","ணுண்ணுந் தேனே யமிர்தே யென்னின் னுயிரே யெங்கணாயோ."],"p":"","r":"He asks whether her moon face holds swords or dark carp, a devouring Death, a bright spear or flowers, he cannot tell, and calls her sweet-voiced, doll with firm breasts, creeper, honey, nectar, his own life: where has she gone?","n":"The stock similes for eyes that Civakan has just called the poets' paint return at once in the husband's mouth, unanswered.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1587","lb":"CC1587","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவதத்தன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1588":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1588,"t":["இலவம் போதே ரெழிற்றகைய சீறடிக ளஞ்சி யொல்கிப்","புலவன் சித்தி ரித்த பொற்சிலம்பு நகப்பூ நிலத்துமே","லுலவும் போழ்து மென்னாவி மலர்மேன் மிதித்தொ துங்குவாய்","கலவ மஞ்ஞை யனையாய் கட்காத லொழிகல் லேனால்."],"p":"","r":"He says that her small feet, fair as silk-cotton buds, falter in fear, so that when she walks the golden anklets the craftsman made laugh at the flowers on the ground, and she steps aside onto flowers; peacock-like woman, he cannot give up the love in his eyes.","n":"Line 3 seems to say she treads on his life as on flowers (என்னாவி மலர்மேன் மிதித்து), a lover's conceit; the construction is loose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1588","lb":"CC1588","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவதத்தன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1589":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1589,"t":["பணிசெ யாயத்துப் பந்தாடு கின்றாயைக் கண்டு மாழ்கிப்","பிணிசெய் நோயேன் யான்கிடப் பப்பிறர் வாயது கேட்டலுந்","துணிக போது மென விடுத்தாய் போந்தேன் றுயரு ழப்பநீ","மணிசெய் மேகலை யாய்மாற் றந்தாராய் மறைந்தொ ழிதியோ."],"p":"","r":"He recalls that he saw her playing ball among her attendants and swooned, and while he lay lovesick she, hearing of it from others, sent word to be resolved and come; he came, and now, while he suffers, she of the jewelled girdle gives no answer and hides.","n":"The courtship he recalls was her choice as much as his (she sent the word துணிக போதும்), which sharpens the irony of her present flight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kalaviyal","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1589","lb":"CC1589","ev":"The husband recalls how their love began and complains that she will not answer.","cl":[],"ag":["பவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவதத்தன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1590":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1590,"t":["இயக்கி நின்னோ டிணையொக்கு மென்று நலஞ்செ குப்பான்","மயக்கிக் கொண்டு போய்வைத்தா யென்மா தரைத்தந் தருளுநீ","நயப்ப வெல்லாந் தருவலெனத் தொழுது நல்லி யானைதன்","வயப்பிடி கெடுத்து மாழாந்த தொப்ப மதிம யங்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"Addressing a yakshi, he says she must have bewitched his wife and carried her off to destroy her beauty, thinking it equal to her own, and begs her to give back his lady, promising all she desires; worshipping, he grows bewildered like a good elephant that has lost its strong mate.","n":"Line 4 shifts to the narrator's simile. Civakan had wondered at 1570 whether the woman herself was a yakshi (இயக்கிகொல்); now the husband blames a yakshi for taking her, and Civakan will use the same belief at 1600.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","pirivu","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1590","lb":"CC1590","ev":"The husband prays to a yakshi he believes has stolen his wife.","cl":[],"ag":["பவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவதத்தன்","இயக்கி"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1591":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1591,"t":["மல்லற் றெல்வ ளத்து மத்திமநன் னாட்டுவண் டாமரை","புல்லும் பேரூர்ப் புகழ்த்தத்தன் காதற் சினதத் தைக்குஞ்","செல்வநா மற்குஞ் சித்திரமா மாலைக் குஞ்சுற் றத்தார்க்கு","மல்லல் செய்தே னவட் சென்றா லென்னுரைக்கே னென்செய் கேனே."],"p":"","r":"He says that he has brought grief to famous Thathan of the fertile middle country's great town where bees embrace the lotus, to his dear Sinadattai, to Selvanaman, to Chittiramamalai and to all their kin: if he goes back, what will he say, what will he do?","n":"The kin are named but their relation to him and to her (whose parents are which) is not stated in the verse and the division of the names (செல்வநா மற்கு) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","pirivu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1591","lb":"CC1591","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவதத்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1592":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1592,"t":["உண்ணு நீர்வேட் டசைந்தே னெனவுரைப்பக் காட்டு ணாடி","நண்ணிப் பொய்கை தலைப்பட்டு நற்றா மரையி லையினுட்","பண்ணி நீர்கொண்டு வந்தேன் படாமு லைப்பா வாயென்","றண்ண லாற்றா தழுதழுது வெந்துருகி நைகின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He says she told him she was faint with thirst, so he searched the forest, came upon a pond and brought water folded in a fine lotus leaf; and the noble man, unable to bear it, weeps and weeps, burns, melts and wastes.","n":"This is the first plain account of how she came to be alone, and it contradicts the story she told Civakan at 1580 of being carried off by a Vidyadhara and dropped in the forest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vaazhkkaithunai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1592","lb":"CC1592","ev":"The husband tells that his wife sent him for water and was gone when he returned.","cl":[],"ag":["பவதத்தன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவதத்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1593":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1593,"t":["குழைகொள் வாண்முகத்துக் கோல்வளையைக் காணான் குழைந்தழு கின்ற","வழகன் சொல்லுமணி செய்கோதை காமமுங் கண்டுங் கேட்டு","முழவுத் தோளான் முறுவ லித்தீங் கேயிரு நீயென்","றிழையச் சொல்லி யிளையா னிளையானை யெய்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Not finding his bangled wife, the handsome man wept and spoke; the young man with drum-like shoulders, having seen and heard the jewel-garlanded woman's desire, smiled, said softly 'stay here', and went to the young man.","n":"Whom Civakan tells to stay (இருநீ) is not said; the sense that fits is the hidden woman. His smile marks that he alone knows both sides of the story.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1593","lb":"CC1593","ev":"Civakan smiles, bids someone stay where they are, and goes to the weeping husband.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1594":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1594,"t":["என்னை கேளீ ரென்னுற்றீ ரென்ன பெயாபு ரென்றாற்குப்","பொன்னங் குன்றிற் பொலிந்ததோ ணம்பி யொருபொற் பூங்கொடி","யென்னு நீராளை யீங்கே கெடுத்தே னென்பா வத்தாற்","பன்னூற் கேள்வி யுடையேன்யான் பவதத்த னென்பே னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When Civakan asks what has befallen him and what his name is, he says: hero of shoulders shining like a golden hill, by my sin I have lost here a woman like a golden flowering creeper; I am Pavadattan, learned in many treatises.","n":"He ascribes the loss to his own பாவம, though the story has shown it is his wife's doing; and he names his learning as his identity, which Civakan takes up at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kalvi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1594","lb":"CC1594","ev":"Civakan asks the man his trouble and name, and he gives his name as Pavadattan and tells of his loss.","cl":[["one's loss comes from one's own sin","general","reported",3,"pavadattan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","பவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவதத்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1595":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1595,"t":["கைப்பொருள் கொடுத்துங் கற்றல் கற்றபின் கண்ணு மாகு","மெய்ப்பொருள் விளைக்கு நெஞ்சின் மெலிவிற்கோர் துணையு மாகும்","பொய்ப்பொருள் பிறகள் பொன்னாம் புகழுமாந் துணைவி யாக்கு","மிப்பொரு ளெய்தி நின்றீ ரிரங்குவ தென்னை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says that learning, bought even by giving away one's wealth, becomes an eye once learned, a companion to the heart in its weakness, yielding the true thing; all else is false, while learning is gold, fame and a wife to a man: having this, why do you grieve?","n":"துணைவி யாக்கும் is pointed: learning itself is offered as a wife to a man who has lost his wife.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","thunai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1595","lb":"CC1595","ev":"","cl":[["learning is true wealth and a support in grief, all other things being false","general","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","பவதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1596":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1596,"t":["அன்புநூ லாக வின்சொ லலர்தொடுந் தமைந்த காத","லின்பஞ்செய் காமச் சாந்திற் கைபுனைந் தேற்ற மாலை","நன்பகற் சூட்டி விள்ளா தொழுகினு நங்கை மார்க்குப்","பின்செலும் பிறார்க ணுள்ளம் பிணையனார்க் கடியதன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He says that though love's garland, strung with affection for thread, sweet words for flowers, and dipped in the sandal of desire, is worn in full day and never comes apart, for young women a heart that follows others is not hard.","n":"Line 4 (பின்செலும் பிறார்க ணுள்ளம் பிணையனார்க் கடியதன்றே) is read as 'for doe-like women it is not hard for the heart to go after others'; the word பிறார்கண் looks miswritten.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","karpu","kaamam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1596","lb":"CC1596","ev":"","cl":[["however well a man's love binds a woman, her heart can still follow another","general","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","பவதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1597":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1597,"t":["பெண்ணெனப் படுவ கேண்மோ பீடில பிறப்பு நோக்கா","வுண்ணிறை யுடைய வல்ல வொராயிர மனத்த வாகு","மெண்ணிப்பத் தங்கை யிட்டா லிந்திரன் மகளு மாங்கே","வெண்ணெய்க்குன் றெரியுற் றாற்போன் மெலிந்துபின் னிற்கு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He says: listen to what women are: they have no dignity, they do not regard birth, they do not hold their restraint within, they have a thousand minds; if a man counts to ten and presses, even Indra's daughter melts there like a hill of butter in fire and follows.","n":"The speech is aimed at a man who has just praised his wife as true, and the hero knows that this wife has just tried to seduce him; the epic gives its general claim about women an occasion that seems to confirm it, then qualifies it in the next verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","karpu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1597","lb":"CC1597","ev":"","cl":[["women lack inner restraint and yield to persistent pressing","general","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","பவதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1598":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1598,"t":["சாமெனிற் சாத னோத றன்னவன் றணந்த காலைப்","பூமனும் புனைத லின்றிப் பொற்புடன் புலம்ப வைகிக்","காமனை யென்றுஞ் சொல்லார் கணவற்கை தொழுது வாழ்வார்","தேமலர்த் திருவோ டொப்பார் சோந்தவன் செல்ல றீர்ப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"He goes on that there are also women who die if he dies; when the husband is away they wear no flowers and live forlorn in their dignity, never speak of Kama, live worshipping their husband's hands, are like Tiru of the honeyed flower, and end the distress of the man they have joined.","n":"Read against 1597 this is the exception that keeps the rule: chastity is described by the absence of desire (காமனை யென்றுஞ் சொல்லார்) and by the husband's absence, which is precisely the condition Pavadattan's wife has just failed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1598","lb":"CC1598","ev":"","cl":[["a chaste wife dies with her husband, renounces adornment in his absence and lives worshipping him","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","பவதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1599":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1599,"t":["அன்னணின் றோழி யையா வளென்னைக் கண்ட கண்ணாற்","பின்னைத்தான் பிறரை நோக்காப் பெருமட மாது தன்னை","யென்னையா னிழந்து வாழு மாறென விரங்கி னானுக்","கன்னளோ வென்று நக்கா னணிமணி முழவுத் தோளான்."],"p":"","r":"Pavadattan says, sir, his companion is like that: having seen him with her eyes she never afterwards looked at another, a woman of great innocence; how can he live having lost her? To him who grieved so, the man of jewelled drum-like shoulders laughed: 'is she so?'","n":"The husband's claim is exactly what the reader has seen to be false (1581); Civakan's laugh is the only comment the hero makes, and he does not expose her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1599","lb":"CC1599","ev":"Pavadattan insists his wife is of the chaste kind, and Civakan laughs.","cl":[],"ag":["பவதத்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவதத்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1600":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1600,"t":["இனையல்வேண் டாவிம் மந்திரத்தை யோதிநீ யொருவில் லேவள","வனைய வெல்லை சென்றா லியக்கி கொணர்ந் தருளுநீ","புனைசெய் கோல்வளை யைக்கைப் படுதியென் றாங்கவன் போதலு","மனைய மாதரைக் கண்டாங் கடிபுல்லி வீழ்ந்த ரற்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan tells him not to grieve: recite this mantra and go the length of one bowshot, and the yakshi will bring her and give her to you, and you will have your bangled woman; he goes, sees the woman there, embraces her feet, falls and cries out.","n":"The yakshi is the husband's own story; Civakan uses it rather than telling the truth, so that the wife is returned without shame and the husband's belief in her is kept.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","anangu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1600","lb":"CC1600","ev":"Civakan gives Pavadattan a mantra and sends him a bowshot away, where he finds his wife and falls at her feet.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","பவதத்தன்"],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1601":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1601,"t":["பட்ட வெல்லாம் பரியா துரைத்தா னவளுங் கேட்டாள்","விட்டா ளார்வ மவன்க ணிவன்மேன் மைந்துறவினான்","மட்டார் கோதை மனைதுறந்தாண் மைந்தனு மங்கை மேலே","யொட்டி விள்ளா வார்வத்த னாகி யுருவ மோதினான்."],"p":"","r":"He told her all he had suffered without holding back, and she heard; she let go her desire for the other man and turned her love to this one, and her husband, clinging to the garlanded woman with a longing that would not part, praised her beauty.","n":"Line 2 (விட்டா ளார்வ மவன்க ணிவன்மேன் மைந்துறவினான்) and மனைதுறந்தாள் in line 3 are hard to construe; the reading follows the sense that the episode needs, that her desire passes from Civakan back to the husband.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1601","lb":"CC1601","ev":"Pavadattan tells his wife his grief; she gives up her desire for Civakan and returns to him.","cl":[],"ag":["பவதத்தன்","அநங்கமா வீணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1602":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1602,"t":["மெழுகு செய்படம் வீழ்முகின் மத்தகத்","தொழுகும் வெள்ளரு வித்திர ளோடைசூழ்ந்","திழுகு பொன்மதத் தின்வரைக் குஞ்சரந்","தொழுது வேய்முதற் றூசங்கொண் டேறினான்."],"p":"","r":"He bowed to the mountain like a tusker, with clouds falling on its brow like a painted cloth, streams of white waterfalls for its face-band, and gold flowing from it like rut, and climbed it holding to the bamboo's base.","n":"The mountain is described part by part as an elephant (மத்தகம், ஓடை, மதம், குஞ்சரம்), and the climber mounts it like a mahout. வேய்முதற் றூசங்கொண் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1602","lb":"CC1602","ev":"Civakan worships the mountain and climbs it.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1603":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1603,"t":["நிரைத்த தீவினை நீங்க நெடுங்கணார்","வரைக்க ணேறலின் வாலரிப் பொற்சிலம்","புரைத்து மின்னிருண் மேற்கிடந் தாலுமொத்","தரைத்த லத்தக மார்ந்ததொர் பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On one side, as long-eyed women climbed the hill so that their accumulated evil deeds might leave them, their pale gold anklets rubbed and the whole ground there was red with lac, like lightning lying on darkness.","n":"The hill is a shrine whose god is praised in 1608 onward as the ageless First One and the conqueror of Kama, which in this epic marks the Jina; the verse itself names no school, only the pilgrims' purpose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thalam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1603","lb":"CC1603","ev":"Women climb the sacred hill to be rid of their sins.","cl":[["climbing the holy hill removes the evil deeds one has piled up","general","reported",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["நெடுங்கணார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1604":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1604,"t":["சாந்துங் கோதையுந் தண்ணறுஞ் சுண்ணமு","மாய்ந்த பூம்புகை யும்மவி யுஞ்சுமந்","தேந்து பொன்விளக் கேந்தி யிடம்பெறா","மாந்தர் சும்மை மலிந்ததொர் பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On another side the crowd of people carrying sandal paste, garlands, cool fragrant powder, chosen incense and offerings, and holding up golden lamps, was so thick there was no room to stand.","n":"The offerings listed (சாந்து, கோதை, சுண்ணம், புகை) are the same scents and garlands Civakan stripped from the body in 1583; here they are carried to the god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1604","lb":"CC1604","ev":"Crowds of worshippers carrying offerings throng the hill.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:68"},"cc:1605":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1605,"t":["துறந்த மன்னவர் தூமுடி தோள்வளை","நிறங்கொ ளாரம்பைம் பூணிழற் குண்டலம்","பிறங்கு வெங்கதிர் மின்னொடு பின்னிவீழ்ந்","துறங்கு கின்றன போன்றவொர் பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On one side of the mountain the glitter of gems and sunlight looks like the pure crowns, armlets, bright necklaces, ornaments and shining earrings of kings who renounced, fallen and lying tangled together as if asleep.","n":"The landscape is figured through renunciation: what glitters on the hill is likened to the regalia that kings cast off when they took up the ascetic life, so the hill of the Jina's shrine is strewn, in image, with abandoned kingship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1605","lb":"CC1605","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1606":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1606,"t":["கருவித் தேன்கலை கையுறக் கீண்டுடன்","மருவிப் பைங்கறி வாரிப் பழந்தழீஇ","வெருவி நாகம் பிளிற்ற விரைந்துரா","யருவி நின்றதி ரும்மொரு பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On another side a male monkey tears open a honeycomb by hand, gathers green pepper vines and clasps fruit, an elephant trumpets in fright, and a waterfall rushes down and roars.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1606","lb":"CC1606","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1607":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1607,"t":["வெங்க திர்க்கட வுள்வியன் றேர்வரைத்","தங்கு சந்தனக் கோட்டிடைப் பட்டெனப்","பொங்கு மான்குளம் பிற்குடை பொற்றுகண்","மங்கு லாய்த்திசை யாவையு மல்கின்றே"],"p":"","r":"The gold dust churned up by the hooves of the horses, as if the great chariot of the hot-rayed sun god had caught among the sandal boughs on the peak, rises like haze and fills every direction.","n":"மான் in line 3 can be a deer or a horse; read with the sun's chariot of line 1, the hooves are most likely the chariot's horses, and the dust is the sun's light on the slopes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1607","lb":"CC1607","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1608":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1608,"t":["சுனைய நீலமுஞ் சுள்ளியுஞ் சூழ்மலர்","நனைய நாகமுங் கோங்கமு நாறிணர்ச்","சினைய சண்பகம் வேங்கையோ டேற்றுபு","முனைவன் மேற்றுதி முற்றெடுத் தோதினான்."],"p":"","r":"Gathering blue lilies from the springs, சுள்ளி flowers, naga and kongu blossoms and clustered champak with vengai, he offered them and recited in full the praise of the Lord.","n":"முனைவன், the Lord, is the Jina of the hill shrine; the hymn that follows (1609 to 1611) is Civakan's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1608","lb":"CC1608","ev":"Civakan gathers mountain flowers, offers them to the Jina and sings his praise.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1609":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1609,"t":["முனிமை முகடாய மூவா முதல்வன்","றனிமைத் தலைமை தனதுதா னென்ப","தனிமைத் தலைமை தனதுதா னென்றாற்","பனிமலர்தூய் நின்று பழிச்சாவா றென்னே."],"p":"","r":"They say the unageing First One, summit of sagehood, has solitary supremacy as his own; if that supremacy is his, the singer asks, how could we fail to stand and praise him strewing cool flowers?","n":"Each stanza repeats its second line as its third, turning 'they say' (என்ப) into 'if so' (என்றால், ஏல்) and ending on the same question, 'how could we not' (ஆறு என்னே): the hymn argues from what is said of the Lord to the duty of praise. தனிமைத் தலைமை, 'the supremacy of aloneness', may point to the Jina's isolation of the soul from karma (kevala), though the verse does not name it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1609","lb":"CC1609","ev":"Civakan sings the first stanza of his hymn to the Jina.","cl":[["the First One, the summit of sagehood, alone holds solitary supremacy","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1610":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1610,"t":["மலரேந்து சேவடிய மாலென்ப மாலா","லலரேந்தி யஞ்சலிசெய் தஞ்சப் படுவா","னலரேந்தி யஞ்சலிசெய் தஞ்சப் படுமே","லிலரே மலரெனினு மேத்தாவா றென்னே."],"p":"","r":"They say his red feet, which bear flowers, are those that Mal himself, holding blossoms with folded hands, worships in awe; if Mal so worships him, how could we, even lacking flowers, fail to praise him?","n":"If மால் is Vishnu, as the folded hands of a worshipper suggest, the stanza ranks a Vaishnava god below the Jina as his devotee; மால் can also mean simply 'the great one', and line 1's மாலென்ப could then call the feet great. The reading is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1610","lb":"CC1610","ev":"Civakan sings the second stanza of his hymn.","cl":[["even Mal worships the Jina's feet with flowers and folded hands","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1611":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1611,"t":["களிசேர் கணையுடைய காமனையுங் காய்ந்த","வளிசே ரறவாழி யண்ணலிவ னென்ப","ரளிசே ரறவாழி யண்ண லிவனேல்","விளியாக் குணத்துதிநாம் வித்தாவா றென்னே."],"p":"","r":"They say this is the lord of the wheel of dharma full of grace, who scorched even Kama with his maddening arrows; if this is that lord, how could we fail to sow praise of his undying virtues?","n":"காமனையும் காய்ந்த, 'who scorched even Kama', is the feat Saiva tradition gives Siva; the hymn gives it to the Jina, for whom it means the conquest of desire, and joins it to the wheel of dharma (அறவாழி) and compassion (அளி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","kaamam","arul","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1611","lb":"CC1611","ev":"Civakan sings the third stanza of his hymn.","cl":[["the Jina, turner of the wheel of dharma full of compassion, conquered Kama","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அறிவன்"],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1612":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1612,"t":["இன்னண மேத்தி யிறைவ னடிதொழு","தன்ன முறங்கு மணிவரை மேனின்று","பொன்னங் கழலா னிழிந்து பொழிமழை","மின்னி னடந்து மிகுசுரஞ் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Having praised in this way and bowed at the Lord's feet, the man of golden anklets came down from the gem-bright hill where swans sleep and went on through the great wilderness like lightning in pouring rain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1612","lb":"CC1612","ev":"Civakan worships at the Jina's feet, descends the hill and crosses the wilderness.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1613":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1613,"t":["மாலைக் கதிர்வேன் மலங்க மணிமலர்க்","கோலை விடுக ணுருகு கொடியிடை","யேலங் கமழ்குழ லேழை யவரன்ன","வாலைக் கரும்பி னகநா டணைந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He reached the inner country of white sugarcane, a land like the women with eyes that make the bright spear quail and loose flower-arrows, waists like creepers and cardamom-scented hair.","n":"The country is described through its women before he meets any of them; line 2 (கோலை விடுக ணுருகு) is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1613","lb":"CC1613","ev":"Civakan reaches a fertile country.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1614":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1614,"t":["வாழைச்சறு சாடிமட் டயின்று மள்ளர்தாங்","கழைக்கரும் பெறிந்துகண் ணுடைக்கு மெந்திர","மழைக்குர லெனமயி லகவ வார்செந்நெற்","புழைக்கடைப் புனலலைத் தொழுகும் பொற்பிற்றே."],"p":"","r":"It is a land where farm workers who have drunk toddy cut the cane, the presses that crush it roar like thunder so that the peacocks call, and water dashes at the sluice gates of the long red paddy.","n":"Peacocks taking the noise of the cane presses for thunder is a stock sign of a rich land; வாழைச்சறு in line 1 is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1614","lb":"CC1614","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1615":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1615,"t":["தாமரை மலர்தலை யடுத்துத் தண்கமழ்","தூமலர்க் குவளைகா லணைத்துத் தோலடிக்","காமரு பெடைதழீஇ யன்னங் கண்படுந்","தேமலர்த் தடந்தழீஇத் திசைக்கண் மல்கின்றே."],"p":"","r":"Its broad flowering tanks fill every quarter, where the swan sleeps with its head on a lotus, cool lilies at its feet, embracing its lovely mate.","n":"The sleeping, embracing pair of swans here prepares for the quarrelling pair of 1623 to 1625.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","punarchi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1615","lb":"CC1615","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1616":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1616,"t":["கண்பயி லிளங்கமு கெருத்திற் காய்பாபுஇக்","கொண்டிள மந்திக ளெறியக் கோட்டிடைத்","திண்கனி முசுக்கலை சிதறுந் தேம்பொழின்","மண்டமர் கடந்தவன் மகிழ்வொ டேகினான்."],"p":"","r":"He who had overcome the press of battle went on gladly through the honeyed groves, where young female monkeys pluck the nuts of the young areca and throw them and the langur scatters ripe fruit from the boughs.","n":"காய்பாபுஇக் in line 1 is corrupt; the plucking of nuts is a guess from context.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1616","lb":"CC1616","ev":"Civakan travels on through groves.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1617":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1617,"t":["களிறுமாய் கதிர்ச்செநெற் கழனி நாட்டிடை","யொளிறுவே னரபதி நகர மொய்யெனப்","பிளிறுவா ரிடிமுர சார்ப்பப் பெய்கழல்","வெளிறிலாக் கேள்வியான் விருப்பொ டெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Through the country of paddy fields where elephants are hidden by the ripe ears, the man of flawless learning arrived eagerly at the crowded city of the king, as the thundering drums roared.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1617","lb":"CC1617","ev":"Civakan arrives at the king's city.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1618":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1618,"t":["புறநகர் மணமக னொருவன் போதர்வா","னிறைமகன் வினாயினா னென்ன பேரவே","துறைவளர் நாட்டொடு நகரஞ் சொல்லென","வறிகவென் றலரிவாய் கமழக் கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"A bridegroom from the outer town came along, and the prince asked him what the place was called, telling him to name the land and the city; he answered, his flower-like mouth fragrant, 'Know it then.'","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1618","lb":"CC1618","ev":"Civakan meets a bridegroom outside the city and asks him the name of the land and town.","cl":[],"ag":["மணமகன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1619":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1619,"t":["மத்திம தேசமா நாடு மற்றிநாட்","டெத்திசை நிதியமு மிறைகொண் டில்லவர்க்","குய்த்துமூர் கொடுப்பவ ரேம மாபுர","மித்திசைக் கையநீ புதியை போன்மென."],"p":"","r":"The bridegroom says that the land is the Middle Country, and that in it, among those who take in tribute the treasure of every quarter and pour it out to the needy, is this city, Emamapuram; and he tells the stranger that he seems new to these parts.","n":"The city is praised for its givers before it is described, a mark the epic sets on the place where Civakan will find his next bride.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1619","lb":"CC1619","ev":"The bridegroom names the land as the Middle Country and the city as Emamapuram.","cl":[],"ag":["மணமகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணமகன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1620":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1620,"t":["அன்னதே யென்றலி னடிசிற் காலமா","லென்னொடு பேசினாய் தவிர்மற் றீங்கெனப்","பொன்னகர்ப் புக்கபி னறிவல் போகென்றான்","வின்மாபுஇ வாங்கிய வீங்கு தோளினான்."],"p":"","r":"When the man had said 'so it is', the hero of the swelling shoulders that bend the bow told him that, since it was his mealtime and he had stopped to talk, he should go on and leave it there, for Civakan would learn the rest once he entered the golden city.","n":"அறிவல், 'I shall know', is first person, so the dismissal is Civakan's courtesy to a man with his wedding meal waiting, not the bridegroom's rebuff; வின்மாபுஇ in line 4 is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1620","lb":"CC1620","ev":"Civakan lets the bridegroom go and goes into the city.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மணமகன்"],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1621":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1621,"t":["புணர்மருப்பி யானையின் புயல்கொண் மும்மத","மணமகள் கதுப்பென நாறு மாநகர்த்","துணைமலர்க் கண்ணியுஞ் செம்பொன் மாலையு","மிணைமலர்த் தாரினா னிடறி யேகினான்."],"p":"","r":"In the great city that smells like a bride's hair with the triple musth of tusked elephants, the man of paired-flower garland walked on, treading over fallen flower wreaths and red-gold chains.","n":"புணர்மருப்பி in line 1 is likely புணர்மருப்பின்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1621","lb":"CC1621","ev":"Civakan walks through the streets of Emamapuram.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1622":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1622,"t":["வண்டுகொப் புளித்துணு மாலை மார்பனைக்","கண்டுவப் பளித்தவர் கடைக்க ணேக்கற","மண்டபப் பளிக்கறை மருங்கொர் மாநிழல்","கொண்டவற் களித்ததோர் குளிர்கொள் பொய்கையே."],"p":"","r":"To escape the side-glances of the women who saw the garlanded man and gave him their delight, he found in a pavilion of crystal a great shade, and beside it a cool pond that gave him rest.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1622","lb":"CC1622","ev":"Civakan takes refuge from the women's glances in the shade of a crystal pavilion by a pond.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1623":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1623,"t":["வண்சிறைப் பவளச் செவ்வாய்ப் பெடையன்ன மடமை கூரத்","தண்கய நீருட் கண்ட தன்னிழல் பிறிதென் றெண்ணிக்","கண்டனங் கள்வ மற்றுன் காதலி தன்னை நீர்க்கீழ்ப்","பண்டைய மல்லம் வேண்டா படுக்வென் றூடிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The hen swan of coral beak, simple in her innocence, sees her own reflection in the cool water, takes it for another, and sulks at her mate: 'We have seen your beloved under the water, thief; we are not what we were; we want none of you; go.'","n":"The rival is the hen herself: her jealousy is real and its object is an image of her own beauty. Line 4's படுக்வென் is likely a misprint for படுகென்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1623","lb":"CC1623","ev":"By the pond, a hen swan mistakes her reflection for a rival and turns on her mate.","cl":[],"ag":["பெடையன்னம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பெடையன்னம்","சேவல்"],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1624":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1624,"t":["செயிர்ப்பொடு சிவந்து நோக்கிச் சேவலி னகலச் சேவ","லயிர்ப்பதெ னின்னை யல்லா லறியலே னன்றி மூக்கி","னுயிர்ப்பதுன் பணியி னாலே யூடனீ யென்று பல்காற்","பயிர்ப்பறச் சிறகாற் புல்லிப் பணிந்துபாண் செய்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"She glares red with anger and moves away from the drake; the drake says, 'Why do you doubt? I know none but you; I even breathe through my nose at your bidding; do not sulk', and again and again, embracing her with his wings to take away her aversion, he bows and sings to her.","n":"The drake's claim that his very breath is at her command is the humbled lover's plea of the love poems, given to a bird.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","panivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1624","lb":"CC1624","ev":"The drake swan pleads with his mate, embraces her with his wings and courts her with song.","cl":[],"ag":["பெடையன்னம்","சேவல்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சேவல்","பெடையன்னம்"],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1625":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1625,"t":["கலையுணர் மகளிர் நெஞ்சிற் காமத்திற் கனிந்த வூட","னிலையுணர் மைந்தர் நீக்கி நெறியினாற் புணர்ந்த தொப்ப","வலர்மிசைப் பெடையி னூட லன்புகொள் சேவ னீக்கிக்","குலவிய புணர்ச்சி நோக்கிக் குன்றனான் சிந்திக் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As men who understand the state of a sulk ripened by love in the hearts of women skilled in the arts end it and unite with them by the right way, so the loving drake ended the sulk of his mate upon the flowers; and seeing their close union, the man like a hill began to think.","n":"The simile makes the drake a model of the lover who knows the art of ending a sulk (ஊடல் நிலை உணர் ... புணர்ந்தது), and it is this finished cycle of quarrel and union that turns the watching hero, alone, to memory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1625","lb":"CC1625","ev":"The swans are reconciled and Civakan, watching them unite, falls to brooding.","cl":[],"ag":["சேவல்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1626":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1626,"t":["தன்னையான் முகத்தை நோக்கிற் றான்முலை முகத்தை நோக்கும்","பின்னையான் பலவும் பேசிற் றானொன்று மிழற்றும் பைம்பூட்","பொன்னவாஞ் சுணங்கு போர்த்த பொங்கிள முலையி னாளென்","முன்னையாள் போன்று தத்தை முகத்துளே தோன்று கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"He thinks: when I looked at her face she looked at her breasts, when I spoke many things she would lisp one, and now the girl of young swelling breasts spread with golden spots, my first one, Tattai, appears before my face as she was.","n":"என் முன்னையாள், 'my first one', names Gandharvadattai as the first of his wives; the memory is of her shyness, the downcast eyes and the single word, which the swans' quarrel and reconciliation bring back. தன்னையான் in line 1 is best divided தன்னை யான்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","naanam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1626","lb":"CC1626","ev":"Civakan recalls Gandharvadattai and sees her as if before him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1627":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1627,"t":["பரிவுற் றாற்பய னின்றியும் பாவைமார்","முரிவுற் றார்களின் மூர்ச்சனை செய்பவாற்","பிரிவிற் றோன்றிய பேரன் பெனப்படு","மெரியின் மூழ்கி யிறந்து படுங்கொலோ."],"p":"","r":"Since even where grieving is fruitless women swoon like those broken, he wonders whether she will sink into the fire called the great love born of separation and die.","n":"The verse turns on பிரிவில் தோன்றிய பேரன்பு, 'the great love that is born in separation': love is said to grow, not fade, when the lovers part, and to burn like fire. The woman feared for is most likely Tattai of 1626, though பாவைமார் in line 1 speaks of women in general.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1627","lb":"CC1627","ev":"Civakan fears that his absent wife may die of grief at their parting.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1628":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1628,"t":["வாளி யம்பன வாட்டங் கண்ணிதன்","றோளு மென்முலைப் பாரமுந் தொன்னல","நாளு நாளினு நைந்துநைந் துள்சுடப்","பூளை மெல்லணை மேற்புற ளுங்கொலோ."],"p":"","r":"He wonders whether she, whose eyes are like arrows, with her shoulders, soft heavy breasts and old beauty wasting day after day and burning within, tosses on her soft bed of பூளை flowers.","n":"Civakan, who has just left Kemasari without a word, here imagines for another wife exactly the wasting and burning that Kemasari voiced in her lament.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1628","lb":"CC1628","ev":"Civakan imagines his wife wasting and sleepless in his absence.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:69"},"cc:1629":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1629,"t":["உருகி வாடியென் னுற்றது கொல்லெனக்","கருகி வாடிய காமரு கோதை","னிருக ணீரு மிடைமுலை பாய்ந்துகக்","குருகு பாய்தட மாகவ ழுங்கொலோ."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, alone by the pool, wonders whether his garlanded beloved, withered and darkened by grief, now lets tears from both eyes run between her breasts until they make a pond where herons dive.","n":"The beloved is not named in the verse; 1626 has just named தத்தை, so this is Gandharvadattai. The hyperbole of a tear-pond for herons turns his worry into a picture of her body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1629","lb":"CC1629","ev":"Civakan, by the pool outside Hemamapuram, imagines Gandharvadattai weeping for him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1630":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1630,"t":["வண்டு வாழ்பயில் கோதை மணமுதற்","கண்ட ஞான்றுதன் கண்ணெனுங் கைகளா","னொண்டு கொண்டு பருகிய நோக்கமொன்","றுண்டெ னாவி யுருக்கி யிடுவதே."],"p":"","r":"He recalls the look she drank him in with at their first meeting at the wedding, scooping him up with her eyes as if they were hands, and says that one look melts his life.","n":"The eyes are made into hands that scoop and drink (நொண்டு கொண்டு பருகிய), a bold image of looking as consuming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1630","lb":"CC1630","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1631":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1631,"t":["காத லாளுட லுள்ளுயிர் கைவிடி","னேத மென்னுயி ரய்தி யிறக்குமற்","றாத லாலழி வொன்றில ளல்லதூஉ","மாதர் விஞ்சையும் வல்லளு மல்லளோ."],"p":"","r":"He reasons that if his beloved's life had left her body his own would have perished with it, so she has come to no harm; and besides, is she not skilled in the Vidyadhara arts?","n":"The comfort is an inference from the lovers' shared life, backed by her magic (விஞ்சை): as a Vidyadhara's daughter she can guard herself. He uses this to quiet the fear of 1627 that she might die in the fire of parting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1631","lb":"CC1631","ev":"","cl":[["the lives of true lovers are so joined that one cannot die without the other","general","reported",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1632":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1632,"t":["காதன் மிக்குழிக் கற்றவுங் கைகொடா","வாதல் கண்ணகத் தஞ்சனம் போலுமாற்","றாது துற்றுபு தங்கிய வண்டனார்க்","கேத மிற்றென வெண்ணுமென் னெஞ்சரோ."],"p":"","r":"He says that when love grows too strong what one has learned gives no help, as the eye cannot see the collyrium within it, and so his heart keeps fearing harm for the bee-like one who has settled on the pollen.","n":"The collyrium simile: what is nearest the eye is what it cannot see; so learning fails a man in love exactly where it is needed. The last two lines are hard to construe; whether the bee is she or his own heart is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kalvi","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1632","lb":"CC1632","ev":"","cl":[["when love is excessive, learning gives no help","general","reported",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1633":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1633,"t":["நறவெங் கோதையர் நன்னலங் காதலான்","மறவெங் காமத்து வந்துற்ற தீவினைப்","பறவைத் தோ?நர கத்துப் பதைக்குங்கா","லறிவ னல்லதங் கார்சர ணாகுவார்."],"p":"","r":"He then reflects that the evil deeds that come from fierce lust for the beauty of garlanded women make one writhe in hell, and that there no one but the Omniscient can be a refuge.","n":"The turn is sudden: from longing for his own wife he passes to the doctrine that desire itself breeds தீவினை. அறிவன், the Omniscient, is the Jina, as in 1241-1247. Line 3 is corrupt in the edition (a lost letter in பறவைத் தோ?நரகத்து), so the exact picture of hell is lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vinai","naragam","adaikkalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1633","lb":"CC1633","ev":"","cl":[["fierce lust for women's beauty brings evil deeds that torment one in hell","general","asserted",2,"civakan"],["in hell only the Omniscient Jina is a refuge","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1634":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1634,"t":["வேட்கை யூர்தர விம்முற வெய்திய","மாட்சி யுள்ளத்தை மாற்றி மலர்மிடை","காட்சிக் கின்பொய்கைக் காமார் நலனுண்டு","மீட்டு மங்கிருந் தான்விடை யேறனான்."],"p":"","r":"Turning his swelling, desire-driven mind back, the bull-like hero enjoys instead the beauty of the flower-crowded pool and stays on there.","n":"The reflection of 1633 is acted on at once: desire is not killed but redirected to the pleasure of the scene, a householder's restraint rather than a renouncer's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1634","lb":"CC1634","ev":"Civakan masters his longing and stays by the pool.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1635":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1635,"t":["நேரார் நேரு நீணிதி துஞ்சுந் நிறைகோயி","லாரா வெம்போ ராய்தட மித்த னரசற்கு","நாரார் கற்பின் னாகிள வேய்த்தோ ணளினைக்குஞ்","சீராற் றோன்றிச் செல்வமொ டெல்லாந் திருத்தக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator introduces Vijayan, born in a palace full of treasure where enemies come to submit, to King Tadamittan of hard wars and to Nalinai of noble chastity, a prince made perfect in fortune and all else.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","karpu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1635","lb":"CC1635","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1636":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1636,"t":["விண்டார்த் தேய்க்கும் வெம்பரி மான்றேர் விசயன்னென்","றுண்டா நின்றான் றன்புக ழூழி யுலகேத்த","வண்டார் சோலை வார்மண நாறப் புகுகின்றான்","கண்டான் சேர்ந்தான் காளையைக் கல்விக் கடலானே."],"p":"","r":"Vijayan, of the fierce horse-drawn chariot, whose fame the world praises, a sea of learning, enters the fragrant grove, sees the young hero and joins him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","kalvi","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1636","lb":"CC1636","ev":"Prince Vijayan comes into the grove and meets Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1637":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1637,"t":["இந்நாட் டிவ்வூ ரிவ்விட மெய்தா ரிவண்வாழ்வா","ரெந்நாட் டெவ்வூ ரெப்பெய ராய்நீ யுரையென்றாற்","கந்நாட் டவ்வூ ரப்பெய ரல்லாப் பெயர்சொன்னான்","பொய்ந்நாட் டேனும் பொய்யல வாற்றாற் புகழ்வெய் யோன்."],"p":"","r":"Asked whether he lives here or which land, town and name are his, Civakan gives a land, town and name other than his own; the narrator says that though he set up a falsehood, the fame-loving hero did so in a way that was no lie.","n":"Civakan is hiding from Kattiyangaran; the narrator feels bound to defend the false name. The construal of line 4 (பொய்ந்நாட் டேனும் பொய்யல ஆற்றால்) is not certain: it may mean that the lie harmed no one, or that the hero kept truth in conduct while hiding his name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","pugazh","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1637","lb":"CC1637","ev":"Vijayan asks Civakan who he is, and Civakan gives a false land, town and name.","cl":[["a falsehood told without harm, by one who loves honour, is not a lie","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["விசயன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1638":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1638,"t":["வாமான் றிண்டேர் வள்ளலு முள்ளம் மிகைகொண்டெங்","கோமாற் குய்ப்பன் கொள்பயன் மிக்கான் கொலைவேலா","னேமா றில்லா விந்திர னேயும் மிவனொவ்வான்","போமா றாய்வென் பொற்பொடு கூடும் வகையென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayan, his heart overflowing, resolves to bring this spear-bearer to the king for his great worth, for not even Indra is his equal, and to find a way to keep company with him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1638","lb":"CC1638","ev":"Vijayan resolves to take Civakan to his father.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்",""],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1639":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1639,"t":["பூங்கழ லானைப் புண்ணிய நம்பி முகநோக்கி","யீங்கிது நின்னா டிப்பதி நின்னூ ரிதுநின்னில்","வீங்கிய திண்டோள் வெல்புக ழாய்நின் கிளையென்றாற்","காங்கது வெல்லா மண்ணலு நேர்ந்தாங் கமைகென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayan tells him that this land, this city and this house are now his own, and its people his kin, and Civakan accepts, saying let it be so.","n":"The offer answers the false name of 1637: the stranger who gave no true home is given one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","virundhu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1639","lb":"CC1639","ev":"Vijayan offers Civakan his land and house as his own, and Civakan accepts.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1640":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1640,"t":["மன்னவன் சிறுவ னாங்கோர் மாங்கனி யுண்ண லுற்று","மின்னவிர் கணையிற் பல்காற் பிழைப்பெய்து மீண்டு நிற்பப்","பொன்னவிர் கழலி னானப் பொருசிலை கணையின் வாங்கி","யின்னமிர் தூறு கின்ற விருங்கனி யறவெய் திட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The king's son tries to shoot down a mango, misses again and again with his bright arrows, and stops; the hero takes the bow and with one arrow brings down the nectar-dripping fruit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1640","lb":"CC1640","ev":"Vijayan fails to shoot a mango; Civakan shoots it down.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1641":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1641,"t":["எய்தவக் கணையு மாவி னிருங்கனி யதுவும் பூமிக்","கெய்தவச் சிலையி னெல்லை யணுகலு மேந்த னோக்கி","யெய்தவவ் விடத்து நின்றே யெய்தவத் தடக்கை கொண்டாற்","கெய்தச்சென் றைய னாரத் தழுவிக்கொண் டிதனைச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"As the arrow and the fruit come down to earth within reach of the bow, the prince, seeing it, goes to the man who shot without moving from his place, embraces him and speaks.","n":"The point of the shot is that arrow and fruit fall back at the archer's feet; 1642's சென்ற கோல் போந்த ஆறு says the same.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1641","lb":"CC1641","ev":"Vijayan embraces Civakan after the shot.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1642":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1642,"t":["வண்சிலை கொண்ட வாறும் வார்கணை தொடுத்த வாறுங்","கண்கணை வைத்த வாறுங் கற்செய்தோ ளிருந்த வாறுந்","திண்சரம் விட்ட வாறுஞ் சென்றகோல் போந்த வாறுங்","கண்டெலாம் வியந்து நோக்கி வில்லுடைக் கடவு ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Marvelling at how he took the bow, fitted the arrow, set his eye, held his stone-hard shoulders, loosed the shaft and how the arrow came back, Vijayan calls him a god of the bow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1642","lb":"CC1642","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1643":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1643,"t":["மராமர மேழு மெய்த வாங்குவிற் றடக்கை வல்வி","லிராமனை வல்ல னென்ப திசையலாற் கண்ட தில்லை","யுராமன மிவன்க ணின்றி யுவக்குமா செய்வ லென்று","குராமலர்க் காவி னீங்கிக் கோயிலே கோண்டு புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayan says that of Rama, who pierced the seven sal trees, people only say he was skilled, while none has seen it, and resolving to make this man glad without strain, he leaves the grove and takes him to the palace.","n":"Rama's feat is set aside as hearsay (இசையலாற் கண்டதில்லை) against what Vijayan has just seen with his own eyes; the epic rates seen skill over a famous story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1643","lb":"CC1643","ev":"Vijayan brings Civakan from the grove to the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்",""],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1644":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1644,"t":["வழிவரல் வருத்த மோம்பி வயிரப்பூ ணணிந்த மார்ப","னழிகவுள் யானை வேந்தற் கவன்றிற மறியச் சொன்னான்","மொழியெதிர் விரும்பி மன்னன் மூரிவிற் றடக்கை யாற்குக்","கழிபெரு முகமன் கூறிக் காதலங் காளை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"After easing the toil of the road, the diamond-breasted prince tells the king of the stranger's skill, and the king, glad of the words, greets the great-bowed man with high courtesy and calls him a beloved young man.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1644","lb":"CC1644","ev":"Vijayan tells his father of Civakan, and the king welcomes him.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","தடமித்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1645":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1645,"t":["கிலுத்தங்கூர்ப் பரங்க ளென்னு மிரண்டினுட் கிலுத்தஞ் சார்ந்து","நலத்தகு விரல்க ளைந்தி னிம்பர்மூ விரலி னீளஞ்","சிலைத்தழும்பி யானைத் தோலி னூற்றுரை சிறுமீ னொத்த","விலக்கணக் கிடக்கை கண்டே யேவினுக் கரச னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing on his hand the marks of the archer's art, the bowstring scar and the lines likened to elephant hide and small fish, the king calls him a king of the arrow.","n":"The king reads the hand by a learned system of marks (இலக்கணக் கிடக்கை); the technical terms of line 1 (கிலுத்தம், பரம்) and the measure of three fingers are not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1645","lb":"CC1645","ev":"The king reads the archer's marks on Civakan's hand.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தடமித்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1646":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1646,"t":["அண்ணலஞ் சிலைவ லாரு ளமோகமா வாசா னிற்பின்","விண்ணகு வெள்ளி வெற்பின் விஞ்சைய ருலகி னல்லான்","மண்ணகத் தில்லை யென்பார் வாயினை மடங்க வந்தான்","புண்ணகத் துறையும் வேலா னெனப்புகழ்ந் தரசன் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The king praises him: among bowmen, where an unfailing master stands, he has come to silence those who say that none but the Vidyadharas of the silver mountain is his equal on earth.","n":"Line 1 (அமோகமா ஆசான்) is obscure; it may name a famed archery master or call Civakan an unfailing teacher.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1646","lb":"CC1646","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தடமித்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1647":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1647,"t":["விற்றிற னம்பி தேற்றான் விருந்தின னிவனு மன்றி","மற்றுமோர் நால்வ ருள்ளார் மாண்பினால் வளர்ந்த தில்லை","கொற்றநீ கொடுக்கல் வேண்டுங் குறையெனக் குருசி னேர்ந்தா","னற்றைநா ளாதி யாக வவர்களும் பயிலு கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The king says his son has not mastered the bow, and that besides him four others have not grown to excellence, and asks the victorious guest to grant them his skill; the prince agrees, and from that day they train.","n":"விருந்தினன் may call the prince a novice or refer to Civakan as the guest; the request turns the hidden prince into a teacher of princes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","guru","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1647","lb":"CC1647","ev":"The king asks Civakan to teach archery to his son and four others, and Civakan agrees.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்","சீவகன்","நால்வர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தடமித்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1648":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1648,"t":["கழலிற் செந்தா மரையடிகள் புல்லித்தங் காதல் கூர","நிழலி னீங்கார் நினைத்தன நினைப்பி னமைவா னாக்கி","யழலிற் சாரா தகலா தொழுக வொருநா ளவன்போகிப்","பொழிலின் மிக்க தனிற்புக்கான் மணமகளிர் போற்பொ லிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The pupils cling to his lotus feet in love and never leave his shadow; he makes them able to do whatever they think of, and they keep near him as men near a fire, neither too close nor too far; one day he goes out to a grove that shines like a bride.","n":"அழலிற் சாராது அகலாது ஒழுக is the image of Kural 691, in other words, for courtiers with a king; here it is the pupils' conduct toward a teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","kalvi","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1648","lb":"CC1648","ev":"The princes train under Civakan; one day he goes into a flowering grove.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","நால்வர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1649":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1649,"t":["பாசிப் பாசத்துப் பைம்பொ னிரைத்தாலி பூத்த வேங்கை","மாசில்வெண் டுகிலை நீர்தோய்த்து மேற்போர்த்த வண்ண மேபோற்","காசின்மட் டொழுகப் பூத்த வழிஞ்சில்கண் ணார்கவின் கொண்டன","பேசிற் செந்தலைய வெண்கறைய புன்கம் பொரிய ணிந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"In the grove the vengai blooms like a gold necklace strung on moss-green cord, like white cloth dipped in water and thrown over, the azhinjil drips honey, and the punku trees wear red-headed, white-spotted puffed blossom.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1649","lb":"CC1649","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1650":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1650,"t":["கோடு தையாக் குழிசியோ டாரங் கொளக்கு யிற்றிய","வோடு தேர்க்கான் மலர்ந்தன வகுள முயர்சண் பகங்","கூடு கோழிக் கொழுமுள் ளரும்பின வங்கோ சிக","வாடை பூத்தன பாதிரி வெண்க டம்புபந் தணிந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The vakulam, the tall champak, the kongu and other trees have flowered, some likened to pots and chariot wheels, the cock-spur buds have opened, and the patiri and white kadamba wear their round blossoms.","n":"The similes of line 1 (pots, chariot wheels) are hard to fix to particular trees.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1650","lb":"CC1650","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1651":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1651,"t":["வெருகு வேட்பச் சிரிப்பனபோன் முகைத்த முல்லை வெய்யவா","ளரவு பைத்தா வித்தன்ன வங்காந்த ளவிழ்ந்த லர்ந்தன","குரவங் கொண்ட குறும்பூழ்போற் கொழுங்கான் முகைசு மந்தன","குருதிக் கூரெயிறு கூத்தியர்கட் கொண்ட கொடித்த ளவமே."],"p":"","r":"Mullai buds as if laughing to tempt the wild cat, kanthal opening like a cobra's spread hood, kuravam buds like quail, and the talavam with buds like a dancer's red sharp teeth fill the grove.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1651","lb":"CC1651","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1652":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1652,"t":["சொன்ன நன்மலரு மல்லனவும் வீழ்பல வின்சூழ் சுளைகளு","நன்மை நூலி னயந்தோன்ற நன்பொன் விரலி னுதியினாற்","பன்மணியு முத்தும் பவளமும் பைம்பொன்னுங் கோத்தா லொப்ப","வென்ன வமரரு மருளத் தொடுத்தா னினமா லையே."],"p":"","r":"With those flowers and others, and fallen jackfruit segments, he strings a garland by the rules of the art with the tips of his fingers, so that it looks like gems, pearls, coral and gold strung together and even the gods marvel.","n":"The garland-making is one more of the hero's arts done by the book (நன்மை நூலின் நயம் தோன்ற), after archery; it prepares the next scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1652","lb":"CC1652","ev":"Civakan strings a garland of the grove's flowers.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:70"},"cc:1653":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1653,"t":["ஊனுண் சிங்கக் குழவி யெயிற்றே ரொளியெ யிற்றினான்","றேனுண் போதிற் பிணையலும் பந்தும் புனைந்து தேமார்ந்த","நானந் தோய்த்து நனைகலவை நாறு மதந்தெ ளித்தபின்","பானுண் டீஞ்சொல் லாளோர் படுவிவண் டார்ப்பவந் திறைஞ்சினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, whose shining teeth are like a lion cub's, makes a garland and a flower-ball from honeyed blossoms, steeps them in perfume and sprinkles musk, and then a serving woman of milk-sweet speech comes, bees humming about her, and bows to him.","n":"The garland is Civakan's own work (the princess later calls it the one he himself made, 1663); the maid is named only at 1668.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1653","lb":"CC1653","ev":"Civakan makes a perfumed garland and flower-ball, and Kanakamalai's maid comes and bows to him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அநங்கவிலாசினி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1654":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1654,"t":["நெடிய வாட்கண்கள் வாயா விமைப்பெ னுஞ்சொல் லின்மற்றெங்","கொடியிற் கொத்த விவையென்றா ணம்பியுங் கொள்க வென்றான்","வடுவும் வேலு மலருங் கயலும் வனப்ப ழித்தகண்","ணடியஞ் சிலம்பி னாட்குய்த் திறைஞ்சிக் காட்ட வவள்கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The maid says that eyes cannot blink before such things and that they suit her mistress, Civakan tells her to take them, and she carries them to the princess, bows and shows them, and the princess takes them.","n":"Line 1 (வாயா இமைப்பு) is hard to construe; the reading that the maid praises the work so that the eye cannot close is mine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thoothu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1654","lb":"CC1654","ev":"The maid asks for the garland for her mistress, Civakan gives it, and Kanakamalai receives it.","cl":[],"ag":["அநங்கவிலாசினி","சீவகன்","கனகமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1655":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1655,"t":["கொண்டு கோதை மலரெழுத்து மெல்விரலின் மேற்றாங்கி நோக்கும்","வண்டு சேர்ந்த குழலாள் வருமுலைகள் பாய வண்டார்","விண்டு தேன்று ளிப்ப வேற்றடங்கண் டாமாடு நாடகங்","கண்டு வாழா தவர்வாழ்க்கை யெல்லாஞ் சவரர் வாழ்க்கையே."],"p":"","r":"Holding the garland with its flower-lettering on her soft fingers, the princess gazes at it while bees settle on her hair and drip honey, and the narrator says that those who have not seen the dance of her spear-like eyes live a life no better than hill hunters'.","n":"மலரெழுத்து suggests the garland carries letters worked in flowers, a message as well as a gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1655","lb":"CC1655","ev":"Kanakamalai holds Civakan's garland and gazes at it.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1656":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1656,"t":["ஆம்ப னாறு மரக்கார் பவழ வாயா ரமுதன்னார்","பாம்பு பைத்தாங் கனைய பவழப் படவர வல்குலார்","தாம்ப லருமருட் டவகி றவழுந் தண்பூ வணைக்","காம்பின் மென்றோள் கவின்வளர வைகல் கலப்பென் பவே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator gives the common saying that for women with coral lips, nectar-sweet and with hips like a hooded serpent, daily union on a cool flower bed makes their bamboo-soft shoulders grow in beauty.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', marks this and 1657 as received wisdom about love set in the narrative pause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","nalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1656","lb":"CC1656","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1657":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1657,"t":["ஆகந் தானோர் மணிப்பலகை யாக முலைக ணாயாகப்","போகக் கேற்ற புனைபவழ வல்குல் கழக மாக","வேக வின்பக் காமக் கவறாட லியைவ தன்றே","லாக நோற்றிட் டடங்க லாண்மைக் கழகென் பவே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator turns the woman's body into a dice game, chest as the gem board, breasts as dice and hips as the gaming hall, and says that if this headlong game of love cannot be played, then restraint won by penance is, as they say, the beauty of manliness.","n":"The maxim is general, not tied to a school in the verse, though penance (நோற்று) and restraint are the Jain epic's later end for Civakan; it sits between the princess receiving the garland and her lament, as if a comment on Civakan's hesitation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","aanmai","soodhu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1657","lb":"CC1657","ev":"","cl":[["when love's pleasure is not to be had, restraint gained through penance is what becomes a man","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1658":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1658,"t":["பின்னி விட்ட பிடித்தடக்கை யிரண்டு போன்று திரண்டழகார்","கன்னிக் கலிங்க மகிலார்ந்து கவவிக் கிடந்த குறங்கினாண்","மின்னுக் குழையும் பொற்றோடு மிளிர வெருத்த மிடங்கோட்டி","யென்னு மிமையா ணினைத்திருந்தா ளியக்கி யிருந்த வெழிலொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The princess sits with thighs like an elephant's trunk under a fine cloth, earrings and gold ear-discs glittering, her neck tilted, unblinking, lost in thought, like a yakshi seated in beauty.","n":"இமையாள், unblinking, is the mark of a goddess or yakshi, and the simile completes it: love has fixed her like an image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamanoy","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1658","lb":"CC1658","ev":"Kanakamalai sits motionless, thinking of Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1659":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1659,"t":["கொடுவெஞ் சிலையைக் கொளையமைத்துக் கொதிநீர்ப் பகழி கொளவாங்கிக்","கடுவெங் குறவ னெயப்பட்ட கன்னிப் பிணையி னிலைகலங்கித்","தொடிதோ ணடப்பத் தோடேம்பத் துணைவெம் முலைகள் பசப்பூர","நெடுமாத் தோகை மென்சாய னெஞ்சிற் கிவ்வா றுரைக்கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Like a young doe struck by a fierce hunter's arrow, she is shaken, her bangled arms grow thin, her shoulders waste and pallor spreads over her breasts, and the peacock-like girl speaks thus to her own heart.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pasalai","nenju","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1659","lb":"CC1659","ev":"Kanakamalai wastes with longing and begins to speak to her heart.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1660":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1660,"t":["ஒன்றே யெயிற்ற தொருபெரும்பே யுலகம் விழுங்க வங்காந்து","நின்றாற் போல நிழலுமிழ்ந்து நெடுவெண் டிங்க ளெயிறிலங்க","வின்றே குருதி வானவா யங்காந் தென்னை விழுங்குவா","னன்றே வந்த திம்மாலை யளியே னாவி யாதாங்கொல்."],"p":"","r":"She sees the evening as a great one-toothed demon gaping to swallow the world, the long white moon its gleaming tooth and the red sky its bloody mouth, come today to swallow her, and asks what will become of her pitiable life.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","uyir","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1660","lb":"CC1660","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகமாலை",""],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1661":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1661,"t":["வருந்தி யீன்றாண் மறந்தொழிந்தாள் வளர்த்தாள் சொற்கேட் டில்கடிந்தாண்","முருந்தின் காறுங் கூழையை முனிவார் நின்னை யென்முனிவார்","பொருந்திற் றன்றா லிதுவென்னாய் பொன்று மளித்திவ் வுயிரென்னாய்","திருந்து சோலைக் கருங்குயிலே சிலம்ப விருந்து கூவுதியால்."],"p":"","r":"She tells the dark cuckoo of the grove that the mother who bore it forgot it and the one who reared it drove it from the house, so who would scold it, yet it never says this is wrong or that her life will perish, but sits and calls on.","n":"The cuckoo raised by another bird (the crow, not named) is reproached as one without kin to teach it pity, so its call is heartless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1661","lb":"CC1661","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகமாலை","குயில்"],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1662":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1662,"t":["பெருவெண் டிங்கண் மாலகப்பூ மலைந்து பெட்ப நகுகின்ற","துருவு கொண்ட குரலன்றி லுயிர்மே லாம்ப லுலாய்நிமிரு","மொருபெண் பாலென் யானாக வுலக மெல்லாம் பாகையாகி","யெரிகொன் றீன்ற விலைப்பலிபோ லிருத்தி யாலென் னின்னுயிரே."],"p":"","r":"With the great white moon, the jasmine evening, the anril's call and the ambal's scent all moving upon her life, she, one woman, finds the whole world her enemy, and tells her life that it stays on like an offering set out for the fire to take.","n":"Lines 2 and 4 are hard to divide; பாகை is read here as பகை, and எரிகொன்று ஈன்ற இலைப்பலி is construed only roughly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","uyir","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1662","lb":"CC1662","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகமாலை",""],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1663":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1663,"t":["வேமென் னெஞ்ச மெய்வெதும்பும் விடுக்கு மாவி வெய்துயிர்க்கும்","பூமென் குழலார் புறநோக்கி நகுவார் நகுவ தாயினேன்","றாம மார்பன் றான் புனைந்த தண்ணென் மாலை புணையாக","யாமக் கடலை நீந்துவேன் யாரு மில்லாத் தமியேனே."],"p":"","r":"Her heart burns, her body is fevered, her breath comes hot and leaving, flower-haired women look at her and laugh until she is a thing to laugh at, and alone with no one she will swim the sea of the night with the cool garland the hero made as her raft.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","alar","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1663","lb":"CC1663","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகமாலை",""],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1664":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1664,"t":["செம்பொற் கடம்பன் செவ்வேலுங் காமன் சிலையிற் றொடுத்தலர்ந்த","வம்பும் வென்ற வரிநெடுங்க ணம்மா மதிவாண் முகத்தினாள்","வம்பு பூத்து முருகுலாய்த் தேன்கொப் புளித்து நின்றதோர்","கொம்பு வெந்தீ யிடைப்பட்ட தொத்தாள் விரைசெய் கோதையே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says the moon-faced girl, whose long streaked eyes outdo Murugan's red spear and the flower arrows from Kama's bow, was like a flowering branch, fragrant and dripping honey, caught in a fierce fire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1664","lb":"CC1664","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1665":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1665,"t":["மணிநிற மாமை சாயல் வளையொடு வண்டு மூசு","மணிநிறப் போர்வை யாய வரும்பெற னாணும் விற்றுப்","பணிநலம் புதிய துண்டான் பன்மலர் மாலை கொண்டேன்","பிணிநிறப் புறஞ்சொ லென்னும் பெருமிஞி றார்ப்ப வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says she sold her dark gem-like colour, her softness, her bangles and the precious modesty that was her covering, and bought with them a garland of many flowers, while the great bee of slander hums about her.","n":"Love is told as a bargain: modesty, called her mantle (போர்வை), is the price, and the gossip (புறஞ்சொல்) is its cost; the garland is all she has in return.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","alar","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1665","lb":"CC1665","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகமாலை",""],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1666":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1666,"t":["காதலான் காதல் போல வகன்று நீண் டலர்ந்தவாட்கட்","போதுலாங் கோதை மாதர் புனைந்தலர் தொடுத்த மாலை","யாதலா லலர தாகா தொழியுமே யழுங்க லென்று","மாதுலா மழலைச் செவ்வாய் மடக்கிளி மொழிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Her young parrot tells her not to grieve, since the garland was strung of flowers by a girl whose eyes spread wide as her lover's love, as though asking whether a thing made of flowers (அலர்) could fail to become talk (அலர்), and so comforts her.","n":"The verse turns on அலர் as flower and as gossip; whether the parrot says the talk will cease or that it cannot be helped depends on how ஒழியுமே is taken, and who strung the garland (line 2) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1666","lb":"CC1666","ev":"Kanakamalai's parrot speaks to comfort her.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கிளி","கனகமாலை"],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1667":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1667,"t":["என்னை யுள்ளம் பிணித்தென் னலங்க வர்ந்தவீர்ந் தாரினான்","றன்னை யானும் பிணிப்பே னெனத்தன் மணிச்செப் பினுண்","மன்னு மாலை கொடுத்தவனுக் குய்த்தீ யெனத்தொ ழுதுகொண்","டன்ன மென்னா வொதுங்கிச் சிலம்பரற் றச்சென் றணுகினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that he has bound her heart and stolen her beauty and she too will bind him, gives the maid a lasting garland from her gem casket to take to him, and the maid takes it with joined hands and goes swan-like, anklets ringing, until she reaches him.","n":"பிணித்து / பிணிப்பேன: the garland that bound her is to be answered by a garland that binds him, so the exchange of flowers is an exchange of bonds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thoothu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1667","lb":"CC1667","ev":"Kanakamalai sends her own garland to Civakan by the maid.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை","அநங்கவிலாசினி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகமாலை","அநங்கவிலாசினி"],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1668":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1668,"t":["அணுகி முன்னின்ற வநங்க விலாசினி யங்கை கூப்பிப்","பிணைய னீட்டப் பெருந்தகை யஃதே லான்முக நோக்கலுந்","துணையி றோகை யென்னங் கைக்குத் தொங்கறொடுப் பாயுநீ","மணிசெய் மென்றோண் மருந்துநீ யாருயிரு நீயே லென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Anangavilasini stands before him, joins her palms and holds out the garland, and when the noble one does not take it but looks at her face, she tells him that he strings garlands for her peerless peacock's hand, he is the medicine for her shoulders and her very life, so let him take it.","n":"Civakan's hesitation is the first sign that taking the garland means more than a courtesy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","marundhu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1668","lb":"CC1668","ev":"The maid offers Kanakamalai's garland; Civakan hesitates and she presses him.","cl":[],"ag":["அநங்கவிலாசினி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அநங்கவிலாசினி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1669":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1669,"t":["மன்னர் கோயி லுறைவார் பொறிசெ றித்த மாண்பினரே","யென்ன வஞ்சி னாயென் றவனைநக் காட்கஃ தன்றுகோதா","யின்ன கொள்கை யேற்கேலா தென்ன விலங்கெ யிற்றினா","ளன்ன மன்ன நடையினா டான்வ ருந்து மெனநேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"She laughs at him, asking why he fears when those who live in kings' palaces keep their seals of honour, and he answers that it is not that, but that such a course does not suit him; then, thinking that the swan-gaited girl will suffer, he agrees.","n":"The split of the dialogue is my reading: the line on palace dwellers with sealed honour (பொறி செறித்த மாண்பினர்) may instead be his scruple and the laughter hers; கோதாய் addresses the maid. He yields from pity for her pain, not from desire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","adakkam","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1669","lb":"CC1669","ev":"Civakan, teased by the maid, says secret love is not his way but consents for the princess's sake.","cl":[["secret dealings in love do not befit him","general","reported",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["அநங்கவிலாசினி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1670":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1670,"t":["குலிக மார்ந்த கொழுந்தா மரையன்ன வண்கை நீட்டி","யொலிய லேற்றா னிஃதூழ் வினையாலுள் ளஞ்சுடு மாலென்ன","விலைகொள் பைம்பூ ணிளமுலையாள் போகிக் கனக மாலை","மெலிய வெம்பி நைகின்றா ளுய்யும் வகைதொ டங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"He holds out his lotus-like hand and takes the garland, saying it comes by former deed and burns the heart, and the maid goes off and begins the means by which Kanakamalai, wasting and fevered, may be saved.","n":"Who says இஃது ஊழ்வினையால் உள்ளஞ் சுடும் is not clear: Civakan as he accepts, or the maid of the princess's fever; the claim is given to Civakan on the first reading. Either way the love is put down to ஊழ்வினை while Civakan's own consent was shown in 1669, so destiny and choice are both given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","kaamanoy","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1670","lb":"CC1670","ev":"Civakan accepts Kanakamalai's garland and the maid begins remedies for her.","cl":[["love's burning comes as the fruit of a former deed","general","reported",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","அநங்கவிலாசினி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1671":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1671,"t":["நீர்செய் காந்த மணிகூந்த ளம்பா வைநீண் டழகியவோ?","செய் சாந்திற் கழுநீர் விரைகம ழும்பூக்கள் கோத்த","வார்செய் தண்டா மரைவளைய மைவரை யின்வெள் ளருவிநீர்","சீர்செய் கோமகளைச் சேர்த்தினாள் சீதஞ் செய்யா தொழிந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"She lays on the princess moonstone that sheds water, fragrant sandal, blossoms of kazhuneer, lotus bracelets on thongs and water from a white mountain waterfall, and none of them cool her.","n":"Line 2 begins with a doubtful syllable in the edition (printed with a query), and the opening words of line 1 divide uncertainly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1671","lb":"CC1671","ev":"The maid applies cooling things to Kanakamalai, without effect.","cl":[],"ag":["அநங்கவிலாசினி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1672":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1672,"t":["பவ்வத் தங்கட் பிறந்து பனிபெயர்க் குந்தண் ணூற்றதாகி","யெவ்வ மன்னர் படவுலகம் விற்கு மரும ணியினைச்","செவ்வ னூலிற் சித்திரிக்கப் பட்டதனைச் சேர்த்திப் பின்னு","மவ்வ னாறுங் குழலாட்கு மற்று மிவைக ணாடினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She also fastens on her a rare gem born in the sea, cool and able to drive off heat, worth the world for which kings suffer, worked on a fine thread, and still more such things on the fragrant-haired girl.","n":"பனிபெயர்க்கும் may mean the gem removes affliction or sheds coolness; the reading is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1672","lb":"CC1672","ev":"The maid puts a cooling sea-gem on Kanakamalai.","cl":[],"ag":["அநங்கவிலாசினி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1673":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1673,"t":["களிசெய் கோசிக நீர்விழக் கடிமாலை மேற்றொ டர்ந்துகீழ்","நளிசெய் தண்பூஞ் சலஞ்சயன மாக்கி நன்னீர் பிலிற்றும்வாய்க்","குளிர்கொள் சாந்தாற்றி பொன்னால வட்டங்கொண் டேந்தி வீசச்","சளிகொள் சந்தின் கொழுஞ்சாந்த மாக முழுது மெழுகினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She lets fragrant water fall over garlands, makes a cool flower couch below them, fans her with a moist fan and a golden round fan, and smears her whole body with rich cool sandal paste.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1673","lb":"CC1673","ev":"The maid makes Kanakamalai a flower bed, fans her and covers her in sandal.","cl":[],"ag":["அநங்கவிலாசினி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1674":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1674,"t":["கொம்மைவெம் முலையிற் சாந்தங் குளிர்செயா தாவி வாட்ட","வம்மென் மாலை முகங்கரிய நீர்துளும்ப நின்று நீடி","வெம்மை மிக்கது வீரன் றொடுத்த விளங்கு மாலை","பொம்ம லோதிக்குத் தானே துணையாம் புணையா யிற்றே."],"p":"","r":"The sandal on her breasts does not cool them but withers her life, the soft garlands blacken and drip, and only the bright garland the hero strung becomes the companion and raft of the girl with heavy hair.","n":"Every remedy fails and the only relief is the token of the one who causes the fever: the garland is both the sickness and its cure, as the maid called him her medicine in 1668.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1674","lb":"CC1674","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1675":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1675,"t":["வாச நீலங் கழுநீர் குவளை படைசாற் றிவந்","தோச னைக்க ணுடையு நெடுங்கட் கனக மாலை","தாசிதூ தாகத் தாமம் புணையாகச் செல்லு நாளுட்","காசில் கல்விக் கடலைக் கரைகண்டார் காளை மாரே."],"p":"","r":"While long-eyed Kanakamalai passes her days with the maid as her messenger and the garland as her raft, the young men reach the farther shore of the faultless sea of learning.","n":"One verse joins the two threads: her sea of night (1663) and the princes' sea of learning, both crossed, hers only with a raft of flowers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","thoothu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1675","lb":"CC1675","ev":"Kanakamalai's love continues through the maid, and the princes complete their training under Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை","காளைமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1676":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1676,"t":["பொருசரஞ் சலாகை வெய்ய புகன்றனர் துரக்கு மாறும்","வருகணை விலக்கு மாறும் வாளமர் நீந்து மாறுங்","கருவியுட் கரக்கு மாறுங் கணைபுறங் காணு மாறும்","விரியமற் றவர்க்குக் காட்ட வீற்றிருந் தவருங் கற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan shows them in full how to drive the fighting arrow and the dart, how to ward off a coming arrow, how to swim through sword battle, how to hide within armour and how to see an arrow's back, and even those who sat in state learned.","n":"வீற்றிருந்தவரும் கற்றார் may mean the king and elders seated in state learned too, or that the princes learned while sitting at ease; கருவியுட் கரக்கும் ஆறு is read as hiding within one's gear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1676","lb":"CC1676","ev":"The princes learn the arts of weapons from Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","காளைமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:71"},"cc:1677":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1677,"t":["வேலுடைத் தடக்கை யார்கள் வேழமேற் சென்ற போழ்திற்","காலிடைக் கரக்கு மாறுங் கையிடைத் திரியு மாறும்","வாலிடை மறியு மாறு மருப்பிடைக் குளிக்கு மாறு","நூலிடைக் கிடந்த வாறே நுனித்தவன் கொடுப்பக் கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The spear-handed princes, riding elephants, take from their keen teacher, exactly as the treatise lays it down, how to hide between the elephant's legs, wheel at its trunk, roll at its tail and plunge between its tusks.","n":"நூலிடைக் கிடந்தவாறே, 'just as it lies in the book', makes the elephant craft a written science that Civakan transmits faithfully rather than a skill he invents.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1677","lb":"CC1677","ev":"Civakan teaches the king's sons the moves of fighting on and around the war elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["மக்கள்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1678":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1678,"t":["கொடிநெடுந் தேரின் போருங் குஞ்சரங் குறித்த போருங்","கடுநடைப் புரவிப் போருங் கரப்பறக் கற்று முற்றி","யிடனறிந் திலங்கும் வைவா ளிருஞ்சிலை குந்த மூன்று","முடனறிந் தும்ப ரார்க்கு முரைப்பருந் தகைய ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"Learning without concealment the fighting of the tall-bannered chariot, of the elephant and of the fast horse, and knowing where each fits the sword, the great bow and the lance, they become warriors whose quality even the gods could hardly describe.","n":"கரப்பற, 'with nothing held back', credits the teacher as much as the pupils: he kept no secret of his art from them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1678","lb":"CC1678","ev":"The princes complete their training in chariot, elephant and horse combat and in sword, bow and lance.","cl":[],"ag":["மக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1679":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1679,"t":["தானையு ளன்றி நின்ற தனியிட மொற்றி மன்னர்","தானைமேற் சென்ற போழ்தும் வென்றியிற் றளர்த லின்றித்","தானையை யுடைக்கும் வெம்போர்த் தருக்கினார் மைந்த ரென்று","தானைசூழ் மன்னற் குய்த்தார் மன்னனுந் தருக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Reporting that the princes, proud in fierce war, can break an army, and do not flag in victory even when kings bear down on their force while they stand alone apart from their own lines, they bring them to the king ringed by his army, and he says 'bring them in'.","n":"Who brings them is not named (உய்த்தார்); line 1, தனியிடம் ஒற்றி, may mean fighting alone apart from the army or something else, and the reading is offered tentatively.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1679","lb":"CC1679","ev":"The princes are presented to their father the king, who orders them brought before him.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்","மக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1680":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1680,"t":["மழையொடு சூழ்ந்து கொண்ட வான்றுகள் சிலையி னீக்கிக்","குழைமுக நெற்றி நக்கக் கோலவிற் பகழி வாங்கி","யிழைபக விமைப்பி னெய்திட் டெறிந்துமின் றிரிவ வேபோல்","விழைவுறு குமரர் புக்குச் சாரியை வியத்த ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"Clearing from the bow the dust that gathers like a cloud, drawing the fine bow's arrow until it touches the earring at the brow, splitting a thread with a shot in the blink of an eye, the eager princes enter the arena and amaze all with their wheeling movements, darting like lightning.","n":"Line 1 (மழையொடு சூழ்ந்து கொண்ட வான்துகள் சிலையின் நீக்கி) is hard to construe: it may be clearing dust or scattering a cloud of arrows; சாரியை names the formal circling movements of combat display.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1680","lb":"CC1680","ev":"The princes display their archery and their circling manoeuvres before the king.","cl":[],"ag":["மக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1681":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1681,"t":["விசயனே விசயன் விற்போர்க் கதம்பனே முருகன் வேற்போர்த்","திசையெலாம் வணக்கும் வாட்போர்க் கந்தணன் செம்பொ னாம","னசைவிலான் யானைத் தேர்ப்போர்க் கசலனே யசல கீர்த்தி","வசையிலான் புரவிச் சேனென் றியாவரும் புகழப் பட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Everyone praises them: in archery this one is Vijaya himself, in the lance another is Murugan, another in the sword bends all directions, one firm in elephant and chariot fighting is Achala of unmoving fame, and another without blemish is master of the horse.","n":"The verse seems to name the princes (விசயன், perhaps அதம்பன் or கதம்பன், அசலன்) each with the arm he excels in, but the run-together script makes several names and their divisions uncertain; the comparisons to Vijaya (Arjuna) and Murugan are the clear part.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","kalvi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1681","lb":"CC1681","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1682":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1682,"t":["காவலன் மக்க ளாக்கங் கண்டுகண் குளிர்ந்து நோக்கி","யேவலா னரச னொன்றோ விருபிறப் பாள னல்லார்க்","காவதன் றின்ன மாட்சி யவனையா னிகளம் பெய்து","காவல்செய் திடுவல் வல்லே காளையைக் கொணர்மி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing his sons' accomplishment the king gazes with cooled eyes and says that such excellence comes from no one but a king or one twice-born; he will put the youth in fetters and keep him under guard: bring the young bull at once.","n":"The 'fetter' (நிகளம்) is a joke whose point 1684 reveals: he means to bind Civakan by marriage. His inference from skill to birth happens to be true, since Civakan is a king's son unknown to him, so the story neither confirms nor answers the claim as a rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pirappu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1682","lb":"CC1682","ev":"The delighted king orders Civakan brought, saying he will fetter him and keep him.","cl":[["such mastery in teaching arms belongs only to one born a king or a twice-born","general","reported",2,"dadamittan"]],"ag":["தடமித்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தடமித்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1683":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1683,"t":["கல்வியுங் கொடிது போலுங் காவலன் காளை தன்னை","யொல்லலன் சிறைசெய் கின்றா னென்றவன் கருதிற் றோரார்","பல்சனம் பாபிந்து நிற்பப் பார்த்திப குமரன் சோர்ந்தான்","வில்வலாற் கொண்டு வேந்தன் வேறிருந் திதனைச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Not grasping what the king meant, the crowd thinks that learning too seems a cruel thing, since the king now cruelly imprisons the youth, and stands pitying him while the prince himself loses heart; the king takes the bowman aside and says this.","n":"The crowd's maxim, கல்வியும் கொடிது போலும், is answered at once: 1684 shows the 'imprisonment' to be a reward, so the reflection stands as a misreading of the king's குறிப்பு. பாபிந்து in line 3 is read as பரிந்து.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kurippu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1683","lb":"CC1683","ev":"The people and Civakan take the king's words for a real arrest, and the king draws Civakan aside to speak.","cl":[["learning can turn against the one who has it, bringing him to harm","general","refuted",1,"city_people"]],"ag":["மாந்தர்","சீவகன்","தடமித்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1684":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1684,"t":["புண்முழு திறைஞ்சுங் கோட்டுப் பொருகளி றனைய தோன்றன்","மண்முழு தன்றி வானும் வந்துகை கூடத் தந்தாய்","கண்முழு துடம்பிற் பெற்றேன் காளைகைம் மாறு காணேன்","பண்முழு துடற்றுந் தீஞ்சொற் பாவைநின் பால ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king tells the prince, like a fighting tusker whose tusks are wholly bowed in wounds, that he has put into his hands not the earth alone but heaven too; he feels he has eyes all over his body, sees no way to repay the young bull, and says the girl whose sweet words outdo all music is his.","n":"The 'fetter' of 1682 turns out to be the bride: the joke depends on the king's word நிகளம் and the crowd's mistaking it. கண்முழுது உடம்பிற் பெற்றேன், 'I have got eyes over my whole body', says his joy needs more eyes to see his sons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","makkal","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1684","lb":"CC1684","ev":"The king offers his daughter to Civakan in return for training his sons.","cl":[["a service that wins one earth and heaven has no repayment that can match it","general","reported",3,"dadamittan"]],"ag":["தடமித்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தடமித்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1685":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1685,"t":["முடிகெழு மன்னன் சொல்ல மொய்கொள்வேற் குருசி றேற்றான்","வடிவமை மனனொன் றாக வாக்கொன்றா மறுத்த லோடுந்","தடிசுவைத் தொளிறும் வேலான் றன்கையான் முன்கை பற்றி","யிடிமுர சனைய சொல்லா லிற்றென விளம்பு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When the crowned king had spoken, the prince of the clustered spear, his mind of one shape and his word of another, declined; then the king, whose spear gleams having tasted flesh, took his forearm in his own hand and spoke with words like a thundering drum.","n":"தேற்றான் at line 1 and the phrase மனனொன்றாக வாக்கொன்றா மறுத்தல் are read as the custom of a first refusal: his heart consents while his words decline. If தேற்றான் means 'reassured', the sense changes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","naanam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1685","lb":"CC1685","ev":"Civakan politely declines in words though willing at heart, and the king grasps his arm and presses the offer.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1686":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1686,"t":["பூவியல் கோயில் கொண்ட பொன்னனா ளனைய நங்கை","காவியங் கண்ணி வந்து பிறத்தலுங் கண்க ளீண்டி","மூவிய றிரித லின்றிச் சாதக முறையிற் செய்தா","ரேவியல் சிலையி னாய்க்கே யுரியளென் றுரைப்ப நேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king says that when the maiden like the golden lady of the lotus, with kaavi-flower eyes, was born, the wise gathered and cast her horoscope by rule without straying from the three, and declared her the due of the one whose bow speeds arrows; and Civakan consents.","n":"The bride is promised not as a reward alone but as fated: the reckoning made her உரியள், 'rightfully belonging', to a master of the bow, and Civakan's archery training is the sign that fulfils it. மூவியல் is unclear (perhaps three factors of the reckoning).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","nimittam","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1686","lb":"CC1686","ev":"The king cites the birth horoscope that destined his daughter for an archer, and Civakan agrees to the marriage.","cl":[["a horoscope cast by rule at birth fixes the man a girl belongs to","general","reported",4,"dadamittan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தடமித்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1687":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1687,"t":["வாருலா முலையி னாட்கும் வரிசிலைத் தடக்கை யாற்குஞ்","சீருலாங் கோலஞ் செய்தார் செப்பினார் வதுவை நன்னாட்","பாரெலா மறிய நின்று படாமுர சார்ப்பத் தீவேட்","டேருலாங் கோதை யின்பத் திளநலம் பருகு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"They dress the girl with the bodice-bound breasts and the long-armed archer in festive finery, announce the good wedding day, and with the whole world knowing, drums sounding, he weds her by the fire and drinks the young delight of the garlanded one.","n":"பாரெலாம் அறிய, 'with all the earth knowing', and the fire (தீவேட்டு) mark this as an open wedding, in contrast to the secret unions of some earlier ilampakams.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1687","lb":"CC1687","ev":"Civakan marries Kanakamalai by the fire rite, publicly, and they are united.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கனகமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1688":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1688,"t":["மோட்டிளங் குரும்பை யன்ன முலைக்கடாக் களிறு முத்தஞ்","சூட்டிய வோடை பொங்க நாணெனுந் தோட்டி மாற்றி","யாட்டிய சாந்த மென்னு முகபடா மழித்து வெம்போ","ரோட்டற வோட்டிப் பைந்தா ருழக்கியிட் டுவந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Her breasts, like young swelling coconuts, become a musth elephant: the pearls are its forehead plate, it throws off the goad called shame, strips the face-cloth called sandal paste, drives the fierce fight to rout and tramples the fresh garland in its joy.","n":"நாணெனுந் தோட்டி, 'the goad called shame', makes நாண் the control the elephant shakes off: in love her modesty is dropped, not lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1688","lb":"CC1688","ev":"The couple's lovemaking is told as the charge of an elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1689":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1689,"t":["ஒண்மணிக் குழைவில் வீச வொளிர்ந்துபொன் னோலை மின்ன","வண்ணமே கலைக ளார்ப்ப வான்சிலம் பொலிப்ப முத்துங்","கண்ணியும் பசும்பொ னாணுங் கதிர்முலை புடைப்பக் காம","ரண்ணலங் குமரன் றன்னொ டாயிழை யாடி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"With jewelled earrings swinging, golden ear-rolls flashing, girdles jingling, anklets ringing and pearls, wreath and gold chain beating on her bright breasts, the woman sports with the lovely prince.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1689","lb":"CC1689","ev":"Kanakamalai and Civakan make love.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1690":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1690,"t":["மூசுதேன் வாரி யல்குற் பட்டபின் முலைக ளென்னு","மாசறு கந்தின் மென்றோண் மணித்தொடர்க் கொளுத்தி வாட்க","ணாசறு வயிரத் தோட்டி நுதலணிந் தமுதச் செவ்வாய்","காசறு கவள மாகக் களிறுகோட் பட்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"After the bee-thronged ford of her hips, the elephant is captured: tied to the flawless posts of her breasts with the jewel-chain of her soft arms, goaded by the faultless diamond of her sword-eyes, the forehead ornament on her brow, and fed her nectared red mouth as its flawless mouthful.","n":"The elephant figure of 1688 is turned round: there her breasts were the rampant elephant; here he is the elephant she captures and tames.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1690","lb":"CC1690","ev":"The lovemaking is told as the capture and taming of an elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1691":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1691,"t":["ஒப்பிணை தனக்கி லாதா னுறுவரை யகல மூழ்கிச்","செப்பிணை யனைய செங்கேழ் வனமுலை பொருதுசேப்பக்","கற்பக மரத்தைப் புல்லிக் கைவிடா தொழிந்து காமத்","துப்புர வுமிழுங் காம வல்லியின் றோற்ற மொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Sinking into the broad hill of the peerless one's chest, her red casket-like breasts reddening as they press, she looks like the desire-vine that clings to the wishing tree and never lets go, giving forth the riches of love.","n":"கற்பக மரம் and காம வல்லி are the wish-granting tree and its creeper of the celestial world; the image makes the union a heavenly enjoyment, the fruit of merit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1691","lb":"CC1691","ev":"Kanakamalai embraces Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1692":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1692,"t":["காய்வுறு வேட்கை தன்னாற் கங்குலும் பகலும் விள்ளான்","வேய்வெறுத் தமைந்த தோளாள் விழுத்திரை யமுத மென்று","சேய்நலங் கடந்த செல்வன் றிருநலந் தெளித்திட் டாற்ற","வாய்விடாள் பருகி யிட்டாண் மடக்கிள்ளை மருட்டுஞ் சொல்லாள்."],"p":"","r":"Burning with desire, never letting him go by night or day, the one with shoulders that shame the bamboo, whose speech beguiles the young parrot, drinks without taking her mouth away the beauty of the one who surpasses Murugan, as if it were the nectar of the great sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1692","lb":"CC1692","ev":"Kanakamalai delights in Civakan night and day.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1693":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1693,"t":["திரையிடைக் கொண்ட வின்னீ ரமுதுயிர் பெற்ற தென்னு","முரையுடைக் கோதை மாத ரொளிநல நுகர்ந்து நாளும்","வரையுடை மார்ப னங்கண் வைகின னென்ப மாதோ","கரைகட லனைய தானைக் காவலன் காத லானே."],"p":"","r":"They say that the beloved of the king whose army is like the shore-bound sea, the mountain-chested one, stayed there day after day enjoying the bright beauty of the garlanded woman, of whom it is said the sea's nectar had taken life.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', puts the narrator at a distance from the scene just as he turns away from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1693","lb":"CC1693","ev":"Civakan stays on in the king's city with his bride.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1694":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1694,"t":["வாளினான் மிடைந்த கண்ணாள் வருமுலைத் தடத்துள் வைகித்","தோளினான் மிடைந்து புல்லித் தொண்டைவா யமுத மாந்திக்","காளைசெல் கின்ற நாளுட் கட்டியங் காரன் மூதூர்","மீளிவேற் குருசிற் குற்றார்க் குற்றது விளம்ப லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"While the young bull lives on, lying among the swelling breasts of the sword-eyed one, clasping her with his arms and drinking the nectar of her coral mouth, I begin to tell what befell, in Kattiyankaran's old city, those who were close to the hero of the fierce spear.","n":"The narrator's own first person (விளம்பல் உற்றேன்) marks the change of scene from Civakan's pleasures to the people he left behind in Rajamapuram.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1694","lb":"CC1694","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1695":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1695,"t":["வெண்மதி யிழந்த மீன்போல் விடலைக்குத் தம்பி மாழாந்","தொண்மதிச் சூழ்ச்சி மிக்கா னுள்ளுழி யுணர்தல் செல்லான்","புண்மதித் துடைந்த போது பொழிந்துமட் டொழுகு நன்னாட்","டுண்மதி வருந்த நாடி யொளிநக ரெய்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The hero's younger brother, desolate as the stars that have lost the white moon, unable to learn where the wise, counsel-rich one was, searched the good land of dripping honey with a grieving mind and came to the bright city.","n":"Line 3 (புண்மதித்து உடைந்த போது) is obscure, and which city ஒளிநகர் means is not said here; the image of stars without the moon is the patikam's own (verse 23).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1695","lb":"CC1695","ev":"Nandattan, failing to find his brother, searches the land and comes to the city.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1696":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1696,"t":["வெள்ளிவெண் மலைக்கு வேந்த னொருமகள் வேற்கட் பாவை","யொள்ளிழை யவளைக் கேட்பா னுறுவலி செல்லு மாங்கண்","வள்ளிதழ்க் கோதை தானே யிட்டதோர் வண்ணந் தன்னைக்","கொள்ளத்தான் முரல லுற்றுக் கோலமை வீணை கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"As the strong one goes to ask the only daughter of the king of the silver mountain, the spear-eyed doll in bright jewels, the woman with the thick-petalled garland takes her well-made vina to play a tune of her own setting.","n":"Gandharvadattai is a Vidyadhara princess with arts beyond the human, which is why the brother comes to her; the song she sets herself (தானே இட்டதோர் வண்ணம்) comes first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1696","lb":"CC1696","ev":"Nandattan goes to Gandharvadattai to ask after Civakan, and she takes up her vina.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1697":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1697,"t":["ஆடகச் செம்பொ னாணி யானெய்வார்ந் தனைய திண்கோன்","மாடக நொண்டு கொண்டு மாத்திரை நிறைய வீக்கிச்","சூடக மணிந்த முன்கைத் தொகுவிரல் சேப்ப வெற்றித்","தோடலர் கோதை கீதந் துணிவினிற் பாடுகின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Fixing the strings of red gold, firm as if poured from molten ghee, tightening the pegs to their full measure until the joined fingers of her bangled hand redden, the woman with the opening garland sings the song with resolve.","n":"மாத்திரை names the measured pitch to which the strings are tightened, the technical vocabulary of the vina the poem has used since the vina contest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1697","lb":"CC1697","ev":"Gandharvadattai tunes her vina and begins to sing.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1698":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1698,"t":["இறுமருங்குல் போதணியி னென்றினைந்து கையி","னறுமலர்கள் சிந்துவார் நண்ணார் துறந்தார்","நண்ணார் துறப்ப நனிவளையுந் தோடுறப்பக்","கண்ணோவா முத்துறைப்பத் தோழி கழிவேனோ."],"p":"","r":"He who feared my slender waist would snap if I wore a flower, and scattered the fragrant flowers from his hand, does not come near; he has left me; as he leaves, my bangles leave my shoulders too and my eyes rain pearls without rest: friend, am I to pass away?","n":"The verb துற runs through the stanza: the lover துறந்தார், and so the bangles துறப்ப; his old tenderness, fearing a flower too heavy for her, is set against his leaving. The plural forms (நண்ணார், துறந்தார்) are honorific.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1698","lb":"CC1698","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1699":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1699,"t":["பூமாலை சூடிற் பொறையாற்றா நுண்மருங்கு","லேமாரா தென்றினைவா ரெண்ணார் துறந்தா","ரெண்ணார் துறப்ப வினவளையுந் தோடுறப்ப","மணணார்வேற் கண்டுயிலா தோழி மருள்வேனோ."],"p":"","r":"He who thought my fine waist could not bear the weight of a flower garland and would not hold, does not think of me; he has left me; as he leaves, my bangles leave my shoulders and my spear-eyes cannot sleep: friend, am I to be bewildered?","n":"The second stanza of the set repeats the first's frame, changing the verb of his neglect (எண்ணார்) and of her suffering (மருள்வேனோ).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1699","lb":"CC1699","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1700":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1700,"t":["வண்டூத வம்மருங்கு னோமென்று பூமாலை","கொண்டோச்சுங் காதலார் கூடார் துறந்தார்","கூடா ரவர்துறப்பக் கோல்வளையுந் தோடுறப்பத்","தோடார்பூங் கண்டுயிலா தோழி துயர்வேனோ."],"p":"","r":"He who, saying my waist ached when the bees buzzed, would wave the flower garland to drive them off, does not join me; he has left me; as he leaves, my rounded bangles leave my shoulders and my flower-eyes cannot sleep: friend, am I to grieve?","n":"The third stanza closes the set: his care went as far as fanning bees from her waist, which makes the absence the sharper. The three stanzas move from death (கழிவேனோ) to bewilderment to grief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1700","lb":"CC1700","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:72"},"cc:1701":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1701,"t":["ஊன்றகர்த் தனைய போன்று மூடெரி முளைப்ப போன்றுந்","தோன்றுபூ விலவத் தங்கட் டொகையணி லனைய பைங்காய்","கான்றமென் பஞ்சி யார்ந்த மெல்லணை யாழ்கை நீக்கித்","தேன்றயங் கிணர்பெய் கோதை சிந்தையி னீட்டி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"Pushing aside with her lute-playing hand the soft cushion stuffed with silk-cotton from pods red as torn flesh and green as squirrels, the garlanded woman lies long lost in thought.","n":"The woman is not named; the yāḻ hand (யாழ்கை) and the next verses' visit of Civakan's younger brother identify her as Gandharvadattai, the vina player.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1701","lb":"CC1701","ev":"Gandharvadattai leaves her cushion and broods on the absent Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1702":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1702,"t":["நுண்ணிய வரியொடு திரண்டு நோக்குநர்","கண்மனங் கவற்றிய காமர் தொண்டைவா","யண்ணலை நினைந்துவெய் துயிர்ப்ப வாய்நலம்","வண்ணத்தின் மழுங்கிவாட் கண்ணி வாடினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking of her lord, she sighs hot breaths, the beauty of her red mouth dulls and the sword-eyed woman wastes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1702","lb":"CC1702","ev":"Gandharvadattai pines for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1703":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1703,"t":["மின்றவழ் மணிவரை மாலை மார்பனை","பொன்றவ ழிளமுலை பொருது புல்லுநா","ளென்றுகொ லெனநினைந் திருந்த செவ்வியுட்","சென்றனன் சீவகற் கிளைய செல்வனே."],"p":"","r":"While she sits wondering when her young breasts will press the garlanded chest of her lord again, Civakan's younger brother comes to her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1703","lb":"CC1703","ev":"Nandattan comes to Gandharvadattai as she longs for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1704":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1704,"t":["ஐவிலி னகல நின்றாங் கடிதொழு திறைஞ்சி னாற்கு","மைவிலை பெற்ற கண்ணாண் மைந்தனை மருண்டு நோக்கிக்","கைவிலுங் கணையு மில்லாக் காமன்போந் திருக்க வென்ன","மொய்வெல்லுங் குருதி வேலான் மூவிற்க ணிறைஞ்சி நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He stands five bow-lengths away and bows at her feet; she looks at him in wonder and bids Kama without bow or arrow come and sit, and the spear-bearer bows again three times.","n":"The distance kept (ஐவில் அகலம்) and the repeated bow mark the deference of a younger brother before his elder brother's wife.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1704","lb":"CC1704","ev":"Nandattan bows to Gandharvadattai from a distance and she bids him sit.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1705":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1705,"t":["திங்கள்வாண் முகமு நோக்கான் றிருமுலைத் தடமு நோக்கா","னங்கதிர்க் கலாப மின்னு மணியல்குற் பரப்பு நோக்கான்","செங்கயற் கண்ணி னாடன் சீறடிச் சிலம்பு நோக்கி","யெங்குளா ரடிக ளென்னா வின்னண மியம்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He looks neither at her moon face, nor her breasts, nor her girdled hips, but only at the anklet on her small foot, and asks where his lord is.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","ozhukkam","piranil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1705","lb":"CC1705","ev":"Nandattan, eyes on her anklet, asks Gandharvadattai where Civakan is.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1706":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1706,"t":["பொறிகுலாய்க் கிடந்த மார்பிற் புண்ணியன் பொன்றி னானேல்","வெறிகுலாய்க் கிடந்த மாலை வெள்வளை முத்த நீக்கி","நெறியினா னோற்ற லொன்றோ நீளெரி புகுத லொன்றோ","வறியலென் கொழுநன் மாய்ந்தா லணிசுமந் திருப்ப தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Nandattan reasons that if the meritorious one were dead she would have put off garland, bangles and pearls and taken either the path of penance or the long fire, for he knows of no wife who bears ornaments when her husband is gone.","n":"The norm is not urged on anyone; it is used as evidence: her ornaments prove to him that his brother lives. The two courses are set side by side with ஒன்றோ ... ஒன்றோ, neither ranked above the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","karpu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1706","lb":"CC1706","ev":"","cl":[["a wife whose husband dies puts off her ornaments and either keeps a vow of penance or enters the fire","general","reported",3,"nandattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1707":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1707,"t":["காய்தழல் கவரப் பட்ட கற்பக மரத்திற் கன்றி","யாய்கழற் குருசில் வாடி யற்புத்தீ யழலு ணிற்ப","வாய்மொழிந் துரைக்க லுற்றாள் வனைகுழற் கற்றை வண்டார்த்","தோய்பிழி துளிக்குங் கண்ணிச் சுரும்புசூழ் கொம்ப னாளே."],"p":"","r":"As the prince withers like a wishing tree caught by fire and stands in the burning of his love for his brother, the bee-hung woman begins to speak.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1707","lb":"CC1707","ev":"Nandattan grieves for his brother and Gandharvadattai begins to answer.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1708":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1708,"t":["மதுமுகத் தலர்ந்த கோதை மாற்றமைந் தற்கு ரைப்பாள்","கொதிமுகக் குருதி வைவேற் குருசிலோ நம்மை யுள்ளான்.","விதிமுக மணங்க ளெய்தி வீற்றிருந் தின்ப முய்ப்ப","மதிமுக மறியு நாமே வாடுவ தென்னை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She answers that the prince with the spear does not think of them; he has gone to the marriages fate brings him and sits enjoying them, and asks why she, who knows it, should waste away.","n":"The sense of மதிமுகம் அறியும் நாமே in line 4 is unclear: it may be 'we who know his ways' or refer to a moon-faced mirror of her lore; the bitterness about விதிமுக மணங்கள், marriages set by fate, is a wife's sulk, not a doctrine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","oozh","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1708","lb":"CC1708","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1709":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1709,"t":["வேண்டிய தெமக்கு நேரின் வில்வலாய் நுமைய னாரைக்","காண்டியென் றுரைப்பக் காளை யெழுமையு மடிமை நேர","மாண்டதோர் விஞ்சை யோதி மதிமுகந் தைவந் திட்டா","ணீண்டது பெரிது மன்றி நினைத்துழி விளக்கிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"She says that if he grants what she asks he will see his brother; the youth agrees to be her servant for seven births, and she recites a fine spell and strokes a moon-like face, which lights up the far place he thinks of as if it were near.","n":"What she strokes (மதிமுகம்) is doubtful: her own face, or a mirror-like surface on which the vision appears; the verse does not say what she asked in return.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinjai","adimai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1709","lb":"CC1709","ev":"Nandattan promises Gandharvadattai lifelong service, and her spell shows him the distant place.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1710":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1710,"t":["பொற்புடை யமளி யங்கட் பூவணைப் பள்ளி மேலாற்","கற்பக மாலை வேய்ந்து கருங்குழற் கைசெய் வானை","முற்படக் கண்டு நோக்கி முறுவல்கொண் முகத்த னாகி","விற்படை நிமிர்ந்த தோளான் றொழுதுமெய் குளிர்ந்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"On a golden couch's flower bed he sees first his brother, crowned with a wishing-tree garland, dressing a woman's dark hair; he smiles, joins his hands and stands with his body cooled.","n":"The woman whose hair Civakan dresses is not named; the ilampakam's story makes her Kanakamalai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinjai","anbu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1710","lb":"CC1710","ev":"In the vision Nandattan sees Civakan dressing his new wife's hair, and worships.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1711":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1711,"t":["செய்த விஞ்சையைத் தேமொழி மாற்றலு","மைய னெஞ்சொடு வண்டிமிர் தாரினான்","பொய்ய தன்மையிற் பூங்கழ லானடிக்","கெய்து கேனரு ளாயென் றிறைஞ்சினான்."],"p":"","r":"When the honey-voiced woman withdraws the spell, he, bewildered at heart since what he saw was only a seeming, bows and begs her grace that he may reach his lord's feet.","n":"பொய்ய தன்மையின் (line 3) is read as the unreality of the image; it could also be a mark of his not lying.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinjai","anbu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1711","lb":"CC1711","ev":"Gandharvadattai ends the vision and Nandattan begs her to send him to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1712":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1712,"t":["மதுக்கை மாலையும் வண்டிமிர் சாந்தமும்","புதுக்கச் சார்ந்தபொன் வாளுஞ் சுரிகையுங்","கதுப்பி னானமுங் காமர் கலங்களும்","பதிக்கட் டம்மெனப் பாவையு மேவினாள்."],"p":"","r":"She orders garlands, sandal paste, a gold sword and a dagger, hair perfume and fine ornaments to be brought to the place.","n":"The division பதிக்கட் டம்மென is doubtful; it is read here as an order to bring the things to the place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinjai","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1712","lb":"CC1712","ev":"Gandharvadattai has gifts and weapons brought for Nandattan's journey.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1713":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1713,"t":["மணியின் மேல்புறம் போர்த்தன்ன மாக்கதிர்","துணிய வீசுந் துளங்கொளி மேனியன்","பணியிற் பல்கலந் தாங்குபு சென்றபி","னணிசெய் கோதையங் காமினி யோதினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The radiant youth puts on the many ornaments as she bids and goes in, and the garlanded woman then recites her spell.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinjai","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1713","lb":"CC1713","ev":"Nandattan adorns himself and Gandharvadattai begins the spell.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1714":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1714,"t":["சாந்தி னான்மெழு கித்தட மாமல","ராய்ந்த தாமங்க ணாற்றி யகிற்புகை","யேந்தி யிட்டிளை யாரொடு நீங்கினாள்","காந்தி வண்டுணுங் கற்பகக் கோதையே."],"p":"","r":"She smears the place with sandal, hangs garlands of chosen flowers, raises agil smoke, and withdraws with her young women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinjai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1714","lb":"CC1714","ev":"Gandharvadattai prepares the chamber with rites and leaves.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1715":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1715,"t":["தூமம் மார்ந்த துகிலணைப் பள்ளிமேற்","காமன் றம்பியிற் காளை கிடந்தபி","னேம மாபுரத் திட்டதொர் மாதெய்வ","நாம நல்லொளி நந்தனை யென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"When the youth, like Kama's younger brother, has lain down on the smoke-scented couch, a great deity sets him down in Emamapuram, they say.","n":"The verse names the deity's act and not Gandharvadattai's; the spell of 1713 is what binds them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinjai","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1715","lb":"CC1715","ev":"A great deity carries the sleeping Nandattan to Emamapuram.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","மாதெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1716":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1716,"t":["மின்னும் பூணும் மிளிர்கதி ராரமும்","பொன்னும் பூத்ததொர் கற்பகப் பூமர","மன்ன காளை யமர்துயி றேறினான்","மன்னும் வெஞ்சுடர் மாக்க லிவர்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Shining like a wishing tree in flower with jewels and gold, the youth rises from sleep as the sun climbs the great hill.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1716","lb":"CC1716","ev":"Nandattan wakes at dawn in the new place.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1717":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1717,"t":["செய்யவாய் நெடிய கண்ணாள் செல்கென விடுக்கப் பட்ட","வெய்யவாட் டடக்கை வீர னிருத்தலும் விசய னென்பான்","கையவாஞ் சிலையி னானைக் கண்டுவந் தருகு சேர்ந்தான்","பையவாய்ப் பரந்த வல்குற் பாவையர்க் கமிர்த மன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"As the hero sent by the red-lipped woman sits there, Vijayan, bow in hand, sees him and comes near.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1717","lb":"CC1717","ev":"Vijayan finds the stranger and approaches him.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1718":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1718,"t":["தெய்வமே கமழு மேனித் திருவொளி கலந்த மார்பி","னையநீ யாரை யென்றாற் கவனுரை கொடாது விட்டான்","பையவே பெயர்ந்து போகிப் பனிமலர்க் கோதை மார்பின்","மையலங் களிறு போலு மைத்துனற் கிதனைச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Asked who he is, the stranger with a god-fragrant body gives no answer, and Vijayan goes quietly back and tells it to his brother-in-law.","n":"மைத்துனன் makes Civakan Vijayan's brother-in-law, through the marriage seen in 1710.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1718","lb":"CC1718","ev":"Nandattan does not answer Vijayan, who goes to tell Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1719":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1719,"t":["சந்தனக் களியும் பூவுந் தமனியக் குடத்து ணீருங்","கெந்தநா றகிலு முத்துங் கிளரொளி விளக்கு மேந்தி","யந்தில்விற் பயிற்றுந் தானம் வழிபட வாங்குச் சென்றாண்","மந்திர மடந்தை யன்னாள் வசுந்தரி வந்து சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayan reports that Vasundari, carrying sandal paste, flowers, water in a gold pot, agil, pearls and a lamp to worship at the bow-practice ground, came and told him what she saw.","n":"1718 says Vijayan told 'this' to Civakan and 1722 closes with என்றான், so 1719 to 1722 are read as his speech, with Vasundari's words quoted inside it; the verse could also be the narrator's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1719","lb":"CC1719","ev":"Vasundari goes to worship at the archery ground and reports a stranger.","cl":[],"ag":["வசுந்தரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1720":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1720,"t":["ஆரகிற் சேக்கை நீங்கி வெறுநிலத் தடிக டாமே","நீரிதிற் கிடந்த தென்கொ லென்றியா னினைந்து போகிச்","சேர்துணை கழறச் சென்றேன் செல்வியோ டாங்குக் கண்டேன்","போர்பல கடந்த வேலோய் மாயங்கொல் போற்றி யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Vasundari tells how she wondered why their lord had left his agil couch to lie on the bare ground, went to tell her companion, and found him there with the princess, and she warns that it may be a magic.","n":"Her அடிகள் is the stranger she took for Civakan; the likeness of the brothers drives the scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1720","lb":"CC1720","ev":"Vasundari finds the double of Civakan on the ground while Civakan is with his wife.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["வசுந்தரி","விசயன்"],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1721":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1721,"t":["கணைகடி கண்ணி சொல்லக் காணிய யானுஞ் சென்றேன்","மணியிலங் கொண்பொன் வைவாள் கேடக மருங்கு வைத்த","விணைகடி சீய மன்னா னிளமையும் வனப்பு மேருந்","துணையமை வடிவுஞ் சொல்லி னிற்பொறி யொற்றிக் கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayan says he went to see as she told him, and found a lion-like youth with sword and shield beside him whose youth, beauty, bearing and form are, in a word, stamped with Civakan's likeness.","n":"சொல்லின் நிற்பொறி ஒற்றிக் கொண்டான் is read as 'he has taken your seal', that is, he looks like you; the image is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1721","lb":"CC1721","ev":"Vijayan goes to look at the stranger.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1722":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1722,"t":["நீண்டதோ ணெடிய செங்க ணீலமாய்ச் சுரிந்த குஞ்சிப்","பூண்டதோ ரார மேனிப் பொன்னுரைத் திட்ட தொக்குங்","காண்டகு காதிற் றாழ்ந்த குண்டலங் குவளைப் பைந்தா","ராண்டகை யழகன் யார்கொ லறியல னவனை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He describes the stranger's long arms, red eyes, dark curled hair, necklace, gold-rubbed skin, hanging earrings and kuvalai garland, and says he does not know who the handsome man is.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1722","lb":"CC1722","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1723":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1723,"t":["இனத்திடை யேறு போலு மெறுழ்வலி யுரைத்த மாற்ற","மனத்திடை மகிழ்ந்து கேட்டு மைந்தனந் தட்ட னேயாம்","புனத்திடை மயில னாளாற் பொருளுரை பெற்று வந்தா","னெனத்தவி ராது சென்றாங் கெய்தின னென்ப வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the words gladly, Civakan knows that the youth is Nandattan, come with the message of the peacock-like woman, and goes without delay to where he is, they say.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1723","lb":"CC1723","ev":"Civakan recognises his brother from the description and goes to him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1724":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1724,"t":["கருமுகிற் பொடித்த வெய்யோன் கடலிடை நடப்ப தேபோற்","றிருமுகஞ் சுடர நோக்கிச் சீவகன் சென்று சேர்ந்தான்","றருமனை யரிதிற் கண்ட தனஞ்சயன் போலத் தம்பி","திருமலர்த் தடக்கை கூப்பிச் சேவடி தொழுது வீழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Like the sun breaking from a black cloud to walk on the sea, Civakan comes with shining face, and his younger brother, like Arjuna seeing Dharma after long, joins his hands and falls worshipping at his feet.","n":"The simile of தனஞ்சயன் and தருமன் sets the brothers in the Pandava pattern of a younger brother's devotion to the eldest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","anbu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1724","lb":"CC1724","ev":"Nandattan falls at Civakan's feet as the brothers meet.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:73"},"cc:1725":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1725,"t":["தாமரைத் தடக்கை கூப்பித் தாண்முதற் கிடந்த தம்பி","தாமரைத் தடத்தை யொத்தான் றமையனும் பருதி யொத்தான்","றாமரைக் குணத்தி னானை மும்முறை தழுவிக் கொண்டு","தாமரைச் செங்க ணானுந் தன்னுறு பரிவு தீர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The younger brother lies at Civakan's feet with joined hands; the elder, compared to the sun as the brother is to a lotus pool, embraces him three times and his own anguish ends.","n":"The four lines all open on தாமரை: the brother is the lotus pool, his virtue lotus-like, the elder lotus-eyed and the sun that opens it, so the reunion is told as the sun meeting the lotus.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1725","lb":"CC1725","ev":"Nandattan falls at Civakan's feet and Civakan embraces him three times.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1726":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1726,"t":["என்னுறு நிலைமை யோரா தெரியுறு தளிரின் வாடிப்","பொன்னுறு மேனி கன்றிப் போயினீர் பொறியி லாதேன்","முன்னுற விதனை யோரேன் மூரிப்பே ரொக்க லெல்லாம்","பின்னுறு பரிவு செய்தேன் பேதையேன் கவல லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan tells his brother that, not knowing his state, he has withered like a scorched shoot; he blames himself as luckless and foolish for not foreseeing this and for bringing grief on all their great kin, and bids him not to grieve.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","idumbai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1726","lb":"CC1726","ev":"Civakan blames himself for the grief his disappearance caused his kin.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1727":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1727,"t":["ஆக்கமுங் கேடு முற்றீ ரடிகளே யல்லிர் மேலைப்","பூக்குலா மலங்கன் மாலைப் புட்கொடி யாற்கு முண்டே","வீக்குவார் முலையி னார்போல் வெய்துயிர்த் துருகி நைய","நோக்கினீ ரென்னை யென்றா னுதியழற் குட்ட மொப்பான்."],"p":"","r":"Nandattan answers that gain and loss have come not to his lord alone, for even the god of the bird banner has had them, and asks why Civakan looked at him sighing and melting like a woman.","n":"புட்கொடியான், the one with the bird (Garuda) banner, is Vishnu; the consolation sets the hero's fall beside a god's reverses. The speaker is named only as one like a pool of pointed fire, so taking him as Nandattan rests on அடிகளே, his word for his elder throughout this chapter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kedu","samanilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1727","lb":"CC1727","ev":"","cl":[["gain and loss befall everyone, even the god who bears the bird banner","general","reported",1,"nandattan"]],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1728":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1728,"t":["குரவரைப் பேண லின்றிக் குறிப்பிகந் தாய பாவந்","தரவந்த பயத்தி னாலித் தாமரைப் பாத நீங்கிப்","பருவருந் துன்ப முற்றேன் பாவியே னென்று சென்னி","திருவடி மிசையின் வைத்துச் சிலம்பநொந் தழுதிட் டானே."],"p":"","r":"Calling himself a sinner, he says that the fruit of the sin of failing to tend his elders and going against their wish parted him from these lotus feet and brought him hard suffering, and he lays his head on the holy feet and weeps aloud.","n":"He reads the separation as his own deed bearing fruit (பாவம் தர வந்த பயம்), where Civakan in 1726 had blamed himself; each brother takes the cause on himself. Who the elders (குரவர்) are, and which wish he crossed, the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","idumbai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1728","lb":"CC1728","ev":"Nandattan lays his head on Civakan's feet and weeps.","cl":[["a sin of neglecting one's elders comes back as suffering","general","reported",1,"nandattan"]],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1729":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1729,"t":["பரிந்தழு கின்ற தம்பி பங்கய மனைய செங்கண்","பொருந்துபு துடைத்து வேண்டா புலம்புதல் காளை யென்று","மருந்தனா ளுறையுங் கோயின் மடுத்துடன் கொண்டு புக்கா","னருந்ததிக் கற்பி னாளை யடிபணிந் தவனுங் கண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan wipes his weeping brother's lotus-red eyes, tells him there is no need to lament, and takes him into the palace where his nectar-like wife lives; the brother bows at the feet of the lady of Arundhati's chastity.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1729","lb":"CC1729","ev":"Civakan leads Nandattan into the palace, where he bows to Kanakamalai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1730":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1730,"t":["கொழுநனைக் குறிப்பி னாலே குமரன்யா ரென்று நோக்கக்","கழுமிய கற்பி னாய்நின் மைத்துன னைய னென்ன","வெழுமையும் பெறுக வின்ன விளங்கிளைச் சுற்ற மென்றாள்","கொழுமலர்த் தடங்கட் செவ்வாய்க் குவிமுலைக் கொம்ப னாளே."],"p":"","r":"With a look Kanakamalai asks her husband who the youth is; told he is her brother-in-law, she says may they have such shining kin in all seven births.","n":"Her question is asked by sign (குறிப்பினாலே) and only her blessing is spoken; the blessing reckons kinship across the seven births (எழுமையும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","kurippu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1730","lb":"CC1730","ev":"Kanakamalai asks by a glance who the youth is, and Civakan names him as her brother-in-law.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1731":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1731,"t":["குடவரை யனைய மார்பிற் குங்கும மெழுதிக் கோல","வடவரை வைரச் சாதி வாலொளி கலந்த பைம்பூண்","டடவரை மார்பின் மின்னத் தம்பியோ டமிர்த முண்டான்","படவர வல்கு லாளும் பான்மையால் விருந்து செய்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, his saffron-painted chest gleaming with diamond ornaments, eats the feast with his brother, and his wife serves it as a guest's welcome in due form.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1731","lb":"CC1731","ev":"Kanakamalai serves a guest's feast to Civakan and Nandattan.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்","கனகமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1732":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1732,"t":["விருந்தவள் செய்த பின்றைத் தம்பியுந் தானும் வேறா","விருந்துழி யென்னைக் காணா துற்றதை யெவன்கொ லென்று","பொருந்தினார் செய்த தெல்லாம் புரைவிடுத் துரைமோ வென்னக்","கருங்கழற் செங்கட் பைந்தார்க் காளையீ துரைக்கின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"After the feast, alone with his brother, Civakan asks what befell when they could not find him and bids him tell, holding nothing back, all that his companions did; the youth begins.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1732","lb":"CC1732","ev":"Civakan asks Nandattan what his companions did after he vanished.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1733":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1733,"t":["புண்ணுமிழ் குருதி போர்த்த பொருகளம் போன்று தோன்றி","யண்ணலங் கதிரு மத்த மடைந்துசெவ் வான்கொ ளந்தித்","துண்ணெனக் களத்தி னீங்கித் தொன்னகர்ப் புறத்துத் தொக்கே","யெண்ணுமின் செய்வ தென்றான் பதுமுக னெரியும் வேலான்."],"p":"","r":"Nandattan says that at evening, when the sun set and the red sky looked like a battlefield soaked in blood, the companions left the field in haste and gathered outside the old city, and Padumukan of the burning spear asked them to consider what to do.","n":"The evening sky is likened to a battlefield (பொருகளம்) at the moment the companions are deciding whether to fight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","soozhchi","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1733","lb":"CC1733","ev":"The companions gather outside the city at dusk and Padumukan asks what they should do.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1734":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1734,"t":["மின்னென மிளிரும் பைம்பூட் புத்திசேன் வெகுண்டு வெய்ய","கன்னவி றோளி னானைக் காண்கலே மாயி னின்னே","மன்னனை வாளி னானே வானகங் காட்டி மூதூர்","தன்னையுஞ் சவட்டிப் போகிச் சாமியைச் சார்து மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Buddhisenan, flaring up, says that if they cannot see their lord now, they will show the king the sky with their swords, trample the old city and go to join their master.","n":"சாமியைச் சார்தும், 'we will reach the master', assumes Civakan is dead: the vow is to die after him once vengeance is taken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","vekuli","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1734","lb":"CC1734","ev":"Buddhisenan proposes to kill the king, wreck the city and follow Civakan in death.","cl":[],"ag":["புத்திசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்திசேனன்",""],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1735":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1735,"t":["சிலையொடு திரண்ட திண்டோட் டேவமா தத்த னென்பான்","மலையுடை யுருமிற் சீறி மாற்றல னுயிரை யுண்ட","லிலையுடைக் கண்ணி யீர்க்கிஃ தெளிதுநங் குருசி லுண்மை","யுலைவினோ டின்மை யாராய்ந் தொறுப்பதே துணிமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Devadattan of the strong bow, raging like thunder on a hill, says that taking the enemy's life is easy for them, but they must first find out whether their lord is truly dead or not, and only then resolve on punishment.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aaraaydhal","oruththal","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1735","lb":"CC1735","ev":"Devadattan argues that they must learn whether Civakan is dead before taking revenge.","cl":[["one must establish the truth of a death before punishing for it","general","reported",3,"devadattan"]],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவதத்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1736":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1736,"t":["பவ்வத்துப் பிறந்த வெய்ய பருதிபோற் றிறலி னாற்குத்","தெவ்வரைக் கிழங்கி னோடுந் தின்றுநீ சொன்ன வாறே","யெவ்வத்தைத் தணித்து மென்றான் சீதத்த னென்ன லோடு","மவ்வல மணந்த தண்டார்ப் பதுமுக னிதனைச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Sridattan says that, as Buddhisenan has said, they will eat up the enemies root and all and so ease their grief; when he has spoken, Padumukan speaks what follows.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vanjinam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1736","lb":"CC1736","ev":"Sridattan backs the call for revenge and Padumukan rises to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீதத்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1737":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1737,"t":["நம்பிநந் தட்டன் கேட்க நங்கட்குக் குரவ ருள்ளார்","தம்பரி வகற்றி யோம்பி நீர்க்கடன் மரபு தாங்கிக்","கம்பஞ்செய் பரிவு நீங்கிக் கற்பிப்பார்க் குவர்த்துச் சொல்லா","ரிம்பரிவ் வுலக மொப்பாய்க் கென்னையா னுரைப்ப தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Padumukan, addressing Nandattan, says that the companions have elders who would bear their grief, care for them and keep the rites of water, and asks what he could advise one who is like the whole world to them.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 are hard: the elders who keep the water rite (நீர்க்கடன்) seem to be the companions' parents, who will mourn them, so Padumukan leaves the final word to Civakan's own brother.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1737","lb":"CC1737","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1738":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1738,"t":["ஓம்படை சாற்றற் பால துள்ளவர்க் காகு மன்றே","யாம்புடை யென்க ணில்லை யங்கையென் கண்க ளாகத்","தேம்படு தாரி னீர்க்குஞ் செல்வற்குஞ் செய்வ செய்தேன்","காம்படு காட்டுத் தீயிற் கனன்றுட னெழுக வென்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"Nandattan says he told them that charging others with protection is for those who have someone at their side; he has no one, has done for them and for the lord what was his to do, and bade them rise together blazing like a forest fire.","n":"ஓம்படை, a solemn charge to protect, is what one leaves to those one leaves behind; he says he has no one left to charge, so the restraint Devadattan and Padumukan urged does not bind him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","thunai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1738","lb":"CC1738","ev":"Nandattan urges the companions to rise at once against the king.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1739":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1739,"t":["கோட்டிளங் குழவித் திங்க ளிரண்டன்ன வெயிற்றுக் கோளே","வேட்டவோர் சிங்கஞ் சூழ்ந்த வேங்கையி னினத்தின் வெய்ய","வாட்படை யெழுந்து வாழ்க சீவக னென்னு மாங்கட்","பாட்டினை யொருவ னெங்கள் பரிவறப் பாடி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Just then a man sang a song, 'long live Civakan, whose sword-army rose fiercer than a pack of tigers round a lion with tusks like two crescent moons', and their grief fell away.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1739","lb":"CC1739","ev":"A passer-by sings a song hailing Civakan, and the companions' grief lifts.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1740":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1740,"t":["வருவர்நங் கேள்வ ரின்னே வாணுதற் பசலை தீர","வுருகிநைந் துடன்று முன்கை வளையுக மெலிய வேண்டா","வருவிமும் மதத்த யானை யதிர்ந்துழிக் காரென் றெண்ணித்","தெரிவில பேதை முல்லை பூத்தன தெளியி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The song tells a woman that her husband will come soon, that she need not melt and grieve till her bangles slip and her brow pales, for the foolish mullai, unable to tell, bloomed thinking the roar of the rutting elephant was the rain cloud.","n":"The song is the old consolation of the friend who denies that the season has come: the flowers have been fooled, so he has not broken his word. Overheard, its 'our husband will come' is taken by the companions as a sign that Civakan lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","pasalai","nimittam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1740","lb":"CC1740","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்",""],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1741":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1741,"t":["பாட்டினைக் கேட்ட லோடும் பழம்பகை நட்பு மாமே","யோட்டியுங் கோறு மன்றே நம்பிதா னுண்மை பெற்றா","னாட்டிடம் பரந்து போகி நாடுது நாங்க ளென்னா","வீட்டமும் வேறு மாகி யிலைப்புரை கிளைத்திட் டேமே."],"p":"","r":"On hearing the song they said old enmity could wait and the killing could come later, since their prince had surely survived; they would spread over the land and search, and so they gathered and parted, each his own way.","n":"பழம்பகை நட்பும் ஆமே is obscure; it may mean that old enmity can turn to friendship, or that the feud can rest for now.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1741","lb":"CC1741","ev":"Taking the song as a sign that Civakan lives, the companions put off revenge and scatter to search for him.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1742":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1742,"t":["மணிபொதி துகிலிற் றோன்று மஞ்சுசூழ் வரைக ணாடி","யணிநகர் யான்சென் றெய்தி மாலைதன் மனையைச் சேர்ந்தேன்","றுணைமலர்க் காந்த ளூழ்த்துச் சொரிவபோற் றோன்றி முன்கை","யணிவளை நலத்தோ டேக வங்ஙன மிருந்து நைவாள்."],"p":"","r":"Crossing misty hills that seem like gems wrapped in cloth, he reached the fine city and came to Maalai's house, where she sat wasting, her bangles slipping from her forearms like falling kaanthal blossoms.","n":"மாலை is taken as Gunamalai, Civakan's wife in the old city, since the mountain lady of 1745 is another.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1742","lb":"CC1742","ev":"Nandattan reaches the city and finds Gunamalai wasting in her house.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1743":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1743,"t":["என்னைக்கண் டடிசி லாக்க வையர்க்கென் றவல நீங்கப்","பொன்னைக்கண் டனைய சாய லவர்புரிந் தடிசி லேந்தத்","துன்னிநோ யுற்ற மஞ்ஞைத் தோற்றம்போ லிருந்த நங்கை","பின்னைநாட் குவளை நீர்வீழ் பெற்றிய கண்ண ளாகி."],"p":"","r":"Seeing him, she had food made for her guest so his sorrow might ease, and when the golden women brought it the lady, who had sat like an ailing peacock, then had eyes like kuvalai dropping water.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1743","lb":"CC1743","ev":"Gunamalai has a meal prepared for Nandattan and then breaks into tears.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1744":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1744,"t":["அடிகளை யின்றி நீரே யுண்ணவும் வல்லீ ரானீர்","கடியிர்நீ ரைய னீரே யெனக்கசிந் துருகிக் காய்பொற்","கொடிதுக ளார்ந்த வண்ணங் குழைந்துமா நிலத்து வீழ்ந்த","பெடைமயிற் சாய லாடன் பேதுகண் டாங்கு மீண்டேன்."],"p":"","r":"She cries that without their lord they can now eat by themselves and calls him hard, then melts and falls to the ground like a peahen; seeing her distress, he turned back.","n":"Her reproach turns the meal she herself ordered into the charge: a brother who can eat has stopped grieving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1744","lb":"CC1744","ev":"Gunamalai reproaches Nandattan for eating without Civakan and collapses; he leaves.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1745":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1745,"t":["செல்வனை யின்று நாடிச் சேவடி தொழுத லொன்றோ","வல்லதிவ் வுடம்பு நீங்க வேற்றுல கடைத லொன்றோ","வெல்லையிவ் விரண்டி னொன்றை யிப்பகன் முடிப்ப லென்னா","மல்லிகைக் கோதை யைம்பான் மலைமகண் மனையைச் சேர்ந்தேன்."],"p":"","r":"Resolving that this very day he would either find his lord and worship his red feet or leave this body and reach another world, he went to the house of the mountain lady with jasmine in her five-part hair.","n":"மலைமகள், daughter of the mountain, is taken as Gandharvadattai, the Vidyadhara princess from the mountain; it is not Parvati.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","marumai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1745","lb":"CC1745","ev":"Nandattan resolves to find Civakan or die that day, and goes to the mountain lady's house.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1746":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1746,"t":["மணியொலி வீணை பண்ணி மாண்டகோ றடவ மாத","ரணிமுலைத் தடத்தி னொற்றி வெப்பராற் றட்ப மாற்றிப்","பிணைமலர்க் கோதை கீதம் பாடயான் பெரிதும் பேதுற்","றிணைமலர்க் கண்ணிக் கொவ்வா விளிவரு கிளவி சொன்னேன்."],"p":"","r":"Her women played the jewel-voiced vina, stroking its fine frets, and cooled the heat with their hands, the garlanded lady sang, and he, much distraught, spoke to her words of reproach unfit for her.","n":"Line 2's cooling (வெப்பரால் தட்பம் மாற்றி) is hard to place; the music seems to be her way of keeping calm while the brother breaks down.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["isai","idumbai","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1746","lb":"CC1746","ev":"Gandharvadattai sings to the vina, and Nandattan speaks harshly to her.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1747":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1747,"t":["சொல்லிய வென்னை நோக்கித் துளங்கனும் மடிகள் பாதம்","புல்லயான் புணர்ப்ப லென்று பொழுதுபோய்ப் பட்ட பின்றை","யெல்லிருள் விஞ்சை யோதி யிவ்வழி யிடுவித் திட்டாள்","சொல்லுமி னடிக ணீரும் போந்தவா றெனக்கு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Looking at him, she told him not to tremble, for she would bring him to his lord's feet; when the day had gone she recited a spell in the deep dark and sent him by this way, and he asks his lord to tell him in turn how he came here.","n":"விஞ்சை, the Vidyadhara's spell, carries a man across the land in a night; she promises reunion at the feet (பாதம் புல்ல), which is how 1725 opens.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","thunai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1747","lb":"CC1747","ev":"Gandharvadattai sends Nandattan to Civakan by a spell, and Nandattan asks Civakan to tell his own story.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1748":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1748,"t":["தாதையா ருவப்பச் செய்வான் றாழ்கச்சிற் பிணிப்புண் டைய","போதரா நின்ற போழ்திற் போர்ப்புலிக் குழாத்திற் சீறிக்","காதனஞ் சுற்ற மெல்லாங் கையிலங் கெஃக மேந்திச்","சாதலே புரிந்து தோன்றுந் தன்மையந் நகரிற் கண்டேன்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan begins: when, to please his father, he was being led bound with a belt, he saw in that city all their dear kin raging like a troop of fighting tigers, spears raised, set on dying.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","saakkaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1748","lb":"CC1748","ev":"Civakan begins to tell how he was led bound from the city while his kin stood ready to die.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுற்றம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:74"},"cc:1749":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1749,"t":["கண்டபி னின்னைக் காண்பேன் கருவரை புலம்பிப் பல்கால்","விண்டுவு முடைய வாலின் வெடித்துராய் வெகுண்டு நோக்கா","வெண்டிசை யோரு மெள்கக் குஞ்சர மிரியப் பாயு","மொண்டிறற் சிங்க மன்ன கதழொளி யுடற்சி கண்டேன்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, telling his brother how he came here, says that in the fight at his arrest he saw his brother's fury, like a lion of shining strength that leaps so that elephants scatter and even the guardians of the eight quarters quail.","n":"Line 1 (கண்டபின் நின்னைக் காண்பேன்) is hard to construe as printed; the reading follows the second-person address and the lion simile of lines 3-4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1749","lb":"CC1749","ev":"Civakan recalls seeing Nandattan rage like a lion in the fight when he was seized.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1750":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1750,"t":["சினந்தலைப் பெருக்கித் தீக்கோ ளுறுப்பினைச் சுருக்கித் தீப்போ","லனன்றுநில் லாத கண்ணா னிறுத்தின செவியிற் றாகி","முனம்புக வடக்கிப் பின்போந் திருந்துபாய் வான மைந்த","வினந்தலைப் புலியோ டொக்குந் தோழர்நின் னிடத்திற் கண்டேன்."],"p":"","r":"He goes on that he saw the companions at his brother's side like a pack of tigers, swelling their anger, drawing in their fiery limbs, ears pricked and eyes unblinking, crouching before they spring.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1750","lb":"CC1750","ev":"Civakan recalls his companions standing like tigers ready to spring.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1751":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1751,"t":["கூடநீர் நின்ற பெற்றி கண்டிப்பா னோக்கு வேற்கொர்","கேடகம் வாளொ டேந்திக் கெடுகவிந் நகர மென்னா","மாடத்தி னுச்சி நின்ற மலைமக டன்மை கண்டே","யாடவர்க் குழுவை யொப்பா யஞ்சினே னதன்க ணென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing how they all stood together, and seeing the mountain's daughter on the top of the mansion with shield and sword crying 'let this city perish', he says, he grew afraid at that.","n":"What he fears is not the enemy but the slaughter and ruin his own side is ready for; the next verse turns that fear into his choice of death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","vaazhkkaithunai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1751","lb":"CC1751","ev":"Civakan recalls Gandharvadattai on the mansion top, armed and cursing the city, and his fear at it.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","காந்தருவதத்தை","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1752":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1752,"t":["பெண்ணிடர் விடுப்ப வாழ்விற் சாதலே பெரிது நன்றென்","றெண்ணினே னமர்கள் வீயு மியல்பினா னெருங்கப் பட்டுக்","கண்ணிநா னியக்கன் றன்னைச் சிந்தித்தேன் கடவுள் வாழ்த்தி","யண்ணல்வந் தழுங்கத் தோன்றி யாங்கென்னைக் கொண்டு போந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He says he judged death far better than living freed from distress by a woman, and, hemmed in as his people were about to die, thought of the yaksha and praised the god, and the lord appeared and carried him off.","n":"பெண் இடர் விடுப்ப வாழ்வின் is taken as 'than a life in which a woman frees me from distress', which fits his earlier refusal to be freed by Gandharvadattai's spells; another division of line 1 is possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maanam","saakkaadu","ninaivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1752","lb":"CC1752","ev":"Civakan, choosing death over rescue by his wife's arts, thinks of the yaksha, who comes and carries him away.","cl":[["for a man, death is far better than a life saved from danger by a woman","general","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1753":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1753,"t":["மந்திர மூன்றுந் தந்து வானவன் விடுப்பச் செல்வேற்","கிந்திர திருவிற் சூழ்ந்த வினமழைக் குழாத்தின் வேழங்","கொந்தழற் காட்டுத் தீயால் வளைப்புண்ட குழாத்தை நோக்கிச்","சிந்தித்துக் கவன்று நிற்பத் திருமழை பொழிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The god gave him three mantras and sent him off, and on the way he saw a herd of elephants like rain clouds under a rainbow ringed by forest fire, and as he stood anxious and grieving for them holy rain poured down.","n":"In his own telling he does nothing but stand and worry; the rain is திருமழை, holy rain, and falls in answer to his compassion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","mandhiram","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1753","lb":"CC1753","ev":"Civakan, given three mantras, sees a herd of elephants trapped by forest fire, and rain falls as he grieves for them.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1754":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1754,"t":["வெல்களிற் றச்ச நீக்கி விரைவொடு வனத்தி னேகிப்","பல்லவ தேய நண்ணித் தனபதி யென்னு மன்ன","னல்வனப் புடைய தேவி திலோத்தமை பெற்ற நம்பி","செல்வன்மற் றுலோக பாலன் றிருமகள் பதுமை யென்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"Having ended the elephants' fear he went swiftly through the forest into the Pallava country, where king Dhanapati's queen Tilottamai had borne the prince Lokapalan, whose sister was Padumai.","n":"The names Dhanapati, Tilottamai and Lokapalan, absent from the verses that told this episode, are given here in Civakan's summary.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1754","lb":"CC1754","ev":"Civakan reaches the Pallava country of king Dhanapati.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1755":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1755,"t":["அரிகுரற் கோழி நாமத் தரவவட் கடித்த தாகத்","திருவிழை யவளைத் தீர்த்தேன் றீர்விலா நண்பு வேண்டிப்","பொருகளி யானை மன்னன் புனையிழை யவளைத் தந்தா","னிருமதி கழிந்த பின்றை யிடையிராப் பொழுதிற் போந்தேன்."],"p":"","r":"When a snake bit her he cured the jewelled girl; the king of fighting elephants, wanting a friendship without end, gave her to him, and after two months he left at midnight.","n":"Line 1 (அரிகுரற் கோழி நாமத்து அரவு) seems to name the snake or the place of the bite in a way not fixed here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","natpu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1755","lb":"CC1755","ev":"Civakan recounts curing Padumai of the snakebite, marrying her, and leaving her at midnight after two months.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தனபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1756":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1756,"t":["வாவிப்புண் ணடையி னாளை வஞ்சித்துத் தக்க நாட்டை","மேவியான் காண லுற்றுச் சார்தலு மிப்ப ருள்ளான்","றூவிப்பொன் மாட மூதூர்ச் சுபத்திர னென்னைக் கண்டே","யாவிக்க ணறிவு போல வளவளா யன்பு பட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Deceiving the woman, he set out to see the Takka country, and on his arrival Subhadran of the old town of gold-washed mansions saw him and loved him as the soul knows knowledge.","n":"Civakan uses the word வஞ்சித்து of his own leaving; he does not excuse it. The phrase வாவிப்புண் அடையினாளை for Padumai is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","pirivu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1756","lb":"CC1756","ev":"Civakan recounts leaving Padumai by deceit and being welcomed by Subhadran in the Takka country.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுபத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1757":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1757,"t":["பண்ணமை தேரி னேறி யவனொடியா னிருந்து போகி","விண்ணுயர் செம்பொன் மாடத் திழிந்தவண் விளங்கப் புக்கேன்","வெண்ணிலா முத்தஞ் சூழ்ந்த வெம்முலைத் தடங்க ணாளை","மண்ணெலாஞ் செல்ல நின்ற மகிழ்ச்சியின் மகிழ்ந்து தந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He rode with Subhadran in his chariot, entered his tall golden mansion, and the merchant, with a joy that spread over the whole earth, gave him his daughter with pearl-ringed breasts and wide eyes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1757","lb":"CC1757","ev":"Civakan recounts Subhadran giving him his daughter Kemasari.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுபத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1758":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1758,"t":["அவ்வழி யிரண்டு திங்கள் கழிந்தபி னவளி னீங்கி","யிவ்வழி நாடு காண்பான் வருதலு மிறைவன் கண்டே","செவ்வழி பாட ராகிச் சிலைத்தொழில் சிறுவர் கற்ப","மைவழி நெடுங்க ணாளைத் தந்தனன் மதலை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"After two months there he left her and came this way to see the land, and the king here saw him, and so that his sons might learn archery in the right way gave him his long-eyed daughter, Civakan said, ending his account.","n":"The marriage is tied to a service: the king's daughter comes with the teaching of the princes' bow-craft.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pirivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1758","lb":"CC1758","ev":"Civakan recounts leaving Kemasari and coming to the middle country, whose king gave him his daughter for teaching his sons archery.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மத்திம தேய மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1759":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1759,"t":["தானுழந் துற்ற வெல்லாந் தம்பியை யுணரக் கூறித்","தேனுழந் தரற்றுந் தாரான் குரவரைச் சிந்தித் தாற்கு","வானிழிந் தாங்குக் கண்ணீர் மார்பக நனைப்பக் கையா","லூனுமிழ்ந் திலங்கும் வேலா னொற்றிமற் றிதனைச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"When he had told his brother all he had gone through, and the brother thought of their elders and wept so that his tears wet his chest like rain from the sky, the hero with the gleaming spear wiped them with his hand and said this.","n":"The verse can also be read with Civakan as the one who thinks of the elders and weeps; the dative சிந்தித்தாற்கு is taken here as the brother.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","anbu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1759","lb":"CC1759","ev":"Civakan finishes his story; Nandattan weeps at the thought of their elders, and Civakan wipes his tears.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1760":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1760,"t":["திண்பொரு ளெய்த லாகுந் தெவ்வரைச் செகுக்க லாகு","நண்பொடு பெண்டிர் மக்கள் யாவையு நண்ண லாகு","மொண்பொரு ளாவ தையா வுடன்பிறப் பாக்க லாகா","வெம்பியை யீங்குப் பெற்றே னென்னெனக் கரிய தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Firm wealth can be won, enemies can be destroyed, friends, wives and children can all be gained, he says, but a brother cannot be made; having got my brother here, what is there hard for me?","n":"He puts his wives among the things that can be gained, in a book named for them; the one bond beyond acquisition is birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1760","lb":"CC1760","ev":"","cl":[["wealth, victory over enemies, friends, wives and children can be gained, but a brother born of the same parents cannot be made","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1761":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1761,"t":["தேனிற் பாலெனச் செல்வன் றம்பியோ","டானி யம்பல கழிய வாயிடை","வேனிற் குன்றெனத் தோழர் வெந்துமெய்","யூனி னைகின்றார் செய்வ துன்னினார்."],"p":"","r":"The prince lived with his brother like milk mixed in honey while many days passed; meanwhile, back home, the companions, wasting like a hill in summer, their bodies withering, thought what they should do.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1761","lb":"CC1761","ev":"Civakan and Nandattan live together while the companions at home waste and take counsel.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1762":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1762,"t":["நாடு மின்னினி நாங்கள் செய்வதென்","றீடி னாலிருந் தெண்ணி நால்வரு","மாடு மஞ்ஞையஞ் சாயற் றத்தைமெய்","வாட லொன்றிலள் வஞ்சமாங் கொலோ."],"p":"","r":"The four sat together and asked what they should do now, and said that the parrot-like Gandharvadattai, with the peacock's grace, shows no wasting of the body at all; could it be deceit?","n":"They read her unwasted body by the convention that a parted wife pales and wastes, and take its absence for a sign of deceit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1762","lb":"CC1762","ev":"The four companions take counsel and suspect Gandharvadattai because she does not waste.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்",""],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1763":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1763,"t":["கள்ள முண்டெனிற் காண்டு நாமென","மௌள வெய்தினார் வினவக் கூறினாள்","வள்ளற் குற்றதும் மறைந்த வண்ணமும்","வெள்ளி வெண்மலை வேந்தன் பாவையே."],"p":"","r":"Saying 'if there is deceit we shall see it', they went gently and asked her, and the daughter of the king of the white silver mountain told them what had befallen the generous one and how he had gone hidden.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","aaraaydhal","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1763","lb":"CC1763","ev":"The companions question Gandharvadattai, who tells them what happened to Civakan and where he is hidden.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1764":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1764,"t":["மற்ற வள்சொலக் கேட்ட மைந்தர்க","ளிற்ற தம்முயி ரியல்பிற் போத்தவட்","பெற்ற மாந்தரிற் பெரிது மெய்குளிர்ந்","தற்ற மன்மையி னவல நீங்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing her, the young men, like those who get back a life that had gone, felt their bodies cool with a gladness greater than that of the parents who bore her, and their grief left them since there was no danger.","n":"பெற்ற மாந்தரின் is taken as her own parents' gladness; it may instead mean 'than those who gain (a treasure)'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1764","lb":"CC1764","ev":"The companions are relieved to learn Civakan is safe.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1765":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1765,"t":["திருவின் சாயறன் சீற டிச்சிலம்","புருவக் குஞ்சிவா யுறுத்தி யொய்யென","வுருகு முள்ளத்தி னுடம்பு வீங்கினார்","பருகு காதலிற் பாடி யாடினார்."],"p":"","r":"They set the anklets of the small feet of her, graceful as the goddess of fortune, on their tufted heads, their bodies swelling with melting hearts, and sang and danced with a love they drank in.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","inbam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1765","lb":"CC1765","ev":"The companions place Gandharvadattai's feet on their heads and sing and dance for joy.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1766":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1766,"t":["பைத்தர வத்திரை சிந்திய பல்கதிர்","மொய்த்தெரி நித்திலம் வைத்தன பல்லின","ளித்திரு வின்னுரு வந்தொழு தார்தம","தெத்துய ருங்கெடு மென்றின சொன்னார்."],"p":"","r":"They said that those who worship the form of this goddess of fortune, whose teeth are pearls like those the hooded snake spits out, will see every sorrow of theirs perish.","n":"Praise turns into a formula of worship for the wife they had just suspected of deceit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1766","lb":"CC1766","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1767":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1767,"t":["ஐயனை யாமவ ணெய்துவ மாயிழை","நொய்தினு ரைபொரு ளுண்டெனி னொய்தென","மையெழுத் தூசியின் மாண்டதொர் தோட்டிடைக்","கைவளர் கோதை கரந்தெழுத் திட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"'We shall go to our lord there; lady, if you have any message, tell it quickly,' they said, and she with the garland on her arm wrote secretly with an inked stylus on a fine palm leaf.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1767","lb":"CC1767","ev":"The companions offer to carry a message, and Gandharvadattai secretly writes a letter on palm leaf.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்","காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1768":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1768,"t":["ஆங்குருக் காரரக் கிட்டதன் மீமிசைப்","பூங்குழை யாற்பொறி யொற்றுபு நீட்டத்","தேங்குழ லாடொழு தாடிசை செல்கெனப்","பாங்கரங் குப்படர் குற்றன ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"She put red lac on it, pressed her seal on it with her flower earring and held it out, and bowing, the honey-haired one said 'go with the sound of victory', and they set off toward him.","n":"ஆடிசை செல்க is taken as a blessing, 'go with resounding fame'; the reading of line 3 is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1768","lb":"CC1768","ev":"Gandharvadattai seals the letter and sends the companions off with it.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1769":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1769,"t":["வேந்திரி யக்கணை வித்திய வெஞ்சிலைக்","காய்ந்திரிக் கும்புரு வக்கருங் கண்ணிய","ராய்ந்தரிக் குந்நற வம்மலர் மாலையை","வேய்ந்தரிக் கும்மிஞி றார்ப்ப விடுத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She sent them off crowned with garlands of honeyed flowers chosen by women whose dark eyes are like bows and arrows, with bees humming over them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1769","lb":"CC1769","ev":"Gandharvadattai garlands the companions and sends them off.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1770":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1770,"t":["அலங்கு வெண்மதி யைப்பசி யடையவப் பகலே","நிலங்கொண் டோங்கின நிரம்பின புகர்சுழி யுடைய","வுலம்பி முன்னிரு தாள்களு முமிழ்வன போல்வ","விலங்கு பாய்வன விடுகணை விலக்குவ கலிமா."],"p":"","r":"The steeds are described: they rise up and fill the ground, bear spotted whorls, leap as if spitting out their forelegs, and turn aside the arrows shot at them.","n":"Line 1 (the white moon made hungry, அப்பகலே) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1770","lb":"CC1770","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1771":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1771,"t":["அள்ளற் சேறரு மணல்புன லருவரைப் படினு","முள்ளம் போற்செல்வ வுரனசை வில்லன வமருட்","கொள்ளி மண்டிலம் போற்கொடி படத்திரிந் திடுவ","வெள்ளி மால்வரைத் தாழ்வதின் மேம்படப் பிறந்த"],"p":"","r":"Through mire, sand, water and hard hills they run like the mind, never wavering in strength; in battle they wheel like a ring of fire so that the banners are drawn out; they are bred finer than those of the slopes of the great silver mountain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1771","lb":"CC1771","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1772":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1772,"t":["ஈரைஞ் ஞாற்றினை யிருபதின் முரணிய தொகைய","வீர ரேறின விளங்கொளிப் பக்கரை யமைந்த","தாரும் புட்டிலு மரற்றுவ சாமரை யணிந்த","வோருங் கூடின மள்ளரு மொலித்தெழுந் தனரே."],"p":"","r":"Warriors mounted these horses, numbered in thousands and twenties, their harness bright, their garlands and bells jingling, chowries set on them, and the soldiers gathered and rose with a roar.","n":"The number (ஈரைஞ்ஞாற்றினை இருபதின் முரணிய தொகைய) can be read as a thousand and twenty or as twenty thousand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1772","lb":"CC1772","ev":"The companions' horsemen mount and set out with a roar.","cl":[],"ag":["வீரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:75"},"cc:1773":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1773,"t":["வடித்த போத்தொடு வன்செல லத்திரி","கடுத்த வொட்டகங் காற்செல்வ யாவையு","நொடிப்பின் மாத்திரை நூற்றுவில் லேகுவ","வெடுத்த பண்ட மியைந்துட னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator lists the party's train, trained horses, swift chariots and fierce camels, beasts that cover a hundred bow-lengths in a snap of the fingers, loaded with the goods they took.","n":"ஒட்டகம், the camel, is a rare beast in this landscape; the verse piles up speed (நொடிப்பின் மாத்திரை நூற்றுவில்) rather than describing any one animal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1773","lb":"CC1773","ev":"The companions' train is made ready with mounts and loaded goods.","cl":[],"ag":["சாத்து"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1774":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1774,"t":["ஞாலம் விற்பன பைங்கிளி நன்னிறத்","தாலு மாபவ ளக்குளம் பார்ந்தன","காலி னொய்யன கண்வெளவு காட்சிய","நால்குப் பண்ணினர் நால்வரு மேறினார்."],"p":"","r":"Four horses, swift of foot, coloured like the green parrot, with coral-like hooves and eye-catching looks, worth the price of the world, are saddled and the four mount them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1774","lb":"CC1774","ev":"The four companions mount four saddled horses.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1775":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1775,"t":["நாளும் புள்ளு நயத்தகு நன்னிலைக்","கோளு மோரையுங் கொண்ட நிமித்தமு","மாளு மாந்தரி னாய்ந்துகொண் டாயிடைத்","தாளி னூக்குபு சாத்தொ டெழுந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Having had men skilled in the art examine the day, the bird omen, the good standing of the planets, the hour and the signs, they set out with the caravan, urging on with their feet.","n":"Five separate reckonings are named (நாள், புள், கோள், ஓரை, நிமித்தம) and handed to specialists (ஆளும் மாந்தர்); the verse records the practice without saying it decides the outcome.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1775","lb":"CC1775","ev":"The companions have the day, omens and hour read and set out with the caravan.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1776":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1776,"t":["பறையுஞ் சங்கும் பரந்தொலித் தார்த்தெழ","வுறைகொள் வாளினோ டொண்சுடர் வேன்மினச்","சிறைய ழிந்ததொர் செம்புனல் போன்றவ","ணறைக டற்படை யார்ப்பொ டெழுந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"With drums and conches roaring all round, sheathed swords and bright spears flashing, the army sets out with a shout like red flood water that has broken its dam.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1776","lb":"CC1776","ev":"The armed escort sets out noisily.","cl":[],"ag":["சாத்து"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1777":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1777,"t":["காய்த்த செந்நெலின் றாழ்கதிர் நெற்றிமேற்","பூத்த முல்லையின் போது பொழிந்துக","நீத்த நீர்வய லன்னமு நாரையு","மேத்தல் சான்முரு டார்ப்ப விரிந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Along the way jasmine flowers shower onto the bent ears of ripe red paddy, and the swans and herons of the flooded fields scatter as the drums sound.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1777","lb":"CC1777","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1778":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1778,"t":["அளகு சேவலொ டாடியங் காய்க்குலை","மிளகு வார்கொடி யூசல் விருப்புறூஉஞ்","சுளகு வார்செவித் தூங்குகைக் குஞ்சர","மிளகு காடிள கப்பரி கொண்டவே."],"p":"","r":"Elephants with winnow-like ears and hanging trunks, set swaying in the pepper forest where hens and cocks play under areca clusters and pepper vines swing, take fright and bolt.","n":"The swing (ஊசல்) of pepper vines and the play of fowl are the settled hill country; the party's noise, not a hunt, is what startles the elephants (இளகப் பரிகொண்ட).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1778","lb":"CC1778","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1779":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1779,"t":["அருவிக் குன்றமு மைவனச் சாரலுங்","குருவி யார்த்தெழு கொய்புனக் கானமுந்","திருவிற் றீர்ந்தவர் தேயமுந் தோந்துபோய்","மருவி மாதவர் பள்ளியுள் விட்டதே"],"p":"","r":"Passing hills with waterfalls, dark-forested slopes, millet fields where sparrows rise crying, and the lands of those who have lost their fortune, the train comes and halts within the hermitage of the great ascetics.","n":"திருவிற் றீர்ந்தவர் தேயம், 'the land of those parted from fortune', is set just before the hermitage where Vijayai, who lost her kingdom, lives; the poet lets the phrase stand ahead of the meeting.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1779","lb":"CC1779","ev":"The train passes through hill and field country and halts at a hermitage.","cl":[],"ag":["சாத்து"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1780":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1780,"t":["வண்டுதுயில் கொண்டுகுயி லாலிமயி லகவி","விண்டுமது விட்டுவிரி போதுபல பொதுளிக்","கொண்டுதளிர் வேய்ந்துசினை தாழ்ந்துநனை யார்ந்தொன்","றுண்டுபொழி லிமையவர்க ளுலகமுறு வதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The grove there, where bees sleep, cuckoos call and peacocks cry, where many flowers open and drip honey, branches bend under tender leaves and buds, is like the world of the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1780","lb":"CC1780","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1781":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1781,"t":["காவிகழு நீர்குவளை யாம்பல்கடிக் கமலந்","தூவிமட நாரைதுணை யன்னம்பயின் முதுமீன்","மேவியுறை வண்டினொடு மல்கிவிழை தகைய","வாவியொடு காவினிடை மாந்தர்பதி கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Among pools full of kaavi, kazhuneer, kuvalai, aambal and fragrant lotus, with young herons, paired swans, old fish and bees, the people make camp between the tanks and the grove.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1781","lb":"CC1781","ev":"The party camps between the pools and the grove of the hermitage.","cl":[],"ag":["சாத்து"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1782":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1782,"t":["ஐயருறை பள்ளியிட மாண்டிழகர் காணச்","செய்கழகர் தாரரவ ரெங்கந்திரி கின்றார்","கொய்தகைய பூம்பொதும்பர்க் குளிருமரப் பலகைச்","செய்யவளிற் சிறிதுமிகை சேயவளைக் கண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"In the hermitage where the ascetics dwell, the young men wander everywhere and, on a cool wooden bench in a bower of plucked flowers, they see a woman a little beyond Lakshmi herself.","n":"The woman is Vijayai, Civakan's mother, though neither the verse nor the young men name her; the reader knows from 347-359 who lives in this hermitage. செய்யவள் is Lakshmi, and the line places her above the goddess even in her ascetic state.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1782","lb":"CC1782","ev":"The companions, wandering the hermitage, see a woman seated on a wooden bench.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1783":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1783,"t":["அந்நுண்டுகிற் கல்லரத்த மல்குலது வருத்தச்","செந்நுண்டுகி லுத்தரியம் புதைந்துசுவல் வருத்த","மைந்நுண்குழற் சிறுவன்மனம் வருத்தவடி வேற்கண்","கைந்நொண்டன கவர்ச்சிநனி வருத்தக்கலுழ்ந் தாற்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her fine ochre-dyed cloth weighs on her hips, the fine red upper cloth weighs on her shoulder where it covers it, her little dark-haired son weighs on her mind, and her eyes, as if plucking at her, weigh on her as she weeps unable to bear it.","n":"The four-fold வருத்த joins the ascetic's garments and the son in one list of burdens: the robe she took for him has not freed her from him. The 'little son' (சிறுவன்) is grown by now; she still thinks of him as the infant she left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","makkal","idumbai","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1783","lb":"CC1783","ev":"The woman sits in her ascetic's dress weeping for her son.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1784":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1784,"t":["மாசொடுமி டைந்துமணி நூற்றனைய வைம்பால்","பூசுதலு மின்றிப்பிணி கொண்டுபுறந் தாழ","வாசமலர் மறைந்தவழி வாமனடிக் கேற்றித்","தோசமறத் துதிகண்மனத் தோதித்தொழு திருந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her five-plaited hair, dimmed with dirt and like threaded sapphire, hangs down her back unanointed and bound by its own neglect; laying fragrant flowers on the feet of the Jina in a hidden place, she recites praises in her mind that her fault may go, and sits worshipping.","n":"வாமன் here is the Jina, the one whose feet receive the flowers; the worship is silent (மனத்து ஓதி) and private (மறைந்த வழி). The hair 'not anointed' (பூசுதலும் இன்றி) is the widow's mark of 349-354, kept now as a penance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kaimmai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1784","lb":"CC1784","ev":"The woman worships at the feet of the Jina, reciting praises silently.","cl":[["praise and flowers at the Jina's feet remove one's fault","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1785":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1785,"t":["சிந்திப்பலென் சிறுவன்றிற மினியென்றெழி னெடுங்கண்","வந்துபனி வார்ந்துமுலைக் கலிங்கமது நனைப்ப","வந்திலிருந் தாளவளுக் கடைந்துமன நடுங்கி","வந்தித்திருந் தார்மகிழ்ந்து காதன்மிக மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Saying 'I will think now on my son's lot', she sits there while tears run from her long eyes and wet the cloth over her breasts; the young men come up to her, their hearts trembling, and bow with joy and great love.","n":"Her thought for her son comes straight after the worship of 1784: the prayer does not end the grief but leads into it. The companions bow with love to a stranger; the reader alone knows she is their prince's mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1785","lb":"CC1785","ev":"The woman weeps thinking of her son, and the young men approach and bow to her.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை",""],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1786":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1786,"t":["வரையுடுத்த பள்ளியிட மாகவதின் மேயோள்","விரையுடுத்த போதுறையும் வேனெடுங்க ணாள்கொ","லுரையுடுத்த நாவுறையு மொண்ணுதல்கொ லன்றித்","திரையுடுத்த தேமொழிகொ லென்றுதெரி கல்லார்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing her dwelling in the hermitage girt by hills, they cannot tell whether she is the goddess with spear-long eyes who lives in the fragrant flower, the bright-browed goddess who lives on the tongue clothed in speech, or the sweet-voiced one clothed by the waves.","n":"The three are Lakshmi of the lotus, Sarasvati of the tongue, and a third named only as clothed in waves (திரையுடுத்த), perhaps the sea-girt earth; the triple உடுத்த ('clothed') ties the three together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1786","lb":"CC1786","ev":"The young men wonder which goddess she is.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1787":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1787,"t":["மங்கலம டிந்ததிரு மாமகளை யொப்பீ","ரிங்குவர வென்னைகுலம் யாதடிகட் கென்ன","வெங்குலமு மெம்வரவும் வேண்டிலெளி தன்றே","நுங்குலமு நும்வரவும் நீருரைமி னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The young men ask her, 'You who are like the great goddess of fortune whose auspiciousness has died, what brings you here, and what is the lady's clan?', and she answers that her clan and her coming are not easy to tell if they would know them, and bids them tell their own clan and coming first.","n":"Lines 1-2 are the young men's question and lines 3-4 her reply. மங்கலம் மடிந்த, 'whose auspiciousness has died', reads her as a widow from her dress. Her refusal keeps the secret of land and lineage the deity kept for her when she came (356).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kaimmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1787","lb":"CC1787","ev":"The young men ask the woman her clan; she declines and asks theirs.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1788":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1788,"t":["மோட்டுமுது நீர்மலங்கு மொய்த்தவிள வாளை","பூட்டுசிலை யிறவினொடு பொருதுதுயின் மடியு","மீட்டமுடை யவர்களுறை யிராசபுர மென்னு","நாட்டமுடை நகரமெம தாகுமுறை பதியே"],"p":"","r":"Devadattan answers that their dwelling place is the city of Rajamapuram, famed for its watchfulness, where young vaalai fish crowding the old deep water sleep after fighting with the bow-shaped prawns.","n":"The speaker of 1788-1796 is fixed only by 1792, where he names himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1788","lb":"CC1788","ev":"Devadattan names Rajamapuram as their city.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவதத்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1789":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1789,"t":["பொன்னுடைய மார்பிற்புகழ் மந்திரிபொ லந்தார்த்","தன்னுடைய நுண்ணுணர்விற் சாகரற்குத் தக்காள்","கொன்னெடிய வாட்கட்குரு தத்தைசீ தத்தன்","மன்னடுங்க வீங்குதிர டோண்மடங்க லன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"He names the first of them: Sitattan, lion-like, with shoulders that make kings tremble, son of Kurudattai of long sword-like eyes, the wife worthy of Sagaran, the golden-chested minister of subtle understanding.","n":"Each companion in 1789-1792 is named through both parents, the mother's beauty and the father's office or skill, so that the list is a list of lineages answering her question about clan. The father here is a minister (மந்திரி); whether this Sitattan is the merchant Sitattan of the sea voyage (493-580) the verses do not settle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1789","lb":"CC1789","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவதத்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1790":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1790,"t":["அளப்பரிய நான்மறையி னானசல னென்பான்","றிளைக்குந்திரு வொப்புடைய திலோத்தமைதன் சிறுவன்","விளைத்திரும்பு மேய்ந்தொழிந்த மிச்சில்வரை மார்ப","னிளைப்பலிவன் றேசுரைப்பிற் புத்திசே னிவ்விருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Next is Buddhisenan, son of Achalan, master of the four Vedas beyond measure, and of Tilottamai, whose beauty matches the goddess of fortune; his chest is like a hill, and Devadattan says he would tire if he told his splendour.","n":"Buddhisenan's father is a man of the four Vedas (நான்மறையினான்); the companions are of mixed birth, brahmin, minister's and merchant's sons, joined only by having been born on one day. Line 3 (விளைத்திரும்பு மேய்ந்தொழிந்த மிச்சில்) is obscure as divided.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1790","lb":"CC1790","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவதத்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1791":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1791,"t":["செட்டிதன பாலன்மனை யாள்சினவு வாட்கட்","பட்டநுதன் மின்னினகு பவித்திரைக்குத் தோன்றி","மட்டுமலர் மார்பின்மத யானையெயி றுழுதாங்","கிட்டகுறி தார்திவளப் பதுமுகனிவ் விருந்தோன்."],"p":"","r":"Then Padumukan, born to Pavittirai of bright brow and angry sword-like eyes, wife of the merchant Danapalan, whose honeyed chest bears the scars a musth elephant ploughed with its tusk.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1791","lb":"CC1791","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவதத்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1792":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1792,"t":["பொன்னகருள் வேந்தன்பெய ராற்பொறியும் பெற்றான்","வின்மரிய தோள்விசய தத்தனுயிர்க் கவசம்","பின்னரிய கற்பினவள் பிரீதிமதி காதற்","றன்மகனென் யானடிக டேவதத்த னென்பேன்."],"p":"","r":"Last he names himself: 'I, sir, am Devadattan, the loving son of Piritimati of unmatched chastity, who is the life's armour of Vijayadattan of bow-scarred shoulders, who won an emblem bearing the king's name in the golden city.'","n":"His mother is called the 'life's armour' (உயிர்க் கவசம்) of his father, chastity praised as a protection to the husband, said to a woman who has outlived hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1792","lb":"CC1792","ev":"Devadattan names himself and his parents.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவதத்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1793":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1793,"t":["எங்கள்வினை யாலிறைவன் வீடியவஞ் ஞான்றே","யெங்களுயிர் நம்பியொடு யாங்கள்பிறந் தேமா","வெங்கடமர் நம்பிக்கிவர் தோழரென வீந்தா","ரெங்கெழிலென் ஞாயிறென வின்னணம்வ ளர்ந்தேம்."],"p":"","r":"He says that on the very day the king perished through their own ill deeds, they were born together with their prince, who is their life; they were given to the prince as his companions, and grew like the sun in beauty.","n":"எங்கள் வினையால் takes the king's death as the companions' own karma, a humble formula the epic gives to several dependants (the deity as maid in 315). He tells this to the king's widow without knowing her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","natpu","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1793","lb":"CC1793","ev":"Devadattan tells how the companions were born on the day of the king's death and given to the prince.","cl":[["a master's fall can be the fruit of his dependants' own deeds","general","reported",1,"devadattan"]],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவதத்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1794":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1794,"t":["யாண்டுநிறைந் தேகியபி னந்தனவற் கிளையார்","மாண்டகுணத் தார்நபுல விபுலரொடு மன்னு","மீண்டவளர்ந் தேந்தவிசி னுச்சிமிசை யெய்தித்","தீண்டரிய வெம்மையொடு திக்கயங்க ளெனவே."],"p":"","r":"When years had passed, Nandattan, the prince's younger brother, with Nabulan and Vipulan of noble qualities, grew up with them and reached the height of the raised seat, like the elephants of the quarters, with a heat none could touch.","n":"Line 4 (தீண்டரிய வெம்மையொடு திக்கயங்கள் எனவே) ends the sentence on a simile without a main verb; the next verse completes the account.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1794","lb":"CC1794","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவதத்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1795":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1795,"t":["விற்றொழிலும் வாட்டொழிலும் வீணைபொரு தொழிலு","மற்றொழிலுந் தோத்தொழிலும் வாரணத்தின் றொழிலு","நற்றொழில வாசியொடு நன்கலைக ணீந்திக்","கற்றனங்கள் யாமுமுடன் கற்பனக ளெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"They all learned together archery, swordplay, the contest of the vina, wrestling, the work of the hide shield, the handling of elephants and the fine art of the horse, swimming across all the arts.","n":"The vina is listed among the martial arts as a contest (வீணைபொரு தொழில்), which is how Civakan used it to win Gandharvadattai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1795","lb":"CC1795","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவதத்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1796":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1796,"t":["வெஞ்சிலையின் வேடர்தொறு மீட்டுவிசும் பேகும்","விஞ்சையரை யன்மகளை வீணைபொரு தெய்திக்","குஞ்சரமும் வென்றுகுண மாலைநல னுண்ட","நம்பியவ னாமமெவ னென்னினிது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"And if she asks the name of the prince who rescued the herds from the hunters of cruel bows, won by vina contest the daughter of the Vidyadhara king who goes back to the sky, overcame the elephant and enjoyed Gunamalai's love, it is this.","n":"Devadattan recounts Civakan's deeds in order (the cattle raid, the vina contest, the elephant and Gunamalai) to the one listener who most needs them and who has not yet been told whose they are; the name itself is held over to the next verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","niraikol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1796","lb":"CC1796","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவதத்தன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:76"},"cc:1797":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1797,"t":["கந்துக்கட னென்றநகர்க் காதிமுது நாய்கன்","முந்திப்பெறப் பட்டமகன் மூரிச்சிலைத் தடக்கைச்","சிந்திப்பவ ரவலமறு சீவகனென் றோழ","னந்திலொரு நாளவனை யரசனொரு தவற்றால்"],"p":"","r":"A friend begins his report: Civakan, the strong-bowed first son taken up by Kantukkatan, the old merchant chief of the city, who ends the grief of all who think of him, was one day, for a fault, seized by the king.","n":"The speaker calls Civakan என் தோழன் and names the foster father, not the true one: he does not yet know whose son he is talking to. Who the king is (Kattiyangaran) is left to the story so far; the fault (தவறு) is not named here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","arasu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1797","lb":"CC1797","ev":"Civakan's companion tells Vijayai that the king seized Civakan for an offence.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1798":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1798,"t":["தொடிகடவழ் வீங்குதிர டோளிறுக யாத்துக்","கடிகடவழ் குழன்மகளிர் கசிந்துமனங் கரியக்","கொடிகடவழ் மாடநகர்க் கொல்லவென மாழ்கி","யிடிகடவழ்ந் திட்டபட நாகமென வீழ்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the king had Civakan's bangled shoulders bound tight to be killed in the city of bannered mansions while the women wept, and the mother, stricken, falls like a hooded snake struck by thunder.","n":"Line 4's வீழ்ந்தாள் shifts from the friend's report to the narrator's, so the verse carries both voices; the falling woman is named only in later verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1798","lb":"CC1798","ev":"Hearing that Civakan was bound to be put to death, Vijayai falls senseless.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1799":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1799,"t":["மாதவப் பெருமை வண்ண மாநகர் நம்பிக் குற்ற","வேதத்தைக் கேட்ட லோடு மிருகணும் பிறழ்ந்து மாழ்கிக்","காதற்றம் மகனுக் குற்ற நவையெனக் கலங்கி வீழ்ந்தா","ராதலா னங்கை யாரே யருள்பெரி துடைய ரென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"On hearing the harm that came to the young lord, the ascetic women of the great hermitage, their eyes rolling, are shaken as though it were harm to their own dear son and fall, and those present say that women indeed have great compassion.","n":"Who falls and who speaks the closing words (என்றார்) is not stated; reading the fallers as the hermitage women and the speakers as the onlookers is a guess from line 1's மாதவப் பெருமை and line 3's தம் மகனுக்கு.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","idumbai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1799","lb":"CC1799","ev":"The ascetic women fall in grief as if Civakan were their own son.","cl":[["women are great in compassion","general","reported",4,"civakan_thozhar"]],"ag":["மாதவ மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1800":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1800,"t":["மாழ்குபு மயங்கி வீழ்ந்த மாபெருந் தேவி தன்னை","யாழ்துய ரவித்தற் கொத்த வரும்பெறல் யோக நாடிக்","காழ்பரிந் தரைத்த சாந்தின் களிதரு நீரிற் றேற்ற","யாழ்புரை கிளவி யாற்றாண் மயங்கிவீழ்ந் தரற்று கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"They seek a fit remedy to quench the great queen's swoon and revive her with water thick with ground sandal, and she of the lute-like voice, unable to bear it, faints again and begins to lament.","n":"The narrator gives her the title மாபெருந் தேவி, the queen's rank she has hidden in the hermitage, just before the friends learn it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1800","lb":"CC1800","ev":"Vijayai is revived with sandal water, faints again and laments.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","மாதவ மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1801":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1801,"t":["கைம்மாண் கடற்படையுட் காவலனை யாண்டொழியப்","பொய்ம்மா மயிலூர்ந்து போகிப் புறங்காட்டுள்","விம்மாந்தி யான்வீழ வீழ்ந்தேன் றுணையாகி","யெம்மானே தோன்றினா யென்னை யொளித்தியோ."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai cries that when the guardian fell amid his sea-wide army and she rode the false peacock to the burning ground and fell there, he, her lord, appeared as her companion, and asks whether he now hides from her.","n":"She calls the newborn எம்மானே and her companion (துணையாகி): the child born in the cremation ground is remembered as the one who stood by her there, and his reported death as his hiding from her (ஒளித்தியோ), the word the dead king's women used of him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","makkal","thunai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1801","lb":"CC1801","ev":"Vijayai recalls the king's death, her flight on the mechanical peacock and her son's birth at the burning ground.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1802":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1802,"t":["கையா ரிலங்கெஃகிற் கந்துக்கடன் கொடுபோய்","மொய்யா ருவகையனாய் முற்றிழைக்குத் தான்கொடுப்ப","நையாள் வளர்த்த சுநந்தை நவையுற வென்","னையாவென் னையாவென் னையா வகன்றனையே."],"p":"","r":"She laments that Kantukkatan, with his bright spear, carried the child off in joy and gave him to his wife, and that Sunandai who reared him without tiring is now struck by this harm, crying 'my lord' again and again because he has gone.","n":"Line 3 நையாள் வளர்த்த சுநந்தை நவையுற வென் divides doubtfully; whether the harm is said to fall on Sunandai or on the speaker turns on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1802","lb":"CC1802","ev":"Vijayai recalls the merchant taking the child to his wife Sunandai, who raised him.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1803":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1803,"t":["மின்னிரைத்த பைம்பூண் விளங்கிலை வேல்வேந்தன்","முன்னுரைத்த மூன்று கனவும் புணையாக","வென்னுயிரைத் தாங்கி யிருந்தேன் வலியாகா","தென்னரசே யென்பூசல் கேளா திறந்தனையே."],"p":"","r":"She says the three dreams the spear-bearing king once spoke of were the raft on which she bore her life, and now her king has died without hearing her cry, and that raft has failed her.","n":"முன்னுரைத்த points back to the king's reading of her dreams before Civakan's birth; she held to the promise of the son's crown and did not let herself weigh the falling tree he would not explain. என்னரசே here addresses the son, the king the dream promised.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","makkal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1803","lb":"CC1803","ev":"Vijayai says the king's reading of her three dreams kept her alive and has now failed.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1804":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1804,"t":["கோவமா வாகிக் குடியோம்பி நின்குடைக்கீழ்ப்","பாவமே செய்தேன் பரிவெலா நீங்கினாற்","போவம்மா வென்றுரைப்பப் போவேன்முன் போயினா","யாவம்மா வம்மாவென் னம்மா வகன்றனையே."],"p":"","r":"She says that hoping to become the king's mother and see him guard the people under his umbrella, she has done only sin; she had thought that when her care was over he would bid her go and she would go, but he has gone before her.","n":"Line 1 கோவமா வாகி is hard to divide (கோ அம்மா ஆகி, 'becoming the king's mother', is one guess), and the sense of the whole verse turns on it. The grief is the reversal of the order of deaths: a mother expected to be sent off by her son.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","makkal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1804","lb":"CC1804","ev":"","cl":[["her loss is the fruit of the sin she herself has done","general","reported",2,"vijayai"]],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1805":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1805,"t":["கோமான் மகனே குருகுலத்தார் போரேறே","யேமாங் கதத்தா ரிறைவாவென் னின்னுயிரே","காமா கடலுட் கலங்கவிழ்த்தேன் கண்ணுணீர்","பூமாண் புனைதாராய் நோக்காது போதியோ."],"p":"","r":"She calls him son of the king, bull of war of the Kuru line, lord of Emamangatham, her dear life, a Kama, and says she has wrecked her ship in the sea, and asks whether he will go without seeing her tears.","n":"In grief she says aloud what she has kept hidden: the titles கோமான் மகனே, குருகுலத்தார் போரேறே and ஏமாங்கதத்தார் இறைவா tell the listening friends that Civakan is the dead king's son.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kudi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1805","lb":"CC1805","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1806":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1806,"t":["கந்தார் களியானைக் காவலனார் கான்முளையை","வந்தார்வாய்த் தீதின்மை கேட்டு மறைந்திருந்து","நொந்தேன் பலகாலு நோயோடே வீகின்றே","னந்தோ வறனேமற் றாற்றேனா லாற்றேனால்."],"p":"","r":"She says that hearing from those who came that the king's son was unharmed, she stayed hidden and grieved many times, and now she wastes away with this sorrow, crying 'alas, Aram, I cannot bear it'.","n":"She addresses அறனே directly, as if the moral order owed her the son for whose sake she stayed hidden. Her concealment (மறைந்திருந்து) was the deity's counsel at the birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","idumbai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1806","lb":"CC1806","ev":"Vijayai tells how she lived hidden, content with reports of her son's safety.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1807":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1807,"t":["முன்னொருகா லென்மகனைக் கண்டேனென் கண்குளிரப்","பின்னொருகாற் காணப் பிழைத்ததென் றேவிர்கா","ளென்னொப்பார் பெண்மகளி ரிவ்வுலகிற் றோன்றற்கென்","றன்னப் பெடைநடையா ளாய்மயில்போல் வீழ்ந்தனளே."],"p":"","r":"She says she once saw her son and her eyes were cooled, but the hope of seeing him once more has failed, and cries that no women like her should be born in this world; then the one with the gait of a hen-swan falls like a peacock.","n":"Line 2 பிழைத்ததென் றேவிர்கா divides doubtfully; reading ஏவிர்காள் as a call to the women around her is a guess. The wish that no woman like her be born turns her grief into a judgement on her own birth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","makkal","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1807","lb":"CC1807","ev":"Vijayai ends her lament and falls senseless again.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","மாதவ மகளிர்"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1808":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1808,"t":["புண்மல்கு மத்தகத்த போர்வேழம் பொற்பழித்த","மண்மல்கு தாரான் பெருமாட்டி வாய்மொழிகேட்","டுண்மல்கு நெஞ்சினரா யொய்யெனவே வெய்துயிராக்","கண்மல்கு நீரார் முகமுகங்க ணோக்கினரே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the words of the queen of the garlanded king who shamed the battle elephants, the friends, hearts brimming, sigh hotly and look into one another's faces with eyes full of tears.","n":"The recognition is wordless: they look at each other's faces. The narrator gives her the title பெருமாட்டி of the king, the knowledge they have just gained.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1808","lb":"CC1808","ev":"The friends realise from her lament that Vijayai is the dead king's queen and Civakan his son.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1809":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1809,"t":["கண்டீர் கருமம் விளைந்தவா றென்றாராய்","வண்டாரார் வண்கடக மின்னத்தங் கைம்மறித்துக்","கொண்டாங் கடல்வேலி கீழ்மகனைக் கூற்றமா","யுண்டா முயிரென் றுவப்பெழுந் தாடினரே."],"p":"","r":"Saying 'see how the deed has ripened', the friends wave their hands with bright bracelets and rise and dance for joy, saying that a life has come into being that will be Death to the base man who seized the sea-girt land.","n":"கருமம் விளைந்தவாறு can be read as the usurper's deed ripening against him or as the fortune now ripening for Civakan; either way the friends read the queen's secret as fate's work. Their joy follows straight on her grief, since they know what she does not, that Civakan lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kootru","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1809","lb":"CC1809","ev":"The friends rejoice that Civakan is the rightful heir who will destroy the usurper.","cl":[["a deed ripens and brings its fruit to the doer","general","reported",1,"civakan_thozhar"],["the usurper who seized the land will meet his death through the rightful heir","general","reported",4,"civakan_thozhar"]],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்",""],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1810":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1810,"t":["வீழ்ந்து மயில்போல் விசயை கிடந்தாளைத்","தாழ்ந்து பலதட்பந் தாஞ்செய்ய வேல்பெற்றுப்","போழ்ந்தகன்ற கண்ணாள் புலம்பா வெழுந்திருப்பச்","சூழ்ந்து தொழுதிறைஞ்சிச் சொன்னா ரவன்றிறமே."],"p":"","r":"As Vijayai lies fallen like a peacock, they bend over her and do many cooling things, and when she of wide spear-split eyes rises lamenting, they surround her, bow and worship and tell her how it stands with him.","n":"They now bow and worship (தொழுது இறைஞ்சி) the woman they had addressed as a hermitage mother: the recognition changes their bearing before it changes their words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1810","lb":"CC1810","ev":"The friends revive Vijayai, bow to her and begin to tell her what became of Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1811":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1811,"t":["கொலைக்களங் குறுகலுங் கொண்டொர் தெய்வத","நிலைக்கள மிதுவென நீக்க நீங்கினா","னிலக்கண மடப்பிடி யியைந்தொர் போதக","மலைக்கணத் திடைமகிழ்ந் தனைய மைந்தனே."],"p":"","r":"The friends tell her that when he neared the execution ground a deity took him, saying 'this is a deity's station', and when it removed him he went, and the young man now rejoices like a tusker calf joined with a well-marked young she-elephant among the herds of the hills.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 (கொண்டொர் தெய்வத / நிலைக்கள மிதுவென) divide doubtfully, so what the deity is said to have declared is unsure. The friends know only that a deity took him; the story told earlier, of the dog he once helped returning as a god, is not in their account.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thunai","kolai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1811","lb":"CC1811","ev":"The friends tell Vijayai that a deity carried Civakan from the execution ground and that he now lives happily with a wife.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1812":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1812,"t":["பூவுடைத் தெரியலான் போர்வை நீத்தினிக்","கோவுடைப் பெருமக னாதல் கொண்டனஞ்","சேவடி சோந்தனந் தொழுது சென்றன","மாவடு நோக்கியுண் மகிழ்ந்து கூறினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The friends say that the garlanded one, putting off his disguise, will now take on being the great son who owns the kingship, and that they have come and bowed at her red feet; she of the mango-slice eyes, rejoicing within, speaks.","n":"போர்வை, 'the covering', is his life as a merchant's son: the friends treat the foster identity as a cloak to be thrown off now the birth is known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1812","lb":"CC1812","ev":"The friends promise that Civakan will claim his kingship, and Vijayai, glad, begins to answer.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1813":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1813,"t":["தரணி காவலன் சச்சந்த னென்பவன்","பரணி நாட்பிறந் தான்பகை யாவையு","மரணி லானென்கட் டங்கிய வன்பினா","லிரணி யன்பட்ட தெம்மிறை யெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai says that the guardian of the earth called Saccandan, born under Bharani, who stood unguarded against all his foes, met the end Hiranya met through the love he kept fixed on her.","n":"பகை யாவையு / மரணி லானென்கட் divides doubtfully (அரண் இலான், 'without defence', is one reading). She blames her husband's death on his love for her, the cause the councillors had named. The comparison with இரணியன் draws on a Vaishnava story in a Jain epic, used only as a figure for a sudden violent death.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kaamam","arasu","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1813","lb":"CC1813","ev":"Vijayai tells the friends who Civakan's father was and how he died.","cl":[["the king fell because of his love for his queen","general","reported",3,"vijayai"]],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1814":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1814,"t":["விசயை யென்றுல கோடிய வீறிலேன்","பசையி னாற்றுஞ்சி யான்பட்ட தீதெலா","மிசைய நம்பிக் கெடுத்துரைத் தென்னுழை","யசைவின் றையனைத் தம்மி னெனச்சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that they should tell Nambi in order all the evil she, Vijayai, whose name went through the world and who is now without glory, suffered when her lord died through his attachment, and bring the young lord to her without fail.","n":"பசையி னாற்றுஞ்சி may mean the king died of attachment or that she lay in it; the verse's account of her suffering turns on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","makkal","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1814","lb":"CC1814","ev":"Vijayai asks the friends to tell Civakan her story and bring him to her.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1815":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1815,"t":["கோதை வேனம்பிக் கல்லதை யிப்பொருள்","யாதுங் கூறின்மின் யாரையுந் தேறன்மி","னேத மின்னன வின்னண மெய்தலாற்","பேதை யாரொடும் பெண்ணொடும் பேசன்மின்."],"p":"","r":"She tells them to speak of this matter to no one but Nambi and to trust no one, and, since harm comes in such ways, not to talk with fools or with women.","n":"A woman who has hidden her own rank for years bars women from the secret; the warning falls on her own sex.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","aaraaydhal","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1815","lb":"CC1815","ev":"Vijayai charges the friends to keep Civakan's birth secret.","cl":[["in danger, trust no one with a secret","general","reported",2,"vijayai"],["a secret must not be spoken to fools or to women","general","reported",4,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1816":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1816,"t":["பகைவ ருள்ளமும் பாம்பின் படர்ச்சியும்","வகைகொண் மேகலை மங்கையர் நெஞ்சமு","மிகைசென் மேகத்து மின்னுஞ்செந் நில்லலா","புகைசெய் வேலினீர் போற்றுபு சென்மினே."],"p":"","r":"She says that the hearts of enemies, the track of a snake, the hearts of girdled women and lightning in the high cloud do not go straight, and tells the men of smoking spears to go on their way guarding themselves.","n":"The list sets women's hearts among things whose course cannot be predicted, next to enemies and snakes; the speaker is the epic's model of the faithful wife, so the maxim is offered as prudence, not grievance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vanjam","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1816","lb":"CC1816","ev":"","cl":[["women's hearts, like enemies' hearts, do not run straight","general","reported",2,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1817":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1817,"t":["வணக்கருஞ் சிலையி னானை யொருமதி யெல்லை நாளுட்","குணத்தொடு மலிந்த பாதங் குறுகயாங் கொணர்ந்த பின்றைப்","பணித்ததே செய்து பற்றார் பகைமுத லடர்த்து மென்றார்","மணிக்கொடி மாசுண் டன்னாண் மற்றதே துணிமி னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The friends promise that within one month they will bring the man of the unbending bow to her virtuous feet and then do as she commands and crush the enemy at the root, and she, like a jewelled creeper soiled, tells them to resolve on that.","n":"The vow sets a term (ஒருமதி எல்லை) the story will have to keep, and the mother's command, not Civakan's, is to start the war on the usurper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arasu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1817","lb":"CC1817","ev":"The friends vow to bring Civakan to his mother within a month, and Vijayai approves.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1818":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1818,"t":["பொறிதவ நெருங்க நோற்றுப் புகரற நிறைந்த கொள்கைச்","செறிதவ விசயை பாதஞ் சென்னியின் வணங்கி மீண்டு","வெறிகமழ் சோலை நண்ணி வேண்டிய வடிசில் கைதொட்","டெறிபடை யெழுக வென்றார் வளையெழுந் தார்த்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Bowing their heads at the feet of Vijayai of close penance, who fasted with her senses held tight and kept a flawless rule of life, they return to the fragrant grove, eat the food they wish and order the army to rise, and the conches rise roaring.","n":"பொறிதவ நெருங்க நோற்று gives the widow's life in the hermitage the language of ascetic sense-restraint, in the verse that sends out an army at her command.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","adakkam","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1818","lb":"CC1818","ev":"The friends take leave of Vijayai, eat in the grove and set the army marching.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1819":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1819,"t":["பைந்துகின் மகளிர் தேன்சோர் பவளவாய் திகழ நாணிச்","சிந்தித்துக் கூந்தல் வாங்கிச் செவ்வணந் துடைப்ப தேபோ","லிந்திர கோபங் கௌவி யிறகுளர் கின்ற மஞ்ஞை","யந்தரத் திவர்ந்த பாய்மா வரும்பொற்றா ரரவத் தாலே."],"p":"","r":"At the din of the golden bells of the horses leaping skyward, a peacock that had caught a red velvet-mite and was preening its feathers looks like silk-clad women who, abashed, their coral lips shining, draw back their hair and wipe away its redness.","n":"The image of shy women wiping away red closes a chapter that has just warned against women's hearts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1819","lb":"CC1819","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:77"},"cc:1820":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1820,"t":["சாந்தின்மேற் றொடுத்த தீந்தேன் றண்மதிக் கோடு போழப்","போந்துமட் டருவி வீழும் பொன்னெடுங் குன்று மந்த","ணேந்துபூங் காவு சூழ்ந்த விரும்புனல் யாறு நீந்தி","மாந்தரே மலிந்த நாடு மடுத்துடன் சென்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Swimming the broad rivers ringed by flowering groves, past tall golden hills where honey hung on the sandal trees, split by the moon's horn, falls in streams of nectar, the company reached a land thick with people.","n":"The verb சென்றது has no named subject; the next verses show it is the band of Civakan's friends travelling in search of him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1820","lb":"CC1820","ev":"Civakan's companions and their force cross hills and rivers and reach a populous country.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1821":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1821,"t":["மதுக்குலா மலங்கன் மாலை மங்கையர் வளர்த்த செந்தீப்","புதுக்கலத் தெழுந்த தீம்பாற் பொங்கலி னுரையிற் பொங்கிக்","கதிர்த்துவெண் மாடந் தோன்றுஞ் செவ்வெயிற் காத நான்கி","னதிக்கரை வந்து விட்டார் நச்செயிற் றரவோ டொப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who were like poison-fanged snakes came to a river bank four kaatham from the city whose white mansions rise like the foam of milk boiling over in new pots.","n":"The companions are named only by a simile of menace, poison-fanged snakes, which prepares the plan of attack that follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1821","lb":"CC1821","ev":"The companions halt on a river bank four kaatham from Emamapuram.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1822":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1822,"t":["மானயா நோக்கியர் மருங்குல் போல்வதோர்","கானயாற் றடைகரைக் கதிர்கண் போழ்கலாத்","தேனயாம் பூம்பொழிற் றிண்ணை வெண்மணற்","றானையா நால்வருந் தணப்பின் றெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"The four, with their force, came without delay to the white-sand bank of a forest river, a grove of honey where sunlight could not pierce, its curve like the waist of doe-eyed women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1822","lb":"CC1822","ev":"The four friends reach a shaded grove on the river bank.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1823":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1823,"t":["வார்ந்துதேன் றுளித்துமட் டுயிர்த்து வார்மண","லார்ந்துபோ தருந்துவி சடுத்த தொத்துமேற்","றூர்ந்துதேன் வண்டொடு துதைந்துள் புக்கவர்","போந்துபோக் கரியதப் பொழிலின் பெற்றியே."],"p":"","r":"Honey dripping, toddy breathing, flowers spread over the long sand and bees crowding the clustered blossoms above, the grove they entered had a nature hard to leave once one had come in.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1823","lb":"CC1823","ev":"The friends enter the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1824":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1824,"t":["தாதணி கொழுநிழ லிருந்து தண்மதுப்","போதணி யலங்கறாழ் பொருவின் மார்பனை","யாதுநா மடைதிற முரைமி னீரெனக்","காதலாற் பதுமுகன் கண்டு கூறினான்."],"p":"","r":"Seated in the rich shade, Padumukan, out of love, asks the others to say how they may reach the peerless-chested man with the honeyed garland.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1824","lb":"CC1824","ev":"Padumukan asks his fellows how they may reach Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1825":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1825,"t":["திருக்கிளர் மன்னவன் சேனை மாநகர்","பொருக்கொளி யினநிரை கோடுங் கொண்டபின்","முருக்கொளி மலரடி மூரி மொய்ம்பனைச்","செருக்களத் தெதிர்ப்படச் சிதைவ தில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Padumukan proposes that once they have seized the bright herds of the glorious king's city, they will meet the mighty one face to face on the field, and no harm will come of it.","n":"The raid is a lure: the friends count on Civakan riding out against cattle raiders as he did for the herdsmen of Rajamapuram, and on recognising him in the fight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","soozhchi","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1825","lb":"CC1825","ev":"Padumukan proposes raiding the king's herds to draw Civakan out to battle.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1826":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1826,"t":["சேட்குலாஞ் சிலையொடு திளைத்த பின்னவர்","வாட்கலாம் வலித்தமர் தொடங்கின் வல்லையே","மீட்கலாம் விருப்புடைத் தெழுக வென்றுதன்","னாட்கெலாஞ் செப்பின னலர்ந்த தாரினான்."],"p":"","r":"After they have traded bows, he says, if the fight comes to swords they can quickly bring him back, so rise eager; so the garlanded one told all his men.","n":"மீட்கலாம் may mean that they can win Civakan back or that they can pull out of the fight; the sense of the plan turns on which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1826","lb":"CC1826","ev":"Padumukan rouses his men for the raid.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","ஆள்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1827":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1827,"t":["இருங்கடன் மணிநிரை யெய்திநாங் கொண்டபின்","னருங்கடி யணிநக ரையனங் கில்லையேற்","பெரும்படை தான்வரிற் பின்றிநீங் கிற்பழி","தரும்படித் தன்றியுஞ் சாற்றுவல் கேண்மினோ."],"p":"","r":"Buddhisenan objects that if, after they take the herd, the lord is not in the guarded city and the great army comes out, turning back will bring blame, and he has more to say.","n":"The objection exposes the flaw in the lure: the plan depends on Civakan being in the city, which no one has checked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","soozhchi","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1827","lb":"CC1827","ev":"Buddhisenan warns that the raid may bring the king's army and not Civakan.","cl":[["to withdraw from a fight once begun brings blame","general","reported",3,"buddhisenan"]],"ag":["புத்திசேனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்திசேனன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1828":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1828,"t":["மஞ்சுசூழ் விசும்பிடை மணந்துமின் மிளிர்வபோல்","வஞ்சமின் மறவர்வாண் மிளிர்ந்துபாய் குருதியுட்","குஞ்சரங் குளிப்பதோர் நீத்தமா மாதலா","லெஞ்சலில் கொள்கையீ ரெண்ணிச்சூழ் மின்களே."],"p":"","r":"Like lightning flashing in a cloud-filled sky, the swords of honest warriors will flash and elephants will plunge in a flood of leaping blood, so, men of unfailing resolve, think and decide.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","padai","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1828","lb":"CC1828","ev":"","cl":[["a battle, once joined, becomes a flood of blood, so it must be weighed before it is begun","general","reported",4,"buddhisenan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்திசேனன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1829":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1829,"t":["என்றனன் புத்திசே னென்னுநான் மறையினா","னன்றதே பொருளென நால்வரு மிருந்துழி","யொன்றிமுன் விடுத்தவர் மூவரொற் றாட்கள்வந்","தின்றிதாற் பட்டதென் றியம்புகின் றார்களே."],"p":"","r":"So spoke Buddhisenan of the four Vedas; as the four sat, agreeing that this was the point, the three spies they had sent ahead came together and announced what had happened that day.","n":"நான்மறையினான் makes Buddhisenan a man of the four Vedas, a Brahmin counsellor among the friends. The three spies who come in agreement (ஒன்றி) recall the Kural's rule that three spies' concurring word is to be trusted, though ஒன்றி may instead go with the sending.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1829","lb":"CC1829","ev":"The friends accept Buddhisenan's counsel, and their three spies return with news.","cl":[],"ag":["புத்திசேனன்","தோழர்","ஒற்றாட்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1830":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1830,"t":["வளையசுந் தரமெனும் வாரண மால்வரை","முளையிளந் திங்கள்போன் முத்துடைக் கோட்டது","கிளையிளம் பிடிகளைஞ் ஞாற்றிடைக் கேழரக்","களையவஞ் சனவரை யனையதக் களிறரோ."],"p":"","r":"The spies tell of Valaya Sundaram, the king's elephant like a great mountain of collyrium, with pearl-filled tusks like the young crescent, the lord of five hundred young cow-elephants.","n":"From here to 1842 the account follows the spies' announcement (இயம்புகின்றார்கள், 1829), and 1838's என்பவே and 1842's என்மரும் keep the voice of report; the passage may also be read as the poet's own narration. Line 3 to 4 (கேழரக் களைய) divides doubtfully.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1830","lb":"CC1830","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1831":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1831,"t":["கடுமதக் களிப்பினாற் காரென முழங்கலின்","விடுகலார் பாகரும் வெருவரக் கொன்றிடப்","பிடியொடுங் கந்தணை வின்றிநீ ருருள்பிளந்","தடுகளி றந்தப்போ திகைபரிந் தழன்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Raging in its fierce must and roaring like a rain cloud, it killed the mahouts who could not let it go, broke from its post and the cow-elephant, burst its bonds and at that hour went wild.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1831","lb":"CC1831","ev":"The king's elephant goes into must, kills its mahouts and breaks loose.","cl":[],"ag":["வளையசுந்தரம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1832":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1832,"t":["கண்ணுமிழ் தீயினாற் சுடநிறங் கரிந்தபோற்","பண்ணுமிழ் வண்டுலாய்ப் பரத்தரா நின்றசீ","ரண்ணலங் களிற்றினை யடக்கினான் சீவகன்","வண்ணமே கலையினார் மனமெனப் படிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan subdued the noble elephant, whose skin seemed scorched by the fire its eyes spat and over which singing bees swarmed, and it grew meek as the minds of women in jewelled girdles.","n":"The simile likens the tamed beast to the hearts of women won by the hero, the epic's recurring picture of Civakan as one who masters both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1832","lb":"CC1832","ev":"Civakan subdues the raging elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","வளையசுந்தரம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1833":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1833,"t":["இறுவரை யிவர்வதோ ரிலங்கெயிற் றரியென","வுறுவரை மார்பினான் றூசங்கொண் டொய்யெனப்","பெறலருங் குஞ்சர மேறலிற் பெருஞ்சன","மறைகடற் றிரையொலித் தாங்கென வார்த்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Like a lion with shining teeth climbing a steep hill, the broad-chested man seized the rope and swiftly mounted the rare elephant, and the great crowd roared like the sea's waves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1833","lb":"CC1833","ev":"Civakan mounts the elephant and the crowd roars.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பெருஞ்சனம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1834":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1834,"t":["அங்கையந் தலத்தினா லப்புதா தையெனக்","கொங்கலர் கண்ணியான் கொம்மைதான் கொட்டலும்","பொங்கிய வுவகையிற் பொலிந்துமாக் களிறவன்","றங்கிய பயிர்த்தொழி றடக்கையாற் செய்ததே."],"p":"","r":"As the garlanded youth patted it with his palm, calling it fondly, the great elephant, swelling with joy, did with its trunk the trained responses it knew.","n":"அப்புதா தையென is read here as fond calls to the beast (அப்பு, தாதை); the word division is doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1834","lb":"CC1834","ev":"Civakan calms the elephant with pats and it answers to his commands.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","வளையசுந்தரம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1835":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1835,"t":["கொட்டையம் புரோசைதா னிருவடங் கொண்டுடன்","கட்டினான் கருவலித் தடக்கையாற் றோட்டியு","மிட்டன னிரண்டுட னிமிழ்க்கொளீஇ யிலங்குபொற்","பட்டமும் பனிவரை மின்னெனக் கட்டினான்."],"p":"","r":"He bound the neck rope doubled with its tassel knot, set the goad in his strong hand, tied on the two bells together and fastened the shining gold forehead plate, like lightning on a snowy hill.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1835","lb":"CC1835","ev":"Civakan harnesses the elephant with rope, goad, bells and forehead plate.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1836":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1836,"t":["கச்சையும் வீக்கினன் கறங்கிரு மணியணிந்","தச்சுறு கொழுந்தொடர் யாப்பழித் தடியிணை","யுச்சியும் புரோசையுட் குளிப்பவுய்த் துறுவலி","மெச்சிமேல் வேந்தனும் விழைதகத் தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"He girded its belt, hung on the two sounding bells, set both his feet in the rope at its head and neck, and showed such strength that even the king above longed to see him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1836","lb":"CC1836","ev":"Civakan finishes the harness and sits ready, admired by the king.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1837":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1837,"t":["கோற்றொடிப் புரிசையுட் கொற்றவ னின்றைய","னேற்றியல் காண்டுநா மிவட்டரு கென்னவே","காற்றெனக் கடலெனக் கருவரை யுருமெனக்","கூற்றெனக் குஞ்சரங் கொண்டுபுக் கானரோ."],"p":"","r":"When the victorious king, standing within the walled women's quarter, said 'let us see the lord's riding; bring it here', Civakan drove the elephant in like wind, like the sea, like thunder on a black hill, like Death.","n":"The king is not named; he is presumably Tadamittan of Emamapuram, Civakan's host. கோற்றொடிப் புரிசை, the enclosure of the bangled women, puts the display before the palace women, among them Kanakamalai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1837","lb":"CC1837","ev":"At the king's bidding Civakan rides the elephant into the palace court.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1838":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1838,"t":["குழவியஞ் செல்வனோர் குன்றுகொண் டொய்யென","வழகிதாப் பறப்பதே போலவு மார்புயன்","மழையையூர்ந் தோடுமோர் வானவன் போலவு","மெழுதலா காவண மிருந்தன னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"They say he sat beyond any painter's power, like a young god flying lightly with a hill in his grasp, or a god riding a rain cloud.","n":"Who the young god carrying a hill is (குழவியஞ் செல்வன்) is not clear from the verse; the image fits the hero high on the mountain-like beast.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1838","lb":"CC1838","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1839":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1839,"t":["வனைகலத் திகிரியும் வாழுயிர் மேற்செலுங்","கனைகடுங் கதழ்பரிக் காலசக் கரமும்போல்","வினைதகு வட்டமும் வீதியும் பத்தியு","மினையவை யேமுற விமைப்பினி னியற்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a potter's spinning wheel, or the wheel of time that runs over living beings with swift horses, he made it trace in a twinkling the circles, the straight courses and the rows that the art requires.","n":"The second simile is காலசக்கரம், the wheel of time that passes over living lives: a reminder of mortality set inside a display of horsemanship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1839","lb":"CC1839","ev":"Civakan puts the elephant through its circling and straight gaits.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1840":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1840,"t":["ஒருவனே களிறுமொன் றோருநூ றாயிரந்","திரிவவே போன்றன திசையெலாங் குஞ்சரக்","குரியவன் னிவனலா லுலகினில் லிலனென","வரிதுணர் வேத்தவை யமைகமற் றென்றதெ."],"p":"","r":"One man and one elephant looked like a hundred thousand wandering in every direction, and the discerning royal assembly declared there was no one else in the world who could own an elephant, and said no more.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","avai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1840","lb":"CC1840","ev":"The king's assembly proclaims Civakan the one true master of elephants.","cl":[],"ag":["வேத்தவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1841":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1841,"t":["வள்ளுகிர் நுதியினால் வரிநுத லுறுத்தலு","முள்ளுணர் குஞ்சரம் ஓய்யென நிற்றலு","மௌளரும் மிருமணி கிணினென விசைத்தன","வெள்ளநீர்ப் பெருஞ்சனம் வியந்துகை விதிர்த்ததே."],"p":"","r":"When he pressed its striped brow with the tip of his nail, the understanding elephant stood still at once, its two bells rang, and the flood-like crowd shook their hands in wonder.","n":"Line 3's மௌளரும் is not a Tamil word as printed; the bells' sound is clear, the opening word is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1841","lb":"CC1841","ev":"At a touch of his nail the elephant halts and the crowd marvels.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","வளையசுந்தரம்","பெருஞ்சனம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1842":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1842,"t":["என்மன நின்மன மென்றிரண் டில்லையாற்","றன்மனத் துளபொரு டான்றனக் குரைப்பதொத்","துன்மன மென்மன மென்பதொத் திழைத்ததா","னன்மனக் குஞ்சர நம்பியோ டென்மரும்."],"p":"","r":"Some say that the good-minded elephant moved with the prince as if saying 'my mind and yours are not two', like one telling himself what is in his own heart.","n":"The words of union, my mind and yours not two, are the lovers' and friends' language, here given to a rider and his beast; என்மரும் marks it as one view among those who saw.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1842","lb":"CC1842","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["வளையசுந்தரம்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒற்றாட்கள்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:78"},"cc:1843":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1843,"t":["தேவனே மகனலன் செல்வன்மற் றென்மரும்","பாவையே நோற்றனள் பாரின்மே லென்மருங்","கோவனும் மக்களுங் குளிர்ந்துதோ ணோக்கினா","ரோவென வையகத் தோசைபோ யுயர்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Some in the crowd call the son a god in his beauty and good fortune, others say the girl must have done penance to win him, while the king and his sons look on his shoulders with cool delight, and the cry of the world rises high.","n":"நோற்றனள் makes Kanakamalai's good match the fruit of her own austerity; the crowd's two sayings set the hero's worth beside the bride's merit, the usual pair of the Cintamani's wedding praises.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","azhagu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1843","lb":"CC1843","ev":"The city hails Civakan after he subdues the elephant, and the king and his sons gaze at him in delight.","cl":[["a woman wins such a husband by penance done before","general","reported",2,"nagar_makkal"]],"ag":["தடமித்தன்","மக்கள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["என்மரும்",""],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1844":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1844,"t":["பிண்டமுண் ணும்பெருங் களிறுபூட் டியவன்","வண்டரும் மோவரும் பாடமா நகர்தொழக்","கொண்டதன் றம்பியுந் தானுங்கோ யில்புகக்","கண்டனங் கண்ணினே யென்றுகண் டவர்சொனார்"],"p":"","r":"Those who saw it say they saw with their own eyes the man who bound the great elephant that eats balls of rice, with his younger brother, enter the palace while bards and heralds sang and the great city bowed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1844","lb":"CC1844","ev":"Civakan and Nandattan enter the palace to the songs of bards as the city bows.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["என்மரும்",""],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1845":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1845,"t":["பாத்தில்சீர்ப் பதுமுகன் படிவவொற் றாளர்சொற்","கோத்தெனக் கொடுத்தனன் கொழுநிதி யுவகையிற்","றூத்திரட் சுறாவினந் தொக்கபோன் மறவரு","மேத்தருஞ் சிலைகைவா ளிலங்குவே லேந்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"At the word of his spies in disguise Padumukan, of matchless fame, joyfully gave them rich wealth, and his warriors, crowding like a shoal of sharks, took up bows, swords and shining spears.","n":"படிவ ஒற்றாளர் are spies in a borrowed guise; the verse does not say whose. The scene shifts abruptly from the palace to the friends' camp, and the reader learns only from 1863 why armed men are gathering.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","padai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1845","lb":"CC1845","ev":"Padumukan rewards the disguised spies who found Civakan, and his men arm themselves.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்","ஒற்றன்","மறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1846":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1846,"t":["வேனிரை வாண்மதில் பிளந்துவெஞ் சமத்திடைத்","தேனிரை களிற்றின்மேற் றிண்குளம் பழுத்துவ","வானிரை வளைப்பதோர் பொருளெனச் சிரித்துடன்","மாநிரை பண்ணினார் வடித்தநூற் கேள்வியார்."],"p":"","r":"Men learned in refined treatises, whose horses had pressed their hooves on bee-swarmed elephants after breaching walls of spears and swords in fierce battle, laughed that rounding up a herd of cows was hardly a task, and harnessed their horses together.","n":"The laugh is double: a cattle raid is trivial for warriors who have stormed fortresses, and this one is a feint meant only to draw Civakan out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1846","lb":"CC1846","ev":"Padumukan's men laugh at the smallness of a cattle raid and ready their horses.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1847":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1847,"t":["விடையுடை யினநிரை விழுங்கன் மேயினார்","துடியொடு சிறுபறை துவைத்த வால்வளை","முடியுல குறநிமிர்ந் தார்த்த மொய்கழ","லடுபடை யிளையரு மரணம் வீசினார்."],"p":"","r":"They went to swallow up the herds with their bulls; small drums, tudi and white conches sounded, and the young warriors of killing arms, with clustered anklets, raised a din that rose to the sky and flung on their armour.","n":"அரணம் வீசினார் is hard to fix: அரணம் may be armour or a defence, and வீசினார் may mean they put it on or threw it aside.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1847","lb":"CC1847","ev":"Padumukan's young warriors set out to seize the king's herds with drums and conches.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1848":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1848,"t":["காந்தளங் கடிமலர்க் கண்ணி நெற்றிய","ராய்ந்தளந் தியற்றிய வத்து ணாடையார்","வேய்ந்துணி யலமரும் புறத்தார் வெஞ்சுட","ரேந்தெழி னவியமு மேந்து தோளினார்."],"p":"","r":"They wore wreaths of fragrant kantal on their brows, carefully measured garments, quivers of split bamboo swinging on their backs, and bright axes on their shoulders.","n":"Kantal wreaths, bamboo quivers and axes are the gear of hill raiders: the companions come dressed as the hunters of the first cattle raid, so the herdsmen take them for real raiders.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1848","lb":"CC1848","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1849":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1849,"t":["கோனுடை யினநிரை காக்குங் கோவலர்","தேனொடு கடிச்சுரும் பரற்றும் தேமலர்க்","கானிடை யினநிரை காவல் போற்றுமி","னானிடை யழித்தபுள் ளென்று கூறினார்."],"p":"","r":"The cowherds who keep the king's herds said that while they watched over the herd in the forest where bees hum over the honeyed flowers, a bird had spoiled things among the cattle.","n":"ஆனிடை அழித்த புள் probably names an ill-omened bird, as in the herdsmen's warning before the first raid (420), but the line can also be read as a bird of prey among the cattle; whether the cowherds speak before or after the raid is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1849","lb":"CC1849","ev":"The king's cowherds speak of a bird that harmed the herd in the forest.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆயர்",""],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1850":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1850,"t":["விடுபொறி யரவென விளங்கு வெஞ்சிலை","யடுகணை சிதறினா ரார்த்த வால்வளை","கடுகின காலிய லிவுளி காண்டலு","முடுகுபு கோவலர் முந்து காற்பெய்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The raiders scattered killing arrows from bows bright as hooded snakes, the white conches roared, and when the cowherds saw the swift horses charging they ran off at full speed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1850","lb":"CC1850","ev":"The raiders shower arrows and the cowherds flee before the horses.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்","ஆயர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1851":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1851,"t":["அளைச்செறி யிரும்புலி யனைய வாடவர்","வளைத்தனர் மணிநிரை வன்க ணாயரும்","விளைத்தனர் வெருவரத் தக்க வெஞ்சொலா","லுளைத்தனர் பூசல்விட் டுணர்த்த வோடினார்."],"p":"","r":"Men like great tigers in their dens surrounded the fine herd; the hard-eyed herdsmen let out fearful cries, raised the alarm and ran to report it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1851","lb":"CC1851","ev":"The raiders surround the herd and the herdsmen run crying to the city.","cl":[],"ag":["மறவர்","ஆயர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1852":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1852,"t":["தோத்தொகைத் தானை மன்னன் சீவகற் கிளைய நம்பி","வார்த்தொகை முழவம் விம்ம மல்லுறழ் தோளி னானை","நீர்த்தொகைக் கழனி நாடு நெடுநகர்ப் பெயரு நுங்கள்","சீர்த்தொகைக் குலனு மெல்லாந் தெரிந்தெமக் குரைமோ வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of the vast army said to Civakan's younger brother, of wrestler's shoulders, while the drums throbbed: tell us your country of watered fields, the name of your great city and the glory of your line.","n":"The question is the one the whole ilampakam turns on: Civakan has come as a stranger and his birth as Saccandan's son is still hidden from his host.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1852","lb":"CC1852","ev":"Tadamittan asks Nandattan his country, city and lineage.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தடமித்தன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1853":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1853,"t":["திருக்குறிப் பன்ன தாயிற் செப்புவ லடிகள் செம்பொ","னரித்தசும் பொழுகு குன்றத் தருவியின் வெரீஇய மஞ்ஞை","பரித்தவை பழன நாரைப் பார்ப்பொடு மருதிற் சேக்கு","முரைத்தகு நாடு மூருங் குலத்துட னுணர வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Nandattan answers that if it please his lord he will tell the land, town and lineage: a land where peacocks frightened by the cascades of hills that spill red gold sleep on marutam trees beside the young of the paddy-field storks.","n":"The peacock of the hills roosting among the marutam storks sets kurinji and marutam side by side: the land is praised as one where every region meets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1853","lb":"CC1853","ev":"Nandattan begins to describe his homeland to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","தடமித்தன்"],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1854":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1854,"t":["பொருகய லுகளிப் பாயப் பூஞ்சிறைக் குமரி யன்னங்","குருகினோ டிரியச் செந்நெற் கொழுங்கதிர் குவளை யெற்ற","முருகுவிண் டிரியத் தீந்தேன் முழங்குநீர்க் கழனி நன்னா","டெரியுமிழ்ந் திலங்கும் வேலோ யேமமாங் கதம தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says the good land where carp leap, swans go with herons, the paddy ears strike the kuvalai flowers and honey spills into the sounding water of the fields is Emangatham, lord of the flaming spear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1854","lb":"CC1854","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","தடமித்தன்"],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1855":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1855,"t":["பூந்துகிற் கொடுத்த தீந்தே னகிற்புகை பொன்ன னார்தங்","கூந்தலிற் குளித்த வண்டு கொப்புளித் திட்ட வாச","மாந்தர்மேற் றவழ்ந்து மாட மிருள்படப் புதையுஞ் செல்வத்","தேந்துபொன் னிஞ்சி மூதூ ரிராசமா புரம தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says the ancient city of golden walls, whose mansions are darkened by the scented smoke of honey and agil from women's hair, drifting over the people, is Rajamapuram.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1855","lb":"CC1855","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","தடமித்தன்"],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1856":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1856,"t":["எங்குல மடிகள் கேட்க வென்றலு மெழுந்தோர் பூசல்","பொங்குளைப் புரவி வெள்ளம் போக்கற வளைத்து முற்றி","யிங்குள நிரையை யெல்லாங் கவர்ந்ததென் றிட்ட போழ்தே","திங்கள் வெண்குடையி னான்றன் றிருச்செவிக் கிசைத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As he said 'let my lord hear our lineage', an outcry broke out that a flood of horses with tossing manes had surrounded and carried off all the herds here, and it reached the ears of the king of the moon-white umbrella.","n":"The interruption falls at the very word குலம்: the brother names land and city but never the line, so Civakan's birth stays hidden one scene longer, and the friends' raid, staged to find him, is what cuts the telling short.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","veruvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1856","lb":"CC1856","ev":"The news of the raid interrupts Nandattan before he names his lineage, and reaches the king.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","ஆயர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1857":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1857,"t":["எரித்திறல் வென்றி வேந்தற் கிற்றென விசைப்பச் சீறி","மருப்புறக் கந்து பாய்ந்து முழங்குமால் களிறு போலத்","திருக்கிளர் மணிசெய் பொற்றூண் டீப்படப் புடைத்துச் செங்க","ணுருத்தெரி தவழ நோக்கி யுடல்சினங் கடவச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Told of it, the victorious king raged like a bull elephant that tusks its post and trumpets, struck a jewelled golden pillar till sparks flew, and with red eyes blazing and anger driving his body, spoke.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1857","lb":"CC1857","ev":"Tadamittan, enraged by the news, strikes a pillar and speaks.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1858":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1858,"t":["நாற்கடற் பரப்பும் வந்து நன்னகர்க் கண்ணுற் றென்ன","வேற்கடற் றானை பாய்மா விளங்கொளி யிவுளித் திண்டோ","கூற்றென முழங்கு மோடைக் குஞ்சரக் குழாத்தோ டேகிப்","பாற்கடற் பரப்பின் வல்லே படுநிரை பெயர்க்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He orders that, as if the four seas had come into the city, the sea of spears, the horses, the strong chariots and the herds of elephants roaring like death go out at once and drive back the seized herd.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1858","lb":"CC1858","ev":"Tadamittan orders his whole army out to recover the herd.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தடமித்தன்",""],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1859":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1859,"t":["கண்ணகன் கடலங் கோடும் பறைகளு முழங்கி விம்ம","விண்ணகத் தியங்கு மேகக் குழாமென நிரைத்த வேழந்","திண்ணுகப் புரவித் திண்டோ விரைந்தன நிரந்த பாய்மா","மண்ணக மலிரக் காலாட் கடல்கிளர்ந் தெழுந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Drums and horns sounded over the broad moat, elephants ranged like herds of cloud in the sky, chariots with strong yokes and horses sped, and the foot soldiers rose like a sea till the earth shook.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1859","lb":"CC1859","ev":"The king's army marches out.","cl":[],"ag":["படை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1860":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1860,"t":["பானிறக் கவரி நெற்றிப் பசுங்கிளி நிறத்த பாய்மாத்","தானுறப் பண்ணித் திண்டோத் தம்பிகோல் கொள்ள வேறிக்","கூனிறக் குழவித் திங்கட் குளிர்கதி ரார மார்பிற்","றேனிறங் கொண்ட கண்ணிச் சீவக குமரன் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Having his horses, green as parrots with milk-white plumes on their brows, yoked to the strong chariot, with his younger brother taking the reins, Civaka Kumaran, his chest hung with a pearl chain cool as the crescent moon, mounted and spoke.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1860","lb":"CC1860","ev":"Civakan mounts his chariot with Nandattan driving and speaks.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1861":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1861,"t":["மன்னவ னிரைகொண் டாரை வளநகர்த் தந்து மன்னன்","பொன்னவிர் கழலிற் றங்கள் புனைமுடி யிடுவி யேனே","லின்னிசை யுலகந் தன்னு ளென்பெயர் சேற லின்றாய்க்","கன்னிய மகளிர் நெஞ்சிற் காமம்போற் கரக்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan vows that if he does not bring those who seized the king's herd to the city and make them lay their crowns at the king's golden anklets, let his name not go out in the world of sweet song but lie hidden like desire in the hearts of maidens.","n":"The pledge is a self-curse on his fame, and its simile is love's: a name hidden like a maiden's unspoken longing. He is staking his reputation for a host king who does not yet know who he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","pugazh","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1861","lb":"CC1861","ev":"Civakan vows to bring the raiders captive to Tadamittan.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1862":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1862,"t":["பார்மலி பரவைத் தானைப் பரப்பிடைப் பறப்ப தேபோ","னீர்மலி கடாத்த கொண்மூ நெற்றிமேன் மின்னி னொய்தாத்","தார்மலி மார்பன் றிண்டோ தோன்றலுந் தறுகண் மைந்தன்","சீர்மலி பகழி யேந்திப் பதுமுகன் சிலைதொட் டானே."],"p":"","r":"As the garlanded hero's strong chariot appeared in the midst of the army that spread over the earth, flying like lightning across a cloud heavy with rain, the bold youth Padumukan took up a fine arrow and set it to his bow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1862","lb":"CC1862","ev":"Padumukan sees Civakan's chariot and fits an arrow to his bow.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1863":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1863,"t":["குடைநிழற் கொற்ற வேந்த னொருமகற் காணக் குன்றா","வடிநிழ லுறைய வந்தே மடியம்யா மென்ன வெய்த","விடுகணை சென்று தோமேற் பின்முனா வீழ்த லோடுந்","தொடுகழற் குருசி னோக்கித் தூத்துகில் வீசி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Padumukan's arrow, sent as if saying 'we your servants have come to see the only son of the victorious king of the shading umbrella and to dwell in the shade of his unfailing feet', flew and fell on the chariot, and the anklet-wearing prince looked at it and waved a white cloth.","n":"என்ன leaves open whether the arrow bore a written message or only meant this; பின்முனா வீழ்தல் may say it fell butt first, a sign it was not shot to harm. The greeting names Civakan as the only son of a crowned king, the lineage his brother had just failed to tell.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","adimai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1863","lb":"CC1863","ev":"Padumukan's arrow carries the companions' greeting to Civakan, who answers by waving a white cloth.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1864":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1864,"t":["ஏந்தலைத் தோழ ரெல்லாமிணையடி தொழுது வீழச்","சேந்தன கண்ணி னாலுந் திண்ணெழிற் றோளி னாலும்","வாய்ந்தவின் சொல்லி னாலு மாலைதாழ் முடியி னாலு","மாய்ந்தவன் சிறப்புச் செய்தா னவலநோ யவருந் தீர்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"All the companions fell worshipping at the hero's feet, and with his reddened eyes, strong shoulders, fitting sweet words and garlanded crown he honoured them, and their grief was ended.","n":"The four instruments of honour, eyes, shoulders, words and crown, mean a look, an embrace, a greeting and a bow of the head; அவலநோய் names the grief that had made them plan revenge and death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","panivu","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1864","lb":"CC1864","ev":"The companions fall at Civakan's feet, he honours them, and their grief ends.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1865":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1865,"t":["கழலவாய்க் கிடந்த நோன்றாட் காளைதன் காத லாரை","நிழலவா யிறைஞ்சி நீங்கா நெடுங்களிற் றெருத்த மேற்றி","யழலவாய்க் கிடந்த வைவே லரசிளங் குமரர் சூழக்","குழலவாய்க் கிடந்த கோதை தாதையூர் கொண்டு புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The young bull of strong anklet-bound legs set his dear friends, who bowed in his shade, on the necks of tall elephants, and with the young princes of fiery spears around him entered the city of the garlanded lady's father.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1865","lb":"CC1865","ev":"Civakan brings his companions on elephants into Kanakamalai's father's city.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்","மக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:79"},"cc:1866":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1866,"t":["வானக்கி நின்று நுடங்குங்கொடி மாட மூதூர்ப்","பானக்க தீஞ்சொற் பவளம்புரை பாவை யன்ன","மானக்க நோக்கின் மாடவார்தொழ மைந்த ரேத்த","யானைக்கு ழாத்தி னிழிந்தாரரி மானொ டொப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"In the old city of mansions whose flags wave in the sky, the companions, like lions come down from a herd of elephants, dismount while the women of the mansions, with sweet speech and doe-like eyes, worship them and the young men praise them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1866","lb":"CC1866","ev":"Civakan's companions dismount from their elephants in Kanakamalai's city amid the townspeople's welcome.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1867":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1867,"t":["செம்பொற் புளகத் திளஞாயிறு செற்ற கோயில்","வம்பிற் றுளும்பு முலைவாணெடுங் கண்மா டவார்","நம்பப் புகுந்து நரதேவ னருளி னெய்திப்","பைம்பொற் புறகக் களற்றானடி தாம்ப ணிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Watched with longing by the long-eyed women, they enter the palace whose red gold outshines the young sun, come in by the king's grace, and bow at the feet of the gold-anklet king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1867","lb":"CC1867","ev":"The companions enter the palace and bow at the king's feet.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","தடமித்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1868":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1868,"t":["வல்லான் புனைந்த வயிரக்குழை வார்ந்து வான்பொற்","பல்பூ ணெருத்திற் பரந்தஞ்சுடர் கால மன்னன்","மல்லார் திரடோண் மருமான்முக நோக்க மைந்த","ரெல்லா மடிக ளெனக்கின்னுயிர்த் தோழ ரென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As the king, his broad chest bright with his jewels, looks at his son-in-law's face, Civakan tells him that all these young men are his friends, dear as his own life.","n":"Civakan addresses his father-in-law as அடிகள், and the king is seen looking at him as மருமான், his son-in-law: the scene places the companions inside a family before it places them in a court.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1868","lb":"CC1868","ev":"Civakan presents the companions to the king as his dearest friends.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தடமித்தன்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1869":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1869,"t":["வார்பொன் முடிமேல் வயிரம்முழச் சேந்த செல்வத்","தார்பொன் னடிசூழ் மணியங்கழ லானை வேந்தன்","கார்மின் னுடங்கு மிடைமங்கையைக் காண்க சென்றென்","றோமின்னு தாரா னருளத்தொழு தேகி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The king of the diamond crown and jewelled anklets graciously bids them go and see the lady whose waist flickers like lightning in a cloud, and they bow and go.","n":"Line 4 opens ஓமின்னு தாரான், which the stanza join leaves hard to divide; the sense that the king bids them go is clear from காண்க சென்று என்று.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1869","lb":"CC1869","ev":"The king sends the companions to meet Kanakamalai.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1870":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1870,"t":["தழுமுற்றும் வாராத் திரடாமங்க டாழ்ந்த கோயின்","முழுமுற்றுந் தானே விளக்காய்மிணிக் கொம்பி னின்றா","ளெழுமுற்றுந் தோளார் தொழுதாரின்ன ரென்று நோக்கக்","கழுமிற்றுக் காதல் கதிர்வெள்வளைத் தோளி னாட்கே."],"p":"","r":"In the palace hung with garlands she stands like a lamp that lights the whole house by itself, a jewelled creeper; the companions bow to her, she looks at them knowing who they are, and affection wells up in the bright-bangled lady.","n":"The opening word தழுமுற்றும் is obscure as printed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1870","lb":"CC1870","ev":"Kanakamalai receives the companions, who bow to her.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1871":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1871,"t":["துறக்கம் மிதுவே யெனுந்தொன்னகர் மன்னன் மங்கை","தொறுக்கொண்ட கள்வ ரிவரோவெனச் சொல்லி நக்காங்","கொறுக்கப் படுவா ரிவரென்றங் கசதி யாடி","வெறுக்கைக் கிழவன் மகளென்ன விருந்து செய்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The princess of the old city they call heaven itself laughs, 'Are these the thieves who drove off the herd? These are the men to be punished,' and having teased them, feasts them as lavishly as a rich man's daughter.","n":"The joke has a ground: the companions had just seized the king's herd to draw Civakan out to battle (1845-1863), so the 'thieves' she would punish are her husband's friends, now her guests. The phrase வெறுக்கைக் கிழவன் மகள் may also glance at Civakan's upbringing in a merchant's house.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1871","lb":"CC1871","ev":"Kanakamalai teases the companions as the cattle thieves and then feasts them.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கனகமாலை","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1872":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1872,"t":["அருந்தீத் தொழிலே புரிந்தான்மறை யாய வெல்லாம்","விருந்தா விரிப்பா னவன்சீவக சாமி வேறா","விருந்தாற்கொ ரோலை கொடுத்தானெரி குண்ட லத்தாற்","பொருந்தார் பொறியைப் புறநீக்குபு நோக்கு கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The companion who keeps only the hard fire-rites and expounds all the Vedas as a feast gives a palm-leaf letter to Civaka Sami, who sits apart, and Civakan, removing the seal pressed by the bright earring, looks into it.","n":"The letter-bearer is named only by his rites; Buddhisenan is the companion called நான்மறையினான் in 1829, so the identification rests on the story. எரி குண்டலத்தால் may name the earring that made the seal (1768) or the earrings Civakan wears.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1872","lb":"CC1872","ev":"A brahmin companion hands Civakan Gandharvadattai's sealed letter and he breaks the seal.","cl":[],"ag":["புத்திசேனன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1873":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1873,"t":["மற்றடிகள் கண்டருளிச் செய்க மலரடிக்கீழ்ச்","சிற்றடிச்சி தத்தையடி வீழ்ச்சி திருவடிகட்","குற்றடிசின் மஞ்சனத்தை யுள்ளுறுத்த காப்பும்","பொற்புடைய வாகவெனப் போற்றி யடிவீழ்ந்தேன்."],"p":"","r":"The letter opens: let the lord see and grant it; under his flower feet his little servant Tattai falls at his feet, and she prays that the protections she has set within for his feet, his food and his bath may keep him well, and falls at his feet again.","n":"Three times in four lines the Vidyadhara princess falls at his feet (அடிவீழ்ச்சி, அடிவீழ்ந்தேன்): the letter's courtesy is a wife's self-abasement written out, which makes the sulking at its end sharper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","panivu","vinjai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1873","lb":"CC1873","ev":"Gandharvadattai's letter opens with her obeisance and the protective charms she sends.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1874":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1874,"t":["வயிரமணிக் கலன்கமழுங் கற்பகநன் மாலை","யுயிரைமதஞ் செய்யுமதுத் தண்டொடுடை யாடை","செயிரினறுஞ் சாந்துசிலை யம்புமணி யயில்வாண்","மயிரெலியின் போர்வையொடெம் மன்னன்விடுத்தானே."],"p":"","r":"She lists what her king has sent: diamond and jewelled ornaments, a fragrant garland of the wish-granting tree, a heady drink, a staff and robes, flawless sandal paste, bow and arrows, a jewelled lance and a sword, and a cloak of the hair-rat's fur.","n":"The gifts mix a bridegroom's finery with arms; மயிர் எலியின் போர்வை is the fabled cloak of a rat's hair, a Vidyadhara wonder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1874","lb":"CC1874","ev":"Gandharvadattai's letter lists the gifts her father the Vidyadhara king sends Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கலுழவேகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1875":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1875,"t":["வந்தவனை யாருமறி யாமன்மறை யாகத்","தந்துதரன் கேட்பவிது சாமிவலித் தானா","வைந்துமதி யெல்லையினை யாண்டுடைய னாகி","யந்திலகன் றான்றமரொ டாங்கணெனச் சொன்னேன்."],"p":"","r":"She writes that when the one who came was brought in secret so that no one knew, and Taran asked, she told him that this was the lord's own resolve: that the jewel of men was there with his kin and would stay there to the term of five months.","n":"The syntax of lines 1-2 (வந்தவனை ... தந்து) is hard to fix, and who 'the one who came' is, Nandattan or Taran, is not certain; the sense that she answered Taran's question with a story of a planned absence is carried on by 1876.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karavu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1875","lb":"CC1875","ev":"Gandharvadattai tells Civakan she gave Taran a covering account of his absence.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","தரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1876":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1876,"t":["பட்டபழி வெள்ளிமலை மேற்பரத்த லஞ்சித்","தொட்டுவிடுத் தேனவனைத் தூதுபிற சொல்லிப்","பட்டபழி காத்துப்புக ழேபரப்பி னல்லால்","விட்டலர்ந்த கோதையல ரால்விளைவ துண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"Fearing that the blame he had fallen into would spread over the Silver Mountain, she sent Taran off with another message; for what can come from a woman with a blossoming garland except to guard against blame that has fallen and spread fame?","n":"The duty she names is served by a falsehood: what she told Taran (1875) was not what had happened, and she says so to her husband without apology. The blame (பழி) is the disgrace of a Vidyadhara's son-in-law vanishing after his arrest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","pugazh","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1876","lb":"CC1876","ev":"Gandharvadattai sends Taran away with a false message to keep Civakan's disappearance from her father's people.","cl":[["a wife's part is to guard her husband from the blame that has befallen him and spread his fame","general","reported",3,"gandharvadattai"]],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","தரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1877":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1877,"t":["அல்லதுவு மெங்கைகுண மாலையவ ளாற்றாள்","செல்லுமதி நோக்கிப்பக லேசிறியை யென்னும்","பல்கதிரை நோக்கிமதி யேபெரியை யென்னு","மெல்லியிது காலையிது வென்பதறி கல்லாள்."],"p":"","r":"Besides, she writes, her younger sister Gunamalai cannot bear it: seeing the moon move she says 'day, you are short', looking at the many-rayed sun she says 'moon, you are long'; the delicate one cannot tell whether it is morning or not.","n":"Gandharvadattai calls her co-wife எங்கை, my younger sister, and pleads her case to their husband: the co-wives are allied in his absence. The confusion of sun and moon is the lovesick woman's loss of the day's order.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1877","lb":"CC1877","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1878":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1878,"t":["அரவுவெகுண் டன்னவக லல்குனிலம் புல்லித்","திருவில்வளைந் தனையதிரு மேகலையி னீங்கிப்","புருவமதி முகமும்புகழ் தோளும்புணர் முலையு","முருவமழிந் தடிச்சியுள் ளாங்கொலுணர் கலனே."],"p":"","r":"Her wide mound like a cobra's hood lies on the ground, the bright girdle like a rainbow has slipped from her, and brow, moon-face, praised shoulders and close-set breasts have lost their form; your servant cannot tell whether she still lives.","n":"அடிச்சி may be Gandharvadattai speaking of herself (your servant does not know) or the subject of the doubt (whether your servant Gunamalai lives); both read.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1878","lb":"CC1878","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1879":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1879,"t":["நாளைவரு நையலென நன்றெனவி ரும்பி","நாளையெனு நாளணிமைத் தோபெரிதுஞ் சேய்த்தோ","நாளையுரை யென்றுகிளி யோடுநகச் சொல்லு","நாளினுமிந் நங்கைதுயர் நாளினுமற் றிதுவே."],"p":"","r":"Told 'he comes tomorrow, do not grieve', she is glad, and laughing she says to her parrot, 'Is the day called tomorrow near, or very far? Tell me when tomorrow is'; every day of this lady's grief is such a day.","n":"The four lines each begin with நாள், and the promise of 'tomorrow' becomes a riddle the girl puts to her parrot, so the comfort itself measures how long the waiting is. Line 4 is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1879","lb":"CC1879","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1880":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1880,"t":["நோக்கவே தளிர்த்து நோக்கா திமைப்பினு நுணுகு நல்லார்","பூக்கம ழமளிச் சேக்கும் புதுமண வாள னார்தா","நீப்பிலார் நெஞ்சி னுள்ளா ராதலா னினைத்தல் செய்யேன்","போக்குவல் பொழுதுந் தாந்தம் பொன்னடி போற்றி யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Of the women who flourish when he looks at them and waste if he does not look even for a blink, she says: the new bridegroom who lies on flower-scented beds dwells in the hearts of those who never leave him, so she will not need to remember him, and will pass her days praising his golden feet.","n":"புதுமணவாளனார், 'the new bridegroom', is the reproach: he has married again in this city. The pledge of worship is the sulk (புலவி, 1881) in its politest form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1880","lb":"CC1880","ev":"Gandharvadattai closes her letter with a sulking pledge of devotion.","cl":[["a faithful wife keeps her husband in her heart through his absence and spends her days worshipping his feet","general","reported",3,"gandharvadattai"]],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1881":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1881,"t":["இலவம்பூ வாக்குண் டன்ன பஞ்சிமெல் லடியி னாடன்","புலவிச்சொற் பொறித்த வோலை திருமுடி துளக்கி நோக்கித்","தலைவைத்த காப்பு விஞ்சை கொண்டபின் றாமஞ் சூழ்ந்து","கொலைவைத்த குருதி வேலான் றோழரைக் குறுகி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Having read with a shake of his crowned head the letter in which the lady of feet soft as silk-cotton had inscribed her sulking words, and having taken the protective spell set for his head, the garlanded hero of the bloodied spear goes over to his companions.","n":"The verse names the letter's tone itself, புலவிச்சொல்: the narrator reads the courteous letter as a lover's sulk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","vinjai","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1881","lb":"CC1881","ev":"Civakan reads the letter, takes up the protective spell it carries and goes to his companions.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1882":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1882,"t":["எங்கோமற் றென்றிறநீர் கேட்டதென் றாற்கெரி மணிப்பூட்","செங்கோன் மணிநெடுந்தோச் செல்வன் காதற் பெருந்தேவி","தங்காத் தவவுரு வந்தாங்கித் தண்டா ரணியத்து","ளங்காத் திருந்தாளைத் தலைப்பட் டைய வறிந்தோமே."],"p":"","r":"When he asks where they heard of his affairs, they tell him that they met the beloved great queen of the king of the just sceptre, who has taken the form of unceasing penance and lives guarded in the Dandaka forest, and learned it from her.","n":"They name Vijayai only as the queen of a king of செங்கோல், and the dead Saccandan is still given the just sceptre that was taken from him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1882","lb":"CC1882","ev":"The companions tell Civakan they found his mother living as an ascetic in the Dandaka forest.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1883":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1883,"t":["என்னேமற் றென்னேநீர் மொழிந்ததென் னேயென விரும்பி","முன்னேமொ ழிந்தாற்போன் முறைநின் றெல்லா முடன்மொழிய","மன்னாஞ் சிந்துவபோன் மலர்ந்த செந்தா மரைக்கண்ணீர்","பொன்னார மார்பின்மேற் பொழியப் புன்க ணுற்றானே."],"p":"","r":"Eagerly asking 'What? What is it you said?', he hears them tell it all again in order as before, and tears from his lotus eyes pour over his golden chest like scattered gems, and he grieves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anbu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1883","lb":"CC1883","ev":"Civakan, hearing of his mother, weeps.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1884":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1884,"t":["அஃதே யடிகளும் முளரோ வென்றாற் கருளுமா","றிஃதா விருந்தவா றென்றார்க் கென்னைப் பெறவல்லார்க்","கெய்தா விடருளவே யெங்கெங் கென்றத் திசைநோக்கி","வெய்தா வடிதொழுது வேந்தன் கோயிற் கெழுந்தானே."],"p":"","r":"He asks whether his lady mother is then alive; told this is how she has lived, he cries that to those who bore him there is no sorrow that does not come, asks where, where she is, turns to face that way, worships her feet in anguish and goes to the king's palace.","n":"என்னைப் பெறவல்லார்க்கு எய்தா இடருளவே: he counts his mother's sufferings as the cost of having borne him, taking the blame on himself rather than on her fate or her enemies.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","sutram","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1884","lb":"CC1884","ev":"Civakan learns his mother lives, worships toward her and goes to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1885":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1885,"t":["இலைவிரவு பூம்பைந்தார் வேந்த னேந்தல் குலங்கேட்பான்","மலைவிரவு நீண்மார்பின் மைந்தன் றோழர் முகநோக்கிக்","கொலைவிரவு கூர்நுதி வேற்குமர னென்னக் குருகுலத்தான்","கலைவிரவு தீஞ்சொல் லார்காம னென்றார் கமழ் தாரார்."],"p":"","r":"The king, wanting to know the hero's clan, looks to the young man's friends, and the garlanded men answer that the prince of the murderous spear is of the Kuru clan, a Kama to the sweet-voiced women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1885","lb":"CC1885","ev":"The companions tell the king that Civakan is of the Kuru clan.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்","தடமித்தன்"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1886":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1886,"t":["அண்ணல் குருகுலத்தா னென்றால் யான்முன் கருதியதென்","ணெண்ணம் வெளிப்பட்டான் கரந்த மைந்த னெரிசெம்பொன்","வண்ண வரைமார்ப முயங்கி நுண்ணூன் மதியாரோ","டெண்ணி வியநெறியால் விடுத்தான் கோயில் புக்கானே."],"p":"","r":"Saying that if the lord is of the Kuru clan then what he had guessed before is so, and that the young man who hid it has come out, the king embraces his golden chest, takes counsel with ministers of subtle learning, sends him on by the proper way, and enters his palace.","n":"Lines 1-2 are the king's words without a verb of speech, and விடுத்தான் may mean he dismissed the companions or sent Civakan on; the run of the story favours Civakan's leave-taking.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","amaichu","karavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1886","lb":"CC1886","ev":"The king, his guess at Civakan's birth confirmed, embraces him, consults his ministers and lets him go.","cl":[],"ag":["தடமித்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1887":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1887,"t":["விள்ளா வியனெடுந்தோ வேந்தன் காதன் மடமகளே","கள்ளாவி கொப்புளிக்குங் கமழ்பூங் கோதா யென்மனத்தி","னுள்ளாவி யுள்ளாய்நீ யொழிந்தா யல்லையெனக் கையிற்","புள்ளாவிச் செங்கழுநீர்க் குவளை செய்தாள் புனைபூணாள்."],"p":"","r":"'Beloved young daughter of the king of broad unyielding shoulders, lady of fragrant flowers that bubble with honey, you are the life within my heart; you are not left behind,' he says, and the jewelled lady ... with the red water-lily in her hand.","n":"Line 4, புள்ளாவிச் செங்கழுநீர்க் குவளை செய்தாள், is not secure: it may say that her hot breath withered the red lily in her hand, a sign of grief at parting, but the reading cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1887","lb":"CC1887","ev":"Civakan takes leave of Kanakamalai, telling her she is the life in his heart.","cl":[],"ag":["கனகமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","கனகமாலை"],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1888":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1888,"t":["வார்முயங்கு மென்முலைய வளைவேய்த் தோளாண் மனமகிழ","நீர்முயங்கு கண்குளிர்ப்பப் புல்லி நீடோ ளவனீங்கித்","தோமுயங்கு தானையான் சிறுவர் சேடா ரகன்மார்பந்","தார்முயங்கிக் கூந்தன்மா விவர்ந்தான் சங்க முரன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Embracing her so that the lady of bangled bamboo-like arms is glad at heart and her tearful eyes are cooled, the long-armed one leaves her, embraces the broad chests of the king's sons, mounts his horse, and the conches sound.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1888","lb":"CC1888","ev":"Civakan embraces Kanakamalai and her brothers and rides out to the sound of conches.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கனகமாலை","மக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:80"},"cc:1889":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1889,"t":["முருகுகொப் புளிக்குங் கண்ணி முறிமிடை படலை மாலைக்","குருதிகொப் புளிக்கும் வேலான் கூந்தன்மா விவர்ந்து செல்ல","வுருவவெஞ் சிலையி னாற்குத் தம்பியிஃ துரைக்கு மொண்பொற்","பருகுபைங் கழலி னாருட் பதுமுகன் கேட்க வென்றே."],"p":"","r":"As Civakan, his spear dripping blood and his garland woven of fragrant flowers and tender leaves, rides off on the maned horse, his younger brother speaks to him so that Padumukan among the gold-anklet warriors may hear.","n":"The bowman (சிலையினான்) is Civakan and the speaker is his தம்பி, Nandattan, who came to him in the last ilampakam; the advice of the next verses is meant for Padumukan, who will carry it out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1889","lb":"CC1889","ev":"Civakan rides out from Kanakamalai's city and his younger brother begins to speak to him in Padumukan's hearing.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1890":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1890,"t":["விழுமணி மாசு மூழ்கிக் கிடந்ததிவ் வுலகம் விற்பக்","கழுவினீர் பொதிந்து சிக்கக் கதிரொளி மறையக் காப்பிற்","றழுவினீ ருலக மெல்லாந் தாமரை யுறையுஞ் செய்யாள்","வழுவினார் தம்மைப் புல்லாள்ட் வாழ்கநுங் கண்ணி மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"The brother blesses Civakan: this world lies like a fine gem sunk in grime, waiting to be washed and to shine again, and the lady of the lotus does not embrace those who slip, so long may his garland flourish.","n":"The gem in grime is most likely the kingdom under the usurper, which Civakan must wash clean; lines 1 and 2 are hard to construe, and line 4 has a stray letter (புல்லாள்ட்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1890","lb":"CC1890","ev":"","cl":[["fortune does not embrace those who fail in their conduct","general","asserted",4,"nandattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1891":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1891,"t":["தொழுததங் கையி னுள்ளுந் துறுமுடி யகத்துஞ் சோர","வழுதகண் ணீரி னுள்ளு மணிகலத் தகத்து மாய்ந்து","பழுதுகண் ணரிந்து கொல்லும் படையுட னெடுங்கும் பற்றா","தொழிகயார் கண்ணுந் தேற்றந் தெளிகுற்றார் விளிகுற் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"He warns that weapons lie hidden in hands joined in worship, in a crown of hair, in weeping tears and in jewelled vessels, and kill when a fault is found, so trust no one's eyes: those who trusted have died.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 take up the wording of Kural 828 (தொழுதகை யுள்ளும் ... அழுதகண் ணீரும்) with words inserted between (தொழுத தம் கையினுள்ளும், அழுத கண்ணீரினுள்ளும்), so it is not marked as an echo under the four-word rule, but the debt is plain. The epic extends the couplet's list to hair and jewel caskets, and closes on a judgment the couplet leaves unsaid: those who trusted are dead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","thelivu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1891","lb":"CC1891","ev":"","cl":[["no outward sign, not even reverence or tears, should be trusted, for those who trusted have perished","general","asserted",4,"nandattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1892":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1892,"t":["தோய்தகை மகளிர்த் தோயின் மெய்யணி நீக்கித் தூய்நீ","ராய்முது மகளிர் தம்மா லரிறபத் திமிரி யாட்டி","வேய்நிறத் தோளி னார்க்கு வெண்டுகின் மாலை சாந்தந்","தானல கலங்கள் சேர்த்தித் தடமுலை தோய்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He orders that any women who are to touch the prince first be stripped of their ornaments, bathed in pure water and rubbed down by old women, then given white silk, garlands, sandal and fresh good ornaments, and only then let them embrace him.","n":"The danger is poison carried on a woman's body or jewels; line 2 (அரிறபத் திமிரி) reads corruptly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1892","lb":"CC1892","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1893":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1893,"t":["வண்ணப்பூ மாலை சாந்தம் வாலணி கலன்க ளாடை","கண்முகத் துறுத்தித் தூய்மை கண்டலாற் கொள்ள வேண்டா","வண்ணலம் புள்ளோ டல்லா வாயிரம் பேடைச் சேவ","லுண்ணுநீ ரமிழ்தங் காக்க யூகமோ டாய்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Garlands, sandal, ornaments and clothes are not to be taken until their purity has been seen with the eyes, and the water and food he takes are to be guarded and tested with birds, the noble swan and the cock with its many hens.","n":"Line 3 and the close of line 4 (யூகமோ டாய்க) are hard to fix: the birds seem to be poison-testers, and யூகம் may be judgment or a name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","aaraaydhal","thooimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1893","lb":"CC1893","ev":"","cl":[["nothing offered should be accepted until its purity has been examined","general","asserted",2,"nandattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1894":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1894,"t":["அஞ்சனக் கோலி னாற்றா நாகமோ ரருவிக் குன்றிற்","குஞ்சாம் புலம்பி வீழக் கூர்நுதி யெயிற்றிற் கொல்லும்","பஞ்சியின் மெல்லி தேனும் பகைசிறி தென்ன வேண்டா","வஞ்சித்தற் காத்தல் வேண்டு மரும்பொரு ளாக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As a snake no thicker than a collyrium stick, on a hill of waterfalls, kills an elephant with its sharp fang so that it falls bellowing, so no enemy should be called small, even one softer than cotton; one must guard against deceit as against the loss of a precious thing.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1894","lb":"CC1894","ev":"","cl":[["no enemy is small enough to be ignored; deceit must be guarded against","general","asserted",3,"nandattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1895":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1895,"t":["பொருந்தலாற் பல்லி போன்றும் போற்றலாற் றாய ரொத்து","மருந்தவர் போன்று காத்து மடங்கலா லாமை போன்றுந்","திருந்துவேற் றெவ்வர் போலத் தீதற வெறிந்து மின்ப","மருந்தினான் மனைவி யொத்து மதலையைக் காமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He bids them guard the one in their charge like a lizard in clinging close, like mothers in care, like great ascetics, like a tortoise in drawing in, like spear-bearing foes in cutting away harm, and like a wife in giving him delight.","n":"மதலை may mean the child (here Civakan, cherished like a son) or the prop of the house; the sense of the ascetics' simile (மருந்தவர்) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","anbu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1895","lb":"CC1895","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1896":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1896,"t":["பூந்துகின் மாலை சாந்தம் புனைகலம் பஞ்ச வாச","மாய்ந்தளந் தியற்றப் பட்ட படிசினீ ரின்ன வெல்லா","மாந்தரின் மடங்க லாற்றற் பதுமுகன் காக்க வென்றாங்","கேந்துபூண் மார்ப னேவ வின்னண மியற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Clothes, garlands, sandal, ornaments, the five scents and the measured prepared water, all these Padumukan, a lion among men, is to guard; so the broad-chested one commanded, and Padumukan did as told.","n":"The one who commands (ஏந்துபூண் மார்பன்) is taken to be the brother who has been speaking since 1889, but the epithet could fit Civakan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thunai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1896","lb":"CC1896","ev":"Nandattan puts Padumukan in charge of everything that touches Civakan, and Padumukan carries out the order.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1897":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1897,"t":["சிறுகண்யா னையினினஞ் சோந்துசே வகங்கொளத்","துறுகலென் றுணர்கலாத் துள்ளிமந் திம்மக","நறியசந் தின்றுணி நாறவெந் தனகள்கொண்","டெறியவெள் கிம்மயிர்க் கவரிமா விரியுமே."],"p":"","r":"On the way, a herd of small-eyed elephants tires, monkeys leap about not knowing a rock from what they take, and a yak, struck by fragrant sandal pieces, shrinks and spreads its hair.","n":"The mountain description opens; the order of who throws what at whom is hard to fix from the joined words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1897","lb":"CC1897","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1898":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1898,"t":["புகழ்வரைச் சென்னிமேற் பூசையிற் பெரியன","பவழமே யனையன பன்மயிர்ப் பேரெலி","யகழுமிங் குலிகமஞ் சனவரைச் சொரிவன","கவழயா னையினுதற் றவழுங்கச் சொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"On the famed hilltop, many-haired rats bigger than cats and red as coral dig up vermilion and pour it on the collyrium-dark hill, like the face-cloth on the brow of an elephant.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1898","lb":"CC1898","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1899":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1899,"t":["அண்ணலங் குன்றின்மேல் வருடைபாய்ந் துழக்கலி","னொண்மணி பலவுடைந் தொருங்கவை தூளியாய்","விண்ணுளு வுண்டென வீழுமா நிலமிசைக்","கண்ணகன் மரமெலாங் கற்பக மொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Where mountain goats leap on the peak, bright gems break and fall as dust, and every broad tree beneath looks like a wishing tree of heaven.","n":"Line 3 (விண்ணுளு வுண்டென) is doubtful; it may compare the falling dust to something in the sky.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1899","lb":"CC1899","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1900":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1900,"t":["மானிடம் பழுத்தன கிலுத்தமற் றவற்றியற்","பான்முரட் பயம்பிடைப் பனைமடிந் தனையன","கானிடைப் பாந்தள்கண் படுப்பன துயிலெழ","வூனுடைப் பொன்முழை யாளிநின் றுலம்புமே."],"p":"","r":"Trees ripen near the deer, pythons lie sleeping in the forest like fallen palms, and when they wake the lion in its gold cave roars.","n":"Line 1 (கிலுத்தமற் றவற்றியற்) is hard to divide and probably corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1900","lb":"CC1900","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1901":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1901,"t":["சாரலந் திமிசிடைச் சந்தனத் தழைவயி","னீரதீம் பூமரம் நிரந்ததக் கோலமு","மேரில வங்கமும் மின்கருப் பூரமும்","மோருநா விகலந் தோசனை கமழுமே."],"p":"","r":"On the slope, among timisu and sandal, the sweet-scented trees, takkolam, clove, camphor and musk spread their fragrance for a yojana.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1901","lb":"CC1901","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1902":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1902,"t":["மைந்தரைப் பார்ப்பன மாமகண் மாக்குழாஞ்","சந்தன மேய்வன தவழ்மதக் களிற்றின","மந்தழைக் காடெலாந் திளைப்பவா மானினஞ்","சிந்தவால் வெடிப்பன சிங்கமெங் கும்முள."],"p":"","r":"Herds of large-eyed beasts stare at the young men, elephants in musth graze the sandal, deer scatter through the leafy woods and lions with lashing tails are everywhere.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1902","lb":"CC1902","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1903":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1903,"t":["வருக்கையின் கனிதொறும் வானரம் பாய்ந்துராய்ப்","பொருப்பெலாம் பொன்கிடந் தொழுகிமேற் றிருவில்வீழ்ந்","தொருக்குலாய் நிலமிசை மிளிர்வதொத் தொளிர்மணி","திருக்கிள ரொளிகுலாய் வானகஞ் செகுக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Monkeys leap from jackfruit to jackfruit, gold flows down the hills like a rainbow fallen to earth, and the glow of gems climbs and splits the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1903","lb":"CC1903","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1904":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1904,"t":["வீழ்பனிப் பாறைக ணெறியெலாம் வெவ்வெயிற்","போழ்தலின் வெண்ணெய்போற் பொழிந்துமட் டொழுகுவ","தாழ்முகில் சூழ்பொழிற் சந்தனக் காற்றசைந்","தாழ்துயர் செய்யுமவ் வருவரைச் சாரலே."],"p":"","r":"On that hard mountain slope the snow-rocks melt in the hot sun and flow like butter and honey, and the sandal wind through cloud-wrapped groves brings deep sorrow.","n":"The sorrow the sandal breeze brings is the separated lover's, a stock pain set into a landscape the hero crosses to reach his mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1904","lb":"CC1904","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1905":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1905,"t":["கூகையுங் கோட்டமுங் குங்கும மும்பரந்","தேகலா காநிலத் தல்கிவிட் டெழுந்துபோய்த்","தோகையு மன்னமுந் தொக்குட னார்ப்பதோர்","நாகநன் காவினு ணயந்துவிட் டார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Through owls, costus and saffron spread over the hard land, the party lodges, rises and goes on, and comes with delight to a fine grove of naga trees where peacocks and swans cry together.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1905","lb":"CC1905","ev":"Civakan's party crosses the hills and halts in a grove of naga trees.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1906":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1906,"t":["பூத்தகி றவழும் போர்வை பூசுசாந் தாற்றி பொன்னூற்","கோத்துநீர் பிலிற்றுங் காந்தங் குங்கும வைரப் பொற்கோய்","சாத்துறி பவழக் கன்னல் சந்தன வால வட்ட","நீத்தவ ரிடத்து நாற்றி நிழன்மணி யுலகஞ் செய்தார்."],"p":"","r":"There they spread cloth and covering, set out sandal, a gold thread-hung moonstone that drips water, saffron and diamond-set gold vessels, coral sugar, sandal fans, and in the place of the renouncers made a world of shining gems.","n":"நீத்தவர் இடத்து, 'in the place of those who have renounced', sets the luxury beside the hermitage where Civakan's mother lives as an ascetic.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1906","lb":"CC1906","ev":"The companions furnish a camp near the ascetics' grove.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1907":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1907,"t":["நித்தில முலையி னார்த நெடுங்கணா னோக்கப் பெற்றுங்","கைத்தலந் தீண்டப் பெற்றுங் கனிந்தன மலர்ந்த காண்க","வைத்தலர் கொய்யத் தாழ்ந்த மரமுயி ரில்லை யென்பார்","பித்தல ராயிற் பேய்க ளென்றவாற் பேச லாமோ."],"p":"","r":"The narrator asks: see how the trees ripen and flower when the pearl-breasted women look at them with long eyes and touch them, and bow down to be plucked; can those who say a tree has no life be anything but madmen or ghouls?","n":"The flowering of trees at a woman's glance or touch is a poetic convention; here it is turned into an argument that plants live, a teaching Jains hold, though the verse names no school. The claim it mocks is answered in the same verse, so it is refuted here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1907","lb":"CC1907","ev":"","cl":[["trees are living beings, as their flowering at a woman's look and touch shows","general","asserted",2,"narrator"],["a tree has no life","general","refuted",3,"tree_life_deniers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1908":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1908,"t":["பொறிமயி லிழியும் பொற்றார் முருகனிற் பொலிந்து மாவி","னெறிமையி னிழிந்து மைந்தன் மணிக்கைமத் திகையை நீக்கி","வெறுமையி னவரைப் போக்கி வெள்ளிடைப் படாத நீரா","லறிமயி லகவுங் கோயி லடிகளைச் செவ்வி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, more splendid than Murugan of the peacock, dismounts from his horse, lays aside the jewelled whip, sends his men away to go plainly and unseen, and says: tell the lady of the temple where peacocks call that it is time to receive me.","n":"He calls his mother அடிகள், the title of an ascetic: the queen he comes to meet is now a renouncer, and he puts off the signs of rank to approach her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","panivu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1908","lb":"CC1908","ev":"Civakan dismounts, dismisses his retinue and sends to ask an audience of the ascetic lady at the shrine.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1909":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1909,"t":["எல்லிருட் கனவிற் கண்டேன் கண்ணிட னாடு மின்னே","பல்லியும் பட்ட பாங்கர் வருங்கொலோ நம்பி யென்று","சொல்லின டேவி நிற்பப் பதுமுகன் றொழுது சோந்து","நல்லடி பணிந்து நம்பி வந்தன னடிக ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The queen says to a woman beside her that she saw a dream in the dark night, her eye throbs on the good side and a lizard has sounded there too: will her son come? Padumukan bows, falls at her good feet and says: lady, the prince has come.","n":"டேவி is தேவி after சொல்லினள் by sandhi; the queen is still called தேவி though she wears the ascetic's robe. Padumukan speaks the last line. In 1909 line 3 சோந்து looks corrupt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","kanavu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1909","lb":"CC1909","ev":"Vijayai reads a dream, a throbbing eye and a lizard's call as signs of her son's coming, and Padumukan announces that he has come.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை",""],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1910":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1910,"t":["எங்கணா னைய னென்றாட் கடியன்யா னடிக ளென்னாப்","பொங்கிவில் லுமிழ்ந்து மின்னும் புனைமணிக் கடக மார்ந்த","தங்கொளித் தடக்கை கூப்பித் தொழுதடி தழுவி வீழ்ந்தா","னங்கிரண் டற்பு முன்னீ ரலைகடல் கலந்த தொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"When she asks where her lord's son is, he says 'I am your servant, lady', joins his bright bangled hands, worships and falls clasping her feet, and the two are like two seas of love mingling.","n":"Vijayai asks the question (ஐயன், the lord, here her son), and Civakan answers it himself with அடியன், 'your servant', the word of a worshipper to an ascetic as well as a son to a mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anbu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1910","lb":"CC1910","ev":"Civakan falls at his mother Vijayai's feet and they embrace for the first time since his birth.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","பதுமுகன்"],"ch":"cc:81"},"cc:1911":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1911,"t":["திருவடி தொழுது வீழ்ந்த சிறுவனைக் கண்ட போழ்தே","வருபனி சுமந்து வாட்கண் வ னமுலை பொழிந்த தீம்பான்","முருகுடை மார்பிற் பாய்ந்து முழுமெயு நனைப்ப மாதர்","வருகவென் களிறென் றேத்தி வாங்குபு தழுவிக் கொண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The moment she sees the boy who has fallen at her holy feet, the mother's eyes fill with tears and sweet milk spills from her breasts over his chest, and crying 'come, my elephant' she draws him into her arms.","n":"The milk that flows on sight of a grown son is the epic's sign of recognised motherhood; it answers the burning-ground birth, where she could not nurse him and gave him up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1911","lb":"CC1911","ev":"Civakan falls at Vijayai's feet; she weeps, her milk flows, and she embraces him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1912":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1912,"t":["காளையாம் பருவ மோராள் காதன்மீக் கூர்த லாலே","வாளையா நெடிய கண்ணான் மகனைமார் பொடுங்கப் புல்லித்","தாளையா முன்பு செய்த தவத்தது விளைவி லாதேன்","றோளையாத் தீர்ந்த தென்றா டொழுதகு தெய்வ மன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Forgetting in her love that he is now a grown youth, the woman like a goddess to be worshipped clasps her son tight to her breast and says that her arms' long ache is over, she who had thought her former penance had borne no fruit.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 (தாளையா ... றோளையாத் தீர்ந்த) are hard to divide; the sense that her penance had seemed fruitless and that her arms' longing is now ended is a reading, not a certainty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","makkal","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1912","lb":"CC1912","ev":"Vijayai holds Civakan to her breast like a child and speaks of her grief ended.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1913":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1913,"t":["வாட்டிறற் குருசி றன்னை வாளம ரகத்து ணீத்துக்","காட்டகத் தும்மை நீத்த கயத்தியேற் காண வந்தீர்","சேட்டிளம் பருதி மார்பிற் சீவக சாமி யீரே","யூட்டரக் குண்ட செந்தா மரையடி நோவ வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She calls herself the base woman who left the king in the sword-fight and left him in the burning forest, and marvels that Civakan Saami, with a chest like the young sun, has come to see her on lotus feet that may be hurt.","n":"கயத்தியேன் is strong self-blame: the story told her flight as the king's command and the child's leaving as forced, but the mother judges herself by the result, not the necessity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pazhi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1913","lb":"CC1913","ev":"Vijayai reproaches herself for abandoning husband and child and greets Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1914":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1914,"t":["கெடலருங் குரைய கொற்றங் கெடப்பிறந் ததுவு மன்றி","நடலையு ளடிகள் வைக நட்புடை யவர்க ணைய","விடைமகன் கொன்ற வின்னா மரத்தினேன் றந்த துன்பக்","கடலகத் தழுந்த வேண்டா களைகவிக் கவலை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan answers that he was born only to see an unperishing victory perish, while his lady mother lived in misery and her well-wishers grieved; he is the baleful tree of the proverb, and she should not sink in the sea of sorrow he has caused, but put away this care.","n":"Mother and son each take the blame on themselves in successive verses. The figure in line 3 (விடைமகன் கொன்ற இன்னா மரத்தினேன், a tree of ill that killed a herdsman?) turns on a doubtful word and a story not told here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","makkal","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1914","lb":"CC1914","ev":"Civakan blames his own birth for the family's ruin and asks his mother to cast off her grief.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1915":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1915,"t":["யானல னௌவை யாவாள் சுநந்தையே யையற் கென்றுங்","கோனலன் றந்தை கந்துக் கடனெனக் குணத்தின் மிக்க","பானிலத் துறையுந் தீந்தே னனையவா யமிர்த மூற","மானலங் கொண்ட நோக்கி மகன்மன மகிழ்ச் சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"With eyes like a deer's and words sweet as honey from a mountain hive, Vijayai says, to gladden her son, that she is not his mother: his mother is Sunandai, always, and his father is not the king but Kandukkatan, great in virtue.","n":"The birth mother and queen gives up both her own claim and the dead king's to the merchant couple who reared him; the verse says she says it so that her son may rejoice, which makes it a gift of gratitude rather than a denial.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","makkal","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1915","lb":"CC1915","ev":"Vijayai tells Civakan that his foster parents, Sunandai and Kandukkatan, are his true mother and father.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1916":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1916,"t":["எனக்குயிர்ச் சிறுவ னாவா னந்தனே யைய னல்லை","வனப்புடைக் குமர னிங்கே வருகென மருங்கு சோத்திப்","புனக்கொடி மாலையோடு பூங்குழ றிருத்திப் போற்றா","ரினத்திடை யேற னானுக் கின்னளி விருந்து செய்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She goes on that her life's own son is Nandan, not he, and calling 'come here, handsome prince' draws Nandattan to her side, sets his garland and flowered hair right, and gives the bull among his foes a feast of tender love.","n":"The exchange is symmetrical: having given Civakan to his foster mother, she takes the foster mother's own son as hers, so that the two brothers are each other's mother's child.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","virundhu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1916","lb":"CC1916","ev":"Vijayai claims Nandattan as her own son, draws him close and tends him.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1917":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1917,"t":["சிறகராற் பார்ப்புப் புல்லித் திருமயி லிருந்த தேபோ","லிறைவிதன் சிறுவர் தம்மை யிருகையி னாலும் புல்லி","முறைமுறை குமரர்க் கெல்லா மொழியமை முகமன் கூறி","யறுசுவை யமிர்த மூட்டி யறுபகல் கழிந்த பின்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"As a peahen sits gathering her chicks under her wings, the queen holds her sons with both arms, gives each of the youths in turn fitting words of welcome, and feeds them six-flavoured nectar until six days have passed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","anbu","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1917","lb":"CC1917","ev":"Vijayai embraces her sons, welcomes Civakan's companions and feeds them for six days.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை","சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1918":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1918,"t":["மரவ நாக மணங்கமழ் சண்பகங்","குரவங் கோங்கங் குடம்புரை காய்வழை","விரவு பூம்பொழில் வேறிருந் தாய்பொரு","ளுருவ மாத ருரைக்குமி தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"In a flowering grove of maravam, nagam, fragrant champak, kuravam, kongam and vazhai, sitting apart, the lady speaks on the matter she has weighed, as the next verses give it.","n":"The ascetic mother, in a hermitage grove, gives not a sermon but a lesson in kingcraft; the setting and the matter pull against each other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1918","lb":"CC1918","ev":"After six days Vijayai sits apart with Civakan in a grove and begins her counsel.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1919":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1919,"t":["நலிவில் குன்றொடு காடுறை நன்பொருட்","புலிய னார்மகட் கோடலும் பூமிமேல்","வலியின் மிக்கவர் தம்மகட் கோடலு","நிலைகொண் மன்னர் வழக்கென நோபவே."],"p":"","r":"She says it is the custom of kings whose rule stands firm to take as wives the daughters of the chiefs who live by the hills and forests, and the daughters of the mighty of the earth.","n":"The verse ends in a doubtful word (நோபவே); the sense 'they hold this to be kings' custom' is taken from the run of the counsel. It forecasts Civakan's many marriages as policy as well as love.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","magatpaal","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1919","lb":"CC1919","ev":"","cl":[["a king secures his rule by marrying the daughters of hill chiefs and of powerful rulers","general","asserted",4,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1920":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1920,"t":["நீதி யாலறத் தந்நிதி யீட்டுத","லாதி யாய வரும்பகை நாட்டுதன்","மோதி முள்ளொடு முட்பகை கண்டிடல்","பேது செய்து பிளந்திடல் பெட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"She lists what is approved: gathering treasure by justice and right, fixing which is the first and gravest enemy, using a thorn to find out the thorn of enmity, and splitting foes by sowing bewilderment among them.","n":"Just acquisition and divide-and-rule stand in one breath; the verse sees no conflict between அறம் in getting wealth and stratagem in breaking enemies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aram","pagai","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1920","lb":"CC1920","ev":"","cl":[["a ruler should gather wealth only by just and righteous means","general","asserted",1,"vijayai"],["enemies are to be found out by setting enemy against enemy and broken by sowing confusion among them","general","asserted",4,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1921":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1921,"t":["ஒற்றர்தங்களை யொற்றரி னாய்தலுங்","கற்ற மாந்தரைக் கண்ணெனக் கோடலுஞ்","சுற்றஞ் சூழ்ந்து பெருக்கலுஞ் சூதரோ","கொற்றங் கொள்குறிக் கொற்றவற் கென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Testing spies by other spies, taking learned men as one's eyes, and enlarging the circle of kin around one: these, they say, are the marks of victory for a king who means to win.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","kalvi","sutram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1921","lb":"CC1921","ev":"","cl":[["a king must check what one spy reports through another","general","asserted",1,"vijayai"],["a king should take learned men as his eyes and widen his kin","general","asserted",2,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1922":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1922,"t":["வென்றி யாக்கலு மேதக வாக்கலுங்","குன்றி னார்களைக் குன்றென வாக்கலு","மன்றி யுங்கல்வி யோடழ காக்கலும்","பொன்றுஞ் சாகத்தி னாய்பொருள் செய்யுமே."],"p":"","r":"Wealth, she tells the son on whose chest fortune sleeps, makes victory, makes eminence, makes the diminished into mountains, and makes beauty along with learning.","n":"பொன்றுஞ் சாகத்தினாய் is best divided பொன் துஞ்சு ஆகத்தினாய், an address to Civakan; the 'diminished' of line 2 are, as 1924 makes plain, the speaker and her son.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vali","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1922","lb":"CC1922","ev":"","cl":[["wealth raises those who have fallen low and brings victory and standing","general","asserted",2,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1923":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1923,"t":["பொன்னி னாகும் பொருபடை யப்படை","தன்னி னாகுந் தரணி தரணியிற்","பின்னை யாகும் பெரும்பொரு ளப்பொருள்","துன்னுங் காலைத்துன் னாதன வில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"From gold comes a fighting army, from that army the land, from the land in turn great wealth, and when that wealth is gathered there is nothing that does not come.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","padai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1923","lb":"CC1923","ev":"","cl":[["wealth buys an army, the army wins land, and land yields more wealth, so that wealth gathered commands everything","general","asserted",4,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1924":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1924,"t":["நிலத்தி னீங்கி நிதியினுந் தேய்ந்துநங்","குலத்திற் குன்றிய கொள்கைய மல்லதூஉங்","கலைக்க ணாளரு மிங்கில்லை காளைநீ","வலித்த தென்னென வள்ளலுங் கூறுவான்."],"p":"","r":"She sets out their state: driven from their land, worn down in treasure, fallen from the standing of their line, and with no learned counsellors here, and asks the young bull what he has resolved; and the generous one replies.","n":"Each item answers a maxim just given: no land for the army-and-land chain of 1923, no treasure for 1922, no learned eyes for 1921. The counsel was a measure of what they lack.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","inmai","kalvi","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1924","lb":"CC1924","ev":"Vijayai lists their losses and asks Civakan what he has decided; he begins to answer.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1925":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1925,"t":["எரியொடு நிகர்க்கு மாற்ற லிடிக்குரற் சிங்க மாங்கோர்","நரியொடு பொருவ தென்றாற் சூழ்ச்சிநற் றுணையொ டென்னாம்","பரிவொடு கவல வேண்டா பாம்பவன் கலுழ னாகுஞ்","சொரிமதுச் சுரும்புண் கண்ணிச் சூழ்கழ னந்த னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan answers that when a thunder-voiced lion with strength like fire fights a jackal, what need is there of strategy and good allies; she should not grieve, for the enemy is a snake and Nandan of the honeyed garland and warrior's anklet is Garuda.","n":"The claim is answered at once: 1927 restores the weighing of time and place, and 1928 has the lion that leaps without thought caught in a net. The young hero repeats in brave words the trust in force over counsel that undid his father.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","soozhchi","thunai","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1925","lb":"CC1925","ev":"Civakan dismisses the need for strategy against Kattiyangaran and names Nandattan as the Garuda to the enemy's snake.","cl":[["against a weak foe a strong man needs neither strategy nor allies","general","refuted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1926":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1926,"t":["கெலுழனோ நந்த னென்னாக் கிளரொளி வனப்பு னானைக்","கலுழத்தன் கையாற் றீண்டிக் காதலிற் களித்து நோக்கி","வலிகெழு வயிரத் தூண்போற் றிரண்டுநீண் டமைந்த திண்டோள்","கலிகெழு நிலத்தைக் காவா தொழியுமோ காளைக் கென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"'Is Nandan a Garuda, then?' she says, and weeping, touches the bright and handsome youth with her hand, gazes at him in love and delight, and asks whether shoulders thick and long as diamond pillars could fail to guard the clamorous earth.","n":"Whether the youth she touches is Nandattan, just named, or Civakan the காளை of line 4, the Tamil does not settle; கலுழ may also be read of the one touched rather than of her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vali","anbu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1926","lb":"CC1926","ev":"Vijayai touches and praises the youth whose shoulders she says will surely guard the earth.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1927":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1927,"t":["இடத்தொடு பொழுது நாடி யெவ்வினைக் கண்ணு மஞ்சார்","மடப்பட லின்றிச் சூழ மதிவல்லார்க் கரிய துண்டோ","கடத்திடைக் காக்கை யொன்றே யாயிரங் கோடி கூகை","யிடத்திடை யழுங்கச் சென்றாங் கின்னுயிர் செகுத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"For those strong in judgment who weigh place and time, fear no undertaking and plan without folly, is anything too hard? In the wilderness a single crow went among the owls in their own place and, while they lay helpless, took the lives of a thousand crore.","n":"The speaker is not named; the verse answers Civakan's 1925 and reads as the mother's. The crow among the owls is a fable known also from the Sanskrit tale collections; here it puts weakness plus judgment above strength.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kaalam","idam","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1927","lb":"CC1927","ev":"","cl":[["those who weigh place and time and plan without folly can achieve anything, even the weak against the many","general","asserted",2,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1928":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1928,"t":["இழைபொறை யாற்ற கில்லா திட்டிடை தளர நின்ற","குழைநிற முகத்தி னார்போற் குறித்ததே துணிந்து செய்யார்","முழையுறை சிங்கம் பொங்கி முழங்கிமேற் பாய்ந்து மைதோய்","வழையுறை வனத்து வன்க ணரிவலைப் பட்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Men who do not resolve and carry through what they intend are like women whose waists sag under the weight of their jewels; and the lion of the cave, swelling and roaring and leaping upon its prey, fell into the net of hard-eyed men in the dark forest.","n":"The lion is Civakan's own image from 1925, turned against him. Lines 1 and 2 are a hard simile; whether they blame the timid or the rash is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vali","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1928","lb":"CC1928","ev":"","cl":[["strength that leaps without judgment is caught, as the roaring lion falls into the hunters' net","general","asserted",3,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1929":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1929,"t":["ஊழிவாய்த் தீயொ டொப்பான் பதுமுக னுரைக்கு மொன்னா","ராழிவாய்த் துஞ்ச மற்றெம் மாற்றலா னெருங்கி வென்று","மாழைநீ ணிதியந் துஞ்சு மாநிலக் கிழமை யெய்தும்","பாழியாற் பிறரை வேண்டேம் பணிப்பதே பாணி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Padumukan, like the fire at the world's end, says that the foes will sleep on the battlefield, that pressing on by their own strength they will win and gain lordship of the great earth where gold lies stored, that they need no others for the fight, and that the lord has only to command.","n":"After the mother's case for allies and stratagem, the friend repeats Civakan's confidence; the verse sets the two counsels side by side without deciding here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vali","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1929","lb":"CC1929","ev":"Padumukan vows that the companions alone will win the kingdom and asks only for orders.","cl":[["the companions' own strength suffices and no outside allies are needed","general","reported",4,"padumukan"]],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பதுமுகன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1930":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1930,"t":["பொருவருங் குரைய மைந்தர் பொம்மென வுரறி மற்றித்","திருவிருந் தகன்ற மார்பன் சேவடி சோந்த யாங்க","ளெரியிருந் தயரு நீர்மை யிருங்கதி ரேற்ற தெவ்வர்","வருபனி யிருளு மாக மதிக்க வெம் மடிக ளென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The peerless youths roar out together that they who have come to the red feet of this broad-chested lord of fortune hold the enemies to be no more than the dew and darkness that come before the great-rayed sun, and their lady should reckon them so.","n":"Line 3 (எரியிருந் தயரு நீர்மை) is obscure; the sun-and-dew figure of line 4 carries the sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1930","lb":"CC1930","ev":"Civakan's companions shout their loyalty and scorn for the enemy.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழர்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1931":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1931,"t":["கார்தோன்ற வேம லருமுல்லை கமலம் வெய்யோன்","றோதோன்ற வேம லருஞ்செம்மனின் மாமன் மற்றுன்","சீர்தோன்ற வேம லருஞ்சென்றவன் சொல்லி னோடே","பார்தோன்ற நின்ற பகையைச் செறற் பாலையென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"As mullai blooms when the rain-cloud appears and the lotus when the sun's chariot appears, so, she says, his maternal uncle will bloom when Civakan's glory appears; he should go to him and, with his word, destroy the enemy who stands open on the earth.","n":"Her last word reinstates what Civakan and his friends set aside: the ally (the uncle) and his counsel (சொல்லினோடே) come before the fight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","sutram","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1931","lb":"CC1931","ev":"Vijayai sends Civakan to his maternal uncle, to strike the enemy with the uncle's counsel.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:82"},"cc:1932":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1932,"t":["நன்றப் பொருளே வலித்தேன்மற் றடிக ணாளைச்","சென்றப் பதியு ளெமர்க்கேயேன துண்மை காட்டி","யன்றைப் பகலே யடியேன்வந் தடைவ னீமே","வென்றிக் களிற்று னுழைச்செல்வது வேண்டு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan accepts his mother's counsel: he will go next day to that city, show his own people the truth, and come back the same day, and he asks her to go ahead to the one of the victorious elephant.","n":"அடிகள் is his address to his mother; the one of the victorious elephant (வென்றிக் களிற்றுன்) is most likely the maternal uncle (மாமன்) she named in 1931, but the verse does not name him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1932","lb":"CC1932","ev":"Civakan tells Vijayai he will go to the city to show his kin the truth and asks her to go to her brother.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","விசயை"],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1933":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1933,"t":["வேற்றைவந் தன்ன நுதிவெம்பாற் கான முன்னி","நூற்றைவ ரோடு நடந்தாணுதி வல்வின் மைந்தன்","காற்றிற் பரிக்குங் கலிமான் மிசைக் காவ லோம்பி","யாற்றற் கமைந்த படையோடதர் முன்னி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The young man of the strong bow set out toward the forest hot as spear-points, walking with his band, his road guarded by riders on wind-swift horses and an army fit for the journey.","n":"நூற்றைவர் gives the size of his band, a hundred and five or five hundred depending on how the compound is read.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1933","lb":"CC1933","ev":"Civakan sets out through the hot forest with his company and a guard of horsemen.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1934":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1934,"t":["மன்றற் கிடனா மணிமால்வரை மார்பன் வான்க","ணின்றெத் திசையு மருவிப்புன னீத்த மோவாக்","குன்றுங் குளிர்நீர்த் தடஞ்சூழ்ந்தன கோல யாறுஞ்","சென்றப் பழனப் படப்பைப்புன னாடு சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Crossing hills where falls never cease and rivers ringed with cool tanks, the hero of the gem-mountain chest reached a river country of fields and groves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1934","lb":"CC1934","ev":"Civakan reaches a fertile river country.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1935":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1935,"t":["காவின்மேற் கடிமலர் தெகிழ்ந்த நாற்றமும்","வாவியு ளினமல ருயிர்த்த வாசமும்","பூவிரி கோதையர் புனைந்த சாந்தமு","மேவலாற் கெதிரெதிர் விருந்து செய்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The scent of flowers opening on the groves, the breath of lotuses in the pools and the sandal the women wore met the newcomer at every turn as feast after feast.","n":"The land itself plays host: the fragrances are said to make விருந்து, the guest's welcome, before any person greets him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1935","lb":"CC1935","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1936":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1936,"t":["கரும்பின்மேற் றொடுத்ததேன் கலிகொ டாமரைச்","சுரும்பின்வாய்த் துளித்தலிற் றுவைத்த வண்டொடு","திருந்தியாழ் முரல்வதோர் தெய்வப் பூம்பொழிற்","பொருந்தினான் புனைமணிப் பொன்செய் பூணினான்."],"p":"","r":"He of the gold-wrought gem ornaments halted in a divine flower grove where bees, drunk on honey dripping from comb into lotuses, hummed like a tuned yaazh.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1936","lb":"CC1936","ev":"Civakan halts in a flowering grove.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1937":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1937,"t":["பொறைவிலங் குயிர்த்தன பொன்செய் மாமணிச்","செறிகழ லிளைஞருஞ் செல்ல னீங்கினார்","நறைவிரி கோதையர் நாம வேலினாற்","கறுசுவை நால்வகை யமுத மாக்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"The pack beasts were unloaded, the young men of gem anklets were rid of their weariness, and the women of fragrant garlands made the four kinds of food of six tastes for the lord of the famed spear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1937","lb":"CC1937","ev":"The company rests and the women prepare a meal for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","கோதையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1938":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1938,"t":["கட்டியி னரிசியும் புழுக்குங் காணமும்","புட்டில்வாய்ச் செறித்தனர் புரவிக் கல்லவு","நெட்டிருங் கரும்பொடு செந்நென் மேய்ந்துநீர்","பட்டன வளநிழற் பரிவு தீர்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Nosebags were filled with jaggery rice, boiled pulse and horse-gram for the horses, while the other beasts grazed tall cane and red paddy, drank, and rested in the rich shade.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1938","lb":"CC1938","ev":"The horses and other beasts are fed and rested.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1939":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1939,"t":["குழிமதுக் குவளையங் கண்ணி வார்குழற்","பிழிமதுக் கோதையார் பேண வின்னமு","தழிமதக் களிறனா னயின்ற பின்னரே","கழிமலர் விழித்தகண் கமலம் பட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"Only after the man like a musth elephant had eaten the sweet food served by the women with dripping garlands did the backwater lotuses, open like eyes, close for the evening.","n":"The closing of the lotuses waits on his meal, so evening is timed to the guest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1939","lb":"CC1939","ev":"Civakan eats the meal the women serve as evening falls.","cl":[],"ag":["கோதையர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1940":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1940,"t":["எரிமணி யிமைத்தன வெழுந்த தீம்புகை","புரிநரம் பிரங்கின புகன்ற தீங்குழ","றிருமணி முழவமுஞ் செம்பொற் பாண்டிலு","மருமணி யின்குர லரவஞ் செய்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Gem lamps glimmered, sweet smoke rose, strings sounded, flutes sang, and the drum and gold cymbals made a pleasant din.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1940","lb":"CC1940","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1941":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1941,"t":["தெளித்தவின் முறுவலம் பவளஞ் செற்றவாய்க்","களிக்கயன் மழைக்கணார் காமங் காழ்கொளீஇ","விளித்தவின் னமிர்துறழ் கீதம் வேனலா","னளித்தபின் னமளியஞ் சேக்கை யெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"After the women of red mouths and rain-dark fish eyes had kindled desire with songs sweeter than nectar, he went to the soft bed.","n":"வேனலான் அளித்த பின் in line 3 is hard to place: it may name the spring god Kama as the giver, or a song of the hot season.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1941","lb":"CC1941","ev":"The women sing to Civakan and he retires to bed.","cl":[],"ag":["கோதையர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1942":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1942,"t":["தீங்கரும் பனுக்கிய திருந்து தோள்களும்","வீங்கெழிற் றோள்களு மிடைந்து வெம்முலை","பூங்குளிர் தாரொடு பொருது பொன்னுக","வீங்கனங் கனையிரு ளெல்லை நீந்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Shoulders strong enough to crush sweet cane and swelling lovely shoulders entwined, hot breasts pressed against his cool garland till its gold dust fell, and so he swam across the dark night.","n":"The verse does not name his partner; the context of 1941 makes it the singing women. The Jain epic tells the hero's night of pleasure without a word of reproach and moves straight to his dawn worship of the Jina.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1942","lb":"CC1942","ev":"Civakan spends the night in love.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1943":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1943,"t":["கனைகதிர்க் கடவுள் கண் விழித்த காலையே","நனைமலர்த் தாமரை நக்க வண்கையாற்","புனைகதிர் திருமுகங் கழுவிப் பூமழை","முனைவனுக் கிறைஞ்சினான் முருக வேளனான்."],"p":"","r":"When the sun opened its eye at dawn, the hero like Murugan washed his radiant face with a hand like a budding lotus, showered flowers and bowed to the Lord (முனைவன்).","n":"முனைவன் is the Jain name for the Jina; the morning rite follows the night of 1942 with no sense of conflict between them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1943","lb":"CC1943","ev":"At dawn Civakan washes and worships the Lord.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1944":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1944,"t":["நாட்கடன் கழித்தபி னாம வேலினான்","வாட்கடி யெழினகர் வண்மை காணிய","தோட்பொலி மணிவளைத் தொய்யின் மாதரார்","வேட்பதோர் வடிவொடு விரைவி னெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"His daily duties done, the hero of the famed spear went quickly, in a form that bangled women desire, to see the wealth of the sword-guarded city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1944","lb":"CC1944","ev":"Civakan goes into the city to see its riches.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1945":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1945,"t":["அலத்தகக் கொழுங்களி யிழுக்கி யஞ்சொலார்","புலத்தலிற் களைந்தபூ ணிடறிப் பொன்னிதழ்","நிலத்துகு மாலைகா றொடர்ந்து நீணகர்","செலக்குறை படாததோர் செல்வ மிக்கதே."],"p":"","r":"The long city's wealth never runs short: lac paste slipping from feet, ornaments cast off in lovers' sulks, golden petals strewn by garlands that trail along the ground.","n":"The wealth is proved by what is wasted: ornaments thrown off in புலத்தல், the lovers' quarrel, lie in the street as litter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1945","lb":"CC1945","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1946":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1946,"t":["கத்தி கைக்கழு நீர்கமழ் கோதையர்","பத்தி யிற்படு சாந்தணி வெம்முலை","சித்தி யிற்படர் சிந்தையி னாரையு","மித்தி சைப்படர் வித்தி நீரவே."],"p":"","r":"The sandal-painted breasts of the women whose garlands smell of the kazhuneer lily turn even those whose minds run toward release (சித்தி) back in this direction.","n":"சித்தி here is the final attainment of the Jain path, not yogic power; the verse is praise of the city's women, and the seekers' lapse is its measure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","veedu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1946","lb":"CC1946","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1947":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1947,"t":["வஞ்சி வாட்டிய வாண்மி னுசுப்பினார்","பஞ்சி யூட்டிய பாடகச் சீறடி","குஞ்சி சூட்டிய மைந்தர் குழாமலா","லிஞ்சி வட்ட மிடம்பிற் தில்லையே"],"p":"","r":"Within the circle of the walls there is no room for anything but crowds of women whose waists shame the vanji creeper, with dyed feet and anklets, and young men in crowned locks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1947","lb":"CC1947","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1948":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1948,"t":["மின்னி னீள்கடம் பின்னெடு வேள்கொலோ","மன்னு மைங்கணை வார்சிலை மைந்தனோ","வென்ன னோவறி யோமுரை யீரெனாப்","பொன்னங் கொம்பனை யார்புலம் பெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"The women like golden creepers lament: is he the tall Vel of the long kadamba, or the son of the five arrows and the long bow? We cannot tell; tell us.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anangu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1948","lb":"CC1948","ev":"The city's women, seeing Civakan, cry out that they cannot tell whether he is Murugan or Kama.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரார்",""],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1949":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1949,"t":["விண்ண கத்திளை யானன்ன மெய்ப்பொறி","பண்ண லைக்கழி மீன்கவர் புள்ளென","வண்ண வார்குழ லேழையர் வாணெடுங்","கண்ணெ லாங்கவர்ந் துண்டிடு கின்றவே."],"p":"","r":"His true form, like a youth of the heavens, seizes and devours all the long eyes of the women, as a bird of the backwater takes fish.","n":"The usual figure is reversed: elsewhere women's eyes are the fish or the arrows; here his beauty is the bird that eats their eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1949","lb":"CC1949","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1950":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1950,"t":["புலாத்தலைத் திகழும் வைவேற் பூங்கழற் காலி னானை","நிலாத்தலைத் திகழும் பைம்பூ ணிழன்மணி வடத்தோ டேந்திக்","குலாய்த்தலைக் கிடந்து மின்னுங் குவிமுலை பாய வெய்தாய்க்","கலாய்த்தொலைப் பருகு வார்போற் கன்னியர் துவன்றி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Maidens crowded upon the man of the flesh-tipped spear, holding up garlands with gem strands, their breasts thrusting, as if they would drink him with their eyes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1950","lb":"CC1950","ev":"Young women crowd round Civakan with garlands.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1951":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1951,"t":["வேனெடுங் கண்க ளம்பா விற்படை சாற்றி யெங்குந்","தேனெடுங் கோதை நல்லார் மைந்தனைத் தெருவி லெய்ய","மானெடு மழைக்க ணோக்கி வானவர் மகளு மொப்பாள்","பானெடுந் தீஞ்சொ லாளோர் பாவைபந் தாடு கின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"While the women everywhere aim their eye-arrows at the young man in the street, a girl like a daughter of the gods, with eyes like a doe's and speech sweet as milk, is playing ball.","n":"The girl is not named here; the ilampakam's title makes her Vimalai. She is absorbed in her game while every other woman is looking at him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1951","lb":"CC1951","ev":"A maiden is playing ball in the street where Civakan walks.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்","விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1952":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1952,"t":["குழன்மலிந்த கோதைமாலை பொங்கவெங் கதிர்ம்முலை","நிழன்மலிந்த நோவடந்நி ழற்படப்ப டைத்தர","வெழின்மணிக்கு ழைவில்வீச வின்பொனோலை மின்செய","வழன்மணிக்க லாபமஞ்சி லம்பொடார்ப்ப வாடுமே."],"p":"","r":"Her garland bounces, her hot breasts and pearl strings shift, her gem earrings swing like bows, her gold ear-leaf flashes, and her girdle and anklets ring as she dances at the ball.","n":"The verse changes to a quick சந்தம் rhythm that moves with the ball.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1952","lb":"CC1952","ev":"Vimalai plays ball.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:83"},"cc:1953":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1953,"t":["அங்கையந்த லத்தகத்த வைந்துபந்த மர்ந்தவை","மங்கையாட மாலைசூழும் வண்டுபோல வந்துடன்","பொங்கிமீதெ ழுந்துபோய்ப்பி றழ்ந்துபாய்த லின்றியே","செங்கயற்கண் புருவந்தம்மு ளுருவஞ்செய்யத் திரியுமே."],"p":"","r":"Five balls rest in the palm of the maiden's hand; as she plays they rise together like bees circling a garland and go up without scattering, and her red-carp eyes and brows travel with their shapes.","n":"The game is ஐந்து பந்து, five balls kept in the air at once; the verse already makes her eyes follow the balls, the eyes that will soon follow Civakan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1953","lb":"CC1953","ev":"Vimalai plays with five balls.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1954":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1954,"t":["மாலையுட்க ரந்தபந்து வந்துகைத்த லத்தவா","மேலநாறி ருங்குழற்பு றத்தவாண்மு கத்தவா","நூலினோநு சுப்புநோவ வுச்சிமாலை யுள்ளவா","மேலெழுந்த மீநிலத்த விரலகைய வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator follows the balls as they hide in her garland and come back to her hand, pass behind her fragrant hair and before her bright face, by a waist thin as thread and at the garland on her crown, and drop back to her fingers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1954","lb":"CC1954","ev":"Vimalai keeps the balls moving round her body.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1955":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1955,"t":["கொண்டுநீங்கல் கோதைவேய்தல் குங்குமம்ம ணிந்துரா","யெண்டிசையு மேணியேற்றி லங்கநிற்றல் பத்தியின்","மணிடி லம்வ ரப்புடைத்தன் மயிலிற்பொங்கி யின்னணம்","வண்டுந்தேனும் பாடமாதர் பந்துமைந்துற் றாடுமே."],"p":"","r":"The figures of the game are named, catching and letting go, garlanding, rising in the eight directions, circling in rows, leaping like a peacock, while bees sing, and so the woman plays her five balls.","n":"The verse is a list of technical moves of the game (கொண்டு நீங்கல், கோதை வேய்தல், எண்டிசையும் ஏற்றல்); some of their exact sense is not recoverable from the words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1955","lb":"CC1955","ev":"Vimalai plays through the named figures of the ball game.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1956":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1956,"t":["பந்துமைந்துற் றாடுவாள்ப ணைம்முலையிற் குங்குமஞ்","சுந்தரப்பொ டிதெளித்த செம்பொற்சுண்ணம் வாணுதற்","றந்துசுட்டி யிட்டசாந்தம் வேரின்வார்ந்தி டைம்முலை","யிந்திரதி ருவினெக்கு ருகியென்ன வீழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"As she plays, the saffron on her breasts, the gold powder sprinkled over her and the sandal of the mark on her forehead run down with sweat between her breasts and fall like the rainbow melting.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1956","lb":"CC1956","ev":"Vimalai sweats as she plays, and her cosmetics run.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1957":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1957,"t":["நன்மணிச்சி லம்பினோடு கிண்கிணிந்ந கந்நகும்","மின்மலர்ந்த முல்லைமாலை நக்கிமிக்கி றந்தெழுந்து","பொன்மலர்ந்த கோதைபந்து பொங்கியொன்று போந்துபாய்ந்து","மின்மலர்ந்த வேலினான்முன் வீதிபுக்கு வீழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"With her anklets and girdle-bells laughing, one ball of the gold-garlanded girl leaps out past her jasmine garland, bounds away and falls in the street before the man of the bright spear.","n":"The narrator gives the ball, not the girl, the move that brings the two together; no one is said to aim it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1957","lb":"CC1957","ev":"One of Vimalai's balls flies out and falls in the street before Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1958":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1958,"t":["வீழ்ந்தபந்தின் மேல்விரைந்து மின்னினுண் ணுசுப்பினாள்","சூழ்ந்தகாசு தோன்றவந்து கின்னெகிழ்ந்து பூங்குழ","றாழ்ந்துகோதை பொங்கிவீழ்ந்து வெம்முலைக டைவரப்","போழ்ந்தகன்ற கண்ணிவந்து பூங்கொடியி னோக்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The slender-waisted girl hurries after the fallen ball, her girdle showing, her hair loosening and her garland tumbling onto her breasts, and comes and looks out with wide eyes like a flowering creeper.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1958","lb":"CC1958","ev":"Vimalai runs out for the ball and looks out into the street.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1959":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1959,"t":["மந்தார மாலை மலர்வேய்ந்து மகிழ்ந்து தீந்தேன்","கந்தாரஞ் செய்து களிவண்டு முரன்று பாடப்","பந்தார்வஞ் செய்து குவளைக்கண் பரப்பி நின்றாள்","செந்தாம ரைமேற் றிருவின்னுரு வெய்தி நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Crowned with mandara flowers, while drunken bees hum in the gandhara mode, she stands longing for the ball with her lily eyes spread wide, standing like Tiru on the red lotus.","n":"பந்தார்வம் is longing for the ball; the line keeps her desire on the game a moment before she sees him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1959","lb":"CC1959","ev":"Vimalai stands at the doorway looking out.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1960":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1960,"t":["நீர்தங்கு திங்கண் மணிநீணிலந் தன்னு ளோங்கிச்","சீர்தங்கு கங்கைத் திருநீர்த்தண் டுவலை மாந்திக்","கார்தங்கி நின்ற கொடிகாளையைக் காண்ட லோடு","பீர்தங்கிப் பெய்யா மலரிற்பிறி தாயி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The creeper on the moonstone terrace, as though fed on cool spray of the Ganga, no sooner sees the young bull than she becomes another, pale as the unopened peerkku flower.","n":"பீர் (the yellow ridge-gourd flower) is the old sign of the pallor of love; here it comes at first sight, not in separation, which is where the akam poems put it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1960","lb":"CC1960","ev":"Vimalai sees Civakan and turns pale.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1961":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1961,"t":["பெண்பா லவர்கட் கணியாய்ப்பிரி யாத நாணுந்","திண்பா னிறையுந் திருமாமையுஞ் சோந்த சாயல்","கண்பாற் கவினும் வளையுங்கவர்ந்த திட்ட கள்வன்","மண்பா லிழிந்த மலரைங்கணை மைந்த னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says the thief who stole her modesty that never left her, her firm restraint, her beautiful dark skin and grace, the beauty of her eyes and her bangles, is the son of the five-flower-arrowed god come down to earth.","n":"She lists what a woman's virtue is made of (நாண், நிறை) beside her skin and bangles, as goods a thief has carried off; the loss is put on him, not on her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","nirai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1961","lb":"CC1961","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விமலை",""],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1962":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1962,"t":["என்றா ணினைந்தா ளிதுபோலுமிவ் வேட்கை வண்ணஞ்","சென்றே படினுஞ் சிறந்தார்க்கு முரைக்க லாவ","தன்றா யரிதா யகத்தேசுட் டுருக்கும் வெந்தீ","யொன்றே யுலகத் துறுநோய்மருந் தில்ல தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She thinks: such is this desire that even if one dies of it, it cannot be told even to one's dearest; it is hard, a hot fire that burns and melts one inside, the one sickness in the world that has no medicine.","n":"Two silences are joined: the sickness cannot be spoken even to the dearest, and it has no cure; the first is shame (naanam), the second her despair.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1962","lb":"CC1962","ev":"","cl":[["desire is the one sickness in the world for which there is no remedy","general","reported",4,"vimalai"]],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விமலை",""],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1963":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1963,"t":["நிறையாது மில்லை நெருப்பிற்சுடுங் காம முண்டேற்","குறையா நிறையி னொருகுன்றியுங் காம மில்லை","பறையா யறையும் பசப்பென்று பகர்ந்து வாடி","யறைவாய்க் கடல்போ லகன்காம மலைப்ப நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says there is no restraint at all where desire that burns like fire exists, and where restraint is whole there is not a kunri seed of desire; the pallor beats it out like a drum, she says, and wilting she stands while desire wide as the roaring sea tosses her.","n":"The claim is put as an either-or, and the very next verse shows her holding herself with நிறை while desire takes her, so the story does not bear out the rule she states.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam","pasalai","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1963","lb":"CC1963","ev":"","cl":[["restraint and desire cannot dwell together: where one is whole, not a trace of the other remains","general","reported",2,"vimalai"]],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விமலை",""],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1964":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1964,"t":["நெஞ்சங் கலங்கி நிறையாற்றுப் படுத்து நின்றா","ளஞ்செங் கழுநீ ரலாந்தம்மதி வாண்மு கத்தே","வஞ்சம் வழங்கா தவன்கண்களி னோக்க மாதோ","தஞ்சம் வழங்கித் தலைக்கொண்டது காம வெந்தீ."],"p":"","r":"Her heart shaken, she stood steadying herself with restraint; but the look of his guileless eyes on her moon-bright face of red-lily eyes gave refuge to desire, and its hot fire took hold of her.","n":"Whose eyes look at whose face in lines 2-3 is not fixed by the words; the reading here takes the look as his, falling on her face.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1964","lb":"CC1964","ev":"Vimalai tries to compose herself as Civakan's look falls on her, and desire takes her.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1965":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1965,"t":["பூவுண்ட கண்ணாள் புருவச்சிலை கோலி யெய்ய","வேவுண்ட நெஞ்சிற் கிடுபுண்மருந் தென்கொ லென்னா","மாவுண்ட நோக்கின் மடவாளை மறித்து நோக்கிக்","கோவுண்ட வேலான் குழைந்தாற்றல னாயி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As the flower-eyed girl bent her brow-bows and shot, he asked what medicine there could be for the wound in his scorched heart, looked back at the doe-eyed maiden, and the spear-bearing lord melted and lost his strength.","n":"His question (என்கொல், line 2) answers her statement two verses back that there is no medicine; the two have now said the same thing without speaking to each other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1965","lb":"CC1965","ev":"Civakan, struck by Vimalai's glance, looks back at her and is overcome.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1966":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1966,"t":["காமக் கடுநோய்க் கனல்சூழ்ந்துடம் பென்னு மற்றிவ்","வீமத்தி னோடு முடனேசுட வேக லாற்றான்","றூமத்தி னார்ந்த துகிலேந்திய வல்கு றாதை","பூமொய்த் திருந்த கடைமேற்புலம் புற்றி ருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"With the fierce fever of desire around him, burning together with this pyre called the body, he could not bear the burning and sat lamenting on the flower-strewn step of the house of the girl's father.","n":"The body is named a pyre (ஈமம்) that burns with the fire of desire; the image is the renouncer's, used here for a lover. Sitting on the father's step (கடை) is, as 1977 will reveal, the sign that fulfils the girl's horoscope.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1966","lb":"CC1966","ev":"Civakan sits down, lovesick, at the step of Vimalai's father's shop.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1967":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1967,"t":["நாவிநோய் செய்த நறுங்குழலா ணாணீலக்","காவிநோய் செய்த கருங்கயற்கட் பூங்கொடியென்","னாவிநோய் செய்த வணங்கென் றறியாதேன்","மேவிநோய் தீர வினாத்தருவா ரில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan laments that he does not know who the goddess is that pains his life, she whose hair shames musk and whose carp eyes shame the blue lily, and that there is no one who will come close and bring him word to end his sickness.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1967","lb":"CC1967","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1968":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1968,"t":["தெண்ணீர்ப் பனிக்கயத்து மட்டவிழ்ந்த தேங்குவளைக்","கண்ணீர்மை காட்டிக் கடல்போ லகன்றவென்","னுண்ணீர்மை யெல்லா மொருநோக்கி னிற்கவர்ந்த","பெண்ணீர்மை மேனாட் பிறந்து மறியுமோ."],"p":"","r":"He asks whether the womanliness that showed the nature of honeyed lily eyes, and with one look stole all his inner nature that was wide as the sea, knew him already in an earlier birth.","n":"மேனாள் பிறந்தும் அறியுமோ: the lover reaches for a former birth to explain how one look could take so much; it is asked, not asserted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","piravi","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1968","lb":"CC1968","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1969":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1969,"t":["கருங்குழலுஞ் செவ்வாயுங் கண்மலருங் காது","மரும்பொழுகு பூண்முலையு மாருயிர்க்கே கூற்றம்","விருந்தினராய் வந்தாரை வெற்றுடலா நோக்கும்","பெருந்திருவி யார்மகள் கொல் பேரியா தாங்கொல்லோ."],"p":"","r":"He says her dark hair, red mouth, flower eyes, ears and jewelled breasts are Death to the dear life of men, she looks at those who come as guests and leaves them empty bodies, and asks whose daughter this woman of great fortune is and what her name is.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1969","lb":"CC1969","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1970":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1970,"t":["வாருடுத்த வெம்முலைய வண்டார்பூங் கோதையைப்","போகொடுத்தார் பெண்ணென்றார் கூற்றமே யென்றிட்டாற்","றாருடுத்த நீண்மார்பர் தம்முயிர்தாம் வேண்டுபவே","னீருடுத்த விந்நகரை நீத்திட டொழியாரோ."],"p":"","r":"He says that those who named the girl of the strap-bound breasts and bee-swarmed garland called her a woman; had they called her Death, would not garlanded men who wanted their lives have left this sea-girt city?","n":"போகொடுத்தார் in line 2 is read as பேர் கொடுத்தார், 'those who gave her a name'; the joke turns on that reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1970","lb":"CC1970","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1971":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1971,"t":["பைங்கண் மணிமகர குண்டலமும் பைந்தோடுந்","திங்கண் முகத்திலங்க்ச் செவ்வா யெயிறிலங்கக்","கொங்குண் குழறாழக் கோட்டெருத்தஞ் செய்தநோக்","கெங்கெங்கே நோக்கினு மங்கங்கே தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"He says the look she cast with her neck turned, her makara earrings and ear-rolls shining on her moon face, her red mouth and teeth shining and her honeyed hair hanging, appears wherever he looks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1971","lb":"CC1971","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1972":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1972,"t":["வாளார் மதிமுகத்த வாளோ வடுப்பிளவோ","தாளார் கழுநீரோ நீலமோ தாமரையோ","நீள்வேலோ வம்போ கயலோ நெடுங்கண்ணோ","கோளார்ந்த கூற்றமோ கொல்வான் றொடங்கினவே."],"p":"","r":"He asks whether her eyes are swords of her bright moon face, or split mango, red lily, blue lily or lotus, long spears, arrows, carp, or long eyes, or Death come full that has begun to kill.","n":"The list runs through the stock likenesses of eyes and ends on Death that has begun to kill (கொல்வான் தொடங்கின), so the last guess is the one that wins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1972","lb":"CC1972","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1973":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1973,"t":["என்றாங்கொன் மாதர் நலமெய்துவ தென்று சிந்தித்","தொன்றார்க் கடந்தான் புலம்புட்கொண் டிருத்த லோடு","மன்றே யமைந்த பசும்பொன்னட ராறு கோடி","குன்றாமல் விற்றான் குளிர்சாகர தத்த னென்பான்."],"p":"","r":"As the conqueror sat lamenting and thinking how he could win the girl's beauty, at that very moment the cool Sagaradattan sold, without any loss, six crores' worth of gold-bright goods that had long lain unsold.","n":"The narrator gives the sale without saying why; the cause, a sign written at Vimalai's birth, is told only by the merchant in 1976-1977.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1973","lb":"CC1973","ev":"While Civakan sits at his step, the merchant Sagaradattan sells six crores' worth of goods at once.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சாகரதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:84"},"cc:1974":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1974,"t":["திருமல்க வந்த திருவேயெனச் சோந்து நாய்கன்","செருமல்கு வேலாய்க் கிடமாலிது வென்று செப்ப","வரிமல்கி வண்டுண் டறைமாமலர்க் கண்ணி மைந்த","னெரிமல்கு செம்பொ னிலமாமனொ டேறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The merchant greets Civakan as Fortune itself come to his house, tells the warrior of the spear that this place is his, and the youth goes up into the red-gold house with the man who is to be his father-in-law.","n":"The narrator already calls the merchant மாமன் (line 4, as divided இல் மாமனொடு) before any word of marriage is spoken: the match is settled in the telling before it is offered.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1974","lb":"CC1974","ev":"Sagaradattan welcomes Civakan as Fortune come in person and takes him up into his golden house.","cl":[],"ag":["சாகரதத்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1975":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1975,"t":["நம்பன் சிறிதே யிடைதந்திது கேட்க நாளு","மம்பொன் னகரு ளமைந்தேன்மற் றெனக்க மைந்தாள்","கம்பம் மிலாதாள் கமலைக்கு விமலை யென்பாள்","செம்பொன் வியக்கு நிறத்தாடிரு வன்ன நீராள்."],"p":"","r":"The merchant asks Civakan to listen a moment: living in this fair golden city, he had by his wife Kamalai a steadfast daughter named Vimalai, whose colour outshines red gold and whose nature is like Fortune's.","n":"கம்பம் இலாதாள் (line 3), 'one without trembling', praises her steadiness before her beauty; the name விமலை, 'the stainless', is set beside her mother's name கமலை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1975","lb":"CC1975","ev":"Sagaradattan tells Civakan of his daughter Vimalai, born to his wife Kamalai.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சாகரதத்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1976":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1976,"t":["பூம்பாவை வந்து பிறந்தாளப் பிறந்த போழ்தே","யாம்பால வெல்லா மறிவாரன் றெழுதி யிட்டார்","தும்பியாது மில்லாக் குளம்போன்றதென் றோமில் பண்டங்","கூம்பாத செல்வக் கொடியேயிது கேண்மோ வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He goes on: when this flower-doll was born, those who know what is to come wrote her fortune that very day, and he bids his hearer listen to what they said of the house and its goods.","n":"Line 3 (தும்பியாதும் இல்லாக் குளம் போன்றது என்று ஓம் இல் பண்டம்) seems to compare the house's stock to a pond, but the construction is obscure as divided; the vocative கூம்பாத செல்வக் கொடியே in line 4 is odd addressed to Civakan and may belong to the quoted prophecy.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1976","lb":"CC1976","ev":"Sagaradattan recalls that at Vimalai's birth the astrologers wrote down her fortune.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சாகரதத்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1977":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1977,"t":["மங்கைக் குரியான் கடையேறும்வந் தேற லோடும்","வங்கந் நிதிய முடன்வீழுமற் றன்றி வீழா","தெங்குந் தனக்கு நிகரில்லவ னேற்ற மார்ப","நங்கைக் கியன்ற நறும்பூவணைப் பள்ளி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The prophecy, as he reports it: the man fit for the girl will come up onto the shop, and when he does the ship-borne goods and stored wealth will all be taken at once; and the chest of that peerless man will be the maiden's fragrant flower-bed.","n":"The sign of the destined husband is a commercial one, fitted to a merchant's house: not a feat of arms or a contest, but goods that would not sell selling the moment he climbs onto the shop.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vaazhkkaithunai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1977","lb":"CC1977","ev":"Sagaradattan repeats the astrologers' sign: the destined husband will sit at the shop and its unsold cargo will sell at once.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சாகரதத்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1978":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1978,"t":["ஏழாண்டின் மேலு மிரண்டாண்டிரண் டெய்தி நின்றாள்","வீழா நிதியு முடன்வீழ்ந்தது வில்வ லாய்க்கே","யூழாயிற் றொல்கு நுசுப்பஃக வுருத்து வீங்கிச்","சூழாரம் வைத்த முலையாணலஞ் சூழ்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Vimalai is now past seven years and two and two more; the wealth that would not sell has sold all at once for the master of the bow; it has come about as fated, so let him take the beauty of the girl with the slender waist and garlanded breasts, the merchant says.","n":"ஊழாயிற்று (line 3) makes the offer of the daughter an act of compliance with destiny rather than the father's choice. The age in line 1 (ஏழாண்டின் மேலும் இரண்டாண்டு இரண்டு) can be summed more than one way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vaazhkkaithunai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1978","lb":"CC1978","ev":"Sagaradattan declares the prophecy fulfilled and offers Vimalai to Civakan.","cl":[["what was foretold at birth has come to pass as destiny, so the marriage must follow","general","reported",3,"sagaradattan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சாகரதத்தன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1979":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1979,"t":["ஏற்ற கைத்தொடி வீழ்ந்தென வேந்தலைத்","தேற்றி னான்றிரு மாநலஞ் செவ்வனே","தோற்ற மாதருந் தோன்றலைக் காண்டலு","மாற்றி னாடன தாவியுந் தாங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"As if a bangle had dropped into an outstretched hand, the merchant duly assured the prince of the girl's great beauty, and the lady, seeing the prince, eased her pain and held on to her life.","n":"The simile in line 1 (ஏற்ற கைத்தொடி வீழ்ந்தென) and the subject of தேற்றினான் are unclear; the reading takes the merchant as the one who reassures.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1979","lb":"CC1979","ev":"Civakan accepts the match, and Vimalai, seeing him, recovers from her lovesickness.","cl":[],"ag":["சாகரதத்தன்","விமலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1980":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1980,"t":["அம்பொற் கொம்பனை யாளையும் வார்கழற்","செம்பொற் குன்றனை யானையுஞ் சீர்பெறப்","பைம்பொ னீணகர்ப் பல்லிய மார்த்தெழ","விம்ப ரில்லாதோ ரின்ப மியற்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"For the woman like a golden branch and the man like a red-gold hill with warrior anklets, the many instruments of the golden city sounded and the people made a joy unmatched on earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1980","lb":"CC1980","ev":"Civakan and Vimalai are married amid the music of the whole city.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1981":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1981,"t":["கட்டி லேறிய காமரு காளையும்","மட்டு வாயவிழ் மாமலர்க் கோதையும்","விட்டு நீங்குத லின்மையின் வீவிலா","ரொட்டி யீருடம் போருயி ராயினார்."],"p":"","r":"The handsome youth who mounted the bed and the lady of the honey-dripping garland never left each other, and so, joined, they became one life in two bodies.","n":"The oneness is given a cause, விட்டு நீங்குதல் இன்மையின், 'because they never parted': the formula holds only while they stay together, which the chapter's end tests.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","uyir","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1981","lb":"CC1981","ev":"Civakan and Vimalai live together as one.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1982":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1982,"t":["நிலவு வெண்கதிர் நீர்மைய பூந்துகில்","கலவங் கண்புதை யாது கனற்றலி","னுலக மூன்று முறுவிலைத் தென்பவே","புலவு வேற்கண்ணி னாண்முலைப் போகமே."],"p":"","r":"The fine cloth like white moonlight and the jewels did not hide her eyes for the burning, and they say the pleasure of the breasts of the woman with spear-like eyes is worth the three worlds.","n":"The valuation is hedged as hearsay (என்பவே, line 3). Line 2 (கலவங் கண்புதையாது கனற்றலின்) is compressed and the sense of the burning is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1982","lb":"CC1982","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1983":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1983,"t":["தேன வாங்கமழ் கண்ணியுந் தெவ்வர்த","மூன வாங்கதிர் வேலுறு காளையுங்","கான வாங்கடி நாறுமென் பள்ளிமேல்","வான வாம்வகை யால்வைகி னார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The lady with the honey-scented garland and the youth with the spear that pierces enemies' flesh stayed on the soft bed fragrant as a forest, in the manner of the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1983","lb":"CC1983","ev":"Civakan and Vimalai spend their days in pleasure on the bridal bed.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1984":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1984,"t":["வெண்மதி நெற்றி தேய்த்து விழுத்தழும் பிருப்ப நீண்ட","வண்ணனன் மாடத் தங்க ணகிற்புகை யமளி யேறிப்","பண்ணமை மகர வீணை நரம்புரீஇப் பாவை பாட","மண்ணமை முழவுத் தோளான் மகிழ்ச்சியுண் மயங்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"In the long fine mansion whose walls bear the scars of rubbing against the white moon, mounting the couch in agil smoke, the doll-like girl tuned the strings of the makara vina and sang, and the man with drum-like shoulders was lost in delight.","n":"Civakan won Gandharvadattai by the vina; here the bride plays and he only listens.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","punarchi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1984","lb":"CC1984","ev":"Vimalai plays the vina and sings for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1985":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1985,"t":["இன்னரிச் சிலம்பொ டேங்கிக் கிண்கிணி யிகலி யார்ப்பப்","பொன்னரி மாலை தாழப் பூஞ்சிகை யவிழ்ந்து சோர","மின்னிருங் கலாபம் வீங்கி மிளிர்ந்துகண் ணிரங்க வெம்பித்","துன்னருங் களிகொள் காமக் கொழுங்கனி சுவைத்து விள்ளான்."],"p":"","r":"With her anklets and girdle-bells crying out against each other, her gold garland hanging, her flowered tresses loosened, her jewelled belt swelling and her eyes melting, he tasted the rich ripe fruit of desire and would not let go.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1985","lb":"CC1985","ev":"Civakan and Vimalai make love.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1986":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1986,"t":["தொழித்துவண் டிமிருங் கோதை துணைமுலை முள்கப் பூம்பட்","டழித்துமட் டொழுகுந் தாரான் மணிவள்ளத் தாய்ந்த தேற","லெழிற்பொலி மாதர்க் கேந்த வினிதினி னுகர்ந்து காமக்","கொழித்திரை கடலுண் மூழ்கிக் கோதைகண் டுயின்ற வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Her garland crushed against his breast and her silk disordered, the man of the honey-dripping garland held up wine in a jewelled cup to the lovely lady, who drank it sweetly and, plunged in the sea of desire, fell asleep.","n":"The epic, Jain by tradition, narrates the drinking of தேறல் in the bridal chamber without comment; no claim about drink is made here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1986","lb":"CC1986","ev":"Civakan offers Vimalai wine from a jewelled cup; she drinks and falls asleep.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1987":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1987,"t":["பாசிலை சுருட்டி மைந்தன் கொடுக்கிய பரந்து மின்னும்","தூசுலா மல்கு றீண்டத் துயிற்கண்கள் விழித்த தோற்றம்","வாசவான் குவளை மெல்ல வாய்விடா நின்ற தொக்கு","மேசுவ தொன்று மில்லா விணைவட முலையி னாட்கே."],"p":"","r":"When the youth, rolling betel leaves for her, touched the silk at her waist, the sleepy eyes of the girl with faultless breasts opened slowly, like a fragrant blue lily opening its mouth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1987","lb":"CC1987","ev":"Civakan wakes Vimalai with betel and a touch.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1988":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1988,"t":["கங்குற்பாற் புகுந்த கள்வ னிவனெனக் கதுப்பிற் றாழ்ந்த","தொங்கலான் முன்கை யாத்துச் சொல்லுநீ வந்த தென்ன","நங்கையான் பசித்து வந்தே னெப்பொரு ணயப்ப தென்றாட்","கங்கலுழ் மேனி யாய்நின் னணிநல வமிழ்த மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Calling him a thief who has crept in by night, she binds his forearm with the garland hanging from her hair and asks why he has come; he says he came hungry, and when she asks what he craves, he answers: the nectar of your beauty.","n":"The arrest is play: the garland is the rope, and the 'thief' confesses a hunger that is a compliment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1988","lb":"CC1988","ev":"Vimalai playfully binds Civakan as a night thief and questions him, and he answers that he hungers for her.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1989":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1989,"t":["செயிர்த்தவள் சிவந்து நோக்கிச் சீறடி சென்னி சோத்தி","யயிர்ப்பதென் பணிசெய் வேனுக் கருளிற்றுப் பொருள தென்ன","வுயிர்ப்பது மோம்பி யொன்று முரையலை யாகி மற்றிப்","பயிர்ப்பில்பூம் பள்ளி வைகு பகட்டெழின் மார்ப வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Feigning anger, she reddens and stares; he sets her small feet on his head and asks why she doubts and what grace she grants her servant; she tells the broad-chested one to hold his breath, say nothing, and stay on the flower-bed.","n":"Line 2 (அயிர்ப்பதென் பணிசெய்வேனுக்கு அருளிற்றுப் பொருளது) is divided obscurely; the reading takes it as Civakan's plea.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","ali","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1989","lb":"CC1989","ev":"Vimalai feigns anger, Civakan bows to her feet, and she bids him be silent and stay.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1990":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1990,"t":["உள்ளிழு துடைய வெம்பி யுற்பல வுருவு கொண்ட","வெள்ளியிற் புனைந்த கோல விளக்கொளி வெறுவி தாக","வள்ளிதழ்க் கோதை வல்லான் வட்டிகை நுதியின் வாங்கிப்","பள்ளிமே லெழுதப் பட்ட பாவைபோ லாயி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"As the ghee within burned down and the light of the lovely lotus-shaped silver lamp grew faint, she became like a figure painted on the bed by a master's brush.","n":"The lamp running dry marks the end of the night that the next verse confirms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1990","lb":"CC1990","ev":"As the lamp burns low, Vimalai lies still as a painted picture.","cl":[],"ag":["விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1991":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1991,"t":["மங்கையர் பண்ணிய மருத யாழ்குழ","னங்கையைப் பிரியுமிந் நம்பி யின்றென","வங்கதற் கிரங்கின வாரும் பேதுறக்","கங்குல்போய் நாட்கடன் கழிந்த தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The marutham-mode lute and flute the women played seemed to grieve that this prince would leave the lady today, so that all were troubled; the night went by and the morning's duties were done.","n":"The music knows what neither lover has said: the parting is announced first by the dawn melody (மருதம்), before Civakan speaks in 1993.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1991","lb":"CC1991","ev":"At dawn the women's music sounds, as if lamenting that Civakan will leave Vimalai.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1992":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1992,"t":["ஏந்துபூங் கோதைக டிருத்தி யேர்படச்","சாந்துகொண் டிளமுலை யெழுதித் தையறன்","காந்தள முகிழ்விரல் கையி னாற்பிடித்","தாய்ந்தவட் கிதுசொலு மலங்கல் வேலினான்."],"p":"","r":"The man of the garlanded spear arranged her raised flower garlands, painted her young breasts with sandal so they shone, took her kanthal-bud fingers in his hand, and spoke to her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1992","lb":"CC1992","ev":"Civakan adorns Vimalai and takes her hand to speak to her.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1993":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1993,"t":["பூவினுட் டாழ்குழற் பொன்செ யேந்தல்குன்","மாவினுட் டாழ்தளிர் மருட்டு மேனியாய்","காவினுட் டோழரைக் கண்டு போதர்வே","னேவினுட் டாழ்சிலை யெறிந்த கோலினே."],"p":"","r":"He tells the woman of flower-hung tresses and body like mango shoots that he will go to the grove to see his friends and come back quicker than an arrow loosed from the bow.","n":"The promise of a return quicker than an arrow is made, but this chapter, the last of the ilampakam, does not show it kept.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","natpu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1993","lb":"CC1993","ev":"Civakan tells Vimalai he will go to see his companions in the grove and return at once.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","விமலை"],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1994":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1994,"t":["என்றவ னுரைத்தலு மெழுது கண்மலர்","நின்றநீ ரிடைமணிப் பாவை நீந்தலின்","மன்றனா றரிவையைத் தெருட்டி மாமணிக்","குன்றனான் கொடியவள் குழைய வேகினான்."],"p":"","r":"When he said this, the pupils of her painted eyes swam in standing tears; he comforted the fragrant woman, and the man like a great jewel-hill went, while she, a creeper, drooped.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1994","lb":"CC1994","ev":"Vimalai weeps; Civakan consoles her and leaves.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","விமலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:85"},"cc:1995":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1995,"t":["வாளி ரண்டு மாறு வைத்த போன்ம ழைக்கண் மாதரார்","நாளி ரண்டு சென்ற வென்று நைய மொய்கொள் காவினுட்","டோளி ரண்டு மன்ன தோழர் தோன்ற லைப்பு ணாந்தபின்","றாளி ரண்டு மேத்தி நின்று தைய னாமம் வேண்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"After two days away, while his brides pined, Civakan meets his companions in the dense grove; they embrace him, praise his feet and ask the name of the woman he has taken.","n":"The opening line's women whose eyes are like two swords set crosswise are the brides who count the two days of his absence, so the ilampakam opens on the cost to the women left behind before it turns to the next one to be won.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1995","lb":"CC1995","ev":"Civakan's companions embrace him in the grove and ask the name of his new bride.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:1996":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1996,"t":["பாடுவண் டிருந்த வன்ன பல்கலை யகலல்குல்","வீடு பெற்ற வரும் வீழும் வெம்மு லைவி மலையென்","றாடு வான ணிந்த சீர ரம்பை யன்ன வாணுத","லூடி னும்பு ணாந்த தொத்தி னியவளு ளாளரோ."],"p":"","r":"Civakan names Vimalai, whose breasts would make even the released fall and who is like Rambha dancing in heaven, and asks whether any woman is sweeter, one who is sweet to join even in a lovers' quarrel.","n":"வீடு பெற்றவரும் வீழும், that even those who have won release would fall for her, is a lover's hyperbole that borrows the language of liberation; the speaker is not named and is taken to be Civakan answering the question of 1995.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","oodal","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1996","lb":"CC1996","ev":"Civakan tells his companions his new bride is Vimalai and boasts of her.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:1997":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1997,"t":["அம்பொ ரைந்து டைய்ய காம னைய்ய னென்ன வந்தண","னம்பு நீர ரல்லர் நன்கு ரங்கு நீர ராயினுந்","தங்கு ரவ்வர் தாங்கொ டுப்பி னெஞ்சு நோந்து தாழ்வர்தாம்","பொங்க ரவ்வ வல்கு லாரெ னப்பு கன்று சொல்லினான்."],"p":"","r":"A Brahmin companion, granting that the lord is Kama with his five arrows, teases that women are not won by trust: whatever their own strength of mind, when their elders give them away they yield, aching at heart.","n":"The speaker is fixed by அந்தணன் at the join of lines 1 and 2 (written வந்தண / னம்பு) and by the sacred thread Civakan names in 2002; the tease deflates the boast of 1996 by crediting the brides' parents rather than the hero.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","varaivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1997","lb":"CC1997","ev":"The Brahmin companion teases Civakan that his brides married him only because their elders gave them.","cl":[["women submit to the husband their elders choose, whether or not their hearts consent","general","reported",3,"anthana_thozhan"]],"ag":["அந்தணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அந்தணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:1998":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1998,"t":["அற்று மன்று கன்னி யம்ம டந்தை மார ணிநலம்","முற்றி னாரை நீடு வைப்பின் மூள்கும் வந்து பாவமுங்","குற்ற மற்று மாகு மென்று கோதை சூழ்ந்து கூறினார்க்","குற்ற டுத்த யாவு யிர்த்தொழிதல் யார்க்கு மொக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Nor is that all, he goes on: parents, saying that sin and fault come upon those who keep a grown maiden long at home, plan her garland and give her, and for anyone so given, to sigh and accept what befalls is the common lot.","n":"பாவம் here is the guardians' sin of delay, not the girl's; the companion uses a household rule to say the hero's eight conquests owed more to custom than to charm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pazhi","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1998","lb":"CC1998","ev":"","cl":[["keeping a grown daughter long unmarried brings sin and fault on her guardians","general","reported",2,"anthana_thozhan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அந்தணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:1999":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":1999,"t":["மதுக்குடம் விரிந்தன மாலை யாரொடும்","புதுக்கடி பொருந்துதி புக்க வூரெலாம்","விதிக்கிடை காணலாம் வீதி மாநகர்","மதிக்கிடை முகத்தியோர் மடந்தை யீண்டையாள்."],"p":"","r":"The companion says Civakan joins in new marriage with honey-garlanded women in every town he enters, but here in the great city, in its streets, is one moon-faced maiden.","n":"விதிக்கிடை காணலாம் is hard to fix: either that what is fated can be seen in the city's streets or that the streets are laid out by rule; the turn from teasing to challenge is clear either way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 1999","lb":"CC1999","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அந்தணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2000":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2000,"t":["ஆடவர் தனதிடத் தருகு போகினு","நாடிமற் றவர்பெயர் நயந்து கேட்பினும்","வீடுவ லுயிரென வெகுளு மற்றவள்","சேடியர் வழிபடச் செல்லுஞ் செல்வியே."],"p":"","r":"If men so much as pass near her quarters, or she hears their names asked after, she rages as though her life would leave her; she goes about with maids attending her as on a goddess.","n":"The companion's account of Suramanjari's horror of men's names restates the vow she made after the powder contest, now become city talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2000","lb":"CC2000","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அந்தணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2001":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2001,"t":["காமனே செல்லினுங் கனன்று காண்கிலாள்","வேமெனக் குடம்பெனும் வேங்கொ டோளியை","யேமுறுத் தவணல நுகரி னெந்தையை","யாமெலா மநங்கமா திலக னென்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Even if Kama himself came she would flare up and not look, saying her body would burn; if you frighten that bamboo-shouldered girl and enjoy her beauty, all of us will hail our lord as Ananga-mathilakan, the crown of Kama.","n":"ஏமுறுத்து, to frighten or overpower, makes the dare one of conquest rather than courtship, and the title offered, அநங்கமா திலகன், puts the prize in renown among the companions.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2001","lb":"CC2001","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அந்தணன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2002":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2002,"t":["தாசியர் முலைக டாக்கத் தளையவிழ்ந் துடைந்த தண்டார்","வாசங்கொண் டிலங்கு முந்நூல் வலம்படக் கிடந்த மார்ப","பேசிய பெயரி னாளைப் பேதுறா தொழிவே னாகி","னாசுமன் பிலாத புன்பெண் கூந்தல்யா னணைவ லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan answers the companion whose chest bears the sacred thread among the garlands crushed by courtesans' breasts: if he does not bewilder the girl of the name just spoken, may he touch the hair of a base woman without love.","n":"The oath's penalty is a self-curse of disgrace: to have to touch a loveless low woman's hair. The addressee's முந்நூல் (sacred thread) is what marks the teasing companion as a Brahmin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sool","maanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2002","lb":"CC2002","ev":"Civakan swears to win Suramanjari, staking his honour on it.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அந்தணன்"],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2003":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2003,"t":["வண்டுதேன் சிலைகொ ணாணா மாந்தளிர் மலர்க ளம்பாக்","கொண்டவன் கோட்டந் தன்னுட் கொடியினைக் கொணர்ந்து நீல","முண்டது காற்றி யாண்பே ரூட்டுவ லுருவக் காமன்","கண்டபொற் படிவஞ் சார்ந்து கரந்திரு நாளை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He tells them to bring the creeper-like girl into the shrine of Kama, whose bow is cane strung with bees and whose arrows are mango shoots and flowers, where he will make her take in a man's name, hiding tomorrow beside the golden image of Kama.","n":"ஆண்பேர் ஊட்டுவல, 'I will feed her a man's name', answers her refusal to hear men's names (2000); line 3's நீலம் உண்டது காற்றி is obscure, and whether he or the friends are to hide (கரந்திரு) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kaamam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2003","lb":"CC2003","ev":"Civakan lays a plan to meet Suramanjari at the shrine of Kama.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2004":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2004,"t":["இழைக்கண் வெம்முலை யிட்டிடை யேந்தல்குன்","மழைக்கண் மாதரை மாலுறு நோய்செய்வான்","முழைக்கண் வாளரி யேறன மொய்ம்பினா","னுழைக்க ணாளர்க் குரைத்தெழுந் தானரோ."],"p":"","r":"Strong as a maned lion in its cave, he told his keen-eyed friends his intent and rose to go, meaning to bring love-sickness on the rain-eyed maiden of the jewelled breasts and slender waist.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2004","lb":"CC2004","ev":"Civakan leaves his companions to set his plan in motion.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2005":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2005,"t":["சோருங் காரிகை யாள்சுர மஞ்சரி","யாரஞ் சூடிய வம்முலைப் பூந்தடந்","தாகு மார்பமுந் தண்ணெனத் தோய்வதற்","கோரு முள்ள முடன்றெழு கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan feels his mind rise in turmoil, longing to bathe coolly in the breasts and chest, hung with pearl strings, of the swooning beauty Suramanjari.","n":"The hunter is already caught: the verse turns from the dare to his own unrest before he has seen her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2005","lb":"CC2005","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2006":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2006,"t":["கடைந்தபொற் செப்பெனக் கதிர்த்து வீங்கின","வடஞ்சுமந் தெழுந்தன மாக்கண் வெம்முலை","மடந்தைதன் முகத்தவென் மனத்தி னுள்ளன","குடங்கையி னெடியன குவளை யுண்கணே."],"p":"","r":"He pictures her breasts swelling like burnished gold caskets beneath their chains and her blue-lily eyes, longer than a cupped palm, as things now dwelling in his mind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2006","lb":"CC2006","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2007":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2007,"t":["ஏத்தரு மல்லிகை மாலை யேந்திய","பூத்தலைக் கருங்குழற் புரியி னாற்புறம்","யாத்துவைத் தலைக்குமிவ் வகுளி லாணலங்","காய்த்தியென் மனத்தினைக் கலக்கு கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Her dark flower-crowned hair, bound behind with a jasmine garland, rouses his male beauty and stirs his mind.","n":"Line 3's இவ் வகுளில் is not clear as written; the sense of a beauty that agitates him is secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2007","lb":"CC2007","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2008":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2008,"t":["சில்லரிக் கிண்கிணி சிலம்புஞ் சீறடிச்","செல்விதன் றிருநலஞ் சேரும் வாயிறா","னல்லலங் கிழவனோ ரந்த ணாளனாய்ச்","செல்லல்யான் றெளிதாக வுடைத்தென் றெண்ணினான்."],"p":"","r":"He reckons that the way to the gate of her beauty is easy: he, lord of distress, will go as a Brahmin.","n":"அல்லலங் கிழவன், the lord of distress, is his self-mocking name for a man in love; the disguise borrows the one status her ban does not fear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vanjam","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2008","lb":"CC2008","ev":"Civakan resolves to disguise himself as a Brahmin to enter Suramanjari's house.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2009":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2009,"t":["அணங்கர வுரித்ததோ லனைய மேனியன்","வணங்குநோன் சிலையென வளைந்த யாக்கையன்","பிணங்குநூன் மார்பினன் பெரிதொர் பொத்தக","முணர்ந்துமூப் பெழுதின தொப்பத் தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"He appears with skin like a snake's slough, body bent like a bowed strong bow, a tangled thread across his chest, as if he had studied a great book and written old age out of it.","n":"The image of old age copied out from a book makes the disguise an act of learning: the youth reproduces decay by rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","yaakkai","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2009","lb":"CC2009","ev":"Civakan takes on the guise of a decrepit old Brahmin.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2010":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2010,"t":["வெண்ணரை யுடம்பினன் விதிர்த்த புள்ளிய","னுண்ணவி ரறுலைய னொசிந்த நோக்கினன்","கண்ணவிர் குடையினன் கைத்தண் டூன்றினன்","பெண்ணலங் காதலிற் பேயு மாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"White-haired and blotched, with trembling flesh and dimmed gaze, carrying an umbrella and leaning on a staff, he became even a ghost for love of a woman's beauty.","n":"The narrator's பேயும் ஆயினான் is comic, but it names the price: the hero's own beauty is given up for desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","mayal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2010","lb":"CC2010","ev":"Civakan completes his old man's guise with umbrella and staff.","cl":[["love of a woman's beauty makes a man take on even a ghastly form","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2011":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2011,"t":["யாப்புடை யாழ்மிட றென்னுந் தோட்டியாற்","றூப்புடை யவணலந் தொடக்கும் பாகனாய்","மூப்பெனு முகபடாம் புதைந்து முற்றிழை","காப்புடை வளநகர் காளை யெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"As a mahout who would tame the elephant of her beauty with the goad of his lute-like throat, hidden under the face-cloth of old age, the young bull reached her guarded, rich mansion.","n":"Old age is a முகபடாம், the cloth over an elephant's face, so the disguise is itself an elephant's trapping while she is the elephant to be goaded by song: the elephant image is used on both sides.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2011","lb":"CC2011","ev":"Civakan in disguise reaches Suramanjari's guarded house.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2012":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2012,"t":["தண்டுவலி யாகநனி தாழ்ந்துதளர்ந் தேங்கிக்","கண்டுகடை காவலர்கள் கழறமுக நோக்கிப்","பண்டையிளங் காலுவப்பன் பாலடிசி லிந்நாட்","கண்டுநயந் தார்தருவ காதலிப்ப னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Leaning on his staff, sinking and panting, seen and rebuked by the gatekeepers, he looks into their faces and says that in his youth he loved milk-rice and today he longs for what kindly people give.","n":"The plea is double: the old man's craving for milk-rice stands for the young man's craving for the girl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","iravu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2012","lb":"CC2012","ev":"The disguised Civakan begs the gatekeepers for milk-rice.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","காவலர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","காவலர்"],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2013":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2013,"t":["கையிற்றொழு தார்கழிய மூப்பிற்செவி கேளார்","மையலவர் போலமனம் பிறந்தவகை சொன்னார்","பையநடக் கென்றுபசிக் கிரங்கியவர் விடுத்தார்","தொய்யின் முலை யவர்கள் கடைத் தோன்றனனி புக்கான்"],"p":"","r":"Those who saluted him with joined hands passed on; deaf with age, he talked like a madman as his mind moved him, and the guards, pitying his hunger, let him go with 'walk slowly', and he entered the women's gate.","n":"The ban on men is undone by the guards' own compassion for hunger; feigned deafness lets him ignore their rebukes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","arul","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2013","lb":"CC2013","ev":"The gatekeepers, pitying the old Brahmin's hunger, let him into the women's quarters.","cl":[],"ag":["காவலர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2014":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2014,"t":["கோதையொடு தாழ்ந்துகுழல் பொங்கிஞிமி றார்ப்ப","வோதமணி மாலையொடு பூண்பிறழ வோடி","யேதமிது போமினென வென்னுமுரை யீயா","னூதவுகு தன்மையினொ டொல்கியுற நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The women run up, hair loosened under garlands with bees buzzing, pearl strings and ornaments swinging, crying that this is a fault and he must go; he gives no answer and stands swaying as if about to fall.","n":"ஊதவுகு தன்மையின், as if he would drop at a breath, is the guise kept up under pressure; the exact sense of the phrase is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2014","lb":"CC2014","ev":"Suramanjari's women rush out to drive off the intruder, who stands silent and swaying.","cl":[],"ag":["சேடியர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2015":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2015,"t":["கச்சுவிசித் தியாத்தகதிர் முலையர்மணி யயில்வா","ணச்சுநுனை யம்புசிலை நடுங்கவுட னேந்தி","யச்சமுறுத் தமதுபுளித் தாங்குத்தம தீஞ்சொல்","வெச்சென்றிடச் சொல்லிவிரி கோதையவர் சூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The women, breasts bound in bodices, take up jewelled swords, sharp spears, arrows and bows that tremble in their hands, and, turning their sweet words sour and hot to frighten him, surround him.","n":"The weapons tremble (நடுங்க) in the women's hands, so the threat is half display; the image of sweetness turned sour (அமது புளித்தாங்கு) is of milk going off.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2015","lb":"CC2015","ev":"Suramanjari's armed women surround the old man and threaten him.","cl":[],"ag":["சேடியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2016":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2016,"t":["பாவமிது நோவவுரை யன்மின் முதுபார்ப்பார்","சாவர்தொடி னேகடிது கண்டவகை வண்ண","மோவியர்தம் பாவையினொ டொப்பரிய நங்கை","யேவல்வகை கண்டறிது மென்றுசிலர் சொன்னார்."],"p":"","r":"Some of them say: this is sin, do not speak to hurt him; old Brahmins die if they are touched; let us learn the command of our lady, beyond compare with the painters' dolls, about what we have seen.","n":"The Brahmin's protected status, the very thing Civakan chose the guise for (2008), now works as planned: it turns the guards' anger into scruple and carries the decision to Suramanjari herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","kolai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2016","lb":"CC2016","ev":"Some of the women restrain the rest and resolve to ask Suramanjari what to do with the old Brahmin.","cl":[["to wound an old Brahmin, even by harsh words, is sin","general","reported",1,"suramanjari_sediyar"]],"ag":["சேடியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சேடியர்",""],"ch":"cc:86"},"cc:2017":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2017,"t":["கையவளை மையகுழ லையரிய வாட்க","ணையுமிடை வெய்யமுலை நங்கையொரு பார்ப்பா","னுய்வதில னூழின்முது மூப்பினொடும் வந்தான்","செய்வதுரை நொய்தினெனச் சேறுமெழு கென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"A maid tells the lady that a brahmin with no means of living has come, brought by fate in extreme old age, and urges her to rise and come, saying it is easy to grant what he asks.","n":"The brahmin is Civakan in the old man's guise taken in the previous verses; the maid's ஊழின் (line 3), 'by fate', makes his age and poverty his lot, the first of many words in the scene that are true of the disguise and false of the man. The epithets of line 1 to 2 may describe the maid or the lady addressed, so who speaks to whom is a reading from context.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","mooppu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2017","lb":"CC2017","ev":"A maid announces to Suramanjari that an aged brahmin has come to the house.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2018":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2018,"t":["மாலைபல தாழ்ந்துமதுப் பிலிற்றிமணங் கமழுங்","கோலவகிற் றேய்வைகொழுஞ் சாந்தமுலை மெழுகிப்","பாலைமணி யாழ்மழலை பசும்பொனிலத் திழிவாள்","சோலைவரை மேலிழியுந் தோகைமயி லொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Garlanded, dripping honey, her breasts smeared with agil and thick sandal paste, speaking with the lisp of a jewelled lute in the paalai mode, she comes down from the golden upper floor like a peahen descending a mountain grove.","n":"She has kept to the upper storey since her vow to look on no man; this descent toward a male visitor is the first breach of the seclusion, made safe in her eyes by his age.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2018","lb":"CC2018","ev":"Suramanjari descends from the upper floor of her mansion.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2019":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2019,"t":["சீறடிய கிண்கிணிசி லம்பொடுசி லம்ப","வேறுபடு மேகலைகண் மெல்லெனமி ழற்றச்","சேறுபடு கோதைமிசை வண்டுதிசை பாட","நாறுமலர்க் கொம்பர்நடை கற்பதென வந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her small feet's anklet bells and anklets chiming, her girdles murmuring softly, bees singing over her garland, she comes as if a fragrant flowering branch were learning to walk.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2019","lb":"CC2019","ev":"Suramanjari walks out to meet the visitor.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2020":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2020,"t":["வந்தவர வென்னையென வாட்கண்மட வாய்கேள்","சிந்தைநலி கின்றதிரு நீர்க்குமரி யாட","வந்திலதி னாயபய னென்னைமொழி கென்றாண்","முந்திநலி கின்றமுது மூப்பொழிவு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"She asks why he has come; he tells the sword-eyed maiden that he means to bathe in the sacred waters of Kumari, which trouble his mind, and when she asks what gain comes of it he says, the end of the old age that afflicts him.","n":"The verse is a dialogue (her என்றாள் line 3, his என்றான் line 4). The claim is feigned: a youth asks to be cured of an age he does not have, and the story laughs at it through Suramanjari in the next two verses rather than refuting it in argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","mooppu","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2020","lb":"CC2020","ev":"Suramanjari asks the old brahmin his errand, and he says he is going to bathe at Kumari to be rid of old age.","cl":[["bathing in the sacred waters of Kumari takes away old age","general","reported",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2021":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2021,"t":["நறவிரிய நாறுகுழ லாள்பெரிது நக்குப்","பிறருமுள ரோபெறுநர் பேணிமொழி கென்னத்","துறையறிந்து சேர்ந்துதொழு தாடுநரி லென்றாற்","கறிதிர்பிற நீவிரென வையமிலை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The lady with honey-scented hair laughs aloud and asks him to say truly whether there are others who have gained this, and he says that among those who know the ford, go there, worship and bathe, they will see others, without doubt.","n":"Her laugh (பெரிது நக்கு, line 1) is the hinge of the trick: once the old man is ridiculous he is harmless, and the woman who vowed to see no man will now serve him with her own hands.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","mooppu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2021","lb":"CC2021","ev":"Suramanjari laughs and asks if anyone has been made young by the bath; he insists that some have.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2022":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2022,"t":["செத்தமர மொய்த்தமழை யாற்பெயரு மென்பார்","பித்தரிவ குற்றபிணி தீர்த்துமென வெண்ணி","யத்தமென மிக்கசுட ரங்கதிர்சு ருக்கு","மொய்த்தமணி மாடமிசை யத்தகவ டைந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Thinking that those who say a dead tree swarmed by rain will sprout again are mad, and that she will at least cure the ailment this man has, she goes up to the jewelled mansion whose crowded gems shrink the sun's bright rays.","n":"Her scepticism is sound common sense, and it is the reason the disguise holds: what she is sure cannot happen is exactly what, in the story's terms, is about to happen before her eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mooppu","pedhaimai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2022","lb":"CC2022","ev":"Suramanjari dismisses the hope of youth regained and resolves to relieve the old man's present want.","cl":[["old age cannot be undone, as a dead tree cannot be revived by rain","general","reported",1,"suramanjari"]],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2023":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2023,"t":["வடிவமிது மூப்பளிது வார்பவள வல்லிக்","கடிகைதுவர் வாய்கமலங் கண்ணொடடி வண்ணங்","கொடிதுபசி கூர்ந்துளது கோல்வளையி னீரே","யடிசில்கடி தாக்கியிவ ணேகொணர்மி னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She says that this is his form and his old age is pitiable, notes the coral-creeper mouth and the lotus hue of eyes and feet, says he is cruelly hungry, and tells the bangled maids to make food quickly and bring it here.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 list a red mouth and lotus-coloured eyes and feet in a man just called pitifully old: the youth shows through the disguise, and she sees it without reading it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","pasi","mooppu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2023","lb":"CC2023","ev":"Suramanjari orders her maids to cook food at once for the hungry old man.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","கோல்வளையினீர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுரமஞ்சரி","கோல்வளையினீர்"],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2024":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2024,"t":["நானமுரைத் தாங்குநறு நீரவனை யாட்டி","மேனிகிளர் வெண்டுகிலும் விழுப்பொனிய னூலும்","பானலங்கொ டீங்கிளவி பவித்திரமு நல்கத்","தானமர்ந்து தாங்கியமை தவிசின்மிசை யிருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Rubbing him with fragrant powder and bathing him in scented water, the sweet-voiced one gives him a gleaming white cloth, a sacred thread of fine gold and the darbha ring, and he sits down at ease on the seat set for him.","n":"The gifts are the marks of the brahmin (நூல், பவித்திரம்): her hospitality completes the disguise she is taken in by.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2024","lb":"CC2024","ev":"The old brahmin is bathed, dressed in white with thread and ring, and seated.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2025":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2025,"t":["திங்கணலஞ் சூழ்ந்ததிரு மீன்களெனச் செம்பொற்","பொங்குகதிர் மின்னுபுகழ்க் கலங்கள்பல பரப்பி","யிங்குசுவை யின்னமுத மேந்தமிகு சான்றோ","னெங்குமிலை யின்னசுவை யென்றுடன யின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Spreading many golden vessels bright as the stars about the moon, the women bring sweet food like nectar, and the great worthy one eats, saying there is no such taste anywhere.","n":"மிகு சான்றோன் (line 3) is said with a smile: the honour paid to an old man of learning falls on a young lover in disguise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2025","lb":"CC2025","ev":"The guest is served on golden vessels and eats with praise for the food.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கோல்வளையினீர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2026":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2026,"t":["தமிழ்தழிய சாயலவர் தங்குமலர்த் தூநீ","ருமிழ்கரக மேந்தவுர வோனமர்ந்து பூசி","யமிழ்தனைய பஞ்சமுக வாசமமைத் தாய்ந்த","கமழ்திரையுங் காட்டவவை கொண்டுகவு ளடுத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Women whose grace is entwined with Tamil hold a vessel pouring pure flower-water; the strong one sits and rinses, and when they show him betel leaf with the five fragrances prepared like nectar he takes it into his cheek.","n":"தமிழ் தழிய சாயலவர் (line 1), women whose softness embraces Tamil, uses the language's name as a word for sweetness; உரவோன், 'the strong one', slips past the old man's frailty to the hero underneath.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2026","lb":"CC2026","ev":"After the meal the guest rinses his mouth and is given betel.","cl":[],"ag":["கோல்வளையினீர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2027":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2027,"t":["வல்லதெனை யென்னமறை வல்லன்மட வாயா","னெல்லையெவ னென்னப்பொரு ளெய்திமுடி காறுஞ்","சொல்லுமினு நீவிர்கற்ற காலமெனத் தேன்சோர்","சில்லென்கிளிக் கிளவியது சிந்தையில னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"She asks what he is skilled in; he says he is master of the Veda; asked how far, he says to the meaning and the very end; asked how long he studied, he tells the honey-voiced, parrot-speaking maiden that he does not remember.","n":"The last answer is the joke: the man who claims the Veda to its end has no years of study to show, because the years are false.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","vesham","mooppu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2027","lb":"CC2027","ev":"Suramanjari questions the brahmin about his learning, and he claims the whole Veda but cannot say when he learned it.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2028":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2028,"t":["இன்னவர்க ளில்லைநிலத் தென்றுவியந் தேத்தி","யன்னமன மென்னடையி னாளமர்ந்து நோக்கப்","பின்னையிவள் போகுதிறம் பேசுமென வெண்ணித்","தன்னஞ்சிறி தேதுயின்று தாழவவ ணக்காள்."],"p":"","r":"Marvelling that there are no such men on earth, she praises him and sits gazing, and he, thinking that she will next speak of his leaving, nods off a little and droops.","n":"Line 4 is hard to divide; the sense that his sleep is feigned to forestall dismissal follows from line 3 (போகு திறம் பேசும் என எண்ணி) and the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2028","lb":"CC2028","ev":"Suramanjari praises the old man; he feigns drowsiness so as not to be sent away.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2029":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2029,"t":["கோலமணி வாய்க்குவளை வாட்கண்மட வாளைச்","சாலமுது மூப்புடைய சாமிமுக நோக்கிக்","காலுமிக நோஞ்சிறிது கண்ணுந்துயில் குற்றே","னேலங்கமழ் கோதையிதற் கென்செய்குரை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Looking at the maiden of the lily eyes, the very old master says his legs ache badly and his eyes are heavy with sleep, and asks the lady of the cardamom-scented garland what she will do for it.","n":"சாமி, 'master', is the title the epic gives Civakan himself (சீவகசாமி); applied to the old brahmin it is a quiet signal of who he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mooppu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2029","lb":"CC2029","ev":"The old brahmin complains of weariness and asks her for a place to rest.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2030":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2030,"t":["மட்டுவிரி கோதைமது வார்குழலி னாடன்","பட்டுநிணர் கட்டில்பரி வின்றியுறை கென்றா","ளிட்டவணை மேலினிது மெல்லெனவ சைந்தான்","கட்டழல்செய் காமக்கட லைக்கடைய லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The lady of the honey-dripping garland tells him to rest without care on the couch spread with silk; he lies down gently on the bed laid for him, and begins to churn an ocean of desire that burns like fierce fire.","n":"Line 4 tears the disguise for the reader: the frail guest in the woman's own chambers is a lover, and the house she guarded against men now has one inside it by her own leave.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2030","lb":"CC2030","ev":"Suramanjari gives the old man a silk couch in her mansion, and he lies on it burning with desire.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2031":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2031,"t":["காலையொடு தாழ்ந்துகதிர் பட்டதுக லங்கி","மாலையொடு வந்துமதி தோன்றமகிழ் தோன்றி","வேலனைய கண்ணியர்தம் வீழ்துணைவர் திண்டோள்","கோலமுலை யாலெழுதக் கூடியதை யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The day sinks, the sun sets in gloom, evening comes, the moon rises and joy with it, and the spear-eyed women join their beloved husbands, painting their strong shoulders with their breasts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2031","lb":"CC2031","ev":"Night falls over the city and the women go to their husbands.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","துணைவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2032":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2032,"t":["ஏந்துமலர்ச் சேக்கையகில் வளர்த்தவிடு புகையு","மாய்ந்தமலர்க் கோதையமிர் துயிர்க்குநறும் புகையுங்","கூந்தலகிற் புகையுந்துகிற் கொழுமெனறும் புகையும்","வாய்ந்தவரை மழையினுயர் மாடத்தெழுந் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Agil smoke raised on flowered beds, the scented smoke breathing from wilted garlands, the agil smoke of hair and the fragrant smoke of perfumed cloth all rise from mansions taller than cloud-capped hills.","n":"Four kinds of smoke (புகை) in four lines: the night's lovemaking given by scent alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2032","lb":"CC2032","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2033":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2033,"t":["ஆசிலடு பாலமிர்தஞ் சிறியவயின் றம்பூங்","காசில்படி மாலைகல நொய்யமதி கவற்குந்","தூசுநறுஞ் சாந்தினிய தோடிவைக டாங்கி","மாசின்மட வார்கண்மணி வீணைநரம் புளர்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Having eaten a little of the faultless milk-food, wearing light garlands, ear ornaments, fine cloth and fragrant sandal, the spotless maidens pluck the strings of the jewelled vina.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2033","lb":"CC2033","ev":"The young women of the city eat lightly, dress, and play the vina.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2034":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2034,"t":["பூஞ்சதங்கை மாலைபுகழ்க் குஞ்சிப்பொரு வில்லார்","வீங்குதிர டோளுந்தட மார்பும்விரை மெழுகித்","தீங்கரும்பு மென்றனைய வின்பவளச் செவ்வாய்த்","தேங்கொளமிர் தார்ந்துசெழுந் தார்குழையச் சோந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Peerless men with garland-crowned hair smear their strong shoulders and broad chests with fragrance, drink the nectar of coral lips sweet as sugarcane, and swoon as their rich garlands are crushed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2034","lb":"CC2034","ev":"The city's men lie with their women through the night.","cl":[],"ag":["துணைவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2035":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2035,"t":["பொன்னறையு ளின்னமளிப் பூவணையின் மேலான்","முன்னியதன் மன்றலது முந்துறமு டிப்பான்","மன்னுமொரு கீதமது ரம்படமு ரன்றாற்","கின்னமிர்த மாகவிளை யாருமது கேட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"On the flower couch in the golden chamber he, to bring on first the wedding he intends, hums a melody sweetly, and all who hear it take it as nectar.","n":"முன்னிய தன் மன்றல் (line 2): the song is a means to the marriage. The city's night of lovers (2031 to 2034) frames a house where the one man present is still playing the old man.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["isai","vathuvai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2035","lb":"CC2035","ev":"At night the old brahmin sings softly in the mansion, and the household listens.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கோல்வளையினீர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2036":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2036,"t":["மன்ம தன்ம ணிக்கு ரன்ம ருட்டு மென்று மால்கொள்வா","ரின்ன தின்றி யக்க ரின்னி யக்க வந்த தென்றுதம்","பின்னு முன்னு நோக்கு வார் பேது சால வெய்துவார்","கன்னி தன்ம னத்தி ழைத்த காளை நாமம் வாழ்த்துவார்."],"p":"","r":"Wondering whether it is the voice of the god of love's jewel bell, that nothing like it exists, whether it is the music of the yakshas, they look behind and before, are deeply bewildered, and praise the name of the youth the maiden has wrought into her mind.","n":"கன்னி தன் மனத்து இழைத்த காளை (line 4): the youth she has fashioned in her mind is Civakan, for whom alone she said she would break her seclusion; the song is recognised by its likeness to him before anyone guesses that it is him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2036","lb":"CC2036","ev":"The women of the house, bewildered by the song, are reminded of Civakan and praise his name.","cl":[],"ag":["கோல்வளையினீர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2037":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2037,"t":["கொம்பி னொத்தொ துங்கி யுங்கு ழங்கன் மாலை தாங்கியு","மம்பி னொத்த கண்ணி னார டிக்க லம்ம ரற்றவுந்","நம்பி தந்த கீதமேந யந்து காண வோடினார்","வெம்பு வேட்கை வேனி லானின் வேற லானு மாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"Shrinking like branches, wearing curled garlands, the arrow-eyed women run to see, their anklets crying out, longing for the song the noble one gave, and he becomes none other than the god of spring whose desire burns.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2037","lb":"CC2037","ev":"The women run toward the singer, and the old man seems to them the god of love.","cl":[],"ag":["கோல்வளையினீர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2038":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2038,"t":["தாம மாலை வார்கு ழற்ற டங்க ணார்க்கி டங்கழி","காம னன்ன காளை தன்க ருத்தொ டொத்த தாகலான்","மாம லர்த்தெ ரியலான்ம ணிமிடற்றி டைக்கிடந்த","சாம கீத மற்று மொன்று சாமி நன்கு பாடினான்."],"p":"","r":"Because it matched the thought of the long-eyed women with garlanded hair, the youth like Kama, the master, sang well another Sama chant that lay in his jewelled throat.","n":"சாம கீதம் (line 4): the song of the brahmin's own Veda keeps up the disguise while doing the lover's work; the verse calls the singer காமன் அன்ன காளை and சாமி in one breath.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","vedham","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2038","lb":"CC2038","ev":"The disguised Civakan sings a second Sama melody for the women.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:87"},"cc:2039":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2039,"t":["கள்ள மூப்பி னந்த ணன்க னிந்தகீத வீதியே","வள்ளி வென்ற நுண்ணி டைம ழைம லர்த்த டங்கணார்","புள்ளு வம்ம திம்ம கன்புணர்த்த வோசை மேற்புகன்","றுள்ளம் வைத்த மாம யிற்கு ழாத்தி னோடி யெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the ripe song that the falsely aged Brahmin sends down the street, the wide-eyed maidens of Suramanjari's mansion run to it like a flock of peacocks, their hearts set on the sound.","n":"The narrator names the disguise at the outset (கள்ள மூப்பு, a false old age) and calls the singer's art புள்ளுவம், trickery, so the reader is never in doubt that the music is bait.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2039","lb":"CC2039","ev":"Suramanjari's maidens run toward the disguised Civakan's singing.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழிமார்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2040":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2040,"t":["இனிச்சிறி தெழுந்து வீங்கி யிட்டிடை கோறு நாங்க","ளெனக்கொறு கொறுப்ப போலு மிளமுலைப் பரவை யல்குற்","கனிப்பொறை மலிந்த காமர் கற்பக மணிக்கொம் பொப்பாள்","பனிப்பிறைப் பூணி னான்றன் பாண்வலைச் சென்று பட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"Suramanjari herself, a gem-branch of the wishing tree whose young breasts seem to grumble that a little more swelling will break her slender waist, goes and falls into the singer's net of song.","n":"பாண்வலை, the net of song, makes the woman who shut out men the quarry of a hunt; she goes to it on her own feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2040","lb":"CC2040","ev":"Suramanjari is drawn to the singer.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2041":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2041,"t":["அடிக்கல மரற்ற வல்குற் கலைகலந் தொலிப்ப வந்து","முடிப்பதென் பெரிது மூத்தேன் முற்றிழை யரிவை யென்ன","வடிக்கணா ணக்கு நாணித் தோழியை மறைந்து மின்னுக்","கொடிக்குழாத் திடையோர் கோலக் குளிர்மணிக் கொம்பி னின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She comes with anklets crying and girdle sounding, and when the old man says that he is very old and can do nothing, she laughs, is ashamed, and hides behind her maid among the throng of women.","n":"The disclaimer முடிப்பதென் பெரிது மூத்தேன் is the disguise talking: by protesting his age he lets her come near a man without breaking her vow in her own eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","vesham","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2041","lb":"CC2041","ev":"Suramanjari arrives; the old man disclaims his age, and she laughs and hides behind her maid.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2042":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2042,"t":["இளையவற் காணின் மன்னோ வென்செய்வீர் நீவி ரென்ன","விளைமதுக் கண்ணி மைந்தர் விளிகெனத் தோழி கூற","முலையெயிற் றிவளை யாரு மொழிந்தன ரில்லை யென்றோ","வுளைவது பிறிது முண்டோ வொண்டொடி மாதர்க் கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The old man asks what they would do if they saw a young man; the maid answers 'may honey-garlanded young men perish!'; and he asks whether the lady grieves because no one has spoken to her, or for some other cause.","n":"His second question offers the lady a softer cause for her grief, that no one has courted her, in place of the anger she professes: he is testing whether the hatred is wounded pride.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","igal","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2042","lb":"CC2042","ev":"The disguised Civakan probes the maid about the lady's hatred of men, and the maid curses young men.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தோழி"],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2043":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2043,"t":["வாய்ந்தவிம் மாதர் சுண்ணஞ் சீவகன் பழித்த பின்றைக்","காய்ந்தன ளென்று கூறக் காளைமற் றிவட்குத் தீயான்","மாய்ந்தனன் போலு மென்ன மாதரா ரொருங்கு வாழ்த்தி","யாய்ந்தன மைய னுய்ந்தா னறிந்தன மதனை யென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The maid explains that the lady has been angry ever since Civakan disparaged her powder; the old man says that young man must be as good as dead to her; and the women bless him together, saying they have weighed it and know the lord came through.","n":"Civakan, unrecognised, pronounces his own sentence (தீயான் மாய்ந்தனன் போலும்). Line 4 (ஆய்ந்தன மைய னுய்ந்தா னறிந்தன மதனை) is hard to construe; whether ஐயன் is the old man or Civakan, and in what sense he 'escaped', is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["igal","maanam","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2043","lb":"CC2043","ev":"The maid names the powder verdict as the cause of the lady's anger, and the disguised Civakan condemns the young man who gave it.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி","சீவகன்","தோழிமார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழிமார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2044":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2044,"t":["காலுற்ற காம வல்லிக் கொடியெனக் கலங்கி நங்கை","மாலுற்று மயங்க யாங்கண் மடக்கிளி தூது விட்டேஞ்","சேலுற்ற நெடுங்கட் செவ்வாய்த் தத்தைதன் செல்வங் கண்டே","பாலுற்ற பவளச் செவ்வாய்த் தத்தையாற் பரிவு தீர்ந்தேம்."],"p":"","r":"The maids say that when their lady was shaken like a love-creeper in the wind and bewildered, they sent a young parrot as messenger, and that by a parrot-mouthed one, having seen Tattai's good fortune, their distress was eased.","n":"தத்தை means both a parrot and Gandharvadattai, Civakan's first bride; the verse plays on both and I cannot fix who sent which messenger or whose distress ended. It may glance at Gunamalai's parrot messenger to Civakan earlier in the book.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2044","lb":"CC2044","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தோழிமார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழிமார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2045":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2045,"t":["அன்பொட்டி யெமக்கோர் கீதம் பாடுமி னடித்தி யாரு","முன்பட்ட தொழிந்து நுங்கண் மூகவியர் முனிவு தீர்ந்தார்","பொன்றெட்டேம் யாமு நும்மைப் போகொட்டோம் பாடல் கேளா","தென்பட்டு விடினு மென்றா ரிலங்குபூங் கொம்பொ டொப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The flower-branch women beg the old man to sing them a song out of kindness, saying the serving women have forgotten what passed and dropped their anger, and that whatever happens they will not let him go without hearing it.","n":"பொன்றெட்டேம் (line 3) is obscure as divided; the reading of it is left open. The women who shut men out now refuse to let a man leave.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2045","lb":"CC2045","ev":"The women press the old man to sing.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழிமார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழிமார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2046":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2046,"t":["பாடுதும் பாவை பொற்பே பற்றிமற் றெமக்கு நல்கி","னாடமைத் தோளி னீரஃ தொட்டுமேற் கேண்மி னென்ன","நாடியார் பேயைக் காண்பார் நங்கைகா ளிதுவு மாமே","யாடுவ தொன்று மன்றிவ் வாண்மக னுரைப்ப தென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The old man says he will sing if the lady grants him something touching her beauty, and bids them agree before they listen; the women ask one another who would go looking to see a demon, and say this man's words are no game.","n":"What he asks (பாவை பொற்பே பற்றி) is veiled; the women's reply about seeking out a பேய் shows they hear a demand on the lady herself behind the old man's words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kurippu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2046","lb":"CC2046","ev":"The old man sets a condition for his song, and the women are alarmed by it.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழிமார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழிமார்",""],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2047":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2047,"t":["பட்டுலாய்க் கிடந்த செம்பொன் பவளமோ டிமைக்கு மல்கு","லொட்டினா ளதனை யோரோ துலம்பொரு தோளி னானும்","பட்டவா ணுதலி னாய்க்குப் பாடுவல் காமன் றந்த","தொட்டிமை யுடைய வீணைச் செவிச்சுமை யமிர்த மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"She of the silk-clad waist agrees to it, and the strong-shouldered one says he will sing for her of the bright forehead the nectar that burdens the ear, from the vina that Kama gave.","n":"She binds herself to an unknown condition for the sake of a song: the pledge will be called in when he is revealed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2047","lb":"CC2047","ev":"Suramanjari accepts the condition, and Civakan agrees to sing.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2048":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2048,"t":["வயிரவில் லுமிலும் பைம்பூண் வனமுலை மகளிர் தம்மு","ளுயிர்பெற வெழுதப் பட்ட வோலியப் பாவை யொப்பாள்","செயிரில்வாண் முகத்தை நோக்கித் தேன்பொதிந் தமுத மூறப்","பயிரிலா நரம்பிற் கீதம் பாடிய தொடங்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Looking at her flawless face, she like a painted doll come to life among the women, he begins to sing on faultless strings a song that drips honey and flows with nectar.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2048","lb":"CC2048","ev":"Civakan begins his song, looking at Suramanjari.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2049":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2049,"t":["தொடித்தோள் வளைநெகிழத் தொய்யின் முலைமேல்","வடிக்கேழ் மலர்நெடுங்கண் வார்புயலுங் காலும்","வார்புயலுங் காலும் வளைநெகிழு நந்திறத்த","தார்வமுறு நெஞ்ச மழூங்குவிக்கு மாலை."],"p":"","r":"In the first stanza of his song, evening comes, making bangles slip from the arms and rain pour from long eyes onto the breasts, and wears down the longing heart of women like us.","n":"The song speaks as a woman in separation (நந்திறத்தது, 'of our kind'), so the man who has come to win the lady sings her the lament of a woman who already loves; the refrain repeats line 2's close at the head of line 3.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","melivu","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2049","lb":"CC2049","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2050":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2050,"t":["ஐதேந் தகலல்கு லாவித் தழலுயிராக்","கைசோர்ந் தணலூன்றிக் கண்ணீர் கவுளலைப்பக்","கண்ணீர் கவுளலைப்பக் கையற்றி யாமினையப்","புண்ணீரும் வேலிற் புகுந்ததான் மாலை."],"p":"","r":"In the second stanza evening, laying the hips low and turning breath to fire, makes the hand slip and press the ground while tears beat on the cheeks, and enters like a spear into a running wound.","n":"புண்ணீரும் வேலிற் புகுந்ததால்: evening is not a fresh blow but a spear pushed into a wound already open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2050","lb":"CC2050","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2051":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2051,"t":["அவிழ்ந்தேந்து பூங்கோதை யாகத் தலர்ந்த","முகிழ்ந்தேந் திளமுலைமேற் பொன்பசலை பூப்பப்","பொன்பசலை பூப்பப் பொருகயற்கண் முத்தரும்ப","வன்புருகு நெஞ்ச மழுங்குவிக்கு மாலை."],"p":"","r":"In the third stanza, with the flower garland loosened on the breast and golden pallor blooming on the budding breasts while pearls of tears gather in the fish-like eyes, evening wears down the heart melting in love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pasalai","melivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2051","lb":"CC2051","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2052":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2052,"t":["பாடினான் றேவ கீதம் பண்ணினுக் கரசன் பாடச்","சூடக மகளிர் சோர்ந்து செருக்கிய மஞ்ஞை யொத்தா","ராடகச் செம்பொற் பாவை யந்தணற் புகழ்ந்து செம்பொன்","மாடம்புக் கநங்கற் பேணி வரங்கொள்வ னாளை யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"He sang the divine song, a king of melody, and the bangled women swooned like proud peacocks; the golden doll praised the Brahmin and said that tomorrow she would enter the golden temple, worship Anangan and win a boon.","n":"She does not say what boon; the evening-song has done its work, and the next verses show it is Civakan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vazhipaadu","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2052","lb":"CC2052","ev":"The women swoon at the song, and Suramanjari resolves to pray to Kama for a boon the next day.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி","தோழிமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2053":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2053,"t":["மடலணி பெண்ணை யீன்ற மணிமருள் குரும்பை மான","வுடலணி யாவி நைய வுருத்தெழு முலையி னாளு","மடலணி தோழி மாரு மார்வத்திற் கழும விப்பாற்","கடலணி திலகம் போலக் கதிர்திரை முளைத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"While she with breasts like the gem-bright young fruit of the palmyra, her life wasting in her body, and her friends were all consumed with longing, the sun rose from the sea like its forehead mark.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2053","lb":"CC2053","ev":"Night passes in longing and the sun rises.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2054":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2054,"t":["பொன்னியன் மணியுந் தாருங் கண்ணியும் புனைந்து செம்பொன்","மின்னியற் பட்டஞ் சோத்தி யானெய்பால் வெறுப்ப வூட்டி","மன்னியற் பாண்டில் பண்ணி மடந்தைகோல் கொள்ள வைய","மின்னியற் பாவை யேற்பத் தோழியோ டேறி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The bulls are dressed with gem bells, garlands and gold forehead plates and fed full with ghee and milk, the carriage is made ready, a woman takes the goad, and Suramanjari climbs into it with her maid.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2054","lb":"CC2054","ev":"Suramanjari sets out in her bullock carriage with her maid.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2055":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2055,"t":["ஆடவ ரிரிய வேகி யஞ்சொல்லார் சூழக் காமன்","மாடத்து ளிழிந்து மற்றவ் வள்ளலை மறைய வைத்துச்","சூடமை மாலை சாந்தம் விளக்கொடு தூப மேந்திச்","சேடியர் தொழுது நிற்பத் திருமகள் பரவு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Men scatter from her path as she goes ringed by sweet-voiced women; she alights at Kama's temple, where the generous one has been hidden, and while her attendants stand worshipping with garlands, sandal, lamp and incense, she prays.","n":"ஆடவர் இரிய: the royal order her father bought still clears men from her road even as she goes to pray for one. The line does not say who hid Civakan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2055","lb":"CC2055","ev":"Suramanjari reaches Kama's temple, where Civakan lies hidden, and begins to worship.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","தோழிமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2056":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2056,"t":["பொன்னிலஞ் சென்னி புல்ல விடமுழந் தாளை யூன்றி","மின்னவிர் மாலை மென்பூங் குழல்வலத் தோளின் வீழக்","கன்னியங் கமுகின் கண்போற் கலனணி யெருத்தங் கோட்டித்","தன்னிரு கையுங் கூப்பித் தையலீ துரைக்கு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Her head touching the golden floor, her left knee planted, her soft garlanded hair falling on her right shoulder and her neck bent, the maiden joins both hands and speaks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2056","lb":"CC2056","ev":"Suramanjari prostrates herself before Kama.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2057":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2057,"t":["தாமரைச் செங்கட் செவ்வாய்த் தமனியக் குழையி னாயோர்","காமமிங் குடையேன் காளை சீவக னகலஞ் சோத்தின்","மாமணி மகர மம்பு வண்சிலைக் கரும்பு மான்றேர்","பூமலி மார்ப வீவ லூரொடும் பொலிய வென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She prays to the lotus-eyed god with golden earrings that she has one desire, and that if she may join the chest of the young bull Civakan she will give him a makara banner, arrows, a sugarcane bow and a horse chariot, so that he shines with his whole town.","n":"The offerings are Kama's own emblems (மகரம், அம்பு, கரும்புச் சிலை): she promises to arm the god who has just conquered her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kaamam","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2057","lb":"CC2057","ev":"Suramanjari vows offerings to Kama if she may marry Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுரமஞ்சரி","ஐங்கணைக் கிழவன்"],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2058":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2058,"t":["மட்டவிழ் கோதை பெற்றாய் மனமகிழ் காத லானை","யிட்டிடை நோவ நில்லா தெழுகென வேந்த றோழன்","பட்டிமை யுரைத்த தோராள் பரவிய தெய்வந் தான்வாய்","விட்டுரைத் திட்ட தென்றே வேற்கணாள் பரவி மீண்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"'You have won the beloved who gladdens your heart; rise, do not stand so that your slender waist aches': the king's friend spoke this trick, and the spear-eyed one, not knowing it, took it that the god she worshipped had spoken with its own mouth, and praised it and went home.","n":"The text names only வேந்தன் தோழன்; that this is Buddhisenan is from the story, not the verse. The narrator calls the oracle பட்டிமை, a deceit, and says outright that she did not see through it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2058","lb":"CC2058","ev":"Civakan's companion, hidden in the shrine, answers her prayer in the god's voice, and she leaves believing Kama has spoken.","cl":[],"ag":["புத்திசேனன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["புத்திசேனன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2059":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2059,"t":["அடியிறை கொண்ட செம்பொ னாடகச் சிலம்பி னாளக்","கடியறை மருங்கி னின்ற மைந்தனைக் கண்டு நாணி","வடியுறு கடைக்க ணோக்க நெஞ்சுதுட் கென்ன வார்பூங்","கொடியுற வொசிந்து நின்றாள் குழைமுகத் திருவோ டொப்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"She of the golden anklets, seeing the young man standing by the fragrant chamber, is ashamed; when her sidelong glance meets him her heart starts, and she stands bending like a long creeper, like Tiru herself.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2059","lb":"CC2059","ev":"Suramanjari sees Civakan in the temple and stands abashed.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:88"},"cc:2060":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2060,"t":["இலங்குபொன் னோலை மின்ன வின்முகஞ் சிறிது கோட்டி","யலங்கலுங் குழலுந் தாழ வருமணிக் குழையோர் காதிற்","கலந்தொளி கான்று நின்று கதிர்விடு திருவில் வீச","நலங்கனிந் துருகி நின்றா ணாமவேற் காமர் கண்ணாள்."],"p":"","r":"Suramanjari stands before Civakan with her face a little turned, garland and hair hanging, one gem earring throwing light like a rainbow, melting with love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2060","lb":"CC2060","ev":"Suramanjari stands melting before Civakan in Kama's shrine.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2061":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2061,"t":["எரிமணிக் கலாபத் திட்ட விந்திர நீல மென்னு","மொருமணி யுந்தி நேரே யொருகதி ருமிழ்வ தேபோ","லருமணிப் பூணி னாட னவ்வயி றணிந்த கோலத்","திருமயி ரொழுக்கம் வந்தென் றிண்ணிறை கவர்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says the line of fine hair rising from her jewelled navel, like a single ray shot upward from a sapphire, came and carried off his firm restraint.","n":"The restraint that is lost here is the man's own (என் திண்ணிறை), in the chapter whose heroine had vowed that no one would break hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2061","lb":"CC2061","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2062":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2062,"t":["தேறினேன் றெய்வ மென்றே தீண்டிலே னாயி னுய்யேன்","சீறடி பரவ வந்தே னருளெனத் தொழுது சோந்து","நாறிருங் குழலி னாளை நாகணை விடையிற் புல்லிக்","கோறொடுத் தநங்க னெய்யக் குழைந்துதார் திவண்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Saying that he took her for a goddess, that he will not live unless he touches her, and that he came to worship her small feet and begs her grace, Civakan bows, faints with love and embraces her on the couch while Anangan shoots his arrows.","n":"தேறினேன் தெய்வம் என்றே may also mean that she trusted the god's answer of 2058, which was his friend's trick; either way the worship she offered Kama for him is now offered by him to her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2062","lb":"CC2062","ev":"Civakan worships Suramanjari as a goddess and embraces her.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2063":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2063,"t":["கலைபுறஞ் சூழ்ந்த வல்குற் கார்மயிற் சாய லாளு","மலைபுறங் கண்ட மார்பின் வாங்குவிற் றடக்கை யானு","மிலைபுறங் கண்ட கண்ணி யின்றமி ழியற்கை யின்ப","நிலைபெற நெறியிற் றுய்த்தார் நிகர்தமக் கிலாத நீரார்."],"p":"","r":"The woman of the peacock's grace and the man of the hill-defeating chest, matchless both, enjoyed by the proper way the pleasure of the natural union of sweet Tamil so that it would last.","n":"இன்றமிழ் இயற்கை இன்பம் names the Tamil grammar of love: the first secret union (இயற்கைப் புணர்ச்சி) of the akam poems, and நெறியின் claims the union keeps that rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2063","lb":"CC2063","ev":"Civakan and Suramanjari are united in secret.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2064":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2064,"t":["குங்குமங் குயின்ற கொம்மைக் குவிமுலை குளிர்ப்பத் தைவந்","தங்கலுழ் மேனி யல்குற் காசுடன் றிருத்தி யம்பொற்","பொங்குபூஞ் சிலம்பிற் போர்த்த பூந்துக ளவித்து மாதர்","கொங்கலர் கோதை சூட்டிக் குழனலந் திருத்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Afterwards he strokes her saffron-painted breasts to cool them, sets right the beads of her girdle, brushes the pollen from her anklets, crowns her with a garland and arranges her hair.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2064","lb":"CC2064","ev":"Civakan adorns Suramanjari again after their union.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2065":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2065,"t":["வானார்கமழ் மதுவுஞ் சாந்து மேந்தி மதுத்துளித்து வண்டுஞ் சுரும்பு மூசுந்","தேனார்பூங் கோதாய் நினக்குக் காமன் சிலையிரண்டுஞ் செவ்வனே கோலித் தந்தான்","றானாரப் பண்ணித் தடறு நீக்கித் தண்குருதி தோய்த்துத் தகைமை சான்ற","வூனார்ந்த வோரிணை யம்புந் தந்தா னென்னை யுளனாக வேண்டி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan tells her that Kama bent both his bows straight and gave them to her as brows, and gave her a pair of flesh-fed arrows dipped in cool blood as eyes, because he wanted him to live.","n":"The closing என்னை உளனாக வேண்டினானே is turned: the weapons that wound him are said to be given so that he may stay alive, which is the lover's paradox and may also mean that Kama wanted him to exist only as her captive.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2065","lb":"CC2065","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2066":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2066,"t":["கண்ணக்க கண்ணிக் கமழ்பூங் குழற்க ரும்போ தீஞ்சொ லாள்கதிர் முலைகளின்","வண்ணக்கு வானு நிலனுமெல் லாம்விலை யேமழை மின்னு நுசுப்பி னாளைப்","பெண்ணுக் கணியாக வேண்டி மேலைப் பெரியோர் பெருமான் படைத்தா னென்று","புண்ணக்க வேலான் புகழ நாணிப் பூநோக்கிப் பூக்கொசிந்த கொம்பொத் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"When Civakan praises her, saying that sky and earth together are the price of her breasts and that the lord of the ancient great ones made her as an ornament for womankind, she is shy, looks down at the flowers and bends like a branch heavy with blossom.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2066","lb":"CC2066","ev":"Civakan praises Suramanjari and she bows her head in shyness.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2067":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2067,"t":["இறங்கிய மாதர் தன்னை யெரிமணிக் கடகக் கையாற்","குறங்கின்மேற் றழுவி வைத்துக் கோதையங் குருதி வேலா","னறந்தலை நீங்கக் காக்கு மரசன்யா னாக நாளைச்","சிறந்தநின் னலத்தைச் சேரே னாய்விடிற் செல்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Taking the bowed girl on his thigh, Civakan swears that if he does not join her beauty tomorrow he may become a king whose guarding lets dharma leave, and bids her go.","n":"The oath is a self-curse, and the penalty he names is a king's failure, though he passes as a merchant's son: the hidden prince swears by the sceptre he has not yet regained. நாளை promises marriage, which 2078 keeps. The reading of அறந்தலை நீங்கக் காக்கும் as 'guarding so that dharma departs' is the plainest the words allow but is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sool","kodungol","varaivu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2067","lb":"CC2067","ev":"Civakan swears an oath to wed Suramanjari the next day and sends her away.","cl":[["to be a king under whose guard dharma departs is the worst fate a man can call down on himself","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2068":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2068,"t":["வில்லிடு மணிசெ யாழி மெல்விரல் விதியிற் கூப்பி","நல்லடி பணிந்து நிற்ப நங்கைநீ நடுங்க வேண்டா","செல்கெனச் சிலம்பு செம்பொற் கிண்கிணி மிழற்ற வொல்கி","யல்குற்கா சொலிப்ப வாயம் பாவைசென் றெய்தி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"She folds her ringed fingers in due form and bows at his feet; told not to tremble but to go, she sways away with anklets and girdle chiming and reaches her company.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2068","lb":"CC2068","ev":"Suramanjari bows to Civakan, leaves him and rejoins her companions.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2069":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2069,"t":["பருமணிப் படங்கொ ணாகப் பையெனப் பரந்த வல்கு","லெரிமணிப் பூணி னானுக் கின்னல மொழிய வேகித்","திருமணிச் சிவிகை யேறிச் செம்பொனீண் மாடம் புக்காள்","விரிமணி விளங்கு மாலை வெம்முலை வேற்க ணாளே."],"p":"","r":"Leaving her sweetness with the jewelled man, the girl of the spear-like eyes mounts the gem palanquin and enters her golden mansion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2069","lb":"CC2069","ev":"Suramanjari returns home in her palanquin.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2070":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2070,"t":["திருவிற்றான் மாரி கற்பான் றுவலைநாட் செய்வ தேபோ","லுருவிற்றாய்த் துளிக்குந் தேற லோங்குதார் மார்பன் றோழர்","பொருவிற்றா நம்பி காம திலகனென் றிருந்த போழ்திற்","செருவிற்றாழ் நுதலி னாள்கண் மணத்திறஞ் செப்பு கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"While Civakan, his garland dripping honey like a rainbow that learns to rain, sits as the peerless prince Kamatilakan, his friends go to speak of marriage for the girl of the bow-like brows.","n":"காமதிலகன் may be a name under which the friends present him, as he is in disguise in this ilampakam, or only an epithet; the verse does not settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2070","lb":"CC2070","ev":"Civakan's friends go to ask for Suramanjari in marriage.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2071":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2071,"t":["கனைகடலமுதுந் தேனுங் கலந்துகொண் டெழுதப் பட்ட","புனைகொடி பூத்த தேபோற் பொறுக்கலா நுசுப்பிற் பாவை","நனைகுடைந் துண்டு தேக்கி நன்மணி வண்டு பாடும்","புனைகடி மாலை மாதர் திறத்திது மொழிந்து விட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"They make their proposal concerning the girl, a flowering creeper painted with the sea's nectar and honey, whose waist can hardly bear her, wearing a garland where bees sing.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2071","lb":"CC2071","ev":"Civakan's friends ask for Suramanjari in marriage.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2072":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2072,"t":["ஐயற்கென் றுரைத்த மாற்றங் கேட்டலு மலங்க னாய்கன்","வெய்யதேன் வாய்க்கொண் டாற்போல் விழுங்கலோ டுமிழ்த றேற்றான்","செய்வதெ னோற்றி லாதே னோற்றிலா டிறத்தி னென்று","மையல்கொண் டிருப்ப வப்பாற் குமரிதன் மதியிற் சூழ்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing that she is asked for the master, the merchant, like a man with hot honey in his mouth, can neither swallow nor spit, and grieves that he and his daughter alike have done no penance, while the girl turns the matter over in her own mind.","n":"The father who bought a royal order to keep men from her (907) is caught between the offer and her vow; he blames neither the suitor nor her but their lack of earlier penance (நோற்றிலாதேன் நோற்றிலாள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","varaivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2072","lb":"CC2072","ev":"Suramanjari's father hears the proposal and cannot decide, while she deliberates.","cl":[["what befalls a man and his child follows the penance they did or did not do","general","reported",3,"suramanjari_thanthai"]],"ag":["தந்தை","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2073":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2073,"t":["பொற்பமை தாமக் கந்து பொருந்திய மின்னுப் போல","வெற்பக வெரியு மாலைப் பவளத்தூண் பொருந்தி யின்னீர்க்","கற்பெனு மாலை வீசி நாணெனுங் களிவண் டோப்பிச்","சொற்புக ரின்றித் தோழிக் கறத்தினோ டரிவை நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Leaning on a coral pillar like lightning on a garlanded post, waving the garland of chastity and driving off the drunken bee of shame, she stands before her friend and tells her, with no flaw in her words, as dharma requires.","n":"அறத்தினோடு நின்றாள் is the akam convention அறத்தொடு நிற்றல், disclosing a secret union so that it may end in marriage; the image sets chastity above shame, since she must put shame away for chastity's sake.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","naanam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2073","lb":"CC2073","ev":"Suramanjari discloses her union with Civakan to her friend.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2074":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2074,"t":["வழிவளர் மயிலஞ் சாயற் பவளப்பூம் பாவை யன்ன","கழிவளர் கயற்க ணங்கை கற்பினை யறிந்து தோழி","யழிமது மாலை சோத்தி யடிபணிந் தார வாழ்த்திப்","பொழிமதுப் புயலைங் கூந்தற் செவிலியைப் பொருந்திச் சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Knowing her chastity, the friend puts a garland on her, bows at her feet, blesses her and goes to tell the foster mother.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2074","lb":"CC2074","ev":"Suramanjari's friend tells the foster mother.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2075":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2075,"t":["நனைவளர் கோதை நற்றாய் நங்கைக்கீ துள்ள மென்று","சுனைவளர் குவளை யுண்கட் சுமதிக்குச் செவிலி செப்பக்","கனயிருட் கனவிற் கண்டேன் காமர்பூம் பொய்கை வற்ற","வனையதாங் கன்னி நீரின் றற்றதா நங்கைக் கென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"The foster mother tells Sumati, the girl's mother, that this is the girl's heart, and the mother says she saw in a dream in the thick dark a lovely flower pond run dry, and that so today the girl's maidenhood has gone.","n":"Who speaks the dream is not marked: கண்டேன் ... என்றாள் follows the foster mother's report and is most naturally the mother's answer, but it may continue the foster mother's words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kalaviyal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2075","lb":"CC2075","ev":"The foster mother tells Sumati, who recalls a dream that foretold the loss of the girl's maidenhood.","cl":[],"ag":["செவிலி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தாய்","செவிலி"],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2076":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2076,"t":["கெண்டையுஞ் சிலையுந் திங்க ளிளமையுங் கிடந்து தேங்கொ","டொண்டையங் கனியு முத்துந் தொழதக வணிந்து தூங்குங்","குண்டல முடைய திங்க ளிதுவெனு முகத்தி தாதை","வண்புகழ்க் குபேர தத்தன் கேட்டனன் மனைவி சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells Kuberadattan of great fame, father of the girl whose face is a moon with fish, bow, coral, pearls and earrings in it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2076","lb":"CC2076","ev":"Sumati tells Kuberadattan of their daughter's union.","cl":[],"ag":["தாய்","தந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2077":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2077,"t":["செருவிளைத் தனலும் வேலோய் சிறுமுதுக் குறைவி தானே","பெருவளைப் பிட்டுக் காத்த கற்பிது போலு மையன்","கரிவிளைத் தாய்ந்த சுண்ணம் வாட்டின னென்று கண்டாய்","திருவிளை தேம்பெய் மாரி பாற்கடற் பெய்த தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"She tells him that their small, too-wise daughter kept this chastity of hers against all, and that the very man who made her powder lose has won her: the honey rain of fortune has fallen into the sea of milk.","n":"The mother turns the girl's grievance into proof: the vow taken in anger at Civakan's verdict (904, 908) is read as chastity kept for Civakan. பெருவளைப் பிட்டுக் காத்த is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","oozh","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2077","lb":"CC2077","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தாய்","தந்தை"],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2078":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2078,"t":["கேட்பது விரும்பி நாய்கன் கிளைக்கெலா முணர்த்தி யார்க்கும்","வேட்பன வடிசி லாடை விழுக்கலன் மாலை சாந்தங்","கோட்குறை வின்றி யாக்கிக் குழுமியங் கறங்கி யார்ப்ப","நாட்கடி மாலை யாற்கு நங்கையை நல்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Glad of what he hears, the merchant tells all his kin, prepares food, cloth, jewels, garlands and sandal for everyone without lack, and with drums sounding gives his daughter to the man of the fresh garland.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","sutram","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2078","lb":"CC2078","ev":"Kuberadattan gives Suramanjari to Civakan in marriage.","cl":[],"ag":["தந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2079":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2079,"t":["பரியகஞ் சிலம்பு பைம்பொற் கிண்கிணி யார்ந்த பாதத்","தரிவைய ராடன் மிக்கா ரருமணி வீணை வல்லா","ருரியநூற் றெண்மர் செம்பொ னொன்றரைக் கோடி மூன்று","ரெரியழன் முன்னர் நோந்தே னென்மகட் கென்று சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"He says that before the fire he has given his daughter a hundred and eight women with anklets, skilled in dance and the vina, one and a half crore of red gold, and three villages.","n":"The bride is given before the fire (எரியழல் முன்னர்), and with her a dowry the father declares himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2079","lb":"CC2079","ev":"Kuberadattan declares the dowry given with his daughter before the fire.","cl":[],"ag":["தந்தை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தந்தை",""],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2080":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2080,"t":["மாசறு மணியு முத்தும் வயிரமு மொளிரு மேனி","யாசறு செம்பொ னார்ந்த வலங்கலங் குன்ற னானுந்","தூசுறு பரவை யல்குற் றூமணிக் கொம்ப னாளுங்","காசறக் கலந்த வின்பக் கடலகத் தழுந்தி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"He with jewel-bright body and a chest like a garlanded hill, and she like a gem-hung branch, sink into a sea of pleasure mingled without flaw.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","punarchi","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2080","lb":"CC2080","ev":"Civakan and Suramanjari live in wedded pleasure.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:89"},"cc:2081":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2081,"t":["பொன்வரை பொருத யானைப் புணர்மருப் பனைய வாகித்","தென்வரைச் சாந்து மூழ்கித் திரள்வடஞ் சுமந்து வீங்கி","மின்வளர் மருங்குல் செற்ற வெம்முலை மணிக்கண் சேப்பத்","தொன்னலம் பருகித் தோன்ற றுறக்கம்புக் கவர்க ளொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Her hot breasts, like the joined tusks of an elephant that has battered a golden hill, steeped in southern sandal and swelling under their strings of pearls, redden at their gem tips as he drinks her old beauty, and he is like those who have entered heaven.","n":"The beauty he drinks is தொன்னலம், her beauty of long standing: the woman who had vowed to look on no man has kept it unspent until now.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2081","lb":"CC2081","ev":"Civakan and Suramanjari come together on their first night.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2082":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2082,"t":["வரிக்கழற் குருசின் மார்பு மடந்தைவெம் முலையுந் தம்முட்","செருச்செய்து திளைத்துப் போரிற் சிலம்பொலி கலந்த பாணி","யரிப்பறை யனுங்க வார்க்கு மேகலைக் குரலோ டீண்டிப்","புரிக்குழல் புலம்ப வைகிப் பூவணை விடுக்க லானே."],"p":"","r":"The hero's chest and the girl's breasts make war and revel; the beat of the anklets, the clinking girdle and the drum-like rhythm mingle, her braided hair moans, and he lingers so that he cannot leave the flower bed.","n":"Love-making is told in the vocabulary of battle (செரு, போர்), with the ornaments as the war-drums.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2082","lb":"CC2082","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2083":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2083,"t":["மணியியல் வள்ளத் தேந்த மதுமகிழ்ந் தனந்தர் கூர","வணிமலர்க் குவளைப் பைம்போ தொருகையி னருளி யம்பொற்","பிணையனா ளருகு சேரிற் பேதுறு நுசுப்பென் றெண்ணித்","துணையமை தோள்க டம்மாற் றோன்றறான் புல்லி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Drinking wine from a gem cup until drowsiness came on, he gave her a fresh blue lily with one hand, and thinking that if the golden doe came close her slender waist would be hurt, he held her with his two shoulders.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2083","lb":"CC2083","ev":"Civakan gives Suramanjari a lily and embraces her.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2084":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2084,"t":["மல்லலங் கங்கை போலும் பலர்முயங் கார மார்பிற்","புல்லன்மின் போமின் வேண்டா வென்றவள் புலந்து நீங்க","முல்லையங் கோதை யொன்றும் பிழைப்பிலேன் முனிய னீயென்","றல்லலுற் றரத்த மார்ந்த சீறடி தொழுதிட் டானே."],"p":"","r":"She sulks and pulls away, telling him not to embrace her with a chest that many embrace as they do the broad Ganges; he, in distress, saying he has done no wrong and asking the jasmine-garlanded one not to be angry, worships her small red-dyed feet.","n":"The charge has a literal base in this epic: by this ilampakam the hero has already married several wives, so her figure of the river all may bathe in is a complaint as well as a lover's pretext.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2084","lb":"CC2084","ev":"Suramanjari, jealous, refuses Civakan's embrace, and he bows to her feet.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2085":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2085,"t":["வட்டிகைப் பாவை நோக்கி மகிழ்ந்திருந் திலிரோ வென்னாத்","தொட்டிமை யுருவந் தோன்றச் சுவரையே பொருந்தி நின்றாய்","கட்டழ குடைய நங்கை நீயெனக் கருதிக் கண்ணா","னொட்டியா னோக்கிற் றென்றா னொருபிடி நுசுப்பி னாட்கே."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether he was not delighting in gazing at the painted figure, and he answers the slim-waisted one that she stood pressed to the wall so that her form showed on it, and his eye, taking it for her, the beautiful maid, clung to it.","n":"The construal of line 2 is uncertain: it may be his excuse (she leaned on the wall and her likeness showed there) or part of her charge (he stood fixed at the wall); the reading here takes it as his reply, closed by என்றான்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2085","lb":"CC2085","ev":"Suramanjari accuses Civakan of gazing at a painted figure; he says he took it for her.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2086":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2086,"t":["நுண்டுகி னெகிழ்ந்த வல்குன் மணிபரந் திமைப்ப நொந்து","கண்களை யிடுகக் கோட்டிக் காமத்திற் செயிர்த்து நோக்கிக்","குண்டல மிலங்கக் கோதை கூந்தலோ டவிழ்ந்து சோர","வொண்டொடி யூடி நின்றா ளொளிமணிப் பூங்கொம் பொப்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her fine silk slipping and the gems on her waist flashing, hurt, she narrowed her eyes, glared at him in love's anger, and with earrings shining and her garland coming loose with her hair, the bright-bangled one stood sulking like a flowering branch of bright gems.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2086","lb":"CC2086","ev":"Suramanjari stands apart in her sulk.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2087":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2087,"t":["கிழவனாய்ப் பாடி வந்தென் கீழ்ச்சிறை யிருப்பக் கண்டே","னெழுதிய பாவை நோக்கி யிமையவித் திருப்பக் கண்டே","னொழிகவிக் காம மோரூ ரிரண்டஃக மாயிற் றென்றாங்","கழுதகண் ணீர்கண் மைந்த னாவிபோழ்ந் திட்ட வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"She says she saw him come as an old man singing and sit in her lower chamber, and saw him sit unblinking gazing at the painted figure, and cries away with this love, the one town has become two; and the tears she wept split the young man's life.","n":"She now knows the old singer who sat in her chamber was Civakan in disguise. The phrase ஓரூர் இரண்டஃகமாயிற்று (the one town has become two, or two measures) seems proverbial for a divided love, but its exact sense is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2087","lb":"CC2087","ev":"Suramanjari recalls Civakan's disguise as an old man and his gazing at the painting, and weeps.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2088":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2088,"t":["அலங்கறா தவிழ வஞ்செஞ் சீறடி யணிந்த வம்பூஞ்","சிலம்பின்மேற் சென்னி சோத்திச் சிறியவர் செய்த தீமை","புலம்பலர் பொறுப்ப ரன்றே பெரியவ ரென்று கூறி","யிலங்குவேற் கண்ணி யூட லிளையவ னீக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Setting his head on the anklets of her small red feet so that his garland came undone, and saying that the great bear without complaint the wrongs the small have done, the young man ended the sulk of the woman with spear-bright eyes.","n":"He has just said he did no wrong (2084), yet he pleads as the small one who erred, making her the great one who forgives: the maxim is used as a lover's tactic. The gesture also completes her vow of 899, that she would keep apart until he came defeated and worshipped her anklet feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","porai","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2088","lb":"CC2088","ev":"Civakan lays his head on Suramanjari's anklets and ends her sulk.","cl":[["the great bear without complaint the wrongs done by the small","general","reported",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2089":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2089,"t":["யாழ்கொன்ற கிளவி யாட னழிழ்துறழ் புலவி நீக்கிக்","காழின்றிக் கனிந்த காமக் கொழுங்கனி நுகர்ந்து காதற்","றாழ்கின்ற தாம மார்பன் றையலோ டாடி விள்ளா","னூழ்சென்ற மதியம் வெய்யோ னொட்டியொன் றாய தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Ending the sulk of her whose speech outdoes the yal and is sweeter than nectar, and tasting the ripe fruit of love that has no hard stone, the man with the hanging garland did not leave his play with the girl, and was like the moon and sun joined in one when their time comes round.","n":"ஊழ் here is the turn of time that brings sun and moon together at the new moon, not deeds of a former birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2089","lb":"CC2089","ev":"After the quarrel Civakan and Suramanjari are reunited in love.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2090":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2090,"t":["பச்சிலைப் பட்டு முத்தும் பவளமு மிமைக்கு மல்கு","னச்சிலை வேற்கண் மாதர் நகைமுக முறுவன் மாந்தி","யிச்சையுங் குறிப்பு நோக்கி யெய்வதே கரும மாகக்","கைச்சிலை கணையோ டேந்திக் காமனிக் கடையைக் காப்பான்."],"p":"","r":"Kama, holding bow and arrow, guards this doorway, drinking the laughing face and smile of the woman whose waist shines with green silk, pearls and coral, and watching for desire and signs, his only work to shoot.","n":"The god of love is posted as doorkeeper of their chamber, where earlier her father's guards had kept men out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2090","lb":"CC2090","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஐங்கணைக் கிழவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2091":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2091,"t":["கடிப்பிணை காது சோத்திச் சிகழிகை காத நாறத்","தொடுத்தலர் மாலை சூட்டிக் கிம்புரி முத்த மென்றோ","ளடுத்தணிந் தாகஞ் சாந்தி னணிபெற வெழுதி யல்கு","லுடுத்தபொற் கலாபந் தைவந் தொளிவளை திருத்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He set ornaments in her ears, crowned her braid with a strung garland whose scent carried far, put pearl-tipped bands on her soft arms, painted her body with sandal, stroked the gold girdle on her waist and set her bright bangles right.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2091","lb":"CC2091","ev":"Civakan adorns Suramanjari.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2092":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2092,"t":["இலங்குவெள் ளருவிக் குன்றத் தெழுந்ததண் டகரச் செந்தீ","நலங்கிள ரகிலுந் தேனுங் கட்டியு நன்கு கூட்டிப்","புலம்பற வளர்த்த வம்மென் பூம்புகை யமளி யங்கண்","விலங்கர சனைய காளை வெள்வளைக் கிதனைச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"On the couch fragrant with soft smoke from tagara wood of the hill of white falls, with fine agil, honey and sugar mixed and burned to end loneliness, the youth like a king of beasts said this to the white-bangled girl.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2092","lb":"CC2092","ev":"Civakan prepares to speak to Suramanjari on the couch.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2093":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2093,"t":["கருமநீ கவல வேண்டா கயற்கணாய் பிரிவல் சின்னா","ளருமைநின் கவினைத் தாங்க லதுபொரு ளென்று கூறப்","பெருமநீ வேண்டிற் றல்லால் வேண்டுவ பிறிதொன் றுண்டோ","வொருமைநின் மனத்திற் சென்றே னுவப்பதே யுவப்ப தென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"He tells the carp-eyed girl not to worry about his business, for he will be parted from her only a few days and her duty is to bear her rare beauty; she answers that she wants nothing but what he wants, that she has gone with one mind into his heart, and what pleases him is what pleases her.","n":"The verse holds both voices: lines 1 and 2 are his, lines 3 and 4 hers. The woman who had sworn to shun all men now defines her will as his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","karpu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2093","lb":"CC2093","ev":"Civakan tells Suramanjari he must leave for a few days, and she consents.","cl":[["a wife wants nothing other than what her husband wants, and his joy is her joy","general","reported",3,"suramanjari"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுரமஞ்சரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுரமஞ்சரி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2094":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2094,"t":["நாணொடு மிடைந்த தேங்கொ ணடுக்குறு கிளவி கேட்டே","பூண்வடுப் பொறிப்பப் புல்லிப் புனைநலம் புலம்ப வைகேன்","றேன்மிடை கோதை யென்று திருமக னெழுந்து போகி","வாண்மிடை தோழர் சூழத் தன்மனை மகிழ்ந்து புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing her honey-sweet words woven with modesty, he embraced her so that his ornaments left their marks, told the honey-garlanded one not to pine and let her beauty fade, rose, and went with his sword-bearing friends round him gladly into his own house.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2094","lb":"CC2094","ev":"Civakan leaves Suramanjari and goes with his friends to his foster father's house.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2095":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2095,"t":["புரவியுங் களிறு நோக்கிப் பொன்னெடுந் தேரு நோக்கி","யிரவினும் பகலு மோவா தென்மகன் யாண்டை யானென்","றழுதகண் ணீரி னாலே கைகழீஇ யவலிக் கின்ற","மெழுகெரி முகந்த தொக்குந் தாய்மெலி வகற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He ended the wasting of his mother, who, looking at horses, elephants and tall golden chariots, never ceasing night or day to cry where is my son, had washed her hands in the tears she wept and grieved like a wax figure taken by fire.","n":"The mother scans the horses, elephants and chariots, the things her son rode, as if they could tell her where he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2095","lb":"CC2095","ev":"Civakan returns to his foster mother and ends her grief.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சுநந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2096":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2096,"t":["ஒற்றரு முணர்த லின்றி யுரையவித் துறுப்பி னாலே","சுற்றத்தார்க் குரைப்ப வேண்டித் தொக்குடன் றழுவிக் கொள்வா","ரெற்றுவா ரினைந்து சோர்வார் நம்பியோ நம்பி யென்னா","வுற்றுடன் றழுத கண்ணீர் காலலைத் தொழுகிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"So that not even spies should know, they silenced their speech and wanted to tell the kin by gestures; the kin gathered and embraced him, beat themselves, wilted, and crying Nambi, o Nambi, wept tears that ran down and washed his feet.","n":"His return must stay hidden from Kattiyangaran's spies: even the reunion is held in signs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","otru","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2096","lb":"CC2096","ev":"Civakan's kin greet him in secret, weeping, without words.","cl":[],"ag":["சுற்றம்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2097":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2097,"t":["கந்துகண் கழறக் கல்லென் கடற்றிரை யவிந்த வண்ணம்","வந்தவர் புலம்பு நீங்க மறைபுறப் படுமென் றெண்ணி","யெந்தைதா னிறந்த நாளின் றெனநக ரியம்பி யாரு","மந்தமி லுவகை தன்னா லகங்குளிர்ப் பெய்தி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"When Kandukkan rebuked them the uproar fell still like the waves of the roaring sea, and thinking the secret would get out, he proclaimed in the city that today was the day his father died, and all were cooled within by a joy without end.","n":"The weeping of joy is given out as mourning so that no spy guesses its cause. The verse is read here with கந்துகண் as Kantukkatan and எந்தை as his father; both are the likeliest readings, not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","otru","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2097","lb":"CC2097","ev":"Kantukkatan quiets the kin and announces a death anniversary to explain the weeping.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்துக்கடன்","சுற்றம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2098":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2098,"t":["செங்கயன் மழைக்கட் செவ்வாய்த் தத்தையு மகிழ்ந்து தீஞ்சொ","லெங்கையைச் சென்று காண்மி னடிகளென் றிரந்து கூற","மங்கல வகையிற் சோந்து மதுத்துளி யறாத மாலை","கொங்கலர் கண்ணி சோத்திக் குங்கும மெழுதி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Tattai of the red carp-like eyes, rejoicing, begged him in sweet words to go and see her younger sister, and in auspicious fashion he set a garland of unceasing honey and a fragrant wreath on her and painted her with saffron.","n":"எங்கை, my younger sister, is Gunamalai, called so by her co-wife. Whether the woman he adorns in lines 3 and 4 is Tattai, before he goes, or Gunamalai on his arrival is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","anbu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2098","lb":"CC2098","ev":"Gandharvadattai sends Civakan to see Gunamalai, and he adorns a wife with garland and saffron.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2099":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2099,"t":["தீவினை யுடைய வென்னைத் தீண்டன்மி னடிகள் வேண்டா","பாவியே னென்று நொந்து பரிந்தழு துருகி நையக்","காவியங் கண்ணி யொன்றுங் கவலல்யா னுய்ந்த தெல்லா","நாவியே நாறு மேனி நங்கைநின் றவத்தி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Gunamalai cries do not touch sinful me, lord, there is no need, I am a wrongdoer, and grieves, weeps and melts; he tells the girl with lily eyes not to grieve at all, for all his survival came from her penance, the girl whose body smells of musk.","n":"She calls herself தீவினை உடைய, one with evil deeds, because his seizure followed his rescue of her from the elephant; he turns her guilt into merit. The verse does not name a school for the power of தவம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2099","lb":"CC2099","ev":"Gunamalai blames herself for Civakan's danger; he says her penance saved him.","cl":[["a wife's penance can preserve her husband's life","general","reported",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["குணமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2100":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2100,"t":["அன்னமென் னடையு நோக்குஞ் சாயலு மணியு மேரு","மின்னினுண் ணுசுப்பும் வெய்ய முலைகளு முகமுந் தோன்ற","வென்மனத் தெழுதப் பட்டா யாயினு மரிவை கேளா","யுன்னையான் பிரிந்த நாளோ ரூழியே போன்ற தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He tells her that though her soft gait like a swan's, her look, her grace, her jewels, her beauty, her lightning-slim waist, her breasts and her face were painted in his mind, the day he was parted from her was like an aeon.","n":"Her image painted in his mind did not make the parting short: memory is set against absence and loses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2100","lb":"CC2100","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2101":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2101,"t":["இளையவண் மகிழ்வ கூறி யின்றுயி லமர்ந்து பின்னாள்","விளைபொரு ளாய வெல்லாந் தாதைக்கே வேறு கூறிக்","கிளையவர் சூழ வாமான் வாணிக னாகிக் கேடி","றளையவிழ் தாம மார்பன் றன்னகர் நீங்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Telling the young woman things to delight her, he slept, and the next day he gave all the wealth he had gained to his father, and with his kin around him, as a merchant on a fine horse, the man of the flawless garland left his city.","n":"வாமான் வாணிகனாகி may mean he went as a horse-trader or simply rode as a merchant; either way he leaves in disguise, still hidden from the usurper.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2101","lb":"CC2101","ev":"Civakan gives his gains to Kantukkatan and leaves the city in a merchant's guise.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கந்துக்கடன்","கிளை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:90"},"cc:2102":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2102,"t":["குடம்புரை செருத்தற் குவளைமேய் கயவாய்க் குவிமுலைப் படர்மருப் பெருமை","நடந்தவா யெல்லா நறுமலர் மரையி னாகிலைச் சொரிந்தவந் தீம்பா","றடஞ்சிறை யன்னங் குருகொடு நாரைப் பார்ப்பின மோம்புதண் மருத","மடங்கல்போற் றிறலார் மாமணி கறங்க வளவயற் புள்ளெனக் கழிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan's company, strong as lions, passes through the marutam fields, where a buffalo with udders like pots grazes the kuvalai and its sweet milk, spilled wherever it walks, feeds the young of swans, herons and storks, and their great bells ring as they go.","n":"The verse opens a journey through all five landscapes in order: marutam (2102), neytal (2103), mullai (2104), kurinji (2105) and palai (2106-2107).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2102","lb":"CC2102","ev":"Civakan and his company pass through the farm country of the marutam fields.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2103":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2103,"t":["புரிவளை யலறிப் பூசலிட் டீன்ற பொழிகதிர் நிலத்தில் முழக்கி","வரிவளை சூழும் வலம்புரி யினத்துட் சலஞ்சல மேய்வன நோக்கி","யரிதுண ரன்னம் பெடையெனத் தழுவி யன்மையி னலமர லெய்தித்","திரிதரு நோக்கந் தீதிலார் நோக்கி நெய்தலுங் கைவலத் தொழிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Watching a swan that, among the conches circling a right-whorled conch on the shore, embraces one as its mate and then reels in distress to find it is not, the faultless travellers leave the neytal shore on their right.","n":"The swan's mistake of a conch for its mate is a small figure of desire deceived by likeness, set in the landscape the travellers merely pass.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2103","lb":"CC2103","ev":"The company passes the seashore of the neytal country.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2104":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2104,"t":["கோட்டிளங் கலையுங் கூடுமென் பிணையுங் கொழுங்கதிர் மணிவிளக் கெறிப்பச்","சேட்டிளங் கொன்றைத் திருநிழற் றுஞ்சச் செம்பொறி வண்டவற் றயலே","நாட்டிளம் படியார் நகைமுகம் பருகு நல்லவர் போன்மலர் பருகு","மோட்டிள முல்லை மொய்ம்மலர்க் கான முருகுவந் தெதிர்கொள நடந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Where the buck and doe sleep under the konrai and bees drink the flowers as good men drink the smiling faces of the country's young women, the mullai forest comes out to meet them with its fragrance as they walk.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2104","lb":"CC2104","ev":"The company walks through the mullai forest.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2105":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2105,"t":["குழவிவெண் டிங்கட் கோட்டின்மேற் பாயக் குளிர்புனல் சடைவிரித் தேற்கு","மழலவிர் சூலத் தண்ணலே போல வருவிநீர் மருப்பினி னெறியக்","கழைவளர் குன்றிற் களிறுநின் றாடுங் கடிநறுஞ் சந்தனச் சார","லிழைவளர் முலையார் சாயல்போற் றோகை யிறைகொள்பூங் குறிஞ்சியு மிறந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"They cross the kurinji, where on the bamboo hill a tusker stands and plays with a stream falling on its tusks, like the lord of the bright trident who takes cold water on his spread matted hair with the young white moon, and where peacocks gather like women in their grace.","n":"The elephant under the waterfall is likened to the trident-bearer receiving the river on his matted hair with the crescent, Siva's image used as ornament in a Jain epic, with no claim about the god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2105","lb":"CC2105","ev":"The company crosses the hill country.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2106":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2106,"t":["ஊன்றலைப் பொடித்தாங் கனையசெஞ் சூட்டி னெளிமயிர் வாரண மொருங்கே","கான்றபூங் கடம்பின் கவட்டிடை வளைவாய்ப் பருந்தொடு கவர்குரல் பயிற்று","மான்றவெம் பாலை யழன்மிதித் தன்ன வருஞ்சுரஞ் சுடர்மறை பொழுதி","னூன்றினார் பாய்மா வொளிமதிக் கதிர்போற் சந்தன மொருங்குமெய் புதைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"At sunset they mount their leaping horses for the hard desert track, hot as if one trod on fire, where red-combed jungle fowl call among the kadamba with the hooked-beaked kites, their bodies covered with sandal like moonbeams.","n":"They take the desert by night and against the heat cover themselves with sandal paste.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2106","lb":"CC2106","ev":"At nightfall the company rides into the palai desert.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2107":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2107,"t":["நிலையிலா வுலகி னின்றவண் புகழை வேட்டவ னிதியமே போன்று","மிலைகுலாம் பைம்பூ ணிளமுலைத் தூதி னின்கனித் தொண்டையந் துவர்வாய்க்","கலைவலார் நெஞ்சிற் காமமே போன்றுங் கடவுளர் வெகுளியே போன்று","முலைவிலார் நில்லா தொருபக லுள்ளே யுருப்பவிர் வெஞ்சுரங் கடந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Not stopping, they cross the burning desert within a single day, as quickly as the wealth of one who seeks the generous fame that stands in an unstable world goes from him, as desire goes from the hearts of the learned when a young woman's messenger comes, and as the anger of the holy passes.","n":"The three similes are three things that do not stay, and each is praise: the fame-seeker's wealth leaves because he gives it away, the learned man's desire leaves because he masters it, the anger of the holy leaves because it is brief. கடவுளர் may mean gods or the holy ascetics; the second simile's line (தூதின் ... கலைவலார் நெஞ்சிற் காமம்) is compressed and its sense is my best construal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pugazh","vekuli","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2107","lb":"CC2107","ev":"The company crosses the desert in one day.","cl":[["in an unstable world it is generous fame that stands, and the wealth of one who seeks it does not stay","general","asserted",1,"narrator"],["the anger of holy men does not last","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2108":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2108,"t":["புதுக்கலம் போலும் பூங்கனி யாலும் பொன்னிணர்ப் பிண்டியும் பொருந்தி","மதுக்கலந் தூழ்த்துச் சிலம்பிவீழ் வனபோன் மலர்சொரி வகுளமு மயங்கிக்","கதிர்த்ததண் பூணி கம்புடாழ் பீலிக் கனைகுர னாரைவண் டான","மெதிர்த்ததண் புனல்சூ ழின்னதிக் கரைமே லிளையவ ரயாவுயிர்த் தெழுந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"On the bank of this river, ringed by cool water where bakula trees shed flowers as if honey dripped from them and the hum of herons and bees meets the stream, the young men breathe out their weariness and rise.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2108","lb":"CC2108","ev":"The young men rest on a river bank after the desert and set out again.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2109":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2109,"t":["அள்ளிலைப் பலவி னளிந்து வீழ் சுளையுங் கனிந்துவீழ் வாழையின் பழனும்","புள்ளிவா ழலவன் பொறிவரிக் கமஞ்சூன் ஞெண்டினுக் குய்த்துநோய் தணிப்பான்","பள்ளிவாய் நந்து மாமையும் பணித்துப் பன்மலர் வழிபடக் குறைக்கும்","வெள்ளநீர்ப் படப்பை விதையம்வந் தடைந்தே வேந்தனுக் குணர்த்தமுன் விடுத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Reaching the water-rich groves of Videham, where the spotted crab carries fallen jackfruit and banana to ease the craving of its pregnant mate, they send men ahead to tell the king.","n":"The crab feeding its pregnant mate is the last of the journey's small domestic scenes before the human welcome begins. The third line, on the snail and the turtle, is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2109","lb":"CC2109","ev":"The company reaches Videham and sends word ahead to its king.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2110":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2110,"t":["வீட்டிடந் தோறும் வில்லக விரல்போற் பொருந்திநின் றொருங்கெதிர் கொள்கென்","றேட்டின்மேற் றீட்டித் திருவெழுத் திட்டாங் கிறைவனுந் தமர்களைப் பணிப்ப","நாட்டகத் தமிர்து நளிகட லமிர்து நல்வரை யமிர்தமு மல்லாக்","காட்டகத் தமிர்துங் காண்வரக் குவவிக் கண்ணகன் புறவெதிர் கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The king writes his order on the palm-leaf in the royal hand, that his people in every house should stand close together like the fingers in a bow-guard and receive the guest as one, and they heap up the nectars of the land, the sea, the hills and the forest and go out to meet him on the wide outskirts.","n":"The four nectars (land, sea, hill, forest) gather the landscapes the company has just crossed into the welcome itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","vendhan","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2110","lb":"CC2110","ev":"The king of Videham orders his people by written decree to receive Civakan, and they meet him with the produce of all four lands.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","தமர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2111":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2111,"t":["பொருமத யானைப் புணர்மருப் பேய்ப்பப் பொன்சுமந் தேந்திய முலையா","ரெரிமலர்ச் செவ்வாய் திறந்துதே னூற வேத்துவார் பூக்கடூய்த் தொழுவார்","வருகுலைக் கமுகும் வாழையு நடுவார் வரையுமி ழாவிபோன் மாடத்","தருநறும் புகையு மேந்துவா ரூர்தோ றமரர்த முலகமொத் ததுவே."],"p":"","r":"Women with gold-laden breasts open their red mouths in praise, strew flowers and bow, plant fruiting areca and plantain, and carry fragrant smoke like a mountain's mist, so that every town is like the world of the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2111","lb":"CC2111","ev":"The women of the towns line the road, praising and worshipping the guest.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2112":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2112,"t":["பாடி னருவிப் பயங்கெழு மாமலை","மாட நகரத்து வாயிலுங் கோயிலு","மாடம் பலமு மரங்கமுஞ் சாலையுஞ்","சேடனைக் காணிய சென்றுதொக் கதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The gates and temples, the halls, theatres and avenues of the mansioned city, like a great mountain loud with falls, all gather to see the noble one.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2112","lb":"CC2112","ev":"The whole city gathers to see Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2113":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2113,"t":["பல்கதி ராரமும் பூணும் பருமித்துக்","கொல்சின வெந்தொழிற் கோடேந் திளமுலை","நல்லெழின் மங்கையர் நன்னுதற் சூட்டிய","வெல்கதிர்ப் பட்டம் விளங்கிற் றொருபால்."],"p":"","r":"On one side the bright golden bands tied on the brows of beautiful women, decked with many-rayed necklaces and ornaments, their young breasts fierce as tusks, shine.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2113","lb":"CC2113","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2114":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2114,"t":["சுண்ணமுஞ் சூட்டுஞ் சொரிந்து வார்குழற்","கண்ணிம காரொடு காற்சிலம் பார்த்தெழ","வண்ணலைக் காணிய வார்த்திற் போதரும்","வண்ண மகளிர் வனப்பிற் றொருபால்."],"p":"","r":"On one side is the beauty of the women who come out in throngs to see the lord, scattering powder and wreaths, their long hair and the anklets on their feet sounding as they rise.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2114","lb":"CC2114","ev":"The women run out in crowds to see Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2115":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2115,"t":["எதிர்நலப் பூங்கொடி யெள்ளிய சாயற்","கதிர்நல மங்கையர் காறொடர்ந் தோட","முதிரா விளமுலை முத்தொடு பொங்க","வதிரரிக் கிண்கிணி யார்க்கு மொருபால்."],"p":"","r":"On one side the bright women, whose grace mocks the flowering creeper, run on each other's heels, pearls heaving on their young breasts and their tinkling anklet bells ringing.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2115","lb":"CC2115","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2116":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2116,"t":["கருங்க ணிளமுலை கச்சற வீக்கி","மருங்கு றளர மழைமருண் மாட","நெருங்க விறைகொண்ட நேரிழை யார்தம்","பெருங்க ணலமரும் பெற்றித் தொருபால்."],"p":"","r":"On one side the well-jewelled women who have crowded the cloud-touching mansions, breasts bound tight in bodices and waists sagging, let their large eyes roam.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2116","lb":"CC2116","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2117":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2117,"t":["மின்னு குழையினர் கோதையர் மின்னுயர்","பொன்வரை மாடம் புதையப் பொறிமயி","றுன்னிய கோதைக் குழாமெனத் தொக்கவர்","மன்னிய கோல மலிந்த தொருபால்."],"p":"","r":"On one side, women with flashing earrings and garlands fill the golden mansions until they are hidden, gathered like a flock of spotted peacocks, rich in their fixed array.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2117","lb":"CC2117","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2118":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2118,"t":["பாடன் மகளிரும் பல்கலை யேந்தல்கு","லாடன் மகளிரு மாவண வீதி தொ","றோட வுதிர்ந்த வணிகல முக்கவை","நீடிருள் போழு நிலைமைத் தொருபால்."],"p":"","r":"On one side the singing women and the dancing women with many-stranded girdles run through every market street, and the ornaments that fall and break from them split the long darkness with their light.","n":"The printed line division cuts words across lines (அல்கு/ல், தொ/றோட); read across, the sense is clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2118","lb":"CC2118","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2119":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2119,"t":["கோதையுந் தாரும் பிணங்கக் கொடுங்குழைக்","காதன் மகளிரு மைந்தருங் காணிய","வீதியு மேலும் மிடைந்து மிடைமலர்த்","தாதடுத் தெங்குந் தவிசொத் ததுவே."],"p":"","r":"With women of curved earrings and men alike crowding the street and the terraces above to see, garlands tangle with garlands and the pollen of the flowers makes every place like a spread carpet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2119","lb":"CC2119","ev":"Men and women crowd the street and the roofs.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2120":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2120,"t":["மானக் கவரி மணிவண் டகற்றவங்","கானை யெருத்தத் தமா குமரனிற்","சேனைக் கடலிடைச் செல்வனைக் கண்டுவந்","தேனை யவரு மெடுத்துரைக் கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the lord on the elephant's neck, like the young war god, in the midst of a sea of army while yak-tail whisks drive the bees from him, the others too rejoice and cry out.","n":"அமா குமரனின் is probably for அமர் குமரனின், Kumaran of battle, that is Murugan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2120","lb":"CC2120","ev":"Civakan rides into the city on an elephant amid his army, and the crowd raises a cry.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2121":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2121,"t":["தேமல ரங்கட் டிருவே புகுதக","மாமலர்க் கோதை மணாளன் புகுதக","காமன் புகுதக காளை புகுதக","நாம வெழில்விஞ்சை நம்பி புகுதக."],"p":"","r":"Let fortune enter, with lovely eyes of honeyed flowers; let the bridegroom of the great flower garland enter; let Kama enter, let the bull among men enter; let the famed and handsome Nambi of the Vidyadharas enter.","n":"Each title recalls something told earlier: the bridegroom of the marriages, the Kama the women took him for, the one bound to the Vidyadharas through Gandharvadattai's marriage. திருவே may hail him as fortune itself or as the one who has fortune.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2121","lb":"CC2121","ev":"The crowd hails Civakan as he enters, giving him one title after another.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாந்தர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2122":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2122,"t":["மின்றோய் வரைகொன்ற வேலோன் புகுதக","வின்றேன் கமழ்தா ரியக்கன் புகுதக","வென்றோன் புகுதக வீரன் புகுதக","வென்றே நகர மெதிர்கொண் டதவே."],"p":"","r":"Let the one of the spear who slew the lightning-touching mountain enter; let the Yaksha of the sweet-honeyed garland enter; let the victor enter, let the hero enter: so the city came out to meet him.","n":"The spear-bearer who slew the mountain is Murugan who split the Krauncha; the Yaksha likely recalls Sudanjanan, the yaksha god who befriended him. The acclaim gives Civakan the god's names without the epic claiming he is the god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2122","lb":"CC2122","ev":"The city welcomes Civakan with acclaim.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாந்தர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2123":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2123,"t":["இடிநறுஞ் சுண்ணஞ் சிதறியெச் சாருங்","கடிகமழ் மாலையுங் கண்ணியுஞ் சிந்தித்","துடியடு நுண்ணிடைத் தொண்டையஞ் செவ்வாய்","வடியடு கண்ணியர் வாழ்த்துபு நிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Scattering fragrant pounded powder on every side and showering scented garlands and wreaths, women with waists that shame the drum, red mouths like the tondai fruit and eyes that shame the blade stand blessing him.","n":"துடியடு and வடியடு are best read with அடு, 'to defeat' (a waist that outdoes the drum's narrow middle, eyes that outdo the blade), not as lost -யொடு.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2123","lb":"CC2123","ev":"The women shower Civakan with powder and garlands and bless him.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2124":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2124,"t":["சுரும்பிமிர் மாலை தொழுவனர் நீட்டி","யிரும்பிடி நின்னடை கற்ற வெமக்கு","விரும்பினை யாய்விடின் மெல்ல நடமோ","கருங்கணிற் காமனைக் காணமற் றென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Holding out bee-swarmed garlands in worship, the women say: great she-elephant, we learned our gait from you; if you love us, walk slowly, so that we may see Kama with our dark eyes.","n":"The conventional figure that women walk like a she-elephant becomes a claim of kinship on the beast: having taught us our gait, she owes us a slow one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2124","lb":"CC2124","ev":"The women beg the elephant carrying Civakan to walk slowly so they can look at him longer.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்","பிடி"],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2125":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2125,"t":["மடநடை பெண்மை வனப்பென்ப தோராய்","கடுநடை கற்றாய் கணவ னிழப்பாய்","பிடியலை பாவி யெனப்பூண் பிறழ்ந்து","புடைமுலை விம்மப் புலந்தனர் நிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Others sulk, their ornaments shifting and breasts heaving: you have not understood that a gentle gait is the beauty of womanhood; you have learned a rough gait; you will lose your husband; you are no she-elephant, you wretch.","n":"The rule of womanly gait is put playfully in the mouths of women who are themselves running in the streets after a stranger; the epic neither endorses nor answers it here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2125","lb":"CC2125","ev":"Some of the women scold the elephant for carrying Civakan past too fast.","cl":[["a gentle gait is the beauty of womanhood, and a woman who walks roughly will lose her husband","general","reported",1,"city_women"]],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்","பிடி"],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2126":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2126,"t":["மயிர்வாய்ச் சிறுகட் பெருஞ்செவி மாத்தாட்","செயிர்தீர் திரள்கைச் சிறுபிடி கேள்வ","னயிரா வணத்தொடு சூளுறு மைய","னுயிர்கா வலற்கொண் டுதவநில் வென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"And others say to the tusker, husband of the small she-elephant with hairy mouth, small eyes, large ears and great feet, who vies with Airavata: carry the lord who guards our lives and stand and help us.","n":"The address turns from the she-elephant of 2124-2125 to her mate; which beast Civakan rides the verses leave unclear. The last line (உயிர்கா வலற்கொண் டுதவநில்) is hard to construe: 'the guardian of life' may be Civakan, guardian of their lives, or the elephant asked to guard his.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2126","lb":"CC2126","ev":"Women ask the tusker to halt with Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்","பிடி"],"ch":"cc:91"},"cc:2127":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2127,"t":["கருனைக் கவளந் தருதுங் கமழ்தா","ரருமை யழகிற் கரசனை நாளைத்","திருமலி வீதியெஞ் சேரிக் கொணர்மோ","வெரிமணி மாலை யிளம்பிடி யென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The women of the street call to Civakan's young she-elephant: bring the king of rare beauty to our street tomorrow, and we will give you balls of rice.","n":"The women bargain with the elephant, not the man: desire speaks through a bribe of food (கருனைக் கவளம்) to his mount, as if the beast decides where the prince goes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","virundhu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2127","lb":"CC2127","ev":"The women of the street ask Civakan's she-elephant to bring him to their street the next day.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்","இளம்பிடி"],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2128":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2128,"t":["என்னோர் மருங்கினு மேத்தி யெரிமணிப்","பொன்னார் கலையினர் பொற்பூஞ் சிலம்பினர்","மின்னார் குழையினர் கோதையர் வீதியுண்","மன்ன குமரனை வாழ்த்தி மகிழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"On every side women in gem girdles, gold anklets and bright earrings praise the prince in the street and rejoice.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2128","lb":"CC2128","ev":"The adorned women of the city praise the prince as he passes.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2129":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2129,"t":["விளங்குபாற் கடலிற் பொங்கி வெண்டிரை யெழுவ வேபோற்","றுளங்கொளி மாடத் துச்சித் துகிற்கொடி நுடங்கும் வீதி","யுளங்கழித் துருவப் பைந்தார் மன்னவன் கோயில் சேர்ந்தா","னிளங்கதிர்ப் பருதி பௌவத் திறுவரை யிருந்த தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Through the street where cloth flags wave on the bright mansions like white surf on the milk sea, Civakan reaches the king's palace, like the young sun resting on the mountain at the sea's edge.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2129","lb":"CC2129","ev":"Civakan passes through the bannered street and reaches the king's palace.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2130":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2130,"t":["இழையொளி பரந்த கோயி லினமலர்க் குவளைப் பொற்பூ","விழைதகு கமல வட்டத் திடைவிராய்ப் பூத்த வேபோற்","குழையொளி முகமுங் கோலக் கொழுங்கயற் கண்ணுந் தோன்ற","மழைமின்னுக் குழாத்தின் மாலை மங்கையர் மயங்கி நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"In the palace bright with jewels, the women stand dazed in clusters like lightning in a cloud, their faces and carp-like eyes showing like blue lilies set among lotuses.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2130","lb":"CC2130","ev":"The palace women stand gazing at Civakan, bewildered.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2131":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2131,"t":["எரிக்குழாஞ் சுடரும் வையே லேந்தலைக் கண்டு கோயிற்","றிருக்குழா மனைய பட்டத் தேவியர் மகிழ்ந்து செய்ய","வரிக்குழா நெடுங்க ணாரக் கொப்புளித் துமிழ வம்பூ","விரைக்குழா மாலைத் தேனும் வண்டுமுண் டொழுக நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the bearer of the flaming spear, the crowned queens, like a host of goddesses of fortune, rejoice and stand, their streaked eyes brimming as if spilling over, honey and bees flowing from their garlands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2131","lb":"CC2131","ev":"The king's queens look on Civakan with joy.","cl":[],"ag":["பட்டத் தேவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2132":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2132,"t":["அலங்கறா தவிழ்ந்து சோர வல்குற்பொற் றோரை மின்னச்","சிலம்பின்மேற் பஞ்சி யார்ந்த சீறடி வலத்த தூன்றி","நலந்துறை போய நங்கை தோழியைப் புல்லி நின்றா","ளிலங்கொளி மணித்தொத் தீன்ற வேந்துபொற் கொடியோ டொப்பாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her garland loosening and slipping, her girdle flashing, the maiden accomplished in beauty stands embracing her friend, her small foot set on her anklet, like a golden creeper that has put out clusters of gems.","n":"The verse names her only as நங்கை; that she is the king's daughter Ilakkanai, the bride the patikam promised, is known from the story, not from this verse. Leaning on the friend is the shy girl's stance at first sight.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2132","lb":"CC2132","ev":"A maiden of the palace stands leaning on her friend as Civakan arrives.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2133":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2133,"t":["தாமரைப் போதிற் பூத்த தண்ணறுங் குவளைப் பூப்போற்","காமரு முகத்திற் பூத்த கருமழைத் தடங்கண் டம்மாற்","றேமலர் மார்பி னானை நோக்கினாள் செல்வன் மற்றப்","பூமலர்க் கோதை நெஞ்ச மூழ்கிப்புக் கொளித்திட் டானே."],"p":"","r":"Her dark rain-cloud eyes, flowering in her face like blue lilies in a lotus, look at the man of the honeyed chest, and the prosperous one plunges into the heart of the flower-garlanded girl and hides there.","n":"The look is hers and the entering is his: her eyes act, but it is he who is said to sink in and hide (ஒளித்திட்டான்), so the first-sight love is told as his capture by her glance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2133","lb":"CC2133","ev":"The maiden looks at Civakan and he enters her heart.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2134":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2134,"t":["விண்ணாறு செல்வார் மனம்பேதுறப் போந்து வீங்கிப்","பண்ணாறு சொல்லாண் முலைபாரித்த வென்று நோக்கக்","கண்ணாறு சென்ற களியைங்கணைக் காம னன்ன","புண்ணாறு வேலான் மனமூழ்கினள் பொன்ன னாளே."],"p":"","r":"Her breasts swelling so that even those who travel the sky are troubled, and as he looks on them, the girl like gold plunges into the heart of the one of the wounding spear, who is like Kama of the five arrows.","n":"With 2133 the entering is made mutual: he sinks into her heart, then she into his, the same verb மூழ்கு used both ways.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2134","lb":"CC2134","ev":"The maiden in turn enters Civakan's heart.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2135":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2135,"t":["மைதோய் வரையி னிழியும்புலி போல மைந்தன்","பெய்தாம மாலைப் பிடியின்னிழிந் தேகி மன்னர்","கொய்தாம மாலைக் கொழும்பொன்முடி தேய்த்தி லங்குஞ்","செய்பூங் கழலைக் தொழுதான்சென்னி சோத்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Like a tiger coming down from a dark mountain, the young man gets down from the garlanded she-elephant, goes and bows, his head lowered, at the king's anklet that is rubbed bright by the crowns of kings.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2135","lb":"CC2135","ev":"Civakan dismounts and bows at the king's feet.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2136":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2136,"t":["பொன்னாங் குவட்டிற் பொலிவெய்தித் திரண்ட திண்டோன்","மன்னன் மகிழ்ந்து மருமானை விடாது புல்லித்","தன்னன்பு கூரத் தடந்தாமரைச் செங்கண் முத்த","மின்னும் மணிப்பூண் விரைமார்ப நனைப்ப நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of strong shoulders, rejoicing, embraces his nephew without letting go, and as his love swells, tears like pearls from his red lotus eyes wet the young man's fragrant jewelled chest.","n":"மருமான் is the sister's son: the welcome is the mother's kin receiving the child of the sister whose husband was killed, and the tears carry that past, though the verse names only love (அன்பு).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2136","lb":"CC2136","ev":"Govindan embraces his nephew and weeps over him.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2137":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2137,"t":["ஆனாது வேந்தன் கலுழ்ந்தானெனக் கோயி லெல்லாந்","தானாது மின்றி மயங்கித்தடங் கண்பெய்ம் மாரி","தேனார் மலாபுர்த் தொழுகச்சிலம் பிற்சி லம்புங்","கானார் மயிலின் கணம்போற்கலுழ் வுற்ற தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"When the king wept unceasingly, the whole palace, losing itself, wept too, the rain of tears pouring over flower garlands, like a flock of peacocks crying on a mountain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2137","lb":"CC2137","ev":"The whole palace weeps with the king.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்","கோயில்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2138":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2138,"t":["பகைநரம் பிசையுங் கேளாப் பைங்கதிர்ப் பசும்பொற்கோயில்","வகைநலம் வாடி யெங்கு மழுகுரன் மயங்கி முந்நீ","ரகமடை திறந்த தேபோ லலறக்கோக் கிளைய நங்கை","மிகைநலத் தேவி தானே விலாவணை நீக்கி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"Grief spread through the golden palace where no music was heard, the cries mingling like the sea breaking its dam, until the king's younger sister, the queen of surpassing grace, herself put an end to the lament.","n":"கோக்கு இளைய நங்கை is the king's younger sister, Vijayai, who had gone ahead to her brother (1932): the one with most cause to weep is the one who ends the weeping.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2138","lb":"CC2138","ev":"Vijayai stops the palace's lament.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2139":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2139,"t":["பல்கதிர் மணியும் பொன்னும் பவழமுங் குயிற்றிச் செய்த","செல்வப்பொற் கிடுகு சூழ்ந்த சித்திர கூட மெங்கு","மல்குபூந் தாமந் தாழ்ந்து மணிபுகை கமழ வேந்தன்","வெல்புகழ் பரவ மாதோ விதியுள யெய்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Into the painted hall ringed with gold panels inlaid with gems, gold and coral, hung with garlands and fragrant with incense, the king went in due form while his victorious fame was praised.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2139","lb":"CC2139","ev":"The king enters the painted hall in state.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2140":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2140,"t":["எரிமணி யடைப்பை செம்பொற் படியக மிலங்கு பொன்வாள்","கருமணி முகடு வேய்ந்த கஞ்சனை கவரி கொண்ட","வருமுலை மகளிர் வைத்து வான்றவி சடுத்து நீங்கப்","பெருமக னெண்ணங் கொள்வா னமைச்சரோ டேறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"When the women had set down the gem betel pouch, the gold vessel, the shining sword, the casket and the whisk, laid out the high seat and withdrawn, the great one went up with his ministers to take counsel.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","soozhchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2140","lb":"CC2140","ev":"Govindan goes up with his ministers to take counsel.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்","கோவிந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2141":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2141,"t":["உலந்தநா ளவர்க்குத் தோன்றா தொளிக்குமீன் குளிக்குங் கற்பிற்","புலந்தவே னெடுங்கட் செவ்வாய்ப் புதவிநாட் பயந்த நம்பி","சிலம்புநீர்க் கடலந் தானைச் சீதத்தற் கரசு நாட்டிக்","குலந்தரு கொற்ற வேலான் கொடிநகர் காக்க வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of the lineage-given spear names Civakan the son born of a woman whose chastity outshines the star that hides from those whose days are ended, and orders that Sitattan of the sea-like army be set in rule to guard the bannered city.","n":"The star unseen by the dying is Arundhati, the emblem of a wife's chastity; Vijayai's karpu is said to make it hide. Who is set in rule and over which city is not certain: the line can be read as Civakan setting up Sitattan, or as the king's own order.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","soozhchi","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2141","lb":"CC2141","ev":"Govindan orders that Sitattan be put in charge to guard the city.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவிந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2142":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2142,"t":["மாற்றவ னொற்ற ரொற்றா வகையினின் மறைய நம்பிக்","காற்றின் தோழர்க் கெல்லா மணிகல மடிசி லாடை","வேற்றுமை யின்றி வேண்டுட் டமைத்தன னருளி யிப்பா","லேற்றுரி முரச நாண வெறிதிரை முழக்கிற் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"So that the enemy's spies could not single them out, the king gave all Civakan's companions jewels, food and clothing as he wished, without any distinction from his own, and then spoke in a voice like the roaring sea that shamed the bull-hide drum.","n":"The gifts are a disguise: honouring the guests exactly as his own men makes them invisible to Kattiyankaran's spies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","virundhu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2142","lb":"CC2142","ev":"Govindan provides for Civakan's companions as for his own people so that spies cannot pick them out.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2143":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2143,"t":["கட்டியங் கார னம்மைக் காண்பதே கரும மாக","வொட்டித் தான் விடுத்த வோலை யுளபொரு ளுரைமி னென்னத்","தொட்டுமேற் பொறியை நீக்கி மன்னனைத் தொழுது தோன்ற","விட்டலர் நாகப் பைந்தார் விரிசிகன் கூறு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king bids them tell what is in the letter Kattiyankaran sent, with a pledge, aiming at a meeting; Virisikan of the naga-flower garland breaks the seal, bows to the king and reads it out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2143","lb":"CC2143","ev":"Virisikan opens Kattiyankaran's letter and reads it to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்","விரிசிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2144":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2144,"t":["விதையத்தார் வேந்தன் காண்க கட்டியங் கார னோலை","புதையவிப் பொழிலைப் போர்த்தோர் பொய்ப்பழி பரந்த தென்மேற்","கதையெனக் கருதல் செய்யான் மெய்யெனத் தானுங் கொண்டான்","சிதையவென் னெஞ்சம் போழ்ந்து தெளிப்பினுந் தெளிநர் யாரே."],"p":"","r":"The letter begins: let the king of Videham see this letter of Kattiyankaran; a false blame covering this world has spread over me, people took it as truth and not as a tale, and even if I split my heart to show it, who would be convinced?","n":"The blame he calls false is the killing of Saccandan, which the narration told plainly (290). The letter's voice is the usurper's own; the reader knows it lies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","vanjam","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2144","lb":"CC2144","ev":"In his letter Kattiyankaran protests that the blame laid on him is false.","cl":[],"ag":["விரிசிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","கோவிந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2145":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2145,"t":["படுமணிப் பைம்பொற் சூழிப் பகட்டின மிரியப் பாய்ந்து","கொடிநெடுந் தோக ணூறிக் கொய்யுளை மாக்கள் குத்தி","யிடுகொடி யணிந்த மார்ப ரிருவிசும் பேறச் சீறி","யடுகளிற் றசனி வேக மலமர வதனை நோனான்."],"p":"","r":"The letter goes on: the killing elephant Asanivegam charged, scattering the herds of belled tuskers, smashing the tall-bannered chariots, goring the plumed horses and hurling warriors to the sky, and he could not bear it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2145","lb":"CC2145","ev":"Kattiyankaran's letter tells that the elephant Asanivegam ran amok and the king could not bear it.","cl":[],"ag":["அசனிவேகம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","கோவிந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2146":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2146,"t":["நூற்றுவர் பாகர் தம்மைப் பிளந்துயி ருண்ட தென்னு","மாற்றத்தைக் கேட்டுச் சென்று மதக்களி றடக்கி மேற்கொண்","டாற்றலங் கந்து சோத்தி யாப்புற வீக்கும் போழ்திற்","கூற்றென முழங்கி வீழ்த்துக் கொல்லக்கோ லிளகிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing that it had torn apart a hundred mahouts and eaten their lives, he went, subdued the musth elephant and mounted it, and as he was binding it fast to the post, it roared like Death, threw him down and killed him, and so the sceptre slackened.","n":"This is the usurper's cover story. The letter never names the king; the killed one is Saccandan by the story. கோல் இளகிற்று, 'the sceptre slackened', turns a regicide into an accident of the royal stable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","saakkaadu","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2146","lb":"CC2146","ev":"Kattiyankaran's letter claims the king was killed by the elephant he tried to tame.","cl":[],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","அசனிவேகம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","கோவிந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2147":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2147,"t":["தனக்கியா னுயிரு மீவேன் றான்வரப் பழியு நீங்கு","மெனக்கினி யிறைவன் றானே யிருநிலக் கிழமை வேண்டி","நினைத்துத்தா னெடிதல் செல்லா தென்சொலே தெளிந்து நொய்தாச்","சினக்களி யானை மன்னன் வருகெனச் செப்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"For him I would give even my life; if he comes, the blame will go; henceforth he alone is my lord; not lingering to think of claim to the wide earth, trusting my word, let the king of angry elephants come quickly, the letter says.","n":"The pledge of life and the owning of a new lord repeat the bow Kattiyankaran made to Saccandan when he took the rule (203); the letter is bait to draw the king into reach.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","panivu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2147","lb":"CC2147","ev":"Kattiyankaran's letter invites Govindan to come quickly and promises him loyalty.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","கோவிந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2148":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2148,"t":["ஓலையுட் பொருளைக் கேட்டே யொள்ளெயி றிலங்க நக்குக்","காலனை யளியன் றானே கையினால் விளிக்கு மென்னா","நூல்வலீ ரிவனைக் கொல்லு நுண்மதிச் சூழ்ச்சி யீதே","போல்வதொன் றில்லை யென்றான் புனைமணிப் பொன்செய் பூணான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing what the letter held, the king of gem-set gold ornaments laughed, his bright teeth showing, and said: the wretch calls Death with his own hand; you learned in the books, there is no subtle scheme for killing him like this.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kootru","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2148","lb":"CC2148","ev":"Govindan laughs at the letter and tells his ministers it gives the means to kill Kattiyankaran.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவிந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2149":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2149,"t":["கள்ளத்தா னம்மைக் கொல்லக் கருதினா னாமுந் தன்னைக்","கள்ளத்தா லுயிரை யுண்ணக் கருதினே மிதனை யாரு","முள்ளத்தா லுமிழ வேண்டா வுறுபடை வந்து கூட","வள்ளுவார் முரச மூதூ ரறைகென வருளி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He thought to kill us by guile; we too have resolved to take his life by guile; let none of you let it out of your heart; let the drum of the old city proclaim that the great army gather, the king commanded.","n":"The counter-deceit is a king's policy stated in council, not a maxim the narrator endorses; the verse also orders secrecy (உள்ளத்தால் உமிழ வேண்டா).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","soozhchi","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2149","lb":"CC2149","ev":"Govindan resolves to meet Kattiyankaran's guile with guile and orders the army gathered.","cl":[["one who plots to kill by deceit may rightly be killed by deceit","general","reported",2,"govindan"]],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவிந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2150":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2150,"t":["கட்டியங் கார னோடு காவல னொருவ னானான்","விட்டுநீர் நெல்லும் பொன்னும் வழங்குமின் விளைவ கூறி","னொட்டல னிறைவன் சொன்னீர் நாநும வல்ல வென்னக்","கொட்டினான் றடங்கண் வள்வார்க் குளிறிடி முரச மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The drummer beat the thundering drum: the guardian has become one with Kattiyankaran; give out paddy and gold freely; if you speak of what will come of it, the lord will not allow it; if you speak, your tongues are not your own.","n":"The public face of the plot: a feast of alliance and a threat against anyone who guesses aloud what it is for. விட்டுநீர் is read as 'freely'; the word division is not sure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","natpu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2150","lb":"CC2150","ev":"The drum proclaims in the city that the king has made alliance with Kattiyankaran and forbids talk of it.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசு அறைவோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசு அறைவோன்",""],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2151":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2151,"t":["விண்டவ ருடலங் கீறிச் சுளித்துநின் றழலும் வேழ","மொண்கொடி யுருவத் திண்டே ரொளிமயிர்ப் புரவி பண்ணி","வண்கழ லணிந்து மள்ளர் வாள்வலம் பிடித்து நாளைத்","தெண்டிரைப் பரப்பு நாணத் திருநகர்த் தொகுக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Tomorrow let the musth elephants that rip the bodies of foes, the bannered chariots and the bright-maned horses be made ready, and the warriors in hero-anklets, swords in hand, gather in the city so that the sea is put to shame, he said.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2151","lb":"CC2151","ev":"Govindan orders the elephants, chariots, horses and warriors to gather in the city the next day.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவிந்தன்","அமைச்சர்"],"ch":"cc:92"},"cc:2152":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2152,"t":["ஏற்றுரி போர்த்த வள்வா ரிடிமுர சறைந்த பின்னாட்","காற்றெறி கடலிற் சங்கு முழவமு முரசு மார்ப்பக்","கூற்றுடன் றனைய தானை கொழுநில நெளிய வீண்டிப்","பாற்கடற் பரப்பின் வெள்வாட் சுடரொளி பரந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The day after the strapped, bull-hide drum had proclaimed the muster, conches, muzhavus and drums roared like a wind-beaten sea, and an army fierce as angry Death massed until the rich earth sagged, the gleam of its white swords spreading like the milk ocean.","n":"பின்னாள் ties the muster to the drum of 2150 and 2151, which announced the king's false alliance with the usurper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2152","lb":"CC2152","ev":"The day after the proclamation, the Videha king's army musters to the noise of conch and drum.","cl":[],"ag":["தானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2153":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2153,"t":["புதையிரு ளிரியப் பொங்கிக் குங்குமக் கதிர்க ளோக்கி","யுதையத்தி னெற்றி சோந்த வொண்சுடர்ப் பருதி போலச்","சுதையொளி மாடத் துச்சி வெண்குடை நீழற் றோன்றி","விதையத்தார் வென்றி வேந்தன் விழுப்படை காணு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Like the bright sun that rises on the brow of the eastern mountain scattering the thick dark with saffron rays, the victorious king of the Videhas appears under a white umbrella on top of his lime-white mansion and looks over his fine army.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2153","lb":"CC2153","ev":"The Videha king reviews his army from the roof of his palace.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2154":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2154,"t":["அரும்பனைத் தடக்கை யபரகாத் திரம்வாய் வாலெயி றைந்தினுங் கொல்வ","கருங்கடற் சங்குங் கறந்தவான் பாலுங் கனற்றிய காலுகி ருடைய","பெரும்புலி முழக்கின் மாறெதிர் முழங்கிப் பெருவரை கீண்டிடுந் திறல","திருந்தியே ழுறுப்புந் திண்ணிலந் தோய்வ தீயுமிழ் தறுகணிற் சிறந்த."],"p":"","r":"The war elephants are described: trunks like palm trunks, killing with all five of their weapons, white as sea conch and fresh milk, roaring back at the great tiger's roar, strong enough to tear a mountain, their seven limbs well formed and touching the firm ground, and unmatched in fire-spitting fury.","n":"ஏழுறுப்புந் திண்ணிலந் தோய்வ names the mark of a fine elephant, seven parts reaching the ground; which five parts kill (ஐந்தினுங் கொல்வ) the verse does not list.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2154","lb":"CC2154","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2155":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2155,"t":["கவிழ்மணிப் புடைய கண்ணிழ னாறிற் கனன்றுதந் நிழலொடு மலைவ","வவிழ்புயன் மேக மனையமம் மதத்த வறுபதிற் றறுபதா நாகம்","புகழ்பருந் தார்ப்பப் பூமதம் பொழிவா னின்றன விராயிரங் கவுள்வண்","டிகழ்மதஞ் செறித்த விராயிரத் தைஞ்ஞா றிளையவு மத்துணைக் களிறே."],"p":"","r":"The elephants hung with bells on both flanks rage and fight their own shadows when they catch its scent, dark as loosening rain clouds and running with must, and the poet gives their numbers by the thousand: herds that pour must while kites cry over them, young ones, and as many tuskers.","n":"The counts are built from compounds (அறுபதிற்றறுபது, இராயிரம், இராயிரத்தைஞ்ஞூறு) whose grouping is not certain, so the total cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2155","lb":"CC2155","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2156":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2156,"t":["குந்தமே யயில்வாள் குனிசிலை மூன்றுங் குறைவிலார் கூற்றொடும் பொருவா","ரந்தர மாறா யானைகொண் டேறப் பறக்கெனிற் பறந்திடுந் திறலார்","முந்தமர் தம்முண் முழுமெயு மிரும்பு மேய்ந்திட வெஞ்சமம் விளைத்தார்","கொந்தழ லஞ்சாக் குஞ்சர மிவர்ந்தார் கோடியே விருத்தியா வுடையார்."],"p":"","r":"The elephant riders are masters of lance, sword and bow who would fight Death itself, men who could make their mounts fly at a word, whose whole bodies iron had bitten in earlier battles, who ride elephants unafraid of blazing fire, a crore of them living by this work.","n":"கோடியே விருத்தியா வுடையார் makes them a standing force living by war, not levies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2156","lb":"CC2156","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2157":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2157,"t":["குங்கும நறுநீர் பந்திநின் றாடுங் குதிரையா றாயிரத் திரட்டி","பொங்குவெண் மயிர்சூழ் பொற்படை பொலிந்த வறுபதி னாயிரம் புரவி","வெங்கணை தவிர்ப்ப வெள்ளிவெண் படைய வாய்விடி னிலவரை நில்லாப்","பைங்கதிர்க் கொட்டைக் கவரிசூழ்ந் தணிந்த பகரினத் தொகையன பாய்மா."],"p":"","r":"The horses are counted: twice six thousand that bathe in saffron water in their stalls, sixty thousand with white plumes and gold trappings, armoured in silver against arrows, that would not stay on the ground if let go, and a host past telling hung with green-rayed chowries.","n":"ஆறாயிரத் திரட்டி may be twice six thousand or a doubled troop of six thousand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2157","lb":"CC2157","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2158":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2158,"t":["வேய்நிறக் கரும்பின் வெண்ணிறப் பூம்போன் மிடைந்தொளிர் குந்தமும் வாளுந்","தோநிலை யரவின் றோற்றமே போலுஞ் சிலைகளும் பிறகளுந் துறைபோ","யூனமொன் றில்லா ருயர்குடிப் பிறந்தா ராயிர மடுகளங் கண்டார்","பானிலாப் பூணார் படைத்தொழிற் கலிமாப் பண்ணுறுத் தேறினா ரவரே."],"p":"","r":"The horsemen, skilled to the end in gleaming lance, sword, bows curved like a snake shedding its skin and other arms, flawless men of high birth who have seen a thousand battlefields and wear moon-white ornaments, mount their harnessed war horses.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2158","lb":"CC2158","ev":"The cavalry mount their harnessed horses.","cl":[],"ag":["தானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2159":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2159,"t":["தறுக ணாண்மைய தாமரை நிறத்தன தகைசான்","மறுவில் வான்குளம் புடையன மாளவத் தகத்த","பறையி னாலுவ படுசினை நாவலின் கனிபோற்","குறைவில் கோலத்த குளிர்புனற் சிந்துவின் கரைய."],"p":"","r":"Some of the horses are lotus-coloured and fierce, with flawless hooves, from Malava; others, perfect in form, coloured like the fruit of a full-branched jambu and dancing to the drum, come from the banks of the cool Sindhu.","n":"Whether the verse names one breed or two (Malava and the Sindhu) depends on how its four lines are grouped.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2159","lb":"CC2159","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2160":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2160,"t":["பார சூரவம் பல்லவ மெனும்பதிப் பிறந்த","வீர வாற்றல விளைகடுந் தேறலி னிறத்த","பாரிற் றோசெலிற் பழிபெரி துடைத்தென நாணிச்","சோரும் வார்புய றுளங்கவிண் புகுவன துரகம்."],"p":"","r":"Horses born in lands called Parasa, Suravam and Pallava, heroic in strength and coloured like strong ripe toddy, would think it a great disgrace to walk on the earth and, ashamed, leap into the sky so that the long rain clouds shake.","n":"பார சூரவம் பல்லவம் is a list of foreign horse countries whose division into names is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2160","lb":"CC2160","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2161":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2161,"t":["பீலி மாமயி லெருத்தெனப் பெருவனப் புடைய","மாலை மாரட்டத் தகத்தன வளரிளங் கிளியே","போலு மேனிய பொருகடற் கலத்தின்வந் திழிந்த","கோல நீர்ப்பவ ளக்குளம் புடையன குதிரை."],"p":"","r":"Horses beautiful as the peacock's neck, from the Maratta country, with bodies coloured like young parrots and hooves like sea coral, came down from ships that sail the battling sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2161","lb":"CC2161","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2162":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2162,"t":["இன்ன பொங்குளைப் புரவிபண் ணுறுத்தன வியறோ","பொன்னும் வெள்ளியு மணியினும் பொலிந்து வெண் மதியத்","தன்னை யூர்கொண்ட தகையன தொகைசொலி னறுநூ","றென்னு மீறுடை யிருபதி னாயிர மிறையே."],"p":"","r":"Chariots of fine make, harnessed to such full-maned horses, shine with gold, silver and gems as if they had taken the white moon for their home, and their number is twenty thousand and six hundred.","n":"இயறோ is read as இயல் தேர், and the count as twenty thousand ending in six hundred; வெண் மதியத் தன்னை யூர்கொண்ட is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2162","lb":"CC2162","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2163":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2163,"t":["நொச்சி மாமலர் நிறத்தன நொடிவரு முந்நீ","ருச்சி மாக்கதிர் போற்சுடு மொளிதிக ழயில்வா","ளெச்சத் தல்லவு மெறிபடை பயின்றுதம் மொன்னார்","நிச்சங் கூற்றினுக் கிடுபவர் தேர்மிசை யவரே."],"p":"","r":"The chariot warriors, with swords that burn like the sun at noon over the sea and practised in thrown weapons beyond the sword, feed their enemies to Death every day.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2163","lb":"CC2163","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2164":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2164,"t":["எயிற்றுப்படை யாண்மையினி னிடிக்கும்புலி யொப்பார்","பயிற்றியவில் வாள்பணிக்கும் வேலோடுடன் வல்லார்","துயிற்றியபல் கேள்வியினர் தூற்றிக்கொளப் பட்டா","ரயிற்றுப்படை யார்கண்மத யானைகத னறுப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The foot soldiers are like tigers striking with their teeth, trained in bow, sword and spear alike, hearers of much instruction, picked out from many, and able with sharp weapons to cut down the fury of rutting elephants.","n":"துயிற்றியபல் கேள்வியினர் is obscure; it may mean men whose much learning has settled, or something else.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2164","lb":"CC2164","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2165":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2165,"t":["காலனொடு சூழ்ந்தகடு நோய்களையு மொப்பா","ராலுங்கட றூர்த்தன்மலை யகழ்தலிவை வல்லார்","ஞாலமறி யாண்டொழிலர் நான்கிலக்க முள்ளார்","மேலுநும ராலுரிமை யுட்சிறப்புப் பெற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"They are like Death with its train of fierce diseases, able to fill the roaring sea and dig up mountains, renowned in manly work, four lakhs in number, who have besides won a place of special trust.","n":"மேலும் நுமரால் addresses someone as 'your people'; the addressee is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2165","lb":"CC2165","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2166":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2166,"t":["சிங்கத்துரி போர்த்தசெழுங் கேடகமும் வாளும்","பொங்குமயில் வேலும்பொரு வில்லுமுடன் பரப்பி","மங்குலிடை மின்னுமதி யுஞ்சுடரும் போல","வெங்கட்டொழிற் கூற்றுமரண் சேரவிரிந் தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Lion-skin shields, swords, spears and bows spread out together and flashed like lightning, moon and sun among clouds, so that even Death of the fierce eye took to a fortress.","n":"கூற்றுமரண் சேர is read as Death itself seeking shelter in fear; it could instead say the army spread to reach the enemy's fort.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2166","lb":"CC2166","ev":"The armed host spreads out its shields and weapons.","cl":[],"ag":["தானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2167":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2167,"t":["செம்பொ னீண்முடித் தேர்மன்னர் மன்னற்குப்","பைம்பொ னாழிதொட் டான்படை காட்டினா","னம்பொ னொண்கழ லானயி ராவணம்","வெம்ப வேறினன் வெல்கென வாழ்த்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"The one who wore the ring of fresh gold showed the army to the king of kings, the bright-anklet king mounted his Airavata of an elephant till it grew hot, and the host cheered 'may he win'.","n":"Who ஆழி தொட்டான் is, the commander or another, the verse does not say; அயிராவணம் names the elephant after Indra's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2167","lb":"CC2167","ev":"The army is presented to the king, who mounts his elephant amid cries of victory.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்","ஆழி தொட்டான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2168":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2168,"t":["சிறுவெண் சங்கு முரன்றன திண்முர","சறையு மாக்கடல் காரென வார்த்தன","நெறியி னல்கின புள்ளு நிமித்தமு","மிறைவன் கண்வல னாடிற் றியைந்தரோ."],"p":"","r":"Small white conches sounded, strong drums roared like the sea and the rain cloud, birds and other signs favoured the road, and the king's right eye throbbed in accord.","n":"The right eye's throb is a good sign here for a man; 2173 gives the same throb to the usurper's women as an ill one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2168","lb":"CC2168","ev":"Good omens attend the king's setting out.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2169":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2169,"t":["மல்லல் யானைக் கறங்கு மணியொலி","யல்ல தைங்கதி மான்கொழுந் தாரொலி","கல்லெ னார்ப்பொலி மிக்கொளிர் வாண்மினிற்","செல்லு மாக்கடல் போன்றது சேனையே."],"p":"","r":"With the ringing bells of the great elephants, the clatter of the five-gaited horses, the shout and the flashing swords like lightning, the army moved like a great sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2169","lb":"CC2169","ev":"The army marches.","cl":[],"ag":["தானை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2170":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2170,"t":["மாலை மாமதி வெண்குடை மல்கிய","கோவக் குஞ்சி நிழற்குளிர் பிச்சமுஞ்","சோலை யாய்ச்சொரி மும்மதத் தானிலம்","பாலை போய்மரு தம்பயந் திட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"Under white umbrellas like the evening moon and the cool shade of peacock-feather parasols, with the three musts of the elephants pouring down like a grove's rain, the land the army crossed turned from desert into fertile field.","n":"The hyperbole makes the army a giver of water: பாலை போய் மருதம் பயந்திட்டது turns a march into a change of tinai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2170","lb":"CC2170","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2171":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2171,"t":["மன்றன் மாமயி லார்த்தெழ மானினங்","கன்றி னோடு கலங்கின காற்பெய","வென்றி வேற்படை யஞ்சி வனத்தொடு","குன்றெ லாங்குடி போவன போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Peacocks of the commons rose screaming, herds of deer with their fawns scattered, and fearing the victorious spear-army it looked as if the hills and their forests were moving away.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2171","lb":"CC2171","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2172":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2172,"t":["படுகண் முழவி னிமிழருவி வரையுங் காடும் பலபோகி","யிடுமண் முழவி னிசையோவாவேமாங் கதநாட் டெய்தியபி","னெடுவெண் ணிலவி னெற்றிதோய் நிழலார் செம்பொற் புரிசையே","கடிமண் காவல் கருதினான் கோயி லாகக் கருதினான்."],"p":"","r":"Passing many hills and forests whose falling streams sounded like drums, after reaching the Emangatha country, the king took a golden walled place whose top touched the moonlight as the guard of his camp and as his palace.","n":"கடிமண் காவல் and கோயிலாகக் கருதினான் are read as camp and palace; the doubled கருதினான் is unusual.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2172","lb":"CC2172","ev":"The army reaches the Emangatha country and the king makes camp in a walled enclosure.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2173":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2173,"t":["போக மகளிர் வலக்கண்க டுடித்த பொல்லாக் கனாக்கண்டா","ராக மன்னற் கொளிமழுங்கிற் றஞ்சத் தக்க குரலினாற்","கூகை கோயிற் பகற்குழறக் கொற்ற முரசம் பாடவிந்து","மாக நெய்த்தோர் சொரிந்தெங்கு மண்ணும் விண்ணு மதிர்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"In the usurper's palace the pleasure women's right eyes throbbed and they saw evil dreams, the king's body lost its lustre, owls hooted by day in the palace with dreadful cries, the victory drum lost its voice, blood rained from the sky, and earth and sky shook.","n":"Each sign reverses one of 2168's: the throbbing right eye, the drum that now falls silent, the birds; the verse states the portents and does not say what they portend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","kanavu","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2173","lb":"CC2173","ev":"Ill omens fill Kattiyangaran's palace.","cl":[],"ag":["போக மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2174":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2174,"t":["கூற்றம் வந்து புறத்திறுத்த தறியான் கொழும்பொ னுலகாள்வான்","வீற்றிங் கிருந்தே னெனமகிழ்ந்து வென்றி வேழ மிருநூறுங்","காற்றிற் பரிக்குந் தோநூறுங் கடுங்கா லிவுளி யாயிரமும்","போற்றி விடுத்தான் புனைசெம்பொற் படையே யணிந்து புனைபூணான்."],"p":"","r":"Not knowing that Death had come and camped outside, the ruler of the rich land rejoiced that he sat there in splendour and with care sent two hundred victorious elephants, a hundred wind-swift chariots and a thousand fierce horses, decked in red-gold armour.","n":"கூற்றம் is the camped army itself; the narrator's word makes the guest Death before the host knows it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","vanjam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2174","lb":"CC2174","ev":"Kattiyangaran sends elephants, chariots and horses as gifts to the Videha king, whose army is camped near his city.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2175":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2175,"t":["மன்ன னாங்கோர் மதவேழம் வாரி மணாள னென்பதூஉ","மின்னங் கொடித்தோ விசயமும் புரவி பவன வேகமும்","பொன்னிற புனைந்து தான்போக்க நிகழ்வ தோரான் மகிழ்வெய்தி","முன்யான் விட்ட வினக்களிற்றி னிரட்டி விடுத்தா னெனப்புகழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king there sent back, decked in gold, one rutting elephant called Vari Manalan, the chariot Vijayam with its lightning banner and the horse Pavana Vegam, and the usurper, not seeing what was coming, was delighted and praised him for returning twice the elephants he had sent.","n":"நிகழ்வது ஓரான் gives the narrator's view; the usurper reads three named mounts as more than his hundreds, a measure of worth he does not see is bait.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","natpu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2175","lb":"CC2175","ev":"The Videha king returns named gifts, and Kattiyangaran, deceived, praises his generosity.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2176":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2176,"t":["வீங்குநீர் விதையத் தார்கோன் கட்டியங் காரன் றன்னோ","டாங்கவ னொருவ னாகி யன்பெனு மயில்கொள் வாளால்","வாங்கிக்கொண் டுயிரை யுண்பான் வஞ்சத்தாற் சூழ்ந்த வண்ண","மோங்குநீ ரோத வேலிக் குணரயா முரைத்து மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator announces that he will now tell the world how the Videha king, making himself one with Kattiyangaran, plotted by deceit to seize and devour his life with the sharp sword called love.","n":"அன்பெனு மயில்கொள் வாள் names the false friendship as the weapon, and வஞ்சம் is the narrator's own word for the hero's side's plan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","anbu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2176","lb":"CC2176","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:93"},"cc:2177":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2177,"t":["பெருமகன் காதற் பாவைப் பித்திகைப் பிணையன் மாலை","யொருமக ணோக்கி னாரை யுயிரோடும் போக டாத","திருமக ளவட்குப் பாலா னருந்திரி பன்றி யெய்த","வருமக னாகு மென்றாங் கணிமுர சறைவித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The great king has the drum proclaim that whoever shoots the hard-turning boar will be the husband of his beloved only daughter, a goddess of fortune who lets no man who looks at her go away with his life.","n":"The previous verse (2176) has just said that the Videha king made himself one with Kattiyangaran in friendship so as to take his life by deceit; read after it, the bride-contest is the lure. The king is not named here; he is named கோவிந்தன் in 2205. The daughter is praised, not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","soozhchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2177","lb":"CC2177","ev":"Govindan has the drum proclaim that the man who shoots the revolving boar shall marry his daughter.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2178":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2178,"t":["ஆய்மதக் களிறு திண்டே ரணிமணிப் புரவி யம்பொற்","காய்கதிர்ச் சிவிகை செற்றிக் கலந்த வைநுரைக ளாகத்","தோய்மழை யுலக வெள்ளந்தொன்னகர்த் தொக்க தேபோ","லாய்முடி யரச வெள்ள மணிநக ரீண்டிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Rutting elephants, sturdy chariots, jewelled horses and gold palanquins crowd together like foam on a flood, and a flood of crowned kings gathers in the fair city as the waters of the world's rain gather in the old town.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2178","lb":"CC2178","ev":"Crowned kings with their elephants, chariots, horses and palanquins flood into the city for the contest.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2179":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2179,"t":["நல்லவள் வனப்பு வாங்க நகைமணி மாலை மார்பர்","வில்லன்றே யுவனிப் பாரும் வெங்கணை திருத்து வாரும்","சொல்லுமி னெமக்கு மாங்கோர் சிலைதொட நாளென் பாரும்","பல்சரம் வழங்கு வாரும் பரிவுகொள் பவரு மானார்."],"p":"","r":"Drawn by the girl's beauty, the garlanded kings busy themselves: some peer at the bow, some straighten their fierce arrows, some ask when the day to touch the bow is, some loose many shafts, and some fret.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2179","lb":"CC2179","ev":"The suitor kings prepare their bows and arrows and ask when they may try.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2180":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2180,"t":["பிறையெயிற் றெரிகட் பேழ்வாய்ப் பெருமயிர்ப் பைம்பொற் பன்றி","யறையெனத் திரியு மாய்பொற் பூமியி னிறைந்து மன்ன","ருறுகணை யொன்றும் வில்லு முடன்பிடித் துருவ நேமிப்","பொறிதிரி வதனை நோக்கிப் பூமுடி துளக்கி நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"A gold boar with crescent tusks, burning eyes, gaping mouth and thick bristles turns as if it were proclaiming itself on the golden ground, and the kings, each holding one arrow and the bow, look at the machine turning on its wheel and stand shaking their flowered crowns.","n":"Each king is allowed one arrow (உறுகணை ஒன்றும் வில்லும்); that is the rule the following verses test, one failure to a verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2180","lb":"CC2180","ev":"The kings stand with bow and one arrow, staring at the boar machine turning on its wheel.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2181":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2181,"t":["ஏந்தெழி லாகஞ் சாந்தி னிடுகொடி யெழுதிக் காதிற்","காய்ந்தெரி செம்போற் றோடுங் கனமணிக் குழையு மின்ன","வேந்தருள் வினிதை வேந்தன் வெஞ்சிலை தளர வாங்கி","யாய்ந்தபொற் பன்றி நெற்றி யருந்துக ளார்ப்ப வெய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of Vinitai, sandal creepers painted on his chest and gold and jewelled rings flashing at his ears, bends the fierce bow till it slackens and shoots, but only raises the fine dust about the gold boar's forehead.","n":"நெற்றி அருந்துகள் ஆர்ப்ப may mean the arrow grazed the boar's brow or fell short and kicked up dust; either way the boar is not brought down. The list of failed kings is a comic catalogue, each failure with its own image.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","igal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2181","lb":"CC2181","ev":"The king of Vinitai shoots and his arrow only stirs the dust at the boar's forehead.","cl":[],"ag":["வினிதை வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2182":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2182,"t":["குடர்தொடர் குருதிக் கோட்டுக் குஞ்சர நகரத் தார்கோன்","சுடர்நுதற் பட்ட மின்னச் சுரும்பிமிர் கண்ணி சிந்த","வடர்கதிர்ப் பைம்பொற் பூணு மாரமு மகலத் தொல்கப்","படர்சிலை குழைய வாங்கிப் பன்றியைப் பதைப்ப வெய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the elephant city, his frontlet flashing and his bee-swarmed garland scattering, his jewels sliding off his chest, bends the spreading bow till it sags and shoots, and the boar only shudders.","n":"குஞ்சர நகரம், the elephant city, is taken here as Hastinapura, whose king was among the suitors at Gandharvadattai's contest; the verse does not use that name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2182","lb":"CC2182","ev":"The king of the elephant city shoots and makes the boar quiver but does not fell it.","cl":[],"ag":["அத்தினபுரத்து வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2183":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2183,"t":["வார்மதுத் துளிக்கு மாலை மணிமுடித் தொடுத்து நாலக்","கார்மதங் கடந்த வண்கைக் காம்பிலிக் காவன் மன்ன","னோமதக் கேழ லெய்வா னேறலும் பொறியி னேறுண்","டார்மதக் களிற்று வேந்தர்க் கருநகை யாக வீழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The open-handed guardian king of Kampili, honeyed garland on his jewelled crown, climbs to shoot the boar, is struck by the machine and falls, to the great laughter of the elephant-riding kings.","n":"To shoot, a king must mount the wheel (ஏறலும்); the machine itself throws the unskilled. The laughter of the other kings makes the contest a public shaming as well as a test.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2183","lb":"CC2183","ev":"The king of Kampili climbs the machine to shoot, is thrown by it and falls while the other kings laugh.","cl":[],"ag":["காம்பிலிக் காவல் மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2184":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2184,"t":["முலைவட்டப் பூணு முத்து முள்கலிற் கிழிந்து பொல்லா","விலைவட்டத் தாம மார்பிற் கோசலத் திறைவ னெய்த","குலைவட்டக் கருதி யம்பு வானின்மேற் பூச லுய்ப்பான்","சிலைவட்ட நீங்கி விண்மேற் செவ்வனே யெழுந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of Kosala, his breast-ornaments and pearls torn by the press of embraces, shoots, and his arrow, as if bent on making war on the sky, leaves the bow's curve and rises straight up into the heavens.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","igal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2184","lb":"CC2184","ev":"The king of Kosala's arrow flies straight up into the sky instead of at the boar.","cl":[],"ag":["கோசலத்து இறைவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2185":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2185,"t":["ஊடிய மடந்தை போல வுறுசிலை வாங்க வாரா","தாடெழு வனைய திண்டோ ளவந்திய னதனை நோனா","னாடெழுந் தார்ப்ப மற்றந் நன்சிலை முறித்திட் டம்பை","வாடினன் பிடித்து நின்றான் மண்மகன் போல நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of Avanti, shoulders like a bolt, cannot bear that the bow, like a sulking woman, will not bend to him; as the people roar he breaks the good bow and stands holding the arrow, wilted, like a man of clay.","n":"மண்மகன் போல நின்றான் (line 4), 'stood like a man of clay', plays on the section's name, மண்மகள் இலம்பகம். The bow that will not come like a sulking woman (ஊடிய மடந்தை) joins the contest's two prizes, the bow and the bride.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2185","lb":"CC2185","ev":"The king of Avanti cannot bend the bow, breaks it in anger and stands holding his arrow.","cl":[],"ag":["அவந்தியன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2186":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2186,"t":["பில்கித்தே னொழுகுங் கோதைப் பிறர்மனை யாள்கட் சென்ற","வுள்ளத்தை யுணர்வின் மிக்கா னொழித்திடப் பெயர்ந்த தேபோன்","மல்லனீர் மகத ராசன் றுரந்தகோன் மருள வோடிப்","புல்லியப் பொறியை மோந்து புறக்கொடுத் திட்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As a wise man draws back a heart that has gone out to another man's wife, so the arrow shot by the king of Magadha runs, sniffs at the machine and turns its back.","n":"The simile is double-edged: it gives the retreating arrow the dignity of restraint while it mocks the king whose arrow it is. The rule it assumes is that the heart may go toward another's wife and the wise man's work is to call it back (ஒழித்திடப் பெயர்ந்தது).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","arivu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2186","lb":"CC2186","ev":"The king of Magadha's arrow reaches the machine and turns back.","cl":[["a man of understanding withdraws his heart when it goes out toward another man's wife","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["மகத ராசன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2187":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2187,"t":["தென்வரை பொதியி லார மகிலொடு தேய்த்த தேய்வை","மன்வரை யகலத் தப்பி மணிவடந் திருவில் வீச","மின்னென் விட்ட கோலை விழுங்கக்கண் டழுங்கி வேர்த்துக்","கன்மலிந் திலங்கு திண்டோட் கலிங்கர்கோன் மெலிந்து மீண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"The Kalinga lord, his chest daubed with southern Pothiyil sandal and agil, his jewelled chains flashing like a rainbow, sees the arrow he loosed like lightning swallowed up, and sweating and dismayed he turns back weakened.","n":"What swallows the arrow is not named; it may be the sky or the turning machine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2187","lb":"CC2187","ev":"The Kalinga king's arrow vanishes and he withdraws in dismay.","cl":[],"ag":["கலிங்கர் கோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2188":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2188,"t":["கன்மழைப் பொற்குன் றேந்திக் கணநிரை யன்றுகாத்து","மன்னுயி ரின்று காக்கும் வாரண வாசி மன்னன்","மின்னிழை சுடர வாங்கி விட்டகோ லுற்று றாதாய்","மன்னுயிர் நடுங்க நாணி மண்புக்கு மறைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king of Varanavasi, who once lifted the golden hill against the rain of stones and saved the herds and now protects living beings, bends the bow so that his jewels blaze, but his arrow does not reach; ashamed, while all creatures tremble, it enters the earth and hides.","n":"Lines 1 to 2 give this king the deed of lifting the hill to shelter the herds, the deed of the dark god of the cowherds; whether the verse means he is of that line or ironically lends him the god's deed cannot be settled from the verse. It is the arrow, not the king, that is said to hide for shame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2188","lb":"CC2188","ev":"The Varanavasi king's arrow falls short and buries itself in the ground.","cl":[],"ag":["வாரணவாசி மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2189":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2189,"t":["எரிகதிர்ப் பைம்பொற் சுண்ண மிலங்கமெய்ம் முழுது மப்பிப்","புரிகழ லணிந்த நோன்றாட் போதன புரத்து வேந்த","னரிதினிற் றிகிரி யேறித் திரிந்துகண் சுழன்று சோர்ந்து","விரிகதிர்க் கடவுள் போல வெறுநிலத் தொலிப்ப வீழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of Pothanapuram, his body smeared all over with shining gold powder and warrior anklets on his strong feet, with difficulty mounts the wheel, spins round, his eyes reeling, and faints and falls crashing on the bare ground like the rayed sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2189","lb":"CC2189","ev":"The king of Pothanapuram mounts the wheel, grows dizzy and falls to the ground.","cl":[],"ag":["போதன புரத்து வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2190":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2190,"t":["மலையச்செஞ் சாந்து முந்நீர் வலம்புரி யீன்ற முத்து","மிலைவைத்த கோதை நல்லா ரிளமுலைப் பொறியு மார்ந்து","சிலைவைத்த மார்பிற் றென்னன் றிருமணிப் பன்றி நோக்கித்","தலைவைத்த தம்பு தானுந் தரணிமேற் பாதம் வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The southern king, his bow-scarred chest marked with Malaya sandal, sea-conch pearls and the prints of young women's breasts, looks at the jewelled boar, and the arrow he had set to the bow sets its own foot on the earth.","n":"தென்னன் is the usual name of the Pandya; nothing in the verse ties him to any king of the Sangam poems. The chest marked by women's breasts is the courtly lover who fails as an archer, a contrast the catalogue keeps making.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2190","lb":"CC2190","ev":"The southern king's arrow drops to the ground.","cl":[],"ag":["தென்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2191":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2191,"t":["விற்றிறல் விசய னென்பான் வெங்கணை செவிட்டி நோக்கி","யொற்றுபு திருத்திக் கைம்மே லுருட்டுபு நேமி சோந்தாங்","குற்றதன் சிலையின் வாய்ப்பெய் துடுவமை பகழி வாங்க","விற்றுவின் முறிந்து போயிற் றிமைப்பினி னிலங்கித் திட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"One called Vijayan, mighty with the bow, eyes his fierce arrow, straightens it, rolls it on his hand, comes up to the wheel and sets the star-feathered arrow to his bow, and in the blink of an eye the bow snaps.","n":"This Vijayan, named as a renowned bowman (விற்றிறல் விசயன்), is one of the suitor kings; the verses do not make him the Hemamapuram prince who was Civakan's pupil, and the name is also the epithet of Arjuna. Line 4 விற்றுவின் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2191","lb":"CC2191","ev":"A king named Vijayan readies his arrow at the wheel and his bow breaks.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2192":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2192,"t":["குண்டல மிலங்க வாங்கிக் குனிசிலை யுறையி னீக்கிக்","கொண்டவன் கொழும்பொற் றாரு மாரமு மிளிர வேறிக்","கண்டுகோ னிறைய வாங்கிக் காதுற மறித லோடும்","விண்டுநா ணற்ற தாங்கே விசயனும் வீக்க மற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"His earrings flashing, Vijayan draws the curved bow from its case, mounts with his gold garland and chains glinting, draws the arrow full to the ear, and at that moment the string parts and his swagger is gone.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2192","lb":"CC2192","ev":"Vijayan tries again with another bow; the string snaps and he gives up.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2193":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2193,"t":["உளைவனப் பிருந்த மான்றே ரொளிமுடி மன்ன ரெல்லாம்","வலைவனப் பிருந்த தோளாள் வருமுலைப் போகம் வேண்டி","விளைதவப் பெருமை யோரார் விற்றிறன் மயங்கி யாருங்","களைகலார் பொறியை யாங்கோ ராறுநாள் கழிந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"All the bright-crowned kings of the swift chariots, wanting the pleasure of the girl with bangled shoulders, do not understand the greatness of austerity come to fruit; bewildered in their archery, none can bring down the machine, and six days go by.","n":"விளைதவப் பெருமை ஓரார (line 3) is the narrator's explanation of six days of failure: the kings bring desire (போகம் வேண்டி) and the bow, and lack the merit of past austerity that alone wins her. The verse does not name a school; the ripening of past tapas into present fortune is the epic's way of telling Civakan's successes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaamam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2193","lb":"CC2193","ev":"Six days pass and no king has brought down the boar machine.","cl":[["such a prize is won by the greatness of austerity that has ripened from the past, not by desire and skill at arms","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2194":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2194,"t":["பனைக்கை யானை மன்னர் பணியப் பைம்பொன் முடியிற்","கனைக்குஞ் சுரும்பார் மாலை கமழ மதுவுந் தேனு","நனைக்குங் கழலோன் சிறுவ னாம வெள்வேல் வலவ","னினைக்க லாகா வகையா னேரா ருயிர்மே லெழுந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"While kings with palm-trunked elephants bow so that the bees hum on the garlands of their gold crowns and honey wets the hero's anklets, the son of that anklet-wearer, master of the terrible white spear, rises against the lives of his foes in a way no one could have foreseen.","n":"The verse does not name Civakan; the son (சிறுவன்) of the anklet-wearer whom kings bow to is read as Civakan from 2196 to 2202. நேரார் உயிர்மேல் எழுந்தான் makes his rising an attack on his enemies' lives, not a suit for the bride.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2194","lb":"CC2194","ev":"Civakan rises to take up the contest against his enemies.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2195":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2195,"t":["காரின் முழங்குங் களிறுங் கடலின் முழங்குந் தேரும்","போரின் முழங்கும் புரவிக் கடலும் புகைவாட் கடலுஞ்","சீரின் முழங்கு முரசு மலறுஞ் சிறுவெண் சங்கு","நீரின் முழங்க முழங்கு நீல யானை யிவர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He mounts the blue elephant that trumpets amid elephants roaring like cloud, chariots roaring like sea, a sea of war-horses, a sea of smoking swords, drums thundering in rhythm and small white conches crying like water.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2195","lb":"CC2195","ev":"Civakan mounts a blue elephant amid the din of his army.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2196":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2196,"t":["கல்லார் மணிப்பூண் மார்பிற் காம னிவனே யென்ன","வில்லார் கடலந் தானை வேந்தர் குழாத்துட் டோன்றப்","புல்லான் கண்ணி னோக்கிப் புலிகாண் கலையிற் புலம்பி","யொல்லா னொல்லா னாகி யுயிர்போ யிருந்தான் மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"As the hero appears among the kings with their sea of bow-bearing armies, so that men say this is Kama himself with jewelled ornaments on his chest, the base one looks at him with his eyes, cries out like a deer that has seen a tiger, and sits as if his life had left him.","n":"புல்லான், 'the base one', is the narrator's name for Kattiyangaran here; the man who once had Civakan led out to be killed now sees him alive. Who says காமன் இவனே is not stated, probably the onlookers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","pagai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2196","lb":"CC2196","ev":"Kattiyangaran sees Civakan alive in the assembly and is struck with terror.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2197":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2197,"t":["புலியாப் புறுத்திக் கொண்டேன் போக்கி விட்ட பிழைப்பும்","வலியார் திரடோண் மதனன் னவனைப் பிழைத்த பிழைப்பு","கலியு மென்னை நலியு மென்னக் களிற்றி னுச்சி","யிலையார் கடகத் தடக்கை புடைத்து மெய்சோர்ந் திருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Saying 'the blunder of letting go the tiger I had caught and bound, and the blunder of strong-shouldered Madanan who failed to kill him: this trouble will crush me', he beats the top of the elephant's head with his hand bearing the leaf-patterned bracelet and sits slumped.","n":"He names two blunders, his own release of the bound 'tiger' and Madanan's failure, and does not name the deed itself, the order to kill, as his wrong; his regret is that the killing failed. Madanan had reported Civakan killed (1164) and been rewarded for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","acham","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2197","lb":"CC2197","ev":"Kattiyangaran laments that Civakan escaped him and Madanan, and beats his elephant's head in despair.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்",""],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2198":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2198,"t":["மைபூத் தலர்ந்த மழைக்கண் மாழை மானோ நோக்கிற்","கொய்பூங் கோதை மடவார் கொற்றங் கொள்கென் றேத்தப்","பெய்பூங் கழலான் வேழத் திழிந்து பிறைபோற் குலாய","செய்பூண் சிலைநா ணெறிந்தான் சேரார் நாளுக் கனவே."],"p":"","r":"As women with dark rain-cool eyes and deer glances, wearing plucked flower garlands, cry 'take victory', the hero of the flower anklets comes down from the elephant and twangs the string of his crescent-curved, ornamented bow, a nightmare to the days of his enemies.","n":"சேரார் நாளுக்கு அனவே, 'a dream to the days of the foes', makes the twang a foreboding of death for Kattiyangaran's side; the bride is not mentioned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2198","lb":"CC2198","ev":"Civakan dismounts and twangs his bowstring while the women hail his victory.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2199":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2199,"t":["கனிபடு மொழியி னாடன் காரிகை கவற்ற வந்து","குனிசிலை தோற்ற மன்னர் கொங்குகொப் புளிக்கு நீலப்","பனிமலர்க் காடு போன்றார் படர்சிலை தொடாத வேந்த","ரினிதினின் மலர்ந்த வேரார் தாமரைக் காடு போன்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The kings who came because the girl of fruit-sweet speech troubled their hearts and were beaten by the curved bow are like a grove of blue lilies dripping honey, and the kings who never touched the bow are like a lotus grove sweetly in bloom.","n":"The two groves read the faces: the defeated darkened and drooping like blue lilies, those who stayed out bright like lotuses. Not trying is here safer than trying and failing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2199","lb":"CC2199","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2200":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2200,"t":["போர்த்தகல் விசும்பில் வந்து பொறித்திரி பன்றி மூன்று","நீர்த்தகப் புணர்ந்த போதி னெடுந்தகை மூன்று மற்றுச்","சூர்த்துடன் வீழ நோக்கிச் சுடுசரஞ் சிதற வல்லா","னோர்த்தொன்றே புணர்ப்ப நாடி யொருபகல் காறு நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"When the three revolving boars come rightly together in the wide sky, the noble one skilled in scattering burning arrows watches for the three to fall together, and studying how to bring them into one, stands a whole day.","n":"பன்றி மூன்று (line 1) and நெடுந்தகை மூன்று are obscure: the machine may carry three boars, or a boar and its images, which must line up for one shot to bring all down. That the waiting archer is Civakan is a reading from நெடுந்தகை and from 2202, where he mounts the wheel.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaraaydhal","kaalam","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2200","lb":"CC2200","ev":"Civakan studies the turning machine for a whole day, waiting for the moment to shoot.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2201":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2201,"t":["பொறியின்மே லேற றேற்றா னாணினாற் போதல் செய்யா","னெறியின்வில் லூன்றி நிற்ப நிழன்மணிப் பன்றி யற்று","மறியுமோ வென்று முன்னே மணிமுடி சிதறி வீழ்ந்த","செறிகழன் மன்னர் நக்குத் தீயத்தீ விளைத்துக் கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing him not yet daring to mount the machine and yet out of pride not going away, but standing leaning his bow on the path, the kings whose jewelled crowns had fallen earlier laugh, 'will the shadowed jewel boar break and fall of itself?', and so kindle a fire that will burn them.","n":"The verse reads as the narrator's irony: the mockers' laughter (நக்கு) is the fire they light against themselves (தீயத் தீ விளைத்துக் கொண்டார்), as 2204 will show. Whether the one who stands is Civakan or another hesitant suitor is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["igal","pullarivu","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2201","lb":"CC2201","ev":"The kings who failed mock Civakan as he stands waiting by the machine.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:94"},"cc:2202":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2202,"t":["சிரற்றலை மணிகள் வேய்ந்த திருந்துபொற் றிகிரிச் செம்பொ","னுரற்றலை யுருவப் பன்றி யிடம்வலந் திரிய நம்பன்","விரற்றலைப் புட்டில் வீக்கி வெஞ்சிலை கணையோ டேந்திக்","குரற்றலை வண்டு பொங்கக் குப்புற்று நேமி சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The boar figure stands on a mortar-shaped head above a turning wheel of red gold set with gems, swinging left and right, and the lord binds the finger-guard, takes up the bow with its arrow, and, the bees in his hair rising, closes on the wheel.","n":"The target is a machine: a boar image on a post that swings to either side above a revolving wheel, so the feat is aiming at a moving mark. குப்புற்று (line 4) may mean he leapt or that he bent low; the sense of his approach turns on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2202","lb":"CC2202","ev":"Civakan arms himself and goes up to the revolving wheel that carries the boar target.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2203":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2203,"t":["ஒள்ளழல் வைரப் பூணு மொளிர்மணிக் குழையு மின்ன","வொள்ளழற் கொள்ளி வட்டம் போற்குலாய்ச் சுழலப் பொன்ஞா","ணொள்ளழ னேமி நக்க மண்டல மாக நின்றா","னொள்ளழற் பருதி மேலோர் பருதிநின் றதனை யொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"His diamond ornaments and gem earrings flashing, he wheels round like a whirled firebrand, his gold cord making a circle with the blazing wheel, so that he stands like one sun set upon another.","n":"Each line opens with ஒள்ளழல், 'bright fire', and the verse builds a single figure out of the repetition: the hero, his ornaments and the wheel all become circles of fire, ending in the doubled sun.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2203","lb":"CC2203","ev":"Civakan circles with the turning wheel as he takes aim.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2204":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2204,"t":["அருந்தவக் கிழமை போல விறாதவில் லறாத நாண்வாய்த்","திருந்தினார் சிந்தை போலுந் திண்சரஞ் சுருக்கி மாறா","யிருந்தவன் பொறியும் பன்றி யியற்றரும் பொறியு மற்றாங்","கொருங்குட னுதிர வெய்தா னூழித்தீ யுருமோ டொப்பான்."],"p":"","r":"With a bow that never breaks, like the rare virtue of penance, a string that never parts, and arrows straight like the minds of the refined, he shoots so that the mechanism and the hard-wrought boar device fall shattered together, he himself like the fire of the world's end joined with thunder.","n":"The similes borrow from ascetic and moral life: the bow is unbreakable as penance is, the arrows straight as a refined mind. The feat that wins the contest is praised in the language of virtue just before it opens a war. இருந்தவன் பொறியும் (line 3) is obscure; it may name the device on which the boar was mounted.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","thavam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2204","lb":"CC2204","ev":"Civakan's arrow strikes the target and shatters both the boar and the machine that moved it.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2205":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2205,"t":["இலங்கெயிற் றேன மேவுண் டிருநிலத் திடித்து வீழக்","கலங்குதெண் டிரையுங் காருங் கடுவளி முழக்கு மொப்ப","உலம்புபு முரசங் கொட்டி யொய்யெனச் சேனை யார்ப்பக்","குலம்பகர்ந் தறைந்து கோமான் கோவிந்தன் கூறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The bright-tusked boar takes the arrow and crashes to the ground, the drums are beaten with a roar like the troubled sea, the cloud and the gale, the army shouts, and the lord Govindan proclaims the hero's lineage by drum.","n":"This is the turn of the ilampakam: the contest's winner is named as the dispossessed king's son, so the proclamation of birth is also a declaration of war on the usurper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2205","lb":"CC2205","ev":"The boar falls, and King Govindan has Civakan's lineage proclaimed before the assembled host.","cl":[],"ag":["வேந்தன்","சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2206":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2206,"t":["வானிடை யொருவன் றோன்றி மழையென முழங்கிச் சொல்லுந்","தேனுடை யலங்கல் வெள்வேற் சீவக னென்னுஞ் சிங்கங்","கானுடை யலங்கன் மார்பிற் கட்டியங்கார னென்னும்","வேன்மிடை சோலை வேழத் தின்னுயிர் விழுங்கு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"A figure appears in the sky and, thundering like a rain cloud, declares that the lion called Civakan, of the honeyed garland and white spear, will swallow the sweet life of the elephant called Kattiyangaran, of the garlanded chest.","n":"The prophecy is cast as a beast image, lion against elephant, the old figure of the smaller, fiercer animal killing the larger; the next verses give the usurper the larger army.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kolai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2206","lb":"CC2206","ev":"A voice from the sky foretells that Civakan will kill Kattiyangaran.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஒருவன்",""],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2207":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2207,"t":["விஞ்சையர் வெம்படை கொண்டுவந் தாயென","வஞ்சுவ லோவறி யாயென தாற்றலை","வெஞ்சம மாக்கிடின் வீக்கறுத் துன்னொடு","வஞ்சனை வஞ்ச மறுத்திடு கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"A speaker scorns the threat: shall he fear because the other has come bringing the fierce army of the Vidyadharas? The other does not know his strength; if it comes to fierce battle, let him cut down that pride and end the deceit, with him.","n":"No speaker is named. The mention of the Vidyadhara army suits a word aimed at Civakan's side, and the next verse has Madanan blaze up on hearing the 'famed one', so this is read as Kattiyangaran's answer; the closing clause, who is to cut whose pride and whose deceit is meant, can be read either way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vanjam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2207","lb":"CC2207","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2208":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2208,"t":["சூரியற் காண்டலுஞ் சூரிய காந்தமஃ","தாரழ லெங்ஙனங் கான்றிடு மங்ஙனம்","பேரிசை யானிசை கேட்டலும் பெய்ம்முகிற்","காரிடி போன்மத னன்கனன் றிட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"As the sunstone pours out fire on seeing the sun, so Madanan, hearing the word of the far-famed one, blazes up like the thunder of a raining cloud.","n":"The sunstone makes the anger borrowed: Madanan's fire is kindled by another's words, as the stone's fire is the sun's. Who the பேரிசையான் is depends on the reading of the previous verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2208","lb":"CC2208","ev":"Madanan flares into rage on hearing the words.","cl":[],"ag":["மதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2209":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2209,"t":["காற்படை யுங்களி றுங்கலி மாவொடு","நூற்படு தேரு நொடிப்பினிற் பண்ணி","நாற்படை யுந்தொகுத் தான்மக்க ணச்சிலை","வேற்படை வீரரொர் நூற்றுவர் தொக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"In an instant he readies foot soldiers, elephants, horses and chariots and gathers the fourfold army, and a hundred warrior sons with poisoned-leaf spears assemble.","n":"Whose sons the hundred are is not said in the verse; the usurper's side is meant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2209","lb":"CC2209","ev":"Madanan musters the fourfold army, and a hundred spear-bearing sons gather to it.","cl":[],"ag":["மதனன்","மக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2210":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2210,"t":["விற்றிற லான்வெய்ய தானையும் வீங்குபு","செற்றெழுந் தான்படை யுஞ்சின மொய்ம்பொடு","மற்றவர் மண்டிய வாளமர் ஞாட்பினு","ளுற்றவர்க் குற்றதெ லாமுரைக் குற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet announces that he will tell all that befell those who met in the sword battle between the fierce army of the master of the bow and the army of the one who rose up in wrath.","n":"A rare first-person turn by the narrator (உரைக்குற்றேன், line 4), marking the set battle-piece that follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2210","lb":"CC2210","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2211":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2211,"t":["அத்தமா மணிவரை யனைய தோன்றல","மத்தகத் தருவியின் மணந்த வோடைய","முத்துடை மருப்பின முனைக்கண் போழ்வன","பத்தியிற் பண்ணின பரும யானையே."],"p":"","r":"The armoured elephants are drawn up in rows, like great jewelled mountains, with forehead plates, rut streaming like falls down their brows, and pearl-bearing tusks that split the front.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2211","lb":"CC2211","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2212":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2212,"t":["கோல்பொரு கொடுஞ்சிலை குருதி வெம்படை","மேலவ ரடக்குபு வேழ மேறலின்","மாலிரு விசும்பிடை மணந்த வொண்கொடி","கால்பொரு கதலிகைக் கான மொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"As men with curved bows and bloody weapons mount the elephants, the bright flags crowded in the dark sky look like a forest of banners struck by the wind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2212","lb":"CC2212","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2213":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2213,"t":["குடையுடை நிழலின் கோல மார்ந்தன்","கிடுகுடைக் காப்பின கிளர்பொற் பீடிகை","யடிதொடைக் கமைந்தன வரவத் தோத்தொகை","வடிவுடைத் துகின்முடி வலவர் பண்ணினார்."],"p":"","r":"The charioteers, with fine cloth on their heads, ready the chariots, beautiful under their parasols, guarded with shields, with raised gold seats and quivers fitted to the footboard.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2213","lb":"CC2213","ev":"The charioteers make the chariots ready.","cl":[],"ag":["வலவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2214":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2214,"t":["கொய்யுளைப் புரவிகள் கொளீஇய திண்ணுகம்","பெய்கயி றமைவரப் பிணித்து முள்ளுறீஇச்","செய்கயி றாய்ந்தன சிலையு மல்லவுங்","கையமைத் திளைஞருங் கருவி வீசினார்."],"p":"","r":"They bind the strong yokes with fitted ropes on the trimmed-maned horses and set the goads, and the young men check the bows and other weapons and take them in hand.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2214","lb":"CC2214","ev":"The young warriors harness the horses and take up their weapons.","cl":[],"ag":["இளைஞர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2215":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2215,"t":["பறந்திய றருக்கின பரவை ஞாட்பினுட்","கறங்கெனத் திரிவன கவரி நெற்றிய","பிறந்துழி யறிகெனப் பெரிய நூலவர்","குறங்கெழுத் துடையன குதிரை யென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The horses, proud and quick as flight, wheel like spinning tops in the wide battle, plumed at the brow, and bear marks on their thighs by which those learned in the great treatises may know where they were bred.","n":"Line 3 to 4 refer to a learned lore of horses (பெரிய நூலவர்): the breed and country of a horse read from marks on its body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2215","lb":"CC2215","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2216":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2216,"t":["கொன்னுனைக் குந்தமுஞ் சிலையுங் கூர்நுதி","மின்னிலை வாளொடு மிலேச்ச ரேறலிற்","பொன்னரிப் புட்டிலுந் தாரும் பொங்குபு","முன்னுருத் தார்த்தெழப் புரவி மொய்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"As Mlecchas mount with deadly-tipped lances, bows and lightning-leaf swords, the horses throng, with gold-meshed caparisons and garlands bounding, raging forward with a roar.","n":"Foreign (மிலேச்சர்) horsemen in the army, as in the Sangam memory of Yavana guards; the verse does not say on whose side.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2216","lb":"CC2216","ev":"Mleccha horsemen mount and the cavalry surges forward.","cl":[],"ag":["மிலேச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2217":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2217,"t":["மாலையுங் கண்ணியு மணந்த சென்னிய","ராலுபு செறிகழ லார்க்குங் காலினர்","பாலிகை யிடையறப் பிடித்த பாணியர்","சாலிகை யுடம்பினர் தறுக ணாளரே."],"p":"","r":"The fierce-eyed foot soldiers have garlands and chaplets on their heads, ringing anklets on their feet, short swords gripped hard in their hands, and mail on their bodies.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2217","lb":"CC2217","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["காலாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2218":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2218,"t":["போர்மயிர்க் கேடகம் புளகத் தோற்பரம்","வார்மயிர்க் கிடுகொடு வள்ளித் தண்டையு","நோமரப் பலகையு நிரைத்த தானையோர்","போர்முகப் புலிக்கடல் புகுந்த தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The host with its hair-tufted shields, hide shields, long screens, staves and wooden boards in ranks looks like a sea of tigers that has entered the face of battle.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2218","lb":"CC2218","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2219":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2219,"t":["பார்நனை மதத்த பல்பேய் பருந்தொடு பரவச் செல்லும்","போர்மதக் களிறு பொற்றேர் நான்கரைக் கச்ச மாகு","மோமணிப் புரவி யேழா மிலக்கமேழ் தேவ கோடி","கார்மலி கடலங் காலாள் கற்பகத் தாரி னாற்கே."],"p":"","r":"For the one of the kalpaka garland there are war elephants in rut, attended by crowds of spirits and kites, and golden chariots, four and a half kaccham, horses seven lakh, and foot soldiers seven deva-koti, a sea swelling like a rain cloud.","n":"The verse counts Civakan's army; read with the next it sets the two hosts side by side. The units (கச்சம், இலக்கம், தேவ கோடி) are traditional large numbers and their values are not fixed here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2219","lb":"CC2219","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2220":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2220,"t":["நிழன்மணிப் புரவித் திண்டேர் நிழறுழாய்க் குனிந்து குத்து","மழறிகழ் கதத்த யானை யைந்தரைக் கச்ச மாகு","மெழின்மணிப் புரவி யேழா மிலக்கமேழ் தேவ கோடி","கழன்மலிந் திலங்குங் காலாள் கட்டியங் காரற் கன்றே."],"p":"","r":"For Kattiyangaran, then, there were strong chariots with shining-maned horses and fire-wrathful elephants that stoop and gore, five and a half kaccham, horses seven lakh, and foot soldiers seven deva-koti, bright with anklets.","n":"Against 2219 the usurper's elephants and chariots are one kaccham more, the rest equal: the poem gives the wrongful side the larger force.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2220","lb":"CC2220","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2221":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2221,"t":["குலங்கெழு மகளிர்தங் கோல நீப்பவு","மலங்குளைப் புரவியுங் களிறு மாளவு","நிலமக ணெஞ்சுகை யெறிந்து நையவும்","புலமகன் சீறினன் புகைந்த தெஃகமே."],"p":"","r":"So that the women of noble houses put off their adornment, the tossing-maned horses and the elephants die, and the Earth-woman beats her breast with her hand and pines, the learned one raged, and his spear smoked.","n":"The verse states the war's cost before its glory: widowhood (the noble women's adornment put off), slaughter, and the Earth herself mourning, in an ilampakam named for the Earth-woman (மண்மகள்). Who the புலமகன் is, Civakan or another, the verse does not fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","saakkaadu","padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2221","lb":"CC2221","ev":"The fighting begins, its cost foretold in widowed women, dead beasts and a grieving Earth.","cl":[],"ag":["புலமகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2222":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2222,"t":["குணில்பொரக் குளிறின முரசம் வெள்வளை","பணைபரந் தார்த்தன பம்பை வெம்பின","விணையில வெழுந்ததாழ் பீலி யெங்கணு","முணையினாற் கடலக முழக்க மொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Struck with drumsticks, the war drums roar, the white conches and the kettledrums spread their sound, the pampai drums throb, the tall peacock-feather standards rise everywhere, and the din of the front is like the roar of the sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2222","lb":"CC2222","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2223":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2223,"t":["முடிமன ரெழுதரு பருதி மொய்களி","றுடைதிரை மாக்கல மொளிறு வாட்படை","யடுதிற லெறிசுறா வாகக் காய்ந்தன","கடலிரண் டெதிர்ந்ததோர் கால மொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The crowned kings are like the rising sun, the massed elephants like great ships breaking the waves, and the flashing swords like killing sharks, so that the clash looks like a time when two seas met in wrath.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2223","lb":"CC2223","ev":"The two armies meet.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2224":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2224,"t":["அருங்கணை யடக்கிய வாவ நாழிகை","பெரும்புறத் தலமரப் பிணிந்த கச்சினர்","கருங்கழ லாடவர் கருவில் வாய்க்கொளீஇச்","சொரிந்தனர் கணைமழை விசும்பு தூர்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Warriors in dark anklets, with quivers packed with arrows tossing on their broad backs and tight girdles, set arrows to their black bows and pour a rain of shafts until the sky is filled.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2224","lb":"CC2224","ev":"The archers loose a rain of arrows.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆடவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2225":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2225,"t":["நிணம் பிறங் ககலமுந் தோளு நெற்றியு","மணங்கருஞ் சரங்களி னழுத்தி யையென","மணங்கமழ் வருபுனன் மறலு மாந்தரிற்","பிணங்கமர் மலைந்தனர் பெற்றி யின்னதே."],"p":"","r":"Driving their dark arrows into fat-swollen chests, shoulders and foreheads, they fought a tangled battle like men struggling in a fragrant oncoming flood; such was its nature.","n":"The simile of line 3 sets the slaughter beside the festival play of men and women in scented flood water, a scene the epic elsewhere describes for pleasure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2225","lb":"CC2225","ev":"The armies fight at close quarters with arrows.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2226":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2226,"t":["கழித்தனர் கனலவாள் புகைந்து கண்கடீ","விழித்தன தீந்தன விமைகள் கூற்றெனத்","தெழித்தனர் திறந்தன ரகல மின்னுயி","ரழித்தன ரயிலவ ரரவ மிக்கதே."],"p":"","r":"They drew their burning swords; eyes smoked and burned and lids were scorched; they roared like Death, opened chests and destroyed sweet lives, and the lancers' din grew.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kootru","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2226","lb":"CC2226","ev":"The sword fight kills many.","cl":[],"ag":["அயிலவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:95"},"cc:2227":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2227,"t":["பொருங்களத் தாடவர் பொருவில் பைந்தலை","யரும்பெறற் கண்ணியோ டற்று வீழ்வன","கருங்கனிப் பெண்ணையங் கானங் கால்பொர","விருங்கனி சொரிவன போன்ற வென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says the severed heads of warriors falling with their precious garlands on the field looked like dark ripe palmyra fruit shaken down when the wind strikes a palm grove.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2227","lb":"CC2227","ev":"Warriors' heads are struck off and fall on the field.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2228":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2228,"t":["பணைமுனிந் தாலுவ பைம்பொற் றாரின","கணைவிசை தவிர்ப்பன கவரி நெற்றிய","துணையமை யிளமைய தோற்ற மிக்கன","விணைமயிர்ப் புரவியோ டிவுளி யேற்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator describes the war horses, impatient of the stable, golden-garlanded, plumed on the forehead with yak-tail, swift enough to outrun arrows, young and evenly paired, as they go into the fight with their riders.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2228","lb":"CC2228","ev":"The cavalry rides into the battle.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2229":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2229,"t":["கூருளி முகம்பொரக் குழிசி மாண்டன","வாரொளி யமைந்தன வாய்பொற் சூட்டின","காரொளி மின்னுமிழ் தகைய காலியற்","றேரொடு தேர்தமுட் சிறந்து சேர்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator describes the chariots, their hubs shaped by the sharp chisel, gold-rimmed and flashing like lightning from a cloud, as they close wheel to wheel with the best of the enemy's chariots.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2229","lb":"CC2229","ev":"The chariots close with one another.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2230":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2230,"t":["அஞ்சன மெழுதின கவள மார்ந்தன","குஞ்சரங் கூற்றொரு கொம்மை கொட்வே","வஞ்சன வரைசிற குடைய போல்வன","மஞ்சிவர் குன்றென மலைந்த வேழமே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator describes the war elephants, painted with collyrium and fed on rice balls, their drums beating like Death's, looking like mountains with wings, clashing like cloud-climbed hills.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2230","lb":"CC2230","ev":"The war elephants clash.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2231":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2231,"t":["மாக்கடற் பெருங்கலங் காலின் மாறுபட்","டாக்கிய கயிறரிந் தோடி யெங்கணும்","போக்கறப் பொருவன போன்று தீப்படத்","தாக்கின வரசுவாத் தம்மு ளென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The royal elephants crash into one another so that fire flies, like great sea ships that have snapped their ropes in a contrary wind and run together with no way out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2231","lb":"CC2231","ev":"The royal elephants collide.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2232":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2232,"t":["விடுசரம் விசும்பிடை மிடைந்து வெய்யவன்","படுகதிர் மறைந்திருள் பரந்த தாயிடை","யடுகதி ரயிலொளி யரசர் மாமுடி","விடுகதிர் மணியொளி வெயிலிற் காய்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The loosed arrows crowd the sky until the sun's rays are hidden and darkness spreads, and in that darkness the gleam of killing spears and the gems on kings' crowns burn like sunlight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2232","lb":"CC2232","ev":"Arrows darken the sky over the field.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2233":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2233,"t":["பூண்குலாம் வனமுலைப் பூமி தேவிதான்","காண்கலேன் கடியன கண்ணி னாலெனாச்","சேண்குலாங் கம்பலஞ் செய்ய தொன்றினான்","மாண்குலாங் குணத்தினான் மறைத்திட் டாளரோ."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says that the Earth goddess, with jewelled breasts, saying she cannot look with her eyes at such cruel things, covered herself with a single red blanket, that is, the blood that now covers the field.","n":"The conceit turns the reddened ground into a modesty: Earth, the bride the hero is to win in this ilampakam of the Earth-lady (மண்மகள்), veils her eyes from the war fought for her. Her words are reported speech inside the narration (எனா).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","saakkaadu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2233","lb":"CC2233","ev":"The field is covered in blood, which the poet makes the Earth's red veil.","cl":[],"ag":["பூமி தேவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2234":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2234,"t":["கலைக்கோட்ட வகலல்குற் கணங்குழையார் கதிர்மணிப்பூண்","முலைக்கோட்டா லுழப்பட்ட மொய்ம்மலர்த்தா ரகன்மார்பர்","மலைக்கோட்ட வெழில்வேழந் தவநூறி மதயானைக்","கொலைக்கோட்டா லுழப்பட்டுக் குருதியுட் குளித்தனரே."],"p":"","r":"Men whose broad garlanded chests had been ploughed by the jewelled breasts of women with earrings are now ploughed by the killing tusks of rutting elephants, and bathe in blood.","n":"The verb உழப்பட்ட is used twice, once for love and once for the tusk, so the same chest is worked over first by pleasure and then by death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nilaiyaamai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2234","lb":"CC2234","ev":"Warriors are gored by elephants and lie in blood.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2235":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2235,"t":["மணமாலை மடந்தையர்த மெல்விரலாற் றொடுத்தணிந்த","விணர்மாலை யிருங்குஞ்சி யீர்ங்குருதிப் புனலலைப்ப","நிணமாலைக் குடர்சூடி நெருப்பிமையா நெய்த்தோரிற்","பிணமாலைப் பேய்மகட்குப் பெருவிருந் தயர்ந்தனரே."],"p":"","r":"Warriors whose dark hair had worn the wedding garlands their brides strung with soft fingers, now washed by blood, wear garlands of entrails and make a great feast for the corpse-garlanded ghoul-woman.","n":"The verse runs four garlands in a row (மணமாலை, இணர்மாலை, நிணமாலை, பிணமாலை): the bride's garland gives way to the entrail-garland, so the wedding becomes the ghoul's banquet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2235","lb":"CC2235","ev":"Slain warriors lie in blood, a feast for the ghouls.","cl":[],"ag":["பேய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2236":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2236,"t":["தோலாப்போர் மறமன்னர் தொடித்தோள்க ளெடுத்தோச்சி","மேலாண்மே னெருப்புமிழ்ந்து மின்னிலங்கு மயில்வாளாற்","காலாசோ டறவெறிந்த கனைகழற்கா லலைகடலு","ணீலநீர்ச் சுறாவினம்போ னெய்த்தோருட் பிறழ்ந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The ankleted feet of kings who never lost a battle, cut off with their anklets by flashing swords as they raised their bangled arms and spat fire at one another, writhe in the blood like sharks in the dark sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2236","lb":"CC2236","ev":"Kings' feet are cut off in the sword fight.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2237":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2237,"t":["கருவியூ டுளங்கழிந்து கணைமொய்ப்பக் கதஞ்சிறந்து","குருவிசோ வரைபோன்ற குஞ்சரங் கொடியணிந்த","வுருவத்தே ரிறமுருக்கி யுருணேமி சுமந்தெழுந்து","பருதிசேர் வரைபோலப் பகட்டினம் பரந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Elephants whose armour has been pierced and who are swarmed with arrows, maddened, crush the bannered chariots and rise carrying the rolling wheels on them, spreading like hills the sun has reached.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2237","lb":"CC2237","ev":"Wounded elephants crush the chariots.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2238":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2238,"t":["மாலைவாய் நெடுங்குடைமேன் மதயானைக் கைதுணிந்து","கோலநீள் கொழுங்குருதி கொளவீழ்ந்து கிடந்தன","மேலைநீள் விசும்புறையும் வெண்மதியம் விசும்பிழுக்கி","நீலமா சுணத்தோடு நிலத்திழிந்த தொத்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The cut trunks of rutting elephants lying under tall garlanded parasols in thick blood look like the white moon of the high sky pulled down to earth together with a dark serpent.","n":"The white parasol is the moon and the dark trunk the eclipse serpent; the image of a fallen light swallowed by a serpent returns in 2245 and 2251 for the fallen captains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2238","lb":"CC2238","ev":"Elephants' trunks are cut off and lie under the fallen parasols.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2239":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2239,"t":["அஞ்சன நிறநீக்கி யரத்தம்போர்த் தமருழக்கி","யிங்குலிக விறுவரைபோன் றினக்களி றிடைமிடைந்த","குஞ்சரங்கள் பாய்ந்திடலிற் குமிழிவிட் டுமிழ்குருதி","யிங்குலிக வருவிபோன் றெவ்வாயுந் தோன்றினவே."],"p":"","r":"The elephants, their black colour lost under a cloak of blood, look like vermilion mountains, and the blood that bubbles out where they charge looks like vermilion falls on every side.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2239","lb":"CC2239","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2240":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2240,"t":["குஞ்சரந் தலையடுத்துக் கூந்தன்மாக் காலணையாச்","செஞ்சோற்றுக் கடனீங்கிச் சினவுவாள் பிடித்துடுத்த","பஞ்சிமேற் கிடந்துடைஞாண் பதைத்திலங்கக் கிடந்தாரை","யஞ்சிப்போந் தினநரியோ டோரிநின் றலறுமே."],"p":"","r":"Men lie with an elephant for a pillow at the head and a maned horse at the feet, having paid off the debt of the red rice, still gripping their angry swords on the cloth they wore, their waist cords glinting, while jackals come up in fear and howl over them.","n":"செஞ்சோற்றுக் கடன் names the fighter's death as repayment of a debt, not as glory; the only mourners in the verse are jackals.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","saakkaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2240","lb":"CC2240","ev":"The dead lie on the field and jackals gather and howl.","cl":[["a warrior who dies in battle has paid the debt he owes for the rice his lord fed him","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2241":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2241,"t":["காதலார்க் கமிர்தீந்த கடற் பவழக் கடிகைவா","யேதிலாப் புள்ளுண்ணக் கொடேமென்று வாய்மடித்துக்","காதணிந்த பொற்றோடுங் குண்டலமு நகநகா","வீததைந்த வரைமார்பர் விஞ்சையர்போற் கிடந்தனரே."],"p":"","r":"Warriors bite down their lips, the coral mouths that gave nectar to their lovers, as if saying they will not give them to alien birds to eat, and lie with gold ear-studs and earrings gleaming, broad-chested, like sleeping Vidyadharas.","n":"The bitten lip is the warrior's fury at death; the poet reads it as a lover's refusal to let the mouth his beloved kissed be eaten by birds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","saakkaadu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2241","lb":"CC2241","ev":"The fallen warriors lie with lips bitten.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2242":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2242,"t":["குடர்வாங்கு குறுநரிகள் கொழுநிணப் புலாற்சேற்றுட்","டொடர்வாங்கு கதநாய்போற் றோன்றின தொடித்திண்டோள்","படர்தீரக் கொண்டெழுந்த பறவைகள் படநாக","முடனேகொண் டெழுகின்ற வுவணப்பு ளொத்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Small jackals dragging entrails through the mire of fat and flesh look like fierce dogs pulling at chains, and birds rising with bangled arms look like Garuda rising with hooded snakes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2242","lb":"CC2242","ev":"Jackals and birds feed on the slain.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2243":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2243,"t":["வரையோடு முருமிடிப்ப வளையெயிற்றுக் கொழுங்குருதி","நிரையுளை யரிநன்மா நிலமிசைப் புரள்வனபோற்","புரையறுபொன் மணியோடைப் பொடிப்பொங்கப் பொருதழிந்","தரைசோடு மரசுவா வடுகளத்து ளாழ்ந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Like lions rolling on the ground when thunder strikes the hill, the royal elephants, with flawless gold face-plates, fight in a rising cloud of dust, are destroyed and sink with their kings into the killing field.","n":"Line 2's நிரையுளை அரிநன்மா may be maned lions (the simile) or fine maned horses; read as the simile, it keeps the verse's subject the royal elephants of line 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2243","lb":"CC2243","ev":"Royal elephants fall with their kings on the field.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2244":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2244,"t":["தடம்பெருங் குவளைக்கட் டாழ்குழலார் சாந்தணிந்து","வடந்திளைப்பப் புல்லிய வரைமார்பம் வாள்புல்ல","நடந்தொழுகு குருதியு ணகாக்கிடந்த வெரிமணிப்பூ","ணிடம்படு செவ்வானத் திளம்பிறைபோற் றோன்றினவே."],"p":"","r":"The chest that women with kuvalai eyes and long hair had embraced, sandal-smeared, chains tangling, is now embraced by the sword, and the jewel lying in the flowing blood looks like a young crescent in a wide red sky.","n":"புல்லிய and புல்ல repeat the verb of embrace: the sword takes the women's place on the same chest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nilaiyaamai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2244","lb":"CC2244","ev":"A warrior lies slain in his own blood.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2245":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2245,"t":["காளமா கிருளைப் போழ்ந்து கதிர்சொரி கடவுட் டிங்கள்","கோளரா விழுங்க முந்நீர்க் கொழுந்திரைக் குளித்த தேபோ","னீளம ருழக்கி யானை நெற்றிமேற் றத்தி வெய்ய","வாளின்வாய் மதனன் பட்டான் விசயன்போர் விசயம் பெற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Like the moon that splits black darkness, swallowed by Rahu and plunging into the sea's waves, Madanan, after churning the long battle and leaping onto an elephant's forehead, falls at the mouth of a fierce sword, and Vijayan wins the victory of the fight.","n":"The syntax leaves open whether Madanan or Vijayan leaps onto the elephant's forehead; the next three verses tell the fight at length and end it on the elephant's tusk, not a sword, so 2245 reads as the summary and 2246 to 2248 as its telling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2245","lb":"CC2245","ev":"Madanan falls by the sword and Vijayan wins the fight.","cl":[],"ag":["மதனன்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2246":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2246,"t":["மன்மத னென்னுங் காளை மணியொலிப் புரவித் தேர்மேல்","வின்மழை சொரிந்து கூற்றிற் றெழித்தனன் றலைப்பெய் தார்ப்பக்","கொன்மலி மார்பன் பொற்றேர் கொடுஞ்சிலை யறுப்பச் சீறிய","பொன்வரைப் புலியிற் பாய்ந்து பூமிமேற் றோன்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The youth called Manmathan, on his chariot drawn by bell-ringing horses, rains arrows and roars like Death; when the death-dealing hero cuts the bow on his golden chariot, he leaps down like an angry lion from a golden hill and stands on the ground.","n":"மன்மதன் is taken as the same man as 2245's மதனன் (both are names of Kama), and the bow-cutter as Vijayan; the verse names neither identification.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2246","lb":"CC2246","ev":"Manmathan fights from his chariot; when his bow is cut he leaps down to fight on foot.","cl":[],"ag":["மதனன்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2247":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2247,"t":["நெற்றிமேற் கோல்கண் மூன்று நெருப்புமிழ்ந் தழுந்த வெய்யச்","சுற்றுபு மாலை போலத் தோன்றறன் னுதலிற் சூடிப்","பொற்றதோர் பவழந் தன்மேற் புனைமணி யழுத்தி யாங்குச்","செற்றெயி றழுந்தச் செவ்வாய் கௌவிவா ளுரீஇ னானே."],"p":"","r":"Three arrows sink spitting fire into his forehead and he wears them on his brow like a garland, like gems set on coral; pressing his teeth into his red lip, he draws his sword.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2247","lb":"CC2247","ev":"Struck in the forehead by three arrows, Manmathan bites his lip and draws his sword.","cl":[],"ag":["மதனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2248":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2248,"t":["தோளினா லெஃக மேந்தித் தும்பிமே லிவரக் கையா","னீளமாப் புடைப்பப் பொங்கி நிலத்தவன் கவிழ்ந்து வீழக்","கீளிரண் டாகக் குத்தி யெடுத்திடக் கிளர்பொன் மார்பன்","வாளினாற் றிருகி வீசி மருப்பின்மேற் றுஞ்சி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Lifting his blade on his shoulder he climbs onto the elephant; it strikes him with its long trunk so that he falls face down on the ground, gores him and lifts him torn in two, and the gold-chested warrior, twisting, swings his sword and dies upon the tusk.","n":"Whose elephant it is, and whether the one who dies on the tusk is Manmathan or the elephant's rider, the verse does not say; this reading follows the run of 2246 to 2247.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2248","lb":"CC2248","ev":"Manmathan climbs the elephant, is thrown and gored, strikes with his sword and dies on its tusk.","cl":[],"ag":["மதனன்","தும்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2249":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2249,"t":["நனைகலந் திழியும் பைந்தார் நான்மறை யாளன் பைம்பொற்","புனைகலக் குப்பை யொப்பான் புத்திமா சேனன் பொங்கி","வனைகலத் திகிரித் தோமேன் மன்னரைக் குடுமி கொண்டான்","கனையெரி யழலம் பெய்த கண்ணுதன் மூர்த்தி யொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Buddhisenan, the man of the four Vedas, with honey-dripping garland, like a heap of wrought gold ornaments, surges up and from his chariot with wheels like a potter's seizes kings by their tufts, like the forehead-eyed god who loosed the blazing fire-arrow.","n":"The companion is called நான்மறையாளன், a Brahmin, and the Jain epic likens his fury to Siva burning the three cities with a fiery arrow, using the Saiva myth purely as a simile of rage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2249","lb":"CC2249","ev":"Buddhisenan drives his chariot through the kings and seizes them by the hair.","cl":[],"ag":["புத்திசேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2250":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2250,"t":["செண்பகப் பூங்குன் றொப்பான் றேவமா தத்தன் வெய்தா","விண்புக வுயிரைப் பெய்வான் வீழ்தரு கடாத்த வேழ","மண்பக விடிக்குஞ் சிங்க மெனக்கடாய் மகதர் கோமான்","றெண்கடற் றானை யோட நாணிவேல் செறித்திட் டானே."],"p":"","r":"Devamathattan, like a hill of campaka flowers, fiercely sending lives into the sky, charges like a lion that strikes a rutting elephant so that the earth splits, and the Magadha king, seeing his sea-like army flee, in shame sheathes his spear.","n":"The Magadha king's shame ends in sheathing the spear, not in death: the verse lets the defeated king withdraw.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","naanam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2250","lb":"CC2250","ev":"Devadattan routs the Magadha army, and its king, ashamed, sheathes his spear.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவதத்தன்","மகதர் கோமான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2251":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2251,"t":["சின்னப்பூ வணிந்த குஞ்சிச் சீதத்தன் சினவு பொன்வாண்","மன்னருட் கலிங்கர் கோமான் மத்தகத் திறுப்ப மன்னன்","பொன்னவிர் குழையும் பூணு மாரமுஞ் சுடர வீழ்வான்","மின்னவிர் பருதி முந்நீர்க் கோளொடும் வீழ்வ தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Sridattan, with small flowers in his hair, strikes his angry golden sword into the forehead of the Kalinga king among the kings, and the king falls, his earrings, ornaments and necklace blazing, like the bright sun falling into the sea together with a planet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2251","lb":"CC2251","ev":"Sridattan kills the Kalinga king with his sword.","cl":[],"ag":["சீதத்தன்","கலிங்கர் கோமான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:96"},"cc:2252":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2252,"t":["கொடுஞ்சிலை யுழவன மான்றேர்க் கோவிந்த னென்னுஞ் சிங்க","மடங்கருஞ் சீற்றத் துப்பின் மாரட்ட னென்னும் பொற்குன்","றிடத்துபொற் றூளி பொங்கக் களிற்றொடு மிறங்கி வீழ","வடர்ந்தெரி பொன்செ யம்பி னழன்றிடித் திட்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Govindan, a lion among chariot-bowmen, strikes with blazing gold-worked arrows until the king called Marattan, a mountain of gold, comes down and falls with his elephant in a cloud of gold dust.","n":"The fallen king is likened to a gold hill, so the dust of his fall is gold dust: the praise of the enemy's splendour measures the victor's arrows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2252","lb":"CC2252","ev":"Govindan's arrows bring down the Maratta king together with his elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்","மாரட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2253":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2253,"t":["கோங்குபூத் துதிர்ந்த குன்றிற் பொன்னணி புளகம் வேய்ந்த","பாங்கமை பரும யானைப் பல்லவ தேச மன்னன்","றேங்கமழ் தெரியற் றீம்பூந் தாரவ னூர்ந்த வேழங்","காம்பிலிக் கிறைவ னூர்ந்த களிற்றொடு மலைந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The elephant ridden by the king of the Pallava country, on whose trappings gold lies like kongu flowers shed on a hill, closes with the tusker ridden by the lord of Kampili.","n":"The Pallava king is named Ulokapalan only in 2255 (மற்றவ னுலோக பாலன்); the identification of the two is a reading of that line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2253","lb":"CC2253","ev":"The Pallava king's elephant and the Kampili king's elephant clash.","cl":[],"ag":["உலோகபாலன்","காம்பிலிக் காவல் மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2254":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2254,"t":["கொந்தழல் பிறப்பத் தாக்கிக் கோடுகண் மிடைந்த தீயால்","வெந்தன விலையி லாத சாமரை வீர மன்ன","னந்தரம் புதைய வில்வா யருஞ்சரம் பெய்த மாரி","குந்தத்தால் விலக்கி வெய்ய கூற்றென முழங்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The tusks strike so hard that fire leaps from them and burns the priceless yak-tail whisks, and the heroic king beats back with his lance the rain of arrows that hides the sky and roars like Death.","n":"Which king wards off the arrows is not said; the next verse's மற்றவன் ('the other one') for Ulokapalan suggests this one is the Kampili king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2254","lb":"CC2254","ev":"The Kampili king wards off a rain of arrows with his lance.","cl":[],"ag":["காம்பிலிக் காவல் மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2255":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2255,"t":["மற்றவ னுலோக பாலன் வயங்குபொற் பட்ட மார்ந்த","நெற்றிமே லெய்த கோலைப் பறித்திட வுமிழ்ந்த நெய்த்தோ","ருற்றவன் களிற்றிற் பாயத் தோன்றுவா னுதயத் துச்சி","யொற்றைமாக் கதிரை நீட்டி யொண்சுட ரிகுந்த தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The other, Ulokapalan, pulls out the arrow shot into his gold-banded forehead, and the blood that gushes onto the foe's elephant makes him look like the rising sun stretching out a single great ray.","n":"The wound is turned into ornament: the jet of blood becomes the sun's first ray, so the verse makes the hurt a sign of glory rather than of danger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2255","lb":"CC2255","ev":"Ulokapalan plucks an arrow from his own forehead and fights on.","cl":[],"ag":["உலோகபாலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2256":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2256,"t":["கொடுமரங் குழைய வாங்கிக் கொற்றவ னெய்த கோல்க","ணெடுமொழி மகளிர் கோல நிழன்மணி முலைக ணோபட்","டுடனுழ வுவந்த மார்ப மூழ்கலிற் சிங்கம் போலக்","கடன்மருள் சேனை சிந்தக் காம்பிலி மன்னன் வீழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The conqueror bends his bow until it bows, and his arrows sink into the chest that the breasts of soft-spoken women once ploughed, so that the Kampili king falls like a lion while his sea-like army scatters.","n":"The arrows enter exactly where the women's breasts lay: love's marks on the chest become the place of death, an image the chapter repeats (2273).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2256","lb":"CC2256","ev":"Ulokapalan's arrows kill the king of Kampili, and his army breaks.","cl":[],"ag":["உலோகபாலன்","காம்பிலிக் காவல் மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2257":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2257,"t":["பொன்னிறக் கோங்கம் பொற்பூங் குன்றெனப் பொலிந்த மேனி","நன்னிற மாலின் மேலா நலங்கொடார் நபுல னென்பான்","மின்னிற வெஃக மேந்தி வீங்குநீர் மகதை யார்கோன்","கொன்னிறக் களிற்றி னெற்றிக் கூந்தன்மாப் பாய்வித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Nabulan, whose body glows like a hill of golden kongu blossom and whose beauty passes even Mal's, raises a lightning-bright spear and makes his maned horse leap onto the brow of the Magadha king's elephant.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2257","lb":"CC2257","ev":"Nabulan rides his horse up onto the Magadha king's war elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["நபுலன்","மகதையார் கோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2258":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2258,"t":["ஏந்தறன் கண்கள் வெய்ய விமைத்திட வெறித லோம்பி","நாந்தக வுழவ னாணி நக்குநீ யஞ்சல் கண்டாய்","காய்ந்திலே னென்று வல்லே கலினமாக் குன்றிற் பொங்கிப்","பாய்ந்ததோர் புலியின் மற்றோர் பகட்டின்மேற் பாய்வித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"When the lord's eyes flicker in fear, the swordsman, ashamed, holds back his blow, laughs, tells him not to be afraid since he is not angry, and swiftly makes his horse spring like a tiger onto another elephant.","n":"The shame (நாணி) is the victor's, not the loser's: to strike a man who flinches would dishonour the striker. ஏந்தறன் (line 1) is read as ஏந்தல் தன், the lord's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","naanam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2258","lb":"CC2258","ev":"Nabulan spares the frightened Magadha king, reassures him, and turns to another elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["நபுலன்","மகதையார் கோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2259":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2259,"t":["கைப்படை யொன்று மின்றிக் கைகொட்டிக் குமர னார்ப்ப","மெய்ப்படை வீழ்ந்த நாணி வேழமு மெறிதல் செல்லான்","மைப்பட நெடுங்கண் மாலை மகளிர்தம் வனப்பிற சூழ்ந்து","கைப்படு பொருளி லாதான் காமம்போற் காளை மீண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"When a youth with no weapon in his hand claps and shouts, the young man, ashamed because his foe's arms have fallen, will not strike even his elephant, and turns back like the desire of a man with no money who hovers round the beauty of dark-eyed women.","n":"The simile is unexpected and exact: the warrior who meets no fit foe is like a penniless lover who can only look at courtesans, a desire with nothing to spend itself on. The young man who claps is read as an enemy youth; that he is the same as Nabulan is unlikely since line 4 names the one who turns back காளை.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maram","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2259","lb":"CC2259","ev":"Nabulan refuses to fight a weaponless youth who challenges him, and withdraws.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரன்","நபுலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2260":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2260,"t":["மண்காவலை மகிழாதிவ ணுடனேபுக ழொழிய","விண்காவலை மகிழ்வீர்நனி யுளிரோவென விபுலன்","வண்காரிருண் மின்னேயுமிழ் நெய்வாயதொ ரயில்வாள்","கண்காவல கழுகோம்புவ துயராநனி வினவும்."],"p":"","r":"Vipulan asks the enemy: is there among you anyone who, finding no joy in guarding the earth, would rather guard heaven and leave his fame behind here; and his spear, flashing like lightning in dark night, raised high where the vultures wait, puts the question too.","n":"The taunt is courteous in form, an offer of heaven; line 4 (கண்காவல கழுகோம்புவது) is hard to construe, and the reading that the vultures guard the spear's point is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","pugazh","marumai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2260","lb":"CC2260","ev":"Vipulan challenges the enemy kings to come and die.","cl":[["a warrior who dies in battle leaves his fame on earth and takes up the guarding of heaven","general","asserted",2,"vipulan"]],"ag":["விபுலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விபுலன்","மன்னர்"],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2261":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2261,"t":["வீறின்மையின் விலங்காமென மதவேழமு மெறியா","னேறுண்டவர் நிகராயினும் பிறர்மிச்சிலென் றெறியான்","மாறன்மையின் மறம்வாடுமென் றிளையாரையு மெறியா","னாறன்மையின் முதியாரையு மெறியானயி லுழவன்."],"p":"","r":"The spear-ploughman does not strike even a rutting elephant, since a beast brings no distinction; nor a wounded man, even an equal, since he is another's leavings; nor the young, since without a match valour withers; nor the old, since they lack strength.","n":"Every reason is the striker's honour, not the victim's need: the wounded are refused as leftovers (மிச்சில்), the young because valour would wither, so the restraint is pride as much as mercy. The next verse (2262) at once qualifies the first rule: he will take on elephants if they fall many to one blow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2261","lb":"CC2261","ev":"","cl":[["a warrior should not strike a beast, since there is no distinction in it","general","asserted",1,"narrator"],["a man already wounded by another is not to be struck, being another's leavings","general","asserted",2,"narrator"],["a warrior should not strike the young or the old, who are no match for him","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["விபுலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2262":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2262,"t":["ஒன்றாயினும் பலவாயினு மோரோச்சினு ளெறிய","வென்றாயின மதவேழமு முளவோவென வினவிப்","பொன்றாழ்வரைப் புலிப்போத்தெனப் புனை தார்மிஞி றார்ப்பச்","சென்றானிகல் களிறாயிர மிரியச்சின வேலோன்."],"p":"","r":"Asking whether there are rutting elephants, one or many, worth felling in a single stroke, the angry spearman goes like a tiger cub of the golden slopes, bees humming in his garland, and a thousand hostile elephants flee.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2262","lb":"CC2262","ev":"Vipulan seeks out elephants, and a thousand of them flee before him.","cl":[],"ag":["விபுலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2263":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2263,"t":["புடைதாழ்குழை பொருவில்லுயர் பொன்னோலையொ டெரிய","வுடைநாணொடு கடிவட்டினொ டொளிர்வாளினொ டொருவ","னடையாநிக ரெறிநீயென வதுவோவென நக்கான்","கிடையாயின னிவனேயெனக் கிளராணழ குடையான்."],"p":"","r":"A man with long earrings, a gold ear-leaf, a waist-string and a shining sword comes up and says 'strike, you who have no equal'; the hero laughs 'is that so?' and, glowing with manly beauty, says 'here at last is my match'.","n":"The speakers of the two short speeches are not named; the challenger is read as the ஒருவன் of line 2 and the one who laughs as Vipulan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2263","lb":"CC2263","ev":"A warrior challenges Vipulan to single combat and Vipulan accepts him as a match.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒருவன்","விபுலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2264":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2264,"t":["இன்னீரின திரைமேலிரண் டிளவெஞ்சுட ரிகலி","மின்னோடவை சுழன்றாயிடை விளையாடுகின் றனபோற்","பொன்னாணினர் பொருவில்லியர் புனைகேடகந் திரியாக்","கொன்வாளினர் கொழுந்தாரினர் கொடிமார்பினர் திரிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Like two young suns at war on the waves of sweet water, whirling with lightning between them as if at play, the two, with gold bowstrings, matchless bows, shields and killing swords, garlands and banner-marked chests, circle each other.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2264","lb":"CC2264","ev":"Vipulan and his challenger circle each other in the duel.","cl":[],"ag":["விபுலன்","ஒருவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2265":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2265,"t":["விருந்தாயினை யெறிநீயென விரைமார்பகங் கொடுத்தாற்","கரும்பூணற வெறிந்தாங்கவ னினதூழினி யெனவே","யெரிந்தாரயி லிடைபோழ்ந்தமை யுணராதவ னின்றான்","சொரிந்தார்மல ரரமங்கையர் தொழுதார்விசும் படைந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"'You are my guest; strike', says one, offering his fragrant chest, and the other strikes and breaks his dark ornament; 'now it is your turn', and the blazing spear cleaves the challenger through the middle so that he stands unaware of it; the celestial women shower flowers and bow, and he reaches heaven.","n":"ஊழ் in line 2 (நினதூழினி) means 'turn', not fate. The duel is framed as hospitality: the first blow is the guest's portion. Heaven goes to the man who loses, not the victor. Who says which line is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","saakkaadu","marumai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2265","lb":"CC2265","ev":"Vipulan lets the challenger strike first, then kills him with one blow, and the dead man is received into heaven.","cl":[["a warrior slain fighting fairly is welcomed by the celestial women and goes to heaven","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["விபுலன்","ஒருவன்","அரமங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2266":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2266,"t":["நித்திலக் குப்பை போல நிழலுமிழ்ந் திலங்கு மேனிப்","பத்திப்பூ ணணிந்த மார்பிற் பதுமுகன் பைம்பொற் சூழி","மொய்த்தெறி யோடை நெற்றி மும்மதக் களிற்றின் மேலான்","கைத்தலத் தெஃக மேந்திக் காமுகற் கண்டு காய்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Padumukan, his body glowing like a heap of pearls and his chest hung with rows of ornaments, mounted on a triple-rutting elephant with a gold forehead plate, raises his spear and blazes with anger on seeing Kamukan.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2266","lb":"CC2266","ev":"Padumukan sights Kamukan and moves against him in anger.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்","காமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2267":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2267,"t":["மாற்றவன் சேனை தாக்கித் தளர்ந்தபின் வன்கண் மள்ள","ராற்றலொ டாண்மை தோன்ற வாருயிர் வழங்கி வீழ்ந்தார்","காற்றினாற் புடைக்கப் பட்டுக் கடலுடைந் தோடக் காம","ரேற்றிளஞ் சுறாக்க ளெங்குங் கிடந்தவை போல வொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"After the enemy army attacked and tired, the hard-eyed fighting men, showing strength and manhood, gave up their lives and fell, lying everywhere like young sharks cast up when the wind beats the sea and it breaks.","n":"Which army's men fall is unclear; மாற்றவன் சேனை may be the subject of தாக்கி or its object.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2267","lb":"CC2267","ev":"Warriors of both sides fall in numbers on the field.","cl":[],"ag":["மள்ளர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2268":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2268,"t":["தூசுலாம் பரவை யல்குற் றுணைமுலை மகளி ராடு","மூசல்போற் சேனை யோடப் பதுமுகன் களிற்றை யுந்தி","மாசில்சீர் மழையி னெற்றி மாமதி நுழைவ தேபோற்","காய்சினக் களிற்றி னெற்றி யாழிகொண் டழுத்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As the army sways and runs like the swing of women at play, Padumukan drives his elephant and, as the full moon enters the brow of a cloud, drives a discus into the brow of the furious elephant.","n":"ஆழி is read as a discus or wheel-weapon; the brow it enters is read as Kamukan's elephant's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2268","lb":"CC2268","ev":"Padumukan drives a weapon into the forehead of Kamukan's elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2269":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2269,"t":["பெருவலி யதனை நோனான் பிண்டிபா லத்தை யேந்தி","யருவரை நெற்றிப் பாய்ந்த வாய்மயிற் றோகை போலச்","சொரிமதக் களிற்றின் கும்பத் தழுத்தலிற் றோன்றல் சீறிக்","கருவலித் தடக்கை வாளிற் காளையை வெளவி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Unable to bear that great strength, Kamukan raises a javelin, and as it sinks into the temple of the rutting elephant like a peacock's tail on a mountain's brow, the lord, enraged, takes the young man with his sword.","n":"Read with 2270, the young man taken (காளையை வெளவினான்) is Kamukan, who falls in the next verse; the verse names neither.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2269","lb":"CC2269","ev":"Kamukan wounds Padumukan's elephant with a javelin, and Padumukan kills him with his sword.","cl":[],"ag":["காமுகன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2270":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2270,"t":["தீமுகத் துமிழும் வேற்கட் சில்லரிச் சிலம்பி னார்தங்","காமுகன் களத்து வீழக் கைவிர னுதியிற் சுட்டிப்","பூமுக மாலை மார்பன் பொன்னணி கவச மின்னக்","கோமுகன் கொலைவல் யானை கூற்றெனக் கடாயி னானே."],"p":"","r":"When Kamukan, lover of the anklet-wearing women whose spear-like eyes spit fire, falls on the field, Komukan, his armour flashing and garland on his chest, points with his fingertip and drives his killing elephant on like Death.","n":"The name Kamukan is played on: he is the காமுகன், the lover, of the women with anklets, so his fall is also their loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","kootru","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2270","lb":"CC2270","ev":"Kamukan falls, and Komukan charges Padumukan to avenge him.","cl":[],"ag":["காமுகன்","கோமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2271":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2271,"t":["சாரிகை திரியும் யானை யுழக்கலிற் றரணி தன்மே","லார்கலிக் குருதி வெள்ள மருந்துகள் கழுமி யெங்கும்","வீரியக் காற்றிற் பொங்கி விசும்புபோர்த் தெழுதப் பட்ட","போர்நிலைக் களத்தை யொப்பக் குருதிவான் போர்த்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The elephants wheel and churn the field, the roaring flood of blood on the earth mixes with the dust, rises on a mighty wind and covers the sky, so the heavens are hung with blood like a painted battle scene.","n":"The simile reverses art and life: the real field is compared to a painting of a field, so the slaughter is seen as a spectacle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2271","lb":"CC2271","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2272":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2272,"t":["சென்றது தடக்கை தூணி சேந்தகண் புருவங் கோலி","நின்றவிற் குனிந்த தம்பு நிமிர்ந்தன நீங்கிற் றாவி","வென்றிகொள் சரங்கண் மூழ்கி மெய்ம்மறைத் திட்டு மின்றோய்","குன்றின்மேற் பவழம் போலக் கோமுகன் றோன்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"His hand goes to the quiver, his eyes redden and brows arch, the bent bow sends the arrows straight and lives depart; then victorious arrows sink in and cover his body, and Komukan appears like coral on a lightning-touched hill.","n":"Whether lines 1 to 2 describe Komukan shooting or Padumukan shooting him is not clear; the coral image means he is red with blood, and the verse does not say he dies.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2272","lb":"CC2272","ev":"Komukan shoots and kills, then is himself covered with arrows.","cl":[],"ag":["கோமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2273":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2273,"t":["பனிவரை முளைத்த கோலப் பருப்புடைப் பவழம் போலக்","குனிமருப் புதிரந் தோய்ந்த குஞ்சரங் கொள்ள வுந்திக்","கனிபடு கிளவி யார்தங் கதிர்முலை பொருது சேந்த","துனிவரை மார்பன் சீறிச் சுடுசரஞ் சிதறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Like a beautiful coral branch growing on a snow mountain, the elephant whose curved tusks are dipped in blood is driven in to seize him, and the man whose chest is flushed from pressing women's bright breasts rages and scatters burning arrows.","n":"The two images of red (coral, the flushed chest) join love and slaughter in one figure; who is described is not named, and he is read as Padumukan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2273","lb":"CC2273","ev":"A warrior on an elephant presses the attack, shooting burning arrows.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2274":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2274,"t":["பன்னலம் பஞ்சிக் குன்றம் படரெரி முகந்த தொப்பத்","தன்னிரு கையி னாலுந் தடக்கைமால் யானை யாலு","மின்னுயிர் பருகிச் சேனை யெடுத்துக்கொண் டிரிய வோட்டிக்","கொன்முரண் டோன்ற வெம்பிக் கொலைக்களிற் றுழவ னார்த்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Like fire spreading over a hill of cotton, with both his hands and with his great-trunked elephant he drinks the sweet lives of the army, routs and drives it, and the elephant-rider, his fierce strength showing, roars.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2274","lb":"CC2274","ev":"Padumukan routs the enemy army and roars in triumph.","cl":[],"ag":["பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2275":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2275,"t":["தருக்கொடு குமர னார்ப்பத் தன்சிலை வளைய வாங்கி","யொருக்கவன் கையும் வாயு முளங்கிழித் துடுவந் தோன்றச்","சுருக்குக்கொண் டிட்ட வண்ணந் தோன்றலெய் திடுத லோடு","மருப்பிறக் களிறு குத்தி வயிரந்தான் கழிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As a prince roars in pride, the lord bends his bow and with one arrow tears through his hand, his mouth and his heart so that the feathered end shows; and as the lord shoots, the elephant, its tusk breaking, gores and its strength is spent.","n":"உடு is read as the arrow's feathered butt and வயிரம் as the elephant's hardness or fury; both are doubtful, and the elephant's part in lines 3 to 4 is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2275","lb":"CC2275","ev":"Padumukan kills a boasting prince with a single arrow, and the prince's elephant is broken.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரன்","பதுமுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2276":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2276,"t":["நித்தில மணிவண் டென்னு நெடுமதக் களிறு பாய","முத்துடை மருப்பு வல்லே யுடைந்துமுத் தொழுகு குன்றின்","மத்தக யானை வீழ்ந்து வயிரங்கொண் டொழிந்த தாங்குப்","பத்திரக் கடிப்பு மின்னப் பகடு போர்த்தான்."],"p":"","r":"When the long-rutting elephant called Nittilamanivandu charges, its pearl-bearing tusks break at once, as an elephant falls on a hill that flows with pearls and its fury ends there; the last line, with ear ornaments flashing, is broken.","n":"Line 4 is a foot short and its first words repeat the opening of 2277 (பத்திரக் கடிப்பு மின்ன), so the end of the verse may be lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2276","lb":"CC2276","ev":"The elephant Nittilamanivandu charges and breaks its tusks.","cl":[],"ag":["நித்தில மணிவண்டு"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:97"},"cc:2277":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2277,"t":["பத்திரக் கடிப்பு மின்னப் பங்கியை வம்பிற் கட்டிக்","கொத்தலர்த் தும்பை சூடிக் கோவிந்தன் வாழ்க வென்னாக்","கைத்தலத் தெஃக மேந்திக் காளைபோய் வேறு நின்றான்","மத்தக யானை மன்னர் வயிறெரி தவழ்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"A young warrior binds his hair, wears thumbai, cries long life to Govindan, takes his spear and stands apart, and the kings of the rutting elephants burn inwardly.","n":"The thumbai (line 2) is the battle flower; standing apart (வேறு நின்றான்) marks the champion who seeks single combat, and the enemy's burning belly (line 4) is the first effect of the challenge, before any blow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2277","lb":"CC2277","ev":"A warrior of Govindan's side, hailing his king, takes his stand alone before the enemy kings.","cl":[],"ag":["காளை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2278":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2278,"t":["மேகலைப் பரவை யல்குல் வெள்வளை மகளிர் செஞ்சாந்","தாகத்தைக் கவர்ந்து கொண்ட வணிமுலைத் தடத்து வைகிப்","பாகத்தைப் படாத நெஞ்சிற் பல்லவ தேய மன்னன்","சேவகன் சிங்க நாதன் செருக்களங் குறுகி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Simhanadan, warrior of the Pallava king, who had lain on the breasts of women whose paste he had taken, approaches the battlefield with a heart that knows no half-measures.","n":"The verse joins the lover and the fighter in one man: the sandal paste his chest took from women's breasts is the same chest that now goes to war, a pairing the whole run repeats. பாகத்தைப் படாத நெஞ்சு (line 3) may mean a heart that does not share itself (undivided) or one that does not yield; read as undivided resolve.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kaamam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2278","lb":"CC2278","ev":"Simhanadan, a champion of the Pallava king, comes up to the field.","cl":[],"ag":["சிங்க நாதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2279":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2279,"t":["புனைகதிர் மருப்புத் தாடி மோதிரஞ் செறித்துப் பொன்செய்","கனைகதிர் வாளை யேந்திக் கால்கழ லணிந்து நம்மை","யினையன பட்ட ஞான்றா லிறைவர்க ணினைப்ப தென்றே","முனையழன் முளிபுற் கான மேய்ந்தென நீந்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"A warrior rings his tusk-like beard, takes his gold-bright sword and binds on his anklets, saying that it is for a day like this that lords keep men like him, and swims through the enemy like fire through a dry grass forest.","n":"The speech (lines 2 to 3, closed by என்றே) is the warrior's own account of the bond between lord and fighter: the king's care is a debt that falls due in battle. Who speaks is not named; read as Simhanadan of the verse before.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2279","lb":"CC2279","ev":"The champion arms himself and drives through the enemy ranks like wildfire.","cl":[["a lord sustains his warriors so that they will serve him on the day of battle","general","reported",3,"simhanadan"]],"ag":["சிங்க நாதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2280":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2280,"t":["தாரணி பரவை மார்பிற் குங்கும மெழுதித் தாழ்ந்த","வாரமும் பூணு மின்ன வருவிலைப் பட்டி னங்க","ளோபடக் கிடந்த பொன்ஞா ணிருள்கெட விழிப்ப வெய்ய","பூரண சேனன் வண்கைப் பொருசிலை யேந்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Poornasenan, sandal painted on his broad garlanded chest and his gold cords and silks flashing until the dark is dispelled, takes up his fighting bow.","n":"Which army Poornasenan fights for is not said in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2280","lb":"CC2280","ev":"Poornasenan takes up his bow for the fight.","cl":[],"ag":["பூரண சேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2281":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2281,"t":["ஊனமர் குறடு போல விரும்புண்டு மிகுத்த மார்பிற்","றேனமர் மாலை தாழச் சிலைகுலாய்க் குனிந்த தாங்கண்","மானமர் நோக்கி னாரு மைந்தருங் குழீஇய போருட்","கானமர் காம னெய்த கணையெனச் சிதறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"With his iron-hard chest like a meat-block, garland swaying and bow bent, he scatters arrows through the battle where doe-eyed women and young men had gathered, like the arrows Kama shoots.","n":"The simile turns war arrows into love's arrows: those hit fall as lovers fall to Kama, an inversion the poet keeps returning to in this run. The women in the battle (line 3) may be those watching, or the image may carry over from the festival crowds Kama shoots at.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kaamam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2281","lb":"CC2281","ev":"Poornasenan shoots arrows thick through the battle.","cl":[],"ag":["பூரண சேனன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2282":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2282,"t":["வண்டலை மாலை தாழ மதுவுண்டு களித்து வண்கைப்","புண்டலை வேலை யேந்திப் போர்க்களங் குறுகி வாழ்த்திக்","கண்படு காறு மெந்தை கட்டியங் கார னென்றே","யுண்டொலை யார்க வேலென் றுறுவலி தாக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"A warrior, drunk on toddy and garlanded, takes his wound-headed spear and comes to the field, swearing that until his eyes close his lord is Kattiyangaran, and bids his spear eat its fill as he charges.","n":"The usual warrior's oath of faith to the lord is sworn here to the usurper who killed Civakan's father; the epic gives the enemy's men the same loyalty and courage as its hero's, without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2282","lb":"CC2282","ev":"A fighter of Kattiyangaran's side vows lifelong loyalty to the usurper and attacks.","cl":[["a warrior owes his lord loyalty until death","general","reported",3,"kattiyangaran_kumaran"]],"ag":["குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2283":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2283,"t":["கூற்றென வேழம் வீழாக் கொடிநெடுந் தோக ணூறா","வேற்றவர் தம்மைச் சீறா வேந்திர நூழில் செய்யா","வாற்றலங் குமரன் செல்வா னலைகடற் றிரையி னெற்றி","யேற்றுமீ னிரியப் பாய்ந்த வெறிசுறா வேறு போன்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The strong young man goes on without felling elephants like Death, cutting down their banners or raging at lesser foes, like a fierce shark leaping through the sea so that the fish flee.","n":"His refusal to strike what lies in his path is a point of pride: valour is measured by the foe chosen. That this குமரன் is the drinker of 2282 is a reading from the run; the verse does not name his side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2283","lb":"CC2283","ev":"The young warrior passes over lesser foes looking for one worthy of him.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2284":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2284,"t":["மாலைக்க ணாம்பல் போல மகளிர்தங் குழாத்திற் பட்டார்","கோலவாட் போருட் பட்டாற் குறுமுயற் கூடு கண்டு","சாலத்தாம் பனிக்கும் பொய்கைத் தாமரை நீர ராயின்","ஞாலத்தா ராண்மை யென்னா மெனநகா வருகின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He comes on laughing and saying: if men who are captured among women's crowds like lilies opening at evening tremble in sword-battle like pond lotuses at the moon, what becomes of the manliness of the world?","n":"குறுமுயல் கூடு (line 2), the small hare's house, is the moon, at whose sight the lotus closes; the lily that opens at evening and the lotus that shuts at moonrise set the lover's eagerness against the coward's shrinking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","maram","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2284","lb":"CC2284","ev":"The warrior advances laughing and taunting the fearful.","cl":[["men who are bold in love but tremble in battle have no manliness","general","reported",4,"kattiyangaran_kumaran"]],"ag":["குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2285":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2285,"t":["முடிச்சடை முனிவ னன்று வேள்வியிற் கொண்ட வேற்கண்","மடத்தகை மகளிர் கோல வருமுலை யுழக்கச் சேந்து","கொடிப்பல வணிந்த மார்பிற் கோவிந்தன் வாழ்க வென்று","நடத்துவா னவனை நோக்கி நகாச்சிலை பாரித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing a warrior striding with a chest flushed from women's breasts, who cries long life to Govindan, the other draws his bow without a smile.","n":"Line 1 (the matted-haired sage who once received in sacrifice the spear-eyed women) is an allusion whose story the verse does not tell. Who looks at whom and who draws the bow turns on நடத்துவான் அவனை நோக்கி (line 4); read as Govindan's man drawing on the laughing one, whose laughter is answered by நகா, the unsmiling bow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","kaamam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2285","lb":"CC2285","ev":"A fighter of Govindan's side faces the laughing warrior and bends his bow.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன் வாழ்க என்று நடத்துவான்","குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2286":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2286,"t":["போர்த்தநெய்த் தோர னாகிப் புலாற்பருந் தார்ப்பச் செல்வான்","சீர்த்தகை யவனைக் கண்டென் சினவுவே லின்னு மார்ந்தின்","றூர்த்துயி ருன்னை யுண்ணக் குறைவயி றாரு மென்றாங்","கார்த்தவாய் நிறைய வெய்தா னம்புபெய் தூணி யொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Covered in blood, with kites screaming over him, the young warrior tells his worthy foe that his angry spear is still unfed and will fill its belly today on his life; the other shoots arrows into his shouting mouth until he looks like a quiver.","n":"The boast about the spear's hunger is cut off by arrows filling the mouth that makes it: the verse punishes the speech in the organ of speech. Who is shot is read from context.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2286","lb":"CC2286","ev":"The boasting warrior is shot full of arrows as he shouts his threat.","cl":[],"ag":["குமரன்","கோவிந்தன் வாழ்க என்று நடத்துவான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2287":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2287,"t":["மொய்ப்படு சரங்கண் மூழ்க முனையெயிற் றாளி போல","வப்பணைக் கிடந்த மைந்த னருமணித் திருவில் வீசுஞ்","செப்பிள முலையி னார்கண் சென்றுலாய்ப் பிறழச் சிந்திக்","கைப்பட வெடுத்திட் டாடும் பொலங்கழற் காயு மொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Pierced by thronging arrows, the warrior lies on that bed like a sharp-toothed yali, and is like the gold ball that women with breasts like caskets toss and catch in play.","n":"Two similes in one verse: the beast of the first keeps his fierceness in death, and the women's ball of the second makes the arrow-studded body a plaything, the run's habit of seeing war through the women's world.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","maram","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2287","lb":"CC2287","ev":"The fallen warrior lies on a bed of arrows.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2288":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2288,"t":["புனைகதிர்ப் பொன்செய் நாணிற் குஞ்சியைக் கட்டி நெய்த்தோர்","நனைகதி ரெஃக மேந்தி நந்தன்வாழ் கென்ன நின்ற","வினையொளிர் காளை வேலைக் கடக்கலார் வேந்தர் நின்றார்","கனைகடல் வேலை யெல்லை கடக்கலா வண்ண நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"A youth binds his hair with a gold cord, raises his blood-wet spear and stands crying long life to Nandan, and the kings cannot pass his spear, standing as the roaring sea stands at its shore.","n":"Nandan is taken as Nandattan, Civakan's brother, fighting on his side; the verse gives only the name. The play on வேலை, spear and shore (lines 3 to 4), makes one man's spear a coast the sea of kings cannot cross.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2288","lb":"CC2288","ev":"A warrior hailing Nandan holds the enemy kings at bay by himself.","cl":[],"ag":["காளை","வேந்தர், அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2289":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2289,"t":["நின்றவப் படையு ளானே யொருமக னீலக் குஞ்சி","மன்றல மாலை நெற்றி மழகளி றன்றி வீழான்","வென்றியங் கொளிறும் வெள்வேன் மின்னென வெகுண்டு விட்டான்","சென்றவேல் விருந்து செங்கண் மறவனக் கெதிர்கொண் டானே."],"p":"","r":"One man of that standing army, blue-haired and garlanded, who would not throw at anything but a young elephant, angrily hurls his shining spear like lightning, and the red-eyed warrior receives the spear as a guest.","n":"Two courtesies of war in one verse: the thrower honours the youth by spending on him a spear kept for elephants, and the youth honours the spear by welcoming it as a guest (விருந்து எதிர்கொண்டான்), the host's word for meeting a visitor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","virundhu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2289","lb":"CC2289","ev":"An enemy champion throws his spear at the warrior, who meets it.","cl":[],"ag":["ஒருமகன்","காளை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2290":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2290,"t":["மான்வயி றார்ந்து நோக்கும் வெருவுறு மருளி னோக்கிற்","றேன்வயி றார்ந்த கோதைத் தீஞ்சொலார் கண்கள் போலு","மூன்வயி றார்ந்த வெள்வே லொய்யெனப் பறித்து நக்கான்","கான்வயி றார்ந்து தேக்கிக் களிவண்டு கனைக்குந் தாரான்."],"p":"","r":"The warrior, whose garland hums with drunken bees, plucks out in one pull the white spear that had fed on his flesh, like the frightened, darting eyes of sweet-voiced women, and laughs.","n":"Line 3 (ஊன்வயிறு ஆர்ந்த வெள்வேல் ஒய்யெனப் பறித்து நக்கான்) is the deed of Kural 774 in other words; the spear is likened to women's eyes, which also wound.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","aanmai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2290","lb":"CC2290","ev":"The wounded warrior pulls the spear from his body and laughs.","cl":[],"ag":["காளை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2291":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2291,"t":["விட்டழல் சிந்தி வெள்வேல் விசும்பின்வீழ் மின்னி னொய்தாக்","கட்டழ னெடுங்கண் யாது மிமைத்திலன் மகளி ரோச்சு","மட்டவிழ் மாலை போல மகிழ்ந்துபூண் மார்பத் தேற்றுக்","கட்டழ லெஃகஞ் செல்லக் கானெறி யாயி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As the white spear flies like lightning falling from the sky, he does not blink his fierce long eyes at all, but receives it gladly on his ornamented chest like a garland that women throw, and as the fiery spear passes through, he becomes a path for it.","n":"இமைத்திலன் (line 2) is the unblinking eye of Kural 775. கானெறி ஆயினான் (line 4) is hard: perhaps he became a road for the spear (கால்நெறி), perhaps he went the forest way, that is, died; it is taken as his fall. Whether this spear is the one of 2289 or a second is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2291","lb":"CC2291","ev":"Another spear comes; the warrior takes it on his chest without blinking and falls.","cl":[],"ag":["காளை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2292":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2292,"t":["கவிமதங் கடந்து காமர் வனப்புவீற் றிருந்த கண்ணார்","குவிமுலை நெற்றித் தீந்தேன் கொப்புளித் திட்ட பைந்தார்ச்","செவிமதக் கடலங் கேள்விச் சீவகன் கழல்கள் வாழ்த்திச்","சவிமதுத் தாம மார்பிற் சலநிதி தாக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Salanidhi, learned and broad of hearing as a sea, his garland drenched with honey from the breasts of beautiful women, praises Civakan's feet and attacks.","n":"Line 1 (கவிமதம் கடந்து) may praise beauty past what poets can describe, belonging to the women, or Salanidhi's mastery beyond poets; read as the women's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2292","lb":"CC2292","ev":"Salanidhi, a man of Civakan's side, praises his lord and charges.","cl":[],"ag":["சலநிதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2293":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2293,"t":["குஞ்சரங் குனிய நூறித் தடாயின குருதி வாடன்","னெஞ்சக நுழைந்த வேலைப்பறித்துவான் புண்ணு ணீட்டி","வெஞ்சம நோக்கி நின்று மிறைக்கொளி திருத்து வாற்கண்","டஞ்சிமற் றரசர் யானைக் குழாத்தொடு மிரிந்திட் டாரே."],"p":"","r":"His sword bent from crushing elephants and red with blood, he pulls out the spear that had entered his heart, holds it over the great wound and stands watching the fierce battle while he straightens his sword in a bend, and the kings, afraid, flee with their elephant herds.","n":"மிறைக்கொளீஇ திருத்துவான் (line 3), setting the bent blade in a lever to straighten it, is the image of Purananuru 284, where a warrior straightens his twisted sword on the tusk of the elephant he killed. What he does with the pulled spear (வான் புண்ணுள் நீட்டி, line 2) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","acham","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2293","lb":"CC2293","ev":"Salanidhi, wounded, pulls out the spear and straightens his bent sword, and the enemy kings flee.","cl":[],"ag":["சலநிதி","வேந்தர், அரசர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2294":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2294,"t":["தோட்டுவண் டொலியன் மாலைத் துடியிடை மகளி ராய்ந்த","மோட்டுவெண் முத்த மின்னு முகிழ்முலை யுழுது சாந்தம்","கோட்டுமண் கொண்ட மார்பங் கோதைவாள் குளித்து மூழ்கிக்","கோட்டுமண் கொள்ள நின்றான் குருசின்மண் கொள்ள நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"His chest, which had ploughed the bud-like breasts of slender women wearing pearls and taken their paste, now takes the plunge of swords, and he stands to take the tusk's earth, stands so that his lord may take the earth.","n":"The last line turns on மண் கொள்ள twice: the warrior's chest takes the earth dug up by tusks (the warrior's marking, or the elephant's blow), and his lord takes the earth, the kingdom, the மண்மகள் of the ilampakam's name. The warrior's body is spent for the lord's land.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2294","lb":"CC2294","ev":"Salanidhi stands under the swords so that his lord may win the land.","cl":[],"ag":["சலநிதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2295":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2295,"t":["எரிமணிக் குப்பை போல விருளற விளங்கு மேனித்","திருமணிச் செம்பொன் மார்பிற் சீவகன் சிலைகை யேந்தி","யருமணி யரச ராவி யழலம்பிற் கொள்ளை சாற்றி","விரிமணி விளங்கு மான்றோ விண்டொழ வேறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, his body bright as a heap of glowing gems that drives away the dark, takes his bow in hand, proclaims that he will plunder the lives of the jewelled kings with his fire-arrows, and mounts his jewelled chariot as the gods worship.","n":"Until now the run has shown only his men dying for him; here the lord himself enters. மான்றோ (line 4) is read as மான்தேர், the horse-drawn chariot.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2295","lb":"CC2295","ev":"Civakan takes his bow and mounts his chariot to enter the battle.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2296":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2296,"t":["கருவளி முழக்குங் காருங் கனைகட லொலியுங் கூடி","யருவலிச் சிங்க வார்ப்பு மாங்குடன் கூடிற் றென்னச்","செருவிளை கழனி மள்ள ரார்ப்போடு சிவணிச் செம்பொற்","புரிவளை முரச மார்ப்பப் போர்த்தொழி றொடங்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As if the roar of the black wind, the thundercloud, the roaring sea and the lion had all joined, the shouts of the field-reaping warriors and the twisted conch and drum sound together, and he begins the work of war.","n":"செருவிளை கழனி மள்ளர் (line 3) makes the battlefield a crop field and the warriors its reapers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2296","lb":"CC2296","ev":"Civakan opens battle to the noise of his warriors, conches and drums.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மள்ளர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2297":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2297,"t":["அரசர்த முடியும் பூணு மாரமும் வரன்றி யார்க்கு","முரசமுங் குடையுந் தாரும் பிச்சமுஞ் சுமந்து மாவும்","விரைபரித் தேரு மீர்த்து வேழங்கொண் டொழுகி வெள்ளக்","குரைபுனற் குருதி செல்லக் குமரன்விற் குனிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the prince's bow bends, a roaring flood of blood flows, sweeping kings' crowns, jewels and pearl strings, carrying drums, parasols, garlands and peacock-plume fans, dragging horses and swift chariots and bearing off elephants.","n":"The river carries off exactly the signs of royalty (crown, drum, parasol), so the flood is the end of the kings' rule as well as of their men.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2297","lb":"CC2297","ev":"Civakan's archery fills the field with a river of blood that carries away the enemy kings' regalia.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2298":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2298,"t":["கேழ்கிள ரெரிகட் பேழ்வாய்க் கிளர்பெரும் பாம்பி னோடுஞ்","சூழ்கதிர்க் குழவித் திங்க டுறுவரை வீழ்வ தேபோற்","றாழிருந் தடக்கை யோடுந் தடமருப் பிரண்டு மற்று","வீழ்தரப் பரந்த வப்பு நிழலிற்போர் மயங்கி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Great trunks and tusks fall together, as if a fiery-eyed gaping serpent and the young crescent moon fell together from a mountain, and in the shade of the spreading arrows the fighters are bewildered.","n":"Trunk as serpent and curved tusk as crescent: the image is of an eclipse falling from a hill, the arrows thick enough to cast shade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2298","lb":"CC2298","ev":"Elephants' trunks and tusks are cut down and the fighters lose their way under the arrows.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2299":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2299,"t":["ஆடவ ராண்மை தோற்று மணிகிளர் பவழத் திண்கை","நீடெரி நிலைக்கண் ணாடிப் போர்க்களத் துடைந்த மைந்தர்","காடெரி கவரக் கலலென் கவரிமா விரிந்த வண்ண","மோடக்கண் டுருவப் பைந்தா ரரிச்சந்த னுரைக்கின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the routed young men of the battle, whose coral-strong hands had shamed other men's manliness, flee like yaks scattering from a forest fire, Arichandan of the garland speaks.","n":"Arichandan's side is not named; since those fleeing are the ones Civakan has just broken, he is read as a captain of the opposing army trying to stop a rout. Line 2 (நீடெரி நிலைக்கண்ணாடி) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","aanmai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2299","lb":"CC2299","ev":"Arichandan sees the broken warriors fleeing and addresses them.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிச்சந்தன்","மைந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2300":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2300,"t":["மஞ்சிவர் மின்ன னார்தம் வாலரிச் சிலம்பு சூழ்ந்து","பஞ்சிகொண் டெழுதப் பட்ட சீறடிப் பாய்த லுண்ட","குஞ்சியங் குமரர் தங்கண் மறம்பிறர் கவர்ந்து கொள்ள","வஞ்சியிட் டோடிப் போகி னாண்மையார் கண்ண தம்மா."],"p":"","r":"Arichandan says: if young men whose tufted heads have been kicked by the small feet of lightning-like women, painted with lac and ringed with anklets, let others take their valour and run in fear, where then is manliness?","n":"A lover kicked by his beloved in a quarrel is a stock image of the man who submits to a woman; Arichandan's taunt is that men who bear that should not bear defeat, the same argument the laughing warrior made in 2284.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","maanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2300","lb":"CC2300","ev":"Arichandan shames the fleeing warriors.","cl":[["a warrior who flees in fear and lets others take his valour loses his manliness","general","reported",4,"arichandan"]],"ag":["அரிச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரிச்சந்தன்","மைந்தர்"],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2301":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2301,"t":["உழையின முச்சிக் கோடு கலங்குத லுற்ற போதே","விழைவற விதிர்த்து வீசி விட்டெறிந் திடுவ தொப்பக்","கழலவ ருள்ள மஞ்சிக் கலங்குமே லதனை வல்லே","மழைமினி னீக்கி யிட்டு வன்கண்ண ராப வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He goes on: as deer, when the horns on their heads trouble them, shake and throw them off, so the brave, when their hearts waver in fear, cast it off at once like lightning and become fierce-eyed again.","n":"The speech grants what the taunt denied: fear touches the brave too, and courage is the speed with which it is thrown off. The deer simile in lines 1 to 2 is hard to fix (shedding antlers, or tossing the head); that it continues Arichandan's speech is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","maram","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2301","lb":"CC2301","ev":"","cl":[["brave men also feel fear, but they cast it off at once","general","reported",3,"arichandan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அரிச்சந்தன்","மைந்தர்"],"ch":"cc:98"},"cc:2302":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2302,"t":["தற்புறந் தந்து வைத்த தலைமகற் குதவி வீந்தாற்","கற்பக மாலை சூட்டிக் கடியர மகளிர்த் தோய்வர்","பொற்றசொன் மாலை சூட்டிப் புலவர்கள் புகழக் கன்மே","னிற்பர்தம் வீரந் தோன்ற நெடும்புகழ் பரப்பி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Ariccandan ends his rallying speech: men who die helping the leader who raised and kept them are garlanded with karpaka flowers and embrace the women of heaven, are crowned with poets' garlands of praise, and stand on the hero-stone with their valour shown and their fame spread wide.","n":"Three rewards are stacked, one in each world: heaven (கற்பக மாலை, அரமகளிர்), the poets' word, and the stone on earth. The speaker is named in 2299 (அரிச்சந்தன்); this verse names him only by என்றான், and the next verse shows the men he rallied going down before Civakan's arrows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","nadukal","pugazh","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2302","lb":"CC2302","ev":"Ariccandan finishes urging the routed men back to the fight.","cl":[["a warrior who dies helping the master who sustained him wins heaven's women, poets' praise and a hero-stone","general","reported",1,"ariccandan"]],"ag":["அரிச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரிச்சந்தன்","சேனை"],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2303":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2303,"t":["பச்சிரும் பெஃகிட் டாங்குப் படையைக்கூர்ப் பிடுத லோடுங்","கச்சையுங் கழலும் வீக்கிக் காஞ்சனத் தளிவம் வாய்க்கிட்","டச்சுற முழங்கி யாரா வண்ணலங் குமரன் கையு","ணச்செயிற் றம்பு தின்ன நாளிரை யாக லுற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The men sharpen their weapons as one thrusts a spear into green bamboo, bind on belts and anklets, put gold into their mouths, roar to strike fear, and become the day's food for the arrows in the young prince's hand.","n":"Putting gold in the mouth (காஞ்சனத் தளிவம் வாய்க்கிட்டு) is the warrior's preparation for death; the speech of 2302 is answered at once by the slaughter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2303","lb":"CC2303","ev":"Rallied, the enemy warriors arm and charge, and Civakan's arrows cut them down.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2304":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2304,"t":["வடதிசை யெழுந்த மேகம் வலனுராய் மின்னுச் சூடிக்","குடதிசைச் சோந்து மாரி குளிறுபு சொரிவ தேபோற்","படர்கதிர்ப் பைம்பொற் றிண்டோ பாங்குற விமைப்பி னூர்ந்தா","னடர்சிலை யப்பு மாரி தாரைநின் றிட்ட தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As a cloud rising in the north sweeps rightward wreathed in lightning and pours thundering rain on the west, Civakan drove his chariot in a flash with gold-armoured shoulders, and the rain of arrows from his bow stood like a falling stream.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2304","lb":"CC2304","ev":"Civakan drives into the enemy and pours arrows on them.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2305":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2305,"t":["அற்றுவீழ் தலைகள் யானை யுடலின்மே லழுந்தி நின்ற","பொற்றதிண் சரத்திற் கோத்த பொருசரந் தாள்க ளாகத்","தெற்றிமேற் பூத்த செந்தா மரைமலர் போன்ற செங்கண்","மற்றத்தா துரிஞ்சி யுண்ணும் வண்டின் மொத்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Severed heads lodged on the elephants' bodies, held up on the arrows driven through them as on stalks, looked like red lotuses on a terrace, and their red eyes like bees crowded to sip the pollen.","n":"A battlefield-as-garden figure; it prepares the fuller lotus-pond allegory of 2311.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kalavazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2305","lb":"CC2305","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2306":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2306,"t":["திங்க ளோடுடன் குன்றெலாந் துளங்கி மாநிலஞ் சோவபோற்","சங்க மத்தகத் தலமரத் தரணி மேற்களி றழியவும்","பொங்கு மாநிரை புரளவும் பொலங்கொ டோபல முறியவுஞ்","சிங்கம் போற்றொழித் தார்த்தவன் சிறுவர் தோமிசைத் தோன்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"As if the moon and all the hills shook and the great earth gave way, with conches wailing, elephants falling, horses rolling and gold banners snapping, the sons of the king appeared on their chariots roaring like lions.","n":"அவன் சிறுவர் says only 'his sons'; that they are Kattiyangaran's is read from 2311, where the king's children are the stamens round the king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","makkal","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2306","lb":"CC2306","ev":"The usurper's sons ride out in their chariots against Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2307":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2307,"t":["சந்த னஞ்சொரி தண்கதிர்த் திங்க ளந்தொகை தாம்பல","குங்கு மக்கதிர்க் குழவியஞ் செல்வ னோடுடன் பொருவபோன்","மங்குன் மின்னென வள்ளறேர் மைந்தர் தேரொடு மயங்கலின்","வெங்கண் வில்லுமிழ் வெஞ்சர மிடைந்து வெங்கதிர் மறைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"As when the cool moon with its crowd of stars wrestles with the young sun of saffron rays, the sons' chariots tangled with the generous hero's in a flash like lightning in cloud, and the hot arrows from their bows crowded so thick that they hid the sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2307","lb":"CC2307","ev":"The sons close with Civakan's chariot and their arrows darken the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["மக்கள்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2308":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2308,"t":["குருதி வாளொளி யரவினாற் கொள்ளப் பட்டவெண் டிங்கள்போற்","றிருவ நீர்த்திகழ் வலம்புரி வாய்வைத் தாங்கவன் றெழித்தலும்","பொருவில் கீழ்வளி முழக்கினாற் பூமி மேற்சன நடுங்கிற்றே","யரவ வெஞ்சிலை வளைந்ததே யண்ணல் கண்ணழ லுமிழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Like the white moon seized by a serpent, the hero, red with the blade's light, set the right-whorled conch to his mouth and blew; the earth's people trembled as at the roar of the east wind, his serpent-fierce bow bent, and the lord's eyes spat fire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2308","lb":"CC2308","ev":"Civakan blows his conch and draws his bow in anger.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2309":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2309,"t":["கங்கை மாக்கடற் பாய்வதே போன்று காளைதன் கார்முக","மைந்த ரார்த்தவர் வாயெலா நிறைய வெஞ்சரங் கான்றபி","னெஞ்சம போழ்ந்தழ லம்புண நீங்கி னாருயிர் நீண்முழைச்","சிங்க வேறுகள் கிடந்தபோற் சிறுவர் தோமிசைத் இஞ்சினார்."],"p":"","r":"As the Ganga rushes into the sea, the young bull's bow poured hot arrows till they filled the mouths of the youths who had roared, and pierced through the chest they lay dead on their chariots like lions in a long cave.","n":"The last word, printed இஞ்சினார், is read as 'lay dead' only from the simile; the line is likely corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2309","lb":"CC2309","ev":"Civakan's arrows kill the usurper's sons on their chariots.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2310":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2310,"t":["நிவந்த வெண்குடை வீழவும் வேந்தர் நீள்விசும் பேறவு","முவந்து பேய்க்கண மாடவு மோரி கொள்ளைகொண் டுண்ணவுங்","கவந்த மெங்கணு மாடவுங் களிறு மாவொடு கவிழவுஞ்","சிவந்த சீவக சாமிகண் புருவ மும்முரி முரிந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"White umbrellas fell, kings rose to the far sky, hosts of ghouls danced, jackals feasted, headless trunks danced everywhere, elephants and horses toppled, and the red-eyed Civakasami's brows curved in wrath.","n":"Kings 'climbing the sky' (நீள்விசும்பு ஏற) is the battlefield death 2302 promised as heaven, now told of the enemy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kalavazhi","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2310","lb":"CC2310","ev":"The field fills with the dead as Civakan's anger mounts.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2311":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2311,"t":["பொய்கை போர்க்களம் புறவிதழ் புலவு வாட்படை புல்லித","ழைய கொல்களி றகவித ழரச ரல்லிதன் மக்களா","மையில் கொட்டையம் மன்னனா மலர்ந்த தாமரை வரிசையாற்","பைய வுண்டபின் கொட்டைமேற் பவித்திரத் தும்பி பறந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The battlefield is a lotus pond: the swords are the outer petals, the elephants the inner petals, the kings the stamens, the king's sons the inner ring, and the king himself the seed-pod; and having slowly fed on the flower in order, the holy bee flew up onto the pod.","n":"The allegory turns the slaughter into a bee's orderly feeding from the outside in, so the order of the battle is also the order of a hierarchy, ending at its centre; the bee is called பவித்திரம், holy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavazhi","maram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2311","lb":"CC2311","ev":"Having destroyed the army and the sons, Civakan turns on the king himself.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2312":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2312,"t":["கலைமுத்தங் கொள்ளு மல்குற் கார்மழை மின்ன னார்த","முலைமுத்தங் கொள்ளச் சாந்த மழிந்துதார் முருகு விம்மு","மலைமுத்தங் கொள்ளு மார்பின் மன்னனுங் கண்டு காய்ந்தான்","சிலைமுத்தங் கொள்ளுந் திண்டோட் செம்மலுந் தீயிற் சேந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, whose mountainous chest is kissed by the pearls of women's breasts, saw it and burned with rage, and the noble one, whose strong shoulders the bow kisses, reddened like fire.","n":"The fourfold முத்தம் pun (pearl and kiss) sets the lover's chest of the king against the archer's shoulders of the hero.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2312","lb":"CC2312","ev":"Kattiyangaran and Civakan face each other in fury.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2313":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2313,"t":["தன்மதந் திவண்ட வண்டு தங்கிய காட்டுள் வேழம்","பின்மதஞ் செறித்திட்டு டஞ்சிப் பிடிமறந் திரிந்து போகும்","வென்மதக் களிற்று வெய்ய வசனிவே கத்தின் மேலான்","மின்னுமிழ் மாலை வேந்தன் வீரற்கு விளம்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Seated on the fierce elephant Asanivegam, at whose musk a wild tusker swallows its own must, fears and runs leaving its mate, the king of the flashing garland spoke to the hero.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2313","lb":"CC2313","ev":"Kattiyangaran, mounted on his war elephant, addresses Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2314":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2314,"t":["நல்வினை யுடைய நீரார் நஞ்சுணி னமுத மாகு","மில்லையே லமுது நஞ்சா மின்னதால் வினையி னாக்கங்","கொல்வல்யா னிவனை யென்று மிவன்கொல்லு மென்னை யென்று","மல்லன நினைத்தல் செல்லா ரறிவினாற் பெரிய நீரார்."],"p":"","r":"Kattiyangaran says that for those with good deeds poison drunk becomes nectar, and for those without, nectar becomes poison, such is the working of deeds; so the truly wise never think 'I can kill him' or 'he will kill me'.","n":"The usurper, about to die, gives the whole outcome to deeds and none to his own seizure of the throne. The story has given another cause: Dharmadattan warned at 245 that Fortune would turn from him if he seized the king. Here karma and the ruler's fault are both on the page, in different mouths.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2314","lb":"CC2314","ev":"","cl":[["by good deeds poison becomes nectar, and without them nectar becomes poison","general","reported",1,"kattiyangaran"],["the wise do not think of killing or being killed, since the outcome lies with deeds","general","reported",3,"kattiyangaran"]],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2315":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2315,"t":["அகப்படு பொறியி னாரை யாக்குவா ரியாவ ரம்மா","மிகப்படு பொறியி னாரை வெறியராச் செய்ய லாமோ","நகைக்கதிர் மதியம் வெய்தா நடுங்கச்சுட்டிடுத லுண்டே","பகைக்கதிர்ப் பருதி சந்து மாலியும் பயத்த லுண்டே."],"p":"","r":"He goes on: who can ruin those whose fortune is in their grasp, and can those of great fortune be made empty? the cool-rayed moon may scorch till men tremble, and the hostile sun may yield coolness.","n":"பொறி is fortune as allotted; the reversed moon and sun of lines 3-4 can be read as what fortune can do, or as his own fall. The end of line 4 (சந்து மாலியும்) is unclear as divided.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2315","lb":"CC2315","ev":"","cl":[["no one can undo the fortune that is someone's lot, though it may reverse the natural order","general","reported",1,"kattiyangaran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2316":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2316,"t":["புரிமுத்த மாலைப் பொற்கோல் விளக்கினுட் பெய்த நெய்யுந்","திரியுஞ்சென் றற்ற போழ்தே திருச்சுடர் தேம்பி னல்லா","லெரிமொய்த்துப் பெருக லுண்டோ விருவினை சென்று தேய்ந்தாற்","பரிவுற்றுக் கெடாமற் செல்வம் பற்றியா ரதனை வைப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"A lamp of pearl-strung gold stand does not flare up after its ghee and its wick are spent; its flame only dwindles; so when the two kinds of deed have worn away, who can keep wealth from perishing, however anxiously he guards it?","n":"இருவினை, both good and bad deeds, makes the fuel of wealth all of one's karma, not only merit; the usurper reads his own lost throne as a lamp that has burnt out rather than one taken from its owner.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2316","lb":"CC2316","ev":"","cl":[["when one's twofold deeds are spent, wealth perishes and no care can keep it","general","reported",3,"kattiyangaran"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2317":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2317,"t":["நல்லொளிப் பவளச் செவ்வாய் நன்மணி யெயிறு கோலி","வில்லிட நக்கு வீர னஞ்சினா யென்ன வேந்தன்","வெல்வது விதியி னாகும் வேல்வரி னிமைப்பே னாயிற்","சொல்லிநீ நகவும் பெற்றாய் தோன்றன்மற் றென்னை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The hero, baring his jewel-like teeth, laughed so they flashed and said 'you are afraid'; the king answered that victory comes by fate, but if he blinked when a spear came, then the hero might say so and laugh, and bade him come at him.","n":"Civakan's mockery reads the fate speech of 2314 to 2316 as cowardice; the king separates the two, granting the outcome to fate while keeping courage, the unblinking eye, as his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","oozh","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2317","lb":"CC2317","ev":"Civakan mocks Kattiyangaran's talk of fate as fear, and Kattiyangaran challenges him.","cl":[["victory in battle comes by fate","general","reported",3,"kattiyangaran"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2318":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2318,"t":["பஞ்சிமெல் லடியி னார்தம் பாடகந் திருத்திச் சேந்து","நெஞ்சுநொந் தழுத கண்ணீர் துடைத்தலி னிறைந்த கோல","வஞ்சனக் கலுழி யஞ்சே றாடிய கடக வண்கை","வெஞ்சிலை கொண்டு வெய்ய வுருமென முழங்கிச் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"With the bangled generous hand that had set right the anklets of soft-footed women, wiped the tears they wept when hurt and so been smeared with their collyrium, he seized the cruel bow and spoke roaring like thunder.","n":"The hand is a lover's before it is a warrior's; the next verse's elephant shows it is the king's, and the image echoes 2312's lover's chest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2318","lb":"CC2318","ev":"Kattiyangaran takes up his bow and speaks.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2319":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2319,"t":["இல்லாளை யஞ்சி விருந்தின்முகங் கொன்ற நெஞ்சிற்","புல்லாள னாக மறந்தோற்பி னெனப்பு கைந்து","வில்வா ளழுவம் பிளந்திட்டு வெகுண்டு நோக்கிக்","கொல்யானை யுந்திக் கடைமேலுமொர் கோறொ டுத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"He vows that if he loses in valour he may become a base man of a heart that fears his wife and kills the face of the guest, and fuming he split through the press of bow and sword, glared, drove his killing elephant forward and fitted one last arrow.","n":"The self-curse names faults of the house, not the field: the worst he can imagine becoming is a henpecked man who shames a guest. The vow is in the first person (தோற்பின்), while the description around it is the narrator's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","virundhu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2319","lb":"CC2319","ev":"Kattiyangaran vows, charges on his elephant and sets an arrow to his bow.","cl":[["a man who fears his wife and turns from his guest is base","general","reported",1,"kattiyangaran"]],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கட்டியங்காரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2320":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2320,"t":["தொடுத்தாங்க வம்பு தொடைவாங்கி விடாத முன்ன","மடுத்தாங்க வம்புஞ் சிலையும்மத னாணு மற்றுக்","கடுத்தாங்கு வீழக் கதிர்வான்பிறை யம்பி னெய்தான்","வடித்தாரை வெள்வேல் வயிரம்மணிப் பூணி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Before he could draw and loose the arrow he had fitted, the hero of the sharp white spear and diamond ornaments shot with a bright crescent-headed arrow and cut the arrow, the bow and its string so that they fell.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2320","lb":"CC2320","ev":"Civakan cuts Kattiyangaran's arrow, bow and bowstring with one crescent arrow.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2321":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2321,"t":["அம்புஞ் சிலையு மறுத்தானென் றழன்று பொன்வாள்","வெம்பப் பிடித்து வெகுண்டாங்கவன் தேரின் மேலே","பைம்பொன் முடியான் படப்பாய்ந்திடு கென்று பாய்வான்","செம்பொன் னுலகி னிழிகின்றவொர் தேவ னொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Blazing that his arrow and bow were cut, he gripped his gold sword in fury and leapt at the hero's chariot crying 'let the gold-crowned one fall', like a god descending from the golden world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2321","lb":"CC2321","ev":"Disarmed, Kattiyangaran leaps sword in hand onto Civakan's chariot.","cl":[],"ag":["கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2322":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2322,"t":["மொய்வார் குழலார் முலைப்போர்க்கள மாய மார்பிற்","செய்யோன் செழும்பொற் சரஞ்சென்றன சென்ற தாவி","வெய்தா விழியா வெகுவத்துவர் வாய்ம டியா","மையார் விசும்பின் மதிவீழ்வது போல வீழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The red one's golden arrows went into the chest that had been a battlefield for the breasts of women, and with them his life went; eyes burning, lips bitten, he fell like the moon falling from the dark sky.","n":"The lover's chest of 2312 and 2318 becomes the target; the moon-fall answers 2308 and 2324, where Civakan is the moon in the serpent's mouth and then the sun freed from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2322","lb":"CC2322","ev":"Civakan's arrows pierce Kattiyangaran's chest and he falls dead.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2323":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2323,"t":["கட்டியங் கார னென்னுங் கலியர சழிந்த தாங்குப்","பட்டவிப் பகைமை நீங்கிப் படைத்தொழி லொழிக வென்னாக்","கொட்டினர் முரச மள்ள ரார்த்தனர் குருதிக் கண்ணீர்","விட்டமு தவன்க ணார்வ மண்மக ணீக்கி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"As if the Kali-rule called Kattiyangaran had perished, the warriors beat the drums proclaiming that the enmity was over and the work of arms should cease, and roared; and the Earth goddess, shedding tears of blood, gave her longing to him.","n":"கலியரசு names the usurper's reign as the age of Kali, a rule against order rather than a single wrong. Line 4 (விட்டமு தவன்கண் ஆர்வம் மண்மகள் நீக்கினாள்) can be divided so that the Earth puts away her grief or turns her love to Civakan; the ilampakam's name rests on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","pagai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2323","lb":"CC2323","ev":"With Kattiyangaran dead, Civakan's warriors proclaim the war's end.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை","மண்மகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2324":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2324,"t":["ஒல்லைநீ ருலக மஞ்ச வொளியுமிழ் பருதி தன்னைக்","கல்லெனக் கடலி னெற்றிக் கவுட்படுத் திட்டு நாகம்","பல்பகல் கழிந்த பின்றைப் பன்மணி நாகந் தன்னை","வல்லைவாய் போழ்ந்து போந்தொர் மழகதிர் நின்ற தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan was like the young sun that a serpent had seized in the forehead of the sea and held in its cheek while the world trembled, standing forth after many days when it had split open the jewelled serpent's mouth.","n":"The 'many days' (பல்பகல்) under the serpent are the years of the usurpation since his father's death; the victory is figured as release from an eclipse, not a conquest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2324","lb":"CC2324","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2325":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2325,"t":["கோட்டுமீன் குழாத்தின் மள்ள ரீண்டினர் மன்னர் சூழ்ந்தார்","மோட்டுமீன் சூழாத்தி னெங்குந் தீவிகை மொய்த்த முத்த","மாட்டுநீர்க் கடலி னார்த்த தணிநகர் வென்றி மாலை","கேட்டுநீர் நிறைந்து கேடில் விசயைகண் குளிர்ந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The warriors gathered like the hosts of stars round the moon, kings surrounded him, the city roared like the sea, its lamps thronged like stars; and hearing the victory garland proclaimed, the faultless Vijayai's eyes filled with tears and grew cool.","n":"Vijayai's cooled eyes close the grief that began when she left the city on the peacock machine; கேடில் (faultless, without ruin) is said of her at the moment her ruin is reversed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2325","lb":"CC2325","ev":"The army and kings gather round Civakan, and Vijayai hears of his victory and weeps for joy.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2326":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2326,"t":["அணிமுடி யரசர் மாலை யழனுதி வாள்க ளென்னு","மணிபுனை குடத்தி னெய்த்தோர் மண்ணுநீர் மருள வாட்டிப்","பணைமுலைப் பைம்பொன் மாலைப் பாசிழைப் பூமி தேவி","யிணைமுலை யேக மாக நுகரிய வெய்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"With the crowned kings' garlands and fire-edged swords as jewelled pots and their blood standing for the bathing water, he was bathed as for anointing, and came to enjoy as one the paired breasts of the Earth goddess of gold garlands and fine jewels.","n":"The coronation bath (மண்ணுநீர்) is made of the battlefield, and the winning of the kingdom is told as a marriage, the hero's last bride after his eight wives: this is the ilampakam's மண்மகள்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","naadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2326","lb":"CC2326","ev":"Civakan, as if anointed in the enemy's blood, takes the earth as his bride.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மண்மகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:99"},"cc:2327":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2327,"t":["கண்ணாடி யன்ன கடிமார்பன் சிவந்து நீண்ட","கண்ணாடி வென்று களங்கண்டு நியம முற்றிக்","கண்ணாடி வண்டு பருகுங்கமழ் மாலை மூதூர்க்","கண்ணாடி யானை யவர்கைதொழச் சென்று புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Having won the field and finished the rites of victory, the hero of the fragrant chest enters the ancient city, whose garlands the bees drink, while its people worship him with joined hands.","n":"The verse is a மடக்கு: every line opens with கண்ணாடி in a different sense (a mirror for the chest in line 1, the bees drinking in line 3), and the sense of the word in lines 2 and 4 (a foe's eye, a battle, a kind of elephant) can only be guessed. The opening of the ilampakam named for பூமகள், the goddess of fortune, is his return to his father's city.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2327","lb":"CC2327","ev":"Civakan, victorious, enters the old capital as its people worship him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2328":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2328,"t":["கூடார் புலியு முழைக்கோளரி யேறு மன்ன","கூடார் மெலியக் கொலைவேனினைந் தானை யேத்திக்","கூடார மாலைக் குவிமென்முலைக் கோதை நல்லார்","கூடார மாட மயில்போலக் குழீயி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Praising the one who set his killing spear on the wasting of his foes like a tiger or a lion of the cave, the garlanded young women crowd the high mansions like peacocks.","n":"Again a மடக்கு on கூடார் / கூடாரம்: the enemies (கூடார், those who do not join) and the women's tent-shaped breasts and mansions share one sound.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2328","lb":"CC2328","ev":"The city's women gather on the mansions, praising Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2329":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2329,"t":["மாலைச் செற்றான் மக்களொ டெல்லா முடனேயிம்","மாலைச் செற்றான் வைந்நுனை யம்பின் னிவனென்பார்","மாலைக் கின்றே மாய்ந்தது மாயாப் பழியென்பார்","மாலைக் கேற்ற வார்குழல் வேய்த்தோண் மடநல்லார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with flower-like hair say that this man with his sharp arrows has destroyed the deluded one together with all his sons, and others say that today the blame that would not die has died.","n":"மாயாப் பழி, the blame that does not perish, is said to have perished (மாய்ந்தது): the women speak as if the regicide's stain lay on the city, or on the son who had not yet avenged it, until this day. The Tamil does not say whose blame it was.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","pagai","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2329","lb":"CC2329","ev":"","cl":[["the lasting blame left by the usurper's crime ended with his death and his line's","general","reported",3,"city_women"]],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2330":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2330,"t":["நாகந் நெற்றி நன்மணி சிந்தும் மருவிபோ","னாகந் நெற்றிந் நன்மணி யோடை நறவிம்மு","நாகந் நெற்றிந் நன்மலர் சிந்நி நளிர்செம்பொ","னாகந் நெற்றி மங்கைய ரொத்தார் மடநல்லார்."],"p":"","r":"The young women look like the maidens of the gods' world, as the city's elephants with jewelled forehead-plates, its flowering trees and its hill-streams scattering gems all play on the one word நாகம்.","n":"Each line opens with நாகம் in a different sense (mountain or serpent, elephant, the naga tree, the heavenly world); which sense governs which line is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2330","lb":"CC2330","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2331":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2331,"t":["கோடிக் கொடுங் கூம்புயர் நாவாய் நெடுமாடங்","கோடிப் பட்டிற் கொள்கொடி கூடப் புனைவாருங்","கோடித் தானைக் கொற்றவற் காண்பா னிழைமின்னக்","கோடிச் செம்பொற் கொம்பரின் முன்முன் றொழுவாரும்."],"p":"","r":"Some dress the tall mansions, like ships with high masts, with banners of new silk, and others, jewels flashing like golden creepers, go to the front to worship the victor king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","azhagu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2331","lb":"CC2331","ev":"The city is hung with new silk banners, and women press forward to worship Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2332":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2332,"t":["அம்புகை வல்லில் லார்கழன் மள்ளர் திறலேத்த","வம்புகைக் கொண்டா லாரிவற் கீண்டு நிகராவா","ரம்புகை யார்ந்த வந்துகி லல்குல் லவிர்கோதா","யம்புகைக் காணா மையனைக் கையிற் றொழுதென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"As warriors with bows and anklets praise his strength, the women ask who here could equal him once he takes up the bow, and bid one another to see their lord and worship him with joined hands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2332","lb":"CC2332","ev":"Warriors praise Civakan and the women urge each other to worship him.","cl":[],"ag":["மள்ளர்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்","மகளிர்"],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2333":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2333,"t":["மைத்துன நீண்ட மாமணி மாடம் மிசையேறி","மைத்துன நீண்ட வாட்டடங் கண்ணார் மலர்தூவ","மைத்துன மன்னர் மால்களி றேறிப் புடைசூழ","மைத்துன நீண்ட மாமணி வண்ண னவனொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"As the long-eyed women climb the gem mansions to scatter flowers and his kinsmen kings ride round him on great elephants, he looks like the dark gem-coloured god among his kin.","n":"மைத்துன runs through all four lines; in line 4 the dark god surrounded by his மைத்துனர் is most likely Krishna among his cousins and brothers-in-law, so the procession of kin is likened to the god's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","sutram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2333","lb":"CC2333","ev":"Women shower flowers from the mansions while Civakan rides ringed by his kinsmen kings.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","மைத்துன மன்னர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2334":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2334,"t":["ஊது வண்டரற் றும்முயர் தாமரைப்","போது பூங்கழு நீரொடு பூத்துடன்","வீதி மல்கின போன்மிளிர் வேற்கணு","மாத ரார்முகப் பூவு மலர்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Lotuses where bees hum and blue waterlilies seem to have bloomed together filling the street, for the women's faces, with their flashing spear-eyes, have opened like flowers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2334","lb":"CC2334","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2335":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2335,"t":["வீணை வித்தகன் வேந்தடு வீங்குதோள்","காணுங் காரிகை யார்கதிர் வெம்முலைப்","பூணு மாரமு மீன்றுபொன் பூத்தலர்ந்","தியாண ரூரம ராபதி போன்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the swelling shoulders of the master of the vina who slew the king, the women's breasts with their jewels and garlands break into gold, and the rich city looks like Amaravati, the gods' city.","n":"பொன் பூத்து is the gold-coloured pallor of longing spreading over the women, set beside the gold of their ornaments; the epithet வீணை வித்தகன் recalls how he won Gandharvadattai, so the musician and the king-slayer are one man in the women's eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pasalai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2335","lb":"CC2335","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2336":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2336,"t":["தேம்பெய் கற்பகத் தாரவன் சேர்தலும்","பூம்பெய் கோதைப் புரிசைக் குழாநல","மோம்பு திங்க ளுலந்து சுடர்கண்ட","வாம்ப லாய்மலர்க் காடொத் தழிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"When he of the wishing-tree garland comes near, the beauty of the crowds of garlanded women on the walls wastes away, like a forest of waterlilies that closes when the moon wanes and the sun is seen.","n":"The women are the night-flowering ambal and he the sun: the same arrival that makes the city bloom in 2334 makes these women close.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2336","lb":"CC2336","ev":"The women on the walls wilt with longing as Civakan passes.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2337":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2337,"t":["மாகம் முழக்கின் மணிநாகம் பதைப்ப வேபோ","லாகம் மறவ ரகன்கோயில்புக் கம்பொன் மாலைத்","தோகைம் மடவார் துவர்வாய் துடித் தஞ்ச வெம்பா","வேகம் முடைத்தாய் விழியாத்தொழித் தேகுகென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Like jewelled snakes trembling at thunder in the sky, the warriors burst into the usurper's wide palace, and while the peacock-like women's coral lips quiver in fear, they order them fiercely to leave at once without looking back.","n":"The command in line 4 (விழியாத்தொழித் தேகுகென்றார்) is hard to divide: either 'go without looking' or 'leave everything and go'. The palace is not named as Kattiyangaran's; it is the one the victors now take.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kedu","kadunjol","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2337","lb":"CC2337","ev":"Civakan's warriors enter Kattiyangaran's palace and drive out its women.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை","மடவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2338":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2338,"t":["செய்பாவை யன்னார் சிலம்பார்க்கு மென் சீற டியார்","செய்பூந் தவிசின் மிசையல்லது சேற லில்லார்","மையார்ந்த கண்ணீர் மணிப்பூண்முலை பாய விம்மா","வெய்தா மடவார் வெறுவெந்நிலத் தேகி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The doll-like women with anklets on soft small feet, who had never walked except on made flower-cushions, go out sobbing onto the bare hot ground, kohl-dark tears running over their jewelled breasts.","n":"The verse names no fault of the women and no doctrine: the reversal from cushion to scorching earth is left to speak for itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kedu","nilaiyaamai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2338","lb":"CC2338","ev":"The usurper's women leave the palace on foot across the bare ground.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2339":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2339,"t":["நெருப்புற்ற போல நிலமோந்துழிச் செய்ய வாகிப்","பருக்கென்ற கோல மரற்பல்பழம் போன்று கொப்புள்","வருத்தம் மிழற்றிப் பசும்பொற்சிலம் போசை செய்யச்","செருக்கற்ற பஞ்சி மலர்ச்சீறடி நோவச் சென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Their feet redden as if the earth they smelled were fire, blisters rise like the ripe fruit of the marul plant, their gold anklets seem to cry out their pain, and they go on, their cotton-soft feet aching.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2339","lb":"CC2339","ev":"The women walk on with blistered feet.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2340":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2340,"t":["பொற்பூண் சுமந்து பொருகோட்டை யழித்து வெம்போர்","கற்பா னெழுந்த முலையார்களங் கண்டு நீங்கி","நற்பூ ணணிந்த முலையார்நிலை கால்ச ரிந்து","நெற்றிந் நிறுத்து வடம்வைத்த முலையி னாரும்."],"p":"","r":"Among them go women whose jewelled breasts, risen as if to learn fierce war, have seen the battlefield and turned from it, and others whose stance gives way, with chains laid on their breasts.","n":"The sentence runs into 2341, which lists more groups of women and gives the verb. Lines 1 and 4 (பொருகோட்டை யழித்து, நெற்றிந் நிறுத்து வடம்வைத்த) are obscure; my reading of who these women are is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2340","lb":"CC2340","ev":"The palace women leave in their several groups.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2341":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2341,"t":["செங்காற் குழவி தழீயினார்திங்கள் புக்க நீரா","ரங்கான் முலையி னரும்பால்வரப் பாயினாரும்","பைங்காசு முத்தும் பவழத்தொடு பைம்பொ னார்ந்த","பொங்கார் முலையார் திருமுற்ற நிறைந்து புக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"Women holding red-footed infants, women near their time whose breasts run with milk, and women laden with gold coins, pearls and coral, all crowd into the royal forecourt and fill it.","n":"Infants and women about to give birth are among the expelled: the fall of the usurper's house takes in children who did nothing, as Civakan himself was born in the burning ground after his father's fall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2341","lb":"CC2341","ev":"The expelled women, with infants and the pregnant among them, fill the palace forecourt.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2342":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2342,"t":["பெய்யார் முகிலிற் பிறழ்பூங்கொடி மின்னின் மின்னா","நெய்யார்ந்த கூந்த னிழற்பொன்னரி மாலை சோரக்","கையார் வளையார் புலிகண்ணுறக் கண்டு சோரா","நையாத் துயரா நடுங்கும்பிணை மான்க ளொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Their gold-strung garlands slipping from oiled hair, the bangled women, flashing like flower-creepers in lightning from a cloud that has not rained, tremble in unwasting grief like does that see a tiger before them.","n":"The tiger (புலி) is the victor before their eyes, and the women his prey, the same figure the city's women used of Civakan in praise in 2328.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2342","lb":"CC2342","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மடவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2343":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2343,"t":["வட்டம் மலர்த்தா ரவனாலருள் பெற்று வான்பொற்","பட்டம் மணிந்தா ளிவர்தங்களுள் யாவ னென்ன","மட்டா ரலங்க லவன்மக்களுந் தானு மாதோ","பட்டா ரமருட் பசும்பொன்முடி சூழ வென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"When it is asked which among these was the one who won the favour of the garlanded king and wore the queen's golden fillet, the answer is that he and his sons have fallen in battle, ringed by golden crowns.","n":"Who asks and who answers is not said (என்ன ... என்றார்). The answer does not name a queen: with the king and his heirs dead, the fillet has no wearer, and the question of rank among his women has become empty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kedu","saakkaadu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2343","lb":"CC2343","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:100"},"cc:2344":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2344,"t":["மாலே றனையா னொடுமக்களுக் கஃதோ வென்னா","வேலேறு பெற்ற பிணையின்னனி மாழ்கி வீழ்ந்து","சேலேறு சின்னீ ரிடைச்செல்வன போன்று செங்கண்","மேலேறி மூழ்கிப் பிறழ்ந்தாழ்ந்த விறந்து பட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"Told that the bull-like usurper and his sons have fallen, his queen cries 'has it come to that?', sinks like a doe struck by a spear, her red eyes rolling up and sinking like carp in shallow water, and dies.","n":"The question in 2343 asks which of the women wore the gold fillet the usurper gave her; this verse follows on with her answer in death, so she is taken as his chief queen, though the verse never names her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","vaazhkkaithunai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2344","lb":"CC2344","ev":"Kattiyangaran's queen hears that he and his sons died in the battle, faints and dies.","cl":[],"ag":["பட்டம் அணிந்தாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2345":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2345,"t":["ஐவா யரவி னவிராரழல் போன்று சீறி","வெய்யோ னுயிர்ப்பின் விடுத்தேனென் வெகுளி வெந்தீ","மையா ரணல மணிநாகங் கலுழன் வாய்ப்பட்","டுய்யா வெனநீ ருடன்றுள்ள முருகல் வேண்டா."],"p":"","r":"Civakan tells the usurper's women that his fiery anger, hissing like the five-hooded snake, was let go when the cruel man breathed his last, and that they need not melt in fear like dark serpents caught in Garuda's beak.","n":"The anger is said to have been spent on the one enemy (வெய்யோன் உயிர்ப்பின் விடுத்தேன்): the victor draws a line between the usurper and his household.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","arul","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2345","lb":"CC2345","ev":"Civakan assures the dead usurper's women that his anger ended with the usurper.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மடவார்"],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2346":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2346,"t":["மண்கேழ் மணியி னுழையுந்துகி னூலின் வாய்த்த","நுண்கேழ் நுசுப்பின் மடவீர்நம்மை நோவ செய்யே","னொண்கேழ்க் கழுநீ ரொளிமுத்த முமிழ்வ தேபோற்","பண்கேழ் மொழியீர் நெடுங்கண்பனி வீழ்த்தல் வேண்டா."],"p":"","r":"He goes on: women of waists fine as a thread through a gem, of speech like song, he will do them no harm, and they should not let their long eyes rain tears like the red water-lily spilling pearls.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2346","lb":"CC2346","ev":"Civakan promises the women he will not harm them and bids them stop weeping.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மடவார்"],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2347":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2347,"t":["என்னுங்கட் குள்ள மிலங்கீர்வளைக் கையி னீரே","மன்னிங்கு வாழ்வு தருதும்மவற் றானும் வாழ்மின்","பொன்னிங்குக் கொண்டு புறம்போகியும் வாழ்மின்னென்றான்","வின்னுங்க வீங்கி விழுக்கந்தென நீண்ட தோளான்."],"p":"","r":"The broad-shouldered hero says they have nothing to fear from him: he will give them a living here to live on, or they may take gold and go and live elsewhere.","n":"The choice to stay or leave with gold is the epic's picture of a just conqueror's treatment of the fallen enemy's household; nothing is taken from them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","eegai","ali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2347","lb":"CC2347","ev":"Civakan offers the usurper's women maintenance in the city or gold to leave with.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மடவார்"],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2348":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2348,"t":["தீத்தும்மும் வேலான் றிருவாய்மொழி வான்மு ழக்கம்","வாய்த்தங்குக் கேட்டு மடமஞ்ஞைக் குழாத்தி னேகிக்","காய்த்தெங்கு சூழ்ந்த கரும்பார்தம் பதிகள் புக்கார்","சேய்ச்செந் தவிசி நெருப்பென்றெழுஞ் சீற டியார்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the spear-bearer's words like the thunder of the sky, the women, whose small feet flinch from a red cushion as from fire, go off like a flock of young peahens to their own towns ringed with coconut and cane.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2348","lb":"CC2348","ev":"The usurper's women leave the palace for their home towns.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2349":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2349,"t":["காதார் குழையுங் கடற்சங்கமுங் குங்கு மமும்","போதா ரலங்கற் பொறையும்பொறை யென்று நீக்கித்","தாதார் குவளைத் தடங்கண்முத் துருட்டி விம்மா","மாதார் மயிலன் னவர்சண்பகச் சாம்பலொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Calling them a burden, they put off their ear-rings, sea-conch bangles, saffron and the weight of their flower garlands, roll pearl tears from their kuvalai eyes and sob, and grow pale like the ash of the champak.","n":"The putting off of ornaments is the mark of widowhood; the scene repeats, on the usurper's side, what Saccandan's women did at his death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaimmai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2349","lb":"CC2349","ev":"The widowed women strip off their ornaments and weep.","cl":[],"ag":["மடவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2350":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2350,"t":["ஆய்பொற் புரிசை யணியாரகன் கோயி லெல்லாங்","காய்பொற் கடிகைக் கதிர்க்கைவிளக் கேந்தி மள்ளர்","வேய்பொன் னறையும் பிறவும்விரைந் தாய்ந்த பின்றைச்","சேய்பொற் கமல மகள்கைதொழுச் சென்று புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"When his soldiers, carrying bright lamps in their hands, have quickly searched every gold-walled hall and treasure chamber of the palace, he worships the goddess of the red lotus and goes in.","n":"The goddess of the lotus is Fortune (பூமகள், the ilampakam's name); entering the palace only after the search and her worship marks the taking of the kingdom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2350","lb":"CC2350","ev":"Civakan's soldiers search the palace, and he worships the lotus goddess and enters it.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2351":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2351,"t":["முலையீன்ற பெண்ணைத் திரடாமங்க டாழ்ந்து முற்று","மலையீன்ற மஞ்சின் மணிப்பூம்புகை மல்கி விம்மக்","கலையீன்ற சொல்லார் கமழ்பூவணைக் காவல் கொண்டார்","கொலையீன்ற வேற்கண் ணவர்கூடிய மார்பற் கன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Women of polished speech take watch over the scented flower bed made ready for the hero, with garlands of palm-like strands hanging down and fragrant smoke rising like mist from a hill.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2351","lb":"CC2351","ev":"The palace women prepare and guard Civakan's bed.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2352":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2352,"t":["போர்க்கோல நீக்கிப் புகழ்ப்பொன்னி னெழுதப் பட்ட","வார்க்கோல மாலை முலையார்மண் ணுறுப்ப வாடி","நீர்க்கோலஞ் செய்து நிழல்விட்டுமிழ் மாலை மார்பன்","றார்க்கோல மான்றேர்த் தொகைமாமற் றொழுது சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"He puts off his battle dress, is bathed by women with breasts painted in gold, and, his garland gleaming, bows to his uncle, lord of the host of chariots, and speaks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2352","lb":"CC2352","ev":"Civakan bathes, takes off his armour and bows to his uncle to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2353":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2353,"t":["எண்கொண்ட ஞாட்பி னிரும்பெச்சிற் படுத்த மார்பர்","புண்கொண்டு போற்றிப் புறஞ்செய்கெனப் பொற்ப நோக்கிப்","பண்கொண்ட சொல்லார் தொழப்பாம்பணை யண்ணல் போல","மண்கொண்ட வேலா னடிதைவர வைகி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He asks that the men whose chests the iron fouled in the battle have their wounds tended and be cared for, and then, while sweet-voiced women stroke his feet, the conqueror lies at rest like the lord on the serpent bed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ali","padai","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2353","lb":"CC2353","ev":"Civakan charges his uncle with the care of the wounded and lies down to rest.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","கோவிந்தன்"],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2354":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2354,"t":["வாள்க ளாலே துகைப்புண்டு வரைபுண் கூர்ந்த போல்வேழ","நீள்கால் விசைய நேமித்தே ரிமைத்தார் நிலத்திற் காண்கலாத்","தாள்வல் புரவி பண்ணவிழ்த்த யானை யாவித் தாங்கன்ன","கோள்வா யெஃக மிடம்படுத்த கொழும்புண் மார்ப ரயாவுயிர்த்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Elephants slashed by swords like hills full of wounds, the swift chariots, the horses unsaddled and the tuskers unharnessed, and the warriors with gaping spear wounds in their chests all breathe out in relief.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2354","lb":"CC2354","ev":"The wounded army, its elephants and horses unharnessed, rests after the battle.","cl":[],"ag":["சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2355":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2355,"t":["கொழுவாய் விழுப்புண் குரைப்பொலியுங் கூந்தன் மகளிர் குழைசிதறி","யழுவா ரழுகைக் குரலொலியு மதிர்கண் முரசின் முழக்கொலியுங்","குழவாய்ச் சங்கின் குரலொலியுங் கொலைவல் யானைச் செவிப்புடையு","மெழுவார் யாழு மேத்தொலியு மிறைவன் கேளாத் துயிலேற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Amid the noise of deep wounds, the cries of weeping women scattering their ear-rings, the roll of drums, the conches, the flapping ears of elephants, the lutes and the songs of praise, the lord sleeps as if he heard none of it.","n":"The grief and the triumph of the night are heard together in one list, and the king's sleep is the still centre of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2355","lb":"CC2355","ev":"Civakan sleeps through the night's noise of wounds, mourning and celebration.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2356":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2356,"t":["தொடித்தோண் மகளி ரொருசாரார் துயரக் கடலு ளவர்நீந்த","வடிக்கண் மகளி ரொருசாரார் வரம்பி லின்பக் கடனீந்தப்","பொடித்தான் கதிரோன் றிரைநெற்றிப் புகழ்முப் பழநீர்ப் பளிங்களைஇக்","கடிப்பூ மாலை யவரேந்தக் கமழ்தா மரைக்கண் கழீஇயினான்."],"p":"","r":"While one group of bangled women swims in a sea of sorrow and another in a boundless sea of joy, the sun rises, and as women hold up fragrant garlands he washes his lotus eyes in clear water.","n":"Line 3's முப்பழநீர் is obscure, and which women grieve and which rejoice is left to the reader: the usurper's household against the victor's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","idumbai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2356","lb":"CC2356","ev":"At sunrise Civakan washes his face while some women grieve and others rejoice.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2357":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2357,"t":["முனைவற் றொழுது முடிதுளக்கி முகந்து செம்பொன் கொளவீசி","நினைய லாகா நெடுவாழ்க்கை வென்றிக் கோல விளக்காகப்","புனையப் பட்ட வஞ்சனத்தைப் புகழ வெழுதிப் புனைபூணான்","கனைவண் டார்க்கு மலங்கலுங் கலனு மேற்பத் தாங்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"He bows his crowned head to the Lord, flings out red gold by the handful, has collyrium put on as the lamp of the triumphal adornment for a long life beyond thought, and takes up the bee-loud garland and his ornaments.","n":"முனைவன் is the Jina in this work's usage; worship and a gift come first in the morning order, before the king puts on his ornaments.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","eegai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2357","lb":"CC2357","ev":"Civakan worships the Jina, gives gold and puts on collyrium, garland and ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2358":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2358,"t":["முறிந்த கோல முகிழ்முலையார் பரவ மொய்யார் மணிச்செப்பி","லுறைந்த வெண்பட் டுடுத்தொளிசேர் பஞ்ச வாசங் கவுட்கொண்டு","செறிந்த கமுநீர்ப் பூப்பிடித்துச் சேக்கை மரீஇய சிங்கம்போ","லறிந்தார் தமக்கு மநங்கனா யண்ணல் செம்மாந் திருந்தானே."],"p":"","r":"Praised by women, he puts on white silk kept in a gem casket, holds the five scents in his cheek, takes a kazhuneer flower in hand and sits like a lion on its couch, the noble one proud and a Kama even to the wise.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2358","lb":"CC2358","ev":"Civakan dresses in white silk and sits in state.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மங்கையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2359":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2359,"t":["வார்மீ தாடி வடஞ்சூடிப் பொற்பார்ந் திருந்த வனமுலையா","ரேர்மீ தாடிச் சாந்தெழுதி யிலங்கு முந்நீர் வலம்புரிபோற்","கார்மீ தாடிக் கலம்பொழியுங் கடகத் தடக்கைக் கழலோனைப்","போர்மீ தாடிப் புறங்கண்ட புலால்வேன் மன்னர் புடைசூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The kings with flesh-smelling spears, who had seen the enemy's backs in battle, gather round the hero whose large hand, bright with bracelets, pours out wealth like a cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2359","lb":"CC2359","ev":"The victorious kings gather round Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2360":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2360,"t":["தொல்லை நால்வகைத் தோழருந் தூமணி நெடுந்தேர்","மல்லற் றம்பியு மாமனு மதுவிரி கமழ்தார்ச்","செல்வன் றாதையுஞ் செழுநக ரொடுவள நாடும்","வல்லைத் தொக்கது வளங்கெழு கோயிலு ளொருங்கே."],"p":"","r":"His four kinds of old companions, his younger brother, his uncle, the hero's father, the rich city and the fertile country all gather quickly together in the palace.","n":"The father here (செல்வன் தாதை) can only be Kantukkatan, the foster father, since Saccandan is dead; the court that forms round the new king is his household of old, not the usurper's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","natpu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2360","lb":"CC2360","ev":"Civakan's companions, brother, uncle, father, the city and the country assemble in the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["தோழர்","நந்தட்டன்","கோவிந்தன்","கந்துக்கடன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:101"},"cc:2361":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2361,"t":["துளங்கு வெண்மதி யுகுந்தவெண் கதிர்தொகுத் ததுபோல்","விளங்கு வெள்ளியம் பெருமலை யொழியலந் தெழிலார்","வளங்கொண் மாநகர் மழகதிர் குழீஇயின போலக்","களங்கொண் டீண்டினர் கதிர்முடி விஞ்சையர் பொலிந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The Vidyadhara kings of the silver mountain leave it and gather in Civakan's city in such numbers that they look like the moon's scattered rays drawn together.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2361","lb":"CC2361","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara kings come down from Vellimalai and assemble in the city.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2362":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2362,"t":["எண்ண மென்னினி யெழின்முடி யணிவது துணிமின்","கண்ண னாரொடு கடிகையும் வருகென வரலும்","பண்ணி னார்முடி பழிச்சிய மணிபொனிற் குயிற்றி","யண்ண லாய்கதி ரலமவரப் புலமக ணகவே."],"p":"","r":"Someone urges that there is nothing more to consider, that he should resolve to put on the fine crown and that the lord and the assembly should come, while a crown set with gems is made ready on the god-like hero's head.","n":"Lines 1-2 are an exhortation in the imperative, but the verse does not name who speaks or who கண்ணனார் is; lines 3-4 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2362","lb":"CC2362","mm":1,"ev":"The assembled kings press Civakan to be crowned and a gem-set crown is prepared.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையர்",""],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2363":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2363,"t":["விரியு மாலையன் விளங்கொளி முடியினன் றுளங்கித்","திருவின் மால்வரைக் குலவிய தனையதோர் தேந்தா","ரகுலி போல்வதோ ராரமு மார்பிடைத் துயல","வெரியும் வார்குழை யிமையவ னொருவன்வந் திழிந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"A god with a flowing garland, a shining crown and a jewelled chest-ornament swaying descends, trembling like a golden hill.","n":"Which god is meant is not said; the Cintamani's gods who attend Civakan are not named here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2363","lb":"CC2363","mm":1,"ev":"A god comes down from the sky for the crowning.","cl":[],"ag":["இமையவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2364":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2364,"t":["கொம்மை யார்ந்தன கொடிபட வெழுதின குவிந்த","வம்மை யார்ந்தன வழகிய மணிவட முடைய","வெம்மை செய்வன விழுத்தகு முலைத்திட முடைய","பொம்மெ லோதியர் பொழிமின்னுக் கொடியென விழிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Heavenly women with full breasts, bright jewelled strings and soft hair come down like flashes of lightning from a rain-cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2364","lb":"CC2364","mm":1,"ev":"Celestial women descend in a procession.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2365":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2365,"t":["மையல் யானையின் படுமதங் கெடப்பகட் டரசன்","செய்த மும்மதம் போற்றிசை திசைதொறுங் கமழுந்","தெய்வ வாசத்துத் திருநகர் வாசங்கொண் டொழிய","வெய்யர் தோன்றினர் விசும்பிடைச் சிறப்பொடும் பொலிந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Fierce-looking beings arrive in the sky in splendour, bringing a scent of the divine city that drowns the scent of the rutting elephants of the king's city.","n":"வெய்யர் (the fierce or hot ones) is taken to be the gods of the sky; the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2365","lb":"CC2365","mm":1,"ev":"More heavenly beings arrive in the sky above the city.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2366":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2366,"t":["வெருவி மாநகர் மாந்தர்கள் வியந்துகை விதிர்ப்பப்","பருதி போல்வன பாற்கட னூற்றெட்டுக் குடத்தாற்","பொருவில் பூமழை பொன்மழை யொடுசொரிந் தாட்டி","யெரிபொ னீண்முடி கவித்தனன் பலித்திரற் றொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"The citizens, frightened, clap in wonder; with rains of flowers and gold he bathes Civakan with a hundred and eight pots of water from the milk sea and places the bright gold crown on him, worshipping.","n":"The subject of கவித்தனன் is not named; it is taken to be the god who came down in 2363. The crown is given by heavenly hands, not by a priest or an elder king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2366","lb":"CC2366","mm":1,"ev":"A god bathes Civakan with water from the milk sea and sets the golden crown on his head.","cl":[],"ag":["இமையவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2367":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2367,"t":["தேவ துந்துபி தேவர்கட் கோகையுய்த் துரைப்பா","னாவி யம்புகை யணிகிளர் சுண்ணமோ டெழுந்த","நாவி னேத்தின ரரம்பையர் நரம்பொலி யுளர்ந்த","காவன் மன்னருங் கடிகையுங் கடவது நிறைத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Divine drums sound to announce it to the gods, apsaras praise with the vina's sound and smoke and powder rise, and the guardian kings and the assembly do what is due.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2367","lb":"CC2367","mm":1,"ev":"The apsaras sing and the kings and assembly carry out the rites of the crowning.","cl":[],"ag":["அரம்பையர்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2368":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2368,"t":["திருவ மாமணிக் காம்பொடு திரள்வடந் திளைக்கு","முருவ வெண்மதி யிதுவென வெண்குடை யோங்கிப்","பரவை மாநில மளித்தது களிக்கயன் மழைக்கட்","பொருவில் பூமகட் புணர்ந்தன னிமையவ னெழுந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"A white umbrella rises like the full moon over a pole of gems, and the god who has risen to bless is joined with the matchless Earth-goddess, the king who now guards the sea-girt land.","n":"The verse is built from similes and its last line, where the god rises and joins பூமகள், is ambiguous: it may mean Civakan taking the land as his bride or the god's blessing on it. The ilampakam is named for பூமகள்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2368","lb":"CC2368","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan, crowned, is joined to the goddess of earth as ruler of the land.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2369":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2369,"t":["மின்னுங் கடற்றிரையின் மாமணிக்கை வெண்கவரி விரிந்து வீசப்","பொன்னங் குடைநிழற்றப் பொன்மயமா முழைக்கலங்கள் பொலிந்து தோன்ற","மன்னர் முடியிறைஞ்சி மாமணியங் கழலேந்தி யடியீ டேத்தச்","சின்ன மலர்க்கோதைத் தீஞ்சொலார் போற்றிசைப்பத் திருமால் போந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"With white fly-whisks waving, a golden umbrella shading him and kings bowing to take his feet, he goes forth in state, praised by women of sweet speech, like Vishnu.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","mannar","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2369","lb":"CC2369","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan goes in procession to the throne hall, with kings bowing to his feet.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2370":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2370,"t":["மந்தார மாமாலை மேற்றொடர்ந்து தழுவவராத் தாம மல்கி","யந்தோவென் றஞ்சிறைவண் டேக்கறவின் புகைபோய்க் கழுமி யாய்பொற்","செந்தா மரைமகளே யல்லதுபெண் சாராத திருவின் மிக்க","சிந்தா மணியேய்ந்த சித்திரமா மண்டபத்துச் செல்வன் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"He enters the picture-hall, set with the gem called cintamani, a hall that only the lotus goddess of fortune, and no other woman, has entered.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2370","lb":"CC2370","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan enters the painted hall of the throne.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2371":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2371,"t":["பைங்க ணுளையெருத்திற் பன்மணி வாளெயிற்றுப் பவள நாவிற்","சிங்கா சனத்தின்மேற் சிங்கம்போற் றேர்மன்னர் முடிகள் சூழ","மங்குன் மணிநிற வண்ணன்போல் வார்குழைகடிருவில் வீசச்","செங்கட் கமழ்பைந்தார்ச் செஞ்சுடர்போற் றேர்மன்ன னிருந்தா னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He sits on the lion throne, among kings in their crowns, like a lion, with his earrings flashing, like the sun shining from a dark cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","mannar","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2371","lb":"CC2371","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan takes his seat on the lion throne in the midst of the kings.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2372":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2372,"t":["வார்பிணி முரச நாண வானதிர் முழக்க மேய்ப்பத்","தார்பிணி தாம மார்பன் றம்பியை முகத்து ணோக்கி","யூர்பிணி கோட்டஞ் சீப்பித் துறாதவ னாண்ட நாட்டைப்","பார்பிணி கறையி னீங்கப் படாமுர சறைவி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Looking at his younger brother, Civakan bids him clear the afflictions of the towns and the districts and have the war-drum proclaim that the land his enemy ruled is rid of the stain of its burden.","n":"The enemy's land is described by பிணி (bond or disease) in each half-line; the drum is the royal drum used for orders. The pun is not fixed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2372","lb":"CC2372","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan orders his brother to relieve the towns of their afflictions and to proclaim it by drum.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2373":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2373,"t":["கடவுள ரிடனுங் காசில் கணிபெறு நிலனுங் காமர்","தடவளர் முழங்குஞ் செந்தீ நான்மறை யாளர் தங்க","ளிடவிய நிலத்தோ டெல்லா மிழந்தவர்க் கிரட்டி யாக","வுடனவை விடுமி னென்றா னொளிநிலா வுமிழும் பூணான்."],"p":"","r":"He commands that the lands of the temples, the lands of the learned who read the stars, and the fire-places and fields of the four-Veda Brahmins, taken from them, be given back twofold.","n":"The seizure is not told in these verses; the 'losers' are the gods' shrines, the astrologers and the Brahmins, so the order is also the work's picture of whom a Jain king should protect.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","arasu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2373","lb":"CC2373","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan orders land taken from temples and Brahmins to be restored at double.","cl":[["A king who finds lands taken from the gods' places and the learned must restore them twofold","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2374":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2374,"t":["என்றலுந் தொழுது சென்னி நிலனுறீஇ யெழுந்து போகி","வென்றதிர் முரசம் யானை வீங்கெருத் தேற்றிப் பைம்பொற்","குன்றுகண் டனைய கோலக் கொடிநெடு மாட மூதூர்ச்","சென்றிசை முழங்கச் செல்வன் றிருமுர சறைவிக் கின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing him, the brother bows, touches the ground with his head, has the victorious drum set on a tall elephant and, through the old city of flagged mansions, has the drum of the lord proclaimed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2374","lb":"CC2374","mm":1,"ev":"Nandattan has the royal drum carried on an elephant through the city to proclaim the orders.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2375":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2375,"t":["ஒன்றுடைப் பதிளை யாண்டைக் குறுகட னிறைவன் விட்டா","னின்றுளீ ருலகத் தென்று முடனுளீ ராகி வாழ்மின்","பொன்றுக பசியு நோயும் பொருந்தலில் பகையு மென்ன","மன்றல மறுகு தோறு மணிமுர சார்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The drum announces that the king has remitted the dues for sixteen years and that those who are alive should live together, and that hunger, disease and enmity should perish.","n":"பதிளை யாண்டை is read as sixteen years; the number is not certain, and the reading of குறுகடன் as 'dues' is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","eegai","pasi","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2375","lb":"CC2375","mm":1,"ev":"The drum proclaims remission of dues and the end of hunger, disease and enmity.","cl":[["A king's reign is to be marked by remission of dues and the ending of hunger, disease and enmity","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2376":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2376,"t":["நோக்கொழிந் தொடுங்கி னீர்க்கு நோய்கொளச் சாம்பி னீர்க்கும்","பூக்குழன் மகளிர்க் கொண்டான் புறக்கணித் திடப்பட் டீர்க்கும்","கோத்தரு நிதியம் வாழக் கொற்றவ னகரோ டென்ன","வீக்குவார் முரசங் கொட்டி விழுநக ரறைவித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The drum goes on to those blind, wasted with disease, women left by the husbands who married them, and others cast out, that the stored wealth will be given so that they may live, and the king has it sounded through the city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","eegai","ali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2376","lb":"CC2376","mm":1,"ev":"The king has the drum proclaim that his wealth will support the blind, the sick, abandoned wives and the cast out.","cl":[["A king owes the helpless, the sick and the abandoned a share of the treasury","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2377":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2377,"t":["திருமக னருளப் பெற்றுத் திருநிலத் துறையு மாந்த","ரொருவனுக் கொருத்தி போல வுளமகிழ்ந் தொளியின் வைகிப்","பருவரு பகையு நோயும் பசியுங்கெட் டொழிய விப்பாற்","பெருவிறல் வேந்தர் வேந்தற் குற்றது பேச லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says that, by the grace of Civakan the people live in gladness like one wife to one man, with enemies, disease and hunger gone, and that he will now tell what befell the king of kings.","n":"The last line turns from the praise of Civakan's rule to the next ilampakam; the story it opens ends in the king's renunciation, as the patikam (verse 27) says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","pasi","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2377","lb":"CC2377","mm":1,"ev":"The people live at ease and the narrator moves on to what happened to the king of kings.","cl":[],"ag":["மாந்தர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:102"},"cc:2378":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2378,"t":["அலங்க லேந்திய குங்கும வருவரை மார்பன்","கலந்த காரிகை யவர்களைத் தருகென வருள","விலங்கு மாலைவெள் ளருவிய வெழில்வரை மணந்த","புலம்பு நீள்சுரம் போய்க்கொணர்ந் தருளொடுங் கொடுத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The hero with the saffron-smeared mountain chest bids that the women who were joined with him be brought, and they are fetched through the lonely wasteland and handed over with grace.","n":"The verse names neither the hero nor the women; they are the ones he asked for in the preceding verses, which this chapter's first verse cannot show.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2378","lb":"CC2378","mm":1,"ev":"At the hero's word the women are brought across the desert and given back to him.","cl":[],"ag":["காரிகையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2379":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2379,"t":["மோடு கொண்ணிலா முளைத்தெழு பருதிகண் டறியாப்","பாடு வண்டொடு பறவையு நடுக்குறுங் காப்பின்","மாட மாமணிச் சிவிகையின் மயிலென விழிந்தார்","வீடு கண்டவர் போன்றுமின் னிடுகொடி யனையார்."],"p":"","r":"The women, like peacocks, step down from the guarded jewelled palanquin, lightning-slender, as if they had seen release, in a place where bees and birds that never saw the sun or the moon tremble.","n":"The simile of those who have seen release (வீடு கண்டவர்) is for their relief and not a doctrinal statement.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2379","lb":"CC2379","mm":1,"ev":"The women alight from the palanquin.","cl":[],"ag":["காரிகையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2380":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2380,"t":["அன்று சூடிய மாலைய ராடிய சாந்தர்","பொன்றி வாடிய மேனியர் பொன்னிறை சுருங்கார்","சென்று காதலன் றிருவிரி மரைமல ரடிமே","லொன்றி வீழ்ந்தனர் குவளைக்க ணுவகைமுத் துகவே."],"p":"","r":"Withered in body, in the garlands and sandal of the day before, yet with no loss of gold ornament, they fall at their beloved's lotus feet while pearls of joy drop from their blue-lily eyes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2380","lb":"CC2380","mm":1,"ev":"The women fall at their lord's feet and weep with joy.","cl":[],"ag":["காரிகையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2381":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2381,"t":["இலங்கு பூண்வரை மார்புற வெடுத்தவன் முயங்க","மலங்கி வாட்கண்கள் வருபனி சுமந்துடன் வெருவிக்","கலங்கு நீரிடைக் கலக்குறு கருங்கய லிணைபோற்","புலம்பி யோடின செவியுற நெடியன பொலிந்தே."],"p":"","r":"As he lifts and embraces them against his mountain-like chest, their long eyes, bearing tears and trembling, dart like a pair of carp in stirred water.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2381","lb":"CC2381","mm":1,"ev":"The hero embraces the women, who weep and tremble.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2382":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2382,"t":["வேனல் வாய்ப்பட்டு விரிமுகை தளிரொடு கரிந்த","கானக் கார்முல்லை கார்மழைக் கெதிர்ந்தன போல","மான மங்கையர் வாட்டமும் பரிவுந்தங் கணவன்","றேனெய் மார்பகந் தீண்டலுந் தீர்ந்தொளி சிறந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Like forest jasmine buds scorched in the summer and then meeting the rain clouds, the proud women lose their pining and grief at the touch of their husband's honeyed chest, and shine.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2382","lb":"CC2382","mm":1,"ev":"The women's grief ends in their husband's embrace.","cl":[],"ag":["காரிகையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2383":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2383,"t":["சேலுண் கண்ணியர் சிலம்பொடு திலகமுந் திருத்தி","மாலை நல்லன மதுக்கமழ் தகையன மிலைச்சிக்","கோல மென்முலைக் குங்கும மிடுகொடி யெழுதிச்","சோலை வேய்மரு டோண்முத்துந் தொழுதக வணிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The fish-eyed women set right their anklets and brow marks, wear fragrant garlands, paint the saffron creeper on their breasts and put on bamboo-bright shoulder pearls.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2383","lb":"CC2383","mm":1,"ev":"The women adorn themselves.","cl":[],"ag":["காரிகையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2384":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2384,"t":["நஞ்சு மேய்ந்திளங் களிக்கயன் மதர்ப்பன போல","வஞ்சி வாட்கண்கண் மதர்த்தன வலர்ந்துடன் பிறழப்","பஞ்சு சூழ்மணி மேகலை பரிந்தவை சொரிய","வஞ்சி நுண்ணிடை கவின்பெற வைகினன் மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Their wide eyes, bright like fish that have fed on poison, roll and glance; as the jewelled girdles slip and spill, the slender-waisted ones shine, and he stayed with them.","n":"மாதோ at the end ends the scene; he who stayed is the hero, never named here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2384","lb":"CC2384","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan passes the time with the women.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2385":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2385,"t":["அரிபொற் கிண்கிணி யணிகிளர் சிலம்பொடு சிலம்புந்","திருவச் சீறடிச் செழுமலர்க் கொழுங்கயன் மழைக்க","ணுருவ நுண்ணிடை யொளிமணி வருமுலை யுருவா","ரெரிபொன் மேகலை யிலக்கணை கடிவினை நொடிவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The poet turns from these women to a new bride, Ilakkanai, whose gold-ringed small feet, narrow waist and bright jewelled girdle he describes, and says he will now tell her wedding rite.","n":"The line 'we shall tell' makes the poet speak in the first person plural, as the poets of the Cintamani do when opening a new tale.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vathuvai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2385","lb":"CC2385","mm":1,"ev":"The poet announces the story of Ilakkanai's wedding.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2386":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2386,"t":["ஆழி மால்கட லகன்பெருங் கேள்விக டுறைபோ","யூழி னன்றியு முறுவினை யோரையின் முடிப்பான்","சூழி யானையுந் துளங்குபொற் சிவிகையு முடையான்","வேழ வேந்தற்கு விழுப்பெருங் கணிவிரித் துரைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"A learned king, who finishes coming deeds in their due hour even apart from fate, owner of crowned elephants and a golden litter, has a great astrologer set out the days for the elephant-lord.","n":"Line 2 says he brings about a coming deed in its hour even apart from fate (ஊழின் அன்றியும்); the text does not say whether this places hour above karma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2386","lb":"CC2386","mm":1,"ev":"The astrologer lays out the auspicious days for the king.","cl":[["an astrologer can bring a coming event to pass at its fixed hour even when fate does not decree it","general","reported",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["கணி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2387":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2387,"t":["ஓங்கு கொற்றவற் கோதிய வுயர்பெரு நாளால்","வீங்கு வெள்ளியங் குன்றென விளங்கொளி யுடைய","தேங்கொண் மாலையுந் திலகமு மணிந்ததிண் குணத்த","பாங்கிற் பண்ணின நூற்றெட்டுப் படுமதக் களிறே."],"p":"","r":"On the great day the astrologer named for the king, a hundred and eight rutting elephants are made ready, wearing garlands and brow marks, white as silver hills.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2387","lb":"CC2387","mm":1,"ev":"A hundred and eight elephants are dressed for the day.","cl":[],"ag":["யாவரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2388":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2388,"t":["விளங்கு வெண்டுகி லுடுத்துவெண் சாந்துமெய் பூசித்","துளங்கு மஞ்சிகை துளைச்சிறு காதினுட் டுளங்க","வளங்கொண் மாலைகள் சூடிமுத் தணிந்துவண் முரசங்","களங்கொள் வேழத்தி னேற்றினர் கடிமுர சறைவான்."],"p":"","r":"They dress the proclaimers in white cloth and sandal, hang ear-rings and flowers and pearls on them and seat them on a battle elephant so that they can beat the wedding drum.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2388","lb":"CC2388","mm":1,"ev":"Drummers are set on an elephant to proclaim the wedding.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவான்","யாவரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2389":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2389,"t":["கேண்மின் கேண்மின்கள் யாவரு மினியன கேண்மின்","பூண்மி னித்தில மணிவடம் பூசுமின் சாந்தம்","வாண்மி னுண்ணிடை வருந்தினுஞ் சூட்டணிந் தழகா","ராண மாகிய வருவிலை வண்ணப்பட் டுடுமின்."],"p":"","r":"The proclaimer cries: listen, wear pearl strings and sandal, bear the flower crest though the waist ache, put on fine cloth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2389","lb":"CC2389","mm":1,"ev":"The drum announces that all must dress in finery for the wedding.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவான்","யாவரும்"],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2390":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2390,"t":["பிள்ளை வெண்பிறைச் சிறுநுதற் பெரும்பிட்ட மணிமி","னுள்ள மேனியு மொளிர்மணிக் கலங்களிற் புனைமின்","வள்ளல் வாய்மொழி யான்படு பாலமிர் தல்லா","லுள்ள மேவினும் பிறவுணப் பெறீரெழு நாளும்."],"p":"","r":"Adorn the children and the whole body with jewels, he says; for seven days none may eat anything but the milk-nectar made by the generous lord's word, whatever the heart wants.","n":"The first half of line 1, and what பிட்டம் means, turn on a rare word; the sense of the command is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2390","lb":"CC2390","mm":1,"ev":"The drum commands feasting only on milk-nectar for seven days.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவான்","யாவரும்"],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2391":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2391,"t":["வாழை மல்கிய மணிக்குலைக் கமுகொடு நடுமின்","றாழ நாற்றுமின் றாமங் ளகிற்குடம் பரப்பி","யாழின் பாடலு மாடலு மாங்குதோ றியற்றிப்","போழு மால்விசும் பெனப்பல பொலங்கொடி யெடுமின்."],"p":"","r":"Plant plantain and fruit-laden areca, hang garlands, spread pots of aloe smoke, make lute-song and dance in every place, raise many golden flags like a cloven sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2391","lb":"CC2391","mm":1,"ev":"The drum orders the city to be decked.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முரசறைவான்","யாவரும்"],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2392":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2392,"t":["மாலை வாண்முடி மன்னவன் மணவினை யெழுநாட்","சீல மில்லன சினக்களி றகற்றுகென் றணிந்த","கோல மார்முர சிடியுமிழ் தழங்கென முழங்க","நீல மாக்கட னெடுநகர் வாழ்கென வறைந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"For the seven days of the king's wedding the ill-behaved rutting elephants are to be kept away, and the drum thundered out its order that the great city by the dark sea live.","n":"The king (மன்னவன்) whose wedding it is is not named; it is the king of the earlier verses, not necessarily the hero.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2392","lb":"CC2392","mm":1,"ev":"The drum is beaten and the city is told to keep the wedding days.","cl":[],"ag":["முரசறைவான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2393":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2393,"t":["முரச மார்ந்தபின் மூவிரு நாள்கள்போய்","விரைவொ டெங்கணும் வெள்வளை விம்மின","புரையில் பொன்மணி யாழ்குழ றண்ணுமை","வரவ வானி னதிர்ந்த வணிமுழா."],"p":"","r":"After the drum six days passed, and everywhere white bangles swelled; lutes, flutes, drums and the thannumai sounded like thunder in the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2393","lb":"CC2393","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2394":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2394,"t":["விண்வி ளக்குவ போல்விரி பூந்துகள்","கண்வி ளக்கிக் கலந்தவெண் சாந்தினான்","மண்வி ளக்கி மலர்ப்பலி சிந்தினார்","பண்வி ளக்கிய பைங்கிளி யின்சொலார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with parrot-sweet speech scattered flower dust like sky-lamps, sandal that lit the eyes, and flowers for the bali, brightening the earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2394","lb":"CC2394","mm":1,"ev":"Women sprinkle flowers and sandal for the offering.","cl":[],"ag":["யாவரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2395":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2395,"t":["ஆய்ந்த மோட்டின வான்படு பாலுலை","போந்து பொங்கிய வாவியி னாற்பொலிந்","தேந்து மாடங்க டாமிழி னென்பன","பூந்து கில்புறம் போர்த்தன போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The steam of the milk boiling in pots rose in the mansions like thin silk shrouding them from the outside.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2395","lb":"CC2395","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2396":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2396,"t":["திருவி னல்லவர் செம்மலர்ச் சீறடி","பரவி யூட்டிய பஞ்சரத் தக்களி","விரவி மீநிலஞ் சேர்ந்தொளி பூத்துராய்க்","குருதி வானிலங் கொண்டது போன்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The red lac paint with which fair women, bowing, anointed the soles of the small feet spread over the ground like red sky at dusk.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2396","lb":"CC2396","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2397":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2397,"t":["பால்வெண் டிங்கண் மணிக்கை படுத்தவை","போலு மாடியி னோக்கிப் பொலங்கலக்","கோலஞ் செய்பவர் கோல வெறிப்பினான்","மாலை வண்டின மாலைக்கண் கொண்டவே."],"p":"","r":"The dressers' lamps, looking into the mirror, held the bees at dusk in the gaze of the garlands as moons held in a hand.","n":"The simile of line 1 is hard to follow; the verse's own subject is the mirrors in which dressers see themselves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2397","lb":"CC2397","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2398":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2398,"t":["போக மாமழை போழ்ந்து புதத்தொறு","மாக மேந்துவ பொன்மணித் தோரண","மாக நாற்றின தாம மணிக்குட","மேக மாநகர் வீதி நிரைத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Golden gates rose through the clouds, garlands hung from the banners, and rows of pots lined the streets of the cloud-high city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2398","lb":"CC2398","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2399":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2399,"t":["ஆடன் மங்கையர் கிண்கிணி யார்ப்பொலி","பாட லின்னொலி பண்ணமை யாழொலி","மோடு கொண்முழ விண்முழக் கீண்டிய","மாட மாநகர் மாக்கட லொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Dancers' anklets, songs, lutes and drums rang together so that the mansion city was like a great sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2399","lb":"CC2399","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2400":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2400,"t":["சுந்த ரத்துகள் பூந்துகள் பொற்றுக","ளந்த ரத்தெழு மின்புகை யாலரோ","விந்தி ரன்னகர் சாறயர்ந் திவ்வழி","வந்தி ருந்தது போன்மலி வுற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The dust of flowers and sandal and the smoke of incense seemed as if Indra's city had come to hold a festival here.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2400","lb":"CC2400","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2401":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2401,"t":["நிரந்து கன்னலு நெய்யு நீந்தப்பெய்","திரந்து பாலமிர் தெங்கு மூட்டுவார்","பரந்து பூந்துகில் பன்ம ணிக்கலஞ்","சுரந்து கொள்கெனச் சுமக்க நல்குவார்."],"p":"","r":"They poured sugar and ghee in rivers, gave milk-nectar to all who begged, and bade them take away fine cloth and jewels, even carrying them for them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2401","lb":"CC2401","mm":1,"ev":"The people feed and give to all who come.","cl":[],"ag":["யாவரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2402":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2402,"t":["வருக்கை யின்பழம் வாழை யின்கனி","திருக்கொண் மாங்கனி தெளித்த தேறலைக்","கருப்புச் சாற்றொடு கலந்து கைசெய்து","புரித்த தெங்கிள நீரும்பூ ரிப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"They filled coconuts with jackfruit, plantain and mango fruit, pressed juices and sugarcane juice, and gave them to drink.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2402","lb":"CC2402","mm":1,"ev":"The people give fruit drinks to all.","cl":[],"ag":["யாவரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:103"},"cc:2403":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2403,"t":["கூந்த லேந்திய கமுகங் காய்க்குலை","யாய்ந்த மெல்லிலை பளித மாதியா","மாந்தர் கொள்ளைகொண் டுண்ண மாநில","மேந்த லாம்படித் தன்றி யீட்டுவார்."],"p":"","r":"The city heaps up areca bunches, tender betel leaves, camphor and the rest in such quantity that the great earth can scarcely bear them, for people to take freely and eat.","n":"The lavishness is the point: the goods are laid out as plunder (கொள்ளை) to be taken, so the wedding feast is open to all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2403","lb":"CC2403","mm":1,"ev":"The townspeople pile up areca, betel and camphor for all to take.","cl":[],"ag":["என்மரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2404":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2404,"t":["தூம மார்ந்தன துப்பு ரவ்வுக","ளேம மாயின வேந்தி நிற்றலா","னாம நன்னகர் நன்பொற் கற்பகங்","காம வல்லியுங் களங்கொண் டிட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"Heaps of incense-smoking luxuries, held up as good fortune, make the fine city look as if the golden wish-trees and the wish-creeper had taken it as their ground.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2404","lb":"CC2404","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2405":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2405,"t":["வழுவின் மாந்தரு மாவு மல்கிய","தொழுதி தன்னையான் சுமக்க லேனேனா","முழுது மண்மகண் முற்றும் வாய்திறந்","தழுதிட் டாணெயும் பாலு மாகவே."],"p":"","r":"The earth-maiden, unable to bear the crowd of people and cattle, is imagined opening her whole mouth and weeping, her tears turning into ghee and milk.","n":"A conceit of plenty: the earth's weight of people and herds becomes a flood of ghee and milk. This is the poet's figure, not a doctrinal claim about the earth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2405","lb":"CC2405","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2406":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2406,"t":["கொடியெழுந் தலமருங் கோயில் வாயில்கண்","மடலெழுந் தலமருங் கமுகும் வாழையு","மடியிருந் துகிலுடை மாக்க ணாடியும்","புடைதிரள் பூரண குடமும் பூத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"At the palace gates the banners rise, areca and plantain put out their spathes, and mirrors, silks and full auspicious pots stand in bloom.","n":"The auspicious objects (flags, pots full of water, mirrors, areca and plantain) are named as the marks of a wedding house; the verse gives no event.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2406","lb":"CC2406","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2407":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2407,"t":["கடிமலர் மங்கையர் காய்பொற் கிண்கிணி","யுடைமணி பொற்சிலம் பொலிக்குங் கோயிலுட்","குடைநிழன் மன்னர்தங் கோதைத் தாதுவேய்க்","தடிநிலம் பெறாததோர் செல்வ மார்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"In the palace where women's anklets and bells ring, a wealth is said to have filled the place that the umbrella-shaded kings had not found on earth.","n":"The last two lines (கோதைத் தாதுவேய்த் தடிநிலம் பெறாததோர் செல்வம்) are hard to construe; the sense of 'wealth the kings did not find on earth' is the likeliest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2407","lb":"CC2407","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2408":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2408,"t":["துளங்குபொற் குழைகளுந் தோடுஞ் சுண்ணமுங்","கிளர்ந்தகில் சாந்துபூக் கமழ்ந்து கேழ்கிள","ரிளங்கதி ரெறிமணிப் பூணு மாரமும்","விளங்கிமே லுலகினை வெறுப்பித் திட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"Ear-ornaments, studs, powder, aloe paste and flowers, with gem ornaments and necklaces throwing young rays, shine so that they make the upper world seem to be spurned.","n":"The hyperbole (the heaven-dwellers' world made loathsome beside this display) is the epic's habitual way of praising a festival.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2408","lb":"CC2408","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2409":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2409,"t":["விரிந்துவான் பூத்தென விதானித் தாய்கதி","ரருங்கலப் பொடியினா லாபொற் பூமகண்","மருங்குல்போற் குயிற்றிய நகரின் மங்கலப்","பெருந்தவி சடுத்தனர் பிணையன் மாலையார்."],"p":"","r":"The city, built like the waist of the goddess of the golden lotus and roofed like a sky blossomed with gem-dust, is made ready as the garlanded people set the great auspicious seat.","n":"தவிசு is read as the seat on which the rite is done; the verse does not say who will sit on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2409","lb":"CC2409","mm":1,"ev":"The garlanded people set up the great auspicious seat.","cl":[],"ag":["என்மரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2410":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2410,"t":["நலங்கிளர் காணமு மணியு நன்பொனும்","வலம்புரி முத்தமுங் குவித்த மங்கல","மிலங்கின மணிவிளக் கெழுந்த தீம்புகை","கலந்தவா யிரத்தெண்மர் கவரி யேந்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"Gold, gems and conch pearls are heaped as auspicious offerings, lamps burn and sweet smoke rises, and one thousand and eight attendants raise fly-whisks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2410","lb":"CC2410","mm":1,"ev":"Attendants raise fly-whisks among the heaped auspicious wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயிரத்தெண்மர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2411":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2411,"t":["மங்கலப் பெருங்கணி வகுத்த வோரையான்","மங்கல மன்னவன் வாழ்த்த வேறலு","மங்கல வச்சுதந் தெளித்து வாய்மொழி","மங்கலக் கருவிமுன் னுறுத்தி வாழ்த்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"After the great astrologer fixes the hour, the auspicious king mounts, rice grains are sprinkled, auspicious instruments sound before him, and he is blessed with true words.","n":"Line 2's வேறலு is not clear (perhaps ஏறலு, 'on mounting'); the whole verse is read as the beginning of the rite at the hour the astrologer set.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2411","lb":"CC2411","mm":1,"ev":"The astrologer's hour is fixed, and the king is blessed with rice grains and music.","cl":[],"ag":["கணி","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2412":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2412,"t":["முழங்கின வின்னிய மொய்த்த தேத்தொலி","கொழுங்கயற் கண்ணினார் கொண்டு பொன்னக","லிழிந்தனர் திருமயி ரேற்ப நீரதி","னிழன்றன சாமரை நிரைசங் கார்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"As auspicious instruments sound with a sweet roar, women with plump carp-like eyes take golden vessels and go down to the bath for the sacred hair, while rows of fly-whisks gleam and conchs boom.","n":"திருமயிர் is taken as the hair of the one to be bathed; whether it is the bridegroom's or the bride's is not said in this verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","aasaaram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2412","lb":"CC2412","mm":1,"ev":"Carp-eyed women go down with golden vessels to the bath as instruments sound.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2413":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2413,"t":["பாற்கடன் முளைத்ததோர் பவளப் பூங்கொடி","போற்சுடர்ந் திலங்கொளிப் பொன்செய் கோதையை","நாட்கடி மயிர்வினை நன்பொற் றாமரைப்","பூக்கடி கோயிலாள் புலம்ப வாக்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"The gold-garlanded woman, bright like a coral creeper sprung from the milk-sea, is given her day's fragrant hair-dressing so that the goddess who dwells in the golden lotus is left to lament.","n":"The woman is taken to be Lakshanai, the bride of the ilampakam's title, though she is not named; the closing figure says her beauty leaves the lotus-goddess grieving.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2413","lb":"CC2413","mm":1,"ev":"Attendants dress the bride's hair.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2414":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2414,"t":["விரைத்தலை மாலை சூட்டி மின்னனா ரங்கை சேப்ப","வரைத்தசாந் தணிந்த கோட்ட வாயிரத் தெட்டு வேழ","நிரைத்தன மண்ணு நீர்க்கு முரசொடு முழவம் விம்ம","வரைத்தலைத் துவலை போன்று மதநில நனைப்ப வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Garlands are set on, the women's palms redden, and one thousand and eight elephants, their tusks smeared with ground sandal, are lined up for the bathing water, drums booming and rut-water wetting the ground like mountain spray.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2414","lb":"CC2414","mm":1,"ev":"A thousand and eight sandal-marked elephants are drawn up for the bathing water amid drums.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2415":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2415,"t":["கான்முகம் புதைத்த தெண்ணீர் கவர்ந்துபொற் குடங்க ளார்த்தி","யூன்முகம் புதைத்த வேற்கண் ணவர்களிற் றுச்சி யேற்றி","வான்முகம் புதைத்த பன்மீன் மதியென மருண்டு நோக்கத்","தேன்முகம் புதைத்த மாலைக் குடைநிழற் றிருவிற் றந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Spear-eyed women raise golden pots of clear water onto the elephants' heads while the people stare as at a moon among confused stars, and fortune is given under the garlanded umbrella's shade.","n":"The last line (குடைநிழற் றிருவிற் றந்தார்) can be read as the giving of fortune under the umbrella or as a naming of the pots; I take the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2415","lb":"CC2415","mm":1,"ev":"Women lift the golden pots of bathing water onto the elephants' heads.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2416":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2416,"t":["இழைத்தபொன் னகரின் வெள்ளி யிடுமணை மன்ன ரேத்தக்","குழைப்பொலிந் திலங்கு காதிற் கொற்றவ னிருந்த பின்றை","மழைக்கலின் றெழுந்த வார்கொண் மணிநிற வறுகை நெய்தோய்த்","தெழிற்குழை திருவில் வீச மகளிர்நெய் யேற்று கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"When the king, ear-rings flashing, has sat on the silver seat while kings praise him, women dip durva grass in ghee and anoint him with it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2416","lb":"CC2416","mm":1,"ev":"The king sits on a silver seat and women anoint him with ghee on durva.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மகளிர்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2417":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2417,"t":["மின்னுமிழ் வைரக் கோட்டு விளங்கொளி யிமய மென்னும்","பொன்னெடுங் குன்றம் போலப் பூமிமே னிலவி வைய","நின்னடி நிழலின் வைக நேமியஞ் செல்வ னாகி","மன்னுவாய் திருவோ டென்று வாழ்த்திநெய் யேற்றி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Women bless him as they pour ghee: may he shine on earth like the Himalaya of diamond peaks, may the world lie in the shade of his feet, and may he be lord of the wheel and abide with Fortune.","n":"The blessing wishes sovereignty over the whole earth (நேமியஞ் செல்வன்), a wish, not a fact in the story; it is spoken to the bridegroom, not to a king already crowned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2417","lb":"CC2417","mm":1,"ev":"Women bless the king as they anoint him with ghee.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2418":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2418,"t":["நீடு நீர்மணி நீரு மல்லவு","மாடு நீரன வத்து மண்களு","மூடு மின்னனா ருரிஞ்சி யாட்டினார்","கூடி யின்னியங் குழுமி யார்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The women like lightning rub and bathe him with water of gems and with perfumed waters, and instruments sound together.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2418","lb":"CC2418","mm":1,"ev":"Women rub and bathe the king amid music.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2419":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2419,"t":["திருவ மன்னவன் சென்னித் தேர்மன்னர்","பொருவெண் பொற்குட முமிழும் பொங்குநீர்","பருதி தன்னொளி மறையப் பான்மதி","சொரியுந் தீங்கதிர்த் தோற்ற மொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The water the chariot-kings pour from silver pots over the fortunate king's head looks like the moon's sweet rays pouring down when the sun's light is hidden.","n":"The kings serve the bridegroom with the water of the bath, so the vassal kings of the earlier chapters now do him honour in the rite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","mannar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2419","lb":"CC2419","mm":1,"ev":"The chariot-kings pour water from silver pots over the king's head.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2420":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2420,"t":["துளங்கு மாமணித் தூண்க ணான்கினால்","விளங்கு வெள்ளிவேய்ந் தாய்ந்த மாலைசூழ்","வளங்கொண் மாமணிக் கூடஞ் சேர்த்தினா","ரிளங்க திர்கொலோ விருந்த தென்னவே."],"p":"","r":"A pavilion of gem is raised on four shining gem pillars, silver-roofed and ringed with garlands, so that it looks like the young sun resting there.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2420","lb":"CC2420","mm":1,"ev":"Attendants set up a gem pavilion on four pillars with a silver roof.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2421":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2421,"t":["ஆய்ந்த பானிற மாய்பொற் கம்பலம்","வேய்ந்த பொங்கணை வெண்பொற் கட்டின்மே","னீந்து நித்தில விதான நீழலாற்","கேந்தி னாரணி யேந்து நீர்மையாற்."],"p":"","r":"A rug of golden milk-white wool is spread on the white-gold dais under a pearl canopy, and the seat is made ready for him.","n":"Line 4 (கேந்தினார் அணி ஏந்தும் நீர்மையால்) is unclear in the printed text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2421","lb":"CC2421","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2422":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2422,"t":["ஈரங் கொன்றபி னிருண்ம ணிச்சுடர்","நீர வாய்நிழ லுமிழுங் குஞ்சியை","யார கிற்புகை வெறியி னாலமைத்","தேர்ப டச்செய்தா ரெழுதிற் றென்னவே."],"p":"","r":"When the damp has been dried, his hair, from which the dark gem-light spreads shadow, is scented with aloe smoke until it looks painted.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2422","lb":"CC2422","mm":1,"ev":"Attendants dry and scent his hair with aloe smoke.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2423":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2423,"t":["ஈடில் சந்தன மேந்து தாமரைத்","தோடின் பயில்வினாற் பூசித் தூமலர்","வீடு பெற்றன வின்றொ டென்னவே","சூடி னானரோ சுரும்புண் கண்ணியே."],"p":"","r":"He anoints himself with matchless sandal and puts on a bee-loud garland whose pure flowers seem to have won release that day.","n":"வீடு பெற்றன is a figure for flowers that are lucky to be worn; it makes no claim about release and is not a doctrinal use of veedu.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2423","lb":"CC2423","mm":1,"ev":"He anoints himself with sandal and puts on a garland.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2424":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2424,"t":["மற்ப கம்மலர்ந் தகன்ற மார்பின்மேல்","விற்ப கக்குலா யாரம் வில்லிடக்","கற்ப கம்மலர்ந் தகன்ற தோவெனப்","பொற்ப கப்பொலங் கலங்க டாங்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"On his broad chest the necklace casts a rainbow, as if the wishing tree had blossomed, and he wears gold ornaments.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2424","lb":"CC2424","mm":1,"ev":"He puts on his gold ornaments and necklace.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2425":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2425,"t":["உருவ மார்ந்தன வுரோமப் பட்டுடுத்","தெரியும் வார்குழை சுடர விந்திர","திருவி லன்னதார் திளைப்பத் தேங்குழ","லரிபெய் கண்ணியர்க் கநங்க னாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"In hair-silk and with flashing ear-rings and a garland like Indra's bow, he becomes Kama himself to the women of fragrant hair.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2425","lb":"CC2425","mm":1,"ev":"He dresses in silk and ornaments and looks like Kama to the women.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2426":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2426,"t":["தாவி றாழ்வடந் தயங்க நீருறீஇ","மேலி யச்சுதந் தெளித்த பின்விரைந்","தாவி யம்புகை சுழற்றி யாடியும்","வீவில் வெஞ்சுடர் விளக்குங் காட்டினார்."],"p":"","r":"After the hanging garlands shine and water and rice grains are sprinkled, they wave incense smoke and show the unwavering fierce lamps.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasaaram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2426","lb":"CC2426","mm":1,"ev":"Attendants sprinkle rice and water and wave incense and lamps.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2427":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2427,"t":["உவரி மாக்கட லொல்லென் வெண்டிரை","யிவரி யெழுவபோன் றிலங்கு வெண்மயிர்க்","கவரித் தொகைபல வீசுங் காவல்","ரிவரித் தொகையென்ப தின்றி யாயினார்."],"p":"","r":"The guards wave so many white whisks, like the sea's foam-white waves, that the count of them is lost.","n":"The last line (இவரித் தொகை என்பது இன்றி ஆயினார்) is taken to mean that the whisks passed counting.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2427","lb":"CC2427","mm":1,"ev":"Guards wave countless white fly-whisks.","cl":[],"ag":["காவலர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:104"},"cc:2428":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2428,"t":["அறுகு வெண்மல ரளாய வாசநீ","ரிறைவன் சேவடி கழுவி யேந்திய","மறுவின் மங்கலங் காட்டி னார்மணக்","குறைவில் கைவினைக் கோல மார்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Water scented with white durva flowers washes the lord's red feet, the faultless auspicious light that was lifted is shown, and the wedding fills with finely worked decoration.","n":"இறைவன் may be the king giving the bride or the Jina; the verse does not say, so who is honoured is left open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","ilvaazhkkai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2428","lb":"CC2428","mm":1,"ev":"The rite of washing the lord's feet and showing the auspicious light opens the wedding.","cl":[],"ag":["கதிர்முலையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2429":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2429,"t":["ஊனிமிர் கதிர்வெள்வே லுறைகழித் தனபோலுந்","தேனிமிர் குவளைக்கட் டிருமக் ளனையாளைப்","பானிமிர் கதிர்வெள்ளி மணைமிசைப் பலர்வாழ்த்தி","வானிமிர் கொடியன்னார் மணியணை மிசைவைத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Many bless the girl, whose lily eyes are like bright swords drawn from the sheath and who is like the goddess of wealth, and set her on a silver stool and then on a jewelled couch.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vazhipaadu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2429","lb":"CC2429","mm":1,"ev":"Many bless the bride and seat her on a silver stool, then a jewelled couch.","cl":[],"ag":["பலர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2430":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2430,"t":["வரைவிளை வளர்பொன்னே வலம்புரி யொருமணியே","திரைவிளை யமிர்தம்மே திருவிழை யெனவேத்தி","வரிவளை முழவிம்ம மணிகிள ரொலியைம்பா","லரைவிளை கலைநல்லா ரறுகினெய் யணிந்தனரே."],"p":"","r":"They hail her as gold grown on the mountain, a single right-whorled conch-gem, nectar of the waves and the object of Lakshmi's wish, and the fine women put durva grass in ghee on her to the sound of bangles, drums and conches.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vazhipaadu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2430","lb":"CC2430","mm":1,"ev":"Women praise the bride and anoint her with ghee and durva grass.","cl":[],"ag":["கதிர்முலையவர்","பலர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2431":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2431,"t":["கள்ளவிழ் கமழ்கோதைக் காவலன் றிருமகளை","வெள்ளணி மதயானை விழுமணிக் குடமேற்றித்","தெள்ளறன் மண்ணுந்நீ ராட்டினர் தேமலர்மே","லொள்ளிழை யவளொத்தா ளுருவநுண் ணுசுப்பின்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"They bathe the king's daughter with clear bathing water brought in a fine jewelled pot on a white musth elephant, and she looks like Lakshmi on a flower.","n":"The syntax of ஏற்றி (raised, carried) leaves open whether the pot rides the elephant or the girl is lifted to it; the sense of an elephant-borne bath pot matches Gandharvadattai's bath in 623.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vazhipaadu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2431","lb":"CC2431","mm":1,"ev":"The bride is given the ceremonial bath with water from a jewelled pot carried on an elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["கதிர்முலையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2432":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2432,"t":["வான்மலர் நுரைசூடி மணியணி கலன்சிந்தாத்","தானிள மணலெக்கர்த் தவழ்கதிர் மணியார","மேனைய நறுஞ்சுண்ணங் குங்கும மிடுங்களியாத்","தேனின் மிசைபாடத் தீம்புன னடந்தஃதே."],"p":"","r":"The bath water flows on with flower foam, with gems it does not spill, with scented powder and saffron mixed into mud on the young sand banks, and with bees singing over it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2432","lb":"CC2432","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கதிர்முலையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2433":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2433,"t":["நான்றபொன் மணிமாலை நகுகதிர்ப் பவளத்தூ","ணூன்றின வொளிமுத்த மண்டபத் தொளிர்திங்கள்","கான்றன கதிர்காய்த்தும் வட்டணைக் கதிர்முத்த","மீன்றபொன் விதானத்தின் னீழலுய்த் திரீஇயினரே."],"p":"","r":"They seat her in a hall of pearl pillars set with coral under a golden canopy hung with pearl strings, where rays shine like the moon's.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2433","lb":"CC2433","mm":1,"ev":"The bride is seated under a golden canopy in a pearl hall.","cl":[],"ag":["கதிர்முலையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2434":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2434,"t":["மையணி மதயானை மத்தக வகலல்கு","னெய்யணி குழன்மாலை நிழலுமிழ் குழைமங்கை","மெய்யணி கலன்மாலை மின்னிருந் துகிலேந்திக்","கையணி குழன்மாலைக் கதிர்முலை யவர்சூழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with garland-wreathed hair stand about the bride bearing ornaments, garlands and bright cloth, as she sits shaded like a creeper.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2434","lb":"CC2434","mm":1,"ev":"The attendants gather round the bride with ornaments, garlands and cloth.","cl":[],"ag":["கதிர்முலையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2435":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2435,"t":["அவ்வளை யவிராழிக் கால்பொலிந் தழகார்ந்த","மைவிளை கழுநீர்க்கண் விலாசியு மணியல்குற்","கைவளை யலங்கார மாலையுங் கமழ்கோதை","நைவள மிகுசாய னங்கையைப் புனைகின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The women begin to adorn the soft-voiced girl with bangles, an anklet, the eye-ornament called விலாசி and garlands for the wrist and body.","n":"விலாசி is a rare word whose sense (an ornament for the eye or brow) is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2435","lb":"CC2435","mm":1,"ev":"The attendants begin to dress the bride in ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":["கதிர்முலையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2436":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2436,"t":["யானையு ளரசன்றன் னணிகிளர் வலமருப்பீர்ந்","தூனமி லொளிர்செம்பொன் பதித்தொளி மணியழுத்தி","வான்மண முறச்செய்த மங்கல மணிச்சீப்புத்","தான்முகில் கழிமதிபோற் றன்னுறை நீக்கினளே."],"p":"","r":"Her dresser draws from its sheath, as the moon from cloud, the auspicious comb made from the right tusk of the lord of elephants, set with gold and gems.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2436","lb":"CC2436","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser takes the ivory comb out of its case.","cl":[],"ag":["தெரிவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2437":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2437,"t":["மைந்நூற் றனைய மாவீ ழோதி வகுத்துந் தொகுத்தும் விரித்துங்","கைந்நூற் றிறத்திற் கலப்ப வாரிக் கமழு நானக் கலவை","யைந்நூற் றிறத்தின் னகிலின் னாவி யளைந்து கமழ வூட்டி","யெந்நூற் றிறமு முணர்வா ளெழிலேற் றிமிலின் னேற்ப முடித்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"A woman who knows every treatise parts, gathers and spreads her dark hair according to the hand-books, steeps it in musk paste and agil smoke, and ties it in a knot as handsome as a bull's hump.","n":"The dresser is named only by her skill in the manuals (கைந்நூல், நூல்); dressing the hair is told as a learned art, as Civakan's dressing of the dancer is in 673.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2437","lb":"CC2437","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser combs, perfumes and knots the bride's hair by the rules of the craft.","cl":[],"ag":["தெரிவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2438":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2438,"t":["கரும்புந் தேனும் மமிழ்தும் பாலுங் கலந்த தீஞ்சொன் மடவாட்","கரும்பும் மிலையு மயக்கி யாய்ந்த முல்லைச் சூட்டு மிலைச்சித்","திருந்து திங்கள் சூழ்ந்த மின்னிற் செம்பொற் பட்டஞ் சேர்த்தி","விரும்பும் முத்தம் மாலை நான்ற விழுப்பொன் மகரஞ் செறிந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The sweet-worded dresser fastens jasmine and white-flower garlands, sets a gold band like the moon among lightning on the bride's brow, and ties on a golden makara hung with pearls.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2438","lb":"CC2438","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser sets the flower garlands, forehead band and makara ornament on the bride.","cl":[],"ag":["தெரிவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2439":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2439,"t":["கள்ளுந் தேனும் மொழுகுங் குவளைக் கமழ்பூ நெரித்து வாங்கிக்","கிள்ளை வளைவா ருகிரிற் கிள்ளித் திலகந் திகழப் பொறித்துத்","தெள்ளும் மணிசெய் சுண்ணம் மிலங்கத் திருநீர் நுதலின் னப்பி","யுள்ளம் பருகி மதர்த்த வாட்கண் ணுருவம் மையிற் புனைந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She crushes honey-dripping kuvalai flowers, nips them with her curved nail to print the forehead mark, spreads bright powder on the brow and draws the bride's long heart-drinking eyes in collyrium.","n":"The finger-and-nail setting of the mark is what Vijayai undoes in 350.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2439","lb":"CC2439","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser prints the tilaka and paints the eyes with collyrium.","cl":[],"ag":["தெரிவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2440":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2440,"t":["நாகம் மருப்பி னியன்ற தோடுந் நலங்கொள் கறவுக் குழையும்","போக நீக்கிப் பொருவிஃ றிருவில் லுமிழ்ந்து மின்னுப் பொழியு","மேக மாகி யெரியும் மணியின் னியன்ற கடிப்பு வாங்கி","மேக விசும்பிற் றேவர் விழைய விளங்கச் சேர்த்தி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"She takes off the bride's ear-studs of tusk and the plain ear-rings and sets in their place gem ear-ornaments that burn like a cloud pouring lightning, so bright that the gods in the sky desire her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2440","lb":"CC2440","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser changes the bride's plain ear-ornaments for the gem wedding ear-rings.","cl":[],"ag":["தெரிவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2441":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2441,"t":["விலங்கரம் பொருத சங்கின் வெள்வளை தெளிர்க்கு முன்கை","நலங்கிளர் பவள நன்பொன் விரன்மணி யாழி மின்னக்","கலந்தின்று பணைத்த தோளுங் கவின்வளர் கழுத்து மார்ந்த","வலம்புரி யீன்ற மூத்த மணிநிலா நக்க வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Bangles of conch ring on her forearms, coral and gold finger-rings flash, and a pearl from a right-whorled conch on her neck and full shoulders shines like moonlight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2441","lb":"CC2441","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser puts bangles, rings and the pearl necklace on the bride.","cl":[],"ag":["தெரிவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2442":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2442,"t":["மாமணி முகடு வேய்ந்த மரகத மணிச்செப் பன்ன","தூமணி முலைக டம்மைத் தொழுதகக் கமழுஞ் சாந்திற்","காமரு காம வல்லிக் கொடிகவின் கொண்டு பூத்துத்","தூமணிக் கொழுந்து மென்றோட் டுயல்வர வெழுதி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"On breasts like emerald caskets roofed with gems she draws in sandal paste the creeper of desire, flowering and trembling, down her soft shoulders.","n":"The pattern is called காம வல்லி, the creeper of desire; the poet names the art by the god it serves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2442","lb":"CC2442","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser paints the sandal-paste creeper on the bride's breasts and shoulders.","cl":[],"ag":["தெரிவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2443":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2443,"t":["நாண்சுமக் கலாத நங்கை நகைமின்னு நுசுப்பு நோவப்","பூண்சுமக் கலாத பொன்ஞாண் வடத்தொடு புரள நோக்கிப்","பாண்குலாய் வண்டு பாடும் படுகணை மறந்து காமன்","காண்கிலேன் கடிய வென்னா வுருகிமெய் கரந்திட் டானே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing her waist ache under its smiling lightning and the gold chain roll free of its weight, Kama forgets his bee-song bow and arrows, says he cannot bear to look, melts and hides his body.","n":"நாண்சுமக்கலாத is hard (she who bears no shame or no bow-string); the sense that her beauty disarms the love-god is clear either way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2443","lb":"CC2443","mm":1,"ev":"Kama, seeing the adorned bride, forgets his arrows and hides himself.","cl":[],"ag":["ஐங்கணைக் கிழவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2444":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2444,"t":["அவாக்கிடந் தகன்ற வல்கு லணிகிளர் திருவிற் பூப்பத்","தவாக்கதிர்க் காசு கண்டா ராவியைத் தளரச் சூட்டிக்","கவாய்க்கிடந் தணங்கு நாறுங் கண்கொளாப் பட்டு டுத்தா","ளுவாக்கதிர்க் திங்க ளம்மென் கதிர்விரித் துடுத்த தொத்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"She puts on a girdle of gems whose rays loosen the life of those who look, and a silk their eyes cannot hold, so that she looks like the full moon wearing its own soft rays.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2444","lb":"CC2444","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser girdles the bride and clothes her in fine silk.","cl":[],"ag":["தெரிவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2445":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2445,"t":["இடைச்செறி குறங்கு கௌவிக் கிம்புரி யிளக மின்னும்","புடைச்சிறு பரடு புல்லிக் கிண்கிணி சிலம்போ டார்ப்ப","நடைச்சிறு பாதங் கோல மணிவிர லணிந்து நாகத்","துடைச்சிறு நாவிற் றோகை யிரீஇயினண் மாலை சேர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Thigh-bands, tinkling anklets and gem toe-rings go on, and she is made to sit among garlands.","n":"Lines 3-4 (நாகத் துடைச் சிறு நாவில் தோகை) are hard to construe and the last clause is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2445","lb":"CC2445","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser puts thigh, ankle and toe ornaments on the bride and seats her among garlands.","cl":[],"ag":["தெரிவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2446":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2446,"t":["அம்மல ரடியுங் கையு மணிகிளர் பவழ வாயுஞ்","செம்மலர் நுதலு நாவுந் திருந்தொளி யுகிரோ டங்கேழ்","விம்மிதப் பட்டு வீழ வலத்தக மெழுதி யிட்டா","ளம்மலர்க் கண்ட முள்ளிட் டரிவையைத் தெரிவை தானே."],"p":"","r":"The dresser paints the soft feet, hands and mouth of the girl with red lac and finishes the bride.","n":"வலத்தகம் is probably a misprint for அலத்தகம், the red lac.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2446","lb":"CC2446","mm":1,"ev":"The dresser paints the bride's hands and feet with lac.","cl":[],"ag":["தெரிவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2447":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2447,"t":["வாண்மதர் மழைக்க ணோக்கி வருமுலைத் தடமு நோக்கிக்","காண்வர வகன்ற வல்குற் கண்விருப் புற்று நோக்கிப்","பாணுவண் டாற்றுங் கோலச் சிகழிகைப் படியு நோக்கி","யாண்விருப் புற்று நின்றா ரவ்வளைத் தோளி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The bangled women look at her bright eyes, her breasts, her hips and the knot of her hair in turn and stand with a man's longing for her.","n":"The women, not men, are said to feel ஆண்விருப்பு; the poem lets the dressing itself show how great the beauty is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2447","lb":"CC2447","mm":1,"ev":"The attendants stand gazing at the bride with longing.","cl":[],"ag":["கதிர்முலையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2448":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2448,"t":["தெருள்கலான் படைத்தவன் காணிற் செவ்வனே","மருள்கலா தவர்களு மருள்வர் மம்மர்நோ","யிருளிலா ரெங்ஙன முய்வ ரின்னதா","லருளிலா ரவணல மணிந்த வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says that even her maker, if he saw her, would be bewildered, and even the unbewildered would reel, so how could those in the sickness of delusion escape; such was how the graceless ones adorned her beauty.","n":"இருளிலார் and அருளிலார் in lines 3-4 can be read in more than one way (those free of darkness, those without compassion); the narrator's point is the overpowering of the heart by beauty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2448","lb":"CC2448","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a woman's adorned beauty can bewilder even those not given to bewilderment","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2449":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2449,"t":["அலர்ந்தவந் தாமரை யல்லிப் பாவையைப்","புலந்துகண் சிவந்தன போன்று நீர்பிரிந்","திலங்கிமின் னுமிழ்ந்துலா மேனி யேந்துபொன்","மலர்ந்ததோர் கற்பக மணிக்கொம் பாயினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Fully adorned she stands like a gem branch of the wishing tree blossoming in gold, her body shining with lightning, as though the lotus-goddess had left the opened lotus in a sulk, her eyes red.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2449","lb":"CC2449","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["திருமகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2450":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2450,"t":["மருடகு மல்லிகை மாலை வல்லவன்","பொருடகத் தொடுத்தன் புனைந்த பூஞ்சிகை","யிருடுணித் திடையிடை யியற்றி வெண்ணிலாச்","சுருடுணித் தொருவழித் தொகுத்த தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Her flowered hair looks as if a skilled maker of jasmine garlands had strung it with cuts of darkness between the flowers and gathered scrolls of white moonlight in one place.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2450","lb":"CC2450","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2451":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2451,"t":["கோமக ளுருவமாய்க் கூற்றம் போந்தது","போமினும் முயிருயக் கொண்டு போய்மனங்","காமின் மெனக்கலை சிலம்பு கிண்கிணி","தாமனும் வாயினாற் சாற்று கின்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Death has come in the form of the king's daughter, and her girdle, anklet and bells cry with their own mouths to the onlookers to go while they can, keeping their lives and guarding their hearts.","n":"The warning is spoken by her ornaments, the same cry that onlookers put in the ornaments' mouths for Gandharvadattai in 637.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2451","lb":"CC2451","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["திருமகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2452":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2452,"t":["அருளிலா ரிவடம ரன்ன ராயினு","முருடிரை யுலகெலா முருளு மின்றெனக்","கருதின கவரிசாந் தாற்றி வெண்குடை","யரிவையை மறைத்தன வால வட்டமே."],"p":"","r":"Fearing that even the graceless, though like Hara, would reel and make the world roll today, the chowries, sandal fans and white parasol hide the girl.","n":"The sense of அருளிலார் இவடம் அரன்னராயினும் is uncertain; the plain act, fans and parasol screening her, is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2452","lb":"CC2452","mm":1,"ev":"The attendants shade the bride with fans and a parasol to hide her.","cl":[],"ag":["கதிர்முலையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:105"},"cc:2453":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2453,"t":["கரும்பே தேனே யமிர்தே காமர் மணியாழே","யரும்பார் மலர்மே லணங்கே மழலை யன்னம்மே","சுரும்பார் சோலை மயிலே குயிலே சுடர்வீசும்","பெரும்பூண் மன்னன் பாவாய் பூவாய் பிணைமானே."],"p":"","r":"A string of loving names (sugar-cane, honey, nectar, jewelled vina, swan, peahen, koel, doe) is poured on the king's daughter, addressed as the doll of the jewel-wearing king.","n":"The verse names no speaker; the women of the wedding hall are taken as the speakers from the verses that follow, and 'the king's doll' is Govindan's daughter by the chapter's title.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2453","lb":"CC2453","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பாவை மார்","இலக்கணை"],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2454":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2454,"t":["அம்மெல் லனிச்சம் மலரு மன்னத் தூவியும்","வெம்மை யாமென் றஞ்சி மெல்ல மிதியாத","பொம்மென் னிலவப் பூம்போ தனநின் னடிபோற்றி","இம்மென் கலையா ரிடுவென் றேத்த வொதுங்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"The bride steps aside while her attendants cry that they will lay down a bed softer than anicham flowers and swan down, which she would not tread for fear of its heat, and praise her feet.","n":"The syntax of lines 2 to 4 is hard (whose feet are praised, and who lays what); the reading follows the plain order of the words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2454","lb":"CC2454","mm":1,"ev":"The bride, praised by her attendants, steps aside from a flower-soft path.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2455":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2455,"t":["தூமாண் டுமக் குடமா யிரமாய்ச் சுடர்பொற்றூண்","டாமாயிரமாய்த் தகையார் மணித்தூ ணொருநூறாய்ப்","பூமாண் டாமத் தொகையாற் பொலிந்த குளிர்பந்தர்","வேமா னியர்தம் மகளின் விரும்ப நனிசேர்ந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The bride comes into the cool wedding pavilion, with a thousand gold pillars and a hundred gem pillars and wreaths of flowers, like a daughter of the celestials.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2455","lb":"CC2455","mm":1,"ev":"The bride arrives at the pavilion.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2456":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2456,"t":["தேனார் காமன் சிலையுங் கணையுந் திறைகொண்ட","வானார் மதிவாண் முகமும் மடமான் மதர்நேர்க்குங்","கோனார் மகடன் வடிவு நோக்கிக் குடைமன்ன","ரானார் கண்ணூ டழல்போ யமையா ரானாரே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the form of the king's daughter, whose moon-face and doe-like grace outdo Kaman's bow and arrows, the umbrella kings are scorched in the eyes as by fire and cannot rest.","n":"The kings' pain is the pain of the losing suitors; it makes the bride's worth the measure of what Civakan wins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","azhukkaaru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2456","lb":"CC2456","mm":1,"ev":"The assembled kings, seeing the bride, burn with longing and grief.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2457":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2457,"t":["வண்டலர் கோதை வாட்கண் வனமுலை வளர்த்த தாயர்","கண்டுயி ருண்ணுங் கூற்றங் கயிறுரீஇக் காட்டி யிட்டா","ருண்டுயிர் சிலர்கண் வாழ்கென் றுத்தரா சங்கம் வைத்தார்","தெண்டிரை வேலி யெங்குந் தீதின தாக மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"The nurses who reared the bride, seeing her eyes as Death that drinks lives, pull out the cord and show it, and the verse closes with a wish that evil be across the whole sea-ringed land.","n":"The lines on the nurses' act and the word உத்தராசங்கம் are obscure; the verse closes with the same refrain as 2466 and may be a ward against the evil eye, which the Tamil does not state.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2457","lb":"CC2457","mm":1,"ev":"The bride's nurses show a cord at the sight of her death-dealing eyes.","cl":[],"ag":["தாயர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2458":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2458,"t":["கண்ணினா லின்று கண்டாங் கூற்றினைக் காமர் செவ்வா","யொண்ணுத லுருவக் கோலத் தொருபிடி நுசுப்பிற் றீஞ்சொல்","வண்ணித்த லாவ தில்லா வருமுலை மதர்வை நோக்கிற்","பெண்ணுடைப் பேதை நீர்மைப் பெருந்தடங் கண்ணிற் றம்மா."],"p":"","r":"The onlookers say that today they saw Death with their own eyes: it is the great wide eyes of a woman with a waist like an elephant cow's, whose breasts no praise can describe.","n":"No speaker is named; the kings of 2456 and 2459 are taken, though any watcher could be meant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2458","lb":"CC2458","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்",""],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2459":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2459,"t":["அரத்தக மருளிச் செய்த சீறடி யளிய தம்மாற்","குரற்சிலம் பொலிப்பச் சென்னிக் குஞ்சிமேன் மிதிப்ப நோற்றான்","றிருக்குவாய்க் கிடந்த மார்பிற் சீவக னாங்க ளெல்லாந்","தரித்திலந் தவத்தை யென்று தார்மன்ன ரேமுற் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"The kings, seeing the bride's soft reddened feet, say that Civakan, on whose head-tuft she will tread with sounding anklets, must have done penance, which they themselves could not bear, and fall into grief.","n":"The kings give the win to Civakan's earlier tavam, which the verse does not specify; the Jain epic lets the losers name deeds as the cause of his luck.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaamam","azhukkaaru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2459","lb":"CC2459","mm":1,"ev":"The rival kings lament that Civakan has won the bride.","cl":[["a man wins a rare bride by penance done earlier","general","reported",2,"mannar"]],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மன்னர்",""],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2460":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2460,"t":["கோவிந்த னென்னுஞ் செம்பொற் குன்றின்மேற் பிறந்து கூர்வேற்","சீவக னென்னுஞ் செந்நீர்ப் பவளமா கடலுட் பாய்வான்","பூவுந்தி யமுத யாறு பூங்கொடி நுடங்கப் போந்து","தாவிரி வேள்விச் சாலை மடுவினுட் டாழ்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The nectar river of the bride flows down from the golden mountain Govindan toward the coral sea that is Civakan, but sinks into the pond of the sacrificial hall.","n":"Govindan, the bride's father and Civakan's uncle, is the mountain; the wedding is that of cousins and the sacrificial hall is the bride's place of marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2460","lb":"CC2460","mm":1,"ev":"Govindan's daughter is brought to the sacrificial hall to be given to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை","கோவிந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2461":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2461,"t":["சாணிடை நெடிய வாட்கண் டளையவிழ் குவளை பூப்பப்","பூணுடை முலையின் பாரம் பொறுக்கலாச் சுளிவின் மேலு","நாணட நடுங்கிக் கையா னகைமுகம் புதைத்த தோற்றஞ்","சேணிடை யரவு சேர்ந்த திங்களை யொத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The bride, her fish-long eyes opening like flowers and weighed down by her breasts, trembles with shame and hides her smiling face with her hand, like the moon in the sky caught by a serpent.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2461","lb":"CC2461","mm":1,"ev":"The bride covers her face in shyness.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2462":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2462,"t":["முத்துமிழ் திரைக ளங்க மொய்கொள் பா தால முத்தீ","யொத்தன வேலை வேள்வி யொலிகட னான்கு நாண","வைத்தநான் மறையு நீந்தி வான் குண மென்னுஞ் சாலி","வித்திமே லுலகத் தின்பம் விளைத்து மெய் கண்டநீரார்."],"p":"","r":"The verse likens the sacrifice's sound to the sea and says the truth-seers who have crossed the four Vedas sow the paddy of virtue and bring about the world's joy.","n":"A Jain work describes a Vedic wedding rite without answering it here; the verse leaves open whether the narrator holds the claim or only describes the priests' belief.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","vedham","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2462","lb":"CC2462","mm":1,"ev":"The marriage priests begin the sacrifice.","cl":[["sages who have crossed the four Vedas sow virtue and gain earthly joy through the sacrifice","vedic","reported",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மெய் கண்ட நீரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2463":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2463,"t":["தருமணற் றருப்பை யார்ந்த சமிதையிம் மூன்றி னானும்","விரிமல ரணிந்த கோல வேதிகை யியற்றி யானெ","யொருமணி யகலுட் பெய்தோர் பொன்னக லார்ந்த தூப","மிருமணி யகலு ணீர்பெய் திடவயி னிரீஇயி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The priests build the altar with fine sand, darbha and three sorts of fuel, adorned with flowers, and set incense in one jewel-bowl and water in another.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2463","lb":"CC2463","mm":1,"ev":"The priests make the altar and set out the vessels.","cl":[],"ag":["மெய் கண்ட நீரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2464":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2464,"t":["நெற்பொரி நிறையப் பெய்து நிழலுமிழ் செம்பொன் மூழிக்","கற்புரி கடவு ளான்பா லவியொடு கலப்ப வைத்து","மூற்பெரி யானை யாகத் தருப்பையான் முடிந்து மூன்று","பொற்புரி வரையும் பொய்தீர் சமிதைக ளிரண்டும் வைத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"They fill a gold vessel with parched rice, set it with the ghee for the god of fire, tie it with darbha and lay out the three gold-ringed bars and two pure sticks.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2464","lb":"CC2464","mm":1,"ev":"The priests lay out the offerings.","cl":[],"ag":["மெய் கண்ட நீரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2465":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2465,"t":["மந்திர விதியின் மாண்ட சிறுவிரற் றருப்பை சூழ்ந்து","முந்துநா மொழிந்த நெய்யை முனைமுதிர் தருப்பை தன்னான்","மந்திரித் தமைய முக்கான் மண்ணிமற் றதனை நீக்கிச்","சிந்தித்து மறையிற் செந்தீத் தண்டிலத் தங்கண் வைத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"By the rule of the mantras they take darbha, purify the ghee with it three times, remove it and place it, thinking it over, in the fire-bed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2465","lb":"CC2465","mm":1,"ev":"The priests consecrate the ghee and place it on the fire.","cl":[],"ag":["மெய் கண்ட நீரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2466":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2466,"t":["தண்டிலத் தகத்திற் சாண்மே லெண்விரற் சமிதை நானான்","கெண்டிசை யவரு மேத்தத் துடுப்புநெய் சொரித லோடுங்","கொண்டழற் கடவுள் பொங்கி வலஞ்சுழன் றெழுந்த தென்ப","தெண்டிரை வேலி யெங்குந் திருவிளை யாட மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"As the spoon pours ghee on the eight-finger sticks and the quarters bless, the fire god is said to rise turning right, that fortune may play across the whole sea-ringed land.","n":"The words 'they say' (என்ப) mark the omen as hearsay; the verse does not endorse the rite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","vazhipaadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2466","lb":"CC2466","mm":1,"ev":"The fire rises on the pouring of the ghee.","cl":[["a fire that rises turning right when ghee is poured foretells fortune for the land","vedic","reported",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மெய் கண்ட நீரார்","அழற் கடவுள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2467":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2467,"t":["கரையுடைத் துகிலிற் றோன்றுங் காஞ்சன வட்டின் முந்நீர்த்","திரையிடை வியாழந் தோன்றத் திண்பிணி முழுவுஞ் சங்கு","முரசொடு முழங்கி யார்ப்ப மொய்கொள்வேன் மன்ன ரார்ப்ப","வரசரு ளரச னாய்பொற் கலசநீ ரங்கை யேற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"With gold pot water in the shape of the sea and Jupiter showing, drums, conches and kings roaring, Civakan, king of kings, takes the water into his palm.","n":"The pouring of water into the groom's palm is the sign of giving the bride; the verse does not name the giver, taken as Govindan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","vaazhkkaithunai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2467","lb":"CC2467","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan receives the gift-water that gives him the bride.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2468":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2468,"t":["குளிர்மதி கொண்ட நாகங் கோளைவிடுக் கின்ற தேபோற்","றளிர்புரை கோதை மாதர் தாமரை முகத்தைச் சேர்ந்த","வொளிர்வளைக் கையைச் செல்வன் விடுத்தவ ளிடக்கை பற்றி","வளரெரி வலங்கொண் டாய்பொற் கட்டிறா னேறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As the serpent lets go the moon, Civakan takes the bangled hand of the lotus-faced bride in his left, goes round the fire, and mounts the gold bed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2468","lb":"CC2468","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan takes the bride's hand, circles the fire and sits her on the couch.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2469":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2469,"t":["விளங்கொளி விசும்பிற் பூத்த வருந்ததி காட்டி யான்பால்","வளங்கொளப் பூத்த கோல மலரடி கழீஇய பின்றை","யிளங்கதிர்க் கலத்தி னேந்த வயினிகண் டமர்ந்தி ருந்தான்","றுளங்கெயிற் றழுவை தொல்சீர்த் தோகையோ டிருந்த தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"After the star Arundhati is shown, her feet are washed with milk; he sits looking at the ceremonial offering, like a fanged tiger beside a peahen.","n":"The word அயினி and the person who washes the feet are obscure, so which rites are meant here is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2469","lb":"CC2469","mm":1,"ev":"The couple are shown Arundhati and Civakan sits down with her.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2470":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2470,"t":["பொன்னங் காழிற் பொலிந்த முத்து விதானம் புணர்ந்துதேன்","மன்னு மாலை பலதாழ்ந்து மணப்புகை விம்மி மல்கிய","வன்னத் தூவி யடர்பஞ்சி யவிர்மயி ராதி யாகப்","பன்னிச் சொன்ன பதினைந்தும் படுத்தார் பாவை மார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The maids arrange the fifteen things named for the bed, among them a pearl canopy, garlands, swan down and soft cotton, with scented smoke filling the room.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2470","lb":"CC2470","mm":1,"ev":"The women prepare the bridal bed.","cl":[],"ag":["பாவை மார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2471":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2471,"t":["பனிமயிர் குளிர்ப்பன பஞ்சின் மெல்லிய","கனிமயிர் குளிர்ப்பன கண்கொ ளாதன","வெலிமயிர் போர்வைவைத் தெழினி வாங்கினா","ரொலிமயிரிச் சிகழிகை யுருவக் கொம்பனார்."],"p":"","r":"The slim women put the bed's coverings of cool soft cotton and down, hang a curtain, and draw it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2471","lb":"CC2471","mm":1,"ev":"The women dress the bed and draw the curtain.","cl":[],"ag":["பாவை மார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2472":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2472,"t":["விழுத்தகு மணிச்செவி வெண்பொற் கைவினை","யெழிற்பொலி படியக மிரண்டு பக்கமுந்","தொழிற்பட வைத்தனர் துளும்பு மேகலைக்","கழித்தவே லிரண்டுகண் டனைய கண்ணினார்."],"p":"","r":"The girls, whose eyes are like two drawn spears, set gem-eared lamps and silver-gold mirrors on both sides of the bed.","n":"The words மணிச்செவி and படியகம் are rare; the objects are read as vessels and mirrors but are not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2472","lb":"CC2472","mm":1,"ev":"The women set out lamps and mirrors by the bed.","cl":[],"ag":["பாவை மார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2473":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2473,"t":["அங்கருங் காலி சீவி யூறவைத் தமைக்கப் பட்ட","செங்களி விராய காயுஞ் செம்பழுக் காயுந் தீந்தே","னெங்கணுங் குளிர்த்த வின்னீ ரிளம்பசுங் காயு மூன்றுந்","தங்களி செய்யக் கூட்டித் தையலார் கைசெய் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women prepare a pleasurable mix of the soft, ripe, red-sap and tender cool-water fruits with sweet honey.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2473","lb":"CC2473","mm":1,"ev":"The women prepare the mixed fruit and betel items.","cl":[],"ag":["பாவை மார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2474":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2474,"t":["கைசெய்து கமழு நூறுங் காழ்க்கும்வெள் ளிலையுங் காம","மெய்தநன் குணர்ந்த நீரா ரின்முக வாச மூட்டிப்","பெய்தபொற் செப்பு மாலைப் பெருமணிச் செப்புஞ் சுண்ணந்","தொய்யறப் பெய்த தூநீர்த் தொடுகடற் பவளச் செப்பும்."],"p":"","r":"They add the scented lime, the bitter white leaf, and the cool liquid scents that give the breath a sweet smell, in gold, gem and coral caskets filled with powder.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2474","lb":"CC2474","mm":1,"ev":"The women stock the caskets with betel and scent.","cl":[],"ag":["பாவை மார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2475":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2475,"t":["தாமணி நானச் செப்புஞ் சலஞ்சலக் கலன்பெய் செப்புந்","தூமணித் துகில்க ளார்ந்த வலம்புரித் துலங்கு செப்புங்","காமநீர்க் காம வல்லி கவின்கொண்டு வளர்ந்த தேபோ","னாமவே னெடுங்கட் பாவை நயப்பன வேந்தி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The long-eyed girls carry in caskets of musk, vessels of scent, a conch casket of fine cloth, like the creeper of desire growing in the water of love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2475","lb":"CC2475","mm":1,"ev":"The women bring in the caskets of scent and cloth.","cl":[],"ag":["பாவை மார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2476":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2476,"t":["விரிகதி ரார மின்னித் தாரெனுந் திருவில் வீசிக்","குரிசின்மா மேகம் பெய்த கொழும்புயற் காம மாரி","யரிவைதன் னெஞ்ச மென்னு மகன்குள நிறைந்து வாட்கட்","கரியமை சேறு சிந்திக் கலிங்குக டிறந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan the great cloud lets fall a rain of desire, with pearl strings for lightning and the garland for a rainbow, which fills the tank of the bride's heart, and her kohl runs as mud when the sluices open.","n":"The consummation is told wholly by the figure of a rain-cloud filling a tank; no act is described.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2476","lb":"CC2476","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan and the bride come together.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2477":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2477,"t":["தோக்கையந் துகிலி னாடன் றுணைமுலை பொருது சேந்த","வேக்கொசி விலாத வில்லா னிடுகொடி யகல மின்றேன்","றேக்கிவண் டிமிருங் கோதை செல்வன்றா ருழக்க நைந்து","பூக்கொய்து துவண்ட கொம்பின் பொற்பின ளாயி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"After the bride's close embrace, her cloth dishevelled, breasts reddened, her bee-humming wreath crushed by her husband's garland, she is left like a creeper wilted by the flower-plucking.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2477","lb":"CC2477","mm":1,"ev":"The bride, after the night, lies drained.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:106"},"cc:2478":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2478,"t":["அணித்தகு பவள மேற்பக் கடைந்துமுத் தழுத்தி யம்பொன்","றுணித்தடி விளிம்பு சேர்த்தித் தொழுதகச் செய்த வண்கை","மணிச்சிரற் சிறகு நாண வகுத்தசாந் தால வட்டம்","பணித்தகு மகளிர் வீசிப் பாவையைக் குளிர்ப்பித் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women appointed for the task fan the bride, the living doll, with sandal-wood palm fans framed in coral and pearl and edged with gold, and so cool her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2478","lb":"CC2478","mm":1,"ev":"Appointed women fan the bride to cool her.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2479":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2479,"t":["சேந்து நீண்ட செழுந்தாமரைக் கண்களி","னேந்தி மாண்ட முலைக்கண்களி னெழுதிச்","சாந்த மாக மெழுதித்தகை மாமல","ராய்ந்து சூட்டி யவனஞ்சலி செய்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Her lotus-red eyes and breasts are painted and sandal is smoothed over her, fine flowers are chosen to crown her, and he joins his hands to her in salutation.","n":"The verse names no one but the pronoun in 'அவன் அஞ்சலி செய்தான்'; that he is the groom, and that the painted figure is the bride and not an image, is read from the scene around it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2479","lb":"CC2479","mm":1,"ev":"The bride is painted with sandal and crowned with flowers, and the groom salutes her.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2480":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2480,"t":["மணிசெய் வீணை மழலைக்குழல் பாண்டிலோ","டணிசெய் கோதை யவர்பாடிய கீதம்","பணிவில் சாயல் பருகிப்பவ ளக்கொடி","மணியு முத்துமலர்ந் திட்டதொத் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"The bride drinks in the song the garlanded women sing to the jewelled lute, the soft flute and the drum, and looks like a coral creeper that has blossomed in gems and pearls.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2480","lb":"CC2480","mm":1,"ev":"The bride listens to the women's songs and music.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2481":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2481,"t":["எய்த்து நீர்ச்சிலம் பின்குரன் மேகலை","வித்தி மாதர் வருத்தம்விளைத் தாளெனத்","தத்து நீர்த்தவ ளைக்குரற் கிண்கிணி","யுய்த்தொர் பூசலுட னிட்டன வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Her water-ringing anklet, her girdle and the croaking little bells together raise a clamour, as if crying that the girl has sown distress among the women.","n":"The ornaments are given a voice as petitioners; the verse leaves the 'women' of line 2 unspecified, so the complaint is a conceit about the bride's rivals or her own band.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2481","lb":"CC2481","mm":1,"ev":"Her anklet, girdle and bells jangle together as if in complaint.","cl":[],"ag":["கிண்கிணி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2482":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2482,"t":["ஏந்தி நாங்களுட னேயிடு பூசலை","வேந்தர் வேந்தன் கொடுங்கோ லினனாகி","யாய்ந்து கேட்டு மருளானென் றவிந்தன","சாந்த மேந்துமுலை யாள்கலந் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The ornaments fall silent once the sandal-breasted bride embraces the groom, saying that the king of kings has turned cruel-sceptred and will not even look into, or heed, the clamour they raised together.","n":"A lovers' jest cast in the language of petition and a bent sceptre (கொடுங்கோல்); the 'cruel king' is the bridegroom whose embrace silences the complaint, and no real injustice is told.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","arasu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2482","lb":"CC2482","mm":1,"ev":"The bride embraces Civakan, and her jangling ornaments fall silent.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை","சீவகன்","கிண்கிணி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2483":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2483,"t":["வீடுமலி யுலகினவர் போலவிளை யாடுந்","தோடுமலி கோதையொடு துதைந்தவரை மின்போ","லாடுகொடி யணிந்தவுய ரலங்கல்வரை மார்பன்","கூடுமயிர் களையும்வகை கூறலுறு கின்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator announces that he will now tell how the groom, whose mountain-like chest is hung with garlands and who sports with his garlanded bride as people sport in a world full of bliss, has the grown hair removed.","n":"வீடுமலி உலகினவர் is read as the dwellers of a world rich in bliss, to which the sport of the pair is likened; whether வீடு means the Jain release or the heaven of the gods is not settled by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2483","lb":"CC2483","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2484":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2484,"t":["உச்சிவரை வளர்ந்திளமை யொழிந்தவுயர் திண்கா","ழிச்சவிய வல்லவென வெழுதியவை யூன்றிக்","கச்சுவிளிம் பணிந்ததொழிற் கம்பலவி தான","நச்சுமணி நாகருறை நாகமென விரித்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Workmen mark out and raise tall, mature, solid pillars, then spread over them an embroidered canopy like the serpent-world of the poison-jewelled nagas.","n":"The first two lines are hard to construe; the pillars are an inference from உயர்திண்காழ் and ஊன்றி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2484","lb":"CC2484","mm":1,"ev":"Workmen raise pillars and spread a canopy over the wedding pavilion.","cl":[],"ag":["விரித்தார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2485":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2485,"t":["முத்தக நிறைந்தமுளை யெயிற்றுமத யானை","மத்தகமுந் திருமகடன் வடிவுபட மாதோ","வொத்தகல மெண்முழமென் றோதிநக ரிழைத்தார்","மொய்தெரிசெம் பொற்றுகளி னூன்முடிவு கண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"They lay out the pavilion floor eight arm-lengths across with figures of tusker-heads and of the goddess of fortune, and complete the pattern with threads of fine gold dust.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2485","lb":"CC2485","mm":1,"ev":"Workmen draw the auspicious figures on the pavilion floor.","cl":[],"ag":["விரித்தார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2486":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2486,"t":["உழுந்துபய றுப்பரிசி யப்பமருங் கலங்கள்","கொழுந்துபடக் கூப்பிநனி யாயிர மரக்கால்","செழுந்துபடச் செந்நெனிறைத் தந்நுண்கொடி யறுகின்","கொழுந்துகுறைத் தணிந்துகொலை வேற்கணவ ரமைத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Spear-bearing men set out, in the proper way, offerings of black gram, green gram, rice-cakes and pastries, fill a thousand measures with red rice, and trim the tender tips of arugu grass.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2486","lb":"CC2486","mm":1,"ev":"Men prepare the food offerings and the grass tips for the rite.","cl":[],"ag":["விரித்தார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2487":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2487,"t":["செங்கய லிரட்டைதிரு வார்சுடர்க ணாடி","பொங்குகொடி வார்முரசந் தோட்டிபுணர் கும்ப","மங்கலங்க ளெட்டுமிவை மணியிற்புனைந் தேந்தி","யங்கயற்க ணரிவையர்க டென்கிழக்கி னின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Fine-eyed women stand in the south-east holding, set in gems, the eight auspicious emblems: the pair of red fish, the sign of fortune, lamps, mirror, banner, drum, goad and pot.","n":"The list is read from the compressed words of lines 1 to 3 and the identification of each emblem is partly inferred; the verse itself says only that the eight are held.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2487","lb":"CC2487","mm":1,"ev":"Women stand in the south-east holding the eight auspicious emblems.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிவையர்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2488":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2488,"t":["வெள்ளுருவ மாலைவட கீழிருவர் மின்போ","லொள்ளுருவ வாளுருவி நின்றனர்தென் மேல்பா","லுள்ளுருக நோக்கியுய ருழுத்தகலு மேந்திக்","கள்ளுருவ மாலையவர் கைதொழுது நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Two women stand in the north-east with drawn swords bright as lightning, while garlanded women in the south-west, their hearts melting as they gaze, hold up vessels of black gram and stand with joined hands.","n":"உழுத்தகலும் is read as a vessel of gram; the reading is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2488","lb":"CC2488","mm":1,"ev":"Sword-bearing women and hand-joined women take their places at two corners of the pavilion.","cl":[],"ag":["அரிவையர்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2489":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2489,"t":["தோரைமலர் நீரறுகு துளும்புமணித் தால","மாரவட மேற்றிசைக்க ணிருந்தவலிர் பஞ்சிச்","சீர்நிறைய வரையகலந் திருத்தத்திரு நோக்கும்","வாரமுறைக் கருவிவடக் கிருந்தனகண் மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"In the west stand gem plates brimming with hill-rice flowers, water and arugu grass hung with pearl strings; in the north, cotton and the instruments lie ready in proper turn.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 (இருந்தவலிர் பஞ்சி, திருநோக்கும் வார முறைக் கருவி) are obscure; the cotton and instruments are inferred.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2489","lb":"CC2489","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2490":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2490,"t":["பானுரையி னெய்யவணைப் பைங்கதிர்கள் சிந்தித்","தானிரவி திங்களொடு சார்ந்திருந்த தேபோல்","வேனிரைச்செய் கண்ணியொடு மெல்லென விருந்தான்","வானுயர வோங்குகுடை மன்னர்பெரு மானே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, great king beneath the sky-high umbrella, sits gently beside the spear-eyed bride on the couch, like the sun come to rest with the moon, their rays scattering.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","punarchi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2490","lb":"CC2490","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan and the bride sit together on the wedding couch.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2491":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2491,"t":["குளநென் முன்றிற்கனி தேன்சொரி சோலைக் குளிர்மணி","வளமை மல்கியெரி யம்மட மந்திகை காய்த்துவா","னிளமை யாடியிருக் கும்வனத் தீர்ஞ்சடை மாமுனி","கிளையை நீங்கிக்கிளர் சாபத்தி னாவித னாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"The poet turns to a man who lived in a forest of fruit-groves and mango-boughs with a matted-haired sage, and who, leaving that kin, became by a rising curse a being in his own right.","n":"ஆவிதன் is the doubtful word; the verse is read as the origin of the groomer who appears from 2492, but it does not name his trade.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2491","lb":"CC2491","mm":1,"ev":"A sage's curse sends a man away from his kin in the forest.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2492":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2492,"t":["ஆய்ந்த கேள்வி யவன்கான் முளையாய்வழித் தோன்றினான்","றோய்ந்த கேள்வித் துறைபோ யலங்காரமுந் தோற்றினான்.","வேந்தன் றன்னாற் களிற்றூர் சிறப்பொடு மேயினான்","வாய்ந்த கோல முடையான் பெருமஞ்சிகர்க் கேறனான்."],"p":"","r":"Born in the line that sprouted from the sage's foot, he learned deeply, showed his skill in adornment, was given by the king the honour of riding an elephant to town, and was fit for the great palanquin.","n":"கால்முளை (the foot's sprout) is the only mark of his caste; the verse does not call him a barber, and the king here is the unnamed ruler of the city.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","kalvi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2492","lb":"CC2492","mm":1,"ev":"The groomer is honoured by the king with an elephant ride and a palanquin.","cl":[],"ag":["வித்தகன்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2493":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2493,"t":["நித்தில வடமும் பூணு மாரமு நிழன்று தாழ","வொத்தொளிர் குழைகள் காதி னான்றுபொன் னூச லாடப்","பைத்தர வல்குற் பாவை கரகநீர் சொரியப் பாங்கின்","வித்தகன் பூசி வெள்வேல் வேந்தனுக் கிறைஞ்சி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Hung with pearl strings and ornaments, his ear-rings swinging like golden swings, the skilled man has a doll-like woman pour water from a pitcher, anoints himself and bows to the white-speared king.","n":"The king is not named; he is taken as the king who presides at the wedding.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2493","lb":"CC2493","mm":1,"ev":"The groomer is poured water, anoints himself and bows to the king before his work.","cl":[],"ag":["வித்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2494":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2494,"t":["நச்செயிற் றரவி னோக்கின் யொளிமுடிச் சிதறி னானே.","வச்சிர வண்ணன் காப்ப மன்னரை நடுங்க நோக்கி","வச்சுதங் கொண்டு மன்ன வாழிய ரூழி யென்னா","யுச்சிவண் டிமிரு மாலை னடிமுடித் தெளித்து நங்கை"],"p":"","r":"With Vajravannan as guard, the skilled man glares until the kings tremble, wishes the king life for ages, sprinkles water at the feet and loosens the groom's shining hair-knot.","n":"Line 1 reads as the end of the sentence that line 4 begins; the verse is out of order or corrupt here and its sense is partly guessed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2494","lb":"CC2494","mm":1,"ev":"The groomer loosens the groom's hair-knot after wishing the king long life.","cl":[],"ag":["வித்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2495":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2495,"t":["வாக்கினிற் செய்த பொன்வாண் மங்கல விதியி னேந்தி","யாக்கிய மூர்த்தத் தண்ணல் வலக்கவு ளுறுத்தி யார்ந்த","தேக்கணின் னகிலி னாவி தேக்கிடுங் குழலி னாளை","நோக்கல னுனித்து நொய்தா விடக்கவு ளுறுத்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"At the auspicious moment the lord takes the golden razor by rule, presses the groom's right cheek without so much as a glance at the fragrant-haired bride, and then presses his left.","n":"The 'lord' of line 2 is the groom being shaved; the pressing of the cheek is the shaver's hand on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2495","lb":"CC2495","mm":1,"ev":"The groomer begins to shave Civakan, right cheek first, then left.","cl":[],"ag":["வித்தகன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2496":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2496,"t":["ஆய்ந்தபொன் வாளை நீக்கி யவிர்மதிப் பாகக் கன்மேற்","காய்ந்தவாள் கலப்பத் தேய்த்துப் பூநிறீஇக் காமர் பொன்ஞாண்","டோய்ந்ததன் குறங்கில் வைத்துத் துகிலினிற் றுடைத்துத் தூய்தா","வாய்ந்தகைப் புரட்டி மாதோ மருடகப் பற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He sets down the golden razor, whets another on the stone, wipes it clean on his cloth, lays the gold-corded thing across his thigh, and takes the groom's hand with a turning grip.","n":"The thigh-laid gold cord (பொன்ஞாண்) is hard to place; the verse is read as the razor's preparation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2496","lb":"CC2496","mm":1,"ev":"The groomer whets and wipes the razor and takes up his work.","cl":[],"ag":["வித்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2497":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2497,"t":["ஏற்றி யும்மிழித் தும்மிடை யொற்றியும்","போற்றிச் சந்தனம் பூசுகின் றானெனக்","கூற்ற னான்முகங் கோலஞ்செய் தான்கடற்","றோற்றுஞ் செஞ்சுடர் போலச் சுடர்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Working up and down and pressing in between, as one applies sandal, he dresses the face, which shines like the red sun rising out of the sea.","n":"கூற்றனான் முகம் is hard to read and is left out of the sense given here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2497","lb":"CC2497","mm":1,"ev":"The groomer trims the groom's face until it shines.","cl":[],"ag":["வித்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2498":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2498,"t":["கோதைப் பாரத்தி னானுந்தன் னாணினு","மேதி லான்முக நோக்கு மிளிவினும்","பாத நோக்கிய பான்மதி வாண்முக","மேதமின்றி யெடுத்தனள் மெல்லவே."],"p":"","r":"Weighed down by the garland, held back by her shyness and by the shame of looking at the face of a man new to her, the bride, eyes on his feet, slowly and without fault lifts her moon-face.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2498","lb":"CC2498","mm":1,"ev":"The bride lifts her face slowly to look at the groom.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2499":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2499,"t":["உருவச் செங்கய லொண்ணிறப் புள்வெரீஇ","யிரிய லுற்றன போன்றிணைக் கண்மலர்","வெருவி யோட விசும்பிற குலாவிய","திருவிற் போற்புரு வங்க டிருத்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"As he shapes the brows into a rainbow, the twin fish-red eyes seem frightened birds about to flee into the sky.","n":"The eyes belong to the groom under the razor, though the simile of frightened fish and birds could also be taken of the bride's glance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2499","lb":"CC2499","mm":1,"ev":"The groomer trims the groom's brows.","cl":[],"ag":["வித்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2500":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2500,"t":["ஆர மின்ன வருங்குயந் தான்களைந்","தோரு மொண்டிறற் கத்தரி கைத்தொழி","னீரிற் செய்தடி யேத்துபு நீங்கினான்","றாரன் மாலைத் தயங்கிணர்க் கண்ணியான்."],"p":"","r":"Having removed the hair and finished the fine scissor-work, washed in water, the garlanded man praises the feet and withdraws.","n":"The garlanded man (தாரன் மாலைக் கண்ணியான்) may be the groomer or the groom; I take him as the groomer.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2500","lb":"CC2500","mm":1,"ev":"The groomer finishes, washes, praises the feet and withdraws.","cl":[],"ag":["வித்தகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2501":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2501,"t":["அன்னப் பெடைநடுக்கி யசைந்து தேற்றா நடையாளு","மன்னர் குடைநடுக்கும் மாலை வெள்வேன் மறவோனு","மின்னு மணிக்குடத்தின் வேந்த ரேந்தப் புனலாடிப்","பொன்னங் கடிமலருந் துகிலுஞ் சாந்தும் புனைந்தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The swan-gaited bride and the spear-bearing warrior, whose garland makes kings' umbrellas tremble, bathe from shining gem pitchers lifted by kings and dress in flowers, cloth and sandal.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2501","lb":"CC2501","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan and the bride bathe in water poured by kings and dress.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2502":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2502,"t":["எஞ்சுற்ற மென்றிரங்கா தாக மெல்லாங் கவர்ந்திருந்து","தஞ்சுற்றம் வேண்டாத முலைக்கீழ் வாழ்வு தளர்கின்ற","நஞ்சுற்ற வேனெடுங்கட் பாவை நல்கூர் சிறுநுசுப்பிற்","கஞ்சுற் றுழிப்புலர்ந்தாங் கணிந்தா ரம்ம மணிவடமே."],"p":"","r":"The pearl necklace that took over her whole body, heedless of its own kin, lived under her breasts and dried up (grew limp) where it touched the waist's bodice, and so adorned the spear-eyed doll.","n":"The conceit gives the necklace a will and a family; the grammar of lines 2 and 3 is hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2502","lb":"CC2502","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:107"},"cc:2503":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2503,"t":["சுடுமண் மிசைமாரி சொரியச் சூழ்ந்து சுமந்தெழுந்து","நெடுநன் னிமிராவி நாறு நெய்தோய் தளிர்மேனி","துடிநுண் ணிடைப்பெருந்தோட் டுவர்வா யேழை மலர்மார்பன்","கடிநன் மலர்ப்பள்ளி களிப்பக் காமக் கடலாழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The bridegroom, whose body smells of ghee and whose chest is a flower, sinks into a sea of desire as the bed of flowers delights.","n":"The first half-lines (a smoke-scented, ghee-touched young body) are hard to join, so the sense of the opening is taken from the closing line, where the man sinks in the sea of desire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2503","lb":"CC2503","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan and his bride, on a flower couch, give themselves to love.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2504":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2504,"t":["வழக்கு தாரவன் மார்பிடை மட்டுகப்","புழுங்கு கோதைபொற் பின்றிறம் பேசலாம்","விழுங்கு மேகம் விடாது தழீஇக்கிடந்","தொழிந்த மின்னுக் கொடியொத் தொழிந்திட்டாள்."],"p":"","r":"The bride, clinging to the garlanded chest as lightning clings to the cloud that swallows it, is spent and lies like a vine that has dropped away.","n":"Line 2 is obscure and the simile is read from lines 3 and 4 only.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2504","lb":"CC2504","mm":1,"ev":"The bride lies clasped to Civakan's chest and then goes slack.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2505":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2505,"t":["தாம மார்பனுந் தையலு மெய்யுணர்","வாமி தென்றறி யாது களித்தவர்","தூமங் கொப்புளிக் குந்துகிற் சேக்கைமேற்","காம னப்பணைக் கள்ளுக வைகினார்."],"p":"","r":"The garlanded man and the girl, not knowing it is wrong, delight in each other and lie on a silk bed on which incense smokes, drunk on the wine of love.","n":"The words in line 2 (எய் உணர் வாமி தென்று அறியாது) could mean they do not know how the night passes or that they do not heed what is forbidden; the reading takes the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2505","lb":"CC2505","mm":1,"ev":"The pair lie together on the silk bed, drunk with desire.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2506":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2506,"t":["மாதர்தன் வனப்பு நோக்கி மகிழ்ந்துகண் ணிமைத்தல் செல்லான்","காதலித் திருப்பக் கண்கள் கரிந்துநீர் வரக்கண் டம்ம","பேதைமை பிறரை யுள்ளி யழுபவர்ச் சேர்த வென்றாள்","வேதனை பெருகி வேற்கண் டீயுமிழ்ந் திட்ட வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan gazes at her beauty without blinking until his eyes smart and water; she takes the tears for tears shed thinking of other women, says 'let this folly join those who weep for others', and her spear eyes spit fire in anguish.","n":"Her accusation rests on a misreading: the tears come from not blinking at her, so the sulk is a lovers' quarrel the verse itself shows to be groundless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kan","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2506","lb":"CC2506","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan's tears of rapt gazing make Ilakkanai suspect he is thinking of other women, and she flares in a sulk.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"spk":["இலக்கணை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2507":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2507,"t":["நாறுசாந் தழித்து மாலை பரிந்துநன் கலன்கள் சிந்திச்","சீறுபு செம்பொ னாழி மணிவிர னெரித்து விம்மா","வேறியு மிழிந்து மூழுழ் புருவங்கண் முரிய நொந்து","தேறுநீர் பூத்த செந்தா மரைமுகம் வியர்த்து நின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Angry, she wipes off the sandal, snaps her garland, scatters her jewels, wrings the gold ring on her gem-like finger, sobs, lets her brows crinkle and stands with her lotus face sweating.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","naanam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2507","lb":"CC2507","mm":1,"ev":"Ilakkanai tears off her ornaments in her sulk and stands sobbing.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2508":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2508,"t":["இற்றதென் னாவி யென்னா வெரிமணி யிமைக்கும் பஞ்சிச்","சிற்றடிப் போது புல்லித் திருமகன் கிடப்பச் சேந்து","பொற்றதா மரையிற் போந்து கருமுத்தம் பொழிப் வேபோ","லுற்றுமை கலந்து கண்கள் வெம்பணி யுகுத்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The prince lies clasping her tender feet while her eyes, red as lamps, shed tears like black pearls, and she, her life as if broken, weeps with him.","n":"The simile of tears like black pearls rests on a doubtful word in line 3 (பொழிப் வே).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2508","lb":"CC2508","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan falls at Ilakkanai's feet while she weeps.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2509":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2509,"t":["கொண்டபூ ணின்னைச் சார்ந்து குலாய்க்கொழுந் தீன்ற கொம்பே","கண்டுகண் கரிந்து நீரா யுகுவது கரக்க லாமே","பண்டியான் செய்த பாவப் பயத்தையார்க் குரைப்பென் றேன்காள்","வண்டுகாள் வருடி நங்கை வரந்தர மொழிமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the bough that bore the sprout weep from burning eyes, he asks whom he can tell the fruit of his old sin, and bids the bees stroke her and ask her to give him the boon.","n":"The karma claim is a lover's figure of speech for an undeserved quarrel, not a teaching; and it is the husband, not the narrator, who blames an old deed for a wrong he has not done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","vinai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2509","lb":"CC2509","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan, unable to hide her tears, begs the bees to plead with her for forgiveness.","cl":[["present suffering is the fruit of sin done in an earlier life","general","reported",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2510":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2510,"t":["பூவையுங் கிளியுங் கேட்டுப் புழைமுகம் வைத்து நோக்கிக்","காவலன் மடந்தை யுள்ளங் கற்கொலோ விரும்பு கொல்லோ","சாவம்யா முருகி யொன்றுந் தவறில னருளு நங்கை","பாவையென் றிரத்து மென்ற பறவைக டம்முட் டாமே."],"p":"","r":"The myna and the parrot, hearing, look through the cage-holes and say among themselves: is the king's daughter's heart stone or does it relent; we melt and the prince is faultless; let us beg our gracious lady.","n":"In line 3 the words சாவம் யாம் உருகி are doubtful; the sense of pleading for the faultless man is taken from the whole verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","anbu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2510","lb":"CC2510","mm":1,"ev":"The household parrot and myna, watching the quarrel, decide to plead for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["பூவையும் கிளியும்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பூவையும் கிளியும்",""],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2511":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2511,"t":["பெற்றகூ ழுண்டு நாளும் பிணியழந் திருத்தும் பேதாய்","முற்றிமை சொல்லி னங்கை மூன்றுநா ளடிசில் காட்டாள்","பொற்றொடி தத்தை யீரே பொத்துநும் வாயை யென்றே","கற்பித்தார் பூவை யார்தங் காரணக் கிளவி தம்மால்,"],"p":"","r":"The women, 'myna-like' with their reasoned words, scold the birds: you eat the food she gives every day and still dare to speak; if you say more she will show you no food for three days, so shut your mouths.","n":"The speaker is taken to be the household women (பூவையார்); the word may instead denote the myna, so the speaker is not certain. The birds are addressed as தத்தை (parrot).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","natpu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2511","lb":"CC2511","mm":1,"ev":"The women of the house silence the parrot and myna with the threat of withheld food.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","பூவையும் கிளியும்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்","பூவையும் கிளியும்"],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2512":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2512,"t":["பழியொடு மிடைந்த தேனுஞ் சீறடி பரவி னாற்கு","வழிபடு தெய்வ மாகி வரங்கொடுத் தருளல் வேண்டு","மொழிபடைக் களிறு போல வுயங்கவு முருகி நோக்காப்","பிழிசடு கோதை போலாம் பெண்டிரைக் கெடப்பி றந்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"A speech that, though stained with blame, he who has worshipped her small feet asks that she become the goddess he serves and grant the boon, and that she cease to be like a tied elephant that melts and does not look back.","n":"Line 4 (பிழிசடு கோதை போலாம் பெண்டிரைக் கெடப் பிறந்தாள்) does not parse and the speaker is inferred from the plea; the sense is not settled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","vazhipaadu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2512","lb":"CC2512","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan pleads with Ilakkanai in the language of worship.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2513":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2513,"t":["ஈன்றதா யானு மாக விதனைக்கண் டுயிரை வாழே","னான்றியான் சாவ லென்றே நலக்கிளி நூலின் யாப்ப","மான்றவண் மருண்டு நக்காள் வாழிய வரம்பெற் றேனென்","றான்றவ னாரப் புல்லி யணிநலம் பரவி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The parrot cries that even were it the bride's own mother it would not live to see this, and it will die, and ties itself with a thread; she, bewildered, laughs; he cries 'live long, I have won the boon', embraces her and praises her ornaments.","n":"The sulk ends only when a third party, the pet, stages a death; neither of the lovers yields first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2513","lb":"CC2513","mm":1,"ev":"The parrot's mock hanging breaks Ilakkanai's sulk and Civakan embraces her.","cl":[],"ag":["கிளி","இலக்கணை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கிளி","இலக்கணை"],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2514":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2514,"t":["நிறையோத நீர்நின்று நீடவமே செய்யினும் வாழி நீல","மறையோ விரிவை வரிநெடுங்க ணொக்கிலையால் வாழி நீலங்","கண்ணொவ்வா யேனுங் களித்து நகுதிநின்","வண்ண மிதுவோ மதுவுண்பார் சேரியையோ வாழி நீலம்."],"p":"","r":"Addressing the blue lily, he says it does not match her long dark eyes even if it stays in the flood in long penance, and asks whether its laughing colour is that, or the street of the wine-drinkers; long may the lily live.","n":"The refrain வாழி நீலம் can be taken as a blessing to the lily or as a name; the verse is read as his praise of her eyes against the flower.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2514","lb":"CC2514","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2515":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2515,"t":["பாண்குலாய்ப் படுக்கல் வேண்டா பைங்கிளி பூவை யென்னு","மாண்பிலா தாரை வைத்தா ரென்னுறா ரென்று நக்கு","நாண்குலாய்க் கிடந்த நங்கை நகைமுக வமுத மீந்தாள்","பூண்குலாய்க் கிடந்த மார்பிற் பொன்னெடுங் குன்ற னாற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The shy girl, laughing that the parrot and myna have blamed the faultless, gives the golden-chested lord the nectar of her smiling face, and no lute-song is needed to lay the quarrel to rest.","n":"Line 1 (பாண் குலாய்ப் படுக்கல் வேண்டா) is obscure and is read as 'no song need be sung to soothe her'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2515","lb":"CC2515","mm":1,"ev":"Ilakkanai laughs at the birds' scene and ends the sulk with a smile for Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2516":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2516,"t":["நலங்குவித் தனைய மாதர் நன்னல மாய வெல்லாம்","புலம்புவித் தருளி னீங்கிப் பகைப்புலம் புக்க வேந்திற்","கலங்குவித் தனைய நம்பி கவர்ந்திடக் கலாப மேங்கச்","சிலம்புநொந் திரங்கத் தேன்றார் பரிந்துதே னெழுந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Like a king who enters the hostile land and shakes it, the man shakes her beauty away; the peacock-like plume droops, her anklets weep and the honey-garland snaps so that honey runs.","n":"The conquering-king simile in line 2 and 3 casts lovemaking as invasion of a foe; it is the poet's, and it sits oddly beside the sulk just ended.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","madal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2516","lb":"CC2516","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan and Ilakkanai make love, her garland and anklets breaking.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2517":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2517,"t":["திருநிறக் காம வல்வி திருக்கவின் கொண்டு பூத்துப்","பெருநிறங் கவினி யார்ந்த கற்பகம் பிணைந்த தேபோ","லருநிறக் குருசின் மார்பத் தசைந்தன ளலங்கல் வேலு","நெரிபுறத் தடற்று வாளு நீலமு நிகர்த்த கண்ணாள்."],"p":"","r":"Her eyes like spear, sword and blue lily, she clings to his broad chest as the kalpaka tree entwines the creeper of love that has flowered and grown lovely.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2517","lb":"CC2517","mm":1,"ev":"Ilakkanai clings to Civakan's chest after the reconciliation.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2518":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2518,"t":["மணிக்கண் மாமயிற் சாயன் மாதரு","மணிக்கந் தன்னதோ ளரச சீயமும்","பிணித்த காதலாற் பின்னிச் செல்வழிக்","கணித்த நாள்களேழ் கழிந்த காலையே."],"p":"","r":"The peacock-like woman and the lion-like king, bound by love and twined together, let the seven appointed days go by.","n":"Line 2 (மணிக்கந் தன்னதோள்) is garbled in the edition; the seven days are clear from line 4.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2518","lb":"CC2518","mm":1,"ev":"The couple spend the seven days of the wedding together.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2519":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2519,"t":["சூட்டுஞ் சுண்ணமு மணிந்து சுந்தர","மோட்டி யொண்பொனூ லோங்கு தாரொடு","பூட்டிக் குண்டலம் பொற்பப் பெய்தபின்","மோட்டு முத்தொளிர் வடம்வ ளாயினார்."],"p":"","r":"They are anointed with sandal and powder and adorned with garland, gold thread and earrings, the pearl strings set about them.","n":"The edition prints strange forms here (மோட்டி, மோட்டு) and the subject of the plural verb is taken to be the two.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2519","lb":"CC2519","mm":1,"ev":"The pair dress for the wedding ceremony after the seven days.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","இலக்கணை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2520":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2520,"t":["பானு ரைய்யன பைந்து கிலணிந்","தானி ரைய்யினத் தலங்க லேறனான்","மானி ரைய்யினம் மருளு நோக்கினா","ரூனு யிருணு மொருவ னாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"He wears green silk, the garland on his chest and the line of ornaments, and to the deer-eyed women with their wondering look he becomes one who consumes their flesh and life together.","n":"The last line (ஊனு யிருணு மொருவ னாயினான்) is corrupt; the sense given is only a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2520","lb":"CC2520","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan dresses and is seen by the women.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2521":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2521,"t":["சுநந்தை தன்மகன் சுடர்பொற் சூழித்தே","னினங்க வர்ந்துண விலிற்று மும்மதத்","தநந்த னன்னகை யானை யேறினான்","குனிந்த சாமரை குளிர்சங் கார்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Sunandai's son, the bees drinking from the rutting streams of an elephant of three musths, mounts it while the bowed yak-tail fans and the conches sound.","n":"Civakan is named as Sunandai's son, the foster mother who raised him, in the very verse in which he rides as a king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2521","lb":"CC2521","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan mounts a state elephant with fans and conches.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2522":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2522,"t":["இரும்பிடி நூறு சூழ விறுவரை நின்ற தேபோற்","கரும்பொடு காய்நெற் றுற்றிக் கருப்புரக் கந்தி னின்ற","சுரும்புசூழ் மதத்த சூளா மணியெனுஞ் சூழி யானைப்","பெருந்தகைப் பிணையன் மன்னர் முடிமிதித் தேறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"On the elephant called Culamani, among a hundred cow elephants, laden with sugarcane and paddy, the garlanded king mounts treading on the crowns of kings.","n":"The phrase மன்னர் முடிமிதித்தேறினான் may mean the kings bow so that he mounts over their crowns, or that he rides over them; it is read as a figure for their submission.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2522","lb":"CC2522","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan rides the elephant Culamani, the other kings' crowns beneath him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2523":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2523,"t":["சட்டகம் பொன்னிற் செய்து தண்கதிர் வெள்ளி வேய்ந்து","வட்டநல் வைரம் வாய்ப்ப நிறைத்துமேன் மணிகள் சேர்த்திச்","சுட்டுதற் கரிய முத்தின் றொத்துவாய் நாற்ற முந்நீர்ப்","பட்டவான் பவளக் காம்பின் குடைநிழற் பருதி யொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"His umbrella of gold, silver, diamond and pearl hung with coral stems makes him look like the sun in its shade.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2523","lb":"CC2523","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2524":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2524,"t":["மடற்பனைக் குழாத்திற் பிச்ச நிரைத்தன மன்னர் சூழ்ந்து","புடைக்களி றேறித் திங்கட் பொழிகதிர்க் குப்பை யன்ன","வெடுத்தெறி கவரி வீச வியம்பல முழங்கி யார்ப்பக்","கடற்படை வெள்ளஞ் சூழக் காவலன் வீதி சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The kings crowd round with whisks while he rides an elephant like the moon pouring beams, with drums and shouts, and the king enters the street with his sea of troops.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2524","lb":"CC2524","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan, with the kings about him, enters the street in procession.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2525":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2525,"t":["அடிநில முறுத னாணி யருவருத் தமரி னாலித்","திடுமயிர் சிறக ராக வெழுந்துமேற் பறப்ப போலப்","படுமழைத் துளியிற் பாய்மாப் பரந்தன நிரந்த பொற்றே","ரிடைநில மின்றி வேழ மீண்டின மள்ளர் தொக்கார்."],"p":"","r":"Horses spread like rain, golden chariots fill the ground and elephants and warriors crowd without a gap.","n":"Line 1-2, with the horse's hooves shamed by the ground and the hair flying like wings, is a stock simile and its grammar is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2525","lb":"CC2525","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2526":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2526,"t":["கொழுமடற் பெண்ணை யீன்ற குரும்பையுஞ் செப்புங் கொன்ற","விழைமுலைத் தடத்தி னாடன் கணவனைக் காண வேகிக்","கழுமொலி யரவ வானங் கனைபெயல் கடற்பெய் தன்ன","குழுமொலி யரவ மீண்டிக் கொடிநகர் பொலிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The city of banners is loud like the sea that takes the rain as the women who have killed the palmyra's shoots with their breasts go to see her husband.","n":"The subject in lines 1 and 2 is difficult and the identity of the woman who is said to go to see her husband is not settled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2526","lb":"CC2526","mm":1,"ev":"The people go to see the bridegroom as the city roars.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:108"},"cc:2527":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2527,"t":["ஒள்ளிலைச் சூலந் தெண்ணீ ருலாமுகில் கிழிக்கு மாடக்","கொள்கொடிக் குழாத்தி னாலுங் கொழுநறும் புகையி னாலுந்","தெள்ளுறு சுண்ணத் தாலுந் தேமலர்த் துகளி னாலும்","புள்ளினம் பொழுது காணா புலம்பிக்கூ டடைந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The street is so thick with banner-staffs, perfumed smoke, fine powder and flower-pollen that the birds can no longer tell the time of day and gather in distress.","n":"The scene opens the crowding of the city around the wedding day; nothing here names who is coming, so it is setting only.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2527","lb":"CC2527","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2528":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2528,"t":["பைந்தொடி மகளிர் பாங்கர் பரிந்துநூல் சொரிந்த காசு","சிந்தின தழலென் றஞ்சிச் சிறையன்ன நிலத்தைச் சேரா","விந்திர கோப மாமென் றிளமயில் குனிந்து குத்திச்","சிந்தையிற் றேம்பத் தாமே திருமணி நக்க வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Gems fall from the coin-strings the bangled women have loosed, and a young peahen, taking them for fire or for red velvet mites, shrinks from the ground and pecks at them till the jewels seem to laugh at her.","n":"The peahen's mistake is the poet's proof of how much jewellery has been scattered: loss of ornament is shown as a bird's confusion, not a woman's grief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2528","lb":"CC2528","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2529":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2529,"t":["வெள்ளைமை கலந்த நோக்கிற கிண்கிணி மிழற்றி யார்ப்பப்","பிள்ளைமை காதல் கூரப் பிறழ்ந்துபொற் றோடு வீழத்","துள்ளுபு செலபுஇய தோற்றந் தொடுகழற் காமன் காமத்","துள்ளுயி ரறியப் பெண்ணாய்ப் பிறந்ததோர் தோற்ற மொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Girls with childish, innocent looks, anklets ringing and gold ear-ornaments slipping as they run, seem Kama himself born as a woman so that his heart could know desire.","n":"The simile makes the girls the god of love's own experiment: he becomes female in order to feel what his arrows cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2529","lb":"CC2529","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2530":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2530,"t":["தன்னெறி வளரக் காமன் றான்முலை யிரண்டு மாகி","முன்னரே வளர்கின் றாற்போன் முகிழ்முலை முத்த மேந்திப்","பொன்னெறி மணியிற் பொங்கிக் குழல்புறம் புடைப்ப வோடிப்","பின்னிறீஇ வைத்த போலப் பெதும்பையர் விதும்பி நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The budding girls, their pearl-strung young breasts swelling as though Kama himself were growing them, stand hurrying forward, their hair bulging behind, as if set there for show.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2530","lb":"CC2530","mm":1,"ev":"The young girls of the city press forward to see.","cl":[],"ag":["பெதும்பையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2531":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2531,"t":["அணிநிலா வீசு மாலை யரங்குபுல் லென்னப் போகித்","துணிநிலா வீசு மாலைப் பிறைநுதற் றோழி சேர்ந்து","மணிநிலா வீசு மாலை மங்கையர் மயங்கி நின்றார்","பணிநிலா வீசும் பைம்பொற் கொடிமணி மலர்ந்த தொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The long evening, with its moonlight, leaves the stage empty; the women, joined by friends with crescent brows, stand bewildered in the jewel-lit dusk like a golden creeper hung with bright gems.","n":"Mayakkam (bewilderment) here is the desire the sight is about to cause; the verse prepares for the women's eager speeches in the last four verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","madal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2531","lb":"CC2531","mm":1,"ev":"Women gather in the evening, bewildered, to see the procession.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2532":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2532,"t":["வள்ளுகிர் வரித்த சாந்தின் வனமுலை நோக்கி னாரை","யுள்ளுயி ருண்ணுங் கூற்றி னுடன்றுகண் கரிந்து பொங்கக்","கள்ளுயி ருண்ணு மாலைக் கதுப்பொரு கையி னேந்தி","நள்ளிருள் விளக்கிட் டன்ன நங்கைமார் மல்கி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Women with nail-marked sandal-paste on their breasts, their eyes burning and darkening at those who look at them, hold their wreathed hair aside with one hand and fill the street like lamps lit in the middle of the night.","n":"The hostile figure of Death eating life, borrowed for their glance, turns an angry eye into an erotic one, as the Kural does for a woman's eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2532","lb":"CC2532","mm":1,"ev":"Women crowd the street, lit with jewels.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2533":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2533,"t":["மட்டொளித் துண்ணு மாந்தர் மாண்புபோன் மறைந்து வண்ணப்","பட்டொளித் தொழிய வல்குற் பசுங்கதிர்க் கலாபந் தோன்றக்","குட்டநீர்க் குவளைக் கண்கள் விருந்துண விரும்பி நின்றா","ரட்டுந்தே னணிந்த மாலைப் பவளக்கொம் பணிந்த தொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Women hide their silk and show their jewelled girdles as drinkers hide their cups, and stand eager to give their blue-lily eyes a feast of the sight, like a coral branch hung with honeyed garlands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2533","lb":"CC2533","mm":1,"ev":"Women wait with eyes ready for the sight.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2534":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2534,"t":["பெரும்பொரு ணீதிச் செங்கோற் பெருமக னாக்கம் போலப்","பரந்திட மின்றி மேலாற் படாமுலை குவிந்த கீழா","லரும்பொரு ணீதி கேளா வரசனிற் சுருங்கி நந்து","மருங்குநொந் தொழிய வீதி மடந்தைய ரிடங்கொண் டாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women make room in the street by stepping back, their breasts firm and not sagging, like the wealth of a just ruler that has no free space above and shrinks below, or like a king who will not hear justice.","n":"The sceptre doctrine is not the subject; it is the measure of a breast's firmness. The line shows a poet could assume a just king and an unjust king as a common yardstick for shape.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2534","lb":"CC2534","mm":1,"ev":"Women step back and take places along the street.","cl":[["a king who will not hear justice shrinks and loses what he has, while a just ruler's wealth fills all space","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2535":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2535,"t":["செல்வர்க்கே சிறப்புச் செய்யுந் திருந்துநீர் மாந்தர் போல","வல்குற்கு முலைக்கு மீந்தா ரணிகல மாய வெல்லா","நல்கூர்ந்தார்க் கில்லை சுற்ற மென்றுநுண் ணுசுப்பு நைய","வொல்கிப்போய் மாடஞ் சேர்ந்தா ரொருதடங் குடங்கைக் கண்ணார்."],"p":"","r":"As people honour only the rich, the women give their jewels only to hips and breasts, saying the poor have no kin, and withdraw with waists worn thin into the houses, a pair of big-eyed women among them.","n":"The world's partiality towards wealth is used as a simile for the way the body's rich parts take the jewels while the waist is neglected.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2535","lb":"CC2535","mm":1,"ev":"Women return to their houses, leaving the waist unadorned.","cl":[["people honour those with wealth and abandon the poor as kinless","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2536":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2536,"t":["கார்வளர் மின்னு வீசுங் குண்டலங் காய்பொ னோலை","யேர்வளர் பட்ட மேற்ப வணிந்திருள் சுமந்து திங்க","ணீர்வளர் நீலம் பூத்து நிரைத்தபோ னிரைத்த மேலால்","வார்வளர் முலையி னார்த மாழைவாண் முகங்கண் மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Ear-rings bright as cloud-lightning, gold ear-leaves and a forehead ornament frame the faces of the full-breasted women, who look like a moon bearing darkness with blue lilies in a pool set in rows above.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2536","lb":"CC2536","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2537":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2537,"t":["குறையணி கொண்ட வாறே கோதைகா றொடர வோடிச்","சிறையழி செம்பொ னுந்தித் தேன்பொழிந் தொழுக வேந்திப்","பறையிசை வண்டு பாடப் பாகமே மறைய நின்றார்","பிறையணி கொண்ட வண்ணல் பெண்ணொர்பால் கொண்ட தொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"With their garlands dragging, wreaths half arranged, honey pouring from gold-edged buds as bees drone, the women stand half hidden, like the crescent-crowned god taking a woman into one half of himself.","n":"Civakan's own faith is Jain; the simile uses the Saiva Ardhanarisvara figure, a poet's image without doctrinal claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2537","lb":"CC2537","mm":1,"ev":"Women stand half-hidden behind the crowd.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2538":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2538,"t":["பொன்னரி மாலை பூண்டு பூஞ்சிகை குலாவி முன்கை","மின்னரிச் சிலம்பு தொட்டு விருப்பொடு விரைந்து போவான்","கன்னிய ராடி நோக்கித் தம்மைத்தாங் கண்டு நாணிப்","பின்னவை யணிந்து செல்வா ரிடம்பெறா தொழிந்து போனார்."],"p":"","r":"A man hurrying forward with his gold-streaked garland, flower-topped hair and anklets is watched by maidens who, after looking at themselves in mirrors, grow ashamed, put on more adornments, and fail to find a place.","n":"The hurrying man is probably Civakan himself, but the verse does not name him; the sense of who is seen by whom turns on the unclear subject of போவான்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2538","lb":"CC2538","mm":1,"ev":"Maidens look in mirrors, grow ashamed, and put on more jewels, but find no room in the crowd.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2539":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2539,"t":["முத்துலாய் நடந்த கோல முலைமுதன் முற்ற மெல்லாம்","வித்திய வேங்கை வீயும் விழுப்பொனும் விளங்கக் காமத்","தொத்துநின் றெரிந்து கண்டார் கண்சுடச் சுடர்ந்து நின்றா","ரொத்தொளிர் காம வல்லி யொருங்குபூத் துதிர்ந்த தொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Pearls swinging on their breasts, the courtyards before their houses strewn with gold, and the women glowing so that the sight burns the eyes of those who see them, like a desire-creeper flowering all at once and shedding.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2539","lb":"CC2539","mm":1,"ev":"Women stand blazing with jewels in the courtyards.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2540":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2540,"t":["உகிர்வினை செய்து பஞ்சி யொள்ளொளி யரத்த மூட்டி","யகில்கம ழங்கை சேப்ப வரிவைய ரலங்க றாங்கி","வகிர்படு மழைக்கண் சின்னீர் மாக்கய லெதிர்ந்த வேபோன்","முகில்கிழி மின்னி னோக்கி முரிந்திடை குழைந்து நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with nails reddened with lac and fingers glowing, arms perfumed with aloe, stand bending at the waist under their jewels, their eyes looking like carp glancing at the lightning in the cloud.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2540","lb":"CC2540","mm":1,"ev":"Women stand dressed, waists bent, looking.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2541":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2541,"t":["முனித்தலைக் கண்ணி நெற்றிச் சிறார்முலை முழாலிற் பில்கிப்","புனிற்றுப்பால் பிலிற்றித் தேமா வடுவிறுத் தாங்குப் பாய","நுனிந்துக்கண் ணரக்கி நோக்கா தொசிந்துநின் றார்க ளன்றே","கனிப்பொறை மலிந்து நின்ற கற்பகப் பூங்கொம் பொத்தார்"],"p":"","r":"Girls with milk still springing from their breasts, as a mango's juice springs from a broken unripe fruit, stand with narrowed painted eyes, not looking, bent like a wishing-tree branch heavy with fruit.","n":"The verse's picture of milk springing from the girls' breasts is obscure in line 1; the sense is of fullness, not nursing in a plot sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2541","lb":"CC2541","mm":1,"ev":"Women stand bent, not looking straight.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2542":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2542,"t":["அவிரிழை சுடர முல்லை யலங்கலங் கூந்தல் சோரத்","தவிர்வெய்ய காமந் தாங்கித் தடமுலைக் கால்கள் சாய","விவர்தரு பிறவி யெல்லா மின்னமா கென்று நின்றார்","சுவர்செய்தாங் கெழுதப் பட்ட துகிலிகைப் பாவை யொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"With bright ornaments flashing, jasmine garlands slipping from their hair and their full breasts sinking under the load of desire, the women stand wishing that all their births to come be like this, like painted dolls on a wall.","n":"The wish for all births to be as this one treats rebirth as given and wants to repeat it, not to escape it, the opposite of the work's Jain end in release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","piravi","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2542","lb":"CC2542","mm":1,"ev":"Women, overcome by desire, wish for the same lot in every birth.","cl":[["births recur, and a woman may wish her future births to repeat a present joy","general","reported",3,"nagar_magalir"]],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2543":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2543,"t":["வேரிநா றலங்கன் மாலை மின்னிழை மயங்கி யெங்கும்","பூரித்துப் புதவந் தோறுங் குவளையு மரையும் பூத்துப்","பாரித்துப் பைம்பொ னாகருலகிவண் வீழ்ந்த தேபோன்","மாரிமா மயில னாரு மைந்தரு மயங்கி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Women with bright garlands and fine peacock-like looks, and young men, are all in confusion together, as if a golden world of nagas had fallen here, blossoming blue lilies and red lotuses in every gate.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2543","lb":"CC2543","mm":1,"ev":"Women and young men mingle, confused.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2544":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2544,"t":["கோதைதாழ் குடையி னீழற் கொற்றவன் பருதி யாக","மாதரார் முகங்க ளென்னுந் தாமரை மலர்ந்த தெண்ணீர்க்","காதநான் ககன்ற பொய்கைக் கடிநகர் குவளை பூத்துப்","பேதுறு கின்ற போன்ற பெருமழைக் கண்கண் மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"With the king who wears a hanging-garland umbrella as the sun, the women's faces open like lotuses in a clear pond four leagues wide, and the great eyes seem like blue lilies dazed in the fortress-city.","n":"The king as sun making women's faces open is a court image of the king as the one who causes the city to bloom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2544","lb":"CC2544","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2545":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2545,"t":["மாந்தரு மாவுஞ் செல்ல மயங்கிமே லெழுந்த நீறு","தேந்தரு கோதை யார்தந் தெண்மட்டுத் துவலை மாற்ற","வாய்ந்தபொன் னகர மெங்கு மணிகல வொளியி னாலே","காய்ந்துகண் கலக்கப் பூத்த கற்பக மொத்த தன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Dust raised by men and elephants moving in confusion is laid by the honey-drops from the women's garlands, and the golden city, glittering with jewels, seems to flower like a wishing tree.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2545","lb":"CC2545","mm":1,"ev":"The women's garland-drops lay the dust of the passing procession.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2546":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2546,"t":["பெண்பெற்ற பொலிசை பெற்றார் பிணையனார் பெரிய யாமுங்","கண்பெற்ற பொலிசை பெற்றா மின்றெனக் கரைந்து முந்நீர்","மண்பெற்ற வாயுள் பெற்று மன்னுவாய் மன்ன வென்னாப்","புண்பெற்ற வேலி னான்மேற் பூமழை தூவி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women say that Pinaiyanar has gained a daughter's wealth and that they themselves have gained eyes this day, and shower flowers on the spear-wounded hero, wishing him a life long as the sea-girt earth.","n":"Who Pinaiyanar is - the bride's father or another - the verse does not say; it is a doubtful name for a speaker or object of praise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","aram","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2546","lb":"CC2546","mm":1,"ev":"The women bless Civakan and shower flowers on him as he passes.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2547":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2547,"t":["சுண்ணமேற் சொரிவார் தொழுதுதொங்கல் வீழ்ப்பார்","தண்ணென் சந்தனநீ ரார்ந்துதேன் றுளும்பும்","வண்ணப்பந் தெறிவார் வளையொலிப்ப வோச்சிக்","கண்ணியிட் டெறிவார் கலவைநீர் தெளிப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The women pour powder over him, bow and drop garlands, throw balls full of sandal water and honey, hurl garlands that are tied into wreaths, and sprinkle scented water as he passes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2547","lb":"CC2547","mm":1,"ev":"The women pelt Civakan with powder, balls and garlands as a mark of welcome.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2548":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2548,"t":["முந்துசூர் தடிந்த முருகனம்பி யென்பா","ரைந்துருவ வம்பி னநங்கனென் றயர்வார்","கந்துகன் வளர்த்த சிங்கங்காண்மி னென்பார்","சிந்தையிற் களிப்பார் சேணெடிய கண்ணார்."],"p":"","r":"The women say he is Murugan's younger brother, Anangan with five arrows, a lion fed by Kandukkan; they are glad in their hearts, their long eyes fixed on him.","n":"The comparison with Murugan echoes the kings' battle cry in 812, but then it was fear of the one who beat them; here it is longing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2548","lb":"CC2548","mm":1,"ev":"The women name him in praise as Murugan's brother, Kama and a reared lion.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2549":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2549,"t":["தேசிக முடியுந் திருந்துபட் டுடையும்","பாசமாக நின்று பன்மலர்க் கழுநீர்","மூசிவண் டிமிரும் மொய்யலங்க றாழக்","காசில் காமஞ்செப்பிக் கண்ணினா லிரப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"With turbans and silks slipping so they hang as ropes, and flowers and garlands falling, the women speak a flawless desire and beg him with their eyes.","n":"Lines 1-2 are hard to construe: தேசிகம் and பாசம் may name the headdress and ropes of cloth; the begging with the eyes is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2549","lb":"CC2549","mm":1,"ev":"The women beg Civakan with their eyes, their clothes slipping.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2550":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2550,"t":["வண்டறைந்த தாரான் வண்ணங்கண்ட பின்றைக்","கண்டிலேனென் மாமை கைவளையொ டென்பா","ரொண்டொடி யிவன்றன் னுருவுகண்டு வாழ்வார்","பெண்டிராய்ப் பிறந்தார் பெரியர்போத வென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"After seeing the garlanded one's looks, they say they have lost their own bloom with their bangles, and live seeing his form, praising the women who were born as women as great.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2550","lb":"CC2550","mm":1,"ev":"The women declare that they have lost their bloom and bangles, and live by gazing at him.","cl":[["a woman is great who is born to see such a man, and loses her bloom for desire","general","reported",4,"nagar_magalir"]],"ag":["மாதரார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:109"},"cc:2551":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2551,"t":["கொழித்திரை யோத வேலிக் குமரனைப் பயந்த நங்கை","விழுத்தவ முலக மெல்லாம் விளக்கிநின் றிட்ட தென்பார்","பிழிப்பொலி கோதை போலாம் பெண்டிரிற் பெரிய ணோற்றாள்","கழித்துநின் றறாத கற்பிற் சுநந்தையே யாக வென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The women of the city say that the woman who bore the prince has by her great austerity lit up the whole world, and that the greatest vow among women must be Sunandai's, whose chastity never broke.","n":"The verse echoes the street talk in 465, where the people ask what penance Civakan's mother did. Whether the first woman (the one who bore him) and Sunandai (who raised him) are one or two is not settled by the words, so the praise may belong to either.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","karpu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2551","lb":"CC2551","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a mother's great austerity shows in the glory of her son","general","reported",2,"nagar_magalir"],["the greatest vow among women is a chaste wife's unbroken steadfastness","general","reported",4,"nagar_magalir"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரார்",""],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2552":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2552,"t":["சாந்தகங் கிழிய மாலைத் தடமுலை ஞெமுங்கப் புல்லிச்","சேர்ந்தெழு நங்கை மாரே திருநங்கை மார்க ளல்லார்","கூந்தலு முலையு முத்துங் கோதையுஞ் சுமந்து நைவான்","போந்தவந் நங்கை மார்கள் பொய்ந்நங்கை மார்க ளென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The women say that those women who press against him, crushing their sandalled breasts and garlands as they cling, are not truly fortunate women but false ones, bearing hair, breasts, pearls and garlands only to waste away.","n":"It is unclear whether the speakers mock rival women or pity them; the sense of the last two lines (போந்த அந்நங்கைமார்கள் பொய்ந்நங்கைமார்கள்) turns on that.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2552","lb":"CC2552","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரார்",""],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2553":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2553,"t":["இடம்பட வகன்று நீண்ட விருமலர்த் தடங்க ணென்னுங்","குடங்கையி னொண்டு கொண்டு பருகுவார் குவளைக் கொம்பி","னுடம்பெலாங் கண்க ளாயி னொருவர்க்கு மின்றி யேற்ப","வடங்கவாய் வைத்திட் டாரப் பருகியிட் டீமி னென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The women urge one another to drink in his beauty, their wide dark eyes cupped like palms, each taking her fill so that none is left out.","n":"The third line sets a vine-woman whose whole body became eyes against the sharing; the exact turn of the thought is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2553","lb":"CC2553","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரார்",""],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2554":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2554,"t":["முலைமுத றுறத்த வன்றே மூரித்தா ளாளி யானைத்","தலைநிலம் புரள வெண்கோ டுண்டதே போன்று தன்கைச்","சிலையிடம் பிடித்த ஞான்றே தெவ்வரைச் செகுத்த நம்பி","நிலவுமிழ் குடையி னீழற் றுஞ்சுக வைய மென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The women say that from the day he left the breast, as a lion tears the tusks from the head of a fallen elephant, he slew his foes the moment he took up the bow, and that the world may now sleep in the shade of his moon-bright umbrella.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","arasu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2554","lb":"CC2554","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரார்",""],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2555":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2555,"t":["இந்நகரப் புறங்காட்டி லிவன்பிறந்த வாறுந்","தன்னிகரில் வாணிகனிற் றான்வளர்ந்த வாறுங்","கைந்நிகரில் வேந்தர்தொழப் போந்ததுவுங் கண்டா","லென்னைதவஞ் செய்யா திகழ்ந்திருப்ப தென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"They say that anyone who sees how he was born in this city's burning ground, how he grew up as a matchless merchant's son, and how kings have come to worship him must ask why one should live heedless instead of doing penance.","n":"Civakan's rise from the burning ground (304) is made here into an argument for tapas, in the epic's own mouth, before the last ilampakam where he does take it up. The subject of the last line (the city folk, or each hearer, or Civakan himself) is not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","nilaiyaamai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2555","lb":"CC2555","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a life that has turned as fortune turns should lead one to take up penance and not live heedless","general","reported",4,"nagar_makkal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["என்மரும்",""],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2556":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2556,"t":["பெருமுழங்கு திரைவரைக ணீந்திப்பிணி யுறினுந்","திருமுயங்க லில்லையெனி னில்லைபொரு ளீட்ட","மொரு முழமுஞ் சேறலில ரேனும் பொரு ளூர்க்கே","வரும்வழிவி னாயுழந்து வாழ்கதவ மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"The speakers say that, however a man crosses roaring seas and mountains and suffers sickness, wealth does not come without fortune's favour, while it may come home to those who go not a cubit; so let tapas be taken up with effort.","n":"The Tamil of lines 3 and 4 (the words after ஒருமுழமும் சேறலிலரேனும்) is hard, and the verse's speaker is taken from the neighbouring verses; read it as a loose argument from fortune to tapas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam","thavam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2556","lb":"CC2556","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["gain of wealth depends on fortune, not on travel and toil","general","reported",2,"nagar_makkal"],["tapas should be practised in preference to toil for wealth","general","reported",4,"nagar_makkal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["என்மரும்",""],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2557":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2557,"t":["நஞ்சு குடித் தாலுநவை யின்றுதவ நின்றா","லஞ்சியொளித் தாலுமா ணில்லைதவ முலந்தாற்","குஞ்சரத்தின் கோட்டிடையு முய்வர்தவ மிக்கா","ரஞ்சலில ரென்றுமற னேகளைக ணென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"They say that one strong in tapas takes no harm even from poison and escapes even from between a mighty elephant's tusks, whereas once tapas is spent no hiding in fear saves; so the fearless, with dharma as their refuge, are those rich in tapas.","n":"The words ஆணில்லை and களைகண் are rare, and both the grammar of line 2 and the sense of the closing phrase are read from the whole stanza.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aram","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2557","lb":"CC2557","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["tapas protects from poison and the tusker, and when it is spent no flight saves","general","reported",3,"nagar_makkal"],["dharma itself is the refuge of the fearless who are strong in tapas","general","reported",4,"nagar_makkal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["என்மரும்",""],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2558":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2558,"t":["முரல்வாய சூற்சங்க முடமுட் டாழை முகைவிம் முங்","கரைவாய முத்தீன்று கானன் மேயுங் கடற்சேர்ப்ப","னுரைவாய் நகர்பரவப் போகி யொண்பொ னெயிழ்சூழ்ந்த","விரைவாய் பூம்பிண்டி வேந்தன் கோயிற் கெழுந்தானே."],"p":"","r":"Passing through the city amid its praise, the lord of the shore goes to the temple of the king of the flowering pindi tree, which the verse sets round with pearl-bearing conches and screwpine on the sea's edge.","n":"The one who goes is called கடற்சேர்ப்பன் (lord of the shore), an unusual title for Civakan; he is taken as Civakan because the next verses show him worship and endow the temple. The pindi (asoka) tree is the Jina's tree.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2558","lb":"CC2558","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan goes in procession to the Jina's temple.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2559":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2559,"t":["அருகு மயில்கவ வன்ன மேங்கக் குயில்கூவக்","குருகு பொறையுயிர்க்குங் கொடுமுட் டாழை வெண்டோட்டு","முருகு பொறையுயிர்க்கு மொய்பூங் காவிற் படைநீக்கித்","திருகு கனைகழலான் செம்பொற் கோயில் சேர்ந்தானே."],"p":"","r":"In the flowery grove, where peacocks dance, swans cry and koels call, he puts aside his weapons and arrives at the golden temple of the one with the twisted, resounding anklet.","n":"The verse does not say why he lays down arms; the step before the shrine reads as a mark of reverence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2559","lb":"CC2559","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan sets aside his guard and reaches the golden temple.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2560":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2560,"t":["திறந்த மணிக்கதவந் திசைக ளெல்லா மணந்தேக்கி","மறைந்த வகிற்புகையான் மன்னர் மன்னன் வலஞ்செய்து","பிறந்தே னினிப்பிறவேன் பிறவா தாயைப் பெற்றேனென்","றிறைஞ்சி முடிதுளக்கி யேத்திக் கையாற் றொழுதானே."],"p":"","r":"Circling the gem-doored shrine thick with aloe smoke, the king of kings says that he has been born, will not be born again, and has found the one who is not born; then bows, shakes his head in praise and worships with his hands.","n":"The claim is made by the hero in worship and rests on reaching the unborn one; the verse does not say the Jina acts to free him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","veedu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2560","lb":"CC2560","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan circles the Jina's shrine and worships, declaring that his births end.","cl":[["one who comes to the feet of the unborn one is not born again","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2561":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2561,"t":["திருமறு மார்பினை திலகமுக் குடையினை","யருமறை தாங்கிய வந்தணர் தாதையை","யருமறை தாங்கிய வந்தணர் தாதைநின்","னெரிபுரை மரைமல ரிணையடி தொழுதும்."],"p":"","r":"They hymn the one with the sacred mark on his chest and the three-umbrella crest, father of the sages who bear the rare scripture, and say they worship his fire-bright lotus feet.","n":"The stanza is built by repeating its second line's words in the third (as 2562 and 2563 do), a hymn form and not a text fault; the 'we' (தொழுதும்) leaves it open whether Civakan sings alone or with his company.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2561","lb":"CC2561","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina is the father of the sages who hold the rare scripture","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2562":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2562,"t":["உலகுணர் கடவுளை யுருகெழு திறலினை","நிலவிரி கதிரணி நிகரறு நெறியினை","நிலவிரி கதிரணி நிகரறு நெறியைநின்","னலர்கெழு மரைமல ரடியிணை தொழுதும்."],"p":"","r":"They hymn the god who knows the world, of fearful might, whose path is unequalled in its moon-bright ornament, and worship his lotus feet.","n":"Built like 2561, with the second line's words repeated in the third.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2562","lb":"CC2562","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina knows the whole world and his way has no equal","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2563":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2563,"t":["மறுவற வுணர்ந்தனை மலமறு திகிரியை","பொறிவரம் பாகிய புண்ணிய முதல்வனை","பொறிவரம் பாகிய புண்ணிய முதல்வநின்","னறைவிரி மரைமலர் நகுமடி தொழுதும்."],"p":"","r":"They hymn the one who understood without fault, the wheel that removes stain, the lord of merit who set bounds to the senses, and worship his fragrant lotus feet.","n":"Built like 2561, with the second line's words repeated in the third.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2563","lb":"CC2563","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina, stainless and master of the senses, is the first of merit","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2564":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2564,"t":["நந்தா விளக்குப் புறமாகென நான்கு கோடி","நொந்தார்க் கடந்தான் கொடுத்தான் பின்னை நூறு மூதூர்","கந்தார் கடாத்த களிறுங்கொடித் தேர்க ணூறுஞ்","செந்தா மரைமே னடந்தானடி சேர்த்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan lays at the feet of the one who walked on the red lotus an endowment of four crore, a hundred old towns, rutting tuskers and a hundred flagged chariots, that the lamp which never fails may be kept.","n":"The opening of line 2 (நொந்தார்க் கடந்தான் கொடுத்தான்) can be read as a gift to the afflicted or as a clause about the foe-conqueror; and 'புறம்' for the lamp is read as its endowment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vazhipaadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2564","lb":"CC2564","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan endows the Jina's temple with gold, towns, elephants and chariots.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2565":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2565,"t":["வாடாத மாலை மணிமாலைபொன் மாலை முத்த","நீடார மாலை நிழன்மாண்ட பவழ மாலை","மாடார்ந் திழியு மருவிம்மலர் பொற்ப வேற்றிக்","கூடார்க் கடந்தான் வலங்கொண்டிடஞ் சென்று புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"He offers garlands that never fade, of gems, gold, pearl and coral, hung so that they fall like streams, then the foe-conqueror circles the shrine and goes on to his own place.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2565","lb":"CC2565","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan offers garlands, circles the shrine and goes on.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2566":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2566,"t":["உலமரு நெஞ்சி னொட்டா மன்னவ ரூர்ந்த யானை","வலமருப் பீர்ந்து செய்த மணிகிளர் கட்டி லேறி","நிலமகள் கணவன் வேந்தர் குழாத்திடை நிவந்தி ருந்தான்","புலமகள் புகழப் பொய்தீர் பூமகட் புணர்ந்து மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Seated on a couch of gems made from the right tusks of the elephants his enemies rode, the lord of the earth sits raised among the kings, praised by the goddess of learning and joined to faultless Fortune.","n":"The couch turns the defeated kings' own elephants into his seat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2566","lb":"CC2566","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan sits among the kings on a couch made of the enemy's elephant tusks.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2567":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2567,"t":["எத்துணைத் தவஞ்செய் தான்கொ லென்றெழுந் துலக மேத்த","வித்திய புகழி னாற்கு விருந்தர சியற்றி நாடு","மொத்தன னல்கித் தன்னை யுழந்தனள் வளர்த்த தாய்க்குச்","சித்திரத் தேவிப் பட்டந் திருமக னல்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"While the world rises to ask what penance the man must have done, he gives a feast of rule and a share of land to the one of famed repute, and gives the Chittiratevi title to the mother who toiled to raise him.","n":"The world's cry 'what penance did he do!' recalls the Kural's phrase for a son's repayment to his father (kural:70), but with different words; the verse does not name who receives the feast or which mother is meant (Sunandai is the nurse named in 2551).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","nanri","makkal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2567","lb":"CC2567","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan gives land and rule to the famed one and the queen's title to the mother who raised him.","cl":[["a son repays the one who raised him in rank and land","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2568":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2568,"t":["இனக்களி யானை மன்ன ரிளவுடை யானென் றேத்தத்","தனக்கிளை யானை நாட்டித் தான்றனக் கென்று கூறிச்","சினக்களி யானை மன்னர் மகளிரைச் சேர்த்தி நம்பன்","மனக்கினி துறைக வென்று வளங்கெழு நாடு மீந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"At the kings' praise he installs his younger brother as the heir, calls him his own, joins him to kings' daughters and gives him a rich land, bidding him live as he pleases.","n":"The title இளவுடையான் is read as heir or the younger one; the brother is Nandattan only by inference from the story.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","sutram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2568","lb":"CC2568","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan sets up his younger brother with a kingdom and royal brides.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2569":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2569,"t":["ஆழ்கடல் வையத் தில்லா வருநிதி யரசு நல்ல","சூழ்மணி யாழி செம்பொற் சூட்டொடு கண்ணி காதற்","றோழர்கட் கருளித் தொல்லை யுழந்தவர் தம்மைத் தோன்ற","வாழ்கென நிதியு நாடு மன்னவன் கொடுப்பித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The king gives his dear friends, who suffered long with him, a rule and treasure unmatched on earth, with gem ring, gold crest and garland, and bids them live.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","natpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2569","lb":"CC2569","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan gives his companions treasure and a country.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2570":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2570,"t":["வளர்த்தகைத் தாயர் தம்மை வருகென வருளித் தங்கள்","கிளைக்கெலாஞ் சிறப்புச் செய்து கேட்டவர் மருள வைந்தூர்","விளைத்துள கெடாத வைக லாயிர மிறுப்புத் தண்டக்","கொளக்கொடுத் தயா வுயிர்த்தான் கொற்றவ னென்ப வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He calls the nurses who reared him, honours their kin, and to the amazement of the hearers gives five villages and a thousand days of produce with the right to levy dues, and the king breathes easy.","n":"The last two lines turn on இறுப்புத் தண்டக் கொள; the grant is read as the right to collect dues.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","nanri","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2570","lb":"CC2570","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan rewards the nurses and their families with villages and dues.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2571":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2571,"t":["கைத்தல மந்தி கொண்ட கைமகப் போன்று தன்கட்","பத்திமை விடாது மேனாட் படைக்கல நவின்ற பொற்றேர்","மைத்துன மன்னர்க் கெல்லாம் வளநிதி மணிசெய் மான்றேர்","தத்துநீர் மிசைச்சென் மாவுந் தவழ்மதக் களிறு மீந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"To the kings of his marriage-kin, who clung to him in devotion like the young held by a monkey and had trained with him in arms, he gives gold chariots, wealth, gem-wrought horse chariots, sea-going horses and rutting elephants.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","anbu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2571","lb":"CC2571","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan gives chariots, horses and elephants to his kinsmen kings.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2572":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2572,"t":["கோமகன் கோல மான்றேர்க் கோவிந்த னென்னுங் கொய்தார்","மாமற்கு மடங்க லாற்றற் கட்டியங் கார னென்ற","தீமக னுடைய வெல்லாந் தேர்ந்தனன் கொடுத்துச் செல்வ","னோவலில் கறவை யொத்தா னுலோகபா லற்கு மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Having picked out all that belonged to Kattiyangaran, the wicked son of lion-like strength, he gives it to his uncle Govindan, king of fine chariots, and stays like an inexhaustible cow even to the guardian of the world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","sutram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2572","lb":"CC2572","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan gives the usurper's goods to his uncle Govindan.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2573":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2573,"t":["பேரிடர் தன்க ணீக்கிப் யாய தோழற்","கோரிடஞ் செய்து பொன்னா லவனுரு வியற்றி யூரும்","பாரிடம் பரவ நாட்டி யவனது சரிதை யெல்லாந்","தாருடை மார்பன் கூத்துத் தான்செய்து நடாயி னானே."],"p":"","r":"For the friend who lifted a great trouble from him he makes a place, has his likeness wrought in gold and set up so that the city and the land praise it, and himself has the friend's whole story made into a dance.","n":"The first line reads corrupt (நீக்கிப் யாய). The friend is taken as Sudanjanan, the god who was once the dog Civakan helped (945) and who carried him from trouble, but the verse never names him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nanri","natpu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2573","lb":"CC2573","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan sets up a golden image and a dance of his friend's story.","cl":[["a good done in distress is owed a lasting public memorial","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2574":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2574,"t":["ஊன்விளை யாடும் வைவே லுறுவலி சிந்தித் தேற்பத்","தான்விளை யாடி மேனா ளிருந்ததோர் தகைநல் லாலைத்","தேன்விளை யாடு மாலை யணிந்துபொற் பீடஞ் சேர்த்தி","யான்விளை யாடு மைந்தூ ரதன்புற மாக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Thinking of the strength of his sharp spear, he decks with honey-dripping garlands the fine place where he used to play, sets a golden seat there, and makes five villages its surround.","n":"The noun ஆலை is read as a hall or workplace and not a mill, and what the place was is not fixed by this verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vazhipaadu","nanri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2574","lb":"CC2574","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan adorns the place of his old play and endows it with five villages.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:110"},"cc:2575":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2575,"t":["கொட்ட மேகம ழுங்குளிர் தாமரை","மொட்டின் வீங்கிய வெம்முலை மொய்குழ","லட்டுந் தேனழி யும்மது மாலையார்","பட்ட மெண்மரும் பார்தொழ வெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"The eight women with swollen breasts like lotus buds and honey-dripping garlands reach the rank of crowned queens, and the world bows to them.","n":"The verse does not name the eight; they are read as the wives Civakan has won in the earlier ilampakams, the last being Ilakkanai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","perumai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2575","lb":"CC2575","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan's eight wives attain the crowned rank and are honoured by all.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2576":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2576,"t":["பஞ்சி சூழல்குற் பல்வளை வீங்குதோள்","வஞ்சி நுண்ணிடை வம்பணி வெம்முலை","விஞ்சை யன்மகள் சீறடி வீழ்ந்தன","ரஞ்சி லோதிய ரும்பவிழ் கோதையார்."],"p":"","r":"Women with soft hair and bud-opening garlands fall at the small feet of the Vidyadhara's daughter.","n":"Neither the bowing women nor the Vidyadhara's daughter is named. She is taken to be Gandharvadattai, whom the poem has called a Vidyadhara's daughter, set first among the wives; the bowers may be the other wives or her maids.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","kaamam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2576","lb":"CC2576","mm":1,"ev":"The women of the household bow at the feet of the Vidyadhara's daughter.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2577":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2577,"t":["வீடி லைந்தரைக் கோடி விருத்திமே","னாடி யாயிர நாடொறு நங்கைமார்க்","காடு சாந்தடி சிற்புற மாக்கினான்","கோடு வாலொளிக் குங்குமக் குன்றனான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan settles on the wives an unfailing income of five and a half crore, with a thousand a day, so that bathing, sandal and cooking are provided for.","n":"The sum and the line about daily provision (ஆயிர நாடொறு) are hard to fix: the verse may count a yearly revenue, or an endowment paid daily. Only the fact of a settled provision is certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2577","lb":"CC2577","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan assigns the women a fixed endowment for their daily needs.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2578":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2578,"t":["ஆனை மும்மத மாடிய காடெலா","மானை நோக்கியர் வாய்மது வாடின","வேனன் மல்கிவெண் டேர்சென்ற வெந்நிலம்","பானன் மல்கிவெண் பாலன்னம் பாய்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The forests where elephants once ran in rut now ring with deer-eyed women drinking, and the scorched lands the white chariot crossed are flooded with milk-rice.","n":"The verse says only that wild and dry lands turned to plenty and pleasure under the king; the cause is left to the surrounding verses on his rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2578","lb":"CC2578","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2579":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2579,"t":["மாரி மல்கி வளங்கெழு மண்மகள்","வாரி மல்கி வரம்பில ளாயினா","ளாரி யாவடி சிற்றளி யானெய்வார்ந்","தேரி யாயின வெங்கணு மென்பவே"],"p":"","r":"With rain abundant the earth-woman brims past her bounds, and ghee-rich food lies in lakes everywhere.","n":"The last line is closed with என்பவே, 'they say': the poet gives the plenty as report, not as sight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2579","lb":"CC2579","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2580":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2580,"t":["ஏக வெண்குடை யின்னிழற் றண்ணளி","மாக மாய்க்கட லெல்லை நிழற்றலாற்","போக பூமியும் பொன்கிளர் பூமியு","நாகர் நாகமு நாணி யொழித்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Under his single white umbrella the cool shade of his grace reaches the sea, so that the lands of effortless enjoyment, the golden land and the serpents' world are ashamed and withdraw.","n":"போக பூமி is the Jain cosmos's land of enjoyment without labour. The verse uses it as a measure for praise; it does not teach cosmology here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","perumai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2580","lb":"CC2580","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one king's shelter over the whole earth outdoes the lands of effortless enjoyment","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2581":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2581,"t":["வண்டு மேய்ந்து வரிமுரல் பூஞ்சிகைக்","கெண்டை வென்றகண் ணார்களுங் கேள்வரு","முண்டு மூத்திடை யூறிலர் சேறலாற்","பண்டை யூழியிற் பார்மலி வுற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Fish-eyed women and their husbands eat, grow old and travel without hindrance, so the earth is as full as in the first age.","n":"பண்டை யூழி, 'the ancient age', is the poet's name for a golden age; the verse does not say which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","ilvaazhkkai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2581","lb":"CC2581","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2582":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2582,"t":["செருநாடு செஞ்சுடர்வேற் றிருகுசெம்பொற் கனைகழற்காற்","றிருநாடு தேம்பைந்தார்ச் செல்வன்செவ்வி பெறாதொழிந்து","பெருநாட் டருங்கலங்கள் சுமந்துபேரு மிடம்பெறாஅ","தொருநாட் டரசுணங்க வுரவோன்கொற்ற முயர்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Lords of battle lands and prosperous lands cannot get audience and find no room for the rare ornaments they carry as tribute; the kings of single lands bow, and the able lord's victory stands high.","n":"The names in line 1 (செருநாடு, திருநாடு) may be regions or epithets of kings; the verse is a figure of crowded homage either way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","vendhan","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2582","lb":"CC2582","mm":1,"ev":"Subject kings queue with tribute for an audience with Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2583":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2583,"t":["வலையவர் முன்றிற் பொங்கி வாளென வாளை பாயச்","சிலையவர் குரம்பை யங்கண் மானினஞ்சென்று சேப்ப","நிலைதிரிந் தூழி நீங்கி யுத்தர குருவு மாகிக்","கொலைகடிந் திவற வின்றிக் கோத்தொழி னடத்து மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As fish leap in the fishers' yards and deer fatten on the hunters' ground, the age turns, becomes like Uttarakuru, banishes killing and miserliness, and goes on under one ruler.","n":"Uttarakuru is a land of effortless enjoyment in Jain and Buddhist cosmology. The poet sets the king's peace beside it; he does not say that the cosmic age itself has changed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","aram","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2583","lb":"CC2583","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king's rule that ends killing and miserliness makes his land a golden age like Uttarakuru","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2584":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2584,"t":["கதங்கனல் யானை நெற்றிக் கட்டிய பட்ட மேபோன்","மதங்கமழ் கோதை யல்குன் மனாக்கிடந் திமைத்துக் காமப்","பதம்பல பார்க்குஞ் சாயற் பாவைமற் றநங்க மாலை","விதம்படக் கருதி மாதர் விளைத்தது விளம்ப லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he will tell what the doll-like dancer Anangamalai brought about, whose hair lies on her hip like the gold fillet bound on a rutting elephant's brow and who watches for the moments of love.","n":"This is the poet's turn from praise of the reign to the dancer's story, in the first person (உற்றேன்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2584","lb":"CC2584","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2585":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2585,"t":["ஈர்ந்தண் கோதை யிளையார்குழாத்திடை யாளெங்கோனடி சேர்வலென்","றார்ந்த செந்தா மரைமுகத்தினா ளடிகள்வந்தீங் ககன்கடை யுளாள்","சார்ந்த சாயற் றடமாமுலைத் தையல்வல்லே வருகென் றான்","சேர்ந்து மன்னர் முடிவைரவிற் றிளைக்குஞ் செம்பொற் செறிகழலினான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, whose gold anklets are rubbed by the diamonds of kings' crowns, calls to the lotus-faced young woman who said she would reach her lord's feet and now stands at the outer gate, to come quickly.","n":"The verse never names the woman; she is Anangamalai because the letter that follows is hers (2586-2590) and 2584 has just announced her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2585","lb":"CC2585","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan has Anangamalai, waiting at the gate, called in.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அனங்கமாலை"],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2586":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2586,"t":["அருவிலை நன்கலஞ் செய்போர்வை யன்னநாண வடியொதுங்கிச்சென்","றுருவ மொவ்வா தொசியுநுசுப் பொல்கிக்கோமா னடிதொழுதபின்","மருவின் சாயன் மணிமெல்விரல் கூப்பியோலை மரபினீட்ட","விரவி யென்ன விளங்குமொளி யிறைவன் கொண்டாங் கதுநோக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Anangamalai, ashamed like a swan, slips in, bows at the king's feet, folds her slender hands and hands over a letter in the proper manner; he takes it and reads.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","panivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2586","lb":"CC2586","mm":1,"ev":"Anangamalai presents Civakan with a written letter and he reads it.","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2587":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2587,"t":["அடிகள் கண்டாங் குவந்தருளுக வநங்கமாலை யடிவீழ்ச்சிமுன்","கொடிய வேலான் கொதித்தரங்கி னீக்கிக்கோயிற் சிறைவைத்தபின்","கடிசெய் பைந்தார்க் கமழ்மாலைவேற் கந்து கற்குச் சிறுவயானிப்","படிய னல்காய் பசுமணிகள்வேய்ந் தோங்கும்பைம்பொற் செறிகழலினாய்."],"p":"","r":"The letter asks the lord to see it and be pleased, and recalls that, after she had once bowed to him and the cruel spearman in rage had thrown her out of the stage and put her under guard in the temple, she, a girl bound to Kantukkatan, is asked to be granted a favour.","n":"Lines 3-4 are not fully construable: whether கந்து கற்கு (Kantukkatan's) is the girl's foster-bond, and what is asked, remain unsettled. Line 2 is read as the usurper's anger after her glance at Civakan (685).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","idumbai","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2587","lb":"CC2587","mm":1,"ev":"Anangamalai's letter recalls her bowing to Civakan and the usurper's punishment of her.","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அனங்கமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2588":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2588,"t":["என்ன நாளு மரற்றப்பொறான் விடுப்பப்போகி யினமழைகண்மொய்த்","தன்னந் துஞ்சு மடிக்குடிலினுள் ளன்றியான் கொண்ட நாடகத்தினைத்","துன்னி நம்பி யுருவுதீட்டித் தொங்கல்வேய்ந்து தொழுதாற்றநீ","மன்னர் மன்ன மதிதோய்குடையாய் மகளிர்காம மறைத்தொழி தியோ."],"p":"","r":"The letter goes on that, released because he could no longer bear her crying, she kept to a thatched hut among crowing birds, painted Civakan's image, garlanded and worshipped it; and it asks the king of kings whether he will hide women's love.","n":"The letter is her own account; the poem has shown her devotion only through her letter. The verse's last clause is a plea, not a proven wrong.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pattru","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2588","lb":"CC2588","mm":1,"ev":"Anangamalai writes that she worshipped a painted image of Civakan and asks him not to ignore her.","cl":[["a woman's love, once openly given, should not be left unanswered by the king","general","reported",4,"anangamalai"]],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அனங்கமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2589":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2589,"t":["கண்க டுஞ்சா கதிர்முத்தமே காலுங்கையார் வளைகழலுமாற்","பண்கொள் சொல்லார் மாமைநீங்கிப் பைம்பொன் போர்த்த படாமுலைகளு","மண்கொள் வேன்மன்னர் நண்பின்மையை வையக்கெல்லா முடனறையவோ","பெண்க ளாவி விடுத்ததொழிபவோ பெரியோர்நண்படைந் தார்பெயர்பவோ."],"p":"","r":"She asks whether the world is to be told that the king lacks friendship, whether women are to give up their lives, and whether those who reach the friendship of the great go away.","n":"The first three lines describe her wasting: sleepless eyes, loosened bangles and anklets, lost sheen of the body. They are the cost she lays before him, not a narrative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kaamam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2589","lb":"CC2589","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who have gained a great one's friendship do not abandon it","general","reported",4,"anangamalai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அனங்கமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2590":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2590,"t":["அறனிழலா யுலகளிக்கு நின்னார மாலையணி வெண்குடைப்","புறனிழலி னயலேனோ யான்புல்லா மன்னர்நிணம் பொழியும்வேன்","மறனிழன் மதயானையாய் வந்தவென் றோழி வாமலேகை","திறனழி யாமையின்னே விடுத்தருளுக தேர்வேந்தர் வேந்தனே."],"p":"","r":"Standing under the umbrella that gives the world the shade of dharma, she asks whether she is outside it, and begs the king of kings to grant release now, so that the standing of her friend Vamalekai, who came with her, may not be ruined.","n":"The claim is her appeal, not the narrator's. What is to be released, and why Vamalekai is named, is unclear: the verse says only that the friend's standing depends on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","aram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2590","lb":"CC2590","mm":1,"ev":"Anangamalai petitions Civakan under the figure of his umbrella.","cl":[["a king's white umbrella is the shade of dharma that shelters the world","general","reported",1,"anangamalai"]],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அனங்கமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2591":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2591,"t":["புள்ளும்யாழுங் குழலுமேங்கப் புனைந்துவல்லா னினைந்தியற்றிய","பள்ளிச்செம்பொற் படையமளிமேன் மழலைமணியாழ் தான்வெளவிக்","கொள்ளுந் தீஞ்சொ லலங்காரப்பூங் கொடியைப்புல்லி மணிக்குவட்டினை","யெள்ளிவீங்கித் திரண்டதோண்மேற் குழைவில்வீச விருந்தானே."],"p":"","r":"On a gold bed made by a skilled craftsman while birds, lute and flute sound, Civakan holds the flower-creeper whose sweet words take the lute's voice, his shoulders swelling and his ear-ornament swinging.","n":"The woman is not named; she is Anangamalai from the sequence of the letter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2591","lb":"CC2591","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan embraces Anangamalai on the royal bed.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2592":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2592,"t":["அங்கைசேப்பக் குருகிரங்க வலங்கலம்பூங் குழறுயல்வர","மங்கைநல்லார் பவழவம்மி யரைத்தசாந்த மலர்பெய்மாலை","பொங்குதூமக் கொழுமென்புகை புரிந்தபஞ்ச முகவாசமுந்","தங்குதாம மார்பினாற்குந் தையலாட்குங் கொண்டேந்தினாரே."],"p":"","r":"Women bring sandal ground on coral stones, flower garlands, thick scented smoke and five-fold perfumes to the garland-chested king and to the woman.","n":"The attendants and the couple are known only from the context.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2592","lb":"CC2592","mm":1,"ev":"Attendants bring Civakan and Anangamalai perfumes, sandal and garlands.","cl":[],"ag":["மங்கைநல்லார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2593":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2593,"t":["அருளுமாறென்னை யநங்கமாலை யடித்திதோழி யன்றோவெனத்","தெருளலான்செல்வக் களிமயக்கினாற் றிசைக்குமென்னறி வளக்கியகருதி","மருளிற்சொன்னாய் மறப்பேனோயா னின்னையென்ன மகிழைங்கணை","யுருளுமுத்தார் முகிழ்முலையினா ளுள்ளத்துவகை தோற்றினாளே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says to Anangamalai that in the delight of wealth he said confused words that measured her mind, and asks how he could forget her; the woman with rolling pearl breasts shows her joy.","n":"The verse is hard to divide: the question in line 1 and the confession of lines 2-3 are read as the king's, but the speaker is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2593","lb":"CC2593","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan assures Anangamalai that he has not forgotten her, and she rejoices.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","அனங்கமாலை"],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2594":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2594,"t":["முறுவற்றிங்கண் முகவரங்கின்மேன் முரிந்துநீண்ட புருவக்கைக","ணெறியின்வட்டித்து நீண்டவுண்கண் சென்றும்வந்தும் பிறழ்ந்துமாடப்","பொறிகொள்பூஞ் சிலம்புமேகலைகளும் புணர்ந்தவின்னியங்க ளார்ப்பவேந்த","னறியுநாடகங் கண்டான்பைந்தா ரலர்ந்துமாதர் நலங்குழைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"With her moon-like smile, her bent brows, her darting long eyes, and the sounds of her anklets and girdle in time with the music, the dancer plays before the king, who watches the play, his garland opening and her charm melting.","n":"The king's name வேந்தன் is split across lines 3 and 4, so the agent is tagged by the verb கண்டான்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2594","lb":"CC2594","mm":1,"ev":"Anangamalai dances before Civakan, who watches.","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2595":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2595,"t":["நான்மருப்பின் மதயானை நறியபைந்தா மரைமடந்தையைத்","தேன்மதர்த்த திளைத்தாங்கவன் றிருவின்சாய னலங்கவர்ந்தபி","னூன்மதர்த்த வொளிவேற்கண் ணார்பரவவிவ்வா றொழுகுமன்றே","வானகத்து நிலத்துமில்லா வண்ணமிக்க மணிப்பூணினான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a four-tusked rutting elephant playing with a lotus-lady, Civakan takes the beauty of the Fortune-like woman while the spear-eyed women praise, and so goes on, the lord of jewels without equal in heaven or on earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","perumai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2595","lb":"CC2595","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan lives in pleasure with Anangamalai, praised by the women.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2596":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2596,"t":["நரம்புமீ திறத்தல் செல்லா நல்லிசை முழவும் யாழு","மிரங்குதீங் குழலு மேங்கக் கிண்கிணி சிலம்பொ டார்ப்பப","பரந்தவா ணெடுங்கட் செவ்வாய்த் தேசிகப் பாவை கோல","வரங்கின்மே லாடல் காட்டி யரசனை மகிழ்வித் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"To the sound of the lute, drum and flute, the foreign dancing-doll, her wide eyes and red mouth bright, dances on the stage and pleases the king.","n":"தேசிகப் பாவை, 'the doll of the regional style', is the poet's name for her dancing; the verse does not say she came from elsewhere.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2596","lb":"CC2596","mm":1,"ev":"Anangamalai dances and pleases Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2597":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2597,"t":["கடற்படை மன்னர் தம்மைக் காதலின் விடுத்துக் காமன்","றொடுத்தகோன் மார்பிற் றங்கத் தூமலர்க் கொம்ப னாளை","வடித்தவின் னமிர்தி னாரப் பருகலின் மழைக்கட்செவ்வாய்","துடித்துவண் டுண்ணத்தூங்குஞ் செந்தளி ரொத்ததன்றே."],"p":"","r":"After lovingly releasing the sea-army kings, Civakan drinks the woman like nectar, she who is like a gold shoot on the arrow-wounded king's chest, and she quivers like a tender shoot that the bee drinks.","n":"கடற்படை மன்னர் is read as the kings with the 'sea-wide army' who fought for him in the last war.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2597","lb":"CC2597","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan sends the allied kings home and takes Anangamalai to bed.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","அனங்கமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2598":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2598,"t":["இளமையங் கழனிச் சாய லேருழு தெரிபொன்வேலி","வளைமுயங் குருவ மென்றோள் வரம்புபோய் வனப்புவித்திக்","கிளைநரம்பிசையுங் கூத்துங் கேழ்த்தெழுந் தீன்றகாம","விளைபய னினிதிற் றுய்த்து வீணைவேந் துறையுமாதோ."],"p":"","r":"She, the field of youth ploughed and fenced with gold, her soft shoulders the ridges, bears the seed of beauty; the lord of the lute dwells enjoying the sweet harvest that love ripened from the music and dance.","n":"The field figure turns the woman's body into land worked and harvested; the verse presents it as praise and does not weigh the cost, a point where the Siddhar texts differ.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2598","lb":"CC2598","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan enjoys the fruit of love with Anangamalai.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:111"},"cc:2599":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2599,"t":["நீரேந்தி நெய்ம்மிதந்து நிணம்வாய்ப் பில்கி யழல்விம்மிப்","போரேந்திப் பூவணிந்து புலவுநாறும் புகழ்வேலோன்","காரேந் திடிமுரச மார்ப்பக் காய்பொற் கலன்சிந்திப்","பாரேந்திச் செல்லுநாட் பட்ட தாநாம் பகர்வதே."],"p":"","r":"The poet opens the last ilampakam: the king of the famed spear, with war-drums booming and gold ornaments scattered in gifts, goes about the earth, and what befell on one of those days is now to be told.","n":"The verse names no one; that the spear-bearing king is Civakan is taken from the chapter's place at the end of the work.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2599","lb":"CC2599","mm":1,"ev":"The poet announces the closing episode of Civakan's reign.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2600":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2600,"t":["விண்பாற் சுடர்விலக்கி மேகம் போழ்ந்து விசும்பேந்தி","மண்பாற் றிலகமாய் வான்பூத் தாங்கு மணிமல்கிப்","பண்பால் வரிவண்டுந் தேனும்பாடும் பொழிற்பிண்டி","யெண்பா லிகந்துயர்ந்தாற் கிசைந்த கோயி லியன்றதே."],"p":"","r":"A temple rises for the one who has gone beyond the eight qualities, set in a grove of asoka trees where striped bees and honey-bees sing, its towers touching the sky.","n":"The asoka (பிண்டி) grove marks the Jina's temple, as elsewhere in the epic; the eight qualities are named only as 'eight' (எண்பால்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2600","lb":"CC2600","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2601":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2601,"t":["அடிசிற் கலங் கழீஇக் கருனை யார்ந்த வினவாளை","மடுவின் மதத்துணரா வாழைத் தண்டிற் பலதுஞ்சு","நெடுநீர்க் கழனிசூழ் நியமஞ் சேர்த்தி விழவ யர்ந்து","வடிநீர் நெடுங்கண்ணார் கூத்தும் பாட்டும் வகுத்தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The townsfolk, after washing the feasting vessels, hold a festival in a town ringed by watered fields, with women's dance and song arranged.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2601","lb":"CC2601","mm":1,"ev":"The townspeople hold a festival with dance and song.","cl":[],"ag":["என்மரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2602":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2602,"t":["அல்லி யரும்பதமு மடகுங்காயுங் குளநெல்லு","நல்ல கொழும்பழனுங் கிழங்குந்தந்து நவைதீர்த்தார்க்","கில்லையே கைம்மாறென் றின்பமெல்லா மவர்க்கீந்தாள்","வில்லோன் பெருமாட்டி விளங்குவேற்கண் விசயையே."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai, the archer-king's lady, gives every delight to those who relieved her woe with lily seeds, wild fruit and roots, saying that no return could ever repay them.","n":"The helpers are not named; the foods (lily stamens, wild fruit, roots) are those of a woman living rough, so the verse looks back to her days in the burning ground and the forest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","eegai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2602","lb":"CC2602","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayai rewards with every pleasure those who sustained her in her distress.","cl":[["a kindness done in distress can never be fully repaid","general","asserted",3,"vijayai"]],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2603":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2603,"t":["தனியே துயருழந்து தாழ்ந்து வீழ்ந்த சுடுகாட்டு","ளினியா ளிடர்நீக்கி யேமஞ்சேர்த்தி யுயக்கொண்ட","கனியார் மொழியாட்கு மயிற்குங் காமர் பதிநல்கி","முனியாது தான்காண மொய்கொண்மாடத் தெழுதுவித்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai grants a lovely town to the sweet-voiced woman who sheltered her when she fell alone in the burning ground and gives her peacock-machine its due, and has the scene painted on her high mansion so she can always see it.","n":"The text does not name the 'fruit-voiced one'; the foster mother Sunandai is the likeliest, but the verse leaves it open. Reading of 'மயிற்கு' as the peacock machine is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nanri","arul","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2603","lb":"CC2603","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayai gives a town to her rescuer and has the story of her rescue painted on a mansion wall.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2604":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2604,"t":["அண்ணல் பிறந்தாங் கைஞ்ஞாற் றைவர்க் களந்தான்பால்","வண்ணச் சுவையமுதல் வைக நாளுங் கோவிந்தன்","வெண்ணெ யுருக்கிநெய் வெள்ள மாகச் சொரிந்தூட்டப்","பண்ணிப் பரிவகன்றாள் பைந்தார் வேந்தற் பயந்தாளே."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai recalls how the hero, once born, was reared: five hundred nurses gave him milk and Govindan had butter melted and poured out like a flood to feed him, and she, who had borne the garlanded king, lost her grief.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","eegai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2604","lb":"CC2604","mm":1,"ev":"Govindan has Civakan fed with melted butter in a flood while five hundred nurses suckle him.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவிந்தன்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2605":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2605,"t":["தோடார் புனைகோதை சுநந்தை சேர்ந்து தொழுதாளைப்","பீடார் பெருஞ்சிறுவற் பயந்தீர் வம்மி னெனப்புல்லி","நாடார் புகழாளை நாண மொழிகள் பலகூறிக்","கோடாக் குருகுலத்தை விளக்கிட் டாளை விளக்கினாள்."],"p":"","r":"Sunandai comes and worships; Vijayai embraces her, saying she has borne the great boy, and with many shaming words of praise makes the woman who lit the Kuru line shine in her turn.","n":"'Shame-making words' (நாண மொழிகள்) are words of praise too large for her to accept.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2605","lb":"CC2605","mm":1,"ev":"Sunandai worships Vijayai, who embraces and praises her.","cl":[],"ag":["சுநந்தை","விசயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2606":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2606,"t":["மறையொன் றுரைப்பனபோன் மலர்ந்து நீண்டு செவிவாய்வைத்","துறைகின்ற வோடரிக்க ணுருவக் கொம்பி னெண்மரு","மிறைவி யடிபணிய வெடுத்துப் புல்லி யுலகாளுஞ்","சிறுவர்ப் பயந்திறைவற் றெளிவீ ரென்றா டிருவன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"The eight wives, eyes long and wide as an axe, fall at the lady's feet; she lifts and embraces them and tells them that, as mothers of the sons who will rule the world, they should trust the king.","n":"The speaker is Vijayai by the story, not by a word naming her in these lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","ilvaazhkkai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2606","lb":"CC2606","mm":1,"ev":"The eight queens bow to Vijayai, who embraces them and urges them to trust their lord.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்","விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2607":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2607,"t":["பொங்கு மணிமுடிமேற் பொலிந்தெண் கோதைத் தொகையாகிக்","கங்குற் கனவகத்தே கண்ணுட் டோன்றி வந்தீர்நீ","ரெங்கும் பிரியற்பீ ரென்று கண்கண் மலர்ந்திருந்து","கொங்கு ணறும்பைந்தார்க் கோமா னிங்கே வருகென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai tells them they came into the night's dream as a crown of eight garlands on the king's jewelled crown and begs them never to part; then, eyes wide, she bids the king come.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2607","lb":"CC2607","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayai tells the queens of her dream of the eight garlands and calls Civakan to come.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2608":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2608,"t":["சிங்க நடப்பதுபோற் சேர்ந்துபூத்தூய்ப் பலர்வாழ்த்தத்","தங்கா விருப்பிற்றம் பெருமான்பாத முடிதீட்டி","யெங்கோ பணியென்னா வஞ்சாநடுங்கா விருவிற்கட்","பொங்க விடுதவிசி லிருந்தான்போரே றனையானே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, walking like a lion, has flowers strewn and many bless him; he marks his lord-mother's feet on his head, asks what she commands without fear, and sits on the seat set for him, like a bull in battle.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","anbu","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2608","lb":"CC2608","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan comes, worships his mother, and sits at her bidding.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2609":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2609,"t":["கொற்றவி மகனைநோக்கிக் கூறின ளென்ப நுங்கோக்","குற்றத்தைப் பிறர்கள்கூற வுணர்ந்தனை யாயி னானு","மிற்றென வுரைப்பக் கேண்மோ விலங்குபூ ணலங்கன் மார்பிற்","செற்றவர்ச் செகுத்த வைவேற் சீவக சாமி யென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai turns to her son and says that if he has learned his king's fault from others, she will tell it herself: it is the one who slew his foes with the five-pointed spear, Civaka Swami.","n":"The pronouns are ambiguous: 'your king' (நுங்கோ) may be Saccandan, and the closing address to Civaka Swami may be the poet's or hers. The sense is taken as the opening of a retelling.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pazhi","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2609","lb":"CC2609","mm":1,"ev":"Vijayai begins to tell her son the story of his father's fall.","cl":[],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2610":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2610,"t":["நாகத்தால் விழுங்கப் பட்ட நகைமதிக் கடவுள் போலப்","போகத்தால் விழுங்கப் பட்டுப் புறப்படான் புன்சொ னாணா","னாகத்தா னமைச்சர் நுண்ணூற் றோட்டியா லழுத்தி வெல்லும்","பாகர்க்குந் தொடக்க நில்லாப் பகடுபோற் பொங்கி யிட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Swallowed by pleasure as the shining moon-god is swallowed by the serpent, the old king would not come out, was not ashamed of base talk, and, when his ministers pressed him with fine counsel, he rose up like an elephant that will not be mastered by its drivers.","n":"Saccandan is not named here; the identification rests on the earlier telling of his fall in 261.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pazhi","amaichu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2610","lb":"CC2610","mm":1,"ev":"The king, sunk in pleasure, brushes off his ministers' counsel.","cl":[["a king sunk in pleasure ruins himself by ignoring wise counsel","general","asserted",2,"vijayai"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2611":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2611,"t":["நுண்மதி போன்று தோன்றா நுணுகிய நுசுப்பி னார்தங்","கண்வலைப் பட்ட போழ்தே கடுநவை யரவோ டொக்கும்.","பெண்மையைப் பெண்மை யென்னார் பேருணர் வுடைய நீரா","ரண்ணலைத் தெருட்ட றேற்றா தமைச்சரு மகன்று விட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Once caught in the net of slender-waisted women's eyes, a man is like one with a deadly snake; the wise count womanhood no womanhood, and the ministers gave up trying to rouse the lord.","n":"Line 2 is closed with a full stop in the print; the sense carries to line 3, whose meaning ('the wise do not call womanhood womanhood') is hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","amaichu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2611","lb":"CC2611","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers fail to rouse the king and leave him.","cl":[["a man caught by a woman's eyes is in the grip of a deadly danger","general","asserted",2,"vijayai"]],"ag":["அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2612":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2612,"t":["கற்சிறை யழித்து வெள்ளங் கடற்கவா யாங்குக் கற்றோர்","சொற்சிறை யழித்து வேந்தன் றுணைமுலை துறத்தல் செல்லான்","விற்சிறை கொண்ட போலும் புருவத்து விளங்கு வேற்க","ணற்சிறைப் பட்டுநாடு நகரமுங் காவல் விட்டான்."],"p":"","r":"As flood breaks the stone dam into the sea, the king breaks the dam of the learned men's words, cannot bear to leave the breasts of his queen, and, caught in the prison of her spear-bent brows, leaves land and city unguarded.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","arasu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2612","lb":"CC2612","mm":1,"ev":"The king abandons the guard of the land to stay with his queen.","cl":[["a king's desire can break the restraint of learned counsel and lose the kingdom","general","asserted",2,"vijayai"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2613":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2613,"t":["பிளிறுவார் முரசத் தானைப் பெருமகன் பிழைப்பு நாடிக்","களிறுமென் றுமிழப் பட்ட கவழம்போற் றகர்ந்து நில்லா","தொளிறுவேற் சுற்ற மெல்லா முடைந்தபி னொருவ னானான்","வெளிறுமுன் வித்திப் பின்னை வச்சிரம் விளைத்த லாமோ."],"p":"","r":"The great lord of the roaring drum and army, seeking to save himself, crumbles like a quid spat by an elephant, and when all his kin have broken away he stands alone; sowing the seed late cannot bring a diamond.","n":"'வெளிறு முன் வித்தி' in line 4 is obscure; the sense of a late remedy is a reading, not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","sutram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2613","lb":"CC2613","mm":1,"ev":"The king is left alone when his kin and army break away.","cl":[["remedy sought after ruin has begun is useless, as it is to plant seed after the crop is lost","general","asserted",4,"vijayai"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2614":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2614,"t":["வனைகலக் குயவ னாணின் மன்னரை யறுத்து முற்றிக்","கனைகுர லுருமி னார்ப்பக் காவல னின்னை வேண்டி","வினைமயிற் பொறியி னென்னைப் போக்கிவிண் விரும்பப் புக்கான்","புனைமுடி வேந்த போவல் போற்றென மயங்கி வீழ்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker, in the king's own words, says that the potter-like Kattiyangaran conquered the kings and, roaring like thunder, sent her away by the deed-peacock machine while he himself went up to the sky; the king fell swooning as he called 'I go, protect'.","n":"The calling of the usurper 'one who makes pots' and the order of speakers are uncertain; the line 3 phrase வினைமயில் (deed's peacock) may be an image for the machine as fate's instrument.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2614","lb":"CC2614","mm":1,"ev":"The king sends the queen away on the peacock machine and falls fainting as he takes leave.","cl":[["fate, as a deed's working, carried the king away from her","general","reported",3,"vijayai"]],"ag":["சச்சந்தன்","கட்டியங்காரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2615":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2615,"t":["சீதநீர் தெளித்துச் செம்பொற் றிருந்துசாந் தாற்றி தம்மான்","மாதரார் பலரும் வீச வளர்ந்தெழு சிங்கம் போலப்","போதொடு கலங்கள் சோர வெழுந்துபொன் னார மார்பன்","யாதெனக் கடிகண் முன்னே யருளிய தென்னச் சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"Women sprinkle cold water and fan him with sandal; he rises like a growing lion, flowers and ornaments slipping, and asks what it was that had been said; she tells him all.","n":"The women who fan him are not named; the speaker of the last line is taken as Vijayai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","idumbai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2615","lb":"CC2615","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan, roused from a faint by his women, asks to be told and Vijayai tells him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2616":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2616,"t":["பிறந்துநாம் பெற்ற வாணா ளித்துணை யென்ப தொன்று","மறிந்திலம் வாழ்து மென்னு மவாவினு ளழுந்து கின்றாங்","கறந்துகூற் றுண்ணு ஞான்று கண்புதைத் திரங்கி னல்லா","லிறந்தநாள் யாவர்மீட்பா ரிற்றெனப் பெயர்க்க லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Vijayai says that we are born without even knowing how many days we have and sink in the longing to live; only when the cow is milked do we cover our eyes and weep, but who can bring back the days that are gone?","n":"Her speech is a mother's urging to renounce; the milked cow stands for life being drawn off while one weeps only at the end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","avaa","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2616","lb":"CC2616","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the span of life is unknown and passes unrecoverably; hope to live on blinds one to this","general","asserted",2,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2617":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2617,"t":["சுமைத்தயிர் வேய்ந்த சோற்றிற் றுய்த்தினி தாக நம்மை","யமைத்தநா ளென்னு நாகம் விழுங்கப்பட்ட டன்ன தங்க","ணிமைத்தகண் விழித்த லின்றி யிறந்துபா டெய்து கின்றா","முமைத்துழிச் சொறியப் பெற்றா மூதியம் பெரிதும் பெற்றாம்."],"p":"","r":"The days fixed for us are a serpent swallowing us like sweet curd-sprinkled rice; we die with no wink of the eye, but we are glad of an itch scratched when we blink, as if we had gained a great gift.","n":"Line 1 first half and line 4 (the itch and the blink) are hard; the translation of the closing image is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kootru","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2617","lb":"CC2617","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["days measured out to the living are devouring them, and small pleasures hide this","general","asserted",2,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2618":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2618,"t":["கடுவளி புடைக்கப் பட்ட கணமழைக் குழாத்தி னாமும்","விடுவினை புடைக்கப் பாறி வீற்றுவீற் றாயி னல்லா","லுடனுறை பழக்க மில்லை யொழிமதி யத்தை காதல்","வடுவுடைத் தென்று பின்னு மாபெருந் தேவி சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"As clouds struck by a hard wind are scattered in all directions, so we, struck by the force of deeds, are parted, and have no lasting companionship; she says to give up love for the 'aththai', since love has a flaw.","n":"The word 'அத்தை' in line 3 may be a form of address ('father'/'dear') or the sense of 'those who are with us'; it is not fixed. The school tag 'jain' is given because the claim states the deed's force parting people, in the work's own frame, not because the verse names a school.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pirivu","piravi","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2618","lb":"CC2618","mm":1,"ev":"The great queen tells her son that kin are scattered by the force of deeds and bids him give up attachment.","cl":[["kin are parted by the working of deeds and no companionship is lasting","jain","asserted",2,"vijayai"],["attachment to kin brings pain, so it should be given up","general","asserted",3,"vijayai"]],"ag":["விசயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2619":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2619,"t":["முருந்தனைய தூமுறுவன் முற்றிழையார் சேரி","யிருந்திளமைக் கள்ளுண் டிடைதெரித லின்றிக்","கருந்தலைகள் வெண்டலைக ளாய்க்கழியு முன்னே","யருந்தவமுந் தானமு மாற்றுமினே கண்டீர்."],"p":"","r":"Before bright-toothed youth in the women's street drinks the toddy of youth without seeing its lapse, before black heads turn white, she says, do hard penance and give.","n":"From here the addressees broaden from her son to all who listen, and it is unclear whether the speaker is still Vijayai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","thavam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2619","lb":"CC2619","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one must do penance and give before youth passes into age","general","asserted",4,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை",""],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2620":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2620,"t":["உடற்றும் பிணித்தீ யுடம்பினுயிர் பெய்திட்","டடுத்துணர்வு நெய்யாக வாற்றறுவை யாகக்","குடித்துண்ணுங் கூற்றங் குடில்பிரியா முன்னே","கொடுத்துண்மின் கண்டீர் குணம்புரிமின் கண்டீர்."],"p":"","r":"The fire of disease burns the body, with life poured in as ghee and awareness as oil; before Death, who drinks us up, leaves the house, she says to give, eat and do good.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2620","lb":"CC2620","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the body is consumed by disease and Death, so one must give and do good while it lasts","general","asserted",4,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை",""],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2621":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2621,"t":["உழந்தாலும் புத்தச்சொன் றிட்டூர்த றேற்றா","திழந்தார் பலரா லிடும்பைநீர் யாற்று","ளழுந்துமா லப்பண்டி யச்சிறா முன்னே","கொழுஞ்சீலங் கூலியாக் கொண்டூர்மின் பாகீர்."],"p":"","r":"Many who could not teach themselves to drive the cart of the word of the Buddha-wise were lost; before that cart's axle breaks and it sinks in the river of sorrow, drivers, take good conduct as fare and drive it.","n":"'புத்தச் சொன்று' in line 1 is obscure and may not name the Buddha at all; no school is given from it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","idumbai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2621","lb":"CC2621","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["good conduct (சீலம்) is the fare that carries one across the river of sorrow","general","asserted",4,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை",""],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2622":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2622,"t":["பிறந்தவர்க ளெல்லா மவாப்பெரிய ராகித்","துறந்துபுகழ் வேண்டாரோர் துற்றவிழு மீயா","ரறங்கரிது சேய்த்தென்ப தியாதுமறி யாரேல்","வெறும்பொருள தம்மா விடுத்திடுமி னென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"All who are born are greedy and great; they give up and never seek fame, and they never give a handful to the poor; she says that if people know nothing about virtue being hard and far, then let go of the empty wealth.","n":"Lines 2-3 are tangled; 'துற்ற விழு மீயார்' is taken as 'those who do not give a handful'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","thuravu","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2622","lb":"CC2622","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who give nothing and lack virtue should let go of their empty wealth","general","asserted",4,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை",""],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2623":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2623,"t":["முல்லை முகைசொரிந்தாற் போன்றினிய பாலடிசின் மகளி ரேந்த","நல்ல கருனையா னாள்வாயும் பொற்கலத்து நயந்துண் டார்க","ளல்ல லடைய வடகிடுமி னோட்டகத்தென் றயில்வார்க் கண்டுஞ்","செல்வ நமரங்கா ணினையன்மின் செய்தவமே நினைமின் கண்டீர்."],"p":"","r":"Seeing those who eat sweet milk-rice, served by women in gold bowls, she says do not pity those who starve and do not think we are rich: think of austerity, which alone is what we have done.","n":"Line 3 'அடகிடுமின்' and 'ஓட்டகத்து' are hard; the meaning of feeding the poor rather than rich diners is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","eegai","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2623","lb":"CC2623","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one should think of austerity and not rely on riches","general","asserted",4,"vijayai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விசயை",""],"ch":"cc:112"},"cc:2624":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2624,"t":["அம்பொற் கலத்து ளடுபா லமர்ந்துண்ணா வரிவை யந்தோ","வெம்பிப் பசிநலிய வெவ்வினையின் வேறாயோ ரகல்கை யேந்திக்","கொம்பிற் கொளவொசிந்து பிச்சை யெனக்கூறி நிற்பாட் கண்டு","நம்பன்மின் செல்வ நமரங்கா ணல்லறமே நினைமின் கண்டீர்."],"p":"","r":"The sermon points to a woman who once drank boiled milk from a golden bowl and now, worn by hunger and cut off from her fortune by cruel deeds, stands bent like a creeper with a wide bowl held out, crying 'alms'; seeing her, the rich are told not to trust their wealth and to think on good dharma.","n":"The fall is put to வெவ்வினை (cruel deeds), so the picture is an instance of karma ripening and not mere chance. The same closing refrain, நல்லறமே நினைமின் கண்டீர், ends 2625 and 2626, so the three verses are three sights under one refrain: food, cloth, youth. The verse does not name the speaker or the hearers; the refrain addresses the rich (செல்வ).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam","vinai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2624","lb":"CC2624","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["wealth is no ground for trust, since cruel deeds can bring one fed from gold to beg, so one should turn the mind to good dharma","general","asserted",4,"unnamed_preacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடிகள்",""],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2625":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2625,"t":["வண்ணத் துகிலுடுப்பின் வாய்விட் டழுவதுபோல் வருந்து மல்கு","னண்ணாச் சிறுகூறை பாகமோர் கைபாக முடுத்து நாளு","மண்ணாந் தடகுரீஇ யந்தோ வினையேயென் றழுவாட் கண்டு","நண்ணன்மின் செல்வ நமரங்கா ணல்லறமே நினைமின் கண்டீர்."],"p":"","r":"A second sight: a woman once in bright silk, now in a small torn cloth that she wears half and holds half, who weeps day by day 'alas, my deeds'; the rich are told not to cling to wealth and to think on good dharma.","n":"She herself cries அந்தோ வினையே ('alas, my deeds'), so the sermon takes karma from the sufferer's own mouth. The simile in line 3 (அண்ணாந்த தடகுரீஇ) is hard to construe and is left out of the reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2625","lb":"CC2625","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the fall from silk to rags is the working of one's own deeds, so wealth gives nothing to hold","general","asserted",3,"unnamed_preacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடிகள்",""],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2626":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2626,"t":["மைதிரண்ட வார்குழன்மேல் வண்டார்ப்ப மல்லிகைமென் மாலை சூடிக்","கைதிரண்ட வேலேந்திக் காமன்போற் காரிகையார் மருளச் சென்றா","ரைதிரண்டு கண்டங் குரைப்பவோர் தண்டூன்றி யறிவிற் றள்ளி","நெய்திரண்டாற் போலுமிழ்ந்து நிற்கு மிளமையோ நிலையா தேகாண்."],"p":"","r":"A third sight: a youth in a jasmine garland, spear in hand, whom women gazed at as at Kama, comes to lean on a staff, his understanding thrust out by the phlegm that rattles in his throat, and he stands drooling like melted ghee: youth does not last.","n":"The verse turns on a single person shown before and after, so the loss is felt as one life's, not as a general remark; the refrain here reads நிலையாதே காண் (it does not stay) in place of the refrain of 2624-2625.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2626","lb":"CC2626","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["youth and beauty do not last, for age drives out understanding and leaves the body a ruin","general","asserted",4,"unnamed_preacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடிகள்",""],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2627":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2627,"t":["என்றலுஞ் சுநந்தை சொல்லு மிறைவிதான் கண்ட தையா","நன்றுமஃ தாக வன்றே யாயினு மாக யானு","மொன்றினன் றுறப்ப லென்ன வொள்ளெரி தவழ்ந்த வெண்ணெய்க்","குன்றுபோல் யாது மின்றிக் குழைந்துமெய்ம் மறந்து நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Sunandai answers that the lady has seen it herself, and whether it be so or not she too has joined in and will renounce; at that Civakan stands melted like a hill of butter over a bright fire, with no hold on himself and unmindful of his body.","n":"That இறைவி means the nun who has just told the three sights, and that the one who melts in line 4 is Civakan, are inferred from this verse alone; the speaker of யானும் ஒன்றினன் துறப்பல் could also be read as Civakan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2627","lb":"CC2627","mm":1,"ev":"Sunandai declares that she too will renounce, and Civakan stands melted and unmindful of his body.","cl":[],"ag":["சுநந்தை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுநந்தை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2628":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2628,"t":["ஓருயி ரொழித்திரண் டுடம்பு போவபோ","லாரிய னொழியவங் கௌவை மார்கடாஞ்","சீரிய துறவொடு சிவிகை யேறினார்","மாரியின் மடந்தைமார் கண்கள் வார்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"As if two bodies were going and leaving one life behind, the matrons go in palanquins to their noble renunciation and the noble one is left, and the eyes of the women stream like rain.","n":"ஆரியன் is taken as Civakan, the one the two matrons leave, and the matrons as Vijayai and Sunandai; the verse names neither. In line 2 the matrons are written அங் கௌவை மார்கடாம், read here as அங்கு ஔவைமார்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thuravu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2628","lb":"CC2628","mm":1,"ev":"The two matrons go in palanquins to take the vow, leaving Civakan behind, while the women weep.","cl":[],"ag":["ஔவைமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2629":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2629,"t":["நன்மயிற் பொறியின்மேற் போயா நாளினும்","புன்மையுற் றழுகுரன் மயங்கிப் பூப்பரிந்","திந்நகர் கால்பொரு கடலி னெங்கணு","மன்னனி லாகுல மயங்கிற் றென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The city is told to be thrown into uproar like a sea struck by the wind, with weeping voices mingling and flowers torn off.","n":"Line 1 (நன்மயிற் பொறியின்மேற் போயா நாளினும்) and the words மன்னனி லாகுலம் in line 4 are hard to construe; the reading keeps only the simile and the weeping.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2629","lb":"CC2629","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2630":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2630,"t":["அழுதுபின் னணிநகர் செல்ல வாயிரந்","தொழுதகு சிவிகைகள் சூழப் போயபி","னிழுதமை யெரிசுடர் விளக்கிட் டன்னவள்","பழுதில்சீர்ப் பம்மைதன் பள்ளி நண்ணினாள்."],"p":"","r":"After weeping, she goes through the decked city among a thousand worshipful palanquins and comes to the nunnery of Pammai of unblemished fame.","n":"The singular அன்னவள் is left unnamed; whether she is one of the matrons or a queen is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2630","lb":"CC2630","mm":1,"ev":"A woman, followed by a thousand palanquins, goes to Pammai's nunnery.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கைமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2631":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2631,"t":["அருந்தவக் கொடிக்குழாஞ் சூழ வல்லிபோ","லிருந்தறம் பகர்வுழி யிழிந்து கைதொழு","தொருங்கெமை யுயக்கொண்மி னடிக ளென்றனள்","கருங்கய னெடுந்தடங் கண்ணி யென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"As Pammai sits like a creeper among a throng of creeper-like women ascetics and tells the dharma, the long-eyed woman comes down, joins her hands in worship and begs the lady to take them all in and save them.","n":"The one who asks is not named, and எமை (us) shows she speaks for more than herself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","aram","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2631","lb":"CC2631","mm":1,"ev":"A woman alights before Pammai, worships, and asks to be taken in with the others.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கைமார்","பம்மை"],"ec":[],"spk":["நங்கைமார்","பம்மை"],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2632":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2632,"t":["ஆரழன் முளரி யன்ன வருந்தவ மரிது தானஞ்","சீர்கெழு நிலத்து வித்திச் சீலநீர் கொடுப்பிற் றீந்தேன்","பார்கெழு நிலத்து ணாறிப் பல்புக ழீன்று பின்னாற்","றார்கெழு தேவரின்பந் தையலாய் விளைக்கு மென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Pammai tells her that penance is as hard as a lotus in a fierce fire, but that a gift sown in good ground and watered with virtue (சீலநீர்) grows sweet on the earth, gives many kinds of fame and afterwards gives the pleasures of the garlanded gods.","n":"The reward named is fame and heaven, not release: the nun sets the gift of the householder below the rare penance she is about to grant, in the epic's own order of fruit. The speaker is known only by the closing என்றாள் and the situation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","eegai","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2632","lb":"CC2632","mm":1,"ev":"Pammai tells the woman the reward of a gift sown with virtue, and that penance is harder.","cl":[["a gift sown in good ground and watered with virtue brings fame in this world and the pleasures of the gods after it, while true penance is harder","jain","asserted",3,"pammai"]],"ag":["பம்மை"],"ec":[],"spk":["பம்மை","நங்கைமார்"],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2633":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2633,"t":["அறவுரை பின்னைக்கேட்டு மடிகண்மற் றெமக்குவல்லே","துறவுதந் தருளு கென்னத் தூநக ரிழைத்து மேலா","னறவிரி தாம நாற்றி வானகம் விதானித் தாய்ந்து","திறவிதிற் றவிசு தூபந் திருச்சுடர் விளக்கிட் டாரே."],"p":"","r":"Having heard the dharma again, they ask the lady to grant them renunciation quickly; then they raise a pure hall, hang fragrant garlands, spread a canopy like the sky, and set a seat, incense and bright lamps.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","aram","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2633","lb":"CC2633","mm":1,"ev":"The women ask for renunciation and prepare the hall of ordination.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கைமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2634":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2634,"t":["பாலினாற் சீறடி கழுவிப் பைந்துகி","னூலினா லியன்றன நுனித்த வெண்மைய","காலனைக் கண்புதைத் தாங்கு வெம்முலை","மேல்வளாய் வீக்கினார் விதியி னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"They wash her small feet with milk and, by the rule, bind fine white cloth woven of thread round her breasts, as if blindfolding Death.","n":"The simile காலனைக் கண்புதைத்தாங்கு makes the binding a way of blinding Death; who binds and who is bound is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vesham","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2634","lb":"CC2634","mm":1,"ev":"The women's feet are washed with milk and white cloth is bound on them by rule.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கைமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2635":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2635,"t":["தேனுலா மாலையுங் கலனுஞ் சிந்துபு","பானிலாக் கதிரன வம்மென் பைந்துகி","றானுரலாய்த் தடமுலை முற்றஞ் சூழ்ந்தரோ","வேனிலான் வருநெறி வெண்முள் வித்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"They scatter garlands and ornaments, wrap their breasts' broad court in soft cloth pale as moonlight, and so sow white thorns on the road by which Kama, lord of spring, would come.","n":"Line 3 (தானுரலாய்த் தடமுலை முற்றம் சூழ்ந்தரோ) is hard to construe; the reading follows its general sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thuravu","kaimmai","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2635","lb":"CC2635","mm":1,"ev":"The women throw off garlands and ornaments and put on plain white cloth.","cl":[["renunciation shuts the road by which desire comes","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["நங்கைமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2636":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2636,"t":["முன்னுபு கீழ்த்திசை நோக்கி மொய்ம்மலர்","நன்னிறத் தவிசின்மே லிருந்த நங்கைமா","ரின்மயி ருகுக்கிய விருந்த தோகைய","பன்மயிற் குழாமொத்தார் பாவை மார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Facing east, the women sit on seats of fine blooms and let fall their hair like many peahens shedding feathers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","kaimmai","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2636","lb":"CC2636","mm":1,"ev":"The women sit facing east and shed their hair.","cl":[],"ag":["நங்கைமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2637":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2637,"t":["மணியியல் சீப்பிடச் சிவக்கும் வாணுத","லணியிருங் கூந்தலை யௌவை மார்கடாம்","பணிவிலர் பறித்தனர் பரமன் சொன்னநூற்","றுணிபொருள் சிந்தியாத் துறத்தன் மேயினார்."],"p":"","r":"The matrons, their dark hair reddened by the jewelled comb, pluck it out themselves without a flinch, and enter renunciation pondering the settled meaning of the book the Lord spoke.","n":"Only பரமன் is said; that he is the Jina is the work's setting, not this verse's words. The women pull their own hair out, which the verse presents as done without flinching (பணிவிலர்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","kaimmai","vesham","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2637","lb":"CC2637","mm":1,"ev":"The matrons pluck out their own hair and take the vow.","cl":[["renunciation is entered through reflection on the settled meaning of the scripture the Lord taught","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["நங்கைமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2638":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2638,"t":["கன்னிய ராயிரர் காய்பொற் கொம்பனார்","பொன்னியற் படலிகை யேந்திப் பொன்மயிர்","நன்னிலம் படாமையே யடக்கி நங்கைமார்","தொன்மயி ருகுத்தநன் மயிலிற் றோன்றினார்."],"p":"","r":"A thousand maidens like golden creepers carry gold trays and catch the golden hair so that it does not touch the good ground, and the women who shed their old hair look like dancing peahens.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","kaimmai","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2638","lb":"CC2638","mm":1,"ev":"A thousand maidens catch the women's hair on gold trays.","cl":[],"ag":["கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2639":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2639,"t":["பொற்குடந் திருமணி பொழியப் பெய்தபோ","லெற்புடம் பெண்ணிலாக் குணங்க ளானிறைத்","துற்றுட னுயிர்க்கருள் பரப்பி யோம்பினார்","முற்றுட னுணர்ந்தவ னமுத முன்னினார்"],"p":"","r":"As if pouring jewels into gold pots, they fill the bone-body with virtues, spread and guard compassion over every living thing, and turn their thought to the nectar of the one who knows all.","n":"எற்புடம்பு names the body as bones, a frame to be filled with virtues where the preceding verses took ornaments off it. Line 2 prints பெண்ணிலாக் (womanless), which sits oddly with எண்ணிலாக் (countless); the reading keeps the plain sense of a full body of virtues.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","kollaamai","yaakkai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2639","lb":"CC2639","mm":1,"ev":"The new nuns fill themselves with virtues and compassion and look to the nectar of the all-knowing.","cl":[["the ascetic guards every living being with compassion","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"],["the nectar of release is what the all-knowing one teaches","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["நங்கைமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2640":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2640,"t":["புகழ்ந்துரை மகிழ்ச்சியும் பொற்பில் பல்சன","மிகழ்ந்துரைக் கிரக்கமு மின்றி யங்கநூ","லகழ்ந்துகொண் டரும்பொருள் பொதிந்த நெஞ்சினார்","திகழ்ந்தெரி விளக்கெனத் திலக மாயினார்."],"p":"","r":"Neither glad at praise nor pitying scorn, having dug into the Anga books and packed their hearts with the rare meanings, they became the tilaka of the order, shining like a burning lamp.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samanilai","kalvi","thavam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2640","lb":"CC2640","mm":1,"ev":"The new nuns study the Anga books and shine as the order's ornament.","cl":[["the ascetic is unmoved by praise and by scorn","general","asserted",1,"narrator"],["the ascetic's heart is stored with the meanings of the Anga scriptures","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["நங்கைமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2641":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2641,"t":["அலைமணிக் கவரி மான்றே ரடுகளி யானை பாய்மா","நிலநெளி கடலந் தானை நிரந்துபூச் சுமப்ப மன்னன்","சிலமலர் தானு மேந்திச் சென்றுசீர் பெருக வாழ்த்தி","யிலமலர்ப் பஞ்சிப் பாதத் தெழின்முடி தீட்டி னானே."],"p":"","r":"With elephants, chariots, horses and the sea-like army carrying flowers, the king himself bears a few blooms, goes and praises them richly, and touches his beautiful crown to the feet that are dyed red like cotton and lac.","n":"Whose feet they are is not said; the new nuns of 2636-2640 are the natural referent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","thuravu","arasu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2641","lb":"CC2641","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan and his army carry flowers to the new nuns, and he bows his crowned head at their feet.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சேனை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2642":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2642,"t":["கடியவை முன்பு செய்தேன் கண்ணினாற் காணச் சின்னா","லடிகளிந் நகரி னுள்ளே யுறைகென வண்ணல் கூற","முடிகெழு மன்னற் கொன்று மறுமொழி கொடாது தேவி","படிமம்போன் றிருப்ப நோக்கிப் பம்மைதான் சொல்லி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The lord says he did harsh things before and asks that the lady stay a few days in the city so that he may see her with his own eyes; she gives the crowned king no word, standing like an image, and seeing it Pammai replies.","n":"That the lord is Civakan and that தேவி is a matron now ordained (not a queen of his house) are inferred from the sequence; the verse gives neither a name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thuravu","idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2642","lb":"CC2642","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan asks the lady to stay a few days; she is silent and Pammai answers for her.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தேவி","பம்மை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தேவி"],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2643":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2643,"t":["காதல னல்லை நீயுங் காவல நினக்கி யாமு","மேதில மென்று கண்டா யிருந்தது நங்கை யென்னத்","தாதலர் தாம மார்ப னுரிமையுந் தானு மாதோ","போதவிழ் கண்ணி யீர்த்துப் புனல்வரப் புலம்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Pammai tells the king that he is no longer her lover and that she and the others are strangers to him; and the garlanded man, undone of his claim and himself, wrenches at his flower wreath and laments as his tears pour.","n":"Which side of the break lines 1-2 name (the lady's, or the nuns') is hard to fix; உரிமையுந் தானுமாதோ is read as the king losing both his right and himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2643","lb":"CC2643","mm":1,"ev":"Pammai tells him he has no more claim on her, and Civakan weeps and laments.","cl":[["one who has taken the vow is no longer a husband's own, and the order is a stranger to his claim","jain","asserted",1,"pammai"]],"ag":["பம்மை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பம்மை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2644":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2644,"t":["ஏதில னாயி னாலு மிறைவர்தம் மறத்தை நோக்கக்","காதல னடிக ளென்னக் கண்கனிந் துருகிக் காசின்","மாதவ மகளி ரெல்லா மாபெருந் தேவி யாரை","யேதமொன் றில்லை நம்பிக் கின்னுரை கொடுமி னென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The nuns, moved by the Lords' dharma, say that though he is a stranger to them he is dear to the order, and tell the great queens to give the king gentle words, for he has done no wrong.","n":"The line-by-line sense of lines 1-2 is uncertain and the reading gives only its general turn.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arul","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2644","lb":"CC2644","mm":1,"ev":"The nuns urge the great queens to speak kindly to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவ மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவ மகளிர்","மாபெருந் தேவி"],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2645":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2645,"t":["திரைவள ரிப்பி யீன்ற திருமணி யார மார்பின்","வரைவளர் சாந்த மார்ந்த வைரக்குன் றனைய திண்டோள்","விரைவளர் கோதை வேலோய் வேண்டிய வேண்டி னேமென்","றுரைவிளைத் துரைப்பக் காளை யுள்ளகங் குளிர்ந்து சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Addressing him as the spear-bearer with the diamond-hill shoulders and the garland, they say they have asked what they wished; his heart cools and he speaks.","n":"Who the speakers are is taken from the preceding verse, which sends the nuns' message to the great queens.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2645","lb":"CC2645","mm":1,"ev":"The queens tell Civakan they have asked what they wished, and he answers with a cool heart.","cl":[],"ag":["மாபெருந் தேவி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாபெருந் தேவி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2646":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2646,"t":["அடிகளோ துறக்க வொன்று முற்றவர் யாது மல்லர்","சுடுதுய ரென்கட் செய்தாய் சுநந்தைநீ யௌவை யல்லை","கொடியைநீ கொடியை செய்தாய் கொடியையோ கொடியை யென்னா","விடருற்றோர் சிங்கந் தாய்மு னிருந்தழு கின்ற தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Crying that the lady may go but that the rest who joined are nothing to him, he cries to Sunandai that she has given him a burning grief and is no mother, and calls her cruel again and again, like a lion in distress crying before its mother.","n":"The speaker is taken as Nandattan, Sunandai's son, because the simile has a lion crying before its mother and 2648 answers Nandattan; Civakan, her foster son, is possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai","anbu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2646","lb":"CC2646","mm":1,"ev":"He rebukes Sunandai for leaving, calling her no mother.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சுநந்தை"],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2647":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2647,"t":["சென்றதோ செல்க விப்பாற் றிருமக ளனைய நங்கை","யின்றிவ டுறப்ப யானின் னரசுவந் திருப்பே னாயி","னென்றெனக் கொழியு மம்மா பழியென விளங்கு செம்பொற்","குன்றனான் குளிர்ப்பக் கூறிக் கோயில்புக் கருளு கென்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"A woman answers that what has gone is gone: if, now that this lady has renounced today, she herself sat on in the king's realm, the blame would never leave her; and with these words to cool the golden-hill-like hero she bids him go in to the palace.","n":"Whether இவள் is the speaker herself or the lady who has renounced, and who the speaker is, cannot be fixed from this verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","arasu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2647","lb":"CC2647","mm":1,"ev":"She consoles him and asks him to return to the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["மாபெருந் தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாபெருந் தேவி","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2648":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2648,"t":["பந்தட்ட விலலி னார்தம் படாமுலை கிழித்த பைந்தார்","நந்தட்டன் றன்னை நோக்கி நங்கையா ரடிகள் சொன்னார்","நொந்திட்டு முனிய வேண்டா துறந்தில நும்மை யென்னக்","கந்தட்ட திணிதிண் டோளான் கற்பக மலர்ந்த தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The lady ascetic tells Nandattan, whose garland tore the bosoms of ball-playing women, that he need not be hurt and angry, for they have not given him up; and the strong-shouldered man opens like a wish-tree in bloom.","n":"நங்கையார் அடிகள் is read as the matron who has taken the vow, Nandattan's mother Sunandai; the verse does not name her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","anbu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2648","lb":"CC2648","mm":1,"ev":"The lady ascetic comforts Nandattan that she has not forsaken him.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவி","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவி","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:113"},"cc:2649":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2649,"t":["துறந்த விந்நங்கை மார்தந் தூம லரனைய பாத","முறைந்தவென் சென்னிப் போதின் மிசையவென் றொப்ப வேத்திக்","கறந்தபா லனைய கந்திக் கொம்படுத் துருவப் பைம்பூண்","பிறங்குதார் மார்பன் போந்து பெருமணக் கோயில் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"Having praised the feet of the women who renounced as flowers resting on his head, the garlanded hero goes and enters the great fragrant temple.","n":"The nun-like figure behind கந்தி and the antecedent of the pronouns are not given in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2649","lb":"CC2649","mm":1,"ev":"The hero worships the renounced women's feet and enters the temple.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2650":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2650,"t":["வடிநிரை நெடிய கண்ணார் மாமிமார் விடுப்ப வேகிக்","கடிநிரை சிவிகை யேறிக் கதிர்மணிக் குடைபின் செல்ல","வுடைதிரைப் பரவை யன்ன வொளிறுவேன் மறவர் காப்பக்","கொடிநிரைக் கோயில் புக்கார் குங்குமக் கொடிய னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Sent off by the mothers-in-law, the saffron-vined women ride a gem-canopied palanquin guarded by spearmen and enter the flag-lined palace.","n":"Whether these are the women of the preceding verse or the hero's wives cannot be fixed from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2650","lb":"CC2650","mm":1,"ev":"The women, dismissed by their mothers-in-law, go in procession to the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["குங்குமக் கொடியனார்","மாமிமார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2651":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2651,"t":["முழுதுல கெழிலேத்து மூரிவேற் றானை மன்னன்","றொழுதகு பெருமாட்டி தூமணிப் பாவை யன்னாள்","பொழிதரு மழைமொக்குட் போகம்விட் டாசை நீக்கி","வழிவரு தவமெய்தி வைகின டெய்வ மன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king's honoured lady, like a doll of pure gem, gives up pleasure that is like a rain-bubble, puts away desire and takes up the austerity of her line, living like a goddess.","n":"The poem shows renunciation by a woman of the court before the hero turns to it himself, and gives the bubble image as the reason.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","nilaiyaamai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2651","lb":"CC2651","mm":1,"ev":"The king's lady gives up enjoyment and takes to austerity.","cl":[["worldly pleasure is as short-lived as a rain-bubble and is to be given up with desire","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["பெருமாட்டி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2652":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2652,"t":["உடைதிரை முத்தஞ் சிந்த வோசனிக் கின்ற வன்னம்","படர்கதிர்த் திங்க ளாகப் பரந்துவான் பூத்த தென்னா","வடர்பிணி யவிழு மாம்ப லலைகடற் கானற் சேர்ப்பன்","குடைகெழு வேந்தற் கிப்பா லுற்றது கூற லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet of the sea-shore, with its swans and lilies, says he will now tell what befell the umbrella-king.","n":"A poet's turn to a new matter, in the narrator's own voice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2652","lb":"CC2652","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2653":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2653,"t":["துறவின்பாற் படர்த லஞ்சித் தொத்தொளி முத்துத் தாம","முறைகின்ற வுருவக் கோலச் சிகழிகை மகளி ரின்பத்","திறைவனை மகிழ்ச்சி செய்து மயங்குவா னமைச்ச ரெண்ணி","நிறையநீர் வாவி சாந்தங் கலந்துடன் பூரித் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Fearing that the king will turn towards renunciation, the ministers work to keep him in the pleasure of the women and fill a water-tank with scented water.","n":"The ministers' fear (துறவின்பாற் படர்தல் அஞ்சி) states the cause of the whole water-sport: it is a device against his leaving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2653","lb":"CC2653","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers, fearing his turn to renunciation, prepare a pleasure-pond to hold the king.","cl":[],"ag":["அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2654":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2654,"t":["நீரணி மாட வாவி நேர்ம்புணை நிறைத்து நீணீர்ப்","போரணி மாலை சாந்தம் புனைமணிச் சிவிறி சுண்ணம்","வாரணி முலையி னார்க்கு மன்னற்கும் வகுத்து வாவி","யேரணி கொண்ட விந்நீ ரிறைவகண் டருளு கென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The ministers fill the tank with floats, garlands, sandal paste, gem syringes and powders for the women and the king, and ask the lord to come and see it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2654","lb":"CC2654","mm":1,"ev":"The ministers equip the pool and invite the king to come and see it.","cl":[],"ag":["அமைச்சர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2655":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2655,"t":["கணமலை யரசன் மங்கை கட்டியங் கார னாகப்","பணைமுலை மகளி ரெல்லாம் பவித்திரன் படைய தாக","விணைமலர் மாலை சுண்ண மெரிமணிச் சிவிறி யேந்திப்","புணைபுறந் தழுவித் தூநீர்ப் போர்த்தொழி றொடங்கி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women are drawn into two armies, with the mountain king's daughter taking the enemy general's part and the rest the king's host, and they open a water-war with garlands, powder and syringes.","n":"கட்டியங்காரன் here is a role in the game, not the usurping minister of the story; the mountain-king's daughter is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2655","lb":"CC2655","mm":1,"ev":"The women divide into the king's army and the mock enemy and begin the water-battle.","cl":[],"ag":["கணமலை அரசன் மங்கை","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2656":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2656,"t":["தூமலர் மாலை வாளாச் சுரும்பெழப் புடைத்துந் தேன்சோர்","தாமரைச் சதங்கை மாலை சக்கர மென்ன வீழ்த்துங்","காமரு கணைய மாகக் கண்ணிக ளொழுக விட்டுந்","தோமர மாகத் தொங்கல் சிதறுபு மயங்கி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women strike with flower garlands that scatter bees, hurl lotus chains of bells like discs and let garlands fall like arrows, so that the tangle of garlands is their weapon.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2656","lb":"CC2656","mm":1,"ev":"The women fight with flower garlands used as arms.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2657":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2657,"t":["அரக்குநீர்ச் சிவிறி யேந்தி யாயிரந் தாரை செல்லப்","பரப்பினாள் பாவை தத்தை பைந்தொடி மகளி ரெல்லாந்","தரிக்கிலா ராகித் தாழ்ந்து தடமுகிற் குளிக்கு மின்போற்","செருக்கிய நெடுங்கண் சேப்பச் சீதநீர் மூழ்கி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Gandharvadattai takes up a syringe of red water and sends a thousand streams, and the women, unable to bear it, plunge like lightning into a cloud, with their eyes reddened by the cold water.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2657","lb":"CC2657","mm":1,"ev":"Gandharvadattai sprays the women, who dive into the water.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2658":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2658,"t":["தானக மாட மேறித் தையலார் ததும்பப் பாய்வார்","வானகத் திழியுந் தோகை மடமயிற் குழாங்க ளொத்தார்","தேனின மிரியத் தெண்ணீர்க் குளித்தெழுந் திருவி னன்னார்","பான்மிசை சொரியுந் திங்கள் பனிக்கடன் முளைத்த தொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The women climb the pool's pavilion and leap in like peacocks coming down from the sky, and rise from the clear water like lakshmis or like a moon newly risen on the dewy sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2658","lb":"CC2658","mm":1,"ev":"The women leap from the pavilion into the pool.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2659":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2659,"t":["கண்ணிகொண் டெறிய வஞ்சிக் காறளர்ந் தசைந்து சோர்வார்","சுண்ணமுஞ் சாந்தும் வீழத் தொழுதன ரிரந்து நிற்பா","ரொண்மலர் மாலை யோச்ச வொசிந்துகண் பிறழ வொல்கி","வெண்ணெயிற் குழைந்து நிற்பார் வேற்கணா ராயி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Struck with garlands the women sag and falter, beg with powder and sandal falling, bend to the thrown garlands and melt like butter.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2659","lb":"CC2659","mm":1,"ev":"The women, struck with garlands, melt and plead for mercy.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2660":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2660,"t":["கூந்தலை யொருகை யேந்திக் குங்குமத் தாரை பாயப்","பூந்துகி லொருகை யேந்திப் புகுமிடங் காண்டல் செல்லார்","வேந்தனைச் சரணென் றெய்த விம்முறு துயர நோக்கிக்","காய்ந்துபொற் சிவிறி யேந்திக் கார்மழை பொழிவ தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The women with their hair in one hand and their silk in the other, unable to watch where they step, run to the king for refuge, and he, seeing their distress, takes up a golden syringe and pours like a rain-cloud.","n":"The king's anger is playful: காய்ந்து is the word for it, set against a pour compared to rain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","vendhan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2660","lb":"CC2660","mm":1,"ev":"The women flee to the king, who answers with his own syringe.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2661":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2661,"t":["வீக்கினான் றாரை வெய்தாச் சந்தனத் தளிர்நன் மாலை","யோக்கினார் கண்ணி சுண்ண முடற்றினா ருருவச் சாந்திற்","பூக்கமழ் துகிலுந் தோடு மாலையுஞ் சொரியப் போர்தோற்","றாக்கிய வநங்கன் சேனை யாறலா றாயிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king looses streams, the women let their garlands and powder and perfumed cloths fall, and the army of the love-god, defeated in the war, is routed.","n":"The battle names the love-god's army as the women's, and it is the women who lose; Kama's army is beaten by the king in play.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2661","lb":"CC2661","mm":1,"ev":"The king's streams win; the women's garlands and cloth fall and the love-god's army is routed.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2662":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2662,"t":["அன்னங்க ளாகி யம்பூந் தாமரை யல்லி மேய்வார்","பொன்மயி லாகிக் கூந்தல் போர்த்தனர் குனிந்து நிற்பா","ரின்மலர்க் கமல மாகிப் பூமுகம் பொருந்த வைப்பார்","மின்னுமே கலையுந் தோடுங் கொடுத்தடி தொழுது நிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The beaten women turn into swans among lotuses, peacocks hiding in their hair, lotuses laying faces on his, and give up their girdles and earrings and bow to touch his feet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2662","lb":"CC2662","mm":1,"ev":"The defeated women submit, giving girdles and earrings and touching his feet.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2663":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2663,"t":["பண்ணுரை மகளிர் மாலை பைந்துகில் கவர்ந்து கொள்ளக்","கண்ணுரை மகளிர் சோர்ந்து காரிருட் டிவளு மின்போற்","பெண்ணுரைப் பிடிக்கைக் கூந்தற் பொன்னரி மாலை தாழ","வெண்ணுரை யுடுத்து நின்றார் வேந்தனோக் குண்ண நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The women who have lost their garlands and silk stand wrapped only in a white foam, their lightning-like bodies shaking, looking to the king.","n":"The first two lines compare the women's eyes and the snatching of garlands in a way the verse does not make clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2663","lb":"CC2663","mm":1,"ev":"The women, stripped of garlands and silk by the sport, stand looking at the king.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2664":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2664,"t":["தன்படை யுடையத் தத்தை சந்தனத் தாரை வீக்கி","யொன்பது முகத்தி னோடி யுறுவலி யகலம் பாயப்","பொன்படு சுணங்கு போர்த்த பொங்கிள முலையிற் றூவான்","முன்படு குலிகத் தாரை முழுவலி முறுக்க லுற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Her ranks broken, Gandharvadattai binds on a stream of sandal and, running with nine jets, sprays red powder over the king's wide chest and begins to twist its force against him.","n":"Gandharvadattai is named in 2657 and again here, the one wife who stays to fight on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2664","lb":"CC2664","mm":1,"ev":"Gandharvadattai, her side broken, turns a nine-jet stream of red powder on the king's chest.","cl":[],"ag":["காந்தருவதத்தை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2665":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2665,"t":["மெய்ப்படு தாரை வீழி னோமிவட் கென்ன வஞ்சிக்","கைப்படை மன்ன னிற்பக் கதுப்பயன் மாலை வாங்கிச்","செப்பட முன்கை யாப்பத் திருமகன் றொலைந்து நின்றான்","பைப்புடை யல்கு லாளைப் பாழியாற் படுக்க லுற்றே."],"p":"","r":"A slender woman with a hand-weapon stands before the king and takes his garland; he, undone, stands helpless as she tries to bind and throw the other woman by force.","n":"The pronouns of the last two lines are hard to fix; I read the king as the one who stands helpless.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2665","lb":"CC2665","mm":1,"ev":"A woman disarms the king of his garland and the game turns on him.","cl":[],"ag":["வஞ்சி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2666":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2666,"t":["அடுத்தசாந் தலங்கல் சுண்ண மரும்புனல் கவர வஞ்சி","யுடுத்தபட் டொளிப்ப வொண்பொன் மேகலை யொன்றும் பேசா","கிடப்பமற் றரச னோக்கிக் கெட்டதுன் றுகின்மற் றென்ன","மடத்தகை நாணிப் புல்லி மின்னுச்சேர் பருதி யொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The sandal and powder are carried off by the water, and the slender woman's silk is lost and her gold girdle lies silent; looking at the king she asks what has fallen, and shy, embraces him like the sun joined with lightning.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2666","lb":"CC2666","mm":1,"ev":"A woman loses her silk to the water and, ashamed, embraces the king.","cl":[],"ag":["வஞ்சி","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2667":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2667,"t":["விம்மகிற் புகையின் மேவி யுடம்பினை வேது செய்து","கொம்மென நாவி நாறுங் கூந்தலை யுலர்த்தி நொய்ய","வம்மல ருரோமப் பூம்பட் டுடுத்தபி னனிச்ச மாலை","செம்மலர்த் திருவி னன்னார் சிகழிகைச் சேர்த்தி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"After the sport the women steam their bodies with agil smoke, dry their fragrant hair, put on fine silk and set anicham garlands in their tresses.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2667","lb":"CC2667","mm":1,"ev":"The women dry and dress themselves after the water-sport.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2668":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2668,"t":["கார்கொள் குன்றன கண்கவர் தோளினா","னீர்கொ ணீரணி நின்று கனற்றலின்","வார்கொண் மென்முலை வம்பணி கோதையா","ரேர்கோள் சாயலுண் டாடுமற் றென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"They say the women, with bright garlands, enjoyed the grace of the man with mountain-like shoulders whose body the water had heated.","n":"The metre changes here; the opening lines are hard to read exactly and the verse is closed with என்பவே, 'they say'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2668","lb":"CC2668","mm":1,"ev":"The women enjoy the king's company after the water-sport.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2669":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2669,"t":["வேனில் வாய்க்கதிர் வெம்பலின் மேனிலைத்","தேனு லாங்குளிர் சந்தனச் சேற்றிடைத்","தானு லாய்த்தட மென்முலைத் தங்கினான்","பானி லாக்கதிர் பாய்தரு பள்ளியே."],"p":"","r":"In the heat of summer the king lies on a couch cooled by sandal paste and moon-rays, staying on the breasts of the woman who lies with him.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2669","lb":"CC2669","mm":1,"ev":"The king rests with a woman on a sandal-cooled couch.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2670":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2670,"t":["முழுது மெய்ந்நல மூழ்கலி னீர்சுமந்","தெழுது கண்ணிரங் கப்புரு வக்கொடி","தொழுவ போன்முரி யச்சொரி பூஞ்சிகை","யழுவ போன்றணி நித்தில முக்கவே."],"p":"","r":"Her gem ornaments seem to weep pearls, her brow like a vine bends in worship and the flowers of her hair sink as if she were tired with the whole body's pleasure.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2670","lb":"CC2670","mm":1,"ev":"A woman gives her whole body to pleasure with the king.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2671":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2671,"t":["எழுத்தின் பாடலு மாடலு மென்றிவை","பழத்த கற்பகப் பன்மணிக் கொம்பனா","ரழுத்தி யன்ன வணிவளைத் தோண்மிசைக்","கழிக்கு மைங்கணைக் காமற்குங் காமனே."],"p":"","r":"The women, like coral boughs of the wishing-tree, know writing, song and dance, and the king, in their company, is the very love-god of the five-arrow love-god.","n":"The compliment makes the king the love-god of the love-god, setting him above the god he has just defeated in the water-war of 2661.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2671","lb":"CC2671","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2672":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2672,"t":["நீர்து ளும்பு வயிற்றின் னிழன்முகில்","பார்து ளும்ப முழங்கலிற் பல்கலை","யேர்து ளும்ப வெரீஇயிறை வற்றழீஇக்","கார்து ளும்புகொம் பிற்கவி னெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"Frightened by the thunder of the clouds, the women with their hair like a rain-cloud's shadow embrace the king and gain the beauty of boughs clinging to a tree.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2672","lb":"CC2672","mm":1,"ev":"The women, frightened by thunder, cling to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2673":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2673,"t":["இழிந்து கீழ்நிலை யின்னகிற் சேக்கைமேற்","கிழிந்து சாந்தழி யக்கிளர் மென்முலை","தொழிந்து மட்டொழு கத்துதை தார்பொர","வழிந்த மேகலை யஞ்சிலம் பார்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The women descend to the low agil-scented couch, their sandal paste wiped off, their breasts bruised by garlands, and their girdles and anklets sounding as they fall apart.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2673","lb":"CC2673","mm":1,"ev":"The women lie with the king on the low couch, their paste and girdles undone.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:114"},"cc:2674":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2674,"t":["தேனி றாலன தீஞ்சுவை யின்னடை","யான றாமுலைப் பாலமு தல்லதொன்","றானு மேவல ரச்சுற வெய்திய","மான றாமட நோக்கிய ரென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The verse praises tender, doe-eyed women, said to be like does that have been startled; its middle lines are too obscure to construe with confidence.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 (யான றாமுலைப் பாலமுது, ஒன்றானும் மேவலர்) do not yield a certain sentence, so only the frame of praise is recorded.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2674","lb":"CC2674","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2675":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2675,"t":["கூதிர்வந் துலாவலிற் குவவு மென்முலை","வேதுசெய் சாந்தமும் வெய்ய தேறலும்","போதவிழ் மாலையும் புகையுஞ் சுண்ணமுங்","காதலித் தார்கருங் குவளைக் கண்ணினார்."],"p":"","r":"When the cold season has come and moves about, the dark-lily-eyed women take to warm sandal paste for their full breasts, hot wine, opening-flower garlands, incense smoke and powder.","n":"The season is felt through what the women reach for: warmth and scent stand for the cold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2675","lb":"CC2675","mm":1,"ev":"The women, in the cold season, take to warming pastes, wine, garlands and incense.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2676":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2676,"t":["சுரும்புநின் றறாமலர்த் தொங்க லார்கவி","னரும்புகின் றார்கட லமிர்த மேயெனா","விரும்புகின் றானிள வேனில் வேந்தனைஞ்","சரங்கள்சென் றழுத்தலிற் றரணி மன்னனே."],"p":"","r":"Women in bee-humming garlands put out their beauty like buds, and the earth's king, thinking them sea-nectar, longs for them while the five arrows of spring's king press upon him.","n":"The one pressed by the arrows is called the earth's king (தரணி மன்னன்) without a name; the context makes Civakan likely, but the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2676","lb":"CC2676","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2677":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2677,"t":["குழைமுக மிடவயிற் கோட்டி யேந்திய","வழனிறத் தேறலுண் மதிகண் டையென","நிழன்முகப் பகைகெடப் பருகி நீள்விசும்","புலலெனா நோக்குவாண் மதிகண்டூடினாள்."],"p":"","r":"A woman tilts a cup of fire-coloured wine at her left side, sees the moon in it, and drinks it down as if to destroy a rival's shadow, then sulks as she looks at the sky.","n":"The sulk is aimed at the moon reflected in the wine, not at a person; who she is the verse does not say, though the next verse names Gunamalai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2677","lb":"CC2677","mm":1,"ev":"A woman sees the moon in her wine cup, drinks it and sulks.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2678":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2678,"t":["பருகினேற் கொளித்துநீ பசலை நோயொடு","முருகிப்போ யின்னுமற் றுளையென் றுள்சுடக்","குருதிகண் கொளக்குண மாலை யூடினா","ளுருவத்தா லுறத்தழீஇ யுடற்றி நீக்குவான்"],"p":"","r":"Saying to the moon that she has drunk it and it hid and wasted with pallor yet lingers, Gunamalai sulks with eyes gone red, and he sets out to drive the sulk away by holding her close.","n":"The sulk is built on the moon-face conceit: she treats the reflected moon as a rival who has paled before her. The final line is left open and runs on into the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2678","lb":"CC2678","mm":1,"ev":"Gunamalai sulks at the moon in her cup, and Civakan sets out to end the sulk by embracing her.","cl":[],"ag":["குணமாலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குணமாலை",""],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2679":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2679,"t":["நங்கைநின் முகவொளி யெறிப்ப நன்மதி","யங்கதோ வுள்கறுத் தழகிற் றேய்ந்தது","மங்கைநின் மனத்தினால் வருந்த லென்றவள்","பொங்கிள வனமுலை பொருந்தி னானரோ."],"p":"","r":"He tells her that the moon has darkened within and wasted in beauty before the light of her face, bids her not grieve in her heart, and takes her in his arms.","n":"The quarrel is ended by turning her own moon conceit into praise; he does not argue the sulk but flatters it away.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2679","lb":"CC2679","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan consoles Gunamalai by saying the moon pales before her face, and embraces her.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","குணமாலை"],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2680":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2680,"t":["கொங்கு விம்முபூங் கோதை மாதரார்","பங்க யப்பகைப் பருவம் வந்தென","வெங்கு மில்லன வெலிம யிர்த்தொழிற்","பொங்கு பூம்புகைப் போர்வை மேயினார்."],"p":"","r":"As the dewy season that wilts the lotus arrives, the garlanded women wrap themselves in a cloak of fragrant rising smoke.","n":"The lotus's enemy is the cold season; the women answer it with smoke as a wrap.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2680","lb":"CC2680","mm":1,"ev":"The women wrap themselves in incense smoke against the dewy season.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2681":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2681,"t":["கூந்த லின்புகைக் குவவு மென்முலைச்","சாந்த மேந்திய தமால மாலையு","மாய்ந்து தாங்கின ரரவ மேகலை","காய்ந்து நித்திலங் கடிய சிந்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"With smoke in their hair, sandal on their soft breasts, tamala garlands and serpent-like girdles, the women angrily scatter pearls.","n":"The scattering of pearls from a girdle is the poet's sign of lovers' passion or haste; the verse does not say who angers them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2681","lb":"CC2681","mm":1,"ev":"The women scatter pearls from their girdles.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2682":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2682,"t":["அளிந்த தீம்பழ மிஞ்சி யார்ந்தநீர்","விளைந்த வல்விளை வரிசி வேரியும்","வளைந்த மின்னனார் மகிழ்ந்து சண்பக","முளைந்து மல்லிகை யொலியல் சூடினார்."],"p":"","r":"The women, delighted, drink fruit wine and rice liquor and wear champak and jasmine garlands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2682","lb":"CC2682","mm":1,"ev":"The women drink fruit wine and rice liquor and put on champak and jasmine.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2683":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2683,"t":["தொத்து டைம்மலர்த் தொங்கல் கண்பொர","முத்து டைம்முலைக் கண்க ணொந்தவென்","றெய்த்த டிச்சிலம் பிரங்கு மின்குரல்","கைத்தெ டுத்தலிற் காமந் தாழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Garlands strike the women's eyes and their pearled breasts ache; anklets give a tired sound as hands are lifted, and desire sinks.","n":"The last clause, காமந் தாழ்ந்ததே, can be read as desire lowered or as passion grown heavy; the verse leaves it open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2683","lb":"CC2683","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2684":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2684,"t":["பொன்ப னிப்புறும் பொற்பி னார்நல","மன்ப னித்தலை யணங்க வத்தலை","முன்ப னித்தலை முழுது நீங்கிப்போய்ப்","பின்ப னித்தலை பேண வந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The early dewy season passes entirely and the late dewy season comes to be tended, as the beauty of the women is dressed for it.","n":"The verse marks the turn of the cold seasons, early dew to late dew, and so places the chapter in the months leading to spring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2684","lb":"CC2684","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2685":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2685,"t":["வெள்ளி லோத்திரம் விளங்கும் வெண்மலர்க்","கள்செய் மாலையார் கண்கொ ளாத்துகி","லள்ளி யேந்திய வரத்த வல்குலா","ரொள்ளெ ரிம்மணி யுருவப் பூணினார்"],"p":"","r":"The women are described in white silk and white flower garlands, with red hips and jewelled ornaments gleaming like fire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2685","lb":"CC2685","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2686":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2686,"t":["செந்நெ ருப்புணுஞ் செவ்வெ லிம்மயி","ரந்நெ ருப்பள வாய்பொற் கம்பல","மன்ன ருய்ப்பன மகிழ்ந்து தாங்கினா","ரென்ன ரொப்புமில் லவர்க ளென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Kings give the women red blankets of fine fur warmed at the fire, which the women take gladly, and they are called peerless.","n":"The kings' gift is a cold-season comfort; the verse presents the women as receivers of royal gifts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2686","lb":"CC2686","mm":1,"ev":"Kings give the women fire-warmed red blankets, which they gladly take.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2687":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2687,"t":["ஆட லின்சுவை யமர்ந்து நாடொறும்","பாடன் மெய்ந்நிறீப் பருகிப் பண்சுவைத்","தோடு மாமதி யுரிஞ்சு மொண்பொனின்","மாடக் கீழ்நிலை மகிழ்ந்து வைகினார்."],"p":"","r":"Daily the women live happily in the lower storey of a golden mansion that rubs the moon, delighting in dance and drinking the sound of song.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2687","lb":"CC2687","mm":1,"ev":"The women pass their days in the golden mansion with dance and song.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2688":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2688,"t":["புரிக்குழன் மடந்தையர் பொம்மல் வெம்முலை","திருக்கழற் குருசிறார் திளைக்கும் போரினுட்","செருக்குரற் சிறுபறை சிலம்பு கிண்கிணி","யரிப்பறை மேகலை யாகி யார்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"In the lovers' battle between the curl-haired women and the heroes of the fine anklets, small drums, anklets, bells and girdles sound like the instruments of war.","n":"Lovemaking is figured as a battle, with the ornaments' sound as the war music.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2688","lb":"CC2688","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2689":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2689,"t":["ஏச்செயாச் சிலைநுத லேழை மார்முலைத்","தூச்செயாக் குங்குமந் துதைந்த வண்டினம்","வாய்ச்சியா லிட்டிகை செத்து மாந்தர்தம்","பூச்செயா மேனிபோற் பொலிந்து தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"The innocent women have bow-like brows and saffron on their breasts; bees settle on the paste in swarms, so that it seems laid down like brick, and it shines like the women's flower-hued bodies.","n":"The middle lines, வாய்ச்சியா லிட்டிகை செத்து, are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2689","lb":"CC2689","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2690":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2690,"t":["குரவம் பாவை கொப்புளித்துக் குளிர்சங் கீர்ந்த துகளேபோன்","மரவம் பாவை வயிறாரப் பருகி வாடை யதுநடப்ப","விரவித் தென்றல் விடுதூதா வேனி லாற்கு விருந்தேந்தி","வரவு நோக்கி வயாமரங்க ளிலையூழ்த் திணாபுன் றலர்ந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"As the north wind goes and the southern breeze is sent as envoy, the trees bring out new leaves and flowers to welcome the coming of the spring king.","n":"The coming of spring is told as a guest's arrival: the breeze is the envoy and the trees prepare the welcome.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2690","lb":"CC2690","mm":1,"ev":"The south breeze goes as envoy and the trees put out leaves and flowers to greet spring.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2691":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2691,"t":["இளிவாய்ப் பிரசம் யாழாக விருங்கட் டும்பி குழலாகக்","களிவாய்க் குயில்கண் முழவாகக் கடிபூம் பொழில்க ளரங்காகத்","தளிர்போன் மடவார்தணந்தார்தந் தடந்தோள் வளையு மாமையும்","விளியாக் கொண்டிங் கிளவேனில் விருந்தா வாட றொடக்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"Bees are the lute, dark beetles the flute, cuckoos the drum and the scented groves the stage, and spring begins his feast-dance, taking as his call the loosened bangles and pallor of women whom lovers have left.","n":"The celebration is paid for by the parted women: their wasted wrists are what spring takes up as his song.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","pirivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2691","lb":"CC2691","mm":1,"ev":"Spring begins a feast-dance in the groves, with the bangles and pallor of forsaken women for his call.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2692":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2692,"t":["வேனி லாடும் விருப்பினால் வியன்காய் நெல்லிச் சாந்தரைத்து","நான வெண்ணெய் கதுப்புரைத்து நறுநீ ராடி யமிர்துயிர்க்குந்","தேனா ரகிலின் புகைசேர்த்தி வகுத்து நாவிக் குழம்புறீஇ","யானாப் பளித நறுஞ்சுண்ண முகிரி னுழுதாங் கணிந்தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Out of desire for spring sport the women grind nelli-fruit paste, rub civet-butter into their hair, bathe in scented water, perfume themselves with agil smoke, and put on fragrant powders.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2692","lb":"CC2692","mm":1,"ev":"The women bathe, perfume and powder themselves for the spring sport.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2693":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2693,"t":["முத்தார் மருப்பி னிடைவளைத்த முரண்கொள் யானைத் தடக்கையி","னொத்தே ருடைய மல்லிகையி னொலியன் மாலை யுறுப்படக்கி","வைத்தார் மணிநூற் றனவைம்பால் வளைய முடித்து வான்கழுநீ","ருய்த்தாங் கதனுட் கொளவழுத்திக் குவளைச் செவித்தா துறுத்தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women gather jasmine garlands over their long plaits and set blue water-lilies in their ears.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2693","lb":"CC2693","mm":1,"ev":"The women put jasmine garlands in their plaits and lilies in their ears.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2694":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2694,"t":["புகையார் வண்ணப் பட்டுடுத்துப் பொன்னங் கலைகள் புறஞ்சூழ்ந்து","நகையார் கவுள கிண்கிணியுஞ் சிலம்பு நாய்நாச் சீறடிமேற்","பகைகொண் டார்போற் சுமாஅய்க்கண்பின் பரூஉக்காம் பனைய கணைக் கால் சூழ்ந்","தகையார்ந் திலக்கும் பரியகந் தாமே கவினச் சேர்த்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"The women put on smoke-coloured silk, golden girdles and ringing anklets, and adorn their calves and ankles.","n":"Line 3 has an odd spacing (சுமாஅய்க்கண்பின் பரூஉக்காம் பனைய கணைக் கால் சூழ்ந்) that makes the words hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2694","lb":"CC2694","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2695":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2695,"t":["பிடிக்கை வென்று கடைந்தனபோற் பஞ்சி யார்ந்த திரள் குறங்கு","கடித்துக் கிடந்து கவின்வளருங் காய்பொன் மகரங் கதிர்முலைமே","லுடுத்த சாந்தின் மிசைச்செக்க ரொளிகொண் முந்நாட் பிறையேய்ப்பத்","துடிக்குங் குதிர் சேர் துணைமுத்தந் திருவில் லுமிழ்ந்து சுடர்ந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Thighs, golden makara ornaments and pearl strings on the breasts are described, with the pearls compared to a three-day crescent that gives off light like a rainbow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2695","lb":"CC2695","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2696":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2696,"t":["குழியப் பெரிய கோன்முன்கை மணியார் காந்தட் குவிவிரன்மேற்","கழியப் பெரிய வருவிலைய சிறிய மணிமோ திரங்கனலத்","தழியப் பெரிய தடமென்றோட் சலாகை மின்னத் தாழ்ந்திலங்கும்","விழிகண் மகர குண்டலமுந் தோடுங் காதின் மிளிர்ந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The women's ears gleam with makara-shaped earrings and studs that outweigh their size in price.","n":"The gems are said to be small in size and very great in value.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2696","lb":"CC2696","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2697":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2697,"t":["நாணுள் ளிட்டுச் சுடர்வீசு நன்மா ணிக்க நகுதாலி","பேணி நல்லார் கழுத்தணிந்து பெருங்கண் கருமை விருந்தூட்டி","நீணீர் முத்த நிரைமுறுவல் கடுச்சுட் டுரிஞ்சக் கதிருமிழ்ந்து","தோணீர்க் கடலுட் பவளவாய்த் தொண்டைக் கனிக டொழுதனவே."],"p":"","r":"Bright gem necklaces on the women's necks, large eyes feasting on darkness, pearl-bright teeth and coral lips like fruits all shine and bow.","n":"The last clause is hard to read; the pearl-and-coral figures are plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2697","lb":"CC2697","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2698":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2698,"t":["மாலை மகளி ரணிந்ததற்பின் பஞ்ச வாசங் கவுட்கொண்டு","சோலை மஞ்ஞைத் தொழுதிபோற் றோகை செம்பொ னிலந்திவளக்","காலிற் சிலம்புங் கிண்கிணியுங் கலையு மேங்கக் கதிர்வேலு","நீலக் குவளை நிரையும்போற் கண்ணார் காவி லிருந்தாரே."],"p":"","r":"When evening has come and the women have dressed, they keep five-scent pastes in their cheeks and sit in the grove like flocks of peacocks, with anklets, bells and girdles sounding and their eyes like spear and blue lilies.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2698","lb":"CC2698","mm":1,"ev":"The dressed women sit in the garden at evening.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:115"},"cc:2699":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2699,"t":["மணிவண் டொன்றெ நலம்பருக மலர்ந்த செந்தா மரைத்தடம்போ","லணிவேன் மன்ன னலம்பருக வலர்ந்த வம்பார் மழைக்கண்ணார்","பணியார் பண்ணுப் பிடியூர்ந்து பரூஉக்காற் செந்நெற் கதிர்சூடித்","தணியார் கழனி விளையாடித் தகைபா ராட்டத் தங்கினார்."],"p":"","r":"The king and his bright-eyed women, drunk on one another's beauty like a bee on a red lotus, ride she-elephants, wear sprays of ripe paddy and play in the fields until they stop to praise one another.","n":"Nothing names the king; he is the lord of the preceding verses (the wives' pleasure outing of the closing ilampakam) and is known only from the story so far.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2699","lb":"CC2699","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan and his women play in the paddy fields and halt there, praising each other.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2700":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2700,"t":["எண்ணற் கரிய குங்குமச்சேற் றெழுந்து நான நீர்வளர்ந்து","வண்ணக் குவளை மலரளைஇ மணிக்கோல் வள்ளத் தவனேந்த","வுண்ணற் கினிய மதுமகிழ்ந்தா ரொலியன் மாலை புறந்தாழக்","கண்ணக் கழுநீர் மெல்விரலாற் கிழித்து மோந்தார் கனிவாயார்."],"p":"","r":"The women, in saffron paste and musk water, take a cup of honey wine the king holds out in a jewelled bowl, then tear and smell the red water-lily garlands with soft fingers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2700","lb":"CC2700","mm":1,"ev":"The king hands his women wine in a jewelled cup, and they drink and tear flower garlands.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2701":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2701,"t":["இவ்வா றெங்கும் விளையாடி யிளையான் மார்பி னலம்பருகிச்","செவ்வாய் விளர்த்துத் தோண்மெலிந்து மணிக்கோல் வள்ளத் தவனேந்த","செய்ய முலையின் முகங்கருகி யனிச்ச மலரும் பொறையாகி","யொவ்வாப் பஞ்சி மெல்லணைமே லசைந்தா ரொண்பொற் கொடியன்னார்."],"p":"","r":"After play on the young king's chest, the women's red lips pale, their shoulders thin and their breasts darken as they sink wearily onto the cotton bed.","n":"Line 2 repeats the second line of 2700 word for word, a sign the refrain has been copied across, or a deliberate echo.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2701","lb":"CC2701","mm":1,"ev":"The women, worn out from love, lie down on the soft bed.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2702":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2702,"t":["தீம்பால் சுமந்து முலைவீங்கித் திருமுத்தீன்ற வலம்புரிபோற்","காம்பேர் தோளார் களிறீன்றார் கடைக டோறுங் கடிமுரசந்","தாம்பாற் பட்ட தனிச்செங்கோற் றரணி மன்னன் மகிழ்தூங்கி","யோம்பா தொண்பொன் சொரிமாரி யுலக முண்ணச் சிதறினான்."],"p":"","r":"The wives bear sons, and at every gate of the city war-drums are struck; the king, glad, pours out gold like rain for the world to take.","n":"The only 'king' named is the one with the sole bright sceptre; the verse links the gold to his joy rather than to any rule of giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","eegai","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2702","lb":"CC2702","mm":1,"ev":"The queens bear sons and the king scatters gold throughout the city in joy.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2703":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2703,"t":["காடி யாட்டித் தராய்ச் சாறுங் கன்னன் மணியு நறுநெய்யுங்","கூடச் செம்பொன் கொளத்தேய்த்துக் கொண்டு நாளும் வாயுறீஇப்","பாடற் கினிய பகுவாயுங் கண்ணும் பெருக வுகிருறுத்தித்","தேடித் தீந்தேன் றிப்பிலிதேய்த் தண்ணா வுரிஞ்சி மூக்குயர்த்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who tend the infants rub them with fermented rice-water, herb juice, gem-paste and ghee, trim the nails, and press the mouth, eyes and nose with honey and long pepper so they will grow fair.","n":"The subject is never named; I take it to be nurses and mothers, which the verse does not fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2703","lb":"CC2703","mm":1,"ev":"The sons' carers bathe and shape the infants with oils, juices and honey.","cl":[],"ag":["வளர்ப்பார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2704":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2704,"t":["யாழுங் குழலு மணிமுழவு மரங்க மெல்லாம் பரந்திசைப்பத்","தோழன் விண்ணோ னவட்டோன்றி வயங்காக் கூத்து வயங்கியபின்","காழார் வெள்ளி மலைமேலுங் காவன் மன்னார் கடிநகர்க்கும்","வீழா வோகை யவன்விட்டான் விண்பெற் றாரின் விரும்பினார்."],"p":"","r":"As lute, flute and drum sound everywhere, a divine friend appears to the king, dances a dance never before shown, and sends out a gladness that reaches the Silver Mountain and the kings' cities, so that all rejoice as if they had won heaven.","n":"The god is not named; the book's earlier verses show Civakan's divine friend, and I take him for that god. Who 'let go' the joy in line 4 (the god or the king) is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2704","lb":"CC2704","mm":1,"ev":"A heavenly friend of the king appears and dances, and the festival of the births spreads over the Vidyadhara mountain and the kings' cities.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2705":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2705,"t":["தத்த நிலனு முயர்விழிவும் பகையு நட்புந்தந்தசையும்","வைத்து வழுவில் சாதகமும் வகுத்த பின்னர்த் தொகுத்தநாட்","சச்சந் தணனே சுதஞ்சணனே தரணி கந்துக் கடன்விசயன்","றத்தன் பரதன் கோவிந்த னென்று நாமந் தரித்தாரே."],"p":"","r":"After fixing the sons' birth-places, the highs and lows of their charts, their friendships and enmities, and drawing up their horoscopes, the elders name the eight: Sachchandan, Sudanjanan, Dharani, Kandukkadan, Vijayan, Dattan, Bharatan and Govindan.","n":"The names reuse those of the hero's father, his helping god, his foster father and his uncle, so the line keeps the hero's own kin alive in the next generation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2705","lb":"CC2705","mm":1,"ev":"The elders cast the horoscopes and give the eight sons their names.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2706":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2706,"t":["ஐயாண் டெய்தி மையாடி யறிந்தார் கலைகள் படைநவின்றார்","கொய்பூ மாலை குழன்மின்னுங் கொழும்பொற் றொடுங் குண்டலமு","மையன் மார்க டுளக்கின்றி யாலுங் கலிமா வெகுண்டூர்ந்தார்","மொய்யா ரலங்கன் மார்பற்கு முப்ப தாகி நிறைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"At five the sons learn the arts and weapons and ride fierce horses without fear, and for the garlanded king thirty years have now filled.","n":"'ஐயாண்டு' (five years) and the thirty of line 4 can be read as the sons' and the king's ages, but the sentence joins them loosely; I take 'thirty' as the king's years.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kalvi","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2706","lb":"CC2706","mm":1,"ev":"The sons grow, learn the arts and arms and ride horses, as the king's age reaches thirty.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2707":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2707,"t":["பூநிறை செய்த செம்பொற் கோடிகம் புரையு மல்குல்","வீநிறை கொடிய னாரும் வேந்தனு மிருந்த போழ்திற்","றூநிறத் துகிலின் மூடிப் படலிகை கொண்டு வாழ்த்தி","மாநிறத் தளிர்நன் மேனி மல்லிகை மாலை சொன்னாள்."],"p":"","r":"While the queens and the king sit together, a woman with a jasmine garland, wrapping her flower-basket in clean cloth, blesses them and begins to speak.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2707","lb":"CC2707","mm":1,"ev":"A garland-woman brings spring blossoms in a covered basket, blesses the royal couple and begins to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["மல்லிகை மாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2708":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2708,"t":["தடமுலை முகங்கள் சாடிச் சாந்தகங் கிழிந்த மார்பிற்","குடவரை யனைய கோலக் குங்குமக் குவவுத் தோளாய்","தொடைமலர் வெறுக்கை யேந்தித் துன்னினன் வேனில் வேந்த","னிடமது காண்க வென்றா ளிறைவனு மெழுக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The woman, her breasts pressed into the sandal on her chest, offers a tribute of flowers and says that the Spring King has come and that the king should see his place; the king bids them rise.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2708","lb":"CC2708","mm":1,"ev":"The woman announces spring and invites the king to see its seat, and Civakan tells them to go.","cl":[],"ag":["மல்லிகை மாலை","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மல்லிகை மாலை","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2709":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2709,"t":["முடித்தலை முத்த மின்னு முகிழ்முலை முற்ற மெல்லாம்","பொடித்துப் பொன் பிதிரிந்த வாகத் திளையவர் புகழ்ந்து சூழக்","கடுத்தவாள் கனல வேந்திக் கன்னியர் காவ லோம்ப","விடிக்குரற் சீய மொப்பா னிழையொளி விளங்கப் புக்கான்."],"p":"","r":"The king enters the garden, his chest lit by jewels, in the middle of young women who praise him and of maidens who guard him with raised blades, like a lion with a voice of thunder.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2709","lb":"CC2709","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan enters the spring garden, praised and guarded by young women.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கன்னியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2710":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2710,"t":["இலங்குபொன் னார மார்பி னிந்திர னுரிமை சூழக்","கலந்தபொற் காவு காண்பான் காமுறப் புக்க தேபோ","லலங்குபொற் கொம்பு னாரு மன்னனு மாட மாதோ","நலங்கவின் கொண்ட காவு நல்லொளி நந்திற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The grove, shining like the gold garden of Indra's own pleasure, delights the king and the golden-bough women, who play in it as in a celestial park.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2710","lb":"CC2710","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan and his women play in the brightly lit garden.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2711":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2711,"t":["புலவியுண் மகளிர் கூந்தற் போதுகுக் கின்ற தேபோற்","குலவிய சிறகர்ச் செங்கட் கருங்குயில் குடையக் கொம்பர்","நிலவிய தாது பொங்க நீண்மலர் மணலிற் போர்த்துக்","கலவியிற் படுத்த காய்பொற் கம்பல மொத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The grove is compared to a woman's loosened hair strewn with flowers when koels scatter pollen, and to a gold blanket where the fallen blossoms spread like a lovers' bed.","n":"The comparison does not have a plot; it only makes the grove a bed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2711","lb":"CC2711","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2712":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2712,"t":["காசுநூல் பரிந்து சிந்திக் கம்பலத் துக்க தேபோன்","மூசுதேன் வண்டு மொய்த்து முருகுண்டு துயில மஞ்ஞை","மாசில்பூம் பள்ளி வைகி வளர்ந்தெழு மகளி ரொப்பத்","தூசுபோற் சிறக ரன்னந் தொழுதியோ டிரியச் சேர்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Scattered petals look like blanket-threads shed by the bed, where bees drink and sleep, peacocks rest and swans, with wings like cloth, scatter as the women come near.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2712","lb":"CC2712","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2713":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2713,"t":["காதிக்கண் ணரிந்து வென்ற வுலகுணர் கடவுள் காலத்","தாதிக்கண் மரங்கள் போன்ற வஞ்சொலீ ரிதனி னுங்கள்","காதலிற் காண லுற்ற விடமெலாங் காண்மி னென்றா","னீதிக்க ணின்ற செங்கோ னிலவுவீற் றிருந்த பூணான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, whose rule stands fast in justice, tells his sweet-voiced women to see every place they love in the grove, which he likens to the trees of the first age of the god who cut away the harming karmas and knows the world.","n":"The simile of 'the trees of the age of the first god' (the wish-fulfilling trees of the Jain golden age) and the Jina who destroyed the 'ghati' karmas is a Jain frame placed inside a pleasure scene; the grammar of the comparison is hard to settle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2713","lb":"CC2713","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan bids the women explore the whole garden.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2714":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2714,"t":["வானவர் மகளி ரென்ன வார்கயிற் றூச லூர்ந்துங்","கானவர் மகளி ரென்னக் கடிமலர் நல்ல கொய்துந்","தேனிமிர் குன்ற மேறிச் சிலம்பெதிர் சென்று கூயுங்","கோனமர் மகளிர் கானிற் குழாமயில் பிரிவ தொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The royal women swing on long ropes like celestial women, pick fine flowers like forest-women, climb the honey-hung hills and call to answer the echo, scattered like peafowl in the woods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2714","lb":"CC2714","mm":1,"ev":"The royal women swing, gather flowers, climb the hills and call into the echoes.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2715":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2715,"t":["நெடுவரை யருவி யாடிச் சந்தன நிவந்த சோலைப்","படுமதங் கவரும் வண்டு பைந்தளிர்க் கவரி யேந்திப்","பிடிமகிழ்ந் தோப்ப நின்ற பெருங்களிற் றரசு நோக்கி","வடிமதர் மழைக்க ணல்லார் மன்னனை மகிழ்ந்து நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Bathed in the waterfall under sandal groves, a great bull elephant stands while a she-elephant fans him with a tender branch and bees follow his rut; the bright-eyed women watch it and turn, glad, to the king.","n":"The bull-and-cow pair is set up for the women to read their own king and themselves in it; the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2715","lb":"CC2715","mm":1,"ev":"The women stop to watch an elephant pair and then turn to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2716":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2716,"t":["கொழுமடற் குமரி வாழைத் துகிற்சுருள் கொண்டு தோன்றச்","செழுமலர்க் காம வல்லிச் செருக்கயல் சிற்ப மாகக்","கழுமணிச் செம்பொ னாழிக் கைவிர லுகிரிற் கிள்ளி","விழுமுலைச் சூட்டி நின்றார் விண்ணவர் மகளி ரொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Women stand with tender plantain-leaf rolls and young creepers, marking their breasts with a nail as if painting, and look like heavenly women.","n":"What the nail picks and places in line 3 is not stated; I read it as some leaf or flower design on the breast.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2716","lb":"CC2716","mm":1,"ev":"The women adorn themselves and stand before the king like celestial women.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2717":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2717,"t":["கடைதயிர்க் குரல வேங்கை கண்ணுறச் சென்று நண்ணி","மிடைமயிர்க் கவரி நல்லான் கன்றுணக் கண்டு நிற்பார்","புடைதிரண் டெழுந்த பொம்மல் வனமுலை பொறுக்க லாற்றார்","நடைமெரிந் திகலி யன்ன நன்னடை நயந்து நிற்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Women who stood watching a calf suck a long-haired cow, while the milk-churning sound goes on, cannot bear the weight of their own full breasts and look longingly at a swan's fine gait.","n":"The word that makes them envy a gait in line 4 is hard (நடைமெரிந்து இகலி); the milk-and-breast image is clear, the longing less so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2717","lb":"CC2717","mm":1,"ev":"The women watch a calf suckling and long for the grace they see.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2718":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2718,"t":["எம்வயின் வருக வேந்த னிங்கென விரங்கு நல்லியாழ்","வெம்மையின் விழையப் பண்ணி யெஃகுநுண் செவிகள் வீழச்","செம்மையிற் கனிந்த காமத் தூதுவிட் டோத முத்தம்","வெம்முலை மகளிர் வீழ்பூம் பொதும்பருள் விதும்பி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women, playing a lute that cries 'Come here, king,' send amorous messages and, as the king hurries, seek out a flowering bower for love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2718","lb":"CC2718","mm":1,"ev":"The women call Civakan with song and lute and he goes quickly to their bower.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2719":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2719,"t":["பிடிமரு ணடையி னார்தம் பெருங்கவின் குழையப் புல்லித்","தொடைமலர்க் கண்ணி சேர்த்திச் சு ரும்புண மலர்ந்த மாலை","யுடைமது வொழுகச் சூட்டி யுருவத்தார் குழைய வைகிக்","கடிமலர் மகளி ரொத்தார் காவலன் களிவண் டொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"In the bower the king embraces the women until their beauty wilts, sets garlands on them and honey drips on the ground; the women are like flowers, the king like a joyous bee.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2719","lb":"CC2719","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan embraces the women and garlands them in the bower.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2720":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2720,"t":["இழைந்தவர் நலத்தை யெய்தி யினந்திரி யேறு போலக்","குழைந்ததார் நெகிழ்ந்த தானைக் கொற்றவன் பெயர்ந்து போகி","வழிந்துதேன் வார்ந்து சோரும் வருக்கையி னீழல் சேர்ந்தான்","விழைந்தவக் கடுக னாங்கோர் மந்தியை விளித்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king, his garments loosened after taking their beauty, leaves like a bull from a herd and sits in the shade of a honey-dripping jackfruit tree, where a lusting male monkey calls another female monkey to him.","n":"The male monkey is 'கடுகன்' in the text, probably a form of கடுவன்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2720","lb":"CC2720","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan leaves the women and sits under a jackfruit tree, where a male monkey calls another female.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","கடுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2721":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2721,"t":["அளித்திள மந்தி தன்னை யார்வத்தால் விடாது புல்லி","யொளித்தொரு பொதும்பர்ச் சேர்ந்தாங் கொருசிறை மகிழ்ச்சி யார்ந்து","தளிர்த்தலைப் பொதும்பர் நீங்கித் தம்மின மிரண்டுஞ் சேர்ந்த","களித்தலைக் கூட்டங் காதன் மந்திகண் டிருந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The male monkey holds the young female close, hides with her in a thicket and takes his joy; when he comes out the two troops meet, and his own mate sees the joined pair.","n":"In the line the pair is 'தம்மின் இரண்டும் சேர்ந்த களித்தலைக் கூட்டம்', and the mate sees it; which two joined is not wholly clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2721","lb":"CC2721","mm":1,"ev":"The male monkey takes a young female into a thicket and his mate sees them.","cl":[],"ag":["கடுகன்","மந்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2722":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2722,"t":["பரத்தையர்த் தோய்ந்த மார்பம் பத்தினி மகளிர் தீண்டார்","திருத்தகைத் தன்று தெண்ணீ ராடிநீர் வம்மி னென்ன","வுரைத்ததென் மனத்தி லில்லை யுயர்வரைத் தேனை யுண்பார்","வருத்துங்காஞ் சிரமும் வேம்பும் வாய்க்கொள்வார் யாவர் சொல்லாய்."],"p":"","r":"His mate says that wives do not touch a chest that has lain with courtesans, that she cannot take a bath of 'clear water' as he bids, and asks who would eat bitter strychnine and neem after tasting the mountain honey.","n":"The speaker is a monkey and the 'bath' offered in line 2 is never explained; the verse sets a wife's refusal in an animal's mouth and the work leaves it unanswered here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2722","lb":"CC2722","mm":1,"ev":"The wronged mate rebukes the male monkey and refuses him.","cl":[["a chaste wife will not touch a chest that has lain with courtesans","general","reported",1,"kaadhal_manthi"]],"ag":["மந்தி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மந்தி","கடுகன்"],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2723":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2723,"t":["ஈங்கினி யென்னை நோக்கி யெவன் செய்தி யெனக்கு வாணா","ணீங்கிற்றுச் சிறிது நிற்பிற் காண்டியா னீயு மென்னத்","தூங்கித்தான் றுளங்க மந்தி தொழுத்தையேன் செய்த தென்று","தாங்குபு தழுவிக் கொண்டு தன்னைத்தான் பழித்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The pair are caught in a dispute of life and death: he asks what she will do now, says his life is spent and that she will see it if she waits, and the she-monkey, swaying, cries 'I, the slave, did this', clasps him and blames herself.","n":"Who says the first lines and who says 'தொழுத்தையேன்' (a feminine word) is not fixed; I take the first speaker as the male and the self-blamer as the female.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2723","lb":"CC2723","mm":1,"ev":"A reconciliation scene in which a monkey clasps its mate and blames itself.","cl":[],"ag":["மந்தி","கடுகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கடுகன்","மந்தி"],"ch":"cc:116"},"cc:2724":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2724,"t":["கண்ணினாற் குற்றங் கண்டுங் காதலன் றெளிப்பத் தேறிப்","பெண்மையாற் பழித்த மந்தி பெருமகி ழுவகை செய்வான்.","றிண்ணிலைப் பலவின் றேங்கொள் தொழுத்தையேன் செய்த தென்று","பண்ணுறு சுளைகள் கையாற் பகுத்துணக் கொடுத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"A she-monkey who had scorned her mate for his fault, won back when he soothed her, calls herself the servant who erred and in great joy divides the sweet jackfruit segments by hand so that they may eat together.","n":"This opens the three-verse monkey scene that turns Civakan from his pleasures; the verse names no onlooker, and the she-monkey's self-blame echoes 2723 (not yet marked), where she embraces her mate and reproaches herself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2724","lb":"CC2724","mm":1,"ev":"A she-monkey, reconciled with her mate, hand-divides a jackfruit for them to share.","cl":[],"ag":["மந்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2725":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2725,"t":["இன்கனி கவரு மந்தி கடுவனோ டிரிய வோட்டி","நன்கனி சிலத னுண்ண நச்சுவேன் மன்ன னோக்கி","யென்பொடு மிடைந்த காம மிழிபொடு வெறுத்து நின்றா","னன்புடை யரிவை கூட்டம் பிறனுழைக் கண்ட தொத்தே."],"p":"","r":"A she-monkey takes the sweet fruit and drives her old mate away while a servant monkey eats some of it; the spear-king looking on is sickened of the desire woven into his bones, as if he had himself seen his loving wife joined to another.","n":"The Tamil in line 2 runs the words together (நச்சுவேன்மன்னன்); it is read here as நச்சுவேல் மன்னன், the spear-king Civakan, and சிலதன் as a second male monkey. The turn from the mate in 2724 to the rival here is why the scene reads as a woman's faithlessness, which is the monkeys' story and Civakan's reading of it, not a claim the verse argues.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2725","lb":"CC2725","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan, watching a she-monkey turn from her mate to another male, is disgusted with desire.","cl":[],"ag":["மந்தி","கடுவன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2726":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2726,"t":["கைப்பழ மிழந்த மந்தி கட்டியங் கார னொத்த","திப்பழந் துரந்து கொண்ட சிலதனு மென்னை யொத்தா","னிப்பழ மின்று போகத் தின்பமே போலு மென்று","மெய்ப்பட வுணர்வு தோன்றி மீட்டிது கூறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The she-monkey who has lost the fruit from her hand is matched with Kattiyangaran who seized a kingdom, and the servant monkey who snatched the fruit is matched with Civakan himself; he says that today's fruit going is like the going of enjoyment, and so true insight rises in him.","n":"The matching of the three monkeys to Kattiyangaran, Civakan and the lost fruit is as the Tamil is cut; who stands for whom is not fully fixed by the words. The insight is given as sight (மெய்ப்பட வுணர்வு), not as a teacher's argument.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","thuravu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2726","lb":"CC2726","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan reads the fruit fight as the story of his own throne and concludes that pleasure is as passing as the fruit.","cl":[["sensual pleasure, like a fruit taken and lost, does not last","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","மந்தி","சிலதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2727":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2727,"t":["மெலியவர் பெற்ற செல்வம் வேரொடுங் கீழ்ந்து வெளவி","வலியவர் கொண்டு மேலை வரம்பிகந் தரம்பு செய்யுங்","கலியது பிறவி கண்டாங் காலத்தா லடங்கி நோற்று","நலிவிலா வுலக மெய்த னல்லதே போலு மென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan reflects that wealth won by the weak is uprooted and seized by the strong who then break every limit, that birth is a strife-filled age (kali), and that to subdue oneself in time, do penance and reach a world without affliction is better.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","thavam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2727","lb":"CC2727","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan resolves that penance leading to a world without suffering is the better course.","cl":[["in this strife-filled birth the strong seize the wealth the weak have won","general","asserted",1,"civakan"],["self-restraint and penance in good time lead to a world free of affliction","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2728":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2728,"t":["நல்வினை யென்னு நன்பொற் கற்பக மகளி ரென்னும்","பல்பழ மணிக்கொம் பீன்று பரிசில்வண் டுண்ணப் பூத்துச்","செல்வப்பொற் சிறுவ ரென்னுந் தாமங்க டாழ்ந்து நின்ற","தொல்கிப்போம் பாவக் காற்றி னொழிகவிப் புணர்ச்சி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says that good deed is a golden wishing-tree bearing women as fruit and children as hanging garlands, which the wind of sin will bring down, and so he says let this union end.","n":"The verse treats the family as a result of earlier merit that will be spent, not as a sin; the wish to end the union is the first step toward his departure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","ilvaazhkkai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2728","lb":"CC2728","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan names his wives and sons the fruit of earlier merit and asks that the bond end.","cl":[["wives and sons are the fruit of earlier good deeds and are lost when evil deeds ripen","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2729":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2729,"t":["வேட்கைமை யென்னு நாவிற் காமவெந் தேறன் மாந்தி","மாட்சியொன் றானு மின்றி மயங்கினேற் கிருளை நீங்கக்","காட்டினார் தேவ ராவர் கைவிளக் கதனை யென்று","தோட்டியாற் றொடக்கப் பட்ட சொரிமதக் களிற்றின் மீண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says he drank the fierce toddy of desire on the tongue of longing and was confused with no virtue at all, and calls those who showed the lamp to remove his darkness gods; then he turns back like a rutting elephant brought round by the goad.","n":"Who 'showed the lamp' is not named in the verse; the monkeys of 2724-2726 or his teachers are both possible, so the verse is read as thanks to whoever gave him sight.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kal","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2729","lb":"CC2729","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan, sobered, turns back from desire.","cl":[["desire is an intoxicant that clouds the mind of one who drinks it","general","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2730":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2730,"t":["கைந்நிறை யெஃக மேந்திக் கனமணிக் குழைவில் வீச","மைந்நிற மணிவண் டார்ப்ப வார்தளிர் கவரி வீச","மெய்ந்நெறி மகிழ்ந்து நின்றான் வேனில்வாய்க் காம னொத்தான்","மொய்ந்நிற மாலை வேய்ந்து முருகுலா முடியி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, spear in hand, with jewelled earrings swinging, dark bees buzzing and fresh-leaf whisks waving, stands rejoicing in his way, like Kama in the spring, in a fragrant crown and garland.","n":"The phrase மெய்ந்நெறி can mean the true path or the way of the body; the verse sets the royal splendour that follows his resolve in 2729 against it without commenting.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2730","lb":"CC2730","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2731":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2731,"t":["நடுச்சிகை முத்துத் தாமம் வாணுத னான்று நக்கப்","படுத்தனர் பைம்பொற் கட்டில் பாடினர் கீதந் தூப","மெடுத்தன ரெழுந்து தேனா ரெரிமணி வீணை யார்த்த","கொடிப்பல பூத்துச் சூழ்ந்த குங்குமக் குன்ற மொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Women lay Civakan on a golden couch, sing to him and light incense while jewelled lutes sound, and he is like a saffron hill covered in flowering creepers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2731","lb":"CC2731","mm":1,"ev":"His wives seat him on a golden couch and sing to him.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2732":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2732,"t":["மெள்ளவே புருவங் கோலி விலங்கிக்கண் பிறழ நோக்கி","முள்ளெயி றிலங்கச் செவ்வாய் முறுவற்றூ தாதி யாக","வள்ளிக்கொண் டுண்ணக் காமங் கனிவித்தார் பனிவிற் றாழ்ந்த","வள்ளிதழ் மாலை மார்பன் வச்சிர மனத்த னானான்."],"p":"","r":"With slow brows, sidelong looks, a gleam of teeth and smiles as go-betweens, the women make desire ripen as they gather it up, but the king in his flower garland grew hard as a thunderbolt in mind.","n":"This is the turning point inside the sequence of pleasures: the same delights that fill 2730-2737 are shown to have lost their hold. The verse says he became hard-minded; it does not say here what he resolved.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2732","lb":"CC2732","mm":1,"ev":"His wives rouse desire in him but he stays unmoved.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2733":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2733,"t":["முலைமுகஞ் சுமந்த முத்தத் தொத்தொளிர் மாலை யாரு","மலைமுகந் தனைய மார்பின் மன்னனு மிருந்த போழ்திற்","கொலைமுகக் களிற னாற்கு நாழிகை சென்று கூறக்","கலைமுக மல்லர் புல்லிக் கமழுநீ ராட்டி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"When told that hours had passed to the king like a killing elephant, skilled attendants take hold of him and bathe him in fragrant water while the women with pearl-hung breasts and the broad-chested king sit together.","n":"கலைமுக மல்லர் (line 4) is hard to fix; it is taken as skilled attendants, wrestler-like, not as a named group.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2733","lb":"CC2733","mm":1,"ev":"Attendants bathe Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2734":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2734,"t":["வெண்டுகின் மாலை சாந்தம் விழுக்கலம் விதியிற் சேர்த்தி","நுண்டுகிற் றிரைகள் சேர்ந்த நூற்றுலா மண்ட பத்துக்","கண்டிரண் முத்த மென்றோட் காவிக்கண் மகளிர் போற்றி","யெண்டிசை மருங்கு மேத்த வினிதினி னேறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Women with lily eyes put on him white cloth, garland and sandal and fine ornaments in due order, and as they praise him in all directions he climbs happily into the pavilion hung with pearls and cloth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2734","lb":"CC2734","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan is dressed and mounts the pearl-hung hall.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2735":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2735,"t":["நெய்வளங் கனிந்து வாச நிறைந்துவான் வறைக ளார்ந்து","குய்வளங் கழுமி வெம்மைத் தீஞ்சுவை குன்ற லின்றி","யைவரு ளொருவ னன்ன வடிசினூன் மடைய னேந்த","மைவரை மாலை மார்பன் வான்சுவை யமிர்ந்த முண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"A cook as skilful as one of the Five serves rice cooked in ghee with fine side dishes and spiced sauces, and the king in his dark garland eats the food of heaven.","n":"'One of the Five' (ஐவருளொருவன்) is taken as the Pandava famed for cooking; the verse does not name him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2735","lb":"CC2735","mm":1,"ev":"A cook serves Civakan's meal and he eats it.","cl":[],"ag":["மடையன்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2736":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2736,"t":["கைப்பொடி சாந்த மேந்திக் கரகநீர் விதியிற் பூசி","மைப்படு மழைக்க ணல்லார் மணிச்செப்பின் வாச நீட்டச்","செப்படு பஞ்ச வாசந் திசையெலாங் கமழ வாய்க்கொண்","டொப்புடை யுறுவர் கோயில் வணங்குது மெழுக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"After attendants hold out fragrant powder and perfumed water, and women offer jewelled caskets of five scents for his mouth, Civakan says let them rise and worship the shrine of the supreme ones.","n":"ஒப்புடை யுறுவர் is read as the Jinas, those who have reached the likeness of the perfected; the sense is the shrine named in 2738.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2736","lb":"CC2736","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan proposes that the household go to worship at the Jina's shrine.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2737":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2737,"t":["ஒருபகல் பூசி னோராண் டொழிவின்றி விடாது நாறும்","பெரியவர் கேண்மை போலும் பெறற்கரும் வாச வெண்ணெ","யரிவையர் பூசி யாடி யகிற்புகை யாவி யூட்டித்","திருவிழை துகிலும் பூணுந் திறப்படத் தாங்கி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women anoint themselves with a fragrant butter that, put on one day, keeps its scent a year like the friendship of great men, steam themselves with aloe smoke and put on rich clothes and ornaments.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2737","lb":"CC2737","mm":1,"ev":"The wives scent and dress themselves for the visit to the shrine.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2738":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2738,"t":["நற்றவஞ் செய்த வீர ருளவழி நயத்து நாடும்","பொற்றதா மரையி னாளிற் பூஞ்சிகை முத்த மின்னக்","கொற்றவற் றொழுது சேர்ந்தார் கொம்பனார் வாமன் கோயின்","மற்றவன் மகிழ்ந்து புக்கு மணிமுடி துளக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Like Lakshmi on her golden lotus seeking heroes of good penance, the wives, their crests bright with pearls, bow to the king and go to the Jina's temple, and he follows gladly and nods his jewelled crown.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2738","lb":"CC2738","mm":1,"ev":"The royal party goes to the Jina's temple.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2739":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2739,"t":["கடிமலர்ப் பிண்டிக் கடவுள் கமலத்","தடிமலர் சூடி யவருலகில் யாரே","யடிமலர் சூடி யவருலக மேத்த","வடிமலர் தூவ வருகின்றா ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Who are those that wear on their heads the lotus-flowers of the feet of the god of the fragrant asoka? They will come to the world, the world praising them and scattering flowers at their feet.","n":"These three verses (2739-2741) are a question-and-answer hymn with the answer in the last words; the voice is the poet's or a choir's, and the verses are read as the hymn the party sings at the shrine, which the Tamil does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2739","lb":"CC2739","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who wear the Jina's feet on their heads are themselves praised and honoured by the world","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2740":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2740,"t":["முத்தணிந்த முக்குடைக்கீழ் மூர்த்தி திருவடியைப்","பத்திமையா னாளும் பணிகின்றார் யாரே","பத்திமையா னாளும் பணிவார் பகட்டெருத்தி","னித்தில வெண்குடைக்கீழ் நீங்காதா ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Who bow daily, with devotion, at the feet of the god beneath the triple pearl umbrella? Those who will not leave the white pearl umbrella above the elephant's neck.","n":"The reward named is worldly sovereignty, not release; the verse sets that apart from the aim of the sermon that follows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2740","lb":"CC2740","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["daily devotion to the Jina leads to kingship under a white umbrella","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2741":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2741,"t":["கருமக் கடல்கடந்த கைவலச் செல்வ","னெரிமலர்ச் சேவடியை யேத்துவார் யாரே","யெரிமலர்ச் சேவடியை யேத்துவார் வான்றோய்","திருமுத் தவிராழிச் செல்வரே யன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Who praise the red-lotus feet of the Lord who crossed the sea of karma by the power of his hand? The lords of the wheel under the three pearl umbrellas who touch the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2741","lb":"CC2741","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one who has crossed the sea of karma is worthy of praise, and his praisers become world-rulers","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2742":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2742,"t":["வண்ண மாமல் மாலை வாய்ந்தன","சுண்ணங் குங்குமந் தூமத் தாற்புனைந்","தண்ணல் சேவடி யருச்சித் தானரோ","விண்ணி லின்பமே விழைந்த வேட்கையான்."],"p":"","r":"One worshipped the Lord's feet with flowers, powders, saffron and incense, longing for the pleasure of the sky.","n":"The verse does not name the worshipper; it may be Civakan, the party, or a type, and the worship is for heaven's pleasure, not release.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2742","lb":"CC2742","mm":1,"ev":"A worshipper honours the Lord's feet.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2743":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2743,"t":["இலங்கு குங்கும மார்ப னேந்துசீர்","நலங்கொள் சாரணர் நாதன் கோயிலை","வலங்கொண் டாய்மலர்ப் பிண்டி மாநிழற்","கலந்த கன்மிசைக் கண்டு வாழ்த்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, bright with saffron on his chest, circles the shrine of the lord of the Charana sages and, seeing him on the stone under the asoka's shade, praises him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2743","lb":"CC2743","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan circles the shrine, sees the Charana lord seated and praises him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சாரணர் நாதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2744":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2744,"t":["உரிமை தன்னொடும் வலங்கொண் டோங்குசீர்த்","திரும கன்பணிந் திருப்பச் செய்தவ","ரிருநி லம்மனற் கின்ப மேயெனப்","பெருநி லம்மனன் பெரிதும் வாழ்த்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan with his wives circles it and bows, then, as the sage sits, the great king praises him greatly, saying that for those who do penance there is pleasure in both worlds.","n":"Line 3 is hard to construe (இருநிலம்மனற்கு); it is read as pleasure for both worlds. The 'great king' of line 4 is taken as Civakan, though the sage himself is not excluded.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2744","lb":"CC2744","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan and his wives circle the shrine, bow and praise the ascetic.","cl":[["those who do penance gain joy in this world and the next","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2745":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2745,"t":["தெருள லேன்செய்த தீவி னையெனு","மிருள்வி லங்கநின் றெரியு நீள்சுட","ரருளு மின்னெனக் கடிக ளென்றனன்","மருள்வி லங்கிய மன்னர் மன்னனே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan, free of the confusion that clouded him, begs the revered one to let his grace, like a tall flame, drive off the darkness called the evil deed he ignorantly did.","n":"The Jain teacher is asked for arul, grace; the work does not say here whether the deed is worn away by the sage's grace or by the hearer's own effort, a point that the sermon after 2748 would settle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arul","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2745","lb":"CC2745","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan asks the ascetic's grace to remove his evil karma.","cl":[["the grace of a holy teacher can clear the darkness of one's own evil deeds as a flame clears darkness","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","சாரணர் நாதன்"],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2746":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2746,"t":["பாற்கடற் பனிமதி பரவைத் தீங்கதிர்","மேற்பட மிகநனி சொரிவ தொப்பவே","நூற்கடன் மாதவ னுனித்த நல்லறங்","கோற்கடன் மன்னனுக் குரைக்கு மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, like the cool moon of the milk sea pouring beyond the sea, the great ascetic will tell the king the good dharma, for the ascetic's duty is the book and the king's duty is the sceptre.","n":"The verse puts two duties side by side, scripture for the ascetic and rule for the king, without ranking them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","sengol","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2746","lb":"CC2746","mm":1,"ev":"The poet announces that the sage will teach the king dharma.","cl":[["the ascetic's duty is to tell dharma to the king, whose own duty is the sceptre","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சாரணர் நாதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2747":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2747,"t":["தேனெய் தோய்ந்தன தீவிய திருமணி யனைய","வானி னுய்ப்பன வரகதி தருவன மதியோ","ரேனை யாவரு மமுதெனப் பருகுவ புகல்வ","மான மில்லுயர் மணிவண்ண னுவலிய வலித்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Words sweet as honey and ghee, like gems, that lead to the sky and give the best state, that the wise drink as nectar: the gem-coloured one resolved to speak them.","n":"The last line has a doubtful word (உவலிய, perhaps நுவலிய 'tell') and 'the gem-coloured one' is taken as the Charana lord, not Civakan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2747","lb":"CC2747","mm":1,"ev":"The sage begins to speak.","cl":[["the sage's teaching gives the highest state","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["சாரணர் நாதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2748":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2748,"t":["அருமையின் னெய்தும் யாக்கையும் யாக்கைய தழிவுந்","திருமெய் நீங்கிய துன்பமுந் தெளிபொருட் டுணிவுங்","குருமை யெய்திய குணநிலை கொடைபெறு பயனும்","பெருமை வீட்டொடும் பேசுவல் கேளிது பெரியோய்."],"p":"","r":"The sage says that he will speak, and the king should listen, of the body so hard to gain and its ending, the pain of leaving the splendid body, the firm grasp of the clear truths, the state of virtues and the fruit of giving, with release.","n":"The verse names the themes and gives no teaching yet; the claims follow in later verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","veedu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2748","lb":"CC2748","mm":1,"ev":"The sage announces the topics of his sermon to Civakan.","cl":[],"ag":["சாரணர் நாதன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர் நாதன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:117"},"cc:2749":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2749,"t":["பரவை வெண்டிரை வடகடற் படுநுகத் துளையுட்","டிரைசெய் தென்கட விட்டதோர் நோன்கழி சிவணி","யரச வத்துளை யகவயிற் செறிந்தென வரிதாற்","பெரிய யோனிகள் பிழைத்திவண் மானிடம் பெறலே."],"p":"","r":"A human birth is rarer than a pole drifting in the northern sea chancing into the yoke-hole of a log from the southern sea; to escape the great wombs and gain the human state is hard.","n":"The sermon opens a run of verses (2749-2753) that each name one more thing that must also be won, so the rarity is built up by addition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2749","lb":"CC2749","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["of all the wombs, a human birth is hardest to gain","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2750":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2750,"t":["விண்டு வேய்நர லூன்விளை கானவ ரிடனுங்","கொண்டு கூர்ம்பனி குலைத்திடு நிலைக்களக் குறும்பு","முண்டு நீரென வுரையினு மரியன வொருவி","மண்டு தீம்புனல் வளங்கெழு நாடெய்த லரிதே."],"p":"","r":"Even among men, to leave behind the hunters' hill-holds and robber outposts, where one cannot even speak of water, and to reach a watered and fertile land is hard.","n":"Lines 1-2 are hard; the reading takes the first two lines as the wild hill-country the birth must avoid.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2750","lb":"CC2750","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["to be born in a good, settled land is hard to gain","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2751":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2751,"t":["வில்லின் மாக்கொன்று வெண்ணிணத் தடிவிளிம்படுத்த","பல்லி னார்களும் படுகடற் பரதவர் முதலா","வெல்லை நீங்கிய விழிதொழி லிழிகுல மொருவி","நல்ல தொல்குலம் பெறுதலு நரபதி யரிதே."],"p":"","r":"Leaving behind the low births of hunters who kill beasts and fishermen of the sea, with their vile callings, the king is told that to be born into a good ancient family is hard.","n":"The verse treats a birth in a hunting or fishing family as lower because of its killing trade, an ethic of non-killing carried into a ranking of birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2751","lb":"CC2751","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["birth in a good family is hard to gain after escaping the low callings of killing","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2752":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2752,"t":["கருவி மாமழை கனைபெயல் பொழிந்தென வழிநா","ளருவி போற்றொடர்ந் தறாதன வரும்பிணி யழலுட்","கருவிற் காய்த்திய கட்டளைப் படிமையிற் பிழையா","துருவின் மிக்கதோ ருடம்பது பெறுதலு மரிதே."],"p":"","r":"A body free of the diseases that pour on one like rain on dark clouds, and unspoiled by the fire of the womb, is also hard to gain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2752","lb":"CC2752","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a sound and perfect body is hard to gain","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2753":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2753,"t":["காம னன்னதோர் கழிவனப் பறிவொடு பெறினு","நாம நாற்கதி நவைதரு நெறிபல வொருவி","வாம னூனெறி வழுவறத் தழுவின ரொழுக","லேம வெண்குடை யிறைவமற் றியாவது மரிதே."],"p":"","r":"Even with the beauty of Kama and knowledge besides, to avoid the fearful paths of the four states and to hold, without fault, to the way of the Jina's teaching is hardest of all, king of the white umbrella.","n":"'வாமன்' is read as the Jina; the 'four states' (நாற்கதி) are the four courses of rebirth (hell, beast, man, god).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2753","lb":"CC2753","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["faultless conduct in the Jina's way, avoiding the four states of rebirth, is hardest to attain","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2754":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2754,"t":["இன்ன தன்மையி னருமையி னெய்திய பொழுதே","பொன்னும் வெள்ளியும் புணர்ந்தென வயிற்றகம் பொருந்தி","மின்னு மொக்குளு மெனநனி வீயினும் வீயும்","பின்னை வெண்ணெயிற் றிரண்டபின் பிழைக்கவும் பெறுமே."],"p":"","r":"Even when a birth is so hard to win, the child may die in the womb like a bubble when the seed mixes like gold and silver; if it does not, it may live once the clot has set like butter.","n":"The verse and the next four trace the dangers that wait for the child before and after birth, so the chance of a full life is made smaller still.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2754","lb":"CC2754","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a life may end in the womb before birth","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2755":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2755,"t":["வெண்ணெ யாயது வீங்குபு கூன்புற யாமை","வண்ண மெய்தலும் வழுக்கவும் பெறுமது வழுக்கா","தொண்மை வாண்மதி யுருவொடு திருவெனத் தோன்றிக்","கண்ண னாரழக் கவிழினுங் கவிழுமற் றறிநீ."],"p":"","r":"The clot may take a tortoise shape and slip; if it does not, it appears with a full-moon form and fortune, yet may still collapse while kin weep.","n":"The last line's கண்ணனார் is hard; read here as the kin or watchers who weep.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2755","lb":"CC2755","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["even a child formed whole may be lost","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2756":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2756,"t":["அழித லின்றியங் கருநிதி யிரவலர்க் கார்த்தி","முழுதும் பேர்பெறு மெல்லையுண் முரியினு முரியும்","வழுவில் பொய்கையுண் மலரென வளர்ந்துமை யாடிக்","கெழீஇயி னாரொடுங் கிளையழக் கெடுதலுங் கெடுமே."],"p":"","r":"One who gives treasure to beggars and wins a great name may still fall like a lotus in a flawless pond that has grown and withered, and perish with his kin in tears.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2756","lb":"CC2756","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["fame and giving do not guard a man from death","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2757":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2757,"t":["கெடுத லவ்வழி யில்லெனிற் கேள்விக டுறைபோய்","வடிகொள் கண்ணியர் மனங்குழைந் தநங்கனென் றிரங்கக்","கொடையுங் கோலமுங் குழகுந்தம் மழகுங்கண் டேத்த","விடையிற் செல்வுழி விளியினும் விளியுமற் றறிநீ."],"p":"","r":"If there is no early death, one may master learning, be wondered at by women as Kama, praised for his gifts and beauty, and yet die as he goes his way.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2757","lb":"CC2757","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["learning, beauty and generosity do not save one from death","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2758":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2758,"t":["எரிபொன் மேகலை யிலங்கரிச் சிலம்பொடு சிலம்பு","மரிபொற் கிண்கிணி யணியிழை யரிவையர்ப் புணர்ந்து","தெரிவில் போகத்துக் கூற்றுவன் செகுத்திடச் சிதைந்து","முரியும் பல்சன முகம்புடைத் தகங்குழைந் தழவே"],"p":"","r":"Many who join women in gold girdles and anklets in pleasure that has no thought of the end are cut down by Death, and the living beat their faces and weep.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2758","lb":"CC2758","mm":1,"ev":"Death cuts down men in the midst of pleasure while their kin beat their faces and weep.","cl":[["pleasure without thought of the end is cut off by Death","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2759":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2759,"t":["கோதை மங்கையர் குவிமுலைத் தடத்திடைக் குளித்துக்","காதன் மக்களைக் கண்டுவந் தினிதினிற் கழிப்பப்","பேது செய்பிணிப் பெரும்புலி பாய்ந்திடப் பிணமா","மோத மாக்கட லுடைகலத் தவருற்ற துறவே."],"p":"","r":"Those who bathe in the breasts of garlanded women, pass their days with their dear children, and are then struck by disease like a tiger become corpses, like those whose ship is broken in the roaring sea.","n":"The last words are hard; the ship-wreck simile is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2759","lb":"CC2759","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["household pleasures end in death by sickness as suddenly as a wreck at sea","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2760":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2760,"t":["காமம் பைப்பயக் கழியத்தங் கடைப்பிடி சுருங்கி","யூமர் போலத்த முரையவிந் துறுப்பினி லுரையாத்","தூய்மை யில்குளந் தூம்புவிட் டாம்பொரு ளுணர்த்தி","யீம மேறுத லொருதலை யிகலமர் கடந்தோய்."],"p":"","r":"The verse speaks to a king who has beaten fierce foes about a slow ebbing, in which desire passes, memory shrinks, and the speaker is silent as the mute.","n":"The whole verse is hard and its connection to the sermon is not clear; the reading is a guess from a few words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2760","lb":"CC2760","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2761":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2761,"t":["தேங்கொள் பூங்கண்ணித் திருமுடித் திலகவெண் குடையோ","யீங்கி தன்னியு மிமையவ ரமையலர்க் கடந்த","தாங்கு மாவண்கைச் சக்கர மிக்குயர் பிறரும்","யாங்க ணாரவ ரூரொடு பேரெமக் குரையாய்."],"p":"","r":"A request is made to the king with the tilaka and white umbrella: tell us the towns and names of the other great kings, the wheel-lords who conquered the gods' enemies and others who rose higher.","n":"The speaker and the asker are not named; the verse reads as a request that precedes the teaching of 2762 on, but this is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2761","lb":"CC2761","mm":1,"ev":"Someone asks the king to name the towns and names of the great ruling kings of the past.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2762":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2762,"t":["வெவ்வினை செய்யு மாந்த ருயிரெனு நிலத்து வித்தி","யவ்வினை விளையு ளுண்ணும் மவ்விடத் தவர்க டுன்ப","மிவ்வென வுரைத்து மென்று நினைப்பினும் பனிக்கு முள்ளஞ்","செவ்விதிற் சிறிது கூறக் கேண்மதி செல்வ வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that those who do evil sow their deeds in the field called the soul and eat the harvest, and tells the rich king to hear a little of their pain, which makes the heart shiver even to think of.","n":"The farming image (sow, ripen, eat) is the verse's own; it makes the karma a crop, not a punishment handed down by a judge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","naragam","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2762","lb":"CC2762","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher begins to describe the hell that evil deeds ripen into.","cl":[["evil deeds sown in the soul are eaten later as suffering","jain","asserted",1,"muni"],["hell's suffering is great beyond telling","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2763":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2763,"t":["ஊழ்வினை துரப்ப வோடி யொன்றுமூழ்த் தத்தி னுள்ளே","சூழ்குலைப் பெண்ணை நெற்றித் தொடுத்ததீங் கனிக ளூழ்த்து","வீழ்வன போல வீழ்ந்து வெருவரத் தக்க துன்பத்","தாழ்துய ருழப்ப வூணு மருநவை நஞ்சு கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Driven by the ripening of their deeds, they run, and sorrow falls on them like sweet fruits dropping from the palm's crown, a poison whose fault cannot be borne.","n":"Line 1 with the 'drive' of ஊழ்வினை puts the cause in the deed, with no jailer named; the verse's palm-fruit simile is hard in lines 2-3.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2763","lb":"CC2763","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["old deeds drive the soul into suffering","jain","asserted",1,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2764":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2764,"t":["இட்டிவேல் குந்தங் கூர்வா ளெரிநுனைச் சுரிகை கூட","நட்டவை நிரைத்த பூமி நவையுடை நரகர் பொங்கி","யுட்பட வெழுந்து வீழ்ந்தாங் கூன்றகர்த் திட்ட வண்ண","மெட்டெலாத் திசையுஞ் சிந்திக் கிடப்பவா லடக்க மில்லார்."],"p":"","r":"Hell-beings rise boiling and fall on a ground planted in rows with spears, lances, swords and fire-tipped daggers, and lie scattered in all directions, those without restraint.","n":"No jailer is named; the hell-beings rise and fall by their own surge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2764","lb":"CC2764","mm":1,"ev":"Hell-beings rise and fall onto a field of fixed blades.","cl":[["those without restraint fall into a hell of blades","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2765":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2765,"t":["வெந்தடி தின்ற வெந்நோய் வேகத்தான் மீட்டு மாலை","பைந்தொடி மகளி ராடும் பந்தென வெழுந்து பொங்கி","வந்துடைந் துருகி வீழ்ந்து மாழ்குபு கிடப்பர் கண்டாய்","கந்தடு வெகுளி வேகக் கடாமுகக் களிற்று வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Through the burning of their disease they are tossed up like the balls women play with, burst, melt, fall back and lie wasted, says the teacher to the elephant-king of burning anger.","n":"'Elephant-king' is the address; line 1's cooked-flesh detail is hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2765","lb":"CC2765","mm":1,"ev":"Hell-beings bounce up and fall again in their own burning.","cl":[["hell's suffering falls on those whose own anger and deeds earned it","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2766":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2766,"t":["வயிரமுண் ணிரைத்து நீண்ட வார்சினை யிலவ மேற்றிச்","செயிரிற்றீ மடுப்பர் கீழாற் சென்னுனைக் கழுவி லேற்றி","மயிருக்கொன் றாக வாங்கி யகைத் தகைத் திடுவர் மன்னா","வுயிரைப்பே துறுத்து மாந்த ருயிரைப்பே துறுக்கு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"They are hauled on a tall, iron-spiked silk-cotton tree, fire is lit below, they are put on stakes, and cut apart hair by hair, as they once pained living beings.","n":"Here tormentors (the plurals மடுப்பர், ஏற்றி) act; the verse names them only as 'they'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2766","lb":"CC2766","mm":1,"ev":"Hell-beings are impaled on a spiked tree, burned and cut hair by hair.","cl":[["the hell-beings suffer as they made living beings suffer","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2767":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2767,"t":["துடிக்குரற் குரல பேழ்வாய்த் தொடர்ப்பிணி யுறுத்த செந்நாய்","மடுத்திட வைர வூசி வாளெயி றழுந்தக் கௌவிப்","புடைத்திட வலறி யாற்றார் பொன்றினும் பொன்றல் செல்லா","ருடுப்பினம் வேட்டஞ் செய்தா ருழப்பவாற் றுன்ப மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Red dogs with drum-like voices and gaping mouths, bound by chains, bite with diamond-needle teeth; the victims cry out, but though they die they do not die, for those who hunted beasts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2767","lb":"CC2767","mm":1,"ev":"Hell-dogs tear at those who hunted, who do not die.","cl":[["hunters are made to suffer as the hunted did, without the release of death","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2768":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2768,"t":["வாளைமீன் றடிக டின்றார் வருகென வுருக வெந்த","பாளத்தைக் கொடிற்றி னேந்திப் பகுத்துவாய் புகுத்த லாற்றா","ருளைக்கொண் டோடு கின்றா ருள்ளடி யூசி பாயத்","தாளொற்றித் தப்பி வீழ்ந்தார் தறிவலை மானிற் பட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who ate the flesh of fish are told to come, and melted metal is held in tongs and thrust into their mouths; they cannot bear it, run, are pierced by needles in the soles and are caught in nets like deer.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2768","lb":"CC2768","mm":1,"ev":"Fish-eaters are forced to swallow molten metal and are driven into nets.","cl":[["flesh-eating is punished in hell by being made to swallow molten metal","jain","asserted",1,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2769":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2769,"t":["காதலாள் கரிந்து நையக் கடியவே களைந்து கன்றி","யேதிலான் றார நம்பி யெளிதென விறந்த பாவத்","தூதுலை யுருக வெந்த வொள்ளழற் செப்புப் பாவை","யாதகா தென்னப் புல்லி யலறுமால் யானை வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The man who took another's wife, despising his own wedded love, is made in hell to embrace a red-hot copper image of a woman and cry out, as the teacher tells the elephant-king.","n":"The phrase 'ஏதிலான் தார நம்பி' means a man who trusted another's wife as easy to take; the poem makes the former pleasure return as the torment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","piranil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2769","lb":"CC2769","mm":1,"ev":"An adulterer in hell embraces a glowing copper woman.","cl":[["adultery is punished by the burning embrace of a red-hot image","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2770":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2770,"t":["சிலையினான் மாக்கள் கொன்று செழுங்கடல் வேட்ட மாடி","வலையினான் மீன்கள் வாரி வாழுயிர்க் கூற்ற மான","கொலைநரைக் குழம்பி தன்னுட் கொந்தழ லழுத்தி யிட்டு","நலிகுவர் நாளு நாளு நரகரை நாம வேலோய்."],"p":"","r":"Those who killed beasts with the bow, or took fish with nets, as the Death of living things, are thrown daily into a seething froth of fire by the tormentors, o king of the fierce spear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2770","lb":"CC2770","mm":1,"ev":"Killers of beasts and fish are cast into a boiling froth in hell.","cl":[["those who kill beasts and fish for sport or trade are tormented in hell daily","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2771":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2771,"t":["பாரகங் கழுநர் போலப் பரூஉத்தடி பலரு மேந்தி","வீரநோய் வெகுளி தோற்றி விழுப்பற வதுக்கி யிட்டுக்","காரகற் பொரிப்பர் கண்ணுட் சுரிகையை நடுவர் நெஞ்சிற்","பாரக்கூர்ந் தறிக ணட்டுப் பனையெனப் பிளப்பர் மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"The tormentors, with thick sticks, pound the hell-beings in anger, roast them in a black pan, thrust daggers into their eyes, and drive stakes through their hearts and split them like palms.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2771","lb":"CC2771","mm":1,"ev":"Hell-beings are pounded, roasted and impaled.","cl":[["hell punishes with violence that has no end","jain","asserted",4,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2772":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2772,"t":["நாப்புடை பெயர்த்த லாற்றார் நயந்துநீர் வேட்டு நோக்கிப்","பூப்புடை யணிந்த பொய்கை புக்குநீ ருண்ண லுற்றாற்","சீப்படு குழம்ப தாகிச் செல்லலுற் றந்தோ வென்னக்","கூப்பிடு குரலாய் நிற்பர் குறைப்பனைக் குழாங்க ளொத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Unable to move their tongues for thirst, they look longingly at water, enter a lotus-covered pool to drink, and find the water turned to pus, and cry out in pain like a stand of cut palms.","n":"The word 'pasi' is used for the longing, the verse itself names thirst.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2772","lb":"CC2772","mm":1,"ev":"Thirsty hell-beings go to drink in a pool and the water turns to pus.","cl":[["hell mocks desire with water that turns to pus","jain","asserted",3,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2773":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2773,"t":["நறுமலர்த் தாம நான்று நானநீர் பிலிற்றும் பந்தர்க்","குறுகலுங் குடநெய் பெய்த கொந்தழல் போன்று பொங்கிப்","பறையல கனைய வெண்பாற் பசுங்கழற் குண்டு பைங்க","ணுறுதுயர் நரகர் தம்மை யுருகச்சுட் டிடுங்க ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the hell-beings approach a canopy hung with fragrant garlands that drips perfumed water, it surges like fire fed with ghee and burns them, as they cry in pain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2773","lb":"CC2773","mm":1,"ev":"A pleasant-seeming canopy flares up and burns hell-beings who come near.","cl":[["what seems pleasant in hell becomes a burning","jain","asserted",2,"muni"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முனிவரன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:118"},"cc:2774":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2774,"t":["வெந்துருக் குற்ற செம்பின் விதவையு ளழுத்தி யிட்டு","மெந்திர வூச லேற்றி யெரியுண மடுத்துஞ் செக்கிற்","சுந்தெழுந் தரைத்தும் போகச் சுண்ணமா நுணுக்கி யிட்டு","மந்தரத் தனைய துன்பம் வைகலு முழப்ப மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"The teacher begins the tortures of hell: sinners are plunged in molten copper, set on a machine swing to be burnt, ground in an oil-press and pounded to powder, so that a pain heavy as a mountain is their daily labour.","n":"The verse names no sin; the cause comes in the verses that follow (2776 and after).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2774","lb":"CC2774","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher describes the daily tortures of the hell-dwellers.","cl":[["those who have done evil suffer endless bodily torture in hell each day","jain","asserted",4,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2775":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2775,"t":["உழும்பகட் டெருது போல வுரனறு தாள ராகிக்","கொழுங்களி யளற்றுள் வீழ்ந்துங் கொழும்புகை மடுக்கப் பட்டு","மழுந்துமிந் நரகந் தன்னுட் செல்பவர் யார்கொ லென்னி","னெழுந்துவண் டிமிரும் பைந்தா ரிறைவநீ கேண்மோ வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher asks who goes into the hell where men, strengthless as ploughing oxen, fall in rich mire and sink under thick smoke, and tells the king to listen.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2775","lb":"CC2775","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher turns from the hell's tortures to ask who is sent there, and bids the king listen.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2776":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2776,"t":["கொல்வதே கன்றி நின்றார் கொடியவர் கடிய நீரா","ரில்லையே யிம்மை யல்லா லும்மையு முயிரு மென்பா","ரல்லதுந் தவமு மில்லை தானமு மிழவென் பாருஞ்","செல்பவந் நரகந் தன்னுட் டீவினைத் தேர்க ளூர்ந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Those who are bent only on killing, the cruel and hard-hearted, and those who say there is no other world and no soul, and that penance and giving are a loss, go into hell borne in the cars of their evil deeds.","n":"The denial of this world's other and of the soul is put in the mouth of those the verse sends to hell, so the work answers it here by its consequence, not by argument; the account of the deniers is the teacher's, not their own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kolai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2776","lb":"CC2776","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher names the killers and the deniers of the next world as the ones who go to hell.","cl":[["killing beings sends the doer to hell","jain","asserted",1,"aruka_teacher"],["there is no next world or soul, and penance and giving are a waste","lokayata","refuted",2,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2777":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2777,"t":["எரிநீர வேநரக மந்நரகத் துன்பத்","தொருநீர வேவிலங்கு தாமுடைய துன்பம்","பெருநீர வாட்டடங்கட் பெண்ணணங்கு பூந்தா","ரருநீர வேந்தடர்த்த வச்சணங்கு வேலோய்."],"p":"","r":"The verse turns to the pains of hell and of animals, saying hell is of the nature of fire and water and that animals have pains of their own, and calls the king the lord of the spear and the flowery garland.","n":"The wording of lines 1 to 3 is dense and rests on a repeated -நீரவே, so the exact comparison is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2777","lb":"CC2777","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher compares the suffering of hell with that of animals.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2778":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2778,"t":["கழைபொதிர்ப்பத் தேன்சொரிந்து காய்த்தினைக ளார்த்து","மழை தவழுங் குன்றில் வயமா முழங்க","வுழையளிய தாமுறூஉந் துன்பங்க ணின்மேல்","விழைவயரா வேந்துறூஉந் துன்பமே கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher says the sufferings animals bear, as deer cry out on the cloud-touching hill when the strong beast roars, are what a king who never tires of desire will also undergo.","n":"Line 4 turns on வேந்து உறூஉம் துன்பம், which can mean either that kings suffer like the deer or that kings cause such suffering; I take the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["noy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2778","lb":"CC2778","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher compares the fear of deer before the lion with the suffering a king incurs.","cl":[["a king's pleasures bring him pain like that of hunted beasts","jain","asserted",4,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2779":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2779,"t":["நிணம்பிலிற்றும் வாயர் நெருப்பிமைக்குங் கண்ணர்","குணனஞ்சர் கூற்றனைய கோணாய் மடுப்பக்","கணமஞ்ஞை யஞ்சிக் கழுத்தொளிப்ப கண்டாய்","மணமல்கு பூந்தார் மழைதழீஇய கையாய்."],"p":"","r":"Hounds with dripping mouths and fiery eyes, cruel as Death, are let loose, and flocks of peacocks hide their necks in fear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2779","lb":"CC2779","mm":1,"ev":"Hunters set dogs on peacocks, which cower and hide their necks.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2780":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2780,"t":["மண்ணார மஞ்ச ளுரிஞ்சி மலர்சூட்டிக்","கண்ணார் மறியறுத்துக் கையா லுதிரந்தூ","யுண்ணீரே தேவீ ருவந்தென்ப திவ்வுலக","நண்ணார்க் கடந்தோய் நமனுலகி னான்மடங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher scorns those who smear a lamb with turmeric, garland it, cut its throat and sprinkle its blood, and say that the gods enjoy this; he says that in Yama's world this is repaid fourfold.","n":"The view that gods delight in the offered blood is set in order to be answered in line 4 by the recompense in Yama's world; the speaker's account of it is that of a critic. நான்மடங்கு may mean fourfold or the fourth part, so the measure is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","aaguthi","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2780","lb":"CC2780","mm":1,"ev":"Worshippers slaughter a goat for the gods; the teacher tells the king what awaits them.","cl":[["the gods are pleased by the blood of a slaughtered animal","vedic","refuted",3,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2781":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2781,"t":["மங்கை மனாவனைய மென்சூன் மடவுடும்பு","செங்கண் வரிவரால் செந்நீ ரிளவாளை","வெங்கருனை புல்லுதற்கு வேறுவே றாக்குறைப்ப","வங்காந் தழுகின்ற தார்கண்ணே நோக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Women's relish for roasted meat means a pregnant monitor lizard, a red-eyed carp and a young fish are each cut up separately, and the teacher asks whose eyes would not look on the creature that gapes and wails.","n":"The verse asks the king to see the wound as the animal's own, which is the very appeal of Kural 257.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kolai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2781","lb":"CC2781","mm":1,"ev":"Animals are butchered and roasted to be eaten.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2782":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2782,"t":["கடலரண மாகாது காடரண மாகா","குடலரண மாகாது குன்றரண மாகா","வடுதுயர மூர்ந்தலைப்ப வாங்கரணங் காணாப்","படுதுயரத் தாலே பதைத்தளிய வேமே."],"p":"","r":"Sea, forest, stomach and hill are no refuge: when pain tortures a creature it can find no shelter and perishes in grief.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2782","lb":"CC2782","mm":1,"ev":"Animals in pain find that no place shelters them.","cl":[["in suffering a being has no protector in the sea, forest or hills","general","asserted",4,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2783":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2783,"t":["முழுப்பதகர் தாடுரந்து முட்டாற்றிற் குத்தி","யுழப்பெருது பொன்றப் புடைத்துழுது விட்டாற்","கழித்துண்ணுங் காக்கை கடிவோரு மின்றிப்","புழுச்சொரியத் துன்பம் பொறுக்கலா பொன்றும்."],"p":"","r":"Plough-oxen are driven and beaten by the wicked until they die; crows eat them where no one drives them off, and the worms come out of them in their unbearable pain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2783","lb":"CC2783","mm":1,"ev":"Cruel men beat the plough-ox to death, and crows feed on it.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2784":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2784,"t":["நிரம்பாத நீர்யாற் றிடுமணலு ளாழ்ந்து","பெரும்பரா வாடவர்போற் பெய்பண்டந் தாங்கி","மருங்கொற்றி மூக்கூன்றித் தாடவழ்ந்து வாங்கி","யுரங்கெட் டுறுப்பழுகிப் புல்லுண்ணா பொன்றும்."],"p":"","r":"Pack-oxen sink in the sand of a half-dry river carrying their loads like the great merchants, press their sides and noses to the ground, and die, their bodies rotting, without grazing.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2784","lb":"CC2784","mm":1,"ev":"Burden-bearing oxen die in the river sand.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2785":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2785,"t":["போழ்மதிபோற் கூரிரும்பிற் பூநுதல்கள் போழ்ந்திடவுங்","காழ்நுதியிற் குத்துண்டுங் கார்மழைபோ னின்றதிர்ந்தும்","வீழ்பிடிகள் சிந்தித்தும் வெந்நோய்தம் முள்சுடவெந்","தாழத்த கந்திளக யானை யலம்வருமே."],"p":"","r":"Young bull elephants torn by sharp iron, pierced by spears, trumpeting like rain-clouds and grieving for their cow elephants, roam and are consumed by fever.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2785","lb":"CC2785","mm":1,"ev":"Elephants are hunted with iron and spears.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2786":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2786,"t":["எரிவளைப்ப வெம்புகையுண் டின்னுயிர்விட் டேகு","மரிவளைப்பக் குஞ்சரமு மாலிபோ னீராம்","வரிவளைக்கும் வெண்மயிர்க்கு முத்திற்கு மாந்தர்","திருவளைத்த மார்ப செகுத்திடுவர் தேங்கார்."],"p":"","r":"The hunters smoke the elephant into giving up life for the sake of tusk, hair and pearl, and the teacher addresses the king as the one whose chest wears Fortune.","n":"Line 2 is hard to read in places (மாலிபோல் நீராம்), so the exact picture of the hunt is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2786","lb":"CC2786","mm":1,"ev":"Hunters kill elephants with fire and smoke for their tusks and pearls.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2787":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2787,"t":["வேள்விவாய்க் கண்படுத்தும் வெவ்வினைசெ யாடவர்கை","வாளின்வாய்க் கண்படுத்தும் வாரணத்தி னீருரிபோற்","கோளிமிழ்ப்பு நீள்வலைவாய்க் கண்படுத்து மின்னணமே","நாளுலப்பித் திட்டார் நமரலா தாரெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"Animals are made to lie down at the sacrificial fire, at the sword's edge, and in the long net, and the teacher says that those not kin to us are all worn out in this way.","n":"The verse mentions sacrifice (வேள்வி) among the killings but does not argue against it; that is done in 2780.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kollaamai","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2787","lb":"CC2787","mm":1,"ev":"Animals are slain in sacrifice, by the sword and in nets.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2788":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2788,"t":["கொல்வாருங் கூட்டுட் செறிப்பாரு மாடவர்க","ளல்லாரு நாய்வேட்ட மாடாத மாத்திரையே","யால்லாத பைங்கிளியும் பூவையு மாதியா","வெல்லாங் கிளைபிரித்திட் டேமுறுநோய் செய்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Killers, those who pen birds in cages, and men who are not hunters either, the green parrots and mynas parted from their kin: all of them cause pain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pirivu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2788","lb":"CC2788","mm":1,"ev":"Men cage birds and separate them from their kin.","cl":[["separating creatures from their kin and caging them causes suffering","general","asserted",4,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2789":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2789,"t":["மல்லன் மலையனைய மாதவரை வைதுரைக்கும்","பல்லவரே யன்றிப் பகுட்ததுணாப் பாவிகளு","மல்குல் விலைபகரு மாய்தொடிய ராதியார்","வில்பொருதோண் மன்னா விலங்காய்ப் பிறப்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Those who revile the great ascetics, sinners who do not share their food, and courtesans who sell themselves are told they will be born as animals.","n":"Line 2 reads பகுட்ததுணா for பகுத்துண்ணா, which I take as 'who do not share their food'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2789","lb":"CC2789","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher names the sins whose fruit is rebirth as an animal.","cl":[["reviling ascetics, hoarding food and selling the body lead to rebirth as an animal","jain","asserted",4,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2790":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2790,"t":["தம்மை நிழனோக்கித் தாங்கார் மகிழ்தூங்கிச்","செம்மை மலர்மார்ப மட்டித் திளையார்தோள்","கொம்மைக் குழகாடுங் கோலவரை மார்பர்","வெம்மை மிகுதுன்பம் வேந்தே சிலகேளாய்."],"p":"","r":"Turning from animals, the teacher tells the king he will now hear the fierce pains of men who gaze on their own shadows, sink in pleasure, and play with young women's shoulders.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2790","lb":"CC2790","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher begins the account of human suffering.","cl":[["those who live for pleasure will suffer grave pain","jain","asserted",4,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2791":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2791,"t":["ஈருட் டடிமூடி யீண்டுமலப் பண்டப்","போர்வை புழுமொய்ப்பப் பொல்லாக் குடர்சூடிச்","சார்தற் கரிதாகித் தானின் றறாவள்ள","னீர்வாய்ச் சுரம்போந்தார் தம்மை நினையாரோ."],"p":"","r":"Wrapped in the two membranes of the womb, enclosed in a skin of filth swarming with worms, wearing foul entrails, those who have come into the wilderness of birth should be thought of.","n":"The grammar of lines 3 and 4 is hard, but the picture of the body as a bag of filth is clear; the reading of the wilderness as birth is mine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2791","lb":"CC2791","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher describes the suffering of the body in the womb and birth.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2792":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2792,"t":["அஞ்சொன் மடவார்த மார்வக் களிபொங்க","நெஞ்சத் தயிலேற்று நீள்வெங் கழுவீர்ந்துங்","குஞ்சிக் களியானைக் கேட்டா லுழப்பட்டுந்","துஞ்சிற் றுலகந்தோ துன்பக் கடலுள்ளே."],"p":"","r":"Men are impaled and sawn for their passion for women, trampled by a mad elephant, and die in the sea of sorrow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kodungol","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2792","lb":"CC2792","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher lists the punishments that befall lovers of women.","cl":[["passion for women leads to punishment and death in a sea of sorrow","jain","asserted",4,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2793":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2793,"t":["பண்ணார் களிறேபோற் பாயோங் குயர்நாவாய்","கண்ணார் கடன்மண்டிக் காற்றிற் கவிழுங்கான்","மண்ணார் மணிப்பூணோய் மக்க ளுறுந்துன்ப","நண்ணா நரகத்தி னான்கா மடியன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The sea-going ship that heaves like a bull elephant capsizes in the wind; the teacher tells the king that what people suffer is a fourth step of hell.","n":"நான்காம் அடி is obscure; I take it to say that the pains of this world are one fourth of hell's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["noy","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2793","lb":"CC2793","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher describes a ship capsizing and says human suffering is part of hell's.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2794":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2794,"t":["செந்தீப் புகையுண்டுஞ் சேற்று ணிலைநின்று","மந்தோ வெனமாற்றா லாற்றப் புடையுண்டுந்","தந்தீ கெனாமுன்கை வீக்கத் தளர்வுற்று","நொந்தார் குடிச்செல்வர் நோன்மை நுகம்பூண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Householders who bear the yoke of duty are choked by smoke, stand in the mire, are struck, and are worn out when their hands are bound before they can say their wrong.","n":"The verse sets household wealth against renunciation, the opposite of Kural 46's argument.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2794","lb":"CC2794","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher describes the suffering of householders under the yoke.","cl":[["the householder's wealth and duty are a yoke of suffering","jain","asserted",4,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2795":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2795,"t":["கண்சூன் றிடப்பட்டுங் கால்கை களைந்தாங்கே","யண்பல் லிறக்கையா லாற்றத் தகர்பெற்று","நுண்சாந் தரைப்பார்போ னோவ முழங்கையாற்","புண்செய் திடப்பட்டும் புன்கணு ழப்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Eyes gouged out, hands and feet cut off, flesh broken by blows, and the wounds worked on with elbows are the pains of the punished.","n":"Lines 2 to 4 are obscure in places.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2795","lb":"CC2795","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher describes the punishments given to criminals.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2796":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2796,"t":["மாலைக் குடைமன்னர் வைய மகற்றுவான்","காலைக் கதிதுன்பங் காவல் பெருந்துன்பஞ்","சோலை மயிலன்னார் தோள்சேர் விலராயின்","வேலைக் கடலேபோற் றுன்பம் விளையுமே."],"p":"","r":"For kings with the garlanded umbrella, taking the world is pain, guarding it is greater pain, and without a woman's embrace the sorrow grows like the sea.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2796","lb":"CC2796","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher says kingship is suffering.","cl":[["the king's conquest and protection of the world bring great sorrow","jain","asserted",2,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2797":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2797,"t":["ஊன்சே ருடம்பென்னு மோங்கன் மரச்சோலை","தான்சேர் பிணியென்னுஞ் செந்தீக் கொடிதங்கிக்","கான்சேர் கவினென்னுங் காமர் மலர்வாடத்","தேன்சேர் மலர்மார்ப தீத்திட் டிறக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"The body is a tall grove of flesh; sickness is a red fire that lodges in it, beauty its flowers withered, and it burns and dies.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","noy","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2797","lb":"CC2797","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher says the body burns and dies like a grove on fire.","cl":[["the body is perishable and consumed by disease","jain","asserted",1,"aruka_teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2798":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2798,"t":["கொட்டுப் பிடிபோலுங் கூனும் குறளாமை","விட்டு நடப்பனபோற் சிந்தும் விளைந்துசீ","யட்டு முயவுநோ யல்லாப் பிறநோயும்","பட்டா ருறுதுன்பம் பன்னிச் சொலலாமோ."],"p":"","r":"The bent back, the shaking, the old age that falls like a ripe fruit, and the other diseases are pains that cannot be fully told.","n":"Line 1 compares the bent back to an elephant's pulled, bent form; the exact comparison is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","noy","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2798","lb":"CC2798","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher closes the account of suffering with old age and disease.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:119"},"cc:2799":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2799,"t":["வேட்டன பெறாமை துன்பம் விழைநரைப் பிரித றுன்ப","மோட்டெழி லிளமை நீங்க மூப்புவந் தடைத றுன்ப","மேட்டெழுத் தறித லின்றி யெள்ளற்பா டுள்ளிட் டெல்லாஞ்","சூட்டணிந் திலங்கும் வேலோய் துன்பமே மாந்தர்க் கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The Jain teacher opens by telling the hero that for men everything is suffering: wishes unmet, parting from those one longs for, bright youth leaving and old age arriving, and being scorned for lack of learning.","n":"The unit closes with என்றான், so the poet reports the teacher's words; the teacher is not named in these verses and is identified only from the sermon's address to Civakan (வேலோய், 2799; வேந்தே, 2807; வேந்தர் வேந்தே, 2810).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pirivu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2799","lb":"CC2799","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher begins his sermon to Civakan with the suffering of the human state.","cl":[["human life is suffering: wants unfulfilled, parting from the loved, loss of youth, old age, contempt","jain","asserted",4,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2800":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2800,"t":["திருவிற்போற் குலாய தேந்தார்த் தேவர்தந் தன்மை செப்பிற்","கருவத்துச் சென்று தோன்றார் கானிலந் தோய்தல் செல்லா","ருருவமே லெழுத லாகா வொளியுமிழ்ந் திலங்கு மேனி","பருதியி னியன்ற தொக்கும் பன்மலர்க் கண்ணி வாடா."],"p":"","r":"The teacher begins on the gods: they are not born in a womb, do not touch the ground, their forms cannot be painted, and their bodies shine like the sun while their garlands never fade.","n":"A description of celestial bodies given as the first step of an argument that even such lives are suffering (2810-2811).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2800","lb":"CC2800","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["gods arise without a womb and do not touch the earth","jain","asserted",2,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2801":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2801,"t":["அங்கையு மடியு நோக்கிற் றாமரை யலர்ந்த தொக்கும்","பங்கய மனைய செங்கண் பகுவொளிப் பவழஞ் செவ்வாய்","செங்கதிர் முறுவன் முத்தின் றெளிநகை திகழுஞ் செய்யாள்","வெங்கடை மழைக்க ணோக்கி வெய்துறத் திரண்ட வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The gods' women are pictured limb by limb: palms and feet like lotuses, red eyes, coral mouths, bright teeth, and a glance that gathers fierce force.","n":"Line 4 (வெங்கடை மழைக்கண் நோக்கி வெய்துறத் திரண்ட) is hard, and the last clause is read only roughly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2801","lb":"CC2801","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2802":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2802,"t":["தாணெடுங் குவளைக் கண்ணித் தளையவிழ் கோதை மாலை","வாண்முடி வயிர வில்லும் வார்குழை சுடரு மார்பிற்","பூணிடை நிலவு மேனி மின்னொடு பொலிந்த தேவ","ரூணுடை யமிர்தம் வேட்டா லுண்பது மனத்தி னாலே."],"p":"","r":"The gods wear jewelled garlands, diamond bows and glowing ear-rings, and when they long for the nectar that is their food they take it by mind alone.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amudham","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2802","lb":"CC2802","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["gods take their food by mere wish of the mind","jain","asserted",4,"teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2803":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2803,"t":["சிதரரி யொழுகி யோடிச் செவியுறப் போழ்ந்து நீண்ட","மதரரி மழைக்க ணம்பா வாங்குவிற் புருவ மாகத்","துதைமணிக் கலாப மின்னத் தொன்மலர்க் காம னம்பு","புதைமலர் மார்பத் தெய்யப் பூவணை மயங்கி வீழ்வார்."],"p":"","r":"The goddesses with long eyes and bows for brows, their girdles gleaming, fall into the flower bed bewildered by the arrows of the god of love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2803","lb":"CC2803","mm":1,"ev":"Celestial women, struck by Kama's arrows, fall into the flower bed.","cl":[],"ag":["தோளியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2804":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2804,"t":["பூத்ததை கொம்பு போன்று பொன்னிழை சுடரு மேனி","யேத்தருங் கொடிய னாரை யிருநடு வாகப் புல்லிக்","காய்த்தியிட் டுள்ளம் வெம்பிக் கடைந்திடு கின்ற காம","நீத்துநீர்க் கடலை நீந்தும் புணையென விடுத்தல் செல்லார்."],"p":"","r":"Gods clasp the flower-branch women to their full and, though lust churns the heart like a sea, will not let them go, as if these women were the raft for crossing it.","n":"The raft image turns on irony: the sea is lust and the raft is the thing that keeps them in it. The reading of நீத்துநீர் is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2804","lb":"CC2804","mm":1,"ev":"The gods embrace their women and do not release them.","cl":[["the gods cling to the objects of lust as though to a raft across the sea of lust and cannot let go","jain","asserted",4,"teacher"]],"ag":["வெய்யர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2805":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2805,"t":["பொங்கல்வெம் முலைக ளென்னும் போதொடு பொருது பூந்தா","ரங்கலந் தொடையன் மாலை கிழிந்தழ கழிய வைகிக்","கொங்கலர் கோதை நல்லார் குரைகட லமிர்த மாகத்","தங்கலர் பருகி யாரார் தாழ்ந்துகண் ணிமைத்தல் செல்லார்."],"p":"","r":"As the women's breasts press and tear the garlands, the gods drink them like a roaring sea of nectar and are never sated, nor do they blink.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2805","lb":"CC2805","mm":1,"ev":"The gods lie with their women and drink them without being satisfied.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2806":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2806,"t":["கருவியின் னிசைக ளார்ப்பக் கற்பக மரத்தி னீழற்","பொருகய லனைய கண்ணும் புருவமு மரவஞ் செய்ய","வரவமே கலைக ளம்பொற் கிண்கிணி சிலம்பொ டார்ப்பத்","திருவனா ராடல் கண்டுந் திருவொடு திளைத்து மானார்."],"p":"","r":"As the dancing goddesses jingle girdles and anklets under the wish-trees to the sound of instruments, the gods watch and take their fill of fortune without feeling it enough.","n":"மானார் is read as 'do not diminish/are not satisfied'; the verse does not say outright that their pleasure fails to satisfy, but the sequence 2805-2810 points that way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2806","lb":"CC2806","mm":1,"ev":"The gods watch the goddesses dance.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யர்","தோளியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2807":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2807,"t":["பனிமுகின் முளைத்த நான்கு பசுங்கதிர்த் திங்க ளொப்பக்","குனிமருப் புழுது மேகங் குஞ்சரங் குனிந்து குத்த","வினிதினி னிலங்கு பொற்றோ டேற்றுமின் குழைகள் பொங்கத்","துனிவிலர் களிற்றோ டாடித் தொழுதகத் கழிப்பர் வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher tells the king that the gods, cool and weary of nothing, pass their days playing with great elephants whose tusks gore the clouds, their gold ear-rings and ear-ornaments flashing.","n":"The close (தொழுதகத் கழிப்பர்) looks corrupt; the sense of spending time in play is kept and nothing is built on the last words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2807","lb":"CC2807","mm":1,"ev":"The gods pass the days in sport with celestial elephants.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2808":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2808,"t":["கடிகைவா ளார மின்னக் கற்பகக் காவு கண்டுந்","தொடிகவி னறாத மென்றோட் டேவியர் சூழ வாம","னடிகையிற் றொழுது பூத்தூ யஞ்சலி செய்து வீடே","முடிகவிப் பிறவி வேண்டே முனைவவென் றிரப்ப வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Surrounded by their bangled consorts in the grove of wish-trees, the beings of heaven worship the Lord's feet with folded hands and flowers and beg that release be completed and birth not wanted.","n":"Line 2 (வாம னடிகையிற் றொழுது) can be read as worship of a named Vamana, of the Lord generally, or of a graceful dancer; the teaching sense, that gods beg release, holds either way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","piravi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2808","lb":"CC2808","mm":1,"ev":"The gods and their women worship the Lord and ask for release.","cl":[["even the gods ask for release from birth, not for more heaven","jain","asserted",4,"teacher"]],"ag":["வெய்யர்","தோளியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2809":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2809,"t":["மலங்குவித் தாவி வாட்டி வாய்நிறை யமிர்தம் பெய்த","விலங்குபொற் கலச மன்ன வெரிமணி முலைகள் பாயக்","கலந்தனர் சென்ற பின்னாட் கதிர்கழன் றிருந்த வெய்யோன்","புலம்புபோற் புலம்பித் தேவர் பொற்புகுத் திருப்ப வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"After the gods have embraced their women, the next day their radiance fails like the sun that has lost its rays, and they grieve in the heavens.","n":"Lines 3-4 are damaged (பொற்புகுத் திருப்ப); the grief-at-fading reading is certain only in outline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aayul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2809","lb":"CC2809","mm":1,"ev":"The gods' splendour fails as their end approaches.","cl":[["a god's radiance fails as his heavenly life runs out","jain","asserted",3,"teacher"]],"ag":["வெய்யர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2810":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2810,"t":["எல்லைமூ வைந்து நாள்க ளுளவென விமைக்குங் கண்ணு","நல்லெழின் மாலை வாடு நஞ்சுடை யமிர்துண் டாரிற்","பல்பகற் றுய்த்த வின்பம் பழுதெனக் கவல்ப கண்டாய்","பில்கித்தே னொழுகும் பைந்தார்ப் பெருநில வேந்தர் வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher tells the king that when fifteen days remain, the gods' eyes begin to blink and their garlands wither, and they grieve, like those who ate poisoned nectar, that the long days of pleasure were worthless.","n":"The pleasure the verses 2800-2809 described is turned inside out: the same heavenly life is recounted as poison in nectar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aayul","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2810","lb":"CC2810","mm":1,"ev":"The gods see the signs of their approaching fall and regret their pleasure.","cl":[["fifteen days before a god falls his eyes blink and his garland fades, and he sees his pleasure as wasted","jain","asserted",3,"teacher"]],"ag":["வெய்யர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2811":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2811,"t":["தேவரே தாமு மாகித் தேவராற் றொழிக்கப் பட்டு","மேவல்செய் திறைஞ்சிக் கேட்டு மணிகமாப் பணிகள் செய்து","நோவது பெரிதுந் துன்ப நோயினுட் பிறத்த றுன்பம்","யாவதுந் துன்ப மன்னா யாக்கைகொண் டவர்கட் கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The teacher concludes that even gods are struck down by other gods and made to bow, hear and serve; being born into disease is suffering, and all is suffering for those who have taken a body.","n":"Line 2 (மணிகமாப் பணிகள்) is garbled; the reading of service under higher gods rests on the other lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","noy","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2811","lb":"CC2811","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher draws the lesson of the gods' lives: embodiment is suffering.","cl":[["all embodied existence, even a god's, is suffering","jain","asserted",4,"teacher"],["gods suffer subjection to other gods","jain","asserted",1,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2812":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2812,"t":["கொங்கு விம்மு குளிர்பிண்டிக் குழவி ஞாயிற் றெழிலேய்ப்பச்","சிங்கஞ் சுமந்த மணியணைமேற் றேவ ரேத்திச் சிறப்பயர","வெங்கு முலக மிருணீங்க விருந்த வெந்தை பெருமானார்","தங்கு செந்தா மரையடியென் றலைய வேயென் றலையவே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord who sits on the lion throne under the cool young asoka, worshipped by the gods and dispelling the darkness of every world, is praised, with a wish that his lotus feet rest on the speaker's head.","n":"The first person (என்றலை) is the teacher's, taken from the sermon's flow; the verse does not name who prays.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2812","lb":"CC2812","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher praises the Jina enthroned beneath the asoka.","cl":[["the Jina enthroned under the asoka and worshipped by the gods removes the darkness from all worlds","jain","asserted",3,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2813":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2813,"t":["இலங்கு செம்பொ னெயின் மூன்று மெரிபொன் முத்தக் குடைமூன்றும்","வலங்கொண் டலர்தூஉ யடியேத்தும் வைய மூன்றும் படைமூன்றும்","கலங்கா துயர்ந்த வதிசயங்கண் மூன்றுங் காமர் நூன்மூன்று","நலங்கொ டீம்பாற் குணக்கடலு முடையார் நம்மை யுடையாரே."],"p":"","r":"The one who has three golden walls, three pearl umbrellas, the homage of three worlds, three weapons, three wonders and three dear books, and a sea of sweet virtues, is said to be our lord.","n":"The threes are listed without names; the identification of the walls, umbrellas and books with Jain tradition is a reading, and படை மூன்றும், நூல் மூன்று are not settled here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2813","lb":"CC2813","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina marked by the threefold signs is the lord to follow","jain","asserted",4,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2814":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2814,"t":["மன்ற னாறு மணிமுடிமேன் மலிந்த சூளா மணிபோலும்","வென்றோர் பெருமா னறவாழி வேந்தன் விரிபூந் தாமரைமேற்","சென்ற திருவா ரடியேத்தித் தெளியும் பொருள்க ளோரைந்து","மன்றி யாறு மொன்பானு மாகு மென்பா ரறவோரே."],"p":"","r":"The virtuous, praising the feet of the king of the wheel of dharma who walked on the open lotus, say that the things to be understood are five, six and nine.","n":"The verse gives the numbers only; that they stand for the Jain groupings of five extended things, six substances and nine principles is a reading from the tradition rather than from the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thelivu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2814","lb":"CC2814","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher reports what the righteous hold about the categories of reality.","cl":[["the objects of knowledge fall into groups of five, six and nine","jain","asserted",4,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2815":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2815,"t":["பெரிய வின்பத் திந்திரனும் பெட்ட செய்கைச் சிறுகுரங்கு","முரிய செய்கை வினைப்பயத்தை யுண்ணு மெனவே யுணர்ந்தவனை","யரிய ரென்ன மகிழாது மெளிய ரென்ன விகழாது","மிருசார் வினையுந் தெளிந்தாரே யிறைவ னூலுந் தெளிந்தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing that mighty Indra and a small monkey alike eat the fruit of their own deeds, one neither rejoices at the great nor despises the lowly; those who have understood the two kinds of deeds have understood the Lord's scripture.","n":"Karma is equalizing here: rank in heaven or earth shields no one. The verse says understanding both deeds and the Lord's text go together, so right knowledge is the way, not praise or grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","samanilai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2815","lb":"CC2815","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["every being, from Indra to a monkey, eats the fruit of its own deeds","jain","asserted",2,"teacher"],["one who knows this is neither elated by the great nor scornful of the humble","general","asserted",3,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2816":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2816,"t":["உறுவர்ப் பேண லுவர்ப்பின்மை யுலையா வின்பந் தலைநிற்ற","லறிவர் சிறப்பிற் கெதிர்விரும்ப லழிந்தோர் நிறுத்த லறம்பகர்தல்","சிறியா ரினத்துச் சேர்வின்மை சினங்கை விடுதல் செருக்கவித்த","லிறைவ னறத்து ளார்க்கெல்லா மினிய ராத லிதுதெளிவே"],"p":"","r":"The marks of the one who follows the Lord's dharma are honouring the worthy, freedom from disgust, steady joy, holding the learned in honour, upholding the fallen, teaching dharma, avoiding the mean, letting anger go and curbing pride.","n":"This looks like a list of the marks of right faith, but the verse's items do not map one for one onto the stock list of eight, so none is asserted here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","ozhukkam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2816","lb":"CC2816","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the follower of the Jina's dharma honours the worthy, avoids the mean, gives up anger and pride, and is kind to all","jain","asserted",4,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2817":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2817,"t":["செறியச் சொன்ன பொருடெளிந்தார் சேரார் விலங்கிற் பெண்ணாகார்","குறுகார் நரக மோரேழுங் கீழ்முத் தேவர் குழாந்தீண்டா","ரறியா துரைத்தே னதுநிற்க வாறே நரக மாகாத","பொறியார் போக பூமியுள் விலங்குமாவ ரொரு சாரார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who have understood what was said briefly are not reborn as beasts or women, do not enter the seven hells, and do not mix with the lowest gods; the speaker adds that he spoke without full knowledge and that some who escape hell may still become beasts in the lands of enjoyment.","n":"That female birth is ranked as a fall is the sermon's view; the claim is reported, not argued. Line 3 (அறியாது உரைத்தேன் அது நிற்க) is read as the teacher's modest restriction, and its sense is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","naragam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2817","lb":"CC2817","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those of right understanding are not reborn as animals or women, nor in the seven hells nor among the lowest gods","jain","asserted",2,"teacher"],["some who avoid hell are still born as animals in the lands of enjoyment","jain","asserted",4,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2818":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2818,"t":["ஏத்தருந் திருமணி யிலங்கு நீர்மைய","கோத்தன போற்குண நூற்றுக் கோடியுங்","காத்தன காவல பதினெண் ணாயிரம்","பாத்தன பண்ணவர் சீல மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Sila, the discipline of conduct, is said to be like strung gems: a hundred crore virtues guarded by eighteen thousand guards and divided out by the learned.","n":"The number eighteen thousand matches a Jain tradition of counting the divisions of sila; the verse gives only the number.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","adakkam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2818","lb":"CC2818","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["sila is a body of 18,000 guarding rules and a hundred crore virtues","jain","asserted",3,"teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2819":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2819,"t":["மொய்யமர் ஞாட்பினுண் முரண்கொண் மன்னவர்","மெய்புகு பொன்னணி கவச மொப்பன","மையலைம் பொறிமதம் வாட்டி வைகலுஞ்","செய்வினை நுணுக்குவ சீல மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Sila is said to be like the golden armour of warring kings: it withers the intoxication of the five senses and wears away the deeds one does each day.","n":"செய்வினை நுணுக்குவ is read as thinning karma, not as making one's works finer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","vinai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2819","lb":"CC2819","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["sila blunts the intoxication of the senses and thins one's deeds","jain","asserted",3,"teacher"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2820":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2820,"t":["மணித்துண ரனையதங் குஞ்சி வண்கையாற்","பணித்தனர் பறித்தலிற் பரவை மாநிலந்","துணித்தொரு துணிசுமந் தனைய திண்பொறை","யணித்தகு முடியினா யாதி யாகவே."],"p":"","r":"The teacher addresses the crowned king and speaks of ascetics who, as bidden, pluck out their gem-like hair with their own hands, bearing it with a patience as heavy as earth and sea.","n":"The details of lines 3-4 (துணித்தொரு துணிசுமந்தனைய) are obscure and the sense of earth-heavy patience is the safest reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","porai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2820","lb":"CC2820","mm":1,"ev":"Ascetics pluck out their hair as a discipline.","cl":[["plucking out the hair is borne with patience as an austerity","jain","asserted",3,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2821":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2821,"t":["பெரியவாட் டடங்கட் செவ்வாய்ப் பிறர்மனை பிழைக்கு மாந்தர்","மாபுஇயவாய்ப் புறஞ்சொற் கூர்முண் மத்திகைப் புடையு மன்றி","யொருவர்வா யுமிழப் பட்ட தம்பல மொருவர் வாய்க்கொண்","டரியவை செய்ப வையத் தாண்பிறந் தார்க ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Men who wrong others' wives, who add the goad of slander, and who take into their mouth what another has spat out, do hard things, are they, the verse asks, men born on earth?","n":"The verse's irony (are they men?) and the unfamiliar spat-out betel make the force uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranil","purangooral","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2821","lb":"CC2821","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["adultery and slander and taking another's spat-out leavings are wrongs unworthy of a man","general","asserted",4,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2822":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2822,"t":["ஒழுக்கமே யன்றித் தங்க ளுள்ளுணர் வழிக்கு மட்டும்","புழுப்பயி றேனு மன்றிப் பிறவற்றின் புண்ணு மாந்தி","விழுப்பய னிழக்கு மாந்தர் வெறுவிலங் கென்று மிக்கார்","பழித்தன வொழித்தல் சீலம் பார்மிசை யவர்கட் கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Sila is not conduct alone: those who destroy their inner knowledge by eating honey full of worms and feeding on the wounds of other creatures lose a great gain and are called mere beasts by the great; giving up what they condemn is sila, the teacher said.","n":"பிறவற்றின் புண் ('the wound of other things') is read as flesh, which the Kural also calls another creature's wound; the verse does not use the word புலால்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","pulaal","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2822","lb":"CC2822","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["sila is to give up what the great condemn, including honey with worms and flesh","jain","asserted",4,"teacher"],["those who destroy inner discernment through such food are beasts","general","asserted",3,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவோர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2823":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2823,"t":["நன்னிலத் திட்ட வித்தி னயம்வர விளைந்து செல்வம்","பின்னிலம் பெருக வீனும் பெறலருங் கொடையும் பேசிற்","புன்னிலத் திட்ட வித்திற் புற்கென விளைந்து போக","மின்னெனத் துறக்குந் தானத் தியற்கையும் விரித்து மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The poet reports that the teacher also explained the gift: seed in good soil grows pleasantly and yields wealth to enrich the field, while seed in barren soil gives thin crops and passes like lightning.","n":"Here the poet reports what the teacher expounded (விரித்து), so the voice shifts from the teacher's address to narration.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","payan","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2823","lb":"CC2823","mm":1,"ev":"The teacher expounds the nature and fruit of giving.","cl":[["a gift to a worthy recipient grows into great wealth; a gift to the unworthy yields little and vanishes","jain","asserted",3,"teacher"]],"ag":["அறவோர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:120"},"cc:2824":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2824,"t":["ஐவகைப் பொறியும் வாட்டி யாமையி னடங்கி யைந்தின்","மெய்வகை தெரியுஞ் சிந்தை விளக்குநின் றெரிய விட்டுப்","பொய்கொலை களவு காம மவாவிருள் புகாது போற்றிச்","செய்தவ நுனித்த சீலக் கனைகதிர்த் திங்க ளொப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The sage describes the ascetics who wither the five senses, steady the mind that sees the true nature of things, and guard themselves from lying, killing, theft and lust, shining like the cool moon.","n":"The verse and the next four read as the sage's own continuing talk on gifts to ascetics, though no speaker is named; the narrator may be speaking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thavam","ozhukkam","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2824","lb":"CC2824","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["an ascetic must wither the five senses and keep from lying, killing, theft and lust","jain","asserted",3,"madhavan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2825":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2825,"t":["வாய்ச்சிவா யுறுத்தி மாந்தர் மயிர்தொறுஞ் செத்தி னாலும்","பூச்சுறு சாந்த மேந்திப் புகழ்ந்தடி பணிந்த போதுந்","தூக்கியிவ் விரண்டு நோக்கித் தொல்வினை யென்று தேறி","நாச்செறு பராவு கொள்ளார் நமர்பிற ரென்று முள்ளார்."],"p":"","r":"Whether men torment these ascetics with the adze at every hair-root or bow at their feet with sandal paste, the ascetics weigh both, take them as old deeds, and call no one kin or stranger.","n":"வாய்ச்சி is taken here as the adze; the cruelty named is slow and deliberate, so the ideal is not a forbearing of a moment's insult but of torture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samanilai","vinai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2825","lb":"CC2825","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["ill-treatment and honour alike are the fruit of old deeds and must be met with equal mind","jain","asserted",3,"madhavan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2826":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2826,"t":["பாற்கதிர்த் திங்கட் கொட்பிற் பருமித்த களிறு போல","நூற்கதி கொண்டு கண்ணா னுகத்தள வெல்லை நோக்கி","மேற்கதிக் கேணி யாய விழுத்தவர் மனையில் வந்தாற்","காற்கொசி கொம்பு போலக் கைதொழு திறைஞ்சி மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"With eyes fixed a yoke's length ahead as the scripture teaches, like a rutting elephant under the moon, the ascetic comes to the house of the worthy householder, who bows to him like a bent bough.","n":"Who is called விழுத்தவர் (the worthy) and whether the bower is the householder or the ascetic is not fixed by the grammar; the reading follows the next verse, where the householder washes the feet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","panivu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2826","lb":"CC2826","mm":1,"ev":"An ascetic walking by the rule of the scripture reaches a householder's house and is greeted with a bow.","cl":[["a householder must greet a visiting ascetic with joined hands and a bow","jain","asserted",4,"madhavan"]],"ag":["முனிவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2827":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2827,"t":["தொடிக்கையாற் றொழுது வாழ்த்தித் தூமணி நிலத்து ளேற்றிப்","பொடிப்புனை துகிலி னீக்கிப் புகழ்ந்தடி கழீஇய பின்றை","யடுத்தசாந் தகிலி னாவி யாய்மல ரருச்சித் தானார்","கொடுப்பர்நா லமிர்த மூன்றிற் குணம்புரிந் தடங்கி னார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The householder joins his hands, seats the ascetic on a clean floor, removes his dusty cloth, washes and praises his feet, worships with sandal, aloe smoke and flowers, and gives the four nectars to the one restrained in the three.","n":"The four nectars are not named in the verse. Reading 'in the three' as mind, speech and body is the usual Jain reading of the three restraints, supplied here from outside the Tamil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2827","lb":"CC2827","mm":1,"ev":"The householder receives the ascetic, washes his feet, worships him and gives him food.","cl":[["a gift is rightly given to one restrained in the three (mind, speech and body) after worship of his feet","jain","asserted",4,"madhavan"]],"ag":["இல்லறத்தான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2828":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2828,"t":["ஒன்பது வகையி னோதிற் றுத்தமர்க் காகு மார்ந்த","வின்பத மருளி யீத லிடையென மொழிப யார்க்குந்","துன்புற விலங்கு கொன்று சொரிந்துசோ றூட்டினார்க்கு","நன்பொருள் வழங்கி னார்க்கும் பயனமக் கறிய லாகா."],"p":"","r":"The sage ranks gifts: given in the nine-fold way to the best it is highest, giving in kindness is middle, and for those who kill animals to feed others, though they give good wealth, the fruit cannot be reckoned by us.","n":"The syntax of lines 3-4 is hard; whether the fruit is 'unknowable' or simply 'not counted' is not fixed. The 'middle' gift (இடை) is the one 2841 later rewards.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kollaamai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2828","lb":"CC2828","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a gift paid for by killing animals yields no fruit that can be reckoned","jain","asserted",3,"madhavan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2829":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2829,"t":["கூற்றுநா வலறுவ தனைய கூரிலை","யேற்றநீர்த் துளும்புவா ளிறைவ வீங்கினிப்","போற்றினை கேண்மதி பொருவில் புண்ணியர்க்","காற்றிய கொடைப்பய னறியக் கூறுவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The sage turns to the king, whose sharp spear is like Death's tongue, tells him to listen well, and promises to tell the fruit of gifts made to those of peerless merit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2829","lb":"CC2829","mm":1,"ev":"The sage begins to tell the king what gifts to the worthy yield.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2830":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2830,"t":["கடிப்புவா ரங்குலி கொளீஇய கைதுரந்","தடுத்துவார் மயிர்த்துதி யலற வூதலிற்","பொடித்தபொற் றாமரை யனைய பொங்கழ","லிடைக்கிடந் தெவ்வள விரும்பு காய்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"A hand with ringed fingers drives the bellows and the hair of the bellows roars, so that the fire, like a bursting golden lotus, heats the iron lying in it as far as the iron will take.","n":"The sentence continues into 2831, where the heated iron takes water in proportion to its heating; this verse gives only the forge, and its meaning waits on the next.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","payan","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2830","lb":"CC2830","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2831":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2831,"t":["காய்ந்தவவ் வளவினாற் கௌவு நீரதொத்","தாய்ந்தறி கொடையின தளவிற் புண்ணியந்","தோய்ந்துயி ருடம்பிவ ணொழியத் தொக்கநாள்","வீந்துபோய் வயிற்றகம் விதியி னெய்துமே."],"p":"","r":"As heated iron drinks water in the measure of its heating, merit is in the measure of the gift; the soul steeped in it leaves the body and, after the fixed days, enters a womb by fate.","n":"The iron and water give a quantitative law of merit, which the Kural (222) and the Purananuru (134) both refuse to put on a ledger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2831","lb":"CC2831","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the merit of a gift is exactly as great as the gift, and it carries the soul to its next birth","jain","asserted",2,"madhavan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2832":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2832,"t":["திங்கணா ளொன்பதும் வயிற்றிற் சேர்ந்தபின்","வங்கவான் றுகில்பொதி மணிசெய் பாவைபோ","லங்கவ ரிரட்டைக ளாகித் தோன்றலுஞ்","சிங்கினா ரிருமுது குரவ ரென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"After nine months in the womb the giver is born as one of a pair of twins like a jewelled doll wrapped in fine cloth, and their two elder parents pass away.","n":"சிங்கினார் is read as 'passed away' (the parents' death at the birth), not as a sneeze; this is the sense the sequel supports.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","eegai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2832","lb":"CC2832","mm":1,"ev":"Twins are born in the land of enjoyment, and their parents die.","cl":[["those who gave rightly are reborn as twins in the land of enjoyment","jain","asserted",3,"madhavan"]],"ag":["இரட்டைகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2833":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2833,"t":["இற்றவர் தேவராய்ப் பிறப்ப வீண்டுடல்","பற்றிய விசும்பிடைப் பரவு மாமுகி","றெற்றென வீந்தெனச் சிதைந்து போகுமான்","மற்றவம் மக்கடம் வண்ணஞ் செப்புவாம்."],"p":"","r":"The dead parents are born as gods, their bodies in the heavens dissolving like cloud in the sky; the sage says he will now describe the children.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2833","lb":"CC2833","mm":1,"ev":"The parents die and are born as gods.","cl":[["those who die in the land of enjoyment are reborn as gods","jain","asserted",1,"madhavan"]],"ag":["இருமுது குரவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2834":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2834,"t":["பிறந்தவக் குழவிகள் பிறர்கள் யாவரும்","புறந்தர லின்றியே வளர்ந்து செல்லுநா","ளறைந்தன ரொன்றிலா வைம்ப தாயிடை","நிறைந்தனர் கலைகுண முறுப்பு நீரவே."],"p":"","r":"The children grow up with no care from anyone and reach full measure in arts, qualities and limbs within the fifty days less one.","n":"The unit of fifty less one is not named; days is assumed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2834","lb":"CC2834","mm":1,"ev":"The twins grow to perfection with no rearing.","cl":[],"ag":["இரட்டைகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2835":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2835,"t":["சோலைமீ னரும்பித் திங்கட் சுடரொடு பூத்த தேபோன்","மாலையுங் கலனு மீன்று வடகமுந் துகிலு நான்று","காலையு மிரவு மில்லாக் கற்பக மரத்தி னீழற்","பாலையாழ் மழலை வேறாய்ப் பன்மணிக் கொம்பி னின்றாள்."],"p":"","r":"Under a wishing tree that gives garlands, ornaments, bracelets and cloth, a girl whose voice is like the palai lute stands among gem-set boughs, like a grove of stars and moon in flower.","n":"There is no day or night under the tree; the setting is outside the world of cause and labour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2835","lb":"CC2835","mm":1,"ev":"A girl of the land of enjoyment stands under a wishing tree.","cl":[],"ag":["பாவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2836":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2836,"t":["இலங்குபொற் குவடு சாந்த மெழுதிய தனைய தோண்மே","னலங்கிளர் குழைக ணான்று சாந்தின்வாய் நக்கி மின்னக்","கலங்கலந் தகன்ற மார்பிற் கற்பக மாலை தாழ","விலங்கர சனைய காளை வேனில்வேந் தென்னச் சேர்ந்தான்"],"p":"","r":"A youth, spring's king to the eye, rises with sandal on his shoulders, ear-ornaments shining and a wishing-tree garland hanging on his chest, and comes up to her.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2836","lb":"CC2836","mm":1,"ev":"The youth of the land of enjoyment approaches the girl.","cl":[],"ag":["காளை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2837":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2837,"t":["குண்டலங் குலவி மின்னப் பொன்னரி மாலை தாழத்","தெண்கட லமிர்திற் செய்த பாவையிற் பாவை நிற்ப","விண்டலர் மாலை மார்பன் விதியினாற் சென்று மாதோ","கண்டனன் கலந்த வுள்ளங் காதலி னொருவ ரானார்."],"p":"","r":"As he comes by fate, ear-rings shining and his garland swinging, he sees her standing like a doll made of sea nectar, and their hearts are joined in love as one.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2837","lb":"CC2837","mm":1,"ev":"The youth sees the girl and the two fall in love.","cl":[],"ag":["காளை","பாவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2838":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2838,"t":["கொதிநுனைக் காம னம்பு கொப்புளித் துமிழ்ந்து காம","மதுநிறை பெய்து விம்மு மணிக்குட மிரண்டு போல","நுதிமுக முத்தஞ் சூடி நோக்குந ராவி வாட்ட","விதிமுலை வெய்ய வாகித் தாரொடு மிடைந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The girl's breasts, like two jewel pitchers brimming with desire's wine, wearing pearls at their tips, burn the eyes of those who look and press against the garland.","n":"Pure description of the girl; it builds the picture of enjoyment as the first fruit of giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2838","lb":"CC2838","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2839":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2839,"t":["இமைத்தநுங் கண்க ளென்னை யிகழ்ந்தனி ரென்று சீற","வமைத்துநின் னழகு கோல மாரவுண் டறுக்க லாற்றா","திமைத்தன வஞ்சி யென்ன விளையவள் சிலம்பிற் குஞ்சி","நமைத்தபூந் தாமந் தோய நகைமுக விருந்து பெற்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The younger girl's eyes, blinking in a quarrel of love, seem to rebuke him for slighting her, and when his hair garland touches her anklets as he bows, he is given the feast of a smile.","n":"The quarrel and appeasement of the akam tradition is carried into the heaven-like land; the love is not tested by lack or separation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2839","lb":"CC2839","mm":1,"ev":"After a lovers' quarrel the youth bows at her feet and is forgiven with a smile.","cl":[],"ag":["பாவை","காளை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2840":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2840,"t":["இன்னகி லாவி விம்மு மெழுநிலை மாடஞ் சேர்ந்தும்","பொன்மலர்க் காவு புக்கும் புரிமணி வீணை யோர்த்து","நன்மலர் நான வாவி நீரணி நயந்துஞ் செல்வத்","தொன்னலம் பருகிக் காமத் தொங்கலாற் பிணிக்கப் பட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"They go to tall mansions, golden groves, play the jewelled lute, bathe in flower pools and drink old wealth, and are bound by the garland of desire.","n":"'Bound' (பிணிக்கப் பட்டார்) holds the verse's turn: the reward is itself a binding, which 2610 shows ruining Saccandan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2840","lb":"CC2840","mm":1,"ev":"The pair spend their days in pleasure in the land of enjoyment.","cl":[["pleasure enjoyed there is the fruit of old merit and also a bond","jain","asserted",4,"madhavan"]],"ag":["காளை","பாவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2841":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2841,"t":["பூமுற்றுந் தடங்க ணாளும் பொன்னெடுங் குன்ற னானுங்","காமுற்று நினைந்த வெல்லாங் கற்பக மரங்க ளேந்தத்","தாமுற்றுக் கழிப்பர் தான மிடையது செய்த நீரா","ரேமுற்றுக் கரும பூமி யிருநிதிக் கிழமை வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Whatever the wide-eyed and the golden-hilled youth wish for, the wishing trees give; those who did the middle sort of gift enjoy this and spend it, O king, lord of the two treasures of the world of deeds.","n":"'The world of deeds' (கரும பூமி) is where the king lives, set against this land where nothing is done and all is enjoyed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2841","lb":"CC2841","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a gift of the middle sort is rewarded by whatever one desires in the land of enjoyment","jain","asserted",3,"madhavan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2842":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2842,"t":["அடங்கலர்க் கீந்த தானப் பயத்தினா லலறு முந்நீர்த்","தடங்கட னடுவுட் டீவு பலவுள வவற்றுட் டோன்றி","யுடம்பொடு முகங்க ளொவ்வா ரூழ்கனி மாந்தி வாழ்வர்","மடங்கலஞ் சீற்றத் துப்பின் மானவேன் மன்ன ரேறே."],"p":"","r":"Those who gave to the unrestrained are born, by the fruit of that gift, on one of the many islands in the roaring sea, with bodies and faces unlike those of men, eating the fruit of their fate.","n":"The recipient decides the fruit: the same giving, given to one who has no restraint, is repaid with a debased birth, and the doctrine is told to Civakan, who is called a lion in rage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2842","lb":"CC2842","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a gift to the unrestrained is rewarded by birth with a strange body on a sea-island","jain","asserted",1,"madhavan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2843":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2843,"t":["செப்பிய சீல மென்னுந் திருமணி மாலை சூழ்ந்தார்","கப்பத்து ளமர ராவர் காட்சியி னமிர்த முண்டா","ரொப்பநீ ருலக மெல்லா மொருகுடை நிழற்றி யின்பங்","கைப்படுத் தலங்க லாழிக் காவல ராவர் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"Those who wear the jewel-garland of virtue become gods in the heavens; others rule the whole earth under one umbrella and take delight in their wheel of command, O lord.","n":"The verse ranks the fruit of virtue above the fruit of worldly rule. Who rules under one umbrella (the cakravartin) is a recipient of the second sort, which the king hearing it may recognise as himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2843","lb":"CC2843","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["virtue (sila) brings rebirth as a god in a heaven","jain","asserted",1,"madhavan"],["another sort of merit brings a world-ruler's umbrella and delights","jain","asserted",3,"madhavan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2844":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2844,"t":["வீட்டின தியற்கைநாம் விளம்பிற் றீங்கதிர்ப்","பாட்டரும் பனிமதி பழித்த முக்குடை","மோட்டிருங் கொழுமலர்ப் பிண்டி மூர்த்திநூ","லீட்டிய பொருளகத் தியன்ற தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The sage turns to the nature of release, saying it lies in the scripture of the lord under the three umbrellas, who sits beneath the flowering pindi tree.","n":"The three umbrellas and the pindi (ashoka) tree are the Jina's regalia in the Jain account; here they name the source of authority for what follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","samayam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2844","lb":"CC2844","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["release is taught in the scripture of the Jina under the pindi tree","jain","asserted",4,"madhavan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2845":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2845,"t":["உள்பொரு ளிதுவென வுணர்தன் ஞானமாந்","தெள்ளிதி னப்பொரு டெளிதல் காட்சியாம்","விள்ளற விருமையும் விளங்கத் தன்னுளே","யொள்ளிதிற் றரித்தலை யொழுக்க மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing 'this is the real thing' is knowledge, seeing it clearly is faith, and holding both firmly within oneself, with both kinds of pain dropped, is conduct.","n":"The Jain triad of knowledge, faith and conduct is set out as a definition; the 'two' in line 3 is not named in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","meyyunarvu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2845","lb":"CC2845","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["right knowledge is knowing what is real","jain","asserted",1,"madhavan"],["right faith is clear seeing of it","jain","asserted",2,"madhavan"],["right conduct is holding both firmly within, free of the two bonds","jain","asserted",4,"madhavan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2846":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2846,"t":["கூடிய மும்மையுஞ் சுடர்ந்த கொந்தழ","னீடிய வினைமர நிரைத்துச் சுட்டிட","வீடெனப் படும்வினை விடுதல் பெற்ற தங்","காடெழிற் றோளினா யநந்த நான்மையே."],"p":"","r":"These three together are like a blazing fire that burns the tree of deeds standing in rows, and release is to be free of deeds; the fourfold infinite is then won.","n":"Karma is destroyed by effort, not by a god or by grace; the verse closes with the 'fourfold' (அநந்த நான்மை) that 2847 spells out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vinai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2846","lb":"CC2846","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["knowledge, faith and conduct together burn away karma, and that is release","jain","asserted",3,"madhavan"],["release gives the fourfold infinite","jain","asserted",4,"madhavan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2847":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2847,"t":["கடையிலா வறிவொடு காட்சி வீரியங்","கிடையிலா வின்பமுங் கிளந்த வல்லவு","முடையதங் குணங்களோ டோங்கி விண்டொழ","வடைதலான் மேலுல கறியப் பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"These are the four: endless knowledge, endless seeing, endless power and endless joy, with the qualities named; those who have them rise to the upper world.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","inbam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2847","lb":"CC2847","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the liberated soul has endless knowledge, perception, energy and bliss and goes up to the upper world","jain","asserted",4,"madhavan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2848":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2848,"t":["மாதவ னெனப்பெயர் வரையி னவ்வரை","யேதமி லெயிறணி பவள வாய்த்தொடுத்","தாதியி லறவுரை யருவி வீழ்ந்தென","மாதுயர் மலங்கெட மன்ன னாடினான்."],"p":"","r":"The sage, named 'great ascetic', is like a mountain whose coral-lipped mouth lets the water of the first dharma fall like a stream, and the king bathes in it to lose the stain of great sorrow.","n":"The sage is called 'great ascetic' but not by another name in these verses; the metaphor makes hearing a bath.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aram","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2848","lb":"CC2848","mm":1,"ev":"The king, hearing the sage's sermon, is cleansed of sorrow as if bathing in a falling stream.","cl":[["the sage's teaching of dharma washes away the stain of great sorrow","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","மாதவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:121"},"cc:2849":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2849,"t":["எல்லையி லறவுரை யினிய கேட்டபின்","றொல்லையெம் பிறவியுந் தொகுத்த பாவமும்","வல்லையே பணிமின மடிகளென்றனன்","மல்லைவென் றகன்றுபொன் மலர்ந்த மார்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"Having heard the sermon of endless dharma, the golden-chested victor tells his people to bow quickly to the lord's feet, for it clears the sins of old births.","n":"The speaker is not named; the golden-chested victor of line 4 is taken to be Civakan, the hearer of the sermon, and the people addressed are not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2849","lb":"CC2849","mm":1,"ev":"The king, after the sermon, bids his people worship the lord's feet to be rid of the sins of earlier births.","cl":[["worship of the lord's feet clears the sin gathered through earlier births","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2850":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2850,"t":["கதிர்விடு திருமணி யங்கைக் கொண்டதொத்","தெதிர்வது மிறந்தது மெய்தி நின்றது","மதிர்வறு தவவிளக் கெறிப்பக் கண்டவன்","பதரறு திருமொழி பணிக்கு மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The hearer, looking at the sage as at a bright gem held in the palm, sees one who has known what comes and what is past, and says that his flawless sacred words will command them.","n":"The grammar of the first three lines is hard; who sees and speaks is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","thavam","guru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2850","lb":"CC2850","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2851":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2851,"t":["முழுநீர் வளைமேய் தலின்முத் தொழுகிப்","பொழிநீர் நிலவின் னிருள்போழ்ந் தரிசிக்","கழுநீ ரொழுகக் கழுநீர் மலருந்","தழுநீ ரதுதா தகியென் றுளதே."],"p":"","r":"A water scene in flowers and pearls: conches graze and let pearls fall, moonlit water breaks the dark, and kazhuneer lilies bloom in the pool as the dhatagi flower is said to grow.","n":"The words of the last line are obscure; read only as scenery that opens the new place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","idam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2851","lb":"CC2851","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2852":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2852,"t":["கயல்பாய்ந் துகளக் கடியன் னம்வெரீஇ","வியனீள் சுடர்வெண் மதிசேர் வதுபோ","லயலே யலர்தா மரைசேர்ந் துறையும்","வயல் சூழ்ந் தனவூர் வளமார்ந் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Villages ringed by fields are rich: carp leap, swans take fright and fly, and then settle by the open lotus like the white moon joining the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2852","lb":"CC2852","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2853":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2853,"t":["அவணத் தவர்கூந் தலகிற் புகையைச்","சிவணிச் சிறுகால் கமுகம் பொழில்சேர்ந்","துவணுய்த் திடமஞ் செனநின் றுலவும்","பவணத் தொருபாங் கினதா லளிதோ."],"p":"","r":"On one side of the mansions the breeze, carrying the smoke of women's hair, passes through areca groves and stands still in the houses like a guest.","n":"The line about the breeze standing still as 'a halt' is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2853","lb":"CC2853","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2854":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2854,"t":["மதியுஞ் சுடரும் வழிகா ணலுறாப்","பொதியும் மகிலின் புகையுங் கொடியு","நிதியின் கிழவன் னினிதா வுறையும்","பதிபொன் னகரின் படிகொண் டதுவே."],"p":"","r":"Smoke of aloe and banners crowd the way so that moon and sun cannot find their path, and the city rivals the golden town where the lord of wealth lives.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2854","lb":"CC2854","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2855":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2855,"t":["ஏம மாகிய துப்புர வெய்திய","பூமி மாதில கம்மெனும் பொன்கிளர்","நாம நன்னகர் வீதிக டாமெலாங்","காம வல்லி கிடந்தன போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The city named Bhumitilaka, the ear-ornament of the earth, is a safe and golden place whose streets lie like creepers of desire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2855","lb":"CC2855","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2856":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2856,"t":["பைங்கழன் மன்னர் மன்னன் பவணமா தேவ னென்பான்","சங்கினுண் முத்த மொப்பாள் சயமதி பயந்த நம்பி","யைங்கணைக் காம னன்னா னசோதர னரச சீயந்","தங்கிய கேள்வி யாற்குத் தையலார்ச் சேர்த்தி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Pavanamadevan, king of kings, joins to women his son Asodharan, whom Sayamathi bore, a lion in learning and like the love-god himself.","n":"The Tamil says only 'joined to women'; whether this is marriage is not stated. The verse was printed in eight half-lines and joined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kaamam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2856","lb":"CC2856","mm":1,"ev":"King Pavanamadevan gives his son Asodharan women to wed or keep.","cl":[],"ag":["பவணமாதேவன்","அசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2857":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2857,"t":["இளமுலை பொருது தேந்தா ரெழில்குழைந் தழிய வைகிக்","கிளைநரம் பிசையுங் கூத்துங் கிளர்ந்தவை கனற்ற நாளும்","வளைமயங் குருவ மென்றோள் வாய்நலம் பருகி மைந்தன்","விளைமதுத் தேறன் மாந்தி வெற்றிப்போ ரநங்க னானான்."],"p":"","r":"Day after day the prince drinks the beauty of the women's mouths and the old wine, and in song and dance and strong drink becomes a victor in the war of love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2857","lb":"CC2857","mm":1,"ev":"The prince passes his days in love, music and wine with the women.","cl":[],"ag":["அசோதரன்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2858":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2858,"t":["இலங்கரி பரந்த வாட்க ணிளையவர் புலவி நீங்கச்","சிலம்பெனும் வண்டு பாடச் சீறடிப் போது புல்லி","யலங்கல்வாய்ச் சென்னி சேர்த்தி யரிமதர் மழைக்கண் பில்க","நலங்கவர்ந் துண்டு நண்ணார் நாமுறக் கழிக்கு மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"When the women's quarrel of love is over, he clasps their small feet while his anklets sing like bees, puts his garland head to them, and takes their charms.","n":"The end of line 4 turns to a general lament that the unwise let such days pass; the claim is not marked for that reason.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2858","lb":"CC2858","mm":1,"ev":"The prince wins back the women's favour after a lovers' quarrel.","cl":[],"ag":["அசோதரன்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2859":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2859,"t":["மங்கையர் தம்மொடு மடங்கன் மொய்ம்பினான்","பங்கயப் பனித்தடஞ் சேரப் பார்ப்பனஞ்","செங்கயற் பேரின மிரியச் செவ்வனே","பொங்கிமேற் பறந்துவிண் புதைந்த தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"With the women he goes to the cool lotus lake, and the swans and carp scatter and a flock rises into the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2859","lb":"CC2859","mm":1,"ev":"The prince and the women reach the lake and frighten off the swans and carp.","cl":[],"ag":["அசோதரன்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2860":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2860,"t":["வேய்ந்தவெண் டாமரைக் கோதைபோல விசும்பிற் பறக்கின்ற வெள்ளையன்ன","மாய்ந்த முகிலாடைத் திங்கட்கண்ணி யாகாய மென்னு மரிவைசாயற்","றோய்ந்ததன் காதலன் பற்ற வற்றுச்சொரிகின்ற மேகலைபோல் வீழ்ந்தவாளை","பாய்ந்து துகைப்பக் கிழிந்தகூழைப் பனித்தா மரைசூழ் பகற்கோயிலே."],"p":"","r":"A swan flying like a white lotus wreath in the sky, the sky as a woman whose girdle slips as her lover seizes her, and the carp that leap and tear the lotus, make the pool a day-temple.","n":"The conceit is strained across eight half-lines joined into four; the sense of 'day-temple' is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2860","lb":"CC2860","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2861":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2861,"t":["விரும்புபொற் றட்டிடை வெள்ளிக் கிண்ணமார்ந்","திருந்தன போன்றிள வன்னப் பார்ப்பினம்","பொருந்துபொற் றாமரை யொடுங்கிப் புக்கொளித்","திருந்தகண் டானிளங் கோக்க ணம்பியே."],"p":"","r":"The young swan chicks, like silver cups on a golden dish, press into the golden lotus to hide, but the young lord's eyes find them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2861","lb":"CC2861","mm":1,"ev":"Swan chicks hide in the lotus and the prince looks them out.","cl":[],"ag":["அசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2862":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2862,"t":["உரிமையுட் பட்டிருந் தொளிக்கின் றார்களைப்","பெருமநீ கொணர்கெனப் பேசு காஞ்சுகி","யொருமகற் கீந்தனன் கோயில் புக்கன","னெரிமுயங் கிலங்குவேற் காளை யென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The chamberlain, bidden to bring those who hide in their right places, gives them to a man and enters the palace, and the youth with the burning spear goes in.","n":"Who the 'hidden ones' are and who gives and who receives is unclear; read as the swan chicks.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2862","lb":"CC2862","mm":1,"ev":"The chamberlain gives the hidden ones to one man and the prince enters the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["காஞ்சுகி","அசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2863":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2863,"t":["வடமலைப் பொன்னனார் மகிழ்ந்து தாமரைத்","தடமுறை வீர்க்கிவை தடங்க ளல்லவே","வடமுலை யெனநடாய் வருடிப் பாலமு","துடனுறீஇ யோம்பினார் தேம்பெய் கோதையார்."],"p":"","r":"The women, gold as the northern hills, tell the swans that these are not their pools, and hold them to their breasts, stroking and feeding them nectar-milk.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2863","lb":"CC2863","mm":1,"ev":"The women take the swan chicks to their breasts and tend them.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2864":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2864,"t":["கண்டா னொருநாட் கதிர்மாமுடி மன்னர் மன்னன்","றண்டா மரைசூழ் தடத்திற்பிரித் தார்கள் யாரே","யொண்டா ரிளங்கோவென் றுழையவர் கூற வல்லே","கொண்டீங்கு வம்மின் கொலைவேலவன் றன்னை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"One day the king of kings sees the party at the lotus pool, asks who has separated them, hears from his attendants that it is the young prince, and orders the spear-bearer brought.","n":"The Tamil asks who 'separated' them without saying whom; read as the swans and their young.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2864","lb":"CC2864","mm":1,"ev":"The king sees the prince's party at the pool, asks who has done this, and has the prince summoned.","cl":[],"ag":["பவணமாதேவன்","உழையவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2865":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2865,"t":["படுகண் முழவும் பசும்பொன்மணி யாழு மேங்க","விடுகுந் நுசுப்பி னவராட விருந்த நம்பி","யடிகட் கடிக ளருளிற்றென் றிறைஞ்ச வல்லே","கடிவிம்மு தாரான் கழல்கையிற் றொழுது சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"As drums and lutes sound and the slim-waisted women dance, the attendants tell the seated prince that the lord has sent for him, and he goes and bows at his feet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2865","lb":"CC2865","mm":1,"ev":"The prince is told of the summons and goes, hands joined, to his father.","cl":[],"ag":["உழையவர்","அசோதரன்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2866":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2866,"t":["அணிசே ரிடக்கை விரலால் வலத்தோண்","மணிசேர் வளைவாய் வதின்வைத் துவலத்","தணிமோ திரஞ்சூழ் விரல்வாய் புதையாப்","பணியா முடியாற் பணிந்தா னிலையோன்."],"p":"","r":"Holding his right shoulder with the left hand and keeping fingers from his mouth, the prince, wearing the ring and bowing his head, bows to the old king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2866","lb":"CC2866","mm":1,"ev":"The prince makes the bow of a son before the king.","cl":[],"ag":["அசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2867":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2867,"t":["கிளைப்பிரி வருஞ்சிறை யிரண்டுங் கேட்டியேல்","விளைக்கிய வித்தனா யிருமற் றீங்கெனத்","திளைக்குமா மணிக்குழை சுடரச் செப்பினான்","வளைக்கையார் கவரிகொண் டெறிய மன்னனே."],"p":"","r":"While women of the bangled hands fan him with whisks, the king, jewelled ear-rings blazing, tells him: if he would hear of the two pains of parting from kin, he must stay and be the seed that grew them.","n":"The syntax of lines 1-2 is difficult; the speaker and the seed image are read from the last line, where 'the king' ends the sentence, and the sense of 'two kinds of parting' is a best reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2867","lb":"CC2867","mm":1,"ev":"The king begins his lesson to the prince while the women fan him.","cl":[],"ag":["பவணமாதேவன்","மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவணமாதேவன்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2868":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2868,"t":["அறம்பெரிய கூறின் னலங்கலணி வேலோய்","மறம்புரிகொ ணெஞ்சம்வழி யாப்புகுதந் தீண்டிச்","செறும்பெரிய தீவினைகள் சென்றுகடி தோடி","யுறும்பெரிய துன்ப முயிர்க் கொலையும் வேண்டா"],"p":"","r":"The king tells him that when virtue is great and the heart turns to wrong, the dire deeds that swell run and strike, and the killing of life most of all brings great suffering, so he must not kill.","n":"The verse is the first of five that run on: killing, lying, theft, another's wife, and wealth got by deceit; the same four are named as sins the ascetic guards against at 2824. Here they are taught to a layman prince.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vinai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2868","lb":"CC2868","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["killing a life brings deeds that ripen into great suffering, so it must be shunned","jain","asserted",4,"pavanamadevan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பவணமாதேவன்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2869":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2869,"t":["மெய்யுரை விளங்குமணி மேலுலக கோபுரங்க","ளையமிலை நின்றபுகழ் வையகத்து மன்னு","மையல்விளை மாநரக கோபுரங்கள் கண்டீர்","பொய்யுரையும் வேண்டா புறத்திடுமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He says that the towers of the upper world rise for the one who speaks true and are lasting, while the towers of the great hell are for the lie; so the prince is bidden to throw lying out.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","marumai","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2869","lb":"CC2869","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["truthful speech raises one to heaven, falsehood to hell","jain","asserted",3,"pavanamadevan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பவணமாதேவன்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2870":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2870,"t":["முளரிமுக நாகமுளை யெயிறுழுது கீற","வளவிறுயர் செய்வரிவண் மன்னரத னாலும்","விளைவரிய மாதுயரம் வீழ்கதியு ளுய்க்குங்","களவுகட னாகக் கடிந்திடுதல் சூதே."],"p":"","r":"He says that kings torture a thief, tearing him with the tusks of the lotus-faced elephant, and that the great unseen misery drops him into a fallen birth, so theft and the gambler's trade must be given up.","n":"The last line pairs theft with gambling (சூது); the reading takes both as to be given up. The lines are hard in parts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","vinai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2870","lb":"CC2870","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["theft is punished by kings here and by a fall to a bad birth hereafter","jain","asserted",3,"pavanamadevan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பவணமாதேவன்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2871":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2871,"t":["மடத்தகைய நல்லார் மனங்கரிய மற்றார்","பிடர்த்தலையொள் வாள்போற் பிறர்மனைகள் சேரி","னெடுப்பரிய துன்பத் திடைப்படுவ ரின்னா","நடுக்குடைய காமம் விடுத்திடுத னன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He says that one who goes to others' wives, who is like a bright sword over his own neck, falls into heavy suffering, and that to put down such unsteady desire is good.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","kaamam","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2871","lb":"CC2871","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["lust for another man's wife brings heavy suffering, so desire must be put down","jain","asserted",3,"pavanamadevan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பவணமாதேவன்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2872":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2872,"t":["தெருளிற்பொருள் வானுலக மேறுதற்குச் செம்பொ","னிருளில்படு கால்புகழ்வித் தில்லையெனி னெல்லா","வருளுநக வையநக வைம்பொறியு நையப்","பொருளுநக வீட்டும்பொருள் யாதும்பொரு ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"He says that wealth which, once it is clear, would not take one to the heavenly world is no wealth: gold laughs, the senses laugh, and so does all that earns it.","n":"The verse was printed in eight half-lines and joined; the lines on laughing gold and senses are read loosely and the argument may be a different one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","avaa","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2872","lb":"CC2872","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["wealth that does not lead to heaven or release is not worth the name","jain","asserted",4,"pavanamadevan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பவணமாதேவன்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2873":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2873,"t":["பொய்யொடு மிடைந்தபொரு ளாசையுரு ளாய","மைபடும்வி னைத்துகள் வழக்குநெறி மாயஞ்","செய்தபொருள் பெய்தகலன் செம்மைசுடு செந்தீக்","கைதவ நுனித்தகவ றாடலொழி கென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He ends by telling the prince to abandon wealth-greed tangled with lies, the dust of deeds, the crooked path, deceit, and the dice cunningly played.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","vaaimai","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2873","lb":"CC2873","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["greed for wealth through lies, deceit and gambling must be abandoned","jain","asserted",4,"pavanamadevan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பவணமாதேவன்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:122"},"cc:2874":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2874,"t":["காமமுடை யார்கறுவொ டார்வமுடை யாருந்","தாமமொடு சாந்துபுனை வார்பசியி னுண்பா","ரேமமுடை யார்களிவ ரல்லரிவை யில்லா","வாமனடி யல்லபிற வந்தியன்மி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that people ruled by desire, anger and greed, those who deck themselves with garlands and sandal, and those who eat only from hunger are not a refuge, and bids the hearer bow to no feet but those of the lord who has none of these.","n":"The speaker is not named; the verse follows a sermon that begins at 2867 and is taken here as the same voice. வாமன் is read as a title of the passionless lord, not a name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2874","lb":"CC2874","mm":1,"ev":"The elder tells the prince to worship only the lord free of passion.","cl":[["only the lord free of desire, anger and greed is worth worshipping, not those who are ruled by them","jain","asserted",4,"elder"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடிகள்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2875":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2875,"t":["பூவைகிளி தோகைபுண ரன்னமொடு பன்மா","யாவையவை தங்கிளையி னீங்கியழ வாங்கிக்","காவல்செய்து வைத்தவர்க டங்கிளையி னீங்கிப்","போவர்புகழ் நம்பியிது பொற்பிலது கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Parrots, mynas, peacocks, swans and many other birds cry out when they are torn from their kin and shut under guard; the speaker tells the renowned prince that those who do this will themselves be parted from their kin, and that the keeping of them is unseemly.","n":"This answers the scene at 2864, where the birds are found taken from the pond. The verse says the captors will leave their kin; it does not say when or by what mechanism.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","innaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2875","lb":"CC2875","mm":1,"ev":"The elder rebukes the prince for keeping caged birds away from their flocks.","cl":[["those who tear creatures from their kin and cage them will themselves be parted from their kin","jain","asserted",3,"elder"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடிகள்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2876":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2876,"t":["அல்லித்தா ளற்ற போது மறாதநூ லதனைப் போலத்","தொல்லைத்தம் முடம்பு நீங்கத் தீவினை தொடர்ந்து நீங்காப்","புல்லிக்கொண் டுயிரைச் சூழ்ந்து புக்குழிப் புக்குப் பின்னின்","றெல்லையி றுன்ப வெந்தீச் சுட்டெரித் திடுங்க ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the thread in a lotus stalk does not break when the stalk is torn, so the deed of earlier lives does not leave when the body leaves: it clings to the soul, enters whatever body it enters, follows behind and burns it with the fierce fire of endless suffering.","n":"The thread image makes karma a continuous strand that outlasts each body; the verse names no way to cut it, so its remedy has to come from the verses that follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2876","lb":"CC2876","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["evil deeds cling to the soul through every change of body and burn it with suffering","jain","asserted",3,"elder"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடிகள்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2877":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2877,"t":["அறவிய மனத்த ராகி யாருயிர்க் கருளைச் செய்யிற்","பறவையு நிழலும் போலப் பழவினை யுயிரோ டாடி","மறவியொன் றானு மின்றி மனத்ததே சுரக்கும் பால","கறவையிற் கறக்குந் தம்மாற் காமுறப் பட்ட வெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"If one turns the mind to kindness toward living souls, the old deed plays with the soul as a bird plays with its own shadow, and without a lapse yields from the mind whatever one desires, as a cow gives milk to the milker.","n":"The cow and the bird with its shadow are the two comparisons; the syntax of lines 2-4 is hard and the comparison of the second is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","vinai","payan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2877","lb":"CC2877","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["kindness to living souls makes the old deed yield what one desires","jain","asserted",1,"elder"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடிகள்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2878":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2878,"t":["நெடுமணி யூபத் திட்ட தவழ்நடை யாமை நீணீர்த்","தொடுமணிக் குவளைப் பட்டந் துணையொடு நினைப்ப தேபோற்","கடுமணிக் கயற்க ணல்லார் காமமும் பொருளுஞ் சிந்தித்","தடுமணி யாவி நீப்பா ரறிவினாற் சிறிய நீரார்."],"p":"","r":"A tortoise set on a jewelled post in deep water thinks of its mate in the blue lily bed, and so women with keen fish-like eyes who dwell on desire and wealth lose their lives, being small in understanding.","n":"The grammar of lines 3-4 is hard, so who is said to give up life (the women, or the men who think of them) is not fixed; the simile is read as one of longing that does not release.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2878","lb":"CC2878","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who dwell on desire and wealth lose their lives through small understanding","jain","asserted",4,"elder"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடிகள்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2879":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2879,"t":["வீறழி வினைசெய் காலன் வைரவாள் வலையிற் பட்டாற்","சாறழி குவளை மாலை யவரையுந் தனமு நீக்கி","யாறிழி வரையிற் றோன்று மறநனி நினைப்பர் செம்பொ","னேறெழி னெறியி னேறி யிருவிசும் பாளு நீரார்."],"p":"","r":"When Death, who ruins all splendour, catches a man in his diamond net, the wise leave garlanded women and wealth and think only of dharma, which shows itself like a river coming down a mountain, and so climb a golden way to rule the two heavens.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aram","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2879","lb":"CC2879","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["at death wealth and wives are left and only dharma remains to carry one to heaven","jain","asserted",3,"elder"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடிகள்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2880":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2880,"t":["துன்னிமற் றறத்தைக் கேட்டே துகினெருப் புற்ற தேபொன்","மின்னுத்தார் மார்பன் மெய்வெந் தாலியி னுருகிப் பெண்பா","லன்னப்பார்ப் பன்று கொண்ட தடத்திடை விடுவித் திட்டான்","பின்னைத்தன் கிளைகள் கூட்டம் பெருந்தகை வித்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Having listened to dharma, the prince melts like cloth touched by fire, lets the female cygnet he had taken go free in the pool, and then does something with the gathering of its kin that the last word leaves unclear.","n":"The last word வித்தினான் ('sowed') is read here as bringing the flock together again; the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","kollaamai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2880","lb":"CC2880","mm":1,"ev":"Asodharan releases the cygnet he had captured into the pond and rejoins it to its flock.","cl":[],"ag":["அசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2881":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2881,"t":["மெய்ப்படு முதுபுண் டீர்ப்பான் மேவிய முயற்சி போல","வொப்புடைக் காமந் தன்னை யுவர்ப்பினோ டொழித்துப் பாவ","மிப்படித் திதுவென் றஞ்சிப் பிறவிநோய் வெருவி னாலே","மைப்படு மழைக்க ணல்லார் வாய்க்கொண்ட வமுத மொப்பான்."],"p":"","r":"Like effort to cure an old wound, the prince casts out matching desire with disgust, fearing that this is sin and dreading the disease of birth, and is like one holding nectar in his mouth.","n":"The last line's comparison to nectar in the mouths of dark-eyed women is hard to place and may refer to the prince's earlier pleasure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thuravu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2881","lb":"CC2881","mm":1,"ev":"Asodharan gives up desire out of fear of sin and rebirth.","cl":[["desire is a sin and birth a disease from which one must be healed by giving it up","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2882":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2882,"t":["ஆளியாற் பாயப் பட்ட வடுகளி யானை போல","வாளிவிற் றடக்கை மைந்தன் வாய்விட்டுப் புலம்பிக் காம","நாளினு நஞ்சு துய்த்தே னச்சறை யாக நன்பொற்","றோளியர்த் துறந்து தூய்தாத் தவஞ்செய்வ லடிக ளென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a spotted elephant sprung on by a lion, the bowman cries aloud that he has tasted desire's poison day after day, and will leave the golden-armed women and do pure tapas, lord.","n":"The speaker is read as the prince and the addressee அடிகள் as his father, since the next verse has a king answer the youth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2882","lb":"CC2882","mm":1,"ev":"Asodharan cries out that he will give up the women and do tapas.","cl":[["desire is a poison that one has been eating, and tapas is its cure","jain","reported",3,"asodharan"]],"ag":["அசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அசோதரன்","பவணமாதேவன்"],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2883":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2883,"t":["சிறுவன்வாய் மொழியைக் கேட்டே தேர்மன்னன் றானுஞ் சொன்னா","னுறுகளிற் றுழவ மற்றுன் னொளிமுடித் தாய மெய்தி","யறைகடல் வேலி காத்துன் னலங்கல்வேற் றாய மெல்லாம்","பெறுதகு புதல்வற் கீந்து பின்னைநீ துறத்தி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Hearing the youth, the chariot-king answers that he should first take his bright-crowned share, guard the kingdom bounded by the roaring sea, give all to a worthy son and only then renounce.","n":"Whether the chariot-king is the father is not said; the verse answers a youth's vow with the order of duties, and the story that follows does not wait for a son.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","thuravu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2883","lb":"CC2883","mm":1,"ev":"The king tells the prince to rule and pass on the kingdom before he renounces.","cl":[["a king should rule and hand on his kingdom to a son before renouncing","jain","asserted",4,"pavanamadevan"]],"ag":["பவணமாதேவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பவணமாதேவன்","அசோதரன்"],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2884":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2884,"t":["கொலைச்சிறை யுய்ந்து போகு மொருவனைக் குறுக வோடி","யலைத்தனர் கொண்டு பற்றி யருஞ்சிறை யழுத்து கின்றார்","தொலைப்பருஞ் சுற்றத் தாரோ பகைவரோ வடிக ளென்ன","விலைப்பெரு மணியை முந்நீர் நடுக்கடல் வீழ்த்த தொத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a man who has escaped the prison of killing and is run down, seized and thrust into a hard cell again, he asks whether these kin he cannot shake off are kin or enemies, and is like one who throws a priceless gem into mid-ocean.","n":"The question is in the youth's mouth and the gem comparison may be the narrator's; the next verse gives his answer.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","pattru","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2884","lb":"CC2884","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["kin who bind one to the world are enemies rather than kin","jain","reported",3,"asodharan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அசோதரன்","பவணமாதேவன்"],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2885":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2885,"t":["காதல மல்ல மேனாட் கழிந்தநம் பிறவி தம்மு","ளேதிலம் யாங்க ளெல்லா மினிக்கொளு முடம்பினானு","மாதலாற் சுற்ற மில்லை யதுபட்ட வாறென் றம்பூந்","தாதலர் மார்ப னற்புத் தளையறப் பரிந்திட் டானே."],"p":"","r":"He reasons that those about him are not his own, since in past births they were strangers and in the bodies to come they will be strangers again, so there is no kin; and the garlanded one cuts the bond of affection through.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","piravi","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2885","lb":"CC2885","mm":1,"ev":"Asodharan reasons that no one is truly kin and cuts the bonds of affection.","cl":[["no one is kin across births, since all were strangers before and will be strangers again","jain","asserted",2,"asodharan"]],"ag":["அசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அசோதரன்",""],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2886":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2886,"t":["நற்பொறி குயிற்றி வல்லான் செய்ததோர் நன்பொற் பாவை","பொற்பொறி கழல வெல்லாப் பொறிகளுங் கழல்வ தேபோற்","சொற்பொறி சோர வெல்லாப் பொறிகளுஞ் சோர்ந்து நம்ப","னிற்பொறி யின்ப நீக்கி யிராயிரர் சூழச் சென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"As a jointed doll made by a master falls apart in every joint when its pins are loosed, so every sense fails when the joint of speech fails; the prince leaves the delight of the senses and goes surrounded by two thousand.","n":"The doll image describes the body's failing as a mechanism; the verse then turns to the prince's leaving without saying that his leaving is this failing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thuravu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2886","lb":"CC2886","mm":1,"ev":"Asodharan leaves his pleasures and goes out with two thousand companions.","cl":[["the body is a jointed contrivance whose senses all fail together when one joint gives way","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2887":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2887,"t":["தணக்கிறப் பறித்த போதுந் தானளை விடுத்தல் செல்லா","நிணப்புடை யுடும்ப னாரை யாதினா னீக்க லாகு","மணப்புடை மாலை மார்ப னொருசொலே யேது வாகக்","கணைக்கவி னழித்த கண்ணார்த் துறந்துபோய்க் கடவு ளானான்."],"p":"","r":"As the flesh-smelling monitor lizard that clings to its hole when a plank is torn away can be moved by some means, so on the cause of a single word the garlanded prince left the women with arrow-bright eyes and became a holy man.","n":"கடவுளானான் is read as 'became a holy one', one who has taken the vow, not as becoming a deity. The details of the lizard simile are not fully clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","thuravu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2887","lb":"CC2887","mm":1,"ev":"Asodharan abandons the women and becomes an ascetic after one word.","cl":[["the firmest clinging to the world can be broken by a single word","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அசோதரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2888":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2888,"t":["தூமமார்ந் தணங்கு நாறுஞ் சுரும்புசூழ் தாரி னானுந்","தாமமா ரொலிய லைம்பாற் சயமதித் திருவு மார்ந்த","காமமா சுண்ட காதற் கதிர்வளைத் தோளி னாரு","நாமநாற் கதியு மஞ்சி நற்றவத் துச்சி கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The prince and the women with shining bangles and garlanded hair, fearing the dreaded four states of birth, took the summit of fine tapas.","n":"நாற்கதி is the four courses of rebirth (hell, beast, human, god) of Jain teaching; the verse does not list them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2888","lb":"CC2888","mm":1,"ev":"Asodharan and his wives take the vow of tapas.","cl":[["the four states of birth are to be feared and escaped by tapas","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["அசோதரன்","தோளியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2889":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2889,"t":["ஆசார நாணத் தவஞ்செய் தலர்க் கற்ப கத்தார்ச்","சாசார னென்னுந் தகைசாலொளித் தேவர் கோவாய்","மாசார மாய மணிவானுல காண்டு வந்தாய்","தூசார்ந்த வல்குற் றுளும்புந்நலந் தாரொ டென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"A speaker greets one who by tapas done in regard for conduct and shame became a bright god, lord of the Kalpa-dwellers called Sasaran, and has come ruling a jewelled sky-world.","n":"Neither speaker nor addressee is named; the addressee may be Civakan, whose earlier birth the sermon tells, or the prince now a god. The last line is hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","marumai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2889","lb":"CC2889","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["tapas done with regard for conduct and shame earns lordship of heaven","jain","asserted",1,"elder"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடிகள்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2890":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2890,"t":["மின்னார் சிலம்பிற் சிலம்புங்குர லன்ன மேனாண்","மன்னா பிரித்தாய் பிரிந்தாய்சிறை வைத்த தனாற்","பொன்னார மார்ப சிறைப்பட்டனை போலு மென்றா","னின்னாப் பிறவிப் பிணிக்கின்மருந் தாய சொல்லான்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the garlanded king that in earlier days he parted others and so seems to have been shut in a prison himself, and the speaker's words are medicine for the disease of woeful births.","n":"This returns to the cage of 2875 and puts the repayment in the same form; who is addressed is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2890","lb":"CC2890","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one who cages and parts others is in turn caged and parted","jain","asserted",3,"elder"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடிகள்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2891":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2891,"t":["மஞ்சிவர் மணிவரை யனைய மாதவன்","வஞ்சமி லறவுரை பொதிந்த வாய்மொழி","யஞ்சின னிருந்துழி யம்பு வீழ்ந்தென","நஞ்சுமிழ் வேலினா னடுங்க வீழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"At the words of the great ascetic, mountain-like and full of a sinless sermon, the king with the poison-spitting spear sits in fear and trembles as though an arrow had fallen.","n":"The king is read as Civakan; the verse names neither. The sermon's content is not given here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","acham","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2891","lb":"CC2891","mm":1,"ev":"The king trembles at the ascetic's sermon.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மாதவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2892":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2892,"t":["வாரணி மணித்துடி மருட்டு நுண்ணிடைக்","காரணி மயிலனார் சூழக் காவல","னேரணி மணிமுடி யிறைஞ்சி யேத்தினான்","சீரணி மாதவர் செழும்பொற் பாதமே."],"p":"","r":"The king, ringed by peacock-like women, bows his jewelled crown and praises the glorious feet of the ascetics.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2892","lb":"CC2892","mm":1,"ev":"The king bows his crown at the ascetics' feet and praises them.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2893":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2893,"t":["நலத்திரு மடமக ணயந்த தாமரை","நிலத்திருந் திருசுடர் நிமிர்ந்து செல்வபோ","லுலப்பருந் தவத்தினா லோங்கு சாரணர்","செலத்திரு விசும்பொளி சிறந்த தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The Charanas, high in great tapas, rise like the sun and moon, and the sky grows bright as they go.","n":"The Charanas are the sky-going ascetics of Jain tradition; the verse reports their rising and does not teach how it is done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2893","lb":"CC2893","mm":1,"ev":"The Charanas ascend through the sky.","cl":[["ascetics of great tapas travel the sky","jain","reported",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2894":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2894,"t":["சாரணர் போயபின் சாந்த மேந்திய","வாரணி வனமுலை வஞ்சிக் கொம்பனார்","போரணி புலவுவேற் கண்கள் பூத்தன","நீரணி குவளைநீர் நிறைந்த போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"When the Charanas have gone, the eyes of the vine-slender women with sandal-pasted breasts, bright as spears, brim over like blue lilies filled with water.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2894","lb":"CC2894","mm":1,"ev":"The women weep after the Charanas leave.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2895":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2895,"t":["பொன்வரை நிலாக்கதிர் பொழிந்து போர்த்தபோற்","றென்வரைச் சந்தனந் திளைக்கு மார்பினான்","மின்னிவர் நுசுப்பினார் மெலிய மெல்லவே","யின்னுரை கொடான்கொடிக் கோயி லெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, his chest bright with southern sandal like a golden mountain under the moon, gives the slender women no kind word and goes to the banner-hung palace.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2895","lb":"CC2895","mm":1,"ev":"The king leaves the women without a word and goes to the palace.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2896":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2896,"t":["அஞ்சுரை பொழிந்தபா லன்ன மென்மயிர்ப்","பஞ்சிமெல் லணையின்மேற் பரவை யல்குலார்","மஞ்சிவர் மதிமுக மழுங்க வைகினார்","நஞ்சுயிர்த் தணிநலங் கரிந்து நையவே."],"p":"","r":"On a soft cotton couch the women with sea-wide hips lie with their cloud-dark moon faces dimmed, their beauty withered by sighs of poison.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2896","lb":"CC2896","mm":1,"ev":"The women lie wasting on their couch.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2897":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2897,"t":["வெள்ளெயிற் றரவு மேய்ந்த மிச்சிலின் மெலிந்து மேகப்","புள்வயிற் பிறந்த புட்போ லொன்றலா துரைத்த றேற்றார்","கள்வயிற் றலர்ந்த கோதைக் கலாபவில் லுமிழு மல்கு","லொள்ளெயிற் றவர்கள் பொன்பூத் தொளிமணி யுருவ நீத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The women, who thin like what the white-fanged snake has eaten and leaves, who can say nothing but one thing like the cloud-bird, lose their golden pallor and the colour of their bright gems.","n":"The two comparisons (the snake's leavings and the bird of the cloud) are hard to place; the sense of loss is clear but the figure is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2897","lb":"CC2897","mm":1,"ev":"The women lose their colour and ornament in grief.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2898":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2898,"t":["கிளிச்சொலி னினிய சொல்லார் கிண்கிணி சிலம்பொ டேங்கக்","குளித்துநீ ரிரண்டு கோலக் கொழுங்கயல் பிறழ்ப வேபோற்","களித்துநீர் சுமந்து வாட்கண் கலாஅய்ப்பிறழ்ந் தலமந் தாட","வளித்ததா ரலங்க லாழி யவன்றுற வுரைத்து மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The women with parrot-sweet speech, their anklets crying out, weep and toss like carp in water, because the king in his garland and ring has told them of his renunciation.","n":"The verse says only that he spoke of renunciation; his words are not given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thuravu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2898","lb":"CC2898","mm":1,"ev":"The women weep because the king has told them he will renounce.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:123"},"cc:2899":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2899,"t":["புனைமருப் பழுந்தக் குத்திப் புலியொடு பொருது வென்ற","கனைகுர லுருமுச் சீற்றக் கதழ்விடை யுரிவை போர்த்த","துனைகுரன் முரசத் தானைத் தோன்றலைத் தம்மி னென்றா","னனைமல ரலங்கற் கண்ணி நந்தனுந் தொழுது சேர்ந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan sends for the lord of the drum-sounding army, and Nandan, in a wreath of wet flowers, bows and comes up to him.","n":"The verb 'said' has no named subject and 'the lord of the army' is not named; Civakan and Nandan are read from 2903, where the younger brother is called Nandan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","panivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2899","lb":"CC2899","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan orders Nandan to be brought, and Nandan bows and approaches.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2900":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2900,"t":["கொடியணி யலங்கன் மார்பிற் குங்குமக் குன்ற மன்னா","னடிபணிந் தருளு வாழி யரசரு ளரச வென்னப்","படுசின வெகுளி நாகப் பைத்தலை பனித்து மாழ்க","விடியுமிழ் முரச நாண வின்னண மியம்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandan, bowing at his feet, begs him to be gracious as king of kings, and his voice is likened to thunder that shames the drum and makes the angry cobra's hood shake.","n":"Lines 2-3 are a simile of a loud voice that the cobra and the drum cannot match; the grammar linking the cobra clause to the speech is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","anbu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2900","lb":"CC2900","mm":1,"ev":"Nandan bows at Civakan's feet and asks for his grace.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2901":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2901,"t":["ஊனுடைக் கோட்டு நாகான் சுரிமுக வேற்றை யூர்ந்து","தேனுடைக் குவளைச் செங்கேழ் நாகிளந் தேரை புல்லிக்","கானுடைக் கழனிச் செந்நெற் கதிரணைத் துஞ்சு நாடு","வேன்மிடை தானைத் தாயம் வீற்றிருந் தாண்மோ வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan tells Nandan to sit enthroned and rule the inheritance of the spear-crowded army, in a land where creatures sleep on the ears of red rice.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","naadu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2901","lb":"CC2901","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan offers Nandan the kingdom.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2902":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2902,"t":["கரும்பலாற் காடொன் றில்லாக் கழனிசூழ் பழன நாடுஞ்","சுரும்புலாங் கண்ணி விண்ணோர் துறக்கமும் வீடும் வேண்டே","னரும்புலா யலர்ந்த வம்மென் றாமரை யனைய பாதம்","விரும்பியான் வழிபட் டன்றோ வாழ்வதென் வாழ்க்கை யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Nandan turns down the rich land of cane fields, the heaven of the bee-haunted gods and release alike, and asks whether his life is not to live worshipping the lotus-like feet he loves.","n":"Naming வீடு beside country and heaven makes the refusal total. The verse does not name whose feet are meant; the context of the offer in 2901 reads them as Civakan's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","veedu","panivu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2902","lb":"CC2902","mm":1,"ev":"Nandan refuses the kingdom and says he wants only to serve his brother.","cl":[["serving the beloved elder brother's feet is a worthier life than kingdom, heaven or release","general","reported",4,"nandattan"]],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நந்தட்டன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2903":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2903,"t":["குன்றென மருண்டு கோல மணிவண்டுங் குழாங்கொ டேனுஞ்","சென்றுமொய்த் திமிரும் யானைச் சீவகற் கிளைய நம்பி","மன்றல்வீற் றிருந்து மின்னு மணிக்குவ டனைய தோளா","னொன்றுமற் றரசு வேண்டா னுவப்பதே வேண்டி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The younger brother, shoulders like a bright blue lily, wants no kingdom at all and asks only for what delights him.","n":"What 'delights' him is left open; read after 2902 it is the service of his brother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2903","lb":"CC2903","mm":1,"ev":"Nandan declines any kingdom.","cl":[],"ag":["நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2904":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2904,"t":["பொலிவுடைத் தாகு மேனும் பொள்ளலிவ் வுடம்பென் றெண்ணீ","வலியுடை மருப்பி னல்லால் வாரணந் தடக்கை வையா","தொலியுடை யுருமுப் போன்று நிலப்படா தூன்றின் வைவேற்","கலிகடிந் துலகங் காக்குங் காளையைக் கொணர்மி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Reasoning that the body, however handsome, is hollow, Civakan tells his men to bring the youth who will guard the world, as an elephant lays its trunk on nothing but its strong tusk.","n":"Opening on the body's hollowness before naming an heir makes the choice of successor follow from seeing the body as unreliable. Lines 2-3 (the elephant's trunk resting on its tusk, thunder not falling to earth) are two obscure similes; I take them to mean resting weight on a sound support.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","arasu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2904","lb":"CC2904","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan orders the youth who will protect the world to be brought.","cl":[["the body, though handsome, is hollow and cannot be relied on","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2905":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2905,"t":["கழுமணி யார மார்பிற் காவலன் மக்கள் காய்பொ","னெழுவளர்ந் தனைய திண்டோ ளிளையவர் தம்முண் மூத்த","தழுமலர்க் கொம்பு போலுந் தத்தைநாட் பயந்த நம்பி","விழுமணிப் பூணி னானை வீற்றிரீஇ விதியிற் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"The king seats the eldest of the young princes, the jewelled son of Gandharvadattai, and instructs him by rule.","n":"Gandharvadattai is the Tattai of line 3, as in the earlier ilampakams; 2906-2911 read as the instruction that begins here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","arasu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2905","lb":"CC2905","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan seats his eldest son and begins to instruct him.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","தத்தைநாட் பயந்த நம்பி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2906":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2906,"t":["பால்வளை பரந்து மேயும் படுகடல் வளாக மெல்லாங்","கோல்வளை யாமற் காத்துன் குடைநிழற் றுஞ்ச நோக்கி","நூல்விளைந் தனைய நுண்சொற் புலவரோ டறத்தை யோம்பின்","மேல்விளை யாத வின்பம் வேந்தமற் றில்லை கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan tells the heir that if he guards the sea-ringed earth under his umbrella with the sceptre unbent and keeps dharma with poets whose fine words are ripe as treatises, a king has no higher joy.","n":"The joy of kingship is ranked highest by a king who is about to give it up; the claim is for the heir, not for Civakan's own last aim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","sengol","arasu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2906","lb":"CC2906","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who guards the earth with an unbent sceptre and keeps dharma with the learned has no higher joy","general","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தத்தைநாட் பயந்த நம்பி"],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2907":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2907,"t":["வாய்ப்படுங் கேடு மின்றாம் வரிசையி னரிந்து நாளுங்","காய்த்தநெற் கவளந் தீற்றிற் களிறுதான் கழனி மேயின்","வாய்ப்பட லின்றிப் பொன்றும் வல்லனாய் மன்னன் கொள்ளி","னீத்தநீர் ஞால மெல்லா நிதிநின்று சுரக்கு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"A ruler who takes his revenue in measure, as an elephant is fed balls of ripe paddy cut each day, loses nothing, whereas the elephant that grazes the field itself perishes; the land he has watered then pours out wealth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kodungol","selvam","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2907","lb":"CC2907","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who takes revenue in measure keeps the treasure flowing, while one who grabs ruins the field","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தத்தைநாட் பயந்த நம்பி"],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2908":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2908,"t":["நெல்லுயிர் மாந்தர்க் கெல்லா நீருயி ரிரண்டுஞ் செப்பிற்","புல்லுயிர் புகைந்து பொங்கு முழங்கழ விலங்கு வாட்கை","மல்லலங் களிற்று மாலை வெண்குடை மன்னர் கண்டாய்","நல்லுயிர் ஞாலந் தன்னு ணாமவே னம்பி யென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Of rice and water, both called the life of men, he says the king with blazing spear, white umbrella and elephants is the good life of the earth that people dread.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","uyir","vendhan","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2908","lb":"CC2908","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the king is the life of the world, as rice and water are the life of men","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தத்தைநாட் பயந்த நம்பி"],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2909":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2909,"t":["ஆர்வலஞ் சூழ்ந்த வாழி யலைமணித் தேரை வல்லா","னேர்நிலத் தூருமாயி னீடுபல் காலஞ் செல்லு","மூர்நில மறித றேற்றா தூருமேன் முறிந்து வீழுந்","தார்நில மார்ப வேந்தர் தன்மையு மன்ன தாமே."],"p":"","r":"A driver who knows his chariot can run it long on a ploughed field, but if he drives without learning the ground it breaks and falls; kings are the same, he warns.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","arivu","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2909","lb":"CC2909","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king must know the land he governs as a charioteer knows the ground","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தத்தைநாட் பயந்த நம்பி"],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2910":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2910,"t":["காய்ந்தெறி கடுங்கற் றன்னைக் கவுட்கொண்ட களிறு போல","வாய்ந்தறி வுடைய ராகி யருளொடு வெகுளி மாற்றி","வேந்தர்தாம் விழைப வெல்லாம் வெளிப்படார் மறைத்தல் கண்டாய்","நாந்தக வுழவ ரேறே நன்பொரு ளாவ தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Like an elephant that holds a hurled stone in its cheek, learned kings put away anger with mercy and keep what they desire hidden, which he calls the real wealth, addressing the heir as a bull among swordsmen.","n":"நாந்தக உழவர் is read as the warriors of the nandaka sword; the compound is rare.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vekuli","arul","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2910","lb":"CC2910","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["wise kings put away anger with mercy and do not show what they desire","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தத்தைநாட் பயந்த நம்பி"],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2911":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2911,"t":["குடிபழி யாமை யோம்பிற் கொற்றவேன் மன்னர் மற்று","னடிவழிப் படுவர் கண்டா யரும்புகழ் கெடுத லஞ்சி","நொடியலோ ரெழுத்தும் பொய்யை நுண்கலை நீத்த நீந்திக்","கொடியெடுத் தவர்க்கு நல்கு கொழித்துணர் குமர வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"If he guards his people from blame, other kings will follow his path; fearing the loss of fame he should not speak even one false syllable, and he should give to those who have crossed the arts and raised their banner.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","pugazh","arasu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2911","lb":"CC2911","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a king who keeps his people from blame is followed by other kings","general","asserted",1,"civakan"],["a king must not speak even one false syllable, for fear of losing fame","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","தத்தைநாட் பயந்த நம்பி"],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2912":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2912,"t":["சேனடந் தாங்கு மோடிச் சென்றுலாய்ப் பிறழும் வாட்கண்","மான்மட நோக்கின் மாதர் மாலைநாட் பயந்த மைந்தன்","கானடந் தனைய மான்றேர்க் காளையைக் காவன் மன்னன்","றானுட னணிந்து தன்போ லிளவர சாக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The king himself dresses a youth, son of a doe-eyed lady and splendid as a forest of deer-drawn chariots, and makes him crown prince like himself.","n":"The verse does not say whether this youth is the prince of 2905 or another son.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2912","lb":"CC2912","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan makes a youth crown prince.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","காளை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2913":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2913,"t":["கூரெயி றணிந்த கொவ்வைக் கொழுங்கனிக் கோலச் செவ்வா","யேரணி மயிலஞ் சாய லிலக்கணை யீன்ற சிங்கஞ்","சீருடைச் செம்பொற் கண்ணிச் சிறுவனைச் செம்பொன் மாரி","பேரறைந் துலக முண்ணப் பெருநம்பி யாக வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"He decrees that the lion-like boy whom Ilakkanai bore, in a gold garland, shall become the great lord while a shower of gold is announced for the world to enjoy.","n":"பொன் மாரி is a lavish public gift; the golden shower comes from the king's treasure, not from the heavens.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","eegai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2913","lb":"CC2913","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan orders his son by Ilakkanai made great lord and a golden shower proclaimed.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","சிறுவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2914":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2914,"t":["தன்கழ றொழாத மன்னர் தாஞ்சுமந் தேந்தி நின்ற","பொன்றிக ழுருவிற் றம்பி புதல்வனைத் தந்து போற்றி","மின்றிகழ் முடியுஞ் சூட்டி வீற்றிரீஇ வேந்து செய்தான்","குன்றினங் குழீஇய போலுங் குஞ்சரக் குழாத்தி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The younger of golden form, whom kings who never worshipped his anklets raised aloft, hands over the boy, honours him, crowns him with a lightning-bright crown and sets him on the throne among elephants massed like hills.","n":"Who is the 'younger' of golden form is not stated. Nandan, who has just refused the kingdom, is the likelier giver and Civakan the one who crowns, but the verse does not separate them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2914","lb":"CC2914","mm":1,"ev":"The boy is given over, honoured, crowned and enthroned.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","நந்தட்டன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2915":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2915,"t":["நிலஞ்செதி ளெடுக்கு மான்றேர் நித்திலம் விளைந்து முற்றி","நலஞ்செய்த வைரக் கோட்ட நாறுமும் மதத்த நாகங்","குலஞ்செய்த குமரர்க் கெல்லாம் கொடுத்தன னிதியு நாடு","முலஞ்செய்த வைரக் குன்ற மோரிரண்ட டனைய தோளான்."],"p":"","r":"The king, whose shoulders are like two diamond hills, gives all the princes of the line elephants whose tusks bear pearls, and the treasure and the country.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2915","lb":"CC2915","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan gives treasure, country and elephants to the princes.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2916":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2916,"t":["நூற்கிடங் கொடுத்த கேள்வி நுண்செவி மண்கொண் ஞாட்பில்","வேற்கிடங் கொடுத்த மார்பின் வில்வலான் றோழர் மக்க","ணாற்கடல் வளாகங் காக்கு நம்பிதன் கண்க ளாகப்","பாற்கடற் கேள்வி யாரைப் பழிப்பற நாட்டி னானே."],"p":"","r":"He sets the learned and blameless sons of his bowman comrades as the eyes of the young lord who will guard the four-sea earth.","n":"'Eyes' for counsellors is an old figure for a king's advisers; the comrades' sons inherit their fathers' place beside the throne.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","thunai","amaichu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2916","lb":"CC2916","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan appoints his comrades' sons as the young king's counsellors.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2917":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2917,"t":["காவல ரகல மென்னுங் கழனியு ளுழுது காமர்","மாவலம் விளைத்த கோட்டு மழகளிற் றரச னன்னான்","பூவலர் கொடிய னாரை விடுக்கிய கோயில் புக்கான்","றூவல ரொலிய லார்தம் வலக்கண்க டுடித்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king, who ploughed the field of kings' chests and reaped elephants, enters the palace to release his women, whose right eyes begin to throb.","n":"The throbbing of the right eye is told as an omen; the verse does not call it ill, and the closing phrase of line 4 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thuravu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2917","lb":"CC2917","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan goes to the women's palace to release them, and their right eyes throb.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2918":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2918,"t":["செம்பொனாற் செறிய வேய்ந்து திருமணி முகடு கொண்ட","வெம்புநீள் சுடருஞ் சென்னி விலங்கிய மாட மெய்தி","யம்பொனாற் றெளிந்த பாவை யனையவர்த் தம்மி னென்றான்","பைம்பொனால் வளர்க்கப் பட்ட பனைதிரண் டனைய தோளான்."],"p":"","r":"In a mansion roofed with gold and jewels whose peak the sun's fire grazes, the king tells his people to bring the gold-doll women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2918","lb":"CC2918","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan orders his wives brought to the mansion.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்",""],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2919":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2919,"t":["தின்பளித மாலைத்திர டாமந் திகழ் தீம்பூ","நன்கொளிசெய் தாமநறும் பூநவின்ற தாமம்","பொன்றெளித்த தாமம்புரி முத்தமிளிர் தாம","மின்றெளித்த மின்னுமணி வீழ்ந்ததிர டாமம்."],"p":"","r":"Garlands are listed one after another, of crystal, strung flowers, fragrant flowers, gold, pearls and jewels, hanging in masses.","n":"The verse is a list with no verb; it runs into 2920.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2919","lb":"CC2919","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2920":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2920,"t":["ஈன்றமயில் போனெடிய தாமத் திடையெங்கு","மான்றுமணம் விம்முபுகை மல்கிநுரை யேபோற்","றோன்றுமணிக் காலமளித் தூவணையின் மேலார்","மூன்றுலகம் விற்குமுலை முற்றிழையி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Among long garlands, in incense smoke rising like foam, the women whose breasts could buy three worlds lie on soft beds laid in jewelled rooms.","n":"The picture of luxury is set just before the women are told of the king's intent.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2920","lb":"CC2920","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2921":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2921,"t":["இன்னதரு ளென்றிளைய ரேத்தஞிமி றார்ப்ப","மின்னினிடை நோவமிளிர் மேகலைகண் மின்னப்","பொன்னரிய கிண்கிணியும் பூஞ்சிலம்பு மேங்க","மன்னனடி சேர்ந்திறைஞ்சி வாழியென நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"As the young women hail it as grace, bees hum, slim waists ache under shining girdles and gold anklets ring, the women stand before the king, bow at his feet and cry 'live long'.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2921","lb":"CC2921","mm":1,"ev":"The women come before Civakan and bow at his feet.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2922":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2922,"t":["கலவமயில் கால்குவித்த போலுங்கம ழைம்பா","னிலவுமணி மேகலைநி லாவுமிழும் பைம்பூ","ணிலவமலர் வாயினணி கூரெயிற்றி னீரே","யுலவுமனம் வைத்துறுதி கேண்மினமி தென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan addresses the women, with hair like peacock tails and moon-bright girdles, and bids them hold the wandering mind steady and hear the lasting good, which he calls nectar.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","manam","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2922","lb":"CC2922","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan asks his wives to listen to what is lastingly good.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2923":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2923,"t":["வாயழ லுயிர்க்கு மாழி மன்னவன் குறிப்பு நோக்கி","வேயழத் திரண்ட மென்றோள் வெம்முலைப் பரவை யல்குற்","றோய்பிழி யலங்க லார்தந் தொன்னலந் தொலைந்து வாடிக்","காயழற் கொடியைச் சேர்ந்த கற்பக மாலை யொத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Reading the wheel-king's sign, the garlanded women lose their old beauty and fade like kalpaka garlands laid on a vine of burning fire.","n":"வாயழல் உயிர்க்கும் may be the women's hot sighs or a figure for the king's wheel; I take it as sighs, which makes the simile one of grief.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nilaiyaamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2923","lb":"CC2923","mm":1,"ev":"The women read Civakan's intent and wither.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:124"},"cc:2924":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2924,"t":["கருங்கடற் பிறப்பி னல்லால் வலம்புரி காணுங் காலைப்","பெருங்குளத் தென்றுந் தோன்றா பிறைநுதற் பிணைய னீரே.","யருங்கொடைத் தான மாய்ந்த வருந்தவந் தெரியின் மண்மேன்","மருங்குடை யவர்கட் கல்லான் மற்றையர்க் காவ துண்டே."],"p":"","r":"As the conch and pearl are found only in the dark sea and never in a big pool, so the rare austerity that ripens from giving is found in few on earth, though lesser folk may share some of it.","n":"The last two lines turn on a doubtful syntax (மருங்குடை யவர்கட் கல்லான்); the contrast between the few and the rest is read from the sense, not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thavam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2924","lb":"CC2924","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the rare austerity born of giving is found only in a few","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2925":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2925,"t":["அட்டுநீ ரருவிக் குன்றத் தல்லது வைரந் தோன்றா","குட்டநீர்க் குளத்தி னல்லாற் குப்பைமேற் குவளை பூவா","விட்டுநீர் வினவிக் கேண்மின் விழுத்தகை யவர்க ளல்லாற்","பட்டது பகுத்துண் பாரிப் பார்மிசை யில்லை கண்டீர்."],"p":"","r":"As diamonds come only from the hill of the pouring stream and the blue lily blooms only in clear water, so only the noble share what they have; listen and ask, he says, for none else on earth does it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","panpu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2925","lb":"CC2925","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["only the noble share what they have with others","general","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2926":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2926,"t":["நரம்பொலி பரந்த கோயி னன்னுதன் மகளிர் தூவும்","பெரும்பலிச் சோற்றி னீதல் பெரிதரி தாகு மேனுஞ்","சுரும்பொலி கோதை யார்த மனைவயிற் றூண்டோ றூட்டு","மரும்பலி யனைத்து மீயி னதுபொருட் குன்று கண்டீர்."],"p":"","r":"Though giving out of the offering-rice that women scatter in the temple is hard, one who gives the rare alms at every house-pillar of the women's homes gains a mountain of wealth.","n":"The picture of feeding at each pillar of a household is hard to place and the sense of 'offering' (பலி) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2926","lb":"CC2926","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["gifts of food given house after house turn into a mountain of wealth","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2927":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2927,"t":["அற்றவர் வருத்த நீக்கி யாருயிர் கொண்டு நிற்குந்","துற்றவி ழீதல் செம்பொற் றுறக்கத்திற் கேணி யாகு","முற்றுயி ரோம்பித் தீந்தே னூனொடு துறப்பின் யார்க்கு","மற்றுரை யில்லை மண்ணும் விண்ணுநும் மடிய வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Food given to the hungry that removes their distress and keeps their life is a well in the golden heaven, and one who guards every life and gives up honey and flesh has nothing to be said against him: earth and sky are his servants.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kollaamai","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2927","lb":"CC2927","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["food given to the destitute is a well of reward in heaven","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"],["one who protects all lives and abstains from honey and flesh owns earth and sky","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2928":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2928,"t":["மாலைப் பந்தும் மாலையு மேந்தி மதுவார்பூஞ்","சோலைம் மஞ்ஞைச் சூழ்வளை யார்தோள் விளையாடி","ஞாலங் காக்கும் மன்னவ ராவார் நறவுண்ணாச்","சீலங் காக்குஞ் சிற்றுப காரம் முடையாரே."],"p":"","r":"Kings who rule the world, after playing in the garden with bangled girls, are those who guard the habit of not drinking liquor, a small service.","n":"The verse names the cause only by hint (சிற்றுபகாரம், 'small help'); the later-birth reading is supplied from the sermon's frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","arasu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2928","lb":"CC2928","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a small kindness joined to abstaining from liquor brings kingship in a later birth","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2929":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2929,"t":["மாசித் திங்கண் மாசின சின்னத் துணிமுள்ளி","னூசித் துன்ன மூசிய வாடை யுடையாகப்","பேசிப் பாவாய் பிச்சையெ னக்கை யகலேந்திக்","கூசிக் கூசி நிற்பார் கொடுத்துண் டறியாதார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who never ate after giving stand shrinking in Masi-month cold, in cloth stitched from thorn-torn rags, begging 'a morsel' with the hand held out.","n":"The cause (never giving) is stated in the last line only; karma is implied, not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","nilaiyaamai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2929","lb":"CC2929","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan shows his wives a beggar shivering in rags with an outstretched hand, who never gave.","cl":[["beggary now is the fruit of never having given","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2930":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2930,"t":["காட்டகத் தொருமகன் றுரக்கு மாக்கலை","யோட்டுடைத் தாமெனி னுய்யு நங்களை","யாட்டியிட் டாருயி ரளைந்து கூற்றுவ","னீட்டிய விளைமதுப் போல வுண்ணுமே."],"p":"","r":"Death is shown as a hunter driving a great deer in the forest, who, if we have no shelter, will mix up our lives and drink them like a ripened liquor he has laid by.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are hard to parse (காட்டகத் தொருமகன் துரக்கும் மாக்கலை; ஓட்டுடைத் தாமெனின்) and the simile is read tentatively.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","nilaiyaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2930","lb":"CC2930","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["Death gathers and takes the life of all who have no refuge","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2931":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2931,"t":["புள்ளுவர் கையினு முய்யும் புள்ளுள","கள்ளவிழ் கோதையீர் காண்மி னல்வினை","யொள்ளியா னொருமக னுரைத்த தென்னன்மின்","றெள்ளியீ ரறத்திறந் தெரிந்து கொண்மினே."],"p":"","r":"Even from the hands of fowlers some birds escape; he bids the women look, and not dismiss with 'what' the word of a wise man, but learn the way of dharma.","n":"The addressee is the women; the bird image is read as an escape that dharma gives.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","arivu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2931","lb":"CC2931","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["good deeds are a way of escape from Death, as some birds escape the fowler","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2932":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2932,"t":["மாய்தலும் பிறத்தலும் வளர்ந்து வீங்கலுந்","தேய்தலு முடைமையைத் திங்கள் செப்புமால்","வாய்புகப் பெய்யினும் வழுக்கி னல்லறங்","காய்வது கலதிமைப் பால தாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The moon tells of dying, birth, growth and waning; to be angry with good dharma when it fails is what belongs to fools.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are obscure (வாய்புகப் பெய்யினும் வழுக்கின்); the sense that scorn of dharma is folly is the best fit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2932","lb":"CC2932","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["birth, growth, waning and death are the nature of all things, as the moon shows","general","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2933":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2933,"t":["புள்ளிநீர் வீழ்ந்தது பெருகிப் புன்புலா","லுள்வளர்ந் தொருவழித் தோன்றிப் பேரற","முள்குமேன் முழுப்புலாற் குரம்பை யுய்ந்துபோய்","வெள்ளநீ ரின்பமே விளைக்கு மென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"A drop of water falls, grows within foul flesh and appears in a place; if the great virtue is thought of, the hut of flesh will be escaped and will yield only the pleasure of floods, so they say.","n":"Line 4's 'flood-water pleasure' is obscure; the verse is reported hearsay (என்பவே).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","aram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2933","lb":"CC2933","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the body begins as a drop that grows in foul flesh","general","asserted",1,"civakan"],["one who thinks of great dharma escapes the hut of flesh and gains lasting pleasure","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2934":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2934,"t":["பாற்றுளி பவளநீர் பெருகி யூன்றிரண்","டூற்றுநீர்க் குறும்புழை யுய்ந்து போந்தபின்","சேற்றுநீர்க் குழியுளே யழுந்திச் செல்கதிக்","காற்றுணாப் பெறாதழு தலறி வீழுமே."],"p":"","r":"Drops of milk and coral-water swell into flesh, it escapes by a narrow channel, then sinks in a pool of muddy water and, having no provision for the road, weeps and falls.","n":"'Provision for the journey' (கால்துணா, line 4) is read as stored good deeds; the word is not explained in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","yaakkai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2934","lb":"CC2934","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["birth is passage through foul water and a helpless fall, without provision of deeds for the next road","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2935":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2935,"t":["திருந்திய நல்லறச் செம்பொற் கற்பகம்","பொருந்திய பொருளொடு போகம் பூத்தலால்","வருந்தினு மறந்திற மறத்த லோம்புமின்","கரும்பெனத் திரண்டதோட் கால வேற்கணீர்."],"p":"","r":"Good dharma is a golden wishing-tree that blossoms with wealth and pleasure, so though they are troubled, the women should not forget the way of dharma.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2935","lb":"CC2935","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["dharma yields wealth and enjoyment and must not be forgotten even in distress","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2936":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2936,"t":["மந்திர மருந்திவை யில்லை யாய்விடி","னைந்தலை யரவினை யாவர் தீண்டுவார்","சுந்தரச் சுரும்புசூழ் மாலை யில்லையேன்","மைந்தரு மகளிரை மருங்கு சேர்கிலார்."],"p":"","r":"Without charm and medicine no one would touch a five-headed snake; without the garland of bees, men would not come near women.","n":"The snake simile for desire is the sermon's; the verse's logic (adornments make desire) is inferred from the paired lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2936","lb":"CC2936","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a woman's allure depends on her adornments, as a snake's danger is met only with charm and medicine","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2937":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2937,"t":["பொன்றுலாம் பொன்னனீர் தருதும் பாகுநீர்","தின்றலாற் சிறுவரை யானுஞ் சொற்சில","வின்றெலா மெம்மருங் கிருந்து பேசினால்","வென்றுலாம் வேற்கணீர் விழுத்தக் கீர்களே."],"p":"","r":"He tells the golden women that they shall give nothing but sweet liquid, and asks them to sit beside him and hear a few words, for they are worthy to win.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are obscure (தருதும் பாகுநீர் தின்றலாற்); the speaker and the offer are read from the context.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","arivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2937","lb":"CC2937","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2938":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2938,"t":["மெய்ப்படு சாந்தும் பூவு மிகநனி கமழு மேனுங்","கைப்படு சாந்தும் பூவுங் கொண்டலாற் கலக்க லாகா","வைப்படு பித்து நெய்த்தோ ரசும்புசோ ரழுகற் புன்றோற்","பொய்ப்பட வுரைத்த துண்டோ பொன்னனீர் நம்மு ணாமால்."],"p":"","r":"Though the body smells strongly of sandal and flower, the sandal and flower are only what was put on by hand; underneath are fat, blood, oozing pus and soiled skin, and he asks if there is any lie in that.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2938","lb":"CC2938","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the body is foul beneath its perfumes and ornaments","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2939":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2939,"t":["அனிச்சத்தம் போது போலத் தொடுப்பவே குழைந்து மாழ்கி","யினிச்செத்தாம் பிறந்த போழ்தே யென்றுநா மிதனை யெண்ணித்","தனிச்சித்தம் வைத்த றேற்றாந் தளர்ந்துகண் பரப்பி நோக்கிப்","பனித்துமென் றுற்ற போழ்தே பழுதிலா வறிவி னென்னாம்."],"p":"","r":"Like the anichcham flower they droop as soon as they are touched; we think 'we die from the day we are born', and what use is clear knowledge when, shaking and gazing about, one says 'we shall tremble'?","n":"The last two lines compress an argument; the sense that knowledge should be used before the shaking of death is read from the simile.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arivu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2939","lb":"CC2939","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["a person is dying from birth, so fear at the end shows knowledge has not been used","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2940":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2940,"t":["நீனிறங் கொண்ட வைம்பா னிழன்மணி யுருவ நீங்கிப்","பானிறங் கொண்டு வெய்ய படாமுலை யையிற் றூங்கி","வேனிற மழைக்கண் டாமு மிமைகுறைந் தழுகி மேனி","தானிறங் கரக்குங் காலந் தையலீர் மெய்ய தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The blue colour of the five-part hair leaves, the breasts hang pale and sagging, the eyes lose their lustre and the body hides its colour: this, women, is not the body's true state.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2940","lb":"CC2940","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["youth's beauty fades and the body is not what it seems","general","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2941":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2941,"t":["குஞ்சர மயாவுயிர்த் தனைய குய்கம","ழஞ்சுவை யடிசிலை யமர்ந்துண் டார்கடா","மிஞ்சிமா நகரிடும் பிச்சை யேற்றலா","லஞ்சினேன் றுறப்பல்யா னார்வ மில்லையே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker, who ate rich food scented with ghee, fears the alms that must be begged in the great city, and says he will renounce but has no desire.","n":"Whether the fear of begging is the wives' answer to the sermon or part of Civakan's own argument is not settled; the verb துறப்பல் (I will renounce) and ஆர்வமில்லையே (there is no desire) may be read as a refusal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pasi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2941","lb":"CC2941","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["renunciation requires giving up fine food and begging alms","jain","reported",3,"civakan_manaiviyar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2942":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2942,"t":["ஒருவர்தம் வலிகெடு முடன்று பொங்கிமே","லிருவர்மற் றியைந்தெழுந் திருப்பி னென்பபோ","லுருவநுண் ணுசுப்பிற விருந்த வொண்மணிப்","பரியகட் படாமுலைப் பைம்பொற் கொம்பனீர்."],"p":"","r":"Two who join in anger against one will break the strength of that one, he says to the slender-waisted, gem-adorned women.","n":"The argument is incomplete; the verse looks like an introduction to the renunciation announcement but the logic of two against one is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2942","lb":"CC2942","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2943":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2943,"t":["காதலங் கழிந்தநா ளிதனி னிப்புற","மேதில மென்றசொற் செவிச்சென் றெய்தலு","மாதரார் மழைமலர்த் தடங்கண் மல்குநீர்","போதுலா மார்பின்வாய்ப் பொழிந்து வீழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"When the word that from the day love is gone they are strangers reached the women's ears, tears from their wide eyes poured down on their flowered breasts.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2943","lb":"CC2943","mm":1,"ev":"The wives hear Civakan say that they are now strangers to him, and they weep.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2944":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2944,"t":["செருக்கி நிணந்தின்று சிவந்து மன்ன ருயிர்செற்ற","நெருப்புத் தலைநெடுவேற் கண்ணார் கண்ணீர் நிழன்மணிப்பூட்","பரப்பி னிடைப்பாய்ந்து குளமாய்ப் பாலார் படாமுலையை","வருத்தி மணிநெடுங்கோட் டருவி போல வீழ்ந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The tears of the spear-eyed women, whose spear-like looks fed on flesh and killed kings, fall across the jewelled chains and gather in a pool, then pour down like a waterfall on their breasts.","n":"The simile of the waterfall is the narrator's; the opening 'ate flesh and killed kings' refers to the spear, not to the women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2944","lb":"CC2944","mm":1,"ev":"The wives weep until their tears flood their jewelled chests.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2945":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2945,"t":["அழலேந்து வெங்கடுஞ்சொ லுருமே றுண்டாங் கலர்சிந்தி","நிழலேந்து பூங்கொடிக ணிலஞ்சேர்ந் தாங்கு நிலஞ்சேர்ந்து","கழலேந்து சேவடிக்கீழ்க் கண்ணீர் வெள்ளங் கலநிரப்பக்","குழலேங்கு மாறேங்கி யழுதார் கோதை மடவாரே."],"p":"","r":"Like flowers fallen on the ground after a thunderbolt of harsh words, the garlanded women fall at the feet, fill a vessel with their tears and cry as their hair sways.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2945","lb":"CC2945","mm":1,"ev":"The wives fall at Civakan's feet and weep.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2946":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2946,"t":["குலிகவஞ் சேற்று ணாறிக் குங்கும நீரு ளோங்கிப்","பொலிகென வண்டு பாடப் பூத்ததா மரைகள் போலு","மொலிகழ லடிக ணுங்ககீழ்ப் பிழைத்ததென் னுரைமி னென்னப்","புலிநிழற் பட்ட மான்போற் போழுயி ராகி நின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"They stand like a deer in the shadow of a tiger, their lives split, and ask what fault they committed under his feet, whose feet are like lotuses growing in red paste.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2946","lb":"CC2946","mm":1,"ev":"The wives ask what wrong they did, and stand in terror.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2947":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2947,"t":["அருந்தவி சாகியெம்மைச் சுமந்தயா வுயிர்த்த வாண்மைப்","பெருந்தகு குறங்கு காணீர் பெண்ணுயி ரளிய தாமே","வருந்துமா லென்று நோக்கீர் வாடுமா லாவி யென்னீர்","விருந்தினர்போல நின்றீர் வெற்றுடல் காண்மி னென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The women say: see the thighs that bore us as a rare couch; do not think the women's lives will be pitiful and pained; look at the empty bodies when you stand like guests.","n":"The Tamil of lines 1-3 is dense; the sense of an appeal against the king's coldness is read from the whole.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","yaakkai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2947","lb":"CC2947","mm":1,"ev":"The wives appeal to Civakan to look at what is left of them as he stands like a guest.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2948":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2948,"t":["கோதையுந் துகிலு மேந்திக் குங்கும மெழுதிக் கொய்பூந்","தாதுகொண் டளகத் தப்பித் தடமுலை வருடிச் சேர்ந்து","காதல்கொண் டிருந்த காமர் கைவிர லளிய நீரு","மேதில லாகிக் கோமா னெண்ணமே யெண்ணி னீரே."],"p":"","r":"Holding garland and cloth, applying kunkumam, putting pollen in their hair and stroking their breasts, those lovers' fingers are pitiable; you too became strangers and thought only of the king's mind.","n":"The speaker and the addressee are doubtful: the verse may be the wives' own reproach or the narrator's comment on them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2948","lb":"CC2948","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மனைவியர்",""],"ch":"cc:125"},"cc:2949":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2949,"t":["பஞ்சிகொண் டெழுதி யார்ந்த சீறடி பனித்த லஞ்சிக்","குஞ்சிமே லேற்ற கோமான் கொப்புளித் திட்ட வெம்மை","வஞ்சித்தீர் மணிசெய் தோள்காள் வாங்குபு தழுவிக் கொள்ளீர்","நெஞ்சநீர் வலியீ ராகி நிற்பிரோ நீரு மென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The wives, speaking to the jewelled shoulders of their lord, recall the favour he showed their painted feet and ask whether the shoulders will now embrace them or stand hard of heart.","n":"They address his body parts, not him; the lament runs through shoulders, chest, tongue, eyes and crown in the verses that follow. The sense of the second line is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2949","lb":"CC2949","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan's wives reproach his shoulders as he prepares to leave them.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2950":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2950,"t":["முட்டுவட் டனைய கோல முத்துலாய்க் கிடந்து மின்ன","மட்டுவிட் டலர்ந்த கோதை மதுவொடு மயங்கி நாளு","மொட்டியிட் டுறைய வெங்கட் குயரணை யாய மார்ப","நட்புவிட் டொழியு மாயி னன்மையார் கண்ண தம்மா."],"p":"","r":"Speaking to the chest where pearls and wreaths lay, the wives say that if the old friendship is dropped, that chest is no longer the refuge it was.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2950","lb":"CC2950","mm":1,"ev":"The wives reproach his chest.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2951":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2951,"t":["மாக்கவின் வளரத் தீண்டி மணிநகை நக்கு நாளும்","பூக்கவி னார்ந்த பைந்தார் புனைமதுத் தேனொ டேந்தித்","தாக்கியெம் முலைக டம்மை நெருங்கினாய் தரணி மன்னி","னீக்கிநீ யெம்மை நோக்காய் நீத்தியோ நீயு மென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The wives remind the chest that it pressed their breasts in love and ask whether he will now cast them off and not look at them once he rules the earth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2951","lb":"CC2951","mm":1,"ev":"The wives accuse him of leaving them for his rule.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2952":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2952,"t":["அன்னமே தோகை நல்யா ழமுதமே யாய்ந்த தீந்தே","னின்னரே நங்கை மாரென் றேத்திய பவளச் செந்நா","வென்னைநீ கண்ட தெம்மை யிரண்டுநா வாயி னாயே","மன்னன்போ லீர மின்றி வலித்தனை வாழி யென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The wives say the tongue that praised them as swan, peacock, lute and nectar has shown two tongues, and call him, like a king, pitiless and hard.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","inchol","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2952","lb":"CC2952","mm":1,"ev":"The wives reproach his tongue for its broken praise.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2953":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2953,"t":["பூணினா னெருங்க நொந்து பொதிர்த்தன வெம்பி யென்று","நாணினால் வருத்தந் தீர்ப்பா னன்முலைக் கண்க டம்மைப்","பேணிநீ ரெழுதி யோம்பிப் பேரின்பங் கொண்டு தந்தீர்","காண்மினோ வின்றெம் வண்ணங் கண்ணிலீர் கண்க ளென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The wives tell his eyes that they cared for their breasts and gave them great joy, and now, eyeless, do not see the state they are in.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2953","lb":"CC2953","mm":1,"ev":"The wives reproach his eyes.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2954":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2954,"t":["சென்னிமேன் மிதித்த வஞ்செஞ் சீறடித் திருவில் வீச","மின்னிவா ளாரஞ் சிந்த வெறுநிலத் துறைந்து நீயெ","மின்னகை முறுவல் பார்த்தா யின்றெம தாவி பார்த்தாய்","மன்னிய மாலை வண்டார் மணிமுடி வாழி யென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"The wives tell the jewelled crown that it looked on their smiles and now looks on the loss of their lives, and bid it 'live long' in bitter irony.","n":"The 'long life' is an irony; the crown that once took their feet now sees them destroyed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2954","lb":"CC2954","mm":1,"ev":"The wives reproach his crown with bitter blessing.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2955":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2955,"t":["வீங்குபாற் கடலு நஞ்சாய் விளைந்ததால் விரிந்த வெய்யோன்","பாங்கிலா விருளை யீன்று பார்மறைத் திட்ட தாலோ","தீங்கதிர்த் திங்கள் செந்தீச் சொரிந்ததாற் றிசைக ளெல்லாந்","தாங்குமா றென்னை யாவி தரிக்கிலேந் தேவிர் காளோ."],"p":"","r":"The wives cry that the sea of milk has turned to poison, the sun has borne darkness and the moon pours fire, and ask how they can hold their lives, calling on the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","pirivu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2955","lb":"CC2955","mm":1,"ev":"The wives cry out that the world has turned against them.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2956":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2956,"t":["விண்ணோர் மடமகள்கொல் விஞ்சைமக ளேகொல்","கண்ணார் கழிவனப்பிற் காந்தருவ தத்தையென்","றெண்ணாய வாணெடுங்கண் மெய்கொள்ள வேமுற்றுப்","பண்ணாற்பயின்றீ ரினியென் பயில்வீரே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks whether the woman of the long eyes, named Gandharvadattai, is a heavenly maid or a Vidyadhara girl, and asks who will now take up the music she knew.","n":"The addressees and speaker are not fixed by the Tamil; the verse may be a wife's plea about Gandharvadattai or Gandharvadattai's own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kalvi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2956","lb":"CC2956","mm":1,"ev":"One wife turns her lament to Gandharvadattai and the music they shared.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2957":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2957,"t":["கொல்வே னெடுங்கட் குணமாலை குஞ்சரத்தா","லல்லனோ யுற்றாளுக் கன்று களிறடர்த்துப்","புல்லிப் புணர்முலையின் பூங்குவட்டின் மேலுறைந்தா","யெல்லேமற் றெம்பெருமாற் கின்றிவளு மின்னாளோ."],"p":"","r":"A wife promises to kill the long-eyed Gunamalai, who was caught by the elephant and was rescued when he killed it and embraced her, and asks whether she too is of that kind now.","n":"The verse reminds him of the rescue to show what he owes; the threat to kill is more likely a cry of despair than a plan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2957","lb":"CC2957","mm":1,"ev":"A wife recalls the elephant's attack on Gunamalai and his rescue.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2958":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2958,"t":["தூம்புடைய வெள்ளெயிற்றுத் துத்தியழ னாகப்","பாம்புடைய நோக்கிப் பதுமை பவழவாய்","தேம்புடைய வின்னமுதாச் சேர்ந்தாய்க் கினியதுவே","யாம்புடைய நஞ்சடங்கிற் றின்றூறிற் றாகாதே."],"p":"","r":"A wife recalls Padumai, whom the snake bit and who revived when he took her coral mouth as nectar, and asks whether the venom has now come back on them.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2958","lb":"CC2958","mm":1,"ev":"A wife recalls the cure of Padumai.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2959":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2959,"t":["தாழ்ந்துலவி மென்முலைமேற் றண்ணாரம் வில்விலங்கப்","போழ்ந்தகன்ற கண்ணினா லேப்பெற்றுப் போகலாய்","தாழ்ந்தமர ரின்னமிர்தந் தக்கநாட் டாகாதே","வீழ்ந்ததென வீழ்ந்தாய்நீ யின்றதுவும் விட்டாயோ."],"p":"","r":"A wife reminds him that her eyes pierced his chest through the sandal paste and asks whether he will let that joy, once gained, go.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2959","lb":"CC2959","mm":1,"ev":"A wife recalls their first love and asks him not to leave it.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2960":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2960,"t":["கண்ணோ கயலோ கழுநீரோ காவியோ","பெண்ணோ வமுதோ பிணையோ வெனப்பிதற்றித்","துண்ணென் சிலைத்தொழிலுங் காட்டிமுன் னின்புற்றீர்","புண்மேற் கிழிபோற் றுறத்தல் பொருளாமோ."],"p":"","r":"A wife says that he who raved over their eyes, lips and bodies, and showed his bow, now gives them up like a rag laid on a wound, and asks if renouncing is worth anything.","n":"Here the wives put renunciation as a question of what is fitting; the Naladiyar and Kural praise it as the best course.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2960","lb":"CC2960","mm":1,"ev":"A wife questions the worth of his renunciation.","cl":[["renouncing those who love is not a thing of worth","general","reported",4,"civakan_wives"]],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2961":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2961,"t":["பொன்னகர வீதி புகுந்தீர் பொழிமுகிலின்","மின்னி னிடைநுடங்க நின்றாடன் வேனெடுங்கண்","மன்ன னகரெல்லாம் போர்ப்ப வலைப்பட்டீர்க்","கின்னே யொளியிழந்த வின்னா விடுகினவோ."],"p":"","r":"A wife says that those who entered the golden city's street and were caught in the net of a dancing woman's long eyes now find that the light has gone and ask whether the wreath is a pain.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2961","lb":"CC2961","mm":1,"ev":"A wife recalls how he was caught in love's net.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2962":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2962,"t":["செங்கச் சிளமுலையார் திண்கறையூர் பல்லினார்","மங்கையர்கள் காப்ப மகிழ்ந்தாளை நீமகிழ்ந்து","பங்கயமே போல்வாளைப் பார்ப்பானாய்ப் பண்ணணைத்துத்","தங்கினாய் கோவே துறத்த றகவாமோ."],"p":"","r":"A wife says that he who delighted in the one like a lotus, guarded by women, and joined her as a brahmin with music, is wrong to give them up, and asks if renouncing is fitting.","n":"It recalls the episode in which Civakan came as a brahmin; the wife it names is not fixed by the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2962","lb":"CC2962","mm":1,"ev":"A wife recalls his stay as a brahmin and asks if renouncing is fitting.","cl":[["a husband who took a wife's love ought not to forsake her","general","reported",4,"civakan_wives"]],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2963":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2963,"t":["புல்லா ருயிர்செகுத்த பொன்னந் திணிதோளாய்","மல்லா ரகன்மார்ப மட்டேந்தி வாய்மடுத்திட்","டெல்லாருங் காண விலக்கணையோ டாடினா","யல்லாந் தவணடுங்க வன்பி னகல்வாயோ."],"p":"","r":"A wife says that he, strong-shouldered and the killer of enemies, drank and danced with Lakshmanai before all, and asks whether he will leave in affection while others tremble.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2963","lb":"CC2963","mm":1,"ev":"A wife recalls how Civakan danced with Lakshmanai.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2964":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2964,"t":["கல்லோ மரனு மிரங்கக் கலுழ்ந்துருகி","யெல்லாத் திசைதோறு மீண்டி யினமயிர்போற்","சொல்லாத் துயர்வார் தொழுவா ரழுவாரா","யல்லாந் தகன்கோயி லாழ்கடல்போ லாயிற்றே."],"p":"","r":"The palace, with sobbing women worshipping him and weeping in every direction, becomes like a deep sea, as even stones and trees melt.","n":"The verse moves from the wives' own words to the narrator's sight of the whole palace in grief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2964","lb":"CC2964","mm":1,"ev":"The women of the palace weep and worship him as he prepares to go.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2965":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2965,"t":["பூப்பரிவார் பொன்செய் கலம்பரிவார் பொன்வளையை","நீப்பி ரெனப்புடைப்பார் நீடாமஞ் சிந்துவா","ரேப்பெற்ற மான்பிணைபோ லேங்குவா ரின்னுயிரைக்","காப்பரேற் காவலனார் காவாரோ வின்றேன்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Some women beat their golden bangles, scatter garlands and tremble like caught does, asking whether the king will guard their lives or leave them unguarded.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","pirivu","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2965","lb":"CC2965","mm":1,"ev":"The women strip off ornaments and weep as the king departs.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2966":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2966,"t":["கழுநீருந் தாமரையுங் கண்டனவே போலு","முழுநீர்வேற் கண்ணும் முகமு முலறிச்","செழுநீர் மணிக்கொடிகள் காழகஞ் சேர்கொம்பா","யழுநீர வாயலறி யல்லாப்ப போன்றாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women, weeping with faces like water-lilies, are like jewelled flags on a mast that tremble and cry.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2966","lb":"CC2966","mm":1,"ev":"The women cry out in grief.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2967":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2967,"t":["பண்ணார் பணைமுழவம் பாடவிந்து பன்மணியாழ் மழலை நீங்கிப்","புண்ணார் புனைகுழலு மேங்கா புனைபாண்டி லிரங்கா வான்பூங்","கண்ணா ரொலிகவுள கிண்கிணியு மஞ்சிலம்புங் கலையு மாரா","மண்ணார் வலம்புரியும் வாய்மடங்கிக் கோன்கோயின் மடிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The drums, the lutes, the flutes and the tinkling anklets all fall silent, and the king's palace is as if dead.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","nilaiyaamai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2967","lb":"CC2967","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2968":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2968,"t":["அணியார் மணியரக்கு வட்டழுத்தி வைத்தனைய செங்கண் மாத்தாட்","பிணியார் பெருந்துருத்தி யன்ன பெருங்கடவுள பிறையேர் கோட்ட","பணியார் கமழ்கடாத் தண்ண லரசுவாப் பண்ணார் பாய்மா","விணையாது மில்லாத கண்ணீர்வீழ்த் துண்ணாநின் றினைந்த தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Great tuskers, war-horses and the rest stand silent and shed tears, taking no food, in the palace of the departing king.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","arul","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2968","lb":"CC2968","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2969":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2969,"t":["கழித்த கடிப்பிணையுங் கைவளையு மாலையுங் களைந்து முத்துந்","தொழித்த நறுஞ்சாந்துஞ் சுண்ணமும் பன்மணியுங் கலனுஞ் சிந்தி","விழித்து வியன்கோயில் பன்மீன் பரந்திமைக்கும் பனியார் வானம்","பழித்துப் பசும்பொன் னுலகு குடிபோயிற் றொத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Women, having cast off earrings, bangles, wreaths, sandal and jewels, make the palace look like a sky robbed of stars, as if the golden world had moved out.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","pirivu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2969","lb":"CC2969","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2970":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2970,"t":["அழலார் சுரையெயிற்று வெஞ்சினவைந் தலைசுமந்த வெகுளி நாக","நிழலார் திருமணியுந் தேவர் திருமுடிமே னிலவி வீசுஞ்","சுழலார் பசும்பொன்னும் வேய்ந்து சொரிகதிர்மென் பஞ்சி யார்ந்த","கழலா னகர மமுது கடைகடல்போற் கலங்கிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king's city, rich in jewels and gold, is stirred like the sea churned for nectar.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","nilaiyaamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2970","lb":"CC2970","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2971":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2971,"t":["நீர்நிறை குளத்து மாரி சொரிந்தென நறுநெய் துள்ளு","நேர்நிறை பொரியுங் குய்யும் வறைகளு நிவந்த வாசம்","பார்நிறை யடிசிற் பள்ளி தளியொடு சாலை யெல்லா","மூர்நிறை யுயிர்த்த லின்றி யுயிர்சென்ற போன்ற வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The kitchens, almshouses and halls, once rich in ghee, rice and savoury, now stand as if every living breath had gone from the town.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","nilaiyaamai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2971","lb":"CC2971","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2972":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2972,"t":["கோட்புலிச் சுழல்கண் ணன்ன கொழுஞ்சுவைக் கருனை முல்லை","மோட்டிள முகையின் மொய்கொள் கொக்குகிர் நிமிரல் வெண்சோ","றூட்டுறு கறிகொ டேமாங் கனிசுவைத் தயிரோ டேந்தி","வேட்டவர்ப் பெறாது வீதி வெறுநிலங் கிடந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The costly rice, the sweet dishes and the curd lie wasted on the empty street, since those who wanted them are not there.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","nilaiyaamai","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2972","lb":"CC2972","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2973":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2973,"t":["மைந்தர்தம் வண்கை யான்முன் மணிவள்ளத் தெடுத்த தேறல்","பைந்துகின் மகளிர் மேவார் பாசிழை பசும்பொன் மாலை","சிந்தியே கரந்தார் சொற்போன் மெய்யின்கட் சேர்த லின்றாய்ச்","சந்தனச் சாந்தொ டாரந் தாங்கவி னிழந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The cup of wine in the youth's hand is untouched, the maids leave their jewels and garlands, and sandal and pearls lose their beauty.","n":"","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","nilaiyaamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2973","lb":"CC2973","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:126"},"cc:2974":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2974,"t":["தாழிவாய் மறைக்குந் தண்ணென் றடம்பெருங் குவளைக் கண்ணார்","மூழிவாய் முல்லை மாலை முலைமுக முரிந்து நக்க","யாழின்வாய் முழவம் விம்ம வாட்டொழிந் தயர்ந்து தீந்தே","னூழிவாய்க் கொண்ட தொக்கும் பாடலு மொழிந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"In the palace the lute, the drum and the women's song fall silent, the women's jasmine garlands crushed at their breasts, and the singing that was like sweet honey is gone.","n":"The verse gives no cause for the silence; the following verses supply it, the king's coming departure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2974","lb":"CC2974","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2975":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2975,"t":["அருங்கல நிறைந்த வம்பூம் பவளக்கா றிகழம் பைம்பொற்","பெருங்கிடு கென்னுங் கோலப் பேரிமை பொருந்தி மெல்ல","வொருங்குட னகர மெல்லா முரங்குவ தொத்த தொல்லென்","கருட்கடல் கல்லென் சும்மை கரந்தது மொத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The whole city falls hushed as if asleep, and the roar like a dark sea that filled it is hidden.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2975","lb":"CC2975","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2976":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2976,"t":["கலையுலாய் நிமிர்ந்த வல்குற் கடல்விளை யமுத மன்னார்","முலையுலாய் நிமிர்ந்த மொய்தார் முழவுமுத் துரிஞ்சி மின்னச்","சிலையுலாய் நிமிர்ந்த மார்பன் றிருநகர் தெருள்க லாதாய்","நிலையிலா வுலகின் றன்மை நீர்மைமீக் கூறிற் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The palace of the bowman-chested king, with its women and its drums, can teach what the unsteady world is like to anyone who has not yet understood it.","n":"The line 3 phrase தெருள்கலாதாய் is read here as 'to one who has not yet understood'; the verse turns the palace's splendour into a lesson.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2976","lb":"CC2976","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the world is unstable and its pleasures do not stay","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2977":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2977,"t":["கூந்த லகிற்புகையும் வேள்விக் கொழும்புகையு","மேந்து துகிற்புகையு மாலைக் கிடும்புகையு","மாய்ந்த பொருளொருவர்க் கீயா வதிலோப","மாந்தர் புகழேபோற் றோன்றா மறைந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The smoke of hair-incense, of oblations, of lifted cloth and of garlands is gone, vanished like the fame of misers who give nothing of what they have gathered.","n":"வேள்விக் கொழும்புகை may be oblation smoke of a household rite; the verse does not name a school for it and uses it only as one of the things that vanish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2977","lb":"CC2977","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who never give what they have gathered leave no fame behind","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2978":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2978,"t":["புல்லுண் புரவி புலம்பு விடுகுரல்போ","னல்லவளை போழரவ நாரை நரல்குரல்போற்","கல்லா விளையர் கலங்காச் சிரிப்பொலியுங்","கொல்யானைச் சங்கொலியுங் கூடா தொழிந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The neighing of the grass-eating horses, the cries of the conch-cutters and the herons, the careless laughter of young boys and the trumpet of the killer elephant and the conch all cease.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2978","lb":"CC2978","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2979":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2979,"t":["பொற்புடை பூமாலை சாந்தம் புனைகலன்கள்","கற்புடைய மங்கையரிற் காவ லவையிழந்த","நற்புடைய தேனார் நறவு நயம்புல்லார்","சொற்பொருள்போல் வேட்கப் படாசோர்ந் தொழிந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Flowers, garlands, sandal and ornaments, and the drink of honey that the women guarded, lose their hold and are no longer wanted, as the words of a text lose their sense for those who do not value it.","n":"Line 4's simile (சொற்பொருள்போல்) is hard: the sense may be that things are dropped like words of no concern; the reading here is a best guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2979","lb":"CC2979","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2980":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2980,"t":["தீம்பால் கிளிமறந்து தேவ ரவிமடங்கித்","தூம்பார் நெடுங்கைம்மாத் தீங்கரும்பு துற்றாவா","யாம்பா லுரைமடங்கி யாரும் பிறர்பிறராய்க்","காம்பார் நடுவிருட்கட் காடேபோ லாயிற்றே."],"p":"","r":"The parrot forgets the sweet milk, the gods' offerings stop, the elephants no longer eat sugarcane, speech halts and all become strangers, and the city turns to a forest in the dark of night.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2980","lb":"CC2980","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2981":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2981,"t":["நீர்முழங்கு நீல நெடுமேக மால்யானைத்","தேர்முழங்கு தானைத் திருமாலின் முன்றுறப்பான்","பார்முழங்கு தெண்டிரைபோற் செல்வந்தம் பாலர்க்கிந்","தூர்முழுது நாடு முரவோன்றாள் சேர்ந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"As the king prepares to renounce before the great lord, the wealth of the land that rolled like the roaring sea passes to the young ones and the city and country come to the feet of the strong one.","n":"Who is 'the great lord' (திருமால்) and who 'the strong one' (உரவோன்) is not fixed in the Tamil; the reading takes the first as Civakan and the second as his heir. The sons are not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2981","lb":"CC2981","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2982":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2982,"t":["கொல்லுலைப் பொங்கழற் கிடந்த கூரிலை","மல்லல்வே லிரண்டொரு மதியுள் வைத்தபோற்","செல்லநீண் டகன்றகஞ் சிவந்த கண்ணினா","ரல்லலுற் றழுபவர்க் கரசன் சொல்லினான்."],"p":"","r":"With eyes red and long like spearheads set in the forge's flame, the women weep in grief, and the king speaks to them.","n":"The last line introduces the speech that fills the next verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2982","lb":"CC2982","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan speaks to the weeping wives, beginning his teaching to them.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2983":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2983,"t":["நற்றவம் பரவை ஞால நாமுட னிறுப்பின் வைய","மற்றமி றவத்திற் கென்று மையவி யனைத்து மாற்றா","திற்றென வுணர்ந்து நிற்பிற் றிருமக ளென்று நீங்காள்","பற்றொடே நிற்பி னென்றுந் திருமகள் பற்றல் செல்லாள்."],"p":"","r":"The king tells them that if the whole earth and sea were set against good penance they would not equal it, and that Fortune, who never leaves the one who knows that even a world of wealth is short of that and so lets go, will never reach the one who holds on with attachment.","n":"The Tamil of lines 1 to 4 is dense and the logic of 'never leaves' against 'never clings' reads as a paradox: wealth comes to the unattached. In the first ilampakam Fortune had left the clinging king (232).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","selvam","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2983","lb":"CC2983","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan teaches the wives the worth of penance over wealth.","cl":[["good penance outweighs all the wealth of the world","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"],["Fortune stays with the one who lets go and never clings to the one who holds on","general","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2984":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2984,"t":["உப்பிலிப் புழுக்கல் காட்டுட் புலைமக னுகுப்ப வேகக்","கைப்பலி யுண்டி யானும் வெள்ளின்மேற் கவிழ நீரு","மைப்பொலி கண்ணி னீரான் மனையக மெழுகி வாழ","விப்பொருள் வேண்டு கின்றீ ரிதனைநீர் கேண்மி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan asks if they want wealth that ends with unsalted rice poured by an outcaste in the burning ground, a rice-ball overturned on the bier and a wife smearing the house with her eye-black tears, and bids them listen.","n":"வெள்ளின் in line 2 is read as the bier-frame; the picture of the funeral is pieced together from rare words and cannot be fixed exactly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2984","lb":"CC2984","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan sets before the wives the funeral to which wealth leads and asks them to hear him.","cl":[["wealth ends at the burning ground with a funeral and a widow's tears","general","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2985":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2985,"t":["கொல்சின யானை பார்க்குங் கூருகிர்த் தறுக ணாளி","யில்லெலி பார்த்து நோக்கி யிறப்பின்கீ ழிருந்த லுண்டே","பல்வினை வெள்ள நீந்திப் பகாவின்பம் பருகி னல்லா","னல்வினை விளையு ளென்னு நஞ்சினுட் குளித்த லுண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says that even the lion that looks at the elephant may at the end sit beneath death, and that unless one crosses the flood of many deeds and drinks undivided bliss, one is bathing in the poison called the fruit of good deeds.","n":"The Jain view that merit as well as sin binds is stated strongly: நல்வினை விளையுள் is poison. Compare the sermon at 2846 where release is freedom from deeds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2985","lb":"CC2985","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan teaches that even good deeds have a fruit that binds.","cl":[["the fruit of good deeds is itself poison; undivided bliss comes only by crossing the flood of deeds","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2986":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2986,"t":["ஆற்றிய மக்க ளென்னு மருந்தவ மிலார்க ளாகிற்","போற்றிய மணியும் பொன்னும் பின்செலா பொன்ன னீரே","வேற்றுவ ரென்று நில்லா விழுப்பொருள் பரவை ஞால","நோற்பவர்க் குரிய வாகு நோன்மின் நீரு மேன்றான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan says that if their children lack the great penance the guarded gems and gold will not follow them, and that the wealth of the sea-girt earth belongs to those who do penance, so they should do it.","n":"Line 1 is hard: ஆற்றிய மக்கள் என்னும் அருந்தவம் is read as 'the great penance called children'; the sense may be otherwise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","selvam","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2986","lb":"CC2986","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan urges the golden-adorned wives to take up penance.","cl":[["gems and gold do not follow the dead","general","asserted",2,"civakan"],["the wealth of the earth is for those who do penance","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2987":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2987,"t":["காதலஞ் சேற்றுட் பாய்ந்த மதியெனுங் கலங்க னீரை","யூதுவண் டுடுத்த தாரா னுவர்ப்பினி னுரிஞ்சித் தேற்ற","மாதரார் நெஞ்சந் தேறி மாதவஞ் செய்து மென்றார்","காதலான் காத லென்னு நிகளத்தா னெடுங்க ணாரே."],"p":"","r":"The long-eyed women's hearts, muddied by the clay of love, are cleared when the garlanded king stirs and settles them with disgust, and they say they will do great penance, though still held by the fetter of love for him.","n":"The verse's own image is of muddy water cleared by a stirrer; love for the husband is named the fetter that still binds the women as they decide.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","thuravu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2987","lb":"CC2987","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan's teaching clears the wives' minds and they decide to take up penance.","cl":[["disgust for pleasure clears a mind muddied by love","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2988":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2988,"t":["தூமஞ்சால் கோதை யீரே தொல்வினை நீத்த நீந்தி","நாமஞ்சால் கதியி னீங்கி நன்பொன்மே லுலகி னுச்சி","யேமஞ்சா லின்பம் வேண்டி னென்னோடும் வம்மி னென்றான்","காமஞ்சாய்த் தடர்த்து வென்ற காஞ்சனக் குன்ற மன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Civakan calls the wives to cross the old deeds, leave the famed rebirth and come with him if they want the secure bliss at the top of the golden world above.","n":"The summit of the world above is the Jain siddha abode; the verse says nothing of a god who grants it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2988","lb":"CC2988","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan invites the wives to renounce with him and reach release.","cl":[["crossing old deeds leads to bliss at the top of the world above","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2989":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2989,"t":["நாடக நயந்து காண்பார் நலங்கிளர் கண்கள் சூன்று","மாடகக் கலத்து ளான்பா லமிர்தினை நயந்துண் பாரை","நீடகம் வெகுண்டுங் கையாற் பிடித்துநீ றட்டி யிட்டேங்","கோடக மணிந்த கோல முடியினாய் துறத்து மென்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The wives answer that to cling to pleasure now would be like gouging out one's eyes to watch a dance or throwing nectar from a jewelled vessel into the ash, and tell the crowned one that they will renounce.","n":"Lines 1 to 3 are hard: the images of gouged eyes and ash-covered nectar are read as the wives' own comparison, but the Tamil is unclear on who does these things.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2989","lb":"CC2989","mm":1,"ev":"The wives accept Civakan's call and declare they will renounce.","cl":[["clinging to pleasure destroys the very thing pleasure is sought for","general","reported",3,"civakan_manaiviyar"]],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2990":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2990,"t":["சாந்தங் கிழிய முயங்கித் தடமலராற்","கூந்தல் வழிபட்ட கோவேநீ செல்லுலகில்","வாய்ந்தடியேம் வந்துன் வழிபடுநா ளின்றேபோற்","காய்ந்தருளல் கண்டா யெனத்தொழுதார் காரிகையார்."],"p":"","r":"The wives bow to the king whose hair they used to serve with flowers and ask him not to be angry when they come in the world he reaches to serve him as today.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","panivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2990","lb":"CC2990","mm":1,"ev":"The wives bow to Civakan and ask to serve him in the world he goes to.","cl":[["a wife may follow and serve her husband in the world beyond","general","reported",3,"civakan_manaiviyar"]],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மனைவியர்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2991":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2991,"t":["தெண்டிரை நீத்த நீந்தித் தீங்கதிர் சுமந்து திங்கள்","விண்படர்ந் தனைய மாலை வெண்குடை வேந்தர் வேந்தன்","கண்டிரண் முத்த மாலைக் கதிர்முலை நங்கை மாரை","வெண்டிரை வியக்குங் கேள்வி விசயைகண் ணபயம் வைத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of kings, having crossed the clear flood of the sea of learning, sees the pearl-garlanded women and entrusts them to the care of Vijayai, wise in lore.","n":"'Care' is read from அபயம் வைத்தான், which can also mean he gave them a promise of safety; the verse does not state that they join her order, though the story elsewhere has her renounce.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","anbu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2991","lb":"CC2991","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan places his wives under his mother Vijayai's protection.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2992":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2992,"t":["கடிமலர் நிறைந்து பூத்த கற்பகக் கொம்புங் காமர்","வடிமலர் மலர்ந்த காம வல்லியுந் தம்மைத் தாமே","யுடைமலர் கொய்து போக வுகுத்திடு கின்ற தொத்தார்","படைமலர் நெடுங்க ணல்லார் பாசிழை நீக்கு கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The wives lay aside their jewelled ornaments, like the wishing-tree branch and the vine of desire shedding the flowers that they themselves strip.","n":"The vine of desire (காமவல்லி) is the image: what had been the plant of desire is shown shedding its own flowers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2992","lb":"CC2992","mm":1,"ev":"The wives remove their ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2993":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2993,"t":["தழுமலர்த் தாம நான்று சந்தகின் மணந்து விம்முஞ்","செழுமணி நிலத்துச் செம்பொற் றிருமுத்த விதான நீழ","லெழுமையும் பெறுக வென்னு மெழின்முலை நெற்றி சூழ்ந்தார்","கழுமிய துகிலிற் காமன் கண்புடைத் திரங்க மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Under the pearl canopy on the gem floor the garlanded women, wishing that this may be theirs for seven births, wrap their breasts and brows in cloth while the god of love is said to beat his eyes and grieve.","n":"The last two lines are obscure and the reading is tentative: it may be the women wishing 'may we have this for seven births' or the jewels falling away.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2993","lb":"CC2993","mm":1,"ev":"The wives put on the renunciant's cloth as the god of love mourns.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2994":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2994,"t":["நறும்புகை நான நாவிக் குழம்பொடு பளிதச் சுண்ண","மறிந்தவ ராய்ந்த மாலை யணிந்தபைங் கூந்த லாய்பொ","னிறந்தரு கொம்பு நீலக் கதிர்க்கற்றை யுமிழ்வ வேபோற்","செறிந்திருந் துகுத்துச் செம்பொற் குணக்கொடி யாயி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"With perfume smoke, musk and powders put aside and the fragrant garlands of their hair taken off, the women become golden vines of virtue.","n":"The vine image (குணக்கொடி) turns the women into plants of virtue as they strip themselves of the scents of desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2994","lb":"CC2994","mm":1,"ev":"The wives strip off their perfumes and garlands and become renunciants.","cl":[],"ag":["மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2995":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2995,"t":["இலம்பெரி தெனவிரந் தவர்கட் கேந்திய","கலஞ்சொரி காவலன் கடகக் கையிணை","புலம்பிரிந் துயர்ந்தன விரண்டு பொன்னிற","வலம்புரி மணிசொரி கின்ற போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The king's bangled hands pour out the gifts to those who ask because they are in great want, as if two gold conches were pouring gems.","n":"The giving here is the last act before renunciation, with nothing to be had in return for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2995","lb":"CC2995","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan gives gifts to beggars before he leaves.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2996":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2996,"t":["என்பரிந் தெரிதலைக் கொள்ள வீண்டிய","வன்பரிந் திடுகலா வுலக மார்கென","மின்சொரி வெண்கலம் வீசும் வண்கைகள்","பொன்சொரி தாமரைப் போது போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"His generous hands pour out white vessels like gold-showering lotuses, so that the world may eat its fill.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are obscure (என்பரிந்து எரிதலை): the reading takes them as the plea of the world in hunger.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2996","lb":"CC2996","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan gives out vessels of food and gold to all the world.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2997":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2997,"t":["பூந்துகில் புனைகல மாலை பூசுசாந்","தாய்ந்துல குணவுவந் தருளி மாமணி","காந்திய கற்பகக் கான மாயினா","னேந்திய மணிமுடி யிறைவ னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The king gives away the fine clothes, ornaments, garlands and sandal that the world enjoys with delight and becomes a grove of wishing trees that rain gems.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2997","lb":"CC2997","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan gives his clothes, ornaments and garlands to the world.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2998":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2998,"t":["தேய்பிறை யுருவக் கேணித் தேறுநீர் மலர்ந்த தேனா","ராய்நிறக் குவளை யஞ்சிக் குறுவிழிக் கொள்ளும் வாட்கண்","வேய்நிறை யழித்த மென்றோள் விசயையைத் தொழுது வாழ்த்திச்","சேய்நிறச் சிவிகை சேர்ந்தான் றேவர்கொண் டேகி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan worships and blesses Vijayai, whose tender arms put the bamboo to shame, and boards the red palanquin that the gods then lift and carry away.","n":"The palanquin that had carried the mothers to renunciation (2628) now carries the son, and the gods, not men, bear it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","veedu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2998","lb":"CC2998","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan bows to his mother, boards the palanquin and is carried off by the gods.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","வெய்யர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:127"},"cc:2999":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":2999,"t":["நரம்பெழுந் திரங்கின வீணை நன்குழல்","பரந்துபண் ணுயிர்த்தன பைய மெல்லவே","விருந்துபட் டியம்பின முழவம் வீங்கொலி","சுரந்தன சுடர்மணிப் பாண்டி லென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The lutes and flutes sound softly, the drums answer in a guest's welcome, and the bright gem-set instruments are said to pour out their sound as the festival begins.","n":"The last line reports the sound as hearsay (என்பவே), a poet's formula for scenes he did not see.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 2999","lb":"CC2999","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3000":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3000,"t":["மங்குலா யகிற்புகை மணந்து கற்பகப்","பொங்குபூ மாலைகள் பொலிந்து பூஞ்சுணந்","தங்கியித் தரணியும் விசும்புந் தாமரோ","செங்கதிர்த் திருமணிச் செப்புப் போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Aloe smoke, wish-tree garlands and heaped flowers make earth and sky look like a red-gemmed casket.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3000","lb":"CC3000","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3001":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3001,"t":["திலக முக்குடைச் செல்வன் றிருநகர்","பலரு மேத்தினர் பாடின ராடினர்","குலவு பல்லியங் கூடிக் குழுமிநின்","றுலக வெள்ள மொலிப்பது போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"Many praise, sing and dance at the city of the lord of the three umbrellas, and the massed instruments sound like a flood of the world roaring.","n":"The 'lord of the three umbrellas' (முக்குடைச் செல்வன்) is the Jina, whose city is the assembly Civakan is approaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3001","lb":"CC3001","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3002":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3002,"t":["கானி ரைத்தன காவொடு பூம்பொய்கை","தேனி ரைத்தன செம்பொ னெடுமதின்","மேனி ரைத்தன் வெண்கொடி யக்கொடி","வானு ரிப்பன போன்று மணந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Groves, lotus tanks, bee-filled gardens, tall golden walls and white banners are said to spread as if they had joined the sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3002","lb":"CC3002","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3003":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3003,"t":["கோல முற்றிய கோடுயர் தூபையுஞ்","சூல நெற்றிய கோபுரத் தோற்றமு","ஞால முற்றிய பொன்வரை நன்றரோ","கால முற்றுடன் கண்ணுற்ற போன்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The raised pinnacles, the trident-topped gate-towers and the golden hill-like heights look as if time had brought all the earth together for the eye to see.","n":"Line 4 (காலமுற்றுடன் கண்ணுற்ற போன்றவே) is hard to parse; the comparison is read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3003","lb":"CC3003","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3004":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3004,"t":["வாயிற் றோரணங் கற்பக மாலைதாழ்ந்","தேயிற் றிந்திரன் பொன்னக ரின்புறம்","போயிற் றேயகி லின்புகை போர்த்துராய்","ஞாயிற் றொள்ளொளி நைய நடந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Gate arches hung with wish-tree garlands, spread over the walls, are said to outshine Indra's golden city while aloe smoke veils the sun.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3004","lb":"CC3004","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3005":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3005,"t":["செய்ய தாமரைப் பூவினுட் டேங்கமழ்","பொய்யில் சீர்த்திவெண் டாமரை பூத்தபோன்","றையஞ் செய்தடு பானிறப் புள்ளின","மையி றாமரை மத்தகஞ் சேர்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"White birds on the lotus tops look like a white lotus of glory opening in a red lotus, and the poet adds that one could doubt whether they were real.","n":"Line 3 (ஐயஞ்செய்தடுபா னிறப்புள்ளின) is hard to parse, so the poet's doubt is only partly clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3005","lb":"CC3005","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3006":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3006,"t":["மல்லன் மாக்கட லன்ன கிடங்கணிந்","தொல்லென் சும்மைய புள்ளொலித் தோங்கிய","செல்வ நீர்த்திருக் கோயிலிம் மண்மிசை","யில்லை யேற்றுறக் கம்மினி தென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The moat like a great sea and the temple with its roaring birds are so fine that, the verse says, heaven would be sweet only if this did not exist on earth.","n":"The construction of line 3-4 (இல்லை யேற்றுறக்கம் இனிதென்பவே) can be read two ways, as a boast that heaven is lesser or as 'heaven would be sweet if there were no such place'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3006","lb":"CC3006","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3007":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3007,"t":["விளங்கொளி விசும்பறுத் திழிந்து மின்னுதார்த்","துளங்கொளி மணிவணன் றொழுது துன்னினான்","வளங்கெழு மணிவரை நெற்றிப் பாற்கட","லிளங்கதிர்ப் பருதியொத் திறைவன் றோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"A radiant figure comes down from the sky, worships and draws near, and the lord appears like a young sun over the milk-sea on a gem mountain.","n":"It is not clear whether the one who descends is Civakan, a god, or the one who appears; line 4 names the lord (இறைவன்) as the one who shows himself, and I read him as the Jina.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3007","lb":"CC3007","mm":1,"ev":"A radiant figure descends, worships and approaches, and the lord appears.","cl":[],"ag":["புங்கவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3008":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3008,"t":["வினையுதிர்த் தவர்வடி வின்ன தென்னவே","வனைகதிர்த் தடக்கைவைத் திருந்த வாமனார்","கனைகதிர்த் திருமுக மருக்க னாகவான்","புனைமலர்த் தாமரை பூத்த தொத்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The one who has shed his deeds sits with his hand placed, his face like the sun and the lotus opened at its rising.","n":"வினையுதிர்த்தவர் (those who shed deeds) is the verse's own name for the liberated, a Jain sense that the narrator holds rather than argues.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3008","lb":"CC3008","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the one who has shed his deeds has a form like this, radiant as the sun and the opened lotus","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["புங்கவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3009":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3009,"t":["இரிந்தன விருவினை யிலிர்ந்த மெய்ம்மயிர்","சொரிந்தன கண்பனி துதித்துக் காதலா","லரிந்தது மணிமிட றலர்பெய்ம் மாரிதூஉய்த்","திரிந்தனன் வலமுறை திலக மன்னனே."],"p":"","r":"At the sight the king's hair stands up, tears pour, and with love he praises, scatters flowers like rain and circles to the right.","n":"Line 1 (இரிந்தன விருவினை) may mean the deeds flee at his seeing or that his hairs stand; the claim is taken as the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3009","lb":"CC3009","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan praises with tears, scatters flowers and walks round the Jina keeping him on his right.","cl":[["at the sight of the liberated one the two kinds of deed scatter","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3010":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3010,"t":["முத்தொளிர் தாமமு முருவ மாமணித்","தொத்தொளிர் தாமமுஞ் சொரிபொற் றாமமுந்","தத்துநீர்த் தண்கடற் பவழத் தாமமும்","வைத்தபூந் தாமமு மலிந்து தாழ்ந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Pearl garlands, gem clusters, golden and coral wreaths and flower strings hang down in profusion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3010","lb":"CC3010","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3011":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3011,"t":["மணிவரை யெறிதிரை மணந்து சூழ்ந்தபோ","லணிமயிர்க் கவரிக ளமர ரேந்தினார்","துணிமணி முக்குடை சொரிந்த தீங்கதிர்","பணிமணிக் காரிருள் பருகு கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The gods hold up whisks as if the sea waves had met a gem mountain, and the sweet rays of the three umbrellas drink the dark.","n":"The whisks and the three umbrellas are the marks of the Jina's assembly; the verse does not say so in words, and the sense is from the scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3011","lb":"CC3011","mm":1,"ev":"The gods fan the Jina with whisks under the three umbrellas.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரீர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3012":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3012,"t":["முழாத்திரண் மொய்ம்மலர்த் தாமந் தாழ்ந்துமேல்","வழாத்திரு மலரெலா மலர்ந்து வண்டினங்","குழாத்தொடு மிறைகொளக் குனிந்து கூய்க்குயில்","விழாக்கொள விரிந்தது வீரன் பிண்டியே."],"p":"","r":"The Jina's pindi tree opens its flowers, bees gather, and the cuckoo calls as if keeping a festival.","n":"வீரன் பிண்டியே names the tree as the hero's, the ashoka that marks the Jina.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3012","lb":"CC3012","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3013":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3013,"t":["பிண்டியின் கொழுநிழற் பிறவி நோய்கெட","விண்டிலர் கனைகதிர் வீரன் றோன்னினா","னுண்டிவ ணறவமிர் துண்மி னோவெனக்","கொண்டன கோடணை கொற்ற முற்றமே."],"p":"","r":"In the pindi's thick shade, to end the disease of birth, the hero appears and drums announce that all should eat the nectar of dharma.","n":"Line 2 has a misprint (றோன்னினா for தோன்றினான்); the sense is that he appeared.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piravi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3013","lb":"CC3013","mm":1,"ev":"The Jina appears under the pindi tree and the drums call all to eat the nectar of dharma.","cl":[["the Jina's presence under the tree ends the disease of birth, and his dharma is a nectar to be eaten","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["புங்கவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3014":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3014,"t":["வானவர் மலர்மழை சொரிய மன்னிய","வூனிவர் பிறவியை யொழிக்கு முத்தமன்","றேனிமிர் தாமரை திளைக்குஞ் சேவடி","கோனமர்ந் தேத்திய குறுகி னானரோ."],"p":"","r":"The gods rain flowers and the king reaches the faultless lord whose red feet sit on a honeyed lotus, the one who ends the births of ascetics.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3014","lb":"CC3014","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan reaches the Jina's feet while the gods rain flowers.","cl":[["the supreme lord ends the births of ascetics","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","அமரீர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3015":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3015,"t":["குருகுலஞ் சீவக குமரன் கோத்திர","மருகலில் காசிப மடிகள் வாழியென்","றெரிமணி முடிநில முறுத்தி யேத்தினான்","புரிமணி வீணைகள் புலம்ப வென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The one who bows touches his gem crown to the ground and praises, saying that the scion of Civakan's line and the Kasyapa lord may live, while jewelled lutes ring out.","n":"Lines 1-2 are hard to parse: the names சீவக குமரன் and காசிப அடிகள் stand in a run of titles and it is not clear whether the praise is of the Jina or the king's own line, and the word மருகலில் is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3015","lb":"CC3015","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan bows with his crown to the ground and praises the Kasyapa lord.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3016":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3016,"t":["மன்னவன் றுறவெனத் துறத்தன் மாண்பெனப்","பொன்வரை வாய்திறந் தாங்குப் புங்கவ","னின்னுரை யெயிறுவில் லுமிழ வீழ்ந்தது","மின்னியோர் வியன்மழை முழங்கிற் றொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The lord's sweet word, that a king's renunciation is the excellent thing, falls like a rain-cloud's thunder out of his mouth, like gold hill opened.","n":"The word of the Jina is given as a narrated report in the poet's words, not in direct speech, and the sense of the second line (the hill opening its mouth) is a simile for the voice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","iraivan","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3016","lb":"CC3016","mm":1,"ev":"The Jina tells Civakan that the king's renunciation is the excellent course.","cl":[["for a king, to renounce is the excellent thing","jain","asserted",1,"jinan"]],"ag":["புங்கவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புங்கவன்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3017":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3017,"t":["காய்களிற்றி னிடைமருப்பிற் கவளம்போன் றேமாராக் கதியுட் டோன்றி","யாங்களிய வெவ்வினையி னல்லாப்புற் றஞ்சினே னறிந்தார் கோவே","வேய்களிய வண்டறைய விரிந்தலர்ந்த தாமரையின் விரைசேர் போதின்","வாயொளியே பெறநடந்த மலரடியை வலங்கொண்டார் வருந்தார் போலும்."],"p":"","r":"Speaking to the lord of the knowers, the singer says he was born in the unsafe course like a morsel between an elephant's tusks and feared his deeds, and that those who circle the lotus-treading feet seem not to suffer.","n":"The speaker is not named; the 'I' who feared is read as Civakan or as a worshipper in the host. Lines 3-4 are hard to parse. The stanza was printed in eight half-lines and joined into four.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3017","lb":"CC3017","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["beings are born in the dangerous course through evil deeds and fear their fruit","jain","asserted",2,"hymn_voice"],["those who circle the Jina's feet do not suffer","jain","asserted",4,"hymn_voice"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நின்","புங்கவன்"],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3018":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3018,"t":["சேடார்செம்பொற் றிருமணிவைரத் தொத்தணிந் துலகோம்பும்","வாடாமாலை வார்தளர்ப்பிண்டி வாமநின் குணநாளும்","பாடாதாரைப் பாடாதுலகம் பண்ணவர் நின்னடிப்பூச்","சூடாதார்தாள் சூடார்மலைச் சுடர்மணி நெடுமுடியே."],"p":"","r":"In praise to the lord whose garland never fades under the pindi, the singer says that the world does not sing of those who do not sing his virtues and that those who do not wear the flowers of his feet are not worn by the gods.","n":"The second half of the stanza is hard to parse (பாடாதாரைப் பாடாது உலகம், சூடார்மலை ...), so the claim is a loose reading. The stanza was printed in eight half-lines and joined into four.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3018","lb":"CC3018","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who do not praise the Jina's virtues are not praised in return","jain","asserted",3,"hymn_voice"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நின்","புங்கவன்"],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3019":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3019,"t":["வைய மூன்று முடனேத்த வளருந் திங்கள் வாளெயிற்","றைய வரிமான் மணியணைமே லமாந்தோய் நின்னை யமராதார்","வெய்ய வெந்நோய் வினையுதைப்ப வீழ்ந்து துன்பக் கடலழுந்தி","நெய்யு நுண்ணூ னாழிகையி னிரம்பா நின்று சுழல்வரே."],"p":"","r":"The singer says that those who do not reach the lord on his gem seat, praised by all three worlds, are kicked by their fierce deeds, fall into the sea of sorrow and whirl like thread in a weaver's spool.","n":"The comparison in line 4 is of a thread wound on the shuttle (நெய்யும் நுண்ணூல் நாழிகையில் நிரம்பா நின்று): the same thread goes round without end. The stanza was printed in eight half-lines and joined into four.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3019","lb":"CC3019","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who do not come to the Jina fall under their deeds and whirl in a sea of sorrow","jain","asserted",3,"hymn_voice"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நின்","புங்கவன்"],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3020":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3020,"t":["தொழுதிப் பன்மீன் குழாஞ்சூழத் துளும்பா திருந்த திங்கள்போன்","முழுதும் வைய முடனேத்த முதுவாய் வலவை யாயிருந்","தழுது வினைக ளல்லாப்ப வறைந்தோய் நின்சொ லறைந்தார்கள்","பழுதி னறுநெய்க் கடற்சுடர்போற் பல்லாண் டெல்லாம் பரியாரே."],"p":"","r":"The singer addresses the lord, calm as a moon among stars, who sat as the ancient master and spoke while beings wept under their deeds, and says that those who say his word will not suffer for countless years.","n":"Line 2 (முதுவாய் வலவையாயிருந்து) is hard to parse; வலவை reads as a skilled speaker. Line 4's first half is repeated at the start of 3021's last line, which suggests a refrain or a copyist's slip. The stanza was printed in eight half-lines and joined into four.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3020","lb":"CC3020","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who repeat the Jina's word are freed of suffering for ages","jain","asserted",4,"hymn_voice"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நின்","புங்கவன்"],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3021":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3021,"t":["செழும்பொன் வேய்ந்து மணியழுத்தித் திருவார் வைர நிரைத்ததனுட்","கொழுந்து மலருங் கொளக்குயிற்றிக் குலாய சிங்கா தனத்தின்மே","லெழுந்த பருதி யிருந்தாற்போ யழுந்தேன் வந்துன் னடியடைந்தே","பழுதி னறுநெய்க் கடற்சுடர்போற் னருவாய்ப் போத லழகிதோ."],"p":"","r":"The singer says he sank in the sea of birth, came to the lord's feet on a golden throne of gems, and asks whether it would be fair to go again.","n":"The word னருவாய் in line 4 is corrupt and the question's sense cannot be fixed. The stanza was printed in eight half-lines and joined into four.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3021","lb":"CC3021","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the one who has come to the Jina's feet has risen out of the sea of birth","jain","asserted",3,"hymn_voice"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நின்","புங்கவன்"],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3022":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3022,"t":["குண்டலமும் பொற்றோடும் வைந்தாருங் குளிர் முத்தும்","வண்டலம்பு மாலையு மணித்தொத்து நிலந்திவள","விண்டலர்பூந் தாமரையின் விரைத்தும்ப மேனடந்த","வண்டலர்பூந் திருவடியை மணிமுடியின் வணங்கினான்."],"p":"","r":"The king bows with his gem crown, with his ear-rings, gold bands, garland of pearls and flower cluster shining on the ground, to the feet that walked over the lotus with its bees.","n":"The lotus under the walking feet is the Jina's own seat among the lotuses in the assembly; the verse names no person, and the 'king' is taken from the crown.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3022","lb":"CC3022","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan bows with his crown to the Jina's feet.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3023":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3023,"t":["நிலவிலகி யுயிரோம்பி நிமிர்ந்தொளிர்ந்து பசிபகைநோ","யுலகமிருள் கெடவிழிக்கு மொண்மணி யறவாழி","யலகையிலாக் குணக்கடலை யகன்ஞான வரம்பானை","விலையிலா மணிமுடியான் விண்வியப்ப விறைஞ்சினான்."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the jewel crown bows, while the sky wonders, to the sea of countless virtues, the wheel of dharma that guards life and clears the world's darkness of hunger, hatred and disease, and whose limit is wisdom.","n":"The verse names both அறவாழி (wheel of dharma) and குணக்கடல் (sea of virtues): the same Tamil element ஆழி/கடல் is both a wheel and a sea here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aram","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3023","lb":"CC3023","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan bows to the Jina with the gods wondering.","cl":[["the Jina's wheel of dharma guards life and clears the world's darkness of hunger, hatred and disease","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:128"},"cc:3024":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3024,"t":["தூய்த்திரண் மணித்தாமஞ் சொரிந்துபொன் னிலநக்கப்","பூத்திரண் மணிமாலைப் போர்ச்சிங்கம் போதகம்போ","லேத்தரிய குணக்கடலை யிகலின்ப வரம்பானைத்","தோத்திரத்தாற் றொழுதிறைஞ்சித் துறப்பேனென் றெழுந்திருந்தான்."],"p":"","r":"Having praised the Jina, a sea of virtues and the limit of all joy, Civakan rises from his seat resolved to renounce.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3024","lb":"CC3024","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan hymns and bows to the Jina and rises, resolved to renounce.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3025":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3025,"t":["முடியணி யமரரு முலைநல் லார்களும்","புடைபணிந் திருந்தவப் புலவன் பொன்னகர்","கடிமலர்க் கற்பகங் காம வல்லியோ","டிடைவிரா யெங்கணும் பூத்த தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Gods with crowns and women bow around the sage, and his golden city blooms like wishing trees tangled with love-vines.","n":"The two kinds of onlooker, gods and women, are the verse's only actors; they bow and do nothing else, so it is scene-setting for the renunciation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3025","lb":"CC3025","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3026":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3026,"t":["ஒத்தொளி பெருகிய வுருவப் பொன்னகர்","வித்தகன் வலஞ்செய்து விழுப்பொற் பூமிபோய்","மத்தக மயிரென வளர்த்த கைவினைச்","சித்திரக் காவகஞ் செல்வ னெய்தினான்."],"p":"","r":"Circling the glorious golden city of the wise Jina, Civakan goes on to the painted grove like a mass of hair.","n":"'செல்வன்' as Civakan is read from the story; the verse names no one, and the hair simile in line 3 is hard to place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3026","lb":"CC3026","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan circles the Jina's city and reaches the pleasure grove where he will cast off his state.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3027":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3027,"t":["ஏமநீ ருலகமோ ரிம்மிப் பாலென","நாமவே னரபதி நீக்கி நன்கலந்","தூமமார் மாலையுந் துறக்கின் றானரோ","காமனார் கலங்கழிக் கின்ற தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Counting the sea-ringed world as a mere drop of milk, the spear-bearing king puts off his fine ornaments and fragrant garlands, as if Kama were stripping off his gear.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","selvam","pattru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3027","lb":"CC3027","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan strips off his jewels and garlands.","cl":[["the whole world is as nothing beside renunciation","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3028":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3028,"t":["மணியுறை கழிப்பது போல மங்கலப்","பணிவரு பைந்துகி னீக்கிப் பாற்கட","லணிபெற வரும்பிய வருக்க னாமெனத்","திணிநிலத் தியன்றதோர் திலக மாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"He takes off the silk of royal state like a gem from its sheath, and stands like the sun risen from the milk sea, an earthly tilaka.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3028","lb":"CC3028","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan removes his royal garments.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3029":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3029,"t":["மலிந்தநன் மாலைகள் வண்ணப் பூந்துகி","னலிந்துமின் னகுமணி நன்பொற் பேரிழை","மெலிந்தனென் சுமந்தென நீக்கி மேனிலைப்","பொலிந்ததோர் கற்பகம் போலத் தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"He puts off garlands, coloured cloth and gem-set ornaments as burdens he was tired of carrying, and shines like a wishing tree.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3029","lb":"CC3029","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan lays aside the last garlands and ornaments.","cl":[["royal ornaments are burdens to the one who would renounce","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3030":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3030,"t":["திருந்திய கீழ்த்திசை நோக்கிச் செவ்வனே","யிருந்ததோ ரிடிகுரற் சிங்கம் பொங்கிமேற்","சுரிந்ததன் னுளைமயிர் துறப்ப தொத்தன","னெரிந்தெழு மிளஞ்சுட ரிலங்கு மார்பினான்."],"p":"","r":"Facing east, bright-chested, he seems a roaring lion shaking off its mane as he tears out his hair.","n":"The plucking of the hair (the Jain way of tonsure) is put by simile alone; the verb 'துறப்பது' carries both leaving and shedding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3030","lb":"CC3030","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan faces east and pulls out his hair.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3031":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3031,"t":["அஞ்சுடர்த் தாமரைக் கையி னான்மணிக்","குஞ்சிவெண் படலிகைக் குமர னீப்பது","செஞ்சுடர்க் கருங்கதிர்க் கற்றை தேறுநீர்","மஞ்சுடை மதியினுட் சொரிவ தொத்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Prince Civakan lets the plucked hair fall from his lotus hand into a white tray, like pouring dark rays into the moon.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3031","lb":"CC3031","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan drops his plucked hair into a white tray.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3032":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3032,"t":["வேலைவாய் மணியிலை யூழ்த்து வீழ்ப்பதோர்","காலைவாய் கற்பக மரத்திற் காவலன்","மாலைவா யகிறவழ் குஞ்சி மாற்றலிற்","சோலைவாய்ச் சுரும்பினந் தொழுது சொன்னவே."],"p":"","r":"The hair set aside, the bees of the grove, like a kalpaka tree dropping jewelled leaves at dawn, bow to the king and speak.","n":"Lines 1 to 3 are hard to parse; the bees as speakers are what the last line gives.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3032","lb":"CC3032","mm":1,"ev":"The bees of the grove bow to the king after his tonsure and speak.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரும்பு, வண்டு, தேன், ஞிமிறு","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3033":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3033,"t":["தங்கிடை யிலாத்திருக் கேசந் தன்னையுங்","கொங்குடைக் கோதையுங் கொய்து நீக்கினாய்","நுங்கடை நோக்கிநாம் வாழும் வாக்கைய","மெங்கிடை யவரினி யெங்குச் செல்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The bees complain that he has cut off his hair and garland, which they lived by, and ask where they are to go now.","n":"The bees' lament is a poet's device for what the lord's former life lost; the verse does not argue against renunciation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3033","lb":"CC3033","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["renunciation leaves behind those who lived by one's body and ornament","general","reported",3,"bees"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுரும்பு, வண்டு, தேன், ஞிமிறு","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3034":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3034,"t":["என்றன தேனின மிரங்கு வண்டொடு","சென்றன விடுக்கிய செல்வன் பொன்மயி","ரின்றொடு தொழுதன நும்மை யாமென","மன்றலுண் டவைவலங் கொண்டு சென்றவே."],"p":"","r":"The bees, grieving, go with the garland's swarm and, bowing to the king's golden hair, circle it and depart.","n":"The sense of 'என்றன தேனினம்' and the second line is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3034","lb":"CC3034","mm":1,"ev":"The bees circle the cast-off hair and leave.","cl":[],"ag":["சுரும்பு, வண்டு, தேன், ஞிமிறு"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3035":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3035,"t":["மேற்படு கற்பக மாலை வேய்ந்துபொ","னேற்புடைப் படலிகை யெடுத்துக் கொண்டுபோய்","நாற்கடல் கடந்தவ னமோவென் றிட்டிடப்","பாற்கடல் பனிமதி போல வீழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Gods lift the golden tray with its kalpaka-garland, cry 'homage to him who crossed the four seas,' and let it fall like the moon into the milk sea.","n":"The cry 'நமோ' is the homage-word of the Jain mantra; the verse does not itself say which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3035","lb":"CC3035","mm":1,"ev":"The gods carry off Civakan's hair, cry homage, and lay it in the milk sea.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3036":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3036,"t":["ஏவா விருந்த வடிக ளிவர்வாய்ச்சொற்","கோவா மணிகொழித்துக் கொண்டாலே போலுமாற்","சாவா கிடந்தார் செவிச்சார்த்தி னப்பொழுதே","மூவா வமரராய் முத்தணிந்து தோன்றுவரே."],"p":"","r":"Whoever whispers these unstrung-gem words into the ears of the dying becomes, at that hour, a deathless god adorned with pearls.","n":"'ஏவா இருந்த அடிகள்' is hard to place and the speaker may be a charge from the god Sutanjanan; the claim recalls verse 945, where Civakan gave the dog the mantra.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3036","lb":"CC3036","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the mantra heard at the hour of death brings birth among the gods","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3037":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3037,"t":["தோளா மணிகுவித்தாற் போன்றிலங்கு தொல்குலத்துச்","சூளா மணியாய்ச் சுடர விருந்தானை","வாளார் முடிவைர விற்றிளைத்து வண்டரற்றுந்","தாளார வேத்திப்போய்த் தன்கோயில் புக்கானே."],"p":"","r":"The god, having praised Civakan the crest-jewel of an old line at his feet, enters his own shrine.","n":"The subject 'புக்கானே' is read as Sutanjanan from the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3037","lb":"CC3037","mm":1,"ev":"Sutanjanan, after praising Civakan's feet, goes into his own palace.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3038":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3038,"t":["புக்கான் சுதஞ்சணனும் பொற்றா மரைமகளிர்","தொக்காலே போலுந்தன் றேவிக் குழாஞ்சூழ","மிக்கான் குணம்பாடி யாடி மிகுதீம்பா","றொக்க கடல்போற் சுதங்கணி றைந்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"Sutanjanan enters with his consorts like a crowd of golden lotus maidens, and sings and dances the virtue of the lord until his palace overflows like a sea of sweet milk.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3038","lb":"CC3038","mm":1,"ev":"Sutanjanan and his women sing and dance in praise of Civakan's virtue.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3039":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3039,"t":["பற்றாவஞ் செற்ற முதலாகப் பாம்புரிபோன்","முற்றத் துறந்து முனிகளா யெல்லாரு","முற்றுயிர்க்குத் தீம்பால் சுரந்தோம்பி யுள்ளத்து","மற்றிருள் சேரா மணிவிளக்கு வைத்தாரே."],"p":"","r":"All of them, dropping attachment and hatred like a snake's skin, become ascetics, shed the milk of kindness on every life, and set a gem lamp so that darkness does not reach the mind.","n":"'எல்லாரும்' is the queens, kin and followers by the story; the verse does not list them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arul","kollaamai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3039","lb":"CC3039","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan's kin and followers renounce and become ascetics.","cl":[["giving up attachment and hatred wholly makes the ascetic, whose kindness shields the mind from darkness","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["எல்லாரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3040":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3040,"t":["கோமா னடிசாரக் குஞ்சுரங்கள் செல்வனபோற்","பூமாண் டிருக்கோயிற் புங்கவன்றாள் சேர்ந்தேத்தித்","தாமார்ந்த சீலக் கடலாடிச் சங்கினத்துட்","டூமாண் வலம்புரியின் றோற்றம்போற் புக்காரே."],"p":"","r":"Following the lord like elephants, they reach the Jina's temple, praise his feet, bathe in the sea of virtue and enter like a right-turned conch among ordinary shells.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3040","lb":"CC3040","mm":1,"ev":"They worship at the Jina's feet and enter the order.","cl":[],"ag":["எல்லாரும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3041":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3041,"t":["மட்டலர் வனமலர்ப் பிண்டி வாமனார்","விட்டலர் தாமரைப் பாதம் வீங்கிரு","ளட்டலர் பருதியி னளிக்கச் செல்லுநாட்","பட்டதோர் பொருளினிப் பழிச்சு கின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"As the lotus feet of the lord of the flowering pindi tree shine like a sun on thick dark, the poet turns to praise what then happened.","n":"A link verse that turns from Civakan's renunciation to the closing account of the work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3041","lb":"CC3041","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3042":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3042,"t":["கயலின முகளிப் பாய முல்லையம் பொதும்பிற் காமர்","புயலின மொக்குள் வன்கட் குறுமுயல் புலம்பிக் குன்றத்","தயல்வளர் கின்ற வாமான் குழவியோ டிரிந்து செந்நெல்","வயல்வளர் கரும்பிற் பாயு மகதநா டென்ப துண்டே."],"p":"","r":"A land called Magadha lies where fish leap, mullai groves bloom and deer graze by paddy fields and cane.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3042","lb":"CC3042","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3043":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3043,"t":["இரும்பிடி தழீஇய யானை யிழிமதங் கலந்து சேறாய்ச்","சுரும்பொடு மணிவண் டார்க்குந் துகிற்கொடி மாட வீதிப்","பெருங்கடி நகரம் பேசி னிராசமா கிருக மென்ப","ரருங்கடி யமரர் கோமா னணிநக ராய தொன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Its chief city, Rajagriha, with rutting elephants, flag-hung mansions and bright streets, is said to be a match for the city of the king of the gods.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3043","lb":"CC3043","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3044":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3044,"t":["எரிமிடைந் தனைய மாலை யினமணி திருவில் வீசுந்","திருமுடி யார மார்பிற் சேணிக னென்ப நாம","மருமுடி மன்னர் சூழ வலரணி பிண்டி வேந்தன்","றிருவடி விருந்து செய்வான் றிரண்முர சறைவித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Senikan, whose crown and garlands flash like fire, orders the drum beaten for a feast in honour of the Jina's feet while kings throng.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3044","lb":"CC3044","mm":1,"ev":"King Senikan has the drum proclaim a feast for the feet of the Jina, lord of the pindi tree.","cl":[],"ag":["சேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3045":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3045,"t":["பொன்னா வழியாற் புகழ்நாவழித் மெல்கோன்","மின்னார் மணிப்பூ ணவன்மேவிவிண் காறு நாறு","முன்னோர் வகுத்த முகவாசம் பொதிந்த வெந்நீர்","மன்னார வாய்க்கொண் டுமிழ்ந்தான் மணிமாலை வேலோன்."],"p":"","r":"The king, having cleaned his mouth with scented water, spits it out before going to worship.","n":"The first two lines are corrupt as printed and cannot be parsed; the sense of rinsing the mouth rests on line 3 alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3045","lb":"CC3045","mm":1,"ev":"Senikan rinses his mouth with scented water.","cl":[],"ag":["சேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3046":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3046,"t":["தீம்பா னுரைபோற் றிகழ்வெண்பட் டுடுத்து வண்டார்","தேம்பாய சாந்த மெழுகிக்கலன் றேறன் மாலை","தாம்பால தாங்கிப் புகழ்தாமரைக் குன்ற மன்ன","வாம்பான் மயிர்வேய்ந் தயிராவண மேறி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Dressed in milk-white silk, sandalled, garlanded and with his hair bound, he mounts a white elephant like a lotus mountain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3046","lb":"CC3046","mm":1,"ev":"Senikan dresses and mounts his elephant to go to the Jina.","cl":[],"ag":["சேணிகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3047":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3047,"t":["எறிசுரும் பரற்று மாலை யெரிமணிச் செப்பு வெள்ளம்","பொறிவரி வண்டு பாடும் பூஞ்சுண்ண நிறைந்த பொற்செப்","பறிவரி துணர்வு நாணித் தலைபணித் தஞ்சுஞ் சாந்தஞ்","செறியிரும் பவழச் செப்புத் தெண்கடற் றிரையி னேரே."],"p":"","r":"Caskets of fragrant flower-dust, loaded with bees, are carried as offerings, each like a wave of the clear sea.","n":"The verse is a list of offerings carried; line 3's 'தலைபணித்தஞ்சும்' is hard to place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3047","lb":"CC3047","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3048":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3048,"t":["வந்துதேன் மயங்கி மூசு மலயச்செஞ் சாந்த மார்ந்த","சந்தனச் செப்புங் கங்கை தருமண லலகை யாற்றாச்","சுந்தரம் பெய்த யானைத் தூமருப் பியன்ற வெண்செப்","பந்தரத் தலர்ந்த பன்மீ னெனைத்துள வனைத்து மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Caskets of sandal paste and ivory, as many as the stars in the sky, are likewise carried.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3048","lb":"CC3048","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:129"},"cc:3049":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3049,"t":["மைபொதி குவளை வாட்கண் மல்லிகைக் கோதை நல்லார்","நெய்பொதி நெஞ்சின் மன்னர் நிலம்பிறக் கிடுவ போலுங்","கொய்சுவற் புரவி மான்றேர் குழுமணி யோடை யானை","மெய்பொதிந் துயர்ந்த கோமான் விரைப்பலி சுமந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the land, with women of lotus eyes, horses, chariots and elephants in his train, comes carrying fragrant offerings to the Lord's assembly.","n":"The Tamil never names the king; the next verses and the question in 3059 point to the king who hears Sudharman. Lines 2-3 compare the horses and chariots to something that gives out land, which is hard to parse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","perumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3049","lb":"CC3049","mm":1,"ev":"The king comes in state to the Lord's assembly carrying the fragrant offering (bali).","cl":[],"ag":["காவன் மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3050":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3050,"t":["கொடிக்குழாங் குஞ்சி பிச்சக் குழாநிறை கோல மாலை","முடிக்குழா மூரி வானம் பால்சொரி கின்ற தொக்குங்","குடைக்குழா மிவற்றின் பாங்கர்க் குளித்தது குளிர்சங் கார்க்கும்","படைக்குழாம் பாரிற் செல்லும் பாற்கடல் பழித்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The king's host of banners, crests, garlands, crowns, umbrellas and troops fills the earth like a milk-sea poured out, outdoing the sea of milk itself.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3050","lb":"CC3050","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["படை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3051":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3051,"t":["கனைகடல் கவரச் செல்லுங் கணமழைத் தொழுதி போலு","நனைமலர்ப் பிண்டி நாத னல்லறங் கொள்ளை சாற்றிப்","புனைமுடி மன்ன ரீண்டிப் பொன்னெயிற் புறத்து விட்டார்","வினையுடைத் தின்ப வெள்ளம் விரும்பிய வேட்கை யானே."],"p":"","r":"Kings who gathered to hear the Lord of the pindi tree proclaim his good dharma freely left their retinues outside the golden rampart, out of longing for the flood of bliss.","n":"Line 4, வினையுடைத் தின்ப வெள்ளம், can be read as the bliss of those with karma or as bliss that belongs to the one without it; the sense is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","iraivan","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3051","lb":"CC3051","mm":1,"ev":"Kings assemble to hear the Lord's dharma and leave their retinues outside the golden wall.","cl":[],"ag":["மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3052":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3052,"t":["வண்டுசூழ் பூப்பலி சுமந்து தான்வலங்","கொண்டுசூழ்ந் தெழுமுறை யிறைஞ்சிக் கோனடி","யெண்டிசை யவர்களும் மருள வேத்தினான்","வெண்டிரைப் புணரிசூழ் வேலி வேந்தனே."],"p":"","r":"The king carries the bee-ringed flower offering, circles the Lord to the right, bows and praises the Lord's feet until the dwellers of the eight quarters marvel.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3052","lb":"CC3052","mm":1,"ev":"The king circles the Lord, worships his feet and praises him before the whole assembly.","cl":[],"ag":["காவன் மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3053":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3053,"t":["பகல்வளர் பவழச் செந்தீப் பருதிமுற் பட்ட தேபோ","லிகல்வினை யெறிந்த கோமா னிணையடி யொளியிற் றோன்றா","தகல்விசும் புறையுந் தேவ ரொளியவிந் திருப்ப மன்னன்","முகிழ்கிழி மதியம் போலு முனிக்குழா நோக்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"Like a coral-red fire in front of the sun, the lights of the sky-dwelling gods dim before the glow of the feet of the Lord who cut down his hostile karma, and the king looks on the crowd of sages.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3053","lb":"CC3053","mm":1,"ev":"The king looks over the assembly of sages while the gods' lights fade beside the Lord's feet.","cl":[["the Lord has cut away the karma that makes enmity, and his feet outshine the gods","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["காவன் மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3054":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3054,"t":["கண்வெறி போக வாங்கோர் கடுந்தவ னுருவ நோக்கி","யொண்ணெறி யொருவிக் கோமா ணொளிதிரண் டிருந்த தாங்கொல்","விண்ணெறி வழுவி வீழ்ந்த விண்ணவ னொருவன் கொல்லென்","றெண்ணெறி யாது மோரா திருந்திது கூறி னானே"],"p":"","r":"Dazzled and taking a stern ascetic's shape for a god fallen from his path, the king speaks hastily without weighing what he sees.","n":"Who is the 'lord' of line 2 and who speaks the question is not settled by the Tamil; this reading takes the king as the one who 'says this' in line 4 and the 'unweighed' guess as his. The next verses answer it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaraaydhal","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3054","lb":"CC3054","mm":1,"ev":"The king, seeing a radiant ascetic, wonders aloud whether a god has fallen from the sky.","cl":[],"ag":["காவன் மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3055":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3055,"t":["விளங்கொளி விசும்பறுத் திழிந்து விண்ணவ","னிளங்கதி ரெனத்துறந் திருப்பக் கண்டனம்","வளங்கெழு முக்குடை யடிகள் வாய்மொழி","துளங்கின னெனத் தொழு திறைஞ்சி னானரோ."],"p":"","r":"The speaker reports seeing a god descend in bright light and sit renouncing like a young sun, trembling at the word of the Lord of the three umbrellas, and bowing to him.","n":"The speaker is probably the king putting his question to Sudharman, but line 4 sounds like the narrator; the one who bows may be the god Civakan of 3057-3058, which the Tamil does not yet say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3055","lb":"CC3055","mm":1,"ev":"A god comes down from the sky, sits as an ascetic and bows to the Lord.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவக சாமி"],"ec":[],"spk":["காவன் மன்னன்","சுதன்மர்"],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3056":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3056,"t":["மன்னவ கேண்மதி வானில் வாழ்பவர்","பொன்னியல் கற்பகப் போக பூமியா","ரென்னதுந் துறவல ரிறைவன் வாய்மொழி","சொன்னவா றல்லதெப் பொருளுந் தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Sudharman tells the king that the dwellers of heaven, in the golden wishing-tree land of enjoyment, renounce nothing, and that nothing is seen except as the Lord's word has said.","n":"That Sudharman is the speaker comes from 3059-3061; in this verse only the address to the king shows a teacher is speaking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","thuravu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3056","lb":"CC3056","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["those who live in the heavens of enjoyment cannot give anything up","jain","asserted",3,"sudharmar"],["nothing is seen that departs from what the Lord's word has said","jain","asserted",3,"sudharmar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதன்மர்","காவன் மன்னன்"],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3057":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3057,"t":["அடிகளுக் கிடமருங் கிருந்த வாய்மலர்க்","கடிகமழ் தாமரைக் கண்ணி னானிவன்","வடிவமே வாய்திறந் துரைக்கும் வானவ","னொடிவறு பேரொளி யுட்கத் தக்கதே."],"p":"","r":"Beside the Lord sits a lotus-eyed god whose very form seems to speak without his opening his mouth, and whose unfailing light is fearsome to look at.","n":"The verse does not name the god; 3058 does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oli","perumai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3057","lb":"CC3057","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவக சாமி"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3058":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3058,"t":["திருவி னோடகன்ற மார்பிற் சீவக சாமி யென்பா","னுருவினோ டொளியு நோக்கி னொப்புமை யுலகி னில்லை","மருவினா ரிமைத்து நோக்கின் மனம்பிறி தாகி நிற்பா","ரரிதிவன் முகத்து நோக்க லழகொளி யன்ன வென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says of Civakan, whose chest is parted from Fortune, that his form and light have no equal on earth and that those who gaze on him with unblinking eyes find their minds changed.","n":"Naming the god Civakan Swami here answers the king's guess in 3054: the radiant figure is the hero, now seen as a god. Who says 'என்றான்' is read as Sudharman.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","perumai","oli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3058","lb":"CC3058","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதன்மர்","காவன் மன்னன்"],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3059":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3059,"t":["மாதவன் சரிதமுந் துறந்த வண்ணமு","மேதமின் றியம்புமி னடிக ளோவெனப்","போதலர் புனைமுடி யிறைஞ்சி யேத்தினான்","காதலிற் கணந்தொழக் காவன் மன்னனே."],"p":"","r":"The king bows and asks Sudharman to tell, without fault, the history of the great ascetic and how he gave up his life.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","kelvi","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3059","lb":"CC3059","mm":1,"ev":"The king bows and asks Sudharman to tell the ascetic's story and his renunciation.","cl":[],"ag":["காவன் மன்னன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காவன் மன்னன்","சுதன்மர்"],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3060":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3060,"t":["பாட்டருங் கேவலப் பரவை மாக்கடற்","கூட்டருங் கொழுந்திரை முகந்து மாமுனி","மோட்டிரு மணிமுகின் முழங்கிப் பெய்தலி","னூட்டரு மறவமிர் துலக முண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The great sage's words rain from the cloud over the ocean of unmatched complete knowledge, and the world drinks the nectar of the unfailing dharma.","n":"The rain-cloud picture is the narrator's praise of the sage; whether the 'great sage' here is Sudharman or the Lord is not fixed by the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","aram","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3060","lb":"CC3060","mm":1,"ev":"Sudharman speaks and the world takes in his teaching.","cl":[["the teaching comes from the boundless ocean of complete knowledge and nourishes the world like nectar","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சுதன்மர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3061":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3061,"t":["சீவகன் றிருவின மாக யாமென","நாவகந் தழும்பநின் றேத்தி நன்றரோ","காவல னாதியாக் கணங்கள் கைதொழப்","பாவமில் சுதன்மராற் பாடப் பட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The gathered hosts, with the king first, praise Sudharman, the sinless teacher, saying that they would be of Civakan's company.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","kelvi","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3061","lb":"CC3061","mm":1,"ev":"The assembly praises Sudharman and wishes to share Civakan's fortune.","cl":[],"ag":["காவன் மன்னன்","சுதன்மர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3062":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3062,"t":["முல்லைசூழ் முல்லை வேலி முயலொடு கவரி மேயுங்","கொல்லைசூழ் குன்றத் துச்சிக் குருசினோற் றுயர்ந்த வாறும்","வில்லுமிழ்ந் திலங்கு மேனி விழுத்தவ நங்கை மார்கண்","மல்லலங் குமரர் வான்மேற் சென்றதும் வகுக்க லுற்றேன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet announces that he will tell how Civakan suffered on the hill-top and how the young princes and the ascetic women went up to heaven.","n":"The 'I will tell' voice may be the narrator's or Sudharman's; verse 3063 onward is read as the telling.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3062","lb":"CC3062","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3063":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3063,"t":["முழுதுமுந் திரிகைப் பழச் சோலைத்தே","னொழுகிநின் றசும்பும் முயர் சந்தனத்","தொழுதிக் குன்றந் துளும்பச்சென் றெய்தினான்","பழுதில் வாய்மொழிப் பண்ணவ னென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Civakan goes to the sandalwood hills dripping with honey from the jack-fruit groves, so that the hill trembles with his coming.","n":"The speaker's closing 'என்பவே' (they say) marks this as reported story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","idam","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3063","lb":"CC3063","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan reaches the sandalwood hill.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3064":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3064,"t":["நணிதினெண் வினையின் னவைகண் ணிறீஇத்","துணிய வீர்ந்திடுந் துப்பமை சிந்தையான்","மணியின் மேன்மணி கட்டிய தொத்ததற்","கணியு மாயலர் ஞாயிறு மாயினான்."],"p":"","r":"With a will that cuts the eight karmas into pieces, he becomes like gem set on gem and a sun that cannot be approached.","n":"Karma is destroyed here by the ascetic's own strength of mind, not by grace or by a god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3064","lb":"CC3064","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan cuts the eight karmas by the firmness of his mind.","cl":[["the eight karmas can be cut away by an ascetic's firm will","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3065":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3065,"t":["குன்றின்வீ ழருவிக் குரல் கோடணைச்","சென்றெலாத் திசையுஞ் சிலம்பின் மிசை","நின்றனன் னிறை வம்மின் நீரென","வொன்றி நின்றதி ரும்மொரு பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"The sound of waterfalls on the hill goes out in every direction and seems to call all to come, while on one side of the mountain he stands above.","n":"Lines 3-4 are hard to parse (a call 'come, fill up'); the waterfall as voice is my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3065","lb":"CC3065","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3066":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3066,"t":["செம்பொன் பின்னிய போற்றினைக் காவலர்","வெம்பு மும்மத வேழம் விலக்குவார்","தம்புனத் தெறிமாமணி சந்து பாய்ந்","தும்பர் மீனெனத் தோன்றுமொர் பாலெலா"],"p":"","r":"On one side of the hill, guards of the golden-bound millet fields drive back rutting elephants, and flung gems fly through the air like stars.","n":"The last half-line is cut off in the source, and the Tamil is hard to parse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3066","lb":"CC3066","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["காவலர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3067":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3067,"t":["யானை குங்கும மாடி யருவரைத்","தேனெய் வார்சுனை யுண்டு திளைத்துடன்","கானமாப் பிடி கன்றொடு நாடக","மூனமின்றி நின் றாடுமொர் பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On another side elephants bathe in saffron, drink from honey-flowing springs, and dance with their mates and calves, without harm.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3067","lb":"CC3067","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3068":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3068,"t":["வரியநாக மணிச் சுடர் மல்கிய","பொருவில் பொன்முழைப் போர்ப்புலிப் போதக","மரிய கின்னரர் பாட வமர்ந்துதம்","முருவந் தோன்ற வுறங்குமொர் பாலெலாம்."],"p":"","r":"On another side striped tigers with their cubs sleep in gold caves lit by serpent-gems, while the kinnaras sing and show their forms.","n":"The peace between beasts is described, not explained; the verse does not say it comes from the ascetic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3068","lb":"CC3068","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3069":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3069,"t":["பழுத்த தீம்பல வின்கனி வாழையின்","விழுக்கு லைக்கனி மாங்கனி வீழ்ந்தவை","தொழித்து மந்தி துணங்கை யயர்ந்துதே","னழிக்கு மஞ்சுனை யாடுமொர் பாலெலாம"],"p":"","r":"On another side monkeys leave fallen jack, plantain and mango fruit, dance in the revel, and bathe in the honey-spoiled spring.","n":"The last half-line is cut off in the source.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3069","lb":"CC3069","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3070":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3070,"t":["நளிசிலம் பதனி னுச்சி நாட்டிய பொன்செய் கந்தி","னொளியொடு சுடர வெம்பி யுருத்தெழு கனலி வட்டந்","தெளிகடல் சுடுவ தொத்துத் தீயுமிழ் திங்க ணான்கும்","விளிவரு குரைய ஞான வேழமேற் கொண்டு நின்றான்."],"p":"","r":"In the four months of the sun's burning ring, as if it would scorch the sea, he stands on the elephant of knowledge beside a gold post, unmoved by the heat that spits fire.","n":"This is the first of three four-month seasons, summer (70), rains (71) and cold (72).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","porai","arivu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3070","lb":"CC3070","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan endures the four hot months standing in tapas.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3071":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3071,"t":["பார்க்கடல் பருகி மேகம் பாம்பினம் பதைப்ப மின்னி","வார்ப்பிணி முரசி னார்த்து மண்பக விடித்து வான","நீர்த்திரள் பளிக்குத் தூணி சொரிந்திட நின்று வென்றான்.","மூர்த்தியாய் முனிவ ரேத்து முனிக்களி றனைய கோமான்."],"p":"","r":"Like a sage-elephant, he overcomes the rains: clouds drink the sea, lightning frightens snakes, thunder breaks the ground, and sheets of water pour down while he stands.","n":"Line 3 ends with a full stop in the source and line 4 gives the subject: the subject carries over from 3070.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","porai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3071","lb":"CC3071","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan stands through the four months of rain and storm.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3072":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3072,"t":["திங்கணான் கவையு நீங்கத் திசைச்செல்வார் மடிந்து தேங்கொள்","பங்கயப் பகைவந் தென்னப் பனிவரை யுருவி வீசு","மங்குல்சூழ் வாடைக் கொல்கான் வெள்ளிடை வதிந்து மாதோ","விங்குநான் காய திங்க ளின்னுயி ரோம்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"When the four months of the cold north wind and mist have passed, he lived in the open field, and in these four months he guarded sweet life.","n":"'இன்னுயிர் ஓம்பினான்' may mean he protected creatures' lives (ahimsa in the rains-retreat) or his own; the Tamil leaves both open. Line 1 and 2 are hard to parse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","porai","kaalam","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3072","lb":"CC3072","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan stays out in the open through the four cold months.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3073":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3073,"t":["வடிமலர் நெடுங்க ணாரு மைந்தரும் வரவு பார்த்தங்","கடிமலர் பரவ வேறி யாரமிர் தரிதிற் கொள்வான்","கடிமலர்க் கமலத் தன்ன கையினை மறித்துக் கொள்ளான்","முடிதவக் கடலை நீந்தி யின்னண முற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"His wives and sons come to watch for him and worship his feet, and he, who would take the nectar-like food with difficulty, holds back the lotus hand and takes none; thus he crosses the sea of austerity and ends.","n":"The ending of the verse (அரிதிற் கொள்வான் ... கையினை மறித்துக் கொள்ளான்) can be read as a refusal of food that leads to his end, or as a gesture of withdrawal; the Tamil does not use the word for death by fasting, so I do not tag it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","undi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3073","lb":"CC3073","mm":1,"ev":"His family comes to him; he refuses food and completes his austerity.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்","மனைவியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:130"},"cc:3074":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3074,"t":["ஒளிறுதேர் ஞானம் பாய்மா வின்னுயி ரோம்ப லோடைக்","களிறுநற் சிந்தை காலாள் கருணையாங் கவசஞ் சீலம்","வெளிறில்வாள் விளங்கு செம்பொன் வட்டமெய்ப் பொருள்க ளாகப்","பிளிறுசெய் கருமத் தெவ்வர் பெருமதின் முற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The hero besieges the great walled fort of his foes, with knowledge as his chariot, protecting life as his horses, thought as his rutting elephant, mercy as armour, virtue as a flawless sword and the true things as the golden discs.","n":"The whole passage, 3074 to 3082, is an allegory: the army is the ascetic's virtues, the foes are the passions and the karmas that bind. The text does not say so in a plain sentence, it simply lets the war language carry it; the siege is a siege of karma.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","padai","arivu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3074","lb":"CC3074","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan lays siege to the fortress of his enemies, armed with the inner qualities named as an army.","cl":[["knowledge, protection of life, mercy and virtue are the weapons and armour with which the self conquers its enemies","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3075":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3075,"t":["உறக்கெனு மோடை யானை யூணெனு முருவத் திண்டேர்","மறப்பெனும் புரவி வெள்ளம் வந்தடை பிணிசெய் காலாட்","டிறப்படப் பண்ணிப் பொல்லாச் சிந்தனை வாயில் போந்து","சுறக்கட லனைய தானை துளங்கப்போர் செய்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Sleep as a rutting elephant, food as a firm chariot, forgetfulness as a flood of horses, and disease as foot-soldiers come against him; he kills them and the sea-like host of bad thought, which had come in at the gate, is shaken.","n":"The four-fold army of the foe mirrors the king's own army of 3074: elephant, chariot, horse, foot; the epic fits the ascetic's war to the shape of the royal war it has told many times before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vinai","adakkam","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3075","lb":"CC3075","mm":1,"ev":"The enemy host of sleep, appetite, forgetfulness and disease, with the thoughts that let them in, is broken in battle.","cl":[["sleep, appetite and heedlessness are enemies of the ascetic to be destroyed","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3076":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3076,"t":["தெளிவறுத் தெழுவர் பட்டா ரீரெண்மர் திளைத்து வீழ்ந்தார்","களிறுகா லுதைப்ப வெண்மர் கவிழ்ந்தனர் களத்தி னுள்ளே","பிளிறிவீழ் பேடி பெண்ணோ யறுவகைத் துவர்ப்பும் பேசி","னளிபடு சிந்தை யென்னு மாழிவாய் வீழ்ந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Seven and sixteen of the foes fall, eight are trampled by elephants, the neuter, the woman and the sick are named with the six 'astringent' things, and all are swallowed in the whirl of the thought named 'pity'.","n":"The numbers (seven, sixteen, eight) and the neuter, woman and sickness read as the Jain lists of the lesser passions and the three sexes of desire; this is my reading of a list the verse names without saying what it counts, and the half-lines are corrupt in places.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vinai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3076","lb":"CC3076","mm":1,"ev":"Groups of the enemy fall in the field one after another.","cl":[["the passions, in their several kinds, are destroyed one by one in the field of the mind","jain","reported",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3077":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3077,"t":["மயக்கப்போர் மன்னன் மக்கண் மந்திரி யவரும் வீழ","வியப்புறு வேத வில்வாய். வேட்கையம் பெடுத்திட் டெய்யக்","கலக்கமி லசுப மென்னுங் குந்தத்தாற் கணைபெய்ம் மாரி","விலக்கித்திண் வெறுப்பு வாளால் விரைந்துயி ரவனை யுண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"With the sons and the minister of the king named 'delusion' fallen, the bow of the Veda-desires shoots a rain of arrows, but he wards it off with the spear called 'impurity' and quickly takes the king's life with the sword of dislike.","n":"Line 2 has a full stop in the middle and a corrupt word division. மயக்கப்போர் மன்னன் is the king of confusion (the delusion-karma), and வேத here is the three drives of sex, not the scripture; this is the Jain use, and the verse leaves it for the reader to know.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kaamam","vinai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3077","lb":"CC3077","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan kills the king Delusion after his sons and minister have fallen.","cl":[["delusion is the king of the enemies; the three desires (vedam) are its bow, and dislike and the contemplation of impurity are what defeat it","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3078":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3078,"t":["கரும்பெறி கடிகை போன்றுங் கதலிகைப் போழ்கள் போன்று","மரும்பொறிப் பகைவர் தம்மை யுறுப்பறத் துணித்து மீர்ந்து","மருந்தெறி பிணியைக் கொல்லு மருத்துவன் போன்று மாதோ","விருந்தெறிந் தெறியு மூவர் மேற்படை யியற்றி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As a hunter cuts the sugarcane, as a physician cuts out a disease with medicine, he cuts the enemies limb from limb and throws a weapon against the three guests.","n":"The simile of a physician who kills the disease is the verse's own account of what the cutting is: not cruelty but cure. The three that remain are unnamed here and the rest of the passage does not say what they are.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vinai","kolai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3078","lb":"CC3078","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan cuts down his enemies and turns on the remaining three.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3079":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3079,"t":["செழுமல ராவி நீங்கு மெல்லையிற் செறிந்து காயங்","கழுமிய வுதிரம் போல விமைப்பினுட் கரந்து நீங்கக்","கொழுமலர்க் குவளைக் கண்ணிக் கூற்றுயி ருண்பதே போல்","விழுமிய தெவ்வர் வாணாள் வீழ்ந்துக வெம்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"As the spirit leaves a body and the blood dries within the wound, the lives of the foes pour away in a blink and fall; Death, in a garland of kuvalai, seems to drink their lives, and he is angry.","n":"The image of Death garlanded with flowers and drinking lives makes the ascetic himself Death to the karmas; it is the opposite of the Jain horror at killing a living being, and the verse can use it only because the foes are not living things.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kootru","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3079","lb":"CC3079","mm":1,"ev":"The enemies' lives leave them at once as Civakan strikes.","cl":[],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3080":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3080,"t":["குரோதனே மானன் மாயன் கூர்ப்புடை யுலோப னென்பார்","விரோதித்து விரலிற் சுட்டி வெருவரத் தாக்க வீர","னிரோதனை யம்பிற் கொன்றா னித்தைநீள் பசலைப் பேரோர்","விராகெனும் வேலின் வீழ வெகுண்டன னவரும் வீழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"The four passions, Anger, Pride, Deceit and Greed, rise against him and point at him with their fingers; the hero kills one, Nirodhanai, with an arrow, and Pride and the rest fall to the spear called 'Viraagam', each of them dying in anger.","n":"Kural 360 names three roots of all pain (desire, anger, confusion); here the Jain list is of four (anger, pride, deceit, greed), the same four that the Jain tradition calls the kasayas. The text gives the spear its name only in the single word விராகு, which I read as the Sanskrit-derived 'virāga', dispassion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","pagai","veedu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3080","lb":"CC3080","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan kills the four passions in turn, with arrow and spear.","cl":[["anger, pride, deceit and greed are the four passions to be destroyed, the last by dispassion","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","குரோதன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3081":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3081,"t":["புணரிபோற் சிறுபுண் கேள்விப் படையொடு புகைந்து பொங்கி","யுணர்வொடு காட்சி பேறென் றிடையுறு கோக்க ளேற்றா","ரிணரெரி முழக்க மன்ன சுக்கிலத் தியான மென்னுங்","கணையெறிந் துகைப்ப வீழ்ந்து காற்படை சூழப் பட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"Like the sea his lesser foes rise in a mist of petty learning, and the feeble lords who stand between knowledge, sight and attainment cannot hold; he fires the arrow named the pure meditation, like a roar of blazing fire, and they fall and are surrounded by his foot-soldiers.","n":"சுக்கிலத் தியானம் is the named Jain 'white' meditation, the last of the four meditations; the verse gives it as a weapon. The 'feeble lords of knowledge, sight and attainment' are the obstructing karmas, which I read from the Jain scheme rather than from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","arivu","pagai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3081","lb":"CC3081","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan fires the arrow of pure meditation and the remaining foes fall.","cl":[["the highest meditation, the pure white meditation (sukla dhyana), destroys the last of the binding karmas","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3082":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3082,"t":["காதிப்போர் மன்னர் வீழக் கணையெரி சிதறி வெய்யோ","னோதிய வகையி னொன்றி யுலகுச்சி முளைத்த தேபோல்","வீதிபோ யுலக மூன்றும் விழுங்கியிட் டலோக நுங்கி","யாதியந் தகன்ற நான்மைக் கொடியெடுத் திறைமை கொண்டான்."],"p":"","r":"When the four destroying kings have fallen, he sprouts like the sun at the world's summit, the fire of his arrows spreading, swallows the three worlds and then the world of non-world, and lifts the banner of the fourfold infinite, taking sovereignty.","n":"காதிப்போர் மன்னர் are the four ghati karmas, those that obscure knowledge, sight, bliss and power; the verse's 'fourfold' (நான்மை) is the four infinites listed in 2847. The second line is difficult and I read it as the sun rising from the world's top; it is the moment the work calls attaining the supreme knowledge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","arivu","iraivan","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3082","lb":"CC3082","mm":1,"ev":"Civakan destroys the four destroying karmas and attains omniscience, taking sovereignty over the three worlds.","cl":[["when the four destroying karmas fall, the soul knows all three worlds and the non-world (aloka) and holds the four infinites","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3083":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3083,"t":["பசும்பொனி னுலகிற் றேவர் பயிர்வளை முரச மார்ப்ப","வசும்புசேர் களிறு திண்டே ரலைமணிப் புரவி வேங்கை","விசும்பியங் கரியோ டாளி விடைமயி லன்ன நாக","நயந்தவை பிறவு மூர்ந்து நாதன்றாள் கோயில் கொண்டார்."],"p":"","r":"The gods in the golden world beat the drums that roar, bringing elephants, chariots, horses, lions, peacocks and nagas, and take their place in the temple of the lord's feet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3083","lb":"CC3083","mm":1,"ev":"The gods bring their mounts and gather at the Lord's temple to worship.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3084":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3084,"t":["நறுமலர் மாலை சாந்தம் பரூஉத்துளித் துவலை நன்னீர்க்","கறைமுகில் சொரியக் காய்பொற் கற்பக மாலை யேந்திச்","சிறகுறப் பரப்பி யன்னம் பறப்பன போல வீண்டி","நிறைகடல் விஞ்சை வேந்தர் நீணில மன்னர் சேர்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Vidyadhara kings and kings of the earth come, as swans spread their wings, bearing fragrant flowers, garlands, sandal, and bright water from dark clouds, and the heavenly wishing-tree garlands.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3084","lb":"CC3084","mm":1,"ev":"The Vidyadhara and earth kings arrive with offerings.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சை வேந்தர்","நீணில மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3085":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3085,"t":["விண்ணியங் கருக்கன் வீழ்ந்து மீனிலங் கொள்வ தேபோன்","மண்ணெலாம் பைம்பொன் மாரி மலர்மழை சொரிந்து வாழ்த்தி","யெண்ணிலாத் தொழில்க டோற்றி யிந்திரர் மருள வாடிக்","கண்முழு துடம்பிற் றோன்றிச் சுதஞ்சணன் களிப்புற் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Like the sun falling from the sky to take the earth, the god Sudanjanan scatters a rain of gold and flowers, blesses, shows endless dances to make the Indras marvel, and is glad, his body full of eyes.","n":"Sudanjanan is the god who in earlier ilampakams had been the yaksha rescued by Civakan and acted as his friend and helper; the verse does not say so and the reader must carry that from earlier. The body of eyes is my reading of கண் முழுது உடம்பிற் றோன்றி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3085","lb":"CC3085","mm":1,"ev":"The god Sudanjanan, once Civakan's friend, rains gold and dances before the Lord.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதஞ்சணன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3086":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3086,"t":["குளித்தெழு வயிர முத்தத் தொத்தெரி கொண்டு மின்ன","வளித்துல கோம்பு மாலை யகன்குடை கவித்த தாங்கு","வளிப்பொர வுளருந் திங்கட் கதிரெனக் கவரி பொங்கத்","தெளித்துவில் லுமிழுஞ் செம்பொ னாசனஞ் சேர்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The golden seat comes with the umbrella shining like a jewelled canopy, the fans flowing like moonlight; the seat that pours its brightness is reached.","n":"The umbrella, fans and seat are the royal insignia given to the Arhat; the preacher is thus given the royal regalia of the king that he put off, which the verse accepts without comment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3086","lb":"CC3086","mm":1,"ev":"The Lord's golden seat, with umbrella and fans, is set up in the assembly.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3087":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3087,"t":["மணியுமிழ் திருக்கேசம் வானவர கிற்புகையும்","பிணியவிழ்ந்த கற்பகமும் பெயர்ந்தோடக் கமழமா","றுணியரு வினையெறிந்தாற் கதுநாற்றஞ் சொல்லலா","மணிதிக ழரசுவா வதன்கடாஞ் சாற்றாதோ."],"p":"","r":"The scent of his jewelled hair, of the gods' incense and the Kalpaka garland that had wandered away is surpassed by the scent of the one who cut the deeds that could not be cut; if the royal ... does not tell it, who will?","n":"The last line is corrupt, the word அரசுவா is not a word I can read, and the sense of the closing rhetorical question is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3087","lb":"CC3087","mm":1,"ev":"The scent of the Lord's body surpasses all heavenly perfumes.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3088":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3088,"t":["முழங்குதிரு மணிமுறுவன் முருக்கிதழ் கொடிப்பவளத்","தழக்குரல்வாய் தளையவிழ்ந்த மந்தாரந் தவநாறு","மழுங்கல் சூழ் வினைவெறுத்தாற் கதுநாற்ற மறியலாம்","வழங்குபொன் வரைவளரும் பைங்கண்மா வுரையாதோ."],"p":"","r":"The Lord's shining teeth, coral lips and the sound of his voice outshine the Mandara tree; his scent is known as that of the one who hates the deeds, and the green-eyed one (a lion or elephant) of the golden hill cannot say it.","n":"The last line names a 'green-eyed great one' (பைங்கண்மா) whom I read as a beast of the golden mountain; the sense is the rhetorical question again, that even the best of beasts cannot speak of it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3088","lb":"CC3088","mm":1,"ev":"The splendour of the Lord's body is praised above the heavenly trees.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3089":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3089,"t":["உறுப்பெலா மொளியுமிழ்ந் துணர்வரிதா யிருசுடருங்","குறைத்தடுக்கிக் குவித்ததோர் குன்றேபோன் றிலங்குமால்","வெறுத்திரு வினையுதிர்த்தாற் கதுவண்ணம் விளம்பலாங்","கறுப்பொழிந்த கனையெரிவாய்க் காரிரும்பே கரியன்றே."],"p":"","r":"His whole body gives out light too bright to perceive, like a hill made of the sun and the moon gathered in; he who shook off the two deeds can be described, but is not the dark iron in a blazing fire black no longer?","n":"The last line is difficult. கறுப்பொழிந்த ... கரியன்றே I read as the comparison of iron which has lost its black in the fire; if so the Lord's colour is the glow of burnt-out karma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3089","lb":"CC3089","mm":1,"ev":"The Lord's body is shown shining beyond description.","cl":[["one who has shed the two kinds of deed shines with a light no eye can take in","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3090":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3090,"t":["வானோ ரேந்து மலர்மாரி வண்ணச்சாந்தம் பூஞ்சுண்ணங்","கனார் பிண்டிக் கமழ் தாமங் கறையார் முகிலி னிறங்காட்டுந்","தேனார் புகைக ளிவையெல்லாந் திகைப்பத் திகைகண் மணநாறி","யானா கமழுந் திருவவிப்போ தமரர்முடிமே லணிந்தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The flowers that the gods rain down, the coloured sandal, the flower dust, the pindi garlands and the incense that shows the colour of dark clouds fill the quarters with scent, and the gods wear the Lord's fragrance on their crowns.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3090","lb":"CC3090","mm":1,"ev":"The gods rain flowers and perfumes and wear the Lord's fragrance on their heads.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3091":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3091,"t":["சுறவுக்கொடிக் கடவுளொடு காலற்றொலைத் தோயெம்","பிறவியறு கென்றுபிற சிந்தை யிலராகி","நறவமலர் வேய்ந்துநறுஞ் சாந்துநில மெழுகித்","துறவுநெறிக் கடவுளடி தூம மொடுதொழுதார்."],"p":"","r":"The worshippers, with the god who bears the fish banner and Death slain, give up all other thought, pray 'let our birth end', and wearing flowers, sweeping the floor with sandal, worship the feet of the Lord of the path of renunciation with incense.","n":"சுறவுக்கொடிக் கடவுள் is Kaman, the god of desire whose banner is the fish; the Lord is praised for having destroyed him and Death, which makes the Arhat's victory a defeat of desire and mortality together. The verse is in a different metre from those before it, and 3091 to 3097 read as the worshippers' hymn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","piravi","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3091","lb":"CC3091","mm":1,"ev":"The worshippers pray for the end of birth and worship the Lord's feet.","cl":[["the Lord of renunciation destroyed Desire and Death, and worship of his feet is for the end of births","jain","asserted",1,"worshippers"]],"ag":["உறுவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3092":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3092,"t":["பாலனைய சிந்தைசுட ரப்படர்செய் காதி","நாலுமுட னேயரிந்து நான்மைவரம் பாகிக்","காலமொரு மூன்றுமுட னேயுணர்ந்த கடவுள்","கோலமலர்ச் சேவடிகள் கொண்டுதொழு தும்யாம்."],"p":"","r":"We worship the Lord who cut the four deeds at once, whose pure thought blazes, who knew the three times together, and whose feet are flowers, ends the four-fold ...","n":"Here the hymn first speaks as 'we' (தொழுதும் யாம்), a change of voice from the narrator of 3074 to 3091; I read the speaker as the congregation of worshippers. The line 'நான்மைவரம்பாகி' I read as 'become the limit of the fourfold', the four infinites.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3092","lb":"CC3092","mm":1,"ev":"A hymn in the first person praises the Lord's feet.","cl":[["the Lord cut the four destroying karmas together and knows the three times","jain","asserted",2,"worshippers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உறுவார்",""],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3093":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3093,"t":["முழங்குகட னெற்றிமுளைத் தெழுந்தசுடரேபோ","லழுங்கல்வினை யலறநிமிர்ந் தாங்குலக மூன்றும்","விழுங்கியுமி ழாதுகுணம் வித்தியிருந் தோய்நின்","னிழுங்கில்குணச் சேவடிக ளேத்தித்தொழ தும்யாம்."],"p":"","r":"Like the sun risen from the roaring sea, you rose against the wailing deeds, swallowing the three worlds without casting them out, the seed of qualities, and we praise your faultless feet.","n":"The 'swallowing the three worlds' repeats the image of 3082, now as praise: where the narrator told it as a victory, the hymn tells it as a quality of the Lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3093","lb":"CC3093","mm":1,"ev":"The worshippers praise the Lord for having risen like the sun over the deeds.","cl":[["the Lord knows the three worlds without being stained by them, sowing qualities in himself","jain","asserted",3,"worshippers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உறுவார்",""],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3094":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3094,"t":["ஏத்தரிய பல்குணங்கட் கெல்லைவரம் பாகி","நீத்தவரு ளிந்திரனை நின்று தொழு தமரர்","நாத்தழும்ப வேத்தித்தவ நங்கையவர் நண்ணித்","தோத்திரங்க ளோதித்துகண் மாசுதுணிக் கின்றார்."],"p":"","r":"The gods stand and worship the Lord, praising him with tongues that grow numb, and the nuns of penance come and sing hymns to wash off the dust of the eyes, praising the one who has left the unpraiseable qualities beyond limit.","n":"The nuns of penance appear here in the same assembly as the gods; the verse gives women a place in the hymn of the Lord, which earlier ilampakams had also done by sending the queens to the nun's life.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thavam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3094","lb":"CC3094","mm":1,"ev":"Gods and nuns praise the Lord with hymns.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யர்","தவ நங்கை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3095":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3095,"t":["செய்தவ னேவினை சேரு மதற்கெனு","மையமின் றாயலர் தாமரை மேலடி","மொய்ம்மலர் தூய்முனி யாது வணங்குது","மெய்யுல கிற்கு விளம்பிய வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Worship of the Lord's lotus feet, with no doubt that the doer of penance gets the deed's fruit, and the speaker salutes with pure flowers, 'the king who spoke of the true world'.","n":"The line 'செய்தவனே வினை சேரும் அதற்கு' reads as 'the doer is reached by the deed for it'; the whole verse is a salutation closing in the form of a later benediction, and its metre differs from the narrative verses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3095","lb":"CC3095","mm":1,"ev":"A worshipper salutes the Lord's feet and states the law of deeds.","cl":[["the doer of the deed receives its fruit, without doubt","jain","asserted",1,"worshippers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உறுவார்",""],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3096":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3096,"t":["நல்லன வேயென நாடி யோர்புடை","யல்லன வேயறை கின்றபுன் னாதர்கள்","பல்வினைக் கும்முலைத் தாய்பயந் தாரவர்","சொல்லுவ நீசுக தாவுரை யாயே."],"p":"","r":"The petty lords who seek out only the unsound as good, and speak what is not good, have borne the mother who bears many deeds; speak, Sugata, of what they say.","n":"சுகதா (Sugata) is a name the Buddhists use for the Buddha; here a Jain hymn addresses it to the Lord, and either the title is common to all the teachers who have reached release or the verse is borrowed. The line 'பல்வினைக்கும்முலைத் தாய்பயந்தாரவர்' is difficult.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","vinai","nindhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3096","lb":"CC3096","mm":1,"ev":"A worshipper asks the Lord to speak against the false teachers.","cl":[["teachers who speak what is not good are mothers who give birth to many deeds","general","reported",2,"worshippers"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["உறுவார்",""],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3097":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3097,"t":["மதியறி யாக்குணத் தோனடி வாழ்த்தி","நிதியறை போனிறைந் தார்நிக ரில்லாத்","துதியறை யாத்தொழு தார்மலர் சிந்தா","விதியறி யும்படி வீரனை மாதோ."],"p":"","r":"Having praised the feet of the one whose virtue the mind cannot know, those who worship the unbroken praise bow, filled like a treasure house, so that the heroes know the fate that does not fall.","n":"The last verse of the ilampakam as numbered here closes in praise; the grammar of the last two lines is hard and I give only the gist.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3097","lb":"CC3097","mm":1,"ev":"The worshippers conclude their praise and bow.","cl":[],"ag":["உறுவார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:131"},"cc:3098":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3098,"t":["தீவினைக் குழவி செற்ற மெனும்பெயர்ச் செவிலி கையுள்","வீவினை யின்றிக் காம முலையுண்டு வளர்ந்து வீங்கித்","தாவினை யின்றி வெந்நோய்க் கதிகளுட் டவழு மென்ற","கோவினை யன்றி யெந்நாக் கோதையர்க் கூற லுண்டே"],"p":"","r":"The women say that the bad deed is a child handed to a nurse named anger, which drinks the breast of desire, grows, and crawls among the painful states of rebirth, and that they have no word of their own beyond the king who told it so.","n":"Stanza printed in eight half-lines and joined by the edition; the last line's grammar is hard and the speaker is inferred from the 'we' of the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3098","lb":"CC3098","mm":1,"ev":"The wives repeat the king's parable of the bad-deed child and its nurse.","cl":[["a bad deed is like a child nursed by anger and fed on desire that then wanders among painful births","jain","asserted",1,"civakan_wives"]],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3099":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3099,"t":["நல்வினைக் குழவி நன்னீர்த் தயாவெனுஞ் செவிலி நாளும்","புல்லிக்கொண் டெடுப்பப் பொம்மென் மணிமுலை கவர்ந்து வீங்கிச்","செல்லுமாற் றேவர் கோவா யெனுமிருள் கழிந்த சொல்லா","வல்லிமே னடந்த கோவே யச்சத்து ணீங்கி னோமே."],"p":"","r":"The good deed is a child nursed by the nurse called compassion, who embraces and raises it, and the women say that, freed from the darkness of 'be our king among the gods', they have gone beyond fear.","n":"The pairing of two nurses, anger and compassion, makes the two kinds of deed two upbringings rather than two amounts; the verse does not say how one becomes the other's nurse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arul","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3099","lb":"CC3099","mm":1,"ev":"The wives answer the parable with its counterpart of the good-deed child and declare themselves free of fear.","cl":[["a good deed is a child nursed by compassion that lifts the soul upward","jain","asserted",1,"civakan_wives"]],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3100":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3100,"t":["மணியினுக் கொளியக மலர்க்கு மல்கிய","வணியமை யங்குளிர் வாச மல்லதூஉந்","திணியிமி லேற்றினுக் கொதுக்கஞ் செல்வநின்","னிணைமலர்ச் சேவடி கொடுத்த வென்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Beyond the light a gem gives and the cool scent a flower gives, the feet are said to give shelter to the dense gnat-swarm of the crowd, and the verse addresses the Lord's red feet that gave this.","n":"The addressee is not named in the verse; 'the Lord of wealth' is read as the Jina from the surrounding hymn.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3100","lb":"CC3100","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina's feet shelter all who come to them, beyond what any pleasure of the senses gives","jain","asserted",3,"civakan_wives"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","வாமன்"],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3101":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3101,"t":["இகலிருண் முழுமுத றுமிய வீண்டுநீர்ப்","பகல்சுமந் தெழுதரும் பருதி யன்னநின்","னிகலிரு மரைமல ரளித்த சேவடி","தொகலருங் கருவினை துணிக்கு மெஃகமே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord's feet, like the sun that rises bearing the day to break the dark at its root, are a spear that cuts the hard-gathered deeds.","n":"The feet are a weapon rather than a giver of grace: they cut karma as a spear cuts, which differs from a god who forgives it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3101","lb":"CC3101","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["worship of the Jina's feet cuts through accumulated karma","jain","asserted",4,"civakan_wives"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மயிலனார்","வாமன்"],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3102":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3102,"t":["மீன்றயங்கு திங்கண் முகநெடுங்கண் மெல்லியலார்","தேன்றயங்கு செந்நாவிற் சின்மென் கிளிக்கிளவி","வான்றயங்கு வாமன் குணம்பாட வாழியரோ","கான்றயங்கி நில்லா கருவினைகாற் பெய்தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The slender women of moon faces and parrot voices are told to sing the Lord's qualities, for the deeds that fell on them have been stopped and do not stay.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3102","lb":"CC3102","mm":1,"ev":"The women sing the qualities of the Lord and their karma ceases to settle.","cl":[["singing the Jina's qualities stops karma from settling","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3103":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3103,"t":["மதியம்பொழி தீங்கதிர்கள் பருகிமல ராம்பல்","பொதியவிழ்ந்து தேன்றுளிப்ப போன்றுபொரு வில்லார்","விதியிற்களித் தாரறிவன் விழுக்குணங்க ளேத்தித்","துதியிற்றெழு தார்துளங்கு முள்ளமது நீத்தார்."],"p":"","r":"As the night-lily opens drinking the moon's rays and drips honey, so those who hail the qualities of the one of perfect knowledge, with no wavering mind, are said to leave their shaking hearts behind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","manam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3103","lb":"CC3103","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["praise of the omniscient one removes the wavering of the mind","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3104":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3104,"t":["ஆர்ந்தகுணச் செல்வனடித் தாமரைக ளேத்திச்","சேர்ந்துதவ வீரர்திசை சிலம்பத் துதியோதித்","தூர்ந்தவிரு டுணிக்குஞ்சுடர் தொழுதருளு கென்றார்","கூர்ந்தமிழ்த மாரியெனக் கொற்றவனுஞ் சொன்னான்."],"p":"","r":"Having worshipped the feet of the Lord of fullness in company with the ascetic heroes, the women ask to worship the light that cuts the dark, and the king gives his answer like a rain of nectar.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3104","lb":"CC3104","mm":1,"ev":"The wives ask to worship the Lord and the king answers them.","cl":[],"ag":["மயிலனார்","சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3105":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3105,"t":["இன்பமற் றென்னும் பேரா னெழுந்தபுற் கற்றை தீற்றித்","துன்பத்தைச் சுரக்கு நான்கு கதியெனுந் தொழுவிற் றோன்றி","நின்றபற் றார்வ நீக்கி நிருமலன் பாதஞ் சேரி","னன்புவிற் றுண்டு போகிச் சிவகதி யடைய லாமே."],"p":"","r":"The king says that desire is a bundle of grass that tempts souls into the four states of rebirth, a pen that yields suffering, and that to give up clinging and reach the stainless one's feet is to sell love for the road to the state of release.","n":"The four states (gods, humans, animals and hell) are not listed in the verse; they are supplied from the Jain scheme the word implies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","veedu","pattru","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3105","lb":"CC3105","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["clinging draws the soul into the four states of rebirth, and giving it up at the Jina's feet leads to release","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மயிலனார்"],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3106":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3106,"t":["வாட்கையம் மைந்த ராயும் வனமுலை மகளி ராயும்","வேட்கையை மிகுத்து வித்திப் பிறவிநோய் விளைத்து வீயாத்","தேட்கையிற் கொண்ட தொக்கு நிச்சநோய்ச் செற்றப் புன்றோற்","பூட்கையை முனியின் வாமன் பொன்னடி தொழுமி னென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"The king tells them to worship the golden feet and to hate the strong young men and the full-breasted women who sow desire and reap the disease of birth, since the body so kept is a skin that holds disease.","n":"The king speaks as a ruler who has just given up many wives; the 'hate the women' language is the sermon's, addressed to women who are about to renounce.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3106","lb":"CC3106","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["desire for the body is the seed from which the disease of birth grows","jain","asserted",2,"civakan"],["the body kept for pleasure is only a bundle of disease and hatred","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மயிலனார்"],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3107":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3107,"t":["தன்னுயிர் தான்பரிந் தோம்பு மாறுபோன்","மன்னுயிர் வைகலு மோம்பி வாழுமே","லின்னுயிர்க் கிறைவனா யின்ப மூர்த்தியாய்ப்","பொன்னுயி ராய்ப்பிறந் துயர்ந்து போகுமே."],"p":"","r":"If a person protects living beings every day as he guards his own life, he will be born lord of sweet lives, an image of joy, a golden soul that rises beyond.","n":"The reward is rank in a later birth, which makes non-injury a ladder rather than an end; Kural 327 sets the same rule against the loss of one's own life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","arul","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3107","lb":"CC3107","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one who protects all lives as one's own is reborn in a higher state","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மயிலனார்"],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3108":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3108,"t":["நெருப்புயிர்க் காக்கிநோய் செய்யி னிச்சமும்","முருப்புயி ரிருவினை யுதைப்ப வீழ்ந்தபின்","புரிப்புரிக் கொண்டுபோய்ப் பொரிந்து சுட்டிட","விருப்புயி ராகிவெந் தெரியுள் வீழுமே."],"p":"","r":"If a person makes fire a life for food and does harm, the two deeds kick him down and the fire of the world draws him in, roasts him and sends him into burning flame.","n":"The first two lines are hard (the nouns turn on a word 'நெருப்புயிர்'); the sense of harm for food is taken from the verbs, so the image of harm by fire is the better-supported reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kollaamai","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3108","lb":"CC3108","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one who harms living beings for his food is cast down by karma into a burning hell","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மயிலனார்"],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3109":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3109,"t":["மழைக்குர லுருமுவா வோத மாக்கடற்","பிழைத்ததோ ரருமணி பெற்ற தொக்குமாற்","குழைத்தலைப் பிண்டியான் குளிர்கொ ணல்லறந்","தழைத்தலைச் சந்தனப் பொதும்பர்ச் சார்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The teaching under the pindi tree is like a rare gem got by one who escaped the great sea of roaring waves, and the good law has taken refuge in a shaded grove of sandal.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piravi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3109","lb":"CC3109","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["the Jina's law is a rare gem to one who has escaped the sea of birth","jain","asserted",3,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மயிலனார்"],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3110":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3110,"t":["மல்குபூங் கற்பக மரத்தி னீழலா","னல்குவா னொருவனை நயந்து நாடுமோ","பில்குபூம் பிண்டியா னமிர்துண் டார்பிறர்","செல்வங் கண்டதற் கவாச்சிந்தை செய்யுமோ."],"p":"","r":"One who lives in the shade of the wishing-tree will not seek another to give to him, and one who has eaten the nectar under the pindi will not set his mind on the wealth of others.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","avaa","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3110","lb":"CC3110","mm":1,"ev":"","cl":[["one who has had the Jina's teaching no longer craves other people's wealth","jain","asserted",4,"civakan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மயிலனார்"],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3111":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3111,"t":["மணியுயிர் பொன்னுயிர் மாண்ட வெள்ளியி","னணியுயிர் செம்புயி ரிரும்பு போலவாம்","பிணியுயி ரிறுதியாப் பேசி னேனினித்","துணிமின மெனத்தொழு திறைஞ்சி வாழ்த்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"The king grades souls by metals, from gem and gold through silver and copper to iron, and says he has told this to the end of disease, and urges them to decide, whereupon the women worship and bless him.","n":"The metal grades are given as a list of ranks; the verse does not say which deeds put a soul at which rank.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3111","lb":"CC3111","mm":1,"ev":"The king ends the sermon and the women bow and bless him.","cl":[["souls differ in worth like metals according to the deeds that bind them","jain","asserted",1,"civakan"]],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சீவகன்","மயிலனார்"],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3112":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3112,"t":["விண்ணின்மேன் மலர்மழை பொழிய வீங்குபாற்","றெண்ணிலாத் திருமதி சொரியத் தேமலர்","மண்ணின்மேன் மழகதிர் நடப்ப தொத்ததே","யண்ணலா ருலாய்நிமிர்ந் தளித்த வண்ணமே."],"p":"","r":"Flowers rain from the sky, a full moon seems to pour milk, and honey blossoms seem to walk on the earth as the Lord walks and grants his sight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3112","lb":"CC3112","mm":1,"ev":"The Lord walks the earth and the heavens shower flowers on him.","cl":[],"ag":["வாமன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3113":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3113,"t":["பான்மிடை யமிர்து போன்று பருகலாம் பயத்த வாகி","வானிடை முழக்கிற் கூறி வாலற வமிர்த மூட்டித்","தேனுடை மலர்கள் சிந்தித் திசைதொழச் சென்ற பின்னாட்","டானுடை யுலகங் கொள்ளச் சாமிநாள் சார்ந்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord's teaching, like milk and nectar, sounded in the sky and fed the pure nectar, and flowers were scattered and the directions bowed, and then the day came for him to take the world he had earned.","n":"'சாமிநாள்' is read as the day of final release from the line that follows it; the verse itself does not say the word for death.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","arul","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3113","lb":"CC3113","mm":1,"ev":"After his last sermon the Lord reaches the day of his passing.","cl":[],"ag":["வாமன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3114":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3114,"t":["உழவித்தி யுறுதி கொள்வார் கொண்டுய்யப் போகல் வேண்டித்","தொழுவித்தி யறத்தை வைத்துத் துளங்கிமி லேறு சேர்ந்த","குழவித்தண் டிங்க ளன்ன விருக்கைய னாகிக் கோமான்","விழவித்தாய் வீடு பெற்றான் விளங்கிநால் வினையும் வென்றே."],"p":"","r":"To make those who want gain take their lasting good, the lord set virtue and sat, like the young cool moon, on a seat of his own, and having conquered the four karmas he reached release in festival.","n":"'Four karmas' is read as the four that remain after the omniscient one has gone; the verse does not name them. The subject 'kōmān' is the Lord and not Civakan, who comes next.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","vinai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3114","lb":"CC3114","mm":1,"ev":"The Lord, having conquered four karmas, reaches release.","cl":[["conquest of the four binding karmas is what leads to release","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["வாமன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3115":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3115,"t":["துந்துபி கறங்க வார்த்துத் துகிற்கொடி நுடங்க வேந்தி","யந்தரம் விளங்க வெங்கு மணிகமூர்ந் தமர ரீண்டி","வந்துபொன் மாரி சிந்தி மலர்மழை சொரிந்து சாந்துங்","கெந்தநா றகிலுங் கூட்டிக் கிளர்முடி யுறுத்தி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"Gods gather with drums beating and cloth banners waving, pour a golden rain and flowers, mix sandal and aloe fragrance and crown the Lord.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3115","lb":"CC3115","mm":1,"ev":"The gods honour the Lord's passing with rain of gold, flowers and perfume.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3116":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3116,"t":["முளைத்தெழு பருதி மொய்கொண் முழங்கழற் குளித்த தேபோற்","றிளைத்தெழு கொடிகள் செந்தீத் திருமணி யுடம்பு நுங்க","விளைத்தபின் விண்ணு மண்ணு மங்கலம் வகையிற் செய்து","வளைப்பொலி கடலி னார்த்து வலங்கொண்டு நடந்த வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The flags seem to be swallowed by the red flame, as the rising sun is by the fire, as the bright jewelled body is burnt, and then gods and earth do their auspicious rites, circle the pyre and move away like a shell-loud sea.","n":"The verse does not say whose body it is; the context of the Lord's release in 3114 supports the Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","iraivan","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3116","lb":"CC3116","mm":1,"ev":"The jewel-bright body of the Lord burns in the flame, and the gods circle it and go away.","cl":[],"ag":["அமரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3117":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3117,"t":["கேவல மடந்தை யென்னுங் கேழ்கிளர் நெடிய வாட்கட்","பூவலர் முல்லைக் கண்ணிப் பொன்னொரு பாக மாகக்","காவலன் றானொர் கூறாக் கண்ணிமை யாது புல்லி","மூவுல குச்சி யின்பக் கடலினுண் மூழ்கி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The king, whose bright eyes are those of a maiden named Kevala, the knowledge that sees all, embraces her with unblinking eyes, the garland of jasmine on one side and gold on the other, and sinks into a sea of pleasure at the peak of the three worlds.","n":"The grammar makes the king's identity open: he could be the Lord or Civakan; the book's last ilampakam and 3118 let the reader take Civakan. The maiden is a figure of knowledge and no real woman.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","inbam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3117","lb":"CC3117","mm":1,"ev":"The king, now omniscient, embraces the maiden Kevala and sinks in the sea of joy at the top of the three worlds.","cl":[["omniscience is a bliss at the height of the three worlds, pictured as the embrace of a maiden","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்","கேவல மடந்தை"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3118":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3118,"t":["பிரிதலும் பிணியு மூப்புஞ் சாதலும் பிறப்பு மில்லா","வரிவையைப் புல்லி யம்பொ னணிகிளர் மாடத் தின்றேன்","சொரிமது மாலை சாந்தங் குங்குமஞ் சுண்ணந் தேம்பாய்","விரிபுகை விளக்கு விண்ணோ ரேந்தமற் றுறையு மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Embracing the woman who has no parting, disease, old age, death or birth, he lives in a tall gold mansion where gods carry wine, garlands, sandal, saffron, powder, smoke and lamps.","n":"The verse mixes heaven's service (wine, garlands) with release; it does not separate the god's heaven from the freed soul's state, and 'இன்றேன்' in line 2 looks corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","inbam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3118","lb":"CC3118","mm":1,"ev":"He lives with the maiden free of ageing and death, served by gods.","cl":[["the liberated state has no parting, disease, old age, death or birth","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["சீவகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3119":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3119,"t":["வல்லவன் வடித்த வேல்போன் மலர்ந்துநீண் டகன்ற வாட்கண்","மெல்லவே யுறவி யோம்பி யொதுங்கியு மிருந்து நின்று","முல்லையஞ் சூட்டு வேயின் முரிந்துபோ நுசுப்பி னல்லார்","மல்லற்குன் றேந்தி யன்ன மாதவ முற்றி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"The women of spear-bright wide eyes, who walked softly, sat and stood like jasmine crests, with bending waists, reached the great austerity, like one holding up a mountain.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3119","lb":"CC3119","mm":1,"ev":"The women take up great austerity.","cl":[["the women's renunciation is a great effort like holding up a mountain","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3120":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3120,"t":["சூழ்பொற் பாவையைச் சூழ்ந்து புல்லிய","காழகப் பச்சை போன்று கண்டெறூஉ","மாழை நோக்கினார் மேனி மாசுகொண்","டேழைப் பெண்பிறப் பிடியச் சிந்தித்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Looking at the bodies they had adorned, as one looks at a green bracelet clinging to a gold doll, the women see the dirt on them and think how to crush the poor birth of woman.","n":"The poem presents this as the women's own thought, but the narrator's phrase 'ஏழைப் பெண்பிறப்பு' (the poor birth of woman) passes the verdict on women's birth as the work's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thuravu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3120","lb":"CC3120","mm":1,"ev":"The women see the stain on their own bodies and resolve to end birth as women.","cl":[["a woman's body is stained and her birth lowly, to be ended by renunciation","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3121":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3121,"t":["ஆசை யார்வமோ டைய மின்றியே","யோசை போயுல குண்ண நோற்றிபி","னேசு பெண்ணொழித் திந்தி ரர்களாய்த்","தூய ஞானமாய்த் துறக்க மெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"Without desire, love or doubt, the women, who did their austerity until the sound of the world ate it, cast off the despised state of woman, became Indras, pure knowledge, and reached heaven.","n":"Heaven (துறக்கம்) is the end named, not release; the poem's women reach the gods' world, one step below the state the king reached in 3117-3118.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","veedu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3121","lb":"CC3121","mm":1,"ev":"The women, having shed their womanhood by austerity, are reborn as Indras and reach heaven.","cl":[["austerity ends the birth of woman and brings a man's state in heaven","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மயிலனார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:132"},"cc:3122":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3122,"t":["காம வல்லிகள் கலந்து புல்லிய","பூமென் கற்பகப் பொன்ம ரங்கள்போற்","றாம வார்குழற் றைய லார்முலை","யேம மாகிய வின்ப மெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"Men embrace the long-haired women as wishing-trees are wrapped in creepers of desire, and reach the pleasure that is the comfort of their breasts.","n":"The verse names no one; that these are the hero's household is carried from the ilampakam, not from the words. The simile of the heavenly wishing-tree already puts the bed in the language of paradise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3122","lb":"CC3122","mm":1,"ev":"The lovers of the court lie in each other's arms and take their pleasure.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர் ஆடவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3123":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3123,"t":["கலவி யாகிய காமத் தின்பயன்","புலவி யாதலாற் பொன்னங் கொம்பனா","ருலவு கண்மல ரூடற் செவ்விநோக்","கிலைகொள் பூணினா ரிதயம் போழ்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The fruit of the pleasure of union is the lovers' quarrel; the wet-eyed glances of the golden-boughed women pierce the hearts of the men in their leaf-patterned jewels.","n":"Union and sulking are set as seed and fruit, so the quarrel is a part of the pleasure and not its break.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3123","lb":"CC3123","mm":1,"ev":"The women sulk and their glances pierce the men's hearts.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர் ஆடவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3124":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3124,"t":["பூவி னுள்ளவள் புகுந்து முள்ளத்தா","ணாவிற் பெண்பெயர் நிவிற்றி னீரெனக்","காவிக் கண்கடை யிடுகக் காற்சிலம்","பாவித் தார்த்தன வம்மென் குஞ்சியே."],"p":"","r":"The lily-eyed women cast reproachful side-glances while their anklets ring and the men's hair is stirred; the play of the lovers' quarrel goes on.","n":"The first two lines turn on a clause (பெண்பெயர் நிவிற்றினீர்) that I cannot parse with confidence; the reading rests on lines 3 and 4 only.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3124","lb":"CC3124","mm":1,"ev":"The women reproach the men in play, with glances and sounding anklets.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர் ஆடவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3125":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3125,"t":["நெஞ்சி னேரிழை வருந்து மென்றுபூங்","குஞ்சி யேற்றது குறிக்கொ ணீயெனாப்","பஞ்சின் மெல்லடிப் பாவை பூநுதா","லஞ்சி னார்க்கதோர் தவற தாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"A soft-footed, flower-browed woman fears that the flowers set in the man's hair have pained the lady's heart, and the verse calls such fear a fault of its own.","n":"Who is meant by 'the lady' and whose fault the last line names are not fixed by the words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3125","lb":"CC3125","mm":1,"ev":"A woman frets that she has hurt another's feelings with the flowers in a man's hair.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர் ஆடவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3126":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3126,"t":["தவளைக் கிண்கிணித் தாமஞ் சேர்த்தியுங்","குவளைக் கண்மலர்க் கோலம் வாழ்த்தியு","மிவளைக் கண்டகண் ணிமைக்கு மோவெனாத்","திவளத் தேமலர்க் கண்ணி சேர்த்தியும்."],"p":"","r":"The lovers fasten the white anklet strings, praise the lily-eyed beauty and set a honeyed wreath on her, asking whether eyes that saw her could ever blink.","n":"The sentence is carried by three participles and ends in verse 3127, so the last line is read as an offering of the wreath; this is the less certain half.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3126","lb":"CC3126","mm":1,"ev":"The men dress the women and praise their eyes.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர் ஆடவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3127":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3127,"t":["பன்ம ணிக்கதிர்ப் பரவை மேகலை","மின்ன ணிந்துகத் திருத்தி வெம்முலைப்","பொன்ன ணிந்துபூஞ் சுண்ணந் தைவர","நன்ம ணிக்குழை யிரண்டு நக்கவே."],"p":"","r":"The women, bright as lightning, put on girdles of many gems, settle them, brush pollen powder over their golden breasts, and their two gem earrings seem to laugh.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3127","lb":"CC3127","mm":1,"ev":"The women adorn themselves after love.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர் ஆடவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3128":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3128,"t":["செய்த நீர்மையார் செயப்பட் டார்கடா","மெய்தி யாவையும் முணர்க வென்பபோன்","மைய வாங்குழன் மடந்தை குண்டல","நைய நின்றெலா நாண நக்கவே."],"p":"","r":"The earrings of the dark-haired woman, shaking, seem to laugh so that all feel shame, as if saying that those who did the deed and those it was done to will each come to know everything.","n":"The saying ascribed to the earrings echoes a wording of deed and its object (செய்த, செயப்பட்டார்), which a reader may take as a faint hint at karma; the verse does not draw the doctrine out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3128","lb":"CC3128","mm":1,"ev":"The woman's earrings shake in laughter and shame those present.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர் ஆடவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3129":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3129,"t":["செல்வக் கிண்கிணி சிலம்பத் தேன்சொரி","முல்லைக் கண்ணிகள் சிந்த மொய்ந்நலம்","புல்லிப் பூண்டதார் புரள மேகலை","யல்குல் வாய்திறந் தாவித் தார்த்தவே."],"p":"","r":"The anklets sound, the jasmine garlands scatter, the worn wreaths roll about, and the girdles on the women's hips gape and gasp in the heat of love.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3129","lb":"CC3129","mm":1,"ev":"The lovers' union breaks the women's ornaments.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர் ஆடவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3130":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3130,"t":["இலங்கு கொம்பனார் காம மென்னும்பேர்","கலந்த கள்ளினைக் கைசெய் தையென","மலர்ந்து வாய்வைத்தார் மணிகொள் வள்ளத்தே","நலங்கொள் சாயலார் நடுங்கி நையவே."],"p":"","r":"The slender women take up, as if it were a drink named desire mixed with toddy, a gem cup and drink, trembling and wilting.","n":"Desire is given the name of the drink itself; the drink is a figure for the lovers' pleasure and is not a separate scene of drinking.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3130","lb":"CC3130","mm":1,"ev":"The women drink from gem cups and tremble.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர் ஆடவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3131":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3131,"t":["வெம்மை கொண்டதே னமிர்த மெல்லவே","யம்மை யஞ்சொலா ரார வுண்டவர்","தம்மைத் தாமகிழ்ந் துறைய வித்தலைச்","செம்மை மாதவர்க் குற்ற செப்புவாம்."],"p":"","r":"After the women of soft speech have drunk to the full the honeyed nectar of heat and lived on rejoicing, the narrator turns to tell what befell the righteous ascetics.","n":"The turn from the bed to the ascetics (செம்மை மாதவர்) is the hinge of the chapter: the poet gives no argument, he lays the two lives side by side.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thuravu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3131","lb":"CC3131","mm":1,"ev":"The narrator closes the scene of pleasure and turns to the ascetics.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3132":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3132,"t":["நாள்கண்கூடிய நகைவெண் டிங்கள்போற்","காளை நந்தனுந் தோழன் மார்களு","நாளு நாளினு நடுங்க நற்றவந்","தாளி னீட்டினார் தம்மைத் தாம்பெற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Like the white moon on a full-moon day, the youth Nandan and his companions, while each day others tremble, pile up good penance by their own effort and gain themselves.","n":"That 'the youth' (காளை) is Civakan and not a second person cannot be told from the words; Nandan is named, and the group is read as ascetics only from the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3132","lb":"CC3132","mm":1,"ev":"Nandan and his companions practise penance.","cl":[["a man gains himself by the penance he piles up with his own effort","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["நந்தன்","தோழன்மார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3133":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3133,"t":["பாவ னைமாபுஇப் பட்டினி யொடுந்","தீவினை கழுஉந் தீர்த்தன் வந்தியாப்","பூவுண் வண்டெனக் கொட்பிற் புண்ணியர்","நாவின் வேட்கையு நஞ்சி னஞ்சினார்."],"p":"","r":"With meditation and fasting, which wash off evil deeds, and by reverence to the Teacher (தீர்த்தன்), the righteous move like bees on flowers and fear even the tongue's craving as they fear poison.","n":"தீர்த்தன் may be the Tirthankara or a bathing-place figure; the epic's Jain setting makes the first likelier.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3133","lb":"CC3133","mm":1,"ev":"The ascetics fast and meditate and fear the craving of the tongue.","cl":[["fasting joined to meditation washes off evil deeds","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"],["the craving of the tongue is to be feared as poison","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["தோழன்மார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3134":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3134,"t":["கருவிற் கட்டிய காலம் வந்தென","வுருவ வெண்பிறைக் கோட்டி னோங்கிய","வருவிக் குன்றின்மேன் முடித்திட் டைவருந்","திருவின் றோற்றம்போற் றேவ ராயினார்."],"p":"","r":"When the time bound in the womb at conception came, the five of them ended their lives on a mountain that rises like a white crescent horn, and became gods, as splendid as the appearance of Tiru.","n":"The ascetics' penance here ends in heaven, not yet in release; the verses after it spend seven stanzas on heavenly pleasure, which is a reward that the Jain scheme counts below release.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","marumai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3134","lb":"CC3134","mm":1,"ev":"The five ascetics die on a mountain and are reborn as gods.","cl":[["the span of a life is fixed at conception and ends when its time comes","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"],["penance ends in rebirth as a god","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["தேவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3135":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3135,"t":["அனங்கனைத் தவஞ்செய வழன்று கண்டவர்","மனங்களைக் கவர்ந்திடு மணிக்கண் வெம்முலைப்","பொனங்கொடி மயிலனார்ப் புல்ல மாப்பிடி","யினம்பயில் கடாக்களிற் றின்ப மெய்தினார்."],"p":"","r":"The gods who once burned in penance against the god of love now enjoy the dark-eyed, peacock-like women as bull elephants enjoy the herds of cow elephants.","n":"The first line is read as a reversal: those who fought desire through penance now live in it. The identification of the gods with the ascetics is from the sequence of verses, not from a word in this one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3135","lb":"CC3135","mm":1,"ev":"The new gods take their pleasure with the heavenly women.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3136":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3136,"t":["காதணிந்த தோடொருபான் மின்னு வீசக் குழையொருபாற் றிருவில் வீசத்","தாதணிந்த தாமங்க ளொருபாற் சோரத் தாமரைக்கண் டாமிரங்கப் புருவு மாட","மாதணிந்த நோக்கினா ரல்குற் காசு மணிமழலைக் கிண்கிணியுஞ் சிலம்பு மேங்கப்","போதணிந்த தாருடையப் பொருது பொங்கிப் புணர்முலைகள் போர்க்களந்தாங் கண்ட வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The heavenly women's earrings, bows of brows and garlands tremble and fall, their girdles and anklets ring, and the breasts meet like armies on the field of love.","n":"The battle of the breasts is the same figure that verse 3140 pursues with a weapon of sulking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3136","lb":"CC3136","mm":1,"ev":"The gods and the women join in love-play, imagined as a battle.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3137":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3137,"t":["முழுதார மின்னுமுலைக் குவட்டினான்மொய்ம் மார்பிற்குங் குமச்சே றிழுக்கிவீழ","வுழுதார்வம் வித்தி யுலப்பிலாத நுகர்ச்சிவிளைத் தலர்ந்தகற் பகத்தின்கீ","ழெழுதார் மணிக்குவளைக் கண்வலையுட்பட் டிமையார்கள் காமமறு சுழியுளாழ்ந்","திழுதார்மென் பள்ளிப்பூந் தாதுபொங்க விருவர் பலராகி யின்புறுபவே."],"p":"","r":"Saffron paste rubs off the breasts and chests, a plough of eager love sows a harvest of endless enjoyment, and the immortals sink in the whirlpool of desire, two becoming many in their pleasure.","n":"The gods are said to be caught in a net of desire and a whirlpool, images that elsewhere in the work belong to bondage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3137","lb":"CC3137","mm":1,"ev":"The gods lose themselves in love.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3138":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3138,"t":["மண்கனிந்த பொன்முழவ மழையின் விம்ம மாமணியாழ் தீங்குழல்க ளிரங்கப் பாண்டில்","பண்கனியப் பாவைமார் பைம்பொற் றோடுங் குண்டலமுந் தாம்பதைப்ப விருந்துபாட","விண்கனியக் கிண்கிணியுஞ் சிலம்பு மார்ப்ப முரிவுருவ வேனெடுங்கண் விருந்து செய்யக்","கண்கனிய நாடகங்கண் டமரர்காமக் கொழுந்தீன்று தந்தவந்தா மகிழ்ந்தா ரன்றே."],"p":"","r":"To the beat of drums, the lute, the flute and the dance, the gods feast their eyes on the women and take joy in the garden of desire.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3138","lb":"CC3138","mm":1,"ev":"The gods watch dance and music and are feasted by the women.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3139":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3139,"t":["முருகுடைந்த பூங்கோதை முத்தணிந்த தோளா","ரொருகுடங்கைக் கண்ணா லுளங்கிழிய வேவுண்","டருகடைந்த சாந்தழிய வம்முலைமேல் வீழ்ந்தார்","திருவடைந்த நீண்மார்பிற் றேந்துளிக்குந் தாரார்."],"p":"","r":"The women with flowers in their hair and pearls on their shoulders pierce the men's hearts with sidelong looks, and the men fall on their breasts, smearing the sandal paste.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3139","lb":"CC3139","mm":1,"ev":"The gods are pierced by glances and fall on the women's breasts.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3140":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3140,"t":["நிலவி யொளியுமிழு நீளிலைவேற் கண்ணார்","கலவித்தூ தாகிய காமக்கை காய்த்திப்","புலவிப் படைபயிலப் பூச்செய்த கோல","முலவித் துறக்க மொளிபூத்த தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The women's spear-bright eyes, with the hand of love as messenger, join in the sulking-war, and by their play the heaven of the gods shines.","n":"Love is a war here in which sulking is the arms and the hand is the messenger, a figure that borrows the vocabulary of kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3140","lb":"CC3140","mm":1,"ev":"The gods and women quarrel and make up in the play of love.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3141":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3141,"t":["புருவச் சிலைநுதற் பொன்றுஞ்சு மல்கு","லுருவத் துடியிடையா ருடலுப் பாகத்","திருவிற் றிகழ்காமத் தேன்பருகித் தேவர்","பொருவற் கரிய புலக்கடலு ளாழ்ந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"Drinking the honey of desire in the bodies of the slender-waisted women, the gods sink in a sea of senses beyond rival.","n":"The sinking (ஆழ்ந்தார்) is praise in the poet's mouth, but the sea of senses recalls the image of birth as a sea that the last verses name as a thing to cross.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3141","lb":"CC3141","mm":1,"ev":"The gods drink their fill of pleasure and sink in the sea of sense.","cl":[["the pleasures of the gods' world are a sea in which they sink, and they are without a rival","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["தேவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3142":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3142,"t":["முகடு மணியழுத்தி முள்வைர முள்வேய்ந்து முத்தம் வாய்ச்சூழ்ந்","தகடு பசுமணியார்ந் தங்காந் திருள்பருகி யடுபால் விம்மிப்","பகடு படவடுக்கிப் பண்ணவனார் தம்மொளிமே னின்றாற் போலுந்","தகடு படுசெம்பொன் முக்குடையான் றாளிணையென் றலைவைத் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"The poet compares the Arhat's feet, set upon a gem-studded pedestal bright as the Lord's own radiance, and places them on his head.","n":"முக்குடையான் is the Arhat, the three umbrellas being his sign in Jain worship; the rest is a long simile for his golden pedestal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3142","lb":"CC3142","mm":1,"ev":"The poet bows his head to the feet of the three-umbrella Lord.","cl":[["the Arhat of the three umbrellas is to be worshipped at his feet","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["புலவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3143":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3143,"t":["முந்நீர் வலம்புரி சோர்ந்தசைந்து வாய்முரன்று முழங்கி யீன்ற","மெய்ந்நீர்த் திருமுத் திருபத்தேழ் கோத்துமிழ்ந்து திருவில் வீசுஞ்","செந்நீர்த் திரள்வடம்போற் சிந்தா மணியோதி யுணர்ந்தார் கேட்டா","ரிந்நீர ராயுயர்வ ரேந்துபூந் தாமரையாள் காப்பா ளாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who read and understand the Cintamani, which is like the pearls a conch gave up from the sea strung into a fine rope, and those who hear it, will rise in this way, and the lotus goddess will guard them.","n":"The fruit named is worldly, since Tiru, the lotus goddess of fortune, guards the hearers; the epic's last ilampakam is called release, but its closing blessing is of prosperity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","payan","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3143","lb":"CC3143","mm":1,"ev":"The poet states the fruit of reading and hearing his work.","cl":[["those who learn and hear this work rise in the world and are guarded by Tiru","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3144":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3144,"t":["செந்தா மரைக்குச் செழுநாற்றங் கொடுத்த தேங்கொ","ளந்தா மரையா ளகலத்தவன் பாத மேத்திச்","சிந்தா மணியின் சரிதஞ்சி தர்ந்தேன் றெருண்டார்","நந்தா விளக்குச் சுடர்நன்மணி நாட்டப் பெற்றே."],"p":"","r":"Having sung the feet of the Lord on whose chest the lotus goddess dwells, the poet says that he has scattered the story of the Cintamani, and that the wise gain an undying lamp and good gem.","n":"The Lord with Tiru on his chest is a figure the Vaishnava tradition also uses; here the three-umbrella Arhat is meant by the context of the closing verses, which is the less certain of two readings.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3144","lb":"CC3144","mm":1,"ev":"The poet ends the telling of the story with praise of the Lord's feet.","cl":[["the Lord's feet are to be praised, and those who understand the story gain an undying light","jain","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["புலவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"cc:3145":{"book":"civaka_cintamani","num":3145,"t":["செய்வினை யென்னு முந்நீர்த் திரையிடை முளைத்துத் தேங்கொண்","மைவினை மறுவி லாத மதியெனுந் திங்கண் மாதோ","மொய்வினை யிருள்கண் போழு முக்குடை மூர்த்தி பாதங்","கைவினை செய்த சொற்பூக் கைதொழு தேத்தி னேனே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, raising his hands, praises with the flowers of his words the feet of the three-umbrella Lord, a spotless moon that rises from the sea of deeds and breaks the dark of deeds' binding.","n":"The sea is of deeds (செய்வினை), not of births, so the work ends on the figure of karma as the water to be crossed, and gives its closing act to a hymn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","vazhipaadu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Civaka Cintamani 3145","lb":"CC3145","mm":1,"ev":"The poet offers his poem as a flower at the Lord's feet and ends the work.","cl":[["the Lord's feet free one from the sea of deeds and the darkness of karma","jain","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["புலவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"cc:133"},"kd:1":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":1,"t":["முன்றான் பெருமைக்க ணின்றான்முடி வெய்து காறு","நன்றே நினைந்தான் குணமேமொழித் தான்ற னக்கென்","றொன்றானு முள்ளான் பிறர்க்கேயுறு திக்கு ழந்தான்","அன்றே யிறைவ னவன்றாள்சர ணாங்க ளன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The invocation takes as lord and refuge the one who stood in greatness from the first, who until he reached the end thought only good and spoke only virtue, kept nothing for himself and laboured only for the good of others.","n":"The lord is not named; he is defined by a career (முன் ... முடிவு எய்து காறு, from the beginning until the end was reached) spent wholly for others (பிறர்க்கே உறுதிக்கு உழந்தான்), not by creation or omniscience. That selfless career across a long course is the mark of the Buddha-to-be, and the claim is tagged Buddhist on that mark; the verse itself does not say Buddha.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","adaikkalam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 1","lb":"KD1","ev":"","cl":[["the lord to take refuge in is the one who, from first to last, thought only good, spoke only virtue and toiled for others with no thought for himself","buddhist","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:2":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":2,"t":["நோய்க்குற்ற மாந்தர் மருந்தின்சுவை நோக்க கில்லார்","தீக்குற்ற காத லுடையார்புகைத் தீமை யோரார்","போய்க்குற்றமூன்று மறுத்தான்புகழ் கூறு வேற்கென்","வாய்க்குற்றசொல்லின் வழுவும்வழு வல்ல வன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As the sick do not weigh the taste of medicine and those who need fire do not reckon the harm of its smoke, the poet says, so for one praising the fame of him who cut away the three faults, the slips of his own speech are no slips.","n":"The apology does not ask pardon; it argues that the worth of the object cancels the fault of the words, using two images in which a lesser harm is accepted for a greater need. The three faults (குற்றம் மூன்றும்) are named only by number.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","sol","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 2","lb":"KD2","ev":"","cl":[["faults of wording in praise of the one who cut off the three faults are not to be counted as faults","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:3":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":3,"t":["வாயுவினை நோக்கியுள மாண்டவய நாவாய்","ஆயுவினை நோக்கியுள வாழ்க்கையது வேபோல்","தீயவினை நோக்குமியல் சிந்தனையு மில்லாத்","தூயவனை நோக்கியுள துப்புரவு மெல்லாம்."],"p":"","r":"As a well-built ship is made with an eye to the wind and a life is lived with an eye to its span, so all purity looks to the pure one who has not even the thought that leans toward evil deeds.","n":"துப்புரவு may mean purity or, as often in older Tamil, the means of living and enjoyment; on the second reading the verse says all that sustains us depends on the pure one. The two similes, ship to wind and life to lifespan, make the dependence one of guidance rather than creation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","iraivan","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 3","lb":"KD3","ev":"","cl":[["all purity depends on turning toward the pure one who has no thought of evil deeds","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:4":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":4,"t":["போர்த்தலுடை நீக்குதல் பொடித்துகண் மெய்பூசல்","கூர்த்தபனி யாற்றுதல் குளித்தழலு ணிற்றல்","சார்த்தரிடு பிச்சையர் சடைத்தலைய ராதல்","வார்த்தையிவை செய்தவ மடிந்தொழுக லென்றான்."],"p":"","r":"An unnamed speaker lists wrapping the body or stripping it bare, smearing it with dust, enduring biting cold, bathing and standing in fire, living on alms and wearing matted hair, and says these are mere words: the tapas to practise is to live subdued.","n":"The list takes in practices of several orders at once: going naked was the mark of one Jain order, ash and matted hair of Saiva and Vedic ascetics, standing in fire of the five-fires penance. The reading of the last line (வார்த்தை இவை; செய்தவம் மடிந்து ஒழுகல்) as 'these are talk; tapas is to live restrained' is mine; மடிந்து could also mean 'as one already dead'. சார்த்தர் (line 3) may be caravan merchants who give the alms.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vesham","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 4","lb":"KD4","ev":"","cl":[["going without clothes is a practice of tapas","jain","refuted",1,"unnamed_speaker"],["bodily mortifications such as ash, cold water, fire, alms and matted hair make up tapas","general","refuted",3,"unnamed_speaker"],["true tapas is a restrained, subdued way of living, not bodily display","general","asserted",4,"unnamed_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["என்றான்",""],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:5":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":5,"t":["வகையெழிற் றோள்க ளென்றும் மணிநிறக் குஞ்சி யென்றும்","புகழெழ விகற்பிக் கின்ற பொருளில் காமத்தை மற்றோர்","தொகையெழுங் காத றன்னாற் றுய்த்தியாந் துடைத்து மென்பா","ரகையழ லழுவந் தன்னை நெய்யினா லவிக்க லாமோ."],"p":"","r":"Some say they will wipe out the senseless desire that is dressed up in praise of shapely shoulders and gem-dark hair by enjoying it with love; the verse asks whether a blazing pit of fire can be put out with ghee.","n":"The body praised is a man's: குஞ்சி is a man's tuft of hair and the shoulders are the hero's. The verse also blames the praise itself (புகழ் எழ விகற்பிக்கின்ற), the poetic convention that inflates desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 5","lb":"KD5","ev":"","cl":[["desire can be wiped out by satisfying it","general","refuted",3,"narrator"],["indulgence feeds desire as ghee feeds fire and cannot quench it","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:6":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":6,"t":["அனலென நினைப்பிற் பொத்தி யகந்தலைக் கொண்ட காமக்","கனலினை யுவர்ப்பு நீராற் கடையற வவித்து மென்னார்","நினைவிலாப் புணர்ச்சி தன்னா னீக்குது மென்று நிற்பார்","புனலினைப் புனலினாலே யாவர் போகாமை வைப்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who will not put out the fire of desire, lodged within like a flame, with the water of revulsion, and insist they will remove it by thoughtless union, are like one who would stop a flood with more flood.","n":"உவர்ப்பு names the remedy: revulsion, the deliberate turning against the body that verses 11 to 14 then practise. The verse pairs with 5: there the wrong remedy is fuel on fire, here water on water.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 6","lb":"KD6","ev":"","cl":[["desire is removed by union","general","refuted",3,"narrator"],["the fire of desire is quenched only by the water of revulsion","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:7":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":7,"t":["போதர வுயிர்த்த வாவி புகவுயிர்க் கின்ற தேனு","மூதிய மென்று கொள்வ ருணர்வினான் மிக்க நீரா","ராதலா லழிதன் மாலைப் பொருள்களுக் கழிதல் வேண்டா","காதலா னழுது மென்பார் கண்ணனி களைய லுற்றார்."],"p":"","r":"Those of surpassing understanding count it gain each time a breath that went out comes back in; so for things whose nature is to perish their perishing need not be mourned, and those who say they weep from love are only stripping their own eyes.","n":"The argument inverts ordinary reckoning: life is not a possession that death takes away but a run of returned breaths, each a windfall. The sense of the last half-line (கண் அணி களையல் உற்றார்) is not certain; it may mean they ruin their eyes with weeping or lose the eye of understanding.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","saakkaadu","praanan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 7","lb":"KD7","ev":"","cl":[["things whose nature is to perish should not be mourned when they perish","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:8":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":8,"t":["அரவின மரக்க ராளி யவைகளுஞ் சிறிது தம்மை","மருவினாற் றீய வாகா வரம்பில்கா லத்து ளென்றும்","பிரிவில மாகித் தன்சொற் பேணியே யொழுகு நங்கட்","கொருபொழு திரங்க மாட்டாக் கூற்றின்யா ருய்து மென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Even snakes, demons and lions do no harm to those who stay near them a little while; but from Death, who will not pity for one moment us who through boundless time have never left him and have lived obeying his word, who can escape?","n":"வரம்பில் காலத்துள் (through limitless time) assumes the round of births: we have been Death's servants life after life, and long service earns no mercy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 8","lb":"KD8","ev":"","cl":[["Death spares no one, though beings have served him through endless births","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:9":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":9,"t":["பாளையாந் தன்மை செத்தும் பாலனாந் தன்மை செத்தும்","காளையாந் தன்மை செத்துங் காமுறு மிளமை செத்தும்","மீளுமிவ் வியல்பு மின்னே மேல்வரு மூப்பு மாகி","நாளுநாட் சாகின் றாமா னமக்குநா மழாத தென்னோ."],"p":"","r":"The foetus has died, the child has died, the youth has died, the desiring young adult has died; this same course now brings old age, so we die day by day, and the verse asks why we do not weep for ourselves.","n":"Each stage of life is spoken of as a death (செத்தும), so death is not one event at the end but the steady form of living.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 9","lb":"KD9","ev":"","cl":[["one dies daily, each stage of life dying into the next","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:10":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":10,"t":["கோள்வலைப்பட்டுச் சாவாங் கொலைக்களங் குறித்துச் சென்றே","மீளினு மீளக் காண்டு மீட்சியொன் றானு மில்லா","நாளடி யிடுத றோன்று நம்முயிர் பருகுங் கூற்றின்","வாளின்வாய்த் தலைவைப் பாக்குச் செல்கின்றோம் வாழ்கின் றோமோ."],"p":"","r":"Caught in the net and led to the killing ground, one might yet see a return; but each day's step has no return, and we walk to set our heads at the edge of the sword of Death that drinks our life: is this living?","n":"The image is of a condemned prisoner led to execution: ordinary life is that walk, only with no chance of reprieve.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","nilaiyaamai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 10","lb":"KD10","ev":"","cl":[["each day is a step without return toward death, so living is a march to execution","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:11":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":11,"t":["நன்கன நாறுமி தென்றிவ் வுடம்பு நயக்கின்ற தாயி","னென்பது வாயில்க டோறு முண்ணின் றழுக்குச் சொரியத்","தின்பதொர் நாயு மிழுப்பத் திசைதொறுஞ் சீப்பில்கு போழ்தி","னின்பநன் னாற்ற மிதன்க ணெவ்வகை யாற்கொள்ள லாமே."],"p":"","r":"If one loves this body thinking it smells sweet, when filth pours from every opening, a dog drags it about to eat and it rots in every direction, the verse asks how any fragrance can be found in it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 11","lb":"KD11","ev":"","cl":[["the body, foul from every opening and food for dogs, holds nothing sweet to desire","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:12":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":12,"t":["மாறுகொள் மந்தர மென்று மரகத வீங்கெழு வென்றும்","தேறிடத் தோள்க டிறத்தே திறத்துளிக் காமுற்ற தாயிற்","பாறொடு நாய்க ளசிப்பப் பறிப்பறிப் பற்றிய போழ்தி","னேறிய வித்தசை தன்மாட்டின்புற லாவதிங் கென்னோ?"],"p":"","r":"If one desires shoulders praised as rivals of Mount Mandara and as swelling emerald bars, the verse asks what delight there is in that flesh when vultures and dogs tear and pluck at it.","n":"Again the praised body is a man's: Mandara and the bar (எழு) are the stock similes for a hero's shoulders, so the contemplation of foulness is aimed at desire for a man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 12","lb":"KD12","ev":"","cl":[["desire for a body is undone by seeing it as carrion for vultures and dogs","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:13":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":13,"t":["உறுப்புக் கடாமுடன் கூடி யொன்றா யிருந்த பெரும்பை","மறைப்பில் விழைவிற்குச் சார்வாய் மயக்குவ தேலிவ் வுறுப்புக்","குறைத்தன போல வழுகிக் குறைந்து குறைந்து சொரிய","வெறுப்பிற் கிடந்த பொழுதின் வேண்டப் படுவது முண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"If the great bag in which the limbs and their fluids are bound into one, hidden and serving as a prop for longing, deludes us, the verse asks whether anything will be wanted of it when its limbs rot away as if cut and it lies loathsome.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 13","lb":"KD13","ev":"","cl":[["what deludes desire is only a covered bag of limbs and fluids that will rot","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:14":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":14,"t":["எனதெனச் சிந்தித்த லான்மற் றிவ்வுடம் பின்பத்துக் காமேற்","றினைப்பெய்த புன்கத்தைப் போலச் சிறியவு மூத்தவு மாகி","நுனைய புழுக்குலந் தம்மா னுகரவும் வாழவும் பட்ட","வினைய வுடம்பினைப் பாவி யானென தென்னலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Because one thinks of it as 'mine', this body is taken for pleasure; yet it is a body made by deeds, where swarms of worms small and grown feed and live, and the verse asks whether a sinner can call it 'I' and 'mine'.","n":"The denial is argued from the body's other tenants: the worms already live off it, so the claim 'mine' is false on its face. வினைய உடம்பு makes the body itself a product of past deeds. Line 2 (இனைப் பெய்த புன்கத்தைப் போல) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","pattru","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 14","lb":"KD14","ev":"","cl":[["the body, a product of deeds and a home for worms, cannot be called 'I' or 'mine'","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:15":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":15,"t":["இறந்த நற்குண மெய்தற் கரியவா","யுறைந்த தம்மையெல் லாமுட னாக்குவான்","பிறந்த மூர்த்தியொத் தான்றிங்கள் வெண்குடை","யறங்கொள் கோலண்ணன் மும்மத யானையான்."],"p":"","r":"The king of the moon-white umbrella, the sceptre that holds to dharma and the elephant in triple must is praised as an embodied form, born to bring together all the good qualities that are hard to attain.","n":"The sense of இறந்த (line 1), 'surpassing' or 'gone away', changes whether the king gathers rare virtues or restores lost ones.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 15","lb":"KD15","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:16":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":16,"t":["சீற்றஞ் செற்றுப்பொய் நீக்கிச்செங் கோலினாற்","கூற்றங் காய்ந்து கொடுக்க வெனுந்துணை","மாற்ற மேநவின் றான்றடு மாற்றத்துத்","தோற்றந் தன்னையுங் காமுறத் தோன்றினான்."],"p":"","r":"Having crushed anger and put away falsehood, warding off death with his sceptre, the king spoke no word but 'give', and appeared so that even the bewildered longed for the sight of him.","n":"கூற்றம் காய்ந்து may mean that his rule kept death from his subjects or that he gave up putting men to death. The last line's தடுமாற்றத்துத் தோற்றம் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","eegai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 16","lb":"KD16","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அண்ணல்"],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:17":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":17,"t":["மண்ணுளார் தம்மைப் போல்வார் மாட்டதே யன்று வாய்மை","நண்ணினார் திறத்துங் குற்றங் குற்றமே நல்ல வாகா","விண்ணுளார் புகழ்தற் கொத்த விழுமியோ னெற்றி போழ்ந்த","கண்ணுளான் கண்டந் தன்மேற் கறையையார் கறையன் றென்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Not only in earthly people like us but in those who have reached truth, a fault stays a fault and does not turn good: who would say the stain on the throat of the exalted one praised by the gods, whose forehead is split by an eye, is not a stain?","n":"The example is the Saiva god, named by his forehead eye and stained throat; the verse uses the mark that Saiva praise treats as a sign of his grace as proof that even a god carries a fault. It stands against the poet's own plea in 2.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 17","lb":"KD17","ev":"","cl":[["a fault remains a fault even in those who have reached truth and in gods; greatness does not make it good","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:18":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":18,"t":["மறிப மறியு மலிர்ப மலிரும்","பெறுப பெரும்பெற் றிழப்ப விழக்கும்","அறிவ தறிவா ரழுங்கா ருவவா","ருறுவ துறுமென் றுரைப்பது நன்று."],"p":"","r":"What turns back will turn back, what swells will swell, what is to be gained is gained and what is to be lost is lost; those who know this neither grieve nor exult, and it is good to say that what is to come will come.","n":"The verse names no agent of the outcome, neither god nor deed; equanimity rests only on the certainty that what is due arrives. Verse 19 supplies the cause as deeds (வினை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","samanilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 18","lb":"KD18","ev":"","cl":[["what is due will come, so the wise neither grieve at loss nor exult at gain","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"kd:19":{"book":"kundalakesi","num":19,"t":["வேரிக் கமழ்தா ரரசன்விடு கென்ற போழ்தும்","தாரித்த லாகா வகையாற்கொலை சூழ்ந்த பின்னும்","பூரித்தல் வாடுதலென் றிவற்றாற்பொலி வின்றிநின்றான்","பாரித்த தெல்லாம் வினையின்பய னென்ன வல்லான்."],"p":"","r":"Both when the king of the fragrant garland said 'let him go' and after a death was plotted against him in an unbearable way, the man stood with no swelling or wilting, able to say that all that had unfolded was the fruit of deeds.","n":"The verse tells an event but not whose: the man is unnamed, and who plotted the killing (கொலை சூழ்ந்த) is not stated; it may be the same king. The fragment does not let the story be rebuilt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","samanilai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kundalakesi 19","lb":"KD19","ev":"A king orders a man released, and later his killing is plotted; through both the man stays unmoved.","cl":[["all that befalls one is the fruit of one's deeds","general","asserted",4,"steadfast_man"]],"ag":["அரசன்","நின்றான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"kd:1"},"mm:0:1-18":{"book":"manimekalai","num":1,"t":["இளங் கதிர் ஞாயிறு எள்ளும் தோற்றத்து","விளங்கு ஒளி மேனி விரி சடையாட்டி","பொன் திகழ் நெடு வரை உச்சித் தோன்றி","தென் திசைப் பெயர்ந்த இத் தீவத் தெய்வதம்","சாகைச் சம்பு தன் கீழ் நின்று","மா நில மடந்தைக்கு வரும் துயர் கேட்டு","வெந் திறல் அரக்கர்க்கு வெம் பகை நோற்ற","சம்பு என்பாள் சம்பாபதியினள்","செங்கதிர்ச் செல்வன் திருக் குலம் விளக்கும்","கஞ்ச வேட்கையின் காந்த மன் வேண்ட","அமர முனிவன் அகத்தியன் தனாது","கரகம் கவிழ்த்த காவிரிப் பாவை","செங் குணக்கு ஒழுகி அச் சம்பாபதி அயல்","பொங்கு நீர்ப் பரப்பொடு பொருந்தித் தோன்ற","ஆங்கு இனிது இருந்த அருந் தவ முதியோள்","ஓங்கு நீர்ப் பாவையை உவந்து எதிர்கொண்டு ஆங்கு","ஆணு விசும்பின் ஆகாயகங்கை","வேணவாத் தீர்த்த விளக்கே வா என"],"p":"","r":"The narrator tells how Champa, the shining goddess of this island who came south from the golden mountain and did austerity against the asuras for the sake of the suffering earth, dwelt under the jambu tree at Champapati, and how, when a Chola king of the sun's line asked for water, Agastya overturned his pot and the Kaveri flowed east to that city, where the old ascetic goddess welcomed her as a lamp come to quench a long desire.","n":"The patikam opens not with the heroine but with the founding of Puhar's two names, so the city of the epic is first a goddess's seat. Lines 10 (கஞ்ச வேட்கையின்) and 17-18 (ஆணு விசும்பின் ஆகாயகங்கை வேணவாத் தீர்த்த விளக்கே) are hard: whether the Ganga of the sky is the one whose desire is quenched or a title given to the Kaveri is not clear from the words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","thavam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 0, lines 1-18","lb":"MM0:1-18","ev":"Agastya, at a Chola king's request, overturns his water-pot; the Kaveri flows to Champapati and the goddess Champa welcomes her.","cl":[],"ag":["சம்பு","காந்த மன்","அகத்தியன்","காவிரிப் பாவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:1"},"mm:0:19-31":{"book":"manimekalai","num":2,"t":["பின்னிலை முனியாப் பெருந் தவன் கேட்டு ஈங்கு","'அன்னை கேள் இவ் அருந் தவ முதியோள்","நின்னால் வணங்கும் தகைமையள் வணங்கு' என","பாடல்சால் சிறப்பின் பரதத்து ஓங்கிய","கோடாச் செங்கோல் சோழர் தம் குலக்கொடி","கோள் நிலை திரிந்து கோடை நீடினும்","தான் நிலை திரியாத் தண் தமிழ்ப் பாவை","தொழுதனள் நிற்ப அத் தொல் மூதாட்டி","கழுமிய உவகையின் கவான் கொண்டிருந்து","தெய்வக் கருவும் திசைமுகக் கருவும்","செம்மலர் முதியோன் செய்த அந் நாள்","என் பெயர்ப் படுத்த இவ் விரும் பெயர் மூதூர்","நின் பெயர்ப் படுத்தேன் நீ வாழிய! என"],"p":"","r":"Agastya bids the river bow to the old ascetic goddess; the Kaveri, the Chola banner who never fails her course though the planets stray and summer lengthens, bows, and Champa takes her to her lap and gives the old city, named for herself since Brahma's creation, the river's name as well.","n":"This is how the patikam accounts for the city's two names (இரு பால் பெயரிய, line 32): Champapati and the Kaveri's city. The river is called the daughter of the Cholas of the unbending sceptre, so the land's fertility is tied to the dynasty's justice from the first page.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","naadu","sengol","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 0, lines 19-31","lb":"MM0:19-31","ev":"At Agastya's word the Kaveri bows to Champa, who takes her on her lap and names the city after her.","cl":[],"ag":["அகத்தியன்","காவிரிப் பாவை","சம்பு"],"ec":[],"spk":["அகத்தியன்","காவிரிப் பாவை"],"ch":"mm:1"},"mm:0:32-50":{"book":"manimekalai","num":3,"t":["இரு பால் பெயரிய உரு கெழு மூதூர்","ஒரு நூறு வேள்வி உரவோன் தனக்குப்","பெரு விழா அறைந்ததும் 'பெருகியது அலர்' என","சிதைந்த நெஞ்சின் சித்திராபதி தான்","வயந்த மாலையான் மாதவிக்கு உரைத்ததும்","மணிமேகலை தான் மா மலர் கொய்ய","அணி மலர்ப் பூம்பொழில் அகவயின் சென்றதும்","ஆங்கு அப் பூம்பொழில் அரசு இளங் குமரனைப்","பாங்கில் கண்டு அவள் பளிக்கறை புக்கதும்","பளிக்கறை புக்க பாவையைக் கண்டு அவன்","துளக்குறு நெஞ்சில் துயரொடும் போய பின்","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம் வந்து தோன்றியதும்","மணிமேகலையை மணிபல்லவத்து உய்த்ததும்","உவவன மருங்கின் அவ் உரைசால் தெய்வதம்","சுதமதி தன்னைத் துயில் எடுப்பியதூஉம்","ஆங்கு அத் தீவகத்து ஆய் இழை நல்லாள்","தான் துயில் உணர்ந்து தனித் துயர் உழந்ததும்","உழந்தோள் ஆங்கண் ஓர் ஒளி மணிப் பீடிகைப்","பழம் பிறப்பு எல்லாம் பான்மையின் உணர்ந்ததும்"],"p":"","r":"The patikam begins its summary of the story: Indra's festival is proclaimed in the twice-named city; Chitrapathi, stung that the town's gossip grows, sends word to Madhavi through Vasantamalai; Manimekalai goes to the garden for flowers, sees the prince, hides in the crystal chamber, and he leaves troubled; the goddess Manimekala carries her to Manipallavam and wakes Sutamati; alone on the island she grieves, and at a shining jewel seat she comes to know all her former births.","n":"The summary names the jewel seat (மணிப் பீடிகை) only by its light; that it is the Buddha's seat is left to kaathai 9 onward. The knowledge of former births (பழம் பிறப்பு) is given as an event, not argued, and it is the turn on which the whole renunciation rests.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 0, lines 32-50","lb":"MM0:32-50","ev":"Manimekalai flees the prince into the crystal chamber, is carried by the goddess to Manipallavam, and there learns her former births at the jewel seat.","cl":[],"ag":["சித்திராபதி","வயந்த மாலை","மணிமேகலை","அரசு இளங் குமரன்","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:1"},"mm:0:51-68":{"book":"manimekalai","num":4,"t":["உணர்ந்தோள் முன்னர் உயர் தெய்வம் தோன்றி","'மனம் கவல் ஒழிக!' என மந்திரம் கொடுத்ததும்","தீபதிலகை செவ்வனம் தோன்றி","மா பெரும் பாத்திரம் மடக்கொடிக்கு அளித்ததும்","பாத்திரம் பெற்ற பைந்தொடி தாயரொடு","யாப்புறு மா தவத்து அறவணர்த் தொழுததும்","அறவண அடிகள் ஆபுத்திரன் திறம்","நறு மலர்க் கோதைக்கு நன்கனம் உரைத்ததும்","அங்கைப் பாத்திரம் ஆபுத்திரன்பால்","சிந்தாதேவி கொடுத்த வண்ணமும்","மற்று அப் பாத்திரம் மடக்கொடி ஏந்தி","பிச்சைக்கு அவ் ஊர்ப் பெருந் தெரு அடைந்ததும்","பிச்சை ஏற்ற பெய் வளை கடிஞையில்","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர் பாத்தூண் ஈத்ததும்","காரிகை நல்லாள் காயசண்டிகை வயிற்று","ஆனைத்தீக் கெடுத்து அம்பலம் அடைந்ததும்","அம்பலம் அடைந்தனள் ஆய் இழை என்றே","கொங்கு அலர் நறுந் தார்க் கோமகன் சென்றதும்"],"p":"","r":"The summary continues: the goddess gives her a mantra and bids her cease grieving; Deepatilakai gives her the great bowl; with her mothers she bows to the teacher Aravanar, who tells her of Aputhiran and of how Chinta Devi gave him the bowl; she carries it into the city's street for alms, a chaste wife puts the first food in it, she ends Kayachandigai's elephant-fire hunger, and when she goes to the public hall the prince follows.","n":"The bowl passes from goddess to Aputhiran to Manimekalai, and its first food comes from a பத்தினி (line 64), so the epic's charity starts from a chaste wife's gift; the heroine who renounced the courtesan's life receives first from the epic's figure of wifely virtue. The begging renunciant is the one who ends another's hunger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","iravu","eegai","pathini"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 0, lines 51-68","lb":"MM0:51-68","ev":"Manimekalai receives the bowl, hears its history from Aravanar, begs in Puhar, is first fed by a chaste wife, and ends Kayachandigai's hunger.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","தீபதிலகை","மணிமேகலை","அறவண அடிகள்","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர்","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:1"},"mm:0:69-88":{"book":"manimekalai","num":5,"t":["அம்பலம் அடைந்த அரசு இளங் குமரன்முன்","வஞ்ச விஞ்சையன் மகள் வடிவு ஆகி","மறம் செய் வேலோன் வான் சிறைக்கோட்டம்","அறம் செய் கோட்டம் ஆக்கிய வண்ணமும்","காயசண்டிகை என விஞ்சைக் காஞ்சனன்","ஆய் இழை தன்னை அகலாது அணுகலும்","வஞ்ச விஞ்சையன் மன்னவன் சிறுவனை","மைந்து உடை வாளின் தப்பிய வண்ணமும்","ஐ அரி உண் கண் அவன் துயர் பொறாஅள்","தெய்வக் கிளவியின் தெளிந்த வண்ணமும்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன் 'ஆய் இழை தன்னைச்","சிறை செய்க' என்றதும் சிறைவீடு செய்ததும்","நறு மலர்க் கோதைக்கு நல் அறம் உரைத்து ஆங்கு","ஆய் வளை ஆபுத்திரன் நாடு அடைந்ததும்","ஆங்கு அவன்தன்னோடு அணி இழை போகி","ஓங்கிய மணிபல்லவத்திடை உற்றதும்","உற்றவள் ஆங்கு ஓர் உயர் தவன் வடிவு ஆய்","பொன் கொடி வஞ்சியில் பொருந்திய வண்ணமும்","'நவை அறு நன்பொருள் உரைமினோ' என","சமயக் கணக்கர் தம் திறம் கேட்டதும்"],"p":"","r":"The summary goes on: in Kayachandigai's form Manimekalai turns the king's prison into a house of charity; Kanchanan, taking her for his wife, kills the prince with his sword; she, grieving, is calmed by a divine voice; the king orders her imprisoned and then freed; she teaches dharma to the queen, goes to Aputhiran's land and with him to Manipallavam, takes an ascetic's form in Vanji, and asks the schools' reckoners to state the truth.","n":"Line 72 sets the epic's justice theme in one turn: a prison of the war-making king (மறம் செய் வேலோன்) becomes a house of dharma (அறம் செய் கோட்டம்). Whom she teaches at line 81 is not named: நறு மலர்க் கோதை is read here as the queen, since 58 used the same phrase for Manimekalai as hearer and here she is the teller. The patikam says she heard (கேட்டதும்) the schools, not that she refuted them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","kolai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 0, lines 69-88","lb":"MM0:69-88","ev":"Manimekalai frees the prison, Kanchanan kills the prince, the king jails and releases her, and she travels to Aputhiran's land, Manipallavam and Vanji, where she hears the schools.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","காஞ்சனன்","அரசு இளங் குமரன்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்","ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:1"},"mm:0:89-98":{"book":"manimekalai","num":6,"t":["ஆங்கு அத் தாயரோடு அறவணர்த் தேர்ந்து","பூங்கொடி கச்சி மா நகர் புக்கதும்","புக்கு அவள் கொண்ட பொய் உருக் களைந்து","மற்று அவர் பாதம் வணங்கிய வண்ணமும்","தவத் திறம் பூண்டு தருமம் கேட்டு","'பவத் திறம் அறுக' என பாவை நோற்றதும்","இளங்கோ வேந்தன் அருளிக் கேட்ப","வளம் கெழு கூல வாணிகன் சாத்தன்","மா வண் தமிழ்த் திறம் மணிமேகலை துறவு","ஆறு ஐம் பாட்டினுள் அறிய வைத்தனன் என்"],"p":"","r":"The summary ends: with her mothers she seeks out Aravanar and enters Kanchi, casts off her borrowed form and bows at his feet, takes up the ascetic life, hears the dharma and vows that the round of becoming be cut; and the colophon says that Saathan, the grain merchant, with Ilango the prince graciously listening, set out Manimekalai's renunciation in thirty songs.","n":"The epic's title theme is given at line 97 as மணிமேகலை துறவு, and its count as ஆறு ஐம் பாட்டு (thirty). The roles of the Silappathikaram's patikam are reversed: there Ilango composes and Saathan hears; here Saathan composes and Ilango hears. Whether this colophon is the poet's own or a later frame the text does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","piravi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 0, lines 89-98","lb":"MM0:89-98","ev":"Manimekalai finds Aravanar in Kanchi, bows to him, takes up austerity and vows to end rebirth; Saathan recites the work before Ilango.","cl":[["taking up austerity and hearing the dharma lead to the cutting off of becoming","buddhist","asserted",94,"narrator"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","அறவண அடிகள்","இளங்கோ வேந்தன்","சாத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:1"},"mm:1:1-9":{"book":"manimekalai","num":7,"t":["உலகம் திரியா ஓங்கு உயர் விழுச் சீர்ப்","பலர் புகழ் மூதூர்ப் பண்பு மேம்படீஇய","ஓங்கு உயர் மலயத்து அருந் தவன் உரைப்ப","தூங்கு எயில் எறிந்த தொடித் தோள் செம்பியன்","விண்ணவர் தலைவனை வணங்கி முன் நின்று","'மண்ணகத்து என்தன் வான் பதி தன்னுள்","மேலோர் விழைய விழாக் கோள் எடுத்த","நால் ஏழ் நாளினும் நன்கு இனிது உறைக' என","அமரர் தலைவன் ஆங்கு அது நேர்ந்தது"],"p":"","r":"To raise the old city's renown, at the word of the great ascetic of the Malaya hill, the Chola of braceleted arms who destroyed the hanging fort bowed before the king of the gods and asked him to dwell in his city through the twenty-eight days of the festival the worthy desire, and the king of the gods agreed.","n":"The festival is given a charter, not a date: a sage's counsel, a king's request and a god's consent. The founder is identified only by his feat (தூங்கு எயில் எறிந்த), the hanging fort that the Purananuru also gives to a Chola ancestor; the kaathai does not name the sage, whom tradition takes for Agastya.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","vendhan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 1, lines 1-9","lb":"MM1:1-9","ev":"At the Malaya sage's word the hanging-fort Chola asks Indra to dwell in his city for the twenty-eight days of the festival, and Indra agrees.","cl":[],"ag":["அகத்தியன்","தொடித் தோள் செம்பியன்","விண்ணவர் தலைவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:2"},"mm:1:10-34":{"book":"manimekalai","num":8,"t":["கவராக் கேள்வியோர் கடவார் ஆகலின்","மெய்த் திறம் வழக்கு நன்பொருள் வீடு எனும்","இத் திறம் தம் தம் இயல்பினின் காட்டும்","சமயக் கணக்கரும் தம் துறை போகிய","அமயக் கணக்கரும் அகலார் ஆகி","கரந்து உரு எய்திய கடவுளாளரும்","பரந்து ஒருங்கு ஈண்டிய பாடை மாக்களும்","ஐம் பெருங்குழுவும் எண் பேர் ஆயமும்","வந்து ஒருங்கு குழீஇ 'வான்பதி தன்னுள்","கொடித் தேர்த் தானைக் கொற்றவன் துயரம்","விடுத்த பூதம் விழாக்கோள் மறப்பின்","மடித்த செவ் வாய் வல் எயிறு இலங்க","இடிக் குரல் முழக்கத்து இடும்பை செய்திடும்","தொடுத்த பாசத்து தொல் பதி நரகரைப்","புடைத்து உணும் பூதமும் பொருந்தாதாயிடும்","மா இரு ஞாலத்து அரசு தலையீண்டும்","ஆயிரம்கண்ணோன் விழாக் கால்கொள்க' என","வச்சிரக் கோட்டத்து மணம் கெழு முரசம்","கச்சை யானைப் பிடர்த்தலை ஏற்றி","ஏற்று உரி போர்த்த இடி உறு முழக்கின்","கூற்றுக்கண் விளிக்கும் குருதி வேட்கை","முரசு கடிப்பு இகூஉம் முதுகுடிப் பிறந்தோன்","'திரு விழை மூதூர் வாழ்க!' என்று ஏத்தி","'வானம் மும் மாரி பொழிக! மன்னவன்","கோள் நிலை திரியாக் கோலோன் ஆகுக!"],"p":"","r":"Because the learned do not neglect what is due, the reckoners of the schools who each show truth, method, the good end and release, the reckoners of times, gods in disguise, speakers of many tongues, the five great assemblies and the eight groups gather and say that if the festival is forgotten the spirit that removed the king's distress will roar and torment the city and the spirit that beats and eats the wicked bound in its noose will be displeased, so let the festival of the thousand-eyed begin; the drum is taken from the Vajra temple onto an elephant's neck, and its hereditary drummer hails the city and wishes it three rains a year and a king whose rule does not let the planets leave their course.","n":"The festival is kept out of fear as much as joy: the assemblies' reason is the guardian spirit's anger if it is forgotten, a threat the epic later fulfils, though by another hand (Manimekala deity's curse, kaathai 25). Line 34 can be read as a king whose sceptre keeps the planets in their places, so that rain and just rule are wished for in one breath; the reading of கோள் நிலை திரியா as qualifying the king's rod is mine. The schools' reckoners are listed among the festival's guests, the first sign of the debating hall of line 61.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","anangu","mazhai","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 1, lines 10-34","lb":"MM1:10-34","ev":"The schools, the guests and the king's assemblies gather and call for the festival to begin; the drum is mounted on an elephant and the hereditary drummer begins the proclamation.","cl":[["if the city forgets the festival, the guardian spirit that removed the king's distress will bring affliction on it","general","reported",20,"aimperungkuzhu"],["a spirit binds the wicked of the city with its noose, beats them and devours them","general","reported",24,"aimperungkuzhu"]],"ag":["ஐம் பெருங்குழு","எண் பேர் ஆயம்","முதுகுடிப் பிறந்தோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஐம் பெருங்குழு",""],"ch":"mm:2"},"mm:1:35-51":{"book":"manimekalai","num":9,"t":["தீவகச் சாந்தி செய்தரு நல் நாள்","ஆயிரம்கண்ணோன் தன்னோடு ஆங்கு உள","நால் வேறு தேவரும் நலத்தகு சிறப்பில்","பால் வேறு தேவரும் இப் பதிப் படர்ந்து","மன்னன் கரிகால்வளவன் நீங்கிய நாள்","இந் நகர் போல்வதோர் இயல்பினது ஆகிப்","பொன்நகர் வறிதாப் போதுவர் என்பது","தொல் நிலை உணர்ந்தோர் துணிபொருள் ஆதலின்","தோரண வீதியும் தோம் அறு கோட்டியும்","பூரண கும்பமும் பொலம் பாலிகைகளும்","பாவை விளக்கும் பல உடன் பரப்புமின்","காய்க் குலைக் கமுகும் வாழையும் வஞ்சியும்","பூக் கொடி வல்லியும் கரும்பும் நடுமின்","பத்தி வேதிகைப் பசும் பொன் தூணத்து","முத்துத் தாமம் முறையொடு நாற்றுமின்","விழவு மலி மூதூர் வீதியும் மன்றமும்","பழ மணல் மாற்றுமின் புது மணல் பரப்புமின்"],"p":"","r":"The drummer proclaims that on the good day of the island's rite of peace the thousand-eyed and the gods of four kinds and many classes will come to this city, leaving the golden city of heaven empty, as those who know the ancient order hold for certain, and he bids the people set up arches, golden pots, sprouts in golden trays and lamp-bearing images, plant areca, plantain, vanchi, flowering creepers and sugarcane, hang pearl strings on the gold pillars, and in the streets and squares change the old sand for new.","n":"Lines 39 to 41 are hard: heaven, emptied by the gods' coming, becomes 'like this city on the day king Karikal Valavan left it', which seems to measure heaven's emptiness by Puhar's desolation at the loss of Karikalan, whether at his death or his going away the line does not say. The phrase தீவகச் சாந்தி returns in kaathai 24, where the day it is not performed is the day the city enters the sea.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 1, lines 35-51","lb":"MM1:35-51","ev":"","cl":[["on the festival day the gods leave heaven empty and come to dwell in the city","general","reported",41,"murasu_herald"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["முதுகுடிப் பிறந்தோன்",""],"ch":"mm:2"},"mm:1:52-72":{"book":"manimekalai","num":10,"t":["கதலிகைக் கொடியும் காழ் ஊன்று விலோதமும்","மதலை மாடமும் வாயிலும் சேர்த்துமின்","நுதல் விழி நாட்டத்து இறையோன் முதலா","பதி வாழ் சதுக்கத்துத் தெய்வம் ஈறு ஆக","வேறுவேறு சிறப்பின் வேறுவேறு செய்வினை","ஆறு அறி மரபின் அறிந்தோர் செய்யுமின்","தண் மணல் பந்தரும் தாழ்தரு பொதியிலும்","புண்ணிய நல்லுரை அறிவீர்! பொருந்துமின்","ஒட்டிய சமயத்து உறு பொருள் வாதிகள்","பட்டி மண்டபத்து பாங்கு அறிந்து ஏறுமின்","பற்றாமாக்கள் தம்முடன் ஆயினும்","செற்றமும் கலாமும் செய்யாது அகலுமின்","வெண் மணல் குன்றமும் விரி பூஞ் சோலையும்","தண் மணல் துருத்தியும் தாழ் பூந் துறைகளும்","தேவரும் மக்களும் ஒத்து உடன் திரிதரும்","நால் ஏழ் நாளினும் நன்கு அறிந்தீர் என","ஒளிறு வாள் மறவரும் தேரும் மாவும்","களிறும் சூழ்தர கண் முரசு இயம்பி","'பசியும் பிணியும் பகையும் நீங்கி","வசியும் வளனும் சுரக்க!' என வாழ்த்தி","அணி விழா அறைந்தனன் அகநகர் மருங்கு என்"],"p":"","r":"The drummer bids them raise flags and banners on the mansions and gates, have those who know the proper rites worship every god in its own way from the brow-eyed lord to the deity of the crossroads, bids those who know the good word of merit take their seats in the sand pavilions and halls and the debaters of the schools mount the debating hall, and part without hatred or quarrel even from those who do not share their view, for gods and men will roam together through the twenty-eight days; then, amid swordsmen, chariots, horses and elephants, he beats the drum, wishes that hunger, disease and enmity leave and rain and plenty overflow, and so proclaims the festival through the inner city.","n":"The proclamation makes room for every cult, Saiva and local alike, and sets the schools' debate inside a civic festival under a rule of civility: dispute is allowed, enmity is not. The closing wish names hunger first, the ill the heroine's begging bowl will later answer. The ills of line 70 are those Kural 734 excludes from a land, but in other words, so there is no echo.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","vaadham","igal","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 1, lines 52-72","lb":"MM1:52-72","ev":"The drummer finishes his commands, beats the drum among the troops and proclaims the festival through the inner city.","cl":[["each god is to be worshipped with its own distinct rites by those who know the way","general","reported",57,"murasu_herald"],["debaters of rival schools should part without hatred or quarrel even from those who reject their view","general","asserted",63,"murasu_herald"]],"ag":["முதுகுடிப் பிறந்தோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முதுகுடிப் பிறந்தோன்",""],"ch":"mm:2"},"mm:2:1-16":{"book":"manimekalai","num":11,"t":["நாவல் ஓங்கிய மா பெருந் தீவினுள்","காவல் தெய்வதம் தேவர்கோற்கு எடுத்த","தீவகச் சாந்தி செய்தரு நல் நாள்","மணிமேகலையொடு மாதவி வாராத்","தணியாத் துன்பம் தலைத்தலை மேல் வர","சித்திராபதி தான் செல்லல் உற்று இரங்கி","தத்து அரி நெடுங் கண் தன் மகள் தோழி","வயந்தமாலையை 'வருக' எனக் கூஉய்","'பயம் கெழு மா நகர் அலர் எடுத்து உரை' என","வயந்த மாலையும் மாதவி துறவிக்கு","அயர்ந்து, மெய் வாடிய அழிவினள் ஆதலின்","மணிமேகலையொடு மாதவி இருந்த","அணி மலர் மண்டபத்து அகவயின் செலீஇ","ஆடிய சாயல் ஆய் இழை மடந்தை","வாடிய மேனி கண்டு உளம் வருந்தி","'பொன் நேர் அனையாய்! புகுந்தது கேளாய்!"],"p":"","r":"On the festival day of the island's peace rite for the king of the gods, Chithirapathi, pained that Madhavi and Manimekalai have not come, calls her daughter's friend Vayantamalai and tells her to go and tell them what the town is saying; Vayantamalai, herself wasted by Madhavi's renunciation, goes to the flower hall where the two sit, grieves at the dancer's worn body, and begins to speak.","n":"Chithirapathi does not plead; she sends the town's gossip (அலர்) as her argument, so public talk is the pressure brought to bear on a renunciant. Both women who come to Madhavi are wasted by her choice (அயர்ந்து, மெய் வாடிய; வாடிய மேனி), the friend in grief and Madhavi in austerity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","thuravu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 2, lines 1-16","lb":"MM2:1-16","ev":"Chithirapathi, grieved that Madhavi stays away from the festival, sends Vayantamalai to tell her the town's talk, and Vayantamalai comes to Madhavi in the flower hall.","cl":[],"ag":["சித்திராபதி","வயந்த மாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:3"},"mm:2:17-33":{"book":"manimekalai","num":12,"t":["உன்னோடு இவ் ஊர் உற்றது ஒன்று உண்டுகொல்?","\"வேத்தியல் பொதுவியல் என்று இரு திறத்துக்","கூத்தும் பாட்டும் தூக்கும் துணிவும்","பண் யாழ்க் கரணமும் பாடைப் பாடலும்","தண்ணுமைக் கருவியும் தாழ் தீம் குழலும்","கந்துகக் கருத்தும் மடைநூல் செய்தியும்","சுந்தரச் சுண்ணமும் தூ நீர் ஆடலும்","பாயல் பள்ளியும் பருவத்து ஒழுக்கமும்","காயக் கரணமும் கண்ணியது உணர்தலும்","கட்டுரை வகையும் கரந்து உறை கணக்கும்","வட்டிகைச் செய்தியும் மலர் ஆய்ந்து தொடுத்தலும்","கோலம் கோடலும் கோவையின் கோப்பும்","காலக் கணிதமும் கலைகளின் துணிவும்","நாடக மகளிர்க்கு நன்கனம் வகுத்த","ஓவியச் செந் நூல் உரை நூல் கிடக்கையும்","கற்று துறைபோகிய பொன் தொடி நங்கை","நல் தவம் புரிந்தது நாண் உடைத்து\" என்றே"],"p":"","r":"Vayantamalai reports what the town says of Madhavi: that a woman who learned and mastered every art laid down for dancing women, the court and common styles of dance and song, the lute, drum and flute, the arts of the ball, cookery, perfume, bathing, the bed, the seasons, the body, reading intent, speech, cipher, painting, garlands, adornment, the reckoning of time and the treatises, should have taken up tapas is a shame.","n":"The list is the town's argument, not an ornament: every accomplishment named is a reason, in its eyes, that the dancer belongs to her trade. நல் தவம் (33) is the town's own phrase, so it grants the tapas is good and calls it shameful for her. Several items (கரந்து உறை கணக்கு, வட்டிகைச் செய்தி, கோவையின் கோப்பு) are rare names of arts and are taken here only as items of the list. The claim is answered at once: Vayantamalai herself sets it down to the talk of the many (34-36), and Madhavi replies to it in 38-57, which is why its stance is refuted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","naanam","thavam","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 2, lines 17-33","lb":"MM2:17-33","ev":"Vayantamalai tells Madhavi that the town says her turning to tapas, after mastering the dancer's arts, is shameful.","cl":[["a woman trained in the arts of the dancing women shames herself by taking up tapas","general","refuted",33,"puhar_oorar"]],"ag":["ஊர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வயந்த மாலை","மாதவி"],"ch":"mm:3"},"mm:2:34-41":{"book":"manimekalai","num":13,"t":["அலகு இல் மூதூர் ஆன்றவர் அல்லது","பலர் தொகுபு உரைக்கும் பண்பு இல் வாய்மொழி","'நயம்பாடு இல்லை நாண் உடைத்து' என்ற","வயந்தமாலைக்கு மாதவி உரைக்கும்","'காதலன் உற்ற கடுந் துயர் கேட்டு","போதல்செய்யா உயிரொடு நின்றே","பொன் கொடி மூதூர்ப் பொருளுரை இழந்து","நல் தொடி நங்காய்! நாணுத் துறந்தேன்"],"p":"","r":"Vayantamalai sets the words, 'there is no good in it, it is shameful', down to the ill-bred talk of the many rather than the worthy elders of the old city, and Madhavi answers that she, who stayed alive on hearing of her lover's cruel suffering, already lost the town's good word and gave up her shame.","n":"Madhavi turns the town's charge: the shame is not in her tapas but in her having outlived Kovalan's death (போதல்செய்யா உயிரொடு நின்றே), so renunciation is her answer to a shame already incurred. Whether lines 34 to 36 are Vayantamalai's own gloss on the town's words or part of the report is not certain; the reading takes them as hers, since 37 names her as the one who spoke.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","pirivu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 2, lines 34-41","lb":"MM2:34-41","ev":"Vayantamalai disowns the town's talk as the many's, and Madhavi replies that she gave up shame when she survived the news of her lover's suffering.","cl":[["a woman who stays alive on hearing of her beloved's suffering has given up her shame","general","asserted",41,"madhavi"]],"ag":["வயந்த மாலை","மாதவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி","வயந்த மாலை"],"ch":"mm:3"},"mm:2:42-57":{"book":"manimekalai","num":14,"t":["காதலர் இறப்பின் கனை எரி பொத்தி","ஊது உலைக் குருகின் உயிர்த்து அகத்து அடங்காது","இன் உயிர் ஈவர் ஈயார் ஆயின்","நல் நீர்ப் பொய்கையின் நளி எரி புகுவர்","நளி எரி புகாஅர் ஆயின் அன்பரோடு","உடன் உறை வாழ்க்கைக்கு நோற்று உடம்பு அடுவர்","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர் பரப்புநீர் ஞாலத்து","அத் திறத்தாளும் அல்லள் எம் ஆய் இழை","கணவற்கு உற்ற கடுந் துயர் பொறா அள்","மணம் மலி கூந்தல் சிறுபுறம் புதைப்ப","கண்ணீர் ஆடிய கதிர் இள வன முலை","திண்ணிதின் திருகி தீ அழல் பொத்தி","காவலன் பேர் ஊர் கனை எரி ஊட்டிய","மா பெரும் பத்தினி மகள் மணிமேகலை","அருந் தவப் படுத்தல் அல்லது யாவதும்","திருந்தாச் செய்கைத் தீத் தொழில் படாஅள்"],"p":"","r":"Madhavi says that pathini women of the wide world, when their husbands die, give up their lives with sighs like a smith's bellows, or else enter the blazing fire as if a cool pool, or else wear down the body in vows to live again with the beloved, but that Kannaki was of none of these kinds: unable to bear her husband's suffering she twisted off her breast and set the king's city aflame, and Manimekalai, the daughter of that great pathini, will be brought to tapas and never to the evil trade of unreformed conduct.","n":"Madhavi calls Manimekalai the daughter (மகள்) of the great pathini, not her own, so the courtesan's daughter is given Kannaki's lineage and with it a claim to be kept from the courtesan's trade (தீத் தொழில்). The three kinds are ranked by the word ஆயின் (if not), each a fallback from the one before; the third does not say in which life the couple will live together. Madhavi herself fits none of the three either, having lived on, which is the point of her opening in 38 to 41. The second kind, who enters the fire as if it were a cool pool (45), uses the figure of Purananuru 246, and the third, who wears the body down in vows, is the widow's austerity that the queen of that poem refuses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","karpu","thavam","nonbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 2, lines 42-57","lb":"MM2:42-57","ev":"Madhavi tells Vayantamalai the kinds of pathini, sets Kannaki apart from them, and says Manimekalai will be given to tapas.","cl":[["a chaste wife dies at her husband's death, or enters the fire, or wears down her body in vows to rejoin him","general","asserted",48,"madhavi"],["the daughter of a great pathini must be given to tapas and not to the courtesan's trade","general","asserted",57,"madhavi"]],"ag":["மாதவி","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி","வயந்த மாலை"],"ch":"mm:3"},"mm:2:58-75":{"book":"manimekalai","num":15,"t":["ஆங்கனம் அன்றியும் ஆய் இழை கேளாய்","ஈங்கு இம் மாதவர் உறைவிடம் புகுந்தேன்","மற வணம் நீத்த மாசு அறு கேள்வி","அறவண அடிகள் அடிமிசை வீழ்ந்து","மா பெருந் துன்பம் கொண்டு உளம் மயங்கி","காதலன் உற்ற கடுந் துயர் கூறப்","\"பிறந்தோர் உறுவது பெருகிய துன்பம்","பிறவார் உறுவது பெரும் பேர் இன்பம்","பற்றின் வருவது முன்னது பின்னது","அற்றோர் உறுவது அறிக!\" என்று அருளி","ஐவகைச் சீலத்து அமைதியும் காட்டி","\"உய் வகை இவை கொள்\" என்று உரவோன் அருளினன்","மைத் தடங் கண்ணார் தமக்கும் எற் பயந்த","சித்திராபதிக்கும் செப்பு நீ என","ஆங்கு அவள் உரை கேட்டு அரும் பெறல் மா மணி","ஓங்கு திரைப் பெருங் கடல் வீழ்த்தோர் போன்று","மையல் நெஞ்சமொடு வயந்த மாலையும்","கையற்றுப் பெயர்ந்தனள் காரிகை திறத்து என்"],"p":"","r":"Madhavi tells how she came to this dwelling of ascetics and fell at the feet of the teacher Aravanar, told him her lover's suffering, and was taught that the born meet ever-growing suffering and the unborn the greatest bliss, that the one comes of attachment and the other to those who are free of it, and was shown the five precepts as the way to be saved; she bids Vayantamalai tell this to the courtesans and to Chithirapathi, and Vayantamalai leaves helpless, like one who has dropped a rare gem into the sea.","n":"The teaching answers grief, not a question of doctrine: Madhavi brings Aravanar her lover's suffering and is given the cause of all suffering. ஐவகைச் சீலம் are the five lay precepts of the Buddhists, offered as உய் வகை, a way of survival, before any word of release. The closing simile makes Madhavi, lost to the courtesans, the gem dropped in the sea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","pattru","idumbai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 2, lines 58-75","lb":"MM2:58-75","ev":"Madhavi tells how Aravanar taught her the cause of suffering and the five precepts and sends word to Chithirapathi; Vayantamalai goes away in despair.","cl":[["those who are born meet growing suffering and those not born meet the greatest bliss","buddhist","asserted",65,"aravanar"],["suffering comes from attachment and bliss is reached by those freed of it","buddhist","asserted",67,"aravanar"],["the five precepts are the way to be saved","buddhist","asserted",69,"aravanar"]],"ag":["மாதவி","அறவண அடிகள்","வயந்த மாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி","வயந்த மாலை"],"ch":"mm:3"},"mm:3:1-25":{"book":"manimekalai","num":16,"t":["வயந்தமாலைக்கு மாதவி உரைத்த","உயங்கு நோய் வருத்தத்து உரைமுன் தோன்றி","மா மலர் நாற்றம் போல் மணிமேகலைக்கு","ஏது நிகழ்ச்சி எதிர்ந்துளது ஆதலின்","தந்தையும் தாயும் தாம் நனி உழந்த","வெந் துயர் இடும்பை செவிஅகம் வெதுப்ப","காதல் நெஞ்சம் கலங்கிக் காரிகை","மாதர் செங் கண் வரி வனப்பு அழித்து","புலம்பு நீர் உருட்டிப் பொதி அவிழ் நறு மலர்","இலங்கு இதழ் மாலையை இட்டு நீராட்ட","மாதவி மணிமேகலை முகம் நோக்கி","தாமரை தண் மதி சேர்ந்தது போல","காமர் செங் கையின் கண்ணீர் மாற்றி","'தூ நீர் மாலை தூத்தகை இழந்தது","நிகர் மலர் நீயே கொணர்வாய்' என்றலும்","மது மலர்க் குழலியொடு மா மலர் தொடுக்கும்","சுதமதி கேட்டு துயரொடும் கூறும்","'குரவர்க்கு உற்ற கொடுந் துயர் கேட்டு","தணியாத் துன்பம் தலைத்தலை எய்தும்","மணிமேகலை தன் மதி முகம் தன்னுள்","அணி திகழ் நீலத்து ஆய் மலர் ஒட்டிய","கடை மணி உகு நீர் கண்டனன் ஆயின்","படை இட்டு நடுங்கும் காமன் பாவையை","ஆடவர் கண்டால் அகறலும் உண்டோ?","பேடியர் அன்றோ பெற்றியின் நின்றிடின்?"],"p":"","r":"As Madhavi tells Vayantamalai of the suffering of Manimekalai's father and mother, the girl, hearing it, weeps onto the flower garland she is stringing; Madhavi wipes her tears and says the garland, wet with them, has lost its purity, so she must fetch fresh flowers herself, and Sudhamathi, stringing flowers beside her, answers in grief that no man who saw tears falling from the girl's eyes could turn away from one before whom Kama himself would lay down his bow, unless he were no man.","n":"The whole errand to the grove, and so the prince's pursuit, starts from a garland spoiled by tears for Kovalan and Kannaki: Madhavi's word தூத்தகை இழந்தது (14) makes grief a pollution of an offering. Sudhamathi's warning (23-25) is that the renunciant's grief makes her more desirable, not less. The unit was extended by one line, from 24 to 25, because line 25 (பேடியர் அன்றோ ...) ends the question begun at 23.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 3, lines 1-25","lb":"MM3:1-25","ev":"Manimekalai weeps on hearing of her parents' suffering and spoils the garland; Madhavi sends her to gather fresh flowers, and Sudhamathi objects that men will not leave such a girl alone.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","மாதவி","சுதமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:4"},"mm:3:26-41":{"book":"manimekalai","num":17,"t":["ஆங்கனம் அன்றியும் அணி இழை! கேளாய்","ஈங்கு இந் நகரத்து யான் வரும் காரணம்","பாராவாரப் பல் வளம் பழுநிய","காராளர் சண்பையில் கௌசிகன் என்போன்","இருபிறப்பாளன் ஒரு மகள் உள்ளேன்","ஒரு தனி அஞ்சேன் ஒரா நெஞ்சமோடு","ஆராமத்திடை அலர் கொய்வேன் தனை","மாருதவேகன் என்பான் ஓர் விஞ்சையன்","திரு விழை மூதூர் தேவர்கோற்கு எடுத்த","பெரு விழாக் காணும் பெற்றியின் வருவோன்","தாரன் மாலையன் தமனியப் பூணினன்","பாரோர் காணாப் பலர் தொழு படிமையன்","எடுத்தனன் எற் கொண்டு எழுந்தனன் விசும்பில்","படுத்தனன் ஆங்கு அவன் பான்மையேன் ஆயினேன்","ஆங்கு அவன் ஈங்கு எனை அகன்று கண்மாறி","நீங்கினன் தன் பதி நெட்டிடை ஆயினும்"],"p":"","r":"Sudhamathi tells Madhavi why she is in Puhar: she is the only daughter of Kausikan, a twice-born man of Champai, and while she gathered flowers alone and unafraid in a garden, the vidyadhara Marutavegan, coming in garlands and gold to see the great festival held for the king of the gods, seized her, rose into the sky, lay with her, made her his, and then left her here and went off to his own far city.","n":"Her own story is the argument: a girl who picks flowers alone (ஒரு தனி, 31) at festival time can be carried off, and so Manimekalai must not go alone (43). The sky-being comes to the very festival now running, so the danger is present, not past. She tells her abandonment flatly, with no blame word for him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pirivu","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 3, lines 26-41","lb":"MM3:26-41","ev":"Sudhamathi tells how the vidyadhara Marutavegan carried her off from a garden in Champai, made her his and abandoned her in Puhar.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதமதி","மாருதவேகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதமதி","மாதவி"],"ch":"mm:4"},"mm:3:42-51":{"book":"manimekalai","num":18,"t":["மணிப் பூங் கொம்பர் மணிமேகலை தான்","தனித்து அலர் கொய்யும் தகைமையள் அல்லள்","பல் மலர் அடுக்கிய நல் மரப் பந்தர்","இலவந்திகையின் எயில் புறம் போகின்","உலக மன்னவன் உழையோர் ஆங்கு உளர்","விண்ணவர் கோமான் விழாக் கொள் நல் நாள்","மண்ணவர் விழையார் வானவர் அல்லது","பாடு வண்டு இமிரா பல் மரம் யாவையும்","வாடா மா மலர் மாலைகள் தூக்கலின்","\"கைபெய் பாசத்துப் பூதம் காக்கும்\" என்று"],"p":"","r":"Sudhamathi says Manimekalai is not one to gather flowers alone, and goes through the gardens: beyond the wall the Ilavanthikai garden is full of the king's men, and on Indra's festival day men of earth do not seek out the gardens the gods take, whose trees hang unfading garlands and no bee hums in them, so knowing people keep away from the royal park, saying a spirit with a noose in its hand guards it.","n":"The gardens are ruled out one by one for different dangers: the king's men, the gods' claim on the festival day, and a guarding spirit. Unfading garlands and silent bees are the signs of the gods' presence. The sentence that begins at 51 ends at 52 (உய்யானத்திடை உணர்ந்தோர் செல்லார்), in the next unit; the boundary was left in place because other chapters already link to 3:52-68.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","vizhavu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 3, lines 42-51","lb":"MM3:42-51","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதமதி","மாதவி"],"ch":"mm:4"},"mm:3:52-68":{"book":"manimekalai","num":19,"t":["உய்யானத்திடை உணர்ந்தோர் செல்லார்","வெங்கதிர் வெம்மையின் விரி சிறை இழந்த","சம்பாதி இருந்த சம்பாதி வனமும்","தவா நீர்க் காவிரிப் பாவை தன் தாதை","கவேரன் ஆங்கு இருந்த கவேர வனமும்","மூப்பு உடை முதுமைய தாக்கு அணங்கு உடைய","யாப்பு உடைத்தாக அறிந்தோர் எய்தார்","அருளும் அன்பும் ஆர் உயிர் ஓம்பும்","ஒரு பெரும் பூட்கையும் ஒழியா நோன்பின்","பகவனது ஆணையின் பல் மரம் பூக்கும்","உவவனம் என்பது ஒன்று உண்டு அதன் உள்ளது","விளிப்பு அறைபோகாது மெய் புறத்து இடூஉம்","பளிக்கறை மண்டபம் உண்டு அதன் உள்ளது","தூ நிற மா மணிச் சுடர் ஒளி விரிந்த","தாமரைப் பீடிகை தான் உண்டு ஆங்கு இடின்","அரும்பு அவிழ்செய்யும் அலர்ந்தன வாடா","சுரும்பு இனம் மூசா தொல் யாண்டு கழியினும்"],"p":"","r":"Sudhamathi goes on: the knowing do not enter the Champati grove where the vulture Champati, who lost his wings to the sun's heat, once sat, nor the Kavera grove where Kaveran, father of the Kaveri maiden, sat, for both are known to hold old striking spirits; but there is the Uvavanam, where many trees flower by the command of the Bhagavan, whose unfailing vow was grace, love and the great resolve to guard all life, and in it a crystal pavilion that lets no sound out but shows the body outside, with a lotus seat of shining gem where buds laid on it open and flowers never fade and are never swarmed by bees though years pass.","n":"The one safe grove is safe because it belongs to the Buddha (பகவன்), set against two groves of old local spirits; the epic's first Buddhist place in Puhar is introduced as a refuge from anangu. The crystal pavilion that shows the body but keeps sound in is the device the next kaathai uses to shut Manimekalai away from the prince while he can see her. Line 52 finishes the sentence of 3:42-51.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","anangu","nonbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 3, lines 52-68","lb":"MM3:52-68","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதமதி","மாதவி"],"ch":"mm:4"},"mm:3:69-81":{"book":"manimekalai","num":20,"t":["மறந்தேன் அதன் திறம் மாதவி கேளாய்","கடம் பூண்டு ஓர் தெய்வம் கருத்திடை வைத்தோர்","ஆங்கு அவர் அடிக்கு இடின் அவர் அடி தான் உறும்","நீங்காது யாங்கணும் நினைப்பிலராய் இடின்","\"ஈங்கு இதன் காரணம் என்னை?\" என்றியேல்","\"சிந்தை இன்றியும் செய் வினை உறும்\" எனும்","வெந் திறல் நோன்பிகள் விழுமம் கொள்ளவும்","\"செய் வினை, சிந்தை இன்று எனின் யாவதும்","எய்தாது\" என்போர்க்கு ஏது ஆகவும்","பயம் கெழு மா மலர் இட்டுக்காட்ட","மயன் பண்டு இழைத்த மரபினது அது தான்","அவ் வனம் அல்லது அணி இழை! நின் மகள்","செவ்வனம் செல்லும் செம்மை தான் இலள்"],"p":"","r":"Sudhamathi adds what she had forgotten: if those who have taken a vow and hold a god in mind lay flowers on the seat, the flowers reach that god's feet, but if they are laid without any thought, they stay where they are; Maya made the seat so of old, showing it with flowers, to grieve the fierce ascetics who say a deed done even without intention reaches its doer, and to be proof for those who say a deed without intention comes to nothing; and other than that grove, she says, there is no fit place for Madhavi's daughter to go.","n":"This is the epic's first doctrinal claim, made by a device rather than an argument: the seat settles a debate by showing it. The losing side is named only as வெந் திறல் நோன்பிகள், 'vow-keepers of fierce strength' (75); I tag them Jain because the view that an unintended act still binds is theirs and because the next unit's naked, fasting ascetic is called by the same word, உண்ணா நோன்பி (90, 102), but the unit does not name the school. The winning view, that the deed lies in the intention (சிந்தை), is the Buddhist one, and the seat stands in the Buddha's grove (61).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","samayam","nonbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 3, lines 69-81","lb":"MM3:69-81","ev":"","cl":[["a deed done even without intention reaches its doer and bears fruit","jain","refuted",74,"suthamathi"],["a deed done without intention bears no fruit at all","buddhist","asserted",76,"suthamathi"]],"ag":["மயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதமதி","மாதவி"],"ch":"mm:4"},"mm:3:82-103":{"book":"manimekalai","num":21,"t":["'மணிமேகலையொடு மா மலர் கொய்ய","அணி இழை நல்லாய்! யானும் போவல்' என்று","அணிப் பூங் கொம்பர் அவளொடும் கூடி","மணித் தேர் வீதியில் சுதமதி செல்வுழீஇ","சிமிலிக் கரண்டையன் நுழை கோல் பிரம்பினன்","தவல் அருஞ் சிறப்பின் அராந்தாணத்து உளோன்","நாணமும் உடையும் நன்கணம் நீத்து","காணா உயிர்க்கும் கையற்று ஏங்கி","உண்ணா நோன்போடு உயவல் யானையின்","மண்ணா மேனியன் வருவோன் தன்னை","'வந்தீர் அடிகள்! நும் மலர் அடி தொழுதேன்","எம் தம் அடிகள்! எம் உரை கேண்மோ","அழுக்கு உடை யாக்கையில் புகுந்த நும் உயிர்","புழுக்கறைப் பட்டோர் போன்று உளம் வருந்தாது","இம்மையும் மறுமையும் இறுதி இல் இன்பமும்","தன் வயின் தரூஉம் என் தலைமகன் உரைத்தது","கொலையும் உண்டோ கொழு மடல் தெங்கின்","விளை பூந் தேறலில் மெய்த் தவத்தீரே!","உண்டு தெளிந்து இவ் யோகத்து உறு பயன்","கண்டால் எம்மையும் கையுதிர்க்கொணம் என","உண்ணா நோன்பி தன்னொடும் சூளுற்று","'உண்ம்' என இரக்கும் ஓர் களிமகன் பின்னரும்"],"p":"","r":"Saying she too will go with Manimekalai to gather flowers, Sudhamathi sets out with her down the street of chariots, where a naked ascetic of the Jain dwelling, with a water pot in a sling and a cane staff, who has left shame and clothing, grieves even for lives he cannot see and goes unwashed under a vow of fasting, is followed by a drunk who bows to his feet and pleads that the master's soul, lodged in a filthy body, should not suffer like a prisoner in a stinking cell, that his own master said toddy brings this world, the next and endless joy, that there is no killing in coconut toddy, and that if the ascetic drinks and sees the fruit of this yoga he may then cast them off.","n":"The drunk argues on the ascetic's own ground: the Jain rule is not to harm life, and he answers that toddy kills nothing (98), so it should be allowed; he even calls drinking a yoga (100). The ascetic's practice is described without mockery (care for unseen lives, 89), and the comedy falls on the drunk who swears and begs him. Who the drunk's தலைமகன் (97) is, a teacher or a god of drink, the unit does not say; கையுதிர்க்கொணம் (101) is a rare form I read as 'you may cast us off'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kal","thavam","kollaamai","nonbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 3, lines 82-103","lb":"MM3:82-103","ev":"Sudhamathi and Manimekalai walk through the festival street, where a drunk begs a naked Jain ascetic to drink toddy.","cl":[["drinking coconut toddy kills nothing, so an ascetic of non-harm may drink it","general","reported",98,"kalimagan"],["toddy gives this world, the next and endless joy","general","reported",96,"kalimagan"]],"ag":["சுதமதி","மணிமேகலை","உண்ணா நோன்பி","களிமகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:4"},"mm:3:104-115":{"book":"manimekalai","num":22,"t":["கணவிர மாலையின் கட்டிய திரள் புயன்","குவி முகிழ் எருக்கின் கோத்த மாலையன்","சிதவல் துணியொடு சேண் ஓங்கு நெடுஞ் சினைத்","ததர் வீழ்பு ஒடித்துக் கட்டிய உடையினன்","வெண் பலி சாந்தம் மெய்ம் முழுது உரீஇப்","பண்பு இல் கிளவி பலரொடும் உரைத்து ஆங்கு","அழூஉம் விழூஉம் அரற்றும் கூஉம்","தொழூஉம் எழூஉம் சுழலலும் சுழலும்","ஓடலும் ஓடும் ஒரு சிறை ஒதுங்கி","நீடலும் நீடும் நிழலொடு மறலும்","மையல் உற்ற மகன் பின் வருந்தி","கையறு துன்பம் கண்டு நிற்குநரும்"],"p":"","r":"Others stand grieving behind a crazed man who wears oleander and erukku garlands, a rag and broken twigs for clothing, white ash smeared over his whole body, babbles senseless words to all, weeps, falls, wails, shouts, worships, rises, whirls, runs, hides in a corner, stretches out and fights his own shadow, and they watch his helpless suffering.","n":"Ash and erukku are marks the Saiva ascetic wore, and worship (தொழூஉம், 111) is among his acts, so the figure may be a god-possessed devotee seen from outside; but the narrator calls him only மையல் உற்ற மகன் (114), and the crowd's response is pity. The heaped verbs with lengthened vowels (110-113) make the line itself reel.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mayal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 3, lines 104-115","lb":"MM3:104-115","ev":"A crazed man smeared with ash raves and whirls in the street while people watch him in pity.","cl":[],"ag":["மையல் உற்ற மகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:4"},"mm:3:116-125":{"book":"manimekalai","num":23,"t":["சுரியல் தாடி மருள் படு பூங் குழல்","பவளச் செவ் வாய் தவள வாள் நகை","ஒள் அரி நெடுங் கண் வெள்ளி வெண் தோட்டு","கருங் கொடிப் புருவத்து மருங்கு வளை பிறை நுதல்","காந்தள் அம் செங் கை ஏந்து இள வன முலை","அகன்ற அல்குல் அம் நுண் மருங்குல்","இகந்த வட்டுடை எழுது வரிக்கோலத்து","வாணன் பேர் ஊர் மறுகிடைத் தோன்றி","நீள் நிலம் அளந்தோன் மகன் முன் ஆடிய","பேடிக் கோலத்துப் பேடு காண்குநரும்"],"p":"","r":"Others watch a performer in the eunuch dance, with curled beard, braided hair, coral lips, white teeth, long eyes, white ear-rolls, a brow like the crescent, red hands, young breasts, wide hips and a painted costume, the dance that the son of the one who measured the earth danced in the street of Bana's great city.","n":"The costume is a man's beard over a woman's body, the 'turned' figure of the பேடி. The legend (Kama, Vishnu's son, dancing in Bana's city) is the one the Silappathikaram gives among Madhavi's eleven dances, so Madhavi's art is here a street show the renunciant daughter passes by.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 3, lines 116-125","lb":"MM3:116-125","ev":"A street performer dances Kama's eunuch dance before a watching crowd.","cl":[],"ag":["பேடு"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:4"},"mm:3:126-145":{"book":"manimekalai","num":24,"t":["வம்ப மாக்கள் கம்பலை மூதூர்","சுடுமண் ஓங்கிய நெடு நிலை மனைதொறும்","மை அறு படிவத்து வானவர் முதலா","எவ் வகை உயிர்களும் உவமம் காட்டி","வெண் சுதை விளக்கத்து வித்தகர் இயற்றிய","கண் கவர் ஓவியம் கண்டு நிற்குநரும்","விழவு ஆற்றுப் படுத்த கழி பெரு வீதியில்","பொன் நாண் கோத்த நன் மணிக் கோவை","ஐயவி அப்பிய நெய் அணி முச்சி","மயிர்ப் புறம் சுற்றிய கயிற்கடை முக் காழ்","பொலம் பிறைச் சென்னி நலம் பெறத் தாழ","செவ் வாய்க் குதலை மெய் பெறா மழலை","சிந்துபு சில் நீர் ஐம்படை நனைப்ப","அற்றம் காவாச் சுற்று உடைப் பூந் துகில்","தொடுத்த மணிக் கோவை உடுப்பொடு துயல்வர","தளர் நடை தாங்காக் கிளர் பூண் புதல்வரை","பொலந் தேர் மீமிசைப் புகர் முக வேழத்து","இலங்கு தொடி நல்லார் சிலர் நின்று ஏற்றி","'ஆல் அமர் செல்வன் மகன் விழாக் கால்கோள்","காண்மினோ' என கண்டு நிற்குநரும்"],"p":"","r":"In the old city loud with strangers, others stand before the paintings that skilled painters made on the white plaster of every tall brick house, likenesses of every kind of being from the spotless gods down; and in the great festival street, women lift little boys, babbling and drooling on their five-weapon amulets, decked in gold chains, beads and loose silk, onto an elephant on a golden car, saying 'see the opening of the festival of the son of the one under the banyan', and stand watching.","n":"The festival for Indra also holds a procession for the son of Siva (ஆல் அமர் செல்வன் மகன், 144), as the first kaathai bade every god be worshipped. The infants, unable to hold their walk or their speech, stand against the girl in robes who passes them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","ilamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 3, lines 126-145","lb":"MM3:126-145","ev":"Women set their small boys on an elephant to watch the start of Murugan's festival while others gaze at the painted houses.","cl":[],"ag":["நல்லார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:4"},"mm:3:146-157":{"book":"manimekalai","num":25,"t":["விராடன் பேர் ஊர் விசயன் ஆம் பேடியைக்","காணிய சூழ்ந்த கம்பலை மாக்களின்","மணிமேகலை தனை வந்து புறம் சுற்றி","'அணி அமை தோற்றத்து அருந் தவப் படுத்திய","தாயோ கொடியள் தகவு இலள் ஈங்கு இவள்","மா மலர் கொய்ய மலர்வனம் தான் புகின்","நல் இள அன்னம் நாணாது ஆங்கு உள","வல்லுநகொல்லோ மடந்தை தன் நடை?","மா மயில் ஆங்கு உள வந்து முன் நிற்பன","சாயல் கற்பனகொலோ தையல் தன்னுடன்?","பைங் கிளி தாம் உள பாவை தன் கிளவிக்கு","எஞ்சலகொல்லோ? இசையுந அல்ல'"],"p":"","r":"Like the noisy crowd that gathered round Vijaya in his eunuch's guise in Virata's city, all these people gather round Manimekalai and say that the mother who put one so lovely into hard tapas is cruel and without decency, and ask whether, if she enters the grove, the swans there could match her walk, the peacocks learn her grace or the parrots equal her speech, and say it is not fitting.","n":"The crowd's simile casts Manimekalai as Arjuna in disguise, a figure whose true nature is hidden under an assumed form, and so, unintended by the speakers, sees the robe as a costume. Their blame of Madhavi repeats on the street what Chithirapathi sent to her as the town's talk in the last kaathai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","alar","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 3, lines 146-157","lb":"MM3:146-157","ev":"The festival crowd surrounds Manimekalai and blames her mother for putting her to tapas.","cl":[["a mother who sets a beautiful young daughter to hard tapas is cruel and lacks decency","general","reported",150,"puhar_oorar"]],"ag":["ஊர்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஊர்",""],"ch":"mm:4"},"mm:3:158-171":{"book":"manimekalai","num":26,"t":["என்று இவை சொல்லி யாவரும் இனைந்து உக","செந் தளிர்ச் சேவடி நிலம் வடு உறாமல்","குரவமும் மரவமும் குருந்தும் கொன்றையும்","திலகமும் வகுளமும் செங் கால் வெட்சியும்","நரந்தமும் நாகமும் பரந்து அலர் புன்னையும்","பிடவமும் தளவமும் முட முள் தாழையும்","குடசமும் வெதிரமும் கொழுங் கால் அசோகமும்","செருந்தியும் வேங்கையும் பெருஞ் சண்பகமும்","எரி மலர் இலவமும் விரி மலர் பரப்பி","வித்தகர் இயற்றிய விளங்கிய கைவினைச்","சித்திரச் செய்கைப் படாம் போர்த்ததுவே","ஒப்பத் தோன்றிய உவவனம் தன்னைத்","தொழுதனள் காட்டிய சுதமதி தன்னொடு","மலர் கொய்யப் புகுந்தனள் மணிமேகலை என்"],"p":"","r":"As all who said these things grieve, the grove appears as if covered by a painted cloth that skilled hands had made, its kuravam, maravam, kurunthu, konrai, tilakam, vakulam, vetchi, narantham, nagam, punnai, pidavam, thalavam, thazhai, kudasam, bamboo, asoka, serunthi, vengai, sanbakam and ilavam spreading their flowers so that her soft feet should not be marked by the ground; bowing to the Uvavanam that Sudhamathi has shown her, Manimekalai enters it with her to gather flowers.","n":"The list of flowers of every land is a carpet laid for her feet, so the grove receives her as the street did not. She bows (தொழுதனள், 170) before entering: the grove is a shrine, as 3:52-68 said.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 3, lines 158-171","lb":"MM3:158-171","ev":"Manimekalai bows to the Uvavanam and enters it with Sudhamathi to gather flowers.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதமதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:4"},"mm:4:1-18":{"book":"manimekalai","num":27,"t":["'பரிதி அம் செல்வன் விரி கதிர்த் தானைக்கு","இருள் வளைப்புண்ட மருள் படு பூம்பொழில்","குழல் இசை தும்பி கொளுத்திக்காட்ட","மழலை வண்டு இனம் நல் யாழ்செய்ய","வெயில் நுழைபு அறியா குயில் நுழை பொதும்பர்","மயில் ஆடு அரங்கில் மந்தி காண்பன காண்!","மாசு அறத் தெளிந்த மணி நீர் இலஞ்சி","பாசடைப் பரப்பில் பல் மலர் இடை நின்று","ஒரு தனி ஓங்கிய விரை மலர்த் தாமரை","அரச அன்னம் ஆங்கு இனிது இருப்ப","கரை நின்று ஆலும் ஒரு மயில் தனக்கு","கம்புள் சேவல் கனை குரல் முழவா","கொம்பர் இருங் குயில் விளிப்பது காணாய்!","இயங்கு தேர் வீதி எழு துகள் சேர்ந்து","வயங்கு ஒளி மழுங்கிய மாதர் நின் முகம் போல்","விரை மலர்த் தாமரை கரை நின்று ஓங்கிய","கோடு உடை தாழைக் கொழு மடல் அவிழ்ந்த","வால் வெண் சுண்ணம் ஆடியது இது காண்!"],"p":"","r":"Sudhamathi points out the grove to Manimekalai as a stage: bees and beetles make music while a monkey watches a peacock dance, a royal swan sits on a lone lotus in a clear pool while a waterhen drums and a koel calls to another peacock, and a screw-pine's white pollen dusts a lotus as street dust dims the girl's own face.","n":"The grove is described as a theatre (அரங்கு, முழவு, யாழ்), which keeps the courtesan's art in view at the moment Manimekalai has turned from it; the comparison at 15 (a lotus dulled by the dust of chariot streets like your face) quietly notes the grief that has worn her beauty, which Ettikumaran will also see.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 4, lines 1-18","lb":"MM4:1-18","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["சுதமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதமதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:5"},"mm:4:19-26":{"book":"manimekalai","num":28,"t":["மாதர் நின் கண் போது எனச் சேர்ந்து","தாது உண் வண்டு இனம் மீது கடி செங் கையின்","அம் சிறை விரிய அலர்ந்த தாமரைச்","செங் கயல் பாய்ந்து பிறழ்வன கண்டு ஆங்கு","எறிந்து அது பெறா அது இரை இழந்து வருந்தி","மறிந்து நீங்கும் மணிச் சிரல் காண்!' எனப்","பொழிலும் பொய்கையும் சுதமதி காட்ட","மணிமேகலை அம் மலர்வனம் காண்புழி"],"p":"","r":"Sudhamathi ends her tour with bees that mistake the girl's eyes for flowers and a kingfisher that dives at carp in a lotus pool and comes up hungry, and Manimekalai looks over the flowering garden.","n":"The kingfisher that strikes and loses its prey (23 to 24) is set just before the prince enters the story; whether it is meant as a sign of his failed pursuit is a reading, not something the lines say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 4, lines 19-26","lb":"MM4:19-26","ev":"Sudhamathi shows Manimekalai the grove and the pool, and Manimekalai looks at the garden.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதமதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதமதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:5"},"mm:4:27-46":{"book":"manimekalai","num":29,"t":["மதி மருள் வெண்குடை மன்னவன் சிறுவன்","உதயகுமரன் உரு கெழு மீது ஊர்","மீயான் நடுங்க நடுவு நின்று ஓங்கிய","கூம்பு முதல் முறிய வீங்கு பிணி அவிழ்ந்து","கயிறு கால் பரிய வயிறு பாழ்பட்டு ஆங்கு","இதை சிதைந்து ஆர்ப்ப திரை பொரு முந்நீர்","இயங்கு திசை அறியாது யாங்கணும் ஓடி","மயங்கு கால் எடுத்த வங்கம் போல","காழோர் கையற மேலோர் இன்றி","பாகின் பிளவையின் பணை முகம் துடைத்து","கோவியன் வீதியும் கொடித் தேர் வீதியும்","பீடிகைத் தெருவும் பெருங் கலக்குறுத்து ஆங்கு","இரு பால் பெயரிய ஒரு கெழு மூதூர்","ஒரு பால் படாஅது ஒரு வழித் தங்காது","பாகும் பறையும் பருந்தின் பந்தரும்","ஆதுல மாக்களும் அலவுற்று விளிப்ப","நீல மால் வரை நிலனொடு படர்ந்தெனக்","காலவேகம் களி மயக்குற்றென","விடு பரிக் குதிரையின் விரைந்து சென்று எய்தி","கடுங்கண் யானையின் கடாத் திறம் அடக்கி"],"p":"","r":"The mount of the king's son Udayakumaran, the elephant Kalavegam, goes mad with musth, breaks free of its keepers like a storm-tossed ship that has lost mast and ropes, runs through the cowherds' street, the chariot street and the market and throws all Puhar into uproar, until the prince rides it down on a swift horse and quells its frenzy.","n":"The syntax of 28 to 29 (உதயகுமரன் உரு கெழு மீது ஊர் மீயான்) is read here as the rider of the prince's own fearsome mount, so the elephant is his; the simile of the wrecked ship runs eight lines and the main verb comes only at 46. The deed echoes Kovalan's taming of a rogue elephant in the Silappathikaram.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 4, lines 27-46","lb":"MM4:27-46","ev":"The prince's elephant Kalavegam breaks loose in musth and rampages through Puhar; Udayakumaran overtakes it on horseback and subdues it.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","காலவேகம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:5"},"mm:4:47-60":{"book":"manimekalai","num":30,"t":["அணித் தேர்த் தானையொடு அரசு இளங் குமரன்","மணித் தேர்க் கொடுஞ்சி கையான் பற்றி","கார் அலர் கடம்பன் அல்லன் என்பது","ஆரங்கண்ணியின் சாற்றினன் வருவோன்","நாடக மடந்தையர் நலம் கெழு வீதி","ஆடகச் செய்வினை மாடத்து ஆங்கண்","சாளரம் பொளித்த கால் போகு பெரு வழி","வீதி மருங்கு இயன்ற பூ அணைப் பள்ளி","தகரக் குழலாள் தன்னொடு மயங்கி","மகர யாழின் வான் கோடு தழீஇ","வட்டிகைச் செய்தியின் வரைந்த பாவையின்","எட்டிகுமரன் இருந்தோன் தன்னை","'மாதர் தன்னொடு மயங்கினை இருந்தோய்!","யாது நீ உற்ற இடுக்கண்!' என்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"Holding the rail of his jewelled chariot, wearing the aar garland that alone shows he is not Murugan, the prince drives into the dancers' street and finds the merchant's son Ettikumaran lying with a woman on a flower bed by a window, his yal in his arms, still as a painted figure, and asks what trouble has struck him.","n":"The ஆர் (aar) garland is the Chola emblem; the line makes the prince's beauty godlike and his lineage the only mark that tells him from the god. Ettikumaran's stillness is the courtesan quarter's music stopped.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 4, lines 47-60","lb":"MM4:47-60","ev":"Udayakumaran drives into the dancers' street and asks Ettikumaran, who sits frozen beside a woman with his yal, what has troubled him.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","எட்டிகுமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:5"},"mm:4:61-84":{"book":"manimekalai","num":31,"t":["ஆங்கு அது கேட்டு வீங்கு இள முலையொடு","பாங்கில் சென்று தான் தொழுது ஏத்தி","மட்டு அவிழ் அலங்கல் மன்ன குமரற்கு","எட்டிகுமரன் எய்தியது உரைப்போன்","'வகை வரிச் செப்பினுள் வைகிய மலர் போல்","தகை நலம் வாடி மலர் வனம் புகூஉம்","மாதவி பயந்த மணிமேகலையொடு","கோவலன் உற்ற கொடுந் துயர் தோன்ற","நெஞ்சு இறை கொண்ட நீர்மையை நீக்கி","வெம் பகை நரம்பின் என் கைச் செலுத்தியது","இது யான் உற்ற இடும்பை' என்றலும்","மது மலர்த் தாரோன் மனம் மகிழ்வு எய்தி","'ஆங்கு அவள் தன்னை என் அணித் தேர் ஏற்றி","ஈங்கு யான் வருவேன்' என்று அவற்கு உரைத்து ஆங்கு","ஓடு மழை கிழியும் மதியம் போல","மாட வீதியில் மணித் தேர் கடைஇ","கார் அணி பூம்பொழில் கடைமுகம் குறுக அத்","தேர் ஒலி மாதர் செவிமுதல் இசைத்தலும்","\"சித்திராபதியோடு உதயகுமரன் உற்று","என்மேல் வைத்த உள்ளத்தான்\" என","வயந்தமாலை மாதவிக்கு ஒரு நாள்","கிளந்த மாற்றம் கேட்டேன் ஆதலின்","ஆங்கு அவன் தேர் ஒலி போலும் ஆய் இழை!","ஈங்கு என் செவிமுதல் இசைத்தது என் செய்கு?' என"],"p":"","r":"Ettikumaran bows and says that seeing Manimekalai, Madhavi's daughter, entering the grove with her beauty faded like a flower kept in a casket, brought back the cruel grief of Kovalan, so that his hand slipped onto a jarring string; glad at the news, the prince says he will bring her back in his chariot and drives to the grove gate, and Manimekalai, hearing the chariot, recalls Vasanthamaalai telling Madhavi that Udayakumaran, through Chithirapathi, had set his heart on her, and asks what she should do.","n":"Three voices meet here: Ettikumaran (65 to 71), the prince (73 to 74) and Manimekalai (79 to 84). The same sight moves the musician to grief for Kovalan and the prince to gladness; the merchant reads her faded beauty as mourning, the prince as a chance. Line 61 (வீங்கு இள முலையொடு) is read as Ettikumaran rising with the woman beside him; 69 to 70 (நீர்மையை நீக்கி வெம் பகை நரம்பின்) as grief driving the music out of his hand, and neither is sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","idumbai","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 4, lines 61-84","lb":"MM4:61-84","ev":"Ettikumaran tells the prince that the sight of Manimekalai in the grove, recalling Kovalan's fate, threw his playing off; the prince drives to the grove to carry her off, and Manimekalai, hearing his chariot, asks Sudhamathi what to do.","cl":[],"ag":["எட்டிகுமரன்","அரசு இளங் குமரன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:5"},"mm:4:85-104":{"book":"manimekalai","num":32,"t":["அமுது உறு தீம் சொல் ஆய் இழை உரைத்தலும்","சுதமதி கேட்டுத் துளக்குறு மயில் போல்","பளிக்கறை மண்டபம் பாவையைப் 'புகுக' என்று","ஒளித்து அறை தாழ் கோத்து உள்ளகத்து இரீஇ","ஆங்கு அது தனக்கு ஓர் ஐ விலின் கிடக்கை","நீங்காது நின்ற நேர் இழை தன்னை","கல்லென் தானையொடு கடுந் தேர் நிறுத்தி","பல் மலர்ப் பூம்பொழில் பகல் முளைத்தது போல்","பூ மரச் சோலையும் புடையும் பொங்கரும்","தாமரைச் செங் கண் பரப்பினன் வரூஉம்","அரசு இளங் குமரன் 'ஆரும் இல் ஒரு சிறை","ஒரு தனி நின்றாய்! உன் திறம் அறிந்தேன்","வளர் இள வன முலை' மடந்தை மெல் இயல்","தளர் இடை அறியும் தன்மையள்கொல்லோ?","விளையா மழலை விளைந்து மெல் இயல்","முளை எயிறு அரும்பி முத்து நிரைத்தனகொல்?","செங் கயல் நெடுங் கண் செவி மருங்கு ஓடி","வெங் கணை நெடு வேள் வியப்பு உரைக்கும்கொல்?","மாதவர் உறைவிடம் ஒரீஇ மணிமேகலை","தானே தமியள் இங்கு எய்தியது உரை? எனப்"],"p":"","r":"Trembling like a peacock, Sudhamathi bids Manimekalai enter the crystal pavilion, bolts it with her inside and stands five bow-lengths off; the prince halts his chariot, sweeps the grove with his eyes and, finding her alone, asks whether the girl has grown to know her slender waist, whether her lisping speech has ripened and her teeth come in like pearls, whether her long eyes now speak of Kama, and why she has left the ascetics' dwelling to come here alone.","n":"The prince's questions are a catalogue of a girl's coming of age in the stock images of love poetry, asked of a girl who has taken to the ascetics; his last question names exactly the change he does not accept (மாதவர் உறைவிடம் ஒரீஇ). Lines 89 to 90 (ஐ விலின் கிடக்கை) are read as Sudhamathi standing five bow-lengths from the pavilion, and 96 (உன் திறம் அறிந்தேன்) as the prince saying he knows who she is; both are doubtful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilamai","adaikkalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 4, lines 85-104","lb":"MM4:85-104","ev":"Sudhamathi locks Manimekalai inside the crystal pavilion; the prince arrives and questions Sudhamathi about the girl's ripening and why she is there.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதமதி","மணிமேகலை","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:5"},"mm:4:105-125":{"book":"manimekalai","num":33,"t":["பொதி அறைப் பட்டோர் போன்று உளம் வருந்தி","மது மலர்க் கூந்தல் சுதமதி உரைக்கும்","'இளமை நாணி முதுமை எய்தி","உரை முடிவு காட்டிய உரவோன் மருகற்கு","அறிவும் சால்பும் அரசியல் வழக்கும்","செறி வளை மகளிர் செப்பலும் உண்டோ?","அனையது ஆயினும் யான் ஒன்று கிளப்பல்","வினை விளங்கு தடக் கை விறலோய்! கேட்டி","வினையின் வந்தது வினைக்கு விளைவு ஆயது","புனைவன நீங்கின் புலால் புறத்திடுவது","மூப்பு விளிவு உடையது தீப் பிணி இருக்கை","பற்றின் பற்றிடம் குற்றக் கொள்கலம்","புற்று அடங்கு அரவின் செற்றச் சேக்கை","அவலம் கவலை கையாறு அழுங்கல்","தவலா உள்ளம் தன்பால் உடையது","மக்கள் யாக்கை இது என உணர்ந்து","மிக்கோய்! இதனைப் புறமறிப்பாராய்'","என்று அவள் உரைத்த இசை படு தீம் சொல்","சென்று அவன் உள்ளம் சேராமுன்னர்","பளிங்கு புறத்து எறிந்த பவளப் பாவையின்","இளங்கொடி தோன்றுமால் இளங்கோ முன் என்"],"p":"","r":"Distressed as one shut in a closed room, Sudhamathi asks whether women must teach knowledge, worth and royal custom to the heir of the strong one who, ashamed of his youth, put on old age to give a verdict, and then tells him one thing: this human body is born of deeds and is the field where deeds ripen, flesh to be cast out once its finery is gone, bound to age and die, a seat of disease, a hold for attachment, a vessel of faults, a bed of anger like a snake in its anthill, holding sorrow, care, helplessness, lament and a restless mind; knowing it so, he should turn from it; but before her words reach his heart, the girl appears before him through the crystal like a coral doll.","n":"The sermon is answered at once by the story: before it can reach him (123), the hiding place shows her body through the crystal, the very body she has just called flesh. Her opening is a modesty formula that is also a rebuke: the Chola who judged in youth (108) was wise without being told. The run of sorrows at 118 (அவலம் கவலை கையாறு அழுங்கல்) reads like the Buddhist list of griefs, but the unit names no school, so its claims are tagged general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","vinai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 4, lines 105-125","lb":"MM4:105-125","ev":"Sudhamathi tells the prince that the body is a karma-born thing of flesh, sickness and faults and urges him to turn from it; before her words take hold, Manimekalai's form shows through the crystal wall.","cl":[["the human body comes from past deeds and is the place where deeds bear their fruit","general","asserted",113,"suthamathi"],["stripped of its adornments the body is only flesh fit to be thrown out, subject to age, death and disease","general","asserted",114,"suthamathi"],["one who knows the body for what it is should turn his back on it","general","asserted",121,"suthamathi"]],"ag":["சுதமதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதமதி","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:5"},"mm:5:1-17":{"book":"manimekalai","num":34,"t":["இளங்கோன் கண்ட இளம் பொன் பூங்கொடி","விளங்கு ஒளி மேனி விண்ணவர் வியப்ப","பொரு முகப் பளிங்கின் எழினி வீழ்த்து","திருவின் செய்யோள் ஆடிய பாவையின்","விரை மலர் ஐங் கணை மீன விலோதனத்து","உருவிலாளனொடு உருவம் பெயர்ப்ப","ஓவியன் உள்ளத்து உள்ளியது வியப்போன்","காவி அம் கண்ணி ஆகுதல் தெளிந்து","தாழ் ஒளி மண்டபம் தன் கையின் தடைஇச்","சூழ்வோன் சுதமதி தன் முகம் நோக்கி","'சித்திரக் கைவினை திசைதொறும் செறிந்தன!","எத் திறத்தாள் நின் இளங்கொடி? உரை' என","'குருகு பெயர்க் குன்றம் கொன்றோன்' அன்ன நின்","முருகச் செவ்வி முகந்து தன் கண்ணால்","பருகாள் ஆயின் பைந்தொடி நங்கை","ஊழ் தரு தவத்தள் சாப சரத்தி","காமற் கடந்த வாய்மையள்' என்றே"],"p":"","r":"Through the crystal screen the prince sees the girl as if a painted image of Lakshmi stood there to stir Kama, wonders at what the painter must have imagined, then grasps that it is the dark-eyed girl herself, feels along the pavilion with his hand, and asks Sudhamathi what sort of girl hers is; she replies that even if the girl will not drink in his Murugan-like beauty with her eyes, she is one whose austerity comes by fate, whose curse is an arrow, and whose truthfulness has passed beyond Kama.","n":"Lines 1 to 8 are dense: the prince first takes the figure behind the crystal for a painting, and only at 8 does he see a living girl, which keeps the crystal room's double nature (it hides and shows) in play. Sudhamathi's three epithets at 16 to 17 claim for Manimekalai an ascetic's powers she has only just begun to seek: the austerity is called ஊழ் தரு, given by fate, so destiny and not yet her own practice is named as its source.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaamam","vaaimai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 5, lines 1-17","lb":"MM5:1-17","ev":"Udayakumaran sees Manimekalai through the crystal, tries the pavilion with his hand and asks Sudhamathi about her; Sudhamathi warns that the girl's austerity can curse.","cl":[["one whose austerity is given by fate has a curse for a weapon and is beyond desire","general","reported",16,"suthamathi"]],"ag":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","சுதமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதமதி","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:6"},"mm:5:18-31":{"book":"manimekalai","num":35,"t":["தூ மலர்க் கூந்தல் சுதமதி உரைப்ப","'சிறையும் உண்டோ செழும் புனல் மிக்குழீஇ?","நிறையும் உண்டோ காமம் காழ்க்கொளின்?","செவ்வியள் ஆயின் என்? செவ்வியள் ஆக!' என","அவ்விய நெஞ்சமொடு அகல்வோன் ஆயிடை","'அம் செஞ் சாயல்! அராந்தாணத்துள் ஓர்","விஞ்சையன் இட்ட விளங்கு இழை என்றே","கல்லென் பேர் ஊர்ப் பல்லோர் உரையினை","ஆங்கு அவர் உறைவிடம் நீங்கி ஆய் இழை!","ஈங்கு இவள் தன்னோடு எய்தியது உரை' என","'வார் கழல் வேந்தே வாழ்க நின் கண்ணி","தீ நெறிப் படரா நெஞ்சினை ஆகு மதி!","ஈங்கு இவள் தன்னோடு எய்திய காரணம்","வீங்குநீர் ஞாலம் ஆள்வோய்! கேட்டருள்!"],"p":"","r":"The prince retorts that no dam holds a swollen flood and no restraint holds once desire takes root, so let her be as ripe as she likes, and, turning away with a twisted heart, asks Sudhamathi, whom the city says a vidyadhara carried off, why she left her people's place to come here with this girl; she blesses him, prays that his heart not take the evil path, and begs the ruler of the sea-girt earth to hear her reason.","n":"The prince's maxim at 19 to 20 makes desire an irresistible force and so excuses in advance whatever he does; Sudhamathi's answer at 29 treats his heart as still able to choose. The word அராந்தாணம் at 23, the place where the vidyadhara left her, is rare and its sense here is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 5, lines 18-31","lb":"MM5:18-31","ev":"Udayakumaran brushes off Sudhamathi's warning and asks how she came to be with Manimekalai; Sudhamathi begins her account.","cl":[["no restraint can hold once desire has taken hold","general","reported",20,"udayakumaran"]],"ag":["சுதமதி","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:6"},"mm:5:32-41":{"book":"manimekalai","num":36,"t":["யாப்பு உடை உள்ளத்து எம் அனை இழந்தோன்","பார்ப்பன முதுமகன் படிம உண்டியன்","மழை வளம் தரூஉம் அழல் ஓம்பாளன்","பழ வினைப் பயத்தான் பிழை மணம் எய்திய","எற்கெடுத்து இரங்கி தன் தகவு உடைமையின்","குரங்கு செய் கடல் குமரி அம் பெருந் துறைப்","பரந்து செல் மாக்களொடு தேடினன் பெயர்வோன்","கடல் மண்டு பெருந் துறைக் காவிரி ஆடிய","வட மொழியாளரொடு வருவோன் கண்டு ஈங்கு","\"யாங்கனம் வந்தனை என் மகள்?\" என்றே"],"p":"","r":"Sudhamathi tells that her father, an old Brahmin of firm mind who had lost her mother, kept the ritual food rules and tended the fire that brings rain, grieved when through the fruit of old deeds she fell into a wrong union, searched for her among the crowds at the Kumari bathing ghat, and on his way back, coming with Sanskrit-speakers who had bathed in the Kaveri, found her here and asked how she had come.","n":"Sudhamathi gives her abduction two causes in one line (35): the fruit of former deeds and the wrong union itself, without blaming the vidyadhara. Her father's fire is credited with bringing rain (34), a claim the story then sets beside his ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","andhanar","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 5, lines 32-41","lb":"MM5:32-41","ev":"Sudhamathi tells how her Brahmin father searched for her after her abduction and found her in Puhar.","cl":[["a misfortune such as a wrong union comes from the fruit of former deeds","general","asserted",35,"suthamathi"],["the Brahmin's tending of the sacred fire brings rain","vedic","reported",34,"suthamathi"]],"ag":["பார்ப்பன முதுமகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதமதி","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:6"},"mm:5:42-65":{"book":"manimekalai","num":37,"t":["தாங்காக் கண்ணீர் என் தலை உதிர்த்து ஆங்கு","ஓதல் அந்தணர்க்கு ஒவ்வேன் ஆயினும்","காதலன் ஆதலின் கைவிடலீயான்","இரந்து ஊண் தலைக்கொண்டு இந் நகர் மருங்கில்","பரந்து படு மனைதொறும் திரிவோன் ஒரு நாள்","புனிற்று ஆப் பாய்ந்த வயிற்றுப் புண்ணினன்","கணவிர மாலை கைக்கொண்டென்ன","நிணம் நீடு பெருங் குடர் கை அகத்து ஏந்தி","\"என் மகள் இருந்த இடம்\" என்று எண்ணி","தன் உறு துன்பம் தாங்காது புகுந்து","\"சமணீர்காள்! நும் சரண்\" என்றோனை","\"இவன் நீர் அல்ல\" என்று என்னொடும் வெகுண்டு","மை அறு படிவத்து மாதவர் புறத்து எமைக்","கையுதிர்க்கோடலின் கண் நிறை நீரேம்","\"அறவோர் உளீரோ? ஆரும் இலோம்!\" எனப்","புறவோர் வீதியில் புலம்பொடு சாற்ற","மங்குல் தோய் மாட மனைதொறும் புகூஉம்","அங்கையில் கொண்ட பாத்திரம் உடையோன்","கதிர் சுடும் அமயத்துப் பனி மதி முகத்தோன்","பொன்னின் திகழும் பொலம் பூ ஆடையன்","\"என் உற்றனிரோ?\" என்று எமை நோக்கி","அன்புடன் அளைஇய அருள்மொழி அதனால்","அஞ்செவி நிறைந்து நெஞ்சகம் குளிர்ப்பித்து","தன் கைப் பாத்திரம் என் கைத் தந்து ஆங்கு"],"p":"","r":"Weeping over her, her father would not abandon her though she was no longer fit for the learned Brahmins, and lived by begging from house to house in the city, until one day a cow with a new calf gored him; holding his gut in his hands like a red garland he staggered into the place where she lived and begged the Jain monks for refuge, but they, angry with her too, said he was not one of them and drove them out, and as the two cried in the street whether any righteous people were left, a monk with a bowl in his palm and a golden robe, his face like a cool moon at noon, asked what had befallen them, cooled their hearts with kind words and put his bowl into her hand.","n":"The scene runs through three refusals and one answer: her father's own Brahmins (43), the Jain ascetics of spotless form (54), and then the street, where the cry அறவோர் உளீரோ (56) is answered by a monk of a third order. The Jains reject the dying man as not of their faith, so the episode sets the Buddhist's compassion against a refuge limited to one's own; that is the Buddhist work's framing, not a Jain account.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","andhanar","iravu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 5, lines 42-65","lb":"MM5:42-65","ev":"Gored by a cow, Sudhamathi's father seeks refuge with the Jain monks, who turn him and his daughter away; a Buddhist monk with a begging bowl stops to help them.","cl":[],"ag":["பார்ப்பன முதுமகன்","சமணீர்","சங்கதருமன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதமதி","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:6"},"mm:5:66-90":{"book":"manimekalai","num":38,"t":["எந்தைக்கு உற்ற இடும்பை நீங்க","எடுத்தனன் தழீஇ கடுப்பத் தலை ஏற்றி","மாதவர் உறைவிடம் காட்டிய மறையோன்","சா துயர் நீங்கிய தலைவன் தவ முனி","சங்கதருமன் தான் எமக்கு அருளிய","எம் கோன் இயல் குணன் ஏதம் இல் குணப் பொருள்","உலக நோன்பின் பல கதி உணர்ந்து","தனக்கு என வாழாப் பிறர்க்கு உரியாளன்","இன்பச் செவ்வி மன்பதை எய்த","அருளறம் பூண்ட ஒரு பெரும் பூட்கையின்","அறக் கதிர் ஆழி திறப்பட உருட்டி","காமற் கடந்த வாமன் பாதம்","தகைபாராட்டுதல் அல்லது யாவதும்","மிகை நா இல்லேன் வேந்தே வாழ்க!' என","'அம் சொல் ஆய் இழை! இன் திறம் அறிந்தேன்","வஞ்சி நுண் இடை மணிமேகலை தனைச்","சித்திராபதியால் சேர்தலும் உண்டு' என்று","அப் பொழில் ஆங்கு அவன் அயர்ந்து போய பின்","பளிக்கறை திறந்து பனி மதி முகத்துக்","களிக் கயல் பிறழாக் காட்சியள் ஆகி","\"கற்புத் தான் இலள் நல் தவ உணர்வு இலள்","வருணக் காப்பு இலள் பொருள் விலையாட்டி\" என்று","இகழ்ந்தனன் ஆகி நயந்தோன் என்னாது","புதுவோன் பின்றைப் போனது என் நெஞ்சம்","இதுவோ அன்னாய்! காமத்து இயற்கை?"],"p":"","r":"Sudhamathi finishes: the monk lifted her father onto his head, carried him to the dwelling of the ascetics, and this Sangadharuman, who freed him from the grief of death, taught them the nature of their lord, who lives not for himself but for others, turned the wheel of dharma out of compassion so that all beings might reach bliss, and passed beyond Kama, and she has no tongue for anything but his feet; the prince says he knows her case now and that Manimekalai may yet come to him through Chithirapathi, and leaves; Manimekalai opens the crystal room and confesses that though he scorned her as a woman without chastity, without austerity, without caste protection, one who sells herself, her heart went after the stranger, and asks whether this is the nature of desire.","n":"The monk is called மறையோன் at 68, a word for one who knows the sacred lore, and is then named Sangadharuman; the lines read most simply as one person, but whether the bowl-bearer and the teacher are the same is not certain. The words Manimekalai puts in the prince's mouth at 86 to 87 are not spoken by him in this kaathai or the last; she reads his scorn from his manner. Her question is the first crack in her renunciation, and she answers it herself at 91.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","kaamam","nenju","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 5, lines 66-90","lb":"MM5:66-90","ev":"Sudhamathi ends her story of the monk Sangadharuman; Udayakumaran leaves vowing to win Manimekalai through Chithirapathi; Manimekalai comes out and admits her heart has followed him.","cl":[["the Buddha lives not for himself but belongs to others, and turned the wheel of dharma from compassion so that beings may reach bliss","buddhist","asserted",73,"suthamathi"],["the Buddha has passed beyond Kama","buddhist","asserted",77,"suthamathi"],["a courtesan has no chastity, no austerity and no protection of caste, but sells herself for wealth","general","reported",86,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["சங்கதருமன்","அரசு இளங் குமரன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சுதமதி","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:6"},"mm:5:91-105":{"book":"manimekalai","num":39,"t":["'இதுவே ஆயின் கெடுக தன் திறம்!' என","மது மலர்க் குழலாள் மணிமேகலை தான்","சுதமதி தன்னொடும் நின்ற எல்லையுள்","இந்திர கோடணை விழா அணி விரும்பி","வந்து காண்குறூஉம் மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","பதிஅகத்து உறையும் ஓர் பைந்தொடி ஆகி","மணி அறைப் பீடிகை வலம் கொண்டு ஓங்கி","'புலவன் தீர்த்தன் புண்ணியன் புராணன்","உலக நோன்பின் உயர்ந்தோய் என்கோ!","குற்றம் கெடுத்தோய் செற்றம் செறுத்தோய்","முற்ற உணர்ந்த முதல்வா என்கோ!","காமற் கடந்தோய் ஏமம் ஆயோய்","தீ நெறிக் கடும் பகை கடிந்தோய் என்கோ!","ஆயிர ஆரத்து ஆழி அம் திருந்து அடி","நா ஆயிரம் இலேன் ஏத்துவது எவன்?' என்று"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai cries that if this is desire, let its power perish; as she stands with Sudhamathi, the goddess Manimekala, who comes to see the adorned festival of Indra, takes the form of a woman of the city, circles the jewelled seat and praises the Buddha as the wise one, the ford-maker, the meritorious, the ancient, highest in vows for the world, who destroyed fault and curbed hatred, the first who knew fully, who passed beyond Kama, who is refuge and who drove off the fierce enemy of the evil path, ending that she lacks a thousand tongues to praise his wheel-marked feet.","n":"The goddess's first epithets answer Manimekalai's curse on desire: காமற் கடந்தோய் (102) is the epithet Sudhamathi gave the girl at 17 and the Buddha at 77, so the one quality the girl has just failed to hold is the one the praise returns to. The jewelled seat (பீடிகை) is the Buddha's seat in the grove, worshipped in his absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kaamam","kutram","adaikkalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 5, lines 91-105","lb":"MM5:91-105","ev":"Manimekalai curses the power of desire; the goddess Manimekala, disguised as a city woman, circles the Buddha's seat in the grove and praises him.","cl":[["the Buddha destroyed fault, curbed hatred, knew all things fully and is the first","buddhist","asserted",100,"manimekala_deyvam"],["the Buddha passed beyond Kama and is a refuge","buddhist","asserted",102,"manimekala_deyvam"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","புலவன்"],"ch":"mm:6"},"mm:5:106-122":{"book":"manimekalai","num":40,"t":["எரி மணிப் பூங் கொடி இரு நில மருங்கு வந்து","ஒரு தனி திரிவது ஒத்து ஓதியின் ஒதுங்கி","நில வரை இறந்து ஓர் முடங்கு நா நீட்டும்","புல வரை இறந்த புகார் எனும் பூங்கொடி","பல் மலர் சிறந்த நல் நீர் அகழிப்","புள் ஒலி சிறந்த தெள் அரிச் சிலம்பு அடி","ஞாயில் இஞ்சி நகை மணி மேகலை","வாயில் மருங்கு இயன்ற வான் பணைத் தோளி","தருநிலை வச்சிரம் என இரு கோட்டம்","எதிர் எதிர் ஓங்கிய கதிர் இள வன முலை","ஆர் புனை வேந்தற்குப் பேர் அளவு இயற்றி","ஊழி எண்ணி நீடு நின்று ஓங்கிய","ஒரு பெருங் கோயில் திருமுகவாட்டி","குண திசை மருங்கில் நாள் முதிர் மதியமும்","குட திசை மருங்கில் சென்று வீழ் கதிரும்","வெள்ளி வெண் தோட்டொடு பொன் தோடு ஆக","எள் அறு திருமுகம் பொலியப் பெய்தலும்"],"p":"","r":"The narrator turns to evening on Puhar, pictured as a flowering creeper of a woman: its moat her girdle and anklets, its walls and gates her jewelled belt and arms, its two tall temple towers her breasts, its great temple her face, and the rising full moon in the east and the setting sun in the west her silver and gold earrings.","n":"The figure maps the city part by part onto a woman's body and ornaments, and one of the ornaments is named மேகலை (112), the girl's own name; several mappings (108 to 109, 114 to 116) are compressed and their exact correspondences are a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","naadu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 5, lines 106-122","lb":"MM5:106-122","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:6"},"mm:5:123-141":{"book":"manimekalai","num":41,"t":["அன்னச் சேவல் அயர்ந்து விளையாடிய","தன்னுறு பெடையைத் தாமரை அடக்க","பூம் பொதி சிதையக் கிழித்துப் பெடை கொண்டு","ஓங்கு இருந் தெங்கின் உயர் மடல் ஏற","அன்றில் பேடை அரிக் குரல் அழைஇ","சென்று வீழ் பொழுது சேவற்கு இசைப்ப","பவளச் செங் கால் பறவைக் கானத்து","குவளை மேய்ந்த குடக் கண் சேதா","முலை பொழி தீம் பால் எழு துகள் அவிப்ப","கன்று நினை குரல மன்று வழிப் படர","அந்தி அந்தணர் செந் தீப் பேண","பைந் தொடி மகளிர் பலர் விளக்கு எடுப்ப","யாழோர் மருதத்து இன் நரம்பு உளரக்","கோவலர் முல்லைக் குழல் மேற்கொள்ள","அமரக மருங்கில் கணவனை இழந்து","தமர் அகம் புகூஉம் ஒரு மகள் போல","கதிர் ஆற்றுப்படுத்த முதிராத் துன்பமோடு","அந்தி என்னும் பசலை மெய்யாட்டி","வந்து இறுத்தனளால் மா நகர் மருங்கு என்"],"p":"","r":"Evening's signs follow: a swan tears open a lotus that has shut on its mate and carries her to a coconut frond, the anril hen calls her mate at sunset, cows that grazed on lilies return lowing for their calves with milk dripping, Brahmins tend the evening fire, women bring lamps, lute players strum the marutham mode and cowherds take up the mullai flute, and evening comes to the great city with pale skin and grief like a woman who has lost her husband in battle going back to her kin's house.","n":"The closing simile (137 to 140) makes evening a war widow returning to her own people, a figure of loss that falls on the day Manimekalai has confessed her heart's going after the prince; the swan that frees its mate from the closed lotus (123 to 126) may glance at the girl shut in the crystal, but the lines do not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pasalai","kaimmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 5, lines 123-141","lb":"MM5:123-141","ev":"Evening falls on Puhar.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:6"},"mm:6:1-15":{"book":"manimekalai","num":42,"t":["அந்தி மாலை நீங்கிய பின்னர்","வந்து தோன்றிய மலர் கதிர் மண்டிலம்","சான்றோர் தம் கண் எய்திய குற்றம்","தோன்றுவழி விளங்கும் தோற்றம் போல","மாசி அறு விசும்பின் மறு நிறம் கிளர","ஆசு அற விளங்கிய அம் தீம் தண்கதிர்","வெள்ளி வெண் குடத்துப் பால் சொரிவது போல்","கள் அவிழ் பூம் பொழில் இடைஇடைச் சொரிய","உருவு கொண்ட மின்னே போல","திருவில் இட்டுத் திகழ்தரு மேனியள்","ஆதி முதல்வன் அற ஆழி ஆள்வோன்","பாத பீடிகை பணிந்தனள் ஏத்தி","பதிஅகத்து உறையும் ஓர் பைந்தொடி ஆகி","சுதமதி நல்லாள் மதி முகம் நோக்கி","'ஈங்கு நின்றீர் என் உற்றீர்?' என"],"p":"","r":"After the evening has passed the moon rises, its spot showing in the clear sky as a fault shows plainly in the worthy, pouring light over the grove like milk from a silver pot; the goddess Manimekala, a body bright as lightning given form, bows and praises the seat of the first lord who turns the wheel of dharma, takes the shape of a woman of the town and asks Sudhamathi why they are standing there.","n":"The simile at lines 3 to 5 runs the Kural's comparison backwards: the couplet compares the fault of the high-born to the moon's spot, and here the moon's spot is compared to the fault of the worthy, as if the moral were the better-known term. The lord of the wheel of dharma whose foot-seat she worships (11 to 12) is the Buddha by the epic's usage; the unit does not name him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 1-15","lb":"MM6:1-15","ev":"Manimekala comes to the grove at moonrise, worships at the Buddha's seat and, disguised as a townswoman, asks Sudhamathi why she stands there.","cl":[["a fault in the worthy shows as plainly as the spot on the moon","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:16-33":{"book":"manimekalai","num":43,"t":["ஆங்கு அவள் ஆங்கு அவன் கூறியது உரைத்தலும்","'அரசு இளங் குமரன் ஆய் இழை தன் மேல்","தணியா நோக்கம் தவிர்ந்திலனாகி","அறத்தோர் வனம் என்று அகன்றனன் ஆயினும்","புறத்தோர் வீதியில் பொருந்துதல் ஒழியான்","பெருந் தெரு ஒழித்து இப்பெரு வனம் சூழ்ந்த","திருந்து எயில் குடபால் சிறு புழை போகி","மிக்க மாதவர் விரும்பினர் உறையும்","சக்கரவாளக் கோட்டம் புக்கால்","கங்குல் கழியினும் கடு நவை எய்தாது","அங்கு நீர் போம்' என்று அருந் தெய்வம் உரைப்ப","'வஞ்ச விஞ்சையன் மாருதவேகனும்","அம் செஞ் சாயல் நீயும் அல்லது","நெடு நகர் மருங்கின் உள்ளோர் எல்லாம்","சுடுகாட்டுக் கோட்டம் என்று அலது உரையார்","சக்கரவாளக் கோட்டம் அஃது என","மிக்கோய்! கூறிய உரைப் பொருள் அறியேன்","ஈங்கு இதன் காரணம் என்னையோ?' என"],"p":"","r":"When Sudhamathi tells what the prince said, the goddess warns that he has not given up his gaze on the girl and, though he left calling the grove the ascetics' wood, will keep haunting the outer streets, so they should leave by the small western gate into the Chakravala Kottam, where great ascetics live, and come to no harm though the night passes; Sudhamathi answers that apart from the trickster vidyadhara Maruthavegan and the goddess herself everyone in the city calls the place only the cremation-ground shrine, and asks why she gives it another name.","n":"The goddess judges that Sudhamathi's sermon in the previous kaathai has not worked: the prince's look is unabated (தணியா நோக்கம்), and the answer now is refuge in place rather than argument. Sudhamathi's question sets up the whole kaathai as an explanation of a name: what the city calls the burning ground's shrine, the goddess calls by the name of the cosmos.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","adaikkalam","chakkaravaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 16-33","lb":"MM6:16-33","ev":"The goddess tells Sudhamathi to take Manimekalai by the west gate into the Chakravala Kottam; Sudhamathi asks why it bears that name when the city calls it the burning-ground shrine.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதமதி","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:34-47":{"book":"manimekalai","num":44,"t":["ஆங்கு அதன் காரணம் அறியக் கூறுவன்","'மாதவி மகளொடு வல் இருள் வரினும்","நீ கேள்' என்றே நேர் இழை கூறும் 'இந்","நாமப் பேர் ஊர் தன்னொடு தோன்றிய","ஈமப் புறங்காடு ஈங்கு இதன் அயலது","ஊரா நல் தேர் ஓவியப் படுத்துத்","தேவர் புகுதரூஉம் செழுங் கொடி வாயிலும்","நெல்லும் கரும்பும் நீரும் சோலையும்","நல்வழி எழுதிய நலம் கிளர் வாயிலும்","வெள்ளி வெண் சுதை இழுகிய மாடத்து","உள் உரு எழுதா வெள்ளிடை வாயிலும்","மடித்த செவ் வாய் கடுத்த நோக்கின்","தொடுத்த பாசத்துப் பிடித்த சூலத்து","நெடு நிலை மண்ணீடு நின்ற வாயிலும்"],"p":"","r":"The goddess says she will tell the reason, even if deep night comes upon Madhavi's daughter, and begins: beside this place lies the cremation ground that arose with the famous city itself, with four gates, one painted with a chariot that is never driven where the gods enter, one painted with paddy, cane, water and groves, one of plain white plaster with no figure inside, and one where a tall clay figure stands with folded red lips, fierce eyes, noose and trident.","n":"The cemetery is as old as the city (37 to 38): the city of festivals in the earlier kaathais and the place of the dead are founded together. The fourth gate's figure with noose and trident reads as Death's guardian, though the unit does not name him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","chakkaravaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 34-47","lb":"MM6:34-47","ev":"The goddess begins to explain the shrine's name by describing the four gates of the old cremation ground beside it.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:48-63":{"book":"manimekalai","num":45,"t":["நால் பெரு வாயிலும் பாற்பட்டு ஓங்கிய","காப்பு உடை இஞ்சிக் கடி வழங்கு ஆர் இடை","உலையா உள்ளமோடு உயிர்க் கடன் இறுத்தோர்","தலை தூங்கு நெடு மரம் தாழ்ந்து புறம் சுற்றி","பீடிகை ஓங்கிய பெரும் பலி முன்றில்","காடு அமர் செல்வி கழி பெருங் கோட்டமும்","அருந் தவர்க்கு ஆயினும் அரசர்க்கு ஆயினும்","ஒருங்கு உடன் மாய்ந்த பெண்டிர்க்கு ஆயினும்","நால் வேறு வருணப் பால் வேறு காட்டி","இறந்தோர் மருங்கில் சிறந்தோர் செய்த","குறியவும் நெடியவும் குன்று கண்டன்ன","சுடுமண் ஓங்கிய நெடுநிலைக் கோட்டமும்","அருந் திறல் கடவுள் திருந்து பலிக் கந்தமும்","நிறைக் கல் தெற்றியும் மிறைக் களச் சந்தியும்","தண்டும் மண்டையும் பிடித்துக் காவலர்","உண்டு கண் படுக்கும் உறையுள் குடிகையும்"],"p":"","r":"Within the guarded wall stand the great shrine of the goddess who dwells in the forest, where the heads of those who with unwavering heart paid life's debt hang from a tall tree; tall shrines of burnt brick, short and high like hills, raised by the eminent for the dead, whether ascetics, kings or women who died together with their husbands, kept apart by the four varnas; altars of offering to fierce gods, stone platforms, and the huts where guards with staff and skull-bowl eat and sleep.","n":"The cemetery keeps distinctions the dead no longer have: memorials are sorted by varna (56), and ascetics, kings and women who died with their husbands each get their own. The heads hung at the forest goddess's shrine (50 to 51) are of those who offered their own lives; the goddess describes them without comment here, but her account later (164 to 166) bears on what such offerings achieve. Taking உயிர்க் கடன் இறுத்தோர் (50) as those who offered their own heads is the plain sense, not a certain one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","vazhipaadu","nadukal","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 48-63","lb":"MM6:48-63","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:64-77":{"book":"manimekalai","num":46,"t":["தூமக் கொடியும் சுடர்த் தோரணங்களும்","ஈமப் பந்தரும் யாங்கணும் பரந்து","சுடுவோர் இடுவோர் தொடு குழிப் படுப்போர்","தாழ் வயின் அடைப்போர் தாழியில் கவிப்போர்","இரவும் பகலும் இளிவுடன் தரியாது","வருவோர் பெயர்வோர் மாறாச் சும்மையும்","எஞ்சியோர் மருங்கின் ஈமம் சாற்றி","நெஞ்சு நடுக்குறூஉம் நெய்தல் ஓசையும்","துறவோர் இறந்த தொழு விளிப் பூசலும்","பிறவோர் இறந்த அழு விளிப் பூசலும்","நீள் முக நரியின் தீ விளிக் கூவும்","சாவோர்ப் பயிரும் கூகையின் குரலும்","புலவு ஊண் பொருந்திய குராலின் குரலும்","ஊண் தலை துற்றிய ஆண்டலைக் குரலும்"],"p":"","r":"Smoke-flags, flaming arches and funeral pandals are everywhere; those who burn, who lay out, who put in dug pits, who shut in vaults and who cover with urns come and go night and day without pause; the dirge drum that shakes the heart, the cry of worship when an ascetic dies and the wailing when others die, the jackal's howl, the owl that calls the dying, and the cries of the flesh-eating kural and the horned owl fill it.","n":"Lines 66 and 67 list five ways of disposing of the dead side by side, burning, exposure, pit burial, vault and urn, with no one way set above another. Lines 72 and 73 set the ascetic's death, greeted with worship, against the ordinary death, greeted with weeping: the only difference the cemetery records between the renouncer and the rest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 64-77","lb":"MM6:64-77","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:78-93":{"book":"manimekalai","num":47,"t":["நல் நீர்ப் புணரி நளி கடல் ஓதையின்","இன்னா இசை ஒலி என்றும் நின்று அறாது","தான்றியும் ஒடுவையும் உழிஞ்சிலும் ஓங்கி","கான்றையும் சூரையும் கள்ளியும் அடர்ந்து","காய் பசிக் கடும் பேய் கணம் கொண்டு ஈண்டும்","மால் அமர் பெருஞ்சினை வாகை மன்றமும்","வெண் நிணம் தடியொடு மாந்தி மகிழ் சிறந்து","புள் இறைகூரும் வெள்ளில் மன்றமும்","சுடலை நோன்பிகள் ஒடியா உள்ளமொடு","மடைதீ உறுக்கும் வன்னி மன்றமும்","விரத யாக்கையர் உடை தலை தொகுத்து ஆங்கு","இருந் தொடர்ப் படுக்கும் இரத்தி மன்றமும்","பிணம் தின் மாக்கள் நிணம் படு குழிசியில்","விருந்தாட்டு அயரும் வெள்ளிடை மன்றமும்","அழல் பெய் குழிசியும் புழல் பெய் மண்டையும்","வெள்ளில் பாடையும் உள்ளீட்டு அறுவையும்"],"p":"","r":"An unpleasant noise like the roar of the sea never ceases there; thanri, oduvai and ulinjil trees rise and thorn and cactus crowd, bands of starving demons gather, and there are the halls under the vaagai, the vilam where birds feast on fat, the vanni where the cremation-ground vow-keepers light fires with steady hearts, the iratthi where those vowed in body gather skulls, and the open hall where corpse-eaters feast from pots of fat, with fire pots, skull-bowls, bier poles and shrouds lying about.","n":"The ascetics of the burning ground, the சுடலை நோன்பிகள் and விரத யாக்கையர், are listed among the cemetery's sights beside the corpse-eaters, not apart from them; the Buddhist goddess describes them without praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","thavam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 78-93","lb":"MM6:78-93","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:94-104":{"book":"manimekalai","num":48,"t":["பரிந்த மாலையும் உடைந்த கும்பமும்","நெல்லும் பொரியும் சில் பலி அரிசியும்","யாங்கணும் பரந்த ஓங்கு இரும் பறந்தலை","தவத் துறை மாக்கள் மிகப் பெருஞ் செல்வர்","ஈற்று இளம் பெண்டிர் ஆற்றாப் பாலகர்","முதியோர் என்னான் இளையோர் என்னான்","கொடுந்தொழிலாளன் கொன்றனன் குவிப்ப இவ்","அழல் வாய்ச் சுடலை தின்னக் கண்டும்","கழி பெருஞ் செல்வக் கள்ளாட்டு அயர்ந்து","மிக்க நல் அறம் விரும்பாது வாழும்","மக்களின் சிறந்த மடவோர் உண்டோ?"],"p":"","r":"Over the wide field lie torn garlands, broken pots, paddy, puffed rice and grains of offering; and the goddess asks whether, seeing the cruel worker Death kill and heap up ascetics, the very rich, young mothers just delivered, helpless infants, old and young without distinction, for this fiery burning ground to eat, there can be greater fools than people who revel in wealth and drink and live without desiring dharma.","n":"The list of the dead (97 to 99) puts the ascetic first and the infant beside the rich man: tapas, wealth and youth buy no exemption. The conclusion is aram, not fame or giving; the next unit tells of one who saw the same sights and died of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kootru","aram","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 94-104","lb":"MM6:94-104","ev":"","cl":[["death takes ascetic and rich, infant and old alike","general","asserted",99,"manimekala_deyvam"],["those who see the burning ground and still live revelling in wealth and drink without desiring dharma are the greatest fools","general","asserted",103,"manimekala_deyvam"]],"ag":["கொடுந்தொழிலாளன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:105-131":{"book":"manimekalai","num":49,"t":["ஆங்கு அது தன்னை ஓர் அருங் கடி நகர் என","சார்ங்கலன் என்போன் தனி வழிச் சென்றோன்","என்பும் தடியும் உதிரமும் யாக்கை என்று","அன்புறு மாக்கட்கு அறியச் சாற்றி","வழுவொடு கிடந்த புழு ஊன் பிண்டத்து","அலத்தகம் ஊட்டிய அடி நரி வாய்க் கொண்டு","உலப்பு இல் இன்பமோடு உளைக்கும் ஓதையும்","கலைப் புற அல்குல் கழுகு குடைந்து உண்டு","நிலைத்தலை நெடு விளி எடுக்கும் ஓதையும்","கடகம் செறித்த கையைத் தீநாய்","உடையக் கவ்வி ஒடுங்கா ஓதையும்","சாந்தம் தோய்ந்த ஏந்து இள வன முலை","காய்ந்த பசி எருவை கவர்ந்து ஊண் ஓதையும்","பண்பு கொள் யாக்கையின் வெண்பலி அரங்கத்து","மண் கணை முழவம் ஆக ஆங்கு ஓர்","கருந் தலை வாங்கி கை அகத்து ஏந்தி","இரும் பேர் உவகையின் எழுந்து ஓர் பேய் மகள்","புயலோ குழலோ கயலோ கண்ணோ","குமிழோ மூக்கோ இதழோ கவிரோ","பல்லோ முத்தோ என்னாது இரங்காது","கண் தொட்டு உண்டு கவை அடி பெயர்த்து","தண்டாக் களிப்பின் ஆடும் கூத்துக்","கண்டனன் வெரீஇ கடு நவை எய்தி","விண்டு ஓர் திசையின் விளித்தனன் பெயர்ந்து \"ஈங்கு","எம் அனை! காணாய்! ஈமச் சுடலையின்","வெம் முது பேய்க்கு என் உயிர் கொடுத்தேன்\" என","தம் அனை தன் முன் வீழ்ந்து மெய் வைத்தலும்"],"p":"","r":"A Brahmin youth named Sarngalan, going alone and taking the place for a guarded city, comes among sights that proclaim to the attached that the body is bone, flesh and blood: a jackal howling with a lac-dyed foot torn from a worm-ridden lump, a vulture tearing the girdled hips, a dog crunching a braceleted hand, a starving vulture eating sandal-smeared breasts, and a demon woman who lifts a dark head on the ash-white stage, scoops out and eats its eyes without stopping to ask whether hair or cloud, eye or carp, lip or flower, tooth or pearl, and dances; terrified, he flees crying to his mother that he has given his life to the old demon of the burning ground, falls before her and lays down his body.","n":"The demon woman's 'cloud or hair? carp or eye?' (122 to 124) is the lover's comparison of love poetry taken apart limb by limb: what the poems doubt between, she eats without asking. The parts eaten are the parts a lover praises: dyed foot, girdled hips, braceleted hand, sandal-smeared breast. Who 'proclaims' at 107 to 108 is not plain; the sights are read here as the subject. The boy sees exactly what is meant to teach detachment and dies of fear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","saakkaadu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 105-131","lb":"MM6:105-131","ev":"Sarngalan wanders into the burning ground at night, sees a demon woman eating a corpse's eyes and dancing, runs home in terror, and dies before his mother.","cl":[["the body is bone, flesh and blood","general","asserted",107,"manimekala_deyvam"]],"ag":["சார்ங்கலன்","பேய் மகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:132-153":{"book":"manimekalai","num":50,"t":["\"பார்ப்பான் தன்னொடு கண் இழந்து இருந்த இத்","தீத்தொழிலாட்டியேன் சிறுவன் தன்னை","யாரும் இல் தமியேன் என்பது நோக்காது","ஆர் உயிர் உண்டது அணங்கோ? பேயோ?","துறையும் மன்றமும் தொல் வலி மரனும்","உறையுளும் கோட்டமும் காப்பாய்! காவாய்","தகவு இலைகொல்லோ சம்பாபதி!\" என","மகன் மெய் யாக்கையை மார்பு உறத் தழீஇ","ஈமப் புறங்காட்டு எயில் புற வாயிலில்","கோதமை என்பாள் கொடுந் துயர் சாற்ற","\"கடி வழங்கு வாயிலில் கடுந் துயர் எய்தி","இடை இருள் யாமத்து என்னை ஈங்கு அழைத்தனை","என் உற்றனையோ? எனக்கு உரை\" என்றே","பொன்னின் பொலிந்த நிறத்தாள் தோன்ற","\"ஆரும்இலாட்டியேன் அறியாப் பாலகன்","ஈமப் புறங்காட்டு எய்தினோன் தன்னை","அணங்கோ பேயோ ஆர் உயிர் உண்டது","உறங்குவான் போலக் கிடந்தனன் காண்\" என","\"அணங்கும் பேயும் ஆர் உயிர் உண்ணா","பிணங்கு நூல் மார்பன் பேது கந்தாக","ஊழ்வினை வந்து இவன் உயிர் உண்டு கழிந்தது","மா பெருந் துன்பம் நீ ஒழிவாய்\" என்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"His mother Gotami, a woman who lives with a blind Brahmin and has no one else, clasps her son's body at the burning ground's outer gate and cries to Champapathi, guardian of the ghats, halls, old trees, dwellings and shrines, asking whether a demon or a spirit has eaten his life and whether she has no justice; the golden goddess appears asking why she is called at midnight, hears that the helpless boy lies as if asleep, and answers that neither spirits nor demons eat lives: with the Brahmin boy's terror as its occasion, his own former deed came, ate his life and went, so she should give up her grief.","n":"Champapathi denies that a spirit or demon kills and gives the deed (ஊழ்வினை) as the cause, with fright only as its occasion (பேது கந்தாக, 151): the cemetery's demon, so vivid in the previous unit, is reduced to an occasion. Gotami's word for herself, தீத்தொழிலாட்டியேன் (133), blames her own past deeds before the goddess does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","anangu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 132-153","lb":"MM6:132-153","ev":"Gotami carries her dead son to the burning-ground gate and calls on Champapathi, who appears and tells her that his former deed, not a demon, took his life.","cl":[["spirits and demons do not take a life; a former deed ripens and takes it, with fright only as its occasion","general","asserted",152,"champa"]],"ag":["கோதமை","சம்பு"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோதமை","சம்பு"],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:154-167":{"book":"manimekalai","num":51,"t":["\"என் உயிர் கொண்டு இவன் உயிர் தந்தருளில் என்","கண் இல் கணவனை இவன் காத்து ஓம்பிடும்","இவன் உயிர் தந்து என் உயிர் வாங்கு என்றலும்","முது மூதாட்டி இரங்கினள் மொழிவோள்","\"ஐயம் உண்டோ ஆர் உயிர் போனால்","செய்வினை மருங்கின் சென்று பிறப்பு எய்துதல்?","ஆங்கு அது கொணர்ந்து நின் ஆர் இடர் நீக்குதல்","ஈங்கு எனக்கு ஆவது ஒன்று அன்று நீ இரங்கல்","'கொலை அறம் ஆம்' எனும் தொழில் மாக்கள்","அவலப் படிற்று உரை ஆங்கு அது மடவாய்","உலக மன்னவர்க்கு உயிர்க்கு உயிர் ஈவோர்","இலரோ இந்த ஈமப் புறங்காட்டு","அரசர்க்கு அமைந்தன ஆயிரம் கோட்டம்!","நிரயக் கொடு மொழி நீ ஒழிக\" என்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"Gotami begs the goddess to take her life and give back his, so that he may look after her blind husband; the old goddess, pitying her, answers that once a life has gone it surely goes by its deeds into another birth, that bringing it back is not in her power, that the offer is like the false and wretched saying of those whose trade holds that killing is dharma, and asks whether there have not been those who gave their lives for the world's kings, and yet in this burning ground stand a thousand shrines for kings; she bids her leave such hell-bound words.","n":"Champapathi counts a mother's offer of her own life in exchange as killing, and so as the same error as the claim that killing is dharma (162 to 163); 'those whose trade' it is are not named, and the unit does not say whether sacrificers or others are meant. Lines 164 to 166 answer the offer from the cemetery itself: lives given for kings did not keep the kings alive. Reading 164 as 'give their lives in place of the kings' lives' is the plainer sense but not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","piravi","vinai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 154-167","lb":"MM6:154-167","ev":"Gotami offers her life for her son's; Champapathi refuses, saying a departed life goes on by its deeds and that a life given for another saves no one.","cl":[["once a life departs it goes to a new birth according to the deeds done","general","asserted",159,"champa"],["the saying that killing is dharma is a false and wretched word","general","asserted",162,"champa"],["a life given in place of another's does not keep that other alive","general","asserted",164,"champa"]],"ag":["கோதமை","சம்பு"],"ec":[],"spk":["சம்பு","கோதமை"],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:168-187":{"book":"manimekalai","num":52,"t":["\"தேவர் தருவர் வரம் என்று ஒரு முறை","நான்மறை அந்தணர் நல் நூல் உரைக்கும்","மா பெருந் தெய்வம்! நீ அருளாவிடின்","யானோ காவேன் என் உயிர் ஈங்கு\" என","\"ஊழி முதல்வன் உயிர் தரின் அல்லது","ஆழித் தாழி அகவரைத் திரிவோர்","தாம் தரின் யானும் தருகுவன் மடவாய்!","ஈங்கு என் ஆற்றலும் காண்பாய்\" என்றே","நால் வகை மரபின் அரூபப் பிரமரும்","நால் நால் வகையில் உரூபப் பிரமரும்","இரு வகைச் சுடரும் இரு மூவகையின்","பெரு வனப்பு எய்திய தெய்வத கணங்களும்","பல் வகை அசுரரும் படு துயர் உறூஉம்","எண் வகை நரகரும் இரு விசும்பு இயங்கும்","பல் மீன் ஈட்டமும் நாளும் கோளும்","தன் அகத்து அடக்கிய சக்கரவாளத்து","வரம் தரற்கு உரியோர் தமை முன் நிறுத்தி","\"அரந்தை கெடும் இவள் அருந் துயர் இது\" எனச்","சம்பாபதி தான் உரைத்த அம் முறையே","எங்கு வாழ் தேவரும் உரைப்பக் கேட்டே"],"p":"","r":"Gotami says that the Brahmins of the four Vedas declare in their books that the gods grant boons, and that if the great goddess will not grant one she will not keep her own life; Champapathi answers that unless the first one of the aeon gives life, if those who move within the ring of the world can give it she will too, and to show her power she sets before Gotami all who can grant boons in the Chakravala, the four classes of formless Brahmas, the sixteen of Brahmas with form, the two lights, the six kinds of gods, the many asuras, the eight kinds of beings in hell, the stars and planets, and asks them to end her sorrow, and every god there says just what Champapathi said.","n":"Gotami's appeal to the Veda's promise of boons is answered by assembling every god of the cosmos to deny it; the gods here are beings within the world-system, not above it. The ranks named, formless and form Brahmas in four and sixteen kinds within a Chakravala, are the Buddhist cosmology, which is the ground for tagging the claim Buddhist. Who 'the first one of the aeon' (172) is, whose giving Champapathi excepts, the unit does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaravaalam","iraivan","vedham","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 168-187","lb":"MM6:168-187","ev":"Gotami cites the Vedas' promise of boons; Champapathi summons every boon-giving god of the Chakravala, and all confirm that none can give back the boy's life.","cl":[["the gods grant boons to those who ask","vedic","refuted",168,"gotami"],["no being within the world-system, god or Brahma, can give back a departed life","buddhist","asserted",174,"champa"]],"ag":["கோதமை","சம்பு","தேவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சம்பு","கோதமை"],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:188-205":{"book":"manimekalai","num":53,"t":["கோதமை உற்ற கொடுந் துயர் நீங்கி","ஈமச் சுடலையில் மகனை இட்டு இறந்த பின்","சம்பாபதி தன் ஆற்றல் தோன்ற","எங்கு வாழ் தேவரும் கூடிய இடம் தனில்","சூழ் கடல் வளைஇய ஆழி அம் குன்றத்து","நடுவு நின்ற மேருக் குன்றமும்","புடையின் நின்ற எழு வகைக் குன்றமும்","நால் வகை மரபின் மா பெருந் தீவும்","ஓர் ஈர் ஆயிரம் சிற்றிடைத் தீவும்","பிறவும் ஆங்கு அதன் இடவகை உரியன","பெறு முறை மரபின் அறிவு வரக் காட்டி","ஆங்கு வாழ் உயிர்களும் அவ் உயிர் இடங்களும்","பாங்குற மண்ணீட்டில் பண்புற வகுத்து","மிக்க மயனால் இழைக்கப்பட்ட","சக்கரவாளக் கோட்டம் ஈங்கு இது காண்","இடு பிணக் கோட்டத்து எயில் புறம் ஆதலின்","சுடுகாட்டுக் கோட்டம் என்று அலது உரையார்","இதன் வரவு இது' என்று இருந் தெய்வம் உரைக்க"],"p":"","r":"Her grief gone, Gotami places her son in the burning ground and dies; then, to show Champapathi's power, in the place where all the gods had gathered the great Maya modelled in clay the ring-mountain bounded by the sea, Meru standing at the centre, the seven ranges around it, the four great continents, the two thousand small islands and all their regions, with the beings that live in each, so that the order of things could be known; this is the Chakravala Kottam, and because it lies outside the wall of the shrine of the dead, people call it only the cremation-ground shrine; that, says the goddess, is its origin.","n":"Gotami's grief ends because the gods' confession convinced her, and she dies of her own accord (189): the tale does not call it suicide or praise it. The cosmos model stands on the spot where every god admitted he could not undo a death, so the shrine teaches the order of the world from the place of that admission; the name the city uses forgets it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["chakkaravaalam","saakkaadu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 188-205","lb":"MM6:188-205","ev":"Gotami lays her son in the burning ground and dies; Maya models the whole cosmos in clay on the gods' meeting place, and this becomes the Chakravala Kottam.","cl":[],"ag":["கோதமை","மயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:6:206-214":{"book":"manimekalai","num":54,"t":["மதன் இல் நெஞ்சமொடு வான் துயர் எய்தி","பிறந்தோர் வாழ்க்கை சிறந்தோள் உரைப்ப","இறந்து இருள் கூர்ந்த இடை இருள் யாமத்துத்","தூங்கு துயில் எய்திய சுதமதி ஒழியப்","பூங்கொடி தன்னைப் பொருந்தித் தழீஇ","அந்தரம் ஆறா ஆறு ஐந்து யோசனைத்","தென் திசை மருங்கில் சென்று திரை உடுத்த","மணிபல்லவத்திடை மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","அணி இழை தன்னை வைத்து அகன்றது தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"As the goddess tells of the life of those who are born, Sudhamathi, her simple heart overcome by sorrow, falls asleep in the deep midnight; the goddess embraces Manimekalai, carries her thirty yojanas south through the sky to Manipallavam, girded by waves, sets her down there and departs.","n":"The goddess's story of Gotami is called the life of those who are born (பிறந்தோர் வாழ்க்கை, 207), a summary of birth itself; it puts Sudhamathi to sleep and leaves Manimekalai to wake alone on the island of her former births.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 6, lines 206-214","lb":"MM6:206-214","ev":"Sudhamathi falls asleep, and the goddess carries Manimekalai through the sky to Manipallavam and leaves her there.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதமதி","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:7"},"mm:7:1-14":{"book":"manimekalai","num":55,"t":["மணிமேகலை தனை மணிபல்லவத்திடை","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம் வைத்து நீங்கி","மணிமேகலை தனை மலர்ப் பொழில் கண்ட","உதயகுமரன் உறு துயர் எய்தி","'கங்குல் கழியின் என் கை அகத்தாள்' என","பொங்கு மெல் அமளியில் பொருந்தாது இருந்தோன்","முன்னர்த் தோன்றி 'மன்னவன் மகனே!","கோல் நிலை திரிந்திடின் கோள் நிலை திரியும்","கோள் நிலை திரிந்திடின் மாரி வறம் கூரும்","மாரி வறம் கூரின் மன் உயிர் இல்லை","மன் உயிர் எல்லாம் மண் ஆள் வேந்தன்","தன் உயிர் என்னும் தகுதி இன்று ஆகும்","தவத் திறம் பூண்டோள் தன்மேல் வைத்த","அவத் திறம் ஒழிக' என்று அவன்வயின் உரைத்த பின்"],"p":"","r":"Having left Manimekalai on Manipallavam, the goddess Manimekala appears before the sleepless prince Udayakumaran, who expects to have the girl in his hands by morning, and warns him that if the sceptre swerves the planets swerve, rain fails and no life is left, so that the king can no longer be called the life of all beings; he must drop the wrongful intent he has set on one who has taken up tapas.","n":"The goddess treats a prince's desire for a renouncer as a swerving of the sceptre itself, though he is only the heir and has judged no case; the fault is private desire, and the stakes she names are cosmic. Lines 11-12 can be read either as all beings calling the king their life or as all beings being the king's own life; on either reading the title lapses because the beings it rests on are gone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kodungol","mazhai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 7, lines 1-14","lb":"MM7:1-14","ev":"The goddess Manimekala appears to the sleepless prince Udayakumaran and warns him to give up his designs on Manimekalai.","cl":[["when the ruler's sceptre swerves, the planets leave their course, rain fails and living beings perish","general","asserted",8,"manimekala_deyvam"],["a king keeps the standing of being the life of all beings only while his rule holds them alive","general","asserted",11,"manimekala_deyvam"],["to set desire on a woman who has taken up tapas is a wrongful intent a ruler must give up","general","asserted",13,"manimekala_deyvam"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:8"},"mm:7:15-25":{"book":"manimekalai","num":56,"t":["உவவனம் புகுந்து ஆங்கு உறு துயில் கொள்ளும்","சுதமதி தன்னைத் துயிலிடை நீக்கி","'இந்திர கோடணை இந் நகர்க் காண","வந்தேன் அஞ்சல் மணிமேகலை யான்","ஆதிசால் முனிவன் அறவழிப்படூஉம்","ஏது முதிர்ந்தது இளங்கொடிக்கு ஆதலின்","விஞ்சையின் பெயர்த்து நின் விளங்கு இழை தன்னை ஓர்","வஞ்சம் இல் மணிபல்லவத்திடை வைத்தேன்","பண்டைப் பிறப்பும் பண்புற உணர்ந்து ஈங்கு","இன்று ஏழ் நாளில் இந் நகர் மருங்கே","வந்து தோன்றும் மடக்கொடி நல்லாள்"],"p":"","r":"The goddess then enters the Uvavanam, wakes Sudhamathi, tells her not to fear because she is Manimekala come to see the city's Indra festival, and says that since the cause for the young girl to enter the great sage's path of dharma has ripened, she has carried her by magic to Manipallavam, from where, having come to know her former birth, the girl will appear near the city in seven days.","n":"The goddess does not say she chose to act: she acts because a cause (ஏது) has ripened, so the rescue is the working-out of a condition, not a divine decision. Her self-announcement வந்தேன் அஞ்சல் மணிமேகலை யான் is word for word the formula she spoke to Kovalan's shipwrecked ancestor in the Silappathikaram (15, line 33).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ethu","piravi","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 7, lines 15-25","lb":"MM7:15-25","ev":"The goddess wakes Sudhamathi in the Uvavanam and tells her she has carried Manimekalai to Manipallavam, from where the girl will return in seven days knowing her former birth.","cl":[["one enters the Buddha's path of dharma when the cause for it has ripened","buddhist","asserted",20,"manimekala_deyvam"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:8"},"mm:7:26-39":{"book":"manimekalai","num":57,"t":["களிப்பு மாண் செல்வக் காவல் பேர் ஊர்","ஒளித்து உரு எய்தினும் உன்திறம் ஒளியாள்","ஆங்கு அவள் இந் நகர் புகுந்த அந் நாள்","ஈங்கு நிகழ்வன ஏதுப் பல உள","மாதவி தனக்கு யான் வந்த வண்ணமும்","ஏதும் இல் நெறி மகள் எய்திய வண்ணமும்","உரையாய் நீ அவள் என் திறம் உணரும்","\"திரை இரும் பௌவத்துத் தெய்வம் ஒன்று உண்டு\" என","கோவலன் கூறி இக் கொடி இடை தன்னை என்","நாமம் செய்த நல் நாள் நள் இருள்","\"காமன் கையறக் கடு நவை அறுக்கும்","மா பெருந் தவக்கொடி ஈன்றனை\" என்றே","நனவே போலக் கனவு அகத்து உரைத்தேன்","ஈங்கு இவ் வண்ணம் ஆங்கு அவட்கு உரை' என்று"],"p":"","r":"The goddess adds that the girl, even if she enters the city in a changed form, will not hide herself from Sudhamathi, that many things are caused to happen here on the day she comes, and bids her tell Madhavi of this visit, for Madhavi will know her: on the night Kovalan, saying there is a goddess in the sea, gave the child her name, she told Madhavi in a dream as vivid as waking that she had borne a great creeper of tapas who would cut the cruel harm and leave Kama helpless.","n":"The naming that the Silappathikaram tells as gratitude for a rescue at sea is given here a second, hidden meaning: the same night the goddess marked the infant for tapas. The forecast that she will come in another form (ஒளித்து உரு எய்தினும்) points ahead to her disguise as Kayasandigai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kaamam","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 7, lines 26-39","lb":"MM7:26-39","ev":"The goddess bids Sudhamathi tell Madhavi of her visit, recalling the dream she gave Madhavi on the night Kovalan named the child Manimekalai.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:8"},"mm:7:40-53":{"book":"manimekalai","num":58,"t":["அந்தரத்து எழுந்து ஆங்கு அருந் தெய்வம் போய பின்","வெந் துயர் எய்தி சுதமதி எழுந்து ஆங்கு","அகல் மனை அரங்கத்து ஆசிரியர் தம்மொடு","வகை தெரி மாக்கட்கு வட்டணை காட்டி","ஆடல் புணர்க்கும் அரங்கு இயல் மகளிரின்","கூடிய குயிலுவக் கருவி கண் துயின்று","பண்ணுக் கிளை பயிரும் பண் யாழ்த் தீம் தொடை","கொளை வல் ஆயமோடு இசை கூட்டுண்டு","வளை சேர் செங் கை மெல் விரல் உதைத்த","வெம்மை வெய்து உறாது தன்மையில் திரியவும்","பண்பு இல் காதலன் பரத்தமை நோனாது","உண் கண் சிவந்து ஆங்கு ஒல்கு கொடி போன்று","தெருட்டவும் தெருளாது ஊடலோடு துயில்வோர்","விரைப் பூம் பள்ளி வீழ் துணை தழுவவும்"],"p":"","r":"The goddess rises into the sky and leaves; Sudhamathi gets up in burning grief in a city fast asleep, where the drums and lutes of the dancing halls have fallen silent, women who cannot bear their lovers' straying sleep sulking and will not be soothed, and others sleep embracing their lovers on fragrant beds.","n":"The night catalogue that begins here (to line 86) is one long sentence whose subject is Sudhamathi, awake and alone among sleepers; the sleepers' loves and sulks are the courtesan quarter's life that Madhavi and her daughter have left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 7, lines 40-53","lb":"MM7:40-53","ev":"The goddess departs into the sky, and Sudhamathi rises in grief in the sleeping city.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சுதமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:8"},"mm:7:54-69":{"book":"manimekalai","num":59,"t":["தளர் நடை ஆயமொடு தங்காது ஓடி","விளையாடு சிறு தேர் ஈர்த்து மெய் வருந்தி","அமளித் துஞ்சும் ஐம்படைத் தாலி","குதலைச் செவ் வாய் குறு நடைப் புதல்வர்க்குக்","காவல் பெண்டிர் கடிப்பகை எறிந்து","தூபம் காட்டி தூங்கு துயில் வதியவும்","இறை உறை புறவும் நிறை நீர்ப் புள்ளும்","கா உறை பறவையும் நா உள் அழுந்தி","விழவுக் களி அடங்கி முழவுக் கண் துயின்று","பழ விறல் மூதூர் பாயல் கொள் நடு நாள்","கோமகன் கோயில் குறு நீர்க் கன்னலின்","யாமம் கொள்பவர் ஏத்து ஒலி அரவமும்","உறையுள் நின்று ஒடுங்கிய உண்ணா உயக்கத்து","நிறை அழி யானை நெடுங் கூ விளியும்","தேர் வழங்கு தெருவும் சிற்றிடை முடுக்கரும்","ஊர் காப்பாளர் எறி துடி ஓதையும்"],"p":"","r":"The sleeping city goes on: small boys worn out with pulling toy carts sleep wearing the five-weapon amulet while their nurses throw mustard against harm and wave incense, the birds of eaves, waters and groves are hushed, the festival's revelry and drums are still; and at midnight come the praises of the palace watch who keep the hours by the water-clock, the long cry of a starving elephant that has broken its restraint in its stall, and the drums of the town watchmen in the streets and lanes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","anangu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 7, lines 54-69","lb":"MM7:54-69","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:8"},"mm:7:70-84":{"book":"manimekalai","num":60,"t":["முழங்கு நீர் முன் துறைக் கலம் புணர் கம்மியர்","துழந்து அடு கள்ளின் தோப்பி உண்டு அயர்ந்து","பழஞ் செருக்கு உற்ற அனந்தர்ப் பாணியும்","அர வாய்க் கடிப்பகை ஐயவிக் கடிப்பகை","விரவிய மகளிர் ஏந்திய தூமத்து","புதல்வரைப் பயந்த புனிறு தீர் கயக்கம்","தீர் வினை மகளிர் குளன் ஆடு அரவமும்","வலித்த நெஞ்சின் ஆடவர் இன்றியும்","புலிக் கணத்து அன்னோர் பூத சதுக்கத்து","'கொடித் தேர் வேந்தன் கொற்றம் கொள்க' என","இடிக் குரல் முழக்கத்து இடும் பலி ஓதையும்","ஈற்று இளம் பெண்டிர் ஆற்றாப் பாலகர்","கடுஞ் சூல் மகளிர் நெடும் புண் உற்றோர்","தம் துயர் கெடுக்கும் மந்திர மாக்கள்","'மன்றப் பேய்மகள் வந்து கைக்கொள்க' என"],"p":"","r":"More night sounds reach Sudhamathi: the drowsy songs of shipwrights drunk on toddy at the harbour, the bathing of women freed from the impurity of childbirth amid smoke of mustard and snake-repelling herbs, the thunderous cry of warriors like tigers at the crossroads of the bhuta offering bali for the victory of the king of the bannered chariot, and the mantra-men who cure new mothers, infants, pregnant women and the wounded calling on the demoness of the common hall to come and take the offering.","n":"The warriors' cry at 79-80 repeats the Silappathikaram's festival formula for the offering at the bhuta's altar (கொற்றம் கொள்க, உருமுக் குரல்/இடிக் குரல்), here heard as one noise among many in the night. The sense of தீர் வினை மகளிர் (76), women whose task is done or women freed of the birth-pollution, is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["bali","vazhipaadu","mandhiram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 7, lines 70-84","lb":"MM7:70-84","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:8"},"mm:7:85-97":{"book":"manimekalai","num":61,"t":["நின்று எறி பலியின் நெடுங் குரல் ஓதையும்","பல் வேறு ஓதையும் பரந்து ஒருங்கு இசைப்ப","கேட்டு உளம் கலங்கி ஊட்டு இருள் அழுவத்து","முருந்து ஏர் இள நகை நீங்கிப் பூம்பொழில்","திருந்து எயில் குடபால் சிறு புழை போகி","மிக்க மா தெய்வம் வியந்து எடுத்து உரைத்த","சக்கரவாளக் கோட்டத்து ஆங்கண்","பலர் புகத் திறந்த பகு வாய் வாயில்","உலக அறவியின் ஒரு புடை இருத்தலும்","கந்து உடை நெடு நிலைக் காரணம் காட்டிய","அந்தில் எழுதிய அற்புதப் பாவை","மைத் தடங் கண்ணாள் மயங்கினள் வெருவ","திப்பியம் உரைக்கும் தெய்வக் கிளவியின்"],"p":"","r":"Troubled by all these mingled cries, Sudhamathi leaves the grove in thick darkness by the small gate in the western wall, goes to the Chakravala kottam that the goddess had praised, and sits at one side of the world's hall of charity whose wide gate stands open to all; there, as she is bewildered and afraid, the wondrous image painted on the tall pillar speaks with a divine voice.","n":"Sudhamathi takes refuge not in a temple of a god but in the open hall of charity of the burning-ground enclosure, where the speaking image is a painting; the help comes from a figure that shows causes (காரணம் காட்டிய, 94).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","aram","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 7, lines 85-97","lb":"MM7:85-97","ev":"Sudhamathi goes by night to the Chakravala kottam and sits in its hall of charity, where the painted image on the pillar begins to speak.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதமதி","கந்திற்பாவை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:8"},"mm:7:98-109":{"book":"manimekalai","num":62,"t":["'இரவிவன்மன் ஒரு பெரு மகளே!","துரகத் தானைத் துச்சயன் தேவி!","தயங்கு இணர்க் கோதை தாரை சாவுற","மயங்கி யானை முன் மன் உயிர் நீத்தோய்!","காராளர் சண்பையில் கௌசிகன் மகளே!","மாருதவேகனோடு இந் நகர் புகுந்து","தாரை தவ்வை தன்னொடு கூடிய","வீரை ஆகிய சுதமதி கேளாய்!","இன்று ஏழ் நாளில் இடை இருள் யாமத்து","தன் பிறப்பு அதனொடு நின் பிறப்பு உணர்ந்து ஈங்கு","இலக்குமி ஆகிய நினக்கு இளையாள் வரும்","அஞ்சல்' என்று உரைத்தது அவ் உரை கேட்டு"],"p":"","r":"The image addresses Sudhamathi by her former and present births, as the daughter of Ravivarman and queen of Thuchayan of the horse army who, bewildered at Tarai's death, gave up her life before an elephant, and as the daughter of Kausikan of Champai who came to this city with Marutavegan, Virai joined with her elder sister Tarai, now Sudhamathi, and tells her that in seven days at midnight her younger sister, who was Lakshmi, will come knowing both their births, so she should not fear.","n":"The image is first to reveal that the companions are sisters across births; it names only Sudhamathi's past and leaves Madhavi's (Tarai) implicit, which Manimekalai will supply when she repeats the same address in kaathai 11. Which of the sisters died first and how is told only here in passing, so the story behind 100-101 cannot be fixed from this unit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","sutram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 7, lines 98-109","lb":"MM7:98-109","ev":"The pillar image tells Sudhamathi her former birth as Virai and foretells that Manimekalai, once Lakshmi, will come in seven days knowing both their births.","cl":[],"ag":["கந்திற்பாவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கந்திற்பாவை","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:8"},"mm:7:110-134":{"book":"manimekalai","num":63,"t":["நெஞ்சம் நடுக்குறூஉம் நேர் இழை நல்லாள்","காவலாளர் கண் துயில்கொள்ளத்","தூ மென் சேக்கைத் துயில் கண் விழிப்ப","வலம்புரிச் சங்கம் வறிது எழுந்து ஆர்ப்பப்","புலம் புரிச் சங்கம் பொருளொடு முழங்கப்","புகர் முக வாரணம் நெடுங் கூ விளிப்ப","பொறி மயிர் வாரணம் குறுங் கூ விளிப்ப","பணை நிலைப் புரவி பல எழுந்து ஆலப்","பணை நிலைப் புள்ளும் பல எழுந்து ஆலப்","பூம்பொழில் ஆர்கைப் புள் ஒலி சிறப்பப்","பூங்கொடியார் கைப் புள் ஒலி சிறப்பக்","கடவுள் பீடிகைப் பூப் பலி கடைகொளக்","கலம் பகர் பீடிகைப் பூப் பலி கடை கொளக்","குயிலுவர் கடைதொறும் பண் இயம் பரந்து எழக்","கொடுப்போர் கடைதொறும் பண்ணியம் பரந்து எழ","ஊர் துயில் எடுப்ப உரவுநீர் அழுவத்துக்","கார் இருள் சீத்து கதிரவன் முளைத்தலும்","ஏ உறு மஞ்ஞையின் இனைந்து அடி வருந்த","மா நகர் வீதி மருங்கில் போகி","போய கங்குலில் புகுந்ததை எல்லாம்","மாதவி தனக்கு வழு இன்று உரைத்தலும்","நல் மணி இழந்த நாகம் போன்று அவள்","தன் மகள் வாராத் தனித் துயர் உழப்ப","இன் உயிர் இழந்த யாக்கையின் இருந்தனள்","துன்னியது உரைத்த சுதமதி தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"Shaken by the words, Sudhamathi waits as the watchmen sleep and the city wakes, conches sounding, elephants and cocks crying, horses and birds rising, flower offerings laid at the gods' altars and the traders' stalls, music and food spread at every door, until the sun rises from the sea; then, aching like a peacock struck by an arrow, she walks through the streets and tells Madhavi everything that happened in the night, and Madhavi, grieving alone that her daughter has not come, sits like a cobra that has lost its gem, like a body that has lost its life.","n":"The dawn is built of paired lines that repeat a sound with a new sense (பணை நிலைப் புரவி / புள், பூம்பொழில் ஆர்கை / பூங்கொடியார் கை, பண் இயம் / பண்ணியம்), so the whole city's waking is staged as a doubled music against which Madhavi alone does not stir. Madhavi has renounced and been taught that attachment breeds suffering, yet her grief for her child is drawn in the strongest terms of loss. The sense of புலம் புரிச் சங்கம் (114) is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pattru","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 7, lines 110-134","lb":"MM7:110-134","ev":"At dawn Sudhamathi tells Madhavi all that happened in the night, and Madhavi sits stricken at her daughter's absence.","cl":[],"ag":["சுதமதி","மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:8"},"mm:8:1-18":{"book":"manimekalai","num":64,"t":["ஈங்கு இவள் இன்னணம் ஆக இருங் கடல்","வாங்கு திரை உடுத்த மணிபல்லவத்திடை","தத்து நீர் அடைகரை சங்கு உழு தொடுப்பின்","முத்து விளை கழனி முரி செம் பவளமொடு","விரை மரம் உருட்டும் திரை உலாப் பரப்பின்","ஞாழல் ஓங்கிய தாழ் கண் அசும்பின்","ஆம்பலும் குவளையும் தாம் புணர்ந்து மயங்கி","வண்டு உண மலர்ந்த குண்டு நீர் இலஞ்சி","முடக் கால் புன்னையும் மடல் பூந் தாழையும்","வெயில் வரவு ஒழித்த பயில் பூம் பந்தர்","அறல் விளங்கு நிலா மணல் நறு மலர்ப் பள்ளித்","துஞ்சு துயில் எழூஉம் அம் சில் ஓதி","காதல் சுற்றம் மறந்து கடைகொள","வேறு இடத்துப் பிறந்த உயிரே போன்று","பண்டு அறி கிளையொடு பதியும் காணாள்","கண்டு அறியாதன கண்ணில் காணா","நீல மாக் கடல் நெட்டிடை அன்றியும்","காலை ஞாயிறு கதிர் விரித்து முளைப்ப"],"p":"","r":"While Manimekalai is thus carried off, on Manipallavam, an island girdled by the sea where conches plough the shore, pearls grow in the fields with red coral and pools of lily and kuvalai lie under shading punnai and screw-pine, the girl wakes on a bed of flowers in the moon-white sand and, like a life born in another place, sees neither her kin nor her town but only things she never saw, the long blue sea and the morning sun spreading its rays.","n":"The simile at line 14, வேறு இடத்துப் பிறந்த உயிரே போன்று, compares her waking to a rebirth in a strange place, before the next kaathai makes her recover her real former birth on this very island; the waking is already shaped as a birth that has forgotten what came before. The island is drawn as a lush shore (neythal things: conch, pearl, coral, punnai, screw-pine), so her fear is not of a wasteland but of an unknown beauty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","idumbai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 8, lines 1-18","lb":"MM8:1-18","ev":"Manimekalai wakes on the sands of Manipallavam at sunrise and finds neither her companions nor her town.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:9"},"mm:8:19-35":{"book":"manimekalai","num":65,"t":["'உவவன மருங்கினில் ஓர் இடம்கொல் இது!","சுதமதி ஒளித்தாய்! துயரம் செய்தனை!","நனவோ கனவோ என்பதை அறியேன்!","மனம் நடுக்குறூஉம் மாற்றம் தாராய்!","வல் இருள் கழிந்தது மாதவி மயங்கும்","மெல் வளை! வாராய் விட்டு அகன்றனையோ?","விஞ்சையின் தோன்றிய விளங்கு இழை மடவாள்","வஞ்சம் செய்தனள்கொல்லோ? அறியேன்!","ஒரு தனி அஞ்சுவென் திருவே வா!' எனத்","திரை தவழ் பறவையும் விரி சிறைப் பறவையும்","எழுந்து வீழ் சில்லையும் ஒடுங்கு சிறை முழுவலும்","அன்னச் சேவல் அரசன் ஆக","பல் நிறப் புள் இனம் பரந்து ஒருங்கு ஈண்டி","பாசறை மன்னர் பாடி போல","வீசு நீர்ப் பரப்பின் எதிர் எதிர் இருக்கும்","துறையும் துறை சூழ் நெடு மணல் குன்றமும்","யாங்கணும் திரிவோள் பாங்கு இனம் காணாள்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai cries out asking whether this is some spot beside the Uvavanam grove, reproaches Sudhamathi for hiding and causing her pain, says she cannot tell waking from dream and begs for an answer, calls on her friend to come since the night is over and Madhavi will be distraught, wonders whether the shining woman who appeared by sorcery has tricked her, and says she fears being all alone; then she wanders everywhere over the shore and dunes, where flocks of many-coloured birds with the swan as their king gather facing one another like kings in a war camp, and finds none of her companions.","n":"She suspects the goddess who saved her, விஞ்சையின் தோன்றிய விளங்கு இழை மடவாள் (25), of வஞ்சம்: the rescue that the story treats as providence feels to the rescued girl like a betrayal. The bird simile at 30 to 33 (a swan king, flocks massed like kings' encampments, பாசறை மன்னர் பாடி) prepares, in miniature and harmlessly, the war of the two Naga kings over the seat told at the end of the kaathai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","acham","idumbai","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 8, lines 19-35","lb":"MM8:19-35","ev":"Manimekalai calls for Sudhamathi, asks whether she is dreaming, suspects the goddess of a trick, and searches the shore for her companions in vain.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","சுதமதி"],"ch":"mm:9"},"mm:8:36-43":{"book":"manimekalai","num":66,"t":["குரல் தலைக் கூந்தல் குலைந்து பின் வீழ","அரற்றினள் கூஉய் அழுதனள் ஏங்கி","வீழ் துயர் எய்திய விழுமக் கிளவியின்","தாழ் துயர் உறுவோள் தந்தையை உள்ளி","'எம் இதில் படுத்தும் வெவ் வினை உருப்ப","கோல் தொடி மாதரொடு வேற்று நாடு அடைந்து","வை வாள் உழந்த மணிப் பூண் அகலத்து","ஐயாவோ!' என்று அழுவோள் முன்னர்"],"p":"","r":"Her hair coming loose and falling behind, she wails, calls aloud and weeps, and in her deep grief thinks of her father: the cruel deed that casts us into this misery ripened, so that he went to a foreign land with the woman of the round bangles and his jewelled chest suffered the sharp sword, and she cries 'O father!'","n":"Her lament names no king, no goldsmith and no guard: the sword stroke is set down wholly to the ripening of a cruel deed (வெவ் வினை உருப்ப, 40), where the Silappathikaram gives both the bent sceptre and the former deed as causes. And the deed is one that afflicts 'us' (எம், 40): she counts her own abandonment on the island and her father's death as one ripening, though the text does not say whose deed it was. Kannaki is not named and not called mother, only the woman of the round bangles (கோல் தொடி மாதர், 41), though in kaathai 2 Madhavi calls Manimekalai the great pathini's daughter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","idumbai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 8, lines 36-43","lb":"MM8:36-43","ev":"Wandering the shore, Manimekalai weeps and laments her father Kovalan, killed by the sword in a foreign land.","cl":[["a cruel deed, come to ripeness, brought the father to a violent death abroad and the daughter to her present misery","general","asserted",40,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","கோவலன்"],"ch":"mm:9"},"mm:8:44-63":{"book":"manimekalai","num":67,"t":["விரிந்து இலங்கு அவிர் ஒளி சிறந்து கதிர் பரப்பி","உரை பெறு மும் முழம் நிலமிசை ஓங்கித்","திசைதொறும் ஒன்பான் முழ நிலம் அகன்று","விதி மாண் நாடியின் வட்டம் குயின்று","பதும சதுரம் மீமிசை விளங்கி","'அறவோற்கு அமைந்த ஆசனம்' என்றே","நறு மலர் அல்லது பிற மரம் சொரியாது","பறவையும் முதிர் சிறை பாங்கு சென்று அதிராது","தேவர் கோன் இட்ட மா மணிப் பீடிகை","பிறப்பு விளங்கு அவிர் ஒளி அறத்தகை ஆசனம்","கீழ் நில மருங்கின் நாக நாடு ஆளும்","இருவர் மன்னவர் ஒரு வழித் தோன்றி","'எமது ஈது' என்றே எடுக்கல் ஆற்றார்","தம பெரும் பற்று நீங்கலும் நீங்கார்","செங் கண் சிவந்து நெஞ்சு புகையுயிர்த்துத்","தம் பெருஞ் சேனையொடு வெஞ் சமம் புரி நாள்","'இருஞ் செரு ஒழிமின் எமது ஈது' என்றே","பெருந் தவ முனிவன் இருந்து அறம் உரைக்கும்","பொரு அறு சிறப்பின் புரையோர் ஏத்தும்","தரும பீடிகை தோன்றியது ஆங்கு என்"],"p":"","r":"Before her as she weeps there appears the Dharma seat: a jewelled seat spreading bright rays, three cubits high and nine cubits across on every side, its circle laid out by rule and topped with a lotus square, called the seat made for the virtuous one, on which trees let fall only fragrant flowers and near which birds do not beat their wings, set there by the king of the gods, a seat of dharma on which births shine clear; over it two kings of one line who rule the Naga land below, each saying 'this is ours', could neither lift it nor let go of their great attachment and with red eyes and smoking hearts made fierce war with their armies, until the great sage of tapas, saying 'cease this great war, this is ours', sat on it and preached dharma, and the noble praise it.","n":"The kings' claim and the sage's are the same words, எமது ஈது (56, 60): the war is ended not by the sage's refusing to own but by a counter-claim made for dharma by one without attachment, which the kings' own attachment (பெரும் பற்று, 57) could not answer. The seat's peace (no fruit falls on it, no wings beat near it) is set against their red eyes and smoking hearts. பிறப்பு விளங்கு (53) is read here as 'on which births shine clear', which the next kaathai bears out when Manimekalai, worshipping at it, knows her former birth; it could also be read as praise of the seat's own origin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","aram","piravi","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 8, lines 44-63","lb":"MM8:44-63","ev":"The Dharma seat set by Indra appears before the weeping Manimekalai; the narrator tells how two Naga kings warred over it until the great sage stopped the war and preached dharma from it.","cl":[["great attachment to possession, which cannot let go, drives kin to war","general","asserted",57,"narrator"],["the Dharma seat on which the sage preached makes former births shine clear","buddhist","asserted",53,"narrator"]],"ag":["விண்ணவர் தலைவன்","நாக நாடு ஆளும் இருவர் மன்னவர்","பெருந் தவ முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:9"},"mm:9:1-15":{"book":"manimekalai","num":68,"t":["ஆங்கு அது கண்ட ஆய் இழை அறியாள்","காந்தள் அம் செங் கை தலை மேல் குவிந்தன","தலைமேல் குவிந்த கையள் செங் கண்","முலை மேல் கலுழ்ந்து முத்தத் திரள் உகுத்து அதின்","இடமுறை மும் முறை வலமுறை வாரா","கொடி மின் முகிலொடு நிலம் சேர்ந்தென்ன","இறு நுசுப்பு அலச வெறு நிலம் சேர்ந்து ஆங்கு","எழுவோள் பிறப்பு வழு இன்று உணர்ந்து","'தொழு தகை மாதவ! துணி பொருள் உணர்ந்தோய்!","காயங்கரையில் நீ உரைத்ததை எல்லாம்","வாயே ஆகுதல் மயக்கு அற உணர்ந்தேன்","காந்தாரம் என்னும் கழி பெரு நாட்டுப்","பூருவ தேயம் பொறை கெட வாழும்","அத்திபதி எனும் அரசு ஆள் வேந்தன்","மைத்துனன் ஆகிய பிரமதருமன்!"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the seat, Manimekalai joins her hands above her head, weeps, circles it three times and falls like a lightning-creeper with its cloud, and rising with her former birth known without error, she hails the sage Brahmadharma, brother-in-law of King Attipathi of Gandhara, and says that all he spoke on the bank of the Kayangarai she now knows to have come true.","n":"Lines 1 to 8 are narration and line 9 begins her speech, which runs to line 70. She speaks to Brahmadharma as if present, though only the seat is before her: the recognition of the birth and the confirmation of the sage's word (வாயே ஆகுதல், 11) arrive together, so the seat's first gift is proof that a prophecy held.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vazhipaadu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 9, lines 1-15","lb":"MM9:1-15","ev":"Manimekalai worships the jewel seat, falls to the ground, rises knowing her former birth and addresses the sage Brahmadharma.","cl":[["what a true seer foretells comes to pass","general","asserted",11,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","பிரமதருமன்"],"ch":"mm:10"},"mm:9:16-30":{"book":"manimekalai","num":69,"t":["ஆங்கு அவன் தன்பால் அணைந்து அறன் உரைப்போய்","\"தீம் கனி நாவல் ஓங்கும் இத் தீவிடை","இன்று ஏழ் நாளில் இரு நில மாக்கள்","நின்று நடுக்கு எய்த நீள் நில வேந்தே!","பூமி நடுக்குறூஉம் போழ்தத்து இந் நகர்","நாக நல் நாட்டு நானூறு யோசனை","வியன் பாதலத்து வீழ்ந்து கேடு எய்தும்","இதன்பால் ஒழிக\" என இரு நில வேந்தனும்","மா பெரும் பேர் ஊர் மக்கட்கு எல்லாம்","\"ஆவும் மாவும் கொண்டு கழிக\" என்றே","பறையின் சாற்றி நிறை அருந் தானையோடு","இடவயம் என்னும் இரும் பதி நீங்கி","வட வயின் அவந்தி மா நகர்ச் செல்வோன்","காயங்கரை எனும் பேர் யாற்று அடைகரை","சேய் உயர் பூம்பொழில் பாடி யெய்து இருப்ப"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai recalls that the sage went to King Attipathi and warned him that in seven days an earthquake would sink his city four hundred yojanas into the Naga underworld, and that the king had the drum proclaim that all should leave with their cattle and horses, left Idavayam with his army for Avanti in the north, and camped in a flowering grove on the bank of the Kayangarai.","n":"The sage gives no cause for the city's fall: no fault of the king and no curse, only the warning, and the king acts on it at once. Set against the later fall of Puhar, which the epic ties to a forgotten festival and a goddess's anger, this first drowned city is the case of a warning heeded.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kedu","arasu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 9, lines 16-30","lb":"MM9:16-30","ev":"Brahmadharma warns Attipathi that Idavayam will sink in seven days; the king proclaims an evacuation, leaves the city and camps on the bank of the Kayangarai.","cl":[],"ag":["பிரமதருமன்","அத்திபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","பிரமதருமன்"],"ch":"mm:10"},"mm:9:31-41":{"book":"manimekalai","num":70,"t":["எம் கோன் நீ ஆங்கு உரைத்த அந் நாளிடைத்","தங்காது அந் நகர் வீழ்ந்து கேடு எய்தலும்","மருள் அறு புலவ! நின் மலர் அடி அதனை","அரசொடு மக்கள் எல்லாம் ஈண்டிச்","சூழ்ந்தனர் வணங்கித் தாழ்ந்து பல ஏத்திய","அருளறம் பூண்ட ஒரு பேர் இன்பத்து","உலகு துயர் கெடுப்ப அருளிய அந் நாள்","அரவக் கடல் ஒலி அசோதரம் ஆளும்","இரவிவன்மன் ஒரு பெருந்தேவி","அலத்தகச் சீறடி அமுதபதி வயிற்று","இலக்குமி என்னும் பெயர் பெற்றுப் பிறந்தேன்"],"p":"","r":"On the day the sage had named the city fell, and the king and all the people gathered round his feet and praised him while he, bound to the dharma of compassion, taught to end the world's suffering; at that time, she says, she had been born as Lakshmi, daughter of Queen Amuthapathi and King Ravivarman of sea-loud Asotharam.","n":"The sentence ties her birth to 'that day' (அந் நாள், 37), which most naturally means she was living as Lakshmi at the time, not born that day. Her memory of the camp at Kayangarai is all worship and teaching; kaathai 21 will add what she does not say here, that on the same visit her husband killed the cook.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","piravi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 9, lines 31-41","lb":"MM9:31-41","ev":"Idavayam sinks on the foretold day; the king and people worship Brahmadharma, who teaches them; Manimekalai names her former birth as Lakshmi of Asotharam.","cl":[["the dharma of compassion, taught, ends the suffering of the world","buddhist","asserted",36,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["அத்திபதி","மக்கள்","பிரமதருமன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","பிரமதருமன்"],"ch":"mm:10"},"mm:9:42-57":{"book":"manimekalai","num":71,"t":["அத்திபதி எனும் அரசன் பெருந்தேவி","சித்திபுரம் ஆளும் சீதரன் திருமகள்","நீலபதி எனும் நேர் இழை வயிற்றில்","காலை ஞாயிற்றுக் கதிர் போல் தோன்றிய","இராகுலன் தனக்குப் புக்கேன் அவனொடு","பராவரும் மரபின் நின் பாதம் பணிதலும்","\"எட்டு இரு நாளில் இவ் இராகுலன் தன்னைத்","திட்டிவிடம் உணும் செல் உயிர் போனால்","தீ அழல் அவனொடு சேயிழை மூழ்குவை","ஏது நிகழ்ச்சி ஈங்கு இன்று ஆதலின்","கவேர கன்னிப் பெயரொடு விளங்கிய","தவாக் களி மூதூர்ச் சென்று பிறப்பு எய்துதி","அணி இழை! நினக்கு ஓர் அருந் துயர் வரு நாள்","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம் வந்து தோன்றி","அன்று அப் பதியில் ஆர் இருள் எடுத்து","தென் திசை மருங்கில் ஓர் தீவிடை வைத்தலும்"],"p":"","r":"As Lakshmi she married Rahulan, son of Attipathi and Queen Neelapathi, and when she bowed with him at the sage's feet he foretold that within sixteen days Rahulan would die of a snake whose glance is poison and she would sink into his fire, and that since the cause for her awakening had not come to pass there, she would be born in the city named for the Kaveri, and on a day of great sorrow the goddess Manimekala would carry her by night to an island in the south.","n":"The sage foretells the pyre as plainly as the death, with no word of praise or blame for it; the reason he gives for her rebirth in Puhar is that the ripening cause (ஏது நிகழ்ச்சி, 51) is absent 'here', a term the epic will use again for the conditions that must mature before release. Rahulan is the sage's nephew (Attipathi's son, and the sage is Attipathi's மைத்துனன், line 15). எட்டு இரு நாள் (48) is read as sixteen days (eight times two); ten is grammatically possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","ethu","saakkaadu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 9, lines 42-57","lb":"MM9:42-57","ev":"Lakshmi marries Rahulan; when the two bow to Brahmadharma he foretells Rahulan's death by a glance-poison snake, her death in his fire, her rebirth in Puhar and her carrying by Manimekala to a southern island.","cl":[["when the cause for awakening has not ripened in one birth, it comes to pass in a later birth","buddhist","asserted",51,"brahmadharman"]],"ag":["இலக்குமி","இராகுலன்","பிரமதருமன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","பிரமதருமன்"],"ch":"mm:10"},"mm:9:58-71":{"book":"manimekalai","num":72,"t":["வேக வெந் திறல் நாக நாட்டு அரசர்","சின மாசு ஒழித்து மன மாசு தீர்த்து ஆங்கு","அறச் செவி திறந்து மறச் செவி அடைத்து","பிறவிப் பிணி மருத்துவன் இருந்து அறம் உரைக்கும்","திருந்து ஒளி ஆசனம் சென்று கைதொழுதி","அன்றைப் பகலே உன் பிறப்பு உணர்ந்து ஈங்கு","இன்று யான் உரைத்த உரை தெளிவாய்\" என,","சா துயர் கேட்டுத் தளர்ந்து உகு மனத்தேன்","\"காதலன் பிறப்புக் காட்டாயோ?\" என","\"ஆங்கு உனைக் கொணர்ந்த அரும் பெருந் தெய்வம்","பாங்கில் தோன்றி 'பைந்தொடி! கணவனை","ஈங்கு இவன்' என்னும்\" என்று எடுத்து ஓதினை","ஆங்கு அத் தெய்வதம் வாராதோ?\" என","ஏங்கினள் அழூஉம் இளங்கொடி தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"The prophecy goes on: on that island she was to worship the bright seat where the physician of the disease of birth sat and taught dharma, where the Naga kings put away the stain of anger and of mind and opened the ear of dharma and closed the ear of war, and that same day know her birth and see his words were clear; she, faint at the news of death, had asked to be shown her lover's next birth, and he had said the goddess who brought her would appear and say 'this is your husband'; will that goddess not come, she asks, and weeps.","n":"The Buddha is not named: he is the physician of the disease of birth (பிறவிப் பிணி மருத்துவன், 61) and the seat is where he sat. Lakshmi's one question to the sage after hearing her husband would die was not about release but about finding him again, and Manimekalai repeats it now as her first wish after recovering the birth: the attachment the sage foresaw is still at work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","pirivu","manam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 9, lines 58-71","lb":"MM9:58-71","ev":"Manimekalai recalls the sage's promise that at the seat she would know her birth and that a goddess would show her her husband, and weeps asking why that goddess has not come.","cl":[["the teacher of dharma is the physician of the disease of birth","buddhist","asserted",61,"brahmadharman"],["dharma is heard only when the stains of anger and of mind have been cleansed","buddhist","asserted",59,"brahmadharman"]],"ag":["பிரமதருமன்","இலக்குமி","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","பிரமதருமன்"],"ch":"mm:10"},"mm:10:1-15":{"book":"manimekalai","num":73,"t":["'அறவோன் ஆசனத்து ஆய் இழை அறிந்த","பிறவியள் ஆயினள் பெற்றியும் ஐது' என","விரை மலர் ஏந்தி விசும்பூடு இழிந்து","பொரு அறு பூங் கொடி பூமியில் பொலிந்தென","வந்து தோன்றிய மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","முந்தைப் பிறப்பு எய்தி நின்றோள் கேட்ப","'உயிர்கள் எல்லாம் உணர்வு பாழாகி","பொருள் வழங்கு செவித் துளை தூர்ந்து அறிவு இழந்த","வறம் தலை உலகத்து அறம் பாடு சிறக்கச்","சுடர் வழக்கு அற்றுத் தடுமாறுகாலை ஓர்","இள வள ஞாயிறு தோன்றியதென்ன","நீயோ தோன்றினை நின் அடி பணிந்தேன்","நீயே ஆகி நிற்கு அமைந்த இவ் ஆசனம்","நாமிசை வைத்தேன் தலைமிசைக் கொண்டேன்","பூமிசை ஏற்றினேன் புலம்பு அறுக\" என்றே"],"p":"","r":"Knowing that the girl has recovered her former birth at the Buddha's seat, the Manimekala goddess comes down through the sky with fragrant flowers and, while Manimekalai stands listening, praises the Buddha as a young sun that rose when the world had lost its understanding and dharma's light had failed, bows to the seat as his own presence, and asks that her grief end.","n":"The hymn (7 to 15) is read here as the goddess's, since she has just descended and the next unit has her circling the seat; the Tamil does not name the singer, and it could be Manimekalai's own prayer heard by the goddess. The seat is worshipped as the Buddha himself (நீயே ஆகி நிற்கு அமைந்த இவ் ஆசனம்): no image, only the place where he sat. Lines 1 and 2 seem to be the goddess's own thought before she descends.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","aram","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 10, lines 1-15","lb":"MM10:1-15","ev":"The Manimekala goddess descends to Manipallavam and sings praise of the Buddha before his seat as Manimekalai listens.","cl":[["the Buddha appeared to make dharma flourish in a world that had lost understanding, as a sun rises on darkness","buddhist","asserted",11,"manimekala_deyvam"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","பெருந் தவ முனிவன்"],"ch":"mm:11"},"mm:10:16-39":{"book":"manimekalai","num":74,"t":["வலம் கொண்டு ஆசனம் வணங்குவோள் முன்னர்ப்","பொலம் கொடி நிலமிசைச் சேர்ந்தெனப் பொருந்தி","'உன் திருவருளால் என் பிறப்பு உணர்ந்தேன்","என் பெருங் கணவன் யாங்கு உளன்?' என்றலும்","'இலக்குமி கேளாய் இராகுலன் தன்னொடு","புலத்தகை எய்தினை பூம்பொழில் அகவயின்","இடங்கழி காமமொடு அடங்கானாய் அவன்","மடந்தை மெல் இயல் மலர் அடி வணங்குழி","சாதுசக்கரன் மீவிசும்பு திரிவோன்","தெரு மரல் ஒழித்து ஆங்கு இரத்தினத் தீவத்துத்","தரும சக்கரம் உருட்டினன் வருவோன்","வெங்கதிர் அமயத்து வியன் பொழில் அகவயின்","வந்து தோன்றலும் மயங்கினை கலங்கி","மெல் இயல்! கண்டனை மெய்ந் நடுக்குற்றனை","நல்கூர் நுசுப்பினை நாணினை இறைஞ்ச","இராகுலன் \"வந்தோன் யார்?\" என வெகுளலும்","விரா மலர்க் கூந்தல்! அவன் வாய் புதையா","\"வானூடு இழிந்தோன் மலர் அடி வணங்காது","நா நல்கூர்ந்தனை\" என்று அவன் தன்னொடு","பகை அறு பாத்தியன் பாதம் பணிந்து ஆங்கு","\"அமர! கேள் நின் தமர் அலம் ஆயினும்","அம் தீம் தண்ணீர் அமுதொடு கொணர்கேம்","உண்டி யாம் உன் குறிப்பினம்\" என்றலும்","\"எம் அனை! உண்கேன் ஈங்குக் கொணர்க\" என"],"p":"","r":"As the goddess circles and bows to the seat, Manimekalai falls at her feet, thanks her for the knowledge of her birth and asks where her husband is; the goddess recalls how Lakshmi, sulking with Rahulan in a grove, saw the sky-roaming sage Saadhusakkaran come down at noon from turning the wheel of dharma in Ratnadvipa, bowed in awe, silenced her angry husband for not bowing, and with him offered the sage water and food, which he accepted.","n":"The scene is a lovers' quarrel (புலத்தகை, இடங்கழி காமம்) broken into by a holy man: Lakshmi turns from her husband's pleading at her feet to the sage's feet, and it is she, not Rahulan, who knows whom to honour. The sage calls her எம் அனை, 'my mother', as he accepts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vazhipaadu","piravi","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 10, lines 16-39","lb":"MM10:16-39","ev":"Manimekalai asks the goddess where her former husband is; the goddess tells how Lakshmi and Rahulan once fed the sage Saadhusakkaran in a grove.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","மணிமேகலை","இலக்குமி","இராகுலன்","சாதுசக்கரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:11"},"mm:10:40-49":{"book":"manimekalai","num":75,"t":["அந் நாள் அவன் உண்டருளிய அவ் அறம்","நின்னாங்கு ஒழியாது நின் பிறப்பு அறுத்திடும்","உவவன மருங்கில் உன்பால் தோன்றிய","உதயகுமரன் அவன் உன் இராகுலன்","ஆங்கு அவன் அன்றியும் அவன்பால் உள்ளம்","நீங்காத் தன்மை நினக்கும் உண்டு ஆகலின்","கந்தசாலியின் கழி பெரு வித்து ஓர்","வெந்து உகு வெங் களர் வீழ்வது போன்ம் என","அறத்தின் வித்து ஆங்கு ஆகிய உன்னை ஓர்","திறப்படற்கு ஏதுவா சேயிழை! செய்தேன்"],"p":"","r":"The goddess says that the charity the sage ate that day will not stay idle but will cut off Lakshmi's births, that Udayakumaran who came to her in the Uvavanam grove is that same Rahulan, and that because her heart too still clings to him, she carried her away, a seed of dharma about to fall like fine paddy seed on scorched saline ground, to set her on the right path.","n":"This explains Manimekalai's own pull toward the prince and the goddess's taking her to the island: the gift's merit is secure, but a love that survives rebirth threatens to waste it. The goddess does not condemn the love as sin; she calls it the wrong ground for the seed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","eegai","vinai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 10, lines 40-49","lb":"MM10:40-49","ev":"The goddess reveals that Udayakumaran is Manimekalai's former husband Rahulan and that she carried her off to save the merit of her past gift.","cl":[["food given to a holy one does not go to waste but will in time cut off the giver's rebirths","buddhist","asserted",41,"manimekala_deyvam"],["a love carried over from a former birth can waste the merit one has earned, as seed is lost on saline ground","buddhist","asserted",47,"manimekala_deyvam"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:11"},"mm:10:50-70":{"book":"manimekalai","num":76,"t":["இன்னும் கேளாய் இலக்குமி! நீ நின்","தவ்வையர் ஆவோர் தாரையும் வீரையும்","ஆங்கு அவர் தம்மை அங்க நாட்டு அகவயின்","கச்சயம் ஆளும் கழல் கால் வேந்தன்","துச்சயன் என்போன் ஒருவன் கொண்டனன்","அவருடன் ஆங்கு அவன் அகல் மலை ஆடி","கங்கைப் பேர் யாற்று அடைகரை இருந்துழி","மற வணம் நீத்த மாசு அறு கேள்வி","அறவணன் ஆங்கு அவன்பால் சென்றோனை","\"ஈங்கு வந்தீர் யார்?\" என்று எழுந்து அவன்","பாங்கு உளி மாதவன் பாதம் பணிதலும்","\"ஆதி முதல்வன் அற ஆழி ஆள்வோன்","மா துயர் எவ்வம் மக்களை நீக்கி","விலங்கும் தம்முள் வெரூஉம் பகை நீக்கி","உடங்கு உயிர் வாழ்க என்று உள்ளம் கசிந்து உக","தொன்று காலத்து நின்று அறம் உரைத்த","குன்ற மருங்கில் குற்றம் கெடுக்கும்","பாத பங்கயம் கிடத்தலின் ஈங்கு இது","பாதபங்கய மலை எனும் பெயர்த்து ஆயது","தொழுது வலம் கொள்ள வந்தேன் ஈங்கு இப்","பழுது இல் காட்சியீர்! நீயிரும் தொழும்\" என"],"p":"","r":"The goddess goes on: Lakshmi's elder sisters Tharai and Veerai were taken by Thuchayan, king of Kachchayam in Anga, and as he sported with them by the Ganga the flawless sage Aravanan came by; asked who he was, the sage said he had come to circle and worship the hill named for the lotus feet of the first lord of the dharma wheel, who once stood there and taught dharma with a melting heart so that people's sorrow and even the beasts' fear of one another might end, and he bade them worship too.","n":"The goddess quotes Aravanan's speech inside her own, so 61 to 70 is a report two removes deep. The footprints are said to destroy faults (குற்றம் கெடுக்கும், 66) by lying there, which gives the place, not a living teacher, the power. Whether this Aravanan is the Aravana Adigal of Puhar in the story's present the unit does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvadi","arul","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 10, lines 50-70","lb":"MM10:50-70","ev":"King Thuchayan, sporting with Tharai and Veerai by the Ganga, meets the sage Aravanan, who bids them worship the hill of the Buddha's footprints.","cl":[["the first lord of the dharma wheel taught out of compassion that all living things, beasts too, should live together without fear","buddhist","asserted",64,"aravanan"],["the Buddha's footprints on the hill destroy the faults of those who worship them","buddhist","asserted",66,"aravanan"]],"ag":["துச்சயன்","அறவணன்","தாரை","வீரை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:11"},"mm:10:71-93":{"book":"manimekalai","num":77,"t":["அன்று அவன் உரைத்த அவ் உரை பிழையாது","சென்று கைதொழுது சிறப்புச் செய்தலின்","மாதவி ஆகியும் சுதமதி ஆகியும்","கோதை அம் சாயல்! நின்னொடு கூடினர்","அறிபிறப்பு உற்றனை அறம் பாடு அறிந்தனை","பிற அறம் உரைப்போர் பெற்றியும் கேட்குவை","பல் வேறு சமயப் படிற்று உரை எல்லாம்","அல்லி அம் கோதை! கேட்குறும் அந் நாள்","இளையள் வளையோள் என்று உனக்கு யாவரும்","விளை பொருள் உரையார் வேற்று உரு எய்தவும்","அந்தரம் திரியவும் ஆக்கும் இவ் அருந் திறல்","மந்திரம் கொள்க' என வாய்மையின் ஓதி","'மதி நாள் முற்றிய மங்கலத் திருநாள்","பொது அறிவு இகழ்ந்து புலம் உறு மாதவன்","திருவறம் எய்துதல் சித்தம் என்று உணர் நீ","மன் பெரும் பீடிகை வணங்கினை ஏத்தி","நின் 'பதிப் புகுவாய்' என்று எழுந்து ஓங்கி","'மறந்ததும் உண்டு' என மறித்து ஆங்கு இழிந்து","'சிறந்த கொள்கைச் சேயிழை! கேளாய்","மக்கள் யாக்கை உணவின் பிண்டம்","இப் பெரு மந்திரம் இரும் பசி அறுக்கும்' என்று","ஆங்கு அது கொடுத்து ஆங்கு அந்தரம் எழுந்து","நீங்கியது ஆங்கு நெடுந் தெய்வம் தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"Because the two sisters heeded the sage and worshipped at the hill, the goddess says, they have been reborn with Manimekalai as Madhavi and Sudhamathi; now that she knows her birth and dharma's worth she will hear the false teachings of many schools, and since no one will tell a young girl the truth, she gives her a mantra to change form and travel the sky, foretells the great sage's day on the full moon, tells her to worship the seat and go home, then turns back for something forgotten: the human body is a lump of food, and this second mantra cuts off hunger; then she rises and is gone.","n":"Two mantras are given: one for changing form and flying, for hearing the schools in disguise (fulfilled in kaathai 27), and one against hunger, added as an afterthought (மறந்ததும் உண்டு). The goddess calls the other schools' teachings படிற்று உரை, false or deceitful words, before Manimekalai has heard them. Lines 83 to 85 are obscure: the full-moon day on which the great sage 'attains holy dharma' may be the Buddha's festival or a prophecy about Manimekalai's own path.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","pasi","samayam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 10, lines 71-93","lb":"MM10:71-93","ev":"The goddess tells Manimekalai that her sisters of the former birth are Madhavi and Sudhamathi, gives her a mantra for changing form and flying and another against hunger, and departs into the sky.","cl":[["worship of the Buddha's footprints in one birth brings its fruit in the next, as rebirth beside the one who led the way","buddhist","asserted",72,"manimekala_deyvam"],["the teachings of the various other schools are false words","buddhist","asserted",77,"manimekala_deyvam"],["the human body is a lump made of food, so hunger rules it","buddhist","asserted",90,"manimekala_deyvam"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","தாரை","வீரை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:11"},"mm:11:1-8":{"book":"manimekalai","num":78,"t":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம் நீங்கிய பின்னர்","மணிபல்லவத்திடை மணிமேகலை தான்","வெண் மணல் குன்றமும் விரி பூஞ்சோலையும்","தண் மலர்ப்பொய்கையும் தாழ்ந்தனள் நோக்கிக்","காவதம் திரிய கடவுள் கோலத்துத்","தீவதிலகை செவ்வனம் தோன்றிக்","'கலம் கவிழ் மகளிரின் வந்து ஈங்கு எய்திய","இலங்கு தொடி நல்லாய்! யார் நீ?' என்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"Left alone on Manipallavam after the goddess has gone, Manimekalai looks over its white sand hills, groves and pools and wanders a kaatham, until Deepathilakai appears in a deity's form and asks who she is, come here like a woman cast up from a wrecked ship.","n":"The question 'who are you?' (யார் நீ, 8) is the hinge of the kaathai: Manimekalai will answer it with two births, not one name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 11, lines 1-8","lb":"MM11:1-8","ev":"Manimekalai wanders the island and the goddess Deepathilakai appears and asks who she is.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","தீபதிலகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:12"},"mm:11:9-20":{"book":"manimekalai","num":79,"t":["'எப் பிறப்பு அகத்துள் \"யார் நீ\" என்றது","பொன் கொடி அன்னாய்! பொருந்திக் கேளாய்!","போய பிறவியில் பூமி அம் கிழவன்","இராகுலன் மனை யான் இலக்குமி என் பேர்","ஆய பிறவியில் ஆடல் அம் கணிகை","மாதவி ஈன்ற மணிமேகலை யான்","என் பெயர்த் தெய்வம் ஈங்கு எனைக் கொணர இம்","மன் பெரும் பீடிகை என் பிறப்பு உணர்ந்தேன்","ஈங்கு என் வரவு இது ஈங்கு எய்திய பயன் இது","பூங் கொடி அன்னாய் யார் நீ?' என்றலும்","ஆய் இழை தன் பிறப்பு அறிந்தமை அறிந்த","தீவதிலகை செவ்வனம் உரைக்கும்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai answers by asking in which birth the question is meant: in the birth gone by she was Lakshmi, wife of the prince Rahulan, and in this one she is Manimekalai, Madhavi's daughter, brought here by the goddess of her name to know her birth at the jewel seat; then she asks Deepathilakai the same question.","n":"Her reply turns a request for identity into a doctrine of identity: the self who answers spans two lives (போய பிறவியில் ... ஆய பிறவியில், 11, 13), and she names the coming and its use (வரவு, பயன், 17) as if reporting an errand completed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 11, lines 9-20","lb":"MM11:9-20","ev":"Manimekalai tells Deepathilakai she was Lakshmi, Rahulan's wife, in her former birth and is now Madhavi's daughter, and asks who Deepathilakai is.","cl":[["one person continues across births, and the jewel seat lets her know her former birth","buddhist","asserted",16,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","தீபதிலகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","தீபதிலகை"],"ch":"mm:12"},"mm:11:21-36":{"book":"manimekalai","num":80,"t":["'ஈங்கு இதன் அயல் அகத்து இரத்தினத் தீவத்து","ஓங்கு உயர் சமந்தத்து உச்சி மீமிசை","அறவியங் கிழவோன் அடி இணை ஆகிய","பிறவி என்னும் பெருங் கடல் விடூஉம்","அறவி நாவாய் ஆங்கு உளது ஆதலின்","தொழுது வலம் கொண்டு வந்தேன் ஈங்கு","பழுது இல் காட்சி இந் நல் மணிப் பீடிகை","தேவர் கோன் ஏவலின் காவல் பூண்டேன்","தீவதிலகை என் பெயர் இது கேள்","தரும தலைவன் தலைமையின் உரைத்த","பெருமைசால் நல் அறம் பிறழா நோன்பினர்","கண்டு கைதொழுவோர் கண்டதன் பின்னர்","பண்டைப் பிறவியர் ஆகுவர் பைந்தொடி","அரியர் உலகத்து ஆங்கு அவர்க்கு அறமொழி","உரியது உலகத்து ஒருதலையாக","ஆங்கனம் ஆகிய அணி இழை! இது கேள்"],"p":"","r":"Deepathilakai says she has come from worshipping the feet of the Lord of dharma on the peak of Samantam in the Jewel Island, feet that are a ship of dharma across the great sea of births, and that she guards this jewel seat at the command of the king of the gods; those who keep unswervingly the vow of the dharma the Lord taught, on seeing and worshipping it, come to know their former births, and such people are rare.","n":"The gods are put in service to the Buddha's seat: Indra (தேவர் கோன், 28) assigns the guard, and the guardian goddess herself is a pilgrim to the footprint. Lines 34-35 are compressed: the word of dharma is said to be fitting in the world for those rare ones, which may mean that only they are fit to receive teaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","aram","vazhipaadu","nonbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 11, lines 21-36","lb":"MM11:21-36","ev":"Deepathilakai names herself as the seat's guardian, set there by Indra, newly come from worshipping the Buddha's feet on Samantam.","cl":[["the Buddha's feet are a ship that carries beings off the great sea of births","buddhist","asserted",24,"deepathilakai"],["those who keep the vow of the Buddha's dharma and worship the seat come to know their former births","buddhist","asserted",33,"deepathilakai"]],"ag":["தீபதிலகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தீபதிலகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:12"},"mm:11:37-58":{"book":"manimekalai","num":81,"t":["ஈங்கு இப் பெரும் பெயர்ப் பீடிகை முன்னது","மா மலர்க் குவளையும் நெய்தலும் மயங்கிய","கோமுகி என்னும் கொழு நீர் இலஞ்சி","இருது இளவேனிலில் எரி கதிர் இடபத்து","ஒருபதின் மேலும் ஒருமூன்று சென்ற பின்","மீனத்து இடைநிலை மீனத்து அகவையின்","போதித் தலைவனொடு பொருந்தித் தோன்றும்","ஆபுத்திரன் கை அமுதசுரபி எனும்","மா பெரும் பாத்திரம் மடக்கொடி! கேளாய்","அந் நாள் இந் நாள் அப் பொழுது இப் பொழுது","நின்னாங்கு வருவது போலும் நேர் இழை!","ஆங்கு அதில் பெய்த ஆருயிர்மருந்து","வாங்குநர் கைஅகம் வருத்துதல் அல்லது","தான் தொலைவு இல்லாத் தகைமையது ஆகும்","நறு மலர்க் கோதை! நின் ஊர் ஆங்கண்","அறவணன் தன்பால் கேட்குவை இதன் திறம்'","என்று அவள் உரைத்தலும் இளங்கொடி விரும்பி","மன் பெரும் பீடிகை தொழுதனள் வணங்கி","தீவதிலகை தன்னொடும் கூடி","கோமுகி வலம் செய்து கொள்கையின் நிற்றலும்","எழுந்து வலம் புரிந்த இளங்கொடி செங் கையில்","தொழும்தகை மரபின் பாத்திரம் புகுதலும்"],"p":"","r":"Deepathilakai tells of the pool Gomukhi before the seat, where each year on the day the Lord of the Bodhi appeared the great bowl Amudhasurabhi of Aputhiran rises, and says that day and hour have come round now as if for her; the life-medicine put in it never fails and only tires the hands of those who take, and Aravanan in her town will tell her its nature; Manimekalai worships the seat, circles the pool with the goddess, and the bowl comes into her hand.","n":"Lines 40-43 fix the day by season and stars (spring, the sun in Taurus, a count of thirteen days, the middle star); the reckoning is compressed and I cannot fix it, beyond its tie to the Buddha's day (போதித் தலைவனொடு பொருந்தித் தோன்றும், 43). The food is called ஆருயிர்மருந்து, medicine for dear life, and the only limit on the bowl is the tiredness of the receivers' hands (49), so scarcity moves from the giver to the taker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","marundhu","pasi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 11, lines 37-58","lb":"MM11:37-58","ev":"Deepathilakai tells of Aputhiran's bowl rising from the Gomukhi pool; Manimekalai worships and circles the pool, and the bowl rises into her hand.","cl":[],"ag":["தீபதிலகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தீபதிலகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:12"},"mm:11:59-75":{"book":"manimekalai","num":82,"t":["பாத்திரம் பெற்ற பைந் தொடி மடவாள்","மாத்திரை இன்றி மனம் மகிழ்வு எய்தி","'மாரனை வெல்லும் வீர! நின் அடி","தீ நெறிக் கடும் பகை கடிந்தோய்! நின் அடி","பிறர்க்கு அறம் முயலும் பெரியோய்! நின் அடி","துறக்கம் வேண்டாத் தொல்லோய்! நின் அடி","எண் பிறக்கு ஒழிய இறந்தோய்! நின் அடி","கண் பிறர்க்கு அளிக்கும் கண்ணோய்! நின் அடி","தீ மொழிக்கு அடைத்த செவியோய்! நின் அடி","வாய்மொழி சிறந்த நாவோய்! நின் அடி","நரகர் துயர் கெட நடப்போய்! நின் அடி","உரகர் துயரம் ஒழிப்போய்! நின் அடி","வணங்குதல் அல்லது வாழ்த்தல் என் நாவிற்கு","அடங்காது!\" என்ற ஆய் இழை முன்னர்","போதி நீழல் பொருந்தித் தோன்றும்","நாதன் பாதம் நவை கெட ஏத்தித்","தீவதிலகை சேயிழைக்கு உரைக்கும்"],"p":"","r":"Overjoyed at receiving the bowl, Manimekalai praises the Buddha's feet line by line, as the hero who conquered Mara, who drove off the enmity of evil ways, who strives for others' dharma, who did not want heaven, who went beyond reckoning, who gives others sight, whose ears are shut to evil words and whose tongue excels in truth, who walks to end the pain of the hell-dwellers and the serpents, and says her tongue can only bow, not praise; Deepathilakai praises the same feet and turns to speak.","n":"The praise is a litany with the refrain நின் அடி closing ten lines; its first item, மாரனை வெல்லும் வீர (61), names desire's god as the one enemy, and துறக்கம் வேண்டாத் (64) sets the Buddha against the heaven that Vedic and Jain merit aims at. The gift of the bowl is answered not by thanks to the goddess but by praise of the teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arul","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 11, lines 59-75","lb":"MM11:59-75","ev":"Manimekalai, holding the bowl, praises the Buddha's feet, and Deepathilakai praises them and begins to speak to her.","cl":[["the Buddha conquered Mara and, not desiring heaven, strives for the dharma of others","buddhist","asserted",64,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","தீபதிலகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","பெருந் தவ முனிவன்"],"ch":"mm:12"},"mm:11:76-98":{"book":"manimekalai","num":83,"t":["'குடிப் பிறப்பு அழிக்கும் விழுப்பம் கொல்லும்","பிடித்த கல்விப் பெரும் புணை விடூஉம்","நாண் அணி களையும் மாண் எழில் சிதைக்கும்","பூண் முலை மாதரொடு புறங்கடை நிறுத்தும்","பசிப் பிணி என்னும் பாவி அது தீர்த்தோர்","இசைச் சொல் அளவைக்கு என் நா நிமிராது","புல் மரம் புகையப் புகை அழல் பொங்கி","மன் உயிர் மடிய மழைவளம் கரத்தலின்","அரசு தலைநீங்கிய அரு மறை அந்தணன்","இரு நில மருங்கின் யாங்கணும் திரிவோன்","அரும் பசி களைய ஆற்றுவது காணான்","திருந்தா நாய் ஊன் தின்னுதல் உறுவோன்","இந்திர சிறப்புச் செய்வோன் முன்னர்","வந்து தோன்றிய வானவர் பெருந்தகை","மழை வளம் தருதலின் மன் உயிர் ஓங்கி","பிழையா விளையுளும் பெருகியது அன்றோ?","ஆற்றுநர்க்கு அளிப்போர் அறம் விலைபகர்வோர்","ஆற்றா மாக்கள் அரும் பசி களைவோர்","மேற்றே உலகின் மெய்ந்நெறி வாழ்க்கை","மண் திணி ஞாலத்து வாழ்வோர்க்கு எல்லாம்","உண்டி கொடுத்தோர் உயிர் கொடுத்தோரே","உயிர்க் கொடை பூண்ட உரவோய் ஆகி","கயக்கு அறு நல் அறம் கண்டனை என்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"Deepathilakai says her tongue cannot rise to praise those who cure the sinner hunger, which destroys high birth, kills dignity, drops the raft of learning, strips modesty and beauty and leaves a man standing at others' gates with his wife; she recalls a drought in which a Brahmin of the Veda, wandering, was about to eat dog's flesh until the god appeared and gave rain; those who give to the able sell dharma, the world's true way belongs to those who end the hunger of the helpless, those who give food give life, and Manimekalai, having taken up that gift of life, has seen flawless dharma.","n":"Line 96 is Purananuru 18's உண்டி கொடுத்தோர் உயிர்கொடுத் தோரே, a king's counsel to build tanks, here made the creed of an alms-bowl; and kaathai 10:90 has already lent the same poem's உணவின் பிண்டம். The famine tale (82-91) is compressed: அரசு தலைநீங்கிய (84) may be a Brahmin who had left kingship or a land without a king, and the one who performs Indra's rite (88) may be that Brahmin; I read it as the old tale of a hungry sage at a dog's flesh, but the lines do not name him. Line 92 classes gifts to those who can repay as trade in dharma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasi","eegai","undi","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 11, lines 76-98","lb":"MM11:76-98","ev":"Deepathilakai tells Manimekalai that those who cure hunger are beyond praise and that in taking the bowl she has seen true dharma.","cl":[["hunger destroys birth, dignity, learning, modesty and beauty","general","asserted",80,"deepathilakai"],["giving to those able to repay is selling dharma; the true way is to end the hunger of the helpless","general","asserted",92,"deepathilakai"],["those who give food give life","general","asserted",96,"deepathilakai"]],"ag":["தீபதிலகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தீபதிலகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:12"},"mm:11:99-123":{"book":"manimekalai","num":84,"t":["விட்ட பிறப்பில் யான் விரும்பிய காதலன்","திட்டிவிடம் உணச் செல் உயிர் போவுழி","உயிரொடு வேவேன் உணர்வு ஒழி காலத்து","வெயில் விளங்கு அமயத்து விளங்கித் தோன்றிய","சாதுசக்கரன் தனை யான் ஊட்டிய","காலம் போல்வதோர் கனா மயக்கு உற்றேன்","ஆங்கு அதன் பயனே ஆர் உயிர் மருந்து ஆய்","ஈங்கு இப் பாத்திரம் என் கைப் புகுந்தது","நாவலொடு பெயரிய மா பெருந் தீவத்து","வித்தி நல் அறம் விளைந்த அதன் பயன்","துய்ப்போர் தம் மனை துணிச் சிதர் உடுத்து","வயிறு காய் பெரும் பசி அலைத்தற்கு இரங்கி","வெயில் என முனியாது புயல் என மடியாது","புறங்கடை நின்று புன்கண் கூர்ந்து முன்","அறங்கடை நில்லாது அயர்வோர் பலரால்","ஈன்ற குழவி முகம் கண்டு இரங்கி","தீம் பால் சுரப்போள் தன் முலை போன்றே","நெஞ்சு வழிப்படூஉம் விஞ்சைப் பாத்திரத்து","அகன் சுரைப் பெய்த ஆருயிர்மருந்து அவர்","முகம் கண்டு சுரத்தல் காண்டல் வேட்கையேன்' என","மறந்தேன் அதன் திறம் நீ எடுத்து உரைத்தனை","அறம் கரியாக அருள் சுரந்து ஊட்டும்","சிறந்தோர்க்கு அல்லது செவ்வனம் சுரவாது","ஆங்கனம் ஆயினை அதன் பயன் அறிந்தனை","ஈங்கு நின்று எழுவாய்' என்று அவள் உரைப்பத்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai says that in the birth she left, when her husband was dying of the sight-venom and she was about to burn with him, she fell into a dream-like daze like the hour she fed Saathusakkaran, and that deed's fruit has now come to her hand as this bowl; she longs to see its food flow, like a mother's breast at the face of her newborn, for the many who stand ragged at the gates of those enjoying the fruit of dharma sown in this land; Deepathilakai says she herself had forgotten that nature, that the bowl flows truly only for the excellent who feed out of compassion with dharma as witness, and bids her rise.","n":"Karma is on both sides of the gate: the prosperous enjoy the fruit of dharma sown (108-109), and those outside are, on one reading of 112-113, those who did not stand by dharma before; yet the speaker's response to this is the mother's milk, not a verdict. Line 101 (உயிரொடு வேவேன்) and the run 111-113 are compressed and my reading of them is not secure. The bowl's condition (120-121) is the condition Aputhiran set when he let it go (14:93-94).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","eegai","arul","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 11, lines 99-123","lb":"MM11:99-123","ev":"Manimekalai tells that the bowl is the fruit of her feeding Saathusakkaran in her former birth and longs to feed the hungry with it; Deepathilakai says it flows only for the compassionate and bids her rise.","cl":[["a gift to a holy man in a former birth ripens as a means of giving in this one","buddhist","asserted",105,"manimekalai"],["the prosperous enjoy the fruit of dharma sown before","general","asserted",108,"manimekalai"],["the bowl yields only for those who feed out of compassion, with dharma as witness","general","asserted",120,"deepathilakai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","தீபதிலகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","தீபதிலகை"],"ch":"mm:12"},"mm:11:124-146":{"book":"manimekalai","num":85,"t":["தீவதிலகை தன் அடி வணங்கி","மா பெரும் பாத்திரம் மலர்க் கையின் ஏந்திக்","கோமகன் பீடிகை தொழுது வலம் கொண்டு","வானூடு எழுந்து மணிமேகலை தான்","'வழு அறு தெய்வம் வாய்மையின் உரைத்த","எழு நாள் வந்தது என் மகள் வாராள்!","வழுவாய் உண்டு!' என மயங்குவோள் முன்னர்","வந்து தோன்றி அவர் மயக்கம் களைந்து","அந்தில் அவர்க்கு ஓர் அற்புதம் கூறும்","'இரவிவன்மன் ஒரு பெரு மகளே!","துரகத் தானைத் துச்சயன் தேவி!","அமுதபதி வயிற்று அரிதின் தோன்றி","தவ்வையர் ஆகிய தாரையும் வீரையும்","அவ்வையர் ஆயினீர் நும் அடி தொழுதேன்","வாய்வதாக மானிட யாக்கையில்","தீவினை அறுக்கும் செய் தவம் நுமக்கு ஈங்கு","அறவண அடிகள் தம்பால் பெறுமின்","செறி தொடி நல்லீர்! உம் பிறப்பு ஈங்கு இஃது","ஆபுத்திரன் கை அமுதசுரபி எனும்","மா பெரும் பாத்திரம் நீயிரும் தொழும்!' என","தொழுதனர் ஏத்திய தூமொழியாரொடும்","'பழுது அறு மாதவன் பாதம் படர்கேம்","எழுக' என எழுந்தனள் இளங்கொடி தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai bows at Deepathilakai's feet, takes the bowl, worships the seat and rises through the sky, appearing before Madhavi just as she cries that the seventh day the true goddess promised has come and her daughter has not; she clears their bewilderment, hails Madhavi and Sudhamathi as Tharai and Veerai, her elder sisters in the birth when all three were born of Amudhapathi, who have now become her mothers, bids them seek from Aravana Adigal the austerity that cuts evil deeds in this human body, and has them worship the bowl, and they set out for the teacher.","n":"The recognition reverses kinship: her elder sisters of the last birth are her mothers in this one (அவ்வையர் ஆயினீர், 137). The human body is named as the place where austerity can cut evil deeds (138-139), the first time in the kaathai the bowl's dharma of feeding is joined to a path of release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","thavam","vinai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 11, lines 124-146","lb":"MM11:124-146","ev":"Manimekalai flies home with the bowl, finds Madhavi grieving at the seventh day, tells her and Sudhamathi they were her sisters Tharai and Veerai, has them worship the bowl, and they set out for Aravana Adigal.","cl":[["austerity that cuts evil deeds is to be undertaken while one has a human body","buddhist","asserted",139,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","மாதவி","சுதமதி"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:12"},"mm:12:1-14":{"book":"manimekalai","num":86,"t":["ஆங்கு அவர் தம்முடன் 'அறவண அடிகள்","யாங்கு உளர்?' என்றே இளங்கொடி வினாஅய்","நரை முதிர் யாக்கை நடுங்கா நாவின்","உரை மூதாளன் உறைவிடம் குறுகி","மைம் மலர்க் குழலி மாதவன் திருந்து அடி","மும் முறை வணங்கி முறையுளி ஏத்தி","புது மலர்ச் சோலை பொருந்திய வண்ணமும்","உதயகுமரன் ஆங்கு உற்று உரைசெய்ததும்","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம் மணிபல்லவத்திடை","அணி இழை தன்னை அகற்றிய வண்ணமும்","ஆங்கு அத் தீவகத்து அறவோன் ஆசனம்","நீங்கிய பிறப்பு நேர் இழைக்கு அளித்ததும்","அளித்த பிறப்பின் ஆகிய கணவனை","களிக் கயல் நெடுங் கண் கடவுளின் பெற்றதும்"],"p":"","r":"With her two companions Manimekalai asks where Aravana Adigal lives, comes to the dwelling of the white-haired elder whose tongue never falters, bows three times at his feet, praises him, and begins to tell him of the grove, Udayakumaran's words, the goddess carrying her to Manipallavam, the Buddha's seat showing her the birth she had left, and her learning from the goddess of the husband of that birth.","n":"Her story is given as a list of clauses ending in -ததும் and -வண்ணமும், so the poet retells eleven kaathais in a breath without letting her speak in direct words; the seat is called அறவோன் ஆசனம், the virtuous one's seat, without naming the Buddha.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","piravi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 12, lines 1-14","lb":"MM12:1-14","ev":"Manimekalai seeks out Aravana Adigal, bows at his feet and begins to recount what has happened to her since the grove.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:13"},"mm:12:15-30":{"book":"manimekalai","num":87,"t":["'தவ்வையர் ஆகிய தாரையும் வீரையும்","வெவ் வினை உருப்ப விளிந்து கேடு எய்தி","மாதவி ஆகியும் சுதமதி ஆகியும்","கோதை அம் சாயல் நின்னொடும் கூடினர்","ஆங்கு அவர் தம் திறம் அறவணன் தன்பால்","பூங் கொடி நல்லாய்! கேள்' என்று உரைத்ததும்","உரைத்த பூங்கொடி ஒரு மூன்று மந்திரம்","தனக்கு உரைசெய்து தான் ஏகிய வண்ணமும்","தெய்வம் போய பின் தீவதிலகையும்","ஐயெனத் தோன்றி அருளொடும் அடைந்ததும்","அடைந்த தெய்வம் ஆபுத்திரன் கை","வணங்குறு பாத்திரம் வாய்மையின் அளித்ததும்","'ஆபுத்திரன் திறம் அறவணன் தன்பால்","கேள்' என்று உரைத்து கிளர் ஒளி மா தெய்வம்","'போக' என மடந்தை போந்த வண்ணமும்","மாதவன் தன்னை வணங்கினள் உரைத்தலும்"],"p":"","r":"Her report goes on: the goddess told her that her elder sisters of that birth, Tarai and Virai, died when a cruel deed ripened and were reborn as Madhavi and Sudhamathi to be with her, and that she should hear their story from Aravanan; the goddess gave her three mantras and left; Deepathilakai appeared with grace, gave her Aaputhiran's bowl in truth and sent her to hear Aaputhiran's story from the same teacher; and all this she told the ascetic, bowing.","n":"Both divine speakers send her to Aravanar for the stories behind what they reveal, so the unit sets up the elder as the keeper of the past lives the goddesses only name; the deaths of 16 are blamed on a ripened deed before 43 to 48 say how they happened.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 12, lines 15-30","lb":"MM12:15-30","ev":"Manimekalai tells Aravanar how the goddess revealed Tarai and Virai's rebirth and gave her three mantras, and how Deepathilakai gave her Aaputhiran's bowl and sent her to him.","cl":[["a cruel deed, when it ripens, brings death and ruin, and those it strikes are reborn among their former kin","buddhist","asserted",16,"manimekala_deyvam"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","தீபதிலகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:13"},"mm:12:31-42":{"book":"manimekalai","num":88,"t":["மணிமேகலை உரை மாதவன் கேட்டு","தணியா இன்பம் தலைத்தலை மேல் வர","'பொன் தொடி மாதர்! நல் திறம் சிறக்க","உற்று உணர்வாய் நீ இவர் திறம் உரைக்கேன்","நின் நெடுந் தெய்வம் நினக்கு எடுத்து உரைத்த","அந் நாள் அன்றியும் அரு வினை கழூஉம்","ஆதி முதல்வன் அடி இணை ஆகிய","பாதபங்கய மலை பரவிச் செல்வேன்","கச்சயம் ஆளும் கழல் கால் வேந்தன்","துச்சயன் தன்னை ஓர் சூழ் பொழில் கண்டேன்","\"மா பெருந் தானை மன்ன! நின்னொடும்","தேவியர் தமக்கும் தீது இன்றோ?\" என"],"p":"","r":"Hearing her, the great ascetic is filled with joy upon joy, promises to tell her the two women's story, and begins: besides the day her goddess told of, when he was going to worship the Padapankaya hill, the twin feet of the First Lord that wash away hard deeds, he met Duchayan, king of Kachchayam, in a grove and asked whether all was well with him and his queens.","n":"ஆதி முதல்வன், the First Lord, is here the Buddha, whose footprints give the hill its name (kaathai 10, 61 to 68); the Kural's first couplet uses a near phrase (ஆதி பகவன்) for its own unnamed first being. Aravanar speaks of the earlier visit as something the goddess already told, so his account adds a second meeting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["puththan","vazhipaadu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 12, lines 31-42","lb":"MM12:31-42","ev":"Aravanar, delighted, begins to tell how on a later pilgrimage to the Padapankaya hill he met King Duchayan and asked after him and his queens.","cl":[["worship of the First Lord's twin feet washes away hard deeds","buddhist","asserted",36,"aravanar"]],"ag":["அறவண அடிகள்","துச்சயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:13"},"mm:12:43-52":{"book":"manimekalai","num":89,"t":["அழிதகவு உள்ளமொடு அரற்றினன் ஆகி","ஒளி இழை மாதர்க்கு உற்றதை உரைப்போன்","புதுக் கோள் யானைமுன் போற்றாது சென்று","மதுக் களி மயக்கத்து வீரை மாய்ந்ததூஉம்","ஆங்கு அது கேட்டு ஓர் அரமியம் ஏறி","தாங்காது வீழ்ந்து தாரை சாவுற்றதூஉம்","கழி பெருந் துன்பம் காவலன் உரைப்ப","\"பழ வினைப் பயன் நீ பரியல்\" என்று எழுந்தேன்","ஆடும் கூத்தியர் அணியே போல","வேற்று ஓர் அணியொடு வந்தீரோ?' என"],"p":"","r":"The king, broken, told him that Virai, dazed with drink, went heedlessly before a newly captured elephant and was killed, and that Tarai, hearing it, climbed a terrace and fell to her death; Aravanar told him not to grieve over the fruit of old deeds and rose to leave, and now he asks the two women whether they have come back in another costume, like dancers.","n":"Karma and the women's own acts are both given: line 16 calls the deaths a cruel deed ripening, while 46 names the drink and 48 the leap; Aravanar's consolation at 50 lets the first explain the second without excusing it. The closing image of dancers changing costume (51 to 52) fits Madhavi, a dancer, and makes rebirth a change of dress.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kal","saakkaadu","samanilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 12, lines 43-52","lb":"MM12:43-52","ev":"Duchayan tells Aravanar that Virai was killed by an elephant while drunk and Tarai threw herself from a terrace; Aravanar consoles him and leaves.","cl":[["a death that befalls one's own is the fruit of old deeds and is not to be grieved","buddhist","asserted",50,"aravanar"]],"ag":["துச்சயன்","வீரை","தாரை","அறவண அடிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:13"},"mm:12:53-70":{"book":"manimekalai","num":90,"t":["மணிமேகலைமுன் மடக்கொடியார் திறம்","துணி பொருள் மாதவன் சொல்லியும் அமையான்","'பிறவியும் அறவியும் பெற்றியின் உணர்ந்த","நறு மலர்க் கோதாய்! நல்கினை கேளாய்","தரும தலைவன் தலைமையின் உரைத்த","பெருமைசால் நல் அறம் பெருகாதாகி","இறுதி இல் நல் கதி செல்லும் பெரு வழி","அறுகையும் நெருஞ்சியும் அடர்ந்து கண் அடைத்தாங்கு","செயிர் வழங்கு தீக் கதி திறந்து கல்லென்று","உயிர் வழங்கு பெரு நெறி ஒரு திறம் பட்டது","தண் பனி விழுங்கிய செங்கதிர் மண்டிலம்","உண்டு என உணர்தல் அல்லது யாவதும்","கண்டு இனிது விளங்காக் காட்சி போன்றது","சலாகை நுழைந்த மணித் துளை அகவையின்","உலா நீர்ப் பெருங் கடல் ஓடாது ஆயினும்","ஆங்கு அத் துளை வழி உகு நீர் போல","ஈங்கு நல் அறம் எய்தலும் உண்டு எனச்","சொல்லலும் உண்டு யான் சொல்லுதல் தேற்றார்"],"p":"","r":"Not content with the women's story, Aravanar tells Manimekalai that the dharma the Lord of Dharma taught no longer flourishes: the great road to the endless good state is choked with grass and thorns while the road to the evil states stands open, the dharma is known to exist only as a sun swallowed by mist is known, and as sea water still seeps through a needle-bored hole in a gem, good dharma can still be reached here, though people will not credit him when he says so.","n":"Line 55 பிறவியும் அறவியும் is hard: read here as 'birth and dharma' that she has come to know, but அறவி is a rare form. The two similes grade the loss: the mist hides the sun without destroying it, and the hole lets in only a trickle, so decline is not extinction.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","veedu","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 12, lines 53-70","lb":"MM12:53-70","ev":"","cl":[["the dharma the Buddha taught has declined, so the way to release is nearly closed and the way to evil births lies open","buddhist","asserted",58,"aravanar"],["even in the decline good dharma can still be attained, though by few","buddhist","asserted",69,"aravanar"]],"ag":["அறவண அடிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:13"},"mm:12:71-82":{"book":"manimekalai","num":91,"t":["மல்லல் மா ஞாலத்து மக்களே ஆதலின்","சக்கரவாளத்துத் தேவர் எல்லாம்","தொக்கு ஒருங்கு ஈண்டி துடித லோகத்து","மிக்கோன் பாதம் விழுந்தனர் இரப்ப","இருள் பரந்து கிடந்த மலர் தலை உலகத்து","விரி கதிர்ச் செல்வன் தோன்றினன் என்ன","ஈர் எண்ணூற்றோடு ஈர் எட்டு ஆண்டில்","பேர் அறிவாளன் தோன்றும் அதன் பிற்பாடு","பெருங் குள மருங்கில் சுருங்கைச் சிறு வழி","இரும் பெரு நீத்தம் புகுவது போல","அளவாச் சிறு செவி அளப்பு அரு நல் அறம்","உளம் மலி உவகையோடு உயிர் கொளப் புகூஉம்"],"p":"","r":"Because worldly people will not grasp it, all the gods of the cosmic circle gathered in Tushita and fell begging at the feet of the Great One, so that in one thousand six hundred and sixteen years the great wise one will appear like the sun over a dark world, and after that the measureless dharma will pour gladly into small ears as a great flood enters a tank through a narrow sluice.","n":"ஈர் எண்ணூற்றோடு ஈர் எட்டு (77) is read as two eight-hundreds and two eights, 1616 years; the text does not say from what point the years are counted. The sluice simile (79 to 82) answers the needle-hole simile of 66 to 68: the same small opening, but now with a flood behind it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["puththan","aram","kelvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 12, lines 71-82","lb":"MM12:71-82","ev":"","cl":[["at the gods' entreaty in Tushita a Buddha will be born after 1616 years and his dharma will again fill the world","buddhist","asserted",77,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:13"},"mm:12:83-103":{"book":"manimekalai","num":92,"t":["கதிரோன் தோன்றும் காலை ஆங்கு அவன்","அவிர் ஒளி காட்டும் மணியே போன்று","மைத்து இருள் கூர்ந்த மன மாசு தீரப்","புத்த ஞாயிறு தோன்றும்காலை","திங்களும் ஞாயிறும் தீங்கு உறா விளங்க","தங்கா நாள் மீன் தகைமையின் நடக்கும்","வானம் பொய்யாது மா நிலம் வளம்படும்","ஊன் உடை உயிர்கள் உறு துயர் காணா","வளி வலம் கொட்கும் மாதிரம் வளம்படும்","நளி இரு முந்நீர் நலம் பல தரூஉம்","கறவை கன்று ஆர்த்தி கலம் நிறை பொழியும்","பறவை பயன் துய்த்து உறைபதி நீங்கா","விலங்கும் மக்களும் வெரூஉம் பகை நீங்கும்","கலங்கு அஞர் நரகரும் பேயும் கைவிடும்","கூனும் குறளும் ஊமும் செவிடும்","மாவும் மருளும் மன் உயிர் பெறாஅ","அந் நாள் பிறந்து அவன் அருளறம் கேட்டோர்","இன்னாப் பிறவி இகந்தோர் ஆதலின்","போதி மூலம் பொருந்திய சிறப்பின்","நாதன் பாதம் நவை கெட ஏத்துதல்","பிறவி தோறும் மறவேன் மடக்கொடி!"],"p":"","r":"When the Buddha-sun rises to clear the dark stain of minds, Aravanar says, sun and moon will shine unharmed, the stars keep their course, the rain will not fail, the earth, winds and sea will yield, cows will feed their calves and fill the pails, beasts and men will lose their fear of each other, the beings of hell and the ghosts will be freed of torment, and no creature will be born hunchbacked, dwarfed, dumb, deaf or deluded; those born then who hear his dharma of grace will pass beyond painful birth, and so he will not forget, in any birth, to praise the feet of the Lord of the Bodhi tree.","n":"The blessings of 87 to 98 are the old Tamil signs of a just king's reign (unfailing rain, full udders) moved to a Buddha's coming. Aravanar does not expect to escape birth himself before then: his vow is to keep worshipping through every birth until the age of hearing comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["puththan","piravi","arul","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 12, lines 83-103","lb":"MM12:83-103","ev":"","cl":[["a Buddha's appearance sets the heavens, the seasons and all creatures right and frees even the beings of hell from torment","buddhist","asserted",86,"aravanar"],["those who hear a Buddha's dharma of grace pass beyond painful rebirth","buddhist","asserted",99,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:13"},"mm:12:104-121":{"book":"manimekalai","num":93,"t":["மாதர் நின்னால் வருவன இவ் ஊர்","ஏது நிகழ்ச்சி யாவும் பல உள","ஆங்கு அவை நிகழ்ந்த பின்னர் அல்லது","பூங் கொடி மாதர் பொருளுரை பொருந்தாய்!","ஆதி முதல்வன் அருந் துயர் கெடுக்கும்","பாதபங்கய மலை பரசினர் ஆதலின்","ஈங்கு இவர் இருவரும் இளங்கொடி! நின்னோடு","ஓங்கு உயர் போதி உரவோன் திருந்து அடி","தொழுது வலம் கொண்டு தொடர் வினை நீங்கிப்","பழுது இல் நல் நெறிப் படர்குவர் காணாய்","ஆர் உயிர் மருந்து ஆம் அமுதசுரபி எனும்","மா பெரும் பாத்திரம் மடக்கொடி! பெற்றனை","மக்கள் தேவர் என இரு சார்க்கும்","ஒத்த முடிவின் ஓர் அறம் உரைக்கேன்","பசிப் பிணி தீர்த்தல்' என்றே அவரும்","தவப் பெரு நல் அறம் சாற்றினர் ஆதலின்","மடுத்த தீக் கொளிய மன் உயிர்ப் பசி கெட","எடுத்தனள் பாத்திரம் இளங்கொடி தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"Aravanar tells her that many events will happen in the city because of her and that she will not take up the teaching until they have; that Madhavi and Sudhamathi, having worshipped the Padapankaya hill, will with her worship the Bodhi sage's feet, shed their deeds and go the faultless way; and that since she now holds the great bowl Amudhasurabhi, he gives her one dharma alike in its end for men and gods, ending the disease of hunger; and the girl takes up the bowl to quench the burning hunger of all lives.","n":"பொருளுரை பொருந்தாய் (107) is read as a deferral: the doctrine will come after the events, and the epic keeps it back until kaathai 29. Feeding the hungry is given first to a renunciant, not to a householder or king, and is called தவப் பெரு நல் அறம், the great good dharma of tapas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasi","aram","eegai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 12, lines 104-121","lb":"MM12:104-121","ev":"Aravanar defers his teaching, foretells Madhavi's and Sudhamathi's release, and gives Manimekalai the dharma of ending hunger; she takes up the bowl.","cl":[["worship of the Bodhi sage's feet removes the deeds that follow one and leads to the faultless path","buddhist","asserted",112,"aravanar"],["ending the hunger of living beings is a dharma whose end is the same for men and for gods","general","asserted",117,"aravanar"]],"ag":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:13"},"mm:13:1-19":{"book":"manimekalai","num":94,"t":["'மா பெரும் பாத்திரம் மடக்கொடிக்கு அருளிய","ஆபுத்திரன் திறம் அணி இழை! கேளாய்","வாரணாசி ஓர் மறை ஓம்பாளன்","ஆரண உவாத்தி அபஞ்சிகன் என்போன்","பார்ப்பனி சாலி காப்புக் கடைகழிந்து","கொண்டோற் பிழைத்த தண்டம் அஞ்சி","தென் திசைக் குமரி ஆடி வருவோள்","சூல் முதிர் பருவத்து துஞ்சு இருள் இயவிடை","ஈன்ற குழவிக்கு இரங்காள்ஆகி","தோன்றாத் துடவையின் இட்டனள் நீங்க","தாய் இல் தூவாக் குழவித் துயர் கேட்டு ஓர்","ஆ வந்து அணைந்து ஆங்கு அதன் துயர் தீர","நாவான் நக்கி நன் பால் ஊட்டி","போகாது எழு நாள் புறங்காத்து ஓம்ப","வயனங்கோட்டில் ஓர் மறை ஓம்பாளன்","இயவிடை வருவோன் இளம்பூதி என்போன்","குழவி ஏங்கிய கூக் குரல் கேட்டுக்","கழுமிய துன்பமொடு கண்ணீர் உகுத்து ஆங்கு","\"ஆ மகன் அல்லன் என் மகன்\" என்றே"],"p":"","r":"Aravana Adigal begins Aputtiran's story for Manimekalai: Saali, wife of the Varanasi Veda teacher Abanjikan, having wronged her husband and fled his punishment, bears a child on her way back from bathing at Kumari and leaves it in a hidden grove; a cow licks and suckles it and guards it seven days, until the brahmin Ilampoothi of Vayanangodu hears it cry, weeps, and claims it as his own son, not a cow's.","n":"The unit sets the human mother who 'did not pity' the child (இரங்காள் ஆகி, 9) against the cow that comes to end its sorrow (அதன் துயர் தீர, 12); Ilampoothi's first words, 'not a cow's son but my son' (19), anticipate the insult the brahmins will later throw at the boy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","arul","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 13, lines 1-19","lb":"MM13:1-19","ev":"Saali abandons her newborn in a grove near the road; a cow nurses and guards it for seven days, and the brahmin Ilampoothi finds the child and takes it as his son.","cl":[],"ag":["சாலி","ஆ","இளம்பூதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:14"},"mm:13:20-48":{"book":"manimekalai","num":95,"t":["காதலி தன்னொடு கைதொழுது எடுத்து","\"நம்பி பிறந்தான் பொலிக நம் கிளை!\" என","தம் பதிப் பெயர்ந்து தமரொடும் கூடி","மார்பிடை முந்நூல் வனையாமுன்னர்","நாவிடை நல் நூல் நன்கனம் நவிற்றி","ஓத்து உடை அந்தணர்க்கு ஒப்பவை எல்லாம்","நாத் தொலைவு இன்றி நன்கனம் அறிந்த பின்","அப் பதி தன்னுள் ஓர் அந்தணன் மனைவயின்","புக்கோன் ஆங்குப் புலை சூழ் வேள்வியில்","குரூஉத் தொடை மாலை கோட்டிடைச் சுற்றி","வெரூஉப் பகை அஞ்சி வெய்து உயிர்த்துப் புலம்பிக்","கொலை நவில் வேட்டுவர் கொடுமரம் அஞ்சி","வலையிடைப் பட்ட மானே போன்று ஆங்கு","அஞ்சி நின்று அழைக்கும் ஆத் துயர் கண்டு","நெஞ்சு நடுக்குற்று நெடுங் கணீர் உகுத்து","\"கள்ள வினையின் கடுந் துயர் பாழ்பட","நள் இருள் கொண்டு நடக்குவன்\" என்னும்","உள்ளம் கரந்து ஆங்கு ஒரு புடை ஒதுங்கி","அல்லிடை ஆக் கொண்டு அப் பதி அகன்றோன்","கல் அதர் அத்தம் கடவாநின்றுழி","அடர்க் குறு மாக்களொடு அந்தணர் எல்லாம்","கடத்திடை ஆவொடு கையகப்படுத்தி","\"ஆ கொண்டு இந்த ஆர் இடைக் கழிய","நீ மகன் அல்லாய் நிகழ்ந்ததை உரையாய்","புலைச் சிறு மகனே! போக்கப்படுதி\" என்று","அலைக் கோல் அதனால் அறைந்தனர் கேட்ப","ஆட்டி நின்று அலைக்கும் அந்தணர் உவாத்தியைக்","கோட்டினில் குத்திக் குடர் புய்த்துறுத்துக்","காட்டிடை நல் ஆக் கதழ்ந்து கிளர்ந்து ஓட"],"p":"","r":"Ilampoothi and his wife take the boy home and rejoice in a son, and he masters the brahmins' learning before his thread ceremony; entering a brahmin's house where a flesh-ringed sacrifice is prepared, he sees a garlanded cow trembling like a deer in a net, steals it away by night, and is caught on the desert road by the brahmins, who call him no son of theirs but a base boy and beat him with sticks until the cow gores their teacher and runs off into the forest.","n":"The narrator names the rite புலை சூழ் வேள்வி (28), a sacrifice surrounded by what is foul, and the boy's own aim is to end the cow's suffering from 'stealthy work' (கள்ள வினை, 35), so the theft is framed as rescue before the brahmins name it theft. The cow that kills the teacher (46-47) makes the rescued victim the cause of a death, a cost the narration states without comment. Boundary moved: see unit_note.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","aaguthi","arul","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 13, lines 20-48","lb":"MM13:20-48","ev":"The boy steals the cow meant for a sacrifice, is caught and beaten by the brahmins, and the cow gores their teacher to death and escapes.","cl":[["a sacrifice that kills a cow is a foul act","general","asserted",28,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":["இளம்பூதி","காதலி","ஆபுத்திரன்","அந்தணர்","அந்தணர் உவாத்தி","ஆ"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:14"},"mm:13:49-56":{"book":"manimekalai","num":96,"t":["ஆபுத்திரன் தான் ஆங்கு அவர்க்கு உரைப்போன்","\"நோவன செய்யன்மின் நொடிவன கேண்மின்","விடு நில மருங்கில் படு புல் ஆர்ந்து","நெடு நில மருங்கின் மக்கட்கு எல்லாம்","பிறந்த நாள் தொட்டும் சிறந்த தன் தீம் பால்","அறம் தரு நெஞ்சோடு அருள் சுரந்து ஊட்டும்","இதனொடு வந்த செற்றம் என்னை","முது மறை அந்தணிர்! முன்னியது உரைமோ?\""],"p":"","r":"Aputtiran tells the brahmins not to do hurtful things and to hear him: the cow eats the grass of the waste lands and from the day of their birth feeds all people its sweet milk with a heart that gives virtue and overflowing kindness, so what enmity do they have with it?","n":"He defends the cow by what it gives, not by any sanctity of its kind: it eats what no one owns (விடு நில மருங்கின் படு புல், 51) and nourishes everyone, so the brahmins' claim on it as a victim is answered with a claim of debt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","kollaamai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 13, lines 49-56","lb":"MM13:49-56","ev":"Aputtiran asks the brahmins why they bear enmity to a cow that feeds all people.","cl":[["a cow, which nourishes all people with its milk out of kindness, should not be harmed","general","asserted",54,"aaputhiran"]],"ag":["ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆபுத்திரன்","அந்தணர்"],"ch":"mm:14"},"mm:13:57-69":{"book":"manimekalai","num":97,"t":["\"பொன் அணி நேமி வலம் கொள் சக்கரக் கை","மன் உயிர் முதல்வன் மகன் எமக்கு அருளிய","அரு மறை நல் நூல் அறியாது இகழ்ந்தனை","தெருமரல் உள்ளத்துச் சிறியை நீ அவ்","ஆ மகன் ஆதற்கு ஒத்தனை அறியாய்","நீ மகன் அல்லாய் கேள்\" என இகழ்தலும்","\"ஆன் மகன் அசலன் மான் மகன் சிருங்கி","புலி மகன் விரிஞ்சி புரையோர் போற்றும்","நரி மகன் அல்லனோ கேசகம்பளன்","ஈங்கு இவர் நும் குலத்து இருடி கணங்கள் என்று","ஓங்கு உயர் பெருஞ் சிறப்பு உரைத்தலும் உண்டால்","ஆவொடு வந்த அழி குலம் உண்டோ","நான்மறை மாக்காள் நல் நூல் அகத்து?\" என"],"p":"","r":"The brahmins scorn him as one who despised the Veda that Brahma, son of the discus-bearing lord of life, gave them, a small-minded boy fit to be a cow's son and no son of theirs; Aputtiran answers that their own tradition praises the seers Asalan, son of a cow, Sringi, son of a deer, Virinchi, son of a tiger, and Kesakambalan, son of a jackal, as sages of their line, and asks whether the good books of the four Vedas know any lineage ruined by birth from a cow.","n":"The brahmins' insult turns the boy's history into a verdict (ஆ மகன் ஆதற்கு ஒத்தனை, 61); Aputtiran does not reject the Veda but argues from within it. The names at 63-65 are rare and their legends are not told here, so whose tradition supplies them is not clear from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","kudi","vedham","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 13, lines 57-69","lb":"MM13:57-69","ev":"The brahmins revile Aputtiran as a cow's son ignorant of the Veda, and he replies with seers of their tradition born of animals.","cl":[["the Veda was given by Brahma, son of the discus-bearing lord","vedic","reported",58,"velvi_anthanar"],["one who scorns the Veda is no son of the brahmins and fit to be called a cow's son","vedic","refuted",61,"velvi_anthanar"],["birth from an animal does not debase a lineage, since revered seers were so born","general","asserted",68,"aaputhiran"]],"ag":["அந்தணர்","ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆபுத்திரன்","அந்தணர்"],"ch":"mm:14"},"mm:13:70-91":{"book":"manimekalai","num":98,"t":["ஆங்கு அவர் தம்முள் ஓர் அந்தணன் உரைக்கும்","\"ஈங்கு இவன் தன் பிறப்பு யான் அறிகுவன்\" என","\"நடவை வருத்தமொடு நல்கூர் மேனியள்","வடமொழியாட்டி மறை முறை எய்தி","குமரி பாதம் கொள்கையின் வணங்கி","தமரின் தீர்ந்த சாலி என்போள் தனை","'யாது நின் ஊர்? ஈங்கு என் வரவு?' என","மா மறையாட்டி வரு திறம் உரைக்கும்","'வாரணாசி ஓர் மா மறை முதல்வன்","ஆரண உவாத்தி அரும் பெறல் மனைவி யான்","பார்ப்பார்க்கு ஒவ்வாப் பண்பின் ஒழுகி","காப்புக் கடைகழிந்து கணவனை இகழ்ந்தேன்","எறி பயம் உடைமையின் இரியல் மாக்களொடு","தெற்கண் குமரி ஆடிய வருவேன்","பொன் தேர்ச் செழியன் கொற்கை அம் பேர் ஊர்க்","காவதம் கடந்து கோவலர் இருக்கையின்","ஈன்ற குழவிக்கு இரங்கேனாகித்","தோன்றாத் துடவையின் இட்டனன் போந்தேன்","செல் கதி உண்டோ தீவினையேற்கு?' என்று","அல்லல் உற்று அழுத அவள் மகன் ஈங்கு இவன்","சொல்லுதல் தேற்றேன் சொல் பயம் இன்மையின்","புல்லல் ஓம்பன்மின் புலை மகன் இவன்\" என"],"p":"","r":"One of the brahmins says he knows the boy's birth: he met Saali, worn by the road, worshipping at Kumari, and when he asked her town and errand she told him she was the wife of the Varanasi Veda teacher, had behaved unlike a brahmin woman and slighted her husband, had fled in fear of punishment, and on her way back had left her newborn in a grove beyond Korkai, asking whether a sinner like her had any good course left; this boy is her son, so they must not touch him, for he is an outcaste.","n":"Saali's confession (78-88) repeats the narrator's account at 3-10 almost word for word, now inside a hostile witness's speech, so the same facts are used once to explain the child and once to condemn him. The brahmin judges the son by the mother's conduct; Aputtiran answers that verdict in the next unit (92-98).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","karpu","vinai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 13, lines 70-91","lb":"MM13:70-91","ev":"A brahmin reports Saali's confession of her lapse and her abandonment of the child, and declares Aputtiran her son and an outcaste.","cl":[["one who has done evil deeds has no good destiny left","general","reported",88,"saali"],["the son of a brahmin wife who broke her conduct is an outcaste and must not be touched","vedic","refuted",91,"anthanan_oruvan"]],"ag":["ஓர் அந்தணன்","சாலி"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஓர் அந்தணன்","அந்தணர்"],"ch":"mm:14"},"mm:13:92-115":{"book":"manimekalai","num":99,"t":["ஆபுத்திரன் பின்பு அமர் நகை செய்து","\"மா மறை மாக்கள் வரும் குலம் கேண்மோ","முது மறை முதல்வன் முன்னர்த் தோன்றிய","கடவுள் கணிகை காதல் அம் சிறுவர்","அரு மறை முதல்வர் அந்தணர் இருவரும்","புரி நூல் மார்பீர்! பொய் உரை ஆமோ?","சாலிக்கு உண்டோ தவறு?' என உரைத்து","நான்மறை மாக்களை நகுவனன் நிற்ப","\"ஓதல் அந்தணர்க்கு ஒவ்வான்\" என்றே","தாதை பூதியும் தன் மனை கடிதர","\"ஆ கவர் கள்வன்\" என்று அந்தணர் உறைதரும்","கிராமம் எங்கணும் கடிஞையில் கல் இட","மிக்க செல்வத்து விளங்கியோர் வாழும்","தக்கண மதுரை தான் சென்று எய்தி","சிந்தா விளக்கின் செழுங் கலை நியமத்து","அந்தில் முன்றில் அம்பலப் பீடிகைத்","தங்கினன் வதிந்து அத் தக்கணப் பேர் ஊர்","ஐயக் கடிஞை கையின் ஏந்தி","மை அறு சிறப்பின் மனைதொறும் மறுகி","'காணார் கேளார் கால் முடப்பட்டோர்","பேணுநர் இல்லோர் பிணி நடுக்குற்றோர்","யாவரும் வருக' என்று இசைத்து உடன் ஊட்டி","உண்டு ஒழி மிச்சில் உண்டு ஓடு தலை மடுத்து","கண்படைகொள்ளும் காவலன் தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"Aputtiran laughs and tells the brahmins to hear the birth of their own line: the two first brahmin seers of the ancient Veda were the beloved sons of a heavenly courtesan, so what fault has Saali? He is then cast out by his father Poothi as unfit to study among brahmins, stones are dropped in his begging bowl in every brahmin village as 'the cow-stealing thief', and he goes to southern Madurai, lodges in the hall before the temple of Chinthadevi, begs from house to house, calls the blind, the deaf, the lame, the unprotected and the sick to eat, eats what is left and sleeps with his bowl for a pillow.","n":"The tradition names the two seers born of a heavenly courtesan as Vasishtha and Agastya; the Tamil here does not name them. The narrator calls the outcast beggar காவலன் (115), protector, the word for a king, and the begging bowl (கடிஞை, 103, 109) stoned in the villages is the one he will soon exchange for the divine bowl (Manimekalai 14).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirappu","iravu","pasi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 13, lines 92-115","lb":"MM13:92-115","ev":"Aputtiran mocks the brahmins' pride of birth, is disowned by Ilampoothi and driven from the brahmin villages, and settles in Madurai begging to feed the helpless.","cl":[["if revered brahmin seers were born of a courtesan, a mother's lapse cannot debase her son","general","asserted",98,"aaputhiran"],["one not born of a proper brahmin union is unfit for Vedic study","vedic","reported",100,"ilampoothi"]],"ag":["ஆபுத்திரன்","இளம்பூதி","அந்தணர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:14"},"mm:14:1-15":{"book":"manimekalai","num":100,"t":["'ஆங்கு அவற்கு ஒரு நாள் அம்பலப் பீடிகை","பூங் கொடி நல்லாய் புகுந்தது கேளாய்","மாரி நடு நாள் வல் இருள் மயக்கத்து","ஆர் இடை உழந்தோர் அம்பலம் மரீஇ","துயில்வோன் தன்னைத் தொழுதனர் ஏத்தி","\"வயிறு காய் பெரும் பசி மலைக்கும்\" என்றலும்","ஏற்றூண் அல்லது வேற்றூண் இல்லோன்","ஆற்றுவது காணான் ஆர் அஞர் எய்த","\"கேள் இது மாதோ கெடுக நின் தீது\" என","யாவரும் ஏத்தும் இருங் கலை நியமத்துத்","தேவி சிந்தாவிளக்குத் தோன்றி","\"ஏடா! அழியல் எழுந்து இது கொள்ளாய்","நாடு வறம் கூரினும் இவ் ஓடு வறம் கூராது","வாங்குநர் கைஅகம் வருந்துதல் அல்லது","தான் தொலைவு இல்லாத் தகைமையது\" என்றே"],"p":"","r":"Aravana Adigal tells Manimekalai how, on a night of heavy rain, road-worn travellers came to the rest hall where Aputhiran slept and begged him to ease their hunger, and he, who had no food but what he begged, grieved at finding no means, until the goddess Chintadevi of the temple of learning appeared and bade him take a bowl that would not run dry though the land did, whose food would fail only as the hands of the takers tired.","n":"The gift answers Aputhiran's grief at being unable to feed others (8, ஆற்றுவது காணான்), not his own hunger; the goddess's words at 13 to 15 are repeated almost exactly by Deepatilakai when Manimekalai receives the bowl (11:49-50), so the bowl carries the same warrant from its first owner to its second.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","eegai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 14, lines 1-15","lb":"MM14:1-15","ev":"Hungry travellers beg Aputhiran for food on a rainy night; Chintadevi appears and offers him an inexhaustible bowl.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆர் இடை உழந்தோர்","ஆபுத்திரன்","சிந்தாதேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:15"},"mm:14:16-35":{"book":"manimekalai","num":101,"t":["தன் கைப் பாத்திரம் அவன் கைக் கொடுத்தலும்","\"சிந்தாதேவி! செழுங் கலை நியமத்து","நந்தா விளக்கே! நாமிசைப் பாவாய்!","வானோர் தலைவி! மண்ணோர் முதல்வி!","ஏனோர் உற்ற இடர் களைவாய்!\" எனத்","தான் தொழுது ஏத்தித் தலைவியை வணங்கி","ஆங்கு அவர் பசி தீர்த்து அந் நாள் தொட்டு","வாங்கு கை வருந்த மன் உயிர் ஓம்பலின்","மக்களும் மாவும் மரம் சேர் பறவையும்","தொக்கு உடன் ஈண்டிச் சூழ்ந்தன விடாஅ","பழு மரத்து ஈண்டிய பறவையின் எழூஉம்","இழுமென் சும்மை இடை இன்று ஒலிப்ப","ஈண்டுநீர் ஞாலத்து இவன் செயல் இந்திரன்","பாண்டு கம்பளம் துளக்கியது ஆதலின்","தளர்ந்த நடையின் தண்டு கால் ஊன்றி","வளைந்த யாக்கை ஓர் மறையோன் ஆகி","மா இரு ஞாலத்து மன் உயிர் ஓம்பும்","ஆர் உயிர் முதல்வன் தன் முன் தோன்றி","\"இந்திரன் வந்தேன் யாது நின் கருத்து","உன் பெரும் தானத்து உறு பயன் கொள்க\" என"],"p":"","r":"The goddess puts the bowl in his hand; he praises her as the undying lamp of the temple of learning, queen of the gods and first of those on earth, feeds the travellers, and from that day feeds every creature until men, beasts and birds crowd round him with a noise like birds in a fruiting tree, so that his deeds shake Indra's white throne and Indra comes before him as a bent old brahmin with a staff, names himself, and bids him take the fruit of his great giving.","n":"Indra's seat is shaken not by austerity but by feeding (28 to 29), and the god comes disguised as a brahmin to the man whom brahmins had driven out (kaathai 13); the offer of a reward for giving (35) sets up the refusal that follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vazhipaadu","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 14, lines 16-35","lb":"MM14:16-35","ev":"Aputhiran takes the bowl, praises the goddess and feeds all creatures; Indra's throne trembles and Indra comes in a brahmin's form to offer him the reward of his giving.","cl":[],"ag":["சிந்தாதேவி","ஆபுத்திரன்","விண்ணவர் தலைவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:15"},"mm:14:36-48":{"book":"manimekalai","num":102,"t":["வெள்ளை மகன் போல் விலா இற நக்கு ஈங்கு","எள்ளினன் \"போம்\" என்று எடுத்து உரை செய்வோன்","\"ஈண்டுச் செய் வினை ஆண்டு நுகர்ந்திருத்தல்","காண்தரு சிறப்பின் நும் கடவுளர் அல்லது","அறம் செய் மாக்கள் புறங்காத்து ஓம்புநர்","நல் தவம் செய்வோர் பற்று அற முயல்வோர்","யாவரும் இல்லாத் தேவர் நல் நாட்டுக்கு","இறைவன் ஆகிய பெரு விறல் வேந்தே","வருந்தி வந்தோர் அரும் பசி களைந்து அவர்","திருந்து முகம் காட்டும் என் தெய்வக் கடிஞை","உண்டிகொல்லோ உடுப்பனகொல்லோ","பெண்டிர்கொல்லோ பேணுநர்கொல்லோ","யாவை ஈங்கு அளிப்பன தேவர்கோன்?\" என்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"Aputhiran laughs till his ribs ache, says 'Go', and asks the king of a heaven where deeds done here are only enjoyed, where there is no one who does dharma, protects others, does good austerity or strives to cut attachment, what he could give here: food, clothes, women or attendants, to one whose divine bowl takes away the hunger of all who come and brightens their faces.","n":"The scorn is precise: heaven is a place for consuming merit, not for making it, so a reward there would end the giving that earned it. 'வெள்ளை மகன் போல்' (36) is read as 'like a simpleton', laughing without restraint before a god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","eegai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 14, lines 36-48","lb":"MM14:36-48","ev":"Aputhiran laughs at Indra's offer and asks what a god of heaven could give him.","cl":[["heaven is where deeds done on earth are only enjoyed, and no one there does dharma, austerity or strives to cut attachment","general","asserted",38,"aaputhiran"],["one who can feed all who come has nothing to gain from heaven's rewards","general","asserted",44,"aaputhiran"]],"ag":["ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆபுத்திரன்","விண்ணவர் தலைவன்"],"ch":"mm:15"},"mm:14:49-64":{"book":"manimekalai","num":103,"t":["\"புரப்போன் பாத்திரம் பொருந்து ஊண் சுரந்து ஈங்கு","இரப்போர்க் காணாது ஏமாந்திருப்ப","நிரப்பு இன்று எய்திய நீள் நிலம் அடங்கலும்","பரப்பு நீரால் பல் வளம் சுரக்க!\" என","ஆங்கு அவன் பொருட்டால் ஆயிரம்கண்ணோன்","ஓங்கு உயர் பெருஞ் சிறப்பு உலகோர்க்கு அளித்தலும்","பன்னீராண்டு பாண்டி நல் நாடு","மன் உயிர் மடிய மழை வளம் இழந்தது","வசித் தொழில் உதவ மா நிலம் கொழுப்பப்","பசிப்பு உயிர் அறியாப் பான்மைத்து ஆகலின்","ஆர் உயிர் ஓம்புநன் அம்பலப் பீடிகை","ஊண் ஒலி அரவம் ஒடுங்கியது ஆகி","விடரும் தூர்த்தரும் விட்டேற்றாளரும்","நடவை மாக்களும் நகையொடு வைகி","வட்டும் சூதும் வம்பக் கோட்டியும்","முட்டா வாழ்க்கை முறைமையது ஆக"],"p":"","r":"Indra wills that the protector's bowl, still brimming with food, should sit idle for want of beggars, and that the whole land should overflow with plenty from spreading rain; so for Aputhiran's sake the thousand-eyed gives the world great abundance, the Pandya land that had lost its rain for twelve years grows rich and knows no hunger, and the hall's noise of eating falls silent, left to rakes, idlers and wayfarers who laugh, play dice and gamble there.","n":"Indra's answer is a reward in form and a defeat in fact: plenty does not refute Aputhiran's scorn of heaven but takes away the hunger on which his giving depends. The sense of வசித் தொழில் (57) and whether the twelve-year drought (55 to 56) ended with this rain or preceded it are not certain; the same twelve-year formula returns for Savakam's famine at 25:101-102.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","pasi","eegai","soodhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 14, lines 49-64","lb":"MM14:49-64","ev":"Indra sends abundant rain so that no one needs Aputhiran's food, and the rest hall falls silent and becomes a haunt of gamblers and idlers.","cl":[],"ag":["விண்ணவர் தலைவன்","விடர், தூர்த்தர், விட்டேற்றாளர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:15"},"mm:14:65-78":{"book":"manimekalai","num":104,"t":["ஆபுத்திரன் தான் அம்பலம் நீங்கி","ஊரூர் தோறும் உண்போர் வினாஅய்","\"யார் இவன்?\" என்றே யாவரும் இகழ்ந்து ஆங்கு","அருந்த ஏமாந்த ஆர் உயிர் முதல்வனை","\"இருந்தாய் நீயோ!\" என்பார் இன்மையின்","திருவின் செல்வம் பெருங் கடல் கொள்ள","ஒரு தனி வரூஉம் பெருமகன் போல","தானே தமியன் வருவோன் தன்முன்","மாநீர் வங்கம் வந்தோர் வணங்கிச்","\"சாவக நல் நாட்டு தண் பெயல் மறுத்தலின்","ஊன் உயிர் மடிந்தது உரவோய்!\" என்றலும்","\"அமரர் கோன் ஆணையின் அருந்துவோர்ப் பெறாது","குமரி மூத்த என் பாத்திரம் ஏந்தி","அங்கு அந் நாட்டுப் புகுவது என் கருத்து\" என"],"p":"","r":"Aputhiran leaves the hall and goes from town to town asking for anyone who will eat, mocked by all with 'who is this?', alone like a rich man whose fortune the sea has taken, until men from a ship bow to him and tell him that in Savakam the rain has failed and creatures are dying, and he resolves to carry there the bowl that, by the king of the gods' command, has found no eaters and grown old unused.","n":"The giver is now the beggar of beggars, asking for someone to take food; குமரி மூத்த (77), 'grown old a maiden', pictures the bowl as a girl who never married for want of a taker.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","eegai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 14, lines 65-78","lb":"MM14:65-78","ev":"Aputhiran wanders finding no one to feed; shipmen tell him of famine in Savakam, and he decides to go there with the bowl.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆபுத்திரன்","வங்க மாக்கள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:15"},"mm:14:79-94":{"book":"manimekalai","num":105,"t":["வங்க மாக்களொடு மகிழ்வுடன் ஏறி","கால் விசை கடுகக் கடல் கலக்குறுதலின்","மால் இதை மணிபல்லவத்திடை வீழ்த்துத்","தங்கியது ஒரு நாள் தான் ஆங்கு இழிந்தனன்","\"இழிந்தோன் ஏறினன்\" என்று இதை எடுத்து","வழங்கு நீர் வங்கம் வல் இருள் போதலும்","வங்கம் போய பின் வருந்து துயர் எய்தி","அங்கு வாழ்வோர் யாவரும் இன்மையின்","\"மன் உயிர் ஓம்பும் இம் மா பெரும் பாத்திரம்","என் உயிர் ஓம்புதல் யானோ பொறேஎன்","தவம் தீர் மருங்கின் தனித் துயர் உழந்தேன்","சுமந்து என் பாத்திரம்?\" என்றனன் தொழுது","கோமுகி என்னும் கொழு நீர் இலஞ்சியின்","\"ஓர் யாண்டு ஒரு நாள் தோன்று\" என விடுவோன்","\"அருள் அறம் பூண்டு ஆங்கு ஆர் உயிர் ஓம்புநர்","உளர்எனில் அவர் கைப் புகுவாய்\" என்று ஆங்கு"],"p":"","r":"He sails gladly with the shipmen, but a gale drives the ship to Manipallavam, where he goes ashore; the crew, thinking he has re-embarked, sail off in the dark, and alone on the uninhabited island he says he will not use the great bowl that sustains all lives to sustain his own, that he has suffered lone sorrow where his merit ran out, and with folded hands lets it go into the Gomukhi pool, bidding it rise one day each year and enter the hands of any who wear compassion and dharma and sustain lives.","n":"The bowl is refused to its owner because it is for others; Aputhiran's release sets a condition (93 to 94) that Deepatilakai later applies to Manimekalai. தவம் தீர் மருங்கின் (89) is read as the exhausting of his merit, which he gives as the cause of his plight; the unit does not say whether the work agrees.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","arul","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 14, lines 79-94","lb":"MM14:79-94","ev":"Stranded on Manipallavam by the departing ship, Aputhiran consigns the bowl to the Gomukhi pool for a compassionate owner.","cl":[["a vessel that sustains all lives must not be used to sustain the life of the one who holds it","general","asserted",87,"aaputhiran"],["his lone suffering comes where his merit is exhausted","general","reported",89,"aaputhiran"],["the bowl should pass only to those who wear compassion and dharma and sustain lives","general","asserted",93,"aaputhiran"]],"ag":["ஆபுத்திரன்","வங்க மாக்கள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:15"},"mm:14:95-104":{"book":"manimekalai","num":106,"t":["உண்ணா நோன்போடு உயிர் பதிப் பெயர்ப்புழி","அந் நாள் ஆங்கு அவன் தன்பால் சென்றேன்","\"என் உற்றனையோ?\" என்று யான் கேட்பத்","தன் உற்றன பல தான் எடுத்து உரைத்தனன்","குண திசைத் தோன்றி கார் இருள் சீத்துக்","குட திசைச் சென்ற ஞாயிறு போல","மணிபல்லவத்திடை மன் உடம்பு இட்டு","தணியா மன் உயிர் தாங்கும் கருத்தொடு","சாவகம் ஆளும் தலைத் தாள் வேந்தன்","ஆ வயிற்று உதித்தனன் ஆங்கு அவன்தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"As Aputhiran was giving up his life by the vow of not eating, Aravana Adigal went to him and asked what had befallen him, heard his many troubles, and saw him, like the sun that rises in the east, clears the dark and sets in the west, leave his body on Manipallavam with an unquenched purpose of sustaining lives, and be born from a cow's womb to rule Savakam.","n":"The narrator is himself a witness: he was on the island at the death (96 to 98). The birth is shaped by the purpose held at death (102), and he is born in the very land whose famine he had set out to relieve. How சாவகம் ஆளும் தலைத் தாள் வேந்தன் (103) attaches to ஆ வயிற்று உதித்தனன் is not clear; kaathai 15 (15:15-30) confirms the birth from a cow, and 25:64-76 says the childless king took him up.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["unnaanonbu","piravi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 14, lines 95-104","lb":"MM14:95-104","ev":"Aputhiran dies by fasting on Manipallavam, watched by Aravana Adigal, and is reborn from a cow in Savakam.","cl":[["one who dies holding the purpose of sustaining lives is born again where that purpose can be carried out","general","asserted",102,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":["ஆபுத்திரன்","அறவண அடிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:15"},"mm:15:1-14":{"book":"manimekalai","num":107,"t":["'இன்னும் கேளாய் இளங்கொடி மாதே!","அந் நாள் அவனை ஓம்பிய நல் ஆத்","தண்ணென் சாவகத் தவள மால் வரை","மண்முகன் என்னும் மா முனி இடவயின்","பொன்னின் கோட்டது பொன் குளம்பு உடையது","தன் நலம் பிறர் தொழத் தான் சென்று எய்தி","ஈனாமுன்னம் இன் உயிர்க்கு எல்லாம்","தான் முலை சுரந்து தன் பால் ஊட்டலும்","மூன்று காலமும் தோன்ற நன்கு உணர்ந்த","ஆன்ற முனிவன்\" அதன் வயிற்று அகத்து","மழை வளம் சுரப்பவும் மன் உயிர் ஓம்பவும்","உயிர் காவலன் வந்து ஒருவன் தோன்றும்","குடர்த் தொடர் மாலை பூண்பான் அல்லன்","அடர்ப் பொன் முட்டை அகவையினான்\" என"],"p":"","r":"Aravanar goes on with Aputhiran's story: the good cow that once sheltered him, golden-horned and golden-hoofed, came to the great sage Manmukhan by the white mountain of Savakam and, before it had ever calved, gave its milk to every creature, and the sage who knew the three times foretold that from its womb would come a protector of lives to make the rain abound, born not with the cord about him but within a golden egg.","n":"The cow nurses all creatures before calving (ஈனாமுன்னம்), so its gift of milk precedes and announces the birth; and the prophecy names the newborn's work as bringing rain and guarding lives (11-12), the very want that drove Aputhiran from Madurai toward rainless Savakam in the previous kaathai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","arul","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 15, lines 1-14","lb":"MM15:1-14","ev":"In Savakam a golden-horned cow gives its milk to all creatures before calving, and the sage Manmukhan foretells that a protector of lives will be born from its womb in a golden egg.","cl":[],"ag":["நல் ஆ","மண்முகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:16"},"mm:15:15-28":{"book":"manimekalai","num":108,"t":["பிணி நோய் இன்றியும் பிறந்து அறம் செய்ய","மணிபல்லவத்திடை மன் உயிர் நீத்தோன்","தற்காத்து அளித்த தகை ஆ அதனை","ஒல்கா உள்ளத்து ஒழியான் ஆதலின்","ஆங்கு அவ் ஆ வயிற்று அமரர் கணம் உவப்பத்","தீம் கனி நாவல் ஓங்கும் இத் தீவினுக்கு","ஒரு தான் ஆகி உலகு தொழத் தோன்றினன்","பெரியோன் பிறந்த பெற்றியைக் கேள் நீ","இருது இளவேனிலில் எரி கதிர் இடபத்து","ஒருபதின் மேலும் ஒருமூன்று சென்ற பின்","மீனத்து இடைநிலை மீனத்து அகவையின்","போதித் தலைவனொடு பொருந்திய போழ்தத்து","மண்அகம் எல்லாம் மாரி இன்றியும்","புண்ணிய நல் நீர் போதொடு சொரிந்தது"],"p":"","r":"Aravanar says that the one who gave up his life on Manipallavam, because his steady heart never let go of the cow that had once guarded and fed him, was born from that cow's womb to the gods' delight, one alone for this jambu island, and he tells the season of the birth, a day of spring fixed to the Bodhi lord's own, when a rain of holy water and flowers fell over the whole earth without a cloud.","n":"The cause given for the womb he is born from is a bond of the mind: he 'did not let go' of the cow with an unwavering heart (17-18), which reads as gratitude carried through death; kaathai 14 had given his dying intent to sustain lives as the cause of the birth itself. The date at 23-26 is reckoned by the season, the sun in Taurus and the Buddha's day; line 25 (மீனத்து இடைநிலை மீனத்து அகவையின்) is obscure, and the reading does not rest on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","nanri","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 15, lines 15-28","lb":"MM15:15-28","ev":"Aputhiran, who died on Manipallavam, is born from the cow's womb in Savakam in spring, and holy water and flowers rain over the earth without clouds.","cl":[["one who dies with a being held unwavering in mind is reborn from that being's womb","buddhist","asserted",18,"aravanar"]],"ag":["ஆபுத்திரன்","நல் ஆ"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:16"},"mm:15:29-45":{"book":"manimekalai","num":109,"t":["\"போதி மாதவன் பூமியில் தோன்றும்","காலம் அன்றியும் கண்டன சிறப்பு\" என","சக்கரவாளக் கோட்டம் வாழும்","மிக்க மாதவர் விரும்பினர் வியந்து","\"கந்து உடை நெடு நிலை கடவுள் எழுதிய","அந்தில் பாவை அருளும் ஆயிடின்","அறிகுவம்\" என்றே செறி இருள் சேறலும்","\"மணிபல்லவத்திடை மன் உயிர் நீத்தோன்","தணியா உயிர் உய சாவகத்து உதித்தனன்","ஆங்கு அவன் தன் திறம் அறவணன் அறியும்\" என்று","ஈங்கு என் நாவை வருத்தியது இது கேள்","மண் ஆள் வேந்தன் மண்முகன் என்னும்","புண்ணிய முதல்வன் திருந்து அடி வணங்கி","\"மக்களை இல்லேன் மாதவன் அருளால்","பெற்றேன் புதல்வனை\" என்று அவன் வளர்ப்ப","அரைசு ஆள் செல்வம் அவன்பால் உண்மையின்","நிரை தார் வேந்தன் ஆயினன் அவன் தான்"],"p":"","r":"The ascetics of the Chakravala kottam, marvelling that such wonders were seen though it was not the time of a Buddha's coming, went through the thick dark to ask the image a god had painted on the pillar, which told them that the one who died on Manipallavam had arisen in Savakam to save lives and that Aravanan knew his story; that, Aravanar says, is what set his tongue to telling it; and the king of Savakam, bowing at Manmukhan's feet and saying that he had no child and had gained a son by the sage's grace, raised the boy, who became king since the wealth of rule was his.","n":"Aravanar names the pillar image as the source that sends the knowledge to him (36-39), so his account of a rebirth across the sea is given a witness; the childless king takes the egg-born child as a gift of a sage's grace (42-43), and the poem passes over how a foundling took the throne with the bare remark that the wealth of kingship was his (44).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","arasu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 15, lines 29-45","lb":"MM15:29-45","ev":"The pillar image tells the ascetics of the Chakravala kottam that Aputhiran is reborn in Savakam, and the childless king of Savakam takes him from the sage Manmukhan as a son, and he becomes king.","cl":[["wonders such as a cloudless rain of flowers belong to the time when a Buddha appears on earth","buddhist","asserted",29,"chakravala_maadhavar"]],"ag":["மிக்க மாதவர்","பாவை","மண் ஆள் வேந்தன்","ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:16"},"mm:15:46-54":{"book":"manimekalai","num":110,"t":["துறக்க வேந்தன் துய்ப்பிலன்கொல்லோ?","அறக் கோல் வேந்தன் அருளிலன்கொல்லோ","சுரந்து காவிரி புரந்து நீர் பரக்கவும்","நலத்தகை இன்றி நல் உயிர்க்கு எல்லாம்","அலத்தல்காலை ஆகியது ஆய் இழை!","வெண் திரை தந்த அமுதை வானோர்","உண்டு ஒழி மிச்சிலை ஒழித்து வைத்தாங்கு","வறன் ஓடு உலகின் வான் துயர் கெடுக்கும்","அறன் ஓடு ஒழித்தல் ஆய் இழை! தகாது' என"],"p":"","r":"Aravanar asks whether the king of heaven lacks his share or the king of the just sceptre lacks grace, observes that though the Kaveri swells and spreads its water a season of want has come on every living thing, and tells Manimekalai that to put away the bowl that ends the world's great hunger, as the gods put away the leftover nectar the white waves gave them, is not fitting.","n":"The two questions (46-47) seem to clear both the god of rain and the just king, so the want of 48-50 is given no culprit; the sense of துய்ப்பிலன் is uncertain. The comparison at 51-52 turns the gods' nectar, saved and kept after they had eaten, into the type of a hoard withheld from the hungry.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasi","eegai","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 15, lines 46-54","lb":"MM15:46-54","ev":"Aravanar tells Manimekalai that want has come despite the flowing Kaveri and that she must not lay the bowl aside.","cl":[["it is wrong to keep back a means of ending the world's hunger, as the gods kept back the leftover nectar","general","asserted",54,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:16"},"mm:15:55-70":{"book":"manimekalai","num":111,"t":["மாதவன் உரைத்தலும் மணிமேகலை தான்","தாயர் தம்மொடு தாழ்ந்து பல ஏத்தி","கைக்கொண்டு எடுத்த கடவுள் கடிஞையொடு","பிக்குணிக் கோலத்துப் பெருந் தெரு அடைதலும்","ஒலித்து ஒருங்கு ஈண்டிய ஊர்க் குறுமாக்களும்","மெலித்து உகு நெஞ்சின் விடரும் தூர்த்தரும்","கொடிக் கோசம்பிக் கோமகன் ஆகிய","வடித் தேர்த் தானை வத்தவன் தன்னை","வஞ்சம் செய்துழி வான் தளை விடீஇய","உஞ்சையில் தோன்றிய யூகி அந்தணன்","உருவுக்கு ஒவ்வா உறு நோய் கண்டு","பரிவுறு மாக்களின் தாம் பரிவு எய்தி","'உதயகுமரன் உளம் கொண்டு ஒளித்த","மதுமலர்க் குழலாள் வந்து தோன்றி","பிச்சைப் பாத்திரம் கையின் ஏந்தியது","திப்பியம்' என்றே சிந்தை நோய் கூர"],"p":"","r":"When the sage has spoken, Manimekalai bows with her mothers, takes up the divine bowl and goes in a bhikkhuni's garb into the great street, and the town's little folk, rakes and libertines crowd round with noise, pitying her as men once pitied Yugi the Brahmin of Ujjain in a sickness unsuited to his form, when he freed the Vatsa king of Kosambi from his bonds, and they grieve that the honey-haired girl Udayakumaran had set his heart on has come carrying a begging bowl.","n":"The crowd sees disgrace in the bowl (சிந்தை நோய் கூர), while the simile likens her garb to Yugi's feigned disease, a guise that hid a plan to free a king from prison; the onlookers are the same 'rakes and libertines' (விடரும் தூர்த்தரும்) who in kaathai 14 idled in Aputhiran's hall once plenty made his bowl useless. Udayakumaran is only named, not present.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","vesham","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 15, lines 55-70","lb":"MM15:55-70","ev":"Manimekalai bows with her mothers and goes into the great street in a nun's garb with the bowl, and the townsfolk crowd round, grieving that the girl the prince loved now carries a begging bowl.","cl":[],"ag":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை","மாதவி","சுதமதி","ஊர்க் குறுமாக்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:16"},"mm:15:71-86":{"book":"manimekalai","num":112,"t":["மண மனை மறுகில் மாதவி ஈன்ற","அணி மலர்ப் பூங் கொம்பு 'அகம் மலி உவகையின்","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர் பண்புடன் இடூஉம்","பிச்சை ஏற்றல் பெருந் தகவு உடைத்து' எனக்","'குளன் அணி தாமரைக் கொழு மலர் நாப்பண்","ஒரு தனிஓங்கிய திருமலர் போன்று","வான் தருகற்பின் மனை உறை மகளிரின்","தான் தனி ஓங்கிய தகைமையள் அன்றோ","ஆதிரை நல்லாள்? அவள் மனை இம் மனை","நீ புகல்வேண்டும் நேர் இழை!' என்றனள்","வட திசை விஞ்சை மா நகர்த் தோன்றித்","தென் திசைப் பொதியில் ஓர் சிற்றியாற்று அடைகரை","மாதவன் தன்னால் வல் வினை உருப்ப","சாவம் பட்டு தனித் துயர் உறூஉம்","வீவு இல் வெம் பசி வேட்கையொடு திரிதரும்","காயசண்டிகை எனும் காரிகை தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"The flowering branch Madhavi bore says with full gladness that to receive the alms good chaste wives give is a thing of great worth, and Kayasandigai, a woman of the northern vidyadhara city who, her harsh deed ripening through a sage's curse by a stream on southern Pothiyil, wanders in unending fierce hunger, tells her that Athirai stands alone among the house-dwelling wives whose chastity gives rain, like one lotus risen above the rest in a pool, and that this is her house, which she should enter.","n":"The nun's first choice is of a giver, not of food: the bowl is to take its first alms from a pathini, and the one who points the way is herself a woman suffering the fruit of her own deed. Manimekalai is named here as Madhavi's child (மாதவி ஈன்ற) just as she seeks a chaste wife's alms, and Kayasandigai's praise of Athirai's rain-giving karpu (77) answers her claim at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","karpu","pasi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 15, lines 71-86","lb":"MM15:71-86","ev":"Manimekalai says that alms from a chaste wife are worthiest, and Kayasandigai, a vidyadhara woman cursed to endless hunger, tells her to enter Athirai's house.","cl":[["alms received from a chaste wife are of the greatest worth","general","asserted",74,"manimekalai"],["a wife's chastity brings the rain","general","asserted",77,"kaayasandigai"],["a grave deed ripens through a sage's curse into suffering","general","asserted",83,"narrator"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","காயசண்டிகை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:16"},"mm:16:1-21":{"book":"manimekalai","num":113,"t":["'ஈங்கு இவள் செய்தி கேள்' என விஞ்சையர்","பூங்கொடி மாதர்க்குப் புகுந்ததை உரைப்போள்","'ஆதிரை கணவன் ஆய் இழை! கேளாய்","சாதுவன் என்போன் தகவு இலன் ஆகி","அணி இழை தன்னை அகன்றனன் போகி","கணிகை ஒருத்தி கைத்தூண் நல்க","வட்டினும் சூதினும் வான் பொருள் வழங்கி","கெட்ட பொருளின் கிளை கேடுறுதலின்","பேணிய கணிகையும் பிறர் நலம் காட்டி","\"காணம் இலி\" என கையுதிர்க்கோடலும்","வங்கம் போகும் வாணிகர் தம்முடன்","தங்கா வேட்கையின் தானும் செல்வுழி","நளி இரு முந்நீர் வளி கலன் வௌவ","ஒடி மரம் பற்றி ஊர் திரை உதைப்ப","நக்க சாரணர் நாகர் வாழ் மலைப்","பக்கம் சார்ந்து அவர் பான்மையன் ஆயினன்","நாவாய் கேடுற நல் மரம் பற்றிப்","போயினன் தன்னோடு உயிர் உயப் போந்தோர்","\"இடை இருள் யாமத்து எறி திரைப் பெருங் கடல்","உடை கலப் பட்டு ஆங்கு ஒழிந்தோர் தம்முடன்","சாதுவன் தானும் சாவுற்றான்\" என"],"p":"","r":"Kayasandigai begins Aathirai's story for Manimekalai: her husband Saathuvan, lacking worth, left her for a courtesan who fed him, squandered his wealth on dice and gaming, was cast off by the courtesan as a man without a coin once his wealth was gone, sailed off with merchants in restless desire, was wrecked, clung to a broken spar and was washed to the hill of the naked Nagas, while the men who escaped the wreck reported that he had died with the others in the dark sea.","n":"The tale is told as a lesson attached to a house: Kayasandigai sends Manimekalai to Aathirai's door and then explains why it is the right first door, and she begins not with Aathirai's virtue but with her husband's fault (தகவு இலன், line 4). Line 16 tells the reader ahead of time that he came to belong to the Nagas (அவர் பான்மையன் ஆயினன்), so the survivors' report of his death at line 21 is known to be false before Aathirai hears it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","soodhu","selvam","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 16, lines 1-21","lb":"MM16:1-21","ev":"Saathuvan leaves Aathirai for a courtesan, loses his wealth at dice, is cast off, sails with merchants and is shipwrecked; survivors report him dead.","cl":[],"ag":["சாதுவன்","கணிகை","வாணிகர்","உயிர் உயப் போந்தோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:17"},"mm:16:22-43":{"book":"manimekalai","num":114,"t":["ஆதிரை நல்லாள் ஆங்கு அது தான் கேட்டு","\"ஊரீரேயோ! ஒள் அழல் ஈமம்","தாரீரோ?\" எனச் சாற்றினள் கழறி","சுடலைக் கானில் தொடு குழிப்படுத்து","முடலை விறகின் முளி எரி பொத்தி","\"மிக்க என் கணவன் வினைப் பயன் உய்ப்பப்","புக்குழிப் புகுவேன்\" என்று அவள் புகுதலும்","படுத்து உடன் வைத்த பாயல் பள்ளியும்","உடுத்த கூறையும் ஒள் எரி உறா அது","ஆடிய சாந்தமும் அசைந்த கூந்தலில்","சூடிய மாலையும் தொல் நிறம் வழாது","விரை மலர்த் தாமரை ஒரு தனி இருந்த","திருவின் செய்யோள் போன்று இனிது இருப்பத்","\"தீயும் கொல்லாத் தீவினையாட்டியேன்","யாது செய்கேன்?\" என்று அவள் ஏங்கலும்","\"ஆதிரை! கேள் உன் அரும் பெறல் கணவனை","ஊர் திரை கொண்டு ஆங்கு உய்ப்பப் போகி","நக்க சாரணர் நாகர் வாழ் மலைப்","பக்கம் சேர்ந்தனன் பல் யாண்டு இராஅன்","சந்திரதத்தன் எனும் ஓர் வாணிகன்","வங்கம் தன்னொடும் வந்தனன் தோன்றும்","நின் பெருந் துன்பம் ஒழிவாய் நீ\" என"],"p":"","r":"Hearing the report, Aathirai calls on the townsfolk to give her a funeral pyre, digs a pit in the burning ground, lights a fire of dry wood and enters it saying she will go where her husband's deeds have carried him, but the fire touches neither her bed, her cloth, her sandal paste nor the garland in her hair, so that she sits in it unharmed like Lakshmi alone on a lotus, and when she cries that she is a woman of evil deeds whom even fire will not kill, a voice tells her that her husband reached the Naga hill and will come back on the ship of the merchant Chandradattan.","n":"Aathirai reads the miracle backwards: the fire's refusal, which the story offers as proof of her virtue, she takes as proof of her own bad karma (தீயும் கொல்லாத் தீவினையாட்டியேன், line 35). Her reason for entering the fire is also karmic and not only grief: she means to follow her husband into whatever birth his deeds have led him (வினைப் பயன் உய்ப்ப, line 27). The lotus and the goddess on it (lines 33-34) echo the image by which Kayasandigai praised her at the close of the previous kaathai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","karpu","vinai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 16, lines 22-43","lb":"MM16:22-43","ev":"Aathirai enters a funeral fire for her husband; it does not burn her, and a voice from the sky tells her he lives and will return.","cl":[["a wife may follow her dead husband into the place his deeds carry him","general","reported",27,"aathirai"],["a woman whom even fire will not kill is held by her own evil deeds","general","reported",35,"aathirai"]],"ag":["பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர்","அசரீரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:17"},"mm:16:44-59":{"book":"manimekalai","num":115,"t":["அந்தரம் தோன்றி அசரீரி அறைதலும்","ஐ அரி உண் கண் அழு துயர் நீங்கி","பொய்கை புக்கு ஆடிப் போதுவாள் போன்று","மனம் கவல்வு இன்றி மனைஅகம் புகுந்து \"என்","கண் மணி அனையான் கடிது ஈங்கு உறுக!\" என","புண்ணியம் முட்டாள் பொழி மழை தரூஉம்","அரும் பெறல் மரபின் பத்தினிப் பெண்டிரும்","விரும்பினர் தொழூஉம் வியப்பினள் ஆயினள்","ஆங்கு அவள் கணவனும் அலைநீர் அடைகரை","ஓங்கு உயர் பிறங்கல் ஒரு மர நீழல்","மஞ்சு உடை மால் கடல் உழந்த நோய் கூர்ந்து","துஞ்சு துயில்கொள்ள அச் சூர் மலை வாழும்","நக்க சாரணர் நயமிலர் தோன்றி","பக்கம் சேர்ந்து \"பரி புலம்பினன் இவன்","தானே தமியன் வந்தனன் அளியன்","ஊன் உடை இவ் உடம்பு உணவு\" என்று எழுப்பலும்"],"p":"","r":"When the bodiless voice has spoken from the sky, Aathirai's tears cease, she goes home untroubled like a woman coming back from a bath in a pool and prays for her husband's quick return, becoming one whom even the chaste wives who never fail in merit and bring down the rain revere; meanwhile her husband, worn out by the sea, sleeps in a tree's shade on the shore of the high hill, and the unkind naked Nagas who live there find him and rouse him, saying that this lone, pitiable man come by himself is a body of flesh fit for food.","n":"The rank given here goes beyond the rain-bringing wife: the women whose word brings rain themselves worship Aathirai (lines 49-51), so the test by fire sets her above the very standard of chastity. The simile at line 46, a woman returning from bathing in a pool, turns the pyre she has just left into water. The scene then cuts from a wife whose fire would not burn her body to a husband whose body is about to be eaten.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","mazhai","karpu","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 16, lines 44-59","lb":"MM16:44-59","ev":"Aathirai goes home comforted; on the island the naked Nagas find the sleeping Saathuvan and mean to eat him.","cl":[["chaste wives whose merit brings rain themselves worship the wife whom fire would not burn","general","asserted",50,"kaayasandigai"]],"ag":["அசரீரி","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர்","சாதுவன்","நக்க சாரணர் நாகர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:17"},"mm:16:60-77":{"book":"manimekalai","num":116,"t":["மற்று அவர் பாடை மயக்கு அறு மரபின்","கற்றனன் ஆதலின் கடுந் தொழில் மாக்கள்","சுற்றும் நீங்கித் தொழுது உரையாடி","ஆங்கு அவர் உரைப்போர் \"அருந்திறல்! கேளாய்","ஈங்கு எம் குருமகன் இருந்தோன் அவன்பால்","போந்தருள் நீ\" என அவருடன் போகி","கள் அடு குழிசியும் கழி முடை நாற்றமும்","வெள் என்பு உணங்கலும் விரவிய இருக்கையில்","எண்கு தன் பிணவோடு இருந்தது போல","பெண்டுடன் இருந்த பெற்றி நோக்கி","பாடையின் பிணித்து அவன் பான்மையன் ஆகிக்","கோடு உயர் மர நிழல் குளிர்ந்த பின் அவன்","\"ஈங்கு நீ வந்த காரணம் என்?\" என","ஆங்கு அவற்கு அலை கடல் உற்றதை உரைத்தலும்","\"அருந்துதல் இன்றி அலை கடல் உழந்தோன்","வருந்தினன் அளியன் வம்மின் மாக்காள்","நம்பிக்கு இளையள் ஓர் நங்கையைக் கொடுத்து","வெங் களும் ஊனும் வேண்டுவ கொடும்\" என"],"p":"","r":"Because Saathuvan had learned their language well, the fierce men stop, bow and talk with him and lead him to their chief, whom he finds sitting with his woman like a bear with its mate in a place full of liquor pots, a reek of rotting flesh and white bones drying; bound to him by the shared tongue, the chief asks in the cool of a tree why he came, hears of his sufferings at sea, and orders his people to give the starving man a young woman, strong liquor and meat.","n":"Saathuvan is saved by learning, not by virtue: knowing the Nagas' speech (பாடை, lines 60 and 70) turns him from food into a guest. The chief's welcome is generous by his own lights, offering what he holds most valuable, which sets up the coming debate as a clash of goods rather than of kindness and cruelty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","kal","pulaal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 16, lines 60-77","lb":"MM16:60-77","ev":"Saathuvan speaks to the Nagas in their language and is led to their chief, who orders a woman, liquor and meat to be given to him.","cl":[],"ag":["சாதுவன்","நக்க சாரணர் நாகர்","குருமகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:17"},"mm:16:78-89":{"book":"manimekalai","num":117,"t":["அவ் உரை கேட்ட சாதுவன் அயர்ந்து","\"வெவ்உரை கேட்டேன் வேண்டேன்\" என்றலும்","\"பெண்டிரும் உண்டியும் இன்றுஎனின் மாக்கட்கு","உண்டோ ஞாலத்து உறு பயன்? உண்டுஎனின்","காண்குவம் யாங்களும் காட்டுவாயாக\" என","தூண்டிய சினத்தினன் \"சொல்\" என சொல்லும்","\"மயக்கும் கள்ளும் மன் உயிர் கோறலும்","கயக்கு அறு மாக்கள் கடிந்தனர் கேளாய்","பிறந்தவர் சாதலும் இறந்தவர் பிறத்தலும்","உறங்கலும் விழித்தலும் போன்றது உண்மையின்","'நல் அறம் செய்வோர் நல் உலகு அடைதலும்","அல் அறம் செய்வோர் அரு நரகு அடைதலும்"],"p":"","r":"Saathuvan, dismayed, says he has heard a cruel word and wants none of it; the chief, provoked to anger, asks what use there is for men in the world without women and food and bids him show it if there is any; Saathuvan answers that the faultless have forbidden deluding liquor and the killing of living beings, because dying and being born are like sleeping and waking, and the doers of good reach a good world and the doers of ill a hard hell.","n":"The man who lost everything to a courtesan and to dice now refuses a woman and liquor offered as hospitality, so the refusal reads as the reversal of his fall in the first unit. His reason for abstaining is not disgust but a world-view: abstinence follows from rebirth and its fruits. Line 86-87 puts the sleep-and-waking simile of Kural 339 in other words (உறங்கலும் விழித்தலும்), too far from the couplet's wording to count as an echo. The sentence runs on into line 90.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","kollaamai","piravi","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 16, lines 78-89","lb":"MM16:78-89","ev":"Saathuvan refuses the woman, liquor and meat; the angry chief challenges him and Saathuvan explains why the wise forbid liquor and killing.","cl":[["without women and food there is no gain for men in this world","general","refuted",80,"naga_guru"],["the faultless have forbidden liquor and the killing of living beings","general","asserted",84,"saathuvan"],["death and birth follow one another as sleep and waking do","general","asserted",86,"saathuvan"],["those who do good reach a good world and those who do ill reach hell","general","asserted",88,"saathuvan"]],"ag":["சாதுவன்","குருமகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:17"},"mm:16:90-106":{"book":"manimekalai","num":118,"t":["உண்டு' என உணர்தலின் உரவோர் களைந்தனர்","கண்டனை ஆக!\" என கடு நகை எய்தி","\"உடம்பு விட்டு ஓடும் உயிர் உருக் கொண்டு ஓர்","இடம் புகும் என்றே எமக்கு ஈங்கு உரைத்தாய்","அவ் உயிர் எவ்வணம் போய்ப் புகும், அவ் வகை","செவ்வனம் உரை\" எனச் சினவாது \"இது கேள்","உற்றதை உணரும் உடல் உயிர் வாழ்வுழி","மற்றைய உடம்பே மன் உயிர் நீங்கிடின்","தடிந்து எரியூட்டினும் தான் உணராதுஎனின்","உடம்பிடைப் போனது ஒன்று உண்டு என உணர் நீ","போனார் தமக்கு ஓர் புக்கில் உண்டு என்பது","யானோ அல்லேன் யாவரும் உணர்குவர்","உடம்பு ஈண்டு ஒழிய உயிர் பல காவதம்","கடந்து சேண் சேறல் கனவினும் காண்குவை","ஆங்கனம் போகி அவ் உயிர் செய் வினை","பூண்ட யாக்கையின் புகுவது தெளி நீ\"","என்று அவன் உரைத்தலும் எரி விழி நாகனும்"],"p":"","r":"Saathuvan ends that the wise gave these things up because they understood this; the chief laughs harshly and demands to be told plainly how a life that runs out of the body takes on a form and enters another place; Saathuvan, without anger, argues that the body feels while life dwells in it, but once life leaves, the body feels nothing though it is cut and burned, so something has gone out of it; that the departed have a dwelling everyone knows, not he alone; that in dreams the life goes many leagues while the body stays here; and that, going so, the life enters the body its own deeds have put on.","n":"Saathuvan answers a request for a mechanism with two proofs by inference: the corpse's insensibility (lines 96-99) and the dream journey (lines 102-103), each leading to the conclusion that something leaves the body. It is a lay merchant's argument, not a monk's, and it speaks of a life (உயிர்) that travels and enters a new body, which the Buddhist teaching later in the epic would put differently; the text names no school for it. The chief had already granted the soul in his question (line 92 repeats what he was told), so the argument persuades him without his ever conceding the premise in words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","vinai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 16, lines 90-106","lb":"MM16:90-106","ev":"The chief mockingly asks how a life can pass into another body, and Saathuvan answers him with the corpse and the dream.","cl":[["since a body cut and burned after death feels nothing, something that felt has gone out of it","general","asserted",99,"saathuvan"],["in dreams the life travels far while the body stays, so the life can leave the body","general","asserted",102,"saathuvan"],["the departing life enters the body that its own deeds have fitted to it","general","asserted",104,"saathuvan"]],"ag":["குருமகன்","சாதுவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:17"},"mm:16:107-116":{"book":"manimekalai","num":119,"t":["நன்று அறி செட்டி நல் அடி வீழ்ந்து","\"கள்ளும் ஊனும் கைவிடின் இவ் உடம்பு","உள் உறை வாழ் உயிர் ஓம்புதல் ஆற்றேன்","தமக்கு ஒழி மரபின் சாவுறுகாறும்","எமக்கு ஆம் நல் அறம் எடுத்து உரை\" என்றலும்","\"நன்று சொன்னாய்! நல் நெறிப் படர்குவை","உன் தனக்கு ஒல்லும் நெறி அறம் உரைத்தேன்","உடை கல மாக்கள் உயிர் உய்ந்து ஈங்கு உறின்","அடு தொழில் ஒழிந்து அவர் ஆர் உயிர் ஓம்பி","மூத்து விளி மா ஒழித்து எவ் உயிர்மாட்டும்"],"p":"","r":"The chief falls at the feet of the merchant who knows the good and says that if he gives up liquor and meat he cannot keep alive the life within this body, so he asks to be told a good dharma that suits them until they die in their own way; Saathuvan praises the request, says he will tell the dharma within his reach, and bids him, if shipwrecked men come here alive, to stop killing and protect their lives, and, leaving aside the beasts that die of old age, to give up harm toward every living thing.","n":"Saathuvan does not ask the Nagas for the full abstinence he practises; he scales dharma to what they can bear (உன் தனக்கு ஒல்லும் நெறி அறம், line 113). The sense of line 116 is not certain: மூத்து விளி மா ஒழித்து seems to set aside animals that die of age as the one flesh still allowed, flesh that needs no killing, but it may instead belong with the prohibition. The command closes at line 117.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","kollaamai","pulaal","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 16, lines 107-116","lb":"MM16:107-116","ev":"The chief, persuaded, asks for a dharma he can keep, and Saathuvan gives him a lesser rule: spare the shipwrecked and harm no living thing.","cl":[["without liquor and meat the life in this body cannot be kept","general","reported",108,"naga_guru"],["dharma may be fitted to what a person can keep: spare the shipwrecked, eat only what dies of age, and harm no living being","general","asserted",113,"saathuvan"]],"ag":["குருமகன்","சாதுவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:17"},"mm:16:117-135":{"book":"manimekalai","num":120,"t":["தீத்திறம் ஒழிக!\" எனச் சிறுமகன் உரைப்போன்","\"ஈங்கு எமக்கு ஆகும் இவ் அறம் செய்கேம்","ஆங்கு உனக்கு ஆகும் அரும் பொருள் கொள்க\" எனப்","\"பண்டும் பண்டும் கலம் கவிழ் மாக்களை","உண்டேம் அவர் தம் உறு பொருள் ஈங்கு இவை","விரை மரம் மென் துகில் விழு நிதிக் குப்பையோடு","இவை இவை கொள்க\" என எடுத்தனன் கொணர்ந்து","சந்திரதத்தன் என்னும் வாணிகன்","வங்கம் சேர்ந்ததில் வந்து உடன் ஏறி","இந் நகர் புகுந்து ஈங்கு இவளொடு வாழ்ந்து","தன் மனை நன் பல தானமும் செய்தனன்","ஆங்கனம் ஆகிய ஆதிரை கையால்","பூங் கொடி நல்லாய்! பிச்சை பெறுக!\" என","மனைஅகம் புகுந்து மணிமேகலை தான்","புனையா ஓவியம் போல நிற்றலும்","தொழுது வலம் கொண்டு துயர் அறு கிளவியோடு","அமுதசுரபியின் அகன் சுரை நிறைதர","'பார்அகம் அடங்கலும் பசிப் பிணி அறுக' என","ஆதிரை இட்டனள் ஆருயிர்மருந்து என்"],"p":"","r":"The chief promises to keep this dharma and bids Saathuvan take the precious goods fit for him, the fragrant wood, fine cloth and heaps of treasure of men from wrecked ships whom they had eaten before; Saathuvan boards Chandradattan's ship when it comes, returns to this city, lives with Aathirai and gives many gifts from his house; Kayasandigai bids Manimekalai take alms from such an Aathirai's hand, and when Manimekalai enters and stands like an unadorned picture, Aathirai bows, circles her and fills the wide bowl Amudasurabi, saying 'may the disease of hunger be cut off from the whole earth', and gives the food that is medicine for precious life.","n":"The wealth that ends the story is the plunder of men the Nagas ate, now given as a teacher's fee and spent on charity at home; the cannibal's hoard becomes the merchant's alms. The voice's prophecy is fulfilled in a single line (line 124). Kayasandigai's speech ends at line 129, and lines 130-135 are the poet's narration. Aathirai's wish at line 134 turns one gift into a vow for the whole earth, and line 135 names the food ஆருயிர்மருந்து, a name the bowl's story uses elsewhere.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi","iravu","pathini"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 16, lines 117-135","lb":"MM16:117-135","ev":"Saathuvan returns home rich on Chandradattan's ship; Aathirai puts the first alms into Manimekalai's bowl.","cl":[["food given to the hungry is the medicine that saves life","general","asserted",135,"narrator"]],"ag":["குருமகன்","சாதுவன்","சந்திரதத்தன்","மணிமேகலை","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:17"},"mm:17:1-16":{"book":"manimekalai","num":121,"t":["பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர் பாத்தூண் ஏற்ற","பிச்சைப் பாத்திரப் பெருஞ் சோற்று அமலை","அறத்தின் ஈட்டிய ஒண் பொருள் அறவோன்","திறத்து வழிப்படூஉம் செய்கை போல","வாங்கு கை வருந்த மன் உயிர்க்கு அளித்துத்","தான் தொலைவு இல்லாத் தகைமை நோக்கி","யானைத்தீ நோய் அகவயிற்று அடக்கிய","காயசண்டிகை எனும் காரிகை வணங்கி","'நெடியோன் மயங்கி நிலமிசைத் தோன்றி","அடல் அரு முந்நீர் அடைத்த ஞான்று","குரங்கு கொணர்ந்து எறிந்த நெடு மலை எல்லாம்","அணங்கு உடை அளக்கர் வயிறு புக்காங்கு","இட்டது ஆற்றாக் கட்டு அழல் கடும் பசிப்","பட்டேன் என் தன் பழ வினைப் பயத்தால்","அன்னை கேள் நீ ஆர் உயிர் மருத்துவி","துன்னிய என் நோய் துடைப்பாய்!' என்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"The narrator likens the bowl, whose first food a chaste wife gave, to wealth won by dharma that flows the way of the virtuous: Manimekalai gives to living beings till the receivers' hands ache and it never runs low, and Kayasandigai, who carries the elephant-fire disease in her belly, bows and asks her, as physician of life, to cure a hunger that swallows whatever is given as the sea swallowed the hills the monkeys threw when the Tall One bridged it, a hunger she says came from her old deeds.","n":"Two causes meet in one sentence of Kayasandigai's: the hunger is a disease (நோய், 7 and 16) and the fruit of old deeds (பழ வினைப் பயத்தால், 14), so the cure she seeks is food from another's hand, not merit of her own. The epic names Vishnu's descent as Rama with the word மயங்கி (9), 'bewildered' or 'having taken on confusion', a casual Buddhist touch on a Vaishnava story that the line does not develop. She calls Manimekalai ஆர் உயிர் மருத்துவி, the Sangam title of the giver as physician of hunger turned feminine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","eegai","vinai","pathini"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 17, lines 1-16","lb":"MM17:1-16","ev":"Manimekalai feeds many from the alms bowl, and Kayasandigai, bowing, begs her to cure her insatiable hunger.","cl":[["wealth earned by dharma and spent in the way of the virtuous does not run out","general","asserted",3,"narrator"],["present suffering is the fruit of one's own old deeds","general","reported",14,"kaayasandigai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","காயசண்டிகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:18"},"mm:17:17-32":{"book":"manimekalai","num":122,"t":["எடுத்த பாத்திரத்து ஏந்திய அமுதம்","பிடித்து அவள் கையில் பேணினள் பெய்தலும்","வயிறு காய் பெரும் பசி நீங்கி மற்று அவள்","துயரம் நீங்கித் தொழுதனள் உரைக்கும்","'மாசு இல்வாள் ஒளி வட திசைச் சேடிக்","காசு இல் காஞ்சனபுரக் கடி நகர் உள்ளேன்","விஞ்சையன் தன்னொடு என் வெவ் வினை உருப்பத்","தென் திசைப் பொதியில் காணிய வந்தேன்","கடுவரல் அருவிக் கடும் புனல் கொழித்த","இடு மணல் கான் யாற்று இயைந்து ஒருங்கு இருந்தேன்","புரி நூல் மார்பின் திரி புரி வார் சடை","மரவுரி உடையன் விருச்சிகன் என்போன்","பெருங் குலைப் பெண்ணைக் கருங் கனி அனையது ஓர்","இருங் கனி நாவல் பழம் ஒன்று ஏந்தி","தேக்கு இலை வைத்துச் சேண் நாறு பரப்பின்","பூக் கமழ் பொய்கை ஆடச் சென்றோன்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai puts food from the bowl into Kayasandigai's hand, her burning hunger and grief leave her, and she begins her story: she lived in Kanchanapuram on the northern ridge of the vidyadharas, came south with her vidyadhara to see Pothiyil as her cruel deed was ripening, and stayed by a forest river where Viruchikan, a sage with the sacred thread, twisted matted locks and bark clothes, set a great jambu fruit like a palm fruit on a teak leaf and went to bathe in a lotus pool.","n":"The journey itself is credited to the ripening deed (என் வெவ் வினை உருப்ப, 23) before anything wrong is done: karma here moves the traveller to the place of the offence. Viruchikan's marks (thread, matted hair, bark) are those of a Brahmin forest ascetic, not a Buddhist monk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pasi","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 17, lines 17-32","lb":"MM17:17-32","ev":"Kayasandigai, fed, tells how she came south with her vidyadhara and how the sage Viruchikan left his fruit on a leaf to bathe.","cl":[["a ripening evil deed draws its bearer to the place where it will come to fruit","general","reported",23,"kaayasandigai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","காயசண்டிகை","காஞ்சனன்","விருச்சிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:18"},"mm:17:33-48":{"book":"manimekalai","num":123,"t":["தீவினை உருத்தலின் செருக்கொடு சென்றேன்","காலால் அந்தக் கருங் கனி சிதைத்தேன்","உண்டல் வேட்கையின் வரூஉம் விருச்சிகன்","கண்டனன் என்னைக் கருங் கனிச் சிதைவுடன்","\"சீர் திகழ் நாவலில் திப்பியம் ஆனது","ஈர் ஆறு ஆண்டில் ஒரு கனி தருவது","அக் கனி உண்டோர் ஆறு ஈர் ஆண்டு","மக்கள் யாக்கையின் வரும் பசி நீங்குவர்","பன்னீராண்டில் ஒரு நாள் அல்லது","உண்ணா நோன்பினேன் உண் கனி சிதைத்தாய்!","அந்தரம் செல்லும் மந்திரம் இழந்து","தந்தித் தீயால் தனித் துயர் உழந்து","முந்நால் ஆண்டில் முதிர் கனி நான் ஈங்கு","உண்ணும் நாள் உன் உறு பசி களைக!\" என","அந் நாள் ஆங்கு அவன் இட்ட சாபம்","இந் நாள் போலும் இளங்கொடி! கெடுத்தனை!"],"p":"","r":"Driven by her ripening evil deed she went proudly and crushed the fruit with her foot; Viruchikan, coming to eat, saw it and cursed her: the divine jambu gives one fruit in twelve years that frees a human body from hunger for twelve years, he fasts but one day in twelve years, and since she crushed his food she shall lose her sky-travelling mantra and suffer alone the elephant-fire until, twelve years on, he eats a ripe fruit here; and she tells Manimekalai that the curse of that day seems to end today, for she has destroyed it.","n":"The curse is measured exactly against the sage's loss: he must now fast twelve more years, and she must hunger until he eats. Her own account puts the cause first in her deed (தீவினை உருத்தலின், 33) and only then in her pride (செருக்கு). Line 48 may mean the term falls today or that Manimekalai's food has ended it early.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saabam","vinai","thavam","nonbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 17, lines 33-48","lb":"MM17:33-48","ev":"Kayasandigai crushes Viruchikan's fruit, and he curses her with ceaseless hunger and the loss of her sky mantra for twelve years.","cl":[["an evil deed, when it ripens, drives one to the very act that brings punishment","general","reported",33,"kaayasandigai"],["an ascetic's fast gives his word the power to lay a binding curse","general","reported",42,"viruchikan"]],"ag":["காயசண்டிகை","விருச்சிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:18"},"mm:17:49-70":{"book":"manimekalai","num":124,"t":["வாடு பசி உழந்து மா முனி போய பின்","பாடு இமிழ் அருவிப் பய மலை ஒழிந்து என்","அலவலைச் செய்திக்கு அஞ்சினன் அகன்ற","இலகு ஒளி விஞ்சையன் விழுமமோடு எய்தி","\"ஆர் அணங்கு ஆகிய அருந் தவன் தன்னால்","காரணம் இன்றியும் கடு நோய் உழந்தனை!","வானூடு எழுக\" என மந்திரம் மறந்தேன்!","ஊன் உயிர் நீங்கும் உருப்பொடு தோன்றி","வயிறு காய் பெரும் பசி வருத்தும் என்றேற்கு","தீம் கனி கிழங்கு செழுங் காய் நல்லன","ஆங்கு அவன் கொணரவும் ஆற்றேன்ஆக","நீங்கல் ஆற்றான் நெடுந் துயர் எய்தி","ஆங்கு அவன் ஆங்கு எனக்கு அருளொடும் உரைப்போன்","\"சம்புத் தீவினுள் தமிழக மருங்கில்","கம்பம் இல்லாக் கழி பெருஞ் செல்வர்","ஆற்றா மாக்கட்கு ஆற்றும் துணை ஆகி","நோற்றோர் உறைவது ஓர் நோன் நகர் உண்டால்","பல நாள் ஆயினும் நிலனொடு போகி","அப் பதிப் புகுக\" என்று அவன் அருள்செய்ய","இப் பதிப் புகுந்து ஈங்கு யான் உறைகின்றேன்","இந்திர கோடணை விழவு அணி வரு நாள்","வந்து தோன்றி இம் மா நகர் மருங்கே"],"p":"","r":"When the sage, himself left to wasting hunger, had gone, the vidyadhara who had fled in fear of her rash act came back grieving, told her she suffered without cause at the hands of a terrible ascetic and bade her rise into the sky, but she had forgotten the mantra; the fruits, roots and vegetables he fetched could not satisfy her, and, unable to leave her, he told her kindly of a strong city in the Tamil land of Jambudvipa where unshaken rich men help those who cannot help themselves and ascetics dwell, and bade her go there by land, so she came to this city.","n":"Her husband calls the curse causeless (காரணம் இன்றியும், 54) where she has just named her own deed as its cause: the unit keeps both views without settling them. Puhar is introduced by its charity, ஆற்றா மாக்கட்கு ஆற்றும் துணை (64), which plays on the verb ஆற்று as Kural 225 does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","arul","eegai","saabam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 17, lines 49-70","lb":"MM17:49-70","ev":"The vidyadhara returns, finds she has lost her sky mantra and cannot be fed, and sends her on foot to Puhar, the Tamil city of givers.","cl":[["an ascetic's curse can fall on one who has given no cause","general","reported",54,"kaanchanan"]],"ag":["விருச்சிகன்","காஞ்சனன்","காயசண்டிகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:18"},"mm:17:71-82":{"book":"manimekalai","num":125,"t":["என் உறு பெரும் பசி கண்டனன் இரங்கி","பின் வரும் யாண்டு அவன் எண்ணினன் கழியும்","தணிவு இல் வெம் பசி தவிர்த்தனை வணங்கினேன்","மணிமேகலை! என் வான் பதிப் படர்கேன்","துக்கம் துடைக்கும் துகள் அறு மாதவர்","சக்கரவாளக் கோட்டம் உண்டு ஆங்கு அதில்","பலர் புகத் திறந்த பகு வாய் வாயில்","உலக அறவி ஒன்று உண்டு அதனிடை","ஊர்ஊர் ஆங்கண் உறு பசி உழந்தோர்","ஆரும் இன்மையின் அரும் பிணி உற்றோர்","இடுவோர்த் தேர்ந்து ஆங்கு இருப்போர் பலரால்","வடு வாழ் கூந்தல்! அதன்பால் போக' என்று"],"p":"","r":"On the day of Indra's festival the vidyadhara comes to the city, sees her hunger and pities her, counting the years to come; she bows to Manimekalai for removing her unabating hunger, says she will now go to her sky city, and tells her of the public hall in the Chakravala kottam of the stainless ascetics, with a wide gate open to all, where the hungry of every town and the sick who have no one wait, choosing their givers, and bids her go there.","n":"The hall is described from the side of the receivers: they wait and choose who will give (இடுவோர்த் தேர்ந்து, 81), not the givers choosing whom to feed. Line 72 is unclear (see source_issues).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi","noy","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 17, lines 71-82","lb":"MM17:71-82","ev":"Kayasandigai thanks Manimekalai, prepares to return to her sky city, and directs her to the public hall where the hungry and friendless sick wait.","cl":[],"ag":["காஞ்சனன்","காயசண்டிகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:18"},"mm:17:83-98":{"book":"manimekalai","num":126,"t":["ஆங்கு அவள் போகிய பின்னர் ஆய் இழை","ஓங்கிய வீதியின் ஒரு புடை ஒதுங்கி","வல முறை மும் முறை வந்தனை செய்து அவ்","உலக அறவியின் ஒரு தனி ஏறி","பதியோர் தம்மொடு பலர் தொழுது ஏத்தும்","முதியோள் கோட்டம் மும்மையின் வணங்கிக்","கந்து உடை நெடு நிலைக் காரணம் காட்டிய","தம் துணைப் பாவையைத் தான் தொழுது ஏத்தி","வெயில் சுட வெம்பிய வேய் கரி கானத்துக்","கருவி மா மழை தோன்றியதென்ன","பசி தின வருந்திய பைதல் மாக்கட்கு","அமுதசுரபியோடு ஆய் இழை தோன்றி","'ஆபுத்திரன் கை அமுதசுரபி இஃது","யாவரும் வருக ஏற்போர் தாம்!' என","ஊண் ஒலி அரவத்து ஒலி எழுந்தன்றே","யாணர்ப் பேர் ஊர் அம்பல மருங்கு என்"],"p":"","r":"When Kayasandigai has gone, Manimekalai steps aside in the high street, circles the public hall three times, enters it, worships the shrine of the old goddess whom the townsfolk praise and the pillar image that showed her the cause, and like a rain cloud over a sun-scorched bamboo forest appears with the Amudhasurabhi before the hunger-wasted, calling that this is Aputhiran's bowl and all who would receive should come, and the noise of eating rises by the hall of the rich city.","n":"The closing ஊண் ஒலி அரவத்து ஒலி (97) and யாணர் (98) are the words of Purananuru 173's feast at a patron's house; here the feast has no patron, and the giver names the bowl's former owner rather than herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 17, lines 83-98","lb":"MM17:83-98","ev":"Manimekalai worships at the public hall and, holding up Aputhiran's bowl, calls all the hungry to come, and they eat.","cl":[],"ag":["காயசண்டிகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:18"},"mm:18:1-16":{"book":"manimekalai","num":127,"t":["ஆங்கு அது கேட்டு ஆங்கு அரும் புண் அகவயின்","தீத் துறு செங் கோல் சென்று சுட்டாங்குக்","கொதித்த உள்ளமொடு குரம்பு கொண்டு ஏறி","விதுப்புறு நெஞ்சினள் வெய்து உயிர்த்துக் கலங்கித்","'தீர்ப்பல் இவ் அறம்!' என சித்திராபதி தான்","கூத்து இயல் மடந்தையர்க்கு எல்லாம் கூறும்","'கோவலன் இறந்த பின் கொடுந் துயர் எய்தி","மாதவி மாதவர் பள்ளியுள் அடைந்தது","நகுதக்கன்றே! நல் நெடும் பேர் ஊர்","இது தக்கு என்போர்க்கு எள் உரை ஆயது!","காதலன் வீய கடுந் துயர் எய்திப்","போதல்செய்யா உயிரொடு புலந்து","நளி இரும் பொய்கை ஆடுநர் போல","முளி எரிப் புகூஉம் முது குடிப் பிறந்த","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர் அல்லேம் பலர் தம்","கைத்தூண் வாழ்க்கைக் கடவியம் அன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Burning with anger at the news of Manimekalai's begging, Chithirapathi vows to end 'this dharma' and tells the dancing women that Madhavi's retreat to the monks' house after Kovalan's death has made the city laugh, since women of their house are not pathini wives of old families who, unable to outlive a husband, enter fire as if bathing in a pool, but live on the hands of many.","n":"Chithirapathi reuses Madhavi's own words from kaathai 2: 'staying alive though the lover died' (போதல்செய்யா உயிரொடு, 12) and the widow who enters fire as a pool (13-14). Madhavi used them to confess her shame; her mother uses them to argue that a courtesan owes no widow's grief at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","pathini","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 18, lines 1-16","lb":"MM18:1-16","ev":"Hearing that Manimekalai begs in the public hall, Chithirapathi resolves to end her daughter's and granddaughter's renunciation and addresses the dancing women.","cl":[["a courtesan, who lives on many men's gifts, owes none of the pathini's deaths or vows at her lover's death","general","reported",15,"chithirapathi"]],"ag":["சித்திராபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சித்திராபதி","கூத்து இயல் மடந்தையர்"],"ch":"mm:19"},"mm:18:17-36":{"book":"manimekalai","num":128,"t":["பாண் மகன் பட்டுழிப் படூஉம் பான்மை இல்","யாழ் இனம் போலும் இயல்பினம் அன்றியும்","நறுந் தாது உண்டு நயன் இல் காலை","வறும் பூத் துறக்கும் வண்டு போல்குவம்","வினை ஒழிகாலைத் திருவின் செல்வி","அனையேம் ஆகி ஆடவர்த் துறப்பேம்","தாபதக் கோலம் தாங்கினம் என்பது","யாவரும் நகூஉம் இயல்பினது அன்றே?","மாதவி ஈன்ற மணிமேகலை வல்லி","போது அவிழ் செவ்வி பொருந்துதல் விரும்பிய","உதயகுமரன் ஆம் உலகு ஆள் வண்டின்","சிதையா உள்ளம் செவ்விதின் அருந்தக்","கைக்கொண்டு ஆங்கு அவள் ஏந்திய கடிஞையைப்","பிச்சை மாக்கள் பிறர் கைக் காட்டி","மற்று அவன் தன்னால் மணிமேகலை தனைப்","பொன் தேர்க் கொண்டு போதேன் ஆகின்","சுடுமண் ஏற்றி அரங்கு சூழ் போகி","வடுவொடு வாழும் மடந்தையர் தம்மோர்","அனையேன் ஆகி அரங்கக் கூத்தியர்","மனைஅகம் புகாஅ மரபினன்' என்றே"],"p":"","r":"Chithirapathi goes on that courtesans are not like a lute that dies with its bard but like bees that leave a drained flower, and like Fortune herself leave a man when his merit runs out, so that their taking an ascetic's dress is a joke; and she swears that unless she has Udayakumaran show Manimekalai's begging bowl to the beggars and carry her off in his golden car, she will be paraded round the stage carrying burnt brick like a disgraced dancer and never enter a dancer's house again.","n":"She names her own penalty in the form of a vow (the next unit calls it வஞ்சினம், 37), with a professional disgrace as the forfeit. Her picture of courtesans as bees on a drained flower (19-20) is the moralists' charge made from inside the profession, and offered as its rule.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","varaivinmagalir","thuravu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 18, lines 17-36","lb":"MM18:17-36","ev":"Chithirapathi swears before the dancing women that she will have the prince carry Manimekalai off or suffer a courtesan's public disgrace.","cl":[["a courtesan leaves a man when his merit is spent, as Fortune does","general","reported",21,"chithirapathi"],["an ascetic's dress on a courtesan is a thing for everyone to laugh at","general","reported",24,"chithirapathi"]],"ag":["சித்திராபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சித்திராபதி","கூத்து இயல் மடந்தையர்"],"ch":"mm:19"},"mm:18:37-54":{"book":"manimekalai","num":129,"t":["வஞ்சினம் சாற்றி நெஞ்சு புகையுயிர்த்து","வஞ்சக் கிளவி மாண்பொடு தேர்ந்து","செறி வளை நல்லார் சிலர் புறம் சூழக்","குறு வியர் பொடித்த கோல வாள் முகத்தள்","கடுந் தேர் வீதி காலில் போகி","இளங்கோ வேந்தன் இருப்பிடம் குறுகி","அரவ வண்டொடு தேன் இனம் ஆர்க்கும்","தரு மணல் ஞெமிரிய திரு நாறு ஒரு சிறைப்","பவழத் தூணத்து பசும் பொன் செஞ் சுவர்த்","திகழ் ஒளி நித்திலச் சித்திர விதானத்து","விளங்கு ஒளி பரந்த பளிங்கு செய் மண்டபத்து","துளங்கும் மான் ஊர்தித் தூ மலர்ப் பள்ளி","வெண் திரை விரிந்த வெண் நிறச் சாமரை","கொண்டு இரு மருங்கும் கோதையர் வீச","இருந்தோன் திருந்து அடி பொருந்தி நின்று ஏத்தித்","திருந்து எயிறு இலங்கச் செவ்வியின் நக்கு அவன்","'மாதவி மணிமேகலையுடன் எய்திய","தாபதக் கோலம் தவறு இன்றோ?' என"],"p":"","r":"Having sworn her vow and chosen deceitful words, Chithirapathi walks through the chariot street with a few bangled women to the young prince, who lies in a crystal hall on a couch with women fanning him, bows at his feet, and when she smiles he asks whether the ascetic dress Madhavi took with Manimekalai is still unbroken.","n":"The splendour of the prince's hall (43-50) is set between the courtesan's sweat-beaded face and her lie: she comes on foot along a chariot road to a man who has not moved. The question at 53-54 is the prince's, sounding out the vow before she makes her offer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","arasu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 18, lines 37-54","lb":"MM18:37-54","ev":"Chithirapathi goes to Udayakumaran's palace, bows to him, and he asks after Madhavi's and Manimekalai's ascetic vow.","cl":[],"ag":["சித்திராபதி","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:19"},"mm:18:55-76":{"book":"manimekalai","num":130,"t":["'அரிது பெறு சிறப்பின் குருகு கருவுயிர்ப்ப","ஒரு தனி ஓங்கிய திரு மணிக் காஞ்சி","பாடல்சால் சிறப்பின் பரதத்து ஓங்கிய","நாடகம் விரும்ப நல் நலம் கவினிக்","காமர் செவ்விக் கடி மலர் அவிழ்ந்தது","உதயகுமரன் எனும் ஒரு வண்டு உணீஇய","விரைவொடு வந்தேன் வியன் பெரு மூதூர்ப்","பாழ்ம்ம் பறந்தலை அம்பலத்து ஆயது","வாழ்க நின் கண்ணி! வாய் வாள் வேந்து!' என","ஓங்கிய பௌவத்து உடைகலப் பட்டோன்","வான் புணை பெற்றென மற்று அவட்கு உரைப்போன்","\"மேவிய பளிங்கின் விருந்தின் பாவை இஃது","ஓவியச் செய்தி\" என்று ஒழிவேன் முன்னர்","காந்தள் அம் செங் கை தளை பிணி விடாஅ","ஏந்து இள வன முலை இறை நெரித்ததூஉம்","ஒத்து ஒளிர் பவளத்துள் ஒளி சிறந்த","முத்துக் கூர்த்தன்ன முள் எயிற்று அமுதம்","அருந்த ஏமாந்த ஆர் உயிர் தளிர்ப்ப","விருந்தின் மூரல் அரும்பியதூஉம்","மா இதழ்க் குவளை மலர் புறத்து ஓட்டிக்","காய் வேல் வென்ற கருங் கயல் நெடுங் கண்","\"அறிவு பிறிதாகியது ஆய் இழை தனக்கு\" என"],"p":"","r":"Chithirapathi answers that the flower has opened for the bee called Udayakumaran and now stands in the ruined public hall, and the prince, like a shipwrecked man given a raft, tells her how he dismissed the girl in the crystal pavilion as a painted image until her hands, breasts, smile and eyes told him she had already guessed his mind.","n":"Lines 55-63 are Chithirapathi's answer and 64-76 the prince's reply; the opening image of the jewelled kanchi where the heron breeds and the flower blooms for Bharata's dance (55-59) is obscure, and the speaker division rests on the quotative என at 63 and 65.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 18, lines 55-76","lb":"MM18:55-76","ev":"Chithirapathi tells the prince that Manimekalai is in the public hall, and he recounts seeing her in the crystal pavilion.","cl":[],"ag":["சித்திராபதி","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","சித்திராபதி"],"ch":"mm:19"},"mm:18:77-89":{"book":"manimekalai","num":131,"t":["செவிஅகம் புகூஉச் சென்ற செவ்வியும்","பளிங்கு புறத்து எறிந்த பவளப் பாவை \"என்","உளம் கொண்டு ஒளித்தாள் உயிர்க் காப்பிட்டு\" என்று","இடை இருள் யாமத்து இருந்தேன் முன்னர்ப்","பொன் திகழ் மேனி ஒருத்தி தோன்றிச்","செங்கோல் காட்டிச் \"செய் தவம் புரிந்த","அங்கு அவள் தன் திறம் அயர்ப்பாய்\" என்றனள்","தெய்வம்கொல்லோ? திப்பியம்கொல்லோ?","எய்யா மையலேன் யான்! என்று அவன் சொலச்","சித்திராபதி தான் சிறு நகை எய்தி","'அத் திறம் விடுவாய் அரசு இளங் குருசில்!","காமக் கள்ளாட்டிடை மயக்குற்றன","தேவர்க்கு ஆயினும் சிலவோ செப்பின்?"],"p":"","r":"The prince ends that the coral doll in the crystal stole his heart, and that at midnight a golden woman appeared, showed him a sceptre and told him to forget the girl who has taken up austerity, so that he cannot tell whether it was a god or a vision; Chithirapathi laughs and tells him to let it go, since even gods are confused in the drunken play of desire.","n":"The goddess's warning in kaathai 7 was an argument about the sceptre, rain and the people's lives; the prince remembers only a sceptre shown and an order, and treats it as a riddle (84-85), which lets the courtesan dissolve it with a laugh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","mayal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 18, lines 77-89","lb":"MM18:77-89","ev":"Udayakumaran tells Chithirapathi of the goddess who warned him at midnight with a sceptre, and Chithirapathi laughs it off.","cl":[["desire confuses even the gods","general","reported",88,"chithirapathi"]],"ag":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சித்திராபதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","சித்திராபதி"],"ch":"mm:19"},"mm:18:90-97":{"book":"manimekalai","num":132,"t":["மாதவன் மடந்தைக்கு வருந்து துயர் எய்தி","ஆயிரம் செங் கண் அமரர் கோன் பெற்றதும்","மேருக் குன்றத்து ஊரும் நீர்ச் சரவணத்து","அருந் திறல் முனிவர்க்கு ஆர் அணங்கு ஆகிய","பெரும் பெயர்ப் பெண்டிர்பின்பு உளம் போக்கிய","அங்கி மனையாள் அவரவர் வடிவு ஆய்த்","தங்கா வேட்கை தனை அவண் தணித்ததூஉம்","கேட்டும் அறிதியோ வாள் திறல் குருசில்?"],"p":"","r":"Chithirapathi gives her examples: the king of the gods who got a thousand eyes for his longing after a sage's wife, and the fire god's wife who took the forms of the sages' wives at the lake on Meru to quench his desire for them.","n":"She draws on stories of gods to make the prince's desire seem natural; the second story turns on the wife of Agni (அங்கி மனையாள்) assuming other women's forms, which anticipates Manimekalai taking another woman's form later in the kaathai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 18, lines 90-97","lb":"MM18:90-97","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["சித்திராபதி","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:19"},"mm:18:98-123":{"book":"manimekalai","num":133,"t":["கன்னிக் காவலும் கடியின் காவலும்","தன் உறு கணவன் சாவுறின் காவலும்","நிறையின் காத்துப் பிறர் பிறர்க் காணாது","கொண்டோன் அல்லது தெய்வமும் பேணாப்","பெண்டிர் தம் குடியில் பிறந்தாள் அல்லள்","நாடவர் காண நல் அரங்கு ஏறி","ஆடலும் பாடலும் அழகும் காட்டி","சுருப்பு நாண் கருப்பு வில் அருப்புக் கணை தூவச்","செருக் கயல் நெடுங் கண் சுருக்கு வலைப் படுத்துக்","கண்டோர் நெஞ்சம் கொண்டு அகம் புக்குப்","பண் தேர் மொழியின் பயன் பல வாங்கி","வண்டின் துறக்கும் கொண்டி மகளிரைப்","பான்மையின் பிணித்துப் படிற்று உரை அடக்குதல்","கோன்முறை அன்றோ குமரற்கு?' என்றலும்","உதயகுமரன் உள்ளம் பிறழ்ந்து","விரை பரி நெடுந் தேர்மேல் சென்று ஏறி","ஆய் இழை இருந்த அம்பலம் எய்தி","காடு அமர் செல்வி கடிப் பசி களைய","ஓடு கைக்கொண்டு நின்று ஊட்டுநள் போலத்","தீப் பசி மாக்கட்குச் செழுஞ் சோறு ஈத்துப்","பாத்திரம் ஏந்திய பாவையைக் கண்டலும்","இடங்கழி காமமொடு அடங்காண் ஆகி","'உடம்போடு என் தன் உள்ளகம் புகுந்து என்","நெஞ்சம் கவர்ந்த வஞ்சக் கள்வி","நோற்றூண் வாழ்க்கையின் நொசி தவம் தாங்கி","ஏற்றூண் விரும்பிய காரணம் என்? என"],"p":"","r":"Chithirapathi argues that Manimekalai is not born of the families whose women guard themselves as maiden, wife and widow and worship no god but the man who took them, but of dancers who capture men with art and leave them like bees, so binding such a woman and silencing her false talk is a prince's right; inflamed, the prince drives to the hall, sees her feeding the hungry like the forest goddess feeding with a skull bowl, and asks himself why the thief of his heart has taken up begging.","n":"The definition of the chaste family at 101 (கொண்டோன் அல்லது தெய்வமும் பேணா) is the Kural 55 ideal in other words; here it serves to exclude Manimekalai from the protection chastity gives. The goddess's speech in kaathai 7 (7:1-14) had already told the prince the opposite of 'கோன்முறை' (111): that wronging a woman under austerity bends the sceptre. The work does not answer Chithirapathi in this unit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","karpu","nirai","kol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 18, lines 98-123","lb":"MM18:98-123","ev":"Chithirapathi urges the prince to seize Manimekalai as royal custom allows; he drives to the public hall and sees her feeding the hungry from her bowl.","cl":[["a woman of chaste family guards herself as maiden, wife and widow and worships no god but her husband","general","reported",101,"chithirapathi"],["it is royal custom for a prince to bind a courtesan and silence her false talk","general","reported",111,"chithirapathi"]],"ag":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சித்திராபதி","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:19"},"mm:18:124-142":{"book":"manimekalai","num":134,"t":["தானே தமியள் நின்றோள் முன்னர்","யானே கேட்டல் இயல்பு' எனச் சென்று","'நல்லாய்! என்கொல் நல் தவம் புரிந்தது?","சொல்லாய்' என்று துணிந்துடன் கேட்ப","'என் அமர் காதலன் இராகுலன் ஈங்கு இவன்","தன் அடி தொழுதலும் தகவு!' என வணங்கி","'அறைபோய் நெஞ்சம் அவன்பால் அணுகினும்","இறை வளை முன்கை ஈங்கு இவன் பற்றினும்","தொன்று காதலன் சொல் எதிர் மறுத்தல்","நன்றி அன்று!' என நடுங்கினள் மயங்கி","'கேட்டது மொழியேன் கேள்வியாளரின்","தோட்ட செவியை நீ ஆகுவை ஆம் எனின்","பிறத்தலும் மூத்தலும் பிணிப்பட்டு இரங்கலும்","இறத்தலும் உடையது இடும்பைக் கொள்கலம்","மக்கள் யாக்கை இது என உணர்ந்து","மிக்க நல் அறம் விரும்புதல் புரிந்தேன்","மண்டு அமர் முருக்கும் களிறு அனையார்க்கு","பெண்டிர் கூறும் பேர் அறிவு உண்டோ","கேட்டனை ஆயின் வேட்டது செய்க!' என"],"p":"","r":"The prince asks her why she has taken up austerity; Manimekalai, knowing him for her husband Rahula of a former birth, bows to him, admits her heart goes out to him, and, since it is wrong to refuse a former husband's word, answers that she has understood the human body to be a vessel of suffering that is born, ages, sickens and dies, and so chose the higher dharma, adds that warriors do not heed women's wisdom, and tells him to do as he likes.","n":"She keeps the former-birth rule (132-133) by answering exactly the question he asked, and makes the answer a refusal. Her words at 136-138 repeat Sudhamathi's lesson to the same prince in kaathai 4 (மக்கள் யாக்கை இது என உணர்ந்து). The jab at 140-141 expects the teaching to fail.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","idumbai","thuravu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 18, lines 124-142","lb":"MM18:124-142","ev":"Udayakumaran questions Manimekalai in the hall; she bows to him as her former husband and tells him why she renounced.","cl":[["it is not right to refuse the word of one's husband from a former birth","general","reported",132,"manimekalai"],["the human body is a vessel of suffering marked by birth, ageing, disease and death, and knowing this one should turn to dharma","buddhist","asserted",138,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:19"},"mm:18:143-156":{"book":"manimekalai","num":135,"t":["வாள் திறல் குருசிலை மடக்கொடி நீங்கி","முத்தை முதல்வி முதியாள் இருந்த","குச்சரக் குடிகை தன் அகம் புக்கு ஆங்கு","'ஆடவர் செய்தி அறிகுநர் யார்?' எனத்","தோடு அலர் கோதையைத் தொழுதனன் ஏத்தி","மாய விஞ்சை மந்திரம் ஓதிக்","காயசண்டிகை எனும் காரிகை வடிவு ஆய்","மணிமேகலை தான் வந்து தோன்ற","அணி மலர்த் தாரோன் அவள்பால் புக்குக்","குச்சரக் குடிகைக் குமரியை மரீஇப்","'பிச்சைப் பாத்திரம் பெரும் பசி உழந்த","காயசண்டிகை தன் கையில் காட்டி","மாயையின் ஒளித்த மணிமேகலை தனை","ஈங்கு இம் மண்ணீட்டு யார் என உணர்கேன்?"],"p":"","r":"Leaving the prince, Manimekalai enters the Gujarat-built shrine where the old first goddess sits, prays to her that none can know what men will do, recites the illusion-mantra and takes the form of Kaayasandigai; the prince comes in, approaches the goddess and demands to know how, with Kaayasandigai showing her begging bowl, he can tell which is Manimekalai hidden by illusion.","n":"The 'old one' (முதியாள், 144) and 'the maiden of the shrine' (குமரி, 152) are taken here as the shrine's goddess, whom the prince addresses as a clay image at 156; the unit does not name her, and her identity with the city goddess Champapati is not stated here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","maayai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 18, lines 143-156","lb":"MM18:143-156","ev":"Manimekalai hides in the old goddess's shrine and takes Kaayasandigai's form by a mantra; the prince follows and questions the goddess.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:19"},"mm:18:157-172":{"book":"manimekalai","num":136,"t":["ஆங்கு அவள் இவள் என்று அருளாய் ஆயிடின்","பல் நாள் ஆயினும் பாடுகிடப்பேன்!","இன்னும் கேளாய் இமையோர் பாவாய்!","பவளச் செவ் வாய்த் தவள வாள் நகையும்","அஞ்சனம் சேராச் செங் கயல் நெடுங் கணும்","முரிந்து கடை நெரிய வரிந்த சிலைப் புருவமும்","குவி முள் கருவியும் கோணமும் கூர் நுனைக்","கவை முள் கருவியும் ஆகிக் கடிகொள","கல்விப் பாகரின் காப்பு வலை ஓட்டி","வல் வாய் யாழின் மெல்லிதின் விளங்க","முதுக்குறை முதுமொழி எடுத்துக் காட்டிப்","புதுக் கோள் யானை வேட்டம் வாய்ந்தென","முதியாள்! உன் தன் கோட்டம் புகுந்த","மதி வாள் முகத்து மணிமேகலை தனை","ஒழியப் போகேன் உன் அடி தொட்டேன்","இது குறை' என்றனன் இறைமகன் தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"The prince tells the goddess that unless she shows him which one is Manimekalai he will lie at her shrine for many days, describes how the girl's lips, eyes and brows worked on him like an elephant-keeper's goads while she quoted old wisdom to tame him as a newly caught elephant, and swears by the goddess's feet that he will not leave without her.","n":"He casts her teaching as a capture: her sayings (முதுக்குறை முதுமொழி, 167) are one more instrument of the elephant hunt, so the sermon on the body is heard as seduction. The same phrase returns in kaathai 20 when Kaanchanan watches her speak to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 18, lines 157-172","lb":"MM18:157-172","ev":"Udayakumaran vows at the goddess's feet to stay at the shrine until he is given Manimekalai.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","முதியாள்"],"ch":"mm:19"},"mm:19:1-28":{"book":"manimekalai","num":137,"t":["முதியாள் திருந்து அடி மும்மையின் வணங்கி","மது மலர்த் தாரோன் வஞ்சினம் கூற","'ஏடு அவிழ் தாரோய்! எம் கோமகள் முன்","நாடாது துணிந்து நா நல்கூர்ந்தனை' என","வித்தகர் இயற்றிய விளங்கிய கைவினைச்","சித்திரம் ஒன்று தெய்வம் கூறலும்","உதயகுமரன் உள்ளம் கலங்கி","பொதி அறைப் பட்டோர் போன்று மெய் வருந்தி","\"அங்கு அவள் தன் திறம் அயர்ப்பாய்\" என்றே","செங்கோல் காட்டிய தெய்வமும் திப்பியம்","பை அரவு அல்குல் பலர் பசி களையக்","கையில் ஏந்திய பாத்திரம் திப்பியம்","\"முத்தை முதல்வி அடி பிழைத்தாய்\" எனச்","சித்திரம் உரைத்த இதூஉம் திப்பியம்","இந் நிலை எல்லாம் இளங்கொடி செய்தியின்","பின் அறிவாம்' எனப் பெயர்வோன் தன்னை","அகல் வாய் ஞாலம் ஆர் இருள் உண்ண","பகல் அரசு ஓட்டி பணை எழுந்து ஆர்ப்ப","மாலை நெற்றி வான் பிறைக் கோட்டு","நீல யானை மேலோர் இன்றிக்","காமர் செங் கை நீட்டி வண்டு படு","பூ நாறு கடாஅம் செருக்கி கால் கிளர்ந்து","நிறை அழி தோற்றமொடு தொடர முறைமையின்","நகர நம்பியர் வளையோர் தம்முடன்","மகர வீணையின் கிளை நரம்பு வடித்த","இளி புணர் இன் சீர் எஃகு உளம் கிழிப்பப்","பொறாஅ நெஞ்சில் புகை எரி பொத்தி","பறாஅக் குருகின் உயிர்த்து அவன் போய பின்"],"p":"","r":"After the prince bows three times at the old goddess's feet and swears his vow, a deity in a crafted image rebukes him for speaking rashly before 'our lady'; shaken, he counts three wonders (the deity who showed him the sceptre and told him to forget her, the bowl she carries to end many people's hunger, and the image's charge that he wronged the 'first lady' of old) and resolves to learn their meaning from the girl's later deeds, and he goes off as evening, pictured as a riderless musth elephant with the crescent for its tusk, hounds him and the city youths' lute music tears his heart.","n":"Whom the image means by எம் கோமகள் (3) and முத்தை முதல்வி (13) is not settled by this unit: the old goddess of the shrine, or Manimekalai as his wife of a former birth (kaathai 10 told her the prince was Rahulan). The prince does not yield; he defers (பின் அறிவாம், 16). Evening comes 'with an aspect that destroys restraint' (நிறை அழி தோற்றமொடு, 23), so the loss of nirai is cast as something that pursues him from outside.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anangu","nirai","vanjinam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 19, lines 1-28","lb":"MM19:1-28","ev":"Rebuked by a deity speaking from a crafted image, Udayakumaran, shaken, decides to learn more from the girl's deeds and leaves the shrine at nightfall in torment.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","சித்திரம்","நகர நம்பியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:20"},"mm:19:29-50":{"book":"manimekalai","num":138,"t":["உறையுள் குடிகை உள்வரிக் கொண்ட","மறு இல் செய்கை மணிமேகலை தான்","'மாதவி மகள் ஆய் மன்றம் திரிதரின்","காவலன் மகனோ கைவிடலீ யான்!'","காய்பசியாட்டி காயசண்டிகை என","ஊர் முழுது அறியும் உருவம் கொண்டே","ஆற்றா மாக்கட்கு ஆற்றும் துணை ஆகி","\"ஏற்றலும் இடுதலும் இரப்போர் கடன் அவர்","மேற்சென்று அளித்தல் விழுத்தகைத்து\" என்றே","நூற்பொருள் உணர்ந்தோர் நுனித்தனர் ஆம்' என","முதியாள் கோட்டத்து அகவயின் இருந்த","அமுதசுரபியை அங்கையின் வாங்கிப்","பதிஅகம் திரிதரும் பைந் தொடி நங்கை","அதிர் கழல் வேந்தன் அடி பிழைத்தாரை","ஒறுக்கும் தண்டத்து உறு சிறைக்கோட்டம்","விருப்பொடும் புகுந்து வெய்து உயிர்த்துப் புலம்பி","ஆங்குப் பசியுறும் ஆர் உயிர் மாக்களை","வாங்கு கைஅகம் வருந்த நின்று ஊட்டலும்","'ஊட்டிய பாத்திரம் ஒன்று' என வியந்து","கோட்டம் காவலர் 'கோமகன் தனக்கு இப்","பாத்திர தானமும் பைந்தொடி செய்தியும்","யாப்பு உடைத்தாக இசைத்தும்' என்று ஏகி"],"p":"","r":"Inside the shrine's chamber Manimekalai reasons that if she goes about the hall as Madhavi's daughter the prince will not let her be, so she takes the form of Kayachandigai, known to the whole city as a woman of burning hunger, citing the learned that for an alms-seeker taking and giving are both duty but going to the needy to give is the finer thing; she takes the Amudhasurabhi from the goddess's shrine, enters the king's prison for those who offended him, and with a sigh feeds the hungry there until her hand aches, and the guards, marvelling that one bowl fed them all, go to report the gift and the girl to the king.","n":"The disguise has two motives in one breath: to escape the prince (31-32) and to serve the helpless (35). Kayachandigai's known insatiable hunger makes the borrowed body a fitting carrier for a bowl that never empties. She enters the prison 'with desire' (விருப்பொடும், 44): the prison is chosen, not stumbled on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","eegai","iravu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 19, lines 29-50","lb":"MM19:29-50","ev":"Manimekalai takes Kayachandigai's form, carries the bowl into the king's prison and feeds the prisoners, and the guards go to tell the king.","cl":[["for one who lives on alms, both receiving and giving are duties, and going out to the needy to give is the higher act","general","asserted",36,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","கோட்டம் காவலர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:20"},"mm:19:51-66":{"book":"manimekalai","num":139,"t":["நெடியோன் குறள் உரு ஆகி நிமிர்ந்து தன்","அடியில் படியை அடக்கிய அந் நாள்","நீரின் பெய்த மூரி வார் சிலை","மாவலி மருமான் சீர் கெழு திரு மகள்","சீர்த்தி என்னும் திருத் தகு தேவியொடு","போது அவிழ் பூம்பொழில் புகுந்தனன் புக்குக்","கொம்பர்த் தும்பி குழல் இசை காட்டக்","பொங்கர் வண்டு இனம் நல் யாழ்செய்ய","வரிக் குயில் பாட மா மயில் ஆடும்","விரைப் பூம் பந்தர் கண்டு உளம் சிறந்தும்","புணர் துணை நீங்கிய பொய்கை அன்னமொடு","மட மயில் பேடையும் தோகையும் கூடி","இரு சிறைக் விரித்து ஆங்கு எழுந்து உடன் கொட்பன","ஒரு சிறைக் கண்டு ஆங்கு உள் மகிழ்வு எய்தி","'மாமணி வண்ணனும் தம்முனும் பிஞ்ஞையும்","ஆடிய குரவை இஃது ஆம்' என நோக்கியும்"],"p":"","r":"Meanwhile the king, with Queen Seerthi, daughter of the line of Mavali who poured the gift-water when the tall god took a dwarf's form and measured the earth, enters a flowering grove where bees, beetles, koels and peacocks make a concert, and seeing peahens, peacocks and a mateless swan whirl together he says it is the kuravai that the gem-coloured god, his elder brother and Pinnai danced.","n":"The queen's line is traced to Mavali, the king who lost the earth by his gift to the dwarf, while the king amuses himself; the grove scene runs alongside Manimekalai's prison feeding, which the guards are on their way to report.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam","vendhan","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 19, lines 51-66","lb":"MM19:51-66","ev":"The Chola king enters a pleasure grove with his queen and reads the birds' whirling as the divine kuravai.","cl":[],"ag":["அறை கழல் வேந்தன்","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:20"},"mm:19:67-78":{"book":"manimekalai","num":140,"t":["கோங்கு அலர் சேர்ந்த மாங்கனி தன்னைப்","பாங்குற இருந்த பல் பொறி மஞ்ஞையைச்","செம் பொன் தட்டில் தீம் பால் ஏந்திப்","பைங் கிளி ஊட்டும் ஓர் பாவை ஆம்' என்றும்","அணி மலர்ப் பூம்பொழில் அகவயின் இருந்த","பிணவுக் குரங்கு ஏற்றி பெரு மதர் மழைக் கண்","மடவோர்க்கு இயற்றிய மா மணி ஊசல்","கடுவன் ஊக்குவது கண்டு நகை எய்தியும்","பாசிலை செறிந்த பசுங் கால் கழையொடு","வால் வீ செறிந்த மராஅம் கண்டு","நெடியோன் முன்னொடு நின்றனன் ஆம் என","தொடி சேர் செங் கையின் தொழுது நின்று ஏத்தியும்"],"p":"","r":"The king goes on finding pictures in the grove: a peacock beside a mango and kongu blossom is a doll feeding milk from a gold plate to a parrot, a male monkey swinging a female on the jewelled swing made for women makes him laugh, and green bamboo standing by white-flowered maraam is the tall god standing with his elder brother, to which he joins bangled hands in worship.","n":"The bangled hand (தொடி சேர் செங் கை, 78) could be the queen's, but the run of participles (நோக்கியும், நகை எய்தியும், ஏத்தியும்) keeps the king as subject.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 19, lines 67-78","lb":"MM19:67-78","ev":"The king sees likenesses in the grove, laughs at the monkeys on the swing and worships a bamboo and maraam pair as Krishna and his brother.","cl":[],"ag":["அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:20"},"mm:19:79-92":{"book":"manimekalai","num":141,"t":["ஆடல் கூத்தினோடு அவிநயம் தெரிவோர்","நாடகக் காப்பிய நல் நூல் நுனிப்போர்","பண் யாழ் நரம்பில் பண்ணு முறை நிறுப்போர்","தண்ணுமைக் கருவிக் கண் எறி தெரிவோர்","குழலொடு கண்டம் கொளச் சீர் நிறுப்போர்","பழுநிய பாடல் பலரொடு மகிழ்வோர்","ஆரம் பரிந்த முத்தம் கோப்போர்","ஈரம் புலர்ந்த சாந்தம் திமிர்வோர்","குங்கும வருணம் கொங்கையின் இழைப்போர்","அம் செங்கழுநீர் ஆய் இதழ் பிணைப்போர்","நல் நெடுங் கூந்தல் நறு விரை குடைவோர்","பொன்னின் ஆடியில் பொருந்துபு நிற்போர்","ஆங்கு அவர் தம்மோடு அகல் இரு வானத்து","வேந்தனின் சென்று விளையாட்டு அயர்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"With women who know dance and gesture, who know the drama-poems, set the modes on the lute, beat the drum, keep time with flute and voice, string pearls, rub on sandal, paint saffron on their breasts, braid lilies, perfume their hair and stand before golden mirrors, the king plays like the king of the wide sky.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 19, lines 79-92","lb":"MM19:79-92","ev":"The king sports in the grove with women skilled in dance, music and adornment.","cl":[],"ag":["அவிநயம் தெரிவோர்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:20"},"mm:19:93-106":{"book":"manimekalai","num":142,"t":["குருந்தும் தளவும் திருந்து மலர்ச் செருந்தியும்","முருகு விரி முல்லையும் கருவிளம் பொங்கரும்","பொருந்துபு நின்று திருந்து நகை செய்து","குறுங் கால் நகுலமும் நெடுஞ் செவி முயலும்","பிறழ்ந்து பாய் மானும் இறும்பு அகலா வெறியும்","'வம்' எனக் கூஉய் மகிழ் துணையொடு தன்","செம்மலர்ச் செங் கை காட்டுபு நின்று","மன்னவன் தானும் மலர்க் கணை மைந்தனும்","இன் இளவேனிலும் இளங்கால் செல்வனும்","எந்திரக் கிணறும் இடும் கல் குன்றமும்","வந்து வீழ் அருவியும் மலர்ப் பூம் பந்தரும்","பரப்பு நீர்ப் பொய்கையும் கரப்பு நீர்க் கேணியும்","ஒளித்து உறை இடங்களும் பளிக்கறைப் பள்ளியும்","யாங்கணும் திரிந்து தாழ்ந்து விளையாடி"],"p":"","r":"Standing among kurunthu, jasmine and other shrubs, the king smiles and calls a mongoose, a hare, a leaping deer and a lurking creature to come, beckoning with his hand beside his beloved companion, and with Kama, spring and the young south wind as his fellows he wanders and plays among machine-wells, built hills, falls, ponds, hidden pools, hiding places and crystal chambers.","n":"Kama, spring and the breeze are named as the king's companions (100-101), so the king's play is drawn as a festival of desire at the hour his son is being turned back by a goddess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","vendhan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 19, lines 93-106","lb":"MM19:93-106","ev":"The king plays with animals and wanders through the grove's artificial wells, hills, pools and crystal rooms.","cl":[],"ag":["அறை கழல் வேந்தன்","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:20"},"mm:19:107-116":{"book":"manimekalai","num":143,"t":["மகத வினைஞரும் மராட்டக் கம்மரும்","அவந்திக் கொல்லரும் யவனத் தச்சரும்","தண் தமிழ் வினைஞ்அர் தம்மொடு கூடிக்","கொண்டு இனிது இயற்றிய கண் கவர் செய்வினைப்","பவளத் திரள் கால் பல் மணிப் போதிகைத்","தவள நித்திலத் தாமம் தாழ்ந்த","கோணச் சந்தி மாண் வினை விதானத்துத்","தமனியம் வேய்ந்த வகை பெறு வனப்பின்","பைஞ் சேறு மெழுகாப் பசும் பொன் மண்டபத்து","இந்திர திருவன் சென்று இனிது ஏறலும்"],"p":"","r":"The king, Indra-like, mounts a pavilion of green gold that Magadha artisans, Maratha smiths, Avanti metalworkers and Yavana carpenters made with Tamil craftsmen, with coral pillars, jewelled capitals, hanging strings of pearl and a gold-sheathed canopy.","n":"The list of foreign craftsmen working with Tamil ones (107-109) is a picture of Puhar's reach through trade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 19, lines 107-116","lb":"MM19:107-116","ev":"The king ascends the golden pavilion built by craftsmen from many lands.","cl":[],"ag":["அறை கழல் வேந்தன்","வினைஞர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:20"},"mm:19:117-138":{"book":"manimekalai","num":144,"t":["வாயிலுக்கு இசைத்து மன்னவன் அருளால்","சேய் நிலத்து அன்றியும் செவ்வியின் வணங்கி","எஞ்சா மண் நசை இகல் உளம் துரப்ப","வஞ்சியின் இருந்து வஞ்சி சூடி","முறம் செவி யானையும் தேரும் மாவும்","மறம் கெழு நெடு வாள் வயவரும் மிடைந்த","தலைத் தார்ச் சேனையொடு மலைத்துத் தலைவந்தோர்","சிலைக் கயல் நெடுங் கொடி செரு வேல் தடக் கை","ஆர் புனை தெரியல் இளங்கோன் தன்னால்","காரியாற்றுக் கொண்ட காவல் வெண்குடை","வலி கெழு தடக் கை மாவண்கிள்ளி!","ஒளியொடு வாழி ஊழிதோறு ஊழி!","வாழி எம் கோ மன்னவர் பெருந்தகை!","கேள் இது மன்னோ! கெடுக நின் பகைஞர்","யானைத்தீ நோய்க்கு அயர்ந்து மெய் வாடி இம்","மா நகர்த் திரியும் ஓர் வம்ப மாதர்","அருஞ் சிறைக்கோட்டத்து அகவயின் புகுந்து","பெரும் பெயர் மன்ன! நின் பெயர் வாழ்த்தி","ஐயப் பாத்திரம் ஒன்று கொண்டு ஆங்கு","மொய் கொள் மாக்கள் மொசிக்க ஊண் சுரந்தனள்","ஊழிதோறு ஊழி உலகம் காத்து","வாழி எம் கோ மன்னவ!' என்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"Announced at the gate and admitted by the king's grace, the prison guard bows and praises Maavankilli, whose protecting umbrella was won at Kaariyaaru by the young prince over the bow-and-fish banners that came out of Vanji wearing the vanji flower in hunger for land, and then reports that a stranger woman, wasted by the elephant-fire disease, entered the prison, blessed the king's name, and with one begging bowl made food flow for the crowding prisoners.","n":"The bow and fish of 124 are the Chera and Pandya emblems, so the enemies who came from Vanji appear to be their alliance; whether the young prince (இளங்கோன், 125) is Udayakumaran is not said. The guard frames the prisoners' feeding as a blessing on the king's name (134), before the king has done anything.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","pasi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 19, lines 117-138","lb":"MM19:117-138","ev":"The prison guard is admitted, praises the king's victory at Kaariyaaru and reports the stranger woman who feeds the prisoners from one bowl.","cl":[],"ag":["கோட்டம் காவலர்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோட்டம் காவலர்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"mm:20"},"mm:19:139-162":{"book":"manimekalai","num":145,"t":["'வருக வருக மடக்கொடி தான்' என்று","அருள் புரி நெஞ்சமொடு அரசன் கூறலின்","வாயிலாளரின் மடக்கொடி தான் சென்று","'ஆய் கழல் வேந்தன் அருள் வாழிய!' எனத்","'தாங்கு அருந் தன்மைத் தவத்தோய் நீ யார்?","யாங்கு ஆகியது இவ் ஏந்திய கடிஞை?' என்று","அரசன் கூறலும் ஆய் இழை உரைக்கும்","'விரைத் தார் வேந்தே! நீ நீடு வாழி!","விஞ்சை மகள் யான் விழவு அணி மூதூர்","வஞ்சம் திரிந்தேன் வாழிய பெருந்தகை!","வானம் வாய்க்க! மண் வளம் பெருகுக!","தீது இன்றாக கோமகற்கு! ஈங்கு ஈது","ஐயக் கடிஞை அம்பல மருங்கு ஓர்","தெய்வம் தந்தது திப்பியம் ஆயது","யானைத்தீ நோய் அரும் பசி கெடுத்தது","ஊன் உடை மாக்கட்கு உயிர் மருந்து இது' என","'யான் செயற்பாலது என் இளங்கொடிக்கு?' என்று","வேந்தன் கூற மெல் இயல் உரைக்கும்","'சிறையோர் கோட்டம் சீத்து அருள் நெஞ்சத்து","அறவோர்க்கு ஆக்குமது வாழியர்!' என","அருஞ் சிறை விட்டு ஆங்கு ஆய் இழை உரைத்த","பெருந் தவர் தம்மால் பெரும் பொருள் எய்த","கறையோர் இல்லாச் சிறையோர் கோட்டம்","அறவோர்க்கு ஆக்கினன் அரசு ஆள் வேந்து என்"],"p":"","r":"The king graciously summons her; she comes and blesses him, and asked who she is and how she came by the bowl, she says she is a vidyadhara woman who has wandered the festive old city in disguise, blesses rain, earth and the prince, and says a deity near the hall gave the bowl, which became divine, ended her elephant-fire hunger and is life-medicine for embodied beings; when the king asks what he should do for her, she asks him to clear the prison and make it a house for those who do dharma, and he releases the prisoners and makes the prison into such a house.","n":"Her self-account is true and false at once: she calls herself a vidyadhara woman (விஞ்சை மகள், 147), which is Kayachandigai's identity, while saying she has wandered the city in வஞ்சம் (148), which names her disguise. Her blessing 'no harm to the prince' (150) comes just before the prince is killed because of that disguise. The king does not punish or pardon one offender; he abolishes the prison as an institution at a beggar's request. Line 160 (through the great ascetics, great wealth gained) is compressed: whether the ascetics are given the building, or the king gains merit through them, is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","arul","sirai","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 19, lines 139-162","lb":"MM19:139-162","ev":"Manimekalai, in Kayachandigai's form, tells the king of the bowl and asks him to turn the prison into a house of dharma, and he frees the prisoners and does so.","cl":[["a king should clear his prison and give it over, with compassion, to those who practise dharma","general","asserted",157,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["அறை கழல் வேந்தன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:20"},"mm:20:1-18":{"book":"manimekalai","num":146,"t":["அரசன் ஆணையின் ஆய் இழை அருளால்","நிரயக் கொடுஞ் சிறை நீக்கிய கோட்டம்","தீப் பிறப்பு உழந்தோர் செய் வினைப் பயத்தான்","யாப்பு உடை நல் பிறப்பு எய்தினர் போலப்","பொருள் புரி நெஞ்சின் புலவோன் கோயிலும்","அருள் புரி நெஞ்சத்து அறவோர் பள்ளியும்","அட்டில் சாலையும் அருந்துநர் சாலையும்","கட்டு உடைச் செல்வக் களிப்பு உடைத்து ஆக","ஆய் இழை சென்றதூஉம் ஆங்கு அவள் தனக்கு","வீயா விழுச் சீர் வேந்தன் பணித்ததூஉம்","சிறையோர் கோட்டம் சீத்து அருள் நெஞ்சத்து","அறவோர் கோட்டம் ஆக்கிய வண்ணமும்","கேட்டனன் ஆகி 'அத் தோட்டு ஆர் குழலியை","மதியோர் எள்ளினும் மன்னவன் காயினும்","பொதியில் நீங்கிய பொழுதில் சென்று","பற்றினன் கொண்டு என் பொன் தேர் ஏற்றி","கற்று அறி விச்சையும் கேட்டு அவள் உரைக்கும்","முதுக்குறை முதுமொழி கேட்குவன்' என்றே"],"p":"","r":"The narrator recalls that the prison, freed of its hellish cruelty by the king's order and Manimekalai's compassion, has become a place of temple, monks' dwelling, kitchen and dining hall; hearing all this, Udayakumaran resolves that, whatever the wise say and however the king rages, he will seize her when she leaves the hall, set her in his golden chariot and hear her learning and her wise old sayings.","n":"The opening simile (3-4) compares the changed prison to those who, after suffering evil births, reach a good birth through the fruit of their deeds, so the chapter that ends on a killing paid for by deeds opens with deeds as a means of rising. The prince's resolve names the two checks he will override, the wise (மதியோர்) and the king (மன்னவன்), the very sceptre whose order made the hall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","aram","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 20, lines 1-18","lb":"MM20:1-18","ev":"Udayakumaran hears that Manimekalai has turned the prison into a house of charity by the king's leave and resolves to carry her off in his chariot.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:21"},"mm:20:19-32":{"book":"manimekalai","num":147,"t":["மதுக் கமழ் தாரோன் மனம் கொண்டு எழுந்து","பலர் பசி களைய பாவை தான் ஒதுங்கிய","உலக அறவியின் ஊடு சென்று ஏறலும்","'மழை சூழ் குடுமிப் பொதியில் குன்றத்துக்","கழை வளர் கான் யாற்று பழப் வினைப் பயத்தான்","மாதவன் மாதர்க்கு இட்ட சாபம்","ஈர் ஆறு ஆண்டு வந்தது வாராள்","காயசண்டிகை!' எனக் கையறவு எய்தி","காஞ்சனன் என்னும் அவள் தன் கணவன்","ஓங்கிய மூதூர் உள் வந்து இழிந்து","பூத சதுக்கமும் பூ மரச் சோலையும்","மாதவர் இடங்களும் மன்றமும் பொதியிலும்","தேர்ந்தனன் திரிவோன் ஏந்து இள வன முலை","மாந்தர் பசி நோய் மாற்றக் கண்டு ஆங்கு"],"p":"","r":"The prince sets out for the public alms-hall where Manimekalai feeds the hungry; meanwhile Kanchanan, the vidyadhara husband of Kayasandikai, despairing that the twelve years of the sage's curse are over and she has not returned, comes down into the old city, searches its squares, groves, hermitages and halls, and sees the girl relieving people's hunger.","n":"Kanchanan's lament (22-26) attributes the curse itself to the fruit of an old deed (பழ வினைப் பயத்தான், 23), so even before he acts the chapter frames his story in deeds. What he sees is Manimekalai in Kayasandikai's form, and the narration lets the reader know this while he does not.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pasi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 20, lines 19-32","lb":"MM20:19-32","ev":"Udayakumaran heads for the alms-hall while Kanchanan, come to fetch his wife at the end of her curse, searches Puhar and finds Manimekalai, in his wife's form, feeding the hungry.","cl":[["a sage's curse that falls on a person is the fruit of that person's old deed","general","reported",23,"kaanchanan"]],"ag":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","காஞ்சனன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:21"},"mm:20:33-48":{"book":"manimekalai","num":148,"t":["'இன்று நின் கையின் ஏந்திய பாத்திரம்","ஒன்றே ஆயினும் உண்போர் பலரால்","ஆனைத்தீ நோய் அரும் பசி களைய","வான வாழ்க்கையர் அருளினர்கொல்?' எனப்","பழைமைக் கட்டுரை பல பாராட்டவும்","விழையா உள்ளமொடு அவன்பால் நீங்கி","உதயகுமரன் தன்பால் சென்று","நரை மூதாட்டி ஒருத்தியைக் காட்டி","தண் அறல் வண்ணம் திரிந்து வேறாகி","வெண் மணல் ஆகிய கூந்தல் காணாய்","பிறை நுதல் வண்ணம் காணாயோ நீ","நரைமையின் திரை தோல் தகையின்று ஆயது","விறல் வில் புருவம் இவையும் காணாய்","இறவின் உணங்கல் போன்று வேறாயின","கழுநீர்க் கண் காண் வழுநீர் சுமந்தன","குமிழ் மூக்கு இவை காண் உமிழ் சீ ஒழுக்குவ"],"p":"","r":"Kanchanan greets her fondly, asking whether the gods have ended her elephant-fire hunger now that one bowl in her hand feeds many; unmoved, she leaves him, goes to Udayakumaran, points to a grey-haired old woman and begins to show him, feature by feature, how hair, brow, eyebrows, eyes and nose have turned from their praised likenesses into decay.","n":"Each feature is first named by its lover's simile (dark sand of the stream, crescent brow, bow, kazhuneer flower, kumizh bud) and then shown in the old woman as its opposite (white sand, wrinkled skin, dried prawn, running water, dripping matter), so the lesson is made out of the love poets' own catalogue. The one who gives it is the desired girl herself, disguised as another man's wife, and the unit opens with that husband's tenderness, which she leaves unanswered.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilamai","nilaiyaamai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 20, lines 33-48","lb":"MM20:33-48","ev":"Manimekalai turns away from Kanchanan, who takes her for his wife, and shows Udayakumaran an old woman's face as a lesson on the body.","cl":[["the beauty praised in a woman's features is changed by age into its opposite","general","asserted",41,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["காஞ்சனன்","மணிமேகலை","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:21"},"mm:20:49-69":{"book":"manimekalai","num":149,"t":["நிரை முத்து அனைய நகையும் காணாய்","சுரை வித்து ஏய்ப்பப் பிறழ்ந்து போயின","இலவு இதழ்ச் செவ் வாய் காணாயோ நீ","புலவுப் புண் போல் புலால் புறத்திடுவது","வள்ளைத் தாள் போல் வடி காது இவை காண்","உள் ஊன் வாடிய உணங்கல் போன்றன","இறும்பூது சான்ற முலையும் காணாய்","வெறும் பை போல வீழ்ந்து வேறாயின","தாழ்ந்து ஓசி தெங்கின் மடல் போல் திரங்கி","வீழ்ந்தன இள வேய்த் தோளும் காணாய்","நரம்பொடு விடு தோல் உகிர்த் தொடர் கழன்று","திரங்கிய விரல்கள் இவையும் காணாய்","வாழைத் தண்டே போன்ற குறங்கு இணை","தாழைத் தண்டின் உணங்கல் காணாய்","ஆவக் கணைக்கால் காணாயோ நீ","மேவிய நரம்போடு என்பு புறம் காட்டுவ","தளிர் அடி வண்ணம் காணாயோ நீ","முளி முதிர் தெங்கின் உதிர் காய் உணங்கல்","பூவினும் சாந்தினும் புலால் மறைத்து யாத்து","தூசினும் அணியினும் தொல்லோர் வகுத்த","வஞ்சம் தெரியாய் மன்னவன் மகன்!' என"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai carries the lesson down the old woman's body, teeth, lips, ears, breasts, shoulders, fingers, thighs, calves and feet, each now withered, and ends by telling the prince that he fails to see the deceit those of old set up when they hid the flesh and its smell under flowers, sandal paste, cloth and jewels.","n":"The closing charge (67-69) blames the deceit not on the woman but on custom, தொல்லோர் வகுத்த வஞ்சம், the dress and ornament handed down by those of old. Her teaching works by showing a living body in time rather than a corpse, and it is the prince's own seeing that she calls at fault (தெரியாய்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","vanjam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 20, lines 49-69","lb":"MM20:49-69","ev":"Manimekalai finishes showing the prince the old woman's decayed body and tells him its adornment is a deceit.","cl":[["the adorned body is flesh whose smell is hidden by flowers, paste, cloth and ornament, a deceit established by those of old","general","asserted",67,"manimekalai"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:21"},"mm:20:70-81":{"book":"manimekalai","num":150,"t":["விஞ்சை மகளாய் மெல் இயல் உரைத்தலும்","'தற்பாராட்டும் என் சொல் பயன் கொள்ளாள்","பிறன் பின் செல்லும் பிறன் போல் நோக்கும்","மதுக் கமழ் அலங்கல் மன்னவன் மகற்கு","முதுக்குறை முதுமொழி எடுத்துக் காட்டி","பவளக் கடிகையில் தவள வாள் நகையும்","குவளைச் செங் கணும் குறிப்பொடு வழாஅள்","ஈங்கு இவன் காதலன் ஆதலின் ஏந்து இழை","ஈங்கு ஒழிந்தனள்' என இகல் எரி பொத்தி","மற்றவள் இருந்த மன்றப் பொதியிலுள்","புற்று அடங்கு அரவின் புக்கு ஒளித்து அடங்கினன்","காஞ்சனன் என்னும் கதிர் வாள் விஞ்சையன்"],"p":"","r":"When she has spoken thus as the vidyadhara woman, Kanchanan, reading her refusal of his praise, her following of another man, her looking at him as a stranger and her wise talk and smiling eyes for the prince, concludes that the prince is her lover and the reason she stayed on earth, and burning with rage he slips into the hall where she lodges and hides like a snake in its anthill.","n":"Every sign Kanchanan reads is real (she does ignore him, does go to the prince, does speak to him with care) and every inference is false, because the woman is not his wife and her talk is a lesson against desire. The snake simile (80) begins an image the chapter repeats when he strikes (104-105).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","kurippu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 20, lines 70-81","lb":"MM20:70-81","ev":"Kanchanan decides that the prince is his wife's lover and hides in the hall in anger.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","காஞ்சனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["காஞ்சனன்",""],"ch":"mm:21"},"mm:20:82-103":{"book":"manimekalai","num":151,"t":["ஆங்கு அவள் உரைத்த அரசு இளங் குமரனும்","களையா வேட்கை கையுதிர்க்கொள்ளான்","'வளை சேர் செங் கை மணிமேகலையே","காயசண்டிகை ஆய் கடிஞை ஏந்தி","மாய விஞ்சையின் மனம் மயக்குறுத்தனள்","அம்பல மருங்கில் அயர்ந்து அறிவுரைத்த இவ்","வம்பலன் தன்னொடு இவ் வைகு இருள் ஒழியாள்","இங்கு இவள் செய்தி இடை இருள் யாமத்து","வந்து அறிகுவன்' என மனம் கொண்டு எழுந்து","வான்தேர்ப் பாகனைப் மீன் திகழ் கொடியனை","கருப்பு வில்லியை அருப்புக் கணை மைந்தனை","உயாவுத் துணையாக வயாவொடும் போகி","ஊர் துஞ்சு யாமத்து ஒரு தனி எழுந்து","வேழம் வேட்டு எழும் வெம் புலி போல","கோயில் கழிந்து வாயில் நீங்கி","ஆய் இழை இருந்த அம்பலம் அணைந்து","வேக வெந் தீ நாகம் கிடந்த","போகு உயர் புற்று அளை புகுவான் போல","ஆகம் தோய்ந்த சாந்து அலர் உறுத்த","ஊழ் அடியிட்டு அதன் உள்ளகம் புகுதலும்","ஆங்கு முன் இருந்த அலர் தார் விஞ்சையன்","'ஈங்கு இவன் வந்தனன் இவள்பால்' என்றே"],"p":"","r":"The prince, his desire unspent, tells himself that Manimekalai has taken Kayasandikai's form and bowl to bewitch minds and will not leave the stranger she lectured in the hall tonight, so he will come at midnight to learn what she does; with Kama as his companion he leaves the palace when the town sleeps, like a tiger stalking an elephant, and steps slowly into the hall as if entering a cobra's hole, while the vidyadhara waiting there says, 'here he has come to her'.","n":"The prince also misreads: he suspects her with the stranger (வம்பலன், 88), so each man takes the other for her lover. The narrator's two similes split his part: he goes as a tiger to prey (95) and enters as one going into a hole where a fiery serpent lies (98-99), the hunter already the prey.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 20, lines 82-103","lb":"MM20:82-103","ev":"Udayakumaran, suspecting Manimekalai of a night with a stranger, goes alone at midnight to the hall where Kanchanan lies in wait.","cl":[],"ag":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்","காஞ்சனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அரசு இளங் குமரன்",""],"ch":"mm:21"},"mm:20:104-119":{"book":"manimekalai","num":152,"t":["வெஞ் சின அரவம் நஞ்சு எயிறு அரும்பத்","தன் பெரு வெகுளியின் எழுந்து பை விரித்தென","இருந்தோன் எழுந்து பெரும் பின் சென்று அவன்","சுரும்பு அறை மணித் தோள் துணிய வீசி","'காயசண்டிகையைக் கைக்கொண்டு அந்தரம்","போகுவல்' என்றே அவள்பால் புகுதலும்","நெடு நிலைக் கந்தின் இடவயின் விளங்கக்","கடவுள் எழுதிய பாவை ஆங்கு உரைக்கும்","'அணுகல் அணுகல்! விஞ்சைக் காஞ்சன!","மணிமேகலை அவள் மறைந்து உரு எய்தினள்","காயசண்டிகை தன் கடும் பசி நீங்கி","வானம் போவழி வந்தது கேளாய்","அந்தரம் செல்வோர் அந்தரி இருந்த","விந்த மால் வரை மீமிசைப் போகார்","போவார் உளர்அனின் பொங்கிய சினத்தள்","சாயையின் வாங்கித் தன் வயிற்று இடூஉம்"],"p":"","r":"Rising like an enraged cobra with poison in its fangs, Kanchanan follows the prince and cuts through his shoulder with his sword, then moves toward the girl to carry 'Kayasandikai' into the sky, when the image of a goddess painted on a pillar of the hall speaks: 'Do not come near, Kanchana! She is Manimekalai in a hidden form; hear how Kayasandikai, freed of her hunger, went: those who fly through the sky do not pass over the Vindhya where the goddess dwells, and any who do are seized by their shadow and put in her belly.'","n":"The pillar image, which the narrator says a god drew (கடவுள் எழுதிய பாவை, 111), stops the second wrong before it happens and corrects the first too late. Kayasandikai, released by Manimekalai's food to fly home, was lost on the way, so the husband who came for her end of curse kills over a likeness of a wife who no longer lives.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","vekuli","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 20, lines 104-119","lb":"MM20:104-119","ev":"Kanchanan kills Udayakumaran with his sword and turns to take Manimekalai, but the pillar image tells him who she is and that his wife died over the Vindhya.","cl":[],"ag":["காஞ்சனன்","கடவுள் எழுதிய பாவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கடவுள் எழுதிய பாவை","காஞ்சனன்"],"ch":"mm:21"},"mm:20:120-129":{"book":"manimekalai","num":153,"t":["விந்தம் காக்கும் விந்தா கடிகை","அம் மலைமிசைப் போய் அவள் வயிற்று அடங்கினள்","கைம்மை கொள்ளேல் காஞ்சன! இது கேள்","ஊழ்வினை வந்து இங்கு உதயகுமரனை","ஆர் உயிர் உண்டதுஆயினும் அறியாய்","வெவ் வினை செய்தாய் விஞ்சைக் காஞ்சன!","அவ் வினை நின்னையும் அகலாது ஆங்கு உறும்'","என்று இவை தெய்வம் கூறலும் எழுந்து","கன்றிய நெஞ்சில் கடு வினை உருத்து எழ","விஞ்சையன் போயினன் விலங்கு விண் படர்ந்து என்"],"p":"","r":"The image finishes: Vindha, the guardian of the Vindhya, took Kayasandikai into her belly; do not grieve, Kanchana; though it was the prince's own ripened former deed that came here and took his life, you did not know that, and you have done a cruel deed that will not leave you either; and the vidyadhara, the hard deed rising in his scorched heart, goes off into the sky.","n":"The pillar gives two causes at once and keeps both: the prince died by his own ஊழ்வினை (123), and Kanchanan's killing is still his own வெவ் வினை (125) that will cling to him. That he 'did not know' (அறியாய்) excuses nothing; it is the reason he cannot claim to have been fate's instrument. கைம்மை கொள்ளேல் (122) is read here as 'do not take on mourning'; the word can also name rashness, and the line is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 20, lines 120-129","lb":"MM20:120-129","ev":"The pillar goddess tells Kanchanan that the prince died by his own former deed but that the killing will cling to him, and Kanchanan flies away.","cl":[["a man may die because his own former deed has ripened and come to take his life","general","asserted",123,"kanthirpaavai"],["one who kills does a cruel deed that will not leave him, even when the death was the victim's own ripened deed","general","asserted",125,"kanthirpaavai"]],"ag":["கடவுள் எழுதிய பாவை","காஞ்சனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கடவுள் எழுதிய பாவை","காஞ்சனன்"],"ch":"mm:21"},"mm:21:1-22":{"book":"manimekalai","num":154,"t":["கடவுள் எழுதிய நெடு நிலைக் கந்தின்","குடவயின் அமைத்த நெடு நிலை வாயில்","முதியாள் கோட்டத்து அகவயின் கிடந்த","மது மலர்க் குழலி மயங்கினள் எழுந்து","விஞ்சையன் செய்தியும் வென் வேல் வேந்தன்","மைந்தற்கு உற்றதும் மன்றப் பொதியில்","கந்து உடை நெடு நிலைக் கடவுள் பாவை","அங்கு அவற்கு உரைத்த அற்புதக் கிளவியும்","கேட்டனள் எழுந்து 'கெடுக இவ் உரு' என","தோட்டு அலர்க் குழலி உள்வரி நீங்கித்","'திட்டிவிடம் உண நின் உயிர் போம் நாள்","கட்டு அழல் ஈமத்து என் உயிர் சுட்டேன்","உவவன மருங்கில் நின்பால் உள்ளம்","தவிர்விலேன் ஆதலின் தலைமகள் தோன்றி","மணிபல்லவத்திடை என்னை ஆங்கு உய்த்து","பிணிப்பு அறு மாதவன் பீடிகை காட்டி","என் பிறப்பு உணர்ந்த என்முன் தோன்றி","உன் பிறப்பு எல்லாம் ஒழிவு இன்று உரைத்தலின்","பிறந்தோர் இறத்தலும் இறந்தோர் பிறத்தலும்","அறம் தரு சால்பும் மறம் தரு துன்பமும்","யான் நினக்கு உரைத்து நின் இடர் வினை ஒழிக்கக்","காயசண்டிகை வடிவு ஆனேன் காதல!"],"p":"","r":"Waking in Champapati's shrine beside the pillar carved with a god, Manimekalai learns what the vidyadhara did, what befell the prince and what the pillar image told the killer; crying 'perish this form', she casts off her borrowed shape and laments to the dead prince that she burned herself on his pyre in the birth when he died of the snake's poison glance, that the goddess carried her to Manipallavam where she learned her own and all his births, and that she took Kayachandigai's form only to teach him birth and death and the fruits of dharma and wrong and so end his evil karma.","n":"Her disguise is given a purpose here that the earlier kaathais did not state: it was a teaching form, meant to save him, and it became the cause of his death, since Kanchanan struck him for approaching 'Kayachandigai'. The pillar image's words to Kanchanan (kaathai 20, lines 110 to 126) are what she 'hears' at lines 5 to 8; the text does not say how a woman lying dazed heard them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","pirivu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 21, lines 1-22","lb":"MM21:1-22","ev":"Manimekalai wakes in Champapati's shrine, learns of the prince's killing, throws off Kayachandigai's form and laments over him.","cl":[["those who are born die and the dead are born again, and dharma brings worth as wrong brings suffering","general","asserted",19,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","அரசு இளங் குமரன்"],"ch":"mm:22"},"mm:21:23-34":{"book":"manimekalai","num":155,"t":["வை வாள் விஞ்சையன் மயக்கு உறு வெகுளியின்","வெவ் வினை உருப்ப விளிந்தனையோ!' என","விழுமக் கிளவியின் வெய்து உயிர்த்துப் புலம்பி","அழுதனள் ஏங்கி அயாஉயிர்த்து எழுதலும்","'செல்லல் செல்லல்! சேயரி நெடுங்கண்!","அல்லி அம் தாரோன் தன்பால் செல்லல்!","நினக்கு இவன் மகனாத் தோன்றியதூஉம்","மனக்கு இனியாற்கு நீ மகள் ஆயதூஉம்","பண்டும் பண்டும் பல் பிறப்பு உளவால்","கண்ட பிறவியே அல்ல காரிகை","தடுமாறு பிறவித் தாழ்தரு தோற்றம்","விடுமாறு முயல்வோய்! விழுமம் கொள்ளேல்!"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai asks whether he died by the deluded wrath of the vidyadhara's sword as his cruel karma ripened, weeps and rises to go to him; the pillar deity stops her, saying that he has been her son and she his daughter, in birth after birth, that the birth she has seen is not the only one, and that one striving to leave the low round of wandering births should not grieve.","n":"The deity's argument against grief is not that the dead are well but that kinship itself is unstable across births: the husband of one life was a son in another, so the bond she mourns is one turn among many. The unit's speaker changes at 27; the deity is named only at 35.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","pirivu","veedu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 21, lines 23-34","lb":"MM21:23-34","ev":"Manimekalai weeps and rises to go to the prince's body, and the pillar deity forbids her to go.","cl":[["the dead man's death was his own cruel karma coming to ripeness","general","asserted",24,"manimekalai"],["two beings meet in many births in changing relations, so the present relation is no ground for grief","general","asserted",31,"thuvathikan"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","துவதிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["துவதிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:22"},"mm:21:35-44":{"book":"manimekalai","num":156,"t":["என்று இவை சொல்லி, இருந் தெய்வம் உரைத்தலும்","பொன் திகழ் மேனிப் பூங்கொடி பொருந்திப்","'பொய்யா நாவொடு இப் பொதியிலில் பொருந்திய","தெய்வம் நீயோ? திருவடி தொழுதேன்","விட்ட பிறப்பின் வெய்து உயிர்த்து ஈங்கு இவன்","திட்டிவிடம் உணச் செல் உயிர் போயதும்","நெஞ்சு நடுங்கி நெடுந் துயர் கூர யான்","விஞ்சையன் வாளின் இவன் விளிந்ததூஉம்","அறிதலும் அறிதியோ? அறிந்தனை ஆயின்","பெறுவேன் தில்ல நின் பேர் அருள் ஈங்கு!' என"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai turns to the voice, asks whether it is the deity of unfailing tongue who dwells in this hall, worships its feet, and asks whether it knows why the prince died by the snake's poison glance in the birth left behind and now by the vidyadhara's sword, begging its grace to be told.","n":"Her question pairs two deaths across two births and asks for one cause; the deity's answer will give the second death a cause in the first life, not the snake.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","vazhipaadu","saakkaadu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 21, lines 35-44","lb":"MM21:35-44","ev":"Manimekalai worships the pillar deity and asks it why the prince died twice by violence.","cl":[],"ag":["துவதிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","துவதிகன்"],"ch":"mm:22"},"mm:21:45-60":{"book":"manimekalai","num":157,"t":["'ஐ அரி நெடுங் கண் ஆய் இழை! கேள்' எனத்","தெய்வக் கிளவியில் தெய்வம் கூறும்","'காயங்கரை எனும் பேர் யாற்று அடைகரை","மாயம் இல் மாதவன் வரு பொருள் உரைத்து","மருள் உடை மாக்கள் மன மாசு கழூஉம்","பிரமதருமனைப் பேணினிராகி","\"அடிசில் சிறப்பு யாம் அடிகளுக்கு ஆக்குதல்","விடியல் வேலை வேண்டினம்\" என்றலும்","மாலை நீங்க மனம் மகிழ்வு எய்தி","காலை தோன்ற வேலையின் வரூஉ","நடைத் திறத்து இழுக்கி நல் அடி தளர்ந்து","மடைக் கலம் சிதைய வீழ்ந்த மடையனை","சீலம் நீங்காச் செய் தவத்தோர்க்கு","வேலை பிழைத்த வெகுளி தோன்றத்","தோளும் தலையும் துணிந்து வேறாக","வாளின் தப்பிய வல் வினை அன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The deity answers that in the former birth, on the bank of the Kayangarai, she and Rahulan served the sage Brahmadharman and offered to cook him a special meal at dawn; when the cook, coming at daybreak, slipped and fell and broke the vessels, Rahulan, angry that the meal for the ascetic had failed of its hour, cut him through with the sword so that shoulder and head came apart: that was the cruel deed.","n":"The murder is set inside an act of piety: the anger rises because the gift to the sage is spoiled. The cook's fall is an accident (நடைத் திறத்து இழுக்கி), so the whole weight of the deed lies on the anger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","vekuli","kolai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 21, lines 45-60","lb":"MM21:45-60","ev":"The deity tells how Rahulan, in his former birth, killed with his sword a cook who stumbled and spoilt the meal prepared for Brahmadharman.","cl":[],"ag":["இலக்குமி","இராகுலன்","பிரமதருமன்","மடையன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["துவதிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:22"},"mm:21:61-72":{"book":"manimekalai","num":158,"t":["விரா மலர்க் கூந்தல் மெல் இயல் நின்னோடு","இராகுலன் தன்னை இட்டு அகலாதது","\"தலைவன் காக்கும் தம் பொருட்டு ஆகிய","அவல வெவ் வினை\" என்போர் அறியார்","அறம் செய் காதல் அன்பினின் ஆயினும்","மறம் செய்துளது எனின் வல் வினை ஒழியாது","ஆங்கு அவ் வினை வந்து அணுகும்காலைத்","தீங்கு உறும் உயிரே செய் வினை மருங்கின்","மீண்டுவரு பிறப்பின் மீளினும் மீளும்","ஆங்கு அவ் வினை காண் ஆய் இழை கணவனை","ஈங்கு வந்து இவ் இடர் செய்து ஒழிந்தது","இன்னும் கேளாய் இளங் கொடி நல்லாய்!"],"p":"","r":"The deity draws the lesson: those who say a grievous deed done for one's master is shielded by that master do not know; though done from love of doing dharma, if it is violence its strong karma does not leave, and when it draws near it returns on the life in a later birth; it is that deed which came here and did this harm to her husband.","n":"The deity names and rejects a defence, that the one served answers for a deed done in his service; intention to serve dharma does not change a killing's fruit. Lines 61 to 62 and 67 to 69 are compressed and the exact construction is unclear, though the sense of 66 and 70 to 71 is plain. Kaathai 20 has the same deity tell the killer Kanchanan that his deed too will not leave him (20:120-129): the prince's karma ripens through a new wrong that is itself accountable.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kolai","piravi","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 21, lines 61-72","lb":"MM21:61-72","ev":"","cl":[["a cruel deed done for the sake of the one served is not shielded by him","general","asserted",63,"thuvathikan"],["a violent deed keeps its strong karma even when done out of love of dharma","general","asserted",66,"thuvathikan"],["the karma of a deed returns on its doer in a later birth","general","asserted",69,"thuvathikan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["துவதிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:22"},"mm:21:73-86":{"book":"manimekalai","num":159,"t":["மன்னவன் மகற்கு வருந்து துயர் எய்தி","மாதவர் உணர்த்திய வாய்மொழி கேட்டுக்","காவலன் நின்னையும் காவல்செய்து ஆங்கு இடும்","இடு சிறை நீக்கி இராசமாதேவி","கூட வைக்கும் கொட்பினள் ஆகி","மாதவி மாதவன் மலர் அடி வணங்கித்","தீது கூற அவள் தன்னொடும் சேர்ந்து","மாதவன் உரைத்த வாய்மொழி கேட்டு","காதலி நின்னையும் காவல் நீக்குவள்","அரைசு ஆள் செல்வத்து ஆபுத்திரன்பால்","புரையோர்ப் பேணிப் போகலும் போகுவை","போனால் அவனொடும் பொருளுரை பொருந்தி","மாநீர் வங்கத்து அவனொடும் எழுந்து","மாயம் இல் செய்தி மணிபல்லவம் எனும்"],"p":"","r":"The deity foretells her near future: the king, grieving his son and hearing the ascetics' true report, will imprison her; the queen, set on keeping her near, will take her from the prison; Madhavi, bowing at the great sage's feet and telling him the trouble, will come with him, and on his word the queen will free her; she will go to Aputtiran, now ruling a kingdom, agree with him in true teaching and sail with him to the island of Manipallavam.","n":"The queen's motive is given in one word, கொட்பினள், which can mean scheming or set on a purpose; the following kaathais show her tormenting Manimekalai before she relents, which the prophecy passes over.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ethu","arasu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 21, lines 73-86","lb":"MM21:73-86","ev":"The deity foretells Manimekalai's imprisonment, her release by the queen at the sage's word, and her voyage with Aputtiran.","cl":[],"ag":["அறை கழல் வேந்தன்","நறு மலர்க் கோதை","மாதவி","அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை","ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["துவதிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:22"},"mm:21:87-111":{"book":"manimekalai","num":160,"t":["தீவகத்து இன்னும் சேறலும் உண்டால்","தீவதிலகையின் தன் திறம் கேட்டு","சாவக மன்னன் தன் நாடு அடைந்த பின்","ஆங்கு அத் தீவம் விட்டு அருந் தவன் வடிவு ஆய்","பூங் கொடி வஞ்சி மா நகர் புகுவை","ஆங்கு அந் நகரத்து அறி பொருள் வினாவும்","ஓங்கிய கேள்வி உயர்ந்தோர் பலரால்","\"இறைவன் எம் கோன் எவ் உயிர் அனைத்தும்","முறைமையின் படைத்த முதல்வன்\" என்போர்களும்","\"தன் உரு இல்லோன் பிற உருப் படைப்போன்","அன்னோன் இறைவன் ஆகும்\" என்போர்களும்","\"துன்ப நோன்பு இத் தொடர்ப்பாடு அறுத்து ஆங்கு","இன்ப உலகு உச்சி இருத்தும்\" என்போர்களும்","\"பூத விகாரப் புணர்ப்பு\" என்போர்களும்","பல் வேறு சமயப் படிற்று உரை எல்லாம்","அல்லி அம் கோதை! கேட்குறும் அந் நாள்","\"இறைவனும் இல்லை இறந்தோர் பிறவார்","அறனோடு என்னை?\" என்று அறைந்தோன் தன்னைப்","பிறவியும் அறவியும் பெற்றியின் உணர்ந்த","நறு மலர்க் கோதை! எள்ளினை நகுதி\"","எள்ளினை போலும் இவ் உரை கேட்டு! இங்கு","ஒள்ளியது உரை!\" என உன் பிறப்பு உணர்த்துவை","\"ஆங்கு நிற்கொணர்ந்த அருந் தெய்வம் மயக்க","காம்பு அன தோளி! கனா மயக்கு உற்றனை\"","என்று அவன் உரைக்கும் இளங் கொடி நல்லாய்!"],"p":"","r":"The deity goes on: after the Javaka king learns his own story from Deepatilakai and returns home, she will leave the island in an ascetic's form and enter Vanji, where learned men will set out the schools' false words: that God our lord made all lives in order, that the formless one who makes other forms is God, that painful austerity cuts this bondage and seats one at the top of the world of bliss, that all is a combining of the elements; and when one declares there is no God, the dead are not reborn and dharma is pointless, she who knows birth and dharma will laugh at him, tell him her former birth, and hear him say a deity deluded her and she was deceived by a dream.","n":"The four views at 94 to 100 are not named by school; the creator god may be the Brahma-vadin's and the formless maker the Saiva's of kaathai 27, the austerity that seats the soul at the top of the world reads as Jain, and the combining of elements is the materialist's. The prophecy calls them all படிற்று உரை, false talk, but answers only the materialist. Even his rejoinder is foretold: the epic anticipates the objection that knowledge of past births is a dream sent by a god, and the deity's own role in carrying her off makes it pointed. The two materialist claims are marked refuted because the work answers them: here Manimekalai's laugh and her account of her birth (106 to 108), and in kaathai 27 her argument that he knows even his own parents only by inference (27:284-289). The speakers of 107 to 110 are not marked and are read from the sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","iraivan","marumai","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 21, lines 87-111","lb":"MM21:87-111","ev":"The deity foretells Manimekalai's going to Vanji in an ascetic's form, hearing the schools, and mocking the materialist.","cl":[["God our lord created all living beings in due order","vedic","reported",94,"thuvathikan"],["the one who has no form of his own and makes the other forms is God","saiva","reported",96,"thuvathikan"],["painful austerity cuts the bond of deeds and seats the soul at the summit of the world of bliss","jain","reported",98,"thuvathikan"],["all things are combinations of the transformed elements","lokayata","reported",100,"thuvathikan"],["there is no God, the dead are not born again, and dharma serves nothing","lokayata","refuted",103,"bhuta_vaadhi"],["knowledge of a former birth is a dream-delusion sent by a deity","lokayata","refuted",109,"bhuta_vaadhi"]],"ag":["தீபதிலகை","ஆபுத்திரன்","மணிமேகலை","அறைந்தோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["துவதிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:22"},"mm:21:112-129":{"book":"manimekalai","num":161,"t":["\"அன்று\" என்று அவன் முன் அயர்ந்து ஒழிவாயலை","\"தீவினை உறுதலும் செத்தோர் பிறத்தலும்","வாயே\" என்று மயக்கு ஒழி மடவாய்","வழு அறு மரனும் மண்ணும் கல்லும்","எழுதிய பாவையும் பேசா என்பது","அறிதலும் அறிதியோ? அறியாய்கொல்லோ?","அறியாய் ஆயின் ஆங்கு அது கேளாய்!","முடித்து வரு சிறப்பின் மூதூர் யாங்கணும்","கொடித் தேர் வீதியும் தேவர் கோட்டமும்","முது மர இடங்களும் முது நீர்த் துறைகளும்","பொதியிலும் மன்றமும் பொருந்துபு நாடி","காப்பு உடை மா நகர்க் காவலும் கண்ணி","யாப்பு உடைத்தாக அறிந்தோர் வலித்து","மண்ணினும் கல்லினும் மரத்தினும் சுவரினும்","கண்ணிய தெய்வதம் காட்டுநர் வகுக்க","ஆங்கு அத் தெய்வதம் அவ் இடம் நீங்கா","ஊன் கண்ணினார்கட்கு உற்றதை உரைக்கும்","என் திறம் கேட்டியோ இளங் கொடி நல்லாய்!"],"p":"","r":"The deity tells her not to falter before the materialist but to affirm that evil deeds reach their doer and the dead are reborn; then it explains its own speech: wood, earth, stone and painted images do not speak, but the wise, planning the protection of a guarded city, have craftsmen shape the deities they intend in clay, stone, wood and wall at its streets, temples, old trees, ghats and halls, and those deities never leave their places and tell men of fleshly eyes what will befall.","n":"The deity concedes the sceptic's premise, that an image as material does not speak, and relocates the voice in a deity bound to the place by the city's founders; the image is a seat, not the speaker. The two halves of the unit go together: the materialist will call her knowledge a god's trick, and the deity's account of how gods speak through images is the apparatus he will deny.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","vazhipaadu","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 21, lines 112-129","lb":"MM21:112-129","ev":"","cl":[["evil deeds reach their doer and the dead are born again","general","asserted",113,"thuvathikan"],["wood, earth, stone and painted images do not themselves speak","general","asserted",115,"thuvathikan"],["deities installed by the wise in images at a city's places stay there and tell mortals what will befall","general","asserted",127,"thuvathikan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["துவதிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:22"},"mm:21:130-146":{"book":"manimekalai","num":162,"t":["மன் பெருந் தெய்வ கணங்களின் உள்ளேன்!","துவதிகன் என்பேன் தொன்று முதிர் கந்தின்","மயன் எனக்கு ஒப்பா வகுத்த பாவையின்","நீங்கேன் யான் என் நிலை அது கேளாய்","மாந்தர் அறிவது வானவர் அறியார்","ஓவியச்சேனன் என் உறு துணைத் தோழன்","ஆவதை இந் நகர்க்கு ஆர் உரைத்தனரோ?","அவனுடன் யான் சென்று ஆடு இடம் எல்லாம்","உடன் உறைந்தார் போல் ஒழியாது எழுதி","பூவும் புகையும் பொருந்துவ கொணர்ந்து","நா நனி வருந்த என் நலம் பாராட்டலின்","மணிமேகலை! யான் வரு பொருள் எல்லாம்","துணிவுடன் உரைத்தேன் என் சொல் தேறு' என","\"தேறேன் அல்லேன் தெய்வக் கிளவிகள்","ஈறு கடைபோக எனக்கு அருள்?\" என்றலும்","துவதிகன் உரைக்கும்' சொல்லலும் சொல்லுவேன்","வருவது கேளாய் மடக் கொடி நல்லாய்!"],"p":"","r":"The deity names itself Thuvathikan, one of the great divine host, who never leaves the image Mayan carved in its likeness on the ancient pillar; it speaks of its close companion Oviyachenan, whom the city paints beside it wherever the two went to play, honouring it with flowers and incense, and bids Manimekalai trust its word; she says she does not doubt and asks for its divine words to the end, and it promises to tell what is to come.","n":"Line 134, 'what men know the gods do not know', and line 136, 'who told this city what he is?', are hard to place in the argument; they may mean that the townspeople's worship knows the deity better than it knows itself, but that is a guess. The closing quotation mark at 145 sits oddly, so whether 145 to 146 are narrator or deity is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","vaaimai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 21, lines 130-146","lb":"MM21:130-146","ev":"The pillar deity tells its name and its bond to the image, and Manimekalai asks it to tell her future to the end.","cl":[],"ag":["துவதிகன்","மயன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["துவதிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:22"},"mm:21:147-171":{"book":"manimekalai","num":163,"t":["மன் உயிர் நீங்க மழை வளம் கரந்து","பொன் எயில் காஞ்சி நகர் கவின் அழிய","ஆங்கு அது கேட்டே ஆர் உயிர் மருந்தாய்","ஈங்கு இம் முதியாள் இடவயின் வைத்த","தெய்வப் பாத்திரம் செவ்விதின் வாங்கித்","தையல்! நிற்பயந்தோர் தம்மொடு போகி","அறவணன் தானும் ஆங்கு உளன் ஆதலின்","செறி தொடி! காஞ்சி மா நகர் சேர்குவை","அறவணன் அருளால் ஆய் தொடி! அவ் ஊர்ப்","பிற வணம் ஒழிந்து நின் பெற்றியை ஆகி","வறன் ஓடு உலகில் மழைவளம் தரூஉம்","அறன் ஓடு ஏந்தி ஆர் உயிர் ஓம்புவை","ஆய் தொடிக்கு அவ் ஊர் அறனொடு தோன்றும்","ஏது நிகழ்ச்சி யாவும் பல உள","பிற அறம் உரைத்தோர் பெற்றிமை எல்லாம்","அறவணன் தனக்கு நீ உரைத்த அந் நாள்","தவமும் தருமமும் சார்பின் தோற்றமும்","பவம் அறு மார்க்கமும் பான்மையின் உரைத்து","\"மற இருள் இரிய மன் உயிர் ஏம் உற","அற வெயில் விரித்து ஆங்கு அளப்பு இல் இருத்தியொடு","புத்த ஞாயிறு தோன்றும்காறும்","செத்தும் பிறந்தும் செம்பொருள் காவா","இத் தலம் நீங்கேன் இளங்கொடி! யானும்","தாயரும் நீயும் தவறு இன்றுஆக","வாய்வதாக நின் மனப்பாட்டு அறம்!\" என"],"p":"","r":"Thuvathikan foretells that when rain is withheld and Kanchi of the golden walls loses its beauty as lives perish, she will take the divine vessel kept in this shrine as medicine for lives, go with her mothers to Kanchi where Aravanar also is, cast off her borrowed form there by his grace, and bearing the bowl of dharma that gives rain's plenty to a parched world protect its people; when she tells Aravanar the doctrines of the other schools, he will teach her austerity, dharma, dependent arising and the path that ends becoming, and vow that, dying and being born, he will guard the true doctrine and not leave that place until the Buddha-sun rises.","n":"The bowl is set against drought itself: அறன் ஓடு, 'the bowl of dharma', gives the plenty that rain would give (157 to 158). Aravanar's vow to keep dying and being born until the next Buddha appears is a vow to stay in the round of births for the teaching's sake, which the same kaathai otherwise urges Manimekalai to escape.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","eegai","mazhai","saarbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 21, lines 147-171","lb":"MM21:147-171","ev":"Thuvathikan foretells Manimekalai's journey to famine-struck Kanchi with the bowl, and Aravanar's teaching and vow there.","cl":[["Aravanar teaches austerity, dharma, dependent arising and the path that cuts off becoming","buddhist","asserted",163,"thuvathikan"],["a Buddha-sun will arise to drive off the darkness of wrong and keep living beings safe","buddhist","asserted",167,"aravanar"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","அறவண அடிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["துவதிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:22"},"mm:21:172-190":{"book":"manimekalai","num":164,"t":["ஆங்கு அவன் உரைத்தலும் அவன் மொழி பிழையாய்","பாங்கு இயல் நல் அறம் பலவும் செய்த பின்","கச்சி முற்றத்து நின் உயிர் கடைகொள","உத்தர மகதத்து உறு பிறப்பு எல்லாம்","ஆண் பிறப்பு ஆகி அருளறம் ஒழியாய்","மாண்பொடு தோன்றி மயக்கம் களைந்து","பிறர்க்கு அறம் அருளும் பெரியோன் தனக்குத்","தலைச்சாவகன் ஆய் சார்பு அறுத்து உய்தி","இன்னும் கேட்டியோ நல் நுதல் மடந்தை!","ஊங்கண் ஓங்கிய உரவோன் தன்னை","வாங்கு திரை எடுத்த மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","சாதுசக்கரற்கு ஆர் அமுது ஈத்தோய்!","ஈது நின் பிறப்பு என்பது தெளிந்தே","உவவன மருங்கில் நின்பால் தோன்றி","மணிபல்லவத்திடைக் கொணர்ந்தது கேள் என","துவதிகன் உரைத்தலும் துயர்க் கடல் நீங்கி","அவதி அறிந்த அணி இழை நல்லாள்","வலை ஒழி மஞ்ஞையின் மன மயக்கு ஒழிதலும்","உலகு துயில் எழுப்பினன் மலர் கதிரோன் என்"],"p":"","r":"Thuvathikan foretells that after many deeds of dharma her life will end in Kanchi, and that in every birth after it in northern Magadha she will be born a man, never leave the dharma of compassion, clear away delusion, and become chief disciple of the great one who gives dharma to others, cutting dependence and so being saved; it adds that the Manimekala goddess, who once lifted her ancestor from the waves, came to her in the grove and bore her to Manipallavam because she had fed the sage Sadhuchakkaran; hearing this, Manimekalai leaves her sea of grief like a peacock freed from a net, and the sun wakes the world.","n":"Release is placed several births away and in a male body: the epic's prophecy makes rebirth as a man a step before discipleship, which is the epic's view, stated without comment. The closing simile, a peacock freed from the net, answers the unit's opening grief; what frees her is knowing her births, not any change in the facts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","piravi","arul","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 21, lines 172-190","lb":"MM21:172-190","ev":"Thuvathikan foretells Manimekalai's future births and release and explains why the goddess saved her, and Manimekalai's grief leaves her at dawn.","cl":[["one who keeps the dharma of compassion through births, born a man, becomes the chief disciple of the Buddha and is freed by cutting dependence","buddhist","asserted",179,"thuvathikan"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","துவதிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["துவதிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:22"},"mm:22:1-18":{"book":"manimekalai","num":165,"t":["கடவுள் மண்டிலம் கார் இருள் சீப்ப","நெடு நிலைக் கந்தில் நின்ற பாவையொடு","முதியோள் கோட்டம் வழிபடல் புரிந்தோர்","உதயகுமரற்கு உற்றதை உரைப்ப","சா துயர் கேட்டுச் சக்கரவாளத்து","மாதவர் எல்லாம் மணிமேகலை தனை","'இளங்கொடி! அறிவதும் உண்டோ இது-' என","துளங்காது ஆங்கு அவள் உற்றதை உரைத்தலும்","ஆங்கு அவள் தன்னை ஆர் உயிர் நீங்கிய","வேந்தன் சிறுவனொடு வேறு இடத்து ஒளித்து","மா பெருங் கோயில் வாயிலுக்கு இசைத்து","கோயில் மன்னனைக் குறுகினர் சென்று ஈங்கு","'உயர்ந்து ஓங்கு உச்சி உவா மதிபோல","நிவந்து ஓங்கு வெண்குடை மண்ணகம் நிழல் செய!","வேலும் கோலும் அருட்கண் விழிக்க!","தீது இன்று உருள்க நீ ஏந்திய திகிரி!","நினக்கு என வரைந்த ஆண்டுகள் எல்லாம்","மனக்கு இனிது ஆக வாழிய வேந்தே!"],"p":"","r":"At dawn the worshippers at the old woman's shrine tell the ascetics of the Chakravala shrine what happened to Udayakumaran; the ascetics ask Manimekalai, who tells them unshaken, and then, hiding her with the prince's body, they go to the palace, have themselves announced, and bless the king's umbrella, spear, sceptre, wheel and years.","n":"The ascetics come to the king with a death in which he is implicated, yet open only with blessing; the news itself is withheld for two hundred lines. Lines 11-12 (கோயில் மன்னனைக் குறுகினர் சென்று) use the formula with which Kannaki is brought before the Pandya in Silappathikaram 20.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 1-18","lb":"MM22:1-18","ev":"The ascetics of the Chakravala shrine learn of the prince's death, hear Manimekalai's account, hide her with the body, and go to the palace to bless the king.","cl":[],"ag":["வழிபடல் புரிந்தோர்","மாதவர்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:22:19-38":{"book":"manimekalai","num":166,"t":["இன்றே அல்ல இப் பதி மருங்கில்","கன்றிய காமக் கள்ளாட்டு அயர்ந்து","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர்பால் சென்று அணுகியும்","நல் தவப் பெண்டிர்பின் உளம் போக்கியும்","தீவினை உருப்ப உயிர் ஈறுசெய்தோர்","பார் ஆள் வேந்தே! பண்டும் பலரால்","\"மன் மருங்கு அறுத்த மழு வாள் நெடியோன்","தன் முன் தோன்றல் தகாது ஒழி நீ\" எனக்","கன்னி ஏவலின் காந்த மன்னவன்","\"இந் நகர் காப்போர் யார்?\" என நினைஇ","\"நாவல் அம் தண் பொழில் நண்ணார் நடுக்குறக்","காவல் கணிகை தனக்கு ஆம் காதலன்","இகழ்ந்தோர்க் காயினும் எஞ்சுதல் இல்லோன்","ககந்தன் ஆம்\" எனக் காதலின் கூஉய்","\"அரசு ஆள் உரிமை நின்பால் இன்மையின்","பரசுராமன் நின்பால் வந்து அணுகான்","அமர முனிவன் அகத்தியன் தனாது","துயர் நீங்கு கிளவியின் யான் தோன்று அளவும்","ககந்தன் காத்தல்! காகந்தி\" என்றே","இயைந்த நாமம் இப் பதிக்கு இட்டு ஈங்கு"],"p":"","r":"One ascetic tells the king that not only today but of old men who, drunk with lust, approached chaste wives or sent their hearts after women of penance have died when their evil deed ripened, and begins with old Kakandi: King Kanthan, told by the virgin goddess to keep out of Parasurama's sight, called Kakandhan, his son by the city's courtesan, to guard the town, since one with no right to rule would not draw Parasurama, and the town was named Kakandi.","n":"The general claim is made before any example, and widened from the chaste wife to the woman of penance (நல் தவப் பெண்டிர், 22), which is where Udayakumaran stands. The Kanthan story explains the town's name and introduces Kakandhan, whose lack of a right to rule (33) is what protects him; who the virgin goddess (கன்னி, 27) is, the unit does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","piranil","vinai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 19-38","lb":"MM22:19-38","ev":"King Kanthan, to hide from Parasurama, leaves the city in the keeping of Kakandhan, his courtesan's son, and names it Kakandi.","cl":[["men who in lust approach chaste wives or set their hearts on women ascetics die when their evil deed ripens","general","asserted",23,"maadhavan"]],"ag":["காந்த மன்","ககந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:22:39-56":{"book":"manimekalai","num":167,"t":["உள்வரிக் கொண்டு அவ் உரவோன் பெயர் நாள்","தெள்ளு நீர்க் காவிரி ஆடினள் வரூஉம்","பார்ப்பனி மருதியை பாங்கோர் இன்மையின்","யாப்பறை என்றே எண்ணினன் ஆகி","காவிரி வாயிலில் ககந்தன் சிறுவன்","\"நீ வா\" என்ன நேர் இழை கலங்கி","\"மண் திணி ஞாலத்து மழை வளம் தரூஉம்","பெண்டிர் ஆயின் பிறர் நெஞ்சு புகாஅர்","புக்கேன் பிறன் உளம் புரி நூல் மார்பன்","முத் தீப் பேணும் முறை எனக்கு இல்\" என","மா துயர் எவ்வமொடு மனைஅகம் புகாஅள்","பூத சதுக்கம் புக்கனள் மயங்கிக்","\"கொண்டோர் பிழைத்த குற்றம் தான் இலேன்","கண்டோன் நெஞ்சில் கரப்பு எளிதாயினேன்","வான் தரு கற்பின் மனையறம் பட்டேன்","யான் செய் குற்றம் யான் அறிகில்லேன்","பொய்யினைகொல்லோ பூத சதுக்கத்துத்","தெய்வம் நீ\" எனச் சேயிழை அரற்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"Kakandhan's son, seeing the brahmin wife Maruthi returning alone from her Kaveri bath, calls her to him; she reasons that rain-bringing wives never enter another man's heart, so having entered his she is no longer fit to tend her husband's three fires, will not go home, and at the Bhuta's crossroads protests that she did no wrong against her husband, kept the household chastity that brings rain, and does not know her fault, and asks whether the god there is false.","n":"Maruthi condemns herself for something that happened only in the youth's mind (கண்டோன் நெஞ்சில் கரப்பு எளிதாயினேன், 52): the fault she cannot name is not an act but having been desirable, and the rule that excludes her from the fire rite is one she applies to herself before anyone accuses her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","karpu","mazhai","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 39-56","lb":"MM22:39-56","ev":"Kakandhan's son calls the brahmin wife Maruthi to him at the river; distraught, she goes not home but to the Bhuta's crossroads and accuses its god.","cl":[["wives whose word brings rain never enter another man's heart","general","reported",46,"maruthi"],["a wife who has entered another man's heart may no longer tend her husband's three sacred fires","vedic","reported",48,"maruthi"]],"ag":["மருதி","ககந்தன் சிறுவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மருதி","பூதம்"],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:22:57-79":{"book":"manimekalai","num":168,"t":["மா பெரும் பூதம் தோன்றி \"மடக்கொடி!","நீ கேள்\" என்றே நேர் இழைக்கு உரைக்கும்","\"தெய்வம் தொழா அள் கொழுநன் தொழுது எழுவாள்","பெய் எனப் பெய்யும் பெரு மழை\" என்ற அப்","பொய்யில் புலவன் பொருளுரை தேறாய்!","பிசியும் நொடியும் பிறர் வாய்க் கேட்டு","விசி பிணி முழவின் விழாக் கோள் விரும்பி","கடவுள் பேணல் கடவியை ஆகலின்","மடவரல்! ஏவ மழையும் பெய்யாது","நிறை உடைப் பெண்டிர் தம்மே போல","பிறர் நெஞ்சு சுடூஉம் பெற்றியும் இல்லை","ஆங்கு அவை ஒழிகுவை ஆயின் ஆய் இழை!","ஓங்கு இரு வானத்து மழையும் நின் மொழியது","பெட்டாங்கு ஒழுகும் பெண்டிரைப் போலக்","கட்டாது உன்னை என் கடுந் தொழில் பாசம்","மன் முறை எழு நாள் வைத்து அவன் வழூஉம்","பின்முறை அல்லது என் முறை இல்லை","ஈங்கு எழு நாளில் இளங்கொடி நின்பால்","வாங்கா நெஞ்சின் மயரியை வாளால்","ககந்தன் கேட்டு கடிதலும் உண்டு\" என","இகந்த பூதம் எடுத்து உரைசெய்தது அப்","பூதம் உரைத்த நாளால் ஆங்கு அவன்","தாதை வாளால் தடியவும் பட்டனன்"],"p":"","r":"The great Bhuta appears and tells Maruthi that she has not trusted the poet who never lies on the wife who worships her husband rather than a god, that her love of riddles, gossip and drum-loud festivals and her duty to worship gods have cost her the power to command rain and to scorch the hearts of others as steadfast women do, that if she gives these up the rain will obey her, that its noose will not bind her as it binds straying wives, and that its own justice comes only after the king's seven days, within which Kakandhan will cut down the youth; on the day it named, the youth is killed by his father's sword.","n":"The Bhuta does not blame her for the youth's desire; it blames her worship of gods, festivals and talk, taking the couplet's தெய்வம் தொழாஅள் literally. It also sets an order of justice: the king's punishment first, the spirit's only if the king fails within seven days (72-73), and the king does not fail.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","mazhai","vazhipaadu","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 57-79","lb":"MM22:57-79","ev":"The Bhuta appears to Maruthi, explains her lost power and foretells the youth's punishment; within the seven days his father kills him with the sword.","cl":[["a wife who rises worshipping her husband rather than a god can bid the rain fall","general","asserted",59,"kaaval_bhutam"],["a wife given to festivals, gossip and the worship of gods loses the power to command rain","general","asserted",65,"kaaval_bhutam"],["the spirit's punishment follows only when the king's justice has failed within seven days","general","asserted",73,"kaaval_bhutam"]],"ag":["பூதம்","ககந்தன்"],"ec":[["k:55","quote"]],"spk":["பூதம்","மருதி"],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:22:80-98":{"book":"manimekalai","num":169,"t":["இன்னும் கேளாய் இருங் கடல் உடுத்த","மண் ஆள் செவத்து மன்னவர் ஏறே!","தருமதத்தனும் தன் மாமன் மகள்","பெரு மதர் மழைக் கண் விசாகையும் பேணித்","தெய்வம் காட்டும் திப்பிய ஓவியக்","கைவினை கடந்த கண் கவர் வனப்பினர்","\"மைத்துனன் முறைமையால் யாழோர் மணவினைக்கு","ஒத்தனர்\" என்றே ஊர் முழுது அலர் எழ","புனையா ஓவியம் புறம் போந்தென்ன","மனைஅகம் நீங்கி வாள் நுதல் விசாகை","உலக அறவியினூடு சென்று ஏறி","\"இலகு ஒளிக் கந்தின் எழுதிய பாவாய்!","உலகர் பெரும் பழி ஒழிப்பாய் நீ\" என","\"மா நகருள்ளீர்! மழை தரும் இவள்\" என","நா உடைப் பாவை நங்கையை எடுத்தலும்","\"தெய்வம் காட்டித் தெளித்திலேன் ஆயின்","மையல் ஊரோ மன மாசு ஒழியாது","மைத்துனன் மனையாள் மறு பிறப்பு ஆகுவேன்","இப் பிறப்பு இவனொடும் கூடேன்\" என்றே"],"p":"","r":"The ascetic goes on: the cousins Dharmadattan and Visakai were so beautiful that the whole town said they were fit for a lovers' marriage; Visakai left her house, climbed into the Ulaka Aravi hall, asked the image painted on its pillar to clear the slander, and when the speaking image told the city that she is a woman who brings rain, vowed that, since only a god's word could clear the town's mind, she would be her cousin's wife in her next birth and not join him in this one.","n":"The image clears her by naming her a rain-bringer (மழை தரும் இவள், 93), the very power Maruthi lost; yet Visakai answers vindication with renunciation, as if the god's word cleared her name but not the occasion for talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","pathini","nonbu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 80-98","lb":"MM22:80-98","ev":"Slandered with her cousin, Visakai goes to the pillar image in the Ulaka Aravi, which proclaims her chaste, and vows not to marry him in this birth.","cl":[],"ag":["விசாகை","நா உடைப் பாவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:22:99-114":{"book":"manimekalai","num":170,"t":["நற்றாய் தனக்கு நல் திறம் சாற்றி","மற்று அவள் கன்னி மாடத்து அடைந்த பின்","தருமதத்தனும் தந்தையும் தாயரும்","பெரு நகர் தன்னைப் பிறகிட்டு ஏகி","\"தாழ்தரு துன்பம் தலையெடுத்தாய்\" என","நா உடைப் பாவையை நலம் பல ஏத்தி","மிக்கோர் உறையும் விழுப் பெருஞ் செல்வத்துத்","தக்கண மதுரை தான் சென்று அடைந்த பின்","தருமதத்தனும் \"தன் மாமன் மகள்","விரி தரு பூங் குழல் விசாகையை அல்லது","பெண்டிரைப் பேணேன் இப் பிறப்பு ஒழிக!\" எனக்","கொண்ட விரதம் தன்னுள் கூறி","வாணிக மரபின் வரு பொருள் ஈட்டி","நீள் நிதிச் செல்வன் ஆய் நீள் நில வேந்தனின்","எட்டிப் பூப் பெற்று இரு முப்பதிற்று யாண்டு","ஒட்டிய செல்வத்து உயர்ந்தோன் ஆயினன்"],"p":"","r":"Visakai tells her mother and shuts herself in the maidens' chamber; Dharmadattan and his parents leave the city praising the image and settle in southern Madurai, where he vows to cherish no woman but Visakai and to let this birth go, and grows so rich in trade that the king gives him the etti flower, until he has reached sixty.","n":"His vow mirrors hers from the other side: she refuses him in this birth, he refuses every other woman, so both give up this birth's marriage and keep the bond.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nonbu","selvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 99-114","lb":"MM22:99-114","ev":"Visakai enters the maidens' chamber; Dharmadattan leaves with his parents for southern Madurai, vows to love no other woman, and grows rich and honoured until old age.","cl":[],"ag":["விசாகை","தருமதத்தன்","தந்தையும் தாயரும்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:22:115-126":{"book":"manimekalai","num":171,"t":["அந்தணாளன் ஒருவன் சென்று \"ஈங்கு","என் செய்தனையோ இரு நிதிச் செல்வ?","'பத்தினி இல்லோர் பல அறம் செய்யினும்","புத்தேள் உலகம் புகாஅர்' என்பது","கேட்டும் அறிதியோ? கேட்டனைஆயின்","நீட்டித்திராது நின் நகர் அடைக!\" எனத்","தக்கண மதுரை தான் வறிது ஆக","இப் பதிப் புகுந்தனன் இரு நில வேந்தே!","மற்று அவன் இவ் ஊர் வந்தமை கேட்டு","பொன் தொடி விசாகையும் மனைப் புறம்போந்து","நல்லாள் நாணாள் பல்லோர் நாப்பண்","அல்லவை கடிந்த அவன்பால் சென்று"],"p":"","r":"A brahmin asks the rich Dharmadattan what he has done with his life, whether he has heard that those without a chaste wife do not enter the world of the gods however many good deeds they do, and bids him go home at once; he leaves Madurai and returns, and Visakai, hearing of it, comes out of her house and, unashamed before the crowd, goes to him.","n":"The brahmin's saying makes a wife a man's condition for heaven; the next unit has Visakai answer it, putting virtue alone in that place (137-138). The school tag follows the speaker, a brahmin quoting a maxim; the unit names no text.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","marumai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 115-126","lb":"MM22:115-126","ev":"A brahmin sends the aged Dharmadattan home to marry, and Visakai goes out before the crowd to meet him.","cl":[["men without a chaste wife do not reach the world of the gods however many good deeds they do","vedic","refuted",117,"anthanaalan"]],"ag":["அந்தணாளன்","விசாகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அந்தணாளன்","தருமதத்தன்"],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:22:127-142":{"book":"manimekalai","num":172,"t":["\"நம்முள் நாம் அறிந்திலம் நம்மை முன் நாள்","மம்மர் செய்த வனப்பு யாங்கு ஒளித்தன","ஆறு ஐந்து இரட்டி யாண்டு உனக்கு ஆயது என்","நாறு ஐங் கூந்தலும் நரை விராவுற்றன","இளமையும் காமமும் யாங்கு ஒளித்தனவோ?","உளன் இல்லாள! எனக்கு ஈங்கு உரையாய்","இப் பிறப்பு ஆயின் யான் நின் அடி அடையேன்","அப் பிறப்பு யான் நின் அடித்தொழில் கேட்குவன்","இளமையும் நில்லாது யாக்கையும் நில்லாது","வளவிய வான் பெருஞ் செல்வமும் நில்லா","புத்தேள் உலகம் புதல்வரும் தாரார்","மிக்க அறமே விழுத் துணை ஆவது","தானம் செய்' என தருமதத்தனும்","மாமன் மகள்பால் வான் பொருள் காட்டி","ஆங்கு அவன் அவளுடன் செய்த நல் அறம்","ஓங்கு இரு வானத்து மீனினும் பலவால்"],"p":"","r":"Visakai tells Dharmadattan that they never knew each other, asks where the beauty that bewildered them has hidden now that he is sixty and his hair is grey, says she will not come to his feet in this birth but will serve him in the next, reminds him that youth, body and wealth do not last and that sons do not give heaven, only great virtue being a true companion, and bids him give; he shows her his wealth, and the good they did together outnumbers the stars.","n":"She addresses him as a householder without a wife (உளன் இல்லாள, 132), granting the brahmin's premise only to answer it: not a wife and not sons but virtue is the companion, and the two do their charity together while keeping her vow apart. The narrator's count (more than the stars) endorses her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","eegai","aram","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 127-142","lb":"MM22:127-142","ev":"Visakai refuses marriage in this birth but turns the aged Dharmadattan to charity, which they practise together.","cl":[["youth, the body and wealth do not last","general","asserted",135,"visaakai"],["sons do not win heaven for a man; only great virtue is a lasting companion","general","asserted",138,"visaakai"]],"ag":["விசாகை","தருமதத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசாகை","தருமதத்தன்"],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:22:143-158":{"book":"manimekalai","num":173,"t":["குமரி மூத்த அக் கொடுங் குழை நல்லாள்","அமரன் அருளால் அகல் நகர் இடூஉம்","படு பழி நீங்கி பல்லோர் நாப்பண்","கொடி மிடை வீதியில் வருவோள் குழல்மேல்","மருதி பொருட்டால் மடிந்தோன் தம்முன்","கருகிய நெஞ்சினன் காமம் காழ்கொளச்","சுரி இரும் பித்தை சூழ்ந்து புறந் தாழ்ந்த","விரி பூ மாலை விரும்பினன் வாங்கி","\"தொல்லோர் கூறிய மணம் ஈது ஆம்\" என","எல் அவிழ் தாரோன் இடுவான் வேண்டி","மாலை வாங்க ஏறிய செங் கை","நீலக் குஞ்சி நீங்காது ஆகலின்","\"ஏறிய செங் கை இழிந்திலது இந்தக்","காரிகை பொருட்டு\" எனக் ககந்தன் கேட்டுக்","கடுஞ் சினம் திருகி மகன் துயர் நோக்கான்","மைந்தன் தன்னை வாளால் எறிந்தனன்"],"p":"","r":"As the aged virgin, cleared of slander by the god's grace, walks the bannered street, Kakandhan's elder son, whose brother died over Maruthi, burns with desire, takes the garland from her hair saying this is the marriage the ancients spoke of, and lifts it to wear; his raised hand will not come away from his hair, and Kakandhan, hearing it was for this woman, in fierce anger and without regard for his grief as a father, strikes his son with his sword.","n":"The second son repeats the first son's offence against a woman now old, and claims an ancient form of marriage for it; the miracle answers him before the father does. Kakandhan, himself only a keeper of the city with no right to rule, twice executes his own son, which is the example the ascetic is preparing for the king. Whose head the hand sticks to turns on குஞ்சி (154), usually a man's hair, and on what இடுவான் (152) takes as object; the reading here takes it as his own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","sengol","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 143-158","lb":"MM22:143-158","ev":"Kakandhan's elder son seizes the aged Visakai's garland as a token of marriage, his hand sticks fast, and his father kills him with the sword.","cl":[["taking a woman's garland and wearing it is the marriage the ancients described","general","refuted",151,"kakandhan_mootha_magan"]],"ag":["விசாகை","தம்முன்","ககந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:22:159-176":{"book":"manimekalai","num":174,"t":["ஊழிதோறு ஊழி உலகம் காத்து","வாழி எம் கோ மன்னவ! என்று","மாதவர் தம்முள் ஓர் மாதவன் கூறலும்","வீயா விழுச் சீர் வேந்தன் கேட்டனன்","\"இன்றே அல்ல\" என்று எடுத்து உரைத்து","நன்று அறி மாதவிர்! நலம் பல காட்டினிர்","இன்றும் உளதோ இவ் வினை? உரைம்' என","வென்றி நெடு வேல் வேந்தன் கேட்ப","'தீது இன்று ஆக செங்கோல் வேந்து!' என","மாதவர் தம்முள் ஓர் மாதவன் உரைக்கும்","'முடி பொருள் உணர்ந்தோர் முது நீர் உலகில்","கடியப் பட்டன ஐந்து உள அவற்றில்","கள்ளும் பொய்யும் களவும் கொலையும்","தள்ளாது ஆகும் காமம் \"தம்பால்","ஆங்கு அது கடிந்தோர் அல்லவை கடிந்தோர்\" என","நீங்கினர் அன்றே நிறை தவ மாக்கள்","நீங்கார் அன்றே நீள் நில வேந்தே!","தாங்கா நரகம் தன்னிடை உழப்போர்"],"p":"","r":"The ascetic closes with a blessing; the king, catching his 'not only today', asks whether such a deed has happened today too, and the ascetic answers that those who know the final truth name five things to be shunned, toddy, lying, theft, killing and lust, of which lust is hardest to throw off; ascetics renounce it as the shunning of all evil, and those who do not renounce it suffer in unbearable hell.","n":"The king himself asks for the present case, so the verdict that follows comes from his mouth. The five match the Buddhist precepts, but the unit attributes them only to those who know the final truth (முடி பொருள் உணர்ந்தோர், 169), so the claims are tagged general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naragam","thuravu","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 159-176","lb":"MM22:159-176","ev":"The king asks whether such a deed has happened today, and an ascetic begins his answer with the five things to be shunned.","cl":[["toddy, lying, theft, killing and lust are the five things to be shunned","general","asserted",171,"maadhavan"],["lust is hardest to throw off, and who shuns it has shunned all evil","general","asserted",173,"maadhavan"],["those who do not renounce lust suffer in unbearable hell","general","asserted",176,"maadhavan"]],"ag":["மாதவன்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:22:177-204":{"book":"manimekalai","num":175,"t":["சே அரி நெடுங் கண் சித்திராபதி மகள்","காதலன் உற்ற கடுந் துயர் பொறாஅள்","மாதவி மாதவர் பள்ளியுள் அடைந்தனள்","மற்று அவள் பெற்ற மணிமேகலை தான்","முற்றா முலையினள் முதிராக் கிளவியள்","\"செய்குவன் தவம்\" என சிற்றிலும் பேர் இலும்","ஐயம் கொண்டு உண்டு அம்பலம் அடைந்தனள்","ஆங்கு அவள் அவ் இயல்பினளே ஆயினும்","நீங்கான் அவளை நிழல் போல் யாங்கணும்","காரிகை பொருட்டால் காமம் காழ்கொள","ஆர் இருள் அஞ்சான் அம்பலம் அடைந்தனன்","காயசண்டிகை வடிவு ஆயினள் காரிகை","காயசண்டிகையும் ஆங்கு உளள் ஆதலின்","காயசண்டிகை தன் கணவன் ஆகிய","வாய் வாள் விஞ்சையன் ஒருவன் தோன்றி","\"ஈங்கு இவள் பொருட்டால் வந்தனன் இவன்\" என","ஆங்கு அவன் தீவினை உருத்தது ஆகலின்","மதி மருள் வெண்குடை மன்ன! நின் மகன்","உதயகுமரன் ஒழியானாக","ஆங்கு அவள் தன்னை அம்பலத்து ஏற்றி","ஓங்கு இருள் யாமத்து இவனை ஆங்கு உய்த்து","காயசண்டிகை தன் கணவன் ஆகிய","வாய் வாள் விஞ்சையன் தன்னையும் கூஉய்","\"விஞ்சை மகள்பால் இவன் வந்தனன்\" என","வஞ்ச விஞ்சையன் மனத்தையும் கலக்கி","ஆங்கு அவன் தன் கை வாளால் அம்பலத்து","ஈங்கு இவன் தன்னை எறிந்தது\" என்று ஏத்தி","மாதவர் தம்முள் ஓர் மாதவன் உரைத்தலும்"],"p":"","r":"The ascetic tells the king that Madhavi, unable to bear her lover's suffering, entered the monks' dwelling, that her young daughter Manimekalai, resolved on penance, begged from house to house and came to the hall, that the prince nevertheless followed her like a shadow and, fearless of the dark, came to the hall for her, where she had taken Kayachandikai's form; and that since his evil deed had ripened it brought her to the hall, led him there at midnight, called Kayachandikai's husband, deceived his mind with 'he came for the vidyadhara's wife', and cut the prince down by that man's sword.","n":"The grammatical subject of the killing (எறிந்தது, 203) is the ripened evil deed of 193, not the vidyadhara, whose hand is only its instrument; the ascetic thus tells the father his son's death without naming a human culprit. Whether one ascetic or several speak (161, 168, 204) the text leaves open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","kaamam","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 177-204","lb":"MM22:177-204","ev":"The ascetic tells the king how his son, pursuing Manimekalai to the hall by night, was killed by Kayachandikai's vidyadhara husband.","cl":[["the prince's own ripened evil deed brought about his death, using the vidyadhara's hand","general","asserted",193,"maadhavan"]],"ag":["மாதவி","மணிமேகலை","அரசு இளங் குமரன்","காஞ்சனன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவன்","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:22:205-215":{"book":"manimekalai","num":176,"t":["சோழிக ஏனாதி தன் முகம் நோக்கி","'யான் செயற்பாலது இளங்கோன் தன்னைத்","தான் செய்ததனால் தகவு இலன் விஞ்சையன்","மாதவர் நோன்பும் மடவார் கற்பும்","காவலன் காவல் இன்றுஎனின் இன்றால் ","\"மகனை முறைசெய்த மன்னவன் வழி ஓர்","துயர் வினையாளன் தோன்றினான்\" என்பது","வேந்தர் தம் செவி உறுவதன் முன்னம்","ஈங்கு இவன் தன்னையும் ஈமத்து ஏற்றி","கணிகை மகளையும் காவல் செய்க' என்றனன்","அணி கிளர் நெடு முடி அரசு ஆள் வேந்து என்"],"p":"","r":"The king turns to his general Chozhika Enathi and says that the vidyadhara did to the prince what the king himself should have done, that without a guardian's guarding there can be neither ascetics' vows nor women's chastity, and orders that before other kings hear that an evil-doer was born in the line of the king who did justice on his own son, the prince be put on the pyre and the courtesan's daughter be put under guard.","n":"The king takes the ascetics' examples as meant, but his justice is half concealment: the body is to be burned before the story reaches other kings, and the girl, whom the stories have shown blameless, is imprisoned. தகவு இலன் (207) may say the vidyadhara is not at fault or that he lacked the standing to act; either way he goes unpunished. The ancestor 'who did justice on his son' (210) is the Chola of the calf whom Kannaki cites in Silappathikaram 20.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","nonbu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 22, lines 205-215","lb":"MM22:205-215","ev":"The king declares the vidyadhara's killing of his son a just act, orders the body burned and Manimekalai put under guard.","cl":[["a king must punish his own son's wrong as he would any other's","general","reported",206,"chola_vendhan"],["without the guardian's protection there are no ascetics' vows and no women's chastity","general","asserted",209,"chola_vendhan"]],"ag":["அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறை கழல் வேந்தன்","சோழிக ஏனாதி"],"ch":"mm:23"},"mm:23:1-16":{"book":"manimekalai","num":177,"t":["மன்னவன் அருளால் வாசந்தவை எனும்","நல் நெடுங் கூந்தல் நரை மூதாட்டி","அரசற்கு ஆயினும் குமரற்கு ஆயினும்","திரு நிலக் கிழமைத் தேவியர்க்கு ஆயினும்","கட்டுரை விரித்தும் கற்றவை பகர்ந்தும்","பட்டவை துடைக்கும் பயம் கெழு மொழியினள்","இலங்கு அரி நெடுங் கண் இராசமாதேவி","கலங்கு அஞ்அர் ஒழியக் கடிது சென்று எய்தி","அழுது அடி வீழாது ஆய் இழை தன்னைத்","தொழுது முன் நின்று தோன்ற வாழ்த்தி","'கொற்றம் கொண்டு குடி புறங்காத்து","செற்றத் தெவ்வர் தேஎம் தமது ஆக்கியும்","தருப்பையில் கிடத்தி வாளில் போழ்ந்து","\"செருப் புகல் மன்னர் செல்வுழிச் செல்க\" என","மூத்து விளிதல் இக் குடிப் பிறந்தோர்க்கு","நாப் புடைபெயராது நாணுத் தகவுடைத்தே"],"p":"","r":"By the king's leave the old woman Vasanthavai, whose counsel soothes king, prince and queen alike, goes to the grieving queen Rajamadevi, bows to her without weeping at her feet, and reminds her that in this conquering line even a king who dies old is laid on darbha grass and cut with a sword so that he may go where battle-loving kings go, since a quiet death is a shame to the house.","n":"Her consolation is a rebuke: she does not weep with the queen (9, அழுது அடி வீழாது) and offers the warrior code's rite for a non-heroic death, the rite Purananuru 93 describes, as the measure by which the prince's death is to be judged.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 23, lines 1-16","lb":"MM23:1-16","ev":"Vasanthavai goes to the grieving queen, bows, and tells her of the rite by which kings of this line who die old are cut with a sword on darbha grass.","cl":[["for those born in the royal line, to die of old age without a wound is a shame","general","reported",15,"vaasanthavai"]],"ag":["வாசந்தவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["வாசந்தவை","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ch":"mm:24"},"mm:23:17-40":{"book":"manimekalai","num":178,"t":["தன் மண் காத்தன்று பிறர் மண் கொண்டன்று","என் எனப் படுமோ நின் மகன் மடிந்தது?","மன்பதை காக்கும் மன்னவன் தன் முன்","துன்பம் கொள்ளேல்' என்று அவள் போய பின்","கையாற்று உள்ளம் கரந்து அகத்து அடக்கி","பொய்யாற்று ஒழுக்கம் கொண்டு புறம் மறைத்து","'வஞ்சம் செய்குவன் மணிமேகலையை' என்று","அம் சில் ஓதி அரசனுக்கு ஒரு நாள்","'பிறர் பின் செல்லாப் பிக்குணிக் கோலத்து","அறிவு திரிந்தோன் அரசியல் தான் இலன்","கரும்பு உடைத் தடக் கைக் காமன் கையற","அரும் பெறல் இளமை பெரும்பிறிதாக்கும்","அறிவு தலைப்பட்ட ஆய் இழை தனக்குச்","சிறை தக்கன்று செங்கோல் வேந்து!' எனச்","'சிறப்பின் பாலார் மக்கள் அல்லார்","மறப்பின் பாலார் மன்னர்க்கு' என்பது","அறிந்தனைஆயின் இவ் ஆய் இழை தன்னைச்","செறிந்த சிறை நோய் தீர்க்க' என்று இறை சொல","'என்னோடு இருப்பினும் இருக்க இவ் இளங்கொடி","தன் ஓடு எடுப்பினும் தகைக்குநர் இல்' என்று","அங்கு அவள் தனைக் கூஉய் அவள் தன்னோடு","கொங்கு அவிழ் குழலாள் கோயிலுள் புக்கு ஆங்கு","'அறிவு திரித்து இவ் அகல் நகர் எல்லாம்","எறிதரு கோலம் யான் செய்குவல்' என்றே"],"p":"","r":"Vasanthavai ends by asking what the prince's death can be called, since he died neither guarding his own land nor taking another's, and bids the queen not grieve before the king; the queen hides her grief, resolves to deceive Manimekalai, and tells the king that the prince had lost his wits and his kingly conduct while the girl who has risen to wisdom does not deserve prison, so the king lets her take Manimekalai into her palace, where the queen privately vows to turn the girl's mind and make her a spectacle for the city.","n":"The queen's plea for release is true in content and false in aim: the narrator frames it with her hidden grief and false conduct (21 to 23) and her private vow (39 to 40). Lines 31 to 34 quote a saying on whom kings should honour and whom set aside, and who speaks 31 to 36 is not plain: the queen may continue to 34 and the king answer at 35 to 36, or the king may speak from 31. The words கையாற்று and பொய்யாற்று ஒழுக்கம் (21 to 22) return in Manimekalai's teaching at 124 to 125.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","koodaozhukkam","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 23, lines 17-40","lb":"MM23:17-40","ev":"The queen, concealing her grief and intent, persuades the king to release Manimekalai from prison into her keeping and takes her into the palace.","cl":[["a prince's death is honourable only when he dies guarding his own land or seizing another's","general","reported",17,"vaasanthavai"],["imprisonment does not befit one who has turned to wisdom and given up youth's pleasures","general","reported",30,"arasi"]],"ag":["வாசந்தவை","நறு மலர்க் கோதை","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:24"},"mm:23:41-56":{"book":"manimekalai","num":179,"t":["மயல் பகை ஊட்ட மறு பிறப்பு உணர்ந்தாள்","அயர்ப்பது செய்யா அறிவினள் ஆகக்","கல்லா இளைஞன் ஒருவனைக் கூஉய்","'வல்லாங்குச் செய்து மணிமேகலை தன்","இணை வளர் இள முலை ஏந்து எழில் ஆகத்துப்","புணர் குறி செய்து \"பொருந்தினள்\" என்னும்","பான்மைக் கட்டுரை பலர்க்கு உரை' என்றே","காணம் பலவும் கைந் நிறை கொடுப்ப","ஆங்கு அவன் சென்று அவ் ஆய் இழை இருந்த","பாங்கில் ஒரு சிறைப்பாடு சென்று அணைதலும்","'தேவி வஞ்சம் இது' எனத் தெளிந்து","நா இயல் மந்திரம் நடுங்காது ஓதி","ஆண்மைக் கோலத்து ஆய் இழை இருப்ப","காணம் பெற்றோன் கடுந் துயர் எய்தி","'அரசர் உரிமை இல் ஆடவர் அணுகார்","நிரயக் கொடு மகள் நினைப்பு அறியேன்' என்று"],"p":"","r":"The queen feeds Manimekalai a drug meant to derange her, but having known her former birth she stays clear-minded; the queen then pays an unlettered youth a handful of coins to go to her, make marks of lovemaking on her body and tell everyone she has lain with him, and when he comes to her room Manimekalai, seeing the queen's trick, chants her mantra and sits in a man's form, so that the youth, finding a man in the royal women's quarters, flees in fear, calling the queen a woman fit for hell.","n":"The trick aims at the one thing an ascetic woman's standing rests on, report (46 to 47, பலர்க்கு உரை); the mantra of changing form given her in kaathai 10 answers it, and the hired youth, not the narrator, is the first to name the queen's cruelty (56).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","mayal","mandhiram","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 23, lines 41-56","lb":"MM23:41-56","ev":"The queen drugs Manimekalai and hires a youth to dishonour her; Manimekalai stays clear-minded and takes a man's form by a mantra, and the youth flees.","cl":[["one who has come to know her former birth is not deluded","buddhist","asserted",41,"narrator"]],"ag":["நறு மலர்க் கோதை","மணிமேகலை","கல்லா இளைஞன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:24"},"mm:23:57-71":{"book":"manimekalai","num":180,"t":["அகநகர் கைவிட்டு ஆங்கு அவன் போயபின்","'மகனை நோய் செய்தாளை வைப்பது என்?' என்று","'உய்யா நோயின் ஊண் ஒழிந்தனள்' என","பொய்ந் நோய் காட்டிப் புழுக்கறை அடைப்ப","ஊண் ஒழி மந்திரம் உடைமையின் அந்த","வாள் நுதல் மேனி வருந்தாது இருப்ப","ஐயென விம்மி ஆய் இழை நடுங்கி","செய் தவத்தாட்டியைச் சிறுமை செய்தேன்","என் மகற்கு உற்ற இடுக்கண் பொறாது","பொன் நேர் அனையாய்! பொறுக்க\" என்று அவள் தொழ","'நீலபதி தன் வயிற்றில் தோன்றிய","ஏலம் கமழ் தார் இராகுலன் தன்னை","அழற்கண் நாகம் ஆர் உயிர் உண்ண","விழித்தல் ஆற்றேன் என் உயிர் சுடு நாள்","யாங்கு இருந்து அழுதனை இளங்கோன் தனக்கு?"],"p":"","r":"Asking why she should keep the one who brought ruin on her son, the queen gives out that Manimekalai has stopped eating from an incurable illness and shuts her in a stifling cell, but the mantra that does away with food keeps her unharmed; the queen trembles, bows and begs forgiveness for wronging an ascetic out of grief for her son, and Manimekalai begins her answer by asking where the queen was weeping on the day the serpent with fiery eyes took the life of Rahulan, son of Neelapathi, and she herself, unable to bear it, burned her own life.","n":"The false illness (59, ஊண் ஒழிந்தனள்) is made literally true by the mantra (61, ஊண் ஒழி மந்திரம்) without harm, so the lie defeats itself. Manimekalai's question turns on the same life having died before: the queen did not weep for Rahulan, and weeps now for Udayakumaran, who is that Rahulan reborn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","mandhiram","piravi","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 23, lines 57-71","lb":"MM23:57-71","ev":"The queen starves Manimekalai in a closed cell under a pretended illness; the mantra keeps her well, and the queen repents and begs her forgiveness.","cl":[],"ag":["நறு மலர்க் கோதை","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:24"},"mm:23:72-85":{"book":"manimekalai","num":181,"t":["பூங்கொடி நல்லாய்! பொருந்தாது செய்தனை","உடற்கு அழுதனையோ? உயிர்க்கு அழுதனையோ?","உடற்கு அழுதனையேல் உன்மகன் தன்னை","எடுத்துப் புறங்காட்டு இட்டனர் யாரே?","உயிர்க்கு அழுதனையேல் உயிர் புகும் புக்கில்","செயப்பாட்டு வினையால் தெரிந்து உணர்வு அரியது","அவ் உயிர்க்கு அன்பினை ஆயின் ஆய் தொடி!","எவ் உயிர்க்கு ஆயினும் இரங்கல் வேண்டும்","மற்று உன் மகனை மாபெருந்தேவி","செற்ற கள்வன் செய்தது கேளாய்","மடைக் கலம் சிதைய வீழ்ந்த மடையனை","உடல் துணிசெய்து ஆங்கு உருத்து எழும் வல் வினை","நஞ்சு விழி அரவின் நல் உயிர் வாங்கி","விஞ்சையன் வாளால் வீட்டியது அன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai tells the queen she has done what is unfitting and asks whether she weeps for the body or the life: if for the body, who but the mourners cast her son into the burning ground, and if for the life, where it enters next is set by its deeds and hard to know, so that one who loves that life should pity every life; and she tells her that the thief who killed her son was his own violent deed, the cutting down of a cook who fell and broke the serving vessels, which rose in wrath, took his life once by a serpent and ended it now by the vidyadhara's sword.","n":"The 'thief' (கள்வன், 81) who killed the prince is the deed itself, not Kanchanan, whose sword is only its instrument (85); the deed is said to 'rise in wrath' (83, உருத்து எழும்), the verb of the Silappathikaram's moral of karma. Manimekalai speaks of a life (உயிர்) entering a dwelling (புக்கில், 76) by its deeds; the text puts the Buddhist teaching in these words without raising the question of a lasting self.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","uyir","yaakkai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 23, lines 72-85","lb":"MM23:72-85","ev":"Manimekalai answers the queen's grief and tells her that the prince's death was the fruit of his killing of a cook in a former birth.","cl":[["grief for the body is idle, since the mourners themselves carry it to the burning ground","buddhist","asserted",74,"manimekalai"],["where a life goes after death is fixed by its own deeds and is hard to know","buddhist","asserted",77,"manimekalai"],["one who loves a single life must show pity to every life","buddhist","asserted",79,"manimekalai"],["a violent deed of a former birth rises again and takes the doer's life, here twice, by serpent and by sword","buddhist","asserted",83,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ch":"mm:24"},"mm:23:86-97":{"book":"manimekalai","num":182,"t":["\"யாங்கு அறிந்தனையோ ஈங்கு இது நீ? எனின்","பூங் கொடி நல்லாய்! புகுந்தது இது என","மொய்ம் மலர்ப் பூம்பொழில் புக்கது முதலா","தெய்வக் கட்டுரை தெளிந்ததை ஈறா","உற்றதை எல்லாம் ஒழிவு இன்று உரைத்து","மற்றும் உரை செயும் மணிமேகலை தான்","'மயல் பகை ஊட்டினை மறு பிறப்பு உணர்ந்தேன்","அயர்ப்பதுசெய்யா அறிவினேன் ஆயினேன்","கல்லாக் கயவன் கார் இருள் தான் வர","நல்லாய்! ஆண் உரு நான் கொண்டிருந்தேன்","ஊண் ஒழி மந்திரம் உடைமையின் அன்றோ","மாண் இழை செய்த வஞ்சம் பிழைத்தது?"],"p":"","r":"Saying that if the queen asks how she knows this she will tell, Manimekalai recounts everything from entering the flowering grove to grasping the deity's words, and then says that because the queen's drug found her already knowing her former birth she was not deluded, that when the unlettered lout came in the dark she held a man's form, and that it was her mantra against hunger that made the queen's deceit fail.","n":"Lines 92 to 94 repeat lines 41 to 43 almost word for word, with the narrator's third person turned into her first: she tells the queen her own plot, so that the confession the queen made in 64 to 66 is met with full knowledge, not with discovery.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","mandhiram","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 23, lines 86-97","lb":"MM23:86-97","ev":"Manimekalai tells the queen her story from the grove onward and how each of the queen's tricks failed.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ch":"mm:24"},"mm:23:98-112":{"book":"manimekalai","num":183,"t":["அந்தரம் சேறலும் அயல் உருக் கோடலும்","சிந்தையில் கொண்டிலேன் சென்ற பிறவியில்","காதலற் பயந்தோய்! கடுந் துயர் களைந்து","தீது உறு வெவ் வினை தீர்ப்பதுபொருட்டால்","தையால்! உன் தன் தடுமாற்று அவலத்து","எய்யா மையல் தீர்ந்து இன் உரை கேளாய்","ஆள்பவர் கலக்குற மயங்கிய நல் நாட்டுக்","காருக மடந்தை கணவனும் கைவிட","ஈன்ற குழவியொடு தான் வேறாகி","மான்று ஓர் திசை போய் வரையாள் வாழ்வுழி","புதல்வன் தன்னை ஓர் புரி நூல் மார்பன்","பதியோர் அறியாப் பான்மையின் வளர்க்க","ஆங்கு அப் புதல்வன் அவள் திறம் அறியான்","தான் புணர்ந்து அறிந்து பின் தன் உயிர் நீத்ததும்","நீர் நசை வேட்கையின் நெடுங் கடம் உழலும்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai says she did not set her mind on flying off or changing her form to escape, but stayed so as to remove the grief of the queen who bore her beloved of the former birth and to end her evil deed, and bids her hear sweet words: in a land thrown into turmoil by its rulers, a weaver's wife abandoned by her husband and parted from her infant went off and lived as a woman without bounds, and the son, raised by a brahmin without the townsfolk knowing whose he was, lay with her unknowing and, learning the truth, gave up his life.","n":"The stories that follow (104 to 123) answer, one by one, to unchaste desire, killing, drink, falsehood and theft, which 126 to 129 gather as 'these' to be renounced; the text does not name the set. Whether 98 to 99 means she refrained from using her powers against the queen, or that she lacked them in her former birth, turns on where 'சென்ற பிறவியில்' attaches; காருக மடந்தை is read as a weaver's wife.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vinai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 23, lines 98-112","lb":"MM23:98-112","ev":"Manimekalai tells the queen of a son who unknowingly lay with his abandoned mother and gave up his life on learning it.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ch":"mm:24"},"mm:23:113-130":{"book":"manimekalai","num":184,"t":["சூல் முதிர் மட மான் வயிறு கிழித்து ஓடக்","கான வேட்டுவன் கடுங் கணை துரப்ப","மான் மறி விழுந்தது கண்டு மனம் மயங்கி","பயிர்க் குரல் கேட்டு அதன் பான்மையன் ஆகி","உயிர்ப்பொடு செங் கண் உகுத்த நீர் கண்டு","ஓட்டி எய்தோன் ஓர் உயிர் துறந்ததும்","கேட்டும் அறிதியோ வாள் தடங் கண்ணி","கடாஅ யானைமுன் கள் காமுற்றோர்","விடாஅது சென்று அதன் வெண் கோட்டு வீழ்வது","உண்ட கள்ளின் உறு செருக்கு ஆவது","கண்டும் அறிதியோ காரிகை நல்லாய்","பொய்யாற்று ஒழுக்கம் பொருள் எனக் கொண்டோர்","கையாற்று அவலம் கடந்ததும் உண்டோ?","'களவு ஏர் வாழ்க்கையர் உறூஉம் கடுந் துயர்","இள வேய்த் தோளாய்க்கு இது' என வேண்டா","மன் பேர் உலகத்து வாழ்வோர்க்கு இங்கு இவை","துன்பம் தருவன துறத்தல் வேண்டும்","கற்ற கல்வி அன்றால் காரிகை!"],"p":"","r":"She goes on: a forest hunter shot a doe big with young so that the fawn fell from her torn belly, and seeing the tears in her red eye he gave up his life; drunkards who go before a rutting elephant are gored on its white tusk, such is the pride of toddy; those who take the way of falsehood as their gain never pass beyond helpless grief; and the queen should not tell herself that such sufferings belong to thieves alone, for to all who live in this great world these bring sorrow and must be renounced, and learning that is merely learned is not learning.","n":"124 to 125 turn on the queen the very words the narrator used of her at 21 to 22 (கையாற்று, பொய்யாற்று ஒழுக்கம்): the general lesson is aimed at her own conduct. Line 130 runs into the next unit's definition of true learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","kal","vaaimai","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 23, lines 113-130","lb":"MM23:113-130","ev":"Manimekalai tells of a hunter who died of remorse at a dying doe's tears and of drunkards gored by an elephant, and urges the queen to renounce such ways.","cl":[["those who take falsehood as their way of gain never escape helpless grief","general","asserted",124,"manimekalai"],["the sufferings that follow these deeds are not for thieves alone; all who live in the world must renounce them","buddhist","asserted",128,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ch":"mm:24"},"mm:23:131-147":{"book":"manimekalai","num":185,"t":["செற்றம் செறுத்தோர் முற்ற உணர்ந்தோர்","மல்லல் மா ஞாலத்து வாழ்வோர் என்போர்","அல்லல் மாக்கட்கு இல்லது நிரப்புநர்","திருந்து ஏர் எல் வளை! செல் உலகு அறிந்தோர்","வருந்தி வந்தோர் அரும் பசி களைந்தோர்","துன்பம் அறுக்கும் துணி பொருள் உணர்ந்தோர்","மன்பதைக்கு எல்லாம் அன்பு ஒழியார்' என","ஞான நல் நீர் நன்கனம் தெளித்து","தேன் ஆர் ஓதி செவிமுதல் வார்த்து","மகன் துயர் நெருப்பா மனம் விறகு ஆக","அகம் சுடு வெந் தீ ஆய் இழை அவிப்ப","தேறு படு சில் நீர் போலத் தெளிந்து","மாறு கொண்டு ஓரா மனத்தினள் ஆகி","ஆங்கு அவள் தொழுதலும் ஆய் இழை பொறாஅள்","தான் தொழுது ஏத்தி 'தகுதி செய்திலை'","காதலற் பயந்தோய் அன்றியும் காவலன்","மாபெருந்தேவி' என்று எதிர் வணங்கினள் என்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai ends that the truly learned are those who have curbed hatred and understood fully, who supply what the suffering lack, who know the world to come, who end the hunger of those who come wearied, who grasp the firm truth that cuts off sorrow, and whose love for all creatures never ceases; so she pours the clear water of wisdom into the queen's ear and quenches the fire of grief that had her mind for fuel, and the queen, cleared like water settled by the clearing-nut and no longer hostile, bows to her, but Manimekalai cannot bear it, bows and praises her in return, saying she has done what is not fitting, for she is the mother of her beloved and the king's great queen.","n":"The queen's bow (144) would set the ascetic above the queen; Manimekalai's returned bow (147) restores the order of kinship from her former birth, so the reconciliation is completed in family terms as well as doctrinal ones. The grief-as-fire image (140 to 141) and the clearing-nut image (142) make the teaching a medicine rather than a refutation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arul","thelivu","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 23, lines 131-147","lb":"MM23:131-147","ev":"The queen's grief is quenched by Manimekalai's teaching; she bows to Manimekalai, who bows to her in return.","cl":[["true learning is curbing hatred, relieving the hunger and want of others, grasping the truth that ends sorrow and keeping love for all creatures","buddhist","asserted",131,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:24"},"mm:24:1-26":{"book":"manimekalai","num":186,"t":["மன்ன குமரனை வஞ்சம் புணர்த்த","தொல் முது கணிகை தன் சூழ்ச்சியில் போயவன்","விஞ்சையன் வாளின் விளிந்தோன் என்பது","நெஞ்சு நடுக்குறக் கேட்டு மெய் வருந்தி","மாதவி மகள் தனை வான் சிறை நீக்கக்","காவலன் தேவி கால்கீழ் வீழ்ந்து ஆங்கு","'அரவு ஏர் அல்குல் அருந் தவ மடவார்","உரவோற்கு அளித்த ஒருபத்து ஒருவரும்","ஆயிரம்கண்ணோன் அவிநயம் வழூஉக்கொள","மா இரு ஞாலத்துத் தோன்றிய ஐவரும்","ஆங்கு அவன் புதல்வனோடு அருந் தவன் முனிந்த","ஓங்கிய சிறப்பின் ஒருநூற்று நால்வரும்","திருக் கிளர் மணி முடித் தேவர் கோன் தன் முன்","உருப்பசி முனிந்த என் குலத்து ஒருத்தியும்","ஒன்று கடை நின்ற ஆறு இருபதின்மர் இத்","தோன்று படு மா நகர்த் தோன்றிய நாள் முதல்","யான் உறு துன்பம் யாவரும் பட்டிலர்","மாபெருந்தேவி! மாதர் யாரினும்","பூவிலை ஈத்தவன் பொன்றினன் என்று","மாதவி மாதவர் பள்ளியுள் அடைந்ததும்","பரந்து படு மனைதொறும் பாத்திரம் ஏந்தி","அரங்கக் கூத்தி சென்று ஐயம் கொண்டதும்","நகுதல் அல்லது நாடகக் கணிகையர்","தகுதி என்னார் தன்மை அன்மையின்","மன்னவன் மகனே அன்றியும் மாதரால்","இந் நகர் உறூஉம் இடுக்கணும் உண்டால்!"],"p":"","r":"Shaken to hear that the prince, drawn by her scheme, has died by the vidyadhara's sword, the old courtesan falls at the queen's feet to get Madhavi's daughter out of prison, and laments that of all the hundred and twenty-one heavenly dancers cursed down to earth since the city arose, her line included, none suffered as she has: when the man who paid the flower-price died, Madhavi went into the monks' abode and the stage dancer took a bowl from house to house, a thing people only laugh at in courtesans; and besides the prince, she warns, the city faces a calamity through a woman.","n":"The count adds up: eleven given to Indra, five born on earth for a slip in gesture, a hundred and four cursed with Indra's son, and Urvasi of her own line make the ஒன்று கடை நின்ற ஆறு இருபதின்மர் (6 x 20 + 1) of line 15. Chithirapathi measures disgrace by the courtesan line's own heavenly charter, not by any law of conduct; and her own scheme set the prince on the road to his death (lines 1-3), which she does not mention to the queen. Whose woman threatens the city (மாதரால், 25) is left open here; the story that follows is of Pilivalai, but lines 72-73 point at Manimekalai. The claim is answered by the queen at 77-82, who calls the courtesan's life itself base.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","iravu","saabam","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 24, lines 1-26","lb":"MM24:1-26","ev":"Hearing of the prince's death, Chithirapathi falls at the queen's feet to have Manimekalai released and laments Madhavi's turn to the begging bowl.","cl":[["taking the begging bowl is unfitting for a dancing courtesan and earns only ridicule","general","refuted",23,"chithirapathi"]],"ag":["சித்திராபதி","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சித்திராபதி","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ch":"mm:25"},"mm:24:27-51":{"book":"manimekalai","num":187,"t":["உம்பளம் தழீஇய உயர் மணல் நெடுங் கோட்டு","பொங்கு திரை உலாவும் புன்னை அம் கானல்","கிளர் மணி நெடுமுடிக்கிள்ளி முன்னா","இளவேனில் இறுப்ப இறும்பூது சான்ற","பூ நாறு சோலை யாரும் இல் ஒரு சிறை","தானே தமியள் ஒருத்தி தோன்ற","\"இன்னள் ஆர்கொல் ஈங்கு இவள்?\" என்று","மன்னவன் அறியான் மயக்கம் எய்தாக்","கண்ட கண்ணினும் கேட்ட செவியினும்","உண்ட வாயினும் உயிர்த்த மூக்கினும்","உற்று உணர் உடம்பினும் வெற்றிச் சிலைக் காமன்","மயிலையும் செயலையும் மாவும் குவளையும்","பயில் இதழ்க் கமலமும் பருவத்து அலர்ந்த","மலர் வாய் அம்பின் வாசம் கமழப்","பலர் புறங்கண்டோன் பணிந்து தொழில் கேட்ப","ஒரு மதி எல்லை கழிப்பினும் உரையாள்","பொரு அறு பூங்கொடி போயின அந் நாள்","\"யாங்கு ஒளித்தனள் அவ் இளங்கொடி!\" என்றே","வேந்தரை அட்டோன் மெல் இயல் தேர்வுழி","நிலத்தில் குளித்து நெடு விசும்பு ஏறி","சலத்தில் திரியும் ஓர் சாரணன் தோன்ற","மன்னவன் அவனை வணங்கி முன் நின்று","\"என் உயிர் அனையாள் ஈங்கு ஒளித்தாள் உளள்","அன்னாள் ஒருத்தியைக் கண்டிரோ அடிகள்?","சொல்லுமின்\" என்று தொழ அவன் உரைப்பான்"],"p":"","r":"Chithirapathi tells how the Chola Nedumudi Killi, in a lonely flowering grove of the seaside punnai shore in early spring, met a girl alone, was overcome by love through all five senses under Kama's flower-arrows, served her for a month without learning who she was, and, when she vanished, searched for her until a sky-walking sage appeared and the king bowed and asked him if he had seen her.","n":"The king who 'saw the backs of many' and 'slew kings' (41, 45) is made to bow and serve; the five senses of 35-37 (seen, heard, eaten, breathed, touched) are the same five gates the Buddhist sermon of 101-122 will count among the links of birth, though Chithirapathi tells it as a love story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pirivu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 24, lines 27-51","lb":"MM24:27-51","ev":"In Chithirapathi's story, King Killi loves an unknown girl in a seaside grove for a month, loses her, and asks a sky-walking sage where she is.","cl":[],"ag":["நெடுமுடிக்கிள்ளி","பீலிவளை","சாரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சித்திராபதி","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ch":"mm:25"},"mm:24:52-74":{"book":"manimekalai","num":188,"t":["\"கண்டிலேன் ஆயினும் காரிகை தன்னைப்","பண்டு அறிவுடையேன் பார்த்திப கேளாய்","நாக நாடு நடுக்கு இன்று ஆள்பவன்","வாகை வேலோன் வளைவணன் தேவி","வாசமயிலை வயிற்றுள் தோன்றிய","பீலிவளை என்போள் பிறந்த அந் நாள்","\"இரவி குலத்து ஒருவன் இணை முலை தோய","கருவொடு வரும்\" எனக் கணி எடுத்து உரைத்தனன்","ஆங்கு அப் புதல்வன் வரூஉம் அல்லது","பூங்கொடி வாராள் புலம்பல்! இது கேள்","தீவகச் சாந்தி செய்யா நாள் உன்","காவல் மா நகர் கடல் வயிறு புகூஉம்","மணிமேகலை தன் வாய்மொழியால் அது","தணியாது இந்திர சாபம் உண்டு ஆகலின்","ஆங்குப் பதி அழிதலும் ஈங்குப் பதி கெடுதலும்","வேந்தரை அட்டோய்! மெய் எனக் கொண்டு இக்","காசு இல் மா நகர் கடல் வயிறு புகாமல்","வாசவன் விழாக் கோள் மறவேல்\" என்று","மாதவன் போயின அந் நாள் தொட்டும் இக்","காவல் மா நகர் கலக்கு ஒழியாதால்","தன் பெயர் மடந்தை துயருறுமாயின்","மன் பெருந் தெய்வம் வருதலும் உண்டு என","அஞ்சினேன் அரசன் தேவி!' என்று ஏத்தி"],"p":"","r":"Chithirapathi gives the sage's answer: the girl is Pilivalai, daughter of the Naga king Valaivanan, of whom it was foretold at her birth that she would unite with one of the sun's line and return with child, so she will not come back except with that son; and on the day the island's peace rite is not kept, Puhar will enter the sea, which the Manimekala goddess's word cannot stop because Indra's curse stands, so the king must never forget Vasava's festival; since then, she says, the city has never been free of dread, and she fears the great goddess may come if the girl who bears her name is harmed.","n":"Chithirapathi's real argument is the last two lines: Manimekalai was named after the goddess, so harming her may bring the goddess, and the city's sinking is the threat behind her plea. The sage's warning is conditional, a rite to keep, unlike the fixed date given by the Buddhist sage of kaathai 9. Line 66 (ஆங்குப் பதி அழிதலும் ஈங்குப் பதி கெடுதலும்) is hard to place: 'that city' and 'this city' may be the Naga city and Puhar, or two moments of Puhar.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saabam","vizhavu","kedu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 24, lines 52-74","lb":"MM24:52-74","ev":"In Chithirapathi's story the sage names Pilivalai, foretells her son's coming, and warns that Puhar will sink into the sea if Indra's festival is ever neglected; Chithirapathi says she fears the goddess's coming.","cl":[["the Manimekala goddess's word cannot undo Indra's curse on the city","general","reported",65,"saaranan"]],"ag":["சாரணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சித்திராபதி","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ch":"mm:25"},"mm:24:75-88":{"book":"manimekalai","num":189,"t":["'நல் மனம் பிறந்த நாடகக் கணிகையை","என் மனைத் தருக' என இராசமாதேவி","'கள்ளும் பொய்யும் காமமும் கொலையும்","உள்ளக் களவும் என்று உரவோர் துறந்தவை","தலைமையாக் கொண்ட நின் தலைமை இல் வாழ்க்கை","புலைமை என்று அஞ்சிப் போந்த பூங்கொடி","நின்னொடு போந்து நின் மனைப் புகுதாள்","என்னொடு இருக்கும்' என்று ஈங்கு இவை சொல்வுழி","மணிமேகலை திறம் மாதவி கேட்டு","துணி கயம் துகள் படத் துளங்கிய அதுபோல்","தெளியாச் சிந்தையள் சுதமதிக்கு உரைத்து","வளி எறி கொம்பின் வருந்தி மெய்ந் நடுங்கி","அறவணர் அடி வீழ்ந்து ஆங்கு அவர் தம்முடன்","மற வேல் மன்னவன் தேவி தன்பால் வரத்"],"p":"","r":"Chithirapathi asks for the dancer whose mind has turned good to be given to her house, and the queen refuses: the girl fled the life of drink, lying, lust, killing and deceit that the wise renounce and that Chithirapathi's house takes as its chief, fearing it base, and she will stay with the queen; Madhavi, hearing of her daughter, troubled like a stirred pool and shaking like a wind-tossed branch, tells Sudhamathi, falls at Aravanar's feet and comes with him to the queen.","n":"The queen, who had imprisoned Manimekalai, now speaks the renouncer's list against the courtesan's house; the five she names (கள், பொய், காமம், கொலை, உள்ளக் களவு) are close to the Buddhist precepts, but the unit attributes them only to உரவோர், 'the strong', so the claim is tagged general. நல் மனம் பிறந்த (75) is Chithirapathi's own phrase, used to win the girl back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kal","thuravu","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 24, lines 75-88","lb":"MM24:75-88","ev":"The queen refuses to hand Manimekalai to Chithirapathi; Madhavi, alarmed, brings Aravanar to the queen.","cl":[["drink, lying, lust, killing and deceit are what the wise renounce, and a life built on them is base","general","asserted",78,"arasi"]],"ag":["சித்திராபதி","நறு மலர்க் கோதை","மாதவி","சுதமதி","அறவண அடிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நறு மலர்க் கோதை","சித்திராபதி"],"ch":"mm:25"},"mm:24:89-100":{"book":"manimekalai","num":190,"t":["தேவியும் ஆயமும் சித்திராபதியும்","மாதவி மகளும் மாதவர்க் காண்டலும்","எழுந்து எதிர்சென்று ஆங்கு இணை வளைக் கையால்","தொழும்தகை மாதவன் துணை அடி வணங்க","'அறிவு உண்டாக' என்று ஆங்கு அவன் கூறலும்","இணை வளை நல்லாள் இராசமாதேவி","அருந் தவர்க்கு அமைந்த ஆசனம் காட்டி","திருந்து அடி விளக்கிச் சிறப்புச் செய்த பின்","\"யாண்டு பல புக்க நும் இணை அடி வருந்த என்","காண்தகு நல்வினை நும்மை ஈங்கு அழைத்தது","நாத் தொலைவு இல்லைஆயினும் தளர்ந்து","மூத்த இவ் யாக்கை வாழ்க பல்லாண்டு!' என"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the sage, the queen, her women, Chithirapathi and Manimekalai rise and go to meet him and bow at his feet; he blesses them with 'may wisdom arise', and the queen seats him, washes his feet, honours him, and says her good deeds have brought him after many years and wishes his tired, aged body long life, though his tongue has not failed.","n":"Even Chithirapathi rises and bows. The sage's blessing is for wisdom, the queen's for the body's long life; the next unit answers her wish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","guru","yaakkai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 24, lines 89-100","lb":"MM24:89-100","ev":"All rise and bow to Aravanar, who blesses them; the queen washes his feet and wishes him long life.","cl":[["a meeting with a holy man is the fruit of one's own good deeds","general","reported",98,"arasi"]],"ag":["நறு மலர்க் கோதை","ஆயம்","சித்திராபதி","மணிமேகலை","அறவண அடிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நறு மலர்க் கோதை","அறவண அடிகள்"],"ch":"mm:25"},"mm:24:101-122":{"book":"manimekalai","num":191,"t":["'தேவி கேளாய்! செய் தவ யாக்கையின்","மேவினேன் ஆயினும் வீழ் கதிர் போன்றேன்","பிறந்தார் \"மூத்தார் பிணி நோய் உற்றார்","இறந்தார்\" என்கை இயல்பே இது கேள்","பேதைமை செய்கை உணர்வே அருஉரு","வாயில் ஊறே நுகர்வே வேட்கை","பற்றே பவமே தோற்றம் வினைப் பயன்","இற்று என வகுத்த இயல்பு ஈர் ஆறும்","பிறந்தோர் அறியின் பெரும் பேறு அறிகுவர்","அறியாராயின் ஆழ் நரகு அறிகுவர்","\"பேதைமை என்பது யாது?\" என வினவின்","ஓதிய இவற்றை உணராது மயங்கி","இயற்படு பொருளால் கண்டது மறந்து","முயற்கோடு உண்டு எனக் கேட்டது தெளிதல்","உலகம் மூன்றினும் உயிர் ஆம் உலகம்","அலகு இல பல் உயிர் அறு வகைத்து ஆகும்","மக்களும் தேவரும் பிரமரும் நரகரும்","தொக்க விலங்கும் பேயும் என்றே","நல்வினை தீவினை என்று இரு வகையான்","சொல்லப்பட்ட கருவினுள் தோன்றி","வினைப் பயன் விளையும்காலை உயிர்கட்கு","மனப் பேர் இன்பமும் கவலையும் காட்டும்"],"p":"","r":"Aravanar answers that though he lives in a body given to austerity he is like the setting sun, for to be born, grow old, fall ill and die is the nature of things, and he teaches the twelve links from ignorance through action, consciousness, name and form, the six gates, contact, feeling, craving, grasping, becoming and birth to the fruit of deeds, saying that those who know them gain the great reward and those who don't go down to hell; ignorance is failing to know these, forgetting what is seen in the nature of things and trusting hearsay that a hare has horns; and living beings are of six kinds, born by good and bad deeds and shown joy and sorrow when the deeds ripen.","n":"The queen's wish for long life is turned without rebuke into the first lesson: ageing is nature (இயல்பே, 104), not a misfortune to be wished away. The list of twelve ends with வினைப் பயன் where the old age and death of the usual list would be, and those come back as the opening sentence. Ignorance is defined epistemically, as trusting what one heard over what one saw (113-114), which kaathai 29's means of knowledge will develop.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","pedhaimai","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 24, lines 101-122","lb":"MM24:101-122","ev":"Aravanar answers the queen's blessing and begins to teach the twelve links of dependent arising and the six kinds of beings.","cl":[["whatever is born grows old, falls ill and dies by nature","buddhist","asserted",104,"aravanar"],["birth runs through twelve linked conditions from ignorance to the fruit of deeds","buddhist","asserted",108,"aravanar"],["those who know the twelve links gain the great reward and those who do not go to deep hell","buddhist","asserted",109,"aravanar"],["ignorance is trusting hearsay over what is seen, like believing a hare has horns","buddhist","asserted",114,"aravanar"],["beings are of six kinds, born by good and bad deeds, which ripen as joy and sorrow","buddhist","asserted",121,"aravanar"]],"ag":["அறவண அடிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ch":"mm:25"},"mm:24:123-140":{"book":"manimekalai","num":192,"t":["\"தீவினை என்பது யாது?\" என வினவின்","ஆய் தொடி நல்லாய்! ஆங்கு அது கேளாய்","கொலையே களவே காமத் தீவிழைவு","உலையா உடம்பில் தோன்றுவ முன்றும்","பொய்யே குறளை கடுஞ் சொல் பயன் இல்","சொல் எனச் சொல்லில் தோன்றுவ நான்கும்","வெஃகல் வெகுளல் பொல்லாக் காட்சி என்று","உள்ளம் தன்னின் உருப்பன மூன்றும் எனப்","பத்து வகையால் பயன் தெரி புலவர்","இத் திறம் படரார் படர்குவர் ஆயின்","விலங்கும் பேயும் நரகரும் ஆகி","கலங்கிய உள்ளக் கவலையில் தோன்றுவர்","\"நல்வினை என்பது யாது?\" என வினவின்","சொல்லிய பத்தின் தொகுதியின் நீங்கி","சீலம் தாங்கித்தானம் தலைநின்று","மேல் என வகுத்த ஒரு மூன்று திறத்து","தேவரும் மக்களும் பிரமரும் ஆகி","மேவிய மகிழ்ச்சி வினைப் பயன் உண்குவர்"],"p":"","r":"Aravanar defines evil deeds as ten: killing, theft and lustful desire of the body; lying, slander, harsh speech and idle speech of the tongue; and covetousness, anger and wrong view of the mind; the wise do not walk this way, and those who do are born as animals, ghosts and beings of hell in a troubled mind; good deeds are leaving these ten, keeping the precepts and standing first in giving, and those who do them are born as gods, humans and Brahmas and enjoy the fruit.","n":"Good deeds here win only the higher births of the six, gods, humans and Brahmas; they do not end birth, which the previous unit ties to knowing the twelve links. The ten are counted by the door they come through (body, speech, mind), so wrong view (பொல்லாக் காட்சி) is itself a deed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","naragam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 24, lines 123-140","lb":"MM24:123-140","ev":"","cl":[["evil deeds are ten: three of the body, four of speech and three of the mind","buddhist","asserted",131,"aravanar"],["those who do the ten are reborn as animals, ghosts and beings of hell","buddhist","asserted",133,"aravanar"],["those who avoid the ten, keep the precepts and give are reborn as gods, humans and Brahmas and enjoy the fruit","buddhist","asserted",139,"aravanar"]],"ag":["அறவண அடிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ch":"mm:25"},"mm:24:141-158":{"book":"manimekalai","num":193,"t":["அரைசன் தேவியொடு ஆய் இழை நல்லீர்!","புரை தீர் நல் அறம் போற்றிக் கேண்மின்","மறு பிறப்பு உணர்ந்த மணிமேகலை நீ!","பிற அறம் கேட்ட பின் நாள் வந்து உனக்கு","இத் திறம் பலவும் இவற்றின் பகுதியும்","முத்து ஏர் நகையாய்! முன்னுறக் கூறுவல்'","என்று அவன் எழுதலும் இளங்கொடி எழுந்து","நன்று அறி மாதவன் நல் அடி வணங்கி","'தேவியும் ஆயமும் சித்திராபதியும்","மாதவர் நல் மொழி மறவாது உய்ம்மின்","இந் நகர் மருங்கின் யான் உறைவேன் ஆயின்","\"மன்னவன் மகற்கு இவள் வரும் கூற்று\" என்குவர்","ஆபுத்திரன் நாடு அடைந்து அதன் பின் நாள்","மாசு இல் மணிபல்லவம் தொழுது ஏத்தி","வஞ்சியுள் புக்கு மா பத்தினி தனக்கு","எஞ்சா நல் அறம் யாங்கணும் செய்குவல்","\"எனக்கு இடர் உண்டு\" என்று இரங்கல் வேண்டா","மனக்கு இனியீர்!\" என்று அவரையும் வணங்கி"],"p":"","r":"Aravanar bids the queen and the women hold to this dharma, and tells Manimekalai, who knows her former birth, that after she has heard the other doctrines he will tell her all of it in full; he rises, and she bows at his feet and tells the queen, the women and Chithirapathi not to forget his words, saying that if she stays they will call her death to the king's son, so she will go to Aputhiran's land, then worship at Manipallavam, then enter Vanji and do good deeds everywhere for the great pathini, and they need not grieve for her.","n":"Aravanar's order is a method: Manimekalai is to hear the rival doctrines first and be taught the full dharma after (144), which is the shape of kaathais 27 to 30. Her own itinerary (153-156) names the next stages of the epic, and the pathini of Vanji is Kannaki, her father's wife.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","pathini","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 24, lines 141-158","lb":"MM24:141-158","ev":"Aravanar promises to teach Manimekalai fully later; she bows, takes leave of the queen and Chithirapathi, and says she will go to Aputhiran's land, Manipallavam and Vanji.","cl":[],"ag":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","நறு மலர்க் கோதை"],"ch":"mm:25"},"mm:24:159-176":{"book":"manimekalai","num":194,"t":["வெந்துறு பொன் போல் வீழ் கதிர் மறைந்த","அந்தி மாலை ஆய் இழை போகி","உலக அறவியும் முதியாள் குடிகையும்","இலகு ஒளிக் கந்தமும் ஏத்தி வலம் கொண்டு","அந்தரம் ஆறாப் பறந்து சென்று ஆய் இழை","இந்திரன் மருமான் இரும் பதிப் புறத்து ஓர்","பூம்பொழில் அகவயின் இழிந்து பொறையுயிர்த்து","ஆங்கு வாழ் மாதவன் அடி இணை வணங்கி","'இந் நகர்ப் பேர் யாது? இந் நகர் ஆளும்","மன்னவன் யார்?\" என மாதவன் கூறும்","'நாகபுரம் இது நல் நகர் ஆள்வோன்","பூமிசந்திரன் மகன் புண்ணியராசன்","ஈங்கு இவன் பிறந்த அந் நாள் தொட்டும்","ஓங்கு உயர் வானத்துப் பெயல் பிழைப்பு அறியாது","மண்ணும் மரனும் வளம் பல தரூஉம்","உள் நின்று உருக்கும் நோய் உயிர்க்கு இல்' என","தகை மலர்த் தாரோன் தன் திறம் கூறினன்","அகை மலர்ப் பூம்பொழில் அருந் தவன் தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"At dusk Manimekalai circles and praises the world's charity hall, the old woman's shrine and the shining pillar, flies through the sky and comes down in a flower grove outside the great city of Indra's descendant, bows to an ascetic there and asks the city's name and king, and he tells her it is Nagapuram, ruled by Punniyarajan, son of Bhumichandran, since whose birth rain has never failed, earth and trees have yielded plenty and no wasting disease has touched living beings.","n":"Punniyarajan is the reborn Aputhiran (the land she named at 153), and the city is called that of Indra's descendant (164): the plenty Indra once sent to idle Aputhiran's bowl now marks his birth as a king. The ascetic credits the rain to the king's birth, not to his rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","arasu","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 24, lines 159-176","lb":"MM24:159-176","ev":"Manimekalai flies from Puhar to a grove outside Nagapuram, where an ascetic tells her the city is ruled by Punniyarajan.","cl":[["since a meritorious king's birth the rain has not failed and no disease has struck his people","general","reported",172,"nagapura_maathavan"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","மாதவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:25"},"mm:25:1-24":{"book":"manimekalai","num":195,"t":["அரசன் உரிமையோடு அப் பொழில் புகுந்து","தருமசாவகன் தன் அடி வணங்கி","அறனும் மறனும் அநித்தமும் நித்தத்","திறனும் துக்கமும் செல் உயிர்ப் புக்கிலும்","சார்பின் தோற்றமும் சார்பு அறுத்து உய்தியும்","ஆரியன் அமைதியும் அமைவுறக் கேட்டு","'பெண் இணை இல்லாப் பெரு வனப்பு உற்றாள்","கண் இணை இயக்கமும் காமனோடு இயங்கா","அங்கையில் பாத்திரம் கொண்டு அறம் கேட்கும்","இங்கு இணை இல்லாள் இவள் யார்?' என்ன","காவலன் தொழுது கஞ்சுகன் உரைப்போன்","'நாவல் அம் தீவில் இந் நங்கையை ஒப்பார்","யாவரும் இல்லை இவள் திறம் எல்லாம்","கிள்ளிவளவனொடு கெழுதகை வேண்டிக்","கள் அவிழ் தாரோய்! கலத்தொடும் போகி","காவிரிப் படப்பை நல் நகர் புக்கேன்","மாதவன் அறவணன் இவள் பிறப்பு உணர்ந்தாங்கு","ஓதினன் என்று யான் அன்றே உரைத்தேன்","ஆங்கு அவள் இவள்! அவ் அகல் நகர் நீங்கி","ஈங்கு வந்தனள்' என்றலும் இளங்கொடி","'நின் கைப் பாத்திரம் என் கைப் புகுந்தது","மன் பெருஞ் செல்வத்து மயங்கினை அறியாய்","அப் பிறப்பு அறிந்திலைஆயினும் ஆ வயிற்று","இப் பிறப்பு அறிந்திலை என் செய்தனையோ?"],"p":"","r":"The king of Savakam enters the grove with his household, worships the teacher Dharmasavakan and hears from him dharma and its opposite, the impermanent and the permanent, suffering, the passage of lives, dependent arising and release by cutting it; seeing a peerless woman with a bowl in her hand listening to dharma, he asks who she is, the chamberlain tells him she is the girl whose birth Aravanan revealed in Puhar, and Manimekalai tells the king that his bowl has come into her hand and asks what he has done, bewildered by wealth, not knowing even this birth of his from a cow's womb.","n":"The teaching the king hears (3 to 6) is a list of Buddhist headings, ending with dependent arising and release by cutting it (சார்பு அறுத்து உய்தி), but it has not woken him; it takes Manimekalai's reproach to do that. Her charge is not that he has forgotten a former life, which few remember, but that wealth has hidden from him even the present one (23 to 24), the cow's womb he was born from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","aram","selvam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 1-24","lb":"MM25:1-24","ev":"The king of Savakam hears dharma from Dharmasavakan, asks who the woman with the bowl is, and Manimekalai tells him his bowl is now in her hand and reproaches him for not knowing his birth.","cl":[["one is saved by cutting the chain of dependent arising","buddhist","asserted",5,"dharmasaavakan"]],"ag":["ஆபுத்திரன்","தருமசாவகன்","கஞ்சுகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:25:25-41":{"book":"manimekalai","num":196,"t":["மணிப்பல்லவம் வலம் கொண்டால் அல்லது","பிணிப்புறு பிறவியின் பெற்றியை அறியாய்","ஆங்கு வருவாய் அரச! நீ' என்று அப்","பூங் கமழ் தாரோன்முன்னர்ப் புகன்று","மை அறு விசும்பின் மடக்கொடி எழுந்து","வெய்யவன் குடபால் வீழாமுன்னர்","வான் நின்று இழிந்து மறி திரை உலாவும்","பூ நாறு அடைகரை எங்கணும் போகி","மணிப்பல்லவம் வலம் கொண்டு மடக்கொடி","பிணிப்பு அறு மாதவன் பீடிகை காண்டலும்","தொழுது வலம் கொள்ள அத் தூ மணிப்பீடிகைப்","பழுது இல் காட்சி தன் பிறப்பு உணர்த்த","'காயங்கரை எனும் பேர் யாற்று அடைகரை","மாயம் இல் மாதவன் தன் அடி பணிந்து","தருமம் கேட்டு தாள் தொழுது ஏத்தி","பெருமகன் தன்னொடும் பெயர்வோர்க்கு எல்லாம்","'விலங்கும் நரகரும் பேய்களும் ஆக்கும்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai tells the king he will not know the nature of bound birth unless he circles Manipallavam and bids him come there, then rises into the clear sky, comes down before sunset on the island's flowered shore, circles it, sees the jewelled seat of the bond-free great ascetic and, as she worships and circles it, the seat's flawless sight makes her know her birth again, and she begins to speak of how on the Kayankarai bank she bowed to the sage, heard dharma and praised him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vazhipaadu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 25-41","lb":"MM25:25-41","ev":"Manimekalai summons the king to Manipallavam, flies there herself, circles the dharma seat and recalls again her former birth.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:25:42-63":{"book":"manimekalai","num":197,"t":["கலங்கு அஞர்த் தீவினை கடிமின் கடிந்தால்","தேவரும் மக்களும் பிரமரும் ஆகுதிர்","ஆகலின் நல்வினை அயராது ஓம்புமின்","புலவன் முழுதும் பொய் இன்று உணர்ந்தோன்","உலகு உயக் கோடற்கு ஒருவன் தோன்றும்","அந் நாள் அவன் அறம் கேட்டோர் அல்லது","இன்னாப் பிறவி இழுக்குநர் இல்லை","மாற்று அருங் கூற்றம் வருவதன் முன்னம்","போற்றுமின் அறம்' எனச் சாற்றிக் காட்டி","நாக் கடிப்பு ஆக வாய்ப் பறை அறைந்தீர்","அவ் உரை கேட்டு நும் அடி தொழுது ஏத்த","வெவ் உரை எங்கட்கு விளம்பினிர் ஆதலின்","\"பெரியவன் தோன்றாமுன்னர் இப் பீடிகை","கரியவன் இட்ட காரணம் தானும்","மன் பெரும் பீடிகை மாய்ந்து உயிர் நீங்கிய","என் பிறப்பு உணர்த்தலும் என்?\" என்று யான் தொழ","\"முற்ற உணர்ந்த முதல்வனை அல்லது","மற்று அப் பீடிகை தன்மிசைப் பொறாஅது","பீடிகை பொறுத்த பின்னர் அல்லது","வானவன் வணங்கான் மற்று அவ் வானவன்","பெருமகற்கு அமைத்து 'பிறந்தார் பிறவியைத்","தரும பீடிகை சாற்றுக' என்றே"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai, speaking as if to the sage of her former birth, recalls his proclamation to all who went with him: put away the evil deeds that make men beasts, hell-beings and ghosts, and you become gods, men and Brahmas, so keep up good deeds unwearied; an all-knowing one will appear to save the world, and none but those who hear his dharma escape painful birth, so practise dharma before death comes; and she recalls that after his harsh word to her she asked why the seat had been placed before the Great One's coming and why it shows her birth, and he answered that it bears none but the all-knowing first one, that the god will not worship it until it has borne him, and that the god set it for him and bade it declare the births of those born.","n":"The 'harsh word' (வெவ் உரை, 53) is the sage's prophecy in kaathai 9 that Rahulan would die within sixteen days and she would enter his pyre; here she places in the same hearing her question about the seat, so the seat's power was explained to her in the life she lost. Good deeds earn better births (43 to 44), but only hearing the coming Buddha's dharma ends birth (47 to 48): the sermon keeps those two apart. Who கரியவன் (55) is, who placed the seat, is not clear; at 182 the seat is said to have been set by the king of the gods. Lines 56 to 57 (மன் பெரும் பீடிகை மாய்ந்து உயிர் நீங்கிய என் பிறப்பு) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","naragam","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 42-63","lb":"MM25:42-63","ev":"Manimekalai recalls the sage's sermon on deeds and rebirth and his explanation of why the dharma seat reveals births.","cl":[["evil deeds lead to birth as beasts, hell-beings and ghosts, and avoiding them leads to birth as gods, men and Brahmas","buddhist","asserted",43,"brahmadharman"],["none escape painful birth except those who hear the dharma of the all-knowing one who is to come","buddhist","asserted",48,"brahmadharman"],["dharma must be practised before death, which cannot be turned back, arrives","general","asserted",50,"brahmadharman"]],"ag":["பிரமதருமன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","பிரமதருமன்"],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:25:64-76":{"book":"manimekalai","num":198,"t":["அருளினன் ஆதலின் ஆய் இழை பிறவியும்","இருள் அறக் காட்டும்\" என்று எடுத்து உரைத்தது","அன்றே போன்றது அருந் தவர் வாய்மொழி","இன்று எனக்கு' என்றே ஏத்தி வலம் கொண்டு","ஈங்கு இவள் இன்னணம் ஆக இறைவனும்","ஆங்கு அப் பொழில் விட்டு அகநகர் புக்கு","தந்தை முனியா தாய் பசு ஆக","வந்த பிறவியும் மா முனி அருளால்","குடர்த் தொடர் மாலை சூழாது ஆங்கு ஓர்","அடர்ப் பொன் முட்டையுள் அடங்கிய வண்ணமும்","மா முனி அருளால் மக்களை இல்லோன்","பூமிசந்திரன் கொடுபோந்த வண்ணமும்","ஆய் தொடி அரிவை அமரசுந்தரி எனும்"],"p":"","r":"The sage's answer ends: because the god so ordained, the seat will show her births too, clearing the dark; Manimekalai says the word the sage spoke that day has come true for her today, praises and circles the seat; meanwhile the king leaves the grove for the inner city and learns of the birth in which a sage was his father and a cow his mother, how by the sage's grace he lay in a golden egg without the cord of the gut, and how childless Bhumichandran took him, hearing it from his mother Amarasundari.","n":"The king learns his former birth twice: from his foster mother in the palace (70 to 77) before the seat shows it to him (134 to 138). The golden egg 'not wound in the gut-chain' (72 to 73) marks this birth as unsoiled by the womb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","arul","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 64-76","lb":"MM25:64-76","ev":"Manimekalai says the sage's word is fulfilled; the king returns to the city and hears from his mother the story of his birth from a cow and his adoption.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","ஆபுத்திரன்","மண் ஆள் வேந்தன்","அமரசுந்தரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:25:77-95":{"book":"manimekalai","num":199,"t":["தாய் வாய்க் கேட்டு தாழ் துயர் எய்தி","இறந்த பிறவியின் யாய் செய்ததூஉம்","பிறந்த பிறவியின் பெற்றியும் நினைந்து","'செரு வேல் மன்னர் செவ்வி பார்த்து உணங்க","அரைசு வீற்றிருந்து புரையோர்ப் பேணி","நாடகம் கண்டு பாடல் பான்மையின்","கேள்வி இன் இசை கேட்டு தேவியர்","ஊடல் செவ்வி பார்த்து நீடாது","பாடகத் தாமரைச் சீறடி பணிந்து","தே மரு கொங்கையில் குங்குமம் எழுதி","அம் கையில் துறு மலர் சுரி குழல் சூட்டி","நறு முகை அமிழ்து உறூஉம் திரு நகை அருந்தி","மதி முகக் கருங் கண் செங் கடை கலக்கக்","கருப்பு வில்லி அருப்புக் கணை தூவ","தருக்கிய காமக் கள்ளாட்டு இகழ்ந்து","தூ அறத் துறத்தல் நன்று' எனச் சாற்றி","'தெளிந்த நாதன் என் செவிமுதல் இட்ட வித்து","ஏதம் இன்றாய் இன்று விளைந்தது","மணிமேகலை தான் காரணம் ஆக' என்று"],"p":"","r":"Grieving at what he hears, the king thinks of what his mother did in the life he lost and of the nature of the birth he now has, and declares that it is good to renounce wholly the intoxicating game of desire that kingship is, with its throne before which kings wait, its dancing and music, its waiting on queens' sulks, painting saffron on their breasts and drinking their smiles under the sugarcane bow's arrows; the seed the clear-sighted lord sowed in his ear, he says, has ripened today without fault, with Manimekalai as its cause.","n":"'What the mother did' (78) is the abandonment of the newborn Aputhiran by Saali in his former birth. The king does not renounce kingship as a burden but as a drunkenness of pleasure (காமக் கள்ளாட்டு, 91): his catalogue is the life of a pleasure-king, not of a protector. The 'clear lord' who sowed the seed (93) is most likely Dharmasavakan, whose teaching he had just heard in the grove. His claim is answered: the minister calls it a wrong judgment by the Buddha's own example (115 to 118), and Manimekalai sends him back to rule with the dharma of grace (226 to 231).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","kaamam","arasu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 77-95","lb":"MM25:77-95","ev":"Hearing his birth story, the king grieves and declares he will give up kingship and its pleasures for renunciation.","cl":[["a king does well to give up kingship and its pleasures wholly for renunciation","general","refuted",92,"aaputhiran"]],"ag":["ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆபுத்திரன்",""],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:25:96-114":{"book":"manimekalai","num":200,"t":["அணி மணி நீள் முடி அரசன் கூற","'மனம் வேறு ஆயினன் மன்' என மந்திரி","சனமித்திரன் அவன் தாள் தொழுது ஏத்தி","'எம் கோ வாழி! என் சொல் கேண்மதி","நும் கோன் உன்னைப் பெறுவதன் முன் நாள்","பன்னீராண்டு இப் பதி கெழு நல் நாடு","மன் உயிர் மடிய மழை வளம் கரந்து ஈங்கு","ஈன்றாள் குழவிக்கு இரங்காளாகி","தான் தனி தின்னும் தகைமையது ஆயது","காய் வெங் கோடையில் கார் தோன்றியதென","நீ தோன்றினையே நிரைத் தார் அண்ணல்!","தோன்றிய பின்னர் தோன்றிய உயிர்கட்கு","வானம் பொய்யாது மண் வளம் பிழையாது","ஊன் உடை உயிர்கள் உறு பசி அறியா","நீ ஒழிகாலை நின் நாடு எல்லாம்","தாய் ஒழி குழவி போலக் கூஉம்","துயர் நிலை உலகம் காத்தல் இன்றி நீ","உயர் நிலை உலகம் வேட்டனை ஆயின்","இறுதி உயிர்கள் எய்தவும் இறைவ!"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the king's mind changed, the minister Janamitran bows and tells him that before his father had him, this land had no rain for twelve years, so that creatures died and a mother would eat alone without pity for her infant; he appeared like a raincloud in a burning summer, and since then the sky has not failed nor the earth its yield, and no creature has known hunger; if he goes, his land will cry like an infant whose mother is gone, and if he seeks the higher world without guarding this suffering one, then even as lives perish, lord,","n":"The minister ties the rain not to the king's rule but to his birth (105 to 109): the merit that returns the rain is Aputhiran's giving in his former life, carried into this one (kaathai 14, 95 to 104). The mother eating alone in the famine (103 to 104) is the reversal of the bowl's ethic of feeding others first. The unit ends mid-sentence; the minister's argument completes at 115 to 118.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","mazhai","pasi","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 96-114","lb":"MM25:96-114","ev":"The minister Janamitran tells the king of the twelve-year famine that ended at his birth and warns that his land will be orphaned if he leaves.","cl":[["the birth of a meritorious king brings back the rain and ends hunger","general","asserted",108,"janamithiran"]],"ag":["சனமித்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சனமித்திரன்","ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:25:115-139":{"book":"manimekalai","num":201,"t":["பெறுதி விரும்பினை ஆகுவை அன்றே!","தன் உயிர்க்கு இரங்கான் பிற உயிர் ஓம்பும்","மன் உயிர் முதல்வன் அறமும் ஈது அன்றால்","மதி மாறு ஒர்ந்தனை மன்னவ!' என்றே","முதுமொழி கூற முதல்வன் கேட்டு","'மணிபல்லவம் வலம் கொள்வதற்கு எழுந்த","தணியா வேட்கை தணித்தற்கு அரிதால்","அரசும் உரிமையும் அகநகர்ச் சுற்றமும்","ஒரு மதி எல்லை காத்தல் நின் கடன்' என","'கலம் செய் கம்மியர் வருக' எனக் கூஉய்","இலங்கு நீர்ப் புணரி எறி கரை எய்தி","வங்கம் ஏறினன் மணிபல்லவத்திடை","தங்காது அக் கலம் சென்று சார்ந்து இறுத்தலும்","புரை தீர் காட்சிப் பூங்கொடி பொருந்தி","அரைசன் கலம் என்று அகம் மகிழ்வு எய்தி","காவலன் தன்னொடும் கடல் திரை உலாவும்","தே மலர்ச் சோலைத் தீவகம் வலம் செய்து","'பெருமகன்! காணாய் பிறப்பு உணர்விக்கும்","தரும பீடிகை இது' எனக் காட்ட","வலம் கொண்டு ஏத்தினன் மன்னவன் மன்னவற்கு","உலந்த பிறவியை உயர் மணிப் பீடிகை","கைஅகத்து எடுத்துக் காண்போர் முகத்தை","மை அறு மண்டிலம் போலக் காட்ட","'என் பிறப்பு அறிந்தேன் என் இடர் தீர்ந்தேன்","தென் தமிழ் மதுரைச் செழுங் கலைப் பாவாய்!"],"p":"","r":"you would gain what you desire, the minister says, yet the dharma of the Buddha, first of all lives, who does not pity his own life and protects the lives of others, is not this, and the king has judged wrongly; the king answers that the unquenchable longing to circle Manipallavam is hard to quiet, charges the minister to guard kingdom, queens and city for one month, summons shipwrights, sails, and reaches the island, where Manimekalai, glad to see the king's ship, circles it with him and shows him the dharma seat, which, as he circles and praises it, shows him his ended birth like a clear mirror held to a face, and he cries that he knows his birth and his grief is ended, hailing the image of the arts of Tamil Madurai.","n":"The minister turns the Buddha's own example against the king's renunciation: the Buddha's mark is that he does not pity his own life (116 to 117), and seeking a higher world for oneself while one's people suffer is pity for oneself. The king does not answer the argument; he asks only for a month to circle the island. Line 118 ஒர்ந்தனை is read as ஓர்ந்தனை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","arasu","piravi","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 115-139","lb":"MM25:115-139","ev":"The minister appeals to the Buddha's example; the king leaves him to guard the realm for a month, sails to Manipallavam, and the dharma seat shows him his former birth.","cl":[["the Buddha's dharma, which pities not one's own life and protects the lives of others, does not leave a suffering world to seek a higher world for oneself","buddhist","asserted",117,"janamithiran"]],"ag":["சனமித்திரன்","ஆபுத்திரன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:25:140-150":{"book":"manimekalai","num":202,"t":["மாரி நடு நாள் வயிறு காய் பசியால்","ஆர் இருள் அஞ்சாது அம்பலம் அணைந்து ஆங்கு","இரந்தூண் வாழ்க்கை என்பால் வந்தோர்க்கு","அருந்து ஊண் காணாது அழுங்குவேன் கையில்","\"நாடு வறம் கூரினும் இவ் ஓடு வறம் கூராது","ஏடா! அழியல் எழுந்து இது கொள்க\" என","அமுதசுரபி அங்கையில் தந்து என்","பவம் அறுவித்த வானோர் பாவாய்!","உணர்வில் தோன்றி உரைப் பொருள் உணர்த்தும்","மணி திகழ் அவிர் ஒளி மடந்தை! நின் அடி","தேவர் ஆயினும் பிரமர் ஆயினும்"],"p":"","r":"The king recalls to the goddess of Madurai how, when hungry beggars came to him in the rest hall in the middle of a rainy night and he grieved with no food to give, she put Amudasurabi in his palm saying the bowl would not fail though the land did, and so cut off his round of becoming; he hails her, who rises in the mind and makes the meaning of words known, and begins to praise her feet, which even gods and Brahmas worship.","n":"The goddess's words at 144 to 145 repeat almost word for word those Aravanar gave her in kaathai 14 (12 to 13): the king's own memory now agrees with the teacher's account. பவம் அறுவித்த (147) is read as 'who cut off my becoming'; it may mean only that she ended his misery.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi","vazhipaadu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 140-150","lb":"MM25:140-150","ev":"The king, knowing his former birth, recalls and praises Chintadevi's gift of the bowl.","cl":[],"ag":["சிந்தாதேவி","ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆபுத்திரன்","சிந்தாதேவி"],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:25:151-174":{"book":"manimekalai","num":203,"t":["நா மாசு கழூஉம் நலம் கிளர் திருந்து அடி","பிறந்த பிறவிகள் பேணுதல் அல்லது","மறந்து வாழேன் மடந்தை!' என்று ஏத்தி","மன்னவன் மணிமேகலையுடன் எழுந்து","தென் மேற்காகச் சென்று திரை உலாம்","கோமுகி என்னும் பொய்கையின் கரை ஓர்","தூ மலர்ப் புன்னைத் துறை நிழல் இருப்ப","ஆபுத்திரனோடு ஆய் இழை இருந்தது","காவல் தெய்வதம் கண்டு உவந்து எய்தி","'அருந்து உயிர் மருந்து முன் அங்கையில் கொண்டு","பெருந் துயர் தீர்த்த அப் பெரியோய்! வந்தனை","அந் நாள் நின்னை அயர்த்துப் போயினர்","பின் நாள் வந்து நின் பெற்றிமை நோக்கி","நின் குறி இருந்து தம் உயிர் நீத்தோர்","ஒன்பது செட்டிகள் உடல் என்பு இவை காண்","ஆங்கு அவர் இட உண்டு அவருடன் வந்தோர்","ஏங்கி மெய் வைத்தோர் என்பும் இவை காண்","ஊர் திரை தொகுத்த உயர் மணல் புதைப்ப","ஆய் மலர்ப் புன்னை அணி நிழல் கீழால்","அன்பு உடை ஆர் உயிர் அரசற்கு அருளிய","என்பு உடை யாக்கை இருந்தது காணாய்","நின் உயிர் கொன்றாய் நின் உயிர்க்கு இரங்கிப்","பின் நாள் வந்த பிறர் உயிர் கொன்றாய்","கொலைவன் அல்லையோ? கொற்றவன் ஆயினை!"],"p":"","r":"Praising her feet, which wash the stain of the tongue, the king vows never to forget them in any birth, then goes with Manimekalai southwest to the Gomukhi pool and sits in the shade of a punnai; the island's guardian goddess, glad to see Aputhiran there, comes and tells him: you who held in your palm the medicine that saves life and ended great suffering, you have come; those who forgot you that day came back later, saw what you had done and gave up their lives where you had lain: see the bones of the nine merchants and of those who ate from their hands and died grieving; see your own bony frame under the sand by the punnai; you killed your own life, and, pitying your life, the others who came later killed theirs: are you not a killer? you have become a king.","n":"The goddess's charge reckons self-starvation as killing (நின் உயிர் கொன்றாய், 172) and lays the later deaths of the merchants and their company on Aputhiran, who set the example; then she turns the charge with a play of sound, கொலைவன் against கொற்றவன் (174): the 'killer' has become a king. Whether this is reproach or wonder is not settled by the Tamil. Line 170 (அன்பு உடை ஆர் உயிர் அரசற்கு அருளிய) is hard: read as the body that, by the loving life it gave up, gave the king his kingship.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kolai","unnaanonbu","yaakkai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 151-174","lb":"MM25:151-174","ev":"At the Gomukhi pool the island's guardian goddess shows the king the bones of the nine merchants who died where he had died and calls him a killer who has become a king.","cl":[["giving up one's own life by fasting is killing, and the one who does it bears the deaths of those who follow him","general","asserted",172,"deepathilakai"]],"ag":["ஆபுத்திரன்","மணிமேகலை","தீபதிலகை","ஒன்பது செட்டிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தீபதிலகை","ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:25:175-199":{"book":"manimekalai","num":204,"t":["பலர் தொழு பாத்திரம் கையின் ஏந்திய","மடவரல் நல்லாய்! நின் தன் மா நகர்","கடல் வயிறு புக்கது காரணம் கேளாய்","நாக நல் நாடு ஆள்வோன் தன் மகள்","பீலிவளை என்பாள் பெண்டிரின் மிக்கோள்","பனிப் பகை வானவன் வழியில் தோன்றிய","புனிற்று இளங் குழவியொடு பூங்கொடி பொருந்தி இத்","தீவகம் வலம் செய்து தேவர் கோன் இட்ட","மா பெரும் பீடிகை வலம் கொண்டு ஏத்துழி","கம்பளச் செட்டி கலம் வந்து இறுப்ப","அங்கு அவன்பால் சென்று அவன் திறம் அறிந்து","\"கொற்றவன் மகன் இவன் கொள்க\" எனக் கொடுத்தலும்","பெற்ற உவகையன் பெரு மகிழ்வு எய்தி","பழுது இல் காட்சிப் பைந்தொடி புதல்வனைத்","தொழுதனன் வாங்கி துறை பிறக்கு ஒழிய","கலம் கொண்டு பெயர்ந்த அன்றே கார் இருள்","இலங்கு நீர் அடைகரை அக் கலம் கெட்டது","கெடு கல மாக்கள் புதல்வனைக் கெடுத்தது","வடி வேல் கிள்ளி மன்னனுக்கு உரைப்ப","மன்னவன் மகனுக்கு உற்றது பொறாஅன்","நல் மணி இழந்த நாகம் போன்று","கானலும் கடலும் கரையும் தேர்வுழி","வானவன் விழாக் கோள் மா நகர் ஒழிந்தது","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம் மற்று அது பொறாஅள்","\"அணி நகர் தன்னை அலை கடல் கொள்க\" என"],"p":"","r":"Turning to Manimekalai, the goddess tells her why her great city went into the sea: Pilivalai, daughter of the Naga king, came with her newborn son by the Chola of the sun's line, circled the island and the seat Indra set, and gave the boy to the merchant Kambalan, whose ship had put in, as the king's son; he took the child with joy, but that night the ship was wrecked on the shore and the boy lost; the Chola, told of it, searched beach, sea and shore like a cobra that has lost its gem, the city's Indra festival lapsed, and the goddess Manimekala, unable to bear that, cried 'let the sea take the city'.","n":"The city's ruin has a chain of causes with no evil intent in it: a mother's gift, a shipwreck, a father's grief, a festival forgotten in that grief, and a goddess's anger at the lapse. The king's search 'like a cobra that has lost its gem' (195) recalls the Naga line of the lost child.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saabam","vizhavu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 175-199","lb":"MM25:175-199","ev":"The guardian goddess tells how the Chola's infant son by Pilivalai was lost in a shipwreck, the grieving king neglected the Indra festival, and Manimekala cursed Puhar to be taken by the sea.","cl":[],"ag":["பீலிவளை","கம்பளச் செட்டி","அறை கழல் வேந்தன்","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தீபதிலகை","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:25:200-221":{"book":"manimekalai","num":205,"t":["இட்டனள் சாபம் பட்டது இதுவால்","கடவுள் மா நகர் கடல் கொள பெயர்ந்த","வடி வேல் தடக் கை வானவன் போல","விரிதிரை வந்து வியல் நகர் விழுங்க","ஒரு தனி போயினன் உலக மன்னவன்","அருந் தவன் தன்னுடன் ஆய் இழை தாயரும்","வருந்தாது ஏகி வஞ்சியுள் புக்கனர்","பரப்பு நீர்ப் பௌவம் பலர் தொழ காப்போள்","உரைத்தன கேட்க உறுகுவை ஆயின் நின்","மன் உயிர் முதல்வனை மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","முன் நாள் எடுத்ததும் அந் நாள் ஆங்கு அவன்","அற அரசு ஆண்டதும் அறவணன் தன்பால்","மறு பிறப்பாட்டி வஞ்சியுள் கேட்பை' என்று","அந்தரத் தீவகத்து அருந் தெய்வம் போய பின்","மன்னவன் இரங்கி மணிமேகலையுடன்","துன்னிய தூ மணல் அகழத் தோன்றி","ஊன் பிணி அவிழவும் உடல் என்பு ஒடுங்கித்","தான் பிணி அவிழாத் தகைமையது ஆகி","வெண் சுதை வேய்ந்து அவண் இருக்கையின் இருந்த","பண்பு கொள் யாக்கையின் படிவம் நோக்கி","மன்னவன் மயங்க மணிமேகலை எழுந்து","'என் உற்றனையோ இலங்கு இதழ்த் தாரோய்?"],"p":"","r":"The goddess ends: the curse was laid and came to pass; as the waves swallowed the city, its king went off alone like Indra leaving his drowned city, and the sage went safely with Manimekalai's mothers to Vanji; if she would hear how Manimekala once rescued her lord of all lives from the sea and how he then ruled as a king of dharma, she will hear it from Aravanan in Vanji; the goddess departs, and the king, grieving, digs the sand with Manimekalai and uncovers his former body, the flesh fallen away but the bones still holding together, seated as if coated in white lime, and the king is bewildered at the sight until Manimekalai rises and asks him what has befallen him.","n":"The skeleton is kept whole (216 to 217): the flesh's bonds have loosened but the bones' have not, and it sits in the posture of the faster as if plastered for a shrine. The sight that might teach the body's impermanence instead bewilders the king (220). Lines 207 to 212 are compressed: 'the guardian of the wide sea' (207) is read as Manimekala, and 'your lord of lives' (209) as the Buddha in a former birth whom she saved from the sea.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","piravi","saabam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 200-221","lb":"MM25:200-221","ev":"The goddess tells of Puhar's drowning and the flight of Manimekalai's mothers to Vanji and departs; the king digs up his own former skeleton and is bewildered.","cl":[],"ag":["அறை கழல் வேந்தன்","அறவண அடிகள்","மாதவி","சுதமதி","தீபதிலகை","ஆபுத்திரன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:25:222-239":{"book":"manimekalai","num":206,"t":["நின் நாடு அடைந்து யான் நின்னை ஈங்கு அழைத்தது","மன்னா! நின் தன் மறு பிறப்பு உணர்த்தி","அந்தரத் தீவினும் அகன் பெருந் தீவினும்","நின் பெயர் நிறுத்த நீள் நிலம் ஆளும்","அரசர் தாமே அருளறம் பூண்டால்","பொருளும் உண்டோ பிற புரை தீர்த்தற்கு?","\"அறம் எனப்படுவது யாது?\" எனக் கேட்பின்","மறவாது இது கேள் மன் உயிர்க்கு எல்லாம்","உண்டியும் உடையும் உறையுளும் அல்லது","கண்டது இல்' எனக் காவலன் உரைக்கும்","'என் நாட்டு ஆயினும் பிறர் நாட்டு ஆயினும்","நல் நுதல்! உரைத்த நல் அறம் செய்கேன்","என் பிறப்பு உணர்த்தி என்னை நீ படைத்தனை","நின்திறம் நீங்கல் ஆற்றேன் யான்' என","'புன்கண் கொள்ளல் நீ போந்ததற்கு இரங்கி நின்","மன் பெரு நல் நாடு வாய் எடுத்து அழைக்கும்","வங்கத்து ஏகுதி வஞ்சியுள் செல்வன்' என்று","அந்தரத்து எழுந்தனள் அணி இழை தான் என்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai tells the king that she came to his land and called him here to show him his former birth so that his name would stand on this island and on the great continent; if kings who rule the wide earth take up the dharma of grace, nothing else is needed to remove faults, and if he asks what dharma is, he should remember that for all living beings it is nothing but food, clothing and shelter; the king vows to do this good dharma in his own land and in others', says she has made him anew by revealing his birth and that he cannot bear to part from her, and she bids him not grieve, for his land calls him back, to go by ship while she goes to Vanji, and rises into the sky.","n":"Manimekalai does not take the king's offered renunciation; she sends him back to rule and defines his dharma as material provision for every life (230), the bowl's work made a king's duty. His reply widens it at once beyond his own kingdom (232). The kaathai ends on என் running into the next.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","arul","arasu","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 25, lines 222-239","lb":"MM25:222-239","ev":"Manimekalai tells the king that a king's dharma is food, clothing and shelter for all, he vows to practise it, and she sends him home by ship and flies to Vanji.","cl":[["if kings take up the dharma of grace, nothing else is needed to remove the world's faults","general","asserted",226,"manimekalai"],["dharma for all living beings is nothing but food, clothing and shelter","general","asserted",230,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","ஆபுத்திரன்"],"ch":"mm:26"},"mm:26:1-13":{"book":"manimekalai","num":207,"t":["அணி இழை அந்தரம் ஆறா எழுந்து","தணியாக் காதல் தாய் கண்ணகியையும்","கொடை கெழு தாதை கோவலன் தன்னையும்","கடவுள் எழுதிய படிமம் காணிய","வேட்கை துரப்ப கோட்டம் புகுந்து","வணங்கி நின்று குணம் பல ஏத்தி","'அற்புக் கடன் நில்லாது நல் தவம் படராது","கற்புக் கடன் பூண்டு நும் கடன் முடித்தது","அருளல் வேண்டும்' என்று அழுது முன் நிற்ப","ஒரு பெரும் பத்தினிக் கடவுள் ஆங்கு உரைப்போள்","'எம் இறைக்கு உற்ற இடுக்கண் பொறாது","வெம்மையின் மதுரை வெவ் அழல் படு நாள்","மதுராபதி எனும் மா பெருந் தெய்வம்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai rises through the sky, enters the shrine to see the image in which her mother Kannaki and her father Kovalan are figured as deities, bows, praises them and weeps that her mother, neither holding to the debt of love nor taking the way of penance, took on the debt of chastity and so discharged what she owed, and asks for her grace; the great pathini goddess there begins to answer by recalling the day Madurai burned and the word of its goddess Mathurapathi.","n":"Manimekalai's two refusals (அற்புக் கடன் நில்லாது, நல் தவம் படராது, 7) answer Madhavi's list in kaathai 2 of what pathinis do when a husband dies: die of grief, enter the fire, or wear the body down in vows; the daughter, like the mother, places Kannaki outside all three. The image is of both parents (தாய் கண்ணகியையும் ... தாதை கோவலன் தன்னையும், 2-3), and Kovalan is called கொடை கெழு, open-handed. கடவுள் எழுதிய படிமம் (4) may mean an image drawn as deities or one a god made; the line does not settle which, and it does not say this is the shrine Senguttuvan built.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathini","karpu","vazhipaadu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 26, lines 1-13","lb":"MM26:1-13","ev":"Manimekalai flies to the shrine of Kannaki and Kovalan, worships their image and begs her mother's grace, and the pathini goddess begins to answer.","cl":[["a wife who takes on the duty of chastity discharges in it what she owes","general","asserted",8,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:27"},"mm:26:14-32":{"book":"manimekalai","num":208,"t":["\"இது நீர் முன் செய் வினையின் பயனால்","காசு இல் பூம்பொழில் கலிங்க நல் நாட்டுத்","தாய மன்னவர் வசுவும் குமரனும்","சிங்கபுரமும் செழு நீர்க் கபிலையும்","அங்கு ஆள்கின்றோர் அடல் செரு உறு நாள்","மூ இரு காவதம் முன்னுநர் இன்றி","யாவரும் வழங்கா இடத்தில் பொருள் வேட்டுப்","பல் கலன் கொண்டு பலர் அறியாமல்","எல் வளையாளோடு அரிபுரம் எய்தி","பண்டக் கலம் பகர் சங்கமன் தன்னைக்","கண்டனர் கூறத் தையல் நின் கணவன்","பார்த்திபன் தொழில் செயும் பரதன் என்னும்","தீத் தொழிலாளன் தெற்றெனப் பற்றி","ஒற்றன் இவன் என உரைத்து மன்னற்கு","குற்றம் இலோனைக் கொலைபுரிந்திட்டனன்","ஆங்கு அவன் மனைவி அழுதனள் அரற்றி","ஏங்கி மெய்பெயர்ப்போள் இறு வரை ஏறி","இட்ட சாபம் கட்டியது ஆகும்","உம்மை வினை வந்து உருத்தல் ஒழியாது\" எனும்"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki repeats what Mathurapathi told her: in Kalinga, when the kinsmen kings Vasu of Singapuram and Kumaran of Kapilai were at war and no one crossed the land between, the merchant Sangaman came secretly with his wife to Haripuram to sell his goods, and Kovalan in a former birth, Bharatan, a man in the king's service, seized him as a spy and had the faultless man killed; his wife Neeli, wailing, climbed a hill and cursed, and that curse has now bound them, for a former deed rising in wrath does not fail to come.","n":"The line that closes the goddess's word (உம்மை வினை வந்து உருத்தல் ஒழியாது, 32) is the Silappathikaram's own phrase (உம்மை வினைவந் துருத்த காலை, 23:171), now heard from Kannaki's mouth: the wife confirms her husband's old crime herself. The Manimekalai calls Bharatan தீத் தொழிலாளன், a doer of evil work (26), and Sangaman குற்றம் இலோன், faultless (28), the very word Kannaki used of herself when she burned Madurai (குற்றமிலேன், Silappathikaram 21:42). Kapilai here (17) is the Kalinga town, not the Buddha's Kapilai of line 44, though the epic lets the one name serve both the city of the old killing and the city of the teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","saabam","piravi","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 26, lines 14-32","lb":"MM26:14-32","ev":"Mathurapathi's account, as Kannaki retells it: Bharatan, Kovalan in a former birth, has the merchant Sangaman killed as a spy, and Sangaman's widow Neeli curses those who caused her grief.","cl":[["a curse laid in grief binds those who caused the grief and comes to fruit in a later birth","general","asserted",31,"madurai_maadheyvam"],["a deed of a former birth rises in wrath and does not fail to reach its doer","general","asserted",32,"madurai_maadheyvam"]],"ag":["வசு","குமரன்","சங்கமன்","பரதன்","நீலி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதுராபதி","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"mm:27"},"mm:26:33-41":{"book":"manimekalai","num":209,"t":["மெய்ம்மைக் கிளவி விளம்பிய பின்னும்","சீற்றம் கொண்டு செழு நகர் சிதைத்தேன்","மேற் செய் நல் வினையின் விண்ணவர்ச் சென்றேம்","அவ் வினை இறுதியின் அடு சினப் பாவம்","எவ் வகையானும் எய்துதல் ஒழியாது","உம்பர் இல் வழி இம்பரில் பல் பிறப்பு","யாங்கணும் இரு வினை உய்த்து உமைப் போல","நீங்கு அரும் பிறவிக் கடலிடை நீந்தி","பிறந்தும் இறந்தும் உழல்வோம் பின்னர்"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki confesses that even after the goddess had spoken that true word she destroyed the rich city in anger; by good deeds done before, she and Kovalan went to the heavens, but when that merit is spent the sin of murderous anger will reach them by some way, and, with no heaven left to them, they will be driven by their two kinds of deeds through many births here, swimming the sea of births that is hard to leave, being born and dying.","n":"This reverses the Silappathikaram, where Kannaki raged at the city as one without fault (குற்றமிலேன், 21:42) and the fire spared the good at her command: here she calls the same act sin (அடு சினப் பாவம், 36) and says she did it after hearing the truth (மெய்ம்மைக் கிளவி விளம்பிய பின்னும், 33), so her anger was not ignorance. Heaven is a reward that runs out (அவ் வினை இறுதியின், 36), not an end. Two causes stand together: the old deed brought the loss, and her own anger is a new deed with its own fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","vinai","piravi","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 26, lines 33-41","lb":"MM26:33-41","ev":"Kannaki tells Manimekalai that she burned Madurai in anger after hearing the truth, went to heaven by former merit, and must yet suffer that anger's fruit in further births.","cl":[["anger that destroys a city is a sin whose fruit must be suffered, even when the anger was provoked","general","asserted",36,"kannaki"],["heaven won by good deeds lasts only until their merit is spent, and then the evil deed still comes to fruit","general","asserted",37,"kannaki"],["beings driven by good and evil deeds are born and die again through many births","general","asserted",39,"kannaki"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:27"},"mm:26:42-63":{"book":"manimekalai","num":210,"t":["\"மறந்தும் மழை மறா மகத நல் நாட்டுக்கு","ஒரு பெருந் திலகம்\" என்று உரவோர் உரைக்கும்","கரவு அரும் பெருமைக் கபிலை அம் பதியின்","அளப்பு அரும் பாரமிதை அளவு இன்று நிறைத்து","துளக்கம் இல் புத்த ஞாயிறு தோன்றிப்","போதிமூலம் பொருந்தி வந்தருளி","தீது அறு நால் வகை வாய்மையும் தெரிந்து","பன்னிரு சார்பின் பகுதித் தோற்றமும்","அந் நிலை எல்லாம் அழிவுறு வகையும்","இற்று என இயம்பி குற்ற வீடு எய்தி","எண் அருஞ் சக்கரவாளம் எங்கணும்","அண்ணல் அறக் கதிர் விரிக்கும்காலை","பைந்தொடி! தந்தையுடனே பகவன்","இந்திர விகாரம் ஏழும் ஏத்துதலின்","துன்பக் கதியில் தோற்றரவு இன்றி","அன்பு உறு மனத்தோடு அவன் அறம் கேட்டு","துறவி உள்ளம் தோன்றித் தொடரும்","பிறவி நீத்த பெற்றியம் ஆகுவம்","அத் திறம் ஆயினும் அநேக காலம்","எத்திறத்தார்க்கும் இருத்தியும் செய்குவம்","நறை கமழ் கூந்தல் நங்கை! நீயும்","முறைமையின் இந்த மூதூர் அகத்தே"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki foretells that in Kapilai of Magadha, the land where rain never fails, the Buddha will appear like a sun, fill the perfections beyond measure, sit at the Bodhi tree, know the four truths and the arising of the twelve links and how they all cease, declare them and reach release from fault; when he spreads the rays of his dharma through the world-systems, she and Kovalan, because they praised the seven Indra viharas of the Lord, will not be born into a state of suffering, will hear his dharma with loving minds, gain the mind of renunciation and leave birth behind, though for a long age before that they will work wonders for people of every kind; and she turns to tell her daughter what she too must do in this old city.","n":"The pathini goddess of the Silappathikaram is kept as a goddess, still giving help to all who ask (இருத்தியும் செய்குவம், 61, a word for wonder-working power), but her release is postponed to a future birth and made to depend on hearing the Buddha, not on her chastity. What saves her from a bad birth is not her karpu but her and Kovalan's worship at the seven Indra viharas (இந்திர விகாரம் ஏழும், 55). இருத்தி and the exact count of the viharas are read from the words alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["puththan","saarbu","veedu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 26, lines 42-63","lb":"MM26:42-63","ev":"Kannaki foretells the Buddha's birth and awakening and her and Kovalan's future release on hearing his dharma.","cl":[["the Buddha, having fulfilled the perfections, knew the four truths and the twelvefold dependent arising and how it ceases","buddhist","asserted",49,"kannaki"],["worship of the Buddha's shrines saves one from birth in a state of suffering","buddhist","asserted",56,"kannaki"],["hearing the Buddha's dharma with a loving mind brings the mind of renunciation and release from birth","buddhist","asserted",59,"kannaki"]],"ag":["பெருந் தவ முனிவன்","கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:27"},"mm:26:64-82":{"book":"manimekalai","num":211,"t":["அவ்வவர் சமயத்து அறி பொருள் கேட்டு","மெய் வகை இன்மை நினக்கே விளங்கிய","பின்னர் பெரியோன் பிடக நெறி கடவாய்","இன்னது இவ் இயல்பு' எனத் தாய் எடுத்து உரைத்தலும்","\"இளையள் வளையோள் என்று உனக்கு யாவரும்","விளை பொருள் உரையார் வேற்று உருக் கொள்க\" என","மை அறு சிறப்பின் தெய்வதம் தந்த","மந்திரம் ஓதி ஓர் மாதவன் வடிவு ஆய்","தேவ குலமும் தெற்றியும் பள்ளியும்","பூ மலர்ப் பொழிலும் பொய்கையும் மிடைந்து","நல் தவ முனிவரும் கற்று அடங்கினரும்","நல் நெறி காணிய தொல் நூல் புலவரும்","எங்கணும் விளங்கிய எயில் புற இருக்கையில்","செங்குட்டுவன் எனும் செங்கோல் வேந்தன்","பூத்த வஞ்சி பூவா வஞ்சியில்","போர்த் தொழில் தானை குஞ்சியில் புனைய","நில நாடு எல்லை தன் மலை நாடென்ன","கைம்மலைக் களிற்று இனம் தம்முள் மயங்க","தேரும் மாவும் செறி கழல் மறவரும்"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki closes her counsel: Manimekalai is to hear from each school what it holds, and when its lack of truth is plain to her she will not stray from the great one's Pitaka path; remembering the goddess's warning that no one would tell a young girl the truth, Manimekalai chants the mantra the deity gave and takes the form of an ascetic, and comes to the dwellings outside the walls, full of temples, platforms, monasteries, groves and pools, ascetics, the learned and old scholars seeking the good way, in the city of Senguttuvan, the king of the just sceptre who wore the vanji of war.","n":"Lines 68 and 69 are, almost word for word, the Manimekala goddess's own warning in kaathai 10 (79-80), set in quotation as Manimekalai recalls it; the speaker here is the narrator, and Kannaki's voice ends at 67. The order of study is fixed in advance: the schools are to be heard so that their falseness may show, which makes kaathai 27's survey a planned stage, not an open inquiry. Kannaki, a goddess of a Tamil cult, is the one who sends her daughter to the Pitakas.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","mandhiram","vesham","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 26, lines 64-82","lb":"MM26:64-82","ev":"Kannaki ends her counsel; Manimekalai takes an ascetic's form by the goddess's mantra and comes to the outskirts of Senguttuvan's Vanchi.","cl":[["the other schools' doctrines, once heard, prove to lack truth, after which one keeps to the Buddha's Pitaka path","buddhist","asserted",65,"kannaki"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","மணிமேகலை","செங்குட்டுவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:27"},"mm:26:83-94":{"book":"manimekalai","num":212,"t":["கார் மயங்கு கடலின் கலி கொளக் கடைஇ","கங்கை அம் பேர் யாற்று அடைகரைத் தங்கி","வங்க நாவியின் அதன் வடக்கு இழிந்து","கனக விசயர் முதல் பல வேந்தர்","அனைவரை வென்று அவர் அம் பொன் முடி மிசை","சிமையம் ஓங்கிய இமைய மால் வரைத்","தெய்வக் கல்லும் தன் திரு முடிமிசைச்","செய் பொன் வாகையும் சேர்த்திய சேரன்","வில் திறல் வெய்யோன் தன் புகழ் விளங்க","பொன் கொடிப் பெயர்ப் படூஉம் பொன் நகர்ப் பொலிந்தனள்","திருந்து நல் ஏது முதிர்ந்துளது ஆதலின்","பொருந்து நால் வாய்மையும் புலப்படுத்தற்கு என்"],"p":"","r":"The narrator praises Senguttuvan, who drove his army like a roaring sea, camped on the bank of the Ganga, crossed it northward by boat, defeated Kanaka, Vijaya and many kings, set the divine stone of the Himalaya on their golden crowns and the vaagai of victory on his own, and says that Manimekalai shone in his golden city named for the golden creeper, for the good cause had ripened that would make the four truths plain to her.","n":"The Chera's northern campaign, two long kaathais of the Silappathikaram, is folded into an epithet of the city, and its prize, the stone for Kannaki's image, is named only as a token of his victory (தெய்வக் கல், 89), though Kannaki has just spoken from her shrine. The last lines (93-94) turn from the king to the cause that is ripening in Manimekalai and run on, as the epic's kaathais do, into the next.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","ethu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 26, lines 83-94","lb":"MM26:83-94","ev":"Manimekalai, in disguise, enters Vanchi, the city of Senguttuvan, conqueror of Kanaka and Vijaya.","cl":[["the four truths become plain to one only when the fitting cause has ripened","buddhist","asserted",93,"narrator"]],"ag":["செங்குட்டுவன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:27"},"mm:27:1-24":{"book":"manimekalai","num":213,"t":["'நவை அறு நன் பொருள் உரைமினோ' என","சமயக் கணக்கர் தம் திறம் சார்ந்து","வைதிக மார்க்கத்து அளவை வாதியை","எய்தினள் எய்தி 'நின் கடைப்பிடி இயம்பு' என","'வேத வியாதனும் கிருதகோடியும்","ஏதம் இல் சைமினி எனும் இவ் ஆசிரியர்","பத்தும் எட்டும் ஆறும் பண்புறத்","தம் தம் வகையால் தாம் பகர்ந்திட்டனர்","காண்டல் கருதல் உவமம் ஆகமம்","ஆண்டைய அருத்தாபத்தியோடு இயல்பு","ஐதிகம் அபாவம் மீட்சி ஒழிவறிவு","எய்தி உண்டாம் நெறி என்று இவை தம்மால்","பொருளின் உண்மை புலங்கொளல் வேண்டும்","மருள் இல் காட்சி ஐ வகை ஆகும்","கண்ணால் வண்ணமும் செவியால் ஓசையும்","நண்ணிய மூக்கால் நாற்றமும் நாவால்","சுவையும் மெய்யால் ஊறும் எனச் சொன்ன","இவை இவை கண்டு கேட்டு உயிர்த்து உண்டு உற்று","துக்கமும் சுகமும் எனத் துயக்கு அற அறிந்து","உயிரும் வாயிலும் மனமும் ஊறு இன்றி","பயில் ஒளியொடு பொருள் இடம் பழுது இன்றி","சுட்டல் திரிதல் கவர்கோடல் தோன்றாது","கிட்டிய தேசம் நாமம் சாதி","குணம் கிரியையின் அறிவது ஆகும்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai goes among the schools' reckoners and asks the Vedic logician his doctrine; he names the teachers who counted ten, eight and six means of knowledge, lists the ten, and defines perception as fivefold sense-knowledge that, free of indefiniteness, error and doubt, grasps a thing's place, name, class, quality and action.","n":"The logician is called வைதிக மார்க்கத்து அளவை வாதி, and his authorities are Vyasa, Kritakoti and Jaimini, so the school is Vedic without being one darsana; the chapter does not tie him to Nyaya or Mimamsa. Lines 9 to 12 name what reads as eleven items for the ten promised (மீட்சி ஒழிவறிவு may be one item, and உண்டாம் நெறி is the உள்ள நெறி of line 55). The account is given by a Buddhist work and is answered in kaathai 29, where perception is stripped of name, class, quality and action.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","samayam","meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 1-24","lb":"MM27:1-24","ev":"Manimekalai comes to the Vedic logician and asks him to state his doctrine; he begins to set out the means of knowledge.","cl":[["the truth of things must be grasped through the means of valid knowledge","vedic","refuted",13,"alavai_vaadhi"],["valid perception is of five kinds, one for each sense","vedic","refuted",14,"alavai_vaadhi"],["sound perception grasps a thing's place, name, class, quality and action","vedic","refuted",24,"alavai_vaadhi"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","அளவை வாதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அளவை வாதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:25-40":{"book":"manimekalai","num":214,"t":["கருத்து அளவு ஆவது","குறிக்கொள் அனுமானத்து அனுமேயத்","தகைமை உணரும் தன்மையது ஆகும்","மூ வகை உற்று அது பொது எச்சம் முதல் ஆம்","பொது எனப்படுவது சாதன சாத்தியம்","இவை அந்நுவயம் இன்றாய் இருந்தும்","கடம் திகழ் யானைக் கான ஒலி கேட்டோன்","உடங்கு \"எழில் யானை அங்கு உண்டு\" என உணர்தல்","எச்சம் என்பது வெள்ள ஏதுவினால்","நிச்சயித்து அத் தலை மழை நிகழ்வு உரைத்தல்","முதல் என மொழிவது கருக்கொள் முகில் கண்டு","\"இது மழை பெய்யும் என இயம்பிடுதல்","என்னும் ஏதுவின் ஒன்று முக் காலம்","தன்னில் ஒன்றில் சார்ந்து உளதாகி","மண்ட உயிர் முதல் மாசு இன்றாகி","காண்டல் பொருளால் கண்டிலது உணர்தல்"],"p":"","r":"The logician defines inference as knowing the inferable through a mark, in three kinds, and gives an example of each: the unseen elephant known by its sound, rain upstream known by the flood, rain to come known from pregnant clouds; a sound reason, tied to one of the three times and free of fault, knows the unseen through the seen.","n":"The three kinds (பொது, எச்சம், முதல்) run from present sign, from effect back to cause, and from cause forward to effect. Lines 29 and 30, on the general kind holding even without concomitance (அந்நுவயம் இன்றாய் இருந்தும்), are hard to construe and the reading here is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alavai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 25-40","lb":"MM27:25-40","ev":"","cl":[["inference knows the thing to be inferred through a mark","vedic","refuted",26,"alavai_vaadhi"],["inference is of three kinds: general, from effect, and from cause","vedic","refuted",28,"alavai_vaadhi"],["a valid reason, tied to one of the three times and free of fault, knows the unseen through the seen","vedic","refuted",40,"alavai_vaadhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அளவை வாதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:41-56":{"book":"manimekalai","num":215,"t":["உவமம் ஆவது ஒப்புமை அளவை","\"கவய மா ஆப் போலும்\" எனக் கருதல்","ஆகம அளவை அறிவன் நூலால்","\"போக புவனம் உண்டு\" எனப் புலங்கொளல்","அருத்தாபத்தி \"ஆய்க்குடி கங்கை","இருக்கும்\" என்றால் கரையில் என்று எண்ணல்","இயல்பு யானைமேல் இருந்தோன் தோட்டிற்கு","அயல் ஒன்று ஈயாது அதுவே கொடுத்தல்","ஐதிகம் என்பது உலகு மறை \"இம் மரத்து","எய்தியது ஓர் பேய் உண்டு\" எனத் தெளிதல்","அபாவம் என்பது இன்மை \"ஓர் பொருளைத்","தவாது அவ் இடத்துத் தான் இலை\" என்றல்","மீட்சி என்பது \"இராமன் வென்றான்\" என","மாட்சி இல் இராவணன் தோற்றமை மதித்தல்","உள்ள நெறி என்பது \"நாராசத் திரிவில்","கொள்ளத் தகுவது காந்தம்\" எனக் கூறல்"],"p":"","r":"The logician gives the remaining means with an example each: comparison (the wild gavaya is like a cow), scripture (from the knower's book, that a world of enjoyment exists), presumption (a village 'on the Ganga' must be on its bank), aptness (the man on the elephant asking for a goad is handed the goad), tradition (a ghost haunts this tree), absence, implication (Rama won, so Ravana lost), and possibility (what turns an iron rod is a magnet).","n":"Scripture counts as its own means here, and its example is an unseen world (போக புவனம்): the logician needs it for exactly what perception and inference cannot reach, which is where the Lokayata at the chapter's end will refuse to follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","aagamam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 41-56","lb":"MM27:41-56","ev":"","cl":[["comparison is a separate means of knowledge","vedic","refuted",41,"alavai_vaadhi"],["scripture, the knower's book, gives valid knowledge of unseen worlds","vedic","refuted",43,"alavai_vaadhi"],["presumption, tradition, absence and the rest are each a means of knowledge in their own right","vedic","refuted",45,"alavai_vaadhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அளவை வாதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:57-85":{"book":"manimekalai","num":216,"t":["எட்டு உள பிரமாண ஆபாசங்கள்","சுட்டுணர்வொடு திரியக் கோடல் ஐயம்","தேராது தெளிதல் கண்டு உணராமை","எய்தும் இல் வழக்கு உணர்ந்ததை உணர்தல்","நினைப்பு என நிகழ்வ சுட்டுணர்வு எனப்படுவது","எனைப் பொருள் உண்மை மாத்திரை காண்டல்","திரியக் கோடல் ஒன்றை ஒன்று என்றல்","விரி கதிர் இப்பியை வெள்ளி என்று உணர்தல்","ஐயம் என்பது ஒன்றை நிச்சயியா","மையல் தறியோ? மகனோ? என்றல்","தேராது தெளிதல் செண்டு வெளியில்","ஓராது தறியை மகன் என உணர்தல்","கண்டு உணராமை கடு மாப் புலி ஒன்று","அண்டலை முதலிய கண்டும் அறியாமை","இல் வழக்கு என்பது முயற்கோடு ஒப்பன","சொல்லின் மாத்திரத்தால் கருத்தில் தோன்றல்","உணர்ந்ததை உணர்தல் உறு பனிக்குத் தீப்","புணர்ந்திடல் மருந்து எனப் புலம் கொள நினைத்தல்","நினைப்பு எனப்படுவது காரணம் நிகழாது","நினக்கு இவர் தாயும் தந்தையும் என்று","பிறர் சொலக் கருதல் இப் பெற்றிய அளவைகள்","பாங்குறும் உலோகாயதமே பௌத்தம்","சாங்கியம் நையாயிகம் வைசேடிகம்","மீமாஞ்சகம் ஆம் சமய ஆசிரியர்","தாம் பிருகற்பதி சினனே கபிலன்","அக்கபாதன் கணாதன் சைமினி","மெய்ப்பிரத்தியம் அனுமானம் சாத்தம்","உவமானம் அருத்தாபத்தி அபாவம்","இவையே இப்போது இயன்று உள அளவைகள்'"],"p":"","r":"The logician lists eight false semblances of knowledge, each with an example (bare awareness that something is there, shell taken for silver, 'post or man?', a post taken for a man without looking, failing to know a tiger seen, the hare's horn, knowing the known, and taking on others' word that these are your parents), then pairs six schools with their teachers and closes that six means of knowledge are current now.","n":"The list at 78 to 85 sets six schools against six teachers and six means, and is most simply read cumulatively: Lokayata with perception alone, the Buddhists with inference added, and so on to Mimamsa with absence. சினன் (Jina) is here the Buddhists' teacher, as ஆதி சினேந்திரன் is the Buddha at 29:47; it is not the Jain Tirthankara. The parents example at 75 to 77, filed here as a fallacy, is the one Manimekalai turns on the materialist at 284.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alavai","samayam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 57-85","lb":"MM27:57-85","ev":"","cl":[["there are eight false semblances of valid knowledge","vedic","refuted",57,"alavai_vaadhi"],["to hold on others' word that these are one's mother and father is memory, a false semblance of knowledge","vedic","refuted",75,"alavai_vaadhi"],["six means of knowledge, perception to absence, are those current now among the six schools","vedic","refuted",85,"alavai_vaadhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அளவை வாதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:86-95":{"book":"manimekalai","num":217,"t":["என்றவன் தன்னை விட்டு 'இறைவன் ஈசன்' என","நின்ற சைவ வாதி நேர்படுதலும்","'பரசும் நின் தெய்வம் எப்படித்து?' என்ன","'இரு சுடரோடு இயமானன் ஐம் பூதம் என்று","எட்டு வகையும் உயிரும் யாக்கையுமய்க்","கட்டி நிற்போனும் கலை உருவினோனும்","படைத்து விளையாடும் பண்பினோனும்","துடைத்துத் துயர் தீர் தோற்றத்தோனும்","தன்னில் வேறு தான் ஒன்று இலோனும்","அன்னோன் இறைவன் ஆகும்' என்று உரைத்தனன்"],"p":"","r":"Leaving the logician, Manimekalai asks the Saiva debater what his god is like; he answers that the Lord is the one who is the eight (the two lights, the sacrificer and the five elements), who binds soul and body, whose form is the arts, who creates and plays, who wipes away sorrow, and who has nothing other than himself.","n":"இரு சுடரோடு இயமானன் ஐம் பூதம் is the eight-formed god, in the oldest form of the list. தன்னில் வேறு தான் ஒன்று இலோன், the one with nothing other than himself, can be read as sole being or as one beyond any second; the line does not settle which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","samayam","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 86-95","lb":"MM27:86-95","ev":"Manimekalai leaves the logician and asks the Saiva debater what his god is like, and he describes Siva.","cl":[["God is the one who is the eight forms: sun, moon, the sacrificer and the five elements","saiva","refuted",89,"saiva_vaadhi"],["God binds the soul to the body, creates the world in play and removes sorrow","saiva","refuted",92,"saiva_vaadhi"],["God has nothing other than himself","saiva","refuted",94,"saiva_vaadhi"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","சைவ வாதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சைவ வாதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:96-107":{"book":"manimekalai","num":218,"t":["'பேர் உலகு எல்லாம்' பிரம வாதி 'ஓர்","தேவன் இட்ட முட்டை' என்றனன்","காதல் கொண்டு கடல்வணன் புராணம்","ஓதினன் 'நாரணன் காப்பு' என்று உரைத்தனன்","'கற்பம் கை சந்தம் கால் எண் கண்","தெற்றென் நிருத்தம் செவி சிக்கை மூக்கு","உற்ற வியாகரணம் முகம் பெற்றுச்","சார்பின் தோன்றா ஆரண வேதக்கு","ஆதி அந்தம் இல்லை அது நெறி' எனும்","வேதியன் உரையின் விதியும் கேட்டு","'மெய்த்திறம் வழக்கு என விளம்புகின்ற","எத் திறத்தினும் இசையாது இவர் உரை' என"],"p":"","r":"In quick succession the Brahma-vadin says the whole world is an egg laid by one god, a devotee recites the purana of the sea-coloured god and says Narayana protects, and the Vedic brahmin says the Veda, with its six limbs as hands, feet, eyes, ears, nose and face, arises from no cause and has no beginning or end; Manimekalai judges that none of their words agrees with any way of truth.","n":"The Veda is called சார்பின் தோன்றா, not arising from dependence: in a Buddhist work, whose own teaching is that everything arises by சார்பு, this is the phrase most open to attack. Manimekalai's verdict at 106 to 107 covers the whole group heard since 86 (இவர் உரை), not only the brahmin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","samayam","andam","anaadhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 96-107","lb":"MM27:96-107","ev":"The Brahma-vadin, a Vaishnava reciter and a Vedic brahmin state their views, and Manimekalai rejects them all.","cl":[["the whole world is an egg laid by one god","vedic","refuted",97,"brahma_vaadhi"],["Narayana is the protector","vaishnava","refuted",99,"vaishnava_vaadhi"],["the Veda with its six limbs arises from no cause and has neither beginning nor end, and it is the path","vedic","refuted",104,"vediyan"],["the words of these speakers agree with no way of truth or accepted usage","buddhist","asserted",107,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["பிரம வாதி","கடல்வணன் புராணம் ஓதியவன்","வேதியன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:108-119":{"book":"manimekalai","num":219,"t":["ஆசீவக நூல் அறிந்த புராணனை","'பேசும் நின் இறை யார்? நூற்பொருள் யாது?' என","'எல்லை இல் பொருள்களில் எங்கும் எப்பொழுதும்","புல்லிக் கிடந்து புலப்படுகின்ற","வரம்பு இல் அறிவன் இறை நூற்பொருள்கள் ஐந்து","உரம் தரும் உயிரோடு ஒரு நால் வகை அணு","அவ் அணு உற்றும் கண்டும் உணர்ந்திடப்","பெய் வகை கூடிப் பிரிவதும் செய்யும்","நிலம் நீர் தீ காற்று என நால் வகையின","மலை மரம் உடம்பு எனத் திரள்வதும் செய்யும்","வெவ்வேறு ஆகி விரிவதும் செய்யும்","அவ் வகை அறிவது உயிர் எனப் படுமே"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai asks Puranan, who knows the Ajivika book, for his god and his categories; he says the god is the knower without limit, present in all things at all times, and the categories are five, the soul and four kinds of atom, which touch, are seen, combine into mountain, tree and body and part again, while the soul is what knows.","n":"புராணன் may be the teacher's name (an Ajivika teacher Purana is known from other traditions) or a description, one learned in the old book; the line allows either. Four kinds of atom without a fifth for space is part of the account as given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","uyir","iraivan","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 108-119","lb":"MM27:108-119","ev":"Manimekalai asks the Ajivika teacher for his god and his doctrine, and he begins.","cl":[["the god is the knower without limit, present in all things at all times","ajivika","refuted",112,"puranan"],["there are five categories: the soul and four kinds of atom","ajivika","refuted",112,"puranan"],["atoms combine into mountains, trees and bodies and part again, while the soul is what knows","ajivika","refuted",117,"puranan"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","புராணன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["புராணன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:120-141":{"book":"manimekalai","num":220,"t":["வற்பம் ஆகி உறும் நிலம் தாழ்ந்து","சொற்படு சீதத்தொடு சுவை உடைத்தாய்","இழினென நிலம் சேர்ந்து ஆழ்வது நீர் தீத்","தெறுதலும் மேல் சேர் இயல்பும் உடைத்து ஆம்","காற்று விலங்கி அசைத்தல் கடன் இவை","வேற்று இயல்பு எய்தும் விபரீதத்தால்","ஆதி இல்லாப் பரமாணுக்கள்","தீதுற்று யாவதும் சிதைவது செய்யா","புதிதாய்ப் பிறந்து ஒன்று ஒன்றில் புகுதா","முது நீர் அணு நில அணுவாய்த் திரியா","ஒன்று இரண்டாகிப் பிளப்பதும் செய்யா","அன்றியும் அவல்போல் பரப்பதும் செய்யா","உலாவும் தாழும் உயர்வதும் செய்யும்","குலாம் மலை பிறவாக் கூடும் பலவும்","பின்னையும் பிரிந்து தம் தன்மைய ஆகும்","மன்னிய வயிரமாய்ச் செறிந்து வற்பமும் ஆம்","வேய் ஆய்த் துளைபடும் பொருளா முளைக்கும்","தேயா மதி போல் செழு நில வரைப்பு ஆம்","நிறைந்த இவ் அணுக்கள் பூதமாய் நிகழின்","குறைந்தும் ஒத்தும் கூடா வரிசையின்","ஒன்று முக்கால் அரை கால் ஆய் உறும்","துன்று மிக்கதனால் பெயர் சொலப்படுமே"],"p":"","r":"The Ajivika gives each element's nature (earth hard and low, water cool and sinking, fire burning and rising, wind moving) and says the atoms are without beginning, never destroyed, never newly born, never entering one another or turning into another kind, never splitting or spreading, yet they move, sink and rise, mass into mountains and diamonds, part again, and in a compound are named for the element that predominates.","n":"The proportions at 140 (one, three quarters, half, quarter) give a rule for naming mixtures by the larger share; the images of bamboo and the unwaning moon at 136 and 137 are examples of what massed atoms become, and their exact sense is not secure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anu","anaadhi","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 120-141","lb":"MM27:120-141","ev":"","cl":[["atoms have no beginning, are never destroyed or newly born, and never change their kind","ajivika","refuted",126,"puranan"],["a compound of atoms is named for the element that predominates in it","ajivika","refuted",141,"puranan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புராணன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:142-158":{"book":"manimekalai","num":221,"t":["இக் குணத்து அடைந்தால் அல்லது நிலன் ஆய்ச்","சிக்கென்பதுவும் நீராய் இழிவதும்","தீயாய்ச் சுடுவதும் காற்றாய் வீசலும்","ஆய தொழிலை அடைந்திடமாட்டா","ஓர் அணுத் தெய்வக் கண்ணோர் உணர்குவர்","தேரார் பூதத் திரட்சியுள் ஏனோர்","மாலைப் போதில் ஒரு மயிர் அறியார்","சாலத் திரள் மயிர் தோற்றுதல் சாலும்","கருமம் பிறப்பும் கரு நீலப் பிறப்பும்","பசும்ம் பிறப்பும் செம்ம் பிறப்பும்","பொன்ன் பிறப்பும் வெண்ண் பிறப்பும்","என்று இவ் ஆறு பிறப்பினும் மேவி","பண்புறு வரிசையின் பாற்பட்டுப் பிறந்தோர்","கழி வெண் பிறப்பில் கலந்து வீடு அணைகுவர்","அழியல் வேண்டார் அது உறற்பாலார்","இது செம்போக்கின் இயல்பு இது தப்பும்","அது மண்டலம் என்று அறியல் வேண்டும்"],"p":"","r":"The Ajivika says atoms act as earth, water, fire or wind only in these qualities; a single atom is seen only by those with divine eyes, as a single hair is not seen at dusk though a mass of hair is; and beings born through six births in order, black, dark blue, green, red, golden and white, reach release in the last white birth, a straight course they cannot fail to reach, while straying from it is the circling round.","n":"The six colour-births are the Ajivika's own scheme as this account gives it. கருமம் பிறப்பு at 150 is read as the dark birth; பசும்ம், செம்ம், பொன்ன், வெண்ண் have an added consonant as printed. அழியல் வேண்டார் அது உறற்பாலார் says those in the course need not perish and are bound to reach it: release here comes by a fixed course, not by effort.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anu","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 142-158","lb":"MM27:142-158","ev":"","cl":[["a single atom is seen only by those with divine sight","ajivika","refuted",146,"puranan"],["beings pass in order through six births by colour and reach release in the last, white birth","ajivika","refuted",155,"puranan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["புராணன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:159-170":{"book":"manimekalai","num":222,"t":["பெறுதலும் இழத்தலும் இடையூறு உறுதலும்","உறும் இடத்து எய்தலும் துக்க சுகம் உறுதலும்","பெரிது அவை நீங்கலும் பிறத்தலும் சாதலும்","கருவில் பட்ட பொழுதே கலக்கும்","இன்பமும் துன்பமும் இவையும் அணு எனத் தகும்","முன் உள ஊழே பின்னும் உறுவிப்பது","மற்கலி நூலின் வகை இது' என்ன","சொல் தடுமாற்றத் தொடர்ச்சியை விட்டு","நிகண்ட வாதியை 'நீ உரை நின்னால்","புகழும் தலைவன் யார்? நூற்பொருள் யாவை,","அப் பொருள் நிகழ்வும் கட்டும் வீடும்","மெய்ப்பட விளம்பு' என விளம்பல் உறுவோன்"],"p":"","r":"The Ajivika ends that gain and loss, hindrance, pleasure and pain, parting, birth and death are all mixed in at the moment of conception, that pleasure and pain are themselves atoms, and that the fate laid down before makes what comes after, which is the doctrine of Makkali's book; Manimekalai leaves this tangle of words and asks the Jain debater for his lord, his categories, how they occur, and bondage and release.","n":"This is the chapter's strongest statement of fate, and it is put in a rival's mouth: everything a life meets is fixed at conception, with no room for deed or effort. The Buddhist narrator's word for it, சொல் தடுமாற்றத் தொடர்ச்சி, is a dismissal, not an argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 159-170","lb":"MM27:159-170","ev":"The Ajivika finishes; Manimekalai leaves him and asks the Jain debater to state his doctrine.","cl":[["gain, loss, pleasure, pain, birth and death are all fixed at the moment of conception","ajivika","refuted",162,"puranan"],["pleasure and pain are themselves atoms","ajivika","refuted",163,"puranan"],["the fate laid down before brings about what comes after","ajivika","refuted",164,"puranan"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","நிகண்ட வாதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["புராணன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:171-182":{"book":"manimekalai","num":223,"t":["'இந்திரர் தொழப்படும் இறைவன் எம் இறைவன்","தந்த நூற்பொருள் தன்மாத்திகாயமும்","அதன்மாத்திகாயமும் கால ஆகாயமும்","தீது இல் சீவனும் பரமாணுக்களும்","நல்வினையும் தீவினையும் அவ் வினையால்","செய்வுறு பந்தமும் வீடும் இத் திறத்த","ஆன்ற பொருள் தன் தன்மையது ஆயும்","தோன்று சார்வு ஒன்றின் தன்மையது ஆயும்","அநித்தமும் நித்தமும் ஆகி நின்று","நுனித்த குணத்து ஓர் கணத்தின் கண்ணே","தோற்றமும் நிலையும் கேடும் என்னும்","மாற்று அரு மூன்றும் ஆக்கலும் உரித்தாம்"],"p":"","r":"The Jain says his lord is the one the Indras worship, and the categories he gave are dharma, adharma, time, space, the faultless soul, atoms, good and bad deeds, the bondage they cause, and release; each thing has its own nature and a nature from what it depends on, is both lasting and passing, and in every moment takes on origination, persistence and destruction.","n":"Dharma and adharma here are not merit and demerit but the media of motion and rest (as 187 to 190 make clear); merit and demerit are named separately at 175. A thing both lasting and passing is the Jain many-sided view of substance and its modes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","samayam","anu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 171-182","lb":"MM27:171-182","ev":"","cl":[["the lord is the one whom the Indras worship","jain","refuted",171,"nigantha_vaadhi"],["the categories are dharma, adharma, time, space, soul, atoms, merit, demerit, bondage and release","jain","refuted",172,"nigantha_vaadhi"],["every thing is both lasting and passing, and in each moment originates, persists and is destroyed","jain","refuted",179,"nigantha_vaadhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நிகண்ட வாதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:183-201":{"book":"manimekalai","num":224,"t":["நிம்பம் முளைத்து நிகழ்தல் நித்தியம்","நிம்பத்து அப் பொருள் அன்மை அநித்தயம்","பயற்றுத் தன்மை கெடாது கும்மாயம்","இயற்றி அப் பயறு அழிதலும் ஏதுத்","தருமாத்திகாயம் தான் எங்கும் உளதாய்","பொருள்களை நடத்தும் பொருந்த நித்தியமா","அப்படித்தாகி அதன் மாத்திகாயமும்","எப் பொருள்களையும் நிறுத்தல் இயற்றும்","காலம் கணிகம் எனும் குறு நிகழ்ச்சியும்","ஏலும் கற்பத்தின் நெடு நிகழ்ச்சியும்","ஆக்கும் ஆகாயம் எல்லாப் பொருட்கும்","பூக்கும் இடம் கொடுக்கும் புரிவிற்று ஆகும்","சீவன் உடம்போடு ஒத்துக் கூடி","தா இல் சுவை முதலிய புலன்களை நுகரும்","ஓர் அணு புற்கலம் புற உரு ஆகும்","சீர்சால் நல்வினை தீவினை அவை செயும்","வரு வழி இரண்டையும் மாற்றி முன்செய்","அரு வினைப் பயன் அனுபவித்து அறுத்திடுதல்","அது வீடு ஆகும்' என்றனன் அவன்பின்"],"p":"","r":"The Jain illustrates with the sprouting neem and with gram cooked into a dish (the kind persists while the form passes), says dharma pervades all and makes things move, adharma makes them stay, time is the instant and the aeon, space gives room to all, the soul joins a body and enjoys the senses, the atom is matter's outward form, and release is to block the inflow of both good and bad deeds and wear out the fruit of past deeds by living it through.","n":"Release needs no grace and no god here: it is the stopping of new deeds and the exhausting of old ones. Both good and bad deeds bind (இரண்டையும் மாற்றி). Line 190's number in the source carries the kaathai number (227-190).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","uyir","anu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 183-201","lb":"MM27:183-201","ev":"","cl":[["dharma pervades all and makes things move; adharma makes them stay","jain","refuted",187,"nigantha_vaadhi"],["the soul joins a body and through it enjoys the senses","jain","refuted",195,"nigantha_vaadhi"],["release is to block the inflow of both good and bad deeds and exhaust past deeds by experiencing their fruit","jain","refuted",199,"nigantha_vaadhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["நிகண்ட வாதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:202-226":{"book":"manimekalai","num":225,"t":["'இது சாங்கிய மதம்' என்று எடுத்து உரைப்போன்","'தனை அறிவு அரிதாய் தான் முக் குணமாய்","மன நிகழ்வு இன்றி மாண்பு அமை பொதுவாய்","எல்லாப் பொருளும் தோன்றுதற்கு இடம் எனச்","சொல்லுதல் மூலப் பகுதி சித்தத்து","மான் என்று உரைத்த புத்தி வெளிப்பட்டு","அதன்கண் ஆகாயம் வெளிப்பட்டு அதன்கண்","வாயு வெளிப்பட்டு அதன்கண் அங்கி","ஆனது வெளிப்பட்டு அதன்கண் அப்பின்","தன்மை வெளிப்பட்டு அதில் மண் வெளிப்பட்டு","அவற்றின் கூட்டத்தில் மனம் வெளிப்பட்டு","ஆர்ப்புறு மனத்து ஆங்கார விகாரமும்","ஆகாயத்தில் செவி ஒலி விகாரமும்","வாயுவில் தொக்கும் ஊறு எனும் விகாரமும்","அங்கியில் கண்ணும் ஒளியும் ஆம் விகாரமும்","தங்கிய அப்பில் வாய் சுவை எனும் விகாரமும்","நிலக்கண் மூக்கு நாற்ற விகாரமும்","சொலப்பட்ட இவற்றில் தொக்கு விகாரமாய்","வாக்கு பாணி பாதம் பாயுரு உபத்தம் என","ஆக்கிய இவை வெளிப்பட்டு இங்கு அறைந்த","பூத விகாரத்தால் மலை மரம் முதல்","ஓதிய வெளிப்பட்டு உலகாய் நிகழ்ந்து","வந்த வழியே இவை சென்று அடங்கி","அந்தம் இல் பிரளயம் ஆய் இறும் அளவும்","ஒன்றாய் எங்கும் பரந்து நித்தியம் ஆம்"],"p":"","r":"The Sankhya speaker says primal nature is hard to know, made of the three qualities, without mind, the common ground from which all things appear; from it in turn appear intellect, space, wind, fire, water, earth, mind and the sense of self, then the senses and their objects from each element and the five organs of action, and from the elements the world of mountains and trees, which returns the way it came into dissolution while primal nature stays one, pervading and eternal.","n":"The order given (intellect, then the elements, then mind and the sense of self) is not the order of the classical Sankhya texts, where the sense of self comes before the senses and elements; the Tamil account should not be taken as the school's own sequence. No creator is named: nature unfolds and withdraws by itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","mukkunam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 202-226","lb":"MM27:202-226","ev":"","cl":[["primal nature, made of the three qualities and without mind, is the ground from which all things appear","vedic","refuted",206,"sankhya_vaadhi"],["intellect, the elements, mind, the sense of self, the senses and the organs of action unfold from primal nature in order","vedic","refuted",207,"sankhya_vaadhi"],["the world returns the way it came into dissolution, while primal nature remains one, pervading and eternal","vedic","refuted",224,"sankhya_vaadhi"]],"ag":["சாங்கிய மதம் உரைப்போன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாங்கிய மதம் உரைப்போன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:227-240":{"book":"manimekalai","num":226,"t":["அறிதற்கு எளிதாய் முக் குணம் அன்றி","பொறி உணர்விக்கும் பொதுவும் அன்றி","எப் பொருளும் தோன்றுதற்கு இடம் அன்றி","அப் பொருள் எல்லாம் அறிந்திடற்கு உணர்வாய்","ஒன்றாய் எங்கும் பரந்து நித்தியமாய்","நின்று உள உணர்வாய் நிகழ்தரும் புருடன்","புலம் ஆர் பொருள்கள் இருபத்தைந்து உள","நிலம் நீர் தீ வளி ஆகாயம்மே","மெய் வாய் கண் மூக்கு செவி தாமே","உறு சுவை ஒளி ஊறு ஓசை நாற்றம்மே","வாக்கு பாணி பாதம் பாயுரு உபத்தம்","ஆக்கும் மனோ புத்தி ஆங்கார சித்தம்","உயிர் எனும் ஆன்மா ஒன்றொடும் ஆம்' எனச்","செயிர் அறச் செப்பிய திறமும் கேட்டு"],"p":"","r":"The Sankhya speaker says the person (purusha) is unlike nature: not of the three qualities, not the ground of things, but the awareness that knows them all, one, pervading and eternal; the principles are twenty-five, the five elements, five sense organs, five objects, five organs of action, mind, intellect, the sense of self, thought, and the soul.","n":"The count of twenty-five here lists thought (சித்தம்) among the inner organs and ends with the soul, without naming primal nature in the list, so the twenty-five are not quite the classical list. அறிதற்கு எளிதாய் at 227 sets purusha against nature, which was hard to know at 203.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","uyir","mukkunam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 227-240","lb":"MM27:227-240","ev":"","cl":[["the person is pure awareness, not of the three qualities, one, pervading and eternal","vedic","refuted",232,"sankhya_vaadhi"],["there are twenty-five principles, ending in the soul","vedic","refuted",233,"sankhya_vaadhi"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாங்கிய மதம் உரைப்போன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:241-262":{"book":"manimekalai","num":227,"t":["'வைசேடிக! நின் வழக்கு உரை' என்ன","'பொய் தீர் பொருளும் குணமும் கருமமும்","சாமானியமும் விசேடமும் கூட்டமும்","ஆம் ஆறு கூறு ஆம் அதில் பொருள் என்பது","குணமும் தொழிலும் உடைத்தாய் எத் தொகைப்","பொருளுக்கும் ஏது ஆம் அப் பொருள் ஒன்பான்","ஞாலம் நீர் தீ வளி ஆகாயம் திசை","காலம் ஆன்மா மனம் இவற்றுள் நிலம்","ஒலி ஊறு நிறம் சுவை நாற்றமொடு ஐந்தும்","பயில் குணம் உடைத்து நின்ற நான்கும்","சுவை முதல் ஒரோ குணம் அவை குறைவு உடைய","ஓசை ஊறு நிறம் நாற்றம் சுவை","மாசு இல் பெருமை சிறுமை வன்மை","மென்மை சீர்மை நொய்ம்மை வடிவம்","என்னும் நீர்மை பக்கம் முதல் அனேகம்","கண்ணிய பொருளின் குணங்கள் ஆகும்","பொருளும் குணமும் கருமம் இயற்றற்கு","உரிய உண்மை தரும் முதல் பொதுத்தான்","போதலும் நிற்றலும் பொதுக் குணம் ஆதலின்","சாதலும் நிகழ்தலும் அப் பொருள் தன்மை","ஒன்று அணு கூட்டம் குணமும் குணியும்' என்று","ஒன்றிய வாதியும் உரைத்தனன் உடனே"],"p":"","r":"Asked for his doctrine, the Vaisheshika says there are six categories, substance, quality, action, generality, particularity and inherence; substance bears quality and action and is the cause of all compounds, and is ninefold (earth, water, fire, wind, space, direction, time, self, mind); earth has all five qualities and the other elements fewer; and generality is the common being that lets substance and quality act.","n":"Line 249 gives earth sound among its five qualities; the Vaisheshika's own texts give sound to space alone, so this may be the account's slip or a different count. Lines 257 to 261 on generality are compressed and their sense is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","anu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 241-262","lb":"MM27:241-262","ev":"Manimekalai asks the Vaisheshika for his doctrine and he sets it out.","cl":[["there are six categories: substance, quality, action, generality, particularity and inherence","vedic","refuted",242,"vaisheshika_vaadhi"],["substance bears quality and action and is ninefold: earth, water, fire, wind, space, direction, time, self and mind","vedic","refuted",246,"vaisheshika_vaadhi"],["earth has five qualities and the other elements fewer","vedic","refuted",249,"vaisheshika_vaadhi"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","வைசேடிகன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வைசேடிகன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:263-276":{"book":"manimekalai","num":228,"t":["'பூத வாதியைப் புகல் நீ' என்னத்","'தாதகிப் பூவும் கட்டியும் இட்டு","மற்றும் கூட்ட மதுக் களி பிறந்தாங்கு","உற்றிடும் பூதத்து உணர்வு தோன்றிடும்","அவ் உணர்வு அவ் அப் பூதத்து அழிவுகளின்","வெவ் வேறு பிரியும் பறை ஓசையின் கெடும்","உயிரொடும் கூட்டிய உணர்வு உடைப் பூதமும்","உயிர் இல்லாத உணர்வு இல் பூதமும்","அவ் அப் பூத வழி அவை பிறக்கும்","மெய் வகை இதுவே வேறு உரை விகற்பமும்","உண்மைப் பொருளும் உலோகாயதன் உணர்வே","கண்கூடு அல்லது கருத்து அளவு அழியும்","இம்மையும் இம்மைப் பயனும் இப் பிறப்பே","பொய்ம்மை மறுமை உண்டாய் வினை துய்த்தல்'"],"p":"","r":"The materialist says that as intoxication arises when dhataki flowers and jaggery are mixed, awareness arises in the combined elements and is lost when they part, like the sound of a drum dying away; beings with and without awareness both come from the elements; perception alone is valid and inference fails; this life and its fruits are all there is, and a next world where deeds are eaten is a lie.","n":"The intoxication simile is the materialist's emblem in Tamil polemic: the Jain Neelakesi puts the same image in his mouth. Line 273 makes the materialist's own understanding the measure of truth, which is the point Manimekalai will press.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","vinai","thaththuvam","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 263-276","lb":"MM27:263-276","ev":"Manimekalai calls on the materialist, and he sets out his doctrine.","cl":[["awareness arises from the elements combined, as intoxication from mixed ingredients","lokayata","refuted",266,"bhuta_vaadhi"],["awareness perishes when the elements separate, like the fading sound of a drum","lokayata","refuted",268,"bhuta_vaadhi"],["perception alone is a means of knowledge; inference fails","lokayata","refuted",274,"bhuta_vaadhi"],["there is only this life; a next world where deeds bear fruit is false","lokayata","refuted",276,"bhuta_vaadhi"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","அறைந்தோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறைந்தோன்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:277-283":{"book":"manimekalai","num":229,"t":["என்றலும் எல்லா மார்க்கமும் கேட்டு","'நன்று அல ஆயினும் நான் மாறு உரைக்கிலேன்","பிறந்த முன் பிறப்பை எய்தப் பெறுதலின்","அறிந்தோர் உண்டோ?' என்று நக்கிடுதலும்","'தெய்வ மயக்கினும் கனா உறு திறத்தினும்","மையல் உறுவார் மனம் வேறு ஆம் வகை","ஐயம் அன்றி இல்லை' என்றலும் 'நின்"],"p":"","r":"Having heard every path, Manimekalai says that though they are not good she will not answer them, and laughing asks whether there are not those who have come to know a birth before this one; the materialist replies that the minds of those deluded by a god or by dreams are changed, and such knowledge is nothing but doubt.","n":"The speakers of 278 to 283 are not named and are fixed here from the sense: the laughing question suits Manimekalai, who learnt her own former birth in kaathai 9, and the rejoinder suits the materialist. His answer names the very means of her knowledge: a deity carried her off and she woke at the Buddha's seat.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","kanavu","samayam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 277-283","lb":"MM27:277-283","ev":"Manimekalai, having heard all the schools, asks the materialist whether no one knows a former birth, and he dismisses such knowledge as delusion.","cl":[["some have come to know a birth before this one","buddhist","asserted",279,"manimekalai"],["knowledge of a former birth comes from possession by a god or from dreams, and is mere doubt","lokayata","refuted",283,"bhuta_vaadhi"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","அறைந்தோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","அறைந்தோன்"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:27:284-289":{"book":"manimekalai","num":230,"t":["தந்தை தாயரை அனுமானத்தால் அலது","இந்த ஞாலத்து எவ் வகை அறிவாய்?","மெய்யுணர்வு இன்றி மெய்ப் பொருள் உணர்வு அரிய","ஐயம் அல்லது இது சொல்லப் பெறாய்' என","உள்வரிக் கோலமோடு உன்னிய பொருள் உரைத்து","ஐவகைச் சமயமும் அறிந்தனள் ஆங்கு என்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai answers that he knows his own father and mother only by inference, that without true understanding the true nature of things is hard to know, and that on his terms his own claim can be stated only as doubt; so, speaking in her assumed form, she learned the five kinds of religion.","n":"The answer turns the materialist's rule on itself: a man who accepts perception alone cannot know his own parents, so his denial of past births is no firmer than the knowledge he calls doubt. The parents example was a fallacy of memory in the logician's list (75 to 77); here it is an inference no one can do without. ஐவகைச் சமயம் counts five where the chapter heard more speakers; which five is not said.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","meyyunarvu","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 27, lines 284-289","lb":"MM27:284-289","ev":"Manimekalai refutes the materialist and, having heard them all, comes to know the five kinds of religion.","cl":[["one knows one's own father and mother only by inference","buddhist","asserted",284,"manimekalai"],["without true understanding the true nature of things is hard to know","buddhist","asserted",286,"manimekalai"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மணிமேகலை","அறைந்தோன்"],"ch":"mm:28"},"mm:28:1-16":{"book":"manimekalai","num":231,"t":["ஆங்கு தாயரோடு அறவணர்த் தேர்ந்து","வாங்கு வில் தானை வானவன் வஞ்சியின்","வேற்று மன்னரும் உழிஞை வெம் படையும்","போல் புறம் சுற்றிய புறக்குடி கடந்து","சுருங்கைத் தூம்பின் மனை வளர் தோகையர்","கருங் குழல் கழீஇய கலவை நீரும்","எந்திர வாவியில் இளைஞரும் மகளிரும்","தம் தமில் ஆடிய சாந்து கழி நீரும்","புவி காவலன் தன் புண்ணிய நல் நாள்","சிவிறியும் கொம்பும் சிதறு விரை நீரும்","மேலை மாதவர் பாதம் விளக்கும்","சீல உபாசகர் செங் கை நறு நீரும்","அறம் செய் மாக்கள் அகில் முதல் புகைத்து","நிறைந்த பந்தல் தசும்பு வார் நீரும்","உறுப்பு முரண் உறாமல் கந்த உத்தியினால்","செறித்து அரைப்போர் தம் செழு மனை நீரும்"],"p":"","r":"Seeking her mothers and Aravanar, Manimekalai leaves the schools' hall and passes through the outer quarters that ring the Chera's Vanji like besieging kings, where the waters of many uses all run together: hair-wash from the houses, the spent sandal water of the pleasure tanks, the scented sprays of the king's birthday, the water lay devotees pour on the monks' feet, the rain from charity pandals and the perfume-grinders' wash.","n":"The list sets the monks' foot-washing (lines 11 and 12) among the city's ordinary waters: Vanji is shown as a city where lay Buddhist practice is already part of the daily scene, which prepares for the monastery she finds at line 72.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 1-16","lb":"MM28:1-16","ev":"Manimekalai, looking for her mothers and Aravanar, passes the outer quarters of Vanji.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:17-30":{"book":"manimekalai","num":232,"t":["என்று இந் நீரே எங்கும் பாய்தலின்","கன்றிய கராமும் இடங்கரும் மீன்களும்","ஒன்றிய புலவு ஒழி உடம்பின ஆகி","தாமரை குவளை கழுநீர் ஆம்பல்","பூமிசைப் பரந்து பொறி வண்டு ஆர்ப்ப","இந்திர தனு என இலங்கு அகழ் உடுத்து","வந்து எறி பொறிகள் வகை மாண்பு உடைய","கடி மதில் ஓங்கிய இடைநிலை வரைப்பில்","பசு மிளை பரந்து பல் தொழில் நிறைந்த","வெள்ளிக் குன்றம் உள் கிழிந்து அன்ன","நெடு நிலைதோறும் நிலாச் சுதை மலரும்","கொடி மிடை வாயில் குறுகினள் புக்கு","கடை காப்பு அமைந்த காவலாளர்","மிடைகொண்டு இயங்கும் வியன் மலி மறுகும்"],"p":"","r":"Those mingled waters flow into the moat so that its crocodiles and fish lose their stink of flesh and its lotuses and lilies hum with bees, and beyond the moat girdling the city like a rainbow and the walls fitted with engines she reaches the bannered gate and goes in among the guards to the broad crowded street.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 17-30","lb":"MM28:17-30","ev":"Manimekalai crosses the moat and walls of Vanji and enters by its gate.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:31-45":{"book":"manimekalai","num":233,"t":["பல் மீன் விலைஞர் வெள் உப்புப் பகருநர்","கள் நொடையாட்டியர் காழியர் கூவியர்","மைந் நிண விலைஞர் பாசவர் வாசவர்","என்னுநர் மறுகும் இருங் கோவேட்களும்","செம்பு செய்ஞ்ஞ்அரும் கஞ்சகாரரும்","பைம்பொன் செய்ஞ்ஞ்அரும் பொன் செய் கொல்லரும்","மரம் கொல் தச்சரும் மண்ணீட்டாளரும்","வரம் தர எழுதிய ஓவிய மாக்களும்","தோலின் துன்னரும் துன்ன வினைஞரும்","மாலைக்காரரும் காலக் கணிதரும்","நலம் தரு பண்ணும் திறனும் வாய்ப்ப","நிலம் கலம் கண்டம் நிகழக் காட்டும்","பாணர் என்று இவர் பல் வகை மறுகும்","விலங்கரம் பொரூஉம் வெள் வளை போழ்நரோடு","இலங்கு மணி வினைஞ்அர் இரீஇய மறுகும்"],"p":"","r":"The narrator lists the streets of Vanji's trades: fish-sellers, salt-sellers, toddy-women, cake-makers, meat-sellers, potters, copper-, brass- and goldsmiths, carpenters, clay-modellers, painters, leather-workers, tailors, garland-makers, astrologers, bards who show the modes on the lute, and the shell-cutters and gem-workers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 31-45","lb":"MM28:31-45","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:46-61":{"book":"manimekalai","num":234,"t":["வேத்தியல் பொது இயல் என்று இவ் இரண்டின்","கூத்து இயல்பு அறிந்த கூத்தியர் மறுகும்","பால் வேறு ஆக எண் வகைப் பட்ட","கூலம் குவைஇய கூல மறுகும்","மாகதர் சூதர் வேதாளிகர் மறுகும்","போகம் புரக்கும் பொதுவர் பொலி மறுகும்","கண் நுழைகல்லா நுண் நூல் கைவினை","வண்ண அறுவையர் வளம் திகழ் மறுகும்","பொன் உரை காண்போர் நல் மனை மறுகும்","பல் மணி பகர்வோர் மன்னிய மறுகும்","மறையோர் அருந் தொழில் குறையா மறுகும்","அரைசு இயல் மறுகும் அமைச்சு இயல் மறுகும்","எனைப் பெருந் தொழில் செய் ஏனோர் மறுகும்","மன்றமும் பொதியிலும் சந்தியும் சதுக்கமும்","புதுக் கோள் யானையும் பொன் தார்ப் புரவியும்","கதிக்கு உற வடிப்போர் கவின் பெறு வீதியும்"],"p":"","r":"The catalogue goes on to the streets of the dancers who know both court and common styles, of the eight kinds of grain, of bards and panegyrists, of herders, weavers of fine cloth, gold-assayers and gem-sellers, the Brahmins' street, the streets of the king's and ministers' households, the halls, squares and crossroads, and the avenues where new elephants and horses are trained.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 46-61","lb":"MM28:46-61","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:62-90":{"book":"manimekalai","num":235,"t":["சேண் ஓங்கு அருவி தாழ்ந்த செய்குன்றமும்","வேணவா மிகுக்கும் விரை மரக் காவும்","விண்ணவர் தங்கள் விசும்பு இடம் மறந்து","நண்ணுதற்கு ஒத்த நல் நீர் இடங்களும்","சாலையும் கூடமும் தமனியப் பொதியிலும்","கோலம் குயின்ற கொள்கை இடங்களும்","கண்டு மகிழ்வுற்று கொண்ட வேடமோடு","அந்தர சாரிகள் அமர்ந்து இனிது உறையும்","இந்திர விகாரம் என எழில் பெற்று","நவை அறு நாதன் நல் அறம் பகர்வோர்","உறையும் பள்ளி புக்கு இறை வளை நல்லாள்","கோவலன் தாதை மா தவம் புரிந்தோன்","பாதம் பணிந்து தன் பாத்திர தானமும்","தானப் பயத்தால் சாவக மன்னவன்","ஊனம் ஒன்று இன்றி உலகு ஆள் செல்வமும்","செல்வற் கொணர்ந்து அத் தீவகப் பீடிகை","ஒல்காது காட்ட பிறப்பினை உணர்ந்ததும்","உணர்ந்தோன் முன்னர் உயர் தெய்வம் தோன்றி","மனம் கவல் கெடுத்ததும் மா நகர் கடல் கொள","அறவண அடிகளும் தாயரும் ஆங்கு விட்டு","இறவாது இப் பதிப் புகுந்தது கேட்டதும்","சாவக மன்னன் தன் நாடு எய்த","தீவகம் விட்டு இத் திரு நகர் புகுந்ததும்","புக்க பின் அந்தப் பொய் உருவுடனே","தக்க சமயிகள் தம் திறம் கேட்டதும்","அவ்வவர் சமயத்து அறி பொருள் எல்லாம்","செவ்விது அன்மையின் சிந்தை வையாததும்","நாதன் நல் அறம் கேட்டலை விரும்பி","மாதவன் தேர்ந்து வந்த வண்ணமும்"],"p":"","r":"Past artificial hills, groves and pools that the gods might leave heaven for, Manimekalai enters a monastery of the Buddha's preachers, fair as an Indra vihara where sky-walkers delight to dwell, bows at the feet of Kovalan's father, now a great ascetic, and recounts her life since the bowl: the gift of the bowl, her former birth shown by the seat, the goddess's comfort, her coming to Vanji after the sea took Puhar, and hearing the schools in a false form and finding none of them straight.","n":"Lines 74 to 90 are a summary of kaathais 11 to 27 in her own report. The monastery is likened to an இந்திர விகாரம் with அந்தர சாரிகள் (69 to 70), the very words the Silappathikaram uses for the place where Kovalan's father renounced in Puhar. Line 85 keeps her in the பொய் உரு she took to hear the schools; she drops it only at line 245.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","vazhipaadu","piravi","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 62-90","lb":"MM28:62-90","ev":"Manimekalai enters a Buddhist monastery in Vanji, bows to Kovalan's father, now an ascetic, and tells him her story.","cl":[["the doctrines of the other schools are not sound and deserve no assent","buddhist","asserted",88,"narrator"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","மாசாத்துவான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:91-100":{"book":"manimekalai","num":236,"t":["சொல்லினள் ஆதலின் 'தூயோய்! நின்னை என்","நல்வினைப் பயன்கொல் நான் கண்டது?' எனத்","'தையல்' கேள் நின் தாதையும் தாயும்","செய்த தீவினையின் செழு நகர் கேடுற","துன்புற விளிந்தமை கேட்டுச் சுகதன்","அன்பு கொள் அறத்திற்கு அருகனேன் ஆதலின்","மனைத்திறவாழ்க்கையை மாயம் என்று உணர்ந்து","தினைத்தனை ஆயினும் செல்வமும் யாக்கையும்","நிலையா என்றே நிலைபெற உணர்ந்தே","மலையா அறத்தின் மா தவம் புரிந்தேன்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai asks the pure one whether seeing him is the fruit of her good deeds, and he answers that when he heard her father and mother had died in sorrow as their evil deeds ruined the city, he, fit for the loving dharma of the Sugata, knew household life for an illusion and wealth and body for things that do not last a moment, and took up the great austerity of unshaken dharma.","n":"Her mother Madhavi is alive, so the தாய் who died with her father must be Kannaki, counted here as Manimekalai's mother. The Silappathikaram gives grief and a great gift as the cause of his renunciation; here he gives the reason in doctrine, that home is மாயம் and wealth and body do not stand, and he makes his fitness for the Sugata's dharma part of the cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","nilaiyaamai","maayai","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 91-100","lb":"MM28:91-100","ev":"Manimekalai asks the ascetic whether meeting him is her good deeds' fruit; he tells her he renounced on hearing of her parents' deaths.","cl":[["Kovalan and Kannaki perished in sorrow through the evil deeds they themselves had done","buddhist","asserted",94,"maasaathuvan"],["household life is an illusion, and wealth and the body do not last even a moment","buddhist","asserted",97,"maasaathuvan"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","மாசாத்துவான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாசாத்துவான்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:101-119":{"book":"manimekalai","num":237,"t":["புரிந்த யான் இப் பூங் கொடிப் பெயர்ப் படூஉம்","திருந்திய நல் நகர் சேர்ந்தது கேளாய்","குடக் கோச் சேரலன் குட்டுவர் பெருந்தகை","விடர்ச் சிலை பொறித்த வேந்தன் முன் நாள்","துப்பு அடு செவ் வாய்த் துடி இடையாரொடும்","இப் பொழில் புகுந்து ஆங்கு இருந்த எல்லையுள்","இலங்கா தீவத்துச் சமனொளி என்னும்","சிலம்பினை எய்தி வலம் கொண்டு மீளும்","தரும சாரணர் தங்கிய குணத்தோர்","கரு முகில் படலத்துக் ககனத்து இயங்குவோர்","அரைசற்கு ஏது அவ் வழி நிகழ்தலின்","புரையோர் தாமும் இப் பூம்பொழில் இழிந்து","கல் தலத்து இருந்துழி காவலன்விரும்பி","முன் தவம் உடைமையின் முனிகளை ஏத்திப்","பங்கயச் சேவடி விளக்கி பான்மையின்","அங்கு அவர்க்கு அறு சுவை நால் வகை அமிழ்தம்","பாத்திரத்து அளித்துப் பலபல சிறப்பொடு","வேத்தவையாரொடும் ஏத்தினன் இறைஞ்சலின்","பிறப்பின் துன்பமும் பிறவா இன்பமும்"],"p":"","r":"He tells her how he came to Vanji: long ago a Chera king of the Kuttuvar, who carved the bow on the mountain cleft, was in this grove with his women when Dharma charanas, sky-going sages returning from circling the peak Samanoli in Lanka, came down and sat on its rock; the king, by austerity done before, praised them, washed their lotus feet, fed them the six-flavoured food of four kinds from a vessel and bowed to them with his court.","n":"The king is named only by his line and the epithet விடர்ச் சிலை பொறித்த வேந்தன், the bow carved in the cleft; the Tamil does not name him. The sages are தரும சாரணர், Buddhist counterparts of the Jain charanas of the Silappathikaram, and Samanoli (the peak now called Adam's Peak) is their place of worship. The king's welcome, feet washed and six tastes served, is the pattern Manimekalai follows at lines 239 to 243.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vazhipaadu","thavam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 101-119","lb":"MM28:101-119","ev":"A Chera king of old meets sky-going Dharma charanas descending into this grove, washes their feet, feeds them and worships them.","cl":[["honouring holy sages comes to a king through austerity done in a former life","buddhist","asserted",114,"maasaathuvan"]],"ag":["குடக் கோச் சேரலன்","தரும சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாசாத்துவான்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:120-137":{"book":"manimekalai","num":238,"t":["அறத்தகை முதல்வன் அருளிய வாய்மை","இன்ப ஆர் அமுது இறைவன் செவிமுதல்","துன்பம் நீங்கச் சொரியும் அந் நாள்","நின் பெருந் தாதைக்கு ஒன்பது வழி முறை","முன்னோன் கோவலன் மன்னவன் தனக்கு","நீங்காக் காதல் பாங்கன் ஆதலின்","தாங்க நல் அறம் தானும் கேட்டு","முன்னோர் முறைமையின் படைத்ததை அன்றி","தன்னான் இயன்ற தனம் பல கோடி","எழு நாள் எல்லையுள் இரவலர்க்கு ஈத்து","தொழு தவம் புரிந்தோன் சுகதற்கு இயற்றிய","வான் ஓங்கு சிமையத்து வால் ஒளிச் சயித்தம்","ஈனோர்க்கு எல்லாம் இடர் கெட இயன்றது","கண்டு தொழுது ஏத்தும் காதலின் வந்து இத்","தண்டாக் காட்சித் தவத்தோர் அருளிக்","\"காவிரிப் பட்டினம் கடல் கொளும்\" என்ற அத்","தூ உரை கேட்டுத் துணிந்து இவண் இருந்தது","இன்னும் கேளாய் நல் நெறி மாதே!"],"p":"","r":"The charanas poured the Buddha's truth, the suffering of birth and the bliss of no birth, into the king's ear; Kovalan, the king's dear friend and Manimekalai's ancestor nine generations back, heard it too, gave away in seven days the crores he had earned himself, beyond his inheritance, became an ascetic and built a towering white caitya to the Sugata for the relief of all who come after; Kovalan's father came to worship it, heard its ascetics foretell that the sea would take Kaveripattinam, and stayed here.","n":"This Kovalan is a namesake ancestor (ஒன்பது வழி முறை முன்னோன்), not Manimekalai's father; the epic gives the family a Buddhist past in Vanji. The distinction at lines 127 to 128 is exact: he gave away what he had earned, not what the ancestors had laid up. The prophecy of the flood (line 135) is why the ascetic is in Vanji and not in drowned Puhar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thuravu","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 120-137","lb":"MM28:120-137","ev":"Kovalan's ancestor hears the charanas, gives away his wealth, renounces and builds a caitya; Kovalan's father, come to worship there, is told Puhar will be drowned and stays in Vanji.","cl":[["birth is suffering and freedom from birth is bliss, as the first teacher of dharma taught","buddhist","asserted",120,"maasaathuvan"],["a caitya to the Buddha is raised to end the distress of all who come after","buddhist","asserted",132,"maasaathuvan"]],"ag":["தரும சாரணர்","குடக் கோச் சேரலன்","முன்னோன் கோவலன்","மாசாத்துவான்","தவத்தோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாசாத்துவான்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:138-149":{"book":"manimekalai","num":239,"t":["\"தீவினை உருப்பச் சென்ற நின் தாதையும்","தேவரில் தோற்றி முன்செய் தவப் பயத்தால்","ஆங்கு அத் தீவினை இன்னும் துய்த்துப்","பூங்கொடி! முன்னவன் போதியில் நல் அறம்","தாங்கிய தவத்தால் தான் தவம் தாங்கிக்","காதலி தன்னொடு கபிலை அம் பதியில்","நாதன் நல் அறம் கேட்டு வீடு எய்தும்\" என்று","அற்புதக் கிளவி அறிந்தோர் கூறச்","சொல் பயன் உணர்ந்தேன் தோகை! யானும்","அந் நாள் ஆங்கு அவன் அற நெறி கேட்குவன்","நின்னது தன்மை அந் நெடு நிலைக் கந்தில் துன்னிய","துவதிகன் உரையின் துணிந்தனை அன்றோ?"],"p":"","r":"He gives the seers' word: her father, who went where his evil deed ripened, has appeared among the gods by the fruit of former austerity, yet must still eat that evil deed, and by the austerity he bore under the Bodhi of the earlier Buddha he will take up austerity again and with his beloved reach release by hearing the Lord's dharma at Kapilai; understanding this, the ascetic resolves to hear that dharma on that day too, and asks whether she did not learn her own nature from Thuvathikan in the pillar.","n":"Merit and evil deed run side by side here: heaven is earned and the evil deed is not cancelled by it, only deferred. Lines 141 to 142 are compressed (முன்னவன் போதியில் நல் அறம் தாங்கிய தவத்தால் தான் தவம் தாங்கி), and whose earlier Bodhi is meant is not fixed. Line 148 carries more words than an akaval line and may join two lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","marumai","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 138-149","lb":"MM28:138-149","ev":"The ascetic reports the seers' prophecy of Kovalan's heaven, remaining karma and future release at Kapilai, and vows to hear the Buddha then himself.","cl":[["an evil deed that has ripened must still be eaten even by one reborn among the gods for former merit","buddhist","asserted",140,"vanji_thavaththor"],["austerity kept under a former Buddha's Bodhi bears fruit in a later birth","buddhist","asserted",142,"vanji_thavaththor"],["release is reached by hearing the Buddha's dharma","buddhist","asserted",144,"vanji_thavaththor"]],"ag":["தவத்தோர்","மாசாத்துவான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாசாத்துவான்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:150-161":{"book":"manimekalai","num":240,"t":["தவ நெறி அறவணன் சாற்றக் கேட்டனன்","ஆங்கு அவன் தானும் நின் அறத்திற்கு ஏது","பூங்கொடி! கச்சி மா நகர் ஆதலின்","மற்று அம் மா நகர் மாதவன் பெயர் நாள்","பொன் தொடி தாயரும் அப் பதிப் படர்ந்தனர்","அன்னதை அன்றியும் அணி இழை! கேளாய்","பொன் எயில் காஞ்சி நாடு கவின் அழிந்து","மன் உயிர் மடிய மழை வளம் கரத்தலின்","அந் நகர் மாதவர்க்கு ஐயம் இடுவோர்","இன்மையின் இந் நகர் எய்தினர் காணாய்","ஆர் உயிர் மருந்தே! அந் நாட்டு அகவயின்","கார் எனத் தோன்றிக் காத்தல் நின் கடன்' என"],"p":"","r":"He tells her that Aravanar declared Kanchi the ground of her dharma and went there, her mothers following, and that because the Kanchi country has lost its rain and its people are dying, its ascetics, finding none to give them alms, have come to Vanji; so it is her duty, medicine of lives, to appear in that land like a rain cloud and save it.","n":"The drought is told first through its effect on the monks: with no givers left, even austerity must migrate. The charge repeats Thuvathikan's foretelling in kaathai 21 almost line for line (பொன் எயில் காஞ்சி, மழை வளம் கர).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","pasi","thavam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 150-161","lb":"MM28:150-161","ev":"The ascetic tells Manimekalai that Aravanar and her mothers have gone to drought-struck Kanchi and charges her to go and save it.","cl":[["one who holds the means to feed a starving land is bound to go and protect it","general","asserted",161,"maasaathuvan"]],"ag":["அறவண அடிகள்","மாதவி","சுதமதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாசாத்துவான்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:162-183":{"book":"manimekalai","num":241,"t":["அருந் தவன் அருள ஆய் இழை வணங்கித்","திருந்திய பாத்திரம் செங் கையின் ஏந்திக்","கொடி மதில் மூதூர்க் குடக்கண் நின்று ஓங்கி","வட திசை மருங்கின் வானத்து இயங்கித்","தேவர் கோமான் காவல் மாநகர்","மண் மிசைக் கிடந்தென வளம் தலைமயங்கிய","பொன் நகர் வறிதாப் புல்லென்று ஆயது","கண்டு உளம் கசிந்த ஒண் தொடி நங்கை","பொன் கொடி மூதூர்ப் புரிசை வலம் கொண்டு","நடு நகர் எல்லை நண்ணினள் இழிந்து","தொடு கழல் கிள்ளி துணை இளங் கிள்ளி","செம் பொன் மாச் சினைத் திருமணிப் பாசடைப்","பைம் பூம் போதிப் பகவற்கு இயற்றிய","சேதியம் தொழுது தென்மேற்கு ஆக","தாது அணி பூம்பொழில் தான் சென்று எய்தலும்","வையம் காவலன் தன் பால் சென்று","கைதொழுது இறைஞ்சி கஞ்சுகன் உரைப்போன்","'கோவலன் மடந்தை குணவதம் புரிந்தோள்","நாவல் அம் தீவில் தான் நனி மிக்கோள்","அங்கையின் ஏந்திய அமுதசுரபியொடு","தங்காது இப் பதித் தருமதவனத்தே","வந்து தோன்றினள் மா மழை போல்' என"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai bows, takes the bowl, rises west of Vanji and flies north to Kanchi, which lies desolate though rich as Indra's city fallen to earth; heart melting, she circles its walls, comes down in the city's centre, worships the caitya that Ilangkilli, ally of Killi, made for the Lord of the Bodhi, and goes to a grove to the south-west; a chamberlain tells the king that Kovalan's daughter, keeper of the virtuous vows, has appeared in the Dharmadavanam with the Amudhasurabhi like a great rain cloud.","n":"The chamberlain names her by her father (கோவலன் மடந்தை) and her vows (குணவதம்), not by her courtesan mother. The Tamil names Ilangkilli as the builder of the caitya; it does not here name the reigning king of Kanchi, though the epic's tradition makes him that king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","kedu","vazhipaadu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 162-183","lb":"MM28:162-183","ev":"Manimekalai flies to drought-stricken Kanchi with the bowl, worships at Ilangkilli's caitya, and a chamberlain reports her arrival to the king.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","கஞ்சுகன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:184-206":{"book":"manimekalai","num":242,"t":["மந்திரச் சுற்றமொடு மன்னனும் விரும்பி","'கந்திற்பாவை கட்டுரை எல்லாம்","வாய் ஆகின்று' என வந்தித்து ஏத்தி","ஆய் வளை நல்லாள் தன்னுழைச் சென்று","'செங்கோல் கோடியோ செய் தவம் பிழைத்தோ","கொங்கு அவிழ் குழலார் கற்புக் குறைபட்டோ","நலத்தகை நல்லாய்! நல் நாடு எல்லாம்","அலத்தல்காலை ஆகியது அறியேன்","மயங்குவேன் முன்னர் ஓர் மா தெய்வம் தோன்றி","\"உயங்காதொழி நின் உயர் தவத்தால் ஓர்","காரிகை தோன்றும் அவள் பெருங் கடிஞையின்","ஆருயிர் மருந்தால் அகல் நிலம் உய்யும்","ஆங்கு அவள் அருளால் அமரர் கோன் ஏவலின்","தாங்கா மாரியும் தான் நனி பொழியும்","அன்னாள் இந்த அகல் நகர் புகுந்த","பின் நாள் நிகழும் பேர் அறம் பலவால்","கார் வறம் கூரினும் நீர் வறம் கூராது","பார் அகம் விதியின் பண்டையோர் இழைத்த","கோமுகி என்னும் கொழு நீர் இலஞ்சியொடு","மா மணிபல்லவம் வந்தது ஈங்கு என","பொய்கையும் பொழிலும் புனைமின்\" என்று அறைந்து அத்","தெய்வதம் போய பின் செய்து யாம் அமைத்தது","இவ் இடம்\" என்றே அவ் இடம் காட்ட அத்"],"p":"","r":"The king and his counsellors, saying the pillar image's words have all come true, go to her, and he tells her that, not knowing whether his sceptre had bent, austerity had failed, or women's chastity had fallen short, so that the whole land was in want, he was bewildered when a great deity appeared and told him not to grieve: by his own high austerity a maiden would come, her great bowl would save the land, at her grace Indra would send rain, and he should build a pool and grove as if Manipallavam with its Gomukhi pool had come here; this, he says, showing her the place, is what they made.","n":"The king tries the three causes the tradition gives for failed rain, the bent sceptre, failed austerity and failed chastity, and the deity answers none of them: it does not say which was at fault but points to a remedy, the renunciant's bowl, and credits the king's own austerity for bringing her. The words அலத்தல்காலை ஆகியது and நலத்தகை repeat Aravanar's lament over Puhar's famine in kaathai 15. How the king knows the pillar image's words (185) is not told here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kodungol","karpu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 184-206","lb":"MM28:184-206","ev":"The Kanchi king goes to Manimekalai and tells how a deity, in the drought, foretold her coming and bade him build a replica of Manipallavam's pool and grove, which he shows her.","cl":[["a land's rain fails when the king's sceptre bends, when austerity fails, or when women's chastity falls short","general","reported",188,"ilangkilli"],["a ruler's own austerity can bring a saviour to his stricken land","general","asserted",193,"maa_deyvam"]],"ag":["இளங் கிள்ளி","மா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளங் கிள்ளி","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:207-219":{"book":"manimekalai","num":243,"t":["தீவகம் போன்ற காஅகம் பொருந்திக்","கண்டு உளம் சிறந்த காரிகை நல்லாள்","'பண்டை எம் பிறப்பினைப் பான்மையின் காட்டிய","அங்கு அப் பீடிகை இது என' அறவோன்","பங்கயப் பீடிகை பான்மையின் வகுத்து","தீவதிலகையும் திரு மணிமேகலா","மா பெருந் தெய்வமும் வந்தித்து ஏத்துதற்கு","ஒத்த கோயிலுள் அத்தகப் புனைந்து","விழவும் சிறப்பும் வேந்தன் இயற்ற","தொழுதகை மாதர் தொழுதனள் ஏத்திப்","பங்கயப் பீடிகை பசிப் பிணி மருந்து எனும்","அங்கையின் ஏந்திய அமுதசுரபியை","வைத்து நின்று 'எல்லா உயிரும் வருக' என"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the grove like the island, she rejoices and takes it for the seat that showed her former birth; a lotus seat of the Virtuous One is laid out in due form, a fitting shrine is made for Deepathilakai and the great goddess Manimekala to be worshipped, the king holds festival, and she worships, sets the Amudhasurabhi, medicine for the disease of hunger, on the lotus seat, and calls, let all living beings come.","n":"Who lays out the seat at lines 210 to 211 is not clear: அறவோன் may be the Buddha whose seat it is, with the king as the builder, or the builder himself. On Manipallavam the bowl rose from the pool once a year into a fit hand; here it is set on the replica seat and offered to every creature.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pasi","vazhipaadu","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 207-219","lb":"MM28:207-219","ev":"A replica of the Buddha's seat and a shrine to the two goddesses are made in the Kanchi grove; Manimekalai sets the bowl on the seat and calls all beings to come.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","இளங் கிள்ளி"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:220-233":{"book":"manimekalai","num":244,"t":["பைத்து அரவு அல்குல் பாவை தன் கிளவியின்","மொய்த்த மூ அறு பாடை மாக்களில்","காணார் கேளார் கால் முடம் ஆனோர்","பேணா மாக்கள் பேசார் பிணித்தோர்","படிவ நோன்பியர் பசி நோய் உற்றோர்","மடி நல்கூர்ந்த மாக்கள் யாவரும்","பல் நூறாயிரம் விலங்கின் தொகுதியும்","மன் உயிர் அடங்கலும் வந்து ஒருங்கு ஈண்டி","அருந்தியோர்க்கு எல்லாம் ஆர் உயிர் மருந்து ஆய்","பெருந் தவர் கைப் பெய் பிச்சையின் பயனும்","நீரும் நிலமும் காலமும் கருவியும்","சீர் பெற வித்திய வித்தின் விளைவும்","பெருகியதென்ன பெரு வளம் சுரப்ப","வசித் தொழில் உதவி வளம் தந்தது என"],"p":"","r":"At her word the peoples of the eighteen tongues, the blind, deaf and lame, the uncared-for, the mute and the sick, the vow-keeping ascetics, the hungry and the destitute, with countless herds of beasts, all gather, and the bowl becomes life's medicine to all who eat, its plenty swelling like the fruit of alms laid in great ascetics' hands and like seed sown with the right water, soil, season and tools, until the rain's work too brings abundance.","n":"The list gives the disabled, the ascetics and the animals alike; the bowl makes no distinction of birth or species. Two images of increase are set side by side: the merit of alms given to ascetics and the harvest of well-sown seed, both depending on conditions. வசித் தொழில் at line 233 is a rare word; read as the work of rain, it fulfils the deity's promise at lines 196 to 197.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","eegai","marundhu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 220-233","lb":"MM28:220-233","ev":"All the hungry people and animals of the land gather and eat from the bowl, and rain returns.","cl":[["alms placed in the hands of great ascetics yield growing fruit, as seed sown with the right water, soil, season and tools","general","asserted",229,"narrator"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","மன் உயிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:28:234-245":{"book":"manimekalai","num":245,"t":["பசிப் பிணி தீர்த்த பாவையை ஏத்திச்","செல்லும்காலை தாயர் தம்முடன்","அல்லவை கடிந்த அறவண அடிகளும்","மல்லல் மூதூர் மன் உயிர் முதல்வி","நல் அறச்சாலை நண்ணினர் சேறலும்","சென்று அவர் தம்மைத் திருவடி வணங்கி","'நன்று' என விரும்பி நல் அடி கழுவி","ஆசனத்து ஏற்றி அறு சுவை நால் வகைப்","போனகம் ஏந்தி பொழுதினில் கொண்டபின்","பாசிலைத் திரையலும் பளிதமும் படைத்து","'வாய்வது ஆக என் மனப்பாட்டு அறம்' என","மாயை விட்டு இறைஞ்சினள் மணிமேகலை என்"],"p":"","r":"As the people go praising the maiden who cured the disease of hunger, her mothers and Aravana Adigal, who drives away what is not right, come to the hall of charity of the city's mother of lives; she bows at his feet, washes them, seats him, serves the six-flavoured food of four kinds in its hour, offers betel and camphor, prays that the dharma in her heart may come true, and, casting off her illusory form, bows down.","n":"மாயை விட்டு is the dropping of the borrowed ascetic's form she took to hear the schools (பொய் உரு, line 85; the patikam's பொய் உருக் களைந்து). Her prayer வாய்வது ஆக என் மனப்பாட்டு அறம் turns into her own vow the blessing Aravanar is foretold to give in kaathai 21. She honours her teacher as a lay giver before she takes vows in kaathai 29.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","eegai","thuravu","maayai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 28, lines 234-245","lb":"MM28:234-245","ev":"Aravanar and her mothers arrive at the hall of charity; Manimekalai washes their feet, feeds them, prays for her dharma and casts off her disguise.","cl":[],"ag":["அறவண அடிகள்","மாதவி","சுதமதி","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:29"},"mm:29:1-16":{"book":"manimekalai","num":246,"t":["இறைஞ்சிய இளங்கொடி தன்னை வாழ்த்தி","அறம் திகழ் நாவின் அறவணன் உரைப்போன்","'வென் வேல் கிள்ளிக்கு நாகநாடு ஆள்வோன்","தன் மகள் பீலிவளை தான் பயந்த","புனிற்று இளங் குழவியைத் தீவகம் பொருந்தி","தனிக் கலக் கம்பளச் செட்டி கைத் தரலும்","வணங்கிக் கொண்டு அவன் வங்கம் ஏற்றிக்","கொணர்ந்திடும் அந் நாள் கூர் இருள் யாமத்து","அடைகரைக்கு அணித்தா அம்பி கெடுதலும்","மரக்கலம் கெடுத்தோன் மைந்தனைக் காணாது","அரைசற்கு உணர்த்தலும் அவன் அயர்வுற்று","விரைவனன் தேடி விழாக்கோள் மறப்பத்","தன் விழாத் தவிர்தலின் வானவர் தலைவன்","நின் உயிர்த் தந்தை நெடுங் குலத்து உதித்த","மன் உயிர் முதல்வன் மகர வேலையுள்","முன்னிய வங்கம் முங்கிக் கேடுற"],"p":"","r":"Blessing the girl who bows to him, Aravanar begins a long account: the Naga king's daughter Pilivalai bore Killi's son on the island and handed the infant to the merchant Kambala Chetti, whose ship broke up near the shore that night; told the child was lost, the grieving king searched for him and forgot Indra's festival, and because his festival lapsed the lord of the gods...","n":"The sentence that begins here runs on to line 34, where the lord of the gods named at line 13 lays the curse; lines 14 to 33 are an inset about Manimekalai's ancestor. The unit tells as past what kaathai 1 gave as a warning: that a lapsed festival brings ruin on the city.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","idumbai","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 1-16","lb":"MM29:1-16","ev":"Aravanar tells how Killi's infant son by Pilivalai was lost when the merchant's ship sank, and how the grieving king forgot Indra's festival.","cl":[],"ag":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை","பீலிவளை","கம்பளச் செட்டி","கிள்ளி"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:17-28":{"book":"manimekalai","num":247,"t":["பொன்னின் ஊசி பசுங் கம்பளத்துத்","துன்னியதென்னத் தொடு கடல் உழந்துழி","எழு நாள் எல்லை இடுக்கண் வந்து எய்தா","வழுவாச் சீலம் வாழ்மையின் கொண்ட","பான்மையின் தனாது பாண்டு கம்பளம்","தான் நடுக்குற்ற தன்மை நோக்கி","\"ஆதி முதல்வன் போதி மூலத்து","நாதன் ஆவோன் நளி நீர்ப் பரப்பின்","எவ்வம் உற்றான் தனது எவ்வம் தீர்\" எனப்","பவ்வத்து எடுத்து \"பாரமிதை முற்றவும்","அற அரசு ஆளவும் அற ஆழி உருட்டவும்","பிறவிதோறு உதவும் பெற்றியள்\" என்றே"],"p":"","r":"The inset: when the ship of her father's ancestor, the first among living beings, sank, he struggled in the sea for seven days without harm because of his unfailing virtue, until Indra saw his pale stone seat tremble and bade the goddess relieve the one who would become the lord at the root of the Bodhi tree, and she lifted him from the sea, being one who helps him in every birth to complete the perfections, rule the kingdom of dharma and turn its wheel.","n":"The ancestor of Kovalan's line is named only as a future Buddha (போதி மூலத்து நாதன் ஆவோன்), so the epic makes Manimekalai's own family the family of a Bodhisattva. The image of line 17 likens the man in the sea to a gold needle stitched into a green blanket. Indra's throne that trembles at a good man's distress is a story pattern the unit takes for granted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","arul","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 17-28","lb":"MM29:17-28","ev":"Aravanar tells how the founder of her father's line, shipwrecked, endured seven days in the sea until Indra's seat trembled and the sea goddess lifted him out.","cl":[["unbroken moral discipline (சீலம்) keeps a future Buddha from harm and moves the gods to help him","buddhist","asserted",20,"aravana_adigal"],["a future Buddha must complete the perfections over many births before he turns the wheel of dharma","buddhist","asserted",26,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":["முதல்வன்","விண்ணவர் தலைவன்","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:29-42":{"book":"manimekalai","num":248,"t":["சாரணர் அறிந்தோர் காரணம் கூற","அந்த உதவிக்கு ஆங்கு அவள் பெயரைத்","தந்தை இட்டனன் நினை தையல் நின் துறவியும்","அன்றே கனவில் நனவென அறைந்த","மென் பூ மேனி மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","என்பவட்கு ஒப்ப அவன் இடு சாபத்து","நகர் கடல் கொள்ள நின் தாயரும் யானும்","பகரும் நின் பொருட்டால் இப் பதிப் 'படர்ந்தனம்'","என்றலும் அறவணன் தாள் இணை இறைஞ்சி","'பொன் திகழ் புத்த பீடிகை போற்றும்","தீவதிலகையும் இத் திறம் செப்பினள்","ஆதலின் அன்ன அணி நகர் மருங்கே","வேற்றுருக் கொண்டு வெவ் வேறு உரைக்கும்","நூல் துறைச் சமய நுண் பொருள் கேட்டே"],"p":"","r":"Because the sky-going sages told the reason, her father named her after the goddess for that help; the goddess Manimekala had announced her renunciation in a dream that night, and by the curse laid in accord with her the sea took the city, so her mothers and Aravanar came to Kanchi for her sake; Manimekalai bows and answers that Deepathilakai of the Buddha seat told her this, and so, in a borrowed form, she heard the subtle doctrines of the schools in that city...","n":"Two speakers share the unit: Aravanar to line 37, Manimekalai from 38. The phrase என்பவட்கு ஒப்ப அவன் இடு சாபத்து leaves open how far the curse is Indra's and how far the goddess's; the epic elsewhere has the goddess carry out the punishment. The naming and the dream were first told by the goddess herself in kaathai 7; here the reason behind the naming is given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","samayam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 29-42","lb":"MM29:29-42","ev":"Aravanar tells that her father named her after the goddess and that a curse let the sea take Puhar, so he and her mothers came to Kanchi; Manimekalai bows and says she heard the schools in Vanji in a borrowed form.","cl":[],"ag":["சாரணர்","கோவலன்","மணிமேகலா தெய்வம்","விண்ணவர் தலைவன்","மாதவி","சுதமதி","அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:43-54":{"book":"manimekalai","num":249,"t":["அவ் உரு என்ன ஐ வகைச் சமயமும்","செவ்விது அன்மையின் சிந்தையின் வைத்திலேன்","அடிகள்! மெய்ப்பொருள் அருளுக' என்ன","'நொடிகுவென் நங்காய்! நுண்ணிதின் கேள் நீ","ஆதி சினேந்திரன் அளவை இரண்டே","ஏதம் இல் பிரத்தியம் கருத்து அளவு என்னச்","சுட்டுணர்வைப் பிரத்தியக்கம் எனச் சொலி","விட்டனர் நாம சாதி குணம் கிரியைகள்","மற்று அவை அனுமானத்தும் அடையும் என","காரண காரிய சாமானியக் கருத்து","ஓரின் பிழைக்கையும் உண்டு பிழையாதது","கனலில் புகைபோல் காரியக் கருத்தே"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai says that because the five kinds of schools were not right she kept none of them in mind, and asks for the true doctrine; Aravanar says the first Jina taught only two means of knowledge, perception and inference: perception is bare awareness that leaves out name, class, quality and action, which belong to inference, and of inference from cause, from effect and from co-presence only that from effect, as smoke to fire, cannot err.","n":"ஆதி சினேந்திரன் is the Buddha under a title the Jains also use. The definition answers kaathai 27's logician point for point: he put name, class, quality and action inside perception, and this teacher puts them outside. The rejection of the schools is Manimekalai's own judgment, made before any teaching; the teacher then gives a method, not a verdict on each school.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","meyyunarvu","samayam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 43-54","lb":"MM29:43-54","ev":"Manimekalai asks Aravanar for the true doctrine, and he begins to teach the two means of knowledge.","cl":[["there are only two means of valid knowledge, perception and inference","buddhist","asserted",47,"aravana_adigal"],["perception is bare awareness, free of name, class, quality and action, which are reached by inference","buddhist","asserted",49,"aravana_adigal"],["inference from an effect to its cause, as from smoke to fire, does not err, while inference from cause or co-presence can","buddhist","asserted",53,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","அறவண அடிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:55-71":{"book":"manimekalai","num":250,"t":["ஏனை அளவைகள் எல்லாம் கருத்தினில்","ஆன முறைமையின் அனுமானம் ஆம் பிற","பக்கம் ஏது திட்டாந்தம் உபநயம்","நிகமனம் என்ன ஐந்து உள அவற்றில்","பக்கம் \"இம் மலை நெருப்புடைத்து\" என்றல்","\"புகையுடைத்து ஆதலால்\" எனல் பொருந்து ஏது","\"வகை அமை அடுக்களை போல்\" திட்டாந்தம்","உபநயம் \"மலையும் புகையுடைத்து\" என்றல்","நிகமனம் \"புகையுடைத்தே நெருப்புடைத்து\" என்றல்","\"நெருப்புடைத்து அல்லாது யாதொன்று அது புகைப்","பொருத்தம் இன்று புனல்போல்\" என்றல்","மேவிய பக்கத்து மீட்சி மொழி ஆய்","வைதன்மிய திட்டாந்தம் ஆகும்","தூய காரிய ஏதுச் சுபாவம்","ஆயின் \"சத்தம் அநித்தம்\" என்றல்","பக்கம் \"பண்ணப்படுதலால்\" எனல்","பக்க தன்ம வசனம் ஆகும்"],"p":"","r":"All other means of knowledge are inference; an inference has five members, thesis, reason, example, application and conclusion, shown on 'this hill has fire, because it has smoke, like a kitchen', with the counter-example 'what has no fire has no smoke, like water'; the pure reasons are of effect and of own nature, as in 'sound is impermanent' with 'because it is made' stated of the subject.","n":"The other means named in kaathai 27 (comparison, scripture, presumption and the rest) are not refuted one by one but absorbed into inference by a single line (55-56). The five-member form is given here only to be cut to three at line 109.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","arivu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 55-71","lb":"MM29:55-71","ev":"","cl":[["every means of knowledge other than perception is a form of inference","buddhist","asserted",55,"aravana_adigal"],["a sound reason is either an effect of what is inferred or of the same nature as it","buddhist","asserted",68,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:72-84":{"book":"manimekalai","num":251,"t":["\"யாதொன்று யாதொன்று பண்ணப்படுவது","அநித்தம் கடம் போல்\" என்றல் சபக்கத்","தொடர்ச்சி \"யாதொன்று அநித்தம் அல்லாதது","பண்ணப் படாதது ஆகாசம் போல்\" எனல்","விபக்கத் தொடர்ச்சி மீட்சி மொழி என்க","அநன்னுவயத்தில் பிரமாணம் ஆவது","\"இவ் வெள்ளிடைக்கண் குடம் இலை\" என்றல்","செவ்விய பக்கம் \"தோன்றாமையில்\" எனல்","பக்க தன்ம வசனம் ஆகும்","\"இன்மையின் கண்டிலம் முயற்கோடு\" என்றல்","அந் நெறிச் சபக்கம் \"யாதொன்று உண்டு அது","தோற்றரவு அடுக்கும் கைந் நெல்லிபோல்\" எனல்","ஏற்ற விபக்கத்து உரை எனல் ஆகும்"],"p":"","r":"The positive example states 'whatever is made is impermanent, like a pot' and the negative 'whatever is not impermanent is not made, like space'; for the reason from non-appearance, 'there is no pot in this open place, because none appears', with 'we do not see the hare's horn, because it is not' and 'whatever exists comes to appear, like a gooseberry in the hand'.","n":"With the effect reason and the own-nature reason of line 68, this gives three kinds of valid reason, the third proving absence. The word naming it, அநன்னுவயத்தில் (77), is doubtful as printed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alavai","arivu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 72-84","lb":"MM29:72-84","ev":"","cl":[["the absence of a perceivable thing is proved by its not appearing where it would appear","buddhist","asserted",79,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:85-108":{"book":"manimekalai","num":252,"t":["இவ்வகை ஏது பொருள் சாதிப்பன","\"என்னை காரியம் புகை சாதித்தது?\" என்னின்","\"புகை உள இடத்து நெருப்பு உண்டு\" என்னும்","அன்னுவயத்தாலும் \"நெருப்பு இலா இடத்துப்","புகை இல்லை\" என்னும் வெதிரேகத்தாலும்","புகஈ நெருப்பைச் சாதித்தது என்னின்","நேரிய புகையில் நிகழ்ந்து உண்டான","ஊர்த்தச் சாமம் கௌடிலச் சாமம்","வாய்த்த நெருப்பின் வரு காரியம் ஆதலின்","மேல் நோக்கிக் கறுத்திருப்ப பகைத்திருப்ப","தாமே நெருப்பைச் சாதிக்க வேண்டும்","அன்னுவயம் சாதிக்கின் \"முன்னும்","கழுதையையும் கணிகையையும்","தம்மில் ஒருகாலத்து ஓர் இடத்தே","அன்னுவயம் கண்டான் பிற்காலத்து","கழுதையைக் கண்ட இடத்தே கணிகையை","அனுமிக்க வேண்டும் அது கூடா\" \"நெருப்பு","இலா இடத்துப் புகை இலை எனல் நேர் அத்","திருத்தகு வெதிரேகம் சாதிக்கும்\" என்னின்","\"நாய் வால் இல்லாக் கழுதையின் பிடரில்","நரி வாலும் இலையா காணப்பட்ட","அதனையே கொண்டு பிறிதோர் இடத்து","நரி வாலினால் நாய் வாலை அனுமித்தல்","அரிதாம்\" அதனால் அதுவும் ஆகாது"],"p":"","r":"Asked how smoke proves fire, the teacher denies that bare co-presence or co-absence does it: the smoke itself, rising and dark as fire's effect, must prove it; if co-presence proved, a man who once saw a donkey with a courtesan would have to infer the courtesan whenever he saw the donkey, and if co-absence proved, a donkey's neck bare of both dog's tail and jackal's tail would let one infer the one tail from the other.","n":"The two jokes carry the argument: a reason proves because it is tied to what it proves by nature (here as effect to cause), not because the two were once seen together or apart. Lines 92 and 94 (ஊர்த்தச் சாமம் கௌடிலச் சாமம், பகைத்திருப்ப) are obscure as printed, so the description of the smoke is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alavai","aaraaydhal","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 85-108","lb":"MM29:85-108","ev":"","cl":[["a reason proves its conclusion through a real tie of nature or causation, not through observed co-presence or co-absence alone","buddhist","asserted",95,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:109-117":{"book":"manimekalai","num":253,"t":["ஒட்டிய உபநயம் நிகமனம் இரண்டும்","திட்டாந்தத்திலே சென்று அடங்கும்","பக்கம் ஏது திட்டாந்தங்கள்","ஒக்க நல்லவும் தீயவும் உள அதில்","வெளிப்பட்டுள்ள தன்மியினையும்","வெளிப்பட்டுள சாத்திய தன்மத்திறம்","பிறிதின் வேறு ஆம் வேறுபாட்டினையும்","தன்கண் சார்த்திய நயம் தருதல் உடையது","நன்கு என் பக்கம் என நாட்டுக அது தான்"],"p":"","r":"Application and conclusion fold into the example, leaving thesis, reason and example, each of which may be good or bad; the thesis is to be set out as what joins an evident subject with an evident property to be proved, marked off from other things.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 109-117","lb":"MM29:109-117","ev":"","cl":[["an inference for others needs only thesis, reason and example; application and conclusion are contained in the example","buddhist","asserted",109,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:118-135":{"book":"manimekalai","num":254,"t":["\"சத்தம் அநித்தம் நித்தம்\" என்று ஒன்றைப்","பற்றி நாட்டப்படுவது தன்மி","சத்தம் சாத்திய தன்மம் ஆவது","நித்த அநித்தம் நிகழும் நல் ஏது","மூன்றாய்த் தோன்றும் மொழிந்த பக்கத்து","ஊன்றி நிற்றலும் சபக்கத்து உண்டாதலும்","விபக்கத்து இன்றியே விடுதலும் சபக்கம்","சாதிக்கின் பொருள் தன்னால் பக்கத்து","ஓதிய பொது வகை ஒன்றி இருத்தல்","சத்த அநித்தம் சாத்தியம் ஆயின்","\"ஒத்த அநித்தம் கட ஆதி போல்\" எனல்","விபக்கம் விளம்பில் \"யாதொன்று யாதொன்று","அநித்தம் அல்லாதது பண்ணப் படாதது","ஆ அகாசம் போல்\" என்று ஆகும்","பண்ணப்படுதலும் செயலிடைத் தோன்றலும்","நண்ணிய பக்கம் சபக்கத்திலும் ஆய்","விபக்கத்து இன்றி அநித்தத்தினுக்கு","மிகத் தரும் ஏதுவாய் விளங்கிற்று என்க"],"p":"","r":"In 'sound is impermanent' the subject is sound and the property to be proved is impermanence; a good reason has three marks, resting in the subject, found among similar cases and wholly absent from dissimilar ones, as 'being made' and 'arising from effort' are found in sound and in pots and never in space.","n":"The test case throughout is the impermanence of sound, which matters to a Buddhist teacher because everything made is impermanent; the logic is taught on a doctrine of the school.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","aaraaydhal","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 118-135","lb":"MM29:118-135","ev":"","cl":[["a valid reason must be present in the subject, present in similar cases and absent from all dissimilar cases","buddhist","asserted",122,"aravana_adigal"],["whatever is made is impermanent","buddhist","asserted",132,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:136-153":{"book":"manimekalai","num":255,"t":["ஏதம் இல் திட்டாந்தம் இரு வகைய","சாதன்மியம் வைதன்மியம் என","சாதன்மியம் எனப்படுவது தானே","\"அநித்தம் கட ஆதி அன்னுவயத்து\" என்கை","வைதன்மிய திட்டாந்தம் \"சாத்தியம்","எய்தா இடத்தில் ஏதுவும் இன்மை\"","இத்திறம் நல்ல சாதனத்து ஒத்தன","தீய பக்கமும் தீய ஏதுவும்","தீய எடுத்துக்காட்டும் ஆவன","பக்கப் போலியும் ஏதுப் போலியும்","திட்டாந்தப் போலியும் ஆஅம் இவற்றுள்","பக்கப்போலி ஒன்பது வகைப்படும்","பிரத்தியக்க விருத்தம் அனுமான","விருத்தம் சுவசன விருத்தம் உலோக","விருத்தம் ஆகம விருத்தம் அப்பிர","சித்த விசேடணம் அப்பிரசித்த","விசேடியம் அப்பிரசித்த உபயம்","அப்பிரசித்த சம்பந்தம் என"],"p":"","r":"The example is of two kinds, by likeness and by difference, and these belong to a sound proof; their bad counterparts are the false thesis, false reason and false example, and the false thesis is of nine kinds, which the teacher names.","n":"The nine are the contradictions of perception, of inference, of one's own words, of common usage and of scripture, and four kinds of term the opponent does not accept. Kaathai 27's logician also listed false appearances, but of knowing; these are false steps in arguing before an opponent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","aabaasam","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 136-153","lb":"MM29:136-153","ev":"","cl":[["a thesis can fail in nine ways even before any reason is given","buddhist","asserted",147,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:154-172":{"book":"manimekalai","num":256,"t":["எண்ணிய இவற்றுள் பிரத்தியக்க விருத்தம்","கண்ணிய காட்சி மாறுகொளல் ஆகும்","\"சத்தம் செவிக்குப் புலன் அன்று\" என்றல்","மற்று அனுமான விருத்தம் ஆவது","கருத்து அளவையை மாறாகக் கூறல்","\"அநித்தியக் கடத்தை நித்தியம்\" என்றல்","சுவசன விருத்தம் தன் சொல் மாறி இயம்பல்","\"என் தாய் மலடி\" என்றே இயம்பல்","உலக விருத்தம் உலகின் மாறாம் உரை","\"இலகு மதி சந்திரன் அல்ல\" என்றல்","ஆகம விருத்தம் தன் நூல் மாறு அறைதல்","அநித்த வாதியா உள்ள வைசேடிகன்","\"அநித்தியத்தை நித்தியம்\" என நுவறல்","அப்பிரசித்த விசேடணம் ஆவது","தத்தம் எதிரிக்குச் சாத்தியம் தெரியாமை","பௌத்தன் மாறாய் நின்ற சாங்கியனைக்","குறித்து \"சத்தம் விநாசி\" என்றால்","அவன் அவிநாசவாதி ஆதலின்","சாத்திய விநாசம் அப்பிரசித்தம் ஆகும்"],"p":"","r":"The teacher gives an example of each false thesis: 'sound is not heard by the ear' goes against perception, 'the pot is eternal' against inference, 'my mother is barren' against one's own words, 'the moon is not the moon' against common usage, a Vaisheshika calling the impermanent eternal against his own scripture, and a Buddhist telling a Sankhya that sound perishes uses a predicate the Sankhya does not accept.","n":"The rival schools enter as test cases, not as opponents to be beaten: the Vaisheshika is called அநித்த வாதி and the Sankhya அவிநாசவாதி, and the rule is that a thesis must be put in terms the hearer grants. Line 163 turns on மதி and சந்திரன், two words for the moon.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","aabaasam","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 154-172","lb":"MM29:154-172","ev":"","cl":[["a thesis that contradicts one's own accepted scripture is false","buddhist","asserted",164,"aravana_adigal"],["sound is impermanent","vedic","reported",165,"aravana_adigal"],["nothing perishes","vedic","reported",171,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:173-190":{"book":"manimekalai","num":257,"t":["அப்பிரசித்த விசேடியம் ஆவது","எதிரிக்குத் தன்மி பிரசித்தம் இன்றி","இருத்தல் சாங்கியன் மாறாய் நின்ற","பௌத்தனைக் குறித்து \"ஆன்மாச் சைதனியவான்\"","என்றால் அவன் அநான்ம வாதி","ஆதலின் தன்மி அப்பிரசித்தம்","அப்பிரசித்த உபயம் ஆவது","மாறு ஆனோர்க்குத் தன்மி சாத்தியம்","ஏறாது அப்பிரசித்தமாய் இருத்தல்","பகர் வைசேடிகன் பௌத்தனைக் குறித்து","\"சுகம் முதலிய தொகைப் பொருட்குக் காரணம்","ஆன்மா\" என்றால் சுகமும் ஆன்மாவும்","தாம் இசையாமையின் அப்பிரசித்த உபயம்","அப்பிரசித்த சம்பந்தம் ஆவது","எதிரிக்கு இசைந்த பொருள் சாதித்தல்","மாறு ஆம் பௌத்தற்கு \"சத்த அநித்தம்\"","கூறில் அவன்ன் கொள்கை அஃது ஆகலில்","வேறு சாதிக்க வேண்டாது ஆகும்"],"p":"","r":"A Sankhya telling a Buddhist 'the soul has consciousness' names a subject the Buddhist, who denies a soul, does not accept; a Vaisheshika telling a Buddhist 'the soul is the cause of pleasure and the rest' uses subject and predicate both unaccepted; and telling a Buddhist 'sound is impermanent' proves what he already holds, which needs no proof.","n":"The Buddhist's denial of the soul (அநான்ம வாதி, 177) is stated in passing, as a fact a debater must allow for, not argued. The rule makes a debate depend on what the other side grants.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","aabaasam","samayam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 173-190","lb":"MM29:173-190","ev":"","cl":[["there is no soul","buddhist","asserted",177,"aravana_adigal"],["the soul is conscious","vedic","reported",176,"aravana_adigal"],["the soul is the cause of pleasure and the other qualities","vedic","reported",183,"aravana_adigal"],["a thesis the opponent already holds is not a thesis to be proved","buddhist","asserted",187,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:191-206":{"book":"manimekalai","num":258,"t":["ஏதுப் போலி ஓதின் மூன்று ஆகும்","அசித்தம் அநைகாந்திகம் விருத்தம்ம் என","உபய அசித்தம் அன்னியதர அசித்தம்","சித்த அசித்தம் ஆசிரய அசித்தம்","என நான்கு அசித்தம் உபய அசித்தம்","சாதன ஏது இருவர்க்கும் இன்றி","\"சத்தம் அநித்தம் கண் புலத்து\" என்றல்","அன்னியதர அசித்தம் மாறு ஆய் நின்றாற்கு","உன்னிய ஏது அன்றாய் ஒழிதல்","\"சத்தம் செயலுறல் அநித்தம்\" என்னின்","சித்த வெளிப்பாடு அல்லது செயலுறல்","உய்த்த சாங்கியனுக்கு அசித்தம் ஆகும்","சித்த அசித்தம் ஆவது","ஏது சங்கயமாய்ச் சாதித்தல்","ஆவி பனி என ஐயுறா நின்றே","\"தூய புகை நெருப்பு உண்டு\" எனத் துணிதல்"],"p":"","r":"False reasons are of three kinds, unestablished, inconclusive and contradictory; the unestablished are four: unaccepted by both sides ('sound is impermanent because seen by the eye'), unaccepted by one ('because produced', to a Sankhya for whom things are manifested, not produced), doubtful (declaring fire on seeing what may be mist or steam), and lacking a subject.","n":"The doubtful kind is named சித்த அசித்தம் (203), though the definition (ஏது சங்கயமாய்) describes a reason in doubt; the name may be corrupt. The Sankhya example matches kaathai 27, where the Sankhya speaker describes every stage of the world as வெளிப்பட்டு, becoming manifest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alavai","aabaasam","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 191-206","lb":"MM29:191-206","ev":"","cl":[["things are not produced but only made manifest","vedic","reported",201,"aravana_adigal"],["a reason that is in doubt cannot ground a firm conclusion","buddhist","asserted",204,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:207-222":{"book":"manimekalai","num":259,"t":["ஆசிரய அசித்தம் மாறு ஆனவனுக்கு","ஏற்ற தன்மி இன்மை காட்டுதல்","\"ஆகாசம் சத்த குணத்தால் பொருளாம்\" என்னின்","\"ஆகாசம் பொருள் அல்ல\" என்பாற்குத்","தன்மி அசித்தம் அநைகாந்திகமும்","சாதாரணம் அசாதாரணம் சபக்கைக","தேசவிருத்தி விபக்க வியாபி","விபக்கைகதேச விருத்தி சபக்க","வியாபி உபயைகதேச விருத்தி","விருத்த வியபிசாரி என்று ஆறு","சாதாரணம் சபக்க விபக்கத்துக்கும்","ஏதுப் பொதுவாய் இருத்தல் \"சத்தம்","அநித்தம் அறியப்படுதலின்\" என்றால்","\"அறியப்படுதல் நித்த அநித்தம் இரண்டுக்கும்","செறியும் கடம் போல் அநித்தத்து அறிவோ?","ஆகாசம் போல நித்தத்து அறிவோ?\""],"p":"","r":"The reason lacking a subject is shown by 'space is a substance because it has the quality of sound', said to one who denies that space is a substance; the inconclusive reasons are six, and the first, the too general, belongs to both similar and dissimilar cases, as 'sound is impermanent because knowable' leaves open whether sound is known like a pot or like space.","n":"The example thesis about space is the Vaisheshika's (space is among his nine substances and sound among the qualities in kaathai 27); who denies it is not said.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","aabaasam","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 207-222","lb":"MM29:207-222","ev":"","cl":[["space is a substance, known by its quality of sound","vedic","reported",209,"aravana_adigal"],["a reason found equally in similar and dissimilar cases proves nothing","buddhist","asserted",217,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:223-233":{"book":"manimekalai","num":260,"t":["என்னல் அசாதாரணம் ஆவது தான்","உன்னிய பக்கத்து உண்டாம் ஏதுச்","சபக்க விபக்கம் தம்மில் இன்றாதல்","\"சத்தம் நித்தம் கேட்கப்படுதலின்\"","என்னின் \"கேட்கப்படல்\" எனும் ஏதுப்","பக்கத்து உள்ளதாயின் அல்லது","சபக்க விபக்கத்து மீட்சித்து ஆதலின்","சங்கயம் எய்தி அநேகாந்திகம் ஆம்","சபக்கைகதேச விருத்தி விபக்க","வியாபி ஆவது ஏதுச் சபக்கத்து","ஓர் இடத்து எய்தி விபக்கத்து எங்கும்"],"p":"","r":"The too narrow reason belongs to the subject alone and to neither similar nor dissimilar cases, as 'sound is eternal because it is heard' leaves doubt, since only sound is heard; the next kind is the reason found in part of the similar cases and throughout the dissimilar ones.","n":"The kaathai's account of false reasons runs on past this chapter's end at 233.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavai","aabaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 223-233","lb":"MM29:223-233","ev":"","cl":[["a reason found only in the subject leaves the conclusion in doubt","buddhist","asserted",230,"aravana_adigal"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:30"},"mm:29:234-253":{"book":"manimekalai","num":261,"t":["உண்டாதல் ஆகும் \"சத்தம் செயலிடைத்","தோன்றாதாகும் அநித்தம் ஆகலின்\"","என்றால் \"அநித்தம்\" என்ற ஏதுச்","செயலிடைத் தோன்றாமைக்குச் சபக்கம்","மின்னினும் ஆகாசத்தினும் மின்னின்","நிகழ்ந்து ஆகாசத்தில் காணாது ஆகலின்","அநித்தம் கட ஆதியின் ஒத்தலின் \"கடம் போல்","அழிந்து செயலில் தோன்றுமோ? மின் போல்","அழிந்து செயலில் தோன்றாதோ?\" எனல்","விபக்கைகதேச விருத்தி சபக்க","வியாபி ஆவது ஏது விபக்கத்து","ஓரிடத்து உற்று சபக்கத்து ஒத்து இயறல்","\"சத்தம் செயலிடைத் தோன்றும் அநித்தம் ஆதலின்\" எனின்","அநித்த ஏதுச் செயலிடைத் தோன்றற்கு","விபக்க ஆகாயத்தினும் மின்னினும்","மின்னின் நிகழ்ந்து ஆகாசத்துக் காணாது","சபக்கக் கட ஆதிகள் தம்மில்","எங்கும் ஆய் ஏகாந்தம் அல்ல \"மின் போல்","அநித்தம் ஆய்ச் செயலிடைத் தோன்றாதோ? கடம்போல்","அநித்தம் ஆய்ச் செயலிடைத் தோன்றுமோ? எனல்"],"p":"","r":"Aravanar finishes the reason found in some similar cases and in every dissimilar one, with sound said not to arise from effort because it is impermanent (lightning arises without effort, space never shows it, the pot does), then gives its mirror, the reason found in every similar case and some dissimilar ones, with sound said to arise from effort because impermanent.","n":"The two fallacies are built on one example turned round, which is why each ends in a pair of questions (240 to 242, 251 to 253): 'like the pot, or like the lightning?'. The teacher's point is that an inconclusive reason leaves the hearer with a question, not a verdict.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","alavai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 234-253","lb":"MM29:234-253","ev":"","cl":[["a reason found in some similar instances and in all dissimilar ones leaves the thesis open to doubt","buddhist","asserted",234,"aravanar"],["a reason found in all similar instances and in only some dissimilar ones is likewise inconclusive","buddhist","asserted",243,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:254-266":{"book":"manimekalai","num":262,"t":["உபயைகதேச விருத்தி ஏதுச்","சபக்கத்தினும் விபக்கத்தினும் ஆகி","ஓர் தேசத்து வர்த்தித்தல் \"சத்தம்","நித்தம் அமூர்த்தம் ஆதலின்\" என்னின்","அமூர்த்த ஏது நித்தத்தினுக்குச்","சபக்க ஆகாச பரமாணுக்களின்","ஆகாசத்து நிகழ்ந்து மூர்த்தம் ஆம்","பரமாணுவின் நிகழாமையானும்","விபக்கமான கட சுக ஆதிகளில்","சுகத்து நிகழ்ந்து கடத்து ஒழிந்தமையினும்","ஏகதேசத்து நிகழ்வது ஏகாந்தம் அன்று","\"அமூர்த்தம் ஆகாசம்போல நித்தமோ?","அமூர்த்தம் சுகம் போல் அநித்தமோ?\" எனல்"],"p":"","r":"The reason found in only part of the similar and part of the dissimilar cases: sound is eternal because incorporeal, where incorporeality is in space but not the corporeal atom, and in pleasure but not the pot, so the hearer can ask whether sound is eternal like space or impermanent like pleasure.","n":"Both the eternal atom (259 to 261) and pleasure as incorporeal and passing (262 to 263) are taken as granted for the example; the teacher does not say here whether he holds them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","alavai","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 254-266","lb":"MM29:254-266","ev":"","cl":[["a reason found in only part of the similar and part of the dissimilar instances is inconclusive","buddhist","asserted",254,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:267-280":{"book":"manimekalai","num":263,"t":["விருத்த வியபிசாரி திருந்தா ஏது ஆய்","விருத்த ஏதுவிற்கும் இடம் கொடுத்தல்","\"சத்தம் அநித்தம் செயலிடைத் தோன்றலின்","ஒத்தது\" எனின் அச் செயலிடைத் தோன்றற்குச்","சபக்கமாயுள்ள கட ஆதி நிற்க","\"சத்தம் நித்தம் கேட்கப்படுதலின்","சத்தத்துவம் போல்\" எனச் சாற்றிடுதல்","இரண்டினும் சங்கயம் ஆய் ஏகாந்தம் அல்ல","விருத்தம் தன்னைத் திருத்தக விளம்பின்","தன்மச் சொரூப விபரீத சாதனம்","தன்ம விசேட விபரீத சாதனம்","தன்மிச் சொரூப விபரீத சாதனம்","தன்மி விசேட விபரீத சாதனம்","என்ன நான்கு வகையது ஆகும் அத்"],"p":"","r":"The last inconclusive reason is the one that admits a contrary reason: 'sound is impermanent because it arises from effort' meets 'sound is eternal because it is heard, like soundness', both proofs stand and both end in doubt; then the contradictory reason is announced in four kinds.","n":"This is the one fallacy that no single proof shows: it appears only when two arguments meet, so it names the stalemate of a debate hall. சங்கயம் and ஏகாந்தம் அல்ல (274) set doubt against one-sided certainty in borrowed Sanskrit; the eternal-sound proof is a rival's, though no school is named here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","alavai","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 267-280","lb":"MM29:267-280","ev":"","cl":[["when a thesis and its contrary are each backed by a reason that looks sound, neither is established and both end in doubt","buddhist","asserted",274,"aravanar"],["the contradictory reason is of four kinds, by the property or the subject, in its bare form or in its specific character","buddhist","asserted",275,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:281-302":{"book":"manimekalai","num":264,"t":["தன்மச் சொரூப விபரீத சாதனம்","சொன்ன ஏதுவின் சாத்திய தன்மத்து","உருவம் கெடுதல் \"சத்தம் நித்தம்","பண்ணப்படுதலின்\" என்றால் பண்ணப்","படுவது அநித்தம் ஆதலின் பண்ணப்பட்ட","ஏதுச் சாத்திய தன்ம நித்தத்தை விட்டு","அநித்தம் சாதித்தலான் விபரீதம்","தன்ம விசேட விபரீத சாதனம்","சொன்ன ஏதுச் சாத்திய தன்மம்","தன்னிடை விசேடம் கெடச் சாதித்தல்","\"கண் முதல் ஓர்க்கும் இந்திரியங்கள்","எண்ணின் பரார்த்தம் தொக்கு நிற்றலினால்","சயன ஆசனங்கள் போல\" என்றால்","\"தொக்கு நிற்றலின்\" என்கின்ற ஏதுச்","சயன ஆசனத்தின் பராத்தம்போல் கண் முதல்","இந்தியங்களியும் பரார்த்தத்தில் சாதித்துச்","சயன ஆசனவானைப் போல் ஆகிக்","கண் முதல் இந்தியத்துக்கும் பரனாய்ச்","சாதிக்கிற நிர் அவயவமாயுள்ள","ஆன்மாவைச் சாவயவமாகச்","சாதித்துச் சாத்திய தன்மத்தின்","விசேடம் கெடுத்தலின் விபரீதம்"],"p":"","r":"The contradictory reason proves the opposite of the property ('sound is eternal because it is made', when the made is impermanent) or destroys its intended character: the argument that the eye and other senses serve another because they are aggregates, like beds and seats, proves that other to be composite, like the user of a bed, and so undoes the partless self it set out to prove.","n":"The text does not name the school at 291 to 302, but the argument from aggregates serving another and the partless self (நிர் அவயவமாயுள்ள ஆன்மா, 299 to 300) are the Sankhya's, whose purusha kaathai 27 describes; the reply turns the Sankhya proof against its own conclusion rather than denying a self outright. Line 296 இந்தியங்களியும் reads as a slip for the senses in the accusative.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","alavai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 281-302","lb":"MM29:281-302","ev":"","cl":[["a reason that proves the opposite of the property to be proved is contradictory","buddhist","asserted",282,"aravanar"],["a reason that undoes the intended specific character of the property to be proved is contradictory","buddhist","asserted",290,"aravanar"],["the senses exist for the sake of another, the self, because they are aggregates, as beds and seats are","vedic","refuted",291,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:303-325":{"book":"manimekalai","num":265,"t":["தன்மிச் சொரூப விபரீத சாதனம்","தன்மியுடைய சொரூப மாத்திரத்தினை","ஏதுத் தானே விபரீதப்படுத்தல்","\"பாவம் திரவியம் கன்மம் அன்று","குணமும் அன்று எத் திரவியம் ஆம் எக்","குண கன்மத்து உண்மையின் வேறாதலால்","சாமானிய விசேடம்போல்\" என்றால்","\"பொருளும் குணமும் கருமமும் ஒன்றாய்","நின்றவற்றின்னிடை உண்மை வேறு ஆதலால்\" என்று","காட்டப்பட்ட ஏது மூன்றினுடை","உண்மை பேதுப்படுத்தும் பொதுவாம்","உண்மை சாத்தியத்து இல்லாமையினும்","திட்டாந்தத்தில் சாமானியம் விசேடம்","போக்கிப் பிறிதொன்று இல்லாமையானும்","பாவம் என்று பகர்ந்த தன்மியினை","அபாவம் ஆக்குதலான் விபரீதம்","தன்மி விசேட விபரீத சாதனம்","தன்மி விசேட அபாவம் சாதித்தல்","முன்னம் காட்டப்பட்ட ஏதுவே","பாவம் ஆகின்றது கருத்தாவுடைய","கிரியையும் குணமும் ஆம் அதனை விபரீதம்","ஆக்கியது ஆதலான் தன்மி விசேடம்","கெடுத்தது தீய எடுத்துக்காட்டு ஆவன"],"p":"","r":"The reason can also undo the subject itself: the argument that existence is not substance, quality or action because it differs from them, like generality and particularity, takes away the common being of all three and leaves existence nothing, turning being into non-being; and the same reason undoes existence's specific character as what gives the notion 'it is'.","n":"Substance, quality, action, generality and particularity are the Vaisheshika's categories, and பொருளும் குணமும் கருமமும் with உண்மை (310 to 311) repeats the wording of the Vaisheshika's own account at 27:257 to 258; the school is not named here. Lines 321 to 325, on the fourth kind, are compressed, and the sense of கருத்தாவுடைய கிரியையும் குணமும் (322 to 323) is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","alavai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 303-325","lb":"MM29:303-325","ev":"","cl":[["a reason that turns the subject itself into its opposite is contradictory","buddhist","asserted",305,"aravanar"],["a reason that proves the absence of the subject's specific character is contradictory","buddhist","asserted",320,"aravanar"],["existence is neither substance, quality nor action, because it is distinct from the being of each, as generality and particularity are","vedic","refuted",306,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:326-339":{"book":"manimekalai","num":266,"t":["தாமே திட்டாந்த ஆபாசங்கள்","திட்டாந்தம் இரு வகைப் படும் என்று முன்","கூறப்பட்டன இங்கண் அவற்றுள்","சாதன்மிய திட்டாந்த ஆபாசம்","ஓதில் ஐந்து வகை உளதாகும்","சாதன தன்ம விகலமும் சாத்திய","தன்ம விகலமும் உபய தன்ம","விகலமும் அநன்னுவயம் விபரீதான்","னுவயம் என்ன வைதன்மிய திட்","டாந்த ஆபாசமும் ஐ வகைய","சாத்தியா வியாவிருத்தி","சாதனா வியாவிருத்தி","உபயா வியாவிருத்தி அவ்வெதிரேகம்","விபரீத வெதிரேகம் என்ன இவற்றுள்"],"p":"","r":"Faulty examples are the semblances of the example, of two kinds as the example was: five by similarity (lacking the reason, lacking the property, lacking both, without the connection, with the connection reversed) and five by difference (not excluding the property, not excluding the reason, excluding neither, without the exclusion, with the exclusion reversed).","n":"The list is a mirror: each failing of a similar example has a counterpart in the dissimilar one, so the teacher can define the second five by reversing the first. Lines 333 to 335 break words across lines (விபரீதான் / னுவயம், திட் / டாந்த), and 336 to 337 are unusually short.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 326-339","lb":"MM29:326-339","ev":"","cl":[["faulty examples are of two kinds, by similarity and by difference, each in five forms","buddhist","asserted",327,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:340-358":{"book":"manimekalai","num":267,"t":["சாதன தன்ம விகலம் ஆவது","திட்டாந்தத்தில் சாதனம் குறைவது","\"சத்தம் நித்தம் அமூர்த்தம் ஆதலான்","யாதொன்று யாதொன்று அமூர்த்தம் அது நித்தம்","ஆதலான் காண்புற்றது பரமாணுவில்\" எனின்","திட்டாந்தப் பரமாணு","நித்தத்தோடு மூர்த்தம் ஆதலான்","சாத்திய தன்ம நித்தத்துவம் நிரம்பிச்","சாதன தன்ம அமூர்த்தத்துவம் குறையும்","சாத்திய தன்ம விகலம் ஆவது","காட்டப்பட்ட திட்டாந்தத்தில்","சாத்திய தன்மம் குறைவுபடுதல்","\"சத்தம் நித்தம் அமூர்த்தம் ஆதலால்","யாதொன்று யாதொன்று அமூர்த்தம் அது நித்தம்","புத்திபோல்\" என்றால்","திட்டாந்தமாகக் காட்டப்பட்ட","புத்தி அமூர்த்தம் ஆகி நின்றே","அநித்தம் ஆதலான் சாதன அமூர்த்தத்துவம்","நிரம்பி சாத்திய நித்தத்துவம் குறையும்"],"p":"","r":"An example by similarity may lack the reason (sound is eternal because incorporeal, like the atom, which is eternal but has form) or lack the property to be proved (like cognition, which is incorporeal but passing).","n":"Both examples defend 'sound is eternal', so the teacher's faulty examples are all on the rival thesis; the eternal, formed atom and the passing cognition (புத்தி) are taken as granted for the lesson.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","alavai","anu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 340-358","lb":"MM29:340-358","ev":"","cl":[["an example that lacks the reason's property cannot support the proof","buddhist","asserted",340,"aravanar"],["an example that lacks the property to be proved cannot support the proof","buddhist","asserted",349,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:359-372":{"book":"manimekalai","num":268,"t":["உபய தன்ம விகலம் ஆவது","காட்டப்பட்ட திட்டாந்தத்திலே","சாத்திய சாதனம் இரண்டும் குறைதல்","அன்றியும் அது தான் சன்னும் அசன்னும்","என்று இரு வகையாம் இவற்றுள் சன்னா உள","உபய தன்ம விகலம் ஆவது","உள்ள பொருட்கண் சாத்திய சாதனம்","கொள்ளும் இரண்டும் குறையக் காட்டுதல்","\"சத்தம் நித்தம் அமூர்த்தம் ஆதலான்","யாதொன்று யாதொன்று அமூர்த்தம் அது நித்தம்","கடம் போல்\" எனின் திட்டாந்தமாகக்","காட்டப்பட்ட கடம் தான் உண்டாகிச்","சாத்தியமாய் உள நித்தத்துவமும்","சாதனமாய் உள அமூர்த்தத்துவமும் குறையும்"],"p":"","r":"An example may lack both properties, and this is of two kinds, of an existing thing or a non-existent one; of an existing thing: sound is eternal because incorporeal, like a pot, which came into being and has form, so has neither.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 359-372","lb":"MM29:359-372","ev":"","cl":[["an example that lacks both the property to be proved and the reason fails, whether the example exists or not","buddhist","asserted",359,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:373-384":{"book":"manimekalai","num":269,"t":["அசன்னா உள்ள உபய தன்ம விகலம்","இல்லாப்பொருட்கண் சாத்திய சாதனம்","என்னும் இரண்டும் குறையக் காட்டுதல்","\"சத்தம் அநித்தம் மூர்த்தம் ஆதலான்","யாதொன்று யாதொன்று மூர்த்தம் அது அநித்தம்","ஆகாசம் போல்\" எனும் திட்டாந்தத்து","சாத்திய தன்மமாய் உள்ள அநித்தமும்","சாதன தன்மமாய் உள்ள மூர்த்தமும்","இரண்டும் \"ஆகாசம் அசத்து\" என்பானுக்கு","அதன்கண் இன்மையானே குறையும்","\"உண்டு\" என்பானுக்கு ஆகாசம் நித்தம்","அமூர்த்தம் ஆதலால் அவனுக்கும் குறையும்"],"p":"","r":"Of a non-existent thing: sound is impermanent because it has form, like space; for one who says space is unreal it has neither property, and for one who says it is real, space is eternal and formless, so it lacks both for him too.","n":"The teacher settles nothing about space: the example fails on either view (381 to 384), so the logic can be taught without deciding the ontology the schools dispute. Who holds 'space is unreal' (ஆகாசம் அசத்து) is not said.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","aakaasam","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 373-384","lb":"MM29:373-384","ev":"","cl":[["an example fails if one party denies it exists, and fails too if, granted, it lacks the properties","buddhist","asserted",381,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:385-401":{"book":"manimekalai","num":270,"t":["அநன்னுவயம் ஆவது சாதன சாத்தியம்","தம்மில் கூட்டம் மாத்திரம் சொல்லாதே","இரண்டனுடைய உண்மையைக் காட்டுதல்","\"சத்தம் அநித்தம் கிருத்தம் ஆதலின்","யாதொன்று யாதொன்று கிருத்தம் அது அநித்தம்\" எனும்","அன்னுவயம் சொல்லாது \"குடத்தின்கண்ணே","கிருத்த அநித்தம் காணப்பட்ட\"","என்றால் அன்னுவயம் தெரியாதாகும்","விபரீதான்னுவயம் வியாபகத்துடைய","அன்னுவயத்தாலே வியாப்பியம் விதித்தல்","\"சத்தம் அநித்தம் கிருத்தத்தால்\" எனின்","\"யாதொன்று யாதொன்று கிருத்தம் அநித்தம்\" என","வியாப்பியத்தால் வியாபக்கத்தைக் கருதாது","\"யாதொன்று யாதொன்று அநித்தம் அது கிருத்தம்\" என","வியாபகத்தால் வியாப்பியத்தைக் கருதுதல்","அப்படிக் கருதின் வியாபகம் வியாப்பியத்தை","இன்றியும் நிகழ்தலின் விபரீதம் ஆம்"],"p":"","r":"Without the connection is to show the reason and property together in a pot without stating 'whatever is made is impermanent'; with the connection reversed is to say 'whatever is impermanent is made', running from the wider to the narrower, which fails because the wider occurs without the narrower.","n":"The correct rule, கிருத்தம் அது அநித்தம், is the Buddhist teaching that whatever is made passes; the teacher states it only as the model of a well-formed connection, and the reversed form fails because some impermanent things are not made, as the lightning of 238 to 241 showed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","alavai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 385-401","lb":"MM29:385-401","ev":"","cl":[["showing two properties together in one instance, without stating the general rule, does not establish the connection","buddhist","asserted",385,"aravanar"],["the rule must run from the narrower property to the wider; stated the other way it fails, since the wider occurs without the narrower","buddhist","asserted",400,"aravanar"],["whatever is made is impermanent","buddhist","asserted",389,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:402-423":{"book":"manimekalai","num":271,"t":["வைதன்மிய திட்டாந்தத்துச்","சாத்தியா வியாவிருத்தி ஆவது","சாதன தன்மம் மீண்டு","சாத்திய தன்மம் மீளாதுஒழிதல்","\"சத்தம் நித்தம் அமூர்த்தத்து\" என்றால்","\"யாதொன்று யாதொன்று நித்தமும் அன்று அது","அமூர்த்தமும் அன்று பரமாணுப் போல்\" எனின்","அப்படித் திட்டாந்தமாகக் காட்டப்பட்ட","பரமாணு நித்தம் ஆய் மூர்த்தம் ஆதலின்","சாதன அமூர்த்தம் மீண்டு","சாத்திய நித்தம் மீளாதுஒழிதல்","சாதனா வியாவிருத்தி ஆவது","சாத்திய தன்மம் மீண்டு","சாதன தன்மம் மீளாது ஒழிதல்","\"சத்தம் நித்தம் அமூர்த்தத்து\" என்றால்","\"யாதொன்று யாதொன்று நித்தம் அன்று அஃது","அமூர்த்தமும் அன்று கன்மம்போல்\" என்றால்","வைதன்மிய திட்டாந்தமாகக்","காட்டப்பட்ட கன்மம்","அமூர்த்தமாய் நின்றே அநித்தம் ஆதலின்","சாத்தியமான நித்தியம் மீண்டு","சாதனமான அமூர்த்தம் மீளாது"],"p":"","r":"An example by difference may fail to exclude the property (sound is eternal because incorporeal: 'whatever is not eternal is not incorporeal, like the atom', which is eternal and formed) or fail to exclude the reason ('like action', which is incorporeal and passing).","n":"Action (கன்மம், 418 to 421) is the incorporeal but passing thing here, a category from the Vaisheshika list; it is not karma in the moral sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","alavai","anu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 402-423","lb":"MM29:402-423","ev":"","cl":[["a dissimilar example must lack the property to be proved; one that keeps it fails","buddhist","asserted",403,"aravanar"],["a dissimilar example must lack the reason; one that keeps it fails","buddhist","asserted",413,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:424-449":{"book":"manimekalai","num":272,"t":["உபயா வியாவிருத்தி காட்டப்பட்ட","வைதன்மிய திட்டாந்தத்தினின்று","சாதன சாத்தியங்கள் மீளாமை அன்றியும்","உண்மையின் உபயா வியாவிருத்தி","இன்மையின் உபயா வியாவிருத்தி","என இருவகை உண்மையின்","உபயா வியாவிருத்தி உள்ள பொருட்கண்","சாத்திய சாதனம் மீளாதபடி","வைதன்மிய திட்டாந்தம் காட்டல்","\"சத்தம் நித்தம் அமூர்த்தம் ஆதலின்\"","என்றாற்கு \"யாதொன்று யாதொன்று நித்தம் அன்று","அமூர்த்தமும் அன்று ஆகாசம்போல்\" என்றால்","\"வைதன்மிய திட்டாந்தமாகக் காட்டப்பட்ட","ஆகாசம் பொருள்\" என்பாற்கு","ஆகாசம் நித்தமும் அமூர்த்தமும் ஆதலான்","சாத்திய நித்தமும் சாதனமா உள்ள","அமூர்த்தமும் இரண்டும் மீண்டில இன்மையின்","உபயா வியாவிருத்தி ஆவது","\"சத்தம் அநித்தம் மூர்த்தம் ஆதலான்\"","என்ற இடத்து \"யாதொன்று யாதொன்று அநித்தம்","மூர்த்தமும் அன்ன்று ஆகாசம் போல்\" என","வைதன்மிய திட்டாந்தம் காட்டில்","\"ஆகாசம் பொருள் அல்ல\" என்பானுக்கு","ஆகாசம் தானே உண்மை இன்மையினால்","சாத்திய அநித்தமும் சாதன மூர்த்தமும்","மீட்சியும் மீளாமையும் இலையாகும்"],"p":"","r":"An example by difference may exclude neither property, again of an existing or a non-existent thing: for one who holds space a real thing, space is eternal and formless and so excludes neither; for one who says space is no thing, it has no being, so nothing is excluded or kept.","n":"Space is again judged by both views (437, 446), as at 381 to 384. Line 443 lacks a negative the sense needs (whatever is not impermanent), and 444 has அன்ன்று; the lines read as corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","aakaasam","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 424-449","lb":"MM29:424-449","ev":"","cl":[["a dissimilar example from which neither property is excluded fails, whether it exists or not","buddhist","asserted",424,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:29:450-472":{"book":"manimekalai","num":273,"t":["அவ்வெதிரேகம் ஆவது சாத்தியம்","இல்லா இடத்துச் சாதனம் இன்மை","சொல்லாதே விடுதல் ஆகும் \"சத்தம்","நித்தம் பண்ணப்படாமையால்\" என்றால்","\"யாதொன்று யாதொன்று நித்தம் அன்று","பண்ணப்படுவது அல்லாது அதுவும்","அன்று\" எனும் இவ் வெதிரேகம் தெரியச்","சொல்லாது \"குடத்தின்கண்ணே பண்ணப்","படுதலும் அநித்தமும் கண்டேம் ஆதலான்\"","என்னின் வெதிரேகம் தெரியாது","விபரீத வெதிரேகம் ஆவது","பிரிவைத் தலைதடுமாறாச் சொல்லுதல்","\"சத்தம் நித்தம் மூர்த்தம் ஆதலின்\"","என்றால் என்று நின்ற இடத்து","\"யாதோர் இடத்து நித்தமும் இல்லை அவ்","இடத்து மூர்த்தமும் இல்லை\" எனாதே","\"யாதோர் இடத்து மூர்த்தமும் இல்லை அவ்","இடத்து நித்தமும் இல்லை\" என்றால்","வெதிரேகம் மாறுகொள்ளும் எனக் கொள்க","நாட்டிய இப்படி தீய சாதனத்தால்","காட்டும் அனுமான ஆபாசத்தின்","மெய்யும் பொய்யும் இத்திற விதியால்","ஐயம் இன்றி அறிந்து கொள் ஆய்ந்து என்"],"p":"","r":"Without the exclusion is to show the pot made and passing without stating that where the property is absent the reason is absent; with the exclusion reversed is to deny the reason before the property; and Aravanar closes by bidding Manimekalai learn by these rules, having examined, to know without doubt what is true and false in the semblances of inference.","n":"The lesson that began with the true means of knowledge ends with false inference, and its last word is a method for the hearer (ஆய்ந்து அறிந்து கொள்), not a doctrine to accept. Line 463 (என்றால் என்று நின்ற இடத்து) reads awkwardly and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aabaasam","meyyunarvu","aaraaydhal","alavai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 29, lines 450-472","lb":"MM29:450-472","ev":"Aravanar ends his account of faulty inference and tells Manimekalai to examine and know true from false by these rules.","cl":[["the exclusion must be stated as a rule, not only shown in an instance","buddhist","asserted",450,"aravanar"],["the exclusion must deny the property to be proved first and the reason after; reversed, it fails","buddhist","asserted",460,"aravanar"],["by these rules one can examine an inference and know without doubt what in it is true and what false","buddhist","asserted",471,"aravanar"]],"ag":["அறவண அடிகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:31"},"mm:30:1-6":{"book":"manimekalai","num":274,"t":["தானம் தாங்கிச் சீலம் தலைநின்று","போன பிறப்பில் புகுந்ததை உணர்ந்தோள்","புத்த தன்ம சங்கம் என்னும்","முத் திற மணியை மும்மையின் வணங்கி","சரணாகதியாய்ச் 'சரண்' சென்று அடைந்தபின்","முரணாத் திருவறமூர்த்தியை மொழிவோன்"],"p":"","r":"Manimekalai, who has known what she entered into in her former birth by holding to giving and keeping the precepts, bows three times to the three jewels of Buddha, Dharma and Sangha and goes to them for refuge, and the teacher begins to speak the form of dharma.","n":"தானம் and சீலம் (line 1) are the two practices she held in her former birth, and the same pair returns at line 78 as what makes a good birth: the teacher's doctrine of good deeds is first shown in the hearer. The teacher is only மொழிவோன், 'the one who speaks'; the story so far makes him Aravana Adigal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","piravi","eegai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 1-6","lb":"MM30:1-6","ev":"Manimekalai takes refuge in the Buddha, the Dharma and the Sangha, and the teacher begins his discourse.","cl":[],"ag":["மணிமேகலை","அறவண அடிகள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:7-33":{"book":"manimekalai","num":275,"t":["'அறிவு வறிதாய் உயிர் நிறை காலத்து","முடி தயங்கு அமரர் முறைமுறை இரப்ப","துடிதலோகம் ஒழியத் தோன்றி","போதி மூலம் பொருந்தியிருந்து","மாரனை வென்று வீரன் ஆகி","குற்றம் மூன்றும் முற்ற அறுக்கும்","வாமன் வாய்மை ஏமக் கட்டுரை","இறந்த காலத்து எண் இல் புத்தர்களும்","சிறந்து அருள் கூர்ந்து திருவாய் மொழிந்தது","ஈர் அறு பொருளின் ஈந்த நெறி உடைத்தாய்ச்","சார்பின் தோன்றி தத்தமில் மீட்டும்","இலக்கு அணத் தொடர்தலின்","மண்டில வகையாய் அறியக் காட்டி","எதிர் முறை ஒப்ப மீட்சியும் ஆகி","ஈங்கு இது இல்லாவழி இல்லாகி","ஈங்கு இது உள்ளவழி உண்டு ஆகலின்","தக்க தக்க சார்பின் தோற்றம் எனச்","சொற்றகப்பட்டும் இலக்கு அணத் தொடர்பால்","கருதப்பட்டும் கண்டம் நான்கு உடைத்தாய்","மருவிய சந்தி வகை மூன்று உடைத்தாய்","தோற்றம் பார்க்கின் மூன்று வகை ஆய்","தோற்றற்கு ஏற்ற காலம் மூன்று உடைத்தாய்","குற்றமும் வினையும் பயனும் விளைந்து","நிலையில வறிய துன்பம் என நோக்க","உலையா வீட்டிற்கு உறுதி ஆகி","நால்வகை வாய்மைக்குச் சார்பு இடன் ஆகி","ஐந்து வகைக் கந்தத்து அமைதி ஆகி"],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that when the world's knowledge had run dry the Buddha came down from the Tushita heaven at the gods' entreaty, sat at the Bodhi tree, conquered Mara and taught, as countless past Buddhas taught, the twelvefold teaching of arising by dependence: where this is not that is not, where this is that is, with four sections, three junctions, three modes and three times, the ground of the four truths and the account of the five aggregates.","n":"Lines 21-22 (ஈங்கு இது இல்லாவழி இல்லாகி / ஈங்கு இது உள்ளவழி உண்டு ஆகலின்) give the general formula of dependence before any of the twelve is named. The long run of ஆகி and உடைத்தாய் from line 16 is a table of contents for the whole kaathai: each item (sections, junctions, times, truths, aggregates, modes of speech, nayas, kinds of answer) is expounded later in this order.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","puththan","veedu","kandham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 7-33","lb":"MM30:7-33","ev":"","cl":[["all the Buddhas of past ages taught one and the same truth","buddhist","asserted",14,"aravanar"],["things arise in dependence: where this is not, that is not; where this is, that is","buddhist","asserted",21,"aravanar"],["seeing fault, deed and fruit as unstable, empty and suffering is the sure way to release","buddhist","asserted",30,"aravanar"]],"ag":["பெருந் தவ முனிவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:34-58":{"book":"manimekalai","num":276,"t":["மெய் வகை ஆறு வழக்கு முகம் எய்தி","நயங்கள் நான்கால் பயன்கள் எய்தி","இயன்ற நால்வகையால் வினா விடை உடைத்தாய்","நின்மதி இன்றி ஊழ்பாடு இன்றிப்","பின்போக்கு அல்லது பொன்றக் கெடாதாய்","பண்ணுநர் இன்றிப் பண்ணப் படாதாய்","யானும் இன்றி என்னதும் இன்றி","போனதும் இன்றி வந்ததும் இன்றி","முடித்தலும் இன்றி முடிவும் இன்றி","வினையும் பயனும் பிறப்பும் வீடும்","இனையன எல்லாம் தானே ஆகிய","பேதைமை செய்கை உணர்வே அருஉரு","வாயில் ஊறே நுகர்வே வேட்கை","பற்றே பவமே தோற்றம் வினைப்பயன்","இற்று என வகுத்த இயல்பு ஈர் ஆறும்","பிறந்தோர் அறியின் பெரும்பேறு அறிகுவர்","அறியார்ஆயின் ஆழ் நரகு அறிகுவர்","\"பேதைமை என்பது யாது?\" என வினவின்","ஓதிய இவற்றை உணராது மயங்கி","இயற்படு பொருளால் கண்டது மறந்து","முயற்கோடு உண்டு எனக் கேட்டது தெளிதல்","உலகம் மூன்றினும் உயிர் ஆம் உலகம்","அலகு இல பல் உயிர் அறு வகைத்து ஆகும்","மக்களும் தேவரும் பிரமரும் நரகரும்","தொக்க விலங்கும் பேயும் என்றே"],"p":"","r":"The teacher says the chain has no maker and is not made, has no I and no mine, no going and no coming, and is itself deed, fruit, birth and release; he names the twelve links, ignorance, action, consciousness, name-and-form, the six gates, contact, feeling, craving, clinging, becoming, birth and the fruit of deeds, says that those who know them gain the great reward and those who do not know hell, and defines ignorance as forgetting what reasoning has shown and trusting the report that a hare has horns, before naming the six kinds of beings.","n":"Line 37 (நின்மதி இன்றி ஊழ்பாடு இன்றி) is compressed: I read it as 'without a governing mind and without fixed fate', but நின்மதி may be read otherwise, and the tension link to the Ajivika rests on ஊழ்பாடு. Ignorance is defined not as plain not-knowing but as preferring hearsay (கேட்டது) to what has been established (கண்டது), with the hare's horn (முயற்கோடு) as the stock example of a non-thing; the same example returns at lines 208 and 216.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","pedhaimai","naragam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 34-58","lb":"MM30:34-58","ev":"","cl":[["the chain of dependent arising has no maker and no fixed fate","buddhist","asserted",37,"aravanar"],["in the chain there is no I and nothing that is mine","buddhist","asserted",40,"aravanar"],["those who know the twelve links gain the great reward; those who do not know them fall to hell","buddhist","asserted",49,"aravanar"],["ignorance is forgetting what reasoning has established and holding as clear what is only heard, as that a hare has horns","buddhist","asserted",54,"aravanar"],["living beings are of six kinds: humans, gods, Brahmas, hell-beings, animals and ghosts","buddhist","asserted",56,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:59-83":{"book":"manimekalai","num":277,"t":["நல்வினை தீவினை என்று இரு வகையால்","சொல்லப்பட்ட கருவில் சார்தலும்","கருவில் பட்ட பொழுதினுள் தோற்றி","வினைப்பயன் விளையுங்காலை உயிர்கட்கு","மனப் பேர் இன்பமும் கவலையும் காட்டும்","\"தீவினை என்பது யாது?\" என வினவின்","ஆய் தொடி நல்லாய்! ஆங்கு அது கேளாய்","கொலையே களவே காமத் தீவிழைவு","உலையா உடம்பில் தோன்றுவ மூன்றும்","பொய்யே குறளை கடுஞ்சொல் பயன் இல்","சொல் எனச் சொல்லில் தோன்றுவ நான்கும்","வெஃகல் வெகுளல் பொல்லாக் காட்சி என்று","உள்ளம் தன்னின் உருப்பன மூன்றும் எனப்","பத்து வகையால் பயன் தெரி புலவர்","இத் திறம் படரார் படர்குவர் ஆயின்","விலங்கும் பேயும் நரகரும் ஆகி","கலங்கிய உள்ளக் கவலையின் தோன்றுவர்","\"நல்வினை என்பது யாது?\" என வினவின்","சொல்லிய பத்தின் தொகுதியின் நீங்கிச்","சீலம் தாங்கித் தானம் தலைநின்று","மேல் என வகுத்த ஒருமூன்று திறத்துத்","தேவரும் மக்களும் பிரமரும் ஆகி","மேவிய மகிழ்ச்சி வினைப்பயன் உண்குவர்","உணர்வு எனப்படுவது உறங்குவோர் உணர்வின்","புரிவு இன்றாகிப் புலன் கொளாததுவே"],"p":"","r":"The teacher says that beings enter the womb by good or bad deeds and that the deed's fruit gives them joy or sorrow; he names the ten bad deeds (killing, theft and lust of the body; lying, slander, harsh and idle words of speech; coveting, anger and wrong view of the mind), says those who do them are born as animals, ghosts and hell-beings, and those who avoid them, keep the precepts and give are born as gods, humans and Brahmas; then he defines consciousness as awareness like a sleeper's, without intention or grasp of objects.","n":"The ten are sorted by where they arise: the body, speech and the mind (உள்ளம் தன்னின் உருப்பன, line 71), so coveting, anger and wrong view count as deeds before any act. Lines 82-83 begin the definitions of the twelve links in the middle of the unit; the address ஆய் தொடி நல்லாய் (line 65) is the teacher's only word to his hearer in the doctrinal part.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","kolai","naragam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 59-83","lb":"MM30:59-83","ev":"","cl":[["beings come to the womb by good or bad deeds, and the ripening deed brings joy or sorrow","buddhist","asserted",59,"aravanar"],["the bad deeds are ten: three of the body, four of speech and three of the mind","buddhist","asserted",72,"aravanar"],["those who do the ten bad deeds are born as animals, ghosts and hell-beings","buddhist","asserted",74,"aravanar"],["those who avoid them, keep the precepts and give are born as gods, humans and Brahmas","buddhist","asserted",78,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:84-108":{"book":"manimekalai","num":278,"t":["அருஉரு என்பது அவ் உணர்வு சார்ந்த","உயிரும் உடம்பும் ஆகும் என்ப","வாயில் ஆறும் ஆயுங்காலை","உள்ளம் உறுவிக்க உறும் இடன் ஆகும்","ஊறு என உரைப்பது உள்ளமும் வாயிலும்","வேறு புலன்களை மேவுதல் என்ப","நுகர்வே உணர்வு புலன்களை நுகர்தல்","வேட்கை விரும்பி நுகர்ச்சி ஆராமை","பற்று எனப்படுவது பசைஇய அறிவே","பவம் எனப்படுவது கரும ஈட்டம்","தரும் முறை இது எனத் தாம்தாம் சார்தல்","பிறப்பு எனப்படுவது அக் கருமப் பெற்றியின்","உறப் புணர் உள்ளம் சார்பொடு கதிகளில்","காரண காரிய உருக்களில் தோன்றல்","பிணி எனப்படுவது சார்பின் பிறிது ஆய்","இயற்கையின் திரிந்து உடம்பு இடும்பை புரிதல்","மூப்பு என மொழிவது அந்தத்து அளவும்","தாக்கும் நிலையாமையின் தாம் தளர்ந்திடுதல்","சாக்காடு என்பது அருஉருத் தன்மை","யாக்கை வீழ் கதிரென மறைந்திடுதல்","பேதைமை சார்வா செய்கை ஆகும்","செய்கை சார்வா உணர்ச்சி ஆகும்","உணர்ச்சி சார்வா அரூரு ஆகும்","அருஉருச் சார்வா வாயில் ஆகும்","வாயில் சார்வா ஊறு ஆகும்மே"],"p":"","r":"The teacher defines the rest of the links: name-and-form as the life and body that go with consciousness, the six gates as the place where contact happens, contact as mind and gate meeting objects, feeling as tasting them, craving as unsated wanting, clinging as sticky knowing, becoming as the heap of karma, birth as appearing in a destiny by that karma, and sickness, aging and death; then he begins the forward chain, each link arising with the one before as its support.","n":"Death is defined as the name-and-form vanishing like the setting sun (lines 102-103), with யாக்கை as the body that falls: no self passes on. Line 106 spells the link அரூரு where every other line has அருஉரு.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","avaa","pattru","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 84-108","lb":"MM30:84-108","ev":"","cl":[["becoming is the accumulation of karma","buddhist","asserted",93,"aravanar"],["each link arises with the one before it as its support: ignorance supports action, action consciousness, and so on","buddhist","asserted",104,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:109-124":{"book":"manimekalai","num":279,"t":["ஊறு சார்ந்து நுகர்ச்சி ஆகும்","நுகர்ச்சி சார்ந்து வேட்கை ஆகும்","வேட்கை சார்ந்து பற்று ஆகும்மே","பற்றின் தோன்றும் கருமத் தொகுதி","கருமத் தொகுதி காரணமாக","வருமே ஏனை வழிமுறைத் தோற்றம்","தோற்றம் சார்பின் மூப்பு பிணி சாக்காடு","அவலம் அரற்றுக் கவலை கையாறு எனத்","தவல் இல் துன்பம் தலைவரும் என்ப","ஊழின் மண்டிலமாச் சூழும் இந் நுகர்ச்சி","பேதைமை மீள செய்கை மீளும்","செய்கை மீள உணர்ச்சி மீளும்","உணர்ச்சி மீள அருஉரு மீளும்","அருஉரு மீள வாயில் மீளும்","வாயில் மீள ஊறு மீளும்","ஊறு மீள நுகர்ச்சி மீளும்"],"p":"","r":"The teacher completes the forward chain (contact gives feeling, feeling craving, craving clinging, clinging the heap of karma, and that the next birth, from which come aging, sickness, death, grief, lament, care and despair, an unending sorrow) and then turns to the reverse: as ignorance ceases, action ceases, and so on link by link.","n":"Line 118 calls the round ஊழின் மண்டிலம், the circle of ஊழ்: the word that elsewhere names fate here names the turning of the chain itself, which line 37 said has no fixed fate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","avaa","piravi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 109-124","lb":"MM30:109-124","ev":"","cl":[["from birth come aging, sickness, death, grief, lament, care and despair, an unending sorrow","buddhist","asserted",115,"aravanar"],["when ignorance ceases action ceases, and each later link ceases with the one before","buddhist","asserted",119,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:125-138":{"book":"manimekalai","num":280,"t":["நுகர்ச்சி மீள வேட்கை மீளும்","வேட்கை மீள பற்று மீளும்","பற்று மீள கருமத் தொகுதி","மீளும் கருமத் தொகுதி மீளத்","தோற்றம் மீளும் தோற்றம் மீளப்","பிறப்பு மீளும் பிறப்பு பிணி மூப்புச்","சாக்காடு அவலம் அரற்றுக் கவலை","கையாறு என்று இக் கடை இல் துன்பம்","எல்லாம் மீளும் இவ் வகையால் மீட்சி","ஆதிக் கண்டம் ஆகும் என்ப","பேதைமை செய்கை என்று இவை இரண்டும்","காரண வகைய ஆதலானே","இரண்டாம் கண்டம் ஆகும் என்ப","உணர்ச்சி அருஉரு வாயில் ஊறே"],"p":"","r":"The teacher finishes the reverse chain, in which birth and all its sorrow cease, and then begins the four sections: the first is this cessation, the second ignorance and action as the causes, and the third begins with consciousness.","n":"The reverse chain has தோற்றம் and பிறப்பு as two steps (lines 129-130), where the list at lines 45-47 has twelve links with தோற்றம் as the eleventh; the edition's text may double a link here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","veedu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 125-138","lb":"MM30:125-138","ev":"","cl":[["by the reverse of the chain all sorrow without end ceases","buddhist","asserted",133,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:139-154":{"book":"manimekalai","num":281,"t":["நுகர்ச்சி என்று நோக்கப்படுவன","முன்னவற்று இயல்பான் துன்னிய ஆதலின்","மூன்றாம் கண்டம் வேட்கை பற்று","கரும ஈட்டம் எனக் கட்டுரைப்பவை","மற்று அப் பெற்றி நுகர்ச்சி ஒழுக்கினுள்","குற்றமும் வினையும் ஆகலானே","நான்காம் கண்டம் பிறப்பே பிணியே","மூப்பே சாவு என மொழிந்திடும் துன்பம்","என இவை பிறப்பில் உழக்கு பயன் ஆதலின்","பிறப்பின் முதல் உணர்வு ஆதிச் சந்தி","நுகர்ச்சி ஒழுக்கொடு விழைவின் கூட்டம்","புகர்ச்சி இன்று அறிவது இரண்டாம் சந்தி","கன்மக் கூட்டத்தொடு வரு பிறப்பிடை","முன்னிச் செல்வது மூன்றாம் சந்தி","மூன்று வகைப் பிறப்பும் மொழியுங்காலை","ஆன்ற பிற மார்க்கத்து ஆய உணர்வே"],"p":"","r":"The teacher completes the four sections (consciousness to feeling, as results of what went before; craving, clinging and becoming, as fault and deed; birth, sickness, aging and death, as the suffering birth brings) and names three junctions: the first awareness of a birth, the knowing of feeling joined with desire, and the karma-heap moving toward the next birth; then he begins the three kinds of birth.","n":"The junctions (சந்தி) at lines 148-152 are the hinges between the sections, but lines 149-150 (புகர்ச்சி இன்று அறிவது) and 154 are hard to construe, and I cannot fix the second junction's sense beyond the joining of feeling and desire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","vinai","kutram","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 139-154","lb":"MM30:139-154","ev":"","cl":[["craving, clinging and becoming are the fault and the deed within the course of feeling","buddhist","asserted",141,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:155-169":{"book":"manimekalai","num":282,"t":["தோன்றல் வீடு எனத் துணிந்து தோன்றியும்","உணர்வு உள் அடங்க உருவாய்த் தோன்றியும்","உணர்வும் உருவும் உடங்கத் தோன்றிப்","புணர்தரு மக்கள் தெய்வம் விலங்கு ஆகையும்","காலம் மூன்றும் கருதுங்காலை","இறந்த காலம் என்னல் வேண்டும்","மறந்த பேதைமை செய்கை ஆனவற்றை","நிகழ்ந்த காலம் என நேரப்படுமே","உணர்வே அருஉரு வாயில் ஊறே","நுகர்வே வேட்கை பற்றே பவமே","தோற்றம் என்று இவை சொல்லுங்காலை","எதிர்காலம் என இசைக்கப்படுமே","பிறப்பே பிணியே மூப்பே சாவே","அவலம் அரற்று கவலை கையாறுகள்","குலவிய குற்றம் எனக் கூறப்படுமே"],"p":"","r":"The teacher gives three kinds of birth (appearing with awareness alone, as form with awareness hidden, and with awareness and form together as humans, gods and animals) and then the three times: ignorance and action belong to the past, consciousness through becoming to the present, and birth with its sorrows to the future; the last line introduces fault.","n":"Each time-label stands before its list: இறந்த காலம் (160) before ignorance and action, நிகழ்ந்த காலம் (162) before consciousness to becoming, எதிர்காலம் (166) before birth and its sorrows; read the other way, the present links would become future. Line 169's குற்றம் opens the next list rather than closing this one. The three births (155-158) are obscure, and line 155's தோன்றல் வீடு எனத் துணிந்து is a reading I cannot fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","piravi","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 155-169","lb":"MM30:155-169","ev":"","cl":[["the twelve links fall across three times: past, present and future","buddhist","asserted",159,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:170-182":{"book":"manimekalai","num":283,"t":["அவாவே பற்றே பேதைமை என்று இவை","புனையும் அடை பவமும் வினை செயல் ஆகும்","உணர்ச்சி அருஉரு வாயில் ஊறே","நுகர்ச்சி பிறப்பு மூப்புப் பிணி சாவு இவை","நிகழ்ச்சிப் பயன் ஆங்கே நேருங்காலை","குற்றமும் வினையும் பயனும் துன்பம்","பெற்ற தோற்றப் பெற்றிகள் நிலையா","எப்பொருளுக்கும் ஆன்மா இலை என","இப்படி உணரும் இவை வீட்டு இயல்பு ஆம்","உணர்வே அருஉரு வாயில் ஊறே","நுகர்வே பிறப்பே பிணி மூப்புச் சாவே","அவலம் அரற்றுக் கவலை கையாறு என","நுவலப் படுவன நோய் ஆகும்மே"],"p":"","r":"The teacher sorts the links into fault (craving, clinging, ignorance), deed (becoming and action) and fruit (consciousness to feeling, birth, aging, sickness, death); seeing that these are sorrow, unstable, and that no thing has a self, is the nature of release; and he names the links from consciousness to despair as the disease.","n":"எப்பொருளுக்கும் ஆன்மா இலை (line 177) is the chapter's one plain statement of no-self, and it is placed as what is to be seen for release, not argued against a rival here. Line 182 begins the four truths with நோய், the disease, the medical frame that runs through the epic's name for the Buddha as the physician of birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","veedu","nilaiyaamai","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 170-182","lb":"MM30:170-182","ev":"","cl":[["fault, deed and fruit are suffering and unstable, and no thing has a self","buddhist","asserted",177,"aravanar"],["seeing them so is the nature of release","buddhist","asserted",178,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:183-197":{"book":"manimekalai","num":284,"t":["அந் நோய் தனக்குப்","பேதைமை செய்கை அவாவே பற்றுக்","கரும ஈட்டம் இவை காரணம் ஆகும்","துன்பம் தோற்றம் பற்றே காரணம்","இன்பம் வீடே பற்றிலி காரணம்","ஒன்றிய உரையே வாய்மை நான்கு ஆவது","உருவு நுகர்ச்சி குறிப்பே பாவனை","உள்ள அறிவு இவை ஐங்கந்தம் ஆவன","அறுவகை வழக்கும் மறு இன்று கிளப்பின்","தொகையே தொடர்ச்சி தன்மை மிகுத்துரை","இயைந்துரை என்ற நான்கினும் இயைந்த","உண்மை வழக்கும் இன்மை வழக்கும்","உள்ளது சார்ந்த உண்மை வழக்கும்","இல்லது சார்ந்த இன்மை வழக்கும்","உள்ளது சார்ந்த இன்மை வழக்கும்"],"p":"","r":"The teacher gives the four truths (the disease, its cause in ignorance, action, craving, clinging and becoming, sorrow caused by birth and clinging, and joy that is release caused by not clinging), names the five aggregates (form, feeling, perception, formation, consciousness), and begins the six modes of speech.","n":"Line 187 (இன்பம் வீடே பற்றிலி காரணம்) names release as joy, so the third and fourth truths are put as a cause and its result, parallel to the first two. The four truths are called வாய்மை, but they are not the virtue of truthfulness, so I have not tagged vaaimai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","pattru","kandham","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 183-197","lb":"MM30:183-197","ev":"","cl":[["the cause of sorrow is birth and clinging; the cause of release, which is joy, is not clinging","buddhist","asserted",186,"aravanar"],["what is spoken of as a person is five aggregates: form, feeling, perception, formation and consciousness","buddhist","asserted",189,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:198-213":{"book":"manimekalai","num":285,"t":["இல்லது சார்ந்த உண்மை வழக்கும் எனச்","சொல்லிய தொகைத் திறம் உடம்பு நீர் நாடு","தொடர்ச்சி வித்து முளை தாள் என்று இந்","நிகழ்ச்சியில் அவற்றை நெல் என வழங்குதல்","இயல்பு மிகுத்துரை ஈறுடைத்து என்றும்","தோன்றிற்று என்றும் மூத்தது என்றும்","மூன்றின் ஒன்றின் இயல்பு மிகுத்துரைத்தல்","இயைந்துரை என்பது எழுத்துப் பல கூடச்","சொல் எனத் தோற்றும் பல நாள் கூடிய","எல்லையைத் திங்கள் என்று வழங்குதல்","உள் வழக்கு உணர்வு இல் வழக்கு முயற்கோடு","உள்ளது சார்ந்த உள் வழக்காகும்","சித்தத்துடனே ஒத்த நுகர்ச்சி","உள்ளது சார்ந்த இல் வழக்காகும்","சித்தம் உற்பவித்தது மின்போல் என்கை","இல்லது சார்ந்த உண்மை வழக்காகும்"],"p":"","r":"The teacher explains the modes of speech by example: the collective name (the body; water; a country), the continuity name (seed, sprout and stalk all called paddy), the name stressing one stage (it perishes, it arose, it aged), the joined name (letters together as a word, days together as a month), and four modes of affirming and denying what exists and what does not, as consciousness, the hare's horn and the mind that flashes up like lightning.","n":"The point of the modes is that names like paddy or month are conventions for collections and continuities, not things in themselves. The examples for the four existence-modes at lines 208-213 are terse, and which example belongs to which mode is my best construal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sol","saarbu","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 198-213","lb":"MM30:198-213","ev":"","cl":[["names such as paddy or month are conventions for continuities and collections","buddhist","asserted",201,"aravanar"],["mind arises and passes like a flash of lightning","buddhist","asserted",212,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:214-229":{"book":"manimekalai","num":286,"t":["காரணம் இன்றிக் காரியம் நேர்தல்","இல்லது சார்ந்த இல் வழக்கு ஆகும்","முயற்கோடு இன்மையின் தோற்றமும் இல் எனல்","நான்கு நயம் எனத் தோன்றப்படுவன","ஒற்றுமை வேற்றுமை புரிவின்மை இயல்பு என்க","காரண காரியம் ஆகிய பொருள்களை","ஒன்றா உணர்தல் ஒற்றுமை நயம் ஆம்","வீற்று வீற்றாக வேதனை கொள்வது","வேற்றுமை நயம் என வேண்டல் வேண்டும்","பொன்றக் கெடா அப் பொருள் வழிப்பொருள்களுக்கு","ஒன்றிய காரணம் உதவு காரியத்தைத்","தருதற்கு உள்ளம் தான் இலை என்றல்","புரிவின்மை நயம் எனப் புகறல் வேண்டும்","நெல் வித்து அகத்துள் நெல் முளை தோற்றும் எனல்","நல்ல இயல்பு நயம் இவற்றில் நாம் கொள்பயன்","தொக்க பொருள் அலது ஒன்று இல்லை என்றும்"],"p":"","r":"The teacher ends the modes of speech (an effect without a cause is the mode of denial resting on what is not, as there is no arising for a hare's horn) and gives the four nayas: oneness (cause and effect seen as one), difference (each felt apart), non-agency (no mind in the cause that intends its effect) and own-nature (paddy seed gives paddy sprout), and begins what is gained from them: that there is nothing but aggregated things.","n":"The non-agency naya (lines 223-226) says the cause has no intention of producing the effect, which applies the chain's 'no maker' (line 39) to every cause and effect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","arivu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 214-229","lb":"MM30:214-229","ev":"","cl":[["an effect does not come without a cause","buddhist","asserted",214,"aravanar"],["a cause has no mind that intends its effect","buddhist","asserted",225,"aravanar"],["there is nothing but aggregated things","buddhist","asserted",229,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:230-243":{"book":"manimekalai","num":287,"t":["அப் பொருளிடைப் பற்று ஆகாது என்றும்","செய்வானொடு கோட்பாடு இலை என்றும்","எய்து காரணத்து ஆம் காரியம் என்றும்","அதுவும் அன்று அது அலாததும் அன்று என்றும்","விதிமுறை தொகையினால் விரிந்த நான்கும்","வினா விடை நான்கு உள","துணிந்து சொல்லல் கூறிட்டு மொழிதல்","வினாவின் விடுத்தல் வாய் வாளாமை எனத்","\"தோன்றியது கெடுமோ? கெடாதோ?\" என்றால்","\"கேடு உண்டு\" என்றல் துணிந்து சொலல் ஆகும்","\"செத்தான் பிறப்பானோ? பிறவானோ?\"","என்று செப்பின்","\"பற்று இறந்தானோ? அல் மகனோ?\" எனல்","மிகக் கூறிட்டு மொழிதல் என விளம்புவர்"],"p":"","r":"The teacher completes what the four nayas yield (no clinging to things, no doer apart from the doing, effects come from causes, and the middle that is neither this nor not-this) and names the four ways of answering a question, illustrating two: 'what has arisen will perish' is the definite answer, and a question whether the dead man will be reborn is answered by asking whether he was free of clinging.","n":"The rebirth question (lines 240-242) is not answered yes or no but turned to the condition: whether one is reborn depends on whether clinging has ended, which is the chain applied to a person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saarbu","pattru","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 230-243","lb":"MM30:230-243","ev":"","cl":[["there is no doer apart from the doing","buddhist","asserted",231,"aravanar"],["whatever has arisen will perish","buddhist","asserted",239,"aravanar"],["whether a dead man is reborn depends on whether he has ended clinging","buddhist","asserted",242,"aravanar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"mm:30:244-264":{"book":"manimekalai","num":288,"t":["வினாவின் விடுத்தல் \"முட்டை முந்திற்றோ","பனை முந்திற்றோ? எனக் கட்டுரை செய்\"","என்றால் \"எம் முட்டைக்கு எப் பனை\" என்றல்","வாய் வாளாமை \"ஆகாயப் பூப்","பழைதோ, புதிதோ?\" என்று புகல்வான்","உரைக்கு மாற்றம் உரையாது இருத்தல்","கட்டும் வீடும் அதன் காரணத்தது","ஒட்டித் தருதற்கு உரியோர் இல்லை","யாம் மேல் உரைத்த பொருள்கட்கு எல்லாம்","காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம் காரணம்","அநித்தம் துக்கம் அநான்மா அசுசி என","தனித்துப் பார்த்துப் பற்று அறுத்திடுதல்","மைத்திரி கருணா முதிதை என்று அறிந்து","திருந்து நல் உணர்வான் செற்றம் அற்றிடுக!","சுருதி சிந்தனா பாவனா தரிசனை","கருதி உய்த்து மயக்கம் கடிக!","இந் நால் வகையான் மனத்திருள் நீங்கு!' என்று","முன் பின் மலையா மங்கல மொழியின்","ஞான தீபம் நன்கனம் காட்டத்","தவத் திறம் பூண்டு தருமம் கேட்டுப்","'பவத் திறம் அறுக!' எனப் பாவை நோற்றனள் என்"],"p":"","r":"The teacher illustrates the last two kinds of answer (the question whether egg or palmyra came first set aside by asking 'which egg, which palmyra?', and silence before a question about the age of a sky-flower), says bondage and release have their own causes and no one can grant them, names desire, anger and delusion as the cause of all he has taught, and prescribes a remedy for each: seeing impermanence, sorrow, no-self and impurity to cut clinging, friendliness, compassion and sympathetic joy to end hatred, and hearing, reflecting, cultivating and seeing to drive off delusion; the narrator says that, the lamp of knowledge shown, Manimekalai took up austerity and vowed to cut the round of becoming.","n":"Lines 250-251 (கட்டும் வீடும் அதன் காரணத்தது / ஒட்டித் தருதற்கு உரியோர் இல்லை) deny any giver of bondage or release. Line 253's காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம் is the same triad, in the same order, as Kural 360; three words fall short of an echo, so it is a link. Line 263 (தவத் திறம் பூண்டு தருமம் கேட்டு) repeats the title of kaathai 29, and line 264 gives this kaathai its title.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","thavam","kaamam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Manimekalai 30, lines 244-264","lb":"MM30:244-264","ev":"Having heard the teaching, Manimekalai takes up austerity and vows to end the round of births.","cl":[["bondage and release arise from their own causes, and no one exists who can grant them","buddhist","asserted",250,"aravanar"],["desire, anger and delusion are the cause of all that has been taught","buddhist","asserted",253,"aravanar"],["clinging is cut by seeing things as impermanent, sorrowful, selfless and impure; hatred by friendliness, compassion and sympathetic joy; delusion by hearing, reflection, cultivation and insight","buddhist","asserted",254,"aravanar"]],"ag":["மணிமேகலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறவண அடிகள்","மணிமேகலை"],"ch":"mm:32"},"si:0:1-11":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":1,"t":["குணவாயில் கோட்டத்து அரசுதுறந்து இருந்த","குடக்கோச் சேரல் இளங்கோ வடிகட்குக்","குன்றக் குறவர் ஒருங்குடன் கூடிப்","பொலம்பூ வேங்கை நலங்கிளர் கொழுநிழல்","ஒருமுலை இழந்தாள்ஓர் திருமா பத்தினிக்கு","அமரர்க்கு அரசன் தமர்வந்து ஈண்டிஅவள்","காதல் கொழுநனைக் காட்டி அவளொடுஎம்","கட்புலம் காண விண்புலம் போயது","இறும்பூது போலும்அஃது அறிந்தருள் நீயென,","அவனுழை இருந்த தண்தமிழ்ச் சாத்தன்","யான்அறி குவன்அது பட்டதுஎன் றுரைப்போன்:"],"p":"","r":"The hill kuravar gather before Ilango, the Chera prince who gave up the throne and lives at the shrine of the eastern gate, and tell him that under a flowering vengai they saw a great pathini who had lost one breast taken up to heaven, with her beloved husband shown to her, by the retinue of the king of the gods; they ask him to find out what this wonder means, and Saathan, sitting beside him, says he knows how it came about.","n":"The epic opens from the outside: the witnesses are hill folk who do not know the woman's name, and the frame puts the story in the mouth of a merchant-poet told to a renouncer prince. The poet himself is introduced by what he gave up (அரசுதுறந்து), so the work's narrator stands from the first line in the posture of renunciation, though the patikam makes no doctrinal claim about it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","thuravu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 0, lines 1-11","lb":"SI0:1-11","ev":"The hill kuravar report to Ilango that they saw a one-breasted woman carried to heaven with her husband, and Saathan offers to explain it.","cl":[],"ag":["குன்றக் குறவர்","இளங்கோ வடிகள்","சாத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குன்றக் குறவர்","இளங்கோ வடிகள்"],"ch":"si:1"},"si:0:12-26":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":2,"t":["ஆரங் கண்ணிச் சோழன் மூதூர்ப்","பேராச் சிறப்பின் புகார்நக ரத்துக்","கோவலன் என்பான்ஓர் வாணிகன் அவ்வூர்","நாடகம் ஏத்தும் நாடகக் கணிகையொடு","ஆடிய கொள்கையின் அரும்பொருள் கேடுறக்","கண்ணகி என்பாள் மனைவி அவள்கால்","பண்ணமை சிலம்பு பகர்தல் வேண்டிப்","பாடல்சால் சிறப்பிற் பாண்டியன் பெருஞ்சீர்","மாட மதுரை புகுந்தனன், அதுகொண்டு","மன்பெரும் பீடிகை மறுகிற் செல்வோன்","பொன்செய் கொல்லன் தன்கைக் காட்டக்","கோப்பெருந் தேவிக்கு அல்லதை இச்சிலம்பு","யாப்புறவு இல்லைஈங்கு இருக்கயென்று ஏகிப்","பண்டுதான் கொண்ட சில்லரிச் சிலம்பினைக்","கண்டனன் பிறன்ஓர் கள்வன் கையென,"],"p":"","r":"Saathan tells how Kovalan, a merchant of the Chola's Puhar, lost his great wealth living with a dancing courtesan, came to Madurai to sell an anklet of his wife Kannaki, and showed it to the royal goldsmith, who told him to wait, went off, and said to the king that he had found, in a stranger's hand, the thief of the anklet he himself had once taken.","n":"The summary already lets the listener know the goldsmith is the thief: பண்டுதான் கொண்ட சிலம்பு, the anklet he himself took earlier, so the accusation is shown as a cover for his own theft before any karma is mentioned. The courtesan is not named here; the loss of wealth is put down to Kovalan's own way of life (ஆடிய கொள்கையின்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","selvam","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 0, lines 12-26","lb":"SI0:12-26","ev":"Kovalan, ruined, brings Kannaki's anklet to Madurai to sell, and the royal goldsmith who had stolen the queen's anklet denounces him as the thief.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","பொன்செய் கொல்லன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாத்தன்","இளங்கோ வடிகள்"],"ch":"si:1"},"si:0:27-36":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":3,"t":["வினைவிளை காலம் ஆதலின் யாவதும்","சினையலர் வேம்பன் தேரா னாகிக்","கன்றிய காவலர்க் கூஉய்அக் கள்வனைக்","கொன்றுஅச் சிலம்பு கொணர்க ஈங்கெனக்","கொலைக்களப் பட்ட கோவலன் மனைவி","நிலைக்களம் காணாள் நெடுங்கண் நீர்உகுத்துப்","பத்தினி யாகலின் பாண்டியன் கேடுற","முத்தார மார்பின் முலைமுகந் திருகி","நிலைகெழு கூடல் நீள்எரி ஊட்டிய","பலர்புகழ் பத்தினி யாகும் இவள்என,"],"p":"","r":"Saathan says that because it was the time for the deed to ripen, the Pandya did not inquire, called his guards and ordered them to kill the thief and bring the anklet; Kovalan's wife, finding no ground to stand on, wept, and being a pathini, twisted off her breast and set fire to Madurai so that the Pandya fell, and this is the woman the kuravar saw, the pathini praised by many.","n":"Two causes stand side by side: the ripening of the deed (வினைவிளை காலம் ஆதலின்) explains why the king failed to inquire (தேரானாகி), and Kannaki's being a pathini (பத்தினி யாகலின்) explains the city's ruin. Karma here does not excuse the king's fault; it is given as the reason the fault happened, and the burning falls on the king all the same.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","kodungol","vinai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 0, lines 27-36","lb":"SI0:27-36","ev":"The Pandya, without inquiring, has Kovalan killed; Kannaki weeps, tears off her breast and burns Madurai.","cl":[["when the time for a deed to ripen comes, even a king fails to judge rightly","general","asserted",27,"saathan"],["a chaste wife's power can bring ruin on a king who wronged her","general","asserted",33,"saathan"]],"ag":["நெடுஞ்செழியன்","காவலர்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாத்தன்","இளங்கோ வடிகள்"],"ch":"si:1"},"si:0:37-54":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":4,"t":["வினைவிளை காலம் என்றீர் யாதுஅவர்","வினைவிளைவு என்ன, விறலோய் கேட்டி","அதிராச் சிறப்பின் மதுரை மூதூர்க்","கொன்றையஞ் சடைமுடி மன்றப் பொதியிலில்","வெள்ளியம் பலத்து நள்ளிருட் கிடந்தேன்","ஆர்அஞர் உற்ற வீரபத் தினிமுன்","மதுரைமா தெய்வம் வந்து தோன்றிக்","கொதியழல் சீற்றம் கொங்கையின் விளைத்தோய்","முதிர்வினை நுங்கட்கு முடிந்தது ஆகலின்","முந்தைப் பிறப்பில் பைந்தொடி கணவனொடு","சிங்கா வண்புகழ்ச் சிங்க புரத்துச்","சங்கமன் என்னும் வாணிகன் மனைவி","இட்ட சாபம் கட்டியது ஆகலின்","வார்ஒலி கூந்தல்நின் மணமகன் தன்னை","ஈர்ஏழ் நாளகத்து எல்லை நீங்கி","வானோர் தங்கள் வடிவின் அல்லதை","ஈனோர் வடிவில் காண்டல் இல்எனக்","கோட்டம்இல் கட்டுரை கேட்டனன் யான்என,"],"p":"","r":"Ilango asks what deed it was that ripened, and Saathan answers that, lying at night in the silver hall of Madurai, he saw the great goddess of Madurai appear before the grieving pathini and tell her that their ripe deed had run its course because of a curse laid in a former birth by the wife of Sangaman, a merchant of Singapuram, and that she would see her husband after fourteen days only in the form of the gods, not in human form; this unbiased word, he says, he heard.","n":"The patikam names only the curse and its author, the widow of Sangaman, and not what was done to Sangaman; the reader learns only in the Katturai Kaathai that Kovalan in that birth had him killed as a spy. Saathan vouches for the account as a witness, not as a sage: he overheard it (கேட்டனன் யான்). Lines 50 to 53 are repeated almost word for word at Silappathikaram 23.173-176.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","saabam","piravi","pathini"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 0, lines 37-54","lb":"SI0:37-54","ev":"Asked about the deed that ripened, Saathan reports overhearing the goddess of Madurai tell Kannaki that a curse from a former birth has come to fruit and that she will see her husband in divine form after fourteen days.","cl":[["a curse laid in a former birth binds and comes to fruit in this one","general","asserted",49,"saathan"],["a deed once ripe runs its course and then ends","general","asserted",45,"madurai_maadheyvam"]],"ag":["இளங்கோ வடிகள்","மதுரைமா தெய்வம்","சாத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாத்தன்","இளங்கோ வடிகள்"],"ch":"si:1"},"si:0:55-68":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":5,"t":["அரைசியல் பிழைத்தோர்க்கு அறம்கூற்று ஆவதூஉம்","உரைசால் பத்தினிக்கு உயர்ந்தோர் ஏத்தலும்","ஊழ்வினை உருத்துவந்து ஊட்டும் என்பதூஉம்","சூழ்வினைச் சிலம்பு காரண மாகச்","சிலப்பதி காரம் என்னும் பெயரால்","நாட்டுதும் யாம்ஓர் பாட்டுடைச் செய்யுள்என,","முடிகெழு வேந்தர் மூவர்க்கும் உரியது","அடிகள் நீரே அருளுகஎன் றார்க்குஅவர்,","மங்கல வாழ்த்துப் பாடலும், குரவர்","மனையறம் படுத்த காதையும், நடம்நவில்","மங்கை மாதவி அரங்கேற்று காதையும்,","அந்தி மாலைச் சிறப்புசெய் காதையும்,","இந்திர விழவூர் எடுத்த காதையும்,","கடலாடு காதையும்,"],"p":"","r":"Ilango declares that, to show that dharma becomes death to those who err in kingship, that the great praise a renowned pathini, and that the deed of the past comes in wrath and makes one eat its fruit, he will set down a song-poem named Silappathikaram with the anklet as its cause; told that it belongs to all three crowned kings and that he himself should compose it, he composes, and the list of its kaathais begins: the benediction, the wedding household, Madhavi's debut, the evening, the Indra festival, the sea bathing.","n":"The three morals are stated as theses the poem will show (ஆவதூஉம், ஏத்தலும், என்பதூஉம்), and the title is derived from the anklet as the cause (சிலம்பு காரணமாக) of all three. The first is narrowed to kings (அரைசியல் பிழைத்தோர்க்கு), where the venba of the Vazhakkurai Kaathai gives it as a general saying about all wrongdoers. The third sits beside the first without being reconciled with it: the poem holds both that the king's error is punished and that the past deed had to come. Who speaks lines 55 to 60 is not marked; the யாம் of நாட்டுதும் can be read as Ilango's resolve, with lines 61 and 62 as Saathan's reply, or as Saathan's proposal; this marking follows the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","pathini","oozh","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 0, lines 55-68","lb":"SI0:55-68","ev":"Ilango resolves to compose the Silappathikaram on the three morals, and is urged to do so as a poem for all three kings.","cl":[["dharma itself becomes death to rulers who go wrong in kingship","general","asserted",55,"ilango"],["the exalted praise a pathini of great renown","general","asserted",56,"ilango"],["the deed of a former time comes in wrath and makes its doer eat its fruit","general","asserted",57,"ilango"]],"ag":["இளங்கோ வடிகள்","சாத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளங்கோ வடிகள்","சாத்தன்"],"ch":"si:1"},"si:0:69-90":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":6,"t":["மடல்அவிழ் கானல்வரியும், வேனில்வந் திறுத்தென","மாதவி இரங்கிய காதையும், தீதுடைக்","கனாத்திறம் உரைத்த காதையும், வினாத்திறத்து","நாடுகாண் காதையும், காடுகாண் காதையும்,","வேட்டுவர் வரியும், தோட்டலர் கோதையொடு","புறஞ்சேரி இறுத்த காதையும், கறங்குஇசை","ஊர்க்காண் காதையும், சீர்சால் நங்கை","அடைக்கலக் காதையும், கொலைக்களக் காதையும்,","ஆய்ச்சியர் குரவையும், தீத்திறம் கேட்ட","துன்ப மாலையும், நண்பகல் நடுங்கிய","ஊர்சூழ் வரியும், சீர்சால் வேந்தனொடு","வழக்குரை காதையும், வஞ்சின மாலையும்,","அழல்படு காதையும், அருந்தெய்வம் தோன்றிக்","கட்டுரை காதையும், மட்டலர் கோதையர்","குன்றக் குரவையும், என்றுஇவை அனைத்துடன்","காட்சி, கால்கோள், நீர்ப்படை, நடுகல்,","வாழ்த்து, வரந்தரு காதையொடு","இவ்வா றைந்தும்","உரையிடை இட்ட பாட்டுடைச் செய்யுள்","உரைசால் அடிகள் அருள மதுரைக்","கூல வாணிகன் சாத்தன் கேட்டனன்,","இது, பால்வகை தெரிந்த பதிகத்தின் மரபென்."],"p":"","r":"The list of the thirty kaathais continues from the Kaanal Vari through Madhavi's lament, the dream, the journey, the killing and the plea, to the burning, the goddess's word, the hill kuravai and the five Vanchi kaathais ending with the Varantharu Kaathai, and the patikam closes by saying that this song-poem with prose set among it was composed by Adigal and heard by Saathan the grain merchant of Madurai.","n":"Line 86 (இவ்வா றைந்தும், six fives) gives the count of thirty, which the list bears out. The closing lines describe the form, verse with prose between (உரையிடை இட்ட பாட்டுடைச் செய்யுள்), and name Saathan as the first hearer, so the frame that began with him telling the story ends with him receiving the poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 0, lines 69-90","lb":"SI0:69-90","ev":"Adigal composes the poem and Saathan, the grain merchant of Madurai, hears it.","cl":[],"ag":["இளங்கோ வடிகள்","சாத்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:1"},"si:1:1-12":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":7,"t":["திங்களைப் போற்றுதும் திங்களைப் போற்றுதும்","கொங்கலர்த்தார்ச் சென்னி குளிர்வெண் குடைபோன்றிவ்","வங்கண் உலகுஅளித்த லான்.","ஞாயிறு போற்றுதும் ஞாயிறு போற்றுதும்","காவிரி நாடன் திகிரிபோல் பொற்கோட்டு","மேரு வலம்திரி தலான்.","மாமழை போற்றுதும் மாமழை போற்றுதும்","நாமநீர் வேலி உலகிற்கு அவனளிபோல்","மேநின்று தாஞ்சுரத்த லான்.","பூம்புகார் போற்றுதும் பூம்புகார் போற்றுதும்","வீங்குநீர் வேலி உலகிற்கு அவன்குலத்தொடு","ஓங்கிப் பரந்துஒழுக லான்."],"p":"","r":"Four short venbas each hail a power twice, the moon, the sun, the great rain and the city Puhar, and give as the reason for praising each that it resembles the Chola: the moon his cool white umbrella sheltering the world, the sun his wheel of rule circling Meru, the rain his bounty pouring on the sea-girt earth, and Puhar his lineage rising and spreading.","n":"The comparison runs backwards from the usual royal praise: the king is not likened to the sun and moon, the heavenly bodies are praised because they are like the king (the ablative ஆன் of அளித்தலான், திரிதலான், சுரத்தலான், ஒழுகலான் gives the reason). The opening worship names no god; nature and the city stand where an invocation would be, and the king is in every stanza without being named except by the titles சென்னி and காவிரி நாடன்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","mazhai","arasu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 1, lines 1-12","lb":"SI1:1-12","ev":"","cl":[["a king's bounty sustains the world as the rain does","general","asserted",8,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:2"},"si:1:13-29":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":8,"t":["ஆங்கு,","பொதியில் ஆயினும் இமயம் ஆயினும்","பதிஎழு அறியாப் பழங்குடி கெழீஇய","பொதுஅறு சிறப்பின் புகாரே ஆயினும்","நடுக்கின்றி நிலைஇய என்பது அல்லதை","ஒடுக்கம் கூறார் உயர்ந்தோர் உண்மையின்","முடித்த கேள்வி முழுதுணர்ந் தோரே.","அதனால்,","நாகநீள் நகரொடு நாகநாடு அதனொடு","போகம்நீள் புகழ்மன்னும் புகார்நகர் அதுதன்னில்","மாகவான் நிகர்வண்கை மாநாய்கன் குலக்கொம்பர்","ஈகைவான் கொடியன்னாள் ஈராறுஆண்டு அகவையாள்,","அவளுந்தான்,","போதில்ஆர் திருவினாள் புகழுடை வடிவென்றும்","தீதிலா வடமீனின் திறம்இவள் திறம்என்றும்","மாதரார் தொழுதுஏத்த வயங்கிய பெருங்குணத்துக்","காதலாள் பெயர்மன்னும் கண்ணகிஎன் பாள்மன்னோ,"],"p":"","r":"The narrator says that the learned, whatever they say of Pothiyil, the Himalaya or Puhar of the ancient families who never leave it, speak of these only as standing unshaken and never of their decline, and in that city he introduces the twelve-year-old daughter of the merchant Maanaaygan, Kannaki, whom women worship as Lakshmi's beauty and the faultless northern star's virtue.","n":"Puhar is set beside two mountains as a thing that does not move, and the claim is given to the wise of complete learning (முடித்த கேள்வி முழுதுணர்ந்தோர்), so the epic opens on a confidence in the city's permanence that its sequel will undo. Kannaki is first named by her father's house and by others' praise: she does nothing in the unit, and the star of the faultless wife (வடமீன், the Arundhati star) is what women see in her before her story begins. Her name ends the unit with மன்னோ, the particle of something past and lasting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kudi","pugazh","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 1, lines 13-29","lb":"SI1:13-29","ev":"The narrator introduces Kannaki, twelve-year-old daughter of the merchant Maanaaygan of Puhar.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:2"},"si:1:30-44":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":9,"t":["ஆங்கு,","பெருநிலம் முழுதாளும் பெருமகன் தலைவைத்த","ஒருதனிக் குடிகளொடு உயர்ந்தோங்கு செல்வத்தான்","வருநிதி பிறர்க்குஆர்த்தும் மாசாத்து வான்என்பான்","இருநிதிக் கிழவன்மகன் ஈரெட்டுஆண்டு அகவையான்,","அவனுந்தான்,","மண்தேய்த்த புகழினான் மதிமுக மடவார்தம்","பண்தேய்த்த மொழியினார் ஆயத்துப் பாராட்டிக்","கண்டுஏத்தும் செவ்வேள்என்று இசைபோக்கிக் காதலால்","கொண்டுஏத்தும் கிழமையான் கோவலன்என் பான்மன்னோ.","அவரை,","இருபெருங் குரவரும் ஒருபெரு நாளால்","மணஅணி காண மகிழ்ந்தனர், மகிழ்ந்துழி","யானை எருத்தத்து அணிஇழையார் மேல்இரீஇ","மாநகர்க்கு ஈந்தார் மணம்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator introduces Kovalan, sixteen, son of the merchant Maasaathuvan, master of great wealth who pours out his coming riches for others and whose house stands with the families that the king of the whole earth honours, a youth whom women hail as Murugan come again; then the two fathers, delighting to see them married, set the bride on an elephant's neck and announce the wedding to the city.","n":"Both introductions are built alike (அவளுந்தான் / அவனுந்தான், ending பாள்மன்னோ / பான்மன்னோ), so bride and groom are presented as a matched pair of houses before they are persons; Kovalan's praise, though, is all of women's admiration (மதிமுக மடவார், ஆயத்துப் பாராட்டி), a hint of the charm that will draw him to Madhavi. The father's wealth is praised for being given away (வருநிதி பிறர்க்குஆர்த்தும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","selvam","eegai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 1, lines 30-44","lb":"SI1:30-44","ev":"The two fathers arrange Kovalan and Kannaki's marriage and announce it to the city from an elephant's back.","cl":[],"ag":["இருபெருங் குரவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:2"},"si:1:45-68":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":10,"t":["அவ்வழி,","முரசுஇயம்பின, முருகுஅதிர்ந்தன, முறைஎழுந்தன பணிலம்,வெண்குடை","அரசுஎழுந்ததொர் படிஎழுந்தன, அகலுள்மங்கல அணிஎழுந்தது.","மாலைதாழ் சென்னி வயிரமணித் தூணகத்து","நீல விதானத்து நித்திலப்பூம் பந்தர்க்கீழ்","வான்ஊர் மதியம் சகடுஅணைய வானத்துச்","சாலி ஒருமீன் தகையாளைக் கோவலன்","மாமுது பார்ப்பான் மறைவழி காட்டிடத்","தீவலம் செய்வது காண்பார்க்கண் நோன்புஎன்னை.","விரையினர் மலரினர் விளங்கு மேனியர்","உரையினர் பாட்டினர் ஒசிந்த நோக்கினர்","சாந்தினர் புகையினர் தயங்கு கோதையர்","ஏந்துஇள முலையினர் இடித்த சுண்ணத்தர்","விளக்கினர் கலத்தினர் விரித்த பாலிகை","முளைக்குட நிரையினர் முகிழ்த்த மூரலர்","போதொடு விரிகூந்தல் பொலன்நறுங் கொடிஅன்னார்","காதலற் பிரியாமல் கவவுக்கை ஞெகிழாமல்","தீதுஅறுக எனஏத்திச் சின்மலர் கொடுதூவி","அங்கண் உலகின் அருந்ததி அன்னாளை","மங்கல நல்அமளி ஏற்றினார், தங்கிய","இப்பால் இமயத்து இருத்திய வாள்வேங்கை","உப்பாலைப் பொன்கோட்டு உழையதா எப்பாலும்","செருமிகு சினவேல் செம்பியன்","ஒருதனி ஆழி உருட்டுவோன் எனவே."],"p":"","r":"Drums, conches and the royal umbrella go out as if a king rode forth; under a pearl canopy on diamond pillars, at the hour the moon joins Rohini, Kovalan circles the fire with Kannaki as the aged Brahmin shows the Vedic way, and the adorned women, scattering flowers and blessing her never to part from her lover nor loosen her embrace, seat the Arundhati-like bride on the marriage couch; the unit closes hailing the Chola who set his tiger on the Himalaya and rolls his single wheel everywhere.","n":"The women's blessing (காதலற் பிரியாமல் கவவுக்கை ஞெகிழாமல் தீதுஅறுக) names exactly the harm the story will bring: separation and loosened arms. Line 53 exclaims what vow (நோன்பு) the onlookers must have kept to see the circling of the fire, treating the sight as the fruit of past observance. The rite is Vedic in form (மறைவழி, தீவலம்), led by a Brahmin, though the families are merchants. How lines 64 to 68 attach is not plain: தங்கிய and எனவே may make the praise of the Chola part of the women's words, or the poet's own close.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","vaazhkkaithunai","nonbu","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 1, lines 45-68","lb":"SI1:45-68","ev":"Kovalan and Kannaki are married with the circling of the fire, and the women bless her and lead her to the marriage couch.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","கண்ணகி","மாமுது பார்ப்பான்","பொலன்நறுங் கொடிஅன்னார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:2"},"si:2:1-11":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":11,"t":["உரைசால் சிறப்பின் அரைசுவிழை திருவின்","பரதர் மலிந்த பயம்கெழு மாநகர்","முழங்குகடல் ஞாலம் முழுவதும் வரினும்","வழங்கத் தவாஅ வளத்தது ஆகி","அரும்பொருள் தருஉம் விருந்தின் தேஎம்","ஒருங்குதொக் கன்ன உடைப்பெரும் பண்டம்","கலத்தினும் காலினும் தருவனர் ஈட்டக்","குலத்திற் குன்றாக் கொழுங்குடிச் செல்வர்","அத்தகு திருவின் அருந்தவம் முடித்தோர்","உத்தர குருவின் ஒப்பத் தோன்றிய","கயமலர்க் கண்ணியும் காதல் கொழுநனும்"],"p":"","r":"The narrator describes Puhar, city of famed merchants, so rich that its stores would not run short if the whole sea-girt world came to it, filled with rare goods brought by ship and cart, home of wealthy houses that never fall from their lineage, and there sets the flower-eyed bride and her loving husband, who appear like those who completed hard austerity and are born to enjoy in Uttarakuru.","n":"The couple's happiness is introduced through a simile of reward: Uttarakuru is the land where the fruit of austerity is enjoyed (அருந்தவம் முடித்தோர்), so the marriage is pictured as a store of merit being spent, and what is spent can run out. The unit is the subject of a sentence that runs on to line 37 without a main verb; the couple are named only by epithets (கயமலர்க் கண்ணியும் காதல் கொழுநனும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","thavam","kudi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 2, lines 1-11","lb":"SI2:1-11","ev":"","cl":[["those who complete hard austerity are born again to enjoy a land of plenty such as Uttarakuru","general","asserted",9,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:3"},"si:2:12-27":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":12,"t":["மயன்விதித் தன்ன மணிக்கால் அமளிமிசை","நெடுநிலை மாடத்து இடைநிலத்து இருந்துழிக்","கழுநீர் ஆம்பல் முழுநெறிக் குவளை","அரும்புபொதி அவிழ்ந்த சுரும்புஇமிர் தாமரை","வயற்பூ வாசம் அளைஇ அயற்பூ","மேதகு தாழை விரியல்வெண் தோட்டுக்","கோதை மாதவி சண்பகப் பொதும்பர்த்","தாதுதேர்ந்து உண்டு மாதர்வாள் முகத்துப்","புரிகுழல் அளகத்துப் புகல்ஏக் கற்றுத்","திரிதரு சுரும்பொடு செவ்வி பார்த்து","மாலைத் தாமத்து மணிநிரைத்து வகுத்த","கோலச் சாளரக் குறுங்கண் நுழைந்து","வண்டொடு புக்க மணவாய்த் தென்றல்","கண்டு மகிழ்வுஎய்திக் காதலில் சிறந்து,","விரைமலர் வாளியொடு வேனில்வீற் றிருக்கும்","நிரைநிலை மாடத்து அரமியம் ஏறி,"],"p":"","r":"Seated on a jewelled couch like Maya's work in the middle storey of their tall mansion, the couple see the fragrant southern breeze enter through the latticed window with bees that have fed on the field flowers and on the thaazhai, the maadhavi creeper and the champak grove, and delighted and deepened in love they climb to the open terrace where spring sits enthroned with its arrows of flowers.","n":"Spring with its flower-arrows (விரைமலர் வாளியொடு வேனில்) is the love god's season, so the breeze is a messenger of desire. Among the scents it carries is the மாதவி creeper (line 18), the same name the dancer bears who will take Kovalan from this house in the next kaathai; the text does not point to it, and the echo may be no more than a reader's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 2, lines 12-27","lb":"SI2:12-27","ev":"Kovalan and Kannaki, delighted by the spring breeze in their chamber, climb to the terrace of their mansion.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணகி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:3"},"si:2:28-52":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":13,"t":["சுரும்புஉணக் கிடந்த நறும்பூஞ் சேக்கைக்","கரும்பும் வல்லியும் பெருந்தோள் எழுதி","முதிர்க்கடல் ஞாலம் முழுவதும் விளக்கும்","கதிர்ஒருங் கிருந்த காட்சி போல,","வண்டுவாய் திறப்ப நெடுநிலா விரிந்த","வெண்தோட்டு மல்லிகை விரியல் மாலையொடு","கழுநீர்ப் பிணையல் முழுநெறி பிறழத்","தாரும் மாலையும் மயங்கிக் கையற்றுத்","தீராக் காதலின் திருமுகம் நோக்கிக்","கோவலன் கூறும்ஓர் குறியாக் கட்டுரை","குழவித் திங்கள் இமையவர் ஏத்த","அழகொடு முடித்த அருமைத்து ஆயினும்","உரிதின் நின்னோடு உடன்பிறப்பு உண்மையின்","பெரியோன் தருக திருநுதல் ஆகஎன,","அடையார் முனையகத்து அமர்மேம் படுநர்க்குப்","படைவழங் குவதுஓர் பண்புண்டு ஆகலின்","உருவி லாளன் ஒருபெருங் கருப்புவில்","இருகரும் புருவ மாக ஈக்க,","மூவா மருந்தின் முன்னர்த் தோன்றலின்","தேவர் கோமான் தெய்வக் காவல்","படைநினக்கு அளிக்கஅதன் இடைநினக்கு இடையென,","அறுமுக ஒருவன்ஓர் பெறுமுறை இன்றியும்","இறுமுறை காணும் இயல்பினின் அன்றே","அம்சுடர் நெடுவேல் ஒன்றுநின் முகத்துச்","செங்கடை மழைக்கண் இரண்டா ஈத்தது?"],"p":"","r":"On the flower bed Kovalan paints sugarcane and creeper on her shoulders, the two shine together like sun and moon lighting the world, their garlands tangle, and gazing at her face in love he speaks: the great god should give her the crescent moon he wears as her brow, since it is her sibling; the bodiless god gave his cane bow as her two brows; the king of gods gave the middle of his thunderbolt as her waist, since she appeared before the nectar; and the six-faced god gave his one spear as her two eyes.","n":"Lines 28 to 37 are the narrator's and the speech begins at 38. The praise makes the gods hand over their weapons, as one gives arms to a champion at the front (lines 42-43), so her body is imagined as an armoury that conquers; the moon being her sibling and her appearing before the nectar both suggest she is likened to Lakshmi, born of the churned sea, though the text does not name her. Line 37 calls the speech குறியாக் கட்டுரை, an unmeasured or unconsidered speech, which may judge Kovalan's excess as well as describe it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 2, lines 28-52","lb":"SI2:28-52","ev":"Kovalan paints his bride's shoulders and begins a long praise of her beauty.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:3"},"si:2:53-72":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":14,"t":["மாஇரும் பீலி மணிநிற மஞ்ஞைநின்","சாயற்கு இடைந்து தண்கான் அடையவும்,","அன்னம் நல்நுதல் மெல்நடைக்கு அழிந்து","நல்நீர்ப் பண்ணை நனிமலர்ச் செறியவும்,","அளிய தாமே சிறுபசுங் கிளியே.","குழலும் யாழும் அமிழ்தும் குழைத்தநின்","மழலைக் கிளவிக்கு வருந்தின வாகியும்","மடநடை மாதுநின் மலர்க்கையின் நீங்காது","உடன்உறைவு மரீஇ ஒருவா ஆயின,","நறுமலர்க் கோதை.நின் நலம்பா ராட்டுநர்","மறுஇல் மங்கல அணியே அன்றியும்","பிறிதுஅணி அணியப் பெற்றதை எவன்கொல்?","பல்இருங் கூந்தல் சின்மலர் அன்றியும்","எல்அவிழ் மாலையொடு என்உற் றனர்கொல்?","நானம் நல்அகில் நறும்புகை அன்றியும்","மான்மதச் சாந்தொடு வந்ததை எவன்கொல்?","திருமுலைத் தடத்திடைத் தொய்யில் அன்றியும்","ஒருகாழ் முத்தமொடு உற்றதை எவன்கொல்?","திங்கள்முத்து அரும்பவும் சிறுகுஇடை வருந்தவும்","இங்குஇவை அணிந்தனர் என்உற் றனர்க்கொல்?"],"p":"","r":"Kovalan goes on: the peacock beaten by her grace has fled to the forest, the swan beaten by her gait hides among the lotus pools, and the parrot, pained by her prattle that is sweeter than flute, lute and nectar, still will not leave her hand; then he asks why those who praise her beauty decked her with ornaments beyond the auspicious one, with garlands beyond the flowers of her hair, musk beyond incense, and a strand of pearls beyond the painting on her breasts, making her small waist suffer.","n":"The parrot is pitied (அளிய தாமே) because it loses to her and still stays, unlike the peacock and swan that withdraw; the catalogue of adornments turns into a reproach of her attendants, the convention that beauty is burdened by what is added to it. The faultless auspicious ornament (மறுஇல் மங்கல அணி, line 63) is the one ornament he allows, the wife's mark, set against the rest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 2, lines 53-72","lb":"SI2:53-72","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:3"},"si:2:73-90":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":15,"t":["மாசறு பொன்னே. வலம்புரி முத்தே.","காசறு விரையே. கரும்பே. தேனே.","அரும்பெறல் பாவாய். ஆர்உயிர் மருந்தே.","பெருங்குடி வாணிகன் பெருமட மகளே.","மலையிடைப் பிறவா மணியே என்கோ?","அலையிடைப் பிறவா அமிழ்தே என்கோ?","யாழிடைப் பிறவா இசையே என்கோ?","தாழ்இருங் கூந்தல் தையால் நின்னைஎன்று","உலவாக் கட்டுரை பலபா ராட்டித்","தயங்குஇணர்க் கோதை தன்னொடு தருக்கி","மயங்குஇணர்த் தாரோன் மகிழ்ந்துசெல் வுழிநாள்,","வாரொலி கூந்தலைப் பேர்இயல் கிழத்தி","மறுப்புஅருங் கேண்மையொடு அறப்பரி சாரமும்","விருந்து புறந்தருஉம் பெருந்தண் வாழ்க்கையும்","வேறுபடு திருவின் வீறுபெறக் காண","உரிமைச் சுற்றமொடு ஒருதனி புணர்க்க","யாண்டுசில கழிந்தன இற்பெருங் கிழமையின்","காண்தகு சிறப்பின் கண்ணகி தனக்குஎன்."],"p":"","r":"Kovalan calls her flawless gold, pearl of the right-whorled conch, pure fragrance, sugarcane, honey, rare doll, medicine of his life, innocent daughter of the great merchant house, and asks whether to call her a gem not born of mountains, nectar not born of the sea, music not born of the lute; so he delights with her, and as the days go the great lady of the house, wishing to see Kannaki's charity and her household that welcomes guests flourish, sets her up apart with her own retinue, and some years pass for Kannaki in the dignity of the house.","n":"Lines 73 to 80 are Kovalan's words, closed by என்று at line 80; from line 81 the narrator speaks. The title's மனையறம் is given its content here: the household is set up so that its charity (அறப் பரிசாரம்) and hospitality (விருந்து புறந்தருஉம்) may be seen, which is the life Kannaki will later say she has lost. 'Some years passed' (யாண்டுசில கழிந்தன) closes the happy time in four words. Who the great lady is the unit does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","virundhu","aram","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 2, lines 73-90","lb":"SI2:73-90","ev":"After Kovalan's praise, an elder lady of the family sets Kannaki up in a household of her own, and some years pass.","cl":[["a household is set up for charity and the welcoming of guests","general","asserted",86,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோவலன்","பேர்இயல் கிழத்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:3"},"si:2:91-94":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":16,"t":["தூமப் பணிகள்ஒன்றித் தோய்ந்தால் எனஒருவார்","காமர் மனைவியெனக் கைகலந்து - நாமம்","தொலையாத இன்பம்எலாம் துன்னினார் மண்மேல்","நிலையாமை கண்டவர்ப்போல் நின்று."],"p":"","r":"A closing venba says that the two, joined like intertwined serpents and never parting, hand in hand as husband and wife, enjoyed every pleasure whose name does not fade, standing on the earth like people who had seen its impermanence.","n":"The last line turns the knowledge of impermanence into a reason for haste in pleasure rather than for renunciation, as the ethics books use it; the simile 'as if they had seen' (கண்டவர்ப்போல்) leaves open whether they did see it. ஒருவார், 'they did not part', is undone in the next kaathai. The image of தூமப் பணிகள் (smoky or dark serpents coiled together) is not certain in its first word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","nilaiyaamai","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 2, lines 91-94","lb":"SI2:91-94","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:3"},"si:3:1-11":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":17,"t":["தெய்வ மால்வரைத் திருமுனி அருள","எய்திய சாபத்து இந்திர சிறுவனொடு","தலைக்கோல் தானத்துச் சாபம் நீங்கிய","மலைப்புஅருஞ் சிறப்பின் வானவர் மகளிர்","சிறப்பிற் குன்றாச் செய்கையொடு பொருந்திய","பிறப்பிற் குன்றாப் பெருந்தோள் மடந்தை","தாதுஅவிழ் புரிகுழல் மாதவி தன்னை","ஆடலும் பாடலும் அழகும் என்றுஇக்","கூறிய மூன்றின் ஒன்றுகுறை படாமல்","ஏழாண்டு இயற்றிஓர் ஈராறு ஆண்டில்","சூழ்கடல் மன்னற்குக் காட்டல் வேண்டி,"],"p":"","r":"Madhavi, of a birth not falling short of the heavenly women freed at the place of the talaikkol from the curse a holy sage of the divine mountain laid on them with Indra's son, trains seven years in dance, song and beauty, lacking in none of the three, to show her art to the sea-girt king in her twelfth year.","n":"The sage gives the curse 'as grace' (அருள எய்திய சாபத்து), so the curse is framed as a gift. Whether Madhavi descends from the freed heavenly women or only matches them (பிறப்பிற் குன்றா, a birth not less than theirs) cannot be fixed from these lines; the sage and the heavenly woman are not named. The three arts are counted as a set that must be whole (ஒன்றுகுறை படாமல்), so beauty stands among the trained skills, not as a gift of nature.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["koothu","kalvi","azhagu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 1-11","lb":"SI3:1-11","ev":"Madhavi trains seven years in dance, song and beauty to be shown to the Chola king in her twelfth year.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:3:12-25":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":18,"t":["இருவகைக் கூத்தின் இலக்கணம் அறிந்து","பலவகைக் கூத்தும் விலக்கினிற் புணர்த்துப்","பதினோர் ஆடலும் பாட்டும் கொட்டும்","விதிமாண் கொள்கையின் விளங்க அறிந்துஆங்கு","ஆடலும் பாடலும் பாணியும் தூக்கும்","கூடிய நெறியின கொளுத்துங் காலைப்","பிண்டியும் பிணையலும் எழிற்கையும் தொழிற்கையும்","கொண்ட வகைஅறிந்து கூத்துவரு காலைக்","கூடை செய்தகை வாரத்துக் களைதலும்","வாரம் செய்தகை கூடையிற் களைதலும்","பிண்டி செய்தகை ஆடலிற் களைதலும்","ஆடல் செய்தகை பிண்டியிற் களைதலும்","குரவையும் வரியும் விரவல செலுத்தி","ஆடற்கு அமைந்த ஆசான் தன்னொடும்,"],"p":"","r":"The dance master is described: he knows the marks of the two kinds of koothu, joins the many kinds to the interludes, knows the eleven dances with their song and drumming, the four kinds of hand gesture and how a gesture of one style is dropped into another, and directs the kuravai and vari without mixing them.","n":"The four lines 20 to 23 are built as a chiasmus (கூடை into வாரம், வாரம் into கூடை, பிண்டி into ஆடல், ஆடல் into பிண்டி): the verse performs the exchange of gestures it describes. The master is praised for knowing where each gesture must stop, not for invention.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","kalvi","alavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 12-25","lb":"SI3:12-25","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆடற்கு அமைந்த ஆசான்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:3:26-36":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":19,"t":["யாழும் குழலும் சீரும் மிடறும்","தாழ்குரல் தண்ணுமை ஆடலொடு இவற்றின்","இசைந்த பாடல் இசையுடன் படுத்து","வரிக்கும் ஆடற்கும் உரிப்பொருள் இயக்கித்","தேசிகத் திருவின் ஓசை கடைப்பிடித்துத்","தேசிகத் திருவின் ஓசை எல்லாம்","ஆசுஇன்று உணர்ந்த அறிவினன் ஆகிக்","கவியது குறிப்பும் ஆடல் தொகுதியும்","பகுதிப் பாடலும் கொளுத்துங் காலை","வசைஅறு கேள்வி வகுத்தனன் விரிக்கும்","அசையா மரபின் இசையோன் தானும்,"],"p":"","r":"The singer is described: he fits the song to yaazh, flute, beat, voice and drum, keeps to the sound of the regional styles and knows them all without fault, and when the poem's intent, the dance and the parts of the song are set together, lays out his flawless learning.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","kelvi","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 26-36","lb":"SI3:26-36","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["இசையோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:3:37-44":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":20,"t":["இமிழ்கடல் வரைப்பில் தமிழகம் அறியத்","தமிழ்முழுது அறிந்த தன்மையன் ஆகி","வேத்தியல் பொதுவியல் என்றுஇரு திறத்தின்","நாட்டிய நன்னூல் நன்கு கடைப்பிடித்து","இசையோன் வக்கிரித் திட்டத்தை உணர்ந்துஆங்கு","அசையா மரபின் அதுபட வைத்து","மாற்றார் செய்த வசைமொழி அறிந்து","நாத்தொலைவு இல்லா நன்னூல் புலவனும்,"],"p":"","r":"The poet is described: knowing all of Tamil so that the Tamil land within the sea knows it, holding to the drama treatise in its two kinds, the king's style and the common style, he understands where the singer bends the melody and sets it in the fixed tradition, and knows the faults that rivals charge.","n":"The poet's office is partly defensive: he must know the blame (வசைமொழி) that others will speak, so the performance is judged against a rule that critics also hold. The pair வேத்தியல் பொதுவியல் recurs in the Manimekalai's list of Madhavi's arts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","koothu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 37-44","lb":"SI3:37-44","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["நன்னூல் புலவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:3:45-55":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":21,"t":["ஆடல் பாடல் இசையே தமிழே","பண்ணே பாணி தூக்கே முடமே","தேசிகம் என்றுஇவை ஆசின் உணர்ந்து","கூடை நிலத்தைக் குறைவுஇன்று மிகுத்துஆங்கு","வார நிலத்தை வாங்குபு வாங்கி","வாங்கிய வாரத்து யாழும் குழலும்","ஏங்கிய மிடறும் இசைவன கேட்பக்","கூர்உகிர்க் கரணம் குறிஅறிந்து சேர்த்தி","ஆக்கலும் அடக்கலும் மீத்திறம் படாமைச்","சித்திரக் கரணம் சிதைவுஇன்றி செலுத்தும்","அத்தகு தண்ணுமை அருந்தொழில் முதல்வனும்,"],"p":"","r":"The drum master is described: knowing dance, song, melody, Tamil, mode, beat, rhythm, the lame measure and the regional styles, he lets the yaazh, flute and voice be heard in the measures of the dance, strikes the stroke at its sign, and swells or restrains it so that it never overpowers.","n":"The drummer's excellence is restraint: ஆக்கலும் அடக்கலும் மீத்திறம் படாமை, raising and holding back without excess. The loudest instrument is praised for not drowning the others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","alavu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 45-55","lb":"SI3:45-55","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["தண்ணுமை முதல்வன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:3:56-69":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":22,"t":["சொல்லிய இயல்பினிற் சித்திர வஞ்சனை","புல்லிய அறிந்து புணர்ப்போன் பண்பின்","வர்த்தனை நான்கும் மயல்அறப் பெய்துஆங்கு","ஏற்றிய குரல்இளி என்றுஇரு நரம்பின்","ஒப்பக் கேட்கும் உணர்வினன் ஆகிப்","பண்அமை முழவின் கண்ணெறி அறிந்து","தண்ணுமை முதல்வன் தன்னொடு பொருந்தி","வண்ணப் பட்டடை யாழ்மேல் வைத்துஆங்கு","இசையோன் பாடிய இசையின் இயற்கை","வந்தது வளர்த்து வருவது ஒற்றி","இன்புற இயக்கி இசைபட வைத்து","வார நிலத்தைக் கேடுஇன்று வளர்த்துஆங்கு","ஈர நிலத்தின் எழுத்துஎழுத்து ஆக","வழுவின்று இசைக்கும் குழலோன் தானும்,"],"p":"","r":"The flute player is described: knowing the fixed figures and the four ornaments, hearing the two strings kural and ili as one, keeping in step with the drum master and the tuned yaazh, he follows the singer's melody, carries on what has come and anticipates what comes, and plays each syllable on the wet stops without fault.","n":"The terms of art (சித்திர வஞ்சனை, வர்த்தனை, ஈர நிலம்) are not explained in the verse, and their exact sense is a reading I cannot fix without commentary.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["koothu","alavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 56-69","lb":"SI3:56-69","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குழலோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:3:70-94":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":23,"t":["ஈர்ஏழ் தொடுத்த செம்முறைக் கேள்வியின்","ஓர்ஏழ் பாலை நிறுத்தல் வேண்டி","வன்மையிற் கிடந்த தார பாகமும்","மென்மையிற் கிடந்த குரலின் பாகமும்","மெய்க்கிளை நரம்பிற் கைக்கிளை கொள்ளக்","கைக்கிளை ஒழித்த பாகமும் பொற்புடைத்","தளராத் தாரம் விளரிக்கு ஈத்துக்","கிளைவழிப் பட்டனள், ஆங்கே கிளையும்","தன்கிளை அழிவுகண்டு அவள்வயிற் சேர","ஏனை மகளிரும் கிளைவழிச் சேர","மேலது உழையிளி கீழது கைக்கிளை","வம்புஉறு மரபின் செம்பாலை ஆயது","இறுதி ஆதி ஆக ஆங்குஅவை","பெறுமுறை வந்த பெற்றியின் நீங்காது","படுமலை செவ்வழி பகர்அரும் பாலைஎனக்","குரல்குரல் ஆகத் தற்கிழமை திரிந்தபின்","முன்னதன் வகையே முறைமையின் திரிந்துஆங்கு","இளிமுத லாகிய ஏர்படு கிழமையும்","கோடி விளரி மேற்செம் பாலைஎன","நீடிக் கிடந்த கேள்விக் கிடக்கையின்","இணைநரம்பு உடையன அணைவுறக் கொண்டுஆங்கு","யாழ்மேற் பாலை இடமுறை மெலியக்","குழல்மேற் கோடி வலமுறை மெலிய","வலிவும் மெலிவும் சமனும் எல்லாம்","பொலியக் கோத்த புலமை யோனுடன்,"],"p":"","r":"The yaazh player is described: to fix the seven palai modes from the fourteen strings, he moves notes by their kin, the high and low parts of the scale passing to one another until the red palai, then the other palais, are set, and he matches the yaazh and flute so that strong, soft and level are all in tune.","n":"The notes are treated as women who leave or join their kin (கிளைவழிப் பட்டனள், ஏனை மகளிரும் கிளைவழிச் சேர): the modal shift is told as a family moving house. The technical sequence (படுமலை, செவ்வழி, கோடி விளரி) is the verse's own, but which note becomes the tonic at each step is beyond what I can fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["koothu","alavu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 70-94","lb":"SI3:70-94","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["புலமையோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:3:95-110":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":24,"t":["எண்ணிய நூலோர் இயல்பினின் வழாஅது","மண்ணகம் ஒருவழி வகுத்தனர் கொண்டு","புண்ணிய நெடுவரைப் போகிய நெடுங்கழைக்","கண்ணிடை ஒருசாண் வளர்ந்தது கொண்டு","நூல்நெறி மரபின் அரங்கம் அளக்கும்","கோல்அளவு இருபத்து நால்விரல் ஆக","எழுகோல் அகலத்து எண்கோல் நீளத்து","ஒருகோல் உயரத்து உறுப்பினது ஆகி","உத்தரப் பலகையொடு அரங்கின் பலகை","வைத்த இடைநிலம் நாற்கோல் ஆக","ஏற்ற வாயில் இரண்டுடன் பொலியத்","தோற்றிய அரங்கில் தொழுதனர் ஏத்தப்","பூதரை எழுதி மேல்நிலை வைத்துத்","தூண்நிழல் புறப்பட மாண்விளக்கு எடுத்துஆங்கு","ஒருமுக எழினியும் பொருமுக எழினியும்","கரந்துவரல் எழினியும் புரிந்துடன் வகுத்துஆங்கு"],"p":"","r":"The stage is built as the treatise's authors prescribe: on chosen ground, with a bamboo from the holy mountain whose internodes are a span apart, a measuring rod of twenty-four finger-breadths fixes it seven rods wide, eight long and one high, four rods between the planks, two doors, the guardian beings painted above and worshipped, lamps set so no pillar casts a shadow, and three kinds of curtain.","n":"Every measure is given as a number from the book (நூல்நெறி மரபின்), and the rod itself is taken from a holy mountain: the stage is a ritual space before it is a theatre. The lamps are placed so the pillars' shadows fall outside, so nothing on the stage is hidden.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alavu","koothu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 95-110","lb":"SI3:95-110","ev":"The stage is measured and built by rule, with painted guardians, lamps and three curtains.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:3:111-128":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":25,"t":["ஓவிய விதானத்து உரைபெறு நித்திலத்து","மாலைத் தாமம் வளையுடன் நாற்றி","விருந்துபடக் கிடந்த அருந்தொழில் அரங்கத்துப்","பேர்இசை மன்னர் பெயர்ப்புறத்து எடுத்த","சீர்இயல் வெண்குடைக் காம்புநனி கொண்டு","கண்இடை நவமணி ஒழுக்கி மண்ணிய","நாவல்அம் பொலம்தகட்டு இடைநிலம் போக்கிக்","காவல் வெண்குடை மன்னவன் கோயில்","இந்திர சிறுவன் சயந்தன் ஆகென","வந்தனை செய்து வழிபடு தலைக்கோல்","புண்ணிய நன்னீர் பொற்குடத்து ஏந்தி","மண்ணிய பின்னர் மாலை அணிந்து","நலம்தரு நாளால் பொலம்பூண் ஓடை","அரசுஉவாத் தடக்கையில் பரசினர் கொண்டு","முரசுஎழுந்து இயம்பப் பல்இயம் ஆர்ப்ப","அரைசொடு பட்ட ஐம்பெருங் குழுவும்","தேர்வலம் செய்து கவிகைக் கொடுப்ப","ஊர்வலம் செய்து புகுந்துமுன் வைத்துஆங்கு,"],"p":"","r":"Pearl garlands are hung from the painted canopy; the talaikkol, cut from the shaft of a white umbrella taken from great kings who fled, its joints set with nine gems and bound in gold, is worshipped in the king's palace as Jayanta son of Indra, bathed from a golden pot, garlanded, set in the state elephant's hand on an auspicious day, and carried round the town by the king and the five great assemblies to be set before the stage.","n":"The dancer's staff of honour is a war trophy: the shaft of a defeated king's umbrella. The emblem of an art is made from the emblem of a lost kingdom, and worshipped as a god, so the dance's highest title carries the Chola's victory and the Indra myth at once. What கவிகைக் கொடுப்ப (line 127) gives, and to whom, is unclear to me.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vendhan","koothu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 111-128","lb":"SI3:111-128","ev":"The talaikkol, worshipped as Jayanta, is bathed, garlanded and carried on the state elephant round the town by the king and the five great assemblies, then set before the stage.","cl":[],"ag":["சென்னி","ஐம்பெருங் குழு"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:3:129-153":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":26,"t":["இயல்பினின் வழாஅ இருக்கை முறைமையின்","குயிலுவ மாக்கள் நெறிப்பட நிற்ப,","வலக்கால் முன்மிதித்து ஏறி அரங்கத்து","வலத்தூண் சேர்தல் வழக்குஎனப் பொருந்தி","இந்நெறி வகையால் இடத்தூண் சேர்ந்த","தொல்நெறி இயற்கைத் தோரிய மகளிரும்","சீர்இயல் பொலிய நீர்அல நீங்க","வாரம் இரண்டும் வரிசையில் பாடப்","பாடிய வாரத்து ஈற்றில்நின்று இசைக்கும்","கூடிய குயிலுவக் கருவிகள் எல்லாம்","குழல்வழி நின்றது யாழே, யாழ்வழித்","தண்ணுமை நின்றது தகவே, தண்ணுமைப்","பின்வழி நின்றது முழவே, முழவொடு","கூடிநின்று இசைத்தது ஆமந் திரிகை","ஆமந் திரிகையொடு அந்தரம் இன்றிக்","கொட்டுஇரண்டு உடையதுஓர் மண்டிலம் ஆகக்","கட்டிய மண்டிலம் பதினொன்று போக்கி","வந்த முறையின் வழிமுறை வழாமல்","அந்தரக் கொட்டுடன் அடங்கிய பின்னர்,","மீத்திறம் படாமை வக்காணம் வகுத்துப்","பாற்பட நின்ற பாலைப் பண்மேல்","நான்கின் ஒரீஇய நன்கனம் அறிந்து","மூன்றுஅளந்து ஒன்று கொட்டி அதனை","ஐந்துமண் டிலத்தால் கூடை போக்கி","வந்தவா ரம்வழி மயங்கிய பின்றை,"],"p":"","r":"The musicians take their places by order; the dancer steps onto the stage right foot first and goes to the right pillar as custom requires, while the senior dancing women at the left pillar sing the two vaaram for good fortune and to keep off harm; the instruments follow one another, yaazh after flute, drum after yaazh, muzhavu after drum, the aamanthirikai with them, through eleven rounds, and then the dance begins on the palai mode in its counted measures.","n":"Madhavi is not named here; she is known from the right-foot entry, a rule of conduct (வழக்குஎனப் பொருந்தி). The order of the instruments is itself a hierarchy, each standing 'behind' (வழி) the one before. The counted measures of lines 148 to 153 are technical and I read only their outline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["koothu","vazhipaadu","alavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 129-153","lb":"SI3:129-153","ev":"Madhavi enters the stage right foot first, the senior dancers sing the two vaaram, the orchestra plays in its order, and the dance begins.","cl":[],"ag":["குயிலுவ மாக்கள்","மாதவி","தோரிய மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:3:154-175":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":27,"t":["ஆறும் நாலும் அம்முறை போக்கிக்","கூறிய ஐந்தின் கொள்கை போலப்","பின்னையும் அம்முறை பேரிய பின்றை,","பொன்இயல் பூங்கொடி புரிந்துடன் வகுத்தென","நாட்டிய நன்னூல் நன்குகடைப் பிடித்துக்","காட்டினள் ஆதலின், காவல் வேந்தன்","இலைப்பூங் கோதை இயல்பினில் வழாமைத்","தலைக்கோல் எய்தித் தலைஅரங்கு ஏறி","விதிமுறைக் கொள்கையின் ஆயிரத்து எண்கழஞ்சு","ஒருமுறை யாகப் பெற்றனள் அதுவே","நூறுபத்து அடுக்கி எட்டுக்கடை நிறுத்த","வீறுஉயர் பசும்பொன் பெறுவதுஇம் மாலை,","மாலை வாங்குநர் சாலும்நம் கொடிக்குஎன","மான்அமர் நோக்கிஓர் கூனிகைக் கொடுத்து","நகர நம்பியர் திரிதரு மறுகில்","பகர்வனர் போல்வதோர் பான்மையின் நிறுத்த,","மாமலர் நெடுங்கண் மாதவி மாலை","கோவலன் வாங்கிக் கூனி தன்னொடு","மணமனை புக்கு மாதவி தன்னொடு","அணைவுறு வைகலின் அயர்ந்தனன் மயங்கி","விடுதல் அறியா விருப்பினன் ஆயினன்.","வடுநீங்கு சிறப்பின்தன் மனையகம் மறந்துஎன்."],"p":"","r":"Having shown the dance as the treatise prescribes, Madhavi receives from the king the leaf garland and the talaikkol title and, by rule, a thousand and eight kazhanju of gold; the doe-eyed woman sets that sum as the price of the garland and sends it by a hunchbacked maid into the street of the town's young men as if for sale; Kovalan buys it, enters the bridal house with Madhavi, and in his union with her, infatuated and knowing no letting go, forgets his own house of flawless distinction.","n":"The garland's price is exactly the king's reward, so the courtesan house turns a royal honour into a sale price. The sale is staged (பகர்வனர் போல்வதோர் பான்மையின், 'in the manner of those who sell'): the street offer is a custom, not a bargain. The narrator passes no verdict on Madhavi; his one judgment is the praise of what Kovalan forgot (வடுநீங்கு சிறப்பின்தன் மனையகம்), and the forgetting is told as infatuation (மயங்கி, விடுதல் அறியா விருப்பினன்), not as fate. Who the doe-eyed woman is, Madhavi's mother or another of the house, the chapter does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mayal","parathai","selvam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 154-175","lb":"SI3:154-175","ev":"The king gives Madhavi the talaikkol and 1008 kazhanju; her garland, priced at that sum, is offered in the street, and Kovalan buys it, goes to live with Madhavi and forgets his home.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி","சென்னி","மான்அமர் நோக்கி","கூனி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:3:176-179":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":28,"t":["எண்ணும் எழுத்தும் இயல்ஐந்தும் பண்நான்கும்","பண்ணின்ற கூத்துப் பதினொன்றும் - மண்ணின்மேல்","போக்கினாள் பூம்புகார்ப் பொற்றொடி மாதவிதன்","வாக்கினால் ஆடரங்கில் வந்து."],"p":"","r":"A closing venba says that Madhavi of Puhar, with her golden bangles, came onto the dancing stage and by her art displayed on earth the counts and letters, the five modes of composition, the four panns and the eleven dances set to music.","n":"The venba sums up the chapter as Madhavi's achievement and says nothing of the sale or of Kovalan: the closing praise and the narrative's last line (Kovalan forgetting his home) are set side by side without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 3, lines 176-179","lb":"SI3:176-179","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:4"},"si:4:1-20":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":29,"t":["விரிகதிர் பரப்பி உலகம்முழுது ஆண்ட","ஒருதனித் திகிரி உரவோன் காணேன்","அங்கண் வானத்து அணிநிலா விரிக்கும்","திங்கள்அம் செல்வன் யாண்டுஉளன் கொல்எனத்","திசைமுகம் பசந்து செம்மலர்க் கண்கள்","முழுநீர் வார முழுமெயும் பனித்துத்","திரைநீர் ஆடை இருநில மடந்தை","அரைசுகெடுத்து அலம்வரும் அல்லற் காலை,","கறைகெழு குடிகள் கைதலை வைப்ப","அறைபோகு குடிகளொடு ஒருதிறம் பற்றி","வலம்படு தானை மன்னர் இல்வழிப்","புலம்பட இறுத்த விருந்தின் மன்னரின்","தாழ்துணை துறந்தோர் தனித்துயர் எய்தக்","காதலர்ப் புணர்ந்தோர் களிமகிழ்வு எய்தக்","குழல்வளர் முல்லையில் கோவலர் தம்மொடு","மழலைத் தும்பி வாய்வைத்து ஊத","அறுகால் குறும்புஎறிந்து அரும்புபொதி வாசம்","சிறுகால் செல்வன் மறுகில் தூற்ற","எல்வளை மகளிர் மணிவிளக்கு எடுப்ப","மல்லல் மூதூர் மாலைவந்து இருத்தென"],"p":"","r":"The poet brings evening on Puhar as a political disaster: the sun, sole wheel-ruler of the world, is gone, the earth like a woman in grief weeps with pale face and wet eyes as if her king were lost, and evening camps in the city like a foreign king who seizes a land whose victorious king is absent, sides with the traitors and leaves the loyal wringing their hands; lovers apart are grieved and lovers together delighted, a bee hums in the jasmine beside the herdsmen, the little breeze scatters scent through the streets and women light the gem lamps.","n":"The whole kaathai is one sentence, and this opening sets its frame: evening as a usurper in a kingless land (lines 8 to 12), a figure drawn from the state rather than from love. Lines 13 and 14 announce the chapter's plan, the same hour bringing grief to the parted and joy to the joined, which the following units fill in house by house. The herdsmen of line 15 are கோவலர், the hero's name in the plural; the chapter does not stress the pun, and I note it only as a sound a listener would hear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","punarchi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 4, lines 1-20","lb":"SI4:1-20","ev":"Evening falls on Puhar and the women of the houses light the gem lamps.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:5"},"si:4:21-34":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":30,"t":["இளையர் ஆயினும் பகைஅரசு கடியும்","செருமாண் தென்னர் குலமுதல் ஆகலின்","அந்திவா னத்து வெண்பிறை தோன்றிப்","புன்கண் மாலைக் குறும்புஎறிந்து ஓட்டிப்","பான்மையில் திரியாது பால்கதிர் பரப்பி","மீன்அரசு ஆண்ட வெள்ளி விளக்கத்து,","இல்வளர் முல்லையொடு மல்லிகை அவிழ்ந்த","பல்பூஞ் சேக்கைப் பள்ளியுள் பொலிந்து","செந்துகிர்க் கோவை சென்றுஏந்து அல்குல்","அம்துகில் மேகலை அசைந்தன வருந்த","நிலவுப்பயன் கொள்ளும் நெடுநிலா முற்றத்துக்","கலவியும் புலவியும் காதலற்கு அளித்துஆங்கு","ஆர்வ நெஞ்சமொடு கோவலற்கு எதிரிக்","கோலம் கொண்ட மாதவி அன்றியும்,"],"p":"","r":"Because it is the founder of the line of the Pandyas, who drive off hostile kings even when young, the white crescent rises in the evening sky, routs the raiding gloom, spreads milk-white rays without swerving from its nature and rules the stars; under its light Madhavi, adorned, on the high moonlit terrace among beds of jasmine and mallikai, gives Kovalan both love-making and the feigned quarrel, meeting him with an eager heart.","n":"The moon that took over the sky from the beaten evening is a Pandya; in a book whose second half turns on a Pandya's justice, its first praise of that line is of a ruler that does not swerve (பான்மையில் திரியாது). Madhavi is given both கலவி and புலவி, union and the lovers' sulk, as a skill she offers Kovalan (அளித்து), an art and not only a feeling. The unit ends on அன்றியும் ('and besides'): she is the first item in a list of the city's nights.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","oodal","kaamam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 4, lines 21-34","lb":"SI4:21-34","ev":"Madhavi, adorned on her moonlit terrace, receives Kovalan with love-making and lovers' quarrels.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:5"},"si:4:35-46":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":31,"t":["குடதிசை மருங்கின் வெள்அயிர் தன்னொடு","குணதிசை மருங்கின் கார்அகில் துறந்து","வடமலைப் பிறந்த வான்கேழ் வட்டத்துத்","தென்மலைப் பிறந்த சந்தனம் மறுகத்","தாமரைக் கொழுமுறித் தாதுபடு செழுமலர்க்","காமரு குவளைக் கழுநீர் மாமலர்ப்","பைந்தளிர்ப் படலை பருஉக்காழ் ஆரம்","சுந்தரச் சுண்ணத் துகளொடு அளைஇச்","சிந்துபு பரிந்த செழும்பூஞ் சேக்கை","மந்தமா ருதத்து மயங்கினர் மலிந்துஆங்கு","ஆவியங் கொழுநர் அகலத்து ஒடுங்கிக்","காவிஅம் கண்ணார் களித்துயில் எய்த"],"p":"","r":"Besides Madhavi, the other wives of the city lie on rich flower beds strewn with western white incense, eastern dark agil, northern stone-ground and southern sandal, lotus, lily and garlands mixed with fine powder, and, drowsy in the gentle south wind, sleep in delight folded on their husbands' chests.","n":"The four directions of the perfumes (குடதிசை, குணதிசை, வடமலை, தென்மலை) make the bed a map of the merchant city's trade, so the joy of these couples is also a picture of Puhar's wealth. Their wedded sleep (கொழுநர் அகலத்து ஒடுங்கி) is the one thing Kannaki, also a wife, lacks in the next unit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","ilvaazhkkai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 4, lines 35-46","lb":"SI4:35-46","ev":"The wives of Puhar sleep in delight on their husbands' chests.","cl":[],"ag":["காவிஅம் கண்ணார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:5"},"si:4:47-57":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":32,"t":["அம்செஞ் சீறடி அணிசிலம்பு ஒழிய","மென்துகில் அல்குல் மேகலை நீங்கக்","கொங்கை முன்றில் குங்குமம் எழுதாள்","மங்கல அணியின் பிறிதுஅணி மகிழாள்","கொடுங்குழை துறந்து வடிந்துவீழ் காதினள்","திங்கள் வாள்முகம் சிறுவியர்ப்பு இரியச்","செங்கயல் நெடுங்கண் அஞ்சனம் மறப்பப்","பவள வாள்நுதல் திலகம் இழப்பத்","தவள வாள்நகை கோவலன் இழப்ப","மைஇருங் கூந்தல் நெய்அணி மறப்பக்","கையறு நெஞ்சத்துக் கண்ணகி அன்றியும்,"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki, with a helpless heart, has left off the anklets from her feet and the girdle from her waist, draws no saffron on her breast, takes pleasure in no ornament but the auspicious one, has put away her earrings, forgets the collyrium of her eyes, loses the tilaka from her brow and the oil from her hair, and her bright smile is lost to Kovalan.","n":"Each line strips one ornament, and the verbs are of absence and forgetting (ஒழிய, நீங்க, மறப்ப, இழப்ப), not of protest: she makes no speech in the chapter. Only one loss is charged to another, the smile Kovalan forfeits (தவள வாள்நகை கோவலன் இழப்ப, line 55). Line 50 (மங்கல அணியின் பிறிதுஅணி மகிழாள்) answers Kovalan's own praise at the wedding night (silappathikaram:2:51-66), and the first anklet of the epic appears here, laid aside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","karpu","melivu","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 4, lines 47-57","lb":"SI4:47-57","ev":"Kannaki, alone at evening, puts off her anklets and every ornament but the auspicious marriage ornament.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:5"},"si:4:58-71":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":33,"t":["காதலர்ப் பிரிந்த மாதர் நோதக","ஊதுஉலைக் குருகின் உயிர்த்தனர் ஒடுங்கி","வேனில் பள்ளி மேவாது கழிந்து","கூதிர்ப் பள்ளிக் குறுங்கண் அடைத்து","மலயத்து ஆரமும் மணிமுத்து ஆரமும்","அலர்முலை ஆகத்து அடையாது வருந்தத்","தாழிக் குவளையொடு தண்செங் கழுநீர்","வீழ்பூஞ் சேக்கை மேவாது கழியத்","துணைபுணர் அன்னத் தூவியிற் செறித்த","இணைஅணை மேம்படத் திருந்துதுயில் பெறாஅது","உடைப்பெருங் கொழுநரோடு ஊடல் காலத்து","இடைக்குமிழ் எறிந்து கடைக்குழை ஓட்டிக்","கலங்கா உள்ளம் கலங்கக் கடைசிவந்து","விலங்கிநிமிர் நெடுங்கண் புலம்புமுத்து உறைப்ப,"],"p":"","r":"The women parted from their lovers sigh like the bellows of a smith's furnace and shrink into themselves; they leave the summer bedroom, close the small windows of the cool-season room, keep sandal and pearl strings off their breasts, shun beds strewn with lily and red waterlily and find no sleep on pillows stuffed with the down of mated swans; and in their sulking with their husbands their long eyes, red at the corners, rain pearls of lonely tears.","n":"Lines 58 and 59 repeat almost word for word the Akananuru's evening of a woman left behind (akananuru:71: காதலர்ப் பிரிந்த புலம்பின் நோதக, and the breath blown in a furnace), so a single heroine's lament becomes one class of the city's women. The swan-down pillow is of 'mated swans' (துணைபுணர் அன்னம்), a comfort made from the union they lack. Lines 68 to 71 shift to the time of ஊடல் with husbands, and I cannot fix whether these are the same parted women remembering past quarrels or another group of wives in a quarrel now.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","kaamanoy","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 4, lines 58-71","lb":"SI4:58-71","ev":"The women parted from their lovers lie sleepless, refusing the comforts of their rooms.","cl":[],"ag":["காதலர்ப் பிரிந்த மாதர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:5"},"si:4:72-84":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":34,"t":["அன்னம் மெல்நடை நன்னீர்ப் பொய்கை","ஆம்பல் நாறும் தேம்பொதி நறுவிரைத்","தாமரைச் செவ்வாய்த் தண்அறல் கூந்தல்","பாண்வாய் வண்டு நோதிறம் பாடக்","காண்வரு குவளைக் கண்மலர் விழிப்பப்","புள்வாய் முரசமொடு பொறிமயிர் வாரணத்து","முள்வாய்ச் சங்கம் முறைமுறை ஆர்ப்ப","உரவுநீர்ப் பரப்பின் ஊர்த்துயில் எடுப்பி","இரவுத் தலைப்பெயரும் வைகறை காறும்","அரைஇருள் யாமத்தும் பகலும் துஞ்சான்","விரைமலர் வாளியொடு கருப்புவில் ஏந்தி","மகர வெல்கொடி மைந்தன் திரிதர","நகரம் காவல் நனிசிறந் ததுஎன்."],"p":"","r":"Until dawn comes and night departs, when the pond, a woman with a swan's gait, lily breath, lotus mouth and hair of sand, wakes to bees singing a mournful mode and opens her blue-lily eyes, and drums and conches sound in turn and rouse the sea-girt town, Kama of the makara banner, carrying his flower arrows and sugarcane bow, roamed sleepless at midnight and by day, and so the city's watch was kept supremely well.","n":"The closing word is a joke on the chapter's political opening: the city that evening invaded like a usurper is guarded all night, and its guard is the god of desire, whose 'patrol' is what kept the parted women awake. நோதிறம், the mode the bees sing at dawn, is a pann of grief, so even the waking of the pond carries the night's sorrow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 4, lines 72-84","lb":"SI4:72-84","ev":"Kama roams Puhar through the night until drums and conches wake the town at dawn.","cl":[],"ag":["மகர வெல்கொடி மைந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:5"},"si:4:85-88":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":35,"t":["கூடினார் பால்நிழலாய்க் கூடார்ப்பால் வெய்தாய்க்","காவலன் வெண்குடைபோல் காட்டிற்றே - கூடிய","மாதவிக்கும் கண்ணகிக்கும் வான்ஊர் மதிவிரிந்து","போதுஅவிழ்க்கும் கங்குல் பொழுது."],"p":"","r":"The closing venba says that the night, when the moon riding the sky spread its light and opened the buds, showed itself to Madhavi who was with her lover and to Kannaki like the guardian king's white umbrella, which is shade to those joined to him and heat to those who are not.","n":"கூடினார், 'those joined', serves both halves of the simile: allies of the king and lovers together. Madhavi is called கூடிய and Kannaki is given no epithet, so the poem's sorting of the two women is done by one word and by its absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","punarchi","pirivu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 4, lines 85-88","lb":"SI4:85-88","ev":"","cl":[["a guardian king's umbrella is cool shade to those joined to him and burns those who are not","general","asserted",85,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:5"},"si:5:1-15":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":36,"t":["அலைநீர் ஆடை மலைமுலை ஆகத்து","ஆரப் பேரியாற்று மாரிக் கூந்தல்","கண்அகன் பரப்பின் மண்ணக மடந்தை","புதைஇருள் படாஅம் போக நீக்கி","உடைய மால்வரை உச்சித் தோன்றி","உலகுவிளங்கு அவிர்ஒளி மலர்கதிர் பரப்பி,","வேயா மாடமும், வியன்கல இருக்கையும்,","மான்கண் காதலர் மாளிகை இடங்களும்,","கயவாய் மருங்கில் காண்போர்த் தடுக்கும்","பயன்அறிவு அறியா யவனர் இருக்கையும்,","கலம்தரு திருவின் புலம்பெயர் மாக்கள்","கலந்துஇருந்து உறையும் இலங்குநீர் வரைப்பும்,","வண்ணமும் சுண்ணமும் தண்நறுஞ் சாந்தமும்","பூவும் புகையும் மேவிய விரையும்","பகர்வனர் திரிதரு நகர வீதியும்,"],"p":"","r":"The sun, rising over the mountain and lifting the dark cloak from the earth pictured as a woman with the sea for dress and hills for breasts, lights up Puhar's terraced mansions, its storerooms, the rich men's houses, the quarter by the harbour where Yavanas live, the shore settlements of foreign traders who came with the ships, and the streets where scents, powders, sandal, flowers and incense are hawked.","n":"The catalogue of Maruvurppakkam begins with the outsiders: the Yavanas whose quarter dazzles onlookers are called people who do not know how things are used (பயன்அறிவு அறியா), and the foreigners are those who came with the ships' wealth and stay mingled with the town. Puhar is first a port.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 1-15","lb":"SI5:1-15","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:16-39":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":37,"t":["பட்டினும் மயிரினும் பருத்தி நூலினும்","கட்டும் நுண்வினைக் காருகர் இருக்கையும்,","தூசும் துகிரும் ஆரமும் அகிலும்","மாசுஅறு முத்தும் மணியும் பொன்னும்","அருங்கல வெறுக்கையோடு அளந்துகடை அறியா","வளம்தலை மயங்கிய நனந்தலை மறுகும்,","பால்வகை தெரிந்த பகுதிப் பண்டமொடு","கூலம் குவித்த கூல வீதியும்,","காழியர் கூவியர் கள்நொடை யாட்டியர்","மீன்விலைப் பரதவர் வெள்உப்புப் பகருநர்","பாசவர் வாசவர் பல்நிண விலைஞரொடு","ஓசுநர் செறிந்த ஊன்மலி இருக்கையும்,","கஞ்ச காரரும் செம்புசெய் குநரும்","மரங்கொல் தச்சரும் கருங்கைக் கொல்லரும்","கண்ணுள் வினைஞரும் மண்ஈட் டாளரும்","பொன்செய் கொல்லரும் நன்கலம் தருநரும்","துன்ன காரரும் தோலின் துன்னரும்","கிழியினும் கிடையினும் தொழில்பல பெருக்கிப்","பழுதுஇல் செய்வினைப் பால்கெழு மாக்களும்","குழலினும் யாழினும் குரல்முதல் ஏழும்","வழுஇன்றி இசைத்து வழித்திறம் காட்டும்","அரும்பெறல் மரபின் பெரும்பாண் இருக்கையும்,","சிறுகுறுங் கைவினைப் பிறர்வினை யாளரொடு","மறுஇன்றி விளங்கும் மருவூர்ப் பாக்கமும்,"],"p":"","r":"The catalogue goes on through the weavers of silk, hair and cotton, the broad streets heaped with cloth, coral, sandal, agil, pearls, gems and gold beyond measuring, the grain street, the sellers of cakes, toddy, fish, salt and meat, the brass-smiths, coppersmiths, carpenters, blacksmiths, painters, potters, goldsmiths, jewellers, tailors and leather-workers, the great bards who play the seven notes faultlessly, and the makers of small crafts, all of which together are the shore quarter, Maruvurppakkam.","n":"Line 21 (வளம்தலை மயங்கிய நனந்தலை மறுகும்) is word for word the closing line of the Pattinappaalai's list of goods pouring into Puhar (its line 193), so the epic's survey of the same city is built on the older poem's praise. The list names crafts and trades by the worker, not the guild or caste, and puts the bards (பெரும்பாண்) among the skilled trades.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kudi","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 16-39","lb":"SI5:16-39","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:40-58":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":38,"t":["கோவியன் வீதியும், கொடித்தேர் வீதியும்,","பீடிகைத் தெருவும், பெருங்குடி வாணிகர்","மாட மறுகும், மறையோர் இருக்கையும்,","வீழ்குடி உழவரொடு விளங்கிய கொள்கை","ஆயுள் வேதரும் காலக் கணிதரும்","பால்வகை தெரிந்த பன்முறை இருக்கையும்,","திருமணி குயிற்றுநர் சிறந்த கொள்கையோடு","அணிவளை போழுநர் அகன்பெரு வீதியும்,","சூதர் மாகதர் வேதா ளிகரொடு","நாழிகைக் கணக்கர் நலம்பெறு கண்ணுளர்","காவல் கணிகையர் ஆடல் கூத்தியர்","பூவிலை மடந்தையர் ஏவல் சிலதியர்","பயில்தொழில் குயிலுவர் பன்முறைக் கருவியர்","நகைவே ழம்பரொடு வகைதெரி இருக்கையும்,","கடும்பரி கடவுநர் களிற்றின் பாகர்","நெடுந்தேர் ஊருநர் கடுங்கண் மறவர்","இருந்துபுறம் சுற்றிய பெரும்பாய் இருக்கையும்,","பீடுகெழு சிறப்பின் பெரியோர் மல்கிய","பாடல்சால் சிறப்பின் பட்டினப் பாக்கமும்,"],"p":"","r":"The second quarter, Pattinappakkam, is listed: the royal street and chariot street, the market square, the mansions of the great merchant families, the Brahmins' quarter, the farmers, the physicians and astrologers, the gem-setters and conch-cutters, the bards, heralds and panegyrists, the time-keepers, actors, courtesans and dancing women, flower-sellers and maids, drummers and players, jesters, and the horsemen, elephant-drivers, charioteers and fierce warriors camped round the palace.","n":"The inner town is ordered around the king: it opens on the royal and chariot streets and closes with the soldiers who ring the palace (இருந்துபுறம் சுற்றிய), with the courtesans (காவல் கணிகையர்) placed among the king's own servants, the world Madhavi belongs to.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","arasu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 40-58","lb":"SI5:40-58","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:59-75":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":39,"t":["இருபெரு வேந்தர் முனையிடம் போல","இருபால் பகுதியின் இடைநிலம் ஆகிய","கடைகால் யாத்த மிடைமரச் சோலைக்","கொடுப்போர் ஓதையும் கொள்வோர் ஓதையும்","நடுக்குஇன்றி நிலைஇய நாள்அங் காடியில்","சித்திரைச் சித்திரத் திங்கள் சேர்ந்தென","வெற்றிவேல் மன்னற்கு உற்றதை ஒழிக்க எனத்","தேவர் கோமான் ஏவலின் போந்த","காவல் பூதத்துக் கடைகெழு பீடிகைப்","புழுக்கலும் நோலையும் விழுக்குஉடை மடையும்","பூவும் புகையும் பொங்கலும் சொரிந்து","துணங்கையர் குரவையர் அணங்குஎழுந்து ஆடிப்","பெருநில மன்னன் இருநிலம் அடங்கலும்","பசியும் பிணியும் பகையும் நீங்கி","வசியும் வளனும் சுரக்க என வாழ்த்தி","மாதர்க் கோலத்து வலவையின் உரைக்கும்","மூதிற் பெண்டிர் ஓதையின் பெயர,"],"p":"","r":"Between the two quarters, like the no-man's-land between two kings' battle lines, lies the day market in a grove of close-set trees loud with buyers and sellers; there, when the month of Chittirai brings the full moon of Chitra, the women of the old warrior houses heap rice, cakes, flowers and incense on the altar of the guardian spirit that Indra sent to remove the king's trouble, dance possessed, and bless the king that hunger, disease and enmity may leave his land and plenty and abundance flow.","n":"The festival begins not with Indra but with the spirit he sent (தேவர் கோமான் ஏவலின் போந்த காவல் பூதம்), and with women who speak as possessed (வலவையின் உரைக்கும்): the blessing for the whole land is uttered in trance. The comparison of the market to the ground between two armies makes peace look like a truce.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anangu","vendhan","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 59-75","lb":"SI5:59-75","ev":"At the Chitra full moon the women of the warrior houses make offerings at the guardian spirit's altar, dance possessed and bless the king.","cl":[],"ag":["மூதிற் பெண்டிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:76-88":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":40,"t":["மருவூர் மருங்கின் மறம்கொள் வீரரும்","பட்டின மருங்கின் படைகெழு மாக்களும்","முந்தச் சென்று முழுப்பலி பீடிகை","வெந்திறல் மன்னற்கு உற்றதை ஒழிக்கவெனப்","பலிக்கொடை புரிந்தோர் வலிக்குவரம்பு ஆகவெனக்","கல்உமிழ் கவணினர் கழிப்பிணிக் கறைத்தோல்","பல்வேல் பரப்பினர் மெய்உறத் தீண்டி","ஆர்த்துக் களம்கொண்டோர் ஆர்அமர் அழுவத்துச்","சூர்த்துக் கடைசிவந்த சுடுனோக்குக் கருந்தலை","வெற்றி வேந்தன் கொற்றம் கொள்கவென","நற்பலி பீடிகை நலம்கொள வைத்துஆங்கு","உயிர்ப்பலி உண்ணும் உருமுக்குரல் முழக்கத்து","மயிர்க்கண் முரசொடு வான்பலி ஊட்டி,"],"p":"","r":"The fighting men of both quarters hurry first to the altar of the great sacrifice and, praying that the king's trouble be removed and that the offering be a bulwark of his strength, the slingers and spearmen who have won hard battles cut off their own dark heads with fierce reddened eyes, set them on the altar crying 'may the victorious king win', and with the thunder of the drum feed the spirit that eats life-offerings.","n":"The life-offering (உயிர்ப்பலி) here is the warriors' own heads offered for the king's victory; the narrator gives it no judgment, setting it among the festival's opening rites. The sense of 'cut off their heads' is taken from the head placed on the altar (கருந்தலை ... நற்பலி பீடிகை ... வைத்து); the verb itself is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vazhipaadu","vendhan","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 76-88","lb":"SI5:76-88","ev":"Warriors of both quarters offer their own heads at the altar for the king's victory.","cl":[],"ag":["மறம்கொள் வீரர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:89-110":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":41,"t":["இருநில மருங்கின் பொருநரைப் பெறாஅச்","செருவெங் காதலின் திருமா வளவன்","வாளும் குடையும் மயிர்க்கண் முரசும்","நாளொடு பெயர்த்து நண்ணார்ப் பெறுகஇம்","மண்ணக மருங்கின்என் வலிகெழு தோள்எனப்","புண்ணியத் திசைமுகம் போகிய அந்நாள்","அசைவுஇல் ஊக்கத்து நசைபிறக்கு ஒழியப்","பகைவிலக் கியதுஇப் பயம்கெழு மலைஎன","இமையவர் உறையும் சிமையப் பிடர்த்தலைக்","கொடுவரி ஒற்றிக் கொள்கையின் பெயர்வோற்கு,","மாநீர் வேலி வச்சிர நன்னாட்டுக்","கோன்இறை கொடுத்த கொற்றப் பந்தரும்","மகதநன் நாட்டு வாள்வாய் வேந்தன்","பகைபுறத்துக் கொடுத்த பட்டிமண் டபமும்,","அவந்தி வேந்தன் உவந்தனன் கொடுத்த","நிவந்துஓங்கு மரபின் தோரண வாயிலும்","பொன்னினும் மணியினும் புனைந்தன ஆயினும்","நுண்வினைக் கம்மியர் காணா மரபின,","துயர்நீங்கு சிறப்பின்அவர் தொல்லோர் உதவிக்கு","மயன்விதித்துக் கொடுத்த மரபின, இவைதாம்","ஒருங்குடன் புணர்ந்துஆங்கு உயர்ந்தோர் ஏத்தும்","அரும்பெறல் மரபின் மண்டபம் அன்றியும்,"],"p":"","r":"The narrator recalls how Thirumavalavan, finding no match for his war-lust on earth, set out to the holy north with sword, umbrella and drum, asking that his arms find foes, and, stopped by the Himalaya, carved his tiger on its peak; on his way back the king of the Vajra land gave him a pavilion, the defeated king of Magadha a hall of debate, and the king of Avanti, gladly, an arched gate, gifts of gold and gems that no craftsman could make, made by Mayan, which now stand together in Puhar as a hall the great praise.","n":"The episode is told as the past (அந்நாள்), to explain monuments of the festival city; the king's name, திருமா வளவன், is the name of the Pattinappaalai's patron, and the Tamil supports no more than that shared name. The mountain halts him rather than any rival (பகைவிலக்கியது இப் பயம்கெழு மலை). Who received Mayan's help in lines 107-108 (அவர் தொல்லோர் உதவிக்கு) is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 89-110","lb":"SI5:89-110","ev":"Thirumavalavan goes north to the Himalaya, sets his tiger emblem there, and receives a pavilion, a debating hall and a gateway from three northern kings.","cl":[],"ag":["திருமா வளவன்","வச்சிர நன்னாட்டுக் கோன்","மகதநன் நாட்டு வேந்தன்","அவந்தி வேந்தன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:111-127":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":42,"t":["வம்ப மாக்கள் தம்பெயர் பொறித்த","கண்ணெழுத்துப் படுத்த எண்ணுப் பல்பொதிக்","கடைமுக வாயிலும் கருந்தாழ்க் காவலும்","உடையோர் காவலும் ஒரீஇய ஆகிக்","கட்போர் உளர்எனின் கடுப்பத் தலைஏற்றிக்","கொட்பின் அல்லது கொடுத்தல் ஈயாது","உள்ளுநர்ப் பனிக்கும் வெள்ளிடை மன்றமும்,","கூனும் குறளும் ஊமும் செவிடும்","அழுகுமெய் யாளரும் முழுகினர் ஆடிப்","பழுதுஇல் காட்சி நன்னிறம் பெற்று","வலம்செயாக் கழியும் இலஞ்சி மன்றமும்,","வஞ்சம் உண்டு மயல்பகை உற்றோர்","நஞ்சம் உண்டு நடுங்குதுயர் உற்றோர்","அழல்வாய் நாகத்து ஆர்எயிறு அழுந்தினர்","கழல்கண் கூளிக் கடுநவைப் பட்டோர்","சுழல வந்து தொழத்துயர் நீங்கும்","நிழல்கால் நெடுங்கல் நின்ற மன்றமும்,"],"p":"","r":"Besides the hall, Puhar has the open court where traders' bundles marked with their names lie unguarded without lock or keeper, because any thief is made to carry his load on his head and whirl round; the court of the pool where the hunchbacked, dwarfs, dumb, deaf and lepers bathe, gain a flawless form and leave after circling it; and the court of the tall stone where those deceived and maddened, poisoned, snake-bitten or struck by demons come round, worship and are freed of their pain.","n":"The courts (மன்றம்) do the work of watchmen and physicians: theft is prevented by the place itself, and healing comes by circling and worship rather than by medicine. The city's safety is presented as a marvel, not as the king's police.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","noy","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 111-127","lb":"SI5:111-127","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:128-134":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":43,"t":["தவம்மறைந்து ஒழுகும் தன்மை இலாளர்","அவம்மறைந்து ஒழுகும் அலவல் பெண்டிர்","அறைபோகு அமைச்சர் பிறர்மனை நயப்போர்","பொய்க்கரி யாளர் புறங்கூற் றாளர்என்","கைக்கொள் பாசத்துக் கைப்படு வோர்எனக்","காதம் நான்கும் கடுங்குரல் எடுப்பிப்","பூதம் புடைத்துஉணும் பூத சதுக்கமும்,"],"p":"","r":"At the crossroads of the spirit, a spirit roars across four kathams that hypocrites who hide behind penance, unchaste women who hide their wrong, ministers who betray, men who desire others' wives, false witnesses and backbiters will fall into the noose in its hand, and it beats and devours them.","n":"The spirit's list is a list of hidden wrongs: each kind of offender is one whose fault is concealed (மறைந்து ஒழுகும் twice), betrays a trust or speaks falsely behind others' backs, so the punishment exposes what ordinary justice cannot see.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","piranil","purangooral","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 128-134","lb":"SI5:128-134","ev":"","cl":[["hidden wrongdoers, the false ascetic, the unfaithful wife, the treacherous minister, the adulterer, the false witness and the backbiter, deserve to be seized and killed","general","asserted",132,"sathukka_bhutam"]],"ag":["பூதம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:135-140":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":44,"t":["அரைசுகோல் கோடினும் அறம்கூறு அவையத்து","உரைநூல் கோடி ஒருதிறம் பற்றினும்","நாவொடு நவிலாது நவைநீர் உகுத்துப்","பாவைநின்று அழுஉம் பாவை மன்றமும்,","மெய்வகை உணர்ந்த விழுமியோர் ஏத்தும்","ஐவகை மன்றத்தும் அரும்பலி உறீஇ,"],"p":"","r":"There is also the court of the image, where if the king's sceptre bends or a judge in the hall of justice bends the law-book and takes one side, the image weeps without a word; and in all five of these courts, praised by those who know the truth, rare offerings are made.","n":"The image weeps for two failures, not one: the king's bent sceptre and the judge who twists the treatise (உரைநூல் கோடி), so the court is a check on the king's justice and on his court's learning. It cannot speak or act (நாவொடு நவிலாது); it can only grieve.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","naduvu","sengol","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 135-140","lb":"SI5:135-140","ev":"","cl":[["when a king's sceptre bends or a judge leans to one side, the wrong is such that even a statue weeps","general","asserted",135,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:141-156":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":45,"t":["வச்சிரக் கோட்டத்து மணம்கெழு முரசம்","கச்சை யானைப் பிடர்த்தலை ஏற்றி,","வால்வெண் களிற்றுஅரசு வயங்கிய கோட்டத்துக்","கால்கோள் விழவின் கடைநிலை சாற்றித்","தங்கிய கொள்கைத் தருநிலைக் கோட்டத்து","மங்கல நெடுங்கொடி வான்உற எடுத்து,","மரகத மணியொடு வயிரம் குயிற்றிப்","பவளத் திரள்கால் பைம்பொன் வேதிகை","நெடுநிலை மாளிகைக் கடைமுகத்து யாங்கணும்","கிம்புரிப் பகுவாய்க் கிளர்முத்து ஒழுக்கத்து","மங்கலம் பொறித்த மகர வாசிகைத்","தோரணம் நிலைஇய தோம்அறு பசும்பொன்","பூரண கும்பத்துப் பொலிந்த பாலிகை","பாவை விளக்குப் பசும்பொன் படாகை","தூமயிர்க் கவரி சுந்தரச் சுண்ணத்து","மேவிய கொள்கை வீதியில் செறிந்துஆங்கு,"],"p":"","r":"The festival drum is taken from the Vajra temple and set on the neck of an elephant, the start of the festival is proclaimed at the temple of the white elephant, the auspicious flag is raised to the sky at the temple of the wish-granting tree, and in every street the gates of the mansions are hung with pearl-mouthed arches, golden pots, lamps held by figures, banners, whisks and fragrant powder.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 141-156","lb":"SI5:141-156","ev":"The festival drum goes out on an elephant, the festival is proclaimed and its flag raised, and the streets are decorated.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:157-168":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":46,"t":["ஐம்பெருங் குழுவும் எண்பேர் ஆயமும்","அரச குமரரும் பரத குமரரும்","கவர்ப்பரிப் புரவியர் களிற்றின் தொகுதியர்","இவர்ப்பரித் தேரினர் இயைந்துஒருங்கு ஈண்டி","அரைசுமேம் படீஇய அகனிலை மருங்கில்","உரைசால் மன்னன் கொற்றம் கொள்கென","மாஇரு ஞாலத்து மன்உயிர் காக்கும்","ஆயிரத்து ஓர்எட்டு அரசுதலைக் கொண்ட","தண்நறுங் காவிரித் தாதுமலி பெருந்துறைப்","புண்ணிய நல்நீர் பொன்குடத்து ஏந்தி","மண்ணகம் மருள வானகம் வியப்ப","விண்ணவர் தலைவனை விழுநீர் ஆட்டி,"],"p":"","r":"The five great councils and the eight groups of attendants, princes, sons of merchants, horsemen, elephant-men and charioteers gather, wishing victory to the famed king, carry in golden pots the holy water of the Kaveri's great ford, and bathe the lord of the gods in it, to the wonder of earth and sky.","n":"Line 164 (ஆயிரத்து ஓர்எட்டு அரசுதலைக் கொண்ட) is unclear: it may describe the ford where a thousand and eight kings bathed, or the pots or kings who bear the water; the reading here leaves it open. The festival's central rite, the bathing of Indra, is performed by the king's councils on the king's behalf.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","vazhipaadu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 157-168","lb":"SI5:157-168","ev":"The king's councils and attendants bathe Indra's image in Kaveri water carried in golden pots.","cl":[],"ag":["ஐம்பெருங் குழு","எண்பேர் ஆயம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:169-186":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":47,"t":["பிறவா யாக்கைப் பெரியோன் கோயிலும்","அறுமுகச் செவ்வேள் அணிதிகழ் கோயிலும்","வால்வளை மேனி வாலியோன் கோயிலும்","நீல மேனி நெடியோன் கோயிலும்","மாலை வெண்குடை மன்னவன் கோயிலும்","மாமுது முதல்வன் வாய்மையின் வழாஅ","நான்மறை மரபின் தீமுறை ஒருபால்,","நால்வகைத் தேவரும் மூவறு கணங்களும்","பால்வகை தெரிந்த பகுதித் தோற்றத்து","வேறுவேறு கடவுளர் சாறுசிறந்து ஒருபால்,","அறவோர் பள்ளியும் அறன்ஓம் படையும்","புறநிலைக் கோட்டத்துப் புண்ணியத் தானமும்","திறவோர் உரைக்கும் செயல்சிறந்து ஒருபால்,","கொடித்தேர் வேந்தனொடு கூடா மன்னர்","அடித்தளை நீக்க அருள்சிறந்து ஒருபால்,","கண்ணு ளாளர் கருவிக் குயிலுவர்","பண்யாழ்ப் புலவர் பாடல் பாணரொடு","எண்அருஞ் சிறப்பின் இசைசிறந்து ஒருபால்,"],"p":"","r":"In the temples of the Unborn One, of six-faced Murugan, of the white-bodied elder brother, of the dark tall god and of the king of the white umbrella, on one side Vedic fire rites follow the ancient way; on another the many classes of gods each have their festival; on another the monasteries of the virtuous and the alms-halls give charity as the wise prescribe; on another kings who would not join the Chola are freed from their fetters; and on another musicians, lute-players and bards make music.","n":"The repeated ஒருபால் ('on one side') sets Vedic sacrifice, the gods' festivals, the charity of the அறவோர் பள்ளி and the release of captive kings side by side without ranking them; the release of the fettered kings is called grace (அருள்). The temple of the 'king of the white umbrella' may be Indra's or a royal shrine; the Tamil does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","samayam","arul","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 169-186","lb":"SI5:169-186","ev":"Rites of every kind go on at once in Puhar's temples, and captive kings are released.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:187-207":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":48,"t":["முழவுக்கண் துயிலாது முடுக்கரும் வீதியும்","விழவுக்களி சிறந்த வியலுள் ஆங்கண்","காதல் கொழுநனைப் பிரிந்துஅலர் எய்தா","மாதர்க் கொடுங்குழை மாதவி தன்னொடு","இல்வளர் முல்லை மல்லிகை மயிலை","தாழிக் குவளை சூழ்செங் கழுநீர்","பயில்பூங் கோதைப் பிணையலிற் பொலிந்து","காமக் களிமகிழ்வு எய்திக் காமர்","பூம்பொதி நறுவிரைப் பொழில்ஆட்டு அமர்ந்து","நாள்மகிழ் இருக்கை நாள்அங் காடியில்","பூமலி கானத்துப் புதுமணம் புக்குப்","புகையும் சாந்தும் புலராது சிறந்து","நகையாடு ஆயத்து நன்மொழி திளைத்துக்","குரல்வாய்ப் பாணரொடு நகரப் பரத்தரொடு","திரிதரு மரபின் கோவலன் போல","இளிவாய் வண்டினொடு இன்இள வேனிலொடு","மலய மாருதம் திரிதரு மறுகில்,","கருமுகில் சுமந்து குறுமுயல் ஒழித்துஆங்கு","இருகருங் கயலொடு இடைக்குமிழ் எழுதி","அங்கண் வானத்து அரவுப்பகை அஞ்சித்","திங்களும் ஈண்டுத் திரிதலும் உண்டுகொல்."],"p":"","r":"In the festive streets where the drums never sleep, the southern breeze wanders with the bees and the young spring like Kovalan, who roams with Madhavi, garlanded in jasmine and lily, a woman never parted from her lover to become the talk of the town, delighting in love, bathing in groves, entering new flower gardens, with bards and town gallants; and the onlookers begin to wonder whether the moon itself, carrying a dark cloud and painting two dark carp with a kumizh flower between them, has come down to wander here for fear of the serpent in the sky.","n":"Kovalan enters the chapter only inside a simile, as the measure of the spring wind's wandering (திரிதரு மரபின் கோவலன் போல), and Madhavi is praised as one who has never been parted from her beloved (காதல் கொழுநனைப் பிரிந்துஅலர் எய்தா), the word கொழுநன், a husband, given to the man who is Kannaki's. The question at lines 204-207 opens a run of wonderings closed by என at line 223; who wonders is not named, and whether the face like the moon is Madhavi's or any festival beauty's is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vizhavu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 187-207","lb":"SI5:187-207","ev":"Kovalan roams the festival streets and gardens with Madhavi.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:208-217":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":49,"t":["நீர்வாய் திங்கள் நீள்நிலத்து அமுதின்","சீர்வாய் துவலைத் திருநீர் மாந்தி","மீன்ஏற்றுக் கொடியோன் மெய்பெற வளர்த்த","வான வல்லி வருதலும் உண்டுகொல்.","இருநில மன்னற்குப் பெருவளம் காட்டத்","திருமகள் புகுந்ததுஇச் செழும்பதி ஆம்என","எரிநிறத்து இலவமும் முல்லையும் அன்றியும்","கருநெடுங் குவளையும் குமிழும் பூத்துஆங்கு","உள்வரி கோலத்து உறுதுணை தேடிக்","கள்ளக் கமலம் திரிதலும் உண்டுகொல்."],"p":"","r":"The wondering goes on: does the heavenly creeper that Kama grew, nourished on the moon's nectar, come here too, and, as if Lakshmi had entered the city to show the king its wealth, does a false lotus wander here with ilavam, mullai, kuvalai and kumizh blooming on it, seeking a partner in its painted disguise?","n":"Each question turns a woman's face into a thing out of place: a creeper from heaven, a lotus walking the streets, its flowers being lips, teeth, eyes and nose. The 'disguise' (உள்வரி கோலம்) suggests the festival's masked or costumed women; the speakers are still the unnamed onlookers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 208-217","lb":"SI5:208-217","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:218-234":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":50,"t":["மன்னவன் செங்கோல் மறுத்தல் அஞ்சிப்","பல்உயிர் பருகும் பகுவாய்க் கூற்றம்","ஆண்மையில் திரிந்துதன் அருந்தொழில் திரியாது","நாண்உடைக் கோலத்து நகைமுகம் கோட்டிப்","பண்மொழி நரம்பின் திவவுயாழ் மிழற்றிப்","பெண்மையில் திரியும் பெற்றியும் உண்டுஎன,","உருவி லாளன் ஒருபெருஞ் சேனை","இகல்அமர் ஆட்டி எதிர்நின்று விலக்கிஅவர்","எழுதுவரி கோலம் முழுமெயும் உறீஇ","விருந்தொடு புக்க பெருந்தோள் கணவரொடு","உடன்உறைவு மரீஇ ஒழுக்கொடு புணர்ந்த","வடமீன் கற்பின் மனையுறை மகளிர்","மாதர்வாள் முகத்து மணித்தோட்டுக் குவளைப்","போது புறங்கொடுத்துப் போகிய செங்கடை","விருந்தின் தீர்ந்திலது ஆயின் யாவதும்","மருந்தும் தரும்கொல்இம் மாநில வரைப்புஎனக்","கையற்று நடுங்கும் நல்வினை நடுநாள்:"],"p":"","r":"Or, they ask, has Death, which drinks many lives, afraid that the king's sceptre will forbid its work, changed its manly form but not its deadly task, and come wandering as a woman with a modest look, a smiling face and a voice like the lute; and against those it meets, Kama's great bodiless army fights, painting their bodies with love's lines, while the wives of Arundhati-like chastity, living with husbands who came home with guests, tremble helpless, wondering whether the earth holds any cure if the red-cornered glance of their eyes does not end with the guest.","n":"The conceit makes the king's just rule the reason women are deadly: under the Chola's sceptre Death cannot kill openly, so it kills as a woman. The last lines (227-234) are hard: whose eye wounds whom, and whether the wives fear their husbands' new loves or suffer their own longing, is not clear from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","sengol","kaamam","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 218-234","lb":"SI5:218-234","ev":"","cl":[["under a king whose sceptre is just, Death cannot do its work openly","general","asserted",218,"narrator"]],"ag":["உருவி லாளன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:5:235-240":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":51,"t":["உள்அகம் நறுந்தாது உறைப்பமீது அழிந்து","கள்உக நடுங்கும் கழுநீர் போலக்","கண்ணகி கருங்கணும் மாதவி செங்கணும்","உள்நிறை கரந்துஅகத்து ஒளித்துநீர் உகுத்தன","எண்ணுமுறை இடத்தினும் வலத்தினும் துடித்தன","விண்ணவர் கோமான் விழவுநாள் அகத்துஎன்."],"p":"","r":"Like a red lily whose heart is so full of pollen that it trembles and spills its honey, Kannaki's dark eyes and Madhavi's red eyes, hiding what filled them within, shed tears, and throbbed, in that order, the left and the right, on the day of the festival of the king of the gods.","n":"The eyes throb 'in the order counted' (எண்ணுமுறை), so the left goes with Kannaki, named first, and the right with Madhavi; read as omens for women, the wife's is the good sign of a husband's return and the courtesan's the bad sign of parting. The chapter's festival thus closes on the two women together, each weeping, at the moment of their fortunes' turn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","pirivu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 5, lines 235-240","lb":"SI5:235-240","ev":"On the festival day Kannaki's left eye and Madhavi's right eye throb and both weep.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணகி","மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:6"},"si:6:1-17":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":52,"t":["வெள்ளி மால்வரை வியன்பெருஞ் சேடிக்","கள்அவிழ் பூம்பொழில் காமக் கடவுட்குக்","கருங்கயல் நெடுங்கண் காதலி தன்னொடு","விருந்தாட்டு அயரும்ஓர் விஞ்சை வீரன்","தென்திசை மருங்கின்ஓர் செழும்பதி தன்னுள்","இந்திர விழவுகொண்டு எடுக்கும்நாள் இதுஎனக்","கடுவிசை அவுணர் கணம்கொண்டு ஈண்டிக்","கொடுவரி ஊக்கத்துக் கோநகர் காத்த","தொடுகழல் மன்னற்குத் தொலைந்தனர் ஆகி","நெஞ்சுஇருள் கூர நிகர்த்துமேல் விட்ட","வஞ்சம் பெயர்த்த மாபெரும் பூதம்","திருந்துவேல் அண்ணற்குத் தேவர்கோன் ஏவ","இருந்துபலி உண்ணும் இடனும் காண்குதும்,","அமரா பதிகாத்து அமரனிற் பெற்றுத்","தமரில் தந்து தகைசால் சிறப்பின்","பொய்வகை இன்றிப் பூமியில் புணர்த்த","ஐவகை மன்றத்து அமைதியும் காண்குதும்,"],"p":"","r":"A vidyadhara warrior, holding a love-feast with his long-eyed beloved in a grove on the slopes of the silver mountain, tells her that this is the day a rich city in the south begins its festival for Indra, and proposes that they go to see the place where the great bhuta sits and eats offerings, the bhuta the king of the gods sent to the lance-bearing king who guarded the royal city against the asuras and turned back their deceit, and the order of the five assembly-halls that king won from the immortal for guarding Amaravati and set upon earth.","n":"Puhar is not named: it enters the epic's second festival chapter as 'a rich city in the south' seen from a heavenly being's distance, and what he wants to see first are its sacred fixtures, which the unit explains as heaven's rewards to a Chola who once guarded Indra's own city. The festival is thus framed as a debt between the gods and the Chola line. The king is named only by epithets (தொடுகழல் மன்னன், திருந்துவேல் அண்ணல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","vendhan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 6, lines 1-17","lb":"SI6:1-17","ev":"A vidyadhara feasting with his beloved on the silver mountain proposes that they fly south to see the Indra festival of Puhar.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையன்","காதலி"],"ch":"si:7"},"si:6:18-26":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":53,"t":["நாரதன் வீணை நயம்தெரி பாடலும்","தோரிய மடந்தை வாரம் பாடலும்","ஆயிரம் கண்ணோன் செவியகம் நிறைய","நாடகம் உருப்பசி நல்காள் ஆகி","மங்கலம் இழப்ப வீணை மண்மிசைத்","தங்குக இவள்எனச் சாபம் பெற்ற","மங்கை மாதவி வழிமுதல் தோன்றிய","அங்குஅரவு அல்குல் ஆடலும் காண்குதும்,","துவர்இதழ்ச் செவ்வாய்த் துடிஇடை யோயே."],"p":"","r":"He goes on: they will hear the song that knows the beauty of Narada's lute and the refrain sung by the leading singer, and see the dance of the snake-hipped girl born in the line of the maiden Madhavi, who, when Urvasi failed in her dance before the thousand-eyed Indra and the music lost its auspiciousness, was cursed to stay on earth with the lute; and he ends by hailing his coral-lipped, drum-waisted beloved.","n":"The syntax of lines 20 to 24 is hard to fix: the one cursed is called மங்கை மாதவி, and whether that is Urvasi herself under another name or a second celestial dancer is not settled by the lines. What is clear is that the earthly Madhavi's art is presented as an inheritance from a heavenly dancer's fall: her dancing on earth is the continuation of a curse. Line 26 closes the vidyadhara's speech with a vocative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saabam","koothu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 6, lines 18-26","lb":"SI6:18-26","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையன்","காதலி"],"ch":"si:7"},"si:6:27-45":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":54,"t":["அமரர் தலைவனை வணங்குதும் யாம்எனச்","சிமையத்து இமையமும் செழுநீர்க் கங்கையும்","உஞ்சையம் பதியும் விஞ்சத்து அடவியும்","வேங்கட மலையும் தாங்கா விளையுள்","காவிரி நாடும் காட்டிப் பின்னர்ப்","பூவிரி படப்பைப் புகார்மருங்கு எய்திச்","சொல்லிய முறைமையில் தொழுதனன் காட்டி","மல்லல் மூதூர் மகிழ்விழாக் காண்போன்","மாயோன் பாணியும் வருணப் பூதர்","நால்வகைப் பாணியும் நலம்பெறு கொள்கை","வான்ஊர் மதியமும் பாடிப் பின்னர்ச்","சீர்இயல் பொலிய நீர்அல நீங்கப்","பாரதி ஆடிய பாரதி அரங்கத்துத்","திரிபுரம் எரியத் தேவர் வேண்ட","எரிமுகப் பேர்அம்பு ஏவல் கேட்ப","உமையவள் ஒருதிறன் ஆக ஓங்கிய","இமையவன் ஆடிய கொடுகொட்டி ஆடலும்,","தேர்முன் நின்ற திசைமுகன் காணப்","பாரதி ஆடிய வியன்பாண்ட ரங்கமும்,"],"p":"","r":"Saying they will worship the lord of the immortals, he shows his beloved the Himalaya, the Ganga, Ujjain, the Vindhya forest, the Venkatam hill and the Kaveri country, reaches Puhar, worships there in due order, sings the songs to Mayon, to the bhutas of the four varnas and to the moon, and then begins to point out the dances: the kodukotti that Siva, with Uma as one half of him, danced when the three cities burned at the gods' request and the fiery arrow obeyed, and the pandarangam he danced with the four-faced Brahma watching from before the chariot.","n":"The flight runs north to south across the whole subcontinent and ends at the Kaveri, so Puhar's festival is set at the end of a sacred geography. The dances are told as myths of origin: each stage form is what a god once danced at a victory, and the Tripura burning is given as a literal event (the gods asked, the arrow obeyed). பாரதி ஆடிய பாரதி அரங்கத்து (line 39) names Siva and his stage by a title whose sense the lines do not fix.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","thirukkoothu","vizhavu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 6, lines 27-45","lb":"SI6:27-45","ev":"The vidyadhara flies with his beloved over the northern mountains and rivers to Puhar, worships Indra, sings, and begins to name the dances.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:7"},"si:6:46-67":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":55,"t":["கஞ்சன் வஞ்சம் கடத்தற் காக","அஞ்சன வண்ணன் ஆடிய ஆடலுள்","அல்லியத் தொகுதியும், அவுணன் கடந்த","மல்லின் ஆடலும், மாக்கடல் நடுவண்","நீர்த்திரை அரங்கத்து நிகர்த்துமுன் நின்ற","சூர்த்திறம் கடந்தோன் ஆடிய துடியும்,","படைவீழ்த்து அவுணர் பையுள் எய்தக்","குடைவீழ்த்து அவர்முன் ஆடிய குடையும்,","வாணன் பேரூர் மறுகிடை நடந்து","நீள்நிலம் அளந்தோன் ஆடிய குடமும்,","ஆண்மை திரிந்த பெண்மைக் கோலத்துக்","காமன் ஆடிய பேடி ஆடலும்,","காய்சின அவுணர் கடுந்தொழில் பொறாஅள்","மாயவள் ஆடிய மரக்கால் ஆடலும்,","செருவெம் கோலம் அவுணர் நீங்கத்","திருவின் செய்யோள் ஆடிய பாவையும்,","வயல்உழை நின்று வடக்கு வாயிலுள்","அயிராணி மடந்தை ஆடிய கடையமும்,","அவரவர் அணியுடன் அவரவர் கொள்கையின்","நிலையும் படிதமும் நீங்கா மரபின்","பதினோர் ஆடலும் பாட்டின் பகுதியும்","விதிமாண் கொள்கையின் விளங்கக் காணாய்."],"p":"","r":"The vidyadhara names the rest of the eleven dances, each a god's dance at a victory: Krishna's alliyam against Kamsa's deceit and his wrestling dance, Murugan's drum dance on the sea's wave-stage after overcoming Sur and his umbrella dance before the disarmed asuras, the pot dance of the one who measured the earth in Bana's city, Kama's dance in a woman's guise, Durga's stilt dance, Lakshmi's doll dance and Indrani's kadaiyam at the northern gate, and bids his beloved see them performed with each one's costume, stances and songs in strict rule.","n":"Almost every dance is the celebration of a god overcoming an asura or a deceit (வஞ்சம்), and the gods are drawn from every cult (Siva, Krishna, Murugan, Kama, Durga, Lakshmi, Indrani) under one festival for Indra. Lines 66 and 67 repeat almost word for word the formula of Madhavi's training in the third kaathai (பதினோர் ஆடலும் ... விதிமாண் கொள்கையின் விளங்க), so the dances learned there are here performed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothu","iraivan","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 6, lines 46-67","lb":"SI6:46-67","ev":"The vidyadhara points out to his beloved the eleven dances of the gods as they are performed at the festival.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["விஞ்சையன்","காதலி"],"ch":"si:7"},"si:6:68-83":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":56,"t":["தாதுஅவிழ் பூம்பொழில் இருந்துயான் கூறிய","மாதவி மரபின் மாதவி இவள்எனக்","காதலிக்கு உரைத்துக் கண்டுமகிழ்வு எய்திய","மேதகு சிறப்பின் விஞ்சையன் அன்றியும்,","அந்தரத்து உள்ளோர் அறியா மரபின்","வந்துகாண் குறூஉம் வானவன் விழவும்","ஆடலும் கோலமும் அணியும் கடைக்கொள","ஊடல் கோலமோடு இருந்தோன் உவப்பப்","பத்துத் துவரினும் ஐந்து விரையினும்","முப்பத்து இருவகை ஓமா லிகையினும்","ஊறின நல்நீர் உரைத்தநெய் வாசம்","நாறுஇருங் கூந்தல் நலம்பெற ஆட்டி,","புகையில் புலர்த்திய பூமென் கூந்தலை","வகைதொறும் மான்மதக் கொழுஞ்சேறு ஊட்டி,","அலத்தகம் ஊட்டிய அம்செஞ் சீறடி","நலத்தகு மெல்விரல் நல்அணி செறீஇப்,"],"p":"","r":"Having told his beloved that this is Madhavi of the line of the Madhavi he spoke of in the grove, the vidyadhara rejoices; and when the festival that the sky-dwellers come to watch unseen, and its dances, costumes and ornaments have ended, Madhavi, to please the man who sat sulking, washes her fragrant hair in water steeped with ten astringents, five scents and thirty-two herbs, dries it in smoke, works musk paste into it, and colours her small feet with lac.","n":"The unit turns from heaven to the bedroom in one sentence: the heavenly watcher's recognition (மாதவி மரபின் மாதவி இவள்) is the last of the celestial frame, and the next person seen is a man sulking after the festival. Why Kovalan sulks the text does not say; that it follows her public dancing is a reader's inference. The man in ஊடல் கோலம் is unnamed here and is Kovalan by line 109.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","oodal","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 6, lines 68-83","lb":"SI6:68-83","ev":"The vidyadhara identifies Madhavi to his beloved; after the festival Madhavi bathes and perfumes her hair and paints her feet to please the sulking Kovalan.","cl":[],"ag":["விஞ்சையன்","மாதவி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:7"},"si:6:84-98":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":57,"t":["பரியகம் நூபுரம் பாடகம் சதங்கை","அரியகம் காலுக்கு அமைவுற அணிந்து,","குறங்கு செறிதிரள் குறங்கினில் செறித்து,","பிறங்கிய முத்தரை முப்பத்து இருகாழ்","நிறம்கிளர் பூந்துகில் நீர்மையின் உடீஇ,","காமர் கண்டிகை தன்னொடு பின்னிய","தூமணித் தோள்வளை தோளுக்கு அணிந்து,","மத்தக மணியொடு வயிரம் கட்டிய","சித்திரச் சூடகம் செம்பொன் கைவளை","பரியகம் வால்வளை பவழப் பல்வளை","அரிமயிர் முன்கைக்கு அமைவுற அணிந்து,","வாளைப் பகுவாய் வணக்குஉறு மோதிரம்","கேழ்கிளர் செங்கேழ் கிளர்மணி மோதிரம்","வாங்குவில் வயிரத்து மரகதத் தாள்செறி","காந்தள் மெல்விரல் கரப்ப அணிந்து,"],"p":"","r":"Madhavi puts five kinds of anklet on her feet, a ring on her thigh, a girdle of thirty-two strands of pearl over fine cloth, armlets of pure gems, bangles of gold, conch and coral on her forearms, and rings shaped like a fish's mouth, of red gems and of diamond and emerald on her soft fingers.","n":"The catalogue is the poem's own display of wealth worn on a body, moving from foot to finger; among its first items are anklets of five names (பரியகம் நூபுரம் பாடகம் சதங்கை அரியகம்), in a story whose turn will rest on one anklet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 6, lines 84-98","lb":"SI6:84-98","ev":"Madhavi adorns her feet, thighs, waist, arms and fingers with anklets, girdle, armlets, bangles and rings.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:7"},"si:6:99-114":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":58,"t":["சங்கிலி நுண்தொடர் பூண்ஞான் புனைவினை","அம்கழுத்து அகவயின் ஆரமோடு அணிந்து,","கயிற்கடை ஒழுகிய காமர் தூமணி","செயத்தகு கோவையின் சிறுபுறம் மறைத்துஆங்கு","இந்திர நீலத்து இடைஇடை திரண்ட","சந்திர பாணி தகைபெறு கடிப்பிணை","அங்காது அகவயின் அழகுற அணிந்து,","தெய்வ உத்தியொடு செழுநீர் வலம்புரி","தொய்யகம் புல்லகம் தொடர்ந்த தலைக்குஅணி","மைஈர் ஓதிக்கு மாண்புற அணிந்து,","கூடலும் ஊடலும் கோவலற்கு அளித்துப்","பாடுஅமை சேக்கைப் பள்ளியுள் இருந்தோள்,","உருகெழு மூதூர் உவவுத்தலை வந்தெனப்","பெருநீர் போகும் இரியல் மாக்களொடு","மடல்அவிழ் கானல் கடல்விளை யாட்டுக்","காண்டல் விருப்பொடு வேண்டினள் ஆகி,"],"p":"","r":"She puts fine chains and a necklace on her throat, a string of gems to hide the small of her back, ear-ornaments of sapphire and moonstone and the god's crest, the conch and other jewels on her dark hair; and she who had given Kovalan both union and sulking and sat on the couch fit for song, when the full moon came to the old city and crowds streamed toward the sea, longed to see the sea-play on the flowering shore and asked for it.","n":"Line 109 makes union and sulking things Madhavi gives (அளித்து) to Kovalan: the woman dispenses both sides of love's play, and the one who sulked was he (line 75). The wish to see the sea-play is hers, so the outing that will part them starts from her desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","azhagu","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 6, lines 99-114","lb":"SI6:99-114","ev":"Adorned, Madhavi, having given Kovalan union and sulking, asks to go to see the full-moon sea bathing on the shore.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:7"},"si:6:115-132":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":59,"t":["பொய்கைத் தாமரைப் புள்வாய் புலம்ப","வைகறை யாமம் வாரணம் காட்ட","வெள்ளி விளக்கம் நள்இருள் கடியத்","தார்அணி மார்பனொடு பேர்அணி அணிந்து","வான வண்கையன் அத்திரி ஏற","மான்அமர் நோக்கியும் வையம் ஏறிக்","கோடிபல அடுக்கிய கொழுநிதிக் குப்பை","மாடமலி மறுகின் பீடிகைத் தெருவின்","மலர்அணி விளக்கத்து மணிவிளக்கு எடுத்துஆங்கு","அலர்க்கொடி அறுகும் நெல்லும் வீசி","மங்கலத் தாசியர் தம்கலன் ஒலிப்ப","இருபுடை மருங்கினும் திரிவனர் பெயரும்","திருமகள் இருக்கை செவ்வனம் கழிந்து","மகர வாரி வளம்தந்து ஓங்கிய","நகர வீதி நடுவண் போகிக்","கலம்தரு திருவின் புலம்பெயர் மாக்கள்","வேலை வாலுகத்து விரிதிரைப் பரப்பில்","கூல மறுகில் கொடிஎடுத்து நுவலும்"],"p":"","r":"At dawn, when the birds of the lotus pond cry, the cock announces the watch and the morning star clears the dark, Kovalan of the rain-like giving hand mounts a mule and deer-eyed Madhavi a carriage, and they pass the street of piled wealth and the altar street where auspicious women carrying lamps scatter flowers, grass and paddy with jingling ornaments, go straight past the seat of the goddess of fortune and down the city's middle street, toward the quarter where foreign merchants raise flags on the sea sand.","n":"Kovalan is named here by his liberality (வான வண்கையன், the man whose hand gives like the sky), and the couple's road runs through the heaped wealth and 'the seat of Tirumagal' (line 127); a reader who knows that his wealth will be gone by the ninth kaathai may hear the passage as a farewell to fortune, but that is interpretation, not the text's statement.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vizhavu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 6, lines 115-132","lb":"SI6:115-132","ev":"At dawn Kovalan rides a mule and Madhavi a carriage through Puhar's rich streets, greeted by auspicious women with lamps, flowers, grass and paddy.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","மாதவி","மங்கலத் தாசியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:7"},"si:6:133-149":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":60,"t":["மாலைச் சேரி மருங்குசென்று எய்தி,","வண்ணமும் சாந்தும் மலரும் சுண்ணமும்","பண்ணியப் பகுதியும் பகர்வோர் விளக்கமும்,","செய்வினைக் கம்மியர் கைவினை விளக்கமும்,","காழியர் மோதகத்து ஊழ்உறு விளக்கமும்,","கூவியர் கார்அகல் குடக்கால் விளக்கமும்,","நொடைநவில் மகடூஉக் கடைகெழு விளக்கமும்,","இடைஇடை மீன்விலை பகர்வோர் விளக்கமும்,","இலங்குநீர் வரைப்பின் கலங்கரை விளக்கமும்,","விலங்குவலைப் பரதவர் மீன்திமில் விளக்கமும்,","பொழிபெயர் தேஎத்தர் ஒழியா விளக்கமும்,","கழிபெரும் பண்டம் காவலர் விளக்கமும்","எண்ணுவரம்பு அறியா இயைந்துஒருங்கு ஈண்டி","இடிக்கலப்பு அன்ன ஈர்அயில் மருங்கில்","கடிப்பகை காணும் காட்சியது ஆகிய","விரைமலர்த் தாமரை வீங்குநீர்ப் பரப்பின்","மருத வேலியின் மாண்புறத் தோன்றும்"],"p":"","r":"They reach the evening quarter by the market, where the lamps of every trade shine together beyond counting, those of sellers of colours, sandal, flowers, powders and goods, of skilled craftsmen, of sweet-makers and pancake-sellers, of women hawkers and fish-sellers, the lighthouse on the shore, the lamps of the net-fishers' boats, of foreign-tongued strangers and of the watchmen of cargo, and beside them the lotus waters and the bounds of the farmland appear.","n":"The lamps are counted by trade, so the catalogue is a census of the port: each light belongs to a livelihood, from the hawker woman to the lighthouse and the foreigners whose speech is strange (பொழிபெயர் தேஎத்தர்). Lines 145 to 147 (the sand like pounded flour, கடிப்பகை காணும் காட்சியது) are obscure and the reading of them is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 6, lines 133-149","lb":"SI6:133-149","ev":"Kovalan and Madhavi pass through the market and shore of Puhar lit by the lamps of every trade.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:7"},"si:6:150-174":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":61,"t":["கைதை வேலி நெய்தல்அம் கானல்","பொய்தல் ஆயமொடு பூங்கொடி பொருந்தி","நிரைநிரை எடுத்த புரைதீர் காட்சிய","மலைப்பல் தாரமும் கடல்பல் தாரமும்","வளம்தலை மயங்கிய துளங்குகல இருக்கை","அரசுஇளங் குமரரும் உரிமைச் சுற்றமும்","பரத குமரரும் பல்வேறு ஆயமும்","ஆடுகள மகளிரும் பாடுகள மகளிரும்","தோடுகொள் மருங்கின் சூழ்தரல் எழினியும்","விண்பொரு பெரும்புகழ்க் கரிகால் வளவன்","தண்பதம் கொள்ளும் தலைநாள் போல","வேறுவேறு கோலத்து வேறுவேறு கம்பலை","சாறுஅயர் களத்து வீறுபெறத் தோன்றிக்","கடற்கரை மெலிக்கும் காவிரிப் பேரியாற்று","இடம்கெட ஈண்டிய நால்வகை வருணத்து","அடங்காக் கம்பலை உடங்குஇயைந்து ஒலிப்ப,","கடல்புலவு கடிந்த மடல்பூந் தாழைச்","சிறைசெய் வேலி அகவயின் ஆங்குஓர்","புன்னை நீழல் புதுமணல் பரப்பில்","ஓவிய எழினி சூழஉடன் போக்கி","விதானித்துப் படுத்த வெண்கால் அமளிமிசை","வருந்துபு நின்ற வசந்த மாலைகைத்","திருந்துகோல் நல்லியாழ் செவ்வனம் வாங்கிக்","கோவலன் தன்னொடும் கொள்கையின் இருந்தனள்","மாமலர் நெடுங்கண் மாதவி தான்என."],"p":"","r":"On the neythal shore hedged with thazhai, where ships unload the goods of hill and sea and princes, merchants' sons, dancing and singing women and many companies gather behind screens, with different costumes and noises, like the first day of Karikal Valavan's festival of the cool water, and the clamour of the four varnas fills the Kaveri's mouth, Madhavi of long flower-eyes sits with Kovalan on a canopied couch under a punnai inside a thazhai fence, taking the tuned lute from Vasanthamaalai.","n":"The crowd is compared to Karikal Valavan's festival with the phrase தண்பதம் கொள்ளும் தலைநாள், the same words (தண்பதம் கொண்டு) that Akananuru 376 uses for Karikal's water festival at Kazhar: the epic recalls the Sangam king as a past glory, not as the ruling Chola. The unit closes with the lute passing into Madhavi's hand, the instrument of the songs in the next kaathai that will part the lovers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","inbam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 6, lines 150-174","lb":"SI6:150-174","ev":"On the crowded Puhar shore Madhavi sits with Kovalan in a screened bower under a punnai and takes the lute from Vasanthamaalai.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி","கோவலன்","வசந்த மாலை"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:7"},"si:6:175-178":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":62,"t":["வேலை மடல்தாழை உட்பொதிந்த வெண்தோட்டு","மாலைத் துயின்ற மணிவண்டு - காலைக்","களிநறவம் தாதுஊதத் தோன்றிற்றே காமர்","தெளிநிற வெங்கதிரோன் தேர்."],"p":"","r":"A closing venba says that the jewel-like bee that slept at evening inside the white petal of the seaside thazhai went at morning to suck the pollen of the honeyed flower, and the beautiful bright sun's chariot appeared.","n":"On its face a dawn scene; read against the story it may picture Kovalan, the bee who spent the night in one flower and leaves it at morning for another, which the next kaathai makes real. That second sense is an interpretation; நறவம் may be honey or the name of a flower.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 6, lines 175-178","lb":"SI6:175-178","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:7"},"si:7:1-20":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":63,"t":["சித்திரப் படத்துள்புக்குச் செழுங்கோட்டின் மலர்புனைந்து","மைத்தடங்கண் மணமகளிர் கோலம்போல் வனப்புஎய்திப்","பத்தரும் கோடும் ஆணியும் நரம்பும்என்று","இத்திறத்துக் குற்றம்நீங்கிய யாழ்கையில் தொழுதுவாங்கி","பண்ணல் பரிவட்டணை ஆராய்தல் தைவரல்","கண்ணிய செலவு விளையாட்டுக் கையூழ்","நண்ணிய குறும்போக்கு என்று நாட்டிய","எண்வகையால் இசைஎழீஇப்","பண்வகையால் பரிவுதீர்ந்து","மரகதமணித் தாள்செறிந்த மணிக்காந்தள் மெல்விரல்கள்","பயிர்வண்டின் கிளைபோலப் பல்நரம்பின் மிசைப்படர","வார்தல் வடித்தல் உந்தல் உறழ்தல்","சீருடன் உருட்டல் தெருட்டல் அள்ளல்","ஏர்உடைப் பட்டடைஎன இசையோர் வகுத்த","எட்டு வகையின் இசைக்கர ணத்துப்","பட்டவகைதன் செவியின்ஓர்த்(து)","ஏவலன், பின் பாணி யாதுஎனக்","கோவலன் கையாழ் நீட்ட, அவனும்","காவிரியை நோக்கினவும் கடல்கானல் வரிப்பாணியும்","மாதவிதன் மனம்மகிழ வாசித்தல் தொடங்குமன்."],"p":"","r":"On the seashore Madhavi takes up the yazh, adorned like a bride and free of faults in body, neck, pegs and strings, worships it, runs through the eight ways of handling the strings and tests by ear the eight ways of fingering, then holds it out to Kovalan asking what song he commands, and he begins to play songs of the Kaveri and of the shore groves to delight her.","n":"The unit is a catalogue of the lute-player's craft (two named lists of eight techniques, lines 5-8 and 12-15), and the instrument is dressed like a bride before the songs begin. The act that matters for the story is small: Madhavi hands the yazh to Kovalan (line 18), so the songs that follow are his choice, and the kaathai's close (line 289) will lay the parting on what the two sang over this yazh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 1-20","lb":"SI7:1-20","ev":"Madhavi tunes the yazh and hands it to Kovalan, who begins to play songs of the Kaveri and the seashore for her.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:21-28":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":64,"t":["திங்கள் மாலை வெண்குடையான்","சென்னி செங்கோல் அதுஒச்சிக்","கங்கை தன்னைப் புணர்ந்தாலும்","புலவாய் வாழி காவேரி.","கங்கை தன்னைப் புணர்ந்தாலும்","புலவாது ஒழிதல் கயல்கண்ணாய்.","மங்கை மாதர் பெருங்கற்புஎன்று","அறிந்தேன் வாழி காவேரி."],"p":"","r":"Kovalan hails the Kaveri: though the Chola of the moon-white umbrella, wielding his just sceptre, unites with the Ganga, she does not sulk, and he says he knows that this not sulking, carp-eyed one, is the great chastity of a noble woman.","n":"The river is the Chola's wife and the Ganga the other woman his northern conquest has taken; the praise of the king is turned into a picture of marriage in which the wife's virtue is to bear his other union without sulking. Sung by a married man to the courtesan he lives with, while his own wife waits at home, the stanza touches all three; the kaathai shows Madhavi reading a hidden meaning in these songs (lines 144-148).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","oodal","vendhan","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 21-28","lb":"SI7:21-28","ev":"","cl":[["a wife who does not sulk when her husband unites with another shows the great chastity of a noble woman","general","reported",27,"kovalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","காவேரி"],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:29-36":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":65,"t":["மன்னும் மாலை வெண்குடையான்","வளையாச் செங்கோல் அதுஓச்சிக்","கன்னி தன்னைப் புணர்ந்தாலும்","புலவாய் வாழி காவேரி.","கன்னி தன்னைப் புணர்ந்தாலும்","புலவாது ஒழிதல் கயல்கண்ணாய்.","மன்னும் மாதர் பெருங்கற்புஎன்று","அறிந்தேன் வாழி காவேரி."],"p":"","r":"The second stanza repeats the praise with the Kanni, the southern maiden river, in place of the Ganga: though the Chola of the unbending sceptre unites with her, the Kaveri does not sulk, and that is a woman's lasting great chastity.","n":"With the Ganga in the north and the Kanni in the south, the two stanzas give the king a rival bride at each end of the land, so the Kaveri's patience covers the whole reach of his conquest. வளையாச் செங்கோல் (line 30) makes the uniting with another a mark of his just rule, not a fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","oodal","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 29-36","lb":"SI7:29-36","ev":"","cl":[["a wife who does not sulk when her husband unites with another keeps a woman's lasting chastity","general","reported",35,"kovalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","காவேரி"],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:37-44":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":66,"t":["உழவர் ஓதை மதகுஓதை","உடைநீர் ஓதை தண்பதங்கொள்","விழவர் ஓதை சிறந்துஆர்ப்ப","நடந்தாய் வாழி காவேரி.","விழவர் ஓதை சிறந்துஆர்ப்ப","நடந்த எல்லாம் வாய்காவா","மழவர் ஓதை வளவன்தன்","வளனே வாழி காவேரி."],"p":"","r":"The third stanza hears the Kaveri walking amid the shouts of ploughmen, the roar of sluices and breaking water and the cries of the festival crowds, and calls all that she has walked through, loud with the Mazhavar warriors, the very wealth of the Chola Valavan.","n":"After two stanzas of the river as wife, this one makes her the source of the king's prosperity, so the couple of river and king is both a marriage and an economy. The sense of வாய்காவா (line 42), said of the Mazhavar, is not settled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","naadu","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 37-44","lb":"SI7:37-44","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","காவேரி"],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:45-52":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":67,"t":["கரியமலர் நெடுங்கண் காரிகைமுன்","கடல்தெய்வம் காட்டிக் காட்டி","அரியசூள் பொய்த்தார் அறன்இலர்என்று","ஏழையம்யாங்கு அறிகோம் ஐய","விரிகதிர் வெண்மதியும் மீன்கணமும்","ஆம்என்றே விளங்கும் வெள்ளைப்","புரிவளையும் முத்தும்கண்டு ஆம்பல்","பொதிஅவிழ்க்கும் புகாரே எம்மூர்."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of the shore girls, the singer asks the lover how they, simple as they are, could have known that the man who showed the sea god again and again before the dark-eyed girl and swore a hard oath would break it and prove without virtue, and names their town as Puhar, where the water lily opens its buds taking white conches and pearls for the moon and stars.","n":"This is the akam friend's charge against a man who has broken his oath, sung as a set piece; the closing refrain (புகாரே எம்மூர்) turns each stanza into a boast of the city. The lily that mistakes conches and pearls for the moon and stars is the same mistake the girl made in trusting appearances.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sool","vaaimai","aram","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 45-52","lb":"SI7:45-52","ev":"","cl":[["those who break a hard-sworn oath are without virtue","general","reported",47,"kovalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:53-60":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":68,"t":["காதலர் ஆகிக் கழிக்கானல்","கையுறைகொண்டு எம்பின் வந்தார்","ஏதிலர் தாமாகி யாம்இரப்ப","நிற்பதையாங்கு அறிகோம் ஐய","மாதரார் கண்ணும் மதிநிழல்நீர்","இணைகொண்டு மலர்ந்த நீலப்","போதும் அறியாது வண்டுஊச","லாடும் புகாரே எம்மூர்."],"p":"","r":"The girls ask how they could have known that the man who became their lover, following them in the backwater grove with gifts, would stand like a stranger while they plead, and name Puhar as their town, where the bee swings unable to tell women's eyes from the blue lilies that bloom paired with the moon's reflection in the water.","n":"The lover's change is set as a turn from காதலர் to ஏதிலர் (lines 53, 55): the same man, first the one who followed with gifts, now the one they must beg. The bee that cannot choose between eye and flower mirrors the lover's wavering.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","pirivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 53-60","lb":"SI7:53-60","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:61-68":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":69,"t":["மோது முதுதிரையால் மொத்துண்டு","போந்துஅசைந்த முரல்வாய்ச் சங்கம்","மாதர் வரிமணல்மேல் வண்டல்","உழுதுஅழிப்ப மாழ்கி ஐய","கோதை பரிந்துஅசைய மெல்விரலால்","கொண்டுஓச்சும் குவளை மாலைப்","போது சிறங்கணிப்பப் போவார்கண்","போகாப் புகாரே எம்மூர்."],"p":"","r":"The third stanza pictures a conch, battered by old waves, ploughing through and spoiling a girl's sand-play on the striped beach, and the girl, distressed, flinging her kuvalai garland at it with soft fingers so that the flowers seem to glance sidelong, and in this Puhar those who pass cannot leave.","n":"Only here, of the three stanzas, there is no accusation; the girl's small anger is enough to hold the passers-by, and the garland's flowers, like eyes, do the looking. The spoiled sand-play is a stock neithal sign of the lover's first coming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 61-68","lb":"SI7:61-68","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:69-75":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":70,"t":["துறைமேய் வலம்புரி தோய்ந்து மணல்உழுத","தோற்றம் மாய்வான்","பொறைமலி பூம்புன்னைப் பூஉதிர்ந்து நுண்தாது","போர்க்கும் கானல்","நிறைமதி வாள்முகத்து நேர்க்கயல்கண் செய்த","உறைமலி உய்யாநோய் ஊர்சுணங்கு மென்முலையே","தீர்க்கும் போலும்."],"p":"","r":"The singer sets the scene of the shore grove, where the trace the conch ploughed in the sand is hidden under the fine pollen of punnai flowers, and wonders whether the incurable sickness that her moon-bright face and carp eyes have given will be cured only by her soft breasts with their golden spots.","n":"The trace in the sand covered by falling pollen may stand for a union that leaves no sign, but the song does not say so. The syntax of தீர்க்கும் போலும் (line 75) leaves open whether the singer hopes or only supposes that the one who caused the sickness can cure it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kan","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 69-75","lb":"SI7:69-75","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:76-82":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":71,"t":["நிணம்கொள் புலால்உணங்கல் நின்றுபுள் ஓப்புதல்","தலைக்கீடு ஆகக்","கணம்கொள் வண்டுஆர்த்து உலாம்கன்னி நறுஞாழல்","கையில் ஏந்தி","மணம்கமழ் பூங்கானல் மன்னிமற்று ஆண்டுஓர்","அணங்குஉறையும் என்பது அறியேன் அறிவேனேல்","அடையேன் மன்னோ."],"p":"","r":"In the man's voice, the singer says that he did not know that in the fragrant shore grove there dwelt a goddess who, with driving birds from drying fish as her pretext, walks holding fresh njaazhal flowers swarmed by bees, and that had he known he would not have come.","n":"The refrain அறியேன் அறிவேனேல் அடையேன் (lines 81-82) makes first sight a danger the man would have avoided if warned; the girl's work, guarding the drying fish, is called a mere pretext for a goddess's presence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 76-82","lb":"SI7:76-82","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:83-89":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":72,"t":["வலைவாழ்நர் சேரி வலைஉணங்கும் முன்றில்","மலர்கை ஏந்தி","விலைமீன் உணங்கல் பொருட்டாக வேண்டுஉருவம்","கொண்டு வேறுஓர்","கொலைவேல் நெடுங்கண் கொடுங்கூற்றம் வாழ்வது","அலைநீர்த்தண் கானல் அறியேன் அறிவேனேல்","அடையேன் மன்னோ."],"p":"","r":"The second stanza says that in the fishers' quarter, where nets dry before the houses, another being, a cruel Death with long eyes like killing spears, has taken a form of its choosing and lives in the cool shore grove, holding flowers, on the pretext of drying fish for sale; he did not know, and had he known he would not have come.","n":"வேண்டுஉருவம் கொண்டு (lines 85-86) makes the girl a disguise: Death has chosen to take a woman's shape, which is stronger than calling her eyes deadly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 83-89","lb":"SI7:83-89","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:90-93":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":73,"t":["கயல்எழுதி வில்எழுதிக் கார்எழுதிக் காமன்","செயல்எழுதித் தீர்ந்தமுகம் திங்களோ காணீர்.","திங்களோ காணீர் திமில்வாழ்நர் சீறூர்க்கே","அம்கண்ஏர் வானத்து அரவுஅஞ்சி வாழ்வதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The singer bids his hearers look: the face on which carp, bow, cloud and Kama's work have been drawn is the moon, which, fearing the serpent in the beautiful sky, has come to live in the boatmen's little village.","n":"The features are listed as things painted (எழுதி) on the face, eyes as carp, brows as bow, hair as cloud, and the moon is given a motive for coming down to earth: fear of the eclipse serpent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 90-93","lb":"SI7:90-93","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:94-97":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":74,"t":["எறிவளைகள் ஆர்ப்ப இருமருங்கும் ஓடும்","கறைகெழுவேல் கண்ணோ கடுங்கூற்றம் காணீர்.","கடுங்கூற்றம் காணீர் கடல்வாழ்நர் சீறூர்க்கே","மடம்கெழுமென் சாயல் மகளா யதுவே."],"p":"","r":"He bids them look again: the stained spear-like eyes that run to either side as her bangles ring are fierce Death, which in the sea-dwellers' little village has become a girl of soft and innocent grace.","n":"The stanza joins the deadliest name to the gentlest: கடுங்கூற்றம் becomes மடம்கெழுமென் சாயல் மகள் (lines 96-97), so innocence is the form the killing takes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 94-97","lb":"SI7:94-97","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:98-101":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":75,"t":["புலவுமீன் வெள்உணங்கல் புள்ஓப்பிக் கண்டார்க்கு","அலவநோய் செய்யும் அணங்குஇதுவோ காணீர்.","அணங்குஇதுவோ காணீர் அடும்புஅமர்த்தண் கானல்","பிணங்குநேர் ஐம்பால்ஓர் பெண்கொண் டதுவே."],"p":"","r":"He bids them look a third time: the goddess who brings anguish to those who see her driving birds from the white drying fish has taken the form of a woman with five-braided hair in the cool shore grove where the adumbu creeper grows.","n":"The three nilaivari stanzas name her moon, Death and goddess in turn, each come down to the fishing village in human form. The force of அலவநோய் (line 99) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 98-101","lb":"SI7:98-101","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:102-105":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":76,"t":["பொழில்தரு நறுமலரே புதுமணம் விரிமணலே","பழுதுஅறு திருமொழியே பணைஇள வனமுலையே","முழுமதி புரைமுகமே முரிபுரு வில்இணையே","எழுதுஅரு மின்இடையே எனைஇடர் செய்தவையே."],"p":"","r":"The singer lists the grove's fragrant flowers, the fresh-scented sand, her flawless words, her young breasts, her moon-like face, her curved brows and her waist too fine to paint, and says these are what brought him distress.","n":"Each line runs two vocatives, and the place and the woman are mixed in one list, so the grove itself is among the causes of the sickness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 102-105","lb":"SI7:102-105","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:106-109":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":77,"t":["திரைவிரி தருதுறையே திருமணல் விரிஇடமே","விரைவிரி நறுமலரே மிடைதரு பொழில்இடமே","மருவிரி புரிகுழலே மதிபுரை திருமுகமே","இருகயல் இணைவிழியே எனைஇடர் செய்தவையே."],"p":"","r":"The second stanza lists the ford where waves spread, the broad sand, the fragrant flowers, the thick grove, her braided hair, her moon-like face and her paired carp eyes as what brought him distress.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 106-109","lb":"SI7:106-109","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:110-113":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":78,"t":["வளைவளர் தருதுறையே மணம்விரி தருபொழிலே","தளைஅவிழ் நறுமலரே தனிஅவள் திரிஇடமே","முளைவளர் இளநகையே முழுமதி புரைமுகமே","இளையவள் இணைமுலையே எனைஇடர் செய்தவையே."],"p":"","r":"The third stanza lists the ford where conches grow, the fragrant grove, the opening flowers, the place where she walks alone, her budding smile, her moon-like face and the young girl's breasts as what brought him distress.","n":"தனிஅவள் திரிஇடமே (line 111) adds the one hint of a meeting: the place where she wanders alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nalam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 110-113","lb":"SI7:110-113","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:114-117":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":79,"t":["கடல்புக்கு உயிர்க்கொன்று வாழ்வர்நின் ஐயர்","உடல்புக்கு உயிர்க்கொன்று வாழ்வைமன் நீயும்","மிடல்புக்கு அடங்காத வெம்முலையோ பாரம்","இடர்புக்கு இடுகும் இடைஇழவல் கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The singer tells the fisher girl that her elders live by going into the sea and taking lives, and she too lives by going into bodies and taking lives, and warns her that under the weight of breasts no strength can contain, her waist, sinking in distress, will be lost.","n":"The fishers' trade of killing is taken as the plain fact of the coast and turned into a compliment: the daughter inherits the family work, killing lives, with her body as the net. The warning about the waist is the usual hyperbole of praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kaamanoy","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 114-117","lb":"SI7:114-117","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:118-121":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":80,"t":["கொடுங்கண் வலையால் உயிர்க்கொல்வான் நுந்தை","நெடுங்கண் வலையால் உயிர்க்கொல்வை மன்நீயும்","வடம்கொள் முலையால் மழைமின்னுப் போல","நுடங்கி உகுமென் நுசுப்புஇழவல் காண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The second stanza says her father kills lives with a crooked-meshed net and she kills lives with the net of her long eyes, and warns her that with breasts bearing strands of pearls her waist, swaying like lightning in rain, will snap.","n":"The pun is in வலை: கொடுங்கண் வலை, the net of crooked mesh, against நெடுங்கண் வலை, the net of long eyes (lines 118-119).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kan","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 118-121","lb":"SI7:118-121","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:8"},"si:7:122-125":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":81,"t":["ஓடும் திமில்கொண்டு உயிர்க்கொள்வர் நின்ஐயர்","கோடும் புருவத்து உயிர்க்கொல்வை மன்நீயும்","பீடும் பிறர்எவ்வம் பாராய் முலைசுமந்து","வாடும் சிறுமென் மருங்குஇழவல் கண்டாய்."],"p":"","r":"The third stanza tells the fisher girl that her brothers take lives with their running boats, and she takes lives with her bending brows, and warns her that, carrying her breasts with no thought for the pain she gives others or for her own state, her small soft waist will waste away and be lost.","n":"The refrain of the three stanzas (114-125) moves the killing from the father's net and the brothers' boats to the girl's eyes and brows: the fisher family's trade in lives becomes her inheritance. கோடும் புருவத்து makes the brow's curve itself the weapon, where the earlier stanzas used the net and the eye.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 122-125","lb":"SI7:122-125","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:126-129":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":82,"t":["பவள உலக்கை கையால் பற்றித்","தவள முத்தம் குறுவாள் செங்கண்","தவள முத்தம் குறுவாள் செங்கண்","குவளை அல்ல கொடிய கொடிய"],"p":"","r":"The singer says the girl who holds a coral pestle in her hand and pounds white pearls has red-lined eyes that are no blue lilies but cruel, cruel things.","n":"Pounding pearls with a coral pestle is a fisher household's work made precious: the girl's ordinary labour is given in the shore's own riches. The line that names her eyes is sung twice, as the song form requires.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 126-129","lb":"SI7:126-129","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:130-133":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":83,"t":["புன்னை நீழல் புலவுத் திரைவாய்","அன்னம் நடப்ப நடப்பாள் செங்கண்","அன்னம் நடப்ப நடப்பாள் செங்கண்","கொன்னே வெய்ய. கூற்றம் கூற்றம்."],"p":"","r":"The singer says the girl who walks as the swan walks on the flesh-smelling shore in the punnai shade has red eyes fierce to no purpose, and calls them Death, Death.","n":"கொன்னே, 'to no end', makes the eyes' cruelty gratuitous: they kill without aiming at anyone, which is the singer's complaint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 130-133","lb":"SI7:130-133","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:134-137":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":84,"t":["கள்வாய் நீலம் கையின் ஏந்திப்","புள்வாய் உணங்கல் கடிவாள் செங்கண்","புள்வாய் உணங்கல் கடிவாள் செங்கண்","வெள்வேல் அல்ல. வெய்ய வெய்ய."],"p":"","r":"The singer says the girl who holds a honey-mouthed blue lily in her hand and drives the birds from the drying fish has red eyes that are not white spears and yet are fierce, fierce.","n":"She carries a flower as the thing she scares birds with, and the singer denies her eyes are spears only to say they are worse: the stanza's work is the reversal of a gentle gesture into a weapon, as in 126-129.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 134-137","lb":"SI7:134-137","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:138-141":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":85,"t":["சேரல் மடஅன்னம் சேரல் நடைஒவ்வாய்","சேரல் மடஅன்னம் சேரல் நடைஒவ்வாய்","ஊர்திரை நீர்வேலி உழக்கித் திரிவாள்பின்","சேரல் மடஅன்னம் சேரல் நடைஒவ்வாய்."],"p":"","r":"The singer tells the young swan not to follow the girl who wanders churning the wave-washed shore, since it cannot match her gait.","n":"This is the last of Kovalan's songs before Madhavi takes the lute. The compliment that the swan is beaten by a woman's walk is one he once paid Kannaki in their bridal chamber; here it goes to an imagined fisher girl, sung before Madhavi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 138-141","lb":"SI7:138-141","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:142-153":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":86,"t":["ஆங்கு, கானல்வரிப் பாடல்கேட்ட","மான்நெடுங்கண் மாதவியும்","மன்னும்ஓர் குறிப்புஉண்டுஇவன்","தன்நிலை மயங்கினான்எனக்","கலவியால் மகிழ்ந்தாள்போல்","புலவியால் யாழ்வாங்கித்","தானும்ஓர் குறிப்பினள்போல்","கானல்வரிப் பாடல்பாணி","நிலத்தெய்வம் வியப்புஎய்த","நீள்நிலத்தோர் மனம்மகிழக்","கலத்தொடு புணர்ந்துஅமைந்த","கண்டத்தால் பாடத்தொடங்கும்மன்."],"p":"","r":"Having heard the kaanal vari, Madhavi decides that Kovalan's songs carry some hidden intent and that he has lost himself in another, and, seeming glad of their union but in a sulk, takes the yazh and, as if she too had a hidden meaning, begins to sing her own kaanal vari in a voice joined to the instrument, so that the land's deity marvels and the people rejoice.","n":"The whole break turns on குறிப்பு: she reads one into his songs (144) and sings as one who has her own (148). Neither intent is confirmed; the poet says only that she seemed (போல்) glad and seemed to have a meaning. The quarrel that ends the union at 286-300 begins here as two readings of songs, not as any act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 142-153","lb":"SI7:142-153","ev":"Madhavi, reading a hidden meaning into Kovalan's songs, takes the yazh in a sulk and begins to sing kaanal vari of her own.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:154-161":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":87,"t":["மருங்கு வண்டு சிறந்துஆர்ப்ப","மணிப்பூ ஆடை அதுபோர்த்துக்","கருங்க யல்கண் விழித்துஒல்கி","நடந்தாய் வாழி காவேரி.","கருங்க யல்கண் விழித்துஒல்கி","நடந்த எல்லாம் நின்கணவன்","திருந்து செங்கோல் வளையாமை","அறிந்தேன் வாழி காவேரி."],"p":"","r":"Madhavi hails the Kaveri, who walks with bees humming at her sides, wearing a garment of jewel flowers and opening her dark kayal eyes, and says she knows that all this graceful going comes from her husband's well-set sceptre not bending.","n":"Madhavi keeps Kovalan's figure of the Kaveri as the Chola's wife but changes what the wife's grace proves: in his stanzas (21-36) it proved her patience with his other unions; in hers it proves his justice. The epic's later turn, Kannaki's case against a king whose sceptre did bend, makes this praise of the Chola sceptre a standard the Pandya will fail.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","karpu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 154-161","lb":"SI7:154-161","ev":"","cl":[["a king's unbending sceptre shows in the ease and plenty of his land, as a wife's bearing shows a just husband","general","asserted",160,"madhavi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி","காவேரி"],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:162-169":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":88,"t":["பூவர் சோலை மயில்ஆலப்","புரிந்து குயில்கள் இசைபாடக்","காமர் மாலை அருகுஅசைய","நடந்தாய் வாழி காவேரி.","காமர் மாலை அருகுஅசைய","நடந்த எல்லாம் நின்கணவன்","நாம வேலின் திறம்கண்டே","அறிந்தேன் வாழி காவேரி."],"p":"","r":"Madhavi hails the Kaveri, who walks as the peacock dances in the flower groves, cuckoos sing and lovely garlands sway at her sides, and says she knows all this going from seeing the strength of her husband's dread spear.","n":"Set beside 154-161, the second stanza grounds the same graceful flow in the king's spear rather than his sceptre: justice and force are given as two causes of one ease, stanza by stanza.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","padai","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 162-169","lb":"SI7:162-169","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி","காவேரி"],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:170-177":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":89,"t":["வாழி அவன்தன் வளநாடு","மகவாய் வளர்க்கும் தாய்ஆகி","ஊழி உய்க்கும் பேர்உதவி","ஒழியாய் வாழி காவேரி.","ஊழி உய்க்கும் பேர்உதவி","ஒழியாது ஒழுகல் உயிர்ஓம்பும்","ஆழி ஆள்வான் பகல்வெய்யோன்","அருளே வாழி காவேரி."],"p":"","r":"Madhavi blesses the Chola's rich land and the Kaveri who, becoming a mother who rears it like a child, never stops her great help that carries the world through the ages, and says that this unceasing flow that sustains life is the grace of the day-bringer who rules the wheel.","n":"ஆழி ஆள்வான் பகல்வெய்யோன் can be the sun, or the Chola as the sun who rules the wheel of command; the evening passage of kaathai 4 calls the sun the world's sole wheel-ruler, so the river's bounty is credited to a ruling power either way. ஊழி here is the age of the world, not fate.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","naadu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 170-177","lb":"SI7:170-177","ev":"","cl":[["the river's unfailing gift that sustains all life is the grace of the ruler of the wheel","general","asserted",177,"madhavi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி","காவேரி"],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:178-181":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":90,"t":["தீங்கதிர் வாள்முகத்தாள் செவ்வாய் மணிமுறுவல் ஒவ்வா வேனும்","வாங்கும்நீர் முத்துஎன்று வைகலும் மால்மகன்போல் வருதிர் ஐய","வீங்குஓதம் தந்து விளங்குஒளிய வெண்முத்தம் விரைசூழ் கானல்","பூங்கோதை கொண்டு விலைஞர்போல் மீளும் புகாரே எம்மூர்."],"p":"","r":"In the shore girl's voice Madhavi tells the man that, though the pearls cannot match the girl's smile, he comes every day like Kama's son saying he wants the pearls the waters bring, and names their town Puhar, where the swelling tide brings shining white pearls to the fragrant shore and goes back, like traders, carrying off flower garlands.","n":"The man's excuse is a buyer's excuse, and the refrain makes the tide itself a merchant bartering pearls for garlands. Sung to Kovalan, a great merchant's son, the stanza mocks a lover who disguises desire as trade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kurippu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 178-181","lb":"SI7:178-181","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:182-185":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":91,"t":["மறையின் மணந்தாரை வன்பரதர் பாக்கத்து மடவார் செங்கை","இறைவளைகள் தூற்றுவதை ஏழையம் எங்ஙனம்யாங்கு அறிகோம் ஐய","நிறைமதியும் மீனும் எனஅன்னம் நீள்புன்னை அரும்பிப் பூத்த","பொறைமலிபூங் கொம்புஏற வண்டுஆம்பல் ஊதும் புகாரே எம்மூர்."],"p":"","r":"In the shore girl's voice Madhavi asks how simple folk like them could know that in the fishing hamlet the bangles slipping from girls' red hands proclaim those who married them in secret, and names Puhar, where swans climbing the budding punnai look like the full moon among stars and bees hum at the water lilies.","n":"The secret is betrayed by the girl's own wasting body: the bangles loosening on a thinning arm do the gossiping (தூற்றுவதை), so the scandal is the man's doing but written on her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 182-185","lb":"SI7:182-185","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:186-189":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":92,"t":["உண்டாரை வெல்நறா ஊண்ஓழியாப் பாக்கத்துள் உறைஒன்று இன்றித்","தண்டாநோய் மாதர் தலைத்தருதி என்பதுயாங்கு அறிகோம் ஐய","வண்டல் திரைஅழிப்பக் கையால் மணல்முகந்து மதிமேல் நீண்ட","புண்தோய்வேல் நீர்மல்க பரதர் கடல்தூர்க்கும் புகாரே எம்மூர்."],"p":"","r":"In the shore girl's voice Madhavi asks how they could know that in the hamlet where strong toddy and food never run out he would bring a girl an unending sickness without any cure, and names Puhar, where girls whose sand-houses the waves have wrecked scoop sand with their hands and, their long spear-like eyes brimming, try to fill up the sea.","n":"The reading turns on உறை (a cure, or a single drop?) and on how புண்தோய்வேல் and பரதர் attach in 188-189; line 189 also carries a repaired character. The image of girls weeping as they try to fill the sea that wrecked their play-houses is clear and makes a child's futile revenge the picture of the girl's grievance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kalaviyal","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 186-189","lb":"SI7:186-189","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:190-193":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":93,"t":["புணர்த்துணையோடு ஆடும் பொறிஅலவன் நோக்கி","இணர்த்ததையும் பூங்கானல் என்னையும் நோக்கி","உணர்வுஒழியப் போன ஒலிதிரைநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பன்","வணர்சுரி ஐம்பாலோய் வண்ணம் உணரேனால்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavi, in the woman's voice, tells her friend of the curled five-plaited hair that the lord of the loud-waved shore, who looked at the spotted crab playing with its mate, at the flowering grove and at her, went away so that her senses left her, and she does not know his mind.","n":"The crab with its mate is the scene of union he watched before leaving, so the stanza sets what he saw against what he did. வண்ணம் உணரேன் is ambiguous between not knowing his nature and not knowing her own state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 190-193","lb":"SI7:190-193","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:194-197":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":94,"t":["தம்முடைய தண்ணளியும் தாமும்தம் மான்தேரும்","எம்மை நினையாது விட்டாரோ விட்டுஅகல்க","அம்மென் இணர அடும்புகாள் அன்னங்காள்","நம்மை மறந்தாரை நாம்மறக்க மாட்டேமால்."],"p":"","r":"Madhavi, in the woman's voice, asks whether he has left them, forgetting with his cool grace and his horse-drawn chariot, says let him go, and tells the soft-clustered adumbu creepers and the swans that she will not forget those who forgot her.","n":"The stanza turns on the shift from 'they' to 'we': he forgot, we will not forget. Sung to Kovalan, the vow of remembering is at once a lament and a reproach, which is how Kovalan hears it in 286-300.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 194-197","lb":"SI7:194-197","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:198-201":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":95,"t":["புன்கண்கூர் மாலைப் புலம்பும்என் கண்ணேபோல்","துன்பம் உழவாய் துயிலப் பெறுதியால்","இன்கள்வாய் நெய்தால்நீ எய்தும் கனவினுள்","வன்கணார் கானல் வரக்கண்டு அறிதியோ?"],"p":"","r":"Madhavi, in the woman's voice, tells the honey-mouthed neythal that, unlike her own eyes which weep in the sorrowful evening, it suffers no pain and gets to sleep, and asks whether in its dreams it sees the cruel man coming to the shore grove.","n":"The neythal closes at dusk, so the flower is said to sleep; she cannot, and so she is shut out even from the dream in which she might see him, and must ask a flower for news of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 198-201","lb":"SI7:198-201","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:202-205":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":96,"t":["புள்இயல்மான் தேர்ஆழி போன வழிஎல்லாம்","தெள்ளுநீர் ஓதம் சிதைத்தாய்மற்று எஞ்செய்கோ?","தெள்ளுநீர் ஓதம் சிதைத்தாய்மற்று எம்மோடுஈங்கு","உள்ளாரோடு உள்ளாய் உணராய்மற்று எஞ்செய்கோ?"],"p":"","r":"Madhavi, in the woman's voice, tells the clear tide that it has broken up every trace of the path where the wheel of his bird-swift horse-chariot went, and asks what she can do, since the tide, which stays with her and with those here, does not understand.","n":"The wheel-track is the last thing left of him, and the tide erases it while staying beside her: the grievance is that the sea is near her and still unfeeling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 202-205","lb":"SI7:202-205","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:206-209":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":97,"t":["நேர்ந்தநம் காதலர் நேமிநெடுந் திண்தேர்","ஊர்ந்த வழிசிதைய ஊர்க்கின்ற ஓதமே","பூந்தண் பொழிலே புணர்ந்துஆடும் அன்னமே","ஈர்ந்தண் துறையே இதுதகாது என்னீரே."],"p":"","r":"Madhavi, in the woman's voice, asks the flower grove, the swans that play in pairs and the cool ford whether they will not tell the tide, which rolls in erasing the path of their pledged lover's tall strong chariot, that this is not right.","n":"நேர்ந்த, 'who pledged', makes the lover one who gave his word; the stanza appeals to the shore's creatures as witnesses to a wrong done to that pledge. The same opening line begins the next stanza (210), which carries the song on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 206-209","lb":"SI7:206-209","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:9"},"si:7:210-213":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":98,"t":["நேர்ந்தநம் காதலர் நேமிநெடுந் திண்தேர்","ஊர்ந்த வழிசிதைய ஊர்ந்தாய்வாழி கடல்ஓதம்","ஊர்ந்த வழிசிதைய ஊர்ந்தாய்மற்(று) எம்மொடு","தீர்ந்தாய்போல் தீர்ந்திலையால் வாழி கடல்ஓதம்."],"p":"","r":"The third stanza of the song to the tide: the singer hails the sea tide that ran over and broke the track her lover's tall wheeled chariot made, and says that it seemed to have done with her but has not.","n":"The three stanzas (202 to 213) turn on one image: the wheel ruts are the only trace the man left, and the tide that wipes them takes even that. The last line (எம்மொடு தீர்ந்தாய்போல் தீர்ந்திலையால்) can be read as the tide seeming to leave with him yet staying to torment her, or as its harm seeming finished and not being so; the unit does not fix which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 210-213","lb":"SI7:210-213","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:214-221":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":99,"t":["நன்நித் திலத்தின் பூண்அணிந்து","நலம்சார் பவளக் கலைஉடுத்துச்","செந்நெல் பழனக் கழனிதொறும்","திரைஉ லாவு கடல்சேர்ப்ப.","புன்னைப் பொதும்பர் மகரத்திண்","கொடியோன் எய்த புதுப்புண்கள்","என்னைக் காணா வகைமறத்தால்","அன்னை காணின் என்செய்கோ?"],"p":"","r":"In a stanza of mixed landscapes the singer hails the lord of the shore, where the waves, wearing pearls and coral, roam even the red-paddy fields, and asks what she will do if her mother sees the fresh wounds that the god of the fish banner shot into her in the punnai grove, wounds she hid by her own guile.","n":"The sea decked in pearl and coral reaches into the paddy fields, which is why the rubric calls these stanzas மயங்கு திணை, mixed landscapes. The heroine fears not the wound but its discovery by the mother, the akam fear of secret love becoming known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 214-221","lb":"SI7:214-221","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:222-229":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":100,"t":["வாரித் தரள நகைசெய்து","வண்செம் பவள வாய்மலர்ந்து","சேரிப் பரதர் வலைமுன்றில்","திரைஉ லாவு கடல்சேர்ப்ப.","மாரிப் பீரத்து அலர்வண்ணம்","மடவாள் கொள்ளக் கடவுள்வரைந்து","ஆர்இக் கொடுமை செய்தார்என்று","அன்னை அறியின் என்செய்கோ?"],"p":"","r":"The second stanza hails the shore where the sea smiles with pearls and opens coral lips before the fishers' net-yards, and asks what she will do if her mother, seeing the girl take the colour of the rain-season peerku flower, decides a god has done this cruelty.","n":"The pallor likened to peerku blossom is the sign of love-sickness, and the feared misreading (கடவுள் வரைந்து, settling it on a god) is the akam mother's mistake that leads to the frenzy rite; the stanza names the mistake without the rite. The phrase is compressed, and its sense as 'the mother fixing on a god as cause' is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","anangu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 222-229","lb":"SI7:222-229","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:230-237":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":101,"t":["புலவுற்று இரங்கி அதுநீங்கப்","பொழில்தண் டலையில் புகுந்துஉதிர்ந்த","கலவைச் செம்மல் மணம்கமழத்","திரைஉ லாவு கடல்சேர்ப்ப.","பலஉற்று ஒருநோய் திணியாத","படர்நோய் மடவாள் தனிஉழப்ப","அலவுற்று இரங்கி அறியாநோய்","அன்னை அறியின் என்செய்கோ?"],"p":"","r":"The third stanza hails the shore whose waves, grieving at their own fish smell, enter the cool grove and come away fragrant with fallen flowers, and asks what she will do if her mother learns of the unknown sickness the girl suffers alone, a longing that no single cause makes firm.","n":"The waves cure their own smell in the grove while the girl's sickness has no cure; பலஉற்று ஒருநோய் திணியாத suggests a sickness built of many pains that no one name fits, which is why the mother could not read it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 230-237","lb":"SI7:230-237","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:238-241":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":102,"t":["இளைஇருள் பரந்ததுவே எல்செய்வான் மறைந்தனனே","களைவுஅரும் புலம்புநீர் கண்பொழீஇ உகுத்தனவே","தளைஅவிழ் மலர்க்குழலாய் தணந்தார்நாட்டு உளதாம்கொல்","வளைநெகிழ எரிசிந்தி வந்தஇம் மருள்மாலை?"],"p":"","r":"The singer tells her companion, woman of loosened flower-hair, that young darkness has spread, the sun has hidden and her eyes pour unstoppable tears, and asks whether this bewildering evening, which came scattering fire and loosening her bangles, exists in the land of the man who left.","n":"The question is barbed: if evening came to his land too, he would feel what she feels and come back. The song is sung to a lover who sits beside her, and the absent man is a persona, which is the ambiguity Kovalan will hear as a confession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 238-241","lb":"SI7:238-241","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:242-245":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":103,"t":["கதிரவன் மறைந்தனனே கார்இருள் பரந்ததுவே","எதிர்மலர் புரைஉண்கண் எவ்வநீர் உகுத்தனவே","புதுமதி புரைமுகத்தாய் போனார்நாட்டு உளதாம்கொல்","மதிஉமிழ்ந்து கதிர்விழுங்கி வந்தஇம் மருள்மாலை?"],"p":"","r":"The second stanza repeats the complaint, the sun gone and dark cloud spreading, the eyes like opposed flowers shedding tears of pain, and asks the moon-faced companion whether this evening, which spat out the moon and swallowed the sun, is found in the land of the one who went.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 242-245","lb":"SI7:242-245","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:246-249":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":104,"t":["பறவைபாட்டு அடங்கினவே பகல்செய்வான் மறைந்தனனே","நிறைநிலா நோய்கூர நெடுங்கண்நீர் உகுத்தனவே","துறுமலர் அவிழ்குழலாய் துறந்தார்நாட்டு உளதாம்கொல்","மறவையாய் என்உயிர்மேல் வந்தஇம் மருள்மாலை?"],"p":"","r":"The third stanza adds that the birds' songs have stopped and the full moon sharpens her sickness, and asks whether this evening, which came like an enemy upon her life, exists in the land of the one who abandoned her.","n":"The verb for the man hardens across the three stanzas, from தணந்தார் (who went apart) to போனார் (who went) to துறந்தார் (who abandoned).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 246-249","lb":"SI7:246-249","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:250-253":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":105,"t":["கைதை வேலிக் கழிவாய் வந்துஎம்","பொய்தல் அழித்துப் போனார் ஒருவர்","பொய்தல் அழித்துப் போனார் அவர்நம்","மையல் மனம்விட்டு அகல்வார் அல்லர்."],"p":"","r":"In the first stanza of the saayal vari the singer tells her companion that a man came by the backwater fenced with screw-pine, spoiled their sand-play and went, and that he will not leave their infatuated heart.","n":"The repetition of the second line in the third is the form's refrain, and it turns the closing claim into a double meaning: he will not leave the heart, though he has left the place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kalaviyal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 250-253","lb":"SI7:250-253","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:254-257":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":106,"t":["கானல் வேலிக் கழிவாய் வந்து","நீநல்கு என்றே நின்றார் ஒருவர்","நீநல்கு என்றே நின்றார் அவர்நம்","மான்நேர் நோக்கம் மறப்பார் அல்லர்."],"p":"","r":"The second stanza says that a man came by the backwater fenced with the shore grove and stood saying 'grant me grace', and that he will not forget their deer-like glance.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kan","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 254-257","lb":"SI7:254-257","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:258-261":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":107,"t":["அன்னம் துணையோடு ஆடக் கண்டு","நென்னல் நோக்கி நின்றார் ஒருவர்","நென்னல் நோக்கி நின்றார் அவர்நம்","பொன்நேர் சுணங்கிற் போவார் அல்லர்."],"p":"","r":"The third stanza says that a man stood yesterday gazing as a swan played with its mate, and that he will not leave the golden freckles on her breast.","n":"The man who watched mated swans has learned the lesson of pairing, which is the stanza's hint that he will return.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 258-261","lb":"SI7:258-261","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:262-265":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":108,"t":["அடையல் குருகே அடையல்எம் கானல்","அடையல் குருகே அடையல்எம் கானல்","உடைதிரைநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பற்கு உறுநோய் உரையாய்","அடையல் குருகே அடையல்எம் கானல்."],"p":"","r":"The singer bids the heron not to settle in their shore grove, repeating the bidding, because it did not tell the lord of the breaking waves the sickness she suffers.","n":"The heron is refused for failing as a messenger; the doubled line 263 is the refrain of the form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 262-265","lb":"SI7:262-265","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:266-269":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":109,"t":["ஆங்கனம் பாடிய ஆயிழை பின்னரும்","காந்தள் மெல்விரல் கைக்கிளை சேர்குரல்","தீந்தொடைச் செவ்வழிப் பாலை இசைஎழீஇப்","பாங்கினில் பாடிஓர் பண்ணும் பெயர்த்தாள்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says that the woman of fine jewels, having sung thus, then raised on the yazh, with her soft kaanthal-like fingers, the sweet sevvazhi palai set on kaikkilai and kural, and sang by its rules, changing to another mode.","n":"The change of mode marks a change of mood: the songs that follow are evening laments in a mode of grief, and the narrator records it as a musician's choice before the reader learns what Kovalan makes of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 266-269","lb":"SI7:266-269","ev":"Madhavi finishes the shore songs and shifts the yazh to another mode for the evening songs.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:270-273":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":110,"t":["நுளையர் விளரி நொடிதரும்தீம் பாலை","இளிகிளையில் கொள்ள இறுத்தாயால் மாலை","இளிகிளையில் கொள்ள இறுத்தாய்மன் நீயேல்","கொளைவல்லாய் என்ஆவி கொள்வாழி மாலை."],"p":"","r":"The singer tells evening that it has struck the sweet palai the fisherfolk sing in vilari so that it settles in ili and kilai, and says that if it is so skilled in melody it may take her life.","n":"Evening is addressed as a fellow musician, and the musical terms (விளரி, இளி, கிளை) describe how the shore's own songs sound at dusk; how exactly the modes are meant to change is beyond what the lines settle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","isai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 270-273","lb":"SI7:270-273","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:274-277":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":111,"t":["பிரிந்தார் பரிந்துஉரைத்த பேர்அருளின் நீழல்","இருந்துஏங்கி வாழ்வார் உயிர்ப்புறத்தாய் மாலை","உயிர்ப்புறத்தாய் நீஆகில் உள்ஆற்றா வேந்தன்","எயில்புறத்து வேந்தனோடு என்ஆதி மாலை."],"p":"","r":"The singer tells evening that it stands outside the lives of those who live pining in the shade of the great grace the departed lover tenderly promised, and asks what it will be, so placed, if not a king outside the fort against a king within who cannot hold out.","n":"The image is a siege: the woman's life is a fort whose inner king, weakened, cannot resist the king camped outside its wall, and the promise of grace is her only shelter. The construction of the last line is compressed and the reading of it as a siege is offered as a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","uyir","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 274-277","lb":"SI7:274-277","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:278-281":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":112,"t":["பையுள்நோய் கூரப் பகல்செய்வான் போய்வீழ","வையமோ கண்புதைப்ப வந்தாய் மருள்மாலை","மாலைநீ ஆயின் மணந்தார் அவர்ஆயின்","ஞாலமோ நல்கூர்ந் ததுவாழி மாலை."],"p":"","r":"The singer says that as her grief grows and the sun goes down, bewildering evening comes and the world shuts its eyes, and that if this is what evening is and this is what the man who wed her is, the world itself has become poor.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 278-281","lb":"SI7:278-281","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி",""],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:282-285":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":113,"t":["தீத்துழை வந்தஇச் செல்லல் மருள்மாலை","தூக்காது துணிந்தஇத் துயர்எஞ்சு கிளவியால்","பூக்கமழ் கனலில் பொய்ச்சூள் பொறுக்க என்று","மாக்கடல் தெய்வம்நின் மலர்அடி வணங்குதும்."],"p":"","r":"The singer, in the last song, says that with these words of grief spoken without weighing them in this bewildering evening that came like fire, she bows at the flower feet of the god of the great sea and asks it to bear the false oath sworn in the fragrant grove.","n":"The plea presumes that the sea god punishes a false oath sworn before it, and the heroine asks it to hold its hand: she shields the man who lied. Line 284 reads கனலில் (in the fire); the sense of the grove (கானல்) suits the song cycle, and the text may be faulty here. Whose false oath is meant is not said, which is the opening for Kovalan's reading of the song as Madhavi's own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sool","vaaimai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 282-285","lb":"SI7:282-285","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி","மாக்கடல் தெய்வம்"],"ch":"si:10"},"si:7:286-300":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":114,"t":["எனக்கேட்டு,","கானல்வரி யான்பாடத் தான்ஒன்றின்மேல் மனம்வைத்து","மாயப்பொய் பலகூட்டும் மாயத்தாள் பாடினாள்என","யாழ்இசைமேல் வைத்துத்தன் ஊழ்வினைவந்து உருத்ததுஆகலின்","உவவுஉற்ற திங்கள்முகத்தாளைக் கவவுக்கை ஞெகிழ்ந்தனனாய்ப்","பொழுதுஈங்குக் கழிந்ததுஆகலின் எழுதும்என்று உடன்எழாது","ஏவலாளர் உடஞ்சூழக் கோவலன்தான் போனபின்னர்,","தாதுஅவிழ் மலர்ச்சோலை ஓதைஆயத்து ஒலிஅவித்துக்","கையற்ற நெஞ்சினளாய் வையத்தி னுள்புக்குக்","காதலனுடன் அன்றியே மாதவிதன் மனைபுக்காள்","ஆங்கு,","மாயிரு ஞாலத்து அரசு தலைவணக்கும்","சூழி யானைச் சுடர்வாள் செம்பியன்","மாலை வெண்குடை கவிப்ப","ஆழி மால்வரை அகவையா எனவே."],"p":"","r":"The narrator says that Kovalan, hearing this, thought that while he had sung the shore songs she, her mind on another, had sung as a woman of lying tricks, and because his former deed had come in wrath through the yazh music he loosened his embrace and, saying the time had passed, left with his servants while she stayed seated, and Madhavi, her heart helpless, quieted her company in the flower grove and went home in her carriage without her lover, while the Chola's garlanded white umbrella shaded the earth.","n":"Two causes are given in one sentence: Kovalan's own judgement of the song (lines 287 to 288, in his voice) and the narrator's ஊழ்வினை வந்து உருத்தது (line 289), the patikam's own phrase for karma. The narrator does not say Madhavi had another in mind; the hidden intent exists only in Kovalan's reading. The closing four lines, opened by ஆங்கு, end the kaathai with a blessing on the Chola king and are not part of the scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","pirivu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 7, lines 286-300","lb":"SI7:286-300","ev":"Kovalan, deciding Madhavi's songs betray love for another, leaves her on the shore, and she goes home alone.","cl":[["a former deed comes in wrath and turns a man against the woman he loves","general","asserted",289,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோவலன்","மாதவி","ஏவலாளர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:10"},"si:8:1-13":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":115,"t":["நெடியோன் குன்றமும் தொடியோள் பெளவமும்","தமிழ்வரம்பு அறுத்த தண்புனல் நல்நாட்டு","மாட மதுரையும் பீடுஆர் உறந்தையும்","கலிகெழு வஞ்சியும் ஒலிபுனல் புகாரும்","அரைசுவீற் றிருந்த உரைசால் சிறப்பின்","மன்னன் மாரன் மகிழ்துணை ஆகிய","இன்இள வேனில் வந்தனன் இவண்என","வளம்கெழு பொதியில் மாமுனி பயந்த","இளங்கால் தூதன் இசைத்தனன் ஆதலின்","மகர வெல்கொடி மைந்தன் சேனை","புகர்அறு கோலம் கொள்ளும்என் பதுபோல்","கொடிமிடை சோலைக் குயிலோன் என்னும்","படையுள் படுவோன் பணிமொழி கூற,"],"p":"","r":"The young southern breeze, messenger born of the great sage of fertile Pothiyil, announces that sweet young spring, the delighting companion of king Maran, has come to the cool land whose Tamil bounds are cut by the hill of the tall god and the sea of the bangled goddess, where kings sit in Madurai, Uraiyur, Vanji and Puhar; and as if the army of the makara-bannered youth were putting on its flawless array, the cuckoo, a soldier in that army, calls the command through the crowded groves.","n":"Spring arrives as a king's affair: a herald (தூதன்), an army (சேனை, படை), an order spoken by a soldier (பணிமொழி). The setting is the whole Tamil land, named by its two bounds and the capitals of all three crowns, not Puhar alone, so the season that will break Kovalan and Madhavi is announced as a law over every Tamil city. The breeze is 'born of the sage' of Pothiyil because the south wind blows from that hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilavenil","kaamam","thoothu","thamizh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 8, lines 1-13","lb":"SI8:1-13","ev":"The southern breeze announces the coming of spring and the cuckoo calls in the groves.","cl":[],"ag":["இளங்கால் தூதன்","குயிலோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:11"},"si:8:14-24":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":116,"t":["மடல்அவிழ் கானல் கடல்விளை யாட்டினுள்","கோவலன் ஊடக் கூடாது ஏகிய","மாமலர் நெடுங்கண் மாதவி விரும்பி","வான்உற நிவந்த மேல்நிலை மருங்கின்","வேனில் பள்ளி ஏறி மாண்இழை","தென்கடல் முத்தும் தென்மலைச் சாந்தும்","தன்கடன் இறுக்கும் தன்மைய ஆதலின்","கொங்கை முன்றில் குங்கும வளாகத்து","மைஅறு சிறப்பின் கையுறை ஏந்தி","அதிரா மரபின் யாழ்கை வாங்கி","மதுர கீதம் பாடினள் மயங்கி,"],"p":"","r":"Madhavi, whom Kovalan left without joining her after he sulked at the sea-sport on the flowering shore, goes in her longing up to the spring chamber on the high terrace, takes the court of saffron on her breasts, where the southern sea's pearls and the southern hill's sandal pay their dues, as her offering, takes up the yaazh of steady tradition and, lost in feeling, sings a sweet song.","n":"Line 15 gives Kovalan's leaving at the end of the Kaanal Vari as a sulk (ஊட) that would not end in union (கூடாது), a lover's quarrel; the narrator there (7:289) gave it as Kovalan's ஊழ்வினை rising to bear fruit. The epic so names two causes for one departure, fate and pique, and neither cancels the other. The pearls and sandal paying tribute (கடன் இறுக்கும்) to her breasts carry on the opening unit's language of kings and dues.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","pirivu","isai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 8, lines 14-24","lb":"SI8:14-24","ev":"Left by the sulking Kovalan, Madhavi climbs to her spring chamber and sings to the yaazh.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:11"},"si:8:25-44":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":117,"t":["ஒன்பான் விருத்தியுள் தலைக்கண் விருத்தி","நன்பால் அமைந்த இருக்கையள் ஆகி,","வலக்கைப் பதாகை கோட்டொடு சேர்த்தி","இடக்கை நால்விரல் மாடகம் தழீஇச்","செம்பகை ஆர்ப்பே கூடம் அதிர்வே","வெம்பகை நீக்கும் விரகுளி அறிந்து,","பிழையா மரபின் ஈர்ஏழ் கோவையை","உழைமுதல் கைக்கிளை இறுவாய் கட்டி,","இணைகிளை பகைநட்பு என்றுஇந் நான்கின்","இசைபுணர் குறிநிலை எய்த நோக்கிக்","குரல்வாய் இளிவாய்க் கேட்டனள் அன்றியும்","வரன்முறை மருங்கின் ஐந்தினும் ஏழினும்","உழைமுதல் ஆகவும் உழைஈறு ஆகவும்","குரல்முதல் ஆகவும் குரல்ஈறு ஆகவும்","அகநிலை மருதமும் புறநிலை மருதமும்","அருகியல் மருதமும் பெருகியல் மருதமும்","நால்வகைச் சாதியும் நலம்பெற நோக்கி,","மூவகை இயக்கமும் முறையுளிக் கழிப்பித்","திறத்து வழிப்படூஉம் தெள்ளிசைக் கரணத்துப்","புறத்துஒரு பாணியில் பூங்கொடி மயங்கி,"],"p":"","r":"Seated in the first of the nine postures, her right hand set to the yaazh's neck and four fingers of her left on the pegs, Madhavi, knowing the skill that removes the string faults named by the treatise, ties the fourteen strings from ulai to kaikkilai, tests the four relations of notes, listens to kural and ili, plays the scales of five and seven beginning and ending on ulai and on kural, the four kinds of marutham and the three movements, until in one outer measure the flowering creeper herself is lost in it.","n":"The catalogue is a performer's checklist, the same craft the yaazh player of 3:70-94 is praised for, now done alone by Madhavi for no audience. The modes she plays are the marutham ones; marutham is in Tamil poetics the landscape of the lovers' quarrel, so the music fits her situation without the poem saying so. All this control ends in மயங்கி: mastery tips into self-loss. Many terms (இணை கிளை பகை நட்பு, மூவகை இயக்கம், புறத்துஒரு பாணி) are technical and I read them only in outline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["isai","mayal","alavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 8, lines 25-44","lb":"SI8:25-44","ev":"Madhavi tunes the yaazh, tests its notes and plays the marutham modes until she loses herself in the music.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:11"},"si:8:45-55":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":118,"t":["சண்பகம் மாதவி தமாலம் கருமுகை","வெண்பூ மல்லிகை வேரொடு மிடைந்த","அம்செங் கழுநீர் ஆய்இதழ்க் கத்திகை","எதிர்ப்பூஞ் செவ்வி இடைநிலத்து யாத்த","முதிர்பூந் தாழை முடங்கல்வெண் தோட்டு","விரைமலர் வாளியின் வியன்நிலம் ஆண்ட","ஒருதனிச் செங்கோல் ஒருமகன் ஆணையின்","ஒருமுகம் அன்றி உலகுதொழுது இறைஞ்சும்","திருமுகம் போக்கும் செவ்வியள் ஆகி,","அலத்தகக் கொழுஞ்சேறு அளைஇ அயலது","பித்திகைக் கொழுமுகை ஆணி கைக்கொண்டு,"],"p":"","r":"Bound up with champak, madhavi, tamaalam, karumugai, white jasmine with vetiver, red kazhuneer and kattigai flowers, Madhavi takes the curved white leaf of a ripe thaazhai, and, ready to send the royal letter that the whole world bows to by order of the one man whose single sceptre rules the earth with fragrant flower-arrows, she dips into the thick red lac and takes a pithigai bud as her stylus.","n":"The letter is called திருமுகம், the word for a king's writ, and Madhavi writes it 'by the order' (ஆணையின்) of Kama's 'single sceptre' (ஒருதனிச் செங்கோல்). The figure is love as sovereign, not a claim about kingship; but the epic that will turn on a bent sceptre in Madurai here gives a sceptre to desire, whose order Kovalan is about to refuse. Her own name, மாதவி, stands in the flower list at line 45.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thoothu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 8, lines 45-55","lb":"SI8:45-55","ev":"Madhavi takes a thaazhai leaf, red lac and a jasmine bud to write a letter to Kovalan.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:11"},"si:8:56-73":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":119,"t":["மன்உயிர் எல்லாம் மகிழ்துணை புணர்க்கும்","இன்இள வேனில் இளவர சாளன்","அந்திப் போதகத்து அரும்பிடர்த் தோன்றிய","திங்கள் செல்வனும் செவ்வியன் அல்லன்","புணர்ந்த மாக்கள் பொழுதுஇடைப் படுப்பினும்","தணந்த மாக்கள் தம்துணை மறப்பினும்","நறும்பூ வாளியின் நல்உயிர் கோடல்","இறும்பூது அன்றுஅஃது அறிந்தீ மின்என","எண்எண் கலையும் இசைந்துஉடன் போக","பண்ணும் திறனும் புறங்கூறு நாவின்","தளைவாய் அவிழ்ந்த தனிப்படு காமத்து","விளையா மழலையின் விரித்துஉரை எழுதி,","பசந்த மேனியள் படர்உறு மாலையின்","வசந்த மாலையை வருகெனக் கூஉய்த்","தூமலர் மாலையின் துணிபொருள் எல்லாம்","கோவலற்கு அளித்துக் கொணர்க ஈங்குஎன","மாலை வாங்கிய வேல்அரி நெடுங்கண்","கூல மறுகிற் கோவலற்கு அளிப்ப,"],"p":"","r":"Madhavi writes that young spring, the prince who joins every living being to its mate, and the moon born in the bud of evening are not kind: if joined lovers let time come between them, or parted lovers forget their partners, it is no wonder that love takes their lives with flower-arrows, so let them know it; she writes this with all sixty-four arts at her command yet in the unformed lisp of a lonely desire, then, pale with longing, calls Vasantamalai and bids her give Kovalan the flower garland with all its meaning and bring him back, and Vasantamalai gives it to him in the grain-market street.","n":"The letter is a warning in the plural and impersonal, not a plea: it never names Kovalan or herself, and puts the blame for delay on the lovers who let time divide them. Line 68 plays three ways on மாலை: the evening of her pain, Vasanta-maalai her messenger, and the flower maalai that carries the writing. Kovalan is found not at home but in the grain-market street.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","thoothu","pasalai","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 8, lines 56-73","lb":"SI8:56-73","ev":"Madhavi writes her letter on the garland and sends Vasantamalai, who gives it to Kovalan in the grain-market street.","cl":[["lovers who let time divide them, or who forget their partners when apart, deserve to have love take their lives","general","reported",60,"madhavi"]],"ag":["மாதவி","வசந்த மாலை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதவி","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:11"},"si:8:74-93":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":120,"t":["திலகமும் அளகமும் சிறுகருஞ் சிலையும்","குவளையும் குமிழும் கொவ்வையும் கொண்ட","மாதர் வாள்முகத்து மதைஇய நோக்கமொடு","காதலின் தோன்றிய கண்கூடு வரியும்,","புயல்சுமந்து வருந்திப் பொழிகதிர் மதியத்துக்","கயல்உலாய்த் திரிதரும் காமர் செவ்வியின்","பாகுபொதி பவளம் திறந்துநிலா உதவிய","நாகுஇள முத்தின் நகைநிலம் காட்டி","வருகென வந்து போகெனப் போகிய","கருநெடுங் கண்ணி காண்வரிக் கோலமும்,","அந்தி மாலை வந்ததற்கு இரங்கிச்","சிந்தை நோய் கூரும்என் சிறுமை நோக்கிக்","கிளிபுரை கிளவியும் மடஅன நடையும்","களிமயில் சாயலும் கரந்தனள் ஆகிச்","செருவேல் நெடுங்கண் சிலதியர் கோலத்து","ஒருதனி வந்த உள்வரி ஆடலும்,","சிலம்புவாய் புலம்பவும் மேகலை ஆர்ப்பவும்","கலம்பெறா நுசுப்பினள் காதல் நோக்கமொடு","திறத்துவேறு ஆயஎன் சிறுமை நோக்கியும்","புறத்துநின்று ஆடிய புன்புற வரியும்,"],"p":"","r":"Kovalan answers by naming, one by one, the love-plays Madhavi has shown him: the glance of eyes meeting in love from a face like a sword; the play of coming when called and going when sent, showing her pearl smile; the inner play when, seeing his distress at evening, she hid her parrot speech, swan gait and peacock grace and came alone dressed as a serving maid; and the outer play when she danced outside with anklets wailing and girdle ringing, looking lovingly at his changed and wretched state.","n":"Each memory is given a technical name ending in வரி (கண்கூடு வரி, காண்வரிக் கோலம், உள்வரி, புன்புற வரி), the names of acted love in the dance treatise. Kovalan is recalling real tenderness, her coming to him disguised as a maid when he was low, but by filing each act under a genre of performance he turns their life together into a repertoire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","koothu","kurippu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 8, lines 74-93","lb":"SI8:74-93","ev":"Kovalan answers Vasantamalai by listing Madhavi's past shows of love as dance roles.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","வசந்த மாலை"],"ch":"si:11"},"si:8:94-110":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":121,"t":["கோதையும் குழலும் தாதுசேர் அளகமும்","ஒருகாழ் முத்தமும் திருமுலைத் தடமும்","மின்இடை வருத்த நன்னுதல் தோன்றிச்","சிறுகுறுந் தொழிலியர் மறுமொழி உய்ப்பப்","புணர்ச்சிஉட் பொதிந்த கலாம்தரு கிளவியின்","இருபுற மொழிப்பொருள் கேட்டனள் ஆகித்","தளர்ந்த சாயல் தகைமென் கூந்தல்","கிளர்ந்துவேறு ஆகிய கிளர்வரிக் கோலமும்,","பிரிந்துஉறை காலத்துப் பரிந்தனள் ஆகி","என்உறு கிளைகட்குத் தன்உறு துயரம்","தேர்ந்துதேர்ந்து உரைத்த தேர்ச்சிவரி அன்றியும்,","வண்டுஅலர் கோதை மாலையுள் மயங்கிக்","கண்டவர்க்கு உரைத்த காட்சி வரியும்,","அடுத்துஅடுத்து அவர்முன் மயங்கிய மயக்கமும்","எடுத்துஅவர் தீர்த்த எடுத்துக்கோள் வரியும்,","ஆடல் மகளே ஆதலின் ஆயிழை.","பாடுபெற் றனஅப் பைந்தொடி தனக்குஎன,"],"p":"","r":"Kovalan goes on: the stirring play when, hearing her maids carry back his replies full of quarrel-making double meanings hidden within union, her grace drooped and her soft hair stirred; the play of careful telling when, parted from him, she told her own grief to his kin; the play of the dazed lover who told her state to whoever saw her; and the play of the lifted swoon when she fainted again and again before them and they raised her: since she is a dancing woman, he says, all of these were fitting achievements for her.","n":"Lines 109 and 110 are the verdict, and the verdict is a refusal (the next unit calls it மறுத்தது): ஆடல் மகள் names her by her calling and makes the calling the proof that her grief was art. The narrator had blamed the first break on Kovalan's ஊழ்வினை (7:289); this second break is in Kovalan's own words and names no fate, so the story lays the choice on him too. The judgment is answered later by Kovalan himself, who reads her second letter and says she is without fault and the fault is his (13:93-95).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","koothu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 8, lines 94-110","lb":"SI8:94-110","ev":"Kovalan refuses the letter, saying every sign of Madhavi's love was a role that suits a dancing woman.","cl":[["a dancing woman's signs of love are performances of her art, not true feeling","general","refuted",109,"kovalan"]],"ag":["கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","வசந்த மாலை"],"ch":"si:11"},"si:8:111-118":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":122,"t":["அணித்தோட்டுத் திருமுகத்து ஆயிழை எழுதிய,","மணித்தோட்டுத் திருமுகம் மறுத்ததற்கு இரங்கி","வாடிய உள்ளத்து வசந்த மாலை","தோடுஅலர் கோதைக்குத் துனைந்துசென்று உரைப்ப","மாலை வாரார் ஆயினும் மாண்இழை.","காலைகாண் குவம்எனக் கையறு நெஞ்சமொடு","பூமலர் அமளிமிசைப் பொருந்தாது வதிந்தனள்","மாமலர் நெடுங்கண் மாதவி தான்என்."],"p":"","r":"Grieving that the jewelled letter written on an ornamented leaf by the jewelled woman was refused, Vasantamalai, her heart wilted, hurries back and tells Madhavi, who says that though he will not come this evening they will see him in the morning, and lies on her flowered bed with a helpless heart, unable to rest.","n":"Her last words set evening against morning (மாலை ... காலை), and the morning she waits for does not bring him: the next kaathai has Kovalan at Kannaki's door. The 'helpless heart' (கையறு நெஞ்சம்) is a phrase her second letter will ask him to take away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 8, lines 111-118","lb":"SI8:111-118","ev":"Vasantamalai reports the refusal, and Madhavi lies sleepless, hoping to see Kovalan in the morning.","cl":[],"ag":["வசந்த மாலை","மாதவி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:11"},"si:8:119-126":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":123,"t":["செந்தா மரைவிரியத் தேமாங் கொழுந்துஒழுக","மைந்தார் அசோகம் மடல்அவிழக் - கொந்தார்","இளவேனில் வந்ததால் என்ஆம்கொல் இன்று","வளவேல்நற் கண்ணி மனம்.","ஊடினீர் எல்லாம் உருஇலான் தன்ஆணை","கூடுமின் என்று குயில்சாற்ற - நீடிய","வேனற்பா ணிக்கலந்தாள் மென்பூந் திருமுகத்தைக்","கானற்பா ணிக்குஅலந்தாய் காண்."],"p":"","r":"Two closing venbas: the first says that with the red lotus opening, mango shoots trickling and the strong ashoka unfolding its buds, clustered young spring has come, and asks what will become today of the heart of the woman with spear-bright eyes; the second says that the cuckoo has proclaimed the formless one's command, 'all you who have sulked, unite', and bids the one who, troubled by the shore song, spurned the soft flower letter of her who joined in the long spring song, to see it.","n":"The second venba sets two songs against each other: the கானல் பாணி, the shore songs of 7 that started the quarrel, and the வேனல் பாணி, the spring song in which Madhavi wrote. I read அலந்தாய் as addressed to Kovalan, who refused the letter, but the line is compressed and the addressee is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilavenil","oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 8, lines 119-126","lb":"SI8:119-126","ev":"","cl":[["all lovers who have quarrelled must reunite, for that is the love god's command","general","reported",123,"kuyilon"]],"ag":["குயிலோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:11"},"si:9:1-28":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":124,"t":["அகநகர் எல்லாம் அரும்புஅவிழ் முல்லை","நிகர்மலர் நெல்லொடு தூஉய்ப் பகல்மாய்ந்த","மாலை மணிவிளக்கம் காட்டி இரவிற்குஓர்","கோலம் கொடிஇடையார் தாம்கொள்ள, மேல்ஓர்நாள்:","மாலதி மாற்றாள் மகவுக்குப் பால்அளிக்கப்","பால்விக்கிப் பாலகன் தான்சோர மாலதியும்","பார்ப்பா னொடுமனையாள் என்மேல் படாதனவிட்டு","ஏற்பன கூறார்என்று ஏங்கி மகக்கொண்டு","அமரர் தருக்கோட்டம் வெள்யானைக் கோட்டம்","புகர்வெள்ளை நாகர்தம் கோட்டம் பகல்வாயில்","உச்சிக் கிழான்கோட்டம் ஊர்க்கோட்டம் வேல்கோட்டம்","வச்சிரக் கோட்டம் புறம்பணையான் வாழ்கோட்டம்","நிக்கந்தக் கோட்டம் நிலாக்கோட்டம் புக்குஎங்கும்","தேவிர்காள் எம்உறுநோய் தீர்ம்அன்று மேவிஓர்","பாசண்டச் சாத்தற்குப் பாடு கிடந்தாளுக்கு,","ஏசும் படிஓர் இளங்கொடியாய் ஆசுஇலாய்","செய்தவம் இல்லோர்க்குத் தேவர் வரம்கொடார்","பொய்உரையே அன்று பொருள்உரையே கையிற்","படுபிணம்தா என்று பறித்துஅவள்கைக் கொண்டு","சுடுகாட்டுக் கோட்டத்துத் தூங்குஇருளில் சென்றுஆங்கு","இடுபிணம் தின்னும் இடாகினிப்பேய் வாங்கி","மடியகத்து இட்டாள் மகவை, இடியுண்ட","மஞ்ஞைபோல் ஏங்கி அழுதாளுக்கு அச்சாத்தன்","அஞ்ஞைநீ ஏங்கி அழல்என்று முன்னை","உயிர்க்குழவி காணாய்என்று அக்குழவி யாய்ஓர்","குயில்பொதும்பர் நீழல் குறுக அயிர்ப்புஇன்றி","மாயக் குழவி எடுத்து மடித்திரைத்துத்","தாய்கைக் கொடுத்தாள்அத் தையலாள், தூய"],"p":"","r":"While the women of the inner city scatter jasmine buds and paddy and show the lamps at dusk, the poet goes back to an earlier day: Maalathi, whose co-wife's baby choked on the milk she gave it, fearing that the Brahmin and his wife would blame her unjustly, carried the child round every shrine of the city begging the gods to cure her grief and lay prostrate before Pasanda Saathan, who came as a young woman, told her that gods grant no boon to those without tapas, took the body to the burning-ground shrine where the corpse-eating demoness swallowed it, then, as she wept, bade her see her child alive and, becoming that child in a koel-haunted grove, put the magic child in its mother's hands.","n":"The catalogue of lines 9 to 13 takes Maalathi through the shrines of Indra's tree, the white elephant, the white Naga, the sun, the city, Vel, the thunderbolt, the god outside the town, the Nigantha (Jain) shrine and the moon: the city's gods stand side by side with no school set above another, and the one who answers is the god of the sects (பாசண்டம்). Saathan's rule (line 17) is stated and then exceeded by his own act: Maalathi has no tapas, he says, yet he gives her back a child, by becoming it. Her fear is of a false charge (படாதன, line 7), the same shape as the false word in Kannaki's dream later in this kaathai. Lines 1 to 4 belong to the frame of the whole kaathai: the tale of Saathan is told as a digression hung on மேல்ஓர்நாள்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vazhipaadu","arul","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 9, lines 1-28","lb":"SI9:1-28","ev":"Maalathi carries her co-wife's dead baby to every shrine; Saathan, as a young woman, gives the body to the burning-ground demoness and then returns to her as the child himself.","cl":[["the gods grant no boon to those who have done no tapas","general","asserted",17,"paasanda_saathan"]],"ag":["மாலதி","பாசண்டச் சாத்தன்","இடாகினிப்பேய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:12"},"si:9:29-44":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":125,"t":["மறையோன்பின் மாணியாய் வான்பொருள் கேள்வித்","துறைபோய் அவர்முடிந்த பின்னர் இறையோனும்","தாயத்தா ரோடும் வழக்குஉரைத்துத் தந்தைக்கும்","தாயர்க்கும் வேண்டும் கடன்கழித்து மேயநாள்","தேவந்தி என்பாள் மனைவி அவளுக்குப்","பூவந்த உண்கண் பொறுக்கென்று மேவித்தன்","மூவா இளநலம் காட்டிஎம் கோட்டத்து","நீவா எனஉரைத்து நீங்குதலும், தூமொழி","ஆர்த்த கணவன் அகன்றனன் போய்எங்கும்","தீர்த்தத் துறைபடிவேன் என்றுஅவனைப் பேர்த்துஇங்ஙன்","மீட்டுத் தருவாய் எனஒன்றன் மேல்இட்டுக்","கோட்டம் வழிபாடு கொண்டிருப்பாள் வாட்டருஞ்சீர்க்","கண்ணகி நல்லாளுக்கு உற்ற குறைஉண்டுஎன்று","எண்ணிய நெஞ்சத்து இனையளாய் நண்ணி","அறுகு சிறுபூளை நெல்லொடு தூஉய்ச் சென்று","பெறுக கணவனோடு என்றாள், பெறுகேன்"],"p":"","r":"The child grew up a Brahmin student, mastered the learning of the scriptures, and after the death of his foster-parents settled the inheritance with the kin and paid the rites owed to father and mothers; he married Devanthi, showed her his own ageless youth, told her to come to his shrine, and left; Devanthi, telling people her husband had gone to bathe at every holy ford and making that her pretext, keeps worship at the shrine to win him back, and now, sensing that Kannaki has a lack, comes scattering arugu, poolai flowers and paddy and blesses her to have her husband.","n":"Devanthi is a wife whose husband is a god, and she worships that god to have the husband back: the rival of Kannaki's way, where the husband is the only god (see the link to Kural 55). Her public story, that he has gone on pilgrimage to the fords (line 38), is a cover she lays on something (ஒன்றன் மேல் இட்டு, line 39), and the blessing she brings is the household rite of arugu and paddy, which Kannaki answers with a dream rather than thanks. That the god does the funeral and inheritance duties of a son (lines 30 to 32) makes his human life complete before he leaves it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","theerththam","pirivu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 9, lines 29-44","lb":"SI9:29-44","ev":"Saathan, grown as a Brahmin, marries Devanthi, reveals himself and leaves; Devanthi worships at his shrine and comes to bless Kannaki.","cl":[],"ag":["பாசண்டச் சாத்தன்","தேவந்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:12"},"si:9:45-54":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":126,"t":["கடுக்கும்என் நெஞ்சம் கனவினால் என்கை","பிடித்தனன் போய்ஓர் பெரும்பதியுள் பட்டேம்","பட்ட பதியில் படாதது ஒருவார்த்தை","இட்டனர் ஊரார் இடுதேள்இட்டு என்தன்மேல்","கோவலற்கு உற்றதுஓர் தீங்குஎன்று அதுகேட்டுக்","காவலன் முன்னர்யான் கட்டுரைத்தேன் காவலனோடு","ஊர்க்குஉற்ற தீங்கும்ஒன்று உண்டால் உரையாடேன்","தீக்குற்றம் போலும் செறிதொடீஇ தீக்குற்றம்","உற்றேனோடு உற்ற உறுவனொடு யான்உற்ற","நல்திறம் கேட்கின் நகைஆகும், பொற்றொடிஇ"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki answers that she will have him, but that her heart is shaking from a dream: he took her hand, they went into a great city, the people there laid on him a word that did not belong to him, as one drops a scorpion, a harm befell Kovalan, she made a plea before the king, and with the king there was a harm to the city too, of which she will not speak; it is like an evil omen, she says, and if her friend heard the good that came to her with the one she had joined, she would laugh.","n":"The dream is the poem's plot in small, told before the journey begins: city, false charge, harm to Kovalan, her plea before the king, the city's harm, and a last good that sounds absurd. She refuses to tell the city's harm (உரையாடேன், line 51), and the final good is told only as something that would be laughed at; the poem's later units show both. The scorpion image (இடுதேள், line 48) makes the charge something thrown on, not earned. Lines 51 to 54 are dense, and which of the repeated உற்ற forms refers to Kovalan and which to the 'harm' is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","pazhi","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 9, lines 45-54","lb":"SI9:45-54","ev":"Kannaki tells Devanthi a dream of a false charge on Kovalan in a great city, her plea before the king and harm to the city.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","தேவந்தி"],"ch":"si:12"},"si:9:55-66":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":127,"t":["கைத்தாயும் அல்லை கணவற்கு ஒருநோன்பு","பொய்த்தாய் பழம்பிறப்பில் போய்க்கெடுக உய்த்துக்","கடலொடு காவிரி சென்றுஅலைக்கும் முன்றில்","மடல்அவிழ் நெய்தல்அம் கானல் தடம்உள","சோமகுண்டம் சூரிய குண்டம் துறைமூழ்கிக்","காமவேள் கோட்டம் தொழுதார் கணவரொடு","தாம்இன் புறுவர் உலகத்துத் தையலார்","போகம்செய் பூமியினும் போய்ப்பிறப்பர் யாம்ஒருநாள்","ஆடுதும் என்ற அணிஇழைக்குஅவ் ஆய்இழையாள்","பீடுஅன்று எனஇருந்த பின்னரே, நீடிய","காவலன் போலும் கடைத்தலையான் வந்துநம்","கோவலன் என்றாள்ஓர் குற்றிளையாள், கோவலனும்"],"p":"","r":"Devanthi answers that Kannaki is not one who has failed her husband in this life, but in a former birth she broke a vow to him, and bids that fault be gone; on the shore grove where the sea and the Kaveri meet there are two tanks, the Moon's and the Sun's, and women who bathe there and worship at the shrine of Kama enjoy their husbands in this world and are born again in the land of enjoyment, so let them bathe one day; Kannaki says it is not worthy and sits on, and then a young serving woman announces that Kovalan has come to the gate.","n":"Devanthi gives a cause for Kannaki's lack, a vow broken in a former birth, and the remedy of a rite; the poem later gives another cause, a curse on Kovalan's own former birth told by the goddess of Madurai (Silappathikaram 23), and does not take up Devanthi's. Kannaki's பீடு அன்று (line 64) refuses the rite for herself without disputing its power: she does not say the tanks fail, only that seeking a husband through Kama's shrine is beneath her, which the poem's later venba turns into a wife whom the gods worship. போகம்செய் பூமி is a land of enjoyment where the next birth is spent; the term is at home in Jain cosmology, but the unit does not tie it to a school. Line 55 (கைத்தாயும் அல்லை) is obscure; I read it as 'you have not wronged him (in this life)', and other readings are possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nonbu","karpu","piravi","theerththam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 9, lines 55-66","lb":"SI9:55-66","ev":"Devanthi urges Kannaki to bathe in the shore tanks and worship at Kama's shrine; Kannaki refuses, and a maid announces Kovalan's return.","cl":[["a vow to her husband broken in a former birth causes a wife's present lack","general","reported",56,"devanthi"],["women who bathe in the Soma and Surya tanks and worship at Kama's shrine enjoy their husbands here and are reborn in the land of enjoyment","general","reported",61,"devanthi"]],"ag":["தேவந்தி","கண்ணகி","குற்றிளையாள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவந்தி","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:12"},"si:9:67-79":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":128,"t":["பாடுஅமை சேக்கையுள் புக்குத்தன் பைந்தொடி","வாடிய மேனி வருத்தம்கண்டு, யாவும்","சலம்புணர் கொள்கைச் சலதியொடு ஆடிக்","குலம்தரு வான்பொருள் குன்றம் தொலைந்த","இலம்பாடு நாணுத் தரும்எனக்கு என்ன,","நலம்கேழ் முறுவல் நகைமுகம் காட்டிச்","சிலம்புஉள கொண்மின் எனச்சேயிழை கேள்இச்","சிலம்பு முதலாகச் சென்ற கலனொடு","உலந்தபொருள் ஈட்டுதல் உற்றேன் மலர்ந்தசீர்","மாட மதுரை யகத்துச்சென்று என்னோடுஇங்கு","ஏடுஅலர் கோதாய். எழுகென்று நீடி","வினைகடைக் கூட்ட வியம்கொண்டான் கங்குல்","கனைசுடர் கால்சீயா முன்."],"p":"","r":"Kovalan enters the bedchamber, sees her wasted body and her pain, and says that having sported with a deceiver of deceitful ways, the poverty of losing the mountain of wealth his family gave him shames him; with a bright smile she offers the anklets, and he answers that with the anklets as his beginning he will earn back the wealth that is gone, and bids her rise and go with him to Madurai; driven by the deed at its term, he sets out before the night's dark is cleared.","n":"Kovalan blames Madhavi (சலதி, line 69) and names his own feeling as shame at poverty, not remorse toward Kannaki; she answers with no word of reproach, only the anklets. His plan is an ordinary remedy, to trade and recover; the narrator's last line gives the plan another author, the deed that drives him to its term (வினைகடைக் கூட்ட, line 78). The anklet enters the story here as capital (சிலம்பு முதலாக, line 74), the thing that will become the charge in the dream.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","varaivinmagalir","vinai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 9, lines 67-79","lb":"SI9:67-79","ev":"Kovalan returns ashamed of his lost wealth, takes Kannaki's offered anklets as capital and leaves with her for Madurai before dawn.","cl":[["a past deed at its term drives a man to the place of its fruit","general","asserted",78,"narrator"],["losing inherited wealth to a deceitful courtesan brings a man shame","general","reported",71,"kovalan"]],"ag":["கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:12"},"si:9:80-83":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":129,"t":["காதலி கண்ட கனவு கருநெடுங்கண்","மாதவிதன் சொல்லை வறிதாக்க - மூதை","வினைகடைக் கூட்ட வியம்கொண்டான் கங்குல்","கனைசுடர் கால்சீயா முன்."],"p":"","r":"The closing venba says that, as the dream his beloved saw and Madhavi's word came to nothing, the deed of old driving him to its end, he set out before the night's dark was cleared.","n":"The venba puts the old deed (மூதை வினை) where the kaathai's last line had only வினை. What 'made Madhavi's word empty' means is not certain: it may be Madhavi's word sent to Kovalan in the preceding kaathai, now come to nothing because he has left with Kannaki; the syntax of காதலி கண்ட கனவு beside it is also open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 9, lines 80-83","lb":"SI9:80-83","ev":"Kovalan sets out from Puhar before dawn.","cl":[["an old deed drives its doer to his end","general","asserted",82,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:12"},"si:10:1-14":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":130,"t":["வான்கண் விழியா வைகறை யாமத்து","மீன்திகழ் விசும்பின் வெண்மதி நீங்கக்","கார்இருள் நின்ற கடைநாள் கங்குல்","ஊழ்வினைக் கடைஇ உள்ளம் துரப்ப","ஏழகத் தகரும் எகினக் கவரியும்","தூமயிர் அன்னமும் துணைஎனத் திரியும்","தாளொடு குயின்ற தகைசால் சிறப்பின்","நீள்நெடு வாயில் நெடுங்கடை கழிந்துஆங்கு,","அணிகிளர் அரவின் அறிதுயில் அமர்ந்த","மணிவண்ணன் கோட்டம் வலம்செயாக் கழிந்து,","பணைஐந்து ஓங்கிய பாசிலைப் போதி","அணிதிகழ் நீழல் அறவோன் திருமொழி","அந்தர சாரிகள் அறைந்தனர் சாற்றும்","இந்திர விகாரம் ஏழுடன் போகி,"],"p":"","r":"Before dawn on the last dark night of the month, with the deed of the past driving them and their own minds pressing, the couple leave by the long gate of their mansion, pass the temple of the sapphire-hued god asleep on the serpent, and go past the seven Indra viharas where sky-walkers proclaim the word of the dharma-teacher under the five-branched bodhi.","n":"Line 4 gives two drivers side by side: ஊழ்வினை pushes and the heart urges (உள்ளம் துரப்ப), so the narrator names the past deed as cause at the very moment the couple act of their own will; Kovalan's own spoken reason, the wish for wealth in Madurai, comes at line 51. The road out of Puhar is a tour of the city's faiths, a Vishnu temple and Buddhist viharas, before the Jain shrine of the next lines. Whether வலம்செயா (line 10) means they circled the temple or passed it without circling turns on the form; I read it as the poetic absolutive, having circled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","samayam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 1-14","lb":"SI10:1-14","ev":"Kovalan and Kannaki leave their Puhar mansion secretly before dawn and pass the Vishnu temple and the Buddhist viharas.","cl":[["a deed of the past drives a person's departure on the path that leads to its fruit","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:15-31":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":131,"t":["புலவுஊண் துறந்து பொய்யா விரதத்து","அவலம் நீத்துஅறிந்து அடங்கிய கொள்கை","மெய்வகை உணர்ந்த விழுமியோர் குழீஇய","ஐவகை நின்ற அருகத் தானத்துச்","சந்தி ஐந்தும் தம்முடன் கூடி","வந்துதலை மயங்கிய வான்பெரு மன்றத்துப்","பொலம்பூம் பிண்டி நலம்கிளர் கொழுநிழல்","நீர்அணி விழவினும் நெடுந்தேர் விழவினும்","சாரணர் வருஉம் தகுதிஉண் டாம்என","உலக நோன்பிகள் ஒருங்குடன் இட்ட","இலகுஒளிச் சிலாதலம் தொழுதுவலம் கொண்டு,","மலைதலைக் கொண்ட பேர்யாறு போலும்","உலக இடைகழி ஒருங்குடன் நீங்கி,","கலையி லாளன் காமர் வேனிலொடு","மலைய மாருதம் மன்னவற்கு இறுக்கும்","பன்மலர் அடுக்கிய நன்மரப் பந்தர்","இலவந் திகையின் எயில்புறம் போகி,"],"p":"","r":"They come to the Arhat shrine of those who gave up flesh and keep an unfailing vow, where lay votaries have set a shining stone seat for the Charanas who come at the festivals, worship it and circle it, and then flow out of the city's passages like a great river leaving the hills, past the outer wall of the king's pleasure garden where the southern breeze pays its tribute with spring.","n":"The seat is worshipped empty: it is set up against the chance (தகுதி) that the sky-walking Charanas will come, which prepares the Charana who appears on such a seat at line 161. உலக நோன்பிகள் are those who keep the vow while living in the world, the lay counterpart of the ascetics described in lines 15 to 17.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","nonbu","vazhipaadu","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 15-31","lb":"SI10:15-31","ev":"The couple worship and circle the Charanas' stone seat at the Jain shrine and leave the city by the garden wall.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:32-51":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":132,"t":["தாழ்பொழில் உடுத்த தண்பதப் பெருவழிக்","காவிரி வாயில் கடைமுகம் கழிந்து,","குடதிசைக் கொண்டு கொழும்புனல் காவிரி","வடபெருங் கோட்டு மலர்ப்பொழில் நுழைந்து,","காவதம் கடந்து கவுந்திப் பள்ளிப்","பூமரப் பொதும்பர்ப் பொருந்தி ஆங்கண்","இறும்கொடி நுசுப்போடு இனைந்துஅடி வருந்தி","நறும்பல் கூந்தல் குறும்பல் உயிர்த்து","முதிராக் கிளவியின் முள்எயிறு இலங்க","மதுரை மூதூர் யாதுஎன வினவ,","ஆறுஐங் காதம்நம் அகல்நாட்டு உம்பர்","நாறுஐங் கூந்தல் நணித்துஎன நக்குத்,","தேமொழி தன்னொடும் சிறையகத்து இருந்த","காவுந்தி ஐயையைக் கண்டுஅடி தொழலும்,","உருவும் குலனும் உயர்ப்பேர் ஒழுக்கமும்","பெருமகன் திருமொழி பிறழா நோன்பும்","உடையீர் என்னோ உறுக ணாளரின்","கடைகழிந்து இங்ஙனம் கருதிய வாறுஎன,","உரையாட்டு இல்லை உறுதவத் தீர்யான்","மதுரை மூதூர் வரைபொருள் வேட்கையேன்."],"p":"","r":"Going west along the Kaveri's north bank they reach after one kaatham the flower grove of the Jain nun Kavunthi's retreat; Kannaki, aching in feet and waist, asks in her child's speech which of these is Madurai, and Kovalan laughs that it is thirty kaathams beyond their land; they worship the nun, who asks why people of their form, family, conduct and vow have left home like the afflicted, and Kovalan answers that he seeks wealth in old Madurai.","n":"Kannaki's question after one kaatham, and Kovalan's laughing answer that thirty is near, measure the distance between her sheltered life and the journey. Kavunthi credits the couple with a vow that never strays from the Great One's word (line 47), which counts them among the lay faithful. Kovalan's stated motive, wealth (வரைபொருள் வேட்கை), stands against the narrator's ஊழ்வினை at line 4.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nonbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 32-51","lb":"SI10:32-51","ev":"Kannaki asks how far Madurai is, the couple meet the Jain nun Kavunthi at her retreat, and Kovalan tells her he goes to Madurai for wealth.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணகி","கோவலன்","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:52-67":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":133,"t":["பாடகச் சீறடி பரல்பகை உழவா","காடுஇடை யிட்ட நாடுநீர் கழிதற்கு","அரிதுஇவள் செவ்வி அறிகுநர் யாரோ","உரியது அன்றுஈங்கு ஒழிகஎன ஒழியீர்","மறஉரை நீத்த மாசுஅறு கேள்வியர்","அறஉரை கேட்டுஆங்கு அறிவனை ஏத்தத்","தென்தமிழ் நன்னாட்டுத் தீதுதீர் மதுரைக்கு","ஒன்றிய உள்ளம் உடையேன் ஆகலின்","போதுவல் யானும் போதுமின் என்ற","காவுந்தி ஐயையைக் கைதொழுது ஏத்தி","அடிகள் நீரே அருளிதிர் ஆயின்இத்","தொடிவளைத் தோளி துயர்த்தீர்த் தேன்என,","கோவலன் காணாய் கொண்ட இந்நெறிக்கு","ஏதம் தருவன யாங்கும்பல கேண்மோ:","வெயில்நிறம் பொறாஅ மெல்இயல் கொண்டு","பயில்பூந் தண்டலைப் படர்குவம் எனினே,"],"p":"","r":"Kavunthi says the forest-broken country is hard going for the girl's small anklet feet and that they will not turn back though told to, so she too, wishing to hear dharma from the learned and praise the Knower, will go to Madurai with them; Kovalan worships her and says her grace would end his wife's distress, and she begins to list the dangers of the road if they take the delicate girl into the shady groves.","n":"The nun's motive for the journey is her own: to hear the teaching at Madurai (lines 56-59). Her joining makes her the couple's guardian on the road, the role she hands on in Madurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","thunai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 52-67","lb":"SI10:52-67","ev":"Kavunthi decides to go with the couple to Madurai, and Kovalan accepts her as their guide.","cl":[],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:68-83":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":134,"t":["மண்பக வீழ்ந்த கிழங்குஅகழ் குழியைச்","சண்பகம் நிறைத்த தாதுசோர் பொங்கர்","பொய்யறைப் படுத்துப் போற்றா மாக்கட்குக்","கையறு துன்பம் காட்டினும் காட்டும்,","உதிர்ப்பூஞ் செம்மலின் ஒதுங்கினர் கழிவோர்","முதிர்த்தேம் பழம்பகை முட்டினும் முட்டும்,","மஞ்சளும் இஞ்சியும் மயங்குஅரில் வலயத்துச்","செஞ்சுளைப் பலவின் பரல்பகை உறுக்கும்.","கயல்நெடுங் கண்ணி காதல் கேள்வ.","வயல்உழைப் படர்க்குவம் எனினே ஆங்குப்","பூநாறு இலஞ்சிப் பொருகயல் ஓட்டி","நீர்நாய் கெளவிய நெடும்புற வாளை","மலங்குமிளிர் செறுவின் விலங்கப் பாயின்","கலங்கலும் உண்டுஇக் காரிகை, ஆங்கண்","கரும்பில் தொடுத்த பெருந்தேன் சிதைந்து","சுரும்புசூழ் பொய்கைத் தூநீர் கலக்கும்"],"p":"","r":"Kavunthi warns that in the groves a pit dug for tubers and hidden under fallen champak pollen can trap the unwary, ripe jackfruit and thorny jack-seeds in the turmeric and ginger thickets can hurt, and if they take the fields instead a vaalai fish leaping from an otter's jaws may frighten the girl, and honey dripping from the cane may cloud the pond.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","acham","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 68-83","lb":"SI10:68-83","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:84-97":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":135,"t":["அடங்கா வேட்கையின் அறிவுஅஞர் எய்திக்","குடங்கையின் கொண்டு கொள்ளவும் கூடும்,","குறுநர் இட்ட குவளைஅம் போதொடு","பொறிவரி வண்டினம் பொருந்திய கிடக்கை","நெறிசெல் வருத்தத்து நீர்அஞர் எய்தி","அறியாது அடிஆங்கு இடுதலும் கூடும்,","எறிநீர் அடைகரை இயக்கம் தன்னில்","பொறிமாண் அலவனும் நந்தும் போற்றாது","ஊழ்அடி ஒதுக்கத்து உறுநோய் காணின்","தாழ்தரு துன்பம் தாங்கவும் ஒண்ணா,","வயலும் சோலையும் அல்லது யாங்கணும்","அயல்படக் கிடந்த நெறிஆங்கு இல்லை","நெறிஇருங் குஞ்சி நீவெய் யோளொடு","குறிஅறிந்து அவைஅவை குறுகாது ஓம்புஎன,"],"p":"","r":"She goes on: the girl, thirsty past bearing, might scoop that honeyed water in her palm or step unknowing on lilies thick with bees, and on the shore a careless step may harm the crab and the snail, a grief that cannot be borne; since there is no path but fields and groves, Kovalan must read the signs with his beloved and avoid each one.","n":"The danger in lines 90-93 runs both ways: the step may hurt the girl and it may hurt the crab and the snail she does not see, and the nun counts the second as a sorrow beyond bearing. ஊழ் here (line 92) is the order of the steps, not fate. The warning against drinking honeyed water may reflect a Jain avoidance of honey, but the lines give thirst and confusion of mind (அறிவு அஞர்) as the danger, not a rule.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","arul","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 84-97","lb":"SI10:84-97","ev":"","cl":[["a careless step that harms even small creatures like the crab and the snail is a grief to be guarded against","general","asserted",92,"kavunthi"]],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:98-114":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":136,"t":["தோம்அறு கடிஞையும் சுவல்மேல் அறுவையும்","காவுந்தி ஐயைகைப் பீலியும் கொண்டு","மொழிப்பொருள் தெய்வம் வழித்துணை ஆகெனப்","பழிப்புஅருஞ் சிறப்பின் வழிப்படர் புரிந்தோர்,","கரியவன் புகையினும் புகைக்கொடி தோன்றினும்","விரிகதிர் வெள்ளி தென்புலம் படரினும்","கால்பொரு நிவப்பின் கடுங்குரல் ஏற்றொடும்","சூல்முதிர் கொண்மூப் பெயல்வளம் சுரப்பக்","குடமலைப் பிறந்த கொழும்பல் தாரமொடு","கடல்வளன் எதிரக் கயவாய் நெரிக்கும்","காவிரிப் புதுநீர்க் கடுவரல் வாய்த்தலை","ஓஇறந்து ஒலிக்கும் ஒலியே அல்லது","ஆம்பியும் கிழாரும் வீங்குஇசை ஏத்தமும்","ஓங்குநீர்ப் பிழாவும் ஒலித்தல் செல்லாக்","கழனிச் செந்நெல் கரும்புசூழ் மருங்கில்","பழனத் தாமரைப் பைம்பூங் கானத்துக்","கம்புட் கோழியும் கனைகுரல் நாரையும்"],"p":"","r":"Kavunthi takes up her flawless begging bowl, the cloth for her shoulder and the peacock-feather brush, and praying that the god who is the meaning of the word be their companion they set out into the Chola country, where even when Saturn smokes, a comet shows and Venus moves south, the new flood of the Kaveri comes down with the hills' goods to meet the sea's wealth, so that only its roar is heard at the sluices and no water-lift creaks, among paddy, cane, lotus pools and waterbirds.","n":"The omens of lines 102-103 are the Sangam formula for drought, kept here to praise the Kaveri, which fails no one when the sky fails. மொழிப்பொருள் தெய்வம் (line 100) I take as the god whose word is the truth, but the phrase is compressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","naadu","uzhavu","nonbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 98-114","lb":"SI10:98-114","ev":"Kavunthi takes her bowl, cloth and peacock-feather brush, and the three set out through the Kaveri's fields.","cl":[],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:115-131":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":137,"t":["செங்கால் அன்னமும் பைங்கால் கொக்கும்","கானக் கோழியும் நீர்நிறக் காக்கையும்","உள்ளும் ஊரலும் புள்ளும் புதாவும்","வெல்போர் வேந்தர் முனையிடம் போலப்","பல்வேறு குழூஉக்குரல் பரந்த ஓதையும்,","உழாஅ நுண்தொளி உள்புக்கு அழுந்திய","கழாஅமயிர் யாக்கைச் செங்கண் காரான்","சொரிபுறம் உரிஞ்சப் புரிஞெகிழ்பு உற்ற","குமரிக் கூட்டில் கொழும்பல் உணவு","கவரிச் செந்நெல் காய்த்தலைச் சொரியக்","கருங்கை வினைஞரும் களமருங் கூடி","ஒருங்குநின்று ஆர்க்கும் ஒலியே அன்றியும்,","கடிமலர் களைந்து முடிநாறு அழுத்தித்","தொடிவளைத் தோளும் ஆகமும் தோய்ந்து","சேறுஆடு கோலமொடு வீறுபெறத் தோன்றிச்","செங்கயல் நெடுங்கண் சின்மொழிக் கடைசியர்","வெங்கள் தொலைச்சிய விருந்திற் பாணியும்,"],"p":"","r":"The fields ring with the many cries of geese, herons, waterhens and crows like the camp of a victorious king, with the shouting of the dark-handed labourers when a mud-caked buffalo rubbing against the granary spills its paddy, and with the new song of the field-women who, having drunk hot toddy, transplant seedlings with mud on their bangled arms.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","naadu","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 115-131","lb":"SI10:115-131","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:132-155":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":138,"t":["கொழுங்கொடி அறுகையும் குவளையும் கலந்து","விளங்குகதிர்த் தொடுத்த விரியல் சூட்டிப்","பார்உடைப் பனர்ப்போல் பழிச்சினர் கைதொழ","ஏரொடு நின்றோர் ஏர்மங் கலமும்,","அரிந்துகால் குவித்தோர் அரிகடா வுறுத்த","பெருஞ்செய்ந் நெல்லின் முகவைப் பாட்டும்,","தெண்கிணைப் பொருநர் செருக்குடன் எடுத்த","மண்கனை முழவின் மகிழ்இசை ஓதையும்,","பேர்யாற்று அடைகரை நீரிற் கேட்டுஆங்கு","ஆர்வ நெஞ்சமோடு அவலம் கொள்ளார்,","உழைப்புலிக் கொடித்தேர் உரவோன் கொற்றமொடு","மழைக்கரு உயிர்க்கும் அழல்திகழ் அட்டில்","மறையோர் ஆக்கிய ஆவூதி நறும்புகை","இறைஉயர் மாடம் எங்கணும் போர்த்து","மஞ்சுசூழ் மலையின் மாணத் தோன்றும்","மங்கல மறையோர் இருக்கை அன்றியும்,","பரப்புநீர்க் காவிரிப் பாவைதன் புதல்வர்","இரப்போர் சுற்றமும் புரப்போர் கொற்றமும்","உழவிடை விளைப்போர் பழவிறல் ஊர்களும்,","பொங்கழி ஆலைப் புகையொடும் பரந்து","மங்குல் வானத்து மலையின் தோன்றும்","ஊர்இடை யிட்ட நாடுஉடன் கண்டு","காவதம் அல்லது கடவார் ஆகிப்","பன்னாள் தங்கிச் செல்நாள் ஒருநாள்:"],"p":"","r":"With the plough festival of the ploughmen who wear arugu and lily, the reaping songs at the threshing floors and the drums of the kinai bards in their ears, the travellers go on without grief past the Brahmins' auspicious settlements, whose sacrificial smoke wraps the mansions like mist on hills under the tiger-flag king's might, and past the old towns of the Kaveri's sons, moving only a kaatham a day and halting many days.","n":"The Vedic sacrifice is praised as part of the land's good order (மங்கல மறையோர் இருக்கை) in the same journey that the Jain nun leads; the text sets the faiths side by side and does not weigh them here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","naadu","aaguthi","kinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 132-155","lb":"SI10:132-155","ev":"The three travel slowly through the Chola country, a kaatham a day, halting many days.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","கண்ணகி","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:156-169":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":139,"t":["ஆற்றுவீ அரங்கத்து வீற்றுவீற்று ஆகிக்","குரங்குஅமை உடுத்த மரம்பயில் அடுக்கத்து,","வானவர் உறையும் பூநாறு ஒருசிறைப்","பட்டினப் பாக்கம் விட்டனர் நீங்காப்","பெரும்பெயர் ஐயர் ஒருங்குடன் இட்ட","இலங்குஒளிச் சிலாதலம் மேல்இருந் தருளிப்","பெருமகன் அதிசயம் பிறழா வாய்மைத்","தருமம் சாற்றும் சாரணர் தோன்றப்,","பண்டைத் தொல்வினை பாறுக என்றே","கண்டுஅறி கவுந்தியொடு கால்உற வீழ்ந்தோர்","வந்த காரணம் வயங்கிய கொள்கைச்","சிந்தை விளக்கில் தெரிந்தோன் ஆயினும்","ஆர்வமும் செற்றமும் அகல நீக்கிய","வீரன் ஆகலின் விழுமம் கொள்ளான்,"],"p":"","r":"One day, at a grove on a river island near a place where the gods dwell, a Charana appears on the stone seat set by the great ascetics, proclaiming the true dharma of the Great One's wonder; the travellers fall at his feet with Kavunthi praying that their old deed may perish, and though he knows by the lamp of his mind why they have come, he does not grieve, being a hero who has put away desire and hatred.","n":"The Charana foresees what is coming to the couple and says nothing of it; the narrator explains his silence by his freedom from desire and hatred (ஆர்வமும் செற்றமும்), not by indifference. Their prayer, பண்டைத் தொல்வினை பாறுக, asks for the very thing his sermon will say cannot be had.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thavam","samanilai","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 156-169","lb":"SI10:156-169","ev":"A Jain Charana appears on the stone seat, and the travellers and Kavunthi fall at his feet praying that their old deeds perish.","cl":[["one who has put away desire and hatred does not grieve even when he foresees another's suffering","jain","asserted",168,"narrator"]],"ag":["சாரணர்","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:170-191":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":140,"t":["கழிப்பெருஞ் சிறப்பின் கவுந்தி காணாய்:","ஒழிகென ஒழியாது ஊட்டும் வல்வினை","இட்ட வித்தின் எதிர்வந்து எய்தி","ஒட்டுங் காலை ஒழிக்கவும் ஒண்ணா","கடுங்கால் நெடுவெளி இடும்சுடர் என்ன","ஒருங்குடன் நில்லா உடம்பிடை உயிர்கள்","அறிவன் அறவோன் அறிவுவரம்பு இகந்தோன்","செறிவன் சினேந்திரன் சித்தன் பகவன்","தரும முதல்வன் தலைவன் தருமன்","பொருளன் புனிதன் புராணன் புலவன்","சினவரன் தேவன் சிவகதி நாயகன்","பரமன் குணவதன் பரத்தில் ஒளியோன்","தத்துவன் சாதுவன் சாரணன் காரணன்","சித்தன் பெரியவன் செம்மல் திகழ்ஒளி","இறைவன் குரவன் இயல்குணன் எம்கோன்","குறைவில் புகழோன் குணப்பெருங் கோமான்","சங்கரன் ஈசன் சயம்பு சதுமுகன்","அங்கம் பயந்தோன் அருகன் அருள்முனி","பண்ணவன் எண்குணன் பாத்தில் பழம்பொருள்","விண்ணவன் வேத முதல்வன் விளங்குஓளி","ஓதிய வேதத்து ஒளிஉறின் அல்லது","போதார் பிறவிப் பொதிஅறை யோர்எனச்"],"p":"","r":"The Charana tells Kavunthi that the strong deed, which will not go when bidden, comes back like a sown seed to face its doer and cannot be put off once it clings, that lives do not stay long in bodies, like a lamp set in a windy open place, and that, unless the light of the Veda spoken by the Lord reaches them, souls will not open the bundle of births; he names that Lord by a long run of titles, Knower, Jina, Siddha, Arhat, the one of eight qualities, Sankara, Isa, the self-born and four-faced.","n":"The verb ஊட்டும் is the patikam's (ஊழ்வினை உருத்துவந்து ஊட்டும், line 57 of the patikam), so the epic's third moral is here given to a Jain sage as doctrine, with one addition the patikam lacks: a way out of birth through the Jina's word. The titles take names other schools use for their own gods (சங்கரன், ஈசன், சதுமுகன், வேத முதல்வன்) and claim them for the Arhat. The syntax of lines 174-175 is compressed; I read the lamp in the wind as the image of life in the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","nilaiyaamai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 170-191","lb":"SI10:170-191","ev":"","cl":[["a strong deed, once sown like a seed, returns to its doer and cannot be put off when it clings","jain","asserted",171,"saaranar"],["life stays in the body no longer than a lamp in a windy open place","general","asserted",174,"saaranar"],["souls are freed from the bundle of births only when the light of the Jina's spoken teaching reaches them","jain","asserted",190,"saaranar"]],"ag":["சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:192-213":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":141,"t":["சாரணர் வாய்மொழி கேட்டுத் தவமுதல்","காவுந்தி யும்தன் கைதலை மேற்கொண்டு","ஒருமூன்று அவித்தோன் ஓதிய ஞானத்","திருமொழிக்கு அல்லதுஎன் செவியகம் திறவா,","காமனை வென்றோன் ஆயிரத்து எட்டு","நாமம் அல்லது நவிலாது என்நா,","ஐவரை வென்றோன் அடியிணை அல்லது","கைவரைக் காணினும் காணா என்கண்,","அருள்அறம் பூண்டோன் திருமெய்க்கு அல்லதுஎன்","பொருள்இல் யாக்கை பூமியில் பொருந்தாது,","அருகர் அறவன் அறிவோற்கு அல்லதுஎன்","இருகையும் கூடி ஒருவழிக் குவியா,","மலர்மிசை நடந்தோன் மலர்அடி அல்லதுஎன்","தலைமிசை உச்சி தான்அணிப் பொறாஅது","இறுதிஇல் இன்பத்து இறைமொழிக்கு அல்லது","மறுதிர ஓதிஎன் மனம்புடை பெயராது","என்றவன் இசைமொழி ஏத்தக் கேட்டுஅதற்கு","ஒன்றிய மாதவர் உயர்மிசை ஓங்கி","நிவந்துஆங்கு ஒருமுழம் நீள்நிலம் நீங்கிப்","பவம்தரு பாசம் கவுந்தி கெடுகென்று","அந்தரம் ஆறாப் படர்வோர்த் தொழுது","பந்தம் அறுகெனப் பணிந்தனர் போந்து,"],"p":"","r":"Hearing the Charana, Kavunthi lays her hands on her head and vows that her ears open only to the word of the one who quenched the three, her tongue speaks only the thousand and eight names of the conqueror of desire, her eyes see only the feet of the conqueror of the five, her body touches earth only before his holy form, her hands join only for the Arhat, her head bears only his flower feet and her mind moves only for his word; the sage rises a cubit from the ground, blesses her that the bond which brings birth may perish, and goes through the sky, and the travellers worship and go on.","n":"The vow goes organ by organ, ears, tongue, eyes, body, hands, head, mind, and each line has the same 'only for him' (அல்லது) shape, so devotion is described as the closing of every sense to all else. The Charana's blessing names பாசம் and பவம்: the bond is what brings birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","paasam","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 192-213","lb":"SI10:192-213","ev":"Kavunthi vows every sense to the Arhat, and the Charana blesses her and departs through the sky.","cl":[["the bond that brings rebirth can be destroyed","jain","asserted",211,"saaranar"]],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","சாரணர்"],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:214-228":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":142,"t":["கார்அணி பூம்பொழில் காவிரிப் பேர்யாற்று","நீர்அணி மாடத்து நெடுந்துறை போகி","மாதரும் கணவனும் மாதவத் தாட்டியும்","தீதுதீர் நியமத் தென்கரை எய்திப்","போதுசூழ் கிடக்கைஓர் பூம்பொழில் இருந்துழி","வம்பப் பரத்தை வறுமொழி யாளனொடு","கொங்குஅலர் பூம்பொழில் குறுகினர் சென்றோர்","காமனும் தேவியும் போலும் ஈங்குஇவர்","ஆர்எனக் கேட்டுஈங்கு அறிகுவம் என்றே,","நோற்றுஉணல் யாக்கை நொசிதவத் தீர்உடன்","ஆற்றுவழிப் பட்டோர் ஆர்என வினவ,என்","மக்கள் காணீர் மானிட யாக்கையர்","பக்கம் நீங்குமின் பரிபுலம் பினர்என,","உடன்வயிற் றோர்க்கள் ஒருங்குடன் வாழ்க்கை","கடவதும் உண்டோ ? கற்றறிந் தீர்எனத்,"],"p":"","r":"Crossing the Kaveri by a ghat to its south bank, the three rest in a grove, where a wanton courtesan and an idle-tongued man come up, ask the fasting nun who these two, like Kama and his consort, are; when she answers that they are her children, human beings, and bids them stand off from the weary pair, the two jeer whether people born of one womb live together as man and wife, and call her learned.","n":"Kavunthi's 'my children' is the nun's shield for a married couple, and the mockers turn it into an insinuation of incest. நோற்றுஉணல் யாக்கை (line 223), the body that eats only after fasting, is how they address her, and her austerity is what gives force to what follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","parathai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 214-228","lb":"SI10:214-228","ev":"A courtesan and a loose-tongued man mock Kavunthi and the couple, insinuating that the pair are siblings living as lovers.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணகி","கோவலன்","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","வம்பப் பரத்தை","வறுமொழி யாளன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:13"},"si:10:229-248":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":143,"t":["தீமொழி கேட்டுச் செவியகம் புதைத்துக்","காதலன் முன்னர்க் கண்ணகி நடுங்க,","எள்ளுநர் போலும்இவர் என்பூங் கோதையை","முள்உடைக் காட்டின் முதுநரி ஆகெனக்","கவுந்தி இட்ட தவம்தரு சாபம்","கட்டியது ஆதலின், பட்டதை அறியார்","குறுநரி நெடுங்குரல் கூவிளி கேட்டு","நறுமலர்க் கோதையும் நம்பியும் நடுங்கி,","நெறியின் நீங்கியோர் நீர்அல கூறினும்","அறியா மைஎன்று அறியல் வேண்டும்","செய்தவத் தீர்நும் திருமுன் பிழைத்தோர்க்கு","உய்திக் காலம் உரையீ ரோஎன,","அறியா மையின்இன்று இழிபிறப்பு உற்றோர்","உறையூர் நொச்சி ஒருபுடை ஒதுங்கிப்","பன்னிரு மதியம் படர்நோய் உழந்தபின்","முன்னை உருவம் பெறுகஈங்கு இவர்எனச்","சாபவிடை செய்து, தவப்பெருஞ் சிறப்பின்","காவுந்தி ஐயையும் தேவியும் கணவனும்","முறம்செவி வாரணம் முஞ்சமம் முருக்கிய","புறஞ்சிறை வாரணம் புக்கனர் புரிந்துஎன்."],"p":"","r":"Kannaki stops her ears and trembles before her husband; Kavunthi, calling the two scorners of her flower-garlanded girl, curses them to become old jackals of the thorn forest, and the curse that her penance gives takes hold; when the couple, trembling at the jackals' howls, plead that the improper words of those who have left the path are ignorance and ask her to name a time of release, she rules that, having fallen into low birth by ignorance, they shall suffer twelve months by the Uraiyur hedge and then regain their former form, and the three enter Uraiyur.","n":"Lines 233-234 use the same formula as the patikam's curse of the former birth (இட்ட சாபம் கட்டியது ஆகலின், patikam line 49), but this curse is given a term and a return to former form, at the plea of the very couple who were wronged. The pardon comes from the victims: it is Kovalan and Kannaki who name the insult ignorance, and Kavunthi takes their word (அறியாமையின், line 241) into her release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saabam","thavam","porai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 10, lines 229-248","lb":"SI10:229-248","ev":"Kavunthi curses the two mockers into jackals and, at the couple's plea, sets a twelve-month term of release, and the three enter Uraiyur.","cl":[["an ascetic's penance gives her curse the power to take hold","general","asserted",233,"narrator"],["the wrong words of those who have left the right path should be taken as ignorance and pardoned","general","asserted",237,"kovalan"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்","வம்பப் பரத்தை","வறுமொழி யாளன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:13"},"si:pukar_kandam_katturai:1-20":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":144,"t":["முடிஉடை வேந்தர் மூவ ருள்ளும்","தொடிவிளங்கு தடக்கைச் சோழர்க்குலத்து உதித்தோர்","அறனும் மறனும் ஆற்றலும் அவர்தம்","பழவிறல் மூதூர்ப் பண்புமேம் படுதலும்","விழவுமலி சிறப்பும் விண்ணவர் வரவும்","ஒடியா இன்பத்து அவர்உறை நாட்டுக்","குடியும் கூழின் பெருக்கமும் அவர்தம்","தெய்வக் காவிரித் தீதுதீர் சிறப்பும்","பொய்யா வானம் புதுப்புனல் பொழிதலும்","அரங்கும் ஆடலும் தூக்கும் வரியும்","பரந்துஇசை எய்திய பாரதி விருத்தியும்","திணைநிலை வரியும் இணைநிலை வரியும்","அணைவுறக் கிடந்த யாழின் தொகுதியும்","ஈர்ஏழ் சகோடமும் இடநிலைப் பாலையும்","தாரத்து ஆக்கமும் தான்தெரி பண்ணும்","ஊரகத் தேரும் ஒளியுடைப் பாணியும்","என்றுஇவை அனைத்தும் பிறபொருள் வைப்போடு","ஒன்றித் தோன்றும் தனிக்கோள் நிலைமையும்","ஒரு பரிசா நோக்கிக் கிடந்த","புகார்க் காண்டம் முற்றிற்று."],"p":"","r":"The narrator sums up the Puhar book as one that keeps in single view the righteousness, valour and strength of those born in the Chola line among the three crowned kings, the excellence of their ancient city, its festivals and the visits of the gods, the people and abundant food of their land, the evil-removing divine Kaveri, the unfailing sky pouring new floods, and the arts of stage, dance, rhythm, vari songs, the lute and its modes, and declares the book complete.","n":"The same formula, nearly word for word, closes the Madurai and Vanchi books with the Pandya and Chera lines put in place of the Chola, so the praise of aram, maram and an unfailing sky belongs to the frame given every crowned line, not to a judgment on this one. What differs is the close: the Madurai summary looks at its book as one with the Pandya Nedunjeliyan who died on his throne with his queen, the Vanchi summary with Senguttuvan, while this one names no king and gives most of its lines to music and dance (lines 10 to 16, the arts of Madhavi's kaathais). The music terms of lines 13 to 16 (சகோடம், இடநிலைப் பாலை, தாரத்து ஆக்கம், ஊரகத் தேர்) are technical and are left unglossed here; the reading does not turn on them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","naadu","isai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram pukar_kandam_katturai, lines 1-20","lb":"SIpukar_kandam_katturai:1-20","ev":"","cl":[["the divine Kaveri removes evil from the land it waters","general","asserted",8,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:14"},"si:pukar_kandam_katturai:21-24":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":145,"t":["காலை அரும்பி மலரும் கதிரவனும்","மாலை மதியமும்போல் வாழியரோ - வேலை","அகழால் அமைந்த அவனிக்கு மாலைப்","புகழால் அமைந்த புகார்."],"p":"","r":"A closing venba wishes that Puhar, which fame has made for the earth whose moat is the sea, may live on like the sun that buds and blooms in the morning and the moon of the evening.","n":"The wish for the city's lasting life is a blessing, not a claim that it will last; the poem calls the sea the earth's moat (வேலை அகழால் அமைந்த அவனி), the sea that the Manimekalai later makes the city's end. The kandam opened (silappathikaram:1:1-12) by praising the moon and the sun for being like the Chola; here they are the measure for the city, and no king is named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram pukar_kandam_katturai, lines 21-24","lb":"SIpukar_kandam_katturai:21-24","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:14"},"si:11:1-14":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":146,"t":["திங்கள்மூன் றடுக்கிய திருமுக் குடைக்கீழ்ச்","செங்கதிர் ஞாயிற்றுத் திகழொளி சிறந்து","கோதைதாழ் பிண்டிக் கொழுநிழ லிருந்த","ஆதியில் தோற்றத் தறிவனை வணங்கிக்","கந்தன் பள்ளிக் கடவுளர்க் கெல்லாம்","அந்தி லரங்கத் தகன்பொழி லகவயிற்","சாரணர் கூறிய தகைசால் நன்மொழி","மாதவத் தாட்டியும் மாண்புற மொழிந்தாங்கு","அன்றவ ருறைவிடத் தல்கின ரடங்கித்","தென்றிசை மருங்கிற் செலவு விருப்புற்று","வைகறை யாமத்து வாரணங் கழிந்து","வெய்யவன் குணதிசை விளங்கித் தோன்ற","வளநீர்ப் பண்ணையும் வாவியும் பொலிந்ததோர்","இளமரக் கானத் திருக்கை புக்குழி"],"p":"","r":"After worshipping the Arhat seated under the triple umbrella beneath the flowering pindi tree, and after the Charanas and then the great ascetic woman had spoken their good words at the shrine grove, the three stay the night at their lodging and, eager to go south, set off at the last watch before dawn and enter a grove of young trees beside watered fields and ponds.","n":"The chapter opens in an explicitly Jain setting (the triple umbrella, the pindi or asoka tree, the Charanas, அறிவன் for the omniscient one), so the Vaishnava pilgrim who appears next is met by travellers who have just come from another shrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 1-14","lb":"SI11:1-14","ev":"Kovalan, Kannaki and Kavunthi worship at a Jain shrine, rest the night, and set out south at dawn into a grove.","cl":[],"ag":["சாரணர்","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:15-30":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":147,"t":["வாழ்க எங்கோ மன்னவர் பெருந்தகை","ஊழிதொ றூழிதொ றுலகங் காக்க","அடியிற் றன்னள வரசர்க் குணர்த்தி","வடிவேல் எறிந்த வான்பகை பொறாது","பஃறுளி யாற்றுடன் பன்மலை யடுக்கத்துக்","குமரிக் கோடுங் கொடுங்கடல் கொள்ள","வடதிசைக் கங்கையும் இமயமுங் கொண்டு","தென்றிசை யாண்ட தென்னவன் வாழி","திங்கட் செல்வன் திருக்குலம் விளங்கச்","செங்கணா யிரத்தோன் திறல்விளங் காரம்","பொங்கொளி மார்பிற் பூண்டோன் வாழி","முடிவளை யுடைத்தோன் முதல்வன் சென்னியென்று","இடியுடைப் பெருமழை யெய்தா தேகப்","பிழையா விளையுட் பெருவளஞ் சுரப்ப","மழைபிணித் தாண்ட மன்னவன் வாழ்கெனத்","தீதுதீர் சிறப்பின் தென்னனை வாழ்த்தி"],"p":"","r":"A voice in the grove hails the Pandya: may he guard the world age after age, he whose lance the sea would not bear and who, when the sea took the Pahruli river and the Kumari peak with its hills, took the Ganga and the Himalaya in the north and ruled the south; he of the lunar line who wears Indra's necklace on his chest, who broke a crown, and who, when the thundering clouds withheld their rain, bound them and made the harvest unfailing.","n":"Lines 17 to 22 tie the loss of southern land to the sea to a king's quarrel with it and set a northern conquest against it; the epic gives this as praise, not as history. Line 26 (முடிவளை உடைத்தோன்) is compressed and whose crown was broken is not stated. The praise of a faultless line (தீதுதீர் சிறப்பின் தென்னனை, line 30) is spoken on the road to the city where the reigning Pandya will condemn himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","mazhai","pugazh","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 15-30","lb":"SI11:15-30","ev":"An old brahmin sitting in the grove sings a blessing on the Pandya kings.","cl":[["a king of the right line can bind the clouds and make the harvest unfailing","general","reported",29,"maangaattu_maraiyon"]],"ag":["மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்",""],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:31-56":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":148,"t":["மாமுது மறையோன் வந்திருந் தோனை","யாது நும்மூர் ஈங்கென் வரவெனக்","கோவலன் கேட்பக் குன்றாச் சிறப்பின்","மாமறை யாளன் வருபொருள் உரைப்போன்","நீல மேகம் நெடும்பொற் குன்றத்துப்","பால்விரிந் தகலாது படிந்தது போல","ஆயிரம் விரித்தெழு தலையுடை அருந்திறற்","பாயற் பள்ளிப் பலர்தொழு தேத்த","விரிதிரைக் காவிரி வியன்பெரு துருத்தித்","திருவமர் மார்பன் கிடந்த வண்ணமும்","வீங்குநீ ரருவி வேங்கட மென்னும்","ஓங்குயர் மலையத் துச்சி மீமிசை","விரிகதிர் ஞாயிறுந் திங்களும் விளங்கி","இருமருங் கோங்கிய இடைநிலைத் தானத்து","மின்னுக்கோடி யுடுத்து விளங்குவிற் பூண்டு","நன்னிற மேகம் நின்றது போலப்","பகையணங் காழியும் பால்வெண் சங்கமும்","தகைபெறு தாமரைக் கையி னேந்தி","நலங்கிளர் ஆரம் மார்பிற் பூண்டு","பொலம்பூ வாடையிற் பொலிந்து தோன்றிய","செங்கண் நெடியோன் நின்ற வண்ணமும்","என்கண் காட்டென் றென்னுளங் கவற்ற","வந்தேன் குடமலை மாங்காட் டுள்ளேன்","தென்னவன் நாட்டுச் சிறப்புஞ் செய்கையும்","கண்மணி குளிர்ப்பக் கண்டே னாதலின்","வாழ்த்திவந் திருந்தேன் இதுவென் வரவெனத்"],"p":"","r":"Kovalan asks the old brahmin where he comes from and why he is here, and the brahmin of Maankadu in the western hills answers that his heart pressed him to see with his own eyes the form in which Vishnu lies on the thousand-hooded serpent at the river island of the Kaveri and the form in which he stands, conch and discus in hand like a dark cloud girt with lightning, on the peak of Venkatam between sun and moon, and that having seen the Pandya land's excellence he stayed to praise it.","n":"The pilgrim's wish is to see (என்கண் காட்டு, line 52): his devotion is to the god's visible postures, reclining and standing, which the chapter will set beside the Jain nun's inward discipline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 31-56","lb":"SI11:31-56","ev":"Kovalan questions the brahmin, who says he is on pilgrimage to see Vishnu at Srirangam and Venkatam and stopped to praise the Pandya land.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:57-71":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":149,"t":["தீத்திறம் புரிந்தோன் செப்பக் கேட்டு","மாமறை முதல்வ மதுரைச் செந்நெறி","கூறு நீயெனக் கோவலற் குரைக்கும்","கோத்தொழி லாளரொடு கொற்றவன் கோடி","வேத்தியல் இழந்த வியனிலம் போல","வேனலங் கிழவனொடு வெங்கதிர் வேந்தன்","தானலந் திருகத் தன்மையிற் குன்றி","முல்லையுங் குறிஞ்சியும் முறைமையின் திரிந்து","நல்லியல் பிழந்து நடுங்குதுய ருறுத்துப்","பாலை யென்பதோர் படிவங் கொள்ளும்","காலை எய்தினிர் காரிகை தன்னுடன்","அறையும் பொறையும் ஆரிடை மயக்கமும்","நிறைநீர் வேலியும் முறைபடக் கிடந்தஇந்","நெடும்பேர் அத்தம் நீந்திச் சென்று","கொடும்பை நெடுங்குளக் கோட்டகம் புக்கால்"],"p":"","r":"Kovalan asks the brahmin for the straight road to Madurai, and the brahmin tells him he has come with his lady at the season when summer and the burning sun, like a wide land that has lost its royal order because its king bent with his officers, twist the mullai and kurinji out of their nature into the form called paalai, and that he must cross this long waste of rock, hill and maze-like paths to the bank of the great tank of Kodumbai, where the road forks like the trident of the crescent-crowned god.","n":"The simile at lines 60 to 66 makes paalai a state of land rather than a region: forest and hill turned by heat into waste, as a kingdom is turned by a bent sceptre (கொற்றவன் கோடி). It is spoken on the road to the kingdom whose sceptre will bend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","idukkan","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 57-71","lb":"SI11:57-71","ev":"Kovalan asks the way to Madurai and the brahmin describes the summer waste up to the three-way fork at Kodumbai.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:72-86":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":150,"t":["பிறைமுடிக் கண்ணிப் பெரியோன் ஏந்திய","அறைவாய்ச் சூலத் தருநெறி கவர்க்கும்","வலம்படக் கிடந்த வழிநீர் துணியின்","அலறுதலை மராமும் உலறுதலை ஓமையும்","பொரியரை உழிஞ்சிலும் புன்முளி மூங்கிலும்","வரிமரல் திரங்கிய கரிபுறக் கிடக்கையும்","நீர்நசைஇ வேட்கையின் மானின்று விளிக்கும்","கானமும் எயினர் கடமுங் கடந்தால்","ஐவன வெண்ணெலும் அறைக்கட் கரும்பும்","கொய்பூந் தினையும் கொழும்புன வரகும்","காயமும் மஞ்சளும் ஆய்கொடிக் கவலையும்","வாழையும் கமுகும் தாழ்குலைத் தெங்கும்","மாவும் பலாவும் சூழடுத் தோங்கிய","தென்னவன் சிறுமலை திகழ்ந்து தோன்றும்","அம்மலை வலங்கொண் டகன்பதிச் செல்லுமின்"],"p":"","r":"If they take the right-hand branch, the brahmin says, past dead kadambu and omai, scorched ulinjil and dry bamboo, where deer cry for water, and through the hunters' pass, they will see the Pandya's Sirumalai rising among rice, cane, millet, turmeric, plantain, areca, coconut, mango and jack; keeping that hill on the right, they should go on to the wide city.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 72-86","lb":"SI11:72-86","ev":"The brahmin describes the right-hand road through the waste past Sirumalai to Madurai.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்",""],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:87-103":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":151,"t":["அவ்வழிப் படரீ ராயி னிடத்துச்","செவ்வழிப் பண்ணிற் சிறைவண் டரற்றும்","தடந்தாழ் வயலொடு தண்பூங் காவொடு","கடம்பல கிடந்த காடுடன் கழிந்து","திருமால் குன்றத்துச் செல்குவி ராயின்","பெருமால் கெடுக்கும் பிலமுண் டாங்கு","விண்ணோர் ஏத்தும் வியத்தகு மரபிற்","புண்ணிய சரவணம் பவகா ரணியோடு","இட்ட சித்தி யெனும்பெயர் போகி","விட்டு நீங்கா விளங்கிய பொய்கை","முட்டாச் சிறப்பின் மூன்றுள வாங்குப்","புண்ணிய சரவணம் பொருந்துவி ராயின்","விண்ணவர் கோமான் விழுநூ லெய்துவிர்","பவகா ரணி படிந் தாடுவி ராயிற்","பவகா ரணத்திற் பழம்பிறப் பெய்துவிர்","இட்ட சித்தி எய்துவி ராயின்","இட்ட சித்தி எய்துவிர் நீரே"],"p":"","r":"If they take the left-hand branch, past fields, groves and many forests to Thirumal's hill, there is a cave that dispels great confusion and three pools praised by the gods, Punniya Saravanam, Bhavakaarini and Ishtasiddhi: bathing in the first gives the treatise of the king of the gods, in the second the knowledge of one's former birth, and in the third whatever one wishes.","n":"The brahmin offers three goods from water: learning, memory of past lives, and wishes granted. Kavunthi answers the second directly at lines 156 to 157 without denying the pools' power; see 150-165.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","piravi","siththi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 87-103","lb":"SI11:87-103","ev":"The brahmin describes the left-hand road to Thirumal's hill, its cave and its three pools.","cl":[["bathing in the pool Punniya Saravanam gives the treatise of the king of the gods","vaishnava","reported",99,"maangaattu_maraiyon"],["bathing in the pool Bhavakaarini brings knowledge of one's former birth","vaishnava","reported",101,"maangaattu_maraiyon"],["bathing in the pool Ishtasiddhi gains whatever one wishes","vaishnava","reported",103,"maangaattu_maraiyon"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்",""],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:104-117":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":152,"t":["ஆங்குப் பிலம்புக வேண்டுதி ராயின்","ஓங்குயர் மலையத் துயர்ந்தோற் றொழுது","சிந்தையில் அவன்றன் சேவடி வைத்து","வந்தனை மும்முறை மலைவலம் செய்தால்","நிலம்பக வீழ்ந்த சிலம்பாற் றகன்றலைப்","பொலங்கொடி மின்னிற் புயலைங் கூந்தற்","கடிமல ரவிழ்ந்த கன்னிகா ரத்துத்","தொடிவளைத் தோளி ஒருத்தி தோன்றி","இம்மைக் கின்பமும் மறுமைக் கின்பமும்","இம்மையு மறுமையும் இரண்டும் இன்றியோர்","செம்மையில் நிற்பதுஞ் செப்புமின் நீயிர்இவ்","வரைத்தாள் வாழ்வேன் வரோத்தமை என்பேன்","உரைத்தார்க் குரியேன் உரைத்தீ ராயின்","திருத்தக் கீர்க்குத் திறந்தேன் கதவெனும்"],"p":"","r":"To enter the cave, the brahmin says, they must worship the high one of the hill, set his feet in their minds and walk round the hill three times; then by the Silambaaru a bangled woman will appear among karnikara blossoms and ask them to tell her the joy of this life, the joy of the next, and the state that stands in perfection beyond both, saying she is Varottamai who lives at the hill's foot, belongs to those who answer, and will open the door to the worthy.","n":"Varottamai's riddle lays out a scale of three ends, joy here, joy hereafter, and a third beyond both, which looks like release; the chapter never gives the answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","inbam","marumai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 104-117","lb":"SI11:104-117","ev":"The brahmin explains the rite for entering the cave and the guardian woman's riddle at its door.","cl":[["the cave opens to one who keeps the hill god's feet in mind and walks round the hill three times","vaishnava","reported",106,"maangaattu_maraiyon"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்",""],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:118-140":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":153,"t":["கதவந் திறந்தவள் காட்டிய நன்னெறிப்","புதவம் பலவுள போகிடை கழியன","ஒட்டுப் புதவமொன் றுண்டதன் உம்பர்","வட்டிகைப் பூங்கொடி வந்து தோன்றி","இறுதியில் இன்பம் எனக்கீங் குரைத்தாற்","பெறுதிர் போலும்நீர் பேணிய பொருளெனும்","உரையீ ராயினும் உறுகண் செய்யேன்","நெடுவழிப் புறத்து நீக்குவல் நும்மெனும்","உரைத்தார் உளரெனின் உரைத்த மூன்றின்","கரைப்படுத் தாங்குக் காட்டினள் பெயரும்","அருமறை மருங்கின் ஐந்தினும் எட்டினும்","வருமுறை எழுத்தின் மந்திர மிரண்டும்","ஒருமுறை யாக உளங்கொண் டோதி","வேண்டிய தொன்றின் விரும்பினி ராடிற்","காண்டகு மரபின வல்ல மற்றவை","மற்றவை நினையாது மலைமிசை நின்றோன்","பொற்றா மரைத்தாள் உள்ளம் பொருந்துமின்","உள்ளம் பொருந்துவி ராயின் மற்றவன்","புள்ளணி நீள்கொடி புணர்நிலை தோன்றும்","தோன்றிய பின்னவன் துணைமலர்த் தாளிணை","ஏன்றுதுயர் கெடுக்கும் இன்பம் எய்தி","மாண்புடை மரபின் மதுரைக் கேகுமின்","காண்டகு பிலத்தின் காட்சி யீதாங்கு"],"p":"","r":"Inside, the brahmin goes on, are many gates; above a joined gate a slender maiden will ask them to tell her the joy that has no end and promise them what they seek, or else send them unharmed back to the road, and to those who answer she shows the three pools; there, reciting together the two mantras of five and of eight letters, they may bathe in the one they want, but better, thinking of none of these, to fix the heart on the golden lotus feet of the god on the hill, whose bird banner will then appear, and having reached the joy of his feet that ends sorrow, to go on to Madurai.","n":"The brahmin himself ranks the pools below devotion (மற்றவை நினையாது, line 133), so the pilgrim's account already moves from outer water to the god's feet. Lines 128 to 132, on the two mantras and what is not to be seen, are compressed and my reading of them is tentative; the text says only five and eight letters, not which formulas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","mandhiram","theerththam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 118-140","lb":"SI11:118-140","ev":"The brahmin describes the inner gates, a second riddle, the mantras for the pools, and the higher way of devotion to the hill god.","cl":[["fixing the heart on the hill god's feet, not on the pools' boons, brings the joy that ends sorrow","vaishnava","reported",134,"maangaattu_maraiyon"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்",""],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:141-149":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":154,"t":["அந்நெறிப் படரீ ராயின் இடையது","செந்நெறி யாகும் தேம்பொழி லுடுத்த","ஊரிடை யிட்ட காடுபல கடந்தால்","ஆரிடை யுண்டோர் ஆரஞர்த் தெய்வம்","நடுக்கஞ் சாலா நயத்தின் தோன்றி","இடுக்கண் செய்யா தியங்குநர்த் தாங்கும்","மடுத்துடன் கிடக்கும் மதுரைப் பெருவழி","நீள்நிலங் கடந்த நெடுமுடி அண்ணல்","தாள்தொழு தகையேன் போகுவல் யானென"],"p":"","r":"If they take neither branch, the middle way is the straight road: past forests set between towns there is on the hard way a goddess of great affliction who appears gently and, doing no harm, holds back those who pass, and then the Madurai highway; as for himself, the brahmin says, he goes to worship the feet of the tall-crowned lord who strode across the wide earth.","n":"The warning is brief and its last verb (தாங்கும், line 146) can mean to hold back or to bear up travellers; Kovalan recalls it at lines 192 to 193 as a warning of a deluding goddess, which supports the first sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 141-149","lb":"SI11:141-149","ev":"The brahmin describes the middle road, warns of a goddess on it, and takes his leave to worship Vishnu.","cl":[],"ag":["மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்",""],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:150-165":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":155,"t":["மாமறை யோன்வாய் வழித்திறம் கேட்ட","காவுந்தி யையையோர் கட்டுரை சொல்லும்","நலம்புரி கொள்கை நான்மறை யாள","பிலம்புக வேண்டும் பெற்றி ஈங்கில்லை","கப்பத் திந்திரன் காட்டிய நூலின்","மெய்ப்பாட் டியற்கையின் விளங்கக் காணாய்","இறந்த பிறப்பின் எய்திய வெல்லாம்","பிறந்த பிறப்பிற் காணா யோநீ","வாய்மையின் வழாது மன்னுயி ரோம்புநர்க்கு","யாவது முண்டோ எய்தா அரும்பொருள்","காமுறு தெய்வங் கண்டடி பணிய","நீபோ யாங்களும் நீள்நெறிப் படர்குதும்","என்றம் மறையோற் கிசைமொழி யுணர்த்திக்","குன்றாக் கொள்கைக் கோவலன் றன்னுடன்","அன்றைப் பகலோர் அரும்பதித் தங்கிப்","பின்றையும் அவ்வழிப் பெயர்ந்துசெல் வழிநாட்"],"p":"","r":"Kavunthi answers the brahmin that there is no need to enter the cave: does he not see clearly, by the book that the Indra of the heavens taught, that what was gained in past births is seen in this one, and is there any rare thing beyond the reach of those who never swerve from truth and who protect living beings; let him go and worship the god he loves, and they will take their long road; so the travellers part from him, rest that day in a town and go on.","n":"She does not deny the pools: she says their ends are reached by truth and care for life, and answers Bhavakaarini's promise of one's former birth (line 101) with the claim that past births show in this one. She then sends the brahmin courteously to his own god. கப்பத்து இந்திரன் (line 154) seems to be the Indra of a heaven (கற்பம்) and the book a scripture; which book is not said. The narrator calls her reply a கட்டுரை, a weighty word, which I take as endorsement.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","kollaamai","piravi","theerththam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 150-165","lb":"SI11:150-165","ev":"Kavunthi tells the brahmin the cave is unnecessary and sends him to his god; the travellers rest a day in a town and go on.","cl":[["what one gained in past births can be known in this birth, as a scripture taught by Indra shows, without entering the cave","jain","asserted",157,"kavunthi"],["nothing is beyond the reach of those who never swerve from truth and who protect living beings","general","asserted",159,"kavunthi"]],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","மாங்காட்டு மறையோன்"],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:166-191":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":156,"t":["கருந்தடங் கண்ணியும் கவுந்தி யடிகளும்","வகுந்துசெல் வருத்தத்து வழிமருங் கிருப்ப","இடைநெறிக் கிடந்த இயவுகொள் மருங்கின்","புடைநெறிப் போயோர் பொய்கையிற் சென்று","நீர்நசைஇ வேட்கையின் நெடுந்துறை நிற்பக்","கானுறை தெய்வம் காதலிற் சென்று","நயந்த காதலின் நல்குவன் இவனென","வயந்த மாலை வடிவில் தோன்றிக்","கொடிநடுக் குற்றது போல ஆங்கவன்","அடிமுதல் வீழ்ந்தாங் கருங்கணீர் உகுத்து","வாச மாலையின் எழுதிய மாற்றம்","தீதிலேன் பிழைமொழி செப்பினை யாதலின்","கோவலன் செய்தான் கொடுமையென் றென்முன்","மாதவி மயங்கி வான்துய ருற்று","மேலோ ராயினும் நூலோ ராயினும்","பால்வகை தெரிந்த பகுதியோ ராயினும்","பிணியெனக் கொண்டு பிறக்கிட் டொழியும்","கணிகையர் வாழ்க்கை கடையே போன்மெனச்","செவ்வரி ஒழுகிய செழுங்கடை மழைக்கண்","வெண்முத் துதிர்த்து வெண்ணிலாத் திகழும்","தண்முத் தொருகாழ் தன்கையாற் பரிந்து","துனியுற் றென்னையுந் துறந்தன ளாதலின்","மதுரை மூதூர் மாநகர்ப் போந்தது","எதிர்வழிப் பட்டோர் எனக்காங் குரைப்பச்","சாத்தொடு போந்து தனித்துயர் உழந்தேன்","பாத்தரும் பண்பநின் பணிமொழி யாதென"],"p":"","r":"While Kannaki and Kavunthi rest by the road, Kovalan goes by a side path to a pool, thirsty, and the forest goddess, thinking he will yield to love, appears in the form of Vasantamalai, falls at his feet weeping and tells him that over the message written in the garland Madhavi, distraught and saying Kovalan had been cruel, declared the courtesan's life the lowest, one that the noble, the learned and the discerning cast off as a disease, tore off her string of pearls and in anger dismissed her too, so that she came with a caravan to Madurai and now asks his command.","n":"The goddess's story takes up the thread left loose in the Venil kaathai, where Kovalan refused Madhavi's garland letter (8:94-118), and puts in Madhavi's mouth a verdict on her own calling which the later story has her live out in renouncing it. Lines 176 to 177 (தீதிலேன் பிழைமொழி செப்பினை) are hard to divide between Madhavi's words and the maid's; I read them as Madhavi blaming the maid for her message.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","anangu","varaivinmagalir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 166-191","lb":"SI11:166-191","ev":"The forest goddess, in the form of Madhavi's maid Vasantamalai, meets Kovalan at a pool and tells him Madhavi has cast her off.","cl":[["the courtesan's life is the lowest, one the noble, the learned and the discerning cast off as a disease","general","reported",183,"vanasaarini"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்","வனசாரிணி"],"ec":[],"spk":["வனசாரிணி","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:192-202":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":157,"t":["மயக்குந் தெய்வமிவ் வன்காட் டுண்டென","வியத்தகு மறையோன் விளம்பின னாதலின்","வஞ்சம் பெயர்க்கும் மந்திரத் தால்இவ்","ஐஞ்சி லோதியை அறிகுவன் யானெனக்","கோவலன் நாவிற் கூறிய மந்திரம்","பாய்கலைப் பாவை மந்திர மாதலின்","வனசா ரிணியான் மயக்கஞ் செய்தேன்","புனமயிற் சாயற்கும் புண்ணிய முதல்விக்கும்","என்திறம் உரையா தேகென் றேகத்","தாமரைப் பாசடைத் தண்ணீர் கொணர்ந் தாங்கு","அயாவுறு மடந்தை அருந்துயர் தீர்த்து"],"p":"","r":"Because the brahmin had said a deluding goddess haunts this forest, Kovalan resolves to know the woman by the mantra that undoes deceit, and since the mantra he speaks is that of the goddess Paaykalaippaavai, the spirit confesses that she is Vanasaarini who deluded him, begs him not to tell his wife or the holy lady, and goes; Kovalan then brings cool water in a lotus leaf and relieves Kannaki's thirst.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vanjam","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 192-202","lb":"SI11:192-202","ev":"Kovalan unmasks the goddess with a mantra; she confesses and leaves, and he brings water to Kannaki.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","வனசாரிணி","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:15"},"si:11:203-216":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":158,"t":["மீதுசெல் வெங்கதிர் வெம்மையின் தொடங்கத்","தீதியல் கானஞ் செலவரி தென்று","கோவலன் றன்னொடும் கொடுங்குழை மாதொடும்","மாதவத் தாட்டியும் மயங்கதர் அழுவத்துக்","குரவமும் மரவமும் கோங்கமும் வேங்கையும்","விரவிய பூம்பொழில் விளங்கிய இருக்கை","ஆரிடை யத்தத் தியங்குந ரல்லது","மாரி வளம்பெறா வில்லேர் உழவர்","கூற்றுறழ் முன்பொடு கொடுவில் ஏந்தி","வேற்றுப்புலம் போகிநல் வெற்றங் கொடுத்துக்","கழிபே ராண்மைக் கடன்பார்த் திருக்கும்","விழிநுதற் குமரி விண்ணோர் பாவை","மையறு சிறப்பின் வான நாடி","ஐயைதன் கோட்டம் அடைந்தனர் ஆங்கென்."],"p":"","r":"As the sun climbs hot and the forest grows hard to cross, Kovalan, Kannaki and the ascetic come through the bewildering waste to the shrine of Aiyai in a grove of kuravam, maravam, kongu and vengai, the virgin goddess with the eye in her brow, to whom the bow-ploughing hunters, who get no wealth from rain but only from those who cross the waste, bring the spoils of raids on other lands as they wait on the duty of great valour.","n":"Lines 209 to 210 say the hunters' only harvest is the traveller: they plough with the bow. The travellers reach the shrine of the people who live by the road's danger, which sets up the next kaathai, the hunters' song.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","maram","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 11, lines 203-216","lb":"SI11:203-216","ev":"The three travellers reach the shrine of the hunters' goddess Aiyai as the midday heat begins.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","கண்ணகி","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:15"},"si:12:1-19":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":159,"t":["கடுங்கதிர் திருகலின் நடுங்கஞர் எய்தி","ஆறுசெல் வருத்தத்துச் சீறடி சிவப்ப","நறும்பல் கூந்தல் குறும்பல உயிர்த் தாங்கு","ஐயை கோட்டத் தெய்யா வொருசிறை","வருந்துநோய் தணிய இருந்தனர் உப்பால்","வழங்குவில் தடக்கை மறக்குடித் தாயத்துப்","பழங்கட னுற்ற முழங்குவாய்ச் சாலினி","தெய்வ முற்று மெய்ம்மயிர் நிறுத்துக்","கையெடுத் தோச்சிக் கானவர் வியப்ப","இடுமுள் வேலி எயினர்கூட் டுண்ணும்","நடுவூர் மன்றத் தடிபெயர்த் தாடிக்","கல்லென் பேரூர்க் கணநிரை சிறந்தன","வல்வில் எயினர் மன்றுபாழ் பட்டன","மறக்குடித் தாயத்து வழிவளஞ் சுரவாது","அறக்குடி போலவிந் தடங்கினர் எயினரும்","கலையமர் செல்வி கடனுணின் அல்லது","சிலையமர் வென்றி கொடுப்போ ளல்லள்","மட்டுண் வாழ்க்கை வேண்டுதி ராயின்","கட்டுண் மாக்கள் கடந்தரும் எனவாங்கு"],"p":"","r":"Footsore from the road under the scorching sun, Kannaki rests with her husband in a corner of Aiyai's shrine, while Saalini, a woman of the hunters' fighting clan bound to its old duty to the goddess, is seized by the deity, dances in the village common and says that the goddess of the stag gives victory to the bow only when her due is paid, since the hunters' cattle-raids have failed and they have fallen quiet.","n":"Lines 12-15 set the cause of the rite: the herds in the big villages thrive, the hunters' common lies waste, and the fighting clan, its raiding yielding nothing, has subsided 'like a clan of dharma' (அறக்குடி போல, line 15), so the poet makes peace the hunters' misfortune. Lines 16-19 are Saalini's words, closed by எனவாங்கு; the sense of 18-19 (மட்டுண் வாழ்க்கை, கட்டுண் மாக்கள் கடந்தரும்) is not secure, but the demand is plain: the goddess's debt (கடன்) must be paid before she gives victory.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kotravai","maram","vazhipaadu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 1-19","lb":"SI12:1-19","ev":"Kovalan and Kannaki rest at Aiyai's shrine, and Saalini, possessed by the goddess, dances in the hunters' common and says the goddess will give victory only when her due is paid.","cl":[["the goddess of the stag grants victory in war only to those who pay her due offering","general","reported",16,"saalini"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","கோவலன்","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","சாலினி","எயினர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:20-44":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":160,"t":["இட்டுத் தலையெண்ணும் எயின ரல்லது","சுட்டுத் தலைபோகாத் தொல்குடிக் குமரியைச்","சிறுவெள் ளரவின் குருளைநாண் சுற்றிக்","குறுநெறிக் கூந்தல் நெடுமுடி கட்டி","இளைசூழ் படப்பை இழுக்கிய வேனத்து","வளைவெண் கோடு பறித்து மற்றது","முளைவெண் திங்க ளென்னச் சாத்தி","மறங்கொள் வயப்புலி வாய்பிளந்து பெற்ற","மாலை வெண்பல் தாலிநிரை பூட்டி","வரியும் புள்ளியு மயங்கு வான்புறத்து","உரிவை மேகலை உடீஇப் பரிவொடு","கருவில் வாங்கிக் கையகத்துக் கொடுத்துத்","திரிதரு கோட்டுக் கலைமே லேற்றிப்","பாவையுங் கிளியுந் தூவி அஞ்சிறைக்","கானக் கோழியும் நீனிற மஞ்ஞையும்","பந்துங் கழங்குந் தந்தனர் பரசி","வண்ணமுஞ் சுண்ணமுந் தண்ணறுஞ் சாந்தமும்","புழுக்கலும் நோலையும் விழுக்குடை மடையும்","பூவும் புகையும் மேவிய விரையும்","ஏவல் எயிற்றியர் ஏந்தினர் பின்வர","ஆறெறி பறையுஞ் சூறைச் சின்னமும்","கோடும் குழலும் பீடுகெழு மணியும்","கணங்கொண்டு துவைப்ப அணங்குமுன் னிறீஇ","விலைப்பலி உண்ணும் மலர்பலி பீடிகைக்","கலைப்பரி ஊர்தியைக் கைதொழு தேத்தி"],"p":"","r":"The hunters take a maiden of their old unyielding clan, bind her hair with a young white snake, set a boar's curved tusk on it like the crescent moon, hang a row of tiger's teeth on her as a tali, gird her with a striped and spotted skin, put a bow in her hand and seat her on a stag with twisted horns, and, with dolls, parrots, jungle fowl, peacocks, balls and play-stones laid before her and the women of the clan carrying colours, powders, sandal, boiled grain, sesame cakes, rich rice, flowers and incense behind, they sound drums, horns, flutes and bells and worship the goddess of the stag at her altar of bought offerings.","n":"The unit turns a hunter girl into the goddess by costume: each item (snake, boar tusk as crescent, tiger teeth, skin girdle, bow, stag mount) is one of the goddess's emblems that the later songs give back to the goddess herself (lines 58-62, 99-104). The opening phrase (இட்டுத் தலையெண்ணும் எயினரல்லது சுட்டுத் தலைபோகா, lines 20-21) praises the girl's clan as one that never bows its head to any but its own, but the exact sense of the two head-phrases is not secure. விலைப்பலி (line 43) is an offering given in return, as a price, which fits Saalini's talk of the goddess's due.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kotravai","vazhipaadu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 20-44","lb":"SI12:20-44","ev":"The hunters dress a maiden of their clan as the goddess, seat her on a stag, and with offerings, drums and horns worship the goddess at her altar.","cl":[],"ag":["எயினர்","தொல்குடிக் குமரி","எயிற்றியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:45-53":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":161,"t":["இணைமலர்ச் சீறடி இனைந்தனள் வருந்திக்","கணவனோ டிருந்த மணமலி கூந்தலை","இவளோ, கொங்கச் செல்வி குடமலை யாட்டி","தென்றமிழ்ப் பாவை செய்த தவக்கொழுந்து","ஒருமா மணியாய் உலகிற் கோங்கிய","திருமா மணியெனத் தெய்வமுற் றுரைப்பப்","பேதுறவு மொழிந்தனள் மூதறி வாட்டியென்று","அரும்பெறற் கணவன் பெரும்புறத் தொடுங்கி","விருந்தின் மூரல் அரும்பினள் நிற்ப"],"p":"","r":"As Kannaki, her small feet aching, sits with her husband, the possessed Saalini points to her and says that this woman is the goddess of Kongu and of the western hills, the shoot of the penance done by the Tamil maiden, a single great jewel risen to light the world; Kannaki, thinking the old wise woman has spoken in confusion, shrinks behind her husband's broad back with the shy smile of a stranger.","n":"This is the first time in the story anyone calls Kannaki divine, and it comes from a possessed hunter woman in a desert shrine, not from the great. Kannaki's own judgement is that the words are பேதுறவு, bewilderment (line 51); the story will prove the priestess right. The three regions she names (கொங்கு, குடமலை, தென்றமிழ், lines 47-48) are where Kannaki will later be worshipped, so the speech reads as prophecy, though the unit itself does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","naanam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 45-53","lb":"SI12:45-53","ev":"Possessed, Saalini hails Kannaki as a great jewel born for the world, and Kannaki, taking the words for confusion, hides behind her husband and smiles.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணகி","கோவலன்","சாலினி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாலினி",""],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:54-74":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":162,"t":["மதியின் வெண்தோடு சூடுஞ் சென்னி","நுதல்கிழித்து விழித்த இமையா நாட்டத்துப்","பவள வாய்ச்சி தவளவாள் நகைச்சி","நஞ்சுண்டு கறுத்த கண்டி வெஞ்சினத்து","அரவுநாண் பூட்டி நெடுமலை வளைத்தோள்","துளையெயிற் றுரகக் கச்சுடை முலைச்சி","வளையுடைக் கையிற் சூல மேந்தி","கரியின் உரிவை போர்த் தணங் காகிய","அரியின் உரிவை மேகலை யாட்டி","சிலம்புங் கழலும் புலம்புஞ் சீறடி","வலம்படு கொற்றத்து வாய்வாட் கொற்றவை","இரண்டுவே றுருவில் திரண்டதோள் அவுணன்","தலைமிசை நின்ற தையல் பலர்தொழும்","அமரி குமரி கவுரி சமரி","சூலி நீலி மாலவற் கிளங்கிளை","ஐயை செய்யவள் வெய்யவாள் தடக்கைப்","பாய்கலைப் பாவை பைந்தொடிப் பாவை","ஆய்கலைப் பாவை அருங்கலப் பாவை","தமர்தொழ வந்த குமரிக் கோலத்து","அமரிளங் குமரியும் அருளினள்","வரியுறு செய்கை வாய்ந்ததா லெனவே;"],"p":"","r":"The goddess is hailed in a long chain of names: she wears the crescent, has the unwinking eye in her split forehead, coral lips and white teeth, a throat dark from the poison, bends the great mountain with a snake for string, wears a cobra as breast band, holds the trident, wears the elephant's hide and the lion's skin, is Kotravai of the true sword, the maiden standing on the head of the two-formed asura, Amari, Kumari, Gauri, Samari, Shooli, Neeli, the younger kin of Mal, Aiyai, the red one, the doll of the leaping stag; and it is declared that she, in the maiden's guise come for her people to worship, has given her grace and the vari rite has borne fruit.","n":"The epithets fuse the hunters' war goddess with Shiva (crescent, forehead eye, dark throat, bending Meru with a snake, elephant hide), with the slayer of the buffalo-demon (line 65-66) and with Vishnu, whose younger kin she is called (மாலவற் கிளங்கிளை, line 68). The unit ends with எனவே, so these are someone's words, most likely the hunters' or Saalini's; the text does not name the speaker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kotravai","vazhipaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 54-74","lb":"SI12:54-74","ev":"The goddess in the guise of a maiden is declared to have given her grace, and the vari rite is said to have succeeded.","cl":[],"ag":["கொற்றவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்",""],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:75-78":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":163,"t":["நாகம் நாறு நரந்தம் நிரந்தன","ஆவும் ஆரமும் ஓங்கின எங்கணும்","சேவும் மாவும் செறிந்தன கண்ணுதல்","பாகம் ஆளுடை யாள்பலி முன்றிலே;"],"p":"","r":"The first song of the court: before the shrine of the goddess who rules half the body of the forehead-eyed god, nagam and narantham spread their scent, aa and sandal trees grow tall everywhere, and red deer and other beasts crowd together.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kotravai","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 75-78","lb":"SI12:75-78","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்",""],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:79-82":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":164,"t":["செம்பொன் வேங்கை சொரிந்தன சேயிதழ்","கொம்பர் நல்லில வங்கள் குவிந்தன","பொங்கர் வெண்பொரி சிந்தின புன்கிளந்","திங்கள் வாழ்சடை யாள்திரு முன்றிலே;"],"p":"","r":"The second song: before the shrine of the goddess whose matted hair holds the moon, the vengai sheds its golden flowers, the red-petalled silk-cotton heaps its blossoms and the punku scatters white puffed-rice flowers.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kotravai","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 79-82","lb":"SI12:79-82","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்",""],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:83-86":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":165,"t":["மரவம் பாதிரி புன்னை மணங்கமழ்","குரவம் கோங்கம் மலர்ந்தன கொம்பர்மேல்","அரவ வண்டினம் ஆர்த்துடன் யாழ்செய்யும்","திருவ மாற்கிளை யாள்திரு முன்றிலே;"],"p":"","r":"The third song: before the shrine of the goddess who is the kin of Tirumal, maravam, paathiri, punnai, kuravam and kongam bloom on their branches and swarms of bees hum together like a yazh.","n":"The three refrains give the goddess's court to three figures in turn: Shiva's half (line 78), the one with the moon in her matted hair (line 82), and the kin of Mal (line 86), so the court of the forest goddess is placed between the two great gods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kotravai","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 83-86","lb":"SI12:83-86","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்",""],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:87-90":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":166,"t":["கொற்றவை கொண்ட அணிகொண்டு நின்றவிப்","பொற்றொடி மாதர் தவமென்னை கொல்லோ","பொற்றொடி மாதர் பிறந்த குடிப்பிறந்த","விற்றொழில் வேடர் குலனே குலனும்;"],"p":"","r":"The singers ask what penance this golden-bangled girl did, that she stands wearing Kotravai's own adornment, and answer that the clan of bowmen hunters in which she was born is the clan among clans.","n":"The question (தவமென்னை கொல்லோ) treats the girl's present glory as the fruit of some penance, and the answer moves the credit from her to her birth-clan: the girl's merit makes the hunters' clan the first of clans. The same pattern is sung twice more with different names for goddess and clan (91-94, 95-98).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kudi","kotravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 87-90","lb":"SI12:87-90","ev":"","cl":[["a clan becomes the first of clans through a daughter born in it who bears the goddess's form","general","reported",90,"eyinar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்",""],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:91-94":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":167,"t":["ஐயை திருவின் அணிகொண்டு நின்றவிப்","பையர வல்குல் தவமென்னை கொல்லோ","பையர வல்குல் பிறந்த குடிப்பிறந்த","எய்வில் எயினர் குலனே குலனும்;"],"p":"","r":"The second stanza asks what penance this girl did, that she stands wearing the adornment of Aiyai, and answers that the clan of the Eyinar of the shooting bow, in which she was born, is the clan among clans.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kudi","kotravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 91-94","lb":"SI12:91-94","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்",""],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:95-98":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":168,"t":["பாய்கலைப் பாவை அணிகொண்டு நின்றவிவ்","ஆய்தொடி நல்லாள் தவமென்னை கொல்லோ","ஆய்தொடி நல்லாள் பிறந்த குடிப்பிறந்த","வேய்வில் எயினர் குலனே குலனும்;"],"p":"","r":"The third stanza asks what penance this well-bangled girl did, that she stands wearing the adornment of the doll of the leaping stag, and answers that the clan of the Eyinar with bamboo bows, in which she was born, is the clan among clans.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kudi","kotravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 95-98","lb":"SI12:95-98","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்",""],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:99-102":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":169,"t":["ஆனைத்தோல் போர்த்துப் புலியின் உரியுடுத்துக்","கானத் தெருமைக் கருந்தலைமேல் நின்றாயால்","வானோர் வணங்க மறைமேல் மறையாகி","ஞானக் கொழுந்தாய் நடுக்கின்றி யேநிற்பாய்;"],"p":"","r":"The singers tell the goddess that she stands on the black head of the forest buffalo, wrapped in an elephant's hide and girt with a tiger's skin, and that, worshipped by the gods, she stands unshaken as the secret beyond the Vedas and the shoot of wisdom.","n":"The same stanza joins the cult image (the buffalo's head under her feet, the hides) to the highest doctrinal title: மறைமேல் மறை, the mystery above the Veda. Elephant hide and tiger skin are Shiva's emblems elsewhere, here worn by the goddess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kotravai","vedham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 99-102","lb":"SI12:99-102","ev":"","cl":[["the goddess is the hidden truth above the Vedas and the shoot of wisdom","saiva","reported",101,"eyinar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","கொற்றவை"],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:103-106":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":170,"t":["வரிவளைக்கை வாளேந்தி மாமயிடற் செற்றுக்","கரியதிரி கோட்டுக் கலைமிசைமேல் நின்றாயால்","அரியரன்பூ மேலோன் அகமலர்மேல் மன்னும்","விரிகதிரஞ் சோதி விளக்காகி யேநிற்பாய்;"],"p":"","r":"The singers tell the goddess that, sword in her bangled hand, she killed the great buffalo-demon and stands on the black stag with twisted horns, and that she abides as the light of spreading rays in the heart-lotus of Hari, Hara and the one on the flower.","n":"The claim places the goddess as the light within all three gods, Vishnu, Shiva and Brahma alike, so she is not ranked under Shiva here; the saiva tag on the claim follows the cycle's other stanzas (99, 109), which make her Shiva's, and should be read with that caution.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kotravai","oli","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 103-106","lb":"SI12:103-106","ev":"","cl":[["the goddess abides as the light in the hearts of Hari, Hara and Brahma","saiva","reported",105,"eyinar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","கொற்றவை"],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:107-110":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":171,"t":["சங்கமும் சக்கரமும் தாமரைக் கையேந்திச்","செங்கண் அரிமால் சினவிடைமேல் நின்றாயால்","கங்கை முடிக்கணிந்த கண்ணுதலோன் பாகத்து","மங்கை உருவாய் மறையேத்த வேநிற்பாய்;"],"p":"","r":"The singers tell the goddess that, holding conch, discus and lotus in her hands, she stands on the angry bull of the red-eyed one, and that she stands as the woman's form in the share of the forehead-eyed god who wears the Ganga in his hair, praised by the Veda.","n":"The goddess carries Vishnu's conch and discus while standing as the left half of Shiva, so one stanza gives her both great gods. The phrase செங்கண் அரிமால் சினவிடை (line 108) can be read as the angry bull or as a lion with Mal's name; the mount is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kotravai","saththi","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 107-110","lb":"SI12:107-110","ev":"","cl":[["the goddess is the woman's form in the half of Shiva's body, praised by the Veda","saiva","reported",110,"eyinar"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","கொற்றவை"],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:111-115":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":172,"t":["ஆங்குக்,","கொன்றையுந் துளவமும் குழுமத் தொடுத்த","துன்று மலர்ப்பிணையல் தோள்மே லிட்டாங்கு","அசுரர் வாட அமரர்க் காடிய","குமரிக் கோலத்துக் கூத்துள் படுமே;"],"p":"","r":"Thus, with a thick garland of kondrai and tulasi flowers woven together laid on her shoulders, the maiden enters the dance that the goddess in maiden form danced for the gods when the asuras withered.","n":"The garland joins Shiva's kondrai and Vishnu's tulasi, as the songs before it joined the two gods' emblems. Whether the subject of கூத்துள் படுமே is the dressed hunter girl entering the dance, or the song itself counting as part of the goddess's dance, is not clear from the lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["koothu","kotravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 111-115","lb":"SI12:111-115","ev":"Garlanded with kondrai and tulasi, the dressed maiden enters the goddess's victory dance.","cl":[],"ag":["தொல்குடிக் குமரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:16"},"si:12:116-119":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":173,"t":["ஆய்பொன் னரிச்சிலம்பும் சூடகமும் மேகலையும் ஆர்ப்ப வார்ப்ப","மாயஞ்செய் வாளவுணர் வீழநங்கை மரக்கான்மேல் வாளமலை யாடும் போலும்","மாயஞ்செய் வாளவுணர் வீழநங்கை மரக்கான்மேல் வாளமலை யாடுமாயின்","காயாமலர்மேனி யேத்திவானோர் கைபெய் மலர்மாரி காட்டும் போலும்;"],"p":"","r":"The hunters sing that the goddess, her fine anklets, wristlets and girdle ringing, seems to dance the victory sword-dance on stilts as the deceiving asuras fall, and that the gods, praising her body dark as the kaya flower, seem to shower flowers from their hands.","n":"Every line ends in போலும் (it seems): the singers describe the costumed girl of the rite as if she were the goddess dancing her battle, so the song is a seeing-as rather than a report. The dance on stilts (மரக்கால்) over the falling asuras is the myth's dance of victory; the dark body (காயா மலர்மேனி) ties her to Mal, whose younger kin the first half of the kaathai calls her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","maram","saththi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 116-119","lb":"SI12:116-119","ev":"The goddess dances on stilts as the asuras fall and the gods shower flowers on her.","cl":[],"ag":["கொற்றவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்",""],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:120-123":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":174,"t":["உட்குடைச் சீறூ ரொருமகன்ஆ னிரைகொள்ள உற்ற காலை","வெட்சி மலர்புனைய வெள்வா ளுழத்தியும் வேண்டும் போலும்","வெட்சி மலர்புனைய வெள்வா ளுழத்தியும் வேண்டின் வேற்றூர்க்","கட்சியுட் காரி கடிய குரலிசைத்துக் காட்டும் போலும்;"],"p":"","r":"When a man of the small village inside the forest sets out to seize cattle, the goddess who plies the white sword seems to want to wear the vetchi flower too, and if she does, the kaari bird in the enemy village's forest seems to cry its harsh omen.","n":"The goddess takes the raider's own badge, the vetchi flower of cattle seizure, so the raid becomes her act; the omen bird crying in the enemy's grove (கட்சி) is the sign of their loss. The song turns on condition (வேண்டின்): her wish is what makes the omen fall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","maram","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 120-123","lb":"SI12:120-123","ev":"A hunter sets out to seize cattle and the goddess takes the vetchi with him while an omen bird cries in the enemy's grove.","cl":[["the goddess takes up the raider's vetchi, and her will shows itself in omens over the enemy's land","general","reported",121,"eyinar"]],"ag":["கொற்றவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்",""],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:124-127":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":175,"t":["கள்விலை யாட்டி மறுப்பப் பொறாமறவன் கைவில் ஏந்திப்","புள்ளும் வழிப்படரப் புல்லார் நிரைகருதிப் போகும் போலும்","புள்ளும் வழிப்படரப் புல்லார் நிரை கருதிப் போகுங் காலைக்","கொள்ளும் கொடியெடுத்துக் கொற்றவையும் கொடுமரமுன் செல்லும் போலும்"],"p":"","r":"When the woman who sells toddy refuses him, the warrior who cannot bear it takes his bow and goes, with birds leading the way, to seize the cattle of his foes, and as he goes the goddess of victory seems to raise her banner and walk before his bow.","n":"The raid starts from a small want: the toddy seller will not pour (மறுப்ப), so the hunter goes for cattle to pay her. The song sets this trade motive and the goddess's banner side by side without embarrassment; the birds on the road (புள்ளும் வழிப்படர) are the omens of the way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","kal","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 124-127","lb":"SI12:124-127","ev":"Refused toddy by the toddy seller, a hunter takes his bow and goes to raid cattle, and the goddess goes before him with her banner.","cl":[["the goddess of victory walks before the raider's bow when he goes for the enemy's cattle","general","reported",127,"eyinar"]],"ag":["கள்விலை யாட்டி","எயினர்","கொற்றவை"],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்",""],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:128-131":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":176,"t":["இளமா எயிற்றி இவைகாண் நின் னையர்","தலைநாளை வேட்டத்துத் தந்தநல் ஆனிரைகள்","கொல்லன் துடியன் கொளைபுணர் சீர்வல்ல","நல்லியாழ்ப் பாணர்தம் முன்றில் நிறைந்தன;"],"p":"","r":"The singers bid the young hunter woman look: the fine herds her brothers brought back from yesterday's raid now fill the yards of the smith, the drummer and the skilled yazh-playing bards.","n":"The spoil is not kept: it goes at once to the craftsmen and musicians of the raid (the smith who makes the weapons, the துடி drummer, the bards). The address to the girl (இளமா எயிற்றி) makes her the witness of her brothers' valour shown as giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 128-131","lb":"SI12:128-131","ev":"The herds seized in the raid are given out and fill the yards of the smith, the drummer and the bards.","cl":[],"ag":["எயினர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","எயிற்றி"],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:132-135":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":177,"t":["முருந்தேர் இளநகை காணாய்நின் னையர்","கரந்தை யலறக் கவர்ந்த இனநிரைகள்","கள்விலை யாட்டிநல் வேய்தெரி கானவன்","புள்வாய்ப்புச் சொன்னகணி முன்றில் நிறைந்தன;"],"p":"","r":"Again the young woman is bidden to look: the herds her brothers seized while the karanthai men cried out fill the yards of the toddy seller, the forest man who picks good bamboo, and the augur who read the bird omen.","n":"The list pays the debts of the raid one by one: the toddy seller who refused credit in lines 124-127 is now paid in cattle, and the augur (கணி) is paid for the omen that sent them out. கரந்தை here names the defenders of the herd by their flower.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","eegai","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 132-135","lb":"SI12:132-135","ev":"The seized herds fill the yards of the toddy seller, the bamboo-cutter and the augur.","cl":[],"ag":["எயினர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","எயிற்றி"],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:136-140":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":178,"t":["கயமல ருண்கண்ணாய் காணாய்நின் னையர்","அயலூர் அலற எறிந்தநல் ஆனிரைகள்","நயனில் மொழியின் நரைமுது தாடி","எயினர் எயிற்றியர் முன்றில் நிறைந்தன;","துறைப்பாட்டுமடை."],"p":"","r":"A third time the young woman is bidden to look: the herds her brothers drove off while the neighbouring village wailed fill the yards of the grey-bearded old Eyinar and Eyinar women, and a rubric marks the songs to the goddess that follow.","n":"The third song ends with the elders of the hunters' own kin; the neighbouring village's wail (அயலூர் அலற) is the price, stated plainly. Line 138 (நயனில் மொழியின்) is hard: it may describe the old men's rough speech; the sense is not secure. Line 140, துறைப்பாட்டுமடை, is a rubric, not a verse line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","eegai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 136-140","lb":"SI12:136-140","ev":"The herds seized from a neighbouring village fill the yards of the old Eyinar men and women.","cl":[],"ag":["எயினர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","எயிற்றி"],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:141-144":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":179,"t":["சுடரொடு திரிதரு முனிவரும் அமரரும்","இடர்கெட அருளும்நின் இணையடி தொழுதேம்","அடல்வலி எயினர்நின் அடிதொடு கடனிது","மிடறுகு குருதிகொள் விறல்தரு விலையே;"],"p":"","r":"The hunters say they worship the goddess's paired feet, which grant grace to the sages and gods who wander with the sun so their distress ends, and that this is the due the mighty Eyinar owe as they touch her feet: blood flowing from the throat, the price of the victory she gives.","n":"The hymn joins the goddess's grace (அருளும்) and the price of blood (விலையே) in one stanza: victory is a purchase and blood the payment. Whose throat bleeds is not said: an animal's, a captive's, or the hunters' own offered blood; I leave it open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","bali","kolai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 141-144","lb":"SI12:141-144","ev":"The hunters touch the goddess's feet and offer throat-blood as their due for victory.","cl":[["the victory the goddess grants is bought with blood offered to her as the hunters' due","general","reported",144,"eyinar"]],"ag":["எயினர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","கொற்றவை"],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:145-148":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":180,"t":["அணிமுடி அமரர்தம் அரசொடு பணிதரு","மணியுரு வினைநின மலரடி தொழுதேம்","கணநிறை பெறுவிறல் எயினிடு கடனிது","நிணனுகு குருதிகொள் நிகரடு விலையே;"],"p":"","r":"The hunters worship her flower feet, jewel-formed, to which the crowned gods and their king bow, and say this is the due offered by the victorious Eyinar who come in a full band: blood with fat dripping from it, the price that crushes their rivals.","n":"The second stanza repeats the first's frame (தொழுதேம் ... கடனிது ... விலையே) with the gods' king in place of the sages, and fat (நிணன்) added to the blood: the offering is flesh as well as blood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","bali","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 145-148","lb":"SI12:145-148","ev":"The hunters offer blood and fat at the goddess's feet as the price of victory over rivals.","cl":[],"ag":["எயினர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","கொற்றவை"],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:149-152":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":181,"t":["துடியொடு சிறுபறை வயிரொடு துவைசெய","வெடிபட வருபவர் எயினர்கள் அரையிருள்","அடுபுலி யனையவர் குமரிநின் அடிதொடு","படுகடன் இதுவுகு பலிமுக மடையே;"],"p":"","r":"The Eyinar who come bursting out at midnight like killing tigers, with drum, small drum and horn sounding, touch the maiden goddess's feet, and this, they say, is the due they owe: the offering of food at the head of the bali.","n":"The night raid and the night offering are the same band in the same noise: the drums that sound for the attack (வெடிபட வருபவர்) sound for the rite. படுகடன், the debt that has fallen due, makes the bali an obligation rather than a gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["bali","vazhipaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 149-152","lb":"SI12:149-152","ev":"The hunters come at midnight with drums and lay the bali at the goddess's feet.","cl":[["the bali is a debt the hunters owe the goddess, not a gift they may withhold","general","reported",152,"eyinar"]],"ag":["எயினர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","கொற்றவை"],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:153-156":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":182,"t":["வம்பலர் பல்கி வழியும் வளம்பட","அம்புடை வல்வில் எயின்கடன் உண்குவாய்","சங்கரி அந்தரி நீலி சடாமுடிச்","செங்கண் அரவு பிறையுடன் சேர்த்துவாய்;"],"p":"","r":"The hunters pray that travellers may multiply and the road grow rich, and ask the goddess, Sankari, Antari, Neeli, who sets the red-eyed snake and the crescent on her matted crown, to eat the due of the Eyinar of strong bows and arrows.","n":"To a highway robber a road rich with travellers (வம்பலர் பல்கி) is a rich harvest: the prayer for safe, busy roads is, from these singers, a prayer for prey. The goddess carries Siva's snake and crescent, so the hymn makes her his consort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["bali","saththi","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 153-156","lb":"SI12:153-156","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","கொற்றவை"],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:157-160":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":183,"t":["துண்ணென் துடியொடு துஞ்சூர் எறிதரு","கண்ணில் எயினர் இடுகடன் உண்குவாய்","விண்ணோர் அமுதுண்டுஞ் சாவ ஒருவரும்","உண்ணாத நஞ்சுண் டிருந்தருள் செய்குவாய்;"],"p":"","r":"They ask her to eat the due of the pitiless Eyinar who fall on sleeping villages with the startling drum, and praise her who, while the gods who ate nectar still die, ate the poison none would eat and sits granting grace.","n":"Two contrasts are packed together: the gods who drank amrita still die, while she who drank poison lives and gives grace; and the hunters who have no eyes for pity (கண்ணில்) are fed by her grace. The poison-swallowing is Siva's act; the hymn gives it to the goddess as his other half.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["bali","saththi","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 157-160","lb":"SI12:157-160","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","கொற்றவை"],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:161-164":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":184,"t":["பொருள்கொண்டு புண்செயி னல்லதை யார்க்கும்","அருளில் எயினர் இடுகடன் உண்குவாய்","மருதின் நடந்துநின் மாமன்செய் வஞ்ச","உருளுஞ் சகடம் உதைத்தருள் செய்குவாய்;"],"p":"","r":"They ask her to eat the due of the graceless Eyinar, who show no mercy to anyone but take his goods and wound him, and praise her who walked between the marutham trees and kicked to pieces the rolling cart of deceit her uncle sent.","n":"The singers call themselves அருளில் (without grace) in the same stanza that praises the goddess's grace (அருள் செய்குவாய்); the hymn does not ask her to change them, only to eat their due. The feats of the marutham trees and the cart sent by the uncle are Krishna's, given to her as Mal's kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["bali","arul","kalavu","saththi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 161-164","lb":"SI12:161-164","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்","கொற்றவை"],"ch":"si:17"},"si:12:165-168":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":185,"t":["மறைமுது முதல்வன் பின்னர் மேய","பொறையுயர் பொதியிற் பொருப்பன் பிறர்நாட்டுக்","கட்சியும் கரந்தையும் பாழ்பட","வெட்சி சூடுக விறல்வெய் யோனே."],"p":"","r":"The vari closes with a blessing: may the hill-lord of high Pothiyil, mighty and eager for victory, wear the vetchi so that the groves and karanthai of other lands go to ruin.","n":"The hunters' raid becomes the king's war: the vetchi they wore for cattle is wished on the Pandya, the lord of Pothiyil, unnamed here. Line 165 (மறைமுது முதல்வன் பின்னர் மேய) is obscure: it may set the king after an ancient first lord, but the reference cannot be fixed. The kaathai that hailed Kannaki as the goddess ends by praying for the victory of the king who will die before her word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["niraikol","vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 12, lines 165-168","lb":"SI12:165-168","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எயினர்",""],"ch":"si:17"},"si:13:1-10":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":186,"t":["பெண்ணணி கோலம் பெயர்ந்தபிற் பாடு","புண்ணிய முதல்வி திருந்தடி பொருந்திக்","கடுங்கதிர் வேனிலிக் காரிகை பொறாஅள்","படிந்தில சீறடி பரல்வெங் கானத்துக்","கோள்வல் உளியமுங் கொடும்புற் றகழா","வாள்வரி வேங்கையும் மான்கணம் மறலா","அரவுஞ் சூரும் இரைதேர் முதலையும்","உருமுஞ் சார்ந்தவர்க் குறுகண் செய்யா","செங்கோல் தென்னவர் காக்கும் நாடென","எங்கணும் போகிய இசையோ பெரிதே"],"p":"","r":"After the forest guise has been put off, someone at the holy lady's feet reasons that this young woman cannot bear the fierce summer sun and her small feet have never trodden the hot pebbled forest, and that the fame has spread everywhere of the land guarded by the Pandyas of the straight sceptre, where the bear does not dig the anthill, the tiger does not attack the deer, and serpent, demon, crocodile and thunder do no harm to those who depend on it.","n":"The safety of the road is given as reputation (இசை, line 10), heard of rather than seen, and it reaches past men to beasts, demons and thunder. The text does not name the speaker: the deliberation runs into the next unit and ends with குரவரும் நேர்ந்த (line 14), the elder's consent, so it is most likely Kovalan proposing night travel to the ascetic. The praise of the Pandya's straight sceptre is set on the threshold of the realm where the Pandya will kill Kovalan without inquiry.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","naadu","vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 13, lines 1-10","lb":"SI13:1-10","ev":"On the road to Madurai the travellers weigh the summer heat against the safety of the Pandya's land before going on.","cl":[["in the land of a king whose sceptre is straight, even wild beasts, demons and thunder do no harm to those who rely on it","general","reported",9,"kovalan"]],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ch":"si:18"},"si:13:11-28":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":187,"t":["பகலொளி தன்னினும் பல்லுயி ரோம்பும்","நிலவொளி விளக்கின் நீளிடை மருங்கின்","இரவிடைக் கழிதற் கேத மில்லெனக்","குரவரும் நேர்ந்த கொள்கையி னமர்ந்து","கொடுங்கோல் வேந்தன் குடிகள் போலப்","படுங்கதி ரமையம் பார்த்திருந் தோர்க்குப்","பன்மீன் தானையொடு பாற்கதிர் பரப்பித்","தென்னவன் குலமுதற் செல்வன் தோன்றித்","தாரகைக் கோவையும் சந்தின் குழம்பும்","சீரிள வனமுலை சேரா தொழியவும்","தாதுசேர் கழுநீர்த் தண்பூம் பிணையல்","போதுசேர் பூங்குழற் பொருந்தா தொழியவும்","பைந்தளிர் ஆரமொடு பல்பூங் குறுமுறி","செந்தளிர் மேனி சேரா தொழியவும்","மலயத் தோங்கி மதுரையின் வளர்ந்து","புலவர் நாவிற் பொருந்திய தென்றலொடு","பானிலா வெண்கதிர் பாவைமேற் சொரிய","வேனில் திங்களும் வேண்டுதி யென்றே"],"p":"","r":"Since the moonlight protects living beings better than daylight and there is no danger in passing the long road by night, the elder agrees, and the travellers, who wait for the hour of sunset like the subjects of a cruel king, see the moon, the founder of the Pandya line, rise with its host of stars spreading milk-white rays, while the Earth asks the summer moon, with the southern breeze born on Malaya and grown in Madurai, to pour its white light on the young woman whom no star-garland, sandal paste, flower garland or leaf-chaplet now touches.","n":"The sun is likened to a cruel king whose subjects wait for him to set (line 15), and the moon that brings relief is the Pandya's own ancestor (line 18): the image of a bad reign and a protecting lineage are set side by side in the passage that admits the travellers to the Pandya land. The Earth's request (lines 19-28) is spoken before she is named in the next unit (பார்மகள், line 29); that the 'doll' (பாவை) is Kannaki, unadorned in her exile, is my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kudi","vendhan","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 13, lines 11-28","lb":"SI13:11-28","ev":"With the elder's consent the travellers wait for sunset and set out by night as the moon rises.","cl":[],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","திங்கள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:18"},"si:13:29-50":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":188,"t":["பார்மகள் அயாவுயிர்த் தடங்கிய பின்னர்","ஆரிடை உழந்த மாதரை நோக்கிக்","கொடுவரி மறுகும் குடிஞை கூப்பிடும்","இடிதரும் உளியமும் இனையா தேகெனத்","தொடிவளைச் செங்கை தோளிற் காட்டி","மறவுரை நீத்த மாசறு கேள்வி","அறவுரை கேட்டாங் காரிடைகழிந்து","வேனல் வீற்றிருந்த வேய்கரி கானத்துக்","கான வாரணங் கதிர்வர வியம்ப","வரிநவில் கொள்கை மறைநூல் வழுக்கத்துப்","புரிநூல் மார்பர் உறைபதிச் சேர்ந்து","மாதவத் தாட்டியொடு காதலி தன்னையோர்","தீதுதீர் சிறப்பின் சிறையகத் திருத்தி","இடுமுள் வேலி நீங்கி ஆங்கோர்","நெடுநெறி மருங்கின் நீர்தலைப் படுவோன்","காதலி தன்னொடு கானகம் போந்ததற்","கூதுலைக் குருகின் உயிர்த்தனன் கலங்கி","உட்புலம் புறுதலின் உருவந் திரியக்","கட்புல மயக்கத்துக் கௌசிகன் தெரியான்","கோவலன் பிரியக் கொடுந்துய ரெய்திய","மாமலர் நெடுங்கண் மாதவி போன்றிவ்","அருந்திறல் வேனிற் கலர்களைந் துடனே"],"p":"","r":"When the Earth has sighed and grown still, Kovalan, seeing his wife toil on the hard road, gives her his shoulder for her bangled hand and bids her not fear the roaming tiger, the calling owl and the growling bear; having heard the ascetic's teaching of dharma, free of harsh words, they pass the road, and at cock-crow reach a village of thread-wearing Brahmins versed in the Vedas, where he leaves his wife with the ascetic in a safe enclosure and goes beyond the thorn fence for water, sighing like a smith's bellows that he has brought her into the forest, so changed by grief that Kausikan does not know him.","n":"The bellows simile (ஊதுலைக் குருகின், line 45) gives Kovalan's grief a cause he owns: he is pained that his wife has come with him into the forest (line 44). The ascetic's teaching is described only as அறவுரை free of the speech of violence (மறவுரை நீத்த, line 34); its content is not given. The last three lines begin Kausikan's address to a creeper, which the next unit completes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","kelvi","aram","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 13, lines 29-50","lb":"SI13:29-50","ev":"Kovalan leads Kannaki through the night forest to a Brahmin village, leaves her with the ascetic and goes for water, unrecognised by Kausikan.","cl":[],"ag":["பார்மகள்","கோவலன்","கண்ணகி","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோசிக மாணி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:18"},"si:13:51-75":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":189,"t":["வருந்தினை போலுநீ மாதவி யென்றோர்","பாசிலைக் குருகின் பந்தரிற் பொருந்தி","கோசிக மாணி கூறக் கேட்டே","யாதுநீ கூறிய உரையீ திங்கெனத்","தீதிலன் கண்டேன் எனச்சென் றெய்திக்","கோசிக மாணி கொள்கையின் உரைப்போன்","இருநிதிக் கிழவனும் பெருமனைக் கிழத்தியும்","அருமணி இழந்த நாகம் போன்றதும்","இன்னுயிர் இழந்த யாக்கை யென்னத்","துன்னிய சுற்றம் துயர்க்கடல் வீழ்ந்ததும்","ஏவ லாளர் யாங்கணுஞ் சென்று","கோவலன் தேடிக் கொணர்கெனப் பெயர்ந்ததும்","பெருமகன் ஏவ லல்ல தியாங்கணும்","அரசே தஞ்சமென் றருங்கான் அடைந்த","அருந்திறல் பிரிந்த அயோத்தி போலப்","பெரும்பெயர் மூதூர் பெரும்பே துற்றதும்","வசந்த மாலைவாய் மாதவி கேட்டுப்","பசந்த மேனியள் படர்நோ யுற்று","நெடுநிலை மாடத் திடைநிலத் தாங்கோர்","படையமை சேக்கைப் பள்ளியுள் வீழ்ந்ததும்","வீழ்துய ருற்றோள் விழுமங் கேட்டுத்","தாழ்துயர் எய்தித் தான்சென் றிருந்ததும்","இருந்துயர் உற்றோள் இணையடி தொழுதேன்","வருந்துயர் நீக்கென மலர்க்கையின் எழுதிக்","கண்மணி யனையாற்குக் காட்டுக வென்றே"],"p":"","r":"Resting in the shade of a green-leaved kurukkatthi arbour, Kausikan addresses the creeper as Madhavi, saying it suffers in the harsh summer with its flowers shed like Madhavi of the long eyes when Kovalan left her; Kovalan asks what he means, and Kausikan, saying he has found him unharmed, tells how Kovalan's father and mother became like serpents that had lost their gem, the kin fell into a sea of grief like a body without life, servants went everywhere to seek him, the great city was bewildered like Ayodhya when the hero left for the forest, and Madhavi, hearing from Vasanthamaalai, grew pale and fell on her couch, and when he went to her in her grief she wrote with her own hand, worshipping Kovalan's feet and asking him to end her sorrow, bidding Kausikan show it to him.","n":"Kausikan's speech to the creeper is a ruse of purpose (கொள்கையின் உரைப்போன், line 56): the creeper's name is the courtesan's, and the word Madhavi draws out the man he cannot recognise. The Ayodhya simile (lines 63-66) sets Puhar's loss of one merchant's son beside the city that lost Rama; the sense of அரசே தஞ்சம் (line 64) in the simile is not clear to me. Kovalan's family and Madhavi grieve in one report, the household and the courtesan side by side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 13, lines 51-75","lb":"SI13:51-75","ev":"Kausikan, sent from Puhar, recognises Kovalan and tells him of his parents' grief, the city's search and Madhavi's collapse and letter.","cl":[],"ag":["கோசிக மாணி","கோவலன்","மாசாத்துவான்","பெருமனைக் கிழத்தி","வசந்த மாலை","மாதவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோசிக மாணி","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:18"},"si:13:76-92":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":190,"t":["மண்ணுடை முடங்கல் மாதவி யீத்ததும்","ஈத்த வோலைகொண் டிடைநெறித் திரிந்து","தீத்திறம் புரிந்தோன் சென்ற தேயமும்","வழிமருங் கிருந்து மாசற உரைத்தாங்கு","அழிவுடை உள்ளத் தாரஞ ராட்டி","போதவிழ் புரிகுழற் பூங்கொடி நங்கை","மாதவி யோலை மலர்க்கையின் நீட்ட","உடனுறை காலத் துரைத்தநெய் வாசம்","குறுநெறிக் கூந்தல் மண்பொறி உணர்த்திக்","காட்டிய தாதலிற் கைவிட லீயான்","ஏட்டகம் விரித் தாங் கெய்திய துணர்வோன்","அடிகள் முன்னர் யானடி வீழ்ந்தேன்","வடியாக் கிளவி மனக்கொளல் வேண்டும்","குரவர்பணி அன்றியுங் குலப்பிறப் பாட்டியோ","டிரவிடைக் கழிதற் கென்பிழைப் பறியாது","கையறு நெஞ்சம் கடியல் வேண்டும்","பொய்தீர் காட்சிப் புரையோய் போற்றி"],"p":"","r":"Kausikan says Madhavi gave him the clay-sealed letter and that he has wandered the roads and lands with it, and when he holds it out, Kovalan, knowing by the scent of the oil from her curled hair pressed into the clay seal the fragrance she spoke of when they lived together, cannot let it go, opens it and reads: she falls at his feet and asks him to take her guileless words to heart, she does not know her fault that made him leave by night with his high-born wife, against the service owed to his elders, and she asks him to remove the confusion of her helpless heart, hailing him as one of flawless sight.","n":"The letter names his elders' claim (குரவர்பணி, line 89) and his wife's birth (குலப்பிறப் பாட்டியொடு, lines 89-90), and asks for no return: it only asks what her fault was. The letter is the courtesan's speech in her own hand; the narrator voices the frame. தீத்திறம் புரிந்தோன் (line 78) I read as Kausikan naming himself as a Brahmin who keeps the sacred fire; it could refer to someone else.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kutram","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 13, lines 76-92","lb":"SI13:76-92","ev":"Kausikan hands Kovalan Madhavi's letter, which he knows by the scent of her hair on the seal, and he reads her plea.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி","கோசிக மாணி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:18"},"si:13:93-114":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":191,"t":["என்றவள் எழுதிய இசைமொழி யுணர்ந்து","தன்றீ திலளெனத் தளர்ச்சி நீங்கி","என்தீ தென்றே எய்திய துணர்ந்தாங்","கெற்பயந் தோற்கிம் மண்ணுடை முடங்கல்","பொற்புடைத் தாகப் பொருளுரை பொருந்தியது","மாசில் குரவர் மலரடி தொழுதேன்","கோசிக மாணி காட்டெனக் கொடுத்து","நடுக்கங் களைந்தவர் நல்லகம் பொருந்திய","இடுக்கண் களைதற் கீண்டெனப் போக்கி","மாசில் கற்பின் மனைவியோ டிருந்த","ஆசில் கொள்கை அறவிபால் அணைந்தாங்கு","ஆடியல் கொள்கை அந்தரி கோலம்","பாடும் பாணரிற் பாங்குறச் சேர்ந்து","செந்திறம் புரிந்த செங்கோட் டியாழில்","தந்திரி கரத்தொடு திவவுறுத் தியாஅத்து","ஒற்றுறுப் புடைமையிற் பற்றுவழிச் சேர்த்தி","உழைமுதல் கைக்கிளை யிறுவாய்க் கட்டி","வரன்முறை வந்த மூவகைத் தானத்து","பாய்கலைப் பாவை பாடற் பாணி","ஆசான் திறத்தின் அமைவரக் கேட்டுப்","பாடற் பாணி அளைஇ அவரொடு","கூடற் காவதம் கூறுமின் நீரெனக்"],"p":"","r":"Understanding the words she wrote, Kovalan decides she is without fault, his weariness leaves him, and seeing that the fault was his own, he says the letter's sense fits as well for his parents, bids Kausikan show it to them with his worship of their feet and sends him off to relieve their distress; then he returns to the ascetic, who sits with his wife of spotless chastity, joins a group of bards who sing the dance of the goddess, tunes the yazh with them and hears the paalai melody of the goddess of the leaping deer, and, singing along, asks them how far it is to Koodal.","n":"This is the one place in the journey where Kovalan names the cause of his ruin as his own fault (என்தீது, line 95), and he does it in answer to the courtesan, whom he clears (தன்றீ திலள், line 94). Later Kannaki tells the king that ஊழ்வினை drove him to Madurai (kaathai 20, line 69). The same letter, sent on to his parents, serves as his own plea to them. The musical lines (106-112) are technical and I have not tried to fix their terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","sutram","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 13, lines 93-114","lb":"SI13:93-114","ev":"Kovalan, reading Madhavi's letter, holds her blameless and himself at fault, sends it on to his parents by Kausikan, and joins wandering bards on the road, asking them the distance to Madurai.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","கோசிக மாணி","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","பாணர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:18"},"si:13:115-134":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":192,"t":["காழகிற் சாந்தம் கமழ்பூங் குங்குமம்","நாவிக் குழம்பு நலங்கொள் தேய்வை","மான்மதச் சாந்தம் மணங்கமழ் தெய்வத்","தேமென் கொழுஞ்சே றாடி யாங்குத்","தாதுசேர் கழுநீர் சண்பகக் கோதையொடு","மாதவி மல்லிகை மனைவளர் முல்லைப்","போதுவிரி தொடையல் பூவணை பொருந்தி","அட்டிற் புகையும் அகலங் காடி","முட்டாக் கூவியர் மோதகப் புகையும்","மைந்தரும் மகளிரும் மாடத் தெடுத்த","அந்தீம் புகையும் ஆகுதிப் புகையும்","பல்வேறு பூம்புகை அளைஇ வெல்போர்","விளங்குபூண் மார்பிற் பாண்டியன் கோயிலின்","அளந்துணர் வறியா ஆருயிர் பிணிக்கும்","கலவைக் கூட்டம் காண்வரத் தோன்றிப்","புலவர் செந்நாப் பொருந்திய நிவப்பின்","பொதியில் தென்றல் போலா தீங்கு","மதுரைத் தென்றல் வந்தது காணீர்","நனிசேய்த் தன்றவன் திருமலி மூதூர்","தனிநீர் கழியினுந் தகைக்குநர் இல்லென"],"p":"","r":"The bards answer that the Madurai breeze has come here, unlike the Pothiyil breeze of the poets' tongues: it carries the smells of eaglewood and sandal paste, saffron, civet and musk, the flowers of the houses, the smoke of kitchens and of the cake-sellers' sweets, the incense men and women burn in the mansions and the smoke of the fire offerings, mingled with the fragrance of the Pandya's palace, so his prosperous old city is not far off, and even if they go alone none will stop them.","n":"Madurai is first reached by smell, and the city's breath mixes the kitchens, the merchants, the lovers' incense and the sacrificial fire with the king's own palace. The breeze itself is the Pandya land's (lines 25-26 of this kaathai already sent it from Malaya through Madurai). The closing assurance of safety for the lone traveller (line 134) repeats the opening fame of the Pandya's road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 13, lines 115-134","lb":"SI13:115-134","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பாணர்","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:18"},"si:13:135-150":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":193,"t":["முன்னாள் முறைமையின் இருந்தவ முதல்வியொடு","பின்னையும் அல்லிடைப் பெயர்ந்தனர் பெயர்ந்தாங்கு","அருந்தெறற் கடவுள் அகன்பெருங் கோயிலும்","பெரும்பெயர் மன்னவன் பேரிசைக் கோயிலும்","பால்கெழு சிறப்பிற் பல்லியஞ் சிறந்த","காலை முரசக் கனைகுரல் ஓதையும்","நான்மறை அந்தணர் நவின்ற ஓதையும்","மாதவ ரோதி மலிந்த ஓதையும்","மீளா வென்றி வேந்தன் சிறப்பொடு","வாளோர் எடுத்த நாளணி முழவமும்","போரிற் கொண்ட பொருகரி முழக்கமும்","வாரிக் கொண்ட வயக்கரி முழக்கமும்","பணைநிலைப் புரவி ஆலும் ஓதையும்","கிணைநிலைப் பொருநர் வைகறைப் பாணியும்","கார்க்கடல் ஒலியிற் கலிகெழு கூடல்","ஆர்ப்பொலி எதிர்கொள ஆரஞர் நீங்கிக்"],"p":"","r":"With the ascetic as before they travel on in the dark, and the uproar of Koodal, loud as the dark sea, comes out to meet them and ends their sorrow: the morning drums of the great temple of the fierce god and of the king's palace, the chant of the Brahmins of the four Vedas, the recitation of the ascetics, the day drum of the warriors in the king's honour, the trumpeting of elephants taken in war and in pits, the neighing of horses in their stalls and the dawn songs of the kinai bards.","n":"The city's dawn is heard, not seen, from outside the walls, and it heals the travellers' grief (ஆரஞர் நீங்கி, line 150). The catalogue sets the temple and the palace side by side and the Brahmins' chant beside the ascetics' (மாதவர், line 142), and several items, the stalled horse (பணைநிலைப் புரவி, line 147) and the captured elephants, are those of the Sangam Madurai dawn in Maduraikkanchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","thavam","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 13, lines 135-150","lb":"SI13:135-150","ev":"Travelling on by night, the three hear Madurai's dawn sounds and their grief lifts.","cl":[],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:18"},"si:13:151-175":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":194,"t":["குரவமும் வகுளமும் கோங்கமும் வேங்கையும்","மரவமும் நாகமும் திலகமும் மருதமும்","சேடலும் செருந்தியும் செண்பக ஓங்கலும்","பாடலம் தன்னொடு பன்மலர் விரிந்து","குருகும் தளவமும் கொழுங்கொடி முசுண்டையும்","விரிமலர் அதிரலும் வெண்கூ தாளமும்","குடசமும் வெதிரமும் கொழுங்கொடிப் பகன்றையும்","பிடவமும் மயிலையும் பிணங்கரில் மணந்த","கொடுங்கரை மேகலைக் கோவை யாங்கணும்","மிடைந்துசூழ் போகிய அகன்றேந் தல்குல்","வாலுகங் குவைஇய மலர்ப்பூந் துருத்தி","பால்புடைக் கொண்டு பன்மல ரோங்கி","எதிரெதிர் விளங்கிய கதிரிள வனமுலை","கரைநின் றுதிர்த்த கவிரிதழ்ச் செவ்வாய்","அருவி முல்லை அணிநகை யாட்டி","விலங்குநிமிர்ந் தொழுகிய கருங்கயல் நெடுங்கண்","விரைமலர் நீங்கா அவிரறற் கூந்தல்","உலகுபுரந் தூட்டும் உயர்பே ரொழுக்கத்துப்","புலவர் நாவிற் பொருந்திய பூங்கொடி","வையை என்ற பொய்யாக் குலக்கொடி","தையற் குறுவது தானறிந் தனள்போல்","புண்ணிய நறுமல ராடை போர்த்துக்","கண்ணிறை நெடுநீர் கரந்தனள் அடக்கிப்","புனல்யா றன்றிது பூம்புனல் யாறென","அனநடை மாதரும் ஐயனுந் தொழுது"],"p":"","r":"The Vaigai is drawn as a woman whose banks of kuravam, vakulam, kongu, vengai and many other flowering trees are her girdle, whose sand heaps are her hips, whose flowered islet is her breasts, whose fallen kavir petals her red mouth, mullai her smile, carp her long eyes and dark water her flowered hair, the never-failing creeper of the Pandya clan, of high conduct that feeds the world, who, as if she knew what was to befall the young wife, wraps herself in a garment of holy fragrant flowers and hides her eye-filling flood, so that the woman of the swan's gait and her lord worship her, saying this is not a river of water but a river of flowers.","n":"The river's flowers, which the travellers read as beauty and worship, are given by the narrator as a veil over her tears (கண்ணிறை நெடுநீர் கரந்தனள், line 173): she knows the wife's fate and hides it. 'Never-failing' (பொய்யா, line 170) and 'feeds the world' are the Pandya river's old praise, and the poet names it as the poets' Vaigai (புலவர் நாவிற் பொருந்திய, line 169). Only the reader is told the river's grief; the couple see a river of flowers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","idumbai","vazhipaadu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 13, lines 151-175","lb":"SI13:151-175","ev":"The travellers reach the Vaigai, covered in flowers, and worship it as a river of flowers.","cl":[],"ag":["வையை","கண்ணகி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:18"},"si:13:176-196":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":195,"t":["பரிமுக அம்பியும் கரிமுக அம்பியும்","அரிமுக அம்பியும் அருந்துறை யியக்கும்","பெருந்துறை மருங்கிற் பெயரா தாங்கண்","மாதவத் தாட்டியொடு மரப்புணை போகித்","தேமலர் நறும்பொழில் தென்கரை யெய்தி","வானவர் உறையும் மதுரை வலங்கொளத்","தான்நனி பெரிதுந் தகவுடைத் தென்றாங்கு","அருமிளை யுடுத்த அகழிசூழ் போகிக்","கருநெடுங் குவளையும் ஆம்பலும் கமலமும்","தையலும் கணவனும் தனித்துறு துயரம்","ஐய மின்றி அறிந்தன போலப்","பண்ணீர் வண்டு பரிந் தினைந் தேங்கிக்","கண்ணீர் கொண்டு காலுற நடுங்கப்","போருழந் தெடுத்த ஆரெயில் நெடுங்கொடி","வாரலென் பனபோல் மறித்துக்கை காட்டப்","புள்ளணி கழனியும் பொழிலும் பொருந்தி","வெள்ளநீர்ப் பண்ணையும் விரிநீர் ஏரியும்","காய்க்குலைத் தெங்கும் வாழையும் கமுகும்","வேய்த்திரள் பந்தரும் விளங்கிய இருக்கை","அறம்புரி மாந்தர் அன்றிச் சேராப்","புறஞ்சிறை மூதூர் புக்கனர் புரிந்தென்."],"p":"","r":"Passing by the great ford where boats with horse, elephant and lion faces ply, they cross with the ascetic on a log raft to the flowering southern bank, and, judging it fitting to go round Madurai where the gods dwell, they skirt the moat girded with guard forest, where the blue lily, white lily and lotus, as if they knew for certain the lone sorrow that would come to the wife and her husband, weep and tremble with humming bees, and the long war-flags on the fort wave back as if saying 'do not come', and through fields, groves, tanks, lakes and gardens of coconut, plantain, areca and bamboo they enter the outer settlement of the old city where only those devoted to dharma dwell.","n":"Two omens are given as the narrator's 'as if' (போல, lines 186 and 190): the moat's flowers weep and the fort's flags wave the couple away. Both say plainly what the Vaigai's flowers hid. The couple avoid the royal ferry for a humble raft, and their first lodging in Madurai is the outer quarter of those devoted to dharma (lines 195-196), outside the walls where the king's justice will fail them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","idumbai","aran","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 13, lines 176-196","lb":"SI13:176-196","ev":"The three cross the Vaigai on a raft, skirt Madurai's moat past weeping flowers and waving flags, and enter the city's outer settlement.","cl":[],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கண்ணகி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:18"},"si:14:1-14":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":196,"t":["புறஞ்சிறைப் பொழிலும் பிறங்குநீர்ப் பண்ணையும்","இறங்குகதிர்க் கழனியும் புள்ளெழுந் தார்ப்பப்","புலரி வைகறைப் பொய்கைத் தாமரை","மலர்பொதி அவிழ்த்த உலகுதொழு மண்டிலம்","வேந்துதலை பனிப்ப ஏந்துவாட் செழியன்","ஒங்குயர் கூடல் ஊர்துயி லெடுப்ப","நுதல்விழி நாட்டத் திறையோன் கோயிலும்","உவணச் சேவ லுயர்த்தோன் நியமமும்","மேழிவல னுயர்த்த வெள்ளை நகரமும்","கோழிச் சேவற் கொடியோன் கோட்டமும்","அறத்துறை விளங்கிய அறவோர் பள்ளியும்","மறத்துறை விளங்கிய மன்னவன் கோயிலும்","வால்வெண் சங்கொடு வகைபெற் றோங்கிய","காலை முரசங் கனைகுரல் இயம்பக்"],"p":"","r":"At dawn, as birds rise crying from the groves, pools and fields and the sun opens the lotus, Madurai of the sword-bearing Seliyan wakes, and in the temples of the three-eyed god, of the one who raised the eagle banner, of the white one with the plough banner and of the one with the cock banner, in the monasteries of the righteous and in the king's palace the morning drums sound with the conch.","n":"The list sets the shrines of four gods and the பள்ளி of the அறவோர் (the renouncers' monasteries) beside the palace, the seat of மறம், as one waking city: line 11's அறத்துறை and line 12's மறத்துறை are paired, and Kavunthi will use the same two words a few lines on (27-28) for the paths of harm and of virtue. The sun is called the orb the world worships that makes kings tremble (line 5), a mild foretaste in a kaathai that ends at this king's city.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","samayam","vendhan","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 1-14","lb":"SI14:1-14","ev":"Dawn breaks over Madurai and the morning drums sound in its temples, monasteries and palace.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:15-24":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":197,"t":["கோவலன் சென்று கொள்கையி னிருந்த","காவுந்தி ஐயையைக் கைதொழு தேத்தி","நெறியின் நீங்கியோர் நீர்மையே னாகி","நறுமலர் மேனி நடுங்குதுய ரெய்த","அறியாத் தேயத் தாரிடை யுழந்து","சிறுமை யுற்றேன் செய்தவத் தீர்யான்","தொன்னகர் மருங்கின் மன்னர் பின்னோர்க்கு","என்னிலை யுணர்த்தி யான்வருங் காறும்","பாதக் காப்பினள் பைந்தொடி யாகலின்","ஏத முண்டோ அடிக ளீங் கென்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"Kovalan goes to Kavunthi, who keeps to her vows, worships her and says that, having become like those who strayed from the right path, he has brought distress on his tender wife and suffered among strangers in an unknown land, and asks whether, until he has made his state known to the merchants of the old city and returned, any harm could come to her here in the lady's keeping.","n":"Kovalan names his own fault (நெறியின் நீங்கியோர் நீர்மையேன், line 17) before Kavunthi names karma (line 27): the two causes, his choice and the ripening deed, are set side by side within a few lines, and the nun's answer moves the weight from the first to the second. மன்னர் பின்னோர் (line 21), 'those after the kings', is read here as the merchant order, to whom Kovalan as a merchant's son would go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","ozhukkam","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 15-24","lb":"SI14:15-24","ev":"Kovalan asks Kavunthi to keep Kannaki safe while he goes into Madurai to make himself known to its merchants.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:25-49":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":198,"t":["கவுந்தி கூறுங் காதலி தன்னொடு","தவந்தீர் மருங்கின் தனித்துயர் உழந்தோய்","மறத்துறை நீங்குமின் வல்வினை யூட்டுமென்","றறந்துறை மாக்கள் திறத்திற் சாற்றி","நாக்கடிப் பாக வாய்ப்பறை யறையினும்","யாப்பறை மாக்கள் இயல்பிற் கொள்ளார்","தீதுடை வெவ்வினை யுருத்த காலைப்","பேதைமை கந்தாப் பெரும்பே துறுவர்","ஒய்யா வினைப்பயன் உண்ணுங் காலைக்","கையாறு கொள்ளார் கற்றறி மாக்கள்","பிரிதல் துன்பமும் புணர்தல் துன்பமும்","உருவி லாளன் ஒறுக்குந் துன்பமும்","புரிகுழல் மாதர்ப் புணந்தோர்க் கல்லது","ஒருதனி வாழ்க்கை உரவோர்க் கில்லை","பெண்டிரும் உண்டியும் இன்ப மென்றுலகிற்","கொண்டோ ருறூஉங் கொள்ளாத் துன்பம்","கண்டன ராகிக் கடவுளர் வரைந்த","காமஞ் சார்பாக் காதலின் உழந்தாங்கு","ஏமஞ் சாரா இடும்பை எய்தினர்","இன்றே யல்லால் இறந்தோர் பலரால்","தொன்று படவரூஉந் தொன்மைத் தாதலின்","தாதை ஏவலின் மாதுடன் போகிக்","காதலி நீங்கக் கடுந்துய ருழந்தோன்","வேத முதல்வற் பயந்தோ னென்பது","நீயறிந் திலையோ நெடுமொழி யன்றோ"],"p":"","r":"Kavunthi tells Kovalan that the unlearned do not take the drum-like cry of the righteous to leave the ways of harm because the hard deed will feed its fruit, and are bewildered when that deed ripens, while the learned do not despair when they eat the fruit of their deeds; that the pains of parting, of union and of the bodiless god's punishment belong to those who join with women and not to the strong who live alone; that those who took women and food for pleasure have met grief ever since; and that he surely knows the old story of the one who went with his wife at his father's word and suffered when she was taken from him.","n":"Kavunthi's word for the deed's coming, உருத்த (line 31), is the verb of the patikam's third moral (ஊழ்வினை உருத்துவந்து ஊட்டும்): the epic's thesis is first spoken inside the story here, by a nun consoling the man it will kill. She names neither hero of her exempla: the one 'who begot the first of the Veda' (line 48) is the god who fathered Brahma, here in his birth as the prince exiled with his wife, and the reader must supply the name. Her account of desire as the root of suffering is a renouncer's, yet she does not urge Kovalan to leave his wife; the speech consoles a householder with an ascetic's diagnosis. தவந்தீர் மருங்கின் (line 26) may mean that his lone suffering comes from a lack of past penance; the reading does not turn on it. The unit does not name a school; the speaker is the Jain nun of the earlier kaathais.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","thuravu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 25-49","lb":"SI14:25-49","ev":"Kavunthi answers Kovalan with a teaching on karma and on the suffering that comes through desire, citing the prince who lost his wife in exile.","cl":[["a hard deed brings its fruit back on the doer, so one should leave the ways of harm","general","asserted",27,"kavunthi"],["the learned do not despair when they eat the fruit of their deeds, while the unlearned are bewildered by it","general","asserted",34,"kavunthi"],["the pains of parting, union and desire belong to those joined with women, not to the strong who live alone","general","asserted",38,"kavunthi"],["those who hold women and food to be pleasure meet a suffering they did not reckon on","general","asserted",40,"kavunthi"]],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:50-61":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":199,"t":["வல்லா டாயத்து மண்ணர சிழந்து","மெல்லியல் தன்னுடன் வெங்கா னடைந்தோன்","காதலிற் பிரிந்தோ னல்லன் காதலி","தீதொடு படூஉஞ் சிறுமைய ளல்லள்","அடவிக் கானகத் தாயிழை தன்னை","இடையிருள் யாமத் திட்டு நீக்கியது","வல்வினை யன்றோ மடந்தைதன் பிழையெனச்","சொல்லலும் உண்டேற் சொல்லா யோநீ","அனையையும் அல்லை ஆயிழை தன்னொடு","பிரியா வாழ்க்கை பெற்றனை யன்றே","வருந்தா தேகி மன்னவன் கூடல்","பொருந்துழி யறிந்து போதீங் கென்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"Kavunthi adds the example of the king who lost his land at the dice and went into the cruel forest with his wife, not parted from her for want of love nor she guilty of any fault, and asks whether his leaving her in the forest at midnight was not strong karma, and whether Kovalan would not say so if anyone blamed the woman; Kovalan, she says, is not even like him, for he has a life never parted from his wife, so let him go without grief, learn a fitting place in the king's Koodal and come back.","n":"The nun's comfort is that Kovalan, unlike the dice-ruined king, keeps his wife at his side (பிரியா வாழ்க்கை, line 59); the story will part them within days, and Kannaki will name the same driving deed (ஊழ்வினை) in the Pandya's court. The exemplum clears the abandoned wife of blame and lays the abandonment on karma rather than on the husband's will. Line 57 (சொல்லலும் உண்டேற் சொல்லா யோநீ) is compressed; it is read here as an appeal to Kovalan to answer anyone who blames the wife.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pirivu","vaazhkkaithunai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 50-61","lb":"SI14:50-61","ev":"Kavunthi cites the dice-ruined king who abandoned his wife in the forest and sends Kovalan into Madurai to find a lodging.","cl":[["a husband's abandonment of his innocent wife was the work of strong karma, not her fault","general","asserted",56,"kavunthi"]],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:62-76":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":200,"t":["இளைசூழ் மிளையொடு வளைவுடன் கிடந்த","இலங்குநீர்ப் பரப்பின் வலம்புண ரகழியில்","பெருங்கை யானை இனநிரை பெயரும்","சுருங்கை வீதி மருங்கிற் போகிக்","கடிமதில் வாயில் காவலிற் சிறந்த","அடல்வாள் யவனர்க் கயிராது புக்காங்கு","ஆயிரங் கண்ணோன் அருங்கலச் செப்பு","வாய்திறந் தன்ன மதிலக வரைப்பில்","குடகாற் றெறிந்து கொடுநுடங்கு மறுகின்","கடைகழி மகளிர் காதலஞ் செல்வரொடு","வருபுனல் வையை மருதோங்கு முன்றுறை","விரிபூந் துருத்தி வெண்மண லடைகரை","ஓங்குநீர் மாடமொடு நாவா யியக்கிப்","பூம்புணை தழீஇப் புனலாட் டமர்ந்து","தண்ணறு முல்லையுந் தாழ்நீர்க் குவளையும்"],"p":"","r":"Kovalan goes along the tunnel road beside the moat where elephant herds pass, enters the guarded gate unsuspected by its Yavana swordsmen, and comes into the walled city that opens like Indra's jewel casket, with banners waving in the west wind, where courtesans with their lovers play in the Vaigai's flood at the marutham-shaded ford and wear mullai, kuvalai and other flowers.","n":"The Yavana guards at the gate are a detail of the city's wealth and order; that they do not question the stranger is the first of the many ways the city lets Kovalan in without knowing him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 62-76","lb":"SI14:62-76","ev":"Kovalan enters Madurai through its guarded gate and begins his walk through its streets.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:77-101":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":201,"t":["கண்ணவிழ் நெய்தலுங் கதுப்புற அடைச்சி","வெண்பூ மல்லிகை விரியலொடு தொடர்ந்த","தண்செங் கழுநீர்த் தாதுவிரி பிணையல்","கொற்கையம் பெருந்துறை முத்தொடு பூண்டு","தெக்கண மலயச் செழுஞ்சே றாடிப்","பொற்கொடி மூதூர்ப் பொழிலாட் டமர்ந்தாங்கு","எற்படு பொழுதின் இளநிலா முன்றில்","தாழ்தரு கோலந் தகை பாராட்ட","வீழ்பூஞ் சேக்கை மேலினி திருந்தாங்கு","அரத்தப் பூம்பட் டரைமிசை யுடீஇக்","குரற்றலைக் கூந்தற் குடசம் பொருந்திச்","சிறுமலைச் சிலம்பின் செங்கூ தாளமொடு","நறுமலர்க் குறிஞ்சி நாண்மலர் வேய்ந்து","குங்கும வருணங் கொங்கையி னிழைத்துச்","செங்கொடு வேரிச் செழும்பூம் பிணையல்","சிந்துரச் சுண்ணஞ் சேர்ந்த மேனியில்","அந்துகிர்க் கோவை அணியொடு பூண்டு","மலைச்சிற கரிந்த வச்சிர வேந்தற்குக்","கலிகெழு கூடற் செவ்வணி காட்டக்","காரர சாளன் வாடையொடு வரூஉம்","கால மன்றியும் நூலோர் சிறப்பின்","முகில்தோய் மாடத் தகில்தரு விறகின்","மடவரல் மகளிர் தடவுநெருப் பமர்ந்து","நறுஞ்சாந் தகலத்து நம்பியர் தம்மொடு","குறுங்கண் அடைக்கும் கூதிர்க் காலையும்"],"p":"","r":"The courtesans, garlanded with lilies, jasmine and red waterlily and wearing Korkai pearls, smear Malaya sandal and play in the groves, sit at evening on moonlit terraces in red silk with hill flowers, saffron and coral to show Indra the red adornment of Koodal, and, besides the season when the cloud-king comes with the north wind, warm themselves by agil fires with their men and close the small windows in the cold.","n":"The description runs through the seasons in a single sentence (lines 70-145): each season is a new setting for the same pleasures of the courtesans' quarter, and their dress follows the seasons' colours. Which season each sub-scene names is not always clear from the Tamil, and the reading keeps to what the lines show.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","punarchi","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 77-101","lb":"SI14:77-101","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:102-111":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":202,"t":["வளமனை மகளிரும் மைந்தரும் விரும்பி","இளநிலா முன்றிலின் இளவெயில் நுகர","விரிகதிர் மண்டிலந் தெற்கேர்பு வெண்மழை","அரிதில் தோன்றும் அச்சிரக் காலையும்","ஆங்க தன்றியும் ஓங்கிரும் பரப்பின்","வங்க ஈட்டத்துத் தொண்டியோ ரிட்ட","அகிலும் துகிலும் ஆரமும் வாசமும்","தொகுகருப் பூரமுஞ் சுமந்துடன் வந்த","கொண்டலொடு புகுந்து கோமகன் கூடல்","வெங்கண் நெடுவேள் வில்விழாக் காணும்"],"p":"","r":"In the early dew season, when the sun has gone south and white clouds rarely show, the men and women of rich houses enjoy the young sun on moonlit terraces, and besides that the east wind comes in bearing the agil, silk, sandal, scents and camphor that the people of Thondi have loaded on their ships.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 102-111","lb":"SI14:102-111","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:112-127":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":203,"t":["பங்குனி முயக்கத்துப் பனியர சியாண்டுளன்","கோதை மாதவி கொழுங்கொடி யெடுப்பக்","காவும் கானமும் கடிமல ரேந்தத்","தென்னவன் பொதியில் தென்றலொடு புகுந்து","மன்னவன் கூடல் மகிழ்துணை தழூஉம்","இன்னிள வேனில் யாண்டுளன் கொல்லென்று","உருவக் கொடியோ ருடைப்பெருங் கொழுநரொடு","பருவ மெண்ணும் படர்தீர் காலைக்","கன்றம ராயமொடு களிற்றினம் நடுங்க","என்றூழ் நின்ற குன்றுகெழு நன்னாட்டுக்","காடுதீப் பிறப்பக் கனையெரி பொத்திக்","கோடையொடு புகுந்து கூட லாண்ட","வேனில் வேந்தன் வேற்றுப்புலம் படர","ஓசனிக் கின்ற உறுவெயிற் கடைநாள்","வையமுஞ் சிவிகையும் மணிக்கால் அமளியும்","உய்யா னத்தின் உறுதுணை மகிழ்ச்சியும்"],"p":"","r":"Wondering where the king of dew has gone at the union of Panguni and where the young spring is, which comes with the south wind from Pothiyil when the madhavi creeper lifts its banner, the women count the seasons with their husbands; and when the summer king who ruled Koodal with forest fires departs on the last day of fierce heat, carriages, palanquins and jewelled couches are given them.","n":"Line 113's கோதை மாதவி is the madhavi creeper whose flowering marks spring, but in an epic whose Madhavi is the courtesan Kovalan left, the creeper raising its banner over a courtesans' quarter is hard to read without her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 112-127","lb":"SI14:112-127","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:128-145":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":204,"t":["சாமரைக் கவரியுந் தமனிய அடைப்பையும்","கூர்நுனை வாளுங் கோமகன் கொடுப்பப்","பெற்ற செல்வம் பிறழா வாழ்க்கைப்","பொற்றொடி மடந்தையர் புதுமணம் புணர்ந்து","செம்பொன் வள்ளத்துச் சிலதிய ரேந்திய","அந்தீந் தேறல் மாந்தினர் மயங்கிப்","பொறிவரி வண்டினம் புல்லுவழி அன்றியும்","நறுமலர் மாலையின் வறிதிடங் கடிந்தாங்கு","இலவிதழ்ச் செவ்வாய் இளமுத் தரும்பப்","புலவிக் காலத்துப் போற்றா துரைத்த","காவியங் கண்ணார் கட்டுரை எட்டுக்கும்","நாவொடு நவிலா நகைபடு கிளவியும்","அஞ்செங் கழுநீர் அரும்பவிழ்த் தன்ன","செங்கயல் நெடுங்கட் செழுங்கடைப் பூசலும்","கொலைவிற் புருவத்துக் கொழுங்கடை சுருளத்","திலகச் சிறுநுதல் அரும்பிய வியரும்","செவ்வி பார்க்குஞ் செழுங்குடிச் செல்வரொடு","வையங் காவலர் மகிழ்தரும் வீதியும்"],"p":"","r":"Given chowries, gold betel-pouches and sharp swords by the prince, the women of unfailing wealth take new lovers, drink the toddy their maids bear in gold bowls, and in their sulks show the unspoken laugh, the fight of the eye's corner, the curled brow and the sweat on the small forehead, in the street where rich men who wait on their moods and the kings of the earth take delight.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","oodal","selvam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 128-145","lb":"SI14:128-145","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:146-167":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":205,"t":["சுடுமண் ஏறா வடுநீங்கு சிறப்பின்","முடியர சொடுங்குங் கடிமனை வாழ்க்கை","வேத்தியல் பொதுவியல் எனவிரு திறத்து","மாத்திரை யறிந்து மயங்கா மரபின்","ஆடலும் வரியும் பாணியுந் தூக்கும்","கூடிய குயிலுவக் கருவியும் உணர்ந்து","நால்வகை மரபின் அவினயக் களத்தினும்","ஏழ்வகை நிலத்தினும் எய்திய விரிக்கும்","மலைப்பருஞ் சிறப்பின் தலைக்கோ லரிவையும்","வாரம் பாடுந் தோரிய மடந்தையும்","தலைப்பாட்டுக் கூத்தியும் இடைப்பட்டுக் கூத்தியும்","நால்வேறு வகையின் நயத்தகு மரபின்","எட்டுக் கடைநிறுத்த ஆயிரத் தெண்கழஞ்சு","முட்டா வைகல் முறைமையின் வழாஅத்","தாக்கணங் கனையார் நோக்குவலைப் பட்டாங்கு","அரும்பெறல் அறிவும் பெரும்பிறி தாகத்","தவத்தோ ராயினுந் தகைமலர் வண்டின்","நகைப்பதம் பார்க்கும் இளையோ ராயினும்","காம விருந்தின் மடவோ ராயினும்","ஏம வைகல் இன்றுயில் வதியும்","பண்ணுங் கிளையும் பழித்த தீஞ்சொல்","எண்ணெண் கலையோர் இருபெரு வீதியும்"],"p":"","r":"There are also the two great streets of those of the sixty-four arts, whose sweet words outdo song: the houses of flawless fame before which crowned kings bow, where women who know the two styles of dance, its measures, the vari, songs and beats and the instruments, the four kinds of gesture and the stage of seven levels, the talaikkol dancer, the singer of the vaaram and the dancers of the lead and middle songs, receive the thousand and eight kalanju by fixed rule each day, and in the net of whose glances even ascetics lose their hard-won wisdom, while young men and the fools of love's feast sleep secure.","n":"The fee, ஆயிரத் தெண்கழஞ்சு with எட்டுக் கடைநிறுத்த, and the praise வடுநீங்கு சிறப்பின் are the words used when Madhavi received her title and price in Puhar; here they describe a daily rate across a whole profession. சுடுமண் ஏறா (line 146) is obscure and the reading leaves it aside; தாக்கணங்கு (line 160) is the goddess who strikes those who look on her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kalvi","thavam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 146-167","lb":"SI14:146-167","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:168-179":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":206,"t":["வையமும் பாண்டிலும் மணித்தேர்க் கொடுஞ்சியும்","மெய்புகு கவசமும் வீழ்மணித் தோட்டியும்","அதள்புனை அரணமும் அரியா யோகமும்","வளைதரு குழியமும் வால்வெண் கவரியும்","ஏனப் படமும் கிடுகின் படமும்","கானப் படமும் காழூன்று கடிகையும்","செம்பிற் செய்நவும் கஞ்சத் தொழிலவும்","வம்பின் முடிநவும் மாலையிற் புனைநவும்","வேதினத் துப்பவும் கோடுகடை தொழிலவும்","புகையவும் சாந்தவும் பூவிற் புனைநவும்","வகைதெரி வறியா வளந்தலை மயங்கிய","அரசுவிழை திருவின் அங்காடி வீதியும்"],"p":"","r":"There is the bazaar street, desired by kings for its wealth, where carriages, chariot parts, armour, goads, leather shields, chowries and shields of boar-hide and of wood lie heaped with work in copper and bronze, things of silk and garlands, polished wares, worked horn and ivory, incense, sandal and flowers, so mixed in plenty that their kinds cannot be told apart.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 168-179","lb":"SI14:168-179","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:180-200":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":207,"t":["காக பாதமும் களங்கமும் விந்துவும்","ஏகையும் நீங்கி இயல்பிற் குன்றா","நூலவர் நொடிந்த நுழைநுண் கோடி","நால்வகை வருணத்து நலங்கேழ் ஒளியவும்","ஏகையும் மாலையும் இருளொடு துறந்த","பாசார் மேனிப் பசுங்கதிர் ஒளியவும்","பதுமமும் நீலமும் விந்தமும் படிதமும்","விதிமுறை பிழையா விளங்கிய சாதியும்","பூச உருவின் பொலந்தெளித் தனையவும்","தீதறு கதிரொளித் தெண்மட் டுருவவும்","இருள்தெளித் தனையவும் இருவே றுருவவும்","ஒருமைத் தோற்றத் தைவேறு வனப்பின்","இலங்குகதிர் விடூஉம் நலங்கெழு மணிகளும்","காற்றினும் மண்ணினும் கல்லினும் நீரினும்","தோற்றிய குற்றந் துகளறத் துணிந்தவும்","சந்திர குருவே அங்காரக னென","வந்த நீர்மைய வட்டத் தொகுதியும்","கருப்பத் துளையவும் கல்லிடை முடங்கலும்","திருக்கு நீங்கிய செங்கொடி வல்லியும்","வகைதெரி மாக்கள் தொகைபெற் றோங்கிப்","பகைதெறல் அறியாப் பயங்கெழு வீதியும்"],"p":"","r":"There is the gem street, safe from any enemy, where men who can tell gems apart gather: diamonds free of the four flaws the treatises name, in the colours of the four orders, emeralds free of dark spots, rubies of every lawful kind, topaz, crystal and stones dark or of two colours, gems whose flaws from wind, earth, stone or water have been cut away, pearls round as the moon, Jupiter and Mars, and coral without twist.","n":"Each gem is named with the flaws it lacks, so the street is a street of judgment as much as of wealth: its people know by rule what is true and what is false in a stone. The epic will bring a gem-filled anklet before this king, and his verdict will be made without any such testing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","selvam","alavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 180-200","lb":"SI14:180-200","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:19"},"si:14:201-218":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":208,"t":["சாத ரூபம் கிளிச்சிறை ஆடகம்","சாம்பூ நதமென ஓங்கிய கொள்கையின்","பொலந்தெரி மாக்கள் கலங்கஞ ரொழித்தாங்கு","இலங்குகொடி யெடுக்கும் நலங்கிளர் வீதியும்","நூலினும் மயிரினும் நுழைநூற் பட்டினும்","பால்வகை தெரியாப் பன்னூ றடுக்கத்து","நறுமடி செறிந்த அறுவை வீதியும்","நிறைக்கோல் துலாத்தர் பறைக்கட் பராரையர்","அம்பண வளவையர் எங்கணுந் திரிதரக்","கால மன்றியும் கருங்கறி மூடையொடு","கூலங் குவித்த கூல வீதியும்","பால்வேறு தெரிந்த நால்வேறு தெருவும்","அந்தியும் சதுக்கமும் ஆவண வீதியும்","மன்றமும் கவலையும் மறுகும் திரிந்து","விசும்பகடு திருகிய வெங்கதிர் நுழையாப்","பசுங்கொடிப் படாகைப் பந்தர் நீழல்","காவலன் பேரூர் கண்டுமகிழ் வெய்திக்","கோவலன் பெயர்ந்தனன் கொடிமதிற் புறத்தென்."],"p":"","r":"Past the gold street, where assayers of the four kinds of gold raise their flags free of doubt, the cloth street of cotton, hair and silk in bundles past counting, and the grain street where weighers, measurers and those with the measuring pot go everywhere among heaped grain and sacks of pepper, Kovalan wanders the four-caste streets, junctions, squares, halls and lanes, and, glad at having seen the king's great city under the shade of banners the sun cannot pierce, goes back outside the walls.","n":"பொலந்தெரி மாக்கள் (line 203), the men who test gold, are the trade of the goldsmith who will accuse Kovalan; the tour ends in pleasure (மகிழ்வெய்தி) at the city whose verdict will kill him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 14, lines 201-218","lb":"SI14:201-218","ev":"Kovalan walks through the gold, cloth and grain streets and, pleased with the city, returns outside its walls.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:19"},"si:15:1-20":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":209,"t":["நிலந்தரு திருவின் நிழல்வாய் நேமி","கடம்பூண் டுருட்டும் கௌரியர் பெருஞ்சீர்க்","கோலின் செம்மையும் குடையின் தண்மையும்","வேலின் கொற்றமும் விளங்கிய கொள்கைப்","பதியெழு வறியாப் பண்புமேம் பட்ட","மதுரை மூதூர் மாநகர் கண்டாங்கு","அறந்தரு நெஞ்சின் அறவோர் பல்கிய","புறஞ்சிறை மூதூர்ப் பொழிலிடம் புகுந்து","தீதுதீர் மதுரையும் தென்னவன் கொற்றமும்","மாதவத் தாட்டிக்குக் கோவலன் கூறுழித்","தாழ்நீர் வேலித் தலைச் செங்கானத்து","நான்மறை முற்றிய நலம்புரி கொள்கை","மாமறை முதல்வன் மாடலன் என்போன்","மாதவ முனிவன் மலைவலங் கொண்டு","குமரியம் பெருந்துறை கொள்கையிற் படிந்து","தமர்முதற் பெயர்வோன் தாழ்பொழி லாங்கண்","வகுந்துசெல் வருத்தத்து வான்றுயர் நீங்கக்","கவுந்தி இடவயிற் புகுந்தோன் தன்னைக்","கோவலன் சென்று சேவடி வணங்க","நாவ லந்தணன் தானவின் றுரைப்போன்"],"p":"","r":"Having seen Madurai, whose kings' straight sceptre, cool umbrella and victorious spear keep a city that has never had to flee, Kovalan returns to the grove of the ascetics' outer quarter and tells Kavunthi of the evil-free city and the Pandya's might; meanwhile the Brahmin Maadalan of Thalaichengaanam, returning from circling the sage's hill and bathing at Kumari, enters the grove to rest, Kovalan bows at his feet, and the Brahmin begins to speak.","n":"Kovalan's report of a Madurai 'free of evil' (தீதுதீர் மதுரை) and of the straightness of its kings' sceptre (கோலின் செம்மை) is the last praise of the Pandya before the killing; the next kaathai will show that sceptre bend on him. Line 7 calls the outer quarter the place of those with dharma-bearing hearts, which line 107 turns into the reason Kovalan must leave it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","vendhan","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 1-20","lb":"SI15:1-20","ev":"Kovalan returns from seeing Madurai to the ascetics' grove and tells Kavunthi of it; the Brahmin Maadalan arrives from Kumari and Kovalan bows to him.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","மாடலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:21-41":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":210,"t":["வேந்துறு சிறப்பின் விழுச்சீ ரெய்திய","மாந்தளிர் மேனி மாதவி மடந்தை","பால்வாய்க் குழவி பயந்தன ளெடுத்து","வாலா மைந்நாள் நீங்கிய பின்னர்","மாமுது கணிகையர் மாதவி மகட்கு","நாம நல்லுரை நாட்டுது மென்று","தாமின் புறூஉந் தகைமொழி கேட் டாங்கு","இடையிருள் யாமத் தெறிதிரைப் பெருங்கடல்","உடைகலப் பட்ட எங்கோன் முன்னாள்","புண்ணிய தானம் புரிந்தோ னாகலின்","நண்ணுவழி இன்றி நாள்சில நீந்த","இந்திரன் ஏவலின் ஈங்கு வாழ்வேன்","வந்தேன் அஞ்சல் மணிமே கலையான்","உன்பெருந் தானத் துறுதி யொழியாது","துன்ப நீங்கித் துயர்க்கட லொழிகென","விஞ்சையிற் பெயர்த்து விழுமந் தீர்த்த","எங்குல தெய்வப் பெயரீங் கிடுகென","அணிமே கலையார் ஆயிரங் கணிகையர்","மணிமே கலையென வாழ்த்திய ஞான்று","மங்கல மடந்தை மாதவி தன்னொடு","செம்பொன் மாரி செங்கையிற் பொழிய"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan recalls the day the elder courtesans proposed to name the infant daughter of Madhavi, and Kovalan said that an ancestor of his, shipwrecked at midnight on the sea, had been kept afloat because of his meritorious gifts until the goddess Manimekalai came at Indra's bidding, told him not to fear because the merit of his great giving does not fail, and saved him; so the child must bear the name of that family deity, and a thousand courtesans hailed her Manimekalai while Kovalan, with Madhavi, poured gold from his hands.","n":"This is the first naming of Manimekalai in the epic, and it ties her name to a sea goddess who rescues for merit, the role the goddess plays for Manimekalai herself in the sequel. The rescue is credited to giving (புண்ணிய தானம், பெருந் தானம்), not to devotion, and the goddess acts on Indra's command (இந்திரன் ஏவலின்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kudi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 21-41","lb":"SI15:21-41","ev":"At the naming of Madhavi's daughter Kovalan has her called Manimekalai after the goddess who saved his shipwrecked ancestor, and pours out gold gifts.","cl":[["the merit of great giving does not fail and brings rescue in disaster","general","asserted",34,"manimekala_deyvam"]],"ag":["மாமுது கணிகையர்","மணிமேகலை","மாதவி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:42-53":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":211,"t":["ஞான நன்னெறி நல்வரம் பாயோன்","தானங் கொள்ளுந் தகைமையின் வருவோன்","தளர்ந்த நடையில் தண்டுகா லூன்றி","வளைந்த யாக்கை மறையோன் றன்னைப்","பாகுகழிந் தியாங்கணும் பறைபட வரூஉம்","வேக யானை வெம்மையிற் கைக்கொள","ஓய்யெனத் தெழித்தாங் குயர்பிறப் பாளனைக்","கையகத் தொழித்ததன் கையகம் புக்குப்","பொய்பொரு முடங்குகை வெண்கோட் டடங்கி","மையிருங் குன்றின் விஞ்சையன் ஏய்ப்பப்","பிடர்த்தலை இருந்து பெருஞ்சினம் பிறழாக்","கடக்களி றடக்கிய கருணை மறவ"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan goes on: on that day an aged Brahmin, bent and leaning on a staff, coming to receive gifts, was seized by a rogue elephant that had thrown off its driver, and Kovalan, shouting, freed the man from its trunk, entered between its tusks, sat on its neck like a vidyadhara on a black hill and tamed it, and Maadalan hails him as the compassionate warrior who subdued the elephant.","n":"The vocative கருணை மறவ joins compassion and martial courage in a merchant; the feat is told as one more of Kovalan's good deeds in a list that will end in the question why such a man suffers (lines 91 to 94).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","maram","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 42-53","lb":"SI15:42-53","ev":"A rogue elephant seizes an old Brahmin who came for gifts, and Kovalan frees him and tames the elephant.","cl":[],"ag":["மறையோன்","வேக யானை","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:54-75":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":212,"t":["பிள்ளை நகுலம் பெரும்பிறி தாக","எள்ளிய மனையோள் இனைந்துபின் செல்ல","வடதிசைப் பெயரும் மாமறை யாளன்","கடவ தன்றுநின் கைத்தூண் வாழ்க்கை","வடமொழி வாசகஞ் செய்த நல்லேடு","கடனறி மாந்தர் கைந்நீ கொடுக் கெனப்","பீடிகைத் தெருவிற் பெருங்குடி வாணிகர்","மாட மறுகின் மனைதொறு மறுகிக்","கருமக் கழிபலங் கொண்மி னோவெனும்","அருமறை யாட்டியை அணுகக் கூஉய்","யாதுநீ யுற்ற இடர்ஈ தென்னென","மாதர்தா னுற்ற வான்துயர் செப்பி","இப்பொரு ளெழுதிய இதழிது வாங்கிக்","கைப்பொருள் தந்தென் கடுந்துயர் களைகென","அஞ்சல் உன்றன் அருந்துயர் களைகேன்","நெஞ்சுறு துயரம் நீங்குக என்றாங்கு","ஓத்துடை அந்தணர் உரைநூற் கிடக்கையில்","தீத்திறம் புரிந்தோள் செய்துயர் நீங்கத்","தானஞ் செய் தவள் தன்றுயர் நீக்கிக்","கானம் போன கணவனைக் கூட்டி","ஒல்காச் செல்வத் துறுபொருள் கொடுத்து","நல்வழிப் படுத்த செல்லாச் செல்வ"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan tells how a Brahmin, after the mongoose they kept as a child came to its death, left for the north with his grieving wife following, telling her that living on food from her hand was no longer his duty and giving her a leaf written in Sanskrit to be placed in the hands of men who know duty; she went from house to house through the merchants' streets crying for someone to take the fruit of her deed, and Kovalan called her, heard her trouble, took the leaf, gave her wealth, had her wrong removed by gifts as the Brahmins' books lay down, brought back her husband and set them on a good path, and Maadalan hails him as one whose wealth never fails.","n":"The text says only that the mongoose came to its death (பெரும்பிறிது ஆக) and that the wife was scorned (எள்ளிய); that she herself caused the death is implied by line 71, which calls her the one who did a harmful deed (தீத்திறம் புரிந்தோள்), not told. Kovalan does not take the sin on himself: he pays for the expiation the Brahmins' law books prescribe, and the merchant's wealth is what makes the rite possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","vinai","andhanar","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 54-75","lb":"SI15:54-75","ev":"A Brahmin leaves his wife after their pet mongoose dies; Kovalan pays for her expiation by gifts and brings her husband back.","cl":[["a husband may refuse to live on the food of a wife who has done a grave wrong","vedic","reported",57,"maamaraiyaalan"],["a wrong deed's sin is removed by gifts made as the Brahmins' law books prescribe","vedic","asserted",70,"maadalan"]],"ag":["மாமறை யாளன்","அருமறை யாட்டி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:76-94":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":213,"t":["பத்தினி யொருத்தி படிற்றுரை எய்த","மற்றவள் கணவற்கு வறியோன் ஒருவன்","அறியாக் கரிபொய்த் தறைந்துணும் பூதத்துக்","கறைகெழு பாசத்துக் கையகப் படலும்","பட்டோன் றவ்வை படுதுயர் கண்டு","கட்டிய பாசத்துக் கடிதுசென் றெய்தி","என்னுயிர் கொண்டீங் கிவனுயிர் தாவென","நன்னெடும் பூதம் நல்கா தாகி","நரக னுயிர்க்கு நல்லுயிர் கொண்டு","பரகதி யிழக்கும் பண்பீங் கில்லை","ஒழிக நின் கருத்தென உயிர்முன் புடைப்ப","அழிதரு முள்ளத் தவளொடும் போந்தவன்","சுற்றத் தோர்க்கும் தொடர்புறு கிளைகட்கும்","பற்றிய கிளைஞரின் பசிப்பிணி யறுத்துப்","பல்லாண்டு புரந்த இல்லோர் செம்மல்","இம்மைச் செய்தன யானறி நல்வினை","உம்மைப் பயன்கொல் ஒருதனி யுழந்தித்","திருத்தகு மாமணிக் கொழுந்துடன் போந்தது","விருத்தகோ பால நீயென வினவக்"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan tells how a poor man who bore false witness to a chaste wife's husband about what he did not know was caught in the noose of the spirit that beats and devours, how Kovalan, seeing the man's mother grieve, ran to the spirit and offered his own life for the man's, and how the spirit refused, saying it had no way of taking a good life for a hell-bound one and losing the higher state, and struck the man dead before him; Kovalan went away with the grieving woman and for many years fed the dead man's kin, and Maadalan, having listed these good deeds of this birth, asks whether it is the fruit of a former birth that Kovalan now suffers alone with his jewel of a wife.","n":"Maadalan's question (உம்மைப் பயன்கொல்) is the first time in the Madurai book that a character reasons from undeserved suffering to a deed of a former birth: the good deeds of this life (இம்மைச் செய்தன யானறி நல்வினை) are known and cannot explain it, so the cause must lie further back. It is put as a question, not a verdict. The spirit's refusal shows its justice cannot be bought even with a good man's life. விருத்தகோபால (line 94) is an address to Kovalan; whether விருத்த means 'old', 'vowed' or something else here is not clear. Whether தவ்வை (line 80) is the man's mother or his elder sister is not settled by the line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","naragam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 76-94","lb":"SI15:76-94","ev":"The spirit seizes a false witness; Kovalan offers his life for the man's, the spirit refuses and kills him, and Kovalan supports the dead man's family for years.","cl":[["one who bears false witness is seized and devoured by the spirit","general","asserted",78,"maadalan"],["a good life may not be taken in exchange for a sinner's, for that would cost the higher state","general","reported",84,"sathukka_bhutam"],["suffering not explained by the good deeds of this life is the fruit of deeds of a former birth","general","reported",92,"maadalan"]],"ag":["வறியோன்","தவ்வை","பூதம்","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:95-106":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":214,"t":["கோவலன் கூறுமோர் குறுமகன் தன்னால்","காவல் வேந்தன் கடிநகர் தன்னில்","நாறைங் கூந்தல் நடுங்குதுய ரெய்தக்","கூறைகோட் பட்டுக் கோட்டுமா ஊரவும்","அணித்தகு புரிகுழ லாயிழை தன்னொடும்","பிணிப்பறுத் தோர்தம் பெற்றி யெய்தவும்","மாமலர் வாளி வறுநிலத் தெறிந்து","காமக் கடவுள் கையற் றேங்க","அணிதிகழ் போதி அறவோன் றன்முன்","மணிமேகலையை மாதவி யளிப்பவும்","நனவுபோல நள்ளிருள் யாமத்துக்","கனவு கண்டேன் கடிதீங் குறுமென"],"p":"","r":"Kovalan answers with a dream he had at midnight as vivid as waking: in the guarded city of the king, because of a petty man, his wife fell into trembling distress, his garment was seized and he rode a horned beast; then, with his wife, he reached the state of those who have cut their bonds, Kama flung his flower arrows on the bare ground and stood helpless, and Madhavi gave Manimekalai before the virtuous one of the shining Bodhi tree; and he fears that something bad will soon come.","n":"The dream compresses the rest of the story in order: the petty man (குறுமகன்) is the goldsmith to come, the seized garment and the ride on a horned beast are signs of death, the couple's reaching the state of the unbound (பிணிப்பறுத்தோர் தம் பெற்றி) is their afterlife, and Madhavi's giving her daughter before the Buddha (போதி அறவோன்) is the Manimekalai's opening. Kovalan reads only the first part, as a coming harm; he does not answer Maadalan's question about a former birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","thuravu","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 95-106","lb":"SI15:95-106","ev":"Kovalan tells Maadalan a dream of his wife's distress, his own seizure and death-ride, and Madhavi's giving their daughter to the Buddha.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","மாடலன்"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:107-114":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":215,"t":["அறத்துறை மாக்கட் கல்ல திந்தப்","புறச்சிறை யிருக்கை பொருந்தா தாகலின்","அரைசர் பின்னோர் அகநகர் மருங்கினின்","உரையிற் கொள்வரிங் கொழிகநின் இருப்புக்","காதலி தன்னொடு கதிர்செல் வதன்முன்","மாட மதுரை மாநகர் புகுகென","மாதவத் தாட்டியும் மாமறை முதல்வனும்","கோவலன் றனக்குக் கூறுங் காலை"],"p":"","r":"Kavunthi and Maadalan tell Kovalan that this outer quarter is fit only for those on the path of dharma, that the merchants of the inner city will receive him at the mention of his name, and that he should enter great Madurai with his wife before the sun sets.","n":"Lines 107 and 108 make the ascetics' quarter unfit for a householder, so the couple must leave the place the narration called 'dharma-bearing' (line 7) for the city where the killing will happen. அரைசர் பின்னோர் ('those after the kings') names the merchant class by rank.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 107-114","lb":"SI15:107-114","ev":"Kavunthi and Maadalan urge Kovalan to leave the ascetics' quarter and enter Madurai with his wife before sunset.","cl":[],"ag":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","மாடலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:115-130":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":216,"t":["அறம்புரி நெஞ்சின் அறவோர் பல்கிய","புறஞ்சிறை மூதூர்ப் பூங்கண் இயக்கிக்குப்","பான்மடை கொடுத்துப் பண்பிற் பெயர்வோள்","ஆயர் முதுமகள் மாதரி என்போள்","காவுந்தி ஐயையைக் கண்டடி தொழலும்","ஆகாத் தோம்பி ஆப்பயன் அளிக்கும்","கோவலர் வாழ்க்கையோர் கொடும்பா டில்லை","தீதிலள் முதுமகள் செவ்வியள் அளியள்","மாதரி தன்னுடன் மடந்தையை இருத்துதற்கு","ஏதம் இன்றென எண்ணின ளாகி","மாதரி கேளிம் மடந்தைதன் கணவன்","தாதையைக் கேட்கில் தன்குல வாணர்","அரும்பொருள் பெறுநரின் விருந்தெதிர் கொண்டு","கருந்தடங் கண்ணியொடு கடிமனைப் படுத்துவர்","உடைப்பெருஞ் செல்வர் மனைப்புகு மளவும்","இடைக்குல மடந்தைக் கடைக்கலந் தந்தேன்"],"p":"","r":"As they speak, the old herdswoman Maadari, coming back from offering milk rice to the yakshi of the outer quarter, worships Kavunthi; Kavunthi, judging that the life of herders who tend cows and give their yield has no crookedness and that this old woman is blameless and kind, tells her that when Kovalan's people hear whose son he is they will receive him like a guest of great worth, and that until he enters a rich man's house she gives Kannaki to Maadari as a charge in her keeping.","n":"This is the act that names the kaathai: அடைக்கலம் here is a ward placed in another's protection, the sense the rest of the chapter builds on, not the Saiva self-surrender to the Lord that the concept usually tags elsewhere. Kavunthi's trust rests on a judgment of the herders' way of life as a whole (கோவலர் வாழ்க்கை), not on knowledge of Maadari herself. பூங்கண் இயக்கி is a yakshi goddess, and Maadari, a herdswoman, worships her and a Jain ascetic alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adaikkalam","virundhu","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 115-130","lb":"SI15:115-130","ev":"Maadari worships Kavunthi, who judges her trustworthy and entrusts Kannaki to her keeping.","cl":[["the life of herders who tend cows and give their yield has no crookedness","general","asserted",121,"kavunthi"]],"ag":["மாதரி","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","மாதரி"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:131-148":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":217,"t":["மங்கல மடந்தையை நன்னீ ராட்டிச்","செங்கயல் நெடுங்கண் அஞ்சனந் தீட்டித்","தேமென் கூந்தற் சின்மலர் பெய்து","தூமடி உடீஇத் தொல்லோர் சிறப்பின்","ஆயமும் காவலும் ஆயிழை தனக்குத்","தாயும் நீயே யாகித் தாங்கிங்கு","என்னொடு போந்த இளங்கொடி நங்கைதன்","வண்ணச் சீறடி மண்மகள் அறிந்திலள்","கடுங்கதிர் வெம்மையிற் காதலன் றனக்கு","நடுங்குதுய ரெய்தி நாப்புலர வாடித்","தன்துயர்காணாத் தகைசால் பூங்கொடி","இன்துணை மகளிர்க் கின்றியமையாக்","கற்புக் கடம்பூண்ட இத்தெய்வ மல்லது","பொற்புடைத் தெய்வம் யாங்கண் டிலமால்","வானம் பொய்யாது வளம்பிழைப் பறியாது","நீணில வேந்தர் கொற்றம் சிதையாது","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர் இருந்தநா டென்னும்","அத்தகு நல்லுரை அறியா யோநீ"],"p":"","r":"Kavunthi tells Maadari to bathe Kannaki, line her eyes, put flowers in her hair, dress her in clean cloth and be her companions, guard and mother; she says the earth never knew this girl's small feet, that in the scorching sun she grieved for her husband's pain and never for her own, that she has seen no goddess fairer than this one who bears the vow of the chastity a wife cannot be without, and asks whether Maadari does not know the good saying that where chaste wives live the sky does not fail, plenty does not falter and the victory of kings does not decay.","n":"Kavunthi calls Kannaki a deity (இத்தெய்வம்), the strongest claim yet about her, and grounds it in her bearing pain for her husband and not for herself. The saying of lines 145 to 147 is quoted as common wisdom (அத்தகு நல்லுரை), not as Jain teaching, and it pledges the king's victory to the presence of pathini women: the Pandya of this very city will fall because of one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","karpu","mazhai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 131-148","lb":"SI15:131-148","ev":"","cl":[["chastity is the one thing a wife cannot be without","general","asserted",143,"kavunthi"],["where chaste wives live, the rain does not fail, plenty does not falter and the king's victory does not decay","general","asserted",147,"kavunthi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","மாதரி"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:149-162":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":218,"t":["தவத்தோர் அடக்கலம் தான்சிறி தாயினும்","மிகப்பே ரின்பம் தருமது கேளாய்","காவிரிப் படப்பைப் பட்டினந் தன்னுள்","பூவிரி பிண்டிப் பொதுநீங்கு திருநிழல்","உலக நோன்பிகள் ஒருங்குடன் இட்ட","இலகொளிச் சிலாதல மேலிருந் தருளித்","தருமஞ் சாற்றுஞ் சாரணர் தம்முன்","திருவில் இட்டுத் திகழ்தரு மேனியன்","தாரன் மாலையன் தமனியப் பூணினன்","பாரோர் காணாப் பலர்தொழு படிமையன்","கருவிரற் குரங்கின் கையொரு பாகத்துப்","பெருவிறல் வானவன் வந்துநின் றோனைச்","சாவக ரெல்லாம் சாரணர்த் தொழுதீங்கு","யாதிவன் வரவென இறையோன் கூறும்"],"p":"","r":"Kavunthi adds that a charge held for the ascetics, however small, yields very great joy, and tells a story: at Puhar, under the flowering asoka on the shining stone seat the world's ascetics had set up, where the charanas preached dharma, there appeared a god with a body bright as a rainbow, garlands and gold ornaments, a form the world cannot see, with a black-fingered monkey's hand on one part of him, and when the lay hearers worshipped the charanas and asked why he came, the chief of them spoke.","n":"தவத்தோர் அடைக்கலம் can be read as a charge entrusted by ascetics, as Kavunthi has just entrusted Kannaki, or as refuge given to ascetics; the story that follows (a monkey that a sage bids a housewife cherish) fits the first. The setting is Jain throughout: the asoka (பிண்டி), the charanas (சாரணர்), the lay hearers (சாவகர்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adaikkalam","thavam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 149-162","lb":"SI15:149-162","ev":"At Puhar a god with a monkey's hand appears before the Jain charanas, and the lay hearers ask why.","cl":[["keeping a charge entrusted by ascetics, however small, brings very great joy","jain","asserted",149,"kavunthi"]],"ag":["தேவ குமரன்","சாவகர்","சாரணர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","மாதரி"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:163-173":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":219,"t":["எட்டி சாயலன் இருந்தோன் றனது","பட்டினி நோன்பிகள் பலர்புகு மனையிலோர்","மாதவ முதல்வனை மனைப்பெருங் கிழத்தி","ஏதம் நீங்க எதிர்கொள் அமயத்து","ஊர்ச்சிறு குரங்கொன் றொதுங்கிஉள் புக்குப்","பாற்படு மாதவன் பாதம் பொருந்தி","உண்டொழி மிச்சிலும் உகுத்த நீரும்","தண்டா வேட்கையில் தான்சிறி தருந்தி","எதிர்முகம் நோக்கிய இன்பச் செவ்வியை","அதிராக் கொள்கை அறிவனும் நயந்துநின்","மக்களின் ஓம்பு மனைக்கிழத் தீயென"],"p":"","r":"The chief charana tells that in the house of Etti Saayalan, where many fasting ascetics came, as the lady of the house was receiving a great ascetic, a small town monkey crept in, touched his feet, drank a little of the leftover food and the water he had spilled, and looked up into his face, and the sage, pleased, told the lady to cherish it as her own children.","n":"The monkey's merit begins with eating an ascetic's leftovers (மிச்சில்) and looking into his face, and with the sage's word that turns an animal into a ward: the story is built to fit Kavunthi's maxim of a charge held for ascetics.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","thavam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 163-173","lb":"SI15:163-173","ev":"A monkey eats a visiting ascetic's leftovers in Saayalan's house, and the ascetic bids the housewife raise it as her child.","cl":[],"ag":["சாயலன் மனைவி","குரங்கு","மாதவ முதல்வன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்","சாவகர்"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:174-191":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":220,"t":["மிக்கோன் கூறிய மெய்மொழி ஓம்பிக்","காதற் குரங்கு கடைநா ளெய்தவும்","தானஞ் செய்வுழி அதற்கொரு கூறு","தீதறு கென்றே செய்தன ளாதலின்","மத்திம நன்னாட்டு வாரணந் தன்னுள்","உத்தர கௌத்தற் கொருமக னாகி","உருவினும் திருவினும் உணர்வினுந் தோன்றிப்","பெருவிறல் தானம் பலவுஞ் செய்தாங்கு","எண்ணால் ஆண்டின் இறந்தபிற் பாடு","விண்ணோர் வடிவம் பெற்றன னாதலின்","பெற்ற செல்வப் பெரும்பய னெல்லாம்","தற்காத் தளித்தோள் தானச் சிறப்பெனப்","பண்டைப் பிறப்பிற் குரங்கின் சிறுகை","கொண்டொரு பாகத்துக் கொள்கையிற் புணர்ந்த","சாயலன் மனைவி தானந் தன்னால்","ஆயினன் இவ்வடிவு அறிமி னோவெனச்","சாவகர்க் கெல்லாம் சாற்றினன் காட்டத்","தேவ குமரன் தோன்றினன் என்றலும்"],"p":"","r":"The charana goes on: keeping the sage's true word, the woman cared for the monkey, and when it died she set aside a share of her gifts in its name so its evil might end; so it was born the son of Uttara Kauttan in Varanasi of the middle country, excellent in form, fortune and understanding, made many gifts, and after thirty-two years died and took the form of a god; and, saying that all the fruit of this was the virtue of the gift of the woman who had raised him, and that the monkey's small hand on part of the god's body shows he became this form through Saayalan's wife's gift, the chief showed him to the hearers, and the god's son appeared.","n":"Merit here passes from one being to another: the woman's gift, made for the monkey's share (அதற்கொரு கூறு), raises it to a human birth, and that birth's own gifts raise it to the gods. The god's body keeps the monkey's hand, so the reward of merit is itself a birth that still bears the mark of the one before. That the woman made the gift so that the monkey's evil should end (தீதறுக) treats an animal birth as the fruit of evil to be cleared.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","piravi","vinai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 174-191","lb":"SI15:174-191","ev":"The charana tells how the monkey, through the housewife's gift in its name, was reborn a prince and then a god, and shows the god to the hearers.","cl":[["a gift made in another being's name can raise that being to a better birth","jain","asserted",176,"saaranar"],["the gifts of a human birth lead after death to a god's form","jain","asserted",183,"saaranar"]],"ag":["சாயலன் மனைவி","சாரணர்","தேவ குமரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாரணர்","சாவகர்"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:192-199":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":221,"t":["சாரணர் கூறிய தகைசால் நன்மொழி","ஆரணங் காக அறந்தலைப் பட்டோர்","அன்றப் பதியுள் அருந்தவ மாக்களும்","தன்தெறல் வாழ்க்கைச் சாவக மாக்களும்","இட்ட தானத் தெட்டியும் மனைவியும்","முட்டா இன்பத்து முடிவுல கெய்தினர்","கேட்டனை யாயினித் தோட்டார் குழலியொடு","நீட்டித் திராது நீபோ கென்றே"],"p":"","r":"Kavunthi ends that those who took the charanas' good words as precious and turned to dharma, the ascetics of that city, the lay hearers who lived restrained lives and the merchant and his wife of the gift, reached the final world of unbroken joy; and she tells Maadari that, having heard this, she should go with the girl without delay.","n":"The giving couple reach not another heaven but the final world (முடிவுலகு), and the line ties this to hearing the charanas and turning to dharma (அறந்தலைப் பட்டோர்), not to the gift alone. Kavunthi tells the story to bind Maadari to her charge; the story promises great joy to the keeper, which the later kaathais do not give Maadari.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","eegai","aram","adaikkalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 192-199","lb":"SI15:192-199","ev":"","cl":[["those who heed the charanas' teaching and turn to dharma, lay givers among them, reach the final world of unending joy","jain","asserted",197,"kavunthi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","மாதரி"],"ch":"si:20"},"si:15:200-219":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":222,"t":["கவுந்தி கூற உவந்தன ளேத்தி","வளரிள வனமுலை வாங்கமைப் பணைத்தோள்","முளையிள வெண்பல் முதுக்குறை நங்கையொடு","சென்ற ஞாயிற்றுச் செல்சுடர் அமயத்துக்","கன்றுதேர் ஆவின் கனைகுரல் இயம்ப","மறித்தோள் நவியத்து உறிக்கா வாளரொடு","செறிவளை ஆய்ச்சியர் சிலர் புறஞ் சூழ","மிளையும் கிடங்கும் வளைவிற் பொறியும்","கருவிர லூகமும் கல்லுமிழ் கவணும்","பரிவுறு வெந்நெயும் பாகடு குழிசியும்","காய்பொன் உலையும் கல்லிடு கூடையும்","தூண்டிலும் தொடக்கும் ஆண்டலை அடுப்பும்","கவையும் கழுவும் புதையும் புழையும்","ஐயவித் துலாமும் கைபெயர் ஊசியும்","சென்றெறி சிரலும் பன்றியும் பணையும்","எழுவுஞ் சீப்பும் முழுவிறற் கணையமும்","கோலும் குந்தமும் வேலும் பிறவும்","ஞாயிலும் சிறந்து நாட்கொடி நுடங்கும்","வாயில் கழிந்துதன் மனைபுக் கனளால்","கோவலர் மடந்தை கொள்கையிற் புணர்ந்தென்."],"p":"","r":"When Kavunthi has spoken, Maadari praises her gladly and, as the sun goes down and cows call for their calves, sets out with the wise young Kannaki, herdsmen with axes on their shoulders and pot-slings and herdswomen around them, passes through the gate of Madurai, bristling beyond its moat with the fort's engines, bows, slings, boiling oil and molten metal, hooks, stakes and iron bars, spears and every other device, where the day's banners wave, and enters her house, holding to what she had taken on.","n":"The long list of war engines on Madurai's wall (lines 207 to 216) is the city's defence against siege at the very moment it admits the woman whose wrong will burn it. Among the engines is a black-fingered monkey (கருவிர லூகம்), set against the black-fingered monkey (கருவிரற் குரங்கு, line 159) of Kavunthi's tale. The closing word கொள்கையிற் புணர்ந்து says Maadari took on the charge as her resolve.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","adaikkalam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 15, lines 200-219","lb":"SI15:200-219","ev":"Maadari takes Kannaki through Madurai's fortified gate at sunset and brings her home.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரி","கண்ணகி","உறிக்கா வாளர்","ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:20"},"si:16:1-16":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":223,"t":["அரும்பெறற் பாவையை அடைக்கலம் பெற்ற","இரும்பே ருவகையின் இடைக்குல மடந்தை","அளைவிலை யுணவின் ஆய்ச்சியர் தம்மொடு","மிளைசூழ் கோவலர் இருக்கை யன்றிப்","பூவ லூட்டிய புனைமாண் பந்தர்க்","காவற் சிற்றிற் கடிமனைப் படுத்துச்","செறிவளை யாய்ச்சியர் சிலருடன் கூடி","நறுமலர்க் கோதையை நாணீ ராட்டிக்","கூடல் மகளிர் கோலங் கொள்ளும்","ஆடகப் பைம்பூ ணருவிலை யழிப்பச்","செய்யாக் கோலமொடு வந்தீர்க் கென்மகள்","ஐயை காணீ ரடித்தொழி லாட்டி","பொன்னிற் பொதிந்தேன் புனைபூங் கோதை","என்னுடன் நங்கையீங் கிருக்கெனத் தொழுது","மாதவத் தாட்டி வழித்துயர் நீக்கி","ஏத மில்லா இடந்தலைப் படுத்தினள்"],"p":"","r":"Glad to have received Kannaki as a ward, the herdswoman Maadari lodges her in a guarded little house apart from the herders' dwellings, has the herdswomen bathe her, tells her that her daughter Aiyai will be her servant and that she will keep her as if wrapped in gold, and so settles her in a place she thinks free of harm.","n":"Maadari's praise turns Kannaki's lack of ornament into a beauty that spoils the worth of the Madurai women's gold (செய்யாக் கோலம்), and she bows to her ward. The last line's ஏதம் இல்லா இடம் repeats the word with which Kavunthi judged it safe to leave Kannaki with Maadari (15:124, ஏதம் இன்று), so the 'harmless place' is named on the day of the killing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adaikkalam","virundhu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 1-16","lb":"SI16:1-16","ev":"Maadari lodges Kannaki in a guarded house, has her bathed and gives her Aiyai as a servant.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரி","ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:17-32":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":224,"t":["நோதக வுண்டோ நும்மக னார்க்கினிச்","சாவக நோன்பிக ளடிக ளாதலின்","நாத்தூண் நங்கையொடு நாள்வழிப் படூஉம்","அடிசி லாக்குதற் கமைந்தநற் கலங்கள்","நெடியா தளிமின் நீரெனக் கூற","இடைக்குல மடந்தையர் இயல்பிற் குன்றா","மடைக்கலந் தன்னொடு மாண்புடை மரபிற்","கோளிப் பாகற் கொழுங்கனித் திரள்காய்","வாள்வரிக் கொடுங்காய் மாதுளம் பசுங்காய்","மாவின் கனியொடு வாழைத் தீங்கனி","சாலி யரிசி தம்பாற் பயனொடு","கோல்வளை மாதே கொள்கெனக் கொடுப்ப","மெல்விரல் சிவப்பப் பல்வேறு பசுங்காய்","கொடுவாய்க் குயத்து விடுவாய் செய்யத்","திருமுகம் வியர்த்தது செங்கண் சேந்தன","கரிபுற அட்டில் கண்டனள் பெயர"],"p":"","r":"Maadari bids the herdswomen bring at once good vessels for cooking the day's meal, since Kovalan is a lord who keeps the lay disciple's vow, and they bring the vessels with gourds, vegetables, pomegranate, mango and plantain, rice and milk, which Kannaki takes and begins to cut and cook until her face sweats and her eyes redden in the smoky kitchen.","n":"சாவக நோன்பிகள் names Kovalan as one under the vow of a lay disciple; in the preceding kaathai the word சாவகர் is used for the lay hearers of the Jain charanas (15:190, 195), and the meal of the day (நாள்வழிப் படூஉம் அடிசில்) may point to the lay rule of eating before dark, though the Tamil does not say so. The merchant's daughter cooking with her own hands, her soft fingers reddening, is the first housekeeping she has done since Puhar. நும்மகனார் and நாத்தூண் நங்கை (the husband's sister) give the herders kin-terms for their guests, but whom each names is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nonbu","virundhu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 17-32","lb":"SI16:17-32","ev":"The herdswomen bring vessels and provisions at Maadari's word, and Kannaki starts cooking.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரி","ஆய்ச்சியர்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:33-47":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":225,"t":["வையெரி மூட்டிய ஐயை தன்னொடு","கையறி மடைமையிற் காதலற் காக்கித்","தாலப் புல்லின் வால்வெண் தோட்டுக்","கைவன் மகடூஉக் கவின்பெறப் புனைந்த","செய்வினைத் தவிசிற் செல்வன் இருந்தபின்","கடிமல ரங்கையிற் காதல னடிநீர்","சுடுமண் மண்டையில் தொழுதனள் மாற்றி","மண்ணக மடந்தையை மயக்கொழிப் பனள்போல்","தண்ணீர் தெளித்துத் தன்கையால் தடவிக்","குமரி வாழையின் குருத்தகம் விரித்தீங்கு","அமுத முண்க அடிக ளீங்கென","அரசர் பின்னோர்க் கருமறை மருங்கின்","உரிய வெல்லாம் ஒருமுறை கழித்தாங்கு","ஆயர் பாடியின் அசோதைபெற் றெடுத்த","பூவைப் புதுமலர் வண்ணன் கொல்லோ"],"p":"","r":"With Aiyai lighting the fire, Kannaki cooks with her untrained hands, seats Kovalan on a mat of palm leaf, washes his feet from an earthen bowl, sprinkles water on the ground as if to clear the earth goddess's distress, spreads a young plantain leaf and bids him eat, and he eats after completing the rites proper to the merchant order, while the herders' women begin to wonder whether he is the dark god Yasoda bore.","n":"Kovalan, just called a lay disciple under vow, first performs what the sacred text (அருமறை) prescribes for those after kings (அரசர் பின்னோர், the merchant order), so the meal joins a vow-keeper's diet to caste rite without comment. The water sprinkled 'as if to clear the bewilderment of the earth-woman' (மண்ணக மடந்தையை மயக்கொழிப் பனள்போல், line 40) returns at line 215, when the same earth-woman's grief grows under his blood. The unit ends in the middle of the women's question, which runs on into the next.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","vazhipaadu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 33-47","lb":"SI16:33-47","ev":"Kannaki cooks with Aiyai's help, washes Kovalan's feet and serves him, and he eats after his rites.","cl":[],"ag":["ஐயை","கண்ணகி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:48-61":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":226,"t":["நல்லமு துண்ணும் நம்பி யீங்குப்","பல்வளைத் தோளியும் பண்டுநங் குலத்துத்","தொழுனை யாற்றினுள் தூமணி வண்ணனை","விழுமந் தீர்த்த விளக்குக் கொல்லென","ஐயையுந் தவ்வையும் விம்மிதம் எய்திக்","கண்கொளா நமக் கிவர் காட்சி யீங்கென","உண்டினி திருந்த உயர்பே ராளற்கு","அம்மென் திரையலோ டடைக்கா யீத்த","மையீ ரோதியை வருகெனப் பொருந்திக்","கல்லதர் அத்தம் கடக்க யாவதும்","வல்லுந கொல்லோ மடந்தைமெல் லடியென","வெம்முனை யருஞ்சுரம் போந்ததற் கிரங்கி","எம்முது குரவர் என்னுற் றனர்கொல்","மாயங் கொல்லோ வல்வினை கொல்லோ"],"p":"","r":"Aiyai and her mother marvel that the man eating is the god of the flower-dark hue and the woman the lamp of their own clan who once relieved his distress in the Yamuna, a sight their eyes cannot hold; Kannaki gives Kovalan betel, and he draws her close, asks how her soft feet bore the stony desert path, and wonders aloud what has befallen his old parents and whether this is illusion or a strong former deed.","n":"The herders see their guests as Krishna and his herd-clan consort (the Tamil does not name Pinnai here), the pair whose kuravai Maadari will dance in the next kaathai to ward off coming harm. Kovalan's மாயங் கொல்லோ வல்வினை கொல்லோ is a question, not a claim: he does not yet know whether to blame illusion or karma, and in the next lines he blames himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 48-61","lb":"SI16:48-61","ev":"Aiyai and Maadari liken the couple to Krishna and his consort; Kovalan, given betel, asks Kannaki how she bore the road.","cl":[],"ag":["ஐயை","மாதரி","கண்ணகி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஐயை",""],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:62-73":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":227,"t":["யானுளங் கலங்கி யாவதும் அறியேன்","வறுமொழி யாளரொடு வம்பப் பரத்தரொடு","குறுமொழி கோட்டி நெடுநகை புக்குப்","பொச்சாப் புண்டு பொருளுரை யாளர்","நச்சுக்கொன் றேற்கும் நன்னெறி யுண்டோ","இருமுது குரவ ரேவலும் பிழைத்தேன்","சிறுமுதுக் குறைவிக்குச் சிறுமையுஞ் செய்தேன்","வழுவெனும் பாரேன் மாநகர் மருங்கீண்டு","எழுகென எழுந்தாய் என்செய் தனையென","அறவோர்க் களித்தலும் அந்தண ரோம்பலும்","துறவோர்க் கெதிர்தலும் தொல்லோர் சிறப்பின்","விருந்தெதிர் கோடலும் இழந்த என்னைநும்"],"p":"","r":"Kovalan says his mind is confused and he knows nothing, that keeping company with men of empty talk and idle rakes he grew heedless, that he failed the bidding of his parents and wronged his wise young wife, and asks what she has done in rising to come with him to this city; Kannaki begins her answer, that she had lost the giving to the virtuous, the tending of Brahmins, the receiving of ascetics and the welcoming of guests.","n":"Karma and his own fault stand side by side: the line before asked whether this was வல்வினை, and now he names பொச்சாப்பு, bad company and disobedience as his own failures. Lines 65-66 (பொருளுரை யாளர் நச்சுக்கொன் றேற்கும் நன்னெறி யுண்டோ) are hard; they seem to ask whether there is any good way left for one who has spurned the counsel of the wise, but the sense of நச்சு is doubtful. Kannaki's four lost duties are the householder's duties, which she could not keep without her husband.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","vinai","ilvaazhkkai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 62-73","lb":"SI16:62-73","ev":"Kovalan confesses his heedlessness and wrongs, and Kannaki begins to answer.","cl":[["a wife left by her husband loses the householder's duties of giving to the virtuous, tending Brahmins, receiving ascetics and welcoming guests","general","asserted",71,"kannaki"]],"ag":["கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோவலன்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:74-90":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":228,"t":["பெருமக டன்னொடும் பெரும்பெயர்த் தலைத்தாள்","மன்பெருஞ் சிறப்பின் மாநிதிக் கிழவன்","முந்தை நில்லா முனிவிகந் தனனா","அற்புளஞ் சிறந்தாங் கருண்மொழி அளைஇ","எற்பா ராட்ட யானகத் தொளித்த","நோயும் துன்பமும் நொடிவது போலுமென்","வாயல் முறுவற்கவர் உள்ளகம் வருந்தப்","போற்றா வொழுக்கம் புரிந்தீர் யாவதும்","மாற்றா உள்ள வாழ்க்கையே னாதலின்","ஏற்றெழுந் தனன்யான் என்றவள் கூறக்","குடிமுதற் சுற்றமும் குற்றிளை யோரும்","அடியோர் பாங்கும் ஆயமும் நீங்கி","நாணமும் மடனும் நல்லோ ரேத்தும்","பேணிய கற்பும் பெருந்துணை யாக","என்னொடு போந்தீங் கென்றுயர் களைந்த","பொன்னே கொடியே புனைபூங் கோதாய்","நாணின் பாவாய் நீணில விளக்கே"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki goes on: when his mother and his wealthy father set aside their old anger and praised her lovingly, her smile that seemed to deny the grief she hid only pained them more; though he followed unworthy ways, she, whose life does not gainsay him, rose and came; Kovalan answers by praising her, who left kin, servants and companions with modesty, innocence and chastity as her only companions and has removed his sorrow, as gold, creeper, doll of modesty and lamp of the wide earth.","n":"Kannaki's reply blames his conduct plainly (போற்றா வொழுக்கம் புரிந்தீர்) while setting her own rule as not opposing him (மாற்றா உள்ள வாழ்க்கை); the epic lets her name the fault and still follow. The grief she hid behind a smile before his parents is told only here. Kovalan's praise counts karpu as a companion (பெருந்துணை) in place of the kin and servants she left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","naanam","vaazhkkaithunai","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 74-90","lb":"SI16:74-90","ev":"Kannaki tells why she followed Kovalan, and he praises her chastity and companionship.","cl":[["a chaste wife does not gainsay her husband even when his conduct is unworthy","general","asserted",82,"kannaki"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:91-112":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":229,"t":["கற்பின் கொழுந்தே பொற்பின் செல்வி","சீறடிச் சிலம்பி னொன்றுகொண் டியான்போய்","மாறி வருவன் மயங்கா தொழிகெனக்","கருங்கயல் நெடுங்கட் காதலி தன்னை","ஒருங்குடன் தழீஇ உழையோ ரில்லா","ஒருதனி கண்டுதன் உள்ளகம் வெதும்பி","வருபனி கரந்த கண்ண னாகிப்","பல்லான் கோவல ரில்லம் நீங்கி","வல்லா நடையின் மறுகிற் செல்வோன்","இமிலே றெதிர்ந்த திழுக்கென அறியான்","தன்குலம் அறியுந் தகுதியன் றாதலின்","தாதெரு மன்றந் தானுடன் கழிந்து","மாதர் வீதி மறுகிடை நடந்து","பீடிகைத் தெருவிற் பெயர்வோன் ஆங்கண்","கண்ணுள் வினைஞர் கைவினை முற்றிய","நுண்வினைக் கொல்லர் நூற்றுவர் பின்வர","மெய்ப்பை புக்கு விலங்குநடைச் செலவின்","கைக்கோற் கொல்லனைக் கண்டன னாகித்","தென்னவன் பெயரொடு சிறப்புப் பெற்ற","பொன்வினைக் கொல்லன் இவனெனப் பொருந்திக்","காவலன் றேவிக் காவதோர் காற்கணி","நீவிலை யிடுதற் காதி யோவென"],"p":"","r":"Calling her the shoot of chastity, Kovalan says he will take one anklet from her small foot, go and exchange it and return, embraces her alone with burning heart and hidden tears, and leaves; walking unsteadily through the streets he does not know as an ill omen the humped bull that comes against him, since reading such signs is not his class's knowledge, and in the bazaar street he meets a goldsmith with a hundred craftsmen behind him and asks whether he can price an anklet fit for the queen.","n":"The narrator excuses Kovalan's blindness to the omen by his birth (தன்குலம் அறியுந் தகுதியன்று), not by his fault; the herders, whose knowledge it is, will read their own bull's tears as coming harm in the next kaathai. The goldsmith is drawn before he speaks, walking aslant (விலங்குநடை) in a tunic with tongs in hand, and Kovalan takes him for the craftsman honoured with the Pandya's name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam","pirivu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 91-112","lb":"SI16:91-112","ev":"Kovalan takes one anklet, leaves Kannaki, passes an ill omen unheeded and asks a goldsmith to price the anklet.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலன்","பொன்செய் கொல்லன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:113-129":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":230,"t":["அடியேன் அறியே னாயினும் வேந்தர்","முடிமுதற் கலன்கள் சமைப்பேன் யானெனக்","கூற்றத் தூதன் கைதொழு தேத்தப்","போற்றருஞ் சிலம்பின் பொதிவா யவிழ்த்தனன்","மத்தக மணியோடு வயிரம் கட்டிய","பத்திக் கேவணப் பசும்பொற் குடைச்சூற்","சித்திரச் சிலம்பின் செய்வினை யெல்லாம்","பொய்த்தொழிற் கொல்லன் புரிந்துடன் நோக்கிக்","கோப்பெருந் தேவிக் கல்லதை இச்சிலம்பு","யாப்புற வில்லை யெனமுன் போந்து","விறல்மிகு வேந்தற்கு விளம்பியான் வரவென்","சிறுகுடி லங்கண் இருமின் நீரெனக்","கோவலன் சென்றக் குறுமக னிருக்கையோர்","தேவ கோட்டச் சிறையகம் புக்கபின்","கரந்தியான் கொண்ட காலணி ஈங்குப்","பரந்து வெளிப்படா முன்னம் மன்னற்குப்","புலம்பெயர் புதுவனிற் போக்குவன் யானெனக்"],"p":"","r":"The goldsmith, a messenger of Death, bows and says he makes the crowns and jewels of kings, examines the anklet's workmanship, declares that it fits none but the queen, tells Kovalan to wait in his hut near the temple while he informs the king, and resolves to pass off on this stranger the queen's anklet that he himself had stolen before his theft comes to light.","n":"The narrator reveals the goldsmith as the real thief here, before the accusation, so the reader watches the whole frame-up knowingly. His verdict that the anklet fits only the queen is the claim the breaking of the anklet will disprove (20:74-91), since Kannaki's holds gems and the queen's pearls.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","vanjam","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 113-129","lb":"SI16:113-129","ev":"The goldsmith inspects the anklet, lodges Kovalan in his hut and plans to blame him for his own theft.","cl":[],"ag":["பொன்செய் கொல்லன்","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பொன்செய் கொல்லன்","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:130-147":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":231,"t":["கலங்கா வுள்ளம் கரந்தனன் செல்வோன்","கூடன் மகளிர் ஆடல் தோற்றமும்","பாடற் பகுதியும் பண்ணின் பயங்களும்","காவல னுள்ளம் கவர்ந்தன என்றுதன்","ஊட லுள்ளம் உள்கரந் தொளித்துத்","தலைநோய் வருத்தந் தன்மே லிட்டுக்","குலமுதல் தேவி கூடா தேக","மந்திரச் சுற்றம் நீங்கி மன்னவன்","சிந்தரி நெடுங்கட் சிலதியர் தம்மொடு","கோப்பெருந் தேவி கோயில் நோக்கிக்","காப்புடை வாயிற் கடைகாண் அகவையின்","வீழ்ந்தனன் கிடந்து தாழ்ந்துபல ஏத்திக்","கன்னக மின்றியும் கவைக்கோ லின்றியும்","துன்னிய மந்திரந் துணையெனக் கொண்டு","வாயி லாளரை மயக்குதுயி லுறுத்துக்","கோயிற் சிலம்பு கொண்ட கள்வன்","கல்லென் பேரூர்க் காவலர்க் கரந்தென்","சில்லைச் சிறுகுடி லகத்திருந் தோனென"],"p":"","r":"The goldsmith goes on with calm hidden heart; the king, whose queen, sulking because the dances and songs of Madurai's women had captured his heart, had pleaded a headache and gone away, leaves his council for her palace with her maids, and at its gate the goldsmith falls at his feet and tells him that the thief who took the palace anklet without breaching wall or using a forked pole, putting the gatekeepers to sleep by a spell, is hiding in his hut.","n":"The accusation reaches the king when he has left his ministers (மந்திரச் சுற்றம் நீங்கி) to make peace with a sulking queen; the epic gives this setting without naming it a cause, and in the next lines the narrator gives the cause as the ripening of a deed. The goldsmith's tale of a thief working by mantra prepares his later lecture on thieves' arts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","vanjam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 130-147","lb":"SI16:130-147","ev":"At the queen's gate the goldsmith tells the king that the thief of the palace anklet is in his hut.","cl":[],"ag":["பொன்செய் கொல்லன்","கோப்பெருந்தேவி","நெடுஞ்செழியன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பொன்செய் கொல்லன்","நெடுஞ்செழியன்"],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:148-163":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":232,"t":["வினைவிளை கால மாதலின் யாவதும்","சினையலர் வேம்பன் தேரான் ஆகி","ஊர்காப் பாளரைக் கூவி ஈங்கென்","தாழ்பூங் கோதை தன்காற் சிலம்பு","கன்றிய கள்வன் கைய தாகில்","கொன்றச் சிலம்பு கொணர்க ஈங்கெனக்","காவலன் ஏவக் கருந்தொழிற் கொல்லனும்","ஏவ லுள்ளத் தெண்ணியது முடித்தெனத்","தீவினை முதிர்வலைச் சென்றுபட் டிருந்த","கோவலன் றன்னைக் குறுகின னாகி","வலம்படு தானை மன்னவன் ஏவச்","சிலம்பு காணிய வந்தோர் இவரெனச்","செய்வினைச் சிலம்பின் செய்தி யெல்லாம்","பொய்வினைக் கொல்லன் புரிந்துடன் காட்ட","இலக்கண முறைமையின் இருந்தோன் ஈங்கிவன்","கொலைப்படு மகனலன் என்றுகூறும்"],"p":"","r":"Since it was the time for the deed to ripen, the neem-garlanded king, inquiring into nothing, calls the city guards and orders them to kill the thief if he holds the queen's anklet and bring it; the goldsmith, his purpose achieved, brings them to Kovalan, caught in the net of his ripened evil deed, and shows them the anklet, but the guards say that a man who bears himself by the marks of good birth is not one to be put to death.","n":"One ripening is given two subjects: the time of the deed (வினைவிளை காலம்) makes the king fail to inquire, and the net of the matured evil deed (தீவினை முதிர்வலை) holds Kovalan, so whose deed ripens is left open between them. The order கொன்றச் சிலம்பு கொணர்க condemns before any hearing. The guards' doubt is the one hesitation in the chain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","therithal","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 148-163","lb":"SI16:148-163","ev":"The king, without inquiry, orders the thief killed; the goldsmith leads the guards to Kovalan, who they say does not look like a thief.","cl":[["when the time for a deed to ripen comes, even a king fails to inquire","general","asserted",148,"narrator"],["a man who bears the marks of good birth is not one to be put to death","general","reported",163,"kaavalar"]],"ag":["நெடுஞ்செழியன்","காவலர்","பொன்செய் கொல்லன்","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:164-177":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":233,"t":["அருந்திறல் மாக்களை அகநகைத் துரைத்துக்","கருந்தொழிற் கொல்லன் காட்டின னுரைப்போன்","மந்திரம் தெய்வம் மருந்தே நிமித்தம்","தந்திரம் இடனே காலம் கருவியென்று","எட்டுட னன்றே இழுக்குடை மரபிற்","கட்டுண் மாக்கள் துணையெனத் திரிவது","மருந்திற் பட்டீ ராயின் யாவரும்","பெரும்பெயர் மன்னனிற் பெருநவைப் பட்டீர்","மந்திர நாவிடை வழுத்துவ ராயின்","இந்திர குமரரின் யாங்காண் குவமோ","தெய்வத் தோற்றம் தெளிகுவ ராயின்","கையகத் துறுபொருள் காட்டியும் பெயர்குவர்","மருந்தின் நங்கண் மயக்குவ ராயின்","இருந்தோம் பெயரும் இடனுமா ருண்டோ"],"p":"","r":"Laughing inwardly at the guards, the goldsmith lectures them that thieves roam with eight aids, mantra, deity, drug, omen, technique, place, time and tool, and that if they use a drug the guards will suffer worse than from the king, if mantra they will be as invisible as Indra's sons, if a divine form they will show the stolen thing in hand and still go, and if they cloud the guards' eyes with drugs, the guards will not even keep their names and place.","n":"The real thief makes himself the expert on theft and turns the guards' good judgement of Kovalan's bearing into a sign of the thief's skill: the better the man looks, the more dangerous. The eightfold list is set out as a lore with its own order (இழுக்குடை மரபு), named a faulty tradition even in his mouth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","vanjam","mandhiram","nimittam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 164-177","lb":"SI16:164-177","ev":"The goldsmith lectures the guards on the eight aids of thieves.","cl":[["thieves work by eight aids: mantra, deity, drug, omen, technique, place, time and tool","general","reported",166,"porkollan"]],"ag":["பொன்செய் கொல்லன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பொன்செய் கொல்லன்","காவலர்"],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:178-193":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":234,"t":["நிமித்தம் வாய்த்திடி னல்ல தியாவதும்","புகற்கிலா அரும்பொருள் வந்துகைப் புகுதினும்","தந்திர கரணம் எண்ணுவ ராயின்","இந்திரன் மார்பத் தாரமும் எய்துவர்","இவ்விடம் இப்பொருள் கோடற் கிடமெனின்","அவ்விடத் தவரை யார்காண் கிற்பார்","காலங் கருதி அவர்பொருள் கையுறின்","மேலோ ராயினும் விலக்கலு முண்டோ","கருவி கொண்டவர் அரும்பொருள் கையுறின்","இருநில மருங்கின் யார்காண் கிற்பார்","இரவே பகலே என்றிரண் டில்லை","கரவிடங் கேட்பினோர் புகலிட மில்லை","தூதர் கோலத்து வாயிலின் இருந்து","மாதர் கோலத்து வல்லிருட் புக்கு","விளக்கு நிழலில் துளக்கிலன் சென்றாங்கு","இளங்கோ வேந்தன் துளங்கொளி ஆரம்"],"p":"","r":"The goldsmith goes on through the other aids, that with a true omen a priceless thing falls into their hands, with technique they would take even the garland from Indra's chest, with the right place and time no one sees or stops them, with tools no one on earth can catch them, and night and day are one to them, and begins a tale of a thief who in a messenger's guise and then a woman's slipped through the dark and the lamp's shadow toward a prince's shining necklace.","n":"The lecture goes item by item in the order of the list (omen, technique, place, time, tool), and its claim that the thief has no refuge if one asks for his hiding place (கரவிடங் கேட்பினோர் புகலிட மில்லை) presses the guards to act at once. The unit breaks in the middle of the tale, whose verb comes in line 194.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","vanjam","nimittam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 178-193","lb":"SI16:178-193","ev":"","cl":[["a thief armed with these arts can be seen by no one, by night or by day","general","reported",188,"porkollan"]],"ag":["பொன்செய் கொல்லன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பொன்செய் கொல்லன்","காவலர்"],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:194-217":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":235,"t":["வெயிலிடு வயிரத்து மின்னின் வாங்கத்","துயில்கண் விழித்தோன் தோளிற் காணான்","உடைவாள் உருவ உறைகை வாங்கி","எறிதொறுஞ் செறித்த இயல்பிற் காற்றான்","மல்லிற் காண மணித்தூண் காட்டிக்","கல்வியிற் பெயர்ந்த கள்வன் றன்னைக்","கண்டோர் உளரெனிற் காட்டும் ஈங்கிவர்க்","குண்டோ வுலகத் தொப்போ ரென்றக்","கருந்தொழிற் கொல்லன் சொல்ல ஆங்கோர்","திருந்துவேற் றடக்கை இளையோன் கூறும்","நிலனகழ் உளியன் நீலத் தானையன்","கலன்நசை வேட்கையிற் கடும்புலி போன்று","மாரி நடுநாள் வல்லிருள் மயக்கத்து","ஊர்மடி கங்குல் ஒருவன் தோன்றக்","கைவாள் உருவஎன் கைவாள் வாங்க","எவ்வாய் மருங்கினும் யானவற் கண்டிலேன்","அரிதிவர் செய்தி அலைக்கும் வேந்தனும்","உரிய தொன் றுரைமின் உறுபடை யீரெனக்","கல்லாக் களிமக னொருவன் கையில்","வெள்வாள் எறிந்தனன் விலங்கூ டறுத்தது","புண்ணுமிழ் குருதி பொழிந்துடன் பரப்ப","மண்ணக மடந்தை வான்றுயர் கூரக்","காவலன் செங்கோல் வளைஇய வீழ்ந்தனன்","கோவலன் பண்டை ஊழ்வினை உருத்தென்."],"p":"","r":"The goldsmith ends his tale, how the thief took the prince's necklace, dodged his sword and slipped away, and asks who in the world can match such men; a young spearman tells of a thief with a digging chisel and blue cloth who took his own sword from his hand one rainy midnight and vanished, and bids the guards do what is fit, since such men's deeds are hard to know and the king will punish them; then a drunken, unlettered guard throws his sword and cuts Kovalan across, his blood floods the ground and the earth goddess grieves, and Kovalan falls as the king's sceptre bends, his former deed come in wrath.","n":"The killer is not the king or the guards who doubted but a கல்லாக் களிமகன், a drunken man without learning, so the bent sceptre works through its least fit instrument. The last two lines give two causes in one sentence, the king's sceptre bent (செங்கோல் வளைஇய) and Kovalan's former deed come in wrath (பண்டை ஊழ்வினை உருத்து), the patikam's first and third morals at once. Line 210 (அலைக்கும் வேந்தனும்) is read as the king who will punish them, but the sense is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","kodungol","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 194-217","lb":"SI16:194-217","ev":"After the goldsmith's and a young guard's tales of uncatchable thieves, a drunken guard cuts Kovalan down with his sword.","cl":[["a man's former deed comes in wrath and brings his death","general","asserted",217,"narrator"],["when an innocent man is killed at the king's order, the king's just sceptre has bent","general","asserted",216,"narrator"]],"ag":["பொன்செய் கொல்லன்","இளையோன்","களிமகன்","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:21"},"si:16:218-221":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":236,"t":["நண்ணும் இருவினையும் நண்ணுமின்கள் நல்லறமே","கண்ணகி தன் கேள்வன் காரணத்தான்-மண்ணில்","வளையாத செங்கோல் வளைந்ததே பண்டை","விளைவாகி வந்த வினை."],"p":"","r":"The closing venba bids all, since both kinds of deed will surely reach them, to turn to good dharma, for the never-bending sceptre bent on earth because of Kannaki's husband, the fruit of an old deed come to ripeness.","n":"The moral drawn is not only that kings should judge carefully but that every hearer should do good, since good and evil deeds alike come back; the bent sceptre is made the fruit of Kovalan's old deed, which lifts the blame from the king in the same breath as it names the bending.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","aram","sengol","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 16, lines 218-221","lb":"SI16:218-221","ev":"","cl":[["both good and evil deeds come back to their doer, so one should turn to good dharma","general","asserted",218,"narrator"],["the bending of a just king's sceptre can be the fruit of another's former deed","general","asserted",220,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:21"},"si:17:1-10":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":237,"t":["கயலெழுதிய இமயநெற்றியின்","அயலெழுதிய புலியும்வில்லும்","நாவலந்தண் பொழின்மன்னர்","ஏவல்கேட்பப் பாரரசாண்ட","மாலை வெண்குடைப் பாண்டியன் கோயிலில்","காலை முரசங் கனைகுர லியம்புமாகலின்","நெய்ம்முறை நமக்கின் றாமென்று","ஐயைதன் மகளைக் கூஉய்க்","கடைகயிறு மத்துங் கொண்டு","இடைமுதுமகள் வந்துதோன்றுமன்;"],"p":"","r":"Because the morning drum will sound in the palace of the Pandya, whose fish is inscribed on the brow of the Himalaya beside the tiger and the bow and whose orders the kings of Navalam obey, the old herdswoman, saying that today is their turn to bring ghee, calls her daughter Aiyai and comes out with churning rope and churn.","n":"The kaathai opens on the Pandya's glory and the ordinary round of service his house draws from the herders (நெய்ம்முறை, their turn to supply ghee) on the morning after his men have killed Kovalan; the king is praised by his emblem and not named. Unlike the Kalittokai's boast that the fish replaced the tiger and the bow, the fish here stands beside them (அயலெழுதிய).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 1-10","lb":"SI17:1-10","ev":"Madari, saying it is their turn to supply ghee to the palace, calls her daughter Aiyai and comes with churning rope and churn.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:11-12":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":238,"t":["குடப்பால் உறையா குவியிமில் ஏற்றின்","மடக்கணீர் சோரும் வருவதொன் றுண்டு;"],"p":"","r":"The first sign: the milk in the pots will not set, and tears run from the eyes of the bull with the heaped hump; something is coming.","n":"Each of the three couplets ends on the same refrain (வருவதொன் றுண்டு), and the prose that follows (lines 17 to 20) gathers them as the old woman's own reading, so the voice is hers though no line names her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 11-12","lb":"SI17:11-12","ev":"The milk in the pots fails to curdle and the bull sheds tears.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:13-14":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":239,"t":["உறிநறு வெண்ணெய் உருகா உருகும்","மறிதெறித் தாடா வருவதொன் றுண்டு;"],"p":"","r":"The second sign: the fragrant butter in the hanging pot does not melt, and the lambs do not leap and play; something is coming.","n":"Line 13 reads உருகா உருகும், which taken literally sets 'does not melt' against 'melts'; the prose restatement at lines 18 and 19 has only உருகாமையும் (its not melting), and this reading follows it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nimittam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 13-14","lb":"SI17:13-14","ev":"The butter will not melt and the lambs lie still.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:15-16":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":240,"t":["நான்முலை யாயம் நடுங்குபு நின்றிரங்கும்","மான்மணி வீழும் வருவதொன் றுண்டு;"],"p":"","r":"The third sign: the cows stand trembling and lowing in pity, and the bells of the cattle fall; something is coming.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nimittam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 15-16","lb":"SI17:15-16","ev":"The cows tremble and low, and the cattle's bells drop to the ground.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:17-26":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":241,"t":["குடத்துப்பா லுறையாமையும் குவியிமி லேற்றின்","மடக்கண்ணீர் சோர்தலும் உறியில் வெண்ணெ","யுருகாமையும் மறி முடங்கியாடாமையும் மான்மணி","நிலத்தற்று வீழ்தலும் வருவதோர் துன்பமுண்டென","மகளை நோக்கி மனமயங்காதே மண்ணின்","மாதர்க்கணியாகிய கண்ணகியுந்தான்காண","ஆயர்பாடியில் எருமன்றத்து மாயவனுடன் தம்முன் ஆடிய","வாலசரிதை நாடகங்களில் வேல் நெடுங்கண்","பிஞ்ஞையோ டாடிய குரவை யாடுதும் யாமென்றாள்","கறவை கன்று துயர் நீங்குகவெனவே;"],"p":"","r":"Summing up the signs as a sorrow to come, the old woman tells her daughter not to be troubled: with Kannaki, the ornament of the women of the earth, looking on, they will dance the kuravai that Mayavan danced with his elder brother and with long-eyed Pinnai among the boyhood plays of the cattle-yard of Ayarpadi, so that the distress of the cows and calves may pass.","n":"The remedy is a rite: a re-enactment of Krishna's childhood dance among the herds, set against an evil the signs do not name. The reader knows what the herders do not, that Kovalan was killed the day before (16:194-217), so the distress of cows and calves the dance is meant to lift stands for a loss already done. Kannaki is placed as the dance's onlooker, not a dancer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuravai","nimittam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 17-26","lb":"SI17:17-26","ev":"Madari reads the omens as a coming sorrow and resolves to dance the kuravai of Mayavan before Kannaki to avert it.","cl":[["dancing the kuravai that Mayavan danced among the herds will drive away the distress the omens foretell","vaishnava","reported",25,"maadhari"]],"ag":["மாதரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:27-28":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":242,"t":["காரி கதனஞ்சான் பாய்ந்தானைக் காமுறுமிவ்","வேரி மலர்க் கோதையாள்;"],"p":"","r":"This girl, garlanded with honeyed flowers, will love the man who, unafraid of the black bull's fury, leapt upon it.","n":"The pledge is the bull-catch formula of the herders (காரி கதன் அஞ்சான், as in Kalittokai 104), but here no bull is penned and no man leaps: the old woman names each girl by the bull her tamer would take, as the first of the dance's roles. The speaker is inferred from lines 41 to 44, where she sets the seven girls in order.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 27-28","lb":"SI17:27-28","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:29-30":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":243,"t":["நெற்றிச் செகிலை யடர்த்தாற் குரியவிப்","பொற்றொடி மாதராள் தோள்;"],"p":"","r":"The shoulders of this girl with golden bangles belong to the man who subdues the red bull with the mark on its brow.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 29-30","lb":"SI17:29-30","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:31-32":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":244,"t":["மல்லல் மழவிடை யூர்ந்தாற் குரியளிம்","முல்லையம் பூங்குழல் தான்;"],"p":"","r":"This girl with mullai flowers in her hair belongs to the man who rides the strong young bull.","n":"This couplet and lines 37 to 38 both pledge a girl to the rider of a young bull (மழவிடை யூர்ந்தாற்கு), told apart only by the epithets மல்லல் and வென்றி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 31-32","lb":"SI17:31-32","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:33-34":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":245,"t":["நுண்பொறி வெள்ளை யடர்த்தாற்கே யாகுமிப்","பெண்கொடி மாதர்தன் தோள்;"],"p":"","r":"The shoulders of this creeper-like girl go only to the man who subdues the white bull with fine spots.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 33-34","lb":"SI17:33-34","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:35-36":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":246,"t":["பொற்பொறி வெள்ளை யடர்த்தார்க்கே யாகுமிந்","நற்கொடி மென்முலை தான்;"],"p":"","r":"This good, creeper-like girl with soft breasts goes only to the men who subdue the white bull with golden spots.","n":"Nearly the same couplet as lines 33 to 34, with பொற்பொறி for நுண்பொறி and the plural அடர்த்தார்க்கே for the singular.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 35-36","lb":"SI17:35-36","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:37-38":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":247,"t":["வென்றி மழவிடை யூர்ந்தாற் குரியளிக்","கொன்றையம் பூங்குழ லாள்;"],"p":"","r":"This girl with konrai flowers in her hair belongs to the man who rides the victorious young bull.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 37-38","lb":"SI17:37-38","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:39-40":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":248,"t":["தூநிற வெள்ளை அடர்த்தாற் குரியளிப்","பூவைப் புதுமல ராள்;"],"p":"","r":"This girl like a fresh poovai flower belongs to the man who subdues the pure white bull.","n":"With the black bull of line 27, this makes seven bulls for seven girls.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 39-40","lb":"SI17:39-40","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாதரி","ஐயை"],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:41-50":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":249,"t":["ஆங்கு,","தொழுவிடை ஏறு குறித்து வளர்த்தார்","எழுவரிளங் கோதை யார்","என்றுதன் மகளை நோக்கித்","தொன்றுபடு முறையால் நிறுத்தி","இடைமுது மகளிவர்க்குப்","படைத்துக்கோட் பெயரிடுவாள்","குடமுதல் இடமுறை யாக்குரல் துத்தம்","கைக்கிளை உழைஇளி விளரி தாரமென","விரிதரு பூங்குழல் வேண்டிய பெயரே;"],"p":"","r":"So, saying to her daughter that these are the seven young girls for whom bulls were raised in the pen, the old herdswoman sets them in the ancient order and names them, going round from the west, after the seven notes: kural, tuttam, kaikkilai, uzhai, ili, vilari and taram, the names the flower-haired one wanted.","n":"The ring of dancers is laid out as the circle of the seven notes, so the dance is also a scale. Whose wish the closing words name (விரிதரு பூங்குழல்) is not clear: the old woman, her daughter, or each girl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 41-50","lb":"SI17:41-50","ev":"Madari sets the seven herd girls in a ring and names them after the seven notes.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரி","எழுவர் இளங்கோதையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:51-54":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":250,"t":["மாயவன் என்றாள் குரலை விறல்வெள்ளை","ஆயவன் என்றாள் இளிதன்னை- ஆய்மகள்","பின்னையாம் என்றாளோர் துத்தத்தை மற்றையார்","முன்னையாம் என்றாள் முறை;"],"p":"","r":"She called kural Mayavan, ili the victorious Vellai (his elder brother), and tuttam the herd girl Pinnai, and said the rest keep their former names in order.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 51-54","lb":"SI17:51-54","ev":"Madari gives three of the notes the roles of Mayavan, his elder brother and Pinnai.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:55-58":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":251,"t":["மாயவன் சீருளார் பிஞ்ஞையுந் தாரமும்","வால்வெள்ளை சீரார் உழையும் விளரியும்","கைக்கிளை பிஞ்ஞை இடத்தாள் வலத்துளாள்","முத்தைக்கு நல்விளரி தான்;"],"p":"","r":"She sets the places: Pinnai and taram stand in Mayavan's measure, uzhai and vilari in white Vellai's, kaikkilai stands at Pinnai's left, and vilari to the right.","n":"The places seem to follow the concords between the notes, so who stands beside whom is a matter of the scale. Line 58 (முத்தைக்கு நல்விளரி தான்) is obscure, and vilari is named in both lines 56 and 58.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["isai","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 55-58","lb":"SI17:55-58","ev":"Madari assigns each dancer her place in the ring.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:59-64":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":252,"t":["அவருள்,","வண்டுழாய் மாலையை மாயவன் மேலிட்டுத்","தண்டாக் குரவைதான் உள்படுவாள்-கொண்டசீர்","வையம் அளந்தான்றன் மார்பின் திருநோக்காப்","பெய்வளைக் கையாள்நம் பின்னைதா னாமென்றே","ஐயென்றா ளாயர் மகள்;"],"p":"","r":"Of them, the one who lays the cool tulasi garland on Mayavan enters the ceaseless kuravai, and the herd girl cries out in wonder that this bangled one is their own Pinnai, set beside the Tiru on the chest of him who measured the world.","n":"The sense of திருநோக்கா (line 62) is unsure: whether Pinnai is one Tiru looks upon, or one who does not look to Tiru, the herd girl ranks the herders' own Pinnai with the goddess on Vishnu's chest. Which herd girl cries out (ஆயர் மகள், line 64) is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuravai","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 59-64","lb":"SI17:59-64","ev":"The girl playing Pinnai garlands the girl playing Mayavan with tulasi and joins the dance.","cl":[],"ag":["எழுவர் இளங்கோதையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:65-75":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":253,"t":["அவர் தாம்,","செந்நிலை மண்டிலத்தாற் கற்கடகக் கைகோஒத்து","அந்நிலையே யாடற்சீ ராய்ந்துளார்- முன்னைக்","குரற்கொடி தன்கிளையை நோக்கிப் பரப்புற்ற","கொல்லைப் புனத்துக் குருந்தொசித்தாற் பாடுதும்","முல்லைத்தீம் பாணி யென்றாள்;","எனாக்,","குரன்மந்த மாக இளிசம னாக","வரன்முறையே துத்தம் வலியா உரனிலா","மந்தம் விளரி பிடிப்பாள் அவள்நட்பின்","பின்றையைப் பாட்டெடுப் பாள்;"],"p":"","r":"Standing in a true circle with their hands joined in the crab clasp, they work out the dance measures; the first, kural, looks to her kin and says they will sing the sweet mullai tune of the one who broke the kurundu tree in the field, and as she takes kural low, ili level, tuttam strong and vilari low in their due order, her companion takes up the song after her.","n":"The kurundu tree recalls a feat of Krishna's boyhood, so the tune chosen is itself a story of the god among the herds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuravai","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 65-75","lb":"SI17:65-75","ev":"The seven girls join hands in the ring and begin the mullai song.","cl":[],"ag":["எழுவர் இளங்கோதையார்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:76-78":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":254,"t":["கன்று குணிலாக் கனியுதிர்த்த மாயவன்","இன்றுநம் ஆனுள் வருமேல் அவன்வாயில்","கொன்றையந் தீங்குழல் கேளாமோ தோழீ;"],"p":"","r":"The first song: if Mayavan, who knocked down the fruit using a calf as his throwing stick, comes among our cows today, shall we not hear from his lips the sweet konrai flute, friend?","n":"The wish that the herders' god come among the cows today (இன்று) is the rite's answer to the omens, but the herdsmen's guest, whom the household likened to the gem-coloured god (16:48-61), is already dead. The konrai flute is the herders' flute of konrai pods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuravai","vazhipaadu","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 76-78","lb":"SI17:76-78","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["எழுவர் இளங்கோதையார்",""],"ch":"si:22"},"si:17:79-81":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":255,"t":["பாம்பு கயிறாக் கடல்கடைந்த மாயவன்","ஈங்குநம் ஆனுள் வருமேல் அவன்வாயில்","ஆம்பலந் தீங்குழல் கேளாமோ தோழீ;"],"p":"","r":"The herd girls open the kuravai song: if the wonder-working one who churned the sea with a snake for rope came among their cattle, would they not hear the sweet aambal-mode flute at his lips?","n":"The cosmic deed (the churning) and the herd-village wish (his flute among 'our cattle', நம் ஆனுள்) are set in one line pair; each stanza of this song names one deed of Maayavan and one flute mode (கொன்றை, ஆம்பல், முல்லை), so the song is also a demonstration of modes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuravai","iraivan","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 79-81","lb":"SI17:79-81","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:82-86":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":256,"t":["கொல்லையஞ் சாரற் குருந்தொசித்த மாயவன்","எல்லைநம் ஆனுள் வருமேல் அவன்வாயில்","முல்லையந் தீங்குழல் கேளாமோ தோழீ;","தொழுனைத் துறைவனோ டாடிய பின்னை","அணிநிறம் பாடுகேம் யாம்;"],"p":"","r":"The third stanza wishes for the mullai flute of the one who broke the kurundu tree on the hill slope, and the girls turn to sing the beauty of Pinnai who danced with the lord of the Yamuna ford.","n":"Lines 85-86 are the turn of the song from Maayavan to Pinnai (பின்னை அணிநிறம் பாடுகேம் யாம்), which the next three stanzas carry out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuravai","iraivan","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 82-86","lb":"SI17:82-86","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:87-90":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":257,"t":["இறுமென் சாயல் நுடங்க நுடங்கி","அறுவை யொளித்தான் வடிவென் கோயாம்","அறுவை யொளித்தான் அயர அயரும்","நறுமென் சாயல் முகமென் கோயாம்;"],"p":"","r":"The girls ask what words could tell the form of him who hid the garment as Pinnai's soft grace swayed, and the face of her who wilts as he plays on.","n":"The stanza is built as a chiasmus: each half-line answers the other, his form and her face, so the lovers' play is shown as mutual. The garment hidden (அறுவை யொளித்தான்) is the god's prank at the river.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kuravai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 87-90","lb":"SI17:87-90","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:91-94":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":258,"t":["வஞ்சஞ் செய்தான் தொழுனைப் புனலுள்","நெஞ்சங் கவர்ந்தாள் நிறையென் கோயாம்","நெஞ்சங் கவர்ந்தாள் நிறையும் வளையும்","வஞ்சஞ் செய்தான் வடிவென் கோயாம்;"],"p":"","r":"The girls ask what to call the self-possession of her who stole the heart of the trickster in the Yamuna, and the form of the trickster who took her self-possession and her bangles.","n":"நிறை, a woman's self-containment, is here lost and won in a game: she takes his heart and he takes her நிறை and bangles. Who seized whose heart turns on how கவர்ந்தாள் is construed; the reading taken is that she captured his.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 91-94","lb":"SI17:91-94","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:95-98":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":259,"t":["தையல் கலையும் வளையும் இழந்தே","கையி லொளித்தாள் முகமென் கோயாம்","கையி லொளித்தாள் முகங்கண் டழுங்கி","மைய லுழந்தான் வடிவென் கோயாம்;"],"p":"","r":"The girls sing that the maiden, robbed of garment and bangles, hid her face in her hands, and that he, seeing the hidden face, suffered infatuation.","n":"The trickster ends caught himself: மைய லுழந்தான், he laboured in infatuation. The modesty of the hidden face (கையி லொளித்தாள் முகம்) is what defeats him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 95-98","lb":"SI17:95-98","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:99-102":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":260,"t":["கதிர்திகிரி யான்மறைத்த கடல்வண்ணன் இடத்துளாள்","மதிபுரையு நறுமேனித் தம்முனோன் வலத்துளாள்","பொதியவிழ் மலர்க்கூந்தற் பிஞ்ஞைசீர் புறங்காப்பார்","முதுமறைதேர் நாரதனார் முந்தைமுறை நரம்புளர்வார்;"],"p":"","r":"The dance's arrangement is told through the myth: Pinnai stands at the left of the sea-coloured one who hid the sun with his discus and at the right of his moon-fair elder, others keep her measure, and Narada, versed in the old Veda, plucks the strings in their first order.","n":"Part 1 of the kaathai gave each girl the name of a god and a note of the scale; this block names their places in the ring by the gods they play. 'Hid the sun with the discus' (கதிர்திகிரி யான்மறைத்த) alludes to a Krishna story the line does not tell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuravai","koothu","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 99-102","lb":"SI17:99-102","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:103-106":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":261,"t":["மயிலெருத் துறழ்மேனி மாயவன் வலத்துளாள்","பயிலிதழ் மலர்மேனித் தம்முனோன் இடத்துளாள்","கயிலெருத்தம் கோட்டியநம் பின்னைசீர் புறங்காப்பார்","குயிலுவருள் நாரதனார் கொளைபுணர்சீர் நரம்புளர்வார்;"],"p":"","r":"The second stanza turns the ring: Pinnai is now at the right of the peacock-necked Maayavan and the left of his elder, and Narada among the musicians plucks the strings in the tuned measure.","n":"Left and right are exchanged from the stanza before, so the pair of stanzas records the dancers moving round. கயிலெருத்தம் கோட்டிய (line 105) is a rare phrase for Pinnai's bent neck.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuravai","koothu","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 103-106","lb":"SI17:103-106","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:107-110":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":262,"t":["மாயவன்றம் முன்னினொடும் வரிவளைக்கைப் பின்னையொடும்","கோவலர்தஞ் சிறுமியர்கள் குழற்கோதை புறஞ்சோர","ஆய்வளைச்சீர்க் கடிபெயர்த்திட் டசொதையார் தொழுதேத்தத்","தாதெருமன் றத்தாடுங் குரவையோ தகவுடைத்தே;"],"p":"","r":"The dancers praise the kuravai danced on the dusty common by Maayavan with his elder and bangled Pinnai, the herd girls' hair garlands slipping and Asodhai praising with joined hands, as fitting.","n":"The mythic kuravai of Ayarpadi is the model the Madurai herdswomen are copying, so praising it praises their own dance; the herd mother Asodhai worships the child-god she raises.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuravai","koothu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 107-110","lb":"SI17:107-110","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:111-113":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":263,"t":["எல்லாநாம்,","புள்ளூர் கடவுளைப் போற்றுதும் போற்றுதும்","உள்வரிப் பாணியொன் றுற்று;"],"p":"","r":"One of the women calls all of them to praise the god whose mount is the bird, taking up the ulvari tune.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 111-113","lb":"SI17:111-113","ev":"The herdswomen call one another to praise the bird-riding god in the ulvari mode.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்","ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:114-117":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":264,"t":["கோவா மலையாரம் கோத்த கடலாரம்","தேவர்கோன் பூணாரம் தென்னர்கோன் மார்பினவே","தேவர்கோன் பூணாரம் பூண்டான் செழுந்துவரைக்","கோகுல மேய்த்துக் குருந்தொசித்தா னென்பரால்;"],"p":"","r":"The first ulvari stanza says the hill's pearl string, the sea's pearl string and the necklace of the king of the gods are on the chest of the Pandya, and that the one who wears the gods' king's necklace, they say, grazed the herds at Dvaraka and broke the kurundu tree.","n":"The 'inner vari' (உள்வரி) has the singers take on another person: the Pandya is hailed with Krishna's herd-deeds, so king and god are fused. The fusion is ironic in the story, since this king has that day ordered Kovalan's death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","iraivan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 114-117","lb":"SI17:114-117","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:118-121":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":265,"t":["பொன்னிமயக் கோட்டுப் புலிபொறித்து மண்ணாண்டான்","மன்னன் வளவன் மதிற்புகார் வாழ்வேந்தன்","மன்னன் வளவன் மதிற்புகார் வாழ்வேந்தன்","பொன்னன் திகிரிப் பொருபடையா னென்பரால்;"],"p":"","r":"The second stanza hails the Chola Valavan of walled Puhar, who carved his tiger on the Himalaya and ruled the earth, as the one, they say, who bears the golden discus as weapon.","n":"Each of the three crowns is given a mark of Vishnu: the Chola the discus (திகிரி), as the Pandya the herd-deeds and the Chera the churning. The refrain doubles line 120.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 118-121","lb":"SI17:118-121","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:122-125":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":266,"t":["முந்நீரி னுள்புக்கு மூவாக் கடம்பெறிந்தான்","மன்னர்கோச் சேரன் வளவஞ்சி வாழ்வேந்தன்","மன்னர்கோச் சேரன் வளவஞ்சி வாழ்வேந்தன்","கன்னவில் தோளோச்சிக் கடல்கடைந்தா னென்பரால்;"],"p":"","r":"The third stanza hails the Chera of prosperous Vanchi, who went into the sea and threw down the ageless kadambu, as the one who, they say, churned the sea with his stone-strong arms.","n":"The Chera's sea-raid is renamed with the churning of the ocean (கடல்கடைந்தான்), the deed this song gave Maayavan at line 79. The stanza names only 'the Chera, king of kings', not a particular Cheralathan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 122-125","lb":"SI17:122-125","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:126-129":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":267,"t":["வடவரையை மத்தாக்கி வாசுகியை நாணாக்கிக்","கடல்வண்ணன் பண்டொருநாள் கடல்வயிறு கலக்கினையே","கலக்கியகை அசோதையார் கடைகயிற்றாற் கட்டுண்கை","மலர்க்கமல உந்தியாய் மாயமோ மருட்கைத்தே;"],"p":"","r":"Addressing the lotus-naveled god, the women say that he who once churned the sea with the north mountain for staff and Vasuki for rope had the same hand bound by Asodhai's churning cord, and ask whether this is his maayam, for it bewilders them.","n":"Cosmic churning and the herd mother's churning rope (கடைகயிறு, the tool the old herdswoman carried in at line 9) meet in one hand. The refrain மாயமோ மருட்கைத்தே asks rather than explains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 126-129","lb":"SI17:126-129","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்","மாயவன்"],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:130-133":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":268,"t":["அறுபொருள் இவனென்றே அமரர்கணந் தொழுதேத்த","உறுபசியொன் றின்றியே உலகடைய உண்டனையே","உண்டவாய் களவினான் உறிவெண்ணெ யுண்டவாய்","வண்டுழாய் மாலையாய் மாயமோ மருட்கைத்தே;"],"p":"","r":"The women tell the god that, while the hosts of gods praised him as the final truth, he swallowed the whole world without hunger, and the same mouth ate stolen butter from the hanging pot.","n":"Again the god's greatest act and his smallest theft share one organ, here the mouth; 'without hunger' (உறுபசியொன் றின்றியே) marks the swallowing of the world as play, not need.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 130-133","lb":"SI17:130-133","ev":"","cl":[["the gods worship Maal as the ultimate reality","vaishnava","reported",130,"aaychiyar"]],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்","மாயவன்"],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:134-137":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":269,"t":["திரண்டமரர் தொழுதேத்தும் திருமால்நின் செங்கமல","இரண்டடியான் மூவுலகும் இருள்தீர நடந்தனையே","நடந்தஅடி பஞ்சவர்க்குத் தூதாக நடந்தஅடி","மடங்கலாய் மாறட்டாய் மாயமோ மருட்கைத்தே;"],"p":"","r":"The women tell Thirumaal that with his two red-lotus feet he strode the three worlds so their darkness lifted, that the same feet walked as envoy for the Pandavas, and that he became a lion and killed the foe.","n":"The stanza joins the cosmic stride to a humble errand, the envoy's walk, on the same feet (நடந்தஅடி repeated).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 134-137","lb":"SI17:134-137","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்","மாயவன்"],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:138-142":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":270,"t":["மூவுலகும் ஈரடியான் முறைநிரம்பா வகைமுடியத்","தாவியசே வடிசேப்பத் தம்பியொடுங் கான்போந்து","சோவரணும் போர்மடியத் தொல்லிலங்கை கட்டழித்த","சேவகன்சீர் கேளாத செவி என்ன செவியே","திருமால்சீர் கேளாத செவி என்ன செவியே;"],"p":"","r":"Turning to the third person, the women ask what ear it is that has not heard the glory of the hero who, after his feet had spanned the three worlds in two strides, went to the forest with his brother and destroyed old Lanka, and of Thirumaal.","n":"The 'praise in the third person' (படர்க்கைப் பரவல்) defines each sense organ by its devotion: an ear that has not heard his praise is no ear. The stanza ties the Vamana stride to the Rama story as one person's deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paththi","iraivan","thirunaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 138-142","lb":"SI17:138-142","ev":"","cl":[["an ear that does not hear Thirumaal's praise is no ear","vaishnava","reported",141,"aaychiyar"]],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:143-147":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":271,"t":["பெரியவனை மாயவனைப் பேருலக மெல்லாம்","விரிகமல உந்தியுடை விண்ணவனைக் கண்ணும்","திருவடியும் கையும் திருவாயும் செய்ய","கரியவனைக் காணாத கண்ணென்ண கண்ணே","கண்ணிமைத்துக் காண்பார்தம் கண்ணென்ண கண்ணே;"],"p":"","r":"The women ask what eye it is that has not seen the great dark one, the god of the lotus navel who holds all the worlds, whose eyes, feet, hands and mouth are red, and what eyes are those that blink while looking at him.","n":"Line 147 sharpens line 146: even an eye that sees him is faulted if it blinks, since the gods do not blink; the devotee should look unblinking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paththi","iraivan","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 143-147","lb":"SI17:143-147","ev":"","cl":[["an eye that has not seen Maal is no eye","vaishnava","reported",146,"aaychiyar"]],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:17:148-159":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":272,"t":["மடந்தாழு நெஞ்சத்துக் கஞ்சனார் வஞ்சம்","கடந்தானை நூற்றுவர்பால் நாற்றிசையும் போற்றப்","படர்ந்தா ரணமுழங்கப் பஞ்சவர்க்குத் தூது","நடந்தானை ஏத்தாத நாவென்ன நாவே","நாராயணா வென்னா நாவென்ன நாவே;","என்றியாம்,","கோத்த குரவையுள் ஏத்திய தெய்வநம்","ஆத்தலைப் பட்ட துயர்தீர்க்க வேத்தர்","மருள வைகல் வைகல் மாறட்டு","வெற்றி விளைப்பது மன்னோ கொற்றத்து","இடிப்படை வானவன் முடித்தலை யுடைத்த","தொடித்தோட் டென்னவன் கடிப்பிகு முரசே."],"p":"","r":"The women ask what tongue it is that does not praise or name Narayana, who overcame Kamsa's deceit and walked as envoy to the hundred for the Pandavas, then close the kuravai: may the god it praised take away the distress fallen on their cattle, and may the drum of the Pandya, who broke the crown of the thunder-armed god, bring victory day after day.","n":"The close returns to the omens that opened the kaathai (the curd that would not set, the bulls' tears): the dance was a rite to avert them. Its last wish, victory for the Pandya's drum, is set on the day that king's verdict has killed Kovalan, so the blessing turns to irony; the next kaathai brings the news. The god is asked to protect the herd, and the king's fate is asked of his drum.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirunaamam","vendhan","vazhipaadu","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 17, lines 148-159","lb":"SI17:148-159","ev":"The herdswomen end the kuravai praying that the god remove the cattle's distress and that the Pandya's drum bring victory.","cl":[["a tongue that does not say Narayana's name is no tongue","vaishnava","reported",152,"aaychiyar"],["the god praised in the kuravai will take away the distress fallen on the cattle","vaishnava","reported",155,"aaychiyar"]],"ag":["ஆய்ச்சியர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆய்ச்சியர்",""],"ch":"si:23"},"si:18:1-15":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":273,"t":["ஆங்கு,","ஆயர் முதுமகள் ஆடிய சாயலாள்","பூவும் புகையும் புனைசாந்துங் கண்ணியும்","நீடுநீர் வையை நெடுமா லடியேத்தத்","தூவித் துறைபடியப் போயினாள் மேவிக்","குரவை முடிவிலோர் ஊரரவங் கேட்டு","விரைவொடு வந்தாள் உளள்;","அவள்தான்,","சொல்லாடாள் சொல்லாடாள் நின்றாள்அந் நங்கைக்குச்","சொல்லாடும் சொல்லாடுந் தான்;","எல்லாவோ,","காதலற் காண்கிலேன் கலங்கிநோய் கைம்மிகும்","ஊதுலை தோற்க உயிர்க்கும்என் நெஞ்சன்றே","ஊதுலை தோற்க உயிர்க்கும்என் நெஞ்சாயின்","ஏதிலார் சொன்ன தெவன்வாழி யோதோழீ;"],"p":"","r":"Madari, who had gone to the long-flowing Vaigai with flowers, incense and garlands to worship Mal at the end of the kuravai, hurries back on hearing a stir in the town and stands without a word, while Kannaki, unable to find her husband, says her heart heaves like a forge's bellows and asks her friend what the strangers have been saying.","n":"The refrain form (a line, then its second half repeated as the next line with அன்றே turned to ஆயின், closing on 'what did they say, friend?') makes the dread grow through the repetition before any news is told. Madari's silence is itself the message: the poem has her come back with news and not speak it. Lines 8 to 10 play on சொல்லாடாள் and சொல்லாடும், and it is not certain whether they mean Madari's silence speaks to Kannaki or that Kannaki speaks while Madari is silent; the reading here takes the second.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","alar","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 18, lines 1-15","lb":"SI18:1-15","ev":"Madari returns from worship at the Vaigai having heard a rumour, and Kannaki, not finding Kovalan, asks her friend what people are saying.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதரி","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி",""],"ch":"si:24"},"si:18:16-33":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":274,"t":["நண்பகற் போதே நடுக்குநோய் கைம்மிகும்","அன்பனைக் காணாது அலவும்என் நெஞ்சன்றே","அன்பனைக் காணாது அலவும்என் நெஞ்சாயின்","மன்பதை சொன்ன தெவன்வாழி யோதோழீ;","தஞ்சமோ தோழீ தலைவன் வரக்காணேன்","வஞ்சமோ உண்டு மயங்கும்என் நெஞ்சன்றே","வஞ்சமோ உண்டு மயங்கும்என் நெஞ்சாயின்","எஞ்சலார் சொன்ன தெவன்வாழி யோதோழீ;","சொன்னது:-","அரைசுறை கோயில் அணியார் ஞெகிழம்","கரையாமல் வாங்கிய கள்வனாம் என்றே","கரையாமல் வாங்கிய கள்வனாம் என்றே","குரைகழல் மாக்கள் கொலைகுறித் தனரே","எனக் கேட்டு,","பொங்கி எழுந்தாள் விழுந்தாள் பொழிகதிர்த்","திங்கள் முகிலொடுஞ் சேண்நிலம் கொண்டெனச்","செங்கண் சிவப்ப அழுதாள்தன் கேள்வனை","எங்கணா என்னா இனைந்தேங்கி மாழ்குவாள்;"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki's refrain continues that she trembles at noon and suspects some treachery, and the answer comes that men say he was a thief who silently took an anklet from the king's palace and that the guards with ringing anklets set out to kill him; hearing it, she rises, falls like the moon dropping with the clouds to earth, and weeps, calling for her husband.","n":"The charge comes as hearsay: கள்வனாம் ('a thief, they say') is reported speech, and the line is repeated as the song form's refrain, so the town's word is heard twice before Kannaki falls. Her suspicion of வஞ்சம் (line 21) comes before the news and frames it as a deception. The answer does not name who tells it; the reading takes it as what the townsfolk said, carried by the friend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","kolai","alar","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 18, lines 16-33","lb":"SI18:16-33","ev":"Kannaki hears that Kovalan was called the thief of the palace anklet and marked for death by the guards, and falls weeping.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணகி","காவலர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி",""],"ch":"si:24"},"si:18:34-53":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":275,"t":["இன்புறு தங்கணவர் இடரெரி யகமூழ்கத்","துன்புறு வனநோற்றுத் துயருறு மகளிரைப்போல்","மன்பதை அலர்தூற்ற மன்னவன் தவறிழைப்ப","அன்பனை இழந்தேன்யான் அவலங்கொண் டழிவலோ;","நறைமலி வியன்மார்பின் நண்பனை இழந்தேங்கித்","துறைபல திறமூழ்கித் துயருறு மகளிரைப்போல்","மறனொடு திரியுங்கோல் மன்னவன் தவறிழைப்ப","அறனென்னும் மடவோய்யான் அவலங் கொண்டழிவலோ;","தம்முறு பெருங்கணவன் தழலெரி யகமூழ்கக்","கைம்மைகூர் துறைமூழ்குங் கவலைய மகளிரைப்போல்","செம்மையின் இகந்தகோல் தென்னவன் தவறிழைப்ப","இம்மையும் இசையொரீஇ இனைந்தேங்கி அழிவலோ;","காணிகா,","வாய்வதின் வந்த குரவையின் வந்தீண்டும்","ஆய மடமகளி ரெல்லீருங் கேட்டீமின்","ஆய மடமகளி ரெல்லீருங் கேட்டைக்க","பாய்திரை வேலிப் படுபொருள் நீயறிதி","காய்கதிர்ச் செல்வனே கள்வனோ என்கணவன்","கள்வனோ அல்லன் கருங்கயற்கண் மாதராய்","ஒள்ளெரி உண்ணுமிவ் வூரென்ற தொருகுரல்."],"p":"","r":"Kannaki asks whether, now that the king has erred and his sceptre has strayed from right, she must waste away in grief like the women who lose their husbands to the pyre and keep the hard observances of widowhood, losing even her good name in this life; then she bids the kuravai girls listen and asks the sun, who knows all in the sea-girt world, whether her husband is a thief, and a voice answers that he is not, and that fire will eat this city.","n":"In each of the three stanzas the cause is the king's fault alone (மன்னவன் தவறிழைப்ப, with the sceptre that wanders with cruelty and has gone beyond straightness), though the narrator had closed the killing with the king's bent sceptre and Kovalan's former deed together (16:216-217), and Kannaki herself will later name ஊழ்வினை before the king (20:69). Her question sets her apart from the widows who bathe at the ghats and keep the observances: the form 'shall I perish like them?' prepares her refusal of that path. அறனென்னும் மடவோய் (line 41) can be read as an address to Dharma itself as a simpleton that has failed her, or as an address to a naive woman; the sense is uncertain. The voice is unnamed here (ஒருகுரல்); the next kaathai opens என்றனன் வெய்யோன், making it the sun's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kaimmai","pugazh","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 18, lines 34-53","lb":"SI18:34-53","ev":"Kannaki laments the king's wrong, calls the sun to witness whether her husband is a thief, and a voice answers that he is innocent and that fire will consume Madurai.","cl":[["the king's sceptre has strayed from rightness, and the king's error is what has robbed her of her husband","general","asserted",44,"kannaki"],["the sun knows all that is done in the world and can bear witness to a man's innocence","general","asserted",50,"kannaki"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","காய்கதிர்ச் செல்வன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","காய்கதிர்ச் செல்வன்"],"ch":"si:24"},"si:19:1-14":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":276,"t":["என்றனன் வெய்யோன் இலங்கீர் வளைத்தோளி","நின்றிலள் நின்ற சிலம்பொன்று கையேந்தி","முறையில் அரசன்றன் ஊரிருந்து வாழும்","நிறையுடைப் பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர்காள் ஈதொன்று","பட்டேன் படாத துயரம் படுகாலை","உற்றேன் உறாதது உறுவனே ஈதொன்று","கள்வனோ அல்லன் கணவன்என் காற்சிலம்பு","கொள்ளும் விலைப்பொருட்டாற் கொன்றாரே ஈதொன்று","மாதர்த் தகைய மடவார்கண் முன்னரே","காதற் கணவனைக் காண்பனே ஈதொன்று","காதற் கணவனைக் கண்டா லவன்வாயில்","தீதறு நல்லுரை கேட்பனே ஈதொன்று","தீதறு நல்லுரை கேளா தொழிவேனேல்","நோதக்க செய்தாளென் றெள்ளல் இதுவொன்றென்று"],"p":"","r":"With the Sun's word just spoken, Kannaki does not stand still but, holding her one anklet, calls on the chaste wives living in the city of a king without justice to hear her: she has suffered what none has suffered, her husband was no thief but was killed for the price of her anklet, and she will see him before the women and hear a faultless word from his mouth, or let them scorn her as one who did a wrong.","n":"Each of her six statements closes with ஈதொன்று, 'this is one thing', so the speech is built as a count of grievances and vows. She calls her witnesses நிறையுடைப் பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர், giving the women of the city the same standing she claims, and puts herself under their judgment: if she does not hear her husband's word, they may scorn her (எள்ளல்). The charge against the king is already in her address, முறையில் அரசன், before anyone in the city has spoken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","kodungol","kalavu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 19, lines 1-14","lb":"SI19:1-14","ev":"Kannaki, holding her one anklet, calls on the chaste women of Madurai to hear that her husband was killed as a thief for the price of her anklet.","cl":[],"ag":["வெய்யோன்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர்"],"ch":"si:25"},"si:19:15-34":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":277,"t":["அல்லலுற் றாற்றா தழுவாளைக் கண்டேங்கி","மல்லல் மதுரையா ரெல்லாருந் தாமயங்கிக்","களையாத துன்பமிக் காரிகைக்குக் காட்டி","வளையாத செங்கோல் வளைந்த திதுவென்கொல்","மன்னவர் மன்னன் மதிக்குடை வாள்வேந்தன்","தென்னவன் கொற்றம் சிதைந்த திதுவென்கொல்","மண்குளிரச் செய்யும் மறவேல் நெடுந்தகை","தண்குடை வெம்மை விளைத்த திதுவென்கொல்","செம்பொற் சிலம்பொன்று கையேந்தி நம்பொருட்டால்","வம்பப் பெருந்தெய்வம் வந்த திதுவென்கொல்","ஐயரி யுண்கண் அழுதேங்கி யரற்றுவாள்","தெய்வமுற்றாள் போலுந் தகைய ளிதுவென்கொல்","என்பன சொல்லி இனைந்தேங்கி யாற்றவும்","மன்பழி தூற்றுங் குடியதே மாமதுரைக்","கம்பலை மாக்கள் கணவனைத் தாங்காட்டச்","செம்பொற் கொடியனையாள் கண்டாளைத் தான்காணான்","மல்லன்மா ஞாலம் இருளூட்டி மாமலைமேற்","செவ்வென் கதிர்சுருங்கிச் செங்கதிரோன் சென்றொளிப்பப்","புல்லென் மருள்மாலைப் பூங்கொடியாள் பூசலிட","ஒல்லென் ஒலிபடைத்த தூர்;"],"p":"","r":"The people of Madurai, seeing her weep beyond bearing, ask in bewilderment whether this is the unbending sceptre bent, the Pandya's victory broken, the cool umbrella breeding heat, a new great goddess come among them for their sake, a woman possessed; the crowd shows her her husband, she sees him who cannot see her, and as the sun hides behind the mountain her cry fills the dusk and the city resounds.","n":"The townspeople, not Kannaki, are the first to name the king's fault: their refrain இதுவென்கொல் moves from the bent sceptre and the umbrella that cools the earth now giving heat to a stranger goddess (வம்பப் பெருந்தெய்வம்) come 'for our sake', so the city reads one woman's grief at once as the failure of rule and as the arrival of a deity. Madurai is called a city whose people spread the king's blame (மன்பழி தூற்றுங் குடியதே). Line 15 (அல்லலுற் றாற்றா தழுவாளை) is close to Kural 555's opening, but the verb differs and the run is shorter than four words, so it is not marked as an echo. Line 34 is printed ஒலிபடைத்த தூர், probably ஒலிபடைத்தது ஊர.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","sengol","anangu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 19, lines 15-34","lb":"SI19:15-34","ev":"The people of Madurai grieve with Kannaki, ask whether the king's sceptre has bent, and show her Kovalan's body as the sun sets, and her cry fills the city.","cl":[["a king's unjust killing bends his sceptre and turns the shade he gives his people into heat","general","asserted",18,"madurai_makkal"]],"ag":["மதுரையார்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதுரையார்",""],"ch":"si:25"},"si:19:35-50":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":278,"t":["வண்டார் இருங்குஞ்சி மாலைதன் வார்குழன்மேற்","கொண்டாள் தழீஇக் கொழுநன்பாற் காலைவாய்ப்","புண்தாழ் குருதி புறஞ்சோர மாலைவாய்க்","கண்டாள் அவன்றன்னைக் காணாக் கடுந்துயரம்","என்னுறு துயர்கண்டும் இடருறும் இவள்என்னீர்","பொன்னுறு நறுமேனி பொடியாடிக் கிடப்பதோ","மன்னுறு துயர்செய்த மறவினை யறியாதேற்கு","என்னுறு வினைகாணா இதுவென உரையாரோ","யாருமில் மருள்மாலை இடருறு தமியேன்முன்","தார்மலி மணிமார்பம் தரைமூழ்கிக் கிடப்பதோ","பார்மிகு பழிதூற்றப் பாண்டியன் தவறிழைப்ப","ஈர்வதோர் வினைகாணா இதுவென உரையாரோ","கண்பொழி புனல்சோரும் கடுவினை யுடையேன்முன்","புண்பொழி குருதியிராய்ப் பொடியாடிக் கிடப்பதோ","மன்பதை பழிதூற்ற மன்னவன் தவறிழைப்ப","உண்பதோர் வினைகாணா இதுவென உரையாரோ"],"p":"","r":"She who that morning took the garland from her husband's hair for her own tresses sees him at evening with blood flowing from his wounds, and laments that his fragrant golden body lies in the dust before her, alone in the dusk, while she does not know the cruel deed behind the king's harm, and asks whether no one will tell her: this is the deed that tears, the deed that must be eaten, now that the Pandya has erred and the world spreads his blame.","n":"The three stanzas of the lament hold both causes in each closing pair of lines: the king commits the fault (பாண்டியன் தவறிழைப்ப, மன்னவன் தவறிழைப்ப) and yet what has come is a வினை to be eaten (உண்பதோர் வினை), and she calls herself one who has a harsh deed (கடுவினை யுடையேன்). She does not know which deed (மறவினை யறியாதேற்கு); the patikam's goddess will name it. The morning garland and the evening wound frame one day of the marriage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh","kodungol","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 19, lines 35-50","lb":"SI19:35-50","ev":"Kannaki sees Kovalan's wounded body at dusk and laments over it.","cl":[["the fruit of a deed must be eaten even when it arrives through the king's fault","general","asserted",50,"kannaki"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","கோவலன்"],"ch":"si:25"},"si:19:51-75":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":279,"t":["பெண்டிரும் உண்டுகொல் பெண்டிரும் உண்டுகொல்","கொண்ட கொழுந ருறுகுறை தாங்குறூஉம்","பெண்டிரும் உண்டுகொல் பெண்டிரும் உண்டுகொல்","சான்றோரும் உண்டுகொல் சான்றோரும் உண்டுகொல்","ஈன்ற குழுவி எடுத்து வளர்க்குறூஉம்","சான்றோரும் உண்டுகொல் சான்றோரும் உண்டுகொல்","தெய்வமும் உண்டுகொல் தெய்வமும் உண்டுகொல்","வைவாளில் தப்பிய மன்னவன் கூடலில்","தெய்வமும் உண்டுகொல் தெய்வமும் உண்டுகொல்","என்றிவை சொல்லி அழுவாள் கணவன்றன்","பொன்துஞ்சு மார்பம் பொருந்தத் தழீஇக்கொள்ள","நின்றான் எழுந்து நிறைமதி வாள்முகம்","கன்றிய தென்றவள் கண்ணீர்கை யான்மாற்ற","அழுதேங்கி நிலத்தின்வீழ்ந் தாயிழையாள் தன்கணவன்","தொழுதகைய திருந்தடியைத் துணைவளைக்கை யாற்பற்றப்","பழுதொழிந் தெழுந்திருந்தான் பல்லமரர் குழாத்துளான்","எழுதெழில் மலருண்கண் இருந்தைக்க எனப்போனான்","மாயங்கொல் மற்றென்கொல் மருட்டியதோர் தெய்வங்கொல்","போயெங்கு நாடுகேன் பொருளுரையோ இதுவன்று","காய்சினந் தணிந்தன்றிக் கணவனைக் கைகூடேன்","தீவேந்தன் தனைக்கண்டித் திறங்கேட்பல் யானென்றாள்","என்றாள் எழுந்தாள் இடருற்ற தீக்கனா","நின்றாள் நினைந்தாள் நெடுங்கயற்கண் நீர்சோர","நின்றால் நினைந்தாள் நெடுங்கயற்கண் நீர்துடையாச்","சென்றால் அரசன் செழுங்கோயில் வாயில்முன்."],"p":"","r":"Kannaki asks whether there are wives who bear their husbands' affliction, worthy men who raise the children they beget, or any god in the Koodal of a king who erred with the sword; as she embraces her husband's chest he rises, wipes her tears saying her moon face has faded, and when she falls and grasps his feet he rises whole among a host of immortals, bids her stay, and goes; she asks whether it was illusion or a deluding god, vows not to join him until her anger cools and to face the cruel king, and, remembering her evil dream, goes weeping to the palace gate.","n":"Her vow in the opening speech, to hear a faultless word from his mouth, is answered only by a tender word about her faded face and the command to stay (இருந்தைக்க, a rare form), and she herself judges it no word of substance (பொருளுரையோ இதுவன்று). She rejects the vision as a reason to grieve and turns to judgment: காய்சினந் தணிந்தன்றிக் கணவனைக் கைகூடேன். The three questions (பெண்டிரும், சான்றோரும், தெய்வமும் உண்டுகொல்) test the city's wives, its men and its god in turn. Lines 73 and 74 nearly repeat each other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","marumai","kanavu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 19, lines 51-75","lb":"SI19:51-75","ev":"Kovalan rises from death in a vision, wipes Kannaki's tears and departs among the immortals, and Kannaki goes to the king's palace gate to confront him.","cl":[["a true wife bears the affliction that befalls her husband","general","asserted",52,"kannaki"],["no god can be present in the city of a king who has erred with the sword","general","reported",58,"kannaki"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி",""],"ch":"si:25"},"si:20:1-7":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":280,"t":["ஆங்குக்","குடையொடு கோல்வீழ நின்று நடுங்கும்","கடைமணி இன்குரல் காண்பென்காண் எல்லா","திசையிரு நான்கும் அதிர்ந்திடும் அன்றிக்","கதிரை இருள்விழுங்கக் காண்பென்காண் எல்லா","விடுங்கொடி வில்லிர வெம்பகல் வீழும்","கடுங்கதிர் மீனிவை காண்பென்காண் எல்லா"],"p":"","r":"The Pandya queen tells a companion, line by line, the omens she saw in her sleep: the umbrella and sceptre falling, the gate bell trembling, the eight directions shaking, darkness swallowing the sun, a rainbow by night and stars dropping by day.","n":"Every omen is a disorder of the king's world or of the sky, and the first is the fall of the umbrella and sceptre, the signs of just rule; the refrain காண்பென்காண் எல்லா marks it as spoken to a companion. The lines stand in a block of their own, apart from the akaval that follows, and open with ஆங்குக், tying the scene to the moment Kannaki reaches the palace gate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","sengol","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 20, lines 1-7","lb":"SI20:1-7","ev":"The Pandya queen tells a companion her ill-omened dream of the king's umbrella and sceptre falling.","cl":[],"ag":["கோப்பெருந்தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கோப்பெருந்தேவி",""],"ch":"si:26"},"si:20:8-33":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":281,"t":["செங்கோலும் வெண்குடையும்","செறிநிலத்து மறிந்துவீழ்தரும்","நங்கோன்றன் கொற்றவாயில்","மணிநடுங்க நடுங்குமுள்ளம்","இரவுவில்லிடும் பகல்மீன்விழும்","இருநான்கு திசையும் அதிர்ந்திடும்","வருவதோர் துன்பமுண்டு","மன்னவற் கியாம் உரைத்துமென","ஆடியேந்தினர் கலனேந்தினர்","அவிர்ந்துவிளங்கும் அணியிழையினர்","கோடியேந்தினர் பட்டேந்தினர்","கொழுந்திரையலின் செப்பேந்தினர்","வண்ணமேந்தினர் சுண்ணமேந்தினர்","மான்மதத்தின் சாந்தேந்தினர்","கண்ணியேந்தினர் பிணையலேந்தினர்","கவரியேந்தினர் தூபமேந்தினர்","கூனுங்குறளும் ஊமுங்கூடிய","குறுந்தொழிலிளைஞர் செறிந்துசூழ்தர","நரைவிரைஇய நறுங்கூந்தலர்","உரைவிரைஇய பலர்வாழ்த்திட","ஈண்டுநீர் வையங்காக்கும்","பாண்டியன்பெருந் தேவிவாழ்கென","ஆயமுங் காவலுஞ்சென் றடியீடு பரசியேத்தக்","கோப்பெருந் தேவிசென்றுதன் தீக்கனாத் திறமுரைப்ப","அரிமா னேந்திய அமளிமிசை இருந்தனன்","திருவீழ் மார்பின் தென்னவர் கோவே; இப்பால்,"],"p":"","r":"The queen, reading the dream as a coming harm to the king, goes to tell him, attended by women bearing mirror, jewels, cloth, colours, musk, garlands, fly-whisk and incense and by a crowd of hunchbacks and dwarfs, and tells her dream to the king seated on his lion throne.","n":"Lines 8 to 15 restate the omens in the queen's own words and end in her resolve (வருவதோர் துன்பமுண்டு, மன்னவற்கு யாம் உரைத்தும்), so the king is warned before the accuser arrives. The long list of what the attendants carry sets the full ceremony of the court against the lone woman with one anklet who comes next.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kanavu","vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 20, lines 8-33","lb":"SI20:8-33","ev":"The queen, with her retinue, goes to the king on his lion throne and tells him her evil dream.","cl":[],"ag":["கோப்பெருந்தேவி","ஆயம்","நெடுஞ்செழியன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:26"},"si:20:34-54":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":282,"t":["வாயி லோயே வாயி லோயே","அறிவறை போகிய பொறியறு நெஞ்சத்து","இறைமுறை பிழைத்தோன் வாயி லோயே","இணையரிச் சிலம்பொன் றேந்திய கையள்","கணவனை இழந்தாள் கடையகத் தாளென்று","அறிவிப் பாயே அறிவிப் பாயே; என","வாயிலோன், வாழியெங் கொற்கை வேந்தே வாழி","தென்னம் பொருப்பின் தலைவ வாழி","செழிய வாழி தென்னவ வாழி","பழியொடு படராப் பஞ்வ வாழி","அடர்த்தெழு குருதி யடங்காப் பசுந்துணிப்","பிடர்த்தலைப் பீடம் ஏறிய மடக்கொடி","வெற்றிவேற் றடக்கைக் கொற்றவை அல்லள்","அறுவர்க் கிளைய நங்கை இறைவனை","ஆடல்கண் டருளிய அணங்கு சூருடைக்","கானகம் உகந்த காளி தாருகன்","பேருரங் கிழித்த பெண்ணு மல்லள்","செற்றனள் போலும் செயிர்த்தனள் போலும்","பொற்றொழிற் சிலம்பொன் றேந்திய கையள்","கணவனை இழந்தாள் கடையகத் தாளே","கணவனை இழந்தாள் கடையகத் தாளே; என"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki bids the gatekeeper announce to the king who has strayed from right rule that a woman holding one anklet, who has lost her husband, is at the gate, and the gatekeeper, hailing the Pandya, tells him that a woman who is neither Kotravai nor Kali but seems as angry stands there.","n":"Kannaki's message already pronounces the verdict: the king is one whose mind has lost its wisdom and who has erred in the rule of kingship (இறைமுறை பிழைத்தோன்), which recalls the patikam's அரைசியல் பிழைத்தோர். The gatekeeper denies she is the war goddess or Kali while piling up their images, preparing her later standing as a goddess, and hails the king as the Panchava who never walks with blame (பழியொடு படராப் பஞ்சவ) just before the blame falls.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","anangu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 20, lines 34-54","lb":"SI20:34-54","ev":"Kannaki asks the gatekeeper to announce her, and he tells the king that a widow holding one anklet, fierce as a goddess, waits at the gate.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணகி","வாயிலோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வாயிலோன்","நெடுஞ்செழியன்"],"ch":"si:26"},"si:20:55-73":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":283,"t":["வருக மற்றவள் தருக ஈங்கென","வாயில் வந்து கோயில் காட்டக்","கோயில் மன்னனைக் குறுகினள் சென்றுழி","நீர்வார் கண்ணை எம்முன் வந்தோய்","யாரை யோநீ மடக்கொடி யோய்எனத்","தேரா மன்னா செப்புவ துடையேன்","எள்ளறு சிறப்பின் இமையவர் வியப்பப்","புள்ளுறு புன்கண் தீர்த்தோன் அன்றியும்","வாயிற் கடைமணி நடுநா நடுங்க","ஆவின் கடைமணி உகுநீர் நெஞ்சுசுடத் தான்தன்","அரும்பெறற் புதல்வனை ஆழியின் மடித்தோன்","பெரும்பெயர்ப் புகாரென் பதியே அவ்வூர்","ஏசாச் சிறப்பின் இசைவிளங்கு பெருங்குடி","மாசாத்து வாணிகன் மகனை யாகி","வாழ்தல் வேண்டி ஊழ்வினை துரப்பச்","சூழ்கழல் மன்னா நின்னகர்ப் புகுந்தீங்கு","என்காற் சிலம்புபகர்தல் வேண்டி நின்பாற்","கொலைக்களப் பட்ட கோவலன் மனைவி","கண்ணகி யென்பதென் பெயரேயெனப்; பெண்ணணங்கே"],"p":"","r":"Admitted at the king's word, Kannaki answers his question by calling him a king who did not inquire, recalls the Chola kings of her city who relieved a bird's distress and crushed their own son under the chariot wheel for a cow's grief, and names herself as the wife of Kovalan, who came to Madurai driven by former deeds and was killed at the king's order while selling her anklet.","n":"The plea gives two causes in one breath: she indicts the king as தேரா மன்னா, the one who failed to inquire, and yet says Kovalan entered Madurai because ஊழ்வினை drove him (line 69). The two Chola precedents (the kings are not named in the Tamil) set the standard of a justice that spares not even the king's own son, and they are her own city's kings, offered to a Pandya. The king's reply begins on the last line (பெண்ணணங்கே).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","therithal","oozh","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 20, lines 55-73","lb":"SI20:55-73","ev":"Brought before the king, Kannaki names the Chola precedents of justice, her city, her husband's lineage and herself as the wife of the Kovalan he had killed.","cl":[["a just king relieves the suffering even of a bird or a cow, though it cost his own son's life","general","asserted",65,"kannaki"],["a former deed drives a man to the place where it ripens","general","asserted",69,"kannaki"],["a king who punishes without inquiry fails in justice","general","asserted",60,"kannaki"]],"ag":["வாயிலோன்","கண்ணகி","நெடுஞ்செழியன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","நெடுஞ்செழியன்"],"ch":"si:26"},"si:20:74-91":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":284,"t":["கள்வனைக் கோறல் கடுங்கோ லன்று","வெள்வேற் கொற்றங் காண்என ஒள்ளிழை","நற்றிறம் படராக் கொற்கை வேந்தே","என்காற் பொற்சிலம்பு மணியுடை அரியே, எனத்","தேமொழி யுரைத்தது செவ்வை நன்மொழி","யாமுடைச் சிலம்பு முத்துடை அரியே","தருகெனத் தந்து தான்முன் வைப்பக்","கண்ணகி அணிமணிக் காற்சிலம் புடைப்ப","மன்னவன் வாய்முதல் தெறித்தது மணியே, மணி கண்டு","தாழ்ந்த குடையன் தளர்ந்தசெங் கோலன்","பொன்செய் கொல்லன் தன்சொற் கேட்ட","யானோ அரசன் யானே கள்வன்","மன்பதை காக்குந் தென்புலங் காவல்","என்முதற் பிழைத்தது கெடுகவென் ஆயுளென","மன்னவன் மயங்கிவீழ்ந் தனனே தென்னவன்","கோப்பெருந் தேவி குலைந்தனள் நடுங்கிக்","கணவனை இழந்தோர்க்குக் காட்டுவ தில்லென்று","இணையடி தொழுதுவீழ்ந் தனளே மடமொழி."],"p":"","r":"The king answers that killing a thief is no cruel rule but royal justice; Kannaki says her anklet holds gems and the king that the queen's holds pearls; she breaks her anklet, a gem leaps to the king's mouth, and he, his umbrella lowered and sceptre slack, declares that he who heeded the goldsmith is no king but the thief, that the guarding of the southern land has failed first with him, prays his life to end and falls, while the queen, trembling, falls worshipping his feet.","n":"Where the patikam and the narrator of the killing scene give former deeds as the reason the king did not inquire, the king here names only his own fault, heeding the goldsmith's word (பொன்செய் கொல்லன் தன்சொற் கேட்ட), and sentences himself; karma and the king's fault both stand as causes in the story, and he takes the second. தாழ்ந்த குடையன் தளர்ந்தசெங் கோலன் fulfils the queen's dream of the falling umbrella and sceptre. The queen's words before she falls (கணவனை இழந்தோர்க்குக் காட்டுவது இல்) can be read as 'there is nothing left to offer those who lose their husbands'; the line does not say outright that she dies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","sengol","kalavu","aayul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 20, lines 74-91","lb":"SI20:74-91","ev":"Kannaki breaks her anklet and a gem flies to the king's mouth; the king declares himself the thief, wishes his life ended and falls, and the queen falls at his feet.","cl":[["to kill a thief is not cruel rule but the king's due justice","general","reported",74,"nedunjeliyan"],["a king who punishes on a false word is himself the thief","general","asserted",85,"nedunjeliyan"],["a king whose guarding of the people has failed should not live","general","asserted",87,"nedunjeliyan"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","நெடுஞ்செழியன்","கோப்பெருந்தேவி"],"ec":[],"spk":["நெடுஞ்செழியன்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:26"},"si:20:92-95":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":285,"t":["அல்லவை செய்தார்க் கறங்கூற்ற மாமென்னும்","பல்லவையோர் சொல்லும் பழுதன்றே--பொல்லா","வடுவினையே செய்த வயவேந்தன் றேவி","கடுவினையேன் செய்வதூஉங் காண்."],"p":"","r":"A closing venba says the saying of many assemblies, that dharma becomes death to those who do wrong, is not false, and bids the queen of the king who did the scarring wrong see what the speaker, a woman of harsh deeds, will do.","n":"வயவேந்தன் றேவி may be addressed (the queen is told to watch) or may be the subject (see what the queen does, and what I will do); the first-person கடுவினையேன் points to Kannaki, who opens the next kaathai calling the queen கோவேந்தன் தேவி and herself கொடுவினை யாட்டியேன், but the speaker is not named. The first line restates the patikam's first moral (அரைசியல் பிழைத்தோர்க்கு அறம்கூற்று) as a common saying now proved.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","kootru","vinai","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 20, lines 92-95","lb":"SI20:92-95","ev":"","cl":[["dharma itself becomes death to those who do wrong","general","asserted",92,"kannaki"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","கோப்பெருந்தேவி"],"ch":"si:26"},"si:20:96-99":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":286,"t":["காவி யுகுநீருங் கையில் தனிச்சிலம்பும்","ஆவி குடிபோன அவ்வடிவும்--பாவியேன்","காடெல்லாஞ் சூழ்ந்த கருங்குழலுங் கண்டஞ்சிக்","கூடலான் கூடாயி னான்."],"p":"","r":"A second venba says that seeing the tears from her blue-lily eyes, the lone anklet in her hand, her form like one whose life had left, and her dark hair spread like a forest, the lord of Koodal took fright and became a lifeless husk.","n":"The pun கூடலான் கூடு ஆயினான் (the lord of Koodal became a mere frame) makes the city's name the measure of the king's ruin. Here sight alone kills, not the king's own verdict of line 87. The first-person பாவியேன் is unexplained: it may be the poet's cry, or a speaker's who is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","anangu","aayul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 20, lines 96-99","lb":"SI20:96-99","ev":"The Pandya, seeing Kannaki's tears, lone anklet and spread hair, dies of fear.","cl":[],"ag":["நெடுஞ்செழியன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:26"},"si:20:100-103":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":287,"t":["மெய்யிற் பொடியும் விரித்த கருங்குழலும்","கையில் தனிச்சிலம்பும் கண்ணீரும்--வையைக்கோன்","கண்டளவே தோற்றான்அக் காரிகைதன் சொற்செவியில்","உண்டளவே தோற்றான் உயிர்."],"p":"","r":"A third venba says that the lord of the Vaigai, at the sight of her dust-covered body, loosened hair, lone anklet and tears, lost, and when her words entered his ears, lost his life.","n":"தோற்றான் is said twice: he lost first as a party loses a case, at the sight of her (the kaathai is the telling of the case, வழக்குரை), and then lost his life at her words. The three venbas give three accounts of how the king died, by her power, against the akaval's self-sentence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","kodungol","aayul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 20, lines 100-103","lb":"SI20:100-103","ev":"The Pandya, beaten at the sight of Kannaki, dies on hearing her words.","cl":[],"ag":["நெடுஞ்செழியன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:26"},"si:21:1-16":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":288,"t":["கோவேந்தன் தேவி கொடுவினை யாட்டியேன்","யாவுந் தெரியா இயல்பினே னாயினும்","முற்பகற் செய்தான் பிறன்கேடு தன்கேடு","பிற்பகற் காண்குறூஉம் பெற்றியகாண் நற்பகலே","வன்னி மரமும் மடைப்பளியுஞ் சான்றாக","முன்னிறுத்திக் காட்டிய மொய்குழ லாள் பொன்னிக்","கரையின் மணற்பாவை நின்கணவ னாமென்று","உரைசெய்த மாதரொடும் போகாள் திரைவந்து","அழியாது சூழ்போக வாங்குந்தி நின்ற","வரியா ரகலல்குல் மாதர் உரைசான்ற","மன்னன் கரிகால் வளவன்மகள் வஞ்சிக்கோன்","தன்னைப் புனல்கொள்ளத் தான்புனலின் பின்சென்று","கல்நவில் தோளாயோ வென்னக் கடல்வந்து","முன்னிறுத்திக் காட்ட அவனைத் தழீஇக் கொண்டு","பொன்னங் கொடிபோலப் போதந்தாள் மன்னி","மணல்மலி பூங்கானல் வருகலன்கள் நோக்கிக்"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki tells the king's queen that she, a woman of cruel fate who knows nothing, still knows that he who harms another in the forenoon sees his own harm in the afternoon, and begins to list the chaste women of Pukar: one who made a vanni tree and a kitchen stand as witnesses, one who, told in play that a sand doll on the Ponni bank was her husband, would not leave it and was spared by the tide, and Karikal Valavan's daughter, who followed her Vanchi husband into the flood that took him until the sea brought him back to her.","n":"Kannaki calls herself கொடுவினை யாட்டியேன், a woman of harsh deeds or harsh fate, the same self-naming as the last kaathai's closing venba; she places herself under karma while, in the same breath, she threatens the king's house with the forenoon-afternoon law, so the deed's return is both a law and something she is about to carry out. The queen she addresses has just fallen at the king's feet, so the speech is made over the dead or dying. The exempla are told in clipped allusion and depend on stories the poem does not tell; who the sand-doll woman was and what the vanni tree witnessed are not given in these lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vinai","innaa","pathini"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 21, lines 1-16","lb":"SI21:1-16","ev":"Kannaki, addressing the Pandya queen, recalls the chaste women of Pukar one after another.","cl":[["one who harms another in the forenoon sees his own harm come in the afternoon","general","asserted",3,"kannaki"],["a chaste wife's steadfastness can make a tree, a flood and the sea give way to her","general","asserted",14,"kannaki"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","கோப்பெருந்தேவி"],"ch":"si:27"},"si:21:17-39":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":289,"t":["கணவன்வரக் கல்லுருவம் நீத்தாள் இணையாய","மாற்றாள் குழவிவிழத் தன்குழவி யுங்கிணற்று","வீழ்த்தேற்றுக் கொண்டெடுத்த வேற்கண்ணாள் வேற்றொருவன்","நீள்நோக்கங் கண்டு நிறைமதி வாள்முகத்தைத்","தானோர் குரக்குமுக மாகென்று போன","கொழுநன் வரவே குரக்குமுக நீத்த","பழுமணி அல்குற்பூம் பாவை விழுமிய","பெண்ணறி வென்பது பேதைமைத்தே என்றுரைத்த","நுண்ணறிவி னோர்நோக்கம் நோக்காதே எண்ணிலேன்","வண்டல் அயர்விடத் தியானோர் மகள்பெற்றால்","ஒண்டொடி நீயோர் மகற்பெறில் கொண்ட","கொழுநன் அவளுக்கென் றியானுரைத்த மாற்றம்","கெழுமி அவளுரைப்பக் கேட்ட விழுமத்தால்","சிந்தைநோய் கூருந் திருவிலேற் கென்றெடுத்துத்","தந்தைக்குத் தாயுரைப்பக் கேட்டாளாய் முந்தியோர்","கோடிக் கலிங்கம் உடுத்துக் குழல்கட்டி","நீடித் தலையை வணங்கித் தலைசுமந்த","ஆடகப்பூம் பாவை அவள்போல்வார் நீடிய","மட்டார் குழலார் பிறந்த பதிப்பிறந்தேன்","பட்டாங் கியானுமோர் பத்தினியே யாமாகில்","ஒட்டே னரசோ டொழிப்பேன் மதுரையுமென்","பட்டிமையுங் காண்குறுவாய் நீயென்னா விட்டகலா","நான்மாடக் கூடல் மகளிரு மைந்தரும்"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki goes on with the women of Pukar, one who stood turned to stone until her husband came home, one who threw her own child into the well after her co-wife's child and brought both up, one who made her face a monkey's while her husband was away because another man stared, and the girl who, learning that her mother had promised her in play to a friend's son, put on new cloth and went with bowed head to him, then says that she was born in their city and, if she too is truly a pathini, will not let the kingdom live, will destroy Madurai, and the queen will see her vow kept.","n":"Lines 23 to 31 are nested speech: the girl's mother, telling the father, reproaches herself for not heeding the wise who say that a woman's wit is folly (பெண்ணறிவு என்பது பேதைமைத்தே) and for the careless word she spoke at the sand-house game; the saying is reported as a mother's self-blame, not asserted by the poem, and where the mother's speech begins is not certain. The vow is conditional on Kannaki's own virtue (பத்தினியே யாமாகில்), so the burning that follows is presented as the test that proves her a pathini, not only as revenge. The kaathai's name, வஞ்சின மாலை, takes the warrior's vow and gives it to a widow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathini","vanjinam","karpu","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 21, lines 17-39","lb":"SI21:17-39","ev":"Kannaki finishes her list of the chaste women of Pukar, vows to destroy the Pandya's kingdom and Madurai if she is a true pathini, and leaves the palace.","cl":[["a woman's judgement is folly","general","reported",24,"kannaki"],["a true pathini's vow can end a kingdom and destroy its city","general","asserted",37,"kannaki"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","கோப்பெருந்தேவி"],"ch":"si:27"},"si:21:40-57":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":290,"t":["வானக் கடவளரும் மாதவருங் கேட்டீமின்","யானமர் காதலன் தன்னைத் தவறிழைத்த","கோநகர் சீறினேன் குற்றமிலேன் யானென்று","இடமுலை கையால் திருகி மதுரை","வலமுறை மும்முறை வாரா அலமந்து","மட்டார் மறுகின் மணிமுலையை வட்டித்து","விட்டா ளெறிந்தாள் விளங்கிழையாள் வட்டித்த","நீல நிறத்துத் திரிசெக்கர் வார்சடைப்","பால்புரை வெள்ளெயிற்றுப் பார்ப்பனக் கோலத்து","மாலை எரியங்கி வானவன் தான்தோன்றி","மாபத் தினிநின்னை மாணப் பிழைத்தநாள்","பாயெரி இந்தப் பதியூட்டப் பண்டேயோர்","ஏவ லுடையேனா லியார்பிழைப்பா ரீங்கென்னப்","பார்ப்பார் அறவோர் பசுப்பத் தினிப்பெண்டிர்","மூத்தோர் குழவி யெனுமிவரைக் கைவிட்டுத்","தீத்திறத்தார் பக்கமே சேர்கென்று காய்த்திய","பொற்றொடி ஏவப் புகையழல் மண்டிற்றே","நற்றேரான் கூடல் நகர்."],"p":"","r":"Kannaki calls the women and men of Madurai, the gods and the ascetics to hear that she, without fault, has raged against the royal city that wronged her beloved husband, twists off her left breast, circles Madurai three times and flings the breast into the street; the fire god appears as a Brahmin with red matted hair and white teeth and says he long held an order to feed this city to the fire on the day she was wronged and asks whom he should spare, and at her bidding to leave Brahmins, the virtuous, cows, chaste women, elders and children and go only to the side of the wicked, smoke and flame fill Koodal.","n":"Two causes stand side by side: Kannaki's own act and charge (சீறினேன் குற்றமிலேன் யான்) and the fire god's word that he had an order from long before (பண்டேயோர் ஏவ லுடையேன்), which makes the burning already decreed; who gave the order is not said. The fire takes a command from her (ஏவ, line 56), so a god serves the pathini's word. The exemptions limit her rage by a code of protected persons, and the city is burned as the king's, not as the guilty one's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","kodungol","aram","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 21, lines 40-57","lb":"SI21:40-57","ev":"Kannaki proclaims her grievance to Madurai, tears off her left breast and flings it at the city; the fire god appears, she names those to be spared, and Madurai burns.","cl":[["a blameless wife whose husband was wronged by a king may rightly turn her anger on the king's city","general","asserted",42,"kannaki"],["the burning of the city on the day the pathini was wronged had been ordained beforehand","general","asserted",51,"agni"],["Brahmins, the virtuous, cows, chaste women, elders and children must be spared when a city is destroyed, and only the wicked struck","general","asserted",53,"kannaki"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","அங்கி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:27"},"si:21:58-61":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":291,"t":["பொற்பு வழுதியுந்தன் பூவையரும் மாளிகையும்","விற்பொலியுஞ் சேனையுமா வேழமுங்--கற்புண்ணத்","தீத்தரு வெங்கூடற் றெய்வக் கடவுளரும்","மாத்துவத் தான்மறைந்தார் மற்று."],"p":"","r":"The closing venba says that the Pandya, his women, his palace, his army bright with bows and his horses and elephants were consumed as chastity ate fierce Koodal, and that the city's divine guardians vanished too.","n":"The venba names the agent of the burning as கற்பு itself, not Kannaki or the fire god. The sense of மாத்துவத் தான் (line 61) is not secure, so how the gods are said to vanish is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","kodungol","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 21, lines 58-61","lb":"SI21:58-61","ev":"The fire consumes the Pandya's household, palace and army, and the guardian gods of Madurai withdraw.","cl":[["chastity's power can consume a king with his house and army and drive a city's gods away","general","asserted",59,"narrator"]],"ag":["கூடற் றெய்வக் கடவுளர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:27"},"si:22:1-15":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":292,"t":["ஏவல் தெய்வத் தெரிமுகம் திறந்தது","காவல் தெய்வங் கடைமுகம் அடைத்தன","அரைசர் பெருமான் அடுபோர்ச் செழியன்","வளைகோல் இழுக்கத் துயிராணி கொடுத்தாங்கு","இருநில மடந்தைக்குச் செங்கோல் காட்டப்","புரைதீர் கற்பின் தேவி தன்னுடன்","அரைசு கட்டிலில் துஞ்சியது அறியாது","ஆசான் பெருங்கணி அறக்களத்து அந்தணர்","காவிதி மந்திரக் கணக்கர் தம்மொடு","கோயில் மாக்களும் குறுந்தொடி மகளிரும்","ஓவியச் சுற்றத் துரையவிந் திருப்பக்","காழோர் வாதுவர் கடுந்தே ரூருநர்","வாய்வாள் மறவர் மயங்கினர் மலிந்து","கோமகன் கோயிற் கொற்ற வாயில்","தீமுகங் கண்டு தாமிடை கொள்ள"],"p":"","r":"The fire god under command opens its blazing face and the guardian gods shut the city's gates; not knowing that the Pandya, who gave up his life for the fault of his bent sceptre and so showed the earth a straight one, lies dead on his throne with his queen, the court officials sit still as painted figures, and the elephant-men, horsemen, charioteers and swordsmen crowd in confusion at the palace gate and halt at the sight of the fire.","n":"The narrator recasts the king's death as a gift (உயிர் ஆணி கொடுத்து) by which he showed the straight sceptre to the earth (இருநில மடந்தைக்குச் செங்கோல் காட்ட): the death that ends the bent sceptre is itself counted as an act of just rule. The fire is called ஏவல் தெய்வம், the god who takes orders, so the burning is presented as obedience, not as the fire's own wrath. The queen is praised for flawless chastity (புரைதீர் கற்பின்) even as she dies beside the king whose fault brought the fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kodungol","pathini","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 22, lines 1-15","lb":"SI22:1-15","ev":"The fire breaks out and the gates are shut while the court, unaware the king is dead, freezes and the guards halt before the flames.","cl":[["a king who has bent his sceptre restores just rule by giving his life for the fault","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":["அங்கி","கூடற் றெய்வக் கடவுளர்","நெடுஞ்செழியன்","கோயில் மாக்கள்","வாய்வாள் மறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:28"},"si:22:16-36":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":293,"t":["நித்திலப் பைம்பூண் நிலாத்திகழ் அவிரொளித்","தண்கதிர் மதியத் தன்ன மேனியன்","ஒண்கதிர் நித்திலம் பூணொடு புனைந்து","வெண்ணிறத் தாமரை அறுகை நந்தியென்று","இன்னவை முடித்த நன்னிறச் சென்னியன்","நுரையென விரிந்த நுண்பூங் கலிங்கம்","புலரா துடுத்த உடையினன் மலரா","வட்டிகை இளம்பொரி வன்னிகைச் சந்தனம்","கொட்டமோ டரைத்துக் கொண்ட மார்பினன்","தேனும் பாலும் கட்டியும் பெட்பச்","சேர்வன பெறூஉந் தீம்புகை மடையினன்","தீர்த்தக் கரையும் தேவர் கோட்டமும்","ஓத்தின் சாலையும் ஒருங்குடன் நின்று","பிற்பகற் பொழுதிற் பேணினன் ஊர்வோன்","நன்பகல் வரவடி யூன்றிய காலினன்","விரிகுடை தண்டே குண்டிகை காட்டம்","பிரியாத் தருப்பை பிடித்த கையினன்","நாவினும் மார்பினும் நவின்ற நூலினன்","முத்தீ வாழ்க்கை முறைமையின் வழாஅ","வேத முதல்வன் வேள்விக் கருவியோடு","ஆதிப் பூதத்து அதிபதிக் கடவுளும்"],"p":"","r":"The first guardian bhuta of Madurai is described in a Brahmin's form: moon-bright with pearl ornaments, crowned with white lotus and arugu, in unbleached cloth and sandal, receiving offerings of honey, milk and sweet smoke at bathing ghats, temples and Veda schools, carrying umbrella, staff, water-pot, kindling and darbha, the thread on his chest, never failing the rule of the three-fire life, with the sacrificial implements of the Veda.","n":"The edition marks lines 17-33, almost the whole portrait, as doubted by many commentators; the lines it keeps (16, 34-36) give only the pearl ornament, the unfailing three-fire life and the Vedic sacrificial tools, so no claim is built on the fuller description. The four bhutas that follow are each drawn from one order of society, and this one, the 'first bhuta', is the Brahmin's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pootham","andhanar","aaguthi","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 22, lines 16-36","lb":"SI22:16-36","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["ஆதிப் பூதத்து அதிபதிக் கடவுள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:28"},"si:22:37-61":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":294,"t":["வென்றி வெங்கதிர் புரையும் மேனியன்","குன்றா மணிபுனை பூணினன் பூணொடு","முடிமுதற் கலன்கள் பூண்டனன் முடியொடு","சண்பகம் கருவிளை செங்கூ தாளம்","தண்கமழ் பூநீர்ச் சாதியோடு இனையவை","கட்டும் கண்ணியும் தொடுத்த மாலையும்","ஒட்டிய திரணையோடு ஒசிந்த பூவினன்","அங்குலி கையறிந்து அஞ்சுமகன் விரித்த","குங்கும வருணங் கொண்ட மார்பினன்","பொங்கொளி யரத்தப் பூம்பட் டுடையினன்","முகிழ்த்தகைச்","சாலி அயினி பொற்கலத் தேந்தி","ஏலு நற்சுவை இயல்புளிக் கொணர்ந்து","வெம்மையிற் கொள்ளும் மடையினன் செம்மையில்","பவளச் செஞ்சுடர் திகழொளி மேனியன்","ஆழ்கடல் ஞால மாள்வோன் தன்னின்","முரைசொடு வெண்குடை கவரி நெடுங்கொடி","உரைசா லங்குசம் வடிவேல் வடிகயிறு","எனவிவை பிடித்த கையின னாகி","எண்ணருஞ் சிறப்பின் மன்னரை யோட்டி","மன்ணகம் கொண்டு செங்கோல் ஓச்சிக்","கொடுந்தொழில் கடிந்து கொற்றங் கொண்டு","நடும்புகழ் வளர்த்து நானிலம் புரக்கும்","உரைசால் சிறப்பின் நெடியோன் அன்ன","அரைச பூதத்து அருந்திறற் கடவுளும்"],"p":"","r":"The royal guardian bhuta is described: bright as the conquering sun, crowned and jewelled, wearing champak and red flowers, saffron on the chest and red silk, receiving hot rice offered in gold, coral-red in body, holding drum, white umbrella, fly-whisk, banner, goad, spear and rope like the ruler of the sea-girt earth, a god like Nediyon who drives off kings, takes their land, wields the straight sceptre, puts down cruel deeds and guards the four lands.","n":"Lines 37-50 are doubted in the edition; the kept lines 51-61 carry the whole charter of the royal spirit, ending in செங்கோல் ஓச்சிக் கொடுந்தொழில் கடிந்து: wielding the straight sceptre and putting down cruel deeds. That is the very office the Pandya claimed when he had Kovalan killed as a thief, so the portrait of the king's own guardian spirit stands as the measure he failed, just before the spirit leaves him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pootham","sengol","vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 22, lines 37-61","lb":"SI22:37-61","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["அரைச பூதத்து அருந்திறற் கடவுள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:28"},"si:22:62-88":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":295,"t":["செந்நிறப் பசும்பொன் புரையும் மேனியன்","மன்னிய சிறப்பின் மறவேல் மன்னவர்","அரைசுமுடி யொழிய அமைத்த பூணினன்","வாணிக மரபின் நீள்நிலம் ஓம்பி","நாஞ்சிலும் துலாமும் ஏந்திய கையினன்","உரைசால் பொன்னிறங் கொண்ட உடையினன்","வெட்சி தாழை கட்கமழ் ஆம்பல்","சேட னெய்தல் பூளை மருதம்","கூட முடித்த சென்னியன் நீடொளிப்","பொன்னென விரிந்த நன்னிறச் சாந்தம்","தன்னொடு புனைந்த மின்னிற மார்பினன்","கொள்ளும் பயறும் துவரையும் உழுந்தும்","நன்னியம் பலவும் நயந்துடன் அளைஇக்","கொள்ளெனக் கொள்ளும் மடையினன் புடைதரு","நெல்லுடைக் களனே புள்ளுடைக் கழனி","வாணிகப் பீடிகை நீள்நிழற் காஞ்சிப்","பாணிகைக் கொண்டு முற்பகற் பொழுதில்","உள்மகிழ்ந் துண்ணு வோனே அவனே","நாஞ்சிலம் படையும் வாய்ந்துறை துலாமுஞ்","சூழொளித் தாலு மியாழும் ஏந்தி","விலைந்துபத மிகுந்து விருந்துபதம் தந்து","மலையவும் கடலவு மரும்பலம் கொணர்ந்து","விலைய வாக வேண்டுநர்க் களித்தாங்கு","உழவுதொழி லுதவும் பழுதில் வாழ்க்கைக்","கிழவன் என்போன் கிளரொளிச் சென்னியின்","இளம்பிறை சூடிய இறையவன் வடிவினோர்","விளங்கொளிப் பூத வியன்பெருங் கடவுளும்"],"p":"","r":"The third guardian bhuta is described in a merchant's form: golden in body, wearing kings' ornaments but no crown, guarding the land by the trading way, holding plough and balance, garlanded with flowers of every landscape, receiving offerings of pulses at threshing floors, fields and market platforms, bringing goods of hill and sea to those who need them, called the master of the faultless life that sustains farming, a great god in the form of the crescent-wearing lord.","n":"Lines 67-84 are doubted in the edition, so the kept portrait is short: golden body, kings' ornaments without the crown (64), the trading tradition, plough and balance, and the crescent-crowned form (85-88). The doubted lines garland him with vetchi, thazhai, ambal, neythal, poolai and marutham together, flowers of different landscapes, as if trade gathered every region. Lines 81-82 (தாலு, விலைந்துபத) are obscure and were not resolved.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pootham","uzhavu","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 22, lines 62-88","lb":"SI22:62-88","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["விளங்கொளிப் பூத வியன்பெருங் கடவுள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:28"},"si:22:89-106":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":296,"t":["கருவிளை புரையு மேனிய னரியொடு","வெள்ளி புனைந்த பூணினன் தெள்ளொளிக்","காழகம் செறிந்த உடையினன் காழகில்","சாந்து புலர்ந்தகன்ற மார்பினன் ஏந்திய","கோட்டினும் கொடியினும் நீரினும் நிலத்தினும்","காட்டிய பூவிற் கலந்த பித்தையன்","கம்மியர் செய்வினைக் கலப்பை ஏந்திச்","செம்மையின் வரூஉஞ் சிறப்புப் பொருந்தி","மண்ணுறு திருமணி புரையு மேனியன்","ஒண்ணிறக் காழகஞ் சேர்ந்த உடையினன்","ஆடற் கமைந்த அவற்றொடு பொருந்திப்","பாடற் கமைந்த பலதுறை போகிக்","கலிகெழு கூடற் பலிபெறு பூதத்","தலைவ னென்போன் தானுந் தோன்றிக்","கோமுறை பிழைத்த நாளி லிந்நகர்","தீமுறை உண்பதோர் திறனுண் டென்ப","தாமுறை யாக அறிந்தன மாதலின்","யாமுறை போவ தியல்பன் றோவெனக்"],"p":"","r":"The fourth bhuta, dark as the karuvilai flower, in dark cloth with flowers of tree, creeper, water and land in his hair, bearing the artisans' tools and versed in dance and song, the chief of the bhutas that receive offerings in Koodal, appears too; and the bhutas say that there is a rule that on the day the king errs in the order of rule this city is to be eaten by fire, and since they know it as their charge, it is not fitting for them to stay.","n":"Lines 89-96 are doubted in the edition; the kept portrait (97-102) gives the gem-like body, the dark cloth, dance and song, and the title of chief of Koodal's offering-receiving bhutas. The bhutas' words (103-106) give the burning a law of its own: it is the city's lot (திறன்) when the king errs, and the guardians leave because they know it as their duty (முறை), not because they are driven off. Which order of society the fourth bhuta stands for is not named in the kept lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pootham","kodungol","kedu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 22, lines 89-106","lb":"SI22:89-106","ev":"The fourth guardian bhuta appears, and the bhutas declare that the city must burn because its king has erred and that they will leave.","cl":[["when a king errs in the order of rule, his city is due to be consumed by fire","general","asserted",103,"naarpaal_bhutam"]],"ag":["பலிபெறு பூதத் தலைவன்","நாற்பாற் பூதம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:28"},"si:22:107-118":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":297,"t":["கொங்கை குறித்த கொற்ற நங்கைமுன்","நாற்பாற் பூதமும் பாற்பாற் பெயரக்","கூல மறுகும் கொடித்தேர் வீதியும்","பால்வேறு தெரிந்த நால்வேறு தெருவும்","உரக்குரங்கு உயர்த்த ஒண்சிலை உரவோன்","காவெரி யூட்டிய நாள்போற் கலங்க","அறவோர் மருங்கின் அழற்கொடி விடாது","மறவோர் சேரி மயங்கெரி மண்டக்","கறவையும் கன்றும் கனலெரி சேரா","அறவை யாயர் அகன்றெரு அடைந்தன","மறவெங் களிறும் மடப்பிடி நிரைகளும்","விரைபரிக் குதிரையும் புறமதிற் பெயர்ந்தன"],"p":"","r":"Before the victorious lady who aimed her breast, the four bhutas leave each to its own quarter; the grain street, the chariot streets and the four streets of the separate orders are thrown into turmoil as on the day the monkey-bannered bowman fed the forest to the fire; the flame spares the side of the virtuous and crowds into the quarter of the fighting men, the cows and calves, untouched by fire, reach the broad street of the righteous herdsmen, and the war elephants, cow-elephants and swift horses flee beyond the walls.","n":"The fire sorts the city as Kannaki ordered in the previous kaathai: the virtuous (அறவோர்) and the cows are kept from it while the warriors' quarter (மறவோர் சேரி) burns, and the pun அறவோர் / மறவோர் may also mean the righteous against the wicked. விடாது in line 113 is taken as 'not letting the flame loose' on the virtuous, which is how the exemption of 21:53 is fulfilled; read as 'not leaving', it would say the opposite, so the reading is marked uncertain. Line 111, the bowman who burned the forest (the Mahabharata's Arjuna), is doubted in the edition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kedu","pathini","aram","pootham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 22, lines 107-118","lb":"SI22:107-118","ev":"The guardian bhutas leave and the fire sweeps the streets of Madurai, sparing the virtuous and the cows while the warriors' quarter burns and the elephants and horses flee.","cl":[],"ag":["நாற்பாற் பூதம்","அங்கி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:28"},"si:22:119-137":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":298,"t":["சாந்தந் தோய்ந்த ஏந்திள வனமுலை","மைத்தடங் கண்ணார் மைந்தர் தம்முடன்","செப்புவா யவிழ்ந்த தேம்பொதி நறுவிரை","நறுமல ரவிழ்ந்த நாறிரு முச்சித்","துறுமலர்ப் பிணையல் சொரிந்த பூந்துகள்","குங்குமம் எழுதிய கொங்கை முன்றில்","பைங்கா ழாரம் பரிந்தன பரந்த","தூமென் சேக்கைத் துனிப்பதம் பாராக்","காமக் கள்ளாட் டடங்கினர் மயங்கத்","திதலை அல்குல் தேங்கமழ் குழலியர்","குதலைச் செவ்வாய்க் குறுநடைப் புதல்வரொடு","பஞ்சியா ரமளியில் துஞ்சுதுயில் எடுப்பி","வால்நரைக் கூந்தல் மகளிரொடு போத","வருவிருந் தோம்பி மனையற முட்டாப்","பெருமனைக் கிழத்தியர் பெருமகிழ் வெய்தி","இலங்குபூண் மார்பிற் கணவனை இழந்து","சிலம்பின் வென்ற சேயிழை நங்கை","கொங்கைப் பூசல் கொடிதோ வன்றெனப்","பொங்கெரி வானவன் தொழுதனர் ஏத்தினர்"],"p":"","r":"Young women with sandal-smeared breasts and kohl-dark eyes, roused from love-play with their men, stagger in confusion; mothers wake their lisping little sons from cotton beds and go out with the white-haired women; and the mistresses of the great houses, who never failed the household duty of feeding guests, saying with great gladness that the breast-battle of the jewelled lady who lost her husband and won with her anklet is not a cruel thing, worship and praise the god of the blazing fire.","n":"The verdict on the burning is given to the wives who keep household dharma (வருவிருந் தோம்பி மனையற முட்டா), and they meet it with great gladness (பெருமகிழ்வு) and worship of the fire: the women of the city it destroys judge Kannaki's act just. The children and elders who leave together are the ones she exempted (மூத்தோர் குழவி, 21:54).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","ilvaazhkkai","virundhu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 22, lines 119-137","lb":"SI22:119-137","ev":"The townspeople flee the fire with their children and elders, and the housewives of Madurai worship the fire god, calling Kannaki's revenge no cruelty.","cl":[["a wife's fiery vengeance for a husband wrongly killed is not cruelty","general","asserted",136,"manaik_kizhaththiyar"]],"ag":["கூடல் மகளிரும் மைந்தரும்","பெருமனைக் கிழத்தியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:28"},"si:22:138-157":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":299,"t":["எண்ணான் கிரட்டி இருங்கலை பயின்ற","பண்ணியல் மடந்தையர் பயங்கெழு வீதித்","தண்ணுமை முழவம் தாழ்தரு தீங்குழல்","பண்ணுக்கிளை பயிரும் பண்ணியாழ்ப் பாணியொடு","நாடக மடந்தைய ராடரங் கிழந்தாங்கு","எந்நாட் டாள்கொல் யார்மகள் கொல்லோ","இந்நாட் டிவ்வூர் இறைவனை யிழந்து","தேரா மன்னனைச் சிலம்பின் வென்றிவ்","ஊர்தீ யூட்டிய ஒருமக ளென்ன","அந்தி விழவும் ஆரண ஓதையும்","செந்தீ வேட்டலுந் தெய்வம் பரவலும்","மனைவிளக் குறுத்தலும் மாலை அயர்தலும்","வழங்குகுரன் முரசமு மடிந்த மாநகர்க்","காதலற் கெடுத்த நோயொ டுளங்கனன்று","ஊதுலைக் குருகின் உயிர்த்தன ளுயிர்த்து","மறுகிடை மறுகுங் கவலையிற் கவலும்","இயங்கலும் இயங்கும் மயங்கலும் மயங்கும்","ஆரஞ ருற்ற வீரபத் தினிமுன்","கொந்தழல் வெம்மைக் கூரெரி பொறாஅள்","வந்து தோன்றினள் மதுராபதியென்."],"p":"","r":"The dancing women of the sixty-four arts, as if robbed of their stage, ask what land and whose daughter is the lone woman who, having lost her husband in this land and city, defeated the king who did not inquire with her anklet and set this city on fire; the evening festival, Vedic chant, fire offering, worship of gods, lighting of house lamps and the beat of drums all cease; and before the heroic pathini, burning with grief for her lost husband, sighing like a smith's bellows and wandering street to street in anguish, Madurapathi, unable to bear the heat of the fire, comes and appears.","n":"The ceasing of rites (ஆரண ஓதை, செந்தீ வேட்டல், தெய்வம் பரவல்) shows a burning that stops the city's worship altogether, including the Brahmins' fire offerings that her command spared from harm. The city's own goddess comes because she cannot bear the fire (கொந்தழல் வெம்மைக் கூரெரி பொறாஅள்): a deity is put under the pathini's fire. The dancers' question names Kannaki only as 'one woman' (ஒருமகள்) who won with the anklet, so the city knows the deed before it knows her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","kedu","aaguthi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 22, lines 138-157","lb":"SI22:138-157","ev":"The city's dancers ask who the woman is, all rites in Madurai stop, and the goddess of Madurai appears before the grieving Kannaki.","cl":[],"ag":["பண்ணியல் மடந்தையர்","கண்ணகி","மதுரைமா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:28"},"si:22:158-161":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":300,"t":["மாமகளும் நாமகளும் மாமயிடற் செற்றுகந்த","கோமகளும் தாம்படைத்த கொற்றத்தாள்- நாம","முதிரா முலைகுறைத்தாள் முன்னரே வந்தாள்","மதுரா பதியென்னு மாது."],"p":"","r":"The closing venba says that Madurapathi, a lady of victory who holds what Lakshmi, Saraswati and the queen who killed the buffalo demon possess, came before the woman who cut off her young breast.","n":"The venba gives the city goddess the powers of three goddesses at once (மாமகள், நாமகள், the slayer of Mahisha), and still has her come to the mortal woman, not the woman to her. கொற்றத்தாள் with தாம்படைத்த is read as the goddess holding the victory the three possess; the syntax allows other readings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","pathini"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 22, lines 158-161","lb":"SI22:158-161","ev":"The goddess of Madurai comes before Kannaki.","cl":[],"ag":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:28"},"si:23:1-17":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":301,"t":["சடையும் பிறையுந் தாழ்ந்த சென்னிக்","குவளை உண்கண் தவளவாள் முகத்தி","கடையெயிறு அரும்பிய பவளச்செவ் வாய்த்தி","இடைநிலா விரிந்த நித்தில நகைத்தி","இடமருங் கிருண்ட நீல மாயினும்","வலமருங்கு பொன்னிறம் புரையு மேனியள்","இடக்கை பொலம்பூந் தாமரை யேந்தினும்","வலக்கை அம்சுடர்க் கொடுவாள் பிடித்தோள்","வலக்கால் புனைகழல் கட்டினும் இடக்கால்","தனிச்சிலம்பு அரற்றும் தகைமையள் பனித்துறைக்","கொற்கைக் கொண்கன் குமரித் துறைவன்","பொற்கோட்டு வரம்பன் பொதியிற் பொருப்பன்","குலமுதற் கிழத்தி ஆதலின் அலமந்து","ஒருமுலை குறைத்த திருமா பத்தினி","அலமரு திருமுகத் தாயிழை நங்கைதன்","முன்னிலை ஈயாள் பின்னிலைத் தோன்றிக்","கேட்டிசின் வாழி நங்கையென் குறையென"],"p":"","r":"The narrator describes the goddess of Madurai, half dark and half golden, a lotus in her left hand and a sword in her right, a warrior's anklet on one foot and a woman's anklet on the other, who, as the family goddess of the Pandya, does not come before the grieving pathini but appears behind her and asks her to hear her plea.","n":"The goddess's two halves, the woman's anklet (சிலம்பு) on one foot and the hero's anklet (கழல்) on the other, set the book's emblem on a divided body. She stands behind Kannaki (பின்னிலைத் தோன்றி) because she is the Pandya's own family goddess (குலமுதற் கிழத்தி ஆதலின்), and the word she uses is குறை, a petition: the city's deity comes as a pleader for the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","pathini","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 1-17","lb":"SI23:1-17","ev":"The goddess of Madurai appears behind Kannaki and asks her to hear her plea.","cl":[],"ag":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:29"},"si:23:18-32":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":302,"t":["வாட்டிய திருமுகம் வலவயிற் கோட்டி","யாரைநீ யென்பின் வருவோய் என்னுடை","ஆரஞ ரெவ்வ மறிதியோவென","ஆரஞ ரெவ்வ மறிந்தேன் அணிஇழாஅய்","மாபெருங் கூடல் மதுரா பதியென்பேன்","கட்டுரை யாட்டியேன் யானின் கணவற்குப்","பட்ட கவற்சியேன் பைந்தொடி கேட்டி","பெருந்தகைப் பெண்ணொன்று கேளாயென் நெஞ்சம்","வருந்திப் புலம்புறு நோய்","தோழீநீ ஈதொன்று கேட்டியென் கோமகற்கு","ஊழ்வினை வந்தக் கடை","மாதராய் ஈதொன்று கேளுன் கணவற்குத்","தீதுற வந்த வினை; காதின்","மறைநா வோசை யல்ல தியாவதும்","மணிநா வோசை கேட்டது மிலனே"],"p":"","r":"Kannaki turns and asks who follows her and whether she knows her grief; the goddess answers that she knows it, names herself the deity of Madurai, grieved for what befell Kannaki's husband, asks her to hear what came to her king when his former deed arrived and what came to her husband, and says the Pandya kings' ears heard only the sound of the Veda, never the sound of the justice bell.","n":"The goddess gives the one cause to both men: ஊழ்வினை came to her king (line 28) and the deed came to harm Kannaki's husband (line 30), so the king is not the only one whose fault is being judged. Lines 25 to 30 are three short couplet-like pairs set inside the akaval. Her boast that the bell was never rung answers Kannaki's plea before the Pandya, where the Chola's gate bell trembled for the cow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","sengol","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 18-32","lb":"SI23:18-32","ev":"Kannaki asks who follows her, and the goddess names herself and asks to be heard.","cl":[["the king's fall came because his former deed arrived","general","asserted",28,"madurai_maadheyvam"],["a just king's line never needed the bell rung by those wronged","general","asserted",32,"madurai_maadheyvam"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","மதுரைமா தெய்வம்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:29"},"si:23:33-44":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":303,"t":["அடிதொழு திறைஞ்சா மன்ன ரல்லது","குடிபழி தூற்றுங் கோலனு மல்லன்","இன்னுங் கேட்டி நன்னுதல் மடந்தையர்","மடங்கெழு நோக்கின் மதமுகந் திறப்புண்டு","இடங்கழி நெஞ்சத்து இளமை யானை","கல்விப் பாகன் கையகப் படாஅது","ஒல்கா உள்ளத் தோடு மாயினும்","ஒழுக்கொடு புணர்ந்தவிவ் விழுக்குடிப் பிறந்தோர்க்கு","இழுக்கந் தாராது இதுவுங் கேட்டி","உதவா வாழ்க்கைக் கீரந்தை மனைவி","புதவக் கதவம் புடைத்தனன் ஒருநாள்","அரைச வேலி யல்ல தியாவதும்"],"p":"","r":"The goddess says the Pandya's sceptre brings blame on his people only when they are kings who will not bow to him, that in this noble line joined to good conduct even untamed youth, an elephant in must not yet held by the goad of learning, brings no fault, and begins the tale of the wife of the poor brahmin Keeranthai, at whose door someone knocked one night.","n":"The claim that the line's youth stays blameless without learning is made by the line's own goddess, in the chapter that tells how its present king failed. The masculine புடைத்தனன் (line 43) leaves who knocked to the next lines, where the king's response shows it was he.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","sengol","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 33-44","lb":"SI23:33-44","ev":"One night a man knocks at the door of Keeranthai's wife, whose husband is away.","cl":[["the Pandya's sceptre harms none of its people, only kings who refuse to bow","general","asserted",34,"madurai_maadheyvam"],["those born in a noble line joined to good conduct do not fall into fault even in untamed youth","general","asserted",40,"madurai_maadheyvam"]],"ag":["இறைக்குடிப் பிறந்தோன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:29"},"si:23:45-69":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":304,"t":["புரைதீர் வேலி இல்லென மொழிந்து","மன்றத் திருத்திச் சென்றீ ரவ்வழி","இன்றவ் வேலி காவா தோவெனச்","செவிச்சூட் டாணியிற் புகையழல் பொத்தி","நெஞ்சஞ் சுடுதலின் அஞ்சி நடுக்குற்று","வச்சிரத் தடக்கை அமரர் கோமான்","உச்சிப் பொன்முடி ஒளிவளை உடைத்தகை","குறைத்த செங்கோல் குறையாக் கொற்றத்து","இறைக்குடிப் பிறந்தோர்க்கு இழுக்க மின்மை","இன்னுங் கேட்டி நன்வா யாகுதல்","பெருஞ்சோறு பயந்த திருந்துவேல் தடக்கை","திருநிலை பெற்ற பெருநா ளிருக்கை","அறனறி செங்கோல் மறநெறி நெடுவாள்","புறவுநிறை புக்கோன் கறவைமுறை செய்தோன்","பூம்புனற் பழனப் புகார்நகர் வேந்தன்","தாங்கா விளையுள் நன்னா டதனுள்","வலவைப் பார்ப்பான் பராசர னென்போன்","குலவுவேற் சேரன் கொடைத்திறங் கேட்டு","வண்டமிழ் மறையோற்கு வானுறை கொடுத்த","திண்டிறல் நெடுவேற் சேரலற் காண்கெனக்","காடும் நாடும் ஊரும் போகி","நீடுநிலை மலயம் பிற்படச் சென்றாங்கு","ஒன்றுபுரி கொள்கை இருபிறப் பாளர்","முத்தீச் செல்வத்து நான்மறை முற்றி","ஐம்பெரு வேள்வியுஞ் செய்தொழில் ஓம்பும்"],"p":"","r":"Keeranthai's wife cries that her husband left her saying there is no fence but the king's, and asks whether that fence does not guard her today; the king, his heart burned as if a hot nail were driven into his ear, cuts off his own hand, the hand that broke Indra's crown, and the goddess turns to a second tale, of the brahmin Parasaran of the Chola land, who set out to see the Chera famed for his gifts and came among the brahmins of the three fires and four Vedas.","n":"The king punishes his own hand for a wrong of approach that the wife only feared, so the goddess sets a Pandya against the Chola exemplars Kannaki had thrown at him; she even names the Chola of the dove and the calf (line 58) here, as the ruler of Parasaran's land, and the burning heart (நெஞ்சஞ் சுடுதலின்) is the phrase Kannaki used of the Chola who killed his son.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kudi","naanam","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 45-69","lb":"SI23:45-69","ev":"Hearing the woman's reproach, the Pandya cuts off his own hand; Parasaran sets out for the Chera court.","cl":[["the king's protection is the only fence a woman left alone needs","general","reported",45,"keeranthai_manaivi"],["a king of the true line punishes his own body for a fault against his subjects","general","asserted",52,"madurai_maadheyvam"]],"ag":["கீரந்தை மனைவி","இறைக்குடிப் பிறந்தோன்","பராசரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:29"},"si:23:70-85":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":305,"t":["அறுதொழி லந்தணர் பெறுமுறை வகுக்க","நாவலங் கொண்டு நண்ணா ரோட்டிப்","பார்ப்பன வாகை சூடி ஏற்புற","நன்கலங் கொண்டு தன்பதிப் பெயர்வோன்","செங்கோல் தென்னன் திருந்துதொழில் மறையவர்","தங்கா லென்ப தூரே அவ்வூர்ப்","பாசிலை பொதுளிய போதி மன்றத்துத்","தண்டே குண்டிகை வெண்குடை காட்டம்","பண்டச் சிறுபொதி பாதக் காப்பொடு","களைந்தனன் இருப்போன் காவல் வெண்குடை","விளைந்துமுதிர் கொற்றத்து விறலோன் வாழி","கடற்கடம் பெறிந்த காவலன் வாழி","விடர்ச்சிலை பொறித்த வேந்தன் வாழி","பூந்தண் பொருநைப் பொறையன் வாழி","மாந்தரஞ் சேரல் மன்னவன் வாழ்கெனக்","குழலும் குடுமியும் மழலைச் செவ்வாய்த்"],"p":"","r":"Parasaran wins the brahmins' victory garland in debate, receives fine jewels and, going home, rests at Thangal, a brahmins' town of the Pandya, under the pipal of its common hall, setting down his staff, water pot, umbrella and bundle, and hails the Chera who cut the kadamba in the sea and carved his bow on the hill, the Poraiyan of the Porunai, Mantharam Cheral.","n":"A Chola-land brahmin, standing in a Pandya village, sings the Chera's praise; the four வாழி lines join epithets the Sangam books give to different Cheras, and the Tamil does not say whether they name one king or several.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","eegai","vedham","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 70-85","lb":"SI23:70-85","ev":"Parasaran, enriched by the Chera, rests at Thangal and blesses the Chera king.","cl":[],"ag":["பராசரன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பராசரன்",""],"ch":"si:29"},"si:23:86-102":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":306,"t":["தளர்நடை யாயத்துத் தமர்முதல் நீங்கி","விளையாடு சிறாஅ ரெல்லாஞ் சூழ்தரக்","குண்டப் பார்ப்பீ ரென்னோ டோதியென்","பண்டச் சிறுபொதி கொண்டுபோ மின்னெனச்","சீர்த்தகு சிறப்பின் வார்த்திகன் புதல்வன்","ஆலமர் செல்வன் பெயர்கொண்டு வளர்ந்தோன்","பால்நாறு செவ்வாய்ப் படியோர் முன்னர்த்","தளர்நா வாயினும் மறைவிளி வழாஅது","உளமலி உவகையோ டொப்ப வோதத்","தக்கிணன் தன்னை மிக்கோன் வியந்து","முத்தப் பூணூல் அத்தகு புனைகலம்","கடகம் தோட்டொடு கையுறை ஈத்துத்","தன்பதிப் பெயர்ந்தன னாக நன்கலன்","புனைபவும் பூண்பவும் பொறாஅ ராகி","வார்த்திகன் தன்னைக் காத்தன ரோம்பிப்","கோத்தொழி லிளையவர் கோமுறை அன்றிப்","படுபொருள் வௌவிய பார்ப்பா னிவனென"],"p":"","r":"Parasaran calls the playing children to recite with him for his bundle, and Vaarthikan's small son, named after the god under the banyan, recites the Veda without fault though his tongue still lisps; Parasaran, delighted, gives Thakkinan a pearl thread, bangles and earrings and goes home, and the king's officers, unable to bear the boy wearing them, seize Vaarthikan as a brahmin who took treasure without the king's leave.","n":"The charge (கோமுறை அன்றிப் படுபொருள் வௌவிய) is the officers' own; the text gives envy as their motive (பொறாஅராகி), not any order of the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","eegai","kodungol","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 86-102","lb":"SI23:86-102","ev":"Parasaran rewards the boy who recites the Veda, and the king's officers seize the boy's father as a thief.","cl":[],"ag":["பராசரன்","தக்கிணன்","கோத்தொழில் இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:29"},"si:23:103-122":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":307,"t":["இடுசிறைக் கோட்டத் திட்டன ராக","வார்த்திகன் மனைவி கார்த்திகை என்போள்","அலந்தனள் ஏங்கி அழுதனள் நிலத்தில்","புலந்தனள் புரண்டனள் பொங்கினள் அதுகண்டு","மையறு சிறப்பின் ஐயை கோயில்","செய்வினைக் கதவந் திறவா தாகலின்","திறவா தடைத்த திண்ணிலைக் கதவம்","மறவேல் மன்னவன் கேட்டனன் மயங்கிக்","கொடுங்கோ லுண்டுகொல் கொற்றவைக் குற்ற","இடும்பை யாவதும் அறிந்தீ மின்னென","ஏவ லிளையவர் காவலற் றொழுது","வார்த்திகற் கொணர்ந்த வாய்மொழி யுரைப்ப","நீர்த்தன் றிதுவென நெடுமொழி கூறி","அறியா மாக்களின் முறைநிலை திரிந்தவென்","இறைமுறை பிழைத்தது பொறுத்தல்நுங் கடனெனத்","தடம்புனற் கழனித் தங்கால் தன்னுடன்","மடங்கா விளையுள் வயலூர் நல்கிக்","கார்த்திகை கணவன் வார்த்திகன் முன்னர்","இருநில மடந்தைக்குத் திருமார்பு நல்கியவள்","தணியா வேட்கையுஞ் சிறிதுதணித் தனனே"],"p":"","r":"Vaarthikan is put in prison; his wife Kaarthigai weeps and rolls on the ground, the door of Aiyai's temple will not open, and the king, asking whether his sceptre has bent, learns the truth, declares that his rule has strayed through ignorant men and asks the people to bear his fault, gives Thangal and Vayalur to Vaarthikan, and lies prostrate on the earth to soothe Kaarthigai's grief.","n":"The king reads a shut temple door as the sign of a bent sceptre (கொடுங்கோ லுண்டுகொல், line 111) and owns the wrong of his servants as his own (என் இறைமுறை பிழைத்தது). Lines 121 to 122 (திருமார்பு நல்கியவள் ... தணித்தனனே) may mean that he lay with his chest to the earth before her, or something else; the word division is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","sengol","anangu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 103-122","lb":"SI23:103-122","ev":"When Aiyai's temple door stays shut, the king learns of the wrongful arrest, frees Vaarthikan and grants him villages.","cl":[["when a god's door stays shut the king's justice has failed","general","asserted",111,"thangal_thennan"],["a king answers for the wrongs his servants do in his name","general","asserted",117,"thangal_thennan"]],"ag":["கார்த்திகை","ஐயை","மறவேல் மன்னவன்","கோத்தொழில் இளையவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:29"},"si:23:123-137":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":308,"t":["நிலைகெழு கூடல் நீள்நெடு மறுகின்","மலைபுரை மாடம் எங்கணும் கேட்பக்","கலையமர் செல்வி கதவந் திறந்தது","சிறைப்படு கோட்டஞ் சீமின் யாவதுங்","கறைப்படு மாக்கள் கறைவீடு செய்ம்மின்","இடுபொரு ளாயினும் படுபொரு ளாயினும்","உற்றவர்க் குறுதி பெற்றவர்க் காமென","யானை யெருத்தத்து அணிமுரசு இரீஇக்","கோன்முறை யறைந்த கொற்ற வேந்தன்","தான்முறை பிழைத்த தகுதியுங் கேள்நீ","ஆடித் திங்கள் பேரிருட் பக்கத்து","அழல்சேர் குட்டத் தட்டமி ஞான்று","வெள்ளி வாரத்து ஒள்ளெரி யுண்ண","உரைசால் மதுரையோடு அரைசுகே டுறுமெனும்","உரையு முண்டே நிரைதொடி யோயே"],"p":"","r":"The goddess's door opens, the king has it proclaimed by drum on an elephant's neck that the prisons be broken and the guilty set free and that treasure found or laid by belong to those who hold it, and the goddess tells Kannaki to hear how a king of this line that proclaimed justice came to err, for there is also a saying that on the eighth day of the dark half of Aadi, under Krittika, on a Friday, fire will eat Madurai and its king will perish.","n":"Line 131 to 132 may set the proclaiming king against the present one, or say that the same line which proclaimed justice now erred; either way the goddess adds a fixed omen-day (lines 133 to 136) to the deed of a former birth, so the burning is foretold as well as earned.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","oozh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 123-137","lb":"SI23:123-137","ev":"The temple door opens and the king proclaims the release of prisoners; the goddess cites a prophecy of Madurai's burning.","cl":[["the burning of Madurai and the fall of its king were foretold to a fixed day","general","reported",136,"madurai_maadheyvam"]],"ag":["ஐயை","மறவேல் மன்னவன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:29"},"si:23:138-160":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":309,"t":["கடிபொழி லுடுத்த கலிங்கநன் னாட்டு","வடிவேல் தடக்கை வசுவுங் குமரனும்","தீம்புனற் பழனச் சிங்க புரத்தினும்","காம்பெழு கானக் கபில புரத்தினும்","அரைசாள் செல்வத்து நிரைதார் வேந்தர்","வீயாத் திருவின் விழுக்குடிப் பிறந்த","தாய வேந்தர் தம்முள் பகையுற","இருமுக் காவதத் திடைநிலத் தியாங்கணுஞ்","செருவல் வென்றியிற் செல்வோ ரின்மையின்","அரும்பொருள் வேட்கையிற் பெருங்கலன் சுமந்து","கரந்துறை மாக்களிற் காதலி தன்னொடு","சிங்கா வண்புகழ்ச் சிங்க புரத்தினோர்","அங்கா டிப்பட் டருங்கலன் பகரும்","சங்கமன் என்னும் வாணிகன் தன்னை","முந்தைப் பிறப்பிற் பைந்தொடி கணவன்","வெந்திறல் வேந்தற்குக் கோத்தொழில் செய்வோன்","பரத னென்னும் பெயரனக் கோவலன்","விரத நீங்கிய வெறுப்பின னாதலின்","ஒற்றன் இவனெனப் பற்றினன் கொண்டு","வெற்றிவேல் மன்னற்குக் காட்டிக் கொல்வுழிக்","கொலைக்களப் பட்ட சங்கமன் மனைவி","நிலைக்களங் காணாள் நீலி என்போள்","அரசர் முறையோ பரதர் முறையோ"],"p":"","r":"The goddess tells how in Kalinga two kings of one line, Vasu of Singapuram and Kumaran of Kapilapuram, were at war so that none could cross the land between, how the merchant Sangaman came secretly with his wife to sell jewels in the market, and how Kannaki's husband in his former birth, Bharatan, an officer of the king who had left his vow, seized him as a spy and had him killed, so that his widow Neeli cried whether this was the justice of kings or of the Bharatas.","n":"This is the former deed the patikam only names: Kovalan once played the part the goldsmith plays now, bringing a stranger with goods before a king as a criminal and having him killed. The text gives Bharatan's motive as a dislike that came from having left his vow (விரத நீங்கிய வெறுப்பினன்), so his own fault, not only fate, is named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","kolai","saabam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 138-160","lb":"SI23:138-160","ev":"In a former birth Bharatan seizes the merchant Sangaman as a spy and has him killed.","cl":[],"ag":["சங்கமன்","பரதன்","வசு","நீலி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:29"},"si:23:161-176":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":310,"t":["ஊரீர் முறையோ சேரியீர் முறையோவென","மன்றினும் மறுகினும் சென்றனள் பூசலிட்டு","எழுநா ளிரட்டி எல்லை சென்றபின்","தொழுநா ளிதுவெனத் தோன்ற வாழ்த்தி","மலைத்தலை யேறியோர் மால்விசும் பேணியில்","கொலைத்தலை மகனைக் கூடுபு நின்றோள்","எம்முறு துயரம் செய்தோ ரியாவதும்","தம்முறு துயரமிற் றாகுக வென்றே","விழுவோ ளிட்ட வழுவில் சாபம்","பட்டனி ராதலிற் கட்டுரை கேள்நீ","உம்மை வினைவந் துருத்த காலைச்","செம்மையி லோர்க்குச் செய்தவ முதவாது","வாரொலி கூந்தல்நின் மணமகன் தன்னை","ஈரேழ் நாளகத் தெல்லை நீங்கி","வானோர் தங்கள் வடிவின் அல்லதை","ஈனோர் வடிவிற் காண்டல் இல்லென"],"p":"","r":"Neeli cried through the halls and streets, and after fourteen days climbed a hill to join her slain husband in the sky and, falling, laid the curse that those who caused her sorrow should suffer the same; the goddess tells Kannaki that she and her husband are under that curse, that when the deed of a former birth rises penance does not help those not upright, and that she will see her husband within fourteen days, but only in a god's form.","n":"Neeli's curse is for exact repetition (எம்முறு துயரம் ... தம்முறு துயரம்), and the story repays it in kind: a wife again cries through a city's streets after her husband's killing. செம்மையிலோர் (line 172) is not named; it may be Bharatan's past, or a general rule.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saabam","vinai","piravi","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 161-176","lb":"SI23:161-176","ev":"Neeli curses the killers as she falls from a hill; the goddess tells Kannaki she will see Kovalan in fourteen days in divine form.","cl":[["a curse laid in grief by one wronged makes the wrongdoers suffer the same sorrow","general","asserted",169,"madurai_maadheyvam"],["when a former deed rises in wrath, penance does not help those who are not upright","general","asserted",172,"madurai_maadheyvam"]],"ag":["நீலி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:29"},"si:23:177-200":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":311,"t":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம் மாபத் தினிக்கு","விதிமுறை சொல்லி அழல்வீடு கொண்டபின்","கருத்துறு கணவற் கண்டபின் அல்லது","இருத்தலும் இல்லேன் நிற்றலும் இலனெனக்","கொற்றவை வாயிற் பொற்றொடி தகர்த்துக்","கீழ்த்திசை வாயிற் கணவனொடு புகுந்தேன்","மேற்றிசை வாயில் வறியேன் பெயர்கென","இரவும் பகலும் மயங்கினள் கையற்று","உரவுநீர் வையை ஒருகரைக் கொண்டாங்கு","அவல என்னாள் அவலித்து இழிதலின்","மிசைய என்னாள் மிசைவைத் தேறலிற்","கடல்வயிறு கிழித்து மலைநெஞ்சு பிளந்தாங்கு","அவுணரைக் கடந்த சுடரிலை நெடுவேல்","நெடுவேள் குன்றம் அடிவைத் தேறிப்","பூத்த வேங்கைப் பொங்கர்க் கீழோர்","தீத்தொழி லாட்டியேன் யானென் றேங்கி","எழுநா ளிரட்டி எல்லை சென்றபின்","தொழுநா ளிதுவெனத் தோன்ற வாழ்த்திப்","பீடுகெழு நங்கை பெரும்பெய ரேத்தி","வாடா மாமலர் மாரி பெய்தாங்கு","அமரர்க் கரசன் தமர்வந் தேத்தக்","கோநகர் பிழைத்த கோவலன் றன்னொடு","வான வூர்தி ஏறினள் மாதோ","கானமர் புரிகுழற் கண்ணகி தானென்."],"p":"","r":"After the goddess has told the pathini the course of fate and the fire has withdrawn, Kannaki vows neither to sit nor stand until she sees her husband, breaks her bangles at Kotravai's gate, says she came in by the east gate with her husband and goes out by the west alone, follows the Vaigai's bank night and day, climbs Murugan's hill and under a flowering vengai mourns that she is a woman of fiery deeds, and after fourteen days the retinue of the king of the gods praises her, rains flowers and takes her up in the sky car with Kovalan.","n":"Kannaki now calls herself தீத்தொழிலாட்டி, a doer of the deed of fire, where at the burning she declared herself faultless; Kovalan is still கோநகர் பிழைத்த, he who erred against (or perished in) the royal city, even as he is taken to heaven.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","marumai","karpu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 177-200","lb":"SI23:177-200","ev":"Kannaki leaves Madurai by the west gate, climbs the hill and after fourteen days is taken to heaven with Kovalan.","cl":[],"ag":["மதுரைமா தெய்வம்","கண்ணகி","அமரர்க் கரசன் தமர்","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:29"},"si:23:201-204":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":312,"t":["தெய்வந் தொழாஅள் கொழுநற் றொழுவாளைத்","தெய்வந் தொழுந்தகைமை திண்ணிதால்- தெய்வமாய்","மண்ணக மாதர்க் கணியாய கண்ணகி","விண்ணக மாதர்க்கு விருந்து."],"p":"","r":"A closing venba says it is certain that the gods worship the woman who worships her husband rather than a god, for Kannaki, ornament of the women of earth, has become the guest of the women of heaven.","n":"The first line takes up three words of Kural 55's first line (தெய்வந் தொழாஅள் கொழுநற்), short of the four-word echo rule, and changes the reward: not rain at the wife's command but the gods' own worship of her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","pathini","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 23, lines 201-204","lb":"SI23:201-204","ev":"","cl":[["the gods worship the wife who worships her husband rather than a god","general","asserted",202,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:29"},"si:madurai_kandam_katturai:1-20":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":313,"t":["முடிகெழு வேந்தர் மூவ ருள்ளும்","படைவிளங்கு தடக்கைப் பாண்டியர் குலத்தோர்","அறனும் மறனும் ஆற்றலும் அவர்தம்","பழவிறல் மூதூர்ப் பண்புமேம் படுதலும்","விழவுமலி சிறப்பும் விண்ணவர் வரவும்","ஒடியா இன்பத் தவருடை நாட்டுக்","குடியுங் கூழின் பெருக்கமும் அவர்தம்","வையைப் பேரியாறு வளஞ்சுரந் தூட்டலும்","பொய்யா வானம் புதுப்பெயல் பொழிதலும்","ஆரபடி சாத்துவதி யென்றிரு விருத்தியும்","நேரத் தோன்றும் வரியுங் குரவையும்","என்றிவை அனைத்தும் பிறபொருள் வைப்போடு","ஒன்றித் தோன்றும் தனிக்கோள் நிலைமையும்","வடஆரியர் படைகடந்து","தென்றமிழ்நா டொருங்குகாணப்","புரைதீர் கற்பின் தேவி தன்னுடன்","அரைசு கட்டிலில் துஞ்சிய பாண்டியன்","நெடுஞ்செழியனோ டொருபரிசா","நோக்கிக் கிடந்த","மதுரைக் காண்டம் முற்றிற்று."],"p":"","r":"The narrator closes the Madurai book by gathering into one view the righteousness, valour and strength of the Pandya line among the three crowned kings, their old city, its festivals and divine visitors, the people and harvests of their land, the Vaigai that feeds it, the sky that never fails its rain, the two modes of drama and the vari and kuravai songs, all seen together with Nedunjeliyan, who defeated the northern Aryan armies and, before all the Tamil south, came to lie still on his throne-couch with his queen of flawless chastity.","n":"The opening lines repeat, nearly word for word, the frame that closes the Puhar book, with the Vaigai for the Kaveri; so the praise of aram and of a sky that never fails (பொய்யா வானம், line 9) is given to the very line whose king the book has shown condemning an innocent man, and the summary does not take it back. The book's judgment shows only at the close, where the whole book is set beside a death: துஞ்சிய (line 17), 'slept', is the courtly word for a king's dying, and the queen's death at his feet (20:88-91) becomes a shared lying on the royal couch. The summary gives the epithet of flawless chastity (புரைதீர் கற்பின்) to the Pandya's queen, and does not name Kannaki, Kovalan or the burning of the city at all. The epithet வடஆரியர் படைகடந்து (line 14) matches the name under which tradition files Purananuru 183 (ஆரியப் படை கடந்த நெடுஞ்செழியன்); the Tamil here gives the epithet, not a proof that the two are one king, so no link is built on it. Lines 10 to 13 name two dramatic modes (ஆரபடி, சாத்துவதி) and the songs and say they appear together 'with other matter set in' (பிறபொருள் வைப்போடு); the reading does not turn on the technical terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","karpu","saakkaadu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram madurai_kandam_katturai, lines 1-20","lb":"SImadurai_kandam_katturai:1-20","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:30"},"si:24:1-22":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":314,"t":["குருவியோப்பியும் கிளிகடிந்தும் குன்றத்துச் சென்றுவைகி","அருவியாடியும் சுனைகுடைந்தும் அலவுற்று வருவேம்முன்","மலைவேங்கை நறுநிழலின் வள்ளிபோல்வீர் மனநடுங்க","முலையிழந்து வந்துநின்றீர் யாவிரோவென முனியாதே","மணமதுரையோ டரசுகேடுற வல்வினைவந் துருத்தகாலைக்","கணவனையங்கு இழந்துபோந்த கடுவினையேன் யானென்றாள்","என்றலும் இறைஞ்சியஞ் இணைவளைக்கை எதிர்கூப்பி","நின்றஎல்லையுள் வானவரும் நெடுமாரி மலர்பொழிந்து","குன்றவரும் கண்டுநிற்பக் கொழுநனொடு கொண்டுபோயினார்","இவள்போலும் நங்குலக்கோர் இருந்தெய்வம் இல்லையாதலின்","சிறுகுடி யீரே சிறுகுடி யீரே","தெய்வங் கொள்ளுமின் சிறுகுடி யீரே","நிறங்கிளர் அருவிப் பறம்பின் தாழ்வரை","நறுஞ்சினை வேங்கை நன்னிழற் கீழோர்","தெய்வங் கொள்ளுமின் சிறுகுடி யீரே","தொண்டகம் தொடுமின் சிறுபறை தொடுமின்","கோடுவாய் வைம்மின் கொடுமணி இயக்குமின்","குறிஞ்சி பாடுமின் நறும்புகை எடுமின்","பூப்பலி செய்ம்மின் காப்புக்கடை நிறுமின்","பரவுலும் பரவுமின் விரவுமலர் தூவுமின்","ஒருமுலை இழந்த நங்கைக்குப்","பெருமலை துஞ்சாது வளஞ்சுரக் கெனவே;"],"p":"","r":"The hill women tell how, coming back from scaring birds off the millet and bathing in the falls, they met under a mountain vengai a woman who had lost a breast and asked who she was, and she answered that she was the one of cruel deeds who had lost her husband there when strong karma pressed on her and Madurai and its kingship came to ruin; as they bowed, the gods rained flowers and took her away with her husband while the hill folk watched, and the women call the hamlet to take her as its god, with drums, horn, bells, kurinji song, incense and flowers, so that the great hill may never sleep but yield its wealth.","n":"Kannaki gives two causes in one breath: the strong deed of the past that came and pressed (வல்வினைவந் துருத்தகாலை, the patikam's ஊழ்வினை உருத்துவந்து ஊட்டும் and the Madurai goddess's உம்மை வினைவந் துருத்த காலை), and herself as the doer, கடுவினையேன், the woman of cruel deeds. The women compare her to Valli (வள்ளிபோல்வீர்) before they know her, and the reason they give for the cult is plain: no deity of their clan is as great as she (line 10). The prayer asks for the hill's fertility, the boon the Kural gives to the chaste wife's word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","vinai","vazhipaadu","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 1-22","lb":"SI24:1-22","ev":"The hill women meet the one-breasted Kannaki under a vengai, she tells them who she is, the gods take her to heaven with her husband, and the women call the hamlet to worship her.","cl":[["a strong deed of the past came upon her and brought the loss of her husband and the ruin of Madurai's kingship","general","asserted",5,"kannaki"],["a woman like her is a greater deity for the clan than any other and should be taken as its god","general","asserted",10,"kurava_magalir"]],"ag":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்","கண்ணகி","வானவர்","குன்றக் குறவர்","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:23-30":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":315,"t":["ஆங்கொன்று காணாய் அணியிழாய் ஈங்கிதுகாண்","அஞ்சனப் பூமி யரிதாரத் தின்னிடியல்","சிந்துரச் சுண்ணஞ் செறியத் தூய்த் தேங்கமழ்ந்து","இந்திர வில்லின் எழில்கொண் டிழுமென்று","வந்தீங் கிழியு மலையருவி யாடுதுமே;","ஆடுதுமே தோழி யாடுதுமே தோழி","அஞ்சலோம் பென்று நலனுண்டு நல்காதான்","மஞ்சுசூழ் சோலை மலையருவி ஆடுதுமே;"],"p":"","r":"A hill girl bids her friend look at the falls that come down coloured like the rainbow with the earth's collyrium, orpiment and vermilion, and says they will bathe in the falls of the cloud-wrapped grove, the falls of the man who said 'do not fear', enjoyed her beauty and gave no grace.","n":"The lover's 'do not fear' (அஞ்சலோம் பென்று) is the promise of the secret union; the bathing song turns at once from the beauty of the falls to his failure to keep it, which is the complaint the whole kuravai will try to cure by asking for his wedding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","sool","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 23-30","lb":"SI24:23-30","ev":"The hill girls go to bathe in the waterfall, and one complains of the lover who promised and did not return.","cl":[],"ag":["குறமகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குறமகள்","தோழி"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:31-34":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":316,"t":["எற்றொன்றும் காணேம் புலத்தல் அவர்மலைக்","கற்றீண்டி வந்த புதுப்புனல்","கற்றீண்டி வந்த புதுப்புனல் மற்றையார்","உற்றாடி னோம்தோழி நெஞ்சன்றே;"],"p":"","r":"The girl says she finds no cause to sulk, for the fresh flood that came washing the stones of his hill is the water they bathed in, and closes with an obscure line on others and the heart.","n":"The three stanzas differ only in what the water touched on his hill (stone, gold, flowers) and in the verb of bathing with others (உற்று, முன், மீது). The water from his hill stands for the man himself, so bathing in it undoes the quarrel. The last line, மற்றையார் ... ஆடினோம் தோழி நெஞ்சன்றே, may mean that others bathed in it too, or that it was not the heart that sulked; I cannot fix it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 31-34","lb":"SI24:31-34","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குறமகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குறமகள்","தோழி"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:35-38":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":317,"t":["என்னொன்றும் காணேம் புலத்தல் அவர்மலைப்","பொன்னாடி வந்த புதுப்புனல்","பொன்னாடி வந்த புதுப்புனல் மற்றையார்","முன்னாடி னோம்தோழி நெஞ்சன்றே;"],"p":"","r":"The girl says she finds no cause to sulk, for the fresh flood that came washing the gold of his hill is the water they bathed in, and closes with an obscure line on others and the heart.","n":"The three stanzas differ only in what the water touched on his hill (stone, gold, flowers) and in the verb of bathing with others (உற்று, முன், மீது). The water from his hill stands for the man himself, so bathing in it undoes the quarrel. The last line, மற்றையார் ... ஆடினோம் தோழி நெஞ்சன்றே, may mean that others bathed in it too, or that it was not the heart that sulked; I cannot fix it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 35-38","lb":"SI24:35-38","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குறமகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குறமகள்","தோழி"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:39-42":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":318,"t":["யாதொன்றுங் காணேம் புலத்தல் அவர்மலைப்","போதாடி வந்த புதுப்புனல்","போதாடி வந்த புதுப்புனல் மற்றையார்","மீதாடி னோம்தோழி நெஞ்சன்றே;"],"p":"","r":"The girl says she finds no cause to sulk, for the fresh flood that came washing the flowers of his hill is the water they bathed in, and closes with an obscure line on others and the heart.","n":"The three stanzas differ only in what the water touched on his hill (stone, gold, flowers) and in the verb of bathing with others (உற்று, முன், மீது). The water from his hill stands for the man himself, so bathing in it undoes the quarrel. The last line, மற்றையார் ... ஆடினோம் தோழி நெஞ்சன்றே, may mean that others bathed in it too, or that it was not the heart that sulked; I cannot fix it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 39-42","lb":"SI24:39-42","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குறமகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குறமகள்","தோழி"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:43-46":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":319,"t":["உரையினி மாதராய் உண்கண் சிவப்பப்","புரைதீர் புனல்குடைந் தாடினோ மாயின்","உரவுநீர் மாகொன்ற வேலேந்தி ஏத்திக்","குரவை தொடுத்தொன்று பாடுகம்வா தோழி;"],"p":"","r":"Having bathed until their eyes reddened, the girl bids her friend come and, raising praise to the spear that killed the mango of the strong sea, link hands in a kuravai and sing.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuravai","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 43-46","lb":"SI24:43-46","ev":"The girls leave the water and begin the kuravai dance in praise of Murugan's spear.","cl":[],"ag":["குறமகள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குறமகள்","தோழி"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:47-50":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":320,"t":["சீர்கெழு செந்திலும் செங்கோடும் வெண்குன்றும்","ஏரகமு நீங்கா இறைவன்கை வேலன்றே","பாரிரும் பெளவத்தின் உள்புக்குப் பண்டொருநாள்","சூர்மா தடிந்த சுடரிலைய வெள்வேலே;"],"p":"","r":"The first spear song says the spear in the hand of the god who never leaves Senthil, Sengodu, Venkunru and Erakam is the bright-leaved white spear that once went into the dark sea and cut down Sur's mango.","n":"The four hills named are the kuravar's list of Murugan's seats; it is not the six of the guide poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thalam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 47-50","lb":"SI24:47-50","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:51-54":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":321,"t":["அணிமுகங்க ளோராறும் ஈராறு கையும்","இணையின்றித் தானுடையான் ஏந்திய வேலன்றே","பிணிமுகமேற் கொண்டவுணர் பீடழியும் வண்ணம்","மணிவிசும்பிற் கோனேத்த மாறட்ட வெள்வேலே;"],"p":"","r":"The second spear song says it is the spear held by the one who alone has six fair faces and twelve hands, the white spear that, riding Pinimukam, killed so that the asuras lost their pride and the lord of the sky praised him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 51-54","lb":"SI24:51-54","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:55-58":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":322,"t":["சரவணப்பூம் பள்ளியறைத் தாய்மா ரறுவர்","திருமுலைப்பா லுண்டான் திருக்கைவே லன்றே","வருதிகிரி கோலவுணன் மார்பம் பிளந்து","குருகு பெயர்க்குன்றம் கொன்ற நெடுவேலே;"],"p":"","r":"The third spear song says it is the spear of the one who drank at the breasts of six mothers in the Saravana lotus bed, the long spear that split the chest of the asura and killed the mountain named for the crane.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 55-58","lb":"SI24:55-58","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:59-62":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":323,"t":["இறைவளை நல்லாய் இதுநகையா கின்றே","கறிவளர் தண்சிலம்பன் செய்தநோய் தீர்க்க","அறியாள்மற் றன்னை அலர்கடம்பன் என்றே","வெறியாடல் தான்விரும்பி வேலன்வரு கென்றாள்;"],"p":"","r":"The friend laughs to the girl that mother, not knowing how to cure the sickness the lord of the pepper hills caused, says it is the Kadamba god's and, wanting the veri rite, has told the velan to come.","n":"The mother's belief that the god has seized the girl is answered in the next three stanzas (lines 63 to 74), which call the velan and the god himself foolish if they come to cure a sickness a man gave; hence 'refuted'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 59-62","lb":"SI24:59-62","ev":"The girl's mother calls the velan to hold the veri rite for a sickness she thinks Murugan sent.","cl":[["a girl's wasting is the god's seizure and the velan's rite will cure it","general","refuted",61,"annai"]],"ag":["அன்னை"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","குறமகள்"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:63-66":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":324,"t":["ஆய்வளை நல்லாய் இதுநகை யாகின்றே","மாமலை வெற்பனோய் தீர்க்கவரும் வேலன்","வருமாயின் வேலன் மடவன் அவனிற்","குருகு பெயர்க்குன்றங் கொன்றான் மடவன்;"],"p":"","r":"The friend says it is laughable: if the velan comes to cure the sickness the man of the great hill caused, the velan is a fool, and more foolish than he is the one who killed the crane mountain, if he comes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 63-66","lb":"SI24:63-66","ev":"","cl":[["a sickness of love that a man gave is not the god's doing, and no rite of the velan or of the god can cure it","general","asserted",65,"thozhi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","குறமகள்"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:67-70":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":325,"t":["செறிவளைக்கை நல்லாய் இதுநகையா கின்றே","வெறிகமழ் வெற்பனோய் தீர்க்கவரும் வேலன்","வேலன் மடவன் அவனினுந் தான்மடவன்","ஆலமர் செல்வன் புதல்வன் வருமாயின்;"],"p":"","r":"The velan who comes to cure the sickness the man of the fragrant hill caused is a fool, the friend repeats, and more foolish still is the son of the one under the banyan, if he comes.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 67-70","lb":"SI24:67-70","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","குறமகள்"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:71-74":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":326,"t":["நேரிழை நல்லாய் நகையா மலைநாடன்","மார்பு தருவெந்நோய் தீர்க்க வரும்வேலன்","தீர்க்க வரும்வேலன் தன்னினுந் தான்மடவன்","கார்க்கடப்பந் தாரெங் கடவுள் வருமாயின்;"],"p":"","r":"The friend says, not in jest, that if the god of the dark kadamba garland comes to cure the hot sickness the chest of the man of the hill gave, he is more foolish than the velan who comes to cure it.","n":"நகையா (line 71) may be read 'not laughing' against the refrain's இதுநகையாகின்றே, turning the last stanza from jest to earnest; the same girls have just praised this god's spear, and the mockery is of his coming to the wrong rite, not of his power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 71-74","lb":"SI24:71-74","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","குறமகள்"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:75-78":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":327,"t":["வேலவனார் வந்து வெறியாடும் வெங்களத்து","நீலப் பறவைமேல் நேரிழை தன்னோடும்","ஆலமர் செல்வன் புதல்வன் வரும்வந்தால்","மால்வரை வெற்பன் மணவணி வேண்டுதுமே;"],"p":"","r":"If the son of the one under the banyan comes on his blue bird with his bride to the veri ground where the velan dances, the girls say, they will ask him for the wedding of the man of the great hill.","n":"The rite the friend mocked is put to another use: if the god does come, they will ask him not to cure the sickness but to bring the marriage that alone cures it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","veri","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 75-78","lb":"SI24:75-78","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:79-82":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":328,"t":["கயிலைநன் மலையிறை மகனைநின் மதிநுதல்","மயிலியல் மடவரல் மலையர்தம் மகளார்","செயலைய மலர்புரை திருவடி தொழுதேம்","அயல்மணம் ஒழியருள் அவர்மணம் எனவே;"],"p":"","r":"The girls tell the son of the lord of Kailasa that they have worshipped his feet, which are like asoka flowers, asking that the girl of the hill folk, peacock-like with a moon brow, be spared another's marriage and given the marriage of her lover.","n":"அயல்மணம் is the fear behind the prayer: a match the family might make with someone else, which would break the secret bond.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 79-82","lb":"SI24:79-82","ev":"The hill girls pray to Murugan that the girl be married to her lover and not to another.","cl":[["worship of Murugan can bring a wished marriage and avert an unwanted one","general","reported",81,"kurava_magalir"]],"ag":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்","முருகன்"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:83-86":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":329,"t":["மலைமகள் மகனைநின் மதிநுதல் மடவரல்","குலமலை உறைதரு குறவர்தம் மகளார்","நிலையுயர் கடவுள்நின் இணையடி தொழுதேம்","பலரறி மணமவர் படுகுவ ரெனவே;"],"p":"","r":"The girls tell the son of the mountain's daughter, the god of high station, that they have worshipped his feet asking that the girl of the kuravar of the high hills be wed in the marriage that many know.","n":"பலரறி மணம், the marriage many know, is the public wedding that ends the secret union.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 83-86","lb":"SI24:83-86","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்","முருகன்"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:87-90":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":330,"t":["குறமகள் அவளெம குலமகள் அவளொடும்","அறுமுக வொருவநின் அடியிணை தொழுதேம்","துறைமிசை நினதிரு திருவடி தொடுநர்","பெருகநன் மணம்விடு பிழைமண மெனவே;"],"p":"","r":"Calling the six-faced one's kurava wife their own clan's daughter, the girls say they have worshipped his feet with her, asking that those who touch his feet at the bathing place be granted a good marriage and spared a wrong one.","n":"குறமகள் அவள் எம குலமகள்: the hill women claim the god's wife, Valli, as a girl of their own clan, so their prayer goes to a god who is their kinsman by marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["valli","varaivu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 87-90","lb":"SI24:87-90","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்","முருகன்"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:91-100":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":331,"t":["என்றியாம் பாட மறைநின்று கேட்டருளி","மன்றலங் கண்ணி மலைநாடன் போவான்முன்","சென்றேன் அவன்றன் திருவடி கைதொழுது","நின்றேன் உரைத்தது கேள்வாழி தோழி","கடம்பு சூடி உடம்பிடி ஏந்தி","மடந்தை பொருட்டால் வருவ திவ்வூர்","அறுமுகம் இல்லை அணிமயில் இல்லை","குறமகள் இல்லை செறிதோ ளில்லை","கடம்பூண் தெய்வ மாக நேரார்","மடவர் மன்றவிச் சிறுகுடி யோரே;"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the man of the hill, who had heard their song from hiding, was going away when she went before him, worshipped his feet and told him that he comes to this village for a girl's sake wearing kadamba and carrying a spear, yet has no six faces, no peacock and no kurava bride, so the hamlet's people, who will not take him for the kadamba god, are simple folk.","n":"She treats the lover in the god's own forms, bowing at his feet as the girls bowed at Murugan's, and then lists what he lacks of the god. Lines 99 and 100 (கடம்பூண் தெய்வ மாக நேரார் மடவர்) can be read either way: the folk are simple for not seeing that the girl's god is this man, or for taking him for the god; the reading turns on நேரார்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 91-100","lb":"SI24:91-100","ev":"The hidden lover overhears the girls' song, and the friend stops him as he leaves and speaks to him.","cl":[],"ag":["மலைநாடன்","தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","குறமகள்"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:101-117":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":332,"t":["என்றீங்கு,","அலர்பாடு பெற்றமை யானுரைப்பக் கேட்டுப்","புலர்வாடு நெஞ்சம் புறங்கொடுத்துப் போன","மலர்தலை வெற்பன் வரைவானும் போலும்","முலையினால் மாமதுரை கோளிழைத்தாள் காதல்","தலைவனை வானோர் தமராரும் கூடிப்","பலர்தொழு பத்தினிக்குக் காட்டிக் கொடுத்த","நிலையொன்று பாடுதும் யாம்","பாடுகம் வாவாழி தோழியாம் பாடுகம்","பாடுகம் வாவாழி தோழியாம் பாடுகம்","கோமுறை நீங்கக் கொடிமாடக் கூடலைத்","தீமுறை செய்தாளை ஏத்தியாம் பாடுகம்","தீமுறை செய்தாளை ஏத்தியாம் பாடுங்கால்","மாமலை வெற்பன் மணவணி வேண்டுதுமே","பாடுற்றுப்","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர் பரவித் தொழுவாளோர்","பைத்தர வல்குல்நம் பைம்புனத் துள்ளாளே"],"p":"","r":"When she told him that gossip had arisen, the friend says, the man of the hill who had turned away with a withering heart seems now about to marry; so they will sing of the pathini who with her breast destroyed great Madurai, to whom the gods with their kin brought her husband and showed him, singing in praise of her who burned Koodal when the royal order failed, and as they sing they ask for the lover's wedding, for a woman whom chaste wives praise and worship is in their own millet field.","n":"Here the cause of the burning is the king's failure, கோமுறை நீங்க (line 111), where Kannaki in the first unit named the strong deed of the past and her own cruel act: the chapter gives both causes, in her voice and in the hill women's. The love story and the cult are joined: the friend's report of gossip moves the lover to marry, and the girls credit the new goddess of their field with the boon.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","varaivu","alar","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 101-117","lb":"SI24:101-117","ev":"The lover, told that gossip has spread, turns to marry, and the girls begin to sing the praise of the pathini in their field.","cl":[["the royal city was burned because the king's just order failed","general","asserted",111,"kurava_magalir"],["the pathini is one whom chaste wives themselves worship","general","asserted",116,"kurava_magalir"]],"ag":["மலைநாடன்","தோழி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தோழி","குறமகள்"],"ch":"si:31"},"si:24:118-134":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":333,"t":["பைத்தர வல்குல் கணவனை வானோர்கள்","உய்த்துக் கொடுத்தும் உரையோ ஒழியாரே","வானக வாழ்க்கை யமரர் தொழுதேத்தக்","கான நறுவேங்கைக் கீழாளோர் காரிகையே","கான நறுவேங்கைக் கீழாள் கணவனொடும்","வானக வாழ்க்கை மறுதரவோ வில்லாளே","மறுதர வில்லாளை ஏத்திநாம் பாடப்","பெறுகதில் லம்மஇவ் வூருமோர் பெற்றி","பெற்றி யுடையதே பெற்றி யுடையதே","பொற்றொடி மாதர் கணவன் மணங்காணப்","பெற்றி யுடையதிவ் வூர்","என்றியாம்","கொண்டு நிலைபாடி ஆடும் குரவையைக்","கண்டுநம் காதலர் கைவந்தா ரானாது","உண்டு மகிழ்ந்தானா வைகலும் வாழியர்","வில்லெழுதிய இமயத்தொடு","கொல்லி யாண்ட குடவர் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"The girls sing that even after the gods brought her husband to her they do not cease their praise, that the gods of heaven worship the woman under the fragrant forest vengai, that she has gone with her husband to the life of heaven and will not come back, and that the village which sings her has gained a distinction, to see the gold-bangled girl's lover wed; seeing the kuravai, their lovers have come, and they bless themselves to live and feast every day, and the king of the Kudavar who ruled Kolli and the Himalaya where the bow was carved.","n":"மறுதரவில்லாள் (she has no return) closes the story of Kannaki on earth: the hill folk keep her as a deity under the vengai, not as one who will come back. The closing blessing turns from the hill to the Chera king, who in the next kaathai hears of her; the bow carved on the Himalaya is a Chera emblem the Sangam poems give to Cheralathan, and the kuravai does not name which king it means.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathini","varaivu","kuravai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 24, lines 118-134","lb":"SI24:118-134","ev":"The girls finish the kuravai in praise of the pathini, their lovers come to marry them, and they bless the Chera king.","cl":[["a village that sings the praise of the pathini gains the boon of seeing its lovers wed","general","reported",125,"kurava_magalir"]],"ag":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்","மலைநாடன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குன்றக் குறவர் மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:31"},"si:25:1-13":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":334,"t":["மாநீர் வேலிக் கடம்பெறிந்து இமயத்து","வானவர் மருள மலைவிற் பூட்டிய","வானவர் தோன்றல் வாய்வாட் கோதை","விளங்கில வந்தி வெள்ளி மாடத்து","இளங்கோ வேண்மா ளுடனிருந் தருளித்","துஞ்சா முழவின் அருவி ஒலிக்கும்","மஞ்சுசூழ் சோலை மலைகாண் குவமெனப்","பைந்தொடி ஆயமொடு பரந்தொருங் கீண்டி","வஞ்சி முற்றம் நீங்கிச் செல்வோன்","வளமலர்ப் பூம்பொழில் வானவர் மகளிரொடு","விளையாட்டு விரும்பிய விறல்வேல் வானவன்","பொலம்பூங் காவும் புனல்யாற்றுப் பரப்பும்","இலங்குநீர்த் துருத்தியும் இளமரக் காவும்"],"p":"","r":"The Chera king, scion of the line that threw down the kadambu in the sea and set its bow on the Himalaya, sitting in the silver mansion with his queen Ilango Venmal, resolves to see the hills, and leaves Vanchi with his retinue for the groves and river banks.","n":"The queen named here (இளங்கோ வேண்மாள், line 5) is Senguttuvan's wife, not the poet Ilango. Whether the kadambu and Himalaya feats of lines 1 to 2 are the king's own or his line's turns on whether the participles qualify வானவர் or தோன்றல் (line 3); the Patitruppattu preface gives the carved bow to his father Imayavaramban. The kaathai opens as a pleasure trip, so the news of the pathini reaches the king by chance, not by petition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 1-13","lb":"SI25:1-13","ev":"Senguttuvan sets out from Vanchi with his queen and retinue to see the mountain country.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","இளங்கோ வேண்மாள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:32"},"si:25:14-29":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":335,"t":["அரங்கும் பள்ளியும் ஒருங்குடன் பரப்பி","ஒருநூற்று நாற்பது யோசனை விரிந்த","பெருமால் களிற்றுப் பெயர்வோன் போன்று","கோங்கம் வேங்கை தூங்கிணர்க் கொன்றை","நாகம் திலகம் நறுங்கா ழாரம்","உதிர்பூம் பரப்பின் ஒழுகுபுனல் ஒளித்து","மதுகரம் ஞிமிறொடு வண்டினம் பாட","நெடியோன் மார்பி லாரம் போன்று","பெருமலை விலங்கிய பேரியாற் றடைகரை","இடுமண லெக்கர் இயைந்தொருங் கிருப்பக்","குன்றக் குரவையொடு கொடிச்சியர் பாடலும்","வென்றிச் செவ்வேள் வேலன் பாணியும்","தினைக்குறு வள்ளையும் புனத்தெழு விளியும்","நறவுக்கண் ணுடைத்த குறவ ரோதையும்","பறையிசை அருவிப் பயங்கெழும் ஓதையும்","புலியொடு பொரூஉம் புகர்முக வோதையும்"],"p":"","r":"The royal camp spreads its halls on the sandbanks of the great river like the god riding his vast elephant, where fallen flowers hide the stream, and from every side rise the kuravai of the hill women, the velan's song to Murugan, the millet-pounders' vallai and the calls of the field-watchers and toddy-drinking kuravar.","n":"The catalogue of hill sounds (ஓதை, repeated line after line into the next unit) sets the Chera court inside the kurinji world whose people witnessed Kannaki's ascent; the kuravai named at line 24 points back to the previous kaathai's kunra kuravai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","velan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 14-29","lb":"SI25:14-29","ev":"The king's camp is pitched on the river bank amid the songs and noises of the hill people.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","கொடிச்சியர்","குன்றக் குறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:32"},"si:25:30-45":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":336,"t":["கலிகெழு மீமிசைச் சேணோன் ஓதையும்","பயம்பில்வீழ் யானைப் பாக ரோதையும்","இயங்குபடை யரவமோ டியாங்கணு மொலிப்ப","அளந்துகடை யறியா அருங்கலம் சுமந்து","வளந்தலை மயங்கிய வஞ்சி முற்றத்து","இறைமகன் செவ்வி யாங்கணும் பெறாது","திறைசுமந்து நிற்கும் தெவ்வர் போல","யானைவெண் கோடும் அகிலின் குப்பையும்","மான்மயிர்க் கவரியும் மதுவின் குடங்களும்","சந்தனக் குறையுஞ் சிந்துரக் கட்டியும்","அஞ்சனத் திரளும் அணியரி தாரமும்","ஏல வல்லியும் இருங்கறி வல்லியும்","கூவை நூறுங் கொழுங்கொடிக் கவலையும்","தெங்கின் பழனும் தேமாங் கனியும்","பைங்கொடிப் படலையும் பலவின் பழங்களும்","காயமும் கரும்பும் பூமலி கொடியும்"],"p":"","r":"Amid the din of the moving army, the hill people come carrying goods beyond counting, like enemy kings who stand bearing tribute at Vanchi's court without finding the king's hour, and the list begins: tusks, aloe wood, yak-tail whisks, pots of honey, sandal, vermilion, collyrium, orpiment, cardamom, pepper, arrowroot, coconuts, mangoes, jack fruit, cane and flowering creepers.","n":"The simile turns a free gift of hill produce into tribute: the hill folk stand at the king's camp as defeated enemies stand at his court, which prepares the kaathai's turn from wonder to conquest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 30-45","lb":"SI25:30-45","ev":"The hill people arrive at the camp laden with the produce of the mountains.","cl":[],"ag":["குன்றக் குறவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:32"},"si:25:46-63":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":337,"t":["கொழுந்தாட் கமுகின் செழுங்குலைத் தாறும்","பெருங்குலை வாழையின் இருங்கனித் தாறும்","ஆளியி னணங்கும் அரியின் குருளையும்","வாள்வரிப் பறழும் மதகரிக் களபமும்","குரங்கின் குட்டியுங் குடாவடி உளியமும்","வரையாடு வருடையும் மடமான் மறியும்","காசறைக் கருவும் மாசறு நகுலமும்","பீலி மஞ்ஞையும் நாவியின் பிள்ளையும்","கானக் கோழியும் தேன்மொழிக் கிள்ளையும்","மலைமிசை மாக்கள் தலைமிசைக் கொண்டாங்கு","ஏழ்பிறப் படியேம் வாழ்கநின் கொற்றம்","கான வேங்கைக் கீழோர் காரிகை","தான்முலை இழந்து தனித்துய ரெய்தி","வானவர் போற்ற மன்னோடும் கூடி","வானவர் போற்ற வானகம் பெற்றனள்","எந்நாட் டாள்கொல் யார்மகள் கொல்லோ","நின்னாட்டு யாங்கள் நினைப்பினும் அறியேம்","பன்னூ றாயிரத் தாண்டுவா ழியரென"],"p":"","r":"Carrying on their heads plantain and areca clusters and the young of lion, tiger, elephant, bear, mountain goat, deer, musk-deer, mongoose, peacock, civet, jungle fowl and parrot, the hill people hail the king as his servants for seven births and tell him that under a forest vengai a woman who had lost her breast, alone in her grief, joined her husband and went to heaven while the gods praised her; they do not know her country or whose daughter she is, and they wish him long life.","n":"The witnesses repeat the patikam's report to Ilango almost image for image (the vengai, the lost breast, the ascent), but now to the reigning king, and they confess they do not know her (நின்னாட்டு யாங்கள் நினைப்பினும் அறியேம்): the pathini enters the Chera land as a nameless stranger before Saathan names her. Lines 59 and 60 both begin வானவர் போற்ற.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","vendhan","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 46-63","lb":"SI25:46-63","ev":"The hill people offer their gifts and tell the king of the one-breasted woman they saw taken up to heaven under the vengai.","cl":[],"ag":["குன்றக் குறவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குன்றக் குறவர்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:32"},"si:25:64-77":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":338,"t":["மண்களி நெடுவேல் மன்னவற் கண்டு","கண்களி மயக்கத்துக் காதலோ டிருந்த","தண்டமி ழாசான் சாத்தனிஃ துரைக்கும்","ஒண்டொடி மாதர்க் குற்றதை யெல்லாம்","திண்டிறல் வேந்தே செப்பக் கேளாய்","தீவினைச் சிலம்பு காரண மாக","ஆய்தொடி அரிவை கணவற் குற்றதும்","வலம்படு தானை மன்னன் முன்னர்ச்","சிலம்பொடு சென்ற சேயிழை வழக்கும்","செஞ்சிலம் பெறிந்து தேவி முன்னர்","வஞ்சினம் சாற்றிய மாபெரும் பத்தினி","அஞ்சி லோதி அறிகெனப் பெயர்ந்து","முதிரா முலைமுகத் தெழுந்த தீயின்","மதுரை மூதூர் மாநகர் சுட்டதும்"],"p":"","r":"Saathan, the teacher of cool Tamil, who sat beside the king rapt with joy, offers to tell all that befell the woman: how, the anklet of an evil deed being the cause, her husband met his end, how she went with her anklet to plead before the king, and how the great pathini threw down the anklet before the queen, made her vow and burned the old city of Madurai with the fire that rose from her breast.","n":"Saathan's account to the king calls the anklet தீவினைச் சிலம்பு (line 69), placing the evil deed in the object, and leaves out the former-birth curse he explained to Ilango in the patikam; told publicly to a king, the story is the case, the vow and the burning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","vinai","vanjinam","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 64-77","lb":"SI25:64-77","ev":"Saathan begins to tell Senguttuvan the story of Kannaki's loss, her case before the Pandya and the burning of Madurai.","cl":[["an evil deed, through the anklet, caused what befell her husband","general","asserted",69,"saathan"]],"ag":["சாத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாத்தன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:32"},"si:25:78-92":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":339,"t":["அரிமா னேந்திய அமளிமிசை இருந்த","திருவீழ் மார்பின் தென்னர் கோமான்","தயங்கிணர்க் கோதை தன்றுயர் பொறாஅன்","மயங்கினன் கொல்லென மலரடி வருடித்","தலைத்தாள் நெடுமொழி தன்செவி கேளாள்","கலக்கங் கொள்ளாள் கடுந்துயர் பொறாஅள்","மன்னவன் செல்வுழிச் செல்க யானெனத்","தன்னுயிர் கொண்டவ னுயிர்தே டினள்போல்","பெருங்கோப் பெண்டும் ஒருங்குடன் மாய்ந்தனள்","கொற்ற வேந்தன் கொடுங்கோல் தன்மை","இற்றெனக் காட்டி இறைக்குரைப் பனள்போல்","தன்னாட் டாங்கண் தனிமையிற் செல்லாள்","நின்னாட் டகவயின் அடைந்தனள் நங்கையென்று","ஒழிவின் றுரைத்தீண் டூழி யூழி","வழிவழிச் சிறக்கநின் வலம்படு கொற்றமெனத்"],"p":"","r":"Saathan goes on: the Pandya on his lion throne, unable to bear her grief, fell senseless; his queen, stroking his feet and finding no answer, unshaken yet unable to bear the pain, said she would go where the king went and died with him as if seeking the life that had taken hers; and the maiden, as if to show the king the nature of the Pandya's bent sceptre, did not go to her own land alone but came to the Chera's, and Saathan blesses the king's victory for age upon age.","n":"In the Vazhakkurai the queen only trembles and falls at the king's feet; here she dies by her own resolve (மன்னவன் செல்வுழிச் செல்க யான், line 84). Saathan gives the pathini's coming to Chera land a purpose, to show another king what a bent sceptre comes to (lines 87 to 88), which turns a refugee goddess into a lesson for kings. Line 82 (தலைத்தாள் நெடுமொழி தன்செவி கேளாள்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","saakkaadu","vaazhkkaithunai","pathini"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 78-92","lb":"SI25:78-92","ev":"Saathan tells how the Pandya king died, how his queen died with him, and how Kannaki came into the Chera country.","cl":[],"ag":["நெடுஞ்செழியன்","கோப்பெருந்தேவி","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["சாத்தன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:32"},"si:25:93-109":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":340,"t":["தென்னர் கோமான் தீத்திறங் கேட்ட","மன்னர் கோமான் வருந்தினன் உரைப்போன்","எம்மோ ரன்ன வேந்தர்க் குற்ற","செம்மையின் இகந்தசொற் செவிப்புலம் படாமுன்","உயிர்பதிப் பெயர்த்தமை உறுக ஈங்கென","வல்வினை வளைத்த கோலை மன்னவன்","செல்லுயிர் நிமிர்த்துச் செங்கோ லாக்கியது","மழைவளங் கரப்பின் வான்பே ரச்சம்","பிழையுயி ரெய்திற் பெரும்பே ரச்சம்","குடிபுர வுண்டுங் கொடுங்கோ லஞ்சி","மன்பதை காக்கும் நன்குடிப் பிறத்தல்","துன்ப மல்லது தொழுதக வில்லெனத்","துன்னிய துன்பம் துணிந்துவந் துரைத்த","நன்னூற் புலவற்கு நன்கனம் உரைத்தாங்கு","உயிருடன் சென்ற ஒருமகள் தன்னினும்","செயிருடன் வந்தவிச் சேயிழை தன்னினும்","நன்னுதல் வியக்கும் நலத்தோர் யாரென"],"p":"","r":"Senguttuvan, grieving at the Pandya's fiery end, says it is well that the king's life left before word of his straying from justice reached kings like himself, that his departing life straightened into a just sceptre what strong former deeds had bent, and that to be born in a good line that guards the people, in great fear when rain fails and greater fear when any life is wronged, is only suffering and nothing to be revered; then he asks which of the two women the fair-browed queen admires more, the one who went with her husband's life or the one who came here in wrath.","n":"Both causes are given in one line: the sceptre was bent by வல்வினை (line 98), yet the king's own death is what straightens it, so karma bends and the king's choice repairs. This is the patikam's first moral (அரைசியல் பிழைத்தோர்க்கு அறம்கூற்று ஆவது) read by a fellow king as a mercy: death came before the shame. The question at lines 107 to 109 sets the two kinds of wifely virtue side by side for the queen to judge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","sengol","vinai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 93-109","lb":"SI25:93-109","ev":"Senguttuvan laments the Pandya's death, reflects on the burden of kingship and asks his queen which of the two women is greater.","cl":[["a king who strays from justice is better dead before the word of it spreads","general","asserted",97,"senguttuvan"],["strong former deeds bent the king's sceptre and his dying straightened it","general","asserted",98,"senguttuvan"],["a protecting king lives in fear of the rain failing and greater fear of any life being wronged","general","asserted",101,"senguttuvan"],["to be born to a line that guards the people is suffering, not a thing to be revered","general","reported",104,"senguttuvan"]],"ag":["குடவர் கோ"],"ec":[],"spk":["குடவர் கோ","சாத்தன்"],"ch":"si:32"},"si:25:110-121":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":341,"t":["மன்னவன் உரைப்ப மாபெருந் தேவி","காதலன் துன்பம் காணாது கழிந்த","மாதரோ பெருந்திரு வுறுக வானகத்து","அத்திறம் நிற்கநம் அகல்நா டடைந்தவிப்","பத்தினிக் கடவுளைப் பரசல் வேண்டுமென","மாலை வெண்குடை மன்னவன் விரும்பி","நூலறி புலவரை நோக்க ஆங்கவர்","ஒற்கா மரபிற் பொதியி லன்றியும்","விற்றலைக் கொண்ட வியன்பே ரிமயத்துக்","கற்கால் கொள்ளினுங் கடவு ளாகும்","கங்கைப்பேர் யாற்றினும் காவிரிப் புனலினும்","தங்கிய நீர்ப்படை தகவோ உடைத்தெனப்"],"p":"","r":"The great queen answers that the woman who died without seeing her husband's grief may gain great fortune in heaven, but that they must worship this pathini goddess who has come to their wide land; the king, pleased, looks to the learned men, who say that a stone taken from Pothiyil or from the Himalaya will become the goddess, and that bathing it in the Ganges or the Kaveri is fitting.","n":"The queen settles the king's question by dividing the two women: the Pandya queen's reward is in heaven (வானகத்து), Kannaki's is a cult on earth (பத்தினிக் கடவுளைப் பரசல்). The learned men offer two sources, south and north, as equal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","vazhipaadu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 110-121","lb":"SI25:110-121","ev":"The queen proposes that they worship the pathini as a goddess, and the learned men advise on the stone and its bathing.","cl":[["a wife who dies before seeing her husband's grief gains fortune in heaven","general","reported",112,"ilango_venmal"],["a stone from Pothiyil or the Himalaya, bathed in the Ganges or the Kaveri, becomes the goddess","general","reported",119,"nool_ari_pulavar"]],"ag":["இளங்கோ வேண்மாள்","குடவர் கோ","நூலறி புலவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளங்கோ வேண்மாள்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:32"},"si:25:122-136":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":342,"t":["பொதியிற் குன்றத்துக் கற்கால் கொண்டு","முதுநீர்க் காவிரி முன்றுறைப் படுத்தல்","மறத்தகை நெடுவா ளெங்குடிப் பிறந்தோர்க்குச்","சிறப்பொடு வரூஉஞ் செய்கையோ அன்று","புன்மயிர்ச் சடைமுடிப் புலரா வுடுக்கை","முந்நூல் மார்பின் முத்தீச் செல்வத்து","இருபிறப் பாளரொடு பெருமலை யரசன்","மடவதின் மாண்ட மாபெரும் பத்தினிக்","கடவு ளெழுதவோர் கல்தா ரானெனின்","வழிநின்று பயவா மாண்பில் வாழ்க்கை","கழிந்தோ ரொழிந்தோர்க்குக் காட்டிய காஞ்சியும்","முதுகுடிப் பிறந்த முதிராச் செல்வியை","மதிமுடிக் களித்த மகட்பாற் காஞ்சியும்","தென்றிசை யென்றன் வஞ்சியொடு வடதிசை","நின்றெதி ரூன்றிய நீள்பெருங் காஞ்சியும்"],"p":"","r":"Senguttuvan answers that taking a stone from Pothiyil and bathing it at the Kaveri's ford is no deed of honour for those born to his line of the sword, and that if the king of the great northern mountain, with his matted, thread-wearing, three-fire-tending twice-born, will not give a stone to carve the goddess of the great pathini, then the kaanchi themes of lives gone fruitless, of the daughter given to the moon-crowned god, and of the long kaanchi set against the north will follow.","n":"The cult becomes a matter of the king's honour: the near southern stone is rejected because it brings no glory, and the Himalaya's king, whose daughter married Siva (lines 133 to 134), is threatened with the kaanchi of war. The run of kaanchi turai names (lines 131 to 136) is compressed and its exact sense is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathini","maanam","kudi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 122-136","lb":"SI25:122-136","ev":"Senguttuvan rejects the southern stone and resolves to take one from the Himalaya, threatening war if its king refuses.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ"],"ec":[],"spk":["குடவர் கோ","நூலறி புலவர்"],"ch":"si:32"},"si:25:137-151":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":343,"t":["நிலவுக்கதி ரளைந்த நீள்பெருஞ் சென்னி","அலர்மந் தாரமொடு ஆங்கயல் மலர்ந்த","வேங்கையொடு தொடுத்த விளங்குவிறல் மாலை","மேம்பட மலைதலும் காண்குவல் ஈங்கெனக்","குடைநிலை வஞ்சியும் கொற்ற வஞ்சியும்","நெடுமா ராயம் நிலைஇய வஞ்சியும்","வென்றார் விளங்கிய வியன்பெரு வஞ்சியும்","பின்றாச் சிறப்பிற் பெருஞ்சோற்று வஞ்சியும்","குன்றாச் சிறப்பிற் கொற்ற வள்ளையும்","வட்கர் போகிய வான்பனந் தோட்டுடன்","புட்கைச் சேனை பொலியச் சூட்டிப்","பூவா வஞ்சிப் பொன்னகர்ப் புறத்தென்","வாய்வாள் மலைந்த வஞ்சிசூ டுதுமெனப்","பல்யாண்டு வாழ்கநின் கொற்றம் ஈங்கென","வில்லவன் கோதை வேந்தற் குரைக்கும்"],"p":"","r":"The king goes on that they shall see his moon-touching head crowned with a garland of mandaram and vengai, and that his army shall wear the vanchi of the true sword outside the golden city in all its kinds, the umbrella, victory, assembly, conquest and great feast; and Villavan Kothai, wishing the king long years, speaks to him.","n":"The speech is built from the names of the vanchi turai of the grammar of war, so the campaign is announced as a sequence of literary themes. Where the king's words end and Villavan Kothai's begin (line 140, 149 or 150) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 137-151","lb":"SI25:137-151","ev":"Senguttuvan declares that he will wear the vanchi garland and march north.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","வில்லவன் கோதை"],"ec":[],"spk":["குடவர் கோ","நூலறி புலவர்"],"ch":"si:32"},"si:25:152-177":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":344,"t":["நும்போல் வேந்தர் நும்மோ டிகலிக்","கொங்கர்செங் களத்துக் கொடுவரிக் கயற்கொடி","பகைப்புறத்துத் தந்தன ராயினும் ஆங்கவை","திகைமுக வேழத்தின் செவியகம் புக்கன","கொங்கணர் கலிங்கர் கொடுங்கரு நாடர்","பங்களர் கங்கர் பல்வேற் கட்டியர்","வடவா ரியரொடு வண்டமிழ் மயக்கத்துன்","கடமலை வேட்டமென் கட்புலம் பிரியாது","கங்கைப் பேர்யாற்றுக் கடும்புனல் நீத்தம்","எங்கோ மகளை ஆட்டிய அந்நாள்","ஆரிய மன்னர் ஈரைஞ் ஞூற்றுவர்க்கு","ஒருநீ யாகிய செருவெங் கோலம்","கண்விழித்துக் கண்டது கடுங்கட் கூற்றம்","இமிழ்கடல் வேலியைத் தமிழ்நா டாக்கிய","இதுநீ கருதினை யாயின் ஏற்பவர்","முதுநீ ருலகில் முழுவது மில்லை","இமய மால்வரைக்கு எங்கோன் செல்வது","கடவு ளெழுதவோர் கற்கே யாதலின்","வடதிசை மருங்கின் மன்னர்க் கெல்லாம்","தென்றமிழ் நன்னாட்டுச் செழுவிற் கயற்புலி","மண்டலை யேற்ற வரைக வீங்கென","நாவலந் தண்பொழில் நண்ணார் ஒற்றுநம்","காவல் வஞ்சிக் கடைமுகம் பிரியா","வம்பணி யானை வேந்தர் ஒற்றே","தஞ்செவிப் படுக்குந் தகைமைய வன்றோ","அறைபறை யென்றே அழும்பில்வே ளுரைப்ப"],"p":"","r":"Villavan Kothai recalls that kings who rose against him brought the fish banner from the Kongu field and their fame reached the elephants of the quarters, that his hunt among Aryans and Tamils is still before the minister's eyes, and that on the day his mother was bathed in the Ganges, Death saw him stand alone against a thousand Aryan kings; there is none in the world who would oppose him, so let letters bearing the bow, fish and tiger go to the northern kings; and Azhumbil Vel adds that the spies of enemy kings, who never leave Vanchi's gate, will carry the news, so let the drum be beaten.","n":"The minister argues that fame will do the work of war: the Himalaya journey is only for a stone (line 169), and the northern kings need only wear the three Tamil emblems. Line 165 (இமிழ்கடல் வேலியைத் தமிழ்நா டாக்கிய) uses the Patitruppattu preface's phrase for his father. Azhumbil Vel turns the enemy's spies into the king's heralds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh","otru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 152-177","lb":"SI25:152-177","ev":"The minister recounts the king's victories and urges a summons to the northern kings, and Azhumbil Vel advises proclaiming it by drum.","cl":[],"ag":["வில்லவன் கோதை","அழும்பில்வேள்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வில்லவன் கோதை","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:32"},"si:25:178-194":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":345,"t":["நிறையருந் தானை வேந்தனும் நேர்ந்து","கூடார் வஞ்சிக் கூட்டுண்டு சிறந்த","வாடா வஞ்சி மாநகர் புக்கபின்","வாழ்க எங்கோ மன்னவர் பெருந்தகை","ஊழிதொ றூழி யுலகங் காக்கென","விற்றலைக் கொண்ட வியன்பே ரிமயத்தோர்","கற்கொண்டு பெயருமெங் காவலன் ஆதலின்","வடதிசை மருங்கின் மன்ன ரெல்லாம்","இடுதிறை கொடுவந் தெதிரீ ராயின்","கடற்கடம் பெறிந்த கடும்போர் வார்த்தையும்","விடர்ச்சிலை பொறித்த வியன்பெரு வார்த்தையும்","கேட்டு வாழுமின் கேளீ ராயின்","தோட்டுணை துறக்கும் துறவொடு வாழுமின்","தாழ்கழல் மன்னன் தன்றிரு மேனி","வாழ்க சேனா முகமென வாழ்த்தி","இறையிகல் யானை யெருத்தத் தேற்றி","அறைபறை எழுந்ததால் அணிநகர் மருங்கென்."],"p":"","r":"The king consents and enters great Vanchi, and the drum, raised on an elephant's neck, proclaims through the city: long live our lord; since he goes to take a stone from the Himalaya, let the northern kings come with tribute and live hearing of the kadambu thrown down in the sea and the bow carved on the cleft, or else live in a renunciation that leaves their wives; long live the king and his army's front.","n":"Renunciation appears here as a threat: the alternative to tribute is a துறவு that gives up one's wives (line 190), the defeated king's forced exile, in a poem whose author by tradition renounced the Chera throne. The proclaimed feats (lines 187 to 188) are those the kaathai's opening gave to the Chera line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","pugazh","thuravu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 25, lines 178-194","lb":"SI25:178-194","ev":"Senguttuvan returns to Vanchi and the drum proclaims his northern campaign, calling the northern kings to tribute.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","அறைபறை"],"ec":[],"spk":["அறைபறை",""],"ch":"si:32"},"si:26:1-18":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":346,"t":["அறைபறை யெழுந்தபின் அரிமா னேந்திய","முறைமுதற் கட்டில் இறைமக னேற","ஆசான் பெருங்கணி அருந்திற லமைச்சர்","தானைத் தலைவர் தம்மொடு குழீஇ","மன்னர் மன்னன் வாழ்கென் றேத்தி","முன்னிய திசையின் முறைமொழி கேட்ப","வியம்படு தானை விறலோர்க் கெல்லாம்","உயந்த்தோங்கு வெண்குடை உரவோன் கூறும்","இமையத் தாபதர் எமக்கீங் குணர்த்திய","அமையா வாழ்க்கை அரைசர் வாய்மொழி","நம்பா லொழிகுவ தாயி னாங்கஃது","எம்போல் வேந்தர்க் கிகழ்ச்சியுந் தரூஉம்","வடதிசை மருங்கின் மன்னர்தம் முடித்தலைக்","கடவு ளெழுதவோர் கற்கொண் டல்லது","வறிது மீளுமென் வாய்வா ளாகில்","செறிகழல் புனைந்த செருவெங் கோலத்துப்","பகையரசு நடுக்காது பயங்கெழு வைப்பிற்","குடிநடுக் குறூஉங் கோலே னாகென"],"p":"","r":"After the drum proclaims the campaign, the king takes the lion throne before his preceptor, astrologer, ministers and generals and vows that, since the boast of the northern kings reported to him by the Himalayan ascetics would shame kings like him if it went unanswered, his sword will not come back empty without a stone, laid on the crowned heads of the northern kings, from which to carve a god, or else let him be a king whose sceptre makes his own people tremble instead of his foes.","n":"The vow is in the old conditional form: the penalty he accepts is not death or loss in battle but becoming a cruel king to his own subjects, so the fear his sceptre must cause is either the enemy's or his people's. The insult is only reported, carried by ascetics from the Himalaya (இமையத் தாபதர்), and அமையா வாழ்க்கை அரைசர் may mean kings of restless or unworthy life; the words of the boast are not given here. The god to be carved is not yet named as Kannaki in these lines.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","kodungol","maanam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 1-18","lb":"SI26:1-18","ev":"The Chera king, enthroned before his council, vows to bring back a stone for a god on the heads of the northern kings or be a tyrant to his own people.","cl":[["an insult to kings left unanswered brings disgrace on every king like the one insulted","general","reported",12,"senguttuvan"],["a king who fails his war vow deserves to become a king whose sceptre terrifies his own subjects","general","reported",18,"senguttuvan"]],"ag":["குடவர் கோ"],"ec":[],"spk":["குடவர் கோ","விறலோர்"],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:19-31":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":347,"t":["ஆர்புனை தெரியலும் அலர்தார் வேம்பும்","சீர்கெழு மணிமுடிக் கணிந்தோ ரல்லால்","அஞ்சினர்க் களிக்கும் அடுபோ ரண்ணல்நின்","வஞ்சினத் தெதிரும் மன்னரு முளரோ","இமைய வரம்பநின் இகழ்ந்தோ ரல்லர்","அமைகநின் சினமென ஆசான் கூற","ஆறிரு மதியினுங் காருக வடிப்பயின்று","ஐந்து கேள்வியும் அமைந்தோன் எழுந்து","வெந்திறல் வேந்தே வாழ்கநின் கொற்றம்","இருநில மருங்கின் மன்னரெல் லாம்நின்","திருமலர்த் தாமரைச் சேவடி பணியும்","முழுத்தம் ஈங்கிது முன்னிய திசைமேல்","எழுச்சிப் பாலை யாகென் றேத்த"],"p":"","r":"The preceptor asks whether any king, besides the Chola and Pandya who wear the aar and the neem, would stand against his vow, tells him that those two have not scorned him and bids his anger settle; then the astrologer, master of the five branches of reckoning, rises to hail his victory and declares that this very moment is the auspicious hour for marching in the chosen direction.","n":"The preceptor's counsel is double: it flatters (no northern king can face you) and restrains (the only equals, the other two Tamil crowns, are not the offenders), so the anger is turned wholly north. The astrologer's முழுத்தம் makes the departure an act of timing as much as of will.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","kaalam","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 19-31","lb":"SI26:19-31","ev":"The preceptor calms the king's anger and the astrologer declares the hour for setting out north.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆசான்","பெருங்கணி"],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆசான்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:32-53":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":348,"t":["மீளா வென்றி வேந்தன் கேட்டு","வாளுங் குடையும் வடதிசைப் பெயர்க்கென","உரவுமண் சுமந்த அரவுத்தலை பனிப்பப்","பொருந ரார்ப்பொடு முரசெழுந் தொலிப்ப","இரவிடங் கெடுத்த நிரைமணி விளக்கின்","விரவுக்கொடி யடுக்கத்து நிரயத் தானையோடு","ஐம்பெருங் குழுவும் எண்பே ராயமும்","வெம்பரி யானை வேந்தற் கோங்கிய","கரும வினைஞருங் கணக்கியல் வினைஞரும்","தரும வினைஞருந் தந்திர வினைஞரும்","மண்டிணி ஞாலம் ஆள்வோன் வாழ்கெனப்","பிண்ட முண்ணும் பெருங்களிற் றெருத்தின்","மறமிகு வாளும் மாலைவெண் குடையும்","புறநிலைக் கோட்டப் புரிசையிற் புகுத்திப்","புரைதீர் வஞ்சி போந்தையின் தொடுப்போன்","அரைசுவிளங் கவையம் முறையிற் புகுதர","அரும்படைத் தானை யமர்வேட்டுக் கலித்த","பெரும்படைத் தலைவர்க்குப் பெருஞ்சோறு வகுத்துப்","பூவா வஞ்சியிற் பூத்த வஞ்சி","வாய்வாள் நெடுந்தகை மணிமுடிக் கணிந்து","ஞாலங் காவலர் நாட்டிறை பயிரும்","காலை முரசம் கடைமுகத் தெழுதலும்"],"p":"","r":"Hearing the astrologer, the king orders his sword and umbrella moved northward; with drums roaring, the councils and officers hail him, carry the sword and white umbrella on an elephant's neck into the outer shrine, the king who strings the vanji with the palmyra feasts his eager generals with the great rice, wears on his crown the vanji that blossoms in flowerless Vanji, and the morning drum sounds at the gate.","n":"பூவா வஞ்சியிற் பூத்த வஞ்சி plays on the city's name and the vanji flower worn for an invading march: the garland of conquest is said to bloom in a city that bears the flower's name but not the flower. The great rice (பெருஞ்சோறு) shared with the generals binds them to the campaign before it starts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","perunjoru","vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 32-53","lb":"SI26:32-53","ev":"The sword and umbrella are moved north to the outer shrine, the generals are feasted with the great rice, and the king puts on the vanji garland.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","ஐம்பெருங் குழுவும் எண்பே ராயமும்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:54-73":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":349,"t":["நிலவுக்கதிர் முடித்த நீளிருஞ் சென்னி","உலகுபொதி உருவத் துயர்ந்தோன் சேவடி","மறஞ்சேர் வஞ்சி மாலையொடு புனைந்து","இறைஞ்சாக் சென்னி இறைஞ்சி வலங்கொண்டு","மறையோ ரேந்திய ஆவுதி நறும்புகை","நறைகெழு மாலையின் நல்லகம் வருத்தக்","கடக்களி யானைப் பிடர்த்தலை யேரினன்","குடக்கோக் குட்டுவன் கொற்றங் கொள்கென","ஆடக மாடத் தறிதுயல் அமர்ந்தோன்","சேடங் கொண்டு சிலர்நின் றேத்தத்","தெண்ணீர் கரந்த செஞ்சடைக் கடவுள்","வண்ணச் சேவடி மணிமுடி வைத்தலின்","ஆங்கது வாங்கி அணிமணிப் புயத்துத்","தாங்கின னாகித் தகைமையிற் செல்வுழி","நாடக மடந்தையர் ஆடரங் கியாங்கணும்","கூடையிற் பொலிந்து கொற்ற வேந்தே","வாகை தும்பை மணித்தோட்டுப் போந்தையோடு","ஓடை யானையின் உயர்முகத் தோங்க","வெண்குடை நீழலெம் வெள்வளை கவரும்","கண்களி கொள்ளுங் காட்சியை யாகென"],"p":"","r":"The king sets the feet of the moon-crowned god who fills the world upon his head with the vanji garland, bows the head that bows to no one, circles the shrine and mounts his war elephant amid the Brahmins' oblation smoke; when some bring the prasada of the god who sleeps on the serpent in the golden hall, he takes it on his shoulder, since Siva's feet are on his crown, and the dancing women everywhere bless him to go with the victor's flowers on his elephant's brow.","n":"The king worships both gods but ranks them on his body: the matted god's feet on the crown, Vishnu's prasada on the shoulder, and the narrator gives the reason (வைத்தலின்) without comment. இறைஞ்சாச் சென்னி இறைஞ்சி: the head that bows to no king bows to the god. ஆடக மாடம் may name a particular Vishnu shrine; the lines do not say which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","aaguthi","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 54-73","lb":"SI26:54-73","ev":"The king worships Siva before setting out, takes Vishnu's prasada on his shoulder, and is blessed by the dancing women.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","சிலர்","நாடக மடந்தையர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:74-92":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":350,"t":["மாகதப் புலவரும் வைதா ளிகரும்","சூதரும் நல்வலந் தோன்ற வாழ்த்த","யானை வீரரும் இவுளித் தலைவரும்","வாய்வாள் மறவரும் வாள்வல னேத்தத்","தானவர் தம்மேற் றம்பதி நீங்கும்","வானவன் போல வஞ்சி நீங்கித்","தண்டத் தலைவருந் தலைத்தார்ச் சேனையும்","வெண்டலைப் புணரியின் விளிம்புசூழ் போத","மலைமுதுகு நெளிய நிலைநா டதர்பட","உலக மன்னவன் ஒருங்குடன் சென்றாங்கு","ஆலும் புரவி யணித்தேர்த் தானையொடு","நீல கிரியின் நெடும்புறத் திறுத்தாங்கு","ஆடியல் யானையும் தேரும் மாவும்","பீடுகெழு மறவரும் பிறழாக் காப்பிற்","பாடி யிருக்கைப் பகல்வெய் யோன்றன்","இருநில மடந்தைக்குத் திருவடி யளித்தாங்கு","அருந்திறல் மாக்கள் அடியீ டேத்தப்","பெரும்பே ரமளி ஏறிய பின்னர்","இயங்குபடை அரவத் தீண்டொலி இசைப்ப"],"p":"","r":"Bards, panegyrists and heralds, elephant riders, horse captains and sword warriors hail him; like Indra leaving his city against the asuras he leaves Vanji, his army spreading like a white-crested sea, bending mountain backs and cutting paths through the land, camps on the slopes of the Nilagiri, and, praised by his men, lies down on the great couch amid the din of the moving army.","n":"The comparison with Indra marching on the asuras makes the northern kings the demons of the simile before any battle is fought.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 74-92","lb":"SI26:74-92","ev":"Praised by bards and soldiers, the king marches out of Vanji and camps at the Nilagiri.","cl":[],"ag":["மாகதப் புலவரும் வைதாளிகரும் சூதரும்","குடவர் கோ"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:93-104":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":351,"t":["விசும்பியங்கு முனிவர் வியன்நிலம் ஆளும்","இந்திர திருவனைக் காண்குது மென்றே","அந்தரத் திழிந்தாங் கரசுவிளங் கவையத்து","மின்னொளி மயக்கும் மேனியொடு தோன்ற","மன்னவன் எழுந்து வணங்கிநின் றோனைச்","செஞ்சடை வானவன் அருளினில் விளங்க","வஞ்சித் தோன்றிய வானவ கேளாய்","மலயத் தேகுதும் வான்பே ரிமய","நிலயத் தேகுதல் நின்கருத் தாகலின்","அருமறை யந்தணர் ஆங்குளர் வாழ்வோர்","பெருநில மன்ன பேணல்நின் கடனென்று","ஆங்கவர் வாழ்த்திப் போந்ததற் பின்னர்"],"p":"","r":"Sages who travel the sky, wishing to see the Indra-like ruler, descend into the hall where the king rises and bows to them, and tell the Vanavan born in Vanji by the grace of the red-haired god that they are going to Malaya, and since his purpose is the great Himalaya, where Brahmins of the hard Vedas live, guarding them is his duty; they bless him and leave.","n":"The sages turn the campaign's road into a duty: on the way to punish northern kings, the Chera is charged to protect the northern Brahmins. He keeps the charge at the close of the kaathai (lines 248-250), when he orders the Veda-keepers of the north guarded before he takes the stone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","vedham","arasu","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 93-104","lb":"SI26:93-104","ev":"Sky-faring sages visit the camp and charge the king to protect the Brahmins of the Himalaya.","cl":[["protecting the Brahmins who keep the Vedas is a king's duty","vedic","asserted",103,"sky_sages"]],"ag":["விசும்பியங்கு முனிவர்","குடவர் கோ"],"ec":[],"spk":["விசும்பியங்கு முனிவர்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:105-127":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":352,"t":["வீங்குநீர் ஞாலம் ஆள்வோன் வாழ்கெனக்","கொங்கணக் கூத்தரும் கொடுங்கரு நாடரும்","தங்குலக் கோதிய தகைசால் அணியினர்","இருள்படப் பொதுளிய சுருளிருங் குஞ்சி","மருள்படப் பரப்பிய ஒலியல் மாலையர்","வடம்சுமந் தோங்கிய வளர்இள வனமுலைக்","கருங்கயல் நெடுங்கட் காரிகை யாரோடு","இருங்குயில் ஆல இனவண்டு யாழ்செய","அரும்பவிழ் வேனில் வந்தது வாரார்","காதல ரென்னும் மேதகு சிறப்பின்","மாதர்ப் பாணி வரியொடு தோன்றக்","கோல்வளை மாதே கோலங் கொள்ளாய்","காலங் காணாய் கடிதிடித் துரறிக்","காரோ வந்தது காதல ரேறிய","தேரோ வந்தது செய்வினை முடித்தெனக்","காஅர்க் குரவையொடு கருங்கயல் நெடுங்கட்","கோற்றொடி மாதரொடு குடகர் தோன்றத்","தாழ்தரு கோலத்துத் தமரொடு சிறந்து","வாள்வினை முடித்து மறவாள் வேந்தன்","ஊழி வாழியென் றோவர் தோன்றக்","கூத்துள் படுவோன் காட்டிய முறைமையின்","ஏத்தின ரறியா இருங்கலன் நல்கி","வேத்தினம் நடுக்கும் வேலோன் இருந்துழி"],"p":"","r":"Performers from Konkan and Karnataka in the dress of their peoples come with dark-eyed women singing that spring has come and the lover has not, the Kudagar come singing the rainy-season kuravai, asking a bangled girl whether it is the rain cloud that has come or her lover's chariot with his work done, and the Ovar hail the king who will finish his sword-work to live an aeon; he rewards them all with jewels beyond their hopes.","n":"The camp's songs are songs of separation sung to a man setting out: the spring song says the lover has not come, the rain song imagines his chariot returning with his work done (செய்வினை முடித்து), so the performers set the king's campaign in the frame of a woman waiting for the return. கூத்துள் படுவோன் காட்டிய முறைமையின் may mean the rewards followed the rule the dance-master showed; the phrase is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pirivu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 105-127","lb":"SI26:105-127","ev":"Performers from Konkan, Karnataka and Kudagu sing at the camp and the king rewards them.","cl":[],"ag":["கொங்கணக் கூத்தரும் கொடுங்கரு நாடரும்","குடகர்","ஓவர்","குடவர் கோ"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:128-140":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":353,"t":["நாடக மகளிர்ஈ ரைம்பத் திருவரும்","கூடிசைக் குயிலுவர் இருநூற் றெண்மரும்","தொண்ணூற் றறுவகைப் பாசண் டத்துறை","நண்ணிய நூற்றுவர் நகைவே ழம்பரும்","கொடுஞ்சி நெடுந்தேர் ஐம்பதிற் றிரட்டியும்","கடுங்களி யானை ஓரைஞ் ஞூறும்","ஐயீ ராயிரங் கொய்யுளைப் புரவியும்","எய்யா வடவளத் திருபதி னாயிரம்","கண்ணெழுத்துப் படுத்தன கைபுனை சகடமும்","சஞ்சயன் முதலாத் தலைக்கீடு பெற்ற","கஞ்சுக முதல்வர்ஈ ரைஞ்ஞூற் றுவரும்","சேயுயர் விற்கொடிச் செங்கோல் வேந்தே","வாயி லோரென வாயில்வந் திசைப்ப"],"p":"","r":"The gatekeepers announce to the king of the bow banner that there have arrived one hundred and two dancing women, two hundred and eight musicians, a hundred jesters versed in the ninety-six kinds of sect, a hundred chariots, five hundred elephants, ten thousand horses, twenty thousand carts of northern goods marked in writing, and a thousand robed officers led by Sanjayan.","n":"தொண்ணூற் றறுவகைப் பாசண்டத் துறை நண்ணிய நூற்றுவர் நகைவேழம்பர்: the jesters are said to know the ninety-six sects, perhaps because their comedy imitates the sects' teachers; the number ninety-six is the conventional count of the heterodox schools, but the line does not say how the jesters use it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padai","selvam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 128-140","lb":"SI26:128-140","ev":"The gatekeepers announce the arrival of the allied envoys and their gifts.","cl":[],"ag":["வாயிலோர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["வாயிலோர்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:141-155":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":354,"t":["நாடக மகளிரும் நலத்தகு மாக்களும்","கூடிசைக் குயிலுவக் கருவி யாளரும்","சஞ்சயன் றன்னொடு வருக ஈங்கெனச்","செங்கோல் வேந்தன் திருவிளங் கவையத்துச்","சஞ்சயன் புகுந்து தாழ்ந்துபல ஏத்தி","ஆணையிற் புகுந்தஈ ரைம்பத் திருவரொடு","மாண்வினை யாளரை வகைபெறக் காட்டி","வேற்றுமை யின்றி நின்னொடு கலந்த","நூற்றுவர் கன்னருங் கோற்றொழில் வேந்தே","வடதிசை மருங்கின் வானவன் பெயர்வது","கடவு ளெழுதவோர் கற்கே யாயின்","ஓங்கிய இமையத்துக் கற்கால் கொண்டு","வீங்குநீர்க் கங்கை நீர்ப்படை செய்தாங்கு","யாந்தரு மாற்றல மென்றன ரென்று","வீங்குநீர் ஞாலம் ஆள்வோய் வாழ்கென"],"p":"","r":"The king bids the dancers, performers and musicians come in with Sanjayan; Sanjayan enters, bows, presents the hundred and two and the craftsmen, and reports the Nootruvar Kannar's message that if the Chera's march north is only for a stone to carve a god, they themselves will take one from the Himalaya, bathe it in the Ganga and bring it to him.","n":"The allies' offer would give the king his stone without a war; the next unit declines it, so the stone must be taken by battle for the vow to be kept, not merely obtained.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vendhan","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 141-155","lb":"SI26:141-155","ev":"Sanjayan presents the gifts and reports the Nootruvar Kannar's offer to fetch and bathe the stone for the king.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","சஞ்சயன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["சஞ்சயன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:156-171":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":355,"t":["அடல்வேன் மன்னர் ஆருயி ருண்ணும்","கடலந் தானைக் காவல னுரைக்கும்","பால குமரன் மக்கள் மற்றவர்","காவா நாவிற் கனகனும் விசயனும்","விருந்தின் மன்னர் தம்மொடுங் கூடி","அருந்தமி ழாற்றல் அறிந்தில ராங்கெனக்","கூற்றங் கொண்டிச் சேனை செல்வது","நூற்றுவர் கன்னர்க்குச் சாற்றி யாங்குக்","கங்கைப் பேர்யாறு கடத்தற் காவன","வங்கப் பெருநிரை செய்க தாமெனச்","சஞ்சயன் போனபின் கஞ்சுக மாக்கள்","எஞ்சா நாவினர் ஈரைஞ் ஞூற்றுவர்","சந்தின் குப்பையுந் தாழ்நீர் முத்தும்","தென்ன ரிட்ட திறையொடு கொணர்ந்து","கண்ணெழுத் தாளர் காவல் வேந்தன்","மண்ணுடை முடங்கலம் மன்னவர்க் களித்தாங்கு"],"p":"","r":"The king answers that Kanakan and Vijayan, the sons of Balakumaran, of unguarded tongue, joined with visiting kings, have not known the strength of Tamil; let Sanjayan tell the Kannar that an army like Death is coming and have them build rows of boats to cross the Ganga; Sanjayan leaves, and the thousand robed officers bring sandal, pearls and the Pandya's tribute, while the scribes hand the kings sealed letters.","n":"The insult is now named: two kings, Kanakan and Vijayan, whose fault is a careless tongue (காவா நாவின்) and ignorance of அருந்தமிழ் ஆற்றல். The quarrel is cast as the honour of Tamil, not of the Chera alone, which is why the Pandya's tribute arrives in the same breath.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","maanam","padai","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 156-171","lb":"SI26:156-171","ev":"The king refuses the allies' stone, names Kanakan and Vijayan as the offenders, and asks the Kannar for boats to cross the Ganga.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","சஞ்சயன்","கஞ்சுக மாக்கள்","கண்ணெழுத் தாளர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["குடவர் கோ","சஞ்சயன்"],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:172-181":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":356,"t":["ஆங்கவ ரேகிய பின்னர் மன்னிய","வீங்குநீர் ஞாலம் ஆள்வோ னோங்கிய","நாடாள் செல்வர் நலவல னேத்தப்","பாடி யிருக்கை நீங்கிப் பெயர்ந்து","கங்கைப்பே ரியாற்றுக் கன்னரிற் பெற்ற","வங்கப் பரப்பின் வடமருங் கெய்தி","ஆங்கவ ரெதிர்கொள அந்நாடு கழித்தாங்கு","ஒங்குநீர் வேலி உத்தர மரீஇப்","பகைப்புலம் புக்குப் பாசறை யிருந்த","தகைப்பருந் தானை மறவோன் றன்முன்"],"p":"","r":"When the envoys have gone, the ruler of the sea-girt earth, hailed by the lords of the land, leaves his camp, crosses to the northern bank of the Ganga on the Kannar's boats, is received by them, passes through their country and enters the northern land, making camp in enemy territory.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","naadu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 172-181","lb":"SI26:172-181","ev":"The Chera crosses the Ganga on the Kannar's boats and camps in the enemy's northern country.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","நூற்றுவர் கன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:182-199":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":357,"t":["உத்தரன் விசித்திரன் உருத்திரன் பைரவன்","சித்திரன் சிங்கன் தனுத்தரன் சிவேதன்","வடதிசை மருங்கின் மன்னவ ரெல்லாம்","தென்றமி ழாற்றல் காண்குதும் யாமெனக்","கலந்த கேண்மையிற் கனக விசயர்","நிலந்திரைத் தானையொடு நிகர்த்து மேல்வர","இரைதேர் வேட்டத் தெழுந்த அரிமாக்","கரிமாப் பெருநிரை கண்டுளஞ் சிறந்து","பாய்ந்த பண்பிற் பல்வேன் மன்னர்","காஞ்சித் தானையொடு காவலன் மலைப்ப","வெயிற்கதிர் விழுங்கிய துகிற்கொடிப் பந்தர்","வடித்தோற் கொடும்பறை வால்வளை நெடுவயிர்","இடிக்குரல் முரசம் இழுமென் பாண்டில்","உயிர்ப்பலி யுண்ணும் உருமுக்குரல் முழுக்கத்து","மயிர்க்கண் முரசமொடு மாதிரம் அதிரச்","சிலைத்தோ ளாடவர் செருவேற் றடக்கையர்","கறைத்தோன் மறவர் கடுந்தே ரூருநர்","வெண்கோட்டு யானையர் விரைபரிக் குதிரையர்"],"p":"","r":"Before him Uttaran, Vichitran, Rudran, Bhairavan, Chitran, Singan, Dhanuttaran, Svetan and all the kings of the north, saying they will see the strength of southern Tamil, come on in league with Kanaka and Vijaya, and the king falls on them like a lion that, hunting, sees a herd of elephants, while drums, conches and horns shake every quarter and archers, spearmen, charioteers, elephant riders and horsemen close in.","n":"The northern kings take up the very phrase of the quarrel, தென்றமிழ் ஆற்றல் காண்குதும், so the battle is staged as a test of Tamil power. The drum that eats the offering of lives (உயிர்ப்பலி யுண்ணும்) makes the war itself a rite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","thamizh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 182-199","lb":"SI26:182-199","ev":"The northern kings, allied with Kanaka and Vijaya, march to test Tamil strength and the Chera attacks.","cl":[],"ag":["வடதிசை மன்னவர் / ஆரிய அரசர்","கனகன்","விசயன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:200-210":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":358,"t":["மண்கண் கெடுத்தவிம் மாநிலப் பெருந்துகள்","களங்கொள் யானைக் கவிழ்மணி நாவும்","விளங்குகொடி நந்தின் வீங்கிசை நாவும்","நடுங்குதொழி லொழிந்தாங்கு ஒடுங்கியுள் செறியத்","தாருந் தாருந் தாமிடை மயங்கத்","தோளுந் தலையுந் துணிந்துவே றாகிய","சிலைத்தோள் மறவர் உடற்பொறை யடுக்கத்து","எறிபிணம் இடறிய குறையுடற் கவந்தம்","பறைக்கட் பேய்மகள் பாணிக் காடப்","பிணஞ்சுமந் தொழுகிய நிணம்படு குருதியில்","கணங்கொள் பேய்மகள் கதுப்பிகுத் தாட"],"p":"","r":"The great dust of battle blinds the earth and stills the tongues of the elephants' bells and the conches, garlands tangle with garlands, archers' shoulders and heads lie severed in heaps, headless trunks stumble over corpses, and demon women dance to the drum and loosen their hair in the fat-laden blood that carries the bodies.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 200-210","lb":"SI26:200-210","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["பேய்மகள் / மறப்பேய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:211-224":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":359,"t":["அடுந்தேர்த் தானை ஆரிய வரசர்","கடும்படை மாக்களைக் கொன்று களங்குவித்து","நெடுந்தேர்க் கொடுஞ்சியுங் கடுங்களிற் றெருத்தமும்","விடும்பரிக் குதிரையின் வெரிநும் பாழ்பட","எருமைக் கடும்பரி ஊர்வோன் உயிர்த்தொகை","ஒருபக லெல்லையின் உண்ணு மென்பது","ஆரிய வரசர் அமர்க்களத் தறிய","நூழி லாட்டிய சூழ்கழல் வேந்தன்","போந்தையொடு தொடுத்த பருவத் தும்பை","ஓங்கிருஞ் சென்னி மேம்பட மலைய","வாய்வா ளாண்மையின் வண்டமி ழிகழ்ந்த","காய்வேற் றடக்கைக் கனகனும் விசயனும்","ஐம்பத் திருவர் கடுந்தே ராளரொடு","செங்குட் டுவன்றன் சினவலைப் படுதலும்"],"p":"","r":"The king slays the fighting men of the Aryan kings and heaps them on the field, lays waste their chariots, elephants and horses so that the Aryan kings learn on the battlefield that the buffalo-rider eats a whole host of lives in a single day, wears the thumbai with the palmyra after the war dance, and Kanakan and Vijayan, who scorned rich Tamil trusting their swords, fall with fifty-two chariot warriors into Senguttuvan's net of anger.","n":"Death (the buffalo-rider) is made the Chera's teacher: the Aryan kings learn in one day what they did not know of Tamil strength. The capture, not the killing, of Kanakan and Vijayan keeps them for the humiliation that follows (they will carry the stone).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru","thamizh","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 211-224","lb":"SI26:211-224","ev":"Senguttuvan destroys the Aryan kings' armies and captures Kanakan and Vijayan with fifty-two chariot warriors.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","வடதிசை மன்னவர் / ஆரிய அரசர்","கனகன்","விசயன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:225-246":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":360,"t":["சடையினர் உடையினர் சாம்பற் பூச்சினர்","பீடிகைப் பீலிப் பெருநோன் பாளர்","பாடு பாணியர் பல்லியத் தோளினர்","ஆடு கூத்த ராகி யெங்கணும்","ஏந்துவா ளொழியத் தாந்துறை போகிய","விச்சைக் கோலத்து வேண்டுவயிற் படர்தரக்","கச்சை யானைக் காவலர் நடுங்கக்","கோட்டுமாப் பூட்டி வாட்கோ லாக","ஆளழி வாங்கி அதரி திரித்த","வாளே ருழவன் மறக்களம் வாழ்த்தித்","தொடியுடை நெடுங்கை தூங்கத் தூக்கி","முடியுடைக் கருந்தலை முந்துற ஏந்திக்","கடல்வயிறு கலக்கிய ஞாட்புங் கடலகழ்","இலங்கையி லெழுந்த சமரமுங் கடல்வணன்","தேரூர் செருவும் பாடிப் பேரிசை","முன்றேர்க் குரவை முதல்வனைவாழ்த்திப்","பின்றேர்க் குரவைப் பேயாடு பறந்தலை","முடித்தலை யடுப்பிற் பிடர்த்தலைத் தாழித்","தொடித்தோள் துடுப்பின் துழைஇய ஊன்சோறு","மறப்பேய் வாலுவன் வயினறிந் தூட்டச்","சிறப்பூண் கடியினஞ் செங்கோற் கொற்றத்து","அறக்களஞ் செய்தோன் ஊழி வாழ்கென"],"p":"","r":"The other northern kings throw away their swords and slip off wherever they wish in disguise, as matted ascetics, robed monks, ash-smeared devotees, peacock-feather vow-keepers, singers, players and dancers, while the elephant-riding guardians tremble; and the demons praise the battlefield of the sword-ploughman who threshed it with tuskers yoked and the sword for goad, sing of the churning of the sea, the war in Lanka and the dark god's chariot war, dance the kuravai before and behind the chariot, and cook a meat-rice in pots on hearths of crowned heads stirred with bangled arms, which the demon cook serves, hailing the one who made a field of dharma by the victory of the just sceptre.","n":"The disguises cover the whole range of ascetics (matted hair, robes, ash, the peacock-feather fan of the great vow) and performers, so the defeated kings survive by wearing the marks of renunciation and art; the text says only that they went where they wished, not that the victor spared them knowingly. The demons' blessing calls the slaughter an அறக்களம் made by the செங்கோல்: the battle feast is given the name of dharma by those who eat it. விச்சைக் கோலத்து (the guise of the arts they had mastered) is not certain in sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","maram","aram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 225-246","lb":"SI26:225-246","ev":"The surviving northern kings flee in the guise of ascetics and performers, and the demons sing and cook their battle feast for the victor.","cl":[["a war won under a just sceptre makes the battlefield a field of dharma","general","reported",246,"pey"]],"ag":["வடதிசை மன்னவர் / ஆரிய அரசர்","பேய்மகள் / மறப்பேய்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:33"},"si:26:247-254":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":361,"t":["மறக்கள முடித்த வாய்வாட் குட்டுவன்","வடதிசை மருங்கின் மறைகாத் தோம்புநர்","தடவுத்தீ யவியாத் தண்பெரு வாழ்க்கை","காற்றூ தாளரைப் போற்றிக் காமினென","வில்லவன் கோதையொடு வென்றுவினை முடித்த","பல்வேற் றானைப் படைபல ஏவிப்","பொற்கோட் டிமயத்துப் பொருவறு பத்தினிக்","கற்கால் கொண்டனன் காவல னாங்கென்."],"p":"","r":"Kuttuvan, having finished the battle, orders that the northern guardians of the Vedas, whose cool life keeps the great fire unquenched, be protected, sends out his many armies under Villavan Kothai to complete the conquest, and takes from the golden-peaked Himalaya the stone for the peerless pathini.","n":"Only now is the god of the vow (line 14) named: the stone is for the பத்தினி, Kannaki. The order to protect the Veda-keepers keeps the charge of the sky-faring sages (lines 102-103). காற்றூதாளர், those who blow the fire, is a rare word for the fire-tending Brahmins.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathini","vanjinam","andhanar","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 26, lines 247-254","lb":"SI26:247-254","ev":"Senguttuvan orders the northern Brahmins protected, sends his armies on with Villavan Kothai, and takes the stone for the pathini from the Himalaya.","cl":[["a conquering king must protect the Veda-keeping Brahmins of the conquered land","vedic","asserted",250,"senguttuvan"]],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","வில்லவன் கோதை"],"ec":[],"spk":["குடவர் கோ","வில்லவன் கோதை"],"ch":"si:33"},"si:27:1-17":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":362,"t":["வடபே ரிமயத்து வான்றகு சிறப்பிற்","கடவுட் பத்தினிக் கற்கால் கொண்டபின்","சினவேல் முன்பிற் செருவெங் கோலத்துக்","கனக விசயர்தங் கதிர்முடி யேற்றிச்","செறிகழல் வேந்தன் தென்றமி ழாற்றல்","அறியாது மலைந்த ஆரிய மன்னரைச்","செயிர்த்தொழில் முதியோன் செய்தொழில் பெருக","உயிர்த்தொகை யுண்ட வொன்பதிற் றிரட்டியென்று","யாண்டும் மதியும் நாளுங் கடிகையும்","ஈண்டுநீர் ஞாலங் கூட்டி யெண்கொள","வருபெருந் தானை மறக்கள மருங்கின்","ஒருபக லெல்லை உயிர்த்தொகை உண்ட","செங்குட் டுவன்றன் சினவேற் றானையொடு","கங்கைப் பேர்யாற்றுக் கரையகம் புகுந்து","பாற்படு மரபிற் பத்தினிக் கடவுளை","நூற்றிறன் மாக்களி னீர்ப்படை செய்து","மன்பெருங் கோயிலும் மணிமண் டபங்களும்"],"p":"","r":"The narrator recounts that, after the stone for the goddess-pathini had been taken from the Himalaya and set on the crowns of Kanaka and Vijaya, the Chera, whose army had reckoned its slaughter of the northern kings who fought him not knowing Tamil strength, came with his host to the bank of the Ganga and had the pathini deity bathed by men learned in the rite.","n":"The kaathai is named for this bathing (நீர்ப்படை), told in a single subordinate clause (lines 15 to 16) before the poem turns to the camp; the rite is done by நூற்றிறன் மாக்கள, men who know the treatise, not by the king. The pathini is called கடவுட் பத்தினி and பத்தினிக் கடவுள், a goddess, before any temple is built. Lines 7 to 12, where the work of the old death-dealer swells and the army reckons years, months, days and hours, and the eighteen (ஒன்பதிற்று இரட்டி) and the one day's span are given, are compressed and the count is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathini","vazhipaadu","maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 1-17","lb":"SI27:1-17","ev":"Senguttuvan, the northern kings beaten in a day's battle, reaches the Ganga and has the pathini's stone bathed there.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:18-42":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":363,"t":["பொன்புனை யரங்கமும் புனைபூம் பந்தரும்","உரிமைப் பள்ளியும் விரிபூஞ் சோலையும்","திருமலர்ப் பொய்கையும் வரிகாண் அரங்கமும்","பேரிசை மன்னர்க் கேற்பவை பிறவும்","ஆரிய மன்னர் அழகுற அமைத்த","தெள்ளுநீர்க் கங்கைத் தென்கரை யாங்கண்","வெள்ளிடைப் பாடி வேந்தன் புக்கு","நீணில மன்னர் நெஞ்சுபுக லழித்து","வானவர் மகளிரின் வதுவைசூட் டயர்ந்தோர்","உலையா வெஞ்சமம் ஊர்ந்தமர் உழக்கித்","தலையுந் தோளும் விலைபெறக் கிடந்தோர்","நாள்விலைக் கிளையுள் நல்லம ரழுவத்து","வாள்வினை முடித்து மறத்தொடு முடிந்தோர்","குழிகட் பேய்மகள் குரவையிற் றொடுத்து","வழிமருங் கேத்த வாளொடு மடிந்தோர்","கிளைக டம்மொடு கிளர்பூ ணாகத்து","வளையோர் மடிய மடிந்தோர் மைந்தர்","மலைத்துத் தலைவந்தோர் வாளொடு மடியத்","தலைத்தார் வாகை தம்முடிக் கணிந்தோர்","திண்டேர்க் கொடுஞ்சியொடு தேரோர் வீழப்","புண்டோய் குருதியிற் பொலிந்த மைந்தர்","மாற்றருஞ் சிறப்பின் மணிமுடிக் கருந்தலைக்","கூற்றுக்கண் ணோட அரிந்துகளங் கொண்டோர்","நிறஞ்சிதை கவயமொடு நிறப்புண் கூர்ந்து","புறம்பெற வந்த போர்வாள் மறவர்"],"p":"","r":"The king enters the camp on the open ground of the Ganga's south bank, in halls, pavilions, groves and pools that the Arya kings had built for him, and the narrator lists the warriors he will honour: those who broke enemy kings' hearts and were wed like brides of the gods, those who lay with head and shoulders paid for in battle, those who died with their swords as the hollow-eyed demoness sang their praise in her kuravai, those who wore the victor's garland and stood red with their wounds.","n":"The list puts the dead and the living in one catalogue: the dead are given the heavenly marriage (வானவர் மகளிரின் வதுவை) and a demoness's praise, the living are marked by wounds, and all are the same grammatical subject of the honours given in the next lines. The Arya kings' building of the camp for the conqueror (line 22) is tribute, not hospitality.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","saakkaadu","marumai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 18-42","lb":"SI27:18-42","ev":"Senguttuvan enters the camp the Arya kings built for him on the Ganga's south bank.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","ஆரிய மன்னர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:43-56":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":364,"t":["வருக தாமென வாகைப் பொலந்தோடு","பெருநா ளமயம் பிறக்கிடக் கொடுத்துத்","தோடார் போந்தை தும்பையொடு முடித்துப்","பாடுதுறை முற்றிய கொற்ற வேந்தன்","ஆடுகொள் மார்போ டரசுவிளங் கிருக்கையின்","மாடல மறையோன் வந்து தோன்றி","வாழ்க வெங்கோ மாதவி மடந்தை","கானற் பாணி கனக விசயர்தம்","முடித்தலை நெரித்தது முதுநீர் ஞாலம்","அடிப்படுத் தாண்ட அரசே வாழ்கெனப்","பகைபுலத் தரசர் பலரீங் கறியா","நகைத்திறங் கூறினை நான்மறை யாள","யாதுநீ கூறிய உரைப்பொரு ளீங்கென","மாடல மறையோன் மன்னவற் குரைக்கும்"],"p":"","r":"Calling his wounded warriors forward, the king gives them golden vaagai leaves through the long festival hour, and as he sits in state, crowned with palmyra and thumbai, the Brahmin Maadalan appears and hails him, saying that Madhavi's seaside song crushed the crowned heads of Kanaka and Vijaya, and the king asks what this jest, unknown to the kings of the enemy lands, means.","n":"Maadalan's riddle makes a courtesan's song the first cause of a northern war: the kaanal vari split Kovalan from Madhavi, which led to Madurai, to Kannaki, and to the stone the northern kings now carry. The king calls it நகைத்திறம், a jest, and asks for its sense; the next units are the answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","parisil","isai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 43-56","lb":"SI27:43-56","ev":"Senguttuvan rewards his warriors, and Maadalan arrives and tells him Madhavi's song crushed the crowns of Kanaka and Vijaya.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","மாடலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:57-77":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":365,"t":["கானலந் தண்டுறைக் கடல்விளை யாட்டினுள்","மாதவி மடந்தை வரிநவில் பாணியோடு","ஊடற் காலத் தூழ்வினை யுருத்தெழக்","கூடாது பிரிந்து குலக்கொடி தன்னுடன்","மாட மூதூர் மதுரை புக்காங்கு","இலைத்தார் வேந்தன் எழில்வான் எய்தக்","கொலைக்களப் பட்ட கோவலன் மனைவி","குடவர் கோவே நின்னாடு புகுந்து","வடதிசை மன்னர் மணிமுடி யேறினள்","இன்னுங் கேட்டருள் இகல்வேற் றடக்கை","மன்னர் கோவே யான்வருங் காரணம்","மாமுனி பொதியின் மலைவலங் கொண்டு","குமரியம் பெருந்துறை யாடி மீள்வேன்","ஊழ்வினைப் பயன்கொல் உரைசால் சிறப்பின்","வாய்வாட் டென்னவன் மதுரையிற் சென்றேன்","வலம்படு தானை மன்னவன் றன்னைச்","சிலம்பின் வென்றனள் சேயிழை யென்றலும்","தாதெரு மன்றத்து மாதரி யெழுந்து","கோவலன் தீதிலன் கோமகன் பிழைத்தான்","அடைக்கல மிழந்தேன் இடைக்குல மாக்காள்","குடையும் கோலும் பிழைத்த வோவென"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan explains that at the sea-play on the cool shore, in a time of sulking, with Madhavi's vari song, the deed of the past rose in wrath, Kovalan left her and went with his wife to Madurai, was killed, and his wife came into the Chera's land and rose onto the crowns of the northern kings; then he tells why he came: returning from circling Pothiyil and bathing at Kumari, by karma's fruit perhaps he went to Madurai, heard that the woman had beaten the king with her anklet, and saw Madari rise in the common and cry that Kovalan was blameless, the king had erred, and she had lost the one put in her keeping.","n":"Line 59 gives two causes in one breath: the lovers' sulk (ஊடற் காலத்து) and the deed of the past rising (ஊழ்வினை உருத்தெழ). Maadalan then puts even his own arrival at Madurai under karma, as a question (ஊழ்வினைப் பயன்கொல்), while Madari blames the king outright (கோமகன் பிழைத்தான், line 75) and her own failure to guard her charge. So the unit sets karma, the king's fault and a guardian's failure side by side without choosing among them. Madari's அடைக்கலம் is a ward entrusted to her, not a devotee's refuge at a god's feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","kodungol","adaikkalam","pathini"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 57-77","lb":"SI27:57-77","ev":"Maadalan tells Senguttuvan how Kovalan left Madhavi, died in Madurai, and how Madari cried out that the king had erred.","cl":[["the deed of a former birth rose in wrath and parted Kovalan from Madhavi","general","asserted",59,"maadalan"],["Kovalan was without fault and the king erred in killing him","general","asserted",75,"maadhari"],["one who loses a person given into her keeping has failed in her trust","general","asserted",76,"maadhari"]],"ag":["மாதவி","கோவலன்","கண்ணகி","மாதரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:78-95":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":366,"t":["இடையிருள் யாமத் தெரியகம் புக்கதும்","தவந்தரு சிறப்பிற் கவுந்தி சீற்றம்","நிவந்தோங்கு செங்கோல் நீணில வேந்தன்","போகுயிர் தாங்கப் பொறைசா லாட்டி","என்னோ டிவர்வினை உருத்த தோவென","உண்ணா நோன்போ டுயிர்பதிப் பெயர்த்ததும்","பொற்றேர்ச் செழியன் மதுரை மாநகர்க்கு","உற்றது மெல்லாம் ஒழிவின் றுணர்ந்தாங்கு","என்பதிப் பெயர்ந்தேன் என்துயர் போற்றிச்","செம்பியன் மூதூர்ச் சிறந்தோர்க் குரைக்க","மைந்தற் குற்றதும் மடந்தைக் குற்றதும்","செங்கோல் வேந்தற் குற்றதுங் கேட்டுக்","கோவலன் தாதை கொடுந்துய ரெய்தி","மாபெருந் தானமா வான்பொரு ளீத்தாங்கு","இந்திர விகாரம் ஏழுடன் புக்காங்கு","அந்தர சாரிகள் ஆறைம் பதின்மர்","பிறந்த யாக்கைப் பிறப்பற முயன்று","துறந்தோர் தம்முன் துறவி யெய்தவும்"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan goes on: Madari entered the fire in the middle of the night; Kavunthi, the ascetic, in her anger at the king, asking whether the couple's deed had risen against her too, gave up her life by the vow of not eating; having learned all that befell Madurai he went home to Puhar and told it, and Kovalan's father, hearing what came to his son, his son's wife and the king, gave away great wealth, entered the seven Indra viharas and renounced before the three hundred sky-walkers who strive to end the birth of the body.","n":"Kavunthi's question (line 82) is the striking thing: a Jain nun, who has taught that deeds come back to their doers, asks whether the couple's deed has come back on her, the one who led them, and answers with the fasting death. Lines 80 to 81, with the king's departing life and the patient woman, are hard to construe: whether the king's death is the time of her fast, or what she could not bear, is not clear. Kovalan's father renounces at the Indra viharas, which the poem placed in Puhar under the five-branched bodhi (Silappathikaram 10), so his renunciation is in the Buddhist order; the unit does not state the doctrine, only the aim of ending birth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["unnaanonbu","vinai","thuravu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 78-95","lb":"SI27:78-95","ev":"Madari enters the fire, Kavunthi fasts to death, and Kovalan's father, told by Maadalan, gives away his wealth and becomes a renouncer.","cl":[["the deed of those one has guided may rise against oneself","general","reported",82,"kavunthi"],["renouncers strive to end the rebirth of the born body","buddhist","reported",94,"maadalan"]],"ag":["மாதரி","கவுந்தி / காவுந்தி ஐயை","மாடலன்","மாசாத்துவான்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:96-115":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":367,"t":["துறந்தோன் மனைவி மகன்துயர் பொறாஅள்","இறந்ததுய ரெய்தி இரங்கிமெய் விடவும்","கண்ணகி தாதை கடவுளர் கோலத்து","அண்ணலம் பெருந்தவத் தாசீ வகர்முன்","புண்ணிய தானம் புரிந்தறங் கொள்ளவும்","தானம் புரிந்தோன் றன்மனைக் கிழத்தி","நாள்விடூஉ நல்லுயிர் நீத்துமெய் விடவும்","மற்றது கேட்டு மாதவி மடந்தை","நற்றாய் தனக்கு நற்றிறம் படர்கேன்","மணிமே கலையை வான்துயர் உறுக்குங்","கணிகையர் கோலங் காணா தொழிகெனக்","கோதைத் தாமம் குழலொடு களைந்து","போதித் தானம் புரிந்தறங் கொள்ளவும்","என்வாய்க் கேட்டோர் இறந்தோ ருண்மையின்","நன்னீர்க் கங்கை யாடப் போந்தேன்","மன்னர் கோவே வாழ்க ஈங்கெனத்","தோடார் போந்தை தும்பையொடு முடித்த","வாடாவஞ்சி வானவர் பெருந்தகை","மன்னவன் இறந்தபின் வளங்கெழு சிறப்பின்","தென்னவன் நாடு செய்ததீங் குரையென"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan says that Kovalan's mother, unable to bear her son's grief, died; Kannaki's father gave gifts before the Ajivika ascetics in their godlike guise and took their dharma, and his wife died too; and Madhavi, hearing it, told her mother that Manimekalai must never see the courtesan's guise that brings great sorrow, cut off her hair with its garland and took dharma with a gift at the bodhi; since those who heard the news from his mouth died, he has come to bathe in the Ganga; and the king asks what the Pandya land has done since its king died.","n":"The same news ends in renunciation for the two fathers and Madhavi and in death for the two mothers. The two merchants turn to different orders, Kovalan's father to the Indra viharas, Kannaki's father to the Ajivikas (ஆசீவகர், line 99), and Madhavi to the bodhi (போதித் தானம், line 108); the poem names the orders without ranking them. Madhavi's reason is her daughter: she renounces so that Manimekalai will not take up the courtesan's life, the thread the Manimekalai follows. Maadalan bathes in the Ganga because his telling caused deaths (line 109), a messenger's guilt for the news he carried.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","varaivinmagalir","eegai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 96-115","lb":"SI27:96-115","ev":"Kovalan's mother and Kannaki's mother die of grief, Kannaki's father joins the Ajivikas, and Madhavi cuts her hair and renounces; Senguttuvan asks about the Pandya land.","cl":[["the courtesan's guise brings great sorrow to the daughter who takes it up","general","asserted",105,"madhavi"],["one whose words have brought death to others should cleanse himself in the Ganga","general","reported",110,"maadalan"]],"ag":["பெருமனைக் கிழத்தி","மாநாய்கன்","மனைக்கிழத்தி","மாதவி","குடவர் கோ"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:116-137":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":368,"t":["நீடு வாழியரோ நீணில வேந்தென","மாடல மறையோன் மன்னவற் குரைக்கும்நின்","மைத்துன வளவன் கிள்ளியொடு பொருந்தா","ஒத்த பண்பினர் ஒன்பது மன்னர்","இளவரசு பொறாஅர் ஏவல் கேளார்","வளநா டழிக்கும் மாண்பின ராதலின்","ஒன்பது குடையும் ஒருபக லொழித்தவன்","பொன்புனை திகிரி ஒருவழிப் படுத்தோய்","பழையன் காக்கும் குழைபயில் நெடுங்கோட்டு","வேம்புமுதல் தடிந்த ஏந்துவாள் வலத்துப்","போந்தைக் கண்ணிப் பொறைய கேட்டருள்","கொற்கையி லிருந்த வெற்றிவேற் செழியன்","பொற்றொழிற் கொல்லர் ஈரைஞ் ஞூற்றுவர்","ஒருமுலை குறைத்த திருமா பத்தினிக்கு","ஒருபக லெல்லை யுயிர்ப்பலி யூட்டி","உரைசெல வெறுத்த மதுரை மூதூர்","அரைசுகெடுத் தலம்வரும் அல்லற் காலை","தென்புல மருங்கில் தீதுதீர் சிறப்பின்","மன்பதை காக்கும் முறைமுதற் கட்டிலின்","நிரைமணிப் புரவி ஓரழ் பூண்ட","ஒருதனி யாழிக் கடவுட் டேர்மிசைக்","காலைச் செங்கதிர்க் கடவுளே றினனென"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan answers by first praising the king, who, when nine kings of one mind would not bear the young rule of his cousin the Chola Killi, destroyed their nine umbrellas in a single day, and who felled the neem guarded by Pazhaiyan, and then tells that Vetrivel Cheliyan of Korkai, in one day, fed the lives of a thousand goldsmiths as an offering to the pathini who cut off one breast, and, when Madurai had lost its king in its time of distress, mounted the throne of justice as the morning sun mounts the god's chariot.","n":"The new Pandya's first act is an offering of lives (உயிர்ப்பலி) to the pathini: the craft of the one goldsmith who lied is punished a thousandfold, and the poem gives it as worship, not as a sentence. The unit does not say the goldsmiths were guilty. Maadalan's praise of the Chera for the Chola succession shows the three crowned houses bound by kinship: the Chera intervenes for a cousin of his mother's line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["bali","pathini","arasu","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 116-137","lb":"SI27:116-137","ev":"Maadalan tells how Vetrivel Cheliyan offered a thousand goldsmiths to the pathini and took the Pandya throne.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","ஒன்பது மன்னர்","வெற்றிவேற் செழியன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:138-161":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":369,"t":["மாலைத் திங்கள் வழியோன் ஏறினன்","ஊழிதொ றூழி உலகங் காத்து","வாழ்க எங்கோ வாழிய பெரிதென","மறையோன் கூறிய மாற்ற மெல்லாம்","இறையோன் கேட்டாங் கிருந்த எல்லையுள்","அகல்வாய் ஞாலம் ஆரிருள் விழுங்கப்","பகல்செல முதிர்ந்த படர்கூர் மாலைச்","செந்தீப் பரந்த திசைமுகம் விளங்க","அந்திச் செக்கர் வெண்பிறை தோன்றப்","பிறையேர் வண்ணம் பெருந்தகை நோக்க","இறையோன் செவ்வியிற் கணியெழுந் துரைப்போன்","எண்ணான்கு மதியம் வஞ்சி நீங்கியது","மண்ணாள் வேந்தே வாழ்கென் றேத்த","நெடுங்காழ்க் கண்டம் நிரல்பட நிரைத்த","கொடும்பட நெடுமதிற் கொடித்தேர் வீதியுள்","குறியவும் நெடியவுங் குன்றுகண் டன்ன","உறையுள் முடுக்கர் ஒருதிறம் போகி","வித்தகர் கைவினை விளங்கிய கொள்கைச்","சித்திர விதானத்துச் செம்பொற் பீடிகைக்","கோயி லிருக்கைக் கோமகன் ஏறி","வாயி லாலரின் மாடலற் கூஉய்","இளங்கோ வேந்தர் இறந்ததற் பின்னர்","வளங்கெழு நன்னாட்டு மன்னவன் கொற்றமொடு","செங்கோற் றன்மை தீதின் றோவென"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan ends that the heir of the moon's line has ascended to guard the world age after age, and as the king sits listening, evening falls, the sky reddens and the white crescent shows; the astrologer rises and tells him thirty-two months have passed since he left Vanchi; the king goes back through the streets of the camp to his hall, has Maadalan called by the gatekeepers and asks whether, since the young princes died, the Chola's rule and its sceptre are still sound.","n":"The sun-and-moon image of lines 137 to 138 turns the Pandya succession into a change of lights: the line descended from the moon goes on as the sun goes down. The astrologer's count of thirty-two months reminds the king, and the reader, how long the vow for the stone has kept him from home. Who the young princes (இளங்கோ வேந்தர், line 159) were is not said in the unit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kaalam","vendhan","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 138-161","lb":"SI27:138-161","ev":"At evening the astrologer tells Senguttuvan he has been away thirty-two months, and the king asks Maadalan about the Chola's rule.","cl":[],"ag":["கணி","குடவர் கோ","மாடலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:162-172":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":370,"t":["எங்கோ வேந்தே வாழ்கென் றேத்தி","மங்கல மறையோன் மாடலன் உரைக்கும்","வெயில்விளங்கு மணிப்பூண் விண்ணவர் வியப்ப","எயில்மூன் றெறிந்த இகல்வேற் கொற்றமும்","குறுநடைப் புறவின் நெடுந்துயர் தீர","எறிதரு பருந்தின் இடும்பை நீங்க","அரிந்துடம் பிட்டோன் அறந்தரு கோலும்","திரிந்துவே றாகுங் காலமு முண்டோ","தீதோ இல்லைச் செல்லற் காலையுங்","காவிரி புரக்கும் நாடுகிழ வோற்கென்று","அருமறை முதல்வன் சொல்லக் கேட்டே"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan answers that the victory of the Chola line, who destroyed the three forts the gods marvelled at, and the dharma-giving sceptre of the one who cut his own body to end the little dove's distress and the hawk's hunger, can never turn; there is no fault, even in a time of trouble, with the lord of the land the Kaveri guards.","n":"The assurance rests on lineage: the sceptre cannot bend because of what an ancestor did. The dove story, which Kannaki threw at the Pandya as a reproach, is here a guarantee offered to a Chera about a Chola. The answer is given as a rhetorical question (திரிந்துவேறு ஆகுங் காலமும் உண்டோ), not as a report of how the Chola has ruled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arul","kudi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 162-172","lb":"SI27:162-172","ev":"","cl":[["the just sceptre of a line whose ancestor gave his flesh for a dove cannot bend, even in hard times","general","asserted",169,"maadalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:173-187":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":371,"t":["பெருமகன் மறையோற் பேணி யாங்கவற்கு","ஆடகப் பெருநிறை யையைந் திரட்டித்","தோடார் போந்தை வேலோன் றன்னிறை","மாடல மறையோன் கொள்கென் றளித்தாங்கு","ஆரிய மன்னர் ஐயிரு பதின்மரைச்","சீர்கெழு நன்னாட்டுச் செல்கவென் றேவித்","தாபத வேடத் துயிருய்ந்து பிழைத்த","மாபெருந் தானை மன்ன குமரர்","சுருளிடு தாடி மருள்படு பூங்குழல்","அரிபரந் தொழுகிய செழுங்கயல் நெடுங்கண்","விரிவெண் தோட்டு வெண்ணகைத் துவர்வாய்ச்","சூடக வரிவளை ஆடமைப் பணைத்தோள்","வளரிள வனமுலைத் தளரியல் மின்னிடைப்","பாடகச் சீறடி ஆரியப் பேடியோடு","எஞ்சா மன்னர் இறைமொழி மறுக்கும்"],"p":"","r":"The king honours the Brahmin and gives him gold to fifty times his own weight, sends the hundred Arya kings back toward their lands, and with them the princes of great armies who saved their lives in ascetics' guise, the Arya pedis with curled beards, flower-hair, long eyes, bangles and anklets, and the chamberlains who refuse the commands of the defiant kings.","n":"The gold is measured against the king's own weight (தன்னிறை), the giver's body as the unit of the gift. The princes who escaped in ascetic guise (தாபத வேடம்) are a jibe: the renouncer's garb worn as a disguise to live. The pedis are described with a mix of male and female marks (a beard with a woman's eyes, bangles and breasts); what their role was is not said. Whether all these groups go home with the hundred kings or are sent elsewhere, and how ஐயிரு பதின்மர் and ஆடகப் பெருநிறை ஐயைந்து இரட்டி are to be counted, is not certain; the sentence runs on into the next unit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","andhanar","vesham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 173-187","lb":"SI27:173-187","ev":"Senguttuvan gives Maadalan gold of fifty times his weight and sends the captive Arya kings away.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","மாடலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:188-203":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":372,"t":["கஞ்சுக முதல்வர் ஈரைஞ் ஞூற்றூவர்","அரியிற் போந்தை அருந்தமி ழாற்றல்","தெரியாது மலைந்த கனக விசயரை","இருபெரு வேந்தர்க்குக் காட்டிட ஏவித்","திருந்துதுயில் கொள்ளா அளவை யாங்கணும்","பரம்புநீர்க் கங்கைப் பழனப் பாசடைப்","பயிலிளந் தாமரைப் பல்வண்டு யாழ்செய","வெயிலிளஞ் செல்வன் விரிகதிர் பரப்பிக்","குணதிசைக் குன்றத் துயர்மிசைத் தோன்றக்","குடதிசை யாளுங் கொற்ற வேந்தன்","வடதிசைத் தும்பை வாகையொடு முடித்துத்","தென்றிசைப் பெயர்ந்த வென்றித் தானையொடு","நிதிதுஞ்சு வியன்நகர் நீடுநிலை நிவந்து","கதிர்செல வொழித்த கனக மாளிகை","முத்துநிரைக் கொடித்தொடர் முழுவதும் வளைஇய","சித்திர விதானத்துச் செய்பூங் கைவினை"],"p":"","r":"The king sends a thousand chamberlains to show Kanaka and Vijaya, who fought not knowing Tamil strength, to the two other great kings, and before he has slept, as the young sun rises over the eastern hills and bees hum in the Ganga's lotus fields, the western king, wearing the northern thumbai and vaagai, turns south with his army toward his treasure-filled city.","n":"Showing the captives to the Chola and the Pandya makes the northern victory a message to the other two Tamil crowns; the Tamil strength the northerners did not know (அருந்தமிழ் ஆற்றல், line 189) is the same phrase as the kaathai's opening (line 5), so the campaign is framed as Tamil against Arya. From line 200 the unit has already moved to the queen's palace in Vanchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 188-203","lb":"SI27:188-203","ev":"Senguttuvan sends Kanaka and Vijaya to be shown to the Chola and Pandya kings and sets out south for Vanchi.","cl":[],"ag":["கஞ்சுக முதல்வர்","குடவர் கோ"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:204-224":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":373,"t":["இலங்கொளி மணிநிரை யிடையிடை வகுத்த","விலங்கொளி வயிரமொடு பொலந்தகடு போகிய","மடையமை செறுவின் வான்பொற் கட்டில்","புடைதிரள் தமனியப் பொற்கா லமளிமிசை","இணைபுண ரெகினத் திளமயிர் செறித்த","துணையணைப் பள்ளித் துயிலாற்றுப் படுத்தாங்கு","எறிந்துகளங் கொண்ட இயறேர்க் கொற்றம்","அறிந்துரை பயின்ற ஆயச் செவிலியர்","தோட்டுணை துறந்த துயரீங் கொழிகெனப்","பாட்டொடு தொடுத்துப் பல்யாண்டு வாழ்த்தச்","சிறுகுறுங் கூனுங் குறளுஞ் சென்று","பெறுகநின் செவ்வி பெருமகன் வந்தான்","நறுமலர்க் கூந்தல் நாளணி பெறுகென","அமைவிளை தேறல் மாந்திய கானவன்","கவண்விடு புடையூஉக் காவல் கைவிட","வீங்குபுனம் உணீஇய வேண்டி வந்த","ஓங்கியல் யானை தூங்குதுயி லெய்த","வாகை தும்பை வடதிசைச் சூடிய","வேக யானையின் வழியோ நீங்கெனத்","திறத்திறம் பகர்ந்து சேணோங் கிதணத்துக்","குறத்தியர் பாடிய குறிஞ்சிப் பாணியும்"],"p":"","r":"In Vanchi, the queen lies sleepless on a golden bed of swan-down in her gem-set hall; the nurses who know the king's chariot victories sing that her grief at parting will end and wish her long life; the hunchbacks and dwarfs come to tell her that her lord has come and her hair should take its festival adornment; and the hill women on the watch platform sing their kurinji song, telling the wild elephant that came to eat the millet while the toddy-drunk watchman dropped his sling to leave the path of the king's war elephant.","n":"The return is told through the queen's waiting and the voices that reach her: first those of the palace, then the hill women. The kurinji song turns its own landscape's scene (the elephant raiding the millet) into news of the king, so that each region's song, here and in the next units, bends to the same refrain that he has come. The queen's sleep is ambiguous: துயிலாற்றுப் படுத்து suggests she was laid to sleep, while the nurses sing to end her grief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","isai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 204-224","lb":"SI27:204-224","ev":"The queen's nurses and attendants announce the king's coming, and the hill women sing of it in their kurinji song.","cl":[],"ag":["ஆயச் செவிலியர்","கூனும் குறளும்","குறத்தியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:225-239":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":374,"t":["வடதிசை மன்னர் மன்னெயின் முருக்கிக்","கவடி வித்திய கழுதையே ருழவன்","குடவர் கோமான் வந்தான் நாளைப்","படுநுகம் பூணாய் பகடே மன்னர்","அடித்தளை நீக்கும் வெள்ளணி யாமெனும்","தொடுப்பேர் உழவ ரோதைப் பாணியும்","தண்ணான் பொருநை யாடுந ரிட்ட","வண்ணமுஞ் சுண்ணமும் மலரும் பரந்து","விண்ணுறை விற்போல் விளங்கிய பெருந்துறை","வண்டுண மலர்ந்த மணித்தோட்டுக் குவளை","முண்டகக் கோதையொடு முடித்த குஞ்சியின்","முருகுவிரி தாமரை முழுமலர் தோயக்","குருகலர் தாழைக் கோட்டுமிசை யிருந்து","வில்லவன் வந்தான் வியன்பே ரிமயத்துப்","பல்லான் நிரையொடு படர்குவிர் நீரெனக்"],"p":"","r":"The ploughmen sing their own song, that the lord of the west, the ploughman who yoked donkeys and sowed kavadi on the ruined forts of the northern kings, has come, that tomorrow the oxen will not wear the yoke, and that this is the white festival that frees kings from their fetters; and at the Porunai ford, bright with the colours, powders and flowers the bathers left, the cowherd boys perched on a thazhai branch call that Villavan has come back from the Himalaya and the herds should be taken to the water.","n":"The ploughmen's title for the king, the donkey-plough ploughman of the enemy's forts, is a war rite (ploughing a ruined fort and sowing it with worthless seed) that the field-workers sing as their own; the same song gives their oxen a holiday. The white festival that frees kings' fetters (line 229) seems to be a release of captives at the king's return, but the line is brief and its sense not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","vizhavu","isai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 225-239","lb":"SI27:225-239","ev":"The ploughmen and the cowherds of the Porunai sing that the king has come.","cl":[],"ag":["உழவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:34"},"si:27:240-256":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":375,"t":["காவலன் ஆனிரை நீர்த்துறை படீஇக்","கோவலர் ஊதுங் குழலின் பாணியும்","வெண்டிரை பொருத வேலைவா லுகத்துக்","குண்டுநீ ரடைகரைக் குவையிரும் புன்னை","வலம்புரி யீன்ற நலம்புரி முத்தம்","கழங்காடு மகளி ரோதை யாயத்து","வழங்குதொடி முன்கை மலர ஏந்தி","வானவன் வந்தான் வளரிள வனமுலை","தோள்நலம் உணீஇய தும்பை போந்தையொடு","வஞ்சி பாடுதும் மடவீர் யாமெனும்","அஞ்சொற் கிளவியர் அந்தீம் பாணியும்","ஓர்த்துடன் இருந்த கோப்பெருந் தேவி","வால்வளை செறிய வலம்புரி வலனெழ","மாலைவெண் குடைக்கீழ் வாகைச் சென்னியன்","வேக யானையின் மீமிசைப் பொலிந்து","குஞ்சர ஒழுகையிற் கோநக ரெதிர்கொள","வஞ்சியுட் புகுந்தனன் செங்குட் டுவனென்."],"p":"","r":"The cowherds play their flute as the king's herds bathe at the ford, the girls of the shore lift pearls born of the right-whorled conch in their bangled hands for the kazhangu game and sing that Vanavan has come and they will sing the vanji with thumbai and palmyra; the queen listens to all these songs, and the king, under the white umbrella, crowned with vaagai and high on his swift elephant, is met by the city with a train of elephants and enters Vanchi.","n":"The four songs, of hill, field, pasture and shore, make the four landscapes of the Chera land greet one king, and the queen hears them all (line 251) before she sees him. The kaathai ends with the king's name (செங்குட்டுவன்), as the stone, the bathing and the news of Madurai give way to the triumph.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","vendhan","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 27, lines 240-256","lb":"SI27:240-256","ev":"Senguttuvan, met by the city with elephants, enters Vanchi while the shore girls sing and the queen listens.","cl":[],"ag":["கோவலர்","கழங்காடு மகளிர்","கோப்பெருந்தேவி","குடவர் கோ"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:34"},"si:28:1-14":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":376,"t":["தண்மதி யன்ன தமனிய நெடுங்குடை","மண்ணக நிழற்செய மறவா ளேந்திய","நிலந்தரு திருவின் நெடியோன் றனாது","வலம்படு சிறப்பின் வஞ்சி மூதூர்","ஒண்டொடித் தடக்கையின் ஒண்மலர்ப் பலிதூஉய்","வெண்திரி விளக்கம் ஏந்திய மகளிர்","உலக மன்னவன் வாழ்கென் றேத்திப்","பலர்தொழ வந்த மலரவிழ் மாலை","போந்தைக் கண்ணிப் பொலம்பூந் தெரியல்","வேந்துவினை முடித்த ஏந்துவாள் வலத்தர்","யானை வெண்கோடு அழுத்திய மார்பும்","நீள்வேல் கிழித்த நெடும்புண் ஆகமும்","எய்கணை கிழித்த பகட்டெழில் அகலமும்","வைவாள் கிழித்த மணிப்பூண் மார்பமும்"],"p":"","r":"At evening in the Chera's old city of Vanchi, women holding white-wicked lamps scatter flower offerings and hail the king of the world, and the warriors with palmyra garlands who finished his war come home with chests pressed by elephant tusks and torn by spear, arrow and sword.","n":"The opening sentence does not close in this unit: the list of the warriors' wounds is the object of what the women do with them in the next lines. The wounds are counted by weapon, so the homecoming is told through the body of the fighter, not through the king's victory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","maram","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 1-14","lb":"SI28:1-14","ev":"Evening falls on Vanchi; lamp-bearing women hail the king as the wounded warriors return.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:15-24":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":377,"t":["மைம்மல ருண்கண் மடந்தைய ரடங்காக்","கொம்மை வரிமுலை வெம்மை வேதுறீஇ","அகிலுண விரித்த அம்மென் கூந்தல்","முகில்நுழை மதியத்து முரிகருஞ் சிலைக்கீழ்","மகரக் கொடியோன் மலர்க்கணை துரந்து","சிதரரி பரந்த செழுங்கடைத் தூதும்","மருந்தும் ஆயதிம் மாலையென் றேத்த","இருங்கனித் துவர்வாய் இளநிலா விரிப்பக்","கருங்கயல் பிறழுங் காமர் செவ்வியில்","திருந்தெயி றரும்பிய விருந்தின் மூரலும்"],"p":"","r":"The dark-eyed young women soothe those wounds with the warmth of their breasts, their perfumed hair loosened, their glances shot like Kama's flower arrows, and they praise this evening as both the go-between and the medicine, smiling with fresh smiles.","n":"The evening, elsewhere in the tradition the hour when longing hurts, is here called தூதும் மருந்தும் (lines 20-21): it brings the lovers together and heals what war did. The scene sets up, before the brahmin's sermon, the pleasures of a victorious city that the sermon will call passing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","marundhu","maalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 15-24","lb":"SI28:15-24","ev":"The women of Vanchi tend the returned warriors' wounds with their embrace and praise the evening.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:25-38":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":378,"t":["மாந்தளிர் மேனி மடவோர் தம்மால்","ஏந்துபூண் மார்பின் இளையோர்க் களித்துக்","காசறைத் திலகக் கருங்கறை கிடந்த","மாசில்வாள் முகத்து வண்டொடு சுருண்ட","குழலுங் கோதையுங் கோலமுங் காண்மார்","நிழல்கால் மண்டிலம் தம்மெதிர் நிறுத்தி","வணர்கோட்டுச் சீறியாழ் வாங்குபு தழீஇப்","புணர்புரி நரம்பிற் பொருள்படு பத்தர்க்","குரல்குர லாக வருமுறைப் பாலையில்","துத்தங் குரலாத் தொன்முறை யியற்கையின்","அந்தீங் குறிஞ்சி யகவன் மகளிரின்","மைந்தர்க் கோங்கிய வருவிருந் தயர்ந்து","முடிபுறம் உரிஞ்சுங் கழற்காற் குட்டுவன்","குடிபுறந் தருங்கால் திருமுகம் போல"],"p":"","r":"The mango-leaf-skinned women give their delights to the young men, set mirrors before them to see their hair, garlands and adornment, take up the curved small yaazh and play in the old order of the paalai and kurinji modes like the singing women, and so feast the men as guests, while the narrator begins to liken the coming moon to the face of Kuttuvan.","n":"Lines 33-35 use technical terms of tuning (குரல், துத்தம், பாலை, குறிஞ்சி) whose exact sense belongs to the musical lore of the time. The simile at 37-38, the king's face when he protects his people, runs on into the next unit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inbam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 25-38","lb":"SI28:25-38","ev":"The women adorn themselves before mirrors, play the small yaazh and feast the young men.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:39-54":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":379,"t":["உலகுதொழத் தோன்றிய மலர்கதிர் மதியம்","பலர்புகழ் மூதூர்க்குக் காட்டி நீங்க","மைந்தரும் மகளிரும் வழிமொழி கேட்ப","ஐங்கணை நெடுவேள் அரசுவீற் றிருந்த","வெண்ணிலா முன்றிலும் வீழ்பூஞ் சேக்கையும்","மண்ணீட் டரங்கமும் மலர்ப்பூம் பந்தரும்","வெண்கால் அமளியும் விதானவே திகைகளும்","தண்கதிர் மதியம் தான்கடி கொள்ளப்","படுதிரை சூழ்ந்த பயங்கெழு மாநிலத்","திடைநின் றோங்கிய நெடுநிலை மேருவிற்","கொடிமதின் மூதூர் நடுநின் றோங்கிய","தமனிய மாளிகைப் புனைமணி யரங்கின்","வதுவை வேண்மாள் மங்கல மடந்தை","மதியேர் வண்ணங் காணிய வருவழி","எல்வளை மகளிர் ஏந்திய விளக்கம்","பல்லாண் டேத்தப் பரந்தன வொருசார்"],"p":"","r":"The moon rises, worshipped by the world, showing the famed city a face like Kuttuvan's, and takes possession of the moonlit terraces, flower beds, stages, bowers and couches where Kama reigns; and in the jewelled hall of the golden palace that stands in the middle of the walled city like Meru in the middle of the earth, the queen Venmal comes to see the moon's beauty while bangled women carrying lamps spread on one side singing her many years.","n":"காட்டி நீங்க (line 40) and வழிமொழி கேட்ப (41) are hard to fix: whether something departs as the moon shows itself, and whose words the men and women hear, is not clear to me. The queen is named only as வதுவை வேண்மாள் மங்கல மடந்தை.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 39-54","lb":"SI28:39-54","ev":"Queen Venmal comes to the jewelled hall of the palace to see the moon, with lamp-bearing women singing blessings.","cl":[],"ag":["வேண்மாள்","எல்வளை மகளிர், சேடியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:55-64":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":380,"t":["மண்கணை முழவும் வணர்கோட் டியாழும்","பண்கனி பாடலும் பரந்தன வொருசார்","மான்மதச் சாந்தும் வரிவெண் சாந்தும்","கூனுங் குறளுங் கொண்டன வொருசார்","வண்ணமுஞ் சுண்ணமும் மலர்ப்பூம் பிணையலும்","பெண்ணணிப் பேடியர் ஏந்தின ரொருசார்","பூவும் புகையும் மேவிய விரையும்","தூவியஞ் சேக்கை சூழ்ந்தன வொருசார்","ஆடியும் ஆடையும் அணிதரு கலன்களும்","சேடியர் செவ்வியின் ஏந்தின ரொருசார்"],"p":"","r":"On every side the queen's attendants press round: drums and yaazh and ripe songs on one side, hunchbacks and dwarfs carrying musk and white sandal on another, eunuchs dressed as women carrying colours, powders and garlands on another, flowers, incense and perfume round the couch, and maids carrying mirrors, clothes and jewels.","n":"The retinue repeats almost item by item the Pandya queen's train in the twentieth kaathai (mirror, cloth, colours, musk, hunchbacks); here the same splendour surrounds a queen whose city is at peace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 55-64","lb":"SI28:55-64","ev":"The queen's attendants gather round her with music, scents, garlands, mirrors, clothes and jewels.","cl":[],"ag":["எல்வளை மகளிர், சேடியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:65-83":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":381,"t":["ஆங்கவள் தன்னுடன் அணிமணி யரங்கம்","வீங்குநீர் ஞாலம் ஆள்வோன் ஏறித்","திருநிலைச் சேவடிச் சிலம்புவாய் புலம்பவும்","பரிதரு செங்கையிற் படுபறை யார்ப்பவும்","செங்கண் ஆயிரம் திருக்குறிப் பருளவும்","செஞ்சடை சென்று திசைமுகம் அலம்பவும்","பாடகம் பதையாது சூடகந் துளங்காது","மேகலை ஒலியாது மென்முலை அசையாது","வார்குழை ஆடாது மணிக்குழல் அவிழாது","உமையவள் ஒருதிற னாக ஓங்கிய","இமையவன் ஆடிய கொட்டிச் சேதம்","பாத்தரு நால்வகை மறையோர் பறையூர்க்","கூத்தச் சாக்கைய னாடலின் மகிழ்ந்தவன்","ஏத்தி நீங்க இருநிலம் ஆள்வோன்","வேத்தியன் மண்டபம் மேவிய பின்னர்","நீலன் முதலிய கஞ்சுக மாக்கள்","மாடல மறையோன் தன்னொடுந் தோன்றி","வாயி லாளரின் மன்னவற் கிசைத்தபின்","கோயில் மாக்களிற் கொற்றவற் றொழுது"],"p":"","r":"The king who rules the earth ascends the jewelled stage with the queen and watches the koṭṭicētam of the god whose one side is Uma, danced by the Chakkaiyan of Paraiyur of the four-Veda brahmins, with the anklet sounding, the drum beating, the thousand eyes giving their signs and the red matted hair sweeping the quarters on one half while on the other half nothing stirs; pleased, he praises the dancer, goes to the royal hall, and Neelan and the chamberlains come with the brahmin Maadalan, announce themselves through the gatekeepers and bow.","n":"The dance shows the god as half woman: the lines on what moves (anklet, drum, matted hair) and what does not (bangle, girdle, breast, earring, hair knot) divide the one body between Siva's fury and Uma's stillness. The Chakkaiyan acts the god's dance; the god himself is not on the stage. A Saiva performance sits in a Chera court that also hears a Vedic sermon and will found a pathini's temple.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thirukkoothu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 65-83","lb":"SI28:65-83","ev":"The king and queen watch the Chakkaiyan dance Siva's koṭṭicētam; then Neelan and the chamberlains come with Maadalan to the royal hall.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","பறையூர்க் கூத்தச் சாக்கையன்","நீலன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:84-95":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":382,"t":["தும்பை வெம்போர்ச் சூழ்கழல் வேந்தே","செம்பியன் மூதூர்ச் சென்றுபுக் காங்கு","வச்சிர மவந்தி மகதமொடு குழீஇய","சித்திர மண்டபத் திருக்க வேந்தன்","அமரகத் துடைந்த ஆரிய மன்னரொடு","தமரிற் சென்று தகையடி வணங்க","நீளம ரழுவத்து நெடும்பே ராண்மையொடு","வாளுங் குடையும் மறக்களத் தொழித்துக்","கொல்லாக் கோலத் துயிருய்ந் தோரை","வெல்போர்க் கோடல் வெற்றம் அன்றெனத்","தலைத்தேர்த் தானைத் தலைவற் குரைத்தனன்","சிலைத்தார் அகலத்துச் செம்பியர் பெருந்தகை"],"p":"","r":"Neelan reports that when they reached the Chola's old city and went with the Aryan kings beaten in battle to bow before the Chola, seated in the painted hall gathered from Vajra, Avanti and Magadha, the Chola told his army chief that to take in a winning war those who had flung away sword and umbrella on the field and saved their lives in the guise of men who do not kill is no victory.","n":"கொல்லாக் கோலம், the guise of those who do not kill, points back to the end of the Kaal Kol kaathai, where the beaten kings escaped dressed as matted, ash-smeared ascetics, singers and dancers. The Chola's words are a judgment on the Chera's triumph made in the Chera's own envoy's hearing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","kollaamai","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 84-95","lb":"SI28:84-95","ev":"Neelan reports that the Chola, shown the captive Aryan kings, said it was no victory to take men who had saved their lives in ascetics' guise.","cl":[["to take captive in war those who fled the field in the guise of non-killers is no victory","general","reported",93,"cholan"]],"ag":["நீலன்","செம்பியர் பெருந்தகை"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:96-109":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":383,"t":["ஆங்குநின் றகன்றபின் அறக்கோல் வேந்தே","ஓங்குசீர் மதுரை மன்னவற் காண","ஆரிய மன்னர் அமர்க்களத் தெடுத்த","சீரியல் வெண்குடைக் காம்புநனி சிறந்த","சயந்தன் வடிவில் தலைக்கோ லாங்குக்","கயந்தலை யானையிற் கவிகையிற் காட்டி","இமயச் சிமையத் திருங்குயி லாலுவத்","துமையொரு பாகத் தொருவனை வணங்கி","அமர்க்களம் அரசன தாகத் துறந்து","தவப்பெருங் கோலங் கொண்டோர் தம்மேல்","கொதியழற் சீற்றங் கொண்டோன் கொற்றம்","புதுவ தென்றனன் போர்வேற் செழியனென்","றேனை மன்னர் இருவருங் கூறிய","நீண்மொழி யெல்லாம் நீலன் கூறத்"],"p":"","r":"Neelan goes on: when, after the Chola left, they showed the Aryan kings on elephants under the umbrellas taken from them to the Madurai king, the Pandya Cheliyan said that the conquest of a man who turned his burning anger on those who, having bowed to the god whose half is Uma on the Himalaya's peak, gave up the field to the king and took the great guise of penance, was a new kind of victory; and Neelan tells all these long words of the two kings.","n":"புதுவது, 'new', is ironic: a victory over men who had already renounced is no victory. The Pandya calls the Chera கொதியழற் சீற்றம் கொண்டோன் (106), the burning-fire anger the patikam and this kaathai's line 219 give to Kannaki's breast. The Pandya here is the new king at Madurai after the fire, not the Nedunjeliyan who died.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","thavam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 96-109","lb":"SI28:96-109","ev":"Neelan reports the Pandya's taunt that defeating kings who had put on ascetics' guise was a strange victory.","cl":[["burning anger turned on those who have taken the guise of penance wins no honour","general","reported",105,"vetrivel_cheliyan"]],"ag":["நீலன்","வெற்றிவேற் செழியன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["நீலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:110-122":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":384,"t":["தாமரைச் செங்கண் தழனிறங் கொள்ளக்","கோமகன் நகுதலும் குறையாக் கேள்வி","மாடலன் எழுந்து மன்னவர் மன்னே","வாழ்கநின் கொற்றம் வாழ்கவென் றேத்திக்","கறிவளர் சிலம்பிற் றுஞ்சும் யானையின்","சிறுகுரல் நெய்தல் வியுலூ ரெறிந்தபின்","ஆர்புனை தெரியல் ஒன்பது மன்னரை","நேரி வாயில் நிலைச்செரு வென்று","நெடுந்தேர்த் தானையொ டிடும்பிற்புறத் திறுத்துக்","கொடும்போர் கடந்து நெடுங்கட லோட்டி","உடன்றுமேல் வந்த ஆரிய மன்னரைக்","கடும்புனற் கங்கைப் பேர்யாற்று வென்றோய்","நெடுந்தார் வேய்ந்த பெரும்படை வேந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Hearing it, the king's lotus eyes turn the colour of fire and he laughs; Maadalan of unfailing learning rises, hails him as king of kings, and recounts his victories: Viyalur of the small-stemmed neythal destroyed, nine kings beaten in the standing battle at the Neri gate, the camp outside Idumbil, the sea driven back, and the Aryan kings who came against him beaten at the Ganges.","n":"The laugh is anger, not amusement, as the next unit's 'let your anger subside' shows. Maadalan begins with praise before he counsels; the list of victories is close in wording to the preface of the fifth decad of the Patitruppattu.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","vekuli","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 110-122","lb":"SI28:110-122","ev":"The king's eyes redden and he laughs in anger; Maadalan rises and recounts his victories.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","மாடலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:123-138":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":385,"t":["புரையோர் தம்மொடு பொருந்த வுணர்ந்த","அரைச ரேறே யமைகநின் சீற்றம்","மண்ணாள் வேந்தே நின்வா ணாட்கள்","தண்ணான் பெருநை மணலினுஞ் சிறக்க","அகழ்கடல் ஞாலம் ஆள்வோய் வாழி","இகழா தென்சொற் கேட்டல் வேண்டும்","வையங் காவல் பூண்டநின் நல்யாண்டு","ஐயைந் திரட்டிச் சென்றதற் பின்னும்","அறக்கள வேள்வி செய்யா தியாங்கணும்","மறக்கள வேள்வி செய்வோ யாயினை","வேந்துவினை முடித்த ஏந்துவாள் வலத்துப்","போந்தைக் கண்ணிநின் னூங்கணோர் மருங்கில்","கடற்கடம் பெறிந்த காவல னாயினும்","விடர்ச்சிலை பொறித்த விறலோ னாயினும்","நான்மறை யாளன் செய்யுட் கொண்டு","மேல்நிலை யுலகம் விடுத்தோ னாயினும்"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan asks the king, a bull among kings who understands with the wise, to let his anger rest, wishes him days more than the sands of the Porunai, and asks him to hear without scorn: after fifty years of guarding the world he has nowhere performed the sacrifice of the field of dharma, only the sacrifice of the field of war; and he begins to list the king's ancestors, the one who cut the kadamba in the sea, the one who carved the bow on the mountain, the one who took a four-Veda brahmin's poem and sent him to the upper world.","n":"அறக்கள வேள்வி against மறக்கள வேள்வி (131-132) turns the bards' old figure of battle as a sacrifice against the king: his wars were a sacrifice of the wrong kind. The fifty years, ஐயைந்து இரட்டி, use the same count as the gold Maadalan received in the last kaathai. The blessing by the sands of a river is an old formula (Purananuru 9 ends with the sands of the Pahruli).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velvi","aram","maram","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 123-138","lb":"SI28:123-138","ev":"Maadalan bids the king calm his anger and tells him he has performed only the sacrifice of war, never the sacrifice of dharma.","cl":[["a king who has made only the sacrifice of the battlefield, never the sacrifice of dharma, has left his duty undone","vedic","asserted",131,"maadalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:139-154":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":386,"t":["போற்றி மன்னுயிர் முறையிற் கொள்கெனக்","கூற்றுவரை நிறுத்த கொற்றவ னாயினும்","வன்சொல் யவனர் வளநா டாண்டு","பொன்படு நெடுவரை புகுந்தோ னாயினும்","மிகப்பெருந் தானையோடு இருஞ்செரு வோட்டி","அகப்பா எறிந்த அருந்திற லாயினும்","உருகெழு மரபின் அயிரை மண்ணி","இருகடல் நீரும் ஆடினோ னாயினும்","சதுக்கப் பூதரை வஞ்சியுள் தந்து","மதுக்கொள் வேள்வி வேட்டோ னாயினும்","மீக்கூற் றாளர் யாவரும் இன்மையின்","யாக்கை நில்லா தென்பதை யுணர்ந்தோய்","மல்லன்மா ஞாலத்து வாழ்வோர் மருங்கில்","செல்வம் நில்லா தென்பதை வெல்போர்த்","தண்டமிழ் இகழ்ந்த ஆரிய மன்னரின்","கண்டனை யல்லையோ காவல் வேந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan finishes the list, the one who held back Death bidding it take lives only in order, the one who ruled the land of the harsh-tongued Yavanas and entered the golden mountain, the one who destroyed Agappa, the one who bathed in Ayirai and the two seas, the one who brought the crossroads spirits into Vanchi and offered the honey sacrifice, and says that since none of these exalted ones remains the king knows the body does not last, and that he has seen from the Aryan kings who scorned Tamil that wealth does not last.","n":"The ancestors are named by feats, not names; several of the feats (Agappa, Ayirai, the two seas, the bow on the Himalaya, the kadamba) are those of the Patitruppattu's prefaces. The proof that wealth passes is the king's own victory over the Aryans: his triumph is turned into a lesson against triumph.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 139-154","lb":"SI28:139-154","ev":"","cl":[["the body does not last, as no great king of the past remains","general","asserted",150,"maadalan"],["wealth does not last, as the fall of the conquered kings shows","general","asserted",152,"maadalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:155-170":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":387,"t":["இளமை நில்லா தென்பதை எடுத்தீங்கு","உணர்வுடை மாக்கள் உரைக்க வேண்டா","திருஞெமிர் அகலத்துச் செங்கோல் வேந்தே","நரைமுதிர் யாக்கை நீயுங் கண்டனை","விண்ணோர் உருவின் எய்திய நல்லுயிர்","மண்ணோர் உருவின் மறிக்கினும் மறிக்கும்","மக்கள் யாக்கை பூண்ட மன்னுயிர்","மிக்கோய் விலங்கின் எய்தினும் எய்தும்","விலங்கின் யாக்கை விலங்கிய இன்னுயிர்","கலங்கஞர் நரகரைக் காணினுங் காணும்","ஆடுங் கூத்தர்போல் ஆருயிர் ஒருவழிக்","கூடிய கோலத் தொருங்குநின் றியலாது","செய்வினை வழித்தாய் உயிர்செலு மென்பது","பொய்யில் காட்சியோர் பொருளுரை யாதலின்","எழுமுடி மார்பநீ யேந்திய திகிரி","வழிவழிச் சிறக்க வயவாள் வேந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan adds that the wise need not tell the king youth does not last, since he sees his own greying body, and that, as those of unfailing sight teach, a good soul that came in a god's form may turn into a human one, a soul wearing a human body may enter a beast's, and a soul in a beast's body may come among the hell-dwellers, for like dancers the soul does not stay in one costume but goes by the way of the deeds it did; and he blesses the king's wheel of rule.","n":"The four states, gods, humans, beasts and hell-beings, are the scheme of the Jain gatis, but the text names no school: a brahmin cites them as the word of பொய்யில் காட்சியோர். The introducing phrase பொய்யில் ... பொருளுரை is the formula the brief cites from Manimekalai 22 for a Kural quotation, but what follows here is not a Kural couplet. கோலம், costume, is the word the kaathai used of the Aryan kings' disguise (92, 105).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","ilamai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 155-170","lb":"SI28:155-170","ev":"","cl":[["youth does not last","general","asserted",155,"maadalan"],["the soul passes between the bodies of gods, humans, beasts and hell-beings, going by the deeds it did, as a dancer changes costume","general","asserted",167,"maadalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:171-180":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":388,"t":["அரும்பொருட் பரிசிலன் அல்லேன் யானும்","பெரும்பேர் யாக்கை பெற்ற நல்லுயிர்","மலர்தலை யுலகத் துயிர்போகு பொதுநெறி","புலவரை யிறந்தோய் போகுதல் பொறேஎன்","வானவர் போற்றும் வழிநினக் களிக்கும்","நான்மறை மருங்கின் வேள்விப் பார்ப்பான்","அருமறை மருங்கின் அரசர்க் கோங்கிய","பெருநல் வேள்வி நீசெயல் வேண்டும்","நாளைச் செய்குவம் அறமெனில் இன்றே","கேள்வி நல்லுயிர் நீங்கினு நீங்கும்"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan says he is not one who seeks rich gifts, but cannot bear that the king, beyond the poets' measure, should go the common way by which lives leave this world; the brahmin of sacrifice learned in the four Vedas will open to him the way the gods praise, and he must perform the great sacrifice that the Veda sets for kings, for if he says he will do dharma tomorrow, a life rich in learning may leave today.","n":"Maadalan disclaims the gift-seeker's motive just after (in the last kaathai) the king gave him gold by weight. The 'dharma' that must not be deferred is here a Vedic royal sacrifice performed by priests, not the giving or conduct the ethical books usually mean.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velvi","aram","nilaiyaamai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 171-180","lb":"SI28:171-180","ev":"Maadalan urges the king to perform the great Vedic sacrifice without delay.","cl":[["a king should perform the great Vedic sacrifice, which opens the way the gods praise","vedic","asserted",178,"maadalan"],["dharma must not be put off to tomorrow, for life may leave today","general","asserted",179,"maadalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:181-186":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":389,"t":["இதுவென வரைந்து வாழுநா ளுணர்ந்தோர்","முதுநீர் உலகில் முழுவது மில்லை","வேள்விக் கிழத்தி யிவளொடுங் கூடித்","தாழ்கழல் மன்னர் நின்னடி போற்ற","ஊழியோ டூழி யுலகங் காத்து","நீடுவா ழியரோ நெடுந்தகை யென்று"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan ends: no one in the old world has known the fixed span of his days, so may the king, with this lady of the sacrifice, guard the world aeon upon aeon while crowned kings praise his feet, and live long.","n":"The sermon on impermanence closes, as court speech must, with a wish for a reign of aeons; the queen is called வேள்விக் கிழத்தி because a king performs the sacrifice with his wife.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aayul","velvi","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 181-186","lb":"SI28:181-186","ev":"Maadalan closes his counsel with a blessing on the king and queen.","cl":[["no one knows the fixed span of his life","general","asserted",181,"maadalan"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:187-206":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":390,"t":["மறையோன் மறைநா வுழுது வான்பொருள்","இறையோன் செவிசெறு வாக வித்தலின்","வித்திய பெரும்பதம் விளைந்துபத மிகுத்துத்","துய்த்தல் வேட்கையிற் சூழ்கழல் வேந்தன்","நான்மறை மரபின் நயந்தெரி நாவின்","கேள்வி முடித்த வேள்வி மாக்களை","மாடல மறையோன் சொல்லிய முறைமையின்","வேள்விச் சாந்தியின் விழாக்கொள ஏவி","ஆரிய அரசரை அருஞ்சிறை நீக்கிப்","பேரிசை வஞ்சி மூதூர்ப் புறத்துத்","தாழ்நீர் வேலித் தண்மலர்ப் பூம்பொழில்","வேளா விக்கோ மாளிகை காட்டி","நன்பெரு வேள்வி முடித்ததற் பின்னாள்","தம்பெரு நெடுநகர்ச் சார்வதுஞ் சொல்லியம்","மன்னவர்க் கேற்பன செய்க நீயென","வில்லவன் கோதையை விருப்புடன் ஏவிச்","சிறையோர் கோட்டஞ் சீமின் யாங்கணும்","கறைகெழு நல்லூர்க் கறைவீடு செய்ம்மென","அழும்பில் வேளோடு ஆயக் கணக்கரை","முழங்குநீர் வேலி மூதூர் ஏவி"],"p":"","r":"The brahmin having ploughed with his Veda tongue and sown its meaning in the field of the king's ear, the harvest ripens: the king, wishing to enjoy it, orders the Veda-learned priests to hold the sacrifice and its festival as Maadalan prescribed, frees the Aryan kings from prison, lodges them in the Velavikko mansion in the park outside Vanchi, and tells Villavan Kothai to send them home after the sacrifice and do for them what befits kings, orders the prisons opened everywhere and the towns' taxes remitted, and sends Azhumbil Vel with the revenue accountants through the city.","n":"The narrator's ploughing figure (187-189) endorses the sermon: it bears fruit at once in acts of clemency. The release of prisoners and remission of dues are the usual festival acts of a king, here prompted by a lesson on impermanence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velvi","arasu","sengol","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 187-206","lb":"SI28:187-206","ev":"The king orders the sacrifice, frees the Aryan kings and all prisoners, and remits the towns' taxes.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","வில்லவன் கோதை","அழும்பில்வேள்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:207-221":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":391,"t":["அருந்திற லரசர் முறைசெயி னல்லது","பெரும்பெயர்ப் பெண்டிர்க்குக் கற்புச் சிறவாதெனப்","பண்டையோர் உரைத்த தண்டமிழ் நல்லுரை","பார்தொழு தேத்தும் பத்தினி யாகலின்","ஆர்புனை சென்னி யரசற் களித்துச்","செங்கோல் வளைய வுயிர்வா ழாமை","தென்புலங் காவல் மன்னவற் களித்து","வஞ்சினம் வாய்த்தபி னல்லதை யாவதும்","வெஞ்சினம் விளியார் வேந்த ரென்பதை","வடதிசை மருங்கின் மன்னவ ரறியக்","குடதிசை வாழுங் கொற்றவற் களித்து","மதுரை மூதூர் மாநகர் கேடுறக்","கொதியழற் சீற்றம் கொங்கையின் விளைத்து","நன்னா டணைந்து நளிர்சினை வேங்கைப்","பொன்னணி புதுநிழல் பொருந்திய நங்கையை"],"p":"","r":"The narrator recalls the pathini whom the world worships: she gave the garlanded Chola the proof of the ancients' Tamil saying that unless kings rule justly the chastity of famous women does not shine, gave the southern king the lesson that a king does not live once his sceptre bends, gave the western king the lesson, made known to the northern kings, that kings' fierce anger does not cool until their vow is fulfilled, and, having ruined old Madurai with the burning anger she brought forth from her breast, came to the good land and stood in the shade of the flowering vengai.","n":"The three lessons go one to each crowned king and do not match the patikam's three morals: karma is absent, and the third is about the Chera's own vow. What 'gave to the Chola' means (that her chastity's glory waited on the Pandya's justice being tested, or that the Chola learned it from her story) is not plain to me, and I mark the reading uncertain. The phrase கொதியழற் சீற்றம் கொங்கையின் விளைத்து repeats the patikam (line 44).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathini","karpu","sengol","vanjinam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 207-221","lb":"SI28:207-221","ev":"","cl":[["unless kings do justice, the chastity of renowned women does not shine","general","asserted",207,"narrator"],["a king does not live on once his sceptre bends","general","asserted",212,"narrator"],["kings' fierce anger does not cool until their vow is fulfilled","general","asserted",215,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:35"},"si:28:222-234":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":392,"t":["அறக்களத் தந்தணர் ஆசான் பெருங்கணி","சிறப்புடைக் கம்மியர் தம்மொடுஞ் சென்று","மேலோர் விழையும் நூனெறி மாக்கள்","பால்பெற வகுத்த பத்தினிக் கோட்டத்து","இமையவர் உறையும் இமையச் செவ்வரைச்","சிமையச் சென்னித் தெய்வம் பரசிக்","கைவினை முற்றிய தெய்வப் படிமத்து","வித்தகர் இயற்றிய விளங்கிய கோலத்து","முற்றிழை நன்கலம் முழுவதும் பூட்டிப்","பூப்பலி செய்து காப்பக்கடை நிறுத்தி","வேள்வியும் விழாவும் நாடொறும் வகுத்துக்","கடவுள் மங்கலம் செய்கென ஏவினன்","வடதிசை வணக்கிய மன்னவ ரேறென்."],"p":"","r":"The king who bent the north goes with the brahmins of the field of dharma, the teacher, the great astrologer and the skilled artisans, worships the god of the Himalaya's peak, and in the pathini's temple laid out by men of the texts has the divine image carved by masters adorned with every jewel, offers flowers, sets guards at the door, ordains sacrifice and festival every day, and commands that the goddess's auspicious rite be done.","n":"The kaathai is named நடுகல், the hero-stone; the stone fetched from the Himalaya becomes a jewelled image in a temple with Brahmin priests and daily sacrifice, so the old warrior's stone is turned into a goddess's shrine. This fulfils the patikam's உரைசால் பத்தினிக்கு உயர்ந்தோர் ஏத்தல்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","vazhipaadu","nadukal","velvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 28, lines 222-234","lb":"SI28:222-234","ev":"The king installs the jewelled image of the pathini in her temple and ordains daily sacrifice, festival and worship.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","அறக்களத் தந்தணர்","பெருங்கணி","சிறப்புடைக் கம்மியர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:35"},"si:29:1-20":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":393,"t":["குமரியொடு வடவிமயத் தொருமொழிவைத் துலகாண்ட","சேரலாதற்குத் திகழொளி ஞாயிற்றுச் சோழன்மகளீன்ற","மைந்தன் கொங்கர் செங்களம் வேட்டுக் கங்கைப் பேர்","யாற்றுக் கரைபோகிய செங்குட்டுவன் சினஞ் செருக்கி","வஞ்சியுள் வந்திருந்த காலை, வட ஆரிய மன்னர் ஆங்","கோர் மடவரலை மாலைசூட்டி உடனுறைந்த இருக்கை","தன்னில் ஒன்றுமொழி நகையினராய்த் தென்றமிழ் நாடா","ளும் வேந்தர் செருவேட்டுப் புகன்றெழுந்து மின்றவழும்","இமய நெற்றியில் விளங்கு வில் புலி கயல் பொறித்த","நாள் எம்போலும் முடிமன்னர் ஈங்கில்லை போலும் என்ற","வார்த்தை அங்குவாழும் மாதவர் வந்தறிவுறுத்தவிடத்","தாங்கண் உருள்கின்ற மணிவட்டைக் குணில்கொண்டு","துரந்ததுபோல் இமயமால்வரைக் கற்கடவுளாமென்ற","வார்த்தை இடந்துரப்ப, ஆரியநாட்டரசோட்டி அவர்","முடித்தலை அணங்காகிய பேரிமயக் கல்சுமத்திப்","பெயர்ந்து போந்து நயந்த கொள்கையிற் கங்கைப்பேர்","யாற்றிருந்து நங்கைதன்னை நீர்ப்படுத்தி வெஞ்சினந்தரு","வெம்மை நீங்கி வஞ்சிமா நகர்புகுந்து நிலவரசர் நீண்","முடியாற் பலர்தொழு படிமங் காட்டித் தடமுலைப் பூச","லாட்டியைக் கடவுண் மங்கலஞ்செய்தபின்னாள் கண்ணகி"],"p":"","r":"The prose opening tells how Senguttuvan, son of the world-ruling Cheralathan by the Chola's daughter, after the Kongu war and the Ganga campaign, heard from the northern ascetics that the Aryan kings had laughed that no crowned kings like themselves were left in the Tamil south, as on the day bow, tiger and fish were cut on the Himalaya's brow; stung, he routed them, made them carry the Himalaya stone on their crowned heads, bathed the lady in the Ganga, came back to Vanchi with his rage spent, and consecrated the image, worshipped by many kings' crowns, of the pathini as a goddess.","n":"The cause given is not devotion but a slight to Tamil kingship: the taunt touches the Chera's pride, and the stone for the goddess is quarried by humbling the kings who made it, so worship and conquest are one act. The ascetics are the ones who carry the word that starts a war. The stone is first called a stone that is to be a god (கற்கடவுள், line 13) and only after the Ganga bathing and consecration a படிமம் and a goddess; the poem marks the stages by which a stone becomes a deity. The three emblems carved on the Himalaya belong to the three Tamil crowns, not the Chera alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","vendhan","pugazh","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 1-20","lb":"SI29:1-20","ev":"Senguttuvan, told by northern ascetics of the Aryan kings' taunt, defeats them, makes them carry the Himalaya stone, bathes it in the Ganga and consecrates it at Vanchi as the pathini's image.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","ஆரிய மன்னர்","மாதவர்"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:21-36":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":394,"t":["தன் கோட்டத்து மண்ணரசர் திறைகேட்புழி, அலம்வந்த","மதிமுகத்திற் சில செங்கயல் நீர் உமிழப் பொடியாடிய","கருமுகில்தன் புறம்புதைப்ப அறம்பழித்துக் கோவலன்றன்","வினையுருத்துக் குறுமகனாற் கொலையுன்ன காவலன்","றன் இடஞ்சென்ற கண்ணகிதன் கண்ணீர்கண்டு மன்","னரசர் பெருந்தோன்றல் உண்ணீரற் றுயிரிழந்தமை","மாமறையோன் வாய்கேட்டு மாசாத்துவான் தான்றுறப்","பவும் மனைக்கிழத்தி உயிரிழப்பவும் எனைப் பெருந்","துன்பமெய்திக் காவற்பெண்டும் அடித்தோழியும் கடவுட்","சாத்தனுடன் உறைந்த தேவந்தியும் உடன்கூடிச் சேயி","ழையைக் காண்டுமென்று மதுரைமாநகர் புகுந்து முதிரா","முலைப் பூசல்கேட்டு ஆங்கடைக்கலமிழந் துயிரிழந்த","இடைக்குல மகளிடமெய்தி ஐயையவள் மகளோடும்","வையையொரு வழிக்கொண்டு மாமலை மீமிசையேறிக்","கோமகடன் கோயில்புக்கு நங்கைக்குச் சிறப்பயர்ந்த","செங்குட்டுவற்குத் திறமுரைப்பர் மன்;"],"p":"","r":"On a later day, as Senguttuvan was receiving the tribute of earthly kings at Kannaki's shrine, the women of Puhar came: the prose recalls how Kannaki, her moon face streaming with tears and her dark hair loose with dust, reviled dharma when Kovalan's deed rose up and the petty goldsmith had him killed, went to the king, and how the king lost his life at her tears; how Maasaathuvan, told by a Brahmin, renounced and his wife died; and how the foster mother, the maid and Devanthi went to Madurai to see Kannaki, met Maadhari's daughter Aiyai, and with her followed the Vaigai up the great hill to the queen's shrine, where they tell the king what happened.","n":"In one breath the prose names three causes of Kovalan's death: his own former deed rising up (வினையுருத்து), the goldsmith who brought it about (குறுமகனால்), and the king who caused the killing. Kannaki's response is to blame dharma itself (அறம்பழித்து), a protest the poem records without answering here. Maadhari is not named; she is the herding woman who died having lost the charge entrusted to her (அடைக்கலமிழந்து உயிரிழந்த, line 32). The shrine on the hill is reached by following the Vaigai, which ties the Vanchi cult back to Madurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kodungol","thuravu","thirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 21-36","lb":"SI29:21-36","ev":"As Senguttuvan takes tribute at Kannaki's shrine, Kannaki's foster mother, maid and Devanthi, who went to Madurai and came on with Aiyai, arrive and tell him what befell.","cl":[["a man's former deed rose up against him and brought his killing through a petty man","general","asserted",24,"narrator"]],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","கண்ணகி","பொன்செய் கொல்லன்","நெடுஞ்செழியன்","மாசாத்துவான்","பெருமனைக் கிழத்தி","காவற்பெண்டு","அடித்தோழி","தேவந்தி","ஐயை"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:37-41":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":395,"t":["முடிமன்னர் மூவருங் காத்தோம்புந் தெய்வ","வடபே ரிமய மலையிற் பிறந்து","கடுவரற் கங்கைப் புனலாடிப் போந்த","தொடிவளைத் தோளிக்குத் தோழிநான் கண்டீர்","சோணாட்டார் பாவைக்குத் தோழிநான் கண்டீர்;"],"p":"","r":"Devanthi tells the king to see in her the friend of the bangled one who was born on the divine northern Himalaya, guarded by the three crowned kings, and came after bathing in the swift Ganga: the friend of the doll of the Chola country.","n":"The words describe the stone as much as the woman: born in the Himalaya and bathed in the Ganga is the history of the image told in lines 1 to 20, and Devanthi speaks of it as Kannaki herself, so the image and the woman are one in her grief. The refrain names Kannaki by her home country, the Chola land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","natpu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 37-41","lb":"SI29:37-41","ev":"Devanthi, before the image, declares herself the friend of Kannaki.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவந்தி","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:42-46":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":396,"t":["மடம்படு சாயலாள் மாதவி தன்னைக்","கடம்படாள் காதற் கணவன் கைப் பற்றிக்","குடம்புகாக் கூவற் கொடுங்கானம் போந்த","தடம்பெருங் கண்ணிக்குத் தாயர்நான் கண்டீர்","தண்புகார்ப் பாவைக்குத் தாயர்நான் கண்டீர்;"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother says she is the mother of the wide-eyed girl who, taking her loving husband's hand away from soft-mannered Madhavi without reproach, came through the cruel forest of wells no pot can reach: the mother of the doll of cool Puhar.","n":"கடம்படாள் is hard: it may mean that Kannaki did not hold Madhavi to account, or that she owed no duty to her; either way the line sets her taking back her husband apart from any quarrel with the dancer. தாயர் is the foster mother's own title, not birth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 42-46","lb":"SI29:42-46","ev":"The foster mother declares herself Kannaki's mother.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காவற்பெண்டு","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:47-52":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":397,"t":["அடித் தோழி சொல்","தற்பயந்தாட் கில்லை தன்னைப் புறங்காத்த","எற்பயந் தாட்கும் எனக்குமோர் சொல்லில்லை","கற்புக் கடம்பூண்டு காதலன் பின்போந்த","பொற்றொடி நங்கைக்குத் தோழிநான் கண்டீர்","பூம்புகார்ப் பாவைக்குத் தோழிநான் கண்டீர்;"],"p":"","r":"Under the rubric 'the maid's word', the maid says that the gold-bangled lady who took chastity as her duty and followed her lover left without a word to the mother who bore her, to the foster mother who guarded her, or to the maid herself: she is that lady's friend, the friend of the doll of flowering Puhar.","n":"The grievance is that Kannaki left in secret; the same deed that the poem praises as chastity (கற்புக் கடம்பூண்டு) cost those at home a farewell. The phrase கற்புக் கடம்பூண்ட is the one Kavunthi used of Kannaki when she gave her into Maadhari's care (15:131-148, line 143).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","pathini","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 47-52","lb":"SI29:47-52","ev":"Kannaki's maid declares herself her friend and complains she left without a word.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடித்தோழி","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:53-57":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":398,"t":["செய்தவ மில்லாதேன் தீக்கனாக் கேட்டநாள்","எய்த வுணரா திருந்தேன்மற் றென்செய்தேன்","மொய்குழன் மங்கை முலைப்பூசல் கேட்டநாள்","அவ்வை யுயிர்வீவுங் கேட்டாயோ தோழீ","அம்மாமி தன்வீவுங் கேட்டாயோ தோழீ;"],"p":"","r":"Devanthi laments that she, who had done no penance, heard the ill dream and did not grasp what it would bring, and asks her friend whether she has heard that on the day of the outcry of her breast her mother died, and her mother-in-law too.","n":"Devanthi blames her own lack of penance (செய்தவம் இல்லாதேன்) for failing to read the dream; in the dream kaathai she had read it as the fruit of a vow broken in a former birth and prescribed a bath in the tanks, and now she owns that she did not see what it portended. முலைப்பூசல் names the burning of Madurai by the breast. The friend she addresses is Kannaki, now the image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaiyarunilai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 53-57","lb":"SI29:53-57","ev":"Devanthi tells Kannaki that her mother and her mother-in-law died on hearing of the burning.","cl":[["one who lacks the merit of penance cannot grasp what a dream foretells","general","reported",53,"devanthi"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவந்தி","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:58-63":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":399,"t":["காவற்பெண் டரற்று","கோவலன் றன்னைக் குறுமகன் கோளிழைப்பக்","காவலன் றன்னுயிர் நீத்ததுதான் கேட்டேங்கிச்","சாவதுதான் வாழ்வென்று தானம் பலசெய்து","மாசாத்து வான்துறவுங் கேட்டாயோ அன்னை","மாநாய்கன் றன்றுறவுங் கேட்டாயோ அன்னை;"],"p":"","r":"Under the rubric 'the foster mother's lament', she asks whether Kannaki has heard that Maasaathuvan, grieving that the king gave up his life when the petty man brought about Kovalan's death, held that death itself was now his life, made many gifts and renounced, and that Maanaaygan renounced too.","n":"The renunciation is grief's, not doctrine's: the father turns away because life after the news is no life (சாவதுதான் வாழ்வு), and the gifts come as part of leaving the world. The cause of Kovalan's death is put on the petty man, and the king's death is what the father heard of.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","eegai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 58-63","lb":"SI29:58-63","ev":"The foster mother tells Kannaki that both fathers, Maasaathuvan and Maanaaygan, gave gifts and renounced.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["காவற்பெண்டு","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:64-68":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":400,"t":["காதலன் றன்வீவுங் காதலிநீ பட்டதூஉம்","ஏதிலார் தாங்கூறும் ஏச்சுரையுங் கேட்டேங்கிப்","போதியின்கீழ் மாதவர்முன் புண்ணியதா னம்புரிந்த","மாதவி தன்றுறவுங் கேட்டாயோ தோழீ","மணிமே கலைதுறவுங் கேட்டாயோ தோழீ;"],"p":"","r":"The maid asks whether Kannaki has heard that Madhavi, grieving at her lover's death and at Kannaki's fate and hearing the blame of strangers, made gifts of merit before the great ascetics under the Bodhi tree and renounced, and that Manimekalai renounced too.","n":"Madhavi's renunciation is placed under the Bodhi tree before the monks, a Buddhist setting, and is moved partly by what strangers say of her (ஏதிலார் ஏச்சுரை); the story the Manimekalai tells begins here. The text states the act, not a doctrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","eegai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 64-68","lb":"SI29:64-68","ev":"The maid tells Kannaki that Madhavi and Manimekalai renounced before the monks under the Bodhi tree.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["அடித்தோழி","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:69-73":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":401,"t":["ஐயந்தீர் காட்சி யடைக்கலங் காத்தோம்ப","வல்லாதேன் பெற்றேன் மயலென் றுயிர்நீத்த","அவ்வை மகளிவடான் அம்மணம் பட்டிலா","வையெயிற் றையையைக் கண்டாயோ தோழீ","மாமி மடமகளைக் கண்டாயோ தோழீ;"],"p":"","r":"Devanthi, showing Aiyai, asks Kannaki whether she sees the unmarried girl with fine teeth, daughter of the mother who gave up her life saying she had fallen into madness because she could not guard the charge given her by the one of clear vision: the young daughter of her mother-in-law.","n":"ஐயந்தீர் காட்சி, 'of vision freed from doubt', seems to name Kavunthi, the Jain nun who gave Kannaki into Maadhari's care, though it could qualify the charge itself. Maadhari dies of the failure to keep a trust, not of any wrong she did. Calling Maadhari மாமி, mother-in-law, makes Aiyai a kinswoman by the bond of the charge; the kinship term is affectionate and its exact sense is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adaikkalam","kaiyarunilai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 69-73","lb":"SI29:69-73","ev":"Devanthi shows Aiyai to Kannaki and tells of Maadhari's death.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவந்தி"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவந்தி","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:74-77":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":402,"t":["என்னேயிஃ தென்னேயிஃ தென்னேயிஃ தென்னேகொல்","பொன்னஞ் சிலம்பிற் புனைமே கலைவளைக்கை","நல்வயிரப் பொற்றோட்டு நாவலம் பொன்னிழைசேர்","மின்னுக் கொடியொன்று மீவிசும்பிற் றோன்றுமால்;"],"p":"","r":"Senguttuvan cries out three times what this can be: a lightning creeper with a golden anklet, a girdle, bangled arms, fine diamond earrings and ornaments of Jambu gold appears high in the sky.","n":"The king sees first only a flash of jewels and lightning, not a person; the anklet is the first ornament named, the thing the story turned on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","azhagu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 74-77","lb":"SI29:74-77","ev":"Kannaki appears in the sky as a lightning-like figure in jewels, and Senguttuvan cries out in wonder.","cl":[],"ag":["கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["குடவர் கோ",""],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:78-83":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":403,"t":["செங்குட்டுவற்குக் கண்ணகியார்","கடவு ணல்லணி காட்டியது","தென்னவன் தீதிலன் தேவர்கோன் றன்கோயில்","நல்விருந் தாயினான் நானவன் றன்மகள்","வென்வேலான் குன்றில் விளையாட்டு யானகலேன்","என்னோடுந் தோழிமீ ரெல்லீரும் வம்மெல்லாம்;"],"p":"","r":"Under the rubric 'Kannaki shows Senguttuvan her goddess's form', Kannaki says that the Pandya is without fault and has become a welcome guest in the palace of the king of the gods; she is his daughter; she will not leave the playground on the hill of the one of the victorious spear; and she calls her friends to come with her.","n":"This is the sharpest turn in the epic's judgment of the king: the woman who called him a king who strayed from right rule and burned his city now declares him faultless and claims him as father. His self-sentence and death have cleared him. The hill of the spear-bearer is Murugan's, near where the kuravar saw her taken up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","sengol","marumai","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 78-83","lb":"SI29:78-83","ev":"Kannaki, appearing as a goddess, tells Senguttuvan the Pandya is faultless and in Indra's palace, and calls her friends.","cl":[["a king who gave up his life for his own wrong verdict is free of fault and received among the gods","general","asserted",80,"kannaki"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி","நெடுஞ்செழியன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["கண்ணகி","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:84-92":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":404,"t":["வஞ்சியீர் வஞ்சி யிடையீர் மறவேலான்","பஞ்சடி யாயத்தீ ரெல்லீரும் வம்மெல்லாம்;","கொங்கையாற் கூடற் பதிசிதைத்துக் கோவேந்தைச்","செஞ்சிலம்பால் வென்றாளைப் பாடுதும் வம்மெல்லாம்","தென்னவன் றன்மகளைப் பாடுதும் வம்மெல்லாம்;","செங்கோல் வளைய வுயிர்வாழார் பாண்டியரென்","றெங்கோ முறைநா இயம்பஇந் நாடடைந்த","பைந்தொடிப் பாவையைப் பாடுதும் வம்மெல்லாம்","பாண்டியன் றன்மகளைப் பாடுதும் வம்மெல்லாம்;"],"p":"","r":"The women of Vanchi call one another, women with waists like the vanchi creeper and companions of the fierce-speared king, to come and sing her who with her breast ruined the city of Koodal and with her red anklet overcame the king, the Pandya's daughter, the bangled doll who came to this land when our king's just tongue declared that Pandyas do not live once the sceptre bends.","n":"The songs praise Kannaki for destroying a city and defeating a king, and in the same stanza they call her that king's daughter, taking up her own claim. The saying about the Pandyas is put in the mouth of the Chera king (எங்கோ முறைநா), so a rival line honours the Pandyas for dying of their own injustice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","sengol","kodungol","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 84-92","lb":"SI29:84-92","ev":"","cl":[["Pandya kings do not live on once their sceptre bends","general","asserted",89,"vanchi_magalir"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்","மகளிர்"],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:93-96":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":405,"t":["வானவ னெங்கோ மகளென்றாம் வையையார்","கோனவன்றான் பெற்ற கொடியென்றாள்-வானவனை","வாழ்த்துவோம் நாமாக வையையார் கோமானை","வாழ்த்துவாள் தேவ மகள்;"],"p":"","r":"A venba says that while we called her the daughter of our king the Chera, she called herself the creeper born of the king of the Vaigai's people; so let us praise the Chera, and the divine woman will praise the lord of the Vaigai.","n":"Two kings claim one goddess, and the dispute is settled by dividing the praise: the people of Vanchi keep their own king, and the goddess keeps the Pandya, the king who wronged her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","vendhan","pathini"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 93-96","lb":"SI29:93-96","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["ஆயத்தார்",""],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:97-100":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":406,"t":["தொல்லை வினையால் துயருழந்தாள் கண்ணின்நீர்","கொல்ல உயிர் கொடுத்த கோவேந்தன் வாழியரோ,","வாழியரோ வாழி வருபுனல்நீர் வையை","சூழு மதுரையார் கோமான்றன் தொல்குலமே;"],"p":"","r":"Long live the great king who gave his life when the tears of her who suffered by an old deed killed him, long live the ancient line of the lord of Madurai, circled by the Vaigai's flowing water.","n":"Both causes stand in one line: her suffering came by an old deed (தொல்லை வினையால்), and the king died of her tears. The blessing falls on the king who caused the wrong, praised for paying with his life, and on his whole line. Who sings these stanzas is not named; they follow the women's songs.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","vinai","kootru","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 97-100","lb":"SI29:97-100","ev":"","cl":[["her suffering came from a deed of old","general","asserted",97,"vanchi_magalir"],["a king who gives up his life for a wrong done under him is to be praised","general","asserted",98,"vanchi_magalir"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:101-104":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":407,"t":["மலையரையன் பெற்ற மடப்பாவை தன்னை","நிலவரசர் நீண்முடிமேல் ஏற்றினான் வாழியரோ,","வாழியரோ வாழி வருபுனல்நீர்த் தண்பொருநை","சூழ்தரும் வஞ்சியார் கோமான்றன் தொல்குலமே;"],"p":"","r":"Long live the king who set the young doll born of the mountain king on the long crowns of the kings of the earth, long live the ancient line of the lord of Vanchi, circled by the cool Porunai.","n":"மலையரையன் பெற்ற மடப்பாவை fits both the Himalaya stone and the mountain king's daughter, the goddess Uma, so the image is praised in a goddess's terms; the doll set on kings' crowns recalls the defeated kings made to carry the stone. The stanza answers the Pandya's blessing line for line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 101-104","lb":"SI29:101-104","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:36"},"si:29:105-107":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":408,"t":["எல்லா நாம்;","காவிரி நாடனைப் பாடுதும் பாடுதும்","பூவிரி கூந்தல் புகார்;"],"p":"","r":"The last stanza of the benediction calls the company to sing, and sing again, the lord of the Kaveri country and Puhar of the flower-strewn tresses.","n":"The Pandya and Chera stanzas before it (97-100, 101-104) each praise a deed of the king and close with வாழியரோ and the king's old line; this Chola stanza has no deed and no வாழியரோ, only the call to sing, so it is either a shorter form or a broken one. The Chola is named only by his country, and the line gives Puhar the woman's epithet பூவிரி கூந்தல், as if the city were a woman.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 105-107","lb":"SI29:105-107","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:108-112":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":409,"t":["வீங்குநீர் வேலி யுலகாண்டு விண்ணவர்கோன்","ஓங்கரணங் காத்த வுரவோன்யா ரம்மானை","ஓங்கரணங் காத்த வுரவோன் உயர்விசும்பில்","தூங்கெயில் மூன்றெறிந்த சோழன்கா னம்மானை","சோழன் புகார்நகரம் பாடேலோ ரம்மானை;"],"p":"","r":"The first ammanai song asks, as the women toss their play-stones, who ruled the sea-fenced world and guarded the high fortress of the king of the gods, answers that it was the Chola who destroyed the three hanging forts in the sky, and bids them sing his city Puhar.","n":"The ammanai form is a riddle and its answer: one line asks யார் அம்மானை, the next repeats the clue and answers with காண் அம்மானை, and the refrain turns from the king to his city. So the two deeds, guarding Indra's fort and cutting down the hanging forts, are set as one king's, and the song names no Chola king by his own name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 108-112","lb":"SI29:108-112","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:113-117":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":410,"t":["புறவு நிறைபுக்குப் பொன்னுலக மேத்தக்","குறைவில் உடம்பரிந்த கொற்றவன்யா ரம்மானை","குறைவில் உடம்பரிந்த கொற்றவன்முன் வந்த","கறவை முறைசெய்த காவலன்கா ணம்மானை","காவலன் பூம்புகார் பாடேலோ ரம்மானை;"],"p":"","r":"The second ammanai song asks who was the king who, while heaven praised him, entered the scale for a dove and cut his own flawless body, answers that he was the guardian who gave justice to the milch cow that came before him, and bids them sing his flowering Puhar.","n":"The riddle's form makes the body-cutting king and the king who judged for the cow one answer, where Kannaki's plea at the Pandya's court (20:55-73) gave them as two Chola precedents; whether the song means one man or one line is not settled by the Tamil. The deeds Kannaki used to accuse a Pandya are sung here, in the Chera capital, as the plain glory of Puhar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arul","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 113-117","lb":"SI29:113-117","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:118-122":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":411,"t":["கடவரைக ளோரெட்டுங் கண்ணிமையா காண","வடவரைமேல் வாள்வேங்கை யொற்றினன்யா ரம்மானை","வடவரைமேல் வாள்வேங்கை யொற்றினன்றிக் கெட்டுங்","குடைநிழலிற் கொண்டளித்த கொற்றவன்கா னம்மானை","கொற்றவன்றன் பூம்புகார் பாடேலோ ரம்மானை;"],"p":"","r":"The third ammanai song asks who stamped his sword-bright tiger on the northern mountain while the eight mountains of the quarters looked on without blinking, answers that it was the victor who took the eight directions under the shade of his umbrella and protected them, and bids them sing his Puhar.","n":"The song does not name the king; the tiger on the Himalaya is the deed the Puhar book gives to Thirumavalavan (5:89-110), where the mountain halts him. Here the eight guardian mountains only watch, and the halt has become a rule over all eight quarters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 118-122","lb":"SI29:118-122","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:123-128":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":412,"t":["அம்மனை தங்கையிற் கொண்டங் கணியிழையார்","தம்மனையிற் பாடுந் தகையேலோ ரம்மானை","தம்மனையிற் பாடுந் தகையெலாந் தார்வேந்தன்","கொம்மை வரிமுலைமேற் கூடவே யம்மானை","கொம்மை வரிமுலைமேற் கூடிற் குலவேந்தன்","அம்மென் புகார்நகரம் பாடேலோ ரம்மானை;"],"p":"","r":"The fourth ammanai song, of the women themselves, asks what is the worth of the singing the jewelled women do in their homes with the ammanai in their hands, answers that it is that the garlanded king should come to their round, painted breasts, and says that if the king of the line comes there, they will sing his lovely Puhar.","n":"The game turns from the king's deeds to the singers' desire for him: the play-song is offered as a lure for his embrace. The king is again only தார்வேந்தன், குலவேந்தன், not named, and the refrain still ends on Puhar.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","vendhan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 123-128","lb":"SI29:123-128","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:129-132":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":413,"t":["பொன்னிலங்கு பூங்கொடி பொலஞ்செய்கோதை வில்லிட","மின்னிலங்கு மேகலைகள் ஆர்ப்பஆர்ப்ப எங்கணும்","தென்னன் வாழ்க வாழ்கஎன்று சென்றுபந் தடித்துமே","தேவரார மார்பன்வாழ்க என்றுபந் தடித்துமே;"],"p":"","r":"The first ball song shows a girl like a golden creeper, her gold garland flashing and her girdle-bells ringing, running everywhere and striking the ball while she cries long live the Southerner, long live the one whose chest bears the gods' necklace.","n":"The ball songs praise the Pandya alone (தென்னன், and தேவர் ஆர மார்பன், the king who wore the necklace of the king of the gods): sung in the Chera city after the Pandya's death, they follow Kannaki's word just before (78-83) that the Pandya is blameless and is now Indra's guest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 129-132","lb":"SI29:129-132","ev":"The girls play at ball, crying blessings on the Pandya with each stroke.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:133-136":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":414,"t":["பின்னுமுன்னும் எங்கணும் பெயர்ந்துவந் தெழுந்துலாய்","மின்னுமின் னிளங்கொடி வியனிலத் திழிந்தெனத்","தென்னன்வாழ்க வாழ்கஎன்று சென்றுபந் தடித்துமே","தேவரார மார்பன்வாழ்க என்றுபந் தடித்துமே;"],"p":"","r":"The second ball song likens the player, darting back and forth on every side, rising and turning, to a young creeper of lightning come down to the wide earth, as she strikes the ball with the same cries for the Pandya.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 133-136","lb":"SI29:133-136","ev":"A girl runs and leaps after the ball, crying blessings on the Pandya.","cl":[],"ag":["மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:137-140":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":415,"t":["துன்னிவந்து கைத்தலத் திருந்ததில்லை நீணிலம்","தன்னினின்று மந்தரத் தெழுந்ததில்லை தானெனத்","தென்னன்வாழ்க வாழ்கஎன்று சென்றுபந் தடித்துமே","தேவரார மார்பன்வாழ்க என்றுபந் தடித்துமே;"],"p":"","r":"The third ball song says the ball, coming close, never rested in the hand and, rising from the long earth, never reached the heights, and closes with the same cries for the Pandya as it is struck.","n":"Lines 137-138 seem to describe the ball kept always between hand and sky, never caught and never lost upward; மந்தரம் may be the sky or Mount Mandara, and the sense is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 137-140","lb":"SI29:137-140","ev":"The girls keep the ball in the air, crying blessings on the Pandya.","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:141-145":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":416,"t":["வடங்கொள் மணியூசன் மேலிரீஇ ஐயை","உடங்கொருவர் கைநிமிர்த்தாங் கொற்றைமே லூக்கக்","கடம்பு முதல்தடிந்த காவலனைப் பாடிக்","குடங்கைநெடுங் கண்பிறழ ஆடாமோ ஊசல்","கொடுவிற் பொறிபாடி ஆடாமோ ஊசல்;"],"p":"","r":"The first swing song bids the girls seat Aiyai on the jewelled swing hung on ropes, push it together with lifted hands, and swing with long eyes darting, singing the guardian who cut the kadamba down at its root and the emblem of the curved bow.","n":"The swing songs belong to the Chera. கடம்பு முதல் தடிந்த is the Patitruppattu's own phrase for Neduncheralathan's deed (patitruppattu:20), but the song names no king, so the deed is sung as the Chera line's glory before Senguttuvan. ஐயை may be Madari's daughter, whom Devanthi has just pointed out among the heavenly company (72), or a word for a lady; the singers' own swing would make the second reading possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 141-145","lb":"SI29:141-145","ev":"The girls set a companion on the swing and push it as they sing.","cl":[],"ag":["ஐயை"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:146-150":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":417,"t":["ஓரைவ ரீரைம் பதின்மர் உடன்றெழுந்த","போரிற் பெருஞ்சோறு போற்றாது தானளித்த","சேரன் பொறையன் மலையன் திறம்பாடிக்","கார்செய் குழலாட ஆடாமோ ஊசல்","கடம்பெறிந்த வாபாடி ஆடாமோ ஊசல்;"],"p":"","r":"The second swing song sings the might of the Chera, Poraiyan, Malaiyan, who without stint fed the great feast to the war in which the Five and the twice fifty rose against each other, and bids them swing with dark hair flying, singing his cutting of the kadamba.","n":"The feast at the war of the five and the hundred is the deed Purananuru 2 gives by name to Uthiyan Cheralathan; the song gives it to the Chera by his house names (சேரன், பொறையன், மலையன்) and sets it beside the kadamba deed of another king, so it praises the line, not one man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","maram","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 146-150","lb":"SI29:146-150","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:151-155":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":418,"t":["வன்சொல் யவனர் வளநாடு வன்பெருங்கல்","தென்குமரி யாண்ட செருவிற் கயற்புலியான்","மன்பதைகாக் குங்கோமான் மன்னன் திறம்பாடி","மின்செய் இடைநுடங்க ஆடாமோ ஊசல்","விறல்விற் பொறிபாடி ஆடாமோ ஊசல்;"],"p":"","r":"The third swing song sings the might of the king who guards all living people, the lord of the bow who, it seems, holds the fish and the tiger, and who ruled from the rich land of the harsh-tongued Yavanas and the great mountain to Kumari in the south, and bids them swing with slender waists swaying, singing the emblem of the victorious bow.","n":"செருவிற் கயற்புலியான் can be read as the king of the battle-bow who also bears (or has taken) the Pandya's fish and the Chola's tiger; if so, the Chera song folds all three crowns' emblems into its own king, where the Madurai kuravai (17:1-10) set the three side by side on the Himalaya. The reading of the compound is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 151-155","lb":"SI29:151-155","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:156-160":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":419,"t":["தீங்கரும்பு நல்லுலக்கை யாகச் செழுமுத்தம்","பூங்காஞ்சி நீழல் அவைப்பார் புகார்மகளிர்","ஆழிக் கொடித்திண்டேர்ச் செம்பியன் வம்பலர்தார்ப்","பாழித் தடவரைத்தோட் பாடலே பாடல்","பாவைமார் ஆரிக்கும் பாடலே பாடல்;"],"p":"","r":"The first pounding song says the women of Puhar, in the shade of flowering kaanchi, pound rich pearls with sweet sugarcane for pestles, and that the song to sing is the one of the Chembiyan of the strong chariot with the wheel banner and the shoulders like great hills, the one the girls cry out.","n":"Each of the three pounding songs gives one city its women, its pestle and its king: the luxury is formal (pearls in place of grain, cane or coral or ivory in place of a pestle), and the work song is turned to praise. The order here is Chola, Pandya, Chera, ending on the host.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 156-160","lb":"SI29:156-160","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:161-165":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":420,"t":["பாடல்சான் முத்தம் பவழ உலக்கையான்","மாட மதுரை மகளிர் குறுவரே","வானவர்கோன் ஆரம் வயங்கியதோட் பஞ்சவன்றன்","மீனக் கொடிபாடும் பாடலே பாடல்","வேப்பந்தார் நெஞ்சுணக்கும் பாடலே பாடல்;"],"p":"","r":"The second pounding song says the women of towered Madurai pound pearls worthy of song with coral pestles, and that the song to sing is of the fish banner of the Panchavan, whose shoulders shine with the necklace of the king of the gods, the song that sinks into the heart of the neem-garlanded king.","n":"The last line, the song that 'melts' or 'enters' the heart of the neem-garlanded one (வேப்பந்தார் நெஞ்சுணக்கும்), lets the song reach the Pandya himself, now in Indra's world by Kannaki's word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","pugazh","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 161-165","lb":"SI29:161-165","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:166-170":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":421,"t":["சந்துரற் பெய்து தகைசால் அணிமுத்தம்","வஞ்சி மகளிர் குறுவரே வான்கோட்டாற்","கடந்தடுதார்ச் சேரன் கடம்பெறிந்த வார்த்தை","படர்ந்த நிலம்போர்த்த பாடலே பாடல்","பனந்தோ டுளங்கவரும் பாடலே பாடல்;"],"p":"","r":"The third pounding song says the women of Vanchi pound fine pearls in a sandalwood mortar with ivory pestles, and that the song to sing is the one that spread over the land and covered it, the report of the Chera of the conquering garland who cut down the kadamba, the song that wins the palmyra-wearer's heart.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 166-170","lb":"SI29:166-170","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:29:171-174":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":422,"t":["ஆங்கு, நீணில மன்னர் நெடுவிற் பொறையன்நல்","தாள்தொழார் வாழ்த்தல் தமக்கரிது சூளொழிய","எங்கோ மடந்தையும் ஏத்தினாள் நீடுழி","செங்குட் டுவன்வாழ்க என்று."],"p":"","r":"The close says that the kings of the long earth who do not bow at the good feet of Poraiyan of the long bow find it hard to live, and that, her vow fulfilled, our king's lady too praised him: long live Senguttuvan for ages.","n":"எங்கோ மடந்தை takes up the Vanchi women's claim earlier in the kaathai (93) that Kannaki is the Chera's daughter, against the Madurai women's claim that she is the Pandya's; so the close ends with the goddess on the Chera's side. சூளொழிய may mean that her vow (to destroy Madurai) is now spent, or 'without an oath'; and வாழ்த்தல் in line 172 may be a slip for வாழ்தல், which the reading follows. This is the only unit in the song cycle that names a living king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panivu","vendhan","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 29, lines 171-174","lb":"SI29:171-174","ev":"Kannaki, now a goddess, joins in praising Senguttuvan.","cl":[["kings who will not bow to the Chera's feet cannot long prosper","general","asserted",172,"vanchi_magalir"]],"ag":["கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மகளிர்",""],"ch":"si:37"},"si:30:1-23":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":423,"t":["வடதிசை வணக்கிய வானவர் பெருந்தகை","கடவுட் கோலம் கட்புலம் புக்கபின்","தேவந் திகையைச் செவ்விதின் நோக்கி","வாயெடுத் தரற்றிய மணிமே கலையார்","யாதவள் துறத்தற் கேதுவீங் குரையெனக்","கோமகன் கொற்றங் குறைவின் றோங்கி","நாடு பெருவளஞ் சுரக்கென் றேத்தி","அணிமே கலையா ராயத் தோங்கிய","மணிமே கலைதன் வான்றுற வுரைக்கும்","மையீ ரோதி வகைபெறு வனப்பின்","ஐவகை வகுக்கும் பருவங் கொண்டது","செவ்வரி யொழுகிய செழுங்கடை மழைக்கண்","அவ்வியம் அறிந்தன அதுதான் அறிந்திலள்","ஒத்தொளிர் பவளத் துள்ளொளி சிறந்த","நித்தில விளநகை நிரம்பா வளவின","புணர்முலை விழுந்தன புல்லக மகன்றது","தளரிடை நுணுகலுந் தகையல்குல் பரந்தது","குறங்கிணை திரண்டன கோலம் பொறாஅ","நிறங்கிளர் சீறடி நெய்தோய் தளிரின","தலைக்கோ லாசான் பின்னுள னாகக்","குலத்தலை மாக்கள் கொள்கையிற் கொள்ளார்","யாது நின்கருத் தென்செய் கோவென","மாதவி நற்றாய் மாதவிக் குரைப்ப"],"p":"","r":"After the goddess's form has passed from his sight, the Chera king turns to Devanthi and asks who the Manimekalai is for whom she cried out and why she renounced; Devanthi, wishing his rule and land prosperity, begins: the girl had come to full youth, her eyes knew love's craft though she herself did not, her body had ripened in every part, the dance master stood ready behind her, and Madhavi's mother asked Madhavi what she intended and what she should do.","n":"The catalogue of the girl's ripened body (lines 10 to 19) is the courtesan house's reckoning of a daughter ready for the stage, with the dance master already waiting (line 20); the renunciation that follows is set against exactly this readiness. Line 21, that the chief men of the clan would not take her by their custom, can be read as the courtesan house's worry or as the families' refusal of a courtesan's daughter in marriage; the reading leaves it open. Line 13 makes a small irony: the eyes knew the craft of love, she did not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","ilamai","varaivinmagalir","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 30, lines 1-23","lb":"SI30:1-23","ev":"Senguttuvan asks Devanthi why Manimekalai renounced, and Devanthi begins by telling how Madhavi's mother, seeing the girl grown, asked Madhavi what to do with her.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","தேவந்தி","நற்றாய்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவந்தி","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:38"},"si:30:24-34":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":424,"t":["வருகவென் மடமகள் மணிமே கலையென்","றுருவி லாள னொருபெருஞ் சிலையொடு","விரைமலர் வாளி வெறுநிலத் தெறியக்","கோதைத் தாமங் குழலொடு களைந்து","போதித் தானம் புரிந்தறம் படுத்தனள்","ஆங்கது கேட்ட அரசனும் நகரமும்","ஓங்கிய நன்மணி யுறுகடல் வீழ்த்தோர்","தம்மிற் றுன்பந் தாம்நனி யெய்தச்","செம்மொழி மாதவர் சேயிழை நங்கை","தன்றுற வெமக்குச் சாற்றின ளென்றே","அன்புறு நன்மொழி அருளொடுங் கூறினர்"],"p":"","r":"Madhavi called her young daughter Manimekalai and, while Kama flung his great bow and fragrant arrows on the bare ground, removed the garland together with the girl's hair and gave her at the Bodhi seat into the dharma; the king and the city, hearing it, grieved like men who have dropped a fine gem into the sea, and the ascetics of true speech said with love and grace that the girl had declared her renunciation to them.","n":"Who removes the hair is not named: the sentence runs on from Madhavi's call, and the next unit says the girl herself left off her adornment. போதித் தானம் (line 28) can be the gift made at the Bodhi or the Bodhi as a place; either way the giving is the scene of Kovalan's dream (15:101-104), where Madhavi gives Manimekalai before the dharma-one of the Bodhi and Kama casts his arrows on the bare ground, here told as done. The grief of king and city (lines 29 to 31) treats a girl's renunciation as a public loss, the loss of the gem of the town's arts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","kaamam","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 30, lines 24-34","lb":"SI30:24-34","ev":"Madhavi gives Manimekalai to the Buddhist dharma, her hair and garland removed, and Puhar's king and people grieve while the ascetics receive her.","cl":[],"ag":["மாதவி","மணிமேகலை","உருவி லாளன்","சென்னி","செம்மொழி மாதவர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவந்தி","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:38"},"si:30:35-52":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":425,"t":["பருவ மன்றியும் பைந்தொடி நங்கை","திருவிழை கோலம் நீங்கின ளாதலின்","அரற்றினென் என்றாங் கரசற் குரைத்தபின்","குரற்றலைக் கூந்தல் குலைந்துபின் வீழத்","துடித்தனள் புருவந் துவரிதழ்ச் செவ்வாய்","மடித்தெயி றரும்பினள் வருமொழி மயங்கினள்","திருமுகம் வியர்த்தனள் செங்கண் சிவந்தனள்","கைவிட் டோச்சினள் கால்பெயர்த் தெழுந்தனள்","பலரறி வாராத் தெருட்சியள் மருட்சியள்","உலறிய நாவினள் உயர்மொழி கூறிக்","தெய்வமுற் றெழுந்த தேவந்திகைதான்","கொய்தளிர்க் குறிஞ்சிக் கோமான் றன்முன்","கடவுண் மங்கலங் காணிய வந்த","மடமொழி நல்லார் மாணிழை யோருள்","அரட்டன் செட்டிதன் ஆயிழை ஈன்ற","இரட்டையம் பெண்கள் இருவரு மன்றியும்","ஆடக மாடத் தரவணைக் கிடந்தோன்","சேடக் குடும்பியின் சிறுமகள் ஈங்குளள்"],"p":"","r":"Devanthi tells the king she wailed because the girl left off her adornment before her time; then her hair falls loose, her brows quiver, she bites her lip, her words blur, her face sweats, her eyes redden, she flings her arms and rises, and possessed by a god she speaks before the hill-country king: among the women come to see the goddess's consecration are the twin daughters of Arattan Chetti's wife and the young daughter of Sedakkudumbi, servant of the god who lies on the serpent.","n":"The signs of possession are listed one per half-line (lines 38 to 44) in the same piled style as the girl's body in the first unit, so the kaathai sets a body made ready for desire beside a body taken by a god. The voice is not Devanthi's own: the god names himself only at line 69. The three girls are introduced by their present parents before anything is said of who they were.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vazhipaadu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 30, lines 35-52","lb":"SI30:35-52","ev":"Devanthi explains her cry, falls into possession and, before Senguttuvan, points out three girls among the women at the consecration.","cl":[],"ag":["தேவந்தி","பாசண்டச் சாத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["தேவந்தி","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:38"},"si:30:53-64":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":426,"t":["மங்கல மடந்தை கோட்டத் தாங்கண்","செங்கோட் டுயர்வரைச் சேணுயர் சிலம்பிற்","பிணிமுக நெடுங்கற் பிடர்த்தலை நிரம்பிய","அணிகயம் பலவுள ஆங்கவை யிடையது","கடிப்பகை நுண்கலுங் கவரிதழ்க் குறுங்கலும்","இடிக்கலப் பன்ன இழைந்துகு நீரும்","உண்டோர் சுனையத னுள்புக் காடினர்","பண்டைப் பிறவிய ராகுவ ராதலின்","ஆங்கது கொணர்ந்தாங் காயிழை கோட்டத்","தோங்கிருங் கோட்டி யிருந்தோய் உன்கைக்","குறிக்கோட் டகையது கொள்கெனத் தந்தேன்","உறித்தாழ் கரகமும் உன்கைய தன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The possessing voice says that on the high hill by the shrine of the Lady of Good Fortune, among the pools filling the ridge, there is a spring of grinding stones and splashing water in which those who bathe become again the persons of their former birth, and that it brought that water and gave it, in a pot hanging in a sling, into the hand of the one sitting in the assembly at the lady's shrine as a token.","n":"The spring does not purify or grant a wish, the usual work of a holy tank; it makes a past birth come back into the present body. The one addressed (இருந்தோய், line 62) is Maadalan, as line 70 shows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","theerththam","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 30, lines 53-64","lb":"SI30:53-64","ev":"Speaking through Devanthi, the god tells of a spring on the hill that restores former births and says he once gave its water to Maadalan in a pot.","cl":[],"ag":["பாசண்டச் சாத்தன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பாசண்டச் சாத்தன்","மாடலன்"],"ch":"si:38"},"si:30:65-91":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":427,"t":["கதிரொழி காறுங் கடவுட் டன்மை","முதிரா தந்நீர் முத்திற மகளிரைத்","தெளித்தனை யாட்டினிச் சிறுகுறு மகளிர்","ஒளித்த பிறப்பின ராகுவர் காணாய்","பாசண் டன்யான் பார்ப்பனி தன்மேல்","மாடல மறையோய் வந்தே னென்றலும்","மன்னவன் விம்மித மெய்தியம் மாடலன்","தன்முக நோக்கலும் தானனி மகிழ்ந்து","கேளிது மன்னா கெடுகநின் தீயது","மாலதி யென்பாள் மாற்றாள் குழவியைப்","பால்சுரந் தூட்டப் பழவினை யுருத்துக்","கூற்றுயிர் கொள்ளக் குழவிக் கிரங்கி","ஆற்றாத் தன்மையள் ஆரஞ ரெய்திப்","பாசண் டன்பாற் பாடு கிடந்தாட்","காசில் குழவி யதன்வடி வாகி","வந்தனன் அன்னைநீ வான்துய ரொழிகெனச்","செந்திறம் புரிந்தோன் செல்லல் நீக்கிப்","பார்ப்பனி தன்னோடு பண்டைத் தாய்பாற்","காப்பியத் தொல்குடிக் கவின்பெற வளர்ந்து","தேவந் திகையைத் தீவலஞ் செய்து","நாலீ ராண்டு நடந்ததற் பின்னர்","மூவா இளநலங் காட்டியென் கோட்டத்து","நீவா வென்றே நீங்கிய சாத்தன்","மங்கல மடந்தை கோட்டத் தாங்கண்","அங்குறை மறையோ னாகத் தோன்றி","உறித்தாழ் கரகமும் என்கைத் தந்து","குறிக்கோள் கூறிப் போயினன் வாரான்"],"p":"","r":"The voice says the water keeps its divine power till sunset and bids Maadalan sprinkle the three girls so that their hidden births appear, naming itself Paasandan come upon the Brahmin woman; the king is amazed, and Maadalan tells him the story: Maalathi's co-wife's baby, fed at her breast, died when an old deed flared up, she lay in vigil at Paasandan's shrine, the god came in the child's form, grew up in the old Brahmin family, married Devanthi, and after eight years showed his ageless youth, told her to come to his shrine and left; later, appearing as a Brahmin at the shrine of the Lady of Good Fortune, he gave Maadalan the pot and a token and went away for good.","n":"Maadalan's telling repeats the Maalathi story of the dream kaathai (9:1-44) with a change: there Maalathi carried the child round the city's shrines, here she lies in vigil at Paasandan's own shrine, and the child's death is put down to பழவினை (line 75), an old deed flaring up, which the earlier telling does not say. The god's parting words to Devanthi, எம் கோட்டத்து நீ வா, are given here as என் கோட்டத்து நீவா (lines 86 to 87), and the chapter shows how they are kept: he comes back to her not as a husband but as the god who possesses her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","arul","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 30, lines 65-91","lb":"SI30:65-91","ev":"The god tells Maadalan to sprinkle the water on the three girls, and Maadalan tells the amazed king how Saathan came as Maalathi's dead child, married Devanthi and later gave him the pot.","cl":[["a deed of a former time flares up and brings death even on an infant","general","asserted",75,"maadalan"]],"ag":["பாசண்டச் சாத்தன்","குடவர் கோ","மாடலன்","மாலதி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:38"},"si:30:92-111":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":428,"t":["ஆங்கது கொண்டு போந்தே னாதலின்","ஈங்கிம் மறையோ டன்மேற் றோன்றி","அந்நீர் தெளியென் றறிந்தோன் கூறினன்","மன்னர் கோவே மடந்தையர் தம்மேல்","தெளித்தீங் கறிகுவம் என்றவன் தெளிப்ப","ஒளித்த பிறப்புவந் துற்றதை யாதலின்","புகழ்ந்த காதலன் போற்றா வொழுக்கின்","இகழ்ந்ததற் கிரங்கும் என்னையும் நோக்காய்","ஏதில் நன்னாட் டியாருமில் ஒருதனிக்","காதலன் றன்னொடு கடுந்துய ருழந்தாய்","யான்பெறு மகளே என்றுணைத் தோழீ","வான்றுயர் நீக்கும் மாதே வாராய்","என்னோ டிருந்த இலங்கிழை நங்கை","தன்னோ டிடையிருள் தனித்துய ருழந்து","போனதற் கிரங்கிப் புலம்புறு நெஞ்சம்","யானது பொறேஎன் என்மகன் வாராய்","வருபுனல் வையை வான்றுறைப் பெயர்ந்தோன்","உருகெழு மூதூர் ஊர்க்குறு மாக்களின்","வந்தேன் கேட்டேன் மனையிற் காணேன்","எந்தாய் இளையாய் எங்கொளித் தாயோ"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan says that because he brought the water, the knowing god has now appeared on this Brahmin woman and told him to sprinkle it, and he sprinkles the girls; their hidden births return and they cry out: one as a mother to the daughter she bore, who suffered alone with her husband in a strange land, one as a mother whose heart cannot bear that her son went off into the dark with the girl who lived with her, and one who came back from the Vaigai's ford, heard the news, found the house empty and asks the young one where she has hidden.","n":"The girls do not say who they were: the three griefs (for a daughter, for a son and for a girl who had lodged in the house) identify Kannaki's mother, Kovalan's mother and Maadari, and Maadalan names them only in the next units. Lines 102 and 103 run several forms of address together (daughter, companion, lady who ends grief), and how the lines divide among the three voices is not certain. The scene is offered as proof of former birth seen by a whole court.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","anbu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 30, lines 92-111","lb":"SI30:92-111","ev":"Maadalan sprinkles the water on the three girls, and they cry out in the voices of the mothers of Kannaki and Kovalan and of Maadari.","cl":[],"ag":["மாடலன்","முத்திற மகளிர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["முத்திற மகளிர்","கண்ணகி"],"ch":"si:38"},"si:30:112-131":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":429,"t":["என்றாங் கரற்றி இனைந்தினைந் தேங்கிப்","பொன்தாழ் அகலத்துப் போர்வெய் யோன்முன்","குதலைச் செய்வாய்க் குறுந்தொடி மகளிர்","முதியோர் மொழியின் முன்றில் நின்றழத்","தோடலர் போந்தைத் தொடுகழல் வேந்தன்","மாடல மறையோன் றன்முக நோக்க","மன்னர் கோவே வாழ்கென் றேத்தி","முந்நூன் மார்பன் முன்னிய துரைப்போன்","மறையோன் உற்ற வான்துயர் நீங்க","உறைகவுள் வேழக் கையகம் புக்கு","வானோர் வடிவம் பெற்றவன் பெற்ற","காதலி தன்மேற் காதல ராதலின்","மேனிலை யுலகத் தவருடன் போகும்","தாவா நல்லறஞ் செய்தில ரதனால்","அஞ்செஞ் சாய லஞ்சா தணுகும்","வஞ்சி மூதூர் மாநகர் மருங்கிற்","பொற்கொடி தன்மேற் பொருந்திய காதலின்","அற்புளஞ் சிறந்தாங் கரட்டன் செட்டி","மடமொழி நல்லாள் மனமகிழ் சிறப்பின்","உடன்வயிற் றோராய் ஒருங்குடன் றோன்றினர்"],"p":"","r":"When the lisping little girls have wept in the courtyard with the words of old women before the king, he looks at Maadalan, who blesses him and explains: because the mothers loved the wife of the man who entered the elephant's trunk to end a Brahmin's distress and so won the form of the gods, and had not done the unfailing dharma that goes with those of the upper world, they came as twins to Arattan Chetti's wife in old Vanchi, near the woman of the golden creeper whom they loved.","n":"Two fruits of deeds are set side by side: Kovalan's rescue of the Brahmin from the elephant (15:42-53) has earned him a god's form, while the mothers' love, without the dharma that leads upward, has earned them a new birth near the beloved. Love here is not condemned; it decides where they are born. The phrase கையகம் புக்கு (line 121) repeats the wording of the rescue scene (15:49).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","piravi","aram","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 30, lines 112-131","lb":"SI30:112-131","ev":"Maadalan tells the king that Kannaki's and Kovalan's mothers, bound by love for Kannaki, were reborn as Arattan Chetti's twins in Vanchi.","cl":[["saving another from death at the risk of one's own life wins the form of the gods","general","asserted",122,"maadalan"],["those who love a person but have not done the dharma that leads upward are reborn near the one they love rather than going to the upper world","general","asserted",125,"maadalan"]],"ag":["முத்திற மகளிர்","குடவர் கோ","மாடலன்","கோவலன்"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:38"},"si:30:132-146":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":430,"t":["ஆயர் முதுமக ளாயிழை தன்மேல்","போய பிறப்பிற் பொருந்திய காதலின்","ஆடிய குரவையின் அரவணைக் கிடந்தோன்","சேடக் குடும்பியின் சிறுமக ளாயினள்","நற்றிறம் புரிந்தோர் பொற்படி யெய்தலும்","அற்புளஞ் சிறந்தோர் பற்றுவழிச் சேறலும்","அறப்பயன் விளைதலும் மறப்பயன் விளைதலும்","பிறந்தவ ரிறத்தலும் இறந்தவர் பிறத்தலும்","புதுவ தன்றே தொன்றியல் வாழ்க்கை","ஆனே றூர்ந்தோ னருளிற் றோன்றி","மாநிலம் விளக்கிய மன்னவ னாதலின்","செய்தவப் பயன்களுஞ் சிறந்தோர் படிவமும்","கையகத் தனபோற் கண்டனை யன்றே","ஊழிதோ றூழி யுலகங் காத்து","நீடுவா ழியரோ நெடுந்தகை யென்ற"],"p":"","r":"Maadalan goes on that the old herdswoman, out of love for the girl from her former birth, has become Sedakkudumbi's young daughter; that the good reach the golden world, those of loving heart go where their attachment leads, the fruits of virtue and of wrong ripen, the born die and the dead are born, and none of this is new but the ancient way of life; and that the king, born by the grace of the one who rides the bull, has seen the fruit of austerity and the forms of the great as if in his hand, and should live and guard the world age after age.","n":"Lines 136 to 140 are the kaathai's general law, and they finish what the patikam set out (0:55-57): there the deed of the past comes in wrath to be eaten; here good deeds ripen as surely as wrong ones, and love is a force like deed that carries a person to a new birth. The Brahmin calls the Chera born of Siva's grace (line 141); this is his praise of the king, not a claim the poem makes about the pathini cult.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","pattru","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 30, lines 132-146","lb":"SI30:132-146","ev":"Maadalan says Maadari was reborn as Sedakkudumbi's daughter, states the law of births and deeds, and blesses the king.","cl":[["those who did good reach the golden world, and those whose hearts are full of love go where their attachment leads","general","asserted",137,"maadalan"],["the fruits of virtue and of wrongdoing both ripen","general","asserted",138,"maadalan"],["the born die and the dead are born, and this is the ancient order of life, nothing new","general","asserted",139,"maadalan"],["the king was born by the grace of Siva, the rider of the bull","saiva","asserted",141,"maadalan"]],"ag":["மாதரி"],"ec":[],"spk":["மாடலன்","குடவர் கோ"],"ch":"si:38"},"si:30:147-164":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":431,"t":["மாடல மறையோன் றன்னொடு மகிழ்ந்து","பாடல்சால் சிறப்பிற் பாண்டிநன் னாட்டுக்","கலிகெழு கூடல் கதழெரி மண்ட","முலைமுகந் திருகிய மூவா மேனிப்","பத்தினிக் கோட்டப் படிப்புறம் வகுத்து","நித்தல் விழாவணி நிகழ்கென் றேவிப்","பூவும் புகையும் மேவிய விரையும்","தேவந் திகையைச் செய்கென் றருளி","வலமுறை மும்முறை வந்தனன் வணங்கி","உலக மன்னவ நின்றோன் முன்னர்","அருஞ்சிறை நீங்கிய ஆரிய மன்னரும்","பெருஞ்சிறைக் கோட்டம் பிரிந்த மன்னரும்","குடகக் கொங்கரும் மாளுவ வேந்தரும்","கடல்சூ ழிலங்கைக் கயவாகு வேந்தனும்","எந்நாட் டாங்கண் இமய வரம்பனின்","நன்னாட் செய்த நாளணி வேள்வியில்","வந்தீ கென்றே வணங்கினர் வேண்டத்","தந்தேன் வரமென் றேழுந்த தொருகுரல்"],"p":"","r":"Glad with Maadalan, the king endows the temple of the pathini of unaging body who twisted off her breast so that fierce fire spread through Koodal of the Pandya land, orders a festival there every day, gives Devanthi charge of its flowers, incense and scents, and goes round it three times and bows; before him the Aryan kings freed from prison, the kings released from the great prison, the Kongar of Kudagam, the Malava kings and Gajabahu of sea-girt Lanka pray her to come to the festival they will hold in their lands as on this day of the Chera's sacrifice, and a voice rises: I have granted the boon.","n":"The kaathai's name comes from line 164, and the boon is asked by foreign and conquered kings, some just let out of the Chera's prison: the cult of the Madurai pathini spreads through the very kings defeated in the campaign for her stone. The temple is identified by the burning of Madurai, not by Kannaki's name. The epithet இமய வரம்பன் (line 161) is given here to the Chera holding the sacrifice; in the Patitruppattu's second decade the name belongs to Senguttuvan's father, so the two uses should not be merged. Line 162's construction (whose festival, held where) is compressed and the reading follows the plainest sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","vazhipaadu","vizhavu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 30, lines 147-164","lb":"SI30:147-164","ev":"Senguttuvan endows the pathini's temple with daily worship under Devanthi, the visiting kings beg the goddess to come to their lands, and a voice grants the boon.","cl":[],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","மாடலன்","தேவந்தி","ஆரிய மன்னர்","கயவாகு","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"ch":"si:38"},"si:30:165-185":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":432,"t":["ஆங்கது கேட்ட அரசனு மரசரும்","ஓங்கிருந் தானையும் முறையோ டேத்த","வீடுகண் டவர்போல் மெய்ந்நெறி விரும்பிய","மாடல மறையோன் றன்னொடுங் கூடித்","தாழ்கழன் மன்னர் தன்னடி போற்ற","வேள்விச் சாலையின் வேந்தன் போந்தபின்","யானுஞ் சென்றேன் என்னெதி ரெழுந்து","தேவந் திகைமேல் திகழ்ந்து தோன்றி","வஞ்சி மூதூர் மணிமண் டபத்திடை","நுந்தை தாணிழ லிருந்தோய் நின்னை","அரைசுவீற் றிருக்குந் திருப்பொறி யுண்டென்று","உரைசெய் தவன்மே லுருத்து நோக்கிக்","கொங்கவிழ் நறுந்தார்க் கொடித்தேர்த் தானைச்","செங்குட் டுவன்றன் செல்லல் நீங்கப்","பகல்செல் வாயிற் படியோர் தம்முன்","அகலிடப் பாரம் அகல நீக்கிச்","சிந்தை செல்லாச் சேணெடுந் தூரத்து","அந்தமி லின்பத் தரசாள் வேந்தென்று","என்திறம் உரைத்த இமையோ ரிளங்கொடி","தன்திறம் உரைத்த தகைசால் நன்மொழி","தெரிவுறக் கேட்ட திருத்தகு நல்லீர்!"],"p":"","r":"When the king, the kings and the army have praised in turn and the king has left the sacrificial hall with Maadalan, who longs for the true path like those who have seen release, the poet says that he too went, and the young creeper of the gods, shining on Devanthi, rose before him and told his own story: that when he sat in his father's shade in the jewelled hall of Vanchi and a man said he bore the mark of one who will sit as king, he looked at the man in anger and, to end Senguttuvan's distress, put away the burden of the wide earth and became, far beyond where thought can reach, a king ruling endless bliss; and he calls the hearers good people who have heard clearly these words that told her story.","n":"Only here does the narrator say 'I' (யானுஞ் சென்றேன், line 171), and the poem's frame closes in a circle: the goddess whose story the poet told tells the poet's own story, and the poem is named in line 184 as the words that told hers. The patikam (0:1-2) only says that Ilango gave up the throne; this unit gives the cause, a prophecy of kingship that would have set brother against brother, and makes the renunciation an act of care for Senguttuvan. பகல்செல் வாயில் (line 179), the gate of the day's going, places where he lives; the patikam names the eastern-gate shrine, and the unit's phrase is left as written.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","arasu","inbam","veedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 30, lines 165-185","lb":"SI30:165-185","ev":"The poet follows the king out of the sacrificial hall, and the goddess, appearing on Devanthi, tells how he renounced the throne on hearing a prophecy that he would be king.","cl":[["one who gives up the burden of a kingdom becomes king of an endless bliss beyond the reach of thought","general","asserted",182,"kannaki"]],"ag":["குடவர் கோ","மாடலன்","இளங்கோ வடிகள்","கண்ணகி"],"ec":[],"spk":["இளங்கோ வடிகள்","திருத்தகு நல்லீர்"],"ch":"si:38"},"si:30:186-202":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":433,"t":["பரிவும் இடுக்கணும் பாங்குற நீங்குமின்;","தெய்வந் தெளிமின்; தெளிந்தோர்ப் பேணுமின்;","பொய்யுரை அஞ்சுமின்; புறஞ்சொற் போற்றுமின்;","ஊனூண் துறமின்; உயிர்க்கொலை நீங்குமின்;","தானஞ் செய்ம்மின்; தவம்பல தாங்குமின் ;","செய்ந்நன்றி கொல்லன்மின்; தீநட் பிகழ்மின்;","பொய்க்கரி போகன்மின்; பொருண்மொழி நீங்கன்மின்;","அறவோ ரவைக்களம் அகலா தணுகுமின்;","பிறவோ ரவைக்களம் பிழைத்துப் பெயர்மின்;","பிறர்மனை அஞ்சுமின்; பிழையுயிர் ஓம்புமின்;","அறமனை காமின்; அல்லவை கடிமின்;","கள்ளுங் களவுங் காமமும் பொய்யும்","வெள்ளைக் கோட்டியும் விரகினில் ஒழிமின்;","இளமையும் செல்வமும் யாக்கையும் நிலையா","உளநாள் வரையாது ஒல்லுவ தொழியாது","செல்லுந் தேஎத்துக் குறுதுணை தேடுமின்;","மல்லன்மா ஞாலத்து வாழ்வீ ரீங்கென்."],"p":"","r":"The poet bids his hearers put away grief and trouble, trust the god and honour those who trust, fear lying and guard against backbiting, give up eating flesh and killing, give gifts and keep many austerities, not kill a kindness done or keep bad friends, never bear false witness or leave words of substance, keep near the assembly of the virtuous and avoid others, fear another's wife and protect suffering lives, guard the house of dharma and drive off what is not, and shun liquor, theft, lust, lies and idle company; since youth, wealth and body do not last, they should, without waiting on the days they have, seek the true companion for the land they will go to.","n":"After thirty kaathais of story the poem ends in plain commands, almost one to a half-line, and several of them name what the story has shown: false witness (the poor man seized by the spirit, 15:76-94), another's wife, flesh and killing. The list of things to shun (liquor, theft, lust, lies) resembles lay vows of the Jain and Buddhist traditions, but the unit names no school, so the claims are tagged general. The last three lines turn from conduct to impermanence: the reason for all the commands is the journey after death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","nilaiyaamai","marumai","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram 30, lines 186-202","lb":"SI30:186-202","ev":"","cl":[["one should trust the god and honour those who trust","general","asserted",187,"ilango"],["one should give up eating flesh and killing living beings","general","asserted",189,"ilango"],["one should give gifts and keep austerities","general","asserted",190,"ilango"],["one should not kill a kindness done, nor keep evil friends","general","asserted",191,"ilango"],["one should never bear false witness","general","asserted",192,"ilango"],["one should fear another's wife and protect lives in distress","general","asserted",195,"ilango"],["liquor, theft, lust, lies and idle company are to be shunned","general","asserted",197,"ilango"],["youth, wealth and the body do not last, so one must seek a companion for the world one goes to","general","asserted",199,"ilango"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["இளங்கோ வடிகள்","திருத்தகு நல்லீர்"],"ch":"si:38"},"si:vanchi_kandam_katturai:1-15":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":434,"t":["முடியுடை வேந்தர் மூவ ருள்ளும்","குடதிசை யாளுங் கொற்றங் குன்றா","ஆர மார்பிற் சேரர்குலத் துதித்தோர்","அறனும் மறனும் ஆற்றலும் அவர்தம்","பழவிறல் மூதூர்ப் பண்புமேம் படுதலும்","விழவுமலி சிறப்பும் விண்ணவர் வரவும்","ஒடியா இன்பத் தவருறை நாட்டுக்","குடியின் செல்வமுங் கூழின் பெருக்கமும்","வரியுங் குரவையும் விரவிய கொள்கையின்","புறத்துறை மருங்கின் அறத்தொடு பொருந்திய","மறத்துறை முடித்த வாய்வாள் தானையொடு","பொங்கிரும் பரப்பிற் கடல்பிறக் கோட்டிக்","கங்கைப் பேர்யாற் றுக்கரை போகிய","செங்குட் டுவனோ டொருபரிசு நோக்கிக்","கிடந்த வஞ்சிக் காண்ட முற்றிற்று."],"p":"","r":"The narrator closes the Vanchi book by gathering into one view the righteousness, valour and strength of those born in the garlanded Chera line who rule the west among the three crowned kings, the excellence of their old city of ancient victories, its festivals and divine visitors, the wealth of the families and the plenty of grain in their land of unbroken joy, and the vari and kuravai songs, all seen together with Senguttuvan, whose army of unfailing swords completed the deeds of war joined to righteousness, who drove back the sea and went to the bank of the great Ganga, and declares the book complete.","n":"Lines 4 to 8 repeat the frame that closes the Puhar and Madurai books, but the frame is cut short: there is no river and no unfailing sky here, and the arts shrink to the vari and kuravai (line 9), the hill-folk's and herders' songs of this book. Where the Madurai summary ends on a king's death, this one adds a phrase of its own, அறத்தொடு பொருந்திய மறத்துறை (lines 10 to 11), valour 'fitted to aram', and makes war the book's centre. The summary names neither Kannaki nor the Pattini stone for which the march to the north was made (25:113-130); it keeps only the king, his sea and the Ganga. கடல்பிறக் கோட்டி (line 12) is the epithet under which the Patitruppattu's fifth decad names Senguttuvan (கடல் பிறக்கோட்டிய செங்குட்டுவன்); the shared epithet is the Tamil's ground for taking the epic's king as the decad's patron, not proof that the two portraits are one man's history.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","aram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram vanchi_kandam_katturai, lines 1-15","lb":"SIvanchi_kandam_katturai:1-15","ev":"","cl":[["a king's warfare can be waged in keeping with righteousness","general","asserted",10,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:39"},"si:nool_katturai:1-18":{"book":"silappathikaram","num":435,"t":["குமரி வேங்கடங் குணகுட கடலா","மண்டினி மருங்கிற் றண்டமிழ் வரைப்பிற்","செந்தமிழ் கொடுந்தமி ழென்றிரு பகுதியின்","ஐந்திணை மருங்கின் அறம்பொரு ளின்பம்","மக்கள் தேவ ரெனவிரு சார்க்கும்","ஒத்த மரபின் ஒழுக்கொடு புணர","எழுத்தொடு புணர்ந்தசொல் லகத்தெழு பொருளை","இழுக்கா யாப்பின் அகனும் புறனும்","அவற்று வழிப்படூஉஞ் செவ்விசிறந் தோங்கிய","பாடலும் எழாலும் பண்ணும் பாணியும்","அரங்கு விலக்கே ஆடலென் றனைத்தும்","ஒருங்குடன் தழீஇ உடம்படக் கிடந்த","வரியுங் குரவையுஞ் சேதமு மென்றிவை","தெரிவுறு வகையாற் செந்தமி ழியற்கையில்","ஆடிநல் நிழலின் நீடிருங் குன்றம்","காட்டு வார்போற் கருத்துவெளிப் படுத்து","மணிமே கலைமேல் உரைப்பொருள் முற்றிய","சிலப்பதி காரம் முற்றும்."],"p":"","r":"The narrator closes the epic by saying that, in the cool Tamil country bounded by Kumari, Venkatam and the eastern and western seas, with its two kinds of Tamil, the work joins the virtue, wealth and pleasure of the five landscapes, fitting for humans and gods alike, to conduct that keeps the tradition, sets the meaning that rises from words in faultless metre as akam and puram, gathers song, the lute, melody, rhythm, the stage, its rules and dance, and the vari, kuravai and other forms, and lays out its intent as a good mirror shows a vast hill, being the Silappathikaram whose story's matter is completed in Manimekalai.","n":"The close describes the work by its land, its language and its arts, and not by the three morals the patikam announced at 0:55-57: karma, the pathini and the death of the king who erred are not named here, and aram appears only as the first of the three aims of poetry's matter (line 4). Lines 5 and 6 claim the epic's matter is valid for gods as well as humans, which fits a story that ends with Kannaki's ascent and her worship. The simile of lines 15 and 16 claims that a large matter is shown whole in a small compass, as a mirror holds a hill. மணிமேகலைமேல் உரைப்பொருள் முற்றிய (line 17) can be read as the story's matter being completed in the work Manimekalai or upon the person Manimekalai, Kovalan and Madhavi's daughter; either way the line declares the epic's story unfinished within its own covers, and the reading here does not choose between the two.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Silappathikaram nool_katturai, lines 1-18","lb":"SInool_katturai:1-18","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"si:40"},"vp:0":{"book":"valayapathi","num":1,"t":["உலகம் மூன்றும் ஒருங்குடன் ஏத்துமாண்","டிலக மாய திலறறி வனடி","வழுவில் நெஞ்சொடு வாலிதின் ஆற்றவும்","தொழுவல் தொல்வினை நீங்குக என்(று)யான்."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he bows, with a faultless heart and in purity, at the feet of the knower whom the three worlds praise together, so that his ancient deeds may leave him.","n":"The god is named only as அறிவன், the knower, praised by three worlds; no creating role is given, and the hoped-for fruit of worship is the removal of old deeds (தொல்வினை), not a gift of grace. Line 2 is badly divided in the text, so the exact epithet is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vinai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 0","lb":"VP0","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:1":{"book":"valayapathi","num":2,"t":["வினைபல வலியி னாலே வேறுவே(று) யாக்கை யாகி","நனிபல பிறவி தன்னுள் துன்புறூஉம் நல்லு யிர்க்கு","மனிதரின் அரிய(து) ஆகும் தோன்றுதல், தோன்றி னாலும்","இனியவை நுகர எய்தும் செல்வமும் அன்ன தேயாம்."],"p":"","r":"For the soul that suffers through very many births, taking one different body after another by the force of its many deeds, birth as a human is rare, and even when it comes, wealth enough to enjoy sweet things is just as rare.","n":"The verse stacks two rarities: the human body and, inside it, the means of enjoyment. It does not yet say what the rare birth is for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","yaakkai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 1","lb":"VP1","ev":"","cl":[["the soul passes through many bodies by the force of its deeds, and human birth among them is rare","general","asserted",3,"unknown_speaker"],["even a human birth rarely brings wealth enough to enjoy","general","asserted",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:2":{"book":"valayapathi","num":3,"t":["உயர்குடி நனியுள் தோன்றல் ஊனமில் யாக்கை யாதல்","மயர்(வு)அறு கல்வி கேள்வித் தன்மையால் வல்ல ராதல்","பெரி(து)உணர் அறிவே யாதல் பேரறம் கோடல் என்றாங்(கு)","அரி(து)இவை பெறுதல் ஏடா பெற்றவர் மக்கள் என்பார்."],"p":"","r":"Birth in a high family, a body without defect, learning and listening that clear away confusion, an understanding that grasps much, and taking up the great dharma are hard to get, the speaker tells a man, and only those who get them are called human.","n":"The address ஏடா shows a speaker talking to one man, but the fragment does not say who either is. 'Human' (மக்கள்) is made a title earned by a list that runs from birth to dharma, so high birth sits beside virtue as a condition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kalvi","arivu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 2","lb":"VP2","ev":"","cl":[["only one who has high birth, a sound body, learning, understanding and dharma deserves the name of human","general","reported",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:3":{"book":"valayapathi","num":4,"t":["நாடும் ஊரும் நனிபுகழ்ந்(து) ஏத்தலும்","பீ(டு)உ றும்மழை பெய்கெனப் பெய்தலும்","கூடல் ஆற்றவர் நல்லது கூறுங்கால்","பாடு சால்மிகு பத்தினிக்(கு) ஆவதே."],"p":"","r":"Praise from country and town, and rain that falls when bidden to fall, belong, when the able men of Koodal speak of the good, to the illustrious chaste wife.","n":"The rain at the wife's word is one honour in a list, beside the praise of country and town, and it is credited to what the learned of Koodal say. Whether கூடல் ஆற்றவர் means an assembly at Madurai or simply learned men is not clear from the line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathini","karpu","mazhai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 3","lb":"VP3","ev":"","cl":[["the chaste wife earns public praise, and rain falls at her bidding","general","asserted",2,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:4":{"book":"valayapathi","num":5,"t":["பள்ள முதுநீர்ப் பழகினும் மீன்இனம்","வெள்ளம் புதியது காணின் விருப்(பு)உறூஉம்","கள்அவிழ் கோதையர் காமனொ(டு) ஆயினும்","உள்ளம் பிறிதாய் உருகலும் கொள்நீ."],"p":"","r":"Fish used to the old water of a pond still long for a new flood when they see one, and so, the speaker tells someone to know, the heart of a garlanded woman turns and melts for another even if she is with the love god himself.","n":"Addressed to one listener (கொள்நீ, 'take it'), so the view belongs to an unknown speaker in a lost scene, not plainly to the poet. 'Even with Kaman' makes the claim independent of the husband's merit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 4","lb":"VP4","ev":"","cl":[["a woman's heart turns to someone new however worthy her man","general","reported",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:5":{"book":"valayapathi","num":6,"t":["உண்டியுள் காப்(பு)உண்(டு) உறுபொருள் காப்புண்டு","கண்ட விழுப்பொருள் கல்விக்குக் காப்புண்டு","பெண்டிரைக் காப்ப(து) இலமென்று ஞாலத்துக்","கண்டு மொழிந்தனர் கற்(று)அறிந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"There is a guard for food, a guard for wealth and a guard for precious learning, but those who have learned and seen the world said that there is no way to guard women.","n":"The speaker cites the learned (கற்றறிந்தோர்) as the authority. The verse puts women in the list of possessions a man keeps, and then denies that this one can be kept by any outward means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 5","lb":"VP5","ev":"","cl":[["women cannot be guarded by any means a man has","general","asserted",3,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:6":{"book":"valayapathi","num":7,"t":["எத்துணை ஆற்றுள் இடுமணல் நீர்த்துளி","புற்பனி உக்க மரத்(து)இலை நுண்மயிர்","அத்துனை யும்பிறர் அஞ்சொலி னார்மனம்","புக்கனம் என்று பொதியறைப் பட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"As many as the grains of sand in rivers, the drops of water, the dew on grass, the leaves fallen from trees and the fine hairs, so many men thought they had entered the hearts of sweet-speaking women and found themselves shut in a closed room.","n":"பொதியறைப் பட்டார் is read here as being trapped in a sealed chamber, that is, deceived; the image could also be of being suffocated. The counting similes make the deceived men beyond number rather than describing a single case.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 6","lb":"VP6","ev":"","cl":[["countless men who believed they had won a woman's heart were deceived","general","asserted",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:7":{"book":"valayapathi","num":8,"t":["தனிப்பெயல் தண்துளி தாமரை யின்மேல்","வளிப்பெறு மாத்திரை நின்றற்(று) ஒருவன்","அளிப்பவன் காணும் சிறுவரை அல்லால்","துளக்கிலர் நில்லார் துணைவனைக் கையார்."],"p":"","r":"Like a single cool raindrop on a lotus that stays only as long as the wind allows, women are steady only for the short while the man who cherishes them is watching; otherwise they waver and do not stay.","n":"The last phrase துணைவனைக் கையார் is hard: it may mean they do not openly spurn their partner, which would make the inconstancy hidden. The raindrop image ties women's steadiness to outward conditions rather than to an inner guard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","nilaiyaamai","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 7","lb":"VP7","ev":"","cl":[["women keep steady only while their man is watching","general","asserted",3,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:8":{"book":"valayapathi","num":9,"t":["பொறையிலா அறிவு போகப் புணர்விலா இளமை மேவத்","துறையிலா வசன வாவி துகிலிலாக் கோலத் தூய்மை","நறையிலா மாலை கல்வி நலமிலாப் புலமை நன்னீர்ச்","சிறையிலா நகரம் போலும் சேயிலாச் செல்வ மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Wealth without a child is like knowledge without patience, youth without pleasure, a tank without steps, finery without cloth, a garland without fragrance, scholarship without learning's goodness, and a town without a moat of good water.","n":"Each item in the list is a thing that exists but lacks what makes it usable or whole; the child is placed as the completion of wealth, not as a good in itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 8","lb":"VP8","ev":"","cl":[["wealth without a child is incomplete","general","asserted",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:9":{"book":"valayapathi","num":10,"t":["ஆக்கப் படுக்கும்; அருந்தளைவாய்ப் பெய்விக்கும்;","போக்கப் படுக்கும்; புலைநகரத்(து) உய்ப்பிக்கும்;","காக்கப் படுவன இந்திரியம் ஐந்தினும்","நாக்(கு)அல்ல(து) இல்லை நனிபேணும் ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"It can raise a man, cast him into hard fetters, ruin him and send him to the vile hell: of the five senses that must be guarded, none needs guarding so much as the tongue.","n":"The tongue is counted among the five senses (இந்திரியம் ஐந்து), so it is both the organ of taste and of speech; the verse does not choose, and புலைநகரம், a hell called 'vile', can carry a hint of flesh.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","naragam","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 9","lb":"VP9","ev":"","cl":[["of the five senses the tongue most needs guarding, since it can raise a man or send him to hell","general","asserted",3,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:10":{"book":"valayapathi","num":11,"t":["தாரம் நல்லிதந் தாங்கித் தலைநின்மின்","ஊரும் நாடும் உவத்தல் ஒருதலை","வீர வென்றி விறல்மிகு விண்ணவர்","சீரின் ஏத்திச் சிறப்(பு)எதிர் கொள்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Hold to your wife and her good and stand firm: town and country will surely rejoice, and the victorious gods will praise you and come to receive you with honour.","n":"The plural command (தலைநின்மின்) addresses a group. தாரம் நல்லிதந் தாங்கி is read as cherishing one's wife; if so, the same praise from town and country that verse 3 gives the chaste wife is here promised to the faithful husband.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","pugazh","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 10","lb":"VP10","ev":"","cl":[["a man who keeps to his wife is praised on earth and welcomed by the gods","general","asserted",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:11":{"book":"valayapathi","num":12,"t":["பெண்ணின் ஆகிய பேரஞர் பூமியுள்","எண்ணம் மிக்கவர் எண்ணினும் எண்ணிலார்","பின்னி நின்ற பெருவினை மேல்வரும்","என்ன(து) ஆயினும் ஏதில்பெண் நீக்குமின்."],"p":"","r":"The great sorrow that comes from women cannot be counted even by the most thoughtful; the heavy, entangling deed rises against one, so whatever happens, keep away from another man's woman.","n":"The verse opens with a charge against women in general and closes on a narrower command about the other man's wife (ஏதில்பெண்); the karma of line 3 is what joins them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","vinai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 11","lb":"VP11","ev":"","cl":[["going to another man's woman brings on heavy deeds; avoid it whatever comes","general","asserted",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:12":{"book":"valayapathi","num":13,"t":["பொய்யன் மின்;புறம் கூறன்மின்; யாரையும்","வையன் மின்;வடி(வு) அல்லன சொல்லிநீர்","உய்யன் மின்;உயிர் கொன்(று)உண்டு வாழும்நாள்","செய்யன் மின்;சிறி யாரொடு தீயன்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Do not lie, do not backbite, revile no one, do not try to escape by saying what is unfitting, do not spend your days killing living beings and eating them, and do no evil in company with base people.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","purangooral","kollaamai","pulaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 12","lb":"VP12","ev":"","cl":[["one must not lie or backbite","general","asserted",1,"unknown_speaker"],["one must not live by killing creatures and eating them","general","asserted",3,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:13":{"book":"valayapathi","num":14,"t":["கள்ளன் மின்,கள(வு) ஆயின யாவையும்;","கொள்ளன் மின்,கொலை கூடி வரும்அறம்;","எள்ளன் மின்,இலர் என்றெண்னி யாரையும்;","நள்ளன் மின்பிறர் பெண்ணொடு நண்ணன்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Steal nothing that is theft; do not accept a dharma that comes joined with killing; scorn no one as poor; and do not befriend or approach another man's woman.","n":"கொலை கூடி வரும் அறம் rejects any dharma that involves killing; the line names no rite or school, though sacrifice is the obvious case.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","kolai","aram","piranil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 13","lb":"VP13","ev":"","cl":[["a dharma that involves killing is to be refused","general","asserted",2,"unknown_speaker"],["one must not despise the poor","general","asserted",3,"unknown_speaker"],["one must not approach another man's woman","general","asserted",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:14":{"book":"valayapathi","num":15,"t":["துற்றள வாகத் தொகுத்து விரல்வைத்த(து)","எற்றுக்(கு)அஃ(து) என்னின் இதுவதன் காரணம்","அற்றமில் தானம் எனைப்பல ஆயினும்","துற்றவிழ் ஒவ்வாத் துணி(வு)என்னும் ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"If one asks why someone gathered food into a single mouthful and set a finger on it, the reason is the conviction that however many flawless gifts there are, none equals a mouthful of rice.","n":"The verse explains a gesture whose scene is lost; who makes it and for whom cannot be told. The point is a ranking of gifts, with the smallest gift of food placed above the rest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","undi","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 14","lb":"VP14","ev":"Someone gathers food into one mouthful and lays a finger on it, and the gesture is explained.","cl":[["no gift equals a mouthful of food given","general","asserted",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:15":{"book":"valayapathi","num":16,"t":["ஆற்று மின்,அருள் ஆருயிர் மாட்டெலாம்;","தூற்று மின்னறந் தோம்நனி துன்னன்மின்","மாற்று மின்கழி மாயமும் மானமும்","போற்று மின்பொரு ளாஇவை கொண்டுநீர்."],"p":"","r":"Show grace to all precious lives, spread dharma, do not draw near to wrong, cast off excess deceit and pride, and keep these as your wealth.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 15","lb":"VP15","ev":"","cl":[["one must show grace to every living being","general","asserted",1,"unknown_speaker"],["these virtues are to be kept as one's wealth","general","asserted",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:16":{"book":"valayapathi","num":17,"t":["பொருளைப் பொருளாப் பொதிந்தோம்பல் செல்லா(து)","அருளைப் பொருளா அறம்செய்தல் வேண்டும்","அருளைப் பொருளா அறம்செய்து வான்கண்","இருளியல் பெய்தாத(து) என்னோ நமரங்காள்."],"p":"","r":"One should not hoard wealth as if it were wealth but do dharma with grace as one's wealth; when one does dharma so, the speaker asks his own people, how could one come to the dark state in the world above?","n":"The last line is read as இருள் இயல்பு எய்தாதது, not reaching the dark state; the address நமரங்காள் shows a speaker talking to a group he counts as his own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","selvam","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 16","lb":"VP16","ev":"","cl":[["grace, not goods, is the wealth to keep, and dharma done with it keeps one from darkness hereafter","general","asserted",2,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்","நமர்"],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:17":{"book":"valayapathi","num":18,"t":["தகா(து)உயிர் கொல்வானின் மிகாமைஇலை பாவம்","அவாவிலையின் உண்பான் புலால்பெருகல் வேண்டும்","புகாவலை விலங்காய்ப் பொருதுபிற ஊன்கொன்(று)","அவாவிலையில் விற்பானு மாண்டருகல் வேண்டும்."],"p":"","r":"No sin exceeds killing a life wrongly; the one who eats at a price his craving sets wants flesh to multiply, and the one who kills other creatures with nets, as a trapper, and sells the flesh at that price also wants more of them to die.","n":"The verse joins buyer and seller through one price set by craving (அவா விலை): the eater's demand and the trapper's trade feed each other. Line 4's மாண்டருகல் is obscure and read loosely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","kolai","avaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 17","lb":"VP17","ev":"","cl":[["no sin is greater than killing a life wrongly","general","asserted",1,"unknown_speaker"],["the eater's craving and the seller's price together drive the killing of animals","general","asserted",2,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:18":{"book":"valayapathi","num":19,"t":["பிறவிக் கடலகத்(து) ஆராய்ந்(து) உணரின்","தெறுவதிற் குற்றம் இல்லார்களும் இல்லை","அறவகை யோரா விடக்கு மிசைவோர்","குறை(வு)இன்றித் தம்சுற்றம் தின்றனர் ஆவர்."],"p":"","r":"Looked at within the ocean of births, no one is free of fault in killing, and those who eat flesh without weighing dharma have eaten their own kin without remainder.","n":"The argument rests on rebirth: every creature has at some birth been one's kin, so flesh is always a relative's. Line 2 is hard; its குற்றம் may stand for சுற்றம் (kin), which would say that no being is not one's kin.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","piravi","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 18","lb":"VP18","ev":"","cl":[["through the round of births every creature has been one's kin, so eating flesh is eating one's kin","general","asserted",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:19":{"book":"valayapathi","num":20,"t":["உயிர்கள் ஓம்புமின் ஊன்விழைந்(து) உண்ணன்மின்","செயிர்கள் நீங்குமின் செற்றம் இகந்(து)ஒரீஇக்","கதிகள் நல்லுருக் கண்டனர் கைதொழு","மதிகள் போல மறுவிலிர் தோன்றுவீர்."],"p":"","r":"Protect living beings, do not eat flesh from craving, shed your faults and give up anger, and you will be born in good states, spotless like the moons that those who see them worship.","n":"கதி, the state of the next birth, is the term Jain and Buddhist works use for the destinations of rebirth; the verse promises a good one rather than release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","vekuli","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 19","lb":"VP19","ev":"","cl":[["one who protects life, refuses flesh and gives up anger is reborn in a good state","general","asserted",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:20":{"book":"valayapathi","num":21,"t":["பொருளடு போகம் புணர்தல் உறினும்","அருளுதல் சான்ற அருந்தவம் செய்ம்மின்","இருளில் கதிச்சென்(று) இனிஇவண் வாரீர்","தெருளுதல் உறினும் தெருண்மின் அதுவே."],"p":"","r":"Even if you are joined to wealth and pleasure, practise the hard penance that is fit for grace; going to the state free of darkness, you will not come back here again; if you would gain clarity, gain it in that.","n":"Release here is reached by one's own penance, with no god granting it, and it is marked by no return (இனி இவண் வாரீர்). The verse speaks to people still in wealth and pleasure, not to monks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","veedu","arul","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 20","lb":"VP20","ev":"","cl":[["hard penance with grace leads to a state from which there is no return to birth","general","asserted",3,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:21":{"book":"valayapathi","num":22,"t":["தவத்தின் மேலுறை தவத்(து)இறை தனக்(கு)அல(து) அரிதே","மயக்கு நீங்குதல் மனமொழி யடுமெயிற் செறிதல்","உவத்தல் கய்தலொ(டு) இலாதுபல் வகைஉயிர்க்(கு) அருளை","நயத்து நீங்குதல் பொருள்தனை அனையதும் அறிநீ."],"p":"","r":"Except for the lord of penance who dwells above penance, it is hard to end delusion, to hold mind and speech with the body, to be without liking and loathing, to have grace for every kind of life, and to give up wealth with longing; know that as well.","n":"The three restraints of mind, speech and body are named together; only the perfected ascetic (தவத்து இறை) is said to achieve them all. Addressed to one listener (அறிநீ).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","adakkam","arul","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 21","lb":"VP21","ev":"","cl":[["only the perfected ascetic fully ends delusion and restrains mind, speech and body","general","reported",1,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:22":{"book":"valayapathi","num":23,"t":["எண்ணின்றி யேதுணியும்; எவ்வழி யானும் ஓடும்;","உள்நின்(று) உருக்கும்; உரவோர்உரை கோடல் இன்றாம்","நண்ணின்றி யேயும்; நயவாரை நயந்து நிற்கும்;","கண்ணின்று காமம் நனிகாமுறு வாரை வீழ்க்கும்."],"p":"","r":"Desire decides without thinking, runs any way it likes, melts one from within, takes no counsel from the wise, comes without closeness, longs for those who do not love, and, standing in the eye, fells those who desire too much.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 22","lb":"VP22","ev":"","cl":[["desire overrides judgment and the counsel of the wise","general","asserted",2,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:23":{"book":"valayapathi","num":24,"t":["சான்றோர் உவர்ப்பத் தனிநின்று பழிப்ப காணார்;","ஆன்(று)ஆங்(கு) அமைந்த குரவர்மொழி கோடல் ஈயார்;","வான்தாங்கி நின்ற புகழ்மாசு படுப்பர்; காமன்","தாந்தாங்கி விட்ட கணைமெய்ப்படும் ஆயி னக்கால்."],"p":"","r":"When the arrow Kama lets fly strikes the body, men do not see the blame they invite standing alone while the wise recoil, will not take the word of their elders, and stain a fame that held up the sky.","n":"The verse judges the lover from outside, by what he loses in others' eyes; the next verse (24) goes on to the madal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pazhi","pugazh","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 23","lb":"VP23","ev":"","cl":[["one struck by desire loses shame, ignores elders and ruins his fame","general","asserted",4,"unknown_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர் தெரியாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:1"},"vp:24":{"book":"valayapathi","num":25,"t":["மாஎன்(று) உரைத்து மடல்ஏறுப மன்று தோறும்;","யஎன்(று) எருக்கின் இணர்சுடுப; புன்மை கொண்டே","பேய்என்(று) எழுந்து பிறர்ஆர்ப்பவும் நிற்ப; காம","நோய்நன்(கு) எழுந்து நனிகாழ்க் கொள்வதாயி னக்கால்."],"p":"","r":"When the sickness of love takes deep root, men ride the palm-stem calling it a horse in every public square, put on erukku clusters, and stand like the possessed while others jeer.","n":"The verse reworks Kuruntokai 17 almost phrase for phrase (மா என... மடல் ஏறுப, எருக்கு, ஆர்ப்ப, காழ்க்கொள்), but recasts the lover's own boast as a symptom: it adds புன்மை (baseness), பேய் (possession) and நோய் (disease). Line 2 is damaged (see source_issues).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamanoy","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 24","lb":"VP24","ev":"","cl":[["love grown deep-rooted drives a man to base public folly","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:25":{"book":"valayapathi","num":26,"t":["நக்கே விலாஇ றுவர்;நாணுவர்; நாணூம் வேண்டார்;","புக்கே கிடப்பர்; கனவும்நினை கையு மேற்பர்;","துற்றூண் மறப்பர்; அழுவர்;நனி துஞ்சல் இல்லார்;","நற்றோள் மிகைபெ ரிதுநாடறி துன்பம் ஆக்கும்."],"p":"","r":"Lovesick men laugh till their sides split, feel shame and then want none, lie where they have crept in, live on dreams and fancies, forget food, weep and cannot sleep; desire for a woman's fine shoulders makes a suffering the whole country knows.","n":"A catalogue of symptoms in the manner of the akam books, but told from outside and without sympathy; the last line's நாடறி துன்பம் turns private longing into public talk (alar).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","naanam","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 25","lb":"VP25","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:26":{"book":"valayapathi","num":27,"t":["அரசொடு நட்டவர் ஆள்ப விருத்தி","அரவொடு நட்டவர் ஆட்டியும் உண்பர்","புரிவலை முன்கைப் புனையிழை நல்லார்","விரகிலர் என்று விடுத்தனர் முன்னே."],"p":"","r":"Those who befriend kings live off their realm and those who befriend snakes make them dance for a living, but of women with bangled forearms the ancients said no art avails, and let them go.","n":"The sense of விரகிலர் (no art, no means) and the subject of விடுத்தனர் are not certain; the verse may instead mean the women themselves are without guile. This is the fragment's view of women, one of several in the chapter (46, 47).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","thuravu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 26","lb":"VP26","ev":"","cl":[["women cannot be managed by skill as kings and snakes can, so the wise renounce them","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:27":{"book":"valayapathi","num":28,"t":["பீடில் செய்திக ளாற்கள விற்பிறர்","வீடில் பல்பொருள் கொண்ட பயனெனக்","கூடிக் காலொடு கைகளைப் பற்றிவைத்(து)","ஓடல் இன்றி உலையக் குறைக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who carry off by stealth many goods from others' houses reap this fruit: they are gathered, held fast by hand and foot, and cut so they cannot run.","n":"The punishment is presented as the theft's own பயன் (fruit), in this life; the verse does not name the king, only the seizing and cutting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","vinai","kol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 27","lb":"VP27","ev":"","cl":[["the fruit of theft is mutilation at the king's hands","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:28":{"book":"valayapathi","num":29,"t":["பொய்யின் நீங்குமின்; பொய்யின்மை பூண்டுகொண்(டு)","ஐயம் இன்றி அறநெறி ஆற்றுமின்;","வைகல் வேதனை வந்(து)உறல் ஒன்(று)இன்றிக்","கௌவை இல்உல(கு) எய்துதல் கண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"Give up lying, take on truthfulness and walk the path of dharma without doubt: those who do are seen to reach a world without daily pain or reproach.","n":"Truth here is a companion of dharma, worn like an ornament (பூண்டு), not the whole of it; கண்டதே ('this is what has been seen') rests the promise on observation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","aram","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 28","lb":"VP28","ev":"","cl":[["truthfulness together with dharma wins a world free of pain","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:29":{"book":"valayapathi","num":30,"t":["கல்வி இன்மையும் கைப்பொருள் போகலும்","நல்லில் செல்லல்க ளால்நலி(வு) உண்மையும்","பொய்யில் பொய்யடு கூடுதற்(கு) ஆகுதல்","ஐயம் இல்லை அதுகடிந்(து) ஓம்புமின்."],"p":"","r":"Lack of learning, loss of wealth in hand, affliction and being drawn into lie after lie surely follow from something the verse calls only 'that'; forbid it and guard yourselves.","n":"அது in line 4 has no antecedent in the verse: the vice was named in a lost context (drink or gambling would fit the list, but the verse does not say). Line 2's நல்லில் செல்லல் is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","inmai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 29","lb":"VP29","ev":"","cl":[["a certain vice brings ignorance, poverty, affliction and habitual lying","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:30":{"book":"valayapathi","num":31,"t":["உல(கு)உடன் விளங்கவுயர் சீர்த்திநிலை கொள்ளின்","நிலையில்கதி நான்கினிடை நின்றுதடு மாறும்","அலகில்துயர் அஞ்சின்உயிர் அஞ்சவரும் வஞ்சக்","கொலைஒழிமின் என்றுநனி கூறினர் அறிந்தார்."],"p":"","r":"If you want lasting fame that lights the world, and fear the boundless pain of reeling among the four states of birth, give up deceitful killing that terrifies living beings: so the knowing have said.","n":"கதி நான்கு, the four states (gods, humans, beasts, hell), is the Jain reckoning of births; the verse rests its rule on the authority of அறிந்தார் rather than stating it in its own voice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","piravi","pugazh","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 30","lb":"VP30","ev":"","cl":[["killing living beings binds one to wander among the four states of birth","jain","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:31":{"book":"valayapathi","num":32,"t":["வெள்ள மறவி விறல்வேந்தர் தீத்தாயம்","கள்வர்என்(று) இவ்வாறிற் கைகரப்பத் தீர்ந்தகலும்","உள்ளில் உறுபொருளை ஒட்டா(து) ஒழிந்தவர்","எள்ளும் பெருந்துயர்நோய் எவ்வ மிகப்பவே."],"p":"","r":"Wealth slips from the hand and departs by flood, by negligence, by mighty kings, by fire, by kinsmen's claims and by thieves; those who give up the wealth in their house without clinging to it pass beyond the scorned grief of great suffering.","n":"The six thieves of wealth (flood, forgetfulness, kings, fire, heirs, robbers) are a list; the last line could also be read as saying those who cling suffer more, so the verse's direction is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 31","lb":"VP31","ev":"","cl":[["wealth is lost in many ways, and those who let it go without clinging escape its grief","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:32":{"book":"valayapathi","num":33,"t":["ஒழிந்த பிறவறன் உண்டென்பார் உட்க","அழிந்து பிறரவாம் வம்பப் பொருளை","இழந்து சிறிதானும் எய்தா(து) ஒழிந்தார்","அழிந்து பெருந்துயர்நோய்க் கல்லாப் பிலரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who let go of the borrowed wealth that perishes and passes to others, and who did not seek even a little of it, the verse appears to say, are free of great suffering.","n":"வம்பப் பொருள் ('wealth that is a stranger's', borrowed) is the key image; line 1 and the close of line 4 do not yield a sure sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","thuravu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 32","lb":"VP32","ev":"","cl":[["those who renounce fleeting wealth escape great suffering","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:33":{"book":"valayapathi","num":34,"t":["இன்மை இளிவாம் உடைமை உயிர்க்(கு)அச்சம்","மன்னல் சிறிதாய் மயக்கம் பெரிதாகிப்","புன்மை உறுக்கும் புரையில் அரும்பொருளைத்","துன்னா(து) ஒழிந்தார் துறவோ விழுமிதே."],"p":"","r":"Poverty is disgrace and possession is fear for one's life; wealth lasts little, deludes much and brings baseness, so the renunciation of those who never went near it is excellent.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","inmai","thuravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 33","lb":"VP33","ev":"","cl":[["both lacking and having wealth bring harm, so renouncing it is best","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:34":{"book":"valayapathi","num":35,"t":["ஈண்டல் அரிதாய்க் கெடுதல் எளிதாகி","நாண்டல் சிறிதாய் நடுக்கம் பலதரூஉம்","மாண்பில் இயற்கை மருவில் அரும்பொருளை","வேண்டா(து) ஒழிந்தார் விறலோ விழுமிதே."],"p":"","r":"Wealth is hard to gather and easy to lose, gives little joy and much trembling, and is ignoble by nature; the strength of those who never wanted it is excellent.","n":"A companion to 33 with the same closing frame (ஒழிந்தார் ... விழுமிதே), turning from renunciation (துறவு) to the strength (விறல்) it takes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 34","lb":"VP34","ev":"","cl":[["not desiring wealth at all is the true strength","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:35":{"book":"valayapathi","num":36,"t":["இல்லெனின் வாழ்க்கையும் இல்லைஉண் டாய்விடின்","கொல்வர் கயவர் கொளப்பட்டும் வீடுவர்","இல்லையுண் டாய்விடின் இம்மை மறுமைக்கும்","புல்லென்று காட்டும் புணர்வது மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Without wealth there is no living, with it base men kill you and the taken die; whether one has it or not, it shows itself worthless for this life and the next and is nothing to hold.","n":"Line 2's கொளப்பட்டும் வீடுவர் is compressed; it may mean the robbed die, or the rich are seized and killed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","inmai","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 35","lb":"VP35","ev":"","cl":[["wealth, had or lacked, is barren for this world and the next","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:36":{"book":"valayapathi","num":37,"t":["வேற்கண் மடவார் விழை(வு)ஒழிய யாம்விழையக்","கோற்கண் நெறிகாட்டக் கொல்கூற்(று) உழையதா","நாற்ப(து) இகந்தாம் நரைத்தூதும் வந்த(து)இனி","நீத்தல் துணிவாம் நிலையா(து) இளமையே."],"p":"","r":"A speaker says that now the spear-eyed young women no longer desire him, the staff shows his road and killing Death is near; past forty, with grey hair come as messenger, he resolves to renounce, for youth does not last.","n":"The motive is mixed: renunciation is chosen when desire has already left him (மடவார் விழைவு ஒழிய). யாம் may be a plural of dignity or a group; the fragment does not say who speaks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","thuravu","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 36","lb":"VP36","ev":"An unnamed speaker, past forty and greying, resolves to renounce.","cl":[["since youth does not last, renunciation should follow when age comes","general","reported",4,"unnamed_speaker"]],"ag":["பெயர்சுட்டாக் கூற்றாளர்"],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர்சுட்டாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:37":{"book":"valayapathi","num":38,"t":["இளமையும் நிலையாவால் இன்பமும் நின்றவல்ல","வளமையும் அஃதேயால் வைகலும் துன்பவெள்ளம்","உளவென நினையாதே செல்கதிக்(கு) என்றும்என்றும்","விளைநிலம் உழுவார்போல் வித்துநீர் செய்துகொண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Youth does not last, pleasures do not stay, wealth is the same and floods of sorrow come daily; so for the state you will go to, always, like farmers who plough, sow and water, make your provision.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","marumai","uzhavu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 37","lb":"VP37","ev":"","cl":[["merit for the next birth must be cultivated continually, as a farmer tends his field","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:38":{"book":"valayapathi","num":39,"t":["மற்றும் தொடர்ப்பா(டு) எவன்கொல் பிறப்(பு)அறுக்கல்","உற்றார்க்(கு) உடம்பு மிகையவை உள்வழிப்","பற்றா வினையாய்ப் பலபல யோனிகள்","அற்றாய் உழலு மறுத்தற் கரிதே."],"p":"","r":"For those set on cutting off birth, even the body is excess, so what further ties; while these remain, karma clings and the soul wanders among many wombs, hard to escape.","n":"Lines 1-2 are Kural 345 almost word for word; lines 3-4 add the reason the couplet leaves unstated, a chain from attachment to karma to யோனிகள் (wombs).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","yaakkai","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 38","lb":"VP38","ev":"","cl":[["as long as body and attachment remain, karma clings and drives the soul through many births","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[["k:345","echo"]],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:39":{"book":"valayapathi","num":40,"t":["உற்ற உதிரம் ஒழிப்பான் கலிங்கத்தை","மற்றது தோய்த்துக் கழுவுதல் என்னொக்கும்","பற்றினான் ஆகிய பாவத்தை மீட்டும்","பற்றொடு நின்று பறைக்(கு)உறும் ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"Trying to drive off sin born of attachment while still standing in attachment is like washing a blood-stained cloth by dipping it in the same blood.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","vinai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 39","lb":"VP39","ev":"","cl":[["sin born of attachment cannot be removed while one keeps attachment","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:40":{"book":"valayapathi","num":41,"t":["தானம் செய்திலம் தவமும் அன்னதே","கானம் தோய்நில விற்கழி வெய்தினம்","நானம் தோய்குழல் நமக்(கு)உய்தல் உண்டோ","மானம்தீர் கொள்கையார் மாற்றம்பொய் அல்லவால்."],"p":"","r":"A speaker laments: we gave no gifts and did no penance, but spent ourselves in moonlit groves; is there escape for us and the musk-haired one? The word of those whose creed has cast off pride is not false.","n":"நானம் தோய்குழல் may be a vocative to a woman or the woman whose love distracted the speaker; the 'pride-free' ones (மானம்தீர் கொள்கையார்) are probably ascetics, whose warning the speaker now accepts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","thavam","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 40","lb":"VP40","ev":"","cl":[["without giving and austerity there is no escape from what follows","general","reported",1,"unnamed_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர்சுட்டாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:41":{"book":"valayapathi","num":42,"t":["பருவந்து சாலப் பலர்கொல்என்(று) எண்ணி","ஒருவந்தம் உள்ளத்(து) உவத்தல் ஒழிமின்","வெருவந்த துன்பம் விடுக்கும் திறலோன்","ஒருவன் உலகிற்(கு) உளன்என்னும் ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"The verse bids the hearers stop some heartfelt rejoicing (lines 1-2 are obscure) and closes on the saying that there is one mighty being in the world who removes fearful suffering.","n":"Whether the closing saying is affirmed (a deliverer such as a Jina) or held up as a false comfort cannot be fixed from the fragment; என்னும் ஆறே suggests it is the second half of a comparison whose first half is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["idumbai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 41","lb":"VP41","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:42":{"book":"valayapathi","num":43,"t":["உய்த்தொன்றி யேர்தந் துழவுழுது ஆற்றவும்","வித்தின்றிப் பைங்கூழ் விளைக்குறல் என்னொக்கும்","யுய்த்தவம் இல்லான் பொருளடு போகங்கட்","கெய்த்துழந் தேதான் இடர்ப்படு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Yoking oxen and ploughing hard yet expecting the green crop without seed is like the man without austerity toiling and suffering for wealth and pleasures.","n":"Austerity is the seed, not the labour: effort without past merit grows nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","selvam","uzhavu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 42","lb":"VP42","ev":"","cl":[["without the seed of austerity, toil for wealth and pleasure yields only suffering","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:43":{"book":"valayapathi","num":44,"t":["செந்நெலங் கரும்பினொ(டு) இகலும் தீஞ்சுவைக்","கன்னலம் கரும்புதான் கமுகைக் காய்ந்தெழும்","இன்னவை காண்கிலன் என்று பூகமும்","முன்னிய முகில்களான் முகம்பு தைக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"In the rich land the sweet sugarcane, rivalling the red paddy, rises in anger at the areca, and the areca, refusing to look, hides its face in the clouds.","n":"A description of fertile country by rivalry of crops, the kind of country-praise that opens the long epics; nothing in the verse ties it to a place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 43","lb":"VP43","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:44":{"book":"valayapathi","num":45,"t":["குலந்தரும் கல்வி கொணர்ந்து முடிக்கும்","அலந்த கிளைகள் அழிபசி தீர்க்கும்","நிலம்பக வெம்பிய நீள்சுரம் போகிப்","புலம்பில் பொருள்தரப் புன்கண்மை உண்டோ."],"p":"","r":"Learning got from the family brings things to completion, wealth relieves the wasting hunger of struggling kin; is there any shame in crossing the long, ground-splitting wasteland to bring it back?","n":"The voice is an arguer for going after wealth, as in the akam books' parting for wealth; the fragment does not say who argues, and its editor's order sets it among verses that condemn wealth (33-35).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pasi","sutram","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 44","lb":"VP44","ev":"","cl":[["seeking wealth across hard country is honourable because it feeds one's kin","general","reported",4,"unnamed_speaker"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"spk":["பெயர்சுட்டாக் கூற்றாளர்",""],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:45":{"book":"valayapathi","num":46,"t":["கெட்டேம் இதுவெந் நிலையென்று சார்தற்கண்","நட்டவர் அல்லார் நனிமிகு பவர்சுற்றம்","பெட்டது சொல்லிப் பெரி(து)இகழ்ந்(து) ஆற்றவும்","எட்டவந் தோர்இடத்(து) ஏகிநிற்பவே."],"p":"","r":"When a ruined man says 'this is my state' and seeks refuge, the many kin who were no true friends say what they please, scorn him and go stand where he cannot reach them.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","kedu","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 45","lb":"VP45","ev":"","cl":[["kin desert and scorn one who is ruined","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:46":{"book":"valayapathi","num":47,"t":["தெண்ணீர் பரந்து திசைதொறும் போய்க்கெட்ட","எண்ணெய்கொண்(டு) ஈட்டற்(கு) இவறுதல் என்ஒக்கும்","பெண்மனம் பேதித்(து) ஒருப்படுப்பென் என்னும்","எண்ணில் ஒருவன் இயல்(பு)எண்ணும் ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"Hoping to unite a woman's divided mind is like longing to gather back oil that has spread on clear water and gone in every direction.","n":"The fragment's view of women; the simile is of something past recall, not merely difficult.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","manam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 46","lb":"VP46","ev":"","cl":[["a woman's divided mind cannot be made single again","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:47":{"book":"valayapathi","num":48,"t":["நீண்முகை கையாற் கிழித்தது மொக்குறு","மாண்வினைப் பாவை மறைநின்று கேட்குறிற்","பேணலும் அன்பும் பிறந்(து)உழிப் பேதுசெய்(து)","ஆணைப்பெண் ணைய வணைக்குறு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Forcing love is like tearing open a long bud by hand; when care and affection are born, a woman, bewildered, embraces the man of herself.","n":"Line 2 (a finely wrought doll standing hidden and listening) does not fit a sure syntax, and the verse may say instead that love comes by stealth, as one overhears from behind a screen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 47","lb":"VP47","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:2"},"vp:48":{"book":"valayapathi","num":49,"t":["அந்தகன் அந்தகற்(கு) ஆறு சொலல்ஒக்கும்","முந்துசெய் குற்றம் கெடுப்பான் முழுவதும்","நன்(கு)அறி(வு) இல்லான் அதுவறி யாதவற்(கு)","இன்புறு வீட்டின் நெறிசொல்லு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Someone without sound knowledge who offers to destroy wholly the faults done before and tells the way to blissful release to one who does not know it is like a blind man telling a blind man the road.","n":"The verse names no school and no teacher; whose rival guide is meant belonged to the lost story. முந்துசெய் குற்றம் (faults done before) makes the false teacher's offer a promise to clear past deeds, so the charge is that he lacks the knowledge such clearing needs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","pedhaimai","arivu","kutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 48","lb":"VP48","ev":"","cl":[["one who lacks true knowledge cannot guide another to release, however much he promises to remove past faults","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:49":{"book":"valayapathi","num":50,"t":["யாறொடி யாழ்ஞெலி கோனில வார்கொடிப்","பாறொடு பத்தினி மாபோல் ஒழுகென்று","கூறினள் கூத்தி முதிர்ந்தாள் மகட்கிவை","வேறோர் இடத்து வெளிப்பட நன்றாம்."],"p":"","r":"The aged dancer-courtesan tells her daughter to behave like the river, the lute, the fire-drill, the moon, the climbing creeper, the ship, the chaste wife and the beast, and the verse says these are best shown in another place.","n":"Line 4 promises that the comparisons will be opened out elsewhere, and verses 51-60 do so in the order of this list (river 51, lute 52, fire-drill 53, moon 54, creeper 55, ship 56, chaste wife 57, beast 58), with 50's monkey and bee in 59 and 60. The chaste wife stands in a list of things and animals: that placement is the satire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","pathini","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 49","lb":"VP49","ev":"An aged dancer-courtesan instructs her daughter to conduct herself like a list of things, among them the chaste wife.","cl":[],"ag":["கூத்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:50":{"book":"valayapathi","num":51,"t":["ஆய்குரங் கஞ்சிறை வண்டினம் போல்கென்று","பாயிரம் இன்றிப் பயிற்றி மொழிந்தனள்","மேவரும் வான்பொருள் தந்துநின் தோணம்பி","யாரவர் அடைந்தவர்க் கவையும் புரைப."],"p":"","r":"Without preface she adds the monkey and the bright-winged bee as models, and the verse seems to say that these too fit those who come trusting the daughter's shoulders and bringing great wealth.","n":"பாயிரம் இன்றி, without preface, may mean she taught bluntly, with no introduction. நின் in line 3 addresses the daughter, so lines 3-4 may still be the mother's words; the syntax of line 4 is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 50","lb":"VP50","ev":"The old courtesan adds the monkey and the bee to her list of models for her daughter.","cl":[],"ag":["கூத்தி"],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:51":{"book":"valayapathi","num":52,"t":["வாரி பெருகப் பெருகிய காதலை","வாரி சுருங்கச் சுருங்கி விடுதலின்","மாரி பெருகப் பெருகி அறவறும்","வார்புனல் ஆற்றின் வகையும் புரைப."],"p":"","r":"Courtesans are like a river: their love swells as the inflow of wealth swells and shrinks as it shrinks, as the stream rises with the rain and dries away completely.","n":"வாரி serves twice: the flood of a river and the inflow of income. One of the ten glosses (51-60) on the similes the old courtesan lists in 49-50; the gloss follows her list in order. Who speaks the glosses is not stated: they describe courtesans in the third person, so they may be the poet's or the mother's continued teaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 51","lb":"VP51","ev":"","cl":[["a courtesan's love rises and falls exactly with the lover's wealth","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:52":{"book":"valayapathi","num":53,"t":["எங்ஙனம் ஆகிய(து) இப்பொருள் அப்பொருட்(கு)","அங்ஙனம் ஆகிய அன்பினர் ஆதலின்","எங்ஙனம் பட்டனன் பாண்மகன் பாண்மகற்(கு)","அங்ஙனம் ஆகிய யாழும் புரைப."],"p":"","r":"Courtesans are like the lute: whoever holds the wealth, their love is suited to him, as a lute plays for whatever bard takes it up.","n":"The repeated எங்ஙனம் ... அங்ஙனம் (however ... so) makes the love a function of the wealth, whatever its source. One of the ten glosses (51-60) on the similes the old courtesan lists in 49-50; the gloss follows her list in order. Who speaks the glosses is not stated: they describe courtesans in the third person, so they may be the poet's or the mother's continued teaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 52","lb":"VP52","ev":"","cl":[["a courtesan's love belongs to whoever holds the wealth, not to a person","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:53":{"book":"valayapathi","num":54,"t":["கரணம் பலசெய்து கையுற்(று) அவர்கட்","கரண மெனுமிலர் ஆற்றிற் கலந்து","திரணி உபாயத்திற் றிரண்பொருள் கோடற்","கரணி ஞெலிகோல் அமைவர ஒப்ப."],"p":"","r":"Courtesans are like the fire-drill: by many arts and devices, once men are in their hands, they draw wealth out of them as the drill-stick turned in the hands draws fire.","n":"The image is friction: the stick yields fire only while it is worked. Line 2 (கரணம் எனும் இலர்) and line 3's word division are hard, so the exact sense of who lacks means is not certain. One of the ten glosses (51-60) on the similes the old courtesan lists in 49-50; the gloss follows her list in order. Who speaks the glosses is not stated: they describe courtesans in the third person, so they may be the poet's or the mother's continued teaching.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 53","lb":"VP53","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:54":{"book":"valayapathi","num":55,"t":["நாடொறும் நாடொறும் நந்திய காதலை","நாடொறும் நாடொறும் நைய ஒழுகலின்","நாடொறும் நாடொறும் நந்தி உயர்(வு)எய்தி","நாடொறும் தேயும் நகைமதி ஒப்ப."],"p":"","r":"Courtesans are like the bright moon: the love that grows day by day they let waste day by day, as the moon waxes day by day and wanes day by day.","n":"Each line opens with நாடொறும் நாடொறும், so the verse's own form waxes and wanes in a steady rhythm. One of the ten glosses (51-60) on the similes the old courtesan lists in 49-50; the gloss follows her list in order. Who speaks the glosses is not stated: they describe courtesans in the third person, so they may be the poet's or the mother's continued teaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","nilaiyaamai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 54","lb":"VP54","ev":"","cl":[["a courtesan's love is made to wane as surely as it was made to grow","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:55":{"book":"valayapathi","num":56,"t":["வனப்பிலர் ஆயினும் வன்மையி லோரை","நினைத்தவர் மேவர நிற்பமைக் காவர்தாம்","கனைத்துடன் வண்டொடு தேனினம் ஆர்ப்பப்","புனத்திடைப் பூத்த பூங்கொடி ஒப்ப,"],"p":"","r":"Courtesans are like a flowering creeper in the field, humming with bees: even men without beauty, if they are not without means, they cling to and stand by as the creeper takes whatever support it finds.","n":"The reading of வன்மையிலோரை and of line 2 is not certain; the sense that looks or worth do not matter, only means, is the likeliest. One of the ten glosses (51-60) on the similes the old courtesan lists in 49-50; the gloss follows her list in order. Who speaks the glosses is not stated: they describe courtesans in the third person, so they may be the poet's or the mother's continued teaching.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 55","lb":"VP55","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:56":{"book":"valayapathi","num":57,"t":["தங்கண் பிறந்த கழிஅன்பி னார்களை","வண்கண்மை செய்து வலிய விடுதலின்","இன்பொருள் ஏற்றி எழநின்ற வாணிகர்க்(கு)","அங்கண் பரப்பகத் தாழ்கல மொப்ப."],"p":"","r":"Courtesans are like a ship that sinks in the open sea with the merchants who loaded it with fine goods: they harshly cast off those whose love for them has grown excessive.","n":"The merchants have staked everything on the ship, so the image puts the ruin on the side of the most devoted. One of the ten glosses (51-60) on the similes the old courtesan lists in 49-50; the gloss follows her list in order. Who speaks the glosses is not stated: they describe courtesans in the third person, so they may be the poet's or the mother's continued teaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 56","lb":"VP56","ev":"","cl":[["a courtesan ruins those who trust her most, as a ship drowns those who load it","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:57":{"book":"valayapathi","num":58,"t":["ஒத்த பொருளான் உறுதிசெய் வார்களை","எத்திறத் தானும் வழிபட்(டு) ஒழுகலின்","பைத்தர அல்குல்பொற் பாவையி னல்லவர்","பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர் படியும் புரைப."],"p":"","r":"Courtesans resemble chaste wives in conduct: toward men who give them wealth enough, they serve and follow in every way.","n":"The verse grants the courtesan the outward form of the பத்தினி's devotion (வழிபட்டு ஒழுகல்) and puts the whole difference in ஒத்த பொருளான், the condition of matching wealth. One of the ten glosses (51-60) on the similes the old courtesan lists in 49-50; the gloss follows her list in order. Who speaks the glosses is not stated: they describe courtesans in the third person, so they may be the poet's or the mother's continued teaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","pathini","karpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 57","lb":"VP57","ev":"","cl":[["a courtesan's devoted service imitates the chaste wife's but follows the payment","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:58":{"book":"valayapathi","num":59,"t":["வீபொரு ளனை அகன்று பிறனுமோர்","மாபொரு ளான்பக்கம் மாண நயத்தலின்","மேய்புலம் புல்லற மற்றோற் புலம்புகு","மாவும் புரைப மலரன்ன கண்ணார்."],"p":"","r":"Flower-eyed courtesans are like a grazing beast: leaving the man whose wealth has perished, they fix their desire on another man of great wealth, as a beast whose pasture is bare moves into another's field.","n":"One of the ten glosses (51-60) on the similes the old courtesan lists in 49-50; the gloss follows her list in order. Who speaks the glosses is not stated: they describe courtesans in the third person, so they may be the poet's or the mother's continued teaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 58","lb":"VP58","ev":"","cl":[["a courtesan leaves a man when his wealth is gone and moves to a richer one","general","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:59":{"book":"valayapathi","num":60,"t":["நுண்பொரு ளானை நுகர்ந்திட்டு வான்பொருள்","நன்குடை யானை நயந்தனர் கோடலின்","வம்பிள மென்முலை வாள்நெடுங் கண்ணவர்","கொம்பிடை வாழுங் குரங்கும் புரைப."],"p":"","r":"Courtesans are like the monkey that lives in the branches: having used up the man of little wealth, they take the man of great wealth, as a monkey leaps from branch to branch.","n":"The gloss on the monkey added in 50. One of the ten glosses (51-60) on the similes the old courtesan lists in 49-50; the gloss follows her list in order. Who speaks the glosses is not stated: they describe courtesans in the third person, so they may be the poet's or the mother's continued teaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 59","lb":"VP59","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:60":{"book":"valayapathi","num":61,"t":["முருக்(கு)அலர் போற்சிவந்(து) ஒள்ளிய ரேனும்","பருக்கர டில்லவர் பக்கம் நினையார்","அருப்பிள மென்முலை அம்சொ லவர்தாம்","வரிச்சிறை வண்டின் வகையும் புரைப."],"p":"","r":"Courtesans are like the striped-winged bee: however bright and handsome a man may be, like the red flame-of-the-forest flower, they give no thought to one without substance.","n":"The bee passes over the bright முருக்கு blossom because it holds little honey, so beauty without wealth draws no courtesan. பருக்கரடு in line 2 is obscure, and the reading of 'substance' as wealth rests on the series. One of the ten glosses (51-60) on the similes the old courtesan lists in 49-50; the gloss follows her list in order. Who speaks the glosses is not stated: they describe courtesans in the third person, so they may be the poet's or the mother's continued teaching.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","inmai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 60","lb":"VP60","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:61":{"book":"valayapathi","num":62,"t":["மக்க்ள் பயந்து மனையறம் ஆற்றுதல்","தக்க(து) அறிந்தார் தலைமைக் குணமென்ப","பைத்(து)அர(வு) அல்குல் படிற்(று)உரை யாரொடு","துய்த்துக் கழிப்பது தோற்றமொன்(று) இன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Those who know call begetting children and keeping the household's dharma the chief virtue; spending one's life in pleasure with deceitful-tongued women yields nothing.","n":"The verse is attributed to 'those who know' (என்ப), not to the poet. தோற்றம் ஒன்று இன்று may mean no issue at all, against the children of line 1, or simply no good result. A Jain epic by tradition here praises the householder's life, not renunciation; the fragment does not say whether a higher path was set above it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","makkal","varaivinmagalir","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 61","lb":"VP61","ev":"","cl":[["begetting children and keeping household dharma is the highest virtue","general","asserted",2,"narrator"],["a life spent in pleasure with courtesans produces nothing","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:62":{"book":"valayapathi","num":63,"t":["நகைநனி தீது துனிநன்றி யார்க்கும்","பகைநனி தீது பணிந்தீ யாரோடும்","இவைமிகு பொருளென்(று) இறத்தல் இலரே","வகைமிகு வானுல(கு) எய்திவாழ் பவரே."],"p":"","r":"The verse calls mockery very bad and enmity very bad, even, it seems, toward those who do not bow and give, and says those who never transgress, holding these as the weightiest matters, reach the many-tiered heaven and live there.","n":"Line 1's division (துனி நன்றி யார்க்கும், or துனி நன்று இயார்க்கும்) is unclear, so whether sulking is praised, blamed or left out cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","igal","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 62","lb":"VP62","ev":"","cl":[["those who keep from mockery and enmity reach heaven","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:63":{"book":"valayapathi","num":64,"t":["பெண்டிர் மதியார் பெருங்கிளை தானது","கொண்ட விரகர் குறிப்பினின் அஃகுப","வெண்டறை நின்று வெறுக்கை இலராயின்","மண்டினர் போவர்தம் மக்களும் ஒட்டார்."],"p":"","r":"If a man stands in an empty house without wealth, his women will not respect him, his great kin will shrink back at the hints of cunning men, they will all crowd off and leave, and even his own children will not cling to him.","n":"வெண்டறை (bare floor) and the sense of line 2 are uncertain; line 2 also carries a repaired character.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inmai","sutram","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 63","lb":"VP63","ev":"","cl":[["without wealth a man is forsaken by wife, kin and even his children","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:64":{"book":"valayapathi","num":65,"t":["சொல்லவை சொல்லார் சுருங்குபு சூழ்ந்துணர்","நல்லவை யாரும் நன்மதிப் பாரல்லர்","கல்வியும் கைப்பொருள் இல்லார் பயிற்றிய","புல்லென்று போதலை மெய்யென்று கொள்நீ."],"p":"","r":"The poor do not speak in the assembly, the good who weigh things finely do not esteem them, and the learning the poor teach fades away dull: take this as truth.","n":"The addressee, நீ in line 4, is unnamed. The failure is laid on the hearers too: even the நல்லவை, the assembly of the good, discounts the poor man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kalvi","avai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 64","lb":"VP64","ev":"","cl":[["poverty makes even true learning go unvalued","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:65":{"book":"valayapathi","num":66,"t":["தொழுமகன் ஆயினும் துற்றுடை யானைப்","பழுமரம் சூழ்ந்த பறவையின் சூழ்ப","விழுமிய ரேனும் வெறுக்கை உலந்தால்","பழுமரம் வீழ்ந்த பறவையின் போப."],"p":"","r":"Even a servant, if he has food to give, is surrounded as birds surround a fruiting tree; even the noble, once their wealth is spent, are left as birds leave a fruit tree that has fallen.","n":"The two halves turn on one image, the fruit tree standing and fallen, and they reverse rank: the servant (தொழுமகன்) is courted, the noble (விழுமியர்) abandoned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","inmai","sutram","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 65","lb":"VP65","ev":"","cl":[["people gather round wealth and leave when it is gone, whatever a man's birth","general","asserted",3,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:66":{"book":"valayapathi","num":67,"t":["பொருள்இல் குலனும் பொறைமைஇல் நோன்பும்","அருள்இல் அறனும் அமைச்(சு)இல் அரசும்","இருளினுள் இட்ட இருண்மையி தென்றே","மருள்இல் புலவர் மனம்கொண்(டு) உரைப்ப."],"p":"","r":"A lineage without wealth, a vow without forbearance, dharma without grace and a kingdom without ministers: the undeluded poets declare these darkness put inside darkness.","n":"Each good is paired with the condition without which it fails. The verse speaks through மருள் இல் புலவர், the undeluded poets, so it presents itself as received wisdom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","nonbu","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 66","lb":"VP66","ev":"","cl":[["dharma without grace, a vow without forbearance, lineage without wealth and kingship without ministers are void","general","asserted",2,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:67":{"book":"valayapathi","num":68,"t":["துக்கந் துடைக்குந் துகளறு காட்சிய","நிக்கந்த வேடத்(து) இருடி கணங்களை","ஒக்க அடிவீழ்ந்(து) உலகியல் செய்தபின்","அக்கதை யாழ்கொண்(டு) அமைவரப் பண்ணி."],"p":"","r":"Having fallen at the feet of the hosts of sages in the Nirgrantha garb, whose faultless vision wipes away sorrow, and having done the customary courtesies, he set that story to the lute.","n":"நிக்கந்த வேடம் names the Jain monks' garb outright, the clearest Jain mark in these fragments. Who the player is and what அக்கதை (that story) was belonged to the lost context; the verse survives in a commentary on the Silappathikaram.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","panivu","thuravu","isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 67","lb":"VP67","ev":"A lute player bows at the feet of the Jain sages, pays his courtesies, and sets the story to the lute.","cl":[["the faultless vision of the Nirgrantha sages removes sorrow","jain","asserted",1,"narrator"]],"ag":["யாழோன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:68":{"book":"valayapathi","num":69,"t":["பண்ணாற் றிறத்திற் பழுதின்றி மேம்பட்ட","தொண்ணூற்(று) அறுவகைக் கோவையும் வல்லவன்","விண்ணா(று) இயங்கும் விறலவர் ஆயினும்","கண்ணாறி நோக்கிக் கடுநகை செய்வான்."],"p":"","r":"He was a master of all ninety-six kinds of musical sequence, faultless and pre-eminent in melody and mode, and even if those who travel the sky-path were his hearers he would look at them askance and laugh harshly.","n":"The editor places this after 67, but nothing shows it is the same player. The harsh laugh at even the sky-travelling beings marks a pride the verse states without judging it here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","agandhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 68","lb":"VP68","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":["வல்லவன்"],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:69":{"book":"valayapathi","num":70,"t":["அன்றைப் பகற்கழிந் தாளின் றிராப்பகற்","கன்றின் குரலும் கறவை மணிகறங்கக்","கொன்றைப் பழக்குழற் கோவலர் ஆம்பலு","மொன்றல் சுரும்பு நரம்பென ஆர்ப்பவும்."],"p":"","r":"When the day had passed, the calves' cries and the milch cows' bells sounded, and the herdsmen's flutes of konrai pod and ambal, with the bees humming like strings, filled the air.","n":"Pastoral sound is drawn as an orchestra: flutes and bees' hum as strings. The sentence is incomplete (ஆர்ப்பவும், 'and as they sounded'), and line 1's இன்று இராப்பகல் is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["isai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 69","lb":"VP69","ev":"","cl":[],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:70":{"book":"valayapathi","num":71,"t":["நீல நிறத்தனவாய் நெய்கனிந்து போதவிழ்ந்து","கோலம் குயின்ற குழல்வாழி நெஞ்சே","கோலம் குயின்ற குழலும் கொழுஞ்சிகையும்","காலக் கனலெரியின் வேம்வாழி நெஞ்சே","காலக் கனலெரியின் வேவன கண்டாலும்","சால மயங்குவ(து) என்வாழி நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells his heart that the dark, oiled, flower-decked, carefully dressed hair and the rich tuft will burn in the fire of time, and asks why, even seeing them burn, it is still so bewildered.","n":"Each line hands its phrase on to the next (கோலம் குயின்ற குழல், காலக் கனலெரியின்), so the verse moves step by step from adornment to the pyre. The heart has already seen the burning: its fault is not ignorance but bewilderment that survives the sight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","mayal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 70","lb":"VP70","ev":"","cl":[["the adorned body will burn in the fire of time","general","asserted",4,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"},"vp:71":{"book":"valayapathi","num":72,"t":["வித்தகர் செய்த விளங்கு முடிகவித்தார்","மத்தக மாண்பழிதல் காண்வாழி நெஞ்சே","மத்தக மாண்பழிதல் கண்டால் மயங்காதே","உத்தம நன்னெறிக்கண் நில்வாழி நெஞ்சே","உத்தம நன்னெறிக்கண் நின்(று)ஊக்கஞ் செய்தியேற்","சித்தி படர்தல் தெளிவாழி நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker bids his heart see the glory of heads crowned with craftsmen's bright crowns come to ruin, not to be bewildered at it, to stand in the highest good path, and to be sure that if it strives there, attainment will follow.","n":"சித்தி is the Jain word for the released state, and the companion of 70 moves from diagnosis to remedy; but the verse names no school and no god, so the claim is tagged general. The remedy is the heart's own effort (ஊக்கம்), with no refuge in another named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arasu","siththi","ookkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Valayapathi 71","lb":"VP71","ev":"","cl":[["one who stands in the highest path and strives will reach attainment","general","asserted",6,"narrator"]],"ag":[],"ec":[],"ch":"vp:3"}},"links":[{"a":"cc:1","b":"si:10:170-191","type":"qualifies","note":"Both name the Jain god by his qualities: the Cintamani's ஓவாது நின்ற குணத்து and தேவாதி தேவன், the Charana's litany with குணவதன், குணப்பெருங் கோமான், தேவன், and also சங்கரன் ஈசன் சயம்பு.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Silappathikaram's Charana absorbs Saiva and Vedic titles into the Arhat's praise, while the Cintamani's invocation keeps to self-won bliss and qualities, so two Jain epics show two strategies, inclusive litany and bare definition.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:2","b":"si:10:192-213","type":"same_image","note":"The thousand and eight and the feet on the head recur: Kavunthi will speak only the ஆயிரத்து எட்டு நாமம் and bear only the flower feet on her head; the Cintamani's god bears ஆயிரத்து எட்டு marks and the poet sets his lotus feet on his head.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The number 1008 is a fixed token of Arhat worship that one epic attaches to names spoken and the other to marks on the body, while the feet-on-the-head gesture stays the same in a nun's vow and a poet's invocation."},{"a":"cc:4","b":"nk:6","type":"same_image","note":"Both apologies use the moon's spot for the poet's flaw: the Neelakesi's poet will own a fault since even the moon is not spotless; the Cintamani's asks the learned to wash the spot away with their wit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two Jain poets put the same flaw in different hands: the Neelakesi's poet takes it on himself, while the Cintamani's poet makes cleaning it the learned reader's task."},{"a":"cc:4","b":"k:957","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural uses the spot on the moon to say a fault in the well-born is seen from far; the Cintamani uses it for a fault in a moon-full work and asks readers to wash it, not to see it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's moon makes height expose a fault, but the Cintamani turns the same image into a reader's duty of charity, so the exposure the Kural warns of becomes something the audience may decline.","gn":["the poet's request is seen to answer the expectation, stated in the Kural, that a high thing's fault draws every eye","the couplet's warning is shown to depend on the onlooker, who can be asked to clean rather than point"]},{"a":"cc:5","b":"n:133","type":"same_image","note":"Both prize what brackish ground or brine produces: the Naladiyar's salt above rice from good fields, the Cintamani's coral, conch and pearl though they taste of the sea.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar argues that a person's low origin does not lower his learning, and the Cintamani applies the same reasoning to a poem's faulty diction, so the brine image carries one argument about worth and origin from persons to texts.","gn":["the apology is seen resting on an argument the ethics books had already made about rank","the quatrain's claim about birth is shown extending to the standing of a work and its words"]},{"a":"cc:7","b":"cc:9","type":"tension","note":"Verse 7 praises Saccandan's கோடாத செங்கோல் and unfading victory; verse 9 has the same king, sunk in desire and deaf to counsel, lose the kingdom to a plot.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The patikam builds the epic's first lesson from its own praise: the unbending sceptre and unfading victory are undone not by a stronger enemy but by the king's private desire."},{"a":"cc:9","b":"k:448","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says a king with no one to rebuke him perishes though he has no enemy; Saccandan had counsel (சொல்லாறு) and would not hear it.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Cintamani's king shows that having rebuking counsellors is not enough, since desire can make a king deaf to them, so the Kural's safeguard fails at the point of hearing rather than of supply.","gn":["the king's fall is given a named failure of rule, not only a lover's weakness","the couplet's guard is shown to need a king who listens, which the couplet takes for granted"]},{"a":"cc:9","b":"k:901","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says those who set their desire on the home do not reach the fine fruit; the Cintamani's whole frame begins with a king whose desire for his queen grew thick and cost him his throne.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"Held against the Kural's warning about desire fixed on the wife, the patikam locates the whole fault in the husband's excess while calling the queen's chastity unfailing (வீடாத கற்பு), and gives the couplet's lost 'fine fruit' a political price, the kingdom itself.","gn":["the king's fall, told without any doctrine named, gains the Kural's category of being led by desire for the wife as its ethical name","the couplet's unnamed 'fine fruit' is given a concrete price: a throne and, in the story, a life"]},{"a":"cc:14","b":"cc:15","type":"develops","note":"The nectar given to the dying dog in verse 14 and the god who comes down to carry Civakan out of prison in verse 15 are, in the story, one being.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The patikam sets a small mercy and a great rescue one verse apart without naming their link, so the reader learns the epic's working of deeds by noticing that the rescuer is the dog."},{"a":"cc:14","b":"k:102","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural weighs help given at the moment of need as greater than the world; Civakan's gift comes at a dying dog's last moment and is repaid by a god at his own.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural measures timely help by its worth to the one helped, while the Cintamani shows it returned across species and births, so the couplet's weight becomes a debt that the receiver carries into a new life.","gn":["the dog episode gains the Kural's measure of why so small a gift counts","the couplet's timely help is shown being repaid in kind at the giver's own hour of need"]},{"a":"cc:16","b":"cc:14","type":"develops","note":"Verse 14 has Civakan give nectar to a dog dying in the bathing pool; verse 16 has a god free him, arm him with spells and ask to be remembered.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside each other, the dying dog's gift and the god's rescue show the patikam placing a kindness and its return two verses apart, leaving the reader to see the debt repaid before the story names the god."},{"a":"cc:18","b":"cc:16","type":"develops","note":"The spells the god gives in verse 16 are followed by the cure of a snakebite in verse 18, given only as a word of comfort.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam never says how Civakan cures the princess, but read after verse 16 the cure is the god's gift at work, so his first marriage is won by the rescue, not by his own arms."},{"a":"cc:23","b":"cc:10","type":"develops","note":"Verse 10 has the sword-eyed mother keep a vow that her son may live; in verse 23 the grown son, with brother and friends, comes to bow before her.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The mother's austerity, kept apart from her son, is answered when he returns alive to bow to her, and the patikam places that homecoming just before he turns to win back the kingdom she lost."},{"a":"cc:25","b":"cc:13","type":"tension","note":"Verse 13 has Suramanjari, shamed over the scented powder, refuse to look on any male form and shut herself away; verse 25 has her lower her banner.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read together, the two verses frame Suramanjari's vow against men as a challenge the plot exists to undo, so her yielding is told as a conquest like the boar and the enemy in the same verse."},{"a":"cc:25","b":"cc:9","type":"develops","note":"Verse 9 tells how the king, sunk in desire and deaf to counsel, fell to a plot while the queen fled to the burning ground; verse 25 has the son kill the enemy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The usurpation that verse 9 blames on the father's own desire and deafness to counsel is settled only by the son's killing, so the father's fault and the son's revenge frame the whole plot."},{"a":"cc:27","b":"cc:9","type":"tension","note":"Verse 9 has Saccandan, steeped in desire, not hear counsel (சொல்லாறு கேளான்) and lose his throne; verse 27 has his son hear the dharma (அறம் கேட்டு) and give his throne up.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The patikam opens on a king ruined because he would not listen and closes on his son freed because he did, so hearing becomes the hinge between losing a kingdom and leaving one."},{"a":"cc:27","b":"mm:25:115-139","type":"tension","note":"Civakan renounces kingship at its height after hearing the dharma; in the Manimekalai the minister tells the king who wants to leave his throne that the Buddha's dharma, which protects others' lives, is not that.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two epics weigh the same act differently: the Cintamani's patikam praises a just king's leaving the throne once he hears the dharma, while the Manimekalai has a minister argue that a king who abandons the people he guards departs from the Buddha's dharma."},{"a":"cc:27","b":"n:53","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar's best of men renounce house, youth, wealth and strength because they see these do not last; Civakan renounces in full possession of all of them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain grounds renunciation in seeing that goods pass away, while the epic's king renounces while every good still stands and gives fear of kingship, roused by the dharma, as his motive, so impermanence is not the only road to leaving.","gn":["Civakan's sudden turn is set against the standard ethics-book reason, showing that his motive is moral fear of rule rather than loss","the quatrain's list of passing goods is tested on a case where none of them has yet failed"]},{"a":"cc:28","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"The Kural asks what one who keeps household life in dharma could gain by going the outer way; in the Cintamani the whole household, brother, friends and wives, goes that way and reaches a higher state.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's question assumes a householder could gain nothing more by leaving, but the epic answers it by having a just king's whole household leave together and arrive at the state that is ever new.","gn":["the epic's mass renunciation is read as an answer to a Tamil defence of household life, not only as story","the couplet's rhetorical question is shown to be contestable from inside Tamil literature, by a narrative that claims a gain beyond the householder's"]},{"a":"cc:29","b":"k:1103","type":"tension","note":"The Kural's lover asks whether the lotus-eyed one's world can be sweeter than sleep in the beloved's soft arms; Civakan, having tasted a nectar-like bliss, no longer wants the pleasure of women whose beauty makes the heavens bow.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The lover ranks the beloved's arms above heaven, while the epic's hero, having tasted a higher bliss, finds women's embrace unwanted, so the two verses make opposite rankings of the same two goods.","gn":["the patikam's closing simile gains an opponent: it reads as a rebuttal of the lover's ranking rather than a bare statement","the couplet's open question gets an answer from a renouncer, showing what the lover's ranking denies"]},{"a":"cc:29","b":"cc:1","type":"develops","note":"Verse 1 praises the god of gods who reached by himself (தன்னின் எய்தி) the supreme bliss that does not fail; verse 29 ends the patikam with Civakan in a flood of bliss (இன்ப வெள்ளம்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam opens on a god who won unfailing bliss by his own effort and closes on a man who reaches a flood of bliss, so the hero's whole story is framed as walking the road the worshipped one walked, not as receiving a gift from him."},{"a":"cc:29","b":"s:337","type":"qualifies","note":"Pattinathar asks what breasts, union with women and gathered wealth can do against the coming struggle, and says the god put an eyeless needle in his hand; Civakan stops wanting women's pleasure because he has tasted a greater sweetness.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both rich men turn from women and wealth, but the Siddhar's ground is their uselessness before death and a sign from Siva, while the epic's is a higher bliss already tasted, so the same turn can be told as a loss seen through or as a lesser pleasure outgrown.","gn":["the epic's sweet simile is set against a bitter Saiva account of the same turn, showing that the Cintamani makes renunciation a gain of pleasure, not a flight from it","the Siddhar's turn, which rests on death and a god's token, is shown to be one motive among others, since a Jain hero leaves the same goods with no god sending a sign"]},{"a":"cc:32","b":"k:17","type":"develops","note":"Kural 17 states that the sea itself depends on the cloud returning what it took; verses 32 to 38 narrate that loop, from clouds grazing the sea to the flood reaching the sea again.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the couplet's abstract dependence becomes a full itinerary, and the epic's long description gains its shape: it is a circuit of giving back, not a string of pictures.","gn":["a reason for its sea-to-sea ordering: the run enacts a return the couplet says the world depends on","the intermediate stages the couplet skips: mountain, falls, sluices and fields through which the returned water passes"]},{"a":"cc:36","b":"k:215","type":"develops","note":"The Kural likens the wise man's wealth to water filling the village tank; the Cintamani likens the river's flood to the hand of a generous man carrying wealth to those who have nothing.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The tradition's image ran both ways, wealth explained by water and water by giving, and the epic adds movement and direction toward the needy, where the Kural's tank waits to be drawn from.","gn":["a moral precedent that makes the river's simile a statement about how wealth should behave","an active counterpart to its still tank: wealth that goes out town by town to the needy instead of waiting to be drawn"]},{"a":"cc:53","b":"k:978","type":"develops","note":"Kural 978 opposes greatness that bows to smallness that admires itself; the paddy simile sets the base man's erect wealth and the learned man's bow in one stalk at two ages.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that the bow and the raised head can be stages of one thing rather than two kinds of people: what stands erect is empty, and the same stalk bows once it is full.","gn":["a general maxim that its farm image is illustrating, which lifts the last verse of the chapter into a lesson","a picture of how the change from pride to humility happens, by ripening, which the couplet's fixed opposition does not show"]},{"a":"cc:45","b":"k:1033","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1033 says the ploughman alone lives on his own, while all others bow (தொழுது) to eat; in the Cintamani the sower himself bows (தொழுது) to the seed as he sows.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The ploughman whom the Kural sets above all bowing still bows, to the crop on which his independence rests, so his self-sufficiency is itself a dependence.","gn":["a sense of why its farmers' gesture matters: they are the ones the Kural says need bow to no one","a limit: the independence it praises rests on a seed the ploughman must greet with folded hands"]},{"a":"cc:44","b":"si:10:84-97","type":"qualifies","note":"Both epics walk through rich wet fields; the Silappathikaram's Jain nun warns that a careless step may hurt crab and snail, while the Cintamani's narrator delights as the ploughing herds scatter the fish.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The ahimsa scruple that a Jain nun in one epic applies to every footstep in the fields is absent from the Jain poet's own celebration of ploughing, so the epics keep that scruple for the ascetic and not for the land's prosperity."},{"a":"cc:77","b":"k:731","type":"develops","note":"The Kural defines a land as where unfailing yield, the worthy (தக்கார்) and the undiminished wealthy gather; the Cintamani's closing verse on Hemangadam makes it a place for those who do penance and those who make wealth, after ten verses of harvest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic fills the couplet's three-part definition with a whole country, and reads its worthy ones as those who do penance, so the Kural's land is given room for renunciants beside the wealthy.","gn":["the hyperbole of heaven fallen to earth is anchored in a received Tamil definition of what makes a land","the couplet's unspecified தக்கார் receives a concrete reading as ascetics living alongside the rich"]},{"a":"cc:67","b":"k:739","type":"develops","note":"The Kural calls those lands whose riches need no seeking; the Cintamani's pools are strewn with gold beads from broken girdles that no one troubles to gather.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the reapers and threshers of 55 to 59, the gold no one picks up shows the epic taking unsought wealth to mean surplus beyond need after labour, not riches without work.","gn":["the image of the gold-starred pools is read as a mark of the ideal land rather than as mere ornament","the couplet's riches that need no seeking are given a scene where the absence of seeking is visible"]},{"a":"cc:67","b":"si:5:111-127","type":"qualifies","note":"Puhar's open court leaves traders' bundles unguarded because a thief is made to carry his load and whirl; Hemangadam's pools hold gold that lies untaken because no one wants it.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Two epics praise a city by its untaken wealth but give opposite grounds, fear of a punishing power in the Silappathikaram and simple satiety in the Cintamani, so the marvel is not one convention but two theories of order."},{"a":"cc:61","b":"pp:3:130-145","type":"develops","note":"The Sirupanarruppadai paints the kinai bard's hunger in his kitchen, his wife boiling unsalted greens behind a shut door, until a patron's gifts end it; the Cintamani puts the porunar's distress down in half a line, through the carts of paddy and cane.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic keeps the Sangam bard's want only to cancel it at once, and moves its relief from one named patron's gift to the anonymous traffic of a rich land.","gn":["the stock phrase about the kinai bards is seen to carry a whole earlier genre of hunger and rescue","the guide poem's patron-centred relief is set against a later picture where plenty, not a giver, ends the bard's want"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:71","b":"pp:4:311-335","type":"same_image","note":"The Perumpanarruppadai's girls on sky-touching terraces move like peacocks rejoicing on a great hill's slope; the Cintamani's women on the mansion rows look like peacocks spreading their wings in the mist on a high hill.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic inherits the Sangam figure of terrace women as hill peacocks but makes the hill's mist out of the houses' own perfume smoke, turning a natural setting into manufactured luxury.","gn":["the simile is traced to an older description of a port city's rich women","the older image is shown surviving into the epics with its landscape replaced by the household"]},{"a":"cc:72","b":"k:1311","type":"qualifies","note":"In both the sulking woman's charge turns on eyes: in the Kural all women feast on the man with theirs, in the Cintamani the women say his loves are in his eyes, and refuse him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Kural keeps the eye-charge inside the couple's quarrel, while the epic lets the same charge end in snapped girdles and spilled gold, so the sulk becomes a sign of the city's wealth rather than a turn in a love.","gn":["the epic's brief quoted words are recognised as a set move of the sulking wife","the couplet's private reproach is shown being absorbed into public description of a prosperous land"]},{"a":"cc:78","b":"k:913","type":"develops","note":"Both picture the bought embrace through a body: the Kural's woman of wealth embraces a stranger's corpse (பிணந்தழீஇ); the epic's net-woman embraces the flesh of wealth (தசை தழீஇ) and throws the body away.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Set beside the epic's hunter who keeps the meat of wealth and drops the carcass, the couplet's corpse reads naturally as the client handled by the woman, so the disgust in Kural 913 belongs to the trade itself rather than only to the man.","gn":["the epic's hunting conceit is seen reworking the Kural's own corpse image and verb of embrace","the couplet's disputed embracer gains a later poet's figure in which the woman is plainly the one who embraces and the man is the discarded body"]},{"a":"cc:98","b":"cc:78","type":"same_image","note":"The courtesan who ruins whoever falls into her net, refused by the citizens in 78, returns in 98 as the moat that ruins whoever falls into it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vice the poet praises the city for shunning becomes, twenty verses later, the figure of its defence, so the same destroying woman is a danger to citizens and a weapon against enemies."},{"a":"cc:101","b":"k:750","type":"qualifies","note":"The epic arms the wall with self-acting engines and names no defender; the Kural says every excellence of a fort is nothing without men excellent in action.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's picture of a wall that fights by its own machinery is exactly the kind of fort the couplet calls empty, so the Kural supplies the missing term by which this lavish security can fail.","gn":["the catalogue of engines gains a standard that shows what it leaves out: the people who must work them","the couplet's unnamed excellences of a fort gain a concrete inventory of what a later poet thought those excellences were"]},{"a":"cc:103","b":"k:747","type":"qualifies","note":"The Cintamani's wall is armed by Yavana engines that throw molten metal, arrows and stones outward; Kural 747 names three ways a fort is taken, the third (அறைப்படுத்தல்) by working on those inside.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two show that the epic's elaborate machinery answers only the outward assaults of the couplet's list, leaving the inside danger by which this city is in fact lost to its own minister.","gn":["the engine catalogue acquires an ironic edge, since the couplet names the threat no machine can meet","the couplet's abstract third danger gets a concrete case of a fully armed city whose defences all face outward"]},{"a":"cc:107","b":"k:919","type":"tension","note":"Both use வரைவு for the courtesans: the epic says they sell beauty வரைவின்றி, the Kural calls such women வரைவிலா and their arms a mire for base men.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same word that the Kural uses to condemn the courtesan becomes in the Jain epic an item in a city's praise, so the reader sees that a heaven-like city could include the trade the ethical book calls a hell.","gn":["the epic's praise is shown to carry a word the ethical tradition had already loaded with blame","the couplet's condemnation is set against a poem that celebrates the same openness as wealth and beauty"]},{"a":"cc:109","b":"cc:206","type":"develops","note":"Verse 109 has kings linger in the courtesans' quarter out of desire; at 206 a counsellor warns the king that many who gorged on women's pleasure fell into sin and blame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pleasure that the city praise lets kings enjoy without comment is the same pleasure that the story will turn into a warning to its own king, so the description quietly prepares the charge.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:115","b":"si:5:111-127","type":"qualifies","note":"Both cities leave goods unguarded: Puhar's open court keeps bundles safe because a spirit punishes the thief, the Cintamani's merchants leave fallen jewels lying as a matter of their own conduct.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The pair gives two different accounts of why a rich city's goods are safe, one by fear of a guardian spirit's punishment and one by the citizens' surfeit and habit, so honesty is shown as either enforced or assumed."},{"a":"cc:105","b":"si:5:1-15","type":"same_image","note":"The Silappathikaram opens its Puhar with the earth as a woman, sea for garment, hills for breasts, rivers for hair; the Cintamani makes the city wall a maiden with the moat for garment and bastions for breasts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same body-map moves from the whole earth to a single wall, and the Cintamani's word கன்னி turns it into a figure of a fortress never entered, which the Silappathikaram's earth-woman does not carry."},{"a":"cc:106","b":"k:215","type":"develops","note":"Kural 215 likens the wise man's wealth to a full village tank everyone draws from; verse 106 has the inner city's families bring wealth from overseas islands and feed many (பலர்க்கு ஆர்த்தி).","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet says shared wealth is like a common tank but not where it comes from; the epic supplies the source in perilous sea trade and makes feeding many the mark that justifies calling the city heavenly.","gn":["the epic's praise of wealth is given an ethical test, that the wealth is used to fill many","the couplet's image of a public tank gains a whole merchant class acting it out"]},{"a":"cc:146","b":"cc:233","type":"develops","note":"The pavilion where the king dwells with desire burning in him is what a minister later names as the liquor of desire in which the king drowned and left the path of justice.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The lavish pavilion that reads as ornament is the poet's first, unjudged sign of the vice the minister will later blame for the king's ruin."},{"a":"cc:149","b":"cc:201","type":"develops","note":"The garden one cannot leave without losing one's life anticipates the king's words that he cannot bear to part from Vijayai, for which he gives his rule to Kattiyangaran.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The garden's power to hold its owner is the same inability to part that later makes the king hand his kingdom to a minister."},{"a":"cc:141","b":"cc:198","type":"tension","note":"The king introduced with a gracious sceptre and a single command is, some sixty verses later, the king who slips from guarding the land to cling to his queen.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poet sets out the full catalogue of a just king's marks so that the reader can measure how far this king falls from them."},{"a":"cc:148","b":"mm:19:93-106","type":"same_image","note":"Both epics put a king with his beloved in a built pleasure-park of hills, falls, pools and deer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same crafted royal park is, in the Manimekalai, interrupted by news from the prison that turns the king to charity, and in the Cintamani the setting of a desire that costs the king his rule."},{"a":"cc:146","b":"k:901","type":"develops","note":"Kural 901 says men set on their wives miss the fine fruit and men of action do not want that desire; the epic shows a king whose rule is given up for it.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural's flat warning becomes in the epic a seductive scene, so the reader is drawn into the very desire the couplet tells the man of action to refuse.","gn":["The epic verse gets the moral frame its narrator withholds: the king's desire is the kind that a ruler is told to refuse.","The couplet's unnamed 'fine fruit' is given concrete stakes: for a king the loss is his kingdom and his life."]},{"a":"cc:134","b":"k:1120","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's lover says anicham and swan's down are like nerunji to her feet; the epic has a whole city's women whose feet shiver on flowering cotton while they wear anicham.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"A lover's private hyperbole about one woman's feet becomes in the epic a stock trait of a whole class of wealthy women, showing how the Kural's love conceits turned into description.","gn":["The verse's strange shiver of feet on cotton is legible as a known conceit, so its point is degree of wealth, not novelty of image.","The couplet's image is shown to have become public property, attached to a city's luxury rather than one lover's gaze."]},{"a":"cc:131","b":"k:81","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 81 says all of a household's keeping of goods is for caring for guests; the epic lists eating with guests as one item among the city's perfumes, gold and beds.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Where the Kural makes hospitality the purpose that justifies household wealth, the epic sets it inside a catalogue of pleasure, so the duty becomes one ornament of a city that will soon fall.","gn":["The verse's single mention of guests is shown to carry the one moral justification of wealth in a run otherwise given to luxury.","The couplet's rule is seen in a setting where wealth has outgrown the purpose the couplet gives it."]},{"a":"cc:139","b":"t:518","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 518 says that if pujas in the First One's temple stop, kings suffer, the rains' bounty falls and theft spreads; the epic says the Jina's shrines everywhere keep the city's wealth from failing.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"A Saiva manual and a Jain epic share the belief that shrines keep a land prosperous, but the Saiva verse ties it to rites that must not lapse while the Jain verse credits only the shrines' presence.","gn":["The epic's brief claim is set in a wider Tamil belief tying a city's welfare to worship, so it reads as doctrine rather than mere praise.","The Saiva warning is shown to be not only sectarian: a Jain text makes the same causal link without the threat of lapse."]},{"a":"cc:157","b":"cc:290","type":"same_image","note":"In 157 the king is fierce as a poisonous serpent's fire; in 290 he is the sun of the Kuru line swallowed by the great serpent of his minister.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The serpent's wrath that made Saccandan terrible to enemies is turned back on him from inside his own court, so the image of his strength becomes the image of his end."},{"a":"cc:159","b":"cc:290","type":"tension","note":"159 says the whole world rests in the cool shade of Saccandan's umbrella as under a father's feet; 290 says that when the minister swallows him the darkness of the bent sceptre spreads and the people grieve.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The shade praised as the world's settled condition proves to depend on one man's life, since one betrayal turns the straight sceptre's shade into the bent sceptre's darkness."},{"a":"cc:167","b":"cc:181","type":"develops","note":"167 says Vijayai's eyes are poison and nectar that madden even sages; in 181 Saccandan is caught in the net of her long eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The descriptive claim that her eyes overturn the self-controlled is cashed in the plot: the first one they capture is the king whose fall drives the epic."},{"a":"cc:157","b":"cc:162","type":"same_image","note":"The king is an அணங்கு to women (157); the princess is an அணங்கு even to the goddess on the lotus (162).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem sets an afflicting beauty against a greater one before they meet, so the king who wounds others by his looks is the one wounded by hers."},{"a":"cc:167","b":"k:1091","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's man sees two looks in her eyes, one that gives the sickness and one that cures it; the epic says her eyes are poison and nectar at once.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet's private double look of lover's sickness and cure becomes, in an epic, a public force that maddens sages and later unseats a king.","gn":["the epic's poison-and-nectar image is placed in an older Tamil account of the look as both wound and remedy","the couplet's two looks are given consequences beyond the lover, reaching ascetics and the state"]},{"a":"cc:161","b":"k:1088","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's man laments that his might, feared by enemies in battle, broke before her bright forehead; 161 shows a king whose spear killed kings at the height of his fame just before he meets Vijayai.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read with the story that follows, the couplet's lover's hyperbole becomes literal history: the warrior whose strength breaks before a woman is a king, and what breaks is his kingdom.","gn":["the praise of Saccandan's martial peak gains a known pattern that foretells its undoing by beauty","the couplet's complaint gains a case where the broken might is public and political, not only the lover's pride"]},{"a":"cc:161","b":"n:3","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar says kings who rode under the umbrella at the head of armies fall when their deeds turn, and enemies take their wives; 161 shows Saccandan at the full of his fame, the starting point of such a fall.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain gives the general fall of the umbrella king; the epic fills in one fall step by step, with the king's own infatuation as the turn and his queen driven out, so impermanence works through character and not only fortune.","gn":["the epic's high point of praise gains the moral frame within which its listeners would hear it as the top of a wheel","the quatrain's abstract turn of deeds gains a narrated mechanism, desire and a trusted minister"]},{"a":"cc:159","b":"pn:35","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 35 tells the Chola his tall umbrella is not for shade but to shelter suffering subjects, and warns him the world blames kings when things go wrong; 159 says Saccandan's umbrella already shades the world like a father.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam poet gives the umbrella as a duty the king must be urged to keep, the epic as a state already achieved; set together, the epic's praise lacks the warning that the story will soon need.","gn":["the epic's settled praise is seen to omit the counsel that in the Sangam poem accompanies the umbrella","the poem's admonition gains a later narrative in which a praised umbrella king does lose the world's shade"]},{"a":"cc:160","b":"pn:56","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 56 equates the Pandya with four gods, one for each of his powers; 160 equates Saccandan with Dharma, Varuna, Death, Vaman and Kama.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the Sangam form of measuring a king god by god but shifts it from war powers to grace, giving and beauty, and puts the god of desire in the list, which the story makes the king's flaw.","gn":["the epic's god-list is placed in an inherited praise form, so its departures (Dharma, Kama) stand out","the Sangam form is shown surviving into Jain narrative with new gods and an ironic edge"]},{"a":"cc:181","b":"k:1081","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are the man's first sight of the woman and both reach for அணங்கு; the Kural's lover wavers among goddess, peahen and woman, while Sachchandan, on the likelier reading, takes her for an அணங்கு without doubt (ஐயுறான்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's perplexity at first sight is a lover's healthy doubt; the epic gives a king who does not doubt, and his certainty that she is more than human is the first step of the absorption that later costs him his kingdom.","gn":["the king's untroubled certainty is seen as a departure from the expected first-sight wavering, marking his surrender as complete","the couplet's three-way doubt is shown to hold a check that a man without it, a king, loses"]},{"a":"cc:194","b":"k:1101","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's lover says all five senses find their objects in one woman; verse 194 gathers sound, scent, sight and touch in Vijayai and calls the joy inexhaustible.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"What the Kural states as the joy of union, with no cost named, the epic puts in a king, and four verses later shows the cost: a man whose senses are all fed from one source slips from guarding the land (198).","gn":["the sense catalogue is read as a deliberate totality, not ornament, setting up the king's neglect","the couplet's praise of complete delight is given a setting, kingship, where that completeness becomes a danger"]},{"a":"cc:196","b":"k:901","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 901 says men who fix their desire on the wife miss life's fine fruit; 196 measures the king's wedded intimacy as greater than the sea, and the story goes on to make that desire lose him his realm.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's chapter on following the wife assumes a fault in the yielding; the epic gives a blameless, chaste queen (verse 7, வீடாத கற்பின்) and lawful marriage, and still the husband's boundless absorption alone ruins him, so the rule holds without any fault in the woman.","gn":["the celebration of the wedding season acquires its moral edge from a stated rule","the couplet's claim is tested on a case where the wife is beyond reproach, showing the danger lies in the man's desire, not in her"]},{"a":"cc:179","b":"n:46","type":"tension","note":"Verse 179 praises Vijayai's soles as if made without bone or sinew (என்பொடு நரம்பு இன்றி); Naladiyar 46 lists bone (என்பு) and sinew (நரம்பு) among the parts of the body and asks which of them is the garlanded woman.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Two Jain works use the same two words to opposite ends: the epic's praise must imagine the beloved's body without bone and sinew, while the quatrain dissolves her into them, so the courtly portrait depends on the very forgetting the ascetic verse is written to undo.","gn":["the hyperbole of the boneless foot is seen to deny exactly what the renouncer insists on, showing what the praise leaves out","the quatrain's list is shown answering a real literary convention of praise, not an abstract desire"]},{"a":"cc:192","b":"n:44","type":"tension","note":"Verse 192 praises Vijayai's red-cornered eyes as fighting carp (களிக் கயல் பொருவ); Naladiyar 44's renunciant refuses men who call eyes பொருகயல் and sees an eye as a scooped-out palm fruit.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's renunciant names the epic's own stock of praise as the temptation he walks past, and the epic shows that praise doing its work on a king who does not walk past it.","gn":["the stock carp-eye simile is shown to be a recognised snare in the ethical literature, not neutral ornament","the quatrain's 'petty men' who praise eyes are given a full instance in a celebrated poem, and a consequence in the king's fall"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:195","b":"cc:198","type":"develops","note":"195 has the king linger with his wife as a monsoon cloud lingers; 198 says the king slipped from guarding the earth (மண் அகம் காவலின் வழுக்கி) while joined to her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The lingering that the wedding verses present as pure delight is the same act that 198 names as a lapse in the king's guard, so the romantic sequence is already the first step of the political fall."},{"a":"cc:190","b":"cc:199","type":"same_image","note":"In 190 king and queen drink each other as nectar and wine; in 199 they drink the flood of love without pause while fortune slips from the king of the moon-white parasol.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The drinking image moves from mutual delight to unceasing consumption: what was a figure of shared love becomes the measure of the time in which the king's fortune leaks away."},{"a":"cc:188","b":"cc:208","type":"tension","note":"188 likens the wedded pair to the three-eyed god and his goddess joined; in 208 a counsellor cites the god's taking the mountain king's daughter into half his body as a great blame (பெரும் பழி).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same divine union serves the narrator as the highest praise of the couple and the counsellor as a warning of shame from desire, so the epic lets its own simile be turned against the king within twenty verses."},{"a":"cc:187","b":"cc:215","type":"develops","note":"187 says the two were joined by the பான்மை for joining; 215 says the king followed the way of her mind because a former பான்மை (முனம் ஆகிய பான்மை) sprouted, ignoring all warning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"If பான்மை in 187 is read as destiny, the marriage and the king's ruinous absorption have one source in the epic's account, apportioned former deeds, while 187 also names the ministers' reasoning: fate and judgment are both given as causes."},{"a":"cc:183","b":"cc:232","type":"same_image","note":"183 likens Vijayai in her father's palace to Tirumakal on the lotus; 232 has Tirumakal leave Sachchandan without a word, the king left alone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king wins a woman praised as the goddess of fortune and, through his absorption in her, loses the goddess of fortune herself: the epic turns its own epithet into the measure of the cost."},{"a":"cc:204","b":"cc:241","type":"develops","note":"The soothsayer warns that setting power on one man will drive prosperity from the land; in 241 Kattiyankaran, now holding that power, says a deity urges him night and day to kill the king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prophecy names no traitor, only the structure of power, so its fulfilment shows the epic locating the danger in the king's act of delegation before it locates it in the minister's character.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:203","b":"cc:241","type":"tension","note":"Kattiyankaran bows and promises to bear the yoke so that no blame falls on the land; in 241 he asks his council how to kill the king, laying the impulse on a hostile deity.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The minister who vowed blameless guardianship later disowns his own intent by blaming a god, so the story pairs the king's excuse by destiny (213) with the usurper's excuse by possession."},{"a":"cc:219","b":"cc:223","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai's three dreams, which leave her trembling at dawn, are told in 223: the ashoka tree falls and a sprout rises from its root.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The dream that frightens her is a dream of fall and succession, and when the king reads it (225) he speaks only of the heir and keeps silent on the fall, so her fear at 219 rightly answers the half that is withheld."},{"a":"cc:215","b":"cc:233","type":"qualifies","note":"The narrator says past destiny sprouted and the king followed his heart; the minister of 233 tells the king he left the path of justice and bathed in the liquor of lust.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the king's fall two causes in two voices, destiny in the narrator's and the king's own wrong path in the minister's, and does not let either cancel the other."},{"a":"cc:219","b":"si:20:1-7","type":"qualifies","note":"Both queens wake from dreams of the king's fall: the Pandya queen sees umbrella and sceptre drop; Vijayai's dream (told in 223) shows the tree that is the king falling and a sprout rising.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Silappathikaram's queen dreams only the end of a reign, while the Cintamani's queen dreams an end that carries an heir, so the same omen-form serves tragedy in one epic and a story of restoration in the other."},{"a":"cc:213","b":"k:377","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 377 says even the rich enjoy only as the apportioner has apportioned; the king says his pleasures and life-days are measured by past penance and so refuses counsel.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's limit on enjoyment becomes, in a king's mouth, a licence to stop listening, which shows that a doctrine of allotted fruit can be turned against the duty to act on advice.","gn":["the king's refusal is seen to borrow a recognised ethical teaching rather than being bare infatuation","the couplet is shown to be open to a fatalist misuse it does not itself guard against"]},{"a":"cc:205","b":"k:509","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 509 bids a ruler trust no one unexamined; the king vouches for Kattiyankaran by love and past victories and forbids further words.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Loyalty and battle service, the very grounds the king gives, are not the examination the couplet asks for, so the scene shows how trust can feel tested while skipping the test of fitness for power.","gn":["the king's grounds for trust are measured against a stated rule and found to fall short of it","the couplet's bare 'examine' is shown failing in a case where the examiner thinks he already knows the man"]},{"a":"cc:212","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"Kural 448 says a king without rebukers perishes even with no enemy; the king hears Rudradattan's rebuke, rejects it, and the counsellor withdraws.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic shows the middle case the couplet skips, a king who has a rebuker and sends him away, and its ruin comes through the trusted man, so the danger lies in refusing rebuke as much as in lacking it.","gn":["the counsellor's failure is given the weight of a known rule of kingship","the couplet's 'having no rebuker' is shown to include a king who drives rebuke off"]},{"a":"cc:215","b":"k:901","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 901 says men who fix their desire on the wife miss life's fine fruit; the narrator says the king followed his heart to the queen because a past destiny sprouted.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats being led by desire for one's wife as a fault to be avoided, while the epic adds a ripening past deed as its cause, which raises the question the couplet does not face: whether such a man could have chosen otherwise.","gn":["the king's surrender to desire is named as a recognised fault of rulers, not only a love story","the couplet's simple prohibition is set against a story where karma also pushes the man"]},{"a":"cc:221","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 55 praises the wife who rises worshipping her husband and not a god; Vijayai rises, worships the god's feet, and then goes to her lord's feet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A Jain epic keeps the wife's devotion to her husband but sets worship of the god first, so the couplet's exclusive ranking is answered with an order of two duties rather than a choice between them.","gn":["the queen's morning is seen as a deliberate ordering of devotions against a known ideal","the couplet's 'not a god' is shown to be a position a later work could decline while still honouring the husband"]},{"a":"cc:208","b":"t:16","type":"tension","note":"Rudradattan counts the konrai-wearing god's taking the mountain-daughter as his half as a stain and a great blame; Tirumanthiram 16 names the same god by that half-woman form as the one the gods delight in.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same mark of Siva, his body shared with Uma, is a counsellor's example of lust's disgrace in a Jain epic and a title of praise in Tirumular's invocation, so the image itself is where the schools part.","gn":["the counsellor's slight is seen to strike at a form the Saiva poets hold central","the invocation's epithet is shown to be one a rival tradition read as a scandal"]},{"a":"cc:211","b":"n:89","type":"qualifies","note":"Rudradattan argues that lust's blame cannot be hidden and is drummed through the world; Naladiyar 89 says lust burns the mind itself, worse than fire or sun.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The counsellor argues from public disgrace and the quatrain from inward burning, so a reader sees that the ethics of desire had two separate arguments and that the epic, speaking to a king, chose reputation.","gn":["the counsellor's choice of argument by reputation stands out against an argument he did not use","the quatrain's inward harm is set beside the outward cost it leaves out"]},{"a":"cc:225","b":"cc:267","type":"develops","note":"The king reads the crown, garlands and shoot but will not speak of the falling tree; at 267 he tells the queen that all she dreamed that night has come to fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The withheld half of the dream was the king's own death, so his kindly silence at the reading becomes, at the fall of the city, an admission that he knew.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:228","b":"cc:267","type":"tension","note":"Consoling the queen, the king asks whether a dream's nature is truth; at 267 he tells her the dreams have all come true.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The king's dismissal of dreams was comfort he did not believe, since the same man had read the dream by the treatise and later concedes its truth."},{"a":"cc:233","b":"cc:272","type":"develops","note":"Wisdom's remedy for the king's lost sense is to build a rare machine; at 272 the king tells the queen the vehicle is ready and she must go first.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The machine ordered as penance for the lust that cost the kingdom becomes the means of saving the heir, so the fault's remedy is escape rather than reform.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:245","b":"cc:257","type":"tension","note":"Dharmadattan argues that Fortune deserts the man who seizes his maker; Madanan answers that he can speak only with the sword, not the mouth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The counsel fails not by being refuted but by being refused, so the plot proceeds with its warning on record, which the story will make good."},{"a":"cc:232","b":"t:2593","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the wood-apple swallowed by the elephant, whole in shell and emptied within: the Cintamani for a king whom Fortune has left, Tirumular for Siva swallowing soul, states and world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same figure of a shell kept whole while its substance is taken means ruin for a king and consummation for a soul, so Tirumular turns a stock image of loss into one of absorption in Siva.","gn":["the epic's compressed simile is confirmed in its sense of an outwardly intact but emptied reign","the Saiva verse is shown inverting an image whose ordinary use in narrative was loss of fortune"]},{"a":"cc:232","b":"k:638","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural binds the one at the king's side to speak what is good even to a king who destroys counsel; in the Cintamani the counsel comes from Wisdom as minister, after the realm is already given away.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"With the minister's office handed to the usurper, the duty the Kural lays on the counsellor falls to the king's own wisdom, and it speaks only when advice can no longer save the realm.","gn":["the figure of Wisdom as minister is placed within the Kural's account of the counsellor's duty","the couplet's duty is shown with its limit: counsel that comes too late can only plan an escape"]},{"a":"cc:244","b":"k:906","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 906 says the man who fears his wife's shoulders is worthless though he live like the gods; Dharmadattan says the king valued a woman's pleasure above heaven and nectar.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural weighs the wife-ruled man against the gods' life and finds him worthless, while the epic's king makes the same comparison in reverse, and the cost falls not on his worth but on the whole realm.","gn":["the counsellor's charge is seen as the Kural's chapter on being led by a woman, applied to a king","the couplet's private failing is shown with a public cost, a world that sinks with its king"]},{"a":"cc:245","b":"k:110","type":"develops","note":"Kural 110 says there is no escape for one who kills a good done to him; Dharmadattan warns that Fortune will leave the man who seizes the king who made him, and that loyalty secures his line.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's 'no escape' for ingratitude is turned into a political forecast, the loss of fortune and lineage, and offered to a would-be usurper as prudence rather than only as virtue.","gn":["the counsel is grounded in the Kural's ranking of ingratitude as the sin without remedy","the couplet's abstract doom is given a concrete form, a usurper whose line will be uprooted"]},{"a":"cc:250","b":"cc:241","type":"tension","note":"In 241 Kattiyangaran claims a great deity hostile to the king possesses him and urges the killing; 250 calls that a deity of his own mouth, after 247 has made the king himself the great deity.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The usurper's appeal to divine compulsion is answered by moving divinity to the king, so the regicide is recast from obedience to a god into an attack on one."},{"a":"cc:251","b":"cc:259","type":"develops","note":"Dharmadattan forestalls the excuse of sending the king to heaven; in 259 Kattiyangaran uses exactly that excuse, dressed as a promise that the earth will brim with gold.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The counsellor named the usurper's rationalisation before he spoke it, so 259 reads as a man walking into a trap already shown to him."},{"a":"cc:248","b":"cc:261","type":"tension","note":"248 claims the king's splendour guards the world even while he sleeps; 261 shows the king sunk in pleasure, his army given up, and helpless against his minister.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The story refutes the councillor's extravagant praise of royal power: a king who has let go of rule is not protected by his majesty."},{"a":"cc:260","b":"cc:205","type":"develops","note":"In 205 the king refuses the astrologer's warning, saying he has done his part for Kattiyangaran and let him do as he will; 260 lists the king's own grace among the things that will kill him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"அருள் கொல்லும் names the king's trust in 205 as a fatal fault, so his generosity is counted with lust and wealth among the causes of his fall."},{"a":"cc:263","b":"cc:204","type":"develops","note":"The astrologer in 204 warned that if the king put his power in one man the land's fortune would leave; in 263 that man's army surrounds the palace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The siege fulfils a warning the king dismissed, so the fall is shown as foreseen and refused, not as blind chance."},{"a":"cc:267","b":"cc:228","type":"tension","note":"In 228 the king soothed Vijayai by calling belief in dreams a woman's credulity; in 267 he tells her that all she dreamt has come to pass.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The king's dismissal of his wife's dream is reversed by his own admission, which turns his later lecture on detachment in 269 into a man correcting his own earlier error."},{"a":"cc:267","b":"cc:223","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai's dream in 223 of the ashoka tree falling, its garland crowning a shoot, is what the king says in 267 has now come true.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The fallen tree of the dream is the king himself, which the earlier verse could only hint at, while the shoot points ahead to the son not yet born."},{"a":"cc:247","b":"k:388","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 388 calls the ruler who does justice and protects இறை; Dharmadattan calls the king a great deity because his favour makes and his anger burns.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the king's divinity a reward for just rule, while Dharmadattan makes it a matter of the king's power, which lets him use it to protect even a king who has abandoned rule.","gn":["the councillor's claim is seen to drop the condition the Kural sets, which explains why the story can refute it","the couplet's condition is shown to be what separates a just king's sanctity from mere fear of his power"]},{"a":"cc:248","b":"k:547","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 547 says the ruler guards the world and justice guards him if he does not falter; 248 says the king's splendour guards the world even as he sleeps.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Cintamani's king, who sleeps in pleasure and loses everything, illustrates the Kural's condition by its breach: majesty without unfaltering justice guards nothing.","gn":["the verse's claim is placed against a rule that names the condition it leaves out","the couplet's 'if he does not falter' is shown at work in a king who faltered through pleasure, not cruelty"]},{"a":"cc:250","b":"k:691","type":"develops","note":"Kural 691 tells those who serve kings to keep to them as men warming at a fire, neither near nor far; 250 says the king's fire burns those who touch it along with their kin and can be nectar or poison.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's fire is a rule of prudent distance for courtiers, while Dharmadattan extends it to the minister who would seize the fire, and to his whole family.","gn":["the councillor's threat is grounded in a known maxim of service","the couplet's image is carried from caution in service to the fate of a servant who turns on his king"]},{"a":"cc:253","b":"k:110","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 110 says there is no escape for one who kills a benefit; 253 names a minister who kills the king who raised him among those who sink into hell and leprosy.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the Kural's abstract unforgivable sin a concrete political form, the minister's regicide, and spells out where 'no escape' leads.","gn":["the regicide is ranked as ingratitude, the sin the Kural puts beyond remedy","the couplet's general rule receives its gravest case and an explicit punishment"]},{"a":"cc:253","b":"nk:97","type":"develops","note":"Dharmadattan sends the one who kills and eats flesh to hell; Neelakesi 97 shows hell's tormentors burning beings while naming the sins of killing and eating flesh.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Dharmadattan files regicide in one list with killing and flesh-eating, the very sins Neelakesi's tormentors name as they burn the damned, so a political crime is set inside the minor epic's catalogue of injuries to life."},{"a":"cc:255","b":"k:559","type":"develops","note":"Kural 559 says that when the king bends justice the sky will not rain; 255 extends the effects to the planets, the hours, harvest, hunger, women's chastity and dharma.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Cintamani turns the Kural's warning to an unjust king into an argument against a minister seizing power, treating usurpation itself as the bending of the sceptre.","gn":["the councillor's list is anchored in the Kural's best-known statement of the bent sceptre","the couplet's single effect, failed rain, is shown as the first of a cascade that reaches into household morals"]},{"a":"cc:255","b":"tk:50","type":"qualifies","note":"Thirikadugam 50 names three separate 'planets' that cut off rain: a greedy king, deceitful men and a woman who crosses her bounds; 255 makes the bent sceptre the one cause from which failed rain and broken chastity both follow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the Thirikadugam gives the king, the liar and the unchaste woman equal blame for drought, the Cintamani makes women's lapse a symptom of the king's fault.","gn":["the epic's causal order stands out against a list that treats the three as parallel","the verse's planet image, கோள், is shown read in the epic as literal disorder of the planets"]},{"a":"cc:262","b":"t:245","type":"develops","note":"Tirumular says one who comes to seize the world for others or himself has the nature of a pouncing tiger; 262 shows Kattiyangaran's seizure: kin chained, wealth given to those who submit, one umbrella over the land.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Tirumanthiram names the usurper's nature, and the Cintamani shows his method, buying loyalty and chaining kin, and calls the result a lamp of infamy.","gn":["the narrator's verdict of blame is seen to match a doctrine of kingship outside the epic","the verse's tentative line about seizing the world gains a worked case of usurpation by a minister"]},{"a":"cc:260","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"qualifies","note":"Ilango's patikam says dharma becomes death to those who err in kingship and that past deeds ripen; 260 lists Death, the woman, the king's own fault, his wealth and his grace as what will kill the king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Silappathikaram's king dies for an unjust verdict, while the Cintamani's is killed for lust and misplaced trust, so 'erring in kingship' is shown to include neglect, not only injustice."},{"a":"cc:269","b":"n:110","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 110 says what was fixed at the start neither shrinks nor grows, so why grieve in ruin; 269 says birth and death come from one's own deeds and grief is want of understanding.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both forbid grief by appeal to a fixed outcome, but the king speaks while his own fault caused the ruin, so in his mouth deed-fruit is also a confession, which the Naladiyar's impersonal fixed mark is not.","gn":["the king's consolation is placed within the ethical books' argument against grieving","the quatrain's fixed fate is set beside a version in which the fixing is one's own past action"]},{"a":"cc:269","b":"s:365","type":"tension","note":"The Siddhar says weeping and worship are useless because good and evil come as the lotus-born god wrote; the king says birth and death come from the fruit of one's own deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two give the same counsel against grief for opposite reasons: the Siddhar's outcome is written by a creator, the Cintamani's by the sufferer's own deeds, with no god in the account.","gn":["the king's karma is seen as godless by contrast with a fate assigned by Brahma","the Siddhar's fatalism is shown to have a rival ground in self-made deed that leaves worship equally useless"]},{"a":"cc:246","b":"pn:186","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 186 says the world lives by its king, so it is the king's duty to know he is its life; 246 puts kings above the gods.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Purananuru turns the king's exalted place into a duty laid on the king, while Dharmadattan turns it into a ban laid on the subject who would harm him.","gn":["the councillor's exaltation of kingship is seen as one use of a shared idea, aimed at the subject","the poem's claim is shown to cut the other way when a minister, not a king, is the one being warned"]},{"a":"cc:256","b":"cc:262","type":"same_image","note":"In 256 Madanan's sweating, garlanded chest is likened to the Karthikai lamp lit on a hill; in 262 Kattiyangaran's seizure is a blazing lamp of infamy lit on a hilltop.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The festival lamp that first flatters the usurper's ally turns, six verses later, into the sign that shows the usurper's shame to the whole land, so the image passes from praise to blame."},{"a":"cc:260","b":"cc:269","type":"qualifies","note":"Dharmadattan names the king's own fault, his woman and his grace among what kills him; the king, consoling his queen, says death and birth come from the fruit of one's deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The councillor places the cause in faults of this life that the king could have avoided, while the king refers it to deeds in general, so his doctrine of karma softens a blame the earlier verse fixes on him."},{"a":"cc:289","b":"cc:223","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai dreamed that the asoka tree fell and was utterly gone; in 289 the king falls to the earth and goes out like the setting sun.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The half of the dream the king refused to read at 225 is fulfilled in his fall, so the battle is the dream's first image enacted and Civakan, the shoot, is still to come."},{"a":"cc:271","b":"cc:223","type":"same_image","note":"The பிண்டி whose fall in the dream meant the king's death is in 271 the tree of the Jina whose scripture the king offers his wife as consolation.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One tree carries both the loss and the remedy: the royal asoka falls, and the Jina's asoka stands as the ground for not grieving that fall."},{"a":"cc:290","b":"cc:281","type":"same_image","note":"In 281 the discus cuts the king's shield as the snake joins the sun's disc; in 290 the minister is the great snake that swallows the king, sun of the Kuru line.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The eclipse simile moves from the shield to the man, so a battle image becomes the narrator's verdict that the usurpation is a swallowing of the sun."},{"a":"cc:291","b":"cc:259","type":"develops","note":"In 259 Kattiyankaran proposed that the king go to rule heaven as lord of the gods; in 291 the king does go to the gods, as their guest.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The usurper's euphemism for murder comes literally true, but the narrator gives the king the heaven of a warrior and a Jain devotee, not the throne the usurper mockingly offered."},{"a":"cc:290","b":"cc:261","type":"tension","note":"261 makes the king's surrender of his army in pleasure the opening for the treachery; 290 mourns him as the sun of the just sceptre swallowed by the minister alone.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"In death the narrator moves the blame wholly onto the usurper, so the epic holds both a negligent king and a righteous one and lets the lament forget the first."},{"a":"cc:290","b":"k:548","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says a ruler who does not attend and judge perishes by himself; Saccandan, who gave up rule for pleasure, perishes, but by a minister's treachery, and is mourned as just.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic splits the couplet's single cause: neglect opens the door and another's crime walks through, so the ruin is self-made in the Kural's sense yet not self-inflicted.","gn":["the lament gains a standard that names the king's own share in his fall, which 290 leaves unsaid","the couplet's bare தானே கெடும் gains a mechanism, the trusted minister through whom a neglectful king's ruin actually comes"]},{"a":"cc:291","b":"pn:62","type":"develops","note":"Puram 62 ends with the gods of the unfading flowers receiving many guests from a field where two kings died; 291 says the dead king became a guest of the gods, adding the Jina's feet and the mantra-sandal.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain epic keeps the Sangam honour of the fallen king as heaven's guest but makes devotion to the Jina part of his passage, so the warrior's heaven is re-founded on religion.","gn":["the epic's phrase is shown to be the old battlefield honour, which explains why a negligent king's end can still be glorious","the Puram formula, which grounds heaven in fighting alone, is shown being given a religious condition by a later school"]},{"a":"cc:270","b":"s:279","type":"qualifies","note":"Pattinathar asks how many mothers, wives, children and births he has had and has yet to have, and cries to the lord of Ekambam; Saccandan reckons births past the sands of the shore and tells his wife kinship lasts two days.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same reckoning of countless births and passing kin brings the Siddhar to despair and a cry for Siva's help, and brings the Jain king to composure with no god, so the argument's outcome turns on where release is placed.","gn":["the king's consolation is shown to be the renouncer's argument, turned to comfort a wife at a parting rather than to leave the world","the Siddhar's anguished count gains a reader who draws calm from it through scripture, the answer his song looks for only in the Lord"]},{"a":"cc:296","b":"pn:246","type":"qualifies","note":"Perunkoppendu argues against the elders that the widow's life of plain food and gravel beds is not for her and the pyre is a lotus pool; Saccandan's women make the same choice in a simile and a vow of the other world, without the argument.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Sangam queen's reasoned refusal, the epic's wives choose death as a group and by figure alone, while the one queen who must live, Vijayai, is spared the choice by her unborn son.","gn":["The epic verse gains the explicit alternative its women reject, the austere widow's life Purananuru 246 describes.","The Purananuru poem gains a counter-case in the same scene: a queen (Vijayai) whom motherhood bars from the pyre."]},{"a":"cc:297","b":"n:3","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 3 says kings who rode elephants under the umbrella fall when their deeds turn and enemies take their wives; here the usurper mounts the elephant to proclaim himself and takes Fortune.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain names only the fallen king, but the epic shows the other half of the turn: the enemy who rises on the same elephant, a figure the poem's logic marks for the next fall.","gn":["The epic scene gains a general frame in which the usurper's elephant-back triumph is itself unstable.","The quatrain gains a named winner and a cause the poem leaves out: the fallen king's own neglect of rule."]},{"a":"cc:303","b":"k:37","type":"develops","note":"Kural 37 bids the reader look at palanquin-bearer and rider instead of being told what virtue is; 303 offers the queen alone among skulls as sight for those who know sin only by report.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both turn visible fortune into evidence for unseen merit, but the epic applies it to one life reversed from palace to burning ground, and the proof falls on a sufferer the poet does not blame.","gn":["The epic's 'as if to show' gains the general argument from sight that Kural 37 states.","The couplet gains a case where rider and bearer are the same person in one life, which exposes the cost of reading misfortune as proof."]},{"a":"cc:304","b":"mm:6:94-104","type":"tension","note":"Manimekalai 6 lists young mothers just delivered and helpless infants among those Death heaps for the burning ground to eat; in the Cintamani a mother gives birth in the burning ground and its deities bow.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic makes the burning ground the end that spares no new mother or infant, while the Jain epic makes the same ground the birthplace of a king, so the place teaches impermanence in one and providence in the other."},{"a":"cc:309","b":"pn:359","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 359 gives the burning ground of owls, corpse-eating jackals and ghoul-women dancing by pyre lamps that has received even conquerors; 309 has jackals as drums, pyres as lamps, ghosts dancing and owls praising at a prince's birth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same creatures and lights that in the Sangam poem greet the end of kings serve in the epic as the band and lamps of a future king's birth, turning a warning about death into a grim birth-festival.","gn":["Vijayai's bitter question gains its weight from the old image of this ground as where kingdoms end.","The Sangam image gains a reversal it never shows: the ground receiving a beginning rather than an end."]},{"a":"cc:311","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 10: only those who reach the Lord's feet swim the great sea of births. Vijayai asks that the Jina's dharma and her newborn son be her raft across a sea of sorrow.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic keeps the Kural's sea and crossing but moves the sea from rebirth to present grief and doubles the raft, placing a mother's hope in a child beside the lord's dharma.","gn":["Vijayai's plea gains the doctrinal sea and swimmer the couplet fixes.","The couplet gains a human raft alongside the divine one, which it does not allow for."]},{"a":"cc:308","b":"cc:269","type":"qualifies","note":"In 269 Saccandan consoles Vijayai that birth and death come from one's own deeds and that grief shows want of understanding; at the birth she blames her own deeds for the child's wretched birth and grieves.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The doctrine the king offered to end her grief becomes, once he is gone, the ground of her self-accusation, so the same teaching consoles the dying and burdens the survivor."},{"a":"cc:313","b":"cc:223","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai's dream in 223 showed the asoka tree falling and a crowned shoot rising from its foot; 313 shows her as an evening where the sun has set and a young moon has risen.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The fall Saccandan would not interpret in 225 is now fulfilled, and the poet restates the dream's double meaning, loss and rising heir, in a new figure of sun and crescent moon."},{"a":"cc:314","b":"cc:10","type":"develops","note":"The patikam in 10 says a deity stood at the mother's shoulder as companion and ended her distress; 314 shows how: the deity comes in the shape of the hunchbacked maid who never left her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The summary's 'divine companion' turns out to work through disguise, choosing the most familiar servant's form, so the help comes as continuity of the queen's household rather than as a marvel."},{"a":"cc:316","b":"cc:322","type":"develops","note":"The deity foretells that one will come right now to take the child and bids the queen hide; in 322 Kantukkatan reaches the child lying in the burning ground.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prophecy and the command to hide make the mother's absence in 322 an arranged handing-over, not a desertion, which the finding scene alone would not show."},{"a":"cc:320","b":"cc:316","type":"develops","note":"In 316 the deity foretells that one man will come at once and carry the child off; in 320 Kandukkatan appears out of the dark with a sword.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The foundling's rescue is framed as foretold before it happens, so the merchant's chance night visit reads as the working of the prince's destiny."},{"a":"cc:323","b":"cc:361","type":"develops","note":"The hidden voice's blessing சீவ on the child's sneeze becomes, in 361, the name சீவகன் given him by his foster parents.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The hero's name is his lost mother's unseen blessing, given unknowingly a second time by the people who raise him."},{"a":"cc:324","b":"cc:2323","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai's blessing that her son will destroy his old enemy (நுன் பழம் பகை) is fulfilled when Kattiyangaran's rule ends in the Manmagal ilampakam.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic's whole plot of revenge and restoration is set down at the cradle as a mother's word, two thousand verses before it comes true."},{"a":"cc:331","b":"cc:306","type":"develops","note":"In 306 Vijayai imagines the royal order that should have opened the gold hoards for suppliants at a prince's birth; in 331 a merchant opens his own hoard like a mountain of red gold for the same child.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The birth-gifts denied the prince in the burning ground are given after all, but by a stranger who believes the child his own.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:319","b":"cc:343","type":"tension","note":"Vijayai says she has done no good deeds (நல் வினை செய்து இலாதேன்); in 343 the narrator likens the passing night to evil deeds that leave when old good deeds come forward.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The mother's self-condemnation is overruled by the narrator a few verses later, who credits her with a store of former merit that now begins to ripen."},{"a":"cc:337","b":"cc:347","type":"develops","note":"The deity names the ascetics' hermitage as their refuge; in 347 Vijayai reaches it, and the narrator says she suffered through a deed from a former birth.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The road to the hermitage is laid by a deity's counsel, but on arrival the narrator explains the exile as karma, so divine help and former deeds are given together as causes."},{"a":"cc:323","b":"k:1317","type":"same_image","note":"In the Kural the woman's automatic blessing at her lover's sneeze is withdrawn the next moment in jealousy; in the epic the blessing at the infant's sneeze comes from a mother who cannot show herself, and sticks as his name.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The everyday sneeze-blessing, which the Kural shows can be taken back, here becomes the one bond between the hidden mother and her child that cannot be undone.","gn":["the epic scene gains the domestic usage behind it: a blessing said by those who love the sneezer, which makes the hidden voice a mother's","the couplet's small rite gains weight: the same word can name and protect a life, not only open a lovers' quarrel"]},{"a":"cc:325","b":"k:338","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural pictures the life leaving the body as a bird flying from a nest left empty; the epic pictures ascetics leaving the body as an exchange for an imperishable golden one, and uses it for the merchant leaving his dead son for a living child.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Leaving the body is loss in the Kural's figure and gain in the epic's, and the difference is the penance done beforehand.","gn":["the simile gains a contrast that shows what penance adds: without it the body is only an abandoned nest","the couplet gains a limit: the epic admits a departure from the body that is not mere desertion but a passage to a better frame"]},{"a":"cc:319","b":"n:33","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar sets the fool who sighs and breaks when deeds ripen against the wise who say 'it is old desert' and go free; Vijayai says exactly that her deeds are to blame, yet sobs (விம்மா) as she says it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain makes knowing one's karma the cure for grief, but the queen shows that naming one's own deeds as the cause can be itself a form of grieving.","gn":["Vijayai's lament gains a measure: her self-blame is the doctrine the wise hold, held without the calm it is said to bring","the quatrain's clean division between fool and wise gains a case that falls between them, a mother who knows the rule and still breaks"]},{"a":"cc:343","b":"cc:319","type":"tension","note":"In 319 Vijayai cries that she has done no good deeds (நல் வினை செய்து இலாதேன்); 343 has the dawn drive off her night like evil deeds' suffering fleeing when old good deeds come forward.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The narrator quietly contradicts the mother's self-blame: the karma she said she lacked is the image he chooses for her deliverance, so her despair is shown to be mistaken about her own merit."},{"a":"cc:343","b":"n:51","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 51 uses lamp and darkness to state that sin cannot stand before austerity and returns when good deeds are spent; 343 makes the departure of darkness at sunrise a figure for evil deeds' suffering leaving when old merit comes forward, on the road to a hermitage.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The quatrain's doctrine becomes the epic's narrative weather: the dawn that ends Vijayai's flight is the merit-and-darkness image itself, placed just before she enters the very austerity the quatrain says sin cannot face.","gn":["the dawn simile gains a doctrinal warrant, so the reader takes it as a forecast that her turn to tapas will hold off misfortune","the quatrain's abstract lamp gains a dated moment in a life, a widow at sunrise on the way to the ascetics, where its rule is seen applied"]},{"a":"cc:347","b":"k:378","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 378 says drily that the destitute would renounce if only fate let what they must suffer pass them by; 347 has a queen stripped of everything reach the ascetics' grove because a former-birth deed made her suffer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic takes up the case the couplet treats as hypothetical: the destitute queen does renounce, and the following verses show the couplet's point surviving the renunciation, since her fated grief for her son goes on inside the hermitage.","gn":["the verse gains a test of whether entering tapas ends the suffering the narrator ascribes to ஊழ்வினை, which the Kural answers in the negative","the couplet's ironic 'if' gains a concrete instance in which renunciation is chosen from loss, not from strength"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:348","b":"si:18:34-53","type":"tension","note":"Kannaki, her husband killed by the king's error, asks whether she must waste away in grief like the women who keep hard observances when their husbands go to the pyre; Vijayai, her husband killed by a usurper, is led by the ascetic women into exactly such observances.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Two wronged wives face the same choice and the epics give opposite answers: Kannaki refuses the widow's penance and turns her grief on the city, while Vijayai accepts it and keeps her grief for a son still living, which shows the son as the difference."},{"a":"cc:351","b":"pn:253","type":"qualifies","note":"In Purananuru 253 a wife whose husband fell asks how she can go among her kin raising an empty hand without bangles; in 351 Vijayai breaks her own bangles in a hermitage among ascetic women.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The widow's bare hand, which the Sangam wife dreads as a sign shown before her kin, becomes in the Jain epic an act the widow performs herself as part of an ascetic initiation among strangers.","gn":["the breaking of bangles is shown to be the old widow's sign, so the initiation reads as widowhood as much as renunciation","the Sangam poem's shame of the empty hand gains a later setting where the same sign is turned into a chosen discipline"]},{"a":"cc:353","b":"k:341","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 341 holds that from whatever one has let go one suffers no pain; Vijayai lets go her ornaments one by one (349 to 353) but ends with a vow for her son, whom she does not let go and for whom she grieves in 355 and 359.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The piecemeal renunciation the couplet describes is acted out jewel by jewel, and the pain the couplet promises to remove returns exactly at the one tie the queen keeps, which shows the couplet's claim from its other side.","gn":["the stripping scene gains a rule by which to measure it, so the son's survival in her vow reads as the unrenounced attachment","the couplet's 'whatever, whatever' gains an ordered sequence of things given up and one thing held, with the pain located where letting go stops"]},{"a":"cc:354","b":"pn:246","type":"tension","note":"Perunkoppendu refuses the widow's life of plain food, gravel bed and no ghee and chooses the pyre; Vijayai refuses the milk offered her and takes up a widow's diet of greens in the forest.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The diet the Sangam queen calls the lot of wretched widows and rejects for death is accepted by the epic's queen and renamed divinity (வனத்து உறை தெய்வம்), which shows how the Jain frame revalues the widow's abstinence the heroic poem despised.","gn":["the verse's greens and refused milk are recognised as the widow's regimen, not only an ascetic's","Perunkoppendu's scorn gains its counterpart, a queen who lives on and makes the same regimen a way of holiness"]},{"a":"cc:355","b":"pn:248","type":"same_image","note":"In Purananuru 248 the white water lily that was a girl's garment becomes the alli a widow eats; in 355 the stamens of ripe water lilies and wild rice dry in the hermitage courtyard as Vijayai's food.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The water lily carries the widow's fall from adornment to bare food in both poems, but in the epic the lily-food sits beside a nursing calf that sends her thoughts to her son, so the widow's diet is joined to a mother's longing the Sangam widow does not have.","gn":["the passing detail of drying lily stamens is identified as the widow's food, so the hermitage scene is read as widowhood","the Sangam widow's lily gains a later life in which the same food marks a mother waiting for her son"]},{"a":"cc:361","b":"cc:323","type":"develops","note":"When Kantukkatan sneezed over the child in 323, a voice hidden in the branches said சீவ, 'live'; in 361 the child is named Civakan.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The hero's name is the fulfilment of an omen at his finding, so the name itself carries the blessing 'live' given over a child left in a burning ground."},{"a":"cc:362","b":"cc:355","type":"same_image","note":"In 355 the mother in the hermitage sees a forest cow suckling its calf and thinks of her son; in 362 the son drinks the milk of a foster mother and grows.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The nursing that Vijayai can only watch in a beast is happening to her own son at another woman's breast, so the two halves of the story are tied by the milk she cannot give."},{"a":"cc:358","b":"cc:314","type":"develops","note":"In 314 a deity took the form of the hunchback Chanbakamalai, the maid who never left Vijayai's side; in 358 the deity leaves under the errand she gave it, calling its own guise a deceit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The disguise that gave the queen comfort in her worst hour is the same means by which she is left alone, and the deity's own word for it, வஞ்சித்தேன், admits the cost of a kindness worked by pretence."},{"a":"cc:385","b":"cc:200","type":"tension","note":"In 200 the narrator introduces Kattiyankaran as a man of flawless learning (வெளிறு இலாக் கேள்வியான்); in 385 the teacher, telling the story to the son, calls him the unlearned minister (கல்லா மந்திரி).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same minister's learning is affirmed by the narrator at his rise and denied by the teacher after his crime, so the epic counts learning by its fruit in conduct, not by what a man has heard."},{"a":"cc:380","b":"cc:215","type":"tension","note":"In 215 a destiny formed before (முனம் ஆகிய பான்மை) sprouts in Saccandan and drives him after the queen to his ruin; in 380 the old deed (ஊழ் வினை) drives his son to refuge in the Arhat.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic uses one mechanism, a ripened past deed pushing a man, to drown the father in pleasure and lift the son to the Jina, and only the son's case adds his own understanding as a second cause."},{"a":"cc:384","b":"cc:223","type":"same_image","note":"Vijayai dreamt in 223 that the ashoka (பிண்டி) fell and a shoot rose from its foot; in 384 the teacher sits in the shade of an ashoka to tell that shoot of the felled tree.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The heir learns of his father's fall under the very tree that figured the fall in his mother's dream, so the revelation scene closes the dream's image on its fulfilment."},{"a":"cc:376","b":"n:331","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 331 shows people revelling in the world's net while Death waits to seize them, like a turtle sporting in the pot over the fire; Cintamani 376 makes Death the one who laid the net and the revellers people who know it is there.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the quatrain, the epic's step is to take away the excuse of ignorance: the turtle does not know its state, but the teacher's 'we' know Death's net and still turn to pleasure, so folly becomes a failure of will.","gn":["the teacher's irony (பெரியமே) is sharpened by the quatrain's ignorant turtle, against which knowing captives look worse","the quatrain's net and killer gain an agent who sets the snare deliberately and an exit it does not name, austerity"]},{"a":"cc:376","b":"k:348","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 348 says that those who have wholly renounced have reached the head and all others are caught in the net; Cintamani 376 names the net as Death's and austerity (நோற்று) as the way out of it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet divides men into renouncers and the netted, while the epic shows the netted as knowing their state, so renunciation is not only clear seeing but a doing that knowledge alone does not bring.","gn":["the epic's net is tied to the Kural's chapter on renunciation, making நோற்று the renouncer's act","the couplet's bare 'net' is given an owner, Death, and a cause for staying in it, the pull of pleasure"]},{"a":"cc:377","b":"k:335","type":"develops","note":"Kural 335 bids good deeds be rushed before the hiccup stops the tongue; Cintamani 377 asks whether anything intended can be done when the fire takes the body, after the teaching that ends birth has been rarely won.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's urgency rests on how near death may be; the epic adds that the means of release is already in hand and wasted by pampering the senses, so delay is the squandering of a rare gain and not only a race with death.","gn":["the teacher's rhetorical question is grounded in a rule, act before the end","the couplet's general 'good deed' is specified as using a teaching already obtained, and its limit moved from the death rattle to the pyre"]},{"a":"cc:375","b":"k:6","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 6 says that those who stand on the path of the one who quenched the five senses live long; Cintamani 375 sets those who reach the path that wears away hard deeds in a sea of bliss and those burnt by the five-mouthed snake of the senses in a sea of pain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet promises long life on the path of one who quenched the senses, the Jain teacher makes the reward the wearing away of karma and the penalty a sea of suffering, turning a blessing of the householder into a choice between release and pain.","gn":["the epic's senses-snake and its path are tied to the Kural's opening praise of the one who quenched the five","the couplet's 'long life' is shown read by a Jain teacher as the destruction of karma, a reading its words allow but do not state"]},{"a":"cc:379","b":"s:821","type":"same_image","note":"Both build a field guarded by a fence against stray beasts: Cintamani 379's crop is life from infancy to old age, its beasts diseases and its fence the vows (சீல வேலி); Pattinattar's field is the heart, its cattle the five senses, its fence calm (சாந்த வேலி), and its fruit Siva's form.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The shared Tamil figure of the fenced crop carries opposite ends: the Jain teacher fences a life to be kept from harm by vowed discipline, while the Saiva poet fences a devotion that ripens into the god, so the doctrine lies in what each names as fence and fruit.","gn":["the teacher's brief farm image is seen as a choice: he fences life itself and names no fruit beyond it","Pattinattar's devotional field is set against a Jain version where no grace ripens, showing his fruit of the five letters as the Saiva addition"]},{"a":"cc:380","b":"t:1527","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 1527: when the two deeds stand balanced, grace comes as the guru, and when one's own agency ends the impurities go; Cintamani 380: a past good deed drives Civakan and his own understanding reaches out, and he takes refuge in the Arhat.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both put a teacher at the turn toward release, but for Tirumular the teacher is grace descending once deeds cancel and one's agency ends, while for the Jain epic it is the push of a good deed and the youth's own understanding that bring him to the teacher's Lord.","gn":["Civakan's conversion is shown to need no descent of grace: karma and his own understanding suffice","the Saiva claim that one's own agency must end is set against a release story in which agency is one of its two causes"]},{"a":"cc:382","b":"k:49","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 49 calls the householder's life itself what is named virtue; in Cintamani 382 the teacher, once Civakan has taken the Jain refuge, teaches him the householder's dharma alongside the errors of false paths.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain teacher does not set renunciation against the household here but gives the new convert the householder's dharma as his first instruction, so the epic holds the Kural's household virtue as a stage inside the path to release rather than its rival.","gn":["the content of the teacher's instruction, left unstated, is placed against the Kural's account of what household virtue is","the couplet's praise of household life is seen kept, but subordinated, by a tradition whose hero will renounce at the end"]},{"a":"cc:387","b":"cc:404","type":"develops","note":"387 names the birth star Uttirattathi; 404 makes the horoscope the cause of the prince's eloquence and beauty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The birth scene's star is not ornament: the teacher reads Civakan's qualities as written at that birth, so the foundling's worth was fixed before his parentage was known."},{"a":"cc:391","b":"cc:392","type":"tension","note":"The teacher tells Civakan that his enemy still stands and not to lament; the next verse shows the rage this rouses, which the teacher must then restrain.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The consolation that turns grief into thoughts of the enemy produces the very anger the teacher at once has to check, so his own counsel makes the year of restraint necessary."},{"a":"cc:393","b":"cc:396","type":"develops","note":"The teacher asks Civakan to bear no anger for a year; his own penance had been undertaken so that fierce anger would wane.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The request for restraint is the teacher's own discipline passed to his pupil, asked by one who knows how hard anger is to wear down."},{"a":"cc:393","b":"cc:407","type":"tension","note":"The teacher binds Civakan to a year without anger toward Kattiyankaran, yet before leaving writes into his mind the policy of destroying enemies.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The ascetic's restraint on his pupil is a postponement of revenge rather than its renunciation: anger is suspended while the means of war are taught."},{"a":"cc:396","b":"cc:408","type":"develops","note":"Sin overtook the ascetic in his first penance; at the end he goes to resume penance to wear down old deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The teacher's life runs in a circle through Civakan's house: a penance broken by the fruit of past deeds is taken up again, with the wearing away of those deeds now named as its aim."},{"a":"cc:405","b":"cc:386","type":"develops","note":"The mother left the newborn to aram in the forest; the grown son now says penance was his mother and the teacher his father.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The child commended to dharma by a departing mother is answered, in the son's own words, by a parentage of penance and teaching, as if aram had taken him up through the ascetic."},{"a":"cc:400","b":"mm:17:1-16","type":"same_image","note":"Both epics give the elephant-fire hunger and both measure it by mountains poured into the sea: here the silver mountain filling the sea, there the hills the monkeys threw into the ocean.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain and the Buddhist epic share the disease and its image, but the Cintamani cures it with a child's meal in a Jain household while the Manimekalai cures it with the bowl of a renunciant, so each makes its own kind of giver the remedy."},{"a":"cc:396","b":"mm:17:33-48","type":"qualifies","note":"Manimekalai traces Kayasandikai's elephant-fire to a named act, crushing an ascetic's fruit, and his curse; the Cintamani says only that sin came upon the ascetic in his penance.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Set beside the Manimekalai's exact chain of act, curse and term, the Cintamani's unnamed sin shows karma striking even a guileless penitent with no deed in this life to explain it."},{"a":"cc:400","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"The couplet ranks the strength of those who relieve hunger above that of those who endure it; the ascetic's endurance fails and a child's feeding saves him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic stages the couplet's ranking as one scene: the ascetic's strength to bear hunger breaks, and the boy's strength to relieve it restores him and gives Civakan his teacher.","gn":["the meal gains a moral rank: the boy's act stands above the ascetic's vow, not merely beside it","the couplet gains a case in which the endurer is himself rescued by the reliever, so the ranking is shown to be needed, not only stated"]},{"a":"cc:396","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet defines penance as bearing the pain that comes; the ascetic's penance gives way under the hunger-fire and he goes to the city to eat.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic marks a limit to the couplet's definition: there are pains, sent by past deeds, that a penitent cannot bear, and his penance has to be suspended and later resumed.","gn":["the ascetic's lapse is measured against a standard, so his leaving the vow reads as failure under pain rather than mere misfortune","the definition gains a case where the pain that comes is beyond bearing, raising what penance does then"]},{"a":"cc:393","b":"k:303","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet bids one forget anger at anyone at all; the teacher asks Civakan to hold his anger against one man for one year only.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Against the couplet's unlimited forgetting of anger, the epic's ascetic sets a term to restraint, so a teacher who renounced anger for himself still leaves a prince's just anger intact for its time.","gn":["the request is seen to be partial: it is a delay with an end, not the forgetting the ethics books ask","the couplet's 'at anyone at all' is tested by the case of a usurper who killed one's father, where even an ascetic does not ask for it"]},{"a":"cc:406","b":"k:46","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet asks what one gains by leaving a household life lived in dharma; the epic holds that once a man resolves to leave home for penance, the wise speak of no other dharma.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The two meet on a condition the couplet leaves out: the Kural weighs the householder's path before the choice, while the epic speaks of one who has already resolved, for whom the household dharma is no longer an option.","gn":["the epic's claim is placed against the strongest defence of the household, so its scope (one who has resolved) becomes visible","the couplet's rhetorical question gains an answer from a man who has lived both lives, an ascetic who spent years in a merchant's house and returns to penance"]},{"a":"cc:401","b":"k:87","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says the fruit of hospitality is as great as the guest's worth; the merchant's house that fed a hungry stranger gained a Vidyadhara king as the teacher of its son.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the couplet's measure working through time: the guest's worth was hidden when he came hungry to the door, and the fruit appeared only as the education of the boy who fed him.","gn":["the long stay of the cured ascetic is read as the return on a gift, which the verse itself leaves implicit","the couplet's measure by the guest's worth gains a case where that worth was unknown to the host, so hospitality cannot be rationed by it"]},{"a":"cc:408","b":"t:1527","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular has grace come as the guru and end the soul's own doing; in the Cintamani the guru is himself a karma-bound ascetic who goes off to wear down his old deeds by his own penance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two texts set the guru on opposite sides of karma: for Tirumular the guru is the descent of grace that ends one's deeds, while the Cintamani's teacher, having formed his pupil, must still work off his own past by effort.","gn":["the teacher's departure is seen as a doctrinal statement, release by one's own penance with no grace to end it","the Saiva verse's guru as grace gains a clear contrast, a guru who needs release himself, which sharpens what 'guru as grace' claims"]},{"a":"cc:409","b":"n:27","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the rain bubble (மாரி மொக்குளின், படுமழை மொக்குளின்): the Naladiyar for the body that keeps forming and bursting, the epic for the moment the released teacher's form ends.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows the bubble used for opposite ends: in the quatrain its bursting is why the wise resolve to end their wandering, and in the epic the bursting is that resolve completed, the last end of a body that will not form again.","gn":["the epic's simile gains the sense of repeated birth the quatrain gives the bubble, so Accanandi's vanishing reads as the end of the round, not just a death","the quatrain's resolve to end the wandering gains a narrated case of someone who did it, and what its end looks like"]},{"a":"cc:409","b":"s:80","type":"develops","note":"Bhadragiriyar asks when he will cut the sunken root of desire at its base; the epic says Accanandi cut the root of desire (ஆர்வ வேர் அரிந்து) and went on to austerity and release.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same image of desire as a root to be cut is a lament of 'when?' in the Siddhar and a finished first step in the Jain epic, where it is only the start of a path that still needs austerity before release.","gn":["the epic's one-phrase report gains the inner struggle the Siddhar voices, so the reader sees how hard that cutting was meant to be","the Siddhar's longing gains a sequel in another tradition: what is said to follow once the root is cut"]},{"a":"cc:409","b":"k:365","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural calls those free of craving the freed (அற்றவர்); the epic has Accanandi cut desire's root and then perform austerities before he leaves the body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the couplet, the verse separates what the Kural joins: being free of craving comes first, but release from the body comes only after austerity at the Jina's feet.","gn":["the epic's sequence gains the Kural's claim that ending craving is the decisive freedom, which explains why it stands first","the couplet's freedom gains a narrative that spells out what still remains after craving goes: penance and the body's end"]},{"a":"cc:416","b":"cc:437","type":"develops","note":"The omen-reader foretells that the king's army will break leaving umbrella and பிச்சம்; in 437 the army breaks, throwing down elephants, peacock-plumed standards, பிச்சம் and banners.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The first half of the prophecy comes true to the detail of the dropped standards, which gives the reader warrant to trust its second half before Civakan has even set out."},{"a":"cc:416","b":"cc:449","type":"develops","note":"The prophecy ends with the raiders broken by one man in a chariot; in 449 the hunters, stunned by the roar of the generous one's chariot, lose heart and give up the fight.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poet names Civakan only through the hunters' own omen, so the hero's victory is foretold by his enemies before it is told by the narrator."},{"a":"cc:417","b":"cc:450","type":"tension","note":"The hunter boasts that one man in a chariot can do nothing even if he is Death; in 450 the hunters say a chariot-flood has surrounded them, victory is hard, and a hunter lied to them.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The raiders' contempt for a single foe is reversed by their own words, and 450's charge that a hunter lied shows them blaming a speaker rather than their dismissal of the omen."},{"a":"cc:420","b":"cc:415","type":"same_image","note":"Both sides consult birds: the hunters' omen-reader reads a bird's cry before the raid, and the herders' announcer warns of an ill-omened bird; each side goes ahead.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The episode gives an omen to raiders and herders alike and lets both ignore it, so omens here foretell outcomes without ever restraining anyone."},{"a":"cc:429","b":"k:560","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the cows' yield fails when the guardian does not guard; the epic shows the king's herd driven off while the king sits fanned on his throne, and 437-438 his army breaks.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing turns the Kural's cows into the actual herd of the realm: the raid becomes the first public proof that the usurper cannot guard, and clears the place for Civakan, who will.","gn":["the cattle raid gains a political sense beyond adventure: it measures Kattiyankaran as guardian","the couplet's abstract link between guarding and the yield of cows gains a concrete scene of what failure to guard looks like"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:440","b":"cc:479","type":"develops","note":"Nandagon's drum promises the maiden to whoever brings back the herd; in 479 the herd chief, now naming himself Govindan, offers her with seven golden images to Civakan.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The public prize proclaimed to all the city's warriors is paid to the one outsider who answered it, which makes Govindai's marriage a contract kept rather than a match made."},{"a":"cc:438","b":"cc:455","type":"tension","note":"Kattiyangaran sinks in grief when the herd is lost; when he hears Civakan has recovered it he is enraged as if a spear were thrust into a sword wound.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The usurper grieves the loss of his herd and rages at its return, showing that what wounds him is a rival's glory, not the people's loss."},{"a":"cc:446","b":"cc:453","type":"tension","note":"The hunters claim the forest as their deep water where even the strong will fall; in 453 Civakan alone destroys six thousand of them there.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The maxim of one's own ground is turned against the very people who invoked it, so the story shows it fails when the opponent is misjudged."},{"a":"cc:446","b":"k:495","type":"qualifies","note":"The hunters restate the couplet's crocodile, strong in deep water, and apply it to themselves in their forest.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's rule of ground is here voiced by the losing side, so the pair shows the rule cannot by itself predict a fight and must be weighed against the enemy's strength.","gn":["the hunters' boast gains a known maxim of statecraft, so their defeat reads as a misapplied rule and not mere bluster","the couplet gains a case where the strong-on-its-ground party still loses, a limit the bare maxim does not state"]},{"a":"cc:443","b":"pn:9","type":"develops","note":"The Pandya's war proclamation in Puram 9 tells cows, brahmins, women, the sick and the sonless to get clear before battle; Civakan names cows, renunciants, women, children and brahmins as those he must protect.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same list of the protected is in the Sangam poem those a king spares from his own arrows, and in the epic those a warrior must defend from another's raid, so the duty widens from restraint to rescue.","gn":["Civakan's oath gains a Sangam pedigree: his list is the old code of who stands outside war","the Puram poem's code of sparing gains its active counterpart, an obligation to defend the same people when someone else attacks them"]},{"a":"cc:443","b":"t:243","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular threatens unending hell to a king who fails to guard cows, women, the virtuous and the holy; Civakan, not a king, binds himself to the same guard on pain of a eunuch's shame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the duty to guard cows, women and the holy passes from the failed king to a private man, and its sanction moves from hell after death to dishonour in this life.","gn":["the oath gains a measure of what the king himself owed and failed to give, which Civakan supplies","Tirumular's rule, framed for the king's office, gains a case where the office fails and the duty is taken up by one who holds no office"]},{"a":"cc:454","b":"cc:11","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's line வஞ்சம் மறவர் நிரை வள்ளல் விடுத்த வாறும் announces that the liberal one freed the herd from deceitful hunters; 451 to 455 tell it, ending with the herd turned by the bow's sound and no man lost.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam's word வள்ளல் for the rescuer of a herd only makes sense once the telling shows a recovery that is given back to its owners without loss, so the summary's epithet anticipates the bloodless manner of the deed."},{"a":"cc:463","b":"cc:26","type":"develops","note":"The city women bless the unknown youth with பூமியும் புணர்க, 'may he wed the earth too'; the patikam's verse 26 puts his marriage to his uncle's daughter and the spread of his umbrella and sceptre over the land in one verse.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic pairs bride and kingdom twice, once as a crowd's wedding blessing on a merchant's son who does not know his birth and once as the outcome, so the blessing works as an unwitting prophecy of his recovery of the throne."},{"a":"cc:467","b":"cc:29","type":"tension","note":"The women hold that union with Civakan is the reward of penance (அண்ணலைத் தவத்திற் றந்தார்); the patikam ends with Civakan, as one who has eaten nectar, no longer wanting the pleasure of women.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic plants early the crowd's belief that penance buys the pleasure of this man, and its own close makes the same man's penance the end of all such pleasure, so the word தவம் is turned against the hope it first expressed.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:452","b":"si:12:128-131","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Vettuva Vari the herds the hunters' brothers brought back from the raid fill the yards of the smith, the drummer and the yazh bards; in the Cintamani the hunters are routed and the herd turns back to the shouting cowherds.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two epics show the one cattle raid from both ends: the Silappathikaram's hunters' seizure feeds a whole village of dependents, while the Cintamani's recovery returns the same kind of herd to its keepers and leaves the raiders faceless."},{"a":"cc:454","b":"pn:257","type":"qualifies","note":"The Purananuru's warrior, with no chariot and no forest fort, watched the enemy's herd go, counted it and turned it with his bow (சிலையின் மாற்றியோனே); Civakan turns the herd by the bow's twang alone (சிலையின் னொலியால் தொறு மீட்டு).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the Sangam feat of turning a herd with the bow but removes even the drawn arrow and gives the deed to a city's darling, so a poor warrior's unsung skill becomes a prince's proof of worth before the town.","gn":["the Cintamani's bow-sound recovery is shown to rest on an existing heroic type, the lone bowman who turns a herd, rather than on the poet's invention","the Puram poem's quiet single-handed recovery is seen to be the seed of a scene that later poetry could magnify into public triumph and crowd adoration"]},{"a":"cc:465","b":"k:265","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 265 says penance (செய்தவம்) is undertaken because what one wants is got as one wants it; the city women say Civakan's mother must have done penance (செய்தவம் உடையள்) and ask on which mountain to do it so that they too may attain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's frank promise that penance gets what one wants is taken literally by lovesick townswomen, who plan austerity on a mountain to win a man, showing how the Kural's motive can be bent toward the very desire the next couplet condemns.","gn":["the women's talk is shown to repeat a recognised ethical doctrine of the fruits of penance, not merely a crowd's fancy","the couplet's unqualified wording is shown to license a use, penance for erotic gain, that its own chapter would reject"]},{"a":"cc:466","b":"k:1214","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1214 the woman finds pleasure in dreams because they bring the man who gives no grace when she is awake (நல்காரை); the women of 466 ask Civakan to grace them (அருளி) by coming in a dream tomorrow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's dream comforts a woman for a lover who once was hers and now withholds himself, but the Cintamani's crowd asks the dream for a man they have only seen pass, so the convention turns from consolation for loss into the only union a stranger can hope for.","gn":["the crowd's prayer is shown to draw on the akam convention that the dream does what the absent man will not","the couplet's dream-remedy is shown stretching beyond separation to love that never had a meeting, which the couplet's situation does not cover"]},{"a":"cc:475","b":"cc:215","type":"qualifies","note":"At 215 the narrator says the king ignored counsel because a destiny formed before sprouted in him; Nandagon, telling the same fall, blames only the king's own desire and choice.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps two causes for Saccandan's fall in two voices, so a former deed in the narrator's account never excuses the choice his subject remembers."},{"a":"cc:476","b":"cc:26","type":"develops","note":"Nandagon turned back from suicide hoping, like a banyan seed, that the royal line survived; the patikam at 26 has Civakan spread his just sceptre over the land.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The cowherd's private hope is the plan of the whole epic, and he states it to the very heir he thinks may be lost."},{"a":"cc:476","b":"n:197","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar says that when a banyan is eaten hollow its aerial roots hold it up, as a son covers a weakened father; Nandagon's banyan seed is the hope that a son of the fallen king lives.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the quatrain, the herdsman's seed image takes on the son's work of propping a fallen house, which the epic will make a war for a kingdom rather than a household's shame.","gn":["the obscure seed image is given its point: a hidden son is the root that will hold the line up","the household maxim is seen stretched to a dynasty, where the father is dead and the son does not yet know whom he props"]},{"a":"cc:482","b":"cc:483","type":"tension","note":"Nandagon argues from Murukan and Vishnu that lineage should not be thought of in marriage; Civakan at once thinks that a man may not take an unequal wife before one of his own rank.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The hero answers the herdsman's mythic precedent not by denying unequal marriage but by ranking it, so birth still governs the order of wives in a story that lets him marry across it."},{"a":"cc:483","b":"k:972","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says birth is alike for all beings and distinction comes from the work they do; Civakan holds that birth decides which wife a man may take first.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The pair shows the Kural's levelling of birth stopping at the threshold of marriage in the epic, where rank of birth remains a rule of conduct even for a hero honoured for his deeds.","gn":["the hero's private rule is seen against a classic denial that birth confers worth, which makes its reliance on birth visible","the couplet's claim is tested on a case it does not name, the ordering of marriages, where birth kept a force the couplet does not grant it"]},{"a":"cc:479","b":"cc:440","type":"develops","note":"At 440 Nandagon had it proclaimed that the maiden would be given to whoever brought back the herd; at 479 he offers her to Civakan, adding seven golden statues.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The proclamation is kept to the letter, yet the hero's decline at 483-484 shows the prize could be honoured only by being passed to another."},{"a":"cc:484","b":"cc:489","type":"develops","note":"Civakan's acceptance in 484 turns on an obscure kinship word; at 489 he receives the gift water on behalf of Padumukan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read from 489, Civakan's quick acceptance was always an acceptance for his friend, which lets him keep both Nandagon's honour and his own rule of rank."},{"a":"cc:489","b":"kl:102","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Kalittokai the herd girl is cried as the prize of whoever takes the fighting bull, and the taker himself has her; here the herd chief's daughter is the prize for recovering the herd, and the winner passes her to a friend.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the mullai custom of a herd girl won by a public feat, but moves the feat from the herders' bull-pen to a prince's war and lets the prize be transferred, which the bull-catch, where the girl goes to the man who wrestled, does not allow.","gn":["the herders' wedding is seen to rest on an old custom of the herd girl as prize of a feat, which explains the father's proclamation in 440","the bull-catch's bond between feat and husband is shown to be what a later courtly telling could loosen, the girl becoming a gift between men"]},{"a":"cc:491","b":"k:1166","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says the pleasure of love is a sea but its pain, when it strikes, is greater; 491 gives the bridegroom the sea of pleasure that cannot be contained, and no pain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic uses the couplet's measure of pleasure as a sea but leaves out the greater sea of pain that the woman in the Kural speaks from, because the wedding has no separation in it.","gn":["the uncontained sea of 491 is seen to be half of a measure, the half that a married union can claim","the couplet's first clause is given a scene where it stands alone, which shows its second clause belongs to separation"]},{"a":"cc:492","b":"cc:216","type":"same_image","note":"At 216 Saccandan drinks unwearied the nectar of Vijayai and his fall begins; at 491-492 Padumukan drinks Govindai's nectar and loses all awareness in unguarded pleasure.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poet gives the bridegroom the very image of the king whose drinking cost him his kingdom, so the epic's warning about pleasure lies under even its happiest wedding, though the narrator blames no one here."},{"a":"cc:495","b":"cc:510","type":"tension","note":"In 495 Sitattan calls it a failure of understanding to blame former deeds when fortune turns; in 510, in the storm, he makes the fruit of former deeds the only ground on which the good survive.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The merchant's creed of effort over fate lasts only until the sea rises; under the storm the same man falls back on karma, so the epic stages the Kural's own pull between effort and fate as one character's change of mind."},{"a":"cc:497","b":"cc:514","type":"tension","note":"497 ends பொய்யில் பொருளே பொருள்; 514 has the shipwrecked merchant tell himself பொருளைப் பொருள் என்று கொண்டாய், as a reproach.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The wordplay on பொருள் as wealth and as the thing of worth is turned against its speaker, so the wreck reads as a verdict on his creed and not only as bad luck."},{"a":"cc:510","b":"cc:213","type":"develops","note":"In 213 King Saccandan dismisses warning by saying pleasures and days of life are measured out in advance; in 510 Sitattan uses the same doctrine of a measured life-span to steady his crew, and alone survives.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The doctrine of the measured life-span is voiced twice before a disaster, once as an excuse for heedlessness that ends in a king's death and once as courage that ends in rescue, so its worth in the epic depends on what the speaker does with it."},{"a":"cc:497","b":"k:759","type":"qualifies","note":"497 restates Kural 759 (make wealth; no steel is sharper against foes) and joins to it the claims of 760 and 751 as a merchant's motto, a few verses before the sea swallows the wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Put in the mouth of a man about to lose everything at sea, the Kural's praise of wealth as the sharpest steel is shown to say nothing about wealth's own exposure to loss.","gn":["the merchant's motto gains its source and is seen as a borrowed rule of statecraft applied to trade","the couplet gains a test case in which the wealth it praises is destroyed by forces no amount of it can subdue"]},{"a":"cc:509","b":"k:621","type":"develops","note":"509 opens as Kural 621 opens and turns the couplet's laughter into a steel that cuts trouble, spoken by a captain to his crew in a storm that drowns them all but him.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's laugh in trouble becomes a leader's duty to others, and the scene shows its limit: composure saves the one who keeps it but cannot save the ship.","gn":["Sitattan's exhortation gains a known maxim behind it, which explains why he speaks in aphorisms at the height of the storm","the couplet's private resolve gains a public setting, where the person who laughs is responsible for others who perish"]},{"a":"cc:496","b":"k:620","type":"qualifies","note":"496 holds that a resolute man's effort can bring a flood of wealth in a day and that nothing fails to yield to it; Kural 620 holds that tireless effort sees even fate's back.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic puts the Kural's boldest claim for effort in the mouth of a man whose voyage ends in shipwreck, so the claim is made to depend on the life-span and deeds he later invokes.","gn":["the merchant's boast gains the weight of a rule from the Kural's chapter on exertion","the couplet gains a story in which tireless effort does meet fate head on and loses the wealth while the man survives"]},{"a":"cc:498","b":"k:1026","type":"develops","note":"498 gives the merchant's reason for the voyage: men born into a weakening lineage must hold it up. Kural 1026 defines good manliness as making oneself able to rule the house one was born into.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's manliness of the house is the motive that sends a merchant onto the sea, and the verse adds a division the couplet lacks: restoring the line is the men's part, guarding against shame the women's.","gn":["the merchant's resolve gains a named ideal, so the voyage reads as family duty and not greed","the couplet gains a concrete undertaking and a risk: ruling one's house can mean staking one's life on a voyage"]},{"a":"cc:513","b":"n:93","type":"agrees","note":"513 shows the wealth Sitattan guarded and gathered (ஓம்பிப் படைத்த பொருள்) swallowed by the sea while he stands unhurried; Naladiyar 93 holds that wealth, however tightly held, leaves when the deeds that sent it run out.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with the quatrain, the wreck becomes the ending of a merit that had held the wealth, and the merchant's calm becomes the right response to a loss that no grip could prevent.","gn":["the loss of the cargo gains a cause the verse does not give, the exhaustion of the merit that brought it","the quatrain's abstract leaving of wealth gains a sudden, visible event and a man who loses it without clutching"]},{"a":"cc:496","b":"s:641","type":"tension","note":"496 has the merchant hold that effort alone can bring a flood of wealth in a day; Siddhar 641 tells the heart that the wealth it has came by the order of deeds done in earlier lives and was not got today.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two disagree about where wealth comes from, today's effort or earlier lives, and the epic lets its merchant hold the first view only until the storm turns him to the second.","gn":["the merchant's creed gains its direct opposite, which shows how much it denies","the Siddhar's claim gains an opponent who believes the reverse and is then brought round by events"]},{"a":"cc:520","b":"cc:574","type":"tension","note":"Dharan vows to restore all without any deceit (வஞ்சமொன்று இன்றி); in 574 the Vidyadhara lord himself declares the storm was a deceit (வஞ்சம்).","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The word that guarantees the promise is the word later used to explain the whole wreck, so the rescuer's honesty consists in undoing a lie he told."},{"a":"cc:520","b":"cc:582","type":"develops","note":"The 'dead companions' Dharan promises to return meet the merchant alive and laugh that what they suffered at sea was a play (நாடகம்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The impossible promise to raise the dead is kept only because the deaths were staged, which turns the earlier lament for lost friends into an audience's grief."},{"a":"cc:520","b":"k:292","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 292 lets a falsehood stand as truth when it yields good free of fault; Dharan's wreck and his 'no deceit' promise are falsehoods serving his king's purpose of bringing a guardian for the princess.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair tests the couplet's limit: the epic's lie does produce good, but at the cost of a man's real grief for friends he believed drowned, so whether its good is 'free of fault' becomes the question.","gn":["a measure by which to judge Dharan's staged disaster, which the story itself excuses with laughter in 582","a concrete case where the beneficial lie causes real suffering before the benefit arrives, which the couplet's bare rule does not weigh"]},{"a":"cc:533","b":"cc:514","type":"tension","note":"In 514 the shipwrecked merchant is reproached for taking wealth as the one thing of worth; in 533 he enters a city where jewels lie in the street because no one wants them.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The man who lost everything for wealth is led into a place where wealth has no pull, so the description quietly answers the reproach of the wreck."},{"a":"cc:539","b":"cc:11","type":"develops","note":"The astrologer names Rajamapuram as the place of the princess's destined husband; the patikam (11) already tells that the Vidyadhara king's daughter was defeated at the vina.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prophecy that sends the princess to earth is the start of the contest the patikam announced, so her defeat at the vina is the means by which her horoscope comes true."},{"a":"cc:539","b":"cc:554","type":"develops","note":"The astrologer's reading of her பான்மை is taken up by the king in 554, who refuses every suitor because none can stop a portion when its time comes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A horoscope cast at birth becomes, years later, the king's reason for refusing all suitors, so the prophecy governs a political decision rather than merely forecasting a marriage."},{"a":"cc:540","b":"k:376","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 376 says what is not one's portion will not stay however guarded and what is one's portion will not leave; the king, told his daughter's portion lies in Rajamapuram, neither gives her to the gift-bearing kings nor tells them so, but keeps them waiting with his intent hidden.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that knowing one's portion in advance does not end action: the king turns the couplet's resignation into policy, managing powerful suitors while waiting for fate to arrive.","gn":["a general rule of allotted portion behind the king's refusal to accept the suitors' riches","a case where the rule is applied to a daughter rather than to wealth, and where the one who knows the portion must still act with craft to protect it"]},{"a":"cc:544","b":"k:107","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says one remembers for seven births the friendship of those who wiped away one's sorrow; the Vidyadhara king says his people and the merchant's have held friendship for seven generations, bound by fate.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Side by side, the seven-fold span of friendship rests on two different grounds: in the couplet on gratitude for a single act of rescue, in the epic on an inherited tie that fate fixes and no deed has to earn.","gn":["the king's claim gains a measure of what such a bond is normally owed for, showing how much he gives in asking nothing of the merchant","the couplet's seven births gain a case where the span runs through generations of a family rather than one person's rebirths"]},{"a":"cc:553","b":"k:1103","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's lover asks whether the lotus-eyed god's world is sweeter than sleep on a beloved's arms; the Vidyadhara king says even the endless pleasures of the Jain land of enjoyment are misery alone, and sleep in a lover's lap is the gods' own state.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet leaves its comparison with heaven as a question, and the epic answers it outright, turning a lover's boast into a father's argument for giving his daughter in marriage.","gn":["the king's maxim gains the akam voice behind it, the man who first set the embrace against heaven","the couplet's open question gains an answer, and a use outside the lovers' scene, as a reason to marry a daughter"]},{"a":"cc:554","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says nothing is stronger than fate, which gets ahead of every contrivance; the king says that when the destined time comes no one can stop it, and so refuses every royal suitor.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Read with the couplet, the king's appeal to fate is not a surrender but a policy: he uses fate's strength as the reason to refuse every suitor and leave the choice to a contest, where the Kural sees fate only as defeating a plan.","gn":["the king's line gains the Kural's general law, which explains why even begging kings cannot move him","the couplet gains a case where acknowledging fate guides a decision instead of frustrating one"]},{"a":"cc:563","b":"n:274","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar likens a miser's hoard, enjoyed by a stranger, to a lovely daughter of the house who goes to an outsider in due season; the king says daughters are like the conch's pearls, which profit others and never the conch.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same commonplace, that a daughter is raised to go to another, serves the quatrain as an image of loss through folly and serves the epic as a father's consolation, so the pair shows the saying could carry blame or comfort.","gn":["the king's saying gains the quatrain's point that the daughter's going is as certain as a season, which is why it can console","the quatrain's simile gains a scene in which the daughter's leaving is felt by the father, not only used as an image"]},{"a":"cc:553","b":"cc:28","type":"tension","note":"The Vidyadhara king calls living alone the greatest misery even amid heavenly pleasure; the patikam ends the epic with Civakan, his brother, friends and wives renouncing the senses and going to release.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The king's praise of married joy over solitary pleasure is one character's view, and the epic's own close, where the household and its pleasures are renounced for a higher state, rejects it as a final value."},{"a":"cc:554","b":"cc:11","type":"develops","note":"The king refuses all suitors, citing a seer's word and the destined time; the patikam names among the episodes how the Vidyadhara king's daughter was defeated at the vina.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam already tells the outcome the king says no one can prevent, so the father's appeal to destiny is answered by the epic's own plan: the destined man is the hero."},{"a":"cc:574","b":"cc:511","type":"qualifies","note":"Sridattan told his crew in the storm that their suffering was the ripening of old deeds; Dharan now says the wreck was his own staged deceit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic lets the merchant read the wreck as karma and then shows a planned human cause behind it, so the karmic explanation stands beside an agent's plan rather than replacing it."},{"a":"cc:580","b":"cc:513","type":"tension","note":"513 has the sea swallow the merchant's guarded wealth and the loved ones with him; 580 shows the ship and the companions intact.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The loss that the wreck scene narrates as final is revealed as illusion, so the earlier verse's grief was real while its facts were false."},{"a":"cc:582","b":"cc:509","type":"develops","note":"In the storm Sridattan urged laughter in trouble; after the rescue his companions laugh that the wreck was a play.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The laughter urged as courage before the outcome returns as relief after it, which shows how differently the same laugh weighs once the danger is known to be staged."},{"a":"cc:577","b":"cc:563","type":"develops","note":"The astrologer's verdict that the maiden's life lies on the earth is what the king turned into his parting saying that daughters, like a conch's pearls, profit those on the earth.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The king's consoling maxim at the parting rests on the astrologer's prophecy told only now, so his resignation is obedience to a foretold fate as well as a father's wisdom."},{"a":"cc:577","b":"n:52","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar calls those who prattle of astrology the greatest fools; the epic has a king act on an astrologer's word, which proves true.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Two Jain-tradition texts judge the astrologer oppositely: the quatrain dismisses his lore beside the duty death makes urgent, while the epic makes his reading the hinge of its plot. The tension holds only if the quatrain is read as denying astrology's truth, not just its worth.","gn":["the epic's trust in the astrologer is set against a strand of its own tradition that scorns that lore","the quatrain's scorn is shown to stand in a renouncer's frame that a court narrative does not share"]},{"a":"cc:579","b":"n:209","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar says friends who share joys but not sorrows do nothing for each other; Dharan inflicts sorrow on his king's friend to give him joy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's test assumes sorrow comes from outside and asks who will share it, while the epic's friend authors the sorrow himself and stands apart from it, a case the rule of sharing does not cover.","gn":["Dharan's apology is measured against a standard of friendship that his deceit plainly fails","the quatrain's rule meets a friend who causes, rather than shares, the grief, which shows its limit"]},{"a":"cc:609","b":"cc:297","type":"tension","note":"In 297 the usurper has the drum beaten in his own name after burning his king and 'takes Fortune'; in 609 the city's drum blesses first the lord who embraces the earth as his bride.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same drum that announced a murderer's seizure now opens a wedding proclamation by hailing him as the earth's rightful husband, so the formula of royal blessing shows how quickly usurpation becomes the ordinary order of the city."},{"a":"cc:607","b":"cc:556","type":"develops","note":"In 556 the Vidyadhara king asks the merchant to be father and mother to the princess; in 607 the proclamation calls the merchant her father outright.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The private charge to stand in a parent's place becomes, in the public decree, plain fatherhood, so the trust hides the princess's Vidyadhara birth from the city that will compete for her."},{"a":"cc:610","b":"cc:539","type":"develops","note":"The astrologer in 539 places the princess's destined husband in Rajamapuram; 610-612 bring kings from Hastinapuram, Kampili and Varanavasi to win her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read against the horoscope, the far kings' arrival is a journey already in vain, and the open contest is the means by which the prophecy picks its man from the city it named."},{"a":"cc:605","b":"k:263","type":"develops","note":"Kural 263 asks whether others, wanting to provide for renouncers, have forgotten their own penance; the proclamation in 605 bids people give to ascetics and also strive at hard penance themselves.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic's decree answers the couplet's chiding question by refusing the division of labour it criticises: giving to ascetics and doing penance are both laid on every listener.","gn":["a sharp statement of the danger its double command guards against, that support for ascetics could stand in for one's own penance","a public, Jain-framed instance where the couplet's implied rule, that penance is not only for renouncers, is proclaimed to a whole city"]},{"a":"cc:604","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 55 makes the chaste wife command the rain; the benediction of 604 asks for rain and for women's unswerving chastity side by side as goods of the realm.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair shows two ways of joining chastity and rain: the couplet gives one wife's karpu power over the sky, while the city's blessing wishes for both as gifts and gives women's chastity no power at all.","gn":["a sense of how strong a link between chastity and rain the tradition could make, which the benediction leaves unclaimed","a civic setting where the couplet's single wife becomes all the women of a city, wished steadfast rather than credited with power"]},{"a":"cc:605","b":"t:724","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 724 nurtures the body because without it true knowledge cannot be gained; 605 bids all strive at hard penance while still in the body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make the living body the only place where release can be worked for, but the proclamation spends the body in austerity while Tirumular guards it as the means of knowledge, so the same premise yields discipline in one and care in the other.","gn":["a statement of why 'while in the body' matters, which the proclamation's compressed phrase leaves unsaid","a contrasting use of the body as the seat of penance, in a Jain frame, against which Tirumular's nurture of it stands out as a choice"]},{"a":"cc:619","b":"cc:11","type":"tension","note":"The companion is sure the crowned suitors will drown in the flood of the princess's music; the patikam has already told the story as the Vidyadhara king's daughter losing at the vina.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The companion's certainty is half right, since the kings do lose, but the one it never imagines losing is the princess herself, so the reader hears her boast through the outcome the poem announced at the start."},{"a":"cc:621","b":"cc:539","type":"develops","note":"The Vidyadhara astrologer read in the planets that her destined husband lives in Rajamapuram; here an omen-reader in Rajamapuram fixes the contest's hour from the moon's conjunction.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The open contest is framed by two readings of the stars, one naming the city and one naming the hour, so what looks like a public trial of skill is staged as the working out of a decree already read."},{"a":"cc:613","b":"cc:540","type":"develops","note":"Kaluzhavegan kept the suitor kings in hope without telling them his mind; now the kings of the earth arrive in Rajamapuram, the city the horoscope named.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The king's hidden purpose turns his daughter's suitors into unknowing escorts to the city where her destined husband lives, so their rivalry serves a plan they were never told.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:636","b":"cc:29","type":"tension","note":"Those who saw the princess, the narrator says, would never look upon the world of release; at the epic's end Civakan, having tasted the pleasure of women whose beauty makes the heavens bow, wants it no more and goes to release.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The hyperbole that beauty shuts out release is a claim the epic's own ending answers, since its hero is the one who saw all such beauty and still reached release, so the line marks the stake of the story rather than its verdict."},{"a":"cc:636","b":"k:1103","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's lover asks whether the lotus-eyed god's world is sweeter than sleep in the beloved's arms; the Cintamani's narrator says those who saw the princess would not look upon the world of release.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both weigh a woman's beauty against a world beyond this one, but the Jain epic raises the Kural's god's heaven to release from births and gives the preference to dazzled onlookers rather than a lover, which makes it a danger to be overcome rather than a joy to be asked about.","gn":["the epic's hyperbole is shown to be a set figure of the lover's voice inherited from the Kural's third book, not a doctrine of the narrator","the couplet's open question gets an answer from a tradition for which the other world is release, where preferring beauty is a peril to the soul, which the couplet does not consider"]},{"a":"cc:626","b":"n:44","type":"tension","note":"The attendants call the princess's eyes not carp but blue lilies sheltering in the moon and line them with kohl; the Naladiyar's renunciant scorns those who call eyes water-lilies, carp or spears and sees the eye as a scooped-out palm fruit.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The comparisons the Jain quatrain names as the talk of the blind are exactly the ones a Jain epic's attendants weigh and choose between, so the pair shows the same tradition keeping the lover's rhetoric in its epic and rejecting it in its ascetic verse.","gn":["the epic's ornate conceit is set against a reading in which it is the very speech that endangers the renunciant, which the epic leaves unsaid until its last book","the quatrain's anonymous praisers of eyes get a concrete scene, women adorning a bride for a crowd, showing the rhetoric it resists at work in public"]},{"a":"cc:644","b":"k:1081","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's lover cannot tell whether the woman is a goddess, a peahen or a woman; the Cintamani's kings ask how she is not Rambha and then notice that her eyes blink (இமைக்குங்கண் இவையோ).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the lover's doubt can be settled by a sign: where the couplet leaves the heart reeling among three guesses, the epic supplies the blink that separates a mortal from the unblinking gods and ends the doubt.","gn":["the kings' remark is seen as the closing move of a set doubt-scene whose first move the Kural states","the couplet's unresolved question gains a test of the kind its own three guesses never apply"]},{"a":"cc:637","b":"k:1083","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet's man says he never knew Death and knows it now in a woman's eyes; the onlookers tell the suitors that her girdle and breasts are Death and bid them flee, 'we have told you'.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read with the couplet, the onlookers' warning puts the suitors in the state the Kural's man has left behind, knowing Death only by report, and shows that being told does not save anyone, since the crowd stays.","gn":["the warning gains its irony from the couplet's contrast between Death heard of and Death met","the couplet's private discovery gains a public scene in which the same knowledge is offered as advice and goes unheeded"]},{"a":"cc:653","b":"k:1087","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural likens the cloth over a woman's breasts to the face-cloth on a rutting elephant; Vinapati jests that the gods hid her breasts altogether because her eyes already killed.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two take opposite sides of one premise, that a woman's breasts are a danger to be covered, and the pedi's jest pushes it to the end by having the gods remove what the cloth only veils, with the eyes left as the whole of the threat.","gn":["the obscure jest becomes legible as a play on a known figure of the veiled danger","the couplet's covering gains its limit case, a body in which the covered danger is absent and the eyes do all the harm"]},{"a":"cc:640","b":"k:265","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says tavam is striven for because it gets whatever one wants as one wants it; the kings vow that if they lose her they will fight, or else do great penance.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The kings take penance on the couplet's own motive, as a way to the thing desired, and so show the weakness the couplet's next verse attacks: penance offered as the consolation of a failed suitor stays bound to the desire it should end.","gn":["the kings' fallback is seen as a reading of tavam the Kural itself supplies","the couplet's promise of getting what one wants gains a speaker for whom the wanted thing is a woman lost at a contest"]},{"a":"cc:640","b":"cc:467","type":"same_image","note":"In 467 the city women say only those whose penance has earned it may drink Civakan's beauty; in 640 the suitor kings say that if they cannot win Gandharvadattai they will do penance.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the same belief to both sexes, that tavam is the price of a beloved, but turns it round: the women look to past penance as the cause of a lucky union, the kings to future penance as the comfort for a lost one."},{"a":"cc:659","b":"cc:657","type":"tension","note":"At Gandharvadattai's song the trees bud and the birds fall entranced; at the song of the suitors, called faultless and like Ananga in music, the birds fly off as if laughing.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The poet lets the birds give the verdict the narration withholds: the suitors' music is praised in words and condemned by nature, so the contest is judged before any human judge speaks."},{"a":"cc:659","b":"cc:11","type":"tension","note":"The patikam announces that the Vidyadhara king's daughter was defeated at the vina by Civakan; here the assembled kings, faultless masters of music, fail before her.","grade":"forced","better":"develops","te":"Against the patikam the failure of the kings, praised as masters, shows that the victory announced there belongs to no ordinary skill, and raises the merchant's son above a crowd of crowned musicians before he appears.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:657","b":"p:43","type":"same_image","note":"At Krishna's flute in the Thiruppugazh dry trees in the forest sprout and birds come down to the ground; at Gandharvadattai's yazh the trees of the grove put out shoots and the birds forget themselves and fall.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same marvel of music that moves trees and birds is a god's sign in the hymn and a contest's evidence in the epic, where 659 adds the reverse, birds fleeing bad music, so the image becomes a measure as well as a wonder.","gn":["the epic's marvel is shown to use a stock image of divine music, so that the girl is raised to the level of Krishna's flute","the hymn's image gains a version in which nature can also refuse, which the hymn's all-conquering flute never shows"]},{"a":"cc:637","b":"si:7:83-89","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Kaanal Vari the man sings that a cruel Death with spear-like eyes lives in the shore grove in a girl's form, and that had he known he would not have come; in the Cintamani onlookers tell the suitors that she is Death and bid them run while they can.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two epics use the woman-as-Death figure at opposite moments: the Silappathikaram's singer learns it too late and says he would not have come, while the Cintamani's crowd says it beforehand to men who stay anyway, so the warning that comes too late in one comes in time and fails in the other."},{"a":"cc:650","b":"si:7:21-28","type":"tension","note":"Kovalan's yazh song praises the Kaveri for not sulking when the Chola unites with the Ganga and calls that a noble woman's karpu; Gandharvadattai's yazh songs show the cuckoo's mate estranged and won back only when the male coaxes her and bows at her feet.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Two yazh songs on lovers' estrangement take opposite sides: the Silappathikaram's makes the woman's not sulking her virtue, while the Cintamani's makes the man's humbling the end of a just sulk, which fits a heroine who yields only to the man who outdoes her."},{"a":"cc:665","b":"cc:454","type":"same_image","note":"In 454 Civakan returns from the cattle raid like the full moon the Tall One freed from the planet's mouth; in 665 the defeated kings dim like the full moon seized by the serpent.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The eclipse image is split between them: the rivals are the moon swallowed and Civakan the moon released, so the poet marks him as the winner before he has touched the vina."},{"a":"cc:670","b":"cc:455","type":"develops","note":"In 455 one who hears of Civakan's recovery of the herd rages like a man whose last day has come; in 670 the king, burning like gold in fire, stands ready to kill him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ruler's anger first stirred by the hero's fame now becomes a plan to murder him, so Civakan's public successes are the very things that endanger him."},{"a":"cc:664","b":"cc:674","type":"tension","note":"Kattiyankaran tells the suitors that only Kama could win the princess; ten verses later the poet calls Civakan, adorning the dancer, அனங்கன் அன்னான், one like Kama.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The usurper's boast that no Kama is present is refuted by the man he wants dead, so his taunt unknowingly names his rival."},{"a":"cc:679","b":"k:1115","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1115 jokes that her waist will snap if she wears anicham with its stems; the spectators of 679 say the dancer avoids flowers lest her small waist break, and lament that sandal and pearls were hung on her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's lover's joke is turned into a crowd's mock indignation at those who dressed the girl, so the hyperbole of the slender waist becomes a charge against the adorner.","gn":["a known literary formula of the waist too slight for flowers, against which the crowd's outburst reads as conventional praise rather than real concern","a public, theatrical setting in which the private lover's exaggeration is shouted by many and aimed at a culprit"]},{"a":"cc:680","b":"k:1081","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1081: the man seeing her first cannot tell whether she is a goddess, a peahen or a woman; the crowd at the dance names her peacock, swan, golden creeper, lotus goddess or daughter of the gods.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet's one man's bewilderment becomes a whole audience's at a staged dance, so the doubt about her nature is produced by art and costume, not by a chance meeting.","gn":["the Kural's frame of first sight, which shows the crowd's guesses as the confusion of love rather than mere compliment","a case where the woman's seeming divinity is made by adornment and performance, which the couplet's private encounter does not consider"]},{"a":"cc:680","b":"n:48","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 48 mocks those who praise sandal paste and garlands without knowing what the body is; the crowd of 680 calls the sandalled, garlanded dancer a goddess or a daughter of the immortals.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic stages exactly the admiration the Naladiyar scorns and gives it to the whole city without rebuke, so the two works disagree on whether adorned beauty is a fit object of wonder.","gn":["a Jain didactic verdict against which the crowd's rapture can be weighed, which the narrative itself withholds here","a full scene of the praise it condemns, showing how adornment, music and dance build the illusion the quatrain attacks"]},{"a":"cc:682","b":"p:142","type":"same_image","note":"The spectators of 682 waste away like a butter doll in the sun of desire; in Thiruppugazh 142 the poet, melting like wax in fire for women, asks whether he must stay bound to that shame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same melting is in the epic the measure of a dancer's art and in the hymn a bondage to be prayed away, so the image shows how one bodily figure serves praise and penitence.","gn":["a devotional reading of the melting as delusion, which the epic, praising the dance, leaves unsaid","a narrative where the melting is collective and caused by a staged performance, not only by a woman's body"]},{"a":"cc:683","b":"k:1100","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1100: once eyes agree with eyes, spoken words are useless; Anangamaalai sends her love to Civakan by a 'letter' of her eyes carried by her smile, in the middle of a public dance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the couplet's wordless eye-language but makes it one-sided and public, a message sent across a crowd, and shows its cost when the king's enmity follows from it.","gn":["the Kural's rule that the look can replace speech, which makes the eye-letter a full declaration rather than a flirtation","a case where the silent message is unreturned and dangerous, a consequence the couplet's mutual glance does not weigh"]},{"a":"cc:689","b":"cc:316","type":"tension","note":"Kattiyankaran sneers at the merchant's son: is he then king of this city? In 316 the deity tells the queen this child will crush the king who stands against him.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The usurper's mocking question unknowingly names the rightful king, so the story makes the villain voice the prophecy he means to deny."},{"a":"cc:693","b":"cc:455","type":"develops","note":"In 455 an unnamed hearer rages at the news that Civakan recovered the herd with no man lost; in 693 Kattiyankaran, hearing the same deed retold by his men, decides Civakan is the way his kingdom will be taken.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The anger left nameless at the cattle raid is given its owner and its reason here: the deed that saved the city's herds is what tells the usurper he has a rival."},{"a":"cc:691","b":"cc:436","type":"develops","note":"436 tells how the hunters destroyed the king's swordsmen; in 691 men of that army, speaking to Kattiyankaran, recall that they broke twice before Civakan won the herd back.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rout told by the narrator as a battle scene returns as the soldiers' own confession to their king, which is why praise of Civakan reaches the usurper as an indictment of his rule."},{"a":"cc:685","b":"k:701","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 701 makes one who knows a mind before it speaks an ornament to the world; Kattiyankaran, once the minister, reads from the dancer's signs that she loves Civakan and turns the knowledge into grudge and seizure.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that reading an unspoken mind is praised in the Kural for a use the couplet assumes, and in a jealous ruler the same skill feeds only his grudge and his violence.","gn":["a name for the faculty the usurper uses, which the epic shows only in action, and a measure of how far he has fallen from the good counsellor's use of it","a case where the sign-reader serves himself rather than the one he reads, which the couplet's praise does not consider"]},{"a":"cc:692","b":"k:226","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 226 says that ending the ruinous hunger of the destitute is where one should store wealth; the usurper's soldiers recall that Civakan fed them in a thousand pots when they were faint, and the telling makes the king fear him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the couplet's store paying out in loyalty: feeding hungry men lays up for Civakan the hearts of the usurper's own army, which is exactly why the giving is felt as a threat.","gn":["the ethical frame in which the feeding is a virtue and not merely a political move","a case where the return on feeding the hungry is political allegiance, and where that return endangers the giver"]},{"a":"cc:702","b":"k:265","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 265 says tapas is striven for because whatever is wanted comes as it is wanted; the city's women say the princess must have done penance (நோற்றனள்) to enjoy Civakan's beauty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The women apply the couplet's promise to a marriage, so the pair shows penance taken in common talk as the merit behind erotic good fortune, the very use the Kural's next verse warns against.","gn":["the doctrinal basis of the women's idiom, which the epic uses only in passing","evidence of how the couplet's motive of getting what one wants was heard in popular speech, where the wanted thing is a beautiful husband"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:695","b":"t:346","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 346 reads Siva's burning of Kama as the yoga that destroys desire's work; the Cintamani says Civakan is as if the burnt Kama had risen again on earth.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Set beside the Saiva reading, the Jain poet's simile shows the burning of Kama handled as a story whose victim returns, so the myth becomes a measure of a hero's beauty instead of a victory over desire.","gn":["the doctrinal weight of the myth the simile borrows, which the simile does not state","evidence that the Kama-burning circulated as a stock image outside Saiva devotion, with its yogic sense dropped"]},{"a":"cc:714","b":"cc:738","type":"develops","note":"Gandharvadattai's vow to bind the unknown youth with a garland is kept when she fastens the gold garland on his chest after the contest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The garland in 738 is the answer to an inward vow, so the public result of the contest was settled in her heart before a string was touched.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:722","b":"cc:539","type":"develops","note":"The astrologer's reading places the princess's destined husband in Rajamapuram; as Civakan takes the lute the poet calls him கணிபுகழ் காளை, the youth the astrologers praised.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epithet at the moment of playing marks the contest as the working out of the horoscope, so the reader hears the song as a prophecy being fulfilled rather than a match in doubt."},{"a":"cc:720","b":"cc:328","type":"same_image","note":"The lute's wood is born of the earth-maiden and reared by the sky as foster mother; Civakan, born in the burning ground, was handed by Kantukkatan to a foster mother.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The instrument Civakan chooses has his own hidden history, a mother who bore it and another who reared it, while the ones he rejects are faulted for their origin, so his judgement of pedigree reflects on the birth no one at the contest knows.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:731","b":"si:7:142-153","type":"same_image","note":"In both a woman takes the lute after the man's songs and begins her own; the Cintamani's நிலத்தோர் and gods wondering (729) echo the Silappathikaram's நிலத்தெய்வம் வியப்பு எய்த, and both close on பாடத் தொடங்கு-.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same turn at the lute, his songs then hers, ends in a garland in the Cintamani and in Kovalan's leaving in the Silappathikaram, which shows that the outcome lies in how the man hears her reply, as yielding or as a hidden intent."},{"a":"cc:714","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's woman says desire's axe breaks the door of nirai bolted with shame; the princess says the youth has opened the door of her hard nirai and entered her heart's guarded chamber.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Cintamani keeps the Kural's door of nirai but changes the breaker from desire to the man and the outcome from lament to a vow of marriage, so the breach of self-containment can be the start of a lawful bond.","gn":["the princess's image is read with the Kural's exact logic of door and bolt, so her 'hard nirai' is a guarded virtue, not a figure of speech","the couplet's breach, spoken by a woman already in love, gains a first-sight case in an unmarried girl, where it leads to the garland instead of grief"]},{"a":"cc:710","b":"k:1134","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's lover says the flood of desire carries off the raft of shame and manliness; the epic's princess has a sea of desire that overruns the fence of shame round her nirai.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Shame is a raft for the Kural's man and a fence round a prison for the epic's woman, so the same flood threatens a man's means of crossing but a woman's enclosure.","gn":["the princess's sea of desire is placed in the Kural's account of what desire sweeps away, naming shame as the first loss","the couplet, spoken by a man, gains the woman's version, where shame guards nirai rather than carrying her"]},{"a":"cc:722","b":"pp:2:1-22","type":"develops","note":"Both describe the lute part by part with the same names, the bowl (பத்தர், பத்தல்), the pegs (ஆணி), the stem (மருப்பு) and the strings (நரம்பு), and both credit its music with power over the hostile and the wild.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Cintamani takes over the Sangam bard's catalogue of the lute but makes it gold, coral and diamond, and makes knowing the instrument's parts and faults the contest itself, so the bard's working tool becomes the test of a prince.","gn":["the epic's list of parts is shown to be an inherited form of praise, which explains why the lute is described before it is played","the bard's poor lute, praised to win a patron's gift, gains a later setting where judging its wood and strings decides a royal marriage"]},{"a":"cc:737","b":"k:265","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says tapas is striven for here because one gets what one wants as one wants it; the bride's falling garland is read as a call to all the world to do tapas.","grade":"sound","better":"develops","te":"The Cintamani takes the Kural's frankly worldly motive for tapas and fixes it on a bridal union, showing how far that motive reaches in a work whose hero will at last renounce such joys.","gn":["the poet's fancy gains a rule that makes it more than a compliment: the joy is the earned fruit of former tapas","the couplet's unnamed 'what is wanted' is given a concrete object, sensual and married, which the next couplet's condemnation of desire leaves out"]},{"a":"cc:742","b":"cc:27","type":"tension","note":"Kattiyankaran tells cowardly kings to leave their crowns and go to the hermits' forest; the patikam says Civakan renounced at the height of his rule.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"In the villain's mouth giving up the crown for the forest is the punishment of a beaten king, while the epic's own ending makes it the free act of a victorious one, so the book stages the courtly contempt for renunciation it will overturn."},{"a":"cc:748","b":"cc:250","type":"same_image","note":"Dharmadattan warned Kattiyankaran that a king is a fire that burns whoever touches him; Padumukan warns the kings that fire burns when touched.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The fire that was said to guard the true king now guards his unknown son against the same usurper's tools, so the proverb points at Civakan's royal birth before anyone in the hall knows it."},{"a":"cc:753","b":"cc:539","type":"develops","note":"The astrologer read that the princess's destined husband lives in Rajamapuram; Padumukan declares her the chaste bride of Civakan whom no one can take.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the horoscope fixed as her allotted portion becomes, once she has chosen, her own karpu, so the fated union is defended in the hall as a matter of the woman's chastity rather than of the stars."},{"a":"cc:755","b":"cc:744","type":"tension","note":"Kattiyankaran promises the kings fortune if they kill Civakan; Padumukan says his plan is to let them destroy one another and rule alone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Padumukan's reading turns the promise of sovereignty into its opposite, showing the reader that the usurper's incitement of the suitors repeats the method by which he took Saccandan's throne."},{"a":"cc:755","b":"k:633","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural counts dividing others among a minister's skills; Padumukan exposes the minister-turned-king's plan to divide the kings so they perish fighting each other.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The craft of division that the Kural lists without moral comment as a minister's skill is shown here in the hands of a minister who betrayed his king, and the epic names its result as a world made cruel.","gn":["Padumukan's charge gains a recognised name: Kattiyankaran is practising the minister's art of division, not mere spite","the couplet's neutral skill gains a case where it is turned against the realm, which the couplet does not consider"]},{"a":"cc:746","b":"pn:336","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Purananuru poem a king's demand for a girl her father will not give brings war on the town, and the mother who reared her beauty is blamed; here kings rise to seize a bride already won in an open contest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic removes the father's refusal that drives the Sangam bride-war, since the bride's father set a fair contest and she has chosen, so the suitors' war stands as bare lust and the blame moves from the girl's rearing to the kings and their instigator.","gn":["the scene is placed in the old category of the bride-war (மகட்பால்), which shows what it changes","the poem's blame of the mother is shown to be one choice of cause, which a later poet assigns instead to the suitors' desire and to a schemer"]},{"a":"cc:749","b":"k:726","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks what a sword is to those without courage and what learning is to those who fear the assembly; Padumukan mocks kings who lost at the art and turn to the sword.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural tests the sword and learning separately, each by its own courage, but Padumukan adds that one who has failed the test of art cannot seek the same prize by the sword, so the second arena only confirms the first defeat.","gn":["the sarcasm வாழ்க நுங் கலை gains a standard: the kings fail both of the Kural's tests at once","the couplet's paired measures gain a rule of contest about moving from one to the other, which the couplet does not state"]},{"a":"cc:757","b":"pn:369","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 369 sings the Chera's battle as a season of tillage, arrows for rain, heads for crop, ghouls threshing; 757 uses the same sowing-and-harvest field, with ghosts fed on flesh and blood, as a warning to rivals before the fight.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The bard's after-battle praise image, sung to earn a gift, becomes in the epic a combatant's threat spoken before a blow is struck, so the convention moves from rewarding a victory to predicting one.","gn":["the full Sangam convention of the battlefield as a farm, which the verse compresses into four lines without naming it","a use of the convention outside the bard's request for gifts, as an argument about who deserves a bride"]},{"a":"cc:757","b":"cc:820","type":"tension","note":"757 calls the slaughter a harvest that wins the victory; in 820 the victor makes a golden image of the Jina, holds a festival and gives gifts to put away the sin (பாவம்) of the battlefield.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The same killing is first boasted as a crop and then expiated as a sin, so the Jain epic lets the heroic idiom stand in the speeches and corrects it only after the battle is won."},{"a":"cc:759","b":"cc:798","type":"develops","note":"In 759 Civakan tells the princess the kings are already food (இரை) for his arrows; in 798 he greets the kings as guests and feeds (ஊட்டினான்) each of them one or two arrows.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The consolation's figure of kings as food is carried out as a mock feast in which he is host and the arrows are the meal, so his reassurance to her is kept literally."},{"a":"cc:770","b":"k:463","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 463 says the wise do not undertake a deed that loses capital for the sake of gain; the kings tell Civakan, as a merchant's son, that a true trader would save his capital and flee, leaving the girl.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's prudence is turned into a class insult, and the battle that follows shows that the rule cannot say what counts as capital: for the kings it is life and goods, for Civakan it is his name and the bride.","gn":["the general maxim of not risking the principal, which the kings bend into an argument for cowardice","a case where the maxim is quoted by the side that loses, showing that it holds only once one has judged what the capital is"]},{"a":"cc:772","b":"cc:816","type":"tension","note":"In 772 the kings foretell that Civakan will lie pierced by arrows, his beauty and youth wasted; in 816 those same kings vanish like lightning in rain and the Vidyadharas praise his victory.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The kings' prophecy of his death is answered by their own flight, so the scorn they pour on his youth becomes the measure of their defeat."},{"a":"cc:773","b":"pn:71","type":"qualifies","note":"In Purananuru 71 the Pandya swears that if he does not see the backs (புறம்) of the kings who have joined against him, may he be parted from his wife; Civakan swears that unless he sees the kings' backs (புறக்கொடை) and embraces the lady, may his name perish.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vow keeps the Sangam form, allied kings and the sight of their backs, but moves the woman from the forfeit to the prize: the Pandya risks his wife, Civakan stakes his name to win a bride.","gn":["its genre: the வஞ்சினம் of a king facing allied enemies, with the shape of a named forfeit","a variant in which the beloved is what the vow wins rather than what it puts at stake, showing the form can serve courtship as well as kingship"]},{"a":"cc:773","b":"cc:817","type":"develops","note":"Civakan vows to see the kings' backs and then embrace the lady; in 817, having seen the field where the enemies fell, he enters his house with her, surrounded by his friends.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vow's two conditions are kept in its own order, the routed field first and the bride brought home after, so the marriage is presented as the vow's discharge."},{"a":"cc:791","b":"cc:640","type":"tension","note":"In 640 some suitors vowed to plunge into battle only if a man other than the vina's victor took her; in 791 the kings who desired her fight after Civakan has won at the vina.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The kings' war breaks the very terms they set at the contest, so their fight is shown as desire that will not accept its own rule, not as a defence of the bride."},{"a":"cc:798","b":"cc:708","type":"develops","note":"708 records a king who came and fell in the sea of the vina; 798 has Civakan remind the kings that she beat them at the vina before he serves them arrows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Civakan's taunt makes the musical defeat the first round of the battle, so the kings now lose twice to the same bride, once to her skill and once to his."},{"a":"cc:803","b":"cc:28","type":"tension","note":"The patikam's 28 says that his brother, companions and wives renounced and the master of the vina went to the unequalled state; here the same hero laughs with delight at a ghoul dressing in the slain.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Jain epic lets its hero relish slaughter fully before it turns him to renunciation, so the release it promises in 28 is a turning from a violence he once enjoyed, not from one he never knew."},{"a":"cc:783","b":"k:773","type":"develops","note":"Kural 773 calls help to a foe in trouble the cutting edge of valour; 783 has the companions cripple a chariot and then bid its charioteer yoke fresh horses.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic turns the couplet's ஊராண்மை into a battlefield move: the generosity is not mercy that ends the fight but an invitation to go on fighting on equal terms.","gn":["a concept that makes the companions' strange order a recognised height of valour rather than a jest","a concrete act that shows what the help to a stricken foe looks like in a chariot fight"]},{"a":"cc:782","b":"k:776","type":"develops","note":"Kural 776 counts as wasted the days without a noble wound; 782 has the companions offer their chests to the tusk so the scar may lie beautiful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet keeps a sober ledger of wounded days, the epic turns the wound into ornament, so the same ethic moves from a reckoning of duty to an aesthetic of the body.","gn":["the reason a scarred chest should count as beauty: a wound is the record of a day not wasted","the place of the noble wound on the body, the chest that faces the foe, which the couplet does not name"]},{"a":"cc:799","b":"k:202","type":"develops","note":"Kural 202 says evil is to be feared more than fire because evil yields evil; in 799 burning arrows kill the mean-minded kings and warn them off evil even in their next body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic makes the fire itself the preacher: the burning arrow that kills is only the lesser fire, and its warning points to the evil that will follow the dead into the next birth.","gn":["the ground of the arrows' warning, that evil breeds evil and so outlasts the body the fire destroys","a picture of evil outlasting death, which gives the couplet's comparison with fire a span beyond one life"]},{"a":"cc:799","b":"t:2133","type":"develops","note":"Tirumanthiram 2133 has the subtle body, its old deeds following, take up a new body; 799's arrows warn the slain against evil in the body they will take next.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the epic's warning to the dying presupposes the passage Tirumular describes, and Tirumular's mechanism is given a moral use: deeds follow, so the dead are urged to change their conduct across the change of body.","gn":["the mechanism, deeds carried by the subtle body into a new birth, that makes 'the body you will take' literal","an ethical address to the dying that the doctrinal account of transmigration leaves out"]},{"a":"cc:797","b":"n:3","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 3 has kings who rode under the umbrella fall when their deeds give out; 797 shows the kings' white umbrellas overturned by Civakan's arrows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain names only spent deeds as the cause of a king's fall, while the epic shows the immediate cause, an archer and the kings' own desire, and in 799 sorts the fallen by their minds, so karma and the battle are both given as cause.","gn":["a frame in which the overturned umbrellas are a lesson in impermanence and not only a victory","a scene that names the human cause beside the karmic one, which the quatrain's 'other deeds' leaves blank"]},{"a":"cc:804","b":"pn:369","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 369 sings the battlefield as a harvest where the ghoul-woman holds the corpses and jackals thresh, and the bard asks the king for a gift; in 804 ghouls already fed sing over what was given them.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic hands the bard's role to the ghouls: in the Puram poem the singer asks for a share of the field, while here the scavengers have had their share and their song is thanks, so the field is a patron's gift completed.","gn":["the kalavazhi frame that explains why ghouls 'sing what was given', as bards sing a patron's bounty","the reversal that shows how close the Puram bard stands to the scavengers who share the same harvest of corpses"]},{"a":"cc:803","b":"pp:6:24-42","type":"develops","note":"Maduraikkanchi 24-42 sets fat-mouthed demon women dancing the tunangai around a battle-sacrifice whose cook serves flesh-rice to the soldiers; in 803 a pregnant ghoul dresses herself in the field's remains while Civakan watches and laughs.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Sangam poem makes the ghouls' feast part of a rite the king performs, while the epic removes the rite and keeps the dance as a spectacle for the hero's delight, so the motif survives as ornament of heroism.","gn":["the ritual setting from which the battlefield ghoul descends, which makes Civakan's laughter a presiding king's pleasure","a later afterlife of its demon women, cut loose from sacrifice and turned into a toilette parody"]},{"a":"cc:813","b":"cc:554","type":"develops","note":"The Vidyadhara king said he would not give his daughter even if kings with sea-like armies fell at his feet and begged; here the kings who fought for her lose their sea-like army, which breaks like a salt hill in flood.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The father's refusal was spoken before any war, and the battle turns his figure of speech into fact: the same sea of soldiers he would not bow to is now the thing that dissolves.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:823","b":"cc:481","type":"develops","note":"Nandagon promised Civakan to fill the underworld with ghee of red cows, sweet milk and thick curd; here the keeper of the herds brings four hundred thousand pots of curd, milk and ghee to Civakan's wedding.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The herd chief's boast at his own daughter's giving is paid at Civakan's wedding instead, so the bond made by the recovered cattle carries from one marriage of the hero's circle to the next."},{"a":"cc:820","b":"cc:375","type":"qualifies","note":"Civakan's teacher says those great in knowledge reach the path where hard deeds wear away; after the battle Civakan removes the battlefield's sin by an image of the Jina, a festival and gifts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the householder prince a route to clearing sin by rite and giving, beside the teacher's route of knowledge that wears deeds away, so the work holds two Jain answers to bad deeds at once and assigns them by station."},{"a":"cc:815","b":"k:546","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says it is not the spear but the ruler's unbent sceptre that gives victory; the epic says victory goes to the virtuous mind, and a betel leaf thrown by such a man becomes a spear.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet ties victory to the justice of a king's rule, while the epic moves it into each fighter's heart and applies it to a war over a woman, where no sceptre is in question.","gn":["the epic's moral of battle gains a political form, the rule that a just state wins, which its private quarrel over a bride lacks","the couplet gains a test case where the side with fewer arms wins and the cause is put in character rather than in government"]},{"a":"cc:814","b":"k:500","type":"tension","note":"The Kural says a jackal can kill a war elephant when its feet sink in mud, so ground decides; the epic says a single lion kills a herd of elephants, so the nature of the fighter decides.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"Both answer how the few beat the many, but the couplet credits the place of battle and the epic the strength of the one who fights, two incompatible accounts of the same reversal if each is taken as the cause.","gn":["the epic's boast gains a rival explanation it does not consider, that its small band might have won by terrain or timing rather than by nature","the couplet gains a counter-image in which the elephant is the victim of a stronger beast, not of bad ground, which shows its claim is a choice of cause"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:818","b":"pp:7:167-188","type":"develops","note":"In the Nedunalvadai the king walks the camp at midnight to look at the sword wounds of the warriors; in the epic the wounded are laid in ghee, have spearheads cut out, and are given purses and ornaments.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Sangam poem shows care for the wounded as the king's sleepless looking, and the epic turns that care into a practice of treatment and payment, so the reader sees the duty to the wounded grow from presence into provision.","gn":["the epic's list of treatments gains a source in the old ideal of the leader who goes himself to the wounded, which it leaves to friends and women","the Sangam king's visit gains a picture of what was done for the wounded after he looked, which the poem does not tell"]},{"a":"cc:821","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says the two kinds of dark deed do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's true praise; the epic says the Tirthan's feet remove all the sins done, and has Civakan worship there after killing in battle.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's claim covers both good and bad deeds and asks only praise, while the epic narrows it to sins and stages it as a rite after a specific act of killing, showing how a general promise was put to work as expiation.","gn":["the epic's rite gains a measure of how much less it claims than the couplet, which frees from good deeds too","the couplet's abstract Lord gains a named object, the Jina's feet, and a concrete occasion, the sin of war"]},{"a":"cc:821","b":"t:509","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says the holy waters are within the heart and that those who wander the land without bathing in them do not destroy their deeds; Civakan takes the water of a jewelled conch at the Tirthan's feet as part of putting away his sin.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read as part of the sin-clearing rite, the conch water is the outward tirtham Tirumular says cannot destroy deeds, so the pair sets an epic's confidence in rite against a Saiva yogi's insistence on the inward bath.","gn":["the epic's ceremony gains a sharp critic, who would ask whether the water touched the heart","Tirumular's verse gains a case from another faith of the outward practice he dismisses, done sincerely by a hero after war"]},{"a":"cc:844","b":"cc:640","type":"develops","note":"In 640 rival kings vow to plunge into war if another wins the vina contest; 844 reports that a wicked one, burning, said she belonged to the strongest spear and made war.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The suitors' threat comes true not as their own act but through a single instigator who turns their wounded pride into a war, so the contest's law is broken by one man's design."},{"a":"cc:848","b":"cc:554","type":"develops","note":"In 554 the king refused all suitors until the time fate set for his daughter; in 848 he tells the son-in-law that what is to come will come unsought.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king who once used fate to wait for the destined husband now uses it to promise that husband his kingdom, so his trust in destiny moves from patience to assurance."},{"a":"cc:848","b":"k:376","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 376 says what is not one's own will not stay however guarded and what is one's own will not leave though thrown away; 848 says what is to come comes and what is to go goes, as the sea and river receive unasked.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural states the rule of fate about possessions, and the letter applies it to a dispossessed heir in the same breath as an offer of arms, showing fate being cited to back an effort rather than to forbid one.","gn":["the letter's proverbial lines gain the weight of a known maxim on fate and property","the couplet gains a case where 'what is yours will come' is a political promise to a man whose throne is held by another"]},{"a":"cc:848","b":"n:103","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 103 says each one's enjoyments are measured by their own course, as no one rounded the vilam fruit; 848 says the sea need not fetch water nor the river beg rain.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both argue from unmade nature, but the quatrain's fruit shows a lot fixed in advance while the letter's sea and river show a lot that flows in, so the same proof from nature can counsel content or confidence.","gn":["the letter's images are placed in the didactic argument that nature's givens prove fate","the quatrain's measured lot gains a picture of fate as abundance flowing toward the fortunate"]},{"a":"cc:829","b":"k:265","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 265 says tavam is striven for because what is wanted comes as wanted; in 829 the crowd sees the hero's wedding wealth as the fruit of his tavam and wishes it would come to them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The onlookers accept the couplet's premise that tavam yields what one wants, yet only wish for its fruit instead of doing it, which is the very shortcut the couplet rules out.","gn":["the crowd's wish is shown to rest on the doctrine that good fortune is the wages of penance","the couplet gains its counter-case: people who believe its promise and still do not strive"]},{"a":"cc:836","b":"k:1103","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1103 has the lover ask whether the lotus-eyed god's world is sweeter than sleep in the beloved's arms; 836 says even Indra, lord of the gods, could not have had this wedding.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both set a human union above a god's world, but the Kural's lover measures it by private sweetness while the epic narrator measures it by public splendour, so the same boast ranks feeling in one and rank and wealth in the other.","gn":["the narrator's hyperbole joins a known topos of love outranking heaven","the couplet's private boast gains a version voiced in public about a wedding rather than an embrace"]},{"a":"cc:828","b":"k:216","type":"develops","note":"Kural 216 likens wealth in a man of goodwill to a fruit tree ripening in the town's midst; 828 likens the crowd at the hero's house to birds on a tree heavy with fruit.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet's tree is a figure for wealth that others may share, and the epic shows who actually comes to such a tree: a crowd that gives as well as takes, as 829 and 831 go on to say.","gn":["the simile gains the couplet's moral sense that the house's wealth is held for others","the couplet's fruit tree gains a scene showing the throng that gathers and the exchange of gifts both ways"]},{"a":"cc:874","b":"cc:12","type":"develops","note":"The patikam tells that at the festival bath two young women quarrelled over their powders and swore not to bathe if they lost; here the two, Suramanjari and Gunamalai, sit together in the grove free of grief.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam's quarrel is set against this calm first sight of the rivals side by side, so the story's discord grows out of an easy companionship and the festival's own luxury."},{"a":"cc:875","b":"cc:13","type":"develops","note":"The powder is first held out here; the patikam says Suramanjari's powder was beaten and she withdrew vowing to look on no man.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A trifle of pleasure called the powder that bears desire becomes the cause of a vow against all men, so the section's marriage turns on a toiletry rather than a battle."},{"a":"cc:858","b":"pr:12:9-35","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 12 has Madurai dress, perfume itself and mount she-elephants, horses and chariots in a rush to meet the Vaiyai flood; the Cintamani's city sets out with litters, elephants and carts for its spring water-play by the river.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the Sangam river festival's order of dressing, mounting and rushing to the bank, but its festival honours no river or god, and is likened instead to the Jina's assembly, so the old civic rite is re-made as pure pleasure under a renouncer's image.","gn":["a precedent showing that its procession is a set piece of the Tamil river festival rather than an invention","evidence that its catalogue of the crowd's preparations survived as a convention into the later Jain epic, cut loose from the river's worship"]},{"a":"cc:856","b":"t:2162","type":"tension","note":"The Cintamani calls the Jina the lord of faultless kevala; Tirumular calls kevala the soul's sleep of utter stupor under anava, from which karma drives it out.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same word for the soul's state 'alone' is the summit of release for the Jain poet and the lowest darkness of the bound soul for the Saiva, so the two traditions disagree on whether the soul left to itself is free or blind.","gn":["a sense of how pointed its epithet is: kevala names perfection only in a doctrine where the soul alone, freed of karma, is omniscient","a rival use of its technical term, showing that its claim that the solitary soul is in darkness answers a school that called that solitude release"]},{"a":"cc:878","b":"cc:12","type":"develops","note":"The patikam says the two women vowed not to bathe in the festival water if they lost; here the wager is spelt out: the loser forfeits the water-play and pays crores of gold.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam's single vow turns out to be one half of a two-part stake, honour and gold, and only the honour half is followed through the story, which shows which loss the poem thinks matters."},{"a":"cc:879","b":"k:931","type":"qualifies","note":"The women treat the powder contest as a wager (வெல்வது சூது); the Kural says not to want gambling even when one can win, since the winnings are the hook the fish swallows.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic shows a wager whose real stake is not wealth but pride, and whose cost falls in a vow of seclusion on the loser, so the Kural's warning about winnings is widened to what a wager does to the one who loses face.","gn":["the women's game is shown to belong to a kind of act the ethical tradition already condemns, which the narrator's mild கண்ணற்றார் only hints at","the couplet, which counts gambling's harm in money, gets a case where the harm is a loss of honour that reshapes a life"]},{"a":"cc:883","b":"cc:2206","type":"develops","note":"A simile for the maids' eyes mentions Civakan's resolve to cut Kattiyangaran down; much later a voice from the sky proclaims that Civakan will swallow Kattiyangaran's life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The revenge is never dropped even in a scene of scents and maids: the poet plants it as an aside here, so the heavenly proclamation reads as the public fulfilment of a purpose already settled in private."},{"a":"cc:885","b":"k:423","type":"develops","note":"The maid cites the praise of the whole city, the merchant included, against Civakan's verdict; the Kural says understanding is seeing the true substance whoever's mouth one hears a thing from.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the couplet's rule tested where it is hardest, against unanimous acclaim, and shows that the epic does not let the lone judge rest on his authority either but makes him prove the truth by a test both sides accept.","gn":["Civakan's refusal to follow the city is given a principle, so it reads as discernment rather than vanity","the couplet's abstract rule gains a case where the many and the respected are wrong and the truth needs a witness, not a second opinion"]},{"a":"cc:888","b":"k:504","type":"qualifies","note":"The maid argues that moisture makes the bad good and drying makes the good bad; the Kural bids one examine qualities and faults, see which prevail, and take that.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The maid's argument exposes what the couplet assumes, that qualities hold still to be weighed; if condition changes them, weighing by inspection cannot settle a dispute, which is why the epic turns to the bees.","gn":["the maid's retort is seen to strike at the method of judgement itself, not just at Civakan's verdict","the couplet's method gets a limit: it needs qualities that are stable or a test that does not depend on the examiner's eye"]},{"a":"cc:894","b":"k:505","type":"qualifies","note":"Civakan proves the powder by what the bees do with it; the Kural says one's own deeds are the touchstone of greatness and smallness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both put the proof in what a thing does rather than in what is said of it, but the epic adds that a touchstone convinces only when both parties accepted it beforehand, as the maid did in 890 and 891.","gn":["the bee test is shown to rest on a recognised rule of judging by effects, not on a trick","the couplet's touchstone, stated of persons, gains a scene showing it must be agreed in advance to settle a quarrel"]},{"a":"cc:894","b":"cc:2077","type":"develops","note":"Here the bees prove Suramanjari's powder the lesser; in her own ilampakam a woman says the guarded chastity Suramanjari kept is because the lord dimmed the powder he tested by producing a witness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The later verse reads the bee test as the cause of Suramanjari's guarded maidenhood, so a judgement of scents becomes in the story's memory the origin of a chaste seclusion."},{"a":"cc:899","b":"cc:2088","type":"develops","note":"Suramanjari vows to wait until Civakan comes defeated and worships her anklet-feet; in her ilampakam Civakan sets his head on the anklet of her feet, says the great forgive the wrongs of the small, and ends her sulk.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vow is kept to the letter, his head on her anklet, yet the hero turns it by calling his own slight the wrong of a lesser one forgiven by the greater, so her condition is met and her pride still yielded."},{"a":"cc:899","b":"cc:13","type":"develops","note":"The patikam says Suramanjari, beaten, resolved to look on no male form and withdrew into the guarded mansion; here she states the vow's condition, his coming defeated to her feet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The patikam's 'no male form' is in the verse itself a vow with one exception named in advance, the man who shamed her, so the summary hides that her withdrawal is aimed at Civakan."},{"a":"cc:899","b":"k:969","type":"qualifies","note":"Shamed by the verdict, Suramanjari withdraws under a vow; the Kural says those like the yak that will not live once its hair is lost give up life when honour is lost.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic offers a third path to the couplet's choice between life and honour: a withdrawal from the world that keeps the wound open and names the act that will restore her honour.","gn":["Suramanjari's seclusion is seen as a measure of honour like the yak's, not mere sulking","the couplet's stark alternative of death gains a case where honour lost is answered by a vow with terms, which the couplet does not imagine"]},{"a":"cc:908","b":"cc:13","type":"qualifies","note":"The patikam sums up Suramanjari as resolving to look on no male form; 908 has her vow already reserve one exception, Civakan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The vow the outline reports as a refusal of all men is in the story a vow with Civakan written into it, so her seclusion is a waiting as much as a withdrawal."},{"a":"cc:908","b":"cc:2005","type":"develops","note":"908's condition, joining Civakan's chest or no one breaking her nirai, is met when Suramanjari's heart rises to sink into his chest in her own ilampakam.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The exception she sets in anger becomes the desire that ends the vow, so the epic keeps her nirai intact by letting the one permitted man be the one she longs for."},{"a":"cc:907","b":"cc:2003","type":"tension","note":"The father has every carved figure made female so no male form is seen; later Civakan is told to hide by the golden image of Kama in the god of love's temple.","grade":"forced","better":"develops","te":"A ban thorough enough to change the sex of statues still leaves the love god's own image standing, and that is the gap through which the excluded man is brought in."},{"a":"cc:913","b":"k:367","type":"develops","note":"Kural 367 says that if craving is cut off thoroughly, unfailing deeds (தவாவினை) come as one wishes; 913 pictures giving to suppliants as damming the sea called அவா, with தவாவினை at its shore.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic takes the Kural's pair of craving and unfailing deed and makes festival giving the practical means of checking craving, where the couplet names only the cutting itself.","gn":["the doctrinal source of its compressed image: the words அவா and தவாவினை are the couplet's own pairing, which explains why deed and craving share one metaphor","a concrete social act, gold poured out to suppliants on a festival day, as the way craving is held back short of renunciation"]},{"a":"cc:909","b":"n:60","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 60 says the higher ones look to the pain within each pleasure and do not cling; in 909 Gunamalai, after the game that cost her a friend, sees that play meant for joy became the cause of sorrow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's discipline of seeing pain inside pleasure appears in the epic first as a girl's tears over a garden game, an early lesson in a story that ends in renunciation.","gn":["the ascetic frame in which Gunamalai's private grief reads as the first form of the insight that leads to thuravu","a lived case where the pain in pleasure is not bodily or moral but a broken friendship, arising from a contest of beauty"]},{"a":"cc:947","b":"cc:14","type":"develops","note":"The patikam summarizes this episode as the hero giving nectar (அமிர்தம்) to a dog that came to harm at the bathing festival; 947 shows that the nectar is the five padas, swallowed in the heart (பதவமிர்தம் நெஞ்சின் அயின்று).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The patikam's 'nectar' turns out to be a teaching rather than a drink, so the summary's word is the poem's own metaphor for the mantra and not a miracle of healing."},{"a":"cc:948","b":"cc:15","type":"develops","note":"The dog appears as a god in 948; the patikam's next verse tells how a god came down the sky like lightning and carried Civakan out of the king's prison.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the hero's kindness at the festival is the cause of his rescue later: the creature he saved by a mantra returns as the god who saves him, so the plot itself runs on karma's return."},{"a":"cc:934","b":"k:30","type":"tension","note":"Kural 30 says those called அந்தணர் are so because they bear cool kindness to every living being; in 934-936 the men skilled in the four Vedas, with minds of fire, beat a dog to death over a heap of rice.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The epic stages the Kural's definition as a test the Brahmins fail, and gives the cool kindness the Kural requires to the hero instead, so the name and the conduct come apart exactly as the couplet's reasoning allows.","gn":["a standard, from outside the Jain frame, by which the Brahmins' violence is condemned without the epic having to state it","a scene showing what follows when the title is kept and the conduct lost: the couplet's 'because' becomes a charge"]},{"a":"cc:945","b":"t:533","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 533 says those who grieve the ascetics who teach the mantra are born as dogs; in 945 a dog dying with no merit hears a mantra and is born a god.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both texts tie the mantra to rebirth as a dog, but run the scale in opposite directions: Tirumular uses the dog's birth as the punishment for wronging the mantra's teachers, the Jain epic uses the mantra to lift a dog out of that birth even without merit.","gn":["the sharpness of its choice of a dog, which in the Saiva text is the very emblem of a fallen birth","a view in which the mantra's power reaches the lowest birth directly, not only as a sanction protecting its teachers"]},{"a":"cc:946","b":"t:905","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 905 promises that chanting சிவாயநம ends birth, by grace; in 946 the Lord's five padas, taken at death, bring the dog a good state (நற்கதி), a better birth.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both are five-part formulas given at the decisive moment, but the Saiva five letters promise an end to birth through grace, while the Jain five padas here promise a better birth by the dog's own letting go of attachment and fear.","gn":["the measure of how modest its promise is: a good birth, not release, and won by the hearer's renunciation rather than by a god's grace","a rival five-fold formula against which its claim that the five letters end birth stands out as a stronger claim"]},{"a":"cc:944","b":"n:322","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 322 says those not disposed to virtue cannot take it in, as a tanner's dog that chews leather cannot take the taste of milk rice; in 944 a dog pricks its ears, weeps and takes in the teaching.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain uses the dog as the type of the creature who cannot receive dharma; the epic answers with a dog that hears and consents, so receptivity is made a matter of the moment of hearing, not of the hearer's kind.","gn":["the force of the inversion: the dog is the proverbial non-hearer, which makes its listening the point","a limit on its image: the dog's inability is a habit, which the epic shows can be broken at death"]},{"a":"cc:951","b":"cc:3036","type":"develops","note":"In 951 the dog, given the five padas at its ear as it died, is reborn a god; in 3036, in the last ilampakam, those lying at the point of death to whom the ascetic's words are set at the ear appear at once as unaging gods.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The single rescue of a dog becomes, at the end of the epic, a general working of the teacher's word, so the early episode is shown to be the model case of a rule the work states only at its close."},{"a":"cc:951","b":"t:2709","type":"qualifies","note":"Both make a recited formula the agent of a change of state: Tirumular's சிவாயநம is an alchemist's pill that turns the body's cage to gold; the Cintamani's five padas are water that washes off black karma and bring a new god's body after death.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Set side by side, the two mantras do different work: the Saiva syllables transmute the body the devotee already has through the Lord's act, while the Jain padas clear karma so that a better body follows in the next birth, with no Lord who acts.","gn":["a Saiva counterpart in which the mantra changes this body rather than the next, which shows that 951's change comes only with death and a new birth","a case in which the mantra works on one who cannot melt or contemplate, a dying animal, so that devotion of the mind is not what gives the formula its force"]},{"a":"cc:952","b":"cc:960","type":"same_image","note":"952 has fire burn away iron's nature and turn it to gold, with Civakan as the fire; 960 repeats the forge, adds mercury piercing the iron, and uses it to answer the doubt that a dog could become a god.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The image moves from praising the friend as the cause of the change to defending the change itself as lawful, so the narrator relies on one figure first for gratitude and then for doctrine."},{"a":"cc:959","b":"cc:15","type":"develops","note":"959: the god promises to stay like Civakan's shadow and come if he thinks of him in trouble; patikam verse 15 tells how, when the king throws Civakan into prison, a god comes down the sky like lightning and carries him away.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam's rescue is the promise of 959 kept, so the god's later appearance is not a chance intervention but the return of a debt owed for the mantra."},{"a":"cc:960","b":"t:2051","type":"qualifies","note":"Both use iron turned to gold for a change of being: Tirumular says gold once made never turns back to iron, so one who has the guru's grace is not born again; the Cintamani's iron-to-gold is a dog reborn as a god.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same alchemy marks a final release in Tirumular and only a rise within the round of births in the Cintamani, so the image's 'no return' belongs to the Saiva claim, not to the figure itself.","gn":["a reading of its own image as a step and not an end, since the god of 951 is still embodied","an instance where the gold is a higher birth, which shows that the couplet's finality is a doctrinal addition to the metallurgy"]},{"a":"cc:960","b":"t:533","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular says those who grieve the ascetics who taught the mantra are born as dogs (சுணங்கன்); the Cintamani's dog (சுணங்கன்) is raised to a god's birth by the mantra heard from Civakan.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together, the teacher's mantra decides birth in both directions: offending its teachers sinks a man to a dog, and receiving it lifts a dog to a god, so the same word names the bottom of the scale in both books.","gn":["the Saiva warning that the dog's birth is itself the fruit of offending the bearers of the mantra, which deepens the narrator's 'no good in it'","a story in which the same creature climbs back up the scale by the same means that, in the couplet, cast men down"]},{"a":"cc:961","b":"k:9","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 9 says a head that does not bow at the feet of the one of eight qualities (எண்குணத்தான்) is useless; 961 vows to bow the head to no one but the one who removes the eight karmas (எண்வினை) and gives bliss, and sets aside the gods of desire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet's positive command into an exclusion and fills its unexplained 'eight' with a count of karmas destroyed, so the Kural's open epithet is given a Jain content it does not itself state.","gn":["a canonical model, the bowing head of the Kural's first chapter, for its refusal","one reading of its undefined eight, together with the inference that the bow must be withheld from other gods"]},{"a":"cc:962","b":"k:24","type":"develops","note":"Kural 24: one who guards the five senses with the goad of firmness is a seed for the finest ground; 962 builds the whole farm, with zeal as the plough, the body as the field, vows as seed, conduct as water and the five senses as the fence.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet's one image of the guarded self as seed is spelled out as a season of tillage, and its unnamed 'finest ground' gets two harvests, heaven first and release after.","gn":["a classical root for its allegory, in which the guarding of the five senses already leads to the best place","an answer to what வரன் yields, given as a sequence of heaven and release, and a place for vows and conduct beside the guarding of the senses"]},{"a":"cc:962","b":"t:1875","type":"tension","note":"In 962 the practiser yokes the plough, tills his own body and guards it with the senses' fence until release ripens; in Tirumular 1875 the first Lord has taken the poet's soul as his field to plough, and praise and blame fall away.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two pictures of the tilled self disagree on who holds the plough: in the Jain allegory release is the crop of one's own labour, in Tirumular the Lord does the tilling, so the shared farm figure carries the dispute between release by effort and release by grace.","gn":["a Saiva counter-picture that brings out how wholly 962 leaves the work to the practiser, with no god in the field","the Jain alternative it implicitly answers, which makes its passive 'he has taken my soul to plough' a claim about grace and not only an image"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:966","b":"pr:6:26-43","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 6 sets the river play out as a battle array, with bathers drilling rivalry 'as in war' and carrying syringes (சிவிறியர்); in 966 the bathers spray with syringes (சிவிறி) and the play goes through a campaign's whole course, the enemy's back seen, the offender fined with garlands, tribute taken.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Sangam festival's figure of water-play as battle is carried by the epic from the array to the victory and its exactions, and set just before a real terror breaks out (973), so the mock war of desire gives way to a true danger.","gn":["the Sangam convention of the festival as a war of pleasure, which explains why the verse reaches for rout and tribute","a later use of the convention that completes the war figure with conquest and penalty, which the Paripadal's arrays only begin"]},{"a":"cc:973","b":"cc:14","type":"develops","note":"Patikam verse 14 tells that on the same day the hero gave nectar to the dying dog at the bathing festival, he overcame the victorious elephant that had terrified Gunamalai; 973 is the elephant breaking loose as the festival crowd returns.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam binds the mercy to the dog and the mastery of the elephant as one day's deeds, so the chapter's turn from the god's farewell through the water-play to the rampaging tusker is a single day planned as a pair of the hero's virtues."},{"a":"cc:985","b":"cc:14","type":"develops","note":"The patikam gives the episode one line, the victorious elephant that terrified Gunamalai, beaten on the same day Civakan gave nectar to the dying dog; 974-985 tell it as a scene in which the bearers flee, the companion offers to die, and Civakan chooses death over life before he knows who the girl is.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam's pairing of the dog and the elephant on one day is borne out by the scene's terms: both deeds are mercy to a life in danger, not courtship, and the love follows only afterwards.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:978","b":"k:969","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 969 says the honourable give up life when honour is at stake, like the yak that will not live without its hair; 978 has Civakan reckon death better than life when a stranger woman's life is in danger.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic moves the Kural's honour-death from guarding one's own standing to rescuing another, so that honour is measured by whose danger a man will not stand by and watch.","gn":["a rule that names the choice Civakan makes in a single line as a settled standard of honour, not a hero's impulse","a case where the life given up for honour is risked to save someone else, which the couplet's self-regarding image of the yak leaves out"]},{"a":"cc:976","b":"a:18","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 18 the friend tells the lover that if her friend suffers harm for a single day she will not live after; in 976 Gunamalai's companion, facing the elephant, resolves that it should kill her together with her mistress.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The akam friend's stock claim that her life hangs on the heroine's is put to the test in the epic by a real beast, and the companion keeps it, so the convention is shown as a loyalty that can be acted on.","gn":["the akam convention behind the companion's resolve, which makes her offer a known role rather than a passing panic","an enacted instance of the friend's pledge, which the poem only states as a warning to the lover"]},{"a":"cc:984","b":"kl:103","type":"develops","note":"In Kalittokai 103 a herdsman wrenching a charging bull by the ear is likened to Mayon tearing the horse's jaw, and the herd girls will embrace only one who falls between the killer bull's horns; in 984 Civakan facing the elephant is likened to the gem-coloured lord facing the elephant sent to kill him, and Gunamalai's love follows.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both poems make the man who masters a charging beast a Krishna figure whom a girl then loves, but the herders' bull-catch is a set contest whose price the community names, while the epic's rescue is unplanned and the girl chooses for herself.","gn":["the older pattern of beast-taming as a bride-winning feat under a Krishna simile, which explains why the elephant fight leads straight into Gunamalai's love","a later merchant-city version of the pattern in which the danger falls on the girl and the feat is a rescue, not a sport"]},{"a":"cc:993","b":"n:30","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 30 says kin come unasked and go silently, leaving the body as a bird leaves its nest-tree; 993 says Gunamalai spurns her kin's talk like those who know their births.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic borrows the renouncer's detachment from kin, which the Naladiyar grounds in kin's passing, to picture a girl whose turning from family is love for one man, so the same outward withdrawal is shown to have opposite causes.","gn":["the reasoning about kin as passing visitors that the simile's 'those who know their births' assumes but does not state","a sign that the quatrain's lesson had become a known figure of speech, usable even of a lover's absorption"]},{"a":"cc:997","b":"k:270","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 270 says the have-nots are many because few do penance; 997 has Gunamalai ask whether, like the small folk who did no penance, she has any door to escape her longing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's explanation of want by the lack of past tapas is taken up by a lover as an account of why the man she wants is out of reach, showing the doctrine at work in ordinary speech about desire.","gn":["the doctrinal sense of her simile: that what one lacks is owed to penance not done, which turns her cry into a judgment on her own merit","a reading of the couplet's unspecified 'those who lack' as covering the lover's lack, which the couplet leaves open"]},{"a":"cc:999","b":"k:1137","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural praises womanhood that suffers a sea of desire and does not ride the palmyra horse; Gunamalai, with longing like the sea, considers riding it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the couplet's praised restraint from inside, as a near thing: the heroine reaches the very thought of the madal before turning to a go-between, so the Kural's 'does not ride' names a line held under pressure, not an absence of the wish.","gn":["Gunamalai's thought of the madal gains its measure: it is the one act the ethical tradition says a woman forgoes","the couplet's abstract womanhood gains a woman who voices its praise of herself (998) and then wavers, which a gnomic line cannot show"]},{"a":"cc:1018","b":"k:1320","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's wife takes offence even at his adoring gaze, asking whom he is thinking of; Gandharvadattai's sulk blazes at a portrait of another woman painted by her husband.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's comedy of jealousy depends on the husband's innocence, and the epic removes it: here the suspected other woman is real and painted by his own hand, so the sulk is no longer love's game but a just grievance.","gn":["Gandharvadattai's flare is placed in a known type, the wife of the pulavi nunukkam who reads every look as a rival","the couplet's baseless suspicion is set against a case where suspicion is right, showing how much its charm rests on there being no rival"]},{"a":"cc:1017","b":"k:1122","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's lover says his bond with the girl is that of body and life; Civakan tells his sulking wife they are one life in two bodies.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Spoken with another woman's portrait still in his arms, the claim of a single life becomes an appeasing formula, so the Cintamani shows the Kural's declaration of union put to use in a marriage that is about to take a second wife.","gn":["Civakan's line gains the weight of the classic declaration it reworks, which makes its hollowness here visible","the couplet, spoken in first love, gains a later setting that tests whether the claim survives a rival"]},{"a":"cc:1003","b":"cc:976","type":"same_image","note":"Gunamalai stood before the charging elephant looking about with doe eyes and trembling like lightning; Civakan paints exactly her look and her trembling.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The lover's image of the beloved is the instant of her mortal fear, so what he desires and keeps is the moment he saved her, and the rescue becomes the ground of the love."},{"a":"cc:1005","b":"cc:997","type":"same_image","note":"Gunamalai fears she has no way to live, like little people who have done no tapas; Civakan says her very ornaments did penance to be on her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Apart and unknown to each other, both lovers reach for the language of penance: she counts herself among those without its merit, while he grants its merit even to her jewels, so the same idea marks her despair and his worship."},{"a":"cc:1017","b":"cc:1005","type":"tension","note":"Civakan praised Gunamalai so that even her girdle and anklets had done penance; to his wife he calls her the sand of a seep-well beside the Ganges.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Within a few verses the hero ranks the same woman as worthy of the jewels' penance and as a puddle beside his wife, so the poem lets the reader see his words to each woman fitted to the listener."},{"a":"cc:1018","b":"cc:988","type":"same_image","note":"Gunamalai wasted like a garland in a ghee-fed flame; Gandharvadattai's sulk blazes like fire with ghee poured on it.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"One simile serves both women: the fire that consumed the girl's longing now feeds the wife's jealousy, so the poem sets the two women's passions, love and anger, burning from the same man.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:1043","b":"pn:71","type":"develops","note":"Boothappandiyan swears that if he does not see his enemies' backs he may be parted from his wife; Civakan swears that if he does not live with Gunamalai he may be one who turned his weapon on the beaten.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The vanjinam form stakes one domain against the other in both directions: the king pledges his marriage to secure a victory, and the epic hero pledges his warrior honour to secure a marriage, choosing as penalty an attack on fleeing men, the very thing the king's vow hopes to see.","gn":["the Sangam vanjinam kaanji as the source of its self-curse, which the epic turns from a battle vow into a love pledge in a letter","evidence that the vow's frame outlived the battlefield and that a later poet could read its stake, the beloved, as reversible with the warrior's code"]},{"a":"cc:1028","b":"k:1137","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural ranks highest the woman who suffers a sea of desire and does not ride the palmyra horse; the parrot says maidens hide their love-sickness from even their nearest, out of shame.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The silence the couplet praises has a working cost the couplet does not show: because the girl will not speak even to her confidante, the knowledge of her illness has to travel by a go-between who asks the man to diagnose it.","gn":["a rule stating why Gunamalai must use a messenger rather than speak, which the scene assumes but does not argue","a scene showing who carries the woman's unspoken desire once shame forbids her to declare it"]},{"a":"cc:1041","b":"k:1102","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's man says the woman who causes his love-sickness is its only cure; Civakan writes that the little parrot she sent has cured his swelling sickness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the couplet's rule that only the beloved herself can heal, the letter shows the cure passing to her token: a sent message relieves the sickness before any meeting, so the remedy can be borne by a proxy.","gn":["the convention that love's cure is the beloved, against which the parrot's cure reads as a claim about messengers","a case of courtship by letter, where the remedy the couplet confines to the woman's presence is carried by a go-between"]},{"a":"cc:1036","b":"k:265","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says penance is striven for because it gains what one wants as one wants it; Gunamalai says that if Civakan refuses she will win him even by penance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The girl takes the couplet's motive for தவம் at its word and aims it at a husband, which exposes how far the Kural's argument for penance rests on desire rather than release.","gn":["a doctrinal ground for her resolve, which the verse states without explaining why penance should work","a concrete wish, love for a man, that tests the couplet's open promise of getting whatever is wanted"]},{"a":"cc:1031","b":"cc:1045","type":"develops","note":"Civakan pledges to wed her with his word not falling short (சொற்குன்றா); Gunamalai, having read his letter, offers worship so that he may not fail his word (சொற்குன்றான் ஆக).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hero's own phrase comes back as the heroine's prayer, so the pledge he treats as settled she treats as needing divine witnesses, which marks the unequal risk the two carry in a promise of marriage."},{"a":"cc:1037","b":"cc:1039","type":"develops","note":"Gunamalai draws a circle in the sand asking that the union come about without fail; the parrot then lands and she hears the message of his pledge.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The divination is answered not by the circle closing but by the messenger's arrival, so the story replaces the omen with news and lets the girl's private rite be fulfilled by the man's written word."},{"a":"cc:1047","b":"cc:1003","type":"same_image","note":"In 1003 Civakan paints Gunamalai on cloth as she stood trembling before the elephant; in 1047 the nurse says the cousin worships a likeness of her painted on cloth every day.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The nurse's test gives the rival suitor the very gesture Civakan made, so Gunamalai's refusal shows that what binds her is the man she chose, not the devotion paid to her image."},{"a":"cc:1049","b":"cc:978","type":"develops","note":"In 978 Civakan, seeing the girl in danger, judges death better than a life that lets her perish and charges the elephant; in 1049 she calls service at his feet the wage for that toil.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Her refusal of the cousin is set up as repayment of a specific risk, so the epic makes her love the answer to his choosing death over a shamed life."},{"a":"cc:1050","b":"cc:908","type":"develops","note":"After losing the powder contest Suramanjari vows that none but Civakan shall break her reserve and shuts herself away (902, 908); after winning it, Gunamalai vows death or penance if she is denied him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The quarrel Civakan judged ends with both rivals bound by vows only marriage to him can release, the loser by seclusion and the winner by threat of death, so his verdict sets up two of his marriages at once.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:1050","b":"k:54","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 54 says nothing is greater than a woman who has the firmness called karpu; Gunamalai shows such firmness before marriage, against her own parents, for a man she has only chosen.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the couplet's firmness moves from a wife's fidelity back to a maiden's choice, where it shows itself as resisting her parents with death or renunciation rather than as serving a husband.","gn":["the Kural's naming of karpu as திண்மை, a strength, which lets her threat read as firmness of virtue rather than wilfulness","a case of that firmness exercised before any marriage and directed against the girl's own parents, which a couplet about a wife does not foresee"]},{"a":"cc:1050","b":"a:369","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 369 the foster mother laments that her girl refused the rich wedding her kin had prepared and went off across the waste with a youth; Gunamalai, told by her nurse of a match with a rich cousin, names death or penance as her only alternatives.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Beside the Akam girl who answers an unwanted family wedding by elopement, Gunamalai answers by a vow spoken to her nurse, keeping the refusal inside the house where it can move her parents instead of leaving them.","gn":["the akam convention of the refused kin wedding, which marks the nurse's report as the familiar trigger for a heroine's flight that this heroine declines","an outcome in which the same refusal, voiced rather than acted on, ends in a sanctioned wedding, a path the elopement poem never shows"]},{"a":"cc:1052","b":"pp:8:1-22","type":"develops","note":"Kurinjippattu opens with the friend disclosing to the mother the marriage the girl chose past her father's guard; in 1052 the nurse who sounded Gunamalai tells the mother, who tells the father.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic runs the akam disclosure through the whole household chain and adds the nurse's verdict that the match ripens from past penance, so a convention of love poetry becomes a step in a karmic marriage plot.","gn":["the logic of அறத்தொடு நிற்றல், by which the nurse's report reads as a recognised duty to disclose rather than gossip","a narrated sequel in which disclosure reaches the father and produces the wedding, which the idyll's speech to the mother can only argue for"]},{"a":"cc:1059","b":"cc:996","type":"same_image","note":"In 996 Gunamalai says her heart burns like the sacrificial fire; in 1059 Civakan's mother's unfailing chastity is like the flame of the mantra-fed Vedic sacrifice.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same ritual fire stands first for a girl's consuming desire and then for a matron's chastity, so the epic uses one Vedic image for passion and for the discipline that marriage is meant to make of it."},{"a":"cc:1063","b":"cc:1037","type":"develops","note":"In 1037 Gunamalai draws circles in the sand, asking whether she will unite with Civakan if his errand prospers; in 1063 the wedding drums sound.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sand-circle divination she made in doubt is answered by the wedding within the same ilampakam, so the epic lets a folk rite of the anxious lover come true."},{"a":"cc:1069","b":"cc:899","type":"develops","note":"In 899 Suramanjari, hearing Civakan call her powder unfit, vows penance until he comes defeated to worship her anklets; in 1069 Civakan asks Gunamalai whether that harsh word is arrow or nectar to him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hero knows his verdict both won him Gunamalai and earned him a rival's vow against him, so his teasing question names the double fruit of one judgement."},{"a":"cc:1070","b":"k:1316","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1316 the woman turns the man's 'I thought of you' into proof that he had forgotten her; in 1070 Gunamalai turns his praise of a powder into proof that he forgot her and keeps another in his heart (மறந்தீர், மனத்துள் உறைகின்றாள்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's sulking logic, that any word of the lover proves his neglect, becomes in the epic a weapon aimed at a named rival's beauty, so feigned jealousy is how the epic's wife claims the hero among his many loves.","gn":["the epic verse gains a recognised move of the ஊடல் convention that shows its accusation is play, not real suspicion","the couplet gains a rival woman and a remembered contest as the occasion of the twisted word, which it leaves abstract"]},{"a":"cc:1074","b":"k:1109","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1109 names sulking, making up and union as love's rewards; verses 1071, 1072 and 1074 enact the three in order, sulk, appeasement at her feet, embrace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic plays out the couplet's triad in full and then cuts it off with the king's runners, so love's three rewards are set as the calm the hero's karma-laden story breaks.","gn":["the three verses gain a frame that makes them one completed movement rather than a string of descriptions","the couplet gains a narrative cost: its rewards are shown as fragile, interrupted by a power outside the lovers"]},{"a":"cc:1076","b":"cc:14","type":"develops","note":"The patikam (14) tells how Civakan overcame the victorious elephant that frightened Gunamalai; in 1076 that same elephant, ashamed, refuses its food.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The victory the patikam lists as a rescue becomes, through the beast's shame and the mahout's report, the usurper's pretext for seizing the hero, so a deed of protection ripens into danger."},{"a":"cc:1079","b":"k:567","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 567 says harsh words and punishment past all bounds file down a king's conquering strength; in 1079 the king, raging over a sulking elephant, orders the hero seized and his strength rooted out.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows where such excess comes from: a usurper's envy of a subject's fame finds a trivial cause, and the order that follows is the first stroke of the file that will bring his own fall.","gn":["the king's order gains a rule by which the reader can judge it as the self-wearing excess of a bad ruler, not just anger","the couplet gains a source for excessive punishment in wounded pride, and a story in which the file's work can be traced"]},{"a":"cc:1080","b":"si:16:148-163","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Silappathikaram the Pandya, inquiring into nothing, orders the guards to kill the thief and bring the anklet (கொன்றச் சிலம்பு கொணர்க); in 1080 the angry king's men run to kill and bring Civakan's life (கொன்றுயிர் கொணர) on a mahout's word.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same unexamined order to kill and bring yields Kovalan's death and the bent sceptre in one epic but only a binding in the other, because Civakan chooses submission and a god he once helped intervenes; the Cintamani turns the scene of ripened evil into one where restraint and past kindness turn it aside."},{"a":"cc:1089","b":"k:895","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 895 says those who draw a mighty king's anger will survive nowhere; Kantukkatan reasons that to contend with the king is the work of the doomed (விளிகுற்றார்) and sends his son bound.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The merchant applies the couplet's prudence to a king who is a usurper and his son's true father's killer, showing that the rule of not provoking the strong counsels submission even to injustice, a cost the couplet does not weigh.","gn":["Kantukkatan's choice gains the weight of received wisdom, so it reads as prudence rather than cowardice","the couplet gains a case in which obeying it means yielding to an unjust king, which tests its scope"]},{"a":"cc:1090","b":"cc:393","type":"develops","note":"In 393 the teacher asks as his fee that Civakan bear no anger against the usurper for a year, and he agrees; in 1090, recalling his teacher's word, he lets his arms be bound with his anger quenched.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vow made in private as a teacher's fee is what actually disarms the hero before the king's men, so the plot's turn depends on a kept promise rather than on the parents' prudence or the hero's weakness."},{"a":"cc:1092","b":"cc:16","type":"develops","note":"In 1092 the king's men bind Civakan's shoulders with their belts (கச்சின் வீக்கலின்); the patikam (16) tells how the god removed the flower-belt (பூங் கச்சு) that bound his shoulders.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The belt that binds the hero here is the very thing the patikam has the grateful god remove, so the eclipse simile's promise of a passing darkness is already answered at the epic's opening."},{"a":"cc:1093","b":"cc:1090","type":"tension","note":"In 1090 Sunandai and her husband call going under the king's protection the right course; in 1093 she falls into a sea of unending grief as he is led away bound.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The foster mother who counselled submission as virtue cannot bear its result, so the chapter sets the household's prudent counsel against its cost to the one who gave it."},{"a":"cc:1097","b":"k:339","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural likens death to sleep and birth to waking; Civakan uses the waking from heavy sleep for the soul's appearing in the states of birth, adds release as a third motion, and draws from it a refusal to grieve at his own arrest.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural's calm figure of sleep and waking only describes the cycle, while the Cintamani makes it an argument for conduct, so understanding birth as waking becomes the ground for refusing grief, and the cycle gains an exit the couplet never names.","gn":["the hero's consolation is seen to rest on a figure the ethical tradition already held, which makes his calm learned rather than merely brave","the couplet's image is shown as the first step of a larger scheme in which knowing the cycle aims at leaving it"]},{"a":"cc:1097","b":"k:889","type":"same_image","note":"Both measure a small but fatal thing as a split sesame seed: the Kural an inner enmity that holds ruin, Civakan the least attachment, which he calls a fault.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sesame measure moves from a king's household enemy to the attachment inside the self, so the danger the Kural locates in a court the Jain hero locates in the heart that clings.","gn":["Civakan's rule is given a familiar measure of how little it takes to ruin, transferred from politics to the soul","the couplet's warning that a tiny hidden thing contains ruin is extended to attachment, a second enemy within"]},{"a":"cc:1097","b":"cc:269","type":"develops","note":"Saccandan, before his death, tells Vijayai that dying and being born come from one's deeds and that grief comes from want of understanding; his son, now seized by the same usurper, tells the women of his house that one who knows births and release will not grieve.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Father and son meet the usurper's violence with the same doctrinal consolation to the women who love them, but the father dies after speaking it and the son walks on alive, so the teaching recurs as the epic's own answer to tyranny rather than one man's farewell."},{"a":"cc:1108","b":"cc:935","type":"develops","note":"The city women see Civakan's arrest as the fruit of deeds; the dog beaten at the festival was said to have fallen into the net of strong karma, and Civakan freed it with the five mantras.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same karmic explanation covers the beaten dog and the bound hero, but the story has already shown Civakan's own good deed to that dog, so the crowd's 'fruit of deeds' is about to ripen as the god's rescue of him."},{"a":"cc:1109","b":"k:894","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says the weak who harm the strong beckon Death with their hand; the city women say the king who seizes Civakan has clapped his hands at Death and will perish with his line.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The women apply the couplet against the one who holds power, so the 'weak' party is the reigning king and the 'strong' is the bound merchant's son, which makes strength a matter of worth rather than of office.","gn":["the women's curse is seen to rest on a received rule about provoking the stronger, not on mere anger","the couplet is shown to be usable against a king, with the strong one being the person he has in chains"]},{"a":"cc:1109","b":"k:270","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the many lack because few do penance; the city women say they lose Civakan because they did no penance.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet's account of why most people lack is turned into a mourner's self-blame, so lack of penance explains not poverty but the loss of a beloved, and the women accuse themselves while blaming the king and the father in the same verse.","gn":["the lament's first cry is seen to rest on the ethical rule that what one keeps is earned by austerity","the couplet's severe claim is shown working as consolation and self-reproach in grief, a use its general form does not show"]},{"a":"cc:1116","b":"cc:275","type":"same_image","note":"Saccandan, ringed in his palace, was a roaring lion hemmed in at a cave mouth and died fighting; his son, seized by the same usurper, goes as a lion bound with a spider's thread.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The lion image marks the difference between father and son: the father was caught by force and fought to death, the son could break his bond at will and keeps it, so the son survives the trap the father did not."},{"a":"cc:1116","b":"cc:16","type":"develops","note":"The patikam tells that the god Civakan once helped untied the flower-cord with which soldiers had bound his arms.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The bond that holds the lion here is as weak as a spider's thread and is in fact loosed by a god, so the image of 1116 is fulfilled literally: it is never the king's force that holds him."},{"a":"cc:1116","b":"cc:1091","type":"develops","note":"Civakan let himself be bound because his parents asked it and told him his manhood would not be lessened; here he goes bound like a lion held by a spider's thread.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The thread that binds the lion is the parents' word of 1091, so the spider's thread is the image of consent, and the arrest becomes a test of filial obedience rather than of strength."},{"a":"cc:1117","b":"k:691","type":"develops","note":"The Kural tells those who serve hot-tempered kings to be like those warming at a fire, neither too far nor too near; Kantukkatan judges the king's anger a fire that burns all who come near and sends gold to quench it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural's fire teaches distance, while the merchant adds a remedy, gold poured like rain, so the pair shows a courtier's caution and a merchant's purchase as two answers to the same burning king.","gn":["Kantukkatan's payment is seen as a known prudence toward royal anger, not mere fear","the couplet's image is shown to allow a means of putting the fire out, which its advice of keeping distance leaves aside"]},{"a":"cc:1117","b":"cc:1089","type":"develops","note":"Kantukkatan reasoned that opposing a king is the work of the doomed and that sending his son bound would cool the king's anger; now he adds twelve crores of gold to quench it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The father's policy of submission is completed by payment, so between these two verses his defiant words to the crowd (1114-1115) stand as consolation only, and his actual answer to the king is obedience bought with gold."},{"a":"cc:1131","b":"cc:1158","type":"develops","note":"The sage foretold that when the graceless king seized Civakan to kill him a yaksha would carry him off; in 1158 a god lifts Civakan and rises away in the storm while the city mourns.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The rescue the friends are plotting by fire and disguise was already promised to the parents, so the plan of the companions is made needless by a fate a Jain ascetic had foreseen."},{"a":"cc:1130","b":"cc:388","type":"develops","note":"In 388 the merchant takes up the infant 'as the great ascetic had foretold' (அருந்தவன் முந்து கூற); here, years later, the father tells what that foretelling was.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The foundling's adoption, told earlier as a bare fulfilment, turns out to rest on a promise given to a mother in despair, and the same prophecy also held the hero's capture and rescue."},{"a":"cc:1124","b":"cc:325","type":"develops","note":"In 325 the merchant leaves his own dead son at the burning ground and goes off with the royal child; 1124 recalls that Sunandai had borne many children who died before the ascetic came.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The exchange at the burning ground was the last of a run of dead births, so the merchant's taking of the child answers a grief long prepared for, not a chance find."},{"a":"cc:1135","b":"cc:1138","type":"develops","note":"Padumukan guesses that only Civakan's parents could have bound him; the spy reports that the father embraced him by a stratagem and the weeping mother bound his arms.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend's reasoning from the hero's invincibility is confirmed by the report, so the one force the story allows to bind Civakan is filial obedience, not the usurper's army."},{"a":"cc:1138","b":"cc:1116","type":"develops","note":"In 1116 Civakan is led off bound like a lion caught in a spider's thread; the spy's report explains the thread: his parents' embrace and tears.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The spider's thread of 1116 is glossed by the scene of 1138: a weak bond holds the lion because he will not break his parents' hold, and he feels the shame of it toward his friends."},{"a":"cc:1121","b":"cc:1165","type":"tension","note":"Kattiyangaran swears to write 'thief' on Civakan's chest; in 1165 he praises Civakan as without equal in grace, strength, learning and generosity and rewards the man who claims to have killed him.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The usurper's own praise in 1165 shows that his charge of theft was never a judgment, so the bent sceptre here is a king punishing a man he knows to be the best of his subjects."},{"a":"cc:1118","b":"k:550","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 550 likens a king's putting killers to death to weeding a crop; here the king sentences the youth who stopped the royal elephant from killing a girl.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's justice assumes that the king punishes the killer; the epic shows a king whose own beast is the killer punishing the rescuer, so the weeding image fails when the king himself decides what the weed is.","gn":["a rule by which Kantukkatan's question 'if this is a fault, what is virtue?' becomes a charge of inverted justice","a case in which the one who owns the deadly force is the judge, which the couplet's farming image cannot address"]},{"a":"cc:1122","b":"k:269","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 269 says that those who attain the power of austerity can even leap past death; here the ascetic's words, remembered, keep the stricken merchant alive.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural places the power of tapas in the ascetic's own victory over death, while the epic shows it working on householders, through a foretelling whose remembered word revives a father who stood as if dead.","gn":["a name for the force that holds up the merchant: the power of penance, here exercised through speech","a story in which that power reaches beyond the ascetic to the lay people who fed him"]},{"a":"cc:1132","b":"k:1164","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1164 the woman parted from her lover says there is a sea of love but no safe raft to cross it; here Gunamalai, parted from Civakan and near death, crosses the sea of grief on the raft of the ascetic's words.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural's parted woman has no raft because no one sends word; the epic gives its parted woman a raft from outside love altogether, an ascetic's prophecy, so the same plight is solved by religious authority rather than by the lover.","gn":["the convention behind its image: the sea and raft are the parted woman's set terms, and Gunamalai's grief is cast as a lover's","a case where the raft the couplet denies is supplied, and by whom, which shows what the woman of the couplet lacks"]},{"a":"cc:1156","b":"cc:15","type":"develops","note":"The patikam tells that a god came down the sky's path like lightning from a cloud and carried the strong one away from the king's prison; here Sudanjanan feels the omen, learns the danger and descends in storm.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The patikam's single line is filled out with the god's heaven, his omen and his far-seeing knowledge, so the rescue is shown as the answer of a bond, not a stroke of luck."},{"a":"cc:1156","b":"cc:947","type":"develops","note":"In 947 Civakan gives the dying dog the five padas and it leaves its body; here the dog, now a god among goddesses, comes down to save the one who gave it the mantra.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two scenes make the mantra given out of pity into the cause of the hero's deliverance, the Jain epic's way of showing a good deed returning within the same story.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:1154","b":"k:107","type":"develops","note":"Kural 107 says people remember through seven births the friendship of those who wiped away their sorrow; Civakan, facing death, counts on the friend whose death-sorrow he eased in another body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic makes the couplet's seven births literal: a dog that became a god still keeps the debt, and the debtor runs to the man who wiped his tears.","gn":["a moral frame that makes Civakan's appeal to the god a claim of gratitude rather than a call on magic","a case where the remembered friendship crosses an actual rebirth from beast to god, which the couplet only asserts as a span of time"]},{"a":"cc:1149","b":"k:903","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 903 says stooping before one's wife brings a man shame among the good; Gandharvadattai, able to free Civakan, holds back because being saved by his wife would shame his strength and learning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's shame is a man's fear of his wife's power, but here the wife herself fears it on his behalf and keeps her power idle, so the rule's cost falls on the woman who could act.","gn":["a source in Tamil ethics for the code that stops her hand","a scene showing the rule enforced from the woman's side, at the price of leaving the husband in danger"]},{"a":"cc:1152","b":"k:967","type":"develops","note":"Kural 967 holds it better to be called ruined than to live following those who scorn one; Civakan says he does not want to live if he is set free by his wife's spells.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet's preference for ruin over dishonour onto rescue by a wife, so honour can refuse even a loving help, and the story then lets a male friend's help pass as honourable.","gn":["a name, maanam, for the choice Civakan makes in chains","an instance where the dishonourable life is not servitude to enemies but survival by a woman's power, which shows how gendered the couplet's honour could be made"]},{"a":"cc:1163","b":"k:327","type":"develops","note":"Kural 327 forbids taking another's life even to save one's own; Madanan's men, fearing the king, kill a stranger and pass him off as Civakan to save themselves.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The story shows the exact deed the couplet forbids and shows its source in the tyrant's terror, so the rule against killing is seen giving way first where the sceptre is bent.","gn":["the Kural's measure for judging an act the narration only reports","a case of the forbidden trade of life for life driven by fear of a king, which ties the chapter on not killing to the chapter on oppressive rule"]},{"a":"cc:1164","b":"cc:1157","type":"same_image","note":"The storm the god raises in 1157 to hide the rescue is the rain, darkness and wind Madanan cites in 1164 as the reason he had Civakan cut down.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The one storm serves two ends: it hides the god's rescue and gives the killers the alibi that lets the usurper believe his rival dead."},{"a":"cc:1152","b":"cc:1140","type":"tension","note":"Buddhisenan's plan in 1140 is to burn the city and free Civakan by force; Civakan here sets both that and his wife's magic aside and waits on his friend.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The prisoner overrules all the rescues offered him, so his deliverance comes only from the bond he made himself, which is the episode's point about merit."},{"a":"cc:1167","b":"cc:1172","type":"develops","note":"The hero calls his release ஊழ்வினை; five verses later the god who freed him explains that Civakan once eased his suffering as a dog and gave him his body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the 'former deed' the hero blames is no hidden deed of a past birth but his own recent kindness to a dying dog returning to him, so the epic's karma is visible to the reader while the hero speaks of it as unknown.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:1172","b":"cc:943","type":"develops","note":"In 943 Civakan promised the dying dog a god's body shining like the sun's disc if it thought on the mantra; in 1172 the god, now in that body, says the man gave it to him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The promise made through a mantra is remembered by the one who received it as a personal gift of the man, so the epic lets doctrine (the mantra's power) and gratitude (the debt to the giver) both stand as the cause of the rescue."},{"a":"cc:1167","b":"k:379","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 379 asks why those who take good fortune as good should grieve when it turns; Civakan, freed after imprisonment, neither grieves nor rejoices and ascribes both to former deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic answers the couplet's question with a stricter ideal: not only bearing ill as one bears good, but declining joy at good fortune too, since rescue is as much karma's working as captivity.","gn":["the hero's indifference gets a recognised ethical frame, the Kural's reasoning that what fate gives cannot be graded into welcome and unwelcome","the couplet's rhetorical question gets a test case in which the good turn comes through another's hand, a god's, and is still referred to one's own deeds"]},{"a":"cc:1167","b":"pn:192","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 192 says good and ill do not come from others, and the speakers neither rejoice that life is sweet nor curse it as bitter; Civakan is freed by a god yet calls the freeing his own former deed and feels neither joy nor grief.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the Sangam sage's 'not from others' being kept even when the rescue plainly does come from another: the epic folds the rescuer into the hero's own deed, the dog he once helped.","gn":["the hero's two-line equanimity is set against a fuller statement of the same stance, with its image of life carried like a raft by the river","the poem's abstract claim gets a narrative in which an outside helper exists and the claim must explain him, which it does by tracing the help back to the self"]},{"a":"cc:1177","b":"t:2422","type":"tension","note":"The Caranas at Aranapatham wash away their own karma (தம்வினை கழுவுகின்றார்) by renouncing rule; Tirumanthiram 2422 says the soul is cleaned of its dirt as cloth by a washer, and everything that comes to it is the bond of grace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both use the image of washing a stain from the self, but in the Jain epic the ascetic is the washer and the dirt is his karma, while in Tirumular the washer is grace and even the soul's bonds are its soap; the shared image exposes the rival accounts of who does the cleansing.","gn":["the Cintamani's brief phrase about karma-washing is shown to carry a doctrine of self-effort that a Saiva reader would contest","Tirumular's claim that all is grace's bond is sharpened against a named alternative, ascetics who clean themselves without a god"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:1183","b":"k:918","type":"develops","note":"Kural 918 says the embrace of deceiving women is an அணங்கு to those without discerning sense; in 1183 the god warns of ghoul-women who come as pining lovers and, embracing, wring out a man's life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet's metaphor into a literal creature of its geography: the deceiving lover really is a life-drinking demon, and what saves the hero is the god's forewarning, standing in for the 'discerning sense' the couplet demands.","gn":["the ghoul episode is seen to carry the Kural's ethics of the courtesan rather than being a mere marvel of the road","the couplet's அணங்கு, which might be read as hyperbole, gets a narrative that spells out what the word threatens: the embrace that kills"]},{"a":"cc:1188","b":"kl:11","type":"same_image","note":"Kalittokai 11 gives the three tender creatures of the burning waste: the tusker who lets his mate drink first, the dove that fans its mate with its wings, the stag that gives its shadow to the shadeless doe; 1187-1188 give the elephant shielding its calf, the cock bird sheltering the hen with its wings, and the stag shading the doe with its body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Cintamani keeps the palai triad but changes its use: in the Kalittokai the creatures' care is the heroine's ground for hoping the man will return, while here it only measures the heat for a lone traveller with no beloved at home, so the akam sign of love becomes a road-guide's warning.","gn":["the god's description is shown to be a set piece of Tamil palai convention, which explains why these three animals and no others appear","the Kalittokai's images are seen to outlive their love setting, showing that the triad had become a stock mark of the desert itself"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:1176","b":"s:1956","type":"tension","note":"Konganar's Vaalaik Kummi counts going to foreign lands (தேசாந்திரங்களும் செல்லாதே) among things to avoid, with facing a tiger in the forest and courtesans' ways; Civakan calls those who never leave home fish in a well and sets out, and the god's road then holds elephant forests and ghoul-women.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On the same question, whether to go to other lands, the Siddhar's caution and the hero's resolve give opposite advice, and the epic's road is made of exactly the dangers the Siddhar lists, so the epic treats them as the hero's schooling rather than a reason to stay; the conflict holds only if the kummi's line is read as general advice and not as a rule for the adept alone.","gn":["the hero's departure is seen as a choice against a known prudential rule, not a mere plot device","the kummi's terse prohibition gets its strongest counter-case, a traveller whom the dangers it names do not ruin"]},{"a":"cc:1214","b":"cc:21","type":"develops","note":"The patikam tells how the king of the middle country (மத்திம தேய மன்னன்) came out to receive Civakan on seeing his bow; in 1214 the god foretells that meeting (மத்திம தேயம் ஆளும் ... கண்ணுறு குணம்) before the journey begins.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The meeting the summary reports as the king's response to the hero's bow is, in the god's speech, a fortune already laid out on the road, so the poem frames a political welcome as the fruit of a divine guide's foreknowledge."},{"a":"cc:1215","b":"cc:23","type":"same_image","note":"The patikam compares Civakan's younger brother, desolate, to a star that has lost the moon (மதியம் கெடுத்த வய மீன்); 1215 gives the same image (வெண்மதி யிழந்த மீன்போல்) to all his friends.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The stars-without-the-moon figure belongs to the hero's whole circle rather than to one kinsman, so Civakan is the moon to every follower and his absence dims them all alike, brother and friends."},{"a":"cc:1215","b":"cc:22","type":"develops","note":"The patikam says the lion-like royals of the middle country kept Civakan and gave him a girl in marriage; 1215 has the god foretell that the king will give him his daughter while he stays there.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with the patikam, the marriage that looks like the royals' choice to bind a strong stranger was promised in advance by the god, so the hero's marriages come to him as foretold gifts rather than conquests."},{"a":"cc:1200","b":"k:916","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 916 scorns women who spread a base goodness (புன்னலம் பாரிப்பார்) to all; in 1200 the river, sharing its own goodness to feed the world (தன்னுடைய நலம் பகிர்ந்து உலகம் ஊட்டலின்), is called a courtesan with approval.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same act, a நலம் given to everyone, is a mark of baseness in the Kural and of bounty in the epic's simile, which shows that the Kural's contempt lies in the sale of a person and not in the sharing itself.","gn":["a reminder that the courtesan figure carried moral weight, so the epic's praise of the river is a deliberate reversal and not a neutral image","a case where the courtesan's openness is turned to praise once nothing personal is sold, sharpening what exactly the couplet condemns"]},{"a":"cc:1208","b":"k:974","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 974 says greatness exists only for one who holds himself as single-minded women (ஒருமை மகளிர்) do; 1208 likens a straight unforking road to the minds of chaste women (கற்புடை மாதரார் மனம்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both texts use the chaste woman's mind as the model of an undivided line, for a man's greatness in one and for a road in the other, showing that karpu was read as singleness of direction rather than as purity alone.","gn":["a precedent that the chaste mind is a figure of oneness, which explains why the epic chooses it for a road without forks","a concrete picture, a straight road strewn with flowers, of what the couplet's single-mindedness looks like in space"]},{"a":"cc:1205","b":"k:329","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 329 says those whose work is killing are base in the eyes of the discerning; 1205 calls the forest bowmen who kill travellers embodiments of sin (பாவ மூர்த்தியர்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet judges a deed, killing, while the epic fixes the same judgment on a forest people as a class, so the pair shows how the ethics of not-killing could harden into a picture of hunters as sin incarnate.","gn":["the couplet's ground for the label: the hunters are condemned for the work of killing, not for being foresters","evidence of how the principle was applied to whole groups living by the bow, which the couplet's general wording does not show"]},{"a":"cc:1212","b":"cc:1208","type":"tension","note":"1208 likens the straight red road to the minds of chaste women; 1212 likens a road splitting into many forks to the hearts of women on the path of marriage.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"If மணநெறி மகளிர் are married women, the same speech makes women's minds the figure of both the one straight road and the forking one, showing the simile serves the road and carries no settled view of women."},{"a":"cc:1217","b":"cc:945","type":"develops","note":"The god gives Civakan three mantras because Civakan once whispered the five padas into the dying dog's ear (செல்கதி மந்திரம் செவியிற் செப்பிய).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The mantra that carried a dying dog to a god's birth comes back to the hero as mantras of power for his road, so the epic lets an act of compassion bear its fruit in this same life through the very being it saved.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:1234","b":"mm:16:107-116","type":"qualifies","note":"A hill chief tells a visitor he cannot keep his body alive without liquor and meat; in the Manimekalai Saathuvan concedes and gives the Naga chief a reduced dharma, while Civakan presses on and wins full renunciation.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Faced with the same objection from a forest people, the Buddhist epic tailors a lesser rule to what the hearer can bear while the Jain epic refuses any compromise and makes the hunter choose heaven over his body, which shows how differently the two works weigh abstention against a hearer's livelihood."},{"a":"cc:1234","b":"k:251","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks how one who eats another's flesh to swell his own can have grace; the hunter says he needs meat merely to keep his body alive.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's eater fattens himself by choice, while the hunter claims he must eat flesh to survive at all; the epic thus raises the case the couplet passes over and answers it not with grace but with the threat of hell and the promise of heaven.","gn":["Civakan's demand gains a measure from the classic statement that flesh-eating swells one's own flesh at another's cost, which is what 1235 line 1 restates","the couplet gains a hearer who objects from need, not appetite, which tests whether its rule holds for people who live by hunting"]},{"a":"cc:1235","b":"k:255","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says that if one eats flesh hell does not open its mouth; Civakan sets hell for the meat-eater against a god's birth for one who abstains and makes the hunter choose.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet's one-sided warning into a two-way choice with a reward on the other side and has the eater himself pronounce the verdict, so the Kural's rule becomes a conversion by question and answer.","gn":["the epic's hell gains the couplet's sharper image of a mouth that will not reopen, which makes the threat permanent","the couplet gains a positive alternative it does not state, a god's birth bought by the wasting of the body, and a listener who accepts it"]},{"a":"cc:1234","b":"t:199","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular calls flesh-eaters புலையர and sends them to hell; Civakan says the hunters were born low because of former killing and eating.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Tirumular makes baseness a name earned by what one eats now, whatever one's birth, while the epic makes the low birth itself the fruit of past eating, so the two texts tie the same stigma to flesh-eating from opposite ends of the karmic chain.","gn":["Civakan's account of low birth is shown to keep birth-rank as real and deserved, which the comparison with Tirumular's moral use of புலையர் exposes","Tirumular's after-death punishment gains an earlier stage in the chain, a birth already lowered by flesh-eating, which his verse does not consider"]},{"a":"cc:1240","b":"t:1527","type":"tension","note":"The Cintamani's god is worshipped as the one who cut away his own ghati karmas; Tirumular's grace-power comes as guru and removes the soul's bonds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On the reading that the Jina is honoured for destroying his own karmas, the two texts disagree about what a god does with karma: the Jain god is the one who freed himself and is a model to circle, whereas Tirumular's Lord is the agent who frees the bound soul, so the worshipper's release comes from his own effort in one and from descending grace in the other.","gn":["the epic's brief epithet காதி கண் அரிந்த is shown to carry a whole doctrine of self-won release that it states without argument","Tirumular's grace gains a rival account in which no god acts on the soul, which sharpens why his verse insists that one's own agency must end"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:1242","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says those who reach the Lord's feet swim the sea of births; Civakan's hymn worships the Jina's lotus feet from within that sea and asks that attachment's bonds be untied.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Set beside the couplet, the hymn names what reaching the feet is supposed to do in a Jain frame: not a crossing given from outside but the loosening of attachment (பசை), so the same image of feet and sea carries a doctrine of release by unbinding.","gn":["the hymn's sea and feet are placed in the oldest Tamil statement of the image, which shows that the Jain poet is answering a known formula","the couplet's unnamed Lord and unexplained swimming gain one school's account of both: the Lord is the Jina and the swimming is the ending of attachment"]},{"a":"cc:1245","b":"t:343","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular says the three cities Siva destroyed are the work of the three malas; the Jain hymn gives the destroyer of three guarded forts to the Jina, who rose to the world's summit.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both a Saiva and a Jain poet read the three-city myth as the destruction of inner bonds, but they give it to different destroyers, so the pair shows the myth as ground the schools contested and allegorised in rival directions.","gn":["the hymn's bare epithet gains its source: it is Siva's deed, taken over for the Jina, and the allegorical reading of it was already current","Tirumular's correction of 'fools' gains a rival who also refuses the literal myth yet keeps no Siva in it, a sharper opponent than literalism"]},{"a":"cc:1247","b":"t:1527","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular has grace-power come as guru and end the malas when one's own agency ceases; the Jain hymn praises the Jina who ended karma that cannot be ended and taught the path of no return.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"On the reading that the Jina ended his own karma and teaches others to end theirs, the two verses give opposite answers to who removes karma: in the Saiva verse one's own doing must stop and grace acts, in the Jain verse the liberated one is the one whose own effort ended it.","gn":["the hymn's 'karma that cannot be ended' gains its contrast: the Saiva text agrees it cannot be ended by the soul alone but supplies a divine agent the Jain hymn does without","Tirumular's demand that one's own agency end gains a named rival doctrine in which the agent of release is a man who acted, which shows what the demand is arguing against"]},{"a":"cc:1260","b":"n:371","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar says a harlot's love ends when the hand is empty, like a lamp without oil; the Cintamani's dancer calls the saying that chastity breaks for gold a lie and vows herself to one man.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The dancer answers the very proverb the Naladiyar states, in her own voice, so the pair shows the epic giving the woman of the stage a reply that the didactic tradition never lets her make.","gn":["the dancer's 'those who said it lied' gains a named target: a saying of this kind stood in the ethical books","the quatrain's general rule gains a counter-claim from inside the class it judges, which tests whether the rule is observation or stereotype"]},{"a":"cc:1262","b":"cc:685","type":"tension","note":"In Rajamapuram the king saw that the dancer's longing had gone to Civakan and went in with resentment; in Chandirapam the prince follows the dancer's gaze to Civakan and is glad.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The poem stages the same scene twice, a ruler reading a dancer's eyes fixed on Civakan, and makes the ruler's response the measure of him: jealous enmity in the usurper's city, affection and alliance in the Pallava court."},{"a":"cc:1271","b":"cc:18","type":"develops","note":"The patikam told in one line that a snake took the king's daughter's face for the moon and bit her; verse 1271 tells the bite in scene.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam's line already made the bite a praise of her face; the full scene keeps that conceit but sets it at the instant she reaches for a flower she raised, so the compliment and the danger arrive in the same gesture."},{"a":"cc:1285","b":"cc:18","type":"develops","note":"In the patikam Civakan cures her by saying only 'let not your heart melt'; here he tells the household that she will not die and they must not weep.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The patikam's bare word of comfort turns out to be the promise of a man who, unlike every healer before him, first diagnoses and then cures, so the comfort is earned by the lore of the next three verses rather than given as a hero's charm."},{"a":"cc:1289","b":"cc:16","type":"develops","note":"The god freed Civakan and taught him spells, asking to be remembered; Civakan now heals the snakebite by reciting a spell.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The spells given in return for the nectar Civakan once gave a dying dog become the means that win him a princess, so the chain of gratitude the patikam began pays out as a cure, here reached through the mantra and not through any call on the god himself."},{"a":"cc:1280","b":"cc:1290","type":"tension","note":"The astrologer says the bite, given the hour, is hard to cure even for the Lord; within ten verses the venom leaves her at Civakan's gaze.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic sets a reading of the stars that declares death against a cure that follows at once, so the hour the astrologer trusted is shown not to bind, and the outcome turns on the healer's learning rather than on the time of the bite."},{"a":"cc:1284","b":"cc:1275","type":"develops","note":"The king had proclaimed by drum that whoever cures the girl will have land, wealth and the bride; now Civakan is told to sow fame and reap kinship in this land.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The public offer of a prize is restated to one man as a bond of kinship, so the marriage that the drum put up for any healer becomes, before the cure, a relationship offered to Civakan in particular."},{"a":"cc:1286","b":"k:948","type":"develops","note":"The Kural orders the healer to find the disease, its cause and the means that subdue it, then act; Civakan names the causes and kinds of bite and the snake's type before he recites the cure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic stages the couplet's order as a contest: a crowd of healers who act first and diagnose in contradiction all fail, and the one who examines cause and kind before acting succeeds, so the rule is shown by the cost of breaking it.","gn":["Civakan's string of classifications gains a reason for being in the story: it is the diagnostic step the ethics book requires and the rival healers skipped","the couplet's three steps gain a scene of failure beside success, which shows what is lost when treatment comes before finding the cause"]},{"a":"cc:1276","b":"k:941","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says the three humours counted by the learned, beginning with wind, cause disease when they exceed or fall short; one healer blames out-of-balance wind, bile and phlegm for the girl's state.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The humoral theory the Kural states as the general cause of disease is here invoked for a snakebite and fails, so the epic marks a limit the couplet does not: a correct doctrine of illness misleads when the cause is a poison from outside.","gn":["the healer's line is identified as learned medicine, so his failure is the failure of sound theory misapplied, not of quackery","the couplet's account of disease gains a case outside its reach, a harm that does not arise from the body's own balance"]},{"a":"cc:1287","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"The Kural says birth is alike for all living beings and distinction comes from the work they do; Civakan's lore sorts even snakes into four varnas known by the smell of their bite.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read with பிறப்பொக்கும் as denying rank by birth, the couplet stands against a lore that fixes varna in nature down to snakes; the pairing shows that the Cintamani's hero carries birth-rank as plain knowledge even where the Kural, speaking of all living beings, had set it aside.","gn":["the snake lore gains its weight as a claim about birth, which in the story passes as neutral technique","the couplet's 'all living beings' gains a test case among animals, where the opposing view assigns rank by birth alone"]},{"a":"cc:1281","b":"n:52","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar calls those who prattle of astrology the greatest fools; in the epic the astrologer's verdict of death fails while a bird omen of rescue comes true.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain epic does not dismiss divination wholesale as the quatrain does: it splits it, letting the star-reader's verdict be overturned while an omen pointing to a rescuer is fulfilled, so the scorn of the quatrain falls on fatalistic forecasting rather than on every sign.","gn":["the omen and the astrologer gain a measure: the epic's tradition holds such readings in suspicion, which makes the success of the one and the failure of the other a pointed choice","the quatrain's blanket scorn gains a narrative in which signs are tested against outcome, showing which kind of foretelling the scorn fits"]},{"a":"cc:1289","b":"t:705","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular lists removing poison among the powers that yoga brings; Civakan removes the venom by a spell he learned, after seven thousand Brahmins with mantras have failed.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Where the Tirumanthiram makes curing poison a sign of the yogi's attainment, the Cintamani gives it to a young layman as a learned art and lets the ritual mantra-masters fail, so the same power is moved from spiritual rank to skill and from priest to hero.","gn":["Civakan's cure gains its weight as a power the Saiva tradition reserves for adepts, which makes its ease in a householder's hands notable","the siddhi list gains a contrasting account in which poison-cure needs no yoga, only taught knowledge, which sharpens what Tirumular claims for his discipline"]},{"a":"cc:1292","b":"cc:1290","type":"tension","note":"In 1290 the poison leaves Padumai's body as darkness breaks before the sun; two verses later she is bitten again by the snake of desire.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The poet makes the cure the cause of a new sickness: the instant the real venom is drawn out, the girl's first look at her healer lets in a second venom, so the rescue and the love are one event told twice."},{"a":"cc:1296","b":"cc:1215","type":"develops","note":"The god foretold that the king who guards the earth would give Civakan his flawless daughter; the onlookers now say the king will give him the maiden today.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prophecy reaches the crowd as gossip: what the god told Civakan privately is now guessed aloud by strangers who do not even know who he is, so the story's foreknowledge passes from the divine to the public before the king has spoken."},{"a":"cc:1311","b":"cc:1296","type":"develops","note":"The onlookers guess whether he is a Vidyadhara hero or a prince of the gods; Padumai asks her maidens whether he is Kama or a young god, and says she does not know.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The question of the stranger's identity, a public puzzle in 1296, becomes in the girl's mouth a symptom of love: her not knowing who he is is now part of her sickness, since she cannot even name the thief."},{"a":"cc:1313","b":"cc:1308","type":"same_image","note":"Civakan says desire churns him (கடைகின்றது காமமே); Padumai says her heart is the ocean churned for nectar.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet gives the two lovers, lying apart on the same night, one verb: he says it plainly of himself, and she expands it into the whole churning of the ocean, so the image binds their separate laments into a matched pair."},{"a":"cc:1292","b":"t:1621","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular's single five-hooded serpent lives in the anthill of enjoyment and, its hoods drawn into one, makes the body its fit home; the epic's snake of desire lives in the anthill of the body ruled by the five senses and bites again.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both poets house a serpent of the five senses in the body's anthill, but Tirumular's can be stilled by discipline into one hood while the epic's strikes the moment a spell has cured the real snake, showing that a remedy for poison is no remedy for the senses.","gn":["a yogic reading of its anthill and five senses, which makes the love-bite a statement about the body and not only an ornament","a narrative case where the serpent of the senses is shown striking in a living person, against which its taming is measured"]},{"a":"cc:1295","b":"k:1095","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's man says she will not look at him directly but smiles as if narrowing one eye; the epic says Padumai bound Civakan with a look that looked as if it did not look.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The averted look that the Kural treats as a sign the man must decode becomes in the epic an act with an effect: the same not-quite-glance does not hint at love, it ties the hero fast, so the convention moves from evidence to force.","gn":["the convention behind its paradoxical look, which shows the line is a known sign and not a fresh conceit","a scene in which the half-look is followed by its consequence, the man bound, which the couplet leaves unstated"]},{"a":"cc:1298","b":"k:265","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says penance is striven for here because what is desired is obtained as desired; the onlookers say only women who did rare penance before could be born to sleep on Civakan's chest.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The onlookers take the couplet's promise at its word and fill 'whatever is desired' with a husband's embrace, showing how the motive the Kural offers for penance could be heard as making penance the price of pleasure in the next birth.","gn":["a doctrinal rule behind its onlookers' wonder, that what is gained now is the fruit of penance done before","a concrete example of what ordinary people wanted penance to bring, which tests how wide the couplet's 'whatever is desired' is meant to be"]},{"a":"cc:1302","b":"ac:27","type":"develops","note":"The Acharakkovai sets out the mouth-rinsing the great have seen as the way: three sips, wiping, and touching the face's parts with the fingers; Civakan sips three times, wipes three times and touches his face with his fingers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rule book's after-meal rinse appears in the epic, step for step, as a mark of the hero's breeding, performed under the eyes of a king who will next examine him, so the code of conduct works in the story as a credential.","gn":["the source of its unusually exact sequence, which shows the poet is narrating a known rite and not inventing detail","a scene showing the rite performed by a prince as a sign of refinement, beyond the bare prescription"]},{"a":"cc:1303","b":"ac:70","type":"qualifies","note":"The Acharakkovai lists what one does not do in a great man's house: spit, climb a high seat, chew areca, speak out of turn, lie down; in the king's house Civakan sits on a high flower seat, chews areca and lies on a couch.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Civakan does three of the five things forbidden in a great man's house, and does them at the king's command, so the pair shows that the rule binds the dependent and the visitor and is suspended for an honoured guest whom the host himself seats, serves and beds.","gn":["the rule against which its hospitality reads as a deliberate honour and not a mere list of comforts","a limit to the rule: the prohibitions assume a lower visitor, and the host's own gift lifts them"]},{"a":"cc:1308","b":"k:1085","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's man asks whether her look is Death, an eye or a doe and answers that it has all three; Civakan asks whether it is her bow, her eyes, her speech or her look and says he cannot tell.","grade":"sound","better":"develops","te":"Both men ask the same 'is it this or that' of a woman who wounds them, but the Kural settles the question in her look, while Civakan gives it up and lays the cause on his own desire, so the epic turns the couplet's question about her into an admission about himself.","gn":["the convention of the bewildered list of alternatives, which shows the force of its refusal to answer","an alternative ending to the convention, in which the cause moves from the woman's look to the man's longing"]},{"a":"cc:1314","b":"k:1228","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's woman says the herdsman's flute is the burning evening's messenger and a killing weapon; Padumai finds the bull's bell, the moon, the anril and the evening burning like smokeless fire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The evening's pastoral sounds that burn the parted wife of the Kural are given to a girl who has not yet been joined or parted, showing that the epic transfers the whole thurai of evening grief to the first night after first sight.","gn":["the source convention of its list, which shows the bull's bell and the evening as the settled tokens of separation","a case where the separation grief is felt before any union, widening the occasion the couplet presupposes"]},{"a":"cc:1315","b":"k:1171","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's woman asks why her eyes weep, since they showed him to her and so gave the unending sickness; the epic says Padumai lay sick with the love her own eyes had given her.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet's blame of the eyes is spoken long after, by a woman left alone; the epic places the same diagnosis on the very first night, so the eyes are blamed before the love has any history.","gn":["the convention that the eyes are the cause and so the culprit, which gives தன் கண் தரு its edge","an instance of the eyes' guilt at the start of love, where the couplet knows it only in separation"]},{"a":"cc:1327","b":"cc:1326","type":"develops","note":"The question who she is, goddess or woman, is answered in the next verse by the fading garland, blinking eyes and feet on the ground.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together the two verses make the doubt and its clearing a single step of the kalavu sequence, in which the man must establish she is human before he may approach her."},{"a":"cc:1326","b":"k:1081","type":"develops","note":"Both are the man's first sight of the woman and his doubt whether she is a goddess.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet leaves the heart reeling in doubt, while the epic expands the doubt into a list of named goddesses and settles it in the next verse, so the Kural's moment becomes the first stage of a scene that ends in union.","gn":["the epic verse gains the Kural's frame for reading its list of goddesses as the lover's conventional doubt (ஐயம்) and not as worship","the couplet gains a sequel the Kural never gives: the doubt is resolved by signs of humanity and acted on"]},{"a":"cc:1332","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"Both say the woman who causes the lover's sickness is its only cure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the paradox as a lover's hope, but the epic puts it after the union and makes the remedy bodily, the sandal on her breasts, so the cure is something already taken.","gn":["the epic verse gains the couplet's paradox of cause and cure, which shows its compliment is a set argument of love and not a stray conceit","the couplet gains a time and a body: the cure it promises is shown applied, after the meeting rather than before it"]},{"a":"cc:1331","b":"ku:2","type":"develops","note":"In both the man asks bees to judge whether any flower matches the woman, and both name her teeth and hair.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the Kuruntokai's appeal to the bee as the expert in flowers but asks about her teeth against the mullai, and its bees already live in her hair, so the witness the Sangam poem sends out to the flowers has been drawn inside the woman's own beauty.","gn":["the epic verse gains a known model, which shows its address to the bees as a learned borrowing of the oldest Tamil praise poem","the Sangam poem gains a later reading that moves its test from her hair's fragrance to her teeth and puts the bee in her hair"]},{"a":"cc:1334","b":"k:1117","type":"tension","note":"Both compare a woman and her lover's view of her with the moon and its mark.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural uses the moon's mark as the flaw her face does not have, while Civakan uses it as the figure of a bond that cannot be parted, so one image carries opposite values according to whether the lover is praising her face or swearing to their union.","gn":["the epic verse gains the contrast that shows its moon-and-mark pledge reverses the usual praise, making the mark the point and not the blemish","the couplet gains a counter-use that shows its image of the spot as a flaw is a choice, not the only reading of the moon"]},{"a":"cc:1339","b":"cc:1329","type":"develops","note":"The father decides to give Padumai to Civakan just after the two have united in secret in the grove.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The father's reasons, royal marks and a saved life, lead him to the marriage the secret union already requires, so the kalavu of the grove turns into wedlock without the lovers having to ask or confess."},{"a":"cc:1339","b":"cc:440","type":"qualifies","note":"In both a father gives his daughter to the man who has done a deed for him: Nandagon to whoever recovers the herd, Padumai's father to the man who saved her life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Nandagon offers his daughter in advance as a public prize for any warrior, but Padumai's father reasons afterwards from the hero's bodily marks and makes the gift wait on his consent, so the epic's brides-for-deeds are not all of one kind."},{"a":"cc:1340","b":"cc:1296","type":"develops","note":"In 1296 the onlookers say that he alone restored her remaining life and that the king will give the girl to the spearman; here the king, on his minister's counsel, decides exactly that.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The marriage is first the crowd's verdict on the cure and only then the king's decision, so royal policy here ratifies a public judgment that the healer has earned the bride."},{"a":"cc:1342","b":"cc:1294","type":"develops","note":"In 1294 Civakan strokes her whole body as a cure, thinking her life will stay if she is seen, spoken to and touched; here the narrator cites that touch, with the maxim that a woman belongs to the one who touched her, as the ground of the marriage.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the physician's touch made to save a life becomes after the fact a claim on the woman, showing how the epic fuses healing and betrothal into one act of fate."},{"a":"cc:1359","b":"cc:19","type":"develops","note":"The patikam says he delighted in her for two months and then took the road again; this verse gives the two months, the night departure, and his destiny as the chariot that carries him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The patikam reports only the length of the stay, while the narrative adds that the leaving is driven both by the hero's own rising heart and by fortune, which the summary line never says."},{"a":"cc:1359","b":"cc:1340","type":"develops","note":"In 1340 the king reads the match as having come by destiny; in 1359 the hero's destiny (பொறி) is the chariot that takes him away from the same bride after two months.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same fate that the king cites to make the marriage is what carries the husband off, so in this epic destiny grants a union without promising that it will last."},{"a":"cc:1351","b":"k:1101","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says all five senses find their objects in one bright-bangled woman; the epic names the same five, smell, taste, music, the sight of dance and her touch, as the round of the hero's pleasure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet gathers all five senses into one woman, the epic spreads them over perfumes, food, string music and dance with only touch left to her, showing a prince's pleasure as a court of arts rather than the single person the couplet praises.","gn":["the verse's list of senses is recognised as the Kural's fivefold scheme of the lover's delight, not a random catalogue","the couplet's claim of concentration is set against a scene where the senses are served by many things, which shows how strong its claim that one woman suffices really is"]},{"a":"cc:1360","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says the bond between the lover and the girl is that between body and life; the epic has the husband leave his sleeping wife as a good life leaves the body.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet's simile is a promise that the two cannot part; the epic takes the same simile literally, so the husband's going is a death for the wife, which is the cost the couplet's image leaves unstated.","gn":["the departure simile is read as the turning of a known love image, the lovers as body and life, into a picture of loss","the couplet's image is shown to cut both ways: if he is her life, his leaving makes her a lifeless body"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:1362","b":"k:338","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet likens the bond of body and life to a bird flying off and leaving its nest alone; the epic has the abandoned wife as a body searching for its departed life and as a bird searching the nest for its mate.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In the couplet the forsaken body is an empty shell that feels nothing, which is its lesson on impermanence; the epic gives the left-behind body and nest a longing of their own, turning the image of death's indifference into one of grief.","gn":["the double simile is seen to rework the Kural's figure of body, life, bird and nest, so its pathos rests on a known image of death","the couplet's bare picture of the life leaving gains a view from the side of what is left, which the couplet passes over"]},{"a":"cc:1350","b":"ku:49","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Kuruntokai poem the woman prays that even when this life turns into the next he may be her husband; the epic's narrator calls the couple's love one joined through seven births.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"What the akam speaker can only pray for, the epic narrator states as fact, yet ten verses later the hero leaves this bride in the night, so the seven-birth bond in the epic marks the depth of love without securing its continuance.","gn":["the phrase எழுமையும் இயைந்து is seen as the old wish for a husband across births, which the plot then strains","the poem's prayer is set beside a story where love across births is granted and still does not stop separation, which shows why the woman must pray rather than assume"]},{"a":"cc:1383","b":"cc:19","type":"develops","note":"The patikam says only that Civakan delighted in her for two months and then cut through the mountains and swiftly took the road; this run gives the night of that leaving from the side of the wife left behind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam's swift departure is told from the hero's road, and only here does the epic count its cost, a wife who blames birds, lamps and her own eyes and falls to the floor."},{"a":"cc:1385","b":"cc:1017","type":"tension","note":"Pleading with Gunamalai, Civakan asked whether a swan that delighted in the flowering Ganges would go near the white sand of a seep-well; here Padumai is a hen-swan left by a dry stony pool after her mate has quit the Ganges lotus bed.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The hero's own image of a constancy that never leaves the Ganges is turned against him: the swan does leave, and the one stranded at the poor water is the wife he swore by it to another."},{"a":"cc:1364","b":"cc:1000","type":"tension","note":"Gunamalai's parrot answers her plea and flies as her envoy to Civakan; Padumai's myna, swan, peacock and lamp neither stopped him nor will tell where he went.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Set beside the parrot that carried Gunamalai's love, Padumai's mute household creatures show the same convention emptied: the love-messenger exists only when the lover is still within reach.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:1373","b":"k:1179","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's woman says her eyes do not sleep whether he is away or near; Padumai told her eyes not to sleep so she could keep sight of him, and they slept, and he went.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's sleepless eyes are the lover's watch against loss; the epic shows the watch failing once, so the eyes are blamed not for their suffering but for the one night they rested.","gn":["a reason, from the Kural's chapter on the eyes, why the night's sleep is a fault and not a mercy","a case where the eyes' vigilance actually lapses and the feared parting happens, which the couplet only fears"]},{"a":"cc:1370","b":"k:1157","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Kural the bangles slip from the wasting wrist and so announce the parting; Padumai pulls her bracelets off herself, blaming them for having come between her and his embrace.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic turns the couplet's involuntary sign into a deliberate act: the bangle no longer reports the grief but is punished as one of its causes.","gn":["the convention of the bangle as the body's witness to separation, which makes her gesture legible","a scene where the woman acts on the sign herself, so the bangle becomes a culprit rather than a gossip"]},{"a":"cc:1381","b":"k:1154","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1154 asks whether those who trusted the assuring words of one who was gracious can be at fault if he leaves; Padumai calls Civakan one who walks by grace and speaks dharma, and asks why he shows her no grace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet sets the fault on the gap between the lover's word and deed; the epic sharpens it by making the deserter a man praised for grace and dharma, so his own virtue becomes the charge against him.","gn":["a moral frame in which her reproach is not mere grief but an accusation of broken assurance","a deserter whose public virtue is named, so the gap between word and deed is measured against a whole character, not one promise"]},{"a":"cc:1378","b":"k:1307","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's man finds one pain inside the sulk, the worry that union may be delayed; Padumai says that if a husband leaves even in the sweet hour of the sulk, the wife's life leaves her.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The game of sulking assumes the lover stays to be won back; the epic names the case the couplet only worries about, where the partner leaves during the sulk and the delay becomes loss.","gn":["the Kural's view of the sulk as a sweet risk, which explains why she names the sulk-time as the worst moment to be left","the risk carried to its end from the woman's side, where the delay the man fears becomes desertion"]},{"a":"cc:1389","b":"mm:30:109-124","type":"qualifies","note":"The Manimekalai's teacher sets out the forward chain from contact to craving, clinging, becoming and a birth whose fruit is aging, death and grief; the friend's tree runs from the seed of ignorance through deeds and desire to the fruit of death.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The two epics share the shape of a causal chain from ignorance to death, but the Cintamani makes it a single growing tree with love and elation among its branches and uses it to dry a lover's tears, where the Manimekalai gives it as a doctrine of release with named links."},{"a":"cc:1389","b":"k:361","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural calls craving the seed that yields unceasing birth; the friend's tree has ignorance as its seed and desire as its root.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Put beside the couplet, the verse shows a different order of causes: craving is not the first cause but what anchors the tree once ignorance has sown it, so cutting desire alone would not uproot it.","gn":["a known single-cause maxim against which its own order of seed and root stands out","an account of what lies behind craving, which the couplet leaves unasked"]},{"a":"cc:1392","b":"k:630","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural promises distinction to one who takes what hurts as pleasure; the friend says that bearing sorrow as joy is harder than weeping or dying.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's discipline for a man in adversity becomes, in a deserted bride's chamber, the hardest thing in the world, set above death itself in difficulty, so the epic measures the cost the couplet only rewards.","gn":["a recognized rule of endurance, which shows its consolation to be ethical counsel and not just comfort","a test case in grief over a loved one, where the rule is admitted to be harder than dying"]},{"a":"cc:1394","b":"n:23","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 23 has wedding drums turn into funeral drums in the same hall the same day; the friend sees parting within union, age within youth and sickness within health.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The quatrain shows one reversal as an event; the verse turns it into a rule that every good state already holds its opposite, and uses that rule to forbid even a blink of grief.","gn":["a concrete scene of union turned into loss that grounds its abstract list","a generalization of its single reversal into four, with parting from kin named among them"]},{"a":"cc:1393","b":"cc:1360","type":"same_image","note":"In 1360 Civakan slips from Padumai's sleeping arms in the dark like a good life leaving the body; here the friend teaches that parting and death both belong by nature to human birth.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The narrator's simile of departure as death is taken up as doctrine: the parting that was likened to the soul's leaving is now said to be of the same kind as death, and as little to be grieved."},{"a":"cc:1396","b":"cc:1271","type":"develops","note":"The friend says she once foretold a cruel trouble from a fanged snake; 1271 tells how a snake living in the grove bit the princess.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Her old warning, proved by the snakebite that brought Civakan to Padumai, is what gives her new omen of his return its weight."},{"a":"cc:1411","b":"cc:1395","type":"develops","note":"The friend reads the owl's paired call as a sign that he has not left her; Civakan, unrecognized, sends word that her beloved will be seen within ten months.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The omen is confirmed out of the absent man's own mouth, and given a term, so the chapter closes the friend's consolation with the hero's pledge."},{"a":"cc:1400","b":"ku:174","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 174 says that if he leaves her for wealth across the waste, then wealth alone is wealth and grace exists nowhere; Civakan's saying makes leaving for deeds and wealth a man's duty and staying a woman's.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam woman's voice hears the man's going for wealth as the end of grace, while the epic puts the same convention in the hero's mouth as a duty, and uses it, untruly, to cover a departure that is neither for work nor for wealth.","gn":["the akam convention it quotes, with the woman's protest that the hero's maxim leaves out","the man's own statement of the rule as duty, which the woman's lament answers"]},{"a":"cc:1418","b":"mm:3:69-81","type":"qualifies","note":"Civakan's hymn praises the Jina for teaching that a deed's fruit reaches its doer and no other; the Manimekalai names fierce vow-keepers who hold that a deed reaches its doer even without intention, and tells of a seat built to answer them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Seen from the Buddhist epic, the doctrine the Cintamani's hymn takes for granted has a disputed edge, whether a deed done without intention still binds, which the Jain hymn never raises."},{"a":"cc:1419","b":"nk:37","type":"develops","note":"The hymn says the soul is not from anything other than itself; the Neelakesi's sage asks whether a child is the body, made of the parents' blood, or the soul, which came to a human birth by its own former deed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Neelakesi spells out what the Cintamani's brief formula denies: the soul is not produced by the parents or the elements but brings itself into each birth by its own deeds."},{"a":"cc:1420","b":"k:350","type":"tension","note":"The Kural bids one cling to the one free of clinging in order to let clinging go; the Cintamani's hymn says love wears the karmas away and yet, being love, cannot end them.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The couplet makes attachment to the unattached the way out of attachment, while the Jain hymn admits that very attachment and counts it still a bond, so the two disagree on whether devotion can itself be the final cause of freedom, on the reading of காதல் as love of the Jina.","gn":["a sharp foil showing that its paradox denies what the couplet affirms, devotion as the last step to freedom","an objection the couplet does not face, that clinging to the unattached remains a clinging"]},{"a":"cc:1427","b":"k:280","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says neither shaving nor long hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns; Civakan says matted hair, robes and the skull bowl are seeds of a mean birth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet sets the marks aside as unnecessary, the epic counts them as harmful, sowing rebirth, so the pair shows two degrees of the same critique of outward signs.","gn":["a neutral maxim that makes plain how much further its own judgment goes","a case where the marks are not merely superfluous but are taken for the path itself and so mislead"]},{"a":"cc:1430","b":"s:372","type":"qualifies","note":"Pattinathar calls vows, Vedas, Agamas, fire rites, offerings, mantras and pujas all pretence; Civakan mocks standing in the five fires and eating grass, but goes on to urge austerity in the Jina's form (1435).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both reject the Vedic ascetic's fire and diet, but the Siddhar rejects observance as such, while the Jain hero rejects one school's austerities only to set up his own, so the pairing separates a critique of ritual from a rivalry of disciplines.","gn":["a contrast that shows its polemic is not anti-ritual in general, since it keeps tapas","a reasoned reductio, the beast that already lives the practice, where the Siddhar only lists and dismisses"]},{"a":"cc:1431","b":"t:509","type":"qualifies","note":"Civakan says that if plunging in water won release the bear would gain it; Tirumular says the holy waters are within the heart, and those who do not bathe there so their deeds perish wander the land.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both deny that outer bathing destroys deeds, but Tirumular keeps the bath and moves it inward, while the Jain hero drops it and replaces it with knowledge, vision and conduct, so the two show different ways out of the same criticism.","gn":["an inward alternative its own mockery does not offer, which shows its answer is substitution rather than interiorization","a blunt test, the bear in the pool, for the outward bathing its verse dismisses"]},{"a":"cc:1433","b":"s:311","type":"tension","note":"Pattinathar asks what forest life, eating wind, rags and the bowl avail without inner love, and says the Lord's true lovers reach release even living at home beside their women; Civakan says sin born of desire for women cannot be removed by union with women.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The two agree that going to the forest is nothing in itself, but they part on the household: the Saiva poet lets love free one beside one's wife, while the Jain hero makes leaving the wife a condition, so the shared attack on outward renunciation hides opposite views of the married life.","gn":["a rival conclusion from the same premise, which shows that its demand to leave women is a doctrine of its own and not implied by its critique","the strongest objection to its claim, that sin born of desire cannot be cleansed while desire continues"]},{"a":"cc:1434","b":"nk:18","type":"qualifies","note":"The Neelakesi praises those who leave home for forest and hill and strive with knowledge to wear away their deeds; Civakan says leaving the house of women for the forest does not wear away old sin carried within.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Read together, the two Jain epics place the cause of release not in the forest but in the knowledge the Neelakesi names (ஞானமொடு), which the Cintamani's ascetics lack until Civakan supplies it in 1436."},{"a":"cc:1435","b":"n:19","type":"qualifies","note":"Civakan says the body will drop at once like a ripe fruit from its stalk; the Naladiyar warns that a harsh wind can drop the green fruit while the ripe stays on the tree.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic's ripe fruit makes death a ripening that comes in order, but the quatrain's fallen green fruit warns that it need not wait for age, and the two urgings to act now rest on different pictures of when the body falls.","gn":["a caution that its own image of ripeness understates the danger to the young","a renouncer's use of the falling fruit that turns the urge to do good into a call to austerity"]},{"a":"cc:1436","b":"k:357","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says one whose mind ponders and knows what is with certainty need not think again of birth; Civakan says knowledge, right vision and right conduct must all three be full before both kinds of deed pass.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet lets true knowing alone end rebirth, the Jain formula makes knowing only one of three and insists on vision and conduct of non-harm as well, so the pair shows the Kural's chapter on true knowledge as a narrower account of release.","gn":["a single-cause claim against which its insistence on all three, each made full, stands out","a fuller account of what knowing must be joined with before birth ends, which the couplet leaves unsaid"]},{"a":"cc:1437","b":"cc:1432","type":"develops","note":"In 1432 Civakan mocks the ascetics who leave soft beds only to lie with women in leaf huts; in 1437 they put away their households and go to the Jina.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The jibe was not only abuse but a charge the converts accept, so the poem counts their earlier forest life as no renunciation and their conversion as the first true leaving of home."},{"a":"cc:1437","b":"cc:1436","type":"develops","note":"1436 names knowledge, right faith and conduct whose fulness ends both kinds of deed; 1437 has the hearers rise and take the path of release.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The doctrine of the three jewels is tested at once by its hearers, and the epic settles the debate by conversion rather than by any further argument from the ascetics."},{"a":"cc:1438","b":"cc:1460","type":"develops","note":"1438 calls Civakan the man whose body bears the true marks; from 1460 the marks are listed one by one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"An epithet that seems mere praise on the road becomes the evidence by which a stranger is recognised as a destined husband, so the body itself carries the plot."},{"a":"cc:1451","b":"cc:1472","type":"develops","note":"The astrologer foretells that Kemasari's husband is the man at whose sight she will feel sudden shame; in 1472 at first sight her restraint and shame give way and she melts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prophecy names shame as the sign, but its fulfilment is shame overwhelmed by desire, so the virtue that should guard a maiden is made the very place where the destined bond shows itself."},{"a":"cc:1451","b":"cc:539","type":"same_image","note":"Both brides' fathers wait on an astrologer's word about the destined husband: Gandharvadattai's says he lives in Rajamapuram, Kemasari's that she will know him by her own shame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set side by side, the two horoscopes show the epic varying one device: the Vidyadhara princess's husband is found by place and contest, the merchant's daughter's by a sign in her own body that no contest can stage."},{"a":"cc:1453","b":"k:86","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet praises the host who tends the departing guest and watches for the guest to come; Subhadran feeds a thousand and eight guests a day for twelve years watching for one guest who will be his son-in-law.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic takes the couplet's watching for the coming guest literally and turns it to a private end, so hospitality becomes a search, rewarded with a husband in this life rather than with welcome among the gods.","gn":["the father's twelve-year feast is given the standing of a recognised householder's virtue rather than a mere device of the plot","the couplet's 'watching for the guest to come' is shown to admit a host whose care has a hidden aim of his own, a case the couplet does not weigh"]},{"a":"cc:1460","b":"cc:1465","type":"develops","note":"1460 lists Civakan's bodily marks without saying who sees them; in 1465 the father, having examined them, decides he is his daughter's husband and takes him in his chariot.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The catalogue that reads as the poet's praise is revealed as a merchant's inspection, so the marks of a great man work as a test of a bridegroom alongside the astrologer's sign."},{"a":"cc:1465","b":"cc:1453","type":"develops","note":"1453 tells how the merchant fed 1008 people daily for twelve years for the sake of the திருமகன் who would be his daughter's husband; in 1465 he reads the stranger's body and names him திருமகள் கணவன்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The twelve years of feeding were a long wait for a sign, and the sign comes as a body to be read, so the epic turns the merchant's charity into the means by which the fated groom is found."},{"a":"cc:1465","b":"cc:1339","type":"develops","note":"In 1339 Padumai's royal father gives her because Civakan bears marks fit to rule the earth (பொறியினன்); in 1465 the merchant Subhadran gives his consent on the same kind of reading of the body.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Two fathers of different rank, a king and a merchant, both choose the stranger by reading his body rather than asking his birth, which shows that in the epic the hero's hidden royalty is legible on his skin to anyone trained to look."},{"a":"cc:1465","b":"k:84","type":"develops","note":"Kural 84 says the fair goddess dwells in the home of one who tends good guests with a glad face; Subhadran, who has kept a great guest-feeding for years (1451, 1453), receives a guest he calls திருமகள் கணவன்.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's promise that fortune lives in the hospitable house is made literal: the guest the merchant takes in is the lord of fortune himself and becomes the son of the house.","gn":["a reason, in the ethics of hospitality, why the long feeding is rewarded by the arrival of Tirumagal's husband","a story in which the goddess's dwelling in the host's house arrives as a person, a guest who joins the family"]},{"a":"cc:1467","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 10 says those who do not reach the Lord's feet cannot cross the sea of births; 1467 says those who do not praise the Jina with flowers and tongue do not reach the world of release.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural leaves the Lord unnamed and the means as reaching his feet; the Jain hymn names him by the pindi tree and the triple umbrella and makes the condition a concrete act of worship, so the same exclusion becomes sectarian and ritual.","gn":["the general frame of the couplet, which shows that the hymn's threat belongs to a shared Tamil form of praise","an instance of how a Jain reader could fill the Kural's unnamed Lord with the Jina and its reaching with flowers and song"]},{"a":"cc:1468","b":"k:6","type":"develops","note":"Kural 6 praises the one who quenched the five gates of sense (பொறிவாயில் ஐந்தவித்தான்) and promises long life to those who keep his path; 1468 calls the Jina lord of those who slew the five senses (ஐம்பொறியும் அட்டு) and promises heaven to those who sing his feet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's sense-quencher is a teacher whose path is followed; in the hymn he is the head of a class of victors, and the reward moves from long life on earth to the world of the gods.","gn":["a named model for the Jina's epithet, which shows the hymn speaking the Kural's language of sense-conquest","evidence that the couplet's sense-quencher was read in Jain circles as the Jina and his followers"]},{"a":"cc:1468","b":"t:1775","type":"qualifies","note":"In Tirumanthiram 1775 worship of the Lord's feet brings grace by which the worshipper's own five senses are conquered; in 1468 the Jina conquered his own senses, and the worshipper's singing earns heaven, not sense-conquest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both tie the five senses to praise of holy feet, but the conquest is done by different hands: Saiva grace subdues the devotee's senses for him, while in the Jain hymn the conquest belongs to the Jina alone and remains the worshipper's own task.","gn":["a contrast that shows the hymn gives the worshipper no grace that does the work of restraint","a Jain counterpart in which the feet are praised for a conquest already achieved by their owner, not for one they bestow"]},{"a":"cc:1469","b":"k:9","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 9 calls the one to be worshipped எண்குணத்தான், the one of eight qualities, and calls a head that does not bow useless; in 1469 those who do not praise the Jina have the eight qualities taken from their souls by their deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The eight qualities that the Kural gives to the one worshipped are, in the Jain hymn, the soul's own, lost to karma by those who will not praise, so the failure to worship costs the self what the god possesses.","gn":["a source for the term, which shows the hymn reworking a couplet every Tamil hearer knew","a Jain reading in which the eight qualities are the soul's nature, so the couplet's god is what the freed soul becomes"]},{"a":"cc:1469","b":"t:444","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 444 gives the eight qualities (குணம் எண்குணம்) to matted-haired Siva who keeps the world; 1469 has them seized from souls by the robber deeds.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"On my reading of 1469, the two texts disagree about whose the eight qualities are: for Tirumular they are the attributes of a God who protects, for the Jain hymn they are the soul's own and are lost to karma, with no protector named who restores them.","gn":["a Saiva rival that sharpens the hymn's claim that the qualities belong to the soul","a Jain counter-reading in which the eight qualities are not a divine possession but a human one under siege"]},{"a":"cc:1469","b":"cc:1420","type":"develops","note":"In 1420 Civakan praises the Jina who teaches that the eight karmas (எண்வினையும்) pass through love and yet do not pass because of love; 1469 shows the eight qualities of the soul seized by those karmas.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two hymns of this ilampakam make a pair: the eight deeds of 1420 are the robbers of 1469, so the reader sees what the soul loses before the paradox of love and release in 1420 can be weighed."},{"a":"cc:1471","b":"cc:1456","type":"develops","note":"In 1456 Subhadran consoles the grieving mother that the man of merit will meet her daughter's eyes tomorrow (கண்ணுறு நாளை); in 1471 their gazes meet as Civakan enters the city.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The meeting of the eyes is the fulfilment of a precise prediction, so the first-sight love of the next verses is framed as a fate announced in advance rather than a chance encounter."},{"a":"cc:1472","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1251 says the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint bolted with shame; in 1472 Kemasari's inner restraint and modesty (உண்ணிறை நாணும்) break together at the first sight of Civakan.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural's woman says this in separation as a confession of defeat; the epic makes the same breaking happen at first sight and treats it as the sign that the destined man has come, turning a lament into an omen.","gn":["the frame of நிறை as a door with நாண் as its bolt, which explains why the two words break together","a scene that moves the breaking of restraint from the end of a love to its first instant"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:1473","b":"k:1082","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1082 says her returned look is like a striking goddess (தாக்கணங்கு) bringing an army; in 1473 Kemasari's look becomes an afflicting goddess (நோக்கணங்கு) and Civakan wonders whether she is a தாக்கணங்கு or a mortal.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural settles that the woman is human and keeps the goddess as a figure for her look; the epic stops at the doubt and stacks அணங்கு on lute, look and woman, so the hero's own art is made one of the goddesses that strike him.","gn":["the term and its sense of a look that strikes back, which the verse expands in its triple pun","a narrative moment in which the man's doubt is left unresolved and he sinks under it"]},{"a":"cc:1473","b":"cc:1327","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1327 Civakan settles that Padumai is no goddess by three marks (her garland fades, her eyes blink, her feet touch the ground); in 1473 he asks whether Kemasari is a goddess or a daughter and does not settle it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The hero who coolly tested a woman's humanity by signs in one ilampakam is in the next too struck to test, which shows the epic varying the same first-sight doubt from a clear-headed proof to helpless wonder."},{"a":"cc:1475","b":"cc:1451","type":"develops","note":"In 1451 the astrologer foretells that the man at whose sight Kemasari instantly feels shy (கதுமென நாணப் படுவான்) will be her husband; in 1475 she looks at Civakan and turns her eyes away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The averted glance is the very sign the astrologer named, so a lover's ordinary modesty becomes the proof of fate, and the reader knows the marriage is sealed before anyone speaks of it."},{"a":"cc:1482","b":"k:1086","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1086 says her eyes would not cause him trembling pain if her curved brows would straighten and screen them; in 1482 her brow is a strong bow bent at the middle that beats him.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In the Kural the brows could be the eyes' restraint if only they were straight; in the epic the bent brow is itself a weapon in league with eye, hair and breast, so the hope of a screen is gone.","gn":["the idea that the brows might have held the eyes back, which shows what the epic's brow-as-bow has abandoned","an extended scene of capture in which the brow joins the eyes as attacker"]},{"a":"cc:1483","b":"k:1087","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1087 likens the cloth over her firm breasts to the face-cloth over a rutting elephant's eyes; in 1483 her breasts are bound by a bodice as cruel things must be bound.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both treat the covering of the breasts as a restraint put on something dangerous; the epic adds that the binding fails, since the bound breasts still wreck her waist and him, while the Kural's cloth only warns.","gn":["a reading of the bodice as a restraint on a dangerous force, which explains why the verse calls the breasts கொடியன","a sequel to the image, in which the covering does not hold and the danger it marked comes to pass"]},{"a":"cc:1494","b":"k:1103","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's man asks whether Vishnu's world is sweeter than sleep in the beloved's arms; the epic answers by likening the lovers to those beyond birth and death.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Set beside the Kural's open question, the Jain epic lends sexual bliss the very vocabulary of release, then ends that bliss within the chapter by the hero's leaving.","gn":["the epic's simile is shown to answer a known question about whether love can rival the gods' world","the couplet's question receives a narrative test, in which the answer 'yes' holds only for two months"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:1499","b":"k:1124","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's lover says the beloved is life when present and death when she leaves; Civakan offers to live serving her or leave his body, then is himself the one who leaves.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet assumes the woman is the one who departs and the man the one who dies of it; the epic reverses the roles, so the parting the man dreads is the parting he chooses.","gn":["Civakan's live-or-die hyperbole is shown to be a formula of the lover's art, not a vow","the couplet's life-and-death image is seen from the side of the one who goes rather than the one left"]},{"a":"cc:1499","b":"cc:1505","type":"tension","note":"Civakan asks her to command whether he lives to serve her or goes; six verses on he goes without a word to her.","grade":"forced","better":"develops","te":"The hero's offer to let her decide his going is contradicted by a departure she is not even awake to know of."},{"a":"cc:1503","b":"n:7","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar makes the sun Death's daily measure of one's days; the epic tells one day's sun as a whole life from infant to desireless old man, and the lovers go to bed.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic uses the Naladiyar's moral image of the sun as a life measured out, but withholds its moral, letting the lovers' indifference to the dying sun foreshadow the night that ends their union.","gn":["the sunset's life-stages carry the ethical charge of the day as a portion of life eaten by Death","the quatrain's abstract measure gains a concrete picture of a day as infancy, youth and old age"]},{"a":"cc:1505","b":"cc:20","type":"develops","note":"The patikam sums up the Takka episode as living with the merchant's daughter as one life and then leaving her; here the leaving is told.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The patikam's bare 'he left her' becomes a stealthy departure from a sleeping bride, so the summary's single verb hides the secrecy the story stresses."},{"a":"cc:1505","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's woman demands to be told whether her man will go; Civakan leaves while Kemasari sleeps, so that she cannot ask.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The secret night departure is the hero's way of avoiding exactly the plea the couplet voices, and the couplet names what Kemasari is denied: the chance to speak before the going.","gn":["Kemasari's silent loss is given the words she never gets to say","the couplet's plea is shown its counter-move, a leaving timed so the plea cannot be made"]},{"a":"cc:1495","b":"cc:408","type":"qualifies","note":"Here the lovers' delight is called the eating of the good of their deeds; in 408 the teacher leaves to do penance that wears away old deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Within one Jain epic, pleasure counted as the good fruit of deeds is still deeds, the very store that the renouncer's penance sets out to wear away."},{"a":"cc:1513","b":"cc:1502","type":"develops","note":"She now says she did not grasp the words about the bee that Civakan spoke before he left.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the bee speech that sounds like idle love-play in 1502 proves to be his only farewell, and her failure to decode it becomes part of her grief."},{"a":"cc:1530","b":"cc:1502","type":"develops","note":"She holds back her life on the strength of the bee words, in which the mate stays on without grief when the other goes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the covert message doing its work: the image of the mate who waits without grieving, left in place of a goodbye, becomes the rule by which she chooses to live."},{"a":"cc:1509","b":"cc:1530","type":"tension","note":"The life she says in 1509 is leaving her body is held back in 1530.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The two verses frame the lament between a life about to go and a life restrained, and show that what turns it is not his return but her fear of harm to him."},{"a":"cc:1511","b":"cc:1524","type":"same_image","note":"Love said to cling like a shadow in 1511; her actual reflection taken for a companion in 1524.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The love she said should cling like a shadow has gone, and what stays is her literal image, which she then comforts as a friend, so the simile becomes her situation."},{"a":"cc:1517","b":"k:1154","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are a deserted woman weighing the assurance of the man who left.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet asks whether those who trusted an assurance are at fault, and the epic heroine answers both ways at once, calling his word a lie and naming herself the one of evil deeds.","gn":["the epic verse gains the couplet's moral question, so her cry that he lied reads as a judgment on who answers for a broken assurance","the couplet gains the promise in its exact words and a speaker who does not acquit herself, which it never shows"]},{"a":"cc:1522","b":"k:1233","type":"develops","note":"Both make the woman's shoulders the record of union and parting.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In the couplet the shoulders betray the parting to onlookers, while the epic woman questions her shoulders as witnesses of where he went, so the body that tells the secret becomes a confidant.","gn":["the epic verse gains the convention that the shoulders bear the sign of union and parting, which explains why she turns to them","the couplet gains a reason the shoulders should know: he painted them, and they are asked rather than read"]},{"a":"cc:1524","b":"k:1168","type":"develops","note":"Both put the parted woman at night with no companion but herself.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet's woman mocks the night for having only her as companion, and the epic's woman, as alone, makes her own reflection the companion and consoles it, so the loneliness turns inward on the self.","gn":["the epic verse gains the Kural's frame of the night that has no companion, which shows her talking to her image is the extreme of that solitude","the couplet gains a scene where the absence of any companion is acted out, not stated"]},{"a":"cc:1527","b":"cc:1531","type":"same_image","note":"The companion appeals to the deity that dwells in the house; Kemasari then installs the bees as the gods of her house.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The house god that the household turns to for her survival is answered by her own choice of new household gods, the bees of his parting words, so worship shifts from the family's deity to the creature his parting words named."},{"a":"cc:1536","b":"cc:1539","type":"qualifies","note":"The mother warns that dying of separation loses both worlds; the narrator says Kemasari lives only by his words to the bee.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows the doctrinal consolation failing: what keeps the bride alive is not the threat of losing both worlds but one covert sentence of love."},{"a":"cc:1537","b":"n:27","type":"qualifies","note":"Both use the water bubble for what forms and bursts.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The impermanence figure that in the quatrain moves the wise to end their wandering is used by the epic's mother to keep a bride steady in the household until her husband returns.","gn":["the mother's simile gains the quatrain's renunciant force, which shows how strong a medicine she is giving for a lover's absence","the quatrain gains a domestic use of its argument, as comfort rather than a call to leave the world"]},{"a":"cc:1545","b":"k:63","type":"tension","note":"The couplet calls children a man's wealth; the riddle says children of the body are others' and only merit is one's own son.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"On the reading that line 4 denies ordinary children are one's own, the Jain riddle answers the Kural's householder by taking the very word மக்கள் and giving it to merit.","gn":["the riddle gains the Kural's claim as the view it overturns, so its paradox reads as a reply","the couplet's crux over whose deeds and whose wealth is sharpened by a text that assigns the only lasting gain to one's own deeds"]},{"a":"cc:1546","b":"k:263","type":"qualifies","note":"Both speak of householders who provide for renouncers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet chides those who give to renouncers in place of doing austerity themselves; the epic's householder does both, counting the gift to renouncers and his own fasts as two of four equal duties.","gn":["the epic gains the Kural's worry that giving to ascetics can stand in for austerity, which its fourfold scheme answers","the couplet gains a worked-out lay discipline in which its implied rivalry of giving and tapas is resolved"]},{"a":"cc:1547","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"Both define tavam.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural defines austerity as enduring pain and harming none, with no rite named, while the epic's lay teacher defines it as fasting on fixed moon days: the pair shows the same word as an ethic and as a calendar.","gn":["the epic's fasts gain the Kural's ethical core, the bearing of hardship, which gives them their point","the couplet gains a concrete practice by which a householder can take up its definition"]},{"a":"cc:1551","b":"cc:1234","type":"develops","note":"Civakan once bade the forest hunter give up meat, honey and toddy; now a wayfarer bids him give up lust, drink and meat.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The teacher becomes the taught, and the triad changes as it is aimed at him: honey drops out and lust comes in, the vice of a man who has just left a bride."},{"a":"cc:1552","b":"k:251","type":"develops","note":"Both turn on eating flesh to swell one's own flesh.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet asks how the flesh-eater can have compassion; the epic adds the end of the fattened body, carried only to feed vultures, so the cruelty is shown to profit no one but the birds.","gn":["the epic verse gains the couplet's moral charge that flesh-eating defeats arul, beyond its picture of waste","the couplet gains an ending for the body it fattens, which it leaves unstated"]},{"a":"cc:1550","b":"n:20","type":"develops","note":"Both bid people take provisions for the journey because Death is coming.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The quatrain's bundle carried on the shoulder stays a figure; the epic sets it against the food anyone packs for a visit to kin, and says it to a man actually on the road, so the figure is pinned to an everyday act and to the hearer's own journey.","gn":["the epic gains the quatrain's warning that Death takes even the unborn, which makes the provision urgent","the quatrain gains the comparison with the ordinary traveller's food that shows why neglecting it is folly"]},{"a":"cc:1554","b":"t:204","type":"develops","note":"Both warn men against women's smiles.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Tirumular's etti fruit says only that the smile is poison under a fair surface; the Jain sermon says what the poison does, endless rebirth in states of sorrow, and in 1555 offers heaven to those who refuse it.","gn":["the epic gains Tirumular's image of fair leaves over a poisoned fruit, which explains why a smile should be distrusted","Tirumanthiram 204 gains the karmic consequence it does not state, in the Jain terms of the gatis"]},{"a":"cc:1549","b":"k:5","type":"develops","note":"Both say deeds do not cling to those devoted to the Lord.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's unnamed Lord, to whose devotees the two deeds do not cling, is given a Jain identity in the epic, the Great One under the asoka and triple umbrella, so the couplet's claim is read as one about the Jina.","gn":["the epic's hard verse gains a clear parallel for its claim that karma will not cling, which supports the reading of line 4","the couplet gains an early reader who names its Lord, showing one tradition's reading of the Kural's opening chapter"]},{"a":"cc:1557","b":"cc:27","type":"develops","note":"The simile of 1557 sets Civakan's leaving beside men who renounce dancer and wife; 27 tells his final renunciation at the feet of the Knower.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together, the narrator's simile for a lover's journey is an early rehearsal of the end the patikam promises, so the hero already leaves his marriages in the manner of a renouncer long before he renounces."},{"a":"cc:1574","b":"cc:27","type":"develops","note":"In 1574 Civakan, tempted, asks his heart whether a world of women's pleasure ending in death is good; in 27 he hears the dharma and renounces.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that the hero's renunciation has a rehearsal mid-story, but here the reflection serves only to keep his restraint against one woman, and he goes on to marry again, so the epic separates thinking renunciation from doing it."},{"a":"cc:1577","b":"n:47","type":"qualifies","note":"Both list the body as a thing of filth with nine openings that dazzles the deluded; Naladiyar's fool is the lover, Civakan uses the inventory to refuse a woman.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic shows the Naladiyar's anatomy of the body being used by a man at the very moment of seduction, as a defence rather than a sermon, and by a hero who will still take wives, so the disgust is a tool of restraint, not a rule for life.","gn":["Civakan's catalogue is placed in a known tradition of meditating on the body's foulness to cure desire, which marks it as a deliberate discipline and not a passing mood","the quatrain's general mockery of the fool gets a speaker, a woman before him and a test, which it lacks, and the test is passed by one who is no ascetic"]},{"a":"cc:1577","b":"p:894","type":"tension","note":"Arunagirinathar calls the nine-gated body a house Brahma built; the Cintamani's hut of flesh is built by a carpenter that 1578 names as the deed (வினை).","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Holding the two together shows that the same anatomy of the body serves opposite accounts of its maker, a god in the Thiruppugazh and one's own deeds in the Jain epic, and that the disgust can be shared while the theology differs; the contrast rests on reading 1577's builder through 1578.","gn":["the epic verse's unnamed builder is shown to be a doctrinal choice, since a devotional text with the same inventory names a creator god instead","the Thiruppugazh's attribution to Brahma is shown not to follow from the inventory itself, since a text with the same list of body parts gives the work to karma"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:1576","b":"t:152","type":"develops","note":"Tirumular's dead man has his nine gates shut while the loving ones weep and leave; the Cintamani's dying lover has his eyes closed by the beloved and goes.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic turns Tirumular's unnamed mourners into the very women of the man's pleasures, so the lovers who melt in his arms are those who close his eyes, which joins the critique of desire to the meditation on death.","gn":["the epic's refrain gains the Saiva verse's frame of the body's impermanence, showing the lover's death as one case of a general account of the body's end","Tirumular's 'loving ones' (அன்புடையார்) are given a particular identity, the beloved of the bed, which makes his mourning scene a rebuke to desire as well as to attachment"]},{"a":"cc:1578","b":"k:373","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet holds that one's own understanding prevails however many subtle books one learns; the epic says the deed blinds those who have swum through learning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the limit of learning that the couplet states a cause and a scene, the deed covering one's eyes at the moment of desire, so the 'own understanding' that overrides books becomes the karma that defeats even the learned.","gn":["the epic's claim that learning gives no protection is set against a known ethical rule about learning's limit, sharpening its point that knowing is not seeing","the couplet's unstated reason why learning fails is supplied as karma at work in desire, one answer to the question its nuance leaves open"]},{"a":"cc:1583","b":"n:46","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 46 lists entrails, fat, blood, bone, sinews, skin, flesh and grease and asks which part is the garlanded woman; 1583 gives nearly the same list (குடர்கள், வழும்பு, நரம்பு, தோல்) but starts from the adorned hair and adds the three humours.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's renunciant catalogue is put in the mouth of a hero who is not renouncing, as a weapon against another man's wife, so the meditation on the foul body becomes a householder's defence against adultery rather than a step out of the world.","gn":["a recognised ascetic form, the list of tissues, that tells the reader Civakan's reply is a teaching and not merely a rebuff","a dramatic occasion: a particular woman pressing a particular man, against whom the list is used and proves effective"]},{"a":"cc:1583","b":"s:301","type":"tension","note":"Siddhar 301 also strips the body to wind, bile and phlegm and foul matter, but the poet confesses he still runs after women and begs the Lord's grace to free him; Civakan recites the catalogue and simply refuses.","grade":"forced","better":"agrees","te":"The same catalogue that the Saiva poet finds powerless without grace is, in the Jain epic, enough by itself to master desire, which shows the two traditions disagreeing about whether self-knowledge alone can free a man from lust.","gn":["a contrast that brings out how self-reliant Civakan's restraint is: no god is asked for help","a case where the catalogue works without grace, which makes the Siddhar's plea a claim about his tradition and not a fact about the body"]},{"a":"cc:1584","b":"n:41","type":"same_image","note":"Naladiyar 41 says that if skin the size of a fly's wing is torn from the body a stick is needed to drive off crows; 1584 opens the body and sets crows at the eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both poets end the lover's gaze with crows at the body, but the epic uses the image on the living woman standing before the hero, turning a general memento into a refusal spoken to her face.","gn":["a traditional image whose force a Tamil reader already knows, which lets the hero's rebuff be brief","evidence that the crow at the torn skin was a stock figure that a narrative poet could place in a scene"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:1584","b":"cc:1577","type":"same_image","note":"At 1577 Civakan reflects to himself that the body is a flesh-filled hut (ஊன்பயில் குரம்பை) of bone, sinew, blood and skin with nine gates; at 1584 he tells the woman it is a worm-filled hut (புழுப்பயில் குரம்பை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hut image moves from the hero's private thought when the woman first appears to his spoken answer to her, so the reader sees that his rebuff was prepared in silent meditation before she spoke."},{"a":"cc:1585","b":"cc:1482","type":"tension","note":"At 1585 Civakan says the kavya poets have dressed up the flesh to kindle desire; at 1482, lying in Kemasari's house, he himself laments that her hair, brows, eyes and breasts kill him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hero who now condemns the poets' painting of the body was a few scenes before its most eloquent practitioner, so the epic sets its own love poetry under judgement and leaves the reader to weigh whether desire for a wife differs from desire for another's.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:1585","b":"n:44","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 44 refuses the petty men who call eyes water-lilies, carp and spears; 1585 blames the poets of kavya by name for painting brows, eyes and nose on a bag of skin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the Naladiyar's opponents are anonymous fools, the epic names its own craft as the source of the illusion, so the critique of the simile becomes a self-critique of the epic genre.","gn":["a precedent that makes Civakan's attack on poets part of an older ascetic argument rather than a passing jibe","an identification of who the praisers of eyes are: the poets of the great poems, not only foolish lovers"]},{"a":"cc:1592","b":"cc:1580","type":"tension","note":"At 1580 the woman tells Civakan that a Vidyadhara carried her off and his wife made him drop her in the forest; at 1592 her husband says she sent him for water and was gone when he came back.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The husband's plain account exposes the woman's story as a lie told to the hero, so the reader learns that her helpless plight was itself part of the seduction."},{"a":"cc:1599","b":"cc:1581","type":"tension","note":"Pavadattan swears that after seeing him his wife never looked at another man; at 1581 she has begged Civakan to take her or let her die.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The husband's certainty about his wife's chastity is false in the reader's sight, and Civakan's silent laugh, not exposure, is how the epic lets the household survive the truth."},{"a":"cc:1595","b":"k:414","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 414 says listening to the learned is a support in one's faltering (ஒற்கத்தின் ஊற்றாந் துணை); 1595 says learning is a companion to the heart in its weakness (மெலிவிற்கோர் துணை) and asks the learned Pavadattan why he grieves.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic applies the Kural's promise to a man of many treatises and shows it failing: his learning does not console him, and he is comforted only when a mantra returns his wife.","gn":["the authority of a Kural teaching, which makes Civakan's rebuke to the grieving scholar a reasoned one","a test case in which the support learning offers is tried against the loss of a wife and found insufficient"]},{"a":"cc:1597","b":"k:57","type":"tension","note":"Kural 57 says the guard of confinement is useless and the best guard is the one women's own நிறை keeps; 1597 says women do not hold their restraint within (உண்ணிறை யுடைய வல்ல) and yield to pressing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural trusts the guard inside a woman, the epic's hero denies most women have it, and the story of this chapter is offered as his proof, so the two texts disagree on whether a woman's restraint can be relied on at all.","gn":["a precise contrary, the Kural's நிறை, that shows the hero's view as a position taken against a known teaching","a narrative challenge to its trust, in which a wife thought steadfast is not"]},{"a":"cc:1598","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 55 promises that the wife who rises worshipping her husband commands the rain; 1598 describes chaste wives who live worshipping their husband's hands (கணவற்கை தொழுது வாழ்வார்) as a few against the many of 1597.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the Kural's figure of the husband-worshipping wife but makes her a rare exception within women's general frailty, and drops the rain, so her power shrinks to ending her husband's distress.","gn":["a known ideal against which the hero's two-part teaching on women can be measured","a reading in which the husband-worshipping wife is defined by her renunciations in his absence, not by power over nature"]},{"a":"cc:1603","b":"s:910","type":"agrees","note":"Siddhar 910 calls the mountain of Ekambar the one that removes the hard deeds of those who bow (பணிவாரருவினை தீர்க்கும்); in 1603 women climb a hill so that their accumulated evil deeds may leave them (நிரைத்த தீவினை நீங்க).","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"A Jain epic and a Saiva poet share the belief that a sacred hill wears away karma, which shows a common practice of pilgrimage beneath the doctrinal quarrel over how deeds are destroyed.","gn":["evidence that the pilgrims' purpose is a widely held Tamil belief and not a doctrine peculiar to the epic's hill","a parallel from another faith that shows the hill's power over karma as shared ground rather than a Saiva claim alone"]},{"a":"cc:1603","b":"s:696","type":"tension","note":"Siddhar 696 says the poet wandered over every forest and hill until his legs ached without seeing the Lord who moved within his body; 1603 shows crowds climbing a hill to shed their sins.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Within the Saiva tradition itself the climb that the epic describes approvingly is mocked as wasted walking, so the pairing shows the Siddhars turning against a practice that Jains and Saivas otherwise held in common.","gn":["a critic's view that shows what the epic's narrator takes for granted in describing the pilgrims","a picture of the very pilgrimage the Siddhar rejects, with its anklets, lac and crowds"]},{"a":"cc:1609","b":"cc:1467","type":"qualifies","note":"Two hymns to the Jina in neighbouring episodes: the woman's song in 1467 warns that those who do not praise will not reach release; Civakan's hymn argues that praise follows from what the Lord is.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Held together, the two hymns show the epic grounding worship two ways, as a condition of release in the singer's warning and as the only fitting answer to the Lord's nature in Civakan's reasoning, so praise is both means and response."},{"a":"cc:1611","b":"t:346","type":"tension","note":"The Jain hymn calls the Jina the one who scorched Kama; Tirumular makes the destruction of Kama's work Siva's yoga at Korukkai.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The pair shows two traditions claiming the same feat for their own lord and reading it the same inward way, as desire overcome in austerity, so the rivalry is over who did it, not over what it means; the tension holds only if the epithet is read as a title one lord alone may bear.","gn":["the epic hymn gains the Saiva background against which giving the Kama-burning to the Jina is a pointed claim, not a stock epithet","Tirumular's verse gains a rival who claims the same conquest of Kama without a god's grace, by the Jina's own discipline and compassion"]},{"a":"cc:1611","b":"cc:1488","type":"tension","note":"In 1488 Civakan says one woman's beauty made her an enemy to Murugan, Kama, himself and the matted-haired god; here he praises the Jina who scorched Kama.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same hero who, newly in love, ranked Kama among those a woman's beauty conquers, now, having left that woman, praises the one who conquered Kama, so the hymn marks the swing in Civakan between desire and its mastery that the ilampakams keep repeating."},{"a":"cc:1623","b":"kl:70","type":"same_image","note":"In both a hen swan is misled by a reflection in the water.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In the Kalittokai the hen swan runs to the moon's reflection thinking it her mate and is shamed when he appears, an image of a wife who welcomes a straying husband; in the epic the hen takes her own reflection for a rival and sulks, so the same mistake that there yields shame here yields jealousy of herself.","gn":["the epic scene gains the akam convention in which a swan deceived by a reflection stands for a wife's misjudgment of her husband","the Kalittokai's image gains a version in which the false image is the bird's own, which turns misplaced longing into baseless jealousy"]},{"a":"cc:1624","b":"cc:96","type":"same_image","note":"The quarrelling pair of swans: in 96 the drake leaves the flock after a quarrel and sits alone like an ascetic; here the drake ends the quarrel and unites with his mate.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic uses the swan's quarrel for opposite ends, the drake's withdrawal as a picture of the yogi's solitude in 96 and the drake's reconciliation as the sight that draws Civakan back into longing here, so the one image holds both the renunciant's and the lover's path."},{"a":"cc:1625","b":"k:1109","type":"develops","note":"The couplet names quarrel, reconciliation and union as love's rewards; the epic stages that sequence in the swans and has the hero watch it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet lists oodal, understanding and union as what lovers gain, and the epic shows that sequence complete in birds before a man who is parted, so the reward the couplet promises becomes, for the onlooker who lacks it, the cause of pain.","gn":["the epic verse gains the couplet's order of quarrel, reconciliation and union, which explains why the swans' episode ends in union and not in the sulk","the couplet gains a watcher outside the pair, for whom the rewards of love seen in others turn into longing"]},{"a":"cc:1626","b":"k:1094","type":"develops","note":"Both are a man describing the girl's averted gaze: when he looks she looks down.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet catches the girl's averted look in the present as a sign of love, and the epic hero recalls the same look from the past while parted from her, so the gesture that once told him she loved him is what memory hands back in separation.","gn":["the epic verse gains the couplet's reading of the downcast look as a sign of love, not coldness, which is why Civakan treasures it","the couplet gains a later moment, in which the shy look survives as a memory and returns unbidden to a husband far away"]},{"a":"cc:1627","b":"cc:1530","type":"tension","note":"Civakan fears his absent wife will die in the fire of separation; Kemasari, whom he has just left, held back her own life in 1530.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The man who imagines a wife dying of separation has just left another wife who, contrary to his fear, chose to live for his sake, so the pairing shows the deserter's imagination of grief running behind what the deserted woman actually did."},{"a":"cc:1628","b":"cc:1509","type":"develops","note":"Civakan imagines his wife wasting and burning on her bed; in 1509 Kemasari, left by him, cries that her life is leaving her body.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Read together, the lament the epic gave Kemasari in her own voice returns here as the hero's guess about another wife, so the poem lets the reader measure his pity for the absent Tattai against his silence toward the wife he has just left."},{"a":"cc:1631","b":"cc:1627","type":"develops","note":"1627 fears she will die in the fire of parting; 1631 argues she cannot have, since he lives.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the hero answering his own fear with an argument rather than news, using the lovers' shared life as evidence, so that separation grief turns into reasoning."},{"a":"cc:1631","b":"cc:1510","type":"qualifies","note":"Kemasari cited his doctrine that lovers' lives become one to reproach him for leaving her; he now uses the same doctrine to reassure himself about Gandharvadattai.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Held together, the verses show one premise serving opposite ends: for the wife he has just left it proves his desertion wrong, for the absent first wife it proves she is safe, so the doctrine binds him only where it comforts."},{"a":"cc:1631","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"The couplet calls the bond with the girl that of body and life; the epic draws an inference from it during separation.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet's simile into a test a separated lover can run, that one life cannot end while the other goes on, and so shows the image used as reassurance rather than praise.","gn":["the epic verse gains the couplet's settled formula, so the hero's inference rests on a known saying about lovers","the couplet gains a situation of separation and a consequence drawn from its image, which it never states"]},{"a":"cc:1633","b":"mm:22:159-176","type":"qualifies","note":"Both say lust breeds the evil that ends in hell; Manimekalai puts it in an ascetic's sermon on the five things to shun, the Cintamani in the hero's thought about his own wife.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic uses the rule to say lust must be renounced and only ascetics manage it, while the Jain epic lets a married hero apply it mid-romance and simply turn his mind, so the same doctrine works there as a householder's brake, not a call to the robe."},{"a":"cc:1637","b":"k:292","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet allows a falsehood that yields faultless good; the narrator excuses the hero's false name in similar terms.","grade":"sound","better":"develops","te":"The epic gives the couplet's exception a concrete case, a fugitive prince hiding his name from a usurper, and shows a narrator reaching for the exception to keep his hero truthful.","gn":["the epic verse gains a recognised rule under which its awkward excuse of the hero's lie can be judged","the couplet gains a test case where the 'good' is the liar's own safety, which leaves open whether its strict condition is met"]},{"a":"cc:1648","b":"k:691","type":"develops","note":"The couplet tells courtiers to keep near a king like men warming at a fire; the epic gives the same fire conduct to pupils with their teacher.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic moves the rule from court to schoolroom, and since the teacher is a hidden king, the princes unknowingly keep the courtier's rule toward a real king.","gn":["the epic verse gains the couplet's reason for the distance: the one served can burn as well as warm","the couplet gains an application beyond kingship, to any one whose favour is sought, here a master of an art"]},{"a":"cc:1667","b":"cc:1670","type":"develops","note":"Her resolve to bind him with her garland is fulfilled when he takes it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the binding she plans in private take effect only through his hesitant consent, so the exchange of garlands becomes a mutual tie rather than her gift alone."},{"a":"cc:1674","b":"cc:1663","type":"same_image","note":"The hero's garland as her raft: first in her own lament, then in the narrator's account of failed remedies.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The raft she claims in despair in 1663 is confirmed by the narrator in 1674 after every cooling remedy fails, so the image moves from a lover's hope to the story's fact."},{"a":"cc:1670","b":"cc:215","type":"develops","note":"Love explained by a former deed: Civakan's father ran after his queen because an earlier disposition sprouted; Civakan takes the garland saying it comes by ūzhvinai.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Set beside the father's fatal infatuation, which the narrator blames on former deed, the son's acceptance under the same explanation shows the epic marking each of his loves as karma at work, but here with a pause of scruple the father never had."},{"a":"cc:1663","b":"cc:380","type":"same_image","note":"The raft (புணை) on the deep sea: in 380 the Wise One to whom the youth goes for refuge, here the hero's garland on the sea of night.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Jain epic uses one image for refuge from the sea of births and for a girl's hold on her lover's token, so the reader sees desire offering a false copy of the refuge the teacher gives."},{"a":"cc:1663","b":"k:1164","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's woman says there is a sea of love but no safe raft to cross it; Kanakamalai finds her raft in the garland Civakan made.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that the raft the couplet denies can be supplied by a token of the beloved, but only as a raft through the night, not a way out of the sea.","gn":["the epic's raft is read against a known claim that love has none, which makes the garland's comfort pointed","the couplet's abstract missing raft is given a concrete candidate, a love gift, and the epic's later failures of cooling test whether it holds"]},{"a":"cc:1663","b":"k:1167","type":"develops","note":"Both women swim love's flood alone in the night watches (யாமம்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet's woman sees no shore and is simply alone, the epic adds the watchers who laugh at her, so the solitude of the night becomes public shame by day.","gn":["Kanakamalai's 'sea of the night' is placed in the Kural's convention of the midnight flood of love","the couplet's bare 'I alone' gains a social side, the women who mock the lovesick girl"]},{"a":"cc:1665","b":"k:1134","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's lover says love's flood carries off the raft of modesty and manliness; Kanakamalai says she sold her modesty to buy a garland.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet's loss into a trade: modesty is not swept away but given in exchange, and what it bought, the garland, becomes the new raft of 1663 and 1674.","gn":["her bargain is read as the woman's version of the loss the Kural gives to a man","the couplet's image gains a sequel in which the lost raft of modesty is replaced by one of love"]},{"a":"cc:1660","b":"k:1221","type":"develops","note":"Evening as what eats the lives of the separated: the couplet's woman says it is not evening but the hour that devours; Kanakamalai sees it as a demon gaping to swallow her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic makes the couplet's figure of devouring evening into a visible monster, the moon its tooth and the red sky its mouth, showing how a Kural conceit was expanded into scenery.","gn":["her terror of evening is placed in the Kural's convention of the devouring hour","the couplet's bare 'eats life' is given a body and features drawn from the evening sky"]},{"a":"cc:1657","b":"k:935","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural condemns those who clung to dice (கவறு) and the gaming hall (கழகம); the epic makes the woman's body a board, dice and gaming hall for love.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Borrowing the Kural's own words for ruinous gambling, the epic frames love as a game one should give up for penance when it cannot be won, so the condemned dice become a figure for the pleasure renunciation replaces.","gn":["the conceit's choice of dice gains the Kural's moral weight against gaming, which prepares its turn to restraint","the Kural's dice and hall are shown entering a love conceit, a use the couplet does not foresee"]},{"a":"cc:1657","b":"k:1133","type":"tension","note":"The Kural's lover says he once had modesty and manliness and now has only the palmyra horse; the epic's maxim says restraint is the beauty of manliness when love fails.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Against the Kural's man who says love has taken his manliness, the epic defines manliness as the restraint that remains when love is denied, so the same word marks opposite outcomes of desire.","gn":["the epic's maxim is seen as a reply to the Kural's account of love overcoming manliness","the couplet's despair is set against a path the lover does not consider, restraint through penance"]},{"a":"cc:1682","b":"k:409","type":"tension","note":"The king says such excellence belongs only to a king or a twice-born; the couplet says the learned low-born outrank the unlearned high-born.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the epic using learning as a sign that betrays hidden royal birth, the reverse of the Kural's use of learning to override birth rank; the conflict holds on reading the king's line as a general rule rather than a compliment.","gn":["The king's remark is exposed as a claim about birth that the Kural explicitly denies, not just praise of a teacher.","The couplet meets a narrative where its opponent's view is spoken by a sympathetic king and even happens to be right about the man in question."]},{"a":"cc:1684","b":"k:101","type":"qualifies","note":"Both speak of a help weighed against earth and heaven: the couplet says they cannot repay it; the king says the help itself gave him earth and heaven, and finds no requital.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows a king accepting the couplet's verdict that such a debt is unpayable and answering it with a daughter, so the unpayable gift becomes a bond of marriage instead of a repayment.","gn":["The king's 'I see no requital' is placed within the Kural's doctrine of gratitude, which explains why he does not offer wealth.","The couplet's abstract measure of earth and heaven gets a case where the debtor still acts, choosing kinship over payment."]},{"a":"cc:1698","b":"k:1157","type":"develops","note":"Both have the bangles slip as the sign of the man's leaving, and both use the verb துற for his going.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Where the Kural's bangles announce that he has left, the epic's refrain makes the bangles leave as he left, so the body imitates the desertion it reports.","gn":["The epic princess's song is placed in the Kural's grammar of separation, showing the convention it is varying.","The couplet's single image is unfolded into a three-stanza refrain that links his former tenderness to the bangles' going."]},{"a":"cc:1683","b":"cc:1092","type":"same_image","note":"The crowd grieves at the binding of Civakan in Rajamapuram and again at the king's threat of fetters here.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The poet replays Civakan's arrest as comedy: the crowd that once wept for real bonds now weeps for a fetter that proves to be a bride, so the second scene answers the first."},{"a":"cc:1686","b":"cc:1451","type":"same_image","note":"Kemasari's birth reckoning names her husband by a sign; Kanakamalai's names him as a master of the bow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together they show the Cintamani's pattern of licensing each marriage by a birth horoscope, so the hero's many brides are presented as fate fulfilled rather than conquest."},{"a":"cc:1686","b":"cc:539","type":"develops","note":"Gandharvadattai's horoscope placed her destined husband in Rajamapuram; Kanakamalai's names him by his bow.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The first bride's horoscope and this one frame the series: from the first marriage the stars, not the hero's pursuit, name his wives, and here Civakan's teaching of arms itself becomes the sign."},{"a":"cc:1695","b":"cc:23","type":"develops","note":"The patikam foretold that the younger brother, like a star that lost the moon, would come at a woman's bidding; here the image returns and Gandharvadattai appears.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing identifies the patikam's unnamed woman as Gandharvadattai, whose song follows, and shows the brother's search starting exactly as the patikam framed it."},{"a":"cc:1706","b":"cc:351","type":"same_image","note":"In 351 the widowed Vijayai strips her pearls, bangles and rings; here Nandattan reasons from Gandharvadattai's unremoved ornaments that Civakan lives.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The stripping of ornaments that the epic first showed as a widow's grief becomes, read backwards, a sign a kinsman can read, so jewels on a wife are public evidence of a living husband."},{"a":"cc:1706","b":"tk:66","type":"develops","note":"Thirikadugam lists a husbandless woman's adornment among things unfit; Nandattan argues from the same norm that a wife still adorned has a living husband.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The ethical list states the rule as unfitness, while the epic turns that rule into a piece of inference, showing the norm was strong enough to serve as proof of a fact.","gn":["the Cintamani's argument gains a stated norm outside the story, so Nandattan's reasoning rests on a known rule and not on a guess","the bare rule gains a scene in which someone actually relies on it to learn whether a man is dead"]},{"a":"cc:1706","b":"pn:246","type":"qualifies","note":"Puram 246's queen rejects the widow's austerities and chooses the pyre; Nandattan names penance and the fire as two equal courses for a widow.","grade":"sound","better":"agrees","te":"Where the Sangam queen ranks the fire above the hard widow's life, the Jain epic's speaker leaves the two as alternatives, so the pair shows the choice the queen made was one of two recognised paths.","gn":["Nandattan's passing phrase gains the weight of a real choice argued for in the Sangam queen's own voice","the queen's refusal gains a later witness that the austere path she scorned stayed a full alternative to the fire"]},{"a":"cc:1705","b":"k:148","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural praises the manhood that does not look at another's wife; Nandattan, who must meet and speak to his brother's wife, keeps his eyes on her anklet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet forbids the look outright, and the scene shows how the rule is kept when a man must face and address the woman: the gaze goes to her feet, where looking becomes reverence.","gn":["Nandattan's lowered eyes gain a named virtue, so the verse reads as conduct and not only courtesy","the couplet gains the case it does not cover, a kinsman's obliged meeting, and the bodily form its restraint then takes"]},{"a":"cc:1709","b":"cc:1147","type":"same_image","note":"In 1147 Gandharvadattai's recited spells bring armed powers to free Civakan; here her spell shows Civakan far away and then sends his brother to him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Her vinjai, first a weapon of rescue, becomes a means of sight and passage over distance, so the Vidyadhara wife is the household's power to reach Civakan wherever he goes."},{"a":"cc:1723","b":"cc:23","type":"develops","note":"The patikam says the younger brother, desolate as a star that has lost the moon, came to join Civakan at a woman's bidding; here Civakan sees that Nandattan has come with the peacock-like woman's word.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam's 'by a woman's command' is shown to mean Gandharvadattai's spell and bargain, so the summary's single phrase hides a whole scene of grief, vision and magical travel."},{"a":"cc:1747","b":"cc:1725","type":"develops","note":"Gandharvadattai's promise to bring him to his lord's feet is kept when the chapter opens with him lying at Civakan's feet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read after 1747, the opening tableau of the brother at the feet is the exact fulfilment of a Vidyadhara's spoken promise, so the reunion the narrator showed first was her doing."},{"a":"cc:1745","b":"cc:1734","type":"tension","note":"Buddhisenan would die after killing the king; Nandattan resolves to die alone if he cannot find his lord.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same either-find-him-or-die resolve is a public war vow in the companions' mouths and a private one in the brother's, and it is the private one, aimed at no enemy, that brings him to the woman who can reunite them."},{"a":"cc:1744","b":"cc:1731","type":"tension","note":"Gunamalai reproaches him for being able to eat without his lord; at Kanakamalai's table he eats beside him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two meals show that for the women of the epic eating is a measure of grief: the brother's meal without Civakan reads as betrayal, his meal with him as reunion."},{"a":"cc:1748","b":"cc:1091","type":"develops","note":"Civakan's own account of being led bound names his reason, to please his father, and adds what he saw of his kin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The narrator showed him yielding to his parents' plea; his own retelling turns that yielding into filial choice and adds a sight the earlier scene did not report, kin armed and ready to die."},{"a":"cc:1740","b":"ku:251","type":"develops","note":"Both console a waiting woman by saying the flowers have been fooled by a false rain; in the epic the thunder is an elephant's trumpet and the song is overheard by warriors.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The akam friend's denial that the season has come is lifted whole into the epic and read by men as an omen that their lord lives, so a love convention becomes a sign in a war story.","gn":["the epic verse gains its key: the Kuruntokai poem shows that the song is the friend's claim that he has not yet broken his word, which is why it comforts","the Kuruntokai poem's consolation is shown working outside the love situation, as a formula strangers could overhear and apply to their own loss"]},{"a":"cc:1735","b":"k:561","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet defines a king as one who weighs a case before punishing; Devadattan asks the same of companions planning to punish a king.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The pair shows the rule of inquiry before penalty binding not only the ruler but those who would avenge themselves on him, and its first question is not guilt but whether the wrong has happened at all.","gn":["Devadattan's caution gains the couplet's standing as the definition of just punishment, not mere prudence","the couplet gains a case where the punishers are subjects and the punished a king, outside the royal court it assumes"]},{"a":"cc:1734","b":"pn:219","type":"qualifies","note":"Pisirandhaiyar and many others sit fasting with the Chola; Buddhisenan would follow Civakan in death, but only after vengeance and without knowing he is dead.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Against the Purananuru's companions who die beside a living king by his own choice, the epic's vow to follow a lord in death rests on an unverified death and a killing first, and the next verse checks it.","gn":["Buddhisenan's vow gains its model, the companion who dies with his lord, which shows what kind of loyalty he is claiming","the Purananuru poem's sitting-with gains a contrast in which the same loyalty is armed, hasty and turned outward"]},{"a":"cc:1752","b":"cc:1149","type":"develops","note":"Gandharvadattai held back her spells lest a wife's rescue shame him; Civakan says he himself preferred death to being saved by a woman.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Put together, the wife's restraint and the husband's choice turn out to be one scruple about his honour, held by each without the other's knowing, which is why the rescue had to come from the god and not from her."},{"a":"cc:1752","b":"cc:959","type":"develops","note":"The god promised to come if Civakan thought of him in trouble; in his account Civakan thought of him and he came.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The retelling shows that the god's promise worked as a pact of thought: Civakan's only act in the crisis is to think and praise, and the friendship made with the dog he once helped is what saves him."},{"a":"cc:1753","b":"cc:1237","type":"qualifies","note":"The narrator says Civakan relieved the elephants hemmed in by fire; Civakan's own account says rain fell while he stood grieving.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The narrator credits the hero with the rescue, while the hero credits a holy rain that came as he stood helpless, so his compassion is shown to act without any deed and he does not claim it."},{"a":"cc:1756","b":"cc:1405","type":"develops","note":"Padumai, left behind, called him a deceiver; Civakan himself says he left her by deceit.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic lets the hero confirm the abandoned woman's charge in his own word வஞ்சித்து, so her bitterness is not a lover's exaggeration but a judgment he accepts without excuse."},{"a":"cc:1758","b":"cc:1215","type":"develops","note":"The god foretold that the king of the middle country would give him his daughter; Civakan reports that he has.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The god's itinerary is fulfilled step by step, and the pairing shows that the marriage, which Civakan reports as a fee for teaching archery, was already fixed as his fortune before he set out."},{"a":"cc:1761","b":"cc:1215","type":"develops","note":"The god foretold the friends would come to him drooping like stars that have lost the moon; they waste at home and set out.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The friends' wasting like a burnt summer hill is the drooping the god foresaw, so their grief is part of the plan that brings them to him, not a delay in it."},{"a":"cc:1762","b":"cc:1747","type":"tension","note":"The friends suspect Gandharvadattai because her body does not waste; she has already sent his brother to him by her spell.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Read against 1747, the calm the friends take for deceit is knowledge: a wife who knows by her arts where her husband is has no cause to pale, and the convention they judge her by misleads them."},{"a":"cc:1762","b":"k:1185","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet makes the parted woman's pallor spread the moment the man goes; the friends treat its absence as proof of deceit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the Kural's sign of parting into a test applied by men to a wife, and shows the test failing, since a wife who knows her husband is safe does not pale.","gn":["the epic verse gains the rule behind the friends' suspicion, that pallor follows the lover's going at once","the couplet gains a case where its sign is read from outside as evidence and its absence is misjudged"]},{"a":"cc:1760","b":"k:61","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says nothing that can be gained equals wise children; Civakan counts children among the gainable and sets a brother beyond gain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Civakan's list puts children with wealth and wives among the things a man can acquire, and names the brother as the one bond that cannot be got at all, a value the Kural's ranking of gains has no place for.","gn":["the epic verse gains the Kural's frame of 'things that can be gained', which shows how far his praise goes in placing the brother outside it","the couplet gains a rival answer, from kinship by birth rather than by begetting, to its question of the greatest gain"]},{"a":"cc:1760","b":"k:525","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says giving and sweet speech gather kin around a man; Civakan says friends and wives can be gained but a brother cannot be made.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Kural's way of winning kin by gifts and kind words, Civakan's saying separates the circle that can be won from the brother who can only be born, so not all kin answer to the Kural's means.","gn":["the epic verse gains a contrast with the Kural's claim that kin can be drawn by giving, which sharpens what 'cannot be made' means","the couplet gains a limit: the kin it gathers are the acquired circle, not the brother of one's birth"]},{"a":"cc:1783","b":"cc:353","type":"same_image","note":"The ochre-dyed fine cloth (கல் தோய் நுண் கலிங்கம், 353; நுண்டுகில் கல்லரத்தம், 1783) that Vijayai put on 'that the strong one's son may rise'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The robe she took as a vow for her son's rise is still on her years later and is now listed beside the son as a burden, so the renunciation proves to have been made for him rather than from him."},{"a":"cc:1785","b":"cc:359","type":"develops","note":"In 359 she watches the road for news of her son; in 1785 she still sits thinking of his lot when the news walks in.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The waiting begun in 359 is answered not by her messenger but by strangers who stumble on her, so the news she longed for comes unsought and unrecognised."},{"a":"cc:1787","b":"cc:356","type":"develops","note":"In 356 the deity hid her land and lineage when commending her to the ascetics; here she herself refuses to tell her clan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The concealment first done for her by the deity has become her own habit, so that she withholds her identity even from her son's companions and the recognition must wait on their story."},{"a":"cc:1793","b":"cc:785","type":"develops","note":"785 says the companions were born on the very day Civakan was born; 1793 says that day was also the day the king perished, through their own deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The birthday the battle verse gave as a warrior's pedigree is retold to the king's widow as a day of death, and the companions take the guilt of it on themselves."},{"a":"cc:1796","b":"cc:334","type":"develops","note":"In 334 Vijayai heard her son's future greatness foretold; in 1796 his first deeds are recited to her by one who does not know she is his mother.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prophecy she took on faith returns to her as report of deeds done, and the irony that the reciter does not know his listener lets the mother hear her son's fame as a stranger would."},{"a":"cc:1784","b":"k:4","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet promises that those who reach the feet of the one without liking or aversion have no suffering anywhere; Vijayai worships at the Jina's feet and in the next verse weeps for her son.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Placed beside the couplet, the scene shows worship at the passionless one's feet asked to remove a fault while the grief of attachment to a child runs on, so the promised freedom from suffering is a goal still far off, not a present state.","gn":["the epic's silent worship gains a standard, the couplet's 'no suffering at any time', against which her tears in 1785 read as the distance still to go","the couplet gains a case of a worshipper at the right feet whose suffering persists, which tests whether 'reaching' the feet means worship or attainment"]},{"a":"cc:1784","b":"pn:246","type":"tension","note":"The Puram queen refuses to be one of the widows who go unanointed, eat cooked leaves and sleep on gravel, and chooses the pyre; Vijayai lives that very widow's life, unanointed, on greens (354), as a Jain ascetic.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The life the Sangam queen scorns as the wretched alternative to the pyre is the one the Jain epic gives its queen as a penance lived for a son, so the two show the widow's choice valued in opposite ways.","gn":["the epic's unanointed ascetic gains the older measure by which her survival was the lesser choice, sharpening why she lives on: for her son","the Puram poem gains a widow who takes the 'leaf-food' life deliberately and turns it into austerity, a reply its speaker does not imagine"]},{"a":"cc:1803","b":"cc:225","type":"develops","note":"In 225 the king reads the dream's crown as her son and leaves the falling tree unexplained; here she says that reading was the raft that kept her alive.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The half-told dream reading the epic first showed as the king's tact becomes, years later, the single hope a widow lived by, so the omitted part of the omen proves the part she could not afford to hear.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:1801","b":"cc:309","type":"same_image","note":"At the burning ground in 309 she asked the newborn whether this was how kings are born; here she remembers that same birth as the son appearing as her companion.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The birth among corpses, first lamented as a disgrace to a king's son, is remembered as rescue, and the loss she now fears is told as the loss of that companion of the cremation ground."},{"a":"cc:1813","b":"cc:260","type":"develops","note":"In 260 the councillor says the king will be killed by Death, by the woman and by his own fault; here the queen says he died through the love he fixed on her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The cause the councillor named in passing, the woman, is accepted by the woman herself, so the epic's verdict on the king's fall is confirmed by the one it blamed rather than by the narrator."},{"a":"cc:1809","b":"cc:316","type":"develops","note":"At the birth the deity foretold that the boy would crush the king who stood against him; here the friends, learning his birth, hail him as the life that will be Death to the usurper.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A prophecy spoken only to a hidden mother passes, through her grief, into the mouths of armed men, which is how the epic moves a foretold fate from secret knowledge to a plan of war."},{"a":"cc:1809","b":"pz:238","type":"develops","note":"The proverb says one's suffering is one's own old deed and the foe only the sent servant; the friends see the usurper's deed ripen and the heir become its agent of Death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two show the sent-servant idea from both ends: the proverb tells the sufferer not to rage at the agent, while the epic shows men rejoicing to become the agent of another's ripened deed.","gn":["the friends' cry gains a stated account of how a ripened deed uses a human hand, which explains why they can call Civakan Death without calling him the cause","the proverb's consoling rule gains a scene from the agent's side, where the instrument of the deed is glad and armed, which the proverb never considers"]},{"a":"cc:1815","b":"k:509","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural forbids trusting anyone unexamined but bids trust after examination; Vijayai bids the friends trust no one at all with the secret except Civakan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's two-step rule of trust assumes time to examine people, and the epic shows a mother hiding an heir from a usurper dropping the second step altogether, so that danger turns examined trust into blanket silence.","gn":["Vijayai's charge gains a rule against which its absoluteness can be measured, showing how far beyond ordinary prudence her fear has pushed her","the couplet gains a case where its own first clause, தேறற்க யாரையும், is held without the second, marking the limit of the time to examine it presumes"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:1816","b":"n:375","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar's eel-like doubleness belongs to women who sell intimacy; Vijayai, the faithful queen, puts the hearts of girdled women in general among things that do not go straight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain confines double dealing to a class of women and blames the men who embrace them, whereas the epic's chaste widow extends the suspicion to all women when a secret is at stake, so the pair shows the charge widening from a trade to a sex.","gn":["the queen's maxim gains a narrower ethical source, which shows how much she generalises the doubleness the moral books assigned to courtesans","the quatrain gains a woman speaker who uses its suspicion as practical counsel, testing whether its warning was about trade or about women"]},{"a":"cc:1818","b":"k:6","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural praises those who keep the path of the one who quenched the five senses; the epic gives Vijayai that sense-restraint (பொறிதவ நெருங்க நோற்று) in the verse that sends an army out at her word.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes quenching the senses the way to a long, settled life, while the epic shows the same restraint in a widow who still commands a war of revenge, so ascetic control of the senses is shown sitting with, not ending, worldly purpose.","gn":["Vijayai's penance gains the weight of the Kural's opening ideal, so her austerity reads as the highest discipline and not mere widowhood","the couplet's ideal gains a case where it is kept by a householder's widow with an unfinished duty, showing it need not mean withdrawal from affairs"]},{"a":"cc:1825","b":"cc:454","type":"same_image","note":"In 454 Civakan turned back the hunters' raid on the king's herd; here his friends plan a raid on a king's herd to make him ride out.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The cattle raid that first made Civakan a public hero becomes a signal his friends can send him, so the epic treats his response to niraikol as a known trait that others can plan on."},{"a":"cc:1840","b":"cc:1121","type":"tension","note":"Mastering a king's must elephant brings Civakan the usurper's threat to brand him a thief with the elephant's tusk in 1121, and a lawful king's assembly's acclaim here.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same feat earns punishment under Kattiyangaran and honour under Tadamittan, so the epic makes the court's response to Civakan's prowess a measure of the ruler, not of the deed.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:1834","b":"cc:983","type":"develops","note":"With Asanivegam Civakan only dodged between the tusks and drove the beast away; here he mounts, harnesses and pats the must elephant until it obeys gladly.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set side by side, the two elephant scenes show the hero's mastery grown from escaping a beast to commanding and calming one, the step from youthful daring to the skill a king's court rewards."},{"a":"cc:1829","b":"k:589","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural trusts the concurring word of three spies who do not know one another; the friends' three spies come back together (ஒன்றி) with one report.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the Kural's number three but drops its safeguard of mutual ignorance, showing a band of friends using the statecraft rule loosely, with trust in place of cross-checking.","gn":["the friends' three spies are seen to follow a recognised rule of intelligence rather than a chance number","the couplet's condition that spies not know one another stands out once a story shows three who arrive together"]},{"a":"cc:1827","b":"k:467","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says to think before resolving, since rethinking after resolving is a fault; Buddhisenan says that once the raid is made, turning back brings blame, so weigh it first.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Buddhisenan supplies the reason the Kural leaves unstated: in war, the deed once begun shuts off retreat through blame, which is why the thinking must come before the resolve.","gn":["Buddhisenan's caution gains the standing of a general maxim of deliberate action, not mere timidity","the couplet's bare 'lapse' gains a concrete cost, the blame of retreat before a king's army"]},{"a":"cc:1842","b":"k:599","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's huge tusked elephant fears the tiger's attack; Civakan comes at the elephant like a lion (1833), but it ends not in fear but in one mind with him.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Against the Kural's picture of the strong beast cowed by a fiercer one, the epic's elephant is won into agreement, so mastery here is shown as concord after force, not fear alone.","gn":["the epic's claim that rider and beast share one mind gains force against the common image of the elephant ruled by terror","the couplet's image of fear as the end of the contest gains a counter-case in which the stronger spirit ends in rapport"]},{"a":"cc:1831","b":"pp:6:380-393","type":"same_image","note":"Maduraikanchi lists a must elephant that kills its goad-men, breaks its post and roams, beside horses drilled in circling courses; 1831 and 1839 give the same post-breaking elephant and the same circling drill.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Sangam city poem sets the rampaging elephant and the drilled circles side by side as separate noises of the capital, while the epic joins them as two stages of one hero's mastery, riot turned into drill.","gn":["the epic's set piece is seen to draw on a Sangam catalogue of the capital's elephants and horses","the catalogue's separate items gain a story in which one man turns the first into the second"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:1861","b":"pn:72","type":"develops","note":"Neduncheliyan swears that if he fails to take the kings who scorned him, the poets led by Mangudi Marutan may cease to sing of his land; Civakan swears that if he fails to bring the raiders to bow before the king, his name may not go out in the world of song.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic keeps the Purananuru's vanjinam form, a war vow whose penalty is the loss of poets' praise, but its hero swears it for another man's king and against raiders who prove to be his friends, so the oath of fame becomes a set piece the plot quietly empties.","gn":["Civakan's vow gains its form: the vanjinam kanchi self-curse, which shows his oath stakes the same thing a Sangam king staked, fame in song","the Purananuru vow gains a later life in which the curse is softened into a love simile and sworn by a hero in exile on a host's behalf"]},{"a":"cc:1861","b":"cc:1864","type":"tension","note":"Civakan swears to bring the raiders to the city with their crowns at the king's feet; three verses later the raiders fall at his own feet as his companions and ride into the city honoured on elephants.","grade":"wrong","better":"develops","te":"The vow is kept to the letter and reversed in substance: the raiders do come to the city bowing, but as friends at the hero's feet rather than captives at the king's, so the epic turns a war oath into a reunion."},{"a":"cc:1861","b":"cc:443","type":"develops","note":"In the first cattle raid Civakan swears that if he does not protect cows, renunciants, women, children and brahmins he may become like a shameless eunuch; here he swears by his fame to bring raiders to a foreign king.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Set beside the first raid's vow, which rests on the duty to protect the helpless, the second vow rests on fame and loyalty to a host, and the friends' choice of a cattle raid to draw him out repeats the very scene that first made his name."},{"a":"cc:1858","b":"cc:432","type":"same_image","note":"Both kings, told of a cattle raid, order the army to go swiftly and turn back the herd (விரைந்து தொறுமீட்க, 432; வல்லே படுநிரை பெயர்க்க, 1858).","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The repeated order scene makes Tadamittan stand where the usurper Kattiyangaran stood, but where the usurper's army was routed by real hunters, this king's whole army rides out against a raid staged by friends, so his rage meets no enemy.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:1845","b":"k:586","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's spy goes in the guise of renunciants (துறந்தார் படிவத்தர்) and gives nothing away; Padumukan's spies (படிவ ஒற்றாளர்) have found Civakan in disguise and are rewarded on their report.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural writes spying as a king's instrument against enemies, while the epic turns the same disguised spy to a band of friends searching for their lost lord, which shows the technique is a means that serves loyalty as well as statecraft.","gn":["the epic's spies gain from the couplet the sense of படிவம், a borrowed and often ascetic guise, which the verse names but does not explain","the couplet gains a case where the spy's master is not a king and his search is for a friend, not for hidden enemies"]},{"a":"cc:1864","b":"cc:1741","type":"develops","note":"The companions, taking a passing song as a sign that Civakan lived, put off revenge and scattered to search for him; here they find him and their grief ends.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The search the companions began in place of revenge ends in this verse, so the grief that had nearly turned into killing the king is finally discharged by a bow at the hero's feet."},{"a":"cc:1871","b":"cc:1846","type":"tension","note":"The companions seized the king's herd as a ruse to bring Civakan out; the princess greets them as the thieves to be punished.","grade":"forced","better":"develops","te":"Read against the staged raid, her mock sentence shows the household already knows the theft was a summons, so the crime against the king becomes a joke at his table."},{"a":"cc:1872","b":"cc:1768","type":"develops","note":"Gandharvadattai sealed the letter with her earring and handed it to the companions; here it reaches Civakan and the seal is broken.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair closes the errand she began: the earring-seal that made the leaf hers is the thing Civakan must remove to read her, so the seal guards her words until they reach the one reader they were for."},{"a":"cc:1876","b":"k:56","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet asks a wife to guard the good name of the house; Gandharvadattai guards her husband's name among her people by sending a false message.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the wife's duty of guarding the good name pulling against truthfulness: Gandharvadattai keeps the couplet's charge by deceiving her own father's envoy, and names the couplet's duty as her reason.","gn":["her deception gains the standing of a wife's recognised duty, the guarding of the house's name, rather than mere cunning","the couplet's சொற்காத்து gains a hard case in which keeping the name requires a wife to tell something other than what happened"]},{"a":"cc:1877","b":"cc:1744","type":"develops","note":"Nandattan found Gunamalai wasting and reproaching him; Gandharvadattai's letter now reports her worse, unable to tell sun from moon.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the brother's account, the letter shows Gunamalai's grief growing in the interval and makes the co-wife, not the brother, the one who carries it to Civakan."},{"a":"cc:1879","b":"k:1269","type":"develops","note":"The couplet makes one day pass like seven for those waiting for a return; the epic's girl, told 'tomorrow', asks her parrot whether tomorrow is near or far.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that a promised day of return does not shorten the wait but makes it measurable, and the epic turns the couplet's arithmetic into a question the waiting woman cannot answer.","gn":["Gunamalai's question gains the couplet's rule that the fixed day itself is what lengthens time for the one who waits","the couplet's general waiting is given a scene, a comforter's 'tomorrow' and a parrot asked to say when it comes"]},{"a":"cc:1880","b":"k:1125","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet's lover says he would recall her if he forgot, but he knows no forgetting; Gandharvadattai says she need not recall her husband, since he never leaves her heart.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The conceit that one who never forgets has nothing to remember becomes, in the abandoned wife's letter, a reproach: the same words that pledge constancy remind the new bridegroom that she has not moved and he has.","gn":["her line gains its source in the Kural's play on remembering and forgetting, so the sulk is a known figure of love","the couplet's lover's boast is turned around into a wife's, said to a husband who is with another woman, where it carries blame as well as love"]},{"a":"cc:1882","b":"cc:1818","type":"develops","note":"The companions bowed at the feet of Vijayai in her penance grove before setting out; now they tell Civakan whom they met.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the companions' news running in the order the story requires: the mother's ascetic state, seen first by others, reaches her son only as their report, and he must ask to hear it twice."},{"a":"cc:1883","b":"cc:1807","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai fell lamenting that she had seen her son once and failed to see him again; hearing of her, Civakan weeps.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the mother's and the son's tears are the two halves of one grief, each learning of the other only through the companions who carried the story between them."},{"a":"cc:1885","b":"cc:1856","type":"develops","note":"The king had asked Civakan for his clan when the alarm of the raid broke in; now the companions answer the question for him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The interrupted question is finished by others: Civakan's birth, which he was about to tell, is declared by his friends, so the hero's lineage reaches the king as testimony rather than his own claim."},{"a":"cc:1888","b":"cc:1817","type":"develops","note":"The companions told Vijayai they would bring Civakan to her feet within a month; he now leaves Kanakamalai's city with them.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The departure answers the mother's charge: what moves Civakan to leave a new wife is the promise his friends made to his mother, not any call of his own."},{"a":"cc:1891","b":"cc:201","type":"develops","note":"In 201 Saccandan hands his kingdom to Kattiyankaran out of trust; here the son's brother warns that those who trusted have died.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The brother's rule against trusting anyone is drawn from the family's own ruin, so the epic makes prudence an inheritance learned from the father's fatal trust."},{"a":"cc:1891","b":"k:828","type":"develops","note":"The verse takes up the couplet's hands joined in worship and weeping tears that hide a weapon, adds hair and jewel caskets, and ends with those who trusted being dead.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names where a weapon hides; the epic turns it into a bodyguard's order and adds the outcome, showing the rule read as a practical protocol against poisoning.","gn":["the brother's warning gains a known authority, so it reads as received wisdom rather than a young man's fear","the couplet gains a situation and a consequence, a prince among strangers and the death of those who trusted"]},{"a":"cc:1894","b":"k:879","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says to cut a thorn tree while young; the verse says no enemy, however soft and small, should be called small, using the snake that kills an elephant.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's remedy is to strike early, while the epic's is to guard always, so the same fear of a small foe yields attack in one and vigilance in the other.","gn":["the verse's warning gains the couplet's point that a small foe grows, which explains why softness is no safety","the couplet gains an alternative to striking first, the defence of a man who cannot yet fight his enemy openly"]},{"a":"cc:1890","b":"k:617","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet puts the lotus goddess in the effort of the unslothful; the verse says she will not embrace those who slip.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural earns fortune by effort, the epic loses it by a lapse, so prosperity is shown as something kept by conduct and not only won by work.","gn":["the blessing gains the couplet's account of what Lakshmi rewards, so the warning becomes a condition","the couplet gains its negative side: fortune can be lost by one slip as well as missed by sloth"]},{"a":"cc:1907","b":"nk:367","type":"agrees","note":"Neelakesi argues in debate that trees live, as their change with the seasons shows; the Cintamani's narrator says the same from their flowering at a woman's glance.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Jain thesis that plants live appears both as a debater's proof and as a narrator's aside in a love scene, showing the doctrine was common enough to be argued from a poetic convention."},{"a":"cc:1907","b":"nk:370","type":"tension","note":"In Neelakesi 370 the Buddhist Mokkalan likens those who give trees life to men in madness; the Cintamani calls those who deny it madmen or ghouls.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same charge of madness is thrown from both sides of the dispute over plant life, so the insult marks a live sectarian quarrel and not a settled view."},{"a":"cc:1909","b":"cc:359","type":"develops","note":"In 359 Vijayai, failing in spirit, watches the road for news of her son; here her watching ends when Padumukan announces him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The long wait set up at the hermitage is closed by omens and then a messenger, so the mother's watch that began at his birth is fulfilled by the son's own coming rather than news of him."},{"a":"cc:1909","b":"a:289","type":"same_image","note":"In Akananuru 289 a wife waiting for her husband prays at each chirp of the lizard; here Vijayai reads the lizard's call, a dream and a throbbing eye as signs of her son.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The lizard omen of the akam wife waiting for her lover is given to a widowed mother in an ascetic's robe, showing that the epic carries the akam signs of reunion over to a son.","gn":["Vijayai's omens gain their conventional sense from the akam waiting, which makes her longing read as near-erotic in intensity","the akam image gains a non-erotic use, showing the omen signals any longed-for return, not only a lover's"]},{"a":"cc:1910","b":"cc:318","type":"develops","note":"In 318 Vijayai suckles the newborn, puts her husband's ring on him and leaves him; here the grown son falls at her feet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The parting at birth and the reunion are linked by the son's self-naming as her servant, so the child she gave up returns as a worshipper of the renouncer she has become."},{"a":"cc:1913","b":"cc:310","type":"tension","note":"At 310 Vijayai, alone in the burning ground by a hostile city, says she does not know how the child could be raised; at 1913 she names herself the base woman (கயத்தியேன்) who left him there.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The mother who was shown as helpless now judges the same act as her own baseness, so the reunion is weighed down by a guilt the narrator never laid on her."},{"a":"cc:1912","b":"cc:465","type":"tension","note":"At 465 the townspeople say the mother who bore Civakan must have done great penance; at 1912 the mother herself, now an ascetic, speaks of her penance as having seemed fruitless until this meeting.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The crowd's easy inference from a splendid son to a fortunate mother meets the real mother's view from inside the hermitage, where penance felt barren for years."},{"a":"cc:1915","b":"cc:473","type":"develops","note":"At 473 the foster father, more to Civakan than a mother, receives him home; at 1915 the birth mother herself declares Sunandai his mother and Kandukkatan his father.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The foster bond the story showed in action is ratified by the one person entitled to dispute it, turning the merchant's care into a parenthood the queen confers."},{"a":"cc:1918","b":"cc:24","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's 24 says only that Civakan took counsel with his mother (தாயொடு எண்ணி) before entering the city; 1918 to 1931 give that counsel as a full lesson in kingcraft.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The summary's two words turn out to hold the epic's most compact statement of royal policy, placed in the mouth of a widowed ascetic queen."},{"a":"cc:1925","b":"cc:9","type":"tension","note":"The patikam's 9 says Saccandan would not hear counsel (சொல்லாறு கேளான்) and was beaten by a plot (சூழ்ச்சியில் தோற்ற); at 1925 his son asks what need a lion has of strategy (சூழ்ச்சி).","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The son's first reply to his mother repeats his father's fatal contempt for counsel, so the lesson of the patikam must be learned again within the family."},{"a":"cc:1925","b":"cc:1928","type":"tension","note":"1925 makes Civakan a thunder-voiced lion who needs no stratagem against a jackal; 1928 has a cave lion, roaring and leaping, fall into the hunters' net.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The counsel defeats the hero with his own image: the lion stays a lion, but the reply shows that strength without judgment is just what a net is made to catch."},{"a":"cc:1925","b":"k:497","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 497 says no ally is needed but fearlessness, provided one thinks everything through and acts by the place; at 1925 Civakan claims no strategy or allies are needed because he is strong.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Civakan keeps the couplet's conclusion and drops its condition, and the mother's next verses restore exactly the weighing of place that the couplet made the price of going alone.","gn":["Civakan's boast is shown to be a half-quoted rule, which explains why the mother answers with place and time rather than with doubts about his courage","the couplet's proviso (எஞ்சாமை எண்ணி இடத்தால் செயின்) is shown to be the part a brave young man leaves out, so it reads as a warning and not a licence"]},{"a":"cc:1927","b":"k:481","type":"develops","note":"Kural 481 says the crow beats the owl by day and that kings need the right time; 1927 tells of one crow that went among the owls in their own place and killed a thousand crore.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's even match, decided by the hour, becomes in the epic one against countless decided by time, place and cunning together, so judgment is shown to multiply strength and not merely to time it.","gn":["the mother's fable gains the authority of a known royal maxim, so it reads as counsel from the books of kings rather than a nursery tale","the couplet's image gains a story in which the crow's advantage is pressed home by stratagem in the enemy's own ground, beyond what daylight alone gives"]},{"a":"cc:1928","b":"k:500","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 500 has the jackal kill the fearless elephant once its feet sink in the mire; 1928 has the roaring lion caught in the hunters' net in the forest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both reverse the strong beast by its setting, but the epic puts the reversal in a mother's answer to a son who has just called himself the lion, so the maxim is aimed at a person and not at kings in general.","gn":["the epic's lion gains the couplet's structure, in which ground and not courage decides, so the net is read as the place chosen by weaker men","the couplet gains a living addressee, a young claimant who must hear that his own strength is what the trap relies on"]},{"a":"cc:1924","b":"k:445","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 445 says a king walks with his counsellors as his eyes and must choose them with care; 1921 has Vijayai give the same rule, and at 1924 she says there are no learned counsellors here.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet assumes a court to choose counsellors from; the exiled prince has none, so the rule becomes a measure of what he lacks and throws the choice back on his own resolve.","gn":["Vijayai's lament gains force from the rule it cannot meet, showing why she asks what he himself has resolved","the couplet gains its limiting case, a ruler without a court, where the eyes it requires must first be found"]},{"a":"cc:1922","b":"k:751","type":"develops","note":"Kural 751 says nothing but wealth makes people of no account into people of account; 1922 says wealth makes the diminished into mountains, and 1924 names the speaker and her son as those diminished in lineage and treasure.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet, often read as a dry observation about upstarts, onto a fallen royal house, for whom wealth is the way back to the standing birth once gave.","gn":["the mother's maxim gains a sharper edge, since the Kural's rule about the lowly is applied to princes who have become lowly","the couplet gains a case where wealth restores rather than creates standing, which tests whether its rule is only about the low-born"]},{"a":"cc:1923","b":"k:760","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 760 says that for those who have made bright wealth the other two aims, virtue and pleasure, come together; 1923 makes gold yield army, army land, land wealth, and wealth everything.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet makes wealth the way to virtue and pleasure, the mother's chain loops wealth back into power and more wealth, and only her own first condition in 1920, wealth got by justice, ties it to அறம்.","gn":["the chain gains a measure: set against the couplet, it shows how far political counsel has narrowed wealth's ends to power","the couplet's ஒண்பொருள் gains a contrast case, a cycle of wealth for power whose brightness depends wholly on how it was got"]},{"a":"cc:1919","b":"pn:336","type":"tension","note":"1919 counts taking the daughters of hill and forest chiefs as the settled custom of kings whose rule stands; Purananuru 336 shows a king hot for a girl whose father will not give her, and the war that waits on it.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The epic's 'custom' is the king's side of a transaction that the Purananuru shows from the chief's side as a demand refused and a battle coming, so the policy's cost becomes visible.","gn":["the mother's maxim gains the cost it leaves unsaid: a chief's daughter is not always given, and the alliance may first be a war","the Purananuru's refusal gains the ruler's reasoning, marriage as policy for a firm throne, which the poem's anger does not state"]},{"a":"cc:1951","b":"cc:24","type":"develops","note":"The patikam promised that Civakan would enter the city's sky-high streets and fall into the sea of nectar of a maiden born by a boon; 1951 brings him into the street and shows her at her ball.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam's figure of him falling into her sea of nectar is set up with a reversal: in the street it is the other women who shoot their eyes at him, while the destined maiden is first seen paying him no attention at all."},{"a":"cc:1948","b":"cc:812","type":"same_image","note":"Routed kings cried that Civakan was Murugan on his peacock; the city's women now ask whether he is Murugan or Kama.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The likeness to Murugan that the defeated kings shouted in terror returns as a question of desire in the women's mouths, so one image carries both his name in war and his effect in the street."},{"a":"cc:1948","b":"k:1081","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1081's man cannot tell whether the woman is a goddess, a peahen or a woman; here the women cannot tell whether the man is Murugan or Kama.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic gives the first-sight bewilderment of the Kural's lover to a crowd of women looking at a man, and narrows the choice to two gods, so the doubt is no longer whether the beloved is mortal but which divine lover he is.","gn":["the epic verse gains a recognised convention, the lover's dazed question at first sight, which marks the women's cry as love struck at a glance rather than plain praise","the couplet gains a case with a plural female speaker, showing its doubt is a form of first sight that the tradition could turn round, not something tied to the man's voice"]},{"a":"cc:1946","b":"s:381","type":"tension","note":"The epic praises the city's women for drawing back even minds bent on release; the Siddhar reproaches his heart for sinking into lust for women after keeping the company of ascetics.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same lapse, a seeker of release pulled back by women, is the epic's yardstick of beauty and the Siddhar's confession of madness, so what one poet spends as a compliment is the very failure the other's renunciation is built to fight.","gn":["the epic's hyperbole gains the view of the seeker it uses as a measure, for whom being drawn back is ruin and not a tribute to anyone's beauty","the Siddhar's self-reproach gains the voice of the world he turns from, where the same pull is celebrated as a city's glory, so his struggle is against a culture's praise as well as his own heart"]},{"a":"cc:1972","b":"k:1085","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1085 asks whether the maiden's look is Death, an eye or a doe; Civakan asks whether her eyes are swords, mango slices, lilies, lotus, spears, arrows, carp, eyes, or Death come to kill.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Set beside the couplet's three-way doubt, the epic's ten guesses show the question turned into a catalogue that closes by choosing Death, where the couplet leaves the look holding all three at once.","gn":["the verse's list gains a model: it is the couplet's riddle of the look, widened, not a loose string of comparisons","the couplet gains a voiced scene, a hero dazed on a merchant's step, in which its question is asked of one particular woman"]},{"a":"cc:1970","b":"k:1083","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1083: the man never knew Death and now knows it in a woman's warring eyes. Civakan: those who named her called her woman; had they called her Death, men would flee the city.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The pair shows two steps of one discovery: the couplet's man learns that Death has a woman's form, and the epic adds that the name 'woman' hides it, so no one is warned in time.","gn":["the epic's joke about naming is grounded in the couplet's discovery that Death and a woman are one","the couplet's private discovery is made public: if it were known, a whole city would empty"]},{"a":"cc:1962","b":"k:1102","type":"tension","note":"Vimalai calls desire the one sickness with no medicine; Kural 1102 says the woman who causes the sickness is herself its cure.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Read together they show the two sides of one sickness: to the sufferer who cannot speak of it, it has no cure, while the couplet, spoken from the side of union, finds the cure in the beloved, which the story will supply by marriage.","gn":["Vimalai's despair is shown to be a stage, not a verdict, since the couplet names the cure her story reaches","the couplet's paradox gains a woman's voice, where the Kural gives the man's, and the cure turns out to be needed on both sides"]},{"a":"cc:1965","b":"k:1091","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1091: her eyes have two looks, one that gives the sickness and one that is its cure. Civakan, shot by her brows, asks what medicine there is for the wound and looks back at her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's two looks are both hers; the epic gives the second look to him, so the exchange of glances, not one woman's eyes, carries both the wound and the hope of a cure.","gn":["Civakan's question gains an answer the verse does not state: the cure lies in the look itself","the couplet's single-eyed paradox is staged as a mutual exchange in which both lovers fall sick at one glance"]},{"a":"cc:1963","b":"k:1180","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1180: the town reads the secret of those whose eyes are beaten drums. Vimalai: her pallor beats out her desire like a drum (பறையாய் அறையும் பசப்பு).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The drum that proclaims a hidden love sits in the eyes in the couplet and in the skin's pallor in the epic, so the pairing shows the body as a whole betraying what restraint tries to keep.","gn":["Vimalai's drum image is placed in an old line of thought about love that cannot be hidden","the couplet's eyes gain a partner: the skin too announces the secret, and at first sight, not only after long love"]},{"a":"cc:1963","b":"cc:1964","type":"tension","note":"In 1963 Vimalai holds that restraint and desire cannot coexist; in 1964 she stands steadying herself with restraint while desire seizes her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Her rule that restraint and desire exclude each other is contradicted at once by the story, which shows a woman holding restraint and burning with desire at the same moment."},{"a":"cc:1968","b":"ku:40","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 40's lover marvels that strangers' hearts mingled like rain and red earth; Civakan asks whether the woman who took his heart with one look knew him in a former birth.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Facing the same mystery of a stranger's sudden hold, the Sangam lover lets the merging stand without cause, while the Jain epic's hero reaches for a past birth, so the pair shows rebirth entering the love lyric as an explanation.","gn":["Civakan's question is seen as a choice: the old poetry had answered the same wonder without any former life","the Kuruntokai's uncaused union is set against a later frame in which love itself has a karmic past"]},{"a":"cc:1966","b":"s:301","type":"tension","note":"Pattinattar lists the body as a feast thrown to the pyre's fire (ஈமக் கனல்) and a leaf scorched in the fire of desire (காமக் கனல்); Civakan's body, called a pyre (ஈமம்), burns in desire's fever (காமக் கடுநோய்க் கனல்).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same pairing of pyre and desire's fire is a reason to renounce the body in Pattinattar and a lover's complaint in the epic, so the image does not carry its lesson with it: the hero who will one day renounce here uses it only to say how much he burns.","gn":["the epic's hyperbole is shown to borrow the renouncer's figure, which the Cintamani will later take back seriously when Civakan renounces","Pattinattar's figure is seen to be older than his use of it: in love poetry the pyre-body burns without teaching contempt for the body"]},{"a":"cc:1973","b":"cc:1977","type":"develops","note":"In 1973 Sagaradattan suddenly sells six crores of goods while Civakan sits at his step; in 1977 the merchant tells that it was written at Vimalai's birth that goods would sell when her destined husband sat at his shop.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The unexplained sale is the fulfilment of a birth-sign told only afterwards, so the narrator lets the reader see the sign come true before learning that it was one."},{"a":"cc:1961","b":"cc:1948","type":"develops","note":"In 1948 the city's women ask whether he is Murugan or the five-arrowed Kama; in 1961 Vimalai decides he is the son of the five-flower-arrowed god come down to earth.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The question the crowd of women asked and left open is answered by the one woman who will marry him, and for her the answer costs her modesty and restraint."},{"a":"cc:1977","b":"cc:1973","type":"develops","note":"The sign written at Vimalai's birth, that the unsold cargo will sell when her husband climbs onto the shop, is the sale of 1973, made while Civakan sat lovesick at the shop front (1966).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sale the merchant boasted of turns out to be a sign he had waited for since his daughter's birth, so Civakan's idle lovesick sitting is retold as the fulfilment of a prophecy he did not know."},{"a":"cc:1976","b":"cc:539","type":"same_image","note":"Both brides have their husbands fixed by astrologers: Gandharvadattai's horoscope names the city where her destined groom lives (வந்தடை பான்மை), Vimalai's names the sign he will give.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two horoscopes show the epic making each of Civakan's marriages a destiny read in advance, and fitting the sign to the father's world: a king's question about a place, a merchant's about goods that sell."},{"a":"cc:1981","b":"cc:1510","type":"same_image","note":"Here the narrator makes the couple one life in two bodies; in 1510 Kemasari, deserted, throws back at Civakan his own saying that love makes two lives one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The oneness the narrator grants Vimalai is the formula Kemasari already turned against Civakan after he left her, so the reader knows before the dawn music that this union too ends in his going."},{"a":"cc:1993","b":"cc:1504","type":"tension","note":"With Kemasari he rose while she slept and slipped out through the door unseen (1504-1505); with Vimalai he tells her he is going and promises a quick return.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Against the silent night escape from Kemasari, the open farewell to Vimalai looks kinder, yet it ends the same way in a departure, so the change lies in how he leaves, not in whether he stays."},{"a":"cc:1993","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's woman (or her friend) refuses a promise of speedy return: tell it to those who will still be alive. Civakan makes exactly that promise, quicker than an arrow, and Vimalai answers only with tears.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic stages the very speech the couplet refuses to accept, and the bride's silent tears in 1994 are the wordless form of the couplet's retort.","gn":["Civakan's light promise is shown to be the stock comfort the Kural's speaker has already judged worthless.","The couplet's retort gains a concrete farewell scene with the promise spelled out and a listener who cannot answer it."]},{"a":"cc:1981","b":"k:1122","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's lover says his bond with the girl is that of body and life; the narrator says Civakan and Vimalai became one life in two bodies because they never parted.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic makes the couplet's body-and-life bond conditional on never parting, and then has the husband part at the next dawn, which tests the image the couplet offers as a pledge.","gn":["The narrator's formula gains the force of a pledge, so his departure in 1994 reads as a breach of it.","The couplet, a lover's pledge with no sequel, is given a sequel that shows what such a pledge is worth when the lover is a wanderer."],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:1982","b":"n:46","type":"tension","note":"The narrator reports that the pleasure of Vimalai's breasts is worth the three worlds; the Naladiyar, a Jain didactic work, lists entrails, blood and bone and asks where among them the garlanded woman is.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives full voice to the valuation that the Jain didactic tradition exists to unmask, and hedges it as hearsay, so the praise stands as the worldly view that Civakan's later renunciation will have to undo.","gn":["The hyperbole is placed as the very error a Jain reader is taught to dissolve, which gives it weight in the epic's arc toward renunciation.","The quatrain's dissection gains a live instance of the praise it attacks, made within a work of the same religion."]},{"a":"cc:2001","b":"cc:908","type":"tension","note":"The companion dares Civakan to win a girl who would refuse even Kama; in 908 Suramanjari had already excepted Civakan alone from her vow of self-containment.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The dare the companions think impossible was settled in advance by the girl herself, so the reader sees the conquest as a meeting she has already allowed and the hero's oath as courage spent on an open door."},{"a":"cc:2012","b":"cc:899","type":"develops","note":"In 899 Suramanjari vows to wait until the generous one comes defeated and worships her anklet feet; in 2012 Civakan arrives bent, panting and begging at her gate.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Her vow's terms are fulfilled to the letter in comic form, since the proud judge of the powder contest comes stooped and humbled, but in a guise he chose himself, so her victory and his stratagem are the same act."},{"a":"cc:2013","b":"cc:907","type":"tension","note":"In 907 her father buys a royal order with a priceless necklace that ends men's coming and going and even makes the carved figures female; in 2013 the guards let a man in out of pity for an old Brahmin's hunger.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"A ban bought with the three worlds' worth of jewels fails not by force but through the guards' compassion for hunger and age, showing that the exclusion was drawn against young men's bodies and could not see past a guise of decay."},{"a":"cc:2010","b":"n:13","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 13 condemns the old who lean on a staff, toothless and tottering, yet keep their eyes on desire; Civakan, young, puts on that very figure with staff and white hair for love of a woman's beauty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's figure of shame, the old man still ruled by desire, becomes in the epic a costume a young lover wears to reach a woman, so the image meant to turn men from desire is used as an instrument of it.","gn":["the comic disguise gains a moral shadow: the narrator's 'became even a ghost' reads as the quatrain's verdict on desire that will not let go","the quatrain's staff and failing body are shown to be imitable signs, so its warning rests on a look that a lover can counterfeit and that guards pity rather than scorn"]},{"a":"cc:2011","b":"k:586","type":"develops","note":"Kural 586 sends the spy into closed places in the guise of renunciants, undiscovered whatever is done to him; Civakan enters a house closed to men in the guise of an old Brahmin and holds his part under the women's threats (2014-2015).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The statecraft couplet's method, borrowing a status no one guards against, is shown working in love, and its clause 'whatever is done to him' is tested when armed women surround the disguised man and he keeps silent.","gn":["the trickster scene gains a named technique, the spy's guise of the harmless, which explains why the Brahmin disguise works where force could not","the couplet gains a demonstration of the guise's real lever, the protector's scruple, since the women spare him because harming a Brahmin is sin (2016)"]},{"a":"cc:2016","b":"cc:2008","type":"develops","note":"In 2008 Civakan judges the gate easy to pass if he goes as a Brahmin; in 2016 the women hold back their weapons because touching an old Brahmin is sin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The plan's premise is confirmed by the guards' own ethics: the protection owed a Brahmin is what admits the intruder, so a religious scruple becomes the hero's key."},{"a":"cc:2020","b":"p:263","type":"qualifies","note":"Both name bathing at Kumari to be rid of a bodily affliction; the Thiruppugazh poet asks not to go there and be laughed at by many as a diseased wretch, and the epic's old brahmin goes there and is laughed at (பெரிது நக்கு, 2021).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows that the afflicted pilgrim to Kumari was a stock figure of ridicule, and that Civakan chooses that figure as a disguise precisely because a man so laughable is trusted inside a guarded house.","gn":["the epic's pretext is shown to be a recognisable, mocked practice rather than an invented whim, which explains why Suramanjari laughs instead of suspecting him","the shame the Thiruppugazh poet fears is seen acted out, the laugh given by the very person asked for help, and turned to the trickster's profit"]},{"a":"cc:2022","b":"n:11","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar warns that grey hair will come and youth will not return; Suramanjari scoffs that those who think a dead tree revives by rain are mad.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's truth that age is irreversible becomes, in the epic, the very certainty a schemer relies on: because everyone knows the old cannot become young, no one looks for the young man inside the old one.","gn":["Suramanjari's scoff is shown to rest on a sober didactic commonplace, so her being deceived is not folly but the misuse of a sound belief","the quatrain's warning acquires a social use it never considers: the general certainty about age is a cover that disguise can exploit"]},{"a":"cc:2025","b":"k:87","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says the fruit of tending a guest has no fixed measure but is as great as the guest's worth; Suramanjari feeds a guest she takes for a poor old brahmin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet assumes the host's offering and its fruit are reckoned by the guest's true worth; the epic shows a host who cannot see that worth, so the fruit she reaps, the end of her vow, is far beyond what she thought she was giving.","gn":["the feast gains a frame in which its unforeseen consequence is the guest's hidden worth returning on the host","the couplet's measure by the guest's worth is tested where that worth is concealed, showing that the return is fixed by what the guest is, not by what the host believes"]},{"a":"cc:2035","b":"cc:899","type":"tension","note":"In 899 Suramanjari vowed to hold out until Civakan came beaten and worshipped her anklet-feet; here he is inside her house as a begging old brahmin, singing to bring on the wedding.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Her condition is met in mockery: he does come to her low and dependent, a supplicant fed at her hands, yet the humbling is a mask he wears to win her, so the vow is kept in form and defeated in substance."},{"a":"cc:2036","b":"cc:908","type":"develops","note":"In 908 she resolved that only union with Civakan could break her nirai; here her women hear the song and praise the youth wrought into her mind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The single exception she allowed in her seclusion is the one man already inside it, so the guard she set against all men admits the very one it was kept for, unrecognised."},{"a":"cc:2037","b":"cc:729","type":"develops","note":"At Gandharvadattai's contest his song shamed Kama (அனங்கன் நாண, 729); here his song makes him seem none other than the god of spring himself.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The music that won one bride openly, before kings, is now used covertly to win another, and the comparison moves from surpassing the god of love to being taken for him, as the singer's aim shifts from victory to seduction."},{"a":"cc:2056","b":"cc:899","type":"tension","note":"In 899 she vows to keep her vow until Civakan comes defeated and worships her anklet-feet; in 2056 she sets her own head on the temple floor and joins her hands to pray for him.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The prostration she demanded of the man who shamed her is performed by her, in his hidden presence, so the vow is undone in its own terms of who bows to whom."},{"a":"cc:2057","b":"cc:908","type":"develops","note":"908 left one exception in her refusal: union with Civakan's gold-bright chest; 2057 asks Kama for exactly that chest (சீவகன் அகலம் சேர்த்தின்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The exception hidden inside her vow of self-containment becomes the whole content of her prayer once a disguised song has worked on her, so the refusal was a waiting all along."},{"a":"cc:2043","b":"cc:885","type":"develops","note":"In 885 the maid disputes Civakan's powder verdict to his face; in 2043 she repeats the grievance to him in his disguise, and he condemns the young man who gave it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The judge of the powder contest now hears his verdict judged by the loser's household and joins in cursing himself, which is how the disguise wins their trust."},{"a":"cc:2058","b":"cc:2052","type":"develops","note":"In 2052 she resolves to worship Anangan and win a boon; in 2058 the boon is granted aloud, by a man's voice she takes for the god's.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The boon she set out to win is granted, but by Civakan's companions, so the plot turns the god's grace into a human contrivance the narrator names as deceit."},{"a":"cc:2050","b":"k:1228","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1228: the herdsman's sweet flute is evening's envoy and a killing weapon to the woman whose lover is away; 2050 is a sweet vina-song whose evening enters like a spear into a wound.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"In the Kural sweet music wounds a woman because her lover is absent, while in the epic the lover himself plays the music to a woman who has no lover yet, so the evening-song creates the sickness it laments.","gn":["the epic's song gains the Kural's figure of music as evening's weapon, which explains why a lament can work as a seduction","the couplet gains a case where the weapon has a wielder with a purpose, turning the woman's complaint into a strategy someone else can use"]},{"a":"cc:2058","b":"s:1458","type":"develops","note":"Sivavakkiyar asks whether a planted stone will speak; in 2058 a worshipper hears the shrine speak, and the narrator says the voice was a man's trick.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar's scorn assumes the stone is silent; the epic shows a voice supplied to it by a hidden man, so the credulity he mocks can be steered by whoever stands behind the image.","gn":["the comic oracle gains a doctrinal edge, since the Siddhar names the error of taking an image for a speaking god","the Siddhar's rhetorical question gains a narrative case of what follows when worshippers do believe the image speaks: they can be deceived"]},{"a":"cc:2061","b":"cc:908","type":"tension","note":"Suramanjari vowed that no one but Civakan would break her restraint; now Civakan says her beauty has carried off his.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The vow that guarded her நிறை against all men is answered by the loss of his, so the restraint the story set out to test breaks first on the man's side."},{"a":"cc:2062","b":"cc:2057","type":"same_image","note":"She knelt in Kama's shrine and begged the god for Civakan's chest; he now bows to her feet as to a goddess and begs her grace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Worship runs both ways in one shrine: the girl's prayer to Kama for the man is turned into the man's prayer to the girl, so the deity she addressed is replaced by the lovers themselves."},{"a":"cc:2067","b":"cc:2078","type":"develops","note":"Civakan swore to join her tomorrow on pain of becoming a king who lets dharma go; her father gives her to him in marriage.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The oath sworn in secret is kept through the father's public gift, so the secret union is made lawful by the kin rather than by the lovers alone."},{"a":"cc:2067","b":"k:560","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 560 says that if the guardian does not guard, the cows' yield fails and the learned forget their books; Civakan's oath calls such a guardian's fate down on himself if he fails her.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The bent sceptre that the Kural treats as a public disaster becomes, in a lover's oath, the worst private penalty a man can name, which shows how deep the guardian's duty sat as a measure of shame.","gn":["the oath gains its weight: without the couplet's picture of a land ruined by an unguarding king, the curse Civakan names would read as a stock phrase","the couplet gains a use as an oath-penalty, showing that failing to guard was held a dishonour fit to stake a promise on, not only a policy failure"]},{"a":"cc:2072","b":"cc:907","type":"tension","note":"The father bought a king's order to keep men from his daughter; now that a suitor asks for her he can neither accept nor refuse.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"His wealth could shut out a whole city's men but cannot settle a single proposal, and he falls back on the lack of past penance to explain a bind his money made."},{"a":"cc:2072","b":"k:270","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 270 says the destitute are many because few do penance; the rich merchant says he and his daughter have done none.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet ties lack to the absence of penance; the epic puts the same reasoning in a rich man's mouth, so the want that penance would have prevented is a want of good fortune in a child, not of wealth.","gn":["the merchant's cry gains a doctrine behind it, that misfortune is the fruit of austerity not done, which makes his helplessness a moral account rather than a complaint","the couplet gains a case where the one who lacks is wealthy, showing that its இலர் need not mean the poor"]},{"a":"cc:2073","b":"k:1251","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1251 makes restraint a door bolted by shame, broken by desire's axe; Suramanjari drives off the bee of shame and waves the garland of chastity.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"In the couplet restraint stands only while shame bars it; the epic has a woman put shame away in order to keep her chastity, so the two virtues the couplet binds together are parted and ranked.","gn":["the epic image gains a known structure to depart from, so that her casting off shame reads as a choice for chastity rather than a fall","the couplet gains a counter-case in which shame is given up without restraint failing, which shows its door-and-bolt bond is not the only order of the two"]},{"a":"cc:2073","b":"ku:333","type":"develops","note":"In Kuruntokai 333 the friend asks what harm there would be in revealing their secret; in the epic the girl discloses it to her friend, who carries it to the foster mother, then the mother, then the father.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam friend only proposes disclosure; the epic plays out the whole chain of telling through friend, foster mother and mother to the father, which ends in marriage the same day.","gn":["the epic scene gains its name and purpose: it is the akam move of disclosing so that kin will arrange the marriage, not a confession","the Sangam poem's single question gains the full sequence it sets going, showing who carries the secret and to whom"]},{"a":"cc:2075","b":"cc:2063","type":"develops","note":"The mother dreamed that a flower pond ran dry and reads it as the girl's maidenhood gone; the secret union of 2063 is the event.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The dream is read after the event it pictures, so it serves the mother as confirmation of what the foster mother reported rather than as a warning."},{"a":"cc:2077","b":"cc:904","type":"develops","note":"The mother once told the father that their daughter burned with anger at Civakan's verdict on her powder; now she tells him the same man has won her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The grievance that made the girl shun all men is reread as the cause of her chastity for one man, so the anger and the love are shown to have had one object.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:2079","b":"a:86","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 86 recalls a wedding of rice, sand, lamps, water poured with petals and paddy by four mothers, and the kin giving the bride; the epic father gives his daughter before the fire with a dowry he announces.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Sangam wedding, which names no fire and no dowry, the epic's gift before the flame with women, gold and villages shows the giving of a bride recast as a fire rite with property declared.","gn":["the epic verse gains a measure of what is new in it, the fire and the declared dowry, against an older Tamil wedding","the Sangam poem gains a later counterpart that shows by contrast what its rite lacks: a sacred fire and a stated transfer of wealth"]},{"a":"cc:2088","b":"cc:899","type":"develops","note":"Suramanjari vowed in 899 to keep apart until Civakan came defeated (தோற்று) and worshipped her anklet feet; in 2088 he lays his head on her anklets and her sulk ends.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Her vow is kept not by a defeat he admits but by a lover's bow inside a quarrel he says he did not cause, so the pride that began in a contest is satisfied by the rites of love.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:2088","b":"k:1327","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the loser of a lovers' quarrel is the winner, as union shows; Civakan casts himself as the small one who erred, yields, and wins her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the couplet's paradox a plot: a woman who swore to see the man defeated gets his surrender, and his surrender is exactly what wins him her.","gn":["the hero's bow at her feet reads as a known strategy of oodal rather than humiliation","the couplet's abstract loser and winner become a named pair whose defeat had been sworn as a vow"]},{"a":"cc:2088","b":"n:77","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 77 says the great bear faults hard to bear, which is why their friendship is sought; Civakan uses the same maxim to plead with a sulking wife after denying any fault.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"A maxim about the forbearance of the great in friendship turns, in a lover's mouth, into flattery that makes the angry woman the great one and so obliges her to forgive.","gn":["the hero's maxim is seen to be a borrowed rule of friendship pressed into love's service","the quatrain's rule is shown working as rhetoric: naming someone great lays on them the duty to forbear"]},{"a":"cc:2084","b":"k:1311","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1311 the woman refuses his chest because all women feast on him with their eyes; Suramanjari refuses a chest many embrace like the Ganges.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's sulking pretext is that other women merely look at him, but in a polygamous epic where the hero has already married several wives the same refusal names a fact.","gn":["Suramanjari's complaint is placed in the oodal convention, so it reads as play that leads to reunion","the couplet's mock charge is seen with a literal referent, which shows how close the convention runs to real grievance"]},{"a":"cc:2085","b":"k:1320","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1320 she is angry even at his gaze, asking whom he thinks of; here she is angry at his gaze on a painted figure, and he claims he took the picture for her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic supplies the object the couplet leaves imagined, a painted woman on the wall, and turns the lover's defence round: his gaze was faithful because he mistook the picture for her.","gn":["the epic scene inherits a precise oodal type, the jealousy of the gaze","the couplet's suspicion gets a concrete rival and an answer the man can give, which it never offers"]},{"a":"cc:2100","b":"k:1269","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1269 says one day is like seven to those waiting for the absent one's return; Civakan tells Gunamalai the day he was parted from her was like an aeon.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic moves the measure of absence from the waiting woman to the returned man and inflates it from seven days to an aeon, said only once the parting is over and as a gift of words.","gn":["Civakan's hyperbole is placed in the Kural's tradition of time stretched by longing","the couplet's longing, which commentators argue about assigning to the man, is here plainly spoken by a man to the wife he left"]},{"a":"cc:2095","b":"cc:1131","type":"develops","note":"The sage foretold that Civakan would be seized, carried off by a yaksha, and seen again after some days; in 2095 he comes home and his mother's grief ends.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prophecy promised only that he would be seen again, and the return shows its cost: the mother was comforted by the word yet still wept herself thin until he stood before her."},{"a":"cc:2099","b":"cc:1132","type":"develops","note":"In 1132 Gunamalai, near death with grief, clung to the sage's words as a raft; in 2099 Civakan says he survived by her penance.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The grief that the narrator showed Gunamalai surviving by holding to a prophecy is renamed by the hero as தவம், so her endurance becomes the cause of his rescue rather than only her consolation."},{"a":"cc:2107","b":"k:233","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 233 says nothing in the world stands undying except towering fame; 2107 takes the same unstable world and fame that stands, and uses the fame-seeker's wealth as a figure of what vanishes fast.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the price side of the Kural's maxim: the fame that alone stands is bought with wealth that goes as fast as a desert crossed in a day, so lasting renown and fleeting riches are one transaction.","gn":["the simile's ground, why a fame-seeker's wealth should be the very type of the fleeting, which the epic leaves unstated","a concrete measure of how quickly the giver's wealth leaves him, which the couplet's praise of fame never shows"]},{"a":"cc:2107","b":"k:29","type":"develops","note":"Kural 29 speaks of the anger of those who stand on the hill of virtue, held even for a moment; 2107 counts the anger of the holy among things that do not stay.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together, the couplet's crux over whether such anger lasts only a moment or is unbearable for a moment is settled one way in the epic, which takes its brevity as proverbial enough to serve as a simile of speed.","gn":["a source and authority for the figure of the holy man's short-lived anger, which the epic uses without explaining","an early reader's construal of its disputed second line, showing the brevity reading in use as common speech"]},{"a":"cc:2122","b":"cc:1948","type":"develops","note":"The women of another city asked whether Civakan was the Vel of the kadamba or the son of the five arrows; Videham's crowd hails him as both, the spear-bearer who slew the mountain and Kama.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"What was a puzzled question of desire in one city becomes a public litany of titles in the next, so the likeness to Murugan and Kama hardens from a woman's doubt into the name a whole city gives him."},{"a":"cc:2125","b":"cc:2124","type":"tension","note":"In 2124 the women claim they learned their gait from the she-elephant and plead for a slow walk; in 2125 others call the same beast no she-elephant for its rough gait.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The two verses turn one convention, woman's gait as a she-elephant's, against itself: the beast that is the model of womanly walking is scolded for failing womanhood when its speed takes the hero from their sight."},{"a":"cc:2133","b":"cc:26","type":"develops","note":"The patikam lists Civakan's winning of his maternal uncle's daughter beside the spreading of his just sceptre; 2133 shows the first look between them in the uncle's palace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the patikam pairs as a dynastic gain alongside the sceptre begins here as a private exchange of looks before any council has met, so the marriage is grounded in love before it serves the war."},{"a":"cc:2138","b":"cc:302","type":"tension","note":"Vijayai once wept alone in the burning-ground forest for her lord; in her brother's palace she is the one who ends the household's weeping.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The widow whose solitary grief the poem dwelt on now stands among her own kin and stops their lament, so her restraint marks the turn from mourning to the counsel of revenge."},{"a":"cc:2144","b":"cc:294","type":"same_image","note":"Kattiyankaran wept and joined his hands over Saccandan's body; his letter now protests that the blame of that death is false.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The usurper's tears at the pyre and his wounded innocence in the letter are one performance in two forms, which is why the reader, and Govindan, can see through the letter at once.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:2146","b":"cc:290","type":"tension","note":"The narration says the minister, a great snake, swallowed Saccandan; the letter says an elephant threw and killed him and the sceptre merely slackened.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Setting the narrator's account against the usurper's shows the epic staging propaganda: the same death is a cruel sceptre spreading darkness in one telling and a stable accident in the other."},{"a":"cc:2147","b":"cc:203","type":"tension","note":"Kattiyankaran bowed and vowed to bear the yoke of rule blamelessly for Saccandan; now he offers his life and owns Govindan as lord.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The minister's vow of loyal service, already broken once, is reused word for word in spirit as bait, so the story teaches the reader to weigh his pledges by what followed the first one."},{"a":"cc:2149","b":"cc:259","type":"develops","note":"Kattiyankaran plotted to send the king to rule heaven and keep the earth himself; Govindan now resolves to take his life by the same kind of guile.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The guile that won Kattiyankaran the throne becomes the means of his own destruction, as Govindan names the symmetry outright, so the plot's justice is shown as treachery turned back on its maker.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:2142","b":"k:590","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural warns that honouring one's own spy openly lets out the secret; Govindan honours Civakan's companions exactly as his own men so that the enemy's spies cannot pick them out.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet's insight that visible honour leaks a secret works in reverse too: honour spread evenly conceals, so a king can hide his guests by treating them like everyone else.","gn":["the epic verse gains a statecraft rule that explains why the even-handed gifts are a security measure and not plain hospitality","the couplet gains the inverse case, where the same logic of visible favour is used to defeat an enemy's spies rather than to protect one's own"]},{"a":"cc:2147","b":"k:827","type":"develops","note":"Kural 827 says the humble words of an enemy are like a bending bow; Kattiyankaran's letter bows, offers his life and calls Govindan lord.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The letter shows the bowed bow of the couplet in a full text, and Govindan's laughter (2148) shows the reader the couplet applied: the humbler the words, the plainer the threat.","gn":["the epic letter gains a rule by which its humility is read as menace before the story confirms it","the couplet gains a concrete instance with its parts in view: the professed loyalty, the false excuse, the invitation to come close"]},{"a":"cc:2150","b":"k:830","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 830 counsels befriending a feigned friend with the face while letting go in the heart; Govindan proclaims through the city that he has become one with Kattiyankaran while mustering his army.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet speaks of an individual's face and heart; the epic turns the face into a public proclamation and the hidden heart into a state secret guarded by threat, so the mask needs the silence of a whole city.","gn":["the epic proclamation gains the Kural's licence for a face of friendship toward a false friend, which makes the king's feint a known policy and not mere cunning","the couplet gains its political scale: when a king wears the friendly face, his people must wear it too, and the cost is a gag on every tongue"]},{"a":"cc:2176","b":"cc:2149","type":"develops","note":"In 2149 the Videha king tells his counsellors that as the usurper meant to kill him by deceit, they will take his life by deceit, and bids them keep it secret; 2176 has the narrator announce that very plan, with love as its sword.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The secret the king swore his counsellors to keep is opened to the audience but not to the victim, so the listener watches the gifts of 2174 and 2175 knowing what the usurper cannot, and the narrator names the plan deceit without blaming it."},{"a":"cc:2174","b":"cc:2148","type":"develops","note":"In 2148 the king laughs at the letter that the wretch is calling Death with his own hand; in 2174 the usurper does not know that Death has come and camped outside.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The king's jest becomes the narrator's description: the Death the usurper summoned by letter now sits at his gate, and the gifts he sends out are his welcome to it."},{"a":"cc:2173","b":"cc:2168","type":"same_image","note":"The king's right eye throbs as a good sign and his drums roar as he sets out; in the usurper's palace the women's right eyes throb, owls cry by day and the victory drum falls silent.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poet gives the two camps the same signs with opposite values, the throbbing right eye good for the marching king and bad for the palace women, the drum loud in one and dumb in the other, so the omen code is read by sex and by side."},{"a":"cc:2176","b":"k:830","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 830 counsels that when a foe must be treated as a friend, one should befriend with the face and drop friendship within; the Videha king makes himself one with the usurper so as to kill him by the sword of love.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's face-friendship is a defensive mask with an exit, while the epic turns it into the method of a killing and calls it வஞ்சம் even on the hero's side, so the pair shows how far the Kural's permission can be stretched once the foe is a usurper.","gn":["the epic's ruse gains a rule of statecraft that makes a just king's deceit look like prudence rather than treachery","the couplet gains a case where the outward friendship is not a passing shelter but the weapon itself, which its own wording does not reach"]},{"a":"cc:2175","b":"k:882","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 882 bids one fear the bond with enemies who are like kin more than enemies like swords; the usurper delights in the kinsman-like gifts of the king who means to kill him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's warning is spoken to the potential victim, and the epic shows that victim praising the gift that should have frightened him, while placing the kin-like enemy on the side the story favours.","gn":["the scene gains a name for the usurper's error: he guarded against the sword but not against the friend","the couplet gains a plot in which its danger is visible to everyone but the one it warns, and in which the false friend is not the villain"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:2173","b":"pn:20","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 20 says the Chera's guard is not shaken by omen birds coming or going; in the usurper's palace owls cry by day, blood rains and earth and sky shake.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together the two make portents a measure of rule: the praised king's realm is not shaken whatever the birds do, while the usurper's palace is shaken by every sign, though the epic does not itself say his usurpation is the cause.","gn":["the epic's portents gain a puram standard by which a secure realm is one omens cannot move, which marks the usurper's as already lost","the poem's claim gains its counter-case, a palace where the omens do strike, which shows what the praise of immunity is set against"]},{"a":"cc:2177","b":"cc:25","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's summary joins the shooting of the boar to the killing of the enemy in one line; Govindan's proclamation starts the scene that keeps that pairing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read against the patikam, the bride-contest was never chiefly about a bride: the work announced from its opening that the boar and the death of Civakan's enemy come together."},{"a":"cc:2177","b":"cc:2204","type":"develops","note":"The proclamation that the man who shoots the boar shall marry the princess is fulfilled when Civakan brings down machine and boar together.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The impossible condition set in 2177, tested by a catalogue of failing kings, is what makes Civakan's single shot read as proof of a destined claim rather than mere skill."},{"a":"cc:2186","b":"k:148","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural calls not looking at another's wife the great manhood; the epic's simile grants that the heart goes out to her and places virtue in the wise man's recalling it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the couplet's ideal of never looking, the simile shows a working form of the virtue the Kural allows only as perfection: the desire arises and understanding turns it back.","gn":["the simile gains a fixed ethical rule, so the turning arrow is measured against the standard the Kural names as manhood","the couplet gains a picture of restraint as a movement out and back, a second chance the couplet's flat 'not looking' leaves out"]},{"a":"cc:2193","b":"k:265","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says austerity is practised because it brings what is wanted as it is wanted; the narrator explains the kings' six days of failure by their ignorance of the greatness of ripened austerity.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the couplet's promise worked out as a plot: what the kings want, they cannot get by wanting, and the one who wins does so by merit of austerity, so tapas becomes the hidden cause of a marriage.","gn":["the narrator's aside gains a known maxim behind it, making the failures a lesson rather than a joke","the couplet's general 'whatever is wanted' gains a concrete case where desire and skill fail and only earlier merit succeeds"]},{"a":"cc:2197","b":"cc:1164","type":"tension","note":"Madanan swore to the king that he had split Civakan's chest and rolled him over; Kattiyangaran now sees Civakan alive and names Madanan's failure as his ruin.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Together the two verses show the usurper undone by his own system of lies: he rewarded a false report of a killing, and the man he believed dead returns to the very contest set as his trap."},{"a":"cc:2196","b":"cc:1137","type":"same_image","note":"In 1137 the soldiers who hem in Civakan scatter like deer that have seen a killing tiger; here Kattiyangaran himself cries out like a deer that has seen a tiger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same simile moves up from the king's soldiers to the king: the fear Civakan once struck in the men sent against him now reaches the man who sent them, and his own words in 2197 call Civakan the tiger."},{"a":"cc:2197","b":"cc:2206","type":"develops","note":"Kattiyangaran fears that the escaped 'tiger' will crush him; in 2206 a voice from the sky proclaims that the lion Civakan will swallow the life of the elephant Kattiyangaran.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The usurper's private dread in 2197 is answered by a public sky-voice in 2206 with the same beast imagery, so his fear turns out to be accurate foresight rather than panic."},{"a":"cc:2201","b":"cc:2204","type":"tension","note":"The failed kings laugh, asking whether the boar will break and fall of itself; in 2204 Civakan's shot brings down the machine and the boar together.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The mockers' jibe is answered in a few verses: what they said could not happen without effort happens at one shot, so their laughter becomes the 'fire' 2201 says they lit against themselves."},{"a":"cc:2204","b":"cc:25","type":"develops","note":"The prologue's summary joins two acts in one line, that Civakan shot the boar and killed the enemy; 2204 performs the first.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The prologue binds the contest shot to the killing of the usurper, so the reader knows the winning arrow here is the opening blow of the war, not a feat for a bride alone."},{"a":"cc:2204","b":"k:279","type":"tension","note":"The epic likens the hero's arrows to the straight minds of the refined; the couplet holds that the straight arrow is cruel and that form proves nothing about worth.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read against the couplet, the simile shows the epic choosing the arrow's straightness as a sign of virtue at the very moment the arrow starts a war of slaughter, which the Kural warns is the arrow's real nature.","gn":["a check on its own praise: the straight arrow it calls a refined mind is, by the couplet, a killer's shape","a case of exactly the misreading it warns against, where an admired shape stands in for goodness in a famous poem"]},{"a":"cc:2220","b":"k:770","type":"develops","note":"The epic gives the usurper the larger host, and the couplet says that many steadfast men make no army without leaders.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The counts set Kattiyangaran's bigger force against Civakan's smaller one, so the coming defeat of the larger host turns the couplet's rule about leadership over numbers into a plot.","gn":["a rule that explains why the counted advantage of the usurper will not decide the battle","a story where the two armies are counted unit by unit and the larger one is the one fated to lose"]},{"a":"cc:2221","b":"kv:32","type":"tension","note":"Both make the Earth a woman on the battlefield: the Kalavazhi's Earth is wrapped red as in a fine cloth by the king's slaughter, the Cintamani's beats her breast and pines.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same personified Earth that the praise-poem dresses in the victor's blood becomes in the epic a mourner, so the pair shows the one figure serving both the glory and the grief of a war.","gn":["a sense of how pointed its grieving Earth is, set against the battle-praise convention that adorns her","a counter-voice that turns its red-robed Earth from ornament to wound"]},{"a":"cc:2221","b":"pn:25","type":"qualifies","note":"The Purananuru poet asks whether the Pandya's spear did not stop short of ruin when it saw women made widows beat their breasts and shear their hair; the Cintamani names the widows' lost adornment as what the hero's spear will bring.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"In the Sangam poem the sight of widows is what checks the spear, while in the epic the widows are counted in advance as the spear's work, so the pair shows grief for the enemy's women moving from a limit on war to its foreseen price.","gn":["an older measure of a just warrior, who stops at the widows' grief, against which its raging spear is judged","a later story where the same grief is foreseen and does not stop the fighting, which sharpens how rare its king's restraint was"]},{"a":"cc:2223","b":"kv:37","type":"same_image","note":"Both set war as a sea with ships: in the epic the two armies meet as two seas with elephants as ships, in the Kalavazhi the blood drags elephants and drums like boats meeting on the sea after the slaughter.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic uses the sea at the moment of the clash, the Kalavazhi uses it for the blood that remains after, so together they show the sea image passing from the armies' living mass to the flood of their dead.","gn":["the aftermath its own image points to: the seas that meet become a sea of blood","the moment before its blood-sea, when the boats it shows were the living elephants of two hosts"]},{"a":"cc:2245","b":"cc:257","type":"develops","note":"In 257 Madanan told the council he settles things only by the sword, never by the mouth; in 2245 he falls at the mouth of a sword (வாளின்வாய்) in the war that the usurpation he urged has brought on.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The man who refused talk for the sword dies by the sword's 'mouth', so the epic answers his boast with its own wording and makes his death the fruit of his counsel."},{"a":"cc:2251","b":"cc:2245","type":"same_image","note":"Madanan falls like the moon swallowed by Rahu plunging into the sea; the Kalinga king falls like the sun dropping into the sea with a planet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two eclipse similes make the enemy's captains the moon and the sun of their side, so their two deaths together read as the setting of both lights of Kattiyangaran's host."},{"a":"cc:2235","b":"cc:803","type":"tension","note":"In 803, at his first battle, Civakan watches a ghoul dress herself in the slain and laughs with delight; in 2235 the same ghoul's feast is made from bridegrooms whose wedding garlands their brides had strung.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Where the first battle offers the ghoul's feast as spectacle for the hero's laughter, the final war sets it against the brides' garlands, so the same image now carries the cost of war to the women left behind."},{"a":"cc:2240","b":"k:780","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 780 calls a death that fills the eyes of one's patron with tears worth begging for; 2240's warriors have paid their debt of the red rice, and the only ones who cry over them are jackals.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the Kural's logic of a death owed to the lord but removes the weeping patron who gave that death its worth, leaving the debt paid with no one to mourn it.","gn":["the Kural gives the epic's 'debt of red rice' its moral frame, a death owed in return for being sustained","the couplet's patron's tears are shown to be absent on the actual field, where the howling of jackals takes their place"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:2235","b":"pn:26","type":"qualifies","note":"Puram 26 praises a Pandya who cooked a battlefield sacrifice with crowned heads for the hearth, blood for water and bangled arms for ladles; in 2235 the slain warriors themselves are the ghoul-woman's feast, their wedding garlands replaced by entrails.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Sangam battlefield-feast is the victor's rite and his glory, while the Cintamani tells it from the side of the slain, as bridegrooms whose wedding garlands become entrails.","gn":["the epic's ghoul-feast is placed in the old genre of the battlefield sacrifice it inherits","the Puram's feast, sung only from the victor's side, is shown from the dead men's side, where it costs a marriage"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:2233","b":"pn:19","type":"qualifies","note":"In Puram 19 Death itself runs away ashamed from the field where mothers of old houses weep over their sons; in 2233 the Earth goddess, unable to look at the cruelty, veils herself in a red blanket of blood.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both poems make a cosmic power recoil from the field, but the Sangam poem turns the recoil into praise of the king's valour, while the epic gives it to the Earth the hero fights to win, so the prize cannot bear the war waged for her.","gn":["the conceit gains an older pattern in which a cosmic witness flinching from the slaughter measures its scale","the Puram's recoil, used there to praise the victor, is shown able to judge the war instead, when the one who flinches is the land being won"]},{"a":"cc:2244","b":"n:48","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 48 asks those who praise sandal and garlands whether they have not seen vultures tear the body once it breaks; 2244 sets the sandal-smeared chest that women embraced in the blood, embraced now by the sword.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Naladiyar uses the ruined body to turn a man from desire, but the epic shows the same ruin of the beloved body in battle and draws no lesson from it, keeping beauty and slaughter in one image without preaching.","gn":["the epic's image gains a name for what it shows, the body's adornment undone, which it leaves unspoken","the quatrain's argument meets a case where the body is broken by war rather than age, and the poet still sees it as beautiful"]},{"a":"cc:2255","b":"k:774","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's hero pulls the spear from his own body and laughs; Ulokapalan plucks the arrow from his forehead and the gushing blood is likened to the dawn sun's first ray.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Together they show the wound pulled from the body as a fixed sign of the hero, and that the epic turns the Kural's psychological shock, a laugh, into visual glory, so the same act is valued as composure in the one and as splendour in the other.","gn":["the forehead-arrow is recognised as a stock heroic act, not a chance detail, which explains why the verse lingers on it before the kill","the couplet's bare gesture gains a full scene with its consequence: the man who pulls the arrow is the one who kills in the next verse"]},{"a":"cc:2258","b":"k:773","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says help to a foe in misfortune is the cutting edge of valour; Nabulan, seeing the Magadha king flinch, holds his blow, reassures him and goes elsewhere.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet calls kindness to a stricken foe valour's sharpest point, and the epic shows what drives it in the moment: shame in the stronger man (நாணி), so that sparing is felt as a duty to one's own honour rather than as pity.","gn":["Nabulan's laughing restraint is given a name as the height of valour, so it is not read as weakness in the middle of a battle","the couplet's abstract ஊராண்மை acquires a motive and a gesture, the laugh and the word 'do not fear', that it does not describe"]},{"a":"cc:2261","b":"pn:9","type":"qualifies","note":"The Pandya Kudumi warns cows, brahmins, women, the sick and the sonless to take shelter before he shoots; Vipulan will not strike beasts, the wounded, the young or the old.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both armies spare the weak, but on different grounds: the Purananuru's warning is the way of right (அறத்துஆறு) protecting those who cannot fight, while the epic grounds each refusal in the striker's own glory, so the same restraint can rest on dharma or on pride.","gn":["Vipulan's code is seen as the epic's version of an old battle rule, and its distinctive turn, honour rather than protection, stands out","the Pandya's proclamation gains a contrast that shows how particular its reason is: it names the protected by their vulnerability and their rites, not by what striking them would cost the warrior"]},{"a":"cc:2261","b":"k:772","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural prefers a spear that missed an elephant to an arrow that hit a hare; Vipulan will not strike even a rutting elephant, since a beast brings no distinction.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The Kural ranks quarry by size and danger and puts the elephant at the top; the epic's warrior ranks by kind and puts every beast below a man, so the two measures of a worthy target diverge exactly at the elephant.","gn":["the oddity of refusing an elephant in war is sharpened against the proverb that makes the elephant the noblest mark","the couplet's scale of targets is shown to be one of several: a code of honour among men can rank the elephant as no target at all"]},{"a":"cc:2259","b":"cc:2261","type":"develops","note":"Nabulan will not strike an unarmed youth or even his elephant; two verses later the narrator states the code of not striking beasts, the wounded or the young.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The scene acts out first and the rule is stated after, so the code of 2261 reads as a summary of how this whole company fights, not as one man's quirk."},{"a":"cc:2260","b":"cc:2265","type":"develops","note":"Vipulan offers the enemy heaven in exchange for leaving fame on earth; the challenger who answers him is killed and received into heaven by the celestial women.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The taunt turns out to be a literal promise kept: the one man who takes up Vipulan's offer gets exactly the heaven it named, so the challenge is courtesy as well as threat."},{"a":"cc:2265","b":"pn:287","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 287 says warriors who do not run, if they die, enjoy the weddings of flawless women in the higher world; in 2265 the slain challenger is showered with flowers by celestial women and reaches heaven.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Purananuru's reward is promised to one's own unflinching men to steady them; the epic grants it on the field to an enemy killed by the hero's companion, so the heaven of the brave is shown to belong to the fallen of either side.","gn":["the apsaras' welcome is read as the old Puram reward for the unflinching dead, not a passing ornament","the poem's promise, a speech to warriors before battle, is seen fulfilled as an event, and extended to the man who loses a fair duel"]},{"a":"cc:2265","b":"k:86","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says one who tends his guests becomes a good guest of those in the sky; Vipulan calls his challenger a guest and gives him the first blow, and it is the guest, killed, who goes to heaven.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with the couplet, the duel inverts its exchange: in the Kural the host earns heaven by feeding the guest, while on the battlefield the host's hospitality is the first stroke, and heaven goes to the guest he kills.","gn":["the word விருந்து in a duel is shown to carry the full weight of the guest-ethic, so the courtesy is not a figure of speech","the couplet's tie between hosting and heaven is seen working in a setting it never imagines, where the guest is an enemy and the welcome is death"]},{"a":"cc:2256","b":"cc:2273","type":"same_image","note":"The Kampili king dies by arrows in the chest that women's breasts had ploughed; later the warrior whose chest is flushed from pressing women's breasts scatters burning arrows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The chapter uses the lover's chest twice, once as the place where death enters and once as the source of the killer's fire, so the same mark of love signals both the victim and the victor."},{"a":"cc:2290","b":"k:774","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's hero pulls the spear from his own body and laughs; the epic's youth plucks out the spear that fed on his flesh and laughs (பறித்து நக்கான்).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic gives the couplet's laugh a cause the couplet leaves open: the spear had been thrown by a man who kept his spear for elephants, so the wound is an honour and the laugh is the pride of having been judged worth it.","gn":["the youth's laugh is placed in a known code of valour, so it reads as a canonical act rather than bravado","the couplet's bare gesture gets a situation, the foe's choice of target in 2289, that explains why a wound is a matter for joy"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:2291","b":"k:775","type":"develops","note":"The Kural calls it flight when a fierce man blinks at a flung spear; the epic's warrior does not blink at all (இமைத்திலன்) as the spear comes, and takes it on his chest.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set beside the couplet, the verse shows where its standard leads: the unblinking eye is kept to the end, and the warrior who will not blink is the one the spear kills, so the code costs the life it measures.","gn":["the stillness of the eye, one detail among many, is shown to be the decisive proof in the code of courage","the couplet's test is followed to its consequence, a death embraced like a garland, which the couplet's question stops short of"]},{"a":"cc:2289","b":"k:772","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural prefers a spear that missed an elephant to an arrow that hit a hare; the epic's thrower would not cast except at a young elephant, and spends that spear on one warrior.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet ranks targets; the epic shows the ranking used as a courtesy between enemies, a man of the elephant-only code paying a foot soldier the honour of the elephant's spear.","gn":["the thrower's habit is recognised as the standard the couplet names, so his choice reads as tribute to the youth","the couplet's private scale of glory becomes something one warrior can confer on another"]},{"a":"cc:2293","b":"pn:284","type":"same_image","note":"In Purananuru 284 a warrior straightens his twisted sword with an elephant's tusk as lever (மிறையாக ... திருத்தா) and laughs at the fleeing back; Salanidhi straightens his elephant-bent sword in a lever (மிறைக்கொளீஇ திருத்துவான்) and the kings flee.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the epic taking over a Sangam war vignette in its own words, the sword-straightening and the enemy's flight, but turning the Sangam hero's laugh into the foe's fear and making the man a spear-pierced warrior who dies in the next verse.","gn":["Salanidhi's obscure gesture is clarified by the older poem's clearer picture of a sword set against a lever","the Purananuru vignette is shown to have lived on as a set piece of heroic poetry long after the Sangam"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:2279","b":"pn:290","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 290 tells a king to give toddy to the warrior whose forefathers died for his, because he will shield the king from the spear; the epic's warrior says it is for a day like this that lords keep their men.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The two show the same exchange of sustenance for service from both ends: the Sangam poet urges the lord to pay in advance, and the epic's warrior, arming, counts himself as that payment falling due.","gn":["the warrior's brief remark is seen as part of an old covenant between lord and fighter rather than a passing thought","the bard's plea to the king is answered by the warrior's own voice accepting the debt"]},{"a":"cc:2284","b":"cc:2300","type":"same_image","note":"A warrior advancing laughs that men bold among women but trembling in battle have no manliness; Arichandan shames fleeing warriors whose heads women's feet have kicked.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same taunt, the lover who yields to women must not yield in war, is spoken by an attacker scorning cowards and by a captain stopping a rout, which shows the epic's measure of martial shame is drawn from love and serves either army."},{"a":"cc:2302","b":"k:780","type":"develops","note":"Kural 780 says a death that fills with tears the eyes of those who sustained one is worth begging for; Ariccandan's speech promises rewards to men who die helping the leader who raised and kept them (தற்புறந் தந்து வைத்த தலைமகற்கு).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the couplet's patron-bond being used as a battlefield appeal, and that it can be spent on a bad master: the leader these men are asked to die for is the usurper, and the speech wins only their deaths.","gn":["Ariccandan's speech is seen to rest on the moral claim of the sustained man's debt, not merely on bribes of heaven","the couplet's ideal is shown with its blind spot: it weighs the bond to the sustainer and not whether the sustainer deserves the death"]},{"a":"cc:2302","b":"pn:287","type":"develops","note":"The Purananuru poem says warriors who stand under arrows care nothing for fields because, dying, they wed flawless women in the high world; Ariccandan offers the same heavenly women (கடியரமகளிர்) and adds the poets' praise and the hero-stone.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The pairing shows the Sangam promise of heavenly brides surviving into the Jain epic as a commander's rallying formula, now stacked with fame and stone into a three-world reward, while the following verse turns its hearers into food for the hero's arrows.","gn":["the speech is placed in a long tradition of the warrior's reward, which explains why the beaten men return to the fight","the poem's confident claim is set in a story where those who believe it fight for the losing, unjust side"]},{"a":"cc:2314","b":"cc:250","type":"tension","note":"Dharmadattan warned Kattiyangaran that a king is a fire that turns nectar or poison as it will; at his end Kattiyangaran says it is good or bad deeds that turn poison to nectar and nectar to poison.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same nectar-and-poison figure moves from the wronged king's power to impersonal karma, and the shift lets the usurper blame his fall on deeds in general rather than on the king he wronged, as he was warned."},{"a":"cc:2314","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 380 holds nothing is stronger than fate, which forestalls any scheme; Kattiyangaran says the wise do not think of killing or being killed because deeds decide.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the couplet's fatalism to a schemer whose own plot made the war, so the reader sees how the doctrine that fate outruns all devices can also serve to excuse the devices one has already used.","gn":["the speech is seen to quote a sanctioned teaching, so its self-serving use is the point and not an error of doctrine","the couplet gains a test case where fate is invoked by the guilty party at the moment of reckoning"]},{"a":"cc:2316","b":"n:51","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar quatrain says that as darkness rushes back when the lamp's oil runs out, evil settles in when good deeds are spent; Kattiyangaran says that as a lamp dwindles when ghee and wick are gone, wealth perishes when the two kinds of deed wear away.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both make karma the lamp's fuel, but the quatrain teaches renewing merit by austerity while the epic's usurper uses the image to explain the loss of a throne, so the lamp shows its political use: a fall can be read as fuel run out instead of a wrong repaid.","gn":["the speech is seen to draw on a renunciant teaching image, sharpened by counting both good and bad deeds as fuel","the quatrain's image is applied to kingship and wealth in a story, where it can be heard as self-excuse"],"gr":2},{"a":"cc:2317","b":"k:775","type":"develops","note":"Kural 775 counts it flight for the fierce to blink when a spear is thrown; Kattiyangaran says that if he blinks when a spear comes, Civakan may laugh.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The king splits outcome from courage: he grants victory to fate yet stakes his honour on the couplet's unblinking eye, so the pair shows the unblinking gaze as the one part of valour fate cannot take.","gn":["the boast is read against a fixed measure of valour, which answers Civakan's taunt of fear","the couplet's standard is shown held by a man who believes the battle's result is not his to decide"]},{"a":"cc:2319","b":"k:904","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 904 says the man who fears his wife gains no distinction from his deeds; Kattiyangaran's self-curse is that if he loses in valour he may become a base man who fears his wife (இல்லாளை அஞ்சி) and slights a guest.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The vow ranks fear of one's wife as a disgrace equal to defeat in battle, which shows the couplet's household fault serving as a warrior's measure of shame, the worst a king can wish on himself.","gn":["the oath's odd household content is explained: it names a vice the moral tradition already marks as ruin of a man's action","the couplet's fault is seen weighed against cowardice in war, which places it among the gravest failures of manhood"]},{"a":"cc:2319","b":"pn:71","type":"develops","note":"Boothappandiyan's vow wishes on himself, if he fails, separation from his wife, a weak sceptre and the loss of friends; Kattiyangaran's vow wishes on himself fear of his wife and the shame of slighting a guest.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both are conditional self-curses whose penalties are drawn from household and court rather than the field, so the pair shows the vanjinam form keeping its structure into the epic while the epic gives it to the losing usurper, who dies at once.","gn":["the speech is identified as a vanjinam of a known form, with its self-curse clauses","the Sangam vow is seen in a story where it is not fulfilled, so the vow's force is shown as a matter of the speaker's cause"]},{"a":"cc:2323","b":"cc:245","type":"develops","note":"Dharmadattan warned that if Kattiyangaran seized the king who made him, Fortune would turn from him; here his rule, called a Kali-reign, perishes and the war is declared over.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The usurper's fall fulfils the councillor's warning given before the crime, so the story names his own deed as cause even though, dying, he gave everything to karma at 2314 to 2316."},{"a":"cc:2325","b":"cc:1814","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai asked that her son be told all the evil she suffered after her lord died and be brought to her; here hearing his victory her tear-filled eyes grow cool.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Her grief, laid on the son as a charge, is answered by the victory, so the battle closes the mother's wrong and not only the father's loss of the throne."},{"a":"cc:2324","b":"cc:2308","type":"same_image","note":"At 2308 Civakan is likened to the white moon seized by a serpent; at 2324 he is the young sun that splits the serpent's mouth after many days.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The eclipse image moves from moon in the serpent's grip in the thick of the fight to sun breaking free after the king's death, so the hero's figure grows brighter as the usurpation ends."},{"a":"cc:2326","b":"cc:259","type":"tension","note":"Kattiyangaran promised to guard the earth so it would brim with gold once the king had gone to heaven; at the end the Earth goddess, bathed in his allies' blood, is taken by Civakan.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The usurper's claim to be the earth's guardian is reversed by the closing marriage of earth and rightful heir, which shows the ilampakam's title answering his first speech of seizure."},{"a":"cc:2329","b":"cc:324","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai's blessing at Civakan's birth, that he would utterly destroy his old enemy, is fulfilled when the city's women say he has killed the deluded one with all his sons.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The blessing spoken alone in the burning ground is fulfilled in public speech: the city, not the mother, now announces the destruction she foretold."},{"a":"cc:2335","b":"cc:664","type":"tension","note":"Kattiyangaran had told the suitors that only Kama himself could beat Gandharvadattai at the vina and that no such man was present; the women now call the king's slayer the vina-master.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The usurper's own boast that no rival existed names the man who would kill him, so the vina contest becomes the first sign of his fall."},{"a":"cc:2338","b":"cc:303","type":"same_image","note":"Vijayai went from a sky-touching palace to the burning ground alone after the usurper killed her husband; now the usurper's women go from flower-cushions to the bare hot ground after his death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the usurper's household the very fall it gave the queen he wronged, and 303's reading of that fall as sin made visible is left for the reader to carry over."},{"a":"cc:2338","b":"k:555","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says the tears of those who cannot bear oppression wear away the oppressor's wealth; when the usurper falls, the tears in the story are those of his own women.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The ruin that the tears of the oppressed bring on a bent sceptre falls in the story on the oppressor's women, who now weep in their turn.","gn":["the women's tears get a cause beyond the scene: the wealth they lose was worn away by the tears of the city in Saccandan's fall","the couplet's abstract 'wealth' is shown to include people, and its justice is shown to land on those who did not themselves oppress"]},{"a":"cc:2337","b":"n:3","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar says kings who rode in state fall when their deeds give out and their foes take the wives they married; the Cintamani's victors break into the fallen king's palace and drive his women out.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic stages the quatrain's fall of a king but changes its last turn: the victors expel the dead king's wives rather than take them.","gn":["the expulsion, which the verse narrates without comment, gets the quatrain's frame of a fall that comes when one's deeds run out","the quatrain's 'foes take the wives' is shown not as the only end: a victor of the Jain epic sends them away instead"]},{"a":"cc:2343","b":"cc:297","type":"develops","note":"After killing Saccandan the usurper had his own name drummed from an elephant and, the poet said, the dark-hearted man won the goddess of fortune; now he and his sons lie dead and no one can say which of his women wore the queen's fillet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The fortune the usurper seized in 297 is shown at its end as an empty title, and the ilampakam named for that goddess opens by taking her back."},{"a":"cc:2344","b":"cc:299","type":"same_image","note":"Vijayai, hearing the usurper's victory drum, fainted like a doe struck by a spear (எஃகு எறி பிணையின் மாழ்கி); the usurper's queen, hearing of his death, sinks like a doe that has taken a spear (வேலேறு பெற்ற பிணை ... மாழ்கி) and dies.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic gives the usurper's wife the very simile it gave the queen he widowed, so the wrong done at the start comes home in the same figure, except that Vijayai survived to bear the avenger and this woman does not survive.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:2349","b":"cc:295","type":"tension","note":"Saccandan's women stripped off their necklaces, garlands and ear-rings and cried that the king's cruelty was cruel; now the usurper's women put off ear-rings, bangles and garlands as a burden.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The stripping of ornaments that opened the epic as the price of the usurper's deed closes his reign on his own women, showing that the widowhood he caused is the widowhood his household inherits."},{"a":"cc:2357","b":"cc:820","type":"develops","note":"After the first great battle Civakan made a gold image of the Jina, gave wealth and put away the sin of the battlefield with lament; after the final battle he bows to the Jina and flings out gold before putting on his royal ornaments.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read with 820, the morning worship and gift of 2357 are not ornament but the same rite of expiation after killing, now made the first act of the new king's day.","gr":2},{"a":"cc:2353","b":"cc:819","type":"develops","note":"After the earlier battle Padumukan tended the wounded with ghee and cloths and sent them to beds; here Civakan himself charges his uncle with the care of the wounded before he rests.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The care of the wounded moves from a friend's improvised kindness to a king's first order, so the epic marks Civakan's kingship by who is told to look after the men who bled for him."},{"a":"cc:2353","b":"pp:7:167-188","type":"tension","note":"The Nedunalvadai's king walks the camp at midnight, a few men with him, looking each wounded man kindly in the face; Civakan tells his uncle to have the wounded tended and lies down to rest with women stroking his feet.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Sangam king who goes out in the cold to see the wounds himself, the epic's victor delegates the duty and sleeps, which shows how far the later court poem has moved the king's care from presence to command.","gn":["an older measure of royal care for the wounded against which Civakan's delegation and rest stand out as a choice","a contrast in which its king's personal midnight round reads as a distinct ideal, not the only way a poet could honour a king"]},{"a":"cc:2355","b":"pp:5:67-79","type":"tension","note":"The Mullaippattu's king cannot sleep for thinking of the elephant with its trunk cut, the men who fell and the wounded horses; Civakan sleeps through the noise of wounds, weeping women and drums as if he heard none of it.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The two kings face the same night after battle with opposite responses, the one kept awake by the wounded and the other asleep amid their cries, so the epic's sleep is a sign of a war ended where the Sangam king's waking is a sign of one still going on.","gn":["a reason to read Civakan's undisturbed sleep as the peace of a finished war rather than mere indifference","a view of its king's sleeplessness as tied to an unfinished campaign, which the poem alone does not test"]},{"a":"cc:2347","b":"pn:25","type":"develops","note":"Kalladanar asks whether the Pandya's spear did not stop short when he saw the widows of his enemies beating their breasts and cutting their hair; Civakan's anger stops at the usurper's death and he offers the widows a living in the city or gold to leave with.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Where the Sangam poem praises the conqueror only for holding back his spear at the sight of the widows, the epic makes restraint into provision, the victor feeding or endowing the women his war has widowed.","gn":["an older warrior ethic of sparing the enemy's women, which shows Civakan's speech as the fulfilment of a known ideal","an example of how far the restraint it praises could be carried, from not killing to giving the widows a livelihood"]},{"a":"cc:2356","b":"pn:194","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 194 sets the dirge drum in one house beside the wedding drum in another and concludes that the maker of the world is unkind; in 2356 one band of women swims in a sea of sorrow and another in a sea of joy on the same morning, while the hero washes his face.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic puts the Puram poet's split world of grief and joy inside one palace and makes one man's victory its cause, so what the Sangam poet blames on the world's maker is shown here as the plain consequence of a deed.","gn":["a reflective frame that names what is unsettling in its quiet line about two seas of feeling side by side","a concrete case in which the division of grief and joy is made by a human act, not by an unkind creator"]},{"a":"cc:2372","b":"cc:7","type":"develops","note":"The patikam opens with Saccandan, king of the unbending sceptre; Civakan's first order from the throne is to clear his realm of afflictions and have the drum announce it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The son's first act as king is the sceptre's work that his father's reign set at the start, so the epic sets his crowning as the repair of the wrong the story began with.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2377","b":"cc:27","type":"develops","note":"The patikam says that Civakan renounces while kings bring tribute and his sceptre runs just; this verse ends the ilampakam on the people's peace and turns to what befell the king of kings.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The peace of 2377 is the height from which the patikam says he turns away, so the praise of his rule is the setup for the renunciation, not the end of the tale.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2375","b":"k:390","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 390 names giving, kindness, a straight sceptre and care of the people as the four that make a king a light; the proclamation of 2375 turns them into acts: remission of dues and the end of hunger, disease and enmity.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's four virtues become a single proclamation with named relief, which shows what giving and care of the people cost a treasury and who is to benefit.","gn":["The proclamation gains a standard by which its remission and the ending of hunger are measured as the work of a king.","The couplet gains concrete acts, a remitted tax, for virtues it only names."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2376","b":"k:557","type":"develops","note":"Kural 557 says a king's lack of grace is for those who live what the lack of rain is for the earth; 2376 has the king's wealth go as grace to the blind, the sick and the wives abandoned.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the cost of a king's lack of grace in the language of rain, while the epic names those who would be left dry, showing who counts as the people the sceptre owes.","gn":["The proclamation gains the couplet's picture of grace as rain: help that reaches those who cannot ask.","The couplet gains named sufferers, the blind, the sick and the abandoned wife, who are the dry land of its simile."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2371","b":"n:3","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 3 says that even kings who rode in state under the umbrella fall when their deeds turn against them; 2371 seats the victor on the lion throne in the full pomp of such a king, after the usurper's fall.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The throne scene stands on the ground the quatrain describes: the pomp the winner now enjoys is what the usurper lost, so the crowning is shown as one turn of a wheel, not a rest.","gn":["The enthronement gains a warning that the seat is held only while deeds support it.","The quatrain gains a case in which the fallen king's rival, not death, is the agent of the fall."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2366","b":"t:1601","type":"tension","note":"In 2366 a god crowns Civakan with gold and sea-water; Tirumanthiram 1601 says that those who rule the three worlds wear crowns, but the bliss of the kings of the Lord's feet has no measure.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Cintamani lets the crowning be the height of splendour, while the Saiva verse ranks the same crowned kings below those who serve at the feet, so what counts as the highest state is exactly what the two texts divide on.","gn":["The coronation gains a rival measure of rank, in which crowns do not count, against which its splendour can be weighed.","The Tirumanthiram verse gains a full scene of a crowned king receiving the highest earthly honour, so its contrast has a concrete case."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2386","b":"cc:1062","type":"develops","note":"In 1062 the astrologers fix the day and the party sets out for a bride; here the astrologer lays out the days for the elephant-lord and the wedding is carried out on the day named.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The astrologer's fixing of the day is the Cintamani's regular first step in a royal marriage, and here it gains a royal scale: a hundred and eight dressed elephants and a seven-day city festival.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2388","b":"cc:1687","type":"develops","note":"1687 has the wedding announced with the drum and the whole world knowing; 2388 shows how it is done, with the proclaimers dressed in white and sandal and carried on a battle elephant.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The wedding drum is a royal herald's act with its own costume and mount, which the earlier wedding announcement only names.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2388","b":"n:86","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar says a wife married on a fixed day, with the drum beaten that many may know, is the wife a man should keep to; the epic shows that drum beaten from an elephant and the day set by an astrologer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's drum and fixed day are the public face of a marriage, which the epic acts out as a city-wide command.","gn":["the epic's drum is given its moral weight, since a bond announced to all is the bond that shuts the husband off from another's wife","the Naladiyar's drum and fixed day, only named in the quatrain, get a full royal scene: the herald, the elephant, the seven days of orders"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2391","b":"a:86","type":"qualifies","note":"The Akananuru man remembers a wedding of sand spread under a pandal, lamps and garlands at an auspicious dawn; the Cintamani's order has plantain and areca, aloe pots, lute and dance and golden flags across the city.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same wedding adornments grow from a household's sand pandal to a king's command that the whole city be decked, and so show how rank changes the scale and not the parts.","gn":["the epic's royal decking is seen to rest on the household's pandal, sand and lamps, which it enlarges and does not replace","the Akananuru wedding, remembered only in the bride and groom's house, is set beside a city-wide wedding and shows how small and private it is"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2389","b":"cc:605","type":"tension","note":"In 605 the drum proclaims alms to ascetics and hard penance while still in the body; in 2389 the drum cries to wear pearls and sandal and dress in fine cloth.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The one royal drum serves both a call to wear away the body in penance and a call to adorn it, so the epic's ideals of renunciation and of festival sit side by side without being reconciled.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2392","b":"n:23","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar says the same wedding drums that sound in the hall may be funeral drums that very day; here a wedding drum thunders for seven days and the city is told to live.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Cintamani's wedding drum is heard here only as joy, while the Naladiyar hears in the same sound the fragility of the groom's life, so the two give opposing readings of the same instrument.","gn":["the epic's drum gets a shadow of impermanence which the Jain poet leaves unspoken here but may mean the reader to carry from the epic's end","the Naladiyar's warning is given a concrete scene of seven days of festival to be undone, not only the bare image of a drum"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2404","b":"cc:140","type":"same_image","note":"The city's wealth is compared to a grove of wish-granting trees with honey-dripping garlands and ambrosia in the first ilampakam; here the festival goods held up in incense smoke make the city look as if the golden wish-trees and the wish-creeper had taken it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same wishing-tree figure that praised a city's standing wealth is now used for the wealth brought out for a wedding, so the epic's image of plenty follows the occasion.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2412","b":"n:23","type":"qualifies","note":"The wedding instruments sound and the carp-eyed women go to the bath; the Naladiyar quatrain says the drums that sounded as wedding drums may sound that day in that place as funeral drums for the bridegroom.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the drums only their joyful sound, and the quatrain reminds the reader that a hero's wedding festival is not yet a safe ground for pride, which matters in a poem that ends with its hero leaving the throne.","gn":["The festival's drums and blessings, which the verse sounds only for joy, gain the warning that the same drums can turn funereal, which the verse never says.","The quatrain's general rule gains a long, fully dressed wedding of a hero who will later fear kingship, so the lesson meets a concrete story."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2417","b":"cc:225","type":"develops","note":"The king reads Vijayai's dream of the shoot and crown as her son's rising fortune; at the ghee-anointing the women now bless him to be lord of the wheel and to abide with Fortune.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The fortune that the king foretold from the dream is voiced as a blessing in the rite, so the reader sees the prophecy being fulfilled step by step.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2417","b":"cc:463","type":"develops","note":"After the cattle-raid the women wished that the young elephant of a man wed the earth too; at the anointing they now wish that he be wheel-lord and live in Fortune.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The earlier, offhand wish for him to wed the earth returns as a formal blessing in a rite, so the epic marks how far his standing has risen since the cattle-raid.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2425","b":"cc:2358","type":"same_image","note":"After the last battle he is called a Kama even to the wise as he sits like a lion in his silk; at the wedding bath he again becomes Kama to the women of fragrant hair.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poet gives the hero the Kama figure in victory and again in his wedding dress, so the epic ties his beauty first to conquest and then to marriage.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2427","b":"cc:27","type":"tension","note":"The guards wave countless whisks and the festival's wealth is beyond count; the poem's summary says that while kings brought tribute and fortune stayed, Civakan heard the dharma, feared kingship like a snake struck by thunder, and renounced.","grade":"forced","better":"develops","te":"Read with the closing renunciation, the countless whisks mark the height of the splendour he will later flee, so the poem's praise of kingship is held against its end.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2451","b":"cc:637","type":"develops","note":"The onlookers of 637 say the bride's girdle and breasts are Death upon Death and that the anklet bells cry to the suitors to run and save their lives; here Death has come in the king's daughter's form and her girdle, anklet and bells cry to the onlookers to go and guard their hearts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The cry that was gossip at Gandharvadattai's contest becomes the ornaments' own speech at Lakkanai's wedding, so the adornment itself is made the warning.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2451","b":"k:1083","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1083 has a man say he never knew Death and now does, it being a woman's grace and large war-eyes; 2451 says Death has come in a king's daughter's form and her own jewels bid the onlookers flee.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet turns Death into one man's private discovery, while the epic makes it a public warning given by the jewels, so a figure of suffering becomes a danger announced to a crowd.","gn":["a source for the figure of the woman as Death, which shows the jewels' warning is a figure for the pain beauty gives and not an idle boast","a scene of bride, ornaments and a watching crowd that shows what in her dress the poet reads as a weapon, which the couplet leaves to the word பெண்டகை"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2439","b":"cc:350","type":"tension","note":"The dresser nips flowers with her curved nail to print the bride's forehead mark, the opening of a householder's life; Vijayai, turning ascetic, takes the mark off with her finger, with the ear-rings and pearls, to close it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same mark and the same ornaments are put on to begin a marriage and taken off to end a life in the world, so the epic shows adornment as the sign of entry and renunciation as its undoing.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2440","b":"cc:488","type":"same_image","note":"The dresser takes off the bride's tusk studs and plain ear-rings and sets wedding ear-ornaments (கடிப்பு); the herdswomen take off the herd girl's gold ear-studs and gem pendants and set the same auspicious கடிப்பு with a small simple adornment.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same step in the rite is done in the same words for a king's daughter and a herd girl, showing the rite fixed while the stuff of the ornaments shows the wealth of the house.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2437","b":"cc:673","type":"develops","note":"Civakan adorns the dancer by the stage rules, tying the band, crowning, setting sandal and jewels; the bride's dresser, who knows every treatise, dresses the bride's hair by the hand-books.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both scenes make dressing a learned craft with its manuals, so Lakkanai's toilet is told as an art practised by a master and not as vanity.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2431","b":"cc:623","type":"same_image","note":"Gandharvadattai is bathed in scented water brought in gem pots on elephants' heads; the king's daughter is bathed with water in a jewelled pot on a white musth elephant.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The bath on an elephant's pot is the royal bride's rite in two marriages, which marks Lakkanai's wedding as one in the line of the contest bride's.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2443","b":"cc:706","type":"same_image","note":"The city women take Civakan for Kama who has put down his arrows and come in person; here Kama himself forgets his bow and arrows before the adorned bride and hides.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The poem gives the hero the god's looks, then lets the bride overcome the god, which sets her beauty above the hero's.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2442","b":"n:48","type":"tension","note":"The dresser draws the creeper of desire in sandal paste on the bride's breasts; the Naladiyar asks whether those who praise sandal and garlands have not seen vultures tear the body when the axle of its cart breaks.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The painted creeper is told as a high art at its best, so the Naladiyar's corpse image is shown to answer not coarse adornment but the finest.","gn":["the other side of the sandal-paste art, the body that lies under it, which the tender scene never looks at","a worked scene of sandal and garland laid on with great skill, which shows what its warning is aimed at and why it needs saying"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2448","b":"t:204","type":"qualifies","note":"The narrator says her adorned beauty would bewilder even the steady; the Tirumanthiram tells the heart to turn from women who smile and show their breasts, as the etti tree's fruit is good to look at and not to eat.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The narrator and the Saiva teacher agree that adorned beauty overcomes the heart, but the narrator offers it as wonder and the teacher as a poison to resist.","gn":["a remedy, the turning away of the heart, which the narrator names as a sickness but does not prescribe for","an instance of beauty so dressed and so praised that the warning is tested against the strongest case, not left as a general rule"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2447","b":"n:17","type":"tension","note":"The bangled women stand desiring the bride's eyes, breasts, hips and hair; the Naladiyar tells the reader not to desire a woman for her spear-like eyes, for she too will stoop and walk with a staff for eyes.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The gaze that the epic lingers on is the one the Naladiyar answers with the staff of old age, giving the scene's longing a time it will pass through.","gn":["a limit of time on the beauty so intently looked at, which the scene itself does not offer","a particular pair of eyes, hips and hair worth the longing the quatrain says to resist, so its warning has something to weigh against"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2452","b":"cc:2451","type":"develops","note":"After the ornaments cry that Death has come, the chowries, sandal fans and white parasol hide the girl for fear of what the graceless would do to the rolling world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The screening is the household's answer to the danger the ornaments announce, so the verse before it is read as a threat to be guarded against and not praise only.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2460","b":"cc:26","type":"develops","note":"Verse 26 in the opening summary has Civakan winning the nectar-like beauty of his maternal uncle's daughter; 2460 brings that daughter, from Govindan's mountain, to the sacrificial hall to be given.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem's opening promise of this marriage is kept here with Vedic rite and a king's gift-water, so the reader sees the match as one planned from the start with the uncle's daughter given by her father's house.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2477","b":"cc:29","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 29 says Civakan will no longer want the pleasure of women; 2477 shows the pleasure he had in marriage in its fullest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The full wedding night shows what the hero gave up at the end, so his later renunciation of pleasure counts for more than a hero who never knew it.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2461","b":"k:1146","type":"same_image","note":"In Kural 1146 the woman says gossip spread like the serpent seizing the moon after one sight of him; in 2461 the shy bride's hidden face is the moon in the serpent's grasp.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same moon-and-serpent figure stands for exposure in the Kural and for modest hiding in the epic, so the figure is a flexible one for a face that is partly seen.","gn":["The bride's shyness gains a ready sign of what is hidden, a shame that the Kural's woman does not have because her secret is out.","The Kural's couplet gains a contrast in which the same image means a lawful, guarded display, not a scandal."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2463","b":"s:372","type":"tension","note":"The wedding verses lay out darbha, sticks and ghee for the fire with care; siddhar 372 lists observances, vows, the Vedas, fire rites and water offerings as among the outward forms the Siddhar puts aside.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"A Jain poem makes the fire rite the centre of a happy wedding while the Siddhar counts the same fire rite among forms to be left behind, so what counts as a sacred act depends on whether the aim is a household or release.","gn":["The rite's careful detail gains a sceptic who judges all such rites as outward.","The Siddhar's list gains a case of the rite performed for a wedding, a purpose the Siddhar does not address."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2466","b":"t:551","type":"tension","note":"2466 reports the fire rising right as an omen of fortune; Tirumanthiram 551 says the way of sacrifice has no way through while the eight-limbed yoga path leads to samadhi.","grade":"forced","better":"agrees","te":"The epic reads the fire as a sign of worldly fortune, while Tirumular reads the same fire path as a dead end for release, so the same rite is a good for the household and no good for release.","gn":["The omen of fortune gains a view that the fire gives no release.","Tirumular's dismissal gains a scene in which the fire's use is worldly luck and not a claim to release."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2467","b":"n:23","type":"qualifies","note":"In 2467 drums, conches and kings roar as the bridegroom takes the water; Naladiyar 23 says the same wedding drums can sound as funeral drums that very day.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The roar of the wedding drums sets Naladiyar's warning on how soon joy turns, and the epic's later turn to renunciation is its own answer.","gn":["The wedding's loud joy gains the reminder that it may not last.","The quatrain gains a full wedding in which the drums actually sound."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2477","b":"k:1251","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1251 says desire's axe breaks the door of restraint barred with shame; 2477 shows the bride's shame giving way within a sanctioned marriage.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural treats the fall of shame as a defeat, while the epic shows it fall inside a rite-bound marriage, so shame is given up here without the loss the Kural fears.","gn":["The bride's drained state gains a rule against which shame's giving way is not a fault.","The Kural's couplet gains a case in which the bar of shame lifts lawfully."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2482","b":"k:553","type":"tension","note":"The bride's ornaments protest that the 'king of kings' turned cruel-sceptred will not look into or hear their clamour (ஆய்ந்து கேட்டு மருளான்); the couplet says the ruler who does not examine daily and do justice loses his land.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poet borrows the courtroom picture of the deaf king who spurns a plaint to make a lovers' jest, which shows how familiar the Kural's failed-hearing image was as a figure.","gn":["the jest gets its edge from a standard of judging that the verse never states and the couplet supplies","the couplet is shown being used by a poet to joke about, not to condemn, the king who will not hear, which its own grave tone cannot show"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2502","b":"n:120","type":"tension","note":"The wedding dresses the bride in a necklace that seems to own her body; the quatrain says nothing goes with a person but their deeds, and the body they guarded and adorned is no use when Death seizes it.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Set beside the quatrain's warning, the wedding's lavish adornment of a body reads as the thing the Jain moral is aimed at, which the festive verse itself does not say.","gn":["the adornment gains a counter-voice that questions what the necklace and razor-work are for","the quatrain gains a concrete, joyous scene of body-adorning to push against, where it only names the body in general"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2490","b":"cc:26","type":"develops","note":"The patikam promises that Civakan wins the maternal uncle's red-eyed daughter; here he sits beside the spear-eyed bride on the wedding couch.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam's one-line promise is cashed here as a full wedding in which the groom is shaved, bathed and seated, so the reader sees what 'winning her' came to in rite.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2502","b":"cc:27","type":"tension","note":"The wedding spends its verses on adorning the body and delights of marriage; the patikam says that Civakan, afraid of kingship, hears dharma and renounces at the Knower's feet.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The lavish bodily pleasure of this marriage is the very life that the patikam says Civakan will at the end put down, so each wedding adds weight to the later renunciation.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2506","b":"k:1320","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1320 has the sulking woman accuse a man who only sits gazing at her of thinking of someone else; here Civakan's unblinking gaze and watering eyes make Ilakkanai say he weeps thinking of others.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows that the suspicion in the couplet can grow from the very rapture of the gaze, so the quarrel is a love's misreading and not a sign of a wandering heart.","gn":["the sulk is placed in the Kural's recognised pattern of a wife who suspects her husband for what he does with his eyes","a story in which the suspicion is false and ends in laughter, which the couplet does not tell"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2509","b":"k:1329","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1329 has the man wish the sulk would go on and the night lengthen so he might keep pleading; Civakan, pleading through the bees, calls her anger the fruit of his own past sin.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Where the couplet relishes the plea as part of love's game, the epic lets the husband feel the quarrel as an undeserved punishment, so the same scene reads as pleasure or as pain by the speaker's mood.","gn":["his plea is shown to be, in the Kural's terms, a stage of love's play that he has not yet found pleasing","the cost of the pleading, which the couplet leaves out, the fear that one's love is being punished for a past wrong"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2513","b":"k:1327","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1327 says the one who yields in a lovers' quarrel wins; Civakan yields first, but the quarrel ends when the parrot threatens to hang itself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Yielding does not by itself end the sulk in the epic; it takes the comic shock from a third party to break it, which limits the couplet's rule that the loser wins.","gn":["Civakan's earlier abasement is shown to be only half of what wins her, as the Kural's rule would suggest","a case where a rule about the two lovers alone needs an outside agent, here the pet"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2515","b":"n:384","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 384 praises a wife who knows when to sulk and relents sweetly; Ilakkanai relents only when the birds' scene makes her laugh, and gives the smile as her reward.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's wife relents of her own judgment, while the epic's wife is brought round by a joke at her own expense, so the ideal is softened by what the story shows of how quarrels actually end.","gn":["Ilakkanai's smile is read as the sweet relenting the quatrain prizes in a wife","the quatrain's ideal is given a scene that shows what makes a sulk melt"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2513","b":"cc:1000","type":"same_image","note":"Gunamalai's red-beaked parrot, her only refuge, was made her go-between in love; here the household parrot, with its mock death, becomes the go-between that ends a lovers' quarrel.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The parrot appears again as the love-story's helper, but at Gunamalai's side it carries a plea across a distance while at Ilakkanai's it mends a quarrel within the bed-chamber.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2522","b":"cc:2326","type":"develops","note":"2326 had the victor bathed as for anointing with the blood of crowned kings, joined to the Earth goddess; now, riding Culamani, he treads on the crowns of kings.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The anointing promised in the battle's aftermath is fulfilled in the procession, where the defeated and allied kings' crowns are laid beneath the hero as a public sign of his rule.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2524","b":"cc:2327","type":"develops","note":"After the victory, 2327 had him enter the old city worshipped by its people; now, wedded, he rides into the street among kings with whisks and drums.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The victor's private return becomes a king's state procession, with the same city now seeing him as a husband and sovereign.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2534","b":"k:553","type":"qualifies","note":"The verse measures a breast's shrinking against a king who will not hear justice; Kural 553 says such a king loses his land day by day.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The poet treats the sceptre's decline as a stock yardstick to measure the shape of a body, so the epic's court ethic works as common knowledge rather than argument.","gn":["A known doctrine of the king's loss that makes the simile of shrinking legible without explanation.","A sign the couplet's rule was assumed outside moral discourse, used even in description of a woman's body."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2535","b":"k:752","type":"qualifies","note":"The verse says the people honour only the rich and the poor have no kin, using it as a simile; Kural 752 states that everyone scorns the poor and honours the rich.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural states the world's partiality as a fact and the epic applies it as a simile, showing the poet treats it as shared knowledge and as a cost on the neglected waist.","gn":["A flat statement of the rule that the simile of the neglected waist depends on.","A case of the rule seen in an unexpected place, the jewelling of a body, not in money."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2549","b":"k:1100","type":"qualifies","note":"The women speak a desire and beg with their eyes, while Kural 1100 says that when two pairs of eyes agree, mouth-words are of no use.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's silent agreement of two pairs of eyes needs the other side's glance; the women here ask in words and eyes for what he has not yet answered.","gn":["A standard that shows the women's begging as one-sided and so not yet the eye-agreement the Kural names.","A counter-case where eyes and words both ask, showing the couplet's agreement needs two parties."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2548","b":"cc:812","type":"same_image","note":"The kings in the battle cried that Civakan's chariot was the peacock and he was Murugan; the women now call him Murugan's younger brother.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same god-likeness that struck the kings with terror strikes the women with desire, showing the epic fits one figure to two audiences.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2548","b":"cc:473","type":"develops","note":"In 473 his foster father Kantukkatan receives him lovingly as if Indra's son had come; here the women call him a lion fed by Kandukkan.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The merchant's rearing, hidden in the early books, is now the credential in the city's praise, showing his birth in a merchant house is part of how he is seen as a king.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2550","b":"cc:1291","type":"develops","note":"Padumai, after one look at Civakan, lost restraint, bloom and bangles; the women of the city now say they lose their bloom and bangles after seeing him.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"What Padumai suffered alone is now the whole city's lot, so his beauty is shown to unsettle a crowd as well as one girl.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2551","b":"cc:465","type":"same_image","note":"The people of Govindai's ilampakam said that the good mother who bore the lord must have done great penance and asked on which hill she did it; here the women of the city again set a mother's penance behind the son's glory, and name Sunandai as the great vower.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The street's guess that a hero's greatness is a mother's earned penance is first a wonder at his youth and is now a verdict on his rule, and the woman named for it changes from the one who bore him to the one who raised him.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2555","b":"cc:304","type":"develops","note":"At the birth in the burning ground the child shone like the sun from the sea, gem lamps drove off the dark and the place's deities bowed; the people now call up that same birthplace beside his rise as a merchant's son and kings' lord.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The burning-ground birth that was a picture of desolation becomes the first term of a turn of fortune that the epic offers as a reason to take up tapas.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2572","b":"cc:240","type":"develops","note":"Kattiyangaran took the land by a gift of law that was no true gift (வஞ்சம்) and gathered its wealth; Civakan now picks out all that belonged to him and gives it to Govindan, the uncle who stood with him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The usurper's hoard is not destroyed but passes to the kin he had wronged, so the plot closes the fraud of 240 by moving the goods, not by punishing anything beyond the man.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2573","b":"cc:945","type":"develops","note":"Civakan whispered the five mantras into the dying dog's ear, seed that frees one of former karma, and the dog became a yaksha; the friend whose story he now has danced and whose golden image he sets up is, if the friend is that god, the debt repaid for that mercy.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"A kindness to a dying animal is paid back in a rescue and then in the first public memorial the epic records, so the charity of 945 outlasts the dog's death in the city's own ritual.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2567","b":"k:70","type":"qualifies","note":"The world asks 'what penance must he have done' and the king repays the one who reared him with a queen's title; the couplet makes that very kind of question the repayment a son owes his father.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's repayment is a sentence the world speaks; the epic adds a repayment made in rank and land and paid to the nurse, so the debt to one who raised a child need not stay in words and is not only the father's.","gn":["the couplet's frame that the world's astonished 'what penance did he do' is itself the son's gift, which the verse leaves as bare talk","a case where the debt is paid in rank, land and the title of queen, and to a rearing mother, which the couplet's wish for a father's good name never reaches"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2556","b":"k:376","type":"develops","note":"Wealth does not come across seas and mountains without fortune, and may come home to those who go not a cubit; the couplet says what is not one's own will not stay and what is will not leave.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic draws the practical conclusion the couplet leaves unsaid: if wealth is the share fortune gives, the effort spent seeking it is better spent on tapas.","gn":["the couplet's flat rule about fate that grounds why effort for wealth is not the safe course","a seafaring merchant's world of crossings and sickness, which the couplet gives no instance of, and a use for the rule in recommending tapas"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2557","b":"k:269","type":"develops","note":"Poison, hiding and the elephant's tusks are the dangers the strong in tapas escape; the couplet says austerity's power lets one even leap past Death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the power of tapas at its limit and the epic lists the particular harms it turns aside, and adds that when tapas is spent no flight saves.","gn":["the couplet's claim that tapas power reaches even to Death, beyond poison and tusks","named perils (poison, the tusker) and the warning that spent tapas leaves no hiding place, which the couplet's bare 'even leaping past death' lacks"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2564","b":"k:3","type":"same_image","note":"Civakan lays his endowment at the feet of the one who walked on the red lotus; the Kural's opening couplets name the one who walked on the flower and promise long life to those who reach his feet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic names the lotus and puts a temple and a gift at the feet, filling in the figure the couplet leaves open, so it shows one tradition's reading of the lotus-walker as the Jina's temple god.","gn":["the couplet's idea that to reach the lotus-walker's feet is the aim of the faithful and not only a courtesy","a worshipper's act of endowing the feet with lamp, towns and chariots, and a reader who takes the lotus-walker as the Jina, which the couplet does not say"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2573","b":"k:107","type":"develops","note":"Civakan raises a golden image and a dance to the friend who wiped away his trouble, and that friend was once a dog who changed birth; the couplet says the friendship of those who wiped away sorrow is remembered for seven births.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's memory across births becomes a literal chain from dog to god, and the epic makes the memory public and material in image and dance where the couplet leaves it in the heart.","gn":["the couplet's measure that a rescue is owed memory beyond one life, which explains why a gold image is not too much","a case where the friendship does cross births and is kept up in public ritual, which the couplet has only as a promise"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2555","b":"n:53","type":"develops","note":"The people see the hero's birth in the burning ground, rearing as a merchant's son and the kings' worship and ask why one should not do tapas; the quatrain has the best renounce home, youth, beauty, repute, wealth and strength because none lasts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar argues renunciation from the failing of goods in general, and the epic argues it from one life in which a single man has held every state, so the argument rests on a case the hearers have seen.","gn":["the quatrain's list of what fails (home, youth, wealth, strength) as the reason that makes tapas the sensible answer to his rise","a named life that has lived through desolation, rearing and kingship, which the quatrain's list of goods never shows in one person"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2560","b":"t:2585","type":"qualifies","note":"Civakan says he will not be born again, having found the unborn one; Tirumular says that after he renounced and saw the light the lord of the gods set him here so that he would not die and be born again.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both end births by reaching a lord who is not born, but the Saiva verse makes the lord the one who sets the seeker free while the epic verse puts the claim in the worshipper's mouth and does not say the Jina acts.","gn":["the Saiva verse's mechanism, a lord who sets the seeker beyond birth, which Civakan's cry of certainty lacks","a parallel from a worshipper of the unborn Jina who rests his claim on reaching the feet, which shows how the end-of-birth language is shared and the cause differs"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2586","b":"cc:683","type":"same_image","note":"In 683 the dancer wrote a letter in figure, calling her streaked eyes the letter and her smile the friend who carried it; in 2586 she hands over a letter in the proper manner and he reads it.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The earlier letter of eyes only hinted; the written letter now makes the same appeal in words, which shows the epic turning a figure into a document, so her love must now be answered in the open.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2587","b":"cc:686","type":"develops","note":"In 686 the usurper closes on the garlanded dancer and strips her; in 2587 she recounts that, once she had bowed to Civakan, the cruel spearman threw her off the stage and jailed her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The letter gives the dancer's own account of what 686 told from outside, which shows the violence as something she carries as an injury against Civakan, not only as the usurper's crime.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2593","b":"cc:687","type":"develops","note":"In 687 the captive cried out unceasingly to Civakan, the bowman who took her maidenhood; in 2593 the king, now secure, answers 'how could I forget you?'.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The cry in the usurper's grip is answered only after the usurper's fall and a letter, so the epic shows that the king's help comes late, long after her call.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2594","b":"cc:676","type":"same_image","note":"In 676 her eyes and brows moved as she danced and the watchers were overcome; in 2594 the same eyes and brows play while the king himself watches, his garland opening.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The dance that once undid a crowd of strangers now undoes the one man it was aimed at, which shows how the same art, once open to all, has been turned to a single patron.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2598","b":"cc:684","type":"develops","note":"In 684 the dancer rejoiced that his hand had touched her hip and that the toil of bearing young breasts was over; in 2598 the ripened love is enjoyed as a harvest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The joy in 684 was only a promise made on being touched; 2598 shows what became of it, which is the epic's picture of love as a field worked for a harvest.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2598","b":"t:207","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 207 says that union with young women is sweet cane juice in the hand and turns bitter neem in the body; 2598 sets the same pleasure as a sweet harvest of love enjoyed in peace.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Set against the Saiva warning, the epic's closing image of the king resting in love reads as a stage in a life the epic itself will end in renunciation, not a conclusion.","gn":["a counterweight inside the poem's praise of enjoyment, to set against the epic's later turn to the Jina's path","a worked scene of the pleasure it warns against, drawn at its sweetest, which shows why the warning is needed"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2590","b":"pn:35","type":"qualifies","note":"Anangamalai pleads that she not be left outside the shade of Civakan's white umbrella, which she calls the shade of dharma; Purananuru 35 tells Killivalavan that his tall umbrella is not for shade but to shelter suffering subjects who may come to him for justice.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Sangam poem tells a king to keep his umbrella for the sufferer, and the epic shows a sufferer actually using it, which puts the claim to the test of whether a king's sheltering reaches a woman's private petition.","gn":["an older standard of the umbrella as the king's duty to the suffering, which her petition invokes but the epic does not itself state","a petitioner who stands in the position it only imagines, which shows what it asks of a king in practice"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2583","b":"k:545","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 545 says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who wields the sceptre by its nature; 2583 says that under Civakan killing and miserliness are banished and the land becomes like Uttarakuru, with fish leaping into the fishers' yards.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet's rule is carried to its extreme here, where just rule ends killing and scarcity as well as drought, but the epic offers it only as praise, so the couplet's causal claim remains unproved.","gn":["the Kural's measure by which the praise is read, rain and harvest as the sign of a just sceptre","a case where the sign is raised from rain to a golden age, which shows the couplet's limit: it names the sign but not how far a king's rule can reach"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2588","b":"n:371","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 371 says the friendship of harlots ends when the hand is empty, as the lamp's light ends when the oil runs out; in 2588 Anangamalai, a woman of the stage, is jailed and released and keeps worshipping a painted image of Civakan with no payment.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's rule is a claim about women of her station, and her story tests it, which shows the epic treating constancy as the person's, not the profession's.","gn":["a hostile frame that makes her constancy a deliberate answer to a standing charge against dancers","a counter-case in which a woman of the stage keeps love without income, which shows the rule as a generalisation about types, not a law"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2602","b":"k:101","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai says that no return can match those who ended her distress and gives them every delight; the Kural says that help given when none was given before cannot be paid back even by earth and sky.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural states that such help is beyond repayment, and the epic shows what a queen does about it: she gives all she has and still says the debt remains.","gn":["the verse's claim that there is no requital is shown as an ideal measure, with the Kural giving the rule that explains why gratitude must outrun the gift","the Kural's rule for gifts in need is tested against a person with a whole kingdom's power to repay, who still declares it not enough"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2603","b":"cc:302","type":"develops","note":"In 302 Vijayai grieves alone in the burning ground and has no fear; in 2603 she turns the place of her fall and rescue into a painted memorial on her mansion, with a town granted to her rescuer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The burning ground that was her lowest place is made at the end into a thing gratefully remembered, so the reader sees grief turned into a debt honoured.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2607","b":"cc:223","type":"develops","note":"In 223 Vijayai dreamed of a tree falling and a shoot growing with a crown for eight pearl garlands; in 2607 she tells the eight queens they came as that eight-garland crown in her dream and begs them never to part.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The dream of the shoot and the crown is shown fulfilled in the eight wives, and the reader sees that the dream's fruit is the Cintamani's marriage-chain, now held together by the mother who dreamed it.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2610","b":"cc:261","type":"develops","note":"In 261 the usurper planned to kill the king who was sunk in pleasure with his queen; in 2610 Vijayai tells her son that the king was swallowed by pleasure as the moon by the serpent and would not take counsel.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The ruin that the poet told as a plot is retold by the survivor as the king's own failing, so the reader sees that the epic gives both the plotter's cunning and the king's desire as the cause.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2614","b":"cc:299","type":"same_image","note":"In 299 the queen, riding the peacock machine, faints like a struck doe as the usurper's drum sounds; in 2614 the same machine is called the deed's peacock (வினைமயில்) that sent her away while he went to the sky.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The carpenter's flying peacock of 235-239 is named here as fate's instrument, so what was a clever device comes to be read as the working of deeds.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2616","b":"k:335","type":"qualifies","note":"Vijayai says no one knows how many days are given and that the dead cannot be recalled; the Kural says good must be done before the hiccup rises and stops the tongue.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural's urgency is made a mother's plea to a king at the height of pleasure, showing the rule's cost: it must be heard just when it is least welcome.","gn":["the verse's general lament about the span of life gets a concrete deadline of the final hiccup","the Kural's call to hurry is placed in a scene where the one urged is a king surrounded by wives and enjoyment"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2618","b":"n:30","type":"qualifies","note":"Vijayai says deed's force scatters us like clouds and there is no lasting fellowship; the Naladiyar says kin arrive unasked and leave wordless, leaving the body as a bird leaves its nest.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Naladiyar says parting happens at death; Vijayai says it is the working of deeds even in life, which is why she can ask her son to loosen his own attachments now.","gn":["the verse's cloud image of scattering gets the further picture of the bird leaving the body","the Naladiyar's kin-leaving is given a cause in deeds, which it itself leaves unstated"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2619","b":"n:11","type":"agrees","note":"Vijayai urges penance and giving before black hair turns white; the Naladiyar says the wise renounced in childhood, knowing grey hair would come, and the youth-lovers later lean on a staff.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows that the call to act before age is made both to a king in his prime by his mother and as a general rule, so the epic's mother is speaking the Naladiyar's counsel in her own household.","gn":["the verse's address to the young gets the image of the staff in old age that makes the urgency felt","the Naladiyar's general advice is given a speaker who has lost a husband and knows how fast it comes"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2620","b":"t:193","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai says the body is a fire of disease with life as ghee, and urges giving and good before Death leaves; Tirumular says give out the rice before the pot burns near, since the days slip away.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both turn the household fire and the measure of rice into a warning that gifts must go out now; the epic frames it as the end of a king's pleasure, the Saiva verse as ordinary housekeeping.","gn":["the verse's body-as-fire gets a plain rice-pot image for what is given up first","the Tirumanthiram's counsel to give is placed under a Death who drinks the household, with the Jain epic's warning of disease and awareness as fuel"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2623","b":"n:273","type":"qualifies","note":"Vijayai tells diners to think of austerity, not riches, as they eat milk-rice from gold bowls; the Naladiyar says the fool who hoards his wealth and dies is laughed at by the wealth itself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic says wealth is not a thing to rely on at the table; the Naladiyar says what wealth does to the hoarder, and together they show that gifts and penance are what the dying keep.","gn":["the verse's call to austerity gets a figure of wealth laughing at its owner","the Naladiyar's hoarder is set at a banquet table in a king's palace, where the temptation is not stinginess but ease"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2624","b":"n:1","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 1 shows men who ate six-flavoured meals served by a willing wife and then beg gruel here and there; 2624 shows a woman who drank boiled milk from a golden bowl and now holds out a wide bowl for alms.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Naladiyar's quatrain draws the rule that wealth cannot be counted on, and the Cintamani turns the same beggar's fall into a sight the preacher points to while urging the hearers to turn to dharma.","gn":["the sermon verse gets an old, general proof (those who ate richly and then beg) behind its single sight, so the woman reads as a type and not a pity","the quatrain gets a woman, her hunger and the posture of begging bent like a creeper, which it lacks as an anonymous list of eaters, and the sermon's turn from the picture to dharma"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2624","b":"cc:354","type":"same_image","note":"The woman of 2624 would give anything for boiled milk in a golden bowl; in 354 the good women offer milk-nectar in a gold vessel to Vijayai in the ascetic's hermitage, and she will not even look at it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The milk in the gold vessel is the same thing wanted by one and refused by the other, so the epic puts the beggar's lack and the nun's refusal on one object to show what renunciation means as a choice.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2625","b":"cc:303","type":"develops","note":"In 303 the woman who once went into sky-touching palaces like a heavenly maiden is left alone in a forest of white skulls and is said to be shown so that hearers may see sin; 2625 offers another woman in a half-covered rag as the same lesson.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"What the epic first set before the eye as Vijayai's own fall is later handed on as a spoken example, so the story and the sermon the story ends in draw on one fund of images.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2626","b":"n:13","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 13 shows those whose speech fails, who lean on a staff and lose their teeth, yet stay in the house with their eyes on desire; 2626 shows a youth in a garland and spear who comes to lean on a staff and drool.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The sermon shows the young man whose decline the Naladiyar describes only from its end, and so makes the same warning about desire outliving the body's strength.","gn":["the epic's youth gets the Naladiyar's charge that the old man who lingers in the house is blind to the road to safety, which the sermon only implies","the quatrain gets the garland-and-spear picture of the youth before, which it lacks, so its old man is someone who was once the Kama of women's eyes"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2627","b":"cc:27","type":"develops","note":"The patikam says that while kings paid tribute and his sceptre ran, Civakan heard the dharma and was afraid of kingship like a snake; in 2627 Sunandai says she will renounce too and Civakan melts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The turn the patikam gave in one line is shown here as it happens, in the answer of a mother and the dissolving of a king, not as a decision of his own.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2628","b":"t:153","type":"same_image","note":"2628 has the matrons go in palanquins to their renunciation; Tirumanthiram 153 has the lord of the town mount for the last time a palanquin of the burning ground.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One kind of vehicle is shown going two ways: a rich woman's palanquin carries her to a life given up while still alive, and the lord's palanquin carries him to the burning ground whether he chooses or not.","gn":["the epic palanquin gets the Tirumanthiram's irony that the ride of honour ends in the burning ground, so the matrons' ride reads as taking that last ride first and by choice","the Tirumanthiram's palanquin gets a counter-case in which the ride is boarded willingly before death, which its picture of the lord's last journey leaves out"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2632","b":"k:47","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 47 puts the householder who lives the household life rightly first among all who strive; 2632 has the nun say that penance is hard and that a gift sown with virtue yields fame and then the pleasures of the gods.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain nun grades the gift of the householder as a fruit of fame and heaven and keeps the harder penance above it, so the Kural's 'first among strivers' is put against a ladder of fruits.","gn":["the nun's rule that gift watered with virtue gives fame and heaven gets a rival ranking that puts the householder at the head of all effort","the couplet, which does not say what is striven for, gets one answer: for the Jain the striving is for fame and heaven by gift, or for more by penance"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2635","b":"s:19","type":"tension","note":"2635 has the women fling off garlands and ornaments and sow thorns on Kama's road; Siddhar 19 asks when the singer shall leave silk clothes, gold ornaments, pretence and evil deeds and desire the Lord's feet.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both strip off ornament, but one is done as a rite with no god in it and the other is a longing aimed at a god's feet.","gn":["the epic stripping gets the Siddhar's aim, the Lord's feet, which the verse leaves blank, and shows that its rite aims only at closing Kama's road","the Siddhar's longing gets a finished ritual, a fixed hall and day, where the song only asks 'when shall I'"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2635","b":"cc:350","type":"develops","note":"In 350 Vijayai takes off the tilaka, earrings, neck ornaments and the pearls on her breasts when she is received into the hermitage; 2635 has the women scatter garlands and ornaments in a prepared hall.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"What one forlorn queen did alone in a forest is done here as an ordered rite for many, so the epic shows renunciation first as a refuge and last as an institution.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2637","b":"k:280","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 280 says neither shaving nor long hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns; 2637 has the matrons pluck out their own dark hair and enter renunciation pondering the settled meaning of the Lord's book.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain rite of plucking the hair is joined to inward thought in the verse, which is the answer to the Kural's doubt whether the shorn head means anything without giving up.","gn":["the plucking is given a test, whether it comes with giving up what is condemned, which the epic verse supplies only as pondering the scripture","the couplet gets a rite that is neither shaving nor matting, hair plucked by one's own hand, which it does not consider"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2637","b":"cc:349","type":"develops","note":"In 349 Vijayai takes off the gold garland and necklace while her musk-dressed hair grieves; in 2637 the matrons themselves pluck their dark hair without a flinch.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The grief of the hair in the forest is replaced by an act done without bowing, so the epic shows the same hair going first with sorrow and then with composure.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2640","b":"cc:28","type":"develops","note":"The patikam says that his younger brother, friends and richly adorned wives all renounce the things of sense; 2640 shows the new nuns studying the Anga books and shining as tilaka of the order.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"What the patikam announced as a bare fact is shown here as a life of learning and equanimity.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2641","b":"cc:2357","type":"develops","note":"After the last battle Civakan bows to the Jina and flings out gold before putting on his royal ornaments (2357); in 2641 he takes flowers and touches his crowned head to the nuns' feet.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The crown is lowered twice, first before the Jina in victory and last before the women who have given up, so the epic marks the end of his kingship by whom he bows to.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2646","b":"cc:1915","type":"tension","note":"In 1915 Vijayai tells her son that his mother is Sunandai, always, and his father Kandukkatan; in 2646 Sunandai is called 'no mother' for leaving.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The mother whom the epic made the true one by love is accused of not being a mother when her vow takes her away from her son.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2651","b":"n:53","type":"qualifies","note":"The queen puts away pleasure for austerity; Naladiyar 53 says the best of people consider slowly and give up home, youth and beauty without delay.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic shows the quatrain's counsel taken by a woman at the court while her husband is still held by pleasure, which makes giving up one's own household look like a path that is left open and not forced.","gn":["The queen's austerity, narrated in one verse, gets a stated reason: the things she gives up are the short-lived goods of the quatrain.","The quatrain's counsel of quick renunciation meets a case from a royal house, where the one who gives up is a wife and not the head of the family."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2651","b":"cc:1946","type":"tension","note":"In 1946 the breasts of the sandal-painted women turn even those who think of release back; in 2651 a woman of the court puts pleasure down and goes to austerity.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poem keeps both outcomes in front of the reader: the same world of women that can undo a renouncer can also be left by one of its own members.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2661","b":"n:17","type":"qualifies","note":"The king wins the love-god's war in play, with garlands and streams; Naladiyar 17 says youth drops like fruit and the woman desired for her spear-like eyes will walk with a staff.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's delight at its brightest is the thing the quatrain tells the hearer not to desire, so the water-sport is set to be read against the old age it will bring.","gn":["The verse's triumph over the love-god gains a time limit: the women who yield are those who will one day lean on staffs.","The quatrain's warning gains a concrete scene of the pleasure it warns against, with garlands, powder and a king at play."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2667","b":"n:16","type":"tension","note":"The women dress, steam and garland themselves after the play; Naladiyar 16 likens the joy of youth to a lamb nibbling at the garland held out before the slaughter.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same garland that is the sign of pleasure in the Cintamani is the bait of the sacrifice in the quatrain, and the reader sees both uses of one object.","gn":["The garland-wearing at the end of the sport gains a darker reading, in which the adornment is what precedes the loss.","The quatrain's lamb and garland gain a courtly setting where the garland is a gift of love and not a rite."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2673","b":"n:48","type":"same_image","note":"The couch scene rubs off the sandal and tears at the garlands; Naladiyar 48 mocks those who praise sandal paste and garlands without knowing the body they decorate.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both pass through sandal, garland and body, one to delight in them and one to say the body is the cart that vultures will tear at.","gn":["The sandal rubbed off the women's breasts gains the quatrain's view of what lies under it, the body that decays.","The quatrain's sandal and garlands gain a scene in which they are being worn and spoiled in the way that it mocks."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2662","b":"cc:67","type":"develops","note":"In 67 women plunge into the country pools and their girdle strings break and scatter; in 2662 the defeated women in the king's pool give up their girdles and earrings to him.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The scattering of gold that the landscape verse shows as nature's wealth is shown here as a gift laid at the king's feet, which changes whose ornament it is.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2671","b":"cc:1996","type":"develops","note":"In 1996 Civakan praises Vimalai's breasts as enough to make the released fall; in 2671 the king, with women skilled in writing, song and dance, is called the love-god of the love-god.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poem moves from a lover's praise of one woman to the poet's claim for the man among many, the height of pleasure just before the hero's renunciation.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2671","b":"t:203","type":"qualifies","note":"The king is set above the love-god among women; Tirumantiram 203 says even those who hold knowledge are infatuated with women who bewilder and cannot change a mind once bewildered.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's compliment to the king and the Saiva verse's warning describe one hold, and the reader learns the praise is also a statement of how firm that hold is.","gn":["The praise of the king as the love-god's love-god gains a hint that those who hold such knowledge are no safer than the ignorant.","The verse's general warning gains a named king, a court and a chapter just before his own turning away."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2678","b":"cc:2679","type":"develops","note":"The sulk of 2678 is answered by the consoling words and embrace of 2679.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quarrel turns on one conceit: her sulk makes the moon a paled rival, and his answer keeps the moon and makes it pale before her face.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2679","b":"k:1118","type":"qualifies","note":"The husband tells his wife the moon pales before her face and thereby ends her sulk; Kural 1118 tells the moon it would be loved if it shone like her face.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The face-and-moon compliment is shown at work in a quarrel as a way to end a sulk, not only as praise.","gn":["The conceit of the moon outshone by her face has a well-known formulation in a Kural couplet.","A scene in which the same compliment is used to win a wife back from a sulk, which the couplet's address to the moon does not show."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2690","b":"si:8:1-13","type":"same_image","note":"The south breeze is sent as envoy to prepare the groves for spring; in the Silappathikaram's song the same breeze is the messenger who announces spring to the land.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The breeze as spring's herald serves different ends: the Cintamani uses it to dress the groves as a host's welcome, the Silappathikaram to bring the news to a Tamil land.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2691","b":"si:8:119-126","type":"qualifies","note":"Spring's feast-dance in the groves rests on the loosened bangles of forsaken women; the Silappathikaram's closing venbas make spring the command that sulking lovers unite.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same spring brings reunion for sulkers in one telling and shows the cost to women left apart in the other, so the season's joy is not shared by all.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2701","b":"n:17","type":"qualifies","note":"The women of the verse are worn and pale from love itself; Naladiyar 17 tells a man not to crave a woman's spear-like eyes, since age will bend her.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar waits for age to wear a woman's beauty, but the epic shows love's own use wearing it at the bed, long before old age.","gn":["The wan lips and thin shoulders read as the body's early decline, not just tiredness after love.","A young case of the wearing it foretells, where no staff or grey hair is needed to show the same loss."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2706","b":"k:61","type":"qualifies","note":"The sons learn arts, weapons and horse-riding at five; Kural 61 prizes children who come to know what should be known.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet names knowing as the child's worth, while the epic's lesson is the skills of a warrior's house, so what is 'known' differs.","gn":["A measure that asks whether the princes' skill in arms and arts is the knowing a father should prize.","A concrete household where the child's gain is skill at arms, which tests whether the couplet means moral knowing only."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2721","b":"cc:1435","type":"same_image","note":"1435 tells the listener not to gaze on pleasure like a sick old monkey; here a lusting monkey stands in an actual grove.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sermon's monkey of appetite becomes a scene a king may watch, so the lesson reaches him as sight, not speech.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2722","b":"cc:2721","type":"develops","note":"The mate who saw the pair in 2721 now speaks, rebuking the male and refusing his chest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The tryst of 2721 is answered here, so the wrong and its rebuke are told in two steps, and the injured mate's voice is heard.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2722","b":"k:1311","type":"qualifies","note":"The mate refuses a chest that has lain with courtesans; Kural 1311 has a woman refuse a husband's chest, calling him a philanderer.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural's refusal rests on a seen glance, while the monkey's rests on a proved infidelity, so the sulk is shown with a real cause.","gn":["A named, human form of the refusal that makes the monkey's speech a wife's quarrel rather than animal noise.","A case where the fault is real, which tests the couplet's sulk against a cause beyond being looked at."],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2722","b":"t:207","type":"same_image","note":"The mate asks who would take bitter neem after mountain honey; Tirumanthiram 207 says the sweet of young women turns to neem within the body.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same pair of sweet and bitter serves a wife's refusal and a sage's warning, showing the image works both for pride and for renunciation.","gn":["The honey-and-neem pair gains a second use, as the ascetic's verdict on pleasure, behind the wife's refusal.","A lived case of the image, in a quarrel where bitterness is what the mate will not accept, not the end of pleasure."],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2726","b":"cc:2614","type":"develops","note":"2614 tells in Vijayai's words how Kattiyangaran conquered the kings and sent her off by the peacock machine; in 2726 Civakan sets him beside the monkey who seized the fruit and himself beside the one who lost it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The old usurpation, told as injury in the mother's tale, becomes in the monkey scene an instance of seizing and losing that teaches the hero how little taking or holding is worth.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2727","b":"k:250","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural warns one who is about to oppress the weak to think how he stands before the stronger; Civakan sees that the strong seize the weak's wealth and draws not a rule for the strong but a reason to leave.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The warning that assumes a ruler who will listen is answered by a ruler who, having seen the same fact, withdraws from rule altogether.","gn":["the verse's remark on the strong and weak gets a moral measure it lacks, a rule against oppressing the weak, so Civakan's renunciation reads as a response to a recognised wrong","the couplet's appeal to a fear of the stronger is tested against a hero who owns power and finds that no fear or fairness but only leaving settles the matter"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2728","b":"cc:213","type":"develops","note":"In 213 the king says that pleasures and days of life are measured out for those who guarded penance; in 2728 Civakan names wives and children as the fruit of that merit and says the wind of sin will bring the tree down.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The earlier remark that pleasure is a measured ration of merit becomes here a reason to give it up before it runs out.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2728","b":"n:130","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 130 tells the heart that attachment to home and children is idle since nothing but good done goes with it; 2728 names the family itself as the fruit of that good and says it will be lost.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain makes the family a hindrance beside merit, while the verse makes the family a result of merit, so the reader sees that giving up the fruit and keeping the merit are different acts.","gn":["the verse's tree of good deed, which only reports loss, gains the quatrain's directive that the heart should stop clinging and put its hope in good done","the quatrain's contrast of home against good deed gains a case in which the home is itself the earlier good deed's yield, so renouncing it is spending, not rejecting, merit"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2729","b":"cc:233","type":"same_image","note":"In 233 the king's wisdom rebukes him for bathing in the liquor of lust and losing his senses; in 2729 Civakan names desire as toddy drunk on the tongue and turns back like an elephant to the goad.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same intoxication that unmade Saccandan and cost him his throne is seen by his son in time to leave it by choice, which sets father and son side by side.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2739","b":"cc:1245","type":"develops","note":"In 1245 the hymn asks who rose to the summit of the worlds and answers that it is the Lord; in 2739 the question is turned on the worshippers, who are those that wear his feet, and the answer is the honour they receive.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The question-and-answer form that named the Lord in the young Civakan's hymn is now used to name the reward of devotion, so the hymn teaches why one worships and not only whom.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2741","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 10 says that those who reach the Lord's feet swim the sea of births; 2741 praises the Lord who crossed the sea of karma and makes his praisers lords of the wheel under the three umbrellas.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet promises crossing the sea of births, while the verse promises kingship to the worshippers of a crosser, so the two disagree about what a follower gains.","gn":["the verse's reward of sovereignty is set against the couplet's promise of escape from birth, which shows that the hymn's worldly reward is short of the end that the sermon later takes up","the couplet's Lord, whom the Kural leaves unnamed, gets a form here, a crosser of karma under three parasols, and a stated fruit for those who praise him"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2743","b":"cc:1240","type":"develops","note":"In 1240 the young Civakan goes round the temple of the god who cut the ghati karmas and passes on; in 2743 he circles the Charana lord's shrine as an old king and stays to be taught.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The circling that was a stop on a road is here the last one, and what it leads to is a teaching and not a journey.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2745","b":"cc:1247","type":"develops","note":"1247 praises the god of the asoka as the one who ended karma that cannot be ended; 2745 asks the ascetic's grace to end the darkness of the karma Civakan himself did.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hymn's praise of a Lord who ended karma becomes Civakan's own request to have his karma ended, so doctrine turns into a personal petition.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2745","b":"n:51","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 51 says that sin cannot stand before austerity as darkness dies when a lamp comes, and returns when the oil runs out; 2745 asks for grace as a flame that will clear the darkness of the evil deed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain gives the lamp's work a limit, since the dark returns, which raises the question whether the grace Civakan asks is a lasting cure.","gn":["the verse's flame of grace gains the quatrain's warning that a lamp clears darkness only while it burns, which makes the plea more urgent","the quatrain's lamp of austerity is put into the mouth of a king asking for it, and is thus a gift sought from another and not only a thing one earns"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2745","b":"t:114","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 114 has Nandi cut away the tarnish and open the eye of grace so that a light shows that nothing can darken; 2745 asks a Jain ascetic's grace to clear the darkness of karma.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both ask a teacher's grace to clear a stain, but Nandi gives it as a lord who acts on the soul, while the Jain sage is only asked and is a teacher, not a god.","gn":["the verse's plea, which does not say how the darkness goes, is given the Saiva picture of a god who cuts the tarnish, against which the sage's role looks much smaller","the verse's image of Nandi's gift is set against a request made of a man who is no god, so grace is shown there as something that a teacher can only point toward"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2746","b":"k:543","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 543 makes the king's sceptre the origin of the books of the learned and of virtue; 2746 gives the ascetic the duty of telling the king dharma and leaves the king with the sceptre.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"Where the couplet makes dharma depend on the king, the verse has the king come to the ascetic to hear it, so the two disagree about which gives the other its authority.","gn":["the verse's two duties, scripture and sceptre, are put against a couplet that derives the first from the second, which exposes the ranking that the verse leaves open","the couplet's claim that the sceptre is the source of dharma gains a story in which the sceptre's holder goes to be taught and leaves the sceptre"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2748","b":"cc:2616","type":"develops","note":"In 2616 Vijayai tells Civakan in a few lines that we are born not knowing our days and that the past cannot be recalled; in 2748 the sage lists the rare body, its end, the pain of leaving and the release as the subjects of a full teaching.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the mother says as a warning is turned into the heads of a doctrine, so the reader sees which of her points the sermon will take up.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2748","b":"t:307","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 307 calls life and body a close support and the mind on Siva's feet the firm support that ends birth; 2748 lists the body hard to gain and its destruction among the sermon's topics, with release.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both begin from the body, which the Saiva verse treats as a support to be used, while the Jain sage announces it as a thing that is rare and must end.","gn":["the announced sermon gains a Saiva view in which the body is a means that is used on the way and not merely lost","the Tirumanthiram verse's body-as-support is set against a view that treats it as hard to get and bound to end, which adds the Jain sense of its loss to the claim"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2749","b":"cc:377","type":"develops","note":"In 377 the poet laments that the nectar that ends birth is rarely won and yet men pamper the senses; in 2749 the teacher shows the rarity itself, a human birth as unlikely as a drifting pole finding a yoke-hole in the sea.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The earlier complaint is given its measure here, so a reader sees that wasting the human birth is wasting a one-in-a-sea chance.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2749","b":"n:34","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 34 tells one who has this rare body to take its full fruit, as juice from cane; 2749 gives the odds that make the body rare, the pole in the yoke-hole.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's advice to use the body gets a reason in the epic's image of how improbable it is, and the epic's image gets a use to put it to.","gn":["the verse's picture of chance gains the plain duty the rarity implies, to use the body's fruit","the quatrain's word 'rare' gains a concrete measure, with a drifting pole and an unlikely yoke-hole in place of the bare word"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2754","b":"k:338","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 338 says body and life part like a bird leaving its nest; 2754 says the life may be lost even before it leaves the womb, as a bubble bursts.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's picture begins at the nest-leaving; the epic starts earlier, with the embryo itself already at risk, so the loss is not only a late one.","gn":["the verse's tale of the embryo gains the bird-and-nest image of parting that gives the loss its shape","the couplet's parting of body and life gains an earlier start, in the womb, so the risk is shown to begin at conception"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2758","b":"n:25","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 25 says the man who sees kin wailing at a corpse and yet marries and expects joy is answered by the funeral drum; 2758 shows men in the heart of pleasure cut off by Death while the living beat their faces.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain shows the lesson refused, the epic shows the death that teaches it, and together the reader sees that pleasure and mourning are the same scene seen at different times.","gn":["the verse's death in pleasure gains the quatrain's voice of the one who watches and still does not learn","the quatrain gains the verse's scene of pleasure at its height, with the jewelled women and their anklets, so the cost of ignoring the drum is made concrete"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2762","b":"n:33","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 33 sets the fool who sighs when the fruit of past deeds arrives against the wise who accept it; 2762 says the evildoer sows deeds in the soul and eats their harvest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain names the response to the fruit, the epic names how the fruit is formed, so karma is shown as both cause and the way to meet it.","gn":["the verse's farming image of sowing and eating gains the quatrain's wise way of facing the harvest, which the hell scenes never show","the quatrain's 'fruit of past deeds' gains the hell-sermon's scale, which shows how severe that fruit can be"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2762","b":"t:2133","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular says the subtle body eats the fruit in heaven and hell and then takes another body with the old deeds following; 2762 says the evildoer eats the harvest of deeds sown in the soul.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both let the deeds ripen as an experience of hell, but the Saiva verse carries the sufferer on into a new birth by a subtle body, which the Jain verse does not say here.","gn":["the verse's farming image of deeds gains the Saiva account of what survives to carry the deeds, the subtle body","the Tirumanthiram verse gains a vivid account of what 'eating the fruit' means, in the hell scenes that follow"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2763","b":"cc:347","type":"develops","note":"In 347 Vijayai's distress comes because a deed of a former birth stood waiting; in 2763 the same ripening is told of all who do evil, with fruits dropping from the palm.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The single case of the queen's fate and the general teaching that deeds come to fruit are placed side by side, so the reader sees the sermon as the rule behind a plot he already saw.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2764","b":"t:198","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 198 has Death's servants bind and drive the killers into fire-mouthed hell; in 2764 the hell-beings rise and fall on the blades with no jailer named.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Saiva verse puts Yama's servants in charge, while the Jain verse leaves the pain to the deeds and the place, so the two show different views of who runs hell.","gn":["the verse's hell gains the contrast of a judge's servants at work, which brings out its own lack of any","the Tirumanthiram verse gains a hell without servants as a rival picture, in which the ground itself does the work"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2766","b":"s:16","type":"qualifies","note":"Siddhar 16 hopes to avoid being called a sinner and falling into the barren hell, by knowing the breath-thread; 2766 shows what that fall would be, the stake and the fire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar's wish to avoid hell gains a picture of what he flees, and the epic's picture of hell gets a way out the Siddhar names, knowledge of the breath rather than the Jain sermon's virtue.","gn":["the verse's torture gains a different way to avoid it, by a yogic knowing, in place of conduct","the Siddhar's hope gains a precise picture of the hell, so the escape is not a vague wish"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2769","b":"n:84","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 84 asks the adulterer what pleasure he had, since the act brings disgrace, a cut leg and long hell; 2769 sets the adulterer in hell with a glowing copper woman in his arms.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain names the price of the act and the epic shows the price paid, so the pleasure is seen turning into the pain it earned.","gn":["the verse's embrace gains the quatrain's question about what pleasure was gained, which turns the scene into an argument","the quatrain's phrase 'long hell' gains a specific image, the copper woman"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2770","b":"k:255","type":"develops","note":"Kural 255 says that if one eats flesh hell does not open its mouth; 2770 shows the killers of beasts and fish thrown daily into a seething pit.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet says that hell waits, and the epic shows what its mouth holds, so the reader sees the rule enforced by the pain of the killer, not by a judge's word.","gn":["the verse's hell for killers gains the couplet's plain rule that eating flesh is itself the reason","the couplet's 'hell' gains the epic's concrete view of the pit that waits for killers"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2770","b":"cc:2764","type":"develops","note":"2764 shows hell-beings rising and falling on blades; 2770 shows that the same beings are tormented daily by others who throw them in a boiling pit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sermon moves from the bare place of hell to those who work in it, so a reader sees that hell has both a ground that hurts and agents who add to the hurt.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2774","b":"t:198","type":"develops","note":"2774 gives hell's tortures by molten copper and the oil-press without a cause; Tirumanthiram 198 binds those who urged 'kill, stab' and sets them in fire-mouthed hell.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Together they show hell as the fruit of a named sin and as a daily mechanical punishment, which neither shows alone.","gn":["the verse names no crime for its tortures, and gains the sin of urging killing","198 gives only a fiery pit, and gains the machines and repeated daily torment"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2776","b":"cc:253","type":"develops","note":"253 names killing and eating flesh among the sins that sink into leprosy and hell; 2776 names the killers and the deniers of the next world as those who go.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The reader sees the Cintamani widen the list of sinners from conduct (253) to belief, since the one who denies the next world is sent there too.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2776","b":"k:321","type":"develops","note":"2776 sends those bent on killing into hell on the cars of their sins; Kural 321 says killing brings all other evil deeds with it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states killing as the root of other wrongs, and the epic adds the journey to hell as its end.","gn":["the verse gains a rule naming killing as the first of all sins, which it presupposes","the couplet gains a picture of where such deeds lead, which it leaves unsaid"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2780","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"2780 attacks the sacrifice of a lamb for the gods' pleasure with the recompense in Yama's world; Kural 259 weighs a thousand oblations against not killing and eating one creature.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The epic condemns the sacrificer by his punishment while the Kural condemns by comparison of worth, so the reader sees two ways to refuse sacrifice.","gn":["the verse gains a measure of worth for sacrifice set against not killing","the couplet gains a consequence after death and a scene of blood, which it lacks"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2781","b":"k:257","type":"develops","note":"2781 asks whose eyes would look on the creature cut apart and wailing; Kural 257 says flesh would not be eaten by those who saw it as another's wound.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic stages the very seeing that the couplet only wishes for, and shows it fails when appetite rules.","gn":["the verse gains a name for what is being asked, seeing flesh as a wound","the couplet gains a scene of the wounded animal, where it states only a condition"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2789","b":"cc:943","type":"develops","note":"2789 sends those who revile ascetics and the sinful to animal birth; 943 tells a dog that, leaving its dog's body, it will gain a god's body through the mantra.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same Cintamani shows rebirth moving down by sin and up by holy words, so an animal body is not final.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2791","b":"s:41","type":"same_image","note":"2791 shows the body as a skin of filth swarming with worms; Siddhar 41 calls it a worm-nest from which excrement and urine flow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both teach disgust at the body, one to warn of birth, the other to leave the body for the feet of Siva.","gn":["the verse gains a goal beyond disgust, which the Siddhar names as the feet","the Siddhar song gains the womb and birth as the origin of the body's filth"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2794","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"2794 says the householders' wealth is a yoke of toil and pain; Kural 46 asks what one gains by going outside the household life if it is lived by dharma.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Whether household life is a yoke or the best path depends on the claim of 2794, and the two works cannot both hold it.","gn":["the verse gains the Kural's own defence of the householder, which it argues against","the couplet gains a counter-view that names the householder's wealth as the pain"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2796","b":"cc:27","type":"develops","note":"2796 says taking and guarding the earth is pain for kings; 27 says Civakan, fearing kingship like a snake struck by thunder, renounces after hearing dharma.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The hero's renunciation is the answer to this teaching, so the reader sees what the teacher's argument was meant to do to him.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2797","b":"s:63","type":"same_image","note":"2797 says the body is a grove burned by the fire of disease; Siddhar 63 says this life has no standing, like a bubble on water.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Jain teacher and Siddhar agree that the body does not stand, but one asks escape from disease and the other from rebirth by grace.","gn":["the verse gains a prayer for release, which the Siddhar adds","the Siddhar song gains a concrete picture of how the body ends, in disease"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2799","b":"cc:2619","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai urged her son, before bright youth lapses and black hair greys, to do hard penance and give; the teacher now lists the lapse of youth and the coming of old age as one of the standing sufferings of men.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The mother's household warning about youth is shown here as one item in a Jain account of suffering, so her urging reads as the doctrine put plainly for a son who might delay.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2799","b":"n:53","type":"develops","note":"The epic lists unmet wishes, parting, lost youth and old age as suffering; the quatrain lists home, youth, beauty, repute, wealth and strength as things that do not last and the best therefore renounce at once.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic names the pain of loss where the quatrain names its passing, and the quatrain supplies the action, renouncing without delay, that the epic verse leaves unsaid.","gn":["The epic verse gains the Naladiyar's practical conclusion, to renounce at once, which it does not draw itself","The quatrain gains the Jain account of why passing things hurt: wishes unmet and the loved parted from, not mere decay"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2806","b":"cc:29","type":"develops","note":"The gods watch the goddesses dance and still take their fill of fortune without being content; the opening of the ilampakam says the hero has lost his wish for women whose beauty makes the heavens bow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sermon's heavenly pleasures supply the reason for the hero's turn from women in the opening: even the gods' unlimited enjoyment does not satisfy.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2810","b":"t:12","type":"tension","note":"The epic gods, with fifteen days left, blink and see their garlands fade and grieve like those who ate poisoned nectar; the Saiva verse says countless gods have died while the one with the forehead eye stays.","grade":"forced","better":"agrees","te":"The two texts share the mortality of gods but part on the cure: the Jain verse leaves no god to rest on and moves the hearer to release, the Saiva verse moves him to the one undying Lord.","gn":["The epic gains a rival answer to the fall of the gods: an unchanging Lord to rest on, which a Jain account of gods without such a Lord lacks","The Tirumanthiram gains a concrete picture of how a dying god feels, the signs, the regret at wasted pleasure, that it only states in a line"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2811","b":"cc:27","type":"develops","note":"The poet announced early that the hero hears the dharma and, afraid of kingship like a snake struck by thunder, renounces; the sermon's lesson that all embodied life, even a god's, is suffering is the content of that dharma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"This unit is the first body of the doctrine whose effect on the hero the early verse reports in a line, so it shows what the renunciation was a reply to.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2811","b":"n:173","type":"qualifies","note":"The epic teacher says all who have a body suffer, even gods bowing to higher gods; the quatrain says parting, grievous illness and ruin come together to all who take a body and bids the heart cling to the wise.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The quatrain's advice to cling to the wise is shown to need the epic's wider claim, that no heaven escapes the suffering it names, before it becomes more than comfort.","gn":["The epic verse gains from the quatrain a remedy, to cling to the wise who see through birth, where the epic only states the diagnosis","The quatrain gains the extension to the gods: if even heaven is under suffering, no birth, however high, is a place to rest"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2812","b":"cc:511","type":"develops","note":"On the sea the merchants are told not to weep over the suffering ripened deeds have brought but to think on the flower-feet of the lord; here the lotus feet of the Lord under the asoka are praised and wished upon the head.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sea-scene told the crew to take refuge in the feet of the Lord, and this sermon names whose feet they are and why they save.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2814","b":"k:3","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's one who walked on the flower and his noble feet and the epic's wheel-king who walked on the open lotus and his feet which the virtuous praise; both give the lotus walker as the object of devotion.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic names what is learned at those feet, five, six and nine, where the couplet names only the reward of long life on earth.","gn":["The epic verse gains from the couplet the promise attached to reaching the feet, long life on the earth, where it states only knowledge","The couplet gains a lotus-walker with a doctrine, who teaches what is to be known, in place of an unnamed figure"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2814","b":"k:8","type":"qualifies","note":"The epic uses the same compound அறவாழி for the lord of dharma ('wheel of dharma' with வேந்தன், king, set against the Kural's reading of the Lord as a sea of virtue).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic's use of ஆழி with a king points to the wheel of dharma rather than the sea as the compound's meaning, which the couplet's other seas set against it leaves open.","gn":["The epic verse gains the couplet's claim that nothing else can carry one across, which makes devotion the necessary way","The couplet gains an early reading of அறவாழி as the king's wheel of dharma, tied to a doctrine of five, six and nine, not left abstract"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2815","b":"cc:2618","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai said that, like clouds struck by wind, we are scattered by the force of deeds; the teacher says that Indra himself and a small monkey alike eat what their deeds have made.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The mother's saying of parting by deeds becomes a rule with no exemption, since it covers heaven's king, so no rank of birth can be claimed as a refuge.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2815","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says the two deeds born of darkness do not cling to those who praise the Lord; the epic says that those who have understood both deeds and the Lord's scripture neither exult nor scorn, because every being eats its fruit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic sets the escape in understanding, in a world where even Indra eats his deeds, while the couplet sets it in devotion, so the two give different accounts of how the deeds stop clinging.","gn":["The epic verse gains the couplet's promise that the deeds can fail to cling, which it does not state","The couplet gains an account of the mechanism, understanding the two kinds of deeds, and the equal reach of karma over Indra and a monkey, that devotion alone does not give"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2815","b":"t:105","type":"tension","note":"The epic says every being from Indra to a monkey eats the fruit of its own deeds, with release by understanding; the Saiva verse says the Lord stands beyond the two deeds and is the seed of the world.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The two give opposite places to the divine in karma: for the Jain verse no one, however high, stands outside deeds, for the Saiva verse the Lord alone does.","gn":["The epic verse gains the Saiva claim of a Lord beyond the two deeds, a figure its own teaching of universal karma has no place for","The Tirumanthiram gains a direct rival: that the highest being is also bound to eat his deeds, and that knowledge, not the Lord's stance, ends them"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2822","b":"k:257","type":"develops","note":"The epic says those who eat the wounds of other creatures and honey with worms destroy their inner knowledge and are beasts; the couplet says that if men saw flesh as another creature's wound they would not eat it.","grade":"sound","better":"agrees","te":"Both call flesh another creature's wound, but the epic adds the charge that eating it destroys the eater's discernment, so the loss is the eater's own.","gn":["The epic verse gains the couplet's single image of flesh as a wound, which makes the grammar of its பிறவற்றின் புண் clear","The couplet gains an account of what the eater loses, inner discernment and the name of beast, rather than only what the eaten suffers"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2822","b":"k:280","type":"qualifies","note":"The epic defines sila as giving up what the great condemn, and the couplet says neither shaving nor long hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The two agree that sila is the giving up of what is condemned, but the epic's ascetics keep the plucked hair as discipline (2820) where the couplet makes the mark unnecessary.","gn":["The epic verse gains the couplet's insistence that the test of renunciation is what one gives up, not an outward mark, which tests its own list of marks","The couplet gains a Jain case where the mark, plucked hair, is itself an austerity borne with patience, so its dismissal of marks is not complete"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2823","b":"cc:2622","type":"develops","note":"Vijayai urged her son to give and said people never give a handful to the poor; the teacher now gives the reason, that a gift in good soil grows like seed and one in barren soil withers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The mother's plain call to give is answered by an account of why it matters who receives, so giving is a sowing whose crop depends on the field.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2823","b":"n:38","type":"develops","note":"The epic compares gifts to seed in good and poor soil; the quatrain compares even a small virtuous deed that reaches worthy hands to a banyan seed whose shade makes the sky seem small.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both make the recipient decide the harvest, but the epic adds the poor-soil case, where the gift is lost, which the quatrain's banyan does not show.","gn":["The epic verse gains the quatrain's picture of a tiny gift that grows greatly, which gives the good-soil half its scale","The quatrain gains the opposite case, seed in barren soil that yields little, which shows what a gift to the unworthy costs"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2824","b":"k:25","type":"develops","note":"2824 names what the ascetics do to quench the five senses (wither them, keep from four sins); Kural 25 says only that the strength of one who has quenched them is witnessed by Indra.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the strength of one who has quenched the senses without the practice, and the verse gives the practice without the strength, so the two together show both sides.","gn":["the verse's practice of withering the senses gets a measure of what it wins, a strength that heaven's king himself would witness","the Kural's quenching of the five gets a concrete discipline (withering the body, keeping from lying, killing, theft and lust) that its praise leaves open"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2824","b":"t:140","type":"tension","note":"2824 has the ascetic wither the five senses and so subdue them by effort; Tirumanthiram 140 says the senses come under control of themselves once the Lord is met.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"One text holds that the senses are conquered by the ascetic's own labour, the other that they are conquered by meeting the Lord, so the two disagree on who does the work.","gn":["the verse's effortful mortification is shown to be one of two ways to the same end, and the one that leaves out a god","the Tirumanthiram verse's claim that no effort is needed meets its rival, a school that does not wait for grace"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2831","b":"pn:134","type":"tension","note":"2831 says merit is measured by the gift as iron takes water in proportion to its heating, a gift for the next birth; Purananuru 134 says Ay is no trader who buys virtue with gifts for the next life.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The verse sets a gift's reward in an exact ledger, and the Sangam poem names that ledger as what its patron does not do.","gn":["the doctrine of measured merit, shown from outside, looks like the trade it is accused of in the Sangam poem","the poem's refusal of a 'trader's' giving gains a worked-out doctrine of gift-for-fruit that makes the refusal pointed"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2841","b":"k:222","type":"tension","note":"2841 says the middle sort of gift is paid by whatever one wishes in the land of enjoyment; Kural 222 says giving is good even if there were no higher world.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Cintamani ties each gift to its reward, while the Kural cuts giving loose from reward by a hypothetical.","gn":["the verse's rewards (wishing trees for the middle gift) are shown as one of the things the Kural's hypothetical sets aside","the Kural's hypothetical gets a full account of the other-worldly rewards it asks us to imagine away"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2842","b":"k:221","type":"qualifies","note":"2842 ranks gifts by the receiver, with a debased birth for a gift to the unrestrained; Kural 221 says only giving to those who have nothing is giving.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the needy as the only true recipient, but the Cintamani's sage names the restrained, so who deserves a gift is answered in opposite ways.","gn":["the verse's grading of recipients by restraint is set against the Kural's grading by need","the Kural's 'the destitute' is shown as one possible answer, where the sage answers 'the restrained'"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2845","b":"k:356","type":"qualifies","note":"2845 defines release's path as knowledge, faith and conduct together; Kural 356 says those who learned here and saw the real thing reach the path of not coming back.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural pairs learning and seeing, and the Jain verse adds the third (holding it in conduct), so the two give different recipes for the same release.","gn":["the verse's triad is shown as a larger version of the Kural's learning and seeing","the Kural's two-part path is given a third part that the verse insists cannot be left out"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2846","b":"t:117","type":"tension","note":"2846 says knowledge, faith and conduct together are a fire that burns the tree of deeds; Tirumanthiram 117 says the malas burn only in the sun's presence, as the sunstone burns cotton only in the sun.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both burn the impurity by fire, but one fire is the seeker's own three virtues and the other needs the Lord's sun.","gn":["the verse's fire of karma is shown to burn on its own, which the sunstone image says it cannot","the Tirumanthiram's sunstone gets a rival that claims the stone burns without a sun"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2847","b":"k:362","type":"qualifies","note":"2847 names what the freed soul has (endless knowledge, seeing, power and joy); Kural 362 says to want, want not to be born.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural describes release as an absence of birth and desire, and the Cintamani's sage describes it as a fullness, so one calls release emptying and the other filling.","gn":["the verse's release is set beside the Kural's, which describes it only as not being born again","the Kural's release by not wanting gets an account of what the one released then has"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2829","b":"cc:2622","type":"develops","note":"In 2622 Vijayai says that all who are born are greedy, never give a handful, and should give their wealth away; in 2829 the sage promises to tell what gifts to the worthy yield.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The mother's plea to give and the sage's account of what giving earns are two halves of one argument, one urging, one promising.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2840","b":"cc:2610","type":"tension","note":"In 2840 the pair are bound by the garland of desire in the pleasure that their gifts earned; in 2610 Saccandan, swallowed by pleasure, would not come out and fell.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Pleasure that is the fruit of merit is still a bond, and the story of the old king shows what happens when one stays bound.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2848","b":"cc:2619","type":"develops","note":"In 2619 Vijayai tells the people to do hard penance and give before youth lapses; in 2848 the king bathes in the sage's dharma-water and loses the stain of sorrow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The mother's exhortation and the sage's sermon reach the same hearer in the same ilampakam, and the sermon is what makes the king act on them.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2849","b":"cc:2846","type":"develops","note":"2849 has the hearer bid the people bow to the lord's feet to be clear of sins of earlier births; 2846 gave the sermon's release as three together burning the tree of deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sermon sets release as a burning of deeds, and the hearer answers with worship as the first step, so the doctrine becomes a practice.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2867","b":"cc:2861","type":"develops","note":"2861 has the swan chicks hide in the lotus and the prince find them; 2867 has the king speak of the pains of parting from kin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The play with the chicks is read back as an act of parting kin, so a pleasure-scene becomes a moral one.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2868","b":"cc:2824","type":"develops","note":"2824 names lying, killing, theft and lust as what ascetics guard against; 2868 to 2873 teach these to a prince as duties of a layman.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The four sins the ascetic keeps from are given to a layman with their fruits, so the rule is seen to reach a king's son.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2868","b":"k:321","type":"develops","note":"2868 says killing a life brings great suffering; Kural 321 says not killing is virtuous action and killing brings all other evil.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"One tells the rule and the other the fruit, so together the rule has a reason.","gn":["The verse's warning of suffering gets a plain statement of the rule behind it, that not killing is the chief virtue","The Kural's rule gets a scene, a king giving it to a prince who has just scattered the swans, to show who must hear it"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2868","b":"t:200","type":"tension","note":"2868 teaches against killing as a cause of suffering ripening from deeds; Tirumanthiram 200 names killing, theft, lust and lying as sins great as mountains whose giving up reaches Siva's feet.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same sins are shunned in both, but one pays out as karma and the other as arrival at a god's feet.","gn":["The Jain king's rule, which has no god to reach, shows the Saiva reward as a different end for the same practice","Tirumular's giving-up of the four sins, which says what is gained, shows the Jain view that the fruit is only the working of deeds"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2869","b":"k:293","type":"qualifies","note":"2869 says the true word lifts to the towers of the upper world and the lie to hell; Kural 293 says that after lying your own heart burns you.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural puts the punishment of a lie within and the verse sets it in the next world, so the two give two places for the same cost.","gn":["The verse's heaven and hell for speech get an inward witness, the liar's own heart","The Kural's burning heart gets a cosmos, in which what the heart knows is carried to a birth"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2870","b":"k:290","type":"develops","note":"2870 says theft is punished by kings and drops the thief into a fallen birth; Kural 290 says the thief loses his footing and the one who does not steal does not lose the world of the gods.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verse spells the two punishments the Kural gives in one line, in this world and in the next.","gn":["The verse's king-tortured thief gets the Kural's measure that the loss is of the very footing of life","The Kural's lost footing gets a picture, the elephant's tusk and then the fallen birth"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2870","b":"n:157","type":"agrees","note":"2870 teaches the giving up of theft and gambling to the prince; Naladiyar 157 says those of faultless vision do not steal, drink, scorn others or lie.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both put the vows in a list for a lay hearer, so the list of vows seems a common teaching form.","gn":["The verse's vows get a more general frame, the sort of man who keeps them, 'of faultless vision'","The Naladiyar list gets a speaker, a king who gives it to a son with the cost of each breaking"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2871","b":"n:84","type":"develops","note":"2871 says the one who goes to another's wife is like a bright sword over his own neck and falls into heavy suffering; Naladiyar 84 says if caught the leg is cut, the family shamed and hell follows.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both put the cost of adultery in a worldly punishment and a future one, with the same image of a blade.","gn":["The verse's blade image gets the actual penalties it stands for, caught, maimed and shamed","The Naladiyar's list of penalties gets one picture that holds them, the sword that hangs over him"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2871","b":"k:146","type":"qualifies","note":"2871 puts another's wife as a sword over the neck and heavy suffering; Kural 146 says enmity, sin, fear and blame never leave one who crosses another's threshold.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural lists four worldly costs and the verse gives one inward one, so the verse adds fear and the Kural adds blame.","gn":["The verse's suffering gets named costs, enmity and blame, and not only pain","The Kural's costs get the Jain view that adultery is also a deed that sends one downward"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2873","b":"k:283","type":"qualifies","note":"2873 tells the prince to abandon wealth got with lies, deceit and dice; Kural 283 says gain got by stealth seems to grow and then perishes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural says the ill-gotten gain will not last and the verse adds that the greed behind it is itself what to drop.","gn":["The verse's abandoning of greed gets a reason from this life, the loss of the gain","The Kural's lost gain gets the cause it leaves out, the lie and the dice that win it"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2875","b":"n:122","type":"develops","note":"2875 sets caged birds crying for their kin and warns the captors that they will be parted from theirs; Naladiyar 122 says those who cage partridges and quails will go as slaves to strangers in irons.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Together they show the same crime repaid in two forms, loss of kin and loss of freedom, which neither names alone.","gn":["the verse's warning of parting from kin gains a second, bodily form of repayment, irons and service under strangers","the quatrain's repayment for caging gains the grief of the caged, the birds' separation from their flock, that its sentence mirrors"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2880","b":"cc:2875","type":"develops","note":"2875 warns that those who cage birds will be parted from their kin; in 2880 the prince who heard the dharma sets the captured cygnet free in the pool.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The warning and the release show a sermon changing a deed within a few verses, which a rule on its own does not.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2876","b":"n:120","type":"develops","note":"2876 says the deed clings to the soul like a thread in a lotus stalk and follows it into each body; Naladiyar 120 says nothing goes with a person but the deeds done.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One gives the thread image of how a deed follows, the other the fact that only deeds follow, so each answers what the other leaves open.","gn":["the verse's following deed gains the plain statement that it is the only thing that goes with the person, body and wealth being left","the quatrain's 'what goes with a person' gains a picture of how it goes: an unbroken thread that burns as it follows"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2876","b":"t:117","type":"qualifies","note":"2876 gives the old deed as a thread that cannot be torn away and burns the soul; Tirumanthiram 117 says the malas burn like cotton only when the sunstone meets the sun's presence, and not before.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The Jain verse leaves the deed unbroken and the Saiva verse says what ends it, so the Saiva verse limits the Jain one's permanence.","gn":["the thread that follows through every body is shown to have, in a Saiva view, an end at the coming of grace","the malas that grace burns gain a rival account in which the burden is a clinging deed that no god is said to remove"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2883","b":"k:342","type":"qualifies","note":"2883 has the father tell the youth who wants to renounce to rule and pass on the kingdom first; Kural 342 says to renounce while there is time.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The Kural leaves open what counts as time, and the father fixes it as the time after the duties of a king are done.","gn":["the father's delay gains the Kural's urgency, which makes his 'first rule' a hard answer and not a mild one","the Kural's 'while there is time' gains a worked limit, the discharge of a kingdom's duties, that its bare phrase does not give"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2883","b":"n:11","type":"qualifies","note":"2883 tells the youth to rule first and give the kingdom to a son before renouncing; Naladiyar 11 says the wise renounced in childhood, knowing grey hair will come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One praises renouncing at the earliest, the other puts duty before the vow, so they mark two views of when the right time begins.","gn":["the father's order of duties gains the opposing view that foresight, not age, marks the wise and that waiting is the fool's course","the quatrain's praise of early renunciation gains a king's reason for delay, the kingdom that must be handed on"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2884","b":"cc:2885","type":"develops","note":"2884 asks whether the kin he cannot be rid of are kin or enemies; 2885 answers that they are neither, being strangers in past births and in those to come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The answer is a rebirth argument and not a complaint, so the question of kin turns on how many lives one counts.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2885","b":"n:30","type":"develops","note":"2885 says kin were strangers in past births and will be strangers in births to come; Naladiyar 30 says kin come unasked and go wordlessly, leaving the body like a bird its nesting tree.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain holds within one life and the verse extends the same estrangement across births, which turns a sad fact into a ground for cutting the bond.","gn":["the verse's no-kin argument gains the plain picture of kin arriving and leaving unasked in a single life","the quatrain's passing kin gain a reach into past and future births, and with it a reason to cut the tie"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2885","b":"s:55","type":"develops","note":"2885 has the prince reason that there is no kin and cut the bond of love; Siddhar 55 wishes to put kin away, like a lotus leaf on water, with the mind far off.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Siddhar states a wish for detachment and the prince acts on an argument, so together they show the wish and its grounds.","gn":["the prince's cut gains the Siddhar's image of being among kin without being wetted by them","the Siddhar's wish to put kin away gains the argument that kin were strangers across births, which the song never gives"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2886","b":"s:42","type":"same_image","note":"2886 compares the body to a doll that falls apart joint by joint when its pins are loosed; Siddhar 42 asks when the puppet taken up to match the two karmas will be thrown down.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the doll's falling as a mechanism and the Siddhar wants to throw it down, so one explains how the body fails and the other why it is to be cast off.","gn":["the doll's failing joints gain the Siddhar's reason for the body, a payment for two deeds, to be thrown down","the Siddhar's puppet gains an account of how it comes apart, each pin loosing the rest"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2887","b":"k:350","type":"develops","note":"2887 shows a clinging that holds like the monitor's grip broken by a single word; Kural 350 says to cling to the clinging of the one without clinging in order to let clinging go.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the means as a right object of clinging and the verse names a single word, so the means can be a sentence from a teacher.","gn":["the single word that broke the prince's clinging gains the Kural's account of why a word can work: it points to the unclinging one","the Kural's command to cling in order to let go gains a case where one saying did it"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2902","b":"cc:2901","type":"tension","note":"2901 gives Nandan the heritage of the army; 2902 declines it together with heaven and release.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Nandan answers a plain offer of a throne by ranking his brother's feet above every prize the work weighs elsewhere.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2902","b":"t:284","type":"tension","note":"2902 has Nandan turn away from release to worship a brother's feet; Tirumanthiram 284 has devotees who bow in devotion given release in return.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"One text makes devotion the door to release, the other makes devotion a choice that outweighs release.","gn":["the verse's refusal of release reads as a break with the devotional bargain, which gives the refusal weight it lacks alone","the Tirumanthiram claim that devotion earns release meets a devotee who does not ask for the reward"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2904","b":"n:42","type":"develops","note":"2904 calls the handsome body hollow and moves at once to choose an heir; Naladiyar 42 asks the viewer to look at a body whose skin hides its openings as a bag turned inside out.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain teaches the contemplation and the verse shows it driving an abdication.","gn":["the verse's bare word 'hollow' gets a concrete picture of what lies under the handsome surface","the Naladiyar's meditation on the body gets a king who acts on it by giving up his throne"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2904","b":"s:642","type":"qualifies","note":"2904 has Civakan act on the body's hollowness by handing on the realm; Siddhar 642 warns the heart that Yama takes the body from a golden throne and the gold stays behind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar only warns that the throne cannot be carried away, while the epic king acts on the warning and descends from it.","gn":["the verse's choice of an heir is shown as an answer to the death the Siddhar says takes the throne's owner","the Siddhar's warning to the heart meets a ruler who actually leaves the gold throne before Yama comes"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2906","b":"k:546","type":"develops","note":"2906 tells the heir to guard the earth with the sceptre unbent and calls the guarding of dharma a king's highest joy; Kural 546 says the unbent sceptre, not the spear, wins victory.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural says the straight sceptre wins, and the epic gives the heir a motive beyond winning, that it is the best joy a king can have.","gn":["the verse's unbent sceptre gets a stated effect, victory, that it does not name","the Kural's unbent sceptre gets a motive from inside the king's own heart, a joy of keeping dharma"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2907","b":"pn:184","type":"same_image","note":"2907 sets the elephant fed balls of ripe paddy against the elephant that grazes the field and perishes; Purananuru 184 uses the same two elephants for a king who takes his due against one who grabs.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both poets reach for the fed and the loose elephant, and the epic puts the old Sangam figure in the mouth of a king handing on power.","gn":["the verse's image is shown to be an older figure of the taxing king, not the poet's own","the Sangam poem's counsel to a king gets a speaker who is himself a king instructing his successor"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2907","b":"k:552","type":"qualifies","note":"2907 says a king who takes revenue in measure keeps the treasure flowing; Kural 552 says a sceptred king's asking is like a robber's demand at spear-point.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural condemns heavy taking as robbery, and the epic gives a prudent reason for lightness: the field that is grazed bare yields nothing.","gn":["the verse's advice to take in measure gains a moral name for the opposite, robbery under a sceptre","the Kural's charge of robbery gains a worldly argument that the grabbing king ruins his own treasury"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2908","b":"pn:186","type":"develops","note":"2908 says that of rice and water, both life, the king is the world's good life; Purananuru 186 says neither rice nor water is life, the wide world lives by its king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem makes the king life by denying that rice and water are, while the epic keeps them as lives and adds the king beside them.","gn":["the verse's ranking of the king above rice and water is shown as a traditional claim","the Sangam poem's flat denial that rice or water is life gets a gentler form in which they remain lives and the king is a further one"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2909","b":"pn:185","type":"same_image","note":"2909 compares a king to a driver who must know the ground his chariot runs on; Purananuru 185 compares the realm to a cart that sinks if its driver cannot steer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both set a skilled driver against one who does not know the way, and the epic ties the skill to knowing the land.","gn":["the verse's chariot is shown to belong to an old comparison of kingship with driving","the Sangam poem's cart gains the condition that the driver must know the ground, not only steer"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2910","b":"k:590","type":"develops","note":"2910 says learned kings put away anger with mercy and keep what they desire hidden; Kural 590 says a king who honours a spy openly lets out his own secret.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural treats concealment as a rule of spycraft, and the epic treats it as a trained temper in which anger is put away and wishes are kept back.","gn":["the verse's hiding of desire gains a working case in which showing favour betrays a secret","the Kural's rule gains a temperament behind it, mercy that has put anger aside"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2911","b":"k:292","type":"tension","note":"2911 forbids a king even one false syllable for fear of losing fame; Kural 292 allows falsehood to stand as truth if it yields good free of fault.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One text bars all falsehood from the king, the other lets a blameless good justify it.","gn":["the verse's total ban is shown to be stricter than the Kural's exception allows","the Kural's exception gains a counter-case in which a king's word is watched by all and cannot be bent"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2912","b":"cc:2904","type":"develops","note":"2904 orders the youth who will guard the world brought; 2912 shows the king dressing a youth and making him crown prince.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The call for a successor is answered by the king's own hands adorning him.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2914","b":"cc:2913","type":"develops","note":"2913 decrees that the boy become the great lord; 2914 crowns him and seats him among elephants.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The decree is kept in a crowning that shows the whole court's weight behind the boy.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2916","b":"cc:2906","type":"develops","note":"2906 tells the heir to keep dharma with learned poets; 2916 sets the learned, blameless sons of his father's comrades beside him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The advice to rule with the learned is carried out by the old king before he leaves.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2922","b":"cc:2906","type":"qualifies","note":"2906 calls guarding dharma the king's highest joy; 2922 turns to the women and asks them to hear the lasting good.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same speaker ranks the joy of ruling highest for the heir and then offers a different good to those he leaves.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2923","b":"cc:2918","type":"develops","note":"2918 orders the women brought without saying why; 2923 shows them fading once they read the king's intent.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The summons hides its purpose, and the women's withering shows that his intent is a farewell.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2923","b":"n:392","type":"develops","note":"2923 has the women wither like garlands on a burning vine when they read the king's intent; Naladiyar 392 has women without their companions hear the rain's thunder as a funeral drum.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Naladiyar's grief comes after the absence, while the epic's grief comes before it, from the husband's choice.","gn":["the verse's withering is shown as the same grief of parting that the Naladiyar describes, felt in advance","the Naladiyar's sorrow of parting gains a case in which the man leaves by choice and is not away by chance"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2929","b":"cc:2624","type":"develops","note":"2929 shows a man who never gave now begging in cold rags; 2624 shows a woman who drank boiled milk from a gold bowl now worn by hunger and cut off from her fortune.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The first pictures the beggar's state and names his past failure to give, the second pictures a fall from wealth, so together they show beggary as both the fruit of not giving and a turn of fortune.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2929","b":"n:107","type":"develops","note":"2929 says the beggar stands begging because he never gave; Naladiyar 107 says those who stand begging at great gates are living out their former deeds without saying which.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic names the former deed behind the begging, a life of not giving, where the quatrain leaves the cause as deeds in general.","gn":["the beggar of the epic gains the Naladiyar's plain statement that begging in public is the working out of earlier deeds, not an accident","the quatrain gains a named cause, never giving, for the former deeds that fill the begging bowl"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2927","b":"k:222","type":"qualifies","note":"2927 makes food to the hungry a well in heaven and guarding life a title to earth and sky; Kural 222 says that even if there were no higher world, giving alone is good.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the reward of heaven for giving, while the Kural says giving is right even with no reward, so the two differ on whether the gift needs a world to come.","gn":["the heaven-reward of the verse is shown to be an extra, since the Kural holds giving good without it","the Kural's no-heaven giving is given a school's account, Jain karma, in which giving does bring fruit"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2941","b":"n:1","type":"qualifies","note":"2941's speaker, who ate rich ghee-scented food, fears the begging that renunciation brings; Naladiyar 1 says those who once ate six-flavoured food served by a willing wife may end up begging for gruel, so wealth is no support.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain uses the fall to beggary as the argument for renouncing, while the epic's speaker uses the same fall as the reason to fear renouncing.","gn":["the speaker's fear of alms is answered, since the quatrain says such beggary comes anyway to the rich","the quatrain's warning about lost wealth meets a person who feels it and still hesitates"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2938","b":"n:46","type":"same_image","note":"2938 strips the body of its sandal and flower to fat, blood, pus and soiled skin; Naladiyar 46 lists entrails, fat, blood, bone, sinew and skin and asks which part is the woman.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both teach desire away by breaking the body into its parts, and the epic does it to the very women who are the audience.","gn":["the verse's list of fluids gains the quatrain's question of where the woman is to be found among the parts","the quatrain's analysis is placed in a scene where the one who is analysed is listening"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2934","b":"t:451","type":"qualifies","note":"2934 follows the embryo through the channel and into muddy water where it falls weeping without provision for the road; Tirumanthiram 451 has the Lord stay within the womb and form the embryo.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One text lets the body come into the world helpless and left to its deeds, the other has a Lord who forms it, so the same birth is read without and with a maker.","gn":["the epic's helpless birth meets a verse that says a Lord forms the embryo within the womb","the Tirumanthiram's Lord within the womb is set against a Jain account of birth with no one forming it"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2940","b":"s:23","type":"develops","note":"2940 shows the body's colour, breasts and eyes fading with age as the women watch; Siddhar 23 asks when the speaker will see the bones within as white bones and see himself lowered like a cast-off corpse.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic shows the body's fading from the lover's side, the Siddhar from the renouncer's, so one is a loss and the other is a practice.","gn":["the verse's fading colour gains the Siddhar's practice of looking through the flesh to the bones","the Siddhar's look at the bones gains a view of the same body as it loses its beauty to a lover"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2939","b":"cc:2616","type":"develops","note":"2939 says we begin dying from the day we are born and knowledge is useless when one shakes at the end; 2616 has Vijayai say we are born not knowing our days and cover our eyes only when the cow is milked.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The mother's warning in the first sermon returns in the king's own words to his wives, so the doctrine is passed down rather than invented.","gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2957","b":"cc:1068","type":"develops","note":"2957 recalls the rescue of Gunamalai from the elephant, which 1068 shows Civakan teasing her about in the early love.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The wives hold his old rescue against him, so a joke between lovers turns into a debt called in at the parting.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2958","b":"cc:1285","type":"develops","note":"2958 recalls Padumai restored after the snake bite that 1285 shows him curing with his spells.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The cure that made him a hero is cited as the proof that his love gave life, so leaving takes it back.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2951","b":"cc:1097","type":"tension","note":"2951 has the wives ask whether he will cast them off, while 1097 has him say he will not melt in grief and that even a sesame-seed of attachment is a fault.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"His own earlier teaching to weeping women is now turned on him by wives who ask for what he then refused to grant.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2962","b":"n:56","type":"qualifies","note":"2962 asks whether a husband may give up the wife he took, while Naladiyar 56 says a husband cannot easily put away a wife even if she lacks good qualities.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The wife's plea is that the bond cannot be cast off, which the Naladiyar says too but only to praise those who avoid marriage.","gn":["the wife's plea that a husband is bound gains the support of a rule from the moral literature that she does not cite","the Naladiyar's argument that marriage binds hard gains a case where the bond is felt by those left, not by the husband"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2960","b":"n:53","type":"tension","note":"2960 asks whether renouncing wives can be worthy, while Naladiyar 53 says the best renounce home, youth and beauty without delay.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Naladiyar's praise of the best renouncers leaves out the people left behind, whom the wives put forward as the cost.","gn":["the wives' question is given the opposing view that the best of men renounce what they love without delay","the Naladiyar's praise of renouncing gains the weeping wives that its list of things given up does not name"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2965","b":"t:157","type":"same_image","note":"2965 shows the wives stripping bangles and weeping at the king's going, while Tirumanthiram 157 has the wives and kin who wailed turn back at the town's edge.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The epic shows grieving women at a living man's leaving, where the Tirumanthiram shows the same women turning back at a dead man's bier.","gn":["the wives' grief gains a Saiva reading in which wives, kin and children turn back at the edge of town","the Tirumanthiram's turning back gains a living scene where the same grief ends in a king's choosing to go"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2964","b":"cc:1104","type":"same_image","note":"2964 and 1104 both show women beating and weeping at the going of Civakan, once into prison and now into renunciation.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same hero's leaving is wept over twice, first as a captive and then as one who goes freely.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2977","b":"n:9","type":"develops","note":"2977 compares the vanished smoke of incense and garlands to the fame of misers who gave nothing; Naladiyar 9 says the miser who guards his wealth is already counted as one who has lost it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the miser losing twice, his goods in the Naladiyar and his name in the epic, so hoarding gets a fate in memory as well as in wealth.","gn":["the simile of vanishing smoke gets its ground, that the miser is already counted as a loss","the Naladiyar's loss of the hoarder gets a trace in memory, his fame disappearing like the smoke"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2983","b":"k:333","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 333 tells the possessor of wealth to do at once what stays because wealth does not; 2983 adds that Fortune stays with the one who is not attached and never clings to the one who is.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural treats wealth's going as a fact to act on, while the epic makes clinging the cause of its going, so a reader sees what each assumes about why wealth leaves.","gn":["the verse's claim about Fortune and attachment gets the plainer rule that wealth by nature does not stay","the Kural's rule gets a reason for the going: wealth is lost through the very clinging that tries to keep it"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2983","b":"cc:232","type":"qualifies","note":"In 232 Fortune leaves the clinging king without a word; in 2983 Civakan says she never leaves the one who lets go.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The goddess of fortune who abandoned the grasping king in the first ilampakam is shown in the last to stay with the king who gives up, so the story's arc on wealth reverses.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2985","b":"t:258","type":"tension","note":"2985 calls the fruit of good deeds poison and says only crossing the flood of deeds gives bliss; Tirumanthiram 258 makes penance and virtue the boat that carries one across the sea of deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Both see deeds as a sea to cross, but one holds virtue's fruit to be poison and the other makes virtue the boat, so the two disagree on whether good deeds help or only bind.","gn":["the epic's claim that merit too binds meets a text that sets virtue and austerity as the crossing itself","the Tirumanthiram's boat of virtue meets the Jain view that its fruit is a poison to be crossed"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2985","b":"cc:2846","type":"develops","note":"2846 says the three jewels burn the tree of deeds and release is freedom from deeds; 2985 applies this to Civakan's wives, saying even the fruit of good deeds is poison.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sermon's doctrine of freedom from deeds is turned into advice to the wives, and it is made to include good deeds that the sermon does not single out.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2984","b":"t:149","type":"develops","note":"2984 sets wealth against the funeral rice and the wife's tears at the burning ground; Tirumanthiram 149 gives the man's public mansion, palanquin and three crores and then his silent going.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Tirumanthiram shows a life of building and giving with no look back; the epic shows what the household is left with, so together they give the whole before and after.","gn":["the funeral picture is set after a life like the man's, in which a mansion and palanquin were all in public view","the Tirumanthiram's man who did not turn back gets a household to leave, with a widow's tears and a funeral rice"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2987","b":"cc:2607","type":"tension","note":"In 2607 Vijayai begs the eight wives, who came in her dream as eight garlands on the king's crown, never to part; in 2987 they decide to renounce.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The dream's garlands that were never to part are parted by the story's end, showing the epic setting a mother's wish against the wives' own turn from the household.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2988","b":"cc:2727","type":"develops","note":"2727 has Civakan reflect that to subdue oneself in time, do penance and reach a world without affliction is better; in 2988 he speaks it to his wives and calls them to come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The reflection made alone in the earlier chapter becomes an invitation, so the resolve is seen first as thought and then as something shared.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2988","b":"k:346","type":"develops","note":"Kural 346 says the one who cuts 'I' and 'mine' enters a world higher than the gods'; 2988 calls the wives to cross old deeds and reach bliss at the top of the world above.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural names the pride to be cut and the height, the epic names the deeds to be crossed and the place, so each gives what the other leaves open.","gn":["the summit above the world gets a condition the verse does not state, the cutting of 'I' and 'mine'","the Kural's higher world gets a location at the top of the universe and a means in crossing old deeds"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2988","b":"t:219","type":"tension","note":"2988 has the wives cross old deeds by leaving the household and gives no god; Tirumanthiram 219 has the worshipped fire rise and burn the diseases of deeds.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"One text frees from old deeds by the renouncer's own crossing, the other by a fire that burns them when worshipped, which is the split between Jain effort and Saiva rite.","gn":["the call to cross old deeds on one's own meets a text where a worshipped fire does the burning","the Tirumanthiram's fire that burns deeds meets a rival route with no fire and no god"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2991","b":"cc:2607","type":"qualifies","note":"In 2607 Vijayai begs the wives never to part from the king; in 2991 he entrusts them to her as he goes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The mother who wished the wives to stay with her son is the one who now receives them as he leaves, so the wish is kept in a changed form.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2992","b":"s:19","type":"qualifies","note":"2992 has the wives lay aside their ornaments like a vine shedding flowers; Siddhar 19 asks when the speaker will leave silk and gold ornaments and desire the feet.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"The Siddhar's wish to leave ornaments is shown done by women who give them up, but without the god's feet as its aim.","gn":["the stripping of ornaments gets an aim in a god's feet that the epic's scene never names","the Siddhar's wish to leave silk and gold gets a scene where it is carried out, with the cost to those who give up beauty"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2995","b":"k:221","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 221 says giving to the destitute is true giving and the rest a loan with return; 2995 has the king give to those who ask in great want on the eve of leaving.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The king giving just before he leaves the world where any return could come is the case the couplet's test points to, so the epic shows the rule in its purest form.","gn":["the farewell gift is shown to be of the kind the Kural calls true giving, with no return possible","the Kural's test of no return gets a case where the giver leaves the world in which returns could come"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:2998","b":"cc:2628","type":"same_image","note":"In 2628 the matrons go in palanquins to renounce and the king is left; in 2998 he boards a palanquin himself and the gods carry it.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The palanquin of renunciation first carried the mothers by men, and now carries the son by gods, so the epic shows the same vehicle changing in meaning from parting to ascent.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:2998","b":"t:153","type":"same_image","note":"In 2998 the king mounts a palanquin that the gods carry; Tirumanthiram 153 has the lord of the land mount the burning ground's palanquin for the last time.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same royal vehicle is a bier in one and a god-borne chair to release in the other, so the reader sees how renunciation and death can wear the same procession.","gn":["the king's palanquin gets the contrast of the bier in which a king is carried to the burning ground","the Tirumanthiram's royal bier gets a counter-case where the royal palanquin leads to release"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3016","b":"cc:2555","type":"develops","note":"2555 has the city say that anyone who sees how Civakan was born in the burning ground, grew up a merchant's son and was worshipped by kings must ask why live heedless instead of doing penance; 3016 has the Jina tell the king that renunciation is the excellent thing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The people's question about heedlessness at the crowning is answered by the lord's own word here, so the end of the book fulfils what its hero's rise had begun to pose.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3017","b":"cc:2560","type":"develops","note":"2560 has Civakan circle the shrine and say he has been born and will not be born again; 3017 has a worshipper at the end say he was born in a fearful course and feared his deeds, and that those who circle the feet do not suffer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The coronation-day boast of no more births is brought to its ground at the end, where the fear of deeds, not the pride of a king, is what sends the worshipper to the feet.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3023","b":"cc:2563","type":"develops","note":"2563 hymns the lord as the one who understood without fault, the wheel that removes stain, and who set bounds to the senses; 3023 hymns the same lord as the sea of virtues and the wheel of dharma that clears hunger, hatred and disease.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same epithets, wheel and a sea of virtues, recur at the king's last bow, so the earlier hymn's titles are seen to be the book's one settled form of praise.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3016","b":"k:342","type":"develops","note":"Kural 342 says renounce while there is something to renounce, and many things come after; 3016 has the Jina tell a king, who has the most to give up, that renunciation is the excellent course.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural counsels renunciation in general and never says who it is hardest for, and the verse sets it before a crowned king as the hardest case.","gn":["the Jina's counsel to the king gets a general rule that one can renounce only what one still holds, which is why the king's case is the gravest","the Kural's bare 'renounce while it is yours' gets a concrete test, a ruler with a whole kingdom to give up"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3019","b":"k:10","type":"develops","note":"Kural 10 says those who reach the Lord's feet swim the sea of births and others cannot; 3019 says those who do not reach the lord are kicked by their deeds into the sea of sorrow and whirl like thread on a shuttle.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the crossing and the failure to cross but gives no cause or picture for the failure, and the verse supplies deeds that beat the swimmer down and a turning that never ends.","gn":["the verse's sea of sorrow is given the Kural's single condition for crossing it, reaching the lord's feet","the Kural's 'cannot swim' is given a cause (deeds that strike) and a picture (thread wound without end on a shuttle)"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3020","b":"t:105","type":"tension","note":"3020 praises the Jina as the master whose spoken word frees those who repeat it from suffering; Tirumanthiram 105 has the Lord stand beyond the two kinds of deed.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Jain release comes by hearing and keeping the word of one who went before, while the Saiva text puts the Lord beyond deeds so that deeds cannot reach him, and the two disagree on whether a liberated one also helps.","gn":["the praise of a teacher whose word wears away deeds is shown as a path with no god above it who removes them","the Tirumanthiram's Lord beyond both deeds meets a rival in which the one beyond deeds is a teacher who shows a way and does not grant grace"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3022","b":"k:3","type":"same_image","note":"3022 has the king bow to the feet that walked over the lotus with its bees; Kural 3 praises those who reach the feet of the one who walked on the flower.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both place the object of worship on the lotus, the Kural without a name and the epic with a figure the king can bow to, so the stock image is shown being used as a scene.","gn":["the verse's lotus-treading feet are shown as the stock image of the Kural's opening praise, so the king's bow is the act the couplet commends","the Kural's 'one who walked on the flower' gets a staged scene, a lotus in an assembly with a king bowing before it"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3022","b":"k:9","type":"develops","note":"Kural 9 says a head that does not bow at the feet of the one of eight qualities is as useless as a dead sense; 3022 has the king bow with his gem crown, ear-rings and bands shining on the ground.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural gives the cost of a head that does not bow, and the epic shows the most ornamented head in the book putting its crown to use by bowing.","gn":["the king's crown, ear-rings and garlands are given their one use, the bow, which the couplet makes the measure of a head","the Kural's useless head gets its opposite, a head heavy with ornaments that is not useless because it bows"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3023","b":"k:8","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 8 says other seas are hard to swim except for those who reach the feet of the one called அறவாழி; 3023 uses அறவாழி for the wheel of dharma and also calls him a sea of virtues.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural's single word அறவாழி can be a sea or a wheel, and the epic shows the lord named as both in one verse.","gn":["the verse's wheel of dharma and sea of virtues are read as the two sides of one name, so its praise echoes the couplet's term in both senses","the Kural's compound அறவாழி gets a use in which it is a wheel and the sea is named separately, so its single word is seen to carry two images"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3036","b":"cc:945","type":"develops","note":"3036 says the mantra heard at the hour of death brings birth among the gods; 945 shows Civakan whispering it to a dying dog.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The general rule of 3036 and the dog's rebirth in 945 together show the mantra's promise kept, since the dog returns as Sutanjanan who here praises the one who gave it.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3037","b":"cc:945","type":"develops","note":"In 3037 the god Sutanjanan praises Civakan's feet, whom in 945 he met as the dying dog taught the five mantras.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The god's praise repays a debt the dog could not repay: what Civakan gave a dying animal returns as a god's worship.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3027","b":"k:345","type":"qualifies","note":"3027 has the king take off jewels and garlands because the world is a drop of milk; Kural 345 says that for those cutting off births even the body is excess.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic stages the stripping as a royal act step by step, while the Kural makes the body itself the last thing to be let go.","gn":["the king's removal of ornaments is shown to be the first step of a series that reaches the body","the Kural's rule that even the body is excess gets a worked case of a king who has things to lose"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3030","b":"n:53","type":"agrees","note":"3030 has Civakan pluck his hair like a lion shedding its mane; Naladiyar 53 says the best renounce home, youth, wealth and strength at once because none lasts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic does what the quatrain counsels with the same word for leaving, but shows a king with all of them to give up.","gn":["the verse's act of tonsure gets the reason the Naladiyar gives, that none of these things lasts","the quatrain's advice to renounce without delay gets a worked case at the height of a king's power"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3033","b":"s:329","type":"tension","note":"In 3033 the bees mourn the cast-off hair and garland; Siddhar 329 lists a loincloth, a porch and leaves to eat as all one needs once ornament is gone.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Jain epic gives the loss of ornament a lament by those who lived on it, while the Siddhar counts it as pure gain.","gn":["the bees' lament shows the cost of renunciation that the king's calm stripping leaves out","the Siddhar's cheerful poverty is tested against those who lose by it"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3039","b":"k:30","type":"agrees","note":"3039 has the ascetics shed the milk of kindness on every life; Kural 30 names the virtuous, clothed in cool kindness to every being, as the true brahmins.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both define the ascetic by his kindness to all lives, not by any robe or rite.","gn":["the verse's milk of kindness gets a name for the sage whom it makes","the Kural's kindness to every living thing gets the practice of an order that has put away hatred"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3039","b":"t:497","type":"tension","note":"3039 has the freed ascetic reach it by wholly dropping attachment and hatred; Tirumanthiram 497 has the siddhas free of the bonds by becoming Siva and destroying the five malas.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Jain epic makes release the result of the ascetic's own dropping of passions; the Saiva text has it come by becoming Siva.","gn":["the verse's self-made release is set against a release by becoming the Lord","the Tirumanthiram's grace-based freedom meets a school that gives no role to a god"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3044","b":"pn:134","type":"qualifies","note":"3044 has King Senikan call for a feast to the Jina's feet with kings gathered; Purananuru 134 has a patron who does not give to buy merit for the next birth.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The king's feast here is made an act of worship whose fruit the next lines of the work will tell, and so the poem's patron who gives without bargaining is set against it.","gn":["the king's feast to the Jina gets a foil in a patron who refuses to give for reward","the poem's refusal of trade-giving meets a king whose gift is plainly an act of worship"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3064","b":"cc:2846","type":"develops","note":"2846 names knowledge, faith and conduct together as the fire that burns the tree of deeds; 3064 has Civakan cut the eight karmas himself by the firmness of his mind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sermon gives the means of release as three jewels and a fire; the life shows the one quality of mind that does the cutting, so release is both taught and done.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3073","b":"cc:28","type":"develops","note":"28 says only that Civakan goes to the ever-new state; 3073 gives the ending of the austerity by which he gets there, with his family waiting.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The opening summary states the destination in a half-line; the closing shows the cost, the family watching and the hand held back from food.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3051","b":"cc:2844","type":"develops","note":"2844 says release lies in the scripture of the Lord under the three umbrellas and the pindi tree; 3051 shows that same Lord preaching to kings who leave their retinues outside the golden wall.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sermon only names the Lord as the source of the doctrine; the assembly shows what following him costs a king, who leaves his army at the gate.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3056","b":"cc:2843","type":"qualifies","note":"2843 promises that virtue makes gods in heaven; 3056 says the heaven-dwellers of the enjoyment lands renounce nothing and see nothing but what the Lord's word has said.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Heaven is the reward of virtue in one verse, and in the other it is a place where renunciation, and so release, is not done.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3070","b":"k:267","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 267 says austerity burns pain as fire burns gold so that it shines; 3070 has the sun's own fire ring burn on Civakan for four months while he stands on the elephant of knowledge.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the gold-and-fire image of austerity in general; the verse sets it in a particular season of heat and ties the shining to knowledge, not to appearance.","gn":["the verse's four months of burning sun are given the principle that the heat is what makes the ascetic shine","the Kural's gold in fire becomes a named ordeal, a season of heat with an ascetic standing in it"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3072","b":"t:724","type":"tension","note":"3072 has Civakan spend four cold months in the open field, using the body up for release; Tirumanthiram 724 says if the body perishes the life perishes, so the body is nurtured to win knowledge.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One text treats the body as the means to be spent, the other as the means that must be kept alive, so they disagree on what a body is for.","gn":["the verse's exposure of the body is set against a school that says release needs a body kept alive","the Tirumanthiram's nurturing of the body meets an ascetic who spends his on release"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3064","b":"n:51","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 51 says austerity drives sin out like a lamp but that sin returns when the oil of merit runs out; 3064 has the eight karmas cut to pieces by one firm mind.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The quatrain's lamp-and-oil picture makes the effect of austerity last as long as its merit; the epic claims a final cutting, so the two differ on whether it can return.","gn":["the verse's total destruction of karma is tested by the rule that austerity only keeps sin away while the merit lasts","the quatrain's returning darkness meets a claim that the cut is final"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3064","b":"t:120","type":"tension","note":"3064 has Civakan cut the eight karmas by the strength of his own mind; Tirumanthiram 120 has deeds made into seeds that have touched fire and will not sprout, by the one who parts milk from water, the Lord or guru.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The seeds will not sprout in both, but one makes the ascetic himself the burner and the other leaves the burner a god or teacher.","gn":["the verse's self-won end of karma is set against a Saiva reading in which the burning is done for the soul","the Tirumanthiram's burnt seeds meet a Jain account in which no one else burns them"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3072","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 261 defines austerity as bearing the pain that comes and causing none to living beings; 3072 has Civakan live four months in the cold open field and guard sweet life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet joins the endurance and the harmlessness in one definition, and the verse shows them in one season, if the 'guarded life' is the lives of creatures.","gn":["the verse's four months in the open are measured against a rule that endured pain counts only with harmlessness","the Kural's two-part definition is given an ascetic who lives it out in one cold season"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3074","b":"cc:2845","type":"develops","note":"2845 gives knowledge, faith and conduct as the three that make release; 3074 turns knowledge into the chariot, mercy into armour and virtue into the sword.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ilampakam's plain teaching of the three jewels is here made into an army, so the reader sees what the three do and not only that they are three.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3074","b":"cc:2819","type":"same_image","note":"2819 makes virtue (sila) the armour of warring kings; in 3074 mercy is the armour and sila the sword.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same virtue moves from a guard to a weapon, showing that in this ascetic war sila both protects and wins.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3082","b":"cc:2846","type":"develops","note":"2846 says knowledge, faith and conduct together are a fire that burns the tree of deeds; 3082 shows the fire's end: the four destroying kings fall and the three worlds are known.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A teaching that the three burn deeds is fulfilled in the hero's own omniscience, so the doctrine is shown as an event in a life.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3082","b":"cc:2847","type":"develops","note":"2847 lists the four infinites of the liberated, and 3082 lifts the banner of the fourfold infinite.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The list of what release gives is shown as something one person takes, with the world swallowed, and not only a catalogue.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3082","b":"t:113","type":"tension","note":"3082 has Civakan win omniscience by his own war on the karmas; Tirumanthiram 113 has the Lord come down in a body, set his feet on the devotee's head and cut away the stain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One text has the soul win release by its own battle, the other has it given from above, so the two disagree on whose act cuts the deeds.","gn":["the war of the passions is set against a release that is given and not fought for, so its self-reliance becomes a choice","the grace that cuts the stain is set beside a release won by effort, so its giver is seen to be what the Jain refuses"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3093","b":"cc:3082","type":"same_image","note":"3093 repeats 3082's swallowing of the three worlds, there as the narrator's victory, here as praise in the worshippers' mouths.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same image is first an event and then a creed, and the hymn shows how a story becomes what a community says.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3080","b":"k:360","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 360 names three roots of pain, desire, anger and confusion; 3080 names four passions, Anger, Pride, Deceit and Greed, as warriors who must be slain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Jain list of four widens the Kural's three, showing that one counts the roots of pain differently depending on the school.","gn":["the four passions, as warriors, get a rule that when their very names perish pain perishes","the Kural's three roots get a fourth and a fighting form: pride and deceit, and a weapon against them"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3091","b":"k:10","type":"develops","note":"Kural 10 says those who reach the Lord's feet swim the sea of births; 3091 prays 'let our birth end' to the Lord who destroyed Desire and Death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural says the feet carry across, and the verse names who the Lord has beaten (Desire and Death) and why they should be prayed to.","gn":["the prayer for the end of birth is given a wider rule that reaching his feet is the way to cross","the Kural's Lord, who is left unnamed, is shown as one who has killed Desire and Death"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3092","b":"k:9","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 9 praises the one of eight qualities; 3092 praises the one who has cut four deeds together and knows the three times.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"One text counts a Lord by eight qualities and the other by four deeds cut, so the reader sees two different measures of the same worshipped figure.","gn":["a Lord counted by four destroyed karmas is seen beside a Lord counted by eight qualities","the Kural's eight qualities, left unnamed, can be compared with a Jain account of what is cut and known"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3092","b":"cc:3082","type":"develops","note":"3082 tells the cutting of the four destroying karmas and the knowing of the three worlds; 3092 sings it as the Lord's own deed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The event of 3082 becomes the praise of 3092, so the narrator's story is shown as the hymn's ground.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3081","b":"t:219","type":"tension","note":"3081 fires the arrow of pure meditation like a roaring fire to destroy the foes; Tirumanthiram 219 has deeds burnt by the fire that is worshipped and fed with ghee.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"One text burns deeds by an inner meditation like fire, the other by an outer fire kindled in worship, so the two disagree on where the fire is.","gn":["the meditation that burns is seen as an inner fire beside the Saiva fire kindled in ritual","the ritual fire that burns deeds is set beside a Jain fire that burns them with no offering"],"gr":2,"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3098","b":"cc:3099","type":"same_image","note":"3098 gives the bad deed a child nursed by anger and desire; 3099 gives the good deed a child nursed by compassion.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two verses pair the same upbringing image so that the difference between kinds of deed lies in what feeds them.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3101","b":"k:5","type":"develops","note":"3101 has the Lord's feet cut through the hard-gathered karma like a blade; Kural 5 says the two deeds do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's true praise.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The verse gives the means by which the deeds are removed and the Kural gives the assurance that they will not cling.","gn":["the verse's cutting spear is given an outcome, that the deeds do not attach to the one who praises","the Kural's promise that deeds do not cling is given a picture of how, a cutting of the root"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3101","b":"t:219","type":"tension","note":"3101 has worship of the feet cut karma like a weapon; Tirumanthiram 219 says the fire worshipped and risen burns the deeds of ages.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both promise that worship removes karma, but one gives the cutting to an enlightened teacher's feet and the other to a worshipped fire.","gn":["the Jain feet-as-spear gets a rival agent of the same removal, a burning fire that the Jain verse does not use","the Tirumanthiram's burning fire gets a rival that cuts by the feet of a liberated one who does not answer prayer"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3105","b":"k:10","type":"develops","note":"3105 pictures desire as grass that lures souls into the four-state pen and says reaching the pure one's feet lets one go to release; Kural 10 says those who reach the Lord's feet swim the sea of births.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The verse supplies the trap that the Kural's sea leaves unnamed, and the Kural supplies the crossing the verse only names as going.","gn":["the verse's pen of four states gets the picture of a sea crossed, which turns the escape into a swim","the Kural's sea of births gets a reason people are in it, the grass of desire, and a price for crossing, love sold"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3107","b":"k:327","type":"qualifies","note":"3107 says that guarding all lives as one's own brings a high birth; Kural 327 says not to kill even if one's own life is the price.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The verse makes non-injury pay in a later birth, and the Kural cuts the rule loose from payment even at the price of one's life.","gn":["the verse's reward for protection is tested by the case where protection costs the protector his life","the Kural's absolute rule gets the Jain account of what the careful guarder wins in later births"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3108","b":"k:320","type":"develops","note":"3108 has the one who harms for food thrown by two deeds into burning flame; Kural 320 says pains rest on those who caused pain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The verse names the form the returning pain takes, a hell of fire, which the Kural's general law does not.","gn":["the verse's hell is shown as one case of a general law that pain returns to its cause","the Kural's law gets a specific place and fire where the pain lands"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3117","b":"t:134","type":"tension","note":"3117 pictures omniscience as a maiden embraced by the king at the top of the worlds, the self still distinct; Tirumanthiram 134 says those who feel the teacher's word become the reality, like ghee mixed in milk.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both end in bliss, but the Cintamani keeps lover and beloved two, while the Tirumanthiram dissolves the self into what it meets.","gn":["the verse's embrace is shown as release that keeps a separate self, which the milk-and-ghee image of union would remove","the Tirumanthiram's merging gets a rival picture where the freed one stays apart and enjoys"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3118","b":"n:14","type":"qualifies","note":"3118 has the king live with a woman who has no old age or death; Naladiyar 14 has the once-desired woman bent over a staff and a torment.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One text shows what the loved woman will become, and the other the woman who never becomes it.","gn":["the verse's ageless bride is shown as the answer to the old woman the quatrain describes","the Naladiyar's bent old woman gets the Jain promise of a partner who does not age, offered to those who give up the first kind"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3120","b":"n:46","type":"develops","note":"3120 has the women see the dirt on their adorned bodies and think of ending woman-birth; Naladiyar 46 breaks the woman into entrails, fat, blood and bone and asks which part she is.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain gives the analysis of the body's parts and the verse shows women applying a like seeing to themselves.","gn":["the verse's women get the reasoned list of parts that grounds their disgust, so their turn is more than a mood","the Naladiyar's list, offered to men about women, gets a case where women use it on their own bodies"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3120","b":"cc:3106","type":"develops","note":"3106 tells the women to hate the body kept for desire; 3120 shows them doing it, seeing their adorned bodies as stained.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sermon's advice to hate the body and the women's act of seeing it as dirt are the same turn, the teaching and then its keeping.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3116","b":"n:24","type":"tension","note":"3116 has the pyre burn the Lord's jewel-bright body with gods circling and rejoicing; Naladiyar 24 has the drums beat three times and the corpse be covered and the fire taken up.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same flame ends an ordinary corpse in grief and a Lord's body in festival, so the pyre's meaning depends on who is burning.","gn":["the festival pyre is shown in contrast with the common corpse's plain burning, which the gods' honours dress up","the quatrain's grieved burning gets a body whose burning is celebrated because nothing of the person is lost"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3121","b":"cc:3105","type":"develops","note":"3105 says that giving up clinging at the pure one's feet lets one reach the state of release; 3121 shows the women, free of desire and love, reaching heaven.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The promise to the women's sermon is kept, though what they reach is heaven rather than the release the verse named.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3121","b":"k:346","type":"tension","note":"3121 sends the women who gave up desire to heaven as Indras; Kural 346 says one who cuts off 'I' and 'mine' enters a world higher than the gods'.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The verse ends in the gods' world and the Kural says release lies above it, so the same renunciation is given two different ends.","gn":["the verse's heaven is shown as a stop below where the Kural puts the one who has left 'I' and 'mine'","the Kural's world above the gods gets a case of renouncers who stop at the gods' world"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3132","b":"cc:3134","type":"develops","note":"3132 has Nandan and his friends piling up penance by effort; 3134 ends their lives on the mountain with rebirth as gods.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The penance begun in one verse comes to its fruit two verses later, so the story shows the deed ripening into a heavenly birth and not into release.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3133","b":"cc:3145","type":"develops","note":"3133 says fasting and meditation wash off evil deeds; 3145 closes on the Lord's feet as the way across the sea of deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The first verse gives the ascetic's own washing of deeds, the last the worshipper's crossing by the Lord's feet, so the work ends by joining effort and devotion.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3141","b":"cc:3122","type":"same_image","note":"3122 sets the lovers as wishing-trees wrapped in creepers; 3141 puts the gods in a sea of the senses, so one pleasure is drawn at two levels.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poet writes human and heavenly love with the same figures, so the heaven reached by penance is a purer form of the bed that penance left.","mm":1},{"a":"cc:3133","b":"n:51","type":"develops","note":"3133 says fasting and meditation wash off evil deeds; Naladiyar 51 says austerity drives off sin as a lamp drives off dark, but only while the good deeds last.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The story shows the ascetics' fast washing deeds away, and the quatrain adds that the lamp burns only while its oil of good deeds holds.","gn":["the verse's washing of deeds acquires a limit, that austerity works only while the store of good deeds lasts, which is not said in the verse","the quatrain's lamp and oil acquire a case in which a company of ascetics is seen to carry the austerity through to their deaths"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3134","b":"k:346","type":"qualifies","note":"3134 has the penance end in birth as gods; Kural 346 says one who cuts off the pride of I and mine goes to a world above the gods.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic's ascetics end among the gods, but the couplet puts above the gods a world reached only by cutting off the self, so heaven is not the last goal.","gn":["the rebirth among gods is shown as not the top of the scale, which the verse itself does not say","the couplet's world above the gods is given a case of ascetics who stop at heaven short of it"],"mm":1},{"a":"cc:3145","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"3145 ends on the Lord's feet as the way over the sea of deeds; Kural 10 says those who reach the Lord's feet cross the sea of births.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both place the Lord's feet beyond a sea, but the Cintamani names the water as deeds and the Kural as births, so the epic fixes on karma as the thing to be crossed.","gn":["the sea is placed in a fuller tradition of birth as the sea to be crossed, so karma and rebirth are read together","the couplet's sea of births is given a cause by the epic, since the water is made of deeds"],"mm":1},{"a":"kd:2","b":"kd:17","type":"tension","note":"Verse 2 says the slips of speech of one praising the faultless are no slips; verse 17 says a fault stays a fault even in those who have reached truth and in a god.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The fragments hold two standards side by side: the poet excuses faults by the worth of what is praised, while the moral verse refuses to let any height excuse a fault, so the apology rests on devotion, not on a rule about faults.","gr":4},{"a":"kd:2","b":"nk:6","type":"tension","note":"Both poets apologise for faults in their work: the Kundalakesi poet says faults in praise of the faultless one are not faults; the Neelakesi poet says the moon itself has a spot and he will own any fault.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The two rival works answer the same assembly in opposite ways, the Buddhist denying that his slips count and the Jain conceding them, so the debate between the books begins in how each poet stands toward his own fault.","gr":2},{"a":"kd:2","b":"k:360","type":"develops","note":"Verse 2 praises the one who cut off the three faults without naming them; Kural 360 names three, desire, anger and confusion, whose very names must perish for pain to end.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with the couplet, the epic's unnamed three faults take on a Tamil list already current, and the couplet's goal of ending the three is shown as the very mark by which a Buddhist poet recognises his lord.","gn":["the fragment's bare 'three faults' gets a known Tamil naming","the couplet's triad is seen used as a title of the one who has achieved it, not only a goal for the seeker"],"gr":2},{"a":"kd:4","b":"k:280","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 280 says neither shaving nor long hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns; Kundalakesi 4 dismisses a longer list, nakedness, ash, cold, fire, alms and matted hair, as words, and calls tapas a subdued life.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet sets aside only the hair, and tests the renouncer by the world's blame; the fragment sets aside the whole repertoire of bodily austerity and puts the test in inner restraint, so the two differ on where the renouncer's proof lies.","gn":["the fragment's dismissal is seen to extend a point the Kural already made about outward marks","the couplet's narrow example is shown to open onto a general attack on bodily penance"]},{"a":"kd:4","b":"t:1662","type":"tension","note":"Tirumanthiram 1662 makes wearing holy ash the first of the emblems of the flawless Siva-yogi; Kundalakesi 4 puts smearing the body with ash among the practices it calls mere words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same act, ash on the body, is for Tirumular the first sign of the true yogi and for the Kundalakesi a hollow show, so the two traditions disagree on whether an outward mark can belong to tapas at all; the tension depends on reading பொடி பூசல் as ash-smearing.","gn":["the fragment's target is seen to be a practice a living Tamil tradition held sacred","the Saiva emblem is shown under attack from outside, which explains why Tirumular must defend it against hollow wearers"],"gr":2},{"a":"kd:5","b":"k:1148","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1148 says thinking gossip can put out love is like thinking ghee can put out fire; Kundalakesi 5 asks whether a fire-pit can be put out with ghee, of those who would end desire by enjoying it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same proverb of ghee on fire serves the lover, for whom nothing will quench love and that is its strength, and the ascetic, for whom indulgence only feeds desire and that is its danger.","gn":["the fragment's argument is seen to borrow an image already fixed in love poetry and turn it","the couplet's image is shown to carry a warning against desire that the love-speaker does not draw"]},{"a":"kd:7","b":"kd:9","type":"tension","note":"Verse 7 says the wise do not mourn things whose nature is to perish and calls weeping from love a self-injury; verse 9 asks why we do not weep for ourselves, who die day by day.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The call to weep in 9 cannot be the grief that 7 forbids: it is a rhetorical shock meant to make the living see their own dying, so the fragments separate mourning others from waking to one's own mortality."},{"a":"kd:9","b":"n:24","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 24 ends with the dying carrying the dead; Kundalakesi 9 says the foetus, child, youth and young adult have each died in us and we die daily.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain makes the mourners already dying in the moment of a funeral; the fragment moves death inside each life, as a series of deaths of one's own former selves, so the bearer is also already a corpse many times over.","gn":["the fragment's interior deaths gain a public scene where the point is seen","the quatrain's 'the dying' is given a measure: one dies at every stage of growth"],"gr":3},{"a":"kd:10","b":"k:334","type":"develops","note":"Kural 334 says what shows itself as a day is a blade sawing through life; Kundalakesi 10 says each day's step takes us to lay our heads at the edge of Death's sword.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the day itself the blade; the fragment makes the day a step toward a blade held by Death and adds the condemned man's walk, so time becomes not only the cutting but the procession to execution with no reprieve.","gn":["the fragment's image of the executioner's sword is seen to rest on the Kural's day-as-blade","the couplet's still image gains motion and a missing reprieve, which sharpens what 'those who perceive it' would perceive"],"gr":3},{"a":"kd:12","b":"n:48","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 48 asks whether those who praise sandal paste and garlands have not seen vultures tear the stinking cart of the body; Kundalakesi 12 asks what delight is left in shoulders praised as Mandara when vultures and dogs pluck at them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar turns the contemplation of carrion on a body adorned for love, usually the woman's; the fragment turns the same contemplation on a man's heroic shoulders, so the discipline is shown to apply to desire for either sex.","gn":["the fragment is placed in an established Tamil practice of looking at the body as carrion","the quatrain's lesson is shown not to depend on the desired body being a woman's"],"gr":3},{"a":"kd:14","b":"k:346","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 346 says the one who cuts off the pride of 'I' and 'mine' enters a world above the gods'; Kundalakesi 14 asks whether a body of deeds, fed on by worms, can be called 'I' and 'mine'.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet gives the reward for cutting 'I' and 'mine' but not the argument; the fragment gives the argument from the body's foulness and tenants but promises no higher world, so together they separate the case against 'mine' from the hope it is made for.","gn":["the fragment's question is seen to aim at a goal the Kural names","the couplet's bare command gains a reason a hearer can test on his own body"]},{"a":"kd:14","b":"s:354","type":"develops","note":"Pattinathar's verse has fire, worm, earth, kite, jackal and dog each say of the body 'it is mine'; Kundalakesi 14 says worms feed and live in the body while one still calls it 'mine'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The fragment finds the rival owners already at work in the living body, while Pattinathar lines them up at the corpse; held together, the claim 'mine' is false both before death and after it.","gn":["the fragment's denial is extended past death to every scavenger","the Siddhar's after-death scene is shown to have begun in the living body, which sharpens his question of what gain the rearing brought"]},{"a":"kd:17","b":"k:957","type":"develops","note":"Kural 957 says a fault in the well-born stands out like the spot on the moon; Kundalakesi 17 says a fault stays a fault even in those who have reached truth, as the stain on the god's throat is still a stain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet says height makes a fault more visible; the fragment adds that height does not change its nature, and carries the rule up from the well-born to the gods.","gn":["the fragment's claim is grounded in the Kural's moon-spot observation","the couplet's rule is extended beyond human lineage to the divine"]},{"a":"kd:18","b":"k:379","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 379 asks why those who welcome good when it comes should grieve when it does not; Kundalakesi 18 says those who know that what is due will come neither grieve nor exult.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet only rebukes grief and leaves joy at good fortune untouched; the fragment strikes out elation too, so its equanimity is stricter than the Kural's.","gn":["the fragment is seen as answering a question the Kural poses","the couplet's one-sided rebuke is shown to stop short of the full equanimity its premise allows"]},{"a":"kd:18","b":"pn:192","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 192 says good and ill do not come from others, we neither delight in life nor call it bitter, since life moves by its order like a raft on a river; Kundalakesi 18 says what is due comes, so the wise neither grieve nor exult.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Sangam poet draws from the same certainty a rule about others, neither marvelling at the great nor despising the small; the fragment draws only the inner rule, so the pair shows one premise yielding a social ethic in one book and a private discipline in the other.","gn":["the fragment's maxim gains an older Tamil statement of the same order of things","the Sangam poem's equanimity is shown carried into a later religious setting where it becomes a teaching on grief and joy"]},{"a":"kd:18","b":"kd:19","type":"develops","note":"Verse 18 states that the wise neither grieve nor exult because what is due will come; verse 19 shows a man unmoved both by a king's pardon and by a plot to kill him, naming all of it the fruit of deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The maxim of 18 is put to its hardest test in 19, a reprieve and a death plot, and 19 names the cause 18 left unnamed: deeds.","gr":3},{"a":"kd:19","b":"s:365","type":"tension","note":"Pattinathar asks what use weeping is, since good and evil will not come otherwise than as the lotus-born god wrote; Kundalakesi 19 has a man stay unmoved by pardon and death because all that befalls is the fruit of deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both counsel the same steadiness, but the Siddhar grounds it in what a god wrote and the fragment in one's own deeds, so the same calm rests on incompatible accounts of who decides what comes.","gn":["the fragment's karma is set against a theistic account of the same events","the Siddhar's appeal to Brahma's writing is shown to be one choice among Tamil grounds for equanimity"]},{"a":"mm:0:89-98","b":"si:0:69-90","type":"same_image","note":"Silappathikaram's patikam ends with Ilango graciously telling and Saathan the grain merchant hearing (அடிகள் அருள ... சாத்தன் கேட்டனன்); Manimekalai's ends with Ilango graciously hearing and Saathan setting out the work (இளங்கோ வேந்தன் அருளிக் கேட்ப ... சாத்தன் ... அறிய வைத்தனன்), each in thirty parts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two patikams frame the twin epics as one exchange between the same two men, each the listener of the other's poem, so the tradition joins them by their frames and not only by Madhavi's daughter continuing the story."},{"a":"mm:0:19-31","b":"pp:9:1-28","type":"same_image","note":"Pattinappalai opens on the Kaveri that does not fail though the white star strays south and the sky withholds rain; the patikam calls the Kaveri the Tamil maiden who does not leave her course though the planets stray and summer lengthens (கோள் நிலை திரிந்து கோடை நீடினும் தான் நிலை திரியா).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic takes the Sangam praise-formula for Puhar's river and turns it into a person, a Chola daughter who bows to the city's goddess and gives the city its second name, so a boast of the land's yield becomes a founding story.","gn":["the epithet in line 24-25 is shown to be an inherited Sangam formula, placing the epic's Puhar in the Chola praise tradition of Karikalan's city","the idyll's unfailing river gains a myth of origin (Agastya's pot, the king's request) and a person who acts, which the idyll's description does not have"]},{"a":"mm:0:32-50","b":"mm:27:277-283","type":"tension","note":"The narrator states as event that at the jewel seat Manimekalai came to know all her former births (பழம் பிறப்பு எல்லாம் ... உணர்ந்ததும்); in kaathai 27 she asks whether no one has known a former birth, and the materialist answers that such knowledge is the delusion of those led by a god or by dreams. The tension holds on the reading that 281-283 are the materialist's.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The materialist's dismissal names exactly the route by which the patikam says her knowledge came, a goddess carrying her to an island, so the epic lets the one school it answers attack the very ground of its heroine's conversion."},{"a":"mm:0:69-88","b":"mm:27:1-24","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's line for kaathai 27 is that she asked them to state the faultless truth and heard (கேட்டதும்) the schools' doctrines; kaathai 27 lays the schools out one by one and she answers only the materialist.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam's own word for the chapter of the schools is hearing, not defeating, which supports reading most of kaathai 27's accounts as reported views left for the Buddhist teaching of the later kaathais to answer."},{"a":"mm:0:51-68","b":"k:225","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 225 ranks the strength of those who bear hunger below the strength of those who remove it; in the patikam the renunciant who begs with a bowl is the one who ends Kayachandigai's hunger-fire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural sets the one who endures hunger and the one who relieves it as two kinds of strength in rank, while the epic gives both to one person, a begging ascetic whose bowl feeds others, so its Buddhist renunciation removes the Kural's ranking rather than falling under it.","gn":["the bowl scene gains a standard from the ethics books by which the heroine's begging is a higher strength than ascetic endurance, not a lower one","the couplet's contrast between enduring and removing hunger is shown not to be exclusive, since a renunciant can hold both"]},{"a":"mm:0:89-98","b":"k:362","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 362: if one must desire, desire not to be born, and that comes by desiring not to desire; the patikam closes with Manimekalai, having heard the dharma, vowing that becoming be cut off (பவத் திறம் அறுக என ... நோற்றதும்).","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural allows one desire, for birthlessness, and reaches it by turning desire on itself; the epic makes that one desire a vow kept with austerity after hearing a teacher, so the end the Kural states as inner paradox is reached in the epic through instruction and practice.","gn":["the heroine's closing vow is seen to be the single desire the Kural permits, not a lapse from desirelessness","the couplet's wish for birthlessness gains a path, a teacher's dharma and a vow, which the couplet does not name"]},{"a":"mm:1:1-9","b":"pn:39","type":"develops","note":"The Purananuru poet names the ancestors who destroyed the hanging fort as a deed so great that killing is no longer the living Chola's own fame; Manimekalai's opening makes the hanging-fort Chola the founder of Puhar's festival of Indra.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows the epic taking a Sangam war-memory of the Chola line and turning it into a sacred charter, so that a city's yearly rite rests on an ancestor's feat rather than on the reigning king.","gn":["the epic's unnamed founder gains a lineage: he is the ancestor the Sangam court already cited by this one feat","the poem's bare allusion to the fort-breaking ancestors gains a story in which that ancestor is Indra's ally and the patron of a festival"]},{"a":"mm:1:10-34","b":"mm:25:175-199","type":"develops","note":"The assemblies warn that a forgotten festival will bring the guardian spirit's affliction on the city; in kaathai 25 the king, searching for his lost son, neglects the festival and Manimekala deity curses the city to the sea.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The opening warning is the plot's first prophecy, but its fulfilment shifts the punisher from the city's guardian spirit to Manimekala deity, so the epic keeps the civic threat while giving the punishment to its own Buddhist-protecting goddess."},{"a":"mm:1:10-34","b":"si:5:128-134","type":"same_image","note":"Both epics place in Puhar a spirit that seizes the wicked with its noose, beats them and devours them; in the Silappathikaram it punishes named sins, in Manimekalai it is one of the powers the festival keeps appeased.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same noose-bearing spirit changes office between the two epics: a moral police against hypocrites and false witnesses in the first becomes, in the second, a power whose goodwill depends on the city keeping its festival."},{"a":"mm:1:10-34","b":"k:559","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural states that when the king bends justice the sky withholds rain; the drummer's blessing wishes three rains and an unswerving king in the same sentence, as things the festival must secure.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural makes rain follow from the king's justice alone, while the festival treats both rain and just rule as gifts to be prayed for from the gods, so the epic's city does not leave the causal chain to the king.","gn":["the paired wish in lines 33-34 gains its logic: the Kural explains why rain and the sceptre belong in one blessing","the couplet's rule gains a ritual setting in which a city, not trusting the king alone, asks the gods for both ends of it"]},{"a":"mm:1:35-51","b":"mm:24:52-74","type":"develops","note":"The drummer proclaims the good day of the island's rite of peace (தீவகச் சாந்தி); in kaathai 24 a charana tells the king that on the day the rite is not performed his city will enter the sea, by Manimekala deity's word and Indra's curse.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same rite named in the festival proclamation becomes, twenty-three kaathais later, the condition on which the city's survival hangs, so the decorations of the opening are already the terms of a covenant the city will break."},{"a":"mm:1:52-72","b":"k:734","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural defines a land as one that goes on without crushing hunger, unceasing disease and destructive enemies; the drummer ends the proclamation by wishing that hunger, disease and enmity leave the city.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the Kural states as the mark of a true land, the epic's city asks for once a year as a festival blessing, showing that the freedom from the three ills was felt as a gift to be renewed by rite, not a settled condition.","gn":["the herald's closing wish gains a political measure: these three ills are exactly what decides whether Puhar is a land at all","the couplet's definition gains a public voice and an occasion, a drum proclamation in which the city itself asks to meet it"]},{"a":"mm:1:52-72","b":"k:852","type":"develops","note":"The Kural advises not to do harm in hostility even when others act against you; the drummer bids the schools' debaters part without hatred or quarrel even from those who do not accept their view (பற்றாமாக்கள்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic carries the Kural's rule of non-retaliation from private enmity into public doctrinal debate, making civility between rival schools a law of the city's festival.","gn":["the herald's rule for debaters gains an ethical ground beyond festive peace: hostility harms the one who nurses it","the couplet's counsel gains a concrete arena, the debating hall where disagreement is expected but enmity is forbidden"]},{"a":"mm:1:52-72","b":"mm:27:1-24","type":"develops","note":"The festival bids the debaters of the schools mount Puhar's debating hall and part without quarrel; in kaathai 27, after Puhar is lost, Manimekalai goes among the schools of Vanji in disguise and hears each in turn without dispute.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The public hall of debate promised at the opening is never shown in Puhar; it returns only in another city as one renunciant's quiet hearing of the schools, so the epic moves doctrinal inquiry from civic festival to private search."},{"a":"mm:2:17-33","b":"si:3:1-11","type":"develops","note":"The town lists the arts set down for dancing women that Madhavi mastered; Silappathikaram 3 opens on her seven years' training in dance, song and beauty for her first performance before the king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The training that the earlier epic presents as the making of Madhavi's fame becomes in the town's mouth its argument against her leaving that life, so the courtesan's accomplishments bind her to her trade as a claim others hold on her."},{"a":"mm:2:34-41","b":"k:1017","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1017 says those ruled by shame give up life rather than shame for life; Madhavi says that by staying alive after hearing of her lover's suffering she gave up her shame.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read against the couplet, Madhavi's confession places her among those who kept life at the cost of shame, so the shame the town sees in her tapas already lay in her survival, and her renunciation becomes the answer of one who has failed the rule rather than a new disgrace.","gn":["the general rule that makes her confession intelligible: the couplet says why outliving the news of Kovalan's suffering counts as giving up shame","a person who has failed the rule and lives on, which shows a way of answering lost shame, by tapas, that the couplet's choice between shame and death leaves out"]},{"a":"mm:2:42-57","b":"si:21:40-57","type":"develops","note":"Madhavi recounts Kannaki twisting off her breast and setting the king's city aflame; Silappathikaram 21 tells the act.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Madhavi retells the burning as the deed of the mother she assigns to Manimekalai, so the earlier epic's vengeance becomes a lineage by which a courtesan's daughter is claimed for tapas instead of her mother's trade."},{"a":"mm:2:42-57","b":"pn:246","type":"develops","note":"Perunkoppendu tells the elders holding her back that for her, her husband dead, a lotus pond and the pyre's fire are one, and scorns the widow's life of sparse food and bare ground; Madhavi's second kind of pathini enters the blazing fire as into a cool-water pool, and her third wears the body down in vows.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The queen's single act of defiance, in which the pyre is a cool pond and the austere widowhood she refuses is worse than fire, becomes in the epic a ranked typology of the chaste wife where entering the fire is only the second kind and the vowed austerity she despised is admitted as the third, and all three are then set beneath Kannaki.","gn":["the source of the fire-as-cool-pool figure in a widow's own speech against the elders, so that Madhavi's kinds are seen to be drawn from remembered acts and not invented as a scheme","a later frame in which the queen's choice is one recognised option among three, with the widow's austerity she scorned now counted as a path of chastity, which shows how the poem's personal refusal was absorbed into a norm"]},{"a":"mm:2:42-57","b":"mm:30:244-264","type":"develops","note":"Madhavi says Manimekalai will be brought to great tapas and never to the courtesan's trade; at the close of kaathai 30 Manimekalai takes up tapas and vows to end the course of births.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The mother's claim made for her daughter in the third kaathai is fulfilled only in the last, and by the daughter's own vow after hearing the whole doctrine, so the epic runs between a claim made over Manimekalai and her own assent to it."},{"a":"mm:2:58-75","b":"mm:30:34-58","type":"develops","note":"Aravanar's four lines to Madhavi set suffering for the born, bliss for the unborn and attachment as the cause; in kaathai 30 he sets out the twelve links, with attachment (பற்றே) one link among them, and says the born who know them gain the great reward.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same teacher gives the grieving mother a single cause and five precepts, and the daughter the full twelve-link chain, so the epic shows the doctrine scaled to its hearer: consolation and lay discipline first, the analysis of release later."},{"a":"mm:2:58-75","b":"k:349","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 349 says that when attachment ends it cuts off births; Aravanar says suffering comes from attachment, bliss to those free of it, and that birth itself brings growing suffering.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet ties the end of attachment to the end of rebirth but sets no value on birth; Aravanar's lines add that birth is itself suffering and offer a bereaved lay woman five precepts as the way in, not the renouncer's clean cut.","gn":["a bare causal statement, attachment to rebirth, against which the teacher's added judgment that birth is suffering stands out as the Buddhist addition","a scene where its renouncer's rule is given as consolation to a grieving woman still in the world, with lay precepts as the first step"]},{"a":"mm:2:58-75","b":"t:432","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says the Lord who made birth for joy shut it in the suffering of the bond; Aravanar says the born meet growing suffering, the unborn great bliss, and suffering comes from attachment.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both trace suffering to a binding, attachment or the bond, but they disagree about birth itself: for the Buddhist teacher birth is the suffering and not being born is bliss, while for Tirumular birth was made for joy and the bond was laid on it by the same Lord; the conflict holds on reading இன்பப் பிறவி as birth made for joy.","gn":["a theist account in which a maker lays the suffering on birth, which shows that Aravanar names no maker and makes attachment alone the cause","an opposed claim that birth is itself suffering, which shows that the verse's birth made for joy is a position taken, not common ground"]},{"a":"mm:3:1-25","b":"mm:4:61-84","type":"develops","note":"Sudhamathi warns that no man who sees the weeping Manimekalai could turn away; in the next kaathai Ettikumaran's glimpse of her faded face entering the grove sends the prince after her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The warning is fulfilled by exactly the beauty it named, a face dimmed by grief rather than adorned, so the renunciant's sorrow, not her old trade, is what draws the prince into the story."},{"a":"mm:3:26-41","b":"si:6:1-17","type":"same_image","note":"Both have a vidyadhara fly down from the sky to see Puhar's festival for Indra; in the Silappathikaram he brings his beloved as a spectator, in the Manimekalai Marutavegan seizes a brahmin girl and abandons her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The heavenly visitor whom the first epic uses as an admiring eye on Puhar's festival becomes in the second a seducer who strands a girl there, so the same festival that showed Madhavi's art is now a danger to an unguarded girl."},{"a":"mm:3:69-81","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"Kural 34 makes a spotless mind the whole of virtue; the Uvavanam seat shows that a flower laid without intention goes nowhere, as proof that a deed without intention bears no fruit.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet puts the mind first in judging virtue; the seat pushes that principle to the disputed case of the unintended act and makes it a point on which schools divided, which the couplet never has to face.","gn":["a general ethical statement, free of any school, behind the Buddhist side of the dispute","a hard case, the deed done without any thought, that tests how far the mind-first rule reaches"]},{"a":"mm:3:82-103","b":"k:929","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 929 says reasoning with a drunk is like searching with fire under water; here the drunk reasons with a sober ascetic, arguing that toddy kills nothing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic reverses the couplet's direction, letting the drunk be the one who argues, and his argument from non-harm shows that the drunkard's reasons can borrow the ascetic's own principle and still be worthless.","gn":["the rule that makes the drunk's reasoning absurd without the narrator having to answer it","a scene where the drunk himself offers reasons, showing the futility runs both ways"]},{"a":"mm:3:82-103","b":"t:566","type":"qualifies","note":"The drunk calls toddy-drinking a yoga whose fruit the ascetic should taste; Tirumanthiram 566 says breath-control gives the intoxication of toddy without drinking it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The equation of drink and yogic ecstasy runs in opposite directions: the Tirumanthiram borrows toddy's joy as an image for breath-discipline, while the epic's drunk borrows yoga's name to sell toddy, so the pair shows the figure was common enough to be turned either way.","gn":["evidence that 'the yoga of drink' was a live figure the epic could parody","a satirical counter-use that shows the risk in its image of yoga as intoxication"]},{"a":"mm:3:82-103","b":"kd:4","type":"qualifies","note":"Kundalakesi 4 calls nakedness, ash, fire and fasting mere talk and tapas a restrained life; the epic shows a naked fasting ascetic in the street, cared for unseen lives and begged to drink by a drunk.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two Buddhist works keep their distance from bodily austerity by different means, argument in the fragment and street scene in the epic, and the epic allows the naked ascetic a real virtue, his pity for unseen lives, that the fragment's dismissal leaves out."},{"a":"mm:3:116-125","b":"si:6:46-67","type":"same_image","note":"The Silappathikaram lists Kama's eunuch dance and the pot dance in Bana's city among the eleven dances Madhavi performs at Indra's festival; here a street performer dances the eunuch dance, named in nearly the same words (வாணன் பேர் ஊர் மறுகிடை ... நீள் நிலம் அளந்தோன்), as her daughter passes in robes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The dance that was Madhavi's art on the royal stage at one festival is a street show at the next, watched by the crowd while her daughter walks past it to the Buddha's grove, so the repeated formula marks how far the family has moved from that stage."},{"a":"mm:3:146-157","b":"mm:2:17-33","type":"develops","note":"In the last kaathai the town's talk, brought by Vayantamalai, calls Madhavi's tapas shameful; here the festival crowd says it to Manimekalai's face and calls the mother cruel.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The gossip that reached Madhavi indirectly becomes a crowd around the daughter, and its charge shifts from the mother's shame to her cruelty to the girl, so the town's judgment follows the renunciation from one generation to the next."},{"a":"mm:4:105-125","b":"n:47","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain mocks the fool who calls a body of nine oozing openings 'broad-shouldered one'; Sudhamathi answers the prince's love-poetry questions about Manimekalai with the same inside-out view of the body.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic stages the quatrain's cure as speech to a lover just after he has used the love-poem epithets, and then shows it failing: the sight of the girl reaches him before the words do.","gn":["Sudhamathi's catalogue is placed in an existing ethics genre that answers love poetry's epithets one by one.","The quatrain's sure contempt for the fool gets a test case in which the cure is spoken in time and still loses to sight."]},{"a":"mm:4:105-125","b":"s:335","type":"qualifies","note":"Pattinattar calls the body the enjoyment of deeds, gone the moment deeds end, and sends the mind to Siva's feet; Sudhamathi calls it born of deeds and the field where they ripen, and asks only that it be known and turned from.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The two share the premise that the body exists to eat karma but part on the remedy: the Saiva poet needs Siva's feet, while the epic's speaker names no god and puts release in seeing the body truly.","gn":["Sudhamathi's godless remedy stands out once set beside a Saiva statement of the same premise.","Pattinattar's appeal to Siva's feet is shown to be an addition to a premise that a Buddhist epic holds without it."],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:4:105-125","b":"pz:6","type":"qualifies","note":"The proverb verse tells of the young Chola who tied on grey hair and judged a case (உரைமுடிவு) to prove that a family's craft comes half-known; Sudhamathi names the same act (உரை முடிவு காட்டிய உரவோன்) to shame his heir.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The proverb says the line's judgment is inborn, and the epic turns the same story against the line's heir, who must be lectured by a woman, so the inherited craft is invoked because it is missing.","gn":["The allusion at 108 is fixed to a known story whose point is wisdom in youth, which sharpens Sudhamathi's rebuke of a youth acting unwisely.","The proverb's claim that a family's craft comes by birth meets a heir of the same line who lacks it."]},{"a":"mm:4:85-104","b":"mm:3:52-68","type":"develops","note":"Kaathai 3 says the crystal pavilion lets no voice out but shows the body outside (விளிப்பு அறைபோகாது மெய் புறத்து இடூஉம்); Sudhamathi hides Manimekalai in it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hiding place was announced as one that hides speech but shows the body, so the shelter Sudhamathi picks is the one that will display the girl to the prince while she preaches against the body."},{"a":"mm:4:61-84","b":"mm:5:66-90","type":"tension","note":"Manimekalai hears the prince's chariot with fear and asks what to do; in the next kaathai, after he has gone, she confesses that her heart followed the stranger who had scorned her.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The crystal wall kept her body from his hands but not her heart from him, so the flight here and the confession after it show the girl's renunciation is not yet secure."},{"a":"mm:4:27-46","b":"si:15:42-53","type":"same_image","note":"Kovalan tamed a rogue elephant that had seized an old Brahmin; his daughter's pursuer Udayakumaran tames his own elephant gone mad in Puhar.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The father's deed of rescue returns as the prince's feat of strength on his way to chase the daughter, so the same heroic act is shown serving compassion once and desire the second time.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:5:1-17","b":"k:264","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says crushing foes comes through tapas if the ascetic so intends; Sudhamathi warns the prince that Manimekalai's fated austerity carries a curse for an arrow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's power of tapas is invoked here as a shield for a girl who has barely begun austerity, and the prince's shrug at line 21 shows the threat carrying no weight until it is backed by practice.","gn":["Sudhamathi's warning is read as an appeal to a recognised Tamil belief that tapas can harm an enemy, not an empty boast.","The couplet's claim gets a case where the power is asserted on another's behalf and fails to deter, which the couplet's confident statement does not consider."]},{"a":"mm:5:1-17","b":"mm:18:77-89","type":"develops","note":"Sudhamathi tells the prince the girl is given to fated austerity; in kaathai 18 he reports that a golden woman appeared to him at night with a sceptre and told him to forget the girl who had taken up tapas.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The warning comes twice, first from a woman he brushes aside and then from a divine figure, and the prince repeats the second to Chithirapathi without obeying it, so his ruin is shown as a refusal of a warning he understood."},{"a":"mm:5:18-31","b":"k:1251","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet is a woman's admission that the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint; the prince asks whether any restraint exists once desire takes hold.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same claim that in the Kural is a woman's confession of her own defeat becomes, in a prince's mouth, a licence to press on a woman who refuses him, so the epic shows how the love-book's lament can serve as an excuse.","gn":["The prince's maxim is recognised as borrowed from the love poets' woman's voice, which shows how far he has moved it from its place.","The couplet's resignation gets a test: said by the stronger party about another's body, the same words stop being a confession and become a threat."]},{"a":"mm:5:42-65","b":"k:30","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says the அந்தணர் are the virtuous, because they wear cool kindness toward every life; Sudhamathi's Brahmin father, turned off by Brahmins and Jain monks alike, cries for any அறவோர் and is answered by a Buddhist monk, called மறையோன் at 68.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic tells the couplet's redefinition as a story: those who hold the title by birth or by vows fail the dying man, and the title of one who knows the sacred lore passes to the stranger who shows kindness to him.","gn":["The cry அறவோர் உளீரோ gets the couplet's measure of virtue, kindness to every life, which explains why the monks of spotless form fail it.","The couplet's definition by conduct is shown working against real holders of the status, and across a sectarian line the couplet does not name."]},{"a":"mm:5:66-90","b":"k:1255","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says those sick with love cannot know the dignity of not going after one who has turned hostile; Manimekalai says her heart went after the stranger who had scorned her, and asks if this is the nature of desire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's lovesick woman knows her loss of dignity and keeps loving, while Manimekalai, meeting the same fact in herself on a single sight, answers it with a curse on desire (91), the renunciant's reply the love chapter never gives.","gn":["Manimekalai's question is shown to have a known answer in the Tamil love tradition, so her surprise marks how new desire is to her.","The couplet's diagnosis is applied to a girl vowed to austerity, which shows it holding even where the speaker has renounced the love it describes."]},{"a":"mm:5:66-90","b":"pn:182","type":"qualifies","note":"The Purananuru poem says the world endures because there are those who strive not for themselves but for others (தமக்கென முயலா ... பிறர்க்கென முயலுநர்); Sudhamathi calls the Buddha one who lives not for himself but belongs to others (தனக்கு என வாழாப் பிறர்க்கு உரியாளன்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic takes the Sangam formula for a class of honourable men who hold the world up and makes it the mark of one being whose living for others is the turning of the dharma wheel, moving the ideal from social honour to religious compassion.","gn":["The epithet at 73 is heard as a Tamil ideal already named in puram poetry, so the Buddha is praised in terms a Tamil audience held as the highest human worth.","The poem's unnamed many who strive for others are given a single founder as exemplar, whose motive is compassion for all beings rather than fear of blame and love of fame."]},{"a":"mm:5:66-90","b":"mm:18:17-36","type":"develops","note":"The prince leaves saying Manimekalai may yet come to him through Chithirapathi; in kaathai 18 Chithirapathi vows to put the girl in the prince's golden chariot or be shamed among the dancers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prince's passing remark becomes the grandmother's public vow, so the plot against the girl is shown to be carried by her own family's trade rather than by the prince alone."},{"a":"mm:5:91-105","b":"k:8","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says none but those who reach the feet of the அறவாழி அந்தணன் can cross the other seas; the goddess praises the Buddha's feet marked with the thousand-spoked wheel (ஆயிர ஆரத்து ஆழி), whom Sudhamathi has just called the one who turned the wheel of dharma (அறக் கதிர் ஆழி, 76).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same words, அறம், ஆழி and the lord's feet, name the Buddha's wheel of dharma in the epic, which shows that ஆழி in the couplet's epithet could be heard as wheel as well as sea, and that a Buddhist hearer would find the Buddha in a verse that names no one.","gn":["The goddess's praise of the feet is set in the Kural's opening frame, where reaching the feet is the way across.","The couplet's unnamed epithet is shown in use for a named founder, which opens a second sense of ஆழி that the sea reading alone hides."]},{"a":"mm:5:123-141","b":"si:4:1-20","type":"same_image","note":"The Silappathikaram brings evening on Puhar with the earth weeping as if her king were lost, lamps lit by women, the cowherds' mullai flute, and evening 'came and encamped' (மாலைவந்து இருத்தென); the Manimekalai's evening on Puhar has the lamps, the mullai flute and evening that came and encamped (வந்து இறுத்தனளால்) as a woman widowed in battle.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sequel reuses its parent's evening on the same city almost item by item but moves the grief from an earth that has lost her king to a woman who has lost her husband and goes home to her kin, so the evening of Kovalan's daughter carries the bereaved woman's figure that the first epic gave to the land."},{"a":"mm:6:1-15","b":"k:957","type":"same_image","note":"The couplet says a fault in the well-born stands out like the spot on the moon; the epic's moonrise says the moon's spot shows like a fault in the worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic can use the couplet's moral as the familiar term of a comparison for the sky, which shows the saying was common enough to be turned round.","gn":["The odd simile of a moon's spot shown like a worthy man's fault gets its source and its direction.","The couplet is seen working as a known saying that a narrative poet could invert in passing."]},{"a":"mm:6:16-33","b":"mm:4:105-125","type":"develops","note":"Sudhamathi's sermon on the body did not reach the prince before the sight of the girl; here the goddess reports that his gaze has not abated and sends the women into refuge.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic marks the failure of the sermon on the body as a fact the plot acts on: after argument fails, protection becomes a matter of place and then of flight."},{"a":"mm:6:48-63","b":"mm:2:42-57","type":"qualifies","note":"Madhavi ranks first among pathini women those who give up their lives when their husbands die; the cemetery holds brick shrines for women who died together with their husbands, sorted by varna beside ascetics and kings.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The death Madhavi praises as the highest kind of chastity ends, in the goddess's tour, as one more class of memorial in a field whose lesson is that no death is exempt."},{"a":"mm:6:94-104","b":"pn:359","type":"qualifies","note":"The Purananuru poem describes the same burning ground of owls, corpse-eating jackals and dancing demon women, which has taken even conquerors, and urges the patron to give freely and win lasting fame; the goddess draws from it that revelling in wealth instead of desiring dharma is the height of folly.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same memento mori leads the puram poet to gift and fame that outlast the body, and the Buddhist epic to dharma, with no word of fame: the cemetery's lesson changes with what each tradition thinks survives.","gn":["The goddess's verdict is seen to drop the fame and the patron that the older use of the scene required.","The poem's turn from the burning ground to fame is shown to be a choice, since the same scene could argue for renunciation."]},{"a":"mm:6:105-131","b":"n:45","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain's speaker, having seen teeth and bones scattered in the burning ground, will not listen to talk of teeth like pearls; the demon woman eats the eyes without asking 'tooth or pearl', and Sarngalan, seeing it, dies of fright.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sight of the burning ground that steadies the Naladiyar's renouncer kills an unready boy, so the contemplation of the dead body is shown to need a prepared mind and not merely a sight.","gn":["Sarngalan's death is read as a failed contemplation of the body, not only a ghost story.","The quatrain's confident resolve gets a counter-case in which the same evidence destroys the one who sees it."]},{"a":"mm:6:132-153","b":"n:20","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain warns that merciless Death stalks and takes even the child while its mother wails; Gotami wails over her son and asks whether a spirit took him, and Champapathi answers that no spirit takes a life, only the boy's former deed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the quatrain's wailing mother a voice and an answer, and the answer removes the stalking agent the quatrain imagines: what takes the child is his own deed, with fright as its occasion.","gn":["Champapathi's denial of a killing agent stands out against the ethics book's ready personification of Death.","The quatrain's figure of Death as a thief is shown to be one account among others, which a Buddhist epic replaces with karma."]},{"a":"mm:6:154-167","b":"k:259","type":"develops","note":"The couplet sets not killing one creature above a thousand sacrifices; Champapathi calls 'killing is dharma' a false word and counts a mother's offer of her own life for her son's under it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic extends the couplet's rule against killing to giving one's own life in exchange for another's, which the couplet, concerned with animals and sacrifice, does not consider.","gn":["Champapathi's sharp refusal of a mother's offer is seen to rest on the ethics of not killing, not on want of pity.","The couplet's rule is carried to the case of self-offering, where its scope is tested by a sympathetic petitioner."],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:6:154-167","b":"si:5:76-88","type":"tension","note":"In Puhar's festival warriors set their own heads on the altar praying that the offering be a bulwark of the king's strength, and the narrator does not judge it; Champapathi says lives have been given for kings, yet the burning ground holds a thousand kings' shrines.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the plainer reading of lines 164 to 166, the second epic denies what the first epic's rite assumes, that a life given for the king protects him, and it does so in the same city.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:6:168-187","b":"si:9:1-28","type":"tension","note":"Maalathi carries a Brahmin household's dead baby to every shrine, Saathan says the gods give no boon to those without tapas yet hands her a child by becoming it; Gotami, a Brahmin's wife with a dead son, appeals to the gods' boons and every god of the Chakravala says none can give back a life.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The two epics tell the same story of a grieving mother at Puhar's shrines with opposite endings: a god supplies a living child by illusion in one, while in the other the gods together refuse even that, and the mother dies."},{"a":"mm:6:168-187","b":"k:269","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says that for those who have gained the power of tapas even leaping past Death becomes possible; Champapathi, the old ascetic goddess, shows her power by proving that no god can give back a life.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The power over death that tapas earns, in the epic, stops at the one who earned it: even the oldest ascetic goddess and all the gods cannot pass it to the dead.","gn":["Champapathi's display of power is seen as a demonstration of a limit, not a failure of her tapas.","The couplet's 'even' is given a firm boundary: tapas may carry one past death but cannot bring another back."]},{"a":"mm:7:1-14","b":"k:559","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says that when the king bends justice the sky withholds rain; the goddess gives the same chain with the planets inserted between sceptre and rain, and aims it at the heir's desire for a nun.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the rule's reach being widened: what the Kural ties to judging wrongly the epic applies to a prince's private lust, so the sceptre is bent by desire, not only by a verdict.","gn":["The goddess's warning is grounded in a known rule of kingship, which shows she argues from ethics shared beyond her own creed rather than from her power.","The couplet gains a case in which the wrong is not a judgment and the wrongdoer not yet king, which tests how far its bending of justice extends."]},{"a":"mm:7:1-14","b":"pn:186","type":"develops","note":"The Purananuru poem says rice and water are not the world's life, the king is, and he must know it; the goddess says the king's swerving kills life through the failing of rain, so the standing of being the beings' life is lost.","grade":"sound","better":"qualifies","te":"The epic turns the poem's title into a conditional one that a king's own fault cancels, and routes his power over life back through the rain the poem had set below him.","gn":["Lines 11-12 are read against an old formula of kingship that the goddess is citing in order to take away.","The poem's claim that the king, not water, is the world's life gains its limit: in the epic the king governs life only by way of the rain."]},{"a":"mm:7:1-14","b":"mm:20:104-119","type":"develops","note":"The goddess warns the prince to give up his intent on the ascetic girl; he does not, and is cut down by the vidyadhara Kanchanan as he goes to the girl in her disguise.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The warning threatened failing rain and perishing beings, but the fruit comes as one man's sword on the prince alone, so the cosmic stakes she named collapse into a private death."},{"a":"mm:7:15-25","b":"mm:9:42-57","type":"develops","note":"The goddess says only that the cause for the girl's entry into dharma has ripened; at the jewel seat Manimekalai learns that in her birth as Lakshmi a sage foretold that on the day of her trouble the goddess Manimekala would carry her to an island in the south.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bare 'ripened cause' of this kaathai is revealed later as a prophecy made in a former birth, so the goddess is fulfilling an old word, not deciding afresh."},{"a":"mm:7:26-39","b":"si:15:21-41","type":"develops","note":"Maadalan tells how Kovalan named Madhavi's daughter after the sea goddess who saved his ancestor with the words வந்தேன் அஞ்சல் மணிமேகலை யான்; here the goddess, speaking the same words, adds that on that naming night she told Madhavi in a dream that the child would be a creeper of tapas.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the Manimekalai turning a name given in gratitude for merit of giving into a vocation for renunciation, revealed only now by the goddess herself."},{"a":"mm:7:26-39","b":"si:30:24-34","type":"develops","note":"The goddess's dream foretold a creeper of tapas that would leave Kama helpless; in the Silappathikaram's last kaathai Madhavi gives her daughter to the dharma at the Bodhi seat while Kama throws his bow and arrows on the bare ground.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The earlier epic's closing image of Kama disarmed turns out, read with this dream, to be the fulfilment of a prophecy spoken at the child's naming."},{"a":"mm:7:70-84","b":"si:5:76-88","type":"same_image","note":"Both have warriors at the bhuta's altar in Puhar crying that the victorious king take victory (கொற்றம் கொள்க) with a thunder voice as they offer bali; the Silappathikaram stages it as the festival's central rite, the Manimekalai lists it among the night noises a grieving woman hears.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set side by side, the same offering formula moves from the heart of a royal festival to one sound among drunk shipwrights and mantra-men, which shows how the later epic pushes the blood rite to the margin of its story."},{"a":"mm:7:85-97","b":"mm:20:104-119","type":"same_image","note":"The painted image on the pillar of the hall speaks at night to frightened Sudhamathi; later the same image speaks to Kanchanan just after he has killed the prince, telling him the woman he sought was Manimekalai in disguise.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pillar's voice comes once in time, to comfort with knowledge of past births, and once too late, to tell a killer what he did not know, so the same speaking image marks both the gift and the failure of right knowing."},{"a":"mm:7:98-109","b":"mm:11:124-146","type":"develops","note":"The image addresses Sudhamathi with இரவிவன்மன் ஒரு பெரு மகளே, துரகத் தானைத் துச்சயன் தேவி and promises that Lakshmi will come knowing both births; returned from the island, Manimekalai addresses her mothers with the same lines and names them Tarai and Virai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prophecy is fulfilled in the very words of the image, now in the girl's mouth, and the later scene supplies what the image left out: Madhavi too was a sister, Tarai."},{"a":"mm:7:110-134","b":"mm:2:58-75","type":"qualifies","note":"Madhavi tells how the teacher Aravanar taught her that suffering comes of attachment and freedom from it of letting go; here, a renouncer, she sits like a body without life because her daughter has not come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the epic letting its own renouncer fall short of the teaching she received, so taking robes does not by itself end attachment to a child."},{"a":"mm:8:1-18","b":"mm:9:42-57","type":"develops","note":"In the next kaathai Manimekalai, remembering her former birth as Ilakkumi, recalls the sage's word that on a day of grave sorrow the goddess Manimekala would come, lift her up in the dark night and set her on an island in the south.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The waking that she meets as abandonment and a strange rebirth is the foretold step of a prophecy she has not yet remembered, so the epic makes her terror on the shore the unknowing half of a plan the reader learns only afterwards."},{"a":"mm:8:44-63","b":"mm:9:58-71","type":"develops","note":"The sage's prophecy, recalled in kaathai 9, bids her go to the radiant seat where the healer of the disease of birth sits and preaches, where the Naga kings cleared anger and the stain of mind, and worship it, and that very day know her birth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The seat that the narrator first presents as the prize of a war over 'ours' is named in the prophecy by what it did to the warring kings, purging their anger and opening their ears to dharma, so the place that cured possession becomes the place where a grieving girl recovers her past."},{"a":"mm:8:36-43","b":"si:16:194-217","type":"qualifies","note":"Silappathikaram 16 tells how a drunken guard's sword cut Kovalan down as the king's sceptre bent and his former deed came in wrath; his daughter's lament on the island recalls the sword on his chest and the cruel deed ripening.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The earlier epic gives two causes for Kovalan's death, the bent sceptre and the ripened deed, but his daughter's memory keeps only the deed and stretches it over her own plight, so the sequel's first word on the killing drops the king's guilt and makes karma something a family suffers together."},{"a":"mm:8:36-43","b":"n:101","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 101 says an old deed seeks out the one who did it as a calf finds its mother among many cows; Manimekalai says the cruel deed ripened so that her father went to a foreign land and died by the sword, and that it casts 'us' into misery.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's deed that hunts down its own doer explains how karma found Kovalan far from home, yet the daughter's 'us' lets the same ripening fall on her too, which the one-doer rule does not allow, so the pair opens the question whether a deed's fruit can be shared by kin.","gn":["a stated rule that the deed seeks out its own doer, against which her claim to share her father's ripening becomes visible as a widening","a case in which the deed finds its doer in a strange land, and a survivor who counts herself in its fruit, a limit the calf image leaves untested"]},{"a":"mm:8:44-63","b":"k:350","type":"develops","note":"Kural 350 bids one cling to the clinging of the one who has no clinging in order to let clinging go; the two Naga kings, unable to let go of their great attachment to the seat each calls 'ours', stop their war when the great sage calls it 'ours' and preaches from it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's paradox, giving up attachment by attaching to the unattached one, is staged as a scene: the sage does not tell the kings to own nothing but sets his own claim, 'this is ours', in place of theirs, and their war ends because a claim of the one without clinging displaces a claim of clinging.","gn":["a doctrinal key to why the sage answers the kings with the very words of possession they used, instead of a call to give the seat up","a concrete case of the clinging the couplet means (kin kings at war over an object neither can lift) and of the moment the substituted clinging takes hold"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:9:1-15","b":"mm:7:98-109","type":"develops","note":"Manimekala told the sleeping Sudhamathi that within seven days Lakshmi, her younger sister, would come knowing her own birth and Sudhamathi's; here Manimekalai rises from the seat knowing that birth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The goddess's promise to a third person is kept before Manimekalai knows it was made, so the recognition at the seat is staged as the second prophecy fulfilled in the chapter, not a private vision."},{"a":"mm:9:16-30","b":"mm:28:120-137","type":"same_image","note":"In both a Buddhist ascetic foretells that a city will be swallowed, Idavayam by earthquake and Kaveripattinam by the sea, and those who trust the word act on it and are saved from the ruin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic repeats the drowned city as a test of trust in a seer's word: the survivors in both are those who believed a forecast before any sign, which makes heeding the ascetic the epic's form of escape from a city's fate."},{"a":"mm:9:31-41","b":"mm:21:45-60","type":"qualifies","note":"Manimekalai remembers the camp at Kayangarai as a day of worship and the sage's compassionate teaching; the deity of the pillar in kaathai 21 tells that on that same visit Rahulan killed the cook who stumbled with the sage's food.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The recovered memory is partial: what the seat restores to her is the piety of that day, and the deed that will kill her husband has to be supplied later by a god, so knowing one's birth in this epic does not yet mean knowing one's karma."},{"a":"mm:9:42-57","b":"mm:6:206-214","type":"develops","note":"The sage foretold that on a day of sorrow Manimekala would lift her in the dark night and set her on an island in the southern quarter (தென் திசை மருங்கில்); kaathai 6 tells it in the same words: at midnight the goddess carried her south and left her on Manipallavam.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read back from this prophecy, the abduction of kaathai 6 that seemed a goddess's rescue from the prince is shown to be an appointment fixed a birth earlier, which turns chance into the ripening of a cause."},{"a":"mm:9:42-57","b":"mm:21:61-72","type":"develops","note":"The sage foretells Rahulan's death by a glance-poison snake without a cause; the deity of kaathai 21 gives the cause, his killing of the cook, and says Lakshmi's loving refusal to leave him could not turn the deed aside.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together the passages show the epic's karma working in two steps: the prophecy fixes the fruit as certain, and only later is the deed named, with the added point that a wife's devotion, even unto the pyre, does not share or lighten a husband's deed."},{"a":"mm:9:42-57","b":"pn:246","type":"qualifies","note":"Perunkoppendu insists against the elders that her husband's pyre is to her a lotus pool; the sage tells Lakshmi as a plain fact that she will sink into Rahulan's fire, and the epic sends her, through that fire, to a new birth still bound to him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam queen's choice of the pyre is her last word and her karpu's proof, while the Buddhist epic treats the same act as foretold and as no end at all: the bond she dies for carries into the next birth as the attachment that must be undone.","gn":["Lakshmi's foretold death in the fire is set against a known Tamil model of the wife who chooses the pyre, which shows how flatly the sage reports it, without praise.","The queen's claim that the fire and the lotus pool are one meets a work for which the fire leads not to union but to rebirth with the same longing."]},{"a":"mm:9:58-71","b":"mm:10:16-39","type":"develops","note":"The sage promised that the goddess who brought her would appear and say 'this is your husband'; in kaathai 10 Manimekala comes and names Rahulan as Udayakumaran, telling the story of Lakshmi's past.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The husband she longs to be shown turns out to be the prince she has been hiding from, so the promised reunion is delivered as a reason to understand her pull toward him and turn from it."},{"a":"mm:9:58-71","b":"si:23:161-176","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Silappathikaram the goddess of Madurai tells the grieving Kannaki she will see her husband within fourteen days, but only in a god's form; here the grieving Lakshmi is told a goddess will show her husband in a later human birth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two epics answer the same plea of a widow to see her husband again in opposite ways: the first through a heavenly reunion after death, the second through a new birth in which the man shown is the one she must learn to leave."},{"a":"mm:9:58-71","b":"p:277","type":"same_image","note":"The epic calls the teacher whose seat this is the physician of the disease of birth (பிறவிப் பிணி மருத்துவன்); Arunagirinathar calls Murugan of Thiruttani the physician of the disease of becoming (பவரோக வயித்திய நாத) and asks his grace to cut birth's root.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same title names two cures: in the Buddhist epic the physician heals by teaching dharma to those who have cleansed their minds, and his seat alone is left to be worshipped; in the Saiva hymn he heals by grace given to one who calls his name.","gn":["The epithet at line 61 is shown to be a contested title, and the epic's choice to name no god while using it becomes visible.","The hymn's physician-god is seen against an older Tamil use of the title for a teacher whose cure is a doctrine heard, not a grace received."]},{"a":"mm:10:71-93","b":"mm:27:1-24","type":"develops","note":"The goddess foretells that Manimekalai will hear the false words of the many schools and gives her a mantra to take another form; in kaathai 27 she goes among the reckoners of Vanji in an ascetic's borrowed form and questions them one by one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The debate chapter is set up as a test prepared for in advance: the disguise exists because the goddess expects no one to tell a young girl the truth, so the schools are heard under a mask chosen against their condescension."},{"a":"mm:10:40-49","b":"mm:4:105-125","type":"develops","note":"Sudhamathi's sermon on the body born of deeds failed to turn Udayakumaran once he saw Manimekalai; the goddess now reveals that he is her husband of a former birth and that her heart, too, still clings to him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The failure of the grove sermon is given a cause in karma after the fact: the pull between the two was not mere sight but a bond carried across births, which is why words could not break it and a goddess had to carry her away."},{"a":"mm:10:40-49","b":"ku:49","type":"tension","note":"The akam heroine asks that, when this life turns into the next, the same man be her husband and she the one close to his heart; the goddess tells Manimekalai that exactly this has come about, her heart still bound to her former husband, and calls it seed falling on scorched saline ground.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bond across births that the akam poem asks for as the highest pledge of love is, in the Buddhist epic, the very danger to the merit that would end births, so the same wish is fulfilled and judged a loss.","gn":["The goddess's verdict is seen to overturn a fixed ideal of Tamil love poetry, not just a passing desire of the prince.","The heroine's vow gets a sequel in which it is granted, and its cost, the turning of rebirth into a trap, is shown."]},{"a":"mm:10:40-49","b":"n:38","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain says a small act of virtue that reaches worthy hands spreads like a banyan from a tiny seed; the goddess says Lakshmi's meal to a sage will not go to waste but will cut off her births, and also that the giver herself, as a seed of dharma, could be lost on barren ground.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic agrees that a gift to the worthy grows, but changes its harvest from a covering shade of merit to the end of rebirth, and adds that the giver's own attachments can still waste what the gift has sown.","gn":["The goddess's claim at 41 is placed within an ethics-book teaching on giving to the worthy, which marks how far its promised fruit goes beyond that teaching.","The quatrain's growth image is shown to depend on the giver as well as the receiver, a condition it does not state."],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:10:50-70","b":"t:2110","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular bids the seeker hold to the Lord's feet, by whom strong deeds perish, and says the Lord will set him among the gods; Aravanan calls the Buddha's footprints on the hill the feet that destroy faults, and the next unit says the sisters who worshipped there were reborn beside Lakshmi.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both give the feet power over past deeds, but the Saiva feet belong to a Lord who acts now and places the soul, while the Buddhist feet are the trace of a teacher who left long ago, and their fruit comes from the worshippers' own act ripening in the next birth.","gn":["The footprint hill is seen as a Buddhist answer to the feet-devotion of Tamil theism, with the giver of grace replaced by a place and a remembered teaching.","The verse's agent, the Lord who sets the soul among the gods, stands out as the thing a rival tradition does without while keeping the image of the feet."]},{"a":"mm:10:71-93","b":"pn:18","type":"qualifies","note":"Kudapulaviyanar tells the Pandya that food makes the body (உண்டி முதற்றே உணவின் பிண்டம்), so those who bind water to land give body and life, and bids him build tanks; the goddess uses the same phrase, the body is a lump of food, to give Manimekalai a mantra that cuts off her own hunger.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same maxim drives two answers to hunger: the Sangam poet makes it a king's duty to feed a people by irrigation, the epic turns it into an ascetic's private release from hunger, the first step before the bowl that will feed others.","gn":["The goddess's sudden afterthought at 90 is heard as a known saying about kings and food, which foreshadows Manimekalai's later role as feeder of the hungry.","The poem's maxim is shown carried from the king's public works to a single renouncer's body, where the remedy is a mantra, not water."],"gr":3},{"a":"mm:10:71-93","b":"k:225","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet ranks the strength of enduring hunger below the strength of removing others' hunger; the goddess gives Manimekalai the first strength by mantra, and the patikam (manimekalai:0:51-68) says the bowl for feeding others comes next.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic follows the couplet's order of rank but removes the effort from the lower strength: enduring hunger is granted as a gift, so the renouncer is freed for the higher task the couplet values, feeding others.","gn":["The hunger mantra is seen as a step in a known scale of virtue rather than a magic convenience.","The couplet's ranking is shown worked out in a life, where the lesser strength is a means to the greater rather than a rival to it."]},{"a":"mm:11:37-58","b":"mm:14:79-94","type":"develops","note":"Aputhiran, stranded on Manipallavam, let the bowl into Gomukhi bidding it rise once a year and enter the hand of anyone who keeps compassion and guards lives; here it rises into Manimekalai's hand on that day.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bowl's arrival is not a goddess's gift but the meeting of a condition laid down by its last owner, so the kaathai tests Manimekalai before the reader is told what the test was."},{"a":"mm:11:37-58","b":"mm:12:104-121","type":"develops","note":"Deepathilakai says Aravanan will tell Manimekalai the bowl's nature (52); in the next kaathai Aravana Adigal says she has the bowl and gives the one dharma common to men and gods, curing hunger.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The goddess hands over the object and defers its meaning to a human teacher, so the epic places authority over the bowl's use with the Buddhist monk rather than the island's deity."},{"a":"mm:11:37-58","b":"n:99","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar's ascetic's bowl (தவசி கடிஞை) is filled slowly at door after door by householders' small gifts; Manimekalai's bowl, held by a renunciant, never fails and only the takers' hands tire.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Set together, the two bowls reverse the flow between householder and ascetic: the quatrain's begging bowl is the image of a giver's merit filling, while the epic's is an ascetic's vessel that gives without end.","gn":["the bowl's inexhaustibility is seen against the ordinary begging bowl it inverts, which the epic never needs to describe","the quatrain's image of the slowly filled bowl gains its opposite, which shows that its point is about the giver's accumulation, not the bowl"]},{"a":"mm:11:21-36","b":"k:10","type":"develops","note":"Kural 10 says those who reach the Lord's feet swim the great sea of births; Deepathilakai calls the feet of the Lord of dharma on Samantam the ship of dharma that frees from the great sea called birth (23-25).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic takes the Kural's unnamed Lord and his feet as the Buddha's footprint on a named peak and turns swimming into being carried by a ship, so the couplet's open theism becomes a pilgrimage relic served by the gods.","gn":["Deepathilakai's image is shown to rest on the Kural's opening chapter, giving the Buddhist claim the Kural's wording of release","the couplet's இறைவன் is shown open to sectarian claiming, read here by a Buddhist work as the Buddha"]},{"a":"mm:11:76-98","b":"pn:18","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 18 tells a Pandya king that those who give food give life (உண்டி கொடுத்தோர் உயிர்கொடுத் தோரே) and that land waiting on the sky does not serve its ruler, so he must build tanks; Deepathilakai gives the same line (96) after a famine ended only by a god's rain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The maxim moves from a king's public works to a renunciant's alms-bowl, and the remedy for a failing sky moves from tanks a ruler digs to a god's gift and a bowl that never empties.","gn":["line 96 is shown to be a borrowed Sangam maxim, so the goddess's sermon carries the authority of old royal counsel","the poem's maxim gains a later life in which the duty to feed passes from the state to a single compassionate giver"]},{"a":"mm:11:76-98","b":"k:225","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 225 ranks the strength of those who endure hunger, read by its commentators as ascetics, below that of those who remove it; here a goddess tells a girl who has just renounced that removing hunger is beyond praise and the true way of the world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic dissolves the couplet's ranking of giver over ascetic by putting the bowl in a renunciant's hand, so the one who endures hunger becomes the one who ends it.","gn":["the sermon's praise of the hunger-curer is seen as an answer to the Kural's ranking, not only a commonplace","the couplet's opposition between ascetic and giver is shown not to be fixed, since a renunciant may be the giver"]},{"a":"mm:11:76-98","b":"k:1042","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1042 calls poverty 'a sinner' (இன்மை எனவொரு பாவி) that comes with this world and the next both lost; Deepathilakai calls hunger 'the sinner' (பசிப் பிணி என்னும் பாவி, 80) that destroys birth, learning, modesty and beauty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural speaks to the poor man about what poverty takes from him, even merit for the next world; the epic speaks to the one who can cure it, so the merit the poor man loses becomes the giver's highest dharma.","gn":["the list of hunger's ravages is seen to extend a Kural formula, which places the sermon within the ethics books' account of want","the couplet's loss of the next world gains a remedy outside the poor man, in another's gift"]},{"a":"mm:11:99-123","b":"mm:10:40-49","type":"develops","note":"The goddess told Lakshmi that the dharma of the meal Saathusakkaran ate would not leave her and would end her births; here Manimekalai names this bowl as that meal's fruit (105-106).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prophecy's fruit arrives first not as release but as a means of further giving, so in the epic merit ripens into the power to make more merit before it ends births.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:11:99-123","b":"n:279","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar calls liberality milk a cow yields gladly to its calf, against the base who yield only when squeezed; Manimekalai wants the bowl to flow at the hungry's faces as a mother's breast flows at her newborn's (114-118), and Deepathilakai says it flows only for those who feed from compassion (120-121).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the quatrain's distinction between glad and forced giving into a property of the vessel itself, which will not yield to a giver whose gift is not compassion.","gn":["the mother's-breast simile and the bowl's condition are seen to rest on an old ethics image of giving as lactation","the quatrain's moral distinction gains a story in which the manner of giving decides whether anything is given at all"]},{"a":"mm:11:99-123","b":"t:267","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular says joy and sorrow are settled by former deeds and that those who see their joy and still do not give know no dharma; Manimekalai sees the prosperous enjoying the fruit of dharma sown (108-109) while the hungry stand ragged at their gates.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A Saiva and a Buddhist text agree that karma explaining who is fed does not excuse withholding, and the epic adds that the giver's own gift is the deed that will return as a means of giving.","gn":["the unit's view of the rich at their gates is seen to be shared doctrine across the schools, not a Buddhist peculiarity","Tirumular's argument gains a case in which the deed of giving returns, the bowl that is the fruit of a past meal"]},{"a":"mm:11:124-146","b":"mm:7:98-109","type":"develops","note":"The goddess told Sudhamathi, as Veerai, Tharai's younger sister, that in seven days Lakshmi would return knowing her birth; Madhavi cries that the seventh day has come and Manimekalai arrives to name Tharai and Veerai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The promise and its keeping frame Manimekalai's island journey as a timed rebirth-recognition, and Madhavi's near loss of faith on the last day sets the goddess's truthfulness against a mother's fear."},{"a":"mm:12:43-52","b":"mm:10:71-93","type":"qualifies","note":"The goddess says Tarai and Virai were reborn beside Manimekalai because they worshipped at the Padapankaya hill when Aravanar bade them; Aravanar now tells that afterwards one died drunk under an elephant and the other leapt from a terrace.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Held together, the two accounts show the epic letting one act of worship outlast a drunken death and a suicide: the manner of dying does not cancel the merit, which is why the two women now stand beside the heroine."},{"a":"mm:12:15-30","b":"n:108","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain says that knowing people still do blameworthy things because of deeds done before; the epic calls Virai's drunken death and Tarai's fall the ripening of a cruel deed.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic applies the quatrain's explanation to a death and then, at 46 to 48, names the drink and the leap, so a past deed is the cause behind the act while the act itself stays on the record.","gn":["the epic's bare phrase வெவ் வினை உருப்ப gains a stated mechanism: the old deed works by producing the fault itself","the quatrain's abstract puzzle gains a named case in which the blameworthy act is fatal and its doer is reborn"]},{"a":"mm:12:43-52","b":"k:379","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet asks why those who welcome good fortune grieve when it turns; Aravanar tells a king who has lost both queens not to grieve, since it is the fruit of old deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet grounds equanimity in consistency toward one's own fortune; the epic's monk grounds it in karma and speaks it to a mourner, showing the same counsel turned from self-discipline into consolation for another's loss.","gn":["Aravanar's curt consolation gains the Kural's argument from consistency, which keeps it from sounding like indifference","the couplet gains a hard case: bereavement, where the counsel must be said by one person to another"]},{"a":"mm:12:31-42","b":"t:1451","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular bids refuge in the shade of the feet of the one God present in all ten directions, so that the sea of deeds will not reach; Aravanar goes to worship the footprints of the First Lord on one hill because they wash away hard deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both texts make worship of feet dissolve karma, but the Saiva feet are everywhere because their Lord is, while the Buddhist feet are a print left on a particular hill by a teacher of the past, so the shared gesture rests on opposite accounts of where the cleansing power lies.","gn":["the epic's footprint worship is shown to use a devotional idiom the Saiva texts also use, against which its relic-and-place character stands out","Tirumular's claim that the feet are in every direction gains a contrast with a rival tradition whose sacred feet are fixed in one place"]},{"a":"mm:12:83-103","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says only those who reach the Lord's feet cross the great sea of births; Aravanar says those who hear the Buddha's dharma pass beyond painful birth and vows to praise the Bodhi Lord's feet in every birth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic names the couplet's unnamed feet as the Buddha's but moves the crossing onto hearing his teaching, and its worshipper expects many more births before that hearing, so the feet become a lifelong devotion rather than the crossing itself.","gn":["Aravanar's vow gains a reading against the Kural's opening chapter, which shows how much of the shared idiom a Buddhist could take over","the couplet's unnamed Lord gains a test case: a reader of another faith who fills the name and changes the means"]},{"a":"mm:12:83-103","b":"mm:10:1-15","type":"same_image","note":"Freshly aware of her past birth, Manimekalai hails the Buddha at his seat as a young sun risen over a world that had lost its light; Aravanar foretells the next Buddha as a sun rising to clear the stain of minds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sun image moves from praise of a Buddha already come to prophecy of one yet to come, so the epic places its heroine between two dawns, in the age of mist that Aravanar describes at 63 to 65."},{"a":"mm:12:104-121","b":"mm:29:43-54","type":"develops","note":"Aravanar tells Manimekalai she will not take up the teaching until the events her presence causes have happened; seventeen kaathais later she asks him for the true doctrine and he begins with the two means of knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The deferral is kept: the epic withholds its doctrinal core until its heroine has fed the hungry, emptied a prison and heard the other schools, so experience is made the condition of hearing the Buddha's logic."},{"a":"mm:12:104-121","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"The couplet ranks the strength of those who endure hunger below that of those who end others' hunger; an ascetic here names ending hunger as the one dharma equal for men and gods and calls it the dharma of tapas.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic answers the couplet's ranking by giving the higher strength to a renunciant: with the bowl, the one who has left the household can feed others instead of only enduring hunger, and feeding becomes the ascetic's own discipline.","gn":["Aravanar's claim gains the Kural's prior ranking of feeding above endurance, which explains why he can call feeding a form of tapas","the couplet's opposition between ascetic and feeder gains a case where one person holds both roles"]},{"a":"mm:13:1-19","b":"mm:15:15-28","type":"develops","note":"The cow that licks, suckles and guards the abandoned infant here is the cow in whose womb Aputtiran is reborn after he dies on Manipallavam.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The mother who would not pity her child is replaced across two births by the cow, which first nurses him and then bears him, so the name 'cow's son' that the brahmins throw at him as an insult becomes literally true and honoured."},{"a":"mm:13:92-115","b":"mm:14:1-15","type":"develops","note":"Aputtiran begs with an ordinary bowl to feed the helpless and eats the leftovers; in the next kaathai, unable to feed travellers who come in the rain, he is given the inexhaustible bowl by Chinthadevi.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The divine bowl goes to someone already living as a beggar who feeds others before himself, so the epic presents it as answering an alms-giving done with nothing rather than as the source of his charity."},{"a":"mm:13:20-48","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet ranks not taking one creature's life above a thousand oblations; the boy takes the cow from a sacrifice and is caught, beaten and called a thief.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet weighs abstention against sacrifice as a private choice of merit, while the epic shows what acting on it costs inside a sacrificing community: the rescuer is branded a thief and expelled from his caste.","gn":["The boy's theft gains a recognised ethical rule behind it, which the brahmins' charge of theft leaves out.","The couplet's comparison gains a case where refusing the sacrifice carries a social price, which the couplet does not count."],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:13:57-69","b":"n:195","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain calls good and bad caste mere words and makes lineage depend on austerity, learning and effort; Aputtiran cites the brahmins' own seers born of cow, deer, tiger and jackal.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain denies birth rank from outside by naming what truly makes a line, while Aputtiran denies it from inside the brahmins' tradition, so the critique becomes a charge of inconsistency rather than a rival standard.","gn":["Aputtiran's retort is given the general principle that his examples only imply.","The quatrain's bare assertion gains an argument that defenders of birth rank cannot dismiss as foreign to their own books."],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:13:70-91","b":"k:134","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says a brahmin's birth is ruined when his conduct fails; the brahmin witness declares the boy an outcaste because his mother confessed she acted unlike a brahmin wife.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet lets a man's own conduct unmake his birth, and the witness stretches this into a son's birth unmade by his mother's conduct, a step the couplet does not take and one Aputtiran then turns back on the Vedic seers' own mothers.","gn":["The witness's verdict is seen as a twisting of a recognised rule rather than plain prejudice.","The couplet's rule is shown to be open to use as hereditary taint once conduct is read through a mother."]},{"a":"mm:13:92-115","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"The couplet says nothing shames the tongue more than begging, even water for a cow; Aputtiran, the cow's son, begs from house to house to feed the blind, the deaf and the lame.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet holds that even the worthiest cause cannot make asking honourable, while the epic makes begging on others' behalf its hero's virtue and the ground of the divine bowl; the conflict depends on reading the couplet's cow example as covering begging for another's need, which it seems to.","gn":["Aputtiran's begging stands out as a choice made against a known ethic of shame, not mere want.","The couplet's limit case meets a story that answers it by honouring a beggar for others' sake."]},{"a":"mm:13:92-115","b":"s:360","type":"qualifies","note":"The Siddhar says he has resolved to beg from any caste and lacks nothing; Aputtiran, driven from caste with stones in his bowl, begs from every house to feed the helpless and eats what is left.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Begging across caste lines is the Siddhar's chosen freedom and his sufficiency, while for Aputtiran it is what is left after caste expels him, and he turns it outward to feed others, so the same act carries renunciation in one and charity in the other.","gn":["The epic beggar's stripped life is seen as close to a renouncer's, though he claims no renunciation.","The Siddhar's self-sufficiency is seen to leave out the feeding of others that the epic puts at the centre of begging."]},{"a":"mm:14:1-15","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"Kural 225 ranks the strength to bear hunger below the strength to remove it; Aputhiran, who lives on begged food, grieves not at his own hunger but at finding no means to remove the travellers', and it is then that the goddess gives him the bowl.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the couplet's higher strength given to a man with no wealth at all: the epic makes the power to end others' hunger a gift answering grief, not a store of means, which the couplet's comparison of two strengths leaves unexamined.","gn":["a rule that explains why the goddess answers his grief at not feeding others rather than his own need","a case where the higher strength is wanted by one who has only the lower, so the couplet's ranking becomes a longing rather than a description of the rich"]},{"a":"mm:14:16-35","b":"k:25","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 25 makes Indra the witness to the power of one who has quenched the five senses; in the epic Indra's throne trembles at a beggar's feeding of all creatures, and he comes in person to reward it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic moves the test that brings Indra down from the ascetic's restraint to a giver's feeding, and then shows the witness turning rival, since the god's reward will undo the giving it acknowledges.","gn":["the known Tamil rule that Indra's disturbance testifies to a human's power, so the shaken throne reads as a verdict on Aputhiran","a story in which Indra's witness is followed by Indra's interference, which the couplet's bare 'witness enough' does not foresee"]},{"a":"mm:14:36-48","b":"k:222","type":"develops","note":"Kural 222 says giving is good even if there were no higher world; offered heaven's reward, Aputhiran laughs that heaven is a place where no one does dharma or gives, and asks what its king could give him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's hypothetical, giving is good even without heaven, becomes in Aputhiran's mouth a judgment that heaven is worse than earth for a giver, because there is no hunger there for giving to meet.","gn":["a statement of the principle on which his laughter rests, that giving is valued apart from any reward","an argument the couplet does not make: heaven is not merely unnecessary to giving but excludes it"]},{"a":"mm:14:49-64","b":"k:19","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 19 says that if the sky gives no rain, neither giving nor austerity will last; Indra's abundant rain leaves Aputhiran's bowl idle for want of takers, and the hall of giving becomes a gambling den.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Rain is the condition of giving in the couplet, but the epic shows that rain in full measure can end giving too, since giving needs someone in want, so plenty sustains the giver's means and destroys his occasion.","gn":["the Kural's rule that giving depends on rain, against which Indra's rain reads as a pointed reversal","the other limit of its rule: too much of what makes giving possible makes it pointless"]},{"a":"mm:14:49-64","b":"pn:173","type":"qualifies","note":"Puram 173 praises Pannan's house, where the noise of eating (ஊணொலி அரவம்) sounds like birds in a fruiting tree, as the home of the doctor of hunger; the epic uses the fruit-tree birds for the crowd at Aputhiran's hall (26 to 27), then tells how the hall's noise of eating (ஊண் ஒலி அரவம், 60) fell silent once rain removed hunger.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic takes the Sangam praise of a patron's noisy kitchen and shows its condition: the sound that proves a giver's fame lasts only as long as others are hungry, so the doctor of hunger is unmade by a world without the disease.","gn":["the phrase ஊண் ஒலி அரவம் is shown to be the Sangam patron's formula, so its silencing marks the end of Aputhiran as a patron of the poor","Pannan's praise gains a counter-case in which the same sound stops, exposing that the patron's glory depends on the hunger he cures"]},{"a":"mm:14:49-64","b":"mm:11:76-98","type":"qualifies","note":"Deepatilakai tells Manimekalai that in a famine Indra's rain brought plenty and saved the brahmin about to eat dog's flesh; here Indra's rain, sent as a retort to Aputhiran, leaves his bowl idle and his hall to gamblers.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic gives Indra's rain two faces, the mercy that ends a famine and the spite that ends a giver's work, and in both tellings sets the human hand that feeds above the god's plenty, which Deepatilakai's praise alone does not show."},{"a":"mm:14:79-94","b":"k:230","type":"develops","note":"Kural 230 says nothing is more bitter than dying, yet even death is sweet when giving is out of reach; alone on an uninhabited island with no one to feed, Aputhiran will not keep his own life with the bowl, gives it away and starves himself to death.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic takes the couplet's hyperbole literally: a giver cut off from anyone to give to chooses death, and refuses even the means of life that giving had put in his hands.","gn":["a general rule that turns his fast from despair into a judgment on a life without giving","a case where the couplet's ranking is acted on, together with the refusal to turn a giving-vessel to one's own use, which the couplet does not state"]},{"a":"mm:14:79-94","b":"mm:11:37-58","type":"develops","note":"Aputhiran lets the bowl go into the Gomukhi pool to rise one day a year into the hands of one who wears compassion and dharma; in kaathai 11 it rises from Gomukhi into Manimekalai's hands as she circles the pool with Deepatilakai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The receiving scene is the fulfilment of a test set by the bowl's first owner, so the bowl's coming to Manimekalai is a verdict on her compassion as well as a gift.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:14:95-104","b":"mm:25:96-114","type":"develops","note":"Aputhiran dies on Manipallavam with the purpose of sustaining lives and is born in Savakam, where he had meant to carry the bowl against a famine; in kaathai 25 the minister tells the king that before his birth Savakam lost its rain for twelve years, and that since he was born the sky has not failed and no creature has known hunger.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The famine Aputhiran set out to relieve with the bowl is ended by his birth instead, so the purpose held at death is fulfilled by what he becomes rather than by what he carries."},{"a":"mm:14:95-104","b":"si:27:78-95","type":"same_image","note":"Both epics use one formula for death by fasting: Kavunthi 'gave up her life by the vow of not eating' (உண்ணா நோன்போ டுயிர்பதிப் பெயர்த்ததும், 27:83) asking whether the couple's deeds had struck at her too; Aputhiran dies by the same vow (உண்ணா நோன்போடு உயிர் பதிப் பெயர்ப்புழி, 95).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The twin epics give the same fasting death opposite fruits: Kavunthi's ends a life overtaken by others' karma in grief, while Aputhiran's is a passage to a birth in which his purpose of feeding goes on."},{"a":"mm:15:1-14","b":"mm:14:65-78","type":"develops","note":"In kaathai 14 Aputhiran hears that rain has failed in Savakam and sets out to carry his bowl there; here the cow's sage foretells, in Savakam itself, a protector born to make the rain abound and guard lives (மழை வளம் சுரப்பவும் மன் உயிர் ஓம்பவும்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The errand the shipwreck cut short is fulfilled by rebirth: Aputhiran reaches the rainless land not as a beggar with a bowl but as its rain-bringing king, so the epic moves his charity from alms to kingship across a death."},{"a":"mm:15:15-28","b":"mm:14:95-104","type":"qualifies","note":"Kaathai 14 says he left his body on Manipallavam with the intent to sustain unappeased lives and arose from a cow's womb in Savakam; here the cause of that womb is that his unwavering heart never let go of the cow that had guarded him (ஒல்கா உள்ளத்து ஒழியான் ஆதலின்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two passages give rebirth two causes at two levels: compassion for all lives decides the land and the work, while a personal debt of gratitude decides the very mother, so a private bond shapes the birth inside a universal vow."},{"a":"mm:15:46-54","b":"mm:14:49-64","type":"develops","note":"In kaathai 14 Indra's gift of plenty left Aputhiran's bowl with no takers and his hall silent; here Aravanar says want has come again despite the Kaveri and forbids laying the same bowl aside.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bowl's worth is shown to depend wholly on there being hunger: plenty once made it useless and drove its keeper to death, and now a season of want is the reason it must come back into a girl's hands."},{"a":"mm:15:46-54","b":"k:226","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet calls relieving the destitute's ruinous hunger the place where a man of wealth should store it; Aravanar likens laying aside the hunger-ending bowl to the gods keeping back their leftover nectar, and calls it unfit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet keeps a giver's profit in view by making charity a kind of saving, while the epic's bowl has no owner to profit and treats any keeping back as the gods' hoard, so feeding the hungry is pressed past prudence into a duty with no return.","gn":["Aravanar's rule against laying the bowl aside gains a contrast with the ethics book's view of charity as a store for the giver, which shows how far the bowl's keeper is from any owner's interest.","The couplet's banking image gains a case with no depositor at all, a bowl whose holder gains nothing, which exposes the self-regard the image keeps."]},{"a":"mm:15:46-54","b":"k:545","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who wields the sceptre rightly; Aravanar asks whether the king of heaven or the just-sceptred king has failed, and says want has come though the Kaveri flows. The link holds on the reading that the questions at 46-47 clear both.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic grants the Kural's premise that a just king brings plenty and still finds hunger in his land, so hunger becomes a want no sceptre cures and the place where the renouncer's bowl, not the king, must act.","gn":["The rhetorical questions at 46-47 are explained: they answer the Kural's doctrine that want betrays a bent sceptre, which is why the speaker must clear the king before calling for the bowl.","The couplet's promise of plenty under a just king gains a counter-case where both god and king are blameless and the people still hunger."]},{"a":"mm:15:55-70","b":"mm:14:49-64","type":"same_image","note":"When Aputhiran's hall fell silent in plenty, rakes, libertines and idlers (விடரும் தூர்த்தரும் விட்டேற்றாளரும்) took it over with dice; here the same rakes and libertines (விடரும் தூர்த்தரும்) crowd round Manimekalai as she enters the street with the bowl.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The town's idlers mark both ends of the bowl's story: they fill the hall when the bowl is useless and gather in pity when it returns, so the bowl's renewed use is first met by the very crowd that had displaced it."},{"a":"mm:15:55-70","b":"ud:80","type":"same_image","note":"The Udayana Kumara Kaviyam tells how Yugi, feigning madness and sense by turns, passed along the great streets with crowds about him to the prison where the Vatsa king was held; the Manimekalai likens the crowd's pity for Manimekalai with her bowl in the great street to the pity felt for Yugi's feigned sickness when he freed the Vatsa king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The simile reads the nun's garb as a disguise like Yugi's, pitied by onlookers yet hiding a purpose of release, and the later Jain epic's street scene shows the crowd and the prison that the Manimekalai's one-line allusion assumes its hearers know."},{"a":"mm:15:55-70","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"The couplet says nothing is more degrading to the tongue than begging, even water for a cow; Manimekalai enters the street with a begging bowl and, at 71-74, calls receiving a chaste wife's alms a thing of great worth, while the crowd grieves at the sight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The town's grief voices the Kural's shame of asking, and the nun's claim answers it with a begging that asks nothing for herself and seeks the worthiest giver, so the two disagree about whether all asking shames the asker.","gn":["The crowd's pity (சிந்தை நோய் கூர) is shown to rest on the ethics books' contempt for begging, which the nun's alms round has to overcome.","The couplet's limit case, begging even for a cow, meets a beggar whose bowl came down from a cow-born king and feeds others, a case its absolute rule does not consider."]},{"a":"mm:15:71-86","b":"mm:16:117-135","type":"develops","note":"Kayasandigai sends Manimekalai to Athirai's house as the one wife of rain-giving chastity; the next kaathai ends with Athirai putting the first food, the 'medicine for life', into the bowl (ஆதிரை இட்டனள் ஆருயிர்மருந்து).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Manimekalai's rule that the bowl's first alms must come from a pathini is carried out at once, and the giver chosen is a wife whose husband had left her for a courtesan, so the courtesan's daughter's bowl is first filled by such a wife.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:15:71-86","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says the wife who worships her husband and not a god can bid the rain fall; Kayasandigai praises Athirai as standing alone among the house-dwelling wives of rain-giving chastity (வான் தருகற்பின் மனை உறை மகளிரின்) and so the right giver of the bowl's first alms.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The epic takes the couplet's rain-giving wife as a known type and makes it a rank, one supreme among many, and a qualification for feeding a Buddhist bowl, so domestic chastity is set to work for the renouncer's charity.","gn":["Kayasandigai's phrase is shown to rest on the Kural's claim, so her choice of Athirai appeals to a test the hearers already accept.","The couplet, which speaks of any such wife, gains a use: chastity's power over the rain becomes a standard for whose alms are worthiest, a ranking the couplet does not make."]},{"a":"mm:15:71-86","b":"si:21:1-16","type":"same_image","note":"In the Silappathikaram Kannaki lists the chaste wives of Puhar and their wonders as she turns on Madurai; here Kayasandigai names Athirai as the one who stands alone among Puhar's rain-giving wives.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pathinis of the same city serve the twin epics in opposite ways: Kannaki calls on them to back a curse that burns a city, while Manimekalai seeks one of them to give the first food to a bowl that feeds it."},{"a":"mm:16:22-43","b":"mm:16:117-135","type":"develops","note":"The voice in the burning ground names the merchant Chandradattan and his ship; at the end of the tale Saathuvan comes home on Chandradattan's ship, as foretold.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prophecy that stopped Aathirai's death is kept only after Saathuvan has converted his captors, so the voice in effect promised a return that his own learning and argument had still to earn."},{"a":"mm:16:22-43","b":"mm:2:42-57","type":"qualifies","note":"Madhavi listed the ways of the pathini: die at once with a sigh, enter the blazing fire as if it were a cool pool, or wear down the body in vows; Aathirai takes the second way, and the fire will not have her, and she leaves it like a woman coming back from a bath (16:46).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Madhavi's list assumes the fire completes the pathini's choice, but Aathirai's fire refuses her, so the epic shows that a pathini's power can overrule even her own resolve to die and makes Madhavi's simile of the cool pool literally true."},{"a":"mm:16:90-106","b":"mm:27:263-276","type":"tension","note":"Saathuvan argues from the insensible corpse and the dream journey that a life leaves the body and enters another fitted by its deeds; the materialist of kaathai 27 holds that awareness ends when the elements part, that inference is no means of knowledge, and that a next world of fruits is a lie.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The materialist's challenge is met eleven kaathais before he states it, by a merchant using exactly the inference the materialist refuses to accept, so the pair shows that the epic's case for rebirth stands or falls on whether inference counts as knowledge."},{"a":"mm:16:78-89","b":"k:339","type":"develops","note":"Saathuvan tells the Naga chief that the dying of the born and the birth of the dead are like sleeping and waking, the simile of Kural 339 in other words, and draws from it that good deeds lead to a good world and ill to hell.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's simile is a calm statement of impermanence, but Saathuvan turns it into the premise of an argument against liquor and killing, showing that the image of sleep and waking implies a self that wakes again and must answer for its acts.","gn":["Saathuvan's premise gains a formulation already familiar as a saying, which is why he can offer it to a sceptic without proof","the couplet gains a moral consequence it never draws, that what one does before sleeping decides where one wakes"]},{"a":"mm:16:1-21","b":"k:920","type":"develops","note":"Saathuvan is ruined by a courtesan and by dice and cast off penniless; among the Nagas he refuses the woman and liquor offered him, and comes home with treasure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names double-minded women, liquor and dice as the company of those Fortune has left, and the story runs the rule both ways: two of the three strip Saathuvan of wealth, and his refusal of a woman and liquor on the island is what brings fortune back.","gn":["the tale's two halves gain a single rule that ties his ruin to his later refusal","the couplet gains a reversal it does not state, that leaving the three restores what keeping them lost"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:16:44-59","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet's highest wife commands the rain; here the chaste wives who bring down the rain themselves worship Aathirai, whom fire would not burn.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic places a rank above the Kural's summit of wifely power, and grounds it not in daily worship of the husband but in a wife's readiness to die for a husband who had deserted her for a courtesan.","gn":["Aathirai's praise gains a fixed measure, the rain-bringing wife, against which her rank can be stated","the couplet gains the sign that its power is not the highest, and that it can be tested against a husband who did nothing to deserve it"]},{"a":"mm:16:107-116","b":"k:256","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural argues that no one would sell flesh if no one killed to eat; Saathuvan, told the Nagas cannot live without meat, lets them keep only the flesh of beasts that die of age and bids them harm no living thing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats the eater as the cause of all killing, while Saathuvan finds the one flesh no killing supplies and so gives people who cannot give up meat a way to keep the Kural's end without its whole demand.","gn":["Saathuvan's concession gains the reasoning that makes it a principle: what is wrong is the killing that eating causes","the couplet gains a case where its rule must bend to what a people can bear, and a shape the bending can take"]},{"a":"mm:16:90-106","b":"t:2106","type":"qualifies","note":"Saathuvan argues that the departing life enters the body its deeds put on, as a reason for the Nagas to do good; Tirumular bids the reader seek the way by which, leaving this body, one does not enter another.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same passage of the life into a new body is good news in the epic, a reason to act well for a better world, and the thing to be escaped in Tirumular, so the pair shows that a merchant's ethics of rebirth stops short of the release both traditions put beyond it.","gn":["Saathuvan's argument gains the reminder that a good rebirth is still a rebirth, which the epic leaves to its later teaching","Tirumular's verse gains a reasoned account, from the corpse and the dream, of the entry into another body that it simply assumes"]},{"a":"mm:16:117-135","b":"k:227","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the evil disease called hunger will not touch one used to sharing his food; Aathirai fills the inexhaustible bowl saying 'may the disease of hunger be cut off from the whole earth'.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet promises that sharing guards the sharer from the disease of hunger, while Aathirai's gift, poured into a bowl that never empties, aims to cure the disease for everyone, so the pair moves from the giver's safety to a cure for the world.","gn":["Aathirai's phrase பசிப் பிணி gains the couplet's account of hunger as a disease that sharing keeps away","the couplet gains an image of sharing that no longer protects one household but is meant to empty hunger from the earth"]},{"a":"mm:17:1-16","b":"k:227","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says the fire-disease of hunger seldom touches one who habitually shares food (பாத்தூண்); the unit opens on a bowl first filled with a chaste wife's share (பாத்தூண், 1) and on a woman with the elephant-fire disease whose hunger came from old deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's protection covers only the sharer's own body, while the epic shows a fire-hunger that no habit of one's own sharing could have kept off because a former deed sent it, so the cure has to come from someone else's share.","gn":["the epic's alms bowl gains the Kural's name for hunger as a fire-disease that sharing holds off, which makes the bowl a medicine","the couplet gains a case beyond its reach: hunger sent by karma, curable only by another's gift and not prevented by one's own"]},{"a":"mm:17:33-48","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"The couplet ranks the strength of those who endure hunger below that of those who remove it; Viruchikan eats once in twelve years, and when his fruit is crushed his fasting power becomes a curse of hunger on another, which Manimekalai's bowl removes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The story stages the couplet's ranking and adds a reason for it: the endurer's power, once offended, can itself manufacture hunger in others, so only the giver's power ends suffering rather than moving it about.","gn":["the epic's sage and girl gain a stated rule that ranks them, the faster below the feeder","the couplet gains a case where the endurer's strength is not merely lesser but harmful, causing the very hunger the giver must remove"]},{"a":"mm:17:33-48","b":"si:10:229-248","type":"qualifies","note":"Kavunthi's penance-given curse (தவம்தரு சாபம்) turns two scorners into jackals for twelve months; Viruchikan's curse binds Kayasandigai to hunger for twelve years. Both are ascetics' curses with a fixed term, but here the cursed woman names her own ripening deed as the cause and her husband calls the curse causeless.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set beside the Silappathikaram, where the curse simply answers an insult, the Manimekalai makes the ascetic's curse an instrument through which the victim's own karma ripens, and lets one voice call it unjust, so the curse is no longer self-justifying."},{"a":"mm:17:49-70","b":"mm:10:71-93","type":"same_image","note":"The goddess gives Manimekalai a mantra to travel the sky and another that cuts hunger, since the body is a lump of food; Kayasandigai has lost the mantra for going through the sky (55, and அந்தரம் செல்லும் மந்திரம் 43) and suffers a hunger no food fills.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two women are drawn as mirror images: the very powers granted to Manimekalai by mantra, flight and freedom from hunger, are those the curse stripped from Kayasandigai, and Manimekalai restores the second to her by charity, not by mantra."},{"a":"mm:17:83-98","b":"pn:173","type":"develops","note":"In the Purananuru poem the bard hears the noise of eating (ஊணொலி அரவம்) at Pannan's house and asks where the house of the physician of hunger (பசிப்பிணி மருத்துவன்) is; the epic closes with the same noise of eating (ஊண் ஒலி அரவத்து ஒலி, 97) at the public hall, after Kayasandigai has called Manimekalai physician of life (மருத்துவி, 15).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic borrows the Sangam scene of the hunger-healer's feast word for word but moves it from a patron's house, reached by a bard's praise, to a public hall with a gate open to all where anyone who will receive is called in.","gn":["the epic scene gains a Sangam lineage: the girl with the bowl takes the old title of physician of hunger and the very sound of the patron's feast","the poem's question of where the healer's house is, near or far, gains an answer the Sangam world lacked: a public hall that needs no patron and no praise-song"]},{"a":"mm:18:1-16","b":"mm:2:42-57","type":"tension","note":"Madhavi described the pathini widows who die, enter fire as a pool or waste themselves in vows, and confessed she had lived on; Chithirapathi repeats the same figures and phrase to say a courtesan was never bound to any of them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same list of widow's deaths serves the daughter as a measure she has failed and the mother as proof that their caste is exempt, so Madhavi's renunciation is attacked from below as well as judged from above."},{"a":"mm:18:17-36","b":"n:371","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain says a harlot's love goes out like a lamp when the money is gone; Chithirapathi says courtesans leave a drained flower like bees and leave men like Fortune when their merit ends.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The moralist's charge against the courtesan is spoken in the epic by a courtesan as her house's rule, and she uses it to forbid her daughter's grief and renunciation, so the faithlessness the quatrain condemns turns out to be enforced on the women themselves.","gn":["Chithirapathi's boast is shown to be the very account outsiders give of her profession, which she presents as her own code.","The quatrain gains the cost to the woman who tries to leave the life it describes, which its warning to men never considers."]},{"a":"mm:18:77-89","b":"mm:7:1-14","type":"develops","note":"In kaathai 7 the goddess tells the prince that a bent sceptre unsettles the planets, the rain and the people's lives, and bids him drop his designs on the ascetic girl; here he recalls only a woman showing a sceptre and wonders if she was a god.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The warning that tied the prince's lust to the kingdom's rain reaches him only as an image he cannot interpret, which is what lets the courtesan laugh it away and sets up his ruin."},{"a":"mm:18:98-123","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet honours the wife who worships her husband and no god; Chithirapathi describes the families whose women worship no god but the man who took them, to say Manimekalai is not of them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the couplet's ideal working as a boundary of birth: women outside the families it describes are declared fair game, so praise of the chaste wife becomes an argument for coercing a woman who has chosen celibacy.","gn":["Chithirapathi's argument rests on a standard of womanhood the audience already reveres, which is why it can persuade a prince.","The couplet gains a reading of its exclusions: it says nothing of the women it leaves out, and here they are claimed as the prince's to take."]},{"a":"mm:18:98-123","b":"k:912","type":"develops","note":"The couplet warns men off women who weigh the gain and speak of virtue; Chithirapathi describes dancers who take many gains with sweet speech and leave like bees, and tells the prince to bind such a woman.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's advice is to avoid the profit-weighing woman, while the courtesan who describes the same woman advises the prince to seize her, so a portrait meant to protect men becomes a licence to coerce a girl who has left that life.","gn":["The courtesan's self-portrait gains the weight of a recognised moral description, which makes her case for force seem like common sense.","The couplet's type gains a case where the woman so labelled is no longer what the label says, showing how the description outlives the conduct."]},{"a":"mm:18:124-142","b":"mm:4:105-125","type":"develops","note":"Sudhamathi told the prince in the grove that the human body is born of deeds, ages, dies and holds disease and grief; Manimekalai now tells him in nearly the same words why she renounced.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The lesson that did not reach the prince's heart from the companion (4:122-123) is repeated by Manimekalai herself as her own reason, and she expects it to fail again, so the teaching is a test the prince keeps failing rather than an argument he has not heard."},{"a":"mm:18:124-142","b":"mm:10:40-49","type":"develops","note":"The goddess told Manimekalai that Udayakumaran is her Rahula and that her mind has not left him; here she bows to him as her former husband and admits her heart goes to him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The attachment the goddess predicted is admitted openly rather than denied, and Manimekalai turns the former husband's claim on her obedience into the occasion for her refusal."},{"a":"mm:18:124-142","b":"n:52","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain says the best of people reflect on impermanence, disease, old age and death and do their real work; Manimekalai says she understood the body's birth, ageing, disease and death and chose dharma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same reflection that the quatrain treats as a private discipline of the wise is here a defence a woman must speak aloud to a man pursuing her, and she doubts he will listen.","gn":["Manimekalai's Buddhist reason is shown to rest on a reflection the Tamil ethical tradition already commends, not on a doctrine peculiar to her school.","The quatrain gains a scene where the reflection must be argued before someone who does not share it, which its address to the wise leaves out."]},{"a":"mm:18:143-156","b":"mm:10:71-93","type":"develops","note":"The goddess gave Manimekalai a mantra to change her form so that no one would dismiss her teaching as a young girl's; here she uses it to take Kaayasandigai's form and hide from the prince.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The power given for teaching rival schools is first used to escape a pursuer, so the epic's protection of her celibacy and its preparation for debate run through the same gift."},{"a":"mm:18:143-156","b":"mm:20:104-119","type":"develops","note":"Manimekalai's disguise as Kaayasandigai leads Kaanchanan, Kaayasandigai's husband, to think the prince is courting his wife and to kill him; the pillar goddess then calls the death ஊழ்வினை and blames Kaanchanan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The disguise that protects Manimekalai's vow becomes the means of the prince's death, and the epic assigns the death to past deeds while still holding the killer guilty, so her innocent act is one link in a chain of karma."},{"a":"mm:18:157-172","b":"mm:20:70-81","type":"same_image","note":"The prince describes Manimekalai quoting old wisdom (முதுக்குறை முதுமொழி எடுத்துக் காட்டி) as the goads that tamed him; in kaathai 20 Kaanchanan sees the disguised Manimekalai doing the same and takes it for a wife's flirting.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Her act of teaching is read twice as desire, once by the man it is meant to turn away and once by the jealous husband, and the second misreading is fatal."},{"a":"mm:19:1-28","b":"mm:5:18-31","type":"develops","note":"In the grove the prince asked whether any restraint holds once desire takes root; here evening pursues him 'with an aspect that destroys restraint' and he leaves burning and sighing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The prince's line that no நிறை survives rooted desire was his licence to pursue; two kaathais on, the same loss of restraint is drawn as a beast that hounds him away from her, so his excuse becomes his torment."},{"a":"mm:19:29-50","b":"mm:10:71-93","type":"develops","note":"The goddess gave her a mantra to take another form and travel the sky so that no one would refuse her teaching for her youth; here she first uses the other form to evade the prince and enter a prison with the bowl.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The power given so she could hear doctrine as an adult is first spent on escaping desire and feeding prisoners, so the epic puts charity and self-protection before debate in the order of her training."},{"a":"mm:19:29-50","b":"mm:0:69-88","type":"develops","note":"The patikam tells that Kanchanan, taking her for Kayachandigai, approached her and killed the prince; here she chooses that form to keep the prince away.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The disguise she takes to be free of Udayakumaran is what brings Kayachandigai's husband upon him, so her flight from the prince is the cause of his death, which the patikam names and this kaathai does not yet show."},{"a":"mm:19:29-50","b":"k:222","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet calls taking bad even if it were a good path and giving good even without heaven; Manimekalai cites the learned that for an alms-seeker both taking and giving are duty, and going to give is better still.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's flat ranking of giving over taking leaves no place for the mendicant, while the epic makes taking a duty when it serves giving, so the beggar's bowl is let in under the couplet's rule rather than condemned by it.","gn":["Her maxim gains a sharp statement of the view it must answer: that taking is bad whatever the path.","The couplet gains a case it does not cover, the one who takes only in order to give."]},{"a":"mm:19:51-66","b":"si:17:17-26","type":"same_image","note":"The herdswomen dance the kuravai of Mayavan, his elder brother and Pinnai to ward off the omens before Kovalan's death; the Chola king sees that same kuravai in birds whirling in his pleasure grove.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Across the two epics the one divine dance is first a rite of fear performed by herdswomen over coming disaster and then a pleasantry of a king at play, while Kovalan's daughter works in his prison, so the image measures how far the court stands from the suffering it governs."},{"a":"mm:19:117-138","b":"n:99","type":"tension","note":"The quatrain's ascetic's bowl (தவசி கடிஞை) fills slowly by alms at door after door; the guard reports a begging bowl (ஐயப் பாத்திரம்) that, taken into the prison, makes food flow for a crowd.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"The quatrain uses the begging bowl as the image of slow accumulation in the giver, and the epic reverses its direction, making the ascetic's bowl the source that pours out, so in the Buddhist epic the mendicant is the giver and the city the receiver.","gn":["The marvel of the one bowl gains the ordinary picture of a begging bowl it overturns.","The quatrain's image gains a counter-case where the bowl's fullness belongs to the beggar, not the donor."]},{"a":"mm:19:139-162","b":"k:550","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet likens a king's punishing the cruel to weeding a crop; the Chola king, at a beggar's request, empties his prison and makes it a house of dharma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held against the couplet, the epic's king does not deny that the cruel must be punished but turns the instrument of punishment itself into an instrument of charity, so the sceptre is shown by feeding rather than weeding.","gn":["The king's act gains the standard view of royal punishment it departs from.","The couplet's weeding image gains a royal alternative it does not consider, in which the prison is abolished rather than filled."]},{"a":"mm:19:139-162","b":"pn:18","type":"develops","note":"Kudapulaviyanar tells the Pandya that those who give food give life and urges him to build tanks; the disguised girl tells the Chola her bowl is life-medicine for embodied beings and asks him to give the prison to the righteous.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both poems set a counsellor before a king to teach that feeding is giving life, but the Sangam poet asks for waterworks that bring fame and the other world, while the epic's beggar asks the king to give up a prison, so the duty to feed passes from royal works to a renouncer's bowl the king only endorses.","gn":["The girl's plea gains a Tamil royal precedent for the claim that food is life.","The poem's counsel gains a later scene where the one who feeds is not the king, which shifts who holds the life-giving power."]},{"a":"mm:19:139-162","b":"mm:12:104-121","type":"develops","note":"Aravanar called the Amudhasurabhi life-medicine (ஆர் உயிர் மருந்து) and said many events would come to the city through her; she tells the king the bowl is life-medicine (உயிர் மருந்து) and the prison becomes a house of dharma.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Her words to the king repeat her teacher's phrase, and her first public act fulfils his prophecy of events in the city, so the epic shows the teaching passing into civic change through the disciple's voice before she has formally taken the dharma."},{"a":"mm:20:1-18","b":"mm:7:1-14","type":"develops","note":"The goddess Manimekala warned the prince that a swerving sceptre ruins rain and life and told him to drop his wrongful intent toward one who has taken up tapas; here he resolves to seize her even against the wise and the king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prince's death is set up as the end of a refused warning: he was told in advance that his desire for an ascetic was a fault of the sceptre, and he now names the king's anger as one more thing he will override."},{"a":"mm:20:1-18","b":"mm:18:17-36","type":"develops","note":"Chithirapathi vowed that she would have the prince carry Manimekalai off in his golden chariot; here the prince himself plans to set her in his golden chariot.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The courtesan mother's plan becomes the prince's own wish in the same image of the golden chariot, so the reader sees the abduction as her design working through him rather than a whim of his alone."},{"a":"mm:20:19-32","b":"mm:17:33-48","type":"develops","note":"The sage Viruchikan cursed Kayasandikai to twelve years of burning hunger; Kanchanan comes to Puhar because the twelve years are over and she has not come home.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The twelve-year term set in the curse is what brings the husband to the city on the very days Manimekalai wears his wife's form, so the curse that seemed to end with her cure sets up the killing."},{"a":"mm:20:104-119","b":"mm:17:71-82","type":"tension","note":"Freed of her hunger, Kayasandikai told Manimekalai she would go to her city in the sky; the pillar image reveals she was seized over the Vindhya on the way and swallowed by its goddess.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The cure that Manimekalai's bowl gave Kayasandikai did not bring her home: the release she announced ends in a second death, and her husband's search ends in a killing over her likeness."},{"a":"mm:20:49-69","b":"mm:18:124-142","type":"develops","note":"At their first meeting in the hall Manimekalai told the prince in words that the human body is a vessel of birth, age, disease and death; here she shows him an old woman's body feature by feature.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Manimekalai moves from stating the doctrine of the suffering body to making the prince look at one, which shows that she treats his desire as a failure of seeing that a statement did not cure.","gr":3},{"a":"mm:20:70-81","b":"mm:18:157-172","type":"same_image","note":"The prince praised Manimekalai for setting out 'wise old sayings' (முதுக்குறை முதுமொழி) as part of her allure; Kanchanan sees her setting out the same wise old sayings to the prince and takes it as a lover's talk.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same words of wisdom are read by both men as courtship, the prince as charm to be won and the husband as proof of betrayal, so her teaching is misheard twice in the way most fatal to each hearer."},{"a":"mm:20:82-103","b":"mm:22:19-38","type":"develops","note":"The prince goes at midnight to the hall to take Manimekalai and dies; the next kaathai's report to the king sets his death among those of men who approached chaste wives or ascetic women and died as their evil deed ripened.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The midnight approach is later read as one case in a city's record of such deaths, so the narrative's private intrigue becomes a public precedent that the court uses to explain a prince's death to his father."},{"a":"mm:20:120-129","b":"mm:21:45-60","type":"develops","note":"The pillar says the prince died by his own former deed; in the next kaathai the deity tells Manimekalai that deed: in a former birth he cut down a cook with a sword for spoiling a meal for the sage Brahmadharman.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bare claim of ripened karma is given its content one kaathai later, and the content matches the form of the death, a sword for a sword, so the epic makes its karma traceable rather than merely asserted."},{"a":"mm:20:33-48","b":"n:17","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain warns men not to desire the spear-eyed woman because she too will stoop and walk with a staff; Manimekalai, the woman desired, points her pursuer to an old woman and shows him eyes and face already changed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's warning against desire is spoken by a man to men about a woman, while the epic puts it in the mouth of the desired woman as her own defence, turning the lesson on aging into a refusal.","gn":["Manimekalai's demonstration gains a recognized ethical topos, so her act reads as instruction rather than mere evasion.","The quatrain gains a case where the warned man ignores the lesson and dies of the desire, which tests what the warning can do."],"gr":4},{"a":"mm:20:49-69","b":"n:43","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain asks whether spices, flowers in the hair and false finery can remove a foulness the great saw and gave up; Manimekalai says the flowers, paste, cloth and jewels that hide the flesh are a deceit 'those of old' devised.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain credits the great of the past with seeing through the body's adornment, while the epic blames those of old for building it, so the same disguise is a personal vanity in one and an inherited social custom in the other.","gn":["The epic's charge against custom gains a parallel in which tradition is on the side of seeing through adornment, which sharpens how unusual its blame of 'those of old' is.","The quatrain gains an account of where the false finery comes from, which it treats only as something individuals put on."]},{"a":"mm:20:49-69","b":"n:45","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain's renunciant refuses talk of teeth like jasmine buds and pearls because he has seen fallen teeth and bones in the burning ground; Manimekalai shows teeth once like a row of pearls now loosened in a living mouth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain needs the cremation ground to refute the pearl simile, while the epic finds the refutation in a living old woman, so the argument against the body moves from death to aging that every lover can see in the street.","gn":["Manimekalai's catalogue gains the Naladiyar's frame of rejected similes, showing it answers the praise-poets' language point by point.","The quatrain gains a version of its proof that does not wait for death, which widens it from the funeral ground to ordinary life."]},{"a":"mm:20:104-119","b":"s:969","type":"develops","note":"Sivavakkiyar's man, having taken a woman for her form, would cut down any rival who desires her, though his own body will stink and be handed to the cremation keeper; Kanchanan cuts down the prince over a form that is not even his wife's, whose body is already gone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the Siddhar's jealous husband a case where the form (வடிவு) he guards belongs to another woman and his wife's body has already perished, so the jealousy is shown wrong both about whose body it is and about its lasting.","gn":["Kanchanan's killing gains a general diagnosis: it is jealousy over a body, the most perishable of possessions.","The Siddhar's hypothetical gains a narrated case in which the rival is actually killed and the killer's price is named as a deed that clings, beyond the body's decay."]},{"a":"mm:20:120-129","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain says an old deed finds its doer as a calf finds its mother among many cows; the pillar says the prince's ripened deed came here and took his life, and that Kanchanan's deed will reach him too.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the old deed finding its doer across a change of birth and through a stranger's hand, which gives the quatrain's search image a path: the deed reaches the prince by way of another man's mistake.","gn":["The pillar's statement gains the image of the deed as an active seeker, which explains why a chance misidentification could deliver it.","The quatrain gains a case in which one deed's arrival creates a new deed with its own doer, a chain the calf image does not show."]},{"a":"mm:20:120-129","b":"k:208","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says the ruin of evil-doers stays at their feet like a shadow; the pillar tells Kanchanan his cruel deed will not leave him, even though the prince died by his own former deed.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet considers only the doer, while the epic adds that being the instrument of another's ripened karma does not lift the deed from the one who kills, so karma explains the victim's death without excusing the killer.","gn":["The pillar's warning gains the couplet's image of a consequence that never leaves its doer's feet.","The couplet gains a hard case, a killing the victim's own deed called for, in which its rule still holds."],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:20:70-81","b":"k:305","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says unguarded anger kills the one who has it; Kanchanan's anger, kindled by misread signs, kills the prince, and the pillar then tells him the deed will cling to him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic traces how anger turns on its owner: it begins in a misreading of another's signs, kills someone else first, and returns as a deed that will not leave the angry man, which the couplet states as a single blow.","gn":["Kanchanan's rage gains the couplet's judgment that the angry man is his own first victim.","The couplet gains the route by which anger kills its owner: through the deed done to another and its clinging consequence."]},{"a":"mm:21:45-60","b":"mm:9:42-57","type":"develops","note":"Brahmadharman foretold that Rahulan would die of the snake's poison glance; the pillar deity now names the deed that set his deaths in motion: the cook he killed in anger at the sage's meal.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prophecy of kaathai 9 gave only the event; paired with this unit it shows the epic withholding a cause for twelve kaathais and then placing it inside an act of service to the very sage who made the prophecy."},{"a":"mm:21:61-72","b":"mm:20:120-129","type":"develops","note":"To the killer Kanchanan the same deity said that karma took the prince's life yet his own cruel deed would not leave him; to Manimekalai it says the prince's death is the fruit of his own killing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two speeches show the epic's karma as a chain in which every link is also a fresh wrong: the victim's old deed ripens only through a new killing whose doer is held to account in turn."},{"a":"mm:21:61-72","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar's old deed finds its doer as a calf finds its mother among many cows; the deity says the cook's killing came 'here' to strike the man reborn as the prince.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's deed that seeks out its doer becomes in the epic a deed that crosses a death and a new body, so the 'doer' it finds is a person with another name who does not remember the act.","gn":["an image for the deed's active search, which the deity states only as a bare claim","a case that sets the search across births, which the quatrain does not say"]},{"a":"mm:21:61-72","b":"k:319","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says harm done to others comes back on the doer of itself; the deity applies this to a killing done in zeal to serve a holy man and denies that the service exempts it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet does not ask whether a good motive changes the return of harm; the epic answers that it does not, and names the excuse it rejects, that the master served will protect his servant.","gn":["a general rule against which the deity's denial of the pious excuse is measured","a test of whether intention to serve dharma can stop harm from returning, which the couplet leaves open"]},{"a":"mm:21:87-111","b":"mm:27:277-283","type":"develops","note":"The deity foretells that the materialist will call her knowledge of a former birth a dream sent by a deity; in Vanji he says those deluded by a god or by dreams have changed minds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prophecy foretells the opponent's very objection, so when the debate comes the reader already knows the materialist's charge is aimed at a real divine intervention that the epic itself narrated."},{"a":"mm:21:112-129","b":"s:1458","type":"tension","note":"Sivavakkiyar mocks the planted stone called a god and asks whether it will speak while the Lord is within; the pillar deity grants that stone and wood do not speak but says deities the wise install in them stay and speak to men.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both start from the same premise, that the stone itself is mute; they part over whether a power can be lodged in it, the epic answering yes for guardian deities of a city and the Siddhar answering that the Lord is found only within.","gn":["a sharp statement of the sceptic's objection that the deity's account is built to meet","evidence that the 'speaking stone' it mocks had a worked-out defence, the deity bound to its place, rather than naive idol worship"]},{"a":"mm:21:147-171","b":"mm:28:184-206","type":"develops","note":"Thuvathikan foretells famine in Kanchi and Manimekalai's coming with the bowl; when she arrives, the Kanchi king says all the pillar image's words have come true (கந்திற்பாவை கட்டுரை எல்லாம் வாய் ஆகின்று).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic closes its own prophecy by having a king cite the pillar image by name, so the deity's claim at 112 to 129 that installed gods speak true is confirmed inside the story, not only asserted."},{"a":"mm:21:147-171","b":"k:13","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says that if the sky fails, hunger stays and torments the wide world; the deity foretells the bowl of dharma that gives rain's plenty to a parched world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet treats hunger after drought as beyond remedy, the epic answers it with organized charity, the begging bowl standing in for the failed rain.","gn":["a statement of the stakes: drought brings hunger even to a world ringed with water","a remedy the couplet does not consider, feeding by alms when the sky fails"]},{"a":"mm:21:172-190","b":"t:1527","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular's grace-power descends as the guru when good and bad deeds are balanced and the soul becomes Siva; the epic's Manimekalai, after births of compassion, becomes a disciple and is saved by cutting dependence herself (சார்பு அறுத்து உய்தி).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both put a teacher at the threshold of release, but in Tirumular the teacher is grace that ends the soul's own agency, while in the epic the great one only gives dharma and the disciple does the cutting, so they disagree over who acts in release.","gn":["a sharp contrast that shows how far the epic keeps release in the disciple's own hands","a Buddhist counter-model of the teacher-disciple bond without descent of grace"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:22:1-18","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"same_image","note":"Both scenes bring a death before a king with the same approach formula (கோயில் மன்னனைக் குறுகினர்/குறுகினள் சென்று): Kannaki to the Pandya, the ascetics to the Chola.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Kannaki uses the audience to accuse the king of her husband's death, while the ascetics, bringing a father news of his son's death, hide the body and wrap the news in blessing and old stories, so the same entrance shows two ways of telling a king a truth he is implicated in."},{"a":"mm:22:19-38","b":"k:146","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says enmity, sin, fear and blame never leave the man who crosses into another's home; the ascetic says such men die when their evil deed ripens, and adds those who set their hearts on women ascetics.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet's offence is entering a married woman's home; the epic extends it to desire for a renunciant woman, who has no husband's home to violate, and makes death, not lasting disgrace, the outcome.","gn":["The ascetic's general charge gains the Kural's account of what such a man carries while he lives.","The couplet gains a case beyond the married woman, the woman of penance, which its home-centred wording leaves out."]},{"a":"mm:22:39-56","b":"mm:5:42-65","type":"qualifies","note":"Sudhamathi, carried off by a vidyadhara, became unfit for the brahmins yet her father kept her; Maruthi, only desired by a youth, declares herself unfit for her husband's fires.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic twice lets the brahmin purity rule fall on a woman who did nothing; in Sudhamathi's case the father stays by her, while Maruthi excludes herself before anyone else does, so the rule is shown working inside the woman's own conscience."},{"a":"mm:22:57-79","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"The Bhuta quotes the couplet to Maruthi and explains her lost power by her worship of gods, festivals and talk.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the wife's power as a fact; the epic uses it as a test a chaste woman fails, reads தெய்வம் தொழாஅள் literally as a ban on worshipping gods, and adds festivals and gossip, so the couplet becomes a rule with conditions that can be broken.","gn":["Maruthi's loss of power gains a named authority, the poet who never lies, behind the Bhuta's verdict.","The couplet gains a case that spells out what not worshipping a god excludes and what happens to the power when it is not kept."]},{"a":"mm:22:57-79","b":"si:9:55-66","type":"develops","note":"Devanthi urges Kannaki to bathe in the sacred tanks and worship to win her husband back, and Kannaki refuses; the Bhuta tells Maruthi that worship of gods is what cost her the rain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Manimekalai's Bhuta supplies the doctrine behind Kannaki's refusal of the tanks in the Silappathikaram: a pathini's power rests on not turning to gods, so Kannaki's refusal is part of what makes her word able to burn Madurai."},{"a":"mm:22:57-79","b":"si:5:128-134","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Silappathikaram the Bhuta of the crossroads nooses and devours unchaste women who hide their wrong and men who desire others' wives; here the same Bhuta tells Maruthi its noose will not bind her and that it acts only after the king's seven days.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two show the spirit's justice as a reserve behind the king's: it binds the hidden offender, spares the woman who was only desired, and waits seven days for the king to punish the man before acting itself."},{"a":"mm:22:80-98","b":"mm:20:104-119","type":"same_image","note":"The image on the pillar (கந்தின் ... பாவை) of the Ulaka Aravi proclaims Visakai chaste here; in kaathai 20 the same image tells Kanchanan that the woman before him is Manimekalai in disguise.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pillar image is the city's witness for women: it once cleared a slandered virgin by declaring her a rain-bringer, and later declares the truth about a disguised renunciant, but in the second case its word comes after the sword has fallen."},{"a":"mm:22:80-98","b":"si:5:135-140","type":"same_image","note":"The Silappathikaram's image in the court weeps without speaking (நாவொடு நவிலாது) when the king's sceptre bends; the Manimekalai's speaking image (நா உடைப் பாவை) proclaims a woman's innocence to the city.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Puhar's images serve as witnesses of justice in both epics, but one only weeps silently at a bent sceptre while the other speaks and clears a woman, so the pairing shows the speaking image as an active judge where the weeping one is only a sign.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:22:80-98","b":"mm:22:127-142","type":"develops","note":"Visakai vows in youth to be her cousin's wife only in the next birth; at sixty she tells him again she will not come to his feet in this birth but will serve him in the next.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vow made to silence a slander outlives the slander by forty years, so what began as a defence of her name becomes the form their love takes, kept apart in body and joined in shared charity."},{"a":"mm:22:115-126","b":"k:58","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says women who obtain their husbands win great distinction in the world of the gods; the brahmin says men without a chaste wife do not enter it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Taken together, the couplet and the brahmin's maxim make the wife and husband each the other's way to heaven, but the epic gives the maxim to a man urging a sixty-year-old to marry and then has Visakai deny it, so the reciprocal rule is shown as a pressure rather than a truth.","gn":["The brahmin's maxim gains its counterpart in the couplet, which ties a woman's heaven to her husband.","The couplet gains the reverse claim about men, and a scene in which that claim is answered."]},{"a":"mm:22:115-126","b":"mm:22:127-142","type":"tension","note":"The brahmin says men without a chaste wife do not enter the world of the gods however much good they do; Visakai says sons do not give that world and only great virtue is a companion.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same phrase, புத்தேள் உலகம், is used for opposite conditions within twenty lines: the brahmin makes a wife necessary, Visakai makes virtue sufficient, and the narrator sides with her by counting their charity above the stars."},{"a":"mm:22:127-142","b":"k:36","type":"develops","note":"The couplet calls virtue the companion that does not perish when one perishes; Visakai calls great virtue the true companion (விழுத் துணை) in place of youth, body, wealth and sons.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Visakai uses the couplet's idea of virtue as companion to replace the wife-companion the brahmin demanded, so a teaching about virtue outlasting death becomes an answer to the claim that a man needs marriage for heaven.","gn":["Her answer gains the couplet's reason why virtue alone survives.","The couplet's companion gains a concrete rival it displaces: the wife and the sons a householder is told he must have."],"gr":3},{"a":"mm:22:127-142","b":"n:11","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain says the wise, knowing grey hair will come, renounce in childhood; Visakai points to Dharmadattan's grey hair and turns him not to renunciation but to giving.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain offers only two paths, early renunciation or clinging to youth; Visakai, who has kept a vow of chastity since youth, shows a third for the grey-haired householder: shared charity without marriage.","gn":["Visakai's appeal to his grey hair gains the quatrain's standard of what the wise do before it comes.","The quatrain gains a case where the one who saw age coming chose a vow and charity rather than the robe."]},{"a":"mm:22:127-142","b":"k:62","type":"tension","note":"The couplet says evils will not touch a man for seven births if he has children of good character; Visakai says sons do not give heaven.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the reading that the seven births are the parent's own, the couplet credits good children with protecting the parent beyond this life, which Visakai flatly denies, so the pair marks a point where the epic's teacher rejects a household hope the Kural endorses.","gn":["Visakai's denial gains its target: a recognised claim that children carry merit to their parents.","The couplet gains a direct contrary in a text that counts only one's own virtue."]},{"a":"mm:22:143-158","b":"mm:22:205-215","type":"develops","note":"Kakandhan, though grieving, kills his elder son with his own sword for seizing Visakai's garland; the present Chola says the vidyadhara did to his son what he himself should have done.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The ascetic's stories prepare the king's verdict: Kakandhan executes his sons with his own hand, and the Chola, faced with a son already dead, claims that act as his own duty, while also hiding the body from other kings, which Kakandhan never did."},{"a":"mm:22:159-176","b":"t:200","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular calls killing, theft, lust and lying sins as great as mountains, whose giving up leads to Siva's feet; the ascetic names toddy, lying, theft, killing and lust as the five to be shunned and threatens hell to those who keep lust.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two traditions share nearly the same list of sins but use it differently: Tirumular makes their giving up a path to Siva's feet, while the ascetic names no god, singles out lust as the one that contains the rest, and uses the list to warn a king of hell.","gn":["The ascetic's list gains a parallel that shows how far it is shared across schools.","Tirumular's verse gains a godless version of the same discipline, where shunning is an end in itself and its failure leads to hell rather than away from Siva."]},{"a":"mm:22:177-204","b":"k:208","type":"same_image","note":"The prince follows Manimekalai like a shadow (நிழல் போல், 185); the couplet says ruin stays at the evildoer's feet like his shadow.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic inverts the couplet's shadow: the prince makes himself Manimekalai's shadow, but his own evil deed is the shadow at his own feet, and it catches him in the hall where he followed her.","gn":["The prince's pursuit gains the couplet's image of what was following him.","The couplet's shadow gains a scene where the pursuer is himself pursued, so the image shows how the ruin comes."]},{"a":"mm:22:177-204","b":"mm:20:120-129","type":"develops","note":"In kaathai 20 the pillar image tells Kanchanan that ripened karma took the prince's life; here the ascetic tells the king that the prince's evil deed itself struck him by the vidyadhara's hand.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The killing is told twice as the work of karma, but the deity also tells Kanchanan his act was a cruel deed that will follow him, while the ascetic, speaking to the father, leaves the killer's guilt out, so the same explanation is shaped by who hears it."},{"a":"mm:22:205-215","b":"mm:20:120-129","type":"tension","note":"The pillar image tells Kanchanan he has done a cruel deed whose fruit will not leave him; the king says the vidyadhara did what the king should have done.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same killing is a cruel deed with consequences in the deity's eyes and a royal duty done by another's hand in the king's, so the epic sets karmic judgment and royal justice side by side without reconciling them."},{"a":"mm:22:205-215","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki cites the Chola who put his own son under the chariot wheel to shame the Pandya; the Chola king here fears it will be said that in that king's line an evil-doer was born.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The memory Kannaki used to accuse another king becomes a weight on the Chola's own line: he accepts his son's death as justice, but his first thought is to keep the stain on Manu's line from other kings, so the ancestor's example breeds concealment as well as justice."},{"a":"mm:22:205-215","b":"k:560","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says that if the guardian does not guard, the cows' yield fails and the brahmins forget their books; the king says that without the guardian's guarding there are no ascetics' vows and no women's chastity.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The king adapts the couplet's list of what fails without a guardian to ascetics' vows and women's chastity, the two things his son violated, so the general rule becomes the reason he must count his own son's death as just.","gn":["The king's maxim gains the couplet's frame of the guardian's duty, which shows it as a known rule rather than a private excuse.","The couplet's list gains vows and chastity, and a case where the guardian's duty runs against his own son."]},{"a":"mm:23:1-16","b":"pn:93","type":"develops","note":"Both give the rite of laying a king who died of sickness on darbha grass and cutting him with a sword with the words 'go where the warriors go' (செல்வுழிச் செல்க in both); Avvai uses it to praise Athiyaman's battle wounds, Vasanthavai to console and rebuke a queen whose son died in neither war nor old age.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show the rite outliving its Sangam use as praise: in the epic it becomes the standard by which a dead prince is found wanting, so that the warrior code is used to stop a mother's grief rather than to honour a king.","gn":["the Sangam source of the custom, which shows that Vasanthavai is quoting an old royal code of the warrior's death and not inventing a comfort","a later witness that the rite was remembered as a royal custom and could be turned from praise of the wounded against a prince who died without honour"]},{"a":"mm:23:41-56","b":"mm:10:71-93","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekala goddess gave Manimekalai mantras to take another form, travel the sky and end hunger, warning that people would not tell a young woman the truth; here the form-changing mantra defeats the queen's plot.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows why the goddess's gifts were defensive: the mantra of another form, given against those who would not trust a young woman, is first used to save her from a slander about her body.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:23:57-71","b":"mm:9:42-57","type":"develops","note":"The sage foretold that Rahulan would die of the serpent's venomous gaze within sixteen days and that Lakshmi would burn with him; Manimekalai now uses that death to ask the queen why she did not weep for the same life then.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The prophecy of a death in the former birth becomes, in this kaathai, the ground of an argument against grief: the same life has died before unmourned by its present mother, so her grief is for this body only."},{"a":"mm:23:72-85","b":"t:145","type":"develops","note":"Tirumular describes the whole town wailing, renaming the dead man a corpse, burning him and bathing to forget; Manimekalai asks the queen whether she weeps for the body, since mourners themselves cast it into the burning ground, or for the life, whose next dwelling is set by deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Tirumular's observation that mourners burn and forget the body they weep for becomes in the epic the first horn of a dilemma whose second horn turns grief for one life into pity for all lives.","gn":["a plain picture of the mourners' contradiction that her terse question 'who cast him into the burning ground?' assumes","a use for the observation: the Buddhist teacher draws from it an obligation of compassion, not only the impermanence the verse leaves the reader with"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:23:72-85","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The Silappathikaram's patikam sets as a moral that the deed of the past comes in wrath and feeds its fruit (ஊழ்வினை உருத்துவந்து ஊட்டும்); Manimekalai uses the same verb of the prince's former killing of a cook (உருத்து எழும் வல் வினை).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the sequel taking its companion's moral from the frame into a mechanism: here the wrathful deed is named, traced across two births and two deaths, and offered to a mourner as consolation rather than as a verdict on a king."},{"a":"mm:23:72-85","b":"mm:21:45-60","type":"develops","note":"The pillar deity told Manimekalai that the prince's death came from his killing of the cook who broke the vessels meant for the sage Brahmadharma; Manimekalai now passes that revelation to the prince's mother.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same account of a past killing changes use between the two kaathais: revealed to the lover as the reason for his death, it is retold to the mother as the reason not to hate the woman she blamed for it."},{"a":"mm:23:113-130","b":"k:284","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 284 says that a love grown in stealth yields at harvest a sorrow that does not die; Manimekalai tells the queen not to think that the harsh suffering of those who live by stealth is for thieves alone, since these ways bring sorrow to all who live in the world.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet assigns undying sorrow to the thief; the epic refuses to let a listener file that sorrow under a class of criminals and extends it to a queen whose fault was deceit, making the rule about theft a rule about all concealment.","gn":["a known rule on the fruit of stealth, which gives force to the phrase she will not let the queen keep at a distance","a scene in which the couplet's reader is warned against using it to sort the guilty from herself"]},{"a":"mm:23:113-130","b":"mm:23:17-40","type":"develops","note":"The narrator says the queen hid her grief with helpless heart (கையாற்று) and false conduct (பொய்யாற்று ஒழுக்கம்); Manimekalai's maxim says those who take falsehood as their way never pass beyond helpless grief (பொய்யாற்று ஒழுக்கம், கையாற்று அவலம்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the maxim is not general advice but the queen's own conduct described back to her in the narrator's words, so the teaching names her deceit without accusing her."},{"a":"mm:23:131-147","b":"k:2","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 2 asks what learning is worth if the learned do not bow at the feet of the one of pure knowledge; Manimekalai says learning that is merely learned is not learning, and names the learned as those who curb hatred, end hunger and love all creatures.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both refuse learning its own worth, but the couplet completes it in worship while the epic completes it in compassion and the ending of others' hunger, so the pair shows two answers to the same question of what learning is for.","gn":["a classic statement that learning needs a further end, against which her list reads as a choice of ends","a Buddhist answer to its question that puts the further end in conduct toward beings rather than in reverence to a lord"]},{"a":"mm:23:131-147","b":"mm:6:154-167","type":"develops","note":"Gotami, mourning her son at the burning ground, begged the goddess to take her life for his and was told a life gone goes by its deeds; the queen, mourning her son, is taught the same and her grief is quenched.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic answers a mother's grief twice with the same doctrine but to opposite ends: Gotami, refused, dies beside her son, while the queen, taught to turn her love to all lives, is cleared and bows to her son's supposed enemy."},{"a":"mm:24:1-26","b":"si:6:18-26","type":"same_image","note":"The Silappathikaram gives Madhavi's descent from the maiden cursed to earth when Urvasi's dance failed before Indra as the pedigree of her art; Chithirapathi counts the same heavenly dancers, Urvasi of her own line, to measure her shame now that Madhavi begs.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The curse-myth that certifies the courtesan's art in the first epic becomes, in the second, the yardstick by which the courtesan house judges a daughter's renunciation as the worst fall of the line.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:24:1-26","b":"si:30:24-34","type":"tension","note":"In the Silappathikaram, when Madhavi gives her daughter to the dharma, the king and city grieve as men who have dropped a gem in the sea and the ascetics speak kind words; Chithirapathi says such a turn earns a courtesan only laughter.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Held together they show that the courtesan's renunciation has two publics: the city mourns the loss of beauty while the courtesan house reads the same act as ridicule, so neither epic's report of the town's reaction can be taken alone."},{"a":"mm:24:52-74","b":"mm:9:16-30","type":"same_image","note":"Both are a sage's warning that a city will sink: Brahmadharma gives Attipathi a fixed day and the king leads his people out, while the charana makes Puhar's sinking hang on a rite that must never be forgotten and that the goddess cannot override.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the epic's two kinds of prophecy of ruin: a Buddhist sage's fixed fact that is escaped by leaving, and a curse tied to Indra's festival that is escaped only by unbroken observance, which is why the city's fate turns on the king's memory."},{"a":"mm:24:75-88","b":"k:920","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 920 groups double-minded women with drink and dice as the company of men Fortune has left; the queen turns the charge on the courtesan house itself, a life built on drink, lies, lust and deceit, and from which a courtesan's own daughter flees in fear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet sees the courtesan only as a danger to the man, while the epic shows the same house as a danger to the women born in it, so that renunciation is a courtesan's escape, not only a patron's.","gn":["a short traditional judgment that frames the queen's list as the old ethical verdict on the courtesan's world","a case from inside the courtesan's house, where the harm falls on its daughter, which the couplet's man-centred warning never considers"]},{"a":"mm:24:101-122","b":"k:351","type":"develops","note":"Kural 351 says a base birth comes from the confusion that takes the unreal as real; Aravanar puts ignorance first in the chain of birth and defines it as trusting hearsay that a hare has horns.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet names unreal things taken as real without saying which; the epic gives the error a mechanism, hearsay preferred to what is seen, and a place, the first of twelve links, so the couplet's one step to birth becomes a chain.","gn":["an older Tamil statement that ignorance is the root of birth, which shows the Buddhist chain speaking in a known idiom","a definition of its மருள் by a stock example of an unreal thing (the hare's horn) and a causal sequence from error to birth"]},{"a":"mm:24:101-122","b":"t:724","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular nurtures the body because its perishing ends the life before true knowledge is won; when the queen wishes Aravanar's aged body long life, he answers that ageing and death are the nature of the born and turns to teaching.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair sets the Saiva siddha's body kept alive for knowledge against the Buddhist teacher's body let go because knowledge has been reached and must be passed on, so the worth of a long life depends on whether knowledge is still to be won.","gn":["a contrary view that makes Aravanar's refusal of the blessing a position, not just an old man's modesty","a teacher who has the knowledge and still declines the body's prolonging, which tests whether nurturing the body is a means or an end"]},{"a":"mm:24:123-140","b":"t:200","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says those who give up killing, theft, lust and lying reach Siva's feet and its bliss; Aravanar counts the same sins among ten and says that leaving them, with precept and giving, wins only birth as god, human or Brahma.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same abstentions are release in the Saiva verse and only a better birth in the Buddhist sermon, which places release in knowing the twelve links rather than in conduct; the pair shows that the two schools agree on the list and disagree on what it buys.","gn":["a rival claim that abstaining from the great sins reaches the highest, which sharpens the epic's limit on what good deeds can do","a finer count of the sins by body, speech and mind and a denial that abstention alone ends birth, against which its promise can be weighed"]},{"a":"mm:24:141-158","b":"mm:27:1-24","type":"develops","note":"Aravanar tells Manimekalai that after she has heard the other doctrines he will teach her fully; in kaathai 27 she goes among the schools in Vanji and asks the Vedic logician his doctrine.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The survey of schools in kaathai 27 is set up here as a stage of instruction the teacher prescribes, so the rival doctrines are heard as preparation for the Buddhist teaching, not as free inquiry."},{"a":"mm:24:141-158","b":"si:29:64-68","type":"develops","note":"At Kannaki's shrine in Vanji the maid asks the pathini whether she has heard of Madhavi's and Manimekalai's renunciation; Manimekalai here resolves to go to Vanji and do good deeds for the great pathini.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two epics meet at the Vanji shrine from both sides: the Silappathikaram brings news of the girl's renunciation to the pathini, and the Manimekalai sends the renounced girl to make merit for her."},{"a":"mm:24:159-176","b":"mm:14:49-64","type":"develops","note":"Indra, to idle Aputhiran's bowl, sent rain until the land had no hungry; Aputhiran, reborn as Punniyarajan in the city of Indra's descendant, is a king since whose birth the rain has not failed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The plenty that once mocked Aputhiran's charity by leaving no one to feed is now the sign of his merit as king, so the epic turns Indra's rival gift into the reward of the charity it tried to make useless."},{"a":"mm:24:159-176","b":"k:545","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 545 says rain and harvest gather in the land of a king who rules by the sceptre; the ascetic credits Nagapuram's unfailing rain to Punniyarajan's birth, before any rule.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet makes a land's rain the fruit of a king's just rule, the epic makes it the fruit of his former birth's charity, moving the cause of plenty from the sceptre to karma.","gn":["the rule against which the ascetic's claim stands out as a claim about birth, not governance","a case where the king's plenty precedes any act of rule, showing a second source of the link between king and rain that the couplet does not name"]},{"a":"mm:25:1-24","b":"mm:14:95-104","type":"develops","note":"Aravana Adigal told Manimekalai that Aputhiran died with an unquenched purpose of sustaining lives and was born from a cow's womb to rule Savakam; here Manimekalai tells that king that, bewildered by wealth, he knows neither that birth nor the cow's womb of this one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Rebirth carried Aputhiran's purpose into a king's body but not his knowledge of it, so the purpose has to be handed back to him by one who heard his story from outside."},{"a":"mm:25:42-63","b":"k:335","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 335 urges good deeds before the hiccup stops the tongue; the sage of Kayankarai urges dharma before death that cannot be turned back (49 to 50), but adds that good deeds win only better births and that none escape birth except those who hear the coming Buddha.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sermon separates what the couplet joins: haste in good deeds before death decides the next birth, but release waits on a teacher not yet come, so urgency to do good is not urgency to be free.","gn":["a measure of what is shared Tamil ethics in the sermon, so that only its claim at 47 to 48 stands out as Buddhist doctrine","a limit the couplet does not state: good deeds rushed to before death lead on to heaven or a human birth but do not end birth"]},{"a":"mm:25:64-76","b":"mm:9:58-71","type":"develops","note":"In kaathai 9 the sage foretold that on the island seat she would know her birth and find his word clear; at her second visit Manimekalai says the sage's word of that day has come true today, after recalling his account of why the seat reveals births.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The second visit adds the ground of the seat's power, Indra's order made for the coming Buddha, so the fulfilment rests on the Buddha's future presence and not on the seat itself."},{"a":"mm:25:77-95","b":"mm:25:222-239","type":"tension","note":"The king declares it good to renounce kingship wholly, crediting Manimekalai as the cause; Manimekalai, whose call woke him, sends him back to rule and tells him a king's dharma is food, clothing and shelter for all.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic of renunciation turns its heroine against a king's renunciation: the waking she causes is meant to make him a giver on a throne, not an ascetic, so renunciation is not the right end for everyone it moves.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:25:96-114","b":"k:545","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 545 says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who wields the sceptre rightly; the minister says rain returned to Savakam after twelve years when the king was born, before he wielded any sceptre.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic moves the cause of a land's plenty from the king's rule to his birth, that is, to merit carried from a former life of giving, so a king can bring rain before he governs at all.","gn":["a Tamil rule that makes the minister's report a claim about kingship rather than a bare wonder","a case its link between rain and right rule cannot explain: rain that follows a king's coming and precedes his rule"]},{"a":"mm:25:96-114","b":"pn:186","type":"develops","note":"Puram 186 says the world lives not by rice or water but by its king, who must know he is its life; the minister tells the king that if he leaves, his land will cry like an infant whose mother is gone (110 to 111).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam duty that a king know himself the world's life becomes, in a Buddhist epic, the argument that holds a king back from renunciation, so the poem's maxim sets a limit even on seeking release.","gn":["a Sangam warrant that makes the minister's plea a statement of a king's duty rather than a courtier's flattery","an application the poem does not foresee: the king's being the world's life binds him against leaving it for a higher world"]},{"a":"mm:25:115-139","b":"k:244","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 244 says one who sustains lives (மன்னுயிர் ஓம்பி) and holds to grace need fear no deed for his own life; the minister says the Buddha's dharma is not to pity one's own life and to protect other lives (பிற உயிர் ஓம்பும், 116), and charges the king with seeking a higher world for himself.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet promises the protector of lives safety for his own; the minister makes indifference to one's own life the protector's mark, so a king's wish for his own release counts against his grace.","gn":["a Tamil rule that makes protecting others' lives the core of grace, so the minister's appeal reads as more than Buddhist piety","a reversal of its reward: in the epic the protector of lives is the one who does not seek safety for himself, not the one granted it"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:25:140-150","b":"mm:14:1-15","type":"same_image","note":"Aravana Adigal told Manimekalai that Chintadevi gave Aputhiran the bowl on a rainy night saying it would not fail though the land did; the reborn king, his birth shown him by the seat, recalls the same night and the goddess's words almost word for word.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The teacher's story and the seat's revelation agree to the word, so the king's restored memory works in the epic as proof that the seat shows births truly."},{"a":"mm:25:151-174","b":"k:327","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 327 says not to take another's life even at the cost of one's own; the island goddess tells Aputhiran that by starving he killed his own life and that the merchants who came back and died where he lay died for his sake, so that he is a killer.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats giving up one's own life as a price that may be paid to avoid killing; the epic counts giving up one's own life as itself a killing, and one that draws others to die.","gn":["a known Tamil rule against which the goddess's charge reads as a judgment about killing, not only grief for the dead","a case its two lives cannot keep apart: one's own death that becomes the cause of others' deaths"]},{"a":"mm:25:151-174","b":"mm:14:95-104","type":"tension","note":"Aravana Adigal told Aputhiran's death by fasting as a sun that sets in the west to rise again, with an unquenched purpose of sustaining lives; the island goddess calls the same death a killing of his own life that led others to kill theirs.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The epic gives two endorsed voices opposite verdicts on one death, the teacher's passage to a sustaining birth and the guardian's charge of killing, and leaves both standing beside the king who is its fruit."},{"a":"mm:25:200-221","b":"s:23","type":"qualifies","note":"Pathirakiriyar asks when he will see the bones within as white bones and himself lowered like a cast-off corpse; the king digs up his own former body, bones held together as if coated in white lime, and is bewildered.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic grants literally the sight the Siddhar longs for, one's own body as white bones, and shows that the sight alone does not free: the king is bewildered until Manimekalai tells him what it is for.","gn":["a frame in which seeing one's own corpse is a discipline of release, against which the king's bewilderment is a failure to read it","a test of its longing: the sight given in full, without the knowledge that would make it freeing"]},{"a":"mm:25:222-239","b":"pn:189","type":"develops","note":"Puram 189 measures every man's need, king or hunter, by one measure of food and two cloths and concludes that wealth's use is giving; Manimekalai tells the king that dharma for all lives is nothing but food, clothing and shelter.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam measure of human need, used to tell a rich man to give, becomes the entire content of a king's dharma toward every life, and the king at once extends it to other kingdoms.","gn":["a Sangam ground for reducing dharma to three material needs, so the definition reads as a measure of need and not a slight on doctrine","a widening of its argument: what each person needs becomes what a king owes all lives, in his land and in others'"]},{"a":"mm:26:1-13","b":"mm:2:42-57","type":"develops","note":"Madhavi lists what pathinis do when a husband dies (give up life in grief, enter the fire, or wear the body down in vows) and says Kannaki was of none of these kinds; Manimekalai, praying at the image, says her mother neither held to the debt of love nor took the way of penance but took on the debt of chastity.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The daughter repeats her mother Madhavi's typology in her own prayer, so that the family keeps Kannaki outside every ordinary path of the widow, and the next lines let Kannaki herself call the path she did take a sin."},{"a":"mm:26:1-13","b":"si:28:222-234","type":"qualifies","note":"Senguttuvan has the image of the pathini goddess carved from the Himalayan stone and installed in her shrine; the Manimekalai's shrine holds an image of both Kannaki and Kovalan, and the goddess in it speaks.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the Silappathikaram's royal shrine sets up Kannaki alone as a goddess of the realm, the Manimekalai makes the image a family one with the father beside her, fit for a daughter's prayer, which reshapes the cult toward the household the later epic cares about.","gr":3},{"a":"mm:26:14-32","b":"si:23:161-176","type":"qualifies","note":"Both tell Neeli's curse and close it with the same words, உம்மை வினை வந்து உருத்தல்; the Silappathikaram adds that penance does not help the unrighteous when the old deed comes and calls Bharatan a vow-breaker, while the Manimekalai calls him a doer of evil work and Sangaman faultless.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The later epic keeps the karmic verdict word for word but moves it into Kannaki's own mouth and sharpens the contrast of guilty and faultless, so the explanation that consoled her in Madurai becomes in Vanchi her own testimony against her husband's former self."},{"a":"mm:26:14-32","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain says an old deed seeks out its doer as a calf finds its own mother among many cows; Mathurapathi's word traces a killing done as Bharatan in Kalinga to Kovalan's death in Madurai, and says the former deed does not fail to come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows how far the calf can walk: the quatrain gives the deed a homing instinct, and the epic gives it a road across a birth, a kingdom and a change of name, with a widow's curse as the means by which it finds Kovalan.","gn":["the epic's account of a curse crossing births gains a plain maxim that explains why the curse cannot miss its man","the quatrain's abstract seeking deed gains a named case with a route, an agent (Neeli's curse) and a distance of a whole lifetime"]},{"a":"mm:26:33-41","b":"si:21:40-57","type":"tension","note":"In the Silappathikaram Kannaki says she raged at the city as one without fault (சீறினேன் குற்றமிலேன்) and the fire god burns Madurai at her command; in the Manimekalai the same Kannaki says she destroyed the city in anger (சீற்றம் கொண்டு ... சிதைத்தேன்) and calls it the sin of murderous anger, whose fruit awaits her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two epics judge the same act in the same woman's words in opposite ways: the earlier makes the burning the faultless power of chastity, the later makes it a sinful deed that will ripen, and the conflict turns on whether provoked anger can be free of karmic fruit.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:26:33-41","b":"k:305","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says anger left unguarded kills the one who holds it; Kannaki says her anger at Madurai, though it followed a just grievance, is a sin that will reach her after her heavenly merit ends.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Kannaki is the hardest case for the couplet, a woman whose anger an earlier epic praised as just, and the Manimekalai applies the rule to her anyway while stretching its reach across births: the self that anger kills is a future self, after heaven.","gn":["Kannaki's confession gains a stated rule that anger turns on its holder, which explains why a goddess fears her own deed","the couplet gains a test case of righteous anger and a time scale beyond one life, which its two lines do not give"]},{"a":"mm:26:33-41","b":"si:23:201-204","type":"qualifies","note":"The venba says gods worship the woman who worships her husband and that Kannaki has become the guest of the women of heaven; Kannaki here says she reached heaven by former good deeds and will leave it when that merit is spent.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Manimekalai accepts the Silappathikaram's heavenly welcome for Kannaki but makes it a stay that ends, so the earlier epic's final honour becomes one stage of a samsaric account whose real end is still to come."},{"a":"mm:26:42-63","b":"si:29:78-83","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Silappathikaram Kannaki appears in the sky, declares the Pandya faultless and a guest in Indra's palace, and says she will not leave Murugan's hill; in the Manimekalai she foretells her own rebirth, her hearing of the Buddha and her release, while meanwhile working wonders for all.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Buddhist epic does not deny the Pattini cult: it keeps Kannaki present and helpful as the earlier epic does, but makes that presence a long interval before a birth in which she must still hear the Buddha, so the goddess becomes a future disciple."},{"a":"mm:26:42-63","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says only those who reach the Lord's feet swim the great sea of births; Kannaki, though a goddess in heaven, says she must swim the hard sea of births (40) until she hears the Buddha's dharma and leaves birth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with the couplet, the epic shows that even a deified woman is still in the sea, and it names the means of crossing as hearing a teacher's dharma, which the couplet's 'Lord' leaves unnamed and which here replaces devotion with understanding.","gn":["Kannaki's prophecy gains the Kural's image of the sea of births as the frame for her long passage to release","the couplet's unnamed Lord gains a rival reading in which the crossing is made by hearing the Buddha's teaching, and in which gods too are swimmers"]},{"a":"mm:26:64-82","b":"mm:10:71-93","type":"develops","note":"The Manimekala goddess foretold that Manimekalai would hear the false words of many schools and gave her the mantra to take another form because no one would tell a young girl the truth (79-80); here the same two lines are recalled and she chants the mantra and becomes an ascetic.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The disguise is set off by two voices together: the Manimekala goddess supplied the means long before, and Kannaki now supplies the order of study, so the Buddhist sea goddess and the Tamil pathini goddess both direct the girl to the debates."},{"a":"mm:26:83-94","b":"si:27:1-17","type":"same_image","note":"The Silappathikaram tells how the stone for the pathini goddess, taken from the Himalaya, was set on the crowns of Kanaka and Vijaya and the goddess bathed in the Ganga; the Manimekalai names the same stone on the same crowns in a line of praise for Senguttuvan's city.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The stone that in the Silappathikaram makes Kannaki a goddess becomes in the Manimekalai a mark of the king's glory and the name of the city where Kannaki's daughter will learn that her mother's goddesshood is not release."},{"a":"mm:26:83-94","b":"pt:patikam:5","type":"develops","note":"The preface to the Patitruppattu's fifth decade says Senguttuvan went through the forests to take a stone for the goddess Pattini, defeated the Aryan lord and bathed it in the Ganga; the Manimekalai names the stone, the Ganga crossing and the defeat of Kanaka and Vijaya.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the stone-taking already fixed as the defining deed of this Chera in two different kinds of text, and shows the Manimekalai using that known deed only to date and dignify the city where its own Buddhist story continues.","gn":["the epic's brief royal praise gains a second witness that its Senguttuvan is the Chera of the Sangam decade's preface","the preface's one line on the Pattini stone gains the names of the defeated kings and the crowning detail that the epic tradition attached to it"]},{"a":"mm:27:1-24","b":"mm:29:43-54","type":"tension","note":"The logician's perception grasps name, class, quality and action and sits among ten means; Aravanar's Buddhist logic keeps two means and takes name, class, quality and action out of perception.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic stages the theory of knowledge as a reply across two kaathais: what the Vedic logician packs into seeing, the Buddhist teacher moves into inference, so the dispute is over where conceptual labels enter knowledge.","gr":4},{"a":"mm:27:41-56","b":"mm:29:55-71","type":"tension","note":"Comparison, scripture, presumption and the rest are independent means for the logician; the Buddhist teacher folds all other means into inference.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two lists show the Buddhist reduction is aimed at a named opponent: scripture's unseen worlds lose their standing as a separate source once they must be inferred.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:27:57-85","b":"mm:29:43-54","type":"develops","note":"The logician pairs the Buddhists with சினன் and with two means of knowledge; kaathai 29 has ஆதி சினேந்திரன் teaching exactly two.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The later kaathai confirms that சினன் in the list of schools names the Buddha, so the rival's account of Buddhism here is accurate on the count of means.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:27:57-85","b":"mm:27:284-289","type":"tension","note":"The logician files 'these are your parents', taken on another's word, as a false semblance of knowledge; Manimekalai offers knowing one's parents as an inference no one can refuse.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The chapter's opening list and its closing argument use the same example in opposite ways, so the heroine's rebuttal is also a correction of the logician's classification.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:27:86-95","b":"s:959","type":"develops","note":"The Saiva debater defines God as the eight forms; Pattinathar reads the eight forms and Siva's other emblems as teachings, and mocks those who whirl in word and meaning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the debate hall, Pattinathar's verse shows the Saiva poets turning the same eight-form creed from a debating thesis into a teaching the arguers fail to grasp.","gn":["the Saiva's bare list gains a Saiva reading of what each form is meant to teach","Pattinathar's scorn for word-proud disputants gains a scene of such disputants at work"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:27:96-107","b":"t:104","type":"qualifies","note":"Brahma-vadin, Vaishnava and Saiva each name a different maker or keeper; Tirumular says the three gods are one in continuity and that those who divide them quarrel.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"Manimekalai rejects the theistic schools for disagreeing with truth, while Tirumular faults only their division, so the same sectarian quarrel is read as error by the Buddhist and as a misunderstanding by the Saiva.","gn":["the epic's row of competing creators gains an insider's verdict that they need not compete","Tirumular's 'those who divide quarrel' gains a staged instance of the quarrel he condemns"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:27:120-141","b":"s:1363","type":"tension","note":"The Ajivika holds the elemental atoms to be without beginning; Sivavakkiyar says the elements and senses, and even the soul, are not beginningless, and only the truth mingled with them is.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The question of what is beginningless divides the two: for the Ajivika matter itself is eternal, for the Siddhar matter and soul are both made, and only the one truth is without beginning.","gn":["the Ajivika claim gains a direct contrary from a later Tamil voice using the same word","Sivavakkiyar's denial gains a named opponent who held the elements beginningless"],"gr":3},{"a":"mm:27:159-170","b":"k:620","type":"tension","note":"The Ajivika says everything a life meets is fixed at conception by prior fate; the Kural says those who strive without flagging see fate's back.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Against the Ajivika's fixed course, the couplet's claim that effort can rout fate stands out as a deliberate limit on oozh, which the Kural elsewhere (380) calls the strongest power.","gn":["the epic's rival doctrine of total fate gains the Tamil ethical tradition's answer","the couplet gains a full statement of the fatalism it resists"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:27:183-201","b":"t:1602","type":"qualifies","note":"Both end with the two deeds turned back; the Jain does it by blocking new deeds and exhausting old ones, Tirumular by keeping the Lord's feet in mind.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The pair shows Jain and Saiva agreeing that release needs both good and bad deeds stopped, and parting over whether the stopping is one's own labour or follows from holding the Lord.","gn":["the Jain account gains a Saiva parallel that isolates what is distinctive in its godless method","Tirumular's line on the two deeds gains a rival technique for the same end"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:27:202-226","b":"t:385","type":"qualifies","note":"The Sankhya speaker has space, wind, fire, water and earth unfold from intellect; Tirumular gives the same chain from மான் (mahat), in his chapter on creation.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same sequence of elements serves the Sankhya as nature unfolding by itself and Tirumular as part of the Lord's creating, so the cosmology was shared while its cause was disputed.","gn":["the Sankhya account gains a Tamil Saiva reuse of its sequence","Tirumular's uncertain verse gains a fuller statement of the chain it compresses"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:27:227-240","b":"t:451","type":"qualifies","note":"The Sankhya speaker counts twenty-five principles with no maker; Tirumular says the Lord makes the twenty-five within the womb.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Tirumular keeps the Sankhya count and adds a maker who shapes the twenty-five for each embryo, which is the step the Sankhya account leaves out.","gn":["the count of twenty-five gains a later life inside Saiva doctrine","Tirumular's twenty-five gains its source in a school that counted them without a god"],"gr":3},{"a":"mm:27:263-276","b":"nk:858","type":"same_image","note":"The materialist's awareness arising from the elements like intoxication from mixed ingredients appears in the Neelakesi's materialist as well.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Buddhist and Jain polemic both put the liquor simile in the materialist's mouth, which suggests it was the standard Tamil way to state his theory of mind.","gr":4},{"a":"mm:27:263-276","b":"s:1342","type":"tension","note":"The materialist makes awareness a product of the combined elements; Sivavakkiyar asks whether the dancing self is the five elements and answers with release into open space.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The tension depends on reading Sivavakkiyar's questions as rhetorical denials: the self that sees the house of release is not the elements, against the materialist for whom nothing survives their parting.","gn":["the materialist's claim gains a Siddhar who puts the same question and answers it the other way","Sivavakkiyar's question gains the explicit doctrine it rejects"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:27:277-283","b":"mm:9:1-15","type":"tension","note":"The materialist calls knowledge of former births the delusion of possession or dream; in kaathai 9 Manimekalai, carried off by the deity in sleep, knew her former birth without error.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The materialist's objection names the very conditions of the heroine's own knowledge, so the epic's story stands as her unspoken answer to him.","gr":4},{"a":"mm:27:277-283","b":"mm:26:64-82","type":"develops","note":"In kaathai 26 she is told to hear each school's doctrine, see it lacks truth, and only then follow the Buddha's way, and she takes an ascetic's form to do so.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The debate chapter carries out a method laid down beforehand: hearing the schools is a prescribed stage of her training, not an open inquiry.","gr":4},{"a":"mm:27:277-283","b":"nk:885","type":"tension","note":"Here the materialist explains away dream and possession as the mind changed; the Neelakesi's Jain asks him how his elements account for dreams, mantras and omens.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two epics use dream against the materialist in opposite directions: Manimekalai's opponent uses it to dismiss rebirth, Neelakesi uses it as evidence his elements cannot explain.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:27:284-289","b":"k:354","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says the fivefold sense-knowledge is fruitless without true understanding; Manimekalai tells the materialist, who trusts the senses alone, that true things are hard to know without true understanding.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet's general claim becomes a debating move: the heroine uses its contrast between sense-knowledge and true understanding to show that a perception-only rule cannot reach even one's parentage.","gn":["Manimekalai's rebuttal gains a classical Tamil statement of the same limit on the senses","the couplet gains a concrete opponent, the materialist, whom its limit refutes"],"gr":3},{"a":"mm:27:284-289","b":"mm:29:43-54","type":"develops","note":"Having heard the schools in an assumed form, she later tells Aravanar that the five kinds of religion are not right and she has not kept them in mind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The closing line's 'she learned the five kinds of religion' is completed by her verdict in kaathai 29, which turns the survey into a rejection.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:27:284-289","b":"t:1533","type":"qualifies","note":"Manimekalai learns the five kinds of religion on her way to the Buddha's truth; Tirumular says those who saw the six sects did not see him.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both texts make a survey of the sects a stage to pass through, but Manimekalai's ends in reasoned teaching and Tirumular's in clarity about a Lord the sects miss.","gn":["the epic's tour of the schools gains a Saiva verdict on the same exercise","Tirumular's dismissal of the sects gains a detailed record of what the sects said"],"gr":2},{"a":"mm:28:91-100","b":"si:27:78-95","type":"develops","note":"Maadalan reports that Kovalan's father, grieving, gave great gifts and renounced in Puhar's Indra viharas before the sky-walkers; in the Manimekalai the same man, in a Vanji monastery likened to an Indra vihara of sky-walkers, gives his own reason.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Silappathikaram tells his renunciation as a grief-driven act seen from outside; the sequel gives it from inside as doctrine, home as illusion and wealth and body as passing, so one renunciation is told first as a mourning and then as a conversion."},{"a":"mm:28:91-100","b":"n:53","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain has the best of people renounce home and wealth after slow reflection on impermanence; the ascetic renounces when the news of his son's death by evil deeds reaches him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows impermanence learned two ways: the quatrain's sage reasons his way to it, while the epic's merchant has it forced on him by bereavement and only then states it as the doctrine.","gn":["the ascetic's turn gains a known ethical type, the renouncer who sees nothing stands, against which his grief-driven timing stands out","the quatrain's unhurried reflection gains a case where the same insight comes suddenly, through a son's ruin"]},{"a":"mm:28:101-119","b":"a:127","type":"develops","note":"The Akananuru remembers Cheralathan who carved his bow on the Himalaya and heaped tribute at Maanthai; the epic's Chera of the carved bow feeds Buddhist sages in a Vanji grove. The Tamil shares only the epithet of the carved bow and the Chera line, not a name.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Holding them together shows the epic borrowing a Sangam conqueror's epithet to give Vanji's Buddhism a royal founder, turning the king remembered for hoarded tribute into a king remembered for a gift to monks.","gn":["the nameless king of the epic gains a Sangam identity as the bow-carving conqueror, which the epic leaves unstated","the poem's image of the conqueror's hoard gains a later legend in which the same line gives food rather than piles treasure"]},{"a":"mm:28:120-137","b":"mm:25:200-221","type":"develops","note":"The Vanji ascetics foretell that the sea will take Kaveripattinam; in kaathai 25 the sea swallows it by the Manimekala goddess's curse and the ascetic and the mothers go to Vanji.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The flood of Puhar is given two causes in the epic, a prophecy the seers already knew and a goddess's curse on a negligent king, so foreknowledge and a moral cause sit together without either cancelling the other."},{"a":"mm:28:138-149","b":"mm:26:33-41","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki's image says the two went to the gods by former good deeds, the evil deed will still come, and they will wander until the Buddha appears at Kapilai; the Vanji seers foretell the same and add that Kovalan's merit came from austerity under an earlier Buddha's Bodhi.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The second witness confirms the first and supplies its missing cause, so the couple's heaven is traced to a past Buddhist austerity, not to Kannaki's chastity alone."},{"a":"mm:28:138-149","b":"si:23:177-200","type":"qualifies","note":"The Silappathikaram ends Kannaki's story with her ascent in the gods' car with Kovalan; the Manimekalai says that this heaven only defers the evil deed, and that release waits for the Buddha at Kapilai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the apotheosis that closes the first epic becomes a way station in the second: a Buddhist sequel re-reads the pathini's reward as a heaven still bound by deeds."},{"a":"mm:28:138-149","b":"t:2313","type":"tension","note":"Both hold that heaven belongs to the round of deeds; Tirumular frees the soul from heaven, hell and earth by grace, while the seers have Kovalan freed by hearing the Buddha's dharma after he has eaten his deed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two agree that heaven is no escape from karma and part exactly on the way out: Saiva release is given by grace, while the Buddhist release comes after the deed is spent and the teaching heard, with no giver.","gn":["the seers' prophecy gains a rival account of release that makes plain it involves no god","Tirumular's verse gains a named case, Kovalan in heaven still owing his deed, of the soul turned through heaven by karma"]},{"a":"mm:28:150-161","b":"k:19","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says that without rain neither giving nor austerity lasts; the ascetic reports that Kanchi's ascetics, finding no almsgivers in the drought, have left for Vanji.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the couplet's rule as a chain: the drought ends giving first, and austerity, which lives on gifts, then has to leave the land.","gn":["the monks' migration gains a stated principle that explains why drought drives out ascetics","the couplet gains the mechanism it leaves implicit, that austerity fails because giving fails"]},{"a":"mm:28:150-161","b":"mm:21:147-171","type":"develops","note":"Thuvathikan foretold the drought in Kanchi and that she would bring rain there with the bowl; Kovalan's father now gives the same charge and asks whether she did not learn it from Thuvathikan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A goddess's foretelling becomes a human teacher's command, so her mission to Kanchi rests on two witnesses, one divine and one from her own family."},{"a":"mm:28:184-206","b":"k:559","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says the sky withholds rain when the king bends justice; the Kanchi king names the bent sceptre as only one of three possible causes and cannot tell which holds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet's law into a king's anxious self-examination, and then sets it aside: the deity names no fault and sends a renunciant's bowl instead of a reform of rule.","gn":["the king's question gains its source, the received rule that ties rain to the sceptre","the couplet gains a case where the rule cannot be applied, since the drought comes and the ruler cannot find his fault"]},{"a":"mm:28:184-206","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet gives the chaste wife power to bid rain fall; the king asks whether failed chastity among women withheld it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the couplet's power run in reverse: if a wife's chastity can command rain, the community's women can be suspected when it fails, and the epic records the suspicion without confirming it.","gn":["the king's third cause gains the couplet's claim that underlies it","the couplet gains its darker corollary, that drought can be laid at women's door"]},{"a":"mm:28:184-206","b":"mm:15:46-54","type":"develops","note":"In Puhar Aravanar asks whether Indra or the king of the just sceptre is at fault for the famine, in the words அலத்தல்காலை ஆகியது; the Kanchi king uses the same words about his own land and adds women's chastity.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The question first put by the teacher about another's kingdom is put by a king about himself, and both times the epic's answer is the bowl, not a judgment on the ruler."},{"a":"mm:28:162-183","b":"mm:17:83-98","type":"same_image","note":"In Puhar she appears with the bowl like a rain cloud over a scorched bamboo forest; in Kanchi the chamberlain says she has come like a great rain cloud.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The simile becomes literal: in Puhar the cloud is only food for the hungry, while in Kanchi her coming brings real rain to a land in drought."},{"a":"mm:28:207-219","b":"mm:11:37-58","type":"same_image","note":"On Manipallavam the bowl rises from the Gomukhi pool before the Buddha's seat once a year into a fit hand; in Kanchi the king builds a copy of the pool and seat and she sets the bowl on it for all beings.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The holy island is reproduced in a royal city, and the bowl that came out of it to one worthy hand is now placed on the copy of the seat and offered to everyone."},{"a":"mm:28:220-233","b":"k:85","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet asks whether the host who feeds guests need even sow his field; the epic likens the bowl's plenty to alms in ascetics' hands and to seed sown with the right water, soil, season and tools.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make feeding others a kind of sowing, but the couplet promises the giver's field yields unsown, while the epic makes the yield depend on conditions, the worth of the receiver among them.","gn":["the epic's image gains a contrast that shows how conditional its sowing is","the couplet's unconditional promise gains a rival view in which a gift's fruit depends on where and how it is sown"]},{"a":"mm:28:220-233","b":"mm:21:147-171","type":"develops","note":"Thuvathikan foretold that her bowl of dharma would give rain's plenty to a parched world; here the bowl feeds Kanchi and the rain returns.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The fulfilment shows the epic treating feeding as the cause of rain, reversing the usual order in which rain must come before there is food to give."},{"a":"mm:28:234-245","b":"mm:21:147-171","type":"develops","note":"Aravanar, in Thuvathikan's foretelling, blesses her with வாய்வதாக நின் மனப்பாட்டு அறம்; at Kanchi she says it of herself, வாய்வது ஆக என் மனப்பாட்டு அறம்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The teacher's blessing becomes the pupil's own vow in the same words, marking the point where she stops being foretold and begins to will her path."},{"a":"mm:28:234-245","b":"mm:28:101-119","type":"same_image","note":"The Chera king washes the charanas' feet and serves them six-flavoured food of four kinds; Manimekalai washes Aravanar's feet and serves the same.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The lay gift that founded Buddhism in Vanji is repeated by Manimekalai in Kanchi, so her entry into the order is staged as the same act of royal hospitality to monks."},{"a":"mm:29:1-16","b":"mm:1:10-34","type":"develops","note":"Kaathai 1 has the assembled sages warn that if the festival is forgotten the guardian spirit will bring harm; here a king searching for his lost son forgets the festival, and the curse follows.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The warning of the first kaathai turns out to name the cause of Puhar's end, and the lapse comes not from neglect but from a father's grief, so the city falls through the king's love rather than his vice."},{"a":"mm:29:29-42","b":"mm:7:26-39","type":"develops","note":"In kaathai 7 the goddess says Kovalan named the child after her and that she told Madhavi in a dream that the child would be a great ascetic; here Aravanar gives the reason for the naming, the goddess's rescue of the Bodhisattva ancestor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the name Manimekalai is a debt of the family line to a goddess who serves a future Buddha, so the renunciation foretold at her birth is the line's old bond coming due."},{"a":"mm:29:29-42","b":"mm:10:71-93","type":"develops","note":"The goddess foretold that Manimekalai would hear the false teachings of many schools and gave her the mantra to take another form; here Manimekalai reports having heard them so, in Vanji.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The tour of the schools in kaathai 27 was a prescribed stage, not a chance encounter: the goddess set it up so that Manimekalai would reject the schools herself before the teacher speaks."},{"a":"mm:29:29-42","b":"p:138","type":"tension","note":"Both turn from the noise of the schools to a teacher; Arunagiri asks Murugan for an upadesam and a vision past the logicians' quarrel, while Manimekalai's teacher answers with the rules of valid argument.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair shows two answers to the same fatigue with the schools: the Buddhist epic trains the hearer to argue rightly, the Saiva hymn asks to be lifted beyond argument by grace, and the tension holds as long as the logic is read as a path and not only as a tool.","gn":["the epic unit gains a contrast showing that its turn to a teacher leads into more reasoning, not out of it","the hymn's list of quarrelling schools gains a case where one of those schools, the Buddhist, is seen from inside, offering method rather than clamour"]},{"a":"mm:29:43-54","b":"k:423","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet makes understanding the power to find the true substance in whatever one hears from anyone; Manimekalai has heard every school and judged them wanting, but asks a teacher for the true doctrine.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the limit of the couplet's test: a hearer can reject what she hears from many mouths and still lack the means to find the truth, so the teacher gives her a method of knowing rather than an answer.","gn":["Manimekalai's refusal of the schools gains a general standard by which her judgment is measured","the couplet gains a case showing that discernment can stop at rejection, and needs the means of knowledge to go further"]},{"a":"mm:29:43-54","b":"k:355","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet bids one see the true thing whatever nature (தன்மை) a thing appears to have; the Buddhist teacher strips perception of name, class, quality and action and assigns them to inference.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On the Buddhist account the 'nature' the couplet looks past is largely the mind's own labelling, so seeing the true thing becomes a matter of separating bare perception from what inference adds.","gn":["the technical definition of perception gains a moral aim: the true thing the couplet calls understanding","the couplet's open word தன்மை gains one precise reading, as the class, quality and name the mind lays on what it sees"]},{"a":"mm:29:43-54","b":"mm:12:104-121","type":"develops","note":"Aravanar told her that she would not take up the teaching until the events caused by her had come to pass; now, after the prison, Aputhiran's land and the tour of the schools, she asks him for it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The long middle of the epic is framed as the waiting time Aravanar named, and her request now marks that the conditions he set have been met.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:29:55-71","b":"t:51","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says the wise gave up logic and debate (தர்க்க வாதம்), recited the Veda and gained release; the Buddhist teacher folds scripture into inference and makes logic the first part of the true doctrine.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two disagree on the same question, whether scripture stands as its own source of truth above argument, and the Buddhist answer explains why the epic's road to release passes through debate rules that the Saiva verse tells the wise to leave behind.","gn":["the epic's quiet reduction of all other means to inference gains a clear statement of the position it denies","the verse's dismissal of தர்க்க வாதம் gains a concrete instance of the logic it dismisses, taught as a path to release"]},{"a":"mm:29:72-84","b":"k:351","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says that taking what is not a thing for a thing breeds an unworthy birth; the teacher gives a test for absence, that a thing which exists appears like a gooseberry in the hand, and the hare's horn does not.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic turns the couplet's warning into a procedure: what would appear if it were there and does not appear may be judged absent, which gives the confusion the couplet fears a check in method.","gn":["the logic of non-appearance gains a moral stake, since the couplet ties the error it guards against to rebirth","the couplet's மருள் gains a means of correction, a rule for deciding what is not there"]},{"a":"mm:29:136-153","b":"mm:27:57-85","type":"qualifies","note":"The Vedic logician listed false appearances of knowing (shell for silver, post or man); the Buddhist teacher lists false theses, reasons and examples.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Set side by side, the two lists show the Buddhist teacher moving error from the senses to argument: what goes wrong is less a mistaken seeing than a thesis put in terms the other side cannot grant.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:29:173-190","b":"mm:27:227-240","type":"develops","note":"In kaathai 27 the Sankhya speaker describes the person as awareness itself; here 'the soul has consciousness' is the stock example of a thesis whose subject a Buddhist does not accept.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The doctrine Manimekalai heard laid out without reply in kaathai 27 returns as a lesson in debate, answered not by a counter-argument but by the rule that a debater cannot assume a soul his opponent denies."},{"a":"mm:29:173-190","b":"n:313","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain mocks debaters who do not know how to make what they learned reach the hearer; the teacher's false theses are exactly theses that cannot reach the hearer, because he does not grant their terms or already holds the conclusion.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic gives the quatrain's complaint a precise form: an argument fails not only by poor delivery but when its subject or conclusion is foreign or already granted to the one it is aimed at.","gn":["the technical rules gain an ethical frame, as the duty of a speaker to the one he addresses","the quatrain's 'not knowing how to make it reach' gains named cases showing what such a failure is"]},{"a":"mm:29:191-206","b":"mm:27:202-226","type":"develops","note":"The Sankhya speaker of kaathai 27 tells every stage of the world as வெளிப்பட்டு, becoming manifest; here 'because it is produced' is a reason a Sankhya does not accept, since for him things are manifested, not produced.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows that the epic's logic lesson is built on the doctrines Manimekalai heard in Vanji, so the account of the schools in kaathai 27 is accurate enough to be used as the test case here."},{"a":"mm:29:207-222","b":"mm:27:241-262","type":"develops","note":"The Vaisheshika of kaathai 27 counts space among the nine substances and sound among the qualities; here 'space is a substance by its quality of sound' is a thesis whose subject an opponent may deny.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The doctrine stated as settled fact in kaathai 27 is shown here to rest on a subject not every school grants, so the teacher answers the Vaisheshika by the form of debate rather than by contrary doctrine."},{"a":"mm:29:281-302","b":"mm:27:227-240","type":"tension","note":"Kaathai 27's Sankhya speaker gives the person as one, pervading and eternal awareness; here the argument for that person from the senses serving another is shown to prove a composite self.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic answers the Sankhya two kaathais later without denying it outright: its own proof for a partless self, followed through, yields a self with parts, so the refutation is internal."},{"a":"mm:29:303-325","b":"mm:27:241-262","type":"tension","note":"The Vaisheshika says generality gives being (உண்மை) to substance, quality and action; here the argument that being is other than substance, quality and action is shown to make being nothing.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read together, the units show the Buddhist teacher taking the Vaisheshika's own words for being and showing that its category, argued as the Vaisheshika argues it, empties itself."},{"a":"mm:29:424-449","b":"mm:27:241-262","type":"qualifies","note":"The Vaisheshika counts space among the nine substances; the teacher judges examples built on space under both views, that it is a thing and that it is not.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the logic lesson deliberately leaving the schools' quarrel over space unsettled: a proof is faulted on either view, so the method does not depend on deciding the rival's ontology."},{"a":"mm:29:450-472","b":"mm:27:57-85","type":"qualifies","note":"The Vedic logician lists eight false semblances of knowledge, errors of seeing and remembering; the Buddhist teacher ends with the semblances of inference, errors in a stated proof.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two catalogues of error divide the ground differently: the logician locates falsehood in a mistaken cognition, the Buddhist teacher in a badly built argument, which can be checked rule by rule.","gr":2},{"a":"mm:29:450-472","b":"k:357","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says that a mind which examines (ஓர்த்து) and knows what is with certainty (ஒருதலையா) need not think of birth again; Aravanar closes with examine (ஆய்ந்து) and know without doubt (ஐயம் இன்றி).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet names examination and certainty as the way out of birth but gives no procedure; the epic supplies one, and its technical word for certainty, ஏகாந்தம், is the couplet's ஒருதலை in Sanskrit dress.","gn":["the fallacy list gains a stated end beyond winning debates, the certainty the Kural ties to release","the couplet's bare 'examine and be certain' gains tests for when an argument has earned certainty"]},{"a":"mm:29:450-472","b":"t:51","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says the wise gave up logic and disputation (தர்க்க வாதம்) and gained release by reciting the Veda; Aravanar, who folds scripture into inference, ends his teaching of dharma with the rules of disputation.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two give opposite answers to whether argument leads toward release: for Tirumular it is what the wise leave behind, for the Buddhist teacher it is the discipline that sorts true from false before doctrine is heard.","gn":["the epic's logic lesson gains a named Saiva rejection of the very method it teaches","Tirumular's dismissal of argument gains a full example of the argument he dismisses, from a school that grants the Veda no separate authority"]},{"a":"mm:29:267-280","b":"k:353","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural promises the sky to those who have left doubt and become clear; the teacher describes two sound-looking proofs of opposite theses that together leave only doubt (சங்கயம்).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic shows a doubt that more argument cannot remove, since each side's reasoning holds, so the clarity the couplet praises cannot come from matching proofs alone.","gn":["the stalemate fallacy gains its stake: doubt is what the Kural says bars the way to the sky","the couplet's 'leaving doubt' gains a precise case of doubt that reasoning itself produces"],"gr":3},{"a":"mm:29:385-401","b":"k:331","type":"develops","note":"The Kural calls taking what does not last for what lasts the lowest understanding; Aravanar's model rule is 'whatever is made is impermanent', valid only in that direction.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Set beside the couplet, the textbook rule shows impermanence as something proved from being made, with the warning that the converse fails, so knowing the passing is a matter of inference as well as of seeing.","gn":["the stock example gains the moral weight the Kural gives to knowing impermanence","the couplet's demand to see the passing as passing gains a stated form of proof and its limit"]},{"a":"mm:29:281-302","b":"p:138","type":"tension","note":"Arunagirinathar lumps Buddhists with the followers of Kapila and Kanada as clamouring disputants whose books cannot give the teaching; here a Buddhist teacher takes apart arguments of the Sankhya and the Vaisheshika by formal logic.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the same quarrel from inside and outside: what the devotee hears as noise of books, the epic presents as a disciplined refutation, and it is the Buddhist the devotee counts first among the noisy.","gn":["the epic's refutation gains a later Saiva verdict that its whole contest misses the point","the song's list of disputants gains a working example of how those schools actually argued against each other"]},{"a":"mm:30:1-6","b":"mm:10:1-15","type":"develops","note":"The goddess hails Manimekalai for recovering her former birth at the Buddha's seat; here that knowledge of the former birth, in which she held to giving and the precepts, is what she brings to her taking of refuge.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two show that the memory of a former birth is not the end of her awakening but its qualification: what she recovered on Manipallavam is cashed here as the two practices that make her fit to take refuge and hear the doctrine."},{"a":"mm:30:244-264","b":"mm:12:104-121","type":"develops","note":"Aravanar told her that events would happen in the town because of her and that only after they had happened would she take up the teaching; the last lines show her hearing dharma and vowing to end becoming.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows that the epic frames its whole middle, from the prison to the debates of Vanji, as a foretold delay, so that this lesson fulfils a prophecy made eighteen kaathais earlier rather than starting a new episode."},{"a":"mm:30:34-58","b":"mm:27:159-170","type":"tension","note":"The Ajivika says all gain, loss, pleasure, pain, birth and death are fixed at conception by fate laid down before; the teacher says the chain has no fixed fate (ஊழ்பாடு இன்றி) and runs by conditions that can be reversed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kaathai 27 leaves the Ajivika's fixed fate unanswered, and this line supplies the Buddhist reply: the same goods and ills are conditioned links, so cutting a condition can stop them, which fixed fate rules out. The tension depends on reading ஊழ்பாடு as fixed fate."},{"a":"mm:30:34-58","b":"mm:27:96-107","type":"tension","note":"The Brahma-vadin says the world is an egg laid by one god; the teacher says the chain is made by no maker and is not a made thing (பண்ணுநர் இன்றிப் பண்ணப் படாதாய்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows that the creator Manimekalai dismissed without argument in Vanji is excluded here by the structure of the doctrine itself, since a chain in which each link supports the next leaves no place for a maker outside it."},{"a":"mm:30:170-182","b":"mm:27:227-240","type":"tension","note":"The Sankhya speaker holds the person to be the one eternal awareness that knows all; the teacher says no thing has a self and makes awareness (உணர்வு) a link conditioned by action.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show where the Buddhist answer to Sankhya lies: not in denying awareness but in making it the third link of a chain, born of action and ceasing with it, so the eternal knower of kaathai 27 is answered three kaathais later."},{"a":"mm:30:34-58","b":"k:351","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says a joyless birth comes from the confusion that takes what is not real as real; the teacher names ignorance first of twelve links that end in birth and defines it as holding hearsay clear, as that a hare has horns.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows that the Kural's மருள் can be given a precise epistemic fault, trusting report over what has been established, and that for the Buddhist teacher this confusion reaches birth only through ten links between, which the couplet's direct cause compresses.","gn":["a Tamil ethical precedent that already makes confusion about the real the cause of birth, so the first link reads as more than a Buddhist technicality","a concrete instance of the unreal taken as real, the hare's horn believed on hearsay, where the couplet names no example"]},{"a":"mm:30:109-124","b":"k:361","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural calls craving the seed that gives unceasing births to all beings; in the chain craving (வேட்கை) gives clinging, karma and birth, but itself arises from feeling and contact and ceases only when ignorance ceases.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make craving the seed of birth, but the chain gives the seed its own seed: craving depends on feeling and, eleven steps back, on ignorance, so the Buddhist cure begins with knowledge where the Kural's chapter begins with cutting craving.","gn":["evidence that the Tamil ethical tradition placed craving, not ignorance, at the root, which shows the chain's order as a doctrinal choice","an account of where craving comes from and how it is stopped, which the couplet's image of the seed leaves out"]},{"a":"mm:30:244-264","b":"k:360","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says pain perishes when the very names of desire, anger and delusion perish; the teacher names the same three, in the same order, as the cause of all he has taught and gives a separate remedy for each.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the three roots and promises the end of pain but gives no method; the epic, using the same three words, assigns each root its own discipline, contemplation of impermanence for desire, friendliness for anger, hearing and reflection for delusion.","gn":["the Kural's claim that pain ends wholly when even the names of the three go, a stronger end-state than the remedies alone state","a separate practice for each of the three, where the couplet says only that they must perish"]},{"a":"mm:30:244-264","b":"t:1802","type":"tension","note":"The teacher says bondage and release have their own causes and no one exists to grant them; Tirumular says grace put the soul into bonds and grace gives release.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two name the same pair, bondage and release, and give opposite answers to who brings them about: the epic denies any giver, and Tirumular makes one grace the giver of both, so the Buddhist and Saiva accounts of release divide exactly at line 251.","gn":["a named rival position, a giver of both bondage and release, that line 251's denial excludes","a counter-position that shows Tirumular's claim that binding itself is grace is the very claim a causal account without a giver rejects"]},{"a":"mm:30:59-83","b":"t:554","type":"qualifies","note":"The teacher's ten bad deeds include killing, theft, lust and lying, as Tirumular's yama does, but the epic sorts them by body, speech and mind and counts coveting, anger and wrong view as deeds, while Tirumular adds sharing food and shunning drink.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Set side by side, the two lists of ten show the same core abstentions serving different ends: for the epic they decide the next birth, for Tirumular they are the first step of yoga toward Siva, and only the epic counts states of mind as deeds.","gn":["a Saiva counterpart showing the same abstentions used as the first limb of yoga rather than as the ledger of rebirth","the epic's body, speech and mind sorting, which shows that the yama list has no item for coveting or wrong view"]},{"a":"mm:30:84-108","b":"n:59","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar says craving enters by five gates, body, mouth, eyes, nose and ears, and whoever guards them reaches release; the teacher counts six gates, with the mind among them, and places craving three links after the gates.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows two models of the senses: the quatrain makes the five gates the entry of craving and guarding them the path, while the chain adds the mind as a sixth gate and puts contact and feeling between gate and craving, so guarding the gates is not where the chain is cut.","gn":["an ethical practice of guarding the gates that the chain's definitions do not state","the mind as a sixth gate and the steps between sense and craving, which the quatrain's five gates leave out"]},{"a":"mm:30:183-197","b":"k:347","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says griefs keep hold of those who keep hold of attachment; the teacher's truths say sorrow is caused by birth and clinging, and release, which is joy, by not clinging.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural's one-verb picture of grip and grief becomes the second truth, and the epic adds its mirror, release as joy caused by not clinging, so the couplet's warning is shown to be half of a four-part formula.","gn":["a Tamil saying in which the grip one keeps and the grip of sorrow are one act, which the formula's dry causal statement lacks","the positive half the couplet omits: that not clinging is the cause of release, named as joy"]},{"a":"si:0:1-11","b":"si:23:177-200","type":"develops","note":"The kuravar's report of a one-breasted woman taken to heaven under a flowering vengai by the retinue of the king of the gods is the event the Katturai Kaathai narrates at its close, where Kannaki climbs the hill, stands under the vengai and boards the heavenly car with Kovalan.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The patikam gives the ascent as seen by strangers who call it a wonder, while the kaathai gives it from inside, with Kannaki naming herself one who did the deed of fire, so the pair shows the poem framing as a marvel what its heroine felt as guilt."},{"a":"si:0:12-26","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"tension","note":"The goldsmith names Kovalan the thief (கள்வன்) of the anklet; in the Vazhakkurai Kaathai the king, seeing the gem fly from the broken anklet, says that he, who heard the goldsmith, is the thief (யானே கள்வன்).","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The word thief passes in the course of the poem from the accused stranger to the goldsmith's own hidden act and finally to the king's mouth about himself, so the patikam's first accusation is set up to be reversed by the one who acted on it."},{"a":"si:0:27-36","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"qualifies","note":"The patikam says the Pandya did not inquire because the time of the deed's ripening had come; Kannaki addresses him as தேரா மன்னா, the king who did not inquire, and says ஊழ்வினை drove her husband to Madurai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The patikam makes karma the reason the king failed to inquire, but Kannaki uses that failure as her charge against him while giving karma only as what brought Kovalan to the city, so the epic keeps karma and royal fault as two causes and assigns blame only to the second."},{"a":"si:0:27-36","b":"k:548","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says a ruler who does not examine (ஓரா) and do justice falls by himself; the patikam has the Pandya fail to examine (தேரானாகி) and fall, but says he failed because the time of the deed's ripening had come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats the failure to examine as the king's own fault and the ruin as its consequence, and the epic keeps the consequence but places the failure itself inside a karmic order, which raises the question the couplet does not face of how a fated lapse can still be the king's guilt.","gn":["the epic's double cause gains a precise rule from outside the story against which the king's lapse is a fault in law and not only a misfortune","the couplet gains a test case in which the lapse it condemns is said to have been brought on by a past deed, so its self-caused ruin is shown with a second cause behind it"]},{"a":"si:0:27-36","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says the wife who worships her husband rather than a god commands the rain; the patikam says that because Kannaki was a pathini she burned Madurai and brought down its king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The power the couplet gives the devoted wife is a blessing on the world through rain, and the epic turns the same power into fire against a city once her husband is wronged, so the pair shows the chaste wife's word as a force that can destroy as well as nourish.","gn":["Kannaki's burning of the city gains a source in the ethics book's claim that a wife's devotion commands nature","the couplet's promise gains its dark counterpart: the same command over nature used for punishment, which the couplet's rain never suggests"]},{"a":"si:0:37-54","b":"si:23:138-160","type":"develops","note":"The patikam names only the curse of Sangaman's wife from a former birth; the Katturai Kaathai tells that Kovalan, then Bharatan, a king's servant, seized Sangaman as a spy and had him killed, and that his widow Nili, finding no refuge, cried for justice.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the curse is seen to repeat its cause exactly: a merchant falsely taken and killed by royal order, a wife left with no ground to stand on, so Kovalan's death mirrors the one he caused and Kannaki's lament repeats Nili's."},{"a":"si:0:37-54","b":"n:101","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain says an old deed finds its doer as a calf finds its mother among many cows; the patikam has a deed of a former birth in Singapuram find Kovalan in Madurai, and says it has run its course for both of them (நுங்கட்கு).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's deed seeks the one who did it, but the epic's curse reaches across a birth and a country through the word of the victim's widow and falls on the doer's wife as well, so the pair shows the epic widening the quatrain's single doer into a household.","gn":["Saathan's report gains a general law stated without story, that a past deed actively seeks its doer, which makes the goddess's word an instance and not a private verdict","the quatrain gains a case showing how the seeking works, through a curse spoken by another, and that it can fall on one who did not do the deed"]},{"a":"si:0:55-68","b":"si:20:92-95","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's first moral is that dharma becomes death to those who err in kingship; the first venba after the Pandya's death calls this the saying of many assemblies, that dharma is death to wrongdoers, and turns it on the queen as well.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam narrows the saying to kings and claims it as the poem's own thesis, while the venba gives it back to common opinion and extends it to the queen who dies with the king, so the pair shows the poem's moral both resting on a proverb and making it a rule of kingship."},{"a":"si:0:55-68","b":"k:380","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says nothing is stronger than ஊழ், which arrives first even when one devises (சூழினும்) another way; the patikam says the ஊழ்வினை comes in wrath and makes one eat it, through the finely made (சூழ்வினை) anklet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's fate outruns any plan, and the epic gives the plan a body: the anklet Kovalan meant to sell to start life again becomes the instrument of the fate it was meant to escape, and the epic adds that fate comes in anger and feeds its fruit, which the couplet does not say.","gn":["the epic's moral gains a sharper claim about scheming, that devising against fate is itself overtaken, which explains why Kovalan's fresh start ends in his death","the couplet's abstract race between fate and plan gains a concrete object, the anklet, and an emotional colour, the wrath of the returning deed"]},{"a":"si:0:55-68","b":"t:1649","type":"tension","note":"The patikam states as a thesis that the deed of the past comes in wrath and makes one eat it; the Tirumanthiram says that by Siva's grace deeds do not cling (வினை சேரகிலாமை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"If the patikam's line is read as admitting no release, the two give incompatible accounts of whether a past deed must be eaten, and the epic offers no grace that could stop it, since even the gods appear only to honour Kannaki after the deed has run its course; the Tirumanthiram puts grace before the fruit.","gn":["the epic's moral gains its sharpest contrast, which shows that its karma is unmediated: no god in the patikam intervenes before the fruit","the Tirumanthiram's claim gains a rival from the same language and land in which deeds do cling and must be eaten, so its grace is seen as an answer to a live view"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:1:1-12","b":"k:559","type":"develops","note":"The benediction likens the Chola's bounty to the rain pouring from above; the couplet makes rain itself depend on the king's not bending justice.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read against the couplet, the opening simile of rain and royal grace is not mere flattery but the old belief that the sky's giving follows the king's justice, which the epic will put to the test when a Pandya's crooked verdict brings fire rather than rain on his city.","gn":["the simile gains a causal ground: rain and royal grace are linked because a just king's realm is the one the sky waters","the couplet's rule gains a ceremonial setting, sung at a wedding as praise rather than stated as a warning"]},{"a":"si:1:1-12","b":"n:21","type":"tension","note":"Both see the king's white umbrella as the moon; the benediction praises the moon for resembling the Chola's sheltering umbrella, the quatrain says every king under such an umbrella has been carried off as dead.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same moon-umbrella serves the epic's opening as a sign of rule that shelters the world and serves the Naladiyar as proof that such rule ends, so the pair shows the epic beginning in the confidence the ethics book was written to undercut.","gn":["the hymn's royal moon gains a shadow: the image it uses for lasting shelter is the Naladiyar's stock image of the king who dies","the quatrain's umbrella gains an instance of the praise it answers, the court poet's moon that is meant to outlast the king"]},{"a":"si:1:13-29","b":"mm:25:175-199","type":"tension","note":"The epic opens saying the learned speak of Puhar only as standing unshaken, never of its decline; the Manimekalai tells how the city was swallowed by the sea when the Indra festival was neglected and Manimekala Deivam cursed it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Taken together, the two epics make the city whose permanence the wise affirm at the first wedding the city the sea takes in the next generation, so the opening's confidence becomes the first instance of the impermanence the sequel preaches."},{"a":"si:1:30-44","b":"mm:28:62-90","type":"develops","note":"Kovalan's father is introduced as the master of great wealth who pours out his riches for others; in the Manimekalai Manimekalai finds him in a Buddhist monastery (பள்ளி) as one who has taken up great austerity (மா தவம்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The merchant whose giving is praised at his son's wedding ends as a renouncer, so the pair traces in one man the passage from the householder's generosity to the renunciation the Manimekalai holds higher."},{"a":"si:1:45-68","b":"si:3:154-175","type":"tension","note":"The wedding women bless Kannaki never to be parted from her lover nor loosen her embrace; two kaathais later Kovalan buys Madhavi's garland, cannot leave her and forgets his home.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The blessing names the very loss the story brings within two kaathais, so the wedding song works as an unknowing forecast, and the separation reads as a reversal of a rite performed with fire and Veda."},{"a":"si:2:1-11","b":"k:1103","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet asks whether the lotus-eyed god's world is sweeter than sleep on the beloved's soft arms; the epic likens the newly married couple to those who enjoy in Uttarakuru the fruit of completed austerity.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both weigh a couple's joy against a heavenly world, but the couplet ranks love above heaven while the epic makes the couple's joy itself a heaven earned by past austerity, and so something that can be used up, which is how the epic prepares its turn from pleasure to karma.","gn":["the epic's simile gains a contrast: love here is not set above heaven but counted as heaven's wage, a measure of merit rather than a rival to it","the couplet's rhetorical question gains an answer it does not give, that the beloved's arms may be a heaven only for as long as the merit behind them lasts"]},{"a":"si:2:53-72","b":"k:1115","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says the woman who wore anicham flowers with their stems will hear no good drums for her waist; Kovalan asks why her attendants loaded her with garland, musk and pearls, making her small waist suffer.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic takes the Kural's single joke of a waist too fine to bear flowers and spreads it over a whole toilette, moving the lover's praise from the secret meeting into the marriage chamber, where it is the husband who speaks it and the maids who are blamed.","gn":["Kovalan's catalogue gains a known convention behind it, so its reproach of the attendants reads as praise in the established form of நலம்புனைந்துரைத்தல்","the couplet gains a speaker and setting it lacks, a husband in the first days of marriage, showing the convention used outside the lovers' secret union"]},{"a":"si:2:73-90","b":"si:16:62-73","type":"tension","note":"The elder lady sets Kannaki up in a household for charity and the hosting of guests; in Madurai Kannaki says she has lost giving to the virtuous, tending Brahmins, receiving ascetics and welcoming guests.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The household the second kaathai founds for its charity and hospitality is named, duty by duty, as what Kannaki has lost, so the pair shows her grief in Madurai as the loss of a household's dharma, not only of a husband's company."},{"a":"si:2:73-90","b":"si:16:74-90","type":"same_image","note":"In the bridal chamber Kovalan calls Kannaki gold and doll (பொன்னே, பாவாய்) for her beauty; in Madurai, after she has followed him, he calls her gold, creeper and doll of modesty for the steadfastness that brought her with him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same endearments return with a new ground: the words that praised her body in the chamber praise her chastity and endurance on the road, so the pair traces Kovalan's turn from admiring Kannaki's beauty to recognising her virtue."},{"a":"si:2:73-90","b":"k:81","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says all of keeping a house is for tending guests and doing good; the epic's household is set up so that its charity and hospitality may be seen, and the story later takes both away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic founds Kannaki's household on exactly the couplet's purpose, then lets a husband's absence and exile empty it, showing that the householder's dharma the couplet assigns to the house depends on the pair staying in it.","gn":["the narrator's brief line on the household's purpose gains the weight of a stated rule, so its later loss reads as the loss of the household's whole reason","the couplet's rule gains a case of its failure: a wife ready to keep it who cannot once her husband leaves the house"]},{"a":"si:2:91-94","b":"n:25","type":"tension","note":"The quatrain mocks one who sees a corpse carried to the burning ground yet marries and thinks there is joy here; the venba says the newly married couple enjoyed every pleasure like people who had seen the world's impermanence.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same knowledge that nothing on earth lasts is, for the Naladiyar, a reason to give up marriage and its joys and, in the epic's venba, a reason to take them while they last, so the pair sets out the two lessons impermanence can teach.","gn":["the venba's last line gains a rival reading of its own premise, so its haste for pleasure is seen as a choice the ethics book condemns","the quatrain gains the voice it argues against: not a fool blind to death but lovers who claim to have seen it and draw the opposite lesson"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:2:91-94","b":"si:3:154-175","type":"tension","note":"The venba says husband and wife, joined like coiled serpents, never parted; in the next kaathai Kovalan buys Madhavi's garland, cannot leave her and forgets his home.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The closing praise of a union that never parts is reversed within one kaathai, and the venba's own hint of impermanence turns out to be about the marriage itself rather than about the world.","gr":2},{"a":"si:3:1-11","b":"si:3:111-128","type":"same_image","note":"Madhavi's line is traced to heavenly women freed from a sage's curse, shared with Indra's son, at the place of the talaikkol; the talaikkol staff she later receives is worshipped as Jayanta, Indra's son.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The staff of honour Madhavi wins is the figure of the curse's release that her own descent goes back to, so her investiture re-enacts that release at the very moment she enters the bond that will undo Kovalan."},{"a":"si:3:154-175","b":"si:9:67-79","type":"develops","note":"Kovalan buys Madhavi's garland at the price of the king's 1008 kazhanju and forgets his home; returning to Kannaki, he says that sporting with a deceiver (சலதி) has wasted the mountain of wealth his line gave him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The narrator tells the purchase as a rule-bound custom and blames no one, and only later does Kovalan himself call Madhavi a deceiver and the purchase a ruin, so the epic lets the buyer, not the poet, put the blame on the seller."},{"a":"si:3:1-11","b":"mm:2:17-33","type":"develops","note":"The Silappathikaram tells Madhavi's seven years of training to be shown to the king; in the Manimekalai, Vasantamalai lists her arts item by item, opening with the same pair வேத்தியல் பொதுவியல், and reports the town saying her turn to tapas is shameful.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The accomplishment that the first epic crowns with the king's title becomes, in the sequel, the inventory of what Madhavi has given up, so the length of her training is what makes the town read her renunciation as a disgrace."},{"a":"si:3:154-175","b":"mm:2:58-75","type":"develops","note":"Kovalan, infatuated, knows no letting go (விடுதல் அறியா விருப்பினன்) once he buys the garland; in the Manimekalai, Madhavi tells how Aravana taught her that the great sorrow of the born comes from attachment (பற்றின் வருவது) and gave her the five precepts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The sequel's Buddhist teaching names as attachment the cause that the first epic's narrator only shows as an infatuation that cannot let go, so the garland scene becomes the case the doctrine later diagnoses, told to the woman who sold the garland.","gr":2},{"a":"si:3:154-175","b":"k:911","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet warns that the sweet words of bangled women who want wealth and not love bring harm; in the epic the garland priced at the king's reward is sent into the street by the doe-eyed woman, Madhavi speaks no word, and Kovalan buys it of his own infatuation.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic splits the couplet's single figure into a household that sets the price in the king's own coin and a dancer who is silent, so the harm the couplet lays on the woman's sweet speech here comes from a public custom and from the buyer's own desire.","gn":["the scene gains a rule against which Kovalan's purchase can be judged, which the narrator withholds","the couplet gains a case in which no sweet word is spoken and the harm still follows, so its warning must reach the custom and the buyer, not only the woman's speech"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:4:47-57","b":"si:2:53-72","type":"same_image","note":"At the wedding night Kovalan asks why her admirers decked her with other ornaments besides the flawless auspicious one (மறுஇல் மங்கல அணியே அன்றியும் பிறிதுஅணி); alone at evening she delights in no ornament but the auspicious one (மங்கல அணியின் பிறிதுஅணி மகிழாள்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The husband's compliment that she needs no ornament but the marriage one comes true literally as the mark of his desertion, so the poet builds her abandonment out of his own flattery."},{"a":"si:4:47-57","b":"si:9:67-79","type":"develops","note":"The anklets Kannaki leaves off in grief (அணிசிலம்பு ஒழிய) are the anklets she offers with a smile when Kovalan returns ruined and sees her wasted body (சிலம்புஉள கொண்மின்).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epic's fatal anklet enters the story as an ornament set aside in loneliness, and that unworn state is what lets it become the capital for the journey to Madurai."},{"a":"si:4:21-34","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"tension","note":"The crescent is praised as founder of the Pandya line and as ruling the stars without swerving from its nature (பான்மையில் திரியாது); in kaathai 20 a Pandya, umbrella drooping and sceptre slack, calls himself the thief.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The epic's first word on the Pandya line is a praise of unswerving rule set over a scene of Kovalan's straying, and the later verdict turns that praise against its own king; the tension rests on reading the moon's constancy as the line's."},{"a":"si:4:85-88","b":"si:1:1-12","type":"develops","note":"The benediction hails the moon because it is like the Chola's cool white umbrella sheltering the world; the venba makes the moon like a guardian king's white umbrella that is shade to the joined and heat to the rest.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The umbrella that the opening praised as sheltering everyone is here given its second edge, and the edge falls on Kannaki: royal protection, like love, covers only those inside the bond.","gr":2},{"a":"si:4:58-71","b":"a:71","type":"develops","note":"Both have women parted from their lovers at evening (காதலر்ப் பிரிந்த, nearly the same half-line), and both liken their breath to air blown in a smith's furnace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic takes a Sangam paalai heroine's evening lament nearly verbatim and turns it from one woman's crisis into a class of Puhar's women, one entry in a survey of the city's nights.","gn":["the epic's brief list gains the depth of a full lament, the friend, the fear of death in the evening, that its two lines only gesture at","the Akananuru poem gains evidence that its furnace image and half-line had become stock diction a later poet could cite as a type"]},{"a":"si:4:1-20","b":"k:1228","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's woman hears the herdsman's flute as evening's envoy and a killing weapon; the epic sets the herdsmen's music and the humming bee over an evening that grieves the parted and delights the joined.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Heard together, the two show that the flute's cruelty lies in the listener's separation, not in the sound, since the same pastoral music opens a night of delight for others in the epic.","gn":["the epic's impartial evening gains the inside of one parted listener, for whom its music is a weapon","the couplet gains a wider scene in which its deadly flute is, for other hearers, simply the sign of nightfall"]},{"a":"si:4:85-88","b":"k:1229","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's woman says the whole town reels in sorrow when evening spreads; the venba says the same moonlit night is cool to Madhavi with her lover and hot to Kannaki without him.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The parted woman's sense that the whole town suffers with her is set against a city where one night divides two women of the same house's story, which shows her claim is the projection of grief.","gn":["the venba's neat sorting of two women gains the parted woman's own conviction that evening is a public calamity","the couplet's town-wide sorrow is shown to be a single woman's view, since the epic's city is split by the same hour"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:5:16-39","b":"pp:9:185-193","type":"same_image","note":"The epic's line 21, வளம்தலை மயங்கிய நனந்தலை மறுகும், is the Pattinappaalai's line 193 on the same city's streets of mingled wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Pattinappaalai uses the line to close a map of goods arriving from every mountain and sea, while the epic attaches it to a census of Puhar's own craftsmen and traders, so the borrowed praise moves from what flows into the port to who works in it.","gn":["its catalogue is anchored in an older poem's praise of the same streets, which tells the reader this is the canonical Puhar","the older line gains a list of the people behind the wealth, whom its own poem leaves out"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:5:89-110","b":"pp:9:271-291","type":"develops","note":"Pattinappaalai's Thirumavalavan makes the northerners wilt among many regional conquests; the epic's Thirumavalavan marches to the Himalaya and brings back gifts from three named northern kings.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two show a Sangam king's memory being enlarged: one line of the older poem about the north becomes a whole campaign whose trophies are buildings in Puhar, so the epic makes the conquest serve its picture of the city.","gn":["the epic's past king gains a Sangam record under the same name, showing the episode draws on remembered praise","the poem's brief line on the northerners gains a later tradition that turned it into a Himalayan expedition"]},{"a":"si:5:89-110","b":"pn:66","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 66 praises Karikal Valavan's victory at Venni but says the defeated king who fasted to death for shame at his back wound is better than he; the epic's Valavan is checked by no rival, only by the Himalaya.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Purananuru sets a moral limit on the victor through the vanquished man's honour, while the epic removes every human limit and lets only a mountain halt him, so the later memory of the Chola has lost the Sangam poet's check on royal glory.","gn":["the epic's war-lust with no rival left gains a contrast that shows what its praise omits","the poem's lesson that the defeated may be the better man gains a later portrait in which no such man remains"],"gr":3},{"a":"si:5:59-75","b":"mm:1:52-72","type":"same_image","note":"The blessing பசியும் பிணியும் பகையும் நீங்கி வசியும் வளனும் சுரக்க is uttered in both: here by possessed old women at the spirit's altar, in Manimekalai by the royal drummer announcing the festival.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same two lines pass from the trance speech of warrior women at a spirit's altar to the official proclamation of the king's crier, showing the sequel turning a possessed blessing into the festival's civic formula.","gr":2},{"a":"si:5:128-134","b":"mm:1:10-34","type":"develops","note":"Manimekalai's councils warn that if the festival is forgotten, the spirit that relieved the king and the spirit that beats and eats wrongdoers bound in its noose will not stay.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Silappathikaram presents the crossroads spirit as a standing marvel of Puhar, while the Manimekalai makes its presence depend on keeping Indra's festival, so the city's moral guard becomes something that can be lost by neglect."},{"a":"si:5:128-134","b":"k:274","type":"qualifies","note":"The spirit's first victims are those who hide behind penance (தவம்மறைந்து ஒழுகும்); Kural 274 likens hiding in penance to a hunter hiding in a bush to snare birds.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural shows why the false ascetic's concealment is dangerous, that it lets him prey on others, but names no penalty; the epic imagines a city where the concealment is publicly broken and punished with death, setting a sanction beside the couplet's diagnosis.","gn":["the spirit's list gains the couplet's reason why the hidden ascetic is the worst of wrongdoers","the couplet gains a civic answer to the hidden hunter, a power that sees through the bush"]},{"a":"si:5:135-140","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"develops","note":"Puhar has an image that weeps when the sceptre bends; in Madurai the Pandya's sceptre bends and he himself declares 'I am the thief' and dies.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Puhar's check on a bent sceptre is an image that can only weep, while Madurai has no such image and the king must find and judge his own fault at the cost of his life, so the epic moves from a ritual sign of injustice to its full price."},{"a":"si:5:135-140","b":"k:559","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 559: if the king bends justice the sky bends and withholds rain; the epic's image weeps when either the king's sceptre or the judge's reading bends.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both make a mute power answer a bent justice, but the Kural's answer punishes the whole land through the rain while the epic's image only grieves, and the epic adds a second culprit, the judge who twists the law-book, which the couplet does not consider.","gn":["the weeping image gains the Kural's claim that a bent sceptre has consequences beyond the court","the couplet gains the point that justice can be bent by the king's judges as well as by the king"]},{"a":"si:5:218-234","b":"k:1083","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1083's lover learns that Death has a woman's form; the epic says Death took a woman's form because the king's just sceptre forbade its killing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's conceit of the deadly beauty is a lover's discovery, while the epic gives it a political cause, the sceptre that holds Death in check, so a love-figure is turned into praise of the king.","gn":["the epic's wonder gains the Kural's established conceit of Death in a woman's grace","the couplet's figure gains a reason inside a just kingdom, why Death must disguise itself"]},{"a":"si:5:235-240","b":"si:9:55-66","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki's left eye throbs at the festival; in Kaathai 9 a servant announces that Kovalan has come home.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The good omen is fulfilled in Kovalan's return, but the return leads at once to the journey to Madurai, so the sign of reunion is also the first step toward his death."},{"a":"si:5:235-240","b":"si:7:286-300","type":"develops","note":"Madhavi's right eye throbs at the festival; at the end of the Kaanal Vari Kovalan, his former deeds come to fruit, lets go of her and leaves.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bad omen given to Madhavi on Indra's festival is fulfilled at the seashore at the festival's end, where the story names former deeds as the cause, so the omen and the karma explanation frame the same parting from two sides."},{"a":"si:6:1-17","b":"si:5:59-75","type":"develops","note":"The fifth kaathai shows Puhar's warriors and women making offerings at the guardian bhuta's altar that Indra sent; here the vidyadhara tells why it was sent: a Chola guarded Amaravati against the asuras and received the bhuta and the five halls as Indra's gift.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the townspeople's worship and the outsider's origin story show the festival as heaven repaying a Chola's service, which the city's own rite does not explain.","gr":2},{"a":"si:6:18-26","b":"si:3:1-11","type":"develops","note":"The third kaathai says a sage's curse on Indra's son and a celestial woman was lifted at the dance stage, and introduces Madhavi; here a celestial dancer is cursed to earth for failing before Indra, and Madhavi is of her line.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Madhavi's art is given a heavenly genealogy rooted in a fall: the courtesan's dancing on earth is the continuation of a curse that her first performance was said to end.","gr":2},{"a":"si:6:46-67","b":"si:3:1-11","type":"develops","note":"Lines 66-67 repeat the formula of Madhavi's training (பதினோர் ஆடலும் ... விதிமாண் கொள்கையின் விளங்க) from the third kaathai, now as the dances a heavenly watcher sees her perform.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The repeated formula marks what was learned as a girl's curriculum turning into a public festival performance watched even by beings of the sky.","gr":2},{"a":"si:6:27-45","b":"t:343","type":"tension","note":"The epic tells Siva's burning of the three cities as an event (the gods asked, the fiery arrow obeyed) and the origin of a dance staged at a city festival; Tirumular calls those who say Siva burned the three cities fools and makes the cities the three impurities.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same myth stands in one text as a literal victory reenacted by a courtesan for a crowd and in the other as an inner allegory whose literal telling is folly; the conflict depends on reading the epic's account as a statement of event.","gn":["the epic's festival myth is shown to be one available reading, the literal and performative one, which a Saiva yogic text explicitly rejects","Tirumular's 'fools' get a concrete target: a public culture in which the Tripura burning was danced on a stage as history"]},{"a":"si:6:99-114","b":"k:1330","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet's man says sulking is love's sweetness and the embrace after it sulking's sweetness; the epic says Madhavi gave Kovalan both union and sulking, after he had sat in a sulk.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the epic the two halves of the couplet's cycle are things a trained woman gives, so ūdal becomes an art supplied to a lover rather than only a mood that lovers fall into.","gn":["Madhavi's giving of ஊடல் and கூடல் is placed in the Kural's account of love, where sulking followed by union is love's fullest pleasure","the couplet's private cycle is shown inside a courtesan's house, where it can be practised and offered, which raises the question the couplet does not ask, whose feeling it is"]},{"a":"si:6:99-114","b":"n:371","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain says the harlot's love is like a lamp that dies when its oil is spent; here Madhavi, a courtesan, gives Kovalan union and sulking and herself asks for the outing, and in the story it is Kovalan, not she, who breaks off (silappathikaram:7:286-300).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic's courtesan breaks the quatrain's rule from the other side: the love ends, but by the man's suspicion on the shore, not by her turning away when his wealth runs out.","gn":["Madhavi's devotion in this unit is set against the moral literature's verdict on her class, which makes it pointed rather than merely decorative","the quatrain's general rule meets a counter-case in the classical story most read by Tamil readers, which limits it to a type rather than every courtesan"]},{"a":"si:6:133-149","b":"pp:9:111-117","type":"develops","note":"The Pattinappalai's fishermen count the coloured lights of Puhar's mansions at night; the epic counts the shore's lamps by trade, from perfume-sellers and hawkers to the lighthouse, fishing boats and foreign traders.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The idyll's lights are a spectacle watched from the boats, while the epic's are a roll of the port's livelihoods, showing how the later poem turns the same harbour of lights into a social survey.","gn":["the epic's catalogue gains a Sangam precedent for Puhar as a city known by its lights, which marks it as a set piece of the port, not a passing detail","the idyll's anonymous lights gain owners: the epic names who keeps each lamp, filling in the trades behind the glow"]},{"a":"si:6:150-174","b":"a:376","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the phrase of Karikal 'taking the cool water' (தண்பதம் கொள்ளும் / தண்பதம் கொண்டு): in the Akananuru a courtesan recalls how at Karikal's water festival at Kazhar the Kaveri carried off the dancer Atthi, and likens her lover's loss to it; the epic likens the joyful crowd on the shore to the first day of that festival.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The festival the epic uses as a simile of joy is, in the Sangam poem, the scene of a dancer lost and a courtesan abandoned, which is what this shore day will become for Madhavi.","gn":["the simile carries a buried memory of loss at the same festival, so the epic's joyful shore is shadowed before the songs begin","the Akananuru's water festival gains a fuller picture of its crowds, costumes and noise and of its standing as a byword for Puhar's festivity"]},{"a":"si:6:150-174","b":"pp:9:218-228","type":"qualifies","note":"The Pattinappalai's speaker, in the poem for Karikal, swears he will not leave his long-haired woman even if given all of Puhar; the epic, invoking Karikal's festival, seats Kovalan beside Madhavi on Puhar's shore, where before evening he will leave her (silappathikaram:7:286-300).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The idyll's vow that no city outweighs the beloved is tested on the very shore it praised, and the epic's lover fails it amid Puhar's full splendour.","gn":["Kovalan's coming desertion is measured against a famous Sangam refusal to leave a woman for Puhar, which sharpens its cost","the idyll's hyperbole gains a narrative counter-case showing that leaving was the choice it imagined refusing"]},{"a":"si:6:150-174","b":"si:7:286-300","type":"tension","note":"Here the lute passes into Madhavi's hand in a bower set up for pleasure; in the next kaathai the songs she sings on it make Kovalan think her false, and, his ūzhvinai coming to fruit, he leaves her on the shore.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The instrument and the place prepared for the lovers' pleasure become the means and site of their parting, which the next kaathai assigns to karma acting through the lute's music.","gr":2},{"a":"si:7:1-20","b":"si:7:286-300","type":"develops","note":"Madhavi hands Kovalan the yazh and he chooses the songs; at the kaathai's close the narrator says his old deed came to fruit over the yazh music and he left her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the narrator placing the first turn of the story's karma not in a crime or a curse but in a lute passed between lovers at play, so the moral of the patikam that past deeds come to fruit begins with a song chosen for pleasure."},{"a":"si:7:21-28","b":"si:7:154-161","type":"tension","note":"Kovalan's Kaveri proves her chastity by not sulking when the Chola unites with the Ganga; Madhavi's Kaveri walks as she does because her husband's sceptre never bends.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Sung back over the same river, the two stanzas put the burden of fidelity on opposite partners: Kovalan asks the wife to bear the husband's other union, Madhavi makes the river's grace the sign of a husband who keeps straight, so the exchange is a quarrel about who owes faithfulness."},{"a":"si:7:21-28","b":"si:7:142-153","type":"develops","note":"The song praises a wife who does not sulk at her husband's other love; Madhavi hears in Kovalan's songs a hidden intent and takes up the yazh in a sulk disguised as pleasure.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The song's ideal of the wife who never sulks is answered at once by the courtesan's sulking, so the kaathai shows the praise of patient chastity working as a provocation on the one woman who is not his wife."},{"a":"si:7:21-28","b":"ai:42","type":"tension","note":"In the Ainkurunuru the wife begins to push away her husband's chest, broad as the Kaveri's full flood, in sulking; in the vari the Kaveri herself is the wife who never sulks at the Chola's other unions.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam marutham gives the Kaveri's flood to the straying husband and lets the wife sulk at it, while the epic song makes the river the wife and her not sulking the highest chastity, so the same river image moves from licensing the wife's oodal to forbidding it.","gn":["the stanza's demand for a wife without oodal stands out against the older convention in which the wronged wife's sulking was expected and even loved","the quatrain's playful river-chest gains a later reading in which the river is no longer the husband's breadth but the wife's patience"]},{"a":"si:7:29-36","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet makes sulking love's salt, good in right measure and spoiling only when overdone; the stanza calls not sulking at all, even at the husband's other union, a woman's great chastity.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural sets a measure for oodal, the epic song sets it at nothing and names that karpu, so together they show the measure of sulking turning from a matter of love's taste into a test of a wife's virtue.","gn":["the stanza's praise gains a standard to be measured against: by the couplet it asks for no salt at all","the couplet's measure gains its limit case, a song that would remove sulking entirely from a wife's part"]},{"a":"si:7:45-52","b":"a:320","type":"develops","note":"The Akananuru friend asks the lord of the shore whether the oath he swore when his chariot crushed the girls' sand dolls was false too, with the sea as witness; the vari asks how simple girls could know that the man who swore by the sea god would break it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The pair shows the epic lifting the neithal friend's charge, an oath with the sea as witness and the girls' sand-play spoiled, out of the courtship plot where it pressed for marriage and into a sung entertainment, where the charge of broken faith is performed by the man who will himself walk out at the song's end.","gn":["the stanza gains its source situation: the sea-oath and the spoiled sand-play were a friend's demand for marriage before they were a song for a courtesan","the Akananuru poem gains evidence that its situation had become a fixed piece of Puhar song, recognisable enough to be sung by a merchant at the shore festival"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:7:45-52","b":"si:7:286-300","type":"tension","note":"Kovalan sings the girls' blame of a man who swore by the sea god and broke his word; when Madhavi answers in song, he decides she is the deceiver and leaves her.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The singer who voiced the charge against the oath-breaking man ends the kaathai by abandoning his woman and calling her the one full of lying tricks, so the story turns the song's accusation back on the one who sang it."},{"a":"si:7:69-75","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says this woman is herself the cure for the sickness she brings; the vari asks whether the sickness her face and eyes gave can be cured only by her breasts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The song splits the couplet's paradox across the body, the eyes and face causing the sickness and the breasts curing it, which shows how a later lyric turned the Kural's single riddle into a scene of parts.","gn":["the stanza's closing guess gains a known paradox behind it, that the beloved breaks the rule of medicine by being both cause and cure","the couplet gains a concrete setting and a division of roles within the woman's body that it leaves unstated"]},{"a":"si:7:83-89","b":"k:1083","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet's man says he never knew Death before and now knows it in a woman's warring eyes; the vari's man says he did not know Death lived in the shore grove, and had he known he would not have come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both men learn Death in a woman's eyes, but the Kural's man ends in recognition while the song's ends in the wish never to have come, so the pair separates knowing love's danger from wanting to have avoided it.","gn":["the stanza's refrain gains a contrast that shows its regret as a choice: the couplet's speaker accepts what he now knows","the couplet's moment of discovery gains a sequel in which the knowledge comes too late to act on"]},{"a":"si:7:94-97","b":"k:1085","type":"develops","note":"The couplet asks whether the young woman's look is Death, an eye or a doe and finds all three in it; the vari asks whether her eyes are fierce Death and answers that Death has become an innocent girl.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the Kural holds Death and the doe's innocence side by side in one look, the song makes one the form of the other, so the innocent girl is Death in disguise, a step from mixture to transformation.","gn":["the stanza's turn gains a reference point: it resolves into one being what the couplet leaves as three natures","the couplet's list gains a storytelling form in which its opposites are joined by the god of death taking a girl's shape"]},{"a":"si:7:98-101","b":"tn:32","type":"develops","note":"The Thinaimalai man says the girl guarding drying fish turns weapon-eyes on him and it is he, not the fish, she guards; the vari says the goddess who afflicts those who see her driving birds from drying fish has taken a woman's form.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same fish-guarding girl is a wry reversal of guarding in the akam verse and a goddess in disguise in the epic song, so the pair shows the neithal scene moving from the lover's joke to a performance piece of supernatural praise.","gn":["the stanza gains its plain source scene, in which the girl's task and the man's capture are human and comic","the akam verse gains a later form in which its girl is lifted into an anangu, showing how the stock scene was elevated in courtly song"]},{"a":"si:7:114-117","b":"k:329","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet calls those whose work is killing base in the eyes of the discerning; the vari tells the fisher girl that her elders live by killing lives in the sea and she by killing lives in men's bodies.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The ethics book brands killing for a living as base, while the coastal song takes the fishers' killing as the plain inheritance of a family and makes it a compliment, so the pair shows a whole people's livelihood that the Kural's judgment would fall on and that the poetry of the shore never questions.","gn":["the stanza's easy wit about killing gains an ethical weight it does not register: by the Kural's standard the father's trade is base work","the couplet's judgment gains a concrete people it would condemn, the fishing families the epic sings with admiration"]},{"a":"si:7:122-125","b":"k:1086","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says her eyes would not make him tremble if her bent brows would straighten and screen them; the song says she takes lives with her bending brows, as her brothers take lives with boats.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In the Kural the curved brow is the eyes' failed guard, which could save him by straightening, while the song makes the curve itself the killer and the killing a family trade, so the pair shows the same conceit shifted from a hope of rescue to a charge of hereditary guilt.","gn":["the song's brow-as-weapon gains the couplet's sense that the brow's bend is a fault, கோடு, the word for a bent sceptre too","the couplet's private plea gains a social frame, a fisher girl whose people kill for a living, so the lover's charge of murder is cast as inherited rather than chosen"]},{"a":"si:7:138-141","b":"si:2:53-72","type":"same_image","note":"Kovalan tells Kannaki in their bridal chamber that the swan, beaten by her gentle gait, hid among the lotus pools; in the kaanal vari he tells the swan not to follow a fisher girl, whose walk it cannot match.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same compliment Kovalan gave his bride is given, with Madhavi listening, to an imagined shore girl, so the pair shows how readily his praise moves from one woman to another, the very thing Madhavi then reads as a hidden meaning.","gr":2},{"a":"si:7:142-153","b":"si:7:286-300","type":"develops","note":"Madhavi reads a hidden intent into Kovalan's songs and sings as if she had one too; at the end Kovalan says she sang with her mind on another, and leaves her, the narrator saying his former deed had come to fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set together, the two passages give the break two causes: a pair of mutual misreadings of songs that the poet marks as seeming, and the ஊழ்வினை the narrator names when Kovalan goes, so choice and karma are both offered for the same parting."},{"a":"si:7:154-161","b":"pn:35","type":"develops","note":"The Purananuru poem tells Killivalavan that the land of the just sceptre on the Kaveri gets rain when it asks, and that kings are blamed when rains fail; Madhavi's stanza reads the Kaveri's easy flow as proof that the Chola sceptre does not bend.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam counsel makes plenty a duty the king must earn and warns he will be blamed for drought, while the epic song takes the river's plenty as already proving his justice, so the pair shows the counsel hardened into a courtly praise that can no longer imagine the failure the older poem warns of.","gn":["Madhavi's figure gains its political doctrine, that a straight sceptre shows itself in the land's fertility, from an older address to a named Chola","the poem's conditional warning gains a later reception in which the same link between sceptre and Kaveri is sung as settled fact, the form the epic later breaks with the Pandya"]},{"a":"si:7:162-169","b":"k:546","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says it is not the spear but the unbending sceptre that gives a king victory; Madhavi's paired stanzas say the Kaveri's grace shows first her husband's unbending sceptre, then the strength of his spear.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The Kural ranks the sceptre above the spear and removes the spear as a cause, while Madhavi's song sets them side by side as equal signs of one prosperous river, so the pair shows the court song keeping the force the ethical couplet sets aside.","gn":["the stanza's turn from sceptre to spear gains a measure: the Kural shows the spear stanza is the one an ethicist would refuse","the couplet gains a contrary witness, a praise-song that will not drop the spear when it praises the just king"]},{"a":"si:7:170-177","b":"pp:9:1-28","type":"qualifies","note":"Pattinappaalai says the Kaveri does not fail even when the star goes south and the sky withholds rain; Madhavi's stanza says the Kaveri's unceasing help that sustains life is the grace of the ruler of the wheel.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The idyll makes the river's reliability its own, a guarantee standing even against a failed sky, while the epic song moves that same unfailing gift under a ruler's grace, so the pair shows the Kaveri's bounty passing from a fact of the land to a token of the king.","gn":["the stanza's ஒழியாய் gains its older ground, the Sangam guarantee that this river does not fail when the rains do","the idyll's praise of the river gains a later reading in which its constancy is no longer self-standing but credited to the sun-like Chola"]},{"a":"si:7:182-185","b":"si:7:45-52","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kovalan's sarthu vari the shore girl asks how simple folk could know that men who broke their oaths before the sea-god are without dharma; in Madhavi's, she asks how they could know that girls' slipping bangles proclaim the men who married them in secret.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both songs use the same refrain of feigned simplicity, but Kovalan's names the man's broken oath as the wrong while Madhavi's shows the cost landing on the woman's body, whose loosening bangles make the secret public, so the exchange moves blame from the oath to its visible price."},{"a":"si:7:190-193","b":"si:7:286-300","type":"develops","note":"Madhavi's stanza tells of a shore lord who looked at the crab with its mate and at her and then went away, leaving her senseless; at the kaathai's end Kovalan, hearing her songs as aimed at another, lets go of her and leaves.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The persona's lament for a lover who left after seeing a scene of union is followed, within the evening, by the singer's own lover leaving her, so the pair shows Madhavi's song turning from a role into her situation."},{"a":"si:7:194-197","b":"k:1207","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet's woman asks what would become of her if she forgot, since she knows no forgetting and even remembering burns; Madhavi's woman says to the adumbu and the swans, let him go, we will not forget those who forgot us.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the Kural not forgetting is a helpless bind, since memory both burns and keeps her alive, whereas in the song it is announced as a choice with an audience, a reproach to the forgetter, so the pair shows the same refusal to forget used as a lament and as a charge.","gn":["the stanza gains the couplet's inner cost, that the vow not to forget is also a burning she cannot escape","the couplet's private bind gains a public turn, the same memory declared to witnesses as an accusation against the one who forgot"]},{"a":"si:7:198-201","b":"k:1213","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet's woman lives because she sees in dreams the man who gives no grace awake; Madhavi's woman, whose eyes cannot sleep in the evening, asks the sleeping neythal whether it sees the cruel man coming in its dreams.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's comfort depends on sleep, and the song removes it: the sleepless woman is shut out of the dream that keeps the couplet's speaker alive and can only ask a flower for it, so the pair shows the dream-comfort as a privilege of those who can sleep.","gn":["the stanza's question gains its stake: in the Kural the dream is what keeps a deserted woman alive","the couplet gains a limit it does not state, that its consolation fails the woman who cannot sleep at all"]},{"a":"si:7:198-201","b":"ae:60","type":"tension","note":"The Ainthinai Ezhupathu heroine asks whether the sea, like her, does not sleep because the same shore lord took his fill of it and left; Madhavi's woman tells the neythal that, unlike her, it sleeps free of pain.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two neythal women look to the shore for company in sleeplessness and reach opposite answers, one finding in the restless sea a fellow sufferer, the other finding in the closed flower only a sleeper who does not share her pain, so the pair shows the same evening shore read as sympathy and as indifference.","gn":["the stanza's envy of the flower gains a contrast with the older convention in which the shore suffers with the woman","the Sangam poem's consoling fellow-sufferer gains a counter-case in which nature sleeps and the woman is alone"]},{"a":"si:7:202-205","b":"aa:42","type":"develops","note":"The Ainthinai Aimpathu heroine begs the crab not to walk over the tracks of her lover's chariot so she can look at them her fill; Madhavi's woman tells the tide that it has already broken up every trace of that path.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Sangam heroine still holds the wheel-track and pleads with a small creature to spare it, while in the song the sea has already wiped it out and she can only ask what to do, so the pair shows the same last trace of the lover first guarded and then lost.","gn":["the stanza gains the older poem's sense of what the track meant, all that is left to look at","the quatrain's plea gains its sequel, the loss it feared, done not by a crab but by a tide no one can beg"]},{"a":"si:7:286-300","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam states that a former deed comes in wrath and makes its doer eat its fruit; here the narrator uses the same words (ஊழ்வினை, உருத்து) for the moment Kovalan leaves Madhavi.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The first time the narrative names the patikam's third moral at work, the deed ripens not in a crime or a court but in a lover mishearing a song, so the epic's karma enters the story through a private misjudgement long before Madurai."},{"a":"si:7:286-300","b":"si:9:67-79","type":"tension","note":"Here the narrator gives Kovalan's break with Madhavi to his ripening deed; when he returns to Kannaki he gives his ruin to his own play with a deceitful woman (சலதி) and is ashamed.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The same break has two causes in two voices, karma in the narrator's and Madhavi's deceit in Kovalan's, so the epic lets its hero blame the woman while its own frame says the fault came from his past deed."},{"a":"si:7:286-300","b":"k:372","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says a fate of loss makes a man foolish; the epic shows Kovalan, his deed ripening, take a sung convention for a confession and leave.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic supplies the mechanism the couplet only asserts: ripening fate does its work by making a man misread what is in front of him, here a song in an assumed voice, so the folly is the means of the loss.","gn":["the epic's ஊழ்வினை gains a precise maxim for how it works on Kovalan, through a loss of judgement rather than an outside blow","the couplet's abstract 'fate that makes foolish' gains a concrete case of what the folly looks like and what it costs"]},{"a":"si:7:282-285","b":"si:7:45-52","type":"tension","note":"Kovalan sang the girls' charge that a man who swore by the sea god and broke his oath is without virtue; Madhavi's last song bows to the sea god and asks it to bear a false oath.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The two singers take opposite stances to the same broken oath before the same god, his to condemn and hers to plead for pardon, and the plea, left without a named oath-breaker, is what Kovalan hears as her guilt."},{"a":"si:7:282-285","b":"ai:53","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Ainkurunuru the friend says the girl's sickness is not the ford-spirit's doing but the man's oath; Madhavi's heroine asks the sea god not to punish the false oath sworn in the grove.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both rest on the belief that a water deity punishes a false love-oath, but where the Sangam friend turns that belief against the man, the epic's heroine asks the god to spare him, so the same belief serves blame in one and loyal forgiveness in the other.","gn":["the epic song gains the background belief that makes its plea meaningful: an oath sworn at the water draws the god's affliction","the Ainkurunuru poem's belief gains a second use, as something a wronged woman can ask the god to withhold"]},{"a":"si:7:238-241","b":"k:1222","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's woman asks the bewildered evening whether its mate is as hard as hers; Madhavi's heroine asks whether this bewildering evening (மருள்மாலை) exists in the land of the man who left.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both put to evening a question that is really about the absent man's hardness, and the epic sets that question in a performance before a lover who is present, showing how the convention can be heard as a personal charge.","gn":["the epic stanza gains a known form, the address to evening, which marks it as a conventional lament rather than Madhavi's confession","the couplet gains a dramatic setting in which its question is sung aloud and misheard, with consequences for the singer"]},{"a":"si:7:278-281","b":"k:1229","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says that when evening comes the whole town reels and suffers; Madhavi's heroine says the world shuts its eyes and, if evening and her husband are such, the world has become poor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both widen one woman's evening grief to the whole settlement, and the epic adds the lover himself to the cause, so the poverty of the world is laid as much on the husband's nature as on the hour.","gn":["the epic stanza gains a Kural precedent for making the world share the lament","the couplet's reeling town gains a named cause in the husband, which the couplet leaves out"]},{"a":"si:7:270-273","b":"k:1228","type":"develops","note":"The Kural makes the herdsman's flute evening's envoy and killing weapon; Madhavi's heroine says evening has struck the fishers' palai and bids it, if so skilled in melody, take her life.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In both the music of dusk is the instrument that kills the parted woman, but in the epic the song is sung on a yazh by a musician, so the deadly melody of evening is the singer's own art turned on herself.","gn":["the epic stanza gains a precedent for evening's music as a weapon, which explains why the singer offers her life to a melody","the couplet's image gains a second landscape, the fishers' shore in place of the herders' pasture, and a singer who is herself the musician"]},{"a":"si:7:250-253","b":"si:7:61-68","type":"same_image","note":"Kovalan sang of a conch that ploughed through a girl's sand-play and left her grieving; Madhavi sings of a man who came by the backwater, spoiled their sand-play and went.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Madhavi takes Kovalan's own image and puts a man in place of the conch, so the spoiled sand-play changes from a picture of a girl's charm into a charge of desertion, the answer-song turning his playful scene against the lover.","gr":2},{"a":"si:8:14-24","b":"si:7:286-300","type":"develops","note":"At the end of the Kaanal Vari the narrator says Kovalan let go of Madhavi because his ஊழ்வினை rose to bear fruit (line 289); here the same departure is recalled as Kovalan sulking and leaving without union (கோவலன் ஊடக் கூடாது ஏகிய).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One act is given two causes a kaathai apart, past karma ripening and a lover's pique, so the epic's fate does not replace Kovalan's own temper but works through it."},{"a":"si:8:56-73","b":"si:13:51-75","type":"same_image","note":"Both letters are sent through Vasantamalai with the same formula for Madhavi's state (பசந்த மேனியள் படர் ..., line 68 here; 13:67-68), and in both she writes with her own hand.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The repeated wording marks the second letter as a rerun of the first, so the reader can see that what changes between a refused and an accepted letter is Kovalan's state, not Madhavi's message."},{"a":"si:8:94-110","b":"si:13:93-114","type":"tension","note":"Here Kovalan refuses Madhavi's letter, filing her every sign of love as a dancing woman's role; reading her second letter in the forest he decides she is without fault and the fault is his (தன்றீ திலளென ... என்தீ தென்றே, 13:94-95).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kovalan's verdict that a dancer's grief is only art is withdrawn by Kovalan himself once he is ruined and far away, so the story shows the judgment came from his sulk and not from anything in her."},{"a":"si:8:94-110","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says sulking is like salt, and drawing it out a little too long is like a little too much; Kovalan's sulk, begun at the shore, is here drawn out into a refusal of her letter.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Past the couplet's 'little too much' the quarrel stops being a spoiled sweetness and becomes a verdict on the beloved's whole nature, which is how a lovers' sulk turns into the epic's break.","gn":["the refusal gains a measure by which it is an excess of an ordinary lovers' sulk rather than a reasoned judgment of Madhavi","the couplet gains a case showing what the excess it warns of looks like, a rereading of all past tenderness as pretence, with a cost it does not name"]},{"a":"si:8:94-110","b":"a:146","type":"qualifies","note":"In the akam poem the wife refuses the bard sent to plead for her husband because he is a deceiving philanderer (மாயப் பரத்தன்); here the man refuses the go-between sent by the courtesan because her love is a dancer's art.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic stages a marutham refusal of the go-between with the roles turned round, so that the charge of false love the convention lets the wife bring against the man is brought by the man against the courtesan.","gn":["the scene gains its genre: Kovalan's answer is a வாயில் மறுத்தல், and its harshness reads as a convention used against Madhavi","the poem gains a view from the other side of the same convention, where the one refused is the woman and the charge of deceit is shown, later in the story, to be wrong"]},{"a":"si:8:111-118","b":"si:9:55-66","type":"tension","note":"Madhavi lies down saying that though he will not come in the evening she will see him in the morning (காலைகாண் குவம்); in the next kaathai Kovalan comes instead to Kannaki's door (கடைத்தலையான் வந்துநம் கோவலன்).","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The morning Madhavi waits for is the morning Kovalan returns to his wife, so her hope and Kannaki's dream are the two ends of one night, and the courtesan's loss is the wife's recovery."},{"a":"si:8:111-118","b":"si:13:93-114","type":"same_image","note":"Madhavi lies with a helpless heart (கையறு நெஞ்சமொடு, line 116); her second letter ends asking Kovalan to take away the helpless heart (கையறு நெஞ்சம் கடியல் வேண்டும், 13:91).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The phrase carries one night's grief into the later letter, showing that what Madhavi asks of Kovalan there is to undo the state this refusal left her in."},{"a":"si:8:119-126","b":"k:1330","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says sulking is love's pleasure and its pleasure is to come together and embrace; the venba has the cuckoo proclaim love's command to all who have sulked (ஊடினீர்) to unite (கூடுமின்), and chides the one who spurned the letter.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's pleasure rests on a condition, that union follows, and the venba shows that condition failing even under love's own command, so the sulk the Kural treats as seasoning becomes the start of a separation.","gn":["the venba's command gains a stated rule of love, so Kovalan's refusal is a breach of love's own order and not only of Madhavi's hope","the couplet's closing condition பெறின், 'if one gets to', gains a case where one does not get to, and what follows then"]},{"a":"si:9:1-28","b":"k:265","type":"qualifies","note":"Saathan's rule that gods grant no boon without tapas uses the couplet's own word செய்தவம், and then he grants Maalathi a child she has not earned.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with the couplet, the tale shows that the rule linking tapas to its reward is the god's to state and also the god's to override, so grace in the epic stands beside the merit rule rather than inside it.","gn":["the general law behind Saathan's line 17, which shows his gift to Maalathi as an exception to it","a scene where the law is spoken by a god to a woman without tapas and then not applied"]},{"a":"si:9:1-28","b":"si:23:161-176","type":"qualifies","note":"Saathan says gods give no boon to those without tapas; the goddess of Madurai later says that when a former deed comes in wrath, tapas does not help those not upright.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two divine sayings set limits on tapas from both sides: without it no boon, and with it no shield once an old deed has ripened against one who was not upright."},{"a":"si:9:29-44","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Devanthi, whose husband is a god, keeps worship at his shrine to have him back; the couplet sets worship of a god against worship of a husband.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Devanthi's case dissolves the couplet's either-or, since her god is her husband, and so throws Kannaki's refusal of the shrine in the next unit into relief as a choice the couplet's terms describe exactly.","gn":["a measure for Devanthi's piety as the reverse of the couplet's wife, which prepares the contrast with Kannaki","a case the couplet did not foresee, a wife whose husband is himself the god she worships"]},{"a":"si:9:45-54","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"develops","note":"The dream's plea before the king (காவலன் முன்னர் யான் கட்டுரைத்தேன்) is fulfilled when Kannaki stands before the Pandya and argues her case.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set beside the plea, the dream shows that Kannaki's speech before the king was foreseen as her own act from the start, not a chance turn of grief, and that she already knew it would bring harm to the city."},{"a":"si:9:45-54","b":"si:23:177-200","type":"develops","note":"The 'good' that would be laughed at if told is the ending in which Kannaki, after fourteen days, rises to heaven with Kovalan.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing explains why Kannaki calls the dream's ending laughable: a reunion that comes only after death and in the form of gods sounds absurd as a blessing to a living wife."},{"a":"si:9:55-66","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Offered bathing and Kama's shrine as a way to keep her husband, Kannaki says it is not worthy; the couplet praises the wife who worships her husband rather than a god.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The scene shows the couplet's wife as one who refuses a rite offered for her own good, at the cost of the remedy, and that the refusal is framed as dignity (பீடு), not as doubt about the gods.","gn":["the rule that explains Kannaki's refusal without making her a sceptic about the tanks","a lived test in which the wife turns down divine help for her marriage at the moment she most needs it"]},{"a":"si:9:55-66","b":"si:23:161-176","type":"tension","note":"Devanthi blames a vow Kannaki broke to her husband in a former birth; the goddess of Madurai later traces the disaster to a curse on Kovalan's former self, who had Sangaman killed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem offers two former-birth causes for the same ruin and gives the authoritative one to a goddess, so the friend's guess that the fault was the wife's own is left standing as a mistaken reading.","gr":2},{"a":"si:9:55-66","b":"si:23:201-204","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki declines to worship at Kama's shrine for her husband; the Katturai venba says the gods themselves worship her who worships her husband and no god.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The early refusal and the later venba make one arc: the wife who would not seek her husband through a god ends as one the gods receive, so the poem pays out the refusal in the Kural's own terms.","gr":2},{"a":"si:9:67-79","b":"k:380","type":"develops","note":"Kovalan devises a remedy for his ruin, to trade with the anklets in Madurai, and in the same line the narrator says the deed was driving him; the couplet says fate gets there first even when one plans another way.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows fate overtaking a plan by using the plan itself: Kovalan's scheme to recover is the very road by which the deed reaches him, a case the couplet's bare 'it gets there first' does not picture.","gn":["a rule that explains why the narrator credits Kovalan's sensible plan to the deed (line 78)","a concrete mechanism: the counter-plan becomes fate's vehicle rather than being outrun by it"]},{"a":"si:9:67-79","b":"k:920","type":"qualifies","note":"Kovalan blames a deceitful woman for the poverty that shames him; the couplet ranks double-minded women with drink and dice as the company of those Fortune has left.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kovalan speaks the couplet's judgment of himself, but the closing venba and the narrator put his ruin down to the old deed, so the epic lets the Kural's verdict on courtesans stand as a ruined man's account rather than as the cause.","gn":["a standard of the age against which Kovalan's blame of Madhavi sounds conventional and self-excusing","a speaker who applies it to himself in the moment of loss, while the story he is in names another cause"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:9:80-83","b":"n:101","type":"develops","note":"The venba says the old deed drove Kovalan out of Puhar before dawn; the quatrain says an old deed finds its doer as a calf finds its mother among many cows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the quatrain the deed seeks the doer; in the venba the doer is moved toward the deed, so together they show karma working from both ends, the deed seeking and the doer being sent.","gn":["an image for why the venba gives the deed the active verb கடைக்கூட்ட","a narrative case where the search ends by bringing the doer to a distant city, not by the deed arriving at his door"]},{"a":"si:9:80-83","b":"si:23:161-176","type":"develops","note":"The venba's மூதை வினை is unnamed here; the goddess of Madurai later names it as the curse of Sangaman's widow on Kovalan's former self.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the venba sets the deed moving with no content and the later speech fills it in, so the departure from Puhar is the first step of a debt whose terms the reader learns only after the death."},{"a":"si:10:1-14","b":"si:0:27-36","type":"develops","note":"The ஊழ்வினை that drives the couple out of Puhar before dawn is the deed whose ripening time (வினைவிளை காலம்) the patikam gives as the reason the Pandya failed to inquire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic names the past deed both at the first step out of the house and at the hour of the killing, so the whole journey is framed as one deed's ripening, while the travellers themselves give wealth as their reason."},{"a":"si:10:1-14","b":"k:380","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says fate outruns any other plan; the narrator puts ஊழ்வினை behind a journey Kovalan plans, by his own word at line 51, to win back wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows the couplet's claim working from inside a person's own scheme: the plan to recover wealth is not overtaken by fate but is the road fate travels.","gn":["its passing word ஊழ்வினைக் கடைஇ gains the force of a stated rule that no counter-plan can outrun the deed","the couplet's abstract 'it gets there first' gains a case where the victim's own design is the vehicle"]},{"a":"si:10:15-31","b":"k:260","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says all living beings join hands to the one who does not kill and refuses flesh; the epic shows lay votaries worshipping the stone seat set for the flesh-renouncing Charanas even when none is there.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's hyperbole of universal worship becomes an institution in the epic: a built seat honoured in the ascetics' absence, which then becomes the place where a Charana actually appears at line 161.","gn":["the empty seat gains its reason: the refusal of flesh is what earns such worship","the couplet's worship gains a concrete form, a lay cult of the renouncers' seat"]},{"a":"si:10:84-97","b":"k:324","type":"develops","note":"The couplet defines the good path (நல்லாறு, நெறி) as the way that keeps not killing anything in view; Kavunthi's road counsel makes that literal, warning that a careless step on the shore may harm the crab and the snail.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the couplet's path metaphor turns into an actual walk where every footfall must be watched, and the nun counts harm to a snail as a grief equal to the dangers to the traveller herself.","gn":["the nun's warning gains its principle: the attention she asks for is the couplet's சூழும், a constant watch","the couplet's யாதொன்றும் gains a case showing it reaches even crabs and snails underfoot"]},{"a":"si:10:98-114","b":"pp:9:1-28","type":"same_image","note":"Both open on Venus straying south as the sign of drought and say the Kaveri still brings its flood.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic borrows the Pattinappalai's drought omen for the Kaveri on the road out of Puhar, so the couple's departure from the city is set in the same praise that once opened a poem about the city's wealth.","gn":["the epic's omen list gains its source and sense as a Chola praise-formula for the river","the Sangam formula gains a later use in a travel scene, where it marks the plenty the travellers are leaving behind"]},{"a":"si:10:98-114","b":"pn:117","type":"qualifies","note":"Both list Saturn smoking, a comet showing and Venus running south; Kapilar credits the rain that never failed in Pari's land to the king's straight sceptre, while the epic credits the Kaveri's flood to the river.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows the same omen formula assigning the land's safety to different powers: to a just king in Purananuru 117, and to the river itself in the epic.","gn":["the epic's omens gain their weight as signs that normally ruin a land unless something stronger holds","Kapilar's poem gains a counter-case where the formula is used without any king's virtue as the cause"]},{"a":"si:10:170-191","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's third moral, ஊழ்வினை உருத்துவந்து ஊட்டும், reappears with the same verb ஊட்டும் in the Charana's sermon on the strong deed that will not go.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poet's announced moral is put into the mouth of a Jain sage within the story, who adds what the patikam does not: that the Jina's word can end the births the deed feeds."},{"a":"si:10:170-191","b":"n:104","type":"develops","note":"The quatrain says not even ascetics (உறுவர்) can remove what must befall; the epic shows an ascetic who sees the couple's coming fate by his inner lamp and tells them the deed cannot be put off.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the quatrain's limit on ascetic power from the ascetic's side: the Charana knows, does not grieve, and states the rule himself instead of trying to avert the fate.","gn":["the Charana's silence about the couple's fate gains a reason: by the quatrain's rule his knowing could not change it","the quatrain's general uruvar gains a named scene where a seer meets those whose fate he foresees"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:10:170-191","b":"t:1649","type":"tension","note":"The Charana says the strong deed cannot be put off once it clings and that release from births comes through the Jina's spoken teaching; Tirumular says that by Siva's grace deeds do not cling at all.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two give incompatible accounts of whether a ripening deed can be stopped: for the Charana only the cycle of births can be ended, by teaching, while the deed must still be eaten; for Tirumular grace prevents the deed from clinging.","gn":["the Charana's sermon gains a clear rival: what it denies is precisely the grace that removes karma","Tirumular's verse gains a contemporary opponent for whom no god's grace stops a deed once sown"]},{"a":"si:10:192-213","b":"k:9","type":"develops","note":"The couplet calls a head that does not bow to the eight-qualitied one as useless as a sense organ without sense; Kavunthi vows each organ, ears, tongue, eyes, body, hands, head and mind, to the Arhat alone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's single comparison between the bowing head and a dead sense is worked out by the nun into a vow for every sense in turn, and the eight-qualitied one is named in the preceding sermon as the Jain Lord (எண்குணன், line 188).","gn":["the nun's vow gains its root: a sense not turned to the Lord is as if it did not work","the couplet's எண்குணத்தான் gains a reading in which a Jain voice claims the title for the Arhat"]},{"a":"si:10:229-248","b":"si:0:37-54","type":"same_image","note":"Both curses are told in the same formula, a curse given (இட்ட சாபம்) that took hold (கட்டியது); the former-birth curse on Kovalan has no term and ends in his never again being seen in human form, Kavunthi's has a term of twelve months and a return to former shape.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Just before Madurai the epic shows a curse that the wronged can soften and that ends in a restored body, so the reader meets the bounded curse first and the unbounded one, which no plea can term, at the story's end."},{"a":"si:10:229-248","b":"k:160","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet ranks those who fast below those who bear the harsh words of others; Kavunthi, whom the mockers address by her fasting body, curses them, and it is the insulted couple who ask that the words be taken as ignorance.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic splits the couplet's two figures between two people: the faster answers insult with a curse, and the forbearance the couplet ranks higher comes from the young couple who were insulted.","gn":["the couple's plea gains a ranking: by the couplet their forbearance stands above the nun's austerity","the couplet gains a scene showing that fasting and forbearance can come apart, with the insult aimed at the ascetic's wards and not at herself"]},{"a":"si:10:229-248","b":"k:264","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says that crushing foes and raising those one loves come by tapas if one so intends; Kavunthi's penance gives her curse its hold and her word of release its power.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic shows both halves of the couplet in one act of one ascetic: the same tapas that turns the mockers into jackals sets the day they become human again.","gn":["the phrase தவம்தரு சாபம் gains its doctrine: the curse is a use of the power tapas confers","the couplet's 'if one intends' (எண்ணின்) gains a case where the ascetic does intend, and is then moved to limit what she intended"]},{"a":"si:pukar_kandam_katturai:1-20","b":"pp:9:1-28","type":"qualifies","note":"Both praise the Chola land of Puhar through its water; the Pattinappalai grants that the sky may fail and makes the Kaveri the river that never fails, while the epic's summary sets the divine Kaveri beside a sky that itself never fails (பொய்யா வானம்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam poem rests Puhar's plenty on the river precisely because the sky can fail, and the epic's summary removes that concession, making the sky unfailing too in a line it reuses unchanged for the Pandya land, so the pair shows a Puhar-specific boast about the Kaveri turning into a stock praise that any crowned line receives.","gn":["the summary's unfailing sky gains a point of contrast: in the older praise of the same city it was the river, not the sky, that could be trusted","the Pattinappalai's claim about the Kaveri gains a later form in which the river's distinction has been levelled into a formula shared by the three kingdoms"]},{"a":"si:pukar_kandam_katturai:1-20","b":"si:madurai_kandam_katturai:1-20","type":"qualifies","note":"The two summaries share their opening lines word for word (aram, maram, the old city, festivals, the gods' visits, food, the unfailing sky), with the Vaigai for the Kaveri; the Madurai summary ends by viewing its book as one with the Pandya Nedunjeliyan who died on his throne with his chaste queen, the Puhar summary names no king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Because the praise of righteousness and unfailing rain is a fixed frame granted alike to the Chola line and to the Pandya whose verdict burned his city, the epic's judgment of a kingdom lies not in that praise but in what each summary sets at its centre: for Madurai a king who died of his own wrong, for Puhar no king at all but its arts."},{"a":"si:pukar_kandam_katturai:1-20","b":"k:545","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who rules with the sceptre as it should be; the summary lists the Chola line's righteousness beside the unfailing sky and abundant food, and the Madurai summary gives the same lines to the Pandya line.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set beside the couplet, the summaries' line of unfailing rain reads as the Kural's sign of just rule, yet the epic grants it equally to the line whose king it shows killing an innocent man, so the pair shows the epic keeping the sign as ceremony while placing the real test of the sceptre in a single verdict.","gn":["the formula's rain line gains the political meaning the couplet gives it, as a claim that the realm is justly ruled","the couplet gains a case where its sign of good rule is ascribed to a kingdom whose sceptre the story will show bent, which shows that the sign alone cannot vouch for the king"]},{"a":"si:pukar_kandam_katturai:21-24","b":"mm:25:175-199","type":"tension","note":"The venba wishes Puhar, made by fame for the earth whose moat is the sea, to live like the sun and the moon; the Manimekalai tells that when the king, searching for his lost son, left off the Indra festival, Manimekala Deivam bade the sea take the city.","grade":"forced","better":"develops","te":"The sea the venba names only as the earth's protecting moat is the agent that takes the city in the sequel, and the festival the summary counts among Puhar's glories is the rite whose neglect brings it, so the blessing of lasting life holds the terms of its own reversal."},{"a":"si:pukar_kandam_katturai:21-24","b":"si:1:1-12","type":"same_image","note":"The Puhar book opens hailing the moon, the sun and Puhar for resembling the Chola's umbrella, wheel and line, and closes wishing Puhar to live like the morning sun and the evening moon.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The book is framed by the same sun, moon and city, but at the opening the heavenly bodies are praised as likenesses of the king, and at the close they are the measure of the city's own life with the king gone from the comparison, matching a summary that names the Chola line but no Chola."},{"a":"si:11:15-30","b":"k:545","type":"qualifies","note":"The brahmin hails a Pandya who, when the thundering clouds withheld rain, bound them and made the harvest unfailing (மழைபிணித் தாண்ட மன்னவன்); the couplet says rain and harvest gather together in the land of the king who rules by the sceptre's nature.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet makes rain the natural consequence of just rule, while the epic's praise makes the king compel the clouds, so the same belief appears once as a law and once as a dynastic feat that this epic will test when the Pandya's sceptre bends.","gn":["the praise gains a stated rule by which the dynasty's rain-making can later be judged as a duty rather than a marvel","the couplet gains a royal legend showing its abstract link between sceptre and rain told of one named line of kings"]},{"a":"si:11:15-30","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"tension","note":"On the road the brahmin blesses the Pandya line as one of faultless fame (தீதுதீர் சிறப்பின் தென்னனை); in Madurai the reigning Pandya pronounces himself the thief (யானோ அரசன் யானே கள்வன்) and falls dead.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The journey to Madurai opens with a blessing of a flawless lineage, so the king's self-condemnation reads as a fall from inherited fame and not only as one wrong verdict."},{"a":"si:11:15-30","b":"pn:9","type":"develops","note":"Nettimaiyar wishes the Pandya years more than the sands of the Pahruli river of the ancient Pandya; the epic's blessing says the sea took the Pahruli river (பஃறுளி யாற்றுடன்) with the Kumari peak.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Sangam poet measures a king's life by a river that still flows, while the epic remembers the same river as drowned and makes its loss the occasion of a northern conquest, so the river turns from a figure of endurance into a figure of loss.","gn":["the epic's legend gains evidence that Pahruli was a living name in Pandya praise before it was told as lost","the Purananuru blessing gains a later memory in which the river it swore by has vanished, sharpening its wish for long life"]},{"a":"si:11:57-71","b":"k:559","type":"develops","note":"The brahmin likens the summer that turns mullai and kurinji into paalai to a wide land that has lost its royal order when its king bent with his officers (கொற்றவன் கோடி); the couplet says that if the king bends justice the sky will not pour rain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states that a bent king dries the sky, and the brahmin's simile reads a drought landscape as the look of a misruled kingdom, so the waste Kovalan crosses already pictures the bent sceptre waiting in Madurai.","gn":["the simile gains a doctrine that makes the drought the fruit of misrule rather than only a likeness of it","the couplet gains a picture of what its rainless land looks like: forest and hill twisted out of their nature into waste"]},{"a":"si:11:87-103","b":"si:11:150-165","type":"tension","note":"The brahmin promises that bathing in Bhavakaarini brings knowledge of one's former birth (பழம்பிறப் பெய்துவிர்); Kavunthi asks whether he does not see in this birth what was gained in past births (இறந்த பிறப்பின் எய்திய வெல்லாம் பிறந்த பிறப்பிற் காணா யோநீ).","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Jain ascetic answers the pilgrim's offer point for point, moving knowledge of past lives from a sacred pool to the reading of one's present life, so the chapter sets two routes to one end side by side without letting either deny the other."},{"a":"si:11:118-140","b":"s:1028","type":"qualifies","note":"The brahmin tells the traveller to recite the mantras of five and eight letters and bathe in the pool he wants, though better to fix the heart on the god's feet; the Siddhar asks those seeking a holy water where the bathing is, and says the holy water is the five letters within.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both move from outer water toward an inner formula, but the brahmin keeps the pool and makes the letters its key while the Siddhar dissolves the pool into the letters, so the pair shows two stages of one turn from pilgrimage to recitation.","gn":["the epic's rite gains a later voice that removes the pool it still keeps and shows where its own ranking of devotion over water could lead","the Siddhar's attack gains an early literary witness of the practice it rejects, recitation of a letter-formula as the key to a sacred pool"]},{"a":"si:11:150-165","b":"k:265","type":"qualifies","note":"Kavunthi asks whether anything rare is out of reach for those who never swerve from truth and protect living beings, against the brahmin's pool that grants every wish; the couplet says austerity is pursued because it gains whatever is wished as it is wished.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kavunthi uses the Kural's promise that discipline gains every wish against a pool that also grants every wish, and grounds that discipline in truth and non-harm, so the couplet's motive of attainment becomes an argument for ethics over pilgrimage.","gn":["Kavunthi's rhetorical question gains a stated rule that a disciplined life yields whatever is wished","the couplet gains a rival, the wish-granting pool, which shows what its claim for austerity competes with"]},{"a":"si:11:141-149","b":"si:11:192-202","type":"develops","note":"The brahmin warns of a goddess on the hard way who appears gently and holds travellers back; at the pool Kovalan recalls that the wondrous brahmin said a deluding goddess haunts the forest (மயக்குந் தெய்வமிவ் வன்காட் டுண்டென) and unmasks her with a mantra.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pilgrim's road knowledge, which Kavunthi had set aside as unneeded for the cave, is what saves Kovalan in the forest, so the chapter does not let either guide be simply right."},{"a":"si:11:166-191","b":"si:8:94-110","type":"tension","note":"In the Venil kaathai Vasantamalai reports that Kovalan refused Madhavi's letter and Madhavi lies sleepless saying she will see him at dawn; the goddess in Vasantamalai's form tells him Madhavi turned in anger, condemned her calling, tore her pearls and dismissed her maid.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The illusion is built on the one unfinished thread in Kovalan's past, the refused letter, so the forest goddess tempts him with his unresolved bond to Madhavi rather than with a stranger's charm."},{"a":"si:12:45-53","b":"si:20:34-54","type":"develops","note":"Saalini, possessed, calls Kannaki a goddess and the jewel of the world, and Kannaki hides behind her husband; at the Pandya's gate the gatekeeper can describe Kannaki only by saying she is not Kotravai on the bleeding pedestal, nor Kali who tore Daruka's chest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The divinity a hunter priestess proclaims in a desert shrine, and which Kannaki herself laughs off as confusion, becomes visible to a stranger at the palace door, so the pair shows the story turning Saalini's words into what others see, and the shy wife into a figure that can only be named by the war goddess she is denied to be."},{"a":"si:12:45-53","b":"si:0:1-11","type":"develops","note":"The patikam opens with the hill kuravar telling Ilango of the one-breasted pathini taken to heaven before their eyes; the vari has desert hunters' priestess first naming Kannaki divine.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both the frame and the first sign within the story put Kannaki's divinity in the mouths of forest and hill folk, before any king or sage acknowledges it, so the pairing shows the epic consistently letting the people of the wilds be the first witnesses of the pathini."},{"a":"si:12:45-53","b":"ai:245","type":"qualifies","note":"The Ainkurunuru friend asks whether it is a kindness if the old velan of an unerring tradition casts his beads and says the girl's sickness is Murugu; the epic's old wise woman of the clan's ancient duty, possessed, declares Kannaki divine and Kannaki calls it confusion.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the akam convention the possessed diviner of an old line is the one who gets it wrong, mistaking love for a god's seizure, while in the epic the possessed old woman's word is the true one and the hearer's doubt is the mistake, so the pairing shows the epic reversing the Sangam irony about divination to make it carry prophecy.","gn":["Kannaki's dismissal of the speech as பேதுறவு reads as a known stance, the akam scepticism toward the diviner, which the story will overturn","the friend's mockery of the unerring velan gains a later counter-case where the same kind of possessed elder speaks truly"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:12:54-74","b":"pp:1:250-265","type":"develops","note":"The Murukatruppadai hails Murugan among his titles as son of the banyan god, son of the mountain's daughter, and child of Kotravai of victorious war; the vari heaps on Kotravai herself the names of Gauri, Shiva's emblems and the title of Mal's younger kin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam guide poem joins Kotravai to the Saiva household through her son, listing her beside the mountain daughter as Murugan's mother, while the epic's hunters make Kotravai herself absorb Gauri, Shiva's emblems and Vishnu's kinship, so the pair shows the war goddess of the wilds drawn into the great gods' family by two different routes.","gn":["the epic's fusion of names appears as the later step of a joining already begun in the Sangam praise of Murugan as Kotravai's son","the bare title கொற்றவை சிறுவ gains the full portrait of the mother it names, with her mount, weapons and the cult that worships her"]},{"a":"si:12:54-74","b":"t:1046","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular lists the goddess's names (Tripurai, Sundari, Narani, Manonmani and others) and says by them the great one stands as many; the vari lists Amari, Kumari, Gauri, Samari, Shooli, Neeli and others for the hunters' goddess of the stag.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both pile names on one goddess, but the hunters' names come from her weapons, mounts, hides and battle with the asura and end in a rite where a girl wears them, while Tirumular's end in the doctrine that one power stands as many, so the pair shows the same device of the name-chain serving a public rite of costume and possession in one text and an inner theology of a single Shakti in the other.","gn":["the vari's chain of names is seen to make no doctrinal claim of oneness; it names a goddess to be worn and worshipped","Tirumular's doctrine that the one stands as many gains an older, folk chain of names (குமரி, கவுரி, நீலி) that it gathers into his single Shakti"]},{"a":"si:12:107-110","b":"t:555","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular makes knowledge of the First, the meaning of the Veda, together with Parashakti abiding in his half, the mark of the one in niyama; the vari makes the hunters' war goddess the woman's form in the half of the forehead-eyed god, praised by the Veda, while carrying Vishnu's conch and discus.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The half-woman form that Tirumular holds as an object of contemplative knowledge is, in the hunters' song, a goddess with Vishnu's weapons on a fighting mount, so the pair shows the same Saiva image serving yogic discipline in one text and a war cult that will not keep her to one god in the other.","gn":["the stanza's line on Shiva's half is shown to be the Saiva doctrinal image, and its addition of Vishnu's weapons stands out against it","the verse's பாதியுள் மன்னும் பரசக்தி gains an early witness outside the Saiva canon, worshipped by hunters with offerings rather than known in niyama"]},{"a":"si:12:87-90","b":"k:972","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says birth is alike for all beings and distinction comes from the difference in deeds; the vari credits a girl's penance with her glory and then declares her birth-clan, the bow-wielding hunters, the clan among clans.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The song agrees that distinction comes from a deed, the girl's penance, but hands that distinction straight back to the birth-group, the move the couplet's rule forbids, so the pair shows how a low-ranked clan claims rank through one daughter's merit rather than by denying that birth confers it.","gn":["the hunters' boast is shown to rest on a deed and not on birth alone, and to turn that deed into a clan's rank","the couplet's rule gains a case that tests it, where an individual deed's distinction is claimed for a whole clan of a lowly calling"]},{"a":"si:12:124-127","b":"pn:258","type":"develops","note":"Both tie the cattle raid to toddy: in the Purananuru the jar is kept for the raider who will bring a herd; here the toddy seller's refusal sends the hunter out.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show toddy as the raid's credit: the poem's kept jar is advance pay, and the epic's refusal is the unpaid debt the seized herd later settles in the seller's yard.","gn":["the epic's brief motive (a refused drink) gains the Sangam custom behind it, where the raider is paid in toddy before he goes","the Purananuru poem gains the other side of the trade: a seller who will not pour and is paid afterwards in cattle (lines 132-135)"]},{"a":"si:12:128-131","b":"pn:257","type":"develops","note":"The Purananuru raider takes herds yet his own pot shows no curd; the epic names whose yards the seized herds fill.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem's pitied raider who keeps nothing is explained by the epic's list: the herd goes to smith, drummer, bards, augur and toddy seller, so a raid's wealth is a gift economy that leaves the raider poor.","gn":["the list of yards gains its point: giving the herd away is the raider's honour, as the poem's empty curd-pot shows","the poem's riddle (why a cattle-taker has no curd) gains the epic's answer in named receivers"]},{"a":"si:12:141-144","b":"k:259","type":"tension","note":"The hunters pay for victory with blood at the goddess's feet; the Kural ranks not killing above a thousand oblations.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The conflict is about what an offering is worth: the hymn makes killing itself the price that buys the goddess's favour, while the couplet holds that no offering outweighs one life spared; it holds if the throat-blood of line 144 is a slain creature's.","gn":["the hymn's blood price gains a measure from outside the cult, one by which the payment is a loss","the couplet's comparison of oblation and abstention gains a live case where the offering is not ghee but blood"]},{"a":"si:12:149-152","b":"t:1824","type":"qualifies","note":"The hunters owe the goddess a bali as a debt; Tirumular, having nothing to show the god who eats oblations, offers a song as his food offering.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The vari is itself a song sung over the bali, so the hunters give both the blood and the song that Tirumular offers in its place, which shows the song offering was a companion of the blood rite before it was a substitute for it.","gn":["the hunters' debt-offering gains a contrast that shows its song as a second offering, not mere accompaniment","Tirumular's song-oblation gains a scene of the older rite in which song and blood were offered together"]},{"a":"si:12:157-160","b":"t:41","type":"develops","note":"Tirumular praises the lord who swallowed the poison and shares half his body with the lady; the hunters give the poison-swallowing to the goddess herself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hymn moves Siva's defining act of grace to his consort through the shared body Tirumular names, and makes it the ground on which a goddess who eats the pitiless hunters' offerings still grants grace.","gn":["the hymn's attribution gains its basis: the goddess is the half of the poison-eater's body","Tirumular's half-body formula gains a use outside Saiva devotion, where it lets a forest goddess claim Siva's deed"]},{"a":"si:12:161-164","b":"k:755","type":"tension","note":"The Kural says wealth that comes without grace and love should be let go; the hunters name themselves graceless takers of wealth by wounding and ask the gracious goddess to eat their due.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet tells the getter to let graceless wealth roll away, the hymn shows a community whose living is exactly such wealth offering a share of it to a goddess of grace, who is asked not to refuse it; the tension rests on reading the due as paid from the plunder.","gn":["the hunters' self-description gains the ethical name for what they live by: wealth that comes without arul","the couplet's rule gains a case where graceless wealth is not let go but sanctified by offering"]},{"a":"si:12:165-168","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"tension","note":"The hunters close their rite praying that the Pandya of Pothiyil wear the vetchi and ruin other lands; in Madurai the same king's sceptre fails and he falls dead before Kannaki.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The blessing asks for victory over others' lands, but the king's ruin comes from within his own court; the forest rite that hailed Kannaki as the goddess has, unknowing, blessed the king she will undo."},{"a":"si:13:1-10","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"tension","note":"On entering the Pandya land the travellers rely on the fame that the Pandya's straight sceptre keeps even beasts and thunder from harming those who depend on it; in the court the same Pandya, having killed Kovalan, finds himself the thief and his sceptre bent.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the epic letting the travellers stake their lives on a king's reputation for protection and then making that king's failure fall on exactly the stranger who trusted it, so the praise of the sceptre at the border is the premise his own verdict on himself overturns."},{"a":"si:13:1-10","b":"k:545","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who rules by right; the epic's fame says that in the just Pandya's land even beasts, serpents and thunder spare those who depend on it.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Held together, the two show the claim that nature answers a just sceptre turning, in the epic, into a traveller's reason to trust a stranger king, a reputation the story then puts to the test with the travellers' own lives.","gn":["the border praise gains its doctrinal ground: the fame rests on the Kural's teaching that a just reign orders nature itself","the couplet gains a case where its promise is taken as a guarantee by outsiders and so becomes something a king can betray"]},{"a":"si:13:11-28","b":"k:557","type":"same_image","note":"The couplet likens a king's lack of kindness to the world's lack of rain; the epic likens travellers waiting out the scorching sun to subjects waiting for a cruel king to set.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural pictures the bad king through the weather and the epic pictures the weather through the bad king, so the pair shows the image of misrule running both ways, and the epic placing it, unprompted, at the first step into the Pandya's land.","gn":["the simile gains the couplet's moral weight: the cruel king is a drought to all that lives, not just a passing discomfort","the couplet gains a hope its subjects do not voice: that a cruel reign, like a summer day, has an end to wait for"]},{"a":"si:13:93-114","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"tension","note":"On the road Kovalan, reading Madhavi's letter, says the fault was his own; before the king, Kannaki says ஊழ்வினை drove him to Madurai.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The pair shows the epic giving Kovalan's ruin two causes in two voices: his own confession of fault, made privately to clear the courtesan, and his wife's public appeal to former deeds, made to indict the king, so neither account stands alone as the poem's verdict."},{"a":"si:13:93-114","b":"k:911","type":"tension","note":"The couplet says the sweet words of women who seek a man's wealth and not his love bring him harm; here a courtesan writes to a man who has lost his wealth, asking only what her fault was, and he judges her blameless and himself at fault.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The pair sets the Kural's rule that a courtesan's sweet words are for wealth against a courtesan's letter written after the wealth is gone, so the epic tests the rule on the one case it cannot explain and has the man himself withdraw the charge.","gn":["Kovalan's verdict that Madhavi is faultless gains force from the rule it overturns: he clears her against the expected judgment of her class","the couplet gains its limit case, a courtesan whose plea comes when the man has nothing left to give"],"gr":3},{"a":"si:13:135-150","b":"pp:6:654-667","type":"develops","note":"Maduraikkanchi's dawn in Madurai has the Brahmins chanting the Veda, the elephants fed and the horses in their stalls (பணைநிலைப் புரவி); the epic's travellers hear the same dawn, the Vedic chant and the stalled horses' neighing among it, from outside the walls.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic reuses the Sangam catalogue of Madurai's dawn, phrase for phrase in places, but moves its hearer from the king the idyll counsels on impermanence to the strangers whom the city will destroy, so the idyll's warning that such splendour passes is carried out on the city itself.","gn":["the catalogue gains its pedigree: Madurai's dawn is a set piece of the Sangam city, heard in the epic as the city's settled glory","the idyll's counsel that wealth and splendour do not last gains a story in which this very Madurai, with its stalled horses and chanting Brahmins, is burned"]},{"a":"si:13:151-175","b":"pr:thirattu:10","type":"tension","note":"The Paripadal fragment asks whether Madurai's fame can ever prove false as long as the Vaigai flows; the epic calls the Vaigai the never-failing (பொய்யா) creeper of the clan and has her hide her tears for the wife the city will wrong.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Paripadal makes the flowing Vaigai the pledge that Madurai's fame cannot fail; the epic keeps the river unfailing but makes her the first to know that the city's justice will fail, so the pledge passes from the city to the river alone.","gn":["the epic's 'never-failing' river gains the old pledge it echoes, which shows what the river's hidden grief is a grief for","the fragment's confidence gains a story that separates the river's constancy from the city's fame"]},{"a":"si:13:151-175","b":"si:20:100-103","type":"develops","note":"The Vaigai hides tears as if knowing the young wife's fate; in the court the king, called the lord of the Vaigai, loses his life at the hearing of her words.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the river grieving at the entry for the wife and the river's lord dying at her word in the court, so the Vaigai frames Madurai's story from the woman's arrival to the king's death, siding with her in silence before he hears her."},{"a":"si:13:176-196","b":"si:20:1-7","type":"develops","note":"At the moat the flowers weep and the flags wave as if saying 'do not come'; in the palace the queen dreams of the umbrella and sceptre falling and the gate bell shaking.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The pair shows the epic sending warnings to both sides of the coming wrong, to the strangers at the walls and to the queen in the palace, and having neither heeded, so the catastrophe is foreseen in nature and in sleep yet still unfolds."},{"a":"si:14:25-49","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam announces as the epic's third moral that the deed of former birth comes in wrath and feeds its fruit (ஊழ்வினை உருத்துவந்து ஊட்டும்); Kavunthi says the hard deed feeds its fruit and that the unlearned are bewildered when the evil deed comes in wrath (உருத்த காலை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The moral Ilango states from outside the story is first spoken inside it by a nun who uses it to console Kovalan for a hardship already past, so the reader hears the thesis as comfort just before it becomes the account of his death."},{"a":"si:14:50-61","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"tension","note":"Kavunthi assures Kovalan that, unlike the dice-ruined king who left his wife in the forest by strong karma, he has a life never parted from his wife; in the Pandya's court Kannaki says her husband came to Madurai driven by ஊழ்வினை and was killed.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The nun's karma, invoked to show that Kovalan's lot is better than the exiled king's, is the same force Kannaki names when she stands parted from him for ever, so the consolation becomes the epic's irony: the karma Kavunthi uses to excuse a parting is already moving toward a worse one."},{"a":"si:14:25-49","b":"k:379","type":"develops","note":"The couplet asks why those who take good fortune as good grieve when ill fortune comes; Kavunthi says the learned do not despair when they eat the fruit of their deeds, while the unlearned are bewildered when the evil deed ripens.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's argument for calm rests on consistency between good and bad fortune, while the nun rests it on learning and adds a cause, desire for women and food, so the pair shows the Kural's equanimity before fate being turned into a renouncer's diagnosis of where suffering comes from.","gn":["Kavunthi's counsel gains a compact general rule behind it, so her talk of the learned not despairing reads as a recognised teaching rather than private comfort","the couplet gains a hearer and an occasion, a ruined husband in a strange city, and a second argument for calm that it does not make, that the suffering was earned by desire"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:14:25-49","b":"n:56","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain says that because a wife cannot be put away even if she lacks virtue and bears no child, the learned called marriage கடி; Kavunthi says the pains of parting and union belong only to those joined with women, not to the strong who live alone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both make marriage a source of suffering from which the solitary are free, but the Naladiyar argues it to recommend renunciation while Kavunthi states it to a married man and then sends him on with his wife, so the pair shows the renouncer's diagnosis offered without its prescription.","gn":["the nun's claim gains the ethical tradition's own argument for why the bond brings pain, that it cannot be undone","the quatrain gains a case where its view is spoken to a husband who is not told to leave his wife, which limits how far the ascetic diagnosis is meant to bind a householder"]},{"a":"si:14:146-167","b":"si:3:154-175","type":"same_image","note":"In Puhar Madhavi received by the king's rule the thousand and eight kalanju (ஆயிரத்து எண்கழஞ்சு ... எட்டுக்கடை நிறுத்த) with the talaikkol, and Kovalan bought her garland; in Madurai the talaikkol dancers receive the same sum (எட்டுக் கடைநிறுத்த ஆயிரத் தெண்கழஞ்சு) every day by fixed rule, and both passages praise the house as வடுநீங்கு சிறப்பின்.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The price that marked Madhavi's debut as a singular royal honour, and began Kovalan's ruin, turns out in Madurai to be the standing daily fee of a whole profession, and Kovalan walks past it as a man who has already paid it."},{"a":"si:14:146-167","b":"k:915","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says those whose knowledge is refined by good sense do not sink into the common charms of women who are everyone's; the epic says that in the net of the dancers' glances even ascetics' hard-won wisdom turns to something else.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The Kural makes refined knowledge a protection against the courtesan, and the epic's street denies the guarantee by naming ascetics among those caught, so the pair shows the rule holding only for the wise who stay away, not for any wisdom that walks into the street.","gn":["the epic's hyperbole gains its edge from the rule it breaks: the ascetic's fall matters because wisdom was supposed to protect","the couplet gains a limit: its protection is a claim about who abstains, and the epic shows a learned art that even the disciplined are said to fall before"]},{"a":"si:14:146-167","b":"pp:6:562-589","type":"qualifies","note":"The Maduraikkanchi's courtesans of Madurai drain the wealth of rich young men and leave them like bees leaving a spent flower; the epic's courtesans of the same city are masters of the two styles of dance and its treatises, paid a fixed fee by rule, before whom kings bow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam poem sees Madurai's courtesans as the drain on the young rich, while the epic sees them as an institution of learned art with a royal tariff, so the pair shows the epic recasting a city's vice as its high culture, the very shift that made Madhavi's art and Kovalan's ruin the same thing.","gn":["the epic's dancers' quarter gains its older picture, in which the same women of the same city appear as takers of wealth, which the epic's praise silently suppresses","the Sangam poem's courtesans gain an account of their art and its rules, which the poem, seeing them only from the side of the spent lover, does not give"]},{"a":"si:14:180-200","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"tension","note":"Madurai's gem street is full of men who tell gems apart by the flaws the treatises name; before the king Kannaki says her anklet holds gems, the queen's holds pearls, and when she breaks it a gem flies to the king's mouth and he declares himself the thief.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The city Kovalan admires is one where every stone is judged by rule, yet the king condemns him on a goldsmith's word without testing the anklet, and the truth comes only when Kannaki breaks it, so the pairing shows the failure was not of the city's knowledge but of the king's will to use it."},{"a":"si:14:201-218","b":"pp:6:507-526","type":"develops","note":"The Maduraikkanchi lists in Madurai's four streets (நால்வேறு தெருவினும்) the shell-cutters, gem-borers, goldsmiths, gold-assayers and cloth-sellers; the epic closes its tour with the gold-assayers, cloth street, grain street and the four streets (நால்வேறு தெருவும்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic follows the Sangam catalogue of Madurai's trades down to the four streets, but where the Sangam poem lists them as the plenty of a victorious Pandya, the epic shows them through a stranger about to be condemned by one of these trades, so the inherited praise of the city becomes the setting of its fall.","gn":["the epic's tour gains a source and a measure: it is the classic praise of Madurai, which makes its turn toward ruin a turn against a known tradition","the Sangam catalogue gains a later reading in which the gold-assayers it lists among the city's glories include the trade that will bring about the city's burning"]},{"a":"si:15:1-20","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"tension","note":"Kovalan reports a Madurai free of evil whose kings' sceptre is straight (கோலின் செம்மை, தீதுதீர் மதுரை); the Pandya, after killing him, declares himself the thief and his guarding failed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The straightness of the Pandya sceptre is first vouched for by the man it will kill, on the day before his death, so the epic lets the victim's trust in the city's justice set the measure by which the king condemns himself.","gr":2},{"a":"si:15:76-94","b":"si:0:37-54","type":"develops","note":"Maadalan, finding no cause for Kovalan's suffering in his good deeds of this life, asks whether it is the fruit of a former birth; the patikam's goddess names that cause, a curse from a former birth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic reaches its doctrine of former deeds by elimination: a witness who knows only Kovalan's good acts can explain his ruin only by a past birth, and the patikam later confirms the guess with a named curse, so the karmic cause is offered as what remains when the visible life acquits the sufferer."},{"a":"si:15:76-94","b":"si:5:128-134","type":"develops","note":"The crossroads spirit of Puhar announces that it seizes false witnesses and others with its noose and devours them; here it seizes one and refuses Kovalan's offer of his own life in exchange.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The spirit's announced punishment proves rigid in practice: it will not take a good man's life even when he offers it, so the pair shows its justice as exact matching of deed to doer, with no place for the substitution that Kovalan's generosity proposes."},{"a":"si:15:95-106","b":"si:20:1-7","type":"same_image","note":"Kovalan dreams of his wife's distress, his seized garment and a death-ride, then release and his daughter's renunciation; the Pandya queen dreams of the umbrella and sceptre falling and the sky in disorder.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same ruin is dreamed from both sides of the verdict: the victim's dream runs past death into release and his daughter's turn to the Buddha, the queen's stops at the fall of the sceptre, so the dreams divide between them the epic's two morals of ripening deeds and failed kingship."},{"a":"si:15:131-148","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says a wife who worships her husband rather than a god can bid the rain fall; Kavunthi calls Kannaki a deity fairer than any she has seen and quotes a saying that where chaste wives live the sky never fails and kings' victory never decays.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet keeps the wife below the gods yet gives her command of the rain, Kavunthi makes the chaste wife herself the deity and extends her power from rain to the king's victory, which is the very extension the epic will turn against the Pandya when that power destroys his city.","gn":["Kavunthi's saying gains a source for its rain clause in a couplet that ties the wife's power to her refusal of other gods","the couplet gains a reading in which the chaste wife's power reaches past nature to the fortunes of kings, and so becomes a threat as well as a blessing"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:15:131-148","b":"si:0:27-36","type":"tension","note":"Kavunthi quotes the saying that where chaste wives live the kings' victory does not decay; the patikam says that because Kannaki was a pathini she burned Madurai and the Pandya fell.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The saying Kavunthi offers as a pledge of the king's safety is reversed in the same city, where a pathini's presence ends the king, so the pair shows that the epic makes the chaste wife's power conditional on the king's justice rather than a blessing that protects any throne."},{"a":"si:15:174-191","b":"k:222","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet holds that giving is good even if there were no higher world; the charana proves the worth of a gift by showing the god it produced, with the monkey's hand still on him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural deliberately cuts giving loose from any reward in a higher world, while the Jain tale makes a visible heavenly body the evidence for the gift's worth, so the pair separates two defences of giving, one that needs no world beyond and one that rests on it.","gn":["the tale's proof by reward is set against a view that does not need the proof, which shows how much its case rests on the heaven it displays","the couplet's hypothetical 'even if there were no higher world' gains a concrete rival: a contemporary teaching that answers the hypothetical by producing the higher world as witness"]},{"a":"si:15:174-191","b":"t:1647","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular says merit and sin both come with one into birth and both must be cut at the root to reach the Lord; the Jain tale shows merit carrying a monkey to a human birth and then to a god's body that keeps the monkey's hand.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The god who still bears a monkey's hand makes visible Tirumular's point that merit, too, works by giving births: the reward of the gift is another body marked by the last one, which is why the Saiva verse ranks merit as a bond to be uprooted rather than a road to the Lord.","gn":["the tale's god with the monkey's hand gains a reading as a sign that merit's fruit is still a birth, not only a proof of the gift's power","Tirumular's abstract claim that merit binds gains a concrete image of a merit-made body that carries the history of its former birth"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:15:174-191","b":"mm:27:183-201","type":"qualifies","note":"The Jain debater in the Manimekalai says release is to block the inflow of both good and bad deeds and wear out past ones; the Jain charana of the Silappathikaram tells a gift whose merit leads to a god's birth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the Jain doctrine stated in debate and the Jain tale told for lay hearers show the two levels of the same teaching: the doctrine counts good deeds among the bonds, while the tale for householders displays the heavenly birth that good deeds bring, and Kavunthi reserves release itself for those who turn to dharma (line 197)."},{"a":"si:15:200-219","b":"k:747","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet calls a fort that which cannot be taken by siege, storm or work on those inside; Madurai's gate is lined with every engine against attack as Kannaki passes through it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic fills Madurai's walls with the defences the Kural's fort requires at the very moment it lets in the woman whose wrong will burn the city from within, so the pair shows a danger the couplet's three threats do not name: an injustice of the king's own that no engine on the wall can repel.","gn":["the list of engines gains a measure: by the Kural's standard the city is a perfect fort, which sharpens the irony of its fall","the couplet's account of impregnability gains a case where the fort is lost through no siege at all, but through the king's own wrong"]},{"a":"si:16:1-16","b":"si:15:131-148","type":"develops","note":"Kavunthi gave Kannaki as a ward to the herdswoman and charged her to bathe, dress and mother her; Maadari here receives her as a ward, has her bathed and settles her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ascetic's charge is carried out point by point, so the refuge fails not through any lapse of the guardian but on the very day she is kept as promised, and the harm comes from the city's justice, not the host's house."},{"a":"si:16:33-47","b":"si:16:194-217","type":"same_image","note":"Kannaki sprinkles water on the ground as if to clear the earth-woman's distress (மண்ணக மடந்தை, line 40); at the killing the earth-woman's grief grows as Kovalan's blood pours over her (line 215).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The earth that Kannaki soothes with water at his last meal is soaked with his blood the same day, so the poet makes one figure measure the distance between her care and his death."},{"a":"si:16:48-61","b":"si:17:17-26","type":"develops","note":"The herders liken Kovalan and Kannaki to the dark god and the lamp of their clan who relieved his distress; in the next kaathai Maadari dances the kuravai of Krishna and Pinnai so that Kannaki may see it, to ward off a coming harm.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The herders' wonder at their guests becomes a rite: having seen the couple as Krishna and his consort, they dance that pair's kuravai to avert the harm their omens foretell, which has already fallen."},{"a":"si:16:48-61","b":"si:16:194-217","type":"develops","note":"Kovalan asks whether his ruin is illusion or a strong former deed (வல்வினை கொல்லோ); at his death the narrator answers that his former deed came in wrath (பண்டை ஊழ்வினை உருத்து).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The question the victim cannot answer is answered by the narrator only over his body, so the karma he half suspects is confirmed only when it can no longer be acted on, and he had meanwhile blamed himself."},{"a":"si:16:62-73","b":"k:535","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says one who slipped by not guarding beforehand will grieve later over his own fault (தன்பிழை); Kovalan, now in Madurai, grieves that he grew heedless (பொச்சாப் புண்டு) and failed his parents and his wife.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes late regret the heedless man's whole penalty and puts the fault in him alone, while the epic has the heedless man grieve and still lose his life, and lets him ask in the same breath whether karma rather than his own lapse has brought him here.","gn":["Kovalan's confession gains a name and a rule: his is the heedlessness whose fruit is regret that comes too late","the couplet gains a case where regret is not the end of the penalty, and where the one who grieves over his own fault also suspects a former deed"]},{"a":"si:16:62-73","b":"si:9:67-79","type":"develops","note":"In Puhar Kovalan blamed his ruin on sporting with a deceitful woman and the shame of lost wealth; in Madurai he blames his own heedlessness, bad company and disobedience to his parents.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Between the two confessions the blame moves from Madhavi to himself, so the road to Madurai is shown changing his account of his fall even as it carries him to his death."},{"a":"si:16:62-73","b":"k:42","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet makes the householder the support of renouncers, the needy and the dead; Kannaki says that without her husband she lost giving to the virtuous, tending Brahmins, receiving ascetics and welcoming guests.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet speaks of the householder as one person, while Kannaki shows that a wife alone could not keep those duties, so the householder's dharma the Kural assigns depends on the pair being together.","gn":["Kannaki's list gains its standing: what she lost is the very support the Kural makes the householder's office","the couplet gains its cost when the house is broken: the wife left at home cannot be the support it describes"]},{"a":"si:16:74-90","b":"k:56","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet defines the woman who guards herself, tends her husband, keeps their good name and does not flag; Kannaki hid her grief before his parents and followed him without gainsaying him though his conduct was unworthy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet does not say what such a wife owes a husband who wrongs her; Kannaki's answer is to name his fault and follow him all the same, so the epic shows the couplet's tireless wife carried into the ruin her husband makes.","gn":["Kannaki's hidden grief and silence before her in-laws gain a name as keeping the household's good name, part of what defines the wife in the couplet","the couplet gains the hard case it leaves open, a husband who has failed her, and one answer to it"]},{"a":"si:16:91-112","b":"si:9:67-79","type":"develops","note":"In Puhar, Kannaki offered her anklets and Kovalan resolved to recover his lost wealth with them as capital in Madurai; here he takes one anklet to exchange.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The plan made in the bedchamber as a fresh start is carried out here and leads straight to the goldsmith, so the means of recovery chosen in Puhar becomes the instrument of his death."},{"a":"si:16:91-112","b":"si:17:11-12","type":"same_image","note":"Kovalan meets a humped bull in the street and does not know it as an ill omen; in the next kaathai the herdswomen see the tears of their humped bull and say a harm is coming.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same beast gives the same warning to two kinds of people, and only the herders, whose knowledge it is, read it, so the epic makes reading the omen a matter of birth and has the warning understood only after the harm is done."},{"a":"si:16:113-129","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"tension","note":"The goldsmith declares that the anklet fits none but the queen; in the court Kannaki's anklet, broken, shows gems where the queen's holds pearls.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The craftsman's expert verdict, the only evidence against Kovalan, is shown false by the anklet itself, so the case is undone by the very object it was built on."},{"a":"si:16:148-163","b":"n:108","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain says that when people who know what should be known still do blameworthy things, it comes from their own former deeds; the epic says the king inquired into nothing because it was the time for a deed to ripen.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain traces the knowing man's wrong to his own deeds, while the epic leaves open whose deed ripens, the king's or Kovalan's caught in its net, so one man's karma may be what makes another man err.","gn":["the king's failure gains a received explanation: knowing men do wrong when former deeds press them, and blame still attaches","the quatrain gains a case where the deed that ripens may not be the wrongdoer's own, which its wording does not consider"]},{"a":"si:16:164-177","b":"k:287","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet calls stealth a dark knowledge (காரறிவாண்மை) absent in those who love measure; the goldsmith, the real thief, lectures the guards on the eight arts of thieves as an unmatched skill.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural grants that stealth is a kind of knowledge, and the epic shows that knowledge turned from taking goods to taking a life, as the thief's expertise in theft persuades the guards to kill an innocent man.","gn":["the goldsmith's lecture gains a name for what it displays: cleverness that is still darkness","the couplet's dark knowledge gains a use it does not mention, deceiving those who keep the law"]},{"a":"si:16:194-217","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam sets as the epic's third moral that a former deed comes in wrath and makes its doer eat (ஊழ்வினை உருத்துவந்து ஊட்டும்); Kovalan's death is told in the same words (பண்டை ஊழ்வினை உருத்து).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The moral stated as a thesis at the outset is spoken again in its own words at the death, and in the same line as the king's bent sceptre, so the epic gives its first and third morals as two causes of one event."},{"a":"si:16:194-217","b":"n:101","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain says an old deed seeks out its doer as a calf finds its mother among many cows; Kovalan's former deed reaches him through a goldsmith's lie, a king's hasty word and a drunken guard's sword.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's deed finds its doer by itself, while the epic shows it arriving through other people's free and blameworthy acts, so karma here does not excuse the hands it works through.","gn":["Kovalan's death gains the quatrain's image of the deed that cannot miss its doer, which explains why every escape fails","the quatrain's calf gains a path: the seeking deed travels through other people's lies and violence"]},{"a":"si:16:218-221","b":"t:1527","type":"tension","note":"The venba says both deeds will reach their doer and bids men turn to good dharma; Tirumular says that when the two deeds stand balanced, grace comes as the guru and frees the soul from them.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The venba's remedy for the two deeds is to do more good, which on its wording still returns to the doer, while Tirumular's is grace that comes when good and evil are balanced and ends them, so the pair sets merit-making against release by grace; the conflict holds if நல்லறம் is read as producing good fruit.","gn":["the venba's moral gains a contrast that shows it offers no escape from deeds, only better ones","Tirumular's balancing of the two deeds gains a plain statement of the view it moves beyond, that good deeds are the answer to karma"]},{"a":"si:17:1-10","b":"kl:104","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kalittokai's herders open their bull-catch by praising the Pandya who removed the tiger and the bow and set his fish in their place; the epic's herders open their kuravai on the Pandya whose fish is inscribed on the Himalaya beside the tiger and the bow.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same herders' praise of the Pandya emblem is kept in the epic but with the boast of conquest removed, so a work that follows all three kingdoms keeps the mullai custom of beginning with the king while setting the three emblems side by side.","gn":["the opening's praise of the king gains a precedent: Sangam herders already began the bull-catch songs with the Pandya and his fish","the poem's claim that the fish displaced the tiger and the bow gains a later telling that does not keep it, so the claim reads as praise rather than settled history"]},{"a":"si:17:27-28","b":"kl:104","type":"develops","note":"Both pledge a herd girl to the man who, unafraid of the black bull's fury, takes it (காரி கதன் அஞ்சான்); in the Kalittokai her kin give her to the catcher, in the epic no bull is penned.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the bull-catch pledge word for word but drops the contest, so a herders' marriage custom survives as the names of dance roles in a rite against ill omen.","gn":["the pledge gains its origin in a real contest in which a girl's kin give her to the man who takes the bull","the custom gains a later life, recited over girls as a ritual formula with no bull and no catcher"]},{"a":"si:17:17-26","b":"pp:5:1-23","type":"qualifies","note":"In the idyll the old women seek an omen and hear a herd girl tell a distressed tethered calf that its mothers will soon come; in the epic the herd's own signs foretell sorrow and the herdswoman dances so that the cows' and calves' distress may pass.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both read a calf's distress among the herds as a sign about an absent man, but the idyll's chance word promises a return and the epic's signs announce a death the herders do not yet know, so the mullai omen-scene is turned from hope to loss.","gn":["the rite gains its convention: in mullai poetry an omen among the herds speaks of an absent husband, which lets the signs point at Kovalan without naming him","the idyll's comforting omen gains a counterpart in which the same kind of sign foretells a husband's death and a rite fails to turn it"]},{"a":"si:17:11-12","b":"si:16:194-217","type":"develops","note":"The first signs, milk that will not set and a weeping bull, come on the morning after Kovalan has been cut down at the king's word.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read after the killing, the omens are no forecast but the herd registering a death its keepers have not heard of, so the reader holds the meaning the characters lack."},{"a":"si:17:15-16","b":"si:20:1-7","type":"same_image","note":"The herders' omens fall in the cowshed (the cattle's bells drop, the cows tremble); the queen's fall in the court (the falling umbrella and sceptre, the trembling gate bell).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same wrong is signalled first in the house of the victim's hosts and later in the king's own house, each through the things of its own life, so the omens move from the cowshed to the throne as the consequence nears the king."},{"a":"si:17:17-26","b":"si:18:1-15","type":"tension","note":"The kuravai is danced so that the coming distress may pass; at the dance's end an uproar from the town brings the news of Kovalan's death.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The rite meant to turn the sorrow away becomes the frame in which the sorrow arrives, so the herders' remedy is shown powerless against a deed already done."},{"a":"si:17:59-64","b":"si:16:48-61","type":"develops","note":"Aiyai and her mother likened Kannaki to the one of their clan who relieved the gem-coloured god's distress in the Yamuna; here the girl cast as Pinnai garlands the girl cast as Mayavan while Kannaki looks on.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Because the herdswomen had already seen Kannaki as Pinnai and Kovalan as the dark god, the dance's Pinnai garlanding Mayavan plays out before Kannaki the union that has, unknown to her, already ended."},{"a":"si:17:148-159","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"tension","note":"The herdswomen pray that the Pandya's drum bring victory day after day; the same day that king's verdict has killed Kovalan, and the next morning he names himself the thief and dies.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The closing blessing is the stock wish of a herders' song, but placed on the day the sceptre has bent it becomes a prayer for a king already doomed, so the reader hears the epic's justice theme inside a rite meant only to save the cattle."},{"a":"si:17:148-159","b":"kl:104","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 104 ends its herders' kuravai by praising the god of the ancient bright discus and wishing that the world take the one word of the Pandya with the thunder-loud drum; the epic's kuravai to the discus-holder (கதிர்திகிரி, line 99) ends in the same wish for the Pandya's drum.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the Sangam mullai close, god first and then the Pandya's drum, but moves the kuravai from the joy after the bull-catch to a rite against ill omens, so the inherited blessing now carries dread rather than celebration.","gn":["the epic's close is shown to be a set form of the Madurai herders' kuravai, not Ilango's invention, which makes its irony a turn on a known convention","the Kalittokai's conventional wish for the Pandya is shown to be one that a story could test: the epic sets it on the day the same dynasty's justice fails"]},{"a":"si:17:122-125","b":"pt:11","type":"qualifies","note":"Patitruppattu 11 likens Cheralathan's sea-raid and felling of the guarded kadambu to Murugan cutting down Sur in the sea; the epic's herdswomen tell the same Chera deed and say he churned the sea, Vishnu's deed.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both poets lift the Chera's kadambu raid into a god's sea-deed, but each picks the god of its own setting: the Patitruppattu's war poem chooses Murugan, the herders' kuravai chooses Maal, so the same royal memory is divinised differently by genre.","gn":["the epic's bare 'the Chera who threw down the kadambu' gains the Sangam record of who did it and how it was praised, while the epic itself does not name Neduncheralathan","the Patitruppattu's comparison gains a later witness showing the kadambu raid still famous enough to be sung by Pandya herdswomen beside the other two crowns"]},{"a":"si:17:138-142","b":"k:9","type":"develops","note":"Kural 9 says a head that does not bow at the feet of the eight-qualitied one is as useless as a sense organ without its power; the kuravai asks what ear it is that has not heard Thirumaal's praise, and goes on to the eye and the tongue.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's simile, an organ without its function, becomes in the kuravai a definition carried through ear, eye and tongue, so the claim that the body's parts exist for worship is spelled out organ by organ and bound to one named god.","gn":["the epic's refrain gains a recognised ethical form: it is the Kural's argument that an organ is defined by its proper act, applied to devotion","the couplet's unnamed god and single organ gain a sung extension, where the same test is applied to each sense and given a sectarian object"]},{"a":"si:17:126-129","b":"pr:3:63-80","type":"qualifies","note":"Paripadal 3 says the god is everything and the essence within everything and calls him one full of maayam (மாயமார் அனையை); the kuravai asks whether the churner of the sea bound by Asodhai's churning rope is maayam, for it bewilders.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hymn treats the god's maayam as a doctrine to be stated, his being all things at once, while the herdswomen meet it as an unanswered question in a domestic scene, so the same paradox is shown both as learned theology and as village wonder.","gn":["the kuravai's question gains an answer it does not give: the hymn's account of why one god can be both the cosmic churner and a bound child","the hymn's abstract claim gains a concrete case, a single hand that churned the ocean and was tied with a butter-churn's cord"]},{"a":"si:18:1-15","b":"si:17:148-159","type":"qualifies","note":"The kuravai ends praising the Pandya's victorious drum and asking the god to remove the herd's distress; the woman who led it comes back from the river with news of the Pandya's guards killing an innocent man.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set side by side, the herdswomen's blessing on the king's victory is overtaken within lines by the report of his injustice, so the rite meant to avert evil omens is shown failing at the very moment it is completed."},{"a":"si:18:16-33","b":"si:18:34-53","type":"tension","note":"The townsfolk say Kovalan is a thief (கள்வனாம்); the sun's voice answers கள்வனோ அல்லன்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The kaathai sets the hearsay of men, which the king acted on, against the testimony of the all-seeing sun, so the case against Kovalan is answered by a higher witness before it ever reaches the king's court."},{"a":"si:18:16-33","b":"si:19:1-14","type":"develops","note":"The rumour says he stole an anklet from the palace; in the next kaathai Kannaki tells the women of the city that he was no thief and was killed for the price of her own anklet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kannaki turns the private news she received into a public counter-charge, correcting the one fact the rumour got wrong (whose anklet it was), which is the fact the trial will turn on."},{"a":"si:18:34-53","b":"si:21:40-57","type":"develops","note":"The sun's voice says bright fire will eat this city; in the vow, the fire god appears and says he was ordered long ago to eat the city on the day it wronged the great pathini.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The burning of Madurai is announced twice by gods, first by the sun as witness and then by the fire as agent, so Kannaki's act fulfils a sentence already spoken from above rather than a private revenge."},{"a":"si:18:34-53","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"qualifies","note":"Here Kannaki names only the king's error as the cause; before the king she says her husband came to the city driven by ஊழ்வினை.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In private grief Kannaki blames the king alone, but in court she joins karma to his fault, so the two causes the patikam names are shown held by one speaker at two moments, not only by narrator and character."},{"a":"si:18:34-53","b":"pn:246","type":"qualifies","note":"The queen of Bhoothapandiyan refuses the widow's life of alli food and the gravel bed and chooses the pyre; Kannaki asks whether she too must waste away like the widows who keep those observances.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both widows refuse the observances of kaimmai, but the Sangam queen turns to the pyre while Kannaki turns to the sun and the king, so the epic moves the wife's refusal of widowhood from death beside the husband to a demand for justice.","gn":["the lament gains the Sangam measure of what the widows' observances were and that a high-born wife could reject them","the poem's choice between widowhood and the pyre gains a third way, a widow who rejects both to accuse the king"]},{"a":"si:19:1-14","b":"si:19:51-75","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki's vow to see her husband and hear a faultless word from him is kept in the vision, where he speaks only of her faded face and bids her stay.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vow is fulfilled in form but not in substance: the dead husband's word clears no one, so the proof of innocence has to come from Kannaki's own plea at court."},{"a":"si:19:15-34","b":"si:20:1-7","type":"same_image","note":"The townspeople's bent sceptre and cool umbrella breeding heat are the omens the queen sees falling in her dream in the next kaathai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same signs of ruin appear first as the street's verdict in waking speech and then as the palace's dream, so the city knows the king's fall before the court does."},{"a":"si:19:15-34","b":"k:555","type":"develops","note":"The city sees Kannaki weeping beyond bearing (அல்லலுற் றாற்றா தழுவாளை), the very condition the couplet names as the force that wears away a king's wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's tears work slowly as an abrasive on wealth, but the epic makes one woman's tears the moment the people themselves declare the sceptre bent, the first step to the city's burning.","gn":["the citizens' grief gains a known rule that such tears are a weapon against the ruler","the couplet's general 'those who suffer' gains a single witnessed scene in which a whole city reads the tears as a verdict on the king"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:19:15-34","b":"pn:72","type":"develops","note":"In Puram 72 the Sangam Pandya Nedunjeliyan of Talaiyalanganam curses himself, should he fail in his vow, to be a king whose people find no cool shade and spread his blame (குடிபழி தூற்றும்); the epic's Madurai spreads the blame of its king (மன்பழி தூற்றுங் குடி) and finds his cool umbrella breeding heat. The two kings share a title, not an identity: the epic's Nedunjeliyan is not the Sangam one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam king's conditional self-curse, a boast of what will never happen, becomes in the epic a description of what has happened to a later Pandya, so the formula of royal disgrace moves from oath to fulfilment.","gn":["the citizens' words gain the weight of an old Pandya oath that names exactly this disgrace","the oath's imagined penalty gains a story showing a Pandya city actually blaming its king"]},{"a":"si:19:35-50","b":"n:101","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain's old deed seeks out its own doer as a calf finds its mother among many cows; Kannaki laments a deed to be eaten that reached her husband through the king's fault.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the quatrain sends the deed straight back to the one who did it, the epic interposes a guilty king between deed and fruit, so karma and the ruler's fault both stand as causes of one death.","gn":["Kannaki's 'deed that must be eaten' gains the quatrain's account of how a deed finds its owner","the quatrain gains a case where the deed arrives through another's wrongdoing, which its image of the calf leaves out"]},{"a":"si:19:35-50","b":"pn:280","type":"develops","note":"Both show a wife at her husband's wounded chest at the edge of night; the Puram wife reads the omens and dreads the widow's life, while Kannaki asks who will name the deed and blames the king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam lament by the wounded body looks ahead to widowhood, but Kannaki's lament at the same scene turns outward into a charge against the king, so the old mourning form becomes the opening of a plea.","gn":["Kannaki's lament gains the old shape of the wife's watch by the wounded husband, against which its turn to accusation shows","the Puram wife's private grief gains a counterpart in which the same scene is made public and political"]},{"a":"si:19:35-50","b":"si:0:37-54","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki says she does not know the cruel deed behind her loss; the patikam's goddess tells the grieving pathini it was a curse laid in a former birth by the wife of Sangaman.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The heroine laments in ignorance of the cause the poem's frame has already disclosed, so the reader judges her accusation of the king knowing a second cause she does not."},{"a":"si:19:51-75","b":"si:0:37-54","type":"qualifies","note":"The patikam's goddess says Kannaki will see her husband only in the form of the gods; here he rises among a host of immortals (பல்லமரர் குழாத்துளான்) on the day of his death.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vision anticipates the reunion the goddess limits to divine form, and Kannaki's refusal to join him until her anger cools shows the reunion withheld by her own choice, not only by the curse's term."},{"a":"si:19:51-75","b":"si:9:45-54","type":"develops","note":"The closing lines recall the evil dream (இடருற்ற தீக்கனா) in which Kannaki saw a false word laid on her husband in a great city.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kannaki goes to the king remembering the dream that foretold the false charge, so the dream that was dismissed now becomes her warrant for the confrontation it did not foresee."},{"a":"si:20:1-7","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"develops","note":"The queen's dream of the falling umbrella and sceptre is fulfilled in the king's lowered umbrella and slack sceptre at the verdict.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The omen comes first in the queen's private dream and is fulfilled the same hour in public, so the fall of justice is shown as already decided before Kannaki speaks.","gr":4},{"a":"si:20:1-7","b":"pn:229","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the falling star and the broken royal umbrella as signs of a king's death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic borrows the Sangam stock of royal death-omens but moves them into a dream before the death, turning the elegist's after-the-fact proof of grief into a warning that comes too late to be heeded.","gn":["its dream gains a traditional sense: these are the recognised signs that a king will die, not private fears","the poem's omens gain a narrative use, where the same signs foretell a death caused by the king's own wrong"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:20:34-54","b":"si:19:51-75","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki vowed to see the cruel king and ask him the reason; here she stands at his gate and sends in her charge.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The vow made beside the body is kept as a legal summons through the gatekeeper, so her grief is shown taking the form of a formal case rather than a lament.","gr":2},{"a":"si:20:55-73","b":"pn:37","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki's 'he who relieved the distress of the bird' repeats the Purananuru's praise formula for the Chola's ancestor (புள்ளுறு புன்கண் தீர்த்த).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic takes a genealogical compliment that a Sangam poet paid a Chola king and turns it into a legal precedent that a Chola woman holds against a Pandya, showing the ancestor-legend working as a standard of justice across kingdoms.","gn":["Kannaki's precedent gains the weight of an established praise-formula of the Chola line","the formula gains a use beyond flattery: it becomes the measure by which another king is judged"],"gr":4},{"a":"si:20:55-73","b":"si:16:148-163","type":"qualifies","note":"The narrator says the king did not inquire because it was the hour of karma's fruit (தேரான் ஆகி); Kannaki calls him the king who did not inquire (தேரா மன்னா).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same failure to inquire is excused by karma in the narrator's voice and charged as guilt in Kannaki's, and she herself also names karma, so the epic holds both causes together rather than choosing one.","gr":3},{"a":"si:20:55-73","b":"k:541","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet makes inquiry (தேர்ந்துசெய்) part of what justice is; Kannaki's first words to the king name him as the one who did not inquire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats inquiry as within the ruler's power, while the epic shows a ruler failing it in an hour the narrator calls karma's ripening, and still holds him answerable for it.","gn":["Kannaki's charge gains a definition: what the king failed is the first step of justice itself","the couplet gains a case that tests it: a king fails to inquire under the press of former deeds and is still judged by the couplet's standard"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:20:74-91","b":"si:9:55-66","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki dreamed that she spoke before the king and that harm came to the king and the city with him; here she speaks before the king and he falls.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Her dream foretold both the plea and the harm beyond the king, so the king's fall here is only the first part of what the dream foretold, with the city's burning still to come.","gr":2},{"a":"si:20:74-91","b":"si:16:218-221","type":"qualifies","note":"The narrator's venba says the never-bent sceptre bent as the fruit of former karma; the king says only that he heeded the goldsmith and is himself the thief.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The narrator assigns the bent sceptre to karma while the king, judging himself, assigns it wholly to his own act, so the epic lets the wrongdoer refuse the excuse its narrator offers.","gr":2},{"a":"si:20:74-91","b":"k:548","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says a ruler who does not examine and do justice perishes by himself (தானே கெடும்); the Pandya, who did not examine, pronounces his own death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic makes the couplet's 'perishes by himself' literal: no enemy or court condemns the king, he passes the sentence on himself the moment his error is shown, and he was easily approached, so only the failure to examine undoes him.","gn":["the king's self-sentence gains a rule behind it: ruin comes from within to the king who does not examine","the couplet gains a scene that separates its two conditions, an accessible king who still fails to examine, and shows the ruin as self-inflicted in act as well as in result"],"gr":4},{"a":"si:20:92-95","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's first moral, that dharma becomes death to those who err in kingship, is restated here as a common saying proved true.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The moral announced at the outset as the poem's thesis is here called a saying of the assemblies now proved, so the book frames its plot as the demonstration of a received maxim.","gr":3},{"a":"si:20:92-95","b":"si:21:1-16","type":"develops","note":"The venba's 'queen of the king' and the speaker 'of harsh deeds' recur at the opening of the next kaathai, where Kannaki addresses the queen and calls herself கொடுவினை யாட்டியேன்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The next kaathai's opening supports reading this venba as Kannaki's words to the queen, and shows the threat 'see what I will do' carried into the vow against the city.","gr":3},{"a":"si:20:92-95","b":"k:320","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says all pains rest on those who caused pain; the venba says dharma is death to the wrongdoer, but the speaker, who did no wrong, calls herself one of harsh deeds and the wrongdoer's queen suffers too.","grade":"forced","better":"agrees","te":"The epic agrees that harm returns to the one who did it, but shows it spreading to the innocent queen and to the victim who names her own karma, so the couplet's neat return of pain to its doer becomes a wider ruin.","gn":["the venba's maxim gains the couplet's general rule that harm returns to its doer","the couplet gains a case where pain does reach the doer, yet also those around him, which its own wording does not consider"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:20:100-103","b":"k:555","type":"develops","note":"The couplet calls the tears of those who cannot bear their suffering a weapon that wears away wealth; here a widow's tears and words take the king's life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's slow wearing-away of a bad king's wealth by the people's tears becomes, in the epic, the instant death of the king at one wronged woman's tears and words.","gn":["the venba gains a moral law behind its marvel: tears of the wronged are a force against an unjust king","the couplet gains an extreme case, where the weapon of tears acts at once and on the king's life, not slowly on his wealth"],"gr":6},{"a":"si:21:1-16","b":"k:319","type":"qualifies","note":"Kannaki states the forenoon-afternoon law of returning harm, in other words than the couplet's, as her warning to the Pandya's house.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's தாமே says the harm returns of itself with no agent, but the epic puts the law in the mouth of the woman who will herself bring the harm back that same day, so the impersonal return of a deed is shown working through a wronged person's act.","gn":["her threat gains the standing of a known moral law rather than private revenge","the couplet gains a case where the returning harm has a human executor and falls on a whole city, not only on the doer"]},{"a":"si:21:1-16","b":"a:376","type":"develops","note":"Both tell of the husband of Karikal's line seized by the Kaveri flood at a festival; the akam poem leaves him taken, the epic has the sea bring him back to the wife who followed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam memory of a lover lost to the river, which an akam courtesan uses as an image of a man taken away for good, is completed in the epic with a restoration won by the wife's steadfastness, turning a story of loss into an exemplum of chastity's power.","gn":["the clipped allusion gains its back-story: the flood at Kazhar before Karikal in which the husband was seized","the akam image gains a later ending in which the sea gives back what the river took, showing how the tale was retold as a chastity legend"]},{"a":"si:21:17-39","b":"pn:72","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are vanjinam vows in the conditional form 'if I do not, may I', one by a Pandya Nedunjeliyan before battle, one by Kannaki against a Pandya; the epic's king is not shown to be the Talaiyalanganattu victor of the puram poem.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram king stakes his own name as a just king on winning, while Kannaki stakes her name as a pathini on unmaking a king, so the epic turns the royal oath-form against the royal house and makes a widow's virtue the thing pledged.","gn":["her vow gains the recognised form and force of the warrior's pledge, which explains the kaathai's name","the puram oath-form gains a use outside war and kingship, showing that its penalty of being called a cruel king could be fulfilled by another's vow"]},{"a":"si:21:40-57","b":"pn:9","type":"develops","note":"Puram 9's Pandya warns cows, Brahmins, women, the sick and the sonless to take shelter before he attacks; Kannaki tells the fire to spare Brahmins, the virtuous, cows, chaste women, elders and children.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The protected classes of the just king's war reappear as the limit a wronged woman sets on her own vengeance against a Pandya city, so the code meant to restrain kings now restrains the one punishing a king, and is applied to his own capital.","gn":["her list of the spared gains a precedent: it is the established rule of righteous attack, not a sudden mercy","the poem's battle rule gains a case where it binds a non-king and is turned against the Pandya line whose ancestor proclaimed it"]},{"a":"si:21:40-57","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet's wife commands the rain; Kannaki commands the fire god, who waits on her word.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet's wife who worships no god has the rain at her word, the epic shows a god coming in person to take her orders, and the power that brings rain turned to burning a city, so the same claim for the chaste wife's word is shown in its most destructive extent.","gn":["her command over the fire gains a known doctrine behind it: the chaste wife's word moves the elements","the couplet gains a test case showing that the power it grants is not only beneficent and can set a god to destroy"]},{"a":"si:21:58-61","b":"si:9:45-54","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki's dream of a harm to the city with the king, which she would not speak of, is fulfilled in the burning of the Pandya's house and Madurai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The harm she refused to name in her dream turns out to be her own act, so the omen foretold not only the city's fall but the part the dreamer would play in it."},{"a":"si:21:40-57","b":"si:0:37-54","type":"qualifies","note":"The patikam traces the ruin to a curse of a former birth ripening; the fire god here says he held an old order to burn the city, while Kannaki claims the act as her own rage.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read together, the burning is caused three ways at once, by a karmic curse, by a standing divine order and by the wife's own deliberate act, and the epic does not choose between fate and her choice."},{"a":"si:21:17-39","b":"si:20:92-95","type":"develops","note":"The venba's challenge to the queen to see what the woman of harsh deeds will do is made explicit as a vow to destroy the kingdom and Madurai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The vague threat that closed the plea becomes a sworn vow with a condition on her own chastity, so the step from grievance to destruction is taken as an oath, not as a sudden fury."},{"a":"si:22:1-15","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"develops","note":"In the court the Pandya calls himself the thief and dies; here the narrator says he gave his life for the bent sceptre and so showed the earth a straight one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The narrator turns the king's self-condemnation into his last act of just rule, so the same death that proves the sceptre bent is counted as straightening it."},{"a":"si:22:1-15","b":"k:548","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says the king who does not examine and do justice perishes by himself (தானே கெடும்); the Pandya, having failed to examine, dies by his own sentence.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic takes the couplet's 'perishes by himself' literally, as a king's own verdict on himself, and adds what the couplet does not allow for: that the ruin can be read as redeeming the rule it ends.","gn":["the unit's claim that the death showed a straight sceptre gains a rule behind it: a non-examining king's fall is self-caused, not an enemy's work","the couplet gains a case in which self-caused ruin is chosen and becomes the king's one act of justice, which a bare 'he will perish' cannot show"]},{"a":"si:22:1-15","b":"si:21:40-57","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki bids the fire god go to the wicked (ஏவ); this kaathai opens by naming the fire as ஏவல் தெய்வம், the god under command.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The title the fire bears here carries over from her command, so the burning of Madurai is presented as a god's obedience to a woman rather than as divine wrath."},{"a":"si:22:37-61","b":"k:550","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet likens a king's killing of the cruel to weeding a crop; the royal bhuta's office is to put down cruel deeds (கொடுந்தொழில் கடிந்து), and the Pandya killed Kovalan as a thief under that office.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two show the licence to kill wrongdoers, which the Pandya invoked, being the very office whose guardian spirit now abandons his city, so the Kural's weeding image is shown to depend on the king knowing which are the weeds.","gn":["the royal bhuta's charter gains a pointed sense: its duty of putting down the cruel is the duty the Pandya misused","the couplet gains its failure case, a king who weeded out an innocent and whose own guardian spirit then left"]},{"a":"si:22:16-36","b":"t:234","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says a land where Brahmins make the oblation dusk and dawn does not decline and its king prospers; here the Brahmin bhuta of the unfailing three-fire life leaves Madurai because its king erred.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Tirumular makes the Brahmins' rites the protection of king and land, while the epic makes the king's justice the condition of the guardian of those rites staying, so the two run the dependence in opposite directions.","gn":["the Brahmin bhuta's departure gains its weight: it withdraws what the Saiva text holds to be the land's very safeguard","the verse gains a limit: in the epic even the guardian of the three fires cannot keep a city whose king has done wrong"]},{"a":"si:22:89-106","b":"k:559","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says that if the king bends justice the sky withholds rain; the bhutas say that on the day the king errs in the order of rule the city is eaten by fire.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Both bind nature's order to the king's justice, but the epic replaces the slow withdrawal of rain with an immediate fire on the city and gives the city's own guardian spirits the knowledge of the rule, so the couplet's consequence becomes a sentence carried out the same night.","gn":["the bhutas' rule gains a recognised doctrine: misrule upsets the order of nature, which the Kural already states","the couplet gains a sharper instance where the penalty falls at once on the city as a whole, and the guardians themselves stand aside for it"]},{"a":"si:22:89-106","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's first moral is that dharma becomes death to those who err in kingship (அரைசியல் பிழைத்தோர்க்கு அறம்கூற்று); the bhutas name the same fault (கோமுறை பிழைத்த) as the cause of the city's burning.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The patikam promises death to the erring king; this unit extends the penalty from the king to his city and makes it a standing law the guardian spirits obey, which the moral as first stated does not say."},{"a":"si:22:107-118","b":"si:21:40-57","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki bids the fire spare Brahmins, the virtuous, cows, chaste wives, elders and children and go to the wicked; here the flame spares the side of the virtuous, the cows and calves escape, and the warriors' quarter burns.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The exemptions she named are carried out street by street, so the fire is shown as a discriminating judgment rather than a blind disaster, and the warriors' quarter is put among those she called the wicked.","gr":2},{"a":"si:22:107-118","b":"t:243","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular says the king whose task is to protect cows, women, the virtuous and the holy goes to endless hell if he fails them; in the burning Madurai the cows and the virtuous are protected not by the king, who is dead, but by the fire itself.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two list the same protected beings, but the Saiva verse punishes the failing king after death while the epic transfers his duty of protection, in this life, to the wronged wife's fire.","gn":["the fire's sparing gains a recognised code: these are the beings any king is bound to protect","the verse gains a case where the duty survives the king's failure and is fulfilled by another power, which its threat of hell does not consider"]},{"a":"si:22:119-137","b":"si:21:17-39","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki says that if she is truly a pathini she will not let the king and Madurai live; the city's housewives, keepers of household dharma, declare her breast-battle no cruelty and worship the fire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Her conditional oath is answered not by a god but by the wives of the city she burns, so her standing as a pathini is confirmed by the very household virtue she claimed.","gr":2},{"a":"si:22:138-157","b":"k:560","type":"develops","note":"The couplet says that if the guardian does not guard, the cows' yield fails and those of the six duties forget their books; in burning Madurai the Vedic chant and the fire offering cease.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's warning that Vedic learning lapses under an unguarding king is shown happening in a single night, and with the cows spared, so the epic keeps one half of the couplet's penalty and lets the other fall.","gn":["the stopping of the rites gains its doctrinal reading: it is the Kural's named sign of a king who failed to guard","the couplet gains a scene in which its two penalties are separated, the cows spared by the pathini's command while the books' recitation still falls silent"]},{"a":"si:22:138-157","b":"si:4:58-71","type":"same_image","note":"In Puhar's evening the women parted from their lovers sigh like a smith's bellows (ஊதுஉலைக் குருகின் உயிர்த்தனர்); here Kannaki, parted from Kovalan by his death, sighs like the bellows (ஊதுலைக் குருகின் உயிர்த்தனள்) amid a burning city.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same formula moves from the passing grief of lovers apart for a night to a widow's grief that has set a city alight, so the forge in the image becomes a real fire."},{"a":"si:22:138-157","b":"si:23:18-32","type":"develops","note":"Madurapathi appears before Kannaki here; in the next kaathai she names herself and tells her that the harm came to her husband as ஊழ்வினை, a deed come to fruit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The goddess who comes because she cannot bear the fire is the one who will give karma as the cause, so the poem brings in its karmic account only after the pathini's justice has run its course."},{"a":"si:22:158-161","b":"si:23:1-17","type":"develops","note":"The venba gives Madurapathi the powers of Lakshmi, Saraswati and the slayer of Mahisha; the next kaathai shows her body half dark and half gold, lotus in one hand and sword in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The venba's union of gentle and warlike goddesses is drawn in the next kaathai as a body divided down the middle, so the reader sees the city goddess's double nature first named, then pictured.","gr":2},{"a":"si:23:1-17","b":"si:22:138-157","type":"qualifies","note":"The Alarpadu Kaathai ends with the goddess of Madurai appearing before (முன்) the pathini, unable to bear the fire; here she will not stand before her and appears behind her, being the Pandya's family goddess.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the goddess's first coming is driven by the fire, but her place behind Kannaki shows that the city's deity arrives as an embarrassed advocate of the guilty king's house."},{"a":"si:23:18-32","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"qualifies","note":"Kannaki shamed the Pandya with the Chola whose gate bell's tongue trembled for the cow; the goddess replies that the Pandya kings' ears heard the Veda's tongue (மறைநா) and never the bell's tongue (மணிநா).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The goddess turns Kannaki's proof of the Chola's justice into evidence for the Pandya: a line whose bell no one ever had to ring needed no such exemplar, so the dispute over the king's justice becomes a dispute between two dynasties' records.","gr":2},{"a":"si:23:18-32","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's third moral is that the former deed comes in wrath and makes one eat it; the goddess says ஊழ்வினை came to her king as well as to Kannaki's husband.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam keeps karma for the story's sufferers and the death-dealing dharma for the king, but the goddess applies ஊழ்வினை to the king himself, so the first and third morals meet in one person."},{"a":"si:23:33-44","b":"k:952","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says those born of good family do not slip (இழுக்கார்) in conduct; the goddess says those born in the Pandya line joined to conduct (ஒழுக்கொடு) suffer no fault (இழுக்கம்) even in untamed youth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's rule about good birth is spoken by the dynasty's own goddess while she excuses its present king, so the epic shows the rule used as a family's defence at the moment its member has failed.","gn":["the goddess's boast gains a recognised maxim behind it, which is why it carries weight against Kannaki's charge","the couplet gains a test: the chapter itself tells of a king of that line who erred, so good birth does not guarantee what the couplet claims"]},{"a":"si:23:45-69","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"qualifies","note":"Kannaki cited the Chola who killed his own son under the wheel when the cow's tears burned his heart (நெஞ்சுசுட); the goddess answers with a Pandya who cut off his own hand when a woman's reproach burned his heart (நெஞ்சஞ் சுடுதலின்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two speeches match self-punishment for self-punishment, son for hand, showing that the argument over Kovalan's death is being fought with rival royal legends of the same pattern."},{"a":"si:23:70-85","b":"pt:11","type":"develops","note":"Parasaran hails the Chera who cut the kadamba in the sea (கடற்கடம் பெறிந்த) and carved his bow on the hill; Patitruppattu 11 tells the felling of the guarded kadamba after reaching the sea by Imayavaramban Neduncheralathan, whom the Himalaya marks.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the Sangam Chera's signature deed as a blessing formula in a brahmin's mouth, and strings it with Mantharam Cheral and the Porunai's Poraiyan, so the separate Cheras of the decades become one praised house.","gn":["the brahmin's blessing gains a real deed and a named king behind its epithet","the decade's battle gains an afterlife as a stock title, cited in a Pandya village by a Chola-land brahmin as the Chera's fame"]},{"a":"si:23:86-102","b":"si:16:130-147","type":"same_image","note":"The officers seize Vaarthikan because his son wears jewels a stranger gave; the goldsmith tells the king that the stranger with the anklet is the thief of the palace anklet.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The goddess's precedent is the same case as Kovalan's, a man holding valuable jewels wrongly taken for a thief by the king's men, chosen so that the old king's remedy can be measured against the present king's haste."},{"a":"si:23:103-122","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"qualifies","note":"The old Pandya asks whether his sceptre has bent, admits his rule strayed through ignorant men and makes amends; Nedunjeliyan, shown the gem, says he is the thief and dies.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both kings confess the fault as their own, but the ancestor's confession comes before any death and repairs it with land, while Nedunjeliyan's comes after and can only be paid with his life: the difference is when the king inquires.","gr":2},{"a":"si:23:123-137","b":"si:21:40-57","type":"develops","note":"The goddess cites a saying that fire will eat Madurai and its king on a fixed day of Aadi; the fire god says he had a command long before (பண்டே) to burn the city on the day the pathini was wronged.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two separate voices, the omen and the fire god's standing order, make the burning predetermined, so Kannaki's act is shown as the trigger of a sentence already fixed.","gr":2},{"a":"si:23:138-160","b":"si:16:148-163","type":"develops","note":"Bharatan seized a stranger merchant with goods as a spy, showed him to the king and had him killed; in this life Kovalan, a stranger with an anklet, is shown to the king as a thief and killed without inquiry.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The former deed is the present crime with the roles reversed, so Kovalan suffers exactly the false accusation he once made, and the goldsmith takes the place he once held."},{"a":"si:23:161-176","b":"si:19:51-75","type":"develops","note":"Neeli cried through the halls and streets asking whether this was justice and cursed the killers to suffer her sorrow; Kannaki cries through Madurai asking whether there are women, wise men or a god in the city.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Neeli's curse asked for the same sorrow, and Kannaki's lament fulfils it in form as well as fact, a widow's public cry against an unjust killing repeated in a new birth."},{"a":"si:23:161-176","b":"n:51","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain says sin cannot stand before austerity while merit lasts; the goddess says that when a former deed rises in wrath penance does not help those not upright.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both limit what austerity can do, but the quatrain makes merit run out with time, while the goddess makes the bar moral: penance fails against a ripened deed for those who lack uprightness.","gn":["the goddess's saying gains a measure: austerity does guard, but only on terms","the quatrain's limit gains a second condition and a case, the ascetic-guarded couple who still meet the deed of a former birth"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:23:177-200","b":"si:21:40-57","type":"tension","note":"At the burning Kannaki declared to the gods that she was without fault (குற்றமிலேன் யான்); under the vengai she laments that she is a woman of fiery deeds (தீத்தொழி லாட்டியேன் யான்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kannaki's own judgment of the burning reverses once the goddess has shown her the former deed, so the epic lets its heroine see her vengeance as a deed with its own weight just before it raises her to heaven."},{"a":"si:23:201-204","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says the wife who worships her husband rather than a god can command the rain; the venba takes its words and says the gods themselves worship such a wife.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic reads Kural 55 through Kannaki's end: the wife's power over nature becomes a claim on the gods, who now worship her, and the reward follows the burning of a city rather than a gift of rain.","gn":["the venba gains the authority of the couplet's own words for its praise of Kannaki","the couplet gains a case that tests it: its pathini wields fire and destruction, not rain, yet is still praised in its words"]},{"a":"si:madurai_kandam_katturai:1-20","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"develops","note":"The verdict scene ends with the Pandya saying 'am I king? I am the thief', falling dead, and his queen falling at his feet saying there is nothing to show those who have lost their husbands; the summary recalls this as the king who slept on his throne-couch with his queen of flawless chastity.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The summary turns the king's self-condemnation and his wife's instant death into one dignified image of a royal pair at rest, and gives its only epithet of chastity to the queen and does not name Kannaki, so the book's official memory honours the Pandya's atoning death as the thing its whole story is viewed with."},{"a":"si:madurai_kandam_katturai:1-20","b":"k:559","type":"tension","note":"The couplet says that if the ruler bends justice the sky, bending its rain, will not pour; the summary credits the Pandya land, whose king bent justice in this book, with a sky that never fails to pour new rain.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read against the couplet, the summary's line of unfailing rain claims for the Pandya land the very sign the Kural withdraws from a bent sceptre, so the epic's punishment for the bent sceptre falls on the king's own life and the city by fire rather than on the rain, and its stock praise of the land survives the verdict intact.","gn":["the formula line about rain gains a political charge: under the couplet it is a claim that the king ruled justly, which the book itself has refuted","the couplet gains a case in which the bent sceptre is answered by a different penalty than drought, the king's death and his city's burning, which limits the couplet's rain as the only sign of misrule"]},{"a":"si:madurai_kandam_katturai:1-20","b":"pn:246","type":"qualifies","note":"Bhoothapandiyan's queen rebukes the elders who hold her back and chooses the pyre over the widow's austerities, calling fire and a lotus pond one to her; the summary sets the Pandya Nedunjeliyan's queen of flawless chastity lying with him on the throne-couch, her death having come at once at his feet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both remember a Pandya queen who would not outlive her husband, but the Puram queen must argue her way to the pyre against counsel, while the epic's queen dies without fire or argument and the summary counts that death as flawless chastity, so together they show the widow's death moving from a chosen and contested act to a sign of karpu that needs no choosing.","gn":["the summary's praise of the queen gains the older Pandya precedent of a wife who refused widowhood, which shows what kind of chastity புரைதீர் கற்பின் is honouring","the Puram poem gains a later version of its scene in which no elders object and no pyre is needed, which shows the queen's resolve becoming an ideal assumed rather than argued"]},{"a":"si:24:1-22","b":"si:23:161-176","type":"develops","note":"The Madurai goddess told Kannaki that a deed of the former birth had come and pressed (உம்மை வினைவந் துருத்த காலை) and that after fourteen days she would see her husband only in a god's form; here Kannaki uses the same words of herself and the gods bring her husband.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kannaki takes the goddess's karmic account into her own mouth when strangers ask who she is, so the explanation offered to her in Madurai becomes the one she gives of herself, and the prophecy is fulfilled before witnesses outside the city."},{"a":"si:24:1-22","b":"si:0:1-11","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's hill kuravar report to Ilango that they saw a pathini taken to heaven under a vengai; this unit is the hill women's own telling of that sight.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The frame says the epic began from the hill folk's report; the kaathai shows that report already as a cult with drums, incense and a prayer for the hill's wealth, so the poem's source is presented as a worship before it is a story."},{"a":"si:24:1-22","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 380 says nothing is stronger than oozh, which arrives before any device; Kannaki blames the strong deed that pressed on her but also names herself the woman of cruel deeds.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Kannaki grants karma the force the couplet gives oozh yet keeps the burning of Madurai as her own act, so the epic holds fate and the agent's deed together where the couplet speaks only of fate's priority.","gn":["her double phrase (வல்வினை, கடுவினையேன்) is set against a flat statement of fate's power, which brings out that she does not hide behind it","the couplet's abstract fate is shown working through a person who still owns what she did"],"gr":3},{"a":"si:24:59-62","b":"ku:111","type":"develops","note":"In Kuruntokai 111 the mother takes the velan's word that Neduvel caused the girl's sickness, and the friend wants the lover to come so the household may see a great laugh (பெருநகை); here the friend's refrain is இதுநகையாகின்றே over the same mistake.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The laugh at the mother's misreading, a friend's private aside in the short poem, becomes the sung refrain of a public round dance, which shows the old akam situation turned into communal performance.","gn":["the epic's refrain gains a source: the joke on the velan was an established akam situation, not the hill girls' invention","the short poem's single laugh is shown as a convention strong enough to carry four stanzas of a festival song"]},{"a":"si:24:71-74","b":"nt:34","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 34's friend tells Murugan that, knowing the sickness is not his seizure, he came to the veri house at the velan's call, and so, though a god, he is truly foolish (மடவை மன்ற); the kuravai ranks the god as more foolish than the velan if he comes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The single daring address to the god in the Sangam poem becomes a graded series (the velan foolish, the god more so) sung inside a hymn to the same god's spear, so the mockery of his coming to the rite sits beside praise of his power without contradiction.","gn":["the stanza's charge against the god is seen to be a received akam boldness, not irreverence peculiar to the epic","the poem's address gains a setting in worship: the same girls who call the god foolish here have just sung his victories"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:24:63-66","b":"pr:5:1-21","type":"qualifies","note":"Paripadal 5 praises the spear that cut Sur's mango in the sea and split the crane-named mountain and says the velan's praises and veri are neither true nor false; the kuravai names the same crane-mountain killer and calls him foolish if he comes to the velan's rite.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both texts hold the god's deeds fixed while keeping the velan's rite at a distance: the hymn suspends judgement on the rite, and the kuravai laughs at it, which shows that doubt about the diviner was compatible with full praise of Murugan.","gn":["the friend's mockery gains a temple-hymn parallel that also refuses to vouch for the veri","the hymn's guarded 'neither true nor false' is shown in a folk voice that goes further and laughs"]},{"a":"si:24:47-50","b":"pp:1:177-189","type":"qualifies","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai places the god at Erakam among Brahmins who chant the six-syllable mantra at the hours; the kuravai names Erakam with Senthil, Sengodu and Venkunru as hills the god never leaves.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same shrine is claimed by the guide poem for a Brahmin rite and by the hill women for their kuravai, and their list differs from the six seats, so the roster of Murugan's hills was not yet fixed when the epic was made.","gn":["the epic's four names gain a comparison that shows they are a distinct list, with Sengodu and Venkunru outside the six","the guide poem's Erakam is seen also as a hill folk's god's seat, not only a Brahmin site"]},{"a":"si:24:79-82","b":"pr:19:85-105","type":"qualifies","note":"In Paripadal 19 maidens who do not eat the leftover of the god's elephant at Parankunram fail to win husbands; the hill girls pray to Murugan not for marriage in general but that their friend be spared another's match and given her own lover.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The temple poem treats Murugan as giver of husbands to all maidens who honour him, while the kuravai bends the same belief to the akam crisis of the secret union, asking him to prevent the family's match.","gn":["the girls' prayer is seen to rest on an established belief that Murugan grants marriages","the hymn's general belief is shown turned to one lover and against one feared marriage (அயல்மணம்)"]},{"a":"si:24:87-90","b":"p:1000","type":"develops","note":"The kuravai girls call Murugan's kurava wife their own clan's daughter; Thiruppugazh 1000 tells Valli guarding the hunters' millet from her platform and Murugan entering the field.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic's hill women claim Valli as kin and pray to Murugan as an in-law of the clan, while the later devotional song keeps the millet field and the hunter girl but makes the story a model of the god's grace to the devotee, so the kin claim comes first and the theology after.","gn":["the clan claim gains a view of what the Valli story would later carry in devotion","the song's millet field and hunter girl are seen rooted in a hill people's claim to the goddess as their daughter"]},{"a":"si:24:91-100","b":"ai:250","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 250 tells the beads that the one who afflicted the girl is not Vel, lord of their valli forest; the friend here tells the lover that he comes with kadamba and spear but has no six faces, no peacock and no kurava bride.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Sangam friend's bare denial that the god caused the sickness is worked out here as a point-by-point comparison of the man with the god's attributes, which shows the denial leaning on an iconography of Murugan already settled.","gn":["the friend's list gains a precedent: denying the god's seizure was a set move of the friend's speech","the short poem's 'not Vel' is spelled out in the god's own marks, making plain what the girl's lover is being measured against"]},{"a":"si:24:101-117","b":"si:24:1-22","type":"qualifies","note":"Kannaki names a strong deed of the past and her own cruel deed as the cause of Madurai's ruin; the hill women sing that she burned Koodal because the royal order failed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Within one kaathai the burning is put down to karma in the victim's voice and to the king's broken justice in the worshippers', so the chapter repeats the patikam's two morals as two voices rather than choosing one."},{"a":"si:24:101-117","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 55 says the wife who rises worshipping her husband and not a god can bid the rain fall; here the pathini, who held to her husband alone, has become the god whom chaste wives worship, and the hill women ask her to keep their hill fruitful.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The couplet's power of the chaste wife's word passes, after her death, into a cult in which other chaste wives worship her, so the woman the couplet exempts from worshipping a god is herself made one.","gn":["the epic's deification gains a basis in a stated ethical claim about the chaste wife's power over rain and plenty","the couplet is tested at its edge: its own ideal, once dead, becomes an object of the worship the couplet set her free from"]},{"a":"si:24:118-134","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam promises to show that the high praise a renowned pathini (உரைசால் பத்தினிக்கு உயர்ந்தோர் ஏத்தல்); here the gods of heaven worship her and the hill women sing her praise.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The second moral is first fulfilled among the gods and the hill girls of a love song, before any king hears of her, so the praise of the pathini begins at the margin of the kingdom and travels to the Chera court in the next kaathai."},{"a":"si:24:118-134","b":"a:127","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 127 credits Cheralathan with driving off the sea-foe, cutting the kadamba and carving his bow on the Himalaya; the kuravai's closing blessing hails the king of the Kudavar who ruled Kolli and the Himalaya where the bow was carved.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A deed the akam poem ties to one named Chera becomes, in the epic's blessing, an epithet of the reigning king of the Kudavar without a name, which shows a Sangam memory turned into a dynastic title; the Tamil does not say the two kings are one.","gn":["the blessing's 'bow-inscribed Himalaya' gains its Sangam source and reads as inherited fame, not a deed of the king now ruling","the poem's single feat is shown surviving as a formula for the whole line in a later work"]},{"a":"si:25:46-63","b":"si:0:1-11","type":"develops","note":"The hill kuravar's report of the one-breasted woman taken to heaven, made to Ilango in the patikam, is made here to the reigning king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same witnesses tell the same wonder twice, to the prince who renounced and to the king who rules, and only the king's hearing turns it into a cult and a war, so the epic's frame and its last book answer each other."},{"a":"si:25:64-77","b":"si:0:37-54","type":"qualifies","note":"To Ilango, Saathan explains the former-birth curse behind the deed; to the king he tells only the anklet, the case, the vow and the burning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Saathan tailors the story to his hearer: the renouncer is told the karmic cause, the king is told the public case of a bent sceptre, so the epic keeps its two explanations of the disaster apart by audience."},{"a":"si:25:78-92","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"develops","note":"In the Vazhakkurai the queen trembles and falls at the dying king's feet; Saathan reports that she resolved to go where the king went and died with him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The scene of the verdict leaves the queen's end open, and the report to another court completes it as a chosen death, which is what lets the Chera queen weigh her against Kannaki in the next units."},{"a":"si:25:93-109","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"develops","note":"The Pandya named himself the thief and died; Senguttuvan reads that death as the dying life straightening a sceptre bent by strong former deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A second king supplies the interpretation the Pandya scene leaves unstated: the fault is laid on வல்வினை, yet the remedy is the king's own death, so karma and royal responsibility are both kept in one verdict."},{"a":"si:25:93-109","b":"pn:35","type":"qualifies","note":"Vellaikkudi Naganar tells the Chola that the world blames kings when rain fails and bids him ignore such talk and guard the ploughmen; Senguttuvan names rain failing and a life wronged as the protector-king's two great fears.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam counsellor treats blame for drought as unfair public talk a just king can bear, while the epic king takes the same burden inward as fear and ranks one wronged life above drought, so that kingship itself becomes suffering.","gn":["the king's lament gains a known royal topos, the king answerable for rain, against which his ranking of a wronged life above drought stands out","the poem's advice to set aside blame gains a counter-voice: a king who, after seeing a Pandya die of one wrong verdict, finds the burden unbearable rather than ignorable"]},{"a":"si:25:93-109","b":"k:388","type":"tension","note":"The Kural says the king who does justice and protects is set apart as இறை for the people; Senguttuvan says birth into a line that guards the people is suffering and nothing to be revered.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Read with தொழுதக as 'worthy of reverence', the two judge the same office oppositely: the couplet awards the protector divine standing, while the epic king, speaking after a just king's fall, denies that the office deserves reverence at all.","gn":["the lament gains a sharp opposite: the standard honour of the just king that it refuses","the couplet's conditional honour gains the view from inside the office, where the same duty of protection is felt as fear and suffering"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:25:110-121","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's second moral, that the great praise a renowned pathini, begins to be enacted when the Chera queen proposes worshipping her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The moral is fulfilled first by a queen's judgment that sets the Pandya queen's death aside for heaven and chooses Kannaki for worship on earth, so the praise of the great begins in a comparison of two wives."},{"a":"si:25:110-121","b":"pn:246","type":"qualifies","note":"Bhuthapandiyan's queen Perunkoppendu rejects the elders and chooses the pyre after her husband; the Chera queen grants the Pandya queen who died with her king heaven, but chooses Kannaki for worship.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam queen's death after her husband is the summit of a wife's virtue in its own poem, while the epic, granting such a death its heavenly reward, ranks above it the wronged wife who survived to accuse a king.","gn":["the queen's verdict gains the Sangam model of the widow's chosen death that it puts in second place","the poem gains a later judgment in which following one's husband in death is honoured yet ranked below the pathini's wrath"]},{"a":"si:25:122-136","b":"pt:patikam:5","type":"develops","note":"The Patitruppattu preface to the fifth decade lists among Senguttuvan's deeds that he went for a stone for the goddess Pattini and bathed it in the Ganges; the epic shows him choosing the Himalaya stone over the one from Pothiyil.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The preface records the stone as one conquest among many, while the epic gives its reason, a king's honour that refuses the near southern stone, so the pairing shows the pathini's cult being written into the Chera king's record of war.","gn":["the king's resolve gains independent witness that the stone for Pattini belonged to the tradition of Senguttuvan's deeds","the preface's bare phrase கடவுள் பத்தினிக் கற்கோள் gains a scene, a debate over which stone and why"]},{"a":"si:25:152-177","b":"pt:patikam:2","type":"develops","note":"Villavan Kothai credits Senguttuvan with making the sea-fenced land Tamil (இமிழ்கடல் வேலியைத் தமிழ்நா டாக்கிய); the Patitruppattu preface uses nearly the same phrase of his father Imayavaramban.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The minister applies to the son the formula the preface gives the father, so the Chera line's fame works as a shared inheritance that the campaign for the stone is said to renew.","gn":["the minister's praise gains its source, a formula of the Chera line rather than a fresh boast","the preface's phrase gains a later use as an argument that the northern kings will yield without war"]},{"a":"si:25:152-177","b":"pt:43","type":"qualifies","note":"Patitruppattu 43 praises Kuttuvan whose army broke kings from the Himalaya to Kumari; Villavan Kothai argues that because of such victories none in the world will resist him.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Sangam poem praises conquest as done, while the epic minister uses the same fame as a reason no new conquest is needed, so the reputation of past war is offered as the means of a bloodless one.","gn":["the minister's claim that none will resist gains the Sangam record of conquest it rests on","the poem's praise gains a political use, fame as a weapon that spares battle"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:25:178-194","b":"si:25:1-13","type":"same_image","note":"The kadambu thrown down in the sea and the bow set on the Himalaya, epithets of the Chera line in the opening lines, return in the drum's proclamation.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same two feats change use within one kaathai: at the start they adorn a king going to see the hills, at the end they are a threat sent to northern kings, which marks how far the news of the pathini has turned the pleasure trip."},{"a":"si:25:178-194","b":"k:590","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural warns that honouring a spy openly lets out the king's secret; Azhumbil Vel counts on the enemy's spies at Vanchi's gate to carry the proclamation.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats exposure to spies as a leak to be prevented, while the epic court makes the leak its instrument, so the pairing shows intelligence used as a channel of open threat.","gn":["the counsel gains the norm of secrecy that it deliberately inverts","the couplet gains a case where a king wants his purpose known, which marks the limit of its rule"]},{"a":"si:25:178-194","b":"pn:39","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 39 praises the Chola who made the Chera of the Himalaya-carved bow fall and withered வாடா வஞ்சி; the epic's king enters வாடா வஞ்சி and the drum boasts of the carved bow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epithets 'unwithering Vanchi' and 'the bow carved on the Himalaya' are the Chera line's own glory in the epic and the measure of a Chola victory in the Sangam poem, so the pairing shows one stock of Chera fame used by both its owners and its enemies.","gn":["the proclamation gains the knowledge that its boasts were stock epithets that rival poets also used","the poem's paradox of withering the unwithering city gains the Chera's own use of the same name for a city at the height of its power"]},{"a":"si:26:1-18","b":"si:26:247-254","type":"develops","note":"The vow to bring a stone for a god on the northern kings' heads is kept when the king takes the Himalayan stone after capturing Kanakan and Vijayan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The vow names only a god to be carved and a royal insult to be avenged, and the close names the stone as the pathini's, so the epic joins a war of royal honour to the worship of Kannaki only once the vow is kept."},{"a":"si:26:1-18","b":"pn:72","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are royal vows in the conditional form whose penalty is the king's own sceptre turning cruel to his people; the Chera's line almost repeats the Pandya's.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Pandya's vow names only his own subjects as the sufferers of a failed king, while the Chera's sets the choice out plainly, a sceptre that makes either foreign kings or his own people tremble, showing that the vow form took the king's power to terrify as fixed and staked the people's peace on a war abroad.","gn":["the Chera's penalty clause is shown to be the old vanjinam formula, குடிபழி தூற்றும் கோலேன் ஆகுக, carried almost word for word into குடிநடுக் குறூஉங் கோலேன் ஆகென","the formula gains a use against an insult reported from far away by ascetics rather than spoken to the king's face, so the vow answers a rumour of scorn"]},{"a":"si:26:32-53","b":"si:5:89-110","type":"same_image","note":"Both kings open a northern march by moving sword and umbrella at a chosen hour: the Chola with his drum to find foes for his arms, the Chera toward the Himalaya for a stone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Chola went north wanting only enemies for his shoulders, was stopped by the mountain and left his tiger carved on it, while the Chera goes with a cause, a pathini and an insult to Tamil, and carries a stone away from the mountain, so the epic's second northern march gives a moral reason to the rite the first performed for glory alone."},{"a":"si:26:93-104","b":"k:560","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says the six-duty men forget their books if the guardian does not guard; the sages charge the Chera to guard the Veda-keepers of the Himalaya, which lies outside his kingdom.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet ties the Brahmins' learning to their own king's protection, and the epic stretches that duty across the border, so that a king marching to punish northern kings is made guardian of the northern Brahmins, and he keeps the charge in victory (lines 248-250).","gn":["the sages' charge gains a reason: without a guardian the Veda-keepers' learning fails, so the Chera's protection keeps the northern Vedas alive","the couplet gains a case beyond the king's own realm, where the guardian of learning is an invader and the protection is owed to the enemy's Brahmins"]},{"a":"si:26:225-246","b":"pn:26","type":"develops","note":"Both cook a battlefield meal on hearths of crowned heads stirred with bangled arms (முடித்தலை அடுப்பு, தொடித்தோள் துடுப்பு), and both set it beside the king's care for Vedic Brahmins.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poem makes the battlefield cooking the Pandya's own rite beside his completed Vedic sacrifices, and the epic gives the cooking to demons who call the field an அறக்களம் and then has the king order the fire-keeping Brahmins protected, so the epic keeps the old pairing of war-rite and Vedic rite and adds the name of dharma to the slaughter.","gn":["the demons' feast is recognised as the formal battlefield sacrifice of the Pandya praise poem, not loose description of gore","the puram image gains a later life in which the cooks are demons and the field is named a field of dharma, showing the formula become a convention that could be given to any Tamil conqueror"]},{"a":"si:26:225-246","b":"k:586","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet's spy goes in the guise of renouncers to get in and see; the defeated northern kings put on the guise of ascetics and performers to get away.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet treats the renouncer's dress as a tool of the king's intelligence, and the epic shows the same dress used by kings themselves to escape a lost battle, so the guise that hides a spy's purpose also hides a king's shame, and it works because no one strikes a man who looks like a renouncer.","gn":["the kings' flight gains a known device, the renouncer's dress as cover for passing through hostile ground, rather than a chance escape","the couplet's technique gains a case where it is used not by a servant to enter but by the defeated to leave, turning a spy's skill into a mark of a king's disgrace"]},{"a":"si:26:247-254","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's second moral, that the great praise a renowned pathini, begins to be acted out when the Chera takes the stone for the pathini from the Himalaya.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patikam promises only that the high will praise the pathini, and this kaathai shows the praise has to be won by humbling northern kings, so the honour paid to Kannaki and the Chera's conquest are made one act."},{"a":"si:26:247-254","b":"pt:patikam:5","type":"develops","note":"The decade's preface says Senguttuvan went for the stone of the goddess pathini, defeated the Aryan lord and bathed the stone in the Ganga; the kaathai tells the expedition at length. The epic here does not name the king's parents, so the identity rests on name and deed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The preface compresses the campaign to a stone sought, one Aryan lord defeated and a bathing in the Ganga, while the epic gives the war a cause in an insult to Tamil, names two kings Kanakan and Vijayan, and brings the army over the Ganga on allies' boats, so the two show a shared tradition of the pathini's stone told with different enemies and routes.","gn":["the epic's campaign gains an independent notice in the Chera decade's preface, showing the stone for the pathini was part of the Chera king's remembered deeds","the preface's bare clause கடவுட் பத்தினிக் கற்கோள் gains its story: why the stone was sought, from whom it was taken, and that the god was Kannaki"]},{"a":"si:27:1-17","b":"pt:patikam:5","type":"develops","note":"The decade's preface lists among Senguttuvan's deeds that he sought the stone of the goddess-pathini (கடவுள் பத்தினிக் கற்கோள்) and bathed in the Ganga; the epic opens this kaathai with the same phrase and the same river.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the preface, the epic's நீர்ப்படை is seen to rest on a Chera tradition that already called the pathini a goddess and joined her stone to the Ganga, and the epic's contribution is the story that makes her one: who she was and why a king would march north for her.","gn":["the bathing gains outside witness: a Chera praise tradition that names the pathini's stone among the king's conquests","the bare line of the preface gains the woman behind the title and the reason the stone was wanted"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:27:43-56","b":"si:7:142-153","type":"develops","note":"Maadalan says Madhavi's seaside song crushed the crowns of Kanaka and Vijaya; the earlier unit is the moment she takes up the yazh in a sulk to answer Kovalan's songs.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The scene of a lovers' misreading of each other's songs on the beach turns out, in Maadalan's riddle, to be the first link in a chain that ends in a northern war, so the poem asks the reader to see a small private quarrel and a campaign as one sequence of cause."},{"a":"si:27:57-77","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"qualifies","note":"Kannaki told the Pandya that Kovalan came to Madurai driven by former deeds; here Madari cries that Kovalan was blameless and the king erred, and Maadalan puts the parting under karma.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Kannaki in her plea names both karma and the king's failure to inquire, and this report splits those causes between witnesses, the Brahmin naming karma and the herdswoman naming the king, so the epic keeps both explanations alive in the story's aftermath rather than letting either replace the other."},{"a":"si:27:57-77","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam promises that the deed of the past comes in wrath to be eaten and that the great praise a pathini; Maadalan uses the same verb (ஊழ்வினை உருத்தெழ) and says the woman has risen onto the crowns of the northern kings.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The patikam's morals are fulfilled here in a messenger's words: the praise of the high is made literal as kings carrying her stone on their crowns, and the wrathful deed is placed at a courtesan's song, a smaller and more human moment than the former-birth curse the patikam gives as its cause."},{"a":"si:27:78-95","b":"si:10:170-191","type":"qualifies","note":"The Charana taught Kavunthi that the strong deed comes back to face its doer; here she asks whether the couple's deed has risen against her too and dies by fasting.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The doctrine she heard says a deed returns to its own doer, but the nun who led the couple treats their deed as falling on her as their guide, so the epic shows a Jain ascetic acting on a shared responsibility that the Charana's rule of individual return does not name."},{"a":"si:27:78-95","b":"pn:65","type":"qualifies","note":"Both tell a death by refusing food: the Chera Perunjeralathan, ashamed of a wound in his back, sits facing north with his sword; Kavunthi, after the deaths of her wards, gives up life by the vow of not eating.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The fasting death that the Sangam poem shows as a warrior king's answer to shame is in the epic taken up by a Jain nun as an answer to failed guardianship, so the same act carries the honour code into the renouncer's vow.","gn":["Kavunthi's brief death gains a Tamil precedent that makes it read as an answer to dishonour, not only as grief","the king's death gains a counterpart outside the warrior class, showing the act could be chosen by a renouncer for a failure of care rather than of valour"]},{"a":"si:27:96-115","b":"n:53","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain says the best renounce home and wealth after slowly seeing that nothing lasts; here Kannaki's father and Madhavi renounce on hearing of deaths, and the two mothers simply die of the same news.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The quatrain's renunciation comes from calm reflection on impermanence, while in the epic it comes from the shock of bereavement and is only one of two outcomes of the same grief, so the pair shows the distance between the ethics book's ideal path to renunciation and the way the story's people actually reach it.","gn":["the epic's renunciations gain a standard by which they look sudden and grief-driven rather than reasoned","the quatrain's ideal gains a case showing that the same knowledge of loss can end in death as well as in renouncing"]},{"a":"si:27:116-137","b":"pt:patikam:5","type":"develops","note":"The preface says Senguttuvan, son of the Chola Manakkilli, felled Pazhaiyan's neem and made nine heirs of the Chola house fall at the gate; Maadalan praises him for destroying the nine umbrellas that would not bear his cousin Killi's young rule and for felling Pazhaiyan's neem.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the preface's nine Chola kin felled at the gate are given a cause in the epic, a war over the succession of a young Chola who was the Chera's cousin through his Chola mother, so the Sangam list of victories becomes a family quarrel among the crowned houses.","gn":["Maadalan's allusions gain the record of the same deeds and of the Chola mother that explains the word மைத்துனன்","the preface's bare list gains a reason for the war on the nine and a sense of why a Chera would fight in Chola affairs"]},{"a":"si:27:116-137","b":"k:550","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet likens a king's killing of the cruel to weeding the crop; the new Pandya kills a thousand goldsmiths in one day as an offering of lives to the pathini.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's punishment by death is of the cruel, one by one, as care for the crop, while the epic's new king kills a whole craft for one goldsmith's lie and calls it an offering, so the pair shows royal punishment sliding from justice into collective sacrifice to restore a king's standing.","gn":["Cheliyan's act gains a measure from the ethics of the sceptre against which its scale and its form as offering stand out","the couplet gains a case that asks who counts as the weed when a king must atone for a predecessor's wrong"]},{"a":"si:27:162-172","b":"pn:39","type":"develops","note":"Both recite the Chola line's ancestors, the king who entered the scale for the dove and the one who destroyed the hanging fort; the puram poem praises a Chola who withered the Chera's unwithering Vanchi, the epic has a Brahmin recite the same lineage in the Chera's own court.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The praise of the dove-king's line, which in the Sangam poem celebrates a Chola's victory over Vanchi, is spoken in the epic before the king of Vanchi as a pledge that the Chola's sceptre is sound, so the formula once used against the Chera now serves an alliance between cousins.","gn":["Maadalan's assurance gains its source: a fixed Chola praise in which the dove, the fort and the just sceptre go together","the puram formula gains a later use in which the rival it once mocked hears it as reassurance"]},{"a":"si:27:162-172","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"same_image","note":"Kannaki threw the Chola who relieved the bird's distress at the Pandya as a reproach; Maadalan offers the same ancestor to the Chera as proof that the Chola sceptre cannot bend.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The one exemplum works in two directions within the epic, as an accusation of a king who failed and as a guarantee for a king who has not been tested, which shows how the ancestor's deed stands in for an inquiry into the living king's justice."},{"a":"si:27:173-187","b":"pn:43","type":"same_image","note":"The Chola of the puram poem entered the scale with his own body to save a dove from a hawk; the Chera here gives the Brahmin gold measured against his own weight fifty times over, just after the Brahmin has praised that very dove-king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The scale in which a Chola ancestor weighed his flesh against a bird becomes in the Chera's court a scale in which the king's weight is matched in gold for a messenger, so the body-as-measure moves from self-sacrifice to royal gift.","gn":["the weighing gains the Tamil memory of the royal scale, which makes the gift an answer to Maadalan's praise of the dove-king","the dove story gains a later royal practice in which the king's body is weighed for giving, not for suffering"]},{"a":"si:27:225-239","b":"pn:392","type":"develops","note":"The bard of the puram poem hails Athiyaman as the ploughman who yokes white-mouthed donkeys on the bloody fields of captured forts and sows white varagu and horse-gram; the Chera's ploughmen sing their king as the donkey-plough ploughman who sowed kavadi on the northern kings' forts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The terror title that a Sangam bard gives a patron at his gate is in the epic sung by real ploughmen in their field song, beside their oxen's holiday, so the conqueror's mock ploughing of enemy ground is absorbed into the farming folk's festival of his return.","gn":["the ploughmen's epithet gains the rite behind it: ploughing a ruined fort with donkeys and sowing it with poor seed","the bard's title gains a later setting where the king's own ploughmen own it as a song of joy"]},{"a":"si:27:225-239","b":"pn:65","type":"tension","note":"When the Chera Perunjeralathan sits north to die, the ploughmen's cry is forgotten and the village festival forgotten; when Senguttuvan returns, the ploughmen's song and the bathing festival fill the land.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Holding the two Chera poems together shows the ploughmen's song as the land's own measure of its king, silenced by a king's shameful death and loud at a king's triumph, so the epic's return scene answers the Sangam lament with its exact reverse.","gn":["the field songs gain a Sangam frame in which the ploughmen's voice is a sign of the king's fortune","the lament gains its opposite case, showing what the forgotten songs were when the king lived and won"]},{"a":"si:28:84-95","b":"si:26:211-224","type":"develops","note":"The beaten Aryan kings fled the field disguised as matted, ash-smeared ascetics, singers and dancers; here the Chola names that disguise கொல்லாக் கோலம் and denies the Chera any glory for taking them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The ruse that saved the northern kings' lives in battle becomes, in the rival Tamil kings' mouths, the reason the Chera's triumph counts for nothing, so the escape and the taunt together show a victory spoiled by the vanquished's change of dress."},{"a":"si:28:84-95","b":"pn:9","type":"qualifies","note":"Puram 9 praises a Pandya who before battle bids cows, brahmin-natured men, women, the sick and the sonless take shelter; the Chola's dictum spares, after battle, men who have made themselves look like non-killers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The old dharma of war spares people by what they are, but the epic shows warriors claiming that shelter by what they wear, and a king using the rule not to restrain himself but to belittle another king's captives.","gn":["the Chola's taunt gains its ground: an old Tamil rule that war is not waged on those who do not fight","the poem's list gains a hard case it does not foresee, defeated fighters who step into the spared class by disguise"]},{"a":"si:28:110-122","b":"pt:patikam:5","type":"develops","note":"Maadalan's list of the king's victories runs close to the wording of the fifth decad's preface on Senguttuvan: சிறுகுரல் நெய்தல் வியலூர், இடும்பின் புறத்து இறுத்து, the nine kings beaten at the gate in standing battle, the sea driven back, the Aryan beaten at the Ganges.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The preface's bare record of conquests, which names the pathini's stone only as its occasion, becomes in the epic the praise a brahmin recites to cool the king's anger just before telling him that such conquests pass, so the same list serves first as glory and then as the premise of a sermon on impermanence.","gn":["the epic's list gains an independent Sangam witness for the same campaigns in nearly the same words","the preface's terse கடவுள் பத்தினிக் கற்கோள் gains the story of who the pathini was and what the stone became"]},{"a":"si:28:123-138","b":"si:26:247-254","type":"tension","note":"After the northern battle the Chera is hailed as one who made a field of dharma (அறக்களஞ் செய்தோன்) having finished the field of war; Maadalan tells him he has made only the war-field sacrifice and never the dharma-field one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic sets the battlefield hailed as a field of dharma against a brahmin's verdict that the same wars were sacrifice of the wrong field, so the king's highest praise and his counsellor's rebuke use the same two words to opposite ends."},{"a":"si:28:123-138","b":"pt:patikam:3","type":"develops","note":"Among the ancestors Maadalan cites are the king who took a four-Veda man's poem and sent him to the upper world, and (lines 144-146) the one who struck Agappa and bathed in Ayirai and the two seas; the third decad's preface and colophon give Palyanai Selkezhu Kuttuvan these feats and the brahmin poet's reward of heaven through sacrifices.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam tradition's sacrifice that sent a brahmin poet to heaven becomes Maadalan's precedent for the sacrifice he urges on the king, yet the same preface says that king went away to the forest, and the epic's counsellor leaves out the renunciation and keeps only the rite.","gn":["Maadalan's allusions gain the stories behind them: the poet Gautamanar, his reward and the king who gave it","the preface gains a later reading in which its king's sacrifice is remembered and his going to the forest is not"]},{"a":"si:28:123-138","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet ranks not killing one creature to eat above a thousand oblations; Maadalan ranks the sacrifice of dharma above the war-sacrifice in which the king has killed for fifty years.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together they make a three-step ranking that neither states: the Kural puts abstaining from killing above the Vedic rite, while the epic's brahmin puts the Vedic rite above the killing of war and offers it as the remedy for that killing.","gn":["Maadalan's remedy is shown to rest on a ranking the Kural itself would not grant","the couplet gains a case where the oblation is urged as the cure for killing, not as its rival"]},{"a":"si:28:139-154","b":"pn:363","type":"tension","note":"Both tell a king that the rulers before him, many and mighty, are all gone; Puram 363 concludes that he must renounce the whole sea-bounded world before death, Maadalan that he must perform a great sacrifice and rule for aeons.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same proof from dead kings yields opposite counsel: the Sangam poet's impermanence ends in giving up the kingdom, the epic's brahmin's in a rite that keeps the king on the throne, so the argument alone does not decide between renunciation and ritual.","gn":["Maadalan's sermon is seen to stop short of the conclusion the older poem draws from the same premise","the poem's call to renounce is set against a counsellor who uses its argument for a royal sacrifice"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:28:139-154","b":"si:0:1-11","type":"tension","note":"The patikam opens with the Chera prince Ilango, who gave up kingship and lives at the eastern gate; here a Chera king is told that body and wealth do not last and is led to a sacrifice, not to the forest.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Within one royal house the epic shows the two answers to impermanence, the prince who left the throne and the king who is counselled to stay on it and sacrifice, without saying which is right."},{"a":"si:28:155-170","b":"n:26","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar quatrain calls the life a dancer (கூத்தன்) who leaves the skin bag, after which the body's fate does not matter; Maadalan's soul is like dancers who do not stay in one costume but go by their deeds into the bodies of gods, men, beasts or hell-beings.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The quatrain uses the dancer to make the abandoned body worthless, while the epic follows the dancer to its next costume, which its deeds choose, so the same figure moves from indifference to the corpse to anxiety about the next birth.","gn":["Maadalan's simile gains a known ethical use of the dancer image to test against","the quatrain's dancer gains a destination: the epic says where the performer goes after leaving the costume"]},{"a":"si:28:155-170","b":"si:26:211-224","type":"same_image","note":"The beaten Aryan kings escaped as ascetics and as dancers (ஆடு கூத்தர்) in a changed guise (விச்சைக் கோலம்); Maadalan tells their conqueror that the soul too is like dancers who change their கோலம்.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The kings' change of costume to save their lives, mocked by the king's rivals, returns in the sermon as the image of every soul's passage from body to body, so the mockery of the disguised kings recoils on the mocker.","gr":2},{"a":"si:28:171-180","b":"si:27:173-187","type":"tension","note":"In the previous kaathai the king gave Maadalan gold to his own weight, counted as ஐயைந்து இரட்டி; here Maadalan says he is not one who seeks rich gifts.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The counsellor's disclaimer comes after the largest gift in the story, so the epic leaves the reader to weigh whether the sermon is disinterested, without itself saying so."},{"a":"si:28:171-180","b":"k:36","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says do aram without saying 'later'; Maadalan says if the king says 'we will do aram tomorrow', life may leave today, and the aram he means is the Vedic royal sacrifice.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic keeps the couplet's urgency but narrows aram from the whole of virtue to one rite performed by priests for a king, showing how the ethical maxim could be recruited for a particular religious practice.","gn":["Maadalan's urgency gains the authority of a general rule about not deferring virtue","the couplet's unspecified aram is shown filled with one school's content in a court setting"]},{"a":"si:28:207-221","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"qualifies","note":"The patikam's three morals are that dharma becomes death to kings who err, that the great praise a pathini, and that former deeds come to fruit; here the pathini gives three lessons, one to each crowned king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic's late summary turns the patikam's morals into lessons addressed to the three kings and drops karma for the Chera's fulfilled vow, so the story's close weighs kingship more than the former deed the patikam put among its causes."},{"a":"si:28:207-221","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet gives the wife who worships her husband power over the rain on her own; the ancients' saying quoted here makes the glory of famous women's chastity depend on kings doing justice.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's chastity commands nature unaided, but the epic's closing saying makes its public glory wait on the king's justice being tested, so the pathini's power is tied to a failure of the sceptre.","gn":["the saying is set against the best-known claim for a chaste wife's unaided power","the couplet gains a condition the Kural does not state: chastity's power shows itself through the state's justice or injustice"]},{"a":"si:28:207-221","b":"si:26:1-18","type":"develops","note":"The Chera vowed that unless he took a stone for the goddess on the heads of the northern kings his sword should be counted shameful; here the narrator says the pathini taught the western king that kings' anger does not cool until their vow is fulfilled.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Chera's vow, made in anger at reported scorn, is in the end credited to the pathini as one of her lessons, so the king's campaign is retold as her teaching rather than his own design."},{"a":"si:28:222-234","b":"pn:264","type":"develops","note":"Puram 264 tells of a hero-stone set up on gravelly ground, crowned with a red flower garland and peacock feathers, with the fallen man's name carved; the epic's kaathai named நடுகல் installs a stone from the Himalaya as a jewelled image with priests and daily rites.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic takes the hero-stone rite, raised by herdsmen for a fallen fighter, and gives it to a woman whose power is chastity, enlarging it with Brahmin sacrifice and a temple, so the old rite of the warrior becomes the founding of a goddess's cult.","gn":["the kaathai's title gains its source in the rite it adapts: stone, garland, offering","the poem's simple rite is shown transformed into temple worship of a pathini"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:29:1-20","b":"pt:patikam:5","type":"develops","note":"The decade preface on Senguttuvan, son of Nedunjeralathan by the Chola Manakkilli, says in one phrase that he went north wanting a stone for the goddess pathini and bathed it in the Ganga; the epic's prose gives the same parentage and campaign, and adds the Aryan kings' taunt and the stone carried on their heads.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two show the Sangam colophon tradition already knew a Chera king's northern stone for a pathini, and the epic turns that bare record into a cause and a ritual: the stone becomes a goddess only after it is won by humbling the kings who mocked Tamil kingship.","gn":["the epic's prose gains an outside witness for the king's parentage and his stone-seeking campaign, so the cult is set in remembered Chera history","the preface's terse phrase கடவுள் பத்தினிக் கற்கோள் gains the story of whose stone it was and why the king went for it"]},{"a":"si:29:1-20","b":"pt:patikam:2","type":"qualifies","note":"The second decade's preface credits Imayavaramban Nedunjeralathan with carving the bow on the Himalaya; the epic's Aryan kings recall the day bow, tiger and fish were all carved there, and say no such kings are left.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic widens one Chera's boast into the shared emblem of the three Tamil crowns and makes the memory a reproach, so the son's campaign is shown as the redeeming of a glory the north said had lapsed.","gn":["the taunt's 'day the bow was carved' gains a named Chera, Senguttuvan's own father, as its author","the preface's single deed gains a later afterlife as a standard that northern kings could throw back at his heirs"]},{"a":"si:29:21-36","b":"si:23:161-176","type":"develops","note":"Neeli's curse in the former birth is the deed the prose says rose up against Kovalan (வினையுருத்து); the prose also names the goldsmith and the king.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The retelling at Vanchi keeps the goddess's karmic explanation but sets it beside the petty man and the king as agents, and beside Kannaki's reviling of dharma, so the karmic cause is told without excusing the human ones."},{"a":"si:29:53-57","b":"si:9:55-66","type":"qualifies","note":"In the dream kaathai Devanthi read Kannaki's dream as the fruit of a vow broken in a former birth and prescribed a bath in the tanks; here she says she heard the dream and did not grasp what it would bring.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend who once explained the dream by past karma and a ritual remedy now confesses she misread it, so her earlier karmic diagnosis is shown to have been the wrong kind of answer to a warning of the future."},{"a":"si:29:58-63","b":"n:53","type":"qualifies","note":"The quatrain says the best renounce without delay after slowly considering that home, youth, wealth and strength do not last; Maasaathuvan gives his wealth away and renounces only when the news of his son's death makes life seem death.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair separates renunciation reached by reflection from renunciation forced by bereavement: the epic's fathers come to the quatrain's act by the road the quatrain hopes to spare, after the loss has already happened.","gn":["the father's turning gains a measure: it is the right act done late, under grief rather than insight","the quatrain's counsel gains a case showing what the delay it warns against costs, a house emptied before its master leaves it"]},{"a":"si:29:69-73","b":"si:15:149-162","type":"tension","note":"Kavunthi told Maadhari that a charge held for the ascetics, however small, yields very great joy; Maadhari, who could not guard that charge, gave up her life.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The promise of joy from keeping a trust is reversed into death from failing it, and the failure was not hers: the pair shows the epic laying on a guardian the full weight of a duty that the king's act made impossible to keep."},{"a":"si:29:78-83","b":"si:20:34-54","type":"tension","note":"At the palace gate Kannaki called the Pandya a king of ruined sense who strayed from right rule (இறைமுறை பிழைத்தோன்); in the sky she calls him faultless (தீதிலன்) and herself his daughter.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same speaker gives opposite verdicts on the same king, and the epic means both: the turn shows that in its eyes the king's death of his own sentence has cancelled the fault she rightly charged him with."},{"a":"si:29:78-83","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"develops","note":"The king's own sentence, யானோ அரசன் யானே கள்வன், and his death are what Kannaki now rewards by declaring him Indra's guest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the scenes show the self-condemnation as the act that wins the king heaven: the confession that he was the thief is what makes him faultless in the goddess's word."},{"a":"si:29:84-92","b":"k:556","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says a ruler endures by just rule and without it his lustre does not endure; the Vanchi women sing that Pandyas do not live once the sceptre bends, and praise the Pandya for that.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic grants the couplet's link between a bent sceptre and the king's end but reverses its outcome for fame: the Pandya's dying of his own injustice is exactly what makes his line's glory endure and be sung by a rival kingdom.","gn":["the women's saying gains a general rule behind it, so the Pandya's death reads as the law of kingship and not a private collapse","the couplet gains a case where losing life for a bent sceptre restores the lustre it says is lost, a limit on its simple equation"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:29:97-100","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"qualifies","note":"The patikam's morals are that dharma becomes death to those who err in kingship and that an old deed comes to make its doer eat its fruit; the blessing names the old deed as the cause of her suffering and hails the king dharma killed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The closing blessing keeps both of the patikam's causes but changes the moral's tone: the king to whom dharma became death is not left as a warning but praised with his whole line, because he died of it.","gr":2},{"a":"si:29:101-104","b":"si:29:37-41","type":"same_image","note":"Devanthi's friend born on the Himalaya and bathed in the Ganga and the blessing's doll born of the mountain king are the same Himalaya stone, spoken of as Kannaki.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Himalaya origin is given to Kannaki twice, once in a friend's grief and once in a royal blessing, and the second, with its echo of the mountain king's daughter, shows the woman of Puhar being absorbed into the figure of the great goddess."},{"a":"si:29:108-112","b":"pn:39","type":"develops","note":"The Puram poem tells Killivalavan that giving, conquest and justice are not his own fame, since his line already holds the king who entered the scale for the dove and the ancestors who destroyed the hanging fort; the ammanai answers its riddle with the Chola who destroyed the hanging forts and names no living Chola.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the Puram poet's warning that the ancestors' deeds swallow a living Chola's fame comes true in the epic, where Chola praise sung at the Chera court consists of nothing but those ancestral deeds and the city of Puhar.","gn":["the riddle's answer gains its old standing: the hanging fort was already the Chola line's inherited title in the Sangam praise of a named king","the Puram poem's claim that ancestral glory outshines the present king gains a later case in which the line is remembered only by it, in a rival kingdom's women's game"]},{"a":"si:29:113-117","b":"si:20:55-73","type":"same_image","note":"Kannaki before the Pandya recalls the Chola who relieved a bird's distress and the one who crushed his son under the wheel for a cow, to accuse a king who did not inquire; the ammanai sings the dove-king and the cow's justice as the glory of Puhar, after Kannaki has declared the Pandya blameless.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same two Chola precedents change use across the story: in Madurai they are Kannaki's weapon against a Pandya, and in Vanchi, once she has cleared him, they are a girls' refrain beside ball songs hailing that same Pandya, so the standard of justice survives the verdict while losing its edge."},{"a":"si:29:118-122","b":"si:5:89-110","type":"same_image","note":"The Puhar book tells how Thirumavalavan went north, was stopped by the Himalaya and carved his tiger on it; the ammanai sings a king who stamped his tiger on the northern mountain while the eight mountains of the quarters watched, and ruled all eight directions under his umbrella.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set beside the narrative, the song shows the epic's praise turning a halt into a triumph: the mountain that ended Valavan's march becomes a witness, and one carving on one peak becomes rule of the eight quarters."},{"a":"si:29:129-132","b":"si:29:78-83","type":"develops","note":"Kannaki, appearing in the sky to Senguttuvan, says the Southerner is blameless and has become a guest in the palace of the king of the gods, and that she is his daughter; the ball songs that follow hail the Southerner with the gods' necklace at every stroke.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The ball songs are the public form of Kannaki's acquittal: only because she has called the Pandya blameless can the women of the Chera city hail the king who killed her husband, and his old title of the gods' necklace now reads as his place in Indra's hall."},{"a":"si:29:141-145","b":"pt:20","type":"develops","note":"The Patitruppattu poem names Neduncheralathan as the king who went against his foes on an island in the sea and cut down the kadamba at its root (கடம்புமுதல் தடிந்த); the swing song sings the guardian who cut the kadamba at its root (கடம்பு முதல் தடிந்த) and the emblem of the bow, and names no king.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image","te":"The pair shows the Patitruppattu's title for one Chera king passing into the epic as an unnamed dynastic refrain, so that in Senguttuvan's court a deed the Sangam decade credits to Neduncheralathan is sung as the house's own.","gn":["the swing song's unnamed guardian gains an identity: the phrase is the title of Neduncheralathan in the Chera court poetry","the Patitruppattu's title gains evidence of its afterlife, as a stock phrase of the Chera line sung by women at play rather than a bard's praise of one patron"]},{"a":"si:29:146-150","b":"pn:2","type":"qualifies","note":"Murinjiyur Mudinagarayar praises Uthiyan Cheralathan as the one who gave the great feast without limit while the Five and the hundred fought until the hundred fell; the swing song gives the feast at the war of the Five and the twice fifty to the Chera, Poraiyan, Malaiyan, by house names only, and pairs it with the kadamba deed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Puram poem binds the feast to one named king, while the epic gives it to the Chera house in general and sets it beside another king's deed, so the pair shows a single patron's distinction becoming an heirloom any Chera can be sung by.","gn":["the swing song's line gains its source and the king it belonged to, Uthiyan Cheralathan, whom it does not name","the Puram poem's claim gains a measure of its fame: generations later it is still the Chera line's mark, though detached from the king it praised"]},{"a":"si:29:151-155","b":"si:17:1-10","type":"tension","note":"The Madurai herdswomen's book opens on the Pandya whose fish is written on the brow of the Himalaya with the tiger and the bow written beside it; the Vanchi swing song sings the king of the bow who holds the fish and the tiger and rules from the Yavana land to Kumari.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The two songs claim the same three emblems for different kings: Madurai sets the Pandya's fish first with the Chola and Chera signs beside it, and Vanchi folds fish and tiger into the Chera's bow, so the epic's praise of the three crowns is a set of rival boasts that its songs leave standing side by side."},{"a":"si:29:171-174","b":"k:2","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet asks what learning is worth to those who do not bow at the good feet (நற்றாள் தொழாஅர்) of the one of pure knowledge; the close says kings who do not bow at the good feet (நல் தாள் தொழார்) of Poraiyan find it hard to live, and Kannaki too praises Senguttuvan.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The close moves the Kural's phrase for bowing to the god's feet onto a living Chera king and makes the penalty for not bowing the loss of life rather than of learning's fruit, while the deified Kannaki joins the praise, so the epic ends by turning a devotional formula into a political one.","gn":["the closing boast gains the weight of the formula it borrows: bowing at the good feet is the Kural's posture before the god","the couplet's phrase gains a case of its wording transferred from the god to a king, which shows how its language of worship could be turned to royal praise"]},{"a":"si:30:24-34","b":"si:15:95-106","type":"develops","note":"Kovalan's midnight dream at Madurai has Kama casting his flower arrows on the bare ground and Madhavi giving Manimekalai before the dharma-one of the Bodhi; in the last kaathai Devanthi reports both as done, Kama flinging his bow and arrows on bare ground as Madhavi gives her daughter at the Bodhi.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The dream Kovalan feared as a sign of coming harm is fulfilled after his death in its other half, his daughter's renunciation, so the poem treats the dream as true in every part and lets the bad omen and the good one come out of a single night."},{"a":"si:30:65-91","b":"si:9:29-44","type":"develops","note":"In the dream kaathai Saathan, having grown up as Maalathi's child and married Devanthi, shows her his ageless youth, tells her to come to his shrine and leaves, and she keeps worship there to win him back; in the last kaathai Maadalan retells this and the god returns by possessing her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Devanthi's long worship to get her husband back is answered, but not as she asked: the god returns as a voice that takes over her body to reveal others' former births, so the husband she waited for comes back only as an oracle."},{"a":"si:30:112-131","b":"si:15:42-53","type":"develops","note":"Maadalan once praised Kovalan as the compassionate hero who entered the rogue elephant's trunk to free an old Brahmin; in the last kaathai Maadalan says that this man, who entered the elephant's trunk to end the Brahmin's distress, has won the form of the gods.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem names one deed of Kovalan's, the elephant rescue and not his life with Madhavi or his wasting of wealth, as the deed whose fruit is his heavenly form, so the man the story often blames is judged at the end by his single act of risking his life for a stranger.","gr":2},{"a":"si:30:92-111","b":"mm:27:277-283","type":"tension","note":"In the Manimekalai the materialist answers the question whether some have known a former birth by saying that the minds of those deluded by a god or by dreams are changed and such knowledge is mere doubt; in the last kaathai of the Silappathikaram former births are shown through a god possessing a woman and a sprinkled water, before a king's whole court.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The materialist's objection names exactly the means the Silappathikaram uses to prove former birth, a god's possession, so the sister epic records the reply a sceptic would make to this scene, and the scene offers as public proof what that sceptic rejects as the delusion of the possessed.","gr":2},{"a":"si:30:132-146","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"develops","note":"The patikam's third moral is that the deed of the past comes in wrath and makes one eat its fruit; the last kaathai states the law of births in full: the good reach the golden world, the loving go where their attachment leads, the fruits of virtue and wrong both ripen, and the born die and the dead are born.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem opens with karma as a vengeance that falls on Kovalan and closes with karma as an even law that also rewards and that lets love carry the mothers to rebirth beside the pathini, so the frame turns the threat of the opening into a consolation by the end.","gr":2},{"a":"si:30:132-146","b":"k:347","type":"qualifies","note":"The couplet says griefs that never let go hold those who hold attachment and do not let go; Maadalan says those whose hearts are full of love go where their attachment leads, and the mothers, held by love for Kannaki, are reborn near her temple.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The epic agrees with the couplet that attachment binds one to birth, but shows what the couplet does not: a bond of love that leads to a new birth beside the one loved, told without blame, so attachment's hold is the cost the mothers pay for staying near the pathini rather than only a grief.","gn":["the epic's phrase பற்றுவழிச் சேறல் gains from the couplet the warning that the same hold is what keeps a soul from release, which Maadalan's calm statement does not say","the couplet gains a case of attachment that is love for a virtuous woman and whose fruit, a rebirth near her, is told as a mercy, which tests its flat equation of holding with grief"]},{"a":"si:30:132-146","b":"s:300","type":"qualifies","note":"Pattinathar's verse begins its list of the world's reversals with the born dying and the dead being born and turns from them to Siva, who frees from coming and going; Maadalan says the born die and the dead are born and calls it nothing new, the ancient way of life.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same saying about the born and the dead serves the epic as a reassurance that the order of deeds holds and the Siddhar as a reason to loathe that order and seek Siva, so the line itself carries no verdict and its meaning depends on whether release or a good rebirth is the goal.","gn":["the epic's law gains from Pattinathar the reminder that a round of births ordered by deeds is still a round, so the mothers' reunion is not release","the Siddhar's formula gains a setting where it is said without disgust, as the Brahmin's comfort to a king, which shows that its horror in the Siddhar comes from his frame and not from the saying"]},{"a":"si:30:147-164","b":"pt:patikam:5","type":"develops","note":"The preface to the Patitruppattu's fifth decade says Senguttuvan, seeking a stone for the goddess-pathini, went through the forests, defeated the Aryan lord and bathed it in the Ganga; the last kaathai has the Chera endow the pathini's temple and the freed Aryan kings and Gajabahu of Lanka beg her to come to their lands.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam preface records the pathini stone as one deed among a king's conquests; the epic carries the story past the conquest, to the defeated kings themselves asking for the goddess, so the war for the stone becomes the way her worship spreads beyond the Chera land.","gn":["the epic's scene gains from the preface an independent record that a Chera named Senguttuvan was remembered for fetching a pathini's stone from the north, though the preface itself is late","the preface's single phrase about the pathini gains the cult that the epic says grew from it: a temple, a daily festival and a boon asked by foreign kings"],"gr":2},{"a":"si:30:165-185","b":"si:0:1-11","type":"develops","note":"The patikam opens with Ilango living at the eastern-gate shrine after giving up kingship; in the last kaathai the goddess tells why: when a man said he bore the mark of a future king, he looked at him in anger and put away the earth's burden to end Senguttuvan's distress.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poet's renunciation, stated without cause in the first line of the poem, is explained only in its last unit and by the goddess herself, so the poem's frame makes the poet's giving up of a throne one more past act whose meaning the pathini reveals, like the former births of the mothers."},{"a":"si:30:186-202","b":"n:20","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar quatrain, in its chapter on youth not lasting, says that since merciless Death roams looking for his people one should take provisions for the journey in good time; the epic's last lines say youth, wealth and body do not last and bid the hearers seek a true companion for the land they will go to.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The epic folds the Naladiyar's three opening themes, wealth, youth and body not lasting, into one line and draws the same conclusion of provision for the journey, but sets it after a list of concrete deeds, so the provision is named as truthfulness, not killing, giving and the rest rather than left as a figure.","gn":["the epic's closing line gains from the quatrain the image of Death as a hunter who takes even the unborn, which is the urgency behind உளநாள் வரையாது","the quatrain's undefined provisions (தோட்கோப்பு) gain a list of the deeds that make them up, given just before in the epic's commands"]},{"a":"si:30:186-202","b":"k:36","type":"develops","note":"The couplet bids one do virtue without putting it off, because when one perishes it is the companion that does not perish; the epic bids its hearers not wait on their remaining days but seek the true companion for the land they will go to.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read with the couplet, the epic's உறுதுணை is aram, the Kural's undying companion, and the epic adds that the companion must be sought before youth, wealth and body fail, after a story in which the dead carried only their deeds and loves into their next births.","gn":["the epic's unnamed companion gains its identity from the couplet: it is virtue, not a person or a god","the couplet's companion at the hour of perishing gains a destination, the land one goes to, which the epic has just shown as a new birth shaped by deeds and attachments"]},{"a":"si:30:186-202","b":"si:15:76-94","type":"develops","note":"Maadalan told how a poor man who bore false witness against a chaste wife was caught in the noose of the devouring spirit, which would not take Kovalan's life in his place; among the poem's closing commands is never to bear false witness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The command against false witness is the one rule in the final list that the poem had already shown punished on stage, by a spirit that refused even a willing substitute, so the bare imperative carries the weight of a remembered scene in which no one could pay another's penalty."},{"a":"si:vanchi_kandam_katturai:1-15","b":"si:25:122-136","type":"develops","note":"Senguttuvan rejects a stone from the Podiyil hill bathed in the Kaveri as unworthy of those born in his line of valour, and resolves that if the Himalaya's king will not give a stone to carve the great Pattini goddess, he will march north; the summary views the book as one with the king whose army fought with valour fitted to aram and reached the Ganga.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's own reason for the northern war was the honour of his line of valour, with the goddess's stone as its occasion, and the summary keeps exactly that reason and drops the occasion, so the book that began with worship for Kannaki is remembered as a Chera conquest called righteous."},{"a":"si:vanchi_kandam_katturai:1-15","b":"si:madurai_kandam_katturai:1-20","type":"tension","note":"Both summaries open with the same frame (aram, maram, the old city, festivals, the gods' visits, the land's wealth); the Madurai summary keeps the river and the unfailing sky and ends with the Pandya who lay dead on his throne-couch with his chaste queen, the Vanchi summary drops river and sky and ends with Senguttuvan's army at the Ganga, adding valour fitted to aram.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The shared frame lets the difference show: the Pandya book is summed up in a king undone by a wrong verdict, the Chera book in a king whose war is declared righteous, so the epic's last word on aram passes from the court of justice to the battlefield, and neither summary names the woman both books turn on."},{"a":"si:vanchi_kandam_katturai:1-15","b":"pt:patikam:5","type":"develops","note":"The preface to the fifth decad names Senguttuvan, who drove back the sea, as the king who went north to take a stone for the goddess Pattini, defeated the Aryan lord and bathed it in the Ganga; the epic's summary names him by the same epithet (கடல்பிறக் கோட்டி) and the same Ganga, and says nothing of the stone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The decad's preface and the epic's summary give the same king the same two marks, the sea driven back and the Ganga reached, but only the Sangam preface keeps the Pattini stone as the purpose of the march, so the epic, which tells the stone's story at length, lets its own summary remember the expedition as the preface's bare royal conquest.","gn":["the summary's epithet gains its source: கடல்பிறக் கோட்டி is the name under which a Patitruppattu decad was sung for this king, which shows the summary closing the book in the older praise-poets' terms","the preface's brief clause on the Pattini stone gains the epic's full account of why and for whom the stone was sought, and a case where the epic's own closing formula leaves that clause out"]},{"a":"si:nool_katturai:1-18","b":"si:0:55-68","type":"qualifies","note":"The patikam's opening frame names three morals the poem will show (dharma as death to the erring king, the praise of the pathini, the past deed that comes to fruit) and calls the poem a பாட்டுடைச் செய்யுள்; the closing summary describes the finished work by its land, its akam and puram matter, its three aims and its music, dance and song forms, and repeats none of the morals.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two frames of the epic describe it in different terms, the opening as a story built to prove three moral claims and the close as a compendium of Tamil poetics, music and dance, so the work presents itself at the start as an argument and at the end as an art, and the morals are left for the story itself to carry."},{"a":"si:nool_katturai:1-18","b":"mm:0:89-98","type":"develops","note":"The Silappathikaram's last line calls it the work whose story's matter is completed in Manimekalai; the Manimekalai patikam closes by saying that Saathan set out Manimekalai's renunciation (மணிமேகலை துறவு) in thirty songs, with Ilango hearing it, reversing the Silappathikaram patikam, where Ilango sang and Saathan heard.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Each work's frame names the other's author as its listener, and the Silappathikaram declares its own story finished only in a sequel whose subject is a daughter's renunciation, so the pair presents the two epics as one story whose tale of justice and chastity reaches its end in the turn from household life to the robe.","gr":2},{"a":"vp:1","b":"n:34","type":"develops","note":"Both call the human body rare (அரிய, அரும்பெறல் யாக்கை); the Valayapathi derives the rarity from karma's long series of bodies, the Naladiyar draws the duty of pressing virtue from it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's command to use the rare body gains its urgency from the fragment's account of how many karma-driven bodies come before a human one, and the fragment's second rarity, wealth to enjoy, is what the quatrain says to turn into virtue instead.","gn":["the fragment's statement of rarity gets the practical conclusion it leaves unsaid","the quatrain's bare 'rare body' is given its cause in the cycle of deeds"]},{"a":"vp:3","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Both say rain falls at the chaste wife's word; the Kural makes it her one power, the Valayapathi sets it in a list of honours with the praise of country and town and credits it to what the learned of Koodal say.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read beside the fragment, the Kural's single astonishing claim becomes an item of public reputation, cited on authority, so the rain is treated as the community's recognition of the wife rather than her private power.","gn":["the fragment's rain-claim is anchored to the couplet that first stated it","the couplet's claim is seen being received as a cited saying and folded into public honour"]},{"a":"vp:3","b":"vp:4","type":"tension","note":"Verse 3 gives the chaste wife public praise and power over rain; verse 4 says any garlanded woman's heart turns to a new man even if she has the love god.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The surviving fragments hold both the highest claim for a wife's chastity and a denial that any woman's heart stays put, so either the lost story set them in different mouths or it made chastity a rare exception; the conflict depends on reading verse 4 as about all women.","gr":3},{"a":"vp:4","b":"n:370","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the fresh flood (புதுப்புனல், வெள்ளம் புதியது) for women's love; in the Naladiyar it is the courtesan's love that dries when the money stops, in the Valayapathi it is the new water the fish leave the old pond for.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The image changes its target: the Naladiyar confines the fickle flood to women whose love is bought, while the fragment turns it on any woman, even one with a perfect lover, so the charge moves from the market to the marriage.","gn":["the fragment's fish-and-flood image is seen as a reworking of an existing figure of fickle love","the quatrain's limitation of the charge to paid women stands out against a text that drops it"]},{"a":"vp:5","b":"k:57","type":"qualifies","note":"Both deny that women can be kept by outward guarding; the Kural moves the guard inside the woman (நிறைகாக்கும் காப்பு), the Valayapathi says the learned found no guard at all.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The two share a premise, that confinement fails, and part on the conclusion: the Kural trusts the woman's own steadiness, the fragment trusts nothing, which shows that the Kural's inner guard is a choice and not the only way to read the failure of walls.","gn":["the fragment's bleak verdict is shown to reject an available answer rather than to lack one","the couplet's faith in the woman's own guard is set off by a text that refuses it"],"gr":2},{"a":"vp:7","b":"n:362","type":"qualifies","note":"Both make women's faultless time short; the Naladiyar says even under a guard of swords, the Valayapathi says only while the man who cherishes them is watching.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On whether watching helps, the two disagree: for the fragment, the provider's gaze buys a brief steadiness, while for the quatrain even armed guard does not, so the fragment's distrust is milder in what it allows surveillance to achieve.","gn":["the fragment's raindrop image is placed in a tradition of harsh sayings and measured against it","the quatrain's claim that guarding fails is shown to be stronger than a parallel text's"]},{"a":"vp:8","b":"k:61","type":"qualifies","note":"Both prize children; the Kural ranks children who come to understanding above all gains, the Valayapathi makes a child the thing without which wealth is like a garland without scent.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows two grounds for the same praise: the Kural values the child for its own understanding, the fragment for completing a householder's estate, so the fragment's child is an ornament of wealth and the Kural's a good in itself.","gn":["the fragment's list is seen to value the child only in relation to wealth","the couplet's condition அறிவறிந்த stands out as what separates its praise from the estate view"],"gr":2},{"a":"vp:9","b":"k:127","type":"qualifies","note":"Both single out the tongue as what must be guarded above all; the Kural's tongue is speech, the Valayapathi counts it among the five senses and makes it lead to hell.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Placing the tongue among the senses lets the fragment's warning cover taste as well as speech, so the Kural's rule about words is widened into a rule about appetite, which the fragments' verses on flesh then fill.","gn":["the fragment's ranking of the tongue is anchored to a well-known rule","the couplet's tongue of speech is shown being reread as the tongue of taste too"],"gr":2},{"a":"vp:9","b":"vp:19","type":"develops","note":"Verse 9 says the tongue, among the senses, can send one to the vile hell; verse 19 commands not to eat flesh from craving and promises a good birth.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Together the two give the tongue's danger a concrete content in these fragments: the craving tongue is the flesh-eating one, and guarding it decides between hell and a spotless rebirth.","gr":2},{"a":"vp:13","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"Both put not killing above ritual merit; the Kural says abstaining from one killing is better than a thousand oblations, the Valayapathi refuses any dharma that comes with killing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural weighs non-killing against sacrifice and finds it heavier, while the fragment denies that a killing rite is dharma at all, so the pair shows the step from ranking merits to disqualifying a kind of virtue.","gn":["the fragment's flat refusal is seen as a sharpening of an older comparison","the couplet's comparison is shown to stop short of the verdict a later text draws"]},{"a":"vp:14","b":"t:252","type":"qualifies","note":"Both speak of a handful or mouthful of food set aside as a gift; Tirumular says anyone can give it, the Valayapathi says no gift equals it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same small gift is easy in the Tirumanthiram and supreme in the fragment, and held together they say that the gift no one can excuse himself from is also the one no greater gift surpasses.","gn":["the fragment's obscure gesture of a finger set on a mouthful is matched to a known practice of setting food aside before eating","the Saiva verse's minimum gift gains a claim to the highest rank among gifts"]},{"a":"vp:16","b":"k:241","type":"develops","note":"Both set the wealth of grace (அருள்) above wealth of goods; the Kural argues from distribution (goods are found even among the base), the Valayapathi commands that grace be made the wealth and ties it to escape from darkness hereafter.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the reason grace outranks goods, and the fragment gives the practice and its fruit, turning a valuation into a command with an afterlife reward the couplet does not mention.","gn":["the fragment's command gets the argument that justifies its ranking","the couplet's ranking is shown carried into a promise about the next world"]},{"a":"vp:17","b":"k:256","type":"develops","note":"Both trace meat back to a price: the Kural says no one would sell flesh if no one killed for eating, the Valayapathi says the eater's craving sets a price at which the trapper kills and sells.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the buyer answerable for the seller's killing; the fragment adds the trapper's own interest in more deaths, so the guilt runs both ways through the one price.","gn":["the fragment's hard lines are clarified by the couplet's plainer argument","the couplet's one-way blame on the buyer is extended to the seller's motive"],"gr":2},{"a":"vp:17","b":"nk:9","type":"develops","note":"The Valayapathi argues against flesh-eating in a sermon with no opponent; the Neelakesi announces the view that accepts flesh (ஊன்கொண்ட காட்சி) as the first matter its heroine will debate.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Among the Jain-tradition epics the same ethic appears in two forms, as a plain moral exhortation in the fragment and as a doctrine to be defended against a named view in the Neelakesi, which shows meat-eating treated as a dividing line between schools and not only as a private fault.","gr":2},{"a":"vp:18","b":"n:121","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar makes the flesh-eater's belly a burning ground for beasts and birds; the Valayapathi, through rebirth, makes the beasts eaten the eater's own kin.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Put together, the eater's belly becomes a burning ground for his own relatives: the fragment supplies the kinship the quatrain's image lacks, and the quatrain supplies the funeral the fragment's argument implies.","gn":["the fragment's kinship argument gains a vivid image of where the kin end up","the quatrain's burning-ground image gains a reason why the dead in it are mourned as one's own"]},{"a":"vp:20","b":"t:272","type":"tension","note":"The Valayapathi says hard penance fit for grace leads to the dark-free state of no return; Tirumular says even burning one's bones and frying one's flesh does not reach the Lord without love that melts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two disagree on whether one's own austerity reaches the goal: the fragment's release needs no god and comes by penance, while the Tirumanthiram makes the most extreme penance worthless without love for the Lord; the conflict holds if the fragment's தவம் is read as sufficient by itself.","gn":["the fragment's godless route to release is set against the strongest Saiva denial of it","Tirumular's dismissal of austerity is seen answering a real rival claim, not an abstract one"]},{"a":"vp:23","b":"k:1133","type":"qualifies","note":"Both tell of a man whom desire strips of shame and standing; the Kural's lover says it in his own voice as a loss of shame and manliness, the Valayapathi condemns it from outside as blame and stained fame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same loss is a lover's plea in the Kural and a moralist's charge in the fragment, so the pair shows the madal lover judged two ways: as one whose suffering should move his beloved, and as one whose reputation is ruined.","gn":["the fragment's censure is set against the lover's own account it ignores","the couplet's inventory of loss is shown as it looks to the society the lover defies"]},{"a":"vp:24","b":"ku:17","type":"qualifies","note":"The fragment takes over Kuruntokai 17's palm-horse, erukku garland, jeering street and heartwood-hard love, and adds baseness, possession and disease.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same four acts that the Sangam lover names as love's compulsion, in his own voice, become in a renunciant work the symptoms of a sickness to be avoided: the change of speaker is what changes the verdict.","gn":["the source of its catalogue, which shows that the 'disease' was once a lover's threat to win his woman","an outsider's moral reading of its acts, which the lover's own speech never gives"]},{"a":"vp:26","b":"vp:5","type":"develops","note":"Verse 5 says the knowing found no guard for women though food, wealth and learning can be guarded; verse 26 says no art manages them as kings and snakes are managed, so the wise let them go.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two verses carry the claim from 'women cannot be kept' to its renunciant conclusion, 'so give them up'."},{"a":"vp:27","b":"si:20:74-91","type":"qualifies","note":"The fragment states that a thief's fruit is to be seized and cut; the Pandya tells Kannaki that killing a thief is no cruel sceptre, then finds he heeded a goldsmith and was himself the thief.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rule that theft earns the king's punishment holds only when the thief is truly known; the epic shows the same rule, unexamined, turning the punisher into the criminal."},{"a":"vp:28","b":"k:297","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 297 says unbroken truthfulness makes all other virtues unnecessary; verse 28 pairs giving up falsehood with practising dharma and promises a painless world for both.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Truth can be held as the whole of virtue or as one ornament worn with the rest; the fragment takes the second view and pays for it with a promise of the next world that the Kural does not make.","gn":["the Kural's sharper claim, against which its pairing of truth and dharma shows as a choice","a reward named for the truthful, where the couplet only ranks virtues"]},{"a":"vp:30","b":"k:330","type":"develops","note":"Both rest on what the knowing say (என்ப; கூறினர் அறிந்தார்): Kural 330 reasons from a wretched life back to killing; verse 30 names the four states of birth as killing's issue.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The fragment supplies the cosmology, the Jain four states of birth, that the couplet's backward inference from suffering to killing needs but never states.","gn":["a statement of the harsh inference its rule implies: that the wretched now are killers before","an explicit rebirth scheme in place of its bare 'they say'"]},{"a":"vp:33","b":"vp:44","type":"tension","note":"Verse 33 says poverty is disgrace and wealth fear, so those who never approach wealth are best; verse 44 asks what shame there is in crossing the wasteland for wealth that feeds one's kin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set side by side by the anthology's editor, the two answer the same question opposite ways: whether the duty to feed kin makes the pursuit of wealth honourable or only another snare."},{"a":"vp:34","b":"n:2","type":"qualifies","note":"Both start from wealth that will not stay; Naladiyar 2 says share it at once, since it turns like a wheel, while verse 34 praises those who never wanted it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The fleetingness of wealth supports two conclusions, the householder's 'give it while you have it' and the renouncer's 'never take it up', and the fragment chooses the more radical one.","gn":["the householder's alternative, against which its refusal is seen as a choice","the renouncer's conclusion from its own premise"],"gr":2},{"a":"vp:35","b":"k:247","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 247 takes as plain fact that this world is not for those without wealth; verse 35 grants that without wealth there is no living but adds that with it base men kill, and that it fails both this world and the next.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The fragment accepts the Kural's premise and then denies it its consolation: wealth is needed for life and still ruins it, so neither world is secured by it.","gn":["the Kural's plain premise, which it grants before turning it","the dangers of possession that its blunt premise about poverty leaves out"],"gr":2},{"a":"vp:36","b":"n:11","type":"qualifies","note":"Both have grey hair as the sign and the staff as age's companion; Naladiyar 11 says the wise renounced in infancy foreseeing grey, verse 36's speaker resolves only after forty, when grey has come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two measure renunciation by when it comes: the quatrain prizes foresight in childhood, while the fragment shows the ordinary case, a man who turns only when desire has already left him.","gn":["a standard by which its late resolve is the lesser kind","a first-person case of the man its wise contrast pass over, showing what makes him turn at last"],"gr":2},{"a":"vp:37","b":"n:183","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 183 bids one sow seed for the next life without confusion; verse 37 makes it a farmer's whole round, ploughing, sowing and watering, always and always.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Seed for the next life is not one act of sowing but a continuing labour, and the fragment spells out the farming year the quatrain compresses into a word.","gn":["the explicit term மறுமைக்கு வித்து that names what its farmer is sowing","the full labour of the field, which turns its single command into a discipline"]},{"a":"vp:38","b":"k:345","type":"develops","note":"Verse 38 repeats Kural 345 nearly word for word and adds two lines: while the body and ties remain, clinging karma drives the soul through many wombs.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's paradox, that even the body is too much for one who would end birth, gets its reason in the fragment: the body is where karma takes hold.","gn":["the authority of the Kural's own words for its opening","an account of why the body is excess, which the couplet asserts without explaining"],"gr":2},{"a":"vp:39","b":"k:350","type":"tension","note":"Kural 350 commands clinging to the one without clinging in order to let clinging go; verse 39 says removing sin while still in attachment is washing blood with blood.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"If the Kural's holding to the attachment-free one is itself an attachment, the fragment's simile denies that any attachment can cure another, so a release by devotion and a release by pure letting-go part here; the conflict depends on reading the Kural's பற்று as a real attachment.","gn":["a rival remedy, devotion as the cure of attachment, against which its blood-washing simile becomes a claim","a sharp objection that its 'ladder' of attachment must meet"],"gr":2},{"a":"vp:42","b":"vp:37","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 37 holds up the farmer who ploughs, sows and waters as the model of provision; verse 42 shows the ploughman who toils without seed as the fool.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The farmer's image cuts both ways in the fragment: what matters is not the labour, which both farmers do, but whether the seed of austerity was sown.","gr":4},{"a":"vp:42","b":"vp:20","type":"develops","note":"Verse 20 bids those who have wealth and pleasure (பொருளடு போகம்) still do austerity; verse 42 says that without austerity the pursuit of wealth and pleasures (பொருளடு போகங்கள்) is fruitless toil.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair make austerity both the duty of the prosperous and the cause of prosperity itself, so wealth and pleasure never stand apart from it.","gr":2},{"a":"vp:42","b":"k:377","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 377 says even hoarders of millions enjoy only as the apportioner apportioned; verse 42 says that without the seed of austerity the toil for wealth and pleasure bears nothing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both deny that effort alone secures enjoyment, but the Kural names an apportioning agent while the fragment puts the cause in one's own past austerity, a karma without a giver.","gn":["the point that its seed is a form of apportioning, set against a rival account of who apportions","a cause without a personal agent, which makes its வகுத்தான் visible as a choice"]},{"a":"vp:44","b":"a:151","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 151 the woman reports the man's reason that the poor cannot support dependents or laugh among kin, and says his heart has set grace aside; verse 44 gives the same reason, feeding kin's hunger, as one no one could fault.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The argument for leaving to earn wealth is the same in both, but the akam poem lets the one left behind weigh it against grace, while the fragment's voice treats it as needing no answer.","gn":["the cost of its argument, borne by the woman left behind","a direct statement of the man's case (learning, kin's hunger, the hard road) that the poem gives only as reported speech"],"gr":4},{"a":"vp:45","b":"vp:44","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 44 makes feeding hungry kin the reason to seek wealth; verse 45 shows the kin scorning and avoiding a man once he is ruined.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The kin's claim on a man's wealth is not matched by any claim of his on them: their loyalty lasts only as long as the wealth they are owed.","gr":3},{"a":"vp:45","b":"k:796","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 796 finds one benefit in ruin, that it measures one's kin; verse 45 shows that measure taken, the kin speaking as they please and standing out of reach.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural turns desertion into knowledge for later choices, while the fragment gives the desertion without consolation, so the 'benefit' appears as what it costs.","gn":["a frame that makes its bitter scene useful knowledge","the concrete scene of desertion that its 'measuring rod' implies"],"gr":2},{"a":"vp:46","b":"vp:4","type":"develops","note":"Verse 4 says women's hearts melt for another as fish delight in new floodwater; verse 46 says a divided woman's mind can no more be made one than oil spread on water gathered.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The fragment moves from women's hearts straying to their being past recall once divided, which completes its case for giving them up.","gr":4},{"a":"vp:48","b":"t:2048","type":"qualifies","note":"Both use the blind guiding the blind for a false teacher of release: the Valayapathi's அந்தகன் அந்தகற்கு ஆறு சொலல், Tirumular's குருடர்க்குக் கோல்காட்டிச் செல்லும் குருடர்.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Tirumular's blind guide fails for want of a true guru's sight, while the fragment names the missing thing as full knowledge in one who promises to clear past faults, so one proverb serves both a guru-centred and a knowledge-centred account of release.","gn":["the fragment's unnamed false teacher is set beside a worked-out Saiva doctrine of the false guru, which shows how common the charge was","Tirumular's image gains a witness outside Saivism in which the blind guide lacks knowledge that clears past deeds, not a guru's grace"]},{"a":"vp:49","b":"vp:57","type":"develops","note":"Verse 49 puts the பத்தினி among the old courtesan's models; verse 57 explains that model as serving in every way those who give matching wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Only the gloss in 57 shows why a chaste wife stands in a list of rivers, lutes and beasts: her devotion is taken over as a technique that lasts exactly as long as the payment.","gr":3},{"a":"vp:51","b":"n:370","type":"develops","note":"Both compare a courtesan's love to a river that fails with the rain; the Naladiyar's மாரி அறவே அறுமே and வாரி அறவே அறும் lie close to the Valayapathi's lines 3 and 1.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar gives only the ending, love cut off when income ends, while the fragment makes வாரி mean both flood and income and adds the rise, so the love is shown swelling with the wealth as precisely as it dries.","gn":["the fragment is seen reworking a known gnomic comparison, which explains its compression","the quatrain's single pun on வாரி proves capable of a full rise-and-fall cycle the quatrain itself leaves out"],"gr":4},{"a":"vp:57","b":"n:385","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar's wife sets her ever-renewed shyness against women who join many chests for wealth; the Valayapathi says those women serve their payers like பத்தினிப் பெண்டிர்.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they put the difference between wife and courtesan not in the service given, which the fragment says can look the same, but in what the Naladiyar's wife names: a shyness that never wears off and a bond that wealth did not buy.","gn":["the satirical comparison gains the wife's own account of the inner thing the courtesan only imitates","the wife's wonder is sharpened by a text conceding that the courtesan can copy the outward form of her devotion"],"gr":4},{"a":"vp:58","b":"vp:65","type":"develops","note":"The courtesan leaves the man whose wealth has perished for a richer one, like a beast moving to a fresh field; in 65 all followers leave a man whose wealth is spent, like birds from a fallen fruit tree.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Read beside 65, the courtesan's switching is not her special vice but one case of the rule the fragments state for kin and followers alike, so the satire of the courtesan rests on a general claim that people follow wealth."},{"a":"vp:61","b":"k:60","type":"develops","note":"Kural 60 calls good children the ornament of a home's excellence; Valayapathi 61 calls begetting children and keeping household dharma the chief virtue, and a life with deceitful women barren.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The fragment turns the Kural's praise of children into an argument against the courtesan: her union is measured by the household's fruit, children, and found to yield nothing.","gn":["the fragment's verdict on the courtesan's life is grounded in the Kural's ranking of the household's goods","the couplet's praise of children gains a counter-case showing what a life without the household's ornament is judged to lack"]},{"a":"vp:63","b":"k:1047","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1047: one whose poverty has no virtue is looked on as a stranger even by his mother; Valayapathi 63: without wealth, a man's women disregard him, his kin shrink, and his own children will not cling.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural limits its harsh rule to poverty without அறம் and names only the mother, while the fragment drops the limit and extends the desertion to wife, kin and children, which shows how far the claim reaches once its moral condition is removed.","gn":["the fragment's sweeping claim is seen to lack the qualifying condition the Kural attaches","the Kural's qualifier அறஞ்சாரா is shown to do real work once a version without it stands beside it"]},{"a":"vp:64","b":"k:1046","type":"develops","note":"Both say the poor man's well-founded words fail: the Kural's சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும், the Valayapathi's learning that goes புல்லென்று.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural says only that the poor man's meaning goes slack; the fragment names who lets it, the discerning assembly of the good, and carries the loss from his speech to the learning he teaches.","gn":["the fragment's complaint is placed as a development of a known Kural rule rather than an isolated grievance","the couplet's impersonal 'goes slack' gains named hearers, the good who weigh things, among those who discount the poor"],"gr":2},{"a":"vp:65","b":"n:283","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar's bees do not go to the hill when the kanthal is not in flower; the Valayapathi's birds circle a fruiting tree and leave it when it falls.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar shows only the loss, that the poor have no kin, while the fragment adds the converse, a servant with food surrounded and a noble without it deserted, so the pair shows wealth outranking birth in both directions.","gn":["the fragment's bird image is matched by the nature image an ethical anthology used for the same claim","the quatrain's one-way rule gains its converse, that the low-born with wealth win the following the poor lose"]},{"a":"vp:66","b":"k:249","type":"develops","note":"Kural 249 likens virtue done without அருள் to a confused man seeing the truth; Valayapathi 66 lists அருள்இல் அறன் among four goods it calls darkness put inside darkness.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural makes graceless virtue a single paradox, while the fragment sets it in a series with a vow without forbearance, a lineage without wealth and a kingdom without ministers, so grace appears as one of several enabling conditions without which a good is void.","gn":["the fragment's one item on grace gains the Kural's fuller case for grace as the root of virtue","the couplet's claim is shown to fit a general pattern of goods emptied by a missing condition, which the couplet alone does not show"],"gr":3},{"a":"vp:66","b":"k:160","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 160 ranks those who fast below those who bear harsh words; Valayapathi 66 calls a vow (நோன்பு) without forbearance (பொறைமை) darkness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural only ranks fasting below forbearance, but the fragment makes forbearance the condition without which a vow is worthless, a stronger claim that turns a ranking into a dependence.","gn":["the fragment's terse item is given the Kural's reason why bearing insult is the harder discipline","the couplet's ranking is shown being read by a later epic as a requirement rather than an order of merit"],"gr":4},{"a":"vp:70","b":"n:48","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 48 asks whether those who praise sandal and garlands have not seen vultures tear the corpse; Valayapathi 70 says the heart, though it sees the adorned hair burn, is still bewildered.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar treats delight in adornment as ignorance that the sight of the corpse would cure, while the fragment's heart has already seen the burning and is bewildered still, so the pair separates not knowing the body's end from knowing it to no effect.","gn":["the fragment's lament is sharpened by a verse that still trusts sight to cure desire","the quatrain's appeal to what one has seen is shown to have a limit, a heart that sees and is unmoved"]},{"a":"vp:71","b":"s:286","type":"tension","note":"Both start from crowned kings brought to nothing and rebuke those who see it unchanged: Pattinathar's கண்டு பின்னும், the Valayapathi's கண்டால் மயங்காதே. Pattinathar's way out is reaching the feet of the Lord of the golden hall; the Valayapathi's is standing in the highest path and striving.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"From the same sight the two draw opposed remedies, refuge at a god's feet for the Saiva poet and one's own steady effort for the fragment, which names no god; the tension holds on the reading that the fragment's ஊக்கம் is sufficient by itself.","gn":["the fragment's call to effort stands out as godless against a Saiva poem built on the same scene","Pattinathar's appeal to grace is shown to be one answer to a shared reproach, not the only conclusion the reproach allows"],"gr":4}]});
